 indicant et oppositiones in ordinem subordinatum convertunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:AS7","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:FL-01","text":"Regula qua seipsum propter unitatem numericam intensius diligitur et regula qua Deus supra seipsum diligitur cohaerent tantum per transitum non plene demonstratum ab unitate ad causalitatem universalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_52:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:FL-02","text":"Bonum ordinis universalis simultaneitatem creationis suadet, sed permissio creationis angelorum anterioris impedit ne ratio partis ad totum necessitatem temporalem constituat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_53:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:FL-03","text":"Nexus inter originem et gratiam pendet a conclusione probabiliori de gratia concreata, non ab axiomatibus creationis necessario deducta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_54:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:FL-04","text":"Proportio naturae ad gloriam ordinem providentiae manifestat, sed semper vacillat inter congruentiam realem et exclusionem cuiuslibet debiti vel causalitatis naturalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_54:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MP-01","text":"Deus mutatur ab obiecto dilectionis naturalis sub ratione boni universalis in obiectum visionis beatificae; continuitas obiecti cum discontinuo modo attingendi coniungitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_54:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MP-02","text":"Libertas mutatur a defectibilitate electionis ad impossibilitatem peccati, quia perfectio eius a potestate oppositorum ad firmitatem ordinis finis transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_54:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina participationis concentricae in qua causa prima esse, inclinationem et consummationem distribuit sine identitate partium cum fonte.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:A3","CH_GGVC_P_52:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:HSI-02","text":"Subjectum angelicum est forma subsistens sed receptiva, simul pars universi, vector dilectionis et capacitas visionis quae proprium modum per elevationem servat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_53:A8","CH_GGVC_P_54:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:HSI-03","text":"Tempus systematis est arcus punctuatus: initium sine materia, actus sine mora corporali et terminus sine futuro essentiali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_54:DNX9","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga a natura, sed recta distinctio rationum, conversio libera sub gratia et stabilis operatio intra bonum visum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_54:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata participationis, inclinationis naturalis, ordinis partis, elevationis gratuita et clausurae beatificae sufficiunt ad structuram praesentis gradus restituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B02","text":"Nondum plene determinantur modus quo causalitas naturalis electionem deformatam patitur, necessitas gratiae concreatae et nexus exactus inter capacitatem naturalem atque mensuram gloriae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B03","text":"Cohaerentia firmissima est ubi dependentia essendi in dilectionem Dei et indigentiam gratiae transit, atque ubi visio terminalis libertatem sine abolitione naturae confirmat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B04","text":"Reductioni plenae resistunt propositiones probabilisticae de simultaneitate creationis et gratia initiali, usus analogicus loci corporei, atque limes affirmatus inter praemium essentiale et gaudium accidentale.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T172042592077Z_bca9ebce","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 61–63","source_sha256":"6a7df5a8e9de76babf04a0f6dd1505c14d9e171fd7c41decd112f5e156a96925","sha256":"de52b0cf0b24da745d808f826bf67b7bc37267404a17a075ec5ecdd5097437e6","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:20:42.594332+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how the created angelic nature simultaneously receives natural inclination, elective freedom, and supernatural passage to the beatific end without confusion of orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_54:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis establishes the angel as a participated being whose dependence on God grounds the natural order of love, grace elevates created capacity, and the divine vision closes essential motion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:A3","CH_GGVC_P_52:A14","CH_GGVC_P_54:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BAS-03","text":"The argument proceeds through coordinated distinctions: essence is separated from being, natural love from elective love, universal good from beatifying good, and nature from grace and glory, so that they may then be ordered.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_52:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_54:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BAS-04","text":"The movement ascends from the causality of creation to the order of the universe, from order to the love of God, and from natural love to meritorious conversion and terminal vision.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:D1","CH_GGVC_P_52:D7","CH_GGVC_P_54:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BAS-05","text":"The progression is cumulative and spiral, because the same dependence of the creature recurs as participation in being, appetitive ordering, and beatific receptivity, with an amplified function at each return.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_52:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_54:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BAS-06","text":"Elevation never destroys the lower principle: nature remains in intellect, election, grace, and glory as a receptive capacity successively perfected.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:A2","CH_GGVC_P_54:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BAS-07","text":"Freedom is transformed from the election of particulars under the natural end to meritorious conversion under grace, and then to indefectible election within the end seen.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_54:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BAS-08","text":"The closure is essential but not operationally empty: merit and substantial increase cease, while fruition, ministry, and accidental increase remain.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:D9","CH_GGVC_P_54:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every angelic being is participated; therefore neither immateriality nor subsistence exempts the angel from creative causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:A1","CH_GGVC_P_53:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Nature remains a receptive and operative principle through all amplifications of intellect, will, grace, and glory.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:A2","CH_GGVC_P_54:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] The angel's proper good is contained within the good of the whole; hence cosmic order, love above oneself, and the distribution of perfections are governed by the same structure of participation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:A8","CH_GGVC_P_52:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_53:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Love is the root of every appetite, but it is transformed from natural inclination toward God as universal good into charity, merit, and supernatural fruition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:A1","CH_GGVC_P_52:D7","CH_GGVC_P_54:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Election orders particulars under an end received; where the end exceeds the capacity of nature, grace supplies a new motion without abolishing the free act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:A7","CH_GGVC_P_54:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Divine action perfects intellectual nature according to its free mode, not according to a necessity that empties the recipient's operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:A7","CH_GGVC_P_54:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The contingency of creation and the gratuity of glory depend upon the same divine freedom, although divine wisdom distributes both gifts fittingly according to the order of natures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:A6","CH_GGVC_P_54:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Angelic temporality comprises a created beginning and succession of acts without corruption of nature or the measure of bodily motion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_54:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Divine vision perfects the will not by passive immobility, but by indefectible election under the order of the end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:A16","CH_GGVC_P_54:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Attainment of the end closes merit and essential progress, but not fruition, natural operation, or the diffusion of perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:D9","CH_GGVC_P_54:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] God orders the entire structure as cause of being, naturally loved universal good, and supernaturally seen essence, yet always incomprehensible to the finite creature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:A14","CH_GGVC_P_54:A16","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] The degrees of nature, conversion, grace, and glory are coordinated hierarchically without nature producing the supernatural gift or rendering it due to itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_54:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:RMD-01","text":"Nature retains its identity as a created principle, but changes its function from voluntary inclination to incorruptible capacity and finally to a subject preserved in glory.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:A2","CH_GGVC_P_53:A7","CH_GGVC_P_54:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:RMD-02","text":"God returns functionally as producing cause, universal good, and beatific object; these are not parallel names, but degrees of causality, appetibility, and presence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:A1","CH_GGVC_P_52:A14","CH_GGVC_P_54:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:RMD-03","text":"The relation of part to whole migrates from the cosmology of origin into the order of love and thence into the providence of degrees, such that totality becomes the rule of creation, inclination, and consummation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:A8","CH_GGVC_P_52:A13","CH_GGVC_P_54:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:RMD-04","text":"Natural love does not vanish with the advent of charity: it is transformed from the root of the end into a meritorious act, then into fruition and the diffusion of possessed perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:A15","CH_GGVC_P_54:D4","CH_GGVC_P_54:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:RMD-05","text":"Freedom returns with variation: first it elects without discursion, then it is converted under grace, and finally it operates indefectibly within the end seen.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:A8","CH_GGVC_P_54:A7","CH_GGVC_P_54:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:RMD-06","text":"Time passes from the exclusion of eternity to the succession of acts and finally to the closure of the essential future, without reduction to bodily motion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_54:A12","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:RMD-07","text":"Having fulfilled its work of signification, bodily place vanishes as an ontological principle and is absorbed into the order of the spirit's presiding over the body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_53:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:CGD-01","text":"The difference between numerical and specific unity remains a local mechanism of love among angels; it persists at this level only insofar as it explains capacity and intensity according to receptive order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:D5","CH_GGVC_P_54:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:CGD-02","text":"The Empyrean Heaven remains a local and probable determination; the synthesis retains only the reclassification of place into a sign of governing power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:D6","CH_GGVC_P_53:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:CGD-03","text":"The simultaneity of angelic and corporeal creation does not rise to a necessary axiom, but provides a local reason why the perfection of the part is interpreted through the order of the whole.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_53:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:CGD-04","text":"The creation of angels in grace remains the more probable conclusion; at the present level it is amplified only as a nexus between created origin and the capacity for the first meritorious act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:D3","CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:CGD-05","text":"The distinction between essence and being transcends the question of origin, because it extends ontological dependence into the order of love and the need for grace.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_52:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_54:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:CGD-06","text":"The sequence of nature, election, and grace, locally distinguishing acts of will, becomes at this level a schema joining inclination, merit, and glory.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_54:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GNX-01","text":"Immateriality, subsistence, and incorruptibility do not signify being from oneself, eternity, or sufficiency for beatitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GNX-02","text":"Nature is neither opposed to irrationality nor does it alone produce the supernatural end; it is a right but finitely proportioned principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_54:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GNX-03","text":"Freedom is not constituted by necessary discursion, absolute indifference, or the power to choose evil.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_54:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GNX-04","text":"Grace neither substitutes for nature nor becomes a gift due or caused by natural proportion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:NX11","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GNX-05","text":"Self-preservation is not an absolute end, because the creature's love is ordered to the universal good of which it itself is a part.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_52:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:GNX-06","text":"Beatific closure is not the emptying of natural operation or inertia, but the exclusion of new essential merit with the permanence of fruition and ministry.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_54:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that created excellence can gradually pass into the divine mode, because even true vision confers neither being from oneself nor comprehension of God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that the universe is an aggregation of separate substances; otherwise causality, love, and glory could not be coordinated through the order of parts.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_53:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that created act is either autonomous or merely passive, because infused inclination and moving grace require the recipient's proper operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_54:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that every succession is measured by bodily motion or that everything incorruptible is without a beginning.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_54:DNX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the ultimate end can be surpassed indefinitely in a mobile manner, because such progress would destroy the notion of end and fruition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:DNX8","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BSA-01","text":"The system excludes a creature that is its own cause, measure of good, and principle of beatification.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BSA-02","text":"No neutral state outside the divine order is admitted: the angel, as a caused part, naturally inclined and supernaturally orderable, always remains within totality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_54:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BSA-03","text":"The tacitly forbidden possibility is equality of the creature with God, whether through autonomy of being, appetite for ascent, or comprehension in vision.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BSA-04","text":"At the beatific end, the essential future in which the comprehensor returns to the state of wayfarer or transcends the possessed end becomes unavailable.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_54:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:XD-01","text":"The natural primacy of love for God is not an extrinsic law imposed upon the will, but the translation of dependence in being into the order of appetite.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-01","CL_GGVC_21:GA-04","CL_GGVC_21:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_53:A3","CH_GGVC_P_52:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:XD-02","text":"The rectitude of natural inclination is given together with created nature; therefore it neither arises from a self-sufficient subject nor causes the fault of a deformed election.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-01","CL_GGVC_21:GA-02","CL_GGVC_21:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_53:A5","CH_GGVC_P_52:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:XD-03","text":"Supernatural elevation intensifies the structure of part to whole: what is naturally ordered to the universal good is ordered through grace to its immediate vision.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-03","CL_GGVC_21:GA-04","CL_GGVC_21:GA-06","CH_GGVC_P_53:A8","CH_GGVC_P_52:A13","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:XD-04","text":"Divine freedom and angelic freedom are not symmetrical: the former establishes the contingent field of being and end, while the latter operates within received nature and motion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-05","CL_GGVC_21:GA-06","CL_GGVC_21:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_53:A6","CH_GGVC_P_54:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:XD-05","text":"Incorruptibility preserves the subject of operation, but vision of goodness through its essence alone orders its will indefectibly.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-08","CL_GGVC_21:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_53:D4","CH_GGVC_P_54:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:XD-06","text":"Angelic history is finite without corporeal temporality: a created beginning, minimal succession of acts, and irreversible end constitute its measure.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-08","CL_GGVC_21:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_53:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_54:A12","CH_GGVC_P_54:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BSD-01","text":"Ontological dependence is converted into internal agency: received being generates natural appetite, and elevated appetite is consummated in the presence of its cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:A3","CH_GGVC_P_52:A1","CH_GGVC_P_54:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BSD-02","text":"The perfection of agency does not increase through multiplication of alternatives, but through a more certain ordering of elections to the true end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:A17","CH_GGVC_P_54:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BSD-03","text":"Created history has the form of an asymmetrical trajectory: the gift of origin is not a reward, the meritorious way is not the end, and the end does not return to the way.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:A5","CH_GGVC_P_54:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BSD-04","text":"Hierarchy becomes the gratuitous coordination of capacities and gifts, not an autonomous effect of nature nor a distribution lacking order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_54:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:BSD-05","text":"The mode of cognition changes the state of the will: cognition through particular notions admits discord, but the presence of the essence of goodness terminally fixes the order of election.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:D10","CH_GGVC_P_54:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:RLE-01","text":"Participation in being → dependence on the universal good → love of God → motion of grace → vision → confirmed will → diffusion of received order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:A3","CH_GGVC_P_52:A14","CH_GGVC_P_54:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:RLE-02","text":"Nature → inclination → election → conversion → fruition constitutes a path of amplification in which what was first a receptive principle is preserved and perfected at every degree.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_54:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:RLE-03","text":"Apparent contradiction → distinction of formal notions → preservation of both orders → subordination within a broader structure is a recurring methodological circuit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_54:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:RLE-04","text":"Degree of nature → intensity of conversion → measure of grace → degree of glory is a series of escalation, but nature's causality with respect to gift is interrupted at each transition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:D6","CH_GGVC_P_54:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:RLE-05","text":"The attractor of the whole structure is divine presence, which unites causality, appetibility, and beatitude, while never absorbing the creature into itself by assimilating it to itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:A14","CH_GGVC_P_54:A8","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:FGS-B01","text":"Being through participation → creation from nothing → intellectual nature subsisting but not self-sufficient.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:A3","CH_GGVC_P_53:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:FGS-B02","text":"Received nature → voluntary inclination → appetite for perfection → love of proper and universal good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:A3","CH_GGVC_P_52:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:FGS-B03","text":"Angel as part → order of the universe → subordination of proper good → love of God above oneself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:A8","CH_GGVC_P_52:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:FGS-B04","text":"End exceeding created capacity → motion of grace → free conversion of charity → merit of supernatural gift.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:A4","CH_GGVC_P_54:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:FGS-B05","text":"Meritorious act → minimal intellectual succession → vision of the divine essence → confirmation of the will in good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:D5","CH_GGVC_P_54:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:FGS-B06","text":"Degree of glory attained → closure of essential merit → permanence of nature → fruition, ministry, and accidental increase.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:D9","CH_GGVC_P_54:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:AFM-B01","text":"Distinction between Creator and creature → participation in being → angelic beginning → angel as part of the universe → inclination toward universal good → natural primacy of love for God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_53:A8","CH_GGVC_P_52:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:AFM-B02","text":"Initial natural perfection → excess of the beatific end → elevating grace → meritorious conversion → immediate vision → confirmed freedom → closure of essential progress with diffusion of perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_54:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_54:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MME-01","text":"The immaterial is reclassified as a mode of the participated creature, not as a third genus between God and the world.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_53:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MME-02","text":"Nature, freedom, and grace cease to be competitive principles and become causally distinct degrees of one elevated operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_54:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MME-03","text":"The opposition between self-love and love of God collapses, because the creature's proper good is situated within the universal good that causes and perfects it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_52:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MME-04","text":"Time is divided into the beginning of being, succession of acts, and closure of progress, none of which requires bodily motion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_54:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MME-05","text":"Terminal operation is reclassified: after beatitude, the act no longer merits the end but enjoys and diffuses the possessed end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_54:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level treats the origin, love, grace, merit, glory, and terminal state of angels as distinct questions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_54:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level orders those questions through participation in being, the relation of part to whole, and the sequence of nature, grace, and glory.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_54:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level resolves contradictions by distinguishing notions of the object and modes of causality, not by eliminating either member.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_54:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level produces a finitely receptive creature whose history passes from a contingent beginning through a succession of acts to an irreversible end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_54:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological level performs the form of the system: stratified distinctions preserve lower levels, indicate the excess of the end, and convert oppositions into a subordinated order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:AS7","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:FL-01","text":"The rule by which one loves oneself more intensely on account of numerical unity and the rule by which God is loved above oneself cohere only through a transition not fully demonstrated from unity to universal causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_52:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:FL-02","text":"The good of universal order suggests simultaneity of creation, but the permission of prior angelic creation prevents the part-to-whole rationale from establishing temporal necessity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_53:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:FL-03","text":"The nexus between origin and grace depends on the more probable conclusion concerning concreated grace, not on axioms necessarily deduced from creation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_54:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:FL-04","text":"The proportion of nature to glory manifests the order of providence, but always oscillates between real fittingness and the exclusion of every due claim or natural causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_54:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MP-01","text":"God is transformed from the object of natural love under the notion of universal good into the object of beatific vision; continuity of object is joined with a discontinuous mode of attainment.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_54:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MP-02","text":"Freedom is transformed from the defectibility of election to the impossibility of sin, because its perfection is transferred from the power of opposites to the firmness of the order of the end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_54:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_54:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a machine of concentric participation in which the first cause distributes being, inclination, and consummation without identity of the parts with the source.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:A3","CH_GGVC_P_52:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:HSI-02","text":"The angelic subject is a subsisting but receptive form, at once a part of the universe, a vector of love, and a capacity for vision that preserves its proper mode through elevation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_53:A8","CH_GGVC_P_54:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:HSI-03","text":"The system's time is a punctuated arc: beginning without matter, act without bodily delay, and end without an essential future.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_54:DNX9","CH_GGVC_P_54:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is not flight from nature, but the right distinction of notions, free conversion under grace, and stable operation within the good seen.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_54:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of participation, natural inclination, the order of the part, gratuitous elevation, and beatific closure suffice to reconstruct the structure of the present level.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B02","text":"The manner in which natural causality permits deformed election, the necessity of concreated grace, and the exact nexus between natural capacity and the measure of glory are not yet fully determined.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B03","text":"The strongest coherence is where dependence in being passes into love of God and need for grace, and where terminal vision confirms freedom without abolition of nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_52:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B04","text":"Probabilistic propositions concerning the simultaneity of creation and initial grace, the analogical use of bodily place, and the affirmed limit between essential reward and accidental joy resist full reduction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_53:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_54:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_21@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_21:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 61–63","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 60 · Prima pars · Quaestio 61 · Prima pars · Quaestio 62"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 64–66","order":22,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_55","CH_GGVC_P_56","CH_GGVC_P_57"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_22@R001","sha256":"06c675ef957ce84dab0d3ae6cedb18f516577888293ff17ce500d2af3b0392e7","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo culpa intellectualis, poena daemonica et mutabilitas corporalis intra creationem a bono principio productam consistere possint sine natura mala aut principio adverso.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_57:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-02","text":"Responsio globalis naturam creatam bonam servat, malum in defectu ordinis voluntarii locat, poenam ut privationem selectivam constituit et corpus ut bonum particulare restituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit discriminatio recursiva naturae et gratiae, obiecti et modi, creationis et motus, causalitatis directae et ordinationis indirectae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-04","text":"Motus transit a possibilitate defectus voluntarii ad eius fixationem poenalem, deinde ad containmentum providentiale et causalitatem ordinatam mundi corporalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_56:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_57:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-05","text":"Progressio est cumulativa et spiralis: eadem distinctio inter naturam bonam et ordinem deficientem redit moraliter, epistemice, poenaliter et cosmologice, sed functionem singulis reditibus mutat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_56:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-06","text":"Probatio identidem dissolvit transitum illicitum a defectu, nocumento vel tenebris ad naturam malam, causam malignam aut regionem ontologicam independentem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-07","text":"Clausura duplicatur: iudicium finale statum malorum terminat, dum ordinatio universi in bonitatem divinam omnem naturam et causalitatem creatam circumcludit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:A15","CH_GGVC_P_57:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-08","text":"Malitia creata agere potest per consensum, deceptionem et motum, sed nec cogere nec creare nec ordinem providentialem relinquere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Omnis natura creata, spiritualis vel corporalis, bonum naturale retinet quatenus esse et operationem a primo principio participat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A11","CH_GGVC_P_56:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Malum non est substantia positiva, sed defectus rectitudinis, relationis aut ordinis in natura bona praesupposita.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A1","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_57:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Nulla creatura est ultima regula sui actus aut finis; rectitudo et finalitas creata dependent ab ordine divino superiori.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A3","CH_GGVC_P_57:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Distinctio naturae et gratiae primum possibilitatem meriti atque defectus aperit, deinde cognitionem naturalem servat ubi caritas et cognitio affectiva amittuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A14","CH_GGVC_P_56:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Libertas angelica ante electionem est potentia oppositorum, post adhaesionem autem fit determinatio irreversibilis eiusdem agentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A16","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Bonum obiective appetibile potest moraliter deformari per modum appropriationis, finem perversum aut usum insipientem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A6","CH_GGVC_P_57:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Deus causat esse et ordinem boni, non defectum moralem; malitiam creatam tamen indirecte intra providentiam ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:DNX7","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Causalitas creata est limitata et stratificata: potest suggerere, consentire, movere aut transmutare, sed non totam substantiam ex nihilo producere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_57:A4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Poena naturam intellectualem non annihilat, sed gratiam privat, voluntatem frustratur et subiectum ordini sibi contrario alligat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_56:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Ordo totius, non commodum partis separatae, est mensura diversitatis, nocumenti, ministerii et perfectionis creatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A10","CH_GGVC_P_57:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Tempus creatum multipliciter ordinatur: electio angelica successione operationum, ministerium daemonum termino eschatologico, corpora mutatione formarum in materia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A15","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Malum nullum principium, regnum aut spatium ontologicum extra bonum creatum et causalitatem primi principii constituere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_57:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Cognitio veri non efficit necessario rectitudinem voluntatis, caritatem aut participationem beatificam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Participatio ordinata bonum creatum ad Deum refert; appropriatio autonomica idem bonum a regula et fine suo separat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A12","CH_GGVC_P_55:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_57:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-01","text":"Bonum identitatem stabilem retinet, sed munus mutat: est obiectum appetitus, residuum naturae punitae et participatio cosmica quae etiam usum perversum possibilitat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A6","CH_GGVC_P_56:D8","CH_GGVC_P_57:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-02","text":"Natura bona redit functionaliter ut subiectum culpae, fundamentum quod poena non destruit et principium quo malitia corporalis absoluta excluditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_56:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_57:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-03","text":"Libertas mutatur ab apertura conversionum ad adhaesionem definitivam; obstinatio libertatem naturalem non delet, sed potestatem reversionis absorbet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A5","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6","CH_GGVC_P_56:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-04","text":"Cognitio migrat a conditione electionis ad residuum poenae: verum cognitum manet, sed ab amore, sapientia affectiva et rectitudine confessionis separatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-05","text":"Actio angelica redit sub forma causalitatis limitatae: primum suggerit sine coactione, deinde providentialiter impugnat, denique corporalia movet sine influxu creativo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_56:D10","CH_GGVC_P_57:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-06","text":"Poena transit a consequentia culpae ad structuram selectivam quae naturam servat, voluntatem renitentem afficit et situm iuridico-providentialem constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_56:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-07","text":"Tempus migrat a successione discreta actuum angelicorum ad ministerium daemonum provisoriae durationis et ad mutationem corporalem sine defectu causalitatis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-08","text":"Excellentia invertitur: in angelo minuit pronitatem sed auget motivum superbiae; in universo inaequalitas a signo meriti ad instrumentum perfectionis totius transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A17","CH_GGVC_P_57:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-09","text":"Taxonomiae peccatorum, dignitas primi lapsi et multiplicitas locorum poenalium, opere locali peracto, in distinctiones generales affectus, causalitatis et ordinis absorbentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:D4","CH_GGVC_P_55:D14","CH_GGVC_P_56:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-01","text":"Distinctiones inter peccata daemonibus secundum affectum vel reatum convenientia atque quaestio ordinis primi lapsi locales manent; globaliter tantum ostendunt culpam naturae capacitatem sequi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:D4","CH_GGVC_P_55:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-02","text":"Gradus revelationis daemonibus concessae et mechanismus poenae sensibilis infra hunc gradum indeterminati manent neque ad structuram globalem necessarii sunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-03","text":"Doctrina localis de forma, materia et generatione amplificatur tantum quatenus distinctionem universalem creationis a transmutatione causaliter stabilit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A14","CH_GGVC_P_57:A15","CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-04","text":"Bonitas naturalis sursum amplificatur: ex inclinatione intellectuali ad bonum et natura daemonica residuali fit regula communis etiam creaturae corporalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:D7","CH_GGVC_P_56:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-05","text":"Suggestio angelica et motus corporalis unitates coniungunt ut duae species causalitatis secundae quae effectum inducunt sine coactione voluntatis aut creatione formae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_57:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-06","text":"Transitus maior procedit a malo ut defectu boni electi ad poenam ut conservationem naturae, inde ad mundum cuius nocumenta ex ordine totius aestimantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-07","text":"Exemplar mundi corporalis ut poena lapsus spiritualis superatur; historia culpae angelicae non fit principium cosmogenesis aut diversitatis naturalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-08","text":"Titulus daemonis ut potentiae mundanae recodificatur socialiter et moraliter: dominatio sequitur servitutem vel consensum, non auctoritatem creativam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_55:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-01","text":"Nulla natura intellectualis aut corporalis habet malum ut inclinationem essentialem vel principium substantiale.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_57:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-02","text":"Nullum malum purum aut principium malignum independens admittitur iuxta causalitatem unius creatoris.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_57:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-03","text":"Cognitio veri vel boni non identificatur cum caritate, rectitudine voluntatis aut conversione.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-04","text":"Libertas intellectualis non requirit deliberationem sensitivam, discursum temporalem aut mutabilitatem indefinitam post electionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-05","text":"Nulla creatura spiritualis potest ex nihilo creare aut formas corporales influxu formali absoluto producere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_57:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-06","text":"Corpus non est malum secundum naturam neque mundus corporalis poena primitiva lapsus spiritualis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_57:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-07","text":"Utilitas providentialis impugnationis daemonicae culpam agentis non dissolvit neque voluntatem perversam bonam efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_56:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-08","text":"Temporalitas, corruptio et mutatio formarum non inferunt annihilationem, originem malignam aut recessionem rerum a causalitate divina.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_57:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-09","text":"Poena spiritus non reducitur ad passionem corporalem, alterationem organi aut situm localem circumscriptivum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_56:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:DBN-01","text":"Systema requirit ut causalitas essendi et causalitas defectus sint asymmetricae: prima ad Deum reducitur, secunda ad deficientem ordinem actus creati.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:DNX7","CH_GGVC_P_57:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet corruptionem moralem naturam subiectam substantialiter delere; aliter nec culpa ut defectus nec poena ut privatio intelligi posset.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet bonitatem rerum ex commodo privato aestimari, quia nocumentum particulare intra ordinem universi aliam rationem recipit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_57:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet creationem, suggestionem, transmutationem et alligationem poenalem esse species univocae eiusdem causalitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_56:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet omnem inaequalitatem naturalem esse effectum culpae antecedentis aut distributionem poenarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_57:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse debet creaturam posse se constituere principium ultimum sui finis extra participationem et bonitatem divinam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_57:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas profundissime exclusa est regnum mali ontologice sui iuris, quod naturam, causalitatem aut spatium proprium contra primum principium possideat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_57:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSA-02","text":"Autonomia absoluta creaturae intellectualis indisponibilis manet: nec finis ultimus nec visio Dei nec beatitudo ex sola perfectione naturali appropriantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSA-03","text":"Malum sine bono naturali, obiectivo vel operativo quod deformet aut usurpet est ontologice inadmissibile.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSA-04","text":"Nulla zona creata neutralis extra providentiam et ordinem divinitus institutum relinquitur, etiam ubi voluntas creata ordinem illum repudiat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_57:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSA-05","text":"Reversio daemonica post electionem ultimam est absentia structurans: eius impossibilitas poenam, temporalitatem et clausuram eschatologicam ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SNX5","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSA-06","text":"Cosmologia ex culpa praecosmica deducta excluditur; mundus non est reliquum poenale rebellionis spiritualis, sed institutio sapientiae ad perfectionem totius.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-01","text":"Malum per totam structuram parasiticum est: lapsus bonum eligit inordinate, poena bonitatem naturae retinet, corpus autem bonum particulare manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_22:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_56:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-02","text":"Agens spirituale creatum suggerere vel corporalia movere potest, sed neque consensum cogit neque totam substantiam creat; eius causalitas simul moralis et naturalis terminatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-07","CL_GGVC_22:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_57:A16","CH_GGVC_P_57:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-03","text":"Cum poena daemonica electionem actualem sequatur, sed inaequalitas cosmica sapientiae institutionem exprimat, diversitas naturae per se signum culpae esse non potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-09","CL_GGVC_22:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6","CH_GGVC_P_57:A12","CH_GGVC_P_57:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-04","text":"Impugnatio daemonica indirecte ordinari potest ad bonum humanum, sicut bonitas corporalis fit occasio culpae solum per usum; malum igitur in intentione et usu, non in instrumento, consistit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-06","CL_GGVC_22:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_57:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-05","text":"Unus ordo creationis plura tempora continet: decisionem angelicam discretam et irreversibilem, ministerium daemonum provisorium et mutationem corporalem continuabilem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-06","text":"Eadem participatio boni quae creaturam ad Deum refert fit materia culpae quando voluntas finem vel perfectionem propria virtute sibi appropriat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-06","CL_GGVC_22:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_55:A12","CH_GGVC_P_57:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-07","text":"Veritas sine caritate et natura sine rectitudine morali simul permanere possunt; ideo poena spiritualis privativa est potius quam substantiae destructiva.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-04","CL_GGVC_22:GA-09","CL_GGVC_22:GA-13","CH_GGVC_P_56:A3","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-08","text":"Unitas essendi tollit principia creativa independentia, unde daemon potest dominari per servitutem socialem, numquam autem divinitatem creativam adipisci.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","CL_GGVC_22:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_57:D1","CH_GGVC_P_57:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSD-01","text":"Causalitas ontologica est asymmetrica: omne esse positivum ad unum principium reducitur, defectus autem non postulat causam malam parallelamente productivam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_22:GA-07","CL_GGVC_22:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_57:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_55:DNX7","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum intellectuale creatum est centrum agendi dependens: potest se falso constituere quasi principium finis, sed electio eius dependentiam ontologicam non aufert.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-03","CL_GGVC_22:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_55:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSD-03","text":"Epistemologia moralis requirit separationem veri cogniti a modo affectivo quo cognoscens illud ordinat, confitetur aut instrumentum deceptionis facit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-06","CL_GGVC_22:GA-13","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSD-04","text":"Definitivitas actus spiritualis non immobilizat universum: voluntas daemonica manet fixa, dum ministerium eius terminatur et materia corporalis formas mutat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSD-05","text":"Potestas daemonica est politica et parasitica potius quam creativa: oritur ex suggestione, consensu et servitute, atque in subiectionem poenalem et usum providentialem convertitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-07","CL_GGVC_22:GA-08","CL_GGVC_22:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_57:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSD-06","text":"Ordo universalis potest bonitatem naturarum servare et damna particularia continere sine malum moraliter approbando aut commodum singulare in mensuram totius erigendo.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-07","CL_GGVC_22:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_57:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSD-07","text":"Methodus systematis est correctio generis quaestionis: a re mala ad relationem deficientem, ab ignorantia ad privationem gratiae, a productione ad modum causalitatis transit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_22:GA-04","CL_GGVC_22:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_55:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RLE-01","text":"Attractor totius est bonitas divina repraesentata per naturas diversas, ordinem partium et limites causalitatis creatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A11","CH_GGVC_P_55:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RLE-02","text":"Participatio boni → appetitus perfectionis → appropriatio extra regulam → defectus ordinis → privatio beatitudinis → renisus dolorosus circa bonum adhuc appetitum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A12","CH_GGVC_P_55:D2","CH_GGVC_P_56:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RLE-03","text":"Cognitio naturalis servata → veritas sine lumine affectivo → confessio instrumentalis vel deceptiva → voluntas non conversa → obstinatio confirmata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:D3","CH_GGVC_P_56:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RLE-04","text":"Suggestio → consensus non coactus → communio culpae → subiectio poenalis → impugnatio continuata → utilitas providentialis indirecta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_55:D12","CH_GGVC_P_56:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RLE-05","text":"Bonum corporale participatum → attractio → usus insipiens → apparentia mali corporalis → reclassificatio nocumenti secundum naturam et ordinem totius.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:D3","CH_GGVC_P_57:A8","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RLE-06","text":"Excellentia maior → pronitas naturalis minor → motivum superbiae maius → electio spiritualis intensior → immobilitas post adhaesionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_55:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RLE-07","text":"Creatio divina → motus causarum secundarum → transmutatio rerum iam existentium → conservatio essendi → relatio effectuum ad perfectionem universi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A15","CH_GGVC_P_57:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FGS-B01","text":"Communitas essendi in naturis diversis → unum principium creativum → bonitas ontologica omnis creaturae → exclusio naturae vel creatoris mali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A1","CH_GGVC_P_57:A2","CH_GGVC_P_57:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FGS-B02","text":"Natura creata + regula superior + dona gratuita → rectitudo participata, non autonoma → possibilitas defectus sine malitia naturali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A3","CH_GGVC_P_56:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_57:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FGS-B03","text":"Bonum spirituale appetitum + modus propriae virtutis + recusatio subiectionis → appropriatio finis → superbia → culpa.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A9","CH_GGVC_P_55:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FGS-B04","text":"Apprehensio angelica definitiva → adhaesio voluntatis fixa → gratia affectiva ablata, natura servata → frustratio voluntatis → poena spiritualis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:A5","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FGS-B05","text":"Causalitas creata limitata → suggestio, motus et transmutatio → effectus reales sine creatione vel coactione → reassumptio effectuum in providentiam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_56:A13","CH_GGVC_P_57:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FGS-B06","text":"Diversitas naturarum et temporum → ministerium provisorium et mutationes particulares → perfectio universi → iudicium finale et ordinatio in bonitatem divinam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A11","CH_GGVC_P_56:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:AFM-B01","text":"Communitas essendi → unum principium creator → bonitas omnis naturae → distinctio creationis et motus → causalitas secunda limitata → perfectio universi → finis divinus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A2","CH_GGVC_P_57:A4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A15","CH_GGVC_P_57:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:AFM-B02","text":"Voluntas creata sub regula → bonum appropriatum inordinate → electio definitiva → privatio gratiae et renisus → ministerium daemonum temporale → clausura iudicialis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_55:D2","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6","CH_GGVC_P_56:A10","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:AFM-B03","text":"Analogia falsa → divisio modalis → mutatio generis causae → reclassificatio mali → reintegratio naturae, poenae et corporis in unum ordinem causal-finalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MME-01","text":"Oppositio spiritus boni et corporis mali corruit: natura spiritualis potest culpabiliter deficere, dum natura corporalis bonum particulare manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MME-02","text":"Libertas reclassificatur ab indeterminata mutabilitate perpetua ad potestatem electionis cuius adhaesio secundum modum cognitionis fieri potest definitiva.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:A6","CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MME-03","text":"Causalitas describitur ut tabula graduata: Deus creat, causae compositae transmutant, substantiae spirituales movent, suggerentes autem consensum non cogunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A15","CH_GGVC_P_55:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MME-04","text":"Poena ex destructione naturae transfertur ad privationem gratiae, fixationem voluntatis, frustrationem appetitus et apprehensionem ordinis contrarii.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_56:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MME-05","text":"Tempus dividitur in successionem intellectualem discretam, ministerium eschatologice terminatum et mutationem corporalem quae originem divinam non dissolvit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MME-06","text":"Hierarchia et inaequalitas desinunt esse indices simplices bonitatis moralis: excellentia potest motivum superbiae augere, diversitas autem perfectionem cosmicam efficere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_57:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MME-07","text":"Veritas separatur a salute: cognitio naturalis, confessio materialis et subtilitas intellectus possunt manere ubi caritas et sapientia affectiva desunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_56:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei complectitur originem culpae angelicae, cognitionem et poenam daemonum, atque originem, finem et formationem corporum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis omnia regit per dependentiam actus creati a regula, distinctionem naturae et gratiae, atque separationem creationis a motu materiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus quaestiones reconstituit: analogias universalizantes dividit, genus causae mutat et apparentem oppositionem in distinctiones modales resolvit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologico-temporalis producit realitatem creatam bonam sed dependentem, tempora heterogenea et causalitatem divinam quae mutationem sustinet sine defectu morali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologico-performativus assertiones efficit per distinctiones repetitas quae ipsam apparentiam mali substantialis in ordines relationis, usus et causalitatis convertunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FL-01","text":"Universalitas causalitatis divinae et exclusio Dei ut causae defectus cohaerent tantum per distinctionem inter productionem essendi et deficientem modum actus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:DNX7","CH_GGVC_P_57:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FL-02","text":"Inclinatio naturalis ad bonum cum obstinatione perpetua in malo componitur per distinctionem obiecti boni a modo electionis et per definitivitatem adhaesionis, non per dissolutionem tensionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD7","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FL-03","text":"Providentia malitiam daemonum ad utilitatem indirectam ordinat, dum intentio culpabilis intacta manet; nexus inter effectum bonum et agentem malum causaliter fragilis permanet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FL-04","text":"Perfectio universi ad nocumentum particulare explicandum valet solum si teleologia totius iam accepta est; ratio igitur ab axiomatica finalitate dependet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FL-05","text":"Poena spiritualis per apprehensionem et renisum explicatur, sed mechanismus poenae sensibilis et relatio ignis ad naturam incorpoream non plene evolvuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT7","CH_GGVC_P_56:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MP-01","text":"Libertas punctum mutationis attingit ubi potentia ad opposita, sine cessatione naturae liberae, fit immobilis adhaesio electioni iam factae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A5","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MP-02","text":"Corpus mutatur ab apparente muscipula quae a Deo retrahit in bonum participatum cuius vis alliciens moraliter determinatur ab usu.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A8","CH_GGVC_P_57:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MP-03","text":"Inaequalitas mutat campum: inter angelos afficit pronitatem et motivum culpae, in cosmologia autem fit conditio perfectionis non index meriti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_57:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina hierarchica ab uno creatore instituta, in qua causae secundae effectus producunt sine rivalitate et partes ad bonitatem totius referuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A4","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum intellectuale est nodus dependens qui ordinem recipit, bonum eligit et per modum adhaesionis suum statum moralem definit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est structura nidificata: instans decisionis spiritualis, intervallum ministerii daemonici, series mutationum materialium et terminus iudicialis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:HSI-04","text":"Daemon intra hanc machinam est operator parasiticus: non creat, sed suggerit, veritatem instrumentaliter profert et per consensum alienum potestatem acquirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_56:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:HSI-05","text":"Certamen possibile non est fuga a corpore, sed ordinatio usus et consensus sub providentia, qua res bonae participatae a perverso modo appropriationis distinguuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_57:A8","CH_GGVC_P_56:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata extracta sufficiunt ad restituendam structuram praesentis gradus: culpa ut defectus ordinis, poena ut privatio conservans et corpus ut creatum bonum sub causalitate divina.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B02","text":"Firmissimum centrum est coniunctio regulae extrinsecae voluntatis, apprehensionis definitivae et distinctionis creationis a motu rei iam existentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B03","text":"Non satis determinantur dignitas primi lapsi, mensura revelationis daemonicae et mechanismus poenae sensibilis; haec tamen structurae globali non sunt constitutiva.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B04","text":"Systema cohaeret si causatio essendi, defectus actus, conservatio naturae et causalitas secunda stricte distinguuntur; confusio horum graduum cohaerentiam dissolveret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:MRT8","CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat a possibilitate metaphysica defectus per poenam et providentiam ad ordinationem causal-finalem universi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT8","CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B06","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae resistunt processus internus quo electio fit irreversibilis et sufficientia perfectionis totius ad nocumenta particularia explicanda; ibi structura praemissis fortibus nititur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T172128882988Z_d8d5c784","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 64–66","source_sha256":"06c675ef957ce84dab0d3ae6cedb18f516577888293ff17ce500d2af3b0392e7","sha256":"945a25d3e6aaf3a8f790a48c829510b015c5610ef1a5a058fb7df394d5e63b96","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:21:28.885021+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how intellectual fault, demonic punishment, and bodily mutability can subsist within creation produced by a good principle, without an evil nature or an opposing principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_57:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-02","text":"The global response preserves created nature as good, locates evil in the defect of voluntary order, establishes punishment as selective privation, and restores the body as a particular good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-03","text":"The argument proceeds through a recursive distinction of nature and grace, object and mode, creation and motion, direct causality and indirect ordering.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-04","text":"The movement passes from the possibility of voluntary defect to its penal fixation, then to providential containment and the ordered causality of the bodily world.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_56:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_57:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-05","text":"The progression is cumulative and spiral: the same distinction between good nature and deficient order returns morally, epistemically, penally, and cosmologically, but changes function at each return.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_56:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-06","text":"The proof repeatedly dissolves the illicit inference from defect, harm, or darkness to an evil nature, a malignant cause, or an independent ontological region.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-07","text":"The closure is twofold: final judgment terminates the state of the wicked, while the ordering of the universe encompasses every nature and created causality within divine goodness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:A15","CH_GGVC_P_57:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BAS-08","text":"Created malice can act through consent, deception, and motion, but can neither compel nor create nor escape providential order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every created nature, spiritual or bodily, retains natural goodness insofar as it participates in being and operation from the first principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A11","CH_GGVC_P_56:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Evil is not a positive substance, but a defect of rectitude, relation, or order in a presupposed good nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A1","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_57:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] No creature is the ultimate rule of its own act or end; created rectitude and finality depend upon a higher divine order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A3","CH_GGVC_P_57:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The distinction between nature and grace first opens the possibility of merit and defect, then preserves natural knowledge where charity and affective knowledge are lost.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A14","CH_GGVC_P_56:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Angelic freedom before choice is a potency for opposites, but after adherence becomes an irreversible determination of the same agent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A16","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] An objectively desirable good can be morally deformed through the mode of appropriation, a perverse end, or foolish use.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A6","CH_GGVC_P_57:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] God causes being and the order of good, not moral defect; yet He indirectly orders created malice within providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:DNX7","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Created causality is limited and stratified: it can suggest, consent, move, or transmute, but not produce an entire substance from nothing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_57:A4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Punishment does not annihilate intellectual nature, but deprives it of grace, frustrates the will, and binds the subject to an order contrary to itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_56:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] The order of the whole, not the advantage of the separate part, is the measure of diversity, harm, ministry, and created perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A10","CH_GGVC_P_57:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Created time is ordered in multiple ways: angelic choice by succession of operations, the ministry of demons by an eschatological term, bodies by the change of forms in matter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A15","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Evil can establish no principle, kingdom, or ontological space outside created good and the causality of the first principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_57:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Knowledge of truth does not necessarily produce rectitude of will, charity, or beatific participation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Ordered participation refers created good to God; autonomous appropriation separates the same good from its rule and end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A12","CH_GGVC_P_55:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_57:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-01","text":"Good retains a stable identity, but changes function: it is the object of appetite, the residue of punished nature, and cosmic participation that also makes perverse use possible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A6","CH_GGVC_P_56:D8","CH_GGVC_P_57:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-02","text":"Good nature returns functionally as the subject of fault, the foundation that punishment does not destroy, and the principle by which absolute bodily malice is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_56:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_57:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-03","text":"Freedom changes from an openness to conversions into definitive adherence; obstinacy does not destroy natural freedom, but absorbs the power of return.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A5","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6","CH_GGVC_P_56:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-04","text":"Knowledge migrates from a condition of choice to a residue of punishment: known truth remains, but is separated from love, affective wisdom, and rectitude of confession.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-05","text":"Angelic action returns under the form of limited causality: first it suggests without compulsion, then attacks providentially, finally moves bodily things without creative influx.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_56:D10","CH_GGVC_P_57:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-06","text":"Punishment passes from a consequence of fault to a selective structure that preserves nature, affects the resistant will, and establishes a juridical-providential condition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_56:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-07","text":"Time migrates from the discrete succession of angelic acts to demonic ministry of provisional duration and to bodily change without defect in divine causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-08","text":"Excellence is inverted: in the angel it diminishes proneness but increases the motive of pride; in the universe inequality is transferred from a sign of merit to an instrument of the perfection of the whole.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A17","CH_GGVC_P_57:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RMD-09","text":"Taxonomies of sins, the dignity of the first fallen one, and the multiplicity of penal places, once their local work is completed, are absorbed into general distinctions of affection, causality, and order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:D4","CH_GGVC_P_55:D14","CH_GGVC_P_56:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-01","text":"Distinctions among sins fitting demons according to affection or guilt, and the question of the rank of the first fallen one, remain local; globally they show only that fault follows nature's capacity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:D4","CH_GGVC_P_55:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-02","text":"The degree of revelation granted to demons and the mechanism of sensible punishment remain indeterminate below this level and are not necessary to the global structure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-03","text":"The local doctrine concerning form, matter, and generation is amplified only insofar as it causally establishes the universal distinction between creation and transmutation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A14","CH_GGVC_P_57:A15","CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-04","text":"Natural goodness is amplified upward: from intellectual inclination toward good and residual demonic nature it becomes a common rule even of bodily creature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:D7","CH_GGVC_P_56:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-05","text":"Angelic suggestion and bodily motion connect the units as two species of secondary causality that induce an effect without compulsion of the will or creation of form.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_57:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-06","text":"The major transition proceeds from evil as a defect of chosen good to punishment as preservation of nature, thence to a world whose harms are assessed from the order of the whole.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-07","text":"The model of the bodily world as punishment for spiritual fall is surpassed; the history of angelic fault does not become the principle of cosmogenesis or natural diversity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:CGD-08","text":"The title of demon as worldly power is recodified socially and morally: domination follows servitude or consent, not creative authority.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_55:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-01","text":"No intellectual or bodily nature has evil as an essential inclination or substantial principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_57:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-02","text":"No pure evil or independent malignant principle is admitted alongside the causality of one creator.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_57:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-03","text":"Knowledge of truth or good is not identified with charity, rectitude of will, or conversion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-04","text":"Intellectual freedom does not require sensitive deliberation, temporal discourse, or indefinite mutability after choice.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-05","text":"No spiritual creature can create from nothing or produce bodily forms by absolute formal influx.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_57:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-06","text":"The body is not evil according to nature, nor is the bodily world the primitive punishment of spiritual fall.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_57:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-07","text":"The providential utility of demonic attack neither dissolves the agent's fault nor makes the perverse will good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_56:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-08","text":"Temporality, corruption, and the change of forms do not imply annihilation, a malignant origin, or the withdrawal of things from divine causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_57:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:GNX-09","text":"The punishment of spirit is not reduced to bodily passion, alteration of an organ, or a circumscribed local situation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_56:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:DBN-01","text":"The system requires that the causality of being and the causality of defect be asymmetrical: the former is reduced to God, the latter to the deficient order of the created act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:DNX7","CH_GGVC_P_57:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that moral corruption substantially destroys the underlying nature; otherwise neither fault as defect nor punishment as privation could be understood.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that the goodness of things is assessed from private advantage, because particular harm receives another account within the order of the universe.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_57:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that creation, suggestion, transmutation, and penal binding are univocal species of the same causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_56:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that every natural inequality is the effect of antecedent fault or a distribution of punishments.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_57:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that a creature can constitute itself the ultimate principle of its end outside participation and divine goodness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_57:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSA-01","text":"The possibility most deeply excluded is an ontologically self-governing kingdom of evil that possesses its own nature, causality, or space against the first principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_57:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSA-02","text":"Absolute autonomy of the intellectual creature remains unavailable: neither ultimate end nor vision of God nor beatitude is appropriated from natural perfection alone.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSA-03","text":"Evil without a natural, objective, or operative good which it deforms or usurps is ontologically inadmissible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSA-04","text":"No created neutral zone outside providence and divinely instituted order is left, even where the created will repudiates that order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_57:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSA-05","text":"Demonic return after ultimate choice is a structuring absence: its impossibility orders punishment, temporality, and eschatological closure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SNX5","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSA-06","text":"A cosmology deduced from pre-cosmic fault is excluded; the world is not a penal remnant of spiritual rebellion, but an institution of wisdom for the perfection of the whole.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-01","text":"Evil is parasitic throughout the whole structure: the fall chooses good inordinately, punishment retains the goodness of nature, while the body remains a particular good.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_22:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_56:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-02","text":"A created spiritual agent can suggest or move bodily things, but neither compels consent nor creates an entire substance; its causality is bounded both morally and naturally.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-07","CL_GGVC_22:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_57:A16","CH_GGVC_P_57:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-03","text":"Since demonic punishment follows actual choice, but cosmic inequality expresses the institution of wisdom, diversity of nature cannot in itself be a sign of fault.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-09","CL_GGVC_22:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6","CH_GGVC_P_57:A12","CH_GGVC_P_57:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-04","text":"Demonic attack can be indirectly ordered toward human good, just as bodily goodness becomes an occasion of fault only through use; evil therefore consists in intention and use, not in the instrument.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-06","CL_GGVC_22:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_57:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-05","text":"One order of creation contains multiple times: discrete and irreversible angelic decision, provisional demonic ministry, and continuable bodily change.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-06","text":"The same participation in good that refers the creature to God becomes the matter of fault when the will appropriates an end or perfection to itself by its own power.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-06","CL_GGVC_22:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_55:A12","CH_GGVC_P_57:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-07","text":"Truth without charity and nature without moral rectitude can remain together; therefore spiritual punishment is privative rather than destructive of substance.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-04","CL_GGVC_22:GA-09","CL_GGVC_22:GA-13","CH_GGVC_P_56:A3","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:XD-08","text":"The unity of being removes independent creative principles, whence the demon can dominate through social servitude, but can never attain creative divinity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","CL_GGVC_22:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_57:D1","CH_GGVC_P_57:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSD-01","text":"Ontological causality is asymmetrical: every positive being is reduced to one principle, while defect does not require a parallel productively evil cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_22:GA-07","CL_GGVC_22:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_57:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_55:DNX7","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSD-02","text":"The created intellectual subject is a dependent center of acting: it can falsely constitute itself as if the principle of its end, but its choice does not remove ontological dependence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-03","CL_GGVC_22:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_55:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSD-03","text":"Moral epistemology requires the separation of known truth from the affective mode in which the knower orders it, confesses it, or makes it an instrument of deception.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-06","CL_GGVC_22:GA-13","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSD-04","text":"The definitiveness of spiritual act does not immobilize the universe: the demonic will remains fixed, while its ministry ends and bodily matter changes forms.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSD-05","text":"Demonic power is political and parasitic rather than creative: it arises from suggestion, consent, and servitude, and is converted into penal subjection and providential use.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-07","CL_GGVC_22:GA-08","CL_GGVC_22:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_55:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_57:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSD-06","text":"Universal order can preserve the goodness of natures and contain particular harms without morally approving evil or elevating singular advantage into the measure of the whole.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-07","CL_GGVC_22:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_57:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:BSD-07","text":"The method of the system is correction of the kind of question: it passes from an evil thing to a deficient relation, from ignorance to privation of grace, from production to the mode of causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_22:GA-04","CL_GGVC_22:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_55:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RLE-01","text":"The attractor of the whole is divine goodness represented through diverse natures, the order of parts, and the limits of created causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A11","CH_GGVC_P_55:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RLE-02","text":"Participation in good → appetite for perfection → appropriation outside the rule → defect of order → privation of beatitude → painful resistance around the good still desired.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A12","CH_GGVC_P_55:D2","CH_GGVC_P_56:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RLE-03","text":"Preserved natural knowledge → truth without affective light → instrumental or deceptive confession → unconverted will → confirmed obstinacy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:D3","CH_GGVC_P_56:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RLE-04","text":"Suggestion → uncoerced consent → communion of fault → penal subjection → continued attack → indirect providential utility.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_55:D12","CH_GGVC_P_56:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RLE-05","text":"Participated bodily good → attraction → foolish use → appearance of bodily evil → reclassification of harm according to nature and the order of the whole.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:D3","CH_GGVC_P_57:A8","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RLE-06","text":"Greater excellence → lesser natural proneness → greater motive of pride → more intense spiritual choice → immobility after adherence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_55:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:RLE-07","text":"Divine creation → motion of secondary causes → transmutation of already existing things → conservation of being → relation of effects to the perfection of the universe.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A15","CH_GGVC_P_57:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FGS-B01","text":"Community of being in diverse natures → one creative principle → ontological goodness of every creature → exclusion of an evil nature or creator.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A1","CH_GGVC_P_57:A2","CH_GGVC_P_57:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FGS-B02","text":"Created nature + higher rule + gratuitous gifts → participated, not autonomous, rectitude → possibility of defect without natural malice.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A3","CH_GGVC_P_56:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_57:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FGS-B03","text":"Desired spiritual good + mode of one's own power + refusal of subjection → appropriation of the end → pride → fault.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A9","CH_GGVC_P_55:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FGS-B04","text":"Definitive angelic apprehension → fixed adherence of the will → affective grace removed, nature preserved → frustration of the will → spiritual punishment.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:A5","CH_GGVC_P_56:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FGS-B05","text":"Limited created causality → suggestion, motion, and transmutation → real effects without creation or compulsion → reassumption of effects into providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_56:A13","CH_GGVC_P_57:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FGS-B06","text":"Diversity of natures and times → provisional ministry and particular changes → perfection of the universe → final judgment and ordering into divine goodness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A11","CH_GGVC_P_56:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:AFM-B01","text":"Community of being → one creator principle → goodness of every nature → distinction between creation and motion → limited secondary causality → perfection of the universe → divine end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A2","CH_GGVC_P_57:A4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A15","CH_GGVC_P_57:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:AFM-B02","text":"Created will under rule → good appropriated inordinately → definitive choice → privation of grace and resistance → temporal ministry of demons → juridical closure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_55:D2","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6","CH_GGVC_P_56:A10","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:AFM-B03","text":"False analogy → modal division → change in the kind of cause → reclassification of evil → reintegration of nature, punishment, and body into one causal-final order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MME-01","text":"The opposition of good spirit and evil body collapses: spiritual nature can fail culpably, while bodily nature remains a particular good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MME-02","text":"Freedom is reclassified from perpetual indeterminate mutability to the power of choice whose adherence can become definitive according to the mode of knowledge.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:A6","CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MME-03","text":"Causality is described as a graduated chart: God creates, composite causes transmute, spiritual substances move, and those who suggest do not compel consent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A15","CH_GGVC_P_55:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MME-04","text":"Punishment is transferred from the destruction of nature to the privation of grace, fixation of the will, frustration of appetite, and apprehension of a contrary order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_56:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MME-05","text":"Time is divided into discrete intellectual succession, eschatologically terminated ministry, and bodily change that does not dissolve divine origin.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MME-06","text":"Hierarchy and inequality cease to be simple indices of moral goodness: excellence can increase the motive of pride, while diversity can effect cosmic perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_57:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MME-07","text":"Truth is separated from salvation: natural knowledge, material confession, and subtlety of intellect can remain where charity and affective wisdom are absent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_56:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level includes the origin of angelic fault, the knowledge and punishment of demons, and the origin, end, and formation of bodies.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level governs all things through the dependence of created act upon rule, the distinction between nature and grace, and the separation of creation from the motion of matter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level reconstitutes questions: it divides universalizing analogies, changes the genus of cause, and resolves apparent opposition into modal distinctions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological-temporal level produces created reality as good yet dependent, heterogeneous times, and divine causality that sustains change without moral defect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_57:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological-performative level effects assertions through repeated distinctions that convert the very appearance of substantial evil into orders of relation, use, and causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_56:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FL-01","text":"The universality of divine causality and the exclusion of God as cause of defect cohere only through the distinction between production of being and the deficient mode of act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:DNX7","CH_GGVC_P_57:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FL-02","text":"Natural inclination toward good is combined with perpetual obstinacy in evil through the distinction between the object of good and the mode of choice, and through definitiveness of adherence, not through dissolution of the tension.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD7","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FL-03","text":"Providence orders the malice of demons toward indirect utility, while culpable intention remains intact; the link between good effect and evil agent remains causally fragile.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_56:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FL-04","text":"The perfection of the universe is valid for explaining particular harm only if the teleology of the whole is already accepted; the account therefore depends on axiomatic finality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:FL-05","text":"Spiritual punishment is explained through apprehension and resistance, but the mechanism of sensible punishment and the relation of fire to incorporeal nature are not fully developed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT7","CH_GGVC_P_56:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MP-01","text":"Freedom reaches a point of mutation where potency for opposites, without cessation of free nature, becomes immovable adherence to a choice already made.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A5","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MP-02","text":"The body changes from an apparent snare that withdraws from God into a participated good whose attractive force is morally determined by use.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A8","CH_GGVC_P_57:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MP-03","text":"Inequality changes field: among angels it affects proneness and the motive of fault, but in cosmology it becomes a condition of perfection, not an index of merit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_57:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a hierarchical machine instituted by one creator, in which secondary causes produce effects without rivalry and parts are referred to the goodness of the whole.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:A4","CH_GGVC_P_57:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:HSI-02","text":"The intellectual subject is a dependent node that receives order, chooses good, and defines its moral state through the mode of adherence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_56:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:HSI-03","text":"Time is a nested structure: the instant of spiritual decision, the interval of demonic ministry, the series of material changes, and the juridical terminus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_56:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_57:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:HSI-04","text":"Within this machine the demon is a parasitic operator: it does not create, but suggests, puts forth truth instrumentally, and acquires power through another's consent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_57:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_56:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:HSI-05","text":"The possible contest is not flight from the body, but the ordering of use and consent under providence, whereby participated good things are distinguished from a perverse mode of appropriation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:A18","CH_GGVC_P_57:A8","CH_GGVC_P_56:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms suffice to restore the structure of the present level: fault as defect of order, punishment as preserving privation, and body as created good under divine causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest center is the conjunction of the extrinsic rule of the will, definitive apprehension, and the distinction of creation from the motion of an already existing thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B03","text":"The dignity of the first fallen one, the measure of demonic revelation, and the mechanism of sensible punishment are not sufficiently determined; yet these are not constitutive of the global structure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B04","text":"The system coheres if the causation of being, defect of act, preservation of nature, and secondary causality are strictly distinguished; confusion of these levels would dissolve coherence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:MRT8","CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement from the metaphysical possibility of defect through punishment and providence to the causal-final ordering of the universe.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_55:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT8","CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B06","text":"The internal process by which choice becomes irreversible, and the sufficiency of the perfection of the whole for explaining particular harms, resist axiomatic reduction; there the structure rests upon strong premises.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_56:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_57:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_22@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_22:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 64–66","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 63 · Prima pars · Quaestio 64 · Prima pars · Quaestio 65"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 67–69","order":23,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_58","CH_GGVC_P_59","CH_GGVC_P_60"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_23@R001","sha256":"80406190c193d005e94b90358e9d94e3954a52d557158ea18f7a651eb5944e2b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B01","text":"Quaestio centralis est quomodo distinctio creata simul actualis, ordinata, naturaliter possibilis et secundum narrationem Genesis vera intellegi possit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_59:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B02","text":"Thesis globalis statuit creationem non a potentia pura vel chaos actuali procedere, sed ab entibus iam formatis ad ulteriorem formationem, manifestationem et ordinationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B03","text":"Argumentatio terminos distinguit, categorias ontologicas probat, impossibilia naturalia excludit et expositiones residuas secundum gradus certitudinis ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B04","text":"Motus procedit ab aequivocatione et contradictione apparenti ad distinctionem sensuum, ordinum et referentium, deinde ad reconciliationem conditionalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B05","text":"Semantica, ontologia, physica et exegesis se mutuo restringunt: nulla earum sola sensum cosmologicum sufficienter constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_60:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B06","text":"Progressus non est chronologia linearis, sed recursio ramificata qua eadem difficultas sub modis prioritatis, causalitatis, denominationis et narrationis iterum examinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B07","text":"Directio positiva transit a conditionibus communibus distinctionis ad determinationes particulares, non ab absoluta informitate ad primam actualitatem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A12","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B08","text":"Clausura est selectiva et conditionalis: conclusiones demonstratae figuntur, rami impossibiles removentur, plures autem expositiones compatibles sub determinatione incompleta servantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Creatio terminatur ad ens actu; materia igitur omnino informis neque ut res creata neque ut status temporalis subsistere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A1","CH_GGVC_P_58:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Productio substantiae et ulterior formatio distinguuntur, quia forma substantialis esse simpliciter constituit, ornamentum autem vel determinatio ulterior ens iam actuale ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A5","CH_GGVC_P_60:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Informitas a carentia omnis formae ad carentiam decoris, manifestationis vel determinationis particularis transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A7","CH_GGVC_P_58:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Prioritas originis, naturae, formationis, narrationis et temporis distingui debent; unum genus prioritatis aliud non infert.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A8","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Opus distinctionis a principiis communioribus et causaliter dispositivis ad effectus, regiones et determinationes particulares progreditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A12","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Diversitas cosmica non est fragmentatio fortuita, sed ordinata distributio potentiarum, qualitatum, motuum, regionum et formarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_60:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Significatio termini determinatur per primam impositionem, usum extensum et contextum cosmologicum; identitas vocabuli identitatem referentis non praestat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A1","CH_GGVC_P_60:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Veritas Scripturae stabilis manet, sed expositio physica particularis corrigibilis est ubi ratio certa eam falsam demonstrat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:A1","CH_GGVC_P_60:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Ratio naturalis naturam institutam investigat et munere negativo ramos metaphysice vel physice impossibiles excludit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:A7","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Efficacia causalis neque substantialitatem neque independentiam ab agente requirit, quia qualitas activa instrumentaliter operari potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A9","CH_GGVC_P_59:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Tenebrae, informitas relativa et silentium narrationis defectus vel indeterminationes designant, non necessario entia positiva.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:NX14","CH_GGVC_P_59:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Unitas universi per ordinem partium et analogiam functionum constituitur, non per identitatem unius materiae physicae vel univocitatem nominum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A12","CH_GGVC_P_60:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-13","text":"[Mutating] Tempus mensurat successionem motus, dum ordo creationis etiam ordinem naturae, formationis vel narrationis exprimere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A17","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Distinctio terminorum, categoriarum et ordinum est machina communis qua contradictiones apparentes in ramos examinabiles convertuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_60:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] Conflictus stabilis inter verum sensum Scripturae et rationem demonstrativam inadmissibilis est; conflictus residuus ad expositionem corrigibilem pertinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RMD-01","text":"Informitas redit cum mutatione: primo negat omnem formam, deinde significat rem substantialiter actualem cui decor, lux vel ulterior determinatio deest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A7","CH_GGVC_P_58:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RMD-02","text":"Prioritas ab ordine originis ad ordines formationis, causalitatis et narrationis migrat, ita ut successio verborum successionem temporalem non necessario repraesentet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A8","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RMD-03","text":"Distinctio mutatur ab oppositione formae et informitatis in distributionem lucis, regionum, aquarum et caelorum intra realitatem iam constitutam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RMD-04","text":"Lux transit a vocabulo sensibili ad nomen manifestationis, deinde ad qualitatem activam et tandem ad conditionem communem distinctionis cosmicae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A2","CH_GGVC_P_59:A3","CH_GGVC_P_59:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RMD-05","text":"Aqua, firmamentum et caelum functionem obiecti univoci amittunt atque fiunt termini quorum referens ex regione, participatione et cosmologia dependet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:A8","CH_GGVC_P_60:A11","CH_GGVC_P_60:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RMD-06","text":"Communitas variatur secundum campum: materia est communis analogice, lux causaliter, universum autem per ordinem plurium regionum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_59:A12","CH_GGVC_P_60:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RMD-07","text":"Ratio naturalis redit functione mutata: contradictionem ontologicam primo demonstrat, deinde expositiones cosmologicas selective eliminat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:CGD-01","text":"Pluralitas materiae caelestis et elementaris atque positio caeli Empyrei manent determinationes locales; ad hunc gradum tantum regulam pluralitatis ordinum et graduum probationis conferunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A11","CH_GGVC_P_58:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:CGD-02","text":"Definitio luminis ut qualitatis activae manet solutio physica localis, sed eius mediatio inter formam substantialem et effectus sensibiles structuram praesentem amplificat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A9","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:CGD-03","text":"Referentia concreta firmamenti et aquarum manet indeterminata localiter; globaliter inde emergit regula expositionis physicae pluralis sed naturaliter constrictae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:D1","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:CGD-04","text":"Quod inferius ut formatio lucis vel firmamenti tractatur, hoc gradu in unam legem ampliatur: ens actuale per condiciones communes ad distinctiones particulares ordinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:CGD-05","text":"Distinctiones semanticae, ontologicae et cosmologicae unitates coniungunt, quia sensum vocabuli et possibilitatem rei sub eodem examine reciproco ponunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:CGD-06","text":"Chronologia simplex capitulorum superatur: ordo Geneseos fit compositio temporis, naturae, causalitatis, formationis et accommodationis narrativae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_59:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GNX-01","text":"Systema excludit materiam actu existentem sine forma et chaos absolutum tamquam statum temporalem creationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_58:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GNX-02","text":"Excluditur substratum corporeum homogeneum cui omnes formae, motus et modi corruptibilitatis univoce conveniant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A12","CH_GGVC_P_58:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GNX-03","text":"Lumen neque corpus neque forma substantialis neque similitudo intentionalis sine efficacia naturali esse potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_59:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_59:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GNX-04","text":"Tenebrae non constituunt creaturam vel qualitatem positivam luci coordinatam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:NX14","CH_GGVC_P_59:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GNX-05","text":"Expositio litteralis singularis non potest cum veritate Scripturae simpliciter identificari nec vocabula ad referentiam physicam immobilem restringere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_60:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GNX-06","text":"Miraculum ad hoc fictum et conflictus stabilis inter Scripturam et demonstrationem non admittuntur ut solutiones ordinariae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_60:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GNX-07","text":"Tempus non est receptaculum absolutum, neque ordo narrationis necessario est series temporalis plena rerum productarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:DNX6","CH_GGVC_P_59:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet vocabula sacra se ipsa sine historia usus, contextu et distinctione referentium interpretari.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_60:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem prioritatem esse temporalem, alioquin ordo naturae, formatio et dispositio narrativa in unam chronologiam corruerent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A8","CH_GGVC_P_59:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet efficaciam sensibilem substantialitatem probare, quia causalitas instrumentalis inter substantiam et effectum intercedere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_59:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet unitatem universi identitatem materiae, regionis vel rationis denominationis exigere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A12","CH_GGVC_P_60:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet invariabilitatem veritatis invariabilitatem cuiuslibet cosmologiae interpretativae secum ferre.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet sensibiliter manifestum esse mensuram exhaustivam realitatis corporalis vel silentium narrationis negationem rei significare.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:A10","CH_GGVC_P_60:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSA-01","text":"Ante creationem non datur campus neuter compositus ex tempore absoluto, materia pura et extensione nondum determinata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:DNX6","CH_GGVC_P_58:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSA-02","text":"Systema nullum chaos, tenebram vel informitatem positivam tamquam alterum principium originarium iuxta actum formatum admittit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_59:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSA-03","text":"Coincidentia perfecta ordinis ontologici, temporalis et narrativi indisponibilis est; hi ordines tantum per distinctiones coordinantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSA-04","text":"Cosmologia unica, aeque demonstrata in omnibus partibus, manet indisponibilis; certitudo variatur inter demonstrationem, congruentiam et expositionem admissibilem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SNX6","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSA-05","text":"Accessus immediatus ad institutionem primam sine mediatione semantica et cosmologica excluditur; verba revelata referentia sua per interpretationem constrictam recipiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:XD-01","text":"Dies distinctionis non designant transitum a non-ente formato ad primam actualitatem, sed gradus determinationis rerum iam substantialiter productarum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-01","CL_GGVC_23:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_58:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:XD-02","text":"Stabilitas enuntiati revelati compatitur mutationem referentis cosmologici, quia proprietas nominis et veritas propositionis ad ordines diversos pertinent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-07","CL_GGVC_23:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_59:A1","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:XD-03","text":"Epistemologia huius gregis asymmetrica est: demonstratio impossibile removet firmiter, sed non semper unam cosmologiam residuam positive determinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-09","CL_GGVC_23:GA-15","CH_GGVC_P_58:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:XD-04","text":"Principium commune distinctionis non requirit materiam communem: communitas potest esse causalitas luminis vel unitas ordinis inter partes materialiter diversas.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-05","CL_GGVC_23:GA-06","CL_GGVC_23:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_58:A12","CH_GGVC_P_59:A12","CH_GGVC_P_60:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:XD-05","text":"Termini negativi vel indeterminati non introducunt nova entia, sed indicant defectum formae ulterioris, luminis, manifestationis aut determinationis referentis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-03","CL_GGVC_23:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_58:NX14","CH_GGVC_P_59:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_60:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:XD-06","text":"Series dierum non potest directe identificari cum tempore physico, quia tempus sequitur motum, dies possunt motus diversos praesupponere, et narratio ordinem naturae significare potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-04","CL_GGVC_23:GA-13","CH_GGVC_P_58:A17","CH_GGVC_P_59:A15","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSD-01","text":"Ontologia et hermeneutica se reciproce moderantur: possibilitas rei sensum vocabuli restringit, distinctio autem sensuum ontologias falsas impedire potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-07","CL_GGVC_23:GA-09","CL_GGVC_23:XD-02","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSD-02","text":"Realitas creata fit processus determinationis ordinatae, in quo substantiae, qualitates, regiones et manifestationes diversis gradibus constituuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-05","CL_GGVC_23:XD-01","CH_GGVC_P_58:ME7","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSD-03","text":"Temporalitas non continet creationem extrinsecus, sed cum successione motus oritur, dum prioritas ontologica et narrativa sine intervallo temporali manere potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-04","CL_GGVC_23:GA-13","CL_GGVC_23:XD-06","CH_GGVC_P_58:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSD-04","text":"Certitudo theologico-cosmologica distribuitur in nucleum invariantem, exclusiones demonstrativas et zonam modelorum conditionaliter admissibilium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-08","CL_GGVC_23:GA-09","CL_GGVC_23:XD-03","CH_GGVC_P_58:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSD-05","text":"Interpres fit navigator constrictus: neque litteram arbitrario sensu solvit neque cosmologiam particularem cum revelatione sine residuo identificat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-08","CL_GGVC_23:GA-14","CL_GGVC_23:GA-15","CH_GGVC_P_58:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_60:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSD-06","text":"Unitas cosmica emergit ex ordine differentiarum potius quam ex homogeneitate: diversitas materiae, causalitatis et regionum ad unum universum componitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-06","CL_GGVC_23:GA-12","CL_GGVC_23:XD-04","CH_GGVC_P_58:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_60:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RLE-01","text":"Aequivocatio terminorum pluralitatem ramorum parit; examen naturale ramos impossibiles removet, sed residua pluralitas novam determinationem semanticam requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_60:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RLE-02","text":"Actualitas formata permittit informitatem relativam; informitas relativa ulterioris formationis necessitatem constituit; formatio autem novas differentias intra actualitatem producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_58:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RLE-03","text":"Conditio communis distinctionem particularem generat, sed particulares effectus naturam conditionis communis retroactive determinant, sicut lux per suos effectus classificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A12","CH_GGVC_P_59:A14","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RLE-04","text":"Auctoritas enuntiatum retinet, ratio expositionem examinat, semantica expositionem corrigit, et expositio correcta auctoritatem a falsa cosmologia separat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:A1","CH_GGVC_P_60:A2","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RLE-05","text":"Attractor principalis est distinctio ipsa: actus a potentia, substantia a formatione, qualitas a substantia, privatio ab ente et enuntiatum ab expositione separantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_59:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_60:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RLE-06","text":"Clausura ad unum exemplar non convergit, sed ad familiam modelorum circumscriptione negativa stabilitam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FGS-B01","text":"Enuntiatum stabile de creatione actuali vel veritate revelata → constitutio invariantis quod interpretatione deleri non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A1","CH_GGVC_P_60:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FGS-B02","text":"Terminus aequivocus → distinctio primae impositionis, usus extensi et referentis cosmologici → multiplicatio ramorum interpretativorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_60:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FGS-B03","text":"Ramus interpretativus → classificatio per actum, formam, substantiam, qualitatem, privationem, materiam et regionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A5","CH_GGVC_P_59:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FGS-B04","text":"Classificatio ontologica → distinctio prioritatis naturalis, temporalis, formativae et narrativae → dissolutio chronologiae simplicis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A8","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FGS-B05","text":"Examen metaphysicum et naturale → detectio contradictionis vel impossibilitatis → amputatio rami sine abolitione enuntiati stabilis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FGS-B06","text":"Rami superstites → ordinatio a communi ad particulare → compositio modelorum conditionalium → clausura pluralis sed finita.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AFM-B01","text":"Aequivocatio cosmologica → distinctio sensuum → classificatio ontologica → exclusio contradictionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_59:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AFM-B02","text":"Actualitas formata → conditiones communes distinctionis → determinationes particulares → regiones et limites cosmici.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AFM-B03","text":"Examen naturale → remotio impossibilium → conciliatio conditionalis → pluralitas cosmologica circumscripta → clausura graduata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MME-01","text":"Tabula creationis ab enumeratione temporali rerum ad matricem ordinum naturalium, causalium, formativorum et narrativorum convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MME-02","text":"Materia, lux, aqua, firmamentum et caelum ex obiectis simplicibus in nexus functionum, relationum et referentium contextuali ratione selectorum mutantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_60:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MME-03","text":"Oppositio unitatis et pluralitatis dissolvitur: identitas physica, analogia, causalitas communis et unitas ordinis fiunt modi distincti communitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_60:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MME-04","text":"Interpretatio reclassificatur ut selectio modelorum sub duplici constrictione auctoritatis invariabilis et rationis naturalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:A2","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MME-05","text":"Negatio et absentia fiunt instrumenta positiva tabulae: informitas, tenebrae et silentium indicant relationes deficientes vel determinationes nondum explicitas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_59:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_60:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MME-06","text":"Probatio cosmologica distribuitur per gradus, ita ut demonstratio, probabilitas, congruentia et admissibilitas interpretativa non confundantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei continet informitatem, materiam, lucem, firmamentum, aquas, caelos et tempora tamquam quaestiones expressas ordinis Genesis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_59:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis componitur ex relationibus actus et potentiae, formae et materiae, substantiae et qualitatis, regionis et motus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_60:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus conflictum auctoritatum et cosmologiarum in artem distinctionis, exclusionis et reconciliationis conditionalis convertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_60:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis realitatem ut actualitatem formatam atque tempus ut successionis mensuram producit, non ut receptaculum originarium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus et performativus doctrinam distinctionis ipsa forma argumentandi efficit: terminos separat, contradictiones amputat et conclusiones gradu certitudinis limitat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FL-01","text":"Invariabilitas veritatis Scripturae et mobilitas referentium cosmologicorum cohaerent tantum dum flexibilitas semantica in indeterminationem illimitatam non solvitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FL-02","text":"Lux primae diei et sol quartae diei per distinctionem virtutis communis et effectuum particularium conciliantur, sed nexus physice non plene demonstratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A14","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FL-03","text":"Caelum Empyreum ordinem gloriae initio inserit, sed auctoritas et congruentia eius minus firmae sunt quam demonstrationes metaphysicae contra materiam informem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:NX10","CH_GGVC_P_58:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FL-04","text":"Ratio naturalis falsas expositiones excludere potest, sed saepe criterium internum ad unam cosmologiam residuam eligendam non praebet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MP-01","text":"Informitas sensum mutat cum a privatione omnis formae ad imperfectionem relativam rei iam actualis transit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A7","CH_GGVC_P_58:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MP-02","text":"Lux mutatur a sensibili primo significato ad principium universale manifestationis, deinde ad qualitatem activam physicam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A2","CH_GGVC_P_59:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MP-03","text":"Prioritas mutatur ubi successio temporalis in ordinem naturae, formationis vel narrationis resolvitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A8","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MP-04","text":"Aqua et caelum mutantur ab elementis vel locis univocis in vocabula analogica plurium regionum et rationum denominationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:A8","CH_GGVC_P_60:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MP-05","text":"Ratio mutat functionem cum a demonstratione contradictionis ontologicae ad regimen negativum interpretationum cosmologicarum transit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina actualitatis ordinatae, in qua formae substantiales, qualitates activae, media, motus et regiones distinctionem distribuunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_60:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum interpretans non est legislator sensus, sed cartographus intra limites auctoritatis, ontologiae et demonstrationis naturalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_60:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est fluxus mensuratus ex motu, dum structura mundi etiam per simultaneas prioritates naturae et formationis ordinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis est trans ramos semanticos quos ratio amputat sine unius imaginis cosmologicae absoluta dominatione.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de actualitate formata, distinctione ordinum, mobilitate referentium et examine naturali sufficiunt ad machinam huius gregis restituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus inter impossibilitatem materiae actualis sine forma, exclusionem categoriarum falsarum luminis et correctionem expositionis per rationem certam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent caelum Empyreum, relatio lucis primae ad solem et electio inter cosmologias firmamenti atque aquarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B04","text":"Systema cohaeret non per unum exemplar physicum, sed per reditum recursivum distinctionis et per conservationem gradus certitudinis cuiusque conclusionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B05","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae resistunt singulae determinationes physicae probabiles, quia influxus Empyrei, lux prima et referentia aquarum functionem habent sine plena demonstratione univoca.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:MRT7","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T172206982041Z_444bd39f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 67–69","source_sha256":"80406190c193d005e94b90358e9d94e3954a52d557158ea18f7a651eb5944e2b","sha256":"51375e972d185c5c3870600bc167568469cf73f36f4f9e25ee64654d0eddc3b9","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:22:06.982985+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B01","text":"The central question is how created distinction can be understood as simultaneously actual, ordered, naturally possible, and true according to the narrative of Genesis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_59:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B02","text":"The global thesis holds that creation proceeds not from pure potency or actual chaos, but from already formed beings toward further formation, manifestation, and ordering.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B03","text":"The argument distinguishes terms, tests ontological categories, excludes natural impossibilities, and orders remaining explanations according to degrees of certainty.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B04","text":"The movement proceeds from equivocation and apparent contradiction to a distinction of senses, orders, and referents, then to conditional reconciliation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B05","text":"Semantics, ontology, physics, and exegesis mutually constrain one another: none of them alone sufficiently establishes cosmological meaning.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_60:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B06","text":"The progression is not a linear chronology, but a branching recursion by which the same difficulty is re-examined under modes of priority, causality, denomination, and narrative.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B07","text":"The positive direction passes from common conditions of distinction to particular determinations, not from absolute formlessness to first actuality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A12","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AS-B08","text":"The closure is selective and conditional: demonstrated conclusions are fixed, impossible branches are removed, but multiple compatible explanations are retained under incomplete determination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Creation terminates in actual being; therefore wholly formless matter can subsist neither as a created thing nor as a temporal state.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A1","CH_GGVC_P_58:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The production of substance and further formation are distinguished, because substantial form constitutes being simply, whereas adornment or further determination orders an already actual being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A5","CH_GGVC_P_60:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Formlessness shifts from the lack of every form to the lack of adornment, manifestation, or particular determination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A7","CH_GGVC_P_58:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Priorities of origin, nature, formation, narrative, and time must be distinguished; one kind of priority does not entail another.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A8","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] The work of distinction progresses from more common and causally dispositive principles to effects, regions, and particular determinations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A12","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Cosmic diversity is not accidental fragmentation, but an ordered distribution of powers, qualities, motions, regions, and forms.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_60:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] The meaning of a term is determined by first imposition, extended use, and cosmological context; identity of word does not guarantee identity of referent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A1","CH_GGVC_P_60:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] The truth of Scripture remains stable, but a particular physical explanation is corrigible where certain reason demonstrates it false.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:A1","CH_GGVC_P_60:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Natural reason investigates instituted nature and, in a negative function, excludes metaphysically or physically impossible branches.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:A7","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Causal efficacy requires neither substantiality nor independence from an agent, because an active quality can operate instrumentally.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A9","CH_GGVC_P_59:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Darkness, relative formlessness, and narrative silence designate defects or indeterminations, not necessarily positive entities.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:NX14","CH_GGVC_P_59:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] The unity of the universe is constituted through the order of its parts and the analogy of functions, not through identity of a single physical matter or univocity of names.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A12","CH_GGVC_P_60:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-13","text":"[Mutating] Time measures the succession of motion, while the order of creation can also express an order of nature, formation, or narrative.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A17","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] The distinction of terms, categories, and orders is the common mechanism by which apparent contradictions are converted into examinable branches.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_60:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] A stable conflict between the true sense of Scripture and demonstrative reason is inadmissible; any remaining conflict belongs to corrigible explanation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RMD-01","text":"Formlessness returns with mutation: at first it negates every form, then it signifies a substantially actual thing lacking adornment, light, or further determination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A7","CH_GGVC_P_58:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RMD-02","text":"Priority migrates from the order of origin to the orders of formation, causality, and narrative, so that succession of words does not necessarily represent temporal succession.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A8","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RMD-03","text":"Distinction changes from the opposition of form and formlessness into the distribution of light, regions, waters, and heavens within an already constituted reality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RMD-04","text":"Light passes from a sensible word to the name of manifestation, then to an active quality, and finally to a common condition of cosmic distinction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A2","CH_GGVC_P_59:A3","CH_GGVC_P_59:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RMD-05","text":"Water, the firmament, and heaven lose the function of a univocal object and become terms whose referent depends upon region, participation, and cosmology.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:A8","CH_GGVC_P_60:A11","CH_GGVC_P_60:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RMD-06","text":"Commonality varies according to field: matter is common analogically, light causally, and the universe through the order of multiple regions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_59:A12","CH_GGVC_P_60:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RMD-07","text":"Natural reason returns with a changed function: it first demonstrates ontological contradiction, then selectively eliminates cosmological explanations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:CGD-01","text":"The plurality of celestial and elemental matter and the position of the Empyrean heaven remain local determinations; at this level they contribute only the rule of a plurality of orders and degrees of proof.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A11","CH_GGVC_P_58:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:CGD-02","text":"The definition of light as an active quality remains a local physical solution, but its mediation between substantial form and sensible effects amplifies the present structure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A9","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:CGD-03","text":"The concrete reference of the firmament and waters remains locally indeterminate; globally there emerges from this a rule of physical explanation that is plural yet naturally constrained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:D1","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:CGD-04","text":"What is treated below as the formation of light or the firmament is at this level expanded into one law: actual being is ordered through common conditions toward particular distinctions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:CGD-05","text":"Semantic, ontological, and cosmological distinctions join the units, because they place the meaning of a word and the possibility of a thing under the same reciprocal examination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:CGD-06","text":"The simple chronology of chapters is superseded: the order of Genesis becomes a composition of time, nature, causality, formation, and narrative accommodation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_59:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes actually existing matter without form and absolute chaos as a temporal state of creation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_58:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GNX-02","text":"A homogeneous corporeal substratum to which all forms, motions, and modes of corruptibility apply univocally is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A12","CH_GGVC_P_58:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GNX-03","text":"Light can be neither a body, nor a substantial form, nor an intentional likeness without natural efficacy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_59:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_59:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GNX-04","text":"Darkness does not constitute a creature or a positive quality coordinated with light.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:NX14","CH_GGVC_P_59:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GNX-05","text":"A single literal explanation cannot simply be identified with the truth of Scripture, nor can it restrict words to an immutable physical reference.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_60:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GNX-06","text":"A miracle invented for this purpose and a stable conflict between Scripture and demonstration are not admitted as ordinary solutions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_60:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:GNX-07","text":"Time is not an absolute receptacle, nor is narrative order necessarily a full temporal series of produced things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:DNX6","CH_GGVC_P_59:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that sacred words interpret themselves without a history of use, context, and distinction of referents.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_60:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that every priority is temporal, otherwise the order of nature, formation, and narrative disposition would collapse into one chronology.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A8","CH_GGVC_P_59:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that sensible efficacy proves substantiality, because instrumental causality can intervene between substance and effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_59:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that the unity of the universe requires identity of matter, region, or principle of denomination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A12","CH_GGVC_P_60:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that invariability of truth entails invariability of every interpretive cosmology.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that what is sensibly manifest is the exhaustive measure of corporeal reality, or that narrative silence signifies the negation of a thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:A10","CH_GGVC_P_60:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSA-01","text":"Before creation there is no neutral field composed of absolute time, pure matter, and as-yet undetermined extension.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:DNX6","CH_GGVC_P_58:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSA-02","text":"The system admits no chaos, darkness, or positive formlessness as a second original principle alongside formed act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_59:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSA-03","text":"A perfect coincidence of ontological, temporal, and narrative order is unavailable; these orders are coordinated only through distinctions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSA-04","text":"A single cosmology, equally demonstrated in all its parts, remains unavailable; certainty varies between demonstration, congruence, and admissible explanation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SNX6","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSA-05","text":"Immediate access to the first institution without semantic and cosmological mediation is excluded; revealed words receive their referents through constrained interpretation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:XD-01","text":"The days of distinction do not designate a passage from unformed non-being to first actuality, but stages of the determination of things already substantially produced.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-01","CL_GGVC_23:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_58:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:XD-02","text":"The stability of a revealed statement is compatible with the change of a cosmological referent, because the property of a name and the truth of a proposition belong to different orders.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-07","CL_GGVC_23:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_59:A1","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:XD-03","text":"The epistemology of this body is asymmetrical: demonstration firmly removes the impossible, but does not always positively determine one remaining cosmology.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-09","CL_GGVC_23:GA-15","CH_GGVC_P_58:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:XD-04","text":"A common principle of distinction does not require common matter: commonality can be the causality of light or the unity of order among materially diverse parts.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-05","CL_GGVC_23:GA-06","CL_GGVC_23:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_58:A12","CH_GGVC_P_59:A12","CH_GGVC_P_60:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:XD-05","text":"Negative or indeterminate terms do not introduce new entities, but indicate the lack of a further form, light, manifestation, or determination of the referent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-03","CL_GGVC_23:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_58:NX14","CH_GGVC_P_59:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_60:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:XD-06","text":"The series of days cannot be directly identified with physical time, because time follows motion, days can presuppose diverse motions, and narrative can signify an order of nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-04","CL_GGVC_23:GA-13","CH_GGVC_P_58:A17","CH_GGVC_P_59:A15","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSD-01","text":"Ontology and hermeneutics mutually regulate one another: the possibility of a thing constrains the meaning of a word, while the distinction of senses can prevent false ontologies.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-07","CL_GGVC_23:GA-09","CL_GGVC_23:XD-02","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSD-02","text":"Created reality becomes a process of ordered determination, in which substances, qualities, regions, and manifestations are constituted at diverse levels.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-05","CL_GGVC_23:XD-01","CH_GGVC_P_58:ME7","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSD-03","text":"Temporality does not contain creation extrinsically, but arises with the succession of motion, while ontological and narrative priority can remain without a temporal interval.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-04","CL_GGVC_23:GA-13","CL_GGVC_23:XD-06","CH_GGVC_P_58:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSD-04","text":"Theologico-cosmological certainty is distributed into an invariant core, demonstrative exclusions, and a zone of conditionally admissible models.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-08","CL_GGVC_23:GA-09","CL_GGVC_23:XD-03","CH_GGVC_P_58:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSD-05","text":"The interpreter becomes a constrained navigator: neither dissolving the letter into arbitrary meaning nor identifying a particular cosmology with revelation without remainder.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-08","CL_GGVC_23:GA-14","CL_GGVC_23:GA-15","CH_GGVC_P_58:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_60:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:BSD-06","text":"Cosmic unity emerges from the order of differences rather than from homogeneity: diversity of matter, causality, and regions is composed into one universe.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-06","CL_GGVC_23:GA-12","CL_GGVC_23:XD-04","CH_GGVC_P_58:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_60:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RLE-01","text":"Equivocation of terms produces a plurality of branches; natural examination removes impossible branches, but the remaining plurality requires a new semantic determination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_60:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RLE-02","text":"Formed actuality permits relative formlessness; relative formlessness establishes the necessity of further formation; formation, however, produces new differences within actuality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_58:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RLE-03","text":"A common condition generates particular distinction, but particular effects retroactively determine the nature of the common condition, just as light is classified through its effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A12","CH_GGVC_P_59:A14","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RLE-04","text":"Authority retains the statement, reason examines the explanation, semantics corrects the explanation, and the corrected explanation separates authority from a false cosmology.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:A1","CH_GGVC_P_60:A2","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RLE-05","text":"The principal attractor is distinction itself: act is separated from potency, substance from formation, quality from substance, privation from being, and statement from explanation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_59:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_60:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:RLE-06","text":"Closure does not converge upon one model, but upon a family of models stabilized by negative delimitation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FGS-B01","text":"Stable statement concerning actual creation or revealed truth → constitution of an invariant that cannot be erased by interpretation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A1","CH_GGVC_P_60:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FGS-B02","text":"Equivocal term → distinction of first imposition, extended use, and cosmological referent → multiplication of interpretive branches.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_60:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FGS-B03","text":"Interpretive branch → classification through act, form, substance, quality, privation, matter, and region.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A5","CH_GGVC_P_59:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FGS-B04","text":"Ontological classification → distinction of natural, temporal, formative, and narrative priority → dissolution of simple chronology.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A8","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FGS-B05","text":"Metaphysical and natural examination → detection of contradiction or impossibility → pruning of the branch without abolition of the stable statement.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FGS-B06","text":"Surviving branches → ordering from common to particular → composition of conditional models → plural but finite closure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AFM-B01","text":"Cosmological equivocation → distinction of senses → ontological classification → exclusion of contradiction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_59:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AFM-B02","text":"Formed actuality → common conditions of distinction → particular determinations → cosmic regions and limits.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_59:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:AFM-B03","text":"Natural examination → removal of impossibilities → conditional reconciliation → delimited cosmological plurality → graded closure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MME-01","text":"The map of creation is converted from a temporal enumeration of things into a matrix of natural, causal, formative, and narrative orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MME-02","text":"Matter, light, water, the firmament, and heaven change from simple objects into nexuses of functions, relations, and referents selected contextually.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_60:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MME-03","text":"The opposition of unity and plurality is dissolved: physical identity, analogy, common causality, and unity of order become distinct modes of commonality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_60:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MME-04","text":"Interpretation is reclassified as the selection of models under the double constraint of invariable authority and natural reason.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:A2","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MME-05","text":"Negation and absence become positive instruments of the map: formlessness, darkness, and silence indicate deficient relations or determinations not yet explicit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_59:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_60:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MME-06","text":"Cosmological proof is distributed by degrees, so that demonstration, probability, congruence, and interpretive admissibility are not confused.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level contains formlessness, matter, light, the firmament, waters, heavens, and times as explicit questions of the order of Genesis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_59:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_60:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level is composed of the relations of act and potency, form and matter, substance and quality, region and motion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_60:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level converts the conflict of authorities and cosmologies into an art of distinction, exclusion, and conditional reconciliation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_60:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level produces reality as formed actuality and time as the measure of succession, not as an original receptacle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological and performative level enacts the doctrine of distinction through the very form of argumentation: it separates terms, prunes contradictions, and limits conclusions by degree of certainty.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FL-01","text":"The invariability of the truth of Scripture and the mobility of cosmological referents cohere only so long as semantic flexibility does not dissolve into unlimited indetermination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FL-02","text":"The light of the first day and the sun of the fourth day are reconciled through the distinction of common power and particular effects, but their connection is not fully physically demonstrated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A14","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FL-03","text":"The Empyrean heaven inserts an order of glory at the beginning, but its authority and congruence are less firm than metaphysical demonstrations against formless matter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:NX10","CH_GGVC_P_58:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:FL-04","text":"Natural reason can exclude false explanations, but often does not provide an internal criterion for selecting one remaining cosmology.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MP-01","text":"Formlessness changes meaning when it passes from privation of every form to the relative imperfection of an already actual thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A7","CH_GGVC_P_58:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MP-02","text":"Light changes from its first sensible meaning to a universal principle of manifestation, then to a physical active quality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:A2","CH_GGVC_P_59:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MP-03","text":"Priority changes where temporal succession is resolved into an order of nature, formation, or narrative.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:A8","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MP-04","text":"Water and heaven change from univocal elements or places into analogical words of multiple regions and principles of denomination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:A8","CH_GGVC_P_60:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MP-05","text":"Reason changes function when it passes from demonstrating ontological contradiction to the negative governance of cosmological interpretations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a machine of ordered actuality, in which substantial forms, active qualities, media, motions, and regions distribute distinction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_60:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:HSI-02","text":"The interpreting subject is not a legislator of meaning, but a cartographer within the limits of authority, ontology, and natural demonstration.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_60:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:HSI-03","text":"Time is a measured flux from motion, while the structure of the world is also ordered through simultaneous priorities of nature and formation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_59:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:HSI-04","text":"Navigation is possible across semantic branches which reason prunes without the absolute domination of one cosmological image.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_60:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms concerning formed actuality, distinction of orders, mobility of referents, and natural examination suffice to reconstruct the mechanism of this body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the nexus between the impossibility of actual matter without form, the exclusion of false categories of light, and the correction of explanation by certain reason.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B03","text":"The Empyrean heaven, the relation of first light to the sun, and the choice among cosmologies of the firmament and waters remain less determined.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B04","text":"The system coheres not through one physical model, but through the recursive return of distinction and the preservation of the degree of certainty of each conclusion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B05","text":"Individual probable physical determinations resist axiomatic reduction, because the influence of the Empyrean, first light, and the reference of waters have a function without full univocal demonstration.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_58:MRT7","CH_GGVC_P_59:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_60:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_23@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_23:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 67–69","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 66 · Prima pars · Quaestio 67 · Prima pars · Quaestio 68"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 70–72","order":24,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_61","CH_GGVC_P_62","CH_GGVC_P_63"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_24@R001","sha256":"48cdbac069003cfd9bb7310aabd5c0873ee6849a1b7bd845a98e56dab89ae087","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo opera tertiae, quartae et quintae diei formationem elementorum, ornamentum regionum et productionem viventium significent, quin narratio ad chronologiam physicam simplicem reducatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_62:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_63:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-02","text":"Narratio constitutionem mundi per transitum ab informitate terrestri ad regiones distinctas, deinde ad mobilia et viventia eas ornantia, ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_62:A5","CH_GGVC_P_63:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit reconstructiva et dialectica: difficultates litterae solvuntur distinguendo modos ordinis, causalitatis, productionis, motus et denominationis potius quam unam expositionem physicam imponendo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_62:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_63:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-04","text":"Motus principalis procedit a terra visibili et vegetabili ad caelum mobile, inde ad animalia mediis suis relata, ita ut perfectio mundi inferioris condicionem ornatus sequentis efficiat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_62:D1","CH_GGVC_P_63:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-05","text":"Ordo dierum est simul cumulativus et recursivus: dies ornamentorum ad dies distinctionis respondent, dum ordines originis, durationis, ornatus et generationis sibi non coincidunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A1","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-06","text":"Causalitas distribuitur a iussione divina ad formas, virtutes naturales, motores et instrumenta, ita ut efficacia creaturarum servetur sine autonomia causalitatis secundae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-07","text":"Verba cosmologica functionem selectivam et relationalem exercent: apparentia sensibilis narrationem regit, sed neque naturam occultam neque totam rationem causalitatis exhaurit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-08","text":"Clausura est ordinativa, non decisoria: unitas causalitatis divinae et correspondentia dierum stabiliuntur, dum successio temporalis et productio actualis vel virtualis sub pluralitate expositionum manent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Ordo creationis plures rationes irreducibiles continet: originem, durationem, distinctionem, ornamentum, generationem et perfectionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A1","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Constitutio mundi transit ab informitate ad distinctionem intrinsecam et ab hac ad ornamentum per entia regionibus distinctis superaddita.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_62:A1","CH_GGVC_P_63:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] Regiones mundi non sunt receptacula neutra: formae, motus et habitantes eas determinant, vicissimque regiones mobilia et viventia classificatoria ratione ordinant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_62:A5","CH_GGVC_P_63:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Causalitas divina naturas non evacuat, sed formas, virtutes, motus et fines constituit quibus causalitates secundae operantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A6","CH_GGVC_P_62:A17","CH_GGVC_P_63:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Principium activum, materia, instrumentum, medium motus et finis neque inter se neque cum ordine narrationis confundenda sunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D4","CH_GGVC_P_62:D8","CH_GGVC_P_63:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Motus ab effectu formae elementaris ad criterium ornatus et deinde ad principium affinitatis classificatoriae transit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A5","CH_GGVC_P_62:A4","CH_GGVC_P_63:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Nomen scripturale materiam, proprietatem, relationem, signum vel medium designare potest; eius vis ex functione ordinativa, non ex univocitate, pendet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A12","CH_GGVC_P_62:A10","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Selectio eorum quae sensibiliter apparent modum narrationis constituit, non mensuram exhaustivam eorum quae sunt vel causaliter agunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A13","CH_GGVC_P_62:A18","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Omnis efficacia creata ad ordinem superiorem reducitur, ne natura materialis, sidera vel elementa principia sui sufficientia fiant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Productio narrata potest actualitatem rei, institutionem causalem aut potentiam propagativam significare, dummodo prima institutio ab administratione naturali distinguatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D6","CH_GGVC_P_62:A6","CH_GGVC_P_63:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Tempus cosmologicum est stratificatum: origo non necessario durationem, distinctio communis non determinationem specialem, nec ornamentum ordinem generationis simpliciter significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_62:D5","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RMD-01","text":"Informitas primo est defectus distinctionis et decoris; post constitutionem regionum eadem carentia in indigentiam ornamentorum mobilium mutatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_62:A2","CH_GGVC_P_63:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RMD-02","text":"Aqua a forma fluxus et termino congregationis transit ad corpus ornandum et medium motus; functio causalitatis activae universalis ab ea removetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A5","CH_GGVC_P_61:D3","CH_GGVC_P_63:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RMD-03","text":"Terra primo ex occultatione emergit et vegetatione consummatur, deinde fit regio ornata atque extremum relationale ad quod animalia media comparantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_62:A5","CH_GGVC_P_63:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RMD-04","text":"Motus functionem mutat: in elementis proprietatem formae exprimit, in caelo distinctionem ornatus manifestat, in animalibus affinitatem ad medium constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A5","CH_GGVC_P_62:A4","CH_GGVC_P_63:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RMD-05","text":"Productio a duplici intellectu plantarum ad actualitatem luminarium et rursus ad controversiam de actualitate vel virtute animalium migrat; idem vocabulum modum ontologicum stabilem non retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D6","CH_GGVC_P_62:A6","CH_GGVC_P_63:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RMD-06","text":"Apparentia sensibilis a principio selectionis narrativae ad cautionem anti-idololatricam et tandem ad reclassificationem locutionum materialium amplificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_62:D4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RMD-07","text":"Dualitas Augustini et aliorum doctorum non evanescit, sed in distinctionem generalem inter originem, durationem, actualitatem et institutionem virtualem absorbetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:CGD-01","text":"Hypotheses de elevatione, densatione vel receptaculis aquarum manent machinae physicae locales; ad structuram praesentem ascendit sola distinctio inter formationem, motum localem et ordinem temporalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:CGD-02","text":"Quaestio utrum motor spiritualis corpori caelesti formaliter uniatur manet localis; sursum persistit schema instrumenti efficacis cuius virtus a principali agente derivatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:CGD-03","text":"Computatio avium cum inferiori aere et distributio animalium mediorum sunt solutiones locales; principium superius est classificatio secundum relationem dominantem, non essentiam simpliciter separatam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_63:D5","CH_GGVC_P_63:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:CGD-04","text":"Vita occulta plantarum excedit quaestionem localem cum probat apparentiam sensibilem non sufficere ad determinandum statum ontologicum vel causalitatem rei.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A8","CH_GGVC_P_61:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:CGD-05","text":"In gradu praesenti formatio terrae et correspondentia dierum coniunguntur: regiones distinctae fiunt fundamentum ordinativum mobilium quae eas postea ornant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_63:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:CGD-06","text":"Chronologia unica, elementum dominans et scala simplicis perfectionis fiunt exemplaria superata, quia nullum unum criterium totam distributionem dierum, causarum et entium regit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_62:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_63:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat ordinem dierum necessario esse successionem temporalem homogeneam aut unam seriem perfectionis, generationis et ornatus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_62:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GNX-02","text":"Systema negat materiam, elementum vel motum naturalem causalitatem sibi sufficientem habere aut causalitati divinae opponi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_62:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_63:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GNX-03","text":"Systema negat nomina terrae, aquae, caeli, signi, vitae et productionis in omnibus contextibus eandem rationem univocam retinere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_62:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GNX-04","text":"Systema negat locum motus, compositionem materialem, principium activum, dignitatem et ordinem narrationis unum idemque criterium esse.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_63:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_63:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GNX-05","text":"Systema negat efficaciam siderum importare divinitatem, autonomiam vel determinationem actuum liberi arbitrii.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_62:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_62:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnes rationes ordinis cosmici sibi coincidere; aliter correspondentia dierum, praecessio minus perfectorum et pluralitas expositionum contradictiones insolubiles fierent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_63:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_63:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet causalitatem divinam et naturalem esse aemulas, quia motus elementorum, virtus caelestis et generatio naturalis ex prima institutione vim accipiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_62:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_63:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet apparentiam sensibilem naturam rei exhaurire; aliter vita occulta, motor spiritualis et virtutes causales latentes excluderentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_62:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem productionem narratam actualitatem speciei eodem modo exigere; aliter distinctio institutionis causalis, productionis actualis et propagationis dissolveretur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_62:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_63:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas ontologice inadmissibilis est cosmos a materia, motu vel corporibus caelestibus plene sui explicativus et a prima donatione formae, virtutis ac finis independens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_62:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSA-02","text":"Indisponibilis manet narratio quae ex superficie litterae physicam vel biologicam theoriam exhaustivam immediate extrahat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSA-03","text":"Systema circumagitur circa absentiam unius chronologiae et unius modi actualitatis decisivi; haec absentia pluralitatem ordinatam expositionum sustinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:XD-01","text":"Formatio tertiae diei regiones habitabiles constituit quarum distinctio ornamenta quartae et quintae diei distribuit; dies igitur matricem dependentiarum, non seriem eventuum seiunctorum, efficiunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:GA-02","CL_GGVC_24:GA-03","CH_GGVC_P_61:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_62:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_63:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:XD-02","text":"Motus fit nexus inter ontologiam et taxonomiam: formas elementares manifestat, ornamenta a regionibus distinguit et animalia mediis suis relationaliter adscribit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:GA-03","CL_GGVC_24:GA-06","CH_GGVC_P_61:A5","CH_GGVC_P_62:A4","CH_GGVC_P_63:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:XD-03","text":"Distinctio creationis ab administratione causalitatem non abrumpit, quia virtutes propagativae, instrumenta caelestia et motus naturales vim primae institutionis temporaliter explicant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:GA-04","CL_GGVC_24:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_61:A9","CH_GGVC_P_62:A17","CH_GGVC_P_63:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:XD-04","text":"Selectio secundum apparentiam est disciplina attributionis: efficaciam rerum visibilium admittit, sed vetat ex visibilitate divinitatem, autonomiam aut theoriam causalitatis exhaustivam inferre.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:GA-07","CL_GGVC_24:GA-08","CL_GGVC_24:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_61:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:XD-05","text":"Cum luminaria actualiter produci dicantur, plantae autem et animalia causaliter, virtualiter vel actualiter intelligi possint, dies narrationis unum modum productionis omnibus operibus non imponit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:GA-01","CL_GGVC_24:GA-10","CL_GGVC_24:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_61:A10","CH_GGVC_P_62:A6","CH_GGVC_P_63:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:XD-06","text":"Unitas loci, causalitas signi et classificatio animalium demonstrant relationem dominantem posse rem ordinare sine negatione aliarum relationum materialium vel causalium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:GA-05","CL_GGVC_24:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_61:D3","CH_GGVC_P_62:D4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSD-01","text":"Realitas emergit ut ecologia causaliter stratificata, in qua formae, regiones, motores, instrumenta, media et viventia functionibus mutuis potius quam positionibus absolutis definiuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_63:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSD-02","text":"Tempus non est linea unica, sed compositio originis, durationis, temporis communis, determinationum stellarum, ordinis ornatus et processus generationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_62:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSD-03","text":"Cognoscens debet modum denominationis a modo essendi distinguere: quod Scriptura aquae, terrae vel caelo attribuit non necessario materiam, causam aut statum ontologicum univoce designat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D7","CH_GGVC_P_62:D4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSD-04","text":"Iussio divina non est interventus naturae oppositus, sed principium quo natura formas proprias, inclinationes, virtutes generativas et efficaciam instrumentalem acquirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A6","CH_GGVC_P_61:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_63:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSD-05","text":"Exegesis cosmologica fit regimen attributionis potentiae: causalitas creaturis conceditur tantum dum ab idololatria, fatalismo et autosufficientia materiali separatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_62:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RLE-01","text":"Apparens inconvenientia litterae distinctionem conceptualem provocat; distinctio litteram restituit, sed eam in structuram causaliter et temporaliter multiplicem mutat, unde novae difficultates rursus oriuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_62:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RLE-02","text":"Informitas distinctionem regionum requirit, regiones ornamenta propria postulant, ornamenta autem per motum ipsas regiones manifestant; terminus formationis sic fit principium novi circuitus ordinativi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_62:A5","CH_GGVC_P_63:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RLE-03","text":"Efficacia creata periculum autonomiae generat; subordinatio ad causam primam periculum removet, sed efficaciam instrumentalem restituit, unde circuitus causalitatis subordinatae stabilitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RLE-04","text":"Ambiguitas inter productionem actualem et virtualem redit tamquam attractor interpretativus: singulis reditibus non solvitur, sed sub distinctione institutionis primae et administrationis naturae continetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D6","CH_GGVC_P_62:D2","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RLE-05","text":"Visibile et occultum spiram methodologicam formant: narratio visibilia eligit, difficultas occultas causas aperit, sed interpretatio ad ordinem apparentiae redit sine eo absolutizando.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A8","CH_GGVC_P_62:A18","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FGS-B01","text":"Attributio scripturalis immediata → anomalia physica, causalis vel classificatoria → necessitas distinctionis criteriorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_62:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_63:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FGS-B02","text":"Informitas → forma et distinctio regionum → aptitudo ad vitam → indigentia ornamentorum mobilium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_62:A2","CH_GGVC_P_63:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FGS-B03","text":"Confusio ordinum → separatio originis, durationis, distinctionis, ornatus et generationis → pluralitas temporalis ordinata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A1","CH_GGVC_P_62:D5","CH_GGVC_P_63:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FGS-B04","text":"Confusio causarum → discretio agentis, materiae, instrumenti, medii et finis → restitutio causalitatis naturalis subordinatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D4","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_63:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FGS-B05","text":"Regio distincta → mobile proprium → motus relationem ad regionem manifestans → correspondentia inter distinctionem et ornamentum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:A4","CH_GGVC_P_62:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_63:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FGS-B06","text":"Pluralitas lectionum → conservatio differentiarum → subordinatio communis ad institutionem divinam → cohaerentia sine reductione ad expositionem physicam unicam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_62:FGS4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AFM-B01","text":"Occultatio et indecor terrae → distinctio aquae et aridae → vegetatio → terra visibilis, stabilis et habitabilis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AFM-B02","text":"Regiones distinctae → mobilia regionibus correspondentia → luminaria caeli → pisces et aves corporis medii → ornamentum distributum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:AF2","CH_GGVC_P_63:D1","CH_GGVC_P_63:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AFM-B03","text":"Motus et effectus apparentes → separatio motoris, instrumenti, materiae et medii → causalitas naturalis multiplicata → subordinatio ad primam institutionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:AF5","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AFM-B04","text":"Locutio litteralis anomala → mutatio criterii → denominatio relationalis → reclassificatio elementorum et animalium → restitutio ordinis scripturalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AF5","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AFM-B05","text":"Expositiones temporales et causales divergentes → distinctio actus, potentiae et propagationis → unitas causalitatis divinae → clausura interpretativa qualificata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_63:A15","CH_GGVC_P_63:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MME-01","text":"Dies creationis ex partibus chronologiae in coordinatas originis, distinctionis, ornatus, motus et generationis reclassificantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_62:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MME-02","text":"Elementa desinunt esse tantum materias: fiunt indices formae, situs, medii motus, corporis ornandi et relationis classificatoriae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_63:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MME-03","text":"Motus, vita et animationis status separantur, unde mobile potest inanimatum esse, vivum immobiliter apparere, atque animal medio suo sine compositione dominante adscribi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A8","CH_GGVC_P_62:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_63:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MME-04","text":"Causalitas reordinatur ex nexu plano causae et effectus in seriem principii divini, agentis naturalis, instrumenti, materiae, medii et finis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MME-05","text":"Lectio necessaria fit differentialis: verba litterae neque negantur neque immediate absolutizantur, sed per variationes functionis, ambitus et causalitatis examinantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei agitur de congregatione aquarum, vegetatione terrae, productione luminarium atque origine piscium et avium diebus sibi assignatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_62:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali agunt informitas, forma, regio, ornamentum, motus, medium et causalitates distinctae, quibus mundus fit distributio ordinata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_62:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico interpretatio difficultates non delet, sed criterium quaestionis mutat, ne littera in chronologiam, idololatriam aut materialismum causaliter planum cogatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_62:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est ordo formarum et virtutum dependentium, tempus autem plures series originis, distinctionis, motus et generationis continet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_62:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo textus propriam thesim efficit: obiectiones distribuit, distinctiones multiplicat et pluralitatem sub unitate ordinis componit potius quam unam explicationem imponit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT7","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FL-01","text":"Productio causalis Augustini et productio actualis aliorum sub eadem prima institutione componuntur, sed incompatibilitas earum historica vel temporalis non plene solvitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_63:A15","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FL-02","text":"Plantae ut decor consummans terram tractantur, quamvis ornamentum postea stricte definiatur per res a caelo et terra distinctas; terminus decoris igitur inter perfectionem intrinsecam et ornamentum extrinsecum oscillat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D5","CH_GGVC_P_62:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FL-03","text":"Motus simul proprietatem formae, indicium distinctionis et criterium affinitatis praebet, sed nulla ratio demonstrat hos tres usus ad unum principium simpliciter reduci.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A5","CH_GGVC_P_62:A4","CH_GGVC_P_63:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FL-04","text":"Corpus caeleste inanimatum dicitur, sed motus eius substantiam apprehendentem requirit et naturalis etiam ex parte principii activi appellatur; terminologia motoris externi et motus naturalis commissuram fragilem retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:A15","CH_GGVC_P_62:A16","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FL-05","text":"Narratio secundum apparentiam sensibiliter accommodatur, dum solutiones eius formas substantiales, motores spirituales et virtutes occultas introducunt; simplicitas litterae et profunditas metaphysica non perfecte eiusdem altitudinis sunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A13","CH_GGVC_P_62:A18","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MP-01","text":"Aqua mutatur a forma elementari fluente et termino congregationis in regionem ornandam, medium locomotionis et nomen cui causalitas activa universalis denegatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A5","CH_GGVC_P_61:D3","CH_GGVC_P_63:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MP-02","text":"Productio mutatur inter institutionem causalem, actualitatem speciei, virtutem propagativam et generationem naturalem, unde eius sensus ex ordine causali determinandus est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D6","CH_GGVC_P_62:A6","CH_GGVC_P_63:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MP-03","text":"Dies mutatur ab indice chronologiae ad signum ordinis originis, correspondentiae regionum, ornatus et temporum specialium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A1","CH_GGVC_P_62:D5","CH_GGVC_P_63:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MP-04","text":"Vita et animatio disiunguntur: plantae vitam occultam habent, corpus caeleste vitam causaliter efficere potest sine anima formali, animalia autem per virtutes naturales producuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A8","CH_GGVC_P_62:D8","CH_GGVC_P_63:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:HSI-01","text":"Imago latens est machina regionum stratificatarum: terra et aqua formis distinguuntur, caelum et medium mobilibus ornantur, atque omnes partes per causalitates subordinatas communicant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_62:A5","CH_GGVC_P_63:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum intra hanc machinam non est dominus mensurae ontologicae: homo ministerium luminarium recipit, sed creaturae ad integritatem universi potius quam ad utilitatem humanam solam ordinantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:A7","CH_GGVC_P_62:A8","CH_GGVC_P_62:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:HSI-03","text":"Tempus huius imaginis est plexus plurium ordinum, in quo prioritas narrativa, origo causalis, actualitas et processus generationis diversa itinera se intersecantia sequuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_62:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis est ars recte attribuendi: inter apparentiam et naturam, materiam et causam, motum et vitam, efficaciam creatam et dependentiam divinam discernendum est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:D4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de pluralitate ordinum, distinctione et ornatu, causalitate subordinata, mobilitate relationali atque productione actuali vel causali sufficiunt ad structuram gregis restituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est ubi regiones distinctae, mobilia ornantia et causalitates non confusae in unam tabulam mundi ordinati coniunguntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent successio temporalis operum, modus exactus productionis luminarium et actualitas vel virtualitas primarum plantarum atque animalium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat, quia difficultates materiales et litterales in discrimina ordinis, causalitatis, relationis et temporis transformat, sine differentiis localibus in identitatem redigendis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT7","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B05","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae resistit flexibilitas terminorum aquae, productionis, motus et ornatus; haec tamen non est residuum accidentale, sed instrumentum quo expositiones plures sub eodem ordine continentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A12","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T172245441178Z_69a67a28","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 70–72","source_sha256":"48cdbac069003cfd9bb7310aabd5c0873ee6849a1b7bd845a98e56dab89ae087","sha256":"deda7970ff7e7904f57942becdbe10ba7e0179199a594ae992fdd39efec45344","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:22:45.442113+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-01","text":"The system asks how the works of the third, fourth, and fifth days signify the formation of the elements, the adornment of regions, and the production of living beings, without the narrative being reduced to simple physical chronology.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_62:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_63:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-02","text":"The narrative orders the constitution of the world through a transition from terrestrial formlessness to distinct regions, then to the mobiles and living beings adorning them.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_62:A5","CH_GGVC_P_63:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-03","text":"The argumentation is reconstructive and dialectical: difficulties of the letter are resolved by distinguishing modes of order, causality, production, motion, and denomination rather than by imposing a single physical exposition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_62:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_63:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-04","text":"The principal movement proceeds from visible and vegetated earth to the mobile heaven, thence to animals related to their media, so that the perfection of the lower world effects the condition for subsequent adornment.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_62:D1","CH_GGVC_P_63:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-05","text":"The order of the days is at once cumulative and recursive: days of adornment correspond to days of distinction, while the orders of origin, duration, adornment, and generation do not coincide with one another.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A1","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-06","text":"Causality is distributed from divine command to forms, natural powers, movers, and instruments, so that the efficacy of creatures is preserved without autonomy of secondary causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-07","text":"Cosmological terms exercise a selective and relational function: sensible appearance governs the narrative, yet exhausts neither hidden nature nor the whole account of causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AS-08","text":"The closure is ordering, not decisive: the unity of divine causality and the correspondence of the days are established, while temporal succession and actual or virtual production remain under a plurality of expositions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] The order of creation contains several irreducible accounts: origin, duration, distinction, adornment, generation, and perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A1","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The constitution of the world passes from formlessness to intrinsic distinction and from this to adornment through entities superadded to distinct regions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_62:A1","CH_GGVC_P_63:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] The regions of the world are not neutral receptacles: forms, motions, and inhabitants determine them, and conversely the regions order mobiles and living beings according to a classificatory account.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_62:A5","CH_GGVC_P_63:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Divine causality does not evacuate natures, but establishes the forms, powers, motions, and ends by which secondary causalities operate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A6","CH_GGVC_P_62:A17","CH_GGVC_P_63:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] The active principle, matter, instrument, medium of motion, and end must not be confused either with one another or with the order of the narrative.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D4","CH_GGVC_P_62:D8","CH_GGVC_P_63:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Motion passes from an effect of elemental form to a criterion of adornment and then to a principle of classificatory affinity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A5","CH_GGVC_P_62:A4","CH_GGVC_P_63:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] A scriptural name can designate matter, property, relation, sign, or medium; its force depends on its ordering function, not on univocity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A12","CH_GGVC_P_62:A10","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] The selection of what appears sensibly constitutes the mode of narration, not an exhaustive measure of what exists or acts causally.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A13","CH_GGVC_P_62:A18","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Every created efficacy is referred back to a higher order, lest material nature, stars, or elements become self-sufficient principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Narrated production can signify the actuality of a thing, causal institution, or propagative power, provided that first institution is distinguished from natural administration.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D6","CH_GGVC_P_62:A6","CH_GGVC_P_63:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Cosmological time is stratified: origin does not necessarily signify duration, common distinction does not signify special determination, nor does adornment simply signify the order of generation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_62:D5","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RMD-01","text":"Formlessness is first a lack of distinction and beauty; after the constitution of regions, the same lack changes into a need for mobile adornments.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_62:A2","CH_GGVC_P_63:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RMD-02","text":"Water passes from the form of flow and the boundary of gathering to a body to be adorned and a medium of motion; the function of universal active causality is removed from it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A5","CH_GGVC_P_61:D3","CH_GGVC_P_63:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RMD-03","text":"Earth first emerges from concealment and is completed by vegetation; it then becomes an adorned region and a relational terminus to which animals are compared through their media.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_62:A5","CH_GGVC_P_63:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RMD-04","text":"Motion changes function: in the elements it expresses a property of form, in heaven it manifests the distinction of adornment, in animals it establishes affinity to a medium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A5","CH_GGVC_P_62:A4","CH_GGVC_P_63:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RMD-05","text":"Production migrates from a twofold understanding of plants to the actuality of the luminaries and again to controversy concerning the actuality or power of animals; the same term does not retain a stable ontological mode.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D6","CH_GGVC_P_62:A6","CH_GGVC_P_63:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RMD-06","text":"Sensible appearance is expanded from a principle of narrative selection to an anti-idolatrous caution and finally to a reclassification of material expressions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_62:D4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RMD-07","text":"The duality of Augustine and the other doctors does not vanish, but is absorbed into the general distinction between origin, duration, actuality, and virtual institution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:CGD-01","text":"Hypotheses concerning elevation, condensation, or receptacles of waters remain local physical mechanisms; only the distinction between formation, local motion, and temporal order rises to the present structure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:CGD-02","text":"The question whether a spiritual mover is formally united to a celestial body remains local; above it persists the schema of an efficacious instrument whose power derives from the principal agent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:CGD-03","text":"The reckoning of birds with the lower air and the distribution of animals among media are local solutions; the higher principle is classification according to the dominant relation, not simply according to separate essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_63:D5","CH_GGVC_P_63:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:CGD-04","text":"The hidden life of plants exceeds the local question when it proves that sensible appearance does not suffice to determine a thing's ontological status or causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A8","CH_GGVC_P_61:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:CGD-05","text":"At the present level, the formation of earth and the correspondence of the days are joined: distinct regions become the ordering foundation of the mobiles that later adorn them.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_63:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:CGD-06","text":"A single chronology, a dominant element, and a scale of simple perfection become superseded models, because no single criterion governs the whole distribution of days, causes, and beings.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_62:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_63:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GNX-01","text":"The system denies that the order of the days is necessarily a homogeneous temporal succession or a single series of perfection, generation, and adornment.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_62:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GNX-02","text":"The system denies that matter, an element, or natural motion possesses self-sufficient causality or stands opposed to divine causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_62:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_63:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GNX-03","text":"The system denies that the names earth, water, heaven, sign, life, and production retain the same univocal account in all contexts.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_62:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GNX-04","text":"The system denies that place of motion, material composition, active principle, dignity, and order of narration are one and the same criterion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_63:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_63:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:GNX-05","text":"The system denies that the efficacy of stars entails divinity, autonomy, or the determination of acts of free will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_62:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_62:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that all accounts of cosmic order coincide with one another; otherwise the correspondence of the days, the precedence of less perfect things, and the plurality of expositions would become insoluble contradictions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_63:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_63:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that divine and natural causality are rivals, because the motion of the elements, celestial power, and natural generation receive force from first institution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_62:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_63:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that sensible appearance exhausts the nature of a thing; otherwise hidden life, the spiritual mover, and latent causal powers would be excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_62:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that every narrated production requires the actuality of a species in the same way; otherwise the distinction of causal institution, actual production, and propagation would be dissolved.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_62:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_63:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSA-01","text":"Ontologically inadmissible is the possibility of a cosmos fully self-explanatory from matter, motion, or celestial bodies and independent of the first bestowal of form, power, and end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_62:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSA-02","text":"A narrative that immediately derives an exhaustive physical or biological theory from the surface of the letter remains unavailable.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSA-03","text":"The system revolves around the absence of one decisive chronology and one decisive mode of actuality; this absence sustains an ordered plurality of expositions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:XD-01","text":"The formation of the third day constitutes habitable regions whose distinction distributes the adornments of the fourth and fifth days; the days therefore effect a matrix of dependencies, not a series of disconnected events.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:GA-02","CL_GGVC_24:GA-03","CH_GGVC_P_61:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_62:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_63:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:XD-02","text":"Motion becomes the nexus between ontology and taxonomy: it manifests elemental forms, distinguishes adornments from regions, and relationally assigns animals to their media.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:GA-03","CL_GGVC_24:GA-06","CH_GGVC_P_61:A5","CH_GGVC_P_62:A4","CH_GGVC_P_63:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:XD-03","text":"The distinction of creation from administration does not interrupt causality, because propagative powers, celestial instruments, and natural motions temporally unfold the force of first institution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:GA-04","CL_GGVC_24:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_61:A9","CH_GGVC_P_62:A17","CH_GGVC_P_63:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:XD-04","text":"Selection according to appearance is a discipline of attribution: it admits the efficacy of visible things, but forbids inferring from visibility divinity, autonomy, or an exhaustive theory of causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:GA-07","CL_GGVC_24:GA-08","CL_GGVC_24:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_61:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:XD-05","text":"Since luminaries are said to be produced actually, whereas plants and animals can be understood causally, virtually, or actually, the days of the narrative do not impose one mode of production upon all works.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:GA-01","CL_GGVC_24:GA-10","CL_GGVC_24:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_61:A10","CH_GGVC_P_62:A6","CH_GGVC_P_63:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:XD-06","text":"Unity of place, causality of sign, and the classification of animals demonstrate that a dominant relation can order a thing without negating other material or causal relations.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:GA-05","CL_GGVC_24:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_61:D3","CH_GGVC_P_62:D4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSD-01","text":"Reality emerges as a causally stratified ecology, in which forms, regions, movers, instruments, media, and living beings are defined by mutual functions rather than absolute positions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_63:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSD-02","text":"Time is not a single line, but a composition of origin, duration, common time, stellar determinations, order of adornment, and process of generation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_62:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSD-03","text":"The knower must distinguish the mode of denomination from the mode of being: what Scripture attributes to water, earth, or heaven does not necessarily designate matter, cause, or ontological status univocally.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D7","CH_GGVC_P_62:D4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSD-04","text":"Divine command is not an intervention opposed to nature, but the principle by which nature acquires its proper forms, inclinations, generative powers, and instrumental efficacy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A6","CH_GGVC_P_61:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_63:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:BSD-05","text":"Cosmological exegesis becomes a governance of the attribution of power: causality is granted to creatures only insofar as it is separated from idolatry, fatalism, and material self-sufficiency.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_62:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RLE-01","text":"An apparent incongruity of the letter provokes conceptual distinction; the distinction restores the letter, but changes it into a causally and temporally multiple structure, whence new difficulties again arise.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_62:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RLE-02","text":"Formlessness requires the distinction of regions, regions demand their proper adornments, but adornments manifest the regions themselves through motion; the terminus of formation thus becomes the principle of a new ordering circuit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_62:A5","CH_GGVC_P_63:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RLE-03","text":"Created efficacy generates the danger of autonomy; subordination to the first cause removes the danger, but restores instrumental efficacy, whence the circuit of subordinated causality is stabilized.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RLE-04","text":"Ambiguity between actual and virtual production returns as an interpretative attractor: at each return it is not resolved, but is contained under the distinction between first institution and the administration of nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D6","CH_GGVC_P_62:D2","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:RLE-05","text":"The visible and the hidden form a methodological spiral: the narrative selects visible things, difficulty opens hidden causes, but interpretation returns to the order of appearance without absolutizing it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A8","CH_GGVC_P_62:A18","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FGS-B01","text":"Immediate scriptural attribution → physical, causal, or classificatory anomaly → necessity of distinguishing criteria.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_62:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_63:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FGS-B02","text":"Formlessness → form and distinction of regions → aptitude for life → need for mobile adornments.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_62:A2","CH_GGVC_P_63:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FGS-B03","text":"Confusion of orders → separation of origin, duration, distinction, adornment, and generation → ordered temporal plurality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A1","CH_GGVC_P_62:D5","CH_GGVC_P_63:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FGS-B04","text":"Confusion of causes → distinction of agent, matter, instrument, medium, and end → restoration of subordinated natural causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D4","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_63:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FGS-B05","text":"Distinct region → proper mobile → motion manifesting relation to the region → correspondence between distinction and adornment.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:A4","CH_GGVC_P_62:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_63:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FGS-B06","text":"Plurality of readings → preservation of differences → common subordination to divine institution → coherence without reduction to a single physical exposition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_62:FGS4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AFM-B01","text":"Concealment and ugliness of earth → distinction of water and dry land → vegetation → visible, stable, and habitable earth.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AFM-B02","text":"Distinct regions → mobiles corresponding to regions → luminaries of heaven → fish and birds of the intermediate body → distributed adornment.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:AF2","CH_GGVC_P_63:D1","CH_GGVC_P_63:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AFM-B03","text":"Motion and apparent effects → separation of mover, instrument, matter, and medium → multiplied natural causality → subordination to first institution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:AF5","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AFM-B04","text":"Anomalous literal expression → change of criterion → relational denomination → reclassification of elements and animals → restoration of scriptural order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:AF5","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:AFM-B05","text":"Divergent temporal and causal expositions → distinction of act, potency, and propagation → unity of divine causality → qualified interpretative closure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_63:A15","CH_GGVC_P_63:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MME-01","text":"The days of creation are reclassified from parts of chronology into coordinates of origin, distinction, adornment, motion, and generation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_62:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MME-02","text":"The elements cease to be merely materials: they become indices of form, place, medium of motion, body to be adorned, and classificatory relation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_63:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MME-03","text":"Motion, life, and the state of animation are separated, whence a mobile can be inanimate, a living thing can appear immobile, and an animal can be assigned to its medium without dominant composition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A8","CH_GGVC_P_62:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_63:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MME-04","text":"Causality is reordered from a flat nexus of cause and effect into a sequence of divine principle, natural agent, instrument, matter, medium, and end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MME-05","text":"Reading becomes necessarily differential: the words of the letter are neither denied nor immediately absolutized, but examined through variations of function, scope, and causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_62:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of the surface, the matter concerns the gathering of waters, the vegetation of earth, the production of luminaries, and the origin of fish and birds on their assigned days.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_62:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, formlessness, form, region, adornment, motion, medium, and distinct causalities operate, through which the world becomes an ordered distribution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_62:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, interpretation does not delete difficulties, but changes the criterion of the question, lest the letter be forced into chronology, idolatry, or causally flat materialism.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_62:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is an order of dependent forms and powers, while time contains several series of origin, distinction, motion, and generation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_62:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the text effects its own thesis: it distributes objections, multiplies distinctions, and composes plurality under the unity of order rather than imposing a single explanation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT7","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FL-01","text":"Augustine's causal production and the actual production of others are composed under the same first institution, but their historical or temporal incompatibility is not fully resolved.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_63:A15","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FL-02","text":"Plants are treated as an adornment completing earth, although adornment is later strictly defined through things distinct from heaven and earth; the term adornment therefore oscillates between intrinsic perfection and extrinsic adornment.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D5","CH_GGVC_P_62:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FL-03","text":"Motion simultaneously provides a property of form, an indication of distinction, and a criterion of affinity, but no account demonstrates that these three uses are simply reducible to one principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A5","CH_GGVC_P_62:A4","CH_GGVC_P_63:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FL-04","text":"A celestial body is said to be inanimate, but its motion requires an apprehending substance and is also called natural in respect of the active principle; the terminology of external mover and natural motion retains a fragile juncture.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:A15","CH_GGVC_P_62:A16","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:FL-05","text":"The narrative is sensibly accommodated according to appearance, while its solutions introduce substantial forms, spiritual movers, and hidden powers; the simplicity of the letter and metaphysical depth are not perfectly at the same altitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A13","CH_GGVC_P_62:A18","CH_GGVC_P_63:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MP-01","text":"Water changes from a flowing elemental form and a boundary of gathering into a region to be adorned, a medium of locomotion, and a name to which universal active causality is denied.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A5","CH_GGVC_P_61:D3","CH_GGVC_P_63:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MP-02","text":"Production changes among causal institution, actuality of species, propagative power, and natural generation, whence its sense must be determined from the causal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:D6","CH_GGVC_P_62:A6","CH_GGVC_P_63:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MP-03","text":"The day changes from an index of chronology into a sign of the order of origin, correspondence of regions, adornment, and special times.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A1","CH_GGVC_P_62:D5","CH_GGVC_P_63:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MP-04","text":"Life and animation are disjoined: plants have hidden life, a celestial body can causally effect life without a formal soul, while animals are produced through natural powers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A8","CH_GGVC_P_62:D8","CH_GGVC_P_63:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:HSI-01","text":"The hidden image is a machine of stratified regions: earth and water are distinguished by forms, heaven and the medium are adorned by mobiles, and all parts communicate through subordinated causalities.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_62:A5","CH_GGVC_P_63:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:HSI-02","text":"The subject within this machine is not master of the ontological measure: man receives the service of the luminaries, but creatures are ordered to the integrity of the universe rather than to human utility alone.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:A7","CH_GGVC_P_62:A8","CH_GGVC_P_62:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:HSI-03","text":"The time of this image is an interlacing of several orders, in which narrative priority, causal origin, actuality, and the process of generation follow different intersecting paths.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_62:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_63:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is the art of rightly attributing: one must discern between appearance and nature, matter and cause, motion and life, created efficacy and divine dependence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_62:D4","CH_GGVC_P_63:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms concerning plurality of orders, distinction and adornment, subordinated causality, relational mobility, and actual or causal production suffice to restore the structure of the whole.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is where distinct regions, adorning mobiles, and unconfused causalities are joined into one tableau of an ordered world.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B03","text":"The temporal succession of works, the exact mode of production of the luminaries, and the actuality or virtuality of the first plants and animals remain less determined.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement, because it transforms material and literal difficulties into distinctions of order, causality, relation, and time, without reducing local differences to identity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT7","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B05","text":"The flexibility of the terms water, production, motion, and adornment resists axiomatic reduction; yet this is not an accidental remainder, but the instrument by which several expositions are contained under the same order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_61:A12","CH_GGVC_P_62:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_63:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_24@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_24:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 70–72","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 69 · Prima pars · Quaestio 70 · Prima pars · Quaestio 71"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 73–75","order":25,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_64","CH_GGVC_P_65","CH_GGVC_P_66"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_25@R001","sha256":"4360db907c6896c4780329eecb1bff2f87e99f73b7e95dc377e3d7adfe72f02e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo completio sexti diei, quies septimi et pluralitas formularum Genesis unum ordinem institutionis mundi constituant, quamvis operationes et creaturae posteriores permaneant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_66:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B02","text":"Thesis globalis est narrationem non inventarium chronologicum, sed grammaticam causaliter stratificatam esse, cuius numerus ex distinctione et ornatu mundi derivatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B03","text":"Fundamentum ordinis est transitus a partibus mundi distinctis ad complementa propria, quo creaturae non solum enumerantur sed loco functionali collocantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B04","text":"Probatio procedit distinctionibus perfectionis, operationis, novitatis, temporalitatis et causalitatis, quibus contradictiones apparentes in diversos respectus distribuuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_64:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B05","text":"Motus a completione ultimi corporis per clausuram institutionis ad reclassificationem diei ut ordinem perfectionis et cognitionis progreditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_66:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B06","text":"Progressus est cumulativus et recursivus: unusquisque status novam perfectionem affert, sed futura ad causas prioribus operibus insitas redeunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A6","CH_GGVC_P_65:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B07","text":"Clausura non est immobilitas, sed cessatio novae constitutionis cum propagatione rerum et ordinatione creaturae ad quietem in Deo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A9","CH_GGVC_P_65:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B08","text":"Argumentatio fit exegesis ontologico-reconstructiva quae pluralitatem expositionum recipit, sed eam intra totalitatem causaliter apertam et divinitus ordinatam continet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_66:A14","CH_GGVC_P_65:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Productio substantiae, formatio ordinata et administratio rerum sunt modi causalitatis distincti, neque continuatio administrationis novam creationem requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A1","CH_GGVC_P_65:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Integritas mundi generatur cum partes primum distinguuntur ac deinde habitatoribus vel perfectionibus propriis ornantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Perfectio transit a gradu vitae ad integritatem substantiae, operationem finis et ultimam beatitudinem, nec ad unam mensuram reducitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A4","CH_GGVC_P_65:A1","CH_GGVC_P_65:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Dies mensurat ordinem perfectionum creaturae vel cognitionum angelicarum, non durationem aut necessitatem temporalem actionis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Quies terminat productionem novarum constitutionum, non propagationem, conservationem vel motionem rerum iam institutarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:A6","CH_GGVC_P_66:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Prima institutio est totalitas completa sed causaliter aperta, quia futura ex causis institutis emergunt sine additione ontologice extrinseca.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_66:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Diversitas vocabulorum Scripturae indicat diversos gradus vitae, essendi et causandi potius quam synonymiam vel taxonomiam planam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Actio divina successioni, situ, indigentiae et acquisitioni cognitionis non subicitur, quamvis effectus eius in creaturis temporaliter ordinentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_65:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Plures expositiones litterales admittuntur tantum quatenus causalitatem divinam, ordinem rerum et distinctionem creationis a formatione servant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A14","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Valor partis ex integritate universitatis iudicatur, non ex utilitate immediata quam homini praebet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A12","CH_GGVC_P_64:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Consummatio movetur a natura instituta per gratiam ad gloriam, cuius terminus est quies creaturae in Deo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:A5","CH_GGVC_P_65:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Homo singulariter ad imaginem Dei ordinatur et ad beatitudinem capax est, sed utilitas humana mensura valoris ceterarum creaturarum non efficitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A14","CH_GGVC_P_64:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_65:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Septimus dies modum primae institutionis claudit, non efficaciam divinam aut historiam creaturarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_65:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-01","text":"Perfectio, initio gradum vitae designans, mutatur in structuram integritatis, operationis et finis ultimi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A4","CH_GGVC_P_65:A1","CH_GGVC_P_65:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-02","text":"Completio sexti diei localiter ultimum corpus ornat, deinde in integritatem universi et seriem perfectionum mundi amplificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A1","CH_GGVC_P_65:A4","CH_GGVC_P_66:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-03","text":"Dies ab intervallo temporali ad mensuram institutionis, perfectionis vel cognitionis transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-04","text":"Generatio ex corruptione, localis difficultas primae institutionis, absorbetur in maiorem structuram propagationis ex causis iam institutis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A9","CH_GGVC_P_66:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-05","text":"Silentium et repetitio, primum ad benedictionem vel genera relata, redeunt ut grammatica generalis formularum causalium non redundantium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_66:NX10","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-06","text":"Alternatio productionis potentialis et actualis in distinctionem latiorem inter dies temporales, cognitiones angelicas et modos expositionis migrat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A13","CH_GGVC_P_66:A8","CH_GGVC_P_66:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-07","text":"Quies vim invertit: ex cessatione operationis productivae fit positiva possessio boni et fundamentum quietis beatificae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:A9","CH_GGVC_P_65:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-08","text":"Utilitas humana a criterio locali valoris dislocatur et functioni partis intra systema mundi subordinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-09","text":"Nomina zoologica, opere classificatorio peracto, evanescunt in maiorem categoriam complementorum mundi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A5","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-01","text":"Distinctiones inter iumenta, bestias, reptilia et quadrupedia manent locales; earum taxonomia non ordinat synthesim praesentem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A5","CH_GGVC_P_66:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-02","text":"Corruptio et nocivitas animalium sunt quaestiones inferiores, sed sursum amplificantur ut relationes ad statum, usum et integritatem ordinis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:D6","CH_GGVC_P_64:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-03","text":"Productio animalium terrestrium transcendit zoologiam cum fit ultimus ornatus partis mundi et ratio sufficientiae senarii.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A1","CH_GGVC_P_66:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-04","text":"Completio septimi diei coniungit unitates inferiores, quia cessationem novae institutionis cum continuatione mundi ordinati componit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:D2","CH_GGVC_P_66:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-05","text":"Examen formularum Genesis fit codice metastrategico quo vocabula priorum quaestionum ut signa causalium et ontologicorum ordinum releguntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-06","text":"Differentiae inter Augustinum et alios interpretes non abolentur, sed sub invariantibus causalitatis divinae et ordinis mundi continentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:D5","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-07","text":"Benedictio a virtute generationis ad fecunditatem cosmicam transit, sanctificatio autem hanc propagationem ad quietem in Deo ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A8","CH_GGVC_P_65:D5","CH_GGVC_P_65:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-08","text":"Chronologia superficialis superatur, non eliminando successionem, sed subiciendo eam ordini perfectionis vel cognitionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_66:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat creationem esse inventarium aequale rerum aut chronologiam superficialem quae sola ordinem Genesis determinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_66:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GNX-02","text":"Taxonomia rigida non potest distributionem creaturarum per dies aut plenam significationem nominum Scripturae constituere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_66:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GNX-03","text":"Eventus posterior non est propter solam posterioritatem novitas absoluta extra principia primae institutionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_65:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GNX-04","text":"Completio operum non exigit cessationem divinae efficaciae, administrationis aut propagationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_66:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GNX-05","text":"Locutio temporalis, localis vel affectiva mutationem, indigentiam aut cognitionem acquisitam in Deo non significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_65:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GNX-06","text":"Creatura quae usui domestico humano non servit neque superflua neque ordine universi carens censetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_64:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GNX-07","text":"Pluralitas expositionum non est permissio sensus ilimitati neque reductio omnium vocum ad unam significationem exclusivam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_66:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet positionem rei in esse iam identicam esse plenae distinctioni, operationi et consummationi eius.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_65:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet perfectionem, tempus et causalitatem uno modo univoco de Deo, creatura et narratione praedicari.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet futurum ordinem creatum omnino extrinsecum esse fundamentis materialibus, causalibus vel similitudinalibus primae institutionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_65:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet perfectionem Dei usu, cognitione vel acquisitione creaturarum augeri.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet omnia vocabula narrationis ad unum eundemque ordinem semanticum pertinere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_64:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse debet iudicium humanum causae totius ordinis expers sufficientem mensuram bonitatis vel necessitatis creaturae praebere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_64:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas absolute novi, quod nullam praeexistentiam materialem, causalem vel similitudinalem habeat, intra ordinem creatum indisponibilis est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_65:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSA-02","text":"Historia temporalis quae aeternum agere divinum sua successione mensuret ontologice inadmissibilis est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_66:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSA-03","text":"Consummatio creaturae separata a fruitione Dei excluditur; sola cessatio temporalis sanctitatem constituere non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_65:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSA-04","text":"Systema circumagit absentiam coincidentiae plenae inter actionem divinam, tempora creaturarum, categorias taxonomicas et ordines cognitionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_66:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSA-05","text":"Prospectus unicus qui omnes expositiones, tempora et modos essendi absorbeat vetatur; unitas stat in invariantibus, non in reductione differentiarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_66:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-01","text":"Gradus vitae animalium sexti diei fit argumentum de sufficientia senarii, quia ultima viventia non seorsum numerantur sed ultimam partem mundi ornant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A1","CH_GGVC_P_66:A5","CL_GGVC_25:GA-02","CL_GGVC_25:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-02","text":"Clausura institutionis mundum non sterilem sed generativum efficit: corruptio, propagatio et administratio novas formas ex principiis iam datis explicant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A9","CH_GGVC_P_66:A9","CH_GGVC_P_65:SD1","CL_GGVC_25:GA-05","CL_GGVC_25:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-03","text":"Dissensio inter productionem potentialem et actualem transfertur in quaestionem de modo manifestationis, cum idem ordo per successionem temporalem vel cognitionem angelicam repraesentari possit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A13","CH_GGVC_P_66:A8","CH_GGVC_P_66:D5","CL_GGVC_25:GA-04","CL_GGVC_25:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-04","text":"Diversitas nominum viventium et series formularum creatorialium unam semanticam stratificatam componunt, in qua mutatio vocabuli mutationem respectus causalis vel ontologici indicat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A10","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME4","CL_GGVC_25:GA-07","CL_GGVC_25:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-05","text":"Benedictio generationem intra mundum clausum causaliter continuat, sanctificatio vero fecunditatem creaturae ad quietem in Deo convertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A8","CH_GGVC_P_65:D5","CH_GGVC_P_65:A12","CL_GGVC_25:GA-05","CL_GGVC_25:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-06","text":"Homo simul a descriptione communi animalium distinguitur et ab anthropocentrica mensura universi removetur: singularitas eius est teleologica, non valoris exclusivi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A14","CH_GGVC_P_64:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_65:A3","CL_GGVC_25:GA-10","CL_GGVC_25:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-07","text":"Historia naturae, gratiae et gloriae non est secunda creatio, sed series consummationum e principiis primae institutionis explicatarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:A5","CH_GGVC_P_65:D3","CH_GGVC_P_65:SD3","CL_GGVC_25:GA-06","CL_GGVC_25:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-08","text":"Stabilitas sensus litteralis non in una chronologia consistit, sed in invariantibus quae diversas expositiones admittunt et simul circumscribunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A14","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_66:ALT3","CL_GGVC_25:GA-04","CL_GGVC_25:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSD-01","text":"Realitas creata est totalitas iam instituta sed interne productiva, cuius perfectio non impedit novos status ex causis immanentibus emergere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_66:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSD-02","text":"Tempus creatum est ordo mutationum, perfectionum et cognitionum, non mensura efficaciae divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSD-03","text":"Causalitas formam reticularem accipit: substantia, formatio, exemplum, cognitio, administratio, propagatio et finis diversas dependentias eiusdem mundi exprimunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:D3","CH_GGVC_P_66:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSD-04","text":"Cognitio textus requirit divisionem respectuum, quia cohaerentia non ex uno sensu vocum sed ex coordinatione ordinum semanticorum obtinetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_66:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSD-05","text":"Subiectum humanum neque centrum valoris universi neque simplex animal inter alia est, sed pars ordinata quae imaginem divinam et capacitatem beatitudinis gerit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_64:A14","CH_GGVC_P_65:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSD-06","text":"Historia salutis structuram creationis non destruit, sed eam per consummationes naturae, gratiae et gloriae ad finem latentem perducit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_65:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RLE-01","text":"Creatio substantiam ponit → distinctio partes format → ornatus partes complet → quies novam constitutionem claudit → propagatio ex causis institutis mundum iterum explicat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_66:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RLE-02","text":"Apparens incongruentia → distinctio respectuum → reclassificatio vocabuli → restitutio cohaerentiae → nova incongruentia in gradu altiore constituit circuitum exegeticum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_65:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RLE-03","text":"Perfectio vitae → integritas substantiae → operatio ad finem → beatitudo ultima constituit viam amplificationis dominantem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A4","CH_GGVC_P_65:A1","CH_GGVC_P_65:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RLE-04","text":"Pars mundi munus recipit ex toto, sed integritas totius vicissim ex distinctione et complemento partium generatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A12","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RLE-05","text":"Verbum scripturale ad modum causalitatis ducit, modus causalitatis ad modum essendi, et hic ad novam lectionem verbi redit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A10","CH_GGVC_P_66:A11","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RLE-06","text":"Quies est attractor duplex: operationem productivam terminat et desiderium creaturae in bono possesso complet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:A9","CH_GGVC_P_65:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FGS-B01","text":"Actio divina substantiam rerum ponit; haec positio nondum plenam formationem vel consummationem mundi constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A1","CH_GGVC_P_66:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FGS-B02","text":"Substantia data → distinctio partium → ordinatio complementorum → architectura sex statuum perfectionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A2","CH_GGVC_P_66:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FGS-B03","text":"Ornatus sexti diei complet ultimam partem mundi per viventia terrestria et hominem, non per simplicem specierum enumerationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A1","CH_GGVC_P_66:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FGS-B04","text":"Perfectio institutionis → cessatio novae creationis → administratio et propagatio → mundus completus sed operativus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:D2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FGS-B05","text":"Dies et formulae narrationis → distributio ordinum temporalium, cognoscitivorum et causalium → pluralitas expositionum intra invariantes communes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A8","CH_GGVC_P_66:A10","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FGS-B06","text":"Natura instituta → gratia consummans → gloria finalis → quies creaturae in Deo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:A5","CH_GGVC_P_65:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AFM-B01","text":"Productio viventium ultimi corporis → completio ornatus mundi → cessatio novarum constitutionum → propagatio causarum institutarum → consummatio in quiete divina.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_66:FGS5","CH_GGVC_P_65:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AFM-B02","text":"Difficultas verborum → distinctio graduum vitae et causalitatis → reclassificatio formularum → grammatica ontologica narrationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A2","CH_GGVC_P_66:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AFM-B03","text":"Chronologia superficialis → divisio substantiae, formationis et cognitionis → dies ut status perfectionis → pluralitas expositionum circumscripta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:AF2","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AFM-B04","text":"Utilitas humana → integritas universi → singularitas imaginis divinae → finis beatificus non anthropocentricus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_64:A14","CH_GGVC_P_65:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MME-01","text":"Dies reclassificatur ex intervallo chronologico in unitatem perfectionis, cognitionis et institutionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MME-02","text":"Creaturae reclassificantur ex rebus separatim numerandis in complementa partium mundi et membra integritatis universalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_64:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MME-03","text":"Novitas reclassificatur ex mera posterioritate in explicationem principii materialis, causalis vel similitudinalis iam positi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_65:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MME-04","text":"Oppositio actionis et quietis corruit, quia creatio nova, administratio conservativa et fruitio beatifica diversos modos relationis constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_65:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MME-05","text":"Dissensio interpretum reclassificatur ex contradictione absoluta in pluralitatem limitatam circa temporalitatem, statum initialem et modum litterae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MME-06","text":"Anthropocentrismus utilitatis corruit, sed differentia humana servatur ut ordinatio ad imaginem Dei et beatitudinem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_64:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei agitur de animalibus sexti diei, completione et quiete septimi, numero dierum atque proprietate formularum Genesis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_65:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_66:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali mundus est architectura partium distinctarum, complementorum, causarum propagativarum et statuum perfectionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_65:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico contradictio solvitur dividendo respectus et determinando ubi expositiones concordent vel realiter dissentiant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_66:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali actio divina immutabilis distinguitur a propagatione, conservatione et successione statuum creaturae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_65:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico forma distinctiva argumenti id quod asserit perficit: verba dispersa in ordines causales distribuit et contradictionem apparentem in structuram convertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_64:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FL-01","text":"Productio potentialis Augustiniana et productio actualis aliorum interpretum sub causalitate communi continentur, sed differentia de statu initiali rerum integra manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FL-02","text":"Dies temporales et cognitiones angelicae eundem ordinem repraesentare possunt, sed successionem realem diversis modis constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_66:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FL-03","text":"Opus consummatum dicitur quamvis Deus plures creaturas producere possit; completio igitur voluntariae cessationi, non exhaustioni potentiae, innititur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_65:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FL-04","text":"Praeexistentia secundum similitudinem structuram clausam servat, sed eius amplitudo circa animas, incarnationem et gloriam non exacte terminatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_65:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FL-05","text":"Integritas universi anthropocentrismum excludit, dum formatio hominis ad imaginem Dei privilegium teleologicum retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_64:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FL-06","text":"Generatio ex corruptione cum integritate primae institutionis componitur per distinctiones locales, non per principium universaliter aequale.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_65:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MP-01","text":"Perfectio mutatur a gradu vitae viventis in integritatem substantiae, deinde in operationem et finem ultimum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A4","CH_GGVC_P_65:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MP-02","text":"Dies mutatur ab intervallo physico in unitatem ordinis perfectionis vel cognitionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MP-03","text":"Quies mutatur a cessatione productiva in fruitionem positivam et fundamentum beatitudinis creaturae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:A9","CH_GGVC_P_65:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MP-04","text":"Novum mutatur ab eo quod posterius apparet in id quod fundamento materiali, causali vel similitudinali caret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_65:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina architectonica: fundamentum substantiale ponitur, partes distinguuntur, complementis replentur, causis internis propagantur et quiete clauduntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_66:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_66:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:HSI-02","text":"Homo intra hanc machinam est imago Dei et capax quietis, sed non mensura exclusiva utilitatis aut valoris partium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A14","CH_GGVC_P_64:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_65:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est series statuum perfectionis qua natura instituta ad gratiam et gloriam procedit, non fluvius qui actionem divinam vehat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A6","CH_GGVC_P_65:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis est transire a verbo ad respectum causalem, ab eo ad structuram mundi et inde ad fines intra quos expositiones recipi possunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_64:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MRT-B01","text":"Distinctiones creationis, formationis, administrationis, perfectionis et quietis sufficiunt ad restituendam machinam principalem huius gregis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_65:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus inter creationem primam, formationem ordinatam, operationem continuam et quietem divinam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_65:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinatae manent materia informis, relatio dierum temporalium ad cognitiones angelicas et dependentia precisa gratiae atque gloriae a prima institutione.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_65:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MRT-B04","text":"Cohaerentia systematis pendet ex translatione analogica vocum et ex pluralitate expositionum severe circumscripta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat, quia ab ordinatione viventium ad clausuram mundi et inde ad grammaticam causalium ordinum ascendit; exacta tamen chronologia reductioni axiomaticae resistit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT7","CH_GGVC_P_65:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T172328829379Z_cc3544fe","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 73–75","source_sha256":"4360db907c6896c4780329eecb1bff2f87e99f73b7e95dc377e3d7adfe72f02e","sha256":"ddfe68ecc4dafe746f16753ec1c0d446760b3a6c5c459c226b393c97c5f8c503","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:23:28.831640+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B01","text":"The system asks how the completion of the sixth day, the rest of the seventh, and the plurality of Genesis formulas constitute one order of the institution of the world, although subsequent operations and creatures remain.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_66:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B02","text":"The global thesis is that the narrative is not a chronological inventory, but a causally stratified grammar, whose number derives from the distinction and adornment of the world.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B03","text":"The foundation of the order is the transition from distinct parts of the world to their proper complements, whereby creatures are not merely enumerated but placed in a functional position.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B04","text":"The proof proceeds through distinctions of perfection, operation, novelty, temporality, and causality, by which apparent contradictions are distributed among diverse respects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_64:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B05","text":"The movement proceeds from the completion of the final body, through the closure of institution, to the reclassification of day as an order of perfection and cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_66:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B06","text":"The progression is cumulative and recursive: each state brings a new perfection, yet future things return to causes inherent in prior works.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A6","CH_GGVC_P_65:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B07","text":"Closure is not immobility, but the cessation of new institution together with the propagation of things and the ordering of the creature toward rest in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A9","CH_GGVC_P_65:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AS-B08","text":"The argumentation is ontological-reconstructive exegesis that admits a plurality of expositions, but contains it within a causally open and divinely ordered totality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_66:A14","CH_GGVC_P_65:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] The production of substance, ordered formation, and the administration of things are distinct modes of causality, nor does the continuation of administration require new creation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A1","CH_GGVC_P_65:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The integrity of the world is generated when its parts are first distinguished and then adorned with their inhabitants or proper perfections.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Perfection passes from the grade of life to the integrity of substance, the operation of the end, and ultimate beatitude, and is not reduced to one measure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A4","CH_GGVC_P_65:A1","CH_GGVC_P_65:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Day measures the order of the creature's perfections or of angelic cognitions, not the duration or temporal necessity of divine action.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Rest terminates the production of new constitutions, not the propagation, conservation, or motion of things already instituted.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:A6","CH_GGVC_P_66:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] First institution is a complete but causally open totality, because future things emerge from instituted causes without ontologically extrinsic addition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_66:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] The diversity of Scripture's terms indicates diverse grades of life, being, and causing rather than synonymy or a flat taxonomy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Divine action is not subject to succession, place, need, or the acquisition of cognition, although its effects are temporally ordered in creatures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_65:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Multiple literal expositions are admitted only insofar as they preserve divine causality, the order of things, and the distinction of creation from formation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A14","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] The value of a part is judged from the integrity of the universe, not from the immediate usefulness it affords to man.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A12","CH_GGVC_P_64:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Consummation moves from instituted nature through grace toward glory, whose terminus is the creature's rest in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:A5","CH_GGVC_P_65:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Man is singularly ordered to the image of God and capable of beatitude, but human usefulness does not become the measure of the value of other creatures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A14","CH_GGVC_P_64:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_65:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] The seventh day closes the mode of first institution, not divine efficacy or the history of creatures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_65:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-01","text":"Perfection, initially designating the grade of life, is transformed into the structure of integrity, operation, and ultimate end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A4","CH_GGVC_P_65:A1","CH_GGVC_P_65:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-02","text":"The completion of the sixth day locally adorns the final body, then expands into the integrity of the universe and the series of the world's perfections.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A1","CH_GGVC_P_65:A4","CH_GGVC_P_66:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-03","text":"Day is transferred from a temporal interval to a measure of institution, perfection, or cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-04","text":"Generation from corruption, a local difficulty of first institution, is absorbed into a larger structure of propagation from causes already instituted.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A9","CH_GGVC_P_66:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-05","text":"Silence and repetition, first referred to blessing or genera, return as a general grammar of non-redundant causal formulas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_66:NX10","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-06","text":"The alternation of potential and actual production migrates into the broader distinction between temporal days, angelic cognitions, and modes of exposition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A13","CH_GGVC_P_66:A8","CH_GGVC_P_66:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-07","text":"Rest reverses its force: from the cessation of productive operation it becomes the positive possession of the good and the foundation of beatific rest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:A9","CH_GGVC_P_65:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-08","text":"Human usefulness is displaced from a local criterion of value and subordinated to the function of a part within the system of the world.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RMD-09","text":"Zoological names, once their classificatory work is completed, vanish into the larger category of the world's complements.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A5","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-01","text":"Distinctions among beasts of burden, beasts, creeping things, and quadrupeds remain local; their taxonomy does not order the present synthesis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A5","CH_GGVC_P_66:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-02","text":"Corruption and harmfulness in animals are lower questions, but are amplified upward as relations to status, use, and the integrity of order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:D6","CH_GGVC_P_64:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-03","text":"The production of terrestrial animals transcends zoology when it becomes the final adornment of a part of the world and the account of the sufficiency of six.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A1","CH_GGVC_P_66:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-04","text":"The completion of the seventh day joins lower units, because it combines the cessation of new institution with the continuation of the ordered world.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:D2","CH_GGVC_P_66:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-05","text":"The examination of Genesis formulas becomes a metastrategic code by which the terms of prior questions are reread as signs of causal and ontological orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-06","text":"Differences between Augustine and other interpreters are not abolished, but are contained under the invariants of divine causality and worldly order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:D5","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-07","text":"Blessing passes from the power of generation to cosmic fecundity, while sanctification orders this propagation toward rest in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A8","CH_GGVC_P_65:D5","CH_GGVC_P_65:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:CGD-08","text":"Superficial chronology is surpassed, not by eliminating succession, but by subjecting it to an order of perfection or cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_66:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GNX-01","text":"The system denies that creation is an equal inventory of things or a superficial chronology that alone determines the order of Genesis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_66:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GNX-02","text":"A rigid taxonomy cannot establish the distribution of creatures across the days or the full meaning of the names of Scripture.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_66:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GNX-03","text":"A later event is not, by mere posteriority, an absolute novelty outside the principles of first institution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_65:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GNX-04","text":"The completion of works does not require the cessation of divine efficacy, administration, or propagation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_66:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GNX-05","text":"Temporal, local, or affective speech does not signify change, need, or acquired cognition in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_65:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GNX-06","text":"A creature that does not serve human domestic use is judged neither superfluous nor lacking the order of the universe.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_64:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:GNX-07","text":"A plurality of expositions is not permission for unlimited meaning, nor the reduction of all terms to one exclusive meaning.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_66:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that the placement of a thing in being is already identical with its full distinction, operation, and consummation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_65:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that perfection, time, and causality are predicated univocally in one way of God, creature, and narrative.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that the future created order is wholly extrinsic to the material, causal, or similitudinal foundations of first institution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_65:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that the perfection of God is increased by the use, cognition, or acquisition of creatures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that all terms of the narrative belong to one and the same semantic order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_64:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that human judgment, lacking the cause of the whole order, provides a sufficient measure of the goodness or necessity of a creature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_64:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSA-01","text":"The possibility of the absolutely new, which has no material, causal, or similitudinal preexistence, is unavailable within the created order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_65:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSA-02","text":"A temporal history that measures eternal divine action by its own succession is ontologically inadmissible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_66:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSA-03","text":"The consummation of the creature separated from the enjoyment of God is excluded; temporal cessation alone cannot constitute sanctity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_65:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSA-04","text":"The system revolves around the absence of full coincidence among divine action, the times of creatures, taxonomic categories, and orders of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_66:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSA-05","text":"A single perspective that absorbs all expositions, times, and modes of being is forbidden; unity lies in invariants, not in the reduction of differences.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_66:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-01","text":"The grade of life of the animals of the sixth day becomes an argument for the sufficiency of six, because the final living beings are not counted separately but adorn the final part of the world.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A1","CH_GGVC_P_66:A5","CL_GGVC_25:GA-02","CL_GGVC_25:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-02","text":"The closure of institution renders the world not sterile but generative: corruption, propagation, and administration unfold new forms from principles already given.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A9","CH_GGVC_P_66:A9","CH_GGVC_P_65:SD1","CL_GGVC_25:GA-05","CL_GGVC_25:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-03","text":"The disagreement between potential and actual production is transferred into the question of the mode of manifestation, since the same order can be represented through temporal succession or angelic cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A13","CH_GGVC_P_66:A8","CH_GGVC_P_66:D5","CL_GGVC_25:GA-04","CL_GGVC_25:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-04","text":"The diversity of names of living things and the series of creational formulas compose one stratified semantics, in which a change of term indicates a change of causal or ontological respect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A10","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME4","CL_GGVC_25:GA-07","CL_GGVC_25:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-05","text":"Blessing causally continues generation within the closed world, whereas sanctification turns the creature's fecundity toward rest in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A8","CH_GGVC_P_65:D5","CH_GGVC_P_65:A12","CL_GGVC_25:GA-05","CL_GGVC_25:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-06","text":"Man is at once distinguished from the common description of animals and removed from an anthropocentric measure of the universe: his singularity is teleological, not one of exclusive value.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A14","CH_GGVC_P_64:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_65:A3","CL_GGVC_25:GA-10","CL_GGVC_25:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-07","text":"The history of nature, grace, and glory is not a second creation, but a series of consummations unfolded from the principles of first institution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:A5","CH_GGVC_P_65:D3","CH_GGVC_P_65:SD3","CL_GGVC_25:GA-06","CL_GGVC_25:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:XD-08","text":"The stability of the literal sense consists not in one chronology, but in invariants that admit and simultaneously delimit diverse expositions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A14","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_66:ALT3","CL_GGVC_25:GA-04","CL_GGVC_25:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSD-01","text":"Created reality is a totality already instituted but internally productive, whose perfection does not prevent new states from emerging from immanent causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_66:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSD-02","text":"Created time is an order of changes, perfections, and cognitions, not a measure of divine efficacy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSD-03","text":"Causality assumes a networked form: substance, formation, exemplar, cognition, administration, propagation, and end express diverse dependencies of the same world.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:D3","CH_GGVC_P_66:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSD-04","text":"Knowledge of the text requires a division of respects, because coherence is obtained not from one meaning of terms but from the coordination of semantic orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_66:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSD-05","text":"The human subject is neither the center of the universe's value nor a simple animal among others, but an ordered part that bears the divine image and the capacity for beatitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_64:A14","CH_GGVC_P_65:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:BSD-06","text":"The history of salvation does not destroy the structure of creation, but brings it through the consummations of nature, grace, and glory to its latent end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_65:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RLE-01","text":"Creation posits substance → distinction forms parts → adornment completes parts → rest closes new institution → propagation from instituted causes unfolds the world anew.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_66:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RLE-02","text":"Apparent incongruity → distinction of respects → reclassification of term → restoration of coherence → new incongruity at a higher level constitutes an exegetical circuit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_65:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RLE-03","text":"Perfection of life → integrity of substance → operation toward the end → ultimate beatitude constitutes the dominant path of amplification.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A4","CH_GGVC_P_65:A1","CH_GGVC_P_65:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RLE-04","text":"A part of the world receives its function from the whole, but the integrity of the whole is in turn generated from the distinction and completion of the parts.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A12","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RLE-05","text":"A scriptural word leads to a mode of causality, a mode of causality to a mode of being, and this returns to a new reading of the word.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A10","CH_GGVC_P_66:A11","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:RLE-06","text":"Rest is a double attractor: it terminates productive operation and completes the creature's desire in the possessed good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:A9","CH_GGVC_P_65:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FGS-B01","text":"Divine action posits the substance of things; this positing does not yet constitute the full formation or consummation of the world.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A1","CH_GGVC_P_66:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FGS-B02","text":"Given substance → distinction of parts → ordering of complements → architecture of six states of perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A2","CH_GGVC_P_66:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FGS-B03","text":"The adornment of the sixth day completes the final part of the world through terrestrial living beings and man, not through a simple enumeration of species.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A1","CH_GGVC_P_66:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FGS-B04","text":"Perfection of institution → cessation of new creation → administration and propagation → world complete but operative.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:D2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FGS-B05","text":"Days and formulas of the narrative → distribution of temporal, cognitive, and causal orders → plurality of expositions within common invariants.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:A8","CH_GGVC_P_66:A10","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FGS-B06","text":"Instituted nature → consummating grace → final glory → the creature's rest in God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:A5","CH_GGVC_P_65:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AFM-B01","text":"Production of the living beings of the final body → completion of the world's adornment → cessation of new constitutions → propagation of instituted causes → consummation in divine rest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_66:FGS5","CH_GGVC_P_65:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AFM-B02","text":"Difficulty of words → distinction of grades of life and causality → reclassification of formulas → ontological grammar of the narrative.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A2","CH_GGVC_P_66:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AFM-B03","text":"Superficial chronology → division of substance, formation, and cognition → day as a state of perfection → delimited plurality of expositions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:AF2","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:AFM-B04","text":"Human usefulness → integrity of the universe → singularity of the divine image → a beatific end that is not anthropocentric.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_64:A14","CH_GGVC_P_65:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MME-01","text":"Day is reclassified from a chronological interval into a unit of perfection, cognition, and institution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MME-02","text":"Creatures are reclassified from things to be counted separately into complements of parts of the world and members of universal integrity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_64:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MME-03","text":"Novelty is reclassified from mere posteriority into the unfolding of an already posited material, causal, or similitudinal principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_65:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MME-04","text":"The opposition of action and rest collapses, because new creation, conservative administration, and beatific enjoyment constitute diverse modes of relation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_65:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MME-05","text":"The disagreement of interpreters is reclassified from absolute contradiction into a limited plurality concerning temporality, initial state, and the mode of the letter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MME-06","text":"The anthropocentrism of usefulness collapses, but human difference is preserved as an ordering toward the image of God and beatitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_64:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of the surface, the concern is with the animals of the sixth day, the completion and rest of the seventh, the number of days, and the propriety of the Genesis formulas.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_65:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_66:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, the world is an architecture of distinct parts, complements, propagative causes, and states of perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_65:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, contradiction is resolved by dividing respects and determining where expositions agree or truly disagree.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_66:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, immutable divine action is distinguished from the propagation, conservation, and succession of the creature's states.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_65:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological level, the distinctive form of the argument accomplishes what it asserts: it distributes scattered words into causal orders and converts apparent contradiction into structure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_64:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FL-01","text":"Augustinian potential production and the actual production of other interpreters are contained under common causality, but the difference concerning the initial state of things remains intact.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FL-02","text":"Temporal days and angelic cognitions can represent the same order, but constitute real succession in different ways.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_66:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FL-03","text":"The work is said to be consummated although God can produce more creatures; completion therefore rests on voluntary cessation, not on exhaustion of power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_65:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FL-04","text":"Preexistence according to similitude preserves a closed structure, but its extent concerning souls, incarnation, and glory is not precisely delimited.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_65:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FL-05","text":"The integrity of the universe excludes anthropocentrism, while the formation of man in the image of God retains teleological privilege.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_64:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:FL-06","text":"Generation from corruption is reconciled with the integrity of first institution through local distinctions, not through a universally equal principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_65:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MP-01","text":"Perfection changes from the grade of life of a living being into the integrity of substance, then into operation and ultimate end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A4","CH_GGVC_P_65:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MP-02","text":"Day changes from a physical interval into a unit of an order of perfection or cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_66:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MP-03","text":"Rest changes from productive cessation into positive enjoyment and the foundation of the creature's beatitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:A9","CH_GGVC_P_65:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MP-04","text":"The new changes from what appears later into that which lacks a material, causal, or similitudinal foundation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_65:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly an architectural machine: a substantial foundation is posited, parts are distinguished, filled with complements, propagated through internal causes, and closed by rest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_66:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_66:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:HSI-02","text":"Within this machine, man is the image of God and capable of rest, but not the exclusive measure of the usefulness or value of parts.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_64:A14","CH_GGVC_P_64:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_65:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:HSI-03","text":"Time is a series of states of perfection through which instituted nature proceeds toward grace and glory, not a river that carries divine action.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_66:A6","CH_GGVC_P_65:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:HSI-04","text":"Navigation is possible by passing from word to causal respect, from this to the structure of the world, and thence to the boundaries within which expositions may be received.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_65:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_64:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MRT-B01","text":"The distinctions of creation, formation, administration, perfection, and rest suffice to restore the principal machine of this collection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_65:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the nexus among first creation, ordered formation, continuous operation, and divine rest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_65:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MRT-B03","text":"Less determinate remain unformed matter, the relation of temporal days to angelic cognitions, and the precise dependence of grace and glory on first institution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_65:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MRT-B04","text":"The coherence of the system depends on the analogical translation of terms and on a severely delimited plurality of expositions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_66:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_25:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement, because it ascends from the ordering of living beings to the closure of the world and thence to the grammar of causal orders; exact chronology, however, resists axiomatic reduction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_66:MRT7","CH_GGVC_P_65:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_25@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_25:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 73–75","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 72 · Prima pars · Quaestio 73 · Prima pars · Quaestio 74"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 76–78","order":26,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_67","CH_GGVC_P_68","CH_GGVC_P_69"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_26@R001","sha256":"2655778a3deb2d47bbaa7075b7f8f0c78bc00de5c11ed0253dbb44fb65640b08","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo anima, quae operatione intellectiva corpus excedit atque per se subsistit, possit una forma corporis manere et pluralitatem potentiarum organicarum ac non organicarum ordinare.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_69:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BAS-02","text":"Thesis centralis animam constituit formam substantialem unius hominis, unum esse composito communicantem, sed multas potentias secundum operationes et subiecta distinctas producentem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_69:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio ex operationibus manifestis ad principia ontologica regreditur atque attributione actuum, dependentia organica et intermittentia operationum structuram animae reconstructive determinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_69:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BAS-04","text":"Motus ab effectibus vitae ad subsistentiam, inde ad unionem formalem, ab unione ad architecturam potentiarum, et postremo ad earum statum post mortem progreditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_69:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BAS-05","text":"Motus est recursivus et cumulativus: operationes essentiam manifestant, essentia potentias generat, potentiae operationes distribuunt, distributio autem primam ontologiam operationibus iterum probat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_67:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BAS-06","text":"Clausura est asymmetrica, quia mors subsistentiam animae et permanentiam potentiarum non organicarum confirmat, sed simul ostendit animam separatam neque hominem completum neque subiectum actuale totius vitae humanae esse.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_69:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Modus operationis modum essendi et principium formale operantis manifestat; operatio igitur est via epistemica primaria ad structuram ontologicam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:A5","CH_GGVC_P_68:A4","CH_GGVC_P_69:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Vita corporis non ex corporeitate sola, sed ex actu formali quo corpus vivum determinatur procedit; anima proinde est principium vitae et non corpus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:A2","CH_GGVC_P_67:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Idem homo corporeus sentit et intelligit, quia eadem anima est forma corporis et principium intellectivum subsistens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:A2","CH_GGVC_P_68:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Unum esse substantiale unam formam substantialem postulat, quae perfectiones vitales inferiores virtute continet potius quam formis actu aggregatis superadditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:A6","CH_GGVC_P_68:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Independentia intellectus ab organo corporeo pertinet ad potentiam operativam, non ad separationem essentiae animae ab officio formae corporis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_68:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Essentia animae, potentiae et operationes constituunt gradus distinctos: essentia est actus primus, potentiae principia actuum secundorum, operationes autem perfectiones exercitae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A2","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Potentia per actum proprium et actus per rationem formalem obiecti specificatur, non per solam identitatem materialem rei obiectae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A6","CH_GGVC_P_69:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Subiectum potentiae sequitur modum operationis: intellectus et voluntas animae inhaerent, potentiae organicae vero coniuncto.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A11","CH_GGVC_P_69:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Potentiae naturali resultatione et ordine causali ab una essentia procedunt, sine transmutatione animae et sine pluralitate coordinata principiorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Corpus humanum non est subiectum ante animationem completum, sed materia quam anima constituit et secundum exigentias cognitionis sensitivae organice disponit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_68:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Unitas ontologica pluralitatem operativam ordinatam sustinet; unitas substantiae neque homogenitatem operationum neque simplicitatem facultatum requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A5","CH_GGVC_P_69:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Individuatio intellectus et universalitas cognitionis ad diversos ordines pertinent: cognoscens est huius compositi forma, species autem intelligibilis immaterialiter recipitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_68:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-13","text":"[Persistent] Anima est forma creata finita per esse participatum, sed incorruptibilis quatenus subsistens esse suum ex corpore non accipit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:A11","CH_GGVC_P_67:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Mors architecturem operativam scindit: intellectus et voluntas actu permanent, potentiae organicae tantum virtualiter in anima manent, et homo completus cessat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:D11","CH_GGVC_P_67:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-01","text":"Regula ab operatione ad esse stabiliter redit, sed munus mutat: primo subsistentiam animae probat, deinde formam subiecti attribuit, postremo potentias per actus et obiecta distinguit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:A5","CH_GGVC_P_69:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-02","text":"Separatio ab hypothesi animae extrinsecae ad distinctionem potentiae intellectivae ab organo transfertur, deinde in morte statum realem animae sine corpore significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_68:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-03","text":"Subsistentia primo ex operatione propria infertur, deinde completione specifica privatur, tandem naturalem inclinationem ad corpus etiam post separationem retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:D3","CH_GGVC_P_67:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-04","text":"Unitas a compositione animae et corporis ad unicam formam substantialem atque inde ad unam essentiam multas potentias ordinantem amplificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_69:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-05","text":"Corpus ex termino relationis ad animam in materiam ab anima constitutam, deinde in partem subiecti potentiarum organicarum mutatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-06","text":"Potentia ex principio metaphysico receptionis in facultatem operativam migrat atque post mortem etiam modum virtualis remanentiae designat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:A9","CH_GGVC_P_69:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-07","text":"Mediatio invertitur: inter animam et materiam constitutive excluditur, sed inter essentiam, potentias, obiecta et organa operative restauratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_68:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-08","text":"Incorruptibilitas ab absoluta conservatione totius apparatus vitalis ad conservationem stratificatam restringitur, qua non omnia quae ab anima oriuntur actu permanent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:NX11","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-09","text":"Comparatio animae cum angelo, distinctione gradus intellectivi functa, in structura superiore absorbetur ut determinatio confinii spiritualis et corporalis hominis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_69:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:CGD-01","text":"Quaestiones de materia spirituali et de specie animae respectu angeli locales manent; sursum tantum finitudinem animae et naturalem ordinationem ad corpus sustinent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_67:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:CGD-02","text":"Doctrinae de formis elementaribus, mixtione et complexione corporis sunt mechanismi locales dispositionis, non axiomata totius gregis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:D10","CH_GGVC_P_68:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:CGD-03","text":"Distinctiones particulariores rationum obiectorum et ordinum potentiarum localiter facultates classificantes sursum in regulam generalem specificationis et prioritatis multiplicis comprimuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A7","CH_GGVC_P_69:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:CGD-04","text":"Regula operationis et esse omnes unitates transcendit: essentiam, formam, subiectum potentiae et statum post mortem eodem instrumento reconstructivo coniungit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_68:A4","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:CGD-05","text":"Communicatio unius esse commissura principalis fit inter subsistentiam animae, unitatem compositi et pluralitatem potentiarum extra essentiam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:A8","CH_GGVC_P_69:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:CGD-06","text":"Exemplaria animae ut motoris, intellectus communis aut aggregationis formarum, localiter polemica, hoc gradu in limites negativos generales cuiuslibet anthropologiae substantialis convertuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_68:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat corpus qua corpus vitam, intellectum aut unitatem hominis explicare posse.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GNX-02","text":"Systema negat animam esse substantiam humanam completam quae corpus extrinsecus moveat, quia motio sola neque unum hominem neque actionem immanentem constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_68:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GNX-03","text":"Systema intellectum transindividualem omnibus hominibus communem excludit, ne actus intelligendi ab hoc homine tamquam agente principali separetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_68:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GNX-04","text":"Systema pluralitatem animarum vel formarum substantialium in eodem homine repudiat, quia unitatem unius esse dissolveret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_68:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GNX-05","text":"Systema omne medium accidentale vel corporeum in unione substantiali removet, quamvis instrumenta et mediationes in operatione admittat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_68:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GNX-06","text":"Systema negat essentiam animae esse potentiam eius vel unitatem essentiae unicam facultatem importare.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_69:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GNX-07","text":"Systema negat potentias organicas in anima separata actu permanere aut sensorium integrum sine organis consistere posse.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:NX11","CH_GGVC_P_69:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet unitatem subiecti homogenitatem operationum postulare; aliter una forma non posset gradus vitae et potentias diversas continere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet subsistentiam completionem specificam vel personalitatem simpliciter inferre; aliter anima subsistens cum homine completo confunderetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet materiam ante formam substantialem iam corpus organicum actu constitutum esse; aliter anima non daret esse simpliciter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem originem causalitatem temporalem vel transmutationem originantis requirere; aliter potentiae naturaliter ab essentia fluere non possent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem dependentiam a corpore dependentiam ab organo, causam essendi aut inclusionem corporis in essentia significare.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_68:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet universalitatem obiecti identitatem numeralem intellectus exigere; aliter cognitio universalis pluralitatem personarum destrueret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_68:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas creaturae cuius essentia sit sua operatio inadmissibilis est, quia tolleret distinctionem actus primi, potentiae et actus secundi atque creaturam in purum actum converteret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSA-02","text":"Homo completus neque in anima sola neque in corpore ante animationem praesto esse potest; systema circa absentiam utriusque subiecti separatim completi ordinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_67:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSA-03","text":"Continuatio post mortem eiusdem vitae phantasticae et sensitivae impossibilis est; subsistentia animae pretium mutationis radicalis modi operationis exigit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:SNX5","CH_GGVC_P_69:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSA-04","text":"Accessus immediatus ad essentias substantiales cognitioni humanae indisponibilis manet; substantia tantum per operationes, proprietates et accidentia manifesta reconstructive cognoscitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:XD-01","text":"Principium intellectivum eo ipso potest esse forma corporis et subsistens, quia formalitas relationem ad esse compositi constituit, subsistentia autem ex operatione non organica provenit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-03","CL_GGVC_26:GA-05","CL_GGVC_26:GA-13","CH_GGVC_P_67:D3","CH_GGVC_P_68:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:XD-02","text":"Pluralitas operationum non ad pluralitatem formarum substantialium, sed ad pluralitatem potentiarum unius formae releganda est.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-04","CL_GGVC_26:GA-06","CL_GGVC_26:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_68:D9","CH_GGVC_P_69:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:XD-03","text":"Quia sensus est operatio coniuncti, facultates sensitivae non solum organis ut instrumentis utuntur, sed in composito tamquam proprio subiecto inhaerent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-03","CL_GGVC_26:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_67:D6","CH_GGVC_P_69:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:XD-04","text":"Necessitas phantasmatum, dispositio sensitiva corporis et subiectivitas facultatum organicarum unam architecturam embodimenti constituunt, non tres dependentias fortuitas.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-07","CL_GGVC_26:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_67:D13","CH_GGVC_P_68:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:XD-05","text":"Immediatio unionis substantialis cum mediatione potentiarum compatitur, quia prima esse constituit, altera operationes intra substantiam iam constitutam ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-04","CL_GGVC_26:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_68:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:XD-06","text":"Incorruptibilitas animae non conservationem uniformem vitae humanae significat: mors subiectum organicum destruit, potentias non organicas conservat et formam subsistentem incompletam relinquit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-13","CL_GGVC_26:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_67:D11","CH_GGVC_P_69:D11","CH_GGVC_P_68:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:XD-07","text":"Plures animae individuatae idem universale cognoscere possunt, quia individuatio ad relationem formae cum hoc composito, universalitas vero ad modum immaterialem receptionis pertinet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_67:A10","CH_GGVC_P_68:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSD-01","text":"Subiectum humanum est unitas distributa: compositum est subiectum totius vitae humanae, anima potentiarum non organicarum, organa autem loci determinati executionis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-03","CL_GGVC_26:GA-08","CL_GGVC_26:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_68:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_69:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas interna tripliciter ordinatur: forma immediate dat esse, essentia potentias naturaliter producit, potentiae autem per actus et obiecta formaliter determinantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-04","CL_GGVC_26:GA-07","CL_GGVC_26:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_68:A6","CH_GGVC_P_69:A15","CH_GGVC_P_69:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSD-03","text":"Epistemologia systematis est indirecta sed ontologice fecunda: operationes et accidentia non sunt essentia, tamen ad eius structuram cognoscendam necessaria manent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-01","CL_GGVC_26:GA-06","CH_GGVC_P_69:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSD-04","text":"Tempus potentiarum non habet unum ordinem: perfectio a superioribus descendit, generatio ab inferioribus ascendit, mors autem actualitatem organicam in virtualem radicem contrahit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-09","CL_GGVC_26:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_69:D7","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSD-05","text":"Corporalitas est simul condicio positiva cognitionis humanae et fons corruptibilitatis, non defectus extrinsecus animae neque ratio totalis intellectus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-05","CL_GGVC_26:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_68:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_68:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSD-06","text":"Identitas post mortem est realis sed non integra: idem principium subsistens manet, dum identitas hominis completi et apparatus organici non manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-13","CL_GGVC_26:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_67:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_68:D4","CH_GGVC_P_69:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RLE-01","text":"Operatio manifestat essentiam; essentia potentias producit; potentiae operationes specificant; operationes sic ad principium a quo deductae sunt probative redeunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RLE-02","text":"Ordo superioris et inferioris circuitum causalitatis efficit: superius per perfectionem et finem causat, inferius autem per generationem et receptionem condicionem praebet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_69:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RLE-03","text":"Cognitio sensibilia requirit, sensibilia organa, organa corpus dispositum, corpus vero animam cuius modus cognoscendi ipsam dispositionem corporis exigit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_68:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RLE-04","text":"Attractor principalis est unitas non homogenea: quo vehementius unitas substantialis affirmatur, eo accuratius pluralitas potentiarum, subiectorum et ordinum distinguitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_69:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RLE-05","text":"Mors fit experimentum recursionis negativum: subiecti corruptio ostendit quae potentiae animae per se inhaereant et quae tantum ex ea in coniuncto radicentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_69:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FGS-B01","text":"Operationes vitales distinguuntur secundum dependentiam ab organo → ex earum modo infertur modus principii et subiecti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:A5","CH_GGVC_P_68:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FGS-B02","text":"Operatio intellectiva non organica → principium intellectivum subsistens; actus huic homini attributus → idem principium forma intrinseca corporis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:D3","CH_GGVC_P_68:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FGS-B03","text":"Forma intrinseca dat unum esse → una anima virtute continet vitam, sensum et intellectum → pluralitas formarum substantialium excluditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:A8","CH_GGVC_P_68:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FGS-B04","text":"Operatio intermittens non potest esse essentia → inter essentiam et actum interponitur potentia → unum esse multas operationes sustinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A2","CH_GGVC_P_69:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FGS-B05","text":"Potentia per obiectum specificatur → per operationem subiecto distribuitur → per ordines activum, finalem et susceptivum cum aliis potentiis conectitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:D4","CH_GGVC_P_69:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FGS-B06","text":"Modus cognitionis corpus organicum postulat → anima corpus constituit et disponit → corpus pars subiecti operationum organicarum fit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FGS-B07","text":"Corruptio corporis potentias secundum subiecta examinat → non organica actu permanent → organica ad radicem virtualem contrahuntur → anima manet, homo completus non manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:D11","CH_GGVC_P_67:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:AFM-B01","text":"Effectus vitales → principium vitae → subsistentia intellectiva → forma corporis → unum esse → potentiae ordinatae → status differentialis post mortem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:AFM-B02","text":"Unitas compositi → unica forma substantialis → continentia virtualis graduum vitae → pluralitas facultatum sine pluralitate substantiarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:A6","CH_GGVC_P_69:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:AFM-B03","text":"Phantasma → sensus → organum → corpus dispositum → potentia in coniuncto → embodimentum ut structura cognitionis humanae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:D13","CH_GGVC_P_68:D13","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:AFM-B04","text":"Corruptio corporis → amissio subiecti organici → permanentia actualis intellectus et voluntatis → remanentia virtualis inferiorum → incompletio animae separatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_67:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MME-01","text":"Relatio animae et corporis ex relatione duarum rerum prius constitutarum in relationem actus formalis et materiae per actum constitutae convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_68:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MME-02","text":"Separatio intellectus ex situ animae respectu corporis in independentiam determinatae potentiae ab organo reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MME-03","text":"Oppositio unitatis et pluralitatis corruit: gradus vitales et potentiae plurales modi ordinati unius formae fiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_69:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MME-04","text":"Corpus ex instrumento externo in partem subiecti facultatum organicarum et condicionem internam cognitionis humanae transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_69:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MME-05","text":"Individuatio subiecti et universalitas obiecti desinunt esse aemulae, quia ad formam compositi et ad modum receptionis distincte referuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MME-06","text":"Permanentia post mortem ex oppositione conservationis et corruptionis in tabulam actualitatis, virtualitatis et incompletionis reordinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei determinat naturam animae, modum unionis cum corpore, structuram potentiarum et earum permanentiam post mortem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_69:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis disponit unum esse, unam formam, multas potentias, diversos actus, obiecta formalia et subiecta organica vel non organica.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_69:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus quaestiones de contactu, situ et partibus in quaestiones attributionis actus, causalitatis formalis, subiecti et ordinis convertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_69:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis animam inter actum et potentiam constituit, ordinem perfectionis ab ordine generationis distinguit et mortem tamquam mutationem subiecti operativi tractat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_69:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus distinctiones quas affirmat performat: univocationes dissolvit, operationes distribuit et ex responsionibus localibus architecturam ontologicam recursivam efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AF2","CH_GGVC_P_69:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FL-01","text":"Communicatio eiusdem esse animae et composito cum subsistentia animae post corruptionem compositi componitur, sed singularis modus huius esse communis breviter tantum determinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:A8","CH_GGVC_P_68:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FL-02","text":"Anima corpus substantialiter constituit, corpus tamen dispositum ad receptionem animae requiritur; distinctio prioritatum contradictionem impedit, sed ordinem generationis comprimit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_68:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FL-03","text":"Animae per corpora multiplicantur, sed distinctio earum post destructionem corporum manet; principium individuationis persistentis non plene explicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:D6","CH_GGVC_P_68:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FL-04","text":"Intellectus organo independens in statu coniuncto phantasmatibus naturaliter dependet, dum modus cognitionis separatae affirmatur potius quam demonstratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:D13","CH_GGVC_P_67:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FL-05","text":"Omnes potentiae ab anima oriuntur, sed non omnes animam habent pro subiecto; virtualis earum remanentia commissuram servat, cuius modus ontologicus indeterminatus manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:D9","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MP-01","text":"Separatio mutatur ex distantia substantiali in independentiam potentiae ab organo, deinde in statum post mortem qui alium modum operationis exigit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_67:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MP-02","text":"Potentia mutatur ex principio metaphysico receptivitatis in facultatem operativam et tandem in radicem virtualem potentiae organicae destructo subiecto.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:A9","CH_GGVC_P_69:A1","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MP-03","text":"Subiectum ab homine integro ad animam vel coniunctum secundum operationem distribuitur, unde identitas agentis non amplius uno modo praedicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A11","CH_GGVC_P_69:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite apparet ut machina actuum inclusorum: forma materiam actu constituit, essentia potentias producit, potentiae actus ordinant, obiecta actus determinant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_69:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum humanum est centrum non locale sed distributum: una forma tota adest, apex intellectivus organum excedit, facultates inferiores per organa partiuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:D16","CH_GGVC_P_68:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:HSI-03","text":"Tempus huius machinae duplicem directionem habet: generatio ab inferioribus ascendit, perfectio a superioribus descendit, mors autem structuram actualem ad nucleum subsistentem contrahit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:D7","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio cognoscitiva ab operationibus visibilibus ad principia occulta procedit et ab illis principiis ad distributionem operationum revertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata extracta sufficiunt ad structuram huius gregis restituendam, quia naturam animae, unitatem compositi, distinctionem potentiarum et statum post mortem ex eadem regula operationis et esse conectunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_68:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est coniunctio attributionis actus intelligendi, unitatis esse substantialis et distinctionis subiectorum secundum operationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent individuatio animae post corpus, modus cognitionis separatae et status ontologicus virtualitatis potentiarum organicarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_67:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MRT-B04","text":"Cohaerentia pendet ex distinctionibus inter subsistentiam et completionem, principium et subiectum, essentiam et potentiam, dependentiam obiectivam et organicam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat: ab operatione ad essentiam, ab essentia ad formam et potentias, a distributione potentiarum ad embodimentum, atque a corruptione subiecti ad permanentiam differentialem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T172411338984Z_fa353658","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 76–78","source_sha256":"2655778a3deb2d47bbaa7075b7f8f0c78bc00de5c11ed0253dbb44fb65640b08","sha256":"67af3e916680c6d77fce2d18a211e03ee1052d87d1ae0c9c7749f80f98fa4d1d","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:24:11.340501+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how the soul, which exceeds the body in intellectual operation and subsists through itself, can remain the one form of the body and order a plurality of organic and non-organic powers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_69:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BAS-02","text":"The central thesis establishes the soul as the substantial form of one human being, communicating one being to the composite, yet producing many powers distinct according to operations and subjects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_69:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BAS-03","text":"The argument proceeds back from manifest operations to ontological principles and reconstructively determines the structure of the soul through the attribution of acts, organic dependence, and the intermittence of operations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_69:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BAS-04","text":"The movement proceeds from the effects of life to subsistence, thence to formal union, from union to the architecture of powers, and finally to their state after death.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_69:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BAS-05","text":"The movement is recursive and cumulative: operations manifest essence, essence generates powers, powers distribute operations, and the distribution in turn proves the primary ontology again through operations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_67:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BAS-06","text":"The closure is asymmetrical, because death confirms the soul's subsistence and the persistence of non-organic powers, but at the same time shows that the separated soul is neither a complete human being nor the actual subject of the whole of human life.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_69:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] The mode of operation manifests the mode of being and the formal principle of the operator; operation is therefore the primary epistemic path to ontological structure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:A5","CH_GGVC_P_68:A4","CH_GGVC_P_69:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The life of the body proceeds not from corporeity alone, but from the formal act by which the living body is determined; the soul is accordingly the principle of life and not a body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:A2","CH_GGVC_P_67:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] The same corporeal human being senses and understands, because the same soul is the form of the body and a subsistent intellectual principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:A2","CH_GGVC_P_68:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] One substantial being requires one substantial form, which contains lower vital perfections virtually rather than being superadded to actually aggregated forms.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:A6","CH_GGVC_P_68:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The independence of the intellect from a bodily organ pertains to an operative power, not to a separation of the soul's essence from the office of being the form of the body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_68:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] The soul's essence, powers, and operations constitute distinct levels: essence is first act, powers are principles of second acts, while operations are exercised perfections.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A2","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] A power is specified by its proper act, and acts by the formal account of the object, not by the mere material identity of the objected thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A6","CH_GGVC_P_69:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] The subject of a power follows the mode of operation: intellect and will inhere in the soul, whereas organic powers inhere in the composite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A11","CH_GGVC_P_69:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Powers proceed from one essence by natural resultance and causal order, without transformation of the soul and without a coordinated plurality of principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] The human body is not a subject complete prior to animation, but matter which the soul constitutes and organically disposes according to the requirements of sensitive cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_68:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Ontological unity sustains ordered operative plurality; unity of substance requires neither homogeneity of operations nor simplicity of faculties.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A5","CH_GGVC_P_69:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] The individuation of intellect and the universality of cognition pertain to different orders: the knower is the form of this composite, while the intelligible species is received immaterially.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_68:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-13","text":"[Persistent] The soul is a finite created form through participated being, but incorruptible insofar as, being subsistent, it does not receive its being from the body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:A11","CH_GGVC_P_67:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Death severs the operative architecture: intellect and will remain actually, organic powers remain in the soul only virtually, and the complete human being ceases.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:D11","CH_GGVC_P_67:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-01","text":"The rule from operation to being returns consistently, but changes function: first it proves the soul's subsistence, then attributes the form of the subject, and finally distinguishes powers through acts and objects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:A5","CH_GGVC_P_69:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-02","text":"Separation is transferred from the hypothesis of an extrinsic soul to the distinction of intellectual power from organ, and then in death signifies the soul's real state without the body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_68:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-03","text":"Subsistence is first inferred from proper operation, then deprived of specific completeness, and finally retains a natural inclination toward the body even after separation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:D3","CH_GGVC_P_67:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-04","text":"Unity is amplified from the composition of soul and body to a single substantial form and thence to one essence ordering many powers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_69:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-05","text":"The body changes from a term of relation to the soul into matter constituted by the soul, and then into a part of the subject of organic powers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-06","text":"Power migrates from a metaphysical principle of reception into an operative faculty and after death also designates the mode of virtual remaining.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:A9","CH_GGVC_P_69:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-07","text":"Mediation is inverted: it is constitutively excluded between soul and matter, but operatively restored among essence, powers, objects, and organs.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_68:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-08","text":"Incorruptibility is restricted from the absolute preservation of the entire vital apparatus to stratified preservation, whereby not everything arising from the soul actually remains.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:NX11","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RMD-09","text":"The comparison of the soul with an angel, using the distinction of intellectual degree, is absorbed into a higher structure as a determination of the human boundary between spiritual and corporeal.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_69:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:CGD-01","text":"Questions concerning spiritual matter and the species of the soul in relation to an angel remain local; at the higher level they support only the soul's finitude and its natural ordination to the body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_67:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:CGD-02","text":"Doctrines concerning elemental forms, mixture, and the complexion of the body are local mechanisms of disposition, not axioms of the whole group.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:D10","CH_GGVC_P_68:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:CGD-03","text":"More particular distinctions of the accounts of objects and the orders of powers, which locally classify faculties, are compressed at the higher level into the general rule of specification and multiple priority.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A7","CH_GGVC_P_69:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:CGD-04","text":"The rule of operation and being transcends all units: it joins essence, form, the subject of power, and the state after death by the same reconstructive instrument.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_68:A4","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:CGD-05","text":"The communication of one being becomes the principal joint between the soul's subsistence, the unity of the composite, and the plurality of powers outside essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:A8","CH_GGVC_P_69:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:CGD-06","text":"Models of the soul as mover, common intellect, or aggregation of forms, locally polemical, are converted at this level into general negative limits upon any substantial anthropology.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_68:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GNX-01","text":"The system denies that the body as body can explain life, intellect, or the unity of the human being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GNX-02","text":"The system denies that the soul is a complete human substance which moves the body extrinsically, because motion alone constitutes neither one human being nor immanent action.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_68:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GNX-03","text":"The system excludes a transindividual intellect common to all human beings, lest the act of understanding be separated from this human being as principal agent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_68:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GNX-04","text":"The system repudiates a plurality of souls or substantial forms in the same human being, because it would dissolve the unity of one being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_68:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GNX-05","text":"The system removes every accidental or corporeal medium in substantial union, although it admits instruments and mediations in operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_68:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GNX-06","text":"The system denies that the soul's essence is its power or that unity of essence entails a single faculty.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_69:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:GNX-07","text":"The system denies that organic powers remain actually in the separated soul or that an intact sensory faculty can subsist without organs.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:NX11","CH_GGVC_P_69:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that unity of subject requires homogeneity of operations; otherwise one form could not contain diverse grades of life and powers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that subsistence simply entails specific completeness or personality; otherwise the subsistent soul would be confused with the complete human being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that matter prior to substantial form is already an actually constituted organic body; otherwise the soul would not give being simply.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that every origin requires temporal causality or transformation of that from which it originates; otherwise powers could not flow naturally from essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that every dependence on the body signifies dependence on an organ, a cause of being, or inclusion of the body in essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_68:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that the universality of the object requires numerical identity of intellect; otherwise universal cognition would destroy the plurality of persons.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_68:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSA-01","text":"The possibility of a creature whose essence is its operation is inadmissible, because it would remove the distinction between first act, power, and second act and convert the creature into pure act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSA-02","text":"The complete human being can be present neither in the soul alone nor in the body prior to animation; the system is ordered around the absence of each separately complete subject.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_67:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSA-03","text":"The continuation after death of the same phantasmal and sensitive life is impossible; the soul's subsistence exacts the price of a radical change in the mode of operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:SNX5","CH_GGVC_P_69:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSA-04","text":"Immediate access to substantial essences remains unavailable to human cognition; substance is known reconstructively only through manifest operations, properties, and accidents.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:XD-01","text":"An intellectual principle can thereby be both form of the body and subsistent, because formality constitutes a relation to the being of the composite, whereas subsistence proceeds from non-organic operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-03","CL_GGVC_26:GA-05","CL_GGVC_26:GA-13","CH_GGVC_P_67:D3","CH_GGVC_P_68:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:XD-02","text":"A plurality of operations must be referred not to a plurality of substantial forms, but to a plurality of powers of one form.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-04","CL_GGVC_26:GA-06","CL_GGVC_26:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_68:D9","CH_GGVC_P_69:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:XD-03","text":"Because sensation is an operation of the composite, sensitive faculties not only use organs as instruments, but inhere in the composite as their proper subject.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-03","CL_GGVC_26:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_67:D6","CH_GGVC_P_69:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:XD-04","text":"The necessity of phantasms, the sensitive disposition of the body, and the subjectivity of organic faculties constitute one architecture of embodiment, not three contingent dependencies.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-07","CL_GGVC_26:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_67:D13","CH_GGVC_P_68:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:XD-05","text":"The immediacy of substantial union is compatible with the mediation of powers, because the former constitutes being, while the latter orders operations within the substance already constituted.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-04","CL_GGVC_26:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_68:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:XD-06","text":"The soul's incorruptibility does not signify the uniform preservation of human life: death destroys the organic subject, preserves non-organic powers, and leaves the subsistent form incomplete.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-13","CL_GGVC_26:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_67:D11","CH_GGVC_P_69:D11","CH_GGVC_P_68:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:XD-07","text":"Several individuated souls can cognize the same universal, because individuation pertains to the relation of form to this composite, whereas universality pertains to the immaterial mode of reception.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_67:A10","CH_GGVC_P_68:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSD-01","text":"The human subject is a distributed unity: the composite is the subject of the whole of human life, the soul of non-organic powers, and the organs the determinate sites of execution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-03","CL_GGVC_26:GA-08","CL_GGVC_26:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_68:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_69:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSD-02","text":"Internal causality is ordered in three ways: form immediately gives being, essence naturally produces powers, and powers are formally determined through acts and objects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-04","CL_GGVC_26:GA-07","CL_GGVC_26:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_68:A6","CH_GGVC_P_69:A15","CH_GGVC_P_69:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSD-03","text":"The system's epistemology is indirect but ontologically fruitful: operations and accidents are not essence, yet they remain necessary for knowing its structure.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-01","CL_GGVC_26:GA-06","CH_GGVC_P_69:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSD-04","text":"The time of powers does not have one order: perfection descends from higher things, generation ascends from lower things, while death contracts organic actuality into a virtual root.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-09","CL_GGVC_26:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_69:D7","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSD-05","text":"Corporeality is both a positive condition of human cognition and a source of corruptibility, neither an extrinsic defect of the soul nor the total account of intellect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-05","CL_GGVC_26:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_68:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_68:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:BSD-06","text":"Identity after death is real but not integral: the same subsistent principle remains, while the identity of the complete human being and of the organic apparatus does not remain.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-13","CL_GGVC_26:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_67:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_68:D4","CH_GGVC_P_69:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RLE-01","text":"Operation manifests essence; essence produces powers; powers specify operations; thus operations return probatively to the principle from which they were derived.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RLE-02","text":"The order of higher and lower produces a circuit of causality: the higher causes through perfection and end, whereas the lower provides the condition through generation and reception.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_69:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RLE-03","text":"Cognition requires sensibles, sensibles require organs, organs require a disposed body, and the body requires a soul whose mode of knowing itself requires the body's disposition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_68:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RLE-04","text":"The principal attractor is non-homogeneous unity: the more strongly substantial unity is affirmed, the more precisely the plurality of powers, subjects, and orders is distinguished.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_69:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:RLE-05","text":"Death becomes a negative experiment of recursion: the corruption of the subject shows which powers inhere in the soul through themselves and which are rooted in it only in the composite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_69:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FGS-B01","text":"Vital operations are distinguished according to dependence on an organ → from their mode, the mode of the principle and subject is inferred.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:A5","CH_GGVC_P_68:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FGS-B02","text":"Non-organic intellectual operation → subsistent intellectual principle; act attributed to this human being → the same principle is the intrinsic form of the body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:D3","CH_GGVC_P_68:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FGS-B03","text":"An intrinsic form gives one being → one soul virtually contains life, sensation, and intellect → plurality of substantial forms is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:A8","CH_GGVC_P_68:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FGS-B04","text":"An intermittent operation cannot be essence → power is interposed between essence and act → one being sustains many operations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A2","CH_GGVC_P_69:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FGS-B05","text":"A power is specified through its object → through operation it is distributed to a subject → through active, final, and receptive orders it is connected with other powers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:D4","CH_GGVC_P_69:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FGS-B06","text":"The mode of cognition requires an organic body → the soul constitutes and disposes the body → the body becomes a part of the subject of organic operations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FGS-B07","text":"The corruption of the body examines powers according to subjects → non-organic powers remain actually → organic powers are contracted into a virtual root → the soul remains, the complete human being does not remain.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:D11","CH_GGVC_P_67:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:AFM-B01","text":"Vital effects → principle of life → intellectual subsistence → form of the body → one being → ordered powers → differential state after death.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:AFM-B02","text":"Unity of the composite → one substantial form → virtual containment of grades of life → plurality of faculties without plurality of substances.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:A6","CH_GGVC_P_69:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:AFM-B03","text":"Phantasm → sense → organ → disposed body → power in the composite → embodiment as the structure of human cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:D13","CH_GGVC_P_68:D13","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:AFM-B04","text":"Corruption of the body → loss of the organic subject → actual persistence of intellect and will → virtual remaining of lower powers → incompleteness of the separated soul.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_67:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MME-01","text":"The relation of soul and body is converted from the relation of two previously constituted things into the relation of formal act and matter constituted through act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_68:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MME-02","text":"The separation of intellect is reclassified from the soul's situation with respect to the body into the independence of a determinate power from an organ.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MME-03","text":"The opposition of unity and plurality collapses: vital grades and plural powers become ordered modes of one form.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_69:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MME-04","text":"The body is transferred from an external instrument into a part of the subject of organic faculties and an internal condition of human cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_69:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MME-05","text":"The individuation of the subject and the universality of the object cease to be rivals, because they are referred distinctly to the form of the composite and to the mode of reception.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MME-06","text":"Persistence after death is reordered from an opposition between preservation and corruption into a table of actuality, virtuality, and incompleteness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level determines the nature of the soul, the mode of union with the body, the structure of powers, and their persistence after death.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_69:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level disposes one being, one form, many powers, diverse acts, formal objects, and organic or non-organic subjects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_69:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level converts questions concerning contact, situation, and parts into questions of attribution of act, formal causality, subject, and order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_69:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level constitutes the soul between act and power, distinguishes the order of perfection from the order of generation, and treats death as a change of operative subject.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_69:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological level performs the distinctions it affirms: it dissolves univocations, distributes operations, and produces a recursive ontological architecture from local responses.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AF2","CH_GGVC_P_69:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FL-01","text":"The communication of the same being to the soul and the composite is reconciled with the soul's subsistence after the corruption of the composite, but the singular mode of this common being is determined only briefly.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:A8","CH_GGVC_P_68:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FL-02","text":"The soul substantially constitutes the body, yet a body disposed for receiving the soul is required; the distinction of priorities prevents contradiction, but compresses the order of generation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_68:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FL-03","text":"Souls are multiplied through bodies, but their distinction remains after the destruction of bodies; the principle of persistent individuation is not fully explained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:D6","CH_GGVC_P_68:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FL-04","text":"The intellect, independent of an organ, naturally depends on phantasms in the conjoined state, while the mode of separated cognition is affirmed rather than demonstrated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:D13","CH_GGVC_P_67:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:FL-05","text":"All powers arise from the soul, but not all have the soul as subject; their virtual remaining preserves the joint, whose ontological mode remains indeterminate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:D9","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MP-01","text":"Separation changes from substantial distance into the independence of a power from an organ, then into the state after death that requires another mode of operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_67:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MP-02","text":"Power changes from a metaphysical principle of receptivity into an operative faculty and finally into the virtual root of organic power when its subject is destroyed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:A9","CH_GGVC_P_69:A1","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MP-03","text":"The subject is distributed from the whole human being to the soul or composite according to operation, whence the identity of the agent is no longer predicated in one way.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:A11","CH_GGVC_P_69:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:HSI-01","text":"Reality implicitly appears as a machine of enclosed acts: form actually constitutes matter, essence produces powers, powers order acts, and objects determine acts.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_69:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:HSI-02","text":"The human subject is a non-local but distributed center: one whole form is present, the intellectual apex exceeds the organ, and lower faculties are distributed through organs.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:D16","CH_GGVC_P_68:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:HSI-03","text":"The time of this machine has a double direction: generation ascends from lower things, perfection descends from higher things, while death contracts the actual structure into the subsistent nucleus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_69:D7","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:HSI-04","text":"Cognitive navigation proceeds from visible operations to hidden principles and returns from those principles to the distribution of operations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_69:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms suffice to restore the structure of this group, because they connect the nature of the soul, the unity of the composite, the distinction of powers, and the state after death through the same rule of operation and being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_68:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the conjunction of the attribution of the act of understanding, the unity of substantial being, and the distinction of subjects according to operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MRT-B03","text":"The individuation of the soul after the body, the mode of separated cognition, and the ontological status of the virtuality of organic powers remain less determined.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_68:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_67:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MRT-B04","text":"Coherence depends on distinctions between subsistence and completeness, principle and subject, essence and power, objective and organic dependence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_26:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement: from operation to essence, from essence to form and powers, from the distribution of powers to embodiment, and from the corruption of the subject to differential persistence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_67:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_69:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_26@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_26:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 76–78","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 75 · Prima pars · Quaestio 76 · Prima pars · Quaestio 77"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 79–81","order":27,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_70","CH_GGVC_P_71","CH_GGVC_P_72"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_27@R001","sha256":"c6a685a6440807ccfa07f26d1557378668eecf930b959d8456f1a7e9db27d58b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quo criterio operationes vegetativae, sensitivae, intellectivae et appetitivae in potentias distinctas, non redundantes et ordinatas distribuantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_71:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_72:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-02","text":"Thesis globalis statuit potentiam ex ratione formali obiecti et irreducibilitate operationis discerni, dum organum, nomen, status vel identitas materialis rei distinctionem per se non constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A2","CH_GGVC_P_71:A3","CH_GGVC_P_72:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit reconstructio taxonomica quae multiplicationem potentiarum simul generat et coercet, doctrinas aemulas ad communia criteria obiecti, actus et potentiae reducens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_71:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-04","text":"Motus ascendens progreditur ab operationibus corporeis per receptionem sensitivam et phantasmata ad abstractionem, receptionem intellectivam et universalitatem obiecti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_71:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-05","text":"Motus descendens convertit intelligibile universale in bonum appetibile, deinde per apprehensionem particularem, appetitum inferiorem et potentiam exsecutivam ad motum corporalem terminatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_72:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-06","text":"Motus totius est spiralis: gradus superior ex inferiori emergit sine eo absorpto, deinde ad inferiorem redit ut universalitas efficaciam particularem acquirat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX6","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-07","text":"Clausura est operationalis potius quam substantialis, quia cognitio, appetitus et exsecutio distincta manent sed unum processum actionis animalis constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A10","CH_GGVC_P_72:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-08","text":"Systema ordinem sufficientem potentiarum restituit, sed nexus exactus inter criteria obiecti et operationis atque inter appetitum superiorem et inferiorem non omnino formalizatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_72:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Potentia est principium proximum operationis, atque nova potentia admittitur ubi actus necessarius ad principium iam positum reduci nequit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A1","CH_GGVC_P_70:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] Ratio formalis obiecti, non res materialiter sumpta, organum vel nomen facultatis, est centrum distinctionis potentiarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A3","CH_GGVC_P_72:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Multiplicatio potentiarum regitur sufficientia oeconomica: irreducibilitas principii distinguit, differentia accidentalis actus vel vocabuli non distinguit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_71:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Ascensus potentiarum auget universalitatem obiecti et minuit dependentiam formalem ab immutatione materiali, quamvis gradus superiores condicionibus inferioribus uti pergant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_71:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Omne principium creatum transit de potentia in actum per aliquid actu, nec intellectus finitus est actualis possessio omnium intelligibilium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A5","CH_GGVC_P_71:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Cognitio recipit formas rerum aliarum intentionaliter, non ipsam possessionem realem earum, atque hac receptione naturalem determinationem cognoscentis excedit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A8","CH_GGVC_P_72:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Cognitio et appetitus distinguuntur quia res cognoscitur sub ratione entis vel veri, appetitur autem sub ratione boni vel convenientis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_72:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Passivitas a receptione sensibili formae ad receptionem perfectivam intellectus et mobilitatem appetitus transfertur, sine reductione ad corruptionem materialem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A13","CH_GGVC_P_71:A6","CH_GGVC_P_72:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Intellectus humanus duplicatur in principium agens, quod intelligibile actu efficit, et possibile, quod intelligibile actu recipit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_71:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Actio cognitiva propria requirit principium intrinsecum subiecto inhaerens, quamvis virtus intellectiva causam universalem superiorem participet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A9","CH_GGVC_P_71:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Appetitus animalis consequitur apprehensionem et dividitur secundum genus sensibilis vel intelligibilis quod ipsum passive movet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A9","CH_GGVC_P_72:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Efficacia exterior universalis ordinem particularem, appetitum inferiorem et potentiam corporalem exsecutivam requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A10","CH_GGVC_P_72:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-13","text":"[Persistent] Superior potentia inferiorem non abolet; differentia humana potentias communes rationaliter elevat et secundum fines superiores ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Cognitio humana completur ut processus temporalis a phantasmate per abstractionem, habitum et iudicium ad bonum apprehensum atque actionem mediatam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_72:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-01","text":"Criterium rationis formalis identitatem stabilem servat: primum genera potentiarum ordinat, deinde nomina intellectiva contrahit, postremo cognitionem ab appetitu et duos appetitus distinguit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A3","CH_GGVC_P_71:A3","CH_GGVC_P_72:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-02","text":"Principium sufficientiae functionem mutat: in sensu facultates irreducibiles multiplicat, in intellectu vocabula plura ad duas potentias reducit, in appetitu duplicem divisionem ex genere apprehensionis restituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_71:D7","CH_GGVC_P_72:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-03","text":"Receptivitas redit cum variatione: sensus recipit formam intentionalem, intellectus possibilis actum perfectivum, appetitus autem motionem ab apprehenso bono.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A13","CH_GGVC_P_71:A6","CH_GGVC_P_72:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-04","text":"Memoria mutatur a potentia sensitiva retinente intentiones praeteritas in conservationem habitualem specierum intra ipsum intellectum possibilem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A17","CH_GGVC_P_71:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_71:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-05","text":"Appetitus, initio genus commune omni sensitivo, amplificatur in ordinem sensitivum et intellectivum, quorum distinctio ab antecedente apprehensione dependet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A9","CH_GGVC_P_72:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-06","text":"Universalitas ab obiecto intellectus ad rationem appetendi migrat, sed vim exsecutivam non acquirit nisi ad singulare per ordinem inferiorem descendat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A4","CH_GGVC_P_72:A10","CH_GGVC_P_72:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-07","text":"Organum ab origine taxonomica excluditur et in condicionem instrumentalem transfertur: sensus organo utitur, intellectus phantasmatibus dependet, sed potentia ex organo non numeratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A4","CH_GGVC_P_71:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-08","text":"Synderesis et conscientia localiter taxonomiam intellectivam complent, sed in divisione appetitus non redeunt ut facultates novae; vis earum in habitum, applicationem et ordinationem actus absorbetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_72:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:CGD-01","text":"Enumerationes trium potentiarum vegetativarum, quinque sensuum exteriorum et quattuor interiorum structurae locales manent; ad gradum praesentem earum principium distinctionis, non numeri singuli, ascendit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:D1","CH_GGVC_P_70:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:CGD-02","text":"Modi particulares immutationis qui quinque sensus exteriores constituunt ad stratum sensitivum pertinent neque totius ordinis intellectivi et appetitivi mensuram praebent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:CGD-03","text":"Identificatio intellectus superioris cum Deo manet determinatio theologica localis; sursum amplificatur tantum structura participationis causalitatis universalis et virtutis intrinsecae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:D4","CH_GGVC_P_71:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:CGD-04","text":"Criterium obiecti formalis amplificatur globaliter, quia simul taxonomiam sensitivam, compressionem nominum intellectivorum et divisionem appetituum regit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A3","CH_GGVC_P_71:A3","CH_GGVC_P_72:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:CGD-05","text":"Sensus interiores transcendunt statum enumerationis localis cum phantasmata ab eis servata condiciones materiales abstractionis intellectivae fiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:D13","CH_GGVC_P_71:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:CGD-06","text":"Universalitas intellectiva unitates coniungit cum appetitu, quia idem subiectum quod ens et verum universaliter recipit singulare sub ratione universali boni appetere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A4","CH_GGVC_P_72:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:CGD-07","text":"Correspondentia immediata inter nomen psychologicum et potentiam realem obsoleta fit; vocabula secundum potentiam, habitum, actum, officium et finem recodificantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GNX-01","text":"Nulla enumeratio absoluta potentiarum valet sine ratione divisionis quae numeros animarum, modorum vivendi, generum et actuum distinguat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GNX-02","text":"Anatomia, organum et alteratio naturalis neque principium formale potentiae neque explicatio sufficiens sensationis constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GNX-03","text":"Intellectus creatus nec essentia animae nec actus purus omnium intelligibilium esse potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GNX-04","text":"Formae separatae directe participatae, intellectus agens extrinsecus sufficiens et unus intellectus numericus omnibus hominibus communes excluduntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GNX-05","text":"Distinctio actuum aut pluralitas vocabulorum sine irreducibilitate principiorum multiplicationem facultatum legitimam non efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX7","CH_GGVC_P_71:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GNX-06","text":"Appetitus animalis neque inclinationi naturali univoce reducitur neque cum cognitione propter identitatem materialem obiecti confunditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_72:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GNX-07","text":"Universale intelligibile non transit immediate in executionem corporalem; particulare, appetitus inferior et dispositio exsecutiva non eliminari possunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:A11","CH_GGVC_P_72:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet obiectum potentiae ex sola re exteriori materialiter definiri, quia tota differentiatio cognitionis et appetitus aliter corrueret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_72:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem diversitatem operationis vel nominis facultatem realem constituere, alioquin sufficientia taxonomica in proliferationem indefinitam solveretur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX7","CH_GGVC_P_71:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet gradum superiorem inferiorem abolere, quia abstractio, appetitus intellectivus et motus exterior condicionibus sensitivis permanentibus nituntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX6","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet passivitatem corruptionem vel inferioritatem simplicem significare, quia receptio actus est perfectio sensus, intellectus et appetitus mobilis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_71:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet universalitatem cognitionis subiectum transpersonale exigere aut causalitatem superiorem agentiam intrinsecam tollere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas indisponibilis est intellectus humanus finitus qui simul sit actus purus, causa prima sui luminis et possessio actualis omnium intelligibilium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSA-02","text":"Systema circum intervallum irreductibile inter similitudinem intentionalem et possessionem realem obiecti ordinatur; hoc intervallum appetitum et motum generat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSA-03","text":"Cognitio humana ordinaria sine sensibilibus, phantasmatibus et successione, sicut executio sine descensu universalis ad particulare, ontologice indisponibilis manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_72:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSA-04","text":"Facultas unica, partitio unica vel lexicon immediate ontologicum excluditur; unitas systematis est ordo relationum inter potentias, habitus, actus et fines.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-01","text":"Eadem res potest plures potentias movere sine duplicatione materiali obiecti, quia sensus, intellectus et appetitus eam sub rationibus formalibus diversis attingunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-01","CL_GGVC_27:GA-02","CL_GGVC_27:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_70:A3","CH_GGVC_P_71:A3","CH_GGVC_P_72:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-02","text":"Ascensus cognitivus est transductio ordinata qua receptio sensibilis intentionalis materiam phantasticam praebet, intellectus agens abstrahit et possibilis intelligibile actu recipit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-04","CL_GGVC_27:GA-06","CL_GGVC_27:GA-08","CL_GGVC_27:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_70:A13","CH_GGVC_P_71:A7","CH_GGVC_P_71:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-03","text":"Receptio formarum alienarum appetitum postulat quia cognitio obiectum intentionaliter praesentat, sed ipsum ut bonum reale possidendum non confert.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-06","CL_GGVC_27:GA-07","CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_72:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-04","text":"Duplicatio intellectus et duplicatio appetitus rationes diversas sequuntur: intellectus dividitur secundum facere et recipere intelligibile, appetitus secundum genus apprehensionis moventis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-02","CL_GGVC_27:GA-09","CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_71:A12","CH_GGVC_P_72:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-05","text":"Universalitas cognitionis et appetitus pluralitatem subiectorum personalium non tollit, quia singuli intellectus recipiunt universale et singularia sub ratione universali appetunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-09","CL_GGVC_27:GA-10","CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_71:A13","CH_GGVC_P_72:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-06","text":"Actio deliberata requirit catenam distributam: veritas ad opus ordinata fit ratio universalis boni, quae per apprehensionem particularem et appetitum inferiorem potentiam exsecutivam dirigit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-07","CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","CL_GGVC_27:GA-12","CL_GGVC_27:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_71:A16","CH_GGVC_P_72:A11","CH_GGVC_P_70:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-07","text":"Oeconomia potentiarum asymmetriam ordinatam producit: sensus plures propter modos irreducibiles receptionis, intellectus duas potentias correlativas, appetitus duas secundum apprehensionem moventem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-01","CL_GGVC_27:GA-02","CL_GGVC_27:GA-03","CH_GGVC_P_70:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_71:D7","CH_GGVC_P_72:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-08","text":"Rationalitas humana inferiores vires simul elevat et requirit: cogitativa phantasmata praeparat, intellectus universale elicit, appetitus superior autem per inferiorem efficaciam corporalem acquirit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-04","CL_GGVC_27:GA-12","CL_GGVC_27:GA-13","CH_GGVC_P_70:A18","CH_GGVC_P_71:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSD-01","text":"Anima apparet ut architectura transductionis quae alterationem materialem in praesentiam intentionalem, hanc in universalitatem intellectivam, et universalitatem in appetitum ac motum convertit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-04","CL_GGVC_27:GA-06","CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","CL_GGVC_27:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_70:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_71:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum humanum simul personale et universalitatis capax est: individuatio potentiae conditionem proprii actus constituit, non limitem qui veritatem communem destruat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-09","CL_GGVC_27:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_71:A13","CH_GGVC_P_71:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSD-03","text":"Causalitas operationis humanae distribuitur inter lumen superius, virtutem intrinsecam, obiectum apprehensum, appetitum motum et instrumentum corporale; nulla pars sola actum integrum producit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-05","CL_GGVC_27:GA-10","CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","CL_GGVC_27:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_71:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSD-04","text":"Tempus psychicum generatur ex absentia intentionaliter praesentata, retentione praeteriti, discursu successivo et ordinatione ad bonum nondum adeptum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-06","CL_GGVC_27:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_71:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_72:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSD-05","text":"Ordo moralis non postulat facultatem separatam: principia practica habitualia, applicatio conscientiae, appetitus intellectivus et mediatio inferior unum processum normativum distribuunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-03","CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","CL_GGVC_27:GA-12","CL_GGVC_27:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_71:A17","CH_GGVC_P_71:A18","CH_GGVC_P_72:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RLE-01","text":"Circuitus taxonomicus procedit ab actu ad principium proximum, a principio ad obiectum formale, deinde ab obiecto ad probationem utrum potentia distincta necessaria sit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A2","CH_GGVC_P_70:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RLE-02","text":"Via amplificationis cognitivae est praesentia vel absentia sensibilis → phantasma → abstractio → species intelligibilis → habitus → consideratio → discursus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_71:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RLE-03","text":"Via descensus efficaciae est universale intellectum → ratio boni → appetitus intellectivus → particulare apprehensum → appetitus inferior → exsecutio corporalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:A11","CH_GGVC_P_70:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RLE-04","text":"Ratio formalis obiecti est attractor totius structurae, quia ad eam redeunt dissensiones de numero, vocabulo, universalitate, identitate rei et divisione appetitus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A3","CH_GGVC_P_72:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RLE-05","text":"Absentia est attractor dynamicus: res non praesens per memoriam vel intellectum fit intentionaliter praesens, sed distantia realis appetitum et motum servat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A16","CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_72:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RLE-06","text":"Participatio constituit circuitum non occasionalisticum: causa superior lumen confert, virtus intrinseca abstrahit, subiectum recipit, et actus proprius eidem subiecto formaliter inhaeret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A11","CH_GGVC_P_71:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FGS-B01","text":"Operatio vitalis irreducibilis → principium proximum → ratio formalis obiecti → potentia distincta, nisi differentia ad modum, statum, habitum, actum vel finem reduci possit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A1","CH_GGVC_P_71:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FGS-B02","text":"Corpus unitum → vegetativum; res exterior intentionaliter recepta → sensitivum vel intellectivum; res ut bonum → appetitivum; res ut terminus executionis → motivum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A7","CH_GGVC_P_70:A8","CH_GGVC_P_70:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FGS-B03","text":"Immutatio sensibilis → receptio intentionalis → integratio et retentio → intentio particularis → phantasma aptum operationi intellectivae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A13","CH_GGVC_P_70:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FGS-B04","text":"Forma materialis potentia intelligibilis → abstractio agentis → species actu intelligibilis → receptio possibilis → intelligibile habituale.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A7","CH_GGVC_P_71:A8","CH_GGVC_P_71:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FGS-B05","text":"Species habitualiter retenta → consideratio actualis → discursus rationalis → resolutio iudicialis ad principia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A14","CH_GGVC_P_71:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FGS-B06","text":"Receptio formarum alienarum → bonum apprehensum → appetitus proportionatus apprehensioni → divisio sensitiva et intellectiva.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:A3","CH_GGVC_P_72:A5","CH_GGVC_P_72:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FGS-B07","text":"Ratio universalis boni → appetitus superior → particularizatio → appetitus inferior → potentia exsecutiva → motus exterior, servata distinctione cognitionis, inclinationis et executionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:A11","CH_GGVC_P_70:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AFM-B01","text":"Enumerationes discrepantes → distinctio rationum divisionis → obiectum formale → sufficientia oeconomica → ordo graduatus potentiarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_70:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AFM-B02","text":"Corpus vivum → receptio sensibilis → conservatio absentis → phantasma → abstractio → intellectus possibilis habitualiter perfectus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A16","CH_GGVC_P_71:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AFM-B03","text":"Intellectus ut potentia finita → principium agens et possibile → participatio luminis → individuatio personalis → reductio nominum ad habitus, actus et officia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AFM-B04","text":"Veritas apprehensa → ratio boni → appetitus intellectivus → mediatio particularis et sensitiva → exsecutio corporalis → clausura operationalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A16","CH_GGVC_P_72:A8","CH_GGVC_P_72:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MME-01","text":"Ager facultatum ab anatomia organorum ad relationes formales inter obiectum, actum et principium proximum reordinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_72:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MME-02","text":"Passivitas a detrimento materiali in capacitatem perfectivam recipiendi formam, intelligibile vel motionem appetibilem convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_72:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MME-03","text":"Universalitas obiecti ab unitate numerica subiecti separatur, unde veritas communis et appetitus sub ratione universali cum pluralitate personarum componuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_72:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MME-04","text":"Oppositio inter superius et inferius in architecturam mediationis mutatur: superius formaliter ordinat, inferius condiciones receptionis vel executionis praebet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX6","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MME-05","text":"Lexicon mentis reclassificatur: memoria, ratio, intelligentia, superioritas, inferioritas, speculatio et praxis fiunt modi vel officia potentiarum, non facultates automatice distinctae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MME-06","text":"Cognitio, normativitas, appetitus et motus componuntur in unam machinam distributam sine facultate morali separata aut exsecutione directe universali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei, systema potentias vegetativas et sensitivas enumerat, intellectum in agentem et possibilem distinguit, atque appetitum in sensitivum et intellectivum dividit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:D1","CH_GGVC_P_71:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_72:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali, obiectum formale, irreducibilitas operationis, receptio, retentio, abstractio et mediatio exsecutiva totam distributionem regunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_71:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico, quaestiones de numero et identitate facultatum in quaestiones de criterio distinctionis, gradu causalitatis et modo mediationis transferuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_72:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali, subiectum est ens finitum, personale et participatum quod formas alienas successive recipit, retinet, universalizat et ad bona absentia tendit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_72:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo, textus suam thesim efficit dum vocabula multiplicata distinguit, ad principia minora reducit et per transitus ascendentes atque descendentes reconnectit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_71:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FL-01","text":"Relatio inter distinctionem per obiecta formalia et distinctionem per operationes irreducibiles onus maximum sustinet, sed nexus earum non plene formalizatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FL-02","text":"Intellectus sine organo corporeo operatur, sed actus eius ordinarius phantasmatibus et dispositione virium sensitivarum dependet; independentia formalis et dependentia operationalis non facile coincidunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A6","CH_GGVC_P_71:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FL-03","text":"Singularitas rei extra animam et universalitas rationis appetendi commissuram fragilem constituunt, quia idem appetibile inter terminum singularem et formam universalem distribuitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FL-04","text":"Appetitus superior finaliter et formaliter ordinat, inferior autem motum exsequibilem mediat; modus exactus huius causalitatis inter gradus indeterminatus manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MP-01","text":"Passivitas mutatur ab alteratione sensibili ad receptionem intellectivam perfectivam, deinde ad mobilitatem appetitus coram bono apprehenso.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A13","CH_GGVC_P_71:A6","CH_GGVC_P_72:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MP-02","text":"Memoria mutatur a retentione sensitiva formarum et intentionum in statum habitualem specierum intellectivarum, amisso praeterito ut obiecto proprio.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A17","CH_GGVC_P_71:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MP-03","text":"Appetitus mutatur ab inclinatione communi formae ad potentiam animalem consequentem apprehensionem, deinde in ordines sensitivum et intellectivum dividitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:A1","CH_GGVC_P_72:A5","CH_GGVC_P_72:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MP-04","text":"Relatio superioris et inferioris mutatur ab hierarchia obiecti universalioris in circuitum causalitatis descendens, ubi superius sine inferiori ad executionem non sufficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX6","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:HSI-01","text":"Imago occulta est machina graduata transductionis: corpus recipit, sensus intentionalizat, imaginatio retinet, intellectus universalizat, appetitus ordinat et potentia motiva exsequitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_71:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum intra machinam non est centrum clausum, sed principium personale permeabile formis alienis, lumine participato et bonis apprehensis, quod tamen proprios actus retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_71:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:HSI-03","text":"Tempus huius machinae est retentio et anticipatio: praeteritum habitualiter conservatur, absens praesentatur, et bonum nondum possessum motum futurum elicit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_71:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis consistit in ascensu a particulari ad universale et reditu universalis ad particulare, sine confusione graduum aut executione immediata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A15","CH_GGVC_P_72:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata praesentia sufficiunt ad restituendam taxonomiam fundamentalem, ascensum a sensu ad intellectum et descensum ab apprehensione universali ad appetitum atque executionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_71:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_72:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est criterium rationis formalis obiecti coniunctum principio sufficientiae, quia idem criterium multiplicationem facultatum et reductionem vocabulorum moderatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_71:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent relatio inter obiectum et operationem irreducibilem, dependentia intellectus a phantasmatibus, atque causalitas qua appetitus superior inferiorem movet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_71:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_72:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat: discrepantia taxonomica → distinctio formalis → ascensus receptivus → universalitas intellectiva → bonum apprehensum → descensus mediatus ad actum; reductioni resistit modus exactus commissurarum inter gradus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_71:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_72:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T172453192944Z_3bab9d06","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 79–81","source_sha256":"c6a685a6440807ccfa07f26d1557378668eecf930b959d8456f1a7e9db27d58b","sha256":"febed3421191277208525e91c8f22a077856656061dcde365e0f98cf328c46ea","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:24:53.193900+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-01","text":"The system asks by what criterion vegetative, sensitive, intellective, and appetitive operations are distributed into distinct, non-redundant, and ordered powers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_71:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_72:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-02","text":"The global thesis establishes that a power is distinguished by the formal ratio of its object and the irreducibility of its operation, whereas an organ, name, state, or material identity of a thing does not of itself constitute a distinction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A2","CH_GGVC_P_71:A3","CH_GGVC_P_72:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-03","text":"The argument is a taxonomic reconstruction that simultaneously generates and constrains the multiplication of powers, reducing rival doctrines to common criteria of object, act, and power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_71:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-04","text":"The ascending movement proceeds from bodily operations through sensitive reception and phantasms to abstraction, intellective reception, and the universality of the object.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_71:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-05","text":"The descending movement converts the intelligible universal into an appetible good, then terminates in bodily motion through particular apprehension, lower appetite, and executive power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_72:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-06","text":"The movement of the whole is spiral: the higher level emerges from the lower without absorbing it, then returns to the lower so that universality may acquire particular efficacy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX6","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-07","text":"The closure is operational rather than substantial, because cognition, appetite, and execution remain distinct yet constitute one process of animal action.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A10","CH_GGVC_P_72:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AS-08","text":"The system restores a sufficient order of powers, but the exact nexus between the criteria of object and operation, and between higher and lower appetite, is not fully formalized.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_72:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] A power is the proximate principle of operation, and a new power is admitted where an act necessary to it cannot be reduced to an already posited principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A1","CH_GGVC_P_70:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] The formal ratio of the object, not the thing materially taken, the organ, or the name of the faculty, is the center of the distinction of powers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A3","CH_GGVC_P_72:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] The multiplication of powers is governed by economic sufficiency: irreducibility of principle distinguishes, while an accidental difference of act or term does not distinguish.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_71:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The ascent of powers increases the universality of the object and diminishes formal dependence on material alteration, although higher levels continue to use lower conditions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_71:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Every created principle passes from potency to act through something in act, nor is a finite intellect the actual possession of all intelligibles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A5","CH_GGVC_P_71:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Cognition receives the forms of other things intentionally, not their very real possession, and by this reception exceeds the natural determination of the knower.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A8","CH_GGVC_P_72:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Cognition and appetite are distinguished because a thing is known under the ratio of being or truth, but is desired under the ratio of good or suitability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_72:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Passivity is transferred from the sensible reception of form to the perfective reception of intellect and the mobility of appetite, without reduction to material corruption.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A13","CH_GGVC_P_71:A6","CH_GGVC_P_72:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Human intellect is doubled into an active principle, which makes the intelligible actual, and a possible principle, which receives the intelligible in act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_71:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Proper cognitive action requires an intrinsic principle inhering in the subject, although intellective power participates in a higher universal cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A9","CH_GGVC_P_71:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Animal appetite follows apprehension and is divided according to the genus of sensible or intelligible that moves it passively.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A9","CH_GGVC_P_72:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Universal exterior efficacy requires a particular order, lower appetite, and bodily executive power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A10","CH_GGVC_P_72:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-13","text":"[Persistent] A higher power does not abolish a lower one; the human difference rationally elevates common powers and orders them according to higher ends.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Human cognition is completed as a temporal process from phantasm through abstraction, habit, and judgment to the apprehended good and mediated action.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_72:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-01","text":"The criterion of formal ratio preserves a stable identity: first it orders the genera of powers, then contracts intellective names, and finally distinguishes cognition from appetite and the two appetites.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A3","CH_GGVC_P_71:A3","CH_GGVC_P_72:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-02","text":"The principle of sufficiency changes function: in sense it multiplies irreducible faculties, in intellect it reduces several terms to two powers, and in appetite it restores a twofold division from the genus of apprehension.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_71:D7","CH_GGVC_P_72:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-03","text":"Receptivity returns with variation: sense receives an intentional form, possible intellect a perfective act, and appetite a motion from the apprehended good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A13","CH_GGVC_P_71:A6","CH_GGVC_P_72:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-04","text":"Memory changes from a sensitive power retaining past intentions into the habitual preservation of species within possible intellect itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A17","CH_GGVC_P_71:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_71:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-05","text":"Appetite, initially a genus common to every sentient being, is amplified into a sensitive and an intellective order, whose distinction depends on antecedent apprehension.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A9","CH_GGVC_P_72:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-06","text":"Universality migrates from the object of intellect to the ratio of desiring, but acquires no executive force unless it descends to the singular through the lower order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A4","CH_GGVC_P_72:A10","CH_GGVC_P_72:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-07","text":"The organ is excluded from taxonomic origin and transferred into an instrumental condition: sense uses an organ, intellect depends on phantasms, but a power is not counted from the organ.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A4","CH_GGVC_P_71:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RMD-08","text":"Synderesis and conscience locally complete the intellective taxonomy, but do not return in the division of appetite as new faculties; their force is absorbed into habit, application, and ordering of the act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_72:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:CGD-01","text":"Enumerations of three vegetative powers, five exterior senses, and four interior senses remain local structures; at the present level, their principle of distinction, not their individual numbers, ascends.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:D1","CH_GGVC_P_70:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:CGD-02","text":"The particular modes of alteration that constitute the five exterior senses belong to the sensitive stratum and do not provide the measure of the whole intellective and appetitive order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:CGD-03","text":"The identification of higher intellect with God remains a local theological determination; upward, only the structure of participation in universal causality and intrinsic power is amplified.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:D4","CH_GGVC_P_71:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:CGD-04","text":"The criterion of the formal object is amplified globally, because it simultaneously governs sensitive taxonomy, the compression of intellective names, and the division of appetites.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A3","CH_GGVC_P_71:A3","CH_GGVC_P_72:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:CGD-05","text":"The interior senses transcend the status of local enumeration when the phantasms preserved by them become the material conditions of intellective abstraction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:D13","CH_GGVC_P_71:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:CGD-06","text":"Intellective universality joins the units with appetite, because the same subject that universally receives being and truth can desire the singular under the universal ratio of good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A4","CH_GGVC_P_72:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:CGD-07","text":"The immediate correspondence between a psychological name and a real power becomes obsolete; terms are recodified according to power, habit, act, office, and end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GNX-01","text":"No absolute enumeration of powers is valid without a ratio of division that distinguishes numbers of souls, modes of living, genera, and acts.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GNX-02","text":"Anatomy, organ, and natural alteration constitute neither the formal principle of a power nor a sufficient explanation of sensation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GNX-03","text":"Created intellect can be neither the essence of the soul nor the pure act of all intelligibles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GNX-04","text":"Directly participated separate forms, a sufficient extrinsic agent intellect, and one numerically single intellect common to all human beings are excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GNX-05","text":"A distinction of acts or plurality of terms without irreducibility of principles does not effect a legitimate multiplication of faculties.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX7","CH_GGVC_P_71:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GNX-06","text":"Animal appetite is neither reduced univocally to natural inclination nor confused with cognition on account of the material identity of the object.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_72:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:GNX-07","text":"The intelligible universal does not pass immediately into bodily execution; the particular, lower appetite, and executive disposition cannot be eliminated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:A11","CH_GGVC_P_72:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that the object of a power is defined from the exterior thing alone materially, because otherwise the entire differentiation of cognition and appetite would collapse.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_72:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that every diversity of operation or name constitutes a real faculty, otherwise taxonomic sufficiency would dissolve into indefinite proliferation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX7","CH_GGVC_P_71:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that a higher level abolishes a lower one, because abstraction, intellective appetite, and exterior motion rest upon enduring sensitive conditions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX6","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that passivity signifies corruption or simple inferiority, because the reception of act is the perfection of sense, intellect, and mobile appetite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_71:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the universality of cognition requires a transpersonal subject or that higher causality removes intrinsic agency.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSA-01","text":"An impossible possibility is a finite human intellect that is simultaneously pure act, the first cause of its own light, and the actual possession of all intelligibles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSA-02","text":"The system is ordered around the irreducible interval between intentional likeness and real possession of the object; this interval generates appetite and motion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSA-03","text":"Ordinary human cognition without sensibles, phantasms, and succession, like execution without the descent of the universal to the particular, remains ontologically unavailable.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_72:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSA-04","text":"A single faculty, a single partition, or an immediately ontological lexicon is excluded; the unity of the system is an order of relations among powers, habits, acts, and ends.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-01","text":"The same thing can move several powers without material duplication of the object, because sense, intellect, and appetite attain it under different formal ratios.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-01","CL_GGVC_27:GA-02","CL_GGVC_27:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_70:A3","CH_GGVC_P_71:A3","CH_GGVC_P_72:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-02","text":"Cognitive ascent is an ordered transduction in which intentional sensitive reception provides phantastic material, agent intellect abstracts, and possible intellect receives the intelligible in act.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-04","CL_GGVC_27:GA-06","CL_GGVC_27:GA-08","CL_GGVC_27:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_70:A13","CH_GGVC_P_71:A7","CH_GGVC_P_71:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-03","text":"The reception of alien forms requires appetite because cognition presents the object intentionally, but does not confer it as a real good to be possessed.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-06","CL_GGVC_27:GA-07","CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_72:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-04","text":"The doubling of intellect and the doubling of appetite follow different ratios: intellect is divided according to making and receiving the intelligible, appetite according to the genus of moving apprehension.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-02","CL_GGVC_27:GA-09","CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_71:A12","CH_GGVC_P_72:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-05","text":"The universality of cognition and appetite does not remove the plurality of personal subjects, because individual intellects receive the universal and desire singulars under a universal ratio.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-09","CL_GGVC_27:GA-10","CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_71:A13","CH_GGVC_P_72:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-06","text":"Deliberate action requires a distributed chain: truth ordered to work becomes the universal ratio of good, which directs executive power through particular apprehension and lower appetite.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-07","CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","CL_GGVC_27:GA-12","CL_GGVC_27:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_71:A16","CH_GGVC_P_72:A11","CH_GGVC_P_70:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-07","text":"The economy of powers produces an ordered asymmetry: several senses because of irreducible modes of reception, two correlative intellective powers, and two appetites according to moving apprehension.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-01","CL_GGVC_27:GA-02","CL_GGVC_27:GA-03","CH_GGVC_P_70:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_71:D7","CH_GGVC_P_72:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:XD-08","text":"Human rationality simultaneously elevates and requires lower powers: the cogitative prepares phantasms, intellect elicits the universal, while higher appetite acquires bodily efficacy through the lower.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-04","CL_GGVC_27:GA-12","CL_GGVC_27:GA-13","CH_GGVC_P_70:A18","CH_GGVC_P_71:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSD-01","text":"The soul appears as an architecture of transduction that converts material alteration into intentional presence, this into intellective universality, and universality into appetite and motion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-04","CL_GGVC_27:GA-06","CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","CL_GGVC_27:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_70:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_71:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSD-02","text":"The human subject is simultaneously personal and capable of universality: individuation of power constitutes the condition of its proper act, not a limit that destroys common truth.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-09","CL_GGVC_27:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_71:A13","CH_GGVC_P_71:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSD-03","text":"The causality of human operation is distributed among higher light, intrinsic power, apprehended object, moved appetite, and bodily instrument; no part alone produces the integral act.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-05","CL_GGVC_27:GA-10","CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","CL_GGVC_27:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_71:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSD-04","text":"Psychic time is generated from intentionally presented absence, retention of the past, successive discourse, and orientation toward a good not yet acquired.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-06","CL_GGVC_27:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_71:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_72:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:BSD-05","text":"Moral order does not require a separate faculty: habitual practical principles, the application of conscience, intellective appetite, and lower mediation distribute one normative process.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-03","CL_GGVC_27:GA-11","CL_GGVC_27:GA-12","CL_GGVC_27:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_71:A17","CH_GGVC_P_71:A18","CH_GGVC_P_72:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RLE-01","text":"The taxonomic circuit proceeds from act to proximate principle, from principle to formal object, then from object to the proof whether a distinct power is necessary.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A2","CH_GGVC_P_70:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RLE-02","text":"The path of cognitive amplification is sensible presence or absence → phantasm → abstraction → intelligible species → habit → consideration → discourse.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_71:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RLE-03","text":"The path of descending efficacy is understood universal → ratio of good → intellective appetite → apprehended particular → lower appetite → bodily execution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:A11","CH_GGVC_P_70:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RLE-04","text":"The formal ratio of the object is the attractor of the whole structure, because disputes concerning number, term, universality, identity of the thing, and division of appetite return to it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A3","CH_GGVC_P_72:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RLE-05","text":"Absence is a dynamic attractor: a non-present thing becomes intentionally present through memory or intellect, but real distance preserves appetite and motion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A16","CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_72:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:RLE-06","text":"Participation constitutes a non-occasionalist circuit: a higher cause confers light, intrinsic power abstracts, the subject receives, and the proper act formally inheres in that same subject.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A11","CH_GGVC_P_71:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FGS-B01","text":"Irreducible vital operation → proximate principle → formal ratio of object → distinct power, unless the difference can be reduced to mode, state, habit, act, or end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A1","CH_GGVC_P_71:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FGS-B02","text":"United body → vegetative; exterior thing intentionally received → sensitive or intellective; thing as good → appetitive; thing as terminus of execution → motive.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A7","CH_GGVC_P_70:A8","CH_GGVC_P_70:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FGS-B03","text":"Sensible alteration → intentional reception → integration and retention → particular intention → phantasm suited to intellective operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A13","CH_GGVC_P_70:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FGS-B04","text":"Material form potentially intelligible → abstraction by the agent → actually intelligible species → possible reception → habitual intelligible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A7","CH_GGVC_P_71:A8","CH_GGVC_P_71:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FGS-B05","text":"Habitually retained species → actual consideration → rational discourse → judicial resolution to principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A14","CH_GGVC_P_71:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FGS-B06","text":"Reception of alien forms → apprehended good → appetite proportioned to apprehension → sensitive and intellective division.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:A3","CH_GGVC_P_72:A5","CH_GGVC_P_72:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FGS-B07","text":"Universal ratio of good → higher appetite → particularization → lower appetite → executive power → exterior motion, with the distinction of cognition, inclination, and execution preserved.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:A11","CH_GGVC_P_70:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AFM-B01","text":"Discrepant enumerations → distinction of ratios of division → formal object → economic sufficiency → graduated order of powers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_70:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AFM-B02","text":"Living body → sensitive reception → preservation of the absent → phantasm → abstraction → possible intellect habitually perfected.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A16","CH_GGVC_P_71:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AFM-B03","text":"Intellect as finite power → agent and possible principles → participation in light → personal individuation → reduction of names to habits, acts, and offices.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:AFM-B04","text":"Apprehended truth → ratio of good → intellective appetite → particular and sensitive mediation → bodily execution → operational closure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A16","CH_GGVC_P_72:A8","CH_GGVC_P_72:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MME-01","text":"The field of faculties is reordered from the anatomy of organs to formal relations among object, act, and proximate principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_72:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MME-02","text":"Passivity is converted from material detriment into the perfective capacity to receive form, intelligible, or appetible motion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_72:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MME-03","text":"The universality of the object is separated from the numerical unity of the subject, whence common truth and appetite under a universal ratio are combined with a plurality of persons.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_72:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MME-04","text":"The opposition between higher and lower is transformed into an architecture of mediation: the higher formally orders, the lower provides conditions of reception or execution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX6","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MME-05","text":"The lexicon of mind is reclassified: memory, reason, intelligence, superiority, inferiority, speculation, and practice become modes or offices of powers, not automatically distinct faculties.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_71:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MME-06","text":"Cognition, normativity, appetite, and motion are composed into one distributed machine without a separate moral faculty or directly universal execution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of surface, the system enumerates vegetative and sensitive powers, distinguishes intellect into agent and possible, and divides appetite into sensitive and intellective.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:D1","CH_GGVC_P_71:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_72:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, formal object, irreducibility of operation, reception, retention, abstraction, and executive mediation govern the entire distribution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_71:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, questions concerning the number and identity of faculties are transferred into questions concerning the criterion of distinction, degree of causality, and mode of mediation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_72:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, the subject is a finite, personal, and participated being that successively receives, retains, universalizes alien forms, and tends toward absent goods.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_72:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the text enacts its thesis while distinguishing multiplied terms, reducing them to fewer principles, and reconnecting them through ascending and descending transitions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_71:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FL-01","text":"The relation between distinction through formal objects and distinction through irreducible operations bears the greatest burden, but their nexus is not fully formalized.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FL-02","text":"Intellect operates without a bodily organ, but its ordinary act depends on phantasms and the disposition of sensitive powers; formal independence and operational dependence do not easily coincide.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A6","CH_GGVC_P_71:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FL-03","text":"The singularity of the thing outside the soul and the universality of the ratio of desiring constitute a fragile joint, because the same appetible is distributed between a singular terminus and a universal form.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:FL-04","text":"Higher appetite orders finally and formally, whereas lower appetite mediates executable motion; the exact mode of this causality between levels remains indeterminate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MP-01","text":"Passivity changes from sensible alteration to perfective intellective reception, then to the mobility of appetite before the apprehended good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A13","CH_GGVC_P_71:A6","CH_GGVC_P_72:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MP-02","text":"Memory changes from sensitive retention of forms and intentions into the habitual state of intellective species, with the past lost as its proper object.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:A17","CH_GGVC_P_71:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MP-03","text":"Appetite changes from the common inclination of form into an animal power consequent upon apprehension, then is divided into sensitive and intellective orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:A1","CH_GGVC_P_72:A5","CH_GGVC_P_72:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MP-04","text":"The relation of higher and lower changes from a hierarchy of a more universal object into a descending circuit of causality, in which the higher without the lower does not suffice for execution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SNX6","CH_GGVC_P_72:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:HSI-01","text":"The hidden image is a graduated machine of transduction: the body receives, sense intentionalizes, imagination retains, intellect universalizes, appetite orders, and motive power executes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_71:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:HSI-02","text":"The subject within the machine is not a closed center, but a personal principle permeable to alien forms, participated light, and apprehended goods, which nevertheless retains its proper acts.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_72:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_71:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:HSI-03","text":"The time of this machine is retention and anticipation: the past is habitually preserved, the absent is presented, and the good not yet possessed elicits future motion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_71:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation consists in ascent from the particular to the universal and the return of the universal to the particular, without confusion of levels or immediate execution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_71:A15","CH_GGVC_P_72:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to restore the fundamental taxonomy, the ascent from sense to intellect, and the descent from universal apprehension to appetite and execution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_71:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_72:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the criterion of the formal ratio of the object joined to the principle of sufficiency, because the same criterion moderates both the multiplication of faculties and the reduction of terms.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_71:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B03","text":"The relation between object and irreducible operation, the dependence of intellect on phantasms, and the causality by which higher appetite moves the lower remain less determinate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_71:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_72:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement: taxonomic discrepancy → formal distinction → receptive ascent → intellective universality → apprehended good → mediated descent to act; the exact mode of the joints between levels resists reduction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_70:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_71:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_72:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_27@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_27:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 79–81","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 78 · Prima pars · Quaestio 79 · Prima pars · Quaestio 80"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 82–84","order":28,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_73","CH_GGVC_P_74","CH_GGVC_P_75"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_28@R001","sha256":"bbd6d15e956c43b4d5c47cc405930a322f8c243bdedcee1a572690cddd41e6f1","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo appetitus a cognitione distinctus, passionibus affectus et a causa prima motus actum humanum voluntarium constituere possit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_74:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_75:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-02","text":"Thesis globalis libertatem constituit non per remotionem inclinationis, necessitatis aut dependentiae causalis, sed per earum ordinationem intra mediationem rationalem et appetitivam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_74:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_75:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-03","text":"Argumentatio distinctionibus actus, obiecti, finis, causae et respectus falsas oppositiones inter cognitionem et appetitum, necessitatem et libertatem, motionem et voluntarium dissolvit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_74:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-04","text":"Motus ascendit a regimine politico appetitus sensitivi ad iudicium rationalis voluntatis, deinde ad compositionem causalitatis divinae cum causalitate libera creata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_75:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_75:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-05","text":"Progressio est recursiva et mutatione acta: quod in gradu inferiori inclinatio resistibilis est, in gradu voluntatis necessitas finalis fit, atque in electione campus contingentium mediorum aperitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_74:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_75:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-06","text":"Unitas systematis non ex unica potentia vel causalitate oritur, sed ex coordinatione asymmetrica virium quarum causalitas propria a superiori ordine servatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_75:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-07","text":"Libertas terminaliter apparet ut modus causae appetitivae rationaliter mediatae: neque coacta, neque sui ipsius prima causa, neque ab ordine boni soluta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A4","CH_GGVC_P_75:A12","CH_GGVC_P_75:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-08","text":"Clausura est architectonica potius quam explicativa: unitas voluntatis et liberi arbitrii stabilitur, sed modus exactus quo motus divinus electionem propriam efficit nondum plene determinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Cognitio per praesentiam obiecti in cognoscente perficitur, appetitus autem per inclinationem appetentis in obiectum; actus igitur formaliter irreducibiles sunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A2","CH_GGVC_P_73:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Omnis appetitus humanus apprehensionem praesupponit, sed apprehensio sola neque inclinationem neque electionem consummat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A4","CH_GGVC_P_74:A18","CH_GGVC_P_75:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Diversitas relationis ad bonum, impedimentum et finem actus appetitivos distinguit, sed potentias tantum ubi obiecti ratio formalis id postulat multiplicat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_74:A16","CH_GGVC_P_75:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Appetitus sensitivus causalitatem propriam retinet atque rationi subicitur modo politico, unde eius gubernabilitas cum resistentia componitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_75:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Voluntas, quia bonum universale respicit, una manet sub diversis actibus et actus potentiarum particularium imperare potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A8","CH_GGVC_P_74:A15","CH_GGVC_P_74:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Beatitudo est finis naturaliter et necessario appetitus qui electionem non recipit, sed totum campum mediorum eligibilium ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A6","CH_GGVC_P_74:A7","CH_GGVC_P_75:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Necessitas voluntario non simpliciter opponitur: coactio inclinationem destruit, dum necessitas naturalis et finalis ordinem intrinsecum voluntatis constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A2","CH_GGVC_P_74:A3","CH_GGVC_P_74:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Electio ex consilio rationis et acceptatione appetitiva constituitur atque, propter bonum utile quod respicit, principaliter actus appetitivus est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A8","CH_GGVC_P_75:A9","CH_GGVC_P_75:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Ratio practica, quia particularia contingentia conferre potest, iudicium ad opposita aperit et instinctivam determinationem ad unum superat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A3","CH_GGVC_P_75:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Intellectus et voluntas se mutuo movent causalitate asymmetrica: intellectus per finem, voluntas per modum agentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A14","CH_GGVC_P_74:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Liberum arbitrium non est facultas voluntati addita, sed eadem potentia intellectiva appetitiva prout media propter finem eligit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_75:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_75:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Dominium electionis a dominio executionis distinguitur; voluntas actum internum retinere potest etiam ubi effectus exterior impeditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A11","CH_GGVC_P_75:A15","CH_GGVC_P_75:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Causa prima voluntatem ita movet ut causalitas secunda suum modum voluntarium non amittat sed ex ipsa motione superiori accipiat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A13","CH_GGVC_P_75:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-01","text":"Distinctio cognitionis et appetitus identitatem stabilem retinet: praesentia obiecti et inclinatio in obiectum per omnes unitates diversa principia actus manent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A2","CH_GGVC_P_73:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-02","text":"Appetitus a sensualitate ad voluntatem migrat: primo inclinationem singularem sensitivam significat, deinde capacitatem universalem boni quae actus inferiores ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A5","CH_GGVC_P_74:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-03","text":"Divisio concupiscibilis et irascibilis vim suam in appetitu sensitivo servat, sed in voluntate absorbetur in distinctionem actuum finis et mediorum sine multiplicatione potentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_74:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_75:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-04","text":"Mediatio particularis mutatur a cogitativa rationi dirigibili in iudicium practicum quod contingentia confert et alternativas aperit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A10","CH_GGVC_P_75:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-05","text":"Obedientia passionum functionem mutat: ex aptitudine appetitus inferioris ad regimen fit condicio qua inclinationes naturales, corporales et habituales libertatem non abolent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_75:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-06","text":"Necessitas a conservationis exigentia ad distinctionem modalem transfertur, qua necessitas finalis libertatem fundare potest dum coactio eam destruit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A7","CH_GGVC_P_74:A2","CH_GGVC_P_74:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-07","text":"Interpositio voluntatis inter appetitum inferiorem et motum corporis terminaliter in distinctionem electionis ab executione transformatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:D6","CH_GGVC_P_75:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-08","text":"Principium superius motus ab antecedentia primi intelligibilis ad causalitatem primam voluntarium ipsum producentem amplificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A18","CH_GGVC_P_75:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-01","text":"Definitio sensualitatis ut nominis appetitus sensitivi localis manet; ad gradum praesentem ascendit tantum distinctio universalis inter apprehendere et inclinari.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A5","CH_GGVC_P_73:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-02","text":"Interpretatio serpentis et limitatio imperii super sensus exteriores machinae localis partes sunt neque architecturam voluntatis et electionis per se sustinent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_73:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-03","text":"Distinctio ardui a bono simpliciter appetibili sursum amplificatur ut exemplar relationis secundum quam obiecta actus distinguunt, sed ad voluntatem non transfertur ut divisio potentiarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A9","CH_GGVC_P_74:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-04","text":"Triplex distinctio necessitatis unitatem voluntatis cum libero arbitrio coniungit et oppositionem inferiorem inter inclinationem et libertatem reordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A2","CH_GGVC_P_74:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-05","text":"Visio divina manet transitus specialis status epistemici, sed praesentem structuram amplificat ostendendo cognitum nexum posse modalitatem voluntatis mutare.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A11","CH_GGVC_P_74:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-06","text":"Comparatio relativa intellectus et voluntatis non est finis localis, sed pons ad doctrinam causalitatis reciprocae sine hierarchia univoca.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_74:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-07","text":"Quaestio utrum liberum arbitrium sit actus, habitus vel potentia terminaliter solvitur per translationem nominis actus ad potentiam electivam permanentem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_75:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-08","text":"Lexicon passionis, irae et concupiscentiae localiter ambiguum re-codificatur secundum distinctionem inter passionem sensitivam et affectum simplicem voluntatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A17","CH_GGVC_P_74:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-01","text":"Sensualitas neque sensus corporis neque vis cognitiva est; apprehensio est eius praevia condicio, non ipsa inclinatio appetitiva.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_73:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-02","text":"Regimen rationis non est dominatio despotica quae causalitatem, motum aut resistentiam appetitus inferioris excludat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_73:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-03","text":"Voluntarium non excludit omnem necessitatem; sola necessitas contra inclinationem imposita libertati formaliter repugnat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A3","CH_GGVC_P_74:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-04","text":"Ultimus finis neque eligitur neque dominio actuum subicitur, quamvis omnia media eligibilia ordinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_75:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-05","text":"Electio neque potentia pure cognitiva neque habitus est, quia iudicium appetitiva acceptatione compleri et a principio stabili elici debet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_75:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-06","text":"Libertas non est potestas infallibilis effectuum externorum; impeditio executionis dominium electionis interne servatum non tollit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_75:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-07","text":"Causalitas divina et causalitas creata non sunt causae rivales quarum una tantum agere possit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_75:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-08","text":"Voluntas in irascibilem et concupiscibilem non dividitur, quia bonum universale eius non multiplicatur per differentias bonorum particularium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_74:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet libertatem aseitatem causalem requirere, quia causa libera potest a superiori causa moveri sine amissione modi proprii.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A12","CH_GGVC_P_75:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:DBN-02","text":"Falsa esse debet reductio ordinis animae ad unicam hierarchiam vel unicum genus causalitatis, quia eminentia, finis et actio secundum diversos respectus distribuuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_74:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:DBN-03","text":"Falsus esse debet transitus immediatus ab inclinatione ad necessitatem, quia inclinatio sub iudicio rationis manere potest sine determinatione ad unum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A14","CH_GGVC_P_75:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet bonum particulare capacitatem voluntatis exhaurire posse, quia universalitas boni campum pluralium rationum servat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_74:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet subiectum esse principium absolutum sui finis, primae cognitionis et primi motus; libertas intra principia non electa exercetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_75:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse debet libertatem ex successu exteriori mensurari, quia dominium internum electionis sine dominio eventus consistere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_75:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSA-01","text":"Systema circum absentiam subiecti sui ipsius originarii ordinatur: agens liberum neque finem ultimum sibi dat neque primam causalitatem possidet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_75:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSA-02","text":"Impossibilis est libertas extra rationem boni, finis, apprehensionis et causalitatis; indeterminatio absoluta actum eligibilem potius dissolveret quam liberaret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_75:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSA-03","text":"Indisponibilis est dominium absolutum sive passionum sive eventuum: ratio gubernat vim resistibilem, iarum vero electio effectum externum non necessario obtinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_75:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSA-04","text":"Ontologice inadmissibilis est summa-zero causalitatis, qua actio causae primae tantum per subtractionem actionis causae secundae augeri possit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A13","CH_GGVC_P_75:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-01","text":"Quia apprehensio et appetitus diversis actibus perficiuntur, ratio alternativa aperit, sed appetitiva acceptatio electionem consummat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-01","CL_GGVC_28:GA-02","CL_GGVC_28:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_73:A2","CH_GGVC_P_75:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-02","text":"Passiones causaliter reales libertatem non destruunt, dummodo earum inclinatio intra iudicium rationale maneat neque illud ad unum determinet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-04","CL_GGVC_28:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_73:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_75:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-03","text":"Eadem voluntas necessario fertur in beatitudinem et libere media eligit, quia diversitas modalitatis ex ordine obiecti, non ex diversitate potentiae, nascitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-05","CL_GGVC_28:GA-06","CL_GGVC_28:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_74:A6","CH_GGVC_P_75:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-04","text":"Consensus voluntatis, qui motum sensitivum ab executione separat, fit fundamentum distinctionis inter libertatem internam electionis et contingentiam efficientiae externae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_73:A11","CH_GGVC_P_75:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-05","text":"Divisio appetitus sensitivi et unitas voluntatis ex eodem principio derivantur: ambitus formalis obiecti, non pluralitas vocabulorum vel actuum, numerum potentiarum determinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-03","CL_GGVC_28:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_73:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_74:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-06","text":"Electio est nodus recursionis intellectus et voluntatis, quia consilium appetitui obiectum proponit, voluntas autem ulteriorem considerationem et actum imperat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-08","CL_GGVC_28:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_74:A14","CH_GGVC_P_75:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-07","text":"Motus divinus non est coactio, si inclinatio voluntatis non destruitur sed ut modus proprius causae secundae a causa prima efficitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-07","CL_GGVC_28:GA-13","CH_GGVC_P_74:A3","CH_GGVC_P_75:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-08","text":"Modalitas voluntatis ab statu cognitionis dependere potest: bonum particulare manet contingens dum nexus cum beatitudine non demonstratur, sed cognita necessitate nexus voluntatem determinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-06","CL_GGVC_28:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_74:A11","CH_GGVC_P_75:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSD-01","text":"Agens humanum non est facultas simplex, sed architectura graduata in qua appetitus sensibilis, ratio practica, voluntas et causa prima causalitates proprias ordinate exercent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_75:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSD-02","text":"Libertas est proprietas itineris causalis per iudicium contingentium, non proprietas obiecti sine causa aut potentiae sine inclinatione.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:D2","CH_GGVC_P_75:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSD-03","text":"Responsabilitas moralis manet ubi iudicium et acceptatio electiva servantur, etiamsi passiones electionem inclinent aut executio impediatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_75:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSD-04","text":"Tempus actionis articulatur in apprehensionem, inclinationem, consilium, acceptationem et executionem; libertas in mediis intervallis et transitibus, non in momento causis vacuo, consistit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A18","CH_GGVC_P_75:A8","CH_GGVC_P_75:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSD-05","text":"Mutatio cognitionis potest mutare necessitatem appetitus sine mutatione rei appetitae, quia certitudo nexus boni cum beatitudine campum contingentiae claudit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_74:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSD-06","text":"Dependentia et motus per seipsum non sunt opposita: causa secunda se movet secundum formam voluntariam quam ordo causae primae non delet sed causaliter sustinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A12","CH_GGVC_P_75:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RLE-01","text":"Bonum concupiscibile → impedimentum → motus irascibilis → superatio vel defensio → restitutio boni concupiscibilis; irascibilis circuitus subordinatus est, non finis parallelus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_73:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RLE-02","text":"Sensus vel imaginatio → motus appetitus sensitivi → possibilis repugnantia → iudicium rationale → consensus vel prohibitio voluntatis → executio vel interruptio.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A12","CH_GGVC_P_73:D7","CH_GGVC_P_73:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RLE-03","text":"Intellectus bonum proponit → voluntas actum considerationis movet → intellectus ulterius confert → voluntas acceptat; regressus sistitur in principio primo apprehensionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A14","CH_GGVC_P_74:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RLE-04","text":"Beatitudo est attractor immobilis qui particularia bona ordinat sine eorum uno voluntatem exhauriente; stabilitas finis pluralitatem viarum generat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A6","CH_GGVC_P_74:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RLE-05","text":"Finis appetitus → comparatio mediorum → consilium → acceptatio → electio → actus propter finem; finis terminus est respectu appetitus et machina respectu electionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A8","CH_GGVC_P_75:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RLE-06","text":"Ignorantia nexus → contingentia adhaesionis → visio certa → necessitas voluntatis; amplificatio epistemica mutationem modalem producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:D3","CH_GGVC_P_74:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RLE-07","text":"Causa prima → causa voluntaria → motus proprius voluntarius; ordo causalitatis claudit systema sine clausura libertatis in autonomiam absolutam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A13","CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B01","text":"Obiectum apprehensum → praesentia cognitiva in apprehendente; obiectum ut bonum → inclinatio appetitiva in rem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A2","CH_GGVC_P_73:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B02","text":"Bonum sensibile → prosecutio concupiscibilis; impedimentum arduum → resistentia irascibilis → conservatio vel recuperatio boni.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A6","CH_GGVC_P_73:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B03","text":"Motus sensibilis proprius + cogitativa particularis dirigibilis + regimen rationis → appetitus inferior politice ordinatus, non causaliter evacuatus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A10","CH_GGVC_P_73:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B04","text":"Bonum universale + beatitudo naturaliter appetita → una voluntas ordinata ad finem necessarium et capax plurium mediorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A8","CH_GGVC_P_74:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B05","text":"Particularia contingentia → collatio rationis → iudicium ad opposita → acceptatio appetitiva → electio libera.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A3","CH_GGVC_P_75:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B06","text":"Electio interna → consensus executivus → motus exterior contingens; impedimentum effectus non retroactive destruit electionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A11","CH_GGVC_P_75:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B07","text":"Motus causae primae → conservatio modi causae voluntariae → actus causae secundae per se motae → libertas dependens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A13","CH_GGVC_P_75:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B08","text":"Distinctio formalium obiecti et causalitatis → compositio necessitatis, inclinationis et contingentiae → libertas ut ordo appetitivus rationaliter mediatus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_75:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AFM-B01","text":"Ambiguitas cognitionis et sensualitatis → distinctio apprehensionis ab inclinatione → divisio appetitus sensitivi → regimen politicum passionum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AF1","CH_GGVC_P_73:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AFM-B02","text":"Necessitas indistincta → separatio coactionis, naturae et finis → beatitudo necessario volita → contingentia bonorum particularium → libertas mediorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A2","CH_GGVC_P_74:A6","CH_GGVC_P_74:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AFM-B03","text":"Particularia contingentia → collatio rationis → consilium → acceptatio appetitiva → electio interna → executio impedibilis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A3","CH_GGVC_P_75:A8","CH_GGVC_P_75:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AFM-B04","text":"Obiectio ex motu divino → distinctio causae primae et secundae → conservatio modi voluntarii → liberum arbitrium ut causalitas dependens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A13","CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MME-01","text":"Necessitas ex contrario simplici libertatis in genus analogum reordinatur, cuius sola species coactiva voluntarium tollit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_74:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MME-02","text":"Potentiae animae non amplius ex nominibus passionum numerantur, sed ex actibus propriis, rationibus formalibus obiectorum et modis causalitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_74:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MME-03","text":"Libertas ab oppositione inter causatum et autonomum transfertur ad differentiam inter motum coactivum, instinctivum et rationaliter voluntarium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_75:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MME-04","text":"Conflictus interior reclassificatur ut effectus pluralitatis principiorum moventium, non ut simplex defectus gubernationis rationalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_75:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MME-05","text":"Causalitas superior non iam causalitatem inferiorem substituit; eius effectus perfectivus est ut causa secunda secundum proprium modum agat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:D6","CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MME-06","text":"Distinctio electionis ab executione separat responsabilitatem internam a potestate eventuum et libertatem moralem ab efficientia externa.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MME-07","text":"Cognitio acquirit vim modalem: non tantum obiectum manifestat, sed per certitudinem nexus determinare potest utrum voluntas contingenter an necessario feratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_74:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei sensualitas, voluntas et liberum arbitrium distinguuntur secundum appetitum sensitivum, appetitum intellectivum et electionem mediorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali obiecta formalia, mediationes cognitivae, modi causalitatis et transitus ad executionem distribuunt actus sine indiscriminata multiplicatione potentiarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico oppositio inter passionem et rationem vel causam et libertatem substituitur ordinatione causarum, respectuum et graduum appetitus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali subiectum est causa secunda cuius actio per successionem apprehensionis, inclinationis, consilii et consensus fit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo systema ipsam libertatem quam definit imitatur: obiectiones non removet, sed per distinctiones in ordinem causalem capacem earum componendarum traducit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_75:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FL-01","text":"Irascibilis ut potentia distincta ponitur, sed eius passiones a concupiscibili oriuntur et ad illud redeunt; autonomia functionalis et subordinatio finalis imperfecte coincidunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_73:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FL-02","text":"Obedientia politica passionum resistentiam servat, sed limes inter inclinationem rationaliter gubernabilem et iudicium iam impeditum non definitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_75:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FL-03","text":"Indifferentia liberi arbitrii ad bene et male cum necessaria ordinatione voluntatis ad beatitudinem componitur per finem et media, sed pravitas mediorum sub bono apparente manet implicita.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_74:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FL-04","text":"Causalitas prima dicitur efficere ipsum modum voluntarium, sed mechanismus quo determinatio divina electionem secundam non absorbeat ulterius non explicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_75:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FL-05","text":"Beatitudo necessario appetitur, Deus autem ante visionem non necessario eligitur; identitas finis realis cum fine cognito mediatione epistemica fragili pendet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_74:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MP-01","text":"Nomen sensualitatis a campo cognitionis ad actum appetitivum mutatur, unde tota posterior architectura inclinationis aperitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_73:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MP-02","text":"Necessitas ab apparente negatione voluntarii in duplicem functionem mutatur: coactio libertatem destruit, natura et finis eam ordinant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_74:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MP-03","text":"Liberum arbitrium ab actu nominato ad potentiam permanentem, deinde a facultate quasi cognitiva ad actum principaliter appetitivum eiusdem voluntatis transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_75:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MP-04","text":"Regimen rationis mutatur a directione cogitativae particularis in ordinem causalitatis universum, ubi etiam causa prima motum voluntarium sustinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A10","CH_GGVC_P_75:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:HSI-01","text":"Realitas psychica imaginatur ut politia nested: sensus et imaginatio movent, ratio dirigit, voluntas imperat, sed nulla potestas inferior in instrumentum iners redigitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_74:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:HSI-02","text":"Beatitudo fungitur velut attractor immobilis vel polus navigationis; directio ultima fixa est, dum itinera particularia pluribus comparationibus patent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A6","CH_GGVC_P_74:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:HSI-03","text":"Subiectum est causa secunda se movens, non souveranum principium sui; eius proprietas est modus voluntarius intra ordinem causalitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_75:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:HSI-04","text":"Tempus actionis est series portarum: apprehensio, passio, iudicium, acceptatio et executio; in singulis transitus ordinari, impediri vel interrumpi potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_75:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:HSI-05","text":"Certamen humanum non est fuga ab inclinatione, sed navigatio inter bonum appetitum, obstacula ardua et media contingentia sub ratione collata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A9","CH_GGVC_P_75:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B01","text":"Distinctio cognitionis ab appetitu, triplex necessitas, finis necessarius, iudicium contingentium, acceptatio appetitiva et causalitas prima sufficiunt ad structuram huius gregis restituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_74:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est duplex mediatio: ratio ordinat inclinationes per iudicium particulare, voluntas autem transitum ab appetitu ad electionem et executionem regit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_74:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent mensura qua passiones iudicium corrumpunt et modus quo auxilium divinum electionem propriam simul determinat atque conservat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B04","text":"Cohaerentia vera est recursiva: appetitus sensitivus non relinquitur, sed in voluntate, electione et causalitate prima successive re-codificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio motum generativum servat, sed reductioni plenae resistunt transitus epistemicus a beatitudine ad Deum et commissura metaphysica inter motionem primam et electionem secundam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T172537403400Z_7ab4eeb4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 82–84","source_sha256":"bbd6d15e956c43b4d5c47cc405930a322f8c243bdedcee1a572690cddd41e6f1","sha256":"742f37ed554c23ecdb33da396f20cf5c7b499f5ddbddd11267faa973e509cf3c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:25:37.404442+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-01","text":"The system asks how appetite, distinct from cognition, affected by passions and moved by the first cause, can constitute a voluntary human act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_74:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_75:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-02","text":"The global thesis establishes freedom not through the removal of inclination, necessity, or causal dependence, but through their ordering within rational and appetitive mediation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_74:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_75:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-03","text":"Through distinctions of act, object, end, cause, and respect, the argument dissolves false oppositions between cognition and appetite, necessity and freedom, motion and the voluntary.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_74:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-04","text":"The movement ascends from the political governance of sensitive appetite to the judgment of rational will, then to the composition of divine causality with free created causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_75:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_75:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-05","text":"The progression is recursive and driven by mutation: what at the lower level is resistible inclination becomes final necessity at the level of the will, and in choice a field of contingent means is opened.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_74:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_75:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-06","text":"The unity of the system arises not from a single power or causality, but from the asymmetric coordination of powers whose proper causality is preserved by a higher order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_75:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-07","text":"Freedom ultimately appears as the mode of an appetitive cause rationally mediated: neither compelled, nor the first cause of itself, nor released from the order of the good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A4","CH_GGVC_P_75:A12","CH_GGVC_P_75:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AS-08","text":"The closure is architectonic rather than explanatory: the unity of will and free choice is established, but the exact mode by which divine motion effects proper choice is not yet fully determined.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Cognition is perfected through the presence of the object in the knower, whereas appetite is perfected through the inclination of the appetent toward the object; the acts are therefore formally irreducible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A2","CH_GGVC_P_73:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Every human appetite presupposes apprehension, but apprehension alone completes neither inclination nor choice.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A4","CH_GGVC_P_74:A18","CH_GGVC_P_75:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Diversity of relation to the good, impediment, and end distinguishes appetitive acts, but multiplies powers only where the formal account of the object requires it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_74:A16","CH_GGVC_P_75:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Sensitive appetite retains its proper causality and is subjected to reason in a political mode; hence its governability is composed with resistance.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_75:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] The will, because it regards the universal good, remains one under diverse acts and can command the acts of particular powers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A8","CH_GGVC_P_74:A15","CH_GGVC_P_74:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Happiness is an end naturally and necessarily desired that does not admit choice, but orders the whole field of eligible means.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A6","CH_GGVC_P_74:A7","CH_GGVC_P_75:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Necessity is not simply opposed to the voluntary: compulsion destroys inclination, whereas natural and final necessity constitute the intrinsic order of the will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A2","CH_GGVC_P_74:A3","CH_GGVC_P_74:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Choice is constituted from the counsel of reason and appetitive acceptance and, on account of the useful good at which it aims, is principally an appetitive act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A8","CH_GGVC_P_75:A9","CH_GGVC_P_75:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Practical reason, because it can compare contingent particulars, opens judgment to opposites and surpasses instinctive determination to one.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A3","CH_GGVC_P_75:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Intellect and will move one another by asymmetric causality: intellect through the end, will through the mode of agent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A14","CH_GGVC_P_74:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Free choice is not a faculty added to the will, but the same intellectual-appetitive power insofar as it chooses means for the sake of the end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_75:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_75:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Dominion over choice is distinguished from dominion over execution; the will can retain the internal act even where the external effect is impeded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A11","CH_GGVC_P_75:A15","CH_GGVC_P_75:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] The first cause moves the will such that secondary causality does not lose its voluntary mode but receives it from that higher motion itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A13","CH_GGVC_P_75:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-01","text":"The distinction between cognition and appetite retains stable identity: the presence of the object and inclination toward the object remain diverse principles of act throughout all units.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A2","CH_GGVC_P_73:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-02","text":"Appetite migrates from sensuality to will: at first it signifies a singular sensitive inclination, then the universal capacity for good that orders lower acts.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A5","CH_GGVC_P_74:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-03","text":"The division of the concupiscible and irascible retains its force in sensitive appetite, but in the will it is absorbed into the distinction between acts concerning the end and means, without multiplication of power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_74:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_75:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-04","text":"Particular mediation changes from the cogitative power, governable by reason, into practical judgment that compares contingents and opens alternatives.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A10","CH_GGVC_P_75:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-05","text":"The obedience of passions changes function: from the aptitude of lower appetite for governance it becomes the condition under which natural, bodily, and habitual inclinations do not abolish freedom.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_75:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-06","text":"Necessity is transferred from the exigency of preservation to a modal distinction by which final necessity can ground freedom while compulsion destroys it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A7","CH_GGVC_P_74:A2","CH_GGVC_P_74:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-07","text":"The interposition of the will between lower appetite and bodily motion is ultimately transformed into the distinction of choice from execution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:D6","CH_GGVC_P_75:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RMD-08","text":"The higher principle of motion is expanded from the priority of the first intelligible to the first causality producing the voluntary itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A18","CH_GGVC_P_75:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-01","text":"The definition of sensuality as the name of sensitive appetite remains local; only the universal distinction between apprehending and being inclined ascends to the present level.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A5","CH_GGVC_P_73:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-02","text":"The interpretation of the serpent and the limitation of command over the external senses are parts of a local mechanism and do not in themselves sustain the architecture of will and choice.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_73:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-03","text":"The distinction of the arduous from the good simply appetible is amplified upward as a model of the relation according to which objects distinguish acts, but is not transferred to the will as a division of powers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A9","CH_GGVC_P_74:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-04","text":"The threefold distinction of necessity joins the unity of the will with free choice and reorders the lower opposition between inclination and freedom.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A2","CH_GGVC_P_74:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-05","text":"Divine vision remains a special transition of epistemic state, but amplifies the present structure by showing that a known connection can change the modality of the will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A11","CH_GGVC_P_74:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-06","text":"The relative comparison of intellect and will is not a local end, but a bridge to the doctrine of reciprocal causality without univocal hierarchy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_74:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-07","text":"The question whether free choice is an act, habit, or power is ultimately resolved by transferring the name of an act to a permanent elective power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_75:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:CGD-08","text":"The locally ambiguous lexicon of passion, anger, and concupiscence is recodified according to the distinction between sensitive passion and the simple affection of the will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A17","CH_GGVC_P_74:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-01","text":"Sensuality is neither bodily sense nor cognitive power; apprehension is its prior condition, not appetitive inclination itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_73:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-02","text":"The governance of reason is not despotic domination that excludes the causality, motion, or resistance of lower appetite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_73:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-03","text":"The voluntary does not exclude every necessity; only necessity imposed contrary to inclination formally conflicts with freedom.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A3","CH_GGVC_P_74:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-04","text":"The ultimate end is neither chosen nor subjected to dominion over acts, although it orders all eligible means.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_75:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-05","text":"Choice is neither a purely cognitive power nor a habit, because judgment must be completed by appetitive acceptance and elicited from a stable principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_75:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-06","text":"Freedom is not infallible power over external effects; impediment of execution does not remove dominion over choice preserved internally.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_75:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-07","text":"Divine causality and created causality are not rival causes of which only one can act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_75:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:GNX-08","text":"The will is not divided into irascible and concupiscible, because its universal good is not multiplied through differences of particular goods.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_74:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that freedom requires causal aseity, because a free cause can be moved by a higher cause without loss of its proper mode.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A12","CH_GGVC_P_75:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:DBN-02","text":"It must be false to reduce the order of the soul to a single hierarchy or a single kind of causality, because eminence, end, and action are distributed according to diverse respects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_74:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:DBN-03","text":"The immediate transition from inclination to necessity must be false, because inclination can remain under the judgment of reason without determination to one.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A14","CH_GGVC_P_75:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that a particular good can exhaust the capacity of the will, because the universality of the good preserves the field of multiple accounts.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_74:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the subject is the absolute principle of its end, first cognition, and first motion; freedom is exercised within unchosen principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_75:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that freedom is measured by external success, because internal dominion over choice can subsist without dominion over outcome.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_75:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSA-01","text":"The system is ordered around the absence of a subject originating itself: the free agent neither gives itself its ultimate end nor possesses first causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_75:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSA-02","text":"Freedom outside the account of good, end, apprehension, and causality is impossible; absolute indetermination would dissolve rather than free an eligible act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_75:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSA-03","text":"Absolute dominion over either passions or events is unavailable: reason governs a resistible power, while choice does not necessarily obtain its external effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_75:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSA-04","text":"A zero-sum causality is ontologically inadmissible, by which the action of the first cause could increase only through the subtraction of the action of the secondary cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A13","CH_GGVC_P_75:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-01","text":"Because apprehension and appetite are perfected by diverse acts, reason opens alternatives, but appetitive acceptance completes choice.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-01","CL_GGVC_28:GA-02","CL_GGVC_28:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_73:A2","CH_GGVC_P_75:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-02","text":"Causally real passions do not destroy freedom, provided that their inclination remains within rational judgment and does not determine it to one.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-04","CL_GGVC_28:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_73:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_75:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-03","text":"The same will is borne necessarily toward happiness and freely chooses means, because diversity of modality arises from the order of the object, not from diversity of power.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-05","CL_GGVC_28:GA-06","CL_GGVC_28:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_74:A6","CH_GGVC_P_75:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-04","text":"The consent of the will, which separates sensitive motion from execution, becomes the foundation of the distinction between internal freedom of choice and the contingency of external efficacy.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_73:A11","CH_GGVC_P_75:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-05","text":"The division of sensitive appetite and the unity of the will are derived from the same principle: the formal scope of the object, not a plurality of terms or acts, determines the number of powers.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-03","CL_GGVC_28:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_73:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_74:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-06","text":"Choice is the node of the recursion of intellect and will, because counsel proposes an object to appetite, while the will commands further consideration and act.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-08","CL_GGVC_28:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_74:A14","CH_GGVC_P_75:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-07","text":"Divine motion is not compulsion if the inclination of the will is not destroyed but is effected as the proper mode of the secondary cause by the first cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-07","CL_GGVC_28:GA-13","CH_GGVC_P_74:A3","CH_GGVC_P_75:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:XD-08","text":"The modality of the will can depend on the state of cognition: a particular good remains contingent while its connection with happiness is not demonstrated, but the known necessity of the connection determines the will.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-06","CL_GGVC_28:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_74:A11","CH_GGVC_P_75:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSD-01","text":"The human agent is not a simple faculty, but a graded architecture in which sensitive appetite, practical reason, will, and the first cause exercise their proper causalities in order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_75:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSD-02","text":"Freedom is a property of the causal path through judgment of contingents, not a property of an uncaused object or of a power without inclination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:D2","CH_GGVC_P_75:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSD-03","text":"Moral responsibility remains where judgment and elective acceptance are preserved, even if passions incline choice or execution is impeded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_75:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSD-04","text":"The time of action is articulated into apprehension, inclination, counsel, acceptance, and execution; freedom consists in the intermediate intervals and transitions, not in a moment empty of causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A18","CH_GGVC_P_75:A8","CH_GGVC_P_75:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSD-05","text":"A change of cognition can change the necessity of appetite without changing the thing desired, because certainty of the connection of the good with happiness closes the field of contingency.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_74:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:BSD-06","text":"Dependence and self-motion are not opposites: the secondary cause moves itself according to the voluntary form that the order of the first cause does not erase but causally sustains.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A12","CH_GGVC_P_75:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RLE-01","text":"Concupiscible good → impediment → irascible motion → overcoming or defense → restoration of concupiscible good; the irascible circuit is subordinate, not a parallel end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_73:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RLE-02","text":"Sense or imagination → motion of sensitive appetite → possible resistance → rational judgment → consent or prohibition of the will → execution or interruption.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A12","CH_GGVC_P_73:D7","CH_GGVC_P_73:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RLE-03","text":"Intellect proposes the good → will moves the act of consideration → intellect compares further → will accepts; regress is stopped in the first principle of apprehension.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A14","CH_GGVC_P_74:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RLE-04","text":"Happiness is an unmoved attractor that orders particular goods without any one of them exhausting the will; stability of the end generates a plurality of paths.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A6","CH_GGVC_P_74:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RLE-05","text":"End of appetite → comparison of means → counsel → acceptance → choice → act for the sake of the end; the end is a terminus with respect to appetite and an engine with respect to choice.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A8","CH_GGVC_P_75:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RLE-06","text":"Ignorance of the connection → contingency of adherence → certain vision → necessity of the will; epistemic amplification produces modal change.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:D3","CH_GGVC_P_74:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:RLE-07","text":"First cause → voluntary cause → proper voluntary motion; the order of causality closes the system without closing freedom within absolute autonomy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A13","CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B01","text":"Apprehended object → cognitive presence in the apprehender; object as good → appetitive inclination toward the thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A2","CH_GGVC_P_73:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B02","text":"Sensible good → concupiscible pursuit; arduous impediment → irascible resistance → preservation or recovery of the good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A6","CH_GGVC_P_73:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B03","text":"Proper sensitive motion + particular cogitative power governable by reason + governance of reason → lower appetite politically ordered, not causally emptied.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A10","CH_GGVC_P_73:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B04","text":"Universal good + naturally desired happiness → one will ordered to a necessary end and capable of multiple means.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A8","CH_GGVC_P_74:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B05","text":"Contingent particulars → comparison by reason → judgment open to opposites → appetitive acceptance → free choice.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A3","CH_GGVC_P_75:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B06","text":"Internal choice → executive consent → contingent external motion; impediment of the effect does not retroactively destroy choice.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A11","CH_GGVC_P_75:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B07","text":"Motion of the first cause → preservation of the mode of voluntary cause → act of the self-moved secondary cause → dependent freedom.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A13","CH_GGVC_P_75:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B08","text":"Distinction of the formals of object and causality → composition of necessity, inclination, and contingency → freedom as a rationally mediated appetitive order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_75:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AFM-B01","text":"Ambiguity of cognition and sensuality → distinction of apprehension from inclination → division of sensitive appetite → political governance of passions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AF1","CH_GGVC_P_73:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AFM-B02","text":"Undifferentiated necessity → separation of compulsion, nature, and end → necessarily willed happiness → contingency of particular goods → freedom of means.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A2","CH_GGVC_P_74:A6","CH_GGVC_P_74:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AFM-B03","text":"Contingent particulars → comparison by reason → counsel → appetitive acceptance → internal choice → impedible execution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A3","CH_GGVC_P_75:A8","CH_GGVC_P_75:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:AFM-B04","text":"Objection from divine motion → distinction of first and secondary cause → preservation of voluntary mode → free choice as dependent causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:A13","CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MME-01","text":"Necessity is reordered from the simple contrary of freedom into an analogous genus, only the coercive species of which removes the voluntary.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_74:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MME-02","text":"The powers of the soul are no longer counted from the names of passions, but from proper acts, formal accounts of objects, and modes of causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_74:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MME-03","text":"Freedom is transferred from the opposition between caused and autonomous to the difference between compelled, instinctive, and rationally voluntary motion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_75:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MME-04","text":"Interior conflict is reclassified as an effect of a plurality of moving principles, not as a simple defect of rational governance.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_75:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MME-05","text":"Higher causality no longer substitutes for lower causality; its perfective effect is that the secondary cause acts according to its proper mode.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:D6","CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MME-06","text":"The distinction of choice from execution separates internal responsibility from power over events and moral freedom from external efficacy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MME-07","text":"Cognition acquires modal force: it not only manifests the object, but through certainty of the connection can determine whether the will is borne contingently or necessarily.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_74:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of surface, sensuality, will, and free choice are distinguished according to sensitive appetite, intellectual appetite, and choice of means.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, formal objects, cognitive mediations, modes of causality, and transitions to execution distribute acts without indiscriminate multiplication of powers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the opposition between passion and reason or cause and freedom is replaced by an ordering of causes, respects, and levels of appetite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, the subject is a secondary cause whose action occurs through the succession of apprehension, inclination, counsel, and consent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system imitates the freedom that it defines: it does not remove objections, but translates them through distinctions into a causal order capable of composing them.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_75:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FL-01","text":"The irascible is posited as a distinct power, but its passions arise from and return to the concupiscible; functional autonomy and final subordination imperfectly coincide.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_73:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FL-02","text":"The political obedience of passions preserves resistance, but the boundary between rationally governable inclination and already impeded judgment is not defined.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_75:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FL-03","text":"The indifference of free choice to good and evil is composed with the necessary ordering of the will to happiness through end and means, but the perversity of means under an apparent good remains implicit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_74:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FL-04","text":"First causality is said to effect the voluntary mode itself, but the mechanism by which divine determination does not absorb secondary choice is not further explained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_75:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:FL-05","text":"Happiness is necessarily desired, but God is not necessarily chosen before vision; the identity of the real end with the known end depends on fragile epistemic mediation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_74:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MP-01","text":"The name of sensuality changes from the field of cognition to appetitive act, thereby opening the entire subsequent architecture of inclination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_73:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MP-02","text":"Necessity changes from the apparent negation of the voluntary into a double function: compulsion destroys freedom, nature and end order it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_74:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MP-03","text":"Free choice is transferred from a named act to a permanent power, then from a quasi-cognitive faculty to a principally appetitive act of the same will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_75:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MP-04","text":"The governance of reason changes from direction of the particular cogitative power into the entire order of causality, where even the first cause sustains voluntary motion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A10","CH_GGVC_P_75:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:HSI-01","text":"Psychic reality is imagined as a nested polity: sense and imagination move, reason directs, will commands, but no lower power is reduced to an inert instrument.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_74:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:HSI-02","text":"Happiness functions like an unmoved attractor or a navigational pole; the ultimate direction is fixed, while particular routes are open to multiple comparisons.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:A6","CH_GGVC_P_74:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:HSI-03","text":"The subject is a self-moving secondary cause, not the sovereign principle of itself; its property is a voluntary mode within the order of causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_75:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_75:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:HSI-04","text":"The time of action is a series of gates: apprehension, passion, judgment, acceptance, and execution; at each, passage can be ordered, impeded, or interrupted.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_75:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:HSI-05","text":"Human struggle is not flight from inclination, but navigation among the desired good, arduous obstacles, and contingent means compared under reason.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:A9","CH_GGVC_P_75:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B01","text":"The distinction of cognition from appetite, threefold necessity, necessary end, judgment of contingents, appetitive acceptance, and first causality suffice to reconstruct the structure of this corpus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_74:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is double mediation: reason orders inclinations through particular judgment, while the will governs the transition from appetite to choice and execution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_74:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remain the measure in which passions corrupt judgment and the mode in which divine aid simultaneously determines and preserves proper choice.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B04","text":"The true coherence is recursive: sensitive appetite is not abandoned, but is successively recodified in will, choice, and first causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_73:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the generative movement, but the epistemic transition from happiness to God and the metaphysical joint between first motion and secondary choice resist full reduction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_74:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_75:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_28@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_28:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 82–84","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 81 · Prima pars · Quaestio 82 · Prima pars · Quaestio 83"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 85–87","order":29,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_76","CH_GGVC_P_77","CH_GGVC_P_78"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_29@R001","sha256":"5a6caef471e9d25be24affb927858694d8d0bb8d474886e42efbcf5eb4bd1fe0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo intellectus animae corpori coniunctae res materiales cognoscat, operationes suas ordinet atque singularia, infinita, contingentia et futura secundum proprios accessus attingat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B02","text":"Thesis globalis cognitionem humanam constituit ut receptionem immaterialem rei materialis per abstractionem, cuius exercitium tamen conversione ad phantasmata et mediationibus causalibus permanenter temperatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B03","text":"Argumentatio fit reconstructio per distinctiones: aequationes modi cognoscendi cum modo essendi dissolvuntur, media falsa excluduntur, deinde diversi cognitionis modi pro diversis obiecti rationibus distribuuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_78:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B04","text":"Fundamentum est medietas hylomorphica intellectus humani: operatio intellectualis organum transcendit, sed anima ut forma corporis materiam cognitionis, condiciones exercitii et gradus perfectionis a potentiis sensitivis accipit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B05","text":"Motus procedit a vindicatione scientiae corporum ad constructionem abstractionis et operationum intellectualium, ac demum ad distributionem limitum secundum accessum directum, reflexum, successivum et causalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B06","text":"Motus verus est recursivus: phantasma abstractionem nutrit, species intellectum actualizat, intellectus ad phantasma redit, atque hic reditus universalem cognitionem ad singulare, usum et iudicium reconnectit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A13","CH_GGVC_P_77:A5","CH_GGVC_P_78:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B07","text":"Successio et discursivitas non sunt defectus extrinseci, sed forma temporalis intellectus potentialis, quae simul progressum indefinitum cognitionis, errorem compositionis et coniecturam futurorum aperit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_78:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B08","text":"Clausura est graduata, non absoluta: systema non simpliciter cognitionem concedit aut negat, sed ordines accessus et certitudinis distinguit, dum intuitum immediatum, totalem et simultaneum extra statum humanum collocat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Cognoscibile recipitur secundum modum et capacitatem potentiae cognoscentis; proportio obiecti et potentiae omnes operationes atque limites sequentes regit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A1","CH_GGVC_P_77:A1","CH_GGVC_P_78:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] Obiectum proprium intellectus humani corpori coniuncti est natura rei materialis, directe quidem sub ratione universali, ontologice tamen in singularibus existens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A5","CH_GGVC_P_77:A3","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Intellectio formaliter organo corporeo non exercetur, sed eius modus humanus, vigor et actualis operatio statui animae ut formae corporis proportionantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A4","CH_GGVC_P_77:A2","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Sensus et phantasmata materiam cognitionis intellectualis praebent, sed neque causam totalem neque actualem intelligibilitatem per se constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A9","CH_GGVC_P_76:A10","CH_GGVC_P_77:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Intellectus agens phantasmata illuminando naturam abstrahibilem actualizat, intellectus autem possibilis specie intelligibili informatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A8","CH_GGVC_P_77:A6","CH_GGVC_P_78:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Conversio ad phantasmata, primo condicio usus speciei acquisitae, fit etiam mechanismus quo intellectus universalem cognitionem ad singulare reflexe applicat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A13","CH_GGVC_P_77:A5","CH_GGVC_P_78:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Species intelligibilis est medium formale quo res exterior cognoscitur, non obiectum primarium quod rem substituat; realismum igitur mediatio non interrumpit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A9","CH_GGVC_P_77:A14","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Natura communis in singularibus existit, sed abstractio et intentio universalitatis in intellectu sunt; universalitas epistemica singularitatem ontologicam non tollit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A10","CH_GGVC_P_78:A3","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Intellectus potentialis per actus successivos procedit a cognitione confusa ad distinctam; unitas speciei simultaneitatem, pluralitas specierum successionem constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A7","CH_GGVC_P_77:A11","CH_GGVC_P_78:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Universale et necessarium directe intellectui patent, singulare materiale autem sensui directe et intellectui per conversionem reflexam; diversitas accessus non diversitatem rei postulat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:A11","CH_GGVC_P_78:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Contingentia singularis ex materia et necessitas intelligibilis ex forma oriuntur; scientia potest igitur rationem necessariam rerum contingentium attingere sine contingentia eventuum abolenda.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:A10","CH_GGVC_P_78:D6","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Futurum in se intuitui humano non praesens est; humana praecognitio mensuratur ordine causarum, dum simultas totius temporis soli intuitui aeterno attribuitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:A12","CH_GGVC_P_78:A13","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-01","text":"[Identitas stabilis] Proportio potentiae ad obiectum immutata manet et successive originem specierum, abstractionis gradum atque limites singularis, infiniti et futuri ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A1","CH_GGVC_P_78:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-02","text":"[Mutatio] Distinctio formae materialiter existentis a receptione immateriali mutatur in doctrinam abstractionis, quae condiciones individuales praetermittit sine separatione reali affirmanda.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_77:A7","CH_GGVC_P_78:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-03","text":"[Reditus functionalis] Phantasma primo materiam abstractionis praebet, deinde actualem usum speciei sustinet, postremo transitum reflexum ad singulare efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A9","CH_GGVC_P_77:A5","CH_GGVC_P_78:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-04","text":"[Translatio] Species intelligibilis ab effectu acquisitionis transfertur ad medium realismi et deinde ad principium formale quo multa sub ratione unius simul intelliguntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A11","CH_GGVC_P_77:A9","CH_GGVC_P_77:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-05","text":"[Amplificatio] Universalitas ab immateriali modo receptionis amplificatur in directum cognitionis ordinem, salva natura quae in singularibus existit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD7","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-06","text":"[Mutatio] Materia, primo condicio obiecti humani, fit principium individuationis et contingentiae; forma vicissim fit principium universalitatis et necessitatis intelligibilis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A5","CH_GGVC_P_78:A1","CH_GGVC_P_78:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-07","text":"[Inversio] Corporalitas invertitur ab apparenti impedimento in mediationem constitutivam, quamvis dispositio corporis eandem operationem graduare vel impedire possit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD9","CH_GGVC_P_76:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-08","text":"[Absorptio] Cognitio per essentiam, innatismus et influxus separatus evanescunt ut media aemula, sed earum exclusio absorbetur in structuram causalitatis stratificatae, quae causam remotam a proximis mediationibus distinguit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_76:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:CGD-01","text":"[Localis superata] Polemicae contra cognitionem per essentiam, species innatas et formas separatas non manent theses globales independentes, sed campum purgant ad machinam sensitivo-intellectualem constituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:CGD-02","text":"[Amplificata] Abstractio a phantasmatibus ex mechanismo acquisitionis fit regula generalis quae obiectum proprium, universalitatem, successionem et fines directae cognitionis determinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:CGD-03","text":"[Coniungens] Distinctio speciei a re intellecta coniungit causalitatem acquisitionis cum realismo et permittit cognitionem universalem ad singularia applicari sine speciei reificatione.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A9","CH_GGVC_P_77:D3","CH_GGVC_P_78:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:CGD-04","text":"[Transitionalis] Successio, compositio et error, localiter operationes intellectus, fiunt structura temporalis qua infinitum successive, contingens formaliter et futurum causaliter cognoscuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_77:D10","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:CGD-05","text":"[Amplificata] Defectus sensus et imaginativae dispositionis ex probationibus localibus amplificantur in legem praesentis status: independentia formalis ab organo non tollit dependentiam operationalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:D5","CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:CGD-06","text":"[Localis terminalis] Praenuntiationes in somno vel alienatione sensuum structuram globalem non generant; tantum limitem confirmant quo accessus extraordinarius ad impressiones causales, non ad autonomam praescientiam, reducitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:D8","CH_GGVC_P_78:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_78:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GNX-01","text":"Cognitio corporum neque per similitudines corporeas neque per reductionem intellectus ad impressionem sensibilem explicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_77:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GNX-02","text":"Scientia non fundatur in ideis separatis, speciebus innatis aut immediata visione rationum aeternarum; principia superiora mediationes naturales non substituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GNX-03","text":"Abstractio non est separatio realis, et species intelligibilis non est obiectum primarium quod rem exteriorem intra repraesentationem substituat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_77:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_77:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GNX-04","text":"Immaterialitas operationis non importat independentiam absolutam a sensu, imaginatione vel conversione ad phantasmata in statu praesentis vitae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:NX12","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX11","CH_GGVC_P_78:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GNX-05","text":"Intellectui humano negantur cognitio directa singularis materialis, comprehensio actualis infiniti et praesentia futuri in se, non autem omnis cognitio horum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_78:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_78:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GNX-06","text":"Systema excludit unicum ordinem absolutum prioritatis et simultaneitatem omnium cognitorum; prioritas et unitas actus semper secundum rationem formalem determinantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:SNX5","CH_GGVC_P_77:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet modum rei in cognoscente identicum esse modo essendi rei, alioquin cognitio corporis aut materialis fieret aut ad obiecta separata relegaretur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_76:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_77:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet subiectum humanum intra proprias formas claudi aut ex se solo cognitionem producere, quia utrumque relationem ad rem et discrimen veri a falso destrueret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_76:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_77:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet causam remotam cognitionis causis proximis substitui posse; lumen superius sine sensibilibus, phantasmatibus et abstractione cognitionem materialium non determinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:DNX6","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_76:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet infinitatem potentiae intellectivae possessionem actualem infinitorum significare; capacitas inexhausta tantum per successionem operatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_78:D4","CH_GGVC_P_78:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet contingentiam eventus omnem necessitatem scientificam tollere vel abstractionem a tempore futurum praesens efficere; necessitas ad formam, praecognitio ad causalitatem refertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_78:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_78:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSA-01","text":"Absentia ordinans est intuitus humanus qui rem singularem, totalem et temporaliter praesentem sine mediatione simul possideat; talem plenitudinem systema semper extra modum humanum ponit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_77:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_78:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSA-02","text":"Norma intellectus omnino disembodiedi intra statum praesentem indisponibilis est; separatio a corpore cognitionem humanam non explicat nec eius perfectionem immediate metitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_77:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_77:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSA-03","text":"Oppositio absoluta inter repraesentationem internam et rem externam inadmissibilis est, quia ipsa forma immanens est medium quo intellectus ad rem ordinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_77:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSA-04","text":"Tempus humanum non potest in unam praesentiam absolutam nec prioritas in unum ordinem comprimi; aeternitas divina manet limes indisponibilis qui successionem humanam definit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:SNX5","CH_GGVC_P_78:A12","CH_GGVC_P_78:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-01","text":"Abstractio et conversio unum motum duplicem constituunt: intellectus condiciones individuales praetermittit ut universale accipiat, deinde ad phantasma redit ut idem cognitum actualiter exerceat et singulari applicet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-05","CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","CL_GGVC_29:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_77:A5","CH_GGVC_P_78:A4","CH_GGVC_P_78:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-02","text":"Realismus systematis recursivus est: species rem non substituit, sed intellectum formaliter determinat, dum conversio ad phantasma relationem ad modum singularem eiusdem rei reficit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","CL_GGVC_29:GA-07","CL_GGVC_29:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_77:A9","CH_GGVC_P_77:D3","CH_GGVC_P_77:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-03","text":"Dependentia a corpore est selectiva, non reductiva: intellectus sensum transcendit quantum ad modum cognitionis, sed sensu et imaginatione caret materia, exercitio et pleno iudicio.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-03","CL_GGVC_29:GA-04","CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","CH_GGVC_P_76:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD9","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-04","text":"Actio practica distribuitur inter universalem rationem intellectus et apprehensionem sensitivam particularis; neque universale solum neque singulare non intellectum electionem complet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-02","CL_GGVC_29:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_78:D3","CH_GGVC_P_78:D2","CH_GGVC_P_78:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-05","text":"Finitudo intellectus non est clausura quantitatis fixa, sed impossibilitas totalitatis simultaneae coniuncta cum potentia indefinita ad ulteriores actus successivos.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_78:D4","CH_GGVC_P_78:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-06","text":"Certitudo humana est ordini accommodata: necessitas scientifica in rationibus formalibus consistit, certitudo futuri in necessitate causarum, coniectura autem in inclinatione causali frequenti.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-11","CL_GGVC_29:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_78:D6","CH_GGVC_P_78:A13","CH_GGVC_P_78:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-07","text":"Eadem facultas synthetica quae proprietates et causas componendo scientiam auget etiam falsitatem et coniecturam producit, quia rectitudo nexus additi non ex simplici apprehensione sola provenit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-09","CL_GGVC_29:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_77:D10","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_78:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-08","text":"Lumen aeternum et intuitus divinus limites verticales constituunt, non compendia operationis humanae: primum cognitionem causaliter fundat, secundus ostendit quid successioni humanae indisponibile maneat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-04","CL_GGVC_29:GA-05","CL_GGVC_29:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_76:A12","CH_GGVC_P_76:DNX6","CH_GGVC_P_78:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSD-01","text":"Subiectum cognoscens non est spectator clausus, sed operator medius qui singulare sensitivum in universalem formam transducit et universalem formam ad singulare reflexe reducit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-02","CL_GGVC_29:GA-05","CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","CL_GGVC_29:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_76:D3","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSD-02","text":"Tempus cognitionis non est solum mensura exterior, sed structura interna acquisitionis, successionis, conversionis et resolutionis, qua intellectus potentialis scientiam distinctam format.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","CL_GGVC_29:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_77:A18","CH_GGVC_P_77:D9","CH_GGVC_P_78:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSD-03","text":"Status obiecti cogniti relatione ad operationem variatur: eadem res materialis est universalis in apprehensione directa, singularis in reflexione et futura in ordine causarum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-08","CL_GGVC_29:GA-10","CL_GGVC_29:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD7","CH_GGVC_P_78:D1","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSD-04","text":"Scientia certa non requirit immutabilitatem totalem rei vel necessitatem eventus, sed rationem formalem stabilem aut ordinem causalem effectui sufficienter determinatum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-01","CL_GGVC_29:GA-11","CL_GGVC_29:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_76:D2","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_78:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSD-05","text":"Limites sunt generativi: abstractio cognitionem directam singularis excludit sed reflexionem efficit; successio comprehensionem infiniti tollit sed progressum indefinite extensibilem constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","CL_GGVC_29:GA-09","CL_GGVC_29:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_78:D4","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSD-06","text":"Scala humana cognitionis neque causalitate inferiori neque superiori clauditur: sensibilia determinant materiam, intellectus actualizat intelligibilitatem, lumen superius fundat, aeternitas autem terminum comparativum praebet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-03","CL_GGVC_29:GA-04","CL_GGVC_29:GA-05","CL_GGVC_29:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_76:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_76:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_78:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RLE-01","text":"Circuitus principalis est: res sensibilis → operatio sensitiva → phantasma → abstractio activa → species intelligibilis → intellectio universalis → conversio ad phantasma → iudicium de singulari.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A9","CH_GGVC_P_76:A8","CH_GGVC_P_77:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RLE-02","text":"Via amplificationis procedit de potentia ad apprehensionem confusam, distinctionem partium, compositionem habitudinum, ratiocinationem et resolutionem ad principia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A11","CH_GGVC_P_77:A13","CH_GGVC_P_77:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RLE-03","text":"Attractor totius structurae est medietas intellectus humani: omnis transcendentia supra sensum ad dependentiam ab eo redit, omnis dependentia autem abstractione transcenditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RLE-04","text":"Circuitus materiae et formae distribuit modum scientiae: materia singularitatem et contingentiam parit, forma universalitatem et necessitatem, applicatio autem utrumque in iudicio de re coniungit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:A10","CH_GGVC_P_78:D6","CH_GGVC_P_78:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RLE-05","text":"Infinitum fit attractor spiralis, non terminus possessus: negatio totalitatis simultaneae intellectum ad actum ulteriorem semper remittit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:A7","CH_GGVC_P_78:D4","CH_GGVC_P_78:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RLE-06","text":"Series exclusionum cognitionem ad mediationem reducit: quotiens essentia, innatismus, forma separata vel praescientia immediata viam brevem promittunt, systema causas intermedias restituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_77:D1","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FGS-B01","text":"Status constitutivus: anima est forma corporis + intellectus formaliter non organicus → obiectum proprium proportionatum naturae materiali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A2","CH_GGVC_P_76:A4","CH_GGVC_P_76:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FGS-B02","text":"Ingressus materialis: res sensibilis → immutatio coniuncti sensitivi → phantasma singulare, quod cognitionem incipit sed non perficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A9","CH_GGVC_P_76:A10","CH_GGVC_P_77:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FGS-B03","text":"Transformatio formalis: illuminatio intellectus agentis → abstractio condicionum individualium → species intelligibilis → actualizatio intellectus possibilis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A6","CH_GGVC_P_78:A2","CH_GGVC_P_76:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FGS-B04","text":"Relatio obiectiva: species ut medium formale → natura rei ut primum intellectum → universalitas in intellectu → persistentia naturae in singularibus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A9","CH_GGVC_P_77:A10","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FGS-B05","text":"Explicatio discursiva: apprehensio confusa → distinctio → compositio vel divisio → ratiocinatio → veritas aut falsitas secundum convenientiam ordinationis cum re.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A11","CH_GGVC_P_77:A13","CH_GGVC_P_77:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FGS-B06","text":"Distributio terminalis: universale directe → singulare reflexe → infinitum successive → contingens sub ratione formali → futurum in causis → intuitus totius temporis extra modum humanum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_78:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AFM-B01","text":"Scientia corporum vindicata → confusio modi essendi et cognoscendi dissoluta → media falsa exclusa → mediatio sensitivo-intellectualis constituta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_76:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_76:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AFM-B02","text":"Proportio obiecti et potentiae → abstractio a phantasmatibus → species ut medium → universale ut obiectum directum → conversio ad singulare.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A1","CH_GGVC_P_77:A5","CH_GGVC_P_77:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AFM-B03","text":"Potentia → cognitio confusa → distinctio → compositio → discursus → veritas vel error → resolutio ad principia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A11","CH_GGVC_P_77:A13","CH_GGVC_P_77:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AFM-B04","text":"Limites abstractionis → singularitas reflexa → infinitum successivum → contingentia formaliter scibilis → futurum causaliter praecognoscibile → clausura graduata accessus humani.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:MRT7","CH_GGVC_P_78:D6","CH_GGVC_P_78:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MME-01","text":"Corpus reclassificatur ex impedimento extrinseco in condicionem constitutivam cognitionis, sine reductione intellectus ad organum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_77:ME7","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MME-02","text":"Abstractio reclassificatur ex separatione rei in praetermissionem condicionum non essentialium, quae simul universalitatem constituit et reditum ad singulare requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_77:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MME-03","text":"Species intelligibilis reclassificatur ab obiecto terminali ad medium formale, unde immanentia formae et exterioritas rei simul cogitari possunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_77:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MME-04","text":"Oppositio universalis et singularis solvitur in distinctionem accessus directi et reflexi; universalitas epistemica cum singularitate ontologica compatitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD7","CH_GGVC_P_78:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MME-05","text":"Oppositio necessitatis et contingentiae reordinatur: scientia necessitatem rationis formalis, eventus contingentiam materialem, praecognitio autem gradum determinationis causalis sequitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_78:A10","CH_GGVC_P_78:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MME-06","text":"Infinitum, tempus et prioritas desinunt esse totalitates simplices: infinitum est successio inexhausta, tempus cognitionis ordo actuum, prioritas autem relatio ad diversos ordines.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_77:D9","CH_GGVC_P_77:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei examinat cognitionem corporum, abstractionem, species, ordinem operationum atque accessum ad singularia, infinita, contingentia et futura.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_77:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_78:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis cognitionem ordinat per proportionem obiecti et potentiae, cooperationem sensus et intellectus, abstractionem, conversionem, successionem et causalitatem stratificatam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_78:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_76:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus interrogationem affirmativam vel negativam substituit distinctionibus obiecti, medii, modi, ordinis et certitudinis, quibus oppositiones apparentes in accessus graduatos resolvuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_77:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis materiam facit principium singularitatis et contingentiae, formam principium intelligibilitatis necessariae, successionem modum humanum, aeternitatem autem praesentiam divinam totius temporis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_78:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus et performativus propria distinctionum serie efficit quod asserit: errores totalizantes fragmentat, mediationes restituit et obiecta secundum diversos modos cognitionis distribuit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_78:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FL-01","text":"Independentia formalis intellectus ab organo et dependentia actualis a dispositione corporis cohaerent tantum si causalitas operationis a condicione exercitii stricte distinguatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FL-02","text":"Obiectum proprium dicitur natura in materia existens, sed directe cognoscitur abstracta a materia individuali; conversio ad phantasma tensionem continet potius quam mechanismum reflexionis plene explicat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_78:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FL-03","text":"Necessitas intellectus agentis firma est, sed modus quo illuminatio phantasmatis speciem in intellectu possibili causat mechanice subdeterminatus manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_77:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FL-04","text":"Infallibilitas circa quidditatem et fallibilitas compositionis distinctione operationum servantur, sed criterium quo definitio simplex a compositione intrusa discernatur ulteriore structura indiget.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT8","CH_GGVC_P_77:A15","CH_GGVC_P_77:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MP-01","text":"Forma immaterialiter recepta mutatur in naturam per abstractionem consideratam; principium receptionis fit principium operationis selectivae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A1","CH_GGVC_P_77:A7","CH_GGVC_P_77:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MP-02","text":"Species intelligibilis mutatur ab apparenti termino cognitionis in medium formale quo res cognoscitur et unitas actus mensuratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_77:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_77:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MP-03","text":"Corpus mutatur ab impedimento cognitionis in condicionem constitutivam, sed eadem mutatio gradus debilitatis et impedimenti corporaliter causati retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_77:ME7","CH_GGVC_P_76:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MP-04","text":"Necessitas mutatur ab attributo rei immobilis in rationem formalem scientiae contingentium, dum futura secundum gradum necessitatis causae iterum modaliter distribuuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_78:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_78:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite apparet ut machina hylomorphica conversionis cum circuitu retroactivo: materia singulare initium praebet, forma intelligibilitatem constituit, intellectus autem ad phantasma redit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_77:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_78:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est limen potentiale inter sensum et intellectum, non centrum iam actu completum; identitas eius operatione transductiva et ordinibus accessus constituitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A7","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:HSI-03","text":"Tempus humanum est seriale, recursivum et resolutivum; aeternitas divina illud non continuat, sed extrinsecus complectitur ut simultas totius cursus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:NX10","CH_GGVC_P_77:A18","CH_GGVC_P_78:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:HSI-04","text":"Potentia agendi manet navigatio inter universalem rationem et particularem apprehensionem, inter certitudinem causalem et coniecturam, non fuga a mediationibus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:D3","CH_GGVC_P_78:A13","CH_GGVC_P_78:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata proportionis, obiecti materialis, abstractionis, speciei formalis, conversionis, successionis et causalitatis sufficiunt ad machinam huius gregis restituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_78:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B02","text":"Cohaerentia firmissima est in duplici motu abstractionis et conversionis atque in distinctionibus inter rem et speciem, directum et reflexum, formam necessariam et eventum contingentem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_78:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B03","text":"Nondum satis determinantur modus abstractionis causalis, terminus reflexionis ad singulare et coordinatio exacta luminis superioris cum determinatione a sensibilibus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat: scientia corporum stabilitur, compendia immediata excluduntur, mediatio recursiva construitur, operationes explicantur et limites in modos positivos cognitionis convertuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT8","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_78:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B05","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae resistunt explicatio interna abstractionis, criterium reflexionis et discretio impressionum extraordinariarum; haec tamen commissuras locales afficiunt, non structuram generatricem principalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_78:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T172623089730Z_e6601f56","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 85–87","source_sha256":"5a6caef471e9d25be24affb927858694d8d0bb8d474886e42efbcf5eb4bd1fe0","sha256":"47bcd59d3cadd2f9d05c334fe9d23255bba293b92f641c4931da8d438f06b0f7","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:26:23.090685+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B01","text":"The system asks how the intellect of the soul conjoined to a body knows material things, orders its operations, and attains singulars, infinities, contingents, and future things according to their proper modes of access.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B02","text":"The global thesis establishes human cognition as the immaterial reception of a material thing through abstraction, whose exercise is nevertheless permanently regulated by turning to phantasms and by causal mediations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B03","text":"The argument proceeds by reconstruction through distinctions: equations of the mode of knowing with the mode of being are dissolved, false means are excluded, and then diverse modes of cognition are distributed according to diverse aspects of the object.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_78:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B04","text":"The foundation is the hylomorphic mean of the human intellect: intellectual operation transcends an organ, but the soul as the form of the body receives from the sensitive powers the matter of cognition, the conditions of its exercise, and degrees of perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B05","text":"The movement proceeds from the vindication of knowledge of bodies to the construction of abstraction and intellectual operations, and finally to the distribution of limits according to direct, reflexive, successive, and causal access.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B06","text":"The true movement is recursive: the phantasm nourishes abstraction, the species actualizes the intellect, the intellect returns to the phantasm, and this return reconnects universal cognition with the singular, use, and judgment.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A13","CH_GGVC_P_77:A5","CH_GGVC_P_78:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B07","text":"Succession and discursiveness are not extrinsic defects, but the temporal form of the potential intellect, which at once opens indefinite progress of cognition, error of composition, and conjecture concerning future things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_78:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AS-B08","text":"The closure is graduated, not absolute: the system does not simply grant or deny cognition, but distinguishes orders of access and certainty, while placing immediate, total, and simultaneous intuition outside the human state.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] What is knowable is received according to the mode and capacity of the knowing power; the proportion of object and power governs all subsequent operations and limits.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A1","CH_GGVC_P_77:A1","CH_GGVC_P_78:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] The proper object of the human intellect conjoined to a body is the nature of a material thing, directly indeed under the aspect of the universal, but ontologically existing in singulars.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A5","CH_GGVC_P_77:A3","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Understanding is not formally exercised through a bodily organ, but its human mode, vigor, and actual operation are proportioned to the state of the soul as the form of the body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A4","CH_GGVC_P_77:A2","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Sense and phantasms provide the matter of intellectual cognition, but they constitute neither its total cause nor intelligibility in act through themselves.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A9","CH_GGVC_P_76:A10","CH_GGVC_P_77:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] By illuminating phantasms, the agent intellect actualizes the abstractible nature, whereas the possible intellect is informed by an intelligible species.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A8","CH_GGVC_P_77:A6","CH_GGVC_P_78:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Turning to phantasms, initially a condition for the use of the acquired species, also becomes the mechanism by which the intellect reflexively applies universal cognition to the singular.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A13","CH_GGVC_P_77:A5","CH_GGVC_P_78:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The intelligible species is the formal medium through which the external thing is known, not a primary object that substitutes for the thing; mediation therefore does not interrupt realism.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A9","CH_GGVC_P_77:A14","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Common nature exists in singulars, but abstraction and the intention of universality are in the intellect; epistemic universality does not abolish ontological singularity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A10","CH_GGVC_P_78:A3","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] The potential intellect proceeds through successive acts from confused to distinct cognition; the unity of a species establishes simultaneity, the plurality of species succession.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A7","CH_GGVC_P_77:A11","CH_GGVC_P_78:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] The universal and the necessary are directly open to the intellect, whereas the material singular is directly open to sense and to intellect through reflexive turning; diversity of access does not require diversity of the thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:A11","CH_GGVC_P_78:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] The contingency of the singular arises from matter and intelligible necessity from form; science can therefore attain the necessary account of contingent things without abolishing the contingency of events.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:A10","CH_GGVC_P_78:D6","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] The future in itself is not present to human intuition; human foreknowledge is measured by the order of causes, whereas the simultaneity of all time is attributed to eternal intuition alone.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:A12","CH_GGVC_P_78:A13","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] The proportion of power to object remains unchanged and successively orders the origin of species, the degree of abstraction, and the limits of the singular, the infinite, and the future.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A1","CH_GGVC_P_78:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] The distinction between form materially existing and immaterial reception is transformed into the doctrine of abstraction, which passes over individual conditions without affirming real separation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_77:A7","CH_GGVC_P_78:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-03","text":"[Functional return] The phantasm first provides the matter of abstraction, then sustains the actual use of the species, and finally effects the reflexive transition to the singular.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A9","CH_GGVC_P_77:A5","CH_GGVC_P_78:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-04","text":"[Translation] The intelligible species is transferred from an effect of acquisition to a medium of realism and then to the formal principle by which many things are understood simultaneously under the aspect of one.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A11","CH_GGVC_P_77:A9","CH_GGVC_P_77:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-05","text":"[Amplification] Universality is amplified from an immaterial mode of reception into a direct order of cognition, while preserving the nature that exists in singulars.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD7","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-06","text":"[Mutation] Matter, first the condition of the human object, becomes the principle of individuation and contingency; form in turn becomes the principle of universality and intelligible necessity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A5","CH_GGVC_P_78:A1","CH_GGVC_P_78:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-07","text":"[Inversion] Corporeality is inverted from an apparent impediment into constitutive mediation, although bodily disposition can grade or impede that same operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD9","CH_GGVC_P_76:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RMD-08","text":"[Absorption] Cognition through essence, innatism, and separate influx disappear as rival means, but their exclusion is absorbed into a structure of stratified causality that distinguishes a remote cause from proximate mediations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_76:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:CGD-01","text":"[Overcome local] Polemics against cognition through essence, innate species, and separate forms do not remain independent global theses, but clear the field for constituting the sensitive-intellectual machinery.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:CGD-02","text":"[Amplified] Abstraction from phantasms becomes, from a mechanism of acquisition, a general rule determining the proper object, universality, succession, and the limits of direct cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:CGD-03","text":"[Connecting] The distinction of the species from the thing understood joins the causality of acquisition with realism and permits universal cognition to be applied to singulars without reifying the species.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A9","CH_GGVC_P_77:D3","CH_GGVC_P_78:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:CGD-04","text":"[Transitional] Succession, composition, and error, locally operations of the intellect, become the temporal structure through which the infinite is known successively, the contingent formally, and the future causally.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_77:D10","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:CGD-05","text":"[Amplified] Defects of sense and imaginative disposition are amplified from local proofs into a law of the present state: formal independence from an organ does not remove operational dependence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:D5","CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:CGD-06","text":"[Terminal local] Predictions in sleep or in alienation of the senses do not generate a global structure; they only confirm the limit by which extraordinary access is reduced to causal impressions, not to autonomous foreknowledge.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:D8","CH_GGVC_P_78:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_78:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GNX-01","text":"Knowledge of bodies is explained neither through bodily likenesses nor through reducing the intellect to sensible impression.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_77:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GNX-02","text":"Science is not founded upon separate ideas, innate species, or immediate vision of eternal reasons; higher principles do not replace natural mediations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GNX-03","text":"Abstraction is not real separation, and the intelligible species is not a primary object that substitutes for the external thing within representation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_77:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_77:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GNX-04","text":"The immateriality of operation does not entail absolute independence from sense, imagination, or turning to phantasms in the state of the present life.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:NX12","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX11","CH_GGVC_P_78:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GNX-05","text":"Direct cognition of the material singular, actual comprehension of the infinite, and the presence of the future in itself are denied to the human intellect, but not every cognition of these.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_78:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_78:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:GNX-06","text":"The system excludes a single absolute order of priority and the simultaneity of all known things; priority and unity of act are always determined according to formal aspect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:SNX5","CH_GGVC_P_77:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that the mode of the thing in the knower is identical with the mode of being of the thing; otherwise cognition of body would either become material or be relegated to separate objects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_76:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_77:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that the human subject is enclosed within its own forms or produces cognition from itself alone, because either would destroy relation to the thing and the distinction of truth from falsehood.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_76:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_77:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that the remote cause of cognition can replace proximate causes; higher light without sensibles, phantasms, and abstraction does not determine cognition of material things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:DNX6","CH_GGVC_P_76:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_76:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that the infinity of intellectual power signifies the actual possession of infinite things; inexhaustible capacity operates only through succession.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_78:D4","CH_GGVC_P_78:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the contingency of an event removes every scientific necessity or that abstraction from time makes the future present; necessity is referred to form, foreknowledge to causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_78:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_78:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSA-01","text":"The ordering absence is a human intuition that simultaneously possesses a singular, total, and temporally present thing without mediation; the system always places such fullness outside the human mode.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_77:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_78:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSA-02","text":"The norm of an intellect wholly disembodied within the present state is unavailable; separation from the body neither explains human cognition nor immediately measures its perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_77:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_77:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSA-03","text":"An absolute opposition between internal representation and external thing is inadmissible, because the immanent form itself is the medium through which the intellect is ordered to the thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_77:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSA-04","text":"Human time cannot be compressed into one absolute presence, nor priority into one order; divine eternity remains an unavailable limit that defines human succession.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:SNX5","CH_GGVC_P_78:A12","CH_GGVC_P_78:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-01","text":"Abstraction and turning constitute one double movement: the intellect passes over individual conditions in order to receive the universal, then returns to the phantasm in order actually to exercise the same known thing and apply it to the singular.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-05","CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","CL_GGVC_29:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_77:A5","CH_GGVC_P_78:A4","CH_GGVC_P_78:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-02","text":"The system's realism is recursive: the species does not substitute for the thing, but formally determines the intellect, while turning to the phantasm restores the relation to the singular mode of the same thing.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","CL_GGVC_29:GA-07","CL_GGVC_29:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_77:A9","CH_GGVC_P_77:D3","CH_GGVC_P_77:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-03","text":"Dependence on the body is selective, not reductive: the intellect transcends sense with respect to the mode of cognition, but without sense and imagination it lacks matter, exercise, and complete judgment.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-03","CL_GGVC_29:GA-04","CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","CH_GGVC_P_76:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD9","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-04","text":"Practical action is distributed between the universal account of the intellect and sensitive apprehension of the particular; neither the universal alone nor the singular not understood completes choice.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-02","CL_GGVC_29:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_78:D3","CH_GGVC_P_78:D2","CH_GGVC_P_78:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-05","text":"The finitude of the intellect is not a fixed quantitative closure, but the impossibility of simultaneous totality joined with an indefinite power for further successive acts.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_78:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_78:D4","CH_GGVC_P_78:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-06","text":"Human certainty is accommodated to order: scientific necessity consists in formal accounts, certainty of the future in the necessity of causes, but conjecture in frequent causal inclination.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-11","CL_GGVC_29:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_78:D6","CH_GGVC_P_78:A13","CH_GGVC_P_78:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-07","text":"The same synthetic faculty that increases science by composing properties and causes also produces falsehood and conjecture, because correctness of the added connection does not arise from simple apprehension alone.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-09","CL_GGVC_29:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_77:D10","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_78:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:XD-08","text":"Eternal light and divine intuition constitute vertical limits, not shortcuts for human operation: the former causally founds cognition, the latter shows what remains unavailable to human succession.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-04","CL_GGVC_29:GA-05","CL_GGVC_29:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_76:A12","CH_GGVC_P_76:DNX6","CH_GGVC_P_78:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSD-01","text":"The knowing subject is not an enclosed spectator, but an intermediate operator who transduces the sensitive singular into universal form and reflexively reduces universal form to the singular.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-02","CL_GGVC_29:GA-05","CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","CL_GGVC_29:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_76:D3","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSD-02","text":"The time of cognition is not merely an external measure, but an internal structure of acquisition, succession, turning, and resolution, through which the potential intellect forms distinct science.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","CL_GGVC_29:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_77:A18","CH_GGVC_P_77:D9","CH_GGVC_P_78:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSD-03","text":"The status of the known object varies in relation to operation: the same material thing is universal in direct apprehension, singular in reflection, and future in the order of causes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-08","CL_GGVC_29:GA-10","CL_GGVC_29:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD7","CH_GGVC_P_78:D1","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSD-04","text":"Certain science does not require the total immutability of the thing or the necessity of the event, but a stable formal account or a causal order sufficiently determined to an effect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-01","CL_GGVC_29:GA-11","CL_GGVC_29:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_76:D2","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_78:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSD-05","text":"Limits are generative: abstraction excludes direct cognition of the singular but makes reflection possible; succession removes comprehension of the infinite but establishes indefinitely extensible progress.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","CL_GGVC_29:GA-09","CL_GGVC_29:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_78:D4","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:BSD-06","text":"The human scale of cognition is enclosed neither by lower nor higher causality: sensibles determine the matter, intellect actualizes intelligibility, higher light founds, while eternity supplies a comparative limit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-03","CL_GGVC_29:GA-04","CL_GGVC_29:GA-05","CL_GGVC_29:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_76:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_76:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_78:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RLE-01","text":"The principal circuit is: sensible thing → sensitive operation → phantasm → active abstraction → intelligible species → universal understanding → turning to phantasm → judgment concerning the singular.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A9","CH_GGVC_P_76:A8","CH_GGVC_P_77:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RLE-02","text":"The path of amplification proceeds from potency to confused apprehension, distinction of parts, composition of relations, reasoning, and resolution to principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A11","CH_GGVC_P_77:A13","CH_GGVC_P_77:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RLE-03","text":"The attractor of the whole structure is the mean of the human intellect: every transcendence above sense returns to dependence on it, while every dependence is transcended through abstraction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RLE-04","text":"The circuit of matter and form distributes the mode of science: matter produces singularity and contingency, form universality and necessity, while application joins both in judgment concerning the thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:A10","CH_GGVC_P_78:D6","CH_GGVC_P_78:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RLE-05","text":"The infinite becomes a spiral attractor, not a possessed terminus: negation of simultaneous totality always sends the intellect on to a further act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:A7","CH_GGVC_P_78:D4","CH_GGVC_P_78:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:RLE-06","text":"A series of exclusions reduces cognition to mediation: whenever essence, innatism, separate form, or immediate foreknowledge promise a short path, the system restores intermediate causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_77:D1","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FGS-B01","text":"Constitutive state: the soul is the form of the body + the intellect is formally non-organic → a proper object proportioned to material nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A2","CH_GGVC_P_76:A4","CH_GGVC_P_76:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FGS-B02","text":"Material entry: sensible thing → alteration of the sensitive composite → singular phantasm, which begins cognition but does not complete it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A9","CH_GGVC_P_76:A10","CH_GGVC_P_77:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FGS-B03","text":"Formal transformation: illumination by the agent intellect → abstraction of individual conditions → intelligible species → actualization of the possible intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A6","CH_GGVC_P_78:A2","CH_GGVC_P_76:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FGS-B04","text":"Objective relation: species as formal medium → nature of the thing as the first understood → universality in the intellect → persistence of nature in singulars.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A9","CH_GGVC_P_77:A10","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FGS-B05","text":"Discursive explication: confused apprehension → distinction → composition or division → reasoning → truth or falsehood according to the conformity of ordering with the thing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A11","CH_GGVC_P_77:A13","CH_GGVC_P_77:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FGS-B06","text":"Terminal distribution: universal directly → singular reflexively → infinite successively → contingent under a formal aspect → future in causes → intuition of all time outside the human mode.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_78:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AFM-B01","text":"Science of bodies vindicated → confusion of the mode of being and knowing dissolved → false means excluded → sensitive-intellectual mediation constituted.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_76:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_76:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AFM-B02","text":"Proportion of object and power → abstraction from phantasms → species as medium → universal as direct object → turning to the singular.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A1","CH_GGVC_P_77:A5","CH_GGVC_P_77:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AFM-B03","text":"Potency → confused cognition → distinction → composition → discourse → truth or error → resolution to principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:A11","CH_GGVC_P_77:A13","CH_GGVC_P_77:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:AFM-B04","text":"Limits of abstraction → reflexive singularity → successive infinite → formally knowable contingency → causally foreknowable future → graduated closure of human access.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:MRT7","CH_GGVC_P_78:D6","CH_GGVC_P_78:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MME-01","text":"The body is reclassified from an extrinsic impediment into a constitutive condition of cognition, without reducing the intellect to an organ.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_77:ME7","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MME-02","text":"Abstraction is reclassified from separation of the thing into passing over non-essential conditions, which at once constitutes universality and requires a return to the singular.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_77:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MME-03","text":"The intelligible species is reclassified from a terminal object into a formal medium, whereby the immanence of form and the exteriority of the thing can be thought together.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_77:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MME-04","text":"The opposition of universal and singular is resolved into the distinction between direct and reflexive access; epistemic universality is compatible with ontological singularity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD7","CH_GGVC_P_78:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MME-05","text":"The opposition of necessity and contingency is reordered: science follows the necessity of formal account, the event material contingency, but foreknowledge the degree of causal determination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_78:A10","CH_GGVC_P_78:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MME-06","text":"The infinite, time, and priority cease to be simple totalities: the infinite is inexhaustible succession, the time of cognition an order of acts, and priority a relation to diverse orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_77:D9","CH_GGVC_P_77:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level examines cognition of bodies, abstraction, species, the order of operations, and access to singulars, infinities, contingents, and future things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_77:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_78:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level orders cognition through the proportion of object and power, the cooperation of sense and intellect, abstraction, turning, succession, and stratified causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_78:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_76:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level replaces affirmative or negative questioning with distinctions of object, medium, mode, order, and certainty, by which apparent oppositions are resolved into graduated accesses.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_77:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level makes matter the principle of singularity and contingency, form the principle of necessary intelligibility, succession the human mode, and eternity the divine presence of all time.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_78:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological and performative level accomplishes through its own series of distinctions what it asserts: it fragments totalizing errors, restores mediations, and distributes objects according to diverse modes of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_78:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FL-01","text":"The formal independence of the intellect from an organ and its actual dependence on bodily disposition cohere only if the causality of operation is strictly distinguished from the condition of exercise.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_77:SD9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FL-02","text":"The proper object is said to be nature existing in matter, yet it is directly known as abstracted from individual matter; turning to the phantasm contains the tension rather than fully explaining the mechanism of reflection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_78:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FL-03","text":"The necessity of the agent intellect is firm, but the manner in which illumination of the phantasm causes the species in the possible intellect remains mechanically underdetermined.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_77:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:FL-04","text":"Infallibility concerning quiddity and fallibility of composition are preserved by a distinction of operations, but the criterion by which a simple definition is distinguished from intrusive composition requires further structure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT8","CH_GGVC_P_77:A15","CH_GGVC_P_77:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MP-01","text":"A form received immaterially is transformed into nature considered through abstraction; the principle of reception becomes the principle of selective operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A1","CH_GGVC_P_77:A7","CH_GGVC_P_77:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MP-02","text":"The intelligible species is transformed from the apparent terminus of cognition into the formal medium through which the thing is known and the unity of act is measured.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_77:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_77:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MP-03","text":"The body is transformed from an impediment to cognition into a constitutive condition, but this same transformation retains degrees of weakness and bodily caused impediment.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_77:ME7","CH_GGVC_P_76:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MP-04","text":"Necessity is transformed from an attribute of an immobile thing into the formal account of science of contingent things, while future things are again modally distributed according to the degree of necessity of the cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_78:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_78:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:HSI-01","text":"Reality implicitly appears as a hylomorphic machine of turning with a feedback circuit: matter provides the singular beginning, form constitutes intelligibility, while the intellect returns to the phantasm.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_77:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_78:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a potential threshold between sense and intellect, not a center already complete in act; its identity is constituted by transductive operation and orders of access.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:A7","CH_GGVC_P_77:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_78:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:HSI-03","text":"Human time is serial, recursive, and resolutive; divine eternity does not continue it, but encompasses it extrinsically as the simultaneity of the whole course.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_77:NX10","CH_GGVC_P_77:A18","CH_GGVC_P_78:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:HSI-04","text":"The power of acting remains a navigation between universal account and particular apprehension, between causal certainty and conjecture, not an escape from mediations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_78:D3","CH_GGVC_P_78:A13","CH_GGVC_P_78:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of proportion, material object, abstraction, formal species, turning, succession, and causality suffice to restore the machinery of this collection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_78:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest coherence lies in the double movement of abstraction and turning, and in the distinctions between thing and species, direct and reflexive, necessary form and contingent event.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_78:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B03","text":"The mode of causal abstraction, the terminus of reflection upon the singular, and the exact coordination of higher light with determination from sensibles are not yet sufficiently determined.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement: science of bodies is established, immediate shortcuts are excluded, recursive mediation is constructed, operations are explained, and limits are converted into positive modes of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT8","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_78:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B05","text":"The internal explanation of abstraction, the criterion of reflection, and the discrimination of extraordinary impressions resist axiomatic reduction; these nevertheless affect local joints, not the principal generative structure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_76:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_77:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_78:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_29@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_29:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 85–87","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 84 · Prima pars · Quaestio 85 · Prima pars · Quaestio 86"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 88–90","order":30,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_79","CH_GGVC_P_80","CH_GGVC_P_81"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_30@R001","sha256":"27aeda5672f1dbfef1a95e892e1fa4c29774528700446fe583348c5a853ee26f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo una anima intellectiva seipsam et alia cognoscat, dum a statu corporali per limitem immaterialium ad statum separationis transit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_80:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_81:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-02","text":"Cognitio humana globaliter regitur actualitate obiecti, proportione facultatis ad obiectum et modo essendi ex quo modus operandi sequitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_80:A3","CH_GGVC_P_81:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-03","text":"In statu corporali exterioritas materialis intellectum actualizat, ex cuius actu reflexio ad intellectum, habitus et voluntatem redit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_80:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-04","text":"Eodem statu substantiae immateriales non quidditative sed indirecte, per effectus, relationes, rationes communes et remotionem attinguntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-05","text":"Separatio naturam animae non substituit, sed eius modum essendi mutat atque novum regimen intellectionis sine phantasmatibus constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_81:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-06","text":"Argumentatio fit metaphysico-reconstructiva et dialectica, quia disiunctiones absolutas in distinctiones status, proportionis, speciei, modi et ordinationis resolvit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_81:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-07","text":"Motus est reflexivus, cumulativus et mutatione status catastrophicus: reditus ad se per actum terminatur in recodificatione operationis post mortem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_81:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-08","text":"Clausura est stratificata et conditionalis: cognitio naturalis manet limitata in utroque statu, dum plena visio Dei ordini gratuito reservatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:NX16","CH_GGVC_P_81:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Quidquid cognoscitur, cognoscitur secundum aliquam actualitatem obiecti et actualizationem potentiae cognoscentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:A1","CH_GGVC_P_81:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Qualitas cognitionis non ex sola dignitate obiecti, sed ex proportione eius ad facultatem et ex modo recipientis determinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:A3","CH_GGVC_P_81:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Modus operandi intellectus sequitur modum essendi animae, ideo status ontologicus structuram cognitionis constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Natura animae persistit inter unionem et separationem, sed eadem natura modos cognitionis substantialiter diversos exercet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A2","CH_GGVC_P_81:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] In vita praesenti obiectum primum intellectus est natura rei materialis, ad quam intellectus per phantasmata convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:A11","CH_GGVC_P_80:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Interiora animae in statu corporali per actus suos manifestantur, non per nudam praesentiam essentiae vel habitus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:A7","CH_GGVC_P_80:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Corpus est instrumentum epistemicum cognitionis propriae et determinatae, non simplex impedimentum intellectus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A8","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Abstractio materialis formas materiales intelligibiles reddit, sed non continenter transit in apprehensionem quidditatum separatarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Anima separata per species participatas intelligit, sed universalitas luminis secundum capacitatem recipientis restringitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A5","CH_GGVC_P_81:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Continuitas scientiae trans mortem fundatur in permanentia habitus intellectualis sub mutatione modi usus, non in permanentia apparatus sensitivi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A12","CH_GGVC_P_81:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Causalitas divina potest cognitionem naturalem fundare quin Deus fiat primum obiectum cognitum aut omnis influxus divinus fiat gratia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:A12","CH_GGVC_P_81:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Nullus status naturalis animae visionem Dei vel perfectionem beatificam ex se producit; terminus plenus ordine gratuito supervenit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:NX16","CH_GGVC_P_81:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-01","text":"Actualitas identitatem regulativam servat, sed ab actualizatione intellectus ad actualem entitatem obiecti et exclusionem futurorum nondum existentium extenditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_81:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-02","text":"Proportio redit functionaliter mutata: primo ordinem obiecti materialis constituit, deinde accessum ad immaterialia limitat, postremo cognitionem separatae animae graduat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_81:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-03","text":"Conversio ad phantasmata ex lege praesentis status in terminum cessantem mutatur; necessitas eius operationalis est, non essentialis omni intellectioni.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:A2","CH_GGVC_P_81:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-04","text":"Cognitio sui invertitur ab actu mediante, quo anima se intelligere percipit, ad cognitionem sui per seipsam in separatione.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:D1","CH_GGVC_P_81:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-05","text":"Corpus ab organo quod reflexionem praesentem condicionat in bonum epistemicum reclassificatur, cuius amissio libertatem operationalem simul et indeterminationem auget.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-06","text":"Abstractio evanescit ut scala universalis ad immaterialia et absorbetur ut machina localis cognitionis materialis, cui post mortem alius modus specierum succedit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:A6","CH_GGVC_P_81:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-07","text":"Lumen divinum migrat a causa facultatis non primo cognita ad fontem specierum participatarum, manente distinctione cognitionis naturalis a gratia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_81:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-08","text":"Habitus primo per actum cognoscitur, deinde ipse fit principium continuitatis quod mortem intellectualiter superat, licet modum manifestationis amittat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:A9","CH_GGVC_P_81:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-09","text":"Distantia a mensura locali ad distinctionem actualitatis, habitudinis et ordinationis transfertur; spatium vim explicativam amittit, tempus ontologicam retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:D12","CH_GGVC_P_81:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:CGD-01","text":"Cognitio habitus et voluntatis manet explicatio localis interioritatis, sed hoc gradu exemplificat legem globalem qua principia animae per operationes manifestantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_79:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:CGD-02","text":"Refutationes formarum Platonicarum et unionis Averroisticae sunt locales, sed in exclusionem globalem cuiuslibet accessus ex sola similitudine vel participatione luminis amplificantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_80:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:CGD-03","text":"Determinationes singularium post mortem sunt locales; ad structuram praesentem ascendit tantum regula qua habitudo particularis universalitatem medii restringit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:CGD-04","text":"Dissensio de cognitione beatorum res gestas viventium non fit axioma globale, sed indicat clausuram per ordinationem divinam nondum demonstrative stabilitam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:D16","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:CGD-05","text":"Mutatio corporis ex conditione reflexionis in absentiam post mortem unitates coniungit et transitum inter duo regimina cognitionis efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:CGD-06","text":"Difficultas regressus reflexivi infiniti manet localis, quia hoc gradu absorbetur in prioritatem structuralem actus obiecto actualizati.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:CGD-07","text":"Differentia angelorum et Dei ampliatur in legem anti-univocam: gradus superioris intelligibilitatis non componunt unam scalam aequaliter perviam intellectui humano.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_80:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-01","text":"Systema excludit subiectum humanum cui existentia, essentia, actus et cognitio sui uno intuitu transparentia sint.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-02","text":"Excluditur intellectus humanus tamquam facultas universaliter proportionata omni intelligibili, sive corporali sive separato.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-03","text":"Excluditur corpus ut malum extrinsecum vel carcer cuius sola remotio plenitudinem cognitionis restituat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-04","text":"Excluditur mors sive consummatio epistemica absoluta sive deletio omnis intellectionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-05","text":"Excluditur abstractio indefinita quae, materia successive remota, quidditatem substantiae separatae positive producat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-06","text":"Excluditur conversio fundamenti divini cognitionis in primum obiectum atque conversio omnis originis divinae in cognitionem supernaturalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_81:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-07","text":"Excluditur identitas inter speciem intelligibilem persistentem et invariabilem modum intelligendi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-08","text":"Excluditur topologia cognitionis incorporeae secundum propinquitatem et distantiam localem constituta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet naturam intellectivam unum modum operationis immobiliter imponere, quia aliter diversitas statuum sine mutatione naturae intelligi non posset.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet obiectum ontologice nobilius cognitionem semper perfectiorem efficere; perfectio dependet etiam a recipientis capacitate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_80:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet praesentiam rei in anima vel identitatem subiecti cum principio cognitivo sufficere ad cognitionem quidditativam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_79:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet scientiam esse possessionem representationum a statu, organis et modo actualizationis independentem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_81:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet animam humanam naturaliter completam esse extra corpus, quia hoc unionem corporis ab eius bono separaret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet causalitatem divinam et naturalitatem operationis esse genera mutuo exclusiva.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSA-01","text":"Systema circum absentiam mensurae univocae cognitionis divinae, angelicae et humanae ordinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSA-02","text":"Indisponibilis est scala continua qua abstractio a materia sine mutatione regiminis ad increatum perveniat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSA-03","text":"Indisponibilis est communis praesentia naturalis viventium et mortuorum; communicatio eorum mediatione vel ordinatione speciali indiget.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSA-04","text":"Systema cognitionem sui omnino liberam ab exterioritate materiali desiderabilem quidem supponit, sed praesentis vitae intellectui naturaliter negat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSA-05","text":"Impossibilis est terminus naturalis in quo sola separatio plenitudinem intellectualem et beatitudinem simul producat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_81:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-01","text":"Privilegium cognitionis propriae animae non ex communi immaterialitate, sed ex adaequatione actus proprii ad principium quod manifestat oritur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_80:D1","CL_GGVC_30:GA-02","CL_GGVC_30:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-02","text":"Necessitas phantasmatum proprietas status corporalis est, non definitio absoluta intellectus humani, quia anima separata intellectionem alio modo retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:A2","CH_GGVC_P_81:D1","CL_GGVC_30:GA-03","CL_GGVC_30:GA-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-03","text":"Disembodimentum formalem immediationem auget, sed determinationem cognitionis minuit; nobilitas modi et perfectio effectus igitur dissociantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_81:D3","CL_GGVC_30:GA-02","CL_GGVC_30:GA-07","CL_GGVC_30:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-04","text":"Medium cognoscitivum universaliter causale non efficit accessum universaliter aequalem, quia receptio et habitudo obiecta determinate distribuunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A10","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD6","CL_GGVC_30:GA-02","CL_GGVC_30:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-05","text":"Habitus prius per actum suum deprehensus postea fit vehiculum continuitatis, quamquam actus eius post mortem alio regimine elicitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:D4","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD4","CL_GGVC_30:GA-06","CL_GGVC_30:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-06","text":"Causalitas divina duplicem errorem prohibet: nec causa luminis primum obiectum fit, nec influxus naturalis propter originem divinam in gratiam convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD5","CL_GGVC_30:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-07","text":"Topologia cognitionis non spatio sed actualitate et ordine relationis regitur; ideo futura nondum existentia aliter absunt quam praesentia localiter remota.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A15","CH_GGVC_P_81:D14","CL_GGVC_30:GA-01","CL_GGVC_30:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-08","text":"Identitas subiecti trans mortem compatitur inversionem accessus ad se: idem intellectus primo per actum, deinde per seipsum cognoscitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:D1","CH_GGVC_P_81:D4","CL_GGVC_30:GA-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSD-01","text":"Subiectum humanum est natura diachronice persistens cuius identitas non ex invariabili modo cognitionis, sed ex capacitate varios modos sustinendi constat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSD-02","text":"Embodimentum non est appendice extrinseca subiecti, sed pars historiae causalis qua intellectus cognitionem determinatam et habitus permanentes acquirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_81:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSD-03","text":"Perfectio epistemica fit relatio multidimensionalis inter nobilitatem medii, capacitatem recipientis, determinationem obiecti et modum operationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_81:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSD-04","text":"Mors est mutatio regiminis cognoscitivi quae simultanee organorum privationem, libertatem a phantasmatibus, diffusionem cognitionis et selective limitatum accessum producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSD-05","text":"Metaphysica humana est scientia asymptotica: realiter ad immaterialia ascendit, sed ascensum per negationes et habitudines ordinat potius quam quidditates exhibet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:D2","CH_GGVC_P_80:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSD-06","text":"Tempus acquirit structuram ontologicam cognitionis, quia futurum non tantum remotum sed nondum actu ens est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A15","CH_GGVC_P_81:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSD-07","text":"Potentia cognoscitiva animae separatae navigatur per habitum et ordinationem, non per inspectionem indifferenter universalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_81:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RLE-01","text":"Obiectum materiale intellectum actualizat, actus intellectum manifestat, intellectus ad se reflectitur, atque reflexio ad actum tamquam conditionem sui redit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_79:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RLE-02","text":"Habitus actum producit, actus habitum manifestat, cognitio habitus usum dirigit, et usus novum actum elicit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:A9","CH_GGVC_P_79:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RLE-03","text":"Abstractio materialia intelligibilia reddit, limitem immaterialium detegit, remotionem et causalitatem instituit, quae rursus proportionem intellectus finiti confirmant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_80:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RLE-04","text":"Cognitio corporalis species et habitus format, mors dispositiones sensitivas tollit, habitus intellectualis persistit, idemque obiectum novo modo intelligitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_81:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RLE-05","text":"Lumen universale secundum recipiens contrahitur, contractio cognitionem communem vel selectivam producit, atque limitatio novam dependentiam ab ordinatione efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_81:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RLE-06","text":"Separatio a phantasmatibus liberat, sed cognitionem rerum naturalium confundit; confusio ad habitus acquisitos et habitudines determinatas tamquam stabilimenta recurrit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_81:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RLE-07","text":"Actualitas, proportio et modus essendi sunt attractores ad quos quaestiones de interioritate, transcendentia, morte, tempore et memoria iteratim reducuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_81:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B01","text":"Actualitas obiecti + actualitas potentiae → operatio → determinatus modus cognoscendi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B02","text":"Status corporalis → conversio ad phantasmata → forma materialis intelligibilis → actus intellectus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_81:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B03","text":"Actus intellectus → perceptio operationis → cognitio reflexiva potentiae → investigatio habitus et naturae animae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B04","text":"Disproportio ad immateriale → interdictio quidditatis propriae → effectus, relatio et remotio → cognitio indirecta finita.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_80:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B05","text":"Separatio → cessatio phantasmatum → species participatae → receptio secundum capacitatem → cognitio sui directa, aliorum graduata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A5","CH_GGVC_P_81:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B06","text":"Permanentia habitus intellectualis + mutatio modi usus → continuitas asymmetrica scientiae → clausura cognitionis naturalis sub ordine gratiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_81:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:AFM-B01","text":"Actualitas cognoscibilis → status corporalis → phantasmata → obiectum materiale → actus intelligendi → reflexio → cognitio interioritatis mediata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_79:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:AFM-B02","text":"Obiectum materiale proportionatum → limes abstractionis → disproportio immaterialium → cognitio per effectus et remotionem → negatio comprehensionis quidditativae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_80:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:AFM-B03","text":"Mors → mutatio modi essendi → cessatio conversionis ad phantasmata → species participatae → cognitio diffusior sed minus determinata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:AFM-B04","text":"Habitus acquisitus → permanentia intellectualis → novus modus usus → cognitio selectiva secundum habitudinem → terminus sub ordinatione divina.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_81:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MME-01","text":"Oppositio exterioris et interioris dissolvitur, quia exterior materialis intellectum actualizat et interiora per actus ex hac actualizatione emergentes cognoscuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_79:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MME-02","text":"Oppositio materialis et immaterialis desinit esse simplex scala dignitatis atque fit distributio proportionum inter obiecta et modos recipientium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_81:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MME-03","text":"Corpus reclassificatur ex obstaculo extrinseco in condicionem cognitionis propriae, acquisitionis habituum et determinationis intellectualis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MME-04","text":"Mors reclassificatur ex mera corruptione in mutationem regiminis quae continuitatem sub discontinuo modo operationis servat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MME-05","text":"Oppositio cognitionis naturalis et causationis divinae corruit; discrimen transfertur ad modum participationis et ordinem finis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MME-06","text":"Distantia reclassificatur ex quantitate spatiali in compositionem actualitatis, proportionis, habitudinis et ordinationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:ME7","CH_GGVC_P_81:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiali distribuuntur cognitio sui, habituum, voluntatis, substantiarum immaterialium, animae separatae, singularium et viventium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali operantur actualitas, phantasmata, species, proportio, habitus, modus recipientis et ordinatio divina.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_81:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico quaestio a possessione immediata obiecti ad condiciones accessus, mediationis et manifestationis transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_81:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali eadem natura varios modos operationis recipit, dum actualitas separat praesentia remota a futuris nondum entibus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_81:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico forma distinctionum ipsam thesim efficit: oppositiones absolutae iteratim solvuntur per separationem naturae, status, speciei, modi et finis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FL-01","text":"Persistentia eiusdem naturae cum diversitate modorum operandi requirit conceptum status satis efficacem ad mutationem explicandam, sed sine mutatione essentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A2","CH_GGVC_P_81:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FL-02","text":"Negatio praesentis cognitionis per se substantiarum immaterialium cum futura cognitione angelorum componitur per mutationem status, cuius mechanismus tamen tantum generaliter describitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:D1","CH_GGVC_P_81:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FL-03","text":"Modus separatus est simpliciter nobilior, sed cognitionem in anima humana imperfectiorem efficere potest; relativitas ad recipiens tensionem ordinat, non penitus dissolvit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:D3","CH_GGVC_P_81:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FL-04","text":"Unio corporis est naturalis et bona, quamquam separatio libertatem ab organis confert; bonum naturae et amplitudo operationis non perfecte coincidunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A3","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FL-05","text":"Species ex causa universali singularia repraesentare possunt, sed cognitio singularium selectiva manet; principium determinationis non mechanice explicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A10","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FL-06","text":"Scientia formaliter persistit, sed identitas eius sub amissione dispositionum sensitivarum et mutatione usus distinctione valida quidem, at compressa, sustentatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FL-07","text":"Cognitio beatorum de viventibus manet commissura auctoritative inclinata ad Gregorium, non demonstrative clausa contra dubitationem Augustini.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:D16","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MP-01","text":"Notio praesentiae mutatur ab interioritate quae actum ad sui cognitionem requirit in praesentiam separatae animae sibi per se intelligibilem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:D1","CH_GGVC_P_81:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MP-02","text":"Actualitas a regula cognoscibilitatis et actualizationis potentiae in criterium temporale convertitur quo futura nondum entia excluduntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_81:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MP-03","text":"Lumen divinum mutat functionem a causa facultatis non primo obiectivata in fontem specierum separatae animae, manente limite recipientis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_81:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina conversionis in qua actualitas, modus essendi et modus recipiendi obiecta in operationes cognoscitivas traducunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A1","CH_GGVC_P_81:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est idem principium intellectuale per interfaces mutabiles: corpus obiecta determinat, actus interiora manifestat, habitus continuitatem servat, lumen separatum novum accessum aperit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A2","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est campus actualitatis discontinuae, cuius maxima ruptura mors regimen cognitionis mutat, futurum autem cognitioni naturali nondum praebet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A15","CH_GGVC_P_81:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est inspectio universalis, sed transitus per proportionem, habitum, habitudinem et ordinationem; fuga a limite cognitionis in alium limitem recodificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_81:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata actualitatis, proportionis, modi essendi, status et distinctionis speciei a modo sufficiunt ad duo regimina cognitionis et transitum eorum restituenda.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus quo actualitas et modus essendi operationem determinant, proportio autem qualitatem cognitionis calibrat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinatus manet mechanismus impressionis specierum participatarum atque transitus earum in cognitionem communem, confusam vel selectivam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B04","text":"Systema hac magnitudine cohaeret non per progressionem linearem, sed per bifurcationem status, reditum axiomatum sub functionibus mutatis et conservationem asymmetricam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B05","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae resistunt discrimen praesentiae existentialis a quidditate, identitas scientiae sub mutatione usus et clausura auctoritative incompleta cognitionis beatorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T172704894882Z_1e364829","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 88–90","source_sha256":"27aeda5672f1dbfef1a95e892e1fa4c29774528700446fe583348c5a853ee26f","sha256":"46871d8b40644b44ccf11d985fc1d335e8042cbcc0f00c6f26ba7362dda2b2e2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:27:04.895792+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how one intellectual soul knows itself and others while passing from the bodily state, through the boundary of immaterial beings, to the state of separation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_80:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_81:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-02","text":"Human cognition is globally governed by the actuality of the object, the proportion of the faculty to the object, and the mode of being from which the mode of operating follows.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_80:A3","CH_GGVC_P_81:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-03","text":"In the bodily state, material exteriority actualizes the intellect, from whose act reflection returns to the intellect, habits, and will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_80:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-04","text":"In the same state, immaterial substances are attained not quidditatively but indirectly, through effects, relations, common notions, and removal.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-05","text":"Separation does not replace the nature of the soul, but changes its mode of being and establishes a new regime of intellection without phantasms.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_81:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-06","text":"The argument proceeds metaphysically-reconstructively and dialectically, because it resolves absolute disjunctions into distinctions of state, proportion, species, mode, and ordering.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_81:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-07","text":"The movement is reflexive, cumulative, and catastrophic through change of state: the return to itself through act terminates in the recodification of operation after death.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_81:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BAS-08","text":"The closure is stratified and conditional: natural cognition remains limited in both states, while the full vision of God is reserved for the gratuitous order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:NX16","CH_GGVC_P_81:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Whatever is known is known according to some actuality of the object and actualization of the knowing power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:A1","CH_GGVC_P_81:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The quality of cognition is determined not by the dignity of the object alone, but by its proportion to the faculty and by the mode of the recipient.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:A3","CH_GGVC_P_81:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] The mode of operating of the intellect follows the mode of being of the soul; therefore, ontological state constitutes the structure of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The nature of the soul persists between union and separation, but the same nature exercises substantially different modes of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A2","CH_GGVC_P_81:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] In the present life, the primary object of the intellect is the nature of a material thing, toward which the intellect turns through phantasms.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:A11","CH_GGVC_P_80:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] The interior principles of the soul in the bodily state are manifested through their acts, not through the bare presence of essence or habit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:A7","CH_GGVC_P_80:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The body is an epistemic instrument of proper and determinate cognition, not a mere impediment to the intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A8","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Material abstraction renders material forms intelligible, but does not continuously pass into the apprehension of separate quiddities.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] The separated soul understands through participated species, but the universality of light is restricted according to the capacity of the recipient.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A5","CH_GGVC_P_81:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] The continuity of knowledge across death is founded on the permanence of intellectual habit under a change in the mode of use, not on the permanence of the sensitive apparatus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A12","CH_GGVC_P_81:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Divine causality can ground natural cognition without God becoming the first object known or every divine influx becoming grace.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:A12","CH_GGVC_P_81:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] No natural state of the soul produces from itself the vision of God or beatific perfection; the full term supervenes in the gratuitous order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:NX16","CH_GGVC_P_81:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-01","text":"Actuality preserves its regulative identity, but extends from the actualization of the intellect to the actual entity of the object and the exclusion of future things not yet existing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_81:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-02","text":"Proportion returns with a changed function: first it constitutes the order of the material object, then it limits access to immaterial beings, and finally it grades the cognition of the separated soul.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_81:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-03","text":"The turning toward phantasms changes from a law of the present state into a condition that ceases; its necessity is operational, not essential to all intellection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:A2","CH_GGVC_P_81:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-04","text":"Self-knowledge is inverted from the mediate act by which the soul perceives that it understands itself to knowledge of itself through itself in separation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:D1","CH_GGVC_P_81:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-05","text":"The body is reclassified from an organ conditioning present reflection into an epistemic good, whose loss simultaneously increases operational freedom and indetermination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-06","text":"Abstraction vanishes as a universal ladder to immaterial beings and is absorbed as a local mechanism of material cognition, to which another mode of species succeeds after death.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:A6","CH_GGVC_P_81:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-07","text":"Divine light migrates from a not-primarily-known cause of the faculty to a source of participated species, while the distinction of natural cognition from grace remains.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_81:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-08","text":"Habit is first known through act; then it itself becomes a principle of continuity that intellectually survives death, although it loses its mode of manifestation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:A9","CH_GGVC_P_81:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RMD-09","text":"Distance is transferred from local measure to a distinction of actuality, relation, and ordering; space loses explanatory force, while time retains ontological force.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:D12","CH_GGVC_P_81:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:CGD-01","text":"Knowledge of habit and will remains a local explanation of interiority, but at this level it exemplifies the global law by which the principles of the soul are manifested through operations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_79:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:CGD-02","text":"Refutations of Platonic forms and Averroistic unity are local, but are amplified into the global exclusion of every access through mere likeness or participation in light.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_80:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:CGD-03","text":"The determinations of singulars after death are local; only the rule by which particular relation restricts the universality of the medium rises to the present structure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:CGD-04","text":"The disagreement concerning the knowledge of the blessed of the affairs of the living does not become a global axiom, but indicates a closure through divine ordering not yet demonstratively established.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:D16","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:CGD-05","text":"The change of the body from a condition of reflection to absence after death connects units and effects the transition between two regimes of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:CGD-06","text":"The difficulty of infinite reflexive regress remains local, because at this level it is absorbed into the structural priority of the act actualized by the object.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:CGD-07","text":"The difference between angels and God is expanded into an anti-univocal law: degrees of higher intelligibility do not compose one scale equally accessible to the human intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_80:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-01","text":"A human subject for whom existence, essence, act, and self-knowledge are transparent in a single intuition is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-02","text":"The human intellect is excluded as a faculty universally proportioned to every intelligible, whether bodily or separate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-03","text":"The body is excluded as an extrinsic evil or prison whose mere removal restores the fullness of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-04","text":"Death is excluded as either absolute epistemic consummation or the destruction of all intellection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-05","text":"Indefinite abstraction is excluded, which, with matter successively removed, would positively produce the quiddity of a separate substance.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-06","text":"The conversion of the divine foundation of cognition into the first object, and the conversion of every divine origin into supernatural cognition, are excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_81:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-07","text":"The identity between the persistent intelligible species and an invariable mode of understanding is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:GNX-08","text":"A topology of incorporeal cognition constituted according to local proximity and distance is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that intellectual nature immovably imposes one mode of operation, because otherwise the diversity of states without a change of nature could not be understood.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that an ontologically nobler object always makes cognition more perfect; perfection also depends on the capacity of the recipient.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_80:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that the presence of a thing in the soul or the identity of the subject with the cognitive principle suffices for quidditative cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_79:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that knowledge is the possession of representations independent of state, organs, and mode of actualization.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_81:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the human soul is naturally complete outside the body, because this would separate bodily union from its good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that divine causality and the naturalness of operation are mutually exclusive genera.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSA-01","text":"The system is ordered around the absence of a univocal measure of divine, angelic, and human cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSA-02","text":"Unavailable is a continuous scale by which abstraction from matter reaches the uncreated without a change of regime.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSA-03","text":"Unavailable is a common natural presence of the living and the dead; their communication requires mediation or special ordering.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSA-04","text":"The system indeed supposes as desirable self-knowledge wholly free from material exteriority, but naturally denies it to the intellect in the present life.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSA-05","text":"Impossible is a natural term in which separation alone produces simultaneously intellectual fullness and beatitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_81:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-01","text":"The privilege of the soul's self-knowledge arises not from common immateriality, but from the adequation of a proper act to the principle it manifests.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_80:D1","CL_GGVC_30:GA-02","CL_GGVC_30:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-02","text":"The necessity of phantasms is a property of the bodily state, not an absolute definition of the human intellect, because the separated soul retains intellection in another mode.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:A2","CH_GGVC_P_81:D1","CL_GGVC_30:GA-03","CL_GGVC_30:GA-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-03","text":"Disembodiment increases formal immediacy, but diminishes the determination of cognition; therefore, nobility of mode and perfection of effect are dissociated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_81:D3","CL_GGVC_30:GA-02","CL_GGVC_30:GA-07","CL_GGVC_30:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-04","text":"A universally causal cognitive medium does not effect universally equal access, because reception and relation distribute objects determinately.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A10","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD6","CL_GGVC_30:GA-02","CL_GGVC_30:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-05","text":"A habit first apprehended through its act subsequently becomes a vehicle of continuity, although its act is elicited after death under another regime.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:D4","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD4","CL_GGVC_30:GA-06","CL_GGVC_30:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-06","text":"Divine causality prevents a double error: neither does the cause of light become the first object, nor is natural influx converted into grace on account of its divine origin.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD5","CL_GGVC_30:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-07","text":"The topology of cognition is governed not by space but by actuality and the order of relation; therefore, future things not yet existing are absent differently from things locally remote but present.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A15","CH_GGVC_P_81:D14","CL_GGVC_30:GA-01","CL_GGVC_30:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:XD-08","text":"The identity of the subject across death is compatible with an inversion of access to itself: the same intellect is known first through act, then through itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:D1","CH_GGVC_P_81:D4","CL_GGVC_30:GA-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSD-01","text":"The human subject is a diachronically persistent nature whose identity consists not in an invariable mode of cognition, but in the capacity to sustain diverse modes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSD-02","text":"Embodiment is not an extrinsic appendage of the subject, but part of the causal history through which the intellect acquires determinate cognition and permanent habits.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_81:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSD-03","text":"Epistemic perfection becomes a multidimensional relation among the nobility of the medium, the capacity of the recipient, the determination of the object, and the mode of operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_81:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSD-04","text":"Death is a change of cognitive regime that simultaneously produces deprivation of organs, freedom from phantasms, diffusion of cognition, and selectively limited access.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSD-05","text":"Human metaphysics is an asymptotic science: it truly ascends to immaterial beings, but orders the ascent through negations and relations rather than exhibiting quiddities.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:D2","CH_GGVC_P_80:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSD-06","text":"Time acquires an ontological structure of cognition, because the future is not merely remote but not yet actually being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A15","CH_GGVC_P_81:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:BSD-07","text":"The cognitive power of the separated soul is navigated through habit and ordering, not through an indifferently universal inspection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_81:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RLE-01","text":"The material object actualizes the intellect, the act manifests the intellect, the intellect reflects upon itself, and reflection returns to the act as its condition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_79:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RLE-02","text":"Habit produces act, act manifests habit, knowledge of habit directs use, and use elicits a new act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:A9","CH_GGVC_P_79:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RLE-03","text":"Abstraction renders material things intelligible, detects the limit of immaterial beings, establishes removal and causality, which in turn confirm the proportion of the finite intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_80:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RLE-04","text":"Bodily cognition forms species and habits, death removes sensitive dispositions, intellectual habit persists, and the same object is understood in a new mode.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_81:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RLE-05","text":"Universal light is contracted according to the recipient, contraction produces common or selective cognition, and limitation effects a new dependence on ordering.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_81:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RLE-06","text":"Separation from phantasms liberates, but confuses cognition of natural things; confusion recurs to acquired habits and determinate relations as stabilizers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_81:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:RLE-07","text":"Actuality, proportion, and mode of being are attractors to which questions concerning interiority, transcendence, death, time, and memory are repeatedly brought back.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_81:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B01","text":"Actuality of object + actuality of power → operation → determinate mode of knowing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B02","text":"Bodily state → turning toward phantasms → intelligible material form → act of intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_81:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B03","text":"Act of intellect → perception of operation → reflexive cognition of power → investigation of habit and nature of the soul.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B04","text":"Disproportion to the immaterial → prohibition of proper quiddity → effect, relation, and removal → finite indirect cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_80:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B05","text":"Separation → cessation of phantasms → participated species → reception according to capacity → direct self-knowledge, graded knowledge of others.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A5","CH_GGVC_P_81:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B06","text":"Permanence of intellectual habit + change in mode of use → asymmetric continuity of knowledge → closure of natural cognition under the order of grace.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_81:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:AFM-B01","text":"Knowable actuality → bodily state → phantasms → material object → act of understanding → reflection → mediated knowledge of interiority.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_79:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:AFM-B02","text":"Proportioned material object → limit of abstraction → disproportion of immaterial beings → cognition through effects and removal → negation of quidditative comprehension.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_80:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:AFM-B03","text":"Death → change of mode of being → cessation of turning toward phantasms → participated species → more diffuse but less determinate cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:AFM-B04","text":"Acquired habit → intellectual permanence → new mode of use → selective cognition according to relation → term under divine ordering.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_81:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MME-01","text":"The opposition of exterior and interior is dissolved, because the material exterior actualizes the intellect and interior principles are known through acts emerging from this actualization.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_79:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MME-02","text":"The opposition of material and immaterial ceases to be a simple scale of dignity and becomes a distribution of proportions among objects and modes of recipients.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_81:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MME-03","text":"The body is reclassified from an extrinsic obstacle into a condition of proper cognition, acquisition of habits, and intellectual determination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MME-04","text":"Death is reclassified from mere corruption into a change of regime that preserves continuity under a discontinuous mode of operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MME-05","text":"The opposition between natural cognition and divine causation collapses; the distinction is transferred to the mode of participation and the order of the end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MME-06","text":"Distance is reclassified from spatial quantity into a composition of actuality, proportion, relation, and ordering.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:ME7","CH_GGVC_P_81:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B01","text":"At the superficial level are distributed knowledge of self, habits, will, immaterial substances, the separated soul, singulars, and the living.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level operate actuality, phantasms, species, proportion, habit, mode of recipient, and divine ordering.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_81:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the question is transferred from immediate possession of the object to conditions of access, mediation, and manifestation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_81:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, the same nature receives diverse modes of operation, while actuality separates remote present things from future things not yet existing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_81:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological level, the form of distinctions itself effects the thesis: absolute oppositions are repeatedly resolved through separation of nature, state, species, mode, and end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FL-01","text":"The persistence of the same nature with diversity of modes of operation requires a concept of state effective enough to explain change, yet without change of essence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A2","CH_GGVC_P_81:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FL-02","text":"The denial of present cognition through itself of immaterial substances is reconciled with future cognition of angels through a change of state, whose mechanism, however, is described only generally.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:D1","CH_GGVC_P_81:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FL-03","text":"The separate mode is simply nobler, but can make cognition more imperfect in the human soul; relativity to the recipient orders the tension, but does not wholly dissolve it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:D3","CH_GGVC_P_81:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FL-04","text":"Union with the body is natural and good, although separation confers freedom from organs; the good of nature and the scope of operation do not perfectly coincide.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A3","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FL-05","text":"Species from a universal cause can represent singulars, but cognition of singulars remains selective; the principle of determination is not mechanically explained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A10","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FL-06","text":"Knowledge formally persists, but its identity under the loss of sensitive dispositions and change of use is sustained by a distinction that is indeed valid, yet compressed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:FL-07","text":"The knowledge of the blessed concerning the living remains a juncture authoritatively inclined toward Gregorium, not demonstratively closed against the doubt of Augustini.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:D16","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MP-01","text":"The notion of presence changes from an interiority requiring act for knowledge of itself into the presence of the separated soul as intelligible to itself through itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:D1","CH_GGVC_P_81:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MP-02","text":"Actuality is converted from a rule of knowability and actualization of power into a temporal criterion by which future things not yet existing are excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_81:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MP-03","text":"Divine light changes function from a cause of the faculty not primarily objectified into a source of the separated soul's species, while the limit of the recipient remains.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_80:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_81:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a machine of conversion in which actuality, mode of being, and mode of receiving translate objects into cognitive operations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A1","CH_GGVC_P_81:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:HSI-02","text":"The subject is the same intellectual principle through mutable interfaces: the body determines objects, act manifests interior principles, habit preserves continuity, and separate light opens new access.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A2","CH_GGVC_P_81:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:HSI-03","text":"Time is a field of discontinuous actuality, whose greatest rupture, death, changes the regime of cognition, while not yet affording the future to natural cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:A15","CH_GGVC_P_81:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is not universal inspection, but passage through proportion, habit, relation, and ordering; escape from the limit of cognition is recodified into another limit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_81:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of actuality, proportion, mode of being, state, and the distinction of species from mode suffice to reconstruct the two regimes of cognition and their transition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the nexus by which actuality and mode of being determine operation, while proportion calibrates the quality of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B03","text":"The mechanism of the impression of participated species and of their transition into common, confused, or selective cognition remains less determinate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B04","text":"At this magnitude, the system coheres not through linear progression, but through bifurcation of state, return of axioms under changed functions, and asymmetric preservation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B05","text":"Resistant to axiomatic reduction are the distinction of existential presence from quiddity, the identity of knowledge under a change of use, and the authoritatively incomplete closure of the cognition of the blessed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_79:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_81:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_30@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_30:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 88–90","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 87 · Prima pars · Quaestio 88 · Prima pars · Quaestio 89"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 91–93","order":31,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_82","CH_GGVC_P_83","CH_GGVC_P_84"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_31@R001","sha256":"ec08158d70e666b869004e29cd9a0c5b615e79d05284462d40717c7374c508eb","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo origo animae, formatio corporis primi et productio mulieris unam institutionem humanae naturae constituant, servatis differentiis materiae, causae, temporis et relationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_83:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_84:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-02","text":"Thesis centralis est modum essendi et finem proprium rei modum productionis ordinare, dum effectus cui praesuppositum creatum sufficere nequit immediatam actionem divinam postulat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_83:A9","CH_GGVC_P_84:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit per distinctiones ordinum, quibus identitas a participatione, creatio a transmutatione, potentia passiva ab activa et intentio universalis a defectu particulari secernuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_84:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-04","text":"Motus est recursivus et mutatione actus: apparentia inferioritatis, defectus vel posterioritatis ad aliam mensuram transfertur atque in convenientiam finalem, intentionem universalem aut defectum posteriorem reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_84:D2","CH_GGVC_P_82:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-05","text":"Subsistentia spiritualis animae non terminatur in separatione, sed cum passibilitate corporis componitur, quia perfectio humana et cognitio rationalis unionem corporis requirunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-06","text":"Immediata causalitas divina analogice variatur: animam sine subiecto creat, primum corpus sine antecedente generativo format, corpus autem mulieris ex materia extraordinaria supra determinationem naturae producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:D5","CH_GGVC_P_83:D5","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-07","text":"Tempus originis disiungitur a tempore reproductionis et defectus: anima et corpus primitus unam naturam constituunt, institutio prima generationem posteriorem excedit, et origo corporea relationes sociales instituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A14","CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-08","text":"Clausura systematis est teleologica et hylomorphica, non naturalistice causalis: nexus modi essendi, modi fiendi et causae stabilis manet, quamvis physica assumpta et transitus ad hierarchiam socialem commissuras fragiles relinquant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_83:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_84:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Modus essendi effectus modum fiendi determinat, atque modus fiendi genus causae sufficientis definit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_82:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Omne esse humanum creatum est participatum, ideo neque simplicitas animae neque intellectualitas eius identitatem cum actu puro inducit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A1","CH_GGVC_P_82:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Natura humana est compositum cui anima rationalis ut forma subsistens et corpus ut materia intrinseca conveniunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A11","CH_GGVC_P_83:A3","CH_GGVC_P_83:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Immediata actio divina requiritur quoties neque subiectum productionis animae, neque forma humana antecedens, neque virtus activa creata effectui sufficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A10","CH_GGVC_P_83:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_84:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Causa creata intra materiam, formam et processum praesupposita operatur; potentia passiva effectum possibilem facit, non causam creatam sufficientem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A8","CH_GGVC_P_84:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Perfectio creata non absolute, sed secundum finem proprium et ordinem universaliorem aestimatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A1","CH_GGVC_P_84:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Passibilitas et limitatio corporis sunt condiciones positivae cognitionis sensitivae, rationis et universalitatis instrumentalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A6","CH_GGVC_P_83:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Defectus localis aut malum contingens non tollit effectum quem intentio universalis vel bonum commune requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:A8","CH_GGVC_P_84:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Prima institutio humana est ordo originarius non reproductivus, qui condiciones generationis posterioris fundat sed eadem causalitate non explicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:D5","CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Prima perfectio humana excludit tam animam ante corpus perfecte institutam quam corpus humanum completum ante animam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_83:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Origo corporea non solum effectum physicum, sed ordinem coniugalem, domesticum, socialem et symbolicum distribuit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_84:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RMD-01","text":"Subsistentia animae redit ut principium propriae productionis, sed in anthropologia composita functionem mutat: independentiam essendi affirmat sine plenitudine humana separata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A7","CH_GGVC_P_82:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RMD-02","text":"Immediata causalitas divina persistit, sed ab creatione animae ad primam formationem corporis et deinde ad productionem ex materia naturae insolita transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:D5","CH_GGVC_P_83:D5","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RMD-03","text":"Notio defectus eadem verba retinet sed munus mutat: in corpore limitationem teleologicam, in sexu aestimationem particularem ab intentione universali absorptam significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_84:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RMD-04","text":"Potentia passiva e physica primae formationis in theoriam productionis mulieris migrat atque ubique possibilitatem receptivam, non efficientiam creaturalem, designat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A12","CH_GGVC_P_84:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RMD-05","text":"Corpus ab instrumento operationum animae ad signum relationum socialium et sacramentalium amplificatur, sine amissione prioris muneris hylomorphici.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A2","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RMD-06","text":"Praeexistentia temporalis removetur et in praeexistentiam causalem recodificatur: quod ante effectum est potest esse ratio passiva vel ordinatio divina, non res iam actu separata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_83:D12","CH_GGVC_P_84:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RMD-07","text":"Exclusio causalitatis angelicae stabiliter redit ad limites originis creativae vel primae formae; ministerium positivum angelicum autem indeterminatum manet atque in ultima unitate paene evanescit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_83:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_84:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:CGD-01","text":"Oppositio actus puri et potentiae cognoscitivae localiter distinctionem animae a Deo probat; hoc gradu ampliatur in regulam generalem esse creati participati.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A1","CH_GGVC_P_82:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:CGD-02","text":"Physica elementorum, complexionis et organorum manet apparatus localis corporis primi, sed eius effectus superior est thesis limitationis materialis ut condicionis positivae cognitionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A5","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:CGD-03","text":"Distributio sexualis virtutis activae et passivae atque assertio maioris discretionis masculinae non fiunt axiomata universalia huius gregis; sursum transit tantum distinctio inter aestimationem particularem et ordinem universalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:A2","CH_GGVC_P_84:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:CGD-04","text":"Limites causalitatis creatae unitates coniungunt: mediatio sistit ubi creatio, prima forma humana aut effectus supra determinationem materiae requiritur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:CGD-05","text":"Exegesis locutionum Scripturae unitates coniungit quatenus narrationem materialem in distinctiones metaphysicas et sociales convertit; singulae tamen determinationes exegeticae locales manent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:D10","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_84:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:CGD-06","text":"Electio inter creationem materiae additae et conversionem materiae in formatione mulieris non ad structuram globalem ascendit, quia textus utramque possibilitatem apertam relinquit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat animam esse substantiam divinam aut spiritualitatem eius continuitatem ontologicam cum Deo constituere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-02","text":"Systema negat animam rationalem ex potentia materiae educi, quamvis eam actum corporis esse affirmet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-03","text":"Systema negat animam et corpus esse duas substantias humanas completas quae post constitutionem extrinsece coniungantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-04","text":"Systema negat omnem originem humanam per catenam creaturalem, caelestem vel angelicam explicari posse.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_83:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_84:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-05","text":"Systema negat perfectionem corporis consistere in summa cuiusque excellentiae sensitivae, defensivae aut materialis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-06","text":"Systema negat primam institutionem et generationem posteriorem eundem modum causalitatis habere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_84:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-07","text":"Systema negat perfectionem originariam omnem asymmetriam, subiectionem aut distinctionem functionis necessario excludere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-08","text":"Systema negat locutiones Scripturae in pluralitatem causarum creatarum vel inspirationem corporaliter localem univoce resolvi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:DBN-01","text":"Ut systema constet, natura creata causaliter clausa esse non potest, quia origo humana continet effectus quos nulla series transmutationum antecedentium sufficienter producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet spiritualem dignitatem per separationem a corpore vel immunitatem a condicionibus sensitivis mensurari.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_83:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet possibilitatem passivam effectus iam includere potentiam activam causae creatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_84:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet perfectionem creatam possessionem omnium bonorum sine limitatione, detrimento aut asymmetria exigere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet corpus esse materiam neutralem, quia eius passibilitas cognitionem, eius instrumenta actionem et eius origo ordinem socialem portant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas tacite inadmissibilis est natura humana sibi ipsi originem et ultimam mensuram praebens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_84:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSA-02","text":"Systema circum absentiam hominis perfecte disemboditati ordinatur: anima separata permanere potest, sed humanitatem completam separatim constituere nequit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSA-03","text":"Inadmissibilis est universum in quo omne novum ens ex praesupposito creato procedat et prima institutio nihil aliud sit quam generatio naturalis retroiecta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSA-04","text":"Indisponibilis est perfectio originaria absque limitatione materiali, differentia functionali vel ordine interno.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSA-05","text":"Vetitum est bonum commune ita purgare ut omnis condicio ex qua malum contingenter nasci possit ante creationem removeatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_84:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-01","text":"Independentia animae a materia secundum esse et dependentia perfectionis humanae a corpore non contradicunt, quia subsistentia personalis et completio naturae diversa praedicata sunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-03","CL_GGVC_31:GA-06","CH_GGVC_P_82:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_83:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-02","text":"Immediata actio divina non est processus univocus: in anima tollit omne subiectum productionis, in primo corpore supplet absentiam formae generativae, in muliere determinationem ordinariam materiae excedit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","CL_GGVC_31:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_82:D3","CH_GGVC_P_83:D5","CH_GGVC_P_84:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-03","text":"Praeexistentia passiva est relatio effectus possibilis ad actum divinum, non status medius ex quo natura creata efficientiam sufficientem acquirat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","CL_GGVC_31:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_83:D7","CH_GGVC_P_84:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-04","text":"Passibilitas sensibilis et universalitas instrumentalis unum circuitum anthropologicum efficiunt: receptivitas corporis cognitionem inchoat, ratio autem manus ad instrumenta indefinita ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_83:D2","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-05","text":"Simultas animae et corporis atque exceptio primae formationis constituunt tempus originarium punctuale, quod neque praeexistentiam partium neque continuam reproductionem naturalem admittit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-09","CL_GGVC_31:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_82:D7","CH_GGVC_P_83:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-06","text":"Defectus fit praedicatum magnitudine dependens: limitatio organica potest fini rationali convenire, et quod causa particularis deficit ab intentione universali ordinari potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-06","CL_GGVC_31:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_83:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_84:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-07","text":"Narratio originis corporis simul causalitatem et normam distribuit, quia interpretatio formationis humanae transit a metaphysica unionis ad ordinem dominationis, coniunctionis et typologiae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-03","CL_GGVC_31:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-08","text":"Causalitas angelica ad limen originis humanae sistit: potest ministerium circa corpora habere, sed neque animam creare neque primam formam humanam aut corpus mulieris necessario producere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","CL_GGVC_31:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_82:D6","CH_GGVC_P_83:D6","CH_GGVC_P_84:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSD-01","text":"Persona humana secundum animam habet subsistentiam materia excedentem, sed humanitas eius naturaliter incompleta manet sine corpore; independentia ontologica igitur non est autarkia anthropologica.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A8","CH_GGVC_P_82:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas humana tripliciter ordinatur: Deus originem non reproductivam efficit, natura generationem posteriorem continuat, ratio autem per manus instrumenta multiplicat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_83:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSD-03","text":"Tempus anthropologicum non est continuum uniforme, sed distinguit institutionem originariam, reproductionem naturalem et separationem post mortem tamquam ordines causaliter inconvertibiles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:D8","CH_GGVC_P_83:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSD-04","text":"Finitudo corporea non tantum toleratur, sed operationes superiores generat: passibilitas cognitionem, imminutio specializationis universalitatem instrumentalem et distinctio functionum ordinem communem possibilitant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSD-05","text":"Cosmologia fit politica et domestica ubi compositio corporea, distinctio sexualis et situs originis relationes gubernationis, coniugii et communitatis recipiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSD-06","text":"Cognitio systematis requirit lectionem per mutationes scalae, quia eadem res secundum ordinem particularem, universalem, materialem, formalem aut symbolicum diversos status accipit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_84:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RLE-01","text":"Modus essendi → modus fiendi → causa sufficiens → ordo temporalis → perfectio naturalis constituit circuitum principalem, quo conclusio de natura rursus condiciones originis determinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_82:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RLE-02","text":"Defectus apparens → mutatio mensurae → relatio ad finem vel universum → restitutio positiva est via amplificationis communis corpori et differentiae sexuali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_83:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_84:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RLE-03","text":"Possibilitas passiva → absentia causae creatae sufficientis → actio creatoris → effectus supra ordinem naturalem efficit circuitum quo terminus causalitatis naturalis fit initium causalitatis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_84:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RLE-04","text":"Corpus passibile → sensus → ratio → instrumenta → vita domestica format viam qua receptivitas materialis in potentiam intellectualem et socialem amplificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A6","CH_GGVC_P_83:A13","CH_GGVC_P_84:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RLE-05","text":"Locutio scripturalis → difficultas materialis → distinctio metaphysica → ordinatio socialis vel sacramentalis est circuitus performativus quo narratio originis doctrinam producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RLE-06","text":"Attractor totius structurae est homo ut medium compositum: neque spiritus separatus neque animal specializatum, sed ens subsistens, corpore receptivum, instrumentaliter universale et relationaliter ordinatum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:D4","CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FGS-B01","text":"Apparens excellentia vel defectus localis introducitur ut problema, sed mensura eius statim ad modum essendi, finem proprium aut ordinem universaliorem transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_84:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FGS-B02","text":"Modus essendi effectus → requisita productionis → examen praesuppositi materialis, formalis vel activi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_83:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FGS-B03","text":"Praesuppositum sufficiens → operatio causae creatae; praesuppositum insufficiens vel nullum → immediata actio primi agentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A10","CH_GGVC_P_83:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FGS-B04","text":"Actio divina immediata → non separatio partium, sed institutio compositi secundum finem naturalem eius.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A12","CH_GGVC_P_83:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FGS-B05","text":"Compositum institutum → passibilitas sensitiva → cognitio rationalis → universalitas instrumentalis → vita socialis et domestica.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A6","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_84:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FGS-B06","text":"Origo corporea determinata → interpretatio relationis → limites dominationis et servitutis → significatio sacramentalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:A13","CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:AFM-B01","text":"Spiritualitas animae → exclusio consubstantialitatis divinae → subsistentia participata → creatio → causalitas divina immediata → unio originaria cum corpore.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_82:D2","CH_GGVC_P_82:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:AFM-B02","text":"Inferioritas materiae apparens → mutatio ad convenientiam finalem → corpus passibile et temperatum → cognitio sensibilis → ratio et manus → prima formatio divina sine antecedente generativo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_83:D2","CH_GGVC_P_83:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:AFM-B03","text":"Defectus sexualis particularis → intentio universalis → generatio et domus → origo ex viro et latere → ordinatio socialis et sacramentalis → productio divina supra naturam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:A4","CH_GGVC_P_84:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_84:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:AFM-B04","text":"Distinctiones locales → exclusio causalitatis clausae → institutio compositi humani → amplificatio epistemica et socialis corporis → clausura teleologica sub causalitate divina.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_83:D11","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MME-01","text":"Oppositio spiritus et materiae reordinatur: anima materia excedit secundum esse, sed corpus intrinsece requirit secundum naturam et operationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_83:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MME-02","text":"Creatio, formatio prima et generatio fiunt genera causaliter distincta, non gradus unius processus naturalis continui.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_83:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_84:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MME-03","text":"Distinctio defectus et perfectionis fit relativa ad scalam: imminutio localis potest esse aptitudo finalis vel pars ordinis universalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_84:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MME-04","text":"Praeexistentia possibilitatis separatur a praeexistentia causae efficientis, unde potentia passiva sine causalitate activa intelligi potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_84:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MME-05","text":"Corpus reclassificatur ut nexus epistemicus, instrumentalis, cosmicus et socialis, non ut receptaculum extrinsecum animae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_84:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MME-06","text":"Prima institutio speciei reclassificatur ut singularis fundatio ordinis naturalis, cuius causalitas a reproductione iam instituta distinguitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_83:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei determinantur origo animae, materia et forma corporis primi, necessitas mulieris atque causae immediatae harum productionum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_83:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_84:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali operantur hylomorphismus, proportio finis, distinctio causarum et transitus a potentia passiva ad actum creatoris.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A2","CH_GGVC_P_84:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico oppositae aestimationes solvuntur non per simplicem negationem, sed per mutationem ordinis comparationis et distinctionem causalitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_84:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas humana est subsistens sed composita, eius initium punctuale et divinum, eius continuatio naturaliter generativa, eius separatio post mortem defectiva.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_82:A13","CH_GGVC_P_83:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo forma disputativa effectum suum efficit: obiectionem conservat, eius mensuram distinguit et apparentem contradictionem in structuram ordinum convertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_84:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B06","text":"In gradu sociali et sacramentali compositio corporea distribuit coniunctionem, gubernationem et typologiam, ita ut origo physica normam relationis ferat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_84:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FL-01","text":"Subsistentia animae et incompletio eius ut partis naturae humanae cohaerent tantum si esse personale a perfectione speciei stricte distinguitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_82:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FL-02","text":"Thesis corporis optime dispositi nititur physica elementorum, doctrina complexionis et epistemologia sensibili quae intra hunc gregem assumuntur, non demonstrantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FL-03","text":"Eadem formula causalitatis divinae immediatae tres modos productionis tegit, quamvis creatio sine subiecto, formatio ex materia et conversio materiae non sint idem processus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:D3","CH_GGVC_P_84:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FL-04","text":"Transitus a distinctione biologica generationis ad hierarchiam socialem pendet ex praemissa de maiore discretione masculina, quae a principiis causalibus communibus non deducitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:A7","CH_GGVC_P_84:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FL-05","text":"Exegesis metaphysica et socialis locutionum Scripturae structuram servat, sed nexus inter verba singularia et significationes doctrinales ex sola grammatica non concluditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MP-01","text":"Terminus defectus mutatur a privatione simpliciter dicta in limitationem localem quae finem superiorem aut intentionem universaliorem sustinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_84:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MP-02","text":"Praeexistentia mutatur ex assertione temporali in rationem causalem passivam, qua effectus ante actum creatoris possibilis sed nondum productus est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_84:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MP-03","text":"Corpus mutatur ex organo cognitionis in portatorem ordinis socialis et figurae sacramentalis, unde descriptio physica vim normativam acquirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A2","CH_GGVC_P_84:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina causarum stratificata: natura transmutat quod praesupponit, intellectus instrumenta multiplicat, Deus autem possibilitates passivas in prima institutione ad actum producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A10","CH_GGVC_P_83:A13","CH_GGVC_P_84:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum humanum est centrum receptivum et operativum: subsistit per animam, cognoscit per corpus passibile, agit per instrumenta et completur intra relationes naturales.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A7","CH_GGVC_P_83:D2","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:HSI-03","text":"Tempus systematis est punctuatum: institutio divina aperit ordinem, generatio naturalis eum continuat, mors autem separationem defectivam inducit neque ad initium retroconvertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:D5","CH_GGVC_P_82:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga a corpore, sed discretio ordinis in quo limitatio, causalitas et relatio ad finem verum munus cuiusque partis manifestant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_83:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de esse participato, composito humano, proportione finali, limite causae creatae et actione divina immediata sufficiunt ad machinam principalem huius gregis restituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_83:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus quo modus essendi modum productionis et genus causae competentis determinat, deinde perfectio effectus secundum finem proprium aestimatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_83:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B03","text":"Systema hoc gradu cohaeret per reditum recursivum distinctionum inter participatum et purum, naturale et divinum, particulare et universale, activum et passivum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_84:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B04","text":"Minus determinata manent physica complexionis, amplitudo ministerii angelici, electio inter creationem et conversionem materiae, atque fundamentum exactum quarundam significationum exegeticarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_83:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_84:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_83:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B05","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae resistunt transitus a differentia generationis ad hierarchiam socialem et a situ corporeo ad typologiam sacramentalem; hi nexus structuram complent, sed ex machina causalitatis sola non derivantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T172746993184Z_2031b50d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 91–93","source_sha256":"ec08158d70e666b869004e29cd9a0c5b615e79d05284462d40717c7374c508eb","sha256":"1abc282ff6ae6ea778fd41701a2f7d476dc22dcf5598d634ba4217a18a3be8c1","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:27:46.994187+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how the origin of the soul, the formation of the first body, and the production of woman constitute one institution of human nature, while preserving distinctions of matter, cause, time, and relation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_83:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_84:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-02","text":"The central thesis is that the mode of being and proper end of a thing order its mode of production, while an effect for which a created presupposition cannot suffice demands immediate divine action.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_83:A9","CH_GGVC_P_84:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-03","text":"The argument proceeds through distinctions of orders, by which identity is separated from participation, creation from transmutation, passive potency from active potency, and universal intention from particular defect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_84:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-04","text":"The movement is recursive and transformative: the appearance of inferiority, defect, or posteriority is transferred to another measure and reclassified as final fittingness, universal intention, or subsequent defect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_84:D2","CH_GGVC_P_82:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-05","text":"The spiritual subsistence of the soul does not terminate in separation, but is conjoined with the passibility of the body, because human perfection and rational cognition require union with the body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-06","text":"Immediate divine causality is varied analogically: it creates the soul without a subject, forms the first body without a generative antecedent, and produces the body of woman from extraordinary matter beyond the determination of nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:D5","CH_GGVC_P_83:D5","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-07","text":"The time of origin is disjoined from the time of reproduction and defect: soul and body initially constitute one nature, the first institution exceeds subsequent generation, and bodily origin institutes social relations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A14","CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BAS-08","text":"The closure of the system is teleological and hylomorphic, not naturalistically causal: the nexus of mode of being, mode of becoming, and cause remains stable, although the assumed physics and the transition to social hierarchy leave fragile joints.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_83:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_84:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] The mode of being of an effect determines its mode of becoming, and its mode of becoming defines the kind of sufficient cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_82:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Every created human being is participated; therefore neither the simplicity of the soul nor its intellectuality entails identity with pure act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A1","CH_GGVC_P_82:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Human nature is a composite to which the rational soul as subsistent form and the body as intrinsic matter belong.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A11","CH_GGVC_P_83:A3","CH_GGVC_P_83:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Immediate divine action is required whenever neither a subject for the production of the soul, nor an antecedent human form, nor a created active power suffices for the effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A10","CH_GGVC_P_83:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_84:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] A created cause operates within presupposed matter, form, and process; passive potency makes an effect possible, not a created cause sufficient.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A8","CH_GGVC_P_84:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Created perfection is assessed not absolutely, but according to its proper end and a more universal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A1","CH_GGVC_P_84:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The passibility and limitation of the body are positive conditions of sensitive cognition, reason, and instrumental universality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A6","CH_GGVC_P_83:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] A local defect or contingent evil does not remove an effect required by universal intention or the common good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:A8","CH_GGVC_P_84:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] The first human institution is an original non-reproductive order, which grounds the conditions of subsequent generation but is not explained by the same causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:D5","CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] First human perfection excludes both a soul perfectly instituted before the body and a complete human body before the soul.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_83:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Bodily origin distributes not only a physical effect, but also a conjugal, domestic, social, and symbolic order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_84:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RMD-01","text":"The subsistence of the soul returns as the principle of its own production, but in composite anthropology it changes function: it affirms independence in being without separate human fullness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A7","CH_GGVC_P_82:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RMD-02","text":"Immediate divine causality persists, but is transferred from the creation of the soul to the first formation of the body and then to production from matter unusual to nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:D5","CH_GGVC_P_83:D5","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RMD-03","text":"The notion of defect retains the same words but changes function: in the body it signifies teleological limitation, in sex a particular assessment absorbed by universal intention.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_84:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RMD-04","text":"Passive potency migrates from the physics of first formation into the theory of the production of woman and everywhere designates receptive possibility, not creaturely efficiency.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A12","CH_GGVC_P_84:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RMD-05","text":"The body is expanded from an instrument of the soul's operations into a sign of social and sacramental relations, without loss of its prior hylomorphic role.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A2","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RMD-06","text":"Temporal pre-existence is removed and recoded into causal pre-existence: what is before an effect can be a passive principle or divine ordination, not a thing already actually separate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_83:D12","CH_GGVC_P_84:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RMD-07","text":"The exclusion of angelic causality steadily returns to the limits of creative origin or first form; positive angelic ministry, however, remains indeterminate and nearly vanishes in the final unity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_83:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_84:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:CGD-01","text":"The opposition between pure act and cognitive potency locally proves the distinction of the soul from God; at this level it is expanded into the general rule of participated created being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A1","CH_GGVC_P_82:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:CGD-02","text":"The physics of elements, complexion, and organs remains the local apparatus of the first body, but its higher effect is the thesis of material limitation as a positive condition of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A5","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:CGD-03","text":"The sexual distribution of active and passive power and the assertion of greater male discretion do not become universal axioms of this cluster; only the distinction between particular assessment and universal order passes upward.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:A2","CH_GGVC_P_84:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:CGD-04","text":"The limits of created causality join the units: mediation stops where creation, the first human form, or an effect beyond the determination of matter is required.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:CGD-05","text":"The exegesis of scriptural expressions joins the units insofar as it converts material narrative into metaphysical and social distinctions; individual exegetical determinations nevertheless remain local.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:D10","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_84:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:CGD-06","text":"The choice between creation of added matter and conversion of matter in the formation of woman does not ascend to the global structure, because the text leaves both possibilities open.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-01","text":"The system denies that the soul is a divine substance or that its spirituality constitutes ontological continuity with God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-02","text":"The system denies that the rational soul is drawn out from the potency of matter, although it affirms it to be the act of the body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-03","text":"The system denies that soul and body are two complete human substances that are extrinsically conjoined after their constitution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-04","text":"The system denies that every human origin can be explained through a creaturely, celestial, or angelic chain.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_83:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_84:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-05","text":"The system denies that bodily perfection consists in the highest degree of every sensitive, defensive, or material excellence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-06","text":"The system denies that first institution and subsequent generation have the same mode of causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_84:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-07","text":"The system denies that original perfection necessarily excludes every asymmetry, subjection, or distinction of function.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:GNX-08","text":"The system denies that scriptural expressions are univocally reducible to a plurality of created causes or to corporeally local inspiration.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:DBN-01","text":"For the system to stand, created nature cannot be causally closed, because human origin contains effects that no series of antecedent transmutations sufficiently produces.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that spiritual dignity is measured by separation from the body or immunity from sensitive conditions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_83:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that the passive possibility of an effect already includes the active potency of a created cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_84:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that created perfection requires possession of all goods without limitation, loss, or asymmetry.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the body is neutral matter, because its passibility bears cognition, its instruments action, and its origin social order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSA-01","text":"The tacitly inadmissible possibility is human nature providing itself with its own origin and ultimate measure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_84:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSA-02","text":"The system is ordered around the absence of a perfectly disembodied human: the separate soul can remain, but cannot separately constitute complete humanity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSA-03","text":"Inadmissible is a universe in which every new being proceeds from a created presupposition and first institution is nothing other than natural generation projected backward.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSA-04","text":"Original perfection without material limitation, functional difference, or internal order is unavailable.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSA-05","text":"It is forbidden to purify the common good so that every condition from which evil could contingently arise is removed before creation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_84:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-01","text":"The soul's independence from matter according to being and the dependence of human perfection upon the body do not contradict one another, because personal subsistence and completion of nature are different predicates.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-03","CL_GGVC_31:GA-06","CH_GGVC_P_82:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_83:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-02","text":"Immediate divine action is not a univocal process: in the soul it removes every subject of production, in the first body it supplies the absence of a generative form, and in woman it exceeds the ordinary determination of matter.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","CL_GGVC_31:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_82:D3","CH_GGVC_P_83:D5","CH_GGVC_P_84:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-03","text":"Passive pre-existence is the relation of a possible effect to divine act, not an intermediate state from which created nature acquires sufficient efficiency.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","CL_GGVC_31:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_83:D7","CH_GGVC_P_84:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-04","text":"Sensitive passibility and instrumental universality constitute one anthropological circuit: the receptivity of the body initiates cognition, while reason orders hands toward indefinite instruments.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_83:D2","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-05","text":"The simultaneity of soul and body and the exception of first formation constitute a punctual original time, which admits neither pre-existence of the parts nor continuous natural reproduction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-09","CL_GGVC_31:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_82:D7","CH_GGVC_P_83:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-06","text":"Defect becomes a scale-dependent predicate: organic limitation can suit a rational end, and what falls short in a particular cause can be ordered by universal intention.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-06","CL_GGVC_31:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_83:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_84:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-07","text":"The narrative of bodily origin distributes causality and norm simultaneously, because the interpretation of human formation moves from the metaphysics of union to the order of domination, conjunction, and typology.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-03","CL_GGVC_31:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:XD-08","text":"Angelic causality stops at the threshold of human origin: it can have ministry concerning bodies, but can neither create the soul nor necessarily produce the first human form or the body of woman.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","CL_GGVC_31:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_82:D6","CH_GGVC_P_83:D6","CH_GGVC_P_84:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSD-01","text":"The human person has, according to the soul, a subsistence exceeding matter, but its humanity remains naturally incomplete without the body; ontological independence is therefore not anthropological autarky.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A8","CH_GGVC_P_82:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSD-02","text":"Human causality is ordered threefold: God effects non-reproductive origin, nature continues subsequent generation, and reason multiplies instruments through the hands.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_83:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSD-03","text":"Anthropological time is not a uniform continuum, but distinguishes original institution, natural reproduction, and postmortem separation as causally irreversible orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:D8","CH_GGVC_P_83:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSD-04","text":"Bodily finitude is not merely tolerated, but generates higher operations: passibility makes cognition possible, diminution of specialization instrumental universality, and distinction of functions common order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSD-05","text":"Cosmology becomes political and domestic where bodily composition, sexual distinction, and the site of origin receive relations of governance, marriage, and community.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:BSD-06","text":"Knowledge of the system requires reading through changes of scale, because the same thing receives different statuses according to particular, universal, material, formal, or symbolic order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_84:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RLE-01","text":"Mode of being → mode of becoming → sufficient cause → temporal order → natural perfection constitutes the principal circuit, by which the conclusion concerning nature in turn determines the conditions of origin.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_82:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RLE-02","text":"Apparent defect → change of measure → relation to end or whole → positive restoration is the path of amplification common to the body and sexual difference.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_83:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_84:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RLE-03","text":"Passive possibility → absence of a sufficient created cause → action of the creator → effect above natural order produces the circuit whereby the limit of natural causality becomes the beginning of divine causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_84:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RLE-04","text":"Passible body → senses → reason → instruments → domestic life forms the path by which material receptivity is amplified into intellectual and social power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A6","CH_GGVC_P_83:A13","CH_GGVC_P_84:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RLE-05","text":"Scriptural expression → material difficulty → metaphysical distinction → social or sacramental ordering is the performative circuit by which the narrative of origin produces doctrine.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:RLE-06","text":"The attractor of the whole structure is the human as a composite mean: neither separate spirit nor specialized animal, but a subsistent being, bodily receptive, instrumentally universal, and relationally ordered.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:D4","CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FGS-B01","text":"Apparent excellence or local defect is introduced as a problem, but its measure is immediately transferred to the mode of being, proper end, or more universal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_84:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FGS-B02","text":"Mode of being of the effect → requirements of production → examination of the material, formal, or active presupposition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_83:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FGS-B03","text":"Sufficient presupposition → operation of a created cause; insufficient or no presupposition → immediate action of the first agent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A10","CH_GGVC_P_83:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FGS-B04","text":"Immediate divine action → not separation of parts, but institution of the composite according to its natural end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A12","CH_GGVC_P_83:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FGS-B05","text":"Instituted composite → sensitive passibility → rational cognition → instrumental universality → social and domestic life.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A6","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_84:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FGS-B06","text":"Determinate bodily origin → interpretation of relation → limits of domination and servitude → sacramental signification.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:A13","CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:AFM-B01","text":"Spirituality of the soul → exclusion of divine consubstantiality → participated subsistence → creation → immediate divine causality → original union with the body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_82:D2","CH_GGVC_P_82:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:AFM-B02","text":"Apparent inferiority of matter → shift to final fittingness → passible and tempered body → sensitive cognition → reason and hands → first divine formation without a generative antecedent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_83:D2","CH_GGVC_P_83:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:AFM-B03","text":"Particular sexual defect → universal intention → generation and household → origin from man and side → social and sacramental ordering → divine production above nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:A4","CH_GGVC_P_84:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_84:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:AFM-B04","text":"Local distinctions → exclusion of closed causality → institution of the human composite → epistemic and social amplification of the body → teleological closure under divine causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_83:D11","CH_GGVC_P_84:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MME-01","text":"The opposition of spirit and matter is reordered: the soul exceeds matter according to being, but intrinsically requires the body according to nature and operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_83:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MME-02","text":"Creation, first formation, and generation become causally distinct kinds, not stages of one continuous natural process.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_83:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_84:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MME-03","text":"The distinction between defect and perfection becomes relative to scale: local diminution can be final aptitude or part of a universal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_84:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MME-04","text":"Pre-existence of possibility is separated from pre-existence of efficient cause, whence passive potency can be understood without active causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_84:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MME-05","text":"The body is reclassified as an epistemic, instrumental, cosmic, and social nexus, not as an extrinsic receptacle of the soul.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_84:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MME-06","text":"The first institution of the species is reclassified as a singular foundation of natural order, whose causality is distinguished from already instituted reproduction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_83:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the origin of the soul, the matter and form of the first body, the necessity of woman, and the immediate causes of these productions are determined.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_83:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_84:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, hylomorphism, proportion to the end, distinction of causes, and the transition from passive potency to the act of the creator operate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A2","CH_GGVC_P_84:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, opposing assessments are resolved not through simple negation, but through a change in the order of comparison and a distinction of causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_84:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, human reality is subsistent yet composite, its beginning punctual and divine, its continuation naturally generative, its postmortem separation defective.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_82:A13","CH_GGVC_P_83:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the disputational form effects its result: it preserves the objection, distinguishes its measure, and converts apparent contradiction into a structure of orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_84:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B06","text":"At the social and sacramental level, bodily composition distributes conjunction, governance, and typology, so that physical origin bears a norm of relation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_84:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FL-01","text":"The subsistence of the soul and its incompletion as a part of human nature cohere only if personal being is strictly distinguished from the perfection of the species.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_82:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FL-02","text":"The thesis of the best-disposed body rests on the physics of elements, the doctrine of complexion, and sensitive epistemology, which are assumed within this cluster rather than demonstrated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FL-03","text":"The same formula of immediate divine causality covers three modes of production, although creation without a subject, formation from matter, and conversion of matter are not the same process.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:D3","CH_GGVC_P_84:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FL-04","text":"The transition from the biological distinction of generation to social hierarchy depends on the premise of greater male discretion, which is not deduced from the common causal principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:A7","CH_GGVC_P_84:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:FL-05","text":"The metaphysical and social exegesis of scriptural expressions preserves the structure, but the nexus between individual words and doctrinal meanings is not concluded from grammar alone.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MP-01","text":"The term defect changes from privation simply so called into local limitation that supports a higher end or more universal intention.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_84:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MP-02","text":"Pre-existence changes from a temporal assertion into a passive causal principle, whereby the effect is possible before the act of the creator but not yet produced.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_84:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MP-03","text":"The body changes from an organ of cognition into a bearer of social order and sacramental figure, whence physical description acquires normative force.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:A2","CH_GGVC_P_84:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a stratified machine of causes: nature transmutes what it presupposes, intellect multiplies instruments, but God brings passive possibilities into act in the first institution.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A10","CH_GGVC_P_83:A13","CH_GGVC_P_84:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:HSI-02","text":"The human subject is a receptive and operative center: it subsists through the soul, knows through the passible body, acts through instruments, and is completed within natural relations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:A7","CH_GGVC_P_83:D2","CH_GGVC_P_83:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:HSI-03","text":"The time of the system is punctuated: divine institution opens the order, natural generation continues it, while death introduces defective separation and does not revert to the beginning.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:D5","CH_GGVC_P_82:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is not flight from the body, but discernment of the order in which limitation, causality, and relation to the end manifest the true function of each part.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:DNX5","CH_GGVC_P_83:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of participated being, the human composite, final proportion, the limit of created cause, and immediate divine action suffice to reconstruct the principal machine of this cluster.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_83:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the nexus by which mode of being determines mode of production and the kind of competent cause, after which the perfection of the effect is assessed according to its proper end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_83:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B03","text":"At this level the system coheres through the recursive return of distinctions between participated and pure, natural and divine, particular and universal, active and passive.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_82:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_84:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B04","text":"Less determined remain the physics of complexion, the scope of angelic ministry, the choice between creation and conversion of matter, and the exact foundation of certain exegetical significations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_83:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_83:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_84:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_83:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B05","text":"The transitions from difference in generation to social hierarchy and from bodily situation to sacramental typology resist axiomatic reduction; these nexuses complete the structure, but are not derived from the machine of causality alone.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_84:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_84:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_31@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_31:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 91–93","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 90 · Prima pars · Quaestio 91 · Prima pars · Quaestio 92"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 94–96","order":32,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_85","CH_GGVC_P_86","CH_GGVC_P_87"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_32@R001","sha256":"c67529f2888e84d74daa1a3533659057ca97ff13523541abb771268d9cd7f983","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-01","text":"Systema determinat quomodo homo, ut imago intellectualis Dei, in statu originario cognoscat, velit et inferiores vires regat, manens tamen creatura corpori coniuncta et nondum consummata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_86:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_87:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-02","text":"Thesis centralis imaginem Dei in mente ad cognitionem et amorem divinum directa collocat, cui gratia ordinem supernaturalis rectitudinis confert sine abolitione modi naturalis humani.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_86:A5","CH_GGVC_P_87:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio statum originarium reconstructive definit, ambiguitates per distinctiones ordinans et ex effectibus lapsus ea quae naturam excedebant retrogressive inferens.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_86:AS7","CH_GGVC_P_87:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-04","text":"Motus procedit ab exemplari divino per aptitudinem intellectualem et rectitudinem gratuitam ad operationes, progressionem et meritum, deinde bifurcatur inter lapsum et consummationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-05","text":"Motus est recursivus et cumulativus, quia eadem relatio superioris ad inferius iteratur in processione mentali, iudicio cognitivo, affectu et regimine corporis, singulis tamen vicibus functione mutata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A10","CH_GGVC_P_86:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-06","text":"Status innocentiae constituit medium viatorium: naturam integram et gratia ordinatam continet, sed visionem essentiae, immobilitatem voluntatis et perfectionem gloriae nondum possidet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-07","text":"Lapsus non delet naturam intellectualem neque imaginem naturalem, sed conformitatem actualem obscurat, hierarchiam dissolvit et mensuras cognitionis, affectus atque meriti reconfigurat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-08","text":"Clausura est eschatologice aperta: gloria manet attractor perfectionis, dum possibilitas lapsus statum originarium a termino irreversibili distinguit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A13","CH_GGVC_P_87:A9","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Relatio hominis ad Deum est exemplaris et analogica: imago realem convenientiam postulat, non aequalitatem naturae nec identitatem cum exemplari.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A4","CH_GGVC_P_85:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Mens intellectualis est locus formalis imaginis, sed operatur secundum modum animae corpori coniunctae; interioritas intellectualis et conditio corporalis distinguuntur sine separatione anthropologica.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A8","CH_GGVC_P_86:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Imago est structura operativa cognitionis, verbi et amoris, cuius ratio divina non ex forma triadica sola sed ex orientatione actuum ad Deum constituitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A10","CH_GGVC_P_85:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Gratia simul imaginem conformiter actualizat et hierarchiam Deus–ratio–vires inferiores–corpus causaliter instituit, unde integritas iconica, cognitiva et affectiva unam radicem accipiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A13","CH_GGVC_P_87:A3","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Status originarius est integritas naturae et gratiae, non mutatio hominis in substantiam separatam neque anticipatio beatitudinis consummatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Gloria per visionem et fruitionem perfectam imaginem consummat atque voluntatem in Deo stabilit; eius absentia constituit defectibilitatem status primi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A13","CH_GGVC_P_86:A2","CH_GGVC_P_87:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Perfectio creata non est plenitudo indiscriminata, sed sufficientia teleologica diversis mensuris definita: propinquitate ad exemplar, ambitu muneris, ordine passionum et proportione operis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_86:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_87:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Distinctio potentiae, habitus et actus permittit imaginem, scientiam, virtutem et perseverantiam permanere sine operatione actuali continua.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A14","CH_GGVC_P_87:A8","CH_GGVC_P_87:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Lapsus substratum naturale servat, sed ordine interiori per elationem prius rupto conformitatem, iudicium, affectum et obedientiam inferiorum mutat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_86:A16","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Possibilitas peccati demonstrat statum primum neque visionem beatificam neque confirmationem finalem continuisse, quamvis gratia et rectitudo adessent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-01","text":"Imago naturalis identitatem stabilem retinet ut aptitudo mentis ad Deum, etiam cum claritas conformitatis per peccatum minuitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A12","CH_GGVC_P_85:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-02","text":"Gratia a gradu perfectionis imaginis in causam totius rectitudinis migrat: quod primo conformitas mentis est, deinde principium ordinis cognoscitivi, affectivi et corporalis fit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_87:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-03","text":"Integritas functionem mutat ab immunitate intellectus contra falsum assensum ad architecturam supernaturalem quae rationem, appetitum et corpus coordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-04","text":"Distinctio actus et principii redit functionaliter: imaginem sine actu continuo, scientiam cum progressu et virtutem sine materia actuali servat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:D9","CH_GGVC_P_87:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-05","text":"Imperfectio mutatur a distantia analogica exemplaris in ignorantiam non deceptivam et deinde in absentiam fruitionis finalis; defectus non semper corruptionem significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_86:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_87:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-06","text":"Peccatum ab obscuratione imaginis amplificatur in mutationem status: elatio interna aperit deceptionem, dissolutio ordinis inferiores vires emancipat, et difficultas novam proportionem meriti introducit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_86:A16","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-07","text":"Comparatio perfectionis e scala unica discedit: intensitas imaginis, sufficientia cognitionis et proportio meriti in mensuras non convertibiles distribuuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_86:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_87:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-08","text":"Comparationes angelorum, sexuum et passionum singularium munere locali functae in structura superiore absorbentur, ubi intellectualitas, ordo hierarchicus et modus operationis criteria dominantia fiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:D3","CH_GGVC_P_85:D7","CH_GGVC_P_87:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:CGD-01","text":"Comparationes angeli et hominis atque analogiae sexuales manent determinationes locales rationis imaginis; structuram huius gregis non regunt nisi sub criterio intellectualitatis et ordinis originis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:D3","CH_GGVC_P_85:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:CGD-02","text":"Exceptio raptus, duplex remedium contra errorem in rebus ignotis et distributio passionum singularium manent commissurae inferioris gradus, non principia globalia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_87:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:CGD-03","text":"Localizatio imaginis in mente sursum amplificatur in structuram totius hominis, quia eadem mens per rectitudinem cognitionem, appetitum et corpus ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A8","CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:CGD-04","text":"Distinctiones locales inter naturam, infusionem et gratiam coniunguntur in propositionem praesentis gradus: donum supernaturale naturam humanam non substituit, sed eius operationes ordinat et perficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_86:D5","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:CGD-05","text":"Quaestiones inferiores binariae de imagine, ignorantia, passione et virtute superantur per codificationem modalem: naturale vel gratuitum, habituale vel actuale, ordinatum vel dissolutum, absolutum vel proportionale.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_86:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat imaginem creatam aequalitatem, univocitatem vel identitatem naturae cum Deo requirere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_85:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GNX-02","text":"Corpus, figura et sexus excluduntur a ratione formali imaginis, quamvis corpus ordinem totius status recipiat et denominative ad hominem imaginarium pertineat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_87:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GNX-03","text":"Innocentia neque beatitudo, neque omniscientia, neque cognitio angelica est; perfectio originaria limites obiecti et modi humani retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_86:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_87:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GNX-04","text":"Natura humana sola nec conformitatem gratuitam nec obedientiam stabilem inferiorum causaliter sufficit instituere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_87:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_87:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GNX-05","text":"Perfectio non per abolitionem omnis ignorantiae, passionis vel difficultatis definitur, sed per rectam relationem eorum ad rationem, finem et statum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_87:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_87:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GNX-06","text":"Forma mentalis, magnitudo scientiae et difficultas operis obiective neutralia esse non possunt; obiectum, munus et proportio ad facultatem earum valorem determinant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX11","CH_GGVC_P_86:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_87:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet mentem creatam finem autonomum habere posse, quia imago, veritas et rectitudo ex subiectione atque directione ad Deum pendent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_86:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet naturam et gratiam duas formas aemulas constituere; gratia eandem potentiam naturalem actualizat et in architecturam originalis iustitiae ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet omnem integritatem creatam statim esse perfectionem eschatologicam, quia status rectus manere potest viatorius, progressivus et defectibilis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_87:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet apparentiam sensibilem vel phantasticam intellectum necessario determinare, quoniam ratio ordinata repraesentationes inferiores iudicat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_86:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet statuum comparationem ad unam quantitatem reduci posse; integritas, difficultas, opus absolutum, proportio et finis diversos ordines aestimationis constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_87:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_87:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSA-01","text":"Visio immediata essentiae divinae est absentia ordinans: quia indisponibilis manet, cognitio mediata, fides, spes, meritum et lapsus possibilia sunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_86:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_87:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSA-02","text":"Clausura sui sufficiens creaturae est ontologice inadmissibilis; neque mens, neque natura, neque rectitudo suam ultimam rationem ex se producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSA-03","text":"Irreversibilitas status primi indisponibilis manet: sine ea integritas potest vera esse, sed nondum immobilis neque terminalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:A2","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSA-04","text":"Comprehensio exhaustiva Dei, Trinitatis et contingentium circumscriptorum ab homine originario excluditur; systema sufficientiam ordinatam ponit ubi totalitas cognitiva abest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_86:A12","CH_GGVC_P_86:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:XD-01","text":"Iustitia originalis est actualizatio statalis imaginis: gratia orientationem mentis ad Deum in ordinem totius naturae humanae causaliter extendit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-03","CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","CL_GGVC_32:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_85:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:XD-02","text":"Ratio est medium transmissivum systematis: processiones verbi et amoris in mente recipiuntur, veritas ibi iudicatur, et inde appetitus atque corpus ordinem participant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-02","CL_GGVC_32:GA-03","CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","CH_GGVC_P_85:A10","CH_GGVC_P_86:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_87:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:XD-03","text":"Una causalitas gratiae tres effectus coordinat: conformitatem imaginis, libertatem cognitionis ab impedimento inferiori et obedientiam affectivam atque corporalem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","CL_GGVC_32:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_85:A13","CH_GGVC_P_86:A5","CH_GGVC_P_87:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:XD-04","text":"Homo originarius est imago viatoria: rectus et gratia praeditus, sed per cognitionem mediatam, fidem, spem et meritum adhuc ad terminum movetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-05","CL_GGVC_32:GA-06","CL_GGVC_32:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_86:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:XD-05","text":"Lapsus incipit ut inversio interior relationis ad Deum antequam appareat ut deceptio externa aut dissolutio virium inferiorum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-09","CL_GGVC_32:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_85:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_86:A16","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:XD-06","text":"Permanentia structurae et progressus operationis non adversantur: actus virtualiter manet in potentia vel habitu, dum experientia et revelatio modos atque ambitum actualem augent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-07","CL_GGVC_32:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_85:D9","CH_GGVC_P_86:A17","CH_GGVC_P_87:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:XD-07","text":"Immunitas originaria a deceptione est conditionalis, non absoluta: valet dum ordo interior manet, sed elatio voluntatis ipsam condicionem iudicii recti dissolvere potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","CL_GGVC_32:GA-09","CL_GGVC_32:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_86:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_86:A16","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSD-01","text":"Ontologia huius gregis est participativa, scalaris et hierarchica: perfectio consistit in ordinata propinquitate ad exemplar, non in possessione aequali proprietatum divinarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum humanum fit nodus mediationis: a Deo ordinem recipit, in mente cognitionem et amorem format, deinde inferiores vires atque corpus secundum rationem regit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_86:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSD-03","text":"Tempus anthropologicum est ramificatum: creatio et gratia originem constituunt, experientia atque meritum progressionem aperiunt, lapsus ordinem mutat, gloria autem consummationem nondum praesentem praebet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_85:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSD-04","text":"Error cognitivus statum moralem et hierarchicum acquirit: ignorantia potest cum integritate stare, falsus autem assensus rupturam iudicii superioris indicat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_86:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSD-05","text":"Lapsus non est mera subtractio perfectionis, quia minuit integritatem et magnitudinem absolutam operis, sed difficultatem atque proportionem meriti augere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_87:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RLE-01","text":"Directio mentis ad Deum rectam rationem nutrit; ratio inferiores vires ordinat; operationes ordinatae conformitatem imaginis renovant et directionem initialem corroborant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A15","CH_GGVC_P_86:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_87:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RLE-02","text":"Elatio interior subiectionem rationis rumpit; ruptura deceptionem et motus inferiores inordinatos possibilia facit; hi effectus ulteriorem aversionem amplificant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:A16","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RLE-03","text":"Potentia actum continet, actus habitum exercet, habitus futuros actus disponit; circuitus permanentiam imaginis et virtutis cum intermittente operatione componit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:D9","CH_GGVC_P_87:A8","CH_GGVC_P_87:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RLE-04","text":"Aptitudo naturalis, conformitas gratiae et consummatio gloriae constituunt viam amplificationis, sed transitus ad gradum superiorem mutationem modalem potius quam substitutionem subiecti efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A12","CH_GGVC_P_85:A13","CH_GGVC_P_85:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RLE-05","text":"Gloria est attractor terminalis, quia cognitionem mediatam in visionem et voluntatem defectibilem in stabilitatem convertit; status primus circa hunc terminum ordinatur sine eo fruendo.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:A2","CH_GGVC_P_87:A9","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B01","text":"Exemplar divinum → similitudo expressa → limen intellectualitatis → imago proprie dicta.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A3","CH_GGVC_P_85:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B02","text":"Natura intellectualis corpori coniuncta → mens per phantasmata operans → potentia cognitionis et amoris → substratum naturale imaginis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A8","CH_GGVC_P_86:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B03","text":"Gratia → subiectio rationis Deo → obedientia appetitus et corporis → iustitia originalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_87:A3","CH_GGVC_P_87:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B04","text":"Cognitio → verbum interius → amor → iudicium rectum → passiones et virtutes ordinatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A10","CH_GGVC_P_86:A15","CH_GGVC_P_87:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B05","text":"Obiectum divinum + munus humanum + finis supernaturalis → determinatio imaginis, scientiae sufficientis et actionis virtuosae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A15","CH_GGVC_P_86:A10","CH_GGVC_P_87:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B06","text":"Integritas non consummata → progressus et meritum → bifurcatio inter consummationem gloriae et inversionem per superbiam → permanens natura cum ordine status mutato.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:A17","CH_GGVC_P_85:A13","CH_GGVC_P_86:A16","CH_GGVC_P_85:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:AFM-B01","text":"Exemplar divinum → natura intellectualis → mens → verbum et amor → orientatio obiectiva ad Deum → ratio imaginis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A17","CH_GGVC_P_85:A10","CH_GGVC_P_85:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:AFM-B02","text":"Modus humanus corpori coniunctus → gratia originaria → rectitudo rationis → cognitio non impedita → passiones et virtutes ordinatae → meritum viatorium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:A4","CH_GGVC_P_87:A3","CH_GGVC_P_87:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:AFM-B03","text":"Integritas nondum gloriosa → progressus possibilis → elatio interior aut perseverantia → lapsus structuralis aut consummatio → mutatio status imaginis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_87:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_86:A17","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MME-01","text":"Imago Dei reclassificatur ab attributo isolato in architecturam totius hominis, cuius forma in mente residet sed cuius effectus cognitionem, affectum et corpus pervadunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MME-02","text":"Perfectio status ab una scala plenitudinis in tabulam mensurarum convertitur: intensitas repraesentationis, sufficientia teleologica, integritas ordinis et proportio meriti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_86:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_87:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MME-03","text":"Corpus neque locus formalis imaginis neque elementum extrinsecum fit; est terminus inferior regiminis animae et particeps integritatis totius.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_86:A5","CH_GGVC_P_87:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MME-04","text":"Gratia et natura non iam opponuntur ut formae concurrentes: infusio servat rationem cognitionis humanae, dum gratia naturalem aptitudinem in rectitudinem supernaturalem promovet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_86:D5","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MME-05","text":"Lapsus reclassificatur a culpa addita in mutationem topologicam ordinis, quae naturalem imaginem servat sed relationes inter Deum, rationem, appetitum et corpus invertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei examinantur imago Dei, cognitio primi hominis, gratia, passiones, virtutes et meritum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_86:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_87:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali operatur una distributio: mens cognitionem et amorem format, ratio inferiora ordinat, et integritas impedimenta huius ordinis removet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A10","CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico opposita apparentia per distinctiones modales reconstituuntur, ut perfectio sine consummatione, ignorantia sine deceptione et passio sine inordinatione cogitari possint.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas creata est participativa atque processualis: principium perfectum instituitur, actus explicantur, meritum crescit, lapsus ordinem frangit et gloria terminum praebet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico forma distinctionum assertiones efficit, quia contradictiones locales non delet sed in criteria ordinata, modos praedicationis et mensuras non convertibiles distribuit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT8","CH_GGVC_P_87:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FL-01","text":"Imago naturalis omnibus hominibus manet, sed integritas totius ordinis gratiam requirit; relatio exacta inter aptitudinem naturalem et causalitatem gratuitam non omnino explicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A12","CH_GGVC_P_87:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FL-02","text":"Imago principaliter secundum actus attenditur, quamvis sine actu continuo permaneat; continentia virtualis in potentiis et habitibus pontem necessarium sed compressum praebet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A14","CH_GGVC_P_85:D9","CH_GGVC_P_87:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FL-03","text":"Immunitas a deceptione cum possibilitate lapsus componitur solum si peccatum voluntatis ordine causali iudicium falsum antecedit; chronologia interior hanc commissuram sustinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_86:A16","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FL-04","text":"Species infusae cognitionem originariam perficiunt, sed intellectus humanus per conversionem ad phantasmata operatur; coordinatio exacta utriusque modi manet minus determinata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:A4","CH_GGVC_P_86:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FL-05","text":"Scientia initio sufficienter universalis simul progressionem experimentalem admittit; numerus cognitorum et modus sciendi distinguuntur, sed nexus eorum fragilis manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_86:A17","CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MP-01","text":"Ratio imaginis mutatur ab aptitudine naturali in conformitatem gratuitam et deinde in consummationem gloriosam, eodem subiecto manente sed modo participationis crescente.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_85:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MP-02","text":"Integritas mutatur ab immunitate epistemica in structuram globalem originalis iustitiae, unde veritas intellectus pars ordinis amplioris fit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MP-03","text":"Perfectio mutatur ab intensitate similitudinis in sufficientiam muneris et postremo in pluralitatem mensurarum meriti; vocabulum persistit, sed unitatem metricam amittit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_86:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_87:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est cascada ordinata, in qua Deus rationem, ratio appetitum et anima corpus regunt, dum inferiores gradus superiores repraesentant sine eis aequari.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A5","CH_GGVC_P_87:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est speculum operativum et medium transmissionis: intra se verbum et amorem format, supra se exemplar respicit et infra se vires inferiores coordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A10","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est via ramificata inter originem, exercitationem, lapsum et gloriam; progressus non est continuus ascensus, quia inversio interior statum totum mutare potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:HSI-04","text":"Certamen fundamentale est inter circulationem descendentis ordinis et recursivam inversionem superbiae; navigatio possibilis manet per creaturas ad Deum et per rationem ad regimen inferiorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:A6","CH_GGVC_P_86:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_86:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata praesentia sufficiunt ad rationem imaginis, modum cognitionis originariae, causalitatem gratiae, ordinem passionum et virtutum atque pluralitatem meriti restituenda.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_87:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus inter orientationem mentis ad Deum, rectitudinem rationis et obedientiam inferiorum, quia tres unitates hunc nexum diversis campis confirmant.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_87:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinatus manet transitus ab analogica ratione imaginis ad ordinem gratiae atque illatio supernaturalitatis ex amissione obedientiae post peccatum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_87:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B04","text":"Cohaerentia nititur distinctionibus inter actum et habitum, ignorantiam et deceptionem, naturam et integritatem; ubi hae distinctiones comprimuntur, commissurae fragiles apparent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_87:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat: imago intellectualis in statum originarium cognitionis et voluntatis expanditur, deinde per defectibilitatem, lapsum et gloriam tamquam machina temporalis aperitur; reductioni plenae resistunt nexus inter visionem et impeccabilitatem atque inter gratiam initialem et consensum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_87:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T172826625398Z_42a8faf3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 94–96","source_sha256":"c67529f2888e84d74daa1a3533659057ca97ff13523541abb771268d9cd7f983","sha256":"6643d36c1a9948edba36eabdb418c3908a380e62d03709db6a923314de3d5cab","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:28:26.626370+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-01","text":"The system determines how man, as the intellectual image of God, in the original state knows, wills, and governs the lower powers, while remaining a creature joined to a body and not yet consummated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_86:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_87:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-02","text":"The central thesis places the image of God in the mind directed toward divine knowledge and love, to which grace confers the order of supernatural rectitude without abolishing the natural human mode.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_86:A5","CH_GGVC_P_87:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-03","text":"The argument reconstructively defines the original state, ordering ambiguities through distinctions and retrogressively inferring from the effects of the Fall those things that exceeded nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_86:AS7","CH_GGVC_P_87:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-04","text":"The movement proceeds from the divine exemplar through intellectual aptitude and gratuitous rectitude to operations, progress, and merit, then bifurcates between the Fall and consummation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-05","text":"The movement is recursive and cumulative, because the same relation of higher to lower is repeated in mental procession, cognitive judgment, affection, and governance of the body, yet each time with a changed function.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A10","CH_GGVC_P_86:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-06","text":"The state of innocence constitutes a wayfaring middle: it contains nature intact and ordered by grace, but does not yet possess vision of the essence, immovability of the will, or the perfection of glory.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-07","text":"The Fall does not destroy intellectual nature or the natural image, but obscures actual conformity, dissolves hierarchy, and reconfigures the measures of knowledge, affection, and merit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BAS-08","text":"The closure is eschatologically open: glory remains the attractor of perfection, while the possibility of the Fall distinguishes the original state from the irreversible terminus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A13","CH_GGVC_P_87:A9","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Man's relation to God is exemplary and analogical: image requires a real correspondence, not equality of nature or identity with the exemplar.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A4","CH_GGVC_P_85:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The intellectual mind is the formal seat of the image, but operates according to the mode of a soul conjoined to a body; intellectual interiority and bodily condition are distinguished without anthropological separation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A8","CH_GGVC_P_86:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The image is an operative structure of knowledge, word, and love, whose divine character is constituted not by triadic form alone but by the orientation of acts toward God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A10","CH_GGVC_P_85:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Grace simultaneously actualizes the image conformably and causally institutes the hierarchy God–reason–lower powers–body, whence iconic, cognitive, and affective integrity receive one root.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A13","CH_GGVC_P_87:A3","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] The original state is the integrity of nature and grace, not a change of man into a separate substance nor an anticipation of consummated beatitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Glory consummates the image through perfect vision and fruition and stabilizes the will in God; its absence constitutes the defectibility of the first state.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A13","CH_GGVC_P_86:A2","CH_GGVC_P_87:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Created perfection is not indiscriminate fullness, but teleological sufficiency defined by diverse measures: proximity to the exemplar, scope of office, order of passions, and proportion of work.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_86:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_87:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] The distinction of potency, habit, and act allows image, knowledge, virtue, and perseverance to remain without continuous actual operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A14","CH_GGVC_P_87:A8","CH_GGVC_P_87:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] The Fall preserves the natural substrate, but, with the interior order first broken through pride, changes conformity, judgment, affection, and the obedience of lower things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_86:A16","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] The possibility of sin demonstrates that the first state contained neither beatific vision nor final confirmation, although grace and rectitude were present.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-01","text":"The natural image retains stable identity as the aptitude of the mind for God, even when the brightness of conformity is diminished through sin.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A12","CH_GGVC_P_85:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-02","text":"Grace migrates from a degree of perfection of the image into the cause of all rectitude: what is first conformity of mind subsequently becomes the principle of cognitive, affective, and bodily order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_87:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-03","text":"Integrity changes function from the immunity of the intellect against false assent to a supernatural architecture coordinating reason, appetite, and body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-04","text":"The distinction between act and principle returns functionally: it preserves image without continuous act, knowledge with progress, and virtue without actual matter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:D9","CH_GGVC_P_87:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-05","text":"Imperfection changes from analogical distance from the exemplar into non-deceptive ignorance and then into absence of final fruition; defect does not always signify corruption.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_86:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_87:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-06","text":"Sin is amplified from obscuration of the image into a change of state: interior pride opens the way to deception, dissolution of order emancipates the lower powers, and difficulty introduces a new proportion of merit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_86:A16","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-07","text":"The comparison of perfection departs from a single scale: intensity of image, sufficiency of knowledge, and proportion of merit are distributed into non-convertible measures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_86:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_87:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RMD-08","text":"Comparisons of angels, sexes, and individual passions, serving a local function, are absorbed into a higher structure, where intellectuality, hierarchical order, and mode of operation become dominant criteria.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:D3","CH_GGVC_P_85:D7","CH_GGVC_P_87:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:CGD-01","text":"Comparisons of angel and man and sexual analogies remain local determinations of the account of the image; they do not govern the structure of this group except under the criterion of intellectuality and order of origin.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:D3","CH_GGVC_P_85:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:CGD-02","text":"The exception of rapture, the twofold remedy against error in unknown matters, and the distribution of individual passions remain joints of a lower level, not global principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_87:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:CGD-03","text":"The localization of the image in the mind is amplified upward into the structure of the whole human being, because the same mind through rectitude orders knowledge, appetite, and body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A8","CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:CGD-04","text":"Local distinctions between nature, infusion, and grace are joined in the proposition of the present level: the supernatural gift does not replace human nature, but orders and perfects its operations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_86:D5","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:CGD-05","text":"Lower binary questions concerning image, ignorance, passion, and virtue are surpassed by modal codification: natural or gratuitous, habitual or actual, ordered or dissolved, absolute or proportional.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_86:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GNX-01","text":"The system denies that the created image requires equality, univocity, or identity of nature with God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_85:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GNX-02","text":"Body, figure, and sex are excluded from the formal account of the image, although the body receives the order of the whole state and belongs denominatively to imaginal man.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_87:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GNX-03","text":"Innocence is neither beatitude, nor omniscience, nor angelic knowledge; original perfection retains the limits of human object and mode.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_86:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_87:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GNX-04","text":"Human nature alone cannot causally suffice to institute either gratuitous conformity or the stable obedience of lower things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_87:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_87:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GNX-05","text":"Perfection is not defined by the abolition of every ignorance, passion, or difficulty, but by their right relation to reason, end, and state.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_87:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_87:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:GNX-06","text":"Mental form, magnitude of knowledge, and difficulty of work cannot be objectively neutral; object, office, and proportion to faculty determine their value.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX11","CH_GGVC_P_86:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_87:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that the created mind can have an autonomous end, because image, truth, and rectitude depend on subjection and direction toward God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_86:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that nature and grace constitute two rival forms; grace actualizes the same natural potency and orders it into the architecture of original justice.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that every created integrity is immediately eschatological perfection, because a right state can remain wayfaring, progressive, and defectible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_87:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that sensible or phantastic appearance necessarily determines the intellect, since ordered reason judges lower representations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_86:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that comparison of states can be reduced to one quantity; integrity, difficulty, absolute work, proportion, and end constitute diverse orders of evaluation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_87:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_87:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSA-01","text":"Immediate vision of the divine essence is an ordering absence: because it remains unavailable, mediated knowledge, faith, hope, merit, and the Fall are possible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_86:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_87:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSA-02","text":"The self-sufficient closure of the creature is ontologically inadmissible; neither mind, nor nature, nor rectitude produces its own ultimate account from itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSA-03","text":"The irreversibility of the first state remains unavailable: without it integrity can be true, but not yet immovable or terminal.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:A2","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSA-04","text":"Exhaustive comprehension of God, the Trinity, and circumscribed contingents is excluded from original man; the system posits ordered sufficiency where cognitive totality is absent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_86:A12","CH_GGVC_P_86:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:XD-01","text":"Original justice is the statal actualization of the image: grace causally extends the orientation of the mind toward God into the order of the whole of human nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-03","CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","CL_GGVC_32:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_85:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:XD-02","text":"Reason is the transmissive medium of the system: processions of word and love are received in the mind, truth is judged there, and from there appetite and body participate in order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-02","CL_GGVC_32:GA-03","CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","CH_GGVC_P_85:A10","CH_GGVC_P_86:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_87:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:XD-03","text":"One causality of grace coordinates three effects: conformity of the image, freedom of knowledge from lower impediment, and affective and bodily obedience.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","CL_GGVC_32:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_85:A13","CH_GGVC_P_86:A5","CH_GGVC_P_87:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:XD-04","text":"Original man is a wayfaring image: upright and endowed with grace, yet still moving toward the terminus through mediated knowledge, faith, hope, and merit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-05","CL_GGVC_32:GA-06","CL_GGVC_32:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_86:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:XD-05","text":"The Fall begins as an interior inversion of the relation to God before appearing as external deception or dissolution of the lower powers.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-09","CL_GGVC_32:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_85:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_86:A16","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:XD-06","text":"The permanence of structure and the progress of operation are not opposed: act remains virtually in potency or habit, while experience and revelation increase actual modes and scope.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-07","CL_GGVC_32:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_85:D9","CH_GGVC_P_86:A17","CH_GGVC_P_87:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:XD-07","text":"Original immunity from deception is conditional, not absolute: it holds while interior order remains, but pride of the will can dissolve the very condition of right judgment.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","CL_GGVC_32:GA-09","CL_GGVC_32:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_86:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_86:A16","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSD-01","text":"The ontology of this group is participatory, scalar, and hierarchical: perfection consists in ordered proximity to the exemplar, not in equal possession of divine properties.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSD-02","text":"The human subject becomes a node of mediation: it receives order from God, forms knowledge and love in the mind, and then governs the lower powers and body according to reason.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_86:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSD-03","text":"Anthropological time is branched: creation and grace constitute origin, experience and merit open progression, the Fall changes order, while glory affords consummation not yet present.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_85:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSD-04","text":"Cognitive error acquires a moral and hierarchical state: ignorance can stand with integrity, but false assent indicates a rupture of higher judgment.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_86:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:BSD-05","text":"The Fall is not mere subtraction of perfection, because it diminishes integrity and the absolute magnitude of work, but can increase difficulty and the proportion of merit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_87:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RLE-01","text":"The direction of the mind toward God nourishes right reason; reason orders the lower powers; ordered operations renew conformity of the image and strengthen the initial direction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A15","CH_GGVC_P_86:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_87:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RLE-02","text":"Interior pride breaks the subjection of reason; the rupture makes deception and disordered lower motions possible; these effects amplify further aversion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:A16","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RLE-03","text":"Potency contains act, act exercises habit, habit disposes future acts; the circuit combines the permanence of image and virtue with intermittent operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:D9","CH_GGVC_P_87:A8","CH_GGVC_P_87:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RLE-04","text":"Natural aptitude, conformity of grace, and consummation of glory constitute a path of amplification, but transition to a higher degree effects a modal change rather than substitution of the subject.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A12","CH_GGVC_P_85:A13","CH_GGVC_P_85:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:RLE-05","text":"Glory is the terminal attractor, because it converts mediated knowledge into vision and a defectible will into stability; the first state is ordered around this terminus without enjoying it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:A2","CH_GGVC_P_87:A9","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B01","text":"Divine exemplar → expressed likeness → threshold of intellectuality → image properly so called.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A3","CH_GGVC_P_85:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B02","text":"Intellectual nature conjoined to body → mind operating through phantasms → potency for knowledge and love → natural substrate of the image.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A8","CH_GGVC_P_86:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B03","text":"Grace → subjection of reason to God → obedience of appetite and body → original justice.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_87:A3","CH_GGVC_P_87:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B04","text":"Knowledge → interior word → love → right judgment → ordered passions and virtues.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A10","CH_GGVC_P_86:A15","CH_GGVC_P_87:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B05","text":"Divine object + human office + supernatural end → determination of image, sufficient knowledge, and virtuous action.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A15","CH_GGVC_P_86:A10","CH_GGVC_P_87:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B06","text":"Unconsummated integrity → progress and merit → bifurcation between consummation of glory and inversion through pride → permanent nature with changed order of state.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:A17","CH_GGVC_P_85:A13","CH_GGVC_P_86:A16","CH_GGVC_P_85:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:AFM-B01","text":"Divine exemplar → intellectual nature → mind → word and love → objective orientation toward God → account of the image.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A17","CH_GGVC_P_85:A10","CH_GGVC_P_85:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:AFM-B02","text":"Human mode conjoined to body → original grace → rectitude of reason → unimpeded knowledge → ordered passions and virtues → wayfaring merit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:A4","CH_GGVC_P_87:A3","CH_GGVC_P_87:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:AFM-B03","text":"Integrity not yet glorious → possible progress → interior pride or perseverance → structural Fall or consummation → change in the state of the image.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_87:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_86:A17","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MME-01","text":"The image of God is reclassified from an isolated attribute into an architecture of the whole human being, whose form resides in the mind but whose effects pervade knowledge, affection, and body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MME-02","text":"The perfection of the state is converted from a single scale of fullness into a table of measures: intensity of representation, teleological sufficiency, integrity of order, and proportion of merit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_86:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_87:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MME-03","text":"The body becomes neither the formal seat of the image nor an extrinsic element; it is the lower terminus of governance by the soul and a participant in the integrity of the whole.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_86:A5","CH_GGVC_P_87:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MME-04","text":"Grace and nature are no longer opposed as concurrent forms: infusion preserves the account of human knowledge, while grace advances natural aptitude into supernatural rectitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD6","CH_GGVC_P_86:D5","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MME-05","text":"The Fall is reclassified from added guilt into a topological change of order, which preserves the natural image but inverts relations among God, reason, appetite, and body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of surface are examined the image of God, the knowledge of the first man, grace, passions, virtues, and merit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_86:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_87:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level one distribution operates: the mind forms knowledge and love, reason orders lower things, and integrity removes impediments to this order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A10","CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level apparent opposites are reconstructed through modal distinctions, so that perfection without consummation, ignorance without deception, and passion without disorder can be conceived.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level created reality is participatory and processual: a perfect principle is instituted, acts unfold, merit grows, the Fall breaks order, and glory affords the terminus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological level the form of distinctions makes assertions possible, because it does not erase local contradictions but distributes them into ordered criteria, modes of predication, and non-convertible measures.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT8","CH_GGVC_P_87:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FL-01","text":"The natural image remains in all human beings, but integrity of the whole order requires grace; the exact relation between natural aptitude and gratuitous causality is not fully explained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A12","CH_GGVC_P_87:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FL-02","text":"The account of the image attends principally to acts, although it remains without continuous act; virtual containment in powers and habits provides a necessary but compressed bridge.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A14","CH_GGVC_P_85:D9","CH_GGVC_P_87:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FL-03","text":"Immunity from deception is reconciled with the possibility of the Fall only if sin of the will causally precedes false judgment; interior chronology sustains this joint.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_86:A16","CH_GGVC_P_87:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FL-04","text":"Infused species perfect original knowledge, but the human intellect operates by turning toward phantasms; the exact coordination of the two modes remains less determinate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:A4","CH_GGVC_P_86:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:FL-05","text":"Knowledge initially sufficiently universal simultaneously admits experimental progress; the number of things known and the mode of knowing are distinguished, but their connection remains fragile.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_86:A17","CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MP-01","text":"The account of the image changes from natural aptitude into gratuitous conformity and then into glorious consummation, with the same subject remaining but the mode of participation increasing.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_85:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MP-02","text":"Integrity changes from epistemic immunity into the global structure of original justice, whence truth of intellect becomes part of a broader order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_87:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MP-03","text":"Perfection changes from intensity of likeness into sufficiency for office and finally into a plurality of measures of merit; the term persists, but loses metric unity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_86:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_87:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly an ordered cascade, in which God governs reason, reason appetite, and soul body, while lower degrees represent higher ones without equaling them.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A5","CH_GGVC_P_87:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:HSI-02","text":"The subject is an operative mirror and medium of transmission: within itself it forms word and love, above itself it regards the exemplar, and below itself it coordinates the lower powers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:A10","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:HSI-03","text":"Time is a branched path among origin, exercise, the Fall, and glory; progress is not a continuous ascent, because interior inversion can change the whole state.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_86:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_87:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:HSI-04","text":"The fundamental struggle is between the circulation of descending order and the recursive inversion of pride; navigation remains possible through creatures to God and through reason to governance of lower things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_86:A6","CH_GGVC_P_86:FGS6","CH_GGVC_P_86:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to reconstruct the account of the image, the mode of original knowledge, the causality of grace, the order of passions and virtues, and the plurality of merit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_87:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the nexus between the orientation of the mind toward God, the rectitude of reason, and the obedience of lower things, because three units confirm this nexus in diverse fields.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_87:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B03","text":"Less determinate remains the transition from the analogical account of the image to the order of grace and the inference of supernaturality from the loss of obedience after sin.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_87:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B04","text":"Coherence rests upon distinctions between act and habit, ignorance and deception, nature and integrity; where these distinctions are compressed, fragile joints appear.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_87:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement: the intellectual image expands into the original state of knowledge and will, then opens through defectibility, the Fall, and glory as a temporal machine; the connections between vision and impeccability and between initial grace and consent resist complete reduction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_85:MRT9","CH_GGVC_P_86:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_87:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_32@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_32:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 94–96","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 93 · Prima pars · Quaestio 94 · Prima pars · Quaestio 95"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 97–99","order":33,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_88","CH_GGVC_P_89","CH_GGVC_P_90"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_33@R001","sha256":"e46fea0e047c13d5e015ec90d1bfff04688eed321cb6f37c709c36f637be1e15","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo creatura finita, corporalis, animalis et socialis integra esse possit sine abolitione naturae, causalitatis vel differentiarum suarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis ponit innocentiam non ut naturam alteram, sed ut regimen supernaturale quo processus naturales manent dum defectus poenales et inordinationes inhibentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit apparatus distinctionum proportionalium, quo dominium, passio, immortalitas, generatio et libertas secundum subiectum, finem et modum relationis reclassificantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-04","text":"Motus systematis ab exteriori ordine creaturarum ad conservationem corporis, inde ad generationem et societatem extenditur, eandem formam gubernationis in campis diversis iterans.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-05","text":"Motus non est linearis sed spiralis: natura finita assumitur, eius defectus distinguuntur, eius operationes retinentur, et sola inordinatio excluditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-06","text":"Peccatum non creat corporalitatem, sexualitatem, mortalitatem naturalem aut multiplicitatem, sed ordines earum a fine proprio separat et in repugnantiam convertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-07","text":"Innocentia constituit statum medium: corpus manet animale, naturale et temporaliter dependens, sed non adhuc per mortem, libidinem aut conflictum socialem dominatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-08","text":"Clausura systematis negativa est: innocentia nec autarchia naturae nec gloria finalis fieri potest, quia duratio eius condicionalis, mediata et supra se ordinata manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Integritas naturam non evacuat, sed repugnantiam poenalem, defectum culpabilem et inordinationem finalem removet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Constitutio naturalis creaturae manet sub dono conservante; gratia naturam custodit et ordinat, non speciem eius mutat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_90:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Omnis ordinatio legitima proportionalis est capacitati subiecti recipiendi rationem et fini ad quem dirigitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Dominium transit a potestate univoca ad distributionem imperii, usus et gubernationis secundum differentias naturales et finales.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Integritas dependet a catena subiectionum: anima Deo subiecta corpus conservat, ratio vires inferiores ordinat, et superioritas humana ad bonum aliorum convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:A4","CH_GGVC_P_90:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Libertas non est absentia omnis subiectionis, sed conservatio boni proprii vel communis intra directionem non servilem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Passio, mutatio et delectatio non excluduntur simpliciter; excluditur solum id quod dispositionem naturalem destruit aut appetitum a ratione solvit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Immortalitas innocentiae est conservatio superaddita, condicionalis et temporalis, non proprietas intrinseca corporis neque gloria iam possessa.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Vita animalis, nutritio, generatio, delectatio et multiplicatio manent bona naturalia, si ratione et fine proprio ordinantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Peccatum describitur ut conversio facultatum naturalium in relationes inordinatas, non ut institutio ontologica corporis, sexus aut materiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Gloria terminus est qui innocentiam a perfectione ultima separat, quia corpus innocens adhuc vitam animalem et dependentiam temporalem retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Multitudo non est per se causa discordiae; conflictus nascitur ubi voluntas bonum commune vel competentiam debitam deserit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RMD-01","text":"Dominium identitatem verbalem retinet, sed a relatione exteriori inter hominem et creaturas mutatur in grammaticam generalem ordinis, qua corpus, appetitus et societas diriguntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_89:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RMD-02","text":"Integritas recurrit cum variatione: primo est absentia repugnantiae poenalis, deinde conservatio corporis, postea subiectio appetitus et actuum generativorum rationi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_89:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RMD-03","text":"Animalitas non evanescit, sed transit ab apparentia defectus ad substratum naturalium operationum quae sine dissolutione vel libidine perfici possunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RMD-04","text":"Poena mutat campum: exterior inobedientia creaturarum, corruptio corporis et libido inordinata apparent ut species diversae rupturae unius ordinis gubernativi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:A1","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RMD-05","text":"Aequalitas transit ab oppositione ad disparitatem in condicionem communionis ordinatae, ubi differentia non servitutem nec proprietatem exclusivam necessario producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:CGD-01","text":"Distinctio Angelorum, animalium, plantarum et rerum inanimatarum manet apparatus localis; gradu praesentis synthesis emergit regula universalior qua omnis relatio secundum capacitatem et finem determinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:CGD-02","text":"Cibus, lignum vitae, humidum corporale et imminutio virtutis speciei manent mechanismi locali; structura superior est dependentia integrae vitae a remediis finitis et iterandis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:CGD-03","text":"Determinatio physiologica activi masculini et passivi feminini manet localis; gradu gregis persistit tantum compatibilitas inter naturam sexuatam, generationem et regimen rationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:CGD-04","text":"Distinctiones particulares de natura feroci, somno, excretione vel integritate virginali absorbentur in propositionem altiorem quod natura integra operationes retinet dum defectum ordinativum non patitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:CGD-05","text":"Praelatio socialis, conservatio animae super corpus et dominium rationis super appetitum non sunt eadem relatio localiter, sed componuntur in figuram scalaris gubernationis finaliter limitatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat dominium univocum quo possessio, imperium politicum, usus rerum et gubernatio naturae una potestas indistincta fierent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GNX-02","text":"Systema negat innocentiam ut fugam a corpore, sexualitate, vita animali aut causalitate materiali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GNX-03","text":"Systema negat identitatem inter innocentiam et gloriam, quia conservatio temporalis non est incorruptibilitas finalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GNX-04","text":"Systema negat libertatem ut absolutam insubiectionem et pacem ut suppressionem pluralitatis, differentiae vel communionis rerum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_90:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet quod creatura humana sit centrum autonomum ordinis, quia eius omnis potestas manet intra providentiam, finem communem et superioritatem divinam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet quod apparatus naturalis finitus per se durationem indefinitam aut stabilitatem absolutam producere possit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet quod perfectio finalis iam in integritate originaria contineatur, quia status innocens adhuc transitus, ministerium et dependentia est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSA-01","text":"Systema circumagit impossibilitatem ordinis creati sibi ipsi sufficientis, in quo dominium humanum nullam providentiam, mensuram aut superioritatem recognosceret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSA-02","text":"Systema excludit possibilitatem praesentis conditionis quae se ipsam in gloriam concludat, quia innocentia sine termino superiore in perfectionem finalem false converteretur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSA-03","text":"Systema vetat libertatem sine fine recepto, quia libertas quae nulli bono proprio vel communi respondet in servilem appropriationem aut appetitum inordinatum labitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:XD-01","text":"Integritas operatur per propagationem ordinatam subiectionis: relatio animae ad Deum conditionem conservationis corporis praebet, ratio appetitus moderatur, et gubernatio humana superioritatem in bonum directi convertit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-05","CL_GGVC_33:GA-06","CL_GGVC_33:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_89:A4","CH_GGVC_P_90:A10","CH_GGVC_P_88:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:XD-02","text":"Finitudo naturalis non per abolitionem, sed per mediationes iterandas stabilitur: corpus alimento indiget, senectus remedio resistitur, et multitudo humana per generationem augetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-02","CL_GGVC_33:GA-08","CL_GGVC_33:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_89:A11","CH_GGVC_P_90:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:XD-03","text":"Eadem fractura ordinis in tres regiones distribuitur: inobedientiam inferiorum, dissolutionem corporis et libidinem vel discordiam voluntatum; hae non sunt defectus separati, sed effectus diversi subiectionis ruptae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-01","CL_GGVC_33:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_88:A1","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:XD-04","text":"Multitudo intensificat necessitatem directionis, sed non producit per se conflictum; conflictus oritur tantum cum fines singulares a bono communi aut mensura rationali dissociantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-06","CL_GGVC_33:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_88:A13","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:XD-05","text":"Innocentia emergit ut tertius ordo inter naturam corruptibilem et gloriam consummatam: naturam retinet, corruptionem suspendit, gloriam autem non anticipat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-01","CL_GGVC_33:GA-08","CL_GGVC_33:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_89:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSD-01","text":"Mundus innocens reclassificatur ut regimen ordinativum, non ut ontologia altera: eadem creaturae, corpora et potentiae manent, sed aliter ad fines suos disponuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas efficitur stratificata: donum supernaturale non substituit causas naturales, sed earum defectus impedit, ordines servat et dissolutionem non sinit dominari.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_90:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSD-03","text":"Subiectum humanum non est possessor absolutus, sed minister et relatum medium inter providentiam superiorem, naturam inferiorem et bonum commune.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:D3","CH_GGVC_P_88:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSD-04","text":"Hierarchia et communitas rerum non opponuntur necessario: utraque iusta fit cum excellentia, competentia et communicatio ad utilitatem communem convertuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSD-05","text":"Tempus innocentiae non est immobilitas originaria, sed duratio conservata, remediata et ad aliam formam vitae ordinata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_89:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RLE-01","text":"Circuitus principalis descendit a subiectione animae ad Deum ad conservationem corporis, inde ad dominium rationis super appetitum et ad gubernationem socialem; ruptura superioris nexus inferiores in repugnantiam convertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:A4","CH_GGVC_P_90:A10","CH_GGVC_P_88:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RLE-02","text":"Finitudo corporalis generat circuitum conservationis: imminutio materialis alimentum requirit, imminutio virtutis speciei remedium requirit, et duratio innocens mediis repetendis sustinetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RLE-03","text":"Difficultas apparentis contradictionis in distinctionem convertitur, distinctio operationem naturalem restituit, et operatio restituta ampliorem notionem integritatis producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_90:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RLE-04","text":"Attractor systematis est natura integra: ad eum dominium, impassibilitas, generatio, delectatio et communitas referuntur, sed nullum horum per se totam integritatem definit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FGS-B01","text":"Natura corporalis finita + anima incorruptibilis + multiplicitas creaturarum → persistens differentia inter capacitatem, indigentiam et finem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:A4","CH_GGVC_P_89:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FGS-B02","text":"Subiectio animae ad Deum + ordinatio rationis → conservatio corporis et moderatio virium inferiorum sine destructione vitae animalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:A4","CH_GGVC_P_90:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FGS-B03","text":"Differentia subiectorum + proportio finis → distributio imperii, usus, gubernationis, mutationis perfectivae et actus generativi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:A6","CH_GGVC_P_89:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FGS-B04","text":"Operatio naturalis retenta + defectus destructivus exclusus → nutritio, sensatio, generatio, delectatio et communitas sine inordinatione.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:A7","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FGS-B05","text":"Ruptura subiectionis superioris → defectus inferiores emergentes → mors, libido, inobedientia et conflictus ut effectus distributi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FGS-B06","text":"Conservatio condicionalis + remedia finita + horizon gloriae → innocentia ut status medius, non ut clausura ultima.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:A13","CH_GGVC_P_89:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:AFM-B01","text":"Natura finita et corporalis → quaestio de integritate sine mutatione speciei → recusatio identitatis inter naturam et defectum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:AFM-B02","text":"Recusatio defectus essentialis → distinctio inter operationem naturalem et inordinationem poenalem → retentio vitae animalis, nutritionis et generationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:AFM-B03","text":"Retentio operationum naturalium → subordinatio earum rationi, animae et providentiae → dominium, delectatio et generatio secundum finem proprium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:AFM-B04","text":"Ordinatio finita et condicionalis → separatio innocentiae a gloria → positio temporalis creaturae integrae sed nondum consummatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MME-01","text":"Potestas reordinatur: non quaeritur quis simpliciter dominetur, sed quis quem, quo modo et ad cuius bonum dirigat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MME-02","text":"Oppositio inter naturam et innocentiam solvitur, quia natura corporalis et animalis persistit dum sola repugnantia poenalis removetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MME-03","text":"Sexualitas et delectatio reclassificantur ab indiciis corruptionis ad bona sensibilia quorum moralitas ex modo appetitus dependet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MME-04","text":"Pluralitas socialis reclassificatur ab origine necessaria divisionis et contentionis ad campum communionis rectitudine voluntatum conditionatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei tractat dominium animalium, immortalitatem corporis, necessitatem alimenti, generationem, coitum et communionem rerum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis ostendit dispositionem creaturarum per gradus rationis, corporalitatis, prudentiae, finis et capacitatis recipiendi directionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus difficultates resolvit non per negationem processuum, sed per distinctionem inter naturam, donum, defectum et finem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis mundum innocentem constituit ut naturam conservatam, non glorificatam, cuius duratio pendet a gubernatione et mediis finitis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus distinctiones ipsas performative exercet: quod visum est oppositum in ordinem proportionalem reponitur potius quam simpliciter excluditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FL-01","text":"Causa qua virtus animae supernaturalis corpus materiale universaliter a corruptione praeservat affirmatur, sed mechanismus influxus eius incompletus manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FL-02","text":"Generatio a reparatione mortalitatis separatur et ad multiplicationem individuorum refertur, sed nexus necessarius inter corruptibilitatem naturalem et multiplicationem adhuc non plene demonstratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FL-03","text":"Superioritas non servilis solum fine communi legitimatur; transitus tamen ab excellentia naturali vel morali ad ius gubernandi conditionem additam requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MP-01","text":"Dominium mutatur ex vocabulo potestatis in relationem analogicam cuius forma per subiectum, usum et finem distribuitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MP-02","text":"Immortalitas mutatur ex proprietate corporali apparente in regimen conservationis supernaturale, conditionale et temporaliter finitum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MP-03","text":"Coitus mutatur ex signo libidinis in actum naturalem generativum cuius deformitas non est actui essentialis sed appetitui inordinato.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicita est machina graduata dependentiarum, in qua superiora non naturas inferiores abolent sed earum ordines, fines et defectus disponunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum humanum est relatum medium et ministeriale: ratio non dominatur ut possessor absolutus, sed ut ordinator proprii corporis, aliorum et boni communis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est duratio reparabilis et iterativa, in qua alimentum, remedia, generatio et gubernatio conservant quod adhuc non possidet stabilitatem gloriae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de natura permanente, integritate ordinativa, gubernatione proportionata et gloria non anticipata sufficiunt ad restituendum motum globalem gregis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MRT-B02","text":"Restitutio firmissima est ubi natura, donum et gloria distinguuntur; ibi una structura explicat dominium, corporis conservationem et fecunditatem innocentem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MRT-B03","text":"Quod reductioni axiomaticae resistit est mechanismus singularis influxus supernaturalis et mensura concreta qua delectatio, excellentia et gubernatio a deformitate distinguuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T172900456189Z_c4fd7b2b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 97–99","source_sha256":"e46fea0e047c13d5e015ec90d1bfff04688eed321cb6f37c709c36f637be1e15","sha256":"3fcce0808cb99d1c413c3ae9bfbba67b4f8a2c254290cc1baac4204c21c0ab41","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:29:00.457138+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how a finite, bodily, animal, and social creature can be whole without the abolition of its nature, causality, or differences.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis posits innocence not as a second nature, but as a supernatural governance whereby natural processes remain while penal defects and disorders are inhibited.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-03","text":"The argument becomes an apparatus of proportional distinctions, by which dominion, passion, immortality, generation, and liberty are reclassified according to subject, end, and mode of relation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-04","text":"The movement of the system extends from the external order of creatures to the preservation of the body, thence to generation and society, repeating the same form of governance across diverse fields.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-05","text":"The movement is not linear but spiral: finite nature is assumed, its defects are distinguished, its operations are retained, and disorder alone is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-06","text":"Sin does not create corporeality, sexuality, natural mortality, or multiplicity, but separates their orders from their proper end and converts them into contrariety.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-07","text":"Innocence constitutes an intermediate state: the body remains animal, natural, and temporally dependent, but is not yet dominated through death, lust, or social conflict.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BAS-08","text":"The closure of the system is negative: innocence can become neither the autarky of nature nor final glory, because its duration remains conditional, mediated, and ordered beyond itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Wholeness does not evacuate nature, but removes penal contrariety, culpable defect, and final disorder.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The natural constitution of the creature remains under the preserving gift; grace guards and orders nature, rather than changing its species.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_90:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Every legitimate ordering is proportional to the subject's capacity to receive reason and to the end toward which it is directed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Dominion passes from univocal power to a distribution of command, use, and governance according to natural and final differences.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Wholeness depends on a chain of subordinations: the soul subject to God preserves the body, reason orders the lower powers, and human superiority is converted to the good of others.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:A4","CH_GGVC_P_90:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Liberty is not the absence of every subjection, but the preservation of one's own or common good within non-servile direction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Passion, change, and pleasure are not excluded simply; only that which destroys natural disposition or releases appetite from reason is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] The immortality of innocence is superadded, conditional, and temporal preservation, not an intrinsic property of the body nor glory already possessed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Animal life, nourishment, generation, pleasure, and multiplication remain natural goods, if ordered by reason and their proper end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Sin is described as the conversion of natural faculties into disordered relations, not as the ontological institution of body, sex, or matter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Glory is the terminus that separates innocence from ultimate perfection, because the innocent body still retains animal life and temporal dependence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Multitude is not in itself a cause of discord; conflict arises where the will abandons the common good or due competence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RMD-01","text":"Dominion retains verbal identity, but changes from an external relation between human beings and creatures into a general grammar of order by which body, appetite, and society are directed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_89:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RMD-02","text":"Wholeness recurs with variation: first it is the absence of penal contrariety, then the preservation of the body, later the subjection of appetite and generative acts to reason.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_89:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RMD-03","text":"Animality does not vanish, but passes from the appearance of defect to the substrate of natural operations that can be accomplished without dissolution or lust.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RMD-04","text":"Punishment changes its field: the external disobedience of creatures, corruption of the body, and disordered lust appear as diverse forms of the rupture of one governing order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:A1","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RMD-05","text":"Equality passes from opposition to disparity into the condition of ordered communion, where difference does not necessarily produce servitude or exclusive property.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:ME3","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:CGD-01","text":"The distinction of angels, animals, plants, and inanimate things remains a local apparatus; at the level of the present synthesis there emerges a more universal rule by which every relation is determined according to capacity and end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:CGD-02","text":"Food, the tree of life, bodily moisture, and diminution of the strength of the species remain local mechanisms; the higher structure is the dependence of whole life on finite and repeatable remedies.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:CGD-03","text":"The physiological determination of male active and female passive remains local; at the level of the whole there persists only the compatibility between sexed nature, generation, and the governance of reason.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:CGD-04","text":"Particular distinctions concerning ferocious nature, sleep, excretion, or virginal integrity are absorbed into the higher proposition that whole nature retains operations while suffering no defect of order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:CGD-05","text":"Social precedence, the preservation of the soul over the body, and the dominion of reason over appetite are not locally the same relation, but are composed into a figure of scalar governance limited by its end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GNX-01","text":"The system denies univocal dominion whereby possession, political rule, use of things, and governance of nature would become one undifferentiated power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GNX-02","text":"The system denies innocence as flight from the body, sexuality, animal life, or material causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GNX-03","text":"The system denies identity between innocence and glory, because temporal preservation is not final incorruptibility.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:GNX-04","text":"The system denies liberty as absolute non-subjection and peace as the suppression of plurality, difference, or the communion of things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_90:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that the human creature is the autonomous center of order, because all its power remains within providence, the common end, and divine superiority.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that a finite natural apparatus can of itself produce indefinite duration or absolute stability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that final perfection is already contained in original wholeness, because the innocent state is still transition, ministry, and dependence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSA-01","text":"The system revolves around the impossibility of a created order sufficient to itself, in which human dominion would recognize no providence, measure, or superiority.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSA-02","text":"The system excludes the possibility of a present condition that encloses itself in glory, because innocence without a higher terminus would be falsely converted into final perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSA-03","text":"The system forbids liberty without a received end, because liberty that answers to no proper or common good slips into servile appropriation or disordered appetite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:XD-01","text":"Wholeness operates through the ordered propagation of subjection: the relation of the soul to God provides the condition for preservation of the body, reason moderates appetites, and human governance converts superiority into the good of that which is directed.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-05","CL_GGVC_33:GA-06","CL_GGVC_33:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_89:A4","CH_GGVC_P_90:A10","CH_GGVC_P_88:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:XD-02","text":"Natural finitude is stabilized not through abolition but through repeatable mediations: the body needs food, old age is resisted by a remedy, and human multitude increases through generation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-02","CL_GGVC_33:GA-08","CL_GGVC_33:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_89:A11","CH_GGVC_P_90:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:XD-03","text":"The same fracture of order is distributed into three regions: the disobedience of inferiors, dissolution of the body, and lust or discord of wills; these are not separate defects, but diverse effects of broken subjection.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-01","CL_GGVC_33:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_88:A1","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:XD-04","text":"Multitude intensifies the need for direction, but does not of itself produce conflict; conflict arises only when particular ends are dissociated from the common good or rational measure.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-06","CL_GGVC_33:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_88:A13","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:XD-05","text":"Innocence emerges as a third order between corruptible nature and consummated glory: it retains nature, suspends corruption, but does not anticipate glory.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-01","CL_GGVC_33:GA-08","CL_GGVC_33:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_89:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSD-01","text":"The innocent world is reclassified as an ordering governance, not as another ontology: the same creatures, bodies, and powers remain, but are otherwise disposed toward their ends.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSD-02","text":"Causality becomes stratified: the supernatural gift does not substitute for natural causes, but prevents their defects, preserves their orders, and does not allow dissolution to dominate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_90:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSD-03","text":"The human subject is not an absolute possessor, but a minister and related mean between higher providence, lower nature, and the common good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:D3","CH_GGVC_P_88:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSD-04","text":"Hierarchy and the community of things are not necessarily opposed: each becomes just when excellence, competence, and communication are converted to common utility.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:BSD-05","text":"The time of innocence is not original immobility, but preserved, remedied duration ordered toward another form of life.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_89:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RLE-01","text":"The principal circuit descends from the soul's subjection to God to the preservation of the body, thence to the dominion of reason over appetite and to social governance; rupture of the higher bond converts the lower into contrariety.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:A4","CH_GGVC_P_90:A10","CH_GGVC_P_88:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RLE-02","text":"Bodily finitude generates a circuit of preservation: material diminution requires food, diminution of the strength of the species requires a remedy, and innocent duration is sustained by repeated means.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RLE-03","text":"The difficulty of apparent contradiction is converted into distinction, distinction restores natural operation, and restored operation produces a broader notion of wholeness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_90:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:RLE-04","text":"The attractor of the system is whole nature: dominion, impassibility, generation, pleasure, and community are referred to it, but none of these by itself defines whole wholeness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FGS-B01","text":"Finite bodily nature + incorruptible soul + multiplicity of creatures → persistent difference between capacity, need, and end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:A4","CH_GGVC_P_89:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FGS-B02","text":"Subjection of the soul to God + ordering of reason → preservation of the body and moderation of lower powers without destruction of animal life.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:A4","CH_GGVC_P_90:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FGS-B03","text":"Difference of subjects + proportion of end → distribution of command, use, governance, perfective change, and generative act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:A6","CH_GGVC_P_89:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FGS-B04","text":"Natural operation retained + destructive defect excluded → nourishment, sensation, generation, pleasure, and community without disorder.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:A7","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FGS-B05","text":"Rupture of higher subjection → emerging lower defects → death, lust, disobedience, and conflict as distributed effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_90:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FGS-B06","text":"Conditional preservation + finite remedies + horizon of glory → innocence as an intermediate state, not as ultimate closure.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:A13","CH_GGVC_P_89:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:AFM-B01","text":"Finite and bodily nature → question of wholeness without change of species → refusal of identity between nature and defect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:AFM-B02","text":"Refusal of essential defect → distinction between natural operation and penal disorder → retention of animal life, nourishment, and generation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:AFM-B03","text":"Retention of natural operations → their subordination to reason, soul, and providence → dominion, pleasure, and generation according to their proper end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:AFM-B04","text":"Finite and conditional ordering → separation of innocence from glory → temporal position of a creature whole but not yet consummated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MME-01","text":"Power is reordered: the question is not who simply rules, but who directs whom, in what manner, and toward whose good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MME-02","text":"The opposition between nature and innocence is resolved, because bodily and animal nature persists while penal contrariety alone is removed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MME-03","text":"Sexuality and pleasure are reclassified from signs of corruption into sensible goods whose morality depends on the mode of appetite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MME-04","text":"Social plurality is reclassified from a necessary origin of division and contention into a field of communion conditioned by the rectitude of wills.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level treats dominion over animals, immortality of the body, the need for food, generation, intercourse, and the community of things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level shows the disposition of creatures according to degrees of reason, corporeality, prudence, end, and capacity to receive direction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level resolves difficulties not through negation of processes, but through distinction between nature, gift, defect, and end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level establishes the innocent world as preserved, not glorified, nature, whose duration depends on governance and finite means.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological level performatively exercises the distinctions themselves: what seemed opposed is placed within proportional order rather than simply excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FL-01","text":"It is affirmed that the supernatural power of the soul universally preserves the material body from corruption, but the mechanism of its influence remains incomplete.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FL-02","text":"Generation is separated from the repair of mortality and referred to the multiplication of individuals, but the necessary connection between natural corruptibility and multiplication is still not fully demonstrated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:FL-03","text":"Non-servile superiority is legitimized solely by the common end; yet the transition from natural or moral excellence to the right to govern requires an additional condition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MP-01","text":"Dominion changes from a term of power into an analogical relation whose form is distributed by subject, use, and end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MP-02","text":"Immortality changes from an apparent bodily property into a supernatural, conditional, and temporally finite governance of preservation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MP-03","text":"Intercourse changes from a sign of lust into a natural generative act whose deformity is not essential to the act but to disordered appetite.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_90:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:HSI-01","text":"The implicit reality is a graded machine of dependencies, in which higher things do not abolish lower natures but dispose their orders, ends, and defects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:HSI-02","text":"The human subject is a related and ministerial mean: reason rules not as an absolute possessor, but as an orderer of its own body, of others, and of the common good.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_88:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:HSI-03","text":"Time is reparable and iterative duration, in which food, remedies, generation, and governance preserve what does not yet possess the stability of glory.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MRT-B01","text":"Axioms concerning permanent nature, ordering wholeness, proportionate governance, and unanticipated glory suffice to restore the global movement of the whole.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction is strongest where nature, gift, and glory are distinguished; there one structure explains dominion, preservation of the body, and innocent fecundity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_33:MRT-B03","text":"What resists axiomatic reduction is the singular mechanism of supernatural influence and the concrete measure by which pleasure, excellence, and governance are distinguished from deformity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_89:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_33@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_33:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 97–99","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 96 · Prima pars · Quaestio 97 · Prima pars · Quaestio 98"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 100–102","order":34,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_91","CH_GGVC_P_92","CH_GGVC_P_93"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_34@R001","sha256":"f22e39ef9b612944fb4b6fb0ebcb2b07420db5f661e2461070afd5517bf927d6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BAS-01","text":"Quaestio globalis examinat quomodo status innocentiae generationem, pueritiam, diversitatem sexualem, iustitiam originalem et maturationem intellectualem retinere possit sine lapsu in corruptionem, culpam vel rebellionem corporis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_92:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BAS-02","text":"Thesis communis est innocentiam naturam creatam non destruere, sed naturalem processum generationis et maturationis ab impedimentis corruptionis ordinare.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit distributio causarum: generatio speciem assimilans, corpus organice maturans, anima rationalis a Deo infusa, gratia divinitus collata, et libertas personalis vertibilis non in unam causalitatem reducuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BAS-04","text":"Motus systematis non linearis est, sed recursive progressivus: generatio imperfectum initium producit, rectitudo actus aetati convenientes ordinat, maturatio perfectiones posteriores explicat, et beatitudo ordinem generationis terminaliter claudit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:A12","CH_GGVC_P_92:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BAS-05","text":"Perfectionis vocabularium a perfectione statim possessa ad perfectionem distributam per aetatem, officium, naturam speciei, statum innocentiae et consummationem beatificam transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BAS-06","text":"Clausura systematis non est pueritia immediate adulta, sed incompatibilitas inter vitam animalem generativam et visionem Dei consummantem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Potentia corporis vel animi non absolute, sed per actus subiecto secundum aetatem convenientes mensuratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Generatio naturaliter ab imperfectione initiali ad perfectionem procedit; initium imperfectum non per se corruptionem significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:A2","CH_GGVC_P_93:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Innocentia removet repugnantiam corporis et impedimentum corruptionis, sed naturalem maturationem organorum, sensuum et aetatum non abolit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Perfectio humana est composita: diversitas sexuum, successiones aetatum, actus proprii et ordinatio ad bonum commune naturae eam constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Corpus non est animae externum impedimentum, sed medium necessarium per quod cognitio humana et usus rationis temporaliter operantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Iustitia originalis ad naturam speciei pertinet, dum anima rationalis et gratia non per semen, sed immediate per Deum conferuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Generatio assimilat prolem secundum naturam communem, non tamen omnem perfectionem individualem parentis transmittit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Generatio pertinet ad vitam animalem temporalem; confirmatio beatifica pertinet ad statum in quo talis vita cessat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Visio Dei apertam aversionem excludit, sed eadem ratione ordinem generativum non consummat sed terminat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Integritas originaria hereditario communicari potest sine abolitione libertatis personalis et possibilitatis peccati proprii.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Ordo causalis superior distinguit proprietatem communem naturae, perfectionem individualem et donum divinum immediatum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-01","text":"Defectus recurrit, sed mutatur: defectus generationis et aetatis admittitur, defectus corruptionis et rebellionis excluditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:A6","CH_GGVC_P_93:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-02","text":"Iustitia originalis redit ut principium ordinis corporis et naturae speciei, sed non transit in confirmationem beatificam vel impeccabilitatem hereditariam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-03","text":"Generatio migrat ab instrumento multiplicationis ad structuram vitae animalis et simul ad terminum quem visio beatifica superat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_92:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-04","text":"Similitudo filialis dividitur inter assimilatio naturae communis et non-transmissio perfectionum individualium, scientiae Adae vel confirmationis finalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:A2","CH_GGVC_P_93:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-05","text":"Aetas mutatur ab indice privationis ad regulam mensurandi quid potentia, scientia et ratio in statu dato debeant efficere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_93:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-06","text":"Nescientia mutatur ex apparentia ignorantiae in absentiam scientiae nondum debitae, compatibilem cum cognitione sufficienti ad iustitiam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:A7","CH_GGVC_P_93:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-07","text":"Corpus a materia simpliciter subiecta animae transit ad medium sensitivum et organicum sine quo ratio humana perfecte non operatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:A4","CH_GGVC_P_93:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-08","text":"Diversitas sexus invertitur ab eventu particularis causae defectivae ad complementum positivum naturae universalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:CGD-01","text":"Motus sugendi, gradus determinatus potentiae infantilis, electio sexus prolis et proportio numerica sexuum manent propositiones locales, quia structuram causalitatis totius non definiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:D1","CH_GGVC_P_91:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:CGD-02","text":"Distinctio inter imperfectionem generationis et corruptionem amplificatur ex quaestione de motu infantili in regulam globalem totius status innocentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_93:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:CGD-03","text":"Distinctio inter naturam speciei, accidens individuale et collatio divina fit commissura inter generationem corporalem, gratiam et cognitionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:CGD-04","text":"Diversitas sexuum, gradus aetatum et actus proprii absorbentur in unam rationem perfectionis humanae distributae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:CGD-05","text":"Exemplum perfectionis adulta statim a nativitate praesentis superatur et recodificatur ut falsa aequatio innocentiae cum abolitione progressionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GNX-01","text":"Status innocentiae non definitur ut exemptio a lege qua generatum incipit imperfectum et tempore perficitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GNX-02","text":"Perfectio innocentiae non est imminutio omnis differentiae inter infantem, adultum, parentem, progeniem et beatum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GNX-03","text":"Carentia potentiae vel scientiae non per se poena, culpa aut defectus corruptivus est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GNX-04","text":"Similitudo generationis non est causalitas physica totalis; status naturae, anima, gratia et libertas diversis rationibus originem habent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GNX-05","text":"Vita generativa et beatitudo consummata sub una lege stabiliter coexistere non possunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GNX-06","text":"Masculinitas neque maturitas individualis normam sufficientem complementi naturae humanae constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:DBN-01","text":"Systema excludit paradisum ut statum naturae statice consummatae, in quo generatio, progressus et successiones aetatum iam nihil essentiale operarentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:DBN-02","text":"Systema excludit genealogiam moralem totalem, qua parentes vel culpam vel impeccabilitatem personalem prolis necessario producerent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:DBN-03","text":"Systema excludit intellectum humanum incorporeum, cuius cognitio et ratio sine sensibus, organis et maturatione exerceri possent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:DBN-04","text":"Systema excludit explicationem quae omnes pueriles limitationes ad peccatum originale vel corruptionem corporalem reducat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:DBN-05","text":"Systema excludit identitatem inter rectitudinem initialem et confirmationem beatificam, quia altera ordinem temporalem sustinet, altera illum finit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSA-01","text":"Inadmissibilis est rectitudo quae ordinem aetatis, actus proprios creaturae et differentiam graduum perfectionis dissolvat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSA-02","text":"Inadmissibilis est anthropologia pure naturalis, quae originem, gratiam et consummationem ex sola virtute generationis explicare conetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSA-03","text":"Inadmissibilis est natura humana plena quae corpoream mediationem, sexualem diversitatem et temporalem maturationem superfluas habeat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSA-04","text":"Inadmissibilis est conditio simul perfecte generativa et beatifice consummata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:XD-01","text":"Integritas innocentiae non infantem adultum facit, sed actionem infantilem a repugnantia corruptiva liberat, ut perfectio sit relativa ad aetatem sine esse mera imminutio.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-01","CL_GGVC_34:GA-02","CL_GGVC_34:GA-03","CH_GGVC_P_91:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_93:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:XD-02","text":"Eadem generatio potest iustitiam originalem ad naturam communem pertinentem communicare et scientiam individualem Adae non communicare, quia similitude filialis per strata distribuitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-06","CL_GGVC_34:GA-07","CL_GGVC_34:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_92:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_93:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:XD-03","text":"Donum originarium et libertas personalis non sunt termini contrarii: primum ordinem initialem constituit, secunda historiam moralem singularem apertam relinquit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-06","CL_GGVC_34:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_92:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:XD-04","text":"Quia generatio ad vitam animalem et maturatio ad tempus pertinent, confirmatio beatifica non est gradus generativus ulterior, sed ruptura terminalis ordinis generativi.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-02","CL_GGVC_34:GA-08","CL_GGVC_34:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_92:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:XD-05","text":"Maior subiectio corporis animae non tollit necessitatem corporis ut medii cognitionis, sed mutat qualitatem impedimenti a corruptivo in maturativum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-03","CL_GGVC_34:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_93:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_91:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:XD-06","text":"Complementum naturae humanae fit distributum inter diversitatem sexuum et successionem aetatum, ita ut nullus status individualis totae humanitatis mensura sit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-04","CL_GGVC_34:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_91:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_93:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSD-01","text":"Systema producit anthropologiam integritatis temporalis, in qua bonum originarium non est absentia motus sed ordinatio motus ab imperfecto ad perfectum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_93:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas humana reordinatur in architecturam triplicem: natura communis generatur, perfectiones singulares non necessario propagantur, et anima cum gratia immediate a Deo datur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSD-03","text":"Subiectum humanum non est effectus genealogiae clausae, quia ordinem originarium recipit, animam et gratiam immediate accipit, ac libertatem personalem retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSD-04","text":"Corporeitas reclassificatur ut condicio positiva cognitionis et progressionis, non ut residuum lapsus quod innocentia abolere deberet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSD-05","text":"Iustitia potest esse realis ante scientiam consummatam, quia cognitio universalium principiorum actionem rectam dirigit sine omniscientia particularium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSD-06","text":"Beatitudo non perficit generationem quasi eius maximus effectus, sed mutat genus vitae ita ut generatio ultra eam non procedat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RLE-01","text":"Generatio producit initium imperfectum, initium actus aetati proprios exigit, actus ordinati maturationem sustentant, et maturatio ad perfectionem ulteriorem tendit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_93:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RLE-02","text":"Similitudo specifica iteratur per generationem, sed in omni iteratione causalitas divina animam et gratiam a mera replicatione corporali separat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_92:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RLE-03","text":"Cognitio per sensus, maturatio organorum et usus rationis invicem se limitant, ita ut incrementum scientiae non sit simplex additio informationum sed progressiva habilitatio subiecti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RLE-04","text":"Distinctio inter defectum generationis et defectum corruptionis est attractor interpretativus qui motum, scientiam, rationem et passionem puerilem sub eadem regula ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RLE-05","text":"Confirmatio beatifica functio terminalis est: aversionem claudit, vitam animalem finit, et circuitum generationis non amplificat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RLE-06","text":"Attractor centralis est perfectio relativa: eadem natura aliter perfecta est ut infans, generans, maturus, iustus et beatus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FGS-B01","text":"Natura generans → initium imperfectum → corpus, sensus et ratio gradatim dispositi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FGS-B02","text":"Rectitudo originalis → corpus animae subiectum → exclusio rebellionis et corruptionis → retentio limitum aetatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FGS-B03","text":"Actus aetati convenientes → mensura potentiae debitae → imminutio naturalis non assimilatur culpae vel poenae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FGS-B04","text":"Natura speciei → communicatio iustitiae originalis; perfectio individualis → non-transmissio necessaria; anima et gratia → collatio divina immediata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FGS-B05","text":"Diversitas sexuum + vita animalis → generatio communis → perfectio humana distributa per naturam, aetatem et relationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FGS-B06","text":"Visio Dei aperta → impossibilitas aversionis → terminatio vitae animalis → clausura generationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:AFM-B01","text":"Apparens oppositio inter innocentiam et imperfectionem puerilem → separatio generationis a corruptionis defectu.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:AFM-B02","text":"Separatio imperfectionis naturalis a culpa → mensura potentiae per aetatem et actum debitum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:AFM-B03","text":"Similitudo generativa → distinctio naturae speciei ab accidente individuali → transmissio iustitiae sine transmissione omnis perfectionis parentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:AFM-B04","text":"Donum originarium → collatio divina animae et gratiae → recusatio causalitatis seminis totalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:AFM-B05","text":"Corpus sensitivum et maturatio organica → cognitio sufficiens ad iustitiam → scientia et usus rationis tempore perficiendi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:AFM-B06","text":"Vita animalis generativa → libertas personalis vertibilis → negatio impeccabilitatis hereditariae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:AFM-B07","text":"Confirmatio per visionem Dei → terminatio generationis → clausura terminalis ordinis innocentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MME-01","text":"Oppositio natura-gratia reordinatur in structuram stratificatam, ubi gratia naturam non substituit sed ordinem eius a corruptionis impedimento liberat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MME-02","text":"Oppositio defectus-poena dissolvitur, quia limitationes pueriles causaliter distinguuntur a vulneribus corruptionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MME-03","text":"Transmissio genealogica reclassificatur ut selectiva: natura communis assimilatur, proprietas individualis non necessario sequitur, donum divinum non corporaliter traducitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MME-04","text":"Oppositio scientia-ignorantia dividitur in scientiam perfectam, scientiam tempore debitam et nescientiam rerum nondum debitarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MME-05","text":"Sexus et aetas desinunt esse deviationes a norma individui maturi et fiunt dimensiones complementi naturae humanae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MME-06","text":"Generatio et beatitudo non sunt gradus unius processus quantitativi, sed ordines vitae functionaliter distincti.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei continet quaestiones de motu membrorum puerilium, prole utriusque sexus, iustitia originali, gratia, scientia et usu rationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_92:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis consistit in articulatione generationis, maturationis corporis, sensuum, animae rationalis, gratiae et causalitatis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_93:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus objectiones ex infirmitate, ignorantia, similitudine, gratia et beatitudine convertit in distinctiones causarum, temporum, habituum et actuum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_93:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis naturam humanam describit ut corpoream, sexualiter diversam, genealogice ordinatam et per maturationem perfectibilem, sed ad statum beatificum non generativum ordinatam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_92:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus argumentationem ipsam performative ordinat: omnis oppositio simplicis perfectionis dissolvitur per distributionem distinctionum, quae systema graduatum efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FL-01","text":"Donum divinum quod ex principiis naturae non causatur simul ut accidens naturae speciei propagabile describitur; cohaerentia pendet ex separatione status assimilati et causae efficientis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FL-02","text":"Status sine corruptionis impedimento retinet nimiam humiditatem cerebri et impedimentum organicum puerile; nexus inter haec duo plene non explicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FL-03","text":"Cognitio universalium principiorum sufficientium ad iustitiam affirmatur, dum origo et modus talis cognitionis in puero nondum perfecte usum rationis habente relinquitur indeterminatus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FL-04","text":"Sexus femineus ad intentionem naturae universalis pertinet, sed dispositio eius causalis inter corpus, causam particularem et voluntatem generantis non in unam rationem reducitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FL-05","text":"Integritas originaria hereditaria et vertibilitas personalis simul necessariae sunt; systema eas componit distinctione inter statum naturae et actum liberae electionis, non eorum reductione ad unum principium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MP-01","text":"Defectus mutatur ab apparenti nota peccati in distinctionem inter limitationem generativam admissam et corruptionem exclusam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MP-02","text":"Perfectio mutatur ab omni potentia simul praesenti in ordinem actuum propriorum aetati, muneri et statui convenientium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MP-03","text":"Hereditas mutatur ab imagine physicae transfusionis in structuram mixtam assimilationis specificae et donationis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MP-04","text":"Corpus mutatur ab obiecto simplicis subiectionis ad medium cognitionis cuius maturitas ipsam rationis operationem temporaliter disponit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicita est machina graduata, in qua natura generat initia imperfecta, rectitudo ordinat motum, corpus mediat operationem, et beatitudo ultimam transformationem statuit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:A12","CH_GGVC_P_92:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum humanum est ens corporeum et temporale, cuius ordo originarius non tollit necessitatem discendi, crescendi, eligendi et perficiendi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:HSI-03","text":"Tempus non est sola poena lapsus, sed forma positiva maturationis; corruptio vero est superaddita inordinatio quae talem maturationem gravat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:HSI-04","text":"Certamen humanum non est fuga corporis vel generationis, sed discernere quid naturae, aetati, iustitiae, libertati et termino beatifico competat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de generatione imperfecta, rectitudine non-corruptiva, transmissione selectiva, mediatione corporali et confirmatione terminali sufficiunt ad structuram huius gregis restituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissime nititur distinctione inter naturam creatam et corruptionem superadditam, quae motum, scientiam, rationem et defectum puerilem simul ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent causalitas scientiae initialis sufficientis, relatio humiditatis cerebri ad incorruptionem, et nexus inter dispositionem sexus ac voluntatem generantis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_91:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat, si innocentia intelligitur ut ordinatio progressionis naturalis sine corruptione, non ut suppressio corporis, temporis, generationis aut libertatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T172938708554Z_74959b59","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 100–102","source_sha256":"f22e39ef9b612944fb4b6fb0ebcb2b07420db5f661e2461070afd5517bf927d6","sha256":"0f751bda29eac7a235231b94c8e094b44e00fb1f2a5c681e5ed9c2804b2f7aaf","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:29:38.709449+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BAS-01","text":"The global question examines how the state of innocence can retain generation, childhood, sexual diversity, original justice, and intellectual maturation without falling into corruption, guilt, or rebellion of the body.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_92:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BAS-02","text":"The common thesis is that innocence does not destroy created nature, but orders the natural process of generation and maturation by removing the impediments of corruption.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BAS-03","text":"The argument proceeds by a distribution of causes: generation assimilating offspring to the species, the body maturing organically, the rational soul infused by God, grace divinely bestowed, and convertible personal freedom are not reduced to one causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BAS-04","text":"The movement of the system is not linear but recursively progressive: generation produces an imperfect beginning, rectitude orders acts suitable to age, maturation unfolds subsequent perfections, and beatitude terminally closes the order of generation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:A12","CH_GGVC_P_92:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BAS-05","text":"The vocabulary of perfection is transferred from perfection immediately possessed to perfection distributed according to age, office, the nature of the species, the state of innocence, and beatific consummation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BAS-06","text":"The closure of the system is not childhood immediately adult, but the incompatibility between generative animal life and the consummating vision of God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] The power of body or soul is measured not absolutely, but through acts suitable to the subject according to age.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Generation naturally proceeds from initial imperfection to perfection; an imperfect beginning does not in itself signify corruption.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:A2","CH_GGVC_P_93:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Innocence removes the resistance of the body and the impediment of corruption, but does not abolish the natural maturation of organs, senses, and ages.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Human perfection is composite: diversity of sexes, succession of ages, proper acts, and ordering to the common good of nature constitute it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] The body is not an external impediment to the soul, but a necessary medium through which human cognition and the use of reason operate temporally.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Original justice pertains to the nature of the species, whereas the rational soul and grace are conferred not through seed but immediately by God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Generation assimilates offspring according to common nature, yet does not transmit every individual perfection of the parent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Generation pertains to temporal animal life; beatific confirmation pertains to the state in which such life ceases.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] The vision of God excludes open aversion, but for the same reason does not consummate but terminates the generative order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Original integrity can be communicated hereditarily without abolishing personal freedom and the possibility of one's own sin.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] A higher causal order distinguishes the common property of nature, individual perfection, and the immediate divine gift.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-01","text":"Defect recurs but changes: the defect of generation and age is admitted; the defect of corruption and rebellion is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:A6","CH_GGVC_P_93:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-02","text":"Original justice returns as a principle of the order of the body and the nature of the species, but does not pass into beatific confirmation or hereditary impeccability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-03","text":"Generation migrates from an instrument of multiplication to the structure of animal life and at the same time to a limit surpassed by the beatific vision.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_92:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-04","text":"Filial likeness is divided between assimilation of common nature and the non-transmission of individual perfections, Adam's knowledge, or final confirmation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:A2","CH_GGVC_P_93:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-05","text":"Age changes from an index of privation into a rule for measuring what power, knowledge, and reason ought to effect in a given state.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_93:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-06","text":"Nescience changes from the appearance of ignorance into the absence of knowledge not yet due, compatible with knowledge sufficient for justice.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:A7","CH_GGVC_P_93:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-07","text":"The body passes from matter simply subject to the soul to a sensitive and organic medium without which human reason does not operate perfectly.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:A4","CH_GGVC_P_93:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RMD-08","text":"Diversity of sex is inverted from the result of a particular defective cause into a positive complement of universal nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:CGD-01","text":"The motion of suckling, a determinate degree of infantile power, the choice of the sex of offspring, and the numerical proportion of the sexes remain local propositions, because they do not define the causal structure of the whole.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:D1","CH_GGVC_P_91:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:CGD-02","text":"The distinction between the imperfection of generation and corruption is amplified from a question concerning infantile motion into a global rule for the whole state of innocence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_93:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:CGD-03","text":"The distinction between the nature of the species, individual accident, and divine bestowal becomes the joint between bodily generation, grace, and cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:CGD-04","text":"Diversity of sexes, degrees of ages, and proper acts are absorbed into one account of distributed human perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:CGD-05","text":"The example of adult perfection immediately present from birth is surpassed and recoded as a false equation of innocence with the abolition of progress.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GNX-01","text":"The state of innocence is not defined as exemption from the law by which what is generated begins imperfectly and is perfected in time.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GNX-02","text":"The perfection of innocence is not the diminution of every difference between infant, adult, parent, offspring, and the blessed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GNX-03","text":"Lack of power or knowledge is not in itself punishment, guilt, or a corruptive defect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GNX-04","text":"The likeness of generation is not total physical causality; the state of nature, soul, grace, and freedom have origins in different respects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GNX-05","text":"Generative life and consummated beatitude cannot stably coexist under one law.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:GNX-06","text":"Neither masculinity nor individual maturity constitutes a sufficient norm of the complement of human nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:DBN-01","text":"The system excludes paradise as a state of statically consummated nature, in which generation, progress, and successions of ages would already perform nothing essential.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:DBN-02","text":"The system excludes a total moral genealogy, by which parents would necessarily produce either the guilt or the personal impeccability of offspring.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:DBN-03","text":"The system excludes an incorporeal human intellect whose cognition and reasoning could be exercised without senses, organs, and maturation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:DBN-04","text":"The system excludes an explanation that reduces all childhood limitations to original sin or bodily corruption.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:DBN-05","text":"The system excludes identity between initial rectitude and beatific confirmation, because the former sustains temporal order while the latter ends it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSA-01","text":"Rectitude that dissolves the order of age, the proper acts of the creature, and the difference of degrees of perfection is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSA-02","text":"A purely natural anthropology that attempts to explain origin, grace, and consummation from the power of generation alone is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSA-03","text":"A complete human nature for which bodily mediation, sexual diversity, and temporal maturation are superfluous is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSA-04","text":"A condition simultaneously perfectly generative and beatifically consummated is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:XD-01","text":"The integrity of innocence does not make the infant an adult, but frees infantile action from corruptive resistance, so that perfection is relative to age without being mere diminution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-01","CL_GGVC_34:GA-02","CL_GGVC_34:GA-03","CH_GGVC_P_91:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_93:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:XD-02","text":"The same generation can communicate original justice pertaining to common nature and not communicate Adam's individual knowledge, because filial likeness is distributed through layers.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-06","CL_GGVC_34:GA-07","CL_GGVC_34:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_92:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_93:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:XD-03","text":"The original gift and personal freedom are not contrary terms: the former establishes the initial order; the latter leaves open a singular moral history.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-06","CL_GGVC_34:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_92:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:XD-04","text":"Because generation pertains to animal life and maturation to time, beatific confirmation is not a further generative stage, but a terminal rupture of the generative order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-02","CL_GGVC_34:GA-08","CL_GGVC_34:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_92:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:XD-05","text":"Greater subjection of the body to the soul does not remove the necessity of the body as a medium of cognition, but changes the quality of impediment from corruptive to maturational.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-03","CL_GGVC_34:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_93:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_91:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:XD-06","text":"The complement of human nature is distributed between diversity of sexes and succession of ages, so that no individual state is the measure of the whole of humanity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-04","CL_GGVC_34:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_91:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_93:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSD-01","text":"The system produces an anthropology of temporal integrity, in which original good is not the absence of motion but the ordering of motion from imperfect to perfect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_93:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSD-02","text":"Human causality is reordered into a threefold architecture: common nature is generated, singular perfections are not necessarily propagated, and the soul with grace is immediately given by God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSD-03","text":"The human subject is not the effect of a closed genealogy, because it receives the original order, receives soul and grace immediately, and retains personal freedom.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSD-04","text":"Corporeality is reclassified as a positive condition of cognition and progress, not as a remnant of the fall that innocence ought to abolish.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSD-05","text":"Justice can be real before consummate knowledge, because knowledge of universal principles directs right action without omniscience of particulars.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:BSD-06","text":"Beatitude does not perfect generation as though it were its greatest effect, but changes the kind of life so that generation does not proceed beyond it.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RLE-01","text":"Generation produces an imperfect beginning, the beginning requires acts proper to age, ordered acts sustain maturation, and maturation tends toward further perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:FGS2","CH_GGVC_P_93:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RLE-02","text":"Specific likeness is repeated through generation, but in every iteration divine causality separates soul and grace from mere bodily replication.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_92:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RLE-03","text":"Cognition through the senses, maturation of organs, and use of reason mutually limit one another, so that growth in knowledge is not a simple addition of information but a progressive enabling of the subject.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RLE-04","text":"The distinction between the defect of generation and the defect of corruption is an interpretive attractor that orders infantile motion, knowledge, reason, and passion under the same rule.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RLE-05","text":"Beatific confirmation is a terminal function: it closes aversion, ends animal life, and does not amplify the circuit of generation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:RLE-06","text":"The central attractor is relative perfection: the same nature is differently perfect as infant, generator, mature person, just person, and blessed person.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FGS-B01","text":"Generating nature → imperfect beginning → body, senses, and reason gradually disposed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FGS-B02","text":"Original rectitude → body subject to soul → exclusion of rebellion and corruption → retention of the limits of age.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FGS-B03","text":"Acts suitable to age → measure of due power → natural diminution is not assimilated to guilt or punishment.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FGS-B04","text":"Nature of the species → communication of original justice; individual perfection → no necessary transmission; soul and grace → immediate divine bestowal.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FGS-B05","text":"Diversity of sexes + animal life → common generation → human perfection distributed through nature, age, and relation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FGS-B06","text":"Open vision of God → impossibility of aversion → termination of animal life → closure of generation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:AFM-B01","text":"Apparent opposition between innocence and infantile imperfection → separation of generation from the defect of corruption.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:AFM-B02","text":"Separation of natural imperfection from guilt → measure of power by age and due act.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:AFM-B03","text":"Generative likeness → distinction of the nature of the species from individual accident → transmission of justice without transmission of every perfection of the parent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:AFM-B04","text":"Original gift → divine bestowal of soul and grace → rejection of total causality of seed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:AFM-B05","text":"Sensitive body and organic maturation → knowledge sufficient for justice → knowledge and use of reason to be perfected in time.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:AFM-B06","text":"Generative animal life → convertible personal freedom → negation of hereditary impeccability.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:AFM-B07","text":"Confirmation through the vision of God → termination of generation → terminal closure of the order of innocence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MME-01","text":"The nature-grace opposition is reordered into a layered structure, in which grace does not replace nature but frees its order from the impediment of corruption.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MME-02","text":"The defect-punishment opposition is dissolved, because childhood limitations are causally distinguished from the wounds of corruption.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MME-03","text":"Genealogical transmission is reclassified as selective: common nature is assimilated, individual property does not necessarily follow, and the divine gift is not transmitted bodily.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MME-04","text":"The knowledge-ignorance opposition is divided into perfect knowledge, knowledge due in time, and nescience of things not yet due.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MME-05","text":"Sex and age cease to be deviations from the norm of the mature individual and become dimensions of the complement of human nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MME-06","text":"Generation and beatitude are not stages of one quantitative process, but functionally distinct orders of life.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level contains questions concerning the motion of infantile limbs, offspring of both sexes, original justice, grace, knowledge, and the use of reason.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_92:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level consists in the articulation of generation, bodily maturation, senses, the rational soul, grace, and divine causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_93:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level converts objections from weakness, ignorance, likeness, grace, and beatitude into distinctions of causes, times, habits, and acts.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_93:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level describes human nature as bodily, sexually diverse, genealogically ordered, and perfectible through maturation, yet ordered toward a non-generative beatific state.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_92:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological level performatively orders the argumentation itself: every opposition of simple perfection is dissolved through a distribution of distinctions that produces a graduated system.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FL-01","text":"A divine gift not caused by principles of nature is simultaneously described as an accident of the nature of the species capable of propagation; coherence depends upon separating the assimilated state from the divine efficient cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FL-02","text":"A state without the impediment of corruption retains excessive moisture of the brain and an infantile organic impediment; the connection between the two is not fully explained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FL-03","text":"Knowledge of universal principles sufficient for justice is affirmed, while the origin and mode of such knowledge in a child not yet possessing perfect use of reason is left indeterminate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FL-04","text":"The female sex pertains to the intention of universal nature, but its causal disposition among body, particular cause, and the will of the generator is not reduced to one account.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:FL-05","text":"Hereditary original integrity and personal convertibility are both necessary; the system composes them through a distinction between the state of nature and the act of free choice, not through their reduction to one principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MP-01","text":"Defect changes from an apparent mark of sin into a distinction between admitted generative limitation and excluded corruption.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MP-02","text":"Perfection changes from every power simultaneously present into an order of proper acts suitable to age, office, and state.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MP-03","text":"Inheritance changes from the image of physical transfusion into a mixed structure of specific assimilation and divine donation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_92:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MP-04","text":"The body changes from the object of simple subjection into a medium of cognition whose maturity temporally disposes the very operation of reason.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:HSI-01","text":"The implicit reality is a graduated machine, in which nature generates imperfect beginnings, rectitude orders motion, the body mediates operation, and beatitude establishes the final transformation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:A12","CH_GGVC_P_92:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:HSI-02","text":"The human subject is a bodily and temporal being whose original order does not remove the necessity of learning, growing, choosing, and being perfected.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:HSI-03","text":"Time is not merely the punishment of the fall, but a positive form of maturation; corruption, however, is a superadded disorder that burdens such maturation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:HSI-04","text":"The human task is not flight from body or generation, but discerning what pertains to nature, age, justice, freedom, and the beatific end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_91:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms concerning imperfect generation, non-corruptive rectitude, selective transmission, bodily mediation, and terminal confirmation suffice to restore the structure of this group.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MRT-B02","text":"The structure rests most firmly on the distinction between created nature and superadded corruption, which simultaneously orders motion, knowledge, reason, and infantile defect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MRT-B03","text":"The causality of initial knowledge sufficient for justice, the relation of moisture of the brain to incorruption, and the connection between the disposition of sex and the will of the generator remain less determinate.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_91:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_34:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement if innocence is understood as the ordering of natural progress without corruption, not as the suppression of body, time, generation, or freedom.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_93:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_34@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_34:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 100–102","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 99 · Prima pars · Quaestio 100 · Prima pars · Quaestio 101"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 103–105","order":35,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_94","CH_GGVC_P_95","CH_GGVC_P_96"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_35@R001","sha256":"24c9a4e5d371b88bc1f3230f9b004db896eaacac91b1bfad87fce51c0a35bd4d","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo locus homini congruus, gubernatio universalis et permanentia esse creati sub una ratione dependentiae finaliter ordinatae componantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_95:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis statuit creaturam corporalem et participatam a Deo continue conservari, per causas proportionatas gubernari atque ad finem qui originem et statum praesentem excedit dirigi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_95:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio historico-metaphysica rem corpoream prius stabilit, deinde ex ordine effectuum ad gubernationem ascendit, postremo causalitatem permanentiae ab initiali productione distinguit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_95:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-04","text":"Motus verus est regressus profundior a loco singulari gratiae ad ordinem universalem et ab hoc ordine ad influxum essendi quo uterque sustinetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-05","text":"Progressio fit per distinctiones naturae, status et finis, providentiae et executionis, causae primae et causarum secundarum, possibilitatis absolutae et ordinis actualis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A7","CH_GGVC_P_95:A13","CH_GGVC_P_96:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-06","text":"Difficultas empirica, malum, contingentia, corruptibilitas et possibilitas annihilationis non eliminantur, sed ad gradus locales vel modales reducuntur qui dependentiam universalem non dissolvunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D2","CH_GGVC_P_95:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_96:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-07","text":"Directio systematis est teleologica et temporalis: originem naturalem per statum receptum et actionem participatam ad perfectionem extrinsecam ducit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D6","CH_GGVC_P_95:D3","CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-08","text":"Clausura est ontologica sed asymmetrica: extra causam universalem nihil esse potest, quamvis libera potestas subtrahendi influxum maneat et annihilatio actualis ordini rerum non attribuatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_96:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Omne ens creatum participative est et eius permanentia continuitatem communicationis essendi requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:A2","CH_GGVC_P_96:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-02","text":"[Emergent] Creatio, conservatio et gubernatio sunt momenta distincta unius causalitatis qua res producuntur, sustentantur et ad perfectionem diriguntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Extensio causalitatis universalis et extensio finis universalis determinant eandem amplitudinem gubernationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A5","CH_GGVC_P_95:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Causalitas divina immediata et causalitas creata mediata non sunt aemulae, quia executio secundaria ab ordine causae primae recipitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:D9","CH_GGVC_P_96:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Congruentia cuiuslibet medii vel motionis aestimatur secundum naturam, statum et finem recipientis, non secundum normam univocam omnibus rebus impositam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A7","CH_GGVC_P_95:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Gratia et significatio spiritualis naturam corporalem atque historiam non evacuant, sed eis tamquam mediis realibus utuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_94:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Conservatio complectitur et remotiones particulares corrumpentium et causalitatem per se qua ipsum esse continuatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A5","CH_GGVC_P_96:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Defectus ab ordine particulari realis esse potest, sed extra ordinem universalem subsistere non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_95:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Unitas gubernationis diversitatem naturarum, motuum et causarum non minuit, sed eam tamquam formam perfectionis ordinatae generat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A10","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Actio propria creaturae est effectus perfectionis communicatae: creatura bonum recipit, operatur et aliis causaliter communicat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A9","CH_GGVC_P_95:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Non esse nullum principium positivum constituit; defectus, corruptio et annihilatio tantum per privationem vel subtractionem intelliguntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:A11","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Libertas divina communicationem essendi revocabilem absolute facit, sed ordo actualis stabilitatem creaturarum et non annihilationem universalem statuit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:A10","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Assimilatio ad bonum essentiale fit per receptionem boni, conservationem in bono, motionem ad bonum et causalitatem bonitatis in aliis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_95:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Nulla habitatio temporalis neque status creatus intermedius est finis ultimus; consummatio quaeritur in perfectione, non in annihilatione.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_95:A4","CH_GGVC_P_96:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-01","text":"Conservatio redit cum mutatione: primo significat alimenta et temperiem contra corruptionem, deinde causalitatem ontologicam permanentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A6","CH_GGVC_P_96:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-02","text":"Locus corporeus identitatem retinet, sed a situ geographico in medium proportionatum naturae, status et finis transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A2","CH_GGVC_P_94:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-03","text":"Relatio litterae et spiritus stabilis manet: significatio amplificatur, sed semper super rem materialem et historicam fundatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A12","CH_GGVC_P_94:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-04","text":"Mediatio a dono animae et condicionibus corporalibus ad structuram causarum principalium et secundariarum migrat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D3","CH_GGVC_P_96:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-05","text":"Incorruptibilitas mutatur a beneficio locali primi status in problema universale stabilitatis creaturae radicaliter contingentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A4","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-06","text":"Operatio innocens redit ut causalitas participata: actio propria non opponitur gubernationi, sed perfectionem receptam exsequitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A9","CH_GGVC_P_95:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-07","text":"Exterioritas invertitur: locus potest investigationi humanae exterior manere, sed nihil potest extra ordinem causae universalis esse.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_95:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-08","text":"Proprietates orientales, vegetales et climaticae Paradisi opere locali peracto evanescunt, dum earum functio sub distinctionibus causalibus et finalibus absorbetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_95:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-09","text":"Translatio hominis ex Paradiso ambitum extendit et fit exemplar motus omnis gubernationis a bono proximo ad finem extrinsecum ultimum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D6","CH_GGVC_P_95:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-01","text":"Situs orientalis, nobilitas regionis et quaestio de zona aequinoctiali manent determinationes locales nec in axioma universale convertuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A2","CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-02","text":"Origines fluminum et identitas lignorum pertinent ad defensionem corporalitatis Paradisi, non ad structuram generalem gubernationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_94:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-03","text":"Modus quo virtus animae et donum gratuitum incorruptibilitatem corporis efficiunt manet commissura inferior nondum universaliter explicata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-04","text":"Principium quo res spiritualiter significans etiam materialiter subsistit sursum amplificatur in regulam causalitatis divinae per media realia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_96:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-05","text":"Conservatio paradisiaca per animam, alimenta et aerem amplificatur in structuram stratificatam dependentiae a causa prima et causis mediis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D3","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-06","text":"Translatio ex origine terrena per statum gratiae coniungitur cum directione universi ad finem extrinsecum, atque maiorem transitionem teleologicam efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D6","CH_GGVC_P_95:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-07","text":"Paradisus non probat per se gubernationem universalem, sed fit nodus exemplaris cuius intelligibilitas a distinctione ordinum et causalitate essendi recipitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_95:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-08","text":"Occultatio geographica non universalizatur ut doctrina cosmologica; eius functio argumentativa transfertur ad distinctionem inter cognitionem localem et ordinem universalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D2","CH_GGVC_P_95:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-01","text":"Systema excludit interpretationem spiritualem quae rem corpoream et historice gestam dissolvat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-02","text":"Excluditur creatura quae ex natura propria incorruptibilitatem, conservationem vel finem sufficientem possideat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_95:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-03","text":"Excluditur oppositio secundum quam causalitas divina immediata causalitatem creatam mediatam tollere debeat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_96:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-04","text":"Excluditur deductio plurium principiorum ultimorum ex diversitate motuum, conflictibus vel causalitatibus secundis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_95:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-05","text":"Excluditur eventus, malum vel actus voluntarius qui ordinem causae universalis totaliter excedat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:NX10","CH_GGVC_P_95:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-06","text":"Excluditur aequatio corruptionis naturalis cum annihilatione entis completi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_96:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-07","text":"Excluditur Paradisus ut principium naturale, habitatio definitiva aut finis ultimus hominis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_94:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-08","text":"Excluditur transitus immediatus a potentia divina absolute considerata ad assertionem eventus intra ordinem rerum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet mundum post productionem tamquam opus a causa essendi separatum per se permanere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_96:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet media corporalia vel causae secundae independentiam conferre, cum eorum causalitas ipsa a primo ordine causali dependeat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D3","CH_GGVC_P_95:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet subiectum rationale se ipsum gubernando a gubernatione divina eximi.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_95:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet aliquod bonum intramundanum, etiam Paradisus vel ordo universi, rationem finis ultimi obtinere.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_95:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet non esse causalitati positivae symmetricum esse, quasi nihilum proprium principium aut inclinationem habeat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_96:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse debet unitatem ordinis tantum per abolitionem diversitatis, contingentiae et defectus constitui posse.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:D5","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas ontologica maxime inadmissibilis est exterioritas realis a causa universali, quia talis exterioritas cum non esse coincideret.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_95:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSA-02","text":"Inadmissibilis est creatura quae originem a Deo recipiat, sed permanentiam vel finem ex se sola possideat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_96:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSA-03","text":"Nihilum non potest fieri attractor occultus cosmologiae: possibilitas subtrahendi influxum manet, sed consummatio rerum per evanitionem universalem excluditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_96:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSA-04","text":"Systema circumagit absentiam mensurae univocae qua locus, causalitas, necessitas et gubernatio eodem modo in omnibus gradibus iudicentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_95:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSA-05","text":"Habitatio creata sibi sufficiens et definitive clausa est indisponibilis: locus bonus semper statum receptum sustinet et ad finem ulteriorem refertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_94:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-01","text":"Paradisus fit diagramma locale totius causalitatis: res corporalis conservatur per media, regitur per obedientiam et ordinatur ad perfectionem quae locum praesentem excedit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-02","CL_GGVC_35:GA-05","CL_GGVC_35:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_94:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-02","text":"Fundamentum historicum et corporeum requirit ut providentia non per symbola vacua, sed per causas et condiciones materiales vere operetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-04","CL_GGVC_35:GA-06","CH_GGVC_P_94:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-03","text":"Creatio hominis extra Paradisum et translatio subsequens exhibent formam universalem qua principium naturale a fine extrinseco distinguitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-05","CL_GGVC_35:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_94:A11","CH_GGVC_P_95:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-04","text":"Donum animae et condiciones paradisiacae sunt causae secundae conservationis status, dum permanentia ipsius esse principaliter a causa prima dependet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-04","CL_GGVC_35:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_94:D3","CH_GGVC_P_96:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-05","text":"Amissio Paradisi per culpam est defectus verus boni particularis, sed non ruptura ordinis universalis, quia ipsum damnum gratuitatem boni et consequentiam gubernationis manifestat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-08","CL_GGVC_35:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_94:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_95:A16","CH_GGVC_P_95:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-06","text":"Inaccessibilitas empirica, contingentia causarum proximarum et necessitas formalis creaturae indicant limites relationis localis, non independentiam rei ab ordine universali.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-03","CL_GGVC_35:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_94:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_95:D10","CH_GGVC_P_96:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-07","text":"Possibilitas annihilationis radicalem dependentiam creaturae ostendit, sed habitatio temporalis et ordo actualis ad translationem atque conservationem, non ad evanitionem, diriguntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-12","CL_GGVC_35:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_94:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-08","text":"Operatio, custodia et causalitas secundaria sunt modi quibus creatura perfectionem receptam exercet, non modi quibus a primo gubernante se emancipat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-04","CL_GGVC_35:GA-10","CL_GGVC_35:GA-13","CH_GGVC_P_94:A9","CH_GGVC_P_95:A11","CH_GGVC_P_96:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSD-01","text":"Realitas creata non est substantia semel posita et postea autonoma, sed stabilitas continue recepta cui propria causalitas intra dependentiam constituitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:D1","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSD-02","text":"Tempus creaturae est trajectoria conservata ab origine per status mutabiles ad finem, non simplex extensio virtutis finitae nec regressus necessarius in nihilum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_96:D8","CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSD-03","text":"Spatium reordinatur teleologice: locus est dispositio corporalis in qua natura sustentatur, status manifestatur et transitus ad finem praeparatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_94:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSD-04","text":"Potentia agendi creaturae augetur, non minuitur, per dependentiam ordinatam, quia perfectio recepta causalitatem bonitatis in aliis producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A14","CH_GGVC_P_95:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSD-05","text":"Malum, culpa et corruptio statum localem vere mutant, sed nullum alterum universum causaliter constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_95:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSD-06","text":"Cognitio systematis requirit transitum ab apparentia particulari ad gradum causalitatis competentem, quia obiectio fit falsa cum ordinem localem pro universali accipit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_95:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSD-07","text":"Consummatio non est reditus rerum ad nihilum neque permanentia in habitatione intermedia, sed perfectio naturae participatae sub fine extrinseco.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_94:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_95:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RLE-01","text":"Esse receptum → conservatio continua → facultas agendi → communicatio boni → assimilatio amplior ad primam bonitatem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:A2","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RLE-02","text":"Causa prima instituit media → media effectus conservant et movent → ordo mediatorum perfectionem causae primae manifestat → mediatio ipsa iterum ad primam causam reducitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:A8","CH_GGVC_P_95:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RLE-03","text":"Obiectio localis → distinctio graduum → reclassificatio defectus → reinclusio in ordine universali constituit circuitum argumentativum recurrentem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A15","CH_GGVC_P_95:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RLE-04","text":"Donum gratuitum → obedientia custodiens → possibilitas amissionis → manifestatio gratuitatis doni format circuitum paradisiacum status.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A9","CH_GGVC_P_94:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RLE-05","text":"Unitas causae → diversitas naturarum et motuum → causalitas secundaria → perfectio unitatis distributae est via amplificationis gubernationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A10","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RLE-06","text":"Corruptibilitas → media contra corruptionem → conservatio per se → dependentia continua ab influxu essendi profunditatem causalitatis gradatim auget.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_96:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_96:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RLE-07","text":"Attractor systematis est bonum essentiale, ad quod loca, status, operationes, media et defectus per diversos ordines referuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A7","CH_GGVC_P_95:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FGS-B01","text":"Res historica et corporalis → condiciones loci proportionatae → conservatio status creati.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_94:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FGS-B02","text":"Distinctio naturae, status et finis → separatio originis, habitationis et consummationis → transitus teleologicus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A7","CH_GGVC_P_94:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FGS-B03","text":"Productio ex bonitate → conservatio in esse → gubernatio ad perfectionem → continuum causalitatis divinae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A3","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FGS-B04","text":"Providentia immediata → executio per causas secundas → diversitas operationum → unitas ordinis communicata.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A13","CH_GGVC_P_95:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FGS-B05","text":"Defectus particularis → relatio ad causam proximam → reductio ad causam universalem → negatio exterioritatis ontologicae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A15","CH_GGVC_P_95:A17","CH_GGVC_P_95:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FGS-B06","text":"Libera communicatio essendi → possibilitas subtractionis → contingentia radicalis → perseverantia actualis sub ordine instituto.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:A10","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FGS-B07","text":"Negatio autonomiae, causalitatis aemulae et nihili positivi → restitutio positiva mundi ut dependentiae ordinatae, mediatae et finalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_96:D3","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:AFM-B01","text":"Locus corporeus gratiae → congruentia naturae et status → gubernatio universalis → executio mediata → conservatio ontologica → directio ad finem extrinsecum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A2","CH_GGVC_P_95:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:AFM-B02","text":"Difficultas empirica vel defectus localis → distinctio scalae causalis → negatio exterioritatis → clausura universalis sine abolitione contingentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_95:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:AFM-B03","text":"Origo extra habitationem privilegiatam → donum conservans → actio participata → amissibilitas status → translatio finalis sine annihilatione.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A11","CH_GGVC_P_94:A9","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MME-01","text":"Locus ex spatio geometrico in relationem proportionis inter corpus, statum, gratiam et finem reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MME-02","text":"Conservatio ex custodia negativa contra corrumpens in communicationem praesentem ipsius esse mutatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_96:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MME-03","text":"Unitas non amplius uniformitatem significat, sed ordinem distributum diversarum naturarum, motionum et causalitatum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MME-04","text":"Contingentia, casus et malum ex regionibus extra providentiam in proprietates relationum localium ad causas proximas transferuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_95:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MME-05","text":"Causalitas dividitur secundum gradus fiendi, essendi, providentiae et executionis, sine fragmentatione primi principii.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_95:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MME-06","text":"Oppositio inter naturam et gratiam solvitur per statum in quo donum supernaturale media naturalia assumit et ad finem ulteriorem ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D3","CH_GGVC_P_94:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MME-07","text":"Finis cosmologicus ex evanitione vel clausura intramundana in conservationem ordinatam et perfectionem participatam refiguratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_95:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei complectitur Paradisum corporeum, gubernationem omnium rerum et quaestionem de conservatione vel annihilatione creaturae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_95:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis constituitur per participationem essendi, distinctionem causarum principalium et secundariarum atque ordinationem mediorum ad fines.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus obiectiones dissolvit non negando phaenomena, sed mutando scalam qua locus, defectus, causalitas et possibilitas iudicantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_95:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis realitatem ut esse participatum continue receptum, tempus autem ut durationem conservatam et ad finem directam producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus et performativus per reditum objectionis, distinctionis et reinclusionis ipsam unitatem distributam quam affirmat argumentando efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_95:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FL-01","text":"Corporalitas Paradisi firmiter affirmatur, sed situs physicus exactus incompletus manet; occultatio servat doctrinam sine plena determinatione cosmographica.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_94:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FL-02","text":"Conservatio corporis per virtutem animae et eius gratuitus character simul ponuntur, sed nexus causalis inter virtutem naturalem et donum non plene exprimitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_94:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FL-03","text":"Voluntas localiter resistere et culpam contrahere potest, quamvis omnis inclinatio a primo movente ad bonum derivetur; imputabilitas in hac duplici causalitate tensionem retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A18","CH_GGVC_P_95:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FL-04","text":"Immediata causalitas divina et vera causalitas secundarum componuntur per ordinem graduum, sed nexus modalis quo una alteram non absorbet ulterius non demonstratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:D3","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FL-05","text":"Possibilitas annihilationis ex libertate divina sequitur, dum eius non eventus ex naturis creatis et manifestatione bonitatis dependet; secunda conclusio primam non simpliciter convertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MP-01","text":"Conservatio sensum mutat cum a remotione corrumpentis ad dependentiam ipsius esse transit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:A6","CH_GGVC_P_96:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MP-02","text":"Locus sensum mutat cum ex positione terrestri fit expressio ordinis metaphysici inter naturam, gratiam et beatitudinem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A2","CH_GGVC_P_94:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MP-03","text":"Defectus sensum mutat cum ex apparentia exterioritatis fit privatio ordinis particularis intra causalitatem universalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A16","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MP-04","text":"Finis sensum amplificat cum habitatio bona sed temporalis subordinatur bono extrinseco quod totum ordinem gubernationis claudit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_95:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:HSI-01","text":"Imago occulta est mundus tamquam habitatio concentrica: loca particularia, causae mediae et status recipiuntur intra campum unius influxus essendi et unius finis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est nodus participativus qui bonum recipit, per actionem propriam communicat et localiter deficere potest, sed extra ordinem universalem exire non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_95:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est continuata receptio esse per successionem originis, status, amissionis et perfectionis, non motus naturalis versus nihilum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_96:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga ex causalitate divina, sed transitus inter ordines particulares per obedientiam, actionem, defectum et reintegrationem finalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D5","CH_GGVC_P_95:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata participationis, conservationis continuae, gubernationis finalis et mediationis causalis sufficiunt ad machinam principalem huius gregis restituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_95:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est coextensio causae universalis, conservationis essendi et finis universalis, quae ordines locales sine eorum abolitione continet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B03","text":"Systema hoc gradu cohaeret quia Paradisus, gubernatio et conservatio non aggregantur, sed ut gradus localis, universalis et ontologicus eiusdem dependentiae leguntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B04","text":"Nondum satis determinantur situs physicus Paradisi, modus cooperationis animae et gratiae, nexus modalitatis contingentis cum providentia atque ratio plena non annihilationis actualis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_95:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat si progressionem non ut seriem trium quaestionum, sed ut descensum a dispositione status ad gubernationem et ab gubernatione ad causalitatem essendi exprimit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_96:AF1","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B06","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae resistunt singularitas historica Paradisi et libera voluntas divina, quia prima non in purum exemplum dissolvitur et altera ordinem actualem sine necessitate naturali sustinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_96:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T173023276691Z_49348dbc","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 103–105","source_sha256":"24c9a4e5d371b88bc1f3230f9b004db896eaacac91b1bfad87fce51c0a35bd4d","sha256":"822e2685916d70c89a580eba55a1a2682d318a72c095d587988d81bc19a19e72","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:30:23.277639+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how a place fitting for man, universal governance, and the permanence of created being are combined under a single account of finally ordered dependence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_95:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis posits that corporeal and participated creature is continually conserved by God, governed through proportionate causes, and directed to an end that exceeds its origin and present state.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_95:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-03","text":"The historical-metaphysical argument first establishes corporeal reality, then ascends from the order of effects to governance, and finally distinguishes the causality of permanence from initial production.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_95:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-04","text":"The true movement is a deeper regression from the singular place of grace to the universal order and from this order to the influx of being by which both are sustained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-05","text":"The progression occurs through distinctions of nature, state, and end; providence and execution; first cause and secondary causes; absolute possibility and actual order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A7","CH_GGVC_P_95:A13","CH_GGVC_P_96:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-06","text":"Empirical difficulty, evil, contingency, corruptibility, and the possibility of annihilation are not eliminated, but reduced to local or modal levels that do not dissolve universal dependence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D2","CH_GGVC_P_95:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_96:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-07","text":"The direction of the system is teleological and temporal: it leads natural origin through received state and participated action to extrinsic perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D6","CH_GGVC_P_95:D3","CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BAS-08","text":"The closure is ontological but asymmetrical: nothing can be outside the universal cause, although the free power to withdraw influx remains and actual annihilation is not attributed to the order of things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_96:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every created being is participative, and its permanence requires continuity of the communication of being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:A2","CH_GGVC_P_96:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-02","text":"[Emergent] Creation, conservation, and governance are distinct moments of one causality by which things are produced, sustained, and directed to perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] The extension of universal causality and the extension of the universal end determine the same scope of governance.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A5","CH_GGVC_P_95:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Immediate divine causality and mediate created causality are not rivals, because secondary execution is received from the order of the first cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:D9","CH_GGVC_P_96:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The fittingness of any means or motion is assessed according to the nature, state, and end of the recipient, not according to a univocal norm imposed on all things.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A7","CH_GGVC_P_95:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Grace and spiritual signification do not empty corporeal nature and history, but use them as real means.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_94:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Conservation includes both particular removals of corrupting agents and the per se causality by which being itself is continued.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A5","CH_GGVC_P_96:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] A defect from a particular order can be real, but it cannot subsist outside the universal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_95:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] The unity of governance does not diminish the diversity of natures, motions, and causes, but generates it as a form of ordered perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A10","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] The proper action of a creature is an effect of communicated perfection: the creature receives good, acts, and causally communicates it to others.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A9","CH_GGVC_P_95:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Non-being constitutes no positive principle; defect, corruption, and annihilation are understood only through privation or withdrawal.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:A11","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Divine freedom makes the communication of being absolutely revocable, but the actual order establishes the stability of creatures and not universal annihilation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:A10","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Assimilation to essential goodness occurs through the reception of good, conservation in good, motion toward good, and the causality of goodness in others.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_95:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] No temporal habitation or intermediate created state is the ultimate end; consummation is sought in perfection, not annihilation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_95:A4","CH_GGVC_P_96:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-01","text":"Conservation recurs with mutation: at first it signifies nourishment and temperateness against corruption, then the ontological causality of permanence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A6","CH_GGVC_P_96:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-02","text":"Corporeal place retains its identity, but is transferred from geographical location to a medium proportioned to nature, state, and end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A2","CH_GGVC_P_94:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-03","text":"The relation of letter and spirit remains stable: signification is amplified, but always founded upon material and historical reality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A12","CH_GGVC_P_94:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-04","text":"Mediation migrates from the gift of the soul and corporeal conditions to the structure of principal and secondary causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D3","CH_GGVC_P_96:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-05","text":"Incorruptibility changes from a local benefit of the first state into the universal problem of the stability of radically contingent creature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A4","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-06","text":"Innocent operation recurs as participated causality: proper action is not opposed to governance, but carries out received perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A9","CH_GGVC_P_95:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-07","text":"Exteriority is inverted: a place can remain exterior to human investigation, but nothing can be outside the order of the universal cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_95:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-08","text":"The eastern, vegetal, and climatic properties of Paradise vanish once their local function has been accomplished, while their function is absorbed under causal and final distinctions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_95:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RMD-09","text":"The translation of man from Paradise extends the scope and becomes the model of every movement of governance from proximate good to the ultimate extrinsic end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D6","CH_GGVC_P_95:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-01","text":"Eastern location, the nobility of the region, and the question of the equinoctial zone remain local determinations and are not converted into a universal axiom.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A2","CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-02","text":"The origins of rivers and the identity of trees pertain to the defense of the corporeality of Paradise, not to the general structure of governance.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_94:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-03","text":"The manner in which the power of the soul and gratuitous gift bring about the incorruptibility of the body remains a lower-level joint not yet universally explained.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-04","text":"The principle by which something spiritually signifying also subsists materially is amplified upward into a rule of divine causality through real means.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_96:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-05","text":"Paradisiacal conservation through soul, nourishment, and air is amplified into a layered structure of dependence on the first cause and intermediate causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D3","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-06","text":"Translation from earthly origin through the state of grace is joined to the direction of the universe toward an extrinsic end, and produces a greater teleological transition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D6","CH_GGVC_P_95:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-07","text":"Paradise does not by itself prove universal governance, but becomes an exemplary node whose intelligibility is received from the distinction of orders and the causality of being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_95:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:CGD-08","text":"Geographical concealment is not universalized as a cosmological doctrine; its argumentative function is transferred to the distinction between local knowledge and universal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D2","CH_GGVC_P_95:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-01","text":"A spiritual interpretation that dissolves corporeal and historically enacted reality is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-02","text":"A creature that possesses incorruptibility, conservation, or a sufficient end from its own nature is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_95:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-03","text":"The opposition according to which immediate divine causality must remove mediate created causality is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_96:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-04","text":"The deduction of several ultimate principles from diversity of motions, conflicts, or secondary causalities is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_95:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-05","text":"An event, evil, or voluntary act that wholly exceeds the order of the universal cause is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:NX10","CH_GGVC_P_95:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-06","text":"The equation of natural corruption with the annihilation of a complete being is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_96:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-07","text":"Paradise as a natural principle, definitive habitation, or man's ultimate end is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_94:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:GNX-08","text":"The immediate passage from divine power considered absolutely to the assertion of an event within the order of things is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that the world, after production, persists through itself as a work separated from the cause of being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_96:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that corporeal means or secondary causes confer independence, since their very causality depends upon the first causal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D3","CH_GGVC_P_95:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that a rational subject, by governing itself, exempts itself from divine governance.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_95:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that any intramundane good, even Paradise or the order of the universe, obtains the character of ultimate end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_95:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that non-being is symmetrical with positive causality, as though nothingness had its own principle or inclination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_96:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that unity of order can be constituted only through the abolition of diversity, contingency, and defect.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:D5","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSA-01","text":"The most inadmissible ontological possibility is real exteriority from the universal cause, because such exteriority would coincide with non-being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_95:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSA-02","text":"A creature that receives origin from God, but possesses permanence or end from itself alone, is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_96:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSA-03","text":"Nothingness cannot become the hidden attractor of cosmology: the possibility of withdrawing influx remains, but the consummation of things through universal disappearance is excluded.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_96:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSA-04","text":"The system revolves around the absence of a univocal measure by which place, causality, necessity, and governance are judged in the same way at every level.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_95:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSA-05","text":"A self-sufficient and definitively closed created habitation is unavailable: a good place always sustains a received state and is referred to a further end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_94:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-01","text":"Paradise becomes a local diagram of all causality: corporeal reality is conserved through means, governed through obedience, and ordered to a perfection that exceeds its present place.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-02","CL_GGVC_35:GA-05","CL_GGVC_35:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_94:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-02","text":"The historical and corporeal foundation requires that providence operate truly not through empty symbols, but through material causes and conditions.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-04","CL_GGVC_35:GA-06","CH_GGVC_P_94:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-03","text":"The creation of man outside Paradise and his subsequent translation display the universal form by which natural beginning is distinguished from extrinsic end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-05","CL_GGVC_35:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_94:A11","CH_GGVC_P_95:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-04","text":"The gift of the soul and paradisiacal conditions are secondary causes of conserving the state, while the permanence of being itself depends principally on the first cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-04","CL_GGVC_35:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_94:D3","CH_GGVC_P_96:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-05","text":"The loss of Paradise through fault is a real defect of a particular good, but not a rupture of universal order, because the loss itself manifests the gratuitousness of the good and the consequence of governance.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-08","CL_GGVC_35:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_94:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_95:A16","CH_GGVC_P_95:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-06","text":"Empirical inaccessibility, the contingency of proximate causes, and the formal necessity of creature indicate the limits of local relation, not the independence of the thing from universal order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-03","CL_GGVC_35:GA-08","CH_GGVC_P_94:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_95:D10","CH_GGVC_P_96:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-07","text":"The possibility of annihilation shows the radical dependence of creature, but temporal habitation and actual order are directed toward translation and conservation, not disappearance.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-12","CL_GGVC_35:GA-14","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_94:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:XD-08","text":"Operation, custody, and secondary causality are modes by which creature exercises received perfection, not modes by which it emancipates itself from the first governor.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-04","CL_GGVC_35:GA-10","CL_GGVC_35:GA-13","CH_GGVC_P_94:A9","CH_GGVC_P_95:A11","CH_GGVC_P_96:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSD-01","text":"Created reality is not a substance once posited and thereafter autonomous, but continuously received stability in which proper causality is constituted within dependence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:D1","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSD-02","text":"The time of creature is a trajectory conserved from origin through mutable states to an end, not a simple extension of finite power nor a necessary return into nothingness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_96:D8","CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSD-03","text":"Space is teleologically reordered: place is a corporeal disposition in which nature is sustained, state is manifested, and transition to the end is prepared.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_94:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSD-04","text":"The creature's power of acting is increased, not diminished, through ordered dependence, because received perfection produces the causality of goodness in others.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A14","CH_GGVC_P_95:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSD-05","text":"Evil, fault, and corruption truly alter local state, but do not causally constitute another universe.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_95:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSD-06","text":"Knowledge of the system requires passage from particular appearance to the competent level of causality, because an objection becomes false when it takes local order for universal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_95:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:BSD-07","text":"Consummation is neither the return of things to nothingness nor permanence in an intermediate habitation, but the perfection of participated nature under an extrinsic end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_94:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_95:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RLE-01","text":"Received being → continuous conservation → capacity for acting → communication of good → broader assimilation to first goodness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:A2","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RLE-02","text":"The first cause establishes means → means conserve and move effects → the order of mediators manifests the perfection of the first cause → mediation itself is again reduced to the first cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:A8","CH_GGVC_P_95:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RLE-03","text":"Local objection → distinction of levels → reclassification of defect → reinclusion in universal order constitutes a recurrent argumentative circuit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A15","CH_GGVC_P_95:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RLE-04","text":"Gratuitous gift → preserving obedience → possibility of loss → manifestation of the gratuitousness of the gift forms the paradisiacal circuit of state.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A9","CH_GGVC_P_94:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RLE-05","text":"Unity of cause → diversity of natures and motions → secondary causality → perfection of distributed unity is the path of the amplification of governance.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A10","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RLE-06","text":"Corruptibility → means against corruption → conservation per se → continuous dependence on the influx of being gradually deepens causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_96:NX1","CH_GGVC_P_96:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:RLE-07","text":"The attractor of the system is essential goodness, to which places, states, operations, means, and defects are referred through diverse orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A7","CH_GGVC_P_95:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FGS-B01","text":"Historical and corporeal reality → proportionate conditions of place → conservation of created state.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_94:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FGS-B02","text":"Distinction of nature, state, and end → separation of origin, habitation, and consummation → teleological transition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A7","CH_GGVC_P_94:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FGS-B03","text":"Production from goodness → conservation in being → governance toward perfection → continuum of divine causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A3","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FGS-B04","text":"Immediate providence → execution through secondary causes → diversity of operations → communicated unity of order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A13","CH_GGVC_P_95:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FGS-B05","text":"Particular defect → relation to proximate cause → reduction to universal cause → negation of ontological exteriority.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A15","CH_GGVC_P_95:A17","CH_GGVC_P_95:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FGS-B06","text":"Free communication of being → possibility of withdrawal → radical contingency → actual perseverance under established order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:A10","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FGS-B07","text":"Negation of autonomy, rival causality, and positive nothingness → positive restoration of the world as ordered, mediated, and final dependence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_96:D3","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:AFM-B01","text":"Corporeal place of grace → fittingness of nature and state → universal governance → mediate execution → ontological conservation → direction to an extrinsic end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A2","CH_GGVC_P_95:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:AFM-B02","text":"Empirical difficulty or local defect → distinction of causal scale → negation of exteriority → universal closure without abolition of contingency.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_95:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:AFM-B03","text":"Origin outside privileged habitation → conserving gift → participated action → lossability of state → final translation without annihilation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A11","CH_GGVC_P_94:A9","CH_GGVC_P_96:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MME-01","text":"Place is reclassified from geometrical space into a relation of proportion among body, state, grace, and end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MME-02","text":"Conservation changes from negative protection against a corrupting agent into the present communication of being itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_96:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MME-03","text":"Unity no longer signifies uniformity, but a distributed order of diverse natures, motions, and causalities.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MME-04","text":"Contingency, chance, and evil are transferred from regions outside providence into properties of local relations to proximate causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:ME5","CH_GGVC_P_95:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MME-05","text":"Causality is divided according to the levels of becoming, being, providence, and execution, without fragmentation of the first principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_95:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MME-06","text":"The opposition between nature and grace is resolved through a state in which supernatural gift assumes natural means and orders them to a further end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D3","CH_GGVC_P_94:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MME-07","text":"The cosmological end is refigured from disappearance or intramundane closure into ordered conservation and participated perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:SNX3","CH_GGVC_P_95:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level includes corporeal Paradise, the governance of all things, and the question of the conservation or annihilation of creature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_95:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level is constituted through participation in being, the distinction of principal and secondary causes, and the ordering of means to ends.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level dissolves objections not by denying phenomena, but by changing the scale at which place, defect, causality, and possibility are judged.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_95:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level produces reality as participated being continuously received, and time as conserved duration directed to an end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological and performative level, through the return of objection, distinction, and reinclusion, produces in arguing the very distributed unity that it affirms.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_95:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FL-01","text":"The corporeality of Paradise is firmly affirmed, but its exact physical location remains incomplete; concealment preserves the doctrine without full cosmographical determination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_94:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FL-02","text":"Conservation of the body through the power of the soul and its gratuitous character are posited together, but the causal nexus between natural power and gift is not fully expressed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_94:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FL-03","text":"The will can locally resist and incur fault, although every inclination is derived from the first mover toward good; imputability retains tension in this double causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A18","CH_GGVC_P_95:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FL-04","text":"Immediate divine causality and the true causality of secondary causes are combined through an order of levels, but the modal nexus by which one does not absorb the other is not further demonstrated.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:D3","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:FL-05","text":"The possibility of annihilation follows from divine freedom, while its non-occurrence depends on created natures and the manifestation of goodness; the second conclusion does not simply reverse the first.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MP-01","text":"Conservation changes meaning when it passes from the removal of a corrupting agent to dependence of being itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_96:A6","CH_GGVC_P_96:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MP-02","text":"Place changes meaning when from a terrestrial position it becomes an expression of the metaphysical order among nature, grace, and beatitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:A2","CH_GGVC_P_94:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MP-03","text":"Defect changes meaning when from the appearance of exteriority it becomes a privation of particular order within universal causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:A16","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MP-04","text":"End expands in meaning when a good but temporal habitation is subordinated to the extrinsic good that closes the whole order of governance.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_95:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:HSI-01","text":"The hidden image is the world as concentric habitation: particular places, intermediate causes, and states are received within the field of one influx of being and one end.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a participative node that receives good, communicates it through proper action, and can locally fail, but cannot exit universal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_95:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:HSI-03","text":"Time is the continued reception of being through the succession of origin, state, loss, and perfection, not a natural movement toward nothingness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_96:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_96:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is not flight from divine causality, but passage among particular orders through obedience, action, defect, and final reintegration.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:D5","CH_GGVC_P_95:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of participation, continuous conservation, final governance, and causal mediation suffice to reconstruct the principal mechanism of this whole.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_95:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the coextension of universal cause, conservation of being, and universal end, which contains local orders without abolishing them.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B03","text":"The system coheres at this level because Paradise, governance, and conservation are not aggregated, but read as local, universal, and ontological levels of the same dependence.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT6","CH_GGVC_P_95:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_96:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B04","text":"The physical location of Paradise, the mode of cooperation between soul and grace, the nexus of contingent modality with providence, and the full account of actual non-annihilation are not yet sufficiently determined.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:MRT3","CH_GGVC_P_95:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement if it expresses the progression not as a series of three questions, but as a descent from the disposition of state to governance and from governance to the causality of being.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_95:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_96:AF1","CH_GGVC_P_96:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B06","text":"The historical singularity of Paradise and divine free will resist axiomatic reduction, because the former is not dissolved into a pure example and the latter sustains actual order without natural necessity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_94:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_96:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_35@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_35:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 103–105","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 102 · Prima pars · Quaestio 103 · Prima pars · Quaestio 104"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 106–108","order":36,"parents":["CH_GGVC_P_97","CH_GGVC_P_98","CH_GGVC_P_99"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_36@R001","sha256":"b15bdb7da0450bc3894c32a819fb9c87ca84f1e9694bc504422cf97b49963b1e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo causalitas divina immediata, operatio spiritualis creata et communicatio inter intellectus simul constitui possint sine confusione ordinum aut evacuatione agentium secundorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_98:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_99:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BAS-02","text":"Quaestio non amplius inter actionem Dei et actionem creaturae disiungit, sed originem potentiae, ministerium creatum et destinationem voluntariam tamquam modos causalitatis inaequales componit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BAS-03","text":"Thesis globalis est dependentiam totalem agentis creati veram operationem eius constituere, dummodo formatio interior, manifestatio veritatis et ordinatio conceptus secundum principia distincta distribuantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BAS-04","text":"Argumentatio metaphysico-hierarchica oppositiones apparentes resolvit non per aequationem, sed per distinctiones inter causam primam et secundam, illuminationem et persuasionem, veritatem et voluntatem, extensionem et receptionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_98:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_99:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BAS-05","text":"Motus cumulativus et ramificatus a dominio divino super actus ad mediationem intellectualem, inde ad canales selectivos locutionis procedit; clausura eius est topologia ordinata, non cessatio communicationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_99:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Omnis potentia creata originem, conservationem et actualem applicationem a causa prima recipit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A11","CH_GGVC_P_98:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Totalis dependentia a causa prima non abolet operationem propriam causae secundae, sed eam ut perfectionem participatam constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A8","CH_GGVC_P_97:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Immediatio diversimode operatur: Deus intime causat et conservat, angelus autem sine signo corporeo conceptum manifestat, quin principium interioris potentiae efficiat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_99:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Origo luminis intellectualis et motus sufficientis voluntatis soli Deo competit; creatura spiritualis exercitium intellectus confortat, obiectum proponit aut conceptum destinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A5","CH_GGVC_P_98:A7","CH_GGVC_P_98:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Distinctio inter causalitatem originariam et ministerialem ordinat totum systema: communicatio creata vera est, sed neque mentem creat neque voluntatem possidet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_98:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Illuminatio procedit a prima veritate per superiorem ad inferiorem atque verum universalius cognitum capacitati recipientis accommodat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A5","CH_GGVC_P_99:A6","CH_GGVC_P_99:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Voluntas creata non est regula veritatis neque motor sufficiens voluntatis alienae, sed attentionem propriam, persuasionem obiectivam et destinationem conceptus gubernat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A10","CH_GGVC_P_99:A2","CH_GGVC_P_99:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Receptio perfectionis mensuram recipientis sequitur; communicatio igitur communionem auget sine identitate graduum vel modorum cognitionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A12","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Ordo causalis non est una series simplex: ordo naturae, hierarchia illuminationis et circulatio locutionis canales distintos cum directionibus propriis constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A12","CH_GGVC_P_98:A11","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Communicatio intellectualis potest esse illocalis sine eo quod sit publica, quia extensio operationis et determinatio recipientis causis diversis reguntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:A9","CH_GGVC_P_99:A10","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Libertas actus et secretum conceptus ex regimine principii intrinseci oriuntur, non ex independentia causali vel opacitate materiali.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A7","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Miraculum terminum ordinis naturalis indicat, sed non fit exemplar universale cuiuslibet mediationis spiritualis aut cuiuslibet exceptionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_98:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-01","text":"Immediatio ab intima causalitate divina ad manifestationem angelicam sine signo corporeo transit; nomen persistit, sed ratio causalitatis ab origine actus ad absentiam mediationis sensibilis mutatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ME6","CH_GGVC_P_98:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_99:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-02","text":"Motus primo materiam, intellectum et voluntatem sub causa prima comprehendit, deinde restringitur secundum facultatem creaturae spiritualis, tandem fit conversio voluntaria intellectus ad conceptum et auditorem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_98:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_99:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-03","text":"Voluntas mutatur ab obiecto motionis divinae in principium persuasionis limitatae, attentionis propriae et clausurae selectivae conceptus.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A6","CH_GGVC_P_98:A10","CH_GGVC_P_99:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-04","text":"Ordo causarum inclusarum redit ut hierarchia illuminationis unidirectionalis, sed postea a circulatione locutionis voluntariae secernitur neque totam communicationem absorbet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A12","CH_GGVC_P_98:D6","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-05","text":"Universalitas a virtute divina determinabili ad veritatem superiorem accommodandam migrat, sed in communicatione illocali non amplius universalitatem receptionis significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A3","CH_GGVC_P_98:A5","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-06","text":"Manifestatio primo effectum extraordinarium causae occultae designat, deinde traditionem veritatis, tandem ordinationem voluntariam conceptus; identitas vocabuli tres functiones distinctas non aequat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A14","CH_GGVC_P_98:A2","CH_GGVC_P_99:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-07","text":"Miraculum, postquam limitem causalitatis naturalis constituit, munere structurali peracto recedit: eius praetermissio non transfertur in legem ordinariam substantiarum spiritualium.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:FGS5","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-08","text":"Corpus et locus ab obiectis motus in condiciones negativas communicationis mutantur: contactus, signum sensibile et distantia successive removentur, dum voluntas munus discriminationis suscipit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_99:A5","CH_GGVC_P_99:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:CGD-01","text":"Gradatio miraculorum secundum substantiam, subiectum et modum effectus localis manet; ad structuram gregis ascendit tantum principium quod excessus ordinis inferioris ordinem superiorem non destruit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A16","CH_GGVC_P_97:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:CGD-02","text":"Nova revelatio usque ad iudicium proprietas localis historiae angelicae est; sursum amplificatur ut exemplum novitatis materialis intra formam hierarchicam stabilem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A13","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:CGD-03","text":"Locutio ad se ipsum et intermittens consultatio divina localiter explicant actus angelicos; hoc gradu efficiunt distinctionem inter temporalitatem attentionis et stabilitatem potentiae intellectualis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:D2","CH_GGVC_P_99:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:CGD-04","text":"Distinctio causarum primi et secundi ordinis e campo generali causalitatis ad ministerium cognitionis ampliatur atque operationem angelicam sine usurpatione originis interioris sustinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:CGD-05","text":"Mensura recipientis illuminationem et destinationem locutionis coniungit: prima determinat quomodo verum recipiatur, altera quis omnino recipiat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A5","CH_GGVC_P_99:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:CGD-06","text":"Transitus maior fit ab operatione Dei in omni agente ad operationem creaturae in altera creatura, deinde ad structuram communicationis cuius accessus voluntarie regitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_98:D1","CH_GGVC_P_99:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:CGD-07","text":"Exemplar secundum quod omnis relatio spiritualis simpliciter descendit obsolescit: illuminatio descendit, locutio autem voluntaria etiam ascendere aut lateraliter dirigi potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_99:D3","CH_GGVC_P_99:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GNX-01","text":"Systema excludit causalitatem divinam et creatam tamquam vires univocas eiusdem ordinis quae pro eodem spatio efficaciae certent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GNX-02","text":"Excluditur disiunctio qua aut Deus vere operatur aut creatura alterius intellectum vere perficit; negatur tamen creaturae dominium originarium super potentiam interiorem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_98:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GNX-03","text":"Manifestare veritatem non est creare lumen, formare mentem aut efficaciter movere voluntatem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GNX-04","text":"Unidirectionalitas illuminationis non permittitur totam communicationem spiritualem definire, quia locutio voluntaria relationes ascensivas et selectivas recipit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_99:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GNX-05","text":"Absentia loci et corporis neque transparentiam mentium neque publicitatem necessariam cuiuslibet conceptus producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GNX-06","text":"Excluduntur et natura causaliter clausa et exceptio sine ordine; praetermissio ordinis inferioris intra ordinem causae primae manet neque in omnibus campis aequaliter admittitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_98:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GNX-07","text":"Libertas et secretum non fundantur in absentia causae superioris vel in clausura corporis, sed in integritate principii intrinseci et in non-destinatione voluntaria.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_99:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem efficientiam ad unum ordinem causalitatis pertinere; aliter concursus divinus et ministerium angelicum aut duplicationem aut evacuationem agentis creati producerent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet naturam intellectualem communem accessum immediatum ad omnes conceptus efficere, quia tunc voluntas nullum secretum aut destinatarium constituere posset.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_99:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet illuminationem et locutionem esse transmissiones univocas; prima normam veritatis et directionem hierarchicam, secunda dominium destinationis exprimit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet unum exemplar ordinis corporalia et spiritualia eodem modo regere, quia dispensabilitas ordinis naturalis et stabilitas hierarchiae spiritualis dissimiliter ponuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet communicationem perfectionis aequalitatem producentis et recipientis exigere; gradus permanet quia receptio contentum secundum capacitatem contrahit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet voluntatem creatam normam veritatis fieri posse, quamvis ipsa canales attentionis et manifestationis vere instituat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:A8","CH_GGVC_P_99:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas creaturae sibi sufficientis, quae potentiam interiorem a causa prima non recipiat aut perfectionem alterius originarie constituat, ontologice inadmissibilis est.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_98:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSA-02","text":"Impossibilis est reciprocitas qua Deus per locutionem creaturae cognitionem accipiat; omnis relatio ad eum consultationem, receptionem vel admirationem creaturae relinquit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_99:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSA-03","text":"Systema neque interiorem conceptum absolute publicum neque mentem absolute clausam admittit: occultatio et communicatio circa actum destinationis voluntariae ordinantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:A4","CH_GGVC_P_99:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSA-04","text":"Possibilitas ordinis sine causa prima vel exceptionis omnino informis excluditur; etiam circulatio non-hierarchica locutionis intra facultates et fines divinitus datos operatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_98:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-01","text":"Dependentia ontologica agentis spiritualis a Deo condicionem, non impedimentum, causalitatis eius ministerialis constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02","CL_GGVC_36:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_97:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-02","text":"Concursus causarum diversorum ordinum explicat quomodo illuminatio sit vera operatio angeli, quamvis lumen, potentia et norma veritatis a Deo procedant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-02","CL_GGVC_36:GA-04","CL_GGVC_36:GA-06","CH_GGVC_P_97:A9","CH_GGVC_P_98:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-03","text":"Actio circa voluntatem in tres gradus distinguitur: Deus inclinationem intrinsecam efficaciter movet, angelus obiectum persuasive proponit, loquens autem proprium conceptum destinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-04","CL_GGVC_36:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_97:A6","CH_GGVC_P_98:A10","CH_GGVC_P_99:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-04","text":"Hierarchia spiritualis topologiam duplicem habet: veritas illuminativa descendit secundum gradum, dum locutio voluntaria directiones ascensivas, descensivas et selectivas aperit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-06","CL_GGVC_36:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_97:A12","CH_GGVC_P_98:D6","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-05","text":"Distinctio primae veritatis a voluntate creata scindit communicationem in perfectionem communem regulatam et informationem privatam destinatam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-06","CL_GGVC_36:GA-07","CL_GGVC_36:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_98:A7","CH_GGVC_P_99:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-06","text":"Illocalitas operationis et mensura recipientis simul ostendunt extensionem possibilem, aptitudinem receptionis et actualem destinationem esse condiciones irreducibiles.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-08","CL_GGVC_36:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_99:A9","CH_GGVC_P_99:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-07","text":"Comparatio miraculi cum hierarchia angelica demonstrat praetermissionem ordinis esse proprietatem domain-specificam, non consequentiam universalem omnipotentiae divinae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-09","CL_GGVC_36:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_97:A13","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-08","text":"Eadem grammatica principii intrinseci libertatem actus et secretum conceptus sustinet: causa superior facultatem constituit, sed actus proprius secundum inclinationem vel destinationem agentis manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-07","CL_GGVC_36:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_97:A7","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSD-01","text":"Ontologia, epistemologia et communicatio unam structuram stratificatam efficiunt: esse et potentia a Deo, perfectio cognitiva per ministros, accessus ad conceptum per voluntariam destinationem distribuuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-05","CL_GGVC_36:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_97:A11","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum spirituale neque substantia sui iuris neque merus canalis est, sed operator dependens qui perfectionem recipit, secundum modum suum agit et accessum ad propria concepta moderatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-02","CL_GGVC_36:GA-08","CL_GGVC_36:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_97:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_98:A12","CH_GGVC_P_99:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSD-03","text":"Tempus systematis est duplex: conservatio divina continua et forma hierarchica stabilis novitates revelationis, consultationis et actionis successive recipiunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-06","CL_GGVC_36:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_97:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_99:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSD-04","text":"Quanto profundior dependentia creaturae demonstratur, tanto subtilius eius operatio propria distinguitur; intensificatio causalitatis primae non evacuationem, sed articulationem mediationum producit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02","CL_GGVC_36:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_97:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSD-05","text":"Auctoritas spiritualis fit functione canalium distincta: superioritas cognitionis regit illuminationem, sed non concedit dominium totale super voluntatem, secretum aut omnem locutionem inferioris.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-06","CL_GGVC_36:GA-07","CL_GGVC_36:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_98:D6","CH_GGVC_P_99:D3","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSD-06","text":"Miraculum et secretum sunt figurae limitis oppositae: primum ostendit ordinem naturalem non exhaurire causalitatem, alterum ostendit communitatem intellectualem non exhaurire accessum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-10","CL_GGVC_36:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_97:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_99:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSD-07","text":"Cognitio communicata requirit separationem contenti, modi receptionis et iuris accessus: idem verum inaequaliter recipitur, dum conceptus voluntarius etiam omnino retineri potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-08","CL_GGVC_36:GA-10","CL_GGVC_36:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_99:A4","CH_GGVC_P_99:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RLE-01","text":"Causa prima potentiam creatam producit et conservat → creatura proprie operatur → operatio bonum participatum communicat → effectus ordinem causae primae manifestat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A11","CH_GGVC_P_97:A8","CH_GGVC_P_98:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RLE-02","text":"Revelatio divina → cognitio universalior superioris → distinctio secundum capacitatem inferioris → confortatio intellectus → nova materia communicationis hierarchicae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A5","CH_GGVC_P_98:D1","CH_GGVC_P_98:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RLE-03","text":"Conceptus habitualis → attentio mota a voluntate → actualis consideratio → destinatio ad alterum → manifestatio → nova consultatio vel responsio.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:A1","CH_GGVC_P_99:A3","CH_GGVC_P_99:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RLE-04","text":"Via amplificationis procedit a motu materiae et potentiarum interiorum ad causalitatem inter spiritus, inde ad distinctionem canalium, recipientium et graduum publicitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_98:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_99:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RLE-05","text":"Attractor principalis est immediatio ordinata: causa superior intime praesens manet, dum media creata tanto verius operantur quanto accuratius a causalitate originaria distinguuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RLE-06","text":"Retentio conceptus et eius destinatio circuitum aperiunt in quo interioritas non communicationi opponitur, sed materiam selectivam communicationis constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:A4","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FGS-B01","text":"Productio et conservatio potentiae → dependentia ontologica → applicatio ad actum → operatio propria causae secundae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_97:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FGS-B02","text":"Distinctio ordinum causalium → simultas actionis primae et secundae → exclusio competitionis → constitutio ministerii creati.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A9","CH_GGVC_P_97:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FGS-B03","text":"Prima veritas → cognitio superior universalior → accommodatio ad capacitatem inferiorem → illuminatio descendens → perfectio sine aequatione.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A5","CH_GGVC_P_98:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FGS-B04","text":"Voluntas creata → actualis consideratio conceptus → determinatio recipientis → manifestatio sine signo corporeo → locutio directione libera.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:A1","CH_GGVC_P_99:A10","CH_GGVC_P_99:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FGS-B05","text":"Modus recipientis + principium contenti + destinatio loquentis → determinatio qualitatis, directionis et publicitatis communicationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_99:A6","CH_GGVC_P_99:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FGS-B06","text":"Excessus ordinis inferioris → manifestatio contingentiae eius → conservatio ordinis primi; secretum conceptus → manifestatio limitis communitatis → conservatio dominii voluntarii.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:AFM-B01","text":"Dependentia ontologica → concursus non competitivus → distinctio operationum interiorum → ministerium angelicum → illuminatio hierarchica → locutio voluntaria → selectio recipientis → communitas intellectualis ordinata sed non totaliter publica.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_98:D1","CH_GGVC_P_99:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:AFM-B02","text":"Ordo causarum → possibilitas excessus naturalis → stabilitas mediationis spiritualis → bifurcatio veritatis et voluntatis → clausura in pluralitate canalium sub una causa prima.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A13","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MME-01","text":"Causalitas reordinatur ex campo competitivo in verticalem simultatem ordinum, ubi dependentia et actio propria se mutuo constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_97:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MME-02","text":"Immediatio in tres species resolvitur: praesentia causae primae in actu, manifestatio sine signo corporeo et accessus non impeditus distantia.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_99:D1","CH_GGVC_P_99:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MME-03","text":"Hierarchia a praelatione totali in distributionem functionum mutatur: gradus cognitionis regit illuminationem, non omnem locutionem aut voluntatem.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MME-04","text":"Communicatio non amplius per locum aut communitatem naturae describitur, sed per principium contenti, modum recipientis et destinationem voluntariam.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_99:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MME-05","text":"Libertas et secretum ad structuram principii intrinseci referuntur, unde causalitas superior et selectio propria sine contradictione componuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_99:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MME-06","text":"Ordinarium et extraordinarium non sunt absoluta opposita: utrumque per relationem ordinis inferioris ad causam superiorem definitur, sed dispensabilitas secundum campum variatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_98:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei examinantur motus creaturarum, illuminatio angelica, inclinatio voluntatis, locutio, distantia et determinatio auditoris.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_98:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_99:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali operationes distribuuntur inter causam originariam, causas secundas, hierarchiam veritatis et canales voluntariae manifestationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_98:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_99:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico oppositiones inter immediationem et mediationem, libertatem et motionem, hierarchiam et reciprocitatem in distinctiones ordinum et functionum convertuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_98:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_99:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est participatio continue conservata, intra quam formae stabiles ordinis novitates revelationis, attentionis et actionis recipiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_98:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_99:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico forma systematis propriam thesim efficit: singulae attributiones causalitatis per negationes limitantur, deinde in canales non competitivos recompinguntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_98:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_99:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FL-01","text":"Terminus inter motionem divinam intellectus et confortationem angelicam eius exercitii manet subtilis, quia utraque actum cognitionis attingit sed sola prima potentiam et similitudinem causaliter fundat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A5","CH_GGVC_P_98:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FL-02","text":"Persuasio voluntatem inclinare dicitur, sed motus voluntatis creaturae negatur; cohaerentia pendet ex discrimine inter obiectum propositum et causalitatem intrinsecam potentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A10","CH_GGVC_P_98:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FL-03","text":"Invariabilitas descensus illuminationis cum locutione inferioris ad superiorem componitur tantum si hierarchia ad veritatem perfectivam, non ad omnem manifestationem, restringitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_99:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FL-04","text":"Communitas illuminationum et receptio graduata tensionem servant: veritas potest communis dici, quamvis modus, plenitudo et fortasse actualis comprehensio communes non sint.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:D8","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FL-05","text":"Visio mutua angelorum in Verbo cum secreto conceptus voluntarii non plene formalizatur; systema distinctionem inter personam visam et conceptum destinatum praesupponit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FL-06","text":"Ordo naturalis divinitus praeteriri potest, ordo autem spiritualis analogam dispensationem non recipit; ratio huius asymmetriae affirmatur firmius quam ex principiis communibus deducitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_98:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MP-01","text":"Immediatio sensum mutat cum ab actione ontologica causae primae ad manifestationem conceptus sine instrumento corporeo transit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_99:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MP-02","text":"Illuminatio mutatur ab imagine infusionis luminis in operationem epistemicam quae virtutem datam confortat et veritatem distinguit.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_98:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MP-03","text":"Voluntas transit ab eo quod a solo Deo sufficienter movetur ad principium creatum quod attentionem, persuasionem et accessum communicativum ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A9","CH_GGVC_P_99:A1","CH_GGVC_P_99:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MP-04","text":"Hierarchia scalaris in reticulum multiplex mutatur cum fluxui descendenti veritatis circulatio voluntaria locutionis superponitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:D6","CH_GGVC_P_99:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina ordinum inclusorum, in qua fons primus potentias constituit, media creata operationes proprias exercent et canales diversi effectus distribuunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A12","CH_GGVC_P_97:FGS4","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum spirituale est simul relatum et ianua: perfectionem ab alio recipit, sed proprium conceptum retinet vel destinat neque in campum publicum dissolvitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A12","CH_GGVC_P_99:A4","CH_GGVC_P_99:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est fluxus novitatis per structuram stabilem: conservatio continuatur, revelationes accedunt, consultatio intermittitur, hierarchia tamen directionem suam retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_98:A13","CH_GGVC_P_99:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga ab ordine, sed electio recti canalis: causare, illuminare, persuadere, loqui et occultare secundum potestates inaequales discernuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_99:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_99:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata extracta sufficiunt ad gregem restituendum, si dependentia causalitatis creatae, monopolium divinum circa principia interiora, receptio graduata et destinatio voluntaria simul serventur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_98:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_99:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est distinctio originis a ministerio, quia ex ea concursus causalis, illuminatio non creativa et locutio selectiva sine mutua evacuatione derivantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_98:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_99:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent limes persuasionis et motionis voluntatis, ratio invariabilitatis hierarchiae spiritualis et compatibilitas visionis mutuae cum secreto particulari.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_98:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_99:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat, quia a causalitate universali ad mediationem graduatam et inde ad destinationem selectivam progreditur; reductioni tamen resistit analogia imperfecta inter locutionem ad creaturam et locutionem receptivam ad Deum.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:MRT7","CH_GGVC_P_99:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_99:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T173108147105Z_fe7c7b3d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 106–108","source_sha256":"b15bdb7da0450bc3894c32a819fb9c87ca84f1e9694bc504422cf97b49963b1e","sha256":"a03f9e1f973d937b5a659e84f6d0c47747a0c5bc862e7ad0086337145f11852b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:31:08.148049+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how immediate divine causality, created spiritual operation, and communication among intellects can be constituted together without confusion of orders or the emptying of secondary agents.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_98:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_99:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BAS-02","text":"The question no longer disjoins God's action from the creature's action, but combines the origin of power, created ministry, and voluntary destination as unequal modes of causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BAS-03","text":"The global thesis is that the total dependence of the created agent constitutes its true operation, provided that interior formation, manifestation of truth, and ordering of the concept are distributed according to distinct principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BAS-04","text":"The metaphysical-hierarchical argument resolves apparent oppositions not through equation, but through distinctions between primary and secondary cause, illumination and persuasion, truth and will, extension and reception.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:AS3","CH_GGVC_P_98:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_99:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BAS-05","text":"The cumulative and branching movement proceeds from divine dominion over acts to intellectual mediation, thence to selective channels of speech; its closure is an ordered topology, not a cessation of communication.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:AS5","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_99:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every created power receives from the first cause its origin, conservation, and actual application.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A11","CH_GGVC_P_98:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Total dependence on the first cause does not abolish the proper operation of the secondary cause, but constitutes it as a participated perfection.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A8","CH_GGVC_P_97:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Immediacy operates in different ways: God causes and preserves intimately, whereas an angel manifests a concept without a bodily sign, without thereby effecting the principle of interior power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_99:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] The origin of intellectual light and the sufficient motion of the will belong to God alone; the spiritual creature strengthens the exercise of the intellect, proposes an object, or directs a concept.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A5","CH_GGVC_P_98:A7","CH_GGVC_P_98:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] The distinction between original and ministerial causality orders the whole system: created communication is real, but it neither creates the mind nor possesses the will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_98:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Illumination proceeds from the first truth through the higher to the lower and accommodates more universally known truth to the recipient's capacity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A5","CH_GGVC_P_99:A6","CH_GGVC_P_99:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Created will is neither the rule of truth nor the sufficient mover of another's will, but governs its own attention, objective persuasion, and the direction of the concept.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A10","CH_GGVC_P_99:A2","CH_GGVC_P_99:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Reception of perfection follows the measure of the recipient; communication therefore increases communion without identity of degrees or modes of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A12","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Causal order is not one simple series: the order of nature, the hierarchy of illumination, and the circulation of speech constitute distinct channels with their own directions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A12","CH_GGVC_P_98:A11","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Intellectual communication can be non-local without being public, because extension of operation and determination of the recipient are governed by different causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:A9","CH_GGVC_P_99:A10","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Freedom of act and secrecy of concept arise from the governance of the intrinsic principle, not from causal independence or material opacity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A7","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Miracle indicates the limit of the natural order, but does not become a universal model for any spiritual mediation or any exception.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_98:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-01","text":"Immediacy passes from intimate divine causality to angelic manifestation without a bodily sign; the name persists, but the rationale of causality changes from the origin of the act to the absence of sensible mediation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ME6","CH_GGVC_P_98:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_99:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-02","text":"Motion first comprehends matter, intellect, and will under the first cause, then is restricted according to the capacity of the spiritual creature, and finally becomes the voluntary turning of the intellect toward a concept and a hearer.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_98:AS4","CH_GGVC_P_99:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-03","text":"Will changes from the object of divine motion into a principle of limited persuasion, its own attention, and selective closure of the concept.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A6","CH_GGVC_P_98:A10","CH_GGVC_P_99:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-04","text":"The order of included causes returns as a unidirectional hierarchy of illumination, but is later separated from the circulation of voluntary speech and does not absorb all communication.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A12","CH_GGVC_P_98:D6","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-05","text":"Universality migrates from determinable divine power to higher truth to be accommodated, but in non-local communication it no longer signifies universality of reception.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A3","CH_GGVC_P_98:A5","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-06","text":"Manifestation first designates the extraordinary effect of a hidden cause, then the transmission of truth, and finally the voluntary ordering of a concept; identity of the term does not equate three distinct functions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A14","CH_GGVC_P_98:A2","CH_GGVC_P_99:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-07","text":"Miracle, after establishing the limit of natural causality, recedes once its structural role has been fulfilled: its bypassing is not transferred into the ordinary law of spiritual substances.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:FGS5","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RMD-08","text":"Body and place change from objects of motion into negative conditions of communication: contact, sensible sign, and distance are successively removed, while will assumes the role of discrimination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:NX4","CH_GGVC_P_99:A5","CH_GGVC_P_99:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:CGD-01","text":"The gradation of miracles according to substance, subject, and mode of local effect remains; it rises to the structure of the whole only in the principle that the exceeding of a lower order does not destroy a higher order.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A16","CH_GGVC_P_97:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:CGD-02","text":"New revelation until judgment is a local property of angelic history; upward, it is amplified as an example of material novelty within a stable hierarchical form.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A13","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:CGD-03","text":"Speech to oneself and intermittent divine consultation locally explain angelic acts; at this level they effect the distinction between the temporality of attention and the stability of intellectual power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:D2","CH_GGVC_P_99:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:CGD-04","text":"The distinction between causes of the first and second order is extended from the general field of causality to the ministry of cognition and sustains angelic operation without usurpation of interior origin.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:CGD-05","text":"The measure of the recipient joins illumination and the direction of speech: the former determines how truth is received, the latter who receives at all.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A5","CH_GGVC_P_99:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:CGD-06","text":"The major transition proceeds from God's operation in every agent to the creature's operation in another creature, then to a structure of communication whose access is voluntarily governed.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_98:D1","CH_GGVC_P_99:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:CGD-07","text":"The model according to which every spiritual relation simply descends becomes obsolete: illumination descends, but voluntary speech can also ascend or be directed laterally.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_99:D3","CH_GGVC_P_99:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes divine and created causality as univocal powers of the same order competing for the same space of efficacy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GNX-02","text":"The disjunction by which either God truly operates or a creature truly perfects another intellect is excluded; nevertheless, original dominion over interior power is denied to the creature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SNX2","CH_GGVC_P_98:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GNX-03","text":"To manifest truth is not to create light, form the mind, or effectively move the will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GNX-04","text":"The unidirectionality of illumination is not permitted to define all spiritual communication, because voluntary speech admits ascending and selective relations.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_99:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GNX-05","text":"Absence of place and body produces neither transparency of minds nor the necessary publicity of every concept.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GNX-06","text":"Both causally closed nature and exception without order are excluded; bypassing a lower order remains within the order of the first cause and is not equally admitted in all fields.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_98:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:GNX-07","text":"Freedom and secrecy are not founded on the absence of a higher cause or on bodily enclosure, but on the integrity of the intrinsic principle and on voluntary non-direction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:DNX4","CH_GGVC_P_99:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that every efficacy belongs to one order of causality; otherwise divine concurrence and angelic ministry would produce either duplication or the emptying of the created agent.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that common intellectual nature makes immediate access to all concepts, because then the will could establish neither secrecy nor a recipient.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_99:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that illumination and speech are univocal transmissions; the former expresses the norm of truth and hierarchical direction, the latter dominion over destination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that one model of order governs bodily and spiritual things in the same way, because the dispensability of natural order and the stability of spiritual hierarchy are posited differently.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD3","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that communication of perfection requires equality of producer and recipient; degree remains because reception contracts content according to capacity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:AS6","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that created will can become the norm of truth, although it truly establishes channels of attention and manifestation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:A8","CH_GGVC_P_99:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSA-01","text":"The possibility of a self-sufficient creature, which does not receive interior power from the first cause or originally constitutes another's perfection, is ontologically inadmissible.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SNX1","CH_GGVC_P_98:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSA-02","text":"Impossible is the reciprocity by which God receives cognition through the speech of a creature; every relation to him leaves consultation, reception, or admiration to the creature.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_99:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSA-03","text":"The system admits neither an absolutely public interior concept nor an absolutely closed mind: concealment and communication are ordered around the act of voluntary destination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:A4","CH_GGVC_P_99:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSA-04","text":"The possibility of order without a first cause or of a wholly formless exception is excluded; even the non-hierarchical circulation of speech operates within divinely given capacities and limits.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SNX4","CH_GGVC_P_98:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-01","text":"The ontological dependence of the spiritual agent on God constitutes the condition, not the impediment, of its ministerial causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02","CL_GGVC_36:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_97:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-02","text":"The concurrence of causes of different orders explains how illumination is a true operation of the angel, although light, power, and the norm of truth proceed from God.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-02","CL_GGVC_36:GA-04","CL_GGVC_36:GA-06","CH_GGVC_P_97:A9","CH_GGVC_P_98:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-03","text":"Action concerning the will is distinguished into three degrees: God effectively moves intrinsic inclination, the angel persuasively proposes the object, while the speaker directs his own concept.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-04","CL_GGVC_36:GA-07","CH_GGVC_P_97:A6","CH_GGVC_P_98:A10","CH_GGVC_P_99:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-04","text":"Spiritual hierarchy has a double topology: illuminative truth descends according to degree, while voluntary speech opens ascending, descending, and selective directions.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-06","CL_GGVC_36:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_97:A12","CH_GGVC_P_98:D6","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-05","text":"The distinction of the first truth from created will divides communication into regulated common perfection and directed private information.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-06","CL_GGVC_36:GA-07","CL_GGVC_36:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_98:A7","CH_GGVC_P_99:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-06","text":"The non-locality of operation and the measure of the recipient together show that possible extension, aptitude for reception, and actual destination are irreducible conditions.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-08","CL_GGVC_36:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_99:A9","CH_GGVC_P_99:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-07","text":"The comparison of miracle with angelic hierarchy demonstrates that bypassing order is a domain-specific property, not a universal consequence of divine omnipotence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-09","CL_GGVC_36:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_97:A13","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:XD-08","text":"The same grammar of the intrinsic principle sustains freedom of act and secrecy of concept: the higher cause constitutes the capacity, but the proper act remains according to the agent's inclination or destination.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-07","CL_GGVC_36:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_97:A7","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSD-01","text":"Ontology, epistemology, and communication produce one stratified structure: being and power are distributed by God, cognitive perfection through ministers, and access to concept through voluntary destination.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-05","CL_GGVC_36:GA-10","CH_GGVC_P_97:A11","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSD-02","text":"The spiritual subject is neither a substance in its own right nor a mere channel, but a dependent operator that receives perfection, acts according to its own mode, and regulates access to its own concepts.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-02","CL_GGVC_36:GA-08","CL_GGVC_36:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_97:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_98:A12","CH_GGVC_P_99:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSD-03","text":"The time of the system is twofold: continuous divine conservation and stable hierarchical form successively receive novelties of revelation, consultation, and action.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-06","CL_GGVC_36:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_97:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_99:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSD-04","text":"The more deeply the creature's dependence is demonstrated, the more subtly its proper operation is distinguished; intensification of primary causality produces not emptying, but articulation of mediations.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02","CL_GGVC_36:GA-05","CH_GGVC_P_97:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSD-05","text":"Spiritual authority becomes distinct by function of channels: superiority of cognition governs illumination, but does not grant total dominion over the will, secrecy, or every speech of the inferior.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-06","CL_GGVC_36:GA-07","CL_GGVC_36:GA-09","CH_GGVC_P_98:D6","CH_GGVC_P_99:D3","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSD-06","text":"Miracle and secrecy are opposite figures of limit: the former shows that natural order does not exhaust causality, the latter that intellectual community does not exhaust access.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-10","CL_GGVC_36:GA-12","CH_GGVC_P_97:DNX3","CH_GGVC_P_99:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:BSD-07","text":"Communicated cognition requires separation of content, mode of reception, and right of access: the same truth is unequally received, while a voluntary concept can also be wholly retained.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-08","CL_GGVC_36:GA-10","CL_GGVC_36:GA-11","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_99:A4","CH_GGVC_P_99:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RLE-01","text":"The first cause produces and conserves created power → the creature properly operates → the operation communicates participated good → the effect manifests the order of the first cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A11","CH_GGVC_P_97:A8","CH_GGVC_P_98:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RLE-02","text":"Divine revelation → more universal cognition of the higher → distinction according to the capacity of the lower → strengthening of intellect → new matter of hierarchical communication.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A5","CH_GGVC_P_98:D1","CH_GGVC_P_98:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RLE-03","text":"Habitual concept → attention moved by the will → actual consideration → direction toward another → manifestation → new consultation or response.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:A1","CH_GGVC_P_99:A3","CH_GGVC_P_99:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RLE-04","text":"The path of amplification proceeds from the motion of matter and interior powers to causality among spirits, thence to the distinction of channels, recipients, and degrees of publicity.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:AS2","CH_GGVC_P_98:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_99:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RLE-05","text":"The principal attractor is ordered immediacy: the higher cause remains intimately present, while created media operate all the more truly the more accurately they are distinguished from original causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ME2","CH_GGVC_P_98:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:RLE-06","text":"Retention of the concept and its direction open a circuit in which interiority is not opposed to communication, but constitutes its selective matter.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:A4","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FGS-B01","text":"Production and conservation of power → ontological dependence → application to act → proper operation of the secondary cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:FGS1","CH_GGVC_P_97:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FGS-B02","text":"Distinction of causal orders → simultaneity of primary and secondary action → exclusion of competition → constitution of created ministry.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A9","CH_GGVC_P_97:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FGS-B03","text":"First truth → more universal higher cognition → accommodation to lower capacity → descending illumination → perfection without equation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A5","CH_GGVC_P_98:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FGS-B04","text":"Created will → actual consideration of the concept → determination of the recipient → manifestation without a bodily sign → speech with free direction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:A1","CH_GGVC_P_99:A10","CH_GGVC_P_99:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FGS-B05","text":"Mode of recipient + principle of content + direction of speaker → determination of the quality, direction, and publicity of communication.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_99:A6","CH_GGVC_P_99:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FGS-B06","text":"Exceeding of a lower order → manifestation of its contingency → conservation of the first order; secrecy of concept → manifestation of the limit of community → conservation of voluntary dominion.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD4","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:AFM-B01","text":"Ontological dependence → non-competitive concurrence → distinction of interior operations → angelic ministry → hierarchical illumination → voluntary speech → selection of recipient → ordered but not totally public intellectual community.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD2","CH_GGVC_P_98:D1","CH_GGVC_P_99:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:AFM-B02","text":"Order of causes → possibility of natural excess → stability of spiritual mediation → bifurcation of truth and will → closure in a plurality of channels under one first cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A13","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX7","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MME-01","text":"Causality is reordered from a competitive field into the vertical simultaneity of orders, where dependence and proper action mutually constitute one another.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ME1","CH_GGVC_P_97:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MME-02","text":"Immediacy is resolved into three species: presence of the first cause in the act, manifestation without a bodily sign, and access unimpeded by distance.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_99:D1","CH_GGVC_P_99:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MME-03","text":"Hierarchy changes from total prelation into a distribution of functions: degree of cognition governs illumination, not every speech or will.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MME-04","text":"Communication is no longer described through place or community of nature, but through the principle of content, the mode of the recipient, and voluntary destination.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:DNX1","CH_GGVC_P_99:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MME-05","text":"Freedom and secrecy are referred to the structure of the intrinsic principle, from which higher causality and proper selection are composed without contradiction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ME4","CH_GGVC_P_99:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MME-06","text":"The ordinary and the extraordinary are not absolute opposites: each is defined through the relation of lower order to higher cause, but dispensability varies according to field.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD5","CH_GGVC_P_98:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of the surface are examined the motions of creatures, angelic illumination, inclination of will, speech, distance, and determination of the hearer.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ALT1","CH_GGVC_P_98:AS1","CH_GGVC_P_99:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, operations are distributed among original cause, secondary causes, hierarchy of truth, and channels of voluntary manifestation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_98:ALT2","CH_GGVC_P_99:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, oppositions between immediacy and mediation, freedom and motion, hierarchy and reciprocity are converted into distinctions of orders and functions.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_98:ALT3","CH_GGVC_P_99:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is continuously conserved participation, within which stable forms of order receive novelties of revelation, attention, and action.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_98:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_99:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological level, the form of the system effects its own thesis: individual attributions of causality are limited through negations, then rejoined into non-competitive channels.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:NX8","CH_GGVC_P_98:DNX2","CH_GGVC_P_99:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FL-01","text":"The boundary between divine motion of the intellect and angelic strengthening of its exercise remains subtle, because both touch the act of cognition but only the former causally founds power and likeness.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A5","CH_GGVC_P_98:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FL-02","text":"Persuasion is said to incline the will, but motion of a creature's will is denied; coherence depends on the distinction between the proposed object and the intrinsic causality of the power.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A10","CH_GGVC_P_98:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FL-03","text":"The invariability of the descent of illumination is reconciled with speech from the lower to the higher only if hierarchy is restricted to perfective truth, not to every manifestation.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:NX6","CH_GGVC_P_99:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FL-04","text":"The community of illuminations and graduated reception retain a tension: truth can be called common, although mode, fullness, and perhaps actual comprehension are not common.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:D8","CH_GGVC_P_98:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FL-05","text":"The mutual vision of angels in the Word is not fully formalized with the secrecy of voluntary concept; the system presupposes a distinction between the seen person and the directed concept.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_99:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:FL-06","text":"The natural order can be bypassed by God, whereas the spiritual order does not receive an analogous dispensation; the reason for this asymmetry is affirmed more firmly than it is deduced from common principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:NX9","CH_GGVC_P_98:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MP-01","text":"Immediacy changes meaning when it passes from the ontological action of the first cause to manifestation of the concept without a bodily instrument.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:SD1","CH_GGVC_P_99:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MP-02","text":"Illumination changes from the image of infusion of light into an epistemic operation that strengthens a given power and distinguishes truth.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:NX2","CH_GGVC_P_98:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MP-03","text":"Will passes from that which is sufficiently moved by God alone to a created principle that orders attention, persuasion, and communicative access.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A9","CH_GGVC_P_99:A1","CH_GGVC_P_99:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MP-04","text":"Scalar hierarchy changes into a multiple network when the voluntary circulation of speech is superimposed upon the descending flow of truth.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:D6","CH_GGVC_P_99:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a machine of included orders, in which the first source constitutes powers, created media exercise their proper operations, and diverse channels distribute effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:A12","CH_GGVC_P_97:FGS4","CH_GGVC_P_99:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:HSI-02","text":"The spiritual subject is at once related and a gate: it receives perfection from another, but retains or directs its own concept and is not dissolved into the public field.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:A12","CH_GGVC_P_99:A4","CH_GGVC_P_99:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:HSI-03","text":"Time is a flow of novelty through a stable structure: conservation continues, revelations are added, consultation is intermittent, yet hierarchy retains its direction.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:ALT4","CH_GGVC_P_98:A13","CH_GGVC_P_99:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is not flight from order, but selection of the right channel: to cause, illuminate, persuade, speak, and conceal are distinguished according to unequal powers.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:NX5","CH_GGVC_P_99:NX3","CH_GGVC_P_99:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms suffice to reconstruct the whole, if the dependence of created causality, the divine monopoly concerning interior principles, graduated reception, and voluntary destination are preserved together.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_98:MRT1","CH_GGVC_P_99:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the distinction of origin from ministry, because from it are derived causal concurrence, non-creative illumination, and selective speech without mutual emptying.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_98:MRT2","CH_GGVC_P_99:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remain the limit of persuasion and motion of the will, the rationale of the invariability of spiritual hierarchy, and the compatibility of mutual vision with particular secrecy.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_98:MRT4","CH_GGVC_P_98:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_99:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_36:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement, because it proceeds from universal causality to graduated mediation and thence to selective destination; it nevertheless resists reduction through the imperfect analogy between speech to a creature and receptive speech to God.","refs":["CH_GGVC_P_97:MRT7","CH_GGVC_P_99:MRT5","CH_GGVC_P_99:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_36@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_36:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 106–108","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 105 · Prima pars · Quaestio 106 · Prima pars · Quaestio 107"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 109–111","order":37,"parents":["CH_GGVC__100","CH_GGVC__101","CH_GGVC__102"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_37@R001","sha256":"a6a5cb0f62de3dcd3c081d3018e4e0fbdd59162a7fae4ad9661bce2ed5bc5534","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo principatus divinus unicus multitudinem angelicam, potestates lapsas et creaturas corporales ordinet sine confusionibus naturae, dignitatis et causalitatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AS1","CH_GGVC__102:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis affirmat omnem virtutem creatam intra unum ordinem divinum operari, quamvis receptiones, fines et modi efficiendi realiter differant.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A2","CH_GGVC__102:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit per distinctiones reconstructivas quae unitatem principii a pluralitate modorum, naturam a gratia, dignitatem ab efficacia et gubernationem ab informatione separant.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AS3","CH_GGVC__101:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-04","text":"Motus progreditur ab ordine rationali constituto ad probationem eius per casum voluntatis, deinde ad extensionem eius in administrationem corporalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AS4","CH_GGVC__101:AS1","CH_GGVC__102:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-05","text":"Progressus est recursivus et cumulativus: quod primum ut distinctio graduum statuitur, per malitiam et causalitatem corporalem iterum probatur atque limitatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A1","CH_GGVC__102:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-06","text":"Eadem machina discriminatoriam vim auget: gradus ab executione, manifestatio ab illuminatione et praesidentia ab productione formae distinguuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AS6","CH_GGVC__101:A9","CH_GGVC__102:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-07","text":"Ordo superior non delet inferiora neque malum simpliciter prohibet, sed operationes creatas distribuit, subordinat et intra fines universales continet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SD4","CH_GGVC__102:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-08","text":"Clausura est duplex et asymmetrica: creaturae rationales ad communionem sine confusione ordinantur, sed transgressio ordinis totius creaturae soli Deo reservatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:SNX5","CH_GGVC__102:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Unitas ordinis creati fundatur in uno principio divino, intra cuius causalitatem omnis virtus creata manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A2","CH_GGVC__102:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Differentiae hierarchicae oriuntur ex diversis modis recipiendi et exercendi causalitatem, dum virtus particularis universaliori gubernationi subicitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A3","CH_GGVC__102:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Perfectio superior non sola possessione boni, sed eminentia communicativa qua inferiores ordinantur mensuratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A8","CH_GGVC__100:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Distinctio naturae, gratiae et gloriae determinat quid per elevationem, lapsum aut consummationem mutetur et quid permaneat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A1","CH_GGVC__100:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Actio sequitur naturam, sed eius ordinatio secundum finem, dispositionem et applicationem gradatim distribuitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A4","CH_GGVC__100:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Species formalis operationis ex relatione ad finem et ad amplitudinem ordinis sumitur; ideo similis effectus materialis diversas rationes spirituales accipere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A8","CH_GGVC__102:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Superior gubernatio inferiorem causalitatem ordinat, sed non propterea eodem modo efficit quidquid inferior causa efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:NX3","CH_GGVC__102:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Malum ordinem autonomum non constituit, quia natura bona remanet et actio perversa ab externa iustitia continetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A2","CH_GGVC__101:A6","CH_GGVC__101:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Elevatio relationis vel gloriae distinctionem naturarum non delet; communio et praelatio sine transmutatione substantiae efficiuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A18","CH_GGVC__100:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Cognitio creata taxonomias communes et apparentias effectuum attingit, non autem necessario distinctiones individuales aut totum ordinem causarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A9","CH_GGVC__102:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Causalitas spiritualis creata immediate ad motum localem pertingit, non ad informationem materiae neque ad exceptionem ab ordine totius creaturae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:A11","CH_GGVC__102:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Miraculum proprie dictum soli Deo competit, quia Deus solus materiam, formam et ordinem creatum universaliter comprehendit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:A8","CH_GGVC__102:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Conversio et fruitio Dei sunt mensura ultima dignitatis, illuminationis et praelationis, etiam ubi excellentia naturalis aliter distribuitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A15","CH_GGVC__101:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-01","text":"Ordo identitatem functionalem servat dum a principatu sacro ad containmentum daemonum et ad legem universalem causalitatis transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A1","CH_GGVC__101:A6","CH_GGVC__102:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-02","text":"Hierarchia ex divisione cognitionis et officiorum migrat in distinctionem naturae, gratiae et gloriae, ac tandem in distributionem causarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:D2","CH_GGVC__101:ME1","CH_GGVC__102:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-03","text":"Superioritas mutatur a gradu communicativae perfectionis in propinquitatem ad Deum et postea in universalitatem gubernantem, sine reductione harum mensurarum ad unam potentiam.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:SD4","CH_GGVC__101:SNX3","CH_GGVC__102:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-04","text":"Illuminatio, primo modus receptionis hierarchicae, stricte fit communicatio veri ad Deum ordinata; transmissio cognitionis moraliter aversa eius nomen amittit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A3","CH_GGVC__101:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-05","text":"Distinctio gradus ab executione officii redit ut distinctio naturae ab actione et gubernationis ab informatione substantiali.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AS6","CH_GGVC__101:A4","CH_GGVC__102:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-06","text":"Natura et gratia non manent termini paralleli: gratia naturam angelicam proportionate perficit, hominem ultra proportionem elevat et a daemonibus amitti potest sine naturae abolitione.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:SD6","CH_GGVC__101:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-07","text":"Deviatio voluntatis et prodigium sensibile invertuntur: quae extra ordinem apparere videntur fiunt probationes inclusionis in ordine naturali et providentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SD1","CH_GGVC__102:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-08","text":"Nomina novem ordinum et distributio rectorum particularium, opere classificatorio peracto, absorbentur in structuram abstractiorem receptionis, mediationis et officiorum generalium.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:SD3","CH_GGVC__102:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:CGD-01","text":"Collocationes Dionysiana et Gregoriana ordinum manent quaestio taxonomica inferioris gradus; ad praesentem structuram ascendit sola convertibilitas nominum per functiones.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:D7","CH_GGVC__100:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:CGD-02","text":"Assumptio hominum ad ordines angelicos est conclusio localis, sed sursum amplificatur in regulam communionis sine identitate naturae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:D10","CH_GGVC__100:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:CGD-03","text":"Communis nequitia et maior virtus explicant localiter obedientiam daemonum; hoc gradu manet principium latius ordinis operationalis sine caritate.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A7","CH_GGVC__101:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:CGD-04","text":"Doctrina localis de forma et composito praebet commissuram causalitatis, ex qua distinctio globalis inter administrare et substantialiter efficere emergit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:A5","CH_GGVC__102:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:CGD-05","text":"Distinctiones naturae et gratiae, gradus et officii, gubernationis et informationis singulae localiter oriuntur, sed coniunctae principium non-aequivalentiae modorum constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:NX7","CH_GGVC__101:A1","CH_GGVC__102:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:CGD-06","text":"Negatio quod daemones sint animae defunctorum et recusatio tot ordinum quot rerum corporalium species sunt munere locali perfunctae ulterius non gubernant synthesim.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:NX13","CH_GGVC__102:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GNX-01","text":"Systema excludit multitudinem rationalem omnino indifferentem, quia unitas sine gradu receptionis et communicationis confusionem produceret.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GNX-02","text":"Excluditur scala causalis univoca in qua virtus superior omnia opera inferioris eodem modo exerceret.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GNX-03","text":"Malitia non potest naturam bonam abolere neque potestatem creatam in principium inordinatum et sui iuris convertere.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GNX-04","text":"Non omnis manifestatio veri est illuminatio, quia ordinatio intellectus ad Deum pertinet ad rationem formalem actus.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GNX-05","text":"Praesidentia spiritualis non est immediata productio formarum substantialium neque indifferentia inter gubernare et creare.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GNX-06","text":"Effectus insolitus aut praeter causalitatem particularem notam non sufficit ad miraculum proprie constituendum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GNX-07","text":"Consummatio non annihilat gradus, sicut communio beatifica naturas non confundit; mutatur operatio, non fundamentum distinctionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:NX11","CH_GGVC__100:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:DBN-01","text":"Ut systema constet, falsum esse oportet aliquam potestatem creatam coordinate et independenter contra ordinem Dei consistere posse.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SNX2","CH_GGVC__102:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet ordinem realem semper amicitiam, iustitiam intrinsecam aut communionem finis boni requirere.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet excellentiam naturalem ultimam mensuram auctoritatis esse, alioquin praelatio bonorum Angelorum super daemones explicari non posset.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet apparentiam sensibilem vel taxonomiam communem causam totalem et differentias reales exhaustive manifestare.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:DNX4","CH_GGVC__100:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet naturam corporalem sibi sufficientem esse aut universum creatum mediatione graduum carere posse.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:SNX1","CH_GGVC__102:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet adeptionem finis omnem dependentiam illuminationis et omnem relationem hierarchicam abolere.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:DNX6","CH_GGVC__100:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas ontologice inadmissibilis est creatura voluntate vel potestate extra ordinem originarium Dei constituta.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SNX2","CH_GGVC__102:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSA-02","text":"Systema circumagit absentiam aequalitatis absolutae et receptionis immediatae, quia differentia communicativa condicionem ipsius universi ordinati praebet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:DNX2","CH_GGVC__100:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSA-03","text":"Inadmissibile est causalitatem divinam tamquam unam causam creatam inter alias tractare, cum sola ordinem totius creaturae excedere possit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:SNX1","CH_GGVC__102:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSA-04","text":"Totalis comprehensio ordinis absens manet cognitioni creatae et causalitati creatae; haec absentia simul contractionem taxonomiae et clausuram miraculi ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:SNX4","CH_GGVC__102:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-01","text":"Pluralitas hierarchiarum et causarum est distributio interna unius gubernationis divinae, non compositio plurium principatuum independentium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-01","CL_GGVC_37:GA-02","CH_GGVC__100:A2","CH_GGVC__102:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-02","text":"Communicatio perfectionis dignitatem sacram tantum accipit cum ad finem divinum ordinatur; copia scientiae sola gradum illuminativum non constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-03","CL_GGVC_37:GA-06","CL_GGVC_37:GA-13","CH_GGVC__100:A8","CH_GGVC__101:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-03","text":"Lapsus aufert participationem gloriae, sed non naturam neque omnem coordinationem actionum; ordo residuus tamen ab iustitia externa, non a voluntate lapsa, sacram directionem recipit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-04","CL_GGVC_37:GA-08","CH_GGVC__100:A16","CH_GGVC__101:A2","CH_GGVC__101:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-04","text":"Auctoritas spiritualis et amplitudo potentiae naturalis sunt axes distincti: inferior natura potest superiorem naturam regere per maiorem participationem iustitiae divinae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-07","CL_GGVC_37:GA-13","CH_GGVC__101:SNX3","CH_GGVC__102:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-05","text":"Administratio est executio ordinis, non essentia gradus neque productio substantialis; ideo officium mutari potest salva dispositione fundamentali.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-04","CL_GGVC_37:GA-07","CH_GGVC__100:AS6","CH_GGVC__102:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-06","text":"Prodigium daemonicum est causalitas creata nociva sed providentialiter contenta; apparentia miraculi indicat ignorantiam causae, non autonomiam mali.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-08","CL_GGVC_37:GA-10","CL_GGVC_37:GA-12","CH_GGVC__101:A14","CH_GGVC__102:D5","CH_GGVC__102:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-07","text":"Gratia et iustitia relationes dignitatis ultra proportionem naturalem reordinant sine mutatione speciei aut destructione naturae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-04","CL_GGVC_37:GA-09","CL_GGVC_37:GA-13","CH_GGVC__100:A11","CH_GGVC__101:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-08","text":"Administratio corporalis est extensio terminalis processus hierarchici: dispositio universalis descendit ad applicationem localem agentium, non ad creationem novarum naturarum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-05","CL_GGVC_37:GA-07","CL_GGVC_37:GA-11","CH_GGVC__100:A14","CH_GGVC__102:AS4","CH_GGVC__102:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSD-01","text":"Ontologia systematis primatum relationis ordinatae tribuit: eadem natura diversos status hierarchicos recipit secundum principium, finem et modum participationis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-02","CL_GGVC_37:GA-04","CL_GGVC_37:GA-09","CH_GGVC__100:SD1","CH_GGVC__101:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas fit distributa sed non fragmentata: Deus universaliter comprehendit, Angeli disponunt et movent, corpora secundum virtutes proprias effectus producunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-01","CL_GGVC_37:GA-07","CL_GGVC_37:GA-11","CH_GGVC__102:SD1","CH_GGVC__102:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSD-03","text":"Tempus systematis est mutatio status super structuram persistentem: lapsus, via et consummatio operationes alterant sine simplici abolitione naturae vel gradus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-04","CL_GGVC_37:GA-08","CH_GGVC__100:SD5","CH_GGVC__101:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSD-04","text":"Epistemologia requirit transitum ab admiratione et nominibus communibus ad reconstructionem ordinis totalis, quia effectus visibilis suam causam formalem non indicat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-06","CL_GGVC_37:GA-10","CL_GGVC_37:GA-12","CH_GGVC__100:SD3","CH_GGVC__102:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSD-05","text":"Politica implicita distinguit auctoritatem legitimam a sola efficacia: coordinatio coacta potest operari, sed dignitas praelationis ex participatione principii divini derivatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-08","CL_GGVC_37:GA-13","CH_GGVC__101:A6","CH_GGVC__101:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSD-06","text":"Malum est parasiticum et recaptabile: bono naturae utitur, effectus nocivos producit, sed neque ordinem proprium fundat neque fines providentiae transcendit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-01","CL_GGVC_37:GA-08","CL_GGVC_37:GA-12","CH_GGVC__101:SD1","CH_GGVC__101:SD4","CH_GGVC__102:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RLE-01","text":"Perfectio recepta → capacitas communicandi → ordinatio inferiorum → maior manifestatio perfectionis: gradus se per communicationem, non per clausam possessionem, confirmat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A8","CH_GGVC__100:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RLE-02","text":"Finis universalis → dispositio agendorum → applicatio localis → effectus particularis: causalitas descendit spiraliter per modos distinctos.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A14","CH_GGVC__102:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RLE-03","text":"Natura bona retenta → voluntas perversa → actio nociva → permissio et subordinatio → bonum superius elicitum: fuga a fine in circuitum providentiae recaptatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A2","CH_GGVC__101:A14","CH_GGVC__101:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RLE-04","text":"Effectus insolitus → ignorantia causae creatae → attributio miraculi → reconstructio ordinis universalis → reclassificatio prodigii: error epistemicus machinam causalem denuo aperit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:D5","CH_GGVC__102:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RLE-05","text":"Consecutione finis officia conductiva cessant, sed illuminatio conservativa permanet; clausura operationis in novum circuitum conservationis transit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:D9","CH_GGVC__100:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RLE-06","text":"Deus est attractor qui conversionem, dignitatem et limites causalitatis reclassificat: propinquitas ad eum gradum ordinat, aversio nomen illuminationis tollit, transcendere ordinem eius miraculum constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A15","CH_GGVC__101:A12","CH_GGVC__102:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FGS-B01","text":"Unum principium divinum + omnis virtus creata intra ordinem eius → unitas gubernationis sine aequalitate participantium.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A2","CH_GGVC__102:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FGS-B02","text":"Diversi modi receptionis + diversa officia → hierarchiae et gradus tamquam articulationes internas multitudinis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A3","CH_GGVC__100:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FGS-B03","text":"Perfectio participata + excessus communicativus → praelatio → dispositio universalis → applicatio particularis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A8","CH_GGVC__100:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FGS-B04","text":"Natura + gratia + gloria → discrimen inter structuram persistentem, elevationem participativam et operationem mutabilem.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A1","CH_GGVC__100:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FGS-B05","text":"Perversio voluntatis + permanentia naturae → actio nociva ordinabilis → containmentum providentiae sine consecratione mali.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SD1","CH_GGVC__101:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FGS-B06","text":"Universalitas spiritualis + exterioritas motus localis → dispositio agentium corporalium → effectus insolitus intra ordinem creatum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:A11","CH_GGVC__102:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FGS-B07","text":"Inclusio omnis virtutis creatae in ordine universali + comprehensio divina totius compositi → communio creaturarum infra ordinem et miraculum soli Deo proprium.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:A12","CH_GGVC__102:A13","CH_GGVC__100:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:AFM-B01","text":"Unitas principii → diversitas receptionis → gradatio officiorum → distinctio naturae, gratiae et gloriae → probatio ordinis per malum → extensio ad causalitatem corporalem → clausura totius ordinis creati sub Deo.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AS4","CH_GGVC__101:AS2","CH_GGVC__102:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:AFM-B02","text":"Apparens contradictio → distinctio respectuum → anomalum intra ordinem repositum → terminus causalitatis creatae determinatus → unitas superior restituta.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AF2","CH_GGVC__101:SD1","CH_GGVC__102:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MME-01","text":"Hierarchia ex scala nominum in architecturam modorum receptionis, statuum participationis et causarum distributarum reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ME2","CH_GGVC__101:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MME-02","text":"Superioritas scinditur in excellentiam naturalem, dignitatem ex propinquitate divina et universalitatem causaliter gubernantem.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SNX3","CH_GGVC__102:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MME-03","text":"Malum desinit apparere ut exterior ordinis rivalis et fit actio perversa intra naturam bonam, subiectionem externam et permissionem providentialem.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MME-04","text":"Ager causalis denuo describitur per distinctionem comprehensionis divinae, dispositionis angelicae et efficientiae corporalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MME-05","text":"Miraculum ex insolentia phaenomeni transfertur ad relationem effectus cum totalitate ordinis naturae creatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MME-06","text":"Lectio necessaria fit reconstructiva: taxonomiam communem et apparentiam sensibilem ad ordines causalitatis et limites cognitionis referre debet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ME9","CH_GGVC__102:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei tractantur hierarchiae et ordines, status daemonum atque potestas Angelorum circa corpora et miracula.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ALT1","CH_GGVC__101:ALT1","CH_GGVC__102:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali agunt differentiae receptionis, subordinatio actionum et distributio causalitatis inter Deum, spiritus creatos et agentes corporales.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ALT2","CH_GGVC__101:ALT2","CH_GGVC__102:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico apparentia contradictionis solvitur per mutationem quaestionis ab existentia potestatis ad modum, finem et amplitudinem eius.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ALT3","CH_GGVC__101:ALT3","CH_GGVC__102:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali naturae persistunt, relationes participationis mutantur, operationes inter lapsum, viam et consummationem variantur, et compositum manet subiectum generationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ALT4","CH_GGVC__101:ALT4","CH_GGVC__102:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo forma distinctionum ipsam thesim efficit: singula contraexempla intra rationem superiorem reponuntur sine eliminatione differentiae localis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AS3","CH_GGVC__102:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FL-01","text":"Transitus ab universalitate substantiae spiritualis ad necessitatem praesidentiae angelicae super corpora ordinem metaphysicum firmat, sed mechanismum immediatae gubernationis incomplete determinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FL-02","text":"Mensura concreta inter gradum naturae et auctoritatem ex propinquitate divina indeterminata manet, quamvis utriusque ordinis distinctione contradictio temperetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FL-03","text":"Providentia malum ad bonum ordinare dicitur, sed ratio particularis cur haec actio permittatur ex principio generali non deducitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FL-04","text":"Relatio inter ordines communes, officium singulare cuiusque angeli, elevationem hominum et distributionem ministeriorum non completam tabulam correspondentiarum producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:MRT7","CH_GGVC__100:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MP-01","text":"Hierarchia mutatur ex taxonomia graduum angelicorum in formam generalem qua causalitates creatae sub uno ordine distribuuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ME2","CH_GGVC__102:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MP-02","text":"Illuminatio mutatur ex communicatione luminis in actum formaliter definitum per conversionem intellectus ad Deum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MP-03","text":"Praelatio mutatur ex effectu inaequalitatis naturalis in auctoritatem participatam secundum iustitiam et propinquitatem ad principium divinum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MP-04","text":"Miraculum mutatur ex effectu insolito in exceptionem praeter ordinem totius naturae creatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite figuratur ut cascata mediationum: rationes universales per dispositiones et applicationes descendunt, dum omnes gradus circa principium divinum continentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ME5","CH_GGVC__102:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum creatum est nodus receptivus cuius natura persistit, sed cuius status variat secundum illuminationem, aversionem, gratiam et gloriam.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A3","CH_GGVC__101:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est series mutationum operationis circa structuram persistentem: lapsus privat, via conducit, consummatio sistit ministerium conductivum sed conservationem retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:SD5","CH_GGVC__101:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:HSI-04","text":"Certamen possibile non est fuga extra ordinem, sed contentio circa directionem virium intra illum; actio perversa recaptatur, actio angelica disponit, solus Deus totum ordinem excedit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SD4","CH_GGVC__102:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata praesentia sufficiunt ad restituendam divisionem hierarchicam, permanentiam naturae sub lapsu et limites gubernationis angelicae corporalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:MRT1","CH_GGVC__102:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est in distinctionibus principii et receptionis, naturae et gratiae, gradus et operationis, gubernationis et informationis, ordinis particularis et ordinis totalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:MRT2","CH_GGVC__102:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent mechanismus transitus a universalitate spirituali ad gubernationem corporalem et mensura relationis inter excellentiam naturalem et auctoritatem spiritualem.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:MRT5","CH_GGVC__101:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B04","text":"Cohaerentia partim analogia tripartitionis et reconciliatione functionali nomenclaturarum nititur; hae operationes generativae sunt, sed demonstrationem exhaustivam non praebent.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:MRT4","CH_GGVC__100:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat ab unitate principii per probationem mali ad clausuram causalitatis creatae, dum officia individualia et mechanismi particulares reductioni axiomaticae resistunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:MRT9","CH_GGVC__101:MRT7","CH_GGVC__102:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T173150539712Z_e2503a59","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 109–111","source_sha256":"a6a5cb0f62de3dcd3c081d3018e4e0fbdd59162a7fae4ad9661bce2ed5bc5534","sha256":"89e6218a9895fa9cd0935b8500acaa242f72b397d59da9b2bf44403d46076092","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:31:50.541625+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how the one divine sovereignty orders the angelic multitude, fallen powers, and corporeal creatures without confusions of nature, dignity, and causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AS1","CH_GGVC__102:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis affirms that every created power operates within one divine order, although receptions, ends, and modes of effecting really differ.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A2","CH_GGVC__102:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-03","text":"The argument proceeds through reconstructive distinctions that separate the unity of principle from the plurality of modes, nature from grace, dignity from efficacy, and governance from informing.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AS3","CH_GGVC__101:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-04","text":"The movement advances from constituted rational order to its proof through the fall of the will, then to its extension into corporeal administration.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AS4","CH_GGVC__101:AS1","CH_GGVC__102:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-05","text":"The progress is recursive and cumulative: what is first established as a distinction of ranks is again proved and delimited through malice and corporeal causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A1","CH_GGVC__102:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-06","text":"The same mechanism heightens discriminative force: rank is distinguished from execution, manifestation from illumination, and presiding from the production of form.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AS6","CH_GGVC__101:A9","CH_GGVC__102:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-07","text":"The higher order neither destroys lower things nor simply prevents evil, but distributes, subordinates, and contains created operations within universal limits.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SD4","CH_GGVC__102:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BAS-08","text":"The closure is twofold and asymmetrical: rational creatures are ordered to communion without confusion, but transgression of the order of the whole of creation is reserved to God alone.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:SNX5","CH_GGVC__102:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] The unity of created order is founded in one divine principle, within whose causality every created power remains.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A2","CH_GGVC__102:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Hierarchical differences arise from diverse modes of receiving and exercising causality, while particular power is subjected to more universal governance.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A3","CH_GGVC__102:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Higher perfection is measured not by possession of good alone, but by the communicative eminence through which lower beings are ordered.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A8","CH_GGVC__100:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The distinction of nature, grace, and glory determines what is changed through elevation, fall, or consummation and what remains.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A1","CH_GGVC__100:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Action follows nature, but its ordering according to end, disposition, and application is distributed by degrees.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A4","CH_GGVC__100:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] The formal species of an operation is taken from its relation to the end and to the scope of order; therefore a similar material effect can receive diverse spiritual accounts.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A8","CH_GGVC__102:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Higher governance orders lower causality, but does not thereby effect in the same manner whatever the lower cause effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:NX3","CH_GGVC__102:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Evil does not constitute an autonomous order, because nature remains good and perverse action is contained by external justice.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A2","CH_GGVC__101:A6","CH_GGVC__101:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Elevation of relation or glory does not destroy the distinction of natures; communion and prelation are effected without transmutation of substance.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A18","CH_GGVC__100:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Created knowledge reaches common taxonomies and appearances of effects, but not necessarily individual distinctions or the whole order of causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A9","CH_GGVC__102:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Created spiritual causality extends immediately to local motion, not to the informing of matter nor to exception from the order of the whole of creation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:A11","CH_GGVC__102:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] A miracle properly so called belongs to God alone, because God alone universally comprehends matter, form, and created order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:A8","CH_GGVC__102:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Conversion to and enjoyment of God are the ultimate measure of dignity, illumination, and prelation, even where natural excellence is otherwise distributed.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A15","CH_GGVC__101:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-01","text":"Order preserves functional identity while being transferred from sacred sovereignty to the containment of demons and to the universal law of causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A1","CH_GGVC__101:A6","CH_GGVC__102:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-02","text":"Hierarchy migrates from the division of knowledge and offices into the distinction of nature, grace, and glory, and finally into the distribution of causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:D2","CH_GGVC__101:ME1","CH_GGVC__102:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-03","text":"Superiority changes from a degree of communicative perfection into proximity to God and later into governing universality, without reducing these measures to one power.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:SD4","CH_GGVC__101:SNX3","CH_GGVC__102:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-04","text":"Illumination, at first a mode of hierarchical reception, strictly becomes communication of truth ordered to God; morally averted transmission of knowledge loses its name.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A3","CH_GGVC__101:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-05","text":"The distinction of rank from execution of office returns as the distinction of nature from action and governance from substantial informing.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AS6","CH_GGVC__101:A4","CH_GGVC__102:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-06","text":"Nature and grace do not remain parallel terms: grace perfects angelic nature proportionately, elevates man beyond proportion, and can be lost by demons without abolition of nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:SD6","CH_GGVC__101:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-07","text":"Deviation of the will and sensible prodigy are inverted: what seems to appear outside order becomes proof of inclusion within natural order and providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SD1","CH_GGVC__102:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RMD-08","text":"The names of the nine orders and the distribution of particular rulers, once the classificatory work is complete, are absorbed into a more abstract structure of reception, mediation, and general offices.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:SD3","CH_GGVC__102:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:CGD-01","text":"The Dionysian and Gregorian placements of the orders remain a lower-level taxonomic question; only the convertibility of names through functions rises to the present structure.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:D7","CH_GGVC__100:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:CGD-02","text":"The assumption of humans into angelic orders is a local conclusion, but is amplified upward into a rule of communion without identity of nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:D10","CH_GGVC__100:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:CGD-03","text":"Common malice and greater power explain locally the obedience of demons; at this level there remains the broader principle of operational order without charity.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A7","CH_GGVC__101:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:CGD-04","text":"The local doctrine of form and composite provides the causal joint from which the global distinction between administering and substantially effecting emerges.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:A5","CH_GGVC__102:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:CGD-05","text":"The distinctions of nature and grace, rank and office, governance and informing each arise locally, but together constitute the principle of the non-equivalence of modes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:NX7","CH_GGVC__101:A1","CH_GGVC__102:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:CGD-06","text":"The denial that demons are souls of the dead and the rejection of there being as many orders as species of corporeal things, having fulfilled their local function, no longer govern the synthesis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:NX13","CH_GGVC__102:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes a rational multitude wholly indifferent, because unity without degrees of reception and communication would produce confusion.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GNX-02","text":"A univocal causal scale is excluded in which higher power would exercise all the works of the lower in the same manner.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GNX-03","text":"Malice cannot abolish good nature nor convert created power into an unordained and autonomous principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GNX-04","text":"Not every manifestation of truth is illumination, because the ordering of intellect to God belongs to the formal account of the act.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GNX-05","text":"Spiritual presiding is not the immediate production of substantial forms, nor is there indifference between governing and creating.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GNX-06","text":"An unusual effect or one beyond known particular causality does not suffice to constitute a miracle properly.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:GNX-07","text":"Consummation does not annihilate ranks, just as beatific communion does not confuse natures; operation changes, not the foundation of distinction.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:NX11","CH_GGVC__100:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:DBN-01","text":"For the system to hold, it must be false that any created power can stand coordinate and independently against the order of God.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SNX2","CH_GGVC__102:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that real order always requires friendship, intrinsic justice, or communion in a good end.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that natural excellence is the ultimate measure of authority, otherwise the prelation of good Angels over demons could not be explained.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that sensible appearance or common taxonomy exhaustively manifests the total cause and real differences.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:DNX4","CH_GGVC__100:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that corporeal nature is self-sufficient or that the created universe can lack the mediation of degrees.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:SNX1","CH_GGVC__102:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that attainment of the end abolishes every dependence on illumination and every hierarchical relation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:DNX6","CH_GGVC__100:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSA-01","text":"A creature constituted by will or power outside the originating order of God is an ontologically inadmissible possibility.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SNX2","CH_GGVC__102:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSA-02","text":"The system revolves around the absence of absolute equality and immediate reception, because communicative difference provides the condition of the ordered universe itself.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:DNX2","CH_GGVC__100:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSA-03","text":"It is inadmissible to treat divine causality as one created cause among others, since it alone can exceed the order of the whole of creation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:SNX1","CH_GGVC__102:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSA-04","text":"Total comprehension of order remains absent from created knowledge and created causality; this absence simultaneously orders the contraction of taxonomy and the closure of miracle.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:SNX4","CH_GGVC__102:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-01","text":"The plurality of hierarchies and causes is an internal distribution of one divine governance, not a composition of multiple independent sovereignties.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-01","CL_GGVC_37:GA-02","CH_GGVC__100:A2","CH_GGVC__102:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-02","text":"The communication of perfection receives sacred dignity only when ordered to the divine end; abundance of knowledge alone does not constitute an illuminative rank.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-03","CL_GGVC_37:GA-06","CL_GGVC_37:GA-13","CH_GGVC__100:A8","CH_GGVC__101:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-03","text":"The fall removes participation in glory, but not nature nor every coordination of actions; the residual order nevertheless receives sacred direction from external justice, not from the fallen will.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-04","CL_GGVC_37:GA-08","CH_GGVC__100:A16","CH_GGVC__101:A2","CH_GGVC__101:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-04","text":"Spiritual authority and the scope of natural power are distinct axes: an inferior nature can govern a superior nature through greater participation in divine justice.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-07","CL_GGVC_37:GA-13","CH_GGVC__101:SNX3","CH_GGVC__102:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-05","text":"Administration is execution of order, not the essence of rank nor substantial production; therefore office can change while the fundamental disposition is preserved.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-04","CL_GGVC_37:GA-07","CH_GGVC__100:AS6","CH_GGVC__102:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-06","text":"A demonic prodigy is harmful created causality but providentially contained; the appearance of miracle indicates ignorance of the cause, not the autonomy of evil.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-08","CL_GGVC_37:GA-10","CL_GGVC_37:GA-12","CH_GGVC__101:A14","CH_GGVC__102:D5","CH_GGVC__102:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-07","text":"Grace and justice reorder relations of dignity beyond natural proportion without change of species or destruction of nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-04","CL_GGVC_37:GA-09","CL_GGVC_37:GA-13","CH_GGVC__100:A11","CH_GGVC__101:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:XD-08","text":"Corporeal administration is the terminal extension of the hierarchical process: universal disposition descends to the local application of agents, not to the creation of new natures.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-05","CL_GGVC_37:GA-07","CL_GGVC_37:GA-11","CH_GGVC__100:A14","CH_GGVC__102:AS4","CH_GGVC__102:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSD-01","text":"The ontology of the system accords primacy to ordered relation: the same nature receives diverse hierarchical states according to principle, end, and mode of participation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-02","CL_GGVC_37:GA-04","CL_GGVC_37:GA-09","CH_GGVC__100:SD1","CH_GGVC__101:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSD-02","text":"Causality becomes distributed but not fragmented: God universally comprehends, Angels dispose and move, bodies produce effects according to their proper powers.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-01","CL_GGVC_37:GA-07","CL_GGVC_37:GA-11","CH_GGVC__102:SD1","CH_GGVC__102:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSD-03","text":"The system's time is the mutation of state upon a persistent structure: fall, way, and consummation alter operations without simple abolition of nature or rank.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-04","CL_GGVC_37:GA-08","CH_GGVC__100:SD5","CH_GGVC__101:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSD-04","text":"Epistemology requires passage from wonder and common names to reconstruction of the total order, because a visible effect does not indicate its formal cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-06","CL_GGVC_37:GA-10","CL_GGVC_37:GA-12","CH_GGVC__100:SD3","CH_GGVC__102:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSD-05","text":"The implicit politics distinguishes legitimate authority from efficacy alone: coerced coordination can operate, but the dignity of prelation derives from participation in the divine principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-08","CL_GGVC_37:GA-13","CH_GGVC__101:A6","CH_GGVC__101:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:BSD-06","text":"Evil is parasitic and recapturable: it uses the good of nature, produces harmful effects, but neither founds a proper order nor transcends the ends of providence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-01","CL_GGVC_37:GA-08","CL_GGVC_37:GA-12","CH_GGVC__101:SD1","CH_GGVC__101:SD4","CH_GGVC__102:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RLE-01","text":"Received perfection → capacity to communicate → ordering of lower beings → greater manifestation of perfection: rank confirms itself through communication, not through closed possession.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A8","CH_GGVC__100:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RLE-02","text":"Universal end → disposition of things to be done → local application → particular effect: causality descends spirally through distinct modes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A14","CH_GGVC__102:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RLE-03","text":"Good nature retained → perverse will → harmful action → permission and subordination → higher good elicited: flight from the end is recaptured into the circuit of providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A2","CH_GGVC__101:A14","CH_GGVC__101:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RLE-04","text":"Unusual effect → ignorance of created cause → attribution of miracle → reconstruction of universal order → reclassification of prodigy: epistemic error reopens the causal mechanism.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:D5","CH_GGVC__102:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RLE-05","text":"Upon attainment of the end, conducible offices cease, but preservative illumination remains; closure of operation passes into a new circuit of preservation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:D9","CH_GGVC__100:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:RLE-06","text":"God is the attractor that reclassifies conversion, dignity, and the limits of causality: proximity to him orders rank, aversion removes the name of illumination, and transcending his order constitutes miracle.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A15","CH_GGVC__101:A12","CH_GGVC__102:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FGS-B01","text":"One divine principle + every created power within its order → unity of governance without equality of participants.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A2","CH_GGVC__102:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FGS-B02","text":"Diverse modes of reception + diverse offices → hierarchies and ranks as internal articulations of a multitude.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A3","CH_GGVC__100:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FGS-B03","text":"Participated perfection + communicative excess → prelation → universal disposition → particular application.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A8","CH_GGVC__100:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FGS-B04","text":"Nature + grace + glory → distinction between persistent structure, participative elevation, and mutable operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A1","CH_GGVC__100:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FGS-B05","text":"Perversion of will + permanence of nature → orderable harmful action → containment of providence without consecration of evil.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SD1","CH_GGVC__101:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FGS-B06","text":"Spiritual universality + exteriority of local motion → disposition of corporeal agents → unusual effect within created order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:A11","CH_GGVC__102:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FGS-B07","text":"Inclusion of every created power in universal order + divine comprehension of the whole composite → communion of creatures beneath order and miracle proper to God alone.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:A12","CH_GGVC__102:A13","CH_GGVC__100:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:AFM-B01","text":"Unity of principle → diversity of reception → gradation of offices → distinction of nature, grace, and glory → proof of order through evil → extension to corporeal causality → closure of the whole created order under God.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AS4","CH_GGVC__101:AS2","CH_GGVC__102:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:AFM-B02","text":"Apparent contradiction → distinction of respects → anomaly restored within order → limit of created causality determined → higher unity restored.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AF2","CH_GGVC__101:SD1","CH_GGVC__102:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MME-01","text":"Hierarchy is reclassified from a scale of names into an architecture of modes of reception, states of participation, and distributed causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ME2","CH_GGVC__101:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MME-02","text":"Superiority is divided into natural excellence, dignity from divine proximity, and causally governing universality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SNX3","CH_GGVC__102:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MME-03","text":"Evil ceases to appear as an external rival of order and becomes perverse action within good nature, external subjection, and providential permission.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MME-04","text":"The causal field is redescribed through the distinction of divine comprehension, angelic disposition, and corporeal efficiency.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MME-05","text":"Miracle is transferred from the strangeness of the phenomenon to the relation of the effect with the totality of the order of created nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MME-06","text":"Reading becomes necessarily reconstructive: it must refer common taxonomy and sensible appearance to orders of causality and limits of knowledge.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ME9","CH_GGVC__102:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of the surface are treated hierarchies and orders, the state of demons, and the power of Angels concerning bodies and miracles.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ALT1","CH_GGVC__101:ALT1","CH_GGVC__102:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level operate differences of reception, subordination of actions, and distribution of causality among God, created spirits, and corporeal agents.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ALT2","CH_GGVC__101:ALT2","CH_GGVC__102:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the appearance of contradiction is resolved by shifting the question from the existence of power to its mode, end, and scope.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ALT3","CH_GGVC__101:ALT3","CH_GGVC__102:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, natures persist, relations of participation change, operations vary between fall, way, and consummation, and the composite remains the subject of generation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ALT4","CH_GGVC__101:ALT4","CH_GGVC__102:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the form of distinctions effects the thesis itself: individual counterexamples are restored within a higher account without elimination of local difference.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:AS3","CH_GGVC__102:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FL-01","text":"The transition from the universality of spiritual substance to the necessity of angelic presiding over bodies strengthens the metaphysical order, but incompletely determines the mechanism of immediate governance.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FL-02","text":"The concrete measure between rank of nature and authority from divine proximity remains indeterminate, although contradiction is tempered by the distinction of the two orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FL-03","text":"Providence is said to order evil to good, but the particular reason why this action is permitted is not deduced from the general principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:FL-04","text":"The relation among common orders, the singular office of each angel, the elevation of humans, and the distribution of ministries does not produce a complete table of correspondences.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:MRT7","CH_GGVC__100:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MP-01","text":"Hierarchy changes from a taxonomy of angelic ranks into a general form by which created causalities are distributed under one order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ME2","CH_GGVC__102:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MP-02","text":"Illumination changes from communication of light into an act formally defined by the conversion of intellect to God.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MP-03","text":"Prelation changes from an effect of natural inequality into participated authority according to justice and proximity to the divine principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MP-04","text":"Miracle changes from an unusual effect into an exception beyond the order of the whole of created nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly figured as a cascade of mediations: universal accounts descend through dispositions and applications, while all degrees are contained around the divine principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:ME5","CH_GGVC__102:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:HSI-02","text":"The created subject is a receptive node whose nature persists, but whose state varies according to illumination, aversion, grace, and glory.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:A3","CH_GGVC__101:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:HSI-03","text":"Time is a series of mutations of operation around a persistent structure: fall deprives, way leads, consummation halts conducible ministry but retains preservation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:SD5","CH_GGVC__101:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:HSI-04","text":"Possible conflict is not flight outside order, but contention concerning the direction of powers within it; perverse action is recaptured, angelic action disposes, God alone exceeds the whole order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__101:SD4","CH_GGVC__102:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to restore hierarchical division, the permanence of nature under fall, and the limits of angelic corporeal governance.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:MRT1","CH_GGVC__102:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure lies in the distinctions of principle and reception, nature and grace, rank and operation, governance and informing, particular order and total order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:MRT2","CH_GGVC__102:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remain the mechanism of transition from spiritual universality to corporeal governance and the measure of the relation between natural excellence and spiritual authority.","refs":["CH_GGVC__102:MRT5","CH_GGVC__101:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B04","text":"Coherence partly rests upon the analogy of tripartition and the functional reconciliation of nomenclatures; these are generative operations, but do not provide an exhaustive demonstration.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:MRT4","CH_GGVC__100:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement from unity of principle through the proof of evil to the closure of created causality, while individual offices and particular mechanisms resist axiomatic reduction.","refs":["CH_GGVC__100:MRT9","CH_GGVC__101:MRT7","CH_GGVC__102:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_37@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_37:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 109–111","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 108 · Prima pars · Quaestio 109 · Prima pars · Quaestio 110"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 112–114","order":38,"parents":["CH_GGVC__103","CH_GGVC__104","CH_GGVC__105"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_38@R001","sha256":"67da062a51b3a7df99b93496728835f4de5ad80dad28c4f085285a0169517d53","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo angelus intellectum, voluntatem, corpus et vitam singularem hominis regere possit, simulque missioni et custodiae divinae subordinari, sine invasione causalitatis Deo propriae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:AS1","CH_GGVC__104:AS1","CH_GGVC__105:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-02","text":"Thesis centralis causalitatem angelicam constituit ut applicationem particularem, ministerialem et mediatam providentiae universalis, cuius efficacia ex modo recipientis, libertate et ordine divino terminatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:AS2","CH_GGVC__104:AS2","CH_GGVC__105:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit ars distinctionum causalium et relationalium, qua opposita apparentia non tolluntur sed secundum principium, medium, obiectum, tempus et effectum distribuuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:ALT3","CH_GGVC__105:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-04","text":"Motus procedit ab effectu angelico in potentiis humanis ad applicationem localem virtutis, inde ad officium custodiale individuandum et temporaliter ordinandum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:AS6","CH_GGVC__104:AS6","CH_GGVC__105:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-05","text":"Progressio est cumulativa et spiralis: eadem mediatio redit primum ut dispositio potentiae, deinde ut missio, postremo ut regimen continuum personae viatoris.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-06","text":"Hac magnitudine argumentum fit grammatica causalitatis distributae, in qua species muneris et amplitudo obiecti, non sola potentia nuda, locum cuiusque agentis determinant.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SD1","CH_GGVC__104:SD4","CH_GGVC__105:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-07","text":"Clausura systematis defectum, poenam et tribulationem non extra providentiam expellit, sed in permissionem particularem et ordinem iustitiae convertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:AS6","CH_GGVC__105:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-08","text":"Clausura est terminalis tantum quoad officium viae; ipsa providentia manet, dum relatio custodialis mutatur et absentia effectus particularis derelictionem totalem non significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SD6","CH_GGVC__105:D9","CH_GGVC__105:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Providentia universalis inferiora per superiores causas ordinat, ita ut mediatio creata sit forma executionis divinae, non intervallum a Deo vacuum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A1","CH_GGVC__104:A8","CH_GGVC__105:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Deus solus principia intrinseca fidei et voluntatis causat; angelus vero ministerialiter proponit, disponit, illuminat et dirigit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Effectus superior recipienti secundum capacitatem eius communicatur; cognitio humana igitur phantasmatibus, corpore et forma sensibili proportionatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A2","CH_GGVC__103:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Causalitas angelica transit a dispositione corporali et cognitiva ad novam applicationem virtutis, atque inde ad stabile officium providentiae circa personam.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A10","CH_GGVC__104:A2","CH_GGVC__105:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Influxus creatus voluntatem inclinare potest, sed consensum intrinsecum non necessitat; libertas consistit in resistibilitate mediationis, non in absentia omnis influxus.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A8","CH_GGVC__105:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Praesentia angelica est operativa potius quam substantialis: nova applicatio virtutis et permanentia effectus sufficiunt sine motu locali corporali vel praesentia continua ad locum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:D1","CH_GGVC__105:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Hierarchia non est scala immobilis dignitatum, sed distributio cognitionis, imperii, executionis et custodiae secundum universalitatem obiecti.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Contemplatio et operatio exterior non sunt contrariae, quia eadem intelligentia quae divinum ordinem contemplatur actionem particularem regulat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A6","CH_GGVC__104:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Custodia est officium individuatum et temporale personae rationalis in statu viae, fundatum simul in permanentia singularis et in eius defectibilitate practica.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A6","CH_GGVC__105:A10","CH_GGVC__105:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Universalitas providentiae compatitur diversitatem effectuum, quia custodia potest salutem promovere, malum coercere vel defectum iuste permittere.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A15","CH_GGVC__105:SD3","CH_GGVC__105:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Nulla causa angelica ordinem universum transgreditur; mutationes particulares, missiones et permissiones omnes intra providentiam continentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A12","CH_GGVC__105:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Finis et veritas causalitatis angelicae ex eius limitatione oriuntur: angelus realiter agit quia disponit et exsequitur, sed nec principium intrinsecum usurpat nec eventum cogit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SNX3","CH_GGVC__105:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-01","text":"Ordo providentiae identitatem stabilem retinet: in omnibus tribus unitatibus causalitas divina per agentes superiores ad effectus inferiores descendit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A1","CH_GGVC__104:A8","CH_GGVC__105:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-02","text":"Illuminatio functionem mutat: primo est accommodatio veritatis intellectui humano, deinde transmissio intra hierarchiam angelicam, postremo effectus directivus custodiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:AS3","CH_GGVC__104:AS4","CH_GGVC__105:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-03","text":"Applicatio virtutis amplificatur a missione determinata circa corpus ad executionem providentiae circa totam vitam singularis viatoris.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A2","CH_GGVC__105:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-04","text":"Particularitas migrat ab effectu naturali singulari ad praesentiam operativam agentis, atque inde ad salutem et regimen individui permanentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A12","CH_GGVC__104:A1","CH_GGVC__105:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-05","text":"Corpus et locus mutantur ex mediis physiologicis imaginationis et sensus in terminos applicationis missionis; tandem locus separatur ab efficacia continua custodiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A9","CH_GGVC__104:D2","CH_GGVC__105:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-06","text":"Libertas, primo resistentia persuasioni, amplificatur in principium interpretandi cur custodia universalis effectus inaequales et defectus permissos producat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:D3","CH_GGVC__105:A14","CH_GGVC__105:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-07","text":"Hierarchia ex ordine generico superioris et inferioris mutatur in catena functionum, deinde in distributionem officiorum secundum amplitudinem rei administratae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:AS5","CH_GGVC__104:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-08","text":"Ministerium, initio causalitas instrumenti intelligentis, fit officium custodiale temporale; subordinatio ad finem permanet, forma relationis mutatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A4","CH_GGVC__105:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-09","text":"Motus spirituum, humorum et organorum evanescunt ex grammatica principali postquam munus suum peregerunt; in structura superiore servantur ut casus inferior applicationis et effectus custodialis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:D6","CH_GGVC__105:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:CGD-01","text":"Motus spirituum, humorum, phantasmatum et organorum manent machinae locales quibus effectus angelicus in potentiis organicis explicatur; structuram totius gregis soli non constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A9","CH_GGVC__103:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:CGD-02","text":"Numerus ordinum missorum et interpretatio nominum angelicorum manent determinatio localis distributionis; sursum persistit tantum principium quod nomen munus causale significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A14","CH_GGVC__104:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:CGD-03","text":"Initium custodiae a nativitate, tutela fetus et pugna angelica sunt determinationes particulares; ad gradum praesentem ascendunt temporalitas officii et concordia sub cognitione limitata.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A13","CH_GGVC__105:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:CGD-04","text":"Distinctio Dei causae principalis ab angelo causa ministeriali amplificatur sursum, quia intellectum, missionem et custodiam una ratione ordinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:CGD-05","text":"Missio est commissura inter potentiam angelicam et custodiam: convertit capacitatem agendi in relationem operativam determinatam, ex qua officium durabile constitui potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:D1","CH_GGVC__105:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:CGD-06","text":"Ambiguitas localis inter assistere et ministrare absorbetur in distinctionem praesentiae operativae: idem angelus potest Deo assistere, creaturae ministrare et absens localiter efficax manere.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:NX7","CH_GGVC__105:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:CGD-07","text":"Mutationes sensuum extraordinariae et defectus custodiales desinunt esse anomalitates separatae; ad gradum praesentem reclassificantur ut variationes particulares intra ordinem universalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:D8","CH_GGVC__105:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat causalitatem divinam et angelicam in eodem plano concurrere; principium intrinsecum et executio ministerialis nec rivales nec commutabiles sunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:DNX1","CH_GGVC__105:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GNX-02","text":"Angelus nec habitum fidei infundit nec voluntatem ab interiori determinat; eius potestas circa propositionem, dispositionem, persuasionem et executionem terminatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:NX1","CH_GGVC__103:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GNX-03","text":"Cognitio humana non recipit veritatem angelicam nude et incorporaliter; interioritas humana ad phantasmata et condiciones corporales alligatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:NX2","CH_GGVC__103:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GNX-04","text":"Missio non est translatio localis substantiae angelicae; locus sequitur actionem imperatam et novam applicationem virtutis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:NX1","CH_GGVC__104:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GNX-05","text":"Hierarchia non definitur per immobilitatem, otium vel dignitatem nudam; species causalitatis et muneris differentias ordinum constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:SD1","CH_GGVC__104:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GNX-06","text":"Custodia non est cautio infallibilis eventus boni; universalitas eius nec uniformitatem effectuum nec impedimentum omnis culpae requirit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:DNX4","CH_GGVC__105:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GNX-07","text":"Nulla absentia localis, culpa, poena vel tribulatio constituit regionem ontologice exemptam a providentia.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SNX1","CH_GGVC__105:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet quod universalitas divina causas secundas superfluas reddat; aliter illuminatio, missio et custodia nullam causalitatem propriam haberent.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:SNX1","CH_GGVC__105:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet quod causa creata aut autonome agat aut mere passive transmittere possit; angelus est instrumentum intelligens, simul dependens et vere operans.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SNX2","CH_GGVC__104:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet quod recipiens sit materia causaliter indifferens; capacitas cognitiva, corpus et libertas determinantes sunt formae receptionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A2","CH_GGVC__105:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet quod praesentia localis sit condicio substantialis actionis intelligentis; aliter missio sine motu et custodia sine praesentia continua concipi non possent.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:NX1","CH_GGVC__105:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet quod persona sit individuum transiens cuius sola species providentiam principalem mereatur; permanentia singularis individuationem curae sustinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:DNX3","CH_GGVC__105:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse debet quod aliquod malum neque a iustitia fiat neque ab ea permittatur; talis defectus clausuram providentiae dissolveret.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:DNX6","CH_GGVC__105:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas creaturae quae principium intrinsecum boni producat et simul exitum infallibiliter cogat ontologice inadmissibilis est.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SNX1","CH_GGVC__105:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSA-02","text":"Systema circumagit immediationem divinam quae numquam fit absentia mediationis: Deus immediate fundat ordinem, sed eius executio ordinaria per causas secundas transit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:SNX2","CH_GGVC__105:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSA-03","text":"Cognitio exhaustiva ordinis divini manet angelis indisponibilis ante revelationem; concordia voluntatum non requirit aequalitatem cognitionis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A18","CH_GGVC__105:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSA-04","text":"Signum internum infallibile quo illuminatio, custodia vel permissio ex solo effectu cognoscatur non datur; manifestatio effectus a cognitione causae separatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:NX3","CH_GGVC__105:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSA-05","text":"Extra providentiam nulla exterioritas ultima admittitur; quod ut absentia, casus vel defectus apparet intra ordinem universalem reabsorbetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SNX1","CH_GGVC__105:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-01","text":"Cum receptio humana phantasmatibus indigeat, missio novam applicationem virtutis constituat et custodia providentiam exsequatur, praesentia angelica definiri debet per operationem proportionatam circa personam concretam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-03","CL_GGVC_38:GA-04","CL_GGVC_38:GA-06","CH_GGVC__103:A3","CH_GGVC__104:A2","CH_GGVC__105:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-02","text":"Causalitas creata eo verior est quo clarius a productione principii intrinseci et a necessitatione consensus distinguitur; dependentia non evacuat agentem intelligentem, sed speciem eius causalitatis constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-02","CL_GGVC_38:GA-05","CL_GGVC_38:GA-12","CH_GGVC__103:A6","CH_GGVC__104:A4","CH_GGVC__105:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-03","text":"Gradus hierarchicus exprimit amplitudinem obiecti et speciem muneris potius quam quantitatem simplicem potentiae; superiores disponunt universaliora, inferiores exsequuntur particularia.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-07","CH_GGVC__104:A14","CH_GGVC__105:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-04","text":"Transmissio superioris veritatis est series translationum: intellectus angelicus actionem ordinat, virtus determinato obiecto applicatur, et cognitio humana sub signis sensibilibus instruitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-03","CL_GGVC_38:GA-04","CL_GGVC_38:GA-08","CH_GGVC__103:AS3","CH_GGVC__104:A5","CH_GGVC__105:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-05","text":"Quia influxus angelicus resistibilis est, eventus malus neque absentiam custodiae neque negligentiam custodis per se probat; diversitas exitus est effectus coniunctionis mediationis et libertatis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-05","CL_GGVC_38:GA-10","CH_GGVC__103:D3","CH_GGVC__105:SD3","CH_GGVC__105:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-06","text":"Mutatio praeter cursum particularem et defectus permissus eandem structuram participant: ambo ordinem proximum suspendere possunt sine transgressione ordinis universalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-10","CL_GGVC_38:GA-11","CH_GGVC__103:A12","CH_GGVC__105:SD4","CH_GGVC__105:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-07","text":"Custodia continua non requirit continuam actionem exteriorem, quia intelligentia contemplativa officium ordinat et effectus custodialis praesentiam localem superare potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-06","CL_GGVC_38:GA-08","CL_GGVC_38:GA-09","CH_GGVC__104:A6","CH_GGVC__105:NX8","CH_GGVC__105:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-08","text":"Missio et custodia non coincidunt: missio est eventus novae applicationis circa exteriora, custodia vero officium durabile quod plures missiones, illuminationes et permissiones comprehendere potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-04","CL_GGVC_38:GA-09","CH_GGVC__104:A13","CH_GGVC__105:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSD-01","text":"Ontologia emergens est causalitas distributa et asymmetrica: Deus fundat principia et finem, hierarchia cognitionem atque imperium transmittit, angelus particularis exsequitur, homo autem secundum corpus et libertatem recipit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SD1","CH_GGVC__104:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum humanum fit persona incorporata, libera, singulariter permanens et tamen defectibilis, cuius dignitas et indigentia ex eadem natura rationali oriuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A3","CH_GGVC__105:A6","CH_GGVC__105:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSD-03","text":"Tempus systematis est duplex: missio introducit novitatem applicationis, custodia autem durationem officii inter nativitatem et terminum status viae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A2","CH_GGVC__105:D7","CH_GGVC__105:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSD-04","text":"Ordo epistemicus est constitutive inaequalis: recipiens effectum sine causa cognoscere potest, inferiores illuminationem recipiunt, et angeli sententiam divinam sequuntur antequam omnes rationes eius cognoscant.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:NX3","CH_GGVC__104:A12","CH_GGVC__105:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSD-05","text":"Auctoritas reclassificatur ut munus causale ordinatum fini alterius, non ut dominium ontologicum super recipientem; superioritas per dispositionem, executionem et amplitudinem curae mensuratur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:D4","CH_GGVC__104:SD4","CH_GGVC__105:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSD-06","text":"Malum non fit causa positiva providentiae, sed condicio permissiva in qua libertas creata, iustitia divina et cognitio angelica incompleta simul manifestantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SD4","CH_GGVC__105:DNX6","CH_GGVC__105:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSD-07","text":"Methodus distinctionum ipsa doctrinam efficit: dividendo principium ab instrumento, praesentiam ab operatione et voluntatem universalem a particulari, textus ordinem quem asserit formaliter exsequitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:ALT3","CH_GGVC__104:ALT3","CH_GGVC__105:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RLE-01","text":"Circuitus principalis est: providentia universaliter ordinat → hierarchia transmittit → angelus particulariter applicatur → homo libere respondet → eventus aut profectus aut permissus ad providentiam reordinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A1","CH_GGVC__104:A3","CH_GGVC__105:A14","CH_GGVC__105:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RLE-02","text":"Illuminatio spiraliter crescit: veritas superior accommodatur homini, interioriter per ordines communicatur, deinde in custodia redit ut instructio ad salutem.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:AS3","CH_GGVC__104:AS4","CH_GGVC__105:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RLE-03","text":"Via amplificationis procedit a potentia movendi corpus ad applicationem determinatam virtutis, inde ad pluralitatem effectuum custodialium circa totam vitam.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A10","CH_GGVC__104:A2","CH_GGVC__105:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RLE-04","text":"Hierarchia recursive operatur, quia virtus superior in inferiore participante exsequiturur; executio particularis non relinquit superiorem ordinem, sed eum praesentem reddit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A9","CH_GGVC__104:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RLE-05","text":"Defectus circuitum non frangit: angelus potest malum non impedire, permissio ad iudicium divinum refertur, et universalitas providentiae post apparentem absentiam confirmatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:D8","CH_GGVC__105:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RLE-06","text":"Attractor totius structurae est mediatio limitata: quo diligentius potestas angelica a creatione, necessitatione et autonomia excluditur, eo distinctior eius causalitas ministerialis fit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SNX3","CH_GGVC__104:SNX1","CH_GGVC__105:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RLE-07","text":"Terminus viae machinam mutat potius quam delet: custodia cessat ut officium periculi, sed ordo providentiae transit in conregnum, poenam vel aliam relationem terminalem.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SD6","CH_GGVC__105:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B01","text":"Providentia universalis constituit asymmetriam primam, qua variabilia per invariabilia et inferiora per superiora ordinantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A1","CH_GGVC__105:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B02","text":"Asymmetria dividit causalitatem in principium divinum intrinsecum et instrumentum angelicum intelligens, quod ab alio movetur sed vere agit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SD1","CH_GGVC__104:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B03","text":"Causalitas superior proportionatur recipienti per phantasmata, passiones, organa et instructionem, ne communicatio in nudam identitatem ordinum cognitionis collabatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A2","CH_GGVC__103:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B04","text":"Virtus angelica determinato obiecto ex imperio divino applicatur; haec determinatio missionem generat sine translatione substantiali.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A2","CH_GGVC__104:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B05","text":"Missio per hierarchiam distribuitur secundum cognitionem, imperium, executionem et universalitatem obiecti, non secundum dignitatem nudam.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B06","text":"Applicatio particularis stabilitur in custodia, quia persona rationalis singulariter permanet et per tempus viae regimine indiget.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A4","CH_GGVC__105:A6","CH_GGVC__105:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B07","text":"Custodia illuminationem et cohibitionem offert, sed responsio libera effectum variat; efficacia igitur non uniformiter in salutem terminatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A14","CH_GGVC__105:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B08","text":"Defectus permissus ad ordinem iustitiae redit, unde providentia manet universalis, voluntas beata concordat et cognitio finita revelationem exspectat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A15","CH_GGVC__105:SD4","CH_GGVC__105:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:AFM-B01","text":"Potentia angelica limitanda → effectus intellectui et corpori proportionatus → applicatio virtutis ut missio → distributio hierarchica → custodia singularis et temporalis → responsio libera → defectus permissive reordinatus.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:AS6","CH_GGVC__104:AS6","CH_GGVC__105:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:AFM-B02","text":"Apparens defectus mediationis → distinctio absentiae a derelictione → permissio particularis → iustitia divina → concordia voluntatum sub cognitione incompleta → restitutio universalitatis providentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:AS6","CH_GGVC__105:SD4","CH_GGVC__105:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MME-01","text":"Praesentia angelica reclassificatur a situ locali ad relationem operativam, quae per applicationem virtutis incipit et per effectum custodialem sine praesentia locali durare potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:ME1","CH_GGVC__105:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MME-02","text":"Actio angelica reclassificatur ab incursu extrinseco indistincto ad causalitatem distributam, in qua dependentia maxima a Deo cum vera actione creaturae componitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SD1","CH_GGVC__104:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MME-03","text":"Hierarchia reordinatur a distantia statica graduum ad tabulam functionum et ambituum: videre, disponere, imperare, exsequi et custodire universaliora vel particularia.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:ME5","CH_GGVC__105:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MME-04","text":"Interioritas humana nec anima separata nec regio causaliter clausa est; fit nexus intellectus, phantasmatum, corporis, passionis et consensus liberi.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SNX4","CH_GGVC__105:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MME-05","text":"Libertas reclassificatur inter autonomiam sufficientem et necessitatem coactivam: indiget auxilio exteriori, sed facultatem resistendi retinet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:ME2","CH_GGVC__105:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MME-06","text":"Defectus reclassificatur ab indicio absentiae providentiae ad eventum permissum, cuius non-impeditio ab effectu positivo custodiae distinguenda est.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SD4","CH_GGVC__105:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MME-07","text":"Persona singularis fit obiectum directum providentiae non sola ratione speciei, sed propter permanentiam formae rationalis et occultas condiciones salutis propriae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:ME2","CH_GGVC__105:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei aguntur illuminatio intellectus, inclinatio voluntatis, mutatio sensus, missio angelica et custodia singularis hominis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:ALT1","CH_GGVC__105:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali haec obiecta uniuntur per distinctionem causalitatis divinae et angelicae, applicationem virtutis, distributionem hierarchicam et individuationem providentiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:ALT2","CH_GGVC__104:ALT2","CH_GGVC__105:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico certamen interpretativum per distinctiones regitur: immediatum a mediato, interius ab exteriori, praesentia ab operatione et defectus a derelictione separantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:ALT3","CH_GGVC__104:ALT3","CH_GGVC__105:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est ordo causarum participantium, tempus autem successionem applicationis, durationem viae et mutationem relationis custodialis mensurat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:ALT4","CH_GGVC__104:ALT4","CH_GGVC__105:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo forma disputativa ipsum ordinem efficit: objectiones particulatim distinguit, eas causaliter distribuit et ad unitatem providentiae recursu reducit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:AS3","CH_GGVC__105:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FL-01","text":"Causalitas corporalis imaginationis et sensus cum dependentia actuum vitalium a principiis interioribus componitur, sed proportio inter dispositionem organicam et actum determinatum non plene explicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:MRT5","CH_GGVC__103:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FL-02","text":"Natura intellectualis locum per se non postulat, missio tamen locum actionis determinat; distinctio inter substantiam et applicationem tensionem solvit formaliter, non mechanismum praesentiae exhaustive.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:AS1","CH_GGVC__104:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FL-03","text":"Universalitas custodiae cum inaequalitate extrema effectuum componitur, sed criterium internum quo defectus permissivus ab inefficacia discernatur indisponibile manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SD3","CH_GGVC__105:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FL-04","text":"Voluntas universalis salutis et voluntas particularis iustitiae simul servantur; absentia doloris pendet a subordinanda affectione universali circumstantiis iudicii divini.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A17","CH_GGVC__105:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FL-05","text":"Distributio missionum ex nominibus ordinum vim structuralem habet, sed stabilitas eius pendet ab hypothesi quod nomen proprietatem functionalem fideliter manifestet; etiam numerus proportionum interpretative variat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:MRT5","CH_GGVC__104:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MP-01","text":"Illuminatio mutatur ab accommodatione veritatis ad transmissionem hierarchicam, deinde ad directionem custodialem; identitas vocabuli servatur, obiectum et functio ampliantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:AS3","CH_GGVC__104:AS4","CH_GGVC__105:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MP-02","text":"Missio mutatur ab eventu novae applicationis in condicionem exsecutivam officii custodialis; custodia missionem continere potest, sed cum ea non identificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A13","CH_GGVC__105:A4","CH_GGVC__105:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MP-03","text":"Permanentia singularis personae transit in assignationem singularis custodis, sed hic transitus probabilitatem rationalem potius quam necessitatem demonstrativam praebet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:MRT3","CH_GGVC__105:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MP-04","text":"Absentia localis mutatur in permissionem particularem: quod primo defectus praesentiae videri potest fit modus non-impediendi intra permanentem providentiam.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:NX8","CH_GGVC__105:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est reticulum gubernationis nidificatum, in quo visio, imperium, applicatio et custodia causam universalem in effectus particulares traducunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum humanum est nodus incorporatus et liber: suscipit formas, passiones et instructiones, sed nullum influxum creatum in consensum necessarium convertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A3","CH_GGVC__105:A14","CH_GGVC__105:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est series applicationum intra arcum officii: virtus de novo adest, custodia per statum viae durat, et terminus relationem in aliam formam transfert.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A2","CH_GGVC__105:SD6","CH_GGVC__105:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga a providentia, sed motus liber intra campum illuminationis, periculi, cohibitionis et permissionis cui nulla creatura totaliter subtrahitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A10","CH_GGVC__105:D8","CH_GGVC__105:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de providentia distributa, causalitate ministeriali, receptione proportionata, libertate resistibili, applicatione virtutis et custodia temporali sufficiunt ad machinam principalem huius gregis restituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:MRT1","CH_GGVC__104:MRT1","CH_GGVC__105:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est distinctio causalitatis divinae principialis ab angelica executione, quia eadem distinctio illuminationem, missionem et custodiam sine rivalitate coniungit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:MRT2","CH_GGVC__105:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent nexus inter dispositionem corporalem et actum concretum, atque criterium quo defectus iuste permissus ab apparentia custodiae deficientis discernatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:MRT4","CH_GGVC__105:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B04","text":"Cohaerentia in punctis difficilibus distinctionibus semanticis et causalibus nititur; tensionem non delet, sed eam in respectus diversos distribuit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:MRT4","CH_GGVC__105:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat si effectus particulares non separatim leguntur, sed ut gradus a limitatione potestatis angelicae ad missionem, individuationem, temporalitatem et permissionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:MRT6","CH_GGVC__105:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B06","text":"Plena reductio axiomaticae resistunt distributio ordinum ex nominibus et terminus nativitatis, quia utraque conclusio probationibus probabilibus vel interpretationibus variabilibus dependet; hae tamen incertitudines machinam centralem non dissolvunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:MRT5","CH_GGVC__105:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T173236545620Z_787b6f3f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 112–114","source_sha256":"67da062a51b3a7df99b93496728835f4de5ad80dad28c4f085285a0169517d53","sha256":"9580f4fa16ae0fa02c19308d5d1bc36c0219484399669da84097bcd589ce00c0","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:32:36.546509+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how an angel can govern the intellect, will, body, and singular life of a human being, while also being subordinated to divine mission and guardianship, without invading causality proper to God.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:AS1","CH_GGVC__104:AS1","CH_GGVC__105:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-02","text":"The central thesis establishes angelic causality as a particular, ministerial, and mediated application of universal providence, whose efficacy is delimited by the mode of the recipient, freedom, and divine order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:AS2","CH_GGVC__104:AS2","CH_GGVC__105:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-03","text":"The argument becomes an art of causal and relational distinctions, by which apparent opposites are not removed but distributed according to principle, means, object, time, and effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:ALT3","CH_GGVC__105:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-04","text":"The movement proceeds from angelic effect in human powers to the local application of power, thence to a guardian office to be individuated and temporally ordered.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:AS6","CH_GGVC__104:AS6","CH_GGVC__105:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-05","text":"The progression is cumulative and spiral: the same mediation returns first as a disposition of power, then as mission, finally as the continuous governance of the wayfaring person.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-06","text":"At this scale, the argument becomes a grammar of distributed causality, in which the kind of office and the scope of the object, not bare power alone, determine the place of each agent.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SD1","CH_GGVC__104:SD4","CH_GGVC__105:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-07","text":"The closure of the system does not expel defect, punishment, and tribulation from providence, but converts them into particular permission and an order of justice.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:AS6","CH_GGVC__105:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BAS-08","text":"The closure is terminal only with respect to the office of the way; providence itself remains, while the guardian relation changes, and the absence of a particular effect does not signify total abandonment.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SD6","CH_GGVC__105:D9","CH_GGVC__105:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Universal providence orders lower things through higher causes, so that created mediation is a form of divine execution, not an interval empty of God.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A1","CH_GGVC__104:A8","CH_GGVC__105:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] God alone causes the intrinsic principles of faith and will; the angel, however, ministerially proposes, disposes, illuminates, and directs.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] A higher effect is communicated to the recipient according to its capacity; human cognition is therefore proportioned to phantasms, the body, and sensible form.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A2","CH_GGVC__103:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Angelic causality passes from bodily and cognitive disposition to a new application of power, and thence to a stable office of providence concerning the person.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A10","CH_GGVC__104:A2","CH_GGVC__105:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Created influence can incline the will, but does not necessitate intrinsic consent; freedom consists in the resistibility of mediation, not in the absence of every influence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A8","CH_GGVC__105:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Angelic presence is operative rather than substantial: a new application of power and the persistence of effect suffice without bodily local motion or continuous presence in a place.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:D1","CH_GGVC__105:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Hierarchy is not an immobile scale of dignities, but a distribution of cognition, command, execution, and guardianship according to the universality of the object.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Contemplation and outward operation are not contrary, because the same intelligence that contemplates divine order regulates particular action.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A6","CH_GGVC__104:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Guardianship is an individuated and temporal office of the rational person in the state of the way, founded both on the permanence of the singular and on its practical defectibility.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A6","CH_GGVC__105:A10","CH_GGVC__105:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] The universality of providence is compatible with diversity of effects, because guardianship can promote salvation, restrain evil, or justly permit defect.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A15","CH_GGVC__105:SD3","CH_GGVC__105:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] No angelic cause transcends the universal order; particular changes, missions, and permissions are all contained within providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A12","CH_GGVC__105:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] The end and truth of angelic causality arise from its limitation: the angel truly acts because it disposes and executes, but it neither usurps the intrinsic principle nor compels the outcome.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SNX3","CH_GGVC__105:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-01","text":"The order of providence retains a stable identity: in all three units, divine causality descends through higher agents to lower effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A1","CH_GGVC__104:A8","CH_GGVC__105:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-02","text":"Illumination changes function: first it is the accommodation of truth to the human intellect, then transmission within the angelic hierarchy, finally the directive effect of guardianship.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:AS3","CH_GGVC__104:AS4","CH_GGVC__105:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-03","text":"The application of power is amplified from a determinate mission concerning the body to the execution of providence concerning the entire life of the singular wayfarer.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A2","CH_GGVC__105:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-04","text":"Particularity migrates from a singular natural effect to the operative presence of the agent, and thence to the salvation and governance of the enduring individual.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A12","CH_GGVC__104:A1","CH_GGVC__105:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-05","text":"Body and place change from physiological means of imagination and sense into terms of the application of mission; finally, place is separated from the continuous efficacy of guardianship.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A9","CH_GGVC__104:D2","CH_GGVC__105:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-06","text":"Freedom, initially resistance to persuasion, is amplified into a principle for interpreting why universal guardianship produces unequal effects and permitted defects.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:D3","CH_GGVC__105:A14","CH_GGVC__105:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-07","text":"Hierarchy changes from a generic order of higher and lower into a chain of functions, then into a distribution of offices according to the scope of the thing administered.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:AS5","CH_GGVC__104:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-08","text":"Ministry, initially the causality of an intelligent instrument, becomes a temporal guardian office; subordination to the end remains, while the form of relation changes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A4","CH_GGVC__105:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RMD-09","text":"The motions of spirits, humors, and organs vanish from the principal grammar after fulfilling their role; in the higher structure they are preserved as a lower case of application and guardian effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:D6","CH_GGVC__105:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:CGD-01","text":"The motions of spirits, humors, phantasms, and organs remain local mechanisms by which angelic effect in organic powers is explained; by themselves they do not constitute the structure of the whole group.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A9","CH_GGVC__103:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:CGD-02","text":"The number of sent orders and the interpretation of angelic names remain a local determination of distribution; above, only the principle persists that a name signifies a causal office.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A14","CH_GGVC__104:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:CGD-03","text":"The beginning of guardianship from birth, protection of the fetus, and angelic conflict are particular determinations; at the present level, the temporality of office and concord under limited cognition ascend.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A13","CH_GGVC__105:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:CGD-04","text":"The distinction between God as principal cause and the angel as ministerial cause is amplified above, because it orders intellect, mission, and guardianship under one account.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:CGD-05","text":"Mission is the joint between angelic power and guardianship: it converts the capacity to act into a determinate operative relation, from which a durable office can be constituted.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:D1","CH_GGVC__105:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:CGD-06","text":"The local ambiguity between assisting and ministering is absorbed into the distinction of operative presence: the same angel can assist God, minister to the creature, and remain efficacious while locally absent.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:NX7","CH_GGVC__105:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:CGD-07","text":"Extraordinary changes of the senses and guardian defects cease to be separate anomalies; at the present level they are reclassified as particular variations within the universal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:D8","CH_GGVC__105:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GNX-01","text":"The system denies that divine and angelic causality concur on the same plane; intrinsic principle and ministerial execution are neither rivals nor interchangeable.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:DNX1","CH_GGVC__105:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GNX-02","text":"The angel neither infuses the habit of faith nor determines the will from within; its power is limited to proposing, disposing, persuading, and executing.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:NX1","CH_GGVC__103:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GNX-03","text":"Human cognition does not receive angelic truth nakedly and incorporeally; human interiority is bound to phantasms and bodily conditions.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:NX2","CH_GGVC__103:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GNX-04","text":"Mission is not a local translation of angelic substance; place follows commanded action and a new application of power.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:NX1","CH_GGVC__104:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GNX-05","text":"Hierarchy is not defined by immobility, idleness, or bare dignity; the kind of causality and office constitutes the differences of orders.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:SD1","CH_GGVC__104:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GNX-06","text":"Guardianship is not an infallible guarantee of a good outcome; its universality requires neither uniformity of effects nor the prevention of every fault.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:DNX4","CH_GGVC__105:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:GNX-07","text":"No local absence, fault, punishment, or tribulation constitutes a region ontologically exempt from providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SNX1","CH_GGVC__105:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that divine universality renders secondary causes superfluous; otherwise illumination, mission, and guardianship would have no proper causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:SNX1","CH_GGVC__105:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that a created cause either acts autonomously or can transmit merely passively; the angel is an intelligent instrument, at once dependent and truly operative.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SNX2","CH_GGVC__104:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that the recipient is causally indifferent matter; cognitive capacity, body, and freedom are determining forms of reception.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A2","CH_GGVC__105:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that local presence is a substantial condition of intelligent action; otherwise mission without motion and guardianship without continuous presence could not be conceived.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:NX1","CH_GGVC__105:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the person is a passing individual whose species alone merits principal providence; the permanence of the singular sustains the individuation of care.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:DNX3","CH_GGVC__105:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that any evil is neither done by justice nor permitted by it; such a defect would dissolve the closure of providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:DNX6","CH_GGVC__105:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSA-01","text":"The possibility of a creature that produces the intrinsic principle of good and simultaneously compels the outcome infallibly is ontologically inadmissible.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SNX1","CH_GGVC__105:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSA-02","text":"The system revolves around a divine immediacy that never becomes the absence of mediation: God immediately founds the order, but its ordinary execution passes through secondary causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:SNX2","CH_GGVC__105:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSA-03","text":"Exhaustive knowledge of divine order remains unavailable to angels before revelation; concord of wills does not require equality of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A18","CH_GGVC__105:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSA-04","text":"No infallible internal sign is given by which illumination, guardianship, or permission may be known from effect alone; the manifestation of the effect is separated from knowledge of the cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:NX3","CH_GGVC__105:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSA-05","text":"No ultimate exteriority outside providence is admitted; what appears as absence, chance, or defect is reabsorbed within the universal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SNX1","CH_GGVC__105:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-01","text":"Since human reception requires phantasms, mission constitutes a new application of power, and guardianship executes providence, angelic presence must be defined through proportionate operation concerning the concrete person.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-03","CL_GGVC_38:GA-04","CL_GGVC_38:GA-06","CH_GGVC__103:A3","CH_GGVC__104:A2","CH_GGVC__105:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-02","text":"Created causality is truer insofar as it is more clearly distinguished from producing an intrinsic principle and from necessitating consent; dependence does not empty the intelligent agent, but constitutes the kind of its causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-02","CL_GGVC_38:GA-05","CL_GGVC_38:GA-12","CH_GGVC__103:A6","CH_GGVC__104:A4","CH_GGVC__105:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-03","text":"Hierarchical grade expresses the scope of the object and the kind of office rather than a simple quantity of power; higher beings dispose more universal things, lower beings execute particulars.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-07","CH_GGVC__104:A14","CH_GGVC__105:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-04","text":"The transmission of higher truth is a series of translations: angelic intellect orders action, power is applied to a determinate object, and human cognition is instructed under sensible signs.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-03","CL_GGVC_38:GA-04","CL_GGVC_38:GA-08","CH_GGVC__103:AS3","CH_GGVC__104:A5","CH_GGVC__105:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-05","text":"Because angelic influence is resistible, a bad outcome does not by itself prove either the absence of guardianship or the negligence of the guardian; diversity of outcomes is the effect of the conjunction of mediation and freedom.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-05","CL_GGVC_38:GA-10","CH_GGVC__103:D3","CH_GGVC__105:SD3","CH_GGVC__105:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-06","text":"A change outside the particular course and a permitted defect share the same structure: both can suspend the proximate order without transgressing the universal order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-10","CL_GGVC_38:GA-11","CH_GGVC__103:A12","CH_GGVC__105:SD4","CH_GGVC__105:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-07","text":"Continuous guardianship does not require continuous outward action, because contemplative intelligence orders the office and guardian effect can surpass local presence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-06","CL_GGVC_38:GA-08","CL_GGVC_38:GA-09","CH_GGVC__104:A6","CH_GGVC__105:NX8","CH_GGVC__105:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:XD-08","text":"Mission and guardianship do not coincide: mission is the event of a new application concerning exterior things, whereas guardianship is a durable office that can include several missions, illuminations, and permissions.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-04","CL_GGVC_38:GA-09","CH_GGVC__104:A13","CH_GGVC__105:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSD-01","text":"The emerging ontology is distributed and asymmetric causality: God founds principles and end, hierarchy transmits cognition and command, the particular angel executes, while the human receives according to body and freedom.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SD1","CH_GGVC__104:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSD-02","text":"The human subject becomes an embodied, free, singularly enduring, and yet defectible person, whose dignity and need arise from the same rational nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A3","CH_GGVC__105:A6","CH_GGVC__105:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSD-03","text":"The time of the system is twofold: mission introduces novelty of application, while guardianship introduces duration of office between birth and the terminus of the state of the way.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A2","CH_GGVC__105:D7","CH_GGVC__105:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSD-04","text":"The epistemic order is constitutively unequal: a recipient can know an effect without knowing its cause, lower beings receive illumination, and angels follow divine judgment before knowing all its reasons.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:NX3","CH_GGVC__104:A12","CH_GGVC__105:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSD-05","text":"Authority is reclassified as a causal office ordered to the end of another, not as ontological dominion over the recipient; superiority is measured through disposition, execution, and scope of care.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:D4","CH_GGVC__104:SD4","CH_GGVC__105:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSD-06","text":"Evil does not become a positive cause of providence, but a permissive condition in which created freedom, divine justice, and incomplete angelic cognition are manifested together.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SD4","CH_GGVC__105:DNX6","CH_GGVC__105:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:BSD-07","text":"The method of distinctions itself produces the doctrine: by dividing principle from instrument, presence from operation, and universal will from particular will, the text formally executes the order it asserts.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:ALT3","CH_GGVC__104:ALT3","CH_GGVC__105:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RLE-01","text":"The principal circuit is: providence orders universally → hierarchy transmits → the angel applies particularly → the human responds freely → the outcome, whether progress or permission, is reordered to providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A1","CH_GGVC__104:A3","CH_GGVC__105:A14","CH_GGVC__105:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RLE-02","text":"Illumination grows spirally: higher truth is accommodated to the human, communicated internally through the orders, then returns in guardianship as instruction toward salvation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:AS3","CH_GGVC__104:AS4","CH_GGVC__105:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RLE-03","text":"The path of amplification proceeds from the power to move the body to a determinate application of power, thence to a plurality of guardian effects concerning the whole life.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A10","CH_GGVC__104:A2","CH_GGVC__105:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RLE-04","text":"Hierarchy operates recursively, because higher power executes in the participating lower being; particular execution does not leave the higher order, but renders it present.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A9","CH_GGVC__104:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RLE-05","text":"Defect does not break the circuit: the angel may not prevent evil, permission is referred to divine judgment, and the universality of providence is confirmed after apparent absence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:D8","CH_GGVC__105:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RLE-06","text":"The attractor of the entire structure is limited mediation: the more carefully angelic power is excluded from creation, necessitation, and autonomy, the more distinct its ministerial causality becomes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SNX3","CH_GGVC__104:SNX1","CH_GGVC__105:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:RLE-07","text":"The terminus of the way changes the mechanism rather than destroying it: guardianship ceases as an office of peril, but the order of providence passes into co-reigning, punishment, or another terminal relation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SD6","CH_GGVC__105:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B01","text":"Universal providence constitutes the first asymmetry, by which variables are ordered through invariants and lower things through higher ones.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A1","CH_GGVC__105:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B02","text":"Asymmetry divides causality into an intrinsic divine principle and an intelligent angelic instrument, which is moved by another yet truly acts.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SD1","CH_GGVC__104:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B03","text":"Higher causality is proportioned to the recipient through phantasms, passions, organs, and instruction, lest communication collapse into a bare identity of orders of cognition.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A2","CH_GGVC__103:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B04","text":"Angelic power is applied to a determinate object by divine command; this determination generates mission without substantial translation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A2","CH_GGVC__104:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B05","text":"Mission is distributed through hierarchy according to cognition, command, execution, and universality of object, not according to bare dignity.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B06","text":"Particular application is stabilized in guardianship, because the rational person singularly endures and needs governance through the time of the way.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A4","CH_GGVC__105:A6","CH_GGVC__105:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B07","text":"Guardianship offers illumination and restraint, but free response varies the effect; efficacy therefore does not terminate uniformly in salvation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A14","CH_GGVC__105:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FGS-B08","text":"Permitted defect returns to the order of justice, whence providence remains universal, the blessed will agrees, and finite cognition awaits revelation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A15","CH_GGVC__105:SD4","CH_GGVC__105:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:AFM-B01","text":"Angelic power to be limited → effect proportioned to intellect and body → application of power as mission → hierarchical distribution → singular and temporal guardianship → free response → permissively reordered defect.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:AS6","CH_GGVC__104:AS6","CH_GGVC__105:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:AFM-B02","text":"Apparent defect of mediation → distinction of absence from abandonment → particular permission → divine justice → concord of wills under incomplete cognition → restoration of the universality of providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:AS6","CH_GGVC__105:SD4","CH_GGVC__105:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MME-01","text":"Angelic presence is reclassified from local situation to an operative relation, which begins through the application of power and can endure through guardian effect without local presence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:ME1","CH_GGVC__105:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MME-02","text":"Angelic action is reclassified from an indistinct extrinsic incursion to distributed causality, in which maximal dependence on God is joined with the creature's true action.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SD1","CH_GGVC__104:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MME-03","text":"Hierarchy is reordered from a static distance of grades to a map of functions and scopes: to see, dispose, command, execute, and guard universal or particular things.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:ME5","CH_GGVC__105:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MME-04","text":"Human interiority is neither a separate soul nor a causally closed region; it becomes a nexus of intellect, phantasms, body, passion, and free consent.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:SNX4","CH_GGVC__105:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MME-05","text":"Freedom is reclassified between sufficient autonomy and coercive necessity: it needs external assistance, but retains the capacity to resist.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:ME2","CH_GGVC__105:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MME-06","text":"Defect is reclassified from an indication of the absence of providence to a permitted event, whose non-prevention must be distinguished from the positive effect of guardianship.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SD4","CH_GGVC__105:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MME-07","text":"The singular person becomes the direct object of providence not by reason of species alone, but because of the permanence of rational form and the hidden conditions of its proper salvation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:ME2","CH_GGVC__105:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of surface are treated illumination of the intellect, inclination of the will, alteration of sense, angelic mission, and the guardianship of the singular human being.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:ALT1","CH_GGVC__105:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, these objects are united through the distinction of divine and angelic causality, the application of power, hierarchical distribution, and individuation of providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:ALT2","CH_GGVC__104:ALT2","CH_GGVC__105:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the interpretive contest is governed through distinctions: immediate is separated from mediated, interior from exterior, presence from operation, and defect from abandonment.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:ALT3","CH_GGVC__104:ALT3","CH_GGVC__105:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is an order of participating causes, while time measures the succession of application, the duration of the way, and the change of the guardian relation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:ALT4","CH_GGVC__104:ALT4","CH_GGVC__105:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the disputative form produces the order itself: it distinguishes objections one by one, distributes them causally, and returns them by recourse to the unity of providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:AS3","CH_GGVC__105:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FL-01","text":"The bodily causality of imagination and sense is combined with the dependence of vital acts on interior principles, but the proportion between organic disposition and determinate act is not fully explained.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:MRT5","CH_GGVC__103:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FL-02","text":"An intellectual nature does not of itself require place, yet mission determines a place of action; the distinction between substance and application resolves the tension formally, not exhaustively the mechanism of presence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:AS1","CH_GGVC__104:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FL-03","text":"The universality of guardianship is combined with the extreme inequality of effects, but the internal criterion by which permissive defect is discerned from inefficacy remains unavailable.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:SD3","CH_GGVC__105:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FL-04","text":"The universal will of salvation and the particular will of justice are preserved together; the absence of pain depends on subordinating universal affection to the circumstances of divine judgment.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A17","CH_GGVC__105:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:FL-05","text":"The distribution of missions from the names of orders has structural force, but its stability depends on the hypothesis that a name faithfully manifests a functional property; even the number of proportions varies interpretively.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:MRT5","CH_GGVC__104:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MP-01","text":"Illumination changes from the accommodation of truth to hierarchical transmission, then to guardian direction; the identity of the term is preserved, while object and function are amplified.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:AS3","CH_GGVC__104:AS4","CH_GGVC__105:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MP-02","text":"Mission changes from the event of a new application into the executive condition of the guardian office; guardianship can contain mission, but is not identified with it.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A13","CH_GGVC__105:A4","CH_GGVC__105:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MP-03","text":"The singular permanence of the person passes into the assignment of a singular guardian, but this transition provides rational probability rather than demonstrative necessity.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:MRT3","CH_GGVC__105:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MP-04","text":"Local absence changes into particular permission: what can initially seem to be a defect of presence becomes a mode of non-prevention within enduring providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:NX8","CH_GGVC__105:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a nested network of governance, in which vision, command, application, and guardianship translate the universal cause into particular effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:SD1","CH_GGVC__105:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:HSI-02","text":"The human subject is an embodied and free node: it receives forms, passions, and instructions, but converts no created influence into necessary consent.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:A3","CH_GGVC__105:A14","CH_GGVC__105:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:HSI-03","text":"Time is a series of applications within the arc of an office: power is newly present, guardianship endures through the state of the way, and the terminus transfers the relation into another form.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:A2","CH_GGVC__105:SD6","CH_GGVC__105:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is not flight from providence, but free movement within a field of illumination, peril, restraint, and permission from which no creature is wholly withdrawn.","refs":["CH_GGVC__105:A10","CH_GGVC__105:D8","CH_GGVC__105:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B01","text":"Axioms concerning distributed providence, ministerial causality, proportionate reception, resistible freedom, application of power, and temporal guardianship suffice to reconstruct the principal mechanism of this group.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:MRT1","CH_GGVC__104:MRT1","CH_GGVC__105:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the distinction between principal divine causality and angelic execution, because the same distinction joins illumination, mission, and guardianship without rivalry.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:MRT2","CH_GGVC__105:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remain the nexus between bodily disposition and concrete act, and the criterion by which a justly permitted defect is discerned from the appearance of deficient guardianship.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:MRT4","CH_GGVC__105:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B04","text":"Coherence at difficult points rests on semantic and causal distinctions; it does not eliminate tension, but distributes it among diverse respects.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:MRT4","CH_GGVC__105:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement if particular effects are not read separately, but as stages from the limitation of angelic power to mission, individuation, temporality, and permission.","refs":["CH_GGVC__103:MRT6","CH_GGVC__105:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B06","text":"Full axiomatic reduction is resisted by the distribution of orders from names and the terminus of birth, because each conclusion depends on probable proofs or variable interpretations; nevertheless, these uncertainties do not dissolve the central mechanism.","refs":["CH_GGVC__104:MRT5","CH_GGVC__105:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_38@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_38:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 112–114","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 111 · Prima pars · Quaestio 112 · Prima pars · Quaestio 113"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 115–117","order":39,"parents":["CH_GGVC__106","CH_GGVC__107","CH_GGVC__108"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_39@R001","sha256":"251808894155bbcb520a62b404c281b709b14241b6a3b45e02ba47a4ba37009a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo impugnatio daemonica, actio corporalis, causalitas caelestis et fatum intra unam gubernationem divinam ponantur, quin agentia creata, contingentia aut responsabilitas humana dissolvantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS1","CH_GGVC__107:AS1","CH_GGVC__108:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-02","text":"Thesis centralis causalitatem stratificatam et non competitivam statuit: causa superior inferiores ordinat et per eas operatur, neque tamen proprios earum modos agendi absorbet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SD1","CH_GGVC__108:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit per distinctiones graduum, respectuum et modorum causalium, quibus eadem res aliter ad causam proximam, superiorem, naturalem, voluntariam aut divinam refertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:MRT3","CH_GGVC__108:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-04","text":"Motus a limitanda causalitate daemonica transit ad distributionem generalem causalitatis corporalis et caelestis, deinde in definitionem fati ut ordinis causarum secundarum terminatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS4","CH_GGVC__107:AS1","CH_GGVC__108:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-05","text":"Progressus est cumulativus et recursivus: potentiae creatae agunt, recipiuntur, impediuntur et redeunt, dum temporalis mobilitas earum sub ordine superiore immobili continetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS5","CH_GGVC__107:ALT4","CH_GGVC__108:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-06","text":"Terminus anthropologicus totius motus est voluntas inclinabilis sed non necessitabilis, cuius libertas neque absentiam affectionum neque sufficientiam sine auxilio divino significat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A8","CH_GGVC__107:A14","CH_GGVC__106:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-07","text":"Casus et contingentia non constituunt regionem sine ordine, sed discrepantiam inter causas proximas defectibiles et apprehensionem ordinantem causae superioris intellectivae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A18","CH_GGVC__108:A1","CH_GGVC__108:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-08","text":"Clausura systematis est conditionalis, non necessitaria: providentia immobilis causas mobiles ordinat, effectus immediatos sibi reservat et contingentiam creatam intra ordinem mediatum servat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A11","CH_GGVC__108:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Causalitas realis est stratificata et non competitiva; superior causa operationem inferioris constituit vel ordinat potius quam evacuat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SD1","CH_GGVC__107:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Providentia divina potest actiones secundarias, etiam malitiosas aut contingentes, ad finem superiorem ordinare sine conversione intentionis propriae agentis in intentionem divinam.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A2","CH_GGVC__108:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Modus agendi cuiusque causae sequitur eius gradum ontologicum: corpus agit secundum actum et potentiam, intellectus intentionaliter, Deus supra ordinem causalitatis creatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A1","CH_GGVC__107:A2","CH_GGVC__106:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Causae superiores creatae effectus per media particularia producunt; universalitas earum relativa, finita et dispositionibus recipientium contracta manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A17","CH_GGVC__107:SD2","CH_GGVC__107:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Influxus unus effectus diversos generat secundum materiam, dispositionem organicam et concursum causarum, unde receptio pars constitutiva causalitatis fit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A11","CH_GGVC__107:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-06","text":"[Terminal] Causalitas corporalis corpora et potentias organis alligatas attingit, sed intellectum et voluntatem neque directe neque necessario determinat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A12","CH_GGVC__107:A13","CH_GGVC__107:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Daemon, quia substantia intellectualis incorporea est, influxui caelesti non subicitur, quamvis dispositiones naturales, corpora et signa tamquam media intentionaliter adhibeat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A15","CH_GGVC__107:A16","CH_GGVC__107:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Voluntas humana inclinari, perturbari et persuaderi potest, sed consensum retinet per potestatem inclinationes sequendi vel refutandi.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D3","CH_GGVC__107:A14","CH_GGVC__107:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Causalitas indirecta originem universalem inclinationis explicare potest, sed imputatio actus singularis ad consensum liberi arbitrii revertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A9","CH_GGVC__106:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Eventus per accidens non habet causam naturalem ut concursus unus, sed potest ab intellectu superiore sub una ratione apprehendi et ordinari.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A18","CH_GGVC__108:A3","CH_GGVC__108:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Fatum non est vis autonoma, sed dispositio causarum secundarum a Deo dependentium ad effectus providentialiter praeordinatos.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A8","CH_GGVC__108:D4","CH_GGVC__108:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Necessitas quae ex providentia sequitur est conditionalis secundum dependentiam ordinis, non absoluta secundum modum contingentem aut liberum causarum proximarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A11","CH_GGVC__108:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-13","text":"[Persistent] Miraculum proprie dictum totum ordinem activum naturae creatae excedit; effectus extraordinarius naturalis vel phantasticus hunc terminum non attingit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A11","CH_GGVC__106:A12","CH_GGVC__107:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-14","text":"[Mutating] Homo ad culpam per inclinationes creatas et proprium consensum deflectere potest, sed meritum et resistentia perfecta auxilium divinum requirunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A10","CH_GGVC__106:A4","CH_GGVC__106:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-15","text":"[Attractor] Eadem res multiplicibus relationibus causalibus subicitur: contingens vel mala secundum causam proximam manet ordinata secundum causam superiorem, sine confusione respectuum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:MRT3","CH_GGVC__108:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-01","text":"Ordo initio est actus divinus malitiam daemonis extrinsecus referens ad bonum; deinde fit distributio graduum naturalium; tandem nomen fati accipit ut relatio causarum secundarum ad effectus.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS2","CH_GGVC__108:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-02","text":"Inclinatio identitatem functionalem retinet, sed campum mutat: daemon inferiores vires moraliter sollicitat, influxus caelestis organa et passiones naturaliter afficit, voluntas utrumque iudicat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D3","CH_GGVC__107:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-03","text":"Daemon ab hoste exteriora explorante in agentem intellectualem transfertur qui media naturalia eligit, quamvis ab eis nec cognitionem interiorem nec determinationem patiatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A7","CH_GGVC__107:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-04","text":"Miraculum a discrimine inter apparentiam et effectum realem ad terminum metaphysicum totius causalitatis creatae amplificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:SD3","CH_GGVC__107:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-05","text":"Causalitas indirecta a relatione primi peccati ad actus singulares migrat in relationem corporis ad intellectum, semper effectum mediatum sine determinatione immediata significans.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A9","CH_GGVC__107:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-06","text":"Casus mutatur ex concursu qui ut talis causam naturalem non habet in eventum quem intellectus superior intentionaliter unit; negatio causae unius naturalis non evanescit, sed ordine altiore absorbetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A18","CH_GGVC__108:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-07","text":"Fatum primum ut dispositio siderum negatur, deinde sub inversione terminologica restituitur ut structura relationalis causationis creatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:NX1","CH_GGVC__108:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-08","text":"Necessitas ab effectu qui ex causa creata non semper sequitur ad necessitatem conditionalem dependentiae providentialis transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:NX14","CH_GGVC__108:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-09","text":"Rationes seminales postquam agentiam naturae contra inertiam materiae stabiliverunt, in notionem latiorem virtutum naturalium et causarum secundarum absorbentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A5","CH_GGVC__107:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-01","text":"Invidia daemonis, imitatio ministerii divini et recessus eius post victoriam manent mechanismi locales impugnationis, non principia totius ordinis causalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A1","CH_GGVC__106:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-02","text":"Actus et potentia atque rationes seminales sunt fundamenta localia agentiae corporalis; hoc gradu valent quatenus causalitatem creatam realem sed contractam constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:AS4","CH_GGVC__107:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-03","text":"Praedictio astrologica ut in pluribus et consuetudo hominum passiones sequentium manent consequentiae locales influxus organici, non definitio globalis libertatis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:NX12","CH_GGVC__107:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-04","text":"Definitio fati amplificatur supra quaestionem terminologicam localem, quia nomen eius structuram universalem mediationis causarum secundarum claudit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:AS4","CH_GGVC__108:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-05","text":"Distinctiones directi et indirecti, principalis et instrumentalis, per se et per accidens unitates coniungunt atque fiunt grammatica causalis praesentis gradus.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS4","CH_GGVC__107:A4","CH_GGVC__107:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-06","text":"Doctrina effectuum daemonum et doctrina rationum seminalium coniunguntur in limite miraculi: natura potest extraordinarium efficere, sed non proprium ordinem universum transcendere.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D5","CH_GGVC__107:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-07","text":"Distinctio inter usum intentionalem instrumenti et passionem ab instrumento transitionem constituit qua daemon simul intra campum naturalem operatur et extra subiectionem astralem manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX8","CH_GGVC__107:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-08","text":"Regula mediationis non est absoluta: effectus immediate divini providentiae subsunt, sed ex ordine fatali et serie causarum secundarum eximuntur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A12","CH_GGVC__108:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GNX-01","text":"Systema excludit dualismum quo Deus et daemon principia causaliter commensurabilia aut independentia fierent.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:SNX1","CH_GGVC__106:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GNX-02","text":"Excluditur clausura astralis quae corpora, voluntates, intellectus et substantias spirituales sub una necessitate cosmica contineret.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SNX1","CH_GGVC__107:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GNX-03","text":"Nulla causa creata voluntatem immediate cogit: daemon eam tantum inclinat, corpus caeleste autem actus voluntarios directe non causat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:NX4","CH_GGVC__107:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GNX-04","text":"Negatur planum causale homogeneum in quo corpus, anima organica, intellectus, daemon et Deus eodem modo agant aut patiantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SNX2","CH_GGVC__107:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GNX-05","text":"Admiratio sensibilis neque miraculum neque veritatem fontis operationis per se demonstrat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:NX6","CH_GGVC__106:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GNX-06","text":"Positio causae creatae non sufficit ad necessitatem effectus, quia receptio, impedimentum, libertas et concursus contingens cursum causalitatis mutant.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:NX14","CH_GGVC__107:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GNX-07","text":"Fatum neque ipsa providentia neque substantia creata est, sed ordo relationis in causis secundis receptus.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:NX4","CH_GGVC__108:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet dependentiam causalem necessitatem effectus per se importare; aliter causalitas creata contingentiam, impedibilitatem et libertatem tolleret.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX3","CH_GGVC__107:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet causam superiorem inferioris agentiam evacuare; aliter instrumenta naturalia, consensus voluntarius et executio fatalis merae apparentiae fierent.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX2","CH_GGVC__108:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet correlationem temporalem aut ritualem subiectionem causalem probare, quia eadem concordantia ex dispositione materiae vel electione intellectuali oriri potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX4","CH_GGVC__107:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet libertatem consistere in absentia passionum; libertas potius est potestas iudicandi et sequendi vel refutandi inclinationes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX7","CH_GGVC__107:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet naturam creatam totalitatem eventuum explicare posse, quoniam concursus accidentalis causalitatem naturalem per se excedit et ordo causarum ipse a Deo dependet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:DNX2","CH_GGVC__108:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem aggregationem factorum unum ens constituere cui unica causa naturalis assignetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX5","CH_GGVC__107:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:DBN-07","text":"Falsum esse oportet omne agere divinum in seriem causarum secundarum resolvi, quia immediata actio divina limitem externum mediationis constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:DNX5","CH_GGVC__108:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSA-01","text":"Inadmissibilis est causa creata quae iurisdictionem universalem, immediatam et infallibilem super omnes ordines rerum possideat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SNX5","CH_GGVC__107:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSA-02","text":"Inadmissibilis est regio contingentiae omnino providentiae subtracta; contingentia manet modus relationis ad causas proximas, non exterioritas absoluta ab ordine divino.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:SNX2","CH_GGVC__108:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSA-03","text":"Inadmissibilis est collapsus differentiae inter causalitatem divinam et creatam, quia causae secundae non eodem modo quo Deus ordinant aut efficiunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:DNX3","CH_GGVC__106:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSA-04","text":"Inadmissibilis est transparentia eventus qua ex sola spectacularitate, temporalitate aut fortuna eius causa ultima statim legeretur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:SNX3","CH_GGVC__107:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSA-05","text":"Inadmissibilis est necessitas impersonalis ut supremum principium realitatis; fatum est derivatum, contingentia servatur et providentia etiam per causas mobiles operatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:SNX1","CH_GGVC__108:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-01","text":"Impugnatio daemonica et influxus caelestis eandem structuram mediationis participant: vires inferiores vel organicas afficiunt atque voluntatem inclinant, sed consensum necessario non efficiunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-06","CL_GGVC_39:GA-08","CH_GGVC__106:A8","CH_GGVC__107:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-02","text":"Daemon effectus naturales et dispositiones corporeas instrumentaliter adhibere potest quia extra passionem caelestem manet, non quia ordinem naturae excedit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-07","CL_GGVC_39:GA-13","CH_GGVC__106:A12","CH_GGVC__107:A15","CH_GGVC__107:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-03","text":"Effectus daemonice productus potest naturaliter verus et humanis viribus extraordinarius esse, quin miraculum proprie divinum fiat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-07","CL_GGVC_39:GA-13","CH_GGVC__106:D5","CH_GGVC__107:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-04","text":"Fatum astrale impossibile est, quia corpora caelestia neque actus voluntarios directe causant neque concursum accidentalem ut unum effectum naturalem producunt; fatum igitur ad ordinem providentialem causarum secundarum transfertur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-06","CL_GGVC_39:GA-10","CL_GGVC_39:GA-11","CH_GGVC__108:AS3","CH_GGVC__107:A18","CH_GGVC__108:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-05","text":"Concursus fortuitus simul caret causa naturali qua concursus et unitatem intentionalem sub intellectu superiore recipit; oppositio solvitur tantum si gradus causalitatis distinguuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-10","CL_GGVC_39:GA-15","CH_GGVC__107:A18","CH_GGVC__108:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-06","text":"Eadem actio daemonica est moraliter mala ex intentione agentis, naturaliter vel voluntarie mediata in executione, et providentialiter ordinabilis secundum finem superiorem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-02","CL_GGVC_39:GA-07","CL_GGVC_39:GA-15","CH_GGVC__106:D1","CH_GGVC__108:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-07","text":"Libertas humana neque causaliter impermeabilis neque sibi sufficiens est: inclinationes recipit et refutare potest, sed progressus meritorius auxilio divino indiget.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-08","CL_GGVC_39:GA-14","CH_GGVC__106:A10","CH_GGVC__107:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-08","text":"Discernimentum causalitatis ab effectu visibili solo ad examen ordinis naturae, dispositionis recipientis, finis agentis et iuris operationis transfertur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-05","CL_GGVC_39:GA-13","CH_GGVC__106:D6","CH_GGVC__107:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-09","text":"Miraculum et effectus immediate divinus constituunt eundem limitem mediationis: providentiae subsunt, sed neque virtutibus naturalibus activis neque nexibus fati continentur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-11","CL_GGVC_39:GA-13","CH_GGVC__106:A11","CH_GGVC__108:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-10","text":"Contingentia est modus causalitatis particularis defectibilis et scalae relativus, non absentia causae superioris aut dissolutio ordinis universalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-05","CL_GGVC_39:GA-10","CL_GGVC_39:GA-15","CH_GGVC__107:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSD-01","text":"Unus actus plures veritates causales non competitivas sustinere potest: agenti proximo attribuitur secundum proprium modum, causae superiori secundum ordinationem, recipienti secundum dispositionem.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D1","CH_GGVC__108:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum humanum fit organicis influxibus permeabile, epistemice perturbabile et moraliter sollicitabile, sed voluntarie non clausum; libertas eius est gubernatio receptionis, non separatio a causalitate.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:D7","CH_GGVC__107:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSD-03","text":"Tempus systematis est multiplex: tentatio recurrit, causae naturales per praesentiam et absentiam variantur, fatum secundum causas movetur, providentia vero ordinem immobilem servat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS5","CH_GGVC__107:ALT4","CH_GGVC__108:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSD-04","text":"Eventus sensibilis causam suam non exhibet univoce, quia idem phaenomenon ex natura, phantasia, voluntate intellectuali vel operatione divina diversis modis oriri potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:SD3","CH_GGVC__107:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSD-05","text":"Transcendentia divina non solum per excessum supra naturam, sed etiam per libertatem ordinandi causas secundas sine earum destructione et agendi sine mediatione manifestatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A11","CH_GGVC__108:A7","CH_GGVC__108:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSD-06","text":"Responsabilitas moralis non decrescit propter multiplicationem causarum, quia inclinatio, permissio, ordinatio et consensus ad diversos respectus pertinent.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D4","CH_GGVC__108:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSD-07","text":"Contingentia naturalis, libertas humana et ordinatio providentialis non sunt exceptiones mutuo incompatibiles, sed effectus unius architecturae in qua modi inferiores sub ordine superiore conservantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:SD1","CH_GGVC__108:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RLE-01","text":"Exploratio exteriorum → electio inclinationis opportunioris → motus virium inferiorum → consensus vel resistentia → recessus temporalis → possibilitas novae impugnationis constituit circuitum temptationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D2","CH_GGVC__106:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RLE-02","text":"Virtus superior creata → causa particularis instrumentalis → dispositio materiae → effectus variabilis → impedimentum vel concursus per accidens → contingentia constituit circuitum naturae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:D6","CH_GGVC__107:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RLE-03","text":"Providentia → praeordinatio effectus → executio per causas secundas → mobilitas causarum → dependentia conditionaliter immobilis constituit circuitum fati.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A7","CH_GGVC__108:A10","CH_GGVC__108:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RLE-04","text":"Affectio corporis → passio vel imaginatio → iudicium intellectus indirecte affectum → voluntas sequens aut refutans → actio exterior constituit circuitum libertatis situatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:D7","CH_GGVC__107:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RLE-05","text":"Effectus admirabilis → suspensio attributionis immediatae → examen naturae, recipientis, finis et auctoritatis → reclassificatio signi constituit circuitum discernimenti.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:NX6","CH_GGVC__106:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RLE-06","text":"Attractor principalis est mediatio ordinata sine necessitatione: superior causa ad inferiores redit, earum agentiam conservat, effectum recipi secundum dispositionem sinit et ordinem ex discrepantiis restituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SD1","CH_GGVC__107:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RLE-07","text":"Nomen fati per circuitum criticum movetur: determinismus astralis → negatio → separatio providentiae et causarum secundarum → recuperatio relationalis termini.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:NX1","CH_GGVC__108:ME4","CH_GGVC__108:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B01","text":"Differentia ontologica agentis, patientis et potentiae recipiendae ponitur ante omnem attributionem causalem, ne omnia plana actionis homogenizentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SNX2","CH_GGVC__107:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B02","text":"Gradus ontologicus → modus proprius actionis → terminus potestatis definit quid corpus, daemon, voluntas, causa caelestis aut Deus efficere possit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:MRT3","CH_GGVC__107:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B03","text":"Causa superior → media secundaria → actio particularis constituit formam ordinariam executionis, qua universalitas sine monopolio causali operatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A7","CH_GGVC__107:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B04","text":"Influxus receptus → contractio secundum dispositionem → effectus diversificatus vel impeditus constituit principium contingentiae intra causalitatem realem.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:D6","CH_GGVC__107:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B05","text":"Examen termini distinguit inclinationem a necessitatione, effectum naturalem a miraculo, usum instrumenti a passione et causalitatem relativam a universalitate simpliciter.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:ME4","CH_GGVC__107:MRT7","CH_GGVC__107:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B06","text":"Eventus ad causam proximam et superiorem dupliciter refertur; ex hac duplici relatione contingentia localis, ordinatio universalis et responsabilitas propria simul generantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:MRT3","CH_GGVC__108:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B07","text":"Ordo productus secundum media creata fatum dicitur, secundum intellectum divinum providentia, secundum effectum proximum natura, fortuna vel actus voluntarius.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:D4","CH_GGVC__108:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:AFM-B01","text":"Impugnatio daemonica apparenter disproportionata → divisio intentionis malae et ordinationis divinae → limitatio potestatis super voluntatem et naturam → generalizatio graduum causalium → causalitas caelestis impedibilis → contingentia causarum secundarum → fatum providentialiter derivatum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS4","CH_GGVC__107:AS3","CH_GGVC__108:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:AFM-B02","text":"Effectus extraordinarius aut fortuitus → recusatio attributionis simplicis → distinctio causae proximae et superioris → examen mediationis et recipientis → conservatio libertatis vel contingentiae → clausura conditionalis sub providentia.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:SD3","CH_GGVC__108:MRT3","CH_GGVC__108:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MME-01","text":"Ager causalitatis ex plano homogeno in ordinem graduatum reconfiguratur, ubi corpus, potentia organica, voluntas, intellectus creatus et Deus modos causalitatis distinctos exercent.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SNX2","CH_GGVC__107:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MME-02","text":"Libertas ex immunitate contra influxum in facultatem recipiendi inclinationes sine determinatione necessaria reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX7","CH_GGVC__107:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MME-03","text":"Fatum ex necessitate siderali in ordinem relationalem et mediatum causarum creatarum transfertur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:NX1","CH_GGVC__108:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MME-04","text":"Casus ex defectu simplici ordinis in discrepantiam inter concursus naturaliter non unitum et unitatem intentionalem intellectus superioris mutatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A18","CH_GGVC__108:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MME-05","text":"Daemon ex causa universali peccati vel effectu astrali in agentem intellectualem limitatum reclassificatur, qui suggerit, eligit media et naturam instrumentaliter adhibet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A9","CH_GGVC__107:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MME-06","text":"Miraculum ex synonymo admirationis in terminum ontologicum inter causalitatem creatam et operationem divinam convertitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:NX6","CH_GGVC__106:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MME-07","text":"Lectio eventuum fit differentialiter causalis: aspectus, regularitas et correlatio cedunt examini finis, medii, recipientis, gradus ontologici et respectus ad causam.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D6","CH_GGVC__107:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei apparent tentationes daemonum, mutationes corporum, influxus caelestes, signa extraordinaria, eventus fortuiti et quaestio fati.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:ALT1","CH_GGVC__107:ALT1","CH_GGVC__108:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali eadem obiecta distribuuntur per mediationem, instrumentalitatem, dispositionem recipientis, causalitatem directam vel indirectam et differentiam per se atque per accidens.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:ALT2","CH_GGVC__106:AS4","CH_GGVC__107:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico quaestio ab attributione unius causae ad artem discernendi gradus, limites et respectus transit, ita ut opposita apparentia per stratificationem potius quam eliminationem tractentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:ALT3","CH_GGVC__108:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est ordo causarum finitarum, mobilium et impedibilium sub providentia immobili; tempus creatum per successionem, reditum, variationem et concursum generatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:ALT4","CH_GGVC__108:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo textus ipsam causalitatem stratificatam efficit, quia singulas apparentias per distinctiones iteratas contrahit, limitat et sub respectu superiore restituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:AS3","CH_GGVC__108:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FL-01","text":"Concursus per accidens ut talis causam non habet, sed eventus fortuitus sub intellectu superiore intentionaliter ordinatur; cohaerentia pendet ex distinctione causae concursus et causae factorum concurrentium.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A18","CH_GGVC__107:MRT6","CH_GGVC__108:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FL-02","text":"Voluntas a daemone et caelo non immutatur necessario, sed soli Deo eius immutatio attribuitur; modus quo causalitas divina libertatem non destruat hic assumitur potius quam explicatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A8","CH_GGVC__108:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FL-03","text":"Malitia daemonis intra providentiam ordinatur sine mandato salutari ad malum; distinctio intentionum servat differentiam, sed commissura inter permissionem, ordinationem et imputabilitatem subtilis manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D1","CH_GGVC__106:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FL-04","text":"Miraculum ponitur praeter virtutes activas naturales, non tamen praeter omnem potentiam passivam creaturae; relatio inter receptivitatem universalem et potentias agentibus ordinatas breviter tantum determinatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A7","CH_GGVC__107:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FL-05","text":"Universalitas relative maior causalitatis caelestis componenda est cum particularitate recipientium, impedibilitate causarum et irreducibilitate concursus accidentalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:MRT5","CH_GGVC__107:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MP-01","text":"Tentatio mutatur ab experimento simpliciter dicto in structuram cuius species ex fine agentis determinatur; nomen idem manet, functio moralis variatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A5","CH_GGVC__106:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MP-02","text":"Fatum patitur inversionem terminalem: quod ut necessitas stellarum repellitur, ut ordo derivatus causarum secundarum recipitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:NX8","CH_GGVC__108:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MP-03","text":"Contingentia mutatur ab impedibilitate causarum particularium in relationem scalae, qua idem eventus infra casualis et supra intentus est.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SD5","CH_GGVC__108:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MP-04","text":"Daemon mutatur ab impugnatore moraliter descripto in causam intellectualem quae media naturalia eligit sine passione ab eis; haec translatio coniungit ethicam temptationis cum cosmologia causali.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A7","CH_GGVC__107:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est reticulum causarum nidificatarum, in quo vis descendens per media contrahitur et ordo ascendens eventus dispersos ad principium unum refert.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A9","CH_GGVC__108:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum humanum est membrana selectiva inter corpus, passiones, intellectum et voluntatem: influxus admittit, sed in consensum necessario non traducit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A12","CH_GGVC__107:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est mobilitas causarum creatarum sub relatione providentiali immobili, cum reditibus temptationis, successionibus naturalibus et effectibus contingentibus.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS5","CH_GGVC__108:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga ab omni influxu, sed discernimentum causae, moderatio inclinationum, resistentia auxiliata et acceptio contingentiae sine attributione fatalistica.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D6","CH_GGVC__106:ME4","CH_GGVC__108:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de causalitate stratificata, mediatione secundaria, receptione impedibili, voluntate non necessitata, providentia et limite miraculi sufficiunt ad structuram praesentis gregis restituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SD1","CH_GGVC__108:A7","CH_GGVC__106:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B02","text":"Nondum satis determinatur quomodo Deus voluntatem immutet sine abolitione libertatis, neque fundamenta immaterialitatis intellectus et naturae daemonum hoc gradu demonstrantur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A5","CH_GGVC__107:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B03","text":"Systema cohaeret maxime ubi distinctiones causae superioris et inferioris, inclinationis et determinationis, per se et per accidens atque providentiae et fati mutuo se confirmant.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX2","CH_GGVC__107:ME4","CH_GGVC__108:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B04","text":"Firmissimus nucleus est duplex relatio eiusdem effectus, quae agentiam localem et ordinationem universalem sine homogenizatione componit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:MRT3","CH_GGVC__107:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio nititur recursiva applicatione paucarum distinctionum potius quam ordine simpliciter lineari; repetitio directi, indirecti, medii et respectus mutationem campi causalitatis efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS4","CH_GGVC__107:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B06","text":"Motus verus servatur si impugnatio limitata in causalitatem creatam generalem dilatatur et haec in ordinem providentialem causarum secundarum concluditur; singulae rationes cosmologicae huic reductioni adhuc resistunt, sed machina generatrix integra manet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS4","CH_GGVC__107:AS1","CH_GGVC__108:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T173323435757Z_90abc22e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 115–117","source_sha256":"251808894155bbcb520a62b404c281b709b14241b6a3b45e02ba47a4ba37009a","sha256":"2d64550365e25ea2af5180196acc2d0db4ac78f9543f92de776bab884ac285cb","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:33:23.436726+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how demonic assault, bodily action, celestial causality, and fate may be situated within a single divine governance without dissolving created agency, contingency, or human responsibility.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS1","CH_GGVC__107:AS1","CH_GGVC__108:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-02","text":"The central thesis establishes stratified and non-competitive causality: a higher cause orders and operates through lower causes, yet does not absorb their proper modes of acting.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SD1","CH_GGVC__108:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-03","text":"The argument proceeds through distinctions of levels, respects, and causal modes, by which the same thing is related differently to a proximate, higher, natural, voluntary, or divine cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:MRT3","CH_GGVC__108:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-04","text":"The movement passes from limiting demonic causality to a general distribution of bodily and celestial causality, and finally concludes in the definition of fate as the order of secondary causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS4","CH_GGVC__107:AS1","CH_GGVC__108:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-05","text":"The progression is cumulative and recursive: created powers act, are received, impeded, and return, while their temporal mobility is contained under a higher immobile order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS5","CH_GGVC__107:ALT4","CH_GGVC__108:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-06","text":"The anthropological terminus of the entire movement is a will capable of inclination but not necessitation, whose freedom signifies neither the absence of affections nor sufficiency without divine aid.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A8","CH_GGVC__107:A14","CH_GGVC__106:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-07","text":"Chance and contingency do not constitute a region without order, but a discrepancy between defective proximate causes and the ordering apprehension of a higher intellectual cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A18","CH_GGVC__108:A1","CH_GGVC__108:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BAS-08","text":"The closure of the system is conditional, not necessitarian: immobile providence orders mobile causes, reserves immediate effects to itself, and preserves created contingency within a mediated order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A11","CH_GGVC__108:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Real causality is stratified and non-competitive; a higher cause constitutes or orders the operation of a lower rather than evacuating it.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SD1","CH_GGVC__107:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Divine providence can order secondary actions, even malicious or contingent ones, to a higher end without converting the agent's proper intention into a divine intention.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A2","CH_GGVC__108:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] The mode of acting of each cause follows its ontological level: body acts according to act and potency, intellect intentionally, God above the order of created causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A1","CH_GGVC__107:A2","CH_GGVC__106:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Higher created causes produce effects through particular means; their universality remains relative, finite, and contracted by the dispositions of recipients.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A17","CH_GGVC__107:SD2","CH_GGVC__107:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] One influx generates diverse effects according to matter, organic disposition, and the concurrence of causes, whence reception becomes a constitutive part of causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A11","CH_GGVC__107:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-06","text":"[Terminal] Bodily causality reaches bodies and powers bound to organs, but determines neither intellect nor will, either directly or necessarily.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A12","CH_GGVC__107:A13","CH_GGVC__107:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The demon, because it is an incorporeal intellectual substance, is not subject to celestial influx, although it intentionally employs natural dispositions, bodies, and signs as means.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A15","CH_GGVC__107:A16","CH_GGVC__107:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] The human will can be inclined, disturbed, and persuaded, but retains consent through the power to follow or reject inclinations.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D3","CH_GGVC__107:A14","CH_GGVC__107:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Indirect causality can explain the universal origin of an inclination, but the imputation of a singular act returns to the consent of free choice.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A9","CH_GGVC__106:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] An event per accidens has no natural cause as a single concurrence, but can be apprehended and ordered under one account by a higher intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A18","CH_GGVC__108:A3","CH_GGVC__108:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Fate is not an autonomous force, but the disposition of secondary causes dependent on God toward providentially preordained effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A8","CH_GGVC__108:D4","CH_GGVC__108:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] The necessity following from providence is conditional according to dependence of order, not absolute according to the contingent or free mode of proximate causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A11","CH_GGVC__108:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-13","text":"[Persistent] A miracle properly so called exceeds the whole active order of created nature; an extraordinary natural or phantasmal effect does not reach this limit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A11","CH_GGVC__106:A12","CH_GGVC__107:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-14","text":"[Mutating] A human can turn toward fault through created inclinations and personal consent, but merit and perfect resistance require divine aid.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A10","CH_GGVC__106:A4","CH_GGVC__106:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GA-15","text":"[Attractor] The same thing is subject to multiple causal relations: contingent or evil according to a proximate cause, it remains ordered according to a higher cause, without confusion of respects.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:MRT3","CH_GGVC__108:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-01","text":"Order is initially the divine act externally relating the demon's malice to good; it then becomes a distribution of natural levels; finally it receives the name of fate as the relation of secondary causes to effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS2","CH_GGVC__108:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-02","text":"Inclination retains its functional identity but changes field: the demon morally solicits lower powers, celestial influx naturally affects organs and passions, and the will judges both.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D3","CH_GGVC__107:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-03","text":"The demon is transferred from an enemy investigating externals to an intellectual agent that chooses natural means, although it suffers from them neither inner cognition nor determination.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A7","CH_GGVC__107:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-04","text":"Miracle is amplified from the distinction between appearance and real effect to the metaphysical limit of all created causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:SD3","CH_GGVC__107:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-05","text":"Indirect causality migrates from the relation of original sin to singular acts into the relation of body to intellect, always signifying a mediated effect without immediate determination.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A9","CH_GGVC__107:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-06","text":"Chance changes from a concurrence which as such has no natural cause into an event intentionally unified by a higher intellect; the negation of one natural cause does not vanish, but is absorbed by a higher order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A18","CH_GGVC__108:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-07","text":"Fate is first denied as a disposition of stars, then restored under a terminological inversion as the relational structure of created causation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:NX1","CH_GGVC__108:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-08","text":"Necessity is transferred from an effect that does not always follow from a created cause to the conditional necessity of providential dependence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:NX14","CH_GGVC__108:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RMD-09","text":"Seminal reasons, after establishing nature's agency against the inertia of matter, are absorbed into the broader notion of natural powers and secondary causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A5","CH_GGVC__107:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-01","text":"The demon's envy, imitation of divine ministry, and withdrawal after victory remain local mechanisms of assault, not principles of the entire causal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A1","CH_GGVC__106:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-02","text":"Act and potency, together with seminal reasons, are local foundations of bodily agency; at this level they hold insofar as they constitute real yet contracted created causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:AS4","CH_GGVC__107:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-03","text":"Astrological prediction as occurring in most cases, and the habit of humans following passions, remain local consequences of organic influx, not the global definition of freedom.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:NX12","CH_GGVC__107:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-04","text":"The definition of fate is amplified beyond a local terminological question, because its name concludes the universal structure of mediation by secondary causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:AS4","CH_GGVC__108:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-05","text":"The distinctions of direct and indirect, principal and instrumental, per se and per accidens connect the units and become the causal grammar of the present level.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS4","CH_GGVC__107:A4","CH_GGVC__107:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-06","text":"The doctrine of demonic effects and the doctrine of seminal reasons are joined in the limit of miracle: nature can produce the extraordinary, but cannot transcend its own universal order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D5","CH_GGVC__107:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-07","text":"The distinction between intentional use of an instrument and being affected by an instrument constitutes the transition whereby the demon both operates within the natural field and remains outside astral subjection.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX8","CH_GGVC__107:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:CGD-08","text":"The rule of mediation is not absolute: immediately divine effects are subject to providence, but exempted from the order of fate and the series of secondary causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A12","CH_GGVC__108:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes a dualism by which God and demon would become causally commensurable or independent principles.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:SNX1","CH_GGVC__106:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GNX-02","text":"An astral closure is excluded that would contain bodies, wills, intellects, and spiritual substances under one cosmic necessity.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SNX1","CH_GGVC__107:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GNX-03","text":"No created cause immediately compels the will: the demon only inclines it, while a celestial body does not directly cause voluntary acts.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:NX4","CH_GGVC__107:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GNX-04","text":"A homogeneous causal plane is denied, in which body, organic soul, intellect, demon, and God would act or suffer in the same manner.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SNX2","CH_GGVC__107:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GNX-05","text":"Sensible wonder by itself demonstrates neither miracle nor the truth of the source of an operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:NX6","CH_GGVC__106:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GNX-06","text":"The positing of a created cause does not suffice for the necessity of an effect, because reception, impediment, freedom, and contingent concurrence alter the course of causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:NX14","CH_GGVC__107:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:GNX-07","text":"Fate is neither providence itself nor a created substance, but an order of relation received in secondary causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:NX4","CH_GGVC__108:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that causal dependence imports necessity of effect per se; otherwise created causality would remove contingency, impedibility, and freedom.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX3","CH_GGVC__107:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that a higher cause evacuates the agency of a lower; otherwise natural instruments, voluntary consent, and fatal execution would become mere appearances.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX2","CH_GGVC__108:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that temporal or ritual correlation proves causal subjection, since the same concordance can arise from the disposition of matter or intellectual choice.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX4","CH_GGVC__107:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that freedom consists in the absence of passions; freedom is rather the power to judge and to follow or reject inclinations.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX7","CH_GGVC__107:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that created nature can explain the totality of events, since accidental concurrence exceeds natural causality per se and the order of causes itself depends on God.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:DNX2","CH_GGVC__108:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that every aggregation of factors constitutes one being to which a single natural cause is assigned.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX5","CH_GGVC__107:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:DBN-07","text":"It must be false that every divine action is resolved into a series of secondary causes, because immediate divine action constitutes the external limit of mediation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:DNX5","CH_GGVC__108:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSA-01","text":"A created cause possessing universal, immediate, and infallible jurisdiction over all orders of things is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SNX5","CH_GGVC__107:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSA-02","text":"A region of contingency wholly withdrawn from providence is inadmissible; contingency remains a mode of relation to proximate causes, not absolute exteriority to divine order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:SNX2","CH_GGVC__108:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSA-03","text":"The collapse of the difference between divine and created causality is inadmissible, because secondary causes do not order or effect in the same manner as God.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:DNX3","CH_GGVC__106:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSA-04","text":"The transparency of an event whereby its ultimate cause would immediately be read from its spectacle, temporality, or fortune alone is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:SNX3","CH_GGVC__107:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSA-05","text":"Impersonal necessity as the supreme principle of reality is inadmissible; fate is derivative, contingency is preserved, and providence operates even through mobile causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:SNX1","CH_GGVC__108:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-01","text":"Demonic assault and celestial influx share the same structure of mediation: they affect lower or organic powers and incline the will, but do not necessarily produce consent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-06","CL_GGVC_39:GA-08","CH_GGVC__106:A8","CH_GGVC__107:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-02","text":"The demon can instrumentally employ natural effects and bodily dispositions because it remains outside celestial passion, not because it exceeds the order of nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-07","CL_GGVC_39:GA-13","CH_GGVC__106:A12","CH_GGVC__107:A15","CH_GGVC__107:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-03","text":"An effect produced demonically can be naturally real and extraordinary to human powers without becoming a miracle properly divine.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-07","CL_GGVC_39:GA-13","CH_GGVC__106:D5","CH_GGVC__107:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-04","text":"Astral fate is impossible, because celestial bodies neither directly cause voluntary acts nor produce accidental concurrence as one natural effect; fate is therefore transferred to the providential order of secondary causes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-06","CL_GGVC_39:GA-10","CL_GGVC_39:GA-11","CH_GGVC__108:AS3","CH_GGVC__107:A18","CH_GGVC__108:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-05","text":"A fortuitous concurrence simultaneously lacks a natural cause as concurrence and receives intentional unity under a higher intellect; the opposition is resolved only if causal levels are distinguished.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-10","CL_GGVC_39:GA-15","CH_GGVC__107:A18","CH_GGVC__108:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-06","text":"The same demonic action is morally evil from the agent's intention, naturally or voluntarily mediated in execution, and providentially orderable according to a higher end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-02","CL_GGVC_39:GA-07","CL_GGVC_39:GA-15","CH_GGVC__106:D1","CH_GGVC__108:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-07","text":"Human freedom is neither causally impermeable nor self-sufficient: it receives inclinations and can reject them, but meritorious progress requires divine aid.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-08","CL_GGVC_39:GA-14","CH_GGVC__106:A10","CH_GGVC__107:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-08","text":"Discernment of causality is transferred from visible effect alone to examination of the order of nature, the recipient's disposition, the agent's end, and the legitimacy of the operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-05","CL_GGVC_39:GA-13","CH_GGVC__106:D6","CH_GGVC__107:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-09","text":"Miracle and immediately divine effect constitute the same limit of mediation: they are subject to providence, but contained neither by active natural powers nor by the bonds of fate.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-11","CL_GGVC_39:GA-13","CH_GGVC__106:A11","CH_GGVC__108:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:XD-10","text":"Contingency is a mode of defective particular causality and relative to scale, not the absence of a higher cause or dissolution of universal order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-05","CL_GGVC_39:GA-10","CL_GGVC_39:GA-15","CH_GGVC__107:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSD-01","text":"One act can sustain multiple non-competitive causal truths: it is attributed to the proximate agent according to its proper mode, to the higher cause according to ordering, and to the recipient according to disposition.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D1","CH_GGVC__108:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSD-02","text":"The human subject becomes permeable to organic influxes, epistemically disturbable, and morally solicit-able, yet not voluntarily closed; its freedom is governance of reception, not separation from causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:D7","CH_GGVC__107:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSD-03","text":"The system's time is multiple: temptation recurs, natural causes vary through presence and absence, fate moves according to causes, while providence preserves immobile order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS5","CH_GGVC__107:ALT4","CH_GGVC__108:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSD-04","text":"A sensible event does not display its cause univocally, because the same phenomenon can arise in diverse ways from nature, phantasy, intellectual will, or divine operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:SD3","CH_GGVC__107:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSD-05","text":"Divine transcendence manifests not only by excess above nature, but also by the freedom to order secondary causes without destroying them and to act without mediation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A11","CH_GGVC__108:A7","CH_GGVC__108:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSD-06","text":"Moral responsibility does not diminish through the multiplication of causes, because inclination, permission, ordering, and consent belong to different respects.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D4","CH_GGVC__108:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:BSD-07","text":"Natural contingency, human freedom, and providential ordering are not mutually incompatible exceptions, but effects of one architecture in which lower modes are preserved under a higher order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:SD1","CH_GGVC__108:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RLE-01","text":"Exploration of externals → selection of a more opportune inclination → motion of lower powers → consent or resistance → temporal withdrawal → possibility of a new assault constitutes the circuit of temptation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D2","CH_GGVC__106:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RLE-02","text":"Higher created power → particular instrumental cause → disposition of matter → variable effect → impediment or concurrence per accidens → contingency constitutes the circuit of nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:D6","CH_GGVC__107:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RLE-03","text":"Providence → preordination of effect → execution through secondary causes → mobility of causes → conditionally immobile dependence constitutes the circuit of fate.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A7","CH_GGVC__108:A10","CH_GGVC__108:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RLE-04","text":"Bodily affection → passion or imagination → indirectly affected judgment of intellect → will following or rejecting → external action constitutes the circuit of situated freedom.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:D7","CH_GGVC__107:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RLE-05","text":"Wonderful effect → suspension of immediate attribution → examination of nature, recipient, end, and authority → reclassification of the sign constitutes the circuit of discernment.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:NX6","CH_GGVC__106:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RLE-06","text":"The principal attractor is ordered mediation without necessitation: a higher cause returns to lower causes, preserves their agency, permits the effect to be received according to disposition, and restores order from discrepancies.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SD1","CH_GGVC__107:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:RLE-07","text":"The name of fate moves through a critical circuit: astral determinism → negation → separation of providence and secondary causes → relational recovery of the term.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:NX1","CH_GGVC__108:ME4","CH_GGVC__108:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B01","text":"The ontological difference of agent, patient, and power to be received is posited before every causal attribution, lest all planes of action be homogenized.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SNX2","CH_GGVC__107:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B02","text":"Ontological level → proper mode of action → limit of power defines what body, demon, will, celestial cause, or God can effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:MRT3","CH_GGVC__107:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B03","text":"Higher cause → secondary means → particular action constitutes the ordinary form of execution, by which universality operates without causal monopoly.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A7","CH_GGVC__107:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B04","text":"Received influx → contraction according to disposition → diversified or impeded effect constitutes the principle of contingency within real causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:D6","CH_GGVC__107:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B05","text":"Examination of the limit distinguishes inclination from necessitation, natural effect from miracle, use of an instrument from passion, and relative causality from universality simply speaking.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:ME4","CH_GGVC__107:MRT7","CH_GGVC__107:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B06","text":"An event is related in two ways to a proximate and a higher cause; from this twofold relation local contingency, universal ordering, and proper responsibility are generated together.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:MRT3","CH_GGVC__108:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B07","text":"Order produced according to created means is called fate, according to divine intellect providence, and according to proximate effect nature, fortune, or voluntary act.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:D4","CH_GGVC__108:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:AFM-B01","text":"Apparently disproportionate demonic assault → division of evil intention and divine ordering → limitation of power over will and nature → generalization of causal levels → impedible celestial causality → contingency of secondary causes → providentially derived fate.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS4","CH_GGVC__107:AS3","CH_GGVC__108:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:AFM-B02","text":"Extraordinary or fortuitous effect → rejection of simple attribution → distinction of proximate and higher cause → examination of mediation and recipient → preservation of freedom or contingency → conditional closure under providence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:SD3","CH_GGVC__108:MRT3","CH_GGVC__108:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MME-01","text":"The field of causality is reconfigured from a homogeneous plane into a graduated order, in which body, organic power, will, created intellect, and God exercise distinct modes of causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SNX2","CH_GGVC__107:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MME-02","text":"Freedom is reclassified from immunity against influx into the capacity to receive inclinations without necessary determination.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX7","CH_GGVC__107:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MME-03","text":"Fate is transferred from sidereal necessity into a relational and mediated order of created causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:NX1","CH_GGVC__108:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MME-04","text":"Chance changes from a simple lack of order into a discrepancy between concurrence not naturally unified and the intentional unity of a higher intellect.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A18","CH_GGVC__108:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MME-05","text":"The demon is reclassified from a universal cause of sin or astral effect into a limited intellectual agent that suggests, chooses means, and instrumentally employs nature.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A9","CH_GGVC__107:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MME-06","text":"Miracle is converted from a synonym of wonder into an ontological limit between created causality and divine operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:NX6","CH_GGVC__106:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MME-07","text":"The reading of events becomes differentially causal: appearance, regularity, and correlation yield to examination of end, means, recipient, ontological level, and respect to cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D6","CH_GGVC__107:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of surface appear demonic temptations, changes of bodies, celestial influxes, extraordinary signs, fortuitous events, and the question of fate.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:ALT1","CH_GGVC__107:ALT1","CH_GGVC__108:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level the same objects are distributed through mediation, instrumentality, disposition of the recipient, direct or indirect causality, and the difference between per se and per accidens.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:ALT2","CH_GGVC__106:AS4","CH_GGVC__107:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the question passes from attribution to one cause to the art of discerning levels, limits, and respects, so that apparent opposites are treated by stratification rather than elimination.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:ALT3","CH_GGVC__108:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is an order of finite, mobile, and impedible causes under immobile providence; created time is generated through succession, return, variation, and concurrence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:ALT4","CH_GGVC__108:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the text effects stratified causality itself, because through repeated distinctions it contracts, limits, and restores individual appearances under a higher respect.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:AS3","CH_GGVC__108:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FL-01","text":"A concurrence per accidens as such has no cause, but a fortuitous event is intentionally ordered under a higher intellect; coherence depends on the distinction between the cause of concurrence and the cause of concurrent factors.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A18","CH_GGVC__107:MRT6","CH_GGVC__108:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FL-02","text":"The will is not necessarily changed by demon and heaven, but its change is attributed to God alone; the manner in which divine causality does not destroy freedom is assumed here rather than explained.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A8","CH_GGVC__108:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FL-03","text":"The demon's malice is ordered within providence without a salvific command to evil; the distinction of intentions preserves the difference, but the junction between permission, ordering, and imputability remains subtle.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D1","CH_GGVC__106:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FL-04","text":"Miracle is placed beyond active natural powers, yet not beyond every passive power of the creature; the relation between universal receptivity and powers ordered to agents is determined only briefly.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A7","CH_GGVC__107:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:FL-05","text":"The relatively greater universality of celestial causality must be reconciled with the particularity of recipients, the impedibility of causes, and the irreducibility of accidental concurrence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:MRT5","CH_GGVC__107:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MP-01","text":"Temptation changes from an experience simply so called into a structure whose species is determined by the agent's end; the name remains the same, the moral function varies.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A5","CH_GGVC__106:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MP-02","text":"Fate undergoes a terminal inversion: what is rejected as necessity of the stars is received as a derived order of secondary causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:NX8","CH_GGVC__108:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MP-03","text":"Contingency changes from the impedibility of particular causes into a relation of scale, whereby the same event is chance below and intended above.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SD5","CH_GGVC__108:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MP-04","text":"The demon changes from a morally described assailant into an intellectual cause that chooses natural means without suffering from them; this transfer joins the ethics of temptation with causal cosmology.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:A7","CH_GGVC__107:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a network of nested causes, in which descending force is contracted through means and ascending order relates dispersed events to one principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A9","CH_GGVC__108:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:HSI-02","text":"The human subject is a selective membrane between body, passions, intellect, and will: it admits influx, but does not necessarily translate it into consent.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:A12","CH_GGVC__107:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:HSI-03","text":"Time is the mobility of created causes under an immobile providential relation, with returns of temptation, natural successions, and contingent effects.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS5","CH_GGVC__108:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:HSI-04","text":"Navigation is possible not by escape from every influx, but by discernment of cause, moderation of inclinations, aided resistance, and acceptance of contingency without fatalistic attribution.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:D6","CH_GGVC__106:ME4","CH_GGVC__108:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of stratified causality, secondary mediation, impedible reception, non-necessitated will, providence, and the limit of miracle suffice to reconstruct the structure of the present group.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:SD1","CH_GGVC__108:A7","CH_GGVC__106:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B02","text":"It is not yet sufficiently determined how God changes the will without abolishing freedom, nor are the foundations of the immateriality of intellect and the nature of demons demonstrated at this level.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:A5","CH_GGVC__107:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B03","text":"The system coheres most where the distinctions of higher and lower cause, inclination and determination, per se and per accidens, and providence and fate mutually confirm one another.","refs":["CH_GGVC__107:DNX2","CH_GGVC__107:ME4","CH_GGVC__108:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B04","text":"The strongest nucleus is the twofold relation of the same effect, which combines local agency and universal ordering without homogenization.","refs":["CH_GGVC__108:MRT3","CH_GGVC__107:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction relies on the recursive application of a few distinctions rather than on a simply linear order; repetition of direct, indirect, medium, and respect effects the mutation of the field of causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS4","CH_GGVC__107:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B06","text":"The true movement is preserved if limited assault is expanded into general created causality and this is concluded in the providential order of secondary causes; individual cosmological accounts still resist this reduction, but the generative machine remains intact.","refs":["CH_GGVC__106:AS4","CH_GGVC__107:AS1","CH_GGVC__108:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_39@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_39:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 115–117","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 114 · Prima pars · Quaestio 115 · Prima pars · Quaestio 116"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 118–120","order":40,"parents":["CH_GGVC__109","CH_GGVC__110","CH_GGVC__111"],"revision":"CL_GGVC_40@R001","sha256":"6330b2b4908d4da8958a86306a510a4791b9d65829a08a3e2c4b91dd88115282","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo homo, ut compositum spirituale et corporale, scientiam, vitam et similitudinem generativam vere causet sine causalitate ordinem creatum excedente.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS1","CH_GGVC__110:AS1","CH_GGVC__111:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AS-02","text":"Efficacia humana affirmatur ut realis sed distributa: principia intrinseca principaliter agunt, causae creatae externe ministrant vel instrumentaliter disponunt, et causalitas creatrix terminum immaterialem constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD2","CH_GGVC__110:SD2","CH_GGVC__111:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AS-03","text":"Argumentatio apparentiam effectus diagnostice resolvit per modum essendi, operationis, receptionis et mediationis, non per simplicem attributionem effectus agenti proximo.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS3","CH_GGVC__110:AS2","CH_GGVC__111:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AS-04","text":"Motus descendit ab examine actionis humanae exterioris ad originem principiorum humanorum, deinde ad circulationem materiae qua compositum iam constitutum conservatur et propagatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS6","CH_GGVC__110:ME2","CH_GGVC__111:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AS-05","text":"Progressus est recursivus et mutatione actus: eadem causalitas mediata redit in doctrina, semine et nutritione, sed transitus embryonales discontinuos cum continuitate formali corporis adulti coniungit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:D5","CH_GGVC__111:A9","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AS-06","text":"Clausura hominem exhibet ut unitatem formalem processualem cuius actio naturalis corpori et mediis proportionatis innititur, cuius tamen principium intellectivum sola generatione materiali non constituitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A13","CH_GGVC__110:A14","CH_GGVC__111:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Omnis effectus causae attribuendus est secundum proportionem inter modum essendi agentis, virtutem eius, recipiens, obiectum et medium operationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A1","CH_GGVC__110:AS2","CH_GGVC__111:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] Causa creata vere causat cum potentiam recipientis per ministerium, instrumentum vel assimilationem ordinat, quamvis principium intrinsecum actum principaliter perficiat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A10","CH_GGVC__110:A5","CH_GGVC__111:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Transitus de potentia in actum est grammatica communis doctrinae, generationis et nutritionis, sed medium transitus secundum genus effectus mutatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS4","CH_GGVC__110:A2","CH_GGVC__111:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Modus operationis determinat modum essendi, originis et efficaciae; spiritualitas sola nec dominium indefinitum nec independentiam a corpore probat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS5","CH_GGVC__110:A10","CH_GGVC__111:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Virtus corporalis materiam transformare et ad formam disponere potest, sed terminum immaterialem neque ex materia neque per instrumentum corporeum producit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A3","CH_GGVC__110:A9","CH_GGVC__110:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-06","text":"[Terminal] Principium intellectivum, quia subsistens et operatione corpus transcendens, non traducitur semine sed a Deo creatur cum corpus ad formam humanam disponitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A11","CH_GGVC__110:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Homo est unum compositum per se sub una forma substantiali; corpus non est additamentum contingens animae neque anima sola homo completus.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A7","CH_GGVC__110:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Identitas corporis viventis est formaliter continua et materialiter processualis: eadem ordinatio persistit dum materia consumitur, substituitur et assimilatur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:A7","CH_GGVC__111:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Nutritio et generatio sunt gradus ordinati eiusdem causalitatis formalis: prima materiam individuo assimilat, altera materiam virtualiter totalem ad novum individuum dirigit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:AS5","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Transcendentia animae intellectivae materiam non convertit in obiectum imperii immediati, quia actio naturalis humana per corpus proprium et media proportionata exercetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A13","CH_GGVC__109:A14","CH_GGVC__110:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Causalitas divina et causalitas creata non sunt causae aemulae eiusdem ordinis: Deus terminum creatricem praestat, natura autem dispositiones, instrumenta et conversiones reales efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD1","CH_GGVC__110:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-01","text":"Causalitas ministerialis magistri redit ut causalitas instrumentalis seminis et ut ministerium nutritivae erga generativam; identitas functionalis manet, quamvis subiectum et effectus mutentur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A10","CH_GGVC__110:A5","CH_GGVC__111:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-02","text":"Principium intrinsecum transit ab intellectu discipuli ad animam generantis et deinde ad formam viventis assimilantem, ubique causalitatem externam in actum proprium recipientis convertens.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A6","CH_GGVC__110:A5","CH_GGVC__111:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-03","text":"Materia mutatur ab obiecto quod non obedit spiritui creato in subiectum dispositionis seminalis, deinde in fluxum assimilabilem sub forma viventis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A3","CH_GGVC__110:A12","CH_GGVC__111:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-04","text":"Forma primo terminum proportionatae virtutis indicat, deinde formas embryonales substituit, postremo identitatem corporis per mutationem materiae conservat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS5","CH_GGVC__110:A8","CH_GGVC__111:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-05","text":"Semen mutatur ab instrumento quod animam sensitivam causaliter producit in materiam ex alimento derivatam quae totum corpus virtualiter gerit; productio animae intellectivae ab utraque functione excluditur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:AS3","CH_GGVC__111:AS5","CH_GGVC__111:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-06","text":"Separatio spiritualis vim inversam accipit: subsistentiam principii intellectivi confirmat, sed perfectionem hominis et potestatem naturalem in corpora externa minuit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:NX9","CH_GGVC__110:A10","CH_GGVC__110:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-07","text":"Causalitas creatrix, negative reservata ubi immediata productio formae creaturae negatur, positive determinatur in origine animae intellectivae et postea in forma viventis iam constituta tacite absorbetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SNX1","CH_GGVC__110:A14","CH_GGVC__111:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-08","text":"Exemplar materiae originariae fixe transmissae opere structurali peracto evanescit; genealogia et identitas transferuntur ad continuitatem formae et virtutis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:NX2","CH_GGVC__111:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:CGD-01","text":"Quaestiones de illuminatione Angelorum et de miraculis manent probationes locales limitis spiritualis; hoc gradu retinetur regula generalis qua effectus causam suam per se non manifestat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A11","CH_GGVC__109:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:CGD-02","text":"Successio formarum embryonalium et distinctio humidi radicalis sunt machinae inferiores; sursum amplificantur tantum ut exempla liminum ontologicorum et debilitatis virtutis assimilativae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:AS4","CH_GGVC__111:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:CGD-03","text":"Doctrina ministerialis non manet casus epistemicus solitarius, sed exemplar fit causalitatis creatae quae effectum producit disponendo potentiam quam non constituit ex nihilo.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:D1","CH_GGVC__110:D4","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:CGD-04","text":"Semen coniungit gradus inferiores: in ordine generationis est instrumentum virtutis parentis, in ordine nutritionis est alimentum iam ad potentiam totius corporis promotum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:AS3","CH_GGVC__111:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:CGD-05","text":"Unitas intellectus, praeexistentia animae et materia genealogica fixa desinunt esse errores separati; hoc gradu unum exemplar transmissionis substantiae iam completae constituunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:NX3","CH_GGVC__110:NX9","CH_GGVC__111:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat spiritualitatem creatam esse potestatem vagam quae intellectum alienum, materiam externam vel corpus quod non vivificat immediate subiciat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SNX2","CH_GGVC__109:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GNX-02","text":"Scientia et vita non transferuntur in recipiens quasi qualitates iam perfectae; recipiens per principium intrinsecum materialia vel signa sibi proposita actualizat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:NX1","CH_GGVC__109:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GNX-03","text":"Natura corporea non est causa totalis hominis, quia dispositio materiae ad principium intellectivum pertingere non potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:DNX2","CH_GGVC__110:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GNX-04","text":"Identitas individui non fundatur in materia numerice immobili neque corpus vivens materialiter clauditur contra assimilationem externam.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:DNX1","CH_GGVC__111:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GNX-05","text":"Anima humana neque ante corpus ut homo completus praeexistit neque corpus contingenter vel poenaliter assumit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:NX9","CH_GGVC__110:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GNX-06","text":"Eventus extraordinarius, sanctitas agentis vel vehementia imaginationis non sufficiunt ad probandam competentiam naturalem causae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD3","CH_GGVC__109:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet causam veram aut totaliter producere effectum aut nihil causare; aliter magister, generans et virtus nutritiva causalitatem realem amitterent.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:D1","CH_GGVC__110:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet unum modum causalitatis cognitioni, formae corporeae et principio immateriali univoce convenire.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:DNX1","CH_GGVC__110:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet causalitatem divinam et naturalem esse aemulas, quia causa superior terminum attingit sine evacuanda dispositione reali causarum inferiorum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A12","CH_GGVC__110:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem continuitatem temporalem postulare subiectum materialiter immutatum, cum generatio per corruptiones procedat et corpus formatum per substitutionem permaneat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:D5","CH_GGVC__111:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet hominem ad animam separatam vel ad corpus causaliter clausum reduci; utraque reductio unitatem compositi tollit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A13","CH_GGVC__110:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas inadmissibilis est homo qui causam totalem sui intellectus, suae formae et suae materiae intra ordinem creatum possideat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SNX1","CH_GGVC__110:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSA-02","text":"Systema circum exclusionem immedietatis finitae ordinatur: nulla voluntas creata alienam cognitionem, formam immaterialem aut obedientiam materiae sine medio constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SNX2","CH_GGVC__110:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSA-03","text":"Ontologice inadmissibilis est humanitas vel pure spiritualis vel materialiter clausa; homo tantum in commissura formae subsistentis, corporis naturalis et materiae fluentis constat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:SNX2","CH_GGVC__111:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:XD-01","text":"Signa doctrinalia et alimentum extrinsecus datum eandem structuram formalem participant: effectum non transferunt perfectum, sed ab intrinseca virtute recipientis assimilantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-02","CL_GGVC_40:GA-03","CH_GGVC__109:A9","CH_GGVC__111:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:XD-02","text":"Ministerialitas magistri, instrumentalitas seminis et subordinatio nutritivae constituunt unam causalitatem delegatam, cuius efficacia consistit in ordinatione potentiae ad actum proprium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-02","CL_GGVC_40:GA-09","CH_GGVC__109:A10","CH_GGVC__110:A5","CH_GGVC__111:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:XD-03","text":"Circulus generationis humanae transit ab alimento assimilato ad semen, a semine ad dispositionem corporis et a dispositione ad creationem animae intellectivae, sine traductione integrae animae parentis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-05","CL_GGVC_40:GA-06","CL_GGVC_40:GA-09","CH_GGVC__110:D4","CH_GGVC__111:AS5","CH_GGVC__111:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:XD-04","text":"Limen inter causalitatem materialem et immaterialem est simul negativum et productivum: natura ibi cessat ut efficiens ultima, sed manet necessaria ut dispositiva.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-05","CL_GGVC_40:GA-06","CL_GGVC_40:GA-11","CH_GGVC__109:A3","CH_GGVC__110:A9","CH_GGVC__110:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:XD-05","text":"Continuitas embryonis et continuitas corporis iam formati non sunt eiusdem generis: ante formam ultimam adsunt successiones substantiales, post eam substitutio materiae identitatem numericam non solvit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-07","CL_GGVC_40:GA-08","CH_GGVC__110:D5","CH_GGVC__111:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:XD-06","text":"Subsistentia intellectiva transcendit dependentiam organicam operationis propriae, sed non tollit ordinationem naturalem animae ad corpus neque confert imperium naturale in corpora aliena.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-04","CL_GGVC_40:GA-07","CL_GGVC_40:GA-10","CH_GGVC__110:A10","CH_GGVC__110:A13","CH_GGVC__109:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:XD-07","text":"Mors consequitur defectum virtutis quae materiam corpori assimilat et compositum conservat; corruptio corporis statum separationis inducit, non demonstrationem corruptionis principii subsistentis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-06","CL_GGVC_40:GA-08","CL_GGVC_40:GA-10","CH_GGVC__111:A12","CH_GGVC__110:A10","CH_GGVC__110:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSD-01","text":"Homo ontologice constituitur ut nexus asymmetrics causarum: forma intrinseca unitatem praestat, materia fluxum recipit, agentia creata disponunt, et causa divina principium immateriale constituit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD1","CH_GGVC__110:SD2","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSD-02","text":"Potentia agendi humana non est dominium super effectum, sed facultas condiciones ordinandi sub quibus principium proprium alterius vel compositi ad actum procedit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:D1","CH_GGVC__110:D6","CH_GGVC__111:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSD-03","text":"Tempus humanum tres modos coniungit: actualizationem ordinatam cognitionis, saltus substantiales generationis et continuitatem processualem corporis per fluxum materiae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS4","CH_GGVC__110:SD3","CH_GGVC__111:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSD-04","text":"Identitas humana neque materiae permanentia neque animae solitaria praesentia est, sed relatio formalis quae originem corporalem, unitatem compositi et successionem materiae continet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:SD4","CH_GGVC__111:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSD-05","text":"Cognitio causalis requirit transitum ab effectu visibili ad competentiam ontologicam agentis; similitudo, miraculum vel continuitas apparentiae causam sufficientem nondum probant.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD3","CH_GGVC__111:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSD-06","text":"Causalitas divina naturam non substituit, sed terminum attingit ad quem causae corporeae per dispositionem realem ordinantur; dependentia igitur efficaciam secundariam instituit potius quam delet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A12","CH_GGVC__110:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RLE-01","text":"Datum extrinsecum → assimilatio per principium intrinsecum → actus proprius recipientis: hic circuitus signa in scientiam et alimentum in corpus convertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A9","CH_GGVC__111:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RLE-02","text":"Alimentum → potentia corporis → semen → dispositio embryonis → creatio animae → novum vivens: generatio claudit circuitum quo assimilatio individui in propagationem speciei transit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:AS5","CH_GGVC__110:D4","CH_GGVC__110:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RLE-03","text":"Consumptio materiae → restauratio nutritiva → debilitatio virtutis conversivae → restauratio insufficiens → mors: conservatio viventis continet viam propriae cessationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:A12","CH_GGVC__111:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RLE-04","text":"Attractor totius gregis est causalitas proportionata: omnis praetensio efficaciae reducitur ad modum essendi, instrumentum, recipiens et terminum quem agens attingere potest.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A2","CH_GGVC__110:SD1","CH_GGVC__111:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RLE-05","text":"Praetensio potentiae → distinctio ordinum → exclusio immedietatis → reattributio effectus → restitutio causae finitae: negatio iterata systema positivum causarum ordinatarum efficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:FGS6","CH_GGVC__110:SD6","CH_GGVC__111:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RLE-06","text":"Signum, semen et alimentum sunt termini qui machinae fiunt: nihil eorum effectus perfectus est, sed unumquodque transitum ad actum alterius principii instruit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A9","CH_GGVC__110:A6","CH_GGVC__111:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FGS-B01","text":"Effectus apparens → examen attributionis → distinctio causae principalis, ministerialis, instrumentalis et creatricis → determinatio competentiae agentis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A1","CH_GGVC__109:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FGS-B02","text":"Modus operationis → modus essendi → genus originis → ambitus causalitatis proportionatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:AS2","CH_GGVC__110:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FGS-B03","text":"Signum vel materia extrinseca → dispositio vel assimilatio → actio principii intrinseci → effectus proprius recipientis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A9","CH_GGVC__110:A12","CH_GGVC__111:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FGS-B04","text":"Anima generantis → virtus nutritiva → semen instrumentale → corpus dispositum → anima intellectiva creata → unum compositum humanum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A5","CH_GGVC__110:A14","CH_GGVC__111:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FGS-B05","text":"Compositum formatum → assimilatio materiae → continuitas identitatis → generatio ulterior → debilitatio assimilationis → dissolutio corporis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:A7","CH_GGVC__111:A12","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FGS-B06","text":"Exorbitantia causalis detecta → negatio dominationis immediatae → distributio effectus inter causas inaequales → clausura intra ordinem creatum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SNX2","CH_GGVC__110:SNX1","CH_GGVC__111:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AFM-B01","text":"Efficacia humana ambigua → distinctio ordinum causalium → examen proportionis → affirmatio causae creatae limitatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS3","CH_GGVC__109:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AFM-B02","text":"Doctrina qualificata → restrictio potestatis spiritualis → regressus ad modum operationis → divisio originis formarum.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:D1","CH_GGVC__109:SD2","CH_GGVC__110:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AFM-B03","text":"Dispositio materialis → creatio animae intellectivae → unio naturalis animae et corporis → identitas viventis processualis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:D4","CH_GGVC__110:A13","CH_GGVC__111:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AFM-B04","text":"Nutritio → materia seminalis → generatio corporis → terminus creatrix → novum compositum capax nutritionis et generationis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:D7","CH_GGVC__111:AS5","CH_GGVC__110:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AFM-B05","text":"Exclusio causalitatis totalis naturae → exclusio materiae clausae → restitutio hominis ut causae realis, dependentis et formaliter unificatae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SNX1","CH_GGVC__110:DNX2","CH_GGVC__111:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MME-01","text":"Causa reclassificatur ab agente quod effectum completum transfert ad ordinem plurium agentium quorum unum actualizat, aliud disponit, aliud ministrat et aliud creat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:ME1","CH_GGVC__110:ME2","CH_GGVC__111:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MME-02","text":"Materia reclassificatur a subiecto vel patrimonio fixo ad potentiam dispositam, assimilabilem et successive substituibilem sub forma.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A12","CH_GGVC__111:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MME-03","text":"Identitas transfertur a communitate vel permanentia materiae ad continuitatem formae, operationis et ordinationis causalis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:A7","CH_GGVC__110:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MME-04","text":"Oppositio simplex inter spiritum et corpus corruit: principium intellectivum transcendit materiam secundum operationem, sed homo per corpus naturaliter constituitur et agit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS5","CH_GGVC__110:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MME-05","text":"Generatio humana reordinatur ut coordinatio nutritionis, virtutis seminalis, successionis formarum, dispositionis corporis et creationis animae, non ut transmissio substantiae completae.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:D4","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MME-06","text":"Lectio causalis fit regressiva: ab effectu procedendum est ad modum operationis et competentiam agentis, non ab apparentia ad attributionem immediatam.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD3","CH_GGVC__111:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei examinantur doctrina, actio animae, origo animae, generatio corporis, conversio alimenti et constitutio seminis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS2","CH_GGVC__110:AS1","CH_GGVC__111:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali agunt causae principales, ministeriales, instrumentales et creatrices, quarum distributio potentiam in actum per media proportionata reducit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD1","CH_GGVC__110:SD2","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico certamen non inter affirmationem et negationem potentiae, sed inter attributionem indiscretam et distributionem ordinatam causarum constituitur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS3","CH_GGVC__111:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas humana coniungit saltus substantiales, continuitatem formalem, fluxum materialem et subsistentiam principii intellectivi.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:SD3","CH_GGVC__111:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo textus ipsam doctrinam causalem efficit quam describit: obiectiones praebet ut materiam, distinctionibus disponit et per ordinem inferentialem conclusionem actualizat.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS4","CH_GGVC__111:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FL-01","text":"Magister scientiam vere causare dicitur, quamvis neque lumen neque species neque operationem principalem intellectus efficiat; causalitas eius pendet a sensu robusto dispositionis ministerialis.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A8","CH_GGVC__109:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FL-02","text":"Anima intellectiva subsistit sine corpore, sed corpus ad perfectionem humanam naturaliter requiritur; systema subsistentiam partis a plenitudine compositi distinguere debet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A10","CH_GGVC__110:A13","CH_GGVC__110:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FL-03","text":"Homo naturaliter hominem generat, sed animam rationalem non producit; unitas unius generationis igitur ex coordinatione causarum quarum termini inaequales sunt pendet.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:D4","CH_GGVC__110:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FL-04","text":"Embryo per corruptionem formarum substantialium progreditur, dum corpus constitutum per substitutionem materiae idem numero manet; limes inter mutationem substantialem et assimilationem conservativam est commissura necessaria.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:D5","CH_GGVC__111:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MP-01","text":"Semen sensum mutat ab instrumento transmutante materiam ad alimentum iam virtualiter totum corpus; utrumque sensum coniungit virtus formalis a generante derivata.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A6","CH_GGVC__111:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MP-02","text":"Materia transit ab eo quod formam recipit ad id quod disponitur, assimilatur et identitatem materialem amittit; passivitas eius manet, sed mobilitas causalis augetur.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A3","CH_GGVC__111:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MP-03","text":"Forma mutatur a termino qui formam priorem corrumpit in principium quod sub mutatione materiae persistit; differentia magnitudinis inter generationem et nutritionem functionem eius invertit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A8","CH_GGVC__111:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina transformationum inclusarum, in qua causae superiores terminos constituunt et causae inferiores materiam per assimilationem, dispositionem et ministerium transducunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD1","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum humanum est commissura, non monas: forma subsistens, corpus naturaliter formatum, materia fluens et relationes generativae in uno composito conveniunt.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A13","CH_GGVC__110:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:HSI-03","text":"Tempus huius machinae est simul saltatorium et continuatum: formae oriuntur per limina, corpus permanet per fluxum, et virtus conservativa paulatim deficit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:SD3","CH_GGVC__111:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est imperium immediatum, sed electio mediorum proportionatorum: signa ad intellectum, corpus proprium ad actionem, semen ad generationem et alimentum ad conservationem.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A9","CH_GGVC__109:A14","CH_GGVC__110:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de causalitate proportionata, principio intrinseco, mediatione instrumentali, unitate compositi et identitate formali sufficiunt ad structuram gregis restituendam.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:MRT2","CH_GGVC__110:MRT2","CH_GGVC__111:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est distributio effectus inter causas inaequales secundum modum essendi; ea doctrinam, generationem et nutritionem sine aequatione differentiarum coniungit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD1","CH_GGVC__110:SD1","CH_GGVC__111:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B03","text":"Nondum satis determinantur nexus exactus inter ultimam dispositionem et creationem animae neque criterium quo alimentum per sanguinem et semen ad membrum perfecte formatum transit.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:MRT4","CH_GGVC__111:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat, quia ab actione finita per originem compositi ad conservationem materialiter fluentem procedit; reductioni tamen resistunt limina inter dispositionem et formam ultimam atque inter successionem substantialem et identitatem continuam.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:MRT8","CH_GGVC__110:MRT8","CH_GGVC__111:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T173406464110Z_5360fc00","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 118–120","source_sha256":"6330b2b4908d4da8958a86306a510a4791b9d65829a08a3e2c4b91dd88115282","sha256":"622c21e9481a344b8f8d5352e815fab68f3529ee128df69d9390f52bbd9d6bd2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:34:06.465035+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AS-01","text":"The system asks how the human being, as a spiritual and corporeal composite, truly causes knowledge, life, and generative likeness without a causality exceeding the created order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS1","CH_GGVC__110:AS1","CH_GGVC__111:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AS-02","text":"Human efficacy is affirmed as real but distributed: intrinsic principles act principally, created causes minister externally or dispose instrumentally, and creative causality establishes the immaterial terminus.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD2","CH_GGVC__110:SD2","CH_GGVC__111:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AS-03","text":"The argument diagnostically resolves the appearance of an effect through the mode of being, operation, reception, and mediation, not through simple attribution of the effect to the proximate agent.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS3","CH_GGVC__110:AS2","CH_GGVC__111:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AS-04","text":"The movement descends from the examination of exterior human action to the origin of human principles, then to the circulation of matter by which the already constituted composite is preserved and propagated.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS6","CH_GGVC__110:ME2","CH_GGVC__111:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AS-05","text":"The progression is recursive and changes in act: the same mediated causality returns in teaching, semen, and nutrition, yet it joins discontinuous embryonic transitions with the formal continuity of the adult body.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:D5","CH_GGVC__111:A9","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AS-06","text":"The closure presents the human being as a processual formal unity whose natural action relies upon the body and proportionate means, but whose intellective principle is not constituted by material generation alone.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A13","CH_GGVC__110:A14","CH_GGVC__111:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every effect must be attributed to its cause according to the proportion between the agent's mode of being, its power, the recipient, the object, and the medium of operation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A1","CH_GGVC__110:AS2","CH_GGVC__111:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] A created cause truly causes when, through ministry, instrument, or assimilation, it orders the power of the recipient, although the intrinsic principle principally completes the act.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A10","CH_GGVC__110:A5","CH_GGVC__111:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] The transition from potency to act is the common grammar of teaching, generation, and nutrition, but the medium of transition changes according to the kind of effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS4","CH_GGVC__110:A2","CH_GGVC__111:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] The mode of operation determines the mode of being, origin, and efficacy; spirituality alone proves neither indefinite dominion nor independence from the body.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS5","CH_GGVC__110:A10","CH_GGVC__111:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Corporeal power can transform matter and dispose it toward form, but it produces no immaterial terminus either from matter or through a corporeal instrument.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A3","CH_GGVC__110:A9","CH_GGVC__110:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-06","text":"[Terminal] The intellective principle, because it subsists and transcends the body in operation, is not transmitted through semen but is created by God when the body is disposed for human form.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A11","CH_GGVC__110:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] The human being is one composite per se under one substantial form; the body is not a contingent addition to the soul, nor is the soul alone a complete human being.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A7","CH_GGVC__110:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] The identity of the living body is formally continuous and materially processual: the same ordering persists while matter is consumed, replaced, and assimilated.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:A7","CH_GGVC__111:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Nutrition and generation are ordered degrees of the same formal causality: the former assimilates matter to the individual, the latter directs virtually total matter toward a new individual.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:AS5","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] The transcendence of the intellective soul does not turn matter into an object of immediate dominion, because natural human action is exercised through its own body and proportionate means.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A13","CH_GGVC__109:A14","CH_GGVC__110:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Divine causality and created causality are not rival causes of the same order: God supplies the creative terminus, whereas nature effects real dispositions, instruments, and transformations.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD1","CH_GGVC__110:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-01","text":"The ministerial causality of the teacher returns as the instrumental causality of semen and as the ministry of the nutritive power toward the generative; the functional identity remains, although subject and effect change.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A10","CH_GGVC__110:A5","CH_GGVC__111:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-02","text":"The intrinsic principle passes from the intellect of the disciple to the soul of the generator and then to the assimilating form of the living being, everywhere converting external causality into the recipient's proper act.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A6","CH_GGVC__110:A5","CH_GGVC__111:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-03","text":"Matter changes from an object that does not obey created spirit into the subject of seminal disposition, then into an assimilable flow under the form of the living being.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A3","CH_GGVC__110:A12","CH_GGVC__111:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-04","text":"Form first indicates the terminus of proportionate power, then replaces embryonic forms, and finally preserves the body's identity through the change of matter.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS5","CH_GGVC__110:A8","CH_GGVC__111:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-05","text":"Semen changes from an instrument that causally produces the sensitive soul into matter derived from nourishment that virtually bears the whole body; the production of the intellective soul is excluded from both functions.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:AS3","CH_GGVC__111:AS5","CH_GGVC__111:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-06","text":"Spiritual separation assumes an inverse force: it confirms the subsistence of the intellective principle, but diminishes the perfection of the human being and natural power over external bodies.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:NX9","CH_GGVC__110:A10","CH_GGVC__110:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-07","text":"Creative causality, negatively reserved where the immediate production of a creaturely form is denied, is positively determined in the origin of the intellective soul and later tacitly absorbed into the already constituted form of the living being.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SNX1","CH_GGVC__110:A14","CH_GGVC__111:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RMD-08","text":"The model of fixedly transmitted original matter disappears once its structural work is completed; genealogy and identity are transferred to the continuity of form and power.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:NX2","CH_GGVC__111:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:CGD-01","text":"Questions concerning the illumination of Angels and miracles remain local proofs of the spiritual limit; at this level, the general rule is retained whereby an effect does not of itself manifest its cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A11","CH_GGVC__109:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:CGD-02","text":"The succession of embryonic forms and the distinction of radical moisture are lower mechanisms; they are amplified upward only as examples of ontological thresholds and the weakness of assimilative power.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:AS4","CH_GGVC__111:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:CGD-03","text":"Ministerial teaching does not remain an isolated epistemic case, but becomes the model of created causality that produces an effect by disposing a potency which it does not constitute from nothing.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:D1","CH_GGVC__110:D4","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:CGD-04","text":"Semen joins lower levels: in the order of generation it is an instrument of parental power; in the order of nutrition it is nourishment already raised to the potency of the whole body.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:AS3","CH_GGVC__111:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:CGD-05","text":"The unity of intellect, the pre-existence of the soul, and fixed genealogical matter cease to be separate errors; at this level they constitute one model of the transmission of already complete substance.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:NX3","CH_GGVC__110:NX9","CH_GGVC__111:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GNX-01","text":"The system denies that created spirituality is a vague power that immediately subjects another's intellect, external matter, or a body which it does not enliven.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SNX2","CH_GGVC__109:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GNX-02","text":"Knowledge and life are not transferred into a recipient as though they were already perfect qualities; through an intrinsic principle, the recipient actualizes materials or signs presented to it.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:NX1","CH_GGVC__109:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GNX-03","text":"Corporeal nature is not the total cause of the human being, because the disposition of matter cannot reach the intellective principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:DNX2","CH_GGVC__110:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GNX-04","text":"The identity of the individual is not founded in numerically unmoving matter, nor is the living body materially closed against external assimilation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:DNX1","CH_GGVC__111:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GNX-05","text":"The human soul neither pre-exists before the body as a complete human being nor assumes the body contingently or punitively.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:NX9","CH_GGVC__110:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:GNX-06","text":"An extraordinary event, the sanctity of the agent, or the vehemence of imagination do not suffice to prove a cause's natural competence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD3","CH_GGVC__109:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that a true cause either produces an effect totally or causes nothing; otherwise the teacher, the generator, and nutritive power would lose real causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:D1","CH_GGVC__110:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that one mode of causality applies univocally to knowledge, corporeal form, and immaterial principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:DNX1","CH_GGVC__110:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that divine and natural causality are rivals, because the higher cause attains the terminus without emptying the real disposition of lower causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A12","CH_GGVC__110:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that every temporal continuity requires a materially unchanged subject, since generation proceeds through corruptions and the formed body persists through replacement.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:D5","CH_GGVC__111:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the human being is reducible to a separated soul or to a causally closed body; either reduction removes the unity of the composite.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A13","CH_GGVC__110:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSA-01","text":"An inadmissible possibility is a human being possessing within the created order the total cause of his intellect, his form, and his matter.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SNX1","CH_GGVC__110:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSA-02","text":"The system is ordered around the exclusion of finite immediacy: no created will constitutes another's cognition, an immaterial form, or the obedience of matter without a medium.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SNX2","CH_GGVC__110:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSA-03","text":"A humanity either purely spiritual or materially closed is ontologically inadmissible; the human being consists only in the junction of subsistent form, natural body, and flowing matter.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:SNX2","CH_GGVC__111:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:XD-01","text":"Doctrinal signs and externally given nourishment share the same formal structure: they do not transfer a perfect effect, but are assimilated by the recipient's intrinsic power.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-02","CL_GGVC_40:GA-03","CH_GGVC__109:A9","CH_GGVC__111:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:XD-02","text":"The ministeriality of the teacher, the instrumentality of semen, and the subordination of the nutritive power constitute one delegated causality, whose efficacy consists in ordering potency to its proper act.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-02","CL_GGVC_40:GA-09","CH_GGVC__109:A10","CH_GGVC__110:A5","CH_GGVC__111:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:XD-03","text":"The cycle of human generation passes from assimilated nourishment to semen, from semen to the disposition of the body, and from disposition to the creation of the intellective soul, without the transmission of the parent’s entire soul.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-05","CL_GGVC_40:GA-06","CL_GGVC_40:GA-09","CH_GGVC__110:D4","CH_GGVC__111:AS5","CH_GGVC__111:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:XD-04","text":"The threshold between material and immaterial causality is at once negative and productive: nature there ceases as ultimate efficient cause, but remains necessary as dispositive.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-05","CL_GGVC_40:GA-06","CL_GGVC_40:GA-11","CH_GGVC__109:A3","CH_GGVC__110:A9","CH_GGVC__110:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:XD-05","text":"The continuity of the embryo and the continuity of the already formed body are not of the same kind: before the ultimate form there are substantial successions; after it, the replacement of matter does not dissolve numerical identity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-07","CL_GGVC_40:GA-08","CH_GGVC__110:D5","CH_GGVC__111:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:XD-06","text":"Intellective subsistence transcends the organic dependence of proper operation, but does not remove the natural ordering of soul to body or confer natural dominion over alien bodies.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-04","CL_GGVC_40:GA-07","CL_GGVC_40:GA-10","CH_GGVC__110:A10","CH_GGVC__110:A13","CH_GGVC__109:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:XD-07","text":"Death follows from the failure of the power that assimilates matter to the body and preserves the composite; the corruption of the body induces a state of separation, not a demonstration of the corruption of the subsistent principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-06","CL_GGVC_40:GA-08","CL_GGVC_40:GA-10","CH_GGVC__111:A12","CH_GGVC__110:A10","CH_GGVC__110:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSD-01","text":"The human being is ontologically constituted as an asymmetric nexus of causes: intrinsic form supplies unity, matter receives flux, created agents dispose, and the divine cause constitutes the immaterial principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD1","CH_GGVC__110:SD2","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSD-02","text":"Human power of acting is not dominion over an effect, but the capacity to order conditions under which another's proper principle, or that of a composite, proceeds to act.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:D1","CH_GGVC__110:D6","CH_GGVC__111:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSD-03","text":"Human time joins three modes: the ordered actualization of knowledge, the substantial leaps of generation, and the processual continuity of the body through the flow of matter.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS4","CH_GGVC__110:SD3","CH_GGVC__111:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSD-04","text":"Human identity is neither the permanence of matter nor the solitary presence of the soul, but a formal relation containing bodily origin, the unity of the composite, and the succession of matter.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:SD4","CH_GGVC__111:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSD-05","text":"Causal knowledge requires passage from visible effect to the ontological competence of the agent; likeness, miracle, or continuity of appearance do not yet prove a sufficient cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD3","CH_GGVC__111:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:BSD-06","text":"Divine causality does not replace nature, but attains the terminus to which corporeal causes are ordered through real disposition; dependence therefore establishes rather than destroys secondary efficacy.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A12","CH_GGVC__110:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RLE-01","text":"External datum → assimilation through an intrinsic principle → the recipient's proper act: this circuit converts signs into knowledge and nourishment into body.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A9","CH_GGVC__111:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RLE-02","text":"Nourishment → potency of the body → semen → disposition of the embryo → creation of soul → new living being: generation closes the circuit by which the individual's assimilation passes into the propagation of the species.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:AS5","CH_GGVC__110:D4","CH_GGVC__110:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RLE-03","text":"Consumption of matter → nutritive restoration → weakening of transformative power → insufficient restoration → death: the preservation of the living being contains the path of its own cessation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:A12","CH_GGVC__111:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RLE-04","text":"The attractor of the whole group is proportionate causality: every claim of efficacy is reduced to the mode of being, instrument, recipient, and terminus that the agent can attain.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A2","CH_GGVC__110:SD1","CH_GGVC__111:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RLE-05","text":"Claim of power → distinction of orders → exclusion of immediacy → reattribution of effect → restoration of finite cause: repeated negation produces the positive system of ordered causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:FGS6","CH_GGVC__110:SD6","CH_GGVC__111:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:RLE-06","text":"Sign, semen, and nourishment are termini that become mechanisms: none is a perfect effect, but each structures the transition to the act of another principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A9","CH_GGVC__110:A6","CH_GGVC__111:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FGS-B01","text":"Apparent effect → examination of attribution → distinction of principal, ministerial, instrumental, and creative cause → determination of the agent's competence.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A1","CH_GGVC__109:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FGS-B02","text":"Mode of operation → mode of being → kind of origin → scope of proportionate causality.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:AS2","CH_GGVC__110:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FGS-B03","text":"Sign or extrinsic matter → disposition or assimilation → action of the intrinsic principle → recipient's proper effect.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A9","CH_GGVC__110:A12","CH_GGVC__111:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FGS-B04","text":"Soul of the generator → nutritive power → instrumental semen → disposed body → created intellective soul → one human composite.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A5","CH_GGVC__110:A14","CH_GGVC__111:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FGS-B05","text":"Formed composite → assimilation of matter → continuity of identity → further generation → weakening of assimilation → dissolution of the body.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:A7","CH_GGVC__111:A12","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FGS-B06","text":"Causal exorbitance detected → negation of immediate domination → distribution of effect among unequal causes → closure within the created order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SNX2","CH_GGVC__110:SNX1","CH_GGVC__111:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AFM-B01","text":"Ambiguous human efficacy → distinction of causal orders → examination of proportion → affirmation of limited created cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS3","CH_GGVC__109:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AFM-B02","text":"Qualified teaching → restriction of spiritual power → regress to mode of operation → division of the origin of forms.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:D1","CH_GGVC__109:SD2","CH_GGVC__110:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AFM-B03","text":"Material disposition → creation of intellective soul → natural union of soul and body → processual identity of the living being.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:D4","CH_GGVC__110:A13","CH_GGVC__111:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AFM-B04","text":"Nutrition → seminal matter → generation of body → creative terminus → new composite capable of nutrition and generation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:D7","CH_GGVC__111:AS5","CH_GGVC__110:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:AFM-B05","text":"Exclusion of nature's total causality → exclusion of closed matter → restoration of the human being as a real, dependent, and formally unified cause.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SNX1","CH_GGVC__110:DNX2","CH_GGVC__111:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MME-01","text":"Cause is reclassified from an agent that transfers a complete effect to an ordering of several agents, one of which actualizes, another disposes, another ministers, and another creates.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:ME1","CH_GGVC__110:ME2","CH_GGVC__111:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MME-02","text":"Matter is reclassified from a fixed subject or patrimony into a potency disposed, assimilable, and successively replaceable under form.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A12","CH_GGVC__111:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MME-03","text":"Identity is transferred from the commonality or permanence of matter to the continuity of form, operation, and causal ordering.","refs":["CH_GGVC__111:A7","CH_GGVC__110:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MME-04","text":"The simple opposition between spirit and body collapses: the intellective principle transcends matter according to operation, but the human being is naturally constituted and acts through the body.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS5","CH_GGVC__110:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MME-05","text":"Human generation is reordered as the coordination of nutrition, seminal power, succession of forms, disposition of the body, and creation of the soul, not as the transmission of complete substance.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:D4","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MME-06","text":"Causal reading becomes regressive: one must proceed from effect to the mode of operation and the agent's competence, not from appearance to immediate attribution.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD3","CH_GGVC__111:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, teaching, the action of the soul, the origin of the soul, generation of the body, transformation of nourishment, and constitution of semen are examined.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS2","CH_GGVC__110:AS1","CH_GGVC__111:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, principal, ministerial, instrumental, and creative causes operate, whose distribution reduces potency to act through proportionate means.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD1","CH_GGVC__110:SD2","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the conflict is constituted not between affirmation and negation of power, but between indiscriminate attribution and ordered distribution of causes.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS3","CH_GGVC__111:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, human reality joins substantial leaps, formal continuity, material flux, and the subsistence of the intellective principle.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:SD3","CH_GGVC__111:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the text enacts the very causal doctrine it describes: it provides objections as matter, disposes them through distinctions, and actualizes the conclusion through inferential order.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:AS4","CH_GGVC__111:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FL-01","text":"The teacher is said truly to cause knowledge, although he effects neither light nor species nor the principal operation of the intellect; his causality depends upon a robust sense of ministerial disposition.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A8","CH_GGVC__109:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FL-02","text":"The intellective soul subsists without the body, but the body is naturally required for human perfection; the system must distinguish the subsistence of a part from the fullness of the composite.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A10","CH_GGVC__110:A13","CH_GGVC__110:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FL-03","text":"A human being naturally generates a human being, but does not produce the rational soul; the unity of one generation therefore depends upon the coordination of causes whose termini are unequal.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:D4","CH_GGVC__110:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:FL-04","text":"The embryo progresses through the corruption of substantial forms, while the constituted body remains numerically the same through the replacement of matter; the boundary between substantial change and preservative assimilation is a necessary junction.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:D5","CH_GGVC__111:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MP-01","text":"Semen changes its meaning from an instrument transforming matter into nourishment already virtually the whole body; a formal power derived from the generator joins both meanings.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A6","CH_GGVC__111:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MP-02","text":"Matter passes from that which receives form to that which is disposed, assimilated, and loses material identity; its passivity remains, but its causal mobility increases.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A3","CH_GGVC__111:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MP-03","text":"Form changes from a terminus that corrupts a prior form into a principle that persists beneath the change of matter; the difference of magnitude between generation and nutrition reverses its function.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A8","CH_GGVC__111:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a machine of enclosed transformations, in which higher causes constitute termini and lower causes convey matter through assimilation, disposition, and ministry.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD1","CH_GGVC__111:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:HSI-02","text":"The human subject is a junction, not a monad: subsistent form, naturally formed body, flowing matter, and generative relations converge in one composite.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:A13","CH_GGVC__110:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:HSI-03","text":"The time of this machine is at once leaping and continuous: forms arise through thresholds, the body endures through flux, and preservative power gradually fails.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:SD3","CH_GGVC__111:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is not immediate dominion, but the selection of proportionate means: signs for intellect, one's own body for action, semen for generation, and nourishment for preservation.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:A9","CH_GGVC__109:A14","CH_GGVC__110:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B01","text":"Axioms concerning proportionate causality, intrinsic principle, instrumental mediation, unity of the composite, and formal identity suffice to restore the structure of the group.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:MRT2","CH_GGVC__110:MRT2","CH_GGVC__111:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the distribution of effect among unequal causes according to mode of being; it joins teaching, generation, and nutrition without equating their differences.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:SD1","CH_GGVC__110:SD1","CH_GGVC__111:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B03","text":"The exact nexus between ultimate disposition and the creation of the soul, and the criterion by which nourishment passes through blood and semen into a perfectly formed member, are not yet sufficiently determined.","refs":["CH_GGVC__110:MRT4","CH_GGVC__111:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement, because it proceeds from finite action through the origin of the composite to materially flowing preservation; yet it resists reduction at the thresholds between disposition and ultimate form and between substantial succession and continuous identity.","refs":["CH_GGVC__109:MRT8","CH_GGVC__110:MRT8","CH_GGVC__111:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_GGVC_40@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_GGVC_40:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Chapters 118–120","context":"Prima pars · Quaestio 117 · Prima pars · Quaestio 118 · Prima pars · Quaestio 119"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01","layer":"MC","title":"Ggvc L1 01","order":1,"parents":["CL_GGVC_01","CL_GGVC_02","CL_GGVC_03","CL_GGVC_04"],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_01@R001","sha256":"3573bf74de8ed7bf9429c3b49ce5c4d4d463bad2c0c250b4a7d16c5f77605168","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B01","text":"Quaestio centralis est quomodo intellectus finitus principium extra genera, compositionem et mensuram creatam cognoscere possit, cum idem principium finem cognitionis et causam ipsius cognoscentis constituat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-01","CL_GGVC_02:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B02","text":"Thesis globalis sacram doctrinam ut mediationem ordinatam constituit, cuius attractor est Deus tamquam ipsum esse subsistens, ex quo attributa divina et dependentia creaturarum derivantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-02","CL_GGVC_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B03","text":"Argumentatio architectonicam ordinationem, remotionem apophaticam, distinctionem modorum praedicationis et reductionem causaliter analogicam in unam machinam cognitionis componit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-03","CL_GGVC_02:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_03:BAS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B04","text":"Motus ab impedimentis receptionis et effectibus sensibilibus ad remotionem condicionum creatarum procedit, deinde attributa primi actus explicat et creaturas sub causalitate eius denuo interpretatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-04","CL_GGVC_02:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_03:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_04:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B05","text":"Progressio spiralis et cumulativa est: idem esse sub rationibus scientiae, actus, perfectionis, non-receptionis, praesentiae, immutabilitatis, durationis et unitatis recurrit, sed singulis reditibus novam vim generativam acquirit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-05","CL_GGVC_02:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_03:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_04:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B06","text":"Totum metagregem regit asymmetria qua Deus ontologice immediatus est ut causa essendi, intellectui autem humano tantum per revelationem, effectus, analogiam et remotionem aditur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-06","CL_GGVC_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B07","text":"Negatio non in silentium terminatur, sed compositionem, receptionem, successionem et divisionem removendo simplicitatem, plenitudinem, infinitatem, aeternitatem et unitatem positive restituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_04:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B08","text":"Clausura est realis sed non quidditativa: existentia, causalitas et attributa primi principii cognoscuntur, dum modus intrinsecus quo omnia in simplici esse unum sunt incomprehensus manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-07","CL_GGVC_02:BAS-08","CL_GGVC_03:BAS-08","CL_GGVC_04:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Finis supernaturalis, excessus obiecti et receptivitas finita mediationem requirunt; eadem lex receptionis cognitionem humanam et esse creatum inaequaliter ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:GA-01","CL_GGVC_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] Deus est ipsum esse subsistens, actus purus et primum ens simplex, cui nulla actualitas recepta vel distinctio constituens antecedit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GA-02","CL_GGVC_03:GA-01","CL_GGVC_04:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Esse creatum recipitur, ad naturam determinatam contrahitur et conservatione continua dependet, unde proprietas creaturae independentiam non importat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:GA-02","CL_GGVC_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Actualitas est cardo quo esse, perfectio, intelligibilitas, appetibilitas, stabilitas et operatio secundum rationes distinctas conectuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Potentia, compositio, receptio et acquisitio dependentiam implicant; ideo a primo principio removentur, sed in creaturis modos finitudinis et mutationis constituunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GA-03","CL_GGVC_04:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Simplicitas divina non est inopia, sed plenitudo intensiva qua perfectiones effectuum eminenter continentur sine aggregatione partium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GA-04","CL_GGVC_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Participatio causaliter asymmetrica creaturae similitudinem, formam et bonitatem proprias constituit, simul autem dependentiam eius a causa non recepta servat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GA-09","CL_GGVC_03:GA-05","CL_GGVC_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Transcendentia et intimitas non opponuntur: causa quia non recipitur in effectibus eos non componit, quia vero ipsum esse causat omnibus intime adest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:GA-07","CL_GGVC_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Bonitas, infinitas, praesentia, immutabilitas, aeternitas et unitas non sunt accessiones successivae, sed conversiones eiusdem identitatis divinae cum esse sub rationibus diversis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Cognitio primi principii ex effectibus et remotionibus veritatem existentiae atque causalitatis attingit, comprehensionem tamen modi quidditativi non consequitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:GA-09","CL_GGVC_02:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Ordines essendi, intelligendi, causandi, perficiendi et durandi distinguendi sunt; apparentia contradictio saepe ex illegitima horum ordinum contractione oritur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GA-11","CL_GGVC_04:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Deus non est summum membrum generis, temporis, spatii aut numeri communis, sed causa ipsius ordinis quo genera, mensurae, loca et multitudines constituuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_04:AS-08","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-01","text":"Ipsum esse ab obiecto formali doctrinae in actum simplicem, fontem perfectionis, rationem infinitatis, effectum conservatum et fundamentum aeternitatis atque unitatis mutatur, identitate attractoris servata.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_03:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-02","text":"Mediatio a disciplina incipientis ad legem cognitionis humanae migrat, deinde ontologice recodificatur ut participatio qua creatura actualitatem recipit sine identitate cum fonte.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_03:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-03","text":"Actus ab instrumento removendi potentiam in mensuram perfectionis, principium bonitatis, causam praesentiae et radicem immutabilitatis amplificatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_04:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-04","text":"Simplicitas a negatione compositionis transit ad continentiam eminentem perfectionum, identitatem essendi et durationis, ac denique indivisionem quae pluralitatem absolutorum excludit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_04:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-05","text":"Receptio ex limite pedagogico cognoscentis in principium finitudinis ontologicae mutatur: determinat actualitatem creatam, contrahit esse et permanentem dependentiam inducit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-06","text":"Causalitas ab efficientia et exemplaritate ad finalitatem, conservationem et operationem immutabilem extenditur, ita ut origo, praesentia et finis unius dependentiae modi fiant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_03:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-07","text":"Negatio a tutela epistemica contra praedicationem impropriam in operatorium ontologicum mutatur, cuius exclusiones cumulatae attributa positiva primi esse generant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_03:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_04:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-08","text":"Multiplicitas materiarum, nominum, perfectionum, durationum et entium non eliminatur, sed rationibus distinctis distributa in ordinem causalem ad unum principium absorbetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:GA-10","CL_GGVC_02:RMD-09","CL_GGVC_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-01","text":"Distinctio sensuum Scripturae manet machina hermeneutica localis; ad hunc gradum ascendit tantum lex qua multiplicitas significationis fundamento ordinato et non aequivoco continetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-02","text":"Quinque viae singulares non globalizantur; earum reductio a dependentia effectuum ad causam non dependentem fit forma universalis ascensus huius metagregis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-03","text":"Enumeratio modorum compositionis infra manet, dum lex superior persistit qua quidquid partibus, potentia vel causa unionis indiget a primo actu excluditur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-04","text":"Divisiones appetibilis et analysis mali manent determinationes regionales; sursum servantur conversio actualitatis in bonum et subordinatio defectus ad actum quem privat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_02:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-05","text":"Analyses figurae, numeri et successionis non manent catalogus mathematicus, sed praebent regulam qua processus potentialiter indefinitus ab infinitate actuali separatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-06","text":"Praesentia per gratiam manet ramus specialis relationis rationalis; non constituit causalitatem communem, infinitatem aut ubiquitatem, sed eas praesupponit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-07","text":"Unitas aevi manet conclusio conditionalis inferioris gradus; ad structuram praesentem ascendit sola distinctio inter successionem, stabilitatem participatam et aeternitatem absolutam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-01","text":"Systema cognitionem Dei naturaliter immediatam et quidditative comprehensivam excludit, sine eo quod cognitionem realem existentiae et causalitatis dissolvat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-02","text":"Nullum genus ontologicum neutrum Deum et creaturas tamquam membra univoca continet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_03:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-03","text":"Potentia, compositio, receptivitas et perfectio acquirenda a primo principio removentur, quia causalitatem vel actualitatem priorem postularent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_04:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-04","text":"Creatura neque pars vel forma Dei neque ens absolute independens esse potest; participatio absorptionem et autonomiam simul excludit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-05","text":"Spatium, tempus et numerus non sunt receptacula Deo priora, nec praesentia divina extensionem localem aut aeternitas successionem interminatam significat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_04:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-06","text":"Malum positivum, multitudo absolutorum et principia ultima aequalia excluduntur, quia defectus bonum praesupponit et multitudo unitatem aliquam requirit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GNX-07","CL_GGVC_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-07","text":"Causalitas divina et proprietas creaturae non sunt causae competitivae; maior immediatio causae essendi non minuit sed constituit formam propriam effectus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_03:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet accessibilitatem intellectui humano mensuram veritatis esse; aliter excessus obiecti statim in negationem veritatis converteretur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet mediationem veritati extrinsecam aut certitudini contaminantem esse, quia receptio ordinata est modus participationis veritatis, non corruptio fontis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_01:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet simplicitatem vacuitatem significare; sine plenitudine simplici universalitas causalitatis et eminentia perfectionum concidere deberent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet esse commune minimum vel actualitatem axiologice neutralem constitui, quia perfectio et appetibilitas ipsam actualitatem consequuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet causam primam unam causam extrinsecam inter alias esse aut productione initiali absolvi; ipsa causalitas essendi permanentiam effectus sustinet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_03:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet durationem vas autonomum aut unitatem additamentum enti esse; utraque modum essendi exprimit et ontologiam mensurati sequitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_04:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-01","text":"Altissima exclusio est totalitas ontologica Deo prior, intra quam Deus et creaturae coordinate collocarentur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-02","text":"Visio directa quidditatis divinae manet indisponibilis; haec absentia effectus, revelationem, analogiam et remotionem in spatium cognitionis ordinatae cogit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_02:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-03","text":"Inadmissibilis est creatura quae aut extra causalitatem divinam subsistat aut cum essentia divina formaliter identificetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-04","text":"Nulla regio actualis vel possibilis, nullus tertius modus essendi inter participationem et ipsum esse subsistens, extra causalitatem primi principii patet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_03:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-05","text":"Plura principia aequaliter prima et tribunal epistemicum revelationi, rationi et esse divino superordinatum possibilitates systemice vetitae sunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:DBN-06","CL_GGVC_01:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-01","text":"Mediatio epistemica et participatio ontologica sunt formae analogicae eiusdem legis: quod finitum est veritatem vel esse recipit secundum modum proprium, non secundum plenitudinem fontis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-07","CL_GGVC_01:GA-04","CL_GGVC_03:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-02","text":"Receptio est operator communis qui incipientem epistemice, creaturam ontologice et rem mutabilem modaliter finit, dum veritatem, actualitatem et operationem proprias simul possibilitat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-05","CL_GGVC_01:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-03","text":"Exclusionum cumulatio structuram attributivam producit: remotio compositionis, receptionis, successionis et divisionis restituit simplicitatem, infinitatem, immutabilitatem, aeternitatem et unitatem.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-09","CL_GGVC_02:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_04:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-04","text":"Simplicitas et fecunditas causalis directe cohaerent: principium eo plura eminenter continet et communicat quo minus ex perfectionibus receptis componitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-06","CL_GGVC_02:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-05","text":"Non-receptio divini esse distantiam categorialem auget, sed causalitas immediata eiusdem esse distantiam operativam minuit; transcendentia igitur intimitatem causaliter generat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-08","CL_GGVC_02:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_03:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-06","text":"Conservatio continua et immutabilitas primi agentis coniungi possunt tantum si novitas et successio in effectu recepto, non in transitu causae divinae, collocantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-09","CL_GGVC_03:GA-09","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-07","text":"Attributa divina tabulam praedicatorum additorum non constituunt, sed translationes eiusdem simplicis esse inter rationes perfectionis, receptionis, causalitatis, durationis et indivisionis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-11","CL_GGVC_03:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-08","text":"Unitas et dependentia ordinem multitudinis non abolent: creaturae formas proprias retinent, sed earum permanentia et communis coordinatio a primo simplicissimo procedunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-11","CL_GGVC_03:MME-03","CL_GGVC_04:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-01","text":"Ontologia praesentis systematis est participatio verticalis et non competitiva: causa dat esse quo effectus proprie subsistit, non locum ontologicum quem effectus cum causa dividat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-08","CL_GGVC_03:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-02","text":"Epistemologia structuram ontologiae replicat: sicut creatura esse non possidet nisi receptum, ita cognoscens finitus veritatem accipit per media ordinata, non per immediationem absolutam.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-10","CL_GGVC_01:AS-06","CL_GGVC_01:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-03","text":"Attributa divina constituunt coordinatas modales unius actus: bonitas exprimit perfectionem, infinitas non-receptionem, praesentia causalitatem, immutabilitas plenam actualitatem, aeternitas permanentiam et unitas indivisionem.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-09","CL_GGVC_03:MME-01","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-04","text":"Tempus et spatium desinunt esse ambitus communes Deo et creaturis; fiunt modi effectuum receptivorum, successivorum et localiter ordinatorum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-12","CL_GGVC_03:MME-05","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-05","text":"Subiectum humanum fit viator disciplinandus, qui principia recipit, effectus legit, modos praedicationis distinguit et ad causam ascendit sine possessione quidditativa termini.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-10","CL_GGVC_01:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-06","text":"Causalitas primi principii nec serie temporali simplici nec mutatione agentis absolvitur: finis ordinat, efficiens producit, forma constituit et causa immutabilis effectum successivum sustinet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-11","CL_GGVC_02:BSD-07","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-07","text":"Mundus fit simul ordo effectuum realium et campus signorum: dependentia ontologica rerum est ipsa condicio qua cognitio a creaturis ad principium redit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-10","CL_GGVC_01:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-08","text":"Cognitio negativa acquirit vim productivam: quia modos creatos removet, non minus sed aliter determinat principium, custodita tamen incomprehensibilitate modi divini.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-10","CL_GGVC_02:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-01","text":"Effectus ducunt ad causam, causa per remotiones purificatur, attributa causae ex purgatione emergunt, et effectus deinde ut participationes eiusdem causae reinterpretantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_01:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-02","text":"Receptio essendi finitatem generat, finitas dependentiam, dependentia praesentiam conservantem, praesentia permanentiam, permanentia autem receptionem originariam manifestat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_04:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-03","text":"Via actus ascendit ab exclusione potentiae ad plenitudinem perfectionis, inde ad fontem bonitatis et causalitatis, postremo ad immutabilitatem et permanentiam totam simul.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_04:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-04","text":"Via simplicitatis procedit a remotione compositionis ad continentiam eminentem, ab eminentia ad causalitatem universalem et ab indivisione causae ad maximam unitatem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_04:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-05","text":"Circuitus causalis movetur a bono ut fine ad efficientiam, ab efficientia ad formam et actum, atque ab actu perfecto ad bonum appetibile quod circuitum claudit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_03:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-06","text":"Negationes de bonitate non causata, infinitate non quantitativa, praesentia non locali, duratione non successiva et unitate non numerica in actum non receptum convergunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_04:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-07","text":"Attractor omnium circuituum est ipsum esse subsistens, sub quo doctrina, actualitas, perfectio, causalitas, praesentia, durationes et unitates ordinantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_04:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B01","text":"Finis rationem excedens + recipiens finitus → necessitas revelationis et mediationis disciplinatae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:GA-01","CL_GGVC_01:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B02","text":"Effectus sensibiles + esse creatum receptum → dependentia causalis + ascensus ab effectu ad principium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:GA-02","CL_GGVC_01:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B03","text":"Remotio potentiae, compositionis, receptionis et acquisitionis → actus purus et primum esse non causatum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B04","text":"Actus purus + identitas essentiae et esse + simplicitas → continentia eminens perfectionum sine aggregatione.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GA-02","CL_GGVC_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B05","text":"Plenitudo simplicis esse + causalitas efficiens, exemplaris et finalis → participatio, conservatio et praesentia intima in effectibus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:GA-03","CL_GGVC_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B06","text":"Absentia potentiae + absentia successionis + absentia divisionis → immutabilitas + aeternitas tota simul + maxima unitas.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:GA-03","CL_GGVC_04:GA-04","CL_GGVC_04:GA-05","CL_GGVC_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B07","text":"Cognitio causae sine comprehensione quidditativa → reinterpretatio creaturarum ut effectuum, participationum, signorum et viarum reditus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:GA-09","CL_GGVC_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AFM-B01","text":"Impedimenta receptionis → effectus sensibiles → remotio condicionum creatarum → primus actus simplex → explicatio attributorum → ordinatio creaturarum sub causa.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-04","CL_GGVC_02:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_03:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_04:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AFM-B02","text":"Mediatio doctrinae → ascensus ab effectibus → existentia causae → remotiones apophaticae → cognitio analogica → reditus interpretativus ad effectus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AFM-B03","text":"Actualitas → simplicitas → plenitudo bonitatis → non-receptio infinita → praesentia conservans → immutabilitas → aeternitas → unitas.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_04:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AFM-B04","text":"Esse receptum → finitas → forma propria → dependentia continua → permanentia participata → multiplicitas ordinata → reductio ad primum sine absorptione.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:GA-02","CL_GGVC_04:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-01","text":"Pedagogia reclassificatur ut epistemologia operativa: ordo transmissionis fit condicio qua veritas excedens finito intellectui cognoscibilis redditur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-02","text":"Bonitas, infinitas, praesentia, immutabilitas, aeternitas et unitas ex tractatibus separatis in unam topologiam ipsius esse convertuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:MME-01","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-03","text":"Oppositio transcendentiae et immanentiae causalis corruit, quia non-receptio primi actus et immediata causatio esse mutuo se exigunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-04","text":"Oppositio dependentiae et proprietatis creaturae corruit: forma propria non contra causalitatem divinam, sed per participationem eius constituitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-05","text":"Spatium et tempus a receptaculis universalibus in modos effectuum creatorum recodificantur; infinitas et aeternitas ideo neque extensio neque successio sunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:MME-05","CL_GGVC_04:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-06","text":"Unitas et multitudo desinunt esse principia contraria: unum transcendens indivisionem cuiusque entis exprimit, multitudo autem unitates proprias sub ordine communi distribuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:MME-03","CL_GGVC_04:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-07","text":"Lectio valida debet modos praedicationis, ordines prioritatis, rationes causalitatis et respectus mensurae distinguere; oppositio immediata et translatio univoca methodologice excluduntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:MME-08","CL_GGVC_04:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei aguntur constitutio sacrae doctrinae et attributa primi principii, praesertim simplicitas, perfectio, bonitas, infinitas, praesentia, immutabilitas, aeternitas et unitas.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_04:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali actus et potentia, receptio et participatio, causalitas et conservatio, successio et indivisio mechanismum communem constituunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_03:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico systema lectionem reconstituit per distinctionem principiorum concessorum, modorum praedicationis, ordinum causalium et respectuum secundum quos propositiones valent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est ordo ab esse non recepto ad actus receptos, quorum permanentia conservatione, mutatio potentia et successio modo finito essendi reguntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_04:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo forma disputativa ipsam doctrinam exsequitur: obiecta recipit, illationes distinguit, modos creatos removet et reliqua in structuram ampliorem reintegrat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FL-01","text":"Cognitio quidditativa negatur, sed propositiones fortes de identitate essentiae et esse, simplicitate et attributis affirmantur; cohaerentia pendet a discrimine inter veritatem propositionis et comprehensionem modi.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:FL-01","CL_GGVC_01:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FL-02","text":"Analogia et participatio simultatem similitudinis atque excessus sustinent, sed modus positivus communicationis perfectionis non exhaustive formalizatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:FL-03","CL_GGVC_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FL-03","text":"Forma propria creaturae et totalis dependentia eius componuntur per causalitatem non competitivam, cuius ratio constitutiva magis asseritur quam interne explicatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:FL-03","CL_GGVC_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FL-04","text":"Operatio divina effectus novos producit sine mutatione agentis; solutio successionem in effectu collocat, sed amplitudo mutabilitatis extrinsecae commissuram fragilem servat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_04:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FL-05","text":"Revelatio necessaria et demonstrabilitas naturalis divini esse oeconomiam inaequalem constituunt, sed limes inter ministerium rationis et dependentiam conceptualem non ubique determinate figitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:XD-03","CL_GGVC_01:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MP-01","text":"Receptio mutatur a condicione pedagogica incipientis in legem ontologicam qua actualitas creata simul constituitur, contrahitur et dependens fit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:MP-01","CL_GGVC_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MP-02","text":"Actus mutatur ab instrumento exclusionis potentiae in principium commune perfectionis, bonitatis, causalitatis, praesentiae et immobilitatis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MP-03","text":"Simplicitas mutatur a conclusione negativa in densitatem positivam quae perfectiones continet, effectus producit et pluralitatem absolutorum impedit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MP-04","text":"Transcendentia mutatur a separatione apparentemente in condicionem intimitatis causalis, quia principium extra genus effectuum ipsum esse eorum immediate sustentat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:MME-03","CL_GGVC_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MP-05","text":"Duratio et unitas mutantur a quantitate continua et accidente numerico in rationes consequentes modum essendi, stabilitatem et indivisionem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:MME-02","CL_GGVC_04:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:HSI-01","text":"Imago occulta est machina fontis et reditus: esse simplex perfectionem diffundit in actus receptos, qui tamquam effectus et signa cognitionem ad fontem reducunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:HSI-02","text":"Primum principium est centrum extra tabulam quam sustinet: nullo genere, loco, tempore aut numero continetur, sed totum ordinem causaliter mensurat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_04:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:HSI-03","text":"Creatura est nodus receptivus relationum, in quo forma propria, dependentia, permanentia, mutabilitas, unitas et multiplicitas secundum diversos respectus distribuuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:HSI-04","text":"Tempus est successio actus recepti, permanentia est causalitas conservans, aeternitas autem praesentia indivisa cui nihil acquiritur aut amittitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_04:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:HSI-05","text":"Cognoscens est viator qui a compositis et sensibilibus incipit, distinctionibus navigat, modos creatos removet et ad principium redit sine statu spectatoris absoluti.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_04:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de mediatione, actu puro, ipso esse subsistente, receptione creata, participatione, causalitate conservante et indivisione sufficiunt ad nucleum huius metagregis restituendum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_03:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_04:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est identitas primi principii cum esse non recepto, quia ex ea simplicitas, plenitudo, infinitas, praesentia, immutabilitas et unitas ordinate derivantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_03:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_04:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent modus positivus participationis, plena ratio analogiae et nexus quo causalitas divina formas proprias creaturarum constituit sine compositione.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B04","text":"Systema cohaeret per reditum variatum eiusdem legis receptionis et actus, non per seriem linearem attributorum; restitutio hunc motum recursivum servat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_02:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_04:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B05","text":"Reductioni completae resistunt incomprehensibilitas modi divini, praesentia specialis gratiae et unitas aevi conditionaliter stabilita; haec non destruunt nucleum, sed fines legitimos reconstructionis determinant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:MRT-B06","CL_GGVC_03:MRT-B06","CL_GGVC_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T173457323512Z_9440f9a0","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Ggvc L1 01","source_sha256":"3573bf74de8ed7bf9429c3b49ce5c4d4d463bad2c0c250b4a7d16c5f77605168","sha256":"861f160126b21947375c015e56ced8c8bb73dcf5b38c642c113510cb4da7b047","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:34:57.324495+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B01","text":"The central question is how a finite intellect can know a principle beyond genera, composition, and created measure, when that same principle constitutes both the end of cognition and the cause of the knower itself.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-01","CL_GGVC_02:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B02","text":"The global thesis establishes sacred doctrine as an ordered mediation, whose attractor is God as subsistent being itself, from which the divine attributes and the dependence of creatures are derived.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-02","CL_GGVC_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B03","text":"The argument combines architectonic ordering, apophatic removal, distinction of modes of predication, and causally analogical reduction into one machine of cognition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-03","CL_GGVC_02:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_03:BAS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B04","text":"The movement proceeds from impediments of reception and sensible effects to the removal of created conditions, then explicates the attributes of the first act and reinterprets creatures under its causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-04","CL_GGVC_02:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_03:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_04:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B05","text":"The progression is spiral and cumulative: the same being recurs under the aspects of knowledge, act, perfection, non-reception, presence, immutability, duration, and unity, yet at each return acquires a new generative force.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-05","CL_GGVC_02:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_03:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_04:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B06","text":"The entire meta-aggregate is governed by an asymmetry whereby God is ontologically immediate as the cause of being, but is accessible to the human intellect only through revelation, effects, analogy, and removal.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-06","CL_GGVC_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B07","text":"Negation does not terminate in silence, but by removing composition, reception, succession, and division, positively restores simplicity, fullness, infinity, eternity, and unity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_04:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B08","text":"The closure is real but not quidditative: the existence, causality, and attributes of the first principle are known, while the intrinsic mode in which all things are one in simple being remains incomprehended.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-07","CL_GGVC_02:BAS-08","CL_GGVC_03:BAS-08","CL_GGVC_04:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] A supernatural end, excess of the object, and finite receptivity require mediation; the same law of reception unequally orders human cognition and created being.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:GA-01","CL_GGVC_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] God is subsistent being itself, pure act, and the first simple being, to which no received actuality or constitutive distinction is prior.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GA-02","CL_GGVC_03:GA-01","CL_GGVC_04:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Created being is received, contracted to a determinate nature, and dependent on continuous conservation; hence the propriety of the creature does not entail independence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:GA-02","CL_GGVC_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Actuality is the hinge by which being, perfection, intelligibility, desirability, stability, and operation are connected under distinct aspects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Potency, composition, reception, and acquisition imply dependence; therefore they are removed from the first principle, but in creatures constitute modes of finitude and change.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GA-03","CL_GGVC_04:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Divine simplicity is not poverty, but intensive fullness whereby the perfections of effects are contained eminently without aggregation of parts.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GA-04","CL_GGVC_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Causally asymmetric participation constitutes the creature's own likeness, form, and goodness, while at the same time preserving its dependence on the unreceived cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GA-09","CL_GGVC_03:GA-05","CL_GGVC_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Transcendence and intimacy are not opposed: because the cause is not received in effects, it does not compose them; but because it causes being itself, it is intimately present to all.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:GA-07","CL_GGVC_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Goodness, infinity, presence, immutability, eternity, and unity are not successive additions, but conversions of the same divine identity with being under diverse aspects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Knowledge of the first principle from effects and removals attains the truth of existence and causality, yet does not achieve comprehension of the quidditative mode.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:GA-09","CL_GGVC_02:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] The orders of being, understanding, causing, perfecting, and enduring must be distinguished; apparent contradiction often arises from the illegitimate contraction of these orders.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GA-11","CL_GGVC_04:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] God is not the highest member of a genus, time, space, or common number, but the cause of the very order by which genera, measures, places, and multitudes are constituted.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_04:AS-08","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-01","text":"Subsistent being itself is transformed from the formal object of doctrine into simple act, source of perfection, ground of infinity, conserved effect, and foundation of eternity and unity, while the identity of the attractor is preserved.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_03:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-02","text":"Mediation migrates from the discipline of the beginner to the law of human cognition, then is ontologically recodified as participation by which the creature receives actuality without identity with the source.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_03:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-03","text":"Act is amplified from an instrument for removing potency into the measure of perfection, the principle of goodness, the cause of presence, and the root of immutability.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_04:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-04","text":"Simplicity passes from the negation of composition to eminent containment of perfections, identity of being and duration, and finally indivision, which excludes a plurality of absolutes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_04:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-05","text":"Reception is transformed from a pedagogical limit of the knower into the principle of ontological finitude: it determines created actuality, contracts being, and induces permanent dependence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-06","text":"Causality is extended from efficiency and exemplariness to finality, conservation, and immutable operation, so that origin, presence, and end become modes of one dependence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_03:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-07","text":"Negation is transformed from an epistemic safeguard against improper predication into an ontological operator whose cumulative exclusions generate the positive attributes of first being.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_03:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_04:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-08","text":"The multiplicity of matters, names, perfections, durations, and beings is not eliminated, but, distributed under distinct aspects, is absorbed into a causal order directed to one principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:GA-10","CL_GGVC_02:RMD-09","CL_GGVC_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-01","text":"The distinction of the senses of Scripture remains a local hermeneutical machine; at this level only the law ascends whereby multiplicity of meaning is contained by an ordered and non-equivocal foundation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-02","text":"The individual five ways are not globalized; their reduction from dependent effects to a non-dependent cause becomes the universal form of ascent of this meta-aggregate.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-03","text":"The enumeration of modes of composition remains below, while the higher law persists whereby whatever needs parts, potency, or a cause of union is excluded from the first act.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-04","text":"Divisions of the desirable and analyses of evil remain regional determinations; upward there are preserved the conversion of actuality into good and the subordination of defect to the act which it deprives.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_02:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-05","text":"Analyses of figure, number, and succession do not remain a mathematical catalogue, but provide the rule by which a potentially indefinite process is separated from actual infinity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-06","text":"Presence through grace remains a special branch of rational relation; it does not constitute common causality, infinity, or ubiquity, but presupposes them.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-07","text":"The unity of aevum remains a conditional conclusion of a lower level; only the distinction between succession, participated stability, and absolute eternity ascends to the present structure.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes naturally immediate and quidditatively comprehensive knowledge of God, without thereby dissolving real knowledge of existence and causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-02","text":"No neutral ontological genus contains God and creatures as univocal members.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_03:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-03","text":"Potency, composition, receptivity, and perfection to be acquired are removed from the first principle, because they would require prior causality or actuality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_04:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-04","text":"The creature can be neither a part or form of God nor an absolutely independent being; participation simultaneously excludes absorption and autonomy.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-05","text":"Space, time, and number are not receptacles prior to God, nor does divine presence signify local extension or eternity interminable succession.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_04:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-06","text":"Positive evil, a multitude of absolutes, and equal ultimate principles are excluded, because defect presupposes good and multitude requires some unity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GNX-07","CL_GGVC_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-07","text":"Divine causality and the creature's propriety are not competitive causes; greater immediacy of the cause of being does not diminish but constitutes the effect's proper form.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_03:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that accessibility to the human intellect is the measure of truth; otherwise excess of the object would immediately be converted into negation of truth.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that mediation is extrinsic to truth or contaminates certitude, because ordered reception is a mode of participation in truth, not a corruption of the source.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_01:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that simplicity signifies emptiness; without simple fullness, the universality of causality and the eminence of perfections would have to collapse.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that common being is constituted as a minimum or actuality as axiologically neutral, because perfection and desirability follow actuality itself.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the first cause is one extrinsic cause among others or is discharged by initial production; the very causality of being sustains the permanence of the effect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_03:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that duration is an autonomous vessel or unity an addition to being; each expresses a mode of being and follows the ontology of what is measured.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_04:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-01","text":"The highest exclusion is an ontological totality prior to God, within which God and creatures would be coordinately situated.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-02","text":"Direct vision of the divine quiddity remains unavailable; this absence compels effects, revelation, analogy, and removal into the space of ordered cognition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_02:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-03","text":"A creature that either subsists outside divine causality or is formally identified with the divine essence is inadmissible.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-04","text":"No actual or possible region, no third mode of being between participation and subsistent being itself, lies open outside the causality of the first principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_03:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-05","text":"Several equally first principles and an epistemic tribunal superordinate to revelation, reason, and divine being are systemically forbidden possibilities.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:DBN-06","CL_GGVC_01:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-01","text":"Epistemic mediation and ontological participation are analogical forms of the same law: what is finite receives truth or being according to its proper mode, not according to the fullness of the source.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-07","CL_GGVC_01:GA-04","CL_GGVC_03:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-02","text":"Reception is the common operator that finitizes the beginner epistemically, the creature ontologically, and the mutable thing modally, while simultaneously enabling their proper truth, actuality, and operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-05","CL_GGVC_01:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-03","text":"The accumulation of exclusions produces an attributive structure: removal of composition, reception, succession, and division restores simplicity, infinity, immutability, eternity, and unity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-09","CL_GGVC_02:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_04:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-04","text":"Simplicity and causal fecundity directly cohere: the less a principle is composed of received perfections, the more it eminently contains and communicates.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-06","CL_GGVC_02:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-05","text":"The non-reception of divine being increases categorical distance, but the immediate causality of that same being diminishes operative distance; transcendence therefore causally generates intimacy.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-08","CL_GGVC_02:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_03:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-06","text":"Continuous conservation and the immutability of the first agent can be joined only if novelty and succession are located in the received effect, not in the transition of divine causality.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-09","CL_GGVC_03:GA-09","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-07","text":"The divine attributes do not constitute a table of added predicates, but translations of the same simple being among the aspects of perfection, reception, causality, duration, and indivision.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-11","CL_GGVC_03:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-08","text":"Unity and dependence do not abolish the order of multitude: creatures retain their proper forms, but their permanence and common coordination proceed from the first most simple principle.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-11","CL_GGVC_03:MME-03","CL_GGVC_04:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-01","text":"The ontology of the present system is vertical and non-competitive participation: the cause gives the being by which the effect properly subsists, not an ontological place which the effect shares with the cause.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-08","CL_GGVC_03:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-02","text":"Epistemology replicates the structure of ontology: just as the creature possesses being only as received, so the finite knower receives truth through ordered media, not through absolute immediacy.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-10","CL_GGVC_01:AS-06","CL_GGVC_01:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-03","text":"The divine attributes constitute modal coordinates of one act: goodness expresses perfection, infinity non-reception, presence causality, immutability full actuality, eternity permanence, and unity indivision.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-09","CL_GGVC_03:MME-01","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-04","text":"Time and space cease to be common domains of God and creatures; they become modes of receptive, successive, and locally ordered effects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-12","CL_GGVC_03:MME-05","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-05","text":"The human subject becomes a wayfarer to be disciplined, who receives principles, reads effects, distinguishes modes of predication, and ascends to the cause without quidditative possession of the terminus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-10","CL_GGVC_01:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-06","text":"The causality of the first principle is exhausted neither by a simple temporal series nor by a change in the agent: the end orders, the efficient cause produces, form constitutes, and the immutable cause sustains the successive effect.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-11","CL_GGVC_02:BSD-07","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-07","text":"The world becomes at once an order of real effects and a field of signs: the ontological dependence of things is the very condition by which cognition returns from creatures to the principle.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-10","CL_GGVC_01:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-08","text":"Negative knowledge acquires productive force: because it removes created modes, it determines the principle not less but otherwise, while preserving the incomprehensibility of the divine mode.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-10","CL_GGVC_02:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-01","text":"Effects lead to the cause, the cause is purified through removals, the attributes of the cause emerge from purification, and effects are then reinterpreted as participations in that same cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_01:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-02","text":"Reception of being generates finitude, finitude dependence, dependence conserving presence, presence permanence, and permanence in turn manifests original reception.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_04:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-03","text":"The way of act ascends from the exclusion of potency to fullness of perfection, thence to the source of goodness and causality, and finally to immutability and total simultaneous permanence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_04:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-04","text":"The way of simplicity proceeds from removal of composition to eminent containment, from eminence to universal causality, and from indivision of the cause to maximal unity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_04:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-05","text":"The causal circuit moves from good as end to efficiency, from efficiency to form and act, and from perfected act to the desirable good that closes the circuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_03:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-06","text":"Negations concerning uncaused goodness, non-quantitative infinity, non-local presence, non-successive duration, and non-numerical unity converge in unreceived act.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_04:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-07","text":"The attractor of all circuits is subsistent being itself, under which doctrine, actuality, perfection, causality, presence, durations, and unities are ordered.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_04:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B01","text":"End exceeding reason + finite recipient → necessity of revelation and disciplined mediation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:GA-01","CL_GGVC_01:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B02","text":"Sensible effects + received created being → causal dependence + ascent from effect to principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:GA-02","CL_GGVC_01:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B03","text":"Removal of potency, composition, reception, and acquisition → pure act and first uncaused being.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B04","text":"Pure act + identity of essence and being + simplicity → eminent containment of perfections without aggregation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:GA-02","CL_GGVC_02:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B05","text":"Fullness of simple being + efficient, exemplary, and final causality → participation, conservation, and intimate presence in effects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:GA-03","CL_GGVC_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B06","text":"Absence of potency + absence of succession + absence of division → immutability + eternity tota simul + maximal unity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:GA-03","CL_GGVC_04:GA-04","CL_GGVC_04:GA-05","CL_GGVC_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B07","text":"Knowledge of the cause without quidditative comprehension → reinterpretation of creatures as effects, participations, signs, and ways of return.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:GA-09","CL_GGVC_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AFM-B01","text":"Impediments of reception → sensible effects → removal of created conditions → first simple act → explication of attributes → ordering of creatures under the cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:AS-04","CL_GGVC_02:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_03:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_04:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AFM-B02","text":"Mediation of doctrine → ascent from effects → existence of the cause → apophatic removals → analogical knowledge → interpretative return to effects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AFM-B03","text":"Actuality → simplicity → fullness of goodness → infinite non-reception → conserving presence → immutability → eternity → unity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_04:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:AFM-B04","text":"Received being → finitude → proper form → continuous dependence → participated permanence → ordered multiplicity → reduction to the first without absorption.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:GA-02","CL_GGVC_04:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-01","text":"Pedagogy is reclassified as operative epistemology: the order of transmission becomes the condition by which exceeding truth is rendered knowable to a finite intellect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-02","text":"Goodness, infinity, presence, immutability, eternity, and unity are converted from separate treatises into one topology of being itself.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:MME-01","CL_GGVC_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-03","text":"The opposition between transcendence and causal immanence collapses, because the non-reception of the first act and immediate causation of being mutually require one another.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-04","text":"The opposition between dependence and the creature's propriety collapses: proper form is constituted not against divine causality, but through participation in it.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-05","text":"Space and time are recodified from universal receptacles into modes of created effects; infinity and eternity are therefore neither extension nor succession.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:MME-05","CL_GGVC_04:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-06","text":"Unity and multitude cease to be contrary principles: the transcendent one expresses the indivision of each being, while multitude distributes proper unities under a common order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:MME-03","CL_GGVC_04:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-07","text":"A valid reading must distinguish modes of predication, orders of priority, aspects of causality, and respects of measure; immediate opposition and univocal translation are methodologically excluded.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:MME-08","CL_GGVC_04:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the constitution of sacred doctrine and the attributes of the first principle are treated, especially simplicity, perfection, goodness, infinity, presence, immutability, eternity, and unity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_04:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, act and potency, reception and participation, causality and conservation, succession and indivision constitute a common mechanism.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_03:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the system reconstitutes reading through the distinction of conceded principles, modes of predication, causal orders, and respects according to which propositions are valid.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is an order from unreceived being to received acts, whose permanence is governed by conservation, change by potency, and succession by the finite mode of being.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_04:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the disputative form enacts the doctrine itself: it receives objections, distinguishes inferences, removes created modes, and reintegrates what remains into a broader structure.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FL-01","text":"Quidditative knowledge is denied, but strong propositions concerning the identity of essence and being, simplicity, and attributes are affirmed; coherence depends on the distinction between the truth of the proposition and comprehension of the mode.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:FL-01","CL_GGVC_01:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FL-02","text":"Analogy and participation sustain the simultaneity of likeness and excess, but the positive mode of communicating perfection is not exhaustively formalized.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:FL-03","CL_GGVC_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FL-03","text":"The creature's proper form and its total dependence are composed through non-competitive causality, whose constitutive account is asserted more than internally explained.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:FL-03","CL_GGVC_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FL-04","text":"Divine operation produces new effects without change in the agent; the solution locates succession in the effect, but the extent of extrinsic mutability retains a fragile joint.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_04:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:FL-05","text":"Necessary revelation and the natural demonstrability of divine being constitute an unequal economy, but the boundary between the ministry of reason and conceptual dependence is not everywhere determinately fixed.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:XD-03","CL_GGVC_01:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MP-01","text":"Reception is transformed from the pedagogical condition of the beginner into the ontological law by which created actuality is simultaneously constituted, contracted, and rendered dependent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:MP-01","CL_GGVC_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MP-02","text":"Act is transformed from an instrument for excluding potency into the common principle of perfection, goodness, causality, presence, and immobility.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MP-03","text":"Simplicity is transformed from a negative conclusion into a positive density that contains perfections, produces effects, and prevents a plurality of absolutes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MP-04","text":"Transcendence is transformed from apparent separation into the condition of causal intimacy, because the principle beyond the genus of effects immediately sustains their very being.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:MME-03","CL_GGVC_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MP-05","text":"Duration and unity are transformed from continuous quantity and numerical accident into aspects consequent upon the mode of being, stability, and indivision.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:MME-02","CL_GGVC_04:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:HSI-01","text":"The hidden image is a machine of source and return: simple being diffuses perfection into received acts, which as effects and signs lead cognition back to the source.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:HSI-02","text":"The first principle is the center outside the map that it sustains: it is contained by no genus, place, time, or number, but causally measures the whole order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_04:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:HSI-03","text":"The creature is a receptive node of relations, in which proper form, dependence, permanence, mutability, unity, and multiplicity are distributed according to diverse respects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_04:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:HSI-04","text":"Time is the succession of received act, permanence is conserving causality, while eternity is undivided presence to which nothing is acquired or lost.","refs":["CL_GGVC_03:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_04:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:HSI-05","text":"The knower is a wayfarer who begins from composites and sensibles, navigates through distinctions, removes created modes, and returns to the principle without the status of an absolute spectator.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_04:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms concerning mediation, pure act, subsistent being itself, created reception, participation, conserving causality, and indivision suffice to restore the core of this meta-aggregate.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_03:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_04:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the identity of the first principle with unreceived being, because simplicity, fullness, infinity, presence, immutability, and unity are orderly derived from it.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_03:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_04:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B03","text":"The positive mode of participation, the full account of analogy, and the nexus by which divine causality constitutes the proper forms of creatures without composition remain less determined.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B04","text":"The system coheres through the varied return of the same law of reception and act, not through a linear series of attributes; reconstruction preserves this recursive movement.","refs":["CL_GGVC_01:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_02:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_04:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B05","text":"The incomprehensibility of the divine mode, the special presence of grace, and the conditionally established unity of aevum resist complete reduction; these do not destroy the core, but determine the legitimate limits of reconstruction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_02:MRT-B06","CL_GGVC_03:MRT-B06","CL_GGVC_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_01@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_GGVC_L1_01:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Ggvc L1 01","context":"Chapters 01–12 · Prima pars · Prooemium · Prima pars · Quaestio 1 · Prima pars · Quaestio 2 · Prima pars · Quaestio 3 · Prima pars · Quaestio 4 · Prima pars · Quaestio 5 · Prima pars · Quaestio 6 · Prima pars · Quaestio 7 · Prima pars · Quaestio 8 · Prima pars · Quaestio 9 · Prima pars · Quaestio 10 · Prima pars · Quaestio 11"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01","layer":"MC","title":"Ggvc L2 01","order":1,"parents":["MC_GGVC_L1_01","MC_GGVC_L1_02","MC_GGVC_L1_03","MC_GGVC_L1_04"],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L2_01@R001","sha256":"4149d46a336243e858633efdeb3d11cd563d277d2e47ea58d0f8f031dc4e1b76","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo intellectus finitus actum divinum simplicissimum cognoscat, cum ille genera excedat, intrinsecus relationibus personalibus distinguatur et extrinsecus totum ordinem creatum constituat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis Deum ut ipsum esse et unum actum realem constituit, cuius plenitudo sine sui mutatione origines personales, rationes intelligibiles, voluntatem, providentiam et esse participatum sustinet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio purificationem apophaticam, analogiam, distinctionem ordinum et typationem modi significandi coniungit, ut identitas realis servetur sine confusione munerum essentialium, personalium et causalium.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-04","text":"Motus ab effectibus et receptione finita ad causam simplicem ascendit, inde vitam internam per Verbum et Amorem explicat, postremo per providentiam, missionem et creationem ad effectus redit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-05","text":"Progressio spiralis, cumulativa et recursiva est, quia actus, simplicitas, relatio, receptio et communicatio singulis reditibus campum mutant neque identitatem functionis simpliciter repetunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-06","text":"Duo axes asymmetriae totum ordinant: origo distinguit personas aequales sine inferioritate, dependentia autem Deum et creaturam distinguit sine abolitione agentiae creatae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-07","text":"Negatio est operatio generativa: modos creatos removet, falsa genera dissolvit et inde simplicitatem, distinctiones relationis, causalitatem non competitivam atque participationem finitam restituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B07","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-08","text":"Clausura est ontologice ordinata sed epistemice finita: existentia, dependentia, processiones revelatae et finis participantium stabiliuntur, dum modus divinus et decreta singularia comprehensioni vel demonstrationi plenae resistunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B08","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-10","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Deus est ipsum esse subsistens et unus actus realis, cui nulla perfectio recepta, compositio vel actualitas antecedens convenit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Simplicitas omnem compositionem realem excludit, non autem distinctiones rationis, relationes originis aut pluralitatem effectuum quae essentiam non multiplicant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Identitas realis per differentias rationis, relationis, modi significandi et ambitus custoditur; hae differentiae munera essentialia, personalia, notionalia et causalia typant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Actualitas a fundamento essendi et perfectionis in principium cognitionis, voluntatis, processionis, causalitatis, praesentiae et fruitionis amplificatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Plenitudo divina perfectionem communicat sine amissione, indigentia, augmento vel conversione recipientis in fontem.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Receptio et participatio constituunt veritatem, esse, formam, virtutem agendi et elevationem creaturae, simul tamen eius finitudinem et dependentiam servant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Causalitas divina et agentia creata non competunt, quia causa prima ipsum campum essendi et agendi constituit intra quem causa secunda proprie operatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Origo personalis ad intra et operatio essentialis ad extra formaliter distinguuntur: prima personas constituit, altera creaturas communiter producit et conservat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Intelligere et velle sunt unus actus divinus, sed secundum rationes proprias Verbum et Amorem personaliter ordinant atque intelligibilitatem et bonitatem effectuum sustinent.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Apophasis a tutela praedicationis transit in operatorium ontologicum et semanticum quod perfectionem retinet, modum finitum removet et ordines positivos distribuit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Ratio ordinis et origines personales aeternaliter manent, dum novitas, successio, missio et exsecutio in effectibus vel recipientibus temporalibus consistunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Providentia entia, modos necessarios vel contingentes, causas secundas et fines producit sine mutatione causae primae aut uniformi necessitatione effectuum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Ratio naturalis dependentiam, causalitatem essentialem et limites contradictionis attingit, sed origines personales, initium decretum et distributiones singulares revelatione vel voluntate divina terminantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Nullus effectus finitus plenitudinem divinam adaequate repraesentat; ideo pluralitas graduata et mutuo ordinata bonum totius exprimit sine multiplicatione fontis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-15","text":"[Attractor] Unitas sine confusione et distinctio sine divisione est regula terminalis qua personae, rationes, causae et participationes ordinantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-01","text":"[Identitas stabilis] Actus purus manet invarians per attributionem, processionem, creationem et fruitionem: nihil recipit, acquirit aut successive perficit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-02","text":"[Translatio] Receptio migrat a limite epistemico ad constitutionem ontologicam, agentiam causatam et elevationem supernaturalem, retenta semper inadaequatione recipientis ad fontem.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-03","text":"[Inversio] Simplicitas, initio negatio compositionis et obiectio contra pluralitatem, fit plenitudo positiva quae rationes, relationes et effectus sine divisione ordinat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-04","text":"[Mutatio] Relatio transit ab asymmetria causali ad oppositionem originis et subsistentiam personalem, deinde in missione et creatione statum non univocum recipit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-05","text":"[Dislocatio] Processio ab origine immanente ad missionem temporaliter terminatam et emanationem universalis esse migrat, sed identitatem genericam inter hos ordines amittit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-06","text":"[Amplificatio] Intellectus ab accessu creato et mensuratione veri ad auto-intellectionem divinam, processionem Verbi, expressionem creaturarum et fruitionem beatificam extenditur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-07","text":"[Amplificatio] Causalitas ab ascensu epistemico per effectus ad productionem esse, modalitatis, ordinis providentialis, missionis et agentiae secundae dilatatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-08","text":"[Mutatio] Negatio a remotione defectuum localium in machinam constitutivam transit, quae attributa, personas, relationes, tempora et fines positive reordinat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-09","text":"[Dislocatio] Tempus ab ambitu praesupposito ad successionem effectuum, exsecutionem providentiae et innovationem recipientis transfertur, dum aeternitas extra seriem temporalem manet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-10","text":"[Absorptio] Multiplicitas attributorum, rationum, personarum et creaturarum in plenitudinem ordinantem absorbetur, non ut differentia abolita sed ut distinctio distributa.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-01","text":"Distinctiones hermeneuticae et ordines doctrinae manent machinae locales; ad gradum praesentem ascendit lex qua veritas excedens per mediationem ordinatam recipitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-02","text":"Tractationes singularum perfectionum non globalizantur ut series attributorum; earum functio in topologiam modalem unius actus simplicis convertitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-02","MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-03","text":"Taxonomiae idearum, luminis, errorum et potentiarum infra manent, dum lex receptionis secundum modum recipientis totam cognitionem et participationem ordinat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-04","text":"Nomina singularia Patris, Verbi et Imaginis sunt determinationes inferiores; hic earum actio in architecturam communem originis, relationis et intelligibilitatis creaturalis amplificatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-05","text":"Difficultates lexicales circa Spiritum, Amorem et Donum locales manent; sursum persistit grammatica analogica quae praedicata secundum ordinem ontologicum typat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-06","text":"Divisiones voluntatis, electionis et salutis singularis non fiunt axiomata globalia; ascendit tantum structura elevationis gratuita quae naturam perficit sine absorptionе.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-07","text":"Disputationes de initio durationis et signis missionum infra manent; praesentem gradum constituunt dependentia totius esse et translatio novitatis in effectum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-08","text":"Motus localis, accidens, materia et individuum materiale obsoleta fiunt ut mensurae directaе divini; opus eorum negativum in regulam mutationis generis quaestionis absorbetur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-01","text":"Nullum genus vel mensura univoca Deum, personas, creaturas, aeternitatem et tempus tamquam membra coordinate continet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-02","text":"Potentia passiva, compositio, accidens et perfectio acquisita a primo actu removentur, etiam cum relationes et actiones reales affirmantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-03","text":"Causalitas prima et secunda non sunt vires competitivae, neque libertas creata independentiam ab ordine causali universali significat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-05","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-04","text":"Formae, veritates, materia, receptaculum vel tertium ontologicum extra Deum non praebent campum antecedentem cognitioni aut creationi.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-05","text":"Malum, falsitas, casus et chaos non obtinent actualitatem positivam vel principium primum causae divinae aemulum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-06","text":"Processio personalis, missio temporalis et creatio universalis sub uno genere derivationis univocae comprehendi non possunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-07","text":"Creatura neque beatitudinem, gratiam vel filiationem autonome possidet neque ius antecedens ad finem supernaturalem vindicat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-08","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-08","text":"Spatium, tempus, materia et metatempus non sunt receptacula causae primae, sed condiciones vel effectus intra ordinem creatum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet accessibilitatem intellectui finito mensuram veritatis esse aut cognitionem veram comprehensionem exhaustivam postulare.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet simplicitatem vacuitatem vel indistinctionem absolutam significare; aliter plenitudo causalis et relationes personales simul corruerent.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem distinctionem intelligibilem vel realem compositionem absolutorum exigere, quia rationes et oppositiones relativae alios distinctionis modos constituunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-02","MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet dependentiam totalem causae secundae proprietatem, libertatem vel imputabilitatem eius delere.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet causalitatem successionem temporalem vel mutationem causae requirere; novitas in effectu collocatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-04","MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet cognitionem, permissionem vel ordinationem defectus causalitatem positivam eiusdem defectus inducere.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-07","text":"Falsum esse oportet structuram metaphysicam dependentiae decreta contingentia, initium temporale aut distributiones singulares necessario determinare.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-06","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-01","text":"Systema excludit totalitatem ontologicam vel locum iudicialem Deo priorem, intra quem causa prima et effectus commensurari possint.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-02","text":"Altissima absentia ontologica est tertius modus inter ipsum esse subsistens et esse participatum; nullum medium independentem dependentia creaturalis relinquit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-03","text":"Nulla regio intelligibilium, norma veri aut tribunal epistemicum supra Deum rationes rerum vel legitimam communicationem perfectionis mensurat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-04","text":"Creatura quae extra causalitatem consistat vel communicationem divinam in possessionem autonomam convertat est possibilitas systemice inadmissibilis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-05","text":"Effectus singularis qui bonitatem divinam exhaustive repraesentet vel actualitas creata quae amplitudinem divinam metiatur excluduntur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-06","text":"Indisponibilis manet prospectus naturalis qui quidditatem divinam, origines personales et locum singularis creaturae in ordine aeterno simul possideat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-01","text":"Mediatio epistemica et participatio ontologica sunt specificationes unius legis receptionis asymmetricae: finitum veritatem, esse vel operationem recipit secundum modum proprium, non secundum plenitudinem fontis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-02","text":"Articulatio interna actus simplicis et explicatio externa effectuum sunt ordines conexi sed non successivi: processiones constituunt personas, operatio communis autem creaturas producit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-03","text":"Apophasis et grammatica analogica componunt operatorium transscalare quod eandem perfectionem servat, modos finitos removet et statum praedicati secundum ordinem ontologicum mutat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-04","text":"Non-receptio divini esse transcendentiam constituit, sed eadem plenitudo per causalitatem et Donum intimitatem creaturalem producit; distantia categorialis fit condicio praesentiae non localis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-05","text":"Communitas operationis externae simul fundat cognitionem naturalem causae essentialis et impedit ne distinctiones personarum ex effectibus communibus demonstrentur; missio eas manifestat, non constituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-06","text":"Creatio, providentia et causalitas secunda formant architecturam inclusivam: causa prima esse et modos agendi instituit, causae proximae autem effectus necessarios, contingentes vel liberos proprie efficiunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-07","text":"Aeternitas, origines personales et ratio providentiae non sunt tempora priora, sed principia sine successione quorum exsecutio, innovatio et consummatio in effectibus temporalibus distribuuntur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-08","text":"Negativum habet efficaciam epistemicam, moralem et ordinativam, sed non autonomiam ontologicam: privatio, falsitas et culpa semper positivo subiecto vel bono ordine innituntur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-09","text":"Gratia, beatitudo, filiatio et Donum eandem structuram elevationis participant: creatura actu proprio Deum attingit, dum exemplar, virtus et terminus fruitionis ab ipsa non oriuntur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-10","text":"Intelligibilitas universalis ordinis singularia decreta non exhaurit: scientia rationes possibilium aperit, sed actualitas, initium et distributio concreta voluntate libera terminantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-10","MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-01","text":"Realitas ultima est actualitas simplex intrinsecus relationibus originis articulata et extrinsecus per creationem, providentiam et participationem communicata.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas non est series impulsuum intra receptaculum commune, sed topologia asymmetrica quae effectui esse, modum et propriam virtutem agendi tribuit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-03","text":"Subiectum duplicem formam recipit: persona divina est relatio subsistens, creatura autem receptaculum activum cui proprietas et imputabilitas per dependentiam constituuntur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-04","text":"Tempus est modus successionis et exsecutionis creatae, non continuum commune inter Deum et mundum; aeternitas temporalitatem causat quin per eam transeat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-05","text":"Epistemologia structuram ontologiae repetit: sicut creatura esse participat, ita cognoscens veritatem per effectus, analogiam, revelationem et distinctiones typatas recipit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-06","text":"Libertas et contingentia sunt perfectiones modales effectuum, non lacunae causalitatis primae; dependentia intensior propriam operationem constituit potius quam extinguit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-07","text":"Cognitio negativa fit constructiva, quia exclusiones cumulatae non tantum errores removent sed topologiam attributorum, relationum, temporum et participationum producunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-08","text":"Revelatio non complet simpliciter catalogum cognitionis naturalis, sed creationem, providentiam, missionem et finem creaturae secundum origines personales denuo ordinat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-07","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-09","text":"Pluralitas mundi est repraesentatio distributa plenitudinis simplicis: inadaequatio singulorum gradationem postulat, mutua autem ordinatio differentias in bonum totius colligit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-01","text":"Effectus cognitionem causae generant; causa per remotionem purificatur; attributa purificata effectus ut participationes et signa eiusdem causae denuo interpretantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-02","text":"Simplicitas compositionem excludit; exclusio relationes originis postulat; relationes distinctionem sine divisione efficiunt; distinctio sic simplicitatem confirmat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-03","text":"Auto-intellectio divina Verbum producit; Verbum creaturas intelligibiliter exprimit; creaturae intellectum finitum mensurant; cognitio participata ad fontem intelligibilem redit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-02","MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-04","text":"Amor procedens fit Donum; Donum missionem ordinat; receptio inhabitationem et fruitionem efficit; reditus creaturae originem manifestat sed causaliter non constituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-05","text":"Esse receptum finitatem, finitas dependentiam, dependentia conservationem et operatio conservata agentiam propriam generat, quae ad causam essendi refertur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-06","text":"Providentia modos causarum praeparat; causae secundae secundum modos proprios agunt; eventus infallibilitatem ordinis manifestant sine necessitate uniformi.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-05","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-07","text":"Inadaequatio effectus pluralitatem generat; pluralitas gradus distribuit; gradatio complementum totius producit; totum vicissim partes secundum bonum commune mensurat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-06","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-08","text":"Attractor omnium circuituum est plenitudo actualis, simpliciter una, relationaliter distincta et participabiliter communicata, circa quam metaphysica, semantica, providentia et teleologia convergunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-07","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B01","text":"Effectus finitus + cognoscens receptivus → mediatio doctrinalis et ascensus causaliter analogicus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B02","text":"Perfectio manifestata → separatio rationis formalis a modo materiali, passivo et temporali → purgatio apophatica.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-03","MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B03","text":"Perfectio purificata + exclusio receptionis et compositionis → identificatio cum actu simplici et ipso esse subsistente.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B04","text":"Intelligere et velle ut actiones immanentes → processiones non temporales Verbi et Amoris.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B05","text":"Processio immanens → oppositio originis → relatio subsistens → persona distincta sine divisione essentiae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B06","text":"Operatio essentialis communis → rationes rerum + voluntas actualizans + providentia modalis → creatio et conservatio ordinis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:AFM-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B07","text":"Esse et virtus recepta → forma propria + causa secunda + actus liber → elevatio ad finem supernaturalem sine identitate naturae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B08","text":"Participatio finita → inadaequatio singularis → pluralitas graduata → complementum partium → unus mundus ordinatus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B07","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B09","text":"Cognitio effectuum + revelatio originum + participatio Doni → reditus creaturae ad fontem sine comprehensione, reciprocatione causali aut absorptionе.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B07","MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:AFM-B01","text":"Effectus creati → quaestio de causa → purgatio modorum finitorum → actus simplex → attributio analogica → reinterpretatio effectuum ut participationum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:AFM-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_02:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:AFM-B02","text":"Actus simplex → auto-intellectio et voluntas → Verbum et Amor → relationes subsistentes → personae distinctae → operatio externa communis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:AFM-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:AFM-B03","text":"Operatio communis → creatio totius esse → providentia modaliter distincta → causae secundae → missio et gratia → actus proprius creaturae → fruitio finis divini.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:AFM-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-01","text":"Unum et multa desinunt esse contraria quantitativa: unitas pertinet ad plenitudinem fontis, pluralitas ad relationes originis vel participationes finitas.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-02","text":"Transcendentia et intimitas causalis non amplius opponuntur, quia non-receptio primi actus est ipsa condicio praesentiae conservantis et inhabitationis gratuitaе.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-03","text":"Causalitas reclassificatur a conflictu efficientiarum in ordinem inclusivum quo causa prima esse, modos et virtutes causarum secundarum constituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-04","text":"Persona reclassificatur ab individuo absoluto in relationem subsistentem originis, dum creatura fit nodus receptivus et activus participationis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-05","text":"Tempus reclassificatur a receptaculo universali in modum effectuum et exsecutionis, cui aeternitas causaliter adest sine continuatione temporali.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-06","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-06","text":"Negativum reclassificatur in operationes distinctas—remotionem, privationem, permissionem et imputabilitatem—quarum efficacia non importat positivam autonomiam defectus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-07","text":"Ratio et revelatio non constituunt fontes competitores: ratio structuram dependentiae et congruentiae administrat, revelatio origines personales et decreta contingentia aperit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-08","text":"Dependentia et libertas desinunt esse termini inversi, quia agentia finita eo realior est quo forma, modus et virtus operandi causaliter constituuntur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:MP-04","MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei tractantur cognitio et attributa Dei, providentia, processiones, relationes, personae, missiones, creatio, tempus, gratia et finis creaturae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:ALT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali actus et potentia, receptio et participatio, origo et relatio, causalitas et conservatio atque aeternitas et successio machinam communem constituunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:ALT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico systema quaestiones mutat, falsas mensuras generis vel ambitus removet et terminos inter causam primam, causas secundas, relationes internas et effectus externos relocat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est actus aeternus relationaliter distinctus, a quo esse participatum, modi eventuum et successio creaturalis dependent.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:ALT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo argumentum ipsum recipit objectiones, distinguit ambitus, removet modos improprios et differentias in unitatem non aequatricem restituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-01","text":"Cognitio quidditativa negatur, sed propositiones fortes de essentia, actu, relationibus et processionibus affirmantur; cohaerentia pendet a discrimine inter veritatem attributionis et comprehensionem modi.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:FL-01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-02","text":"Unus actus realis plures rationes et duas processiones sustinere debet; distinctio rationis tantum pondus generativum fert ut eius status ontologicus commissura fragilis maneat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-03","text":"Relatio idem esse cum essentia habet et tamen oppositione originis distinguitur; identitas essendi neque oppositionem absorbere neque naturam dividere debet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-02","MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-04","text":"Causalitas prima totalis cum libertate, culpa et imputabilitate componitur per asymmetriam boni causati et defectus permissi, cuius medium causale non exhaustive formalizatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-04","MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-05","text":"Operatio ad extra communis est, sed missio personaliter distribuitur et Verbum creaturas exprimit; nexus inter causalitatem communem et mediationem personalem delicatus manet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-06","text":"Sapientia et bonitas ordinem universalem explicant, sed initium actuale, electio singularis et distributio formarum in voluntate non ulterius demonstrata terminantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-07","text":"Analogia similitudinem realem cum absentia mensurae univocae coniungit, sed proportio positiva inter exemplar infinitum et actum finitum reductioni plenae resistit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-06","MC_GGVC_L1_03:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-01","text":"Simplicitas mutatur a conclusione negativa contra compositionem in plenitudinem communicativam quae distinctiones relationis et pluralitatem effectuum sustinet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:MP-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-02","text":"Relatio mutatur ab asymmetria causalitatis externae in oppositionem internam et subsistentiam personalem, deinde in missione et creatione statum analogicum recipit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-03","text":"Intellectus mutatur ab attributo essentiali et mensura veri in processionem Verbi, expressionem creaturarum et actum beatificum participatum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-04","text":"Processio mutatur ubi ab origine interna ad missionem temporaliter terminatam et creationem universalis esse transfertur, amissa ratione univoca derivationis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-05","text":"Praesentia mutatur a causalitate conservante et mutua inexistentia personarum in inhabitationem cognitivam, amativam et fruitivam recipientis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-06","text":"Creatio mutatur ab eventu imaginato intra tempus in relationem totalis dependentiae, dum quaestio originis ex nihilo a decreto initii temporalis distinguitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-07","text":"Negatio mutatur a censura praedicationis in vim formalem quae attributa, relationes, personas et fines secundum proprios ordines generat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-05","MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina centri non spatialis: actus simplex intrinsecus relationes in personas densat et extrinsecus campum creatum sine receptaculo antecedente producit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-02","text":"Persona divina est nodus subsistens originis, non atomus absolutus; creatura est nodus receptivus et activus qui formam et virtutem participat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L1_01:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est linea effectuum circa fontem aeternum explicata, in qua novitas, actio et consummatio succedunt sine transitu vel incremento principii.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-04","text":"Cognoscens est viator qui effectus legit, distinctionibus navigat, modos finitos removet et ad principium redit sine perspectiva absoluta.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:HSI-05","MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-05","text":"Figura dynamica totius est fluxus non reciproce causal: plenitudo per Verbum et Donum communicatur, creatura autem per cognitionem, amorem et actionem participatam redit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de actu simplici, distinctione typata, relatione originis, participatione, causalitate non competitiva, providentia et communicatione plenitudinis sufficiunt ad nucleum praesentis gradus restituendum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus inter ipsum esse non receptum, unitatem actus, distinctiones relationis et productionem effectuum quorum proprietas per dependentiam constituitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent proportio analogica, efficacia distinctionum rationis, nexus causalitatis communis cum missionibus personalibus, libertas sub causalitate totali et ratio distributionum singularium.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:MRT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B04","text":"Systema cohaeret non per seriem linearem tractatuum, sed per reditum variatum actus, receptionis, relationis et communicationis, singulis reditibus ordine ontologico mutato.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat si ascensum ab effectibus, articulationem intradivinam et descensum per providentiam, Donum atque creationem distinguit; incomprehensibilitas modi divini et contingentia decreti reductioni necessariae resistunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T174239159045Z_27020fbc","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Ggvc L2 01","source_sha256":"4149d46a336243e858633efdeb3d11cd563d277d2e47ea58d0f8f031dc4e1b76","sha256":"b8e4cd87cdbe626e60257988156a2f79cdfd15b81bbb76e308bd5d554b0f8900","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:42:39.159977+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how a finite intellect knows the utterly simple divine act, since it exceeds genera, is internally distinguished by personal relations, and externally constitutes the whole created order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis establishes God as subsistent being itself and one real act, whose fullness, without change in itself, sustains personal origins, intelligible reasons, will, providence, and participated being.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-03","text":"The argument combines apophatic purification, analogy, distinction of orders, and typification of the mode of signifying, so that real identity is preserved without confusion of essential, personal, and causal functions.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-04","text":"The movement ascends from effects and finite reception to the simple cause, thence explains inner life through Word and Love, and finally returns through providence, mission, and creation to effects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-05","text":"The progression is spiral, cumulative, and recursive, because act, simplicity, relation, reception, and communication change their field at each return and do not simply repeat an identity of function.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-06","text":"Two axes of asymmetry order the whole: origin distinguishes equal persons without inferiority, whereas dependence distinguishes God and creature without abolishing created agency.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-07","text":"Negation is a generative operation: it removes created modes, dissolves false genera, and thereby restores simplicity, distinctions of relation, non-competitive causality, and finite participation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B07","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-08","text":"The closure is ontologically ordered but epistemically finite: existence, dependence, revealed processions, and the end of participants are established, while the divine mode and singular decrees resist full comprehension or demonstration.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:AS-B08","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-10","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] God is subsistent being itself and one real act, to which no received perfection, composition, or antecedent actuality belongs.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Simplicity excludes every real composition, but not distinctions of reason, relations of origin, or a plurality of effects that do not multiply the essence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Real identity is maintained through differences of reason, relation, mode of signifying, and scope; these differences typify essential, personal, notional, and causal functions.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Actuality is extended from the foundation of being and perfection into a principle of knowledge, will, procession, causality, presence, and fruition.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Divine fullness communicates perfection without loss, need, increase, or conversion of the recipient into a source.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Reception and participation constitute the creature's truth, being, form, power of acting, and elevation, while at the same time preserving its finitude and dependence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Divine causality and created agency do not compete, because the first cause constitutes the very field of being and acting within which the second cause properly operates.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Personal origin ad intra and essential operation ad extra are formally distinguished: the former constitutes persons, the latter commonly produces and preserves creatures.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Understanding and willing are one divine act, but according to their proper reasons they personally order Word and Love and sustain the intelligibility and goodness of effects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Apophasis passes from safeguarding predication into an ontological and semantic operator that retains perfection, removes finite mode, and distributes positive orders.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] The reason of order and personal origins remain eternally, while novelty, succession, mission, and execution reside in effects or temporal recipients.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Providence produces beings, necessary or contingent modes, second causes, and ends without change of the first cause or uniform necessitation of effects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Natural reason reaches dependence, essential causality, and the limits of contradiction, but personal origins, decreed beginning, and singular distributions are delimited by revelation or divine will.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] No finite effect adequately represents divine fullness; therefore a graded and mutually ordered plurality expresses the good of the whole without multiplication of the source.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-15","text":"[Attractor] Unity without confusion and distinction without division is the terminal rule by which persons, reasons, causes, and participations are ordered.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Pure act remains invariant through attribution, procession, creation, and fruition: it receives, acquires, or is successively perfected by nothing.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-02","text":"[Translation] Reception migrates from an epistemic limit to ontological constitution, caused agency, and supernatural elevation, while the recipient's inadequation to the source is always retained.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-03","text":"[Inversion] Simplicity, initially a negation of composition and an objection against plurality, becomes a positive fullness that orders reasons, relations, and effects without division.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-04","text":"[Mutation] Relation passes from causal asymmetry to opposition of origin and personal subsistence, then assumes a non-univocal status in mission and creation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-05","text":"[Displacement] Procession migrates from immanent origin to temporally terminated mission and emanation of universal being, but loses generic identity among these orders.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-06","text":"[Amplification] Intellect is extended from created access and measurement of the true to divine self-understanding, procession of the Word, expression of creatures, and beatific fruition.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-07","text":"[Amplification] Causality is expanded from epistemic ascent through effects to the production of being, modality, providential order, mission, and secondary agency.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-08","text":"[Mutation] Negation passes from the removal of local defects into a constitutive engine that positively reorders attributes, persons, relations, times, and ends.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-09","text":"[Displacement] Time is transferred from a presupposed scope to the succession of effects, the execution of providence, and the innovation of the recipient, while eternity remains outside the temporal series.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-10","text":"[Absorption] The multiplicity of attributes, reasons, persons, and creatures is absorbed into ordering fullness, not as abolished difference but as distributed distinction.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:RMD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-01","text":"Hermeneutical distinctions and orders of doctrine remain local engines; at the present level arises the law by which transcendent truth is received through ordered mediation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:CGD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-02","text":"Treatments of individual perfections are not globalized as a series of attributes; their function is converted into a modal topology of one simple act.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-02","MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-03","text":"Taxonomies of ideas, light, errors, and powers remain below, while the law of reception according to the mode of the recipient orders all knowledge and participation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-04","text":"The individual names of Father, Word, and Image are lower determinations; here their action is amplified into a common architecture of origin, relation, and created intelligibility.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-05","text":"Lexical difficulties concerning Spirit, Love, and Gift remain local; above persists the analogical grammar that typifies predicates according to ontological order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-06","text":"Divisions of will, choice, and individual salvation do not become global axioms; only the structure of gratuitous elevation rises, which perfects nature without absorption.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-07","text":"Disputes over the beginning of duration and the signs of missions remain below; the present level is constituted by the dependence of all being and the translation of novelty into effect.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-08","text":"Local motion, accident, matter, and the material individual become obsolete as direct measures of the divine; their negative work is absorbed into the rule of changing the genus of the question.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-01","text":"No genus or univocal measure contains God, persons, creatures, eternity, and time as coordinate members.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-02","text":"Passive potency, composition, accident, and acquired perfection are removed from the first act, even when real relations and actions are affirmed.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-03","text":"First and second causality are not competing powers, nor does created freedom signify independence from the universal causal order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-05","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-04","text":"Forms, truths, matter, a receptacle, or a third ontological reality outside God do not provide a field antecedent to knowledge or creation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-05","text":"Evil, falsity, chance, and chaos do not possess positive actuality or a first principle rivaling divine causality.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-06","text":"Personal procession, temporal mission, and universal creation cannot be comprehended under one genus of univocal derivation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-07","text":"The creature neither possesses beatitude, grace, or filiation autonomously nor claims an antecedent right to the supernatural end.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-08","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-08","text":"Space, time, matter, and metatime are not receptacles of the first cause, but conditions or effects within the created order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GNX-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that accessibility to finite intellect is the measure of truth, or that true knowledge requires exhaustive comprehension.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that simplicity signifies emptiness or absolute indistinction; otherwise causal fullness and personal relations would collapse together.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:DBN-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that every intelligible distinction requires real composition of absolutes, because reasons and relative oppositions constitute other modes of distinction.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-02","MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that the total dependence of a second cause destroys its proper character, freedom, or imputability.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that causality requires temporal succession or change of the cause; novelty is located in the effect.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-04","MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that knowledge, permission, or ordering of a defect induces positive causality of that same defect.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-07","text":"It must be false that the metaphysical structure of dependence necessarily determines contingent decrees, temporal beginning, or singular distributions.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-06","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-01","text":"The system excludes an ontological totality or judicial place prior to God, within which the first cause and effects could be commensurated.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-02","text":"The highest ontological absence is a third mode between subsistent being itself and participated being; no independent medium remains for created dependence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-03","text":"No region of intelligibles, norm of truth, or epistemic tribunal above God measures the reasons of things or the legitimate communication of perfection.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-04","text":"A creature existing outside causality or converting divine communication into autonomous possession is a systemically inadmissible possibility.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-05","text":"A singular effect that exhaustively represents divine goodness, or a created actuality that measures divine amplitude, is excluded.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-06","text":"There remains unavailable a natural prospect that simultaneously possesses the divine quiddity, personal origins, and the place of an individual creature in the eternal order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-01","text":"Epistemic mediation and ontological participation are specifications of one law of asymmetric reception: the finite receives truth, being, or operation according to its proper mode, not according to the fullness of the source.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-02","text":"The internal articulation of the simple act and the external explication of effects are connected but non-successive orders: processions constitute persons, whereas common operation produces creatures.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-03","text":"Apophasis and analogical grammar compose a trans-scalar operator that preserves the same perfection, removes finite modes, and changes the status of the predicate according to ontological order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-04","text":"The non-reception of divine being constitutes transcendence, but the same fullness produces created intimacy through causality and Gift; categorical distance becomes the condition of non-local presence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_01:XD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-05","text":"The community of external operation simultaneously grounds natural knowledge of the essential cause and prevents personal distinctions from being demonstrated from common effects; mission manifests them, not constitutes them.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-06","text":"Creation, providence, and secondary causality form an inclusive architecture: the first cause institutes being and modes of acting, while proximate causes properly effect necessary, contingent, or free effects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-07","text":"Eternity, personal origins, and the rationale of providence are not prior times, but principles without succession whose execution, innovation, and consummation are distributed in temporal effects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-08","text":"The negative has epistemic, moral, and ordering efficacy, but not ontological autonomy: privation, falsity, and fault always rest upon a positive subject or good order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-09","text":"Grace, beatitude, filiation, and Gift share the same structure of elevation: the creature attains God by its own act, while the exemplar, power, and term of fruition do not arise from it.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-10","text":"Universal intelligibility of order does not exhaust singular decrees: knowledge discloses the reasons of possibles, but actuality, beginning, and concrete distribution are delimited by free will.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-10","MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-01","text":"Ultimate reality is simple actuality internally articulated by relations of origin and externally communicated through creation, providence, and participation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-02","text":"Causality is not a series of impulses within a common receptacle, but an asymmetric topology that bestows upon the effect being, mode, and its proper power of acting.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-03","text":"Subject receives a double form: a divine person is a subsistent relation, whereas a creature is an active receptacle whose proper character and imputability are constituted through dependence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-04","text":"Time is the mode of created succession and execution, not a continuum common to God and the world; eternity causes temporality without passing through it.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-05","text":"Epistemology repeats the structure of ontology: as the creature participates being, so the knower receives truth through effects, analogy, revelation, and typified distinctions.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-06","text":"Freedom and contingency are modal perfections of effects, not gaps in first causality; more intense dependence constitutes rather than extinguishes proper operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-07","text":"Negative knowledge becomes constructive, because cumulative exclusions not only remove errors but produce the topology of attributes, relations, times, and participations.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:BSD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-08","text":"Revelation does not simply complete the catalogue of natural knowledge, but reorders creation, providence, mission, and the creature's end according to personal origins.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-07","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-09","text":"The plurality of the world is a distributed representation of simple fullness: the inadequation of singulars requires gradation, while mutual ordering gathers differences into the good of the whole.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-01","text":"Effects generate knowledge of the cause; the cause is purified through removal; purified attributes reinterpret effects anew as participations and signs of the same cause.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-02","text":"Simplicity excludes composition; exclusion requires relations of origin; relations effect distinction without division; distinction thus confirms simplicity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-03","text":"Divine self-understanding produces the Word; the Word intelligibly expresses creatures; creatures measure finite intellect; participated knowledge returns to the intelligible source.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-02","MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-04","text":"Proceeding Love becomes Gift; Gift orders mission; reception effects indwelling and fruition; the creature's return manifests origin but does not causally constitute it.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-05","text":"Received being generates finitude, finitude dependence, dependence conservation, and conserved operation proper agency, which is referred to the cause of being.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:RLE-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-06","text":"Providence prepares the modes of causes; second causes act according to their proper modes; events manifest the infallibility of order without uniform necessity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-05","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-07","text":"The inadequation of the effect generates plurality; plurality distributes grades; gradation produces the complement of the whole; the whole in turn measures the parts according to the common good.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-06","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-08","text":"The attractor of all circuits is actual fullness, simply one, relationally distinct, and communicable by participation, around which metaphysics, semantics, providence, and teleology converge.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-07","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B01","text":"Finite effect + receptive knower → doctrinal mediation and causally analogical ascent.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B02","text":"Manifested perfection → separation of formal reason from material, passive, and temporal mode → apophatic purification.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-03","MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B03","text":"Purified perfection + exclusion of reception and composition → identification with simple act and subsistent being itself.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B04","text":"Understanding and willing as immanent actions → non-temporal processions of Word and Love.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B05","text":"Immanent procession → opposition of origin → subsistent relation → distinct person without division of essence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B06","text":"Common essential operation → reasons of things + actualizing will + modal providence → creation and conservation of order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:AFM-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B07","text":"Received being and power → proper form + second cause + free act → elevation to the supernatural end without identity of nature.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B08","text":"Finite participation → singular inadequation → graded plurality → complement of parts → one ordered world.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B07","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B09","text":"Knowledge of effects + revelation of origins + participation in Gift → return of the creature to the source without comprehension, causal reciprocation, or absorption.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:FGS-B07","MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:AFM-B01","text":"Created effects → question concerning cause → purification of finite modes → simple act → analogical attribution → reinterpretation of effects as participations.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:AFM-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_02:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:AFM-B02","text":"Simple act → self-understanding and will → Word and Love → subsistent relations → distinct persons → common external operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:AFM-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:AFM-B03","text":"Common operation → creation of all being → modally distinct providence → second causes → mission and grace → proper act of the creature → fruition of the divine end.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:AFM-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-01","text":"One and many cease to be quantitative contraries: unity belongs to the fullness of the source, plurality to relations of origin or finite participations.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-02","text":"Transcendence and causal intimacy no longer stand opposed, because the non-reception of the first act is itself the condition of conserving presence and gratuitous indwelling.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:MME-03","MC_GGVC_L1_01:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-03","text":"Causality is reclassified from a conflict of efficiencies into an inclusive order in which the first cause constitutes the being, modes, and powers of second causes.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-04","text":"Person is reclassified from an absolute individual into a subsistent relation of origin, while the creature becomes a receptive and active node of participation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-05","text":"Time is reclassified from a universal receptacle into a mode of effects and execution, to which eternity is causally present without temporal continuation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-06","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-06","text":"The negative is reclassified into distinct operations—removal, privation, permission, and imputability—whose efficacy does not entail the positive autonomy of defect.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-07","text":"Reason and revelation do not constitute competing sources: reason administers the structure of dependence and congruity, revelation discloses personal origins and contingent decrees.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-08","text":"Dependence and freedom cease to be inverse terms, because finite agency is more real insofar as form, mode, and power of operating are causally constituted.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:MP-04","MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level are treated knowledge and attributes of God, providence, processions, relations, persons, missions, creation, time, grace, and the creature's end.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:ALT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level act and potency, reception and participation, origin and relation, causality and conservation, and eternity and succession constitute the common machinery.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:ALT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level the system changes questions, removes false measures of genus or scope, and relocates boundaries among the first cause, second causes, internal relations, and external effects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level reality is an eternal act relationally distinct, upon which participated being, modes of events, and created succession depend.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:ALT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level the argument itself receives objections, distinguishes scopes, removes improper modes, and restores differences to non-equalizing unity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-01","text":"Quidditative knowledge is denied, yet strong propositions about essence, act, relations, and processions are affirmed; coherence depends on the distinction between truth of attribution and comprehension of mode.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:FL-01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-02","text":"One real act must sustain multiple reasons and two processions; distinction of reason alone bears such generative weight that its ontological status remains a fragile joint.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-03","text":"Relation has the same being as essence and yet is distinguished by opposition of origin; identity of being must neither absorb opposition nor divide nature.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-02","MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-04","text":"Total first causality is composed with freedom, fault, and imputability through the asymmetry of caused good and permitted defect, whose causal middle is not exhaustively formalized.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-04","MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-05","text":"Operation ad extra is common, but mission is personally distributed and the Word expresses creatures; the nexus between common causality and personal mediation remains delicate.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-06","text":"Wisdom and goodness explain universal order, but actual beginning, singular choice, and distribution of forms terminate in will not further demonstrated.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-07","text":"Analogy combines real likeness with the absence of a univocal measure, but the positive proportion between infinite exemplar and finite act resists full reduction.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-06","MC_GGVC_L1_03:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-01","text":"Simplicity changes from a negative conclusion against composition into communicative fullness that sustains distinctions of relation and plurality of effects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:MP-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-02","text":"Relation changes from the asymmetry of external causality into internal opposition and personal subsistence, then assumes analogical status in mission and creation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-03","text":"Intellect changes from an essential attribute and measure of truth into the procession of the Word, expression of creatures, and participated beatific act.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-04","text":"Procession changes when it is transferred from internal origin to temporally terminated mission and creation of universal being, the univocal rationale of derivation being lost.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-05","text":"Presence changes from conserving causality and mutual inexistence of persons into cognitive, loving, and fruitive indwelling in the recipient.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-06","text":"Creation changes from an event imagined within time into a relation of total dependence, while the question of origin from nothing is distinguished from the decree of temporal beginning.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-07","text":"Negation changes from a censure of predication into a formal power that generates attributes, relations, persons, and ends according to their proper orders.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-05","MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a machine of a non-spatial center: simple act internally condenses relations into persons and externally produces the created field without an antecedent receptacle.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-02","text":"A divine person is a subsistent node of origin, not an absolute atom; a creature is a receptive and active node that participates form and power.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L1_01:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-03","text":"Time is a line of effects unfolded around the eternal source, in which novelty, action, and consummation succeed without passage or increase of the principle.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-04","text":"The knower is a traveler who reads effects, navigates distinctions, removes finite modes, and returns to the principle without absolute perspective.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:HSI-05","MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-05","text":"The dynamic figure of the whole is a non-reciprocally causal flow: fullness is communicated through Word and Gift, while the creature returns through knowledge, love, and participated action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B01","text":"Axioms concerning simple act, typified distinction, relation of origin, participation, non-competitive causality, providence, and communication of fullness suffice to restore the nucleus of the present level.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_02:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the nexus between non-received being itself, unity of act, distinctions of relation, and production of effects whose proper character is constituted through dependence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remain analogical proportion, the efficacy of distinctions of reason, the nexus of common causality with personal missions, freedom under total causality, and the rationale of singular distributions.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:MRT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B04","text":"The system coheres not through a linear series of treatises, but through the varied return of act, reception, relation, and communication, with ontological order changed at each return.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_01:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B05","text":"A reconstruction preserves the true movement if it distinguishes ascent from effects, intradivine articulation, and descent through providence, Gift, and creation; the incomprehensibility of the divine mode and the contingency of decree resist necessary reduction.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L2_01@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_GGVC_L2_01:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Ggvc L2 01","context":"Chapters 01–48 · Prima pars · Prooemium · Prima pars · Quaestio 1 · Prima pars · Quaestio 2 · Prima pars · Quaestio 3 · Prima pars · Quaestio 4 · Prima pars · Quaestio 5 · Prima pars · Quaestio 6 · Prima pars · Quaestio 7 · Prima pars · Quaestio 8 · Prima pars · Quaestio 9 · Prima pars · Quaestio 10 · Prima pars · Quaestio 11 · Prima pars · Quaestio 12 · Prima pars · Quaestio 13 · Prima pars · Quaestio 14 · Prima pars · Quaestio 15 · Prima pars · Quaestio 16 · Prima pars · Quaestio 17 · Prima pars · Quaestio 18 · Prima pars · Quaestio 19 · Prima pars · Quaestio 20 · Prima pars · Quaestio 21 · Prima pars · Quaestio 22 · Prima pars · Quaestio 23 · Prima pars · Quaestio 24 · Prima pars · Quaestio 25 · Prima pars · Quaestio 26 · Prima pars · Quaestio 27 · Prima pars · Quaestio 28 · Prima pars · Quaestio 29 · Prima pars · Quaestio 30 · Prima pars · Quaestio 31 · Prima pars · Quaestio 32 · Prima pars · Quaestio 33 · Prima pars · Quaestio 34 · Prima pars · Quaestio 35 · Prima pars · Quaestio 36 · Prima pars · Quaestio 37 · Prima pars · Quaestio 38 · Prima pars · Quaestio 39 · Prima pars · Quaestio 40 · Prima pars · Quaestio 41 · Prima pars · Quaestio 42 · Prima pars · Quaestio 43 · Prima pars · Quaestio 44 · Prima pars · Quaestio 45 · Prima pars · Quaestio 46 · Prima pars · Quaestio 47"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02","layer":"MC","title":"Ggvc L1 02","order":2,"parents":["CL_GGVC_05","CL_GGVC_06","CL_GGVC_07","CL_GGVC_08"],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_02@R001","sha256":"0db21c9ed31ea0192b56dbcc6442e0edcf5e1464fdb3f08d9584aefd55fbc950","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo unus Deus simplicissimus intellectui creato cognoscibilis sit atque ordinem intelligibilem, moralem et salutarem producat sine compositione, passione aut dependentia.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:AS-B01","CL_GGVC_08:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis essentiam, intellectum, veritatem, vitam, voluntatem et amorem in uno actu realiter identificat, distinctiones autem in rationibus, relationibus et effectibus distribuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GA-03","CL_GGVC_07:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio perfectiones creatas formaliter retinet, modos finitos removet atque attributa purificata in causas ipsarum perfectionum invertit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:AS-B03","CL_GGVC_08:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-04","text":"Motus duplicem directionem ordinat: creatura ab effectibus per cognitionem et nominationem ascendit, dum intellectus divinus per rationes, voluntatem, amorem et providentiam ad effectus descendit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_06:BAS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-05","text":"Progressio spiralis est, quia unus actus successive ut mensura veri, principium vitae, causa modalitatis et architectura providentiae explicatur, singulis amplificationibus structuram priorem servantibus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:AS-05","CL_GGVC_08:BAS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-06","text":"Asymmetria creatoris et creaturae totum motum regit: Deus mensurat et causat, creatura recipit et vere agit, sed neque comprehensione neque independentia fontem adaequat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:GA-07","CL_GGVC_08:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-07","text":"Falsum, malum et defectus intra ordinem positivum recipiuntur ut privationes cognitae, permissae vel imputatae, numquam ut principia exemplaria aut causae primae aemulae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_07:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-08","text":"Clausura architectonica in bonitate et voluntate divina consistit, sed transitus a scientia ad effectum, distributio singularis gratiae et commissura inter permissionem defectus atque culpam explicative incompleta manent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:XD-07","CL_GGVC_08:BAS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Perfectio creaturaliter manifestata Deo attribuitur secundum rationem formalem purificatam, non secundum modum materialem, passivum, compositum aut temporalem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:GA-01","CL_GGVC_05:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Essentia, esse, intelligere, vivere, velle et amare in Deo unum actum realem constituunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GA-03","CL_GGVC_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Simplicitas divina non excludit pluralitatem rationum, nominum, volitorum et bonorum, sed eam extra compositionem fontis ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GA-07","CL_GGVC_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Causalitas intelligibilis asymmetrica est: intellectus divinus rem mensurat et causat, res intellectum creatum mensurat, creatura autem receptum iudicat et exsequitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:GA-05","CL_GGVC_08:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Receptio secundum modum recipientis constituit simul veritatem cognitionis finitae et proprietatem agentiae creatae sine adaequatione aut independentia.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GA-01","CL_GGVC_07:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Causalitas divina non solum esse rerum, sed intelligibilitatem, modos eventuum, ordinem causarum, gradus boni et ordinationem supernaturalem producit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GA-02","CL_GGVC_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Ratio ordinis aeternaliter in Deo manet, dum novitas, successio et exsecutio in effectibus temporalibus distribuuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GA-03","CL_GGVC_05:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Contingentia, necessitas et libertas sunt modi effectuum ab infallibili causalitate prima instituti, non limitationes externas illius causalitatis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GA-08","CL_GGVC_08:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Malum, falsitas et privatio cognosci vel ordinari possunt tantum per ens, verum et bonum quibus dependent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:GA-12","CL_GGVC_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Misericordia seu donatio prima constituit naturam, finem et debitum intra quae iustitia postea operatur; nulla lex creata Deum antecedenter mensurat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GA-05","CL_GGVC_08:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Gratia potentiam creatam elevat ad visionem vel salutem naturae inattingibilem, finitudinem tamen, libertatem et distinctionem ontologicam non abolet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GA-05","CL_GGVC_08:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Creatura eidem actui divino diversis habitudinibus subest: ut intellecta rationem accipit, ut volita in esse determinatur, ut amata bonitatem recipit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:GA-13","CL_GGVC_07:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-13","text":"[Mutating] Apophasis non silentium terminale est, sed operatio constitutiva quae perfectionem servat, modum creatum removet et attributionem analogicam restituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_05:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Participatio perfectionem, cognitionem et agentiam realem creaturae constituit sine identitate cum fonte aut multiplicatione naturae divinae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] Intellectus divinus possibilia et rationes continet, sed actualitas effectuum et distributio singularis bonorum scientiam ad voluntatem terminant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:XD-07","CL_GGVC_08:BAS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-01","text":"Simplicitas ab apparenti limite pluralitatis in principium generativum rationum, effectuum, modorum et ordinum mutatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_07:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-02","text":"Ratio intellecta migrat ab exemplari rei ad mensuram veritatis, inde ad obiectum voluntatis et tandem ad rationem providentialis exsecutionis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_08:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-03","text":"Veritas a conformitate cognitionis creatae ad identitatem primae mensurae transit atque postremo in conformitatem historicam eventus cum modo proviso amplificatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:MP-02","CL_GGVC_08:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-04","text":"Causalitas a via epistemica effectuum ad productionem entis, modalitatis, bonitatis et ordinis salutaris extenditur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_07:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-05","text":"Participatio a conformitate exemplati ad operationem propriam causae secundae et ad dignitatem medii providentialis transfertur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_08:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-06","text":"Libertas vim invertit: ab apparentia exterioritatis ad causam primam fit modus contingens agentiae ab ipsa causa prima institutae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_08:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-07","text":"Defectus a falsitate cognoscitiva ad privationem ontologicam, permissionem cosmologicam et culpam moralem imputabilem migrat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_08:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-08","text":"Tempus ab ordine cognitionis et denominationis creatae in campum universalem exsecutionis rationis aeternae amplificatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_08:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-09","text":"Misericordia ab universali communicatione perfectionis ad donationem specialem gratiae transit, causalitatem tamen debiti antecedentem retinens.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_08:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-01","text":"Taxonomiae graduum luminis, idearum, errorum et generum entium manent instrumenta localia; sursum persistunt tantum leges receptionis, dependentiae et distinctionis modorum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_06:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-02","text":"Distinctiones locales obiecti, potentiae, medii et conceptionis amplificantur in legem generalem qua omnis participatio modum recipientis sequitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_05:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-03","text":"Idea et veritas gradum inferiorem excedunt ubi exemplar divinum, mensuratio rei et obiectivitas cognitionis creatae unam directionem causalem constituunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:CGD-06","CL_GGVC_06:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-04","text":"Vita, voluntas et amor non manent tractatus paralleli, sed rationes quibus exemplar intelligibile in effectum modaliter et bonitative determinatum convertitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_07:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-05","text":"Providentia fit commissura praesentis gradus, quia rationem aeternam, exsecutionem temporalem, causalitatem secundam et ordinem salutis colligit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_08:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-06","text":"Veritas ut nomen locale iustitiae evanescit postquam eius functio in conformitate eventus cum ratione et modo proviso absorpta est.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RMD-10","CL_GGVC_08:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-07","text":"Divisiones signorum voluntatis, comparationes particularium dilectionum et numerus electorum determinationes locales manent, non fundamenta metagregis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_08:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-08","text":"Remotio, permissio et imputatio defectus unitates inferiores coniungunt, quia negativum secundum diversos ordines causalitatis formaliter redistribuunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_07:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-01","text":"Nulla mensura communis vel superior creatoris et creaturae cognitionem, causalitatem aut iustitiam fundat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-02","text":"Formae ideales, veritates aeternae et rationes ordinis extra intellectum divinum subsistentes excluduntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-03","text":"Praedicata neque univoce transferuntur neque propter transcendentiam in silentium absolutum dissolvuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_07:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-04","text":"Pluralitas rationum, cognitorum, volitorum et relationum nullam compositionem aut mutationem in Deo inducit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-05","text":"Causalitas divina et creata non sunt vires competitivae quarum augmentum alteram minuat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-06","text":"Casus absolutus et libertas ut independentia a causa universali ontologice excluduntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_08:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-07","text":"Purum non ens, malum positivum et defectus primae causae aemulus nullam autonomiam obtinent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_08:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-08","text":"Natura rationalis nullum ius, debitum aut sufficientiam intrinsecam ad visionem et finem supernaturalem possidet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:DBN-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet veritatem cognitionis aut nominis comprehensionem exhaustivam rei postulare.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem distinctionem rationis distinctionem realem in primo intellectu constituere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet dependentiam causae secundae propriam eius agentiam vel libertatem destruere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_08:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet infallibilitatem causae primae necessitatem uniformem effectibus imponere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet cognitionem privationis vel mali causalitatem positivam eiusdem defectus inducere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet mensuram meriti, iuris aut aequalitatis donationem primam et distributionem gratuitorum antecedere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-07","text":"Falsum esse oportet causas temporales decretum aeternum constituere, mutare aut corrigere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-01","text":"Nullus locus ontologicus extra ordinem divinum praesto est ex quo causalitas, veritas aut iustitia prima iudicetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-02","text":"Nulla regio intelligibilium supra vel extra Deum rationes rerum aut normas veri continet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-03","text":"Absentia commensurationis creatoris et creaturae totam structuram analogiae, participationis et elevationis ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-04","text":"Chaos sine ratione divina et defectus positivus primae causae aemulus possibilitates inadmissibiles sunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_06:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-05","text":"Nulla mensura ulterior identitatem singularium electorum vel reprobatorum demonstrat; regressus in voluntate divina sistit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-06","text":"Creaturae indisponibilis est prospectus simul quidditativus essentiae divinae et certus proprii loci in ordine aeterno.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_08:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-01","text":"Unus actus simplex per intellectum rationes constituit, per voluntatem effectus determinat, per amorem bonum communicat et per providentiam ordinem temporaliter exsequitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-12","CL_GGVC_07:GA-13","CL_GGVC_08:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-02","text":"Exemplar, veritas, volitio et providentia non sunt architecturae separatae, sed gradus directionales quibus ratio divina fit res mensurata et effectus ordinatus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-15","CL_GGVC_06:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_08:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-03","text":"Descensus causalitatis et ascensus cognitionis circuitum asymmetricum constituunt: Deus rem per rationem causat, res intellectum creatum mensurat, cognitio autem ad causam redit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-05","CL_GGVC_05:XD-03","CL_GGVC_06:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-04","text":"Lex receptionis et lex agentiae dependentis eandem structuram exprimunt: finitum vere cognoscit vel agit quia modum proprium recipit, non quia a fonte separatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-14","CL_GGVC_05:GA-01","CL_GGVC_07:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-05","text":"Veritas causalis in veritatem providentialem amplificatur, quia effectus rationi divinae respondet non tantum secundum esse, sed secundum modum necessarium, contingentem vel liberum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-08","CL_GGVC_08:XD-08","CL_GGVC_08:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-06","text":"Elevatio beatifica et ordinatio salutaris eandem grammaticam gratiae participant: potentia creata ad finem inattingibilem perducitur sine abolitione naturae recipientis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-14","CL_GGVC_05:XD-06","CL_GGVC_08:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-07","text":"Falsitas, malum et culpa unam structuram negativam variant: singula fundamento positivo indigent, sed diversa ratione cognoscuntur, permittuntur vel imputantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-13","CL_GGVC_06:XD-06","CL_GGVC_07:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-08","text":"Donatio prima simul ordinem naturae et campum debiti constituit; ideo misericordia iustitiam non suspendit, sed conditiones eius causaliter antecedit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-11","CL_GGVC_08:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_08:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-09","text":"Asymmetria qua bonum totaliter causatur, culpa autem creaturae imputatur, ordinem providentialem servat sed medium inter derelictionem et actum culpabilem fragile relinquit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-10","CL_GGVC_08:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_08:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-10","text":"Scientia rationes et possibilia explicat, sed sola voluntas actualitatem et distributionem singularem terminat; intelligibilitas universalis igitur clausuram voluntariam non eliminat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-15","CL_GGVC_05:XD-07","CL_GGVC_08:XD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-01","text":"Realitas fit ordo causalitatum intelligibilium inclusarum, in quo unum principium rationes, entia, modos et fines distinguit sine sui divisione.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_08:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-02","text":"Obiectivitas neque a mente creata neque a re solitaria oritur, sed a participatione qua res intellectui divino conformatur et intellectum creatum mensurat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-03","text":"Subiectum creatum est receptaculum activum: mensuratur, elevatur et causatur, sed simul iudicat, agit atque pro actu proprio imputabilitatem suscipit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_08:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-04","text":"Tempus est extensio exsecutiva ordinis aeterni, non mensura communis intra quam causa divina deliberet, discat aut mutetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_08:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-05","text":"Contingentia et libertas non sunt residua deficientis causalitatis, sed effectus modales quos causa prima efficaciter producit per causas proximas.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_08:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-06","text":"Negativum potest formalem efficaciam epistemicam, moralem vel iuridicam habere, quamvis ontologice a subiecto positivo et ordine boni dependeat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:BSD-07","CL_GGVC_08:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-07","text":"Gratia demonstrat perfectionem creatam augeri posse per dependentiam intensiorem: praesentia finis potentiam transformat sine conversione recipientis in fontem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GA-05","CL_GGVC_08:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-08","text":"Architectura generalis ordinis valde intelligibilis est, sed assignatio singularis gratuitorum non ex eadem architectura deducitur et in voluntate simplici terminatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:BSD-08","CL_GGVC_08:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-01","text":"Manifestatio creata rationem formalem suggerit; apophasis imperfectionem removet; attributio divina rationem restituit; causalitas manifestationem ut effectum reinterpretatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-02","text":"Effectus cognitionem causae parit, cognitio nomen format, nomen ad causam corrigitur, causa autem effectus secundum propriam rationem producit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_06:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-03","text":"Cognitio divina rationes possibilium explicat, voluntas terminos actualizat, amor bonum communicat, providentia autem ordinem effectuum per media temporalia complet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:GA-13","CL_GGVC_08:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-04","text":"Simplicitas pluralitatem primo minari videtur, deinde eam intentionaliter et causaliter ordinat, postremo ad unum actum reductam distinctiorem restituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_06:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-05","text":"Providentia causas diversorum modorum praeparat, causae effectus secundum modos proprios efficiunt, effectus autem infallibilitatem ordinis manifestant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-06","text":"Circuitus negativus a positivo incipit, defectum per privationem determinat, cognitionem vel ordinationem eius servat atque autonomiam defectus iterum excludit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_07:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-07","text":"Donum constituit naturam et finem, natura et finis debitum constituunt, debitum iustitiam format, iustitia autem ad gratuitatem primam reducitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-08","text":"Dilectio gratiam ordinat, gratia actum liberum et meritum producit, meritum ad gloriam transit, gloria ordinem aeternum explet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-09","text":"Bonitas divina attractor est qui cognitionem, causalitatem, misericordiam, iustitiam et diversitatem effectuum in unum principium colligit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-10","text":"Reprobatio attractor instabilis est in quo permissio, derelictio, culpa et poena conveniunt sine reductione ad causalitatem univocam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RLE-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B01","text":"Manifestationes et effectus creati → cognitio initialis perfectionum → regressus ad rationes formales.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:AS-03","CL_GGVC_05:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B02","text":"Ratio formalis extracta → remotio modi materialis, passivi et finiti → perfectio analogice purificata.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:FGS-B02","CL_GGVC_05:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B03","text":"Perfectio purificata → identificatio cum essentia simplici → exclusio accidentis et receptionis in Deo.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:FGS-B04","CL_GGVC_05:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B04","text":"Essentia se intelligens → modi propriae imitabilitatis → rationes plurium rerum sine pluralitate reali fontis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:FGS-B01","CL_GGVC_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B05","text":"Rationes intellectae → termini voliti → bona amata → actualitas effectuum distincta ab amplitudine possibilium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:GA-13","CL_GGVC_07:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B06","text":"Actualitas effectuum → constitutio naturarum, modorum et finium → communicatio causalitatis secundis agentibus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GA-02","CL_GGVC_08:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B07","text":"Res mensurata → receptio et actio creata → iudicium, meritum, oratio et alia media temporalis exsecutionis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:FGS-B05","CL_GGVC_08:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B08","text":"Defectus apparens → distinctio cogniti, causati, permissi et imputati → clausura asymmetrica in providentia, praedestinatione et reprobatione.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:FGS-B07","CL_GGVC_08:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:AFM-B01","text":"Effectus creatus → cognitio causae → nominatio analogica → correctio apophatica → elevatio recipientis → praesentia finis sine comprehensione.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_05:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:AFM-B02","text":"Essentia se intelligens → rationes rerum → voluntas actualizans → amor bonum communicans → effectus modaliter distincti → providentia exsecutiva.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_07:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:AFM-B03","text":"Donum primum → natura et debitum → iustitia et misericordia → causae secundae → gratia, actus liber et meritum → gloria vel defectus permissive ordinatus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_08:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-01","text":"Attributa divina reclassificantur ut rationes formales unius actus causaliter fecundi, non ut habitus aut operationes realiter successivae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:MME-01","CL_GGVC_07:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-02","text":"Simplicitas desinit significare uniformitatem sterilem et fit principium pluralitatis intelligibilis, modalitatis et bonitatis ordinatae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_07:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-03","text":"Oppositio subiecti et obiecti mutatur in structuram mensurantis, rei participatae et subiecti mensurati quod simul causa secunda fit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:MME-03","CL_GGVC_08:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-04","text":"Veritas ab adaequatione iudicii ad conformitatem ontologicam et historicam rei cum ratione divina amplificatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:MME-01","CL_GGVC_08:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-05","text":"Causalitas reclassificatur ut productio entis cum proprio modo agendi, non ut impulsus univocus qui agentiam proximam minuat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:MME-04","CL_GGVC_07:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-06","text":"Tempus reclassificatur ut campus exsecutionis et successionis effectuum, non ut ambitus mutationis primi principii.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_05:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-07","text":"Negativum reclassificatur in falsitatem, privationem, culpam, derelictionem et poenam, quarum nulla eandem causalitatem positivam recipit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:MME-04","CL_GGVC_08:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-08","text":"Debitum et iustitia transferuntur ab externa mensura Dei ad relationes internas naturae et finis, dum gratuita supra omne debitum antecedens manent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:MME-02","CL_GGVC_08:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei aguntur cognitio et nominatio Dei, ideae et veritas, vita et voluntas, providentia, libertas, gratia atque distributio salutis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_06:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali omnia per machinam mensurationis asymmetricae et causalitatum inclusarum ordinantur, cuius centrum est actus simplex.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico oppositiones solvuntur relocando terminos inter rationem et modum, causam primam et secundam, aeternitatem et exsecutionem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_06:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est participatio modaliter distincta atque exsecutio temporalis rationis aeternae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo argumentum ipsum purificat, distinguit, redistribuit et reducit, ita formam hierarchicam quam affirmat actu efficiens.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_08:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-01","text":"Pluralitas rationum propriarum rerum distinctionem intelligibilem robustam requirit, quamvis omnis pluralitas realis in Deo negetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-02","text":"Cognitio et nominatio positiva essentiae divinae cum ignorantia quidditativa atque visione immediata non comprehensiva cohaerere debent; medium huius cohaerentiae compresse manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:FL-02","CL_GGVC_05:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-03","text":"Veritas aeterna et certitudo providentiae contingentiam et libertatem affirmant, sed mechanismus interior non-necessitationis plene demonstratus non est.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:FL-06","CL_GGVC_08:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-04","text":"Causalitas prima totalis cum culpa imputabili et reprobatione permissiva componitur per asymmetriam cuius medium causale fragile manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:FL-03","CL_GGVC_08:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-05","text":"Sapientia et bonitas structuram universalem ordinis explicant, sed identitatem singularium electorum ad voluntatem sine ratione ulteriore remittunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:FL-01","CL_GGVC_08:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MP-01","text":"Species mutatur a repraesentatione creata exclusa in praesentiam obiectivam essentiae et tandem in identitatem essentiae cum ratione intelligendi divina.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MP-02","text":"Veritas mutatur a conformitate iudicii in identitatem primae mensurae atque inde in conformitatem eventus cum modo providentialiter statuto.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:MP-02","CL_GGVC_08:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MP-03","text":"Causalitas mutatur ubi cognitio possibilium ad voluntatem determinatricem transit et perfectio intrinseca in ordinem effectuum exteriorum convertitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:FL-04","CL_GGVC_07:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MP-04","text":"Libertas mutatur ab independentia apparenti in modum contingentem causalitatis secundae intra providentiam constitutae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_08:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MP-05","text":"Misericordia mutatur ab universali communicatione perfectionis in donationem supernaturalem, dum iustitia ab ordine debitorum ad legitimam inaequalitatem gratuitorum transit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_08:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:HSI-01","text":"Realitas imaginatur ut architectura concentrica cuius centrum simplex rationes intelligibiles emittit et per ramos causales temporaliter exsequitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_08:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum creatum est receptaculum elevabile et causa secunda causata: recipit formam, modum et virtutem, sed propria operatione respondet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_08:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est circumferentia exsecutiva circa centrum aeternum, in qua conformitates, actiones et eventus variantur sine variatione rationis primae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_08:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga extra ordinem, sed ascensus per effectus et actio per media causalia, distinctionibus apophaticis errorem coercente.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_08:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de actu simplici, participatione, mensuratione asymmetrica, causalitate modaliter productiva, gratia et providentia sufficiunt ad structuram principalem huius metagregis restituendam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_08:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est transitus ab uno actu intelligibili ad pluralitatem rationum, effectuum et causarum secundarum sine multiplicatione fontis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_08:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MRT-B03","text":"Nondum satis determinantur distinctio rationum in Deo, immediatio visionis finitae, non-necessitatio libertatis, causalitas culpae et ratio singularis electionis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_08:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat si repetitiones ut amplificationes functionales, negationes ut structuras positivas et asymmetrias ut principia generativa retinentur; voluntas singulariter distribuens plenae reductioni adhuc resistit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_08:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T173549075884Z_7c8641ce","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Ggvc L1 02","source_sha256":"0db21c9ed31ea0192b56dbcc6442e0edcf5e1464fdb3f08d9584aefd55fbc950","sha256":"447571e561164b0192585ead0f8c07b8c72c8180eae4a3e8c0fd1cd63babec45","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:35:49.076834+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how the one most simple God is knowable to created intellect and produces an intelligible, moral, and salvific order without composition, passivity, or dependence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:AS-B01","CL_GGVC_08:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis really identifies essence, intellect, truth, life, will, and love in one act, while distributing distinctions among notions, relations, and effects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GA-03","CL_GGVC_07:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-03","text":"The argument formally retains created perfections, removes finite modes, and converts purified attributes into causes of those perfections themselves.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:AS-B03","CL_GGVC_08:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-04","text":"The movement orders a double direction: the creature ascends from effects through cognition and naming, while the divine intellect descends to effects through notions, will, love, and providence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_06:BAS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-05","text":"The progression is spiral, because the one act is successively unfolded as the measure of truth, the principle of life, the cause of modality, and the architecture of providence, each amplification preserving the prior structure.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:AS-05","CL_GGVC_08:BAS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-06","text":"The asymmetry of creator and creature governs the entire movement: God measures and causes; the creature receives and truly acts, yet equals the source neither by comprehension nor by independence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:GA-07","CL_GGVC_08:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-07","text":"Falsehood, evil, and defect are received within the positive order as privations known, permitted, or imputed, never as exemplary principles or rival first causes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_07:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BAS-08","text":"The architectural closure consists in divine goodness and will, but the transition from knowledge to effect, the singular distribution of grace, and the junction between permission of defect and guilt remain explanatorily incomplete.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:XD-07","CL_GGVC_08:BAS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] A perfection manifested creaturely is attributed to God according to its purified formal notion, not according to its material, passive, composite, or temporal mode.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:GA-01","CL_GGVC_05:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Essence, being, understanding, living, willing, and loving constitute one real act in God.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GA-03","CL_GGVC_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Divine simplicity does not exclude a plurality of notions, names, objects willed, and goods, but orders it outside composition in the source.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GA-07","CL_GGVC_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Intelligible causality is asymmetrical: the divine intellect measures and causes the thing, the thing measures created intellect, while the creature judges and executes what it has received.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:GA-05","CL_GGVC_08:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Reception according to the mode of the recipient simultaneously establishes the truth of finite cognition and the propriety of created agency without adequation or independence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GA-01","CL_GGVC_07:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Divine causality produces not only the being of things, but intelligibility, modes of events, the order of causes, degrees of goodness, and supernatural ordination.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GA-02","CL_GGVC_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] The notion of order remains eternally in God, while novelty, succession, and execution are distributed in temporal effects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GA-03","CL_GGVC_05:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Contingency, necessity, and freedom are modes of effects instituted by infallible first causality, not external limitations of that causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GA-08","CL_GGVC_08:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Evil, falsehood, and privation can be known or ordered only through the being, truth, and goodness on which they depend.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:GA-12","CL_GGVC_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Mercy, or first donation, establishes nature, end, and what is due, within which justice subsequently operates; no created law antecedently measures God.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GA-05","CL_GGVC_08:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Grace elevates created power to a vision or salvation unattainable by nature, yet does not abolish finitude, freedom, or ontological distinction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GA-05","CL_GGVC_08:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] The creature is subject to the same divine act under diverse respects: as understood it receives a notion, as willed it is determined in being, as loved it receives goodness.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:GA-13","CL_GGVC_07:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-13","text":"[Mutating] Apophasis is not terminal silence, but a constitutive operation that preserves perfection, removes the created mode, and restores analogical attribution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_05:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Participation establishes the creature's real perfection, cognition, and agency without identity with the source or multiplication of the divine nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] Divine intellect contains possibilities and notions, but the actuality of effects and the singular distribution of goods bring knowledge to completion in will.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:XD-07","CL_GGVC_08:BAS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-01","text":"Simplicity is transformed from an apparent limit on plurality into a generative principle of notions, effects, modes, and orders.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_07:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-02","text":"The understood notion migrates from the exemplar of the thing to the measure of truth, thence to the object of will, and finally to the notion of providential execution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_08:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-03","text":"Truth passes from conformity of created cognition to identity with the first measure and finally is amplified into the historical conformity of the event with the provided mode.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:MP-02","CL_GGVC_08:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-04","text":"Causality extends from the epistemic way of effects to the production of being, modality, goodness, and salvific order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_07:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-05","text":"Participation is transferred from the conformity of the exemplified to the proper operation of secondary cause and to the dignity of a providential means.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_08:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-06","text":"Freedom reverses its force: from an appearance of exteriority to the first cause, it becomes a contingent mode of agency instituted by that very first cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_08:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-07","text":"Defect migrates from cognitive falsehood to ontological privation, cosmological permission, and imputable moral guilt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_08:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-08","text":"Time is amplified from the order of created cognition and denomination into the universal field of execution of the eternal notion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_08:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RMD-09","text":"Mercy passes from the universal communication of perfection to the special donation of grace, while retaining causality antecedent to what is due.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_08:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-01","text":"Taxonomies of degrees of light, ideas, errors, and kinds of beings remain local instruments; upward, only the laws of reception, dependence, and distinction of modes persist.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_06:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-02","text":"Local distinctions of object, power, medium, and conception are amplified into the general law by which every participation follows the mode of the recipient.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_05:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-03","text":"Idea and truth exceed the lower level where divine exemplar, measurement of the thing, and the objectivity of created cognition constitute one causal direction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:CGD-06","CL_GGVC_06:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-04","text":"Life, will, and love no longer remain parallel treatises, but are the notions through which the intelligible exemplar is converted into an effect determined modally and in goodness.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_07:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-05","text":"Providence becomes the junction of the present level because it gathers eternal notion, temporal execution, secondary causality, and the order of salvation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_08:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-06","text":"Truth as a local name of justice vanishes once its function has been absorbed into the conformity of the event with the provided notion and mode.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RMD-10","CL_GGVC_08:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-07","text":"Divisions of signs of will, comparisons of particular loves, and the number of the elect remain local determinations, not foundations of the metaaggregate.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_08:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:CGD-08","text":"Removal, permission, and imputation of defect join lower units because they formally redistribute the negative according to diverse orders of causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_07:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-01","text":"No common or higher measure of creator and creature grounds cognition, causality, or justice.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-02","text":"Ideal forms, eternal truths, and notions of order subsisting outside divine intellect are excluded.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-03","text":"Predicates are neither transferred univocally nor dissolved into absolute silence on account of transcendence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_07:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-04","text":"A plurality of notions, things known, things willed, and relations induces no composition or change in God.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-05","text":"Divine and created causality are not competitive forces such that the increase of one diminishes the other.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-06","text":"Absolute chance and freedom as independence from universal cause are ontologically excluded.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_08:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-07","text":"Pure non-being, positive evil, and a defect rivaling the first cause possess no autonomy.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_08:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:GNX-08","text":"Rational nature possesses no right, due, or intrinsic sufficiency for the vision and supernatural end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:DBN-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that the truth of cognition or of a name requires an exhaustive comprehension of the thing.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that every distinction of reason constitutes a real distinction in the first intellect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that dependence of a secondary cause destroys its proper agency or freedom.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_08:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that the infallibility of the first cause imposes uniform necessity on effects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that cognition of privation or evil induces positive causality of that same defect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that a measure of merit, right, or equality precedes first donation and the distribution of gratuitous gifts.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:DBN-07","text":"It must be false that temporal causes establish, alter, or correct the eternal decree.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-01","text":"No ontological position outside the divine order is available from which first causality, truth, or justice may be judged.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-02","text":"No region of intelligibles above or outside God contains the notions of things or norms of truth.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-03","text":"The absence of commensuration between creator and creature orders the entire structure of analogy, participation, and elevation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-04","text":"Chaos without divine notion and positive defect rivaling the first cause are inadmissible possibilities.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_06:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-05","text":"No further measure demonstrates the identity of individual elect or reprobate; regress stops in the divine will.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSA-06","text":"Unavailable to the creature is a prospect simultaneously quidditative of the divine essence and certain of its own place in the eternal order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_08:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-01","text":"One simple act through intellect establishes notions, through will determines effects, through love communicates goodness, and through providence temporally executes order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-12","CL_GGVC_07:GA-13","CL_GGVC_08:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-02","text":"Exemplar, truth, volition, and providence are not separate architectures, but directional stages through which divine notion becomes a measured thing and an ordered effect.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-15","CL_GGVC_06:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_08:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-03","text":"The descent of causality and ascent of cognition constitute an asymmetric circuit: God causes the thing through a notion, the thing measures created intellect, and cognition returns to the cause.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-05","CL_GGVC_05:XD-03","CL_GGVC_06:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-04","text":"The law of reception and the law of dependent agency express the same structure: the finite truly knows or acts because it receives its proper mode, not because it is separated from the source.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-14","CL_GGVC_05:GA-01","CL_GGVC_07:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-05","text":"Causal truth is amplified into providential truth, because the effect corresponds to divine notion not only according to being, but according to necessary, contingent, or free mode.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-08","CL_GGVC_08:XD-08","CL_GGVC_08:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-06","text":"Beatific elevation and salvific ordination share the same grammar of grace: created power is led to an unattainable end without abolition of the receiving nature.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-14","CL_GGVC_05:XD-06","CL_GGVC_08:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-07","text":"Falsehood, evil, and guilt vary one negative structure: each requires a positive foundation, but they are known, permitted, or imputed under different notions.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-13","CL_GGVC_06:XD-06","CL_GGVC_07:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-08","text":"First donation simultaneously establishes the order of nature and the field of what is due; therefore mercy does not suspend justice, but causally precedes its conditions.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-11","CL_GGVC_08:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_08:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-09","text":"The asymmetry whereby goodness is totally caused while guilt is imputed to the creature preserves providential order but leaves fragile the middle between abandonment and culpable act.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-10","CL_GGVC_08:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_08:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:XD-10","text":"Knowledge unfolds notions and possibilities, but will alone completes actuality and singular distribution; universal intelligibility therefore does not eliminate voluntary closure.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_02:GA-15","CL_GGVC_05:XD-07","CL_GGVC_08:XD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes an order of included intelligible causalities, in which one principle distinguishes notions, beings, modes, and ends without division of itself.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_08:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-02","text":"Objectivity arises neither from created mind nor from the solitary thing, but from the participation by which the thing is conformed to divine intellect and measures created intellect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-03","text":"The created subject is an active receptacle: it is measured, elevated, and caused, but simultaneously judges, acts, and receives imputability for its own act.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_08:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-04","text":"Time is the executive extension of eternal order, not a common measure within which the divine cause deliberates, learns, or changes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_08:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-05","text":"Contingency and freedom are not residues of deficient causality, but modal effects which the first cause efficaciously produces through proximate causes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_08:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-06","text":"The negative can have formal epistemic, moral, or juridical efficacy, although ontologically it depends on a positive subject and the order of goodness.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:BSD-07","CL_GGVC_08:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-07","text":"Grace demonstrates that created perfection can be increased through more intense dependence: the presence of the end transforms power without converting the recipient into the source.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:GA-05","CL_GGVC_08:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:BSD-08","text":"The general architecture of order is highly intelligible, but the singular assignment of gratuitous gifts is not deduced from that same architecture and terminates in simple will.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:BSD-08","CL_GGVC_08:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-01","text":"Created manifestations suggest a formal notion; apophasis removes imperfection; divine attribution restores the notion; causality reinterprets manifestation as effect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-02","text":"Effect produces cognition of the cause, cognition forms a name, the name is corrected toward the cause, while the cause produces effects according to its proper notion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_06:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-03","text":"Divine cognition unfolds notions of possibilities, will actualizes termini, love communicates goodness, while providence completes the order of effects through temporal means.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:GA-13","CL_GGVC_08:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-04","text":"Simplicity first seems to threaten plurality, then orders it intentionally and causally, and finally restores it, reduced to one act, as more distinct.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_06:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-05","text":"Providence prepares causes of diverse modes, causes produce effects according to their proper modes, and effects manifest the infallibility of order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-06","text":"The negative circuit begins from the positive, determines defect through privation, preserves cognition or ordination of it, and again excludes the autonomy of defect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_07:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-07","text":"Gift establishes nature and end; nature and end establish what is due; what is due forms justice; justice, however, is reduced to first gratuity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-08","text":"Love orders grace; grace produces free act and merit; merit passes into glory; glory completes eternal order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-09","text":"Divine goodness is the attractor that gathers cognition, causality, mercy, justice, and diversity of effects into one principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:RLE-10","text":"Reprobation is an unstable attractor in which permission, abandonment, guilt, and punishment converge without reduction to univocal causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RLE-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B01","text":"Created manifestations and effects → initial cognition of perfections → regress to formal notions.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:AS-03","CL_GGVC_05:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B02","text":"Extracted formal notion → removal of material, passive, and finite mode → analogically purified perfection.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:FGS-B02","CL_GGVC_05:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B03","text":"Purified perfection → identification with simple essence → exclusion of accident and reception in God.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:FGS-B04","CL_GGVC_05:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B04","text":"Essence understanding itself → modes of its proper imitability → notions of many things without real plurality of the source.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:FGS-B01","CL_GGVC_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B05","text":"Understood notions → willed termini → loved goods → actuality of effects distinct from the scope of possibilities.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:GA-13","CL_GGVC_07:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B06","text":"Actuality of effects → constitution of natures, modes, and ends → communication of causality to secondary agents.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:GA-02","CL_GGVC_08:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B07","text":"Measured thing → created reception and action → judgment, merit, prayer, and other means of temporal execution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:FGS-B05","CL_GGVC_08:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FGS-B08","text":"Apparent defect → distinction of what is known, caused, permitted, and imputed → asymmetric closure in providence, predestination, and reprobation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:FGS-B07","CL_GGVC_08:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:AFM-B01","text":"Created effect → cognition of cause → analogical naming → apophatic correction → elevation of recipient → presence of end without comprehension.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_05:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:AFM-B02","text":"Essence understanding itself → notions of things → actualizing will → love communicating goodness → modally distinct effects → executive providence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_07:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:AFM-B03","text":"First gift → nature and what is due → justice and mercy → secondary causes → grace, free act, and merit → glory or permissively ordered defect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_08:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-01","text":"Divine attributes are reclassified as formal notions of one causally fruitful act, not as really successive habits or operations.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:MME-01","CL_GGVC_07:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-02","text":"Simplicity ceases to signify sterile uniformity and becomes the principle of intelligible plurality, modality, and ordered goodness.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_07:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-03","text":"The opposition of subject and object is transformed into a structure of measurer, participated thing, and measured subject which simultaneously becomes a secondary cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:MME-03","CL_GGVC_08:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-04","text":"Truth is amplified from adequation of judgment to ontological and historical conformity of the thing with divine notion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:MME-01","CL_GGVC_08:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-05","text":"Causality is reclassified as production of being with its proper mode of acting, not as a univocal impulse that diminishes proximate agency.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:MME-04","CL_GGVC_07:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-06","text":"Time is reclassified as the field of execution and succession of effects, not as the sphere of change of the first principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_05:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-07","text":"The negative is reclassified into falsehood, privation, guilt, abandonment, and punishment, none of which receives the same positive causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:MME-04","CL_GGVC_08:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MME-08","text":"What is due and justice are transferred from an external measure of God to internal relations of nature and end, while gratuitous gifts remain above every antecedent due.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:MME-02","CL_GGVC_08:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of surface are treated cognition and naming of God, ideas and truth, life and will, providence, freedom, grace, and the distribution of salvation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_06:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level all things are ordered through the mechanism of asymmetric measurement and included causalities, whose center is the simple act.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level oppositions are resolved by relocating terms between notion and mode, first and second cause, eternity and execution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_06:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level reality is modally distinct participation and temporal execution of eternal notion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level the argument itself purifies, distinguishes, redistributes, and reduces, thereby effectively producing the hierarchical form that it affirms.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_08:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-01","text":"A plurality of proper notions of things requires robust intelligible distinction, although every real plurality in God is denied.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-02","text":"Positive cognition and naming of the divine essence must cohere with quidditative ignorance and immediate non-comprehensive vision; the middle of this coherence remains compressed.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:FL-02","CL_GGVC_05:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-03","text":"Eternal truth and certainty of providence affirm contingency and freedom, but the inner mechanism of non-necessitation is not fully demonstrated.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:FL-06","CL_GGVC_08:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-04","text":"Total first causality is composed with imputable guilt and permissive reprobation through an asymmetry whose causal middle remains fragile.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:FL-03","CL_GGVC_08:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:FL-05","text":"Wisdom and goodness explain the universal structure of order, but refer the identity of individual elect to will without further notion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:FL-01","CL_GGVC_08:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MP-01","text":"Species changes from excluded created representation into objective presence of essence and finally into identity of essence with the divine notion of understanding.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MP-02","text":"Truth changes from conformity of judgment into identity with the first measure and thence into conformity of event with providentially established mode.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:MP-02","CL_GGVC_08:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MP-03","text":"Causality changes where cognition of possibilities passes into determining will and intrinsic perfection is converted into the order of external effects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:FL-04","CL_GGVC_07:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MP-04","text":"Freedom changes from apparent independence into a contingent mode of secondary causality constituted within providence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_08:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MP-05","text":"Mercy changes from universal communication of perfection into supernatural donation, while justice passes from the order of things due to the legitimate inequality of gratuitous gifts.","refs":["CL_GGVC_08:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_08:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality is imagined as a concentric architecture whose simple center emits intelligible notions and temporally executes them through causal branches.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_08:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:HSI-02","text":"The created subject is an elevable receptacle and a caused secondary cause: it receives form, mode, and power, but responds by its own operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_08:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is an executive circumference around the eternal center, in which conformities, actions, and events vary without variation of the first notion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_08:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:HSI-04","text":"Navigation is possible not as escape outside order, but as ascent through effects and action through causal means, with apophatic distinctions restraining error.","refs":["CL_GGVC_06:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_08:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MRT-B01","text":"Axioms concerning simple act, participation, asymmetric measurement, modally productive causality, grace, and providence suffice to restore the principal structure of this metaaggregate.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_08:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the transition from one intelligible act to a plurality of notions, effects, and secondary causes without multiplication of the source.","refs":["CL_GGVC_07:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_08:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MRT-B03","text":"The distinction of notions in God, the immediacy of finite vision, the non-necessitation of freedom, the causality of guilt, and the singular notion of election are not yet sufficiently determined.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_08:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_02:MRT-B04","text":"The restoration preserves the true movement if repetitions are retained as functional amplifications, negations as positive structures, and asymmetries as generative principles; the singularly distributing will still resists complete reduction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_05:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_08:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_02@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_GGVC_L1_02:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Ggvc L1 02","context":"Chapters 13–24 · Prima pars · Quaestio 12 · Prima pars · Quaestio 13 · Prima pars · Quaestio 14 · Prima pars · Quaestio 15 · Prima pars · Quaestio 16 · Prima pars · Quaestio 17 · Prima pars · Quaestio 18 · Prima pars · Quaestio 19 · Prima pars · Quaestio 20 · Prima pars · Quaestio 21 · Prima pars · Quaestio 22 · Prima pars · Quaestio 23"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02","layer":"MC","title":"Ggvc L2 02","order":2,"parents":["MC_GGVC_L1_05","MC_GGVC_L1_06","MC_GGVC_L1_07"],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L2_02@R001","sha256":"cda99e80c739fab2b47a42e78b2720927a243bbc818a7b03619074da477f7fef","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo ordo creatus, a spiritu immateriali per mundum formatum ad hominem incorporatum extensus, simul dependeat, differat et proprie operetur sine dualismo, autonomia aut confusione causarum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis actualitatem receptam ut principium differentiae positivae constituit: participatio formas, potentias, media et operationes multiplicat, non identitatem causae primae dividit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio ab effectibus et difficultatibus ad principia regressu procedit, deinde distinctionibus causalibus, formalibus et analogicis modos finitos relationis restituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-04","text":"Motus a bonitate actus recepti ad differentiationem mundi, ab ordine cosmico ad interioritatem potentiarum, atque ab operatione mediata ad cognitionem, electionem et quietem ascendit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-05","text":"Progressus est spiralis et cumulativus, quia omnis ascensus ad operationem superiorem modum recipientis, distinctionem principiorum et dependentiam causalem iterum imponit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-06","text":"Contradictio apparens mechanismum transitionis praebet: opposita per respectus diversos separantur, sed tensio residua ad gradum causalem vel ontologicum superiorem transfertur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-07","text":"Clausura est productiva et stratificata: institutio terminatur, status quidam confirmantur, sed causae creatae, media contingentia et operationes propriae intra finem datum permanent.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-08","MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-08","text":"Systema fundamentaliter generare conatur ontologiam causalitatis participatae, in qua unitas differentias ordinat, mediatio efficaciam finit, et dependentia agentiam potius constituit quam minuit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Omnis actualitas creata positive bona est; privatio tantum in subiecto, potentia vel actione iam positiva consistere potest.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Esse receptum creaturam simul constituit et terminat, ita ut finitas sit modus positivus participationis, non materia, malum aut mera absentia.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-02","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Unitas creata est pluralitas ordinata, cuius integritas ex distinctione partium, formarum, potentiarum et causarum secundum rationem totius oritur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Causa superior modum proprium causae inferioris instituit et conservat; maior dependentia ontologica igitur efficaciam secundam non necessario minuit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Modus operationis modum essendi manifestat, sed operatio finita semper per applicationem, obiectum, medium vel subiectum proportionatum determinatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Mediatio est condicio positiva efficaciae finitae: substantialem identitatem excludere potest, dum praesentiam, cognitionem, electionem et executionem constituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Potentiae et actus secundum operationes proprias, rationes formales obiectorum et fines distinguuntur, non ex sola materia, organo vel nomine.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Temporalitas creata est distributio successionum secundum modos operationis, non receptaculum univocum commune spiritui, corpori, narrationi et actioni.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Finis universalis et stabilis inclinationem necessariam constituit, sed eo ipso campum mediorum contingentium, iudicii et acceptationis voluntariae aperit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Defectus causae secundae modum actus positivi deordinat sine productione entitatis adversae; imputabilitas oritur ubi iudicium et acceptatio interna ordinem debitum deserunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-01","MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Perfectio terminalis novam constitutionem claudit, non autem fecunditatem principiorum, operationem potentiarum aut ordinationem mediorum ad finem.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Distinctio respectuum est operator globalis qui vocabula purgat, contradictiones distribuit et unitatem analogicam sine reductione differentiarum restituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Hierarchia non est sola scala dignitatis, sed administratio differentiarum qua superiora media comprimunt, inferiora proprios actus servant et partes ad bonum totius ordinantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-01","text":"Participatio identitatem causalem retinet, sed ab esse spiritus ad formationem cosmicam, operationem psychicam et motionem voluntatis successive amplificatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-02","text":"Finitas ab essentia recepta migrat in terminum virtutis, receptivitatem ulterioris formae, incorporationem executionis et ambitum contingentium mediorum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-03","text":"Unitas a graduum ordine ad integritatem mundi, ab integritate ad formam multas potentias continentem, et inde ad coordinationem causarum actus transit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-04","text":"Mediatio functionem invertit: quod in unione substantiali superfluum est, in causalitate, cognitione, appetitu et executione fit necessarium.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-05","text":"Perfectio a gradu ontologico ad completudinem receptivam, integritatem fecundam et quietem terminalem mutatur, immobilitate simplici exclusa.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-06","text":"Tempus a successione spiritualium applicationum ad pluralitatem cosmicarum mensurarum et ad seriem incorporatam cognitionis, appetitus atque executionis transfertur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-07","text":"Libertas ab electione particularium bonorum ad adhaesionem confirmatam et tandem ad iudicium incorporatum mutatur, semper autonomia absoluta exclusa.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-09","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MP-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-08","text":"Corpus a falso principio mali vel limitationis universalis dislocatur atque ut regio bona, materia compositi et medium singulare executionis restituitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-09","text":"Operatio redit ut exteriorizatio virtutis, actualizatio formae, manifestatio naturae et actus imputabilis; identitas vocabuli pluralitatem graduum causalium non tollit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-01","text":"Apparitiones angelicae, corpora assumpta, numerus spirituum et distributio officiorum locales manent; sursum ascendit exteriorizatio virtutis immaterialis per terminos proportionatos.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-02","text":"Materia caelestis, firmamentum, aquae et classificationes regionales manent hypotheses cosmologicae inferiores; praesentem gradum alunt tantum ut exempla differentiae ordinatae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-03","text":"Taxonomiae zoologicae, anatomicae et facultatum opere locali peracto evanescunt; persistent criteria operationis, obiecti formalis, subiecti et finis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-04","text":"Esse receptum, formatio ulterior et resultatio potentiarum amplificantur in legem generalem qua actus primus operationes secundas sine iterata creatione generat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-05","text":"Causalitas spiritualis, cosmica et humana praesentem gradum coniungunt ut modi diversi operationis secundae sub institutione et conservatione prima.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-06","text":"Transitus maior fit ubi ordo cosmicus interiorizatur in formam viventis, potentias cognoscitivas et causalitatem voluntariam, sine abolitione corporis aut principii superioris.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-07","text":"Aequationes limitationis cum materia, unitatis cum homogeneitate, libertatis cum independentia et creationis cum chronologia simplici obsoletae fiunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-08","text":"Purgatio analogica, selectio modelorum et regressus ab actibus ad principia non sunt auxilia localia, sed methodus huius gradus ad ontologiam reconstruendam.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-01","text":"Malum, tenebrae et informitas neque substantiae neque principia positiva sunt, sed carentiae ordinis, rectitudinis, manifestationis aut perfectionis debitae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-02","text":"Materia et corpus neque mali sunt neque fundamentum universale finitudinis; limitatio intellectualis et moralis sine compositione materiali consistere potest.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-03","text":"Creatura nullam efficaciam ex principio autonome possesso habet, neque causalitas eius contra causalitatem primam vel extra ordinem participationis constituitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-04","text":"Unitas neque uniformitatem materiae, neque identitatem potentiarum, neque suppressionem differentiae inter partes et operationes requirit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-05","text":"Immaterialitas neque infinitatem, omniscientiam, ubiquitatem neque transitum immediatum ab universali ad effectum singularem confert.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-03","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-06","text":"Tempus non est continuum absolutum in quod omnes successiones spirituales, corporales, narratives et voluntariae univoce inserantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-07","text":"Libertas neque indifferentia absoluta neque absentia finis, inclinationis aut motionis superioris est; coactio sola modum rationalis adhaesionis destruit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-08","text":"Apparentia, nomen, figura sensibilis et lectio singularis ontologiam rei exhaustive determinare non possunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet causalitatem superiorem et inferiorem quantitates rivales esse; aliter dependentia operationem creatam evacuaret et participatio causaliter sterilis fieret.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet differentiam lapsum ab unitate esse, quia multiplicitas graduum, regionum et potentiarum ipsam perfectionem totius constituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet relationem realem tantum per contactum corporeum vel identitatem naturalem effici, quia virtus, species et obiectum formale modos irreducibiles unionis praebent.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-02","MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet principium superius inferius substituere aut perfectionem subiectum destruere; utrumque unitatem ordinis in absorptionem converteret.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet completionem inertiam vel novitatem creatam interruptionem causalitatis exigere; fecunditas posterior principia instituta explicat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet veritatem stabilem cosmologiam, chronologiam aut vocabularium unicum postulare; invariabilia ontologica a modelis inferioribus corrigibilibus distinguuntur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-01","text":"Exterior neutralis ante vel extra participationem, in quo creatura iam constituta consistere posset, ontologice indisponibilis est.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-02","text":"Altera souveranitas quae esse, causalitatem, ordinem vel finem contra primum principium instituat est possibilitas profundissime exclusa.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-03","text":"Creatura quae sit purus actus, sua operatio, causa sui luminis et principium sui finis contradictionem terminalem systematis constituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-04","text":"Coincidentia plena inter fontem et participans, universale et singulare, aeternum et temporale numquam praesto est; mediatio hanc absentiam operative administrat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-05","text":"Subiectum finitum sibi ut ultimo cognoscibili, appetibili et causali sufficiens inadmissibile est, quia finis prior et bonum extra essentiam eius campum operationis constituunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-01","text":"Participatio est architectura transductiva: actualitas a causa prima per formas, potentias et media ad effectus proprios distribuitur, quin in causis secundis autonomia oriatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-02","text":"Immaterialitas et incorporatio non constituunt oppositionem simplicem: principium intellectuale organum formaliter excedit, sed omnis efficacia finita medium operationis proportionatum requirit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-03","text":"Formatio cosmica et distributio potentiarum psychicarum sunt structurae homologae actualizationis: actus primus subiectum constituit, actus secundus principia iam data explicat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-04","text":"Successio spiritualis, tempora cosmica et series actionis humanae componunt temporalitatem modalem, cuius unitas est coordinatio actualizationum, non mensura homogena.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-05","text":"Relatio partis ad totum recurrit per magnitudines diversas: hierarchia spiritualis, integritas mundi et unitas humana omnes pluralitatem per ordinem, non per similitudinem materialem, componunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-06","text":"Privatio per campos moralem et cosmicum eandem dependentiam ab actualitate bona retinet, sed imputabilitas tantum ubi defectus per iudicium et acceptationem voluntariam transit emergit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-07","text":"Clausura non est uniformis: completudo naturalis ad donum aperitur, status spiritualis confirmari potest, et beatitudo humana media contingentia ordinat; terminatio igitur secundum capacitatem recipientis variatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-08","text":"Purgatio apophatica, distinctio recursiva et regressus ab operationibus ad principia unam methodum reconstructivam efficiunt, quae falsa immediatione remota relationes positivas restituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-01","text":"Realitas emergit ut machina architectonica participationis, in qua actualitas per ordines inclusos descendit et effectus per operationes proprias principia sua manifestant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum creatum non est centrum clausum, sed nodus receptivus et excentricus cuius identitas ex forma, relationibus, potentiis et fine extra essentiam suam constituitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-03","text":"Historia creata est nexus successionum inaequalium, in quo institutiones, revelationes, decisiones, motus et executiones diversa genera novitatis atque clausurae producunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-04","text":"Cognitio finita est indirecta sed generativa: ex operationibus et differentiis formalibus principia restituit, dum vocabula et exemplaria inferiora correctioni manent obnoxia.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-05","text":"Potestas creata est ministerialis: proprietas actus in iudicio, applicatione et executione servatur, sed dominium totius ordinis agenti finito non competit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-06","text":"Causalitas participata non sequitur legem summae nullae: causa superior eo plenius causat quo inferius secundum proprium modum efficacius operari facit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-07","text":"Finalitas stabilis contingentiam ordinat potius quam tollit, quia finis non electus comparationem, consilium et electionem mediorum finitorum generat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-08","text":"Malum et error fiunt perturbationes locales transductionis, ubi actualitas positiva a regula, fine vel competentia sua disiungitur, non regiones ontologicae independentes.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-01","text":"Esse participatum → forma terminans → potentiae distinctae → media proportionata → operationes propriae → manifestatio ordinis → reditus causaliter et appetitive ad principium.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-02","text":"Negatio falsae identitatis → distinctio modi → restitutio relationis positivae → detectio novi excessus → iterata purgatio constituit circuitum apophaticum permanentem.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-03","text":"Partes ordinatae → integritas mundi → forma substantialis → potentiae plurales → actus coordinati efficiunt recursionem unitatis non homogeneae per magnitudines diversas.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-04","text":"Mundus sensibilis → receptio intentionalis → abstractio universalis → bonum apprehensum → electio → executio corporalis → novus effectus mundanus constituit spiralem cognitionis et actionis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-05","text":"Causa superior → institutio modi inferioris → actus proprius causae secundae → effectus realis → manifestatio causalitatis primae constituit viam amplificationis non aemulae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-06","text":"Completio, quies et beatitudo sunt attractores terminales qui novam constitutionem restringunt, sed propagationem, ministerium et electionem mediorum ordinant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-07","text":"Contradictio apparens → divisio respectuum → reclassificatio termini → coordinatio causarum → tensio altior efficit recursionem qua solutio localis novam quaestionem structuralem aperit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B01","text":"Causa prima → actualitas recepta positive bona → distinctio essentiae et operationis → exclusio autonomia et mali substantialis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B02","text":"Actualitas recepta → forma terminans → pluralitas partium vel potentiarum → unitas ordinata secundum bonum totius.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B03","text":"Potentia finita + obiectum formale + medium proportionatum → operatio propria sine identitate substantiali cum termino.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B04","text":"Actus primus constitutivus → principia immanentia → actus secundus → effectus novus ut explicatio causalis, non iterata creatio.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B05","text":"Actus positivus + defectus ordinis debiti → privatio, error aut culpa → imputabilitas graduata secundum cognitionem et acceptationem.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B06","text":"Effectus observatus → distinctio operationis → principium proximum → ratio formalis obiecti → restitutio indirecta architecturae ontologicae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B07","text":"Finis universalis → inclinatio necessaria → media contingentia → iudicium → acceptatio appetitiva → executio impedibilis sub causalitate prima.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B08","text":"Terminatio institutionis + permanentia principiorum → quies productiva → continuatio operationum secundarum intra ordinem stabilitum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:AFM-B01","text":"Bonitas actualitatis receptae → differentiatio formarum → unitas non homogenea → causalitas stratificata → operationes mediatae → agentia dependens → quies productiva.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:AFM-B02","text":"Ordo cosmicus → interiorizatio in forma viventis → pluralitas potentiarum → cognitio universalis → appetitus boni → electio mediorum → executio corporalis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:AFM-B03","text":"Difficultas apparens → purgatio vocabuli → divisio respectuum → reclassificatio causae vel temporis → restitutio relationis → circumscriptione tensionis residualis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-01","text":"Oppositio dependentiae et agentiae corruit: dependentia recte ordinata fit condicio operationis propriae, non imminutio quantitativa libertatis vel causalitatis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-02","text":"Oppositio unitatis et pluralitatis reordinatur, quia differentiae partium et potentiarum sunt materia formalis integritatis, non residuum imperfectum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-03","text":"Mediatio ab indicio distantiae vel imperfectionis convertitur in structuram qua finitum praesentiam, cognitionem et efficaciam propriam acquirit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-04","text":"Oppositio necessitatis et libertatis solvitur per distinctionem finis, inclinationis, coactionis et mediorum contingentium.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-05","text":"Spiritus et corpus non amplius bonum et malum repraesentant, sed modos diversos receptionis et operationis quorum compositio agentiam humanam constituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-06","text":"Completio reclassificatur ex cessatione sterili in integritatem productivam, quae novam institutionem excludit sed effectus ex principiis datis producit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-07","text":"Tempus reclassificatur ex linea universali in tabulam successionum modorumque clausurae, quibus spiritus, cosmos et actio diversa futura recipiunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-07","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-08","text":"Lectio ontologica fit reconstructiva et constricta: ab operationibus ad principia ascendit, vocabula analogice purgat et exemplaria inferiora sub invariabilibus causalibus corrigit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei aguntur spiritus creati, formatio mundi, anima, potentiae, cognitio, voluntas et libertas, sed omnia quaestionem unam de operatione creaturae dependentis explicant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali operantur participatio, forma, distinctio actus et potentiae, obiectum formale, mediatio et coordinatio causarum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico controversiae de corporibus, temporibus, facultatibus et libertate transferuntur in quaestiones de attributione, respectu et ordine causali.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est actualitas recepta et differentialis, cuius subiecta per successiones proprias ad fines non a se institutos moventur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo forma distinctionum ipsam unitatem ordinatam efficit quam asserit, quia differentias servat, subordinat et analogice reconiungit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-01","text":"Universalitas causae primae et imputatio defectus causae secundae cohaerent per distinctionem actus positivi a modo deficienti, sed transitus mechanicus ad defectum voluntarium incompletus manet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-02","text":"Completudo naturalis, congruentia cum gloria et gratuititas elevationis simul affirmantur, sed mensura relationis inter capacitatem receptivam et donum supernaturale non plene formalizatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-03","text":"Confirmatio irreversibilis et quies terminalis operationem atque libertatem servare dicuntur, sed processus quo adhaesio mutabilis stabilitatem irrevocabilem acquirit nondum determinatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-04","text":"Intellectus immaterialis mediationibus intentionalibus indiget, intellectus humanus etiam phantasmatibus et corpore; continuitas analogica horum modorum discontinuam incorporationem tantum partialiter explicat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-05","text":"Veritas ontologica stabilis et mobilitas modelorum cosmologicorum cohaerent tantum si terminus correctionis semanticus finitus est, cuius criterium non ubique univocum manet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-06","text":"Mundus completus causaliter apertus novitatem posteriorum effectuum admittit, sed limes inter explicationem principiorum et additionem novi status instabilis manet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-01","text":"Mediatio mutatur ab eo quod unionem substantialem impediret in condicionem positivam operationis, cognitionis, electionis et executionis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-02","text":"Perfectio mutatur a gradu eminentiae in integritatem totius, deinde in fecunditatem causaliter apertam et quietem terminalem.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MP-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-03","text":"Corpus mutatur a regione cosmica bona in materiam forma constitutam atque tandem in medium intrinsecum singularis cognitionis et actionis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MP-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-04","text":"Libertas mutatur a potentia oppositorum in modum adhaesionis, iudicii et acceptationis, sine conversione in independentiam causalem.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:MP-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-05","text":"Tempus mutatur a situ chronologico in pluralitatem mensurarum operativarum, narrativarum et terminalium, quarum coordinatio identitatem univocam substituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MP-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina architectonica et transductiva: causa prima instituit, formae terminant, potentiae recipiunt, media transferunt et causae secundae exsequuntur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum creatum est nodus distributus et excentricus: spiritus per praesentias analogicas, homo per formam incorporatam et uterque per finem supra essentiam suam ordinatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est plexus arcuum et spiralis actualizationis, in qua principia instituta futuras operationes continent, decisiones vias claudunt et finis seriem ordinat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis est duplex: ab effectibus, sensibilibus et operationibus ad principia ascendere, deinde a universalibus et finibus per media ad effectus singulares descendere.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata actualitatis participatae, unitatis non homogeneae, causalitatis non aemulae, mediationis positivae, privationis et finalitatis productivae sufficiunt ad machinam praesentis gradus restituendam.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus quo forma recepta potentias distinguit, causa superior actus inferiores conservat et differentia ad integritatem totius ordinatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent actualizatio defectibilitatis, irreversibilitas adhaesionis, mensura novitatis post completionem et compositio motionis divinae cum electione finita.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B04","text":"Systema hac magnitudine recursive cohaeret, quia eadem lex participationis per spiritum, cosmum et hominem functionem mutat sine amissione asymmetriae causalis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio motum verum servat si a bonitate recepta per differentiationem, mediationem et operationem ad agentiam atque quietem progreditur; analogiae, exemplaria probabilia et commissurae inter gradus reductioni completae resistunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T174324960414Z_34b398a6","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Ggvc L2 02","source_sha256":"cda99e80c739fab2b47a42e78b2720927a243bbc818a7b03619074da477f7fef","sha256":"41e69214406fce2f22778228df984c083a50985b0a36a3fad54fc19bdfa46aea","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:43:24.962564+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how created order, extending from the immaterial spirit through the formed world to the embodied human being, may at once depend, differ, and operate properly without dualism, autonomy, or confusion of causes.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis establishes received actuality as the principle of positive difference: participation multiplies forms, powers, media, and operations; it does not divide the identity of the first cause.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-03","text":"The argument proceeds regressively from effects and difficulties to principles, then restores finite modes of relation through causal, formal, and analogical distinctions.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-04","text":"The movement ascends from the goodness of received act to the differentiation of the world, from cosmic order to the interiority of powers, and from mediated operation to cognition, choice, and rest.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-05","text":"The progress is spiral and cumulative, because every ascent to a higher operation reimposes the mode of the recipient, the distinction of principles, and causal dependence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-06","text":"Apparent contradiction provides the mechanism of transition: opposites are separated through different respects, but the residual tension is transferred to a higher causal or ontological level.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-07","text":"Closure is productive and stratified: institution terminates, certain states are confirmed, yet created causes, contingent media, and proper operations remain within the given end.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-08","MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-08","text":"The system fundamentally seeks to generate an ontology of participated causality, in which unity orders differences, mediation delimits efficacy, and dependence constitutes rather than diminishes agency.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every created actuality is positively good; privation can consist only in an already positive subject, power, or action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Received being simultaneously constitutes and delimits the creature, such that finitude is a positive mode of participation, not matter, evil, or mere absence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-02","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Created unity is ordered plurality, whose integrity arises from the distinction of parts, forms, powers, and causes according to the account of the whole.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] A higher cause institutes and preserves the proper mode of a lower cause; therefore greater ontological dependence does not necessarily diminish secondary efficacy.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The mode of operation manifests the mode of being, but finite operation is always determined through application, object, medium, or a proportioned subject.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Mediation is a positive condition of finite efficacy: it can exclude substantial identity while constituting presence, cognition, choice, and execution.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Powers and acts are distinguished according to their proper operations, the formal accounts of objects, and ends, not from matter, organ, or name alone.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Created temporality is the distribution of successions according to modes of operation, not a univocal receptacle common to spirit, body, narrative, and action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] A universal and stable end constitutes a necessary inclination, but precisely thereby opens a field of contingent means, judgment, and voluntary acceptance.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] The defect of a secondary cause disorders the mode of a positive act without producing an opposing entity; imputability arises where judgment and internal acceptance abandon due order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-01","MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Terminal perfection closes a new constitution, but not the fecundity of principles, the operation of powers, or the ordering of means to the end.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] The distinction of respects is a global operator that purifies terms, distributes contradictions, and restores analogical unity without reducing differences.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Hierarchy is not merely a scale of dignity, but an administration of differences whereby higher things compress media, lower things preserve their proper acts, and parts are ordered to the good of the whole.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-01","text":"Participation retains causal identity, but is successively amplified from the being of spirit to cosmic formation, psychic operation, and the motion of the will.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-02","text":"Finitude migrates from received essence into the limit of power, receptivity for further form, the embodiment of execution, and the scope of contingent means.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-03","text":"Unity passes from an order of degrees to the integrity of the world, from integrity to a form containing many powers, and thence to the coordination of the causes of acts.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-04","text":"Mediation reverses its function: what is superfluous in substantial union becomes necessary in causality, cognition, appetite, and execution.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-05","text":"Perfection changes from an ontological degree into receptive completeness, fecund integrity, and terminal rest, simple immobility being excluded.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-06","text":"Time is transferred from the succession of spiritual applications to the plurality of cosmic measures and to the embodied series of cognition, appetite, and execution.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-07","text":"Freedom changes from the choice of particular goods to confirmed adherence and finally to embodied judgment, absolute autonomy always being excluded.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-09","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MP-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-08","text":"The body is displaced from the false principle of evil or universal limitation and restored as a good region, the matter of the composite, and the singular medium of execution.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-09","text":"Operation recurs as the exteriorization of power, actualization of form, manifestation of nature, and imputable act; the identity of the term does not remove the plurality of causal levels.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-01","text":"Angelic appearances, assumed bodies, the number of spirits, and the distribution of offices remain local; the exteriorization of immaterial power through proportioned terms ascends.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-02","text":"Celestial matter, the firmament, waters, and regional classifications remain lower cosmological hypotheses; they support the present level only as examples of ordered difference.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-03","text":"Zoological, anatomical, and faculty taxonomies disappear when their local work is completed; the criteria of operation, formal object, subject, and end persist.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-04","text":"Received being, further formation, and the result of powers are amplified into the general law whereby first act generates secondary operations without repeated creation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-05","text":"Spiritual, cosmic, and human causality unite the present level as diverse modes of secondary operation under primary institution and preservation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-06","text":"The major transition occurs where cosmic order is interiorized into the form of a living being, cognitive powers, and voluntary causality, without abolishing the body or the higher principle.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-07","text":"The equations of limitation with matter, unity with homogeneity, freedom with independence, and creation with simple chronology become obsolete.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-08","text":"Analogical purification, selection of models, and regress from acts to principles are not local aids, but this level's method for reconstructing ontology.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-01","text":"Evil, darkness, and formlessness are neither substances nor positive principles, but lacks of due order, rectitude, manifestation, or perfection.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-02","text":"Matter and body are neither evil nor the universal foundation of finitude; intellectual and moral limitation can subsist without material composition.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-03","text":"The creature has no efficacy from an autonomously possessed principle, nor is its causality constituted against primary causality or outside the order of participation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-04","text":"Unity requires neither uniformity of matter, nor identity of powers, nor the suppression of difference among parts and operations.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-05","text":"Immateriality confers neither infinity, omniscience, ubiquity, nor immediate passage from the universal to the singular effect.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-03","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-06","text":"Time is not an absolute continuum into which all spiritual, bodily, narrative, and voluntary successions are inserted univocally.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-07","text":"Freedom is neither absolute indifference nor absence of end, inclination, or higher motion; coercion alone destroys the mode of rational adherence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-08","text":"Appearance, name, sensible figure, and singular reading cannot exhaustively determine the ontology of a thing.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that higher and lower causality are rival quantities; otherwise dependence would empty created operation and participation would become causally sterile.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that difference is a lapse from unity, because the multiplicity of degrees, regions, and powers constitutes the very perfection of the whole.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that real relation is effected only through bodily contact or natural identity, because power, species, and formal object provide irreducible modes of union.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-02","MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that a higher principle substitutes for a lower one or that perfection destroys the subject; either would convert the unity of order into absorption.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that completion requires inertia or that created novelty requires an interruption of causality; subsequent fecundity unfolds instituted principles.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that stable truth requires a single cosmology, chronology, or vocabulary; ontological invariants are distinguished from corrigible lower models.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-01","text":"A neutral exterior before or outside participation, in which an already constituted creature could subsist, is ontologically unavailable.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-02","text":"Another sovereignty that institutes being, causality, order, or end against the first principle is a possibility excluded at the deepest level.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-03","text":"A creature that is pure act, its own operation, the cause of its own light, and the principle of its own end constitutes the terminal contradiction of the system.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-04","text":"Full coincidence between source and participant, universal and singular, eternal and temporal is never available; mediation operationally administers this absence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-05","text":"A finite subject sufficient to itself as its ultimate knowable, appetible, and causal object is inadmissible, because a prior end and a good outside its essence constitute the field of its operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-01","text":"Participation is a transductive architecture: actuality is distributed from the first cause through forms, powers, and media to proper effects, without autonomy arising in secondary causes.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-02","text":"Immateriality and embodiment do not constitute a simple opposition: the intellectual principle formally exceeds the organ, but every finite efficacy requires a proportioned medium of operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-03","text":"Cosmic formation and the distribution of psychic powers are homologous structures of actualization: first act constitutes the subject, second act unfolds principles already given.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-04","text":"Spiritual succession, cosmic times, and the series of human action compose modal temporality, whose unity is the coordination of actualizations, not a homogeneous measure.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-05","text":"The relation of part to whole recurs across diverse magnitudes: spiritual hierarchy, the integrity of the world, and human unity all compose plurality through order, not through material similarity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-06","text":"Privation retains the same dependence on good actuality across the moral and cosmic fields, but imputability emerges only where defect passes through judgment and voluntary acceptance.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-07","text":"Closure is not uniform: natural completeness opens to gift, spiritual status can be confirmed, and human beatitude orders contingent means; termination therefore varies according to the recipient's capacity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-08","text":"Apophatic purification, recursive distinction, and regress from operations to principles constitute one reconstructive method, which, having removed false immediacy, restores positive relations.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-01","text":"Reality emerges as an architectural machine of participation, in which actuality descends through nested orders and effects manifest their principles through proper operations.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-02","text":"The created subject is not a closed center, but a receptive and eccentric node whose identity is constituted from form, relations, powers, and an end outside its essence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-03","text":"Created history is a nexus of unequal successions, in which institutions, revelations, decisions, motions, and executions produce diverse kinds of novelty and closure.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-04","text":"Finite cognition is indirect yet generative: from operations and formal differences it restores principles, while terms and lower models remain subject to correction.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-05","text":"Created power is ministerial: the propriety of the act is preserved in judgment, application, and execution, but dominion over the whole order does not belong to a finite agent.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-06","text":"Participated causality does not follow a zero-sum law: the higher cause causes all the more fully insofar as it makes the lower operate more efficaciously according to its proper mode.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-07","text":"Stable finality orders rather than removes contingency, because an unchosen end generates comparison, deliberation, and the choice of finite means.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-08","text":"Evil and error become local disturbances of transduction, where positive actuality is disjoined from its rule, end, or competence, rather than independent ontological regions.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-01","text":"Participated being → delimiting form → distinct powers → proportioned media → proper operations → manifestation of order → causally and appetitively directed return to the principle.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-02","text":"Negation of false identity → distinction of mode → restoration of positive relation → detection of new excess → repeated purification constitutes a permanent apophatic circuit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-03","text":"Ordered parts → integrity of the world → substantial form → plural powers → coordinated acts produce a recursion of non-homogeneous unity across diverse magnitudes.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-04","text":"Sensible world → intentional reception → universal abstraction → apprehended good → choice → bodily execution → new worldly effect constitutes a spiral of cognition and action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-05","text":"Higher cause → institution of lower mode → proper act of secondary cause → real effect → manifestation of primary causality constitutes a path of non-rival amplification.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-06","text":"Completion, rest, and beatitude are terminal attractors that restrict new constitution, but order propagation, ministry, and the choice of means.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-07","text":"Apparent contradiction → division of respects → reclassification of the term → coordination of causes → higher tension produces a recursion whereby a local solution opens a new structural question.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B01","text":"First cause → positively good received actuality → distinction of essence and operation → exclusion of autonomy and substantial evil.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B02","text":"Received actuality → delimiting form → plurality of parts or powers → unity ordered according to the good of the whole.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B03","text":"Finite power + formal object + proportioned medium → proper operation without substantial identity with the term.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B04","text":"Constitutive first act → immanent principles → second act → new effect as causal unfolding, not repeated creation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B05","text":"Positive act + defect of due order → privation, error, or fault → graded imputability according to cognition and acceptance.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B06","text":"Observed effect → distinction of operation → proximate principle → formal account of the object → indirect restoration of ontological architecture.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B07","text":"Universal end → necessary inclination → contingent means → judgment → appetitive acceptance → impedible execution under primary causality.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B08","text":"Termination of institution + permanence of principles → productive rest → continuation of secondary operations within the established order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:AFM-B01","text":"Goodness of received actuality → differentiation of forms → non-homogeneous unity → stratified causality → mediated operations → dependent agency → productive rest.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:AFM-B02","text":"Cosmic order → interiorization in the form of a living being → plurality of powers → universal cognition → appetite for the good → choice of means → bodily execution.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:AFM-B03","text":"Apparent difficulty → purification of the term → division of respects → reclassification of cause or time → restoration of relation → circumscription of residual tension.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-01","text":"The opposition of dependence and agency collapses: rightly ordered dependence becomes a condition of proper operation, not a quantitative diminution of freedom or causality.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-02","text":"The opposition of unity and plurality is reordered, because differences of parts and powers are the formal matter of integrity, not an imperfect residue.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-03","text":"Mediation is converted from an indication of distance or imperfection into the structure by which the finite acquires presence, cognition, and proper efficacy.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-04","text":"The opposition of necessity and freedom is resolved through the distinction of end, inclination, coercion, and contingent means.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-05","text":"Spirit and body no longer represent good and evil, but diverse modes of reception and operation whose composition constitutes human agency.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-06","text":"Completion is reclassified from sterile cessation into productive integrity, which excludes new institution but produces effects from given principles.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-07","text":"Time is reclassified from a universal line into a map of successions and modes of closure, by which spirit, cosmos, and action receive diverse futures.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-07","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-08","text":"Ontological reading becomes reconstructive and constrained: it ascends from operations to principles, purifies terms analogically, and corrects lower models under causal invariants.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of surface are treated created spirits, the formation of the world, soul, powers, cognition, will, and freedom, but all expound one question concerning the operation of the dependent creature.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level operate participation, form, the distinction of act and potency, formal object, mediation, and coordination of causes.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, controversies concerning bodies, times, faculties, and freedom are transferred into questions concerning attribution, respect, and causal order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is received and differential actuality, whose subjects are moved through their proper successions toward ends not instituted by themselves.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the form of distinctions effects the very ordered unity that it asserts, because it preserves, subordinates, and analogically reunites differences.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-01","text":"The universality of the first cause and the imputation of a secondary cause's defect cohere through the distinction of positive act from deficient mode, but the mechanical transition to voluntary defect remains incomplete.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-02","text":"Natural completeness, congruence with glory, and the gratuitousness of elevation are affirmed together, but the measure of the relation between receptive capacity and supernatural gift is not fully formalized.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-03","text":"Irreversible confirmation and terminal rest are said to preserve operation and freedom, but the process by which mutable adherence acquires irrevocable stability is not yet determined.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-04","text":"Immaterial intellect requires intentional mediations, while human intellect also requires phantasms and body; the analogical continuity of these modes only partially explains discontinuous embodiment.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-05","text":"Stable ontological truth and the mobility of cosmological models cohere only if the semantic limit of correction is finite, the criterion of which does not remain univocal everywhere.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-06","text":"A causally open completed world admits the novelty of subsequent effects, but the boundary between the unfolding of principles and the addition of a new state remains unstable.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-01","text":"Mediation changes from that which would impede substantial union into a positive condition of operation, cognition, choice, and execution.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-02","text":"Perfection changes from a degree of eminence into the integrity of the whole, then into causally open fecundity and terminal rest.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MP-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-03","text":"The body changes from a good cosmic region into matter constituted by form and finally into the intrinsic medium of singular cognition and action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MP-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-04","text":"Freedom changes from a power of opposites into a mode of adherence, judgment, and acceptance, without conversion into causal independence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:MP-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-05","text":"Time changes from a chronological situation into a plurality of operative, narrative, and terminal measures, whose coordination replaces univocal identity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MP-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly an architectural and transductive machine: the first cause institutes, forms delimit, powers receive, media transfer, and secondary causes execute.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-02","text":"The created subject is a distributed and eccentric node: spirit is ordered through analogical presences, the human being through embodied form, and each through an end above its essence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is a plexus of arcs and a spiral of actualization, in which instituted principles contain future operations, decisions close paths, and the end orders the series.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-04","text":"Navigation is possible in two ways: to ascend from effects, sensibles, and operations to principles, then to descend from universals and ends through media to singular effects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of participated actuality, non-homogeneous unity, non-rival causality, positive mediation, privation, and productive finality suffice to restore the machine of the present level.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the nexus whereby received form distinguishes powers, the higher cause preserves lower acts, and difference is ordered to the integrity of the whole.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B03","text":"The actualization of defectibility, the irreversibility of adherence, the measure of novelty after completion, and the composition of divine motion with finite choice remain less determined.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B04","text":"The system coheres recursively at this magnitude, because the same law of participation changes function through spirit, cosmos, and human being without loss of causal asymmetry.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves true motion if it proceeds from received goodness through differentiation, mediation, and operation to agency and rest; analogies, probable models, and joints between levels resist complete reduction.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L2_02@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_GGVC_L2_02:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Ggvc L2 02","context":"Chapters 49–84 · Prima pars · Quaestio 48 · Prima pars · Quaestio 49 · Prima pars · Quaestio 50 · Prima pars · Quaestio 51 · Prima pars · Quaestio 52 · Prima pars · Quaestio 53 · Prima pars · Quaestio 54 · Prima pars · Quaestio 55 · Prima pars · Quaestio 56 · Prima pars · Quaestio 57 · Prima pars · Quaestio 58 · Prima pars · Quaestio 59 · Prima pars · Quaestio 60 · Prima pars · Quaestio 61 · Prima pars · Quaestio 62 · Prima pars · Quaestio 63 · Prima pars · Quaestio 64 · Prima pars · Quaestio 65 · Prima pars · Quaestio 66 · Prima pars · Quaestio 67 · Prima pars · Quaestio 68 · Prima pars · Quaestio 69 · Prima pars · Quaestio 70 · Prima pars · Quaestio 71 · Prima pars · Quaestio 72 · Prima pars · Quaestio 73 · Prima pars · Quaestio 74 · Prima pars · Quaestio 75 · Prima pars · Quaestio 76 · Prima pars · Quaestio 77 · Prima pars · Quaestio 78 · Prima pars · Quaestio 79 · Prima pars · Quaestio 80 · Prima pars · Quaestio 81 · Prima pars · Quaestio 82 · Prima pars · Quaestio 83"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03","layer":"MC","title":"Ggvc L1 03","order":3,"parents":["CL_GGVC_09","CL_GGVC_10","CL_GGVC_11","CL_GGVC_12"],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_03@R001","sha256":"e96b4bb0af717b7e2aa6a4717e8112d220266a16b9fe419dfcd18a1a11ed0669","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo actus divinus simplicissimus simul ordinem creaturae constituat et pluralitatem personalem internam sustineat, sine mutatione, divisione essentiae aut aequivocatione relationum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_11:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-02","text":"Thesis centralis ponit unam actualitatem divinam quae extrinsecus ordinem finalem producit et intrinsecus per origines relationes subsistentes constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-09","CL_GGVC_10:BAS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-03","text":"Simplicitas non tollit distinctionem, quia actus purus potentiam passivam excludit, dum relatio originis eadem essentiae subsistere potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_10:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-04","text":"Operatio ad creaturas essentiam communem manifestat, sed distinctio personarum tantum ex originibus internis provenit; causalitas externa et causalitas originis igitur formaliter separantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_12:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-05","text":"Verbum fit commissura inter vitam internam et ordinem creatum: ex una intellectione divina personaliter procedit et multitudinem effectuum intelligibiliter continet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:GA-11","CL_GGVC_09:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-06","text":"Argumentatio analogica et apophatica modos creaturales removet, rationes formales servat et eas in functiones ontologicas, personales atque teleologicas restituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:XD-06","CL_GGVC_12:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-07","text":"Motus systematis est spiralis et cumulativus: simplicitas, relatio, intellectus, origo et participatio redeunt, sed singulis reditibus ad campum altiorem transferuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_12:BAS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-08","text":"Negationes non vacuum relinquunt, sed distinctiones positivas inter principium et effectum, essentiam et personam, Verbum et creaturas generant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-09","text":"Cognitio naturalis ad causam essentialem ascendit, sed origines personales non possidet; revelatio rationem ab inventione principiorum ad congruentiam et custodiam consequentiarum transfert.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GA-08","CL_GGVC_11:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-10","text":"Clausura est relationalis, teleologica et epistemice finita: unitas sine confusione, distinctio sine divisione et creatura ad Deum ordinata stabiliuntur, sed mysterium non exhaustive demonstratur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BAS-08","CL_GGVC_11:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_12:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Actus purus omnem potentiam passivam, receptionem perfectionis et successionem internam a Deo excludit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Cognitio, voluntas, potentia, esse et intelligere sunt una actualitas divina, quamvis rationes earum diversa officia generativa sustineant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Essentia una communicabilis et personae per origines distinctae ad ordines formales diversos pertinent, ideo unitas et pluralitas non competitive opponuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Origo divina est processio immanens, realis, asymmetrica et non temporalis, quae intra identitatem naturae manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GA-01","CL_GGVC_12:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Relatio originis in Deo non accidit supposito praeconstituto, sed, quia idem esse cum essentia habet, subsistit et personam constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GA-08","CL_GGVC_12:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Relatio interna originis personas constituit, relatio autem Dei ad creaturam causalitatem essentialem communem exprimit neque distinctionem personalem efficit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_11:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Simplicitas ab interdictione compositionis in principium positivum mutatur, quo relationes essentiales subsistere et pluralitas ordinata sine divisione consistere possunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_11:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Intellectualitas transit ab attributo essentiali communi ad processionem personalem Verbi et ad actum creatum quo obiectum divinum participatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GA-02","CL_GGVC_12:GA-06","CL_GGVC_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Participatio conservat contactum realem sine adaequatione: creatura finita per Filium exemplariter ordinatur et Deum actu proprio attingit, sed causam vel obiectum infinitum non exhaurit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-07","CL_GGVC_12:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Una intellectione divina unum Verbum subsistens procedit, quod Patrem perfecte exprimit et multitudinem creaturarum sine multiplicatione personali continet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Nomen divinum valide transfertur tantum si res significata a modo creaturali distinguatur, modus imperfectus removeatur et perfectio formaliter retenta restituatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:GA-01","CL_GGVC_10:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Ratio naturalis essentiam et virtutem communem cognoscere potest, sed origines personales revelatione indigent; post revelationem ratio congruentiam et non-impossibilitatem administrat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GA-08","CL_GGVC_11:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Sermo theologicus est apparatus typatus in quo essentia, persona, relatio, notio, numerus et ambitus grammaticalis diversas operationes exercent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GA-07","CL_GGVC_11:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Unitas sine confusione et distinctio sine divisione est attractor qui simplicitatem, processionem, personam, significationem et causalitatem mutuo restringit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_11:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] Ordo externus clauditur in participatione intellectuali summi boni, ubi Deus est obiectum et finis, actus autem creatus modus finitus fruitionis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-05","CL_GGVC_09:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-01","text":"[Identitas stabilis] Actus purus per omnes campos manet invarians: neque effectum recipit, neque origine interna dividitur, neque fruitione acquirit quod antea carebat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-01","CL_GGVC_10:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-02","text":"[Redditus functionalis] Relatio redit primo ut asymmetria Dei et creaturae, deinde ut oppositio interna, postremo ut subsistentia personalis; respectus persistit, sed vis constitutiva mutatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-11","CL_GGVC_10:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-03","text":"[Mutatio] Intellectus ordinem finalem cognoscit, Verbum personaliter generat et beatitudinem creaturae formaliter consummat; una radix intellectualis tres gradus causalitatis, originis et participationis induit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_12:GA-06","CL_GGVC_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-04","text":"[Amplificatio] Origo transit a fundamento processionis ad principium oppositionis, inde ad criterium nominis personalis et tandem ad condicionem imaginis originatae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_10:BAS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-05","text":"[Absorptio] Verbum ab exemplo processionis intellectualis in commissuram totius structurae absorbetur, quia simul personam Filii, expressionem Patris et intelligibilitatem creaturarum ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_12:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-06","text":"[Inversio] Simplicitas, initio obiectio contra processionem et pluralitatem, fit condicio qua relatio essentialis subsistit et divisio naturae vitatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_11:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-07","text":"[Amplificatio] Negatio a remotione defectuum localium ad machinam constructivam procedit, quae obiecta, modos relationis et limites cognitionis positive distribuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-08","text":"[Translatio] Participatio migrat a gratia et actu beatifico ad filiationem atque imaginem creaturalem, ita ut receptio finis et derivatio ab exemplari unam asymmetriam creatam exprimant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_12:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-09","text":"[Mutatio] Numerus desinit esse quantitas rei simplicis et fit index ordinis formaliter numerati: processiones, relationes, personae et notiones secundum proprios typos computantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_11:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-10","text":"[Evanescentia modi] Motus localis, materia, accidens, receptio passiva et successio evanescunt postquam falsas mensuras originis, relationis et personae exhibuerunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_10:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-01","text":"Metaphora libri, distinctio potentiae absolutae et ordinatae atque analysis actus beatifici manent machinae locales ordinis externi; hoc gradu communem structuram causalitatis participatae praebent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_09:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-02","text":"Analysis conceptionis intellectualis et inclinationis voluntariae localiter duas processiones distinguit; hic autem fundamentum relationum, personarum et distributionis nominum efficit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_10:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-03","text":"Distinctiones grammaticae masculini et neutri, numeri et exclusionis sunt instrumenta inferiora, sed sursum amplificatae doctrinam generalem significationis typatae constituunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_11:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-04","text":"Nomina Patris, Verbi et Imaginis sunt determinationes personales locales; hoc gradu ostendunt modos originis, non essentiam communem, distributionem proprietatum regere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_12:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-05","text":"Verbum coniungit structuras inferiores quae separatae manebant: processionem internam, expressionem creaturarum et participationem intellectualem ordinis finalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_09:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-06","text":"Revelatio personarum operationem creativam communem non convertit in proprietatem personalem, sed creationem et salutem secundum origines iam revelatas denuo ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_11:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-07","text":"Historia erroris et regulae pertinaciae manent determinationes doctrinales locales; sursum servant tantum principium quod status propositionis ab implicatione et modo cognitionis pendet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_11:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-08","text":"Categoriae motus, accidentis, individuationis materialis et productionis exterioris superantur; earum opus negativum retinetur, sed nulla earum structuram praesentis gradus directe constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_10:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-01","text":"Deus non habet potentiam passivam, scientiam mutabilem aut beatitudinem acquisitam, quia haec omnia perfectionem receptam importarent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-02","text":"Origo divina non est causatio exterior, emissio localis, successio temporalis, separatio substantiae aut hierarchia perfectionis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-03","text":"Relationes divinae non sunt accidentia, formae absolutae superadditae vel essentiae multiplicatae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-04","text":"Persona neque universale neque pars neque individuum materiale neque absolutum solitarium est; incommunicabilitas eius origine relationali constituitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_10:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-05","text":"Effectus creati et appropriationes essentiales origines personales non demonstrant, quia operatio externa communis est.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_10:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-06","text":"Contradictio non est obiectum potentiae, sicut ordo actualis non est mensura exhaustiva possibilitatis divinae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_09:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-07","text":"Vocabula creaturalia neque univoce modos suos in Deum transferunt neque in meras metaphoras sine fundamento dissolvuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_12:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-08","text":"Beatitudo, filiatio et imago creata non sunt possessiones autonomae, sed participationes finitae obiecti et exemplaris divini.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_12:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet simplicitatem significare indistinctionem absolutam; aliter processio, relatio et persona evacuantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet pluralitatem realem divisionem essentiae exigere; aliter personae in substantias separatas converterentur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem distinctionem realem distinctionem absolutorum esse, quia oppositio relativa sine partitione naturae operatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet identitatem relationis cum essentia oppositionem originis absorbere; identitas essendi et distinctio respectuum simul requiruntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet asymmetriam originis temporalem prioritatem, inaequalitatem naturae vel dependentiam substantialem inferre.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet actualitatem creatam possibilitatem divinam metiri aut effectum communem vitam personalem causae exhaustive manifestare.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_11:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-07","text":"Falsum esse oportet analogiam aut identitatem univocam aut aequivocationem plenam postulare; ratio significata sine modo creaturali retineri debet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_12:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-01","text":"Regio intelligibilium a Deo independens, quae intellectionem divinam compleat aut Verbum multiplicet, ontologice inadmissibilis est.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-02","text":"Persona divina sine relatione originis est possibilitas interdicta, quia essentia communis sola incommunicabilitatem personalem non constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-03","text":"Creatura autonome beata, filialis aut imaginalis excluditur; omnis consummatio eius ab obiecto divino et exemplari filiali dependet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_12:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-04","text":"Plena possessio naturalis vitae personalis divinae indisponibilis est, quia effectus communem causam indicant sed origines occultas relinquunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-05","text":"Nullum genus, totum vel spatium ontologicum superius admittitur intra quod essentia, personae et creaturae partes comparabiles fierent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-06","text":"Deus mutabilis, passibilis aut tempore perfectus factus est absentia radicalis circa quam tota machina distinctionum ordinatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-01","text":"Exclusio potentiae passivae et subsistentia relationis demonstrant pluralitatem personalem non esse actualizationem latentis capacitatis, sed ordinem aeternum ipsius actus simplicis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-07","CL_GGVC_09:GA-01","CL_GGVC_10:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-02","text":"Relatio ad creaturam et relatio originis sunt duae asymmetriae formaliter distinctae: prima causalitatem communem exprimit, secunda incommunicabilitatem personalem constituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-06","CL_GGVC_10:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_11:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-03","text":"Unitas intellectus divini simul ordinem finalem cognitum et unum Verbum personale sustinet, sed distinctio rationis prohibet ne praedestinatio, generatio et beatitudo in unam relationem confundantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-10","CL_GGVC_12:GA-11","CL_GGVC_09:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-04","text":"Participatio finis divini et mediatio Filii componunt ordinem in quo creatura Deum actu proprio attingit, dum forma filialis eius perfectionem exemplariter regit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-15","CL_GGVC_09:GA-09","CL_GGVC_12:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-05","text":"Communitas operationis externae explicat simul cur ratio naturalis essentiam divinam attingat et cur ex effectibus distinctionem personarum demonstrare nequeat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-12","CL_GGVC_11:GA-08","CL_GGVC_11:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-06","text":"Eadem disciplina analogica attributa essentialia, processiones et nomina personalia gubernat: modus finitus removetur, ratio formalis servatur et munus divinum secundum ordinem proprium restituitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-13","CL_GGVC_10:XD-06","CL_GGVC_12:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-07","text":"Ordo originis sine successione et ordo effectuum temporalium constituunt temporalitatem stratificatam, in qua aeternitas non immobilitatem vacuam sed causalitatem sine mutatione significat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-15","CL_GGVC_10:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-08","text":"Asymmetria interna principii et procedentis aequalitatem naturae servat, asymmetria externa autem causam infinitam et participationem finitam distinguit; earum confusio aut hierarchiam divinam aut divinizationem creaturae produceret.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-14","CL_GGVC_12:GA-03","CL_GGVC_09:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-01","text":"Realitas divina hoc gradu apparet ut actualitas simplex interne relationibus originis articulata et externe ordines finitos causaliter atque teleologice producens.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_09:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas scinditur sine separatione in originem personalem internam et operationem essentialem externam; prima personas distinguit, altera creaturas communiter producit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_11:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-03","text":"Persona non est substantia absoluta relationem postea recipiens, sed modus subsistendi cuius distinctio ex oppositione originis provenit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_11:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-04","text":"Verbum personaliter procedens fit principium intelligibilitatis creaturae sine eo quod creatura causet, informet aut multiplicet cognitionem divinam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_12:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-05","text":"Tempus systematis continet origines aeternas, ordinem possibilitatis, effectus temporales, praeteritum irrevocabile et consummationem finalem, nullo horum successionem in Deo inferente.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_12:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-06","text":"Subiectum creatum est intellectus participans et similitudo recepta, cui actus proprius realis manet quamvis finis, exemplar et potentia perficiendi ab alio deriventur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_12:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-07","text":"Cognitio theologica est praxis translationis regulatae, non inspectio immediata rei: distinguere debet rationem significatam, modum creatum, fontem epistemicum et ambitum propositionis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_11:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-08","text":"Negatio exercet causalitatem methodologicam: removendo impossibilia non solum doctrinam defendit, sed topologiam positivae distinctionis producit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:XD-05","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-01","text":"Actus purus → actiones immanentes → processiones → relationes oppositae → subsistentia personalis constituit circuitum quo simplicitas distinctionem generat et per distinctionem servatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_10:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-02","text":"Pater dicens → Verbum procedens → expressio creaturarum → effectus temporales → cognitio participata refert ordinem externum ad originem intellectualem sine reciprocatione causali.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-03","text":"Finis divinus → electio et causalitas → media creata → actus beatificus → summum bonum participatum est via qua causalitas externa in teleologiam creaturae convertitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_09:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-04","text":"Vocabulum creaturale → separatio modi → retentio rationis → restitutio divina → disciplina ambitus format circuitum semanticum communem attributis et nominibus personalibus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_10:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-05","text":"Obiectio ex compositione vel limitatione → distinctio ordinum → remotio falsae mensurae → affirmatio intensior perfectionis est circuitus dialecticus totius systematis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_10:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-06","text":"Terminus demonstrationis → revelatio originum → congruentia rationalis → custodia consequentiarum efficit ut limitatio epistemica novam agentiam interpretativam generet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_11:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-07","text":"Attractor supremus est actualitas una, relationaliter distincta et participabiliter communicata; circa eam metaphysica, semantica, epistemologia et teleologia convergunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_11:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B01","text":"Actus purus + identitas essentiae et operationum → exclusio potentiae passivae, compositionis et successionis internae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-01","CL_GGVC_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B02","text":"Intelligere et velle ut actiones immanentes → processiones internas quae naturam non dividunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GA-02","CL_GGVC_10:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B03","text":"Processio immanens → ordo principii et procedentis → oppositiones relativae → relationes subsistentes → personae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_10:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B04","text":"Communicatio unius essentiae + oppositio originis → unitas naturae sine confusione personarum et pluralitas personarum sine divisione substantiae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GA-01","CL_GGVC_10:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B05","text":"Distinctio essentiae, personae, relationis et notionis → numeratio typata et grammatica ambitu regulata.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GA-06","CL_GGVC_11:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B06","text":"Processio intellectualis subsistens → Verbum et Filius → Imago perfecta → expressio unica multitudinis creaturarum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:GA-06","CL_GGVC_12:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B07","text":"Expressio creaturarum in Verbo → possibilitas et productio finita → participatio filialis et intellectualis → ordinatio ad summum bonum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:XD-03","CL_GGVC_09:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B08","text":"Causalitas externa communis + origines internae occultae → terminus cognitionis naturalis → revelatio → reconstructio analogica et defensio rationalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:XD-04","CL_GGVC_11:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:AFM-B01","text":"Actus purus → processio immanens → relatio originis → oppositio subsistens → persona → nomen personale → unitas distincta.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_10:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:AFM-B02","text":"Modus creaturalis suspectus → purgatio apophatica → ratio formalis retenta → typus semanticus determinatus → praedicatio divina restituta.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_12:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:AFM-B03","text":"Auto-intellectio divina → Verbum personale → creaturae intelligibiliter expressae → productio temporalis → participatio intellectualis → finis beatificus → clausura teleologica sub mysterio revelato.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_09:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-01","text":"Unitas et pluralitas reclassificantur ex contrariis quantitativis in ordines essentiae communis et relationum subsistentium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:MME-02","CL_GGVC_11:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-02","text":"Persona reclassificatur ex individuo absoluto in nodum subsistentem originis, oppositionis et incommunicabilitatis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_11:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-03","text":"Causalitas reordinatur in operationem essentialem ad extra et originem personalem ad intra, quarum neque identitas neque separatio admittitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_11:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-04","text":"Intellectus fit nexus ontologicus inter ordinem finalem, processionem Verbi, expressionem creaturarum et consummationem beatificam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-06","CL_GGVC_12:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-05","text":"Numerus et grammatica desinunt rem divinam immediate imitari et fiunt operationes typatae quae diversos ordines sine confusione custodiunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:MME-02","CL_GGVC_11:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-06","text":"Apophasis reclassificatur ex pura remotione in methodum generativam quae limites obiecti, originis, significationis et cognitionis constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-07","text":"Revelatio non addit tantum propositiones cognitioni naturali, sed agrum creationis, personae, salutis et interpretationis secundum origines personales reordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:MME-05","CL_GGVC_11:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei tractat cognitionem, potentiam, beatitudinem, processiones, relationes, personas, numerationes atque nomina Patris, Verbi et Imaginis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_12:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis distribuit actum simplicem in operationes essentiales communes, origines personales, relationes subsistentes, effectus creatos et participationes finales.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_09:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus mutationem quaestionis exercet: apparentem contradictionem aut limitationem in errorem typi, ambitus, obiecti vel modi significandi convertit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_12:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis ponit aeternam actualitatem relationaliter ordinatam, campum possibilitatis inexhaustum, effectus temporales et terminum finalem stabilem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_12:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus et performativus propriam doctrinam efficit, quia per distinctionem, exclusionem et restitutionem formam unitatis non aequatricis producit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_12:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-01","text":"Intellectus et voluntas realiter unum sunt, sed rationes eorum duas processiones reales sustinere debent; distinctio rationis hic efficaciam ontologicam maximam fert.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:FL-01","CL_GGVC_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-02","text":"Relatio idem esse cum essentia habet et tamen relationi oppositae realiter distinguitur; nexus inter identitatem essendi et oppositionem manet commissura principalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:FL-02","CL_GGVC_10:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-03","text":"Actus purus omnem potentialitatem passivam excludit, dum processio realem originem requirit; systema originem sine mutatione affirmat, sed analogia huius causalitatis integre non demonstratur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-01","CL_GGVC_10:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-04","text":"Operatio creativa communis personas non distinguit, sed Verbum creaturas personaliter exprimit; relatio inter causalitatem communem et mediationem filialem distinctione ordinum sustinetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-05","text":"Unum Verbum multitudinem creaturarum exprimit sine multiplicatione verbi; modus quo pluralitas respectuum unitatem expressionis non dividat minus determinatus manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:FL-02","CL_GGVC_12:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-06","text":"Participatio similitudinem veram postulat, dum omnis mensura univoca recusatur; proportio inter exemplar divinum et actum creatum nondum plene formalizatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:FL-05","CL_GGVC_12:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-01","text":"Simplicitas mutatur ab obiectione negativa contra pluralitatem in principium positivum subsistentiae relationalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_11:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-02","text":"Relatio mutatur ab asymmetria causalitatis externae in oppositionem internam et tandem in personam subsistentem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_10:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-03","text":"Intellectus mutatur ab attributo essentiali communi in processionem Verbi et deinde in actum beatificum creatum, sine identificatione horum trium officiorum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-06","CL_GGVC_12:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-04","text":"Ratio mutatur a conatu demonstrandi origines personales in disciplinam congruentiae, non-impossibilitatis et consequentiarum doctrinalium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GA-09","CL_GGVC_11:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-05","text":"Negatio mutatur a remotione defectus in operationem generativam quae principium, relationem, personam et participationem positive distribuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-01","text":"Imago minima est machina actualitatis simplicis quae intrinsecus respectus oppositos in personas densat et extrinsecus effectus finitos ad se ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_09:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-02","text":"Persona divina intra hanc machinam est nodus subsistens originis, non atomus clausus neque portio naturae communis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_11:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-03","text":"Creatura est intellectus et similitudo recepta quae per actum proprium ad obiectum infinitum tendit, sed exemplar et finem ex se non producit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_12:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-04","text":"Tempus est ordo duplicatus: aeternitas originum sine successione continet intelligibilitatem effectuum quorum productio, mutatio et consummatio temporaliter distribuuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_12:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-05","text":"Navigatio fit per subtractionem et reconstitutionem: falsus modus removetur, relatio propria detegitur, typus semanticus imponitur et participatio ad attractorem divinum dirigitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_12:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de actu puro, essentia una, processione immanente, relatione subsistente, Verbo unico, significatione analogica et participatione creata ad structuram praesentis gradus restituendam sufficiunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_12:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est catena quae operationem immanentem in originem, originem in relationem, relationem in personam et Verbum in principium intelligibilitatis creaturae convertit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_12:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manet derivatio completa personarum ex quattuor relationibus et duabus processionibus, quia distributio singularum relationum partim ex doctrina praesupposita recipitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:FL-04","CL_GGVC_11:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MRT-B04","text":"Minus determinata etiam manet coordinatio inter ordinem supernaturalem beatificum et mediationem filialem, ne actus essentialis communis in proprietatem personalem tacite convertatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_12:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat, quia a simplici actualitate per originem et significationem ad expressionem creaturarum atque participationem finalem progreditur, non secundum ordinem capitularem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_11:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_12:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MRT-B06","text":"Reductioni plenae resistunt proportio analogica, transitus a rationibus operationum ad processiones reales et revelatio quae origines personales aperit sine earum demonstratione naturali.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_11:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T173637855780Z_fa2f76e2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Ggvc L1 03","source_sha256":"e96b4bb0af717b7e2aa6a4717e8112d220266a16b9fe419dfcd18a1a11ed0669","sha256":"2f6579f256f93efba1b5dbe9bc07c8bb790890779e8ac85d4638d82b59e2517c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:36:37.857186+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how the most simple divine act simultaneously constitutes the order of creation and sustains internal personal plurality, without change, division of essence, or equivocation of relations.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_11:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-02","text":"The central thesis posits one divine actuality which extrinsically produces the final order and intrinsically constitutes subsisting relations through origins.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-09","CL_GGVC_10:BAS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-03","text":"Simplicity does not remove distinction, because pure act excludes passive potency, while a relation of origin can subsist as the same as the essence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_10:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-04","text":"Operation toward creatures manifests the common essence, but the distinction of persons proceeds only from internal origins; external causality and causality of origin are therefore formally separated.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_12:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-05","text":"The Word becomes the joint between internal life and the created order: it proceeds personally from one divine intellection and intelligibly contains a multitude of effects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:GA-11","CL_GGVC_09:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-06","text":"Analogical and apophatic argumentation removes creaturely modes, preserves formal accounts, and restores them in ontological, personal, and teleological functions.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:XD-06","CL_GGVC_12:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-07","text":"The movement of the system is spiral and cumulative: simplicity, relation, intellect, origin, and participation recur, but at each return they are transferred to a higher field.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_12:BAS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-08","text":"Negations do not leave a void, but generate positive distinctions between principle and effect, essence and person, Word and creatures.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-09","text":"Natural knowledge ascends to the essential cause, but does not possess personal origins; revelation transfers reason from discovering principles to congruity and the safeguarding of consequences.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GA-08","CL_GGVC_11:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BAS-10","text":"The closure is relational, teleological, and epistemically finite: unity without confusion, distinction without division, and the creature ordered to God are established, but the mystery is not exhaustively demonstrated.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BAS-08","CL_GGVC_11:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_12:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Pure act excludes from God all passive potency, reception of perfection, and internal succession.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Knowing, willing, power, being, and understanding are one divine actuality, although their accounts sustain diverse generative offices.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] The one communicable essence and persons distinguished through origins belong to diverse formal orders; therefore unity and plurality are not opposed competitively.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Divine origin is an immanent, real, asymmetric, and non-temporal procession which remains within the identity of nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GA-01","CL_GGVC_12:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] A relation of origin in God does not accrue to a preconstituted supposit, but, because it has the same being as the essence, subsists and constitutes a person.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GA-08","CL_GGVC_12:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] The internal relation of origin constitutes persons, whereas the relation of God to the creature expresses common essential causality and does not effect personal distinction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_11:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Simplicity is transformed from a prohibition of composition into a positive principle by which essential relations can subsist and ordered plurality can stand without division.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_11:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Intellectuality passes from a common essential attribute to the personal procession of the Word and to the created act by which the divine object is participated.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GA-02","CL_GGVC_12:GA-06","CL_GGVC_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Participation preserves real contact without adequation: the finite creature is ordered exemplarily through the Son and attains God by its own act, but does not exhaust the infinite cause or object.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-07","CL_GGVC_12:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] From one divine intellection proceeds one subsisting Word, which perfectly expresses the Father and contains a multitude of creatures without personal multiplication.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] A divine name is validly transferred only if the thing signified is distinguished from its creaturely mode, the imperfect mode is removed, and the perfection formally retained is restored.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:GA-01","CL_GGVC_10:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Natural reason can know the common essence and power, but personal origins require revelation; after revelation, reason manages congruity and non-impossibility.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GA-08","CL_GGVC_11:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Theological discourse is a typed apparatus in which essence, person, relation, notion, number, and grammatical scope exercise diverse operations.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GA-07","CL_GGVC_11:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Unity without confusion and distinction without division is the attractor that mutually constrains simplicity, procession, person, signification, and causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_11:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] The external order closes in intellectual participation in the highest good, where God is object and end, while the created act is the finite mode of enjoyment.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-05","CL_GGVC_09:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Pure act remains invariant through all fields: it neither receives an effect, nor is divided by internal origin, nor acquires through enjoyment what it previously lacked.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-01","CL_GGVC_10:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-02","text":"[Functional return] Relation returns first as the asymmetry of God and creature, then as internal opposition, and finally as personal subsistence; the respect persists, but its constitutive force changes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-11","CL_GGVC_10:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] Intellect knows the final order, personally generates the Word, and formally consummates the creature's beatitude; one intellectual root assumes three levels of causality, origin, and participation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_12:GA-06","CL_GGVC_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-04","text":"[Amplification] Origin passes from the foundation of procession to the principle of opposition, thence to the criterion of a personal name, and finally to the condition of an originated image.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_10:BAS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-05","text":"[Absorption] The Word is absorbed from an example of intellectual procession into the joint of the whole structure, because it simultaneously orders the person of the Son, the expression of the Father, and the intelligibility of creatures.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_12:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-06","text":"[Inversion] Simplicity, initially an objection against procession and plurality, becomes the condition by which an essential relation subsists and division of nature is avoided.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_11:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-07","text":"[Amplification] Negation proceeds from removing local defects to a constructive mechanism that positively distributes objects, modes of relation, and limits of knowledge.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-08","text":"[Translation] Participation migrates from grace and the beatific act to filiation and the creaturely image, so that reception of the end and derivation from the exemplar express one created asymmetry.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_12:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-09","text":"[Mutation] Number ceases to be the quantity of a simple thing and becomes the index of a formally numbered order: processions, relations, persons, and notions are counted according to their proper types.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_11:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RMD-10","text":"[Vanishing of mode] Local motion, matter, accident, passive reception, and succession vanish after they have exhibited false measures of origin, relation, and person.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_10:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-01","text":"The metaphor of the book, the distinction of absolute and ordained power, and the analysis of the beatific act remain local mechanisms of the external order; at this level they provide the common structure of participated causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_09:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-02","text":"The analysis of intellectual conception and voluntary inclination locally distinguishes two processions; here, however, it constitutes the foundation of relations, persons, and the distribution of names.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_10:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-03","text":"Grammatical distinctions of masculine and neuter, number, and exclusion are lower instruments, but when amplified upward they constitute a general doctrine of typed signification.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_11:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-04","text":"The names Father, Word, and Image are local personal determinations; at this level they show that modes of origin, not the common essence, govern the distribution of properties.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_12:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-05","text":"The Word joins lower structures that remained separate: internal procession, the expression of creatures, and intellectual participation in the final order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_09:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-06","text":"The revelation of persons does not convert common creative operation into personal property, but reorders creation and salvation according to already revealed origins.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_11:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-07","text":"The history of error and rules of pertinacity remain local doctrinal determinations; upward, they retain only the principle that the status of a proposition depends on implication and mode of knowledge.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_11:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:CGD-08","text":"The categories of motion, accident, material individuation, and external production are surpassed; their negative work is retained, but none of them directly constitutes the structure of the present level.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_10:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-01","text":"God does not have passive potency, mutable knowledge, or acquired beatitude, because all these would imply received perfection.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-02","text":"Divine origin is not external causation, local emission, temporal succession, separation of substance, or hierarchy of perfection.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-03","text":"Divine relations are not accidents, superadded absolute forms, or multiplied essences.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-04","text":"A person is neither a universal, nor a part, nor a material individual, nor a solitary absolute; its incommunicability is constituted by relational origin.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_10:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-05","text":"Created effects and essential appropriations do not demonstrate personal origins, because external operation is common.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_10:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-06","text":"Contradiction is not an object of power, just as the actual order is not an exhaustive measure of divine possibility.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_09:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-07","text":"Creaturely terms neither transfer their modes univocally to God nor dissolve into mere metaphors without foundation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_12:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:GNX-08","text":"Created beatitude, filiation, and image are not autonomous possessions, but finite participations in the divine object and exemplar.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_12:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that simplicity signifies absolute indistinction; otherwise procession, relation, and person are emptied.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that real plurality requires division of essence; otherwise persons would be converted into separate substances.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that every real distinction is a distinction of absolutes, because relative opposition operates without partition of nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that the identity of relation with essence absorbs the opposition of origin; identity of being and distinction of respects are simultaneously required.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the asymmetry of origin entails temporal priority, inequality of nature, or substantial dependence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that created actuality measures divine possibility or that a common effect exhaustively manifests the personal life of the cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_11:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:DBN-07","text":"It must be false that analogy demands either univocal identity or complete equivocation; the account signified must be retained without creaturely mode.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_12:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-01","text":"A realm of intelligibles independent of God, which completes divine intellection or multiplies the Word, is ontologically inadmissible.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-02","text":"A divine person without a relation of origin is a prohibited possibility, because the common essence alone does not constitute personal incommunicability.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-03","text":"An autonomously beatified, filial, or imaginal creature is excluded; all its consummation depends on the divine object and filial exemplar.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_12:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-04","text":"Full natural possession of divine personal life is unavailable, because effects indicate the common cause but leave origins hidden.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-05","text":"No genus, whole, or higher ontological space is admitted within which essence, persons, and creatures would become comparable parts.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSA-06","text":"A mutable or passible God, or God made perfect in time, is the radical absence around which the entire mechanism of distinctions is ordered.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-01","text":"The exclusion of passive potency and the subsistence of relation demonstrate that personal plurality is not the actualization of a latent capacity, but the eternal order of simple act itself.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-07","CL_GGVC_09:GA-01","CL_GGVC_10:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-02","text":"The relation to the creature and the relation of origin are two formally distinct asymmetries: the former expresses common causality, the latter constitutes personal incommunicability.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-06","CL_GGVC_10:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_11:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-03","text":"The unity of divine intellect simultaneously sustains the known final order and one personal Word, but distinction of reason prevents predestination, generation, and beatitude from being confused into one relation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-10","CL_GGVC_12:GA-11","CL_GGVC_09:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-04","text":"Participation in the divine end and mediation by the Son compose an order in which the creature attains God by its own act, while its filial form exemplarily governs its perfection.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-15","CL_GGVC_09:GA-09","CL_GGVC_12:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-05","text":"The commonality of external operation explains both why natural reason attains the divine essence and why it cannot demonstrate the distinction of persons from effects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-12","CL_GGVC_11:GA-08","CL_GGVC_11:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-06","text":"The same analogical discipline governs essential attributes, processions, and personal names: finite mode is removed, the formal account is retained, and the divine office is restored according to its proper order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-13","CL_GGVC_10:XD-06","CL_GGVC_12:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-07","text":"The order of origin without succession and the order of temporal effects constitute stratified temporality, in which eternity signifies not empty immobility but causality without change.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-15","CL_GGVC_10:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:XD-08","text":"The internal asymmetry of principle and proceeding preserves equality of nature, while external asymmetry distinguishes infinite cause and finite participation; their confusion would produce either divine hierarchy or divinization of the creature.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_03:GA-14","CL_GGVC_12:GA-03","CL_GGVC_09:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-01","text":"At this level divine reality appears as a simple actuality internally articulated by relations of origin and externally producing finite orders causally and teleologically.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_09:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-02","text":"Causality is divided without separation into internal personal origin and external essential operation; the former distinguishes persons, the latter commonly produces creatures.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_11:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-03","text":"A person is not an absolute substance that subsequently receives relation, but a mode of subsisting whose distinction proceeds from opposition of origin.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_11:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-04","text":"The personally proceeding Word becomes the principle of creaturely intelligibility without the creature causing, informing, or multiplying divine knowledge.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_12:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-05","text":"The time of the system contains eternal origins, the order of possibility, temporal effects, an irrevocable past, and final consummation, none of these entailing succession in God.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_12:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-06","text":"The created subject is a participating intellect and received likeness, whose own act remains real although its end, exemplar, and power of perfection are derived from another.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_12:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-07","text":"Theological knowledge is a practice of regulated translation, not immediate inspection of the thing: it must distinguish the account signified, creaturely mode, epistemic source, and scope of proposition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_11:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:BSD-08","text":"Negation exercises methodological causality: by removing impossibilities it not only defends doctrine, but produces the topology of positive distinction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:XD-05","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-01","text":"Pure act → immanent actions → processions → opposed relations → personal subsistence constitutes the circuit by which simplicity generates distinction and is preserved through distinction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_10:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-02","text":"Father speaking → proceeding Word → expression of creatures → temporal effects → participated knowledge refers the external order to intellectual origin without causal reciprocity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-03","text":"Divine end → election and causality → created means → beatific act → participated highest good is the path by which external causality is converted into the creature's teleology.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_09:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-04","text":"Creaturely term → separation of mode → retention of account → divine restoration → discipline of scope forms a semantic circuit common to attributes and personal names.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_10:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-05","text":"Objection from composition or limitation → distinction of orders → removal of a false measure → stronger affirmation of perfection is the dialectical circuit of the entire system.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_10:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-06","text":"The limit of demonstration → revelation of origins → rational congruity → safeguarding of consequences causes epistemic limitation to generate new interpretive agency.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_11:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:RLE-07","text":"The supreme attractor is one actuality, relationally distinct and communicable by participation; metaphysics, semantics, epistemology, and teleology converge around it.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_11:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B01","text":"Pure act + identity of essence and operations → exclusion of passive potency, composition, and internal succession.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-01","CL_GGVC_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B02","text":"Understanding and willing as immanent actions → internal processions that do not divide nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GA-02","CL_GGVC_10:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B03","text":"Immanent procession → order of principle and proceeding → relative oppositions → subsisting relations → persons.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_10:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B04","text":"Communication of one essence + opposition of origin → unity of nature without confusion of persons and plurality of persons without division of substance.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GA-01","CL_GGVC_10:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B05","text":"Distinction of essence, person, relation, and notion → typed enumeration and scope-regulated grammar.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GA-06","CL_GGVC_11:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B06","text":"Subsisting intellectual procession → Word and Son → perfect Image → unique expression of a multitude of creatures.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:GA-06","CL_GGVC_12:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B07","text":"Expression of creatures in the Word → possibility and finite production → filial and intellectual participation → ordering to the highest good.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:XD-03","CL_GGVC_09:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FGS-B08","text":"Common external causality + hidden internal origins → limit of natural knowledge → revelation → analogical reconstruction and rational defense.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:XD-04","CL_GGVC_11:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:AFM-B01","text":"Pure act → immanent procession → relation of origin → subsisting opposition → person → personal name → distinct unity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_10:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:AFM-B02","text":"Suspect creaturely mode → apophatic purification → retained formal account → determined semantic type → restored divine predication.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_12:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:AFM-B03","text":"Divine self-intellection → personal Word → creatures intelligibly expressed → temporal production → intellectual participation → beatific end → teleological closure under revealed mystery.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_09:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-01","text":"Unity and plurality are reclassified from quantitative contraries into orders of common essence and subsisting relations.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:MME-02","CL_GGVC_11:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-02","text":"Person is reclassified from an absolute individual into a subsisting node of origin, opposition, and incommunicability.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_11:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-03","text":"Causality is reordered into essential operation ad extra and personal origin ad intra, of which neither identity nor separation is admitted.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_11:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-04","text":"Intellect becomes the ontological nexus between the final order, the procession of the Word, the expression of creatures, and beatific consummation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-06","CL_GGVC_12:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-05","text":"Number and grammar cease immediately to imitate the divine thing and become typed operations that preserve diverse orders without confusion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:MME-02","CL_GGVC_11:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-06","text":"Apophasis is reclassified from pure removal into a generative method that constitutes the limits of object, origin, signification, and knowledge.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MME-07","text":"Revelation does not merely add propositions to natural knowledge, but reorders the field of creation, person, salvation, and interpretation according to personal origins.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:MME-05","CL_GGVC_11:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level treats knowledge, power, beatitude, processions, relations, persons, enumerations, and the names of Father, Word, and Image.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_12:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level distributes simple act into common essential operations, personal origins, subsisting relations, created effects, and final participations.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_09:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level enacts a change of question: it converts apparent contradiction or limitation into an error of type, scope, object, or mode of signifying.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_12:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level posits eternally actualized relational order, an inexhausted field of possibility, temporal effects, and a stable final term.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_12:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological and performative level effects its own doctrine, because through distinction, exclusion, and restoration it produces a form of non-equating unity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_12:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-01","text":"Intellect and will are really one, but their accounts must sustain two real processions; distinction of reason here bears maximal ontological efficacy.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:FL-01","CL_GGVC_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-02","text":"Relation has the same being as essence and yet is really distinguished from the opposed relation; the nexus between identity of being and opposition remains the principal joint.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:FL-02","CL_GGVC_10:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-03","text":"Pure act excludes all passive potentiality, while procession requires real origin; the system affirms origin without change, but the analogy of this causality is not wholly demonstrated.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-01","CL_GGVC_10:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-04","text":"Common creative operation does not distinguish persons, but the Word personally expresses creatures; the relation between common causality and filial mediation is sustained by distinction of orders.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-05","text":"One Word expresses a multitude of creatures without multiplication of the word; the mode by which plurality of respects does not divide unity of expression remains less determined.","refs":["CL_GGVC_12:FL-02","CL_GGVC_12:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:FL-06","text":"Participation demands true likeness, while every univocal measure is refused; the proportion between divine exemplar and created act is not yet fully formalized.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:FL-05","CL_GGVC_12:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-01","text":"Simplicity changes from a negative objection against plurality into a positive principle of relational subsistence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_11:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-02","text":"Relation changes from the asymmetry of external causality into internal opposition and finally into a subsisting person.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_10:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-03","text":"Intellect changes from a common essential attribute into the procession of the Word and then into the created beatific act, without identification of these three offices.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:GA-06","CL_GGVC_12:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-04","text":"Reason changes from an attempt to demonstrate personal origins into a discipline of congruity, non-impossibility, and doctrinal consequences.","refs":["CL_GGVC_11:GA-09","CL_GGVC_11:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MP-05","text":"Negation changes from removal of defect into a generative operation that positively distributes principle, relation, person, and participation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_12:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-01","text":"The minimal image is a mechanism of simple actuality that intrinsically condenses opposed respects into persons and extrinsically orders finite effects to itself.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_09:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-02","text":"Within this mechanism, the divine person is a subsisting node of origin, not a closed atom or a portion of common nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_11:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-03","text":"The creature is a received intellect and likeness which tends through its own act toward the infinite object, but does not produce from itself the exemplar and end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_12:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-04","text":"Time is a doubled order: the eternity of origins without succession contains the intelligibility of effects whose production, change, and consummation are temporally distributed.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_12:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:HSI-05","text":"Navigation occurs through subtraction and reconstitution: the false mode is removed, the proper relation is disclosed, a semantic type is imposed, and participation is directed toward the divine attractor.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_12:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MRT-B01","text":"Axioms concerning pure act, one essence, immanent procession, subsisting relation, the one Word, analogical signification, and created participation suffice to reconstruct the structure of the present level.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_12:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the chain that converts immanent operation into origin, origin into relation, relation into person, and the Word into the principle of creaturely intelligibility.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_12:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MRT-B03","text":"The complete derivation of persons from four relations and two processions remains less determined, because the distribution of individual relations is received partly from presupposed doctrine.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:FL-04","CL_GGVC_11:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MRT-B04","text":"The coordination between the supernatural beatific order and filial mediation also remains less determined, lest the common essential act be tacitly converted into personal property.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_12:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement, because it progresses from simple actuality through origin and signification to the expression of creatures and final participation, not according to chapter order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_09:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_11:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_12:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_03:MRT-B06","text":"Analogical proportion, the transition from accounts of operations to real processions, and revelation, which discloses personal origins without their natural demonstration, resist complete reduction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_10:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_11:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_03@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_GGVC_L1_03:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Ggvc L1 03","context":"Chapters 25–36 · Prima pars · Quaestio 24 · Prima pars · Quaestio 25 · Prima pars · Quaestio 26 · Prima pars · Quaestio 27 · Prima pars · Quaestio 28 · Prima pars · Quaestio 29 · Prima pars · Quaestio 30 · Prima pars · Quaestio 31 · Prima pars · Quaestio 32 · Prima pars · Quaestio 33 · Prima pars · Quaestio 34 · Prima pars · Quaestio 35"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03","layer":"MC","title":"Ggvc L2 03","order":3,"parents":["MC_GGVC_L1_08","MC_GGVC_L1_09","MC_GGVC_L1_10"],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L2_03@R001","sha256":"9b2a5f82931525148046f1ace04ca1856e38c5ef9f4bafcc5084f685bf43f1eb","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo homo et omnis creatura finita identitatem, libertatem atque operationem propriam servent intra dependentiam ontologicam, influxus multiplices et mutationes status.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-02","text":"Thesis globalis statuit modum essendi, proportionem recipientis, medium, statum et finem causalitatem ita contrahere ut dependentia a causa prima agentiam secundam constituat potius quam evacuat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit grammatica attributionis distributae, quae oppositiones per distinctiones ordinum, causarum, receptionum et temporum in relationes non aequivalentes convertit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-04","text":"Motus ab interfacie corporea cognitionis ad communicationem continuam esse atque inde ad reticulum providentiale causarum extenditur, ita ut anthropologia casus intensissimus ontologiae participationis fiat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-05","text":"Progressus est recursivus, spiralis et mutationibus status punctuatus: eadem mediatio redit, sed eius medium, recipiens, ambitus et effectus singulis reditibus mutantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-06","text":"Defectus, corruptio, casus et anomaliae non extra ordinem collocantur, sed in indices limitum causalium, privationum localium et diversarum competentiarum reclassificantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-07","text":"Attractor operativus est mediatio proportionata: limitatio causae creatae eius ambitum restringit, sed eadem restrictio propriam efficaciam, responsabilitatem et facultatem communicandi constituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:RLE-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-08","text":"Clausura est asymmetrica et terminalis: causae creatae usque ad fines suos vere operantur, sed visio beatifica, stabilitas ultima et quaedam initia ontologica actionem divinam immediatam requirunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Esse participatum sub providentia non solum permanentiam creaturae sustentat, sed potentiam eius agendi, recipiendi et communicandi generat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Modus essendi agentis et dispositio recipientis modum operationis, amplitudinem effectus atque mensuram cognitionis determinant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Causalitas prima et causalitates secundae non competitive concurrunt, quia causa prima facultatem et ordinem constituit intra quae causa creata proprie operatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Mediatio ab interfacie cognitionis corporalis in conservationem et communicationem boni, deinde in formam generalem administrationis, instrumenti et applicationis causalis amplificatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Receptio est pars causalitatis, quia influxus secundum proportionem, statum et dispositionem recipientis contrahitur atque in effectum proprium convertitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Corpus transit ab apparenti impedimento vel obiecto gubernationis ad interfaciem constitutivam cognitionis, maturationis, actionis et identitatis compositae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Subiectum finitum est principium intrinsecum, nodus receptivus et membrana selectiva: influxibus patet, sed actum proprium elicit nec consensum necessario cedit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Identitas creata persistit per naturam, formam, habitus et relationes ordinate continuas, non per immobilitatem materiae, mediorum vel operationum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Limites corporis, temporis et competentiae causalis sunt condiciones positivae operationis finitae, quamvis corruptioni, privationi et impedimento subiaceant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Idem effectus pluribus causis secundum respectus inaequales attribui potest, unde cognitio causalis modum receptionis, destinationem et competentiam agentis reconstruere debet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Malum et defectus privationes ordinis particularis manent: localiter imputabilia sunt, sed neque naturam aemulam neque exterioritatem a providentia constituunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Ordo creatus fines intrinsecos habet quos nulla intensificatio naturalis transit: visio divina, miraculum proprie dictum et productio principii intellectivi clausuras immediatae actionis divinae constituunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Bonum ultimum et gloria omnes ordines naturales, morales et causales colligunt, sed creaturam perficiunt sine conversione finitudinis in autonomiam.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-08","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-01","text":"[Identitas stabilis] Proportio recipientis persistit a cognitione corporali ad participationem ontologicam et causalitatem universaliter distributam.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-02","text":"[Reditus functionalis] Mediatio redit ut phantasma, conservatio, communicatio, ministerium et instrumentum; communis eius functio est potentiam alterius ad actum proportionatum ordinare.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-03","text":"[Mutatio] Corpus ab impedimento apparenti ad medium maturationis atque tandem ad co-principium intrinsecum compositi transit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-04","text":"[Translatio] Hierarchia a dominio scalari ad topologiam canalium, functionum et attributionum causalium transfertur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-05","text":"[Amplificatio] Status ab indice operationis humanae in principium temporale generale migrat, quo maturatio, applicatio causae et mutatio regiminis inter se distinguuntur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-06","text":"[Inversio] Limitatio et dependentia vim invertunt: quae defectus autonomiae videbantur fiunt condiciones operationis propriae et competentiae determinatae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-07","text":"[Absorptio] Malum, casus et anomaliae absorbentur in ordinem causalitatis distributae, sed differentia inter privationem, imputabilitatem et ordinationem superiorem non deletur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-08","text":"[Transformatio terminalis] Immediatio desinit esse simplex absentia medii atque fit nomen asymmetricum causalitatis divinae et consummationis quae regimina creata mutat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-01","text":"[Localis] Somnium, phantasmata, humores, geographiae et processus physiologici manent mechanismi locales; praesentem structuram regunt tantum quatenus mediationem et dispositionem recipientis exemplificant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-02","text":"[Amplificata] Mediatio corporis et receptio particularis ascendunt in legem generalem qua omne influxum finitum secundum medium et recipientem transducitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-03","text":"[Coniungens] Participatio essendi, conservatio continua et providentia unitatem praebent intra quam causae particulares et operationes humanae proprie agunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-04","text":"[Transitionalis] Habitus, forma et status sunt vectores continuitatis qui effectus praeteritorum actuum per mutationes medii, materiae et operationis transferunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-05","text":"[Recodificata] Hierarchia et auctoritas recodificantur ut officia proportionata ordinandi condiciones actionis alterius, non ut dominium totale recipientis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-06","text":"[Absorpta] Taxonomiae angelicae, nomenclaturae physiologicae et rationes seminales evanescunt postquam munus discriminandi gradus, media et limina causalia perfecerunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-07","text":"[Non amplificata] Conclusiones sexuales, domesticae et quaedam applicationes exegeticae ad hunc gradum non ascendunt, quia ex causalitate participata et receptione proportionata non necessario sequuntur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-01","text":"Systema excludit planum causale homogeneum in quo Deus, intellectus, voluntas, corpus et materia eodem modo agant vel inter se competitive distribuant effectum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-02","text":"Creatura neque sui causa neque fons propriae permanentiae neque potestas universaliter immediata esse potest.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-03","text":"Humanitas nec ad animam separatam nec ad corpus tamquam carcerem reducitur; perfectio compositionem mutare potest, non eius structuram naturalem falsam reddere.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-03","MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-04","text":"Una mensura univoca perfectionis, cognitionis vel causalitatis omnibus naturis, statibus et recipientibus applicari non potest.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-05","text":"Immediatio creata, transparentia mentium et evidentia causae ex solo effectu excluduntur, quia omnis cognitio finita mediis, accessu et reconstructione limitatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-06","text":"Malum, culpa, casus et corruptio nullum ordinem ontologicum extra providentiam constituunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-07","text":"Consummatio non est intensificatio naturalis status originarii, vitae generativae vel causalitatis creatae, sed transitus ad terminum disproportionatum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet dependentiam causalitatis inferioris agentiam vel libertatem tollere; aliter omnis participatio operationem propriam evacuaret.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet identitatem exigere eandem materiam, idem medium vel eundem modum operationis per omne tempus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:DBN-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet mediationem aut transmissionem passivam aut diminutionem causae principalis esse, quia ministerium et instrumentum effectum vere determinant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet effectum sensibilem causam suam exhaustive demonstrare; aliter miraculum, influxus et causalitas ordinaria sola apparentia distinguerentur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet recipientem esse terminum inertem influxus, quia principium intrinsecum receptionem in actum proprium et accessum selectivum convertit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet ordinem creatum finem suum ultimum intrinsece claudere vel consummationem ex extensione indefinita processus temporalis producere.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-01","text":"Absentia radicalis circa quam systema ordinatur est creatura sui fundamentum, principium intrinsecum et finis ultimus effecta.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-02","text":"Immediatio finita et possessio universalis rei, alterius mentis vel totius ordinis sunt possibilitates indisponibiles.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-03","text":"Exterioritas ontologica a causa prima et providentia est inadmissibilis, quia talis exterioritas non esset alter ordo sed privatio participationis essendi.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-04","text":"Indisponibilis est status creatus qui se ipsum consumment, aversionem irreversibiliter claudat aut finitudinem in beatitudinem naturaliter convertat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-05","text":"Totalis transparentia causalis, mentalis et temporalis abest; systema cognitionem finitam circa accessum selectivum et reconstructionem indirectam constituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-01","text":"Dependentia ontologica, cum causalitatibus non competitive ordinatis coniuncta, efficit ut creatura esse receptum in operationem propriam et communicationem boni convertat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-02","text":"Corpus est convertor causalis: influxus sensibiles, naturales vel spirituales secundum dispositionem organicam recipit et per principium intrinsecum in actus personales transducit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-03","text":"Identitas diachronica requirit continuos vectores inaequales: natura, forma et habitus durant, dum materia, interfacies et modus operationis variantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-04","text":"Attributio pluralis effectus et privativitas mali simul servant providentiam universalem, causalitatem localem et imputabilitatem intentionis perversae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-05","text":"Limites cognitionis, voluntatis et generationis cumulantur in exclusionem causae creatae quae beatitudinem, consensum aut principium intellectivum immediate producat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-06","text":"Discernimentum causale debet ab effectu ad modum essendi, statum recipientis, medium, destinationem et finem regredi, quia apparentia nullum horum exhaustive manifestat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-07","text":"Tempus creatum est compositio regiminum nidificatorum: maturatio continuatur, applicationes causales novantur, lapsus directionem invertit et mors vel consummatio interfaciem mutat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-08","text":"Consummatio terminalis quosdam processus naturales claudit, sed participationem et identitatem creatam perficit potius quam ens annihilat aut finitudinem aufert.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-09","text":"Libertas est receptio selectiva et actus intrinsecus sub influxu, non immunitas absoluta ab inclinatione, auxilio, persuasione vel causalitate superiori.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-01","text":"Ontologia finiti primatum relationis ordinatae constituit: ens creatum intelligitur ex esse recepto, modo participationis et coordinatione causarum, non ex substantia solitaria.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum humanum est transductor historicus, nodus participativus et membrana selectiva quae influxum recipit, consensum moderatur atque effectus actuum in habitibus servat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-03","text":"Causa fit locus attributionum differentialium: creare, conservare, disponere, applicare, permittere, ordinare, recipere et operari sunt actus reales sed incommutabiles.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-04","text":"Libertas emergit ut potentia recipiendi et convertendi influxus sine necessitatione, non ut absentia causae, corporis vel historiae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-05","text":"Tempus est architectura durationum et mutationum inaequalium, ubi maturatio, habitus, officia, lapsus, generationes et transitiones terminales inter se nidificantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-06","text":"Auctoritas valida est officium ordinandi media et condiciones actionis alterius ad bonum commune, non possessio ontologica corporis, voluntatis vel cognitionis eius.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-07","text":"Methodus distinctionum ontologiam quam asserit performat: differentias non delet, sed limites attribuit, nexus restituit et operationes sub unitate non homogenea componit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-01","text":"Causa prima esse et potentiam communicat → creatura proprie operatur → bonum receptum communicat → assimilatio participata ad principium augetur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-02","text":"Influxus per medium recipitur → principium intrinsecum actualizatur → actus habitum generat → habitus receptionem et actus posteriores aliter disponit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-03","text":"Effectus anomalus attributionem suspendit → modus essendi, medium et finis examinantur → effectus inter causas inaequales distribuitur → ordo causalis restituitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-04","text":"Inclinatio recipitur → consensus ordinatur vel avertitur → actus habitum format → habitus subsequentem receptionem corroborat → ordo moraliter stabilitur aut recursive invertitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-05","text":"Mutatio status medium prius claudit → forma, natura vel habitus continuitatem portant → novum regimen operationis aperitur → terminus localis in relationem ulteriorem convertitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-06","text":"Limitatio competentiam definit → competentia actionem propriam stabilit → actio nova media instituit → mediatio ulteriorem causalitatem finitam amplificat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-07","text":"Proportio regit omnes receptiones finitas, providentia omnes causalitates colligit, gloria autem seriem statuum ad clausuram quam natura non producit dirigit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B01","text":"Esse participatum + providentia universalis → permanentia recepta + potentia finita agendi.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B02","text":"Modus essendi agentis + dispositio recipientis + medium + finis → ambitus causalis + forma effectus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B03","text":"Influxus superior → mediatio proportionata → contractio receptiva → actualizatio principii intrinseci → operatio propria.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B04","text":"Corpus compositum → receptio sensibilis et naturalis → transductio intellectualis vel voluntaria → actio incorporata.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B05","text":"Actio propria → habitus et historia → mutatio status → continuitas identitatis sub novo modo operationis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B06","text":"Anomalia apparens → examen competentiae causalis → attributio respectuum inaequalium → reinclusio effectus sub ordine providentiali.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B07","text":"Terminus virtutis creatae + finis disproportionatus → actio divina immediata → mutatio terminalis regiminis sine annihilatione creaturae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AFM-B01","text":"Esse participatum → compositio et potentiae finitae → conservatio continua → causalitas mediata → receptio particularis → operatio propria → ordo providentialis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AFM-B02","text":"Effectus receptus → proportio recipientis → distinctio medii et causae → regressus ad competentiam ontologicam → attributio plurium respectuum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AFM-B03","text":"Institutio → maturatio et exercitium → habitus → lapsus aut ordinatio → mutatio interfaciei → consummatio beatifica.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AFM-B04","text":"Oppositio apparens → distinctio ordinum → restitutio operationis finitae → reclassificatio defectus → reinclusio sub unitate non homogenea.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-01","text":"Causalitas reconfiguratur ex conflictu virium in matricem causarum principalium, secundarum, instrumentalium et receptivarum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-02","text":"Corpus reclassificatur ab obstaculo vel termino passivo in interfaciem transductivam et co-principium actionis humanae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MME-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-03","text":"Oppositio dependentiae et libertatis corruit, quia causalitas recepta facultatem actus proprii constituit sine necessitatione consensus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-04","text":"Perfectio transit a plenitudine univoca ad tabulam finis, capacitatis, status, aetatis, integritatis et consummationis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-05","text":"Hierarchia et auctoritas fiunt reticulum officiorum et canalium, quorum inaequalitas dominium totale non infert.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-06","text":"Miraculum et gloria reclassificantur ut termini asymmetrici ordinis creati, non ut gradus summi eiusdem scalae naturalis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-07","text":"Lectio causalis fit regressiva et differentialiter reconstructiva, quia effectus, nomen vel admiratio causam competentem per se non determinant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei apparent cognitio et compositio humana, conservatio et maturatio creaturae, hierarchiae spirituales, influxus, miracula, generatio et consummatio.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali operantur participatio essendi, proportio recipientis, mediatio, causalitas stratificata, principium intrinsecum et continuitas formalis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico quaestio a possessione potestatis ad discernimentum ambitus, medii, status, finis et respectus attributionis transfertur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est participatio continue sustentata per regimina inaequalia, quorum mutationes identitatem non necessario destruunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo distinctio repetita differentias constituit, limites distribuit et ordinem non homogeneum quem asserit actu efficit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-01","text":"Transcendentia formalis intellectus supra organum et dependentia actualis actus a corpore componuntur per distinctionem principii intrinseci et dispositionis organicae, sed conversio inter hos gradus incomplete determinatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-02","text":"Causalitas divina circa voluntatem cum libertate componitur, sed mechanismus quo motio intrinseca consensum non necessitat manet assumptus potius quam formaliter demonstratus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-03","text":"Creatio animae, influxus gratiae et consummatio sub immediata causalitate divina colliguntur, quamvis coordinationes earum cum dispositionibus naturalibus diversos et incompletos nexus requirant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-04","text":"Identitas per mortem, mutationem status et fluxum materiae servatur, sed criterium unitatis habitus, formae et compositi sub transitionibus radicalibus fragile manet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-05","text":"Providentia actum malum ordinat sine appropriatione intentionis perversae, sed relatio permissionis particularis ad imputabilitatem localem non plene explicatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-06","text":"Reclassificatio hierarchiae ut functio causalis applicationes sociales locales non automatice legitimat, quamvis nonnullae commissurae inferiores transitum a natura ad ordinem socialem praesumant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-01","text":"Corpus mutatur ab obiecto vel impedimento in medium epistemicum, vehiculum maturationis et co-principium substantiale operationis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MP-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-02","text":"Immediatio mutatur ab absentia instrumenti in causalitatem intimam, accessum non impeditum et postremo terminum negatum omni causae finitae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MP-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-03","text":"Perfectio mutatur ab excellentia simplici in proportionem dynamicam et tandem in consummationem quae genus vitae, non solum gradum eius, transformat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MP-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-04","text":"Hierarchia mutatur a serie graduum in architecturam functionum, canalium et attributionum causalium inaequalium.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:MP-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina conversionum et dependentiarum inclusarum, in qua vis descendens per media contrahitur, localiter operatur et ad principium superius reordinatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est limen, nodus et membrana: perfectionem recipit, influxum selecte transducit, consensum gubernat et historiam actuum sub mutatione servat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est via punctuata maturationum et applicationum nidificatarum, in qua habitus accumulantur, directiones invertuntur et regimina terminaliter mutantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga a causalitate, corpore vel ordine, sed discernimentum influxuum et electio mediorum proportionatorum sub fine non autonome possesso.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata participationis essendi, causalitatis non competitivae, receptionis proportionatae, mediationis realis, identitatis mutabilis et finis extrinseci sufficiunt ad machinam principalem huius gradus restituendam.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est coniunctio dependentiae et operationis propriae per distributionem effectuum inter causas, media et recipientes inaequales.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinati manent mechanismi actionis spiritualis circa corpus, motionis divinae circa voluntatem, continuitatis habituum et coordinationis dispositionum naturalium cum actione divina immediata.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B04","text":"Systema cohaeret per applicationem recursivam distinctionum et mutationem functionis terminorum, non per identitatem uniformem mediorum, statuum aut processuum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B05","text":"Reductioni plenae resistunt limina inter dispositionem naturalem et effectum divinum, inter providentiam et imputabilitatem, atque inter continuitatem formalem et mutationem radicalem medii; hae commissurae tamen motum generativum servant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T174410156739Z_5ac6cea3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Ggvc L2 03","source_sha256":"9b2a5f82931525148046f1ace04ca1856e38c5ef9f4bafcc5084f685bf43f1eb","sha256":"7fc15265b9397b8817d07c3505f2a1f303751fc40d3b5ad986f516218550aa51","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:44:10.157677+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-01","text":"The system asks how the human being and every finite creature preserve identity, freedom, and proper operation amid ontological dependence, multiple influences, and changes of state.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-02","text":"The global thesis maintains that the mode of being, the proportion of the recipient, the medium, the state, and the end so contract causality that dependence upon the first cause constitutes rather than evacuates secondary agency.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-03","text":"The argument proceeds by a grammar of distributed attribution, which converts oppositions into non-equivalent relations through distinctions of orders, causes, receptions, and times.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-04","text":"The movement extends from the corporeal interface of cognition to continuous communication of being and thence to the providential network of causes, so that anthropology becomes the most intense case of the ontology of participation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-05","text":"The progression is recursive, spiral, and punctuated by changes of state: the same mediation returns, but its medium, recipient, scope, and effect change upon each return.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-06","text":"Defect, corruption, fall, and anomalies are not placed outside order, but are reclassified as indices of causal limits, local privations, and diverse competencies.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-07","text":"The operative attractor is proportionate mediation: the limitation of the created cause restricts its scope, but that same restriction constitutes its proper efficacy, responsibility, and capacity to communicate.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:RLE-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-08","text":"The closure is asymmetric and terminal: created causes truly operate up to their ends, but the beatific vision, ultimate stability, and certain ontological beginnings require immediate divine action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Participated being under providence not only sustains the creature's permanence, but generates its power of acting, receiving, and communicating.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The mode of being of the agent and the disposition of the recipient determine the mode of operation, the extent of the effect, and the measure of cognition.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] First causality and secondary causalities concur non-competitively, because the first cause establishes the capacity and order within which the created cause properly operates.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Mediation is expanded from the interface of bodily cognition into the preservation and communication of the good, then into a general form of governance, instrument, and causal application.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Reception is part of causality, because influence is contracted according to the proportion, state, and disposition of the recipient and converted into a proper effect.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] The body passes from an apparent impediment or object of governance to a constitutive interface of cognition, maturation, action, and composite identity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The finite subject is an intrinsic principle, a receptive node, and a selective membrane: it is open to influences, but elicits a proper act and does not necessarily yield consent.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Created identity persists through nature, form, habits, and orderly continuous relations, not through the immobility of matter, media, or operations.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] The limits of body, time, and causal competence are positive conditions of finite operation, although subject to corruption, privation, and impediment.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] The same effect can be attributed to several causes according to unequal respects, whence causal cognition must reconstruct the mode of reception, destination, and competence of the agent.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Evil and defect remain privations of a particular order: they are locally imputable, but constitute neither a rival nature nor an exteriority to providence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] The created order has intrinsic limits which no natural intensification surpasses: the divine vision, miracle properly so called, and production of the intellectual principle constitute closures of immediate divine action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] The ultimate good and glory gather together all natural, moral, and causal orders, yet perfect the creature without converting finitude into autonomy.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-08","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] The proportion of the recipient persists from bodily cognition to ontological participation and universally distributed causality.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-02","text":"[Functional return] Mediation returns as phantasm, preservation, communication, ministry, and instrument; its common function is to order another's potency toward proportionate act.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] The body passes from an apparent impediment to a medium of maturation and finally to an intrinsic co-principle of the composite.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-04","text":"[Translation] Hierarchy is transferred from scalar dominion to a topology of channels, functions, and causal attributions.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-05","text":"[Amplification] State migrates from an index of human operation into a general temporal principle by which maturation, application of cause, and change of governance are distinguished from one another.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-06","text":"[Inversion] Limitation and dependence invert in force: what appeared to be defects of autonomy become conditions of proper operation and determinate competence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-07","text":"[Absorption] Evil, fall, and anomalies are absorbed into the order of distributed causality, but the difference between privation, imputability, and higher ordination is not erased.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-08","text":"[Terminal transformation] Immediacy ceases to be the simple absence of a medium and becomes an asymmetric name for divine causality and consummation that changes created regimes.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-01","text":"[Local] Dream, phantasms, humors, geographies, and physiological processes remain local mechanisms; they govern the present structure only insofar as they exemplify mediation and the disposition of the recipient.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-02","text":"[Amplified] Mediation of the body and particular reception rise into a general law by which every finite influence is conveyed according to medium and recipient.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-03","text":"[Connecting] Participation in being, continuous preservation, and providence provide the unity within which particular causes and human operations properly act.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-04","text":"[Transitional] Habits, form, and state are vectors of continuity that transfer the effects of past acts through changes of medium, matter, and operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-05","text":"[Recoded] Hierarchy and authority are recoded as proportionate offices of ordering the conditions of another's action, not as total dominion over the recipient.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-06","text":"[Absorbed] Angelic taxonomies, physiological nomenclatures, and seminal reasons disappear after fulfilling the function of distinguishing degrees, media, and causal thresholds.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-07","text":"[Not amplified] Sexual, domestic, and certain exegetical conclusions do not rise to this level, because they do not necessarily follow from participated causality and proportionate reception.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes a homogeneous causal plane in which God, intellect, will, body, and matter act in the same manner or distribute the effect competitively among themselves.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-02","text":"A creature can be neither its own cause, nor the source of its own permanence, nor a universally immediate power.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-03","text":"Humanity is reduced neither to a separate soul nor to a body as prison; perfection may change the composite, not render its natural structure false.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-03","MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-04","text":"One univocal measure of perfection, cognition, or causality cannot be applied to all natures, states, and recipients.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-05","text":"Created immediacy, transparency of minds, and evidence of cause from effect alone are excluded, because all finite cognition is limited by media, access, and reconstruction.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-06","text":"Evil, guilt, fall, and corruption constitute no ontological order outside providence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-07","text":"Consummation is not a natural intensification of the original state, generative life, or created causality, but a transition to a disproportionate terminus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that dependence on lower causality removes agency or freedom; otherwise every participation would evacuate proper operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that identity requires the same matter, the same medium, or the same mode of operation throughout all time.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:DBN-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that mediation is either passive transmission or diminution of the principal cause, because ministry and instrument truly determine the effect.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that a sensible effect exhaustively demonstrates its cause; otherwise miracle, influence, and ordinary causality would be distinguished by appearance alone.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the recipient is an inert terminus of influence, because the intrinsic principle converts reception into proper act and selective access.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that the created order intrinsically encloses its own ultimate end or produces consummation from the indefinite extension of temporal process.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-01","text":"The radical absence around which the system is ordered is the creature made its own foundation, intrinsic principle, and ultimate end.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-02","text":"Finite immediacy and universal possession of a thing, another mind, or the whole order are unavailable possibilities.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-03","text":"Ontological exteriority from the first cause and providence is inadmissible, because such exteriority would not be another order but a privation of participation in being.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-04","text":"Unavailable is a created state that consummates itself, irreversibly encloses aversion, or naturally converts finitude into beatitude.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-05","text":"Total causal, mental, and temporal transparency is absent; the system constitutes finite cognition around selective access and indirect reconstruction.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-01","text":"Ontological dependence, joined with non-competitively ordered causalities, brings it about that the creature converts received being into proper operation and communication of the good.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-02","text":"The body is a causal converter: it receives sensible, natural, or spiritual influences according to organic disposition and conveys them through the intrinsic principle into personal acts.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-03","text":"Diachronic identity requires unequal continuous vectors: nature, form, and habits endure, while matter, interfaces, and mode of operation vary.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-04","text":"Plural attribution of the effect and the privative character of evil together preserve universal providence, local causality, and imputability of perverse intention.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-05","text":"The limits of cognition, will, and generation accumulate in the exclusion of a created cause that immediately produces beatitude, consent, or an intellectual principle.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-06","text":"Causal discernment must regress from the effect to the mode of being, state of the recipient, medium, destination, and end, because appearance exhaustively manifests none of these.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-07","text":"Created time is a composition of nested regimes: maturation continues, causal applications are renewed, the fall reverses direction, and death or consummation changes the interface.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-08","text":"Terminal consummation closes certain natural processes, but perfects participation and created identity rather than annihilating being or removing finitude.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-09","text":"Freedom is selective reception and intrinsically originating act under influence, not absolute immunity from inclination, aid, persuasion, or higher causality.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-01","text":"The ontology of the finite establishes the primacy of ordered relation: created being is understood from received being, the mode of participation, and coordination of causes, not from solitary substance.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-02","text":"The human subject is a historical transducer, participatory node, and selective membrane that receives influence, regulates consent, and preserves the effects of acts in habits.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-03","text":"Cause becomes the locus of differential attributions: to create, preserve, dispose, apply, permit, order, receive, and operate are real but incommutable acts.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-04","text":"Freedom emerges as the capacity to receive and convert influences without necessitation, not as the absence of cause, body, or history.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-05","text":"Time is an architecture of unequal durations and changes, in which maturation, habits, offices, falls, generations, and terminal transitions are nested within one another.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-06","text":"Valid authority is the office of ordering the media and conditions of another's action toward the common good, not ontological possession of that person's body, will, or cognition.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-07","text":"The method of distinctions performs the ontology it asserts: it does not erase differences, but assigns limits, restores connections, and composes operations under non-homogeneous unity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-01","text":"The first cause communicates being and power → the creature properly operates → it communicates received good → participated assimilation to the principle is increased.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-02","text":"Influence is received through a medium → the intrinsic principle is actualized → act generates habit → habit otherwise disposes reception and subsequent acts.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-03","text":"Anomalous effect suspends attribution → mode of being, medium, and end are examined → the effect is distributed among unequal causes → causal order is restored.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-04","text":"Inclination is received → consent is ordered or turned away → act forms habit → habit reinforces subsequent reception → order is morally stabilized or recursively inverted.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-05","text":"Change of state closes the prior medium → form, nature, or habit bear continuity → a new regime of operation opens → the local terminus is converted into a further relation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-06","text":"Limitation defines competence → competence stabilizes proper action → action establishes new media → mediation amplifies further finite causality.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-07","text":"Proportion governs all finite receptions, providence gathers all causalities, but glory directs the series of states toward a closure that nature does not produce.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B01","text":"Participated being + universal providence → received permanence + finite power of acting.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B02","text":"Mode of being of the agent + disposition of the recipient + medium + end → causal scope + form of effect.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B03","text":"Higher influence → proportionate mediation → receptive contraction → actualization of the intrinsic principle → proper operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B04","text":"Composite body → sensible and natural reception → intellectual or voluntary transduction → embodied action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B05","text":"Proper action → habit and history → change of state → continuity of identity under a new mode of operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B06","text":"Apparent anomaly → examination of causal competence → attribution of unequal respects → reinclusion of the effect under providential order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-08","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B07","text":"Limit of created power + disproportionate end → immediate divine action → terminal change of regime without annihilation of the creature.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AFM-B01","text":"Participated being → composite and finite powers → continuous preservation → mediated causality → particular reception → proper operation → providential order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AFM-B02","text":"Received effect → proportion of the recipient → distinction of medium and cause → regression to ontological competence → attribution of multiple respects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AFM-B03","text":"Institution → maturation and exercise → habit → fall or ordination → change of interface → beatific consummation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:AFM-B04","text":"Apparent opposition → distinction of orders → restoration of finite operation → reclassification of defect → reinclusion under non-homogeneous unity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-01","text":"Causality is reconfigured from a conflict of forces into a matrix of principal, secondary, instrumental, and receptive causes.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-02","text":"The body is reclassified from an obstacle or passive terminus into a transductive interface and co-principle of human action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MME-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-03","text":"The opposition between dependence and freedom collapses, because received causality constitutes the capacity for proper act without necessitation of consent.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-04","text":"Perfection passes from univocal fullness to a map of end, capacity, state, age, integrity, and consummation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-05","text":"Hierarchy and authority become a network of offices and channels, whose inequality does not entail total dominion.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-06","text":"Miracle and glory are reclassified as asymmetric termini of the created order, not as the highest degrees of the same natural scale.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-07","text":"Causal reading becomes regressive and differentially reconstructive, because an effect, a name, or wonder does not by itself determine the competent cause.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of the surface appear human cognition and composition, the preservation and maturation of the creature, spiritual hierarchies, influences, miracles, generation, and consummation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level operate participation in being, proportion of the recipient, mediation, stratified causality, intrinsic principle, and formal continuity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level the question is transferred from possession of power to discernment of scope, medium, state, end, and respect of attribution.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is participation continuously sustained through unequal regimes, whose changes do not necessarily destroy identity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, repeated distinction constitutes differences, distributes limits, and actually effects the non-homogeneous order that it asserts.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-01","text":"The formal transcendence of intellect above the organ and the actual dependence of act upon the body are composed through the distinction of intrinsic principle and organic disposition, but the conversion between these levels is incompletely determined.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-02","text":"Divine causality concerning the will is composed with freedom, but the mechanism by which intrinsic motion does not necessitate consent remains assumed rather than formally demonstrated.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-03","text":"Creation of the soul, influence of grace, and consummation are gathered under immediate divine causality, although their coordination with natural dispositions requires diverse and incomplete connections.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-04","text":"Identity is preserved through death, change of state, and flux of matter, but the criterion of unity of habit, form, and composite under radical transitions remains fragile.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-05","text":"Providence orders the evil act without appropriating the perverse intention, but the relation of particular permission to local imputability is not fully explained.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-07","MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-06","text":"The reclassification of hierarchy as a causal function does not automatically legitimate local social applications, although certain lower joints presuppose a transition from nature to social order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-01","text":"The body changes from object or impediment into an epistemic medium, vehicle of maturation, and substantial co-principle of operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MP-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-02","text":"Immediacy changes from the absence of an instrument into intimate causality, unimpeded access, and finally a terminus denied to every finite cause.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MP-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-03","text":"Perfection changes from simple excellence into dynamic proportion and finally into consummation that transforms the kind of life, not only its degree.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MP-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-04","text":"Hierarchy changes from a series of degrees into an architecture of functions, channels, and unequal causal attributions.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:MP-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a machine of conversions and enclosed dependencies, in which descending power is contracted through media, operates locally, and is reordered to a higher principle.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a threshold, node, and membrane: it receives perfection, selectively transduces influence, governs consent, and preserves the history of acts amid change.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-03","text":"Time is a path punctuated by nested maturations and applications, in which habits accumulate, directions are reversed, and regimes are terminally changed.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is not escape from causality, body, or order, but discernment of influences and choice of proportionate media under an end not autonomously possessed.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of participation in being, non-competitive causality, proportionate reception, real mediation, mutable identity, and extrinsic end suffice to reconstruct the principal machine of this level.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the conjunction of dependence and proper operation through the distribution of effects among unequal causes, media, and recipients.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remain the mechanisms of spiritual action concerning the body, divine motion concerning the will, continuity of habits, and coordination of natural dispositions with immediate divine action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B04","text":"The system coheres through the recursive application of distinctions and the change of function of terms, not through uniform identity of media, states, or processes.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B05","text":"The thresholds between natural disposition and divine effect, between providence and imputability, and between formal continuity and radical change of medium resist full reduction; these joints nevertheless preserve generative movement.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L2_03@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_GGVC_L2_03:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Ggvc L2 03","context":"Chapters 85–120 · Prima pars · Quaestio 84 · Prima pars · Quaestio 85 · Prima pars · Quaestio 86 · Prima pars · Quaestio 87 · Prima pars · Quaestio 88 · Prima pars · Quaestio 89 · Prima pars · Quaestio 90 · Prima pars · Quaestio 91 · Prima pars · Quaestio 92 · Prima pars · Quaestio 93 · Prima pars · Quaestio 94 · Prima pars · Quaestio 95 · Prima pars · Quaestio 96 · Prima pars · Quaestio 97 · Prima pars · Quaestio 98 · Prima pars · Quaestio 99 · Prima pars · Quaestio 100 · Prima pars · Quaestio 101 · Prima pars · Quaestio 102 · Prima pars · Quaestio 103 · Prima pars · Quaestio 104 · Prima pars · Quaestio 105 · Prima pars · Quaestio 106 · Prima pars · Quaestio 107 · Prima pars · Quaestio 108 · Prima pars · Quaestio 109 · Prima pars · Quaestio 110 · Prima pars · Quaestio 111 · Prima pars · Quaestio 112 · Prima pars · Quaestio 113 · Prima pars · Quaestio 114 · Prima pars · Quaestio 115 · Prima pars · Quaestio 116 · Prima pars · Quaestio 117 · Prima pars · Quaestio 118 · Prima pars · Quaestio 119"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04","layer":"MC","title":"Ggvc L1 04","order":4,"parents":["CL_GGVC_13","CL_GGVC_14","CL_GGVC_15","CL_GGVC_16"],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_04@R001","sha256":"768c66f9e5b4b15fb7960143d11e4da3790369d4b181b18cb2a7c67ee474f181","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo una essentia simplex personas distinctas, communicationem personalem et totum esse creatum producat sine divisione, mutatione aut principio antecedente.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_16:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-02","text":"Thesis centralis plenitudinem divinam ponit ut fontem qui intrinsecus relationibus oppositis articulatur et extrinsecus esse participatum communicat, nullo incremento vel detrimento sui.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GA-03","CL_GGVC_16:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-03","text":"Argumentatio per distinctionem rei, modi significandi, perfectionis transferibilis et defectus removendi categorias creatas purgat antequam eas inter ordines ontologicos distribuat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_15:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-04","text":"Amor procedens et Donum constituunt commissuram qua origo personalis, missio temporalis et communicatio creaturalis coniunguntur sine univocatione processionis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_15:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-05","text":"Motus systematis ab articulatione intradivina procedit ad novitatem recipientis, inde ad productionem esse, temporis et pluralitatis ordinatae sub uno principio.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_16:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-06","text":"Progressus est spiralis, recursivus et cumulativus, quia relatio, processio, praesentia et pluralitas redeunt quotiens magnitudo causalitatis mutatur, sed novo statu funguntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:ASP-07","CL_GGVC_16:BAS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-07","text":"Clausura ontologica totam pluralitatem personalem et creatam sub plenitudine communicativa ordinat, differentias tamen non absorbet neque fontes multiplicat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:BAS-08","CL_GGVC_16:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-08","text":"Clausura demonstrativa incompleta manet, quia ratio dependentiam et possibilitatem temporalis initii statuit, decretum autem concretum initii et distributionis revelationi ac sapientiae divinae relinquit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:BAS-08","CL_GGVC_16:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Simplicitas divina omnem compositionem realem excludit, unde pluralitas personarum, rationum causalium et effectuum multiplicationem essentiae vel primi principii efficere nequit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GA-01","CL_GGVC_15:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Relatio realiter opposita est principium distinctionis personalis, quia ipsa essentia subsistens hypostasim constituit sine divisione perfectionis absolutae communis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GA-03","CL_GGVC_15:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Differentia rationis, modi significandi et analogiae servat identitatem realem dum munera essentialia, personalia, notionalia et causalia non confundit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GA-05","CL_GGVC_15:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Origo secundum magnitudinem typatur: intradivine relationem sine inferioritate, in missione identitatem personae missae, in creatione dependentiam totalem effectus significat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_15:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Plenitudo divina communicat sine amissione, indigentia vel augmento, atque hac forma communi naturam, praesentiam, esse et bonitatem distribuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:GA-08","CL_GGVC_15:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] In processionibus intradivinis tota natura indivisa communicatur, dum sola relatio originis actus et personas distinguit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GA-08","CL_GGVC_13:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Amor procedens est primum Donum et operator transitus ab origine personali ad communicationem gratuitam, cuius effectus oeconomicus originem manifestat sed non constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:GA-11","CL_GGVC_13:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Actio ad extra uni principio divino communis manet, sed missio personaliter distribuitur secundum relationem originis et novum terminum in recipiente.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:GA-07","CL_GGVC_15:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Creatio est emanatio totius esse et relatio totalis dependentiae, nullo subiecto, medio aut campo ontologico antecedente.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:GA-01","CL_GGVC_16:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Novitas et temporalitas semper ad effectum vel recipientem transferuntur: origo divina manet aeterna, res autem cum suo tempore producitur aut renovatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_16:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Creatura recipit esse et virtutem agendi intra ordinem creatum; creatura rationalis ulterius per cognitionem, amorem et fruitionem personalis Doni capax efficitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:GA-03","CL_GGVC_13:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Inadaequatio cuiuslibet effectus finiti bonitati divinae pluralitatem graduatam requirit, cuius partes per mutuam ordinationem unum mundum constituunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:GA-08","CL_GGVC_16:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Ratio naturalis structuram dependentiae et limites contradictionis cognoscit, sed liberam determinationem initii temporalis et distributionis concretae non demonstrat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:GA-07","CL_GGVC_16:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-01","text":"Modus significandi identitatem functionalem retinet ut operator conservationis, sed a nominatione Spiritus ad coordinationem personae, actus, missionis et creationis migrat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_15:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-02","text":"Relatio a signo distinctionis in hypostasim constituentem mutatur, deinde originem operationaliter exprimit et tandem diversos status praesentiae, dependentiae et respectus exemplaris suscipit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_15:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-03","text":"Processio ab interioritate subsistente ad missionem temporaliter terminatam et ad emanationem universalis esse migrat, sed identitatem univocam inter hos ordines amittit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_15:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-04","text":"Origo stabiliter asymmetriam indicat, sed ab ordine personali sine inferioritate ad prioritatem causalem sine antecedentia temporali transfertur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:GA-02","CL_GGVC_16:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-05","text":"Amor ab ordine voluntatis in personam subsistentem et primum Donum mutatur, deinde in attractore communicationis absorbetur quo missio et creatio conveniunt sine identitate termini.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_15:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-06","text":"Praesentia amplificatur a mutua inexistentia personarum ad causalitatem universalem et ulterius ad inhabitationem fruitivam creaturae rationalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_15:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-07","text":"«Ex nihilo» primum materiam antecedentem negat, deinde a novitate temporali distinguitur, ita ut creaturalitas in dependentia potius quam in solo initio collocetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_16:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-08","text":"Pluralitas a relationibus personalibus ad missiones, ideas et creaturas migrat; in ultimo campo inadaequatio effectus singularis eam in machinam positivam ordinis convertit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_16:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-09","text":"Obiectiones grammaticales, quantitativae et physicae, opere locali peracto, evanescunt ut exempla et in regulam superiorem mutationis generis quaestionis absorbentur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_15:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-01","text":"Difficultates lexicales circa spiritum, sanctum, amorem et donum locales manent; sursum ascendit regula qua significatio communis sub disciplina originis functionem personalem acquirit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_13:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-02","text":"Tabulae appropriationum et probationes unicitatis singulorum terminorum sunt machinae inferiores; hoc gradu tantum discrimen inter manifestationem communem et constitutionem relationalem sustinetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_14:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-03","text":"Signa missionum, gradus illuminationis et disputationes de infinito praeterito locales sunt; sursum valent ut termini novitatis creatae et limites demonstrationis naturalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_16:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-04","text":"Coordinatio grammaticae, ontologiae relationis et purgationis analogicae amplificatur in methodum praesentis gradus, qua singula praedicata secundum magnitudinem causalitatis typantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:CGD-06","CL_GGVC_15:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-05","text":"Processio Amoris et ratio Doni coniungunt constitutionem personalem cum missione, quia eadem origo aeterna aptitudinem donationis et terminum novum in creatura ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:CGD-06","CL_GGVC_15:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-06","text":"Exemplar univocum processionis et causalitatis creatae obsoletum fit; quod persistit est communicatio perfectionis sub modis irreducibilibus originis, missionis et creationis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_16:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-07","text":"Materia, ideae, formae et causae secundae non sunt principia totius, sed elementa intra campum creatum quibus distributio effectuum particularium exercetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:CGD-03","CL_GGVC_16:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-01","text":"Distinctio personarum neque divisionem essentiae, pluralitatem substantiarum absolutarum neque compositionem primi principii admittit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-02","text":"Relatio, actio et potentia divinae neque accidentia addita, neque motus successivi, neque possibilitates nondum actuatae sunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_14:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-03","text":"Origo personalis nec inferioritatem, imperium dominativum, instrumentum secundum nec distantiam inter personas constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_13:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-04","text":"Missio et generatio divinae nullum motum localem, fieri temporale aut mutationem intrinsecam in Deo inducunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-05","text":"Creatio nullam materiam, nullum subiectum, spatium vacuum aut metatempus tamquam condicionem antecedentem recipit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_16:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-06","text":"Communicatio divina neque datum in possessionem recipientis convertit neque participationem creaturalem in identitatem naturae absorbet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_15:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-07","text":"Nulla regio ontologica independens, causa creata prima aut totalitas extra ordinem unius principii admittitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_16:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet unum genus univocum Deum, processiones personales, missiones et creationem sub eadem ratione derivationis continere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_16:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet subiectum divinum absolutum ante relationes consistere atque proprietates personales postea recipere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:BSA-05","CL_GGVC_14:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet identitatem secundum rem identitatem rationis, modi significandi et substitutionis praedicativae inferre.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_14:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet dependentiam causalem successionem temporalem vel communem mensuram durationis inter primum principium et effectum exigere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_15:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet communicationem perfectionis ex indigentia, fine acquirendo aut necessitate complendi Deum procedere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_15:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet structuram metaphysicam dependentiae decretum contingens de initio et distributione concreta producere posse.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_16:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-01","text":"Altissima absentia est tertium ontologicum inter Deum et creaturam: neque receptaculum, medium, materia neque metatempus causalitatem universalem continet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-02","text":"Nulla mensura univoca essentiam, relationes, aeternitatem, tempus et gradus creatos complecti potest; systema circa hanc indisponibilitatem proportionis communis ordinatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-03","text":"Possibilitas effectus singularis bonitatem divinam exhaustive repraesentantis inadmissibilis est, atque eius exclusio pluralitatem distributam generat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_16:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-04","text":"Systema neque aeternitatem sine communicabilitate neque communicationem quae recipientem in causam originis convertat admittere potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:BSA-05","CL_GGVC_13:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-01","text":"Grammatica analogica fit operator transscalaris: eandem rem simplicem conservat, dum defectus removet et functiones irreducibiles secundum diversos ordines distribuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_15:FGS-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-02","text":"Relatio non habet statum ontologicum uniformem: intradivine subsistit et constituit, in missione personam ordinat ad terminum novum, in creatione dependentiam effectus significat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:XD-07","CL_GGVC_15:FL-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-03","text":"Amor ut Donum convertit processionem personalem in rationem communicationis gratuitae, qua missio personam donat et creatio campum participantium constituit sine confusione terminorum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_15:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-04","text":"Axis originis et axis dependentiae duas asymmetrias non convertibiles efficiunt: personas aequales relatione distinguunt, Deum autem et creaturam participatione inaequali separant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_15:MME-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-05","text":"Unitas operationis ad extra compatitur missiones personaliter distinctas, quia causalitas effectus est communis, dum relatio originis personam missam et rationem attributionis determinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:XD-04","CL_GGVC_15:FL-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-06","text":"Regula novitatis terminalis statuit mutationem numquam in fonte divino sed in recipiente, effectu vel primo limite successionis consistere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_16:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-07","text":"Pluralitas effectuum non multiplicitatem fontis exprimit, sed communicationem simplicis plenitudinis per participationes finitas quarum insufficientia ordinem complementarem postulat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:FGS-B05","CL_GGVC_16:XD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-08","text":"Distinctio rationis et revelationis contingentiam tuetur: metaphysica necessitatem dependentiae demonstrat, sed eventum initii aut distributionem singularium in conclusionem necessariam non convertit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_16:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-01","text":"Realitas fit architectura concentrica sine receptaculo communi: essentia simplex relationibus personalibus articulatur et campum creatum a centro causali producit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_16:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas est hierarchia inclusiva in qua Deus esse et agrum agendi constituit, causae secundae vero effectus particulares ex virtutibus receptis producunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_16:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-03","text":"Tempus desinit esse continens commune et fit effectus successionis creatae, dum aeternitas causalitatem temporalium terminorum sine successione interna sustinet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_15:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-04","text":"Subiectum dupliciter reconstituitur: persona divina est terminus subsistens relationis, creatura autem nodus receptivus et activus cuius agency a participatione dependet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_16:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-05","text":"Epistemologia fit disciplina portarum grammaticalium, apophaticarum et scalarum, quae rationi demonstrationem dependentiae concedit sed decretum contingens et modum divinum non usurpat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_16:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-06","text":"Unitas non amplius uniformitatem significat, sed potentiam plenitudinis simplicis differentias personales et creatas ordinandi sine earum absorptionе.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_16:FGS-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-07","text":"Gratia participationem ontologicam intensificat in praesentiam fruitivam: creatura manet causata, sed capax recipiendi personam sine dominio vel identitate naturae fit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_13:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-01","text":"Simplicitas excludit distinctionem absolutam, exclusio relationem subsistentem postulat, relatio distinctionem sine divisione efficit, atque distinctio sic simplicitatem confirmat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-02","text":"Identitas realis distinctiones semanticas postulat, distinctiones praedicationem ordinant, et praedicatio identitatem ab aequivocatione atque confusione custodit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-03","text":"Relatio terminos personales constituit, termini actum originis determinant, actus relationem dynamicam significat, atque reditus ad relationem statum ontologicum ampliat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-04","text":"Processio Amoris aptitudinem aeternam Doni generat, aptitudo dationem temporalem ordinat, receptio fruitionem producit, sed circuitus causaliter ad originem redire prohibetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_13:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-05","text":"Universalitas effectus excludit subiectum antecedens, exclusio subiecti motum removet, remotio motus relationem dependentiae relinquit, et relatio universalitatem causae confirmat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-06","text":"Inadaequatio effectus finiti pluralitatem generat, pluralitas gradus distribuit, gradatio complementum totius efficit, et bonum totius partes vicissim mensurat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-07","text":"Attractor communis est plenitudo ordinans: intradivine processio amoris, oeconomice communicatio Doni, cosmologice ordo pluralitatis ad unum principium et finem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_16:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B01","text":"Essentia simplex una + pluralitas personalis et creata data → requisitio differentiarum quae unitatem non dividant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:GA-01","CL_GGVC_16:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B02","text":"Categoria creata → separatio perfectionis a defectu → remotio materiae, motus, successionis et indigentiae → translatio analogica.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:FGS-B01","CL_GGVC_16:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B03","text":"Identitas realis + typatio modi significandi → distributio rationum essentialium, personalium, notionalium, oeconomicarum et causalium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_15:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B04","text":"Essentia ut relatio subsistens + oppositio originis + communicatio naturae indivisae → personae distinctae et aequales sine compositione.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:FGS-B03","CL_GGVC_14:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B05","text":"Amor procedens → primum Donum → aptitudo aeterna → missio ad terminum novum → inhabitatio fruitiva sine motu divino.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:FGS-B06","CL_GGVC_15:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B06","text":"Amplitudo effectus usque ad ipsum esse → materia, tempus et spatium intra effectum translata → creatio ut relatio totalis dependentiae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:FGS-B02","CL_GGVC_16:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B07","text":"Participatio finita → pluralitas formarum → gradatio inaequalis → complementum totius → mutua ordinatio partium in uno mundo.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:FGS-B05","CL_GGVC_16:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AFM-B01","text":"Unitas simplex → relatio subsistens → processiones Verbi et Amoris → Amor ut Donum → missio personalis → receptio fruitiva → creatio universalis → pluralitas ordinata → unus mundus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_16:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AFM-B02","text":"Praedicatum creatum → detectio defectus → purgatio analogica → typatio relationis → reclassificatio processionis → mutatio magnitudinis causalitatis → clausura ontologica sine univocatione.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_15:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AFM-B03","text":"Dependentia demonstrabilis → contingentia durationis → limes rationis → initium revelatum → distributio sapienter intenta → ratio singularis allocationis non demonstrata.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_16:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-01","text":"Oppositio inter unum et multa dissolvitur: unitas fontis et pluralitas relationum vel participationum ad rationes diversas pertinent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:MME-01","CL_GGVC_15:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-02","text":"Relatio reclassificatur ab accidente in operator transscalaris constitutionis personalis, missionis et dependentiae, servata diversitate status ontologici.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_15:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-03","text":"Processio desinit esse genus univocum et fit familia ordinata processionis personalis, missionis temporalis et emanationis universalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:MME-02","CL_GGVC_15:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-04","text":"Causalitas distinguitur secundum amplitudinem effectus: origo personalis, operatio communis, creatio totius esse et actio secundaria intra ordinem creatum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:MP-03","CL_GGVC_16:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-05","text":"Tempus reclassificatur a continente praesupposito in effectum creatum et mensuram innovationis recipientis, cui aeternitas non succedit neque continuatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_16:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-06","text":"Perfectio transfertur a maxima aequalitate singulorum ad proportionem totius, in quo diversitas graduata repraesentationem distribuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:MME-03","CL_GGVC_16:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-07","text":"Theologia reordinatur ut praxis limitationis argumenti: grammatica ontologiam custodit, apophasis falsa exemplaria removet, ratio demonstrat structuram, revelatio decretum contingens determinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GA-12","CL_GGVC_16:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei tractantur nomina Spiritus, identitas personarum, missiones, creatio, initium durationis et ordinatio multitudinis mundi.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_16:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali agunt simplicitas, relatio subsistens, origo, terminus receptus, participatio et ordo, quae diversos campos sub una communicatione perfectionis connectunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_16:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico systema quaestionem mutat quotiens grammatica superficialis, quantitas, motus aut causalitas particularis ordinem superiorem falso metiri conantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_16:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas divina est actualitas simplex relationaliter articulata, realitas creata autem participatio ordinata cuius tempus inter effectus numeratur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_16:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo forma argumenti unitatem servat dum rationes distinguit, atque successive removendo falsa principia ipsam ontologiam non compositam efficit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_15:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-01","text":"Identitas relationis, personae et essentiae magnum pondus ontologicum modo significandi imponit; si distinctio semantica deficit, aut simplicitas confundit aut pluralitas dividit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-02","text":"Relatio inter subsistentiam personalem, praedicationem temporalem et respectum exemplarem oscillat, neque unus eius status ontologicus omnes usus continet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-03","text":"Aequalitas absoluta personarum cum asymmetria originis non convertibili coexistere debet; haec tensio distinctionem sine hierarchia sustinet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-04","text":"Operatio ad extra communis est, missio tamen personaliter distribuitur; nexus inter effectum communem, originem propriam et appropriationem manet commissura delicata.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-05","text":"Receptio realis Doni cum negatione dominii componitur, dum donum creatum ad Donum increatum disponit nec tamen personam possidibilem efficit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_15:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-06","text":"Creaturalitas mundi metaphysice demonstratur, actualis autem novitas ex eisdem axiomatibus non sequitur; cohaerentia distinctione dependentiae et decreti revelati nititur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-07","text":"Unitas actualis mundi et distributio singularium sub sapientia ordinantur, sed necessitas unici ordinis possibilis et ratio cuiusque allocationis non plene derivantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:FL-04","CL_GGVC_16:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-01","text":"Amor mutatur ab habitudine essentiali in personam procedentem, deinde in primum Donum et attractorem communicationis oeconomicae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:MP-02","CL_GGVC_13:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-02","text":"Processio mutatur ubi interior origo ad missionem temporaliter terminatam et postea ad emanationem universalis esse transfertur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-03","text":"Praesentia mutatur a mutua inexistentia personarum in causalitatem communem et deinde in inhabitationem cognitivam atque amativam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-04","text":"Creatio mutatur ex eventu vel motu in relationem ontologicam, dum «ex nihilo» a negatione materiae ad quaestionem novitatis temporalis bifurcatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:MP-01","CL_GGVC_16:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-05","text":"Multitudo mutatur ab apparente repugnantia unitati in instrumentum repraesentationis distributae et complementum boni totius.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:MP-03","CL_GGVC_16:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina simplicis actus: centrum non spatiale relationibus personalibus articulatur et campum creatum sine receptaculo praeexistente producit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_16:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:HSI-02","text":"Persona divina est terminus relationis subsistens; creatura est nodus receptivus et activus qui esse, formam et virtutem agendi participat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_16:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est linea cum mundo explicata circa fontem aeternum, non continens per quod Deus transit aut ante quod mundus abest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_16:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio fit per distinctiones originis, identitatis, possessionis, termini et magnitudinis causalis, quibus omnis reversio creaturae in principium constitutivum fontis interdicitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:HSI-05","CL_GGVC_14:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de simplicitate, relatione subsistente, communicatione plenitudinis, missione, creatione universali et pluralitate ordinata sufficiunt ad structuram principalem huius metagregis restituendam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_16:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus quo unitas non dividitur a relationibus, communicatio non minuit plenitudinem, et universalitas effectus omne subiectum antecedens excludit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_16:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B03","text":"Nondum satis determinantur criteria accommodationis nominum, singularum appropriationum necessitas, nexus doni creati et increati, decretum initii atque distributio singularium formarum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_15:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_16:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B04","text":"Systema cohaeret si identitas realis, typatio rationum, asymmetria originis, dependentia participationis et limes rationis simul retinentur neque in unum genus rediguntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_16:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat ab articulatione intradivina per Donum et missionem ad creationem atque ordinem mundi; reductioni plenae resistunt decretum contingens et singularitas distributionis sapientiae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:MRT-B06","CL_GGVC_16:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T173724037878Z_3de033c2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Ggvc L1 04","source_sha256":"768c66f9e5b4b15fb7960143d11e4da3790369d4b181b18cb2a7c67ee474f181","sha256":"1545ddd0c4e7f6dd9d23f6d7ab54468ab9a614f565ad0849d86a7951e7aab321","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:37:24.039813+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-01","text":"The system asks how one simple essence produces distinct persons, personal communication, and the whole of created being without division, change, or an antecedent principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_16:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-02","text":"The central thesis posits divine fullness as a source intrinsically articulated by opposed relations and extrinsically communicating participated being, with no increase or diminishment of itself.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GA-03","CL_GGVC_16:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-03","text":"The argument, through the distinction of thing, mode of signifying, transferable perfection, and removable defect, purges created categories before distributing them among ontological orders.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_15:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-04","text":"Proceeding Love and Gift constitute the joint through which personal origin, temporal mission, and creaturely communication are connected without univocity of procession.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_15:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-05","text":"The movement of the system proceeds from intradivine articulation to the novelty of the recipient, thence to the production of being, time, and ordered plurality under one principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_16:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-06","text":"The progression is spiral, recursive, and cumulative, because relation, procession, presence, and plurality recur whenever the magnitude of causality changes, yet function in a new state.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:ASP-07","CL_GGVC_16:BAS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-07","text":"The ontological closure orders the whole of personal and created plurality under communicative fullness, yet neither absorbs differences nor multiplies sources.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:BAS-08","CL_GGVC_16:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AS-08","text":"The demonstrative closure remains incomplete, because reason establishes dependence and the possibility of a temporal beginning, but leaves the concrete decree of beginning and distribution to revelation and divine wisdom.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:BAS-08","CL_GGVC_16:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Divine simplicity excludes every real composition, whence the plurality of persons, causal accounts, and effects cannot bring about a multiplication of essence or of the first principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GA-01","CL_GGVC_15:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Really opposed relation is the principle of personal distinction, because the subsisting essence itself constitutes hypostasis without division of the common absolute perfection.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GA-03","CL_GGVC_15:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Difference of reason, mode of signifying, and analogy preserves real identity while not confusing essential, personal, notional, and causal functions.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GA-05","CL_GGVC_15:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Origin is typed according to magnitude: intradivinely it signifies relation without inferiority, in mission the identity of the person sent, in creation the total dependence of the effect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_15:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Divine fullness communicates without loss, need, or increase, and by this common form distributes nature, presence, being, and goodness.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:GA-08","CL_GGVC_15:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] In intradivine processions the whole undivided nature is communicated, while only the relation of origin distinguishes acts and persons.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GA-08","CL_GGVC_13:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Proceeding Love is the first Gift and the operator of transition from personal origin to gratuitous communication, whose economic effect manifests but does not constitute origin.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:GA-11","CL_GGVC_13:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Action ad extra remains common to the one divine principle, but mission is personally distributed according to the relation of origin and a new term in the recipient.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:GA-07","CL_GGVC_15:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Creation is the emanation of the whole of being and a relation of total dependence, with no antecedent subject, medium, or ontological field.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:GA-01","CL_GGVC_16:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Novelty and temporality are always transferred to the effect or recipient: divine origin remains eternal, while the thing is produced or renewed with its own time.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_16:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] The creature receives being and the power of acting within the created order; the rational creature is further made capable, through knowledge, love, and enjoyment, of the personal Gift.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:GA-03","CL_GGVC_13:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] The inadequacy of any finite effect to divine goodness requires a graded plurality, whose parts constitute one world through mutual ordering.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:GA-08","CL_GGVC_16:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Natural reason knows the structure of dependence and the limits of contradiction, but does not demonstrate the free determination of temporal beginning and concrete distribution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:GA-07","CL_GGVC_16:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-01","text":"Mode of signifying retains functional identity as an operator of preservation, but migrates from the naming of the Spirit to the coordination of person, act, mission, and creation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_15:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-02","text":"Relation changes from a sign of distinction into a hypostasis-constituting reality, then operationally expresses origin, and finally assumes diverse states of presence, dependence, and exemplary reference.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_15:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-03","text":"Procession migrates from subsisting interiority to temporally terminated mission and to the emanation of universal being, but loses univocal identity among these orders.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_15:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-04","text":"Origin steadily indicates asymmetry, but is transferred from personal order without inferiority to causal priority without temporal antecedence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:GA-02","CL_GGVC_16:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-05","text":"Love changes from the order of will into a subsisting person and the first Gift, then is absorbed into the attractor of communication where mission and creation meet without identity of term.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_15:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-06","text":"Presence is amplified from the mutual indwelling of persons to universal causality and further to the enjoyable indwelling in the rational creature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_15:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-07","text":"«Ex nihilo» first denies antecedent matter, then is distinguished from temporal novelty, such that creatureliness is situated in dependence rather than in beginning alone.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_16:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-08","text":"Plurality migrates from personal relations to missions, ideas, and creatures; in the final field, the inadequacy of the singular effect converts it into a positive engine of order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_16:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RMD-09","text":"Grammatical, quantitative, and physical objections, once their local work is done, vanish as examples and are absorbed into a higher rule of mutation in the kind of question.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_15:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-01","text":"Lexical difficulties concerning spirit, holy, love, and gift remain local; upward rises the rule by which common meaning, under the discipline of origin, acquires a personal function.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_13:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-02","text":"Tables of appropriations and proofs of the uniqueness of individual terms are lower-level mechanisms; at this level only the distinction between common manifestation and relational constitution is maintained.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_14:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-03","text":"Signs of missions, degrees of illumination, and disputes about the infinite past are local; upward they matter as the terms of created novelty and the limits of natural demonstration.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_16:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-04","text":"The coordination of grammar, the ontology of relation, and analogical purification is amplified into the method of the present level, by which individual predicates are typed according to the magnitude of causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:CGD-06","CL_GGVC_15:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-05","text":"The procession of Love and the account of Gift join personal constitution to mission, because the same eternal origin orders the aptitude for donation and a new term in the creature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:CGD-06","CL_GGVC_15:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-06","text":"A univocal model of procession and created causality becomes obsolete; what persists is the communication of perfection under irreducible modes of origin, mission, and creation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_16:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:CGD-07","text":"Matter, ideas, forms, and secondary causes are not principles of the whole, but elements within the created field by which the distribution of particular effects is carried out.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:CGD-03","CL_GGVC_16:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-01","text":"The distinction of persons admits neither division of essence, plurality of absolute substances, nor composition of the first principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-02","text":"Divine relation, action, and power are neither added accidents, nor successive motions, nor possibilities not yet actualized.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_14:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-03","text":"Personal origin constitutes neither inferiority, dominative power, a secondary instrument, nor distance between persons.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_13:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-04","text":"Divine mission and generation induce no local motion, temporal becoming, or intrinsic change in God.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-05","text":"Creation receives no matter, no subject, empty space, or metatime as an antecedent condition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_16:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-06","text":"Divine communication neither converts the gift into the possession of the recipient nor absorbs creaturely participation into identity of nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_15:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:GNX-07","text":"No independent ontological region, first created cause, or totality outside the order of one principle is admitted.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_16:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that one univocal genus contains God, personal processions, missions, and creation under the same account of derivation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_16:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that an absolute divine subject subsists prior to relations and subsequently receives personal properties.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:BSA-05","CL_GGVC_14:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that identity according to the thing entails identity of reason, mode of signifying, and predicative substitution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_14:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that causal dependence requires temporal succession or a common measure of duration between the first principle and the effect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_15:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the communication of perfection proceeds from need, an end to be acquired, or a necessity of completing God.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_15:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that the metaphysical structure of dependence can produce the contingent decree concerning beginning and concrete distribution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_16:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-01","text":"The highest absence is a third ontological reality between God and creature: neither receptacle, medium, matter, nor metatime contains universal causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-02","text":"No univocal measure can encompass essence, relations, eternity, time, and created grades; the system is ordered around this unavailability of common proportion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-03","text":"The possibility of a singular effect exhaustively representing divine goodness is inadmissible, and its exclusion generates distributed plurality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_16:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSA-04","text":"The system can admit neither eternity without communicability nor communication that converts the recipient into the cause of origin.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:BSA-05","CL_GGVC_13:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-01","text":"Analogical grammar becomes a transscalar operator: it preserves the same simple reality, while removing defects and distributing irreducible functions according to diverse orders.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_15:FGS-B01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-02","text":"Relation has no uniform ontological status: intradivinely it subsists and constitutes; in mission it orders the person toward a new term; in creation it signifies the dependence of the effect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:XD-07","CL_GGVC_15:FL-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-03","text":"Love as Gift converts personal procession into the account of gratuitous communication, by which mission gives the person and creation constitutes the field of participants without confusion of terms.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_15:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-04","text":"The axis of origin and the axis of dependence produce two nonconvertible asymmetries: they distinguish equal persons by relation, but separate God and creature by unequal participation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_15:MME-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-05","text":"The unity of operation ad extra is compatible with personally distinct missions, because the causality of the effect is common, while the relation of origin determines the person sent and the account of attribution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:XD-04","CL_GGVC_15:FL-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-06","text":"The rule of terminal novelty establishes that change never resides in the divine source but in the recipient, effect, or first limit of succession.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_16:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-07","text":"The plurality of effects does not express multiplicity of source, but the communication of simple fullness through finite participations whose insufficiency requires a complementary order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:FGS-B05","CL_GGVC_16:XD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:XD-08","text":"The distinction of reason and revelation safeguards contingency: metaphysics demonstrates the necessity of dependence, but does not convert the event of beginning or the distribution of singulars into a necessary conclusion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_16:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes a concentric architecture without a common receptacle: simple essence is articulated by personal relations and produces the created field from a causal center.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_16:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-02","text":"Causality is an inclusive hierarchy in which God constitutes being and the field of acting, while secondary causes produce particular effects from received powers.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_16:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-03","text":"Time ceases to be a common container and becomes an effect of created succession, while eternity sustains the causality of temporal terms without internal succession.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_15:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-04","text":"Subject is reconstituted in two ways: the divine person is a subsisting term of relation, whereas the creature is a receptive and active node whose agency depends on participation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_16:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-05","text":"Epistemology becomes a discipline of grammatical, apophatic, and scalar gates, which grants reason a demonstration of dependence but does not usurp the contingent decree and the divine mode.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_16:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-06","text":"Unity no longer signifies uniformity, but the power of simple fullness to order personal and created differences without their absorption.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_16:FGS-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:BSD-07","text":"Grace intensifies ontological participation into enjoyable presence: the creature remains caused, but becomes capable of receiving the person without mastery or identity of nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_13:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-01","text":"Simplicity excludes absolute distinction, exclusion requires subsisting relation, relation effects distinction without division, and distinction thus confirms simplicity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-02","text":"Real identity requires semantic distinctions, distinctions order predication, and predication safeguards identity from equivocation and confusion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-03","text":"Relation constitutes personal terms, terms determine the act of origin, the act signifies dynamic relation, and the return to relation expands ontological status.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-04","text":"The procession of Love generates the eternal aptitude of Gift, aptitude orders temporal giving, reception produces enjoyment, but the circuit is causally prohibited from returning to origin.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_13:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-05","text":"Universality of effect excludes an antecedent subject, exclusion of subject removes motion, removal of motion leaves a relation of dependence, and relation confirms the universality of the cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-06","text":"The inadequacy of the finite effect generates plurality, plurality distributes grades, gradation effects the complement of the whole, and the good of the whole reciprocally measures the parts.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:RLE-07","text":"The common attractor is ordering fullness: intradivinely the procession of love, economically the communication of Gift, cosmologically the order of plurality toward one principle and end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_16:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B01","text":"One simple essence + given personal and created plurality → requirement of differences that do not divide unity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:GA-01","CL_GGVC_16:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B02","text":"Created category → separation of perfection from defect → removal of matter, motion, succession, and need → analogical translation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:FGS-B01","CL_GGVC_16:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B03","text":"Real identity + typing of mode of signifying → distribution of essential, personal, notional, economic, and causal accounts.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_15:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B04","text":"Essence as subsisting relation + opposition of origin + communication of undivided nature → distinct and equal persons without composition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:FGS-B03","CL_GGVC_14:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B05","text":"Proceeding Love → first Gift → eternal aptitude → mission toward a new term → enjoyable indwelling without divine motion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:FGS-B06","CL_GGVC_15:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B06","text":"Scope of effect extending to being itself → matter, time, and space transferred within the effect → creation as a relation of total dependence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:FGS-B02","CL_GGVC_16:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FGS-B07","text":"Finite participation → plurality of forms → unequal gradation → complement of the whole → mutual ordering of parts in one world.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:FGS-B05","CL_GGVC_16:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AFM-B01","text":"Simple unity → subsisting relation → processions of Word and Love → Love as Gift → personal mission → enjoyable reception → universal creation → ordered plurality → one world.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_16:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AFM-B02","text":"Created predicate → detection of defect → analogical purification → typing of relation → reclassification of procession → change in magnitude of causality → ontological closure without univocity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_15:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:AFM-B03","text":"Demonstrable dependence → contingency of duration → limit of reason → revealed beginning → wisely intended distribution → non-demonstrated account of singular allocation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_16:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-01","text":"The opposition between one and many is dissolved: unity of source and plurality of relations or participations pertain to diverse accounts.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:MME-01","CL_GGVC_15:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-02","text":"Relation is reclassified from accident into a transscalar operator of personal constitution, mission, and dependence, with diversity of ontological status preserved.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_15:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-03","text":"Procession ceases to be a univocal genus and becomes an ordered family of personal procession, temporal mission, and universal emanation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:MME-02","CL_GGVC_15:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-04","text":"Causality is distinguished according to the scope of the effect: personal origin, common operation, creation of the whole of being, and secondary action within the created order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:MP-03","CL_GGVC_16:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-05","text":"Time is reclassified from a presupposed container into a created effect and a measure of novelty in the recipient, to which eternity neither succeeds nor is continued.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_16:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-06","text":"Perfection is transferred from the greatest equality of individuals to the proportion of the whole, in which graded diversity distributes representation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:MME-03","CL_GGVC_16:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MME-07","text":"Theology is reordered as a practice of limiting argument: grammar safeguards ontology, apophasis removes false models, reason demonstrates structure, revelation determines the contingent decree.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:GA-12","CL_GGVC_16:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level are treated the names of the Spirit, the identity of persons, missions, creation, the beginning of duration, and the ordering of the world's multitude.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_16:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level operate simplicity, subsisting relation, origin, received term, participation, and order, which connect diverse fields under one communication of perfection.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_16:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level the system changes the question whenever superficial grammar, quantity, motion, or particular causality attempt falsely to measure a higher order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_16:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, divine reality is simple actuality relationally articulated, while created reality is ordered participation whose time is counted among effects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_16:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the form of argument preserves unity while distinguishing accounts, and by successively removing false principles effects the very non-composite ontology.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_15:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-01","text":"The identity of relation, person, and essence imposes great ontological weight upon mode of signifying; if semantic distinction fails, either simplicity confuses or plurality divides.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-02","text":"The relation between personal subsistence, temporal predication, and exemplary reference oscillates, and no single ontological status of it contains all uses.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-03","text":"The absolute equality of persons must coexist with the nonconvertible asymmetry of origin; this tension sustains distinction without hierarchy.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-04","text":"Operation ad extra is common, yet mission is personally distributed; the connection between common effect, proper origin, and appropriation remains a delicate joint.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-05","text":"The real reception of Gift is combined with the denial of mastery, while the created gift disposes toward the uncreated Gift and yet does not make the person possessable.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_15:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-06","text":"The creatureliness of the world is metaphysically demonstrated, but actual novelty does not follow from the same axioms; coherence rests on the distinction between dependence and revealed decree.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:FL-07","text":"The actual unity of the world and the distribution of singulars are ordered under wisdom, but the necessity of the one possible order and the account of each allocation are not fully derived.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:FL-04","CL_GGVC_16:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-01","text":"Love changes from an essential disposition into a proceeding person, then into the first Gift and the attractor of economic communication.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:MP-02","CL_GGVC_13:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-02","text":"Procession changes where interior origin is transferred to temporally terminated mission and thereafter to the emanation of universal being.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-03","text":"Presence changes from the mutual indwelling of persons into common causality and then into cognitive and loving indwelling.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-04","text":"Creation changes from event or motion into an ontological relation, while «ex nihilo» bifurcates from the negation of matter into the question of temporal novelty.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:MP-01","CL_GGVC_16:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MP-05","text":"Multitude changes from an apparent repugnance to unity into an instrument of distributed representation and the complement of the good of the whole.","refs":["CL_GGVC_16:MP-03","CL_GGVC_16:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a machine of simple act: a non-spatial center is articulated by personal relations and produces the created field without a preexisting receptacle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_16:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:HSI-02","text":"The divine person is a subsisting term of relation; the creature is a receptive and active node that participates in being, form, and the power of acting.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_16:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:HSI-03","text":"Time is a line unfolded with the world around an eternal source, not a container through which God passes or before which the world is absent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_16:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:HSI-04","text":"Navigation occurs through distinctions of origin, identity, possession, term, and causal magnitude, by which every return of the creature into the constitutive principle of the source is forbidden.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:HSI-05","CL_GGVC_14:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B01","text":"Axioms concerning simplicity, subsisting relation, communication of fullness, mission, universal creation, and ordered plurality suffice to restore the principal structure of this meta-aggregate.","refs":["CL_GGVC_15:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_16:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the connection whereby unity is not divided by relations, communication does not diminish fullness, and universality of effect excludes every antecedent subject.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_16:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B03","text":"The criteria for accommodating names, the necessity of individual appropriations, the connection of created and uncreated gift, the decree of beginning, and the distribution of singular forms are not yet sufficiently determined.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_15:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_16:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B04","text":"The system coheres if real identity, the typing of accounts, asymmetry of origin, dependence of participation, and the limit of reason are retained together and not reduced to one genus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_14:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_16:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_04:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement from intradivine articulation through Gift and mission to creation and the order of the world; the contingent decree and the singularity of the distribution of wisdom resist full reduction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_13:MRT-B06","CL_GGVC_16:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_04@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_GGVC_L1_04:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Ggvc L1 04","context":"Chapters 37–48 · Prima pars · Quaestio 36 · Prima pars · Quaestio 37 · Prima pars · Quaestio 38 · Prima pars · Quaestio 39 · Prima pars · Quaestio 40 · Prima pars · Quaestio 41 · Prima pars · Quaestio 42 · Prima pars · Quaestio 43 · Prima pars · Quaestio 44 · Prima pars · Quaestio 45 · Prima pars · Quaestio 46 · Prima pars · Quaestio 47"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05","layer":"MC","title":"Ggvc L1 05","order":5,"parents":["CL_GGVC_17","CL_GGVC_18","CL_GGVC_19","CL_GGVC_20"],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_05@R001","sha256":"3ced54d9117a9d83a708e045bc51629ec9e8a2ee038d9dcf169354bb1c6db170","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo primatus boni et immaterialitas substantiae intellectualis serventur, dum eadem creatura finita, localiter efficax, cognoscens, volens et defectui obnoxia manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-01","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-01","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B01","CL_GGVC_20:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-02","text":"Spiritus creatus constituitur ut actualitas bona et recepta cuius finitas non ex materia vel malo, sed ex termino essentiae, virtutis, medii et competentiae provenit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-09","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-06","CL_GGVC_19:GA-01","CL_GGVC_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio distinctionibus metaphysico-causalibus procedit, quae unionem, praesentiam, operationem et vocabula psychologica secundum modum essendi creaturae purgant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-03","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-03","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B08","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-04","text":"Motus ab asymmetria boni et mali ad substantiam immaterialem, ab ea ad exteriorizationem virtutis, deinde ad mediationem cognitivam et appetitum intellectualem ascendit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-04","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-05","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_20:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-05","text":"Progressio est recursiva et spiralis, quia quaevis perfectio nova ad finitatem receptam redit eamque sub alio modo praesentiae vel operationis iterum determinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-07","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-04","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B05","CL_GGVC_20:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-06","text":"Negatio hic non evacuat agrum, sed falsas aequationes removendo positivam architecturam entis recepti, causalitatis virtualis, similitudinis intentionalis et libertatis ordinatae producit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_17:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_18:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-07","text":"Clausura analogica distinguit corpus circumscriptum, spiritum definitive et intentionaliter praesentem atque Deum infinitum, cognitionem creatam tamen veram et perfectionem naturalem apertam servans.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:ABS-08","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B06","CL_GGVC_20:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-08","text":"Clausura terminalis est participativa, non autonoma: ordo unus formas finitas multiplicat, subiectum creatum excentricum constituit et completionem naturalem ad donum supernaturale aperit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-08","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Omnis actualitas positiva rationem boni habet; malum bonum debitum et subiectum capax praesupponit, neque finitas vel dependentia sola malum constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-01","CL_GGVC_17:GA-02","CL_GGVC_17:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Creatura intellectualis finita esse recipit, ideo essentia eius neque ipsum esse neque actus purus neque identitas omnium principiorum operativorum est.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-09","CL_GGVC_19:GA-01","CL_GGVC_20:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Immaterialitas est modus positivus perfectionis qui corpus naturale et organum excludit, non autem finitudinem, mediationem, causalitatem exteriorem aut successionem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_18:GA-01","CL_GGVC_19:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Finitas eadem secundum campos diversos apparet ut esse receptum, virtus ad unum terminum applicata, essentia medio cognitivo insufficiens et completudo intra ambitum naturae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-09","CL_GGVC_18:GA-05","CL_GGVC_19:GA-08","CL_GGVC_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Essentia, esse, virtus, operatio, intellectus et voluntas in creatura distinguuntur, quamvis operationes modum substantiae sequantur et unitatem subiecti servent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-08","CL_GGVC_19:CGD-03","CL_GGVC_20:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Operatio finita determinatur per applicationem virtutis, unionem formalem obiecti, medium cognoscendi et rationem formalem potentiae, non per solam praesentiam substantiae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-04","CL_GGVC_19:GA-03","CL_GGVC_20:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Praesentia creaturae spiritualis multiplicatur analogice in praesentiam virtualem ad terminum, formalem sui, intentionalem alterius et supernaturalem per Verbum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-06","CL_GGVC_19:GA-09","CL_GGVC_19:MME-04","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Mediatio non infert materialitatem: corpus assumptum, species intelligibilis et Verbum diversis ordinibus relationem efficiunt sine conversione medii in naturam subiecti.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-07","CL_GGVC_19:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Causalitas divina dat esse, ordinem, conservationem, species et intelligibilitatem, dum modos finitos operationis et causalitatem secundam non absorbet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-07","CL_GGVC_19:GA-06","CL_GGVC_19:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Perfectio universi ab ordine graduum ad hierarchiam virtutum et compressionem mediorum transit, ita ut superioritas per eminentem continentiam, non per aggregationem, mensuretur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-04","CL_GGVC_19:GA-07","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Perfectio creata est plenitudo relativa quae aseitatem, omnipraesentiam, omniscientiam et comprehensionem divinam excludit atque receptioni ulterioris doni patet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GNX-06","CL_GGVC_20:GA-06","CL_GGVC_20:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Determinatio intellectus ad verum et libertas voluntatis circa bona particularia cohaerent, quia verum et bonum eandem rem sub rationibus formalibus diversis praesentant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:GA-09","CL_GGVC_20:XD-07","CL_GGVC_20:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Temporalitas spiritualis est successio finitarum applicationum, conceptionum et revelationum, quae nec motum corporeum continuum nec discursum acquisitivum necessario requirit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_18:XD-06","CL_GGVC_20:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Esse participatum est centrum systematis: creaturam positive constituit, a Deo distinguit, mediis proportionatis operari facit et ad principium suum cognoscitive atque appetitive ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RLE-07","CL_GGVC_19:GA-02","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-01","text":"Bonum identitatem ontologicam retinet, sed a fundamento privationis ad ordinem causalitatis, conservationis, intelligibilitatis et appetitus terminalis amplificatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_17:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-02","text":"Immaterialitas a negatione corporis mutatur in principium causalitatis virtualis, cognitionis non organicae et receptionis supernaturaliter non comprehensivae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_18:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_19:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-03","text":"Finitas ab esse recepto migrat in terminum loci, insufficientiam essentiae ut medii totalis et ambitum determinatum perfectionis naturalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-09","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-06","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B05","CL_GGVC_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-04","text":"Operatio redit functione mutata: naturam manifestat, virtutem exteriorizat, cognitionem actualem constituit et sub ratione voluntaria ordinem moralem subiecti format.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_18:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_20:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-05","text":"Praesentia a situ quantitativo discedit et in modos virtuales, formales atque intentionales distribuitur, quorum distinctio identitatem subiecti et obiecti impedit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-06","CL_GGVC_19:GA-09","CL_GGVC_19:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-06","text":"Materia et corpus a condicionibus universalibus finitudinis ad regiones particulares corruptionis, significationis sensibilis et termini operationis dislocantur; species earum locum in cognitione non organica accipiunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_18:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_19:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_20:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-07","text":"Universalitas a communitate obiecti ad eminentiam medii cognoscitivi, deinde ad rationem boni quae appetitum et libertatem constituit, transfertur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_20:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_20:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-08","text":"Perfectio functionem mutat ab ordine qui gradus defectibiles continet ad operationem non indigentem, compressionem cognitivam et completudinem naturalem supernaturali dono apertam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-04","CL_GGVC_18:GA-10","CL_GGVC_19:GA-07","CL_GGVC_20:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-09","text":"Voluntas ab obiecto cognitioni alienae clauso ad potentiam distinctam, regulam iudicii et principium electionis particularis sub bono universali amplificatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_20:MP-02","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-10","text":"Privatio rationem mali stabiliter servat, sed a finitate, incorporeitate et ignorantia naturali separatur; in ordine morali deficitio ut deordinatio voluntaria specialiter redit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_17:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-01","text":"Comparationes culpae et poenae locales manent; ad praesentem gradum ascendit tantum asymmetria universalis inter entitatem positivam operationis et defectum ordinis debiti.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_17:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-02","text":"Condensatio, coloratio et actiones corporum assumptorum sunt mechanismi apparitionis locales; globaliter persistit exteriorizabilitas virtutis finitae sine incarnatione substantiae spiritualis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_18:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-03","text":"Numerus, species et singularitas Angelorum sunt determinationes inferiores; sursum servantur pluralitas formarum, gradatio virtutum et distributio hierarchica cognitionis atque officii.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_19:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-04","text":"Distinctio motus continui et non continui localiter physicam Angelicam ordinat, sed hoc gradu amplificatur in principium successionis spiritualis sine discursu vel tempore corporeo univoco.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-05","text":"Divisio intellectus agentis et possibilis condicionibus cognitionis humanae relinquitur; globaliter persistit articulatio creaturae per potentiam, formam, medium et operationem distincta.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_19:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-06","text":"Distinctio matutini et vespertini manet forma exegetica localis, sed circuitus processionis a principio, cognitionis termini et reditus ad principium structuram superiorem ingreditur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_20:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-07","text":"Transitus ad substantiam immaterialem, exteriorizationem virtutis et species connaturales non sunt argumenta addita, sed mutationes quibus finitas a materia et immediatio a carentia mediationis separantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:CGD-06","CL_GGVC_18:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_19:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-08","text":"Aequationes inter privationem et materiam, spiritum et ubiquitatem, intellectum superiorem et omniscientiam, simplicitatem et identitatem potentiarum obsoletae fiunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_20:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_20:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-01","text":"Malum neque natura positiva neque principium primum est, sed defectus boni debiti intra subiectum et actionem positiva.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_17:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-02","text":"Materia non est fundamentum universale finitudinis, potentiae vel individuationis, neque spiritus creatus corpus naturaliter proprium requirit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_18:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-03","text":"Immaterialitas creaturae neque infinitatem virtualem neque ubiquitatem neque multilocationem neque liberationem ab omni termino causali confert.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_18:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-04","text":"Cognitio intellectualis creata neque experientia sensibili universaliter acquiritur neque propinquitate locali mensuratur neque identitate naturali cum cognito efficitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_19:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-05","text":"Nulla creatura est ipsum intelligere subsistens aut identitas essentiae, intellectus et voluntatis; convertibilitas veri et boni distinctionem potentiarum non delet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_20:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-06","text":"Excellentia intellectus creati nec omniscientiam nec visionem comprehensivam Dei nec possessionem omnium futurorum, actuum liberorum et secretorum efficit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_20:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-01","text":"Systema constare non potest si omnis limitatio vel immaterialitas finita aut malum aut infinitatem implicet; finitas positiva tertium membrum necessarium constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_19:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet omnem relationem realem aut per contactum corporeum aut per identitatem naturalem effici; causalitas virtualis et species intentionalis alios modos introducunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_19:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet perfectionem exigere uniformitatem partium vel abolitionem differentiae inter naturam, gratiam et omniscientiam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_20:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-04","text":"Causa prima non potest ita concipi ut defectum causae secundae, instrumenta cognitionis creatae aut ordinationem subiecti ad bonum extra se absorbeat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_19:DBN-04","CL_GGVC_20:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-05","text":"Categoriarum humanarum distinctio conceptui reali simpliciter isomorpha esse nequit; vocabula corporis, loci, sensus et passionis purganda sunt antequam spiritui attribuantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:DBN-06","CL_GGVC_19:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSA-01","text":"Malum purum sine subiecto bono, actualitate positiva, perfectione debita aut principio proximo deficiente est possibilitas ontologice inadmissibilis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSA-02","text":"Creatura quae esse non recipiat atque ex uno situ creato omnia loca, tempora et actus interiores comprehendat implicite excluditur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_20:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSA-03","text":"Nulla figura sensibilis vel repraesentatio creata naturam spiritualem aut essentiam divinam exhaustive exhibere potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_19:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSA-04","text":"Creatura sibi sufficiens, in se tamquam ultimo cognoscibili et appetibili clausa, simul libera et perfecte autonoma esse non potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_20:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-01","text":"Finitas spiritualis est determinatio positiva quae successive essentiam, virtutem localem, medium cognitivum et ambitum naturalem terminat; ideo neque materia neque privatio neque malum est.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-04","CL_GGVC_17:GA-09","CL_GGVC_18:GA-05","CL_GGVC_19:GA-08","CL_GGVC_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-02","text":"Applicatio virtutis, unio obiecti et ratio formalis sunt structurae homologae determinationis: capacitatem immaterialem ad terminum proprium ordinant sine unione substantiali.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-08","CL_GGVC_18:GA-04","CL_GGVC_19:GA-03","CL_GGVC_20:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-03","text":"Corpus repraesentativum et species intentionalis demonstrant relationem realem posse effectum vel praesentiam constituere sine identitate naturali inter medium, subiectum et terminum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-08","CL_GGVC_18:XD-03","CL_GGVC_19:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-04","text":"Incorruptibilitas substantiae spiritualis cum mutatione locali, successione conceptuum et novitate revelationis componitur, quia stabilitas essendi successionem operationum non excludit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-13","CL_GGVC_17:GA-11","CL_GGVC_18:GA-08","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-05","text":"Providentia unum ordinem ontologicum et intelligibilem constituit: res esse et conservationem accipiunt, intellectus species proportionatas, et cognitio beatifica medium unitarium.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-14","CL_GGVC_17:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_19:GA-06","CL_GGVC_19:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-06","text":"Deordinatio moralis emergit ubi actus positive causatus voluntate ultra competentiam cognitionis naturalis extenditur; defectus iudicii non fit entitas a causa prima producta.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-12","CL_GGVC_17:GA-12","CL_GGVC_20:XD-03","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-07","text":"Hierarchia coniungit pluralitatem formarum, ministerium virtutis, compressionem mediorum et distributionem revelationis, nec superioribus omniscientiam nec inferioribus inutilitatem tribuit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-11","CL_GGVC_17:GA-05","CL_GGVC_18:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_19:GA-07","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-08","text":"Methodus apophatica transversalis est machina positiva: reificationem mali, biologizationem apparitionis, localizationem cognitionis et humanizationem intellectus removet, donec modi proprii spiritus appareant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-08","CL_GGVC_17:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_18:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_19:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-01","text":"Ontologia spiritus creati fit machina participationis exteriorizabilis: actualitas recepta per virtutes, formas et relationes operatur, sed in nullum effectum suam essentiam totaliter diffundit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_18:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_19:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-02","text":"Locus, cognitio et appetitus fiunt topologiae praesentiae distinctae: terminus causaliter attingitur, obiectum intentionaliter adest, bonum autem sub ratione universali inclinationem elicit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_19:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-03","text":"Tempus creaturae spiritualis non est receptaculum univocum, sed multiplicitas successionum operativarum: applicationum localium, conceptionum et revelationum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_18:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_19:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-04","text":"Ordo essendi et ordo cognoscendi coordinantur ab eadem providentia, quae causalitatem rerum, species intellectuum et reditum per Verbum sine confusione constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_19:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_19:MME-05","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-05","text":"Subiectum angelicum emergit ut nodus excentricus: sibi formaliter praesens, mundo virtualiter et intentionaliter apertus, libere activus, sed fonte essendi et fine boni dependens.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_18:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_19:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-06","text":"Malum et error non designant regiones ontologicas, sed disiunctiones locales inter actum positivum, perfectionem debitam, competentiam naturalem et regulam divinam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_20:XD-03","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-07","text":"Hierarchia est administratio finitae participationis: gradus superiores plura per pauciora media continent et inferioribus ministrant, sed scientiam aut causalitatem divinam non usurpant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_19:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-01","text":"Esse receptum → essentia finita → virtutes distinctae → media proportionata → operationes determinatae → dependentia conservativa: terminus cuiusque progressionis ad participationem initialem redit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RLE-07","CL_GGVC_18:GA-05","CL_GGVC_19:GA-08","CL_GGVC_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-02","text":"Negatio reificationis → distinctio modi → restitutio relationis positivae → nova negatio excessus: circuitus apophaticus per malum, corpus, cognitionem et voluntatem iteratur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_18:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_19:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-03","text":"Incorporeitas → virtus exteriorizabilis → unus terminus localis → successio applicationum → tempus proprium: operatio externa finitatem spiritus confirmat potius quam naturam eius corporalem facit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:XD-02","CL_GGVC_18:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_18:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-04","text":"Finitas essentiae → species proportionatae → universalitas eminentior → cognitio boni universalis → voluntas → error competentiae vel receptio revelationis: cognitionis augmentum ad terminum suum recurrit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:AS-B05","CL_GGVC_20:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_20:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_20:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-05","text":"Modus recipientis est attractor classificatorius: ex eo definiuntur terminus localis, unio obiecti, medium cognitionis, ratio formalis potentiae et capacitas doni.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:GA-01","CL_GGVC_19:GA-03","CL_GGVC_18:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-06","text":"Causalitas divina, applicatio virtutis, species intelligibilis et Verbum formant seriem attractorum concentricorum, quorum quisque pluralitatem creatam ordinat sine identitate cum principio.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RLE-07","CL_GGVC_18:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_19:GA-06","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-07","text":"Perfectio limitata est attractor terminalis: quod naturaliter completum est eo ipso non fit autonomum, sed proprium ambitum servando ad bonum infinitum aperitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_20:MP-04","CL_GGVC_20:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FGS-B01","text":"Actualitas positiva → ratio boni → privatio tantum in subiecto bono → exclusio mali substantialis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-01","CL_GGVC_17:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FGS-B02","text":"Esse receptum → essentia terminans → finitas immaterialis → dependentia sine materialitate necessaria.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-09","CL_GGVC_19:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FGS-B03","text":"Finitas creaturae → distinctio essentiae, virtutis et operationis → distinctio intellectus et voluntatis → pluralitas principiorum sub unitate subiecti.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:CGD-03","CL_GGVC_20:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FGS-B04","text":"Potentia immaterialis + terminus proportionatus → applicatio virtualis, species intentionalis aut ratio formalis → praesentia determinata sine unione substantiali.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-04","CL_GGVC_19:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_20:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FGS-B05","text":"Operationes determinatae → localitas virtualis, cognitio intentionalis et electio appetitiva → successiones propriae spiritus creati.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-08","CL_GGVC_19:GA-09","CL_GGVC_20:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FGS-B06","text":"Actus positivus + absentia ordinis debiti → privatio vel error → defectus causae secundae sine principio positivo mali.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-12","CL_GGVC_20:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FGS-B07","text":"Providentia causans et conservans → species et ordines hierarchici → Verbum ut medium supernaturale → reditus creaturae sine comprehensione divina.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11","CL_GGVC_20:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:AFM-B01","text":"Primatus boni → malum privativum → esse receptum → immaterialitas finita → virtus localiter applicata → species intentionalis → universalitas cognitiva → voluntas libera → completudo naturalis aperta → reditus per Verbum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-07","CL_GGVC_18:GA-04","CL_GGVC_19:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_20:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:AFM-B02","text":"Negatio dualismi → negatio corporis naturalis → negatio cognitionis localis → negatio omniscientiae creatae → reclassificatio finitudinis → multiplicatio mediorum → clausura participativa et analogica.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-08","CL_GGVC_18:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_19:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_20:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-01","text":"Finitas reclassificatur a carentia quasi materiali in structuram positivam terminationis per essentiam, virtutem, medium et ambitum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-06","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B05","CL_GGVC_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-02","text":"Praesentia non amplius unicum genus situs significat, sed formalem, virtualem et intentionalem habitudinem secundum modum subiecti atque termini.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-06","CL_GGVC_19:GA-09","CL_GGVC_19:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-03","text":"Mediatio a signo imperfectionis in principium proprium operationis creatae convertitur; excellentia superior media non delet, sed ea comprimit et elevat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-07","CL_GGVC_19:GA-07","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-04","text":"Hierarchia ab ordine dignitatum statico ad distributionem finitarum virtutum, formarum cognoscitivarum, revelationum et officiorum reordinatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-05","text":"Determinatio et libertas desinunt esse opposita, quia intellectus secundum verum et voluntas secundum bonum particulare diversis axibus formalibus moventur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:GA-09","CL_GGVC_20:XD-07","CL_GGVC_20:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-06","text":"Malum et falsitas ad defectus ordinationis reclassificantur, non ad defectum omnis actualitatis; inde responsabilitas secundum competentiam et regulam distribui potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_20:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-07","text":"Tempus ab mensura cosmologica universali in successiones proprias operationum finitarum dividitur, sine attributione aeternitatis creaturae spirituali.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:MME-04","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei aguntur malum, immaterialitas, apparentia, locus, motus, cognitio et voluntas Angelorum; haec obiecta tamen unam quaestionem de modo creaturae spiritualis finitae explicant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-01","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-01","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B01","CL_GGVC_20:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali operantur actus et potentia, essentia et esse, virtus et terminus, forma intelligibilis, medium cognoscendi et ratio formalis obiecti.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_18:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_19:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico difficultates mutantur in quaestiones de competentia categoriarum, modis relationis et legitimis terminis attributionis ad spiritum creatum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_18:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_19:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est ordo actualitatum receptarum, praesentiarum non univocarum et successionum quae inter aeternitatem et motum corporeum consistunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_18:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_19:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo doctrina ipsam purgationem quam affirmat exercet: vocabula dividit, falsas identitates negat et relationes positivas analogice restituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_18:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_19:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-01","text":"Perfectio universi defectibilitatem graduum requirit, sed malum actuale perfectionem non constituit; nexus inter potentiam deficiendi et defectum actualem incompletus manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:FL-01","CL_GGVC_17:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-02","text":"Corpus assumptum simul signum, instrumentum et terminus virtutis ponitur, sed criterium unitatis termini virtualis et relatio harum functionum non plene formalizantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:FL-02","CL_GGVC_18:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-03","text":"Species universalior cognitionem propriorum singularium continere dicitur, sed mensura eminentiae et mechanismus repraesentationis individuationis remanent asserti potius quam demonstrati.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:FL-02","CL_GGVC_20:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-04","text":"Completudo naturalis, suspensio actus et duplex medium cognitionis naturalis ac beatificae coexistunt, sed coordinatio eorum functionalis ulteriorem determinationem requirit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:FL-02","CL_GGVC_20:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-05","text":"Libertas ex iudicio universali elicitur et voluntas iudicium deordinare potest, sed transitus causalis inter apprehensionem, electionem et falsum iudicium non plene explicatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:FL-03","CL_GGVC_20:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MP-01","text":"Materia mutatur a principio generali limitationis in condicionem regionalem corporum, dum corpus mutatur in medium repraesentativum et terminum virtutis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_18:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MP-02","text":"Locus mutatur a situ quantitativo in praesentiam virtualem, deinde praesentia ipsa amplificatur in topologiam formalem et intentionalem cognitionis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-06","CL_GGVC_19:GA-09","CL_GGVC_19:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MP-03","text":"Universalitas mutatur ab ambitu obiecti in compressionem medii et tandem in rationem formalem boni, qua appetitus intellectualis atque libertas generantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_20:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_20:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MP-04","text":"Perfectio mutatur ab apparentia superioritatis absolutae in completudinem localem quae visionem Verbi et ulteriorem revelationem recipit sine conversione in comprehensionem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:MP-04","CL_GGVC_20:AS-07","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina participationis et translationis stratificatae, in qua actualitas per formas, virtutes, media et rationes formales descendit sine divisione primi principii.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_18:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_19:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum angelicum est nodus finitus et excentricus, sibi praesens, terminis exterioribus efficax, aliis intentionaliter apertus et ad bonum supra essentiam suam ordinatus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_18:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_19:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-03","text":"Tempus huius machinae est series asymmetrica et discreta, in qua conservationem substantiae applicationes, conceptiones et revelationes successive variant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_18:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_19:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis fit per custodiam distinctionum et ambituum: spiritus virtutem applicat, similitudines sequitur, terminos cognitionis servat et voluntatem ad principium reducit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_18:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_19:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de primatu boni, esse recepto, immaterialitate positiva, finitudine virtuali, mediatione intentionali, distinctione potentiarum et completudine aperta sufficiunt ad machinam metagregis restituendam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_18:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_19:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_20:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est translatio finitudinis per diversos campos sine eius aequatione cum materia, malo vel ignorantia, atque ordinatio omnium mediorum sub participatione divina.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_18:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_19:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_20:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent actualizatio defectibilitatis, unitas termini virtualis, mensura universalitatis eminentis, repraesentatio singularitatis et causalitas voluntatis in iudicium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_18:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_19:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_20:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat si a purificatione causalitatis ad immaterialitatem, ab immaterialitate ad operationes mediatas et ab his ad intellectum, voluntatem atque reditum supernaturalem procedit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:MRT-B06","CL_GGVC_18:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_19:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_20:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B05","text":"Systema hac magnitudine cohaeret recursive potius quam lineariter; reductioni axiomaticae resistunt analogia sermonis, exegesis localis et commissurae quibus media distincta sub una participatione coordinantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_20:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_20:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T173810396727Z_498ae352","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Ggvc L1 05","source_sha256":"3ced54d9117a9d83a708e045bc51629ec9e8a2ee038d9dcf169354bb1c6db170","sha256":"0079af140cdaaeec438e4958de482154b41be923f80f64cbe0da119dfd838076","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:38:10.397676+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how the primacy of the good and the immateriality of intellectual substance are preserved, while the same creature remains finite, locally effective, knowing, willing, and liable to defect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-01","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-01","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B01","CL_GGVC_20:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-02","text":"The created spirit is constituted as a good and received actuality whose finitude proceeds not from matter or evil, but from the limit of essence, power, medium, and competence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-09","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-06","CL_GGVC_19:GA-01","CL_GGVC_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-03","text":"The argument proceeds by metaphysical-causal distinctions, which purify union, presence, operation, and psychological terms according to the creature's mode of being.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-03","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-03","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B08","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-04","text":"The movement ascends from the asymmetry of good and evil to immaterial substance, from it to the exteriorization of power, then to cognitive mediation and intellectual appetite.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-04","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-05","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_20:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-05","text":"The progression is recursive and spiral, because every new perfection returns to received finitude and determines it anew under another mode of presence or operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-07","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-04","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B05","CL_GGVC_20:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-06","text":"Negation here does not evacuate the field, but, by removing false equations, produces a positive architecture of received being, virtual causality, intentional likeness, and ordered freedom.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_17:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_18:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-07","text":"The analogical closure distinguishes the circumscribed body, the spirit present definitively and intentionally, and infinite God, while preserving created yet true knowledge and open natural perfection.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:ABS-08","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B06","CL_GGVC_20:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BAS-08","text":"The terminal closure is participatory, not autonomous: one order multiplies finite forms, constitutes the created subject as eccentric, and opens natural completion to supernatural gift.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-08","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every positive actuality has the character of the good; evil presupposes a due good and a capable subject, and neither finitude nor dependence alone constitutes evil.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-01","CL_GGVC_17:GA-02","CL_GGVC_17:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The intellectual creature receives finite being; therefore its essence is neither being itself nor pure act nor the identity of all operative principles.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-09","CL_GGVC_19:GA-01","CL_GGVC_20:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Immateriality is a positive mode of perfection that excludes a natural body and organ, but not finitude, mediation, external causality, or succession.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_18:GA-01","CL_GGVC_19:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The same finitude appears according to different fields as received being, power applied to one terminus, essence insufficient as a cognitive medium, and completion within the scope of nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-09","CL_GGVC_18:GA-05","CL_GGVC_19:GA-08","CL_GGVC_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Essence, being, power, operation, intellect, and will are distinct in the creature, although operations follow the mode of substance and preserve the unity of the subject.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-08","CL_GGVC_19:CGD-03","CL_GGVC_20:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Finite operation is determined by the application of power, the formal union of the object, the medium of knowing, and the formal ratio of the power, not by the mere presence of substance.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-04","CL_GGVC_19:GA-03","CL_GGVC_20:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The presence of the spiritual creature is analogically multiplied into virtual presence at a terminus, formal presence of itself, intentional presence of another, and supernatural presence through the Word.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-06","CL_GGVC_19:GA-09","CL_GGVC_19:MME-04","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Mediation does not entail materiality: an assumed body, intelligible species, and the Word effect relation in different orders without converting the medium into the nature of the subject.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-07","CL_GGVC_19:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Divine causality gives being, order, conservation, species, and intelligibility, while not absorbing finite modes of operation and secondary causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-07","CL_GGVC_19:GA-06","CL_GGVC_19:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] The perfection of the universe passes from an order of grades to a hierarchy of powers and a compression of media, such that superiority is measured by eminent containment, not aggregation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-04","CL_GGVC_19:GA-07","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Created perfection is a relative fullness that excludes aseity, omnipresence, omniscience, and divine comprehension, and is open to the reception of a further gift.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GNX-06","CL_GGVC_20:GA-06","CL_GGVC_20:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] The determination of intellect toward the true and the freedom of will concerning particular goods cohere, because the true and the good present the same thing under different formal ratios.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:GA-09","CL_GGVC_20:XD-07","CL_GGVC_20:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Spiritual temporality is the succession of finite applications, conceptions, and revelations, which requires neither continuous bodily motion nor necessarily acquisitive discursus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_18:XD-06","CL_GGVC_20:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Participated being is the center of the system: it positively constitutes the creature, distinguishes it from God, makes it operate through proportionate media, and orders it cognitively and appetitively to its principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RLE-07","CL_GGVC_19:GA-02","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-01","text":"The good retains its ontological identity, but is expanded from the foundation of privation to the order of causality, conservation, intelligibility, and terminal appetite.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_17:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-02","text":"Immateriality is transformed from the negation of body into a principle of virtual causality, non-organic knowledge, and supernaturally non-comprehensive reception.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_18:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_19:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-03","text":"Finitude migrates from received being into the limit of place, the insufficiency of essence as a total medium, and the determined scope of natural perfection.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-09","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-06","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B05","CL_GGVC_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-04","text":"Operation returns with a changed function: it manifests nature, exteriorizes power, constitutes actual knowledge, and, under the voluntary ratio, forms the moral order of the subject.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_18:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_20:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-05","text":"Presence departs from quantitative position and is distributed into virtual, formal, and intentional modes, whose distinction prevents the identity of subject and object.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-06","CL_GGVC_19:GA-09","CL_GGVC_19:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-06","text":"Matter and body are displaced from universal conditions of finitude into particular regions of corruption, sensible signification, and the terminus of operation; species take their place in non-organic knowledge.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_18:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_19:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_20:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-07","text":"Universality is transferred from the community of the object to the eminence of the cognitive medium, then to the ratio of the good that constitutes appetite and freedom.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_20:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_20:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-08","text":"Perfection changes function from an order containing defective grades to non-needy operation, cognitive compression, and natural completion open to supernatural gift.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-04","CL_GGVC_18:GA-10","CL_GGVC_19:GA-07","CL_GGVC_20:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-09","text":"The will is expanded from an object closed to alien knowledge into a distinct power, a rule of judgment, and a principle of particular choice under the universal good.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_20:MP-02","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RMD-10","text":"Privation steadily retains the ratio of evil, but is separated from finitude, incorporeality, and natural ignorance; in the moral order, defect returns specifically as voluntary disorder.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_17:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-01","text":"Comparisons of fault and punishment remain local; only the universal asymmetry between the positive entity of operation and the defect of due order ascends to the present level.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_17:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-02","text":"Condensation, coloration, and actions of assumed bodies are local mechanisms of appearance; globally, the exteriorizability of finite power without incarnation of spiritual substance persists.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_18:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-03","text":"The number, species, and singularity of Angels are lower determinations; above, the plurality of forms, gradation of powers, and hierarchical distribution of knowledge and office are preserved.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_19:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-04","text":"The distinction between continuous and non-continuous motion locally orders Angelic physics, but at this level it is expanded into a principle of spiritual succession without discursus or univocal bodily time.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-05","text":"The division of agent and possible intellect is left to the conditions of human knowledge; globally, the creature's articulation through distinct power, form, medium, and operation persists.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_19:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-06","text":"The distinction of morning and evening remains a local exegetical form, but the circuit of procession from the principle, knowledge of the terminus, and return to the principle enters the higher structure.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_20:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-07","text":"The transition to immaterial substance, the exteriorization of power, and connatural species are not added arguments, but transformations by which finitude is separated from matter and immediacy from the absence of mediation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:CGD-06","CL_GGVC_18:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_19:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:CGD-08","text":"Equations between privation and matter, spirit and ubiquity, higher intellect and omniscience, simplicity and identity of powers become obsolete.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_20:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_20:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-01","text":"Evil is neither a positive nature nor a first principle, but a defect of due good within a positive subject and action.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_17:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-02","text":"Matter is not the universal foundation of finitude, potency, or individuation, nor does the created spirit naturally require a body proper to itself.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_18:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-03","text":"The immateriality of the creature confers neither virtual infinity nor ubiquity nor multilocation nor liberation from every causal limit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_18:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-04","text":"Created intellectual knowledge is neither universally acquired through sensible experience nor measured by local proximity nor effected by natural identity with the known.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_19:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-05","text":"No creature is subsistent understanding itself or the identity of essence, intellect, and will; the convertibility of true and good does not remove the distinction of powers.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_20:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:GNX-06","text":"The excellence of created intellect effects neither omniscience nor a comprehensive vision of God nor possession of all future things, free acts, and secrets.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_20:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-01","text":"The system cannot stand if every finite limitation or immateriality implies either evil or infinity; positive finitude constitutes a necessary third term.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_19:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that every real relation is effected either through bodily contact or through natural identity; virtual causality and intentional species introduce other modes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_19:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that perfection requires uniformity of parts or the abolition of the difference between nature, grace, and omniscience.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_20:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-04","text":"The first cause cannot be conceived so as to absorb the defect of secondary cause, the instruments of created knowledge, or the ordering of the subject to a good outside itself.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_19:DBN-04","CL_GGVC_20:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:DBN-05","text":"The distinction of human categories cannot be simply isomorphic with a real concept; terms of body, place, sense, and passion must be purified before they are attributed to spirit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:DBN-06","CL_GGVC_19:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSA-01","text":"Pure evil without a good subject, positive actuality, due perfection, or a proximate defective principle is an ontologically inadmissible possibility.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSA-02","text":"A creature that does not receive being and comprehends all places, times, and interior acts from one created position is implicitly excluded.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_20:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSA-03","text":"No sensible figure or created representation can exhaustively exhibit spiritual nature or divine essence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_19:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSA-04","text":"A self-sufficient creature, closed within itself as the ultimate knowable and appetible object, cannot be at once free and perfectly autonomous.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_20:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-01","text":"Spiritual finitude is a positive determination that successively limits essence, local power, cognitive medium, and natural scope; therefore it is neither matter nor privation nor evil.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-04","CL_GGVC_17:GA-09","CL_GGVC_18:GA-05","CL_GGVC_19:GA-08","CL_GGVC_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-02","text":"The application of power, union with the object, and formal ratio are homologous structures of determination: they order immaterial capacity to its proper terminus without substantial union.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-08","CL_GGVC_18:GA-04","CL_GGVC_19:GA-03","CL_GGVC_20:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-03","text":"The representative body and intentional species demonstrate that a real relation can constitute effect or presence without natural identity between medium, subject, and terminus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-08","CL_GGVC_18:XD-03","CL_GGVC_19:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-04","text":"The incorruptibility of spiritual substance is compatible with local change, succession of concepts, and novelty of revelation, because stability of being does not exclude succession of operations.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-13","CL_GGVC_17:GA-11","CL_GGVC_18:GA-08","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-05","text":"Providence constitutes one ontological and intelligible order: things receive being and conservation, intellects proportionate species, and beatific knowledge a unitary medium.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-14","CL_GGVC_17:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_19:GA-06","CL_GGVC_19:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-06","text":"Moral disorder emerges where an act positively caused by the will extends beyond the competence of natural knowledge; a defect of judgment does not become an entity produced by the first cause.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-12","CL_GGVC_17:GA-12","CL_GGVC_20:XD-03","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-07","text":"Hierarchy joins plurality of forms, ministry of power, compression of media, and distribution of revelation, attributing neither omniscience to superiors nor uselessness to inferiors.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-11","CL_GGVC_17:GA-05","CL_GGVC_18:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_19:GA-07","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:XD-08","text":"The transversal apophatic method is a positive machine: it removes the reification of evil, the biologization of appearance, the localization of knowledge, and the humanization of intellect, until the proper modes of spirit appear.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_05:GA-08","CL_GGVC_17:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_18:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_19:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-01","text":"The ontology of created spirit becomes a machine of exteriorizable participation: received actuality operates through powers, forms, and relations, yet does not totally diffuse its essence into any effect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_18:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_19:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-02","text":"Place, knowledge, and appetite become distinct topologies of presence: the terminus is causally attained, the object is intentionally present, and the good under a universal ratio elicits inclination.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_19:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-03","text":"The time of the spiritual creature is not a univocal receptacle, but a multiplicity of operative successions: local applications, conceptions, and revelations.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_18:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_19:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-04","text":"The order of being and the order of knowing are coordinated by the same providence, which establishes the causality of things, the species of intellects, and return through the Word without confusion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_19:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_19:MME-05","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-05","text":"The angelic subject emerges as an eccentric node: formally present to itself, virtually and intentionally open to the world, freely active, yet dependent on the source of being and the end of the good.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_18:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_19:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-06","text":"Evil and error do not designate ontological regions, but local disjunctions between positive act, due perfection, natural competence, and divine rule.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_20:XD-03","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:BSD-07","text":"Hierarchy is the administration of finite participation: higher grades contain more through fewer media and minister to inferiors, but do not usurp divine knowledge or causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_19:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-01","text":"Received being → finite essence → distinct powers → proportionate media → determined operations → conservative dependence: the terminus of every progression returns to initial participation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RLE-07","CL_GGVC_18:GA-05","CL_GGVC_19:GA-08","CL_GGVC_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-02","text":"Negation of reification → distinction of mode → restoration of positive relation → new negation of excess: the apophatic circuit is repeated through evil, body, knowledge, and will.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_18:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_19:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-03","text":"Incorporeality → exteriorizable power → one local terminus → succession of applications → proper time: external operation confirms the finitude of spirit rather than making its nature bodily.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:XD-02","CL_GGVC_18:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_18:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-04","text":"Finitude of essence → proportionate species → more eminent universality → knowledge of the universal good → will → error of competence or reception of revelation: the increase of knowledge recurs to its limit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:AS-B05","CL_GGVC_20:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_20:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_20:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-05","text":"The mode of the recipient is a classificatory attractor: from it are defined the local terminus, union with the object, medium of knowledge, formal ratio of power, and capacity for gift.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:GA-01","CL_GGVC_19:GA-03","CL_GGVC_18:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-06","text":"Divine causality, application of power, intelligible species, and the Word form a series of concentric attractors, each of which orders created plurality without identity with the principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RLE-07","CL_GGVC_18:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_19:GA-06","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:RLE-07","text":"Limited perfection is a terminal attractor: what is naturally complete thereby does not become autonomous, but, preserving its proper scope, is opened to the infinite good.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_20:MP-04","CL_GGVC_20:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FGS-B01","text":"Positive actuality → ratio of the good → privation only in a good subject → exclusion of substantial evil.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-01","CL_GGVC_17:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FGS-B02","text":"Received being → limiting essence → immaterial finitude → dependence without necessary materiality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-09","CL_GGVC_19:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FGS-B03","text":"Finitude of the creature → distinction of essence, power, and operation → distinction of intellect and will → plurality of principles under the unity of the subject.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:CGD-03","CL_GGVC_20:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FGS-B04","text":"Immaterial power + proportionate terminus → virtual application, intentional species, or formal ratio → determined presence without substantial union.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-04","CL_GGVC_19:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_20:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FGS-B05","text":"Determined operations → virtual locality, intentional knowledge, and appetitive choice → proper successions of created spirit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-08","CL_GGVC_19:GA-09","CL_GGVC_20:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FGS-B06","text":"Positive act + absence of due order → privation or error → defect of secondary cause without a positive principle of evil.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:GA-12","CL_GGVC_20:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FGS-B07","text":"Causative and conserving providence → species and hierarchical orders → the Word as supernatural medium → return of the creature without divine comprehension.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11","CL_GGVC_20:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:AFM-B01","text":"Primacy of the good → privative evil → received being → finite immateriality → locally applied power → intentional species → cognitive universality → free will → open natural completion → return through the Word.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-07","CL_GGVC_18:GA-04","CL_GGVC_19:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_20:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:AFM-B02","text":"Negation of dualism → negation of natural body → negation of local knowledge → negation of created omniscience → reclassification of finitude → multiplication of media → participatory and analogical closure.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-08","CL_GGVC_18:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_19:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_20:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-01","text":"Finitude is reclassified from a quasi-material lack into a positive structure of limitation through essence, power, medium, and scope.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-06","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B05","CL_GGVC_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-02","text":"Presence no longer signifies a single genus of position, but formal, virtual, and intentional relation according to the mode of subject and terminus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-06","CL_GGVC_19:GA-09","CL_GGVC_19:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-03","text":"Mediation is converted from a sign of imperfection into a proper principle of created operation; higher excellence does not remove media, but compresses and elevates them.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-07","CL_GGVC_19:GA-07","CL_GGVC_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-04","text":"Hierarchy is reordered from a static order of dignities into a distribution of finite powers, cognitive forms, revelations, and offices.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-05","text":"Determination and freedom cease to be opposites, because intellect according to the true and will according to the particular good are moved along different formal axes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:GA-09","CL_GGVC_20:XD-07","CL_GGVC_20:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-06","text":"Evil and falsity are reclassified as defects of ordering, not as a defect of all actuality; hence responsibility can be distributed according to competence and rule.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_20:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MME-07","text":"Time is divided from a universal cosmological measure into proper successions of finite operations, without attributing eternity to the spiritual creature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:MME-04","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, evil, immateriality, appearance, place, motion, knowledge, and the will of Angels are treated; yet these objects explain one question concerning the mode of the finite spiritual creature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:AS-01","CL_GGVC_18:ABS-01","CL_GGVC_19:AS-B01","CL_GGVC_20:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level operate act and potency, essence and being, power and terminus, intelligible form, medium of knowing, and formal ratio of object.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_18:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_19:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, difficulties are transformed into questions concerning the competence of categories, modes of relation, and legitimate limits of attribution to created spirit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_18:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_19:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is an order of received actualities, non-univocal presences, and successions that stand between eternity and bodily motion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_18:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_19:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the doctrine exercises the very purification it affirms: it distinguishes terms, negates false identities, and analogically restores positive relations.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_18:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_19:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_20:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-01","text":"The perfection of the universe requires the defectibility of grades, but actual evil does not constitute perfection; the connection between the power to fail and actual defect remains incomplete.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:FL-01","CL_GGVC_17:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-02","text":"An assumed body is posited simultaneously as sign, instrument, and terminus of power, but the criterion of unity of the virtual terminus and the relation of these functions are not fully formalized.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:FL-02","CL_GGVC_18:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-03","text":"A more universal species is said to contain knowledge of its proper singulars, but the measure of eminence and mechanism for representing individuation remain asserted rather than demonstrated.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:FL-02","CL_GGVC_20:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-04","text":"Natural completion, suspension of act, and the twofold medium of natural and beatific knowledge coexist, but their functional coordination requires further determination.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:FL-02","CL_GGVC_20:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:FL-05","text":"Freedom is elicited from universal judgment and the will can disorder judgment, but the causal transition among apprehension, choice, and false judgment is not fully explained.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:FL-03","CL_GGVC_20:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MP-01","text":"Matter is transformed from a general principle of limitation into a regional condition of bodies, while body is transformed into a representative medium and terminus of power.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_18:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MP-02","text":"Place is transformed from quantitative position into virtual presence, and then presence itself is expanded into the formal and intentional topology of knowledge.","refs":["CL_GGVC_18:GA-06","CL_GGVC_19:GA-09","CL_GGVC_19:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MP-03","text":"Universality is transformed from the scope of the object into compression of the medium and finally into the formal ratio of the good, through which intellectual appetite and freedom are generated.","refs":["CL_GGVC_19:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_20:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_20:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MP-04","text":"Perfection is transformed from the appearance of absolute superiority into local completion that receives the vision of the Word and further revelation without becoming comprehension.","refs":["CL_GGVC_20:MP-04","CL_GGVC_20:AS-07","CL_GGVC_20:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a machine of participation and stratified translation, in which actuality descends through forms, powers, media, and formal ratios without division of the first principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_18:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_19:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-02","text":"The angelic subject is a finite and eccentric node, present to itself, effective at external termini, intentionally open to others, and ordered to a good above its essence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_18:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_19:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-03","text":"The time of this machine is an asymmetrical and discrete series, in which applications, conceptions, and revelations successively vary while the substance is conserved.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_18:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_19:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:HSI-04","text":"Navigation becomes possible through guarding distinctions and scopes: spirit applies power, follows likenesses, preserves the limits of knowledge, and returns the will to the principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_18:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_19:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_20:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B01","text":"Axioms concerning the primacy of the good, received being, positive immateriality, virtual finitude, intentional mediation, distinction of powers, and open completion suffice to reconstruct the meta-aggregate machine.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_18:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_19:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_20:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the translation of finitude through diverse fields without its equation with matter, evil, or ignorance, and the ordering of all media under divine participation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_18:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_19:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_20:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B03","text":"The actualization of defectibility, unity of the virtual terminus, measure of eminent universality, representation of singularity, and causality of the will in judgment remain less determined.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_18:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_19:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_20:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement if it proceeds from the purification of causality to immateriality, from immateriality to mediated operations, and from these to intellect, will, and supernatural return.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:MRT-B06","CL_GGVC_18:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_19:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_20:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_05:MRT-B05","text":"The system at this magnitude coheres recursively rather than linearly; analogies of speech, local exegesis, and the joints by which distinct media are coordinated under one participation resist axiomatic reduction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_17:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_20:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_20:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_05@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_GGVC_L1_05:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Ggvc L1 05","context":"Chapters 49–60 · Prima pars · Quaestio 48 · Prima pars · Quaestio 49 · Prima pars · Quaestio 50 · Prima pars · Quaestio 51 · Prima pars · Quaestio 52 · Prima pars · Quaestio 53 · Prima pars · Quaestio 54 · Prima pars · Quaestio 55 · Prima pars · Quaestio 56 · Prima pars · Quaestio 57 · Prima pars · Quaestio 58 · Prima pars · Quaestio 59"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06","layer":"MC","title":"Ggvc L1 06","order":6,"parents":["CL_GGVC_21","CL_GGVC_22","CL_GGVC_23","CL_GGVC_24"],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_06@R001","sha256":"295cccb2279e5af4bd7ed163e101bb29386f3ac2accb238a5b384373117918dd","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo creaturae spirituales et corporales distinctionem, libertatem, causalitatem atque perfectionem recipiant sine autonomia, dualismo aut confusione ordinum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_22:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_23:AS-B01","CL_GGVC_24:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B02","text":"Thesis globalis omnem actualitatem positivam ut bonum participatum constituit; defectus moralis, informitas et tenebrae non sunt principia positiva, sed modi privationis intra naturam iam creatam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-01","CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","CL_GGVC_23:GA-01","CL_GGVC_24:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B03","text":"Argumentatio per distinctiones recursivas separat esse, naturam, gratiam, defectum, formationem, causalitatem, temporalitatem et denominationem, ut membra apparentia opposita sub ordine superiore conserventur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_22:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_23:AS-B03","CL_GGVC_24:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B04","text":"Motus procedit ab actualitate creata ad distributionem naturarum et regionum, inde ad usum ordinatum vel defectivum potentiarum, atque postremo ad perfectionem, poenam, ornamentum vel clausuram interpretativam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_22:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_23:AS-B07","CL_GGVC_24:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B05","text":"Progressus est spiralis, cumulativus et ramificatus: eadem participatio redit ut receptivitas, causalitas, ordo, libertas, formatio et interpretatio, sed singulis reditibus campum atque functionem mutat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_22:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_23:AS-B06","CL_GGVC_24:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B06","text":"Contradictio apparens fit signum confusionis graduum; correctio non alterum membrum delet, sed genus causae, prioritatis, obiecti vel significationis mutat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:BSD-07","CL_GGVC_23:GA-14","CL_GGVC_24:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B07","text":"Clausura est asymmetrica: status spirituales ad confirmationem vel poenam terminantur, dum expositio cosmologica pluralitatem finitam et conditionalem retinet sub invariabilibus ontologicis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-10","CL_GGVC_22:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_23:AS-B08","CL_GGVC_24:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B08","text":"Machina totius causalitatem divinam et operationem creatam non ut aemulas, sed ut asymmetrice coordinatas generat, ita ut dependentia ipsa possibilitatem actionis, distinctionis et ordinis constituat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-06","CL_GGVC_22:GA-08","CL_GGVC_23:GA-12","CL_GGVC_24:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Omnis natura creata est actualitas bona et participata; neque malum substantiale neque materia omnino informis primum terminum creationis constituere potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","CL_GGVC_23:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Causalitas divina naturis formas, potentias, motus et fines tribuit, ideo operationem creatam perficit potius quam evacuet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-06","CL_GGVC_24:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Bonum partis, gradus naturarum et distributio regionum mensurantur ordine totius, non commodo vel perfectione partis separatae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-03","CL_GGVC_22:GA-10","CL_GGVC_23:GA-12","CL_GGVC_24:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Receptivitas transit a natura intellectuali per gratiam et gloriam ad actualitatem corporalem ulteriori formationi atque ornamento capacem, identitate ordinum non confusa.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-02","CL_GGVC_24:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Defectus praesupponit actualitatem bonam cui rectitudo, manifestatio, decor vel determinatio ulterior deest; privatio numquam principium positivum parallelum fit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-11","CL_GGVC_24:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Potentia agendi creata intra naturam, obiectum et motionem recepta operatur: potest ordini consentire, supernaturali elevatione perfici aut bonum per appropriationem deformare.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Distinctio graduum causalitatis, prioritatis, temporalitatis et significationis est operator globalis quo contradictiones apparentes in relationes ordinatas resolvuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_23:GA-14","CL_GGVC_24:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Tempus creatum est stratificatum: initium essendi, successio actuum, motus corporalis, ordo narrationis, administratio et clausura terminalis diversas mensuras constituunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-08","CL_GGVC_22:GA-11","CL_GGVC_23:GA-13","CL_GGVC_24:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Bonitas divina totum ordinat ut causa essendi, regula actus, finis appetitus et limes interpretationis, sine comprehensione vel aequalitate creaturae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-11","CL_GGVC_22:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Ordo ultimus statum spiritualem confirmare potest et principium mali excludit, dum conflictum inter veritatem revelatam et rationem demonstrativam ad expositiones corrigibiles restringit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-09","CL_GGVC_22:GA-12","CL_GGVC_23:GA-15","CL_GGVC_24:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Cognitio, apparentia et vocabulum non conferunt per se rectitudinem voluntatis neque ontologiam rei exhaustive manifestant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-13","CL_GGVC_23:GA-07","CL_GGVC_24:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Participatio non aemula causalitatem creatam auget secundum modum recipientis, sed omnem efficaciam positivam ad causam primam asymmetrice refert.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-07","CL_GGVC_24:GA-04","CL_GGVC_21:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-01","text":"Participatio identitatem stabilem retinet, sed ab esse angelico ad bonitatem omnis naturae, actualitatem formatam et ecologiam causarum atque regionum extenditur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-01","CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","CL_GGVC_23:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_24:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-02","text":"Relatio partis ad totum migrat ex ordine dilectionis in providentiam, unitatem cosmicam et classificationem regionalem; totalitas fit mensura affectus, causalitatis et dispositionis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_22:GA-10","CL_GGVC_23:GA-12","CL_GGVC_24:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-03","text":"Privatio mutatur ex defectu rectitudinis voluntariae in carentiam manifestationis, decoris vel ulterioris determinationis, semper tamen actualitatem positivam praesupponit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-03","CL_GGVC_24:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-04","text":"Potentia agendi redit ut electio, suggestio, causalitas instrumentalis et motus classificatorius; functio mutatur, sed modus receptus et limitatus persistit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_22:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_23:GA-10","CL_GGVC_24:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-05","text":"Productio migrat a creatione substantiae ad formationem, institutionem virtutis, actualitatem speciei et propagationem, unde idem vocabulum in plures ordines causales dividitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-02","CL_GGVC_24:GA-10","CL_GGVC_24:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-06","text":"Tempus ab initio et successione angelica ad ministerium, motum, formationem, narrationem et generationem transit; unitas eius iam non homogeneitas sed coordinatio mensurarum est.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_22:GA-11","CL_GGVC_23:GA-13","CL_GGVC_24:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-07","text":"Distinctio, initio mechanismus localis quaestionum particularium, in attractorem methodologicum mutatur qui totam ontologiam, causalitatem et exegesim ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_23:GA-14","CL_GGVC_23:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_24:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-01","text":"Differentiae specificae amoris angelici, gratiae initialis, peccatorum daemonum et poenae sensibilis locales manent; sursum ascendit sola lex capacitatis receptivae et fixationis actus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_21:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_22:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_22:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-02","text":"Materia caelestis, caelum Empyreum, firmamentum, aquae et distributio avium manent determinationes cosmologicae probabiles; praesentem structuram sustentant tantum ut casus pluralitatis ordinum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_23:CGD-03","CL_GGVC_24:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_24:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-03","text":"Distinctio essentiae et esse, bonitas naturalis et formatio rerum amplificantur in legem generalem secundum quam actualitas recepta ulteriorem ordinationem sine autosufficientia sustinet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_22:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_23:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_24:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-04","text":"Libertas spiritualis et causalitas naturalis unitates coniungunt ut species operationis secundae quae effectus reales producit sine creatione ex nihilo aut abolitione causae primae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-06","CL_GGVC_22:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_24:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-05","text":"Series naturae, electionis, gratiae et gloriae atque series actualitatis, distinctionis et ornatus non identificantur, sed eandem formam receptivitatis successive perfectae exhibent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:CGD-06","CL_GGVC_24:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-06","text":"Exemplaria corporis mali, cosmogenesis ex culpa, chronologia simplex et criterium cosmologicum unicum obsoleta fiunt, quia confundunt ordines moralitatis, originis, formationis et narrationis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_22:GNX-06","CL_GGVC_23:CGD-06","CL_GGVC_24:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-01","text":"Nulla creatura, zona vel materia neutralis extra ordinem divinum admittitur, neque cosmos potest ex principio sibi sufficiente constitui.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_22:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_23:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_24:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-02","text":"Malum, tenebrae et informitas absoluta non sunt substantiae vel co-principia positiva, sed privationes actualitatis iam bonae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_23:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-03","text":"Causalitas divina et operationes secundae non sunt vires aemulae; creatura nec creare ex nihilo nec a prima institutione efficaciam independentem accipere potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_24:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_24:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-04","text":"Tempus non est receptaculum absolutum, nec ordo dierum, actuum et generationum in unam successionem homogeneam contrahitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GNX-07","CL_GGVC_24:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_24:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-05","text":"Nomen univocum, apparentia sensibilis et lectio litteralis singularis non sufficiunt ad naturam, causalitatem aut referentiam rei exhaustive determinandam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_24:GA-07","CL_GGVC_24:GA-08","CL_GGVC_24:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-06","text":"Libertas non est indifferentia absoluta, et terminus perfectus non est inertia; confirmatio finis operationem conservat dum reversionem vel meritum ulterius excludit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_21:GNX-06","CL_GGVC_22:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet causalitatem essendi et causalitatem defectus symmetrice procedere; aliter malum principium positivum contra primam causam fieret.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_22:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet unitatem ex homogeneitate materiae, temporis vel ordinis constare; aliter distinctio partium et pluralitas prioritatum dissolutionem potius quam perfectionem significarent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:DBN-04","CL_GGVC_24:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet actum creatum aut autonomum aut mere passivum esse, quia dependentia a causa prima ipsam operationem propriam constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_24:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet cognitionem, vocabula vel sensibilia immediatam rectitudinem et plenam ontologiam tradere; mediatio affectiva, semantica et causalis necessario manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_23:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_24:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet perfectionem aut subiectum destruere aut finem indefinite reaperire; natura servanda et clausura irreversibilis simul requiruntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_22:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-01","text":"Altissima absentia est exterior neutralis in quo creatura ante vel extra participationem divinam consistere possit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_22:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_23:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-02","text":"Possibilitas profundissime exclusa est altera souveranitas quae esse, finem, causalitatem vel ordinem contra causam primam instituat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_22:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_24:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-03","text":"Aequalitas creaturae cum fonte suo, sive per autonomiam, appropriationem finis sive comprehensionem, manet ontologice indisponibilis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_22:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-04","text":"Systema circumagit impossibilitatem explicationis totaliter sui sufficientis: nec cosmologia unica nec superficies litterae potest originem, structuram et causalitatem mundi exhaustive claudere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_24:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-01","text":"Defectus moralis et informitas cosmologica eandem structuram apophaticam participant: uterque actualitatem bonam praesupponit et carentiam ordinis ulterioris significat, non naturam adversam.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-03","CL_GGVC_24:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-02","text":"Primatus causalitatis divinae et efficacia creaturae directe, non inverse, coordinantur: quo plenius natura formam et motionem recipit, eo proprius secundum modum suum operatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-12","CL_GGVC_21:GA-06","CL_GGVC_24:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-03","text":"Ordo totius transfert mensuram moralem partis in cosmologiam: bonum proprium, inaequalitas et situs regionalis tantum intra relationem ad universam dispositionem plene intelliguntur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","CL_GGVC_21:GA-03","CL_GGVC_22:GA-10","CL_GGVC_24:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-04","text":"Heterogeneitas temporis vetat ne prius et posterius angelici actus, motus corporei et narrationis Geneseos in unam chronologiam transferantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-08","CL_GGVC_21:GA-08","CL_GGVC_23:GA-04","CL_GGVC_24:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-05","text":"Cognitio veri, significatio vocabuli et apparentia sensibilis sunt media realia sed non sufficientia; unumquodque rectitudine affectiva, contextu et examine causali supplendum est.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-11","CL_GGVC_22:GA-13","CL_GGVC_23:GA-07","CL_GGVC_24:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-06","text":"Systema clausuram ontologicam cum apertura epistemica componit: fines spirituales irreversibiles esse possunt, dum exemplaria cosmologica sub pluralitate finita permanent.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-10","CL_GGVC_21:GA-10","CL_GGVC_22:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_23:AS-B08","CL_GGVC_24:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-07","text":"Creatio substantiae, ulterior formatio et administratio propagativa sunt ordines positivi distincti; unitas primi principii non requirit unum modum productionis omnibus effectibus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-07","CL_GGVC_23:GA-02","CL_GGVC_24:GA-10","CL_GGVC_22:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-01","text":"Differentiatio creata est modus perfectionis positivae, non lapsus ab unitate originaria; malum tantum ordinem differentiarum parasitice deformat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_23:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-02","text":"Perfectio agentiae non consistit in multiplicatione alternativarum, sed in operatione potentiarum receptarum secundum finem; appropriatio autonomica hanc operationem non auget sed deordinat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-12","CL_GGVC_21:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_22:GA-14","CL_GGVC_24:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-03","text":"Historia creata est plexus trajectoriarum inaequalium: initia contingentia, decisiones irreversibiles, motus continuabiles, administrationes temporales et ordines narrativi diversa futura producunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-10","CL_GGVC_21:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_22:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_23:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_24:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-04","text":"Stabilitas veritatis requirit mobilitatem modelorum inferiorum: nucleus ontologicus manet, dum rationes physicae et referentiae semanticae sub probatione corriguntur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-11","CL_GGVC_23:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_23:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_24:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-05","text":"Potestas creata est ministerialis aut instrumentalis; ubi sibi principatum vindicat, fit politica et parasitica potius quam ontologice productiva.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-12","CL_GGVC_22:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_24:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-06","text":"Forma argumentationis ipsam ontologiam performat: distinctiones differentias servant, eas in ordinem distribuunt et unitatem sine identitate univoca efficiunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-07","CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-01","text":"Esse receptum potentiam agendi constituit; actus ordinatus bonum participatum amplius manifestat, unde dependentia ontologica in operationem et diffusionem ordinis recurrit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_21:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_24:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-02","text":"Bonum receptum appropriationem autonomicam patitur; appropriatio defectum parit, defectus privationem et renisum, quos providentia continet sine perversam intentionem abolendo.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-14","CL_GGVC_22:XD-06","CL_GGVC_22:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-03","text":"Actualitas formata informitatem relativam permittit; haec distinctionem postulat, distinctio regiones constituit, regiones ornamenta requirunt, et ornamenta ordinem regionum manifestant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_24:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-04","text":"Aequivocatio vel inconvenientia distinctionem generat; distinctio ramos interpretativos multiplicat, ratio impossibilia amputat, et pluralitas residua novum examen semantico-causale provocat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_23:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_24:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-05","text":"Efficacia creata periculum autonomiae excitat; reductio ad causam primam autonomiam negat, sed causalitatem instrumentalem restituit, unde subordinatio ipsa operationem secundam stabilit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_24:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-06","text":"Attractor communis est distinctio ordinata sub bonitate divina: causa, actus, privatio, regio, tempus et significatio separantur ut in unum ordinem sine confusione redeant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_22:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_23:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FGS-B01","text":"Causa prima → actualitas creata et participata → exclusio mali substantialis atque potentiae purae subsistentis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-01","CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","CL_GGVC_23:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FGS-B02","text":"Actualitas creata → distributio naturarum, formarum et regionum → unitas ordinis differentiarum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-06","CL_GGVC_24:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FGS-B03","text":"Natura receptiva + forma, virtus et motio receptae → operatio propria causae secundae sine rivalitate cum causa prima.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-02","CL_GGVC_24:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FGS-B04","text":"Potentia creata + obiectum bonum + modus appropriationis → actus ordinatus vel defectus voluntarius.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FGS-B05","text":"Actus ordinatus → elevatio, ulterior formatio vel ornamentum → perfectio secundum capacitatem et ordinem totius.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-12","CL_GGVC_24:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FGS-B06","text":"Actus defectivus → privatio rectitudinis sine destructione naturae → poena, frustratio et containmentum providentiale.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_22:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FGS-B07","text":"Tempora heterogenea + distinctio recursiva → clausura terminalis statuum et clausura conditionalis expositionum → cohaerentia sine chronologia vel cosmologia unica.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-11","CL_GGVC_23:GA-14","CL_GGVC_24:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AFM-B01","text":"Participatio essendi → natura ordinata → potentia agendi → consensus vel appropriatio → elevatio aut privatio → confirmatio beatifica vel poenalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_22:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AFM-B02","text":"Actualitas formata → informitas relativa → distinctio formarum et regionum → motus atque causalitates secundae → ornamentum et generatio → perfectio universi.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_24:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AFM-B03","text":"Contradictio apparens → distinctio sensuum, causarum et prioritatum → remotio impossibilium → coordinatio ordinum validorum → pluralitas finita sub clausura qualificata.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_23:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_24:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-01","text":"Oppositio spiritus boni et corporis mali corruit: spiritus potest moraliter deficere, dum corpus et materia bonitatem ontologicam retinent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:MME-01","CL_GGVC_21:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-02","text":"Creatio reclassificatur ab enumeratione eventuum in matricem actualitatis, formationis, distinctionis, ornatus, administrationis et finis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:MME-01","CL_GGVC_24:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-03","text":"Libertas et causalitas cessant significare independentiam; fiunt modi operationis receptae quorum perfectio ex recta ordinatione, non ex indeterminatione, aestimatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_22:MME-02","CL_GGVC_22:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-04","text":"Tempus ex linea unica in tabulam initiorum, successionum intellectualium, motuum corporalium, administrationum et clausurarum mutatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:MME-04","CL_GGVC_22:MME-05","CL_GGVC_24:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-05","text":"Termini negativi fiunt instrumenta ontologica: malum, tenebrae et informitas non nominant entia, sed defectus relationis aut determinationis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:MME-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-06","text":"Interpretatio reclassificatur ut selectio constricta modelorum, in qua auctoritas, ontologia et ratio naturalis terminos possibilitatis communiter determinant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:MME-04","CL_GGVC_24:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei proponit originem et statum angelorum, culpam et poenam daemonum, informitatem, dies, regiones, luminaria, plantas et animalia tamquam quaestiones distinctas.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis eas quaestiones per participationem, privationem, ordinem partium, causalitatem stratificatam, formationem et temporalitates heterogeneas componit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus certamen ex oppositione rerum in discrimen ordinum transfert, ita ut interventio recta genus causae, temporis vel significationis mutet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis realitatem ut actualitatem bonam sed dependentem constituit, cuius historia plures mensuras et clausuras irreversibiles continet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus et performativus ordinem quem asserit ipsa distinctionum iteratione efficit: inferiores differentias servat, subordinat et sub clausura graduata continet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-01","text":"Universalitas causalitatis divinae et imputatio defectus creaturae cohaerent tantum per distinctionem productionis actus positivi a modo deficienti; commissura manet axiomatice necessaria sed non mechanice exhausta.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:FL-01","CL_GGVC_22:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-02","text":"Congruentia naturae cum gloria et utilitas partis intra totum tendunt ad necessitatem teleologicam, quam gratuititas doni et irreducibilitas nocumenti simul restringunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:FL-04","CL_GGVC_22:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-03","text":"Definitivitas electionis spiritualis cum permanentia libertatis et operationis componitur, sed processus quo adhaesio mutabilis fit irreversibilis non plene determinatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:MP-01","CL_GGVC_22:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-04","text":"Invariabilitas veritatis revelatae et mobilitas referentium cosmologicorum cohaerent dum flexibilitas semantica finita manet; criterium huius termini non semper univocum est.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:FL-01","CL_GGVC_23:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-05","text":"Unitas primae institutionis continet productionem actualem, causalem et virtualem, sed relationes historicae inter hos modos atque inter motores spirituales et motus naturales fragiles permanent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:FL-01","CL_GGVC_24:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MP-01","text":"Libertas mutatur a potentia oppositorum ad operationem indefectibilem vel obstinatam, quia centrum eius a pluralitate optionum ad modum adhaesionis transfertur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:MP-02","CL_GGVC_22:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MP-02","text":"Informitas mutatur ab impossibili carentia omnis formae in defectum relativum manifestationis, decoris, distinctionis vel ornatus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:MP-01","CL_GGVC_24:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MP-03","text":"Productio mutatur a constitutione substantiae in formationem, institutionem virtutis, actualitatem speciei et propagationem naturalem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:MP-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MP-04","text":"Prioritas et dies mutantur ab indicibus chronologiae in coordinatas originis, naturae, formationis, narrationis et ornatus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:MP-03","CL_GGVC_24:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina participationis stratificatae, in qua causa prima actualitatem distribuit per naturas, formas, regiones et instrumenta sine absorptionis identitate.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_22:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_23:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_24:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est nodus receptivus et operativus: angelus eligit intra ordinem donatum, interpres autem sensus navigat intra limites auctoritatis, ontologiae et rationis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_22:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_23:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_24:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est plexus arcuum nidificatorum: initium, decisionem, motum, administrationem, generationem et terminum coniungit sine una mensura omnibus communi.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_22:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_23:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_24:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis est ars recte attribuendi: potentiam a fonte, defectum a natura, apparentiam ab ontologia et litteram ab expositione distinguere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_22:HSI-05","CL_GGVC_23:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_24:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata actualitatis participatae, causalitatis non aemulae, privationis, ordinum stratificatorum et interpretationis mediatae sufficiunt ad machinam praesentis metagregis restituendam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est ubi bonum actuale, distinctio creationis a formatione, limitatio causalitatis secundae et ordo partium in unam ontologiam participantem conveniunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B03","text":"Nondum satis determinantur mechanismus irrevocabilitatis angelicae, nexus naturae et gloriae, cosmologiae lucis atque aquarum, et modus actualis vel virtualis productionis viventium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B04","text":"Cohaerentia non ordine lineari, sed reditu distinctionum et mutatione functionum nititur; restitutio verum motum servat quia differentias inferiores non in identitatem complanat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B05","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae resistunt propositiones probabilisticae, referentiae cosmologicae mobiles et transitus inter actualitatem, institutionem et propagationem; haec indeterminatio limites machinae, non defectum centri, designat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B06","CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T173855355718Z_84598bb8","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Ggvc L1 06","source_sha256":"295cccb2279e5af4bd7ed163e101bb29386f3ac2accb238a5b384373117918dd","sha256":"d6250a080121f0d7e6c8bfc2ec4a557b3f7b6ca2cc08e26e37433b649f168404","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:38:55.357726+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B01","text":"The system asks how spiritual and corporeal creatures receive distinction, freedom, causality, and perfection without autonomy, dualism, or confusion of orders.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_22:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_23:AS-B01","CL_GGVC_24:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B02","text":"The global thesis establishes all positive actuality as participated good; moral defect, formlessness, and darkness are not positive principles, but modes of privation within an already created nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-01","CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","CL_GGVC_23:GA-01","CL_GGVC_24:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B03","text":"The argument, through recursive distinctions, separates being, nature, grace, defect, formation, causality, temporality, and denomination, so that apparently opposed members are preserved under a higher order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_22:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_23:AS-B03","CL_GGVC_24:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B04","text":"The movement proceeds from created actuality to the distribution of natures and regions, thence to the ordered or defective use of powers, and finally to perfection, punishment, ornament, or interpretative closure.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_22:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_23:AS-B07","CL_GGVC_24:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B05","text":"The progress is spiral, cumulative, and ramified: the same participation returns as receptivity, causality, order, freedom, formation, and interpretation, but at each return changes field and function.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_22:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_23:AS-B06","CL_GGVC_24:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B06","text":"Apparent contradiction becomes a sign of confusion of levels; correction does not delete either member, but changes the kind of cause, priority, object, or signification.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:BSD-07","CL_GGVC_23:GA-14","CL_GGVC_24:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B07","text":"The closure is asymmetrical: spiritual states terminate in confirmation or punishment, while cosmological exposition retains a finite and conditional plurality under ontological invariants.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-10","CL_GGVC_22:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_23:AS-B08","CL_GGVC_24:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AS-B08","text":"The engine of the whole generates divine causality and created operation not as rivals, but as asymmetrically coordinated, such that dependence itself constitutes the possibility of action, distinction, and order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-06","CL_GGVC_22:GA-08","CL_GGVC_23:GA-12","CL_GGVC_24:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every created nature is good and participated actuality; neither substantial evil nor matter wholly without form can constitute the first term of creation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","CL_GGVC_23:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Divine causality grants natures forms, powers, motions, and ends, and therefore perfects rather than evacuates created operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-06","CL_GGVC_24:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] The good of the part, the grades of natures, and the distribution of regions are measured by the order of the whole, not by the benefit or perfection of the separate part.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-03","CL_GGVC_22:GA-10","CL_GGVC_23:GA-12","CL_GGVC_24:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Receptivity passes from intellectual nature through grace and glory to corporeal actuality capable of further formation and ornament, without confusing the identity of the orders.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-02","CL_GGVC_24:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Defect presupposes a good actuality lacking rectitude, manifestation, beauty, or further determination; privation never becomes a parallel positive principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-11","CL_GGVC_24:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Created power of acting operates as received within nature, object, and motion: it can consent to order, be perfected by supernatural elevation, or deform the good through appropriation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The distinction of grades of causality, priority, temporality, and signification is the global operator by which apparent contradictions are resolved into ordered relations.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_23:GA-14","CL_GGVC_24:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Created time is stratified: the beginning of being, succession of acts, corporeal motion, narrative order, administration, and terminal closure constitute diverse measures.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-08","CL_GGVC_22:GA-11","CL_GGVC_23:GA-13","CL_GGVC_24:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Divine goodness orders the whole as cause of being, rule of act, end of appetite, and limit of interpretation, without creaturely comprehension or equality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-11","CL_GGVC_22:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] The ultimate order can confirm a spiritual state and excludes a principle of evil, while restricting conflict between revealed truth and demonstrative reason to corrigible expositions.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-09","CL_GGVC_22:GA-12","CL_GGVC_23:GA-15","CL_GGVC_24:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Cognition, appearance, and word do not of themselves confer rectitude of will or exhaustively manifest the ontology of a thing.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-13","CL_GGVC_23:GA-07","CL_GGVC_24:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Participation non-rivalrously increases created causality according to the mode of the recipient, but asymmetrically refers every positive efficacy to the first cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-07","CL_GGVC_24:GA-04","CL_GGVC_21:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-01","text":"Participation retains a stable identity, but extends from angelic being to the goodness of every nature, formed actuality, and the ecology of causes and regions.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-01","CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","CL_GGVC_23:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_24:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-02","text":"The relation of part to whole migrates from the order of love into providence, cosmic unity, and regional classification; totality becomes the measure of affection, causality, and disposition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_22:GA-10","CL_GGVC_23:GA-12","CL_GGVC_24:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-03","text":"Privation changes from a defect of voluntary rectitude into a lack of manifestation, beauty, or further determination, yet always presupposes positive actuality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-03","CL_GGVC_24:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-04","text":"The power of acting returns as choice, suggestion, instrumental causality, and classificatory movement; its function changes, but its received and limited mode persists.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_22:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_23:GA-10","CL_GGVC_24:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-05","text":"Production migrates from the creation of substance to formation, the institution of virtue, the actuality of species, and propagation; thus the same term is divided among several causal orders.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-02","CL_GGVC_24:GA-10","CL_GGVC_24:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-06","text":"Time passes from angelic beginning and succession to ministry, motion, formation, narration, and generation; its unity is no longer homogeneity but coordination of measures.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_22:GA-11","CL_GGVC_23:GA-13","CL_GGVC_24:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RMD-07","text":"Distinction, initially a local mechanism for particular questions, changes into a methodological attractor ordering the whole of ontology, causality, and exegesis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_23:GA-14","CL_GGVC_23:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_24:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-01","text":"The specific differences of angelic love, initial grace, demonic sins, and sensible punishment remain local; only the law of receptive capacity and fixation of act ascends.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_21:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_22:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_22:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-02","text":"Celestial matter, the Empyrean heaven, the firmament, the waters, and the distribution of birds remain probable cosmological determinations; they support the present structure only as cases of a plurality of orders.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_23:CGD-03","CL_GGVC_24:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_24:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-03","text":"The distinction of essence and being, natural goodness, and the formation of things are amplified into a general law according to which received actuality sustains further ordering without self-sufficiency.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_22:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_23:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_24:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-04","text":"Spiritual freedom and natural causality are joined as forms of secondary operation that produce real effects without creation ex nihilo or abolition of the first cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-06","CL_GGVC_22:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_24:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-05","text":"The series of nature, choice, grace, and glory, and the series of actuality, distinction, and ornament are not identified, but exhibit the same form of receptivity successively perfected.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:CGD-06","CL_GGVC_24:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:CGD-06","text":"Models of the evil body, cosmogenesis from fault, simple chronology, and a single cosmological criterion become obsolete, because they confuse the orders of morality, origin, formation, and narration.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_22:GNX-06","CL_GGVC_23:CGD-06","CL_GGVC_24:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-01","text":"No creature, zone, or neutral matter outside divine order is admitted, nor can the cosmos be constituted from a self-sufficient principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_22:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_23:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_24:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-02","text":"Evil, darkness, and absolute formlessness are not substances or positive co-principles, but privations of already good actuality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_23:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-03","text":"Divine causality and secondary operations are not rival forces; the creature can neither create ex nihilo nor receive independent efficacy from the first institution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_24:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_24:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-04","text":"Time is not an absolute receptacle, nor is the order of days, acts, and generations compressed into one homogeneous succession.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GNX-07","CL_GGVC_24:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_24:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-05","text":"A univocal name, sensible appearance, and a single literal reading do not suffice to determine exhaustively the nature, causality, or referent of a thing.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_24:GA-07","CL_GGVC_24:GA-08","CL_GGVC_24:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:GNX-06","text":"Freedom is not absolute indifference, and the perfect terminus is not inertia; confirmation of the end preserves operation while excluding further reversal or merit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_21:GNX-06","CL_GGVC_22:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that causality of being and causality of defect proceed symmetrically; otherwise evil would become a positive principle against the first cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_22:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that unity consists in homogeneity of matter, time, or order; otherwise distinction of parts and plurality of priorities would signify dissolution rather than perfection.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:DBN-04","CL_GGVC_24:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that created act is either autonomous or merely passive, because dependence on the first cause constitutes its very proper operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_24:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that cognition, words, or sensible things convey immediate rectitude and full ontology; affective, semantic, and causal mediation necessarily remains.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_23:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_24:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that perfection either destroys the subject or indefinitely reopens the end; preservation of nature and irreversible closure are simultaneously required.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_22:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-01","text":"The highest absence is a neutral exterior in which a creature could subsist before or outside divine participation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_22:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_23:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-02","text":"The possibility most deeply excluded is another sovereignty that establishes being, end, causality, or order against the first cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_22:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_24:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-03","text":"Equality of the creature with its source, whether through autonomy, appropriation of the end, or comprehension, remains ontologically unavailable.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_22:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSA-04","text":"The system revolves around the impossibility of a totally self-sufficient explanation: neither a single cosmology nor the surface of the letter can exhaustively close the origin, structure, and causality of the world.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_24:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-01","text":"Moral defect and cosmological formlessness share the same apophatic structure: each presupposes good actuality and signifies a lack of further order, not an adverse nature.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-03","CL_GGVC_24:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-02","text":"The primacy of divine causality and creaturely efficacy are coordinated directly, not inversely: the more fully a nature receives form and motion, the more properly it operates according to its mode.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-12","CL_GGVC_21:GA-06","CL_GGVC_24:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-03","text":"The order of the whole transfers the moral measure of the part into cosmology: proper good, inequality, and regional situation are fully understood only within relation to the universal disposition.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","CL_GGVC_21:GA-03","CL_GGVC_22:GA-10","CL_GGVC_24:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-04","text":"The heterogeneity of time prevents the prior and posterior of angelic acts, corporeal motions, and the narrative of Genesis from being transferred into one chronology.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-08","CL_GGVC_21:GA-08","CL_GGVC_23:GA-04","CL_GGVC_24:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-05","text":"Knowledge of truth, the signification of a word, and sensible appearance are real but insufficient media; each must be supplemented by affective rectitude, context, and causal examination.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-11","CL_GGVC_22:GA-13","CL_GGVC_23:GA-07","CL_GGVC_24:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-06","text":"The system combines ontological closure with epistemic openness: spiritual ends can be irreversible, while cosmological models remain under finite plurality.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-10","CL_GGVC_21:GA-10","CL_GGVC_22:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_23:AS-B08","CL_GGVC_24:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:XD-07","text":"Creation of substance, further formation, and propagative administration are distinct positive orders; the unity of the first principle does not require one mode of production for all effects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-07","CL_GGVC_23:GA-02","CL_GGVC_24:GA-10","CL_GGVC_22:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-01","text":"Created differentiation is a mode of positive perfection, not a fall from original unity; evil only parasitically deforms the order of differences.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_23:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-02","text":"The perfection of agency does not consist in multiplying alternatives, but in the operation of received powers according to the end; autonomous appropriation does not increase but disorders this operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-12","CL_GGVC_21:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_22:GA-14","CL_GGVC_24:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-03","text":"Created history is a nexus of unequal trajectories: contingent beginnings, irreversible decisions, continuable motions, temporal administrations, and narrative orders produce diverse futures.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-10","CL_GGVC_21:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_22:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_23:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_24:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-04","text":"Stability of truth requires mobility of lower models: the ontological core remains, while physical accounts and semantic referents are corrected under testing.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-11","CL_GGVC_23:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_23:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_24:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-05","text":"Created power is ministerial or instrumental; where it claims primacy for itself, it becomes political and parasitic rather than ontologically productive.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-12","CL_GGVC_22:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_24:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:BSD-06","text":"The form of argumentation performs ontology itself: distinctions preserve differences, distribute them into order, and effect unity without univocal identity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_06:GA-07","CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-01","text":"Received being constitutes the power of acting; ordered act further manifests participated good, whence ontological dependence recurs into operation and diffusion of order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_21:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_24:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-02","text":"Received good admits autonomous appropriation; appropriation produces defect, defect privation and resistance, which providence contains without abolishing perverse intention.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-14","CL_GGVC_22:XD-06","CL_GGVC_22:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-03","text":"Formed actuality permits relative formlessness; this requires distinction, distinction constitutes regions, regions require ornaments, and ornaments manifest the order of regions.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_24:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-04","text":"Equivocation or incongruity generates distinction; distinction multiplies interpretative branches, reason cuts away impossibilities, and residual plurality provokes new semantic-causal examination.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_23:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_24:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-05","text":"Created efficacy arouses the risk of autonomy; reduction to the first cause denies autonomy, but restores instrumental causality, whence subordination itself stabilizes secondary operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_24:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:RLE-06","text":"The common attractor is ordered distinction under divine goodness: cause, act, privation, region, time, and signification are separated so that they may return into one order without confusion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_22:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_23:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FGS-B01","text":"First cause → created and participated actuality → exclusion of substantial evil and subsistent pure potency.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-01","CL_GGVC_22:GA-01","CL_GGVC_23:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FGS-B02","text":"Created actuality → distribution of natures, forms, and regions → unity of the order of differences.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:GA-06","CL_GGVC_24:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FGS-B03","text":"Receptive nature + received form, virtue, and motion → proper operation of the secondary cause without rivalry with the first cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-02","CL_GGVC_24:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FGS-B04","text":"Created power + good object + mode of appropriation → ordered act or voluntary defect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FGS-B05","text":"Ordered act → elevation, further formation, or ornament → perfection according to capacity and the order of the whole.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:GA-12","CL_GGVC_24:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FGS-B06","text":"Defective act → privation of rectitude without destruction of nature → punishment, frustration, and providential containment.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_22:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FGS-B07","text":"Heterogeneous times + recursive distinction → terminal closure of states and conditional closure of expositions → coherence without a single chronology or cosmology.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:GA-11","CL_GGVC_23:GA-14","CL_GGVC_24:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AFM-B01","text":"Participation of being → ordered nature → power of acting → consent or appropriation → elevation or privation → beatific or penal confirmation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_22:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AFM-B02","text":"Formed actuality → relative formlessness → distinction of forms and regions → motion and secondary causalities → ornament and generation → perfection of the universe.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_24:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:AFM-B03","text":"Apparent contradiction → distinction of senses, causes, and priorities → removal of impossibilities → coordination of valid orders → finite plurality under qualified closure.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_23:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_24:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-01","text":"The opposition of good spirit and evil body collapses: spirit can fail morally, while body and matter retain ontological goodness.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:MME-01","CL_GGVC_21:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-02","text":"Creation is reclassified from an enumeration of events into a matrix of actuality, formation, distinction, ornament, administration, and end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:MME-01","CL_GGVC_24:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-03","text":"Freedom and causality cease to signify independence; they become modes of received operation whose perfection is assessed by right ordering, not indetermination.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_22:MME-02","CL_GGVC_22:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-04","text":"Time changes from a single line into a map of beginnings, intellectual successions, corporeal motions, administrations, and closures.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:MME-04","CL_GGVC_22:MME-05","CL_GGVC_24:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-05","text":"Negative terms become ontological instruments: evil, darkness, and formlessness do not name beings, but defects of relation or determination.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:MME-05","CL_GGVC_22:GA-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MME-06","text":"Interpretation is reclassified as constrained selection among models, in which authority, ontology, and natural reason jointly determine the limits of possibility.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:MME-04","CL_GGVC_24:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level presents the origin and state of angels, the fault and punishment of demons, formlessness, days, regions, luminaries, plants, and animals as distinct questions.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level composes these questions through participation, privation, the order of parts, stratified causality, formation, and heterogeneous temporalities.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level transfers the contest from opposition of things to discrimination of orders, such that the correct intervention changes the kind of cause, time, or signification.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level establishes reality as good but dependent actuality, whose history contains multiple measures and irreversible closures.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological and performative level effects the order it asserts through the iteration of distinctions itself: it preserves, subordinates, and contains lower differences under graduated closure.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_22:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_23:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_24:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-01","text":"The universality of divine causality and the imputation of creaturely defect cohere only through the distinction of production of positive act from deficient mode; the joint remains axiomatically necessary but not mechanically exhausted.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:FL-01","CL_GGVC_22:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-02","text":"The congruence of nature with glory and the utility of the part within the whole tend toward teleological necessity, which the gratuity of the gift and the irreducibility of harm simultaneously restrict.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:FL-04","CL_GGVC_22:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-03","text":"The definitiveness of spiritual choice is composed with the permanence of freedom and operation, but the process by which mutable adhesion becomes irreversible is not fully determined.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:MP-01","CL_GGVC_22:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-04","text":"The invariability of revealed truth and the mobility of cosmological referents cohere while semantic flexibility remains finite; the criterion of this limit is not always univocal.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:FL-01","CL_GGVC_23:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:FL-05","text":"The unity of first institution contains actual, causal, and virtual production, but the historical relations among these modes and between spiritual movers and natural motions remain fragile.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:FL-01","CL_GGVC_24:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MP-01","text":"Freedom changes from a power of opposites into indefectible or obstinate operation, because its center is transferred from a plurality of options to the mode of adhesion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:MP-02","CL_GGVC_22:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MP-02","text":"Formlessness changes from the impossible lack of every form into a relative defect of manifestation, beauty, distinction, or ornament.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:MP-01","CL_GGVC_24:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MP-03","text":"Production changes from the constitution of substance into formation, the institution of virtue, the actuality of species, and natural propagation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_24:MP-02","CL_GGVC_23:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MP-04","text":"Priority and day change from indices of chronology into coordinates of origin, nature, formation, narration, and ornament.","refs":["CL_GGVC_23:MP-03","CL_GGVC_24:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a machine of stratified participation, in which the first cause distributes actuality through natures, forms, regions, and instruments without identity of absorption.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_22:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_23:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_24:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a receptive and operative node: the angel chooses within the given order, while the interpreter navigates senses within the limits of authority, ontology, and reason.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_22:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_23:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_24:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-03","text":"Time is a nexus of nested arcs: it joins beginning, decision, motion, administration, generation, and end without one measure common to all.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_22:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_23:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_24:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is the art of correct attribution: distinguishing power from source, defect from nature, appearance from ontology, and letter from exposition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_22:HSI-05","CL_GGVC_23:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_24:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of participated actuality, non-rivalrous causality, privation, stratified orders, and mediated interpretation suffice to reconstruct the machine of the present meta-aggregate.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is where actual good, distinction of creation from formation, limitation of secondary causality, and the order of parts converge in one participatory ontology.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B03","text":"The mechanism of angelic irrevocability, the nexus of nature and glory, the cosmologies of light and waters, and the actual or virtual mode of producing living things are not yet sufficiently determined.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B04","text":"Coherence rests not on linear order, but on the return of distinctions and mutation of functions; reconstruction preserves the true movement because it does not flatten lower differences into identity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_06:MRT-B05","text":"Probabilistic propositions, mobile cosmological referents, and transitions between actuality, institution, and propagation resist axiomatic reduction; this indetermination marks the limits of the machine, not a defect of the center.","refs":["CL_GGVC_21:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_22:MRT-B06","CL_GGVC_23:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_24:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_06@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_GGVC_L1_06:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Ggvc L1 06","context":"Chapters 61–72 · Prima pars · Quaestio 60 · Prima pars · Quaestio 61 · Prima pars · Quaestio 62 · Prima pars · Quaestio 63 · Prima pars · Quaestio 64 · Prima pars · Quaestio 65 · Prima pars · Quaestio 66 · Prima pars · Quaestio 67 · Prima pars · Quaestio 68 · Prima pars · Quaestio 69 · Prima pars · Quaestio 70 · Prima pars · Quaestio 71"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07","layer":"MC","title":"Ggvc L1 07","order":7,"parents":["CL_GGVC_25","CL_GGVC_26","CL_GGVC_27","CL_GGVC_28"],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_07@R001","sha256":"5c43ab0a1df0001804dbc87f998954a91fd0b74df8f59a3af9d060dc7ae78efd","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo ordo creatus iam completus causaliter operari pergat atque causam rationalem dependentem, incorporatam et tamen voluntariam contineat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-06","CL_GGVC_28:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-02","text":"Thesis centralis est unitatem formalem non homogenitatem, sed pluralitatem partium, potentiarum et actuum secundum modos proprios ordinatam constituere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-02","CL_GGVC_26:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-03","text":"Argumentatio ab effectibus et operationibus ad principia formalia regreditur, deinde per obiecta formalia, subiecta et modos causalitatis architecturam latentem restituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_27:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-04","text":"Motus a constitutione mundi ad unitatem compositi, ab hac ad distributionem potentiarum, et inde ad appetitum, electionem atque causalitatem liberam ascendit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_28:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-05","text":"Progressus est cumulativus, recursivus et spiralis: gradus superior ex inferiori emergit, eum non abolet, sed ad illum redit ut universalitas executionem singularem acquirat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:AS-06","CL_GGVC_27:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-06","text":"Contradictiones apparentes solvuntur non suppressione alterius termini, sed divisione respectuum secundum perfectionem, operationem, obiectum, finem et causam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_28:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-07","text":"Quies et beatitudo unum attractorem terminalem efficiunt: finis stabilis progressionem claudit quoad constitutionem, sed propagationem, actionem et pluralitatem mediorum aperit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-11","CL_GGVC_28:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-08","text":"Clausura est stratificata: institutio terminatur, causae creatae operantur, anima separata incomplete subsistit, et libertas intra causalitatem primam potius quam extra eam consistit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:AS-B07","CL_GGVC_26:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_28:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Modus operationis manifestat modum essendi, quamvis principium substantiale tantum indirecte per effectus et actus cognoscatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-01","CL_GGVC_26:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Unitas realis pluralitatem ordinatam requirit: totum per distinctionem partium, anima per potentias diversas, actus humanus per causas coordinatas constituitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-02","CL_GGVC_26:GA-11","CL_GGVC_28:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Principium superius inferius non substituit, sed ei modum proprium essendi, recipiendi vel agendi confert.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-13","CL_GGVC_28:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Perfectio non est immobilitas, sed integritas causaliter fecunda quae novam constitutionem excludit et explicationem principiorum iam datorum permittit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-06","CL_GGVC_25:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Substantia, forma, potentia et operatio gradus irreducibiles constituunt, quorum distinctio unitatem causalem servat sine confusione actus primi et secundi.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-01","CL_GGVC_26:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Potentiae et actus distinguuntur secundum operationem propriam et rationem formalem obiecti, non secundum vocabula, organa aut identitatem materialem rei.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-07","CL_GGVC_27:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Corpus transit ab elemento mundi ordinato ad materiam ab anima constitutam, deinde ad condicionem intrinsecam cognitionis, appetitus et executionis humanae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-10","CL_GGVC_26:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Universalitas intellectiva efficaciam singularem tantum per mediationes sensitivas, appetitivas et corporales acquirit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GNX-07","CL_GGVC_27:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Beatitudo et quies sunt finis non eligibilis qui inclinationem universalem stabilit atque campum contingentium mediorum generat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-06","CL_GGVC_28:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Dependentia a causa superiori cum causalitate propria causae inferioris compatitur, dummodo modus proprius inferioris non destruatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-13","CL_GGVC_28:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Ordo creatus est reticulum causale nested in quo formatio, potentiae, receptio, appetitus et motus diversas transductiones eiusdem actualitatis efficiunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_27:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Actus humanus liber constituitur iudicio contingentium et acceptatione appetitiva, servata distinctione electionis internae ab executione atque causalitate prima.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-08","CL_GGVC_28:GA-12","CL_GGVC_28:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-01","text":"Perfectio a gradu vitae ad integritatem mundi, ab integritate ad operationem potentiarum, et ab operatione ad beatitudinem terminalem mutatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-03","CL_GGVC_25:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-02","text":"Unitas a compositione partium mundi ad unam formam multas potentias ordinantem, deinde ad coordinationem plurium causarum actus humani amplificatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_26:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_28:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-03","text":"Mediatio constitutive excluditur inter animam et materiam, sed operative restituitur inter potentias, obiecta, phantasmata, iudicium, appetitum et executionem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_27:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-04","text":"Passivitas ab alteratione materiali ad receptionem intentionalem, perfectionem intellectivam et mobilitatem appetitus transit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-08","CL_GGVC_27:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-05","text":"Tempus ab ordine dierum ad successionem receptionis, memoriae, appetitus et actionis transfertur, dum aeternitas divina extra hanc successionem manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:MME-01","CL_GGVC_27:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_28:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-06","text":"Quies vim invertit: cessatio constitutionis fit attractor positivus qui propagationem mundi et navigationem mediorum ad finem ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_28:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-07","text":"Causalitas divina a productione et administratione mundi ad participationem luminis atque motionem voluntatis amplificatur, semper causalitatem creatam conservans.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_28:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-08","text":"Taxonomiae zoologicae, anatomicae et lexicales opere locali peracto evanescunt; permanent criteria functionis, operationis, obiecti et finis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:RMD-09","CL_GGVC_27:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-01","text":"Differentiae zoologicae et numerus dierum manent locales; sursum ascendit principium quo partes distinguuntur, ornantur et in integritatem totius includuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_25:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-02","text":"Quaestiones de materia spirituali, complexione corporis et statu animae sunt locales; globaliter valent regula operationis et unitas non homogenea compositi.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_26:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-03","text":"Numeri sensuum et potentiarum manent instrumenta inferioris ordinis; criterium obiecti formalis atque sufficientiae taxonomicae unitates coniungit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_27:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-04","text":"Sensualitas, irascibilis et triplex necessitas sunt machinae locales; ad praesentem gradum ascendit compositio inclinationis, iudicii et libertatis dependentis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_28:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-05","text":"Chronologiae, nomina facultatum et vocabula passionum recodificantur ut indices respectuum causalium, actuum, habitus et finium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_27:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_28:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-06","text":"Transitus maior fit cum causalitas instituta mundi in causalitatem intrinsecam animae, deinde in causalitatem voluntariam causae secundae contrahitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-01","CL_GGVC_28:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat ordinem creatum esse inventarium chronologicum rerum aequalium, quia relatio partis, perfectionis et causae prius quam successio nuda intelligenda est.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-02","text":"Unitas substantiae neque homogenitatem operationum neque facultatem unicam neque suppressionem pluralitatis requirit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_27:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-03","text":"Gradus superior inferiorem non abolet, quia corpus, sensus, phantasmata, appetitus inferior et causa secunda proprias functiones servant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_27:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-04","text":"Universale neque subiectum transpersonale postulat neque immediate in executionem corporalem transit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_27:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-05","text":"Necessitas, inclinatio et dependentia non sunt per se coactio; libertatem destruit tantum motus qui inclinationem rationalem violat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_28:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-06","text":"Subsistentia, perfectio et completio non significant conservationem integram omnium operationum neque subiectum completum extra compositionem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_26:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet causalitatem superiorem et inferiorem esse quantitates rivales, quarum incrementum alterius actionem necessario minuat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet novitatem vel libertatem creatam interruptionem ordinis causalis aut aseitatem subiecti requirere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_28:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet distinctiones reales ex sola materia obiecti, anatomia organi aut pluralitate nominum deduci posse.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_27:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet hominem, corpus vel animam esse subiectum completum ante relationes formales et operationales quibus constituitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_28:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet finem ultimum ab agente creari, eligi aut exhauste possideri posse, quia eius prioritas campum electionis instituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_28:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas creaturae quae sit purus actus, sua operatio et causa prima sui luminis atque finis ontologice inadmissibilis est.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_27:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-02","text":"Systema circum absentiam coincidentiae plenae inter divinum et creatum, universale et singulare, aeternum et temporale ordinatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_27:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-03","text":"Immediatus transitus ab intelligibili universali vel fine ultimo ad effectum singularem impossibilis est; mediatio constitutiva actionis manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_28:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-04","text":"Dominium absolutum passionum, eventuum et causalitatis subiecto creato indisponibile est; proprietas actus servatur sine dominio totius ordinis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_28:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-01","text":"Mundus completus causaliter operatur quia formae et potentiae ex principiis institutis naturaliter resultant; actio posterior est explicatio ordinis, non iterata creatio.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:XD-02","CL_GGVC_26:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-02","text":"Relatio partium ad universum recursu anthropologico contrahitur: una anima integritatem hominis constituit eo ipso quod potentias heterogeneas ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_26:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-03","text":"Coniunctio regulae operationis cum ratione formali obiecti efficit metaphysicam indirectam, quae principia occulta ex actibus distinctis reconstructive determinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-01","CL_GGVC_27:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-04","text":"Libertas humana necessario incorporata est: intellectus et voluntas organa formaliter excedunt, sed phantasmatibus, passionibus et mediatione exsecutiva ad actum singularem indigent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_27:XD-08","CL_GGVC_28:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-05","text":"Quies cosmica et beatitudo voluntatis eandem structuram terminalem exhibent: finis immobilis constitutionem claudit, sed fecunditatem causarum et pluralitatem itinerum ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_28:RLE-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-06","text":"Motio divina voluntatis est analogon administrationis post creationem: causa superior non novam substantiam substituit, sed causam creatam secundum modum proprium agere facit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-05","CL_GGVC_28:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-07","text":"Distinctio respectuum est machina performativa communis exegesi, ontologiae animae, taxonomiae potentiarum et doctrinae libertatis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-08","text":"Mors demonstrat libertatem humanam ad totam architecturam incorporatam pertinere: principium subsistens manet, sed subiectum organicum et cursus integrus actionis franguntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-14","CL_GGVC_26:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-01","text":"Realitas est ordo causarum nested, in quo actus superior principia inferiora constituit atque operationes proprias eorum perficit sine substitutione.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_26:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_28:MME-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum humanum est unitas distributa et participata: personaliter agit, universalitatem recipit, corpore exsequitur et causaliter a principio superiori dependet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_27:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_28:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-03","text":"Tempus creatum est series actualizationum: institutio causaliter praecedit, cognitio successive recipit, appetitus futurum anticipat, et actio per portas mediatas progreditur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_27:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_28:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-04","text":"Cognitio humana est indirecta sed generativa: accessum immediatum ad essentias non habet, tamen ex operationibus, differentiis formalibus et circuitibus causalibus ontologiam restituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_26:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_27:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-05","text":"Responsabilitas moralis in iudicio et acceptatione interna consistit, non in dominio absoluto passionum aut eventuum exteriorum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_28:MME-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-06","text":"Finalitas stabilis est condicio contingentiae ordinatae: necessitas beatitudinis non claudit libertatem, sed pluralitatem mediorum comparabilium aperit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-06","CL_GGVC_28:XD-03","CL_GGVC_28:RLE-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-01","text":"Actio divina substantiam instituit → principia immanentia operationes producunt → operationes formas manifestant → intellectus ordinem reconstructive cognoscit → agens creatum intra eundem ordinem operatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:FGS-B01","CL_GGVC_25:XD-02","CL_GGVC_26:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-02","text":"Partes mundi → integritas universi → una forma hominis → potentiae plurales → actus coordinati constituunt recursionem eiusdem unitatis non homogeneae per magnitudines diversas.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_26:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-03","text":"Receptio sensibilis → phantasma → abstractio → universale → bonum apprehensum → electio → motus corporis efficit spiralem qua cognitio a mundo ascendit et actio ad mundum redit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:XD-02","CL_GGVC_27:XD-06","CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-04","text":"Incongruentia apparens → divisio respectuum → reclassificatio termini → restitutio ordinis → nova tensio in gradu superiore constituit circuitum methodologicum permanentem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-05","text":"Natura instituta → gratia → gloria → quies et bonum universale → consilium mediorum → beatitudo componunt attractorem teleologicum totius structurae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-11","CL_GGVC_28:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-06","text":"Forma super materiam, intellectus super sensum, voluntas super appetitum inferiorem et causa prima super causam secundam agunt eo modo quo superius causalitatem inferioris servat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-13","CL_GGVC_28:MME-05","CL_GGVC_28:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-07","text":"Mors est circuitus negativus probativus: destructio subiecti organici detegit quae operationes ex toto composito et quae ex principio subsistente dependeant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_26:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FGS-B01","text":"Actio prima → substantia et forma → distinctio partium → integritas causaliter aperta.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-01","CL_GGVC_25:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FGS-B02","text":"Forma una → esse substantiale unum → resultatio potentiarum plurium → operationes secundum subiecta distinctae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-04","CL_GGVC_26:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FGS-B03","text":"Operatio irreducibilis → principium proximum → ratio formalis obiecti → distinctio potentiae → restitutio architecturae ontologicae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-01","CL_GGVC_27:GA-01","CL_GGVC_27:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FGS-B04","text":"Corpus dispositum → receptio sensibilis → phantasma → abstractio → species intelligibilis → universalitas personaliter recepta.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:XD-02","CL_GGVC_27:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FGS-B05","text":"Bonum universale → finis necessarius → collatio contingentium → consilium → acceptatio appetitiva → electio → executio impedibilis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-08","CL_GGVC_28:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FGS-B06","text":"Causa prima → conservatio modi causae secundae → actus voluntarius proprius → ordinatio ad finem → quies sine abolitione causalitatis creatae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-13","CL_GGVC_25:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AFM-B01","text":"Institutio substantiae → distinctio partium → ornatus → completio → cessatio novae constitutionis → fecunditas causarum immanentium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:AS-B03","CL_GGVC_25:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AFM-B02","text":"Effectus vitae → anima subsistens et forma corporis → unum esse → potentiae plurales → transductio incorporata.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_27:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AFM-B03","text":"Receptio formarum → abstractio universalis → bonum apprehensum → appetitus → particularizatio → actio corporalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-06","CL_GGVC_27:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AFM-B04","text":"Beatitudo necessaria → media contingentia → iudicium → electio → executio → causalitas prima conservans → quies terminalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-06","CL_GGVC_28:XD-03","CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AFM-B05","text":"Contradictio apparens → distinctio respectuum → reclassificatio ontologica → compositio causarum → tensio residualis circumscripta.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-01","text":"Completio reclassificatur ex immobilitate in integritatem productivam quae futuros status ex causis iam institutis explicat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_25:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-02","text":"Anima et mens ex rebus iuxtapositivis aut fasciculo facultatum in architecturam formalem, incorporatam et transductivam convertuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:MME-01","CL_GGVC_27:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-03","text":"Oppositio universalis et singularis in duplicem motum reordinatur: ascensus abstractionis et descensus mediationis exsecutivae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:MME-04","CL_GGVC_27:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-04","text":"Necessitas et libertas desinunt esse contraria simplicia; coactio excluditur, sed inclinatio naturalis, finis et causalitas superior libertatem ordinant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:MME-01","CL_GGVC_28:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-05","text":"Exegesis, ontologia, psychologia et doctrina actionis unum modum lectionis requirunt: vocabula ad actus, actus ad principia, principia ad respectus causales referuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-06","text":"Homo neque mensura valoris universi neque pars indifferens est, sed locus singularis ubi ordo creatus cognitionem universalem et causalitatem voluntariam exercet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-12","CL_GGVC_25:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei agitur de completione mundi, natura animae, divisione potentiarum, appetitu, voluntate et libero arbitrio.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali agunt forma substantialis, integritas partium, potentiae, obiecta formalia, mediationes et causalitates prima atque secunda.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico quaestiones chronologicae, anatomicae et libertariae in quaestiones de respectu, attributione actus et coordinatione causarum transferuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est actualizatio ordinata potentiarum, subiectum autem compositum finitum quod successive cognoscit, appetit et agit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo systema distinctionibus suis ipsam unitatem non homogeneam efficit quam ontologice affirmat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-01","text":"Institutio dicitur complete causaliter aperta, sed amplitudo novitatis posterioris inter explicationem principiorum et additionem status nondum exacte determinatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-06","CL_GGVC_25:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-02","text":"Unum esse animae et compositi cum subsistentia animae post mortem componitur, sed identitas persistentis principii et identitas hominis completi non plene conciliantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:FL-01","CL_GGVC_26:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-03","text":"Intellectus formaliter organo independens phantasmatibus operationaliter dependet; commissura inter subsistentiam non organicam et cognitionem incorporatam manet fragilis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:FL-02","CL_GGVC_26:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-04","text":"Causa prima modum voluntarium efficere dicitur, sed modus quo determinatio superior electionem secundam non absorbeat ulteriore explicatione indiget.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:FL-04","CL_GGVC_28:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-05","text":"Beatitudo necessario appetita pluralitatem mediorum aperit, sed transitus ad electionem mali sub ratione boni apparentis et ad Deum nondum visum incomplete explicatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:FL-03","CL_GGVC_28:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-01","text":"Perfectio mutatur ex gradu vitae in integritatem totius, exinde in operationem finis et tandem in beatitudinem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:MP-01","CL_GGVC_25:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-02","text":"Mediatio mutatur ex impedimento unionis substantialis in condicionem necessariam operationis, cognitionis, electionis et executionis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_27:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-03","text":"Passivitas mutatur ex alteratione materiali in receptionem perfectivam atque postremo in mobilitatem appetitus coram bono.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:MP-01","CL_GGVC_27:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-04","text":"Necessitas mutatur ex apparente negatione voluntarii in structuram duplicem, qua coactio libertatem tollit, natura autem et finis eam ordinant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:MP-02","CL_GGVC_28:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina architectonica nested: causa prima instituit, forma constituit, potentiae transducunt, appetitus ordinat et causa secunda exsequitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_26:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_27:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum humanum est nodus distributus et politia interna, in qua una forma, organa, facultates, ratio et voluntas proprios actus retinent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_28:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est spiralis actualizationis: principia instituta futuros actus continent, memoria praeteritum retinet, appetitus futurum anticipat et finis seriem claudit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_27:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_28:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis est ascensus ab effectu ad principium et a singulari ad universale, deinde descensus ab universali per electionem ad effectum singularem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_27:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de unitate non homogenea, causalitate stratificata, obiecto formali, mediatione incorporata, fine attractivo et libertate dependenti sufficiunt ad machinam huius metagregis restituendam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus inter formam constituentem, potentias ordinatas et causalitatem superiorem quae actum proprium inferioris conservat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent novitas intra mundum completum, individuatio animae separatae, dependentia intellectus a phantasmatibus et compositio motionis divinae cum electione.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_26:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat, quia a constitutione ordinis per interiorizationem formalem et transductionem psychicam ad causalitatem voluntariam procedit; plena reductio tamen commissuris inter gradus resistit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T173936624733Z_73a16239","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Ggvc L1 07","source_sha256":"5c43ab0a1df0001804dbc87f998954a91fd0b74df8f59a3af9d060dc7ae78efd","sha256":"4bd878eb4a54d7e2c05692a5f593f3b312540925c2c6c67ccaa02640918262a3","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:39:36.627100+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-01","text":"The system asks how the already completed created order continues to operate causally and contains a rational cause that is dependent, embodied, and yet voluntary.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-06","CL_GGVC_28:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-02","text":"The central thesis is that formal unity constitutes not homogeneity, but a plurality of parts, powers, and acts ordered according to their proper modes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-02","CL_GGVC_26:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-03","text":"The argument regresses from effects and operations to formal principles, then reconstructs the hidden architecture through formal objects, subjects, and modes of causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_27:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-04","text":"The movement ascends from the constitution of the world to the unity of the composite, from this to the distribution of powers, and thence to appetite, choice, and free causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_28:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-05","text":"The progression is cumulative, recursive, and spiral: the higher level emerges from the lower, does not abolish it, but returns to it so that universality may acquire singular execution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:AS-06","CL_GGVC_27:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-06","text":"Apparent contradictions are resolved not by suppressing one term, but by distinguishing respects according to perfection, operation, object, end, and cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_28:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-07","text":"Rest and beatitude constitute a single terminal attractor: the stable end closes progression with respect to constitution, but opens propagation, action, and a plurality of means.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-11","CL_GGVC_28:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AS-08","text":"Closure is stratified: institution terminates, created causes operate, the separated soul subsists incompletely, and freedom consists within rather than outside first causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:AS-B07","CL_GGVC_26:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_28:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] The mode of operation manifests the mode of being, although the substantial principle is known only indirectly through effects and acts.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-01","CL_GGVC_26:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Real unity requires ordered plurality: the whole is constituted through the distinction of parts, the soul through diverse powers, and the human act through coordinated causes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-02","CL_GGVC_26:GA-11","CL_GGVC_28:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] A higher principle does not replace a lower one, but confers upon it its proper mode of being, receiving, or acting.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-13","CL_GGVC_28:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Perfection is not immobility, but causally fruitful integrity that excludes new constitution and permits the unfolding of principles already given.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-06","CL_GGVC_25:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Substance, form, power, and operation constitute irreducible levels, whose distinction preserves causal unity without confusing first and second act.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-01","CL_GGVC_26:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Powers and acts are distinguished according to their proper operation and the formal account of the object, not according to words, organs, or the material identity of the thing.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-07","CL_GGVC_27:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] The body passes from an ordered element of the world to matter constituted by the soul, and then to an intrinsic condition of human cognition, appetite, and execution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-10","CL_GGVC_26:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Intellectual universality acquires singular efficacy only through sensitive, appetitive, and bodily mediations.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GNX-07","CL_GGVC_27:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Beatitude and rest are a non-choosable end that stabilizes universal inclination and generates the field of contingent means.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-06","CL_GGVC_28:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Dependence on a higher cause is compatible with the proper causality of a lower cause, provided that the proper mode of the lower is not destroyed.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-13","CL_GGVC_28:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] The created order is a nested causal network in which formation, powers, reception, appetite, and motion effect diverse transductions of the same actuality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_27:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] The free human act is constituted by judgment of contingents and appetitive acceptance, while preserving the distinction of internal choice from execution and first causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-08","CL_GGVC_28:GA-12","CL_GGVC_28:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-01","text":"Perfection shifts from the grade of life to the integrity of the world, from integrity to the operation of powers, and from operation to terminal beatitude.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-03","CL_GGVC_25:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-02","text":"Unity is expanded from the composition of the parts of the world to one form ordering many powers, and then to the coordination of multiple causes of human action.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_26:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_28:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-03","text":"Mediation is constitutively excluded between soul and matter, but is operatively restored among powers, objects, phantasms, judgment, appetite, and execution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_27:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-04","text":"Passivity passes from material alteration to intentional reception, intellectual perfection, and the mobility of appetite.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-08","CL_GGVC_27:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-05","text":"Time is transferred from the order of days to the succession of reception, memory, appetite, and action, while divine eternity remains outside this succession.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:MME-01","CL_GGVC_27:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_28:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-06","text":"Rest reverses its force: the cessation of constitution becomes a positive attractor that orders the propagation of the world and the navigation of means toward the end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_28:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-07","text":"Divine causality is expanded from the production and governance of the world to participation in light and the motion of the will, always preserving created causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_28:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RMD-08","text":"Zoological, anatomical, and lexical taxonomies vanish once their local work is completed; the criteria of function, operation, object, and end remain.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:RMD-09","CL_GGVC_27:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-01","text":"Zoological differences and the number of days remain local; the principle by which parts are distinguished, adorned, and included in the integrity of the whole ascends upward.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_25:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-02","text":"Questions concerning spiritual matter, bodily complexion, and the state of the soul are local; globally, the rule of operation and the non-homogeneous unity of the composite prevail.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_26:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-03","text":"The numbers of senses and powers remain instruments of a lower order; the criterion of the formal object and of taxonomic sufficiency joins the unities.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_27:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-04","text":"Sensuality, the irascible power, and threefold necessity are local mechanisms; to the present level ascends the composition of inclination, judgment, and dependent freedom.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_28:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-05","text":"Chronologies, names of faculties, and terms for passions are recodified as indices of causal relations, acts, dispositions, and ends.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_27:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_28:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:CGD-06","text":"The major transition occurs when the instituted causality of the world is contracted into the intrinsic causality of the soul, and then into the voluntary causality of a secondary cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-01","CL_GGVC_28:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-01","text":"The system denies that the created order is a chronological inventory of equal things, because the relation of part, perfection, and cause must be understood prior to bare succession.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-02","text":"The unity of substance requires neither homogeneity of operations nor a single faculty nor the suppression of plurality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_27:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-03","text":"The higher level does not abolish the lower, because body, senses, phantasms, lower appetite, and secondary cause retain their proper functions.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_27:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-04","text":"The universal neither requires a transpersonal subject nor passes immediately into bodily execution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_27:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-05","text":"Necessity, inclination, and dependence are not in themselves coercion; only motion that violates rational inclination destroys freedom.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_28:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:GNX-06","text":"Subsistence, perfection, and completion do not signify the integral preservation of all operations or a complete subject outside composition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_26:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that higher and lower causality are rival quantities, such that the increase of one necessarily diminishes the action of the other.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that created novelty or freedom requires an interruption of causal order or the aseity of the subject.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_28:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that real distinctions can be deduced from the matter of the object alone, the anatomy of an organ, or the plurality of names.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_27:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that a human being, body, or soul is a complete subject prior to the formal and operational relations by which it is constituted.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_28:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the ultimate end can be created, chosen, or exhaustively possessed by an agent, because its priority institutes the field of choice.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_28:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-01","text":"The possibility of a creature that is pure act, its own operation, and the first cause of its own light and end is ontologically inadmissible.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_27:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-02","text":"The system is ordered around the absence of full coincidence between the divine and the created, the universal and the singular, the eternal and the temporal.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_27:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-03","text":"An immediate transition from the intelligible universal or ultimate end to a singular effect is impossible; the constitutive mediation of action remains.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_28:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSA-04","text":"Absolute mastery of passions, events, and causality is unavailable to a created subject; propriety of act is preserved without mastery of the whole order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_28:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-01","text":"The completed world operates causally because forms and powers naturally result from instituted principles; subsequent action is the unfolding of order, not repeated creation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:XD-02","CL_GGVC_26:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-02","text":"The relation of parts to the universe is contracted through anthropological recursion: one soul constitutes the integrity of the human being precisely by ordering heterogeneous powers.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_26:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-03","text":"The conjunction of the rule of operation with the formal account of the object effects an indirect metaphysics, which reconstructively determines hidden principles from distinct acts.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-01","CL_GGVC_27:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-04","text":"Human freedom is necessarily embodied: intellect and will formally exceed organs, but require phantasms, passions, and executive mediation for singular act.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_27:XD-08","CL_GGVC_28:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-05","text":"Cosmic rest and the beatitude of the will exhibit the same terminal structure: an immobile end closes constitution, but orders the fruitfulness of causes and the plurality of paths.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_28:RLE-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-06","text":"The divine motion of the will is an analogue of governance after creation: the higher cause does not substitute a new substance, but causes the created cause to act according to its proper mode.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-05","CL_GGVC_28:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-07","text":"The distinction of respects is the common performative mechanism of exegesis, the ontology of the soul, the taxonomy of powers, and the doctrine of freedom.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:XD-08","text":"Death demonstrates that human freedom belongs to the entire embodied architecture: the subsisting principle remains, but the organic subject and the integral course of action are broken.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-14","CL_GGVC_26:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-01","text":"Reality is a nested order of causes, in which a higher act constitutes lower principles and perfects their proper operations without substitution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_26:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_28:MME-05","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-02","text":"The human subject is a distributed and participated unity: it acts personally, receives universality, executes bodily, and depends causally on a higher principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_27:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_28:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-03","text":"Created time is a series of actualizations: institution causally precedes, cognition receives successively, appetite anticipates the future, and action advances through mediated gates.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_27:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_28:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-04","text":"Human cognition is indirect yet generative: it does not have immediate access to essences, yet it reconstructs ontology from operations, formal differences, and causal circuits.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_26:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_27:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-05","text":"Moral responsibility consists in judgment and internal acceptance, not in absolute mastery over passions or external events.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_28:MME-06","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:BSD-06","text":"Stable finality is the condition of ordered contingency: the necessity of beatitude does not close freedom, but opens a plurality of comparable means.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-06","CL_GGVC_28:XD-03","CL_GGVC_28:RLE-04","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_07:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-01","text":"Divine action institutes substance → immanent principles produce operations → operations manifest forms → intellect reconstructively knows order → the created agent operates within the same order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:FGS-B01","CL_GGVC_25:XD-02","CL_GGVC_26:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-02","text":"Parts of the world → integrity of the universe → one human form → plural powers → coordinated acts constitute a recursion of the same non-homogeneous unity across diverse magnitudes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_26:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-03","text":"Sensitive reception → phantasm → abstraction → universal → apprehended good → choice → bodily motion effects the spiral by which cognition ascends from the world and action returns to the world.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:XD-02","CL_GGVC_27:XD-06","CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-04","text":"Apparent incongruity → distinction of respects → reclassification of the term → restoration of order → new tension at a higher level constitutes a permanent methodological circuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-05","text":"Instituted nature → grace → glory → rest and universal good → deliberation concerning means → beatitude compose the teleological attractor of the whole structure.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-11","CL_GGVC_28:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-06","text":"Form over matter, intellect over sense, will over lower appetite, and first cause over secondary cause act in the manner by which the higher preserves the causality of the lower.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-13","CL_GGVC_28:MME-05","CL_GGVC_28:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:RLE-07","text":"Death is a negative probative circuit: the destruction of the organic subject reveals which operations depend on the whole composite and which on the subsisting principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_26:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FGS-B01","text":"First action → substance and form → distinction of parts → causally open integrity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-01","CL_GGVC_25:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FGS-B02","text":"One form → one substantial being → resulting plurality of powers → operations distinguished according to subjects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-04","CL_GGVC_26:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FGS-B03","text":"Irreducible operation → proximate principle → formal account of the object → distinction of power → reconstruction of ontological architecture.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:GA-01","CL_GGVC_27:GA-01","CL_GGVC_27:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FGS-B04","text":"Disposed body → sensitive reception → phantasm → abstraction → intelligible species → personally received universality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:XD-02","CL_GGVC_27:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FGS-B05","text":"Universal good → necessary end → comparison of contingents → deliberation → appetitive acceptance → choice → impedible execution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-08","CL_GGVC_28:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FGS-B06","text":"First cause → preservation of the mode of the secondary cause → proper voluntary act → ordination to the end → rest without abolition of created causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-13","CL_GGVC_25:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AFM-B01","text":"Institution of substance → distinction of parts → adornment → completion → cessation of new constitution → fruitfulness of immanent causes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:AS-B03","CL_GGVC_25:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AFM-B02","text":"Effects of life → subsisting soul and form of the body → one being → plural powers → embodied transduction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_27:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AFM-B03","text":"Reception of forms → universal abstraction → apprehended good → appetite → particularization → bodily action.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:GA-06","CL_GGVC_27:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AFM-B04","text":"Necessary beatitude → contingent means → judgment → choice → execution → preserving first causality → terminal rest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:GA-06","CL_GGVC_28:XD-03","CL_GGVC_28:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:AFM-B05","text":"Apparent contradiction → distinction of respects → ontological reclassification → composition of causes → circumscribed residual tension.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-01","text":"Completion is reclassified from immobility into productive integrity that unfolds future states from causes already instituted.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_25:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-02","text":"Soul and mind are converted from juxtaposed things or a bundle of faculties into a formal, embodied, and transductive architecture.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:MME-01","CL_GGVC_27:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-03","text":"The opposition of universal and singular is reordered into a double motion: the ascent of abstraction and the descent of executive mediation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:MME-04","CL_GGVC_27:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-04","text":"Necessity and freedom cease to be simple contraries; coercion is excluded, but natural inclination, end, and higher causality order freedom.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:MME-01","CL_GGVC_28:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-05","text":"Exegesis, ontology, psychology, and the doctrine of action require one mode of reading: terms are referred to acts, acts to principles, and principles to causal respects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MME-06","text":"The human being is neither the measure of the value of the universe nor an indifferent part, but the singular locus where the created order exercises universal cognition and voluntary causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-12","CL_GGVC_25:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of surface, the subject is the completion of the world, the nature of the soul, the division of powers, appetite, will, and free choice.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level operate substantial form, the integrity of parts, powers, formal objects, mediations, and first and second causalities.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, chronological, anatomical, and libertarian questions are transferred into questions of respect, attribution of act, and coordination of causes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is the ordered actualization of powers, while the subject is a finite composite that knows, desires, and acts successively.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system effects by its distinctions the very non-homogeneous unity that it ontologically affirms.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_26:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_27:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_28:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-01","text":"Institution is said to be completely causally open, but the extent of subsequent novelty between the unfolding of principles and the addition of state is not yet exactly determined.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:GA-06","CL_GGVC_25:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-02","text":"The one being of soul and composite is reconciled with the subsistence of the soul after death, but the identity of the persisting principle and the identity of the complete human being are not fully reconciled.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:FL-01","CL_GGVC_26:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-03","text":"Intellect, formally independent of an organ, depends operationally on phantasms; the junction between non-organic subsistence and embodied cognition remains fragile.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:FL-02","CL_GGVC_26:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-04","text":"The first cause is said to effect the voluntary mode, but the manner in which higher determination does not absorb secondary choice requires further explanation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:FL-04","CL_GGVC_28:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:FL-05","text":"Necessarily desired beatitude opens a plurality of means, but the transition to the choice of evil under the aspect of apparent good and to God not yet seen is incompletely explained.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:FL-03","CL_GGVC_28:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-01","text":"Perfection shifts from the grade of life into the integrity of the whole, thence into the operation of the end, and finally into beatitude.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:MP-01","CL_GGVC_25:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-02","text":"Mediation shifts from an impediment to substantial union into a necessary condition of operation, cognition, choice, and execution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_27:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-03","text":"Passivity shifts from material alteration into perfective reception and finally into the mobility of appetite before the good.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:MP-01","CL_GGVC_27:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MP-04","text":"Necessity shifts from an apparent negation of the voluntary into a twofold structure, in which coercion removes freedom, whereas nature and end order it.","refs":["CL_GGVC_28:MP-02","CL_GGVC_28:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a nested architectural machine: the first cause institutes, form constitutes, powers transduce, appetite orders, and the secondary cause executes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_26:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_27:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-02","text":"The human subject is a distributed node and internal polity, in which one form, organs, faculties, reason, and will retain their proper acts.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_28:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-03","text":"Time is a spiral of actualization: instituted principles contain future acts, memory retains the past, appetite anticipates the future, and the end closes the series.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_27:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_28:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:HSI-04","text":"Navigation is possible as ascent from effect to principle and from singular to universal, then descent from universal through choice to singular effect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_27:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms concerning non-homogeneous unity, stratified causality, formal object, embodied mediation, attractive end, and dependent freedom suffice to reconstruct the machine of this metagregis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the nexus between constituting form, ordered powers, and higher causality that preserves the proper act of the lower.","refs":["CL_GGVC_26:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remain novelty within the completed world, the individuation of the separated soul, the dependence of intellect on phantasms, and the composition of divine motion with choice.","refs":["CL_GGVC_25:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_26:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_07:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement, because it proceeds from the constitution of order through formal interiorization and psychic transduction to voluntary causality; full reduction, however, resists the joints between levels.","refs":["CL_GGVC_27:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_28:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_07@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_GGVC_L1_07:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Ggvc L1 07","context":"Chapters 73–84 · Prima pars · Quaestio 72 · Prima pars · Quaestio 73 · Prima pars · Quaestio 74 · Prima pars · Quaestio 75 · Prima pars · Quaestio 76 · Prima pars · Quaestio 77 · Prima pars · Quaestio 78 · Prima pars · Quaestio 79 · Prima pars · Quaestio 80 · Prima pars · Quaestio 81 · Prima pars · Quaestio 82 · Prima pars · Quaestio 83"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08","layer":"MC","title":"Ggvc L1 08","order":8,"parents":["CL_GGVC_29","CL_GGVC_30","CL_GGVC_31","CL_GGVC_32"],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_08@R001","sha256":"f0d784a320ffda4ad0a69c8c2854af4e3022f9330abd1cc5f8009fca1ebf4128","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo homo, simul anima intellectiva et compositum corporeum, per origines, operationes atque mutationes status identitatem servet, cognitionem exerceat et ad perfectionem sibi non autonomam ordinetur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:AS-B01","CL_GGVC_30:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_31:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_32:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis modum operandi, cognoscendi et perficiendi ex modo essendi, proportione recipientis, statu temporali atque fine pendere constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-03","CL_GGVC_31:GA-01","CL_GGVC_32:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio oppositiones absolutas non simpliciter dissolvit, sed eas per distinctiones materiae et formae, naturae et status, potentiae et actus, causae et finis in relationes ordinatas convertit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:AS-B03","CL_GGVC_30:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_31:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-04","text":"Fundamentum est medietas hylomorphica et participativa hominis: intellectus organum formaliter transcendit, sed compositum, corpus et mediationes sensitivae eius obiecta, exercitationem et perfectionem determinant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_31:GA-03","CL_GGVC_32:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-05","text":"Motus procedit ab institutione compositi per cognitionem embodiedam et ordinem gratuitum ad bifurcationes lapsus, separationis et gloriae, quae eandem naturam sub diversis regiminibus recodificant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_32:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_32:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-06","text":"Motus verus est recursivus et mutatione status interruptus: receptio corporalis in intellectum ascendit, intellectus ad phantasma et regimen inferiorum redit, habitus autem effectus priorum actuum per mutationes posteriores vehunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:AS-B06","CL_GGVC_30:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_30:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_32:BAS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-07","text":"Clausura naturalis semper graduata et conditionata manet, quia separatio vel perfectio originaria mediationes mutare potest, sed intuitum divinum, impeccabilitatem et beatitudinem ex natura sola non producit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:AS-B08","CL_GGVC_30:BAS-08","CL_GGVC_30:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-08","text":"Gloria est attractor terminalis qui cognitionem, voluntatem et imaginem consummat, sed non ex abstractione, disembodimento aut sola integritate originaria continenter emergit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-06","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_30:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Modus essendi et status ontologicus modum operationis determinant, dum finis proprius mensuram perfectionis atque genus causae requisitum ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-03","CL_GGVC_31:BAS-02","CL_GGVC_31:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Omnis cognitio et participatio secundum proportionem, capacitatem et modum recipientis contrahitur; dignitas causae vel obiecti accessum aequalem non efficit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-01","CL_GGVC_30:GA-02","CL_GGVC_30:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Homo est compositum animae rationalis et corporis, cui anima subsistentiam materia excedentem habet, sed cui separatio plenitudinem naturae humanae non tribuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-03","CL_GGVC_31:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_31:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Corpus est interfacies constitutiva cognitionis, habitus et actionis: receptivitas eius materiam intelligibilem praebet, operationes determinat et historiam intellectualem efficit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-07","CL_GGVC_31:GA-07","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Transcendentia formalis intellectus supra organum non est autarkia, quia actualis operatio humana obiectis, phantasmatibus, dispositionibus corporalibus et ordine status indiget.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-03","CL_GGVC_29:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Mediationes cognitionis statui indexantur: conversio ad phantasmata regimen vitae corporalis constituit, cessante autem hoc regimine species participatae et habitus alium usum aperiunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","CL_GGVC_30:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_30:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Causalitas divina et causalitates creatae non sunt aemulae: causa prima fundat vel complet quod causae proximae secundum proprium ordinem determinant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_30:GA-11","CL_GGVC_31:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Actio divina immediata ad limina causalitatis creatae occurrit—creationem animae, primam institutionem, ordinem gratiae et consummationem—sine mediationibus naturalibus intra proprium campum abolendis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","CL_GGVC_31:XD-02","CL_GGVC_32:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Identitas humana per mutationes status servatur non per invariabilem operationem, sed per permanentiam naturae, potentiarum et habituum sub mutatione mediorum atque modorum usus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-10","CL_GGVC_32:GA-08","CL_GGVC_32:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Perfectio creata est teleologica et multimetra: limitatio localis, passibilitas vel difficultas secundum finem, statum et proportionem possunt positive ordinari.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-06","CL_GGVC_31:XD-06","CL_GGVC_32:GA-07","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Tempus anthropologicum est series regiminum causaliter inconvertibilium—institutio, exercitium embodiedum, lapsus vel perseverantia, separatio et gloria—non continuum unius operationis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_32:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Visio et fruitio divinae essentiae sunt clausura supernaturalis, non terminus naturalis abstractionis, separationis animae aut integritatis originariae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-12","CL_GGVC_32:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-01","text":"[Identitas stabilis] Proportio potentiae ad obiectum persistit ab abstractione materiali per cognitionem immaterialium usque ad species separatae animae et participationem gratiae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_30:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_32:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-02","text":"[Mutatio] Modus essendi transit a regula locali cognitionis in principium generale quod originem, operationem, separationem et consummationem distribuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-03","CL_GGVC_31:GA-01","CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-03","text":"[Amplificatio] Corpus mutatur ab apparenti impedimento in condicionem epistemicam, instrumentalem et historicam, ac deinde in terminum inferioris ordinis quem mens gratia recta gubernat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_30:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_31:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_32:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-04","text":"[Dislocatio] Conversio ad phantasmata ab invariabili definitione intellectionis removetur et tamquam necessitas propria status corporalis conservatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_30:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_30:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-05","text":"[Translatio] Causalitas divina migrat a fundamento remoto cognitionis ad creationem et primam formationem, deinde ad rectitudinem gratiae et terminum gloriae, semper diverso modo operans.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-11","CL_GGVC_31:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_32:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-06","text":"[Inversio] Defectus et limitatio vim invertunt: quod sub mensura absoluta privatio apparet sub fine proprio potest cognitionem, universalitatem instrumentalem vel meritum generare.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-08","CL_GGVC_31:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-07","text":"[Reditus functionalis] Habitus redit primo ut principium actus cognoscendum per operationem, deinde ut memoria structurata quae identitatem scientiae trans mutationem status vehit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_30:XD-05","CL_GGVC_30:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-08","text":"[Absorptio] Institutio originaria, innocentia, lapsus, mors et gloria desinunt esse eventus separati atque absorbentur in unam topologiam statuum, cuius transitiones media et ordines operationis mutant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-09","CL_GGVC_32:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_32:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-01","text":"[Localis] Explicationes particularium operationum—somnii, reflexionis ad habitum, specierum infusarum, complexionis organicae et passionum singularium—machinas locales praebent, sed structuram praesentem singillatim non regunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:CGD-06","CL_GGVC_30:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_31:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_32:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-02","text":"[Amplificata] Abstractio et phantasma sursum ascendunt non ut definitio omnis intellectionis, sed ut paradigma mediationis corporalis cui alia regimina cognoscitiva comparantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_30:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_30:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-03","text":"[Coniungens] Corpus unit cognitionem, originem et ordinem status, quia idem compositum materialiter recipit, intellectualiter transducit et hierarchice regitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-07","CL_GGVC_31:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_32:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-04","text":"[Transitionalis] Limites causalitatis creatae coniungunt primam institutionem cum gratia et gloria: ubi natura effectum sibi disproportionalem non producit, causa divina novum regimen aperit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_32:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-05","text":"[Recodificata] Mors et lapsus, inferioribus gradibus diversae mutationes, hoc gradu distinguuntur ut duae catastrophe: prior interfaciem operationis mutat, posterior ordinem relationum intra eandem interfaciem invertit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_32:XD-05","CL_GGVC_32:BAS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-06","text":"[Non amplificata] Distributiones sexuales, conclusiones hierarchicae domesticae et quaedam interpretationes exegeticae manent appendices locales, quia ex principiis communibus cognitionis, status et causalitatis non necessario derivantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:CGD-03","CL_GGVC_31:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_31:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-01","text":"Humanitas neque ad operationem organicam reducitur neque in anima separata completur; utrumque extremum compositionem hylomorphicam destruit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_31:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_31:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-02","text":"Systema excludit omnem viam immediatam qua species innatae, abstractio indefinita, separatio vel sola praesentia sui cognitionem universaliter plenam praebeant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_30:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_30:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-03","text":"Corpus non est carcer extrinsecus removendus, sed nec mensura ultima imaginis aut intellectionis; conditio constitutiva distinguitur a ratione formali operationis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_32:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_32:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-04","text":"Natura creata causaliter clausa esse nequit, sed actio divina causas proximas non evacuat neque naturalitatem operationis in gratiam convertit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_30:DBN-06","CL_GGVC_30:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-05","text":"Eadem natura non imponit unum modum operationis immobilem, nec identitas subiecti invariabilitatem mediorum, actuum aut status requirit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_30:XD-08","CL_GGVC_30:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-06","text":"Perfectio non est abolitio indiscriminata limitationis, ignorantiae, passionis, corporis vel difficultatis, sed eorum recta ordinatio ad finem et statum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:DBN-04","CL_GGVC_32:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_32:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet modum essendi rei, modum receptionis et modum cognitionis esse identicos; aliter neque abstractio neque participatio secundum recipiens intelligi posset.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_29:GA-01","CL_GGVC_30:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet subiectum intellectuale sibi immediate transparens et causaliter autonomum esse, quia sui cognitio, rectitudo et consummatio per actum, obiectum et causam superiorem mediantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_30:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_32:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet continuitatem personae aut scientiae identitatem operationis exigere; aliter neque mors neque lapsus mutationem status sine destructione subiecti admitterent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:DBN-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-10","CL_GGVC_32:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet obiectum nobilius, medium minus corporeum aut modus simplicior cognitionem recipienti necessario perfectiorem efficere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_30:XD-03","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet limitationem esse puram privationem: passibilitas, potentia, successio et defectibilitas constituunt condiciones cognitionis, progressus, meriti et mutationis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_31:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_31:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_32:BAS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-01","text":"Absentia ordinans est visio immediata et simultanea quae rem, seipsum, Deum et tempus sine mediatione possideat; omnis cognitio humana naturalis circa huius plenitudinis indisponibilitatem construitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_29:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_32:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-02","text":"Ontologice inadmissibilis est homo sui causa, sui mensura et sui finis, quia esse, institutio, veritas, rectitudo et consummatio participata manent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_32:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_31:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-03","text":"Indisponibilis est humanitas perfecte disembodieda: anima separata subsistit et operatur, sed nec compositionem naturalem nec determinatam plenitudinem cognitionis recuperat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_31:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_30:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-04","text":"Inadmissibilis est mensura univoca qua cognitio divina, angelica, separata et humana vel natura, gratia et gloria in unam scalam continuam redigantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_30:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-05","text":"Irreversibilitas naturaliter possessa ab origine abest; integritas prima defectibilis manet, separatio non beatificat, et sola gloria clausuram stabilientem praebet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_32:GA-10","CL_GGVC_30:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-01","text":"Corpus humanum ita instituitur ut sit medium epistemicum: passibilitas eius non solum compositionem complet, sed ingressum sensitivum cognitionis et determinationem actuum intellectualium causaliter sustinet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-05","CL_GGVC_29:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_31:GA-07","CL_GGVC_30:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-02","text":"Necessitas phantasmatum est naturalis sed statui relativa: indispensabilis est intellectioni animae corpori coniunctae, non autem essentiae omnis operationis eiusdem animae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","CL_GGVC_29:AS-B06","CL_GGVC_30:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_30:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-03","text":"Identitas humana est constantia naturae per interfaces mutabiles, non invariabilitas operationis: corpus, phantasma, habitus et species participatae successive diversa regimina eiusdem principii sustinent.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","CL_GGVC_30:GA-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-10","CL_GGVC_30:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-04","text":"Actio divina immediata condiciones naturae et gratiae instituit potius quam naturam aemulam substituit: creat compositum, aperit ordinem operationum et superaddit rectitudinem quam causae creatae non sufficiunt producere.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-08","CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","CL_GGVC_32:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_32:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-05","text":"Perfectio humana non uno indice mensuratur, sed coordinatis finis, recipientis, status, medii et ordinis; ideo nobilitas modi, amplitudo cognitionis, integritas et meritum divergere possunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-10","CL_GGVC_30:GA-02","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_31:GA-06","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-06","text":"Imago naturalis praebet constantiam sub mutatione, gratia conformitatem actualem, lapsus inversionem ordinis et gloria consummationem; hi gradus idem subiectum modaliter transformant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_32:GA-09","CL_GGVC_32:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-07","text":"Circuitus cognitionis fit circuitus gubernationis: receptio sensibilis in rationem ascendit, ratio universaliter iudicat et ad singularia, appetitum atque corpus ordinanda redit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","CL_GGVC_29:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_32:XD-02","CL_GGVC_32:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-08","text":"Lapsus et mors sunt catastrophe non aequivalentes: lapsus relationem hierarchicam intra statum embodiedum invertit, mors autem medium operationis tollit et novum regimen imperfectum aperit; neutra gloria est.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","CL_GGVC_32:XD-05","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-01","text":"Subiectum humanum est transductor diachronice stabilis: recipit per corpus, universalizat per intellectum, ordinat per rationem et historiam actuum in habitibus conservat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","CL_GGVC_29:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_32:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-02","text":"Cognitio non est possessio statui indifferens repraesentationum, sed relatio ontologice indicata cuius obiectum, medium, certitudo et ambitus cum regimine subiecti variantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-06","CL_GGVC_29:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_30:DBN-04","CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-03","text":"Embodimentum historicitatem intellectus constituit, quia actus corporali mediatione eliciti habitus generant qui post amissionem apparatus sensitivi identitatem cognitivam asymmetrice conservant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_30:XD-05","CL_GGVC_30:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-04","text":"Tempus humanum est machina statuum punctuatorum: institutio condiciones ponit, exercitium potentias explicat, lapsus ordinem invertit, mors medium mutat, gloria autem terminationem gratuitam constituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_32:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-05","text":"Causalitas totius est stratificata et liminalis: causae creatae transmutant praesupposita, intellectus format operationes et habitus, gratia ordinem rectificat, Deus autem initia et fines naturae disproportionatos efficit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-08","CL_GGVC_31:GA-05","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_32:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-06","text":"Finitudo est principium productionis potius quam mera imminutio: limitatio corporis cognitionem, successio scientiam, defectibilitas meritum et absentia visionis motum ad consummationem generant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","CL_GGVC_29:XD-05","CL_GGVC_31:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_32:XD-04","CL_GGVC_32:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-01","text":"Res sensibilis actualizat sensum, phantasma abstractionem alimentat, intellectus universale format, conversio singulare recuperat, atque iudicium operationem corporalem dirigit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_29:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_32:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-02","text":"Actus habitum generat vel manifestat, habitus novos actus disponit, et habitus permanens effectum status prioris in regimen posterius transfert.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_30:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-03","text":"Deus mentem ordinat, ratio inferiores vires gubernat, operationes ordinatae conformitatem renovant, et conformitas directionem mentis ad principium corroborat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","CL_GGVC_32:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-04","text":"Elatio subiectionem rationis rumpit, ruptura errorem et appetitum inordinatum aperit, effectus inferiores ulteriorem aversionem augent, atque lapsus se recursive amplificat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_32:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-05","text":"Possibilitas passiva sine causa creata sufficienti ad limen actionis divinae ducit; effectus divinus deinde ordinem creatum instituit intra quem causae secundariae operari possunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","CL_GGVC_31:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-06","text":"Limitatio apparens ad finem et statum remittitur; hac mutatione mensurae defectus in condicionem operationis convertitur, sed numquam in plenitudinem absolutam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:XD-06","CL_GGVC_31:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_32:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-07","text":"Attractores principales sunt proportio et gloria: proportio omnem statum finitum calibrat, gloria autem series mutationum ordinat tamquam terminus quem nulla intensificatio naturalis attingit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:RLE-07","CL_GGVC_29:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FGS-B01","text":"Esse participatum + finis humanus → compositum animae subsistentis et corporis intrinseci → potentiae proportionatae obiectis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-02","CL_GGVC_31:GA-03","CL_GGVC_29:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FGS-B02","text":"Examen causalitatis → praesuppositum creatum sufficiens: operatio naturalis; praesuppositum insufficiens: actio divina immediata → institutio novi ordinis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-05","CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","CL_GGVC_31:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FGS-B03","text":"Corpus passibile → sensus → phantasma → abstractio → species intelligibilis → cognitio universalis → conversio reflexa et actio particularis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-07","CL_GGVC_29:GA-05","CL_GGVC_29:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FGS-B04","text":"Mens orientata ad Deum → ratio recta → appetitus et corpus ordinata → actus conformes → habitus et meritum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","CL_GGVC_32:XD-02","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FGS-B05","text":"Permanentia naturae et habituum + mutatio status → cessatio medii prioris → novus modus operationis → continuitas asymmetrica sub nova limitatione.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-04","CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B06","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FGS-B06","text":"Integritas viatoria → exercitium et progressus → perseverantia aut elatio → conservatio vel inversio hierarchiae → mutatio status sine deletione naturae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B06","CL_GGVC_32:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FGS-B07","text":"Limites cognitionis naturalis + absentia visionis → fides, spes et motus teleologicus → gratia consummans → visio et fruitio terminales.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:XD-04","CL_GGVC_32:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_32:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:AFM-B01","text":"Esse participatum → causalitas divina originaria → compositum hylomorphicum → corpus epistemicum → cognitio sensitivo-intellectualis → habitus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-02","CL_GGVC_31:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_30:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:AFM-B02","text":"Obiectum materiale → phantasma → abstractio → intellectio universalis → reflexio ad singulare → ratio gubernans inferiores vires.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-02","CL_GGVC_29:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_32:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:AFM-B03","text":"Imago naturalis → gratia conformans → integritas viatoria → progressus → superbia aut perseverantia → lapsus aut ordinatio ad gloriam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:AFM-B04","text":"Vita corporalis → habitus acquisiti → mors → operatio sine phantasmatibus → cognitio separata limitata → consummatio tantum per ordinem gratuitum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MME-01","text":"Corpus reclassificatur ab obstaculo intellectus in infrastructuram cognitionis, habituum, actionis et ordinis totius hominis, sine conversione eius in rationem formalem imaginis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:MME-01","CL_GGVC_30:MME-03","CL_GGVC_31:MME-05","CL_GGVC_32:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MME-02","text":"Abstractio reclassificatur a scala universali versus intelligibilia in operationem propriam status embodiedi, cuius limites alios modos cognitionis exigunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:MME-02","CL_GGVC_30:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_30:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MME-03","text":"Mors reclassificatur neque ut consummatio neque ut deletio, sed ut mutatio interfaciei quae continuitatem subiecti servat et determinationem cognitionis simul minuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_30:MME-04","CL_GGVC_30:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MME-04","text":"Gratia reclassificatur ab additamento extrinseco in principium ordinis quo aptitudo naturalis mentis cognitionem, affectum et corpus ad finem supernaturalem coordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","CL_GGVC_32:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_32:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MME-05","text":"Perfectio transit ab unica scala excellentiae ad tabulam proportionum inter finem, statum, munus, recipientem, integritatem et meritum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_31:MME-03","CL_GGVC_32:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MME-06","text":"Identitas humana reclassificatur ut permanentia phaseologica: natura et habitus durant, dum media cognitionis, ordo interior et relatio ad finem mutantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_32:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei tractantur cognitio materialium et sui, origo animae et corporis, status innocentiae, lapsus, separatio et gloria.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali operantur proportio, hylomorphismus, actualitas, mediatio, habitus, causalitas stratificata et hierarchia rationis ad inferiores vires.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico quaestiones de possessione, defectu et perfectione in quaestiones accessus, status, finis et ordinis comparationis transferuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali homo est compositum participatum per regimina punctuata procedens, cuius natura persistit dum operationes, ordines et media mutantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo distinctiones ipsam machinam efficiunt: oppositiones non delentur, sed in proportiones, causalitates, modos et tempora non convertibilia distribuuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-01","text":"Independentia formalis intellectus ab organo et dependentia actualis a corpore cohaerent solum si essentia facultatis, materia obiecti et condicio exercitii stricte distinguuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:FL-01","CL_GGVC_29:XD-03","CL_GGVC_31:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-02","text":"Eadem natura diversos modos operandi sustinere dicitur, sed conceptus status mechanismum huius mutationis potius designat quam plene explicat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:FL-01","CL_GGVC_30:FL-02","CL_GGVC_30:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-03","text":"Immediata causalitas divina creationem sine subiecto, formationem ex materia, infusionem gratiae et consummationem tegit, quamvis hi actus unam rationem processualem non habeant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:FL-03","CL_GGVC_31:XD-02","CL_GGVC_32:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-04","text":"Immunitas originaria a deceptione cum possibilitate lapsus nititur prioritate causali superbiae voluntatis, cuius chronologia interior necessaria sed compressa manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:FL-03","CL_GGVC_32:XD-07","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-05","text":"Permanentia scientiae trans mortem requirit identitatem habitus sub mutatione radicali medii et usus, sed criterium huius identitatis non ultra distinctionem formalem explicatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:FL-06","CL_GGVC_30:GA-10","CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-06","text":"Transitus ab origine corporea ad hierarchiam socialem et typologiam sacramentalem structuram metagregis non necessario sequitur, quia mediatione praemissarum localium biologicarum et exegeticarum nititur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:FL-04","CL_GGVC_31:FL-05","CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MP-01","text":"Corpus mutatur ab obiecti condicione in interfaciem cognitionis, deinde in vehiculum historiae habituum et terminum regiminis hierarchici.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_31:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_32:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MP-02","text":"Perfectio mutatur ab excellentia simplici in sufficientiam finalem, proportionem recipientis, integritatem status et terminum supernaturalem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-06","CL_GGVC_32:GA-07","CL_GGVC_32:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MP-03","text":"Imago mutatur ab aptitudine naturali mentis in conformitatem gratuitam et postremo in consummationem gloriosam, eodem subiecto sed diversa participationis actualitate manente.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-03","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_32:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina conversionum stratificatarum: materia recipit, sensus transmittit, intellectus universalizat, ratio reordinat, gratia rectificat et Deus limina naturae aperit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_30:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_32:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est limen operativum et memoria phaseologica, non centrum actu completum: identitas eius in translatione inter corpus, intellectum, habitum et statum consistit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_30:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_32:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est via punctuata et ramificata in qua institutio ordinem aperit, exercitium habitus accumulat, lapsus directionem invertit, mors medium mutat et gloria cursum claudit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_30:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_32:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-04","text":"Certamen fundamentale non est spiritus contra corpus, sed ordo participatus contra autonomiam: navigatio possibilis per mediationes, proportionem et rectitudinem procedit, fuga immediata eas destruit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_30:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_32:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata modi essendi, proportionis, compositionis hylomorphicae, mediationis corporalis, causalitatis stratificatae, permanentiae sub mutatione status et gloriae terminalis sufficiunt ad machinam praesentem restituendam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus inter compositionem humanam et pluralitatem regiminum: corpus cognitionem determinat, habitus continuitatem portant, status modum operationis mutat et gratia ordinem ad finem instituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent mechanismus abstractionis et specierum participatarum, coordinatio habitus sub mutatione usus, relatio exacta naturae ad gratiam atque nexus inter visionem et impeccabilitatem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_32:FL-01","CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B04","text":"Systema hac magnitudine cohaeret per recursionem et mutationem modalem, non per seriem uniformem: eadem axiomata redeunt sub functionibus diversis ad originem, cognitionem, lapsum, mortem et gloriam ordinanda.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B05","text":"Reductioni plenae resistunt commissurae quibus physica in normam socialem, mediatio naturalis in ordinem gratuitum et habitus permanens in operationem post mortem transfertur; hae tamen nucleum generativum non destruunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T174020982275Z_9b703ffe","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Ggvc L1 08","source_sha256":"f0d784a320ffda4ad0a69c8c2854af4e3022f9330abd1cc5f8009fca1ebf4128","sha256":"9b40a332dde09a5d769d91153db84a2ac248ffeab8140095de717961422a2ce5","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:40:20.983202+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how the human being, at once an intellectual soul and a bodily composite, preserves identity through origins, operations, and changes of state, exercises cognition, and is ordered toward a perfection not autonomous to itself.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:AS-B01","CL_GGVC_30:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_31:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_32:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis establishes that the mode of operating, knowing, and being perfected depends on the mode of being, the proportion of the recipient, temporal state, and end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-03","CL_GGVC_31:GA-01","CL_GGVC_32:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-03","text":"The argument does not simply dissolve absolute oppositions, but converts them into ordered relations through distinctions of matter and form, nature and state, potency and act, cause and end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:AS-B03","CL_GGVC_30:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_31:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-04","text":"The foundation is the hylomorphic and participatory mean of the human being: the intellect formally transcends the organ, yet the composite, the body, and its sensitive mediations determine its objects, exercise, and perfection.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_31:GA-03","CL_GGVC_32:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-05","text":"The movement proceeds from the institution of the composite through embodied cognition and gratuitous order to the bifurcations of fall, separation, and glory, which recodify the same nature under diverse regimes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_32:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_32:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-06","text":"The true movement is recursive and interrupted by change of state: bodily reception ascends into intellect, intellect returns to the phantasm and the governance of lower powers, while habits carry the effects of prior acts through subsequent changes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:AS-B06","CL_GGVC_30:BAS-07","CL_GGVC_30:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_32:BAS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-07","text":"Natural closure always remains graduated and conditioned, because separation or original perfection can alter mediations, but does not produce divine vision, impeccability, and beatitude from nature alone.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:AS-B08","CL_GGVC_30:BAS-08","CL_GGVC_30:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BAS-08","text":"Glory is the terminal attractor that consummates cognition, will, and image, but does not continuously emerge from abstraction, disembodiment, or original integrity alone.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-06","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_30:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] The mode of being and ontological state determine the mode of operation, while the proper end orders the measure of perfection and the kind of cause required.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-03","CL_GGVC_31:BAS-02","CL_GGVC_31:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Every cognition and participation is contracted according to the proportion, capacity, and mode of the recipient; the dignity of the cause or object does not effect equal access.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-01","CL_GGVC_30:GA-02","CL_GGVC_30:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] The human being is a composite of rational soul and body, whose soul has a subsistence exceeding matter, but to which separation does not confer the fullness of human nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-03","CL_GGVC_31:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_31:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] The body is a constitutive interface of cognition, habit, and action: its receptivity supplies intelligible matter, determines operations, and makes intellectual history possible.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-07","CL_GGVC_31:GA-07","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] The formal transcendence of intellect above the organ is not autarky, because actual human operation requires objects, phantasms, bodily dispositions, and an order of state.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-03","CL_GGVC_29:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] The mediations of cognition are indexed to state: conversion toward phantasms constitutes the regime of bodily life, but when this regime ceases, participated species and habits open another use.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-06","CL_GGVC_30:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_30:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Divine causality and created causalities are not rivals: the first cause founds or completes what proximate causes determine according to their proper order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_30:GA-11","CL_GGVC_31:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Immediate divine action occurs at thresholds of created causality—creation of the soul, first institution, the order of grace, and consummation—without abolishing natural mediations within their proper field.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","CL_GGVC_31:XD-02","CL_GGVC_32:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Human identity is preserved through changes of state not by invariable operation, but by the permanence of nature, powers, and habits under a change of means and modes of use.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-10","CL_GGVC_32:GA-08","CL_GGVC_32:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Created perfection is teleological and multimetric: local limitation, passibility, or difficulty can be positively ordered according to end, state, and proportion.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-06","CL_GGVC_31:XD-06","CL_GGVC_32:GA-07","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Anthropological time is a series of causally irreversible regimes—institution, embodied exercise, fall or perseverance, separation, and glory—not a continuum of one operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_32:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Vision and fruition of the divine essence are supernatural closure, not the natural terminus of abstraction, separation of the soul, or original integrity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-12","CL_GGVC_32:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] The proportion of power to object persists from material abstraction through cognition of immaterial things to the species of the separated soul and participation in grace.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_30:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_32:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] The mode of being passes from a local rule of cognition into a general principle that distributes origin, operation, separation, and consummation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-03","CL_GGVC_31:GA-01","CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-03","text":"[Amplification] The body is transformed from an apparent impediment into an epistemic, instrumental, and historical condition, and then into a terminus of lower order which the mind rightly governs by grace.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_30:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_31:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_32:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-04","text":"[Dislocation] Conversion toward phantasms is removed from an invariable definition of intellection and retained as a necessity proper to the bodily state.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_30:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_30:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-05","text":"[Translation] Divine causality migrates from the remote foundation of cognition to creation and first formation, then to the rectitude of grace and the terminus of glory, always operating in a diverse mode.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-11","CL_GGVC_31:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_32:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-06","text":"[Inversion] Defect and limitation invert their force: what appears as privation under an absolute measure can generate cognition, instrumental universality, or merit under a proper end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-08","CL_GGVC_31:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-07","text":"[Functional return] Habit returns first as a principle of act to be known through operation, then as structured memory that carries the identity of knowledge across a change of state.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_30:XD-05","CL_GGVC_30:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RMD-08","text":"[Absorption] Original institution, innocence, fall, death, and glory cease to be separate events and are absorbed into one topology of states, whose transitions alter media and orders of operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-09","CL_GGVC_32:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_32:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-01","text":"[Local] Explanations of particular operations—dreaming, reflection upon habit, infused species, organic complexion, and individual passions—supply local mechanisms, but do not separately govern the present structure.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:CGD-06","CL_GGVC_30:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_31:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_32:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-02","text":"[Amplified] Abstraction and phantasm rise upward not as the definition of all intellection, but as a paradigm of bodily mediation against which other cognitive regimes are compared.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_30:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_30:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-03","text":"[Connecting] The body unites cognition, origin, and the order of state, because the same composite receives materially, transduces intellectually, and is governed hierarchically.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-07","CL_GGVC_31:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_32:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-04","text":"[Transitional] The limits of created causality join first institution with grace and glory: where nature does not produce an effect disproportionate to itself, divine causality opens a new regime.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_32:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-05","text":"[Recodified] Death and fall, diverse changes at lower levels, are distinguished at this level as two catastrophes: the former alters the interface of operation, the latter inverts the order of relations within the same interface.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_32:XD-05","CL_GGVC_32:BAS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:CGD-06","text":"[Not amplified] Sexual distributions, domestic hierarchical conclusions, and certain exegetical interpretations remain local appendices, because they are not necessarily derived from the common principles of cognition, state, and causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:CGD-03","CL_GGVC_31:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_31:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-01","text":"Humanity is neither reduced to organic operation nor completed in the separated soul; each extreme destroys hylomorphic composition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_31:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_31:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-02","text":"The system excludes every immediate way by which innate species, indefinite abstraction, separation, or self-presence alone might provide universally full cognition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_30:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_30:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-03","text":"The body is not a prison to be extrinsically removed, but neither is it the ultimate measure of image or intellection; a constitutive condition is distinguished from the formal principle of operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_32:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_32:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-04","text":"Created nature cannot be causally closed, but divine action neither evacuates proximate causes nor converts the naturalness of operation into grace.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_30:DBN-06","CL_GGVC_30:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-05","text":"The same nature does not impose one immutable mode of operation, nor does the identity of the subject require invariability of means, acts, or state.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_30:XD-08","CL_GGVC_30:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:GNX-06","text":"Perfection is not the indiscriminate abolition of limitation, ignorance, passion, body, or difficulty, but their right ordering to end and state.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:DBN-04","CL_GGVC_32:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_32:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that the mode of being of a thing, the mode of reception, and the mode of cognition are identical; otherwise neither abstraction nor participation according to the recipient could be understood.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_29:GA-01","CL_GGVC_30:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that the intellectual subject is immediately transparent to itself and causally autonomous, because self-knowledge, rectitude, and consummation are mediated through act, object, and higher cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_30:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_32:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that continuity of person or knowledge requires identity of operation; otherwise neither death nor fall would admit a change of state without destruction of the subject.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:DBN-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-10","CL_GGVC_32:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that a nobler object, a less bodily medium, or a simpler mode necessarily makes cognition more perfect for the recipient.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_30:XD-03","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that limitation is pure privation: passibility, potency, succession, and defectibility constitute conditions of cognition, progress, merit, and change.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_31:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_31:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_32:BAS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-01","text":"The ordering absence is immediate and simultaneous vision that possesses the thing, oneself, God, and time without mediation; all natural human cognition is constructed around the unavailability of this fullness.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_29:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_32:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-02","text":"A human being who is his own cause, measure, and end is ontologically inadmissible, because being, institution, truth, rectitude, and consummation remain participated.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_32:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_31:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-03","text":"Perfectly disembodied humanity is unavailable: the separated soul subsists and operates, but recovers neither natural composition nor determinate fullness of cognition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_31:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_30:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-04","text":"A univocal measure by which divine, angelic, separated, and human cognition, or nature, grace, and glory, are reduced to one continuous scale is inadmissible.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_30:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSA-05","text":"Irreversibility naturally possessed from the origin is absent; first integrity remains defectible, separation does not beatify, and glory alone provides stabilizing closure.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_32:GA-10","CL_GGVC_30:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-01","text":"The human body is so instituted as to be an epistemic medium: its passibility not only completes composition, but causally sustains the sensitive ingress of cognition and the determination of intellectual acts.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-05","CL_GGVC_29:AS-B04","CL_GGVC_31:GA-07","CL_GGVC_30:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-02","text":"The necessity of phantasms is natural but relative to state: it is indispensable to the intellection of a soul conjoined to a body, but not to the essence of every operation of the same soul.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","CL_GGVC_29:AS-B06","CL_GGVC_30:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_30:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-03","text":"Human identity is the constancy of nature through mutable interfaces, not invariability of operation: body, phantasm, habit, and participated species successively sustain diverse regimes of the same principle.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","CL_GGVC_30:GA-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-10","CL_GGVC_30:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-04","text":"Immediate divine action institutes the conditions of nature and grace rather than substituting a rival nature: it creates the composite, opens the order of operations, and superadds a rectitude which created causes do not suffice to produce.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-08","CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","CL_GGVC_32:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_32:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-05","text":"Human perfection is not measured by one index, but by the coordinates of end, recipient, state, medium, and order; therefore nobility of mode, scope of cognition, integrity, and merit can diverge.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-10","CL_GGVC_30:GA-02","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_31:GA-06","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-06","text":"The natural image provides constancy under change, grace actual conformity, fall inversion of order, and glory consummation; these stages modally transform the same subject.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_32:GA-09","CL_GGVC_32:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-07","text":"The circuit of cognition becomes a circuit of governance: sensible reception ascends into reason, reason judges universally, and returns to order singulars, appetite, and body.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","CL_GGVC_29:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_32:XD-02","CL_GGVC_32:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:XD-08","text":"Fall and death are non-equivalent catastrophes: fall inverts the hierarchical relation within the embodied state, whereas death removes the medium of operation and opens a new imperfect regime; neither is glory.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","CL_GGVC_32:XD-05","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-01","text":"The human subject is a diachronically stable transducer: it receives through the body, universalizes through intellect, orders through reason, and preserves the history of acts in habits.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","CL_GGVC_29:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_32:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-02","text":"Cognition is not a state-indifferent possession of representations, but an ontologically indexed relation whose object, medium, certainty, and scope vary with the regime of the subject.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-06","CL_GGVC_29:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_30:DBN-04","CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-03","text":"Embodiment constitutes the historicity of intellect, because acts elicited through bodily mediation generate habits which asymmetrically preserve cognitive identity after the loss of the sensitive apparatus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_30:XD-05","CL_GGVC_30:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-04","text":"Human time is a machine of punctuated states: institution sets conditions, exercise unfolds powers, fall inverts order, death changes the medium, while glory constitutes gratuitous termination.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_32:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-05","text":"The causality of the whole is stratified and liminal: created causes transform presuppositions, intellect forms operations and habits, grace rectifies order, while God effects beginnings and ends disproportionate to nature.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-08","CL_GGVC_31:GA-05","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_32:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:BSD-06","text":"Finitude is a principle of production rather than mere diminishment: bodily limitation generates cognition, succession knowledge, defectibility merit, and absence of vision movement toward consummation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_08:GA-12","CL_GGVC_29:XD-05","CL_GGVC_31:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_32:XD-04","CL_GGVC_32:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-01","text":"The sensible thing actualizes sense, the phantasm nourishes abstraction, intellect forms the universal, conversion recovers the singular, and judgment directs bodily operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_29:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_32:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-02","text":"Act generates or manifests habit, habit disposes new acts, and enduring habit transfers the effect of the prior state into the subsequent regime.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_30:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-03","text":"God orders the mind, reason governs the lower powers, ordered operations renew conformity, and conformity strengthens the direction of the mind toward the principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","CL_GGVC_32:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-04","text":"Pride breaks the subjection of reason, the rupture opens error and disordered appetite, lower effects increase further aversion, and fall recursively amplifies itself.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_32:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-05","text":"Passive possibility without a sufficient created cause leads to the threshold of divine action; the divine effect then institutes the created order within which secondary causes can operate.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:DBN-03","CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","CL_GGVC_31:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-06","text":"Apparent limitation is referred back to end and state; through this change of measure, defect is converted into a condition of operation, but never into absolute fullness.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:XD-06","CL_GGVC_31:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_32:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:RLE-07","text":"The principal attractors are proportion and glory: proportion calibrates every finite state, while glory orders the series of changes as a terminus which no natural intensification attains.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:RLE-07","CL_GGVC_29:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FGS-B01","text":"Participated being + human end → composite of subsistent soul and intrinsic body → powers proportioned to objects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-02","CL_GGVC_31:GA-03","CL_GGVC_29:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FGS-B02","text":"Examination of causality → sufficient created presupposition: natural operation; insufficient presupposition: immediate divine action → institution of a new order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-05","CL_GGVC_31:GA-04","CL_GGVC_31:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FGS-B03","text":"Passible body → sense → phantasm → abstraction → intelligible species → universal cognition → reflexive conversion and particular action.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-07","CL_GGVC_29:GA-05","CL_GGVC_29:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FGS-B04","text":"Mind oriented toward God → right reason → ordered appetite and body → conforming acts → habits and merit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","CL_GGVC_32:XD-02","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FGS-B05","text":"Permanence of nature and habits + change of state → cessation of prior medium → new mode of operation → asymmetric continuity under new limitation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GA-04","CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B06","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FGS-B06","text":"Wayfarer integrity → exercise and progress → perseverance or pride → preservation or inversion of hierarchy → change of state without deletion of nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B06","CL_GGVC_32:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FGS-B07","text":"Limits of natural cognition + absence of vision → faith, hope, and teleological movement → consummating grace → terminal vision and fruition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:XD-04","CL_GGVC_32:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_32:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:AFM-B01","text":"Participated being → originating divine causality → hylomorphic composite → epistemic body → sensitive-intellectual cognition → habits.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-02","CL_GGVC_31:BAS-06","CL_GGVC_30:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:AFM-B02","text":"Material object → phantasm → abstraction → universal intellection → reflection upon the singular → reason governing lower powers.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:GA-02","CL_GGVC_29:RMD-03","CL_GGVC_32:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:AFM-B03","text":"Natural image → conforming grace → wayfarer integrity → progress → pride or perseverance → fall or ordering toward glory.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_32:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:AFM-B04","text":"Bodily life → acquired habits → death → operation without phantasms → limited separated cognition → consummation only through gratuitous order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_30:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_30:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MME-01","text":"The body is reclassified from an obstacle to intellect into an infrastructure of cognition, habits, action, and the order of the whole human being, without being converted into the formal principle of image.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:MME-01","CL_GGVC_30:MME-03","CL_GGVC_31:MME-05","CL_GGVC_32:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MME-02","text":"Abstraction is reclassified from a universal scale toward intelligibles into an operation proper to the embodied state, whose limits require other modes of cognition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:MME-02","CL_GGVC_30:RMD-06","CL_GGVC_30:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MME-03","text":"Death is reclassified neither as consummation nor as deletion, but as a change of interface that preserves continuity of subject while simultaneously reducing the determination of cognition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:GNX-04","CL_GGVC_30:MME-04","CL_GGVC_30:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MME-04","text":"Grace is reclassified from an extrinsic addition into a principle of order by which the natural aptitude of the mind coordinates cognition, affection, and body toward the supernatural end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-04","CL_GGVC_32:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_32:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MME-05","text":"Perfection passes from a single scale of excellence to a table of proportions among end, state, office, recipient, integrity, and merit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_31:MME-03","CL_GGVC_32:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MME-06","text":"Human identity is reclassified as phaseological permanence: nature and habits endure, while the media of cognition, interior order, and relation to end change.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_32:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level are treated cognition of material things and self, the origin of soul and body, the state of innocence, fall, separation, and glory.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level operate proportion, hylomorphism, actuality, mediation, habit, stratified causality, and the hierarchy of reason over lower powers.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, questions concerning possession, defect, and perfection are transferred into questions of access, state, end, and order of comparison.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, the human being is a participated composite proceeding through punctuated regimes, whose nature persists while operations, orders, and media change.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, distinctions themselves constitute the machine: oppositions are not erased, but distributed among proportions, causalities, modes, and non-convertible times.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_30:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_31:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_32:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-01","text":"The formal independence of intellect from the organ and its actual dependence on the body cohere only if the essence of the faculty, the matter of the object, and the condition of exercise are strictly distinguished.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:FL-01","CL_GGVC_29:XD-03","CL_GGVC_31:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-02","text":"The same nature is said to sustain diverse modes of operating, but the concept of state designates rather than fully explains the mechanism of this change.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:FL-01","CL_GGVC_30:FL-02","CL_GGVC_30:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-03","text":"Immediate divine causality covers creation without a subject, formation from matter, infusion of grace, and consummation, although these acts do not have one processual rationale.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:FL-03","CL_GGVC_31:XD-02","CL_GGVC_32:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-04","text":"Original immunity from deception is strained by the possibility of fall through the causal priority of pride of the will, whose interior chronology remains necessary but compressed.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:FL-03","CL_GGVC_32:XD-07","CL_GGVC_32:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-05","text":"The permanence of knowledge through death requires the identity of habit under radical change of medium and use, but the criterion of this identity is not explained beyond formal distinction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:FL-06","CL_GGVC_30:GA-10","CL_GGVC_30:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:FL-06","text":"The transition from bodily origin to social hierarchy and sacramental typology does not necessarily follow the metasystemic structure, because it depends upon the mediation of local biological and exegetical premises.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:FL-04","CL_GGVC_31:FL-05","CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MP-01","text":"The body changes from a condition of the object into an interface of cognition, then into a vehicle of the history of habits and a terminus of hierarchical governance.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_31:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_32:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MP-02","text":"Perfection changes from simple excellence into final sufficiency, proportion of the recipient, integrity of state, and supernatural terminus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_31:GA-06","CL_GGVC_32:GA-07","CL_GGVC_32:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MP-03","text":"Image changes from a natural aptitude of the mind into gratuitous conformity and finally into glorious consummation, with the same subject remaining but with a diverse actuality of participation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_32:GA-03","CL_GGVC_32:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_32:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a machine of stratified conversions: matter receives, sense transmits, intellect universalizes, reason reorders, grace rectifies, and God opens the thresholds of nature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_30:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_32:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-02","text":"The subject is an operative threshold and phaseological memory, not an actually complete center: its identity consists in translation among body, intellect, habit, and state.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_30:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_32:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-03","text":"Time is a punctuated and branching path in which institution opens order, exercise accumulates habits, fall inverts direction, death changes the medium, and glory closes the course.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_30:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_32:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:HSI-04","text":"The fundamental contest is not spirit against body, but participated order against autonomy: possible navigation proceeds through mediations, proportion, and rectitude; immediate flight destroys them.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_30:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_31:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_32:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of mode of being, proportion, hylomorphic composition, bodily mediation, stratified causality, permanence under change of state, and terminal glory suffice to reconstruct the present machine.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the nexus between human composition and the plurality of regimes: the body determines cognition, habits carry continuity, state changes the mode of operation, and grace institutes order toward the end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B03","text":"The mechanisms of abstraction and participated species, the coordination of habits under change of use, the exact relation of nature to grace, and the nexus between vision and impeccability remain less determined.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_32:FL-01","CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B04","text":"The system at this scale coheres through recursion and modal change, not through a uniform series: the same axioms return under diverse functions to order origin, cognition, fall, death, and glory.","refs":["CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_08:MRT-B05","text":"The joints by which physics is transferred into social norm, natural mediation into gratuitous order, and enduring habit into operation after death resist full reduction; nevertheless, these do not destroy the generative core.","refs":["CL_GGVC_29:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_30:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_31:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_32:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_08@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_GGVC_L1_08:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Ggvc L1 08","context":"Chapters 85–96 · Prima pars · Quaestio 84 · Prima pars · Quaestio 85 · Prima pars · Quaestio 86 · Prima pars · Quaestio 87 · Prima pars · Quaestio 88 · Prima pars · Quaestio 89 · Prima pars · Quaestio 90 · Prima pars · Quaestio 91 · Prima pars · Quaestio 92 · Prima pars · Quaestio 93 · Prima pars · Quaestio 94 · Prima pars · Quaestio 95"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09","layer":"MC","title":"Ggvc L1 09","order":9,"parents":["CL_GGVC_33","CL_GGVC_34","CL_GGVC_35","CL_GGVC_36"],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_09@R001","sha256":"463a9e34707197729643623ef514f668ea9f3671b8984cc7d07dbe41edced311","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo creatura finita et totaliter dependens integritatem, progressionem, libertatem atque operationem propriam retineat sine autarchia causali vel confusione ordinum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_33:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis statuit causalitatem divinam naturam creatam non substituere, sed eam conservando, ordinando et applicando ad actum veram causalitatem secundam constituere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-02","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio analogice eandem formam gubernationis per corpus, maturationem, ordinem cosmologicum et communicationem intellectualem distribuit, differentiis mediorum et recipientium servatis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_36:BAS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-04","text":"Motus ab integritate operationum finitarum ad conservationem ipsius esse descendit, deinde ad mediationem et destinationem selectivam effectuum iterum ascendit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_36:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-05","text":"Progressio est spiralis et scalaris: oppositio apparens in ordines distinguitur, operatio legitima restituitur, defectus localis reclassificatur, et totum sub fine extrinseco reincluditur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-06","text":"Malum et corruptio non constituunt alteram naturam, sed effectus diversos rupturae relationum quae tamen extra causalitatem universalem exire nequeunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:XD-03","CL_GGVC_35:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-07","text":"Consummatio neque prolongatio indefinita vitae generativae neque regressus in nihilum est, sed mutatio terminalis qua regimen animalis et temporale ad alium statum transit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-08","text":"Clausura asymmetrica est: creatura numquam originem, permanentiam aut finem ex se possidet, sed ipsa dependentia ordinata eius operationem propriam, interioritatem et communicationem stabilit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Natura creata sub dono ordinante manet eadem natura; integritas eius operationes retinet et solam repugnantiam destructivam excludit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-02","CL_GGVC_35:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Esse participatum permanentiam ex communicatione continua causae primae recipit, non ex sufficientia intrinseca creaturae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Causalitas prima et causalitas secunda non sunt vires univocae competentes; prior potentiam et applicationem sustentat, posterior proprie operatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-04","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Integritas ab absentia repugnantiae poenalis in structuram dynamicam conservationis, maturationis et mediationis ordinatae amplificatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_34:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Perfectio creaturae est relativa capacitati, aetati, statui, officio et fini recipientis, non plenitudo univoca statim possessa.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Corporeitas, tempus, maturatio et mediatio sunt condiciones positivae operationis finitae, quamvis corruptioni et impedimento subiaci possint.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-05","CL_GGVC_34:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Potentia agendi creaturae per dependentiam ordinatam augetur, quia perfectio recepta in operationem propriam et communicationem boni convertitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-10","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Defectus est privatio ordinis particularis aut ruptura relationis debitae; non esse autem nullum principium positivum vel ordinem aemulum constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-08","CL_GGVC_35:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Hierarchia ex dominio scalari in distributionem functionum, graduum receptionis et canalium communicationis mutatur, sine aequalitate vel dominatione totali.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_36:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Bonum essentiale et finis extrinsecus conservationem, maturationem, gubernationem et communicationem in unum ordinem teleologicum colligunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-13","CL_GGVC_35:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Consummatio beatifica aversionem claudit et vitam animalem generativam terminat, sed creaturam non annihilat neque finitudinem eius in autonomiam convertit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-09","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Libertas, historia moralis et secretum conceptus ex principio intrinseco dependenter constituto oriuntur, non ex absentia causae superioris.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:XD-03","CL_GGVC_36:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-01","text":"Integritas identitatem functionis retinet, sed ab ordine corporis et appetitus ad progressionem temporalem, permanentiam ontologicam et operationem communicativam migrat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_35:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-02","text":"Conservatio mutatur a resistentia locali contra corruptionem in communicationem praesentem ipsius esse, cui remedia naturalia subordinantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_35:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-03","text":"Immediatio nomen retinet, sed invertitur ab intima causalitate divina in manifestationem creatam sine instrumento sensibili atque in absentiam impedimenti localis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_36:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-04","text":"Hierarchia transit a catena subiectionum ad topologiam duplicem, in qua veritas descendit secundum gradum, locutio autem pluribus directionibus destinatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_36:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-05","text":"Perfectio recedit ab imagine plenitudinis simultaneae et fit mensura relativa progressionis, status recipientis et termini vitae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_34:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-06","text":"Corpus mutatur ab obiecto gubernando in medium cognitionis et maturationis; ubi communicatio incorporea tractatur, absentia corporis non in transparentiam universalem convertitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_36:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-07","text":"Libertas redit non ut insubiectio, sed ut actus intrinsecus qui bonum, historiam personalem et accessum communicativum intra dependentiam ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-06","CL_GGVC_36:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-01","text":"Determinationes physiologicae, geographicae et miraculorum gradationes manent apparatus locales; earum particularitas axioma commune huius gradus non constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_34:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_35:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_36:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-02","text":"Distinctio inter operationem naturalem et corruptionem sursum amplificatur in regulam qua defectus ex relatione inordinata, non ex natura facultatis, explicatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_33:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-03","text":"Conservatio corporalis, causalitas secundaria et ministerium intellectuale coniunguntur per principium mediationis cuius efficacia ab ordine causae primae recipitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_36:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-04","text":"Mutatio a tutela corporis ad dependentiam essendi non tollit remedia localia, sed eorum vim intra causalitatem ontologicam altiorem collocat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_35:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-05","text":"Descensus illuminationis manet lex localis communicationis perfectivae; non universalizatur in omnem locutionem, persuasionem vel destinationem conceptus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_36:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-06","text":"Explicationes inferiores diversitatis sexualis per causas physiologicas absorbentur in propositionem altiorem de perfectione naturae distributa, non in legem universalem causalitatis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:CGD-03","CL_GGVC_34:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat perfectionem quae corpus, sexualitatem, tempus, differentiam aut mediationem tamquam defectus essentiales evacuat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_34:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-02","text":"Systema negat omnem limitationem naturalem esse culpam, poenam vel corruptionem, quia imperfectio initialis actui et aetati proportionari potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_34:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-03","text":"Systema negat causalitatem divinam et creatam tamquam vires eiusdem ordinis, quarum efficacia altera alteram minuere debeat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_35:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-04","text":"Systema negat creaturam post productionem per se permanere aut originem divinam recipere dum conservationem et finem sibi vindicat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_35:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-05","text":"Systema negat malum, contingentiam vel voluntatem creatam constituere regionem realem extra ordinem causae universalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_35:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-06","text":"Systema negat absentiam corporis aut loci efficere publicitatem necessariam mentium, quia communicatio adhuc receptione et destinatione limitatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_36:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-07","text":"Systema negat statum innocentiae, vitam generativam vel habitationem creatam esse ipsam consummationem finalem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_34:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet creaturam semel productam posse sine communicatione essendi permanere, quia tunc conservatio ab origine ontologice separaretur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_35:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet unam mensuram perfectionis omnibus naturis, aetatibus et intellectibus convenire, quia receptio semper capacitatem recipientis sequitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_36:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet operationem propriam libertatemque causalitate independente indigere; aliter dependentia totalis omne agens secundum evacuaret.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet medium creatum vel causam secundam aut independentiam conferre aut interventum divinum impedire, quia mediatio ipsa causaliter recepta est.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet consummationem esse vel extensionem indefinitam vitae generativae vel attractionem rerum ad nihilum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_35:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-01","text":"Exterioritas ontologica a causa universali est possibilitas radicaliter inadmissibilis, quia non constitueret alterum ordinem sed privationem essendi.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-02","text":"Indisponibilis est habitatio finita quae se in finem ultimum claudat, quoniam omnis status creatus ad perfectionem extrinsecam refertur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:BSA-05","CL_GGVC_35:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-03","text":"Indisponibilis est immediatio creata quae nullam mensuram recipientis, medium operationis aut electionem accessus requirat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-08","CL_GGVC_36:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-04","text":"Systema circa absentiam principii autonomi ordinatur: neque nihilum neque creatura sibi sufficiens potest fons, finis aut norma totius fieri.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_36:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-01","text":"Integritas est stabilitas dynamice sustentata: natura finita per maturationem, conservationem et ordinationem perficitur, non per immobilitatem aut abolitionem processuum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","CL_GGVC_33:GA-01","CL_GGVC_34:GA-02","CL_GGVC_35:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-02","text":"Dependentia ontologica est principium generativum potentiae agendi, quia causa prima sustentat facultatem per quam creatura operationem et bonum communicat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-07","CL_GGVC_35:GA-10","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-03","text":"Quia perfectio secundum recipientem contrahitur, nullus status individualis—infantia, maturitas, superioritas vel cognitio universalior—mensura absoluta totius creaturae est.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-09","CL_GGVC_34:GA-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-04","text":"Ruptura ordinis localis statum creaturae vere deformat, sed counter-ontologiam producere nequit, quia defectus intra causalitatem universalem privativus manet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-08","CL_GGVC_33:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_35:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-05","text":"Mediatio non evanescit cum medium corporale removetur; transit ad capacitatem recipientis, gradum cognitionis et destinationem voluntariam.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-09","CL_GGVC_34:GA-05","CL_GGVC_36:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-06","text":"Interiority nonautonoma libertatem moralem et secretum sustinet, quia donum originarium facultatem constituit sine necessitatione cuiusque actus vel publicitate cuiusque conceptus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-12","CL_GGVC_34:GA-10","CL_GGVC_36:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-07","text":"Consummatio mutat regimen vitae potius quam processum temporalem quantitative augeat aut ens destruat: generatio terminatur, participatio autem perficitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11","CL_GGVC_34:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-01","text":"Realitas creata est communicatio continua quae esse receptum in operationem propriam et operationem in communicationem boni convertit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-10","CL_GGVC_35:GA-01","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-02","text":"Tempus creaturae est maturatio positive constituta et continue conservata, cuius novitates sub forma stabili recipiuntur donec terminus beatificus genus vitae mutet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11","CL_GGVC_34:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-03","text":"Subiectum est nodus participativus et ianua selectiva: bonum recipit, corporaliter vel spiritualiter operatur, et proprios actus atque conceptus ordinat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-12","CL_GGVC_33:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_35:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-04","text":"Cognitio communicata in contentum verum, modum receptionis et ius accessus distinguitur; communitas veritatis igitur nec aequalitatem nec publicitatem totalem infert.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-12","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-07","CL_GGVC_36:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-05","text":"Auctoritas reclassificatur ut functio proportionata bono communi et canali operationis, non ut possessio totalis corporis, voluntatis aut cognitionis alterius.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-10","CL_GGVC_33:MME-01","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-06","text":"Methodus systematis ipsam ontologiam distributam efficit: oppositiones negat, ordines distinguit, operationes restituit et differentias sub causalitate communi reincludit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-09","CL_GGVC_33:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RLE-01","text":"Causa prima potentiam constituit → creatura bonum recipit → proprie operatur → bonum communicat → per causalitatem participatam ad primam bonitatem assimilatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_35:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RLE-02","text":"Contradictio apparens → distinctio graduum → restitutio operationis legitimae → reclassificatio defectus → reinclusio sub ordine universali.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RLE-03","text":"Finitudo → necessitas mediationis → dependentia stabilis → potentia agendi → nova communicatio quae ulteriores mediationes instituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:XD-02","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RLE-04","text":"Defectus localis → amissio boni particularis → impossibilitas exterioritatis ontologicae → manifestatio contingentiae et gratuitatis ordinis integri.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_35:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RLE-05","text":"Generatio → initium imperfectum → actus aetati congrui → maturatio et historia libera → confirmatio beatifica quae circuitum generativum claudit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_34:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RLE-06","text":"Attractor communis est immediatio ordinata: bonum primum intime causat, media proprie operantur, et omnis operatio secundum recipientem atque finem determinatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_35:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FGS-B01","text":"Esse participatum + natura finita → permanentia non autarchica + indigentia mediorum proportionatorum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-01","CL_GGVC_33:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FGS-B02","text":"Conservatio causae primae + causalitas secunda → operatio propria sine competitione causalium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-04","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FGS-B03","text":"Differentia recipientium + proportio finis → gradus perfectionis + distributio functionum et canalium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-03","CL_GGVC_36:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FGS-B04","text":"Operatio naturalis retenta + repugnantia destructiva exclusa → integritas corporalis, temporalis et intellectualis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-09","CL_GGVC_33:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FGS-B05","text":"Perfectio recepta + principium intrinsecum → actio propria + communicatio boni + accessus selectivus.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-10","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FGS-B06","text":"Finis extrinsecus + consummatio beatifica → terminatio regiminis generativi + perfectio participationis sine annihilatione.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:AFM-B01","text":"Natura finita et corporalis → apparens oppositio integritatis et indigentiae → restitutio corporis ut medii operationis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_34:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:AFM-B02","text":"Defectus apparens → separatio limitationis naturalis a corruptione → perfectio relativa aetati, statui et fini.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_33:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:AFM-B03","text":"Conservatio localis → causalitas continua essendi → gubernatio per causas secundas → operatio participata.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_35:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:AFM-B04","text":"Dependentia universalis → multiplicatio mediationum → interioritas et communicatio selectiva → consummatio terminalis ordinis creati.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_34:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-01","text":"Oppositio naturae et gratiae corruit; gratia fit modus superior quo natura, historia et media creata ordinantur sine substitutione.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:MME-01","CL_GGVC_35:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-02","text":"Perfectio ex plenitudine statica in trajectoriam relativam actuum, aetatum, statuum et consummationis reclassificatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_34:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-03","text":"Dependentia causalis ex relatione competitiva in condicionem positivae efficientiae creatae mutatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:MME-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-04","text":"Hierarchia ex praelatione totali in reticulum functionum inaequalium transit, ubi gradus cognitionis, motus voluntatis et directio locutionis non coincidunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:MME-03","CL_GGVC_36:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-05","text":"Spatium et tempus desinunt esse receptacula neutra: locus fit proportio status et finis, tempus autem duratio conservata et perfectibilis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:MME-01","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-06","text":"Communicatio ex simplici translatione contenti in compositionem principii veritatis, modi receptionis et destinationis voluntariae resolvitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:MME-04","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei integritatem corporis, progressionem generationis, habitationem paradisiacam, gubernationem universalem et communicationem spiritualem tamquam casus distinctos exhibet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_34:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis casus illos per participationem essendi, proportionem recipientis, causalitatem secundam et distributionem mediationum componit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus oppositiones simplices in discrimina ordinum, causarum, temporum et functionum convertit, ut interventio rectum gradum causalitatis petat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis realitatem ut participationem continue receptam, tempus vero ut maturationem conservatam et terminaliter transformabilem constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus propriam thesim performat: attributiones causales per negationes limitantur et postea in ordinem distributum recompinguntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-01","text":"Conservatio corporis per animam et donum supernaturale affirmatur, sed nexus inter virtutem naturalem, remedia materialia et influxum ontologicum non plene formalizatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:FL-01","CL_GGVC_35:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-02","text":"Iustitia originaria hereditario communicabilis et collatio divina immediata simul servantur, sed transitus a proprietate naturae speciei ad causam donationis fragilem commissuram relinquit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:FL-01","CL_GGVC_34:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-03","text":"Motio divina universalis cum imputabilitate personali et persuasione creata componitur, sed limes inter propositionem obiecti et inclinationem intrinsecam voluntatis manet subtilis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:FL-03","CL_GGVC_36:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-04","text":"Praetermissio ordinis naturalis admittitur, stabilitas autem hierarchiae spiritualis firmatur; ratio huius asymmetriae non omnino ex principio communi derivatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:FL-06","CL_GGVC_36:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-05","text":"Visio mutua substantiarum spiritualium cum secreto conceptus voluntarii componitur, sed distinctio inter praesentiam personae et accessum ad contentum incompleta manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:FL-05","CL_GGVC_36:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MP-01","text":"Conservatio mutatur a custodia contra corrumpens in dependentiam praesentem qua ipsum esse communicatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:MP-01","CL_GGVC_35:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MP-02","text":"Perfectio mutatur a plenitudine simultanea in ordinem actuum relativorum aetati, statui et capacitati.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:MP-02","CL_GGVC_34:BAS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MP-03","text":"Immediatio mutatur ab intima causalitate primi agentis in manifestationem sine instrumento corporeo et accessum non impeditum distantia.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:MP-01","CL_GGVC_36:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MP-04","text":"Hierarchia mutatur a serie scalari universali in reticulum canalium, quorum directio secundum illuminationem, persuasionem et locutionem variatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:MP-04","CL_GGVC_36:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicita est machina dependentiarum inclusarum, in qua fons primus potentias constituit, media eas exercent et canales diversos effectus distribuunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_36:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est simul nodus et ianua: perfectionem recipit et communicat, sed accessum ad actus vel conceptus secundum principium intrinsecum moderatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_36:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est maturatio conservata per quam novitas in structuram stabilem ingreditur, donec terminus beatificus ipsum regimen temporale transformat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_36:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_34:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga ab ordine, sed electio medii, proportionis et canalis congruentis inter receptionem, operationem, defectum et consummationem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_36:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata participationis, conservationis continuae, perfectionis relativae, causalitatis non competitivae, mediationis et finis extrinseci sufficiunt ad machinam huius metagregis restituendam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_36:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est coniunctio dependentiae et operationis propriae, quia eadem distinctio integritatem naturalem, gubernationem universalem et ministerium spirituale sustinet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_36:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinati manent mechanismus influxus supernaturalis in corpus, causalitas donationis hereditariae, limes persuasionis voluntatis et compatibilitas visionis spiritualis cum secreto.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_36:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat si progressionem a natura ordinata ad causalitatem essendi et inde ad mediationem selectivam retinet; singularitas historica, libertas divina et discontinuatio beatifica reductioni completae resistunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T174102074139Z_a3801207","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Ggvc L1 09","source_sha256":"463a9e34707197729643623ef514f668ea9f3671b8984cc7d07dbe41edced311","sha256":"2b6d97339881c82abb836103d063b5397a1bd9b23762468d9e17013e004a861e","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:41:02.075055+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how a finite and totally dependent creature retains integrity, development, freedom, and proper operation without causal autarchy or confusion of orders.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_33:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis maintains that divine causality does not substitute for created nature, but, by conserving, ordering, and applying it to act, establishes true secondary causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-02","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-03","text":"The argument analogically distributes the same form of governance across the body, maturation, cosmological order, and intellectual communication, while preserving differences of media and recipients.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_36:BAS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-04","text":"The movement descends from the integrity of finite operations to the conservation of being itself, then ascends again to mediation and the selective destination of effects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_36:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-05","text":"The progression is spiral and scalar: apparent opposition is distinguished into orders, legitimate operation is restored, local defect is reclassified, and the whole is reincluded under an extrinsic end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-06","text":"Evil and corruption do not constitute another nature, but diverse effects of a rupture of relations that nevertheless cannot depart from universal causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:XD-03","CL_GGVC_35:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-07","text":"Consummation is neither an indefinite prolongation of generative life nor a regression into nothingness, but a terminal change by which animal and temporal governance passes into another state.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BAS-08","text":"The closure is asymmetrical: the creature never possesses origin, permanence, or end from itself, but this very ordered dependence stabilizes its proper operation, interiority, and communication.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Created nature under an ordering gift remains the same nature; its integrity retains its operations and excludes only destructive incompatibility.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-02","CL_GGVC_35:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Participated being receives permanence from the continuous communication of the first cause, not from the creature's intrinsic sufficiency.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Primary causality and secondary causality are not competing univocal forces; the former sustains power and application, the latter operates properly.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-04","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Integrity is expanded from the absence of penal incompatibility into a dynamic structure of conservation, maturation, and ordered mediation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_34:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The perfection of a creature is relative to the capacity, age, state, office, and end of the recipient, not a univocal fullness immediately possessed.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Corporeality, time, maturation, and mediation are positive conditions of finite operation, although they can be subject to corruption and impediment.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-05","CL_GGVC_34:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The creature's power to act is increased through ordered dependence, because received perfection is converted into proper operation and the communication of good.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-10","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Defect is the privation of a particular order or the rupture of a due relation; non-being, however, constitutes no positive principle or rival order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-08","CL_GGVC_35:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Hierarchy changes from scalar dominion into a distribution of functions, degrees of reception, and channels of communication, without equality or total domination.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_36:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Essential good and the extrinsic end gather conservation, maturation, governance, and communication into one teleological order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-13","CL_GGVC_35:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Beatific consummation closes aversion and terminates generative animal life, but does not annihilate the creature or convert its finitude into autonomy.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GA-09","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Freedom, moral history, and the secrecy of a concept arise from an intrinsically constituted principle dependently established, not from the absence of a higher cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:XD-03","CL_GGVC_36:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-01","text":"Integrity retains its functional identity, but migrates from the order of body and appetite to temporal development, ontological permanence, and communicative operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_35:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-02","text":"Conservation changes from local resistance against corruption into the present communication of being itself, to which natural remedies are subordinated.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_35:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-03","text":"Immediacy retains its name, but is inverted from intimate divine causality into created manifestation without a sensible instrument and into the absence of local impediment.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_36:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-04","text":"Hierarchy passes from a chain of subordinations to a double topology, in which truth descends according to degree, while speech is directed in several directions.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_36:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-05","text":"Perfection recedes from the image of simultaneous fullness and becomes a relative measure of development, the recipient's state, and the term of life.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_34:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-06","text":"The body changes from an object to be governed into a medium of knowledge and maturation; where incorporeal communication is treated, the absence of body is not converted into universal transparency.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_36:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RMD-07","text":"Freedom returns not as insubordination, but as an intrinsic act that orders good, personal history, and communicative access within dependence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-06","CL_GGVC_36:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-01","text":"Physiological and geographical determinations and gradations of miracles remain local apparatuses; their particularity does not constitute a common axiom of this level.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_34:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_35:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_36:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-02","text":"The distinction between natural operation and corruption is amplified upward into a rule by which defect is explained from a disordered relation, not from the nature of a faculty.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_33:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-03","text":"Bodily conservation, secondary causality, and intellectual ministry are joined through the principle of mediation, whose efficacy is received from the order of the first cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_36:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-04","text":"The transition from bodily protection to dependence in being does not abolish local remedies, but places their force within a higher ontological causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_35:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-05","text":"The descent of illumination remains a local law of perfective communication; it is not universalized into every utterance, persuasion, or destination of a concept.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_36:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:CGD-06","text":"Lower explanations of sexual diversity through physiological causes are absorbed into the higher proposition concerning the distributed perfection of nature, not into a universal law of causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:CGD-03","CL_GGVC_34:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-01","text":"The system denies a perfection that evacuates body, sexuality, time, difference, or mediation as essential defects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_34:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-02","text":"The system denies that every natural limitation is guilt, punishment, or corruption, because initial imperfection can be proportionate to act and age.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_34:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-03","text":"The system denies divine and created causality as forces of the same order whose efficacy should diminish one another.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_35:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-04","text":"The system denies that the creature remains through itself after production, or receives divine origin while claiming conservation and end for itself.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_35:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-05","text":"The system denies that evil, contingency, or created will constitutes a real region outside the order of universal cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_35:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-06","text":"The system denies that the absence of body or place produces the necessary publicity of minds, because communication is still limited by reception and destination.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_36:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:GNX-07","text":"The system denies that the state of innocence, generative life, or created habitation is final consummation itself.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_34:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that a creature once produced can remain without the communication of being, for then conservation would be ontologically separated from origin.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_35:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that one measure of perfection suits all natures, ages, and intellects, because reception always follows the capacity of the recipient.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_36:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that proper operation and freedom require independent causality; otherwise total dependence would evacuate every secondary agent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that a created medium or secondary cause either confers independence or impedes divine intervention, because mediation itself is causally received.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that consummation is either the indefinite extension of generative life or the attraction of things toward nothingness.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_35:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-01","text":"Ontological exteriority from the universal cause is a radically inadmissible possibility, because it would not constitute another order but a privation of being.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-02","text":"A finite habitation that closes itself within the ultimate end is unavailable, since every created state is referred to extrinsic perfection.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:BSA-05","CL_GGVC_35:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-03","text":"Created immediacy that requires no measure of the recipient, medium of operation, or choice of access is unavailable.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:GA-08","CL_GGVC_36:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSA-04","text":"The system is ordered around the absence of an autonomous principle: neither nothingness nor a self-sufficient creature can become the source, end, or norm of the whole.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:BSA-03","CL_GGVC_36:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-01","text":"Integrity is dynamically sustained stability: finite nature is perfected through maturation, conservation, and ordering, not through immobility or the abolition of processes.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","CL_GGVC_33:GA-01","CL_GGVC_34:GA-02","CL_GGVC_35:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-02","text":"Ontological dependence is a generative principle of power to act, because the first cause sustains the faculty through which the creature communicates operation and good.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-07","CL_GGVC_35:GA-10","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-03","text":"Because perfection is contracted according to the recipient, no individual state—infancy, maturity, superiority, or more universal knowledge—is the absolute measure of the whole creature.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-09","CL_GGVC_34:GA-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-04","text":"The rupture of local order truly deforms the state of the creature, but cannot produce a counter-ontology, because defect remains privative within universal causality.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-08","CL_GGVC_33:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_35:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-05","text":"Mediation does not vanish when the bodily medium is removed; it passes to the capacity of the recipient, the degree of knowledge, and voluntary destination.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-09","CL_GGVC_34:GA-05","CL_GGVC_36:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-06","text":"Non-autonomous interiority sustains moral freedom and secrecy, because the originating gift constitutes the faculty without necessitating every act or publicizing every concept.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-12","CL_GGVC_34:GA-10","CL_GGVC_36:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:XD-07","text":"Consummation changes the governance of life rather than quantitatively increasing the temporal process or destroying the being: generation is terminated, but participation is perfected.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11","CL_GGVC_34:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-01","text":"Created reality is a continuous communication that converts received being into proper operation and operation into the communication of good.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-10","CL_GGVC_35:GA-01","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-02","text":"The creature's time is positively constituted and continuously conserved maturation, whose novelties are received under a stable form until the beatific term changes the kind of life.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-11","CL_GGVC_34:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-03","text":"The subject is a participatory node and a selective gate: it receives good, operates bodily or spiritually, and orders its own acts and concepts.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-12","CL_GGVC_33:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_35:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-04","text":"Communicated knowledge is distinguished into true content, mode of reception, and right of access; the community of truth therefore entails neither equality nor total publicity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-12","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-07","CL_GGVC_36:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-05","text":"Authority is reclassified as a function proportioned to the common good and the channel of operation, not as total possession of another's body, will, or knowledge.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-10","CL_GGVC_33:MME-01","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:BSD-06","text":"The method of the system enacts distributed ontology itself: it negates oppositions, distinguishes orders, restores operations, and reincludes differences under common causality.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L1_09:GA-09","CL_GGVC_33:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RLE-01","text":"The first cause constitutes power → the creature receives good → operates properly → communicates good → through participated causality is assimilated to the first goodness.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_35:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RLE-02","text":"Apparent contradiction → distinction of degrees → restoration of legitimate operation → reclassification of defect → reinclusion under universal order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RLE-03","text":"Finitude → necessity of mediation → stable dependence → power to act → new communication that establishes further mediations.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:XD-02","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RLE-04","text":"Local defect → loss of a particular good → impossibility of ontological exteriority → manifestation of the contingency and gratuity of integral order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_35:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RLE-05","text":"Generation → imperfect beginning → acts appropriate to age → maturation and free history → beatific confirmation that closes the generative circuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:RLE-01","CL_GGVC_34:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:RLE-06","text":"The common attractor is ordered immediacy: the first good causes intimately, media operate properly, and every operation is determined according to recipient and end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_35:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FGS-B01","text":"Participated being + finite nature → non-autarchic permanence + need for proportioned media.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-01","CL_GGVC_33:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FGS-B02","text":"Conservation by the first cause + secondary causality → proper operation without competition of causes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-04","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FGS-B03","text":"Difference of recipients + proportion of end → degrees of perfection + distribution of functions and channels.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-03","CL_GGVC_36:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FGS-B04","text":"Natural operation retained + destructive incompatibility excluded → bodily, temporal, and intellectual integrity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:GA-09","CL_GGVC_33:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FGS-B05","text":"Received perfection + intrinsic principle → proper action + communication of good + selective access.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:GA-10","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FGS-B06","text":"Extrinsic end + beatific consummation → termination of generative governance + perfection of participation without annihilation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:AFM-B01","text":"Finite and bodily nature → apparent opposition of integrity and need → restoration of the body as a medium of operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_34:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:AFM-B02","text":"Apparent defect → separation of natural limitation from corruption → perfection relative to age, state, and end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_33:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:AFM-B03","text":"Local conservation → continuous causality of being → governance through secondary causes → participated operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_35:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:AFM-B04","text":"Universal dependence → multiplication of mediations → interiority and selective communication → terminal consummation of created order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_34:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-01","text":"The opposition of nature and grace collapses; grace becomes the higher mode by which nature, history, and created media are ordered without substitution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:MME-01","CL_GGVC_35:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-02","text":"Perfection is reclassified from static fullness into a relative trajectory of acts, ages, states, and consummation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_34:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-03","text":"Causal dependence changes from a competitive relation into a condition of positive created efficacy.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:MME-01","CL_GGVC_36:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-04","text":"Hierarchy passes from total precedence into a network of unequal functions, where degrees of knowledge, movements of will, and directions of speech do not coincide.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:MME-03","CL_GGVC_36:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-05","text":"Space and time cease to be neutral receptacles: place becomes a proportion of state and end, while time becomes conserved and perfectible duration.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:MME-01","CL_GGVC_35:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MME-06","text":"Communication is resolved from a simple translation of content into a composition of the principle of truth, mode of reception, and voluntary destination.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:MME-04","CL_GGVC_36:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level presents bodily integrity, generative development, paradisal habitation, universal governance, and spiritual communication as distinct cases.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_34:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level composes those cases through participation in being, the proportion of the recipient, secondary causality, and the distribution of mediations.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level converts simple oppositions into distinctions of orders, causes, times, and functions, so that intervention seeks the proper level of causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level establishes reality as continuously received participation, and time as conserved and terminally transformable maturation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological level performs its own thesis: causal attributions are limited through negations and then recomposed into a distributed order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_36:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-01","text":"The conservation of the body through the soul and a supernatural gift is affirmed, but the nexus among natural power, material remedies, and ontological influx is not fully formalized.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:FL-01","CL_GGVC_35:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-02","text":"Hereditably communicable original justice and immediate divine bestowal are simultaneously maintained, but the transition from a property of the nature of the species to the cause of bestowal leaves a fragile seam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:FL-01","CL_GGVC_34:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-03","text":"Universal divine motion is composed with personal imputability and created persuasion, but the boundary between the presentation of an object and the intrinsic inclination of the will remains subtle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:FL-03","CL_GGVC_36:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-04","text":"The bypassing of natural order is admitted, while the stability of spiritual hierarchy is affirmed; the reason for this asymmetry is not wholly derived from a common principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:FL-06","CL_GGVC_36:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:FL-05","text":"Mutual vision of spiritual substances is composed with the secrecy of voluntary concepts, but the distinction between the presence of the person and access to content remains incomplete.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:FL-05","CL_GGVC_36:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MP-01","text":"Conservation changes from protection against what corrupts into present dependence by which being itself is communicated.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:MP-01","CL_GGVC_35:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MP-02","text":"Perfection changes from simultaneous fullness into an order of acts relative to age, state, and capacity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:MP-02","CL_GGVC_34:BAS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MP-03","text":"Immediacy changes from the intimate causality of the first agent into manifestation without a bodily instrument and access unimpeded by distance.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:MP-01","CL_GGVC_36:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MP-04","text":"Hierarchy changes from a universal scalar series into a network of channels whose direction varies according to illumination, persuasion, and speech.","refs":["CL_GGVC_36:MP-04","CL_GGVC_36:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-01","text":"Implicit reality is a machine of enclosed dependencies, in which the first source constitutes powers, media exercise them, and channels distribute diverse effects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_36:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-02","text":"The subject is at once a node and a gate: it receives and communicates perfection, but regulates access to acts or concepts according to an intrinsic principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_36:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-03","text":"Time is conserved maturation through which novelty enters into a stable structure, until the beatific term transforms temporal governance itself.","refs":["CL_GGVC_34:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_36:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_34:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is not escape from order, but the choice of a suitable medium, proportion, and channel between reception, operation, defect, and consummation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_36:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of participation, continuous conservation, relative perfection, non-competitive causality, mediation, and extrinsic end suffice to reconstruct the machinery of this meta-aggregate.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_36:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the conjunction of dependence and proper operation, because the same distinction sustains natural integrity, universal governance, and spiritual ministry.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_36:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remain the mechanism of supernatural influx into the body, the causality of hereditary bestowal, the boundary of persuasion of the will, and the compatibility of spiritual vision with secrecy.","refs":["CL_GGVC_33:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_36:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_09:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement if it retains the progression from ordered nature to the causality of being and thence to selective mediation; historical singularity, divine freedom, and beatific discontinuity resist complete reduction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_35:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_09@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_GGVC_L1_09:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Ggvc L1 09","context":"Chapters 97–108 · Prima pars · Quaestio 96 · Prima pars · Quaestio 97 · Prima pars · Quaestio 98 · Prima pars · Quaestio 99 · Prima pars · Quaestio 100 · Prima pars · Quaestio 101 · Prima pars · Quaestio 102 · Prima pars · Quaestio 103 · Prima pars · Quaestio 104 · Prima pars · Quaestio 105 · Prima pars · Quaestio 106 · Prima pars · Quaestio 107"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10","layer":"MC","title":"Ggvc L1 10","order":10,"parents":["CL_GGVC_37","CL_GGVC_38","CL_GGVC_39","CL_GGVC_40"],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_10@R001","sha256":"858893673e375ac714ea7fcfaf8d153479164f7d4af79342abd5a96c24165a38","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo impugnatio daemonica, gubernatio angelica, causalitas naturalis et compositio humana sub uno principatu divino vere agant, quin differentiae ontologicae, contingentia aut libertas dissolvantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_40:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis causalitatem stratificatam et non competitivam statuit: efficacia creata realis est quia a causa superiori fundatur, per media proportionata exercetur et terminis recipientis contrahitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:BAS-02","CL_GGVC_40:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit per distinctiones modorum essendi, operandi, recipiendi et ordinandi, quibus effectus inter principium, instrumentum, medium, recipiens et finem distribuitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_39:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_40:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-04","text":"Motus ab ordine hierarchico transit ad applicationem angelicam circa personam, inde ad limitationem influxuum daemonici et caelestis, ac demum ad constitutionem et propagationem compositi humani.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_38:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_39:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_40:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-05","text":"Progressus est recursivus, cumulativus et mutatione actus compositus: eadem mediatio redit ut hierarchia, missio, inclinatio, instrumentum et assimilatio, sed eius terminus singulis reditibus mutatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_38:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_39:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_40:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-06","text":"Hac magnitudine systema fit grammatica causalitatis finitae, cuius veritas non ex dominio totali, sed ex limitatione, proportione et officio determinato nascitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-12","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-07","text":"Anomaliae quae ordinem excedere videntur—malum, defectus, casus, prodigium et mutatio substantialis—reclassificantur ut probationes graduum causalium, non ut exterioritates absolutae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_38:BSA-05","CL_GGVC_39:BAS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-08","text":"Clausura est asymmetrica: nulla causa creata totum ordinem transcendit, dum Deus solus miraculum proprie efficit et principium intellectivum creat; causalitas naturalis tamen dispositionem realem praestat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-12","CL_GGVC_40:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Unitas totius ordinis fundatur in providentia divina, quae virtutes creatas non excludit sed intra unam gubernationem distribuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-01","CL_GGVC_38:GA-01","CL_GGVC_39:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Causalitas superior et inferior non competitive concurrunt: causa superior operationem inferioris constituit, ordinat aut applicat sine eius modi proprii absorptionem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:BAS-02","CL_GGVC_39:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Ambitus cuiusque causae determinatur ex modo essendi agentis, virtute, medio, obiecto et capacitate recipientis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-01","CL_GGVC_39:GA-03","CL_GGVC_38:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Mediatio ministerialis vel instrumentalis est causalitas vera, quia agens dependens potentiam recipientis ad actum ordinat neque in transmissionem passivam evacuatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_40:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Receptio est pars constitutiva causalitatis: influxus unus secundum dispositionem spiritualem, organicam, materialem aut voluntariam contrahitur et effectus diversos producit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-05","CL_GGVC_38:GA-03","CL_GGVC_40:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Principium intrinsecum principaliter actualizat, dum causa exterior proponit, disponit, applicat vel ministrat; haec distinctio cognitionem, voluntatem, generationem et nutritionem ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-02","CL_GGVC_40:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Voluntas creata inclinari, perturbari et persuaderi potest sine necessitatione consensus; libertas tamen auxilium divinum non superfluum reddit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-05","CL_GGVC_39:GA-08","CL_GGVC_39:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Hierarchia ex scala dignitatum in distributionem cognitionis, imperii, executionis et amplitudinis obiecti mutatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-02","CL_GGVC_38:GA-07","CL_GGVC_38:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Unus actus pluribus causis secundum respectus diversos vere attribui potest: agenti proximo secundum proprium modum, recipienti secundum dispositionem et causae superiori secundum ordinationem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-15","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Malum, casus et defectus ordinem autonomum non constituunt; proprios modos locales retinent dum sub intentione vel providentia superiori ordinantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-08","CL_GGVC_39:GA-02","CL_GGVC_39:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Effectus sensibilis causam suam non manifestat exhaustive; cognitio causalis requirit reconstructionem gradus ontologici, medii, finis et competentiae agentis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-10","CL_GGVC_38:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Nulla causa creata totum ordinem naturae transcendit: causalitas spiritualis, caelestis et corporalis proprios terminos habet, dum miraculum proprie dictum supra totum ordinem activum creatum ponitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-11","CL_GGVC_39:GA-13","CL_GGVC_40:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Productio principii intellectivi est actio divina immediata, sed eius unio cum homine dispositionem corporis naturaliter productam praesupponit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-06","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14","text":"[Mutating] Identitas creata potest sub mutatione status, operationis aut materiae persistere, quia continuitas formaliter ordinata non idem est ac immobilitas materialis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_40:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-15","text":"[Attractor] Mediatio limitata est attractor totius systematis: quo accuratius potentia finita ab autonomia, immedietate et necessitatione excluditur, eo distinctius eius proprium agere constituitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_39:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-01","text":"Ordo identitatem functionalem servat, sed migrat a gradatione angelica ad providentiam personalem, ad ordinationem causarum secundarum et tandem ad constitutionem compositi humani.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_38:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_39:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-02","text":"Hierarchia mutatur ex divisione ordinum in catena functionum, ac deinde absorbetur in distributionem generalem causarum principalium, ministerialium et instrumentalium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_38:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-03","text":"Mediatio redit ut administratio, missio, causalitas secundaria et delegatio instrumentalis; identitas eius est ordinare potentiam alterius, non eundem effectum semper producere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_38:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_39:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_40:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-04","text":"Receptio a modo participationis hierarchicae migrat ad phantasmata et libertatem hominis, deinde ad dispositionem materiae et assimilationem viventis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-02","CL_GGVC_38:GA-03","CL_GGVC_39:GA-05","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-05","text":"Anomalum mutatur ex apparentia ordinem excedente in variationem particularem, postea in terminum metaphysicum miraculi et denique in indicium quod causam suam visibiliter non exhibet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_38:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_39:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-06","text":"Fatum inversionem terminalem patitur: ut determinismus astralis negatur, sed ut ordo relationis inter providentiam et causas secundas restituitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_39:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-07","text":"Corpus mutatur ab obiecto administrationis spiritualis et termino localis applicationis in principium constitutivum compositi, cuius materia disponitur, assimilatur et substituitur sub forma.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_38:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_40:GA-07","CL_GGVC_40:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-01","text":"Collocationes nominum et numeri ordinum manent taxonomiae inferiores; ad gradum praesentem ascendit principium quod nomen et gradus munus causale vel modum receptionis significant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_38:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-02","text":"Spiritus, humores, phantasmata et organa sunt mechanismi locales influxus angelici; synthesis retinet tantum necessitatem mediationis organicae et disproportionem inter dispositionem corporalem et actum interiorem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_38:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-03","text":"Invidia daemonis, imitationes ministerii et praedictio astrologica manent casus particulares; sursum persistunt limites agentis intellectualis et influxus caelestis circa voluntatem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_39:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-04","text":"Successio formarum embryonalium et processus physiologici seminis manent machinae inferiores; praesentem structuram gubernant solum ut limina inter dispositionem materialem, formam ultimam et identitatem continuam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-05","text":"Distinctiones naturae et operationis, principalis et instrumentalis, directi et indirecti, per se et per accidens ampliantur in grammaticam communem non-aequivalentiae modorum causalium.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_39:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-06","text":"Missio, fatum et semen sunt termini-commissurae: eventum localem in relationem durabilem, ordinem secundarium aut processum generativum convertunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_39:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_40:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-07","text":"Nomenclaturae, motus physiologici, rationes seminales et materia genealogica fixa, munere explicativo peracto, in structuram abstractiorem mediationis, receptionis et identitatis formalis absorbentur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_38:RMD-09","CL_GGVC_40:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-01","text":"Systema excludit planum causale homogeneum in quo Deus, spiritus creati, voluntas, corpora et materia eodem modo agant.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_39:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-02","text":"Nulla potestas creata potest esse universalis, immediata et independens respectu totius ordinis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_39:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-03","text":"Causalitas divina et creata neque commutabiles neque aemulae sunt; principium intrinsecum, executio ministerialis et dispositio naturalis diversa officia retinent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_40:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-04","text":"Universalitas divina causas secundas non reddit superfluas, sicut libertas creata non requirit absentiam passionum, persuasionum aut auxilii.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_39:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-05","text":"Nulla absentia localis, culpa, contingentia aut corruptio regionem absolute extra providentiam constituit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GNX-07","CL_GGVC_39:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-06","text":"Insolentia, admiratio, sanctitas agentis aut spectaculum effectus non sufficiunt ad probandum miraculum vel competentiam causae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GNX-06","CL_GGVC_39:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_40:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-07","text":"Homo neque ad animam separatam neque ad materiam numerice immobilem reducitur; utraque reductio unitatem processualem compositi destruit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_40:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet causam veram aut effectum totaliter producere aut nihil causare; aliter ministerium, instrumentum et dispositio causalitatem amitterent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_38:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet dependentiam causalem necessitatem effectus inferre aut causam superiorem agentiam inferioris evacuare.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_39:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet ordinem realem communionem intentionis bonae semper requirere; actio mala potest ordinari sine approbatione finis agentis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_39:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet omnem continuitatem temporalem materia immutata niti; identitas formalis potest per substitutionem materiae et mutationem operationis persistere.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:DBN-04","CL_GGVC_40:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet cognitionem creatam totum ordinem comprehendere aut concordiam voluntatum aequalitatem cognitionis postulare.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_38:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-01","text":"Exterioritas ultima a providentia ontologice inadmissibilis est: nulla creatura voluntate, casu aut potestate extra ordinem originarium constitui potest.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_38:BSA-05","CL_GGVC_39:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-02","text":"Immedietas finita est absentia circa quam systema ordinatur: nulla causa creata iurisdictionem universalem et immediatam in omnes ordines possidet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_39:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-03","text":"Inadmissibilis est creatura quae principium intrinsecum boni, intellectus et formae suae totaliter producat atque se causaliter fundet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_40:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-04","text":"Systema excludit necessitatem impersonalem et transparentiam eventus ut principia ultima; neque fatum autonomum neque effectus visibilis ordinem causalitatis exhaustive manifestat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:BSA-05","CL_GGVC_39:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-01","text":"Gubernatio angelica et causalitas humana eandem formam distributam participant: causa exterior ordinat potentiam, sed actus proprius ab intrinseco principio recipientis elicitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-02","CL_GGVC_40:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-02","text":"Receptio coniungit hierarchiam, libertatem et transformationem materialem: influxus superior non transit intactus, sed secundum capacitatem participantis in actum proprium convertitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-02","CL_GGVC_39:GA-05","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-03","text":"Limites potestatis angelicae, daemonicae, caelestis et corporalis cumulantur in exclusionem causae creatae quae voluntatem, formam immaterialem aut totum ordinem immediate determinet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-11","CL_GGVC_39:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_40:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-04","text":"Actus malus potest simul ex natura bona procedere, intentione agentis perversus esse et ad finem superiorem ordinari, quin imputabilitas aut distinctio intentionum deleatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:XD-03","CL_GGVC_39:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-05","text":"Miraculum et creatio animae intellectivae duas clausuras divinae immedietatis constituunt: utrumque virtutem activam creatam excedit, sed creatio animae dispositionem naturalem corporis non evacuat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-12","CL_GGVC_40:GA-06","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-06","text":"Corpus duplicem statum obtinet: est terminus gubernationis spiritualis, sed etiam co-principium intrinsecum actionis humanae; ideo administratio exterior ab informatione substantiali distingui debet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_40:GA-07","CL_GGVC_40:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-07","text":"Tempus systematis componitur ex mutatione status, novitate applicationis, mobilitate causarum et processu generationis; haec tempora diversa sub ordine persistente nidificantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_38:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-08","text":"Discernimentum causale ab effectu visibili regreditur ad ordinem naturae, dispositionem recipientis, finem agentis et competentiam ontologicam causae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_39:XD-08","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-01","text":"Ontologia emergens primatum relationis ordinatae tribuit: ens creatum non ex sola substantia, sed etiam ex modo participationis, receptionis et coordinationis causarum intelligitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-02","text":"Causa reclassificatur ut locus attributionum inaequalium: producere, disponere, applicare, permittere, ordinare et recipere sunt operationes reales sed non commutabiles.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_40:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-03","text":"Subiectum humanum fit persona incorporata et membrana selectiva: influxibus patet, consensum gubernat et identitatem formalem per fluxum materiae conservat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_40:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-04","text":"Tempus non est series homogena, sed compositio durationis officii, reditus temptationis, contingentiae mobilium causarum, saltus substantialium et continuitatis materialiter fluentis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-05","text":"Epistemologia systematis est constitutive indirecta: effectus cognoscitur ante causam, causa per distinctiones restituitur, et comprehensio totalis ordinis creaturae indisponibilis manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-06","text":"Auctoritas et potentia agendi reclassificantur ut officia ordinandi condiciones actionis alterius, non ut possessio totalis effectus vel dominium ontologicum super recipientem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_38:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-07","text":"Transcendentia divina manifestatur non solum per excessum supra naturam, sed per capacitatem fundandi causas secundas, contingentiam et libertatem sine destructione earum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-01","text":"Providentia universalis → distributio per causas superiores → applicatio particularis → receptio contracta → effectus variabilis → relatio effectus ad ordinem superiorem constituit circuitum principalem.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-01","CL_GGVC_39:GA-04","CL_GGVC_39:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-02","text":"Praetensio potestatis → examen modi essendi → distinctio inclinationis a necessitatione → exclusio exorbitantiae → restitutio causalitatis finitae constituit circuitum limitationis positivae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B05","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-03","text":"Natura bona → intentio perversa → actio nociva → permissio vel ordinatio → bonum superius sine consecratione mali constituit circuitum recaptationis providentialis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_38:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_39:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-04","text":"Effectus admirabilis → suspensio attributionis → examen naturae, recipientis, finis et competentiae → reclassificatio causae constituit circuitum epistemicum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_39:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_40:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-05","text":"Alimentum → assimilatio → semen → dispositio embryonis → creatio principii intellectivi → novum vivens → nutritio iterata constituit circuitum generationis et conservationis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-06","text":"Attractor totius structurae est mediatio ordinata sine necessitatione: omnis causa creata eo distinctior fit quo eius modus, medium et terminus accuratius definiuntur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_39:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-07","text":"Terminus operationis localis non machinam simpliciter delet: ministerium mutatur in conservationem, officium viae in relationem terminalem, et causalitas naturalis ad limen actionis divinae perducitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_38:RLE-07","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FGS-B01","text":"Differentia ontologica agentis, patientis et recipientis ponitur ante attributionem effectus, quia gradus essendi modum operationis definit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B01","CL_GGVC_39:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FGS-B02","text":"Modus essendi + virtus + obiectum + medium + capacitas recipientis → ambitus causalis et terminus competentiae agentis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-01","CL_GGVC_38:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FGS-B03","text":"Providentia universalis → causae superiores → media secundaria → applicatio particularis constituit formam ordinariam executionis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-01","CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FGS-B04","text":"Influxus receptus → contractio secundum dispositionem → actualizatio principii intrinseci → effectus diversificatus, resistibilis aut impedibilis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-05","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FGS-B05","text":"Effectus singularis → attributio pluribus causis secundum respectus diversos → conservatio agentiae localis et ordinationis universalis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-15","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FGS-B06","text":"Anomalia apparens → examen ordinis naturae → determinatio termini creati → miraculum aut creatio ut clausura divinae immedietatis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:XD-08","CL_GGVC_39:GA-13","CL_GGVC_40:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FGS-B07","text":"Limitationes cumulatae → exclusio autonomiae et dominationis totalis → causalitas creata proportionata → unitas ordinis sine confusione causarum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-12","CL_GGVC_40:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:AFM-B01","text":"Unitas principii → gradatio hierarchica → applicatio angelica ad personam → limitatio influxuum daemonici et caelestis → ordinatio causarum secundarum → constitutio compositi humani → conservatio et propagatio per media naturalia.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_38:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_39:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_40:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:AFM-B02","text":"Praetensio efficaciae → distinctio ordinum → examen recipientis et medii → exclusio immedietatis finitae → reattributio effectus → restitutio agentis creati intra providentiam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_39:XD-08","CL_GGVC_38:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-01","text":"Ager causalitatis ex plano homogeno in matricem agentium, instrumentorum, recipientium et respectuum reconfiguratur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:MME-01","CL_GGVC_40:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-02","text":"Hierarchia ex scala nominum in architecturam modorum receptionis, functionum et ambituum obiecti transfertur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MME-01","CL_GGVC_38:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-03","text":"Libertas et contingentia ex immunitate contra influxum in modos recipiendi causalitatem sine determinatione necessaria reclassificantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:MME-05","CL_GGVC_39:XD-10","CL_GGVC_39:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-04","text":"Miraculum ex admiratione phaenomeni in terminum ontologicum inter totum ordinem activum creatum et operationem divinam convertitur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MME-05","CL_GGVC_39:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-05","text":"Identitas humana a materia fixa vel anima solitaria ad continuitatem formae, operationis et compositionis processualis transfertur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_40:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-06","text":"Lectio causalis fit regressiva et differentialiter reconstructiva: apparentia, nomen et correlatio cedunt examini modi essendi, finis, medii et recipientis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MME-06","CL_GGVC_39:MME-07","CL_GGVC_40:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-07","text":"Malum ex principio rivalis ordinis in actum localiter imputabilem sed providentialiter ordinabilem reclassificatur, unde multiplicatio causarum responsabilitatem non dissolvit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MME-03","CL_GGVC_38:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei apparent hierarchiae angelicae, custodia, tentationes, influxus caelestes, miracula, doctrina, generatio, nutritio et identitas corporis viventis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali haec obiecta uniuntur per causalitatem distributam, applicationem virtutis, instrumentalitatem, dispositionem recipientis et distinctionem principii intrinseci ab agente exteriori.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico quaestio ab existentia nuda potentiae ad artem discernendi modum, amplitudinem, terminum et respectum causalitatis transfertur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est ordo causarum finitarum sub providentia, in quo formae, relationes et officia persistunt dum operationes, materia et status mutantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo distinctio iterata materiam dispersam ordinat, limites distribuit et ipsum ordinem causalitatis quem asserit formaliter efficit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-01","text":"Deus voluntatem immutare potest sine abolitione libertatis, sed mechanismus huius causalitatis immediate divinae assumitur potius quam explicatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-02","text":"Providentia malum ordinat sine intentionis malae appropriatione, sed ratio permissionis particularis et commissura inter ordinationem atque imputabilitatem incompletae manent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:FL-03","CL_GGVC_39:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-03","text":"Actio angelica circa corpus et praesentia operativa formaliter distinguuntur ab informatione substantiali et praesentia locali, sed mechanismus conversionis dispositionis organicae in actum particularem non plene determinatur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:FL-01","CL_GGVC_38:FL-01","CL_GGVC_38:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-04","text":"Universalitas custodiae cum inaequalitate effectuum componitur, sed criterium internum quo permissio iusta ab inefficacia apparente discernatur indisponibile manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-05","text":"Homo naturaliter hominem generat, sed principium intellectivum non producit; unitas generationis pendet a coordinatione dispositionis naturalis cum termino creato cuius nexus exactus indeterminatus manet.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:FL-03","CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-06","text":"Embryo per successiones substantiales progreditur, corpus autem formatum per substitutionem materiae idem manet; limes inter utramque continuitatem est necessarius sed fragilis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MP-01","text":"Hierarchia mutatur ex taxonomia graduum in formam generalem distributionis causalitatum, functionum et ambituum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MP-01","CL_GGVC_38:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MP-02","text":"Illuminatio et praesentia mutantur ex communicatione localiter imaginata in operationem formaliter definitam per finem, applicationem virtutis et effectum recipientis.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MP-02","CL_GGVC_38:MP-01","CL_GGVC_38:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MP-03","text":"Fatum patitur inversionem terminalem: necessitas astralis excluditur, sed nomen recipitur pro ordine derivato causarum secundarum.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MP-04","text":"Forma mutatur ex termino successionis substantialis in principium identitatis sub fluxu materiae; mutatio magnitudinis inter generationem et nutritionem eius functionem reordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:MP-03","CL_GGVC_40:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est reticulum nidificatum et machina transformationum inclusarum, in qua vis descendens per media contrahitur et effectus ad unum principium reordinantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_38:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_39:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_40:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum creatum est simul nodus receptivus, membrana selectiva et commissura composita: influxum suscipit, consensum gubernat et unitatem sub mutatione servat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_38:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_39:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_40:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est series applicationum et mutationum intra mobilitatem causarum creatarum: saltus formarum, durationes officiorum, reditus inclinationum et fluxus materiae inter se nidificantur.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_38:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_39:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_40:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga a causalitate, sed discernimentum influxuum, moderatio inclinationum et electio mediorum proportionatorum intra ordinem quem nulla creatura totaliter excedit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_38:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_39:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_40:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de providentia universali, causalitate stratificata, receptione proportionata, mediatione reali, libertate non necessitata et limite creato sufficiunt ad machinam principalem huius metagregis restituendam.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est distributio effectus inter causas inaequales, quia eadem distinctio hierarchiam, custodiam, providentiam, libertatem, generationem et nutritionem ordinat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent mechanismus actionis spiritualis circa corpus, causalitas divina circa voluntatem, criterium permissionis particularis et nexus inter ultimam dispositionem corporis atque creationem animae.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B04","text":"Cohaerentia nititur applicatione recursiva paucarum distinctionum et transformatione terminorum inter campos; haec compressio generativa est, sed mechanismos locales et limina ontologica non exhaustive demonstrat.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat si ordinem hierarchicum per applicationem particularem et causalitatem secundariam ad compositionem humanam deducit, dum taxonomiae, mechanismi physiologici et limina generationis reductioni integrae resistunt.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B06","CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B06","CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T174145231249Z_1893e0f7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Ggvc L1 10","source_sha256":"858893673e375ac714ea7fcfaf8d153479164f7d4af79342abd5a96c24165a38","sha256":"02ca9da903c25c99c08435c4481cb7b7f68a84af238cfa2629c3ec14ba97f0a3","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:41:45.232248+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how demonic assault, angelic governance, natural causality, and human composition truly act under one divine principate, without ontological differences, contingency, or freedom being dissolved.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:BAS-01","CL_GGVC_40:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis establishes stratified and non-competitive causality: created efficacy is real because it is grounded by a higher cause, exercised through proportionate means, and contracted by the limits of the recipient.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:BAS-02","CL_GGVC_40:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-03","text":"The argument proceeds through distinctions of modes of being, operating, receiving, and ordering, by which effects are distributed among principle, instrument, medium, recipient, and end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_39:BAS-03","CL_GGVC_40:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-04","text":"The movement passes from hierarchical order to angelic application concerning the person, thence to the limitation of demonic and celestial influences, and finally to the constitution and propagation of the human composite.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_38:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_39:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_40:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-05","text":"The progression is recursive, cumulative, and composed through mutation of act: the same mediation returns as hierarchy, mission, inclination, instrument, and assimilation, but its terminus changes with each return.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_38:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_39:BAS-05","CL_GGVC_40:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-06","text":"At this scale the system becomes a grammar of finite causality, whose truth arises not from total dominion, but from limitation, proportion, and determinate office.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-12","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-07","text":"Anomalies that seem to exceed order—evil, defect, chance, prodigy, and substantial change—are reclassified as tests of causal degrees, not as absolute exteriorities.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_38:BSA-05","CL_GGVC_39:BAS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BAS-08","text":"The closure is asymmetric: no created cause transcends the whole order, whereas God alone properly effects miracle and creates the intellectual principle; natural causality nevertheless provides real disposition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-12","CL_GGVC_40:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] The unity of the whole order is grounded in divine providence, which does not exclude created powers but distributes them within one governance.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-01","CL_GGVC_38:GA-01","CL_GGVC_39:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Higher and lower causality concur non-competitively: the higher cause constitutes, orders, or applies the operation of the lower without absorbing its proper mode.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:BAS-02","CL_GGVC_39:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] The scope of each cause is determined from the agent's mode of being, power, medium, object, and the capacity of the recipient.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-01","CL_GGVC_39:GA-03","CL_GGVC_38:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Ministerial or instrumental mediation is true causality, because a dependent agent orders the recipient's power toward act and is not emptied into passive transmission.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_40:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Reception is a constitutive part of causality: a single influx is contracted according to spiritual, organic, material, or voluntary disposition and produces diverse effects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-05","CL_GGVC_38:GA-03","CL_GGVC_40:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] An intrinsic principle actualizes principally, whereas an exterior cause proposes, disposes, applies, or ministers; this distinction orders cognition, will, generation, and nutrition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-02","CL_GGVC_40:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Created will can be inclined, disturbed, and persuaded without the necessity of consent; freedom nevertheless does not render divine aid superfluous.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-05","CL_GGVC_39:GA-08","CL_GGVC_39:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Hierarchy changes from a scale of dignities into a distribution of cognition, command, execution, and scope of object.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-02","CL_GGVC_38:GA-07","CL_GGVC_38:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] One act can truly be attributed to several causes according to diverse respects: to the proximate agent according to its proper mode, to the recipient according to disposition, and to the higher cause according to ordering.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-15","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Evil, chance, and defect do not constitute an autonomous order; they retain their proper local modes while being ordered under higher intention or providence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-08","CL_GGVC_39:GA-02","CL_GGVC_39:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] A sensible effect does not manifest its cause exhaustively; causal knowledge requires reconstruction of the ontological degree, medium, end, and competence of the agent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-10","CL_GGVC_38:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] No created cause transcends the whole order of nature: spiritual, celestial, and bodily causality have their proper limits, whereas miracle properly so called is placed above the whole created active order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-11","CL_GGVC_39:GA-13","CL_GGVC_40:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] The production of the intellectual principle is an immediate divine action, but its union with the human being presupposes a naturally produced disposition of the body.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-06","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14","text":"[Mutating] Created identity can persist under change of state, operation, or matter, because formally ordered continuity is not the same as material immobility.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_40:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-15","text":"[Attractor] Limited mediation is the attractor of the whole system: the more precisely finite power is excluded from autonomy, immediacy, and necessitation, the more distinctly its proper acting is constituted.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_39:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-01","text":"Order preserves functional identity, but migrates from angelic gradation to personal providence, to the ordering of secondary causes, and finally to the constitution of the human composite.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_38:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_39:RMD-01","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-02","text":"Hierarchy changes from the division of orders into a chain of functions, and is then absorbed into the general distribution of principal, ministerial, and instrumental causes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RMD-02","CL_GGVC_38:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-03","text":"Mediation returns as administration, mission, secondary causality, and instrumental delegation; its identity is to order another's power, not always to produce the same effect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_38:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_39:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_40:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-04","text":"Reception migrates from the mode of hierarchical participation to phantasms and human freedom, then to the disposition of matter and the assimilation of the living being.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-02","CL_GGVC_38:GA-03","CL_GGVC_39:GA-05","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-05","text":"The anomalous changes from an appearance exceeding order into a particular variation, later into the metaphysical limit of miracle, and finally into an indication that does not visibly exhibit its cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_38:CGD-07","CL_GGVC_39:RMD-04","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-06","text":"Fate undergoes a terminal inversion: it is denied as astral determinism, but restored as an order of relation between providence and secondary causes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:RMD-07","CL_GGVC_39:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RMD-07","text":"The body changes from an object of spiritual administration and terminus of local application into a constitutive principle of the composite, whose matter is disposed, assimilated, and replaced under form.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_38:RMD-05","CL_GGVC_40:GA-07","CL_GGVC_40:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-01","text":"Arrangements of names and the number of orders remain lower taxonomies; the principle rises to the present level that name and degree signify a causal function or mode of reception.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_38:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-02","text":"Spirits, humors, phantasms, and organs are local mechanisms of angelic influx; the synthesis retains only the necessity of organic mediation and the disproportion between bodily disposition and interior act.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_38:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-03","text":"The demon's envy, imitations of ministry, and astrological prediction remain particular cases; the limits of the intellectual agent and of celestial influx concerning the will persist at the higher level.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:CGD-01","CL_GGVC_39:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-04","text":"The succession of embryonic forms and physiological processes of semen remain lower mechanisms; they govern the present structure only as thresholds between material disposition, ultimate form, and continuous identity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:CGD-02","CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-05","text":"The distinctions of nature and operation, principal and instrumental, direct and indirect, per se and per accidens are expanded into a common grammar of the non-equivalence of causal modes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_39:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-06","text":"Mission, fate, and semen are joint-terms: they convert a local event into a durable relation, a secondary order, or a generative process.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:CGD-05","CL_GGVC_39:CGD-04","CL_GGVC_40:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:CGD-07","text":"Nomenclatures, physiological motions, seminal accounts, and fixed genealogical matter, once their explanatory function has been performed, are absorbed into the more abstract structure of mediation, reception, and formal identity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RMD-08","CL_GGVC_38:RMD-09","CL_GGVC_40:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes a homogeneous causal plane in which God, created spirits, will, bodies, and matter act in the same manner.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GNX-02","CL_GGVC_39:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-02","text":"No created power can be universal, immediate, and independent with respect to the whole order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_39:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-03","text":"Divine and created causality are neither interchangeable nor rival; intrinsic principle, ministerial execution, and natural disposition retain distinct offices.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GNX-01","CL_GGVC_40:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-04","text":"Divine universality does not render secondary causes superfluous, just as created freedom does not require the absence of passions, persuasions, or aid.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_39:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-05","text":"No local absence, fault, contingency, or corruption constitutes a region absolutely outside providence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GNX-07","CL_GGVC_39:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-06","text":"Unusualness, wonder, the sanctity of an agent, or the spectacle of an effect do not suffice to prove a miracle or the competence of a cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GNX-06","CL_GGVC_39:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_40:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:GNX-07","text":"The human being is reduced neither to a separate soul nor to numerically immobile matter; either reduction destroys the processual unity of the composite.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:DBN-05","CL_GGVC_40:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that a true cause either produces an effect totally or causes nothing; otherwise ministry, instrument, and disposition would lose causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_38:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that causal dependence entails the necessity of an effect or that a higher cause empties the agency of the lower.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:DBN-01","CL_GGVC_39:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that real order always requires communion in good intention; evil action can be ordered without approval of the agent's end.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:DBN-02","CL_GGVC_39:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that all temporal continuity rests upon unchanged matter; formal identity can persist through replacement of matter and change of operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:DBN-04","CL_GGVC_40:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that created knowledge comprehends the whole order or that concord of wills demands equality of knowledge.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BSA-04","CL_GGVC_38:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-01","text":"Ultimate exteriority to providence is ontologically inadmissible: no creature can be constituted outside the originating order by will, chance, or power.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_38:BSA-05","CL_GGVC_39:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-02","text":"Finite immediacy is the absence around which the system is ordered: no created cause possesses universal and immediate jurisdiction over all orders.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:BSA-02","CL_GGVC_39:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-03","text":"A creature that totally produces the intrinsic principle of its own good, intellect, and form and grounds itself causally is inadmissible.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:BSA-01","CL_GGVC_40:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSA-04","text":"The system excludes impersonal necessity and the transparency of event as ultimate principles; neither autonomous fate nor visible effect exhaustively manifests the order of causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:BSA-05","CL_GGVC_39:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-01","text":"Angelic governance and human causality share the same distributed form: an exterior cause orders power, but the proper act is elicited by the intrinsic principle of the recipient.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-02","CL_GGVC_40:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-02","text":"Reception joins hierarchy, freedom, and material transformation: higher influx does not pass through intact, but is converted into a proper act according to the participant's capacity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-02","CL_GGVC_39:GA-05","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-03","text":"The limits of angelic, demonic, celestial, and bodily power accumulate into the exclusion of a created cause that immediately determines will, immaterial form, or the whole order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-11","CL_GGVC_39:GNX-03","CL_GGVC_40:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-04","text":"An evil act can simultaneously proceed from a good nature, be perverse in the agent's intention, and be ordered to a higher end, without imputability or the distinction of intentions being erased.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:XD-03","CL_GGVC_39:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-05","text":"Miracle and the creation of the intellectual soul constitute two closures of divine immediacy: each exceeds created active power, but the creation of the soul does not empty the body's natural disposition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GA-12","CL_GGVC_40:GA-06","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-06","text":"The body has a double status: it is the terminus of spiritual governance, but also an intrinsic co-principle of human action; therefore exterior administration must be distinguished from substantial informing.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:GNX-05","CL_GGVC_40:GA-07","CL_GGVC_40:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-07","text":"The system's time is composed of change of state, novelty of application, mobility of causes, and the process of generation; these diverse times are nested under persisting order.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_38:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:XD-08","text":"Causal discernment regresses from visible effect to the order of nature, the recipient's disposition, the agent's end, and the ontological competence of the cause.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_39:XD-08","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L1_10:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-01","text":"The emerging ontology accords primacy to ordered relation: a created being is understood not from substance alone, but also from the mode of participation, reception, and coordination of causes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-02","text":"Cause is reclassified as a site of unequal attributions: producing, disposing, applying, permitting, ordering, and receiving are real but non-interchangeable operations.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:BSD-01","CL_GGVC_40:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-03","text":"The human subject becomes an embodied person and a selective membrane: it is open to influxes, governs consent, and preserves formal identity through the flux of matter.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-02","CL_GGVC_40:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-04","text":"Time is not a homogeneous series, but a composition of the duration of office, the return of temptation, the contingency of mobile causes, substantial leaps, and materially flowing continuity.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-03","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-05","text":"The system's epistemology is constitutively indirect: effect is known before cause, cause is restored through distinctions, and total comprehension of the order remains unavailable to the creature.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-06","text":"Authority and the power to act are reclassified as offices of ordering the conditions of another's action, not as possession of the total effect or ontological dominion over the recipient.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_38:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:BSD-07","text":"Divine transcendence is manifested not only through excess above nature, but through the capacity to ground secondary causes, contingency, and freedom without destroying them.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:BSD-05","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-01","text":"Universal providence → distribution through higher causes → particular application → contracted reception → variable effect → relation of effect to higher order constitutes the principal circuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-01","CL_GGVC_39:GA-04","CL_GGVC_39:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-02","text":"Claim of power → examination of mode of being → distinction of inclination from necessitation → exclusion of exorbitance → restoration of finite causality constitutes the circuit of positive limitation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B05","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-03","text":"Good nature → perverse intention → harmful action → permission or ordering → higher good without consecration of evil constitutes the circuit of providential recapture.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RLE-03","CL_GGVC_38:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_39:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-04","text":"Wondrous effect → suspension of attribution → examination of nature, recipient, end, and competence → reclassification of cause constitutes the epistemic circuit.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RLE-04","CL_GGVC_39:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_40:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-05","text":"Nourishment → assimilation → semen → disposition of the embryo → creation of the intellectual principle → new living being → repeated nutrition constitutes the circuit of generation and preservation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:RLE-02","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-06","text":"The attractor of the whole structure is ordered mediation without necessitation: every created cause becomes more distinct insofar as its mode, medium, and terminus are defined more precisely.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_39:RLE-06","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:RLE-07","text":"The terminus of local operation does not simply destroy the mechanism: ministry changes into preservation, the office of the way into terminal relation, and natural causality is brought to the threshold of divine action.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_38:RLE-07","CL_GGVC_40:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FGS-B01","text":"The ontological difference of agent, patient, and recipient is posited before the attribution of effect, because degree of being defines the mode of operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B01","CL_GGVC_39:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FGS-B02","text":"Mode of being + power + object + medium + capacity of recipient → causal scope and limit of the agent's competence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:GA-01","CL_GGVC_38:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FGS-B03","text":"Universal providence → higher causes → secondary means → particular application constitutes the ordinary form of execution.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-01","CL_GGVC_39:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FGS-B04","text":"Received influx → contraction according to disposition → actualization of intrinsic principle → diversified, resistible, or impedible effect.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-05","CL_GGVC_40:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FGS-B05","text":"Singular effect → attribution to several causes according to diverse respects → preservation of local agency and universal ordering.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:GA-15","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FGS-B06","text":"Apparent anomaly → examination of the order of nature → determination of the created limit → miracle or creation as closure of divine immediacy.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:XD-08","CL_GGVC_39:GA-13","CL_GGVC_40:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FGS-B07","text":"Accumulated limitations → exclusion of autonomy and total domination → proportionate created causality → unity of order without confusion of causes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:GA-12","CL_GGVC_40:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:AFM-B01","text":"Unity of principle → hierarchical gradation → angelic application to the person → limitation of demonic and celestial influences → ordering of secondary causes → constitution of the human composite → preservation and propagation through natural means.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_38:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_39:BAS-04","CL_GGVC_40:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:AFM-B02","text":"Claim of efficacy → distinction of orders → examination of recipient and medium → exclusion of finite immediacy → reattribution of effect → restoration of the created agent within providence.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:RLE-05","CL_GGVC_39:XD-08","CL_GGVC_38:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-01","text":"The field of causality is reconfigured from a homogeneous plane into a matrix of agents, instruments, recipients, and respects.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:MME-01","CL_GGVC_40:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-02","text":"Hierarchy is transferred from a scale of names into an architecture of modes of reception, functions, and scopes of object.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MME-01","CL_GGVC_38:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-03","text":"Freedom and contingency are reclassified from immunity against influx into modes of receiving causality without necessary determination.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:MME-05","CL_GGVC_39:XD-10","CL_GGVC_39:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-04","text":"Miracle is converted from wonder at a phenomenon into an ontological limit between the whole created active order and divine operation.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MME-05","CL_GGVC_39:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-05","text":"Human identity is transferred from fixed matter or a solitary soul to continuity of form, operation, and processual composition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:BSD-04","CL_GGVC_40:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-06","text":"Causal reading becomes regressive and differentially reconstructive: appearance, name, and correlation yield to examination of mode of being, end, medium, and recipient.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MME-06","CL_GGVC_39:MME-07","CL_GGVC_40:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MME-07","text":"Evil is reclassified from the principle of a rival order into an act locally imputable but providentially orderable, whence the multiplication of causes does not dissolve responsibility.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MME-03","CL_GGVC_38:BSD-06","CL_GGVC_39:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of surface appear angelic hierarchies, guardianship, temptations, celestial influences, miracles, doctrine, generation, nutrition, and the identity of the living body.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B01","CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level these objects are united through distributed causality, application of power, instrumentality, disposition of the recipient, and distinction of intrinsic principle from exterior agent.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B02","CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level the question is transferred from the bare existence of power to the art of discerning the mode, scope, terminus, and respect of causality.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B03","CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level reality is an order of finite causes under providence, in which forms, relations, and offices persist while operations, matter, and states change.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B04","CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level reiterated distinction orders dispersed material, distributes limits, and formally effects the very order of causality that it asserts.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_38:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_39:ALT-B05","CL_GGVC_40:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-01","text":"God can change the will without abolishing freedom, but the mechanism of this immediately divine causality is assumed rather than explained.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-02","text":"Providence orders evil without appropriating evil intention, but the account of particular permission and the joint between ordering and imputability remain incomplete.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:FL-03","CL_GGVC_39:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-03","text":"Angelic action concerning the body and operative presence are formally distinguished from substantial informing and local presence, but the mechanism converting organic disposition into particular act is not fully determined.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:FL-01","CL_GGVC_38:FL-01","CL_GGVC_38:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-04","text":"The universality of guardianship is combined with the inequality of effects, but the internal criterion by which just permission is distinguished from apparent inefficacy remains unavailable.","refs":["CL_GGVC_38:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-05","text":"A human being naturally generates a human being, but does not produce the intellectual principle; the unity of generation depends upon coordination of natural disposition with the created terminus, whose exact nexus remains indeterminate.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:FL-03","CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:FL-06","text":"The embryo progresses through substantial successions, whereas the formed body remains the same through replacement of matter; the boundary between the two continuities is necessary but fragile.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MP-01","text":"Hierarchy changes from a taxonomy of degrees into a general form of the distribution of causalities, functions, and scopes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MP-01","CL_GGVC_38:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MP-02","text":"Illumination and presence change from locally imagined communication into an operation formally defined by end, application of power, and effect in the recipient.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MP-02","CL_GGVC_38:MP-01","CL_GGVC_38:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MP-03","text":"Fate undergoes a terminal inversion: astral necessity is excluded, but the name is received for the derived order of secondary causes.","refs":["CL_GGVC_39:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MP-04","text":"Form changes from the terminus of substantial succession into a principle of identity under the flux of matter; the change of magnitude between generation and nutrition reorders its function.","refs":["CL_GGVC_40:MP-03","CL_GGVC_40:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a nested network and a machine of enclosed transformations, in which descending power is contracted through means and effects are reordered to one principle.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_38:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_39:HSI-01","CL_GGVC_40:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-02","text":"The created subject is at once a receptive node, selective membrane, and composite joint: it receives influx, governs consent, and preserves unity under change.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_38:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_39:HSI-02","CL_GGVC_40:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-03","text":"Time is a series of applications and changes within the mobility of created causes: leaps of forms, durations of offices, returns of inclinations, and flows of matter are nested within one another.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_38:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_39:HSI-03","CL_GGVC_40:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is not escape from causality, but discernment of influxes, regulation of inclinations, and choice of proportionate means within an order that no creature wholly exceeds.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_38:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_39:HSI-04","CL_GGVC_40:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B01","text":"Axioms concerning universal providence, stratified causality, proportionate reception, real mediation, non-necessitated freedom, and created limit suffice to restore the principal mechanism of this meta-aggregate.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B01","CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the distribution of effect among unequal causes, because the same distinction orders hierarchy, guardianship, providence, freedom, generation, and nutrition.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B03","text":"The mechanism of spiritual action concerning the body, divine causality concerning the will, the criterion of particular permission, and the nexus between the body's ultimate disposition and the creation of the soul remain less determined.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B03","CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B02","CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B04","text":"Coherence rests on the recursive application of a few distinctions and the transformation of terms across fields; this is generative compression, but it does not exhaustively demonstrate local mechanisms and ontological thresholds.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B04","CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_GGVC_L1_10:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement if it derives the hierarchical order through particular application and secondary causality toward human composition, while taxonomies, physiological mechanisms, and thresholds of generation resist integral reduction.","refs":["CL_GGVC_37:MRT-B05","CL_GGVC_38:MRT-B06","CL_GGVC_39:MRT-B06","CL_GGVC_40:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_GGVC_L1_10@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_GGVC_L1_10:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title":"Ggvc L1 10","context":"Chapters 109–120 · Prima pars · Quaestio 108 · Prima pars · Quaestio 109 · Prima pars · Quaestio 110 · Prima pars · Quaestio 111 · Prima pars · Quaestio 112 · Prima pars · Quaestio 113 · Prima pars · Quaestio 114 · Prima pars · Quaestio 115 · Prima pars · Quaestio 116 · Prima pars · Quaestio 117 · Prima pars · Quaestio 118 · Prima pars · Quaestio 119"},"title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"WB_GGVC","layer":"WB","title":"Summa Theologiae · First Part","order":1,"parents":["MC_GGVC_L2_01","MC_GGVC_L2_02","MC_GGVC_L2_03"],"revision":"WB_GGVC@R002","sha256":"0efeb0c01c27b5e48114d52f00e61bfb9b8d702ad3ef37b5ec7afbe60e577fa4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo actus divinus simplex distinctionem personalem, ordinem creatum et operationem finitam sustentet, quin unitas confusionem aut dependentia evacuationem agentiae efficiat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B02","text":"Thesis globalis ipsum esse subsistens et actualitatem receptam asymmetrice coniungit: plenitudo fontis non dividitur, sed participationem, differentiam et operationem recipientium constituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B03","text":"Argumentatio per regressum ab effectibus, purificationem apophaticam, analogiam et attributionem distributam genera falsa dissolvit atque ordines causae, relationis, medii et receptionis restituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B04","text":"Motus ab effectibus ad fontem simplicem ascendit, distinctionem intradivinam explicat, deinde per creationem, mediationem et anthropologiam ad operationes creatas atque reditum earum descendit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B05","text":"Progressio spiralis et cumulativa est, quia actus, receptio, mediatio, corpus et finis redeunt, sed mutato recipiente, ambitu, statu et gradu causalitatis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B06","text":"Asymmetria ontologica motum regit: origo distinguit personas sine inferioritate, receptio autem creaturas constituit per ipsam dependentiam quae earum competentiam finit et efficaciam propriam aperit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-06","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B07","text":"Contradictiones apparentes fiunt mechanismi transitionis, quoniam divisio respectuum oppositiones locales reclassificat, quamquam tensionem causalem vel ontologicam ad gradum altiorem saepe transfert.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-06","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B08","text":"Clausura ontologice ordinata, productiva et asymmetrica est: constitutio quaedam terminatur, operationes secundarum causarum permanent, sed consummatio et modus divinus comprehensionem vel potentiam naturalem excedunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-08","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B09","text":"Systema fundamentaliter grammaticam participationis non competitivae generat, in qua mediatio proportionata actualitatem simplicem in differentias ordinatas transducit et gloriam tamquam attractorem terminalem constituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-07","MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Deus est ipsum esse subsistens et actus simplex invarians, qui nihil recipit, acquirit vel ex ordine creato completur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Actualitas creata est positive bona sed recepta; finitas igitur est modus determinatus participationis, non principium malum aut regio a fonte independens.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Unitas vera differentias non delet: in divinis distinctionem sine divisione, in creatis pluralitatem partium, formarum, potentiarum et causarum ordinat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-15","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Causalitas prima et agentia secunda non competunt, quia prima esse, facultatem et ordinem instituit intra quae secundae causae proprie operantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Receptio a limite cognitionis in principium ontologicum et causale amplificatur: influxum contrahit, formam determinat et effectum proprium recipientis efficit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Mediatio est condicio positiva operationis finitae, quae praesentiam, cognitionem, electionem, ministerium et executionem constituit sine identitate recipientis cum fonte.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Modus essendi agentis et dispositio recipientis mensuram operationis determinant, sed obiectum, medium, status et finis ambitum effectus ulterius contrahunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Subiectum creatum non est centrum clausum, sed principium intrinsecum, nodus receptivus et activus qui influxum selecte in actum proprium convertit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Temporalitas est distributio novitatis, successionis et mutationum status in effectibus; aeternitas neque tempus prius neque mensura univoca omnium operationum est.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Malum, falsitas, culpa et casus sunt privationes vel deordinationes actuum positive fundatorum, non principia ontologica causalitati divinae aemula.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Origo personalis ad intra et operatio essentialis ad extra distinguuntur: relationes constituunt personas, operatio communis creaturas, missiones autem origines personales temporaliter manifestant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Ratio naturalis structuram dependentiae et causalitatis attingit, sed origines personales, decreta singularia et fines ordini creato disproportionales revelationem vel actionem divinam immediatam requirunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Plenitudo divina se communicat et creaturam ad bonum ultimum ordinat sine amissione fontis, absorptionе recipientis aut conversione finitudinis in autonomiam.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-14","text":"[Emergent] Apophasis et distinctio respectuum sunt methodus generativa: modos improprios removent, contradictiones distribuunt et relationes positivas secundum ordines inaequales reconstruunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] Perfectio terminalis novam constitutionem claudit, sed operationem principiorum, communicationem boni et identitatem creatam intra regimen transformatum servat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-01","text":"[Mutatio] Actualitas ab actu divino invarianti in esse participatum, formas finitas, potentias et operationes transfertur; identitas vocabuli manet, sed receptio differentiam ontologicam inducit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-02","text":"[Inversio] Simplicitas ab exclusione compositionis in plenitudinem positivam mutatur, quae relationes internas et architecturam effectuum ordinat sine propria multiplicatione.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-03","text":"[Amplificatio] Receptio a conditione cognitionis finitae ad constitutionem creaturae, transductionem influxus et mensuram agentiae amplificatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-04","text":"[Inversio terminalis] Mediatio, ubi unionem substantialem impediret, excluditur; in operatione finita fit necessaria, sed in consummatione divina immediatio regimen mediationum creatarum transcendit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-05","text":"[Translatio] Relatio ab asymmetria causali ad oppositionem originis transit, dum hierarchia a scala dominationis in topologiam relationum, canalium et officiorum causalium transfertur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-06","text":"[Reclassificatio] Corpus a falso principio mali vel impedimento in materiam bonam, interfaciem cognitionis et co-principium intrinsecum actionis humanae convertitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-07","text":"[Dislocatio] Tempus ab ambitu universali ad successiones spirituales, cosmicas et personales dislocatur, deinde per mutationes status in regimina temporalia nidificata distinguitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-09","MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-08","text":"[Mutatio terminalis] Perfectio ab eminentia ontologica ad integritatem, fecunditatem, quietem et consummationem transit; terminatio sic cessationem excludit sed genus operationis mutare potest.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-09","text":"[Absorptio] Negatio a censura locali praedicationis in methodum reconstructivam absorbetur, quae per purgationem et distinctionem ipsam ontologiam non homogeneam format.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-08","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:CGD-01","text":"Nomina personalia et tractationes singularum processionum infra manent; ad gradum totius libri ascendit distinctio inter originem personalem internam et operationem essentialem communem.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-04","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:CGD-02","text":"Apparitiones angelicae, hypotheses cosmologicae, phantasmata et dispositiones physiologicae sunt mechanismi locales; globalis fit lex mediationis proportionatae secundum modum recipientis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:CGD-03","text":"Taxonomiae zoologicae, anatomicae, angelicae et physiologicae opere discriminandi peracto evanescunt; permanent criteria formae, potentiae, obiecti, medii et competentiae causalis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:CGD-04","text":"Conclusiones sexuales, domesticae et applicationes sociales particulares non globalizantur, quia ex principiis participationis et receptionis proportionatae non necessario derivantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:CGD-05","text":"Cosmologiae, chronologiae et vocabularia probabilia corrigibilia manent; ad gradum praesentem ascendunt invariabilia de actualitate recepta, causalitate stratificata et distinctione respectuum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-06","MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:CGD-06","text":"Disputationes locales de revelatione, miraculo et gloria amplificantur in terminum globalem quo potentia naturalis, causalitas creata et consummatio divina formaliter distinguuntur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:CGD-07","text":"Maior transitus praesentis gradus fit cum metaphysica actus simplicis per ordinem cosmicum in formam viventis, anthropologiam et operationem moralem interiorizatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-01","text":"Nullum genus, tribunal vel mensura univoca Deum, personas, creaturas, cognitiones et causalitates tamquam membra coordinata continet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-02","text":"Nullum receptaculum, spatium vel exterior neutralis ante participationem praebetur, intra quod Deus et creatura iam constituti conveniant.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-03","text":"Causalitas prima et secunda neque vires competitivae neque agentes homogenei in plano communi sunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-04","text":"Creatura neque sui causa, neque fons propriae permanentiae, neque possessora autonoma efficaciae vel finis esse potest.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-05","text":"Malum, tenebrae, culpa et casus nullam substantiam vel regionem ontologicam extra bonum et providentiam constituunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-06","text":"Unitas neque uniformitatem, homogeneitatem neque suppressionem pluralitatis requirit; differentia ordinata non est defectus residuus.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-07","text":"Materia et corpus neque principia mali neque fundamenta universalia finitudinis sunt, atque humanitas ad animam separatam non reducitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-08","text":"Tempus non est continuum absolutum commune Deo, spiritui, corpori, narrationi et actioni.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-06","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-09","text":"Consummatio ultima non oritur ex mera intensificatione processus naturalis neque ex clausura intrinseca ordinis creati.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet accessibilitatem intellectui finito vel evidentiam effectus mensuram exhaustivam veritatis et causae constituere.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet simplicitatem vacuitatem vel indistinctionem absolutam significare, quia tunc processiones, relationes et plenitudo causalis simul corruerent.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet differentiam lapsum ab unitate esse, quoniam distinctio personalis et multiplicitas creata perfectionem ordinis constituunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-02","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet dependentiam totalem causae secundae libertatem, proprietatem vel imputabilitatem eius delere.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet mediationem transmissionem passivam vel diminutionem causalitatis esse, quia ipsa operationem finitam et praesentiam proportionatam constituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet recipientem terminum inertem influxus esse; principium intrinsecum receptionem selectivam in actum proprium transformat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-07","text":"Falsum esse oportet causalitatem semper successionem causae vel interruptionem ordinis requirere; novitas effectus principia immutata vel iam instituta explicare potest.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-08","text":"Falsum esse oportet structuram metaphysicam naturalem decreta contingentia vel consummationem supernaturalem ex se necessario producere.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSA-01","text":"Altissima exclusio est exterioritas ontologica a causa prima: nullus locus Deo prior neque regio participationi externa potest dari.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSA-02","text":"Nullus tertius modus independens inter ipsum esse subsistens et esse participatum mediat; omnis mediatio creata iam intra dependentiam consistit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSA-03","text":"Possibilitas inadmissibilis est creatura quae sit suum fundamentum, purus actus, principium sui luminis et finis sui ipsius.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSA-04","text":"Immediatio universalis et transparentia totalis causae, mentis vel ordinis enti finito indisponibiles sunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSA-05","text":"Excluditur effectus singularis qui fontem infinite bonum exhaustive repraesentet vel ex sola apparentia causam suam plene demonstret.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSA-06","text":"Systema circum absentiam ordinis creati sibi sufficientis ordinatur: subiectum finitum neque sibi ultimum bonum est neque se naturaliter consummare potest.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-01","text":"Differentia ontologica inter Deum et creaturam non est differentia specierum intra genus commune, sed asymmetria inter actum non receptum et actualitatem receptam sub providentia.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-01","WB_GGVC:GA-02","WB_GGVC:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-02","text":"Quo profundius causa prima modum causae secundae instituit, eo firmius proprietatem operationis eius fundat; dependentia et agentia ideo crescere simul possunt.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-04","WB_GGVC:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-03","text":"Distinctio personalis operationem externam non partitur in causalitates separatas; origines personales potius modos manifestationis, missionis et receptionis operationis communis ordinant.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-06","WB_GGVC:GA-11","WB_GGVC:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-05","MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-04","text":"Apophasis, purgatio analogica et distinctio recursiva unum operatorium transscalare constituunt, quod de divino, mundo et subiecto finito sine univocatione praedicare sinit.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-03","WB_GGVC:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-05","text":"Epistemologia participationem ontologicam repetit: sicut esse finitum per formam et medium recipitur, ita cognitio per corpus, species et proportionem recipientis ad actum pervenit.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-05","WB_GGVC:GA-06","WB_GGVC:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-06","text":"Aeternitas successionem non continet ut tempus superius, sed regimina temporalia nidificata causat, quorum novitas in effectibus, applicationibus et mutationibus status distribuitur.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-07","text":"Privativitas mali et attributio pluralis effectuum simul providentiam universalem, causalitatem localem et imputabilitatem intentionis perversae servant.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-04","WB_GGVC:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-08","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-08","text":"Pluralitas graduata mundi est explicatio distributa actualitatis receptae: relationes partis ad totum et conservatio continua differentias colligunt sine reductione ad uniformitatem.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-02","WB_GGVC:GA-03","WB_GGVC:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-09","text":"Clausura ultima mutat regimen operationis potius quam creaturam annihilat: potentia naturalis terminatur, actio divina immediata perficit, identitas participata permanet.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-12","WB_GGVC:GA-13","WB_GGVC:GA-15","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-01","text":"Realitas est topologia asymmetrica actualitatis: fons simplex, participationes receptae et relationes ordinatae constituunt ens antequam entia velut substantiae solitariae numerentur.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-01","WB_GGVC:GA-02","WB_GGVC:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum distribuitur secundum modum essendi: persona divina est relatio subsistens, creatura nodus receptivus, homo autem transductor historicus influxuum, actuum et habituum.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-07","WB_GGVC:GA-08","WB_GGVC:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-03","text":"Causalitas non est translatio impulsus intra spatium commune, sed constitutio campi essendi, facultatum et attributionum differentialium.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-04","WB_GGVC:GA-05","WB_GGVC:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-04","text":"Tempus fit architectura effectuum inaequalium sub aeternitate: institutiones, maturationes, decisiones, habitus, lapsus et consummationes diversas formas novitatis producunt.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-09","WB_GGVC:GA-15","MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-05","text":"Cognitio finita est indirecta sed constructiva, quia effectus, operationes et differentiae formales principia manifestant, dum apophasis vetat apparentiam pro comprehensione haberi.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-07","WB_GGVC:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-07","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-06","text":"Libertas est actus receptivus et habitualiter historicus: finis inclinationem dat, iudicium media discernit, consensus influxum ordinat, habitus futuras receptiones reformat.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-05","WB_GGVC:GA-08","WB_GGVC:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-07","text":"Potestas creata est ministerialis et limitata ad competentiam: media et condiciones actionis alterius ordinare potest, sed corpus, voluntatem vel finem eius ontologice possidere nequit.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-04","WB_GGVC:GA-06","WB_GGVC:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-08","text":"Historia creaturalis est processus vere productivus sed non sibi consummativus: principia instituta effectus novos generant, dum finis disproportionatus mutationem terminalem ex alio ordine requirit.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-12","WB_GGVC:GA-13","WB_GGVC:GA-15","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-01","text":"Effectus cognitionem causae generant; purgatio modos improprios removet; causa purificata effectus denuo ut participationes, signa et operationes proportionatas interpretatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-02","text":"Fons actualitatem communicat; participans proprie operatur; bonum receptum communicatur; communicatio assimilat effectum principio sine reciprocatione causalitatis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-03","text":"Influxus per medium recipitur, actus proprius habitum generat, habitus dispositionem mutat, dispositio posteriores receptiones et actus recursive contrahit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-04","text":"Inclinatio in consensum ordinatum vel perversum transit; actus habitum format; habitus ordinem moralem stabilit aut directionem aversionis amplificat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-05","text":"Contradictio apparens divisionem respectuum provocat; solutio localis terminum reclassificat, sed tensionem ad coordinationem causarum vel ordinem ontologicum altiorem transfert.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-07","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-06","text":"Inadaequatio singularis pluralitatem generat; pluralitas integritatem totius format; totum partes mensurat; eadem unitas non homogenea per mundum, potentias et operationes recurrit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-07","MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-07","text":"Mutatio status medium vetus claudit, vectores formales identitatem servant, novum regimen operationis aperitur, et terminus localis in relationem ulteriorem convertitur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-08","text":"Attractor circuituum est plenitudo simplex per mediationem proportionatam communicata et ad gloriam directa, ubi participatio consummatur sine absorptionе.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B01","text":"Effectus finiti et operationes observatae → regressus causalis → quaestio de fonte, principio proximo, recipiente et fine.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B02","text":"Perfectio manifestata → remotio modi materialis, passivi et temporalis → distinctio respectuum → praedicatio analogica purificata.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B03","text":"Perfectio purificata + exclusio receptionis et compositionis → actus simplex → ipsum esse subsistens.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B03","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B04","text":"Intelligere et velle immanentia → processiones non temporales → oppositio originis → relationes subsistentes sine divisione essentiae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B05","text":"Operatio essentialis communis → actualitas recepta → creatio, conservatio et providentia → ordo pluralis sine partitione fontis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B06","text":"Forma recepta → potentiae distinctae → obiecta et media proportionata → actus secundi → integritas non homogenea.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B07","text":"Influxus mediatus → contractio secundum recipientem → actualizatio principii intrinseci → operatio propria → habitus et historia.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B08","text":"Actus positivus + defectus ordinis → privatio vel culpa → distributio attributionum → permissio et ordinatio providentialis sine causa mali positiva.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B09","text":"Terminus virtutis creatae + finis disproportionatus → actio divina immediata → mutatio terminalis regiminis → perfectio identitatis participatae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:AFM-B01","text":"Effectus finitus → regressus causalis → purgatio apophatica → actus simplex → processiones personales → operatio externa communis → actualitas participata → agentia propria → consummatio.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:AFM-B01","MC_GGVC_L2_01:AFM-B02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AFM-B02","text":"Apparentia vel obiectum → examen modi recipientis → distinctio medii et causae → reconstructio competentiae → attributio effectus secundum respectus inaequales.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AFM-B03","text":"Actualitas recepta → forma et potentiae → mediatio proportionata → cognitio et appetitus → electio → executio incorporata → habitus → nova dispositio receptionis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:AFM-B02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AFM-B04","text":"Institutio → maturatio → operationes secundae → lapsus aut ordinatio → clausura medii naturalis → actio divina immediata → gloria sine annihilatione finitudinis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:AFM-B03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-01","text":"Ens reclassificatur a substantia quasi solitaria in actualitatem relatione ad fontem, modum receptionis et ordinem operationis constitutam.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-02","text":"Unitas et pluralitas desinunt esse contraria quantitativa: plenitudo simplicis fontis et integritas partium differentium ad ordines distinctos pertinent.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-03","text":"Causalitas reclassificatur a conflictu virium in matricem constitutivam causarum primarum, secundarum, instrumentalium et receptivarum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-04","text":"Mediatio ab indicio distantiae convertitur in structuram praesentiae et efficaciae finitae, dum immediatio fit terminus asymmetricae actionis divinae.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-05","text":"Spiritus et corpus non amplius bonum et malum distribuunt; corpus fit medium epistemicum, materia compositi et co-principium actionis personalis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-06","text":"Tempus reclassificatur a receptaculo universali in tabulam successionum, maturationum, habituum et transitionum terminalium sub aeternitate non temporali.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-07","text":"Malum et casus reclassificantur ex potestatibus adversis in perturbationes locales actuum bonorum, quae imputabilitatem et ordinationem providentialem diversis respectibus recipiunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-06","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-08","text":"Ratio, revelatio, natura et gratia non sunt ordines competitores: ratio dependentiam reconstruere potest, revelatio origines aperit, gratia autem potentiam naturalem ad finem disproportionatum elevat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-07","MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-09","text":"Hierarchia et auctoritas a dominio substantiali in officia limitata ordinandi canales, media et condiciones operationis alterius transferuntur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei tractantur Deus, processiones, creatio, spiritus, mundus, anima, corpus, cognitio, voluntas, providentia, malum et consummatio.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali actus et potentia, receptio, participatio, relatio, forma, mediatio, causalitas stratificata et finalitas unam machinam distributam efficiunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico quaestiones de identitate, potestate et contradictione transferuntur in quaestiones de ambitu, respectu, attributione, medio, statu et competentia.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est actus aeternus qui participationes finitas per successiones et regimina inaequalia sustentat sine propria mutatione.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo systema distinctionibus repetitis differentias constituit, oppositiones purgat et unitatem non homogeneam quam asserit ipsa forma argumenti efficit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:FL-01","text":"Cognitio quidditativa negatur, sed assertiones fortes de actu, essentia, relatione et causalitate servantur; nexus pendet a discrimine inter veritatem attributionis et comprehensionem modi.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FL-02","text":"Unus actus realis plures rationes et processiones sustinet, dum relatio essentiae identica oppositione originis distinguitur; pondus ontologicum distinctionis rationis fragile manet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-02","MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FL-03","text":"Causalitas prima totalis cum libertate et culpa componitur, sed modus quo motio intrinseca consensum non necessitat neque defectum positive causat plene formalizatus non est.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FL-04","text":"Operatio externa communis est, sed missio personaliter distribuitur; nexus inter causalitatem essentialem et manifestationem secundum origines personales delicatus manet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-05","MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FL-05","text":"Completudo naturalis et receptivitas gratiae simul affirmantur, sed proportio inter dispositionem creatam, influxum gratuitum et effectum immediate divinum incompleta manet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FL-06","text":"Identitas per mortem, mutationem medii et transitionem status servatur, sed criterion unitatis formae, habitus et compositi sub rupturis radicalibus fragile est.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FL-07","text":"Veritas ontologica stabilis a cosmologiis corrigibilibus distinguitur, sed terminus semanticus quo exemplar inferius corrigitur non semper univoce determinatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MP-01","text":"Simplicitas mutatur ab exclusione compositionis in plenitudinem communicativam quae relationes internas et effectus plurales sustinet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-01","MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MP-02","text":"Relatio mutatur ab asymmetria causali in oppositionem originis, subsistentiam personalem et tandem architecturam functionum causalium inaequalium.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MP-03","text":"Mediatio mutatur ab impedimento unionis in condicionem operationis finitae, dum immediatio ab absentia instrumenti in terminum causalitatis divinae transit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MP-04","text":"Corpus mutatur a regione cosmica vel impedimento apparenti in materiam forma constitutam, medium epistemicum et co-principium personale.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MP-05","text":"Perfectio mutatur ab eminentia simplici in integritatem productivam et postremo in consummationem quae genus operationis transformat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MP-06","text":"Tempus mutatur a situ chronologico in pluralitatem durationum, habituum et regiminum terminalium coordinandorum.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MP-07","text":"Negatio mutatur a censura praedicationis in vim formalem quae identitates falsas dissolvit et relationes positivas restituit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-07","MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est centrum non spatiale et machina transductiva: actus simplex relations internas densat, actualitatem communicat et per media causas finitas operari facit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:HSI-02","text":"Persona divina est nodus subsistens originis; creatura est nodus receptivus et excentricus cui forma, potentia, relatio et finis identitatem tribuunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:HSI-03","text":"Subiectum humanum est limen et membrana quae influxus recipit, selecte transducit, consensum moderatur et historiam actuum in habitu servat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:HSI-04","text":"Tempus est plexus arcuum circa fontem aeternum: maturationes, decisiones et applicationes accumulantur, directiones invertuntur, media clauduntur et regimina mutantur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:HSI-05","text":"Navigatio possibilis ab effectibus ad principia ascendit et a finibus per media ad actus descendit; fuga non est exterioritas a causalitate, sed recta ordinatio participationis.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-04","MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata actus simplicis, actualitatis participatae, unitatis non homogeneae, causalitatis non competitivae, receptionis proportionatae, mediationis et finalitatis terminalis sufficiunt ad machinam libri restituendam.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus quo actus non receptus esse participatum constituit, forma potentias distinguit et dependentia operationem propriam causae secundae fundat.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent proportio analogica, efficacia distinctionum rationis, motio voluntatis, actualizatio defectus, continuitas identitatis et coordinatio dispositionis naturalis cum actione divina immediata.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MRT-B04","text":"Systema cohaeret non per seriem uniformem, sed per reditum variatum actus, receptionis, mediationis et distinctionis, quorum functio secundum gradum ontologicum mutatur.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat si ascensum ab effectibus, articulationem fontis, descensum participationis, operationem creatam et clausuram supernaturalem distinguit; limina inter hos ordines reductioni completae resistunt.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261019T174458229897Z_20e51ec1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Summa Theologiae · First Part","source_sha256":"0efeb0c01c27b5e48114d52f00e61bfb9b8d702ad3ef37b5ec7afbe60e577fa4","sha256":"f98de56d27691a008043e408ac2645ea1afea4ad2927657cf4033326cc022eca","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-19T17:44:58.230922+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B01","text":"The system asks how a simple divine act sustains personal distinction, created order, and finite operation, without unity producing confusion or dependencies emptying agency.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B02","text":"The global thesis asymmetrically joins ipsum esse subsistens and received actuality: the fullness of the source is not divided, but constitutes the participation, difference, and operation of recipients.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B03","text":"Argumentation through regression from effects, apophatic purification, analogy, and distributed attribution dissolves false genera and restores orders of cause, relation, medium, and reception.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B04","text":"The movement ascends from effects to the simple source, explicates intradivine distinction, then descends through creation, mediation, and anthropology to created operations and their return.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B05","text":"The progression is spiral and cumulative, because act, reception, mediation, body, and end recur, but with a changed recipient, scope, state, and degree of causality.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B06","text":"Ontological asymmetry governs the movement: origin distinguishes persons without inferiority, whereas reception constitutes creatures through the very dependence that delimits their competence and discloses their proper efficacy.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-06","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B07","text":"Apparent contradictions become mechanisms of transition, since the division of respects reclassifies local oppositions, although it often transfers causal or ontological tension to a higher level.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-06","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B08","text":"Closure is ontologically ordered, productive, and asymmetrical: a certain constitution terminates, operations of secondary causes remain, but consummation and the divine mode exceed natural comprehension or power.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-08","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AS-B09","text":"The system fundamentally generates a grammar of noncompetitive participation, in which proportionate mediation transduces simple actuality into ordered differences and constitutes glory as a terminal attractor.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-07","MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] God is ipsum esse subsistens and invariant simple act, who receives, acquires, or is completed by nothing from the created order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Created actuality is positively good but received; finitude, therefore, is a determinate mode of participation, not an evil principle or a region independent of the source.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] True unity does not delete differences: in the divine it orders distinction without division, and in created things a plurality of parts, forms, powers, and causes.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-15","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Primary causality and secondary agency do not compete, because the former establishes the being, capacity, and order within which secondary causes properly operate.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Reception is expanded from a limit of cognition into an ontological and causal principle: it contracts influx, determines form, and effects the recipient's proper effect.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Mediation is a positive condition of finite operation, constituting presence, cognition, choice, ministry, and execution without the recipient's identity with the source.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] The mode of being of the agent and the disposition of the recipient determine the measure of operation, but object, medium, state, and end further contract the scope of the effect.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] The created subject is not a closed center, but an intrinsic principle, a receptive and active node that selectively converts influx into its own act.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Temporality is the distribution of novelty, succession, and changes of state in effects; eternity is neither prior time nor a univocal measure of all operations.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Evil, falsity, fault, and fall are privations or disorders of positively grounded acts, not ontological principles rivaling divine causality.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Personal origin ad intra and essential operation ad extra are distinguished: relations constitute persons, common operation constitutes creatures, while missions manifest personal origins temporally.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Natural reason attains the structure of dependence and causality, but personal origins, singular decrees, and ends disproportionate to the created order require revelation or immediate divine action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Divine fullness communicates itself and orders the creature to the ultimate good without loss to the source, absorption of the recipient, or conversion of finitude into autonomy.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-14","text":"[Emergent] Apophasis and the distinction of respects are a generative method: they remove improper modes, distribute contradictions, and reconstruct positive relations according to unequal orders.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] Terminal perfection closes a new constitution, but preserves the operation of principles, communication of the good, and created identity within a transformed regime.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-01","text":"[Mutation] Actuality is transferred from invariant divine act into participated being, finite forms, powers, and operations; the identity of the term remains, but reception introduces ontological difference.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-02","text":"[Inversion] Simplicity changes from the exclusion of composition into positive fullness, which orders internal relations and the architecture of effects without its own multiplication.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-03","text":"[Amplification] Reception is amplified from a condition of finite cognition into the constitution of the creature, transduction of influx, and measure of agency.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-04","text":"[Terminal inversion] Mediation, where it would impede substantial union, is excluded; in finite operation it becomes necessary, but in divine consummation immediacy transcends the regime of created mediations.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-05","text":"[Translation] Relation passes from causal asymmetry to opposition of origin, while hierarchy is translated from a scale of domination into a topology of relations, channels, and causal offices.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-06","text":"[Reclassification] The body is converted from a false principle of evil or impediment into good matter, an interface of cognition, and an intrinsic co-principle of human action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-07","text":"[Dislocation] Time is dislocated from a universal scope into spiritual, cosmic, and personal successions, then distinguished through changes of state into nested temporal regimes.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-09","MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-08","text":"[Terminal mutation] Perfection passes from ontological eminence to integrity, fecundity, rest, and consummation; termination thus excludes cessation but can change the kind of operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:RMD-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RMD-09","text":"[Absorption] Negation is absorbed from local censure of predication into a reconstructive method which, through purification and distinction, forms nonhomogeneous ontology itself.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-08","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:CGD-01","text":"Personal names and treatments of individual processions remain below; at the level of the whole book, the distinction between internal personal origin and common essential operation rises.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-04","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:CGD-02","text":"Angelic appearances, cosmological hypotheses, phantasms, and physiological dispositions are local mechanisms; the global law is that of proportionate mediation according to the mode of the recipient.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:CGD-03","text":"Zoological, anatomical, angelic, and physiological taxonomies vanish once their discriminating work is completed; criteria of form, power, object, medium, and causal competence remain.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:CGD-04","text":"Particular sexual, domestic, and social applications are not globalized, because they do not necessarily follow from the principles of participation and proportionate reception.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:CGD-05","text":"Cosmologies, chronologies, and probable vocabularies remain corrigible; invariants concerning received actuality, stratified causality, and the distinction of respects rise to the present level.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-06","MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:CGD-06","text":"Local disputations concerning revelation, miracle, and glory are amplified into the global term by which natural power, created causality, and divine consummation are formally distinguished.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:CGD-07","text":"The major transition of the present level occurs when the metaphysics of simple act is interiorized through cosmic order into the form of the living being, anthropology, and moral operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:CGD-06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-01","text":"No genus, tribunal, or univocal measure contains God, persons, creatures, cognitions, and causalities as coordinated members.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-02","text":"No receptacle, space, or neutral exterior is given before participation, within which God and creature, already constituted, might meet.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-03","text":"Primary and secondary causality are neither competitive forces nor homogeneous agents on a common plane.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-04","text":"The creature can be neither its own cause, nor the source of its own persistence, nor the autonomous possessor of efficacy or end.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-05","text":"Evil, darkness, fault, and fall constitute no substance or ontological region outside goodness and providence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-06","text":"Unity requires neither uniformity, homogeneity, nor suppression of plurality; ordered difference is not a residual defect.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-07","text":"Matter and body are neither principles of evil nor universal foundations of finitude, and humanity is not reduced to the separated soul.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-08","text":"Time is not an absolute continuum common to God, spirit, body, narrative, and action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GNX-06","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:GNX-09","text":"Final consummation does not arise from the mere intensification of a natural process nor from the intrinsic closure of the created order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GNX-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that accessibility to finite intellect or the evidence of an effect constitutes an exhaustive measure of truth and cause.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that simplicity signifies emptiness or absolute indistinction, for then processions, relations, and causal fullness would collapse together.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that difference is a falling away from unity, since personal distinction and created multiplicity constitute the perfection of order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-02","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that the total dependence of a secondary cause destroys its freedom, propriety, or imputability.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that mediation is passive transmission or a diminution of causality, since it itself constitutes finite operation and proportionate presence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that the recipient is an inert terminus of influx; an intrinsic principle transforms selective reception into its own act.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-07","text":"It must be false that causality always requires succession of cause or interruption of order; the novelty of an effect can be explained by unchanged or already established principles.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:DBN-08","text":"It must be false that natural metaphysical structure necessarily produces contingent decrees or supernatural consummation from itself.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:DBN-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSA-01","text":"The highest exclusion is ontological exteriority to the first cause: no place prior to God nor region external to participation can be given.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSA-02","text":"No independent third mode mediates between ipsum esse subsistens and participated being; every created mediation already subsists within dependence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSA-03","text":"An inadmissible possibility is a creature that is its own foundation, pure act, the principle of its own light, and its own end.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSA-04","text":"Universal immediacy and total transparency of cause, mind, or order are unavailable to a finite being.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSA-05","text":"Excluded is a singular effect that exhaustively represents the infinitely good source or fully demonstrates its cause from appearance alone.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSA-06","text":"The system is ordered around the absence of a self-sufficient created order: the finite subject is neither its own ultimate good nor capable of naturally consummating itself.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-01","text":"The ontological difference between God and creature is not a difference of species within a common genus, but an asymmetry between unreceived act and received actuality under providence.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-01","WB_GGVC:GA-02","WB_GGVC:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-02","text":"The more deeply the first cause establishes the mode of the secondary cause, the more firmly it grounds the propriety of its operation; dependence and agency can therefore increase together.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-04","WB_GGVC:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-07","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-03","text":"Personal distinction does not divide external operation into separate causalities; personal origins rather order the modes of manifestation, mission, and reception of common operation.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-06","WB_GGVC:GA-11","WB_GGVC:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-05","MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-04","text":"Apophasis, analogical purification, and recursive distinction constitute one transscalar operation, which permits predication concerning the divine, the world, and the finite subject without univocity.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-03","WB_GGVC:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-05","text":"Epistemology repeats ontological participation: just as finite being is received through form and medium, so cognition arrives at act through body, species, and the proportion of the recipient.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-05","WB_GGVC:GA-06","WB_GGVC:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-06","text":"Eternity does not contain succession as higher time, but causes nested temporal regimes, whose novelty is distributed in effects, applications, and changes of state.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-07","text":"The privative character of evil and the plural attribution of effects together preserve universal providence, local causality, and imputability for perverse intention.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-04","WB_GGVC:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-08","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-08","text":"The graded plurality of the world is a distributed explication of received actuality: relations of part to whole and continuous preservation gather differences without reduction to uniformity.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-02","WB_GGVC:GA-03","WB_GGVC:GA-04","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:XD-09","text":"Ultimate closure changes the regime of operation rather than annihilating the creature: natural power terminates, immediate divine action perfects, participated identity remains.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-12","WB_GGVC:GA-13","WB_GGVC:GA-15","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-13","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-01","text":"Reality is an asymmetrical topology of actuality: a simple source, received participations, and ordered relations constitute being before beings are counted as if solitary substances.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-01","WB_GGVC:GA-02","WB_GGVC:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-02","text":"The subject is distributed according to mode of being: a divine person is a subsistent relation, the creature a receptive node, while the human being is a historical transducer of influxes, acts, and habits.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-07","WB_GGVC:GA-08","WB_GGVC:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-03","text":"Causality is not the translation of an impulse within a common space, but the constitution of a field of being, capacities, and differential attributions.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-04","WB_GGVC:GA-05","WB_GGVC:GA-06","MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-04","text":"Time becomes an architecture of unequal effects under eternity: institutions, maturations, decisions, habits, falls, and consummations produce diverse forms of novelty.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-09","WB_GGVC:GA-15","MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-05","text":"Finite cognition is indirect but constructive, because effects, operations, and formal differences manifest principles, while apophasis forbids appearance to be taken for comprehension.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-07","WB_GGVC:GA-14","MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-07","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-06","text":"Freedom is a receptive and habitually historical act: the end gives inclination, judgment discerns means, consent orders influx, and habit reforms future receptions.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-05","WB_GGVC:GA-08","WB_GGVC:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L2_01:BSD-06","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-07","text":"Created power is ministerial and limited to competence: it can order the means and conditions of another's action, but cannot ontologically possess its body, will, or end.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-04","WB_GGVC:GA-06","WB_GGVC:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:BSD-08","text":"Creaturely history is a truly productive but not self-consummating process: established principles generate new effects, while a disproportionate end requires terminal change from another order.","refs":["WB_GGVC:GA-12","WB_GGVC:GA-13","WB_GGVC:GA-15","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-11","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-01","text":"Effects generate cognition of cause; purification removes improper modes; the purified cause interprets effects anew as participations, signs, and proportionate operations.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-02","text":"The source communicates actuality; the participant properly operates; the received good is communicated; communication assimilates the effect to the principle without reciprocity of causality.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-01","MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-03","text":"Influx is received through a medium, proper act generates habit, habit changes disposition, and disposition recursively contracts subsequent receptions and acts.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-04","text":"Inclination passes into ordered or perverse consent; act forms habit; habit stabilizes moral order or amplifies the direction of aversion.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-05","text":"An apparent contradiction provokes a division of respects; a local solution reclassifies the term, but transfers tension to the coordination of causes or a higher ontological order.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-07","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-06","text":"Singular inadequacy generates plurality; plurality forms the integrity of the whole; the whole measures the parts; the same nonhomogeneous unity recurs through the world, powers, and operations.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-07","MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-07","text":"A change of state closes an old medium, formal vectors preserve identity, a new regime of operation opens, and the local terminus is converted into a further relation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:RLE-08","text":"The attractor of the circuits is simple fullness communicated through proportionate mediation and directed to glory, where participation is consummated without absorption.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:RLE-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-07","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B01","text":"Finite effects and observed operations → causal regression → question concerning source, proximate principle, recipient, and end.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:BAS-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B02","text":"Manifested perfection → removal of material, passive, and temporal mode → distinction of respects → purified analogical predication.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B03","text":"Purified perfection + exclusion of reception and composition → simple act → ipsum esse subsistens.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B03","MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B04","text":"Immanent understanding and willing → non-temporal processions → opposition of origin → subsistent relations without division of essence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-09","MC_GGVC_L2_01:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B05","text":"Common essential operation → received actuality → creation, conservation, and providence → plural order without partition of the source.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:GA-08","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BAS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B06","text":"Received form → distinct powers → proportionate objects and media → second acts → nonhomogeneous integrity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B07","text":"Mediated influx → contraction according to recipient → actualization of intrinsic principle → proper operation → habit and history.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B08","text":"Positive act + defect of order → privation or fault → distribution of attributions → providential permission and ordering without a positive cause of evil.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-10","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FGS-B09","text":"Limit of created power + disproportionate end → immediate divine action → terminal change of regime → perfection of participated identity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-12","MC_GGVC_L2_03:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:AFM-B01","text":"Finite effect → causal regression → apophatic purification → simple act → personal processions → common external operation → participated actuality → proper agency → consummation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:AFM-B01","MC_GGVC_L2_01:AFM-B02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AFM-B02","text":"Appearance or object → examination of the recipient's mode → distinction of medium and cause → reconstruction of competence → attribution of effect according to unequal respects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:FGS-B06","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AFM-B03","text":"Received actuality → form and powers → proportionate mediation → cognition and appetite → choice → embodied execution → habit → new disposition of reception.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:AFM-B02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:AFM-B04","text":"Institution → maturation → secondary operations → fall or ordering → closure of natural medium → immediate divine action → glory without annihilation of finitude.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:AFM-B03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:AS-08","MC_GGVC_L2_03:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-01","text":"Being is reclassified from a quasi-solitary substance into actuality constituted by relation to source, mode of reception, and order of operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-02","text":"Unity and plurality cease to be quantitative contraries: the fullness of the simple source and the integrity of different parts belong to distinct orders.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-03","text":"Causality is reclassified from a conflict of forces into a constitutive matrix of primary, secondary, instrumental, and receptive causes.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-04","text":"Mediation is converted from an indication of distance into a structure of finite presence and efficacy, while immediacy becomes the terminus of asymmetrical divine action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-05","text":"Spirit and body no longer distribute good and evil; the body becomes an epistemic medium, the matter of the composite, and a co-principle of personal action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-06","text":"Time is reclassified from a universal receptacle into a map of successions, maturations, habits, and terminal transitions under nontemporal eternity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-07","text":"Evil and fall are reclassified from adverse powers into local disturbances of good acts, which receive imputability and providential ordering under diverse respects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-06","MC_GGVC_L2_02:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-08","text":"Reason, revelation, nature, and grace are not competing orders: reason can reconstruct dependence, revelation discloses origins, while grace elevates natural power to a disproportionate end.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MME-07","MC_GGVC_L2_01:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MME-09","text":"Hierarchy and authority are transferred from substantial dominion into limited offices of ordering the channels, media, and conditions of another's operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:MME-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level God, processions, creation, spirit, world, soul, body, cognition, will, providence, evil, and consummation are treated.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level act and potency, reception, participation, relation, form, mediation, stratified causality, and finality make one distributed machine.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level questions concerning identity, power, and contradiction are transferred into questions concerning scope, respect, attribution, medium, state, and competence.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level reality is eternal act that sustains finite participations through unequal successions and regimes without its own change.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, through repeated distinctions the system constitutes differences, purifies oppositions, and by the very form of argument effects the nonhomogeneous unity it asserts.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:ALT-B05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:FL-01","text":"Quidditative cognition is denied, but strong assertions concerning act, essence, relation, and causality are maintained; the connection depends on the distinction between truth of attribution and comprehension of mode.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FL-02","text":"One real act sustains several accounts and processions, while relation identical with essence is distinguished by opposition of origin; the ontological weight of the distinction of reason remains fragile.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-02","MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FL-03","text":"Total primary causality is combined with freedom and fault, but the manner in which intrinsic motion neither necessitates consent nor positively causes defect is not fully formalized.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FL-04","text":"External operation is common, but mission is personally distributed; the connection between essential causality and manifestation according to personal origins remains delicate.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:FL-05","MC_GGVC_L2_01:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FL-05","text":"Natural completeness and receptivity to grace are affirmed together, but the proportion between created disposition, gratuitous influx, and immediately divine effect remains incomplete.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FL-06","text":"Identity is preserved through death, change of medium, and transition of state, but the criterion of unity of form, habit, and composite under radical ruptures is fragile.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:FL-04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:FL-07","text":"Stable ontological truth is distinguished from corrigible cosmologies, but the semantic threshold by which the lower model is corrected is not always univocally determined.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:FL-05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MP-01","text":"Simplicity changes from the exclusion of composition into communicative fullness that sustains internal relations and plural effects.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-01","MC_GGVC_L2_01:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MP-02","text":"Relation changes from causal asymmetry into opposition of origin, personal subsistence, and finally an architecture of unequal causal functions.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MP-03","text":"Mediation changes from an impediment to union into a condition of finite operation, while immediacy passes from absence of instrument into the terminus of divine causality.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MP-04","text":"The body changes from a cosmic region or apparent impediment into matter constituted by form, an epistemic medium, and a personal co-principle.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MP-05","text":"Perfection changes from simple eminence into productive integrity and finally into consummation that transforms the kind of operation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MP-06","text":"Time changes from chronological position into a plurality of durations, habits, and terminal regimes to be coordinated.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:MP-05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MP-07","text":"Negation changes from censure of predication into a formal force that dissolves false identities and restores positive relations.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MP-07","MC_GGVC_L2_02:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a nonspatial center and a transductive machine: simple act densifies internal relations, communicates actuality, and through media makes finite causes operate.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:HSI-02","text":"A divine person is a subsistent node of origin; the creature is a receptive and eccentric node to which form, power, relation, and end confer identity.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:HSI-03","text":"The human subject is a threshold and membrane that receives influxes, selectively transduces them, moderates consent, and preserves the history of acts in habit.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:HSI-04","text":"Time is a plexus of arcs around the eternal source: maturations, decisions, and applications accumulate, directions are reversed, media close, and regimes change.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:HSI-05","text":"Possible navigation ascends from effects to principles and descends from ends through media to acts; escape is not exteriority from causality, but the right ordering of participation.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_02:HSI-04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:HSI-04","MC_GGVC_L2_01:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"WB_GGVC:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of simple act, participated actuality, nonhomogeneous unity, noncompetitive causality, proportionate reception, mediation, and terminal finality suffice to restore the machine of the book.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B01","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the nexus by which unreceived act constitutes participated being, form distinguishes powers, and dependence grounds the proper operation of the secondary cause.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B02","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B02","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remain analogical proportion, the efficacy of distinctions of reason, motion of the will, actualization of defect, continuity of identity, and coordination of natural disposition with immediate divine action.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B03","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B03","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MRT-B04","text":"The system coheres not through a uniform series, but through the varied return of act, reception, mediation, and distinction, whose function changes according to ontological level.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B04","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_GGVC:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement if it distinguishes ascent from effects, articulation of the source, descent of participation, created operation, and supernatural closure; the thresholds between these orders resist complete reduction.","refs":["MC_GGVC_L2_01:MRT-B05","MC_GGVC_L2_02:MRT-B05","MC_GGVC_L2_03:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"WB_GGVC@R002","source_variant_id":"canonical:WB_GGVC:WB_GGVC@R002","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"WB","kicker":"Whole book","title":"Summa Theologiae · First Part","context":"","original_title":"Summa Theologiae · Prima pars"},"original_title":"Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title_language_code":"en"}],"seal":"SEAL_GGVC_9FC1720F6D268A62ABA5","original_title":"Summa Theologiae · Prima pars","title_language_code":"en","title_translation":{"schema":"care-title-heading-translation/1","language_code":"en","rendition_type":"machine","originals_preserved":true},"version_work_id":"WORK_STORE_THOMISTICUM_SUMMA_PRIMA","version_label":"Added 17 Oct 2026 · Run 19 Oct 2026 · gpt-5.6-terra / gpt-5.6-sol","version_revision":"cc895dd462302bb1c3c09b4bbe3de851d53b0be0f64f524309677a04c946f570"},{"id":"WORK_STORE_HISTORICAL_MARX_CAPITAL_I_1867","slug":"beta-8e3d28d7330b7735","title":"Capital Volume 1","author":"Karl Marx","edition":"First edition, Hamburg: Otto Meissner, 1867; TextGrid / Kolimo+ TEI transcription","year":"1867","original_year":1867,"publication_year":1867,"description":"","cover_url":"./assets/covers/beta-8e3d28d7330b7735-341d9cd9d3110f51.png","source_url":"https://textgridrep.org/browse/4bnwf.0","source_notices":{"provider":"TextGrid / Kolimo+","source_url":"https://textgridrep.org/browse/4bnwf.0","licence":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/","statement":"CC0 / public-domain text, as stated in the exact TEI header"},"language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","sources":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0","title":"1) Die Waare.","order":1,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"[1]\", \"facs\": \"#f0020\"} -->\n\n\n\n## Erstes Buch.\n Der Produktionsprozess des Kapitals.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## Erstes Kapitel.\n Waare und Geld.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## 1) Die Waare.\n\n\n\n\nDer Reichthum der Gesellschaften, in welchen kapitalistische Pro-\n duktionsweise herrscht, erscheint als eine „ungeheure Waarensammlung“ [Note: Karl Marx: „Zur Kritik der Politischen Oekonomie. Ber-\n lin 1859“, p. 4.] ,\n die einzelne Waare als seine Elementarform. Unsere Untersuchung\n beginnt daher mit der Analyse der Waare.\n\n\n\n\nDie Waare ist zunächst ein äusserer Gegenstand, ein Ding, das durch\n seine Eigenschaften menschliche Bedürfnisse irgend einer Art befriedigt.\n Die Natur dieser Bedürfnisse, ob sie z. B. dem Magen oder der Phantasie\n entspringen, ändert nichts an der Sache [Note: „Desire implies want; it is the appetite of the mind, and as natural as\n hunger to the body . . . . the greatest number (of things) have their value from\n supplying the wants of the mind.“ Nicholas Barbon: „A Discourse on\n coining the new money lighter, in answer to Mr. Locke’s Consi-\n derations etc. London 1696“, p. 2, 3.] . Es handelt sich hier auch\n nicht darum, wie die Sache das menschliche Bedürfniss befriedigt, ob un-\n mittelbar als Lebensmittel, d. h. als Gegenstand des Genusses, oder auf\n einem Umweg, als Produktionsmittel.\n\n\n\n\nJedes nützliche Ding, wie Eisen, Papier u. s. w., ist unter doppeltem\n [Running matter: I. 1] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"2\", \"facs\": \"#f0021\"} --> Gesichtspunkt zu betrachten, nach Qualität und Quantität. Jedes\n solche Ding ist ein Ganzes vieler Eigenschaften und kann daher nach ver-\n schiedenen Seiten nützlich sein. Diese verschiedenen Seiten und daher\n die mannigfachen Gebrauchsweisen der Dinge zu entdecken, ist geschicht-\n liche That [Note: „Things have an intrinsick vertue (diess bei Barbon die specifische Be-\n zeichnung für Gebrauchswerth), which in all places have the same vertue; as\n the loadstone to attract iron“ (l. c. p. 16). Die Eigenschaft des Magnets, Eisen\n anzuziehn, wurde erst nützlich, sobald man vermittelst derselben die magnetische\n Polarität entdeckt hatte.] . So ist die Findung gesellschaftlicher Masse für die Quan-\n tität der nützlichen Dinge. Die Verschiedenheit der Waarenmasse ent-\n springt theils aus der verschiedenen Natur der zu messenden Gegenstände,\n theils aus Convention.\n\n\n\n\nDie Nützlichkeit eines Dings für das menschliche Leben macht es\n zum Gebrauchswerth [Note: „The natural worth of anything consists in its fitness to supply the\n necessities, or serve the conveniences of human life.“ (John Locke: „Some\n Considerations on the Consequences of the Lowering of Interest.\n 1691“ in „Works edit. Lond. 1777“ V. II p. 28). Im 17. Jahrhundert finden\n wir noch häufig bei englischen Schriftstellern „Worth“ für Gebrauchswerth und\n „Value“ für Tauschwerth, ganz im Geist einer Sprache, die es liebt, die\nunmittelbare Sache germanisch und die reflectirte Sache romanisch aus-\n zudrücken.] . Abkürzend nennen wir das nützliche Ding\n selbst oder den Waarenkörper, wie Eisen, Weizen, Diamant u. s. w.,\nGebrauchswerth, Gut, Artikel. Bei Betrachtung der Gebrauchs-\n werthe wird stets quantitative Bestimmtheit vorausgesetzt, wie Dutzend\n Uhren, Elle Leinwand, Tonne Eisen u. s. w. Die Gebrauchswerthe der\n Waaren liefern das Material einer eignen Disciplin, der Waaren-\n kunde [Note: In der bürgerlichen Gesellschaft herrscht die fictio juris, dass jeder Mensch\n als Waarenkäufer eine encyklopädische Waarenkenntniss besitzt.] . Der Gebrauchswerth verwirklicht sich nur im Gebrauch oder\n der Consumtion. Gebrauchswerthe bilden den stofflichen Inhalt\n des Reichthums, welches immer seine gesellschaftliche Form\n sei. In der von uns zu betrachtenden Gesellschaftsform bilden sie zu-\n gleich die stofflichen Träger des — Tauschwerths.\n\n\n\n\nDer Tauschwerth erscheint zunächst als das quantitative Ver-\n hältniss, die Proportion, worin sich Gebrauchswerthe einer Art gegen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"3\", \"facs\": \"#f0022\"} --> Gebrauchswerthe anderer Art austauschen [Note: „La valeur consiste dans le rapport d’échange qui se trouve entre\n telle chose et telle autre, entre telle mesure d’une production et telle mesure d’une\n autre.“ (Le Trosne: „De L’Intérêt Social“. Physiocrates. ed.\n Daire. Paris 1846. p. 889.)] , ein Verhältniss, das bestän-\n dig mit Zeit und Ort wechselt. Der Tauschwerth scheint daher etwas\n Zufälliges und rein Relatives, ein der Waare innerlicher, immanenter\n Tauschwerth (valeur intrinsèque) also eine contradictio in adjecto  [Note: „Nothing can have an intrinsick value“ (N. Barbon l. c. p. 16), oder\n wie Butler sagt:\n„The value of a thing\n Is just as much as it will bring.“] .\n Betrachten wir die Sache näher.\n\n\n\n\nEine einzelne Waare, ein Quarter Weizen z. B. tauscht sich in den\nverschiedensten Proportionen mit andern Artikeln aus. Dennoch\n bleibt sein Tauschwerth unverändert, ob in x Stiefelwichse, y Seide,\n z Gold u. s. w. ausgedrückt. Er muss also von diesen seinen verschiede-\n nen Ausdrucksweisen unterscheidbar sein.\n\n\n\n\nNehmen wir ferner zwei Waaren, z. B. Weizen und Eisen. Welches\n immer ihr Austauschverhältniss, es ist stets darstellbar in einer Gleichung,\n worin ein gegebenes Quantum Weizen irgend einem Quantum Eisen gleich-\n gesetzt wird, z. B. 1 Quarter Weizen = a Ctr. Eisen. Was besagt\n diese Gleichung? Dass derselbe Werth in zwei verschiednen\n Dingen, in 1 Qrtr. Weizen und ebenfalls in a Ctr. Eisen existirt. Beide\n sind also gleich einem Dritten, das an und für sich weder das eine, noch\n das andere ist. Jedes der beiden, soweit es Tauschwerth, muss also, un-\n abhängig von dem andern, auf diess Dritte reducirbar sein.\n\n\n\n\nEin einfaches geometrisches Beispiel veranschauliche diess. Um den\n Flächeninhalt aller gradlinigen Figuren zu bestimmen und zu vergleichen,\n löst man sie in Dreiecke auf. Das Dreieck selbst reducirt man auf einen\n von seiner sichtbaren Figur ganz verschiednen Ausdruck — das halbe\n Produkt seiner Grundlinie mit seiner Höhe. Ebenso sind die Tauschwerthe\n der Waaren zu reduciren auf ein Gemeinsames, wovon sie ein Mehr\n oder Minder darstellen.\n\n\n\n\nDass die Substanz des Tauschwerths ein von der physisch-handgreif-\n lichen Existenz der Waare oder ihrem Dasein als Gebrauchswerth\n [Running matter: 1*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"4\", \"facs\": \"#f0023\"} --> durchaus Verschiednes und Unabhängiges, zeigt ihr Austauschverhältniss\n auf den ersten Blick. Es ist charakterisirt eben durch die Abstraktion\n vom Gebrauchswerth. Dem Tauschwerth nach betrachtet ist näm-\n lich eine Waare grade so gut als jede andre, wenn sie nur in richtiger\n Proportion vorhanden ist [Note: „One sort of wares are as good as another, if the value be equal. There\n is no difference or distinction in things of equal value … One hundred\n pounds worth of lead or iron, is of as great a value as one hundred pounds worth\n of silver and gold.“ (N. Barbon l. c. p. 53 u. 7.)] .\n\n\n\n\nUnabhängig von ihrem Austauschverhältniss oder von der Form,\n worin sie als Tausch-Werthe erscheinen, sind die Waaren daher\n zunächst als Werthe schlechthin zu betrachten [Note: Wenn wir künftig das Wort „Werth“ ohne weitere Bestimmung brau-\n chen, so handelt es sich immer vom Tauschwerth.] .\n\n\n\n\nAls Gebrauchsgegenstände oder Güter sind die Waaren körperlich\n verschiedne Dinge. Ihr Werth sein bildet dagegen ihre Einheit.\n Diese Einheit entspringt nicht aus der Natur, sondern aus der Gesellschaft.\n Die gemeinsame gesellschaftliche Substanz, die sich in\n verschiednen Gebrauchswerthen nur verschieden darstellt, ist — die\n Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nAls Werthe sind die Waaren nichts als krystallisirte Arbeit.\n Die Masseinheit der Arbeit selbst ist die einfache Durchschnitts-\n arbeit, deren Charakter zwar in verschiednen Ländern und Kultur-\n epochen wechselt, aber in einer vorhandnen Gesellschaft gegeben ist.\n Komplicirtere Arbeit gilt nur als potenzirte oder vielmehr multipli-\n cirte einfache Arbeit, so dass z. B. ein kleineres Quantum komplicirter\n Arbeit gleich einem grösseren Quantum einfacher Arbeit. Wie diese\n Reduktion geregelt wird, ist hier gleichgültig. Dass sie beständig vor-\n geht, zeigt die Erfahrung. Eine Waare mag das Produkt der komplicirte-\n sten Arbeit sein. Ihr Werth setzt sie dem Produkt einfacher Arbeit\n gleich und stellt daher selbst nur ein bestimmtes Quantum einfacher Ar-\n beit dar.\n\n\n\n\nEin Gebrauchswerth oder Gut hat also nur einen Werth, weil\nArbeit in ihm vergegenständlicht oder materialisirt ist.\n Wie nun die Grösse seines Werthes messen? Durch das Quantum der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"5\", \"facs\": \"#f0024\"} --> in ihm enthaltenen „werthbildenden Substanz“, der Arbeit. Die Quan-\n tität der Arbeit selbst misst sich an ihrer Zeitdauer und die Arbeits-\n zeit besitzt wieder ihren Massstab an bestimmten Zeittheilen,\n wie Stunde, Tag u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nEs könnte scheinen, dass wenn der Werth einer Waare durch das\n während ihrer Produktion verausgabte Arbeitsquantum bestimmt ist, je\n fauler oder ungeschickter ein Mann, desto werthvoller seine Waare, weil er\n desto mehr Arbeitszeit zu ihrer Verfertigung braucht. Aber nur die\ngesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit zählt als werth-\n bildend. Gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit ist Arbeitszeit, erheischt\n um irgend einen Gebrauchswerth mit den vorhandnen gesellschaftlich - nor-\n malen Produktionsbedingungen und dem gesellschaftlichen Durchschnitts-\n grad von Geschick und Intensivität der Arbeit herzustellen. Nach der\n Einführung des Dampfwebstuhls in England z. B. genügte vielleicht halb\n so viel Arbeit als vorher, um ein gegebenes Quantum Garn in Gewebe zu\n verwandeln. Der englische Handweber brauchte zu dieser Verwandlung\n in der That nach wie vor dieselbe Arbeitszeit, aber das Produkt seiner\n individuellen Arbeitsstunde stellte jetzt nur noch eine halbe gesell-\n schaftliche Arbeitsstunde dar und fiel daher auf die Hälfte seines früheren\n Werths.\n\n\n\n\nEs ist also nur das Quantum gesellschaftlich nothwen-\n diger Arbeit oder die zur Herstellung eines Gebrauchs-\n werths gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit, welche\n seine Werthgrösse bestimmt. Die einzelne Waare gilt hier überhaupt\n als Durchschnittsexemplar ihrer Art [Note: „Toutes les productions d’un même genre ne forment proprement qu’une\n masse, dont le prix se détermine en général et sans égard aux circonstances par-\n ticulières“. (Le Trosne l. c. p. 893.)] . Waaren, worin gleich grosse\n Arbeitsquanta enthalten sind, oder die in derselben Arbeitszeit\n hergestellt werden können, haben daher dieselbe Werthgrösse.\n Der Werth einer Waare verhält sich zum Werth jeder andern Waare, wie\n die zur Produktion der einen nothwendige Arbeitszeit zu der für die Pro-\n duktion der andern nothwendigen Arbeitszeit. „Als Werthe sind alle\n Waaren nur bestimmte Masse festgeronnener Arbeitszeit“ [Note: K. Marx l. c. p. 6.] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"6\", \"facs\": \"#f0025\"} -->\n\nDie Werthgrösse einer Waare bliebe daher constant, wäre die\n zu ihrer Produktion erheischte Arbeitszeit constant. Letztere wech-\n selt aber mit jedem Wechsel in der Produktivkraft der Arbeit.\n Die Produktivkraft der Arbeit ist durch mannigfache Umstände be-\n stimmt, unter andern durch den Durchschnittsgrad des Geschickes der\n Arbeiter, die Entwicklungsstufe der Wissenschaft und ihrer technolo-\n gischen Anwendbarkeit, die gesellschaftliche Combination des Pro-\n duktionsprozesses, den Umfang und die Wirkungsfähigkeit der Pro-\n duktionsmittel, und durch Naturverhältnisse. Dasselbe Quantum\n Arbeit stellt sich z. B. mit günstiger Jahreszeit in 8 Bushel Weizen\n dar, mit ungünstiger in nur 4. Dasselbe Quantum Arbeit liefert mehr\n Metalle in reichhaltigen, als in armen Minen u. s. w. Diamanten kommen\n selten in der Erdrinde vor und ihre Findung kostet daher im Durch-\n schnitt viel Arbeitszeit. Folglich stellen sie in wenig Volumen viel Ar-\n beit dar. Jacob bezweifelt, dass Gold jemals seinen vollen Werth be-\n zahlt hat. Noch mehr gilt diess vom Diamant. Nach Eschwege\n hatte 1823 die achtzigjährige Gesammtausbeute der brasilischen Diamant-\n gruben noch nicht den Werth des 1½jährigen Durchschnittsprodukts der\n brasilischen Zucker- oder Kaffeepflanzungen erreicht. Mit reichhaltigeren\n Gruben würde dasselbe Arbeitsquantum sich in mehr Diamanten darstel-\n len und ihr Werth sinken. Gelingt es mit wenig Arbeit Kohle in Dia-\n mant zu verwandeln, so kann sein Werth unter den von Ziegelsteinen fal-\n len. Allgemein: Je grösser die Produktivkraft der Arbeit, desto kleiner\n die zur Herstellung eines Artikels erheischte Arbeitszeit, desto kleiner die\n in ihm krystallisirte Arbeitsmasse, desto kleiner sein Werth. Umgekehrt,\n je kleiner die Produktivkraft der Arbeit, desto grösser die zur Herstellung\n eines Artikels nothwendige Arbeitszeit, desto grösser sein Werth. Die\nWerthgrösse einer Waare wechselt also direkt wie das Quan-\n tum und umgekehrt wie die Produktivkraft der sich in ihr ver-\n wirklichenden Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nWir kennen jetzt die Substanz des Werths. Es ist die Arbeit.\n Wir kennen sein Grössenmass. Es ist die Arbeitszeit. Seine\nForm, die den Werth eben zum Tausch-Werth stempelt, bleibt zu\n analysiren. Vorher jedoch sind die bereits gefundenen Bestimmungen\n etwas näher zu entwickeln.\n\n\n\n\nEin Ding kann Gebrauchswerth sein, ohne Tauschwerth\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"7\", \"facs\": \"#f0026\"} --> zu sein. Es ist diess der Fall, wenn sein Dasein für den Menschen nicht\n durch Arbeit vermittelt ist. So Luft, jungfräulicher Boden, natürliche\n Wiesen, wildwachsendes Holz u. s. w. Ein Ding kann nützlich und Pro-\n dukt menschlicher Arbeit sein, ohne Waare zu sein. Wer durch sein\n Produkt sein eignes Bedürfniss befriedigt, schafft zwar Gebrauchs-\n werth, aber nicht Waare. Um Waare zu produciren, muss er nicht\n nur Gebrauchswerth produciren, sondern Gebrauchswerth für\n andre, gesellschaftlichen Gebrauchswerth. Endlich kann\n kein Ding Werth sein, ohne Gebrauchsgegenstand zu sein. Ist es nutz-\n los, so ist auch die in ihm enthaltene Arbeit nutzlos, zählt nicht als Arbeit\n und bildet daher keinen Werth.\n\n\n\n\nUrsprünglich erschien uns die Waare als ein Zwieschläch-\n tiges, Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth. Näher betrachtet wird sich\n zeigen, dass auch die in der Waare enthaltene Arbeit zwie-\n schlächtig ist. Dieser Punkt, der von mir zuerst kritisch entwickelt\n wurde [Note: l. c. p. 12, 13 und passim.] , ist der Springpunkt, um den sich das Verständniss der po-\n litischen Oekonomie dreht.\n\n\n\n\nNehmen wir zwei Waaren, etwa einen Rock und 10 Ellen Leinwand.\n Der erstere habe den zweifachen Werth der letzteren, so dass wenn\n 10 Ellen Leinwand = W, der Rock = 2 W.\n\n\n\n\nDer Rock ist ein Gebrauchswerth, der ein besondres Bedürfniss be-\n friedigt. Um ihn hervorzubringen, bedarf es einer bestimmten Art\n zweckmässig produktiver Thätigkeit. Sie ist bestimmt nach\n Zweck, Operationsweise, Gegenstand, Mitteln und Resultat. Die Arbeit,\n deren Nützlichkeit sich so im Gebrauchswerth ihres Produkts oder darin\n darstellt, dass ihr Produkt ein Gebrauchswerth ist, heisse hier der Verein-\n fachung halber kurzweg nützliche Arbeit. Unter diesem Gesichts-\n punkt ist sie stets betrachtet in Bezug auf den Nutzeffekt, dessen\n Hervorbringung sie bezweckt.\n\n\n\n\nWie Rock und Leinwand qualitativ verschiedne Gebrauchs-\n werthe, so sind die ihr Dasein vermittelnden Arbeiten qualitativ\n verschieden — Schneiderarbeit und Weberei. Wären jene\n Dinge nicht qualitativ verschiedne Gebrauchswerthe und daher Produkte\n qualitativ verschiedner nützlicher Arbeiten, so könnten sie sich über-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"8\", \"facs\": \"#f0027\"} --> haupt nicht als Waaren gegenübertreten. Rock tauscht sich nicht aus\n gegen Rock, derselbe Gebrauchswerth nicht gegen denselben Gebrauchswerth.\n\n\n\n\nIn der Gesammtheit der verschiedenartigen Gebrauchswerthe oder\n Waarenkörper erscheint eine Gesammtheit eben so mannigfaltiger, nach\n Gattung, Art, Familie, Unterart, Varietät verschiedner nützlicher Arbeiten\n — eine gesellschaftliche Theilung der Arbeit. Sie ist Exi-\n stenzbedingung der Waarenproduktion, obgleich Waarenproduktion nicht\n umgekehrt Existenzbedingung gesellschaftlicher Arbeitstheilung. In der\n altindischen Gemeinde ist die Arbeit gesellschaftlich getheilt, ohne dass die\nProdukte zu Waaren werden. Oder, ein näher liegendes Beispiel,\n in jeder Fabrik ist die Arbeit systematisch getheilt, aber diese Theilung\n nicht dadurch vermittelt, dass die Arbeiter ihre individuellen Pro-\n dukte austauschen. Nur Produkte selbstständiger und von einander\n unabhängiger Privatarbeiten treten einander als Waaren\n gegenüber.\n\n\n\n\nMan hat also gesehn : In dem Gebrauchswerth jeder Waare steckt\n eine bestimmte zweckmässig produktive Thätigkeit oder nützliche Arbeit.\n Gebrauchswerthe können sich nicht als Waaren gegenübertreten, wenn\n nicht qualitativ verschiedne nützliche Arbeiten in ihnen stecken. In einer\n Gesellschaft, deren Produkte allgemein die Form der Waare anneh-\n men, d. h. in einer Gesellschaft von Waarenproducenten, entwickelt sich\n dieser qualitative Unterschied der nützlichen Arbeiten, welche unabhängig\n von einander als Privatgeschäfte selbstständiger Producenten betrieben\n werden, zu einem vielgliedrigen System, zu einer gesellschaftlichen Thei-\n lung der Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nDem Rock ist es übrigens gleichgültig, ob er vom Schneider oder\n vom Kunden des Schneiders getragen wird. In beiden Fällen wirkt er als\n Gebrauchswerth. Ebensowenig ist das Verhältniss zwischen dem Rock\n und der ihn producirenden Arbeit an und für sich dadurch verändert, dass\n die Schneiderarbeit eigne Profession wird, selbstständiges Glied der gesell-\n schaftlichen Theilung der Arbeit. Wo ihn das Kleidungsbedürfniss zwang,\n hat der Mensch Jahrtausende lang geschneidert, bevor aus einem Menschen\n ein Schneider ward. Aber das Dasein von Rock, Leinwand, jedem nicht\n von Natur vorhandnen Element des stofflichen Reichthums,\n musste immer vermittelt sein durch eine spezielle, zweckmässig produktive\n Thätigkeit, die besondere Naturstoffe besondern menschlichen Bedürfnissen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"9\", \"facs\": \"#f0028\"} --> assimilirt. Als Bildnerin von Gebrauchswerthen, als nützliche Ar-\n beit, ist die Arbeit daher von allen Gesellschaftsformen unabhängige\n Existenzbedingung des Menschen, ewige Naturnothwendigkeit, um den\n Stoffwechsel zwischen Mensch und Natur, also das menschliche Leben zu\n vermitteln.\n\n\n\n\nDie Gebrauchswerthe Rock, Leinwand u. s. w., kurz die Waaren-\n körper, sind Verbindungen von zwei Elementen, Naturstoff\n und Arbeit. Zieht man die Gesammtsumme aller verschiedenen nützlichen\n Arbeiten ab, die in Rock, Leinwand u. s. w. stecken, so bleibt stets ein\n materielles Substrat zurück, das ohne Zuthun des Menschen von Natur vor-\n handen ist. Der Mensch kann in seiner Produktion nur verfahren, wie die\n Natur selbst, d. h. nur die Formen der Stoffe ändern [Note: „Tutti i fenomeni dell’ universo, sieno essi prodotti della mano dell’\n uomo, ovvero delle universali leggi della fisica, non ci danno idea di attuale crea-\n zione, ma unicamente di una modificazione della materia. Accostare\n e separare sono gli unici elementi che l’ingegno umano ritrova analizando l’idea\n della riproduzione; e tanto e riproduzione di valore (Gebrauchswerth, ob-\n gleich Verri hier in seiner Polemik gegen die Physiokraten selbst nicht recht\n weiss, von welcher Sorte Werth er spricht) e di richezze se la terra, l’aria e l’acqua\n ne campi si transmutino in grano, come se colla mano dell’ uomo il glutine di un insetto\n si transmuti in velluto, ovvero alcuni pezzetti di metallo si organizzino a formare una\n ripetizione“. (Pietro Verri: „Meditazioni sulla Economia Poli-\n tica“ (zuerst gedruckt 1773) in der Ausgabe der italienischen Oekonomen von\nCustodi, Parte Moderna, t. XV p. 22.)] . Noch\n mehr. In dieser Arbeit der Formung selbst wird er beständig unterstützt\n von Naturkräften. Arbeit ist also nicht die einzige Quelle der\n von ihr producirten Gebrauchswerthe, des stofflichen\n Reichthums. Die Arbeit ist sein Vater, wie William Petty sagt,\n und die Erde seine Mutter.\n\n\n\n\nGehn wir nun von der Waare, so weit sie Gebrauchsgegenstand, über\n zum Waaren-Werth.\n\n\n\n\nNach unsrer Unterstellung hat der Rock den doppelten Werth der\n Leinwand. Diess ist aber nur ein quantitativer Unterschied, der uns\n zunächst noch nicht interessirt. Wir erinnern daher, dass wenn der\n Werth eines Rockes doppelt so gross als der von 10 Ellen Leinwand,\n 20 Ellen Leinwand dieselbe Werthgrösse haben wie ein Rock.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"10\", \"facs\": \"#f0029\"} --> Als Werthe sind Rock und Leinwand Dinge von gleicher Substanz,\n objektive Ausdrücke gleichartiger Arbeit. Aber Schneider-\n arbeit und Weberei sind qualitativ verschiedne Arbeiten. Es giebt\n jedoch Gesellschaftszustände, worin derselbe Mensch abwechselnd\n schneidert und webt, diese beiden verschiednen Arbeitsweisen daher nur\nModificationen der Arbeit desselben Individuums und\n noch nicht besondre feste Functionen verschiedner Individuen sind, ganz\n wie der Rock, den unser Schneider heute, und die Hosen, die er morgen\n macht, nur Variationen derselben individuellen Arbeit voraussetzen. Der\n Augenschein lehrt ferner, dass in unsrer kapitalistischen Gesellschaft, je\n nach der wechselnden Richtung der Arbeitsnachfrage, eine gegebene\n Portion menschlicher Arbeit abwechselnd in der Form von\n Schneiderei oder in der Form von Weberei zugeführt wird. Dieser Form-\n wechsel der Arbeit mag nicht ohne Friction abgehn, aber er muss gehn.\n Sieht man ab von der Bestimmtheit der produktiven Thätigkeit und daher\n vom nützlichen Charakter der Arbeit, so bleibt das an ihr, dass sie eine\nVerausgabung menschlicher Arbeitskraft ist. Schneider-\n arbeit und Weberei, obgleich qualitativ verschiedne produktive Thätig-\n keiten, sind beide produktive Verausgabung von menschlichem Hirn,\n Muskel, Nerv, Hand u. s. w., und in diesem Sinn beide menschliche\n Arbeit. Es sind nur zwei verschiedne Formen, menschliche Arbeits-\n kraft zu verausgaben. Allerdings muss die menschliche Arbeitskraft selbst\n mehr oder minder entwickelt sein, um in dieser oder jener Form veraus-\n gabt zu werden. Der Werth der Waaren aber stellt menschliche Arbeit\n schlechthin dar, Verausgabung menschlicher Arbeitskraft über-\n haupt. Wie nun in der bürgerlichen Gesellschaft ein General oder Ban-\n quier eine grosse, der Mensch schlechthin dagegen eine sehr schäbige\n Rolle spielt [Note: Vgl. Hegel, „Philosophie des Rechts. Berlin 1840“, p. 250,\n §. 190.] , so steht es hier auch mit der menschlichen Arbeit.\n Sie ist Verausgabung einfacher Arbeitskraft, die jeder gewöhnliche\n Mensch, ohne besondere Entwicklung, in seinem leiblichen Organismus be-\n sitzt. Die Arbeitskraft eines Bauernknechts gelte z. B. für einfache Ar-\n beitskraft, ihre Verausgabung daher für einfache Arbeit oder mensch-\n liche Arbeit ohne weitern Schnörkel, Schneiderarbeit dagegen für\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"11\", \"facs\": \"#f0030\"} --> Verausgabung höher entwickelter Arbeitskraft. Während sich der Arbeits-\n tag des Bauernknechts daher etwa im Werthausdruck von ½ W, stellt\n sich der Arbeitstag des Schneiders im Werthausdrucke von W dar [Note: Der Leser muss aufmerken, dass hier nicht vom Lohn oder Werth die\n Rede ist, den der Arbeiter etwa für einen Arbeitstag erhält, sondern vom Waaren-\nwerth, worin sich sein Arbeitstag vergegenständlicht. Die Kategorie des Ar-\n beitslohns existirt überhaupt noch nicht auf dieser Stufe unsrer Darstellung.] .\n Dieser Unterschied ist jedoch nur quantitativ. Wenn der Rock das\n Produkt eines Arbeitstags des Schneiders, hat er denselben Werth wie das\n Produkt von 2 Arbeitstagen des Bauernknechts. So zählt aber die\n Schneiderarbeit immer nur als multiplicirte Bauernarbeit. Die ver-\n schiednen Proportionen, worin verschiedne Arbeitsarten auf einfache Ar-\n beit als ihre Masseinheit reducirt sind, werden durch einen gesell-\n schaftlichen Prozess hinter dem Rücken der Produzenten festgesetzt und\n scheinen ihnen daher durch das Herkommen gegeben. Der Vereinfachung\n halber gilt uns im Folgenden jede Art Arbeitskraft unmittelbar für ein-\n fache Arbeitskraft, wodurch nur die Mühe der Reduktion erspart wird.\n\n\n\n\nWie also in den Werthen Rock und Leinwand von dem Unter-\n schied ihrer Gebrauchswerthe abstrahirt ist, so in der Arbeit,\n die diese Werthe darstellen, von dem Unterschied der nützlichen\n Formen, worin sie das einemal Schneiderarbeit ist, das andremal\nWeberei. Wie die Gebrauchswerthe Rock und Leinwand Ver-\n bindungen zweckbestimmter, produktiver Thätigkeiten mit Tuch und\n Garn sind, die Werthe Rock und Leinwand dagegen blosse gleich-\n artige Arbeitsgallerten, so gilt auch die in diesen Werthen\n enthaltene Arbeit nicht durch ihr produktives Verhalten zu Tuch und Garn,\n sondern nur als Verausgabung menschlicher Arbeitskraft.\n Bildungselemente der Gebrauchswerthe Rock und Leinwand sind\n Schneiderarbeit und Weberei eben durch ihre verschiednen Qualitäten,\nSubstanz des Rockwerths und Leinwandwerths sind sie nur, soweit\n von ihrer besondern Qualität abstrahirt wird und beide gleiche\n Qualität besitzen, die Qualität menschlicher Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nRock und Leinwand sind aber nicht nur Werthe überhaupt, son-\n dern Werthe von bestimmter Grösse und nach unsrer Unterstellung\n ist der Rock doppelt so viel werth, als 10 Ellen Leinwand. Woher diese\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"12\", \"facs\": \"#f0031\"} --> Verschiedenheit ihrer Werthgrössen? Daher dass die Leinwand nur\n halb so viel Arbeit enthält, als der Rock, sodass zur Produktion des letz-\n tern die Arbeitskraft während doppelt soviel Zeit verausgabt werden\n muss, als zur Produktion der erstern.\n\n\n\n\nWenn also mit Bezug auf den Gebrauchswerth die in der\n Waare enthaltne Arbeit nur qualitativ gilt, gilt sie mit Bezug auf die\nWerthgrösse nur quantitativ, nachdem sie bereits auf menschliche\n Arbeit ohne weitere Qualität reducirt ist. Dort handelt es sich um das\nWie und Was der Arbeit, hier um ihr Wie Viel, ihre Zeitdauer. Da die\n Werthgrösse einer Waare nur das Quantum der in ihr enthaltnen Arbeit\n misst, müssen Waaren in gewisser Proportion stets gleich grosse\n Werthe sein.\n\n\n\n\nBleibt die Produktivkraft sage aller zur Produktion eines Rocks\n erheischten nützlichen Arbeiten unverändert, so steigt die Werthgrösse der\n Röcke mit ihrer eignen Quantität. Wenn 1 Rock x, stellen 2 Röcke 2 x\n Arbeitstage dar u. s. w. Nimm aber an, die zur Produktion eines Rocks\n nothwendige Arbeitszeit steige auf das Doppelte oder falle um die Hälfte.\n Im ersten Fall hat ein Rock soviel Werth als vorher zwei Röcke, im letz-\n tern Fall haben zwei Röcke nur so viel Werth, als vorher einer, obgleich\n in beiden Fällen ein Rock nach wie vor dieselben Dienste leistet und die\n in ihm enthaltne nützliche Arbeit nach wie vor von derselben Güte bleibt.\n Aber das in seiner Produktion verausgabte Arbeits quantum hat sich\n verändert.\n\n\n\n\nEin grössres Quantum Gebrauchswerth bildet an und für sich grös-\n sern stofflichen Reichthum, zwei Röcke mehr als einer. Mit zwei\n Röcken kann man zwei Menschen kleiden, mit einem Rock nur einen Men-\n schen u. s. w. Dennoch kann der steigenden Masse des stofflichen Reich-\n thums ein gleichzeitiger Fall seiner Werthgrösse entsprechen. Diese\n gegensätzliche Bewegung entspringt aus der zwieschlächtigen Be-\n stimmung der Arbeit. Produktivkraft ist natürlich stets Produktivkraft\n nützlicher, konkreter Arbeit. Sie drückt in der That nur den Wirkungs-\n grad zweckbestimmter produktiver Thätigkeit in gegebnem Zeitraum aus.\n Die nützliche Arbeit wird daher reichere oder dürftigere Produktenquelle\n im direkten Verhältniss zum Steigen oder Fallen ihrer Produktiv-\n kraft. Dagegen trifft ein Wechsel der Produktivkraft die im Werth\n dargestellte Arbeit an und für sich gar nicht. Da die Produktivkraft der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"13\", \"facs\": \"#f0032\"} --> konkreten nützlichen Form der Arbeit angehört, kann sie natürlich die\n Arbeit nicht mehr berühren, sobald von ihrer konkreten nützlichen Form\n abstrahirt wird. Dieselbe Arbeit stellt sich daher in denselben Zeit-\n räumen stets in derselben Werthgrösse dar, wie immer die Pro-\n duktivkraft wechsle. Aber sie liefert in demselben Zeitraum ver-\n schiedne Quanta Gebrauchswerthe, mehr wenn die Produktiv-\n kraft steigt, weniger, wenn sie sinkt. Im erstern Fall kann es geschehn,\n dass 2 Röcke weniger Arbeit enthalten als früher einer. Derselbe Wech-\n sel der Produktivkraft, der die Fruchtbarkeit der Arbeit und daher die\n Masse der von ihr gelieferten Gebrauchswerthe vermehrt, kann also die\nWerthgrösse selbst der vermehrten Gesammtmasse vermindern,\n wenn er nämlich die zu ihrer Produktion nothwendige Arbeitszeit ab-\n kürzt. Ebenso umgekehrt.\n\n\n\n\nAus dem Bisherigen folgt, dass in der Waare zwar nicht zwei ver-\n schiedene Sorten Arbeit stecken, wohl aber dieselbe Arbeit verschieden\n und selbst entgegengesetzt bestimmt ist, je nachdem sie auf den Ge-\n brauchswerth der Waare als ihr Produkt oder auf den Waaren-\n Werth als ihren bloss gegenständlichen Ausdruck bezogen wird.\n Wie die Waare vor allem Gebrauchsgegenstand sein muss, um Werth zu\n sein, so muss die Arbeit vor allem nützliche Arbeit, zweckbestimmte pro-\n duktive Thätigkeit sein, um als Verausgabung menschlicher\n Arbeitskraft und daher als menschliche Arbeit schlechthin\n zu zählen.\n\n\n\n\nDa bisher nur noch Werthsubstanz und Werthgrösse bestimmt, wen-\n den wir uns jetzt zur Analyse der Werthform.\n\n\n\n\nKehren wir zunächst wieder zurück zur ersten Erscheinungs-\n form des Waarenwerths.\n\n\n\n\nWir nehmen zwei Quanta Waaren, die gleichviel Arbeitszeit\n zu ihrer Produktion kosten, also gleiche Werthgrössen sind, und\n wir haben 40 Ellen Leinwand = 2 Röcke, oder 40 Ellen Lein-\n wand sind zwei Röcke werth. Wir sehn, dass der Werth der Lein-\n wand in einem bestimmten Quantum von Röcken ausgedrückt ist. Der\nWerth einer Waare, so dargestellt im Gebrauchswerth einer andern\n Waare, heisst ihr relativer Werth.\n\n\n\n\nDer relative Werth einer Waare kann wechseln, obgleich ihr Werth\n constant bleibt. Umgekehrt kann ihr relativer Werth constant bleiben,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"14\", \"facs\": \"#f0033\"} --> obgleich ihr Werth wechselt. Die Gleichung: 40 Ellen Leinwand\n = 2 Röcke setzt nämlich voraus, dass beide Waaren gleich viel Arbeit\n kosten. Mit jedem Wechsel in der Produktivkraft der sie hervorbringen-\n den Arbeiten wechselt aber die zu ihrer Produktion nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit. Betrachten wir den Einfluss solcher Wechsel auf den relativen\n Werth.\n\n\n\n\nI. Der Werth der Leinwand wechsle, während der Rockwerth con-\n stant bleibt. Verdoppelt sich die zur Produktion der Leinwand veraus-\n gabte Arbeitszeit, etwa in Folge zunehmender Unfruchtbarkeit des flachs-\n tragenden Bodens, so verdoppelt sich ihr Werth. Statt 40 Ellen Lein-\n wand = 2 Röcke, hätten wir: 40 Ellen Leinwand = 4 Röcke,\n da 2 Röcke jetzt nur halb so viel Arbeitszeit enthalten als 40 Ellen Lein-\n wand. Nimmt dagegen die zur Produktion der Leinwand nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit um die Hälfte ab, etwa in Folge verbesserter Webstühle, so\n sinkt der Leinwandwerth um die Hälfte. Demgemäss jetzt: 40 Ellen\n Leinwand = 1 Rock. Der relative Werth der Waare A, d. h.\n ihr Werth ausgedrückt in der Waare B, steigt und fällt also direkt\n wie der Werth der Waare A, bei gleichbleibendem Werth der\n Waare B.\n\n\n\n\nII. Der Werth der Leinwand bleibe constant, während der Rock-\nwerth wechsle. Verdoppelt sich unter diesen Umständen die zur Pro-\n duktion des Rockes nothwendige Arbeitszeit, etwa in Folge ungünstiger\n Wollschur, so haben wir statt 40 Ellen Leinwand = 2 Röcke jetzt: 40 El-\n len Leinwand = 1 Rock. Fällt dagegen der Werth des Rocks um\n die Hälfte, so 40 Ellen Leinwand = 4 Röcke. Bei gleichbleibendem\n Werth der Waare A, fällt oder steigt daher ihr relativer, in der Waare\n B ausgedrückter Werth im umgekehrten Verhältniss zum\n Werthwechsel von B.\n\n\n\n\nVergleicht man die verschiedenen Fälle sub I und II, so ergiebt sich,\n dass derselbe Wechsel des relativen Werths aus ganzent-\n gegengesetzten Ursachen entspringen kann. So wird aus\n40 Ellen Leinwand = 2 Röcke 1) die Gleichung 40 Ellen\n Leinwand = 4 Röcke, entweder weil der Werth der Leinwand\n sich verdoppelt oder der Werth der Röcke um die Hälfte fällt, und 2) die\n Gleichung 40 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock, entweder weil der Werth\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"15\", \"facs\": \"#f0034\"} --> der Leinwand um die Hälfte sinkt oder der Werth des Rockes auf das\n Doppelte steigt.\n\n\n\n\nIII. Die zur Produktion von Leinwand und Rock nothwendigen\n Arbeitsquanta wechseln gleichzeitig, in derselben Richtung und derselben\n Proportion. In diesem Falle nach wie vor 40 Ellen Leinwand =\n 2 Röcke, wie immer ihre Werthe verändert seien. Man entdeckt ihren\n Werthwechsel, sobald man sie mit einer dritten Waare vergleicht, deren\n Werth constant blieb. Stiegen oder fielen die Werthe aller Waaren gleich-\n zeitig und in derselben Proportion, so blieben ihre relativen Werthe\n unverändert. Ihren wirklichen Werthwechsel ersähe man daraus, dass in\n derselben Arbeitszeit nun allgemein ein grösseres oder kleineres Waaren-\n quantum als vorher geliefert würde.\n\n\n\n\nIV. Die zur Produktion von Leinwand und Rock resp. nothwendigen\n Arbeitszeiten, und daher ihre Werthe, mögen gleichzeitig in derselben\n Richtung wechseln, aber in ungleichem Grad, oder in entgegengesetzter\n Richtung u. s. w. Der Einfluss aller möglichen derartigen Combinatio-\n nen auf den relativen Werth einer Waare ergiebt sich einfach durch An-\n wendung der Fälle I., II. und III.\n\n\n\n\nWir haben eben untersucht, wie weit Wechsel in der relativen\n Werthgrösse einer Waare, der Leinwand, einen Wechsel ihrer eignen\n Werthgrösse wiederspiegelt, und überhaupt den relativen Werth\n nur nach seiner quantitativen Seite betrachtet. Wir wenden uns\n jetzt zu seiner Form. Wenn der relative Werth Darstellungsform\n des Werths, ist der Ausdruck der Aequivalenz zweier Waaren, wie\n x Waare A = y Waare B oder 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock, die ein-\n fache Form desrelativen Werths.\n\n\n\n\nI. Erste oder einfache Form des relativen Werths:\n 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock. (x Waare A = y Waare B.)\n\n\n\n\nDiese Form ist etwas schwierig zu analysiren, weil sie einfach\n ist [Note: Sie ist gewissermassen die Zellenform oder, wie Hegel sagen würde, das\nAn sich des Geldes.] . Die in ihr enthaltenen unterschiedenen Bestimmungen sind ver-\n hüllt, unentwickelt, abstrakt und daher nur durch einige Anstrengung der\n Abstraktionskraft auseinander- und festzuhalten. So viel ergiebt sich\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"16\", \"facs\": \"#f0035\"} --> aber auf den ersten Blick, dass die Form dieselbe bleibt, ob 20 Ellen\n Leinwand = 1 Rock oder 20 Ellen Leinwand = x Röcke [Note: Die wenigen Oekonomen, die sich, wie J. Bailey, mit der Analyse der\n Werthform beschäftigt haben, konnten zu keinem Resultat kommen, einmal, weil\n sie Werthform und Werth verwechseln, zweitens, weil sie, unter dem rohen Ein-\n fluss des praktischen Bürgers, von vorn herein ausschliesslich die quantitative Be-\n stimmtheit ins Auge fassen. „The command of quantity .... constitutes\nvalue“. („Money and its Vicissitudes“. Lond. 1837, p. 11.) Verfasser:\n J. Bailey.] .\n\n\n\n\nLeinwand kömmt auf die Welt in Gestalt eines Gebrauchswerths\n oder nützlichen Dings. Ihre steifleinene Körperlichkeit oder Natural-\n form ist daher nicht ihre Werthform, sondern deren grades Gegen-\n theil. Ihr eignes Werthsein zeigt sie zunächst dadurch, dass sie sich\n auf eine andre Waare, den Rock, als ihr Gleichesbezieht. Wäre\n sie nicht selbst Werth, so könnte sie sich nicht auf den Rock als Werth,\n als Ihresgleichen, beziehn. Qualitativ setzt sie sich den Rock\n gleich, indem sie sich auf ihn bezieht als Vergegenständlichung\n gleichartiger menschlicher Arbeit, d. h. ihrer eignen\n Werthsubstanz, und sie setzt sich nur einen Rock gleich statt\n x Röcke, weil sie nicht nur Werth überhaupt, sondern Werth von be-\n stimmter Grösse ist, ein Rock aber grade soviel Arbeit enthält als\n 20 Ellen Leinwand. Durch diese Beziehung auf den Rock schlägt die\n Leinwand verschiedne Fliegen mit einer Klappe. Indem sie die andre\n Waare sich als Werth gleichsetzt, bezieht sie sich auf sich\n selbst als Werth. Indem sie sich auf sich selbst als Werth be-\n zieht, unterscheidet sie sich zugleich von sich selbst als Ge-\n brauchswerth. Indem sie ihre Werthgrösse — und Werthgrösse\n ist beides, Werth überhaupt und quantitativ gemessner Werth — im\n Rocke ausdrückt, giebt sie ihrem Werthsein eine von ihrem un-\n mittelbaren Dasein unterschiedne Werthform. Indem sie sich so als ein\n in sich selbst Differenzirtes darstellt, stellt sie sich erst wirklich als\n Waare dar — nützliches Ding, das zugleich Werth ist. Soweit die\n Leinwand Gebrauchswerth, ist sie ein selbstständiges Ding. Ihr\nWerth erscheint dagegen nur im Verhältniss zu andrer Waare,\n dem Rocke z. B., ein Verhältniss, worin die Waarenart Rock ihr quali-\n tativ gleichgesetzt wird und daher in bestimmter Quantität\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"17\", \"facs\": \"#f0036\"} --> gleichgilt, sie ersetzt, mit ihr austauschbar ist. Eigne, vom Gebrauchs-\n werth unterschiedne Form erhält der Werth daher nur durch\n seine Darstellung als Tauschwerth.\n\n\n\n\nDer Ausdruck des Leinwandwerths im Rocke prägt dem Rocke\n selbst eine neue Form auf. In der That, was besagt die Werthform\n der Leinwand? Dass der Rock mit ihr austauschbar ist. Wie er geht\n oder liegt, mit Haut und Haaren, in seiner Naturalform Rock be-\n sitzt er jetzt die Form unmittelbarer Austauschbarkeit mit\n andrer Waare, die Form eines austauschbaren Gebrauchswerths oder\nAequivalents. Die Bestimmung des Aequivalents enthält nicht nur,\n dass eine Waare Werth überhaupt ist, sondern dass sie in ihrer ding-\n lichen Gestalt, in ihrer Gebrauchsform, andrer Waare als Werth\n gilt und daher unmittelbar als Tauschwerth für die andre Waare\n da ist.\n\n\n\n\nAls Werth besteht die Leinwand nur aus Arbeit, bildet eine durch-\n sichtig krystallisirte Arbeitsgallerte. In der Wirklichkeit ist dieser Kry-\n stall jedoch sehr trüb. Soweit Arbeit in ihm zu entdecken, und nicht\n jeder Waarenkörper zeigt die Spur der Arbeit, ist es nicht unterschiedslose\n menschliche Arbeit, sondern Weberei, Spinnerei u. s. w., die auch keines-\n wegs seine einzige Substanz bilden, vielmehr mit Naturstoffen verquickt\n sind. Um Leinwand als bloss dinglichen Ausdruck menschlicher Arbeit\n festzuhalten, muss man von allem absehn, was sie wirklich zum Ding\n macht. Gegenständlichkeit der menschlichen Arbeit, die selbst abstrakt\n ist, ohne weitere Qualität und Inhalt, ist nothwendig abstrakte Gegen-\n ständlichkeit, ein Gedankending. So wird das Flachsgewebe zum\n Hirngespinnst. Aber Waaren sind Sachen. Was sie sind, müssen\n sie sachlich sein oder in ihren eignen sachlichen Beziehungen zeigen. In\n der Produktion der Leinwand ist ein bestimmtes Quantum menschlicher\n Arbeitskraft verausgabt worden. Ihr Werth ist der bloss gegenständ-\n liche Reflex der so verausgabten Arbeit, aber er reflektirt sich nicht\n in ihrem Körper. Er offenbart sich, erhält sinnlichen Ausdruck\n durch ihr Werthverhältniss zum Rock. Indem sie ihn als\n Werth sich gleichsetzt, während sie sich zugleich als Ge-\n brauchsgegenstand von ihm unterscheidet, wird der Rock\n die Erscheinungsform des Leinwand-Werths im Gegensatz zum\n [Running matter: I. 2] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"18\", \"facs\": \"#f0037\"} --> Leinwand-Körper, ihre Werthform im Unterschied von ihrer Na-\n turalform [Note: Man spricht desshalb vom Rockwerth der Leinwand, wenn man ihren\n Werth in Röcken, von ihrem Kornwerth, wenn man ihn in Korn darstellt\n u. s. w. Jeder solcher Ausdruck besagt, dass es ihr Werth ist, der in den Ge-\n brauchswerthen Rock, Korn u. s. w. erscheint.] .\n\n\n\n\nIn dem relativen Werthausdruck: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock\n oder x Leinwand ist y Rockwerth, gilt der Rock zwar nur als Werth\n oder Arbeitsgallerte, aber eben dadurch gilt die Arbeitsgallerte als Rock,\n der Rock als die Form, worin menschliche Arbeit gerinnt [Note: In gewisser Art gehts dem Menschen wie der Waare. Da er weder mit\n einem Spiegel auf die Welt kommt, noch als Fichtescher Philosoph: Ich bin Ich,\n bespiegelt sich der Mensch zuerst nur in einem andern Menschen. Erst durch die\n Beziehung auf den Menschen Paul als seinesgleichen, bezieht sich der Mensch\n Peter auf sich selbst als Mensch. Damit gilt ihm aber auch der Paul mit Haut\n und Haaren, in seiner paulinischen Leiblichkeit, als Erscheinungsform des genus\n Mensch.] . Der Ge-\n brauchswerth Rock wird nur zur Erscheinungsform des Leinwand-Werths,\n weil sich die Leinwand auf das Rockmaterial als unmittelbare\n Materiatur abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit bezieht, also\n Arbeit gleicher Art wie die in ihr selbst vergegenständlichte. Der\n Gegenstand Rock gilt ihr als sinnlich handgreifliche Gegenständlichkeit\n gleichartiger menschlicher Arbeit, daher als Werth in Naturalform. Da\n sie als Werth gleichen Wesens mit dem Rock ist, wird die Naturalform\n Rock so zur Erscheinungsform ihres eignen Werths. Aber die im Ge-\n brauchswerth Rock dargestellte Arbeit ist nicht menschliche Arbeit\n schlechthin, sondern eine bestimmte, nützliche Arbeit, Schneiderarbeit.\n Menschliche Arbeit schlechthin, Verausgabung menschlicher Arbeitskraft,\n ist zwar jeder Bestimmung fähig, aber an und für sich unbestimmt. Ver-\n wirklichen, vergegenständlichen kann sie sich nur, sobald die menschliche\n Arbeitskraft in bestimmter Form verausgabt wird, als bestimmte\n Arbeit, denn nur der bestimmten Arbeit steht ein Naturstoff gegen-\n über, ein äusseres Material, worin sie sich vergegenständlicht. Bloss der\n Hegel’sche „Begriff“ bringt es fertig, sich ohne äussern Stoff zu objek-\n tiviren [Note: „Der Begriff, welcher zunächst nur subjektiv ist, schreitet, ohne dass es] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"19\", \"facs\": \"#f0038\"} -->\n\nDie Leinwand kann sich nicht auf den Rock als Werth oder incar-\n nirte menschliche Arbeit beziehn, ohne sich auf Schneider-\n arbeit als die unmittelbare Verwirklichungsform mensch-\n licher Arbeit zu beziehen. Was jedoch die Leinwand am Gebrauchs-\n werth Rock interessirt, ist weder seine wollne Behäbigkeit, noch sein zu-\n geknöpftes Wesen, noch irgend eine andre nützliche Qualität, die ihn zum\n Gebrauchswerth stempelt. Er dient ihr nur dazu, ihre Werthgegenständ-\n lichkeit im Unterschied von ihrer steifleinenen Gebrauchsgegenständlichkeit\n darzustellen. Sie hätte denselben Zweck erreicht, wenn sie ihren Werth\n in Assa Fötida oder Poudrette oder Stiefelwichse ausgedrückt. Die\nSchneiderarbeit gilt ihr daher ebenfalls nicht, sofern sie zweckmäs-\n sig produktive Thätigkeit, nützliche Arbeit, sondern nur sofern sie als\nbestimmte Arbeit Verwirklichungsform, Vergegenständ-\n lichungsweise menschlicher Arbeit überhaupt ist. Drückte\n die Leinwand ihren Werth statt im Rock in Stiefelwichse aus, so gälte ihr\n auch statt Schneidern Wichsen als die unmittelbare Verwirklichungsform\n abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit [Note: Sofern man nämlich populär die Bereitung der Wichse selbst Wichsen\n heisst.] . Erscheinungsform des Werths oder\n Aequivalent wird ein Gebrauchswerth oder Waarenkörper also nur da-\n durch, dass sich eine andere Waare auf die in ihm enthaltne konkrete,\n nützliche Arbeitsart als die unmittelbare Verwirklichungsform abstrakter\n menschlicher Arbeit bezieht.\n\n\n\n\nWir stehn hier bei dem Springpunkt aller Schwierigkeiten, welche\n das Verständniss der Werthform hindern. Es ist relativ leicht, den\n Werth der Waare von ihrem Gebrauchswerth zu unterscheiden, oder die\n den Gebrauchswerth formende Arbeit von derselben Arbeit, so weit sie\n bloss als Verausgabung mensehlicher Arbeitskraft im Waarenwerth berech-\n net wird. Betrachtet man Waare oder Arbeit in der einen Form, so\n nicht in der andern und vice versa. Diese abstrakten Gegensätze fallen\n von selbst auseinander und sind daher leicht auseinander zu halten. An-\n [Note: dazu eines äusseren Materials oder Stoffs bedarf, seiner eignen Thätigkeit gemäss\n dazu fort, sich zu objektiviren.“ Hegel, „Logik“ p. 367 in der „Encyklo-\n pädie: Erster Theil. Berlin 1840.“] \n [Running matter: 2*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"20\", \"facs\": \"#f0039\"} --> ders mit der Werthform, die nur im Verhältniss von Waare zu Waare\n existirt. Der Gebrauchswerth oder Waarenkörper spielt hier eine neue\n Rolle. Er wird zur Erscheinungsform des Waarenwerths, also seines\n eignen Gegentheils. Ebenso wird die im Gebrauchswerth enthaltene kon-\n krete nützliche Arbeit zu ihrem eignen Gegentheil, zur blossen Verwirk-\n lichungsform abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit. Statt auseinanderzufal-\n len, reflektiren sich die gegensätzlichen Bestimmungen der Waare hier in\n einander. So befremdlich diess auf ersten Blick, erweist es sich bei wei-\n terem Nachdenken als nothwendig. Die Waare ist von Haus aus ein\nzwieschlächtig Ding, Gebrauchswerth und Werth, Produkt nütz-\n licher Arbeit und abstrakte Arbeitsgallerte. Um sich darzustellen als\n das was sie ist, muss sie daher ihre Form verdoppeln. Die Form\n eines Gebrauchswerths besitzt sie von Natur. Es ist ihre Naturalform.\n Werthform erwirbt sie erst im Umgang mit andren Waaren. Aber ihre\n Werthform muss selbst wieder gegenständliche Form sein. Die ein-\n zigen gegenständlichen Formen der Waaren sind ihre Gebrauchsgestalten,\n ihre Naturalformen. Da nun die Naturalform einer Waare, der Leinwand\n z. B., das grade Gegentheil ihrer Werthform ist, muss sie eine andre\n Naturalform, die Naturalform einer andern Waare zu ihrer\nWerthform machen. Was sie nicht unmittelbar für sich selbst, kann\n sie unmittelbar für andre Waare und daher auf einem Umweg für sich\n selbst thun. Sie kann ihren Werth nicht in ihrem eignen Körper oder in\n ihrem eignen Gebrauchswerth ausdrücken, aber sie kann sich auf einen\n andern Gebrauchswerth oder Waarenkörper als unmittelbares Werthdasein\n beziehn. Sie kann sich nicht zu der in ihr selbst, wohl aber zu der in\n andrer Waarenart enthaltenen konkreten Arbeit als blosser Verwirklichungs-\n form abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit verhalten. Sie braucht dazu nur die\n andre Waare sich als Aequivalent gleichzusetzen. Der Gebrauchs-\n werth einer Waare existirt überhaupt nur für eine andre Waare, soweit er\n in dieser Weise zur Erscheinungsform ihres Werths dient. Betrachtet\n man in dem einfachen relativen Werthausdrucke: x Waare A = y Waare\n B nur das quantitative Verhältniss, so findet man auch nur die oben\n entwickelten Gesetze über die Bewegung des relativen Werths, die alle\n darauf beruhn, dass die Werthgrösse der Waaren durch die zu ihrer Pro-\n duktion nothwendige Arbeitszeit bestimmt ist. Betrachtet man aber das\n Werthverhältniss der beiden Waaren nach seiner qualitativen Seite,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"21\", \"facs\": \"#f0040\"} --> so entdeckt man in jenem einfachen Werthausdruck das Geheimniss der\n Werthform und daher, in nuce, des Geldes [Note: Es ist kaum verwunderlich, dass die Oekonomen, ganz unter dem Einfluss\n stofflicher Interessen, den Formgehalt des relativen Werthausdrucks übersehn\n haben, wenn vor Hegel die Logiker von Profession sogar den Forminhalt der\n Urtheils- und Schlussparadigmen übersahen.] .\n\n\n\n\nUnsre Analyse hat gezeigt, dass der relative Werthausdruck\n einer Waare zwei verschiedne Werthformen einschliesst.\n Die Leinwand drückt ihren Werth und ihre bestimmte Werthgrösse\n im Rock aus. Sie stellt ihren Werth dar im Werthverhältniss zu\n einer andern Waare, daher als Tauschwerth. Andrerseits die andre\n Waare, der Rock, worin sie ihren Werth relativ ausdrückt, erhält eben\n dadurch die Form eines mit ihr unmittelbar austauschbaren Gebrauchs-\n werths oder Aequivalents. Beide Formen, relative Werthform\n der einen Waare, Aequivalentform der andern, sind Formen des\nTauschwerths. Beide sind in der That nur Momente, wechsel-\n seitig durcheinander bedingte Bestimmungen, desselben relativen\n Werthausdrucks, aber polarisch vertheilt auf die zwei gleichgesetzten\nWaarenextreme.\n\n\n\n\nQuantitative Bestimmtheit ist nicht in der Aequivalentform\n einer Waare eingeschlossen. Das bestimmte Verhältniss z. B., worin\n Rock Aequivalent von Leinwand ist, entspringt nicht aus seiner Aequiva-\n lentform, der Form seiner unmittelbaren Austauschbarkeit\n mit der Leinwand, sondern aus der Bestimmung der Werthgrösse durch\n Arbeitszeit. Die Leinwand kann ihren eignen Werth nur in Röcken dar-\n stellen, indem sie sich auf ein bestimmtes Rockquantum als gegebenes\n Quantum krystallisirter menschlicher Arbeit bezieht. Aendert sich der\n Rockwerth, so ändert sich auch diese Beziehung. Damit sich aber der\n relative Werth der Leinwand ändere, muss er vorhanden sein, und er kann\n nur gebildet werden bei gegebenem Rockwerth. Ob die Leinwand ihren\n eignen Werth nun in 1, 2 oder x Röcken darstellt, hängt unter dieser\n Voraussetzung ganz von der Werthgrösse einer Elle Leinwand und der\n Ellenanzahl ab, deren Werth in Rockform dargestellt werden soll. Die\nWerthgrösse einer Waare kann sich nur im Gebrauchswerth einer\n andern Waare ausdrücken, als relativer Werth. Die Form eines\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"22\", \"facs\": \"#f0041\"} --> unmittelbar austauschbaren Gebrauchswerths oder Aequivalents erhält\n eine Waare dagegen umgekehrt nur als das Material, worin der Werth\n einer andern Waare ausgedrückt wird.\n\n\n\n\nDiese Unterscheidung ist getrübt durch eine charakteristische Eigen-\n thümlichkeit des relativen Werthausdrucks in seiner einfachen oder ersten\n Form. Die Gleichung: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock, oder\n 20 Ellen Leinwand sind einen Rock werth, schliesst nämlich offenbar die\n identische Gleichung ein: 1 Rock = 20 Ellen Leinwand, oder\n 1 Rock ist 20 Ellen Leinwand werth. Der relative Werthausdruck der\n Leinwand, worin der Rock als Aequivalent figurirt, enthält also rück-\n bezüglich den relativen Werthausdruck des Rocks, worin die Leinwand\n als Aequivalent figurirt.\n\n\n\n\nObgleich beide Bestimmungen der Werthform oder beide Dar-\n stellungsweisen des Waarenwerths als Tauschwerth nur relativ\n sind, scheinen beide nicht in demselben Grad relativ. Im relativen\n Werth der Leinwand: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock, ist der Tausch-\n werth der Leinwand ausdrücklich als ihre Beziehung auf eine andre\n Waare dargestellt. Der Rock seinerseits ist zwar auch nur Aequiva-\n lent, so weit sich die Leinwand auf ihn als Erscheinungsform ihres eig-\n nen Werths und daher mit ihr unmittelbar Austauschbares bezieht. Nur\ninnerhalb dieser Beziehung ist er Aequivalent. Aber er verhält sich\n passiv. Er ergreift keine Initiative. Er findet sich in Beziehung, weil\n sich auf ihn bezogen wird. Der Charakter, der ihm aus dem Verhältniss\n mit der Leinwand erwächst, erscheint daher nicht als Resultat seiner Be-\n ziehung, sondern ohne sein Zuthun vorhanden. Noch mehr. Die\nbestimmte Art und Weise, wie sich die Leinwand auf ihn bezieht,\n ist ganz dazu gemacht, es ihm „anzuthun“, wäre er auch noch so beschei-\n den und keineswegs das Produkt eines „tailor run mad with pride“. Die\n Leinwand bezieht sich nämlich auf den Rock als sinnlich existirende Ma-\n teriatur der menschlichen Arbeit in abstracto und daher als vorhandnen\n Werthkörper. Er ist diess nur, weil und sofern sich die Leinwand in\n dieser bestimmten Weise auf ihn bezieht. Sein Aequivalentsein\n ist so zu sagen nur eine Reflexionsbestimmung der Leinwand.\n Aber es scheint grade umgekehrt. Einerseits giebt er sich selbst nicht\n die Mühe sich zu beziehn. Andrerseits bezieht sich die Leinwand auf ihn,\n nicht um ihn zu etwas zu machen, sondern weil er ohne sie etwas ist.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"23\", \"facs\": \"#f0042\"} --> Das fertige Produkt der Beziehung der Leinwand auf den Rock, seine\n Aequivalentform, seine Bestimmtheit als unmittelbar austauschbarer Ge-\n brauchswerth, scheint ihm daher auch ausserhalb der Beziehung zur\n Leinwand dinglich anzugehören, ganz wie etwa seine Eigenschaft\n warm zu halten. In der ersten oder einfachen Form des relativen\n Werths: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock, ist dieser falsche Schein noch\n nicht befestigt, weil sie unmittelbar auch das Gegentheil aussagt,\n dass der Rock Aequivalent der Leinwand und dass jede der beiden Waa-\n ren diese Bestimmtheit nur besitzt, weil und sofern die andre sie zu ihrem\n relativen Werthausdruck macht [Note: Es ist mit solchen Reflexionsbestimmungen überhaupt ein eignes Ding.\n Dieser Mensch ist z. B. nur König, weil sich andre Menschen als Unterthanen zu\n ihm verhalten. Sie glauben umgekehrt Unterthanen zu sein, weil er König ist.] .\n\n\n\n\nIn der einfachen Form des relativen Werths oder dem Ausdrucke der\n Aequivalenz zweier Waaren, ist die Form entwicklung des Werths für\n beide Waaren gleichmässig, obgleich jedesmal in entgegenge-\n setzter Richtung. Der relative Werthausdruck ist ferner mit\n Bezug auf jede der beiden Waaren einheitlich, denn die Leinwand\n stellt ihren Werth nur in einer Waare dar, dem Rocke und vice versa,\n aber für beide Waaren ist dieser Werthausdruck doppelt, verschieden\n für jede derselben. Endlich ist jede der beiden Waaren nur Aequiva-\n lent für die andre einzelne Waarenart. also nur einzelnes Aequi-\n valent.\n\n\n\n\nSolche Gleichung, wie 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock, oder\n zwanzig Ellen Leinwand sind einen Rock werth, drückt offenbar den\n Werth der Waare nur ganz beschränkt und einseitig aus. Vergleiche ich\n die Leinwand z. B., statt mit Röcken, mit andern Waaren, so erhalte ich\n auch andre relative Werthausdrücke, andre Gleichungen,\n wie 20 Ellen Leinwand = u Kaffee, 20 Ellen Leinwand = v Thee\n u. s. w. Die Leinwand hat eben so viele verschiedne relative\n Werthausdrücke, als es von ihr verschiedne Waaren giebt und die\n Zahl ihrer relativen Werthausdrücke wächst beständig mit der Zahl neu\n auftretender Waarenarten [Note: „The value of any commodity denoting its relation in exchange, we may\n speak of it as .... cornvalue, clothvalue, according to the commodity with which] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"24\", \"facs\": \"#f0043\"} -->\n\nDie erste Form 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock gab zwei\n relative Ausdrücke für den Werth zweier Waaren. Diese zweite\n Form giebt für den Werth derselben Waare die bunteste Mosaik\n relativer Ausdrücke. Auch scheint weder für den Ausdruck der Werth-\ngrösse irgend etwas gewonnen, denn in 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock\n ist die Werthgrösse der Leinwand, die ja in jedem Ausdrucke dieselbe\n bleibt, eben so erschöpfend dargestellt als in 20 Ellen Leinwand = u Thee\n u. s. w., noch für die Formbestimmung des Aequivalents, denn in\n 20 Ellen Leinwand = u Kaffee u. s. w., sind Kaffee u. s. w. nur ein-\n zelne Aequivalente, ganz wie es der Rock war.\n\n\n\n\nDennoch birgt diese zweite Form eine wesentliche Fortentwicklung.\n Es liegt darin nämlich nicht nur, dass die Leinwand ihren Werth zufällig\n bald in Rücken ausdrückt, bald in Kaffee u. s. w., sondern dass sie ihn\nsowohl in Röcken als in Kaffee u. s. w. ausdrückt, entweder in\ndieser Waare oder jener oder der dritten u. s. w. Die Weiterbestim-\n mung zeigt sich, sobald diese zweite oder entfaltete Form des rela-\n tiven Werthausdrucks in ihrem Zusammenhang dargestellt\n wird. Wir erhalten dann:\n\n\n\n\nII. Zweite oder entfaltete Form des relativen Werths:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock oder = u Kaffee oder = v Thee oder\n = x Eisen oder = y Weizen oder = u. s. w. u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nz Waare A = u Waare B oder = v Waare C oder = w Waare D\noder = x Waare E oder = y Waare F oder = u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nZunächst bildet offenbar die erste Form das Grundelement der\n [Note: it is compared; and then there are a thousand different kinds of value,\n as many kinds of value as there are commodities in existence, and all are equally\n real and equally nominal.“ („A Critical Dissertation on the Nature,\n Measure and Causes of Value: chiefly in reference to the writings\n of Mr. Ricardo and his followers. By the Author of Essays on the\n Formation etc. of Opinions. London 1825“, p. 39). S. Bailey, der\n Verfasser dieser anonymen Schrift, die ihrer Zeit viel Lärm in England machte,\n bildet sich ein durch diesen Hinweis auf die kunterbunten relativen Ausdrücke\n desselben Waaren-Werths alle Begriffsbestimmung des Werths vernichtet zu\n haben. Dass er übrigens, trotz eigner Bornirtheit, wunde Flecken der Ricardo’-\n schen Theorie sondirt hat, bewies die Gereiztheit, womit die Ricardo’sche Schule\n ihn angriff, z. B. in der Westminster Review.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"25\", \"facs\": \"#f0044\"} --> zweiten, denn letztere besteht aus vielen einfachen relativen Werthaus-\n drücken, wie 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock, 20 Ellen Leinwand =\n u Kaffee u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nIn der ersten Form: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock kann es\n zufällige Thatsache scheinen, dass diese zwei Waaren in diesem bestimm-\n ten quantitativen Verhältnisse austauschbar sind. In der\n zweiten Form leuchtet dagegen sofort ein von der zufälligen Erscheinung\n wesentlich unterschiedner und sie bestimmender Hintergrund durch. Der\n Werth der Leinwand bleibt gleich gross, ob in Rock oder Kaffee oder\n Eisen u. s. w. dargestellt, in zahllos verschiednen Waaren, den verschie-\n densten Besitzern angehörig. Das zufällige Verhältniss zweier individuel-\n ler Waarenbesitzer fällt fort. Es wird offenbar, dass nicht der Austausch\n die Werthgrösse der Waare, sondern umgekehrt die Werthgrösse der\n Waare ihre Austauschverhältnisse regulirt.\n\n\n\n\nIn dem Ausdruck: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock galt der\n Rock als Erscheinungsform der in der Leinwand vergegenständlichten\n Arbeit. So wurde die in der Leinwand enthaltene Arbeit der im Rock\n enthaltnen gleichgesetzt und daher als gleichartige menschliche Arbeit\n bestimmt. Indess trat diese Bestimmung nicht ausdrücklich hervor.\n Unmittelbar setzt die erste Form die in der Leinwand enthaltne Arbeit\n nur der Schneiderarbeit gleich. Anders die zweite Form. In der end-\n losen, stets verlängerbaren Reihe ihrer relativen Werthausdrücke bezieht\n sich die Leinwand auf alle möglichen Waarenkörper als blosse Erschei-\n nungsformen der in ihr selbst enthaltenen Arbeit. Hier ist der Leinwand-\nWerth daher erst wahrhaft dargestellt als Werth, d. h. Krystall\n menschlicher Arbeit überhaupt.\n\n\n\n\nDie zweite Form besteht aus einer Summe von lauter Gleichungen\n der ersten Form. Jede dieser Gleichungen, wie 20 Ellen Leinwand\n = 1 Rock schliesst aber auch die Rückbeziehung ein: 1 Rock = 20 El-\n len Leinwand, wo der Rock seinen Werth in der Leinwand und\n eben dadurch die Leinwand als Aequivalent darstellt. Da diess nun von\n jedem der zahllosen relativen Werthausdrücke der Leinwand gilt,\n erhalten wir:\n\n\n\n\nIII. Dritte, umgekehrte oder rückbezogene zweite\n Form des relativen Werths:\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"26\", \"facs\": \"#f0045\"} -->\n\n\n\n1 Rock = 20 Ellen Leinwand.\n\n\n\n\nu Kaffee = 20 Ellen Leinwand.\n\n\n\n\nv Thee = 20 Ellen Leinwand.\n\n\n\n\nx Eisen = 20 Ellen Leinwand.\n\n\n\n\ny Weizen = 20 Ellen Leinwand.\n\n\n\n\nu. s. w. = 20 Ellen Leinwand.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDer relative Werthausdruck der Waaren kehrt hier zurück\n in seiner ursprünglichen Gestalt: 1 Rock = 20 Ellen Leinwand. Jedoch\n ist diese einfache Gleichung jetzt weiter entwickelt. Ursprünglich ent-\n hielt sie nur, dass der Rockwerth durch seinen Ausdruck in einer an-\n dern Waare eine vom Gebrauchswerth Rock oder dem Rock-\n körper selbst unterschiedne und unabhängige Form er-\n hält. Jetzt stellt dieselbe Form den Rock auch allen andern Waaren\n gegenüber als Werth dar und ist daher seine allgemein gültige Werth-\n form. Nicht nur der Rock, sondern Kaffee, Eisen, Weizen, kurz alle\n andern Waaren drücken ihren Werth jetzt im Material Leinwand\n aus. Alle stellen sich so einander als dieselbe Materiatur\n menschlicher Arbeit dar. Sie sind nur noch quantitativ ver-\n schieden, wesswegen 1 Rock, u Kaffee, x Eisen u. s. w., d. h. ver-\n schiedne Quanta dieser verschiednen Dinge = 20 Ellen Leinwand,\n gleich demselben Quantum vergegenständlichter menschlicher Arbeit.\n Durch ihren gemeinschaftlichen Werthausdruck im Material Leinwand\nunterscheiden sich also alle Waaren als Tauschwerthe von ihren\n eignen Gebrauchswerthen und beziehn sich zugleich auf einander\n als Werthgrössen, setzen sich qualitativ gleich und verglei-\n chen sich quantitativ. Erst in diesem einheitlichen relativen\n Werthausdruck erscheinen sie alle für einander als Werthe und erhält\n ihr Werth daher erst seine entsprechende Erscheinungsform als\n Tauschwerth. Im Unterschied zur entfalteten Form des rela-\n tiven Werths (Form II), die den Werth einer Waare im Umkreis aller\n andern Waaren darstellt, nennen wir diesen einheitlichen Werth-\n ausdruck die allgemeine relative Werthform.\n\n\n\n\nIn der Form II: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock oder = u Kaffee\noder = v Thee oder = x Eisen u. s. w., worin die Leinwand ihren\n relativen Werthausdruck entfaltet, bezieht sie sich auf jede einzelne\n Waare, Rock, Kaffee u. s. w. als ein besondres Aequivalent und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"27\", \"facs\": \"#f0046\"} --> auf alle zusammen als den Umkreis ihrer besondern Aequivalent-\n formen. Ihr gegenüber gilt keine einzelne Waarenart noch als Aequiva-\n lent schlechthin, wie im einzelnen Aequivalent, sondern nur als be-\n sondres Aequivalent, wovon das eine das andre ausschliesst. In der\n Form III, welche die rückbezogene zweite Form und also in ihr einge-\n schlossen ist, erscheint die Leinwand dagegen als die Gattungsform\n des Aequivalents für alle andern Waaren. Es ist als ob neben und ausser\n Löwen, Tigern, Hasen und allen andern wirklichen Thieren, die gruppirt\n die verschiednen Geschlechter, Arten, Unterarten, Familien u. s. w. des\n Thierreichs bilden, auch noch das Thier existirte, die individuelle Incar-\n nation des ganzen Thierreichs. Ein solches Einzelne, das in sich selbst alle\n wirklich vorhandenen Arten derselben Sache einbegreift, ist ein Allgemei-\n nes, wie Thier, Gott u. s. w. Wie die Leinwand daher einzelnes\n Aequivalent wurde, dadurch dass sich eine andre Waare auf sie als\n Erscheinungsform des Werths bezog, so wird sie als allen Waaren gemein-\n schaftliche Erscheinungsform des Werths das allgemeine Aequiva-\n lent, allgemeiner Werthleib, allgemeine Materiatur der\n abstrakten menschlichen Arbeit. Die in ihr materialisirte be-\n sondre Arbeit gilt daher jetzt als allgemeine Verwirklichungs-\n form der menschlichen Arbeit, als allgemeine Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nBei der Darstellung des Werths der Waare A in der Waare B, wo-\n durch die Waare B einzelnes Aequivalent wird, war es gleichgül-\n tig, von welcher besondern Sorte die Waare B. Nur musste die Kör-\n perlichkeit der Waare B andrer Art sein als die der Waare A, daher\n auch Produkt andrer nützlicher Arbeit. Indem der Rock seinen\n Werth in Leinwand darstellte, bezog er sich auf Leinwand als die ver-\n wirklichte menschliche Arbeit, und eben dadurch auf Leine-\n weberei als die Verwirklichungsform der menschlichen\n Arbeit, aber die besondre Bestimmtheit, welche Leineweberei von\n andern Arbeitsarten unterscheidet, war durchaus gleichgültig. Sie\n musste nur andrer Art sein als die Schneiderarbeit und im übrigen eine\nbestimmte Arbeitsart. Anders sobald die Leinwand allgemeines\n Aequivalent wird. Dieser Gebrauchswerth in seiner besondern\n Bestimmtheit, wodurch er Leinwand im Unterschied von allen andern\n Waarenarten, Kaffee, Eisen u. s. w., wird jetzt die allgemeine Werthform\n aller andern Waaren und daher allgemeines Aequivalent. Die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"28\", \"facs\": \"#f0047\"} --> in ihm dargestellte besondre nützliche Arbeitsart gilt daher jetzt als\nallgemeine Verwirklichungsform der menschlichen Ar-\n beit, als allgemeine Arbeit, grade soweit sie Arbeit von besondrer\n Bestimmtheit ist, Leineweberei im Unterschied nicht nur von Schnei-\n derarbeit, sondern von Kaffeebau, Minenarbeit und allen andern Arbeits-\narten. Umgekehrt gelten alle andren Arbeitsarten, im relativen\n Werthausdruck der Leinwand, des allgemeinen Aequivalents (Form\n II), nur noch als besondre Verwirklichungsformen der mensch-\n lichen Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nAls Werthe sind die Waaren Ausdrücke derselben Einheit,\n der abstrakten menschlichen Arbeit. In der Form des Tauschwerths\n erscheinen sie einander als Werthe und beziehn sich auf einander\nals Werthe. Sie beziehn sich damit zugleich auf die abstrakte mensch-\n liche Arbeit als ihre gemeinsame gesellschaftliche Substanz.\n Ihr gesellschaftliches Verhältniss besteht ausschliesslich darin ein-\n ander als nur quantitativ verschiedne, aber qualitativ gleiche und daher\n durch einander ersetzbare und mit einander vertauschbare Ausdrücke dieser\n ihrer gesellschaftlichen Substanz zu gelten. Als nützliches Ding besitzt\n eine Waare gesellschaftliche Bestimmtheit, soweit sie Gebrauchswerth für\n andre ausser ihrem Besitzer ist, also gesellschaftliche Bedürfnisse befrie-\n digt. Aber gleichgültig, auf wessen Bedürfnisse ihre nützlichen Eigen-\n schaften sie beziehn, sie wird durch dieselben immer nur auf mensch-\n liche Bedürfnisse bezogener Gegenstand, nicht Waare für\nandre Waaren. Nur was blosse Gebrauchsgegenstände in Waaren\n verwandelt, kann sie als Waaren auf einander beziehn und daher in\ngesellschaftlichen Rapport setzen. Es ist diess aber ihr Werth.\n Die Form, worin sie sich als Werthe, als menschliche Arbeitsgallerte\ngelten, ist daher ihre gesellschaftliche Form. Gesellschaft-\n liche Form der Waare und Werthform oder Form der Aus-\n tauschbarkeit sind also eins und dasselbe. Ist die Naturalform einer\n Waare zugleich Werthform, so besitzt sie die Form unmittelbarer\n Austauschbarkeit mit andern Waaren und daher unmittelbar\n gesellschaftliche Form.\n\n\n\n\nDie einfache relative Werthform (Form I) 1 Rock =\n 20 Ellen Leinwand unterscheidet sich von der allgemeinen relati-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"29\", \"facs\": \"#f0048\"} --> ven Werthform 1 Rock = 20 Ellen Leinwand nur dadurch, dass\n diese Gleichung jetzt ein Glied der Reihe bildet\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1 Rock = 20 Ellen Leinwand\n\n\n\n\nu Kaffee = 20 Ellen Leinwand\n\n\n\n\nv Thee = 20 Ellen Leinwand\n\n\n\n\nu. s. w.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSie unterscheidet sich also in der That nur dadurch, dass die Leinwand\n aus einem einzelnen zum allgemeinen Aequivalent fortent-\n wickelt ist. Wenn also im einfachen relativen Werthausdrucke nicht\n die Waare, die ihre Werthgrösse ausdrückt, sondern die Waare,\nworin Werthgrösse ausgedrückt wird, die Form unmittelbarer\n Austauschbarkeit, Aequivalentform, also unmittelbar gesell-\n schaftliche Form erhält, so gilt dasselbe für den allgemeinen relativen\n Werthausdruck. Aber in der einfachen relativen Werthform ist dieser\n Unterschied nur noch formell und verschwindend. Wenn in 1 Rock =\n 20 Ellen Leinwand der Rock seinen Werth relativ, nämlich in Leinwand\n ausdrückt und die Leinwand dadurch Aequivalentform erhält, so schliesst\n dieselbe Gleichung unmittelbar die Rückbeziehung ein: 20 Ellen Lein-\n wand = 1 Rock, worin der Rock die Aequivalentform erhält und der\n Werth der Leinwand relativ ausgedrückt wird. Diese gleichmässige und\n gegenseitige Entwicklung der Werthform beider Waaren als relativer\n Werth und als Aequivalent findet jetzt nicht länger statt. Wird die all-\n gemeine relative Werthform 1 Rock = 20 Ellen Leinwand, wo die Leinwand\nallgemeines Aequivalent, umgekehrt in 20 Ellen Leinwand =\n 1 Rock, so wird der Rock dadurch nicht allgemeines Aequivalent für alle\n andern Waaren, sondern nur ein besondres Aequivalent der Leinwand.\nAllgemein ist die relative Werthform des Rocks nur, weil sie zugleich\n die relative Werthform aller andern Waaren. Was vom Rock, gilt vom\n Kaffee u. s. w. Es folgt daher, dass die allgemeine relative Werthform\n der Waaren sie selbst von der allgemeinen Aequivalentform ausschliesst.\n Umgekehrt ist eine Waare, wie Leinwand, sobald sie die allgemeine\n Aequivalentform besitzt, von der allgemeinen relativen Werthform ausge-\n schlossen. Die allgemeine, mit den andern Waaren einheitliche relativc\n Werthform der Leinwand wäre: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 20 Ellen Lein-\n wand. Diess ist aber eine Tautologie, welche die Werthgrösse dieser\n in allgemeiner Aequivalentform und daher in stets austauschbarer Form\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"30\", \"facs\": \"#f0049\"} --> befindlichen Waare nicht ausdrückt. Vielmehr wird die entfaltete\n relative Werthform: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock oder = u Kaffee\noder = v Thee oder = u. s. w. jetzt zum specifischen relativen\n Werthausdrucke des allgemeinen Aequivalents.\n\n\n\n\nIn dem allgemeinen relativen Werthausdruck der Waaren besitzt jede\n Waare, Rock, Kaffee, Thee u. s. w. eine von ihrer Naturalform ver-\n schiedne Werthform, nämlich die Form Leinwand. Und eben in dieser\n Form beziehn sie sich auf einander als Austauschbare und in quantitativ\n bestimmten Verhältnissen Austauschbare, denn wenn 1 Rock = 20 Ellen\n Leinwand, u Kaffee = 20 Ellen Leinwand u. s. w., so ist auch 1 Rock\n = u Kaffee u. s. w. Indem alle Waaren sich in einer und derselben\n Waare als Werthgrössen bespiegeln, wiederspiegeln sie sich wechselseitig\n als Werthgrössen. Aber die Naturalformen, die sie als Gebrauchsgegen-\n stände besitzen, gelten ihnen wechselseitig nur auf diesem Umweg, also\n nicht unmittelbar als Erscheinungsformen des Werths. Sowie sie unmittel-\n bar sind, sind sie daher nicht unmittelbar austauschbar. Sie besitzen also\n nicht die Form unmittelbarer Austauschbarkeit für einander\n oder ihre gesellschaftlich gültige Form ist eine vermittelte.\n Umgekehrt. Indem alle andern Waaren auf Leinwand als Erscheinungs-\n form des Werths sich beziehen, wird die Naturalform der Leinwand die\nForm ihrer unmittelbaren Austauschbarkeit mit allen Waa-\n ren, daher unmittelbar ihre allgemein gesellschaftliche\n Form.\n\n\n\n\nEine Waare erhält nur die allgemeine Aequivalentform,\n weil und sofern sie allen andern Waaren zur Darstellung ihrer allge-\n meinen relativen, daher nicht unmittelbaren Werthform dient.\n Waaren müssen sich aber relative Werthform überhaupt geben, weil ihre\n Naturalformen nur ihre Gebrauchswerthformen, und sie müssen sich ein-\n heitliche, daher allgemeine relative Werthform geben, um sich alle als\n Werthe, als gleichartige Gallerten menschlicher Arbeit auf einander zu\n beziehen. Eine Waare befindet sich daher nur in der Form unmittel-\n barer Austauschbarkeit mit allen andern Waaren und daher in unmittelbar\n gesellschaftlicher Form, weil und sofern alle andern Waaren sich\nnicht darin befinden, oder weil die Waare überhaupt sich von Haus aus\nnicht in unmittelbar austauschbarer oder gesellschaftlicher Form befindet,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"31\", \"facs\": \"#f0050\"} --> indem ihre unmittelbare Form die Form ihres Gebrauchswerths, nicht ihres\n Werthes.\n\n\n\n\nMan sieht es der Form allgemeiner unmittelbarer Aus-\n tauschbarkeit in der That keineswegs an, dass sie eine gegensätz-\n liche Waarenform ist, von der Form nicht unmittelbarer Austausch-\n barkeit ebenso unzertrennlich, wie die Positivität eines Magnetpols von der\n Negativität des andern. Man kann sich daher einbilden, man könne allen\n Waaren zugleich den Stempel unmittelbarer Austauschbarkeit aufdrücken,\n wie man sich auch einbilden kann, man könne alle Arbeiter zu Kapita-\n listen machen. In der That aber sind allgemeine relative Werth-\n form und allgemeine Aequivalentform die gegensätzlichen, sich\n wechselweis voraussetzenden und wechselweis abstossenden Pole dersel-\n ben gesellschaftlichen Form der Waaren [Note: Für den Kleinbürger, der in der Form der Waarenproduktion das nec plus\n ultra menschlicher Freiheit und individueller Unabhängigkeit erblickt, wäre es\n natürlich sehr wünschenswerth, zugleich der mit dieser Form verbundnen Miss-\n stände überhoben zu sein, namentlich auch der nicht unmittelbaren Aus-\n tauschbarkeit der Waaren. Die Ausmalung dieser Philisterutopie bildet Proud-\n hon’s Socialismus, der, wie ich anderswo gezeigt, nicht einmal das Verdienst der\n Originalität besitzt, vielmehr lange vor ihm von Bray, Gray und Andern weit bes-\n ser entwickelt wurde. Diess verhindert solche Weisheit nicht, heutzutage unter\n dem Namen der „science“ in Frankreich zu grassiren. Nie hat eine Schule mehr\n als die Proudhon’sche mit dem Wort „science“ um sich geworfen, denn\n„wo Begriffe fehlen,\n Da stellt zur rechten Zeit ein Wort sich ein.“] .\n\n\n\n\nAls unmittelbar gesellschaftliche Materiatur der\n Arbeit ist die Leinwand, das allgemeine Aequivalent, Materiatur\n unmittelbar gesellschaftlicher Arbeit, während die andern\n Waarenkörper, welche ihren Werth in Leinwand darstellen, Materiaturen\nnicht unmittelbar gesellschaftlicher Arbeiten sind.\n\n\n\n\nIn der That sind alle Gebrauchswerthe nur Waaren, weil Produkte\n von einander unabhängiger Privatarbeiten, Privatarbeiten,\n die jedoch als besondere, wenn auch verselbständigte, Glieder des natur-\n wüchsigen Systems der Theilung der Arbeit stofflich von einander\n abhängen. Sie hängen so gesellschaftlich zusammen grade durch ihre\nVerschiedenheit, ihre besondre Nützlichkeit. Eben dess-\n wegen produciren sie qualitativ verschiedne Gebrauchswerthe. Wenn\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"32\", \"facs\": \"#f0051\"} --> nicht, so würden diese Gebrauchswerthe nicht zu Waaren für einander.\n Andrerseits macht diese verschiedne nützliche Qualität Produkte noch\n nicht zu Waaren. Producirt eine bäuerliche Familie für ihren eignen\n Consum Rock und Leinwand und Weizen, so treten diese Dinge der Fa-\n milie als verschiedne Produkte ihrer Familienarbeit gegenüber, aber nicht\n sich selbst wechselseitig als Waaren. Wäre die Arbeit unmittelbar\n gesellschaftliche, d. h. gemeinsame Arbeit, so erhielten die Pro-\n dukte den unmittelbar gesellschaftlichen Charakter eines Gemeinprodukts\n für ihre Producenten, aber nicht den Charakter von Waaren für einander.\n Indess haben wir hier nicht weit zu suchen, worin die gesellschaftliche\n Form der in den Waaren enthaltenen und von einander unabhängigen\nPrivatarbeiten besteht. Sie ergab sich bereits aus der Analyse der\n Waare. Ihre gesellschaftliche Form ist ihre Beziehung auf einander als\ngleiche Arbeit, also, da die Gleichheit toto coelo verschiedner\n Arbeiten nur in einer Abstraktion von ihrer Ungleichheit be-\n stehen kann, ihre Beziehung auf einander als menschliche Arbeit\n überhaupt, Verausgabungen menschlicher Arbeitskraft,\n was alle menschlichen Arbeiten, welches immer ihr Inhalt und ihre Opera-\n tionsweise, in der That sind. In jeder gesellschaftlichen Arbeitsform\n sind die Arbeiten der verschiednen Individuen auch als menschliche auf\n einander bezogen, aber hier gilt diese Beziehung selbst als die\nspecifisch gesellschaftliche Form der Arbeiten. Nun besitzt\n aber keine dieser Privatarbeiten in ihrer Naturalform diese specifisch ge-\n sellschaftliche Form abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit, so wenig wie die\n Waare in ihrer Naturalform die gesellschaftliche Form blosser Arbeits-\n gallerte, oder des Werthes, besitzt. Dadurch aber dass die Naturalform\n einer Waare, hier der Leinwand, allgemeine Aequivalentform wird, weil\n sich alle andern Waaren auf dieselbe als Erscheinungsform ihres eignen\n Werths beziehn, wird auch die Leinweberei zur allgemeinen Verwirk-\n lichungsform abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit oder zu Arbeit in unmittelbar\n gesellschaftlicher Form. Der Massstab der „Gesellschaftlichkeit“ muss\n aus der Natur der jeder Produktionsweise eigenthümlichen Verhältnisse,\n nicht aus ihr fremden Vorstellungen entlehnt werden. Wie vorhin gezeigt\n ward, dass die Waare von Natur die unmittelbare Form allgemeiner Aus-\n tauschbarkeit ausschliesst und die allgemeine Aequivalentform daher nur\ngegensätzlich entwickeln kann, so gilt dasselbe für die in den Waaren\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"33\", \"facs\": \"#f0052\"} --> steckenden Privatarbeiten. Da sie nicht unmittelbar gesellschaft-\n liche Arbeit sind, so ist erstens die gesellschaftliche Form eine\n von den Naturalformen der wirklichen nützlichen Arbeiten unterschiedne,\n ihnen fremde, und abstrakte Form, und zweitens erhalten alle Arten Pri-\n vatarbeit ihren gesellschaftlichen Charakter nur gegensätzlich,\n indem sie alle einer ausschliesslichen Art Privatarbeit, hier der Leinewebe-\n rei, gleichgesetzt werden. Dadurch wird letztere die unmittelbare\n und allgemeine Erscheinungsform abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit und so\n Arbeit in unmittelbar gesellschaftlicher Form. Sie stellt sich daher auch\n unmittelbar in einem gesellschaftlich geltenden und allgemein austausch-\n baren Produkt dar.\n\n\n\n\nDer Schein, als ob die Aequivalentform einer Waare aus ihrer eignen\n dinglichen Natur entspringe, statt blosser Reflex der Beziehungen der an-\n dern Waaren zu sein, befestigt sich mit der Fortbildung des einzelnen\n Aequivalents zum allgemeinen, weil die gegensätzlichen Momente der\n Werthform sich nicht mehr gleichmässig für die auf einander bezog-\n nen Waaren entwickeln, weil die allgemeine Aequivalentform eine Waare\n als etwas ganz apartes von allen andern Waaren scheidet und endlich weil\n diese ihre Form in der That nicht mehr das Produkt der Beziehung irgend\n einer einzelnen andern Waare ist.\n\n\n\n\nIndess ist auf unserm jetzigen Standpunkt das allgemeine Aequiva-\n lent noch keineswegs verknöchert. Wie wurde in der That die Leinwand\n in das allgemeine Aequivalent verwandelt? Dadurch, dass sie ihren Werth\n erst in einer einzelnen Waare (Form I), dann in allen andern Waaren der\n Reihe nach relativ darstellte (Form II), und so rückbezüglich alle\n andern Waaren in ihr ihre Werthe relativ darstellten (Form III). Der\n einfache relative Werthausdruck war der Keim, woraus sich die allgemeine\n Aequivalentform der Leinwand entwickelte. Innerhalb dieser Entwicklung\n ändert sie die Rolle. Sie beginnt damit, ihre Werthgrösse in einer\n andern Waare darzustellen und endet damit zum Material für den Werth-\n ausdruck aller andern Waaren zu dienen. Was von der Leinwand, gilt\n von jeder Waare. In ihrem entfalteten relativen Werthausdrucke (Form\n II), der nur aus ihren vielen, einfachen Werthausdrücken besteht,\n figurirt die Leinwand noch nicht als allgemeines Aequivalent. Vielmehr\n bildet hier jeder andre Waarenkörper ihr Aequivalent, ist daher un-\n mittelbar austauschbar mit ihr und kann also die Stelle mit ihr wechseln.\n\n\n [Running matter: I. 3] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"34\", \"facs\": \"#f0053\"} -->\n\nWir erhalten daher schliesslich:\n\n\n\n\nForm IV:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock oder = u Kaffee oder = v Thee oder\n = x Eisen oder = y Weizen oder = u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\n1 Rock = 20 Ellen Leinwand oder = u Kaffee oder = v Thee oder\n = x Eisen oder = y Weizen oder = u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nu Kaffee = 20 Ellen Leinwand oder = 1 Rock oder = v Thee oder\n = x Eisen oder = y Weizen oder = u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nv Thee = u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAber jede dieser Gleichungen rückbezogen ergiebt Rock, Kaffee,\n Thee u. s. w. als allgemeines Aequivalent, daher den Werthausdruck in\n Rock, Kaffee, Thee u. s. w. als allgemeine relative Werthform aller andern\n Waaren. Die allgemeine Aequivalentform kommt immer nur einer Waare\n zu im Gegensatz zu allen andern Waaren; aber sie kommt jeder Waare\n im Gegensatz zu allen andern zu. Stellt aber jede Waare ihre eigne Na-\n turalform allen andern Waaren gegenüber als allgemeine Aequivalentform,\n so schliessen alle Waaren alle von der allgemeinen Aequivalentform aus\n und daher sich selbst von der gesellschaftlich gültigen Darstellung ihrer\n Werthgrössen.\n\n\n\n\nMan sieht: die Analyse der Waare ergiebt alle wesentlichen\n Bestimmungen der Werthform und die Werthform selbst in ihren gegen-\n sätzlichen Momenten, die allgemeine relative Werthform, die\nallgemeine Aequivalentform, endlich die nie abschliessende\nReihe einfacher relativer Werthausdrücke, welche erst eine\n Durchgangsphase in der Entwicklung der Werthform bildet, um schliess-\n lich in die specifisch relative Werthform des allgemeinen\n Aequivalents umzuschlagen. Aber die Analyse der Waare ergab diese\n Formen als Waarenformen überhaupt, die also auch jeder Waare\n zukommen, nur gegensätzlich, so dass wenn die Waare A sich in der\neinen Formbestimmung befindet, die Waaren B, C u. s. w. ihr gegen-\n über die andere annehmen. Das entscheidend Wichtige aber war den\n inneren nothwendigen Zusammenhang zwischen Werthform, Werthsub-\n stanz und Werthgrösse zu entdecken, d. h. ideell ausgedrückt, zu\n beweisen, dass die Werthform aus dem Werthbegriff entspringt [Note: Es ist einer der Grundmängel der klassischen politischen Oekonomie, dass\n es ihr nie gelang, aus der Analyse der Waare und specieller des Waarenwerths] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"35\", \"facs\": \"#f0054\"} -->\n\nEine Waare scheint auf den ersten Blick ein selbstverständliches,\n triviales Ding. Ihre Analyse ergiebt, dass sie ein sehr vertracktes Ding\n ist, voller metaphysischer Spitzfindigkeit und theologischer Mucken. Als\n blosser Gebrauchswerth ist sie ein sinnliches Ding, woran nichts My-\n steriöses, ob ich sie nun unter dem Gesichtspunkt betrachte, dass ihre\n Eigenschaften menschliche Bedürfnisse befriedigen oder dass sie erst als\nProdukt menschlicher Arbeit diese Eigenschaften erhält. Es liegt ab-\n solut nichts räthselhaftes darin, dass der Mensch durch seine Thätigkeit\n die Formen der Naturstoffe in einer ihm nützlichen Weise verändert.\n Die Form des Holzes z. B. wird verändert, wenn man aus ihm einen Tisch\n macht. Nichtsdestoweniger bleibt der Tisch Holz, ein ordinäres sinn-\n [Note: die Form des Werths, die ihn eben zum Tauschwerth macht, herauszufinden.\n Grade in ihren besten Repräsentanten, wie A. Smith und Ricardo, behandelt sie\n die Werthform als etwas ganz Gleichgültiges oder der Natur der Waare selbst\n Aeusserliches. Der Grund ist nicht allein, dass die Analyse der Werthgrösse\n ihre Aufmerksamkeit ganz absorbirt. Er liegt tiefer. Die Werthform des\n Arbeitsprodukts ist die abstrakteste, aber auch allgemeinste Form der bür-\n gerlichen Produktionsweise, die hierdurch als eine besondre Art gesell-\n schaftlicher Produktionsweise und damit zugleich historisch charakterisirt\n wird. Versieht man sie daher für die ewige Naturform gesellschaftlicher Pro-\n duktion, so übersieht man nothwendig auch das Specifische der Werthform, also\n der Waarenform, weiter entwickelt der Geldform, Kapitalform u. s. w.\n Man findet daher bei Oekonomen, welche über das Mass der Werthgrösse durch\n Arbeitszeit durchaus übereinstimmen, die kunterbuntesten und widersprechendsten\n Vorstellungen von Geld, d. h. der fertigen Gestalt des allgemeinen Aequivalents.\n Diess tritt schlagend hervor z. B. bei der Behandlung des Bankwesens, wo mit den\n gemeinplätzlichen Definitionen des Geldes nicht mehr ausgereicht wird. Im Gegen-\n satz entsprang daher ein restaurirtes Merkantilsystem (Ganilh u. s. w.),\n welches im Werth nur die gesellschaftliche Form sieht oder vielmehr nur\n ihren substanzlosen Schein. — Um es ein für allemal zu bemerken, verstehe ich\n unter klassischer politischer Oekonomie alle Oekonomie seit W. Petty,\n die den innern Zusammenhang der bürgerlichen Produktionsverhältnisse er-\n forscht, im Gegensatz zur Vulgärökonomie, die sich nur innerhalb des schein-\n baren Zusammenhangs herumtreibt, für eine plausible Verständlichmachung der\n so zu sagen gröbsten Phänomene und den bürgerlichen Hausbedarf das von der\n wissenschaftlichen Oekonomie längst gelieferte Material stets von neuem wieder-\n kaut, im Uebrigen aber sich darauf beschränkt, die banalen und selbstgefälligen\n Vorstellungen der bürgerlichen Produktionsagenten von ihrer eignen besten Welt\n zu systematisiren, pedantisiren und als ewige Wahrheiten zu proklamiren.] \n [Running matter: 3*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"36\", \"facs\": \"#f0055\"} --> liches Ding. Aber sobald er als Waare auftritt, verwandelt er sich in\n ein sinnlich übersinnliches Ding. Er steht nicht nur mit seinen Füssen\n auf dem Boden, sondern er stellt sich allen andern Waaren gegenüber auf\n den Kopf und entwickelt aus seinem Holzkopf Grillen, viel wunderlicher,\n als wenn er aus freien Stücken zu tanzen begänne [Note: Man erinnert sich, dass China und die Tische zu tanzen anfingen, als alle\n übrige Welt still zu stehn schien — pour encourager les autres.] .\n\n\n\n\nDer mystische Charakter der Waare entspringt also nicht aus ihrem\n Gebrauchswerth. Er entspringt ebensowenig aus den Werth bestimmun-\n gen, für sich selbst betrachtet. Denn erstens, wie verschieden die nütz-\n lichen Arbeiten oder produktiven Thätigkeiten sein mögen, es ist eine\nphysiologische Wahrheit, dass sie Funktionen eines specifisch\nmenschlichen Organismus im Unterschied von andern Organismen\n sind, und dass jede solche Funktion, welches immer ihr Inhalt und ihre\n Form, wesentlich Verausgabung von menschlichem Hirn, Nerv,\n Muskel, Sinnesorgan u. s. w. ist. Was zweitens der Bestimmung der\n Werthgrösse zu Grunde liegt, die Zeitdauer jener Verausgabung oder\n die Quantität der Arbeit, so ist die Quantität sogar sinnfällig von der\nQualität der Arbeit unterscheidbar. In allen Zuständen musste die\n Arbeitszeit, welche die Produktion der Lebensmittel kostet, den Men-\n schen interessiren, obgleich nicht gleichmässig auf verschiednen Entwick-\n lungsstufen. Endlich, sobald die Menschen in irgend einer Weise für ein-\n ander arbeiten, erhält ihre Arbeit auch eine gesellschaftliche Form.\n\n\n\n\nNehmen wir den Robinson auf seiner Insel. Bescheiden, wie er von\n Haus aus ist, hat er doch verschiedenartige Bedürfnisse zu befriedigen und\n muss daher nützliche Arbeiten verschiedner Art verrichten,\n Werkzeuge machen, Möbel fabriciren, Lama zähmen, fischen, jagen u. s. w.\n Vom Beten u. dgl. sprechen wir hier nicht, da unser Robinson daran sein\n Vergnügen findet und derartige Thätigkeit als Erholung betrachtet. Trotz\n der Verschiedenheit seiner produktiven Funktionen weiss er, dass sie nur\n verschiedne Bethätigungsformen desselben Robinson, also nur verschiedne\n Weisen menschlicher Arbeit sind. Die Noth selbst zwingt ihn, seine\nZeit genau zwischen seinen verschiednen Funktionen zu vertheilen. Ob\n die eine mehr, die andre weniger Raum in seiner Gesammtthätigkeit ein-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"37\", \"facs\": \"#f0056\"} --> nimmt, hängt ab von der grössern oder geringern Schwierigkeit, die zur\n Erzielung des bezweckten Nutzeffekts zu überwinden ist. Die Erfahrung\n lehrt ihm das und unser Robinson, der Uhr, Hauptbuch, Tinte und Feder\n aus dem Schiffbruch gerettet, beginnt als guter Engländer bald Buch über\n sich selbst zu führen. Sein Inventarium enthält ein Verzeichniss der Ge-\n brauchsgegenstände, die er besitzt, der verschiednen Verrichtungen,\n die zu ihrer Produktion erheischt sind, endlich der Arbeitszeit, die\n ihm bestimmte Quanta dieser verschiednen Produkte im Durchschnitt\n kosten. Alle Beziehungen zwischen Robinson und den Dingen, die seinen\n selbstgeschaffnen Reichthum bilden, sind hier so einfach und durchsichtig,\n dass selbst Herr M. Wirth sie ohne besondre Geistesanstrengung verstehn\n dürfte. Und dennoch sind darin alle wesentlichen Bestimmungen des\nWerths enthalten.\n\n\n\n\nSetzen wir nun an die Stelle Robinson’s einen Verein freier Menschen,\n die mit gemeinschaftlichen Produktionsmitteln arbeiten und ihre vielen in-\n dividuellen Arbeitskräfte selbstbewusst als eine gesellschaftliche Arbeits-\n kraft verausgaben. Alle Bestimmungen von Robinson’s Arbeit wieder-\n holen sich, nur gesellschaftlich, statt individuell. Ein wesent-\n licher Unterschied tritt jedoch ein. Alle Produkte Robinson’s waren sein\n ausschliesslich persönliches Produkt und daher unmittelbar Gebrauchs-\n gegenstände für ihn. Das Gesammtprodukt des Vereins ist ein gesell-\n schaftliches Produkt. Ein Theil dieses Produkts dient wieder als\n Produktionsmittel. Er bleibt gesellschaftlich. Aber ein anderer Theil\n wird als Lebensmittel von den Vereinsgliedern verzehrt. Er muss daher\n unter sie vertheilt werden. Die Art dieser Vertheilung wird wech-\n seln mit der besondern Art des gesellschaftlichen Produktionsorganismus\n selbst und der entsprechenden geschichtlichen Entwicklungshöhe der Produ-\n zenten. Nur zur Parallele mit der Waarenproduktion setzen wir voraus,\n der Antheil jedes Produzenten an den Lebensmitteln sei bestimmt durch\n seine Arbeitszeit. Die Arbeitszeit würde also eine doppelte Rolle\n spielen. Ihre gesellschaftlich planmässige Vertheilung regelt die richtige\n Proportion der verschiednen Arbeitsfunktionen zu den verschiednen Be-\n dürfnissen. Andrerseits dient die Arbeitszeit zugleich als Mass des indi-\n viduellen Antheils des Produzenten an der Gemeinarbeit und daher auch\n an dem individuell verzehrbaren Theil des Gemeinprodukts. Die gesell-\n schaftlichen Beziehungen der Menschen zu ihren Arbeiten und ihren Ar-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"38\", \"facs\": \"#f0057\"} --> beitsprodukten blieben hier durchsichtig einfach, in der Produktion sowohl\n als in der Distribution.\n\n\n\n\nWoher also der räthselhafte Charakter des Arbeitsprodukts, sobald\n es die Form der Waare annimmt?\n\n\n\n\nWenn die Menschen ihre Produkte auf einander als Werthe beziehn,\n sofern diese Sachen für bloss sachliche Hüllen gleichartig mensch-\n licher Arbeit gelten, so liegt darin zugleich umgekehrt, dass ihre ver-\n schiednen Arbeiten nur als gleichartige menschliche Arbeit gelten in\nsachlicher Hülle. Sie beziehn ihre verschiednen Arbeiten auf ein-\n ander als menschliche Arbeit, indem sie ihre Produkte auf einander\n als Werthe beziehn. Die persönliche Beziehung ist versteckt durch\n die sachliche Form. Es steht daher dem Werth nicht auf der Stirn\n geschrieben, was er ist. Um ihre Produkte auf einander als Waaren zu\n beziehn, sind die Menschen gezwungen, ihre verschiednen Arbeiten abstrakt\n menschlicher Arbeit gleichzusetzen. Sie wissen das nicht, aber sie thun\n es, indem sie das materielle Ding auf die Abstraktion Werth reduciren.\n Es ist diess eine naturwüchsige und daher bewusstlos instinktive Opera-\n tion ihres Hirns, die aus der besondern Weise ihrer materiellen Produktion\n und den Verhältnissen, worin diese Produktion sie versetzt, nothwendig\n herauswächst. Erst ist ihr Verhältniss praktisch da. Zweitens aber,\n weil sie Menschen sind, ist ihr Verhältniss als Verhältniss für\n sie da. Die Art, wie es für sie da ist, oder sich in ihrem Hirn reflektirt,\n entspringt aus der Natur des Verhältnisses selbst. Später suchen sie\n durch die Wissenschaft hinter das Geheimniss ihres eignen gesellschaft-\n lichen Produkts zu kommen, denn die Bestimmung eines Dings als Werth\n ist ihr Produkt, so gut wie die Sprache. Was nun ferner die Werth-\n grösse betrifft, so werden die unabhängig von einander betriebenen, aber,\n weil Glieder der naturwüchsigen Theilung der Arbeit, allsei-\n tig von einander abhängigen Privatarbeiten dadurch fortwährend auf ihr\n gesellschaftlich proportionelles Mass reducirt, dass sich in den zufälligen\n und stets schwankenden Austauschverhältnissen ihrer Pro-\n dukte die zu deren Produktion gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit\n als regelndes Naturgesetz gewaltsam durchsetzt, wie etwa das Gesetz\n der Schwere, wenn einem das Haus über dem Kopf zusammenpurzelt [Note: „Was soll man von einem Gesetze denken, das sich nur durch periodische\n Revolutionen durchsetzen kann? Es ist eben ein Naturgesetz, das auf der] .\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"39\", \"facs\": \"#f0058\"} --> Die Bestimmung der Werthgrösse durch die Arbeitszeit ist daher unter\n den erscheinenden Bewegungen der relativen Waarenwerthe verstecktes\n Geheimniss. Die eigne gesellschaftliche Bewegung der Produzenten be-\n sitzt für sie die Form einer Bewegung von Sachen, unter deren Controle\n sie stehn, statt sie zu controliren. Was nun endlich die Werthform\n betrifft, so ist es ja grade diese Form, welche die gesellschaftlichen Be-\n ziehungen der Privatarbeiter und daher die gesellschaftlichen Bestimmt-\n heiten der Privatarbeiten sachlich verschleiert, statt sie zu offen-\n baren. Wenn ich sage, Rock, Stiefel u. s. w. beziehn sich auf Leinwand\n als allgemeine Materiatur abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit, so springt die\n Verrücktheit dieses Ausdrucks ins Auge. Aber wenn die Produzenten\n von Rock, Stiefel u. s. w. diese Waaren auf die Leinwand als allgemei-\n nes Aequivalent beziehn, erscheint ihnen die gesellschaftliche Bezie-\n hung ihrer Privatarbeiten genau in dieser verrückten Form.\n\n\n\n\nDerartige Formen bilden eben die Kategorien der bürgerlichen\n Oekonomie. Es sind gesellschaftlich gültige, also objektive Gedan-\n kenformen für Produktionsverhältnisse dieser historisch bestimm-\n ten gesellschaftlichen Produktionsweise.\n\n\n\n\nDie Privatproduzenten treten erst in gesellschaftlichen Contakt ver-\n mittelst ihrer Privatprodukte, der Sachen. Die gesellschaftlichen Be-\n ziehungen ihrer Arbeiten sind und erscheinen daher nicht als unmit-\n telbar gesellschaftliche Verhältnisse der Personen in ihren Arbeiten, son-\n dern als sachliche Verhältnisse der Personen oder gesellschaft-\n liche Verhältnisse der Sachen. Die erste und allgemeinste Dar-\n stellung der Sache als eines gesellschaftlichen Dings ist aber die\n Verwandlung des Arbeitsprodukts in Waare.\n\n\n\n\nDer Mysticismus der Waare entspringt also daraus, dass den Privat-\n produzenten die gesellschaftlichen Bestimmungen ihrer Privat-\n arbeiten als gesellschaftliche Naturbestimmtheiten der\n Arbeitsprodukte, dass die gesellschaftlichen Produktions-\n verhältnisse der Personen als gesellschaftliche Verhält-\n [Note: Bewusstlosigkeit der Betheiligten beruht.“ (Friedrich Engels:\n „Umrisse zu Einer Kritik der Nationalökonomie“, p. 103 in\n „Deutsch-Französische Jahrbücher, herausgegeben von Arnold\n Ruge und Karl Marx. Paris 1849.“)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"40\", \"facs\": \"#f0059\"} -->nisse der Sachen zu einander und zu den Personen erscheinen. Die\n Verhältnisse der Privatarbeiter zur gesellschaftlichen Gesammtarbeit ver-\n gegenständlichen sich ihnen gegenüber und existiren daher für sie\n in den Formen von Gegenständen. Für eine Gesellschaft von\n Waarenproducenten, deren allgemein gesellschaftliches Produktionsver-\n hältniss darin besteht, sich zu ihren Produkten als Waaren, also als\nWerthen zu verhalten, und in dieser sachlichen Form ihre Privat-\n arbeiten auf einander zu beziehn als gleiche menschliche Arbeit,\n ist das Christenthum, mit seinem Kultus des abstrakten Menschen,\n namentlich in seiner bürgerlichen Entwicklung, dem Protestantismus, Deis-\n mus u. s. w., die entsprechendste Religionsform. In den altasiati-\n schen, antiken u. s. w. Produktionsweisen spielt die Verwandlung des\n Produkts in Waare, und daher das Dasein der Menschen als Waarenpro-\n duzenten, eine untergeordnete Rolle, die jedoch um so bedeutender wird,\n je mehr die Gemeinwesen in das Stadium ihres Untergangs treten. Eigent-\n liche Handelsvölker existiren nur in den Intermundien der alten Welt, wie Epi-\n kurs Götter, oder wie Juden in den Poren der polnischen Gesellschaft. Jene\n alten gesellschaftlichen Produktionsorganismen sind ausserordentlich viel\n einfacher und durchsichtiger als der bürgerliche, aber sie beruhen entweder\n auf der Unreife des individuellen Menschen, der sich von der Nabelschnur\n des natürlichen Gattungszusammenhangs mit Andern noch nicht losgerissen\n hat, oder auf unmittelbaren Herrschafts- und Knechtschaftsverhältnissen.\n Sie sind bedingt durch eine niedrige Entwicklungsstufe der Produktivkräfte\n der Arbeit und entsprechend befangene Verhältnisse der Menschen inner-\n halb ihres materiellen Lebenserzeugungsprozesses, daher zu einander und\n zur Natur. Diese wirkliche Befangenheit spiegelt sich ideell wieder in\n den alten Natur- und Volksreligionen. Der religiöse Wiederschein\n der wirklichen Welt kann nur verschwinden, sobald die Verhältnisse des\n praktischen Werkeltagslebens den Menschen tagtäglich durchsichtig ver-\n nünftige Beziehungen zu einander und zur Natur darstellen. Die Ver-\n hältnisse können sich aber nur als das darstellen, was sie sind. Die Ge-\n stalt des gesellschaftlichen Lebensprozesses, d. h. des materiellen Pro-\n duktionsprozesses, streift nur ihren mystischen Nebelschleier ab, sobald\n sie als Produkt frei vergesellschafteter Menschen unter deren bewusster\n planmässiger Controle steht. Dazu ist jedoch eine materielle Grundlage\n der Gesellschaft erheischt oder eine Reihe materieller Existenzbedingungen,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"41\", \"facs\": \"#f0060\"} --> welche selbst wieder das naturwüchsige Produkt einer langen und qual-\n vollen Entwicklungsgeschichte sind.\n\n\n\n\nDie politische Oekonomie hat nun zwar, wenn auch unvollkommen [Note: Das Unzulängliche in Ricardo’s Analyse der Werthgrösse — und es\n ist die beste — wird man aus dem dritten und vierten Buch dieser Schrift ersehn.\n Was aber den Werth überhaupt betrifft, so unterscheidet die klassische politische\n Oekonomie nirgendwo ausdrücklich und mit klarem Bewusstsein Arbeit, die sich\n in Werth, von derselben Arbeit, soweit sie sich im Gebrauchswerth\n ihres Produkts darstellt. Sie macht natürlich den Unterschied thatsächlich, da\n sie die Arbeit das einemal quantitativ, das andremal qualitativ betrachtet. Aber\n es fällt ihr nicht ein, dass bloss quantitativer Unterschied der Arbeiten ihre\nqualitative Einheit oder Gleichheit voraussetzt, also ihre Reduktion auf\nabstrakt menschliche Arbeit. Ricardo z. B. erklärt sich einverstanden\n mit Destutt de Tracy, wenn dieser sagt: „As it is certain that our physical\n and moral faculties are alone our original riches, the employment of those faculties,\n labour of some kind, is our original treasure, and that it is always from this\n employment — that all those things are created which we call riches … It is\n certain too, that all those things only represent the labour which\n has created them, and if they have a value, or even two dis-\n tinctvalues, they can only derive them from that (the value) of the\n labour from which they emanate.“ (Ricardo: „The Principles of Pol.\n Econ. 3 ed. Lond. 1821“, p. 334). Wir deuten nur an, dass Ricardo dem\n Destutt seinen eignen tieferen Sinn unterschiebt. Destutt sagt in der That zwar\n einerseits, dass alle Dinge, die den Reichthum bilden, „die Arbeit reprä-\n sentiren, die sie geschaffen hat“, aber andrerseits, dass sie ihre „zwei ver-\n schiedenen Werthe“ (Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth) vom „Werth der\n Arbeit“ erhalten. Er fällt damit in die Flachheit der Vulgärökonomie, die den\nWerth einer Waare (hier der Arbeit) voraussetzt, um dadurch hinterher den\n Werth der anderen Waaren zu bestimmen. Ricardo liest ihn so, dass sowohl\n im Gebrauchswerth als Tauschwerth sich Arbeit (nicht Werth der Arbeit) dar-\n stellt. Er selbst aber scheidet sowenig den zwieschlächtigen Charakter der\nArbeit, die doppelt dargestellt ist, dass er in dem ganzen Kapitel: „Value\n and Riches, Their Distinctive Properties“ sich mühselig mit den\n Trivialitäten eines J. B. Say herumschlagen muss. Am Ende ist er daher auch\n ganz erstaunt, dass Destutt zwar mit ihm selbst über Arbeit als Werthquelle\n und dennoch andererseits mit Say über den Werthbegriff harmonire] ,\n Werth und Werthgrösse analysirt. Sie hat niemals auch nur die Frage\n gestellt, warum sich die Arbeit im Werth und das Mass der Arbeit\n durch ihre Zeitdauer in der Werthgrösse darstellt? Formen, denen es\n auf der Stirn geschrieben steht, dass sie einer Gesellschaftsformation an-\n gehören, worin der Produktionsprozess die Menschen, der Mensch noch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"42\", \"facs\": \"#f0061\"} --> nicht den Produktionsprozess bemeistert, gelten ihrem bürgerlichen Be-\n wusstsein für eben so selbstverständliche Naturnothwendigkeit als die pro-\n duktive Arbeit selbst. Vorbürgerliche Formen des gesellschaftlichen Pro-\n duktionsorganismus werden daher von ihr behandelt, wie etwa von den\n Kirchenvätern vorchristliche Religionen [Note: „Les économistes ont une singulière manière de procéder. Il n’y a\n pour eux que deux sortes d’institution, celles de l’art et celles de la nature. Les\n institutions de la féodalité sont des institutions artificielles, celles de la bour-\n geoisie sont des institutions naturelles. Ils ressemblent en’ ceci aux théologiens,\n qui eux aussi établissent deux sortes de religion. Toute religion qui n’est pas la\n leur est une invention des hommes, tandis que leur propre religion est une émana-\n tion de dieu. — Ainsi il y a eu de l’histoire, mais il n’y en a plus.“ (Karl\n Marx: „Misère de la Philosophie. Réponse à la Philosophie\n de la Misère par M. Proudhon. 1847“, p. 113.) Wahrhaft drollig ist\n Herr Bastiat, der sich einbildet, die alten Griechen und Römer hätten nur von\nRaub gelebt. Wenn man aber viele Jahrhunderte durch von Raub lebt, muss\n doch beständig etwas zu rauben da sein oder der Gegenstand des Raubes sich\n fortwährend reproduciren. Es scheint daher, dass auch Griechen und Römer einen\n Produktionsprozess hatten, also eine Oekonomie, welche ganz so die materielle\n Grundlage ihrer Welt bildete, wie die bürgerliche Oekonomie die der heutigen\n Welt. Oder meint Bastiat etwa, dass eine Produktionsweise, die auf der Sklaven-\n arbeit beruht, auf einem Raubsystem ruht? Er stellt sich dann auf gefähr-\n lichen Boden. Wenn ein Denkriese wie Aristoteles in seiner Würdigung\n der Sklavenarbeit irrte, warum sollte ein Zwergökonom, wie Bastiat, in\n seiner Würdigung der Lohnarbeit richtig gehn? — Ich ergreife diese Gelegen-\n heit, um einen Einwand, der mir beim Erscheinen meiner Schrift „Zur Kritik\n der Pol. Oekonomie. 1859“ von einem deutsch-amerikanischen Blatte gemacht\n wurde, kurz abzuweisen. Es sagte, meine Ansicht, dass die bestimmte Produk-\n tionsweise und die ihr jedesmal entsprechenden Produktionsverhältnisse, kurz „die\n ökonomische Struktur der Gesellschaft die reale Basis sei, worauf sich ein juristi-\n scher und politischer Ueberbau erhebe, und welcher bestimmte gesellschaftliche\n Bewusstseinsformen entsprächen“, dass „die Produktionsweise des materiellen\n Lebens den socialen, politischen und geistigen Lebensprozess überhaupt bedinge“,\n — alles diess sei zwar richtig für die heutige Welt, wo die materiellen Interessen,\n aber nicht für das Mittelalter, wo der Katholicismus, und für Athen und Rom, wo] .\n\n\n\n\nWie sehr ein Theil der Oekonomen von dem der Waarenwelt ankle-\n benden Fetischismus oder dem gegenständlichen Schein der gesell-\n schaftlichen Arbeitsbestimmungen getäuscht wird, beweist u. a. der\n langweilig abgeschmackte Zank über die Rolle der Natur in der Bil-\n dung des Tauschwerths. Da Tauschwerth eine bestimmte gesellschaft-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"43\", \"facs\": \"#f0062\"} --> liche Manier ist, die auf ein Ding verwandte Arbeit auszudrücken, kann\n er nicht mehr Naturstoff enthalten als etwa der Wechselkurs.\n\n\n\n\nAls allgemeinste und unentwickeltste Form der bürgerlichen Pro-\n duktion, welche desswegen auch schon in früheren Produktionsperioden\n erscheint, obgleich nicht in derselben herrschenden, also charakteristischen\n Weise, war die Waarenform noch relativ leicht zu durchschauen.\n Aber konkretere Formen, wie das Kapital z. B.? Der Fetischismus der\n klassischen Oekonomie wird hier handgreiflich.\n\n\n\n\nUm jedoch nicht vorzugreifen, genüge hier noch ein Beispiel bezüg-\n lich der Waarenform selbst. Man hat gesehn, dass in der Beziehung von\n Waare auf Waare, z. B. von Stiefel auf Stiefelknecht, der Gebrauchswerth\n des Stiefelknechts, also die Nützlichkeit seiner wirklichen dinglichen\n Eigenschaften dem Stiefel durchaus gleichgültig ist. Nur als Erschei-\n nungsform ihres eignen Werths interessirt die Stiefelwaare der Stiefel-\n knecht. Könnten die Waaren also sprechen, so würden sie sagen, unser\n Gebrauchswerth mag den Menschen interessiren. Er kömmt uns nicht\n als Dingen zu. Was uns aber dinglich zukömmt, ist unser Werth.\n Unser eigner Verkehr als Waarendinge beweist das. Wir beziehn uns\n nur als Tauschwerthe auf einander. Man höre nun, wie der Oekonom aus\n der Waarenseele heraus spricht: „Werth (Tauschwerth) ist Eigen-\n schaft der Dinge, Reichthum (Gebrauchswerth) des Menschen. Werth\n in diesem Sinn schliesst nothwendig Austausch ein, Reichthum nicht [Note: „Value is a property of things, riches of man. Value, in this\n sense, necessarily implies exchanges, riches do not.“ „Observations on\n some verbal Disputes in Pol. Econ., particularly relating\n to value and to offer and demand. Lond. 1821“, p. 16.] .“\n [Note: die Politik herrschten. Zunächst ist es befremdlich, dass Jemand vorauszusetzen\n beliebt, diese weltbekannten Redensarten über Mittelalter und antike Welt seien\n irgend Jemand unbekannt geblieben. So viel ist klar, dass das Mittelalter nicht\n vom Katholicismus und die antike Welt nicht von der Politik leben konnten.\n Die Art und Weise, wie sie ihr Leben gewannen, erklärt umgekehrt, warum dort\n die Politik, hier der Katholicismus ihre Rollen spielten. Es gehört übrigens wenig\n Bekanntschaft z. B. mit der Geschichte der römischen Republik dazu, um zu\n wissen, dass die Geschichte des Grundeigenthums ihre Geheimgeschichte bildet.\n Andererseits hat schon Don Quixote den Irrthum gebüsst, dass er die fahrende\n Ritterschaft mit allen ökonomischen Formen der Gesellschaft gleich verträglich\n wähnte.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"44\", \"facs\": \"#f0063\"} --> „Reichthum (Gebrauchswerth) ist ein Attribut des Menschen, Werth\n ein Attribut der Waaren. Ein Mensch oder ein Gemeinwesen ist\nreich; eine Perle oder Diamant ist werthvoll … Eine Perle oder\n Diamant hat Werth als Perle oder Diamant [Note: „Riches are the attribute of man, value is the attribute of commodities.\n A man or a community is rich, a pearl or a diamond is valuable … A pearl or\n a diamond is valuable as a pearl or diamond.“ S. Bailey l. c.\n p. 165.] .“ Bisher hat\n noch kein Chemiker Tauschwerth in Perle oder Diamant entdeckt. Unsere\n Verfasser, die besondern Anspruch auf kritische Tiefe machen, finden aber,\n dass der Gebrauchswerth der Sachen unabhängig von ihren sachlichen\n Eigenschaften, dagegen ihr Tauschwerth ihnen als Sachen zukömmt.\n Was sie hierin bestätigt, ist der sonderbare Umstand, dass der Gebrauchs-\n werth der Dinge sich für den Menschen ohne Austausch realisirt,\n also im unmittelbaren Verhältniss zwischen Ding und Mensch, ihr\n Werth umgekehrt nur im Austausch, d. h. in einem gesell-\n schaftlichen Prozess. Wer erinnert sich hier nicht des guten\n Dogberry, der den Nachtwächter Seacoal belehrt: „Ein gut aussehender\n Mann zu sein, ist eine Gabe der Umstände, aber Lesen und Schreiben\n zu können, kömmt von Natur [Note: Der Verfasser der „Observations“ und S. Bailey beschuldigen\n Ricardo, er habe den Tauschwerth aus einem nur Relativen in etwas Ab-\n solutes verwandelt. Umgekehrt. Er hat die Scheinrelativität, die\n diese Dinge, Diamant und Perlen z. B., als Tauschwerthe besitzen, auf das hinter\n dem Schein verborgene wahre Verhältniss reducirt, auf ihre Relativität\n als blosse Ausdrücke menschlicher Arbeit. Wenn die Ricardianer dem Bailey\n grob, aber nicht schlagend antworteten, so nur weil sie bei Ricardo selbst keinen\n Aufschluss über den inneren Zusammenhang zwischen Werth und Tausch-\n werth fanden.] “.\n\n\n\n\nDie Waare ist unmittelbare Einheit von Gebrauchs-\n werth und Tauschwerth, also zweier Entgegengesetzten. Sie ist\n daher ein unmittelbarer Widerspruch. Dieser Widerspruch muss\n sich entwickeln, sobald sie nicht wie bisher analytisch bald unter dem\n Gesichtspunkt des Gebrauchswerths, bald unter dem Gesichtspunkt des\n Tauschwerths betrachtet, sondern als ein Ganzes wirklich auf andere\n Waaren bezogen wird. Die wirkliche Beziehung der Waaren aufein-\n ander ist aber ihr Austauschprozess.\n\n\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"45\", \"facs\": \"#f0064\"} -->","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0001","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"[1]\", \"facs\": \"#f0020\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"1.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0002","text":"## Erstes Buch.\n Der Produktionsprozess des Kapitals.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"1.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0003","text":"## Erstes Kapitel.\n Waare und Geld.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"1.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0004","text":"## 1) Die Waare.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"1.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","text":"Der Reichthum der Gesellschaften, in welchen kapitalistische Pro-\n duktionsweise herrscht, erscheint als eine „ungeheure Waarensammlung“ [Note: Karl Marx: „Zur Kritik der Politischen Oekonomie. Ber-\n lin 1859“, p. 4.] ,\n die einzelne Waare als seine Elementarform. Unsere Untersuchung\n beginnt daher mit der Analyse der Waare.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"1.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0006","text":"Die Waare ist zunächst ein äusserer Gegenstand, ein Ding, das durch\n seine Eigenschaften menschliche Bedürfnisse irgend einer Art befriedigt.\n Die Natur dieser Bedürfnisse, ob sie z. B. dem Magen oder der Phantasie\n entspringen, ändert nichts an der Sache [Note: „Desire implies want; it is the appetite of the mind, and as natural as\n hunger to the body . . . . the greatest number (of things) have their value from\n supplying the wants of the mind.“ Nicholas Barbon: „A Discourse on\n coining the new money lighter, in answer to Mr. Locke’s Consi-\n derations etc. London 1696“, p. 2, 3.] . Es handelt sich hier auch\n nicht darum, wie die Sache das menschliche Bedürfniss befriedigt, ob un-\n mittelbar als Lebensmittel, d. h. als Gegenstand des Genusses, oder auf\n einem Umweg, als Produktionsmittel.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"1.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0007","text":"Jedes nützliche Ding, wie Eisen, Papier u. s. w., ist unter doppeltem\n [Running matter: I. 1] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"2\", \"facs\": \"#f0021\"} --> Gesichtspunkt zu betrachten, nach Qualität und Quantität. Jedes\n solche Ding ist ein Ganzes vieler Eigenschaften und kann daher nach ver-\n schiedenen Seiten nützlich sein. Diese verschiedenen Seiten und daher\n die mannigfachen Gebrauchsweisen der Dinge zu entdecken, ist geschicht-\n liche That [Note: „Things have an intrinsick vertue (diess bei Barbon die specifische Be-\n zeichnung für Gebrauchswerth), which in all places have the same vertue; as\n the loadstone to attract iron“ (l. c. p. 16). Die Eigenschaft des Magnets, Eisen\n anzuziehn, wurde erst nützlich, sobald man vermittelst derselben die magnetische\n Polarität entdeckt hatte.] . So ist die Findung gesellschaftlicher Masse für die Quan-\n tität der nützlichen Dinge.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"1.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0008","text":"Die Verschiedenheit der Waarenmasse ent-\n springt theils aus der verschiedenen Natur der zu messenden Gegenstände,\n theils aus Convention.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"1.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0009","text":"Die Nützlichkeit eines Dings für das menschliche Leben macht es\n zum Gebrauchswerth [Note: „The natural worth of anything consists in its fitness to supply the\n necessities, or serve the conveniences of human life.“ (John Locke: „Some\n Considerations on the Consequences of the Lowering of Interest.\n 1691“ in „Works edit. Lond. 1777“ V. II p. 28). Im 17. Jahrhundert finden\n wir noch häufig bei englischen Schriftstellern „Worth“ für Gebrauchswerth und\n „Value“ für Tauschwerth, ganz im Geist einer Sprache, die es liebt, die\nunmittelbare Sache germanisch und die reflectirte Sache romanisch aus-\n zudrücken.] . Abkürzend nennen wir das nützliche Ding\n selbst oder den Waarenkörper, wie Eisen, Weizen, Diamant u. s. w.,\nGebrauchswerth, Gut, Artikel. Bei Betrachtung der Gebrauchs-\n werthe wird stets quantitative Bestimmtheit vorausgesetzt, wie Dutzend\n Uhren, Elle Leinwand, Tonne Eisen u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"1.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0010","text":"Die Gebrauchswerthe der\n Waaren liefern das Material einer eignen Disciplin, der Waaren-\n kunde [Note: In der bürgerlichen Gesellschaft herrscht die fictio juris, dass jeder Mensch\n als Waarenkäufer eine encyklopädische Waarenkenntniss besitzt.] . Der Gebrauchswerth verwirklicht sich nur im Gebrauch oder\n der Consumtion. Gebrauchswerthe bilden den stofflichen Inhalt\n des Reichthums, welches immer seine gesellschaftliche Form\n sei. In der von uns zu betrachtenden Gesellschaftsform bilden sie zu-\n gleich die stofflichen Träger des — Tauschwerths.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"1.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011","text":"Der Tauschwerth erscheint zunächst als das quantitative Ver-\n hältniss, die Proportion, worin sich Gebrauchswerthe einer Art gegen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"3\", \"facs\": \"#f0022\"} --> Gebrauchswerthe anderer Art austauschen [Note: „La valeur consiste dans le rapport d’échange qui se trouve entre\n telle chose et telle autre, entre telle mesure d’une production et telle mesure d’une\n autre.“ (Le Trosne: „De L’Intérêt Social“. Physiocrates. ed.\n Daire. Paris 1846. p. 889.)] , ein Verhältniss, das bestän-\n dig mit Zeit und Ort wechselt. Der Tauschwerth scheint daher etwas\n Zufälliges und rein Relatives, ein der Waare innerlicher, immanenter\n Tauschwerth (valeur intrinsèque) also eine contradictio in adjecto  [Note: „Nothing can have an intrinsick value“ (N. Barbon l. c. p. 16), oder\n wie Butler sagt:\n„The value of a thing\n Is just as much as it will bring.“] .\n Betrachten wir die Sache näher.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"1.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0012","text":"Eine einzelne Waare, ein Quarter Weizen z. B. tauscht sich in den\nverschiedensten Proportionen mit andern Artikeln aus. Dennoch\n bleibt sein Tauschwerth unverändert, ob in x Stiefelwichse, y Seide,\n z Gold u. s. w. ausgedrückt. Er muss also von diesen seinen verschiede-\n nen Ausdrucksweisen unterscheidbar sein.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"1.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0013","text":"Nehmen wir ferner zwei Waaren, z. B. Weizen und Eisen. Welches\n immer ihr Austauschverhältniss, es ist stets darstellbar in einer Gleichung,\n worin ein gegebenes Quantum Weizen irgend einem Quantum Eisen gleich-\n gesetzt wird, z. B. 1 Quarter Weizen = a Ctr. Eisen. Was besagt\n diese Gleichung? Dass derselbe Werth in zwei verschiednen\n Dingen, in 1 Qrtr. Weizen und ebenfalls in a Ctr. Eisen existirt. Beide\n sind also gleich einem Dritten, das an und für sich weder das eine, noch\n das andere ist. Jedes der beiden, soweit es Tauschwerth, muss also, un-\n abhängig von dem andern, auf diess Dritte reducirbar sein.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"1.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0014","text":"Ein einfaches geometrisches Beispiel veranschauliche diess. Um den\n Flächeninhalt aller gradlinigen Figuren zu bestimmen und zu vergleichen,\n löst man sie in Dreiecke auf. Das Dreieck selbst reducirt man auf einen\n von seiner sichtbaren Figur ganz verschiednen Ausdruck — das halbe\n Produkt seiner Grundlinie mit seiner Höhe. Ebenso sind die Tauschwerthe\n der Waaren zu reduciren auf ein Gemeinsames, wovon sie ein Mehr\n oder Minder darstellen.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"1.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0015","text":"Dass die Substanz des Tauschwerths ein von der physisch-handgreif-\n lichen Existenz der Waare oder ihrem Dasein als Gebrauchswerth\n [Running matter: 1*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"4\", \"facs\": \"#f0023\"} --> durchaus Verschiednes und Unabhängiges, zeigt ihr Austauschverhältniss\n auf den ersten Blick. Es ist charakterisirt eben durch die Abstraktion\n vom Gebrauchswerth. Dem Tauschwerth nach betrachtet ist näm-\n lich eine Waare grade so gut als jede andre, wenn sie nur in richtiger\n Proportion vorhanden ist [Note: „One sort of wares are as good as another, if the value be equal. There\n is no difference or distinction in things of equal value … One hundred\n pounds worth of lead or iron, is of as great a value as one hundred pounds worth\n of silver and gold.“ (N. Barbon l. c. p. 53 u. 7.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"1.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0016","text":"Unabhängig von ihrem Austauschverhältniss oder von der Form,\n worin sie als Tausch-Werthe erscheinen, sind die Waaren daher\n zunächst als Werthe schlechthin zu betrachten [Note: Wenn wir künftig das Wort „Werth“ ohne weitere Bestimmung brau-\n chen, so handelt es sich immer vom Tauschwerth.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"1.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","text":"Als Gebrauchsgegenstände oder Güter sind die Waaren körperlich\n verschiedne Dinge. Ihr Werth sein bildet dagegen ihre Einheit.\n Diese Einheit entspringt nicht aus der Natur, sondern aus der Gesellschaft.\n Die gemeinsame gesellschaftliche Substanz, die sich in\n verschiednen Gebrauchswerthen nur verschieden darstellt, ist — die\n Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"1.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0018","text":"Als Werthe sind die Waaren nichts als krystallisirte Arbeit.\n Die Masseinheit der Arbeit selbst ist die einfache Durchschnitts-\n arbeit, deren Charakter zwar in verschiednen Ländern und Kultur-\n epochen wechselt, aber in einer vorhandnen Gesellschaft gegeben ist.\n Komplicirtere Arbeit gilt nur als potenzirte oder vielmehr multipli-\n cirte einfache Arbeit, so dass z. B. ein kleineres Quantum komplicirter\n Arbeit gleich einem grösseren Quantum einfacher Arbeit. Wie diese\n Reduktion geregelt wird, ist hier gleichgültig. Dass sie beständig vor-\n geht, zeigt die Erfahrung. Eine Waare mag das Produkt der komplicirte-\n sten Arbeit sein. Ihr Werth setzt sie dem Produkt einfacher Arbeit\n gleich und stellt daher selbst nur ein bestimmtes Quantum einfacher Ar-\n beit dar.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"1.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0019","text":"Ein Gebrauchswerth oder Gut hat also nur einen Werth, weil\nArbeit in ihm vergegenständlicht oder materialisirt ist.\n Wie nun die Grösse seines Werthes messen? Durch das Quantum der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"5\", \"facs\": \"#f0024\"} --> in ihm enthaltenen „werthbildenden Substanz“, der Arbeit. Die Quan-\n tität der Arbeit selbst misst sich an ihrer Zeitdauer und die Arbeits-\n zeit besitzt wieder ihren Massstab an bestimmten Zeittheilen,\n wie Stunde, Tag u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"1.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0020","text":"Es könnte scheinen, dass wenn der Werth einer Waare durch das\n während ihrer Produktion verausgabte Arbeitsquantum bestimmt ist, je\n fauler oder ungeschickter ein Mann, desto werthvoller seine Waare, weil er\n desto mehr Arbeitszeit zu ihrer Verfertigung braucht. Aber nur die\ngesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit zählt als werth-\n bildend. Gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit ist Arbeitszeit, erheischt\n um irgend einen Gebrauchswerth mit den vorhandnen gesellschaftlich - nor-\n malen Produktionsbedingungen und dem gesellschaftlichen Durchschnitts-\n grad von Geschick und Intensivität der Arbeit herzustellen. Nach der\n Einführung des Dampfwebstuhls in England z. B. genügte vielleicht halb\n so viel Arbeit als vorher, um ein gegebenes Quantum Garn in Gewebe zu\n verwandeln.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"1.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0021","text":"Der englische Handweber brauchte zu dieser Verwandlung\n in der That nach wie vor dieselbe Arbeitszeit, aber das Produkt seiner\n individuellen Arbeitsstunde stellte jetzt nur noch eine halbe gesell-\n schaftliche Arbeitsstunde dar und fiel daher auf die Hälfte seines früheren\n Werths.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"1.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0022","text":"Es ist also nur das Quantum gesellschaftlich nothwen-\n diger Arbeit oder die zur Herstellung eines Gebrauchs-\n werths gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit, welche\n seine Werthgrösse bestimmt. Die einzelne Waare gilt hier überhaupt\n als Durchschnittsexemplar ihrer Art [Note: „Toutes les productions d’un même genre ne forment proprement qu’une\n masse, dont le prix se détermine en général et sans égard aux circonstances par-\n ticulières“. (Le Trosne l. c. p. 893.)] . Waaren, worin gleich grosse\n Arbeitsquanta enthalten sind, oder die in derselben Arbeitszeit\n hergestellt werden können, haben daher dieselbe Werthgrösse.\n Der Werth einer Waare verhält sich zum Werth jeder andern Waare, wie\n die zur Produktion der einen nothwendige Arbeitszeit zu der für die Pro-\n duktion der andern nothwendigen Arbeitszeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"1.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0023","text":"„Als Werthe sind alle\n Waaren nur bestimmte Masse festgeronnener Arbeitszeit“ [Note: K. Marx l. c. p. 6.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"1.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0024","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"6\", \"facs\": \"#f0025\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"1.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0025","text":"Die Werthgrösse einer Waare bliebe daher constant, wäre die\n zu ihrer Produktion erheischte Arbeitszeit constant. Letztere wech-\n selt aber mit jedem Wechsel in der Produktivkraft der Arbeit.\n Die Produktivkraft der Arbeit ist durch mannigfache Umstände be-\n stimmt, unter andern durch den Durchschnittsgrad des Geschickes der\n Arbeiter, die Entwicklungsstufe der Wissenschaft und ihrer technolo-\n gischen Anwendbarkeit, die gesellschaftliche Combination des Pro-\n duktionsprozesses, den Umfang und die Wirkungsfähigkeit der Pro-\n duktionsmittel, und durch Naturverhältnisse. Dasselbe Quantum\n Arbeit stellt sich z. B. mit günstiger Jahreszeit in 8 Bushel Weizen\n dar, mit ungünstiger in nur 4. Dasselbe Quantum Arbeit liefert mehr\n Metalle in reichhaltigen, als in armen Minen u. s. w. Diamanten kommen\n selten in der Erdrinde vor und ihre Findung kostet daher im Durch-\n schnitt viel Arbeitszeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"1.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0026","text":"Folglich stellen sie in wenig Volumen viel Ar-\n beit dar. Jacob bezweifelt, dass Gold jemals seinen vollen Werth be-\n zahlt hat. Noch mehr gilt diess vom Diamant. Nach Eschwege\n hatte 1823 die achtzigjährige Gesammtausbeute der brasilischen Diamant-\n gruben noch nicht den Werth des 1½jährigen Durchschnittsprodukts der\n brasilischen Zucker- oder Kaffeepflanzungen erreicht. Mit reichhaltigeren\n Gruben würde dasselbe Arbeitsquantum sich in mehr Diamanten darstel-\n len und ihr Werth sinken. Gelingt es mit wenig Arbeit Kohle in Dia-\n mant zu verwandeln, so kann sein Werth unter den von Ziegelsteinen fal-\n len. Allgemein: Je grösser die Produktivkraft der Arbeit, desto kleiner\n die zur Herstellung eines Artikels erheischte Arbeitszeit, desto kleiner die\n in ihm krystallisirte Arbeitsmasse, desto kleiner sein Werth.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"1.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0027","text":"Umgekehrt,\n je kleiner die Produktivkraft der Arbeit, desto grösser die zur Herstellung\n eines Artikels nothwendige Arbeitszeit, desto grösser sein Werth. Die\nWerthgrösse einer Waare wechselt also direkt wie das Quan-\n tum und umgekehrt wie die Produktivkraft der sich in ihr ver-\n wirklichenden Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"1.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0028","text":"Wir kennen jetzt die Substanz des Werths. Es ist die Arbeit.\n Wir kennen sein Grössenmass. Es ist die Arbeitszeit. Seine\nForm, die den Werth eben zum Tausch-Werth stempelt, bleibt zu\n analysiren. Vorher jedoch sind die bereits gefundenen Bestimmungen\n etwas näher zu entwickeln.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"1.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0029","text":"Ein Ding kann Gebrauchswerth sein, ohne Tauschwerth\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"7\", \"facs\": \"#f0026\"} --> zu sein. Es ist diess der Fall, wenn sein Dasein für den Menschen nicht\n durch Arbeit vermittelt ist. So Luft, jungfräulicher Boden, natürliche\n Wiesen, wildwachsendes Holz u. s. w. Ein Ding kann nützlich und Pro-\n dukt menschlicher Arbeit sein, ohne Waare zu sein. Wer durch sein\n Produkt sein eignes Bedürfniss befriedigt, schafft zwar Gebrauchs-\n werth, aber nicht Waare. Um Waare zu produciren, muss er nicht\n nur Gebrauchswerth produciren, sondern Gebrauchswerth für\n andre, gesellschaftlichen Gebrauchswerth. Endlich kann\n kein Ding Werth sein, ohne Gebrauchsgegenstand zu sein. Ist es nutz-\n los, so ist auch die in ihm enthaltene Arbeit nutzlos, zählt nicht als Arbeit\n und bildet daher keinen Werth.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"1.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0030","text":"Ursprünglich erschien uns die Waare als ein Zwieschläch-\n tiges, Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth. Näher betrachtet wird sich\n zeigen, dass auch die in der Waare enthaltene Arbeit zwie-\n schlächtig ist. Dieser Punkt, der von mir zuerst kritisch entwickelt\n wurde [Note: l. c. p. 12, 13 und passim.] , ist der Springpunkt, um den sich das Verständniss der po-\n litischen Oekonomie dreht.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"1.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0031","text":"Nehmen wir zwei Waaren, etwa einen Rock und 10 Ellen Leinwand.\n Der erstere habe den zweifachen Werth der letzteren, so dass wenn\n 10 Ellen Leinwand = W, der Rock = 2 W.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"1.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0032","text":"Der Rock ist ein Gebrauchswerth, der ein besondres Bedürfniss be-\n friedigt. Um ihn hervorzubringen, bedarf es einer bestimmten Art\n zweckmässig produktiver Thätigkeit. Sie ist bestimmt nach\n Zweck, Operationsweise, Gegenstand, Mitteln und Resultat. Die Arbeit,\n deren Nützlichkeit sich so im Gebrauchswerth ihres Produkts oder darin\n darstellt, dass ihr Produkt ein Gebrauchswerth ist, heisse hier der Verein-\n fachung halber kurzweg nützliche Arbeit. Unter diesem Gesichts-\n punkt ist sie stets betrachtet in Bezug auf den Nutzeffekt, dessen\n Hervorbringung sie bezweckt.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"1.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0033","text":"Wie Rock und Leinwand qualitativ verschiedne Gebrauchs-\n werthe, so sind die ihr Dasein vermittelnden Arbeiten qualitativ\n verschieden — Schneiderarbeit und Weberei. Wären jene\n Dinge nicht qualitativ verschiedne Gebrauchswerthe und daher Produkte\n qualitativ verschiedner nützlicher Arbeiten, so könnten sie sich über-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"8\", \"facs\": \"#f0027\"} --> haupt nicht als Waaren gegenübertreten. Rock tauscht sich nicht aus\n gegen Rock, derselbe Gebrauchswerth nicht gegen denselben Gebrauchswerth.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"1.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0034","text":"In der Gesammtheit der verschiedenartigen Gebrauchswerthe oder\n Waarenkörper erscheint eine Gesammtheit eben so mannigfaltiger, nach\n Gattung, Art, Familie, Unterart, Varietät verschiedner nützlicher Arbeiten\n — eine gesellschaftliche Theilung der Arbeit. Sie ist Exi-\n stenzbedingung der Waarenproduktion, obgleich Waarenproduktion nicht\n umgekehrt Existenzbedingung gesellschaftlicher Arbeitstheilung. In der\n altindischen Gemeinde ist die Arbeit gesellschaftlich getheilt, ohne dass die\nProdukte zu Waaren werden. Oder, ein näher liegendes Beispiel,\n in jeder Fabrik ist die Arbeit systematisch getheilt, aber diese Theilung\n nicht dadurch vermittelt, dass die Arbeiter ihre individuellen Pro-\n dukte austauschen. Nur Produkte selbstständiger und von einander\n unabhängiger Privatarbeiten treten einander als Waaren\n gegenüber.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"1.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0035","text":"Man hat also gesehn : In dem Gebrauchswerth jeder Waare steckt\n eine bestimmte zweckmässig produktive Thätigkeit oder nützliche Arbeit.\n Gebrauchswerthe können sich nicht als Waaren gegenübertreten, wenn\n nicht qualitativ verschiedne nützliche Arbeiten in ihnen stecken. In einer\n Gesellschaft, deren Produkte allgemein die Form der Waare anneh-\n men, d. h. in einer Gesellschaft von Waarenproducenten, entwickelt sich\n dieser qualitative Unterschied der nützlichen Arbeiten, welche unabhängig\n von einander als Privatgeschäfte selbstständiger Producenten betrieben\n werden, zu einem vielgliedrigen System, zu einer gesellschaftlichen Thei-\n lung der Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"1.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0036","text":"Dem Rock ist es übrigens gleichgültig, ob er vom Schneider oder\n vom Kunden des Schneiders getragen wird. In beiden Fällen wirkt er als\n Gebrauchswerth. Ebensowenig ist das Verhältniss zwischen dem Rock\n und der ihn producirenden Arbeit an und für sich dadurch verändert, dass\n die Schneiderarbeit eigne Profession wird, selbstständiges Glied der gesell-\n schaftlichen Theilung der Arbeit. Wo ihn das Kleidungsbedürfniss zwang,\n hat der Mensch Jahrtausende lang geschneidert, bevor aus einem Menschen\n ein Schneider ward. Aber das Dasein von Rock, Leinwand, jedem nicht\n von Natur vorhandnen Element des stofflichen Reichthums,\n musste immer vermittelt sein durch eine spezielle, zweckmässig produktive\n Thätigkeit, die besondere Naturstoffe besondern menschlichen Bedürfnissen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"9\", \"facs\": \"#f0028\"} --> assimilirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"1.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0037","text":"Als Bildnerin von Gebrauchswerthen, als nützliche Ar-\n beit, ist die Arbeit daher von allen Gesellschaftsformen unabhängige\n Existenzbedingung des Menschen, ewige Naturnothwendigkeit, um den\n Stoffwechsel zwischen Mensch und Natur, also das menschliche Leben zu\n vermitteln.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"1.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0038","text":"Die Gebrauchswerthe Rock, Leinwand u. s. w., kurz die Waaren-\n körper, sind Verbindungen von zwei Elementen, Naturstoff\n und Arbeit. Zieht man die Gesammtsumme aller verschiedenen nützlichen\n Arbeiten ab, die in Rock, Leinwand u. s. w. stecken, so bleibt stets ein\n materielles Substrat zurück, das ohne Zuthun des Menschen von Natur vor-\n handen ist. Der Mensch kann in seiner Produktion nur verfahren, wie die\n Natur selbst, d. h. nur die Formen der Stoffe ändern [Note: „Tutti i fenomeni dell’ universo, sieno essi prodotti della mano dell’\n uomo, ovvero delle universali leggi della fisica, non ci danno idea di attuale crea-\n zione, ma unicamente di una modificazione della materia.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"1.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0039","text":"Accostare\n e separare sono gli unici elementi che l’ingegno umano ritrova analizando l’idea\n della riproduzione; e tanto e riproduzione di valore (Gebrauchswerth, ob-\n gleich Verri hier in seiner Polemik gegen die Physiokraten selbst nicht recht\n weiss, von welcher Sorte Werth er spricht) e di richezze se la terra, l’aria e l’acqua\n ne campi si transmutino in grano, come se colla mano dell’ uomo il glutine di un insetto\n si transmuti in velluto, ovvero alcuni pezzetti di metallo si organizzino a formare una\n ripetizione“. (Pietro Verri: „Meditazioni sulla Economia Poli-\n tica“ (zuerst gedruckt 1773) in der Ausgabe der italienischen Oekonomen von\nCustodi, Parte Moderna, t. XV p. 22.)] . Noch\n mehr. In dieser Arbeit der Formung selbst wird er beständig unterstützt\n von Naturkräften.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"1.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0040","text":"Arbeit ist also nicht die einzige Quelle der\n von ihr producirten Gebrauchswerthe, des stofflichen\n Reichthums. Die Arbeit ist sein Vater, wie William Petty sagt,\n und die Erde seine Mutter.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"1.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0041","text":"Gehn wir nun von der Waare, so weit sie Gebrauchsgegenstand, über\n zum Waaren-Werth.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"1.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0042","text":"Nach unsrer Unterstellung hat der Rock den doppelten Werth der\n Leinwand. Diess ist aber nur ein quantitativer Unterschied, der uns\n zunächst noch nicht interessirt. Wir erinnern daher, dass wenn der\n Werth eines Rockes doppelt so gross als der von 10 Ellen Leinwand,\n 20 Ellen Leinwand dieselbe Werthgrösse haben wie ein Rock.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"10\", \"facs\": \"#f0029\"} --> Als Werthe sind Rock und Leinwand Dinge von gleicher Substanz,\n objektive Ausdrücke gleichartiger Arbeit. Aber Schneider-\n arbeit und Weberei sind qualitativ verschiedne Arbeiten.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"1.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0043","text":"Es giebt\n jedoch Gesellschaftszustände, worin derselbe Mensch abwechselnd\n schneidert und webt, diese beiden verschiednen Arbeitsweisen daher nur\nModificationen der Arbeit desselben Individuums und\n noch nicht besondre feste Functionen verschiedner Individuen sind, ganz\n wie der Rock, den unser Schneider heute, und die Hosen, die er morgen\n macht, nur Variationen derselben individuellen Arbeit voraussetzen. Der\n Augenschein lehrt ferner, dass in unsrer kapitalistischen Gesellschaft, je\n nach der wechselnden Richtung der Arbeitsnachfrage, eine gegebene\n Portion menschlicher Arbeit abwechselnd in der Form von\n Schneiderei oder in der Form von Weberei zugeführt wird. Dieser Form-\n wechsel der Arbeit mag nicht ohne Friction abgehn, aber er muss gehn.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"1.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0044","text":"Sieht man ab von der Bestimmtheit der produktiven Thätigkeit und daher\n vom nützlichen Charakter der Arbeit, so bleibt das an ihr, dass sie eine\nVerausgabung menschlicher Arbeitskraft ist. Schneider-\n arbeit und Weberei, obgleich qualitativ verschiedne produktive Thätig-\n keiten, sind beide produktive Verausgabung von menschlichem Hirn,\n Muskel, Nerv, Hand u. s. w., und in diesem Sinn beide menschliche\n Arbeit. Es sind nur zwei verschiedne Formen, menschliche Arbeits-\n kraft zu verausgaben. Allerdings muss die menschliche Arbeitskraft selbst\n mehr oder minder entwickelt sein, um in dieser oder jener Form veraus-\n gabt zu werden. Der Werth der Waaren aber stellt menschliche Arbeit\n schlechthin dar, Verausgabung menschlicher Arbeitskraft über-\n haupt.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"1.44"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0045","text":"Wie nun in der bürgerlichen Gesellschaft ein General oder Ban-\n quier eine grosse, der Mensch schlechthin dagegen eine sehr schäbige\n Rolle spielt [Note: Vgl. Hegel, „Philosophie des Rechts. Berlin 1840“, p. 250,\n §. 190.] , so steht es hier auch mit der menschlichen Arbeit.\n Sie ist Verausgabung einfacher Arbeitskraft, die jeder gewöhnliche\n Mensch, ohne besondere Entwicklung, in seinem leiblichen Organismus be-\n sitzt. Die Arbeitskraft eines Bauernknechts gelte z. B. für einfache Ar-\n beitskraft, ihre Verausgabung daher für einfache Arbeit oder mensch-\n liche Arbeit ohne weitern Schnörkel, Schneiderarbeit dagegen für\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"11\", \"facs\": \"#f0030\"} --> Verausgabung höher entwickelter Arbeitskraft.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"1.45"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0046","text":"Während sich der Arbeits-\n tag des Bauernknechts daher etwa im Werthausdruck von ½ W, stellt\n sich der Arbeitstag des Schneiders im Werthausdrucke von W dar [Note: Der Leser muss aufmerken, dass hier nicht vom Lohn oder Werth die\n Rede ist, den der Arbeiter etwa für einen Arbeitstag erhält, sondern vom Waaren-\nwerth, worin sich sein Arbeitstag vergegenständlicht. Die Kategorie des Ar-\n beitslohns existirt überhaupt noch nicht auf dieser Stufe unsrer Darstellung.] .\n Dieser Unterschied ist jedoch nur quantitativ. Wenn der Rock das\n Produkt eines Arbeitstags des Schneiders, hat er denselben Werth wie das\n Produkt von 2 Arbeitstagen des Bauernknechts. So zählt aber die\n Schneiderarbeit immer nur als multiplicirte Bauernarbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"1.46"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0047","text":"Die ver-\n schiednen Proportionen, worin verschiedne Arbeitsarten auf einfache Ar-\n beit als ihre Masseinheit reducirt sind, werden durch einen gesell-\n schaftlichen Prozess hinter dem Rücken der Produzenten festgesetzt und\n scheinen ihnen daher durch das Herkommen gegeben. Der Vereinfachung\n halber gilt uns im Folgenden jede Art Arbeitskraft unmittelbar für ein-\n fache Arbeitskraft, wodurch nur die Mühe der Reduktion erspart wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"1.47"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0048","text":"Wie also in den Werthen Rock und Leinwand von dem Unter-\n schied ihrer Gebrauchswerthe abstrahirt ist, so in der Arbeit,\n die diese Werthe darstellen, von dem Unterschied der nützlichen\n Formen, worin sie das einemal Schneiderarbeit ist, das andremal\nWeberei. Wie die Gebrauchswerthe Rock und Leinwand Ver-\n bindungen zweckbestimmter, produktiver Thätigkeiten mit Tuch und\n Garn sind, die Werthe Rock und Leinwand dagegen blosse gleich-\n artige Arbeitsgallerten, so gilt auch die in diesen Werthen\n enthaltene Arbeit nicht durch ihr produktives Verhalten zu Tuch und Garn,\n sondern nur als Verausgabung menschlicher Arbeitskraft.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"1.48"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0049","text":"Bildungselemente der Gebrauchswerthe Rock und Leinwand sind\n Schneiderarbeit und Weberei eben durch ihre verschiednen Qualitäten,\nSubstanz des Rockwerths und Leinwandwerths sind sie nur, soweit\n von ihrer besondern Qualität abstrahirt wird und beide gleiche\n Qualität besitzen, die Qualität menschlicher Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"1.49"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0050","text":"Rock und Leinwand sind aber nicht nur Werthe überhaupt, son-\n dern Werthe von bestimmter Grösse und nach unsrer Unterstellung\n ist der Rock doppelt so viel werth, als 10 Ellen Leinwand. Woher diese\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"12\", \"facs\": \"#f0031\"} --> Verschiedenheit ihrer Werthgrössen? Daher dass die Leinwand nur\n halb so viel Arbeit enthält, als der Rock, sodass zur Produktion des letz-\n tern die Arbeitskraft während doppelt soviel Zeit verausgabt werden\n muss, als zur Produktion der erstern.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"1.50"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0051","text":"Wenn also mit Bezug auf den Gebrauchswerth die in der\n Waare enthaltne Arbeit nur qualitativ gilt, gilt sie mit Bezug auf die\nWerthgrösse nur quantitativ, nachdem sie bereits auf menschliche\n Arbeit ohne weitere Qualität reducirt ist. Dort handelt es sich um das\nWie und Was der Arbeit, hier um ihr Wie Viel, ihre Zeitdauer. Da die\n Werthgrösse einer Waare nur das Quantum der in ihr enthaltnen Arbeit\n misst, müssen Waaren in gewisser Proportion stets gleich grosse\n Werthe sein.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"1.51"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0052","text":"Bleibt die Produktivkraft sage aller zur Produktion eines Rocks\n erheischten nützlichen Arbeiten unverändert, so steigt die Werthgrösse der\n Röcke mit ihrer eignen Quantität. Wenn 1 Rock x, stellen 2 Röcke 2 x\n Arbeitstage dar u. s. w. Nimm aber an, die zur Produktion eines Rocks\n nothwendige Arbeitszeit steige auf das Doppelte oder falle um die Hälfte.\n Im ersten Fall hat ein Rock soviel Werth als vorher zwei Röcke, im letz-\n tern Fall haben zwei Röcke nur so viel Werth, als vorher einer, obgleich\n in beiden Fällen ein Rock nach wie vor dieselben Dienste leistet und die\n in ihm enthaltne nützliche Arbeit nach wie vor von derselben Güte bleibt.\n Aber das in seiner Produktion verausgabte Arbeits quantum hat sich\n verändert.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"1.52"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0053","text":"Ein grössres Quantum Gebrauchswerth bildet an und für sich grös-\n sern stofflichen Reichthum, zwei Röcke mehr als einer. Mit zwei\n Röcken kann man zwei Menschen kleiden, mit einem Rock nur einen Men-\n schen u. s. w. Dennoch kann der steigenden Masse des stofflichen Reich-\n thums ein gleichzeitiger Fall seiner Werthgrösse entsprechen. Diese\n gegensätzliche Bewegung entspringt aus der zwieschlächtigen Be-\n stimmung der Arbeit. Produktivkraft ist natürlich stets Produktivkraft\n nützlicher, konkreter Arbeit. Sie drückt in der That nur den Wirkungs-\n grad zweckbestimmter produktiver Thätigkeit in gegebnem Zeitraum aus.\n Die nützliche Arbeit wird daher reichere oder dürftigere Produktenquelle\n im direkten Verhältniss zum Steigen oder Fallen ihrer Produktiv-\n kraft. Dagegen trifft ein Wechsel der Produktivkraft die im Werth\n dargestellte Arbeit an und für sich gar nicht.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"1.53"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0054","text":"Da die Produktivkraft der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"13\", \"facs\": \"#f0032\"} --> konkreten nützlichen Form der Arbeit angehört, kann sie natürlich die\n Arbeit nicht mehr berühren, sobald von ihrer konkreten nützlichen Form\n abstrahirt wird. Dieselbe Arbeit stellt sich daher in denselben Zeit-\n räumen stets in derselben Werthgrösse dar, wie immer die Pro-\n duktivkraft wechsle. Aber sie liefert in demselben Zeitraum ver-\n schiedne Quanta Gebrauchswerthe, mehr wenn die Produktiv-\n kraft steigt, weniger, wenn sie sinkt. Im erstern Fall kann es geschehn,\n dass 2 Röcke weniger Arbeit enthalten als früher einer.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"1.54"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0055","text":"Derselbe Wech-\n sel der Produktivkraft, der die Fruchtbarkeit der Arbeit und daher die\n Masse der von ihr gelieferten Gebrauchswerthe vermehrt, kann also die\nWerthgrösse selbst der vermehrten Gesammtmasse vermindern,\n wenn er nämlich die zu ihrer Produktion nothwendige Arbeitszeit ab-\n kürzt. Ebenso umgekehrt.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"1.55"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056","text":"Aus dem Bisherigen folgt, dass in der Waare zwar nicht zwei ver-\n schiedene Sorten Arbeit stecken, wohl aber dieselbe Arbeit verschieden\n und selbst entgegengesetzt bestimmt ist, je nachdem sie auf den Ge-\n brauchswerth der Waare als ihr Produkt oder auf den Waaren-\n Werth als ihren bloss gegenständlichen Ausdruck bezogen wird.\n Wie die Waare vor allem Gebrauchsgegenstand sein muss, um Werth zu\n sein, so muss die Arbeit vor allem nützliche Arbeit, zweckbestimmte pro-\n duktive Thätigkeit sein, um als Verausgabung menschlicher\n Arbeitskraft und daher als menschliche Arbeit schlechthin\n zu zählen.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"1.56"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0057","text":"Da bisher nur noch Werthsubstanz und Werthgrösse bestimmt, wen-\n den wir uns jetzt zur Analyse der Werthform.","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"1.57"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0058","text":"Kehren wir zunächst wieder zurück zur ersten Erscheinungs-\n form des Waarenwerths.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"1.58"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0059","text":"Wir nehmen zwei Quanta Waaren, die gleichviel Arbeitszeit\n zu ihrer Produktion kosten, also gleiche Werthgrössen sind, und\n wir haben 40 Ellen Leinwand = 2 Röcke, oder 40 Ellen Lein-\n wand sind zwei Röcke werth. Wir sehn, dass der Werth der Lein-\n wand in einem bestimmten Quantum von Röcken ausgedrückt ist. Der\nWerth einer Waare, so dargestellt im Gebrauchswerth einer andern\n Waare, heisst ihr relativer Werth.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"1.59"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0060","text":"Der relative Werth einer Waare kann wechseln, obgleich ihr Werth\n constant bleibt. Umgekehrt kann ihr relativer Werth constant bleiben,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"14\", \"facs\": \"#f0033\"} --> obgleich ihr Werth wechselt. Die Gleichung: 40 Ellen Leinwand\n = 2 Röcke setzt nämlich voraus, dass beide Waaren gleich viel Arbeit\n kosten. Mit jedem Wechsel in der Produktivkraft der sie hervorbringen-\n den Arbeiten wechselt aber die zu ihrer Produktion nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit. Betrachten wir den Einfluss solcher Wechsel auf den relativen\n Werth.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"1.60"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0061","text":"I. Der Werth der Leinwand wechsle, während der Rockwerth con-\n stant bleibt. Verdoppelt sich die zur Produktion der Leinwand veraus-\n gabte Arbeitszeit, etwa in Folge zunehmender Unfruchtbarkeit des flachs-\n tragenden Bodens, so verdoppelt sich ihr Werth. Statt 40 Ellen Lein-\n wand = 2 Röcke, hätten wir: 40 Ellen Leinwand = 4 Röcke,\n da 2 Röcke jetzt nur halb so viel Arbeitszeit enthalten als 40 Ellen Lein-\n wand. Nimmt dagegen die zur Produktion der Leinwand nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit um die Hälfte ab, etwa in Folge verbesserter Webstühle, so\n sinkt der Leinwandwerth um die Hälfte. Demgemäss jetzt: 40 Ellen\n Leinwand = 1 Rock. Der relative Werth der Waare A, d. h.\n ihr Werth ausgedrückt in der Waare B, steigt und fällt also direkt\n wie der Werth der Waare A, bei gleichbleibendem Werth der\n Waare B.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"1.61"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0062","text":"II. Der Werth der Leinwand bleibe constant, während der Rock-\nwerth wechsle. Verdoppelt sich unter diesen Umständen die zur Pro-\n duktion des Rockes nothwendige Arbeitszeit, etwa in Folge ungünstiger\n Wollschur, so haben wir statt 40 Ellen Leinwand = 2 Röcke jetzt: 40 El-\n len Leinwand = 1 Rock. Fällt dagegen der Werth des Rocks um\n die Hälfte, so 40 Ellen Leinwand = 4 Röcke. Bei gleichbleibendem\n Werth der Waare A, fällt oder steigt daher ihr relativer, in der Waare\n B ausgedrückter Werth im umgekehrten Verhältniss zum\n Werthwechsel von B.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"1.62"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0063","text":"Vergleicht man die verschiedenen Fälle sub I und II, so ergiebt sich,\n dass derselbe Wechsel des relativen Werths aus ganzent-\n gegengesetzten Ursachen entspringen kann. So wird aus\n40 Ellen Leinwand = 2 Röcke 1) die Gleichung 40 Ellen\n Leinwand = 4 Röcke, entweder weil der Werth der Leinwand\n sich verdoppelt oder der Werth der Röcke um die Hälfte fällt, und 2) die\n Gleichung 40 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock, entweder weil der Werth\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"15\", \"facs\": \"#f0034\"} --> der Leinwand um die Hälfte sinkt oder der Werth des Rockes auf das\n Doppelte steigt.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"1.63"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0064","text":"III. Die zur Produktion von Leinwand und Rock nothwendigen\n Arbeitsquanta wechseln gleichzeitig, in derselben Richtung und derselben\n Proportion. In diesem Falle nach wie vor 40 Ellen Leinwand =\n 2 Röcke, wie immer ihre Werthe verändert seien. Man entdeckt ihren\n Werthwechsel, sobald man sie mit einer dritten Waare vergleicht, deren\n Werth constant blieb. Stiegen oder fielen die Werthe aller Waaren gleich-\n zeitig und in derselben Proportion, so blieben ihre relativen Werthe\n unverändert. Ihren wirklichen Werthwechsel ersähe man daraus, dass in\n derselben Arbeitszeit nun allgemein ein grösseres oder kleineres Waaren-\n quantum als vorher geliefert würde.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"1.64"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0065","text":"IV. Die zur Produktion von Leinwand und Rock resp. nothwendigen\n Arbeitszeiten, und daher ihre Werthe, mögen gleichzeitig in derselben\n Richtung wechseln, aber in ungleichem Grad, oder in entgegengesetzter\n Richtung u. s. w. Der Einfluss aller möglichen derartigen Combinatio-\n nen auf den relativen Werth einer Waare ergiebt sich einfach durch An-\n wendung der Fälle I., II. und III.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"1.65"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0066","text":"Wir haben eben untersucht, wie weit Wechsel in der relativen\n Werthgrösse einer Waare, der Leinwand, einen Wechsel ihrer eignen\n Werthgrösse wiederspiegelt, und überhaupt den relativen Werth\n nur nach seiner quantitativen Seite betrachtet. Wir wenden uns\n jetzt zu seiner Form. Wenn der relative Werth Darstellungsform\n des Werths, ist der Ausdruck der Aequivalenz zweier Waaren, wie\n x Waare A = y Waare B oder 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock, die ein-\n fache Form desrelativen Werths.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"1.66"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0067","text":"I. Erste oder einfache Form des relativen Werths:\n 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock. (x Waare A = y Waare B.)","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"1.67"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0068","text":"Diese Form ist etwas schwierig zu analysiren, weil sie einfach\n ist [Note: Sie ist gewissermassen die Zellenform oder, wie Hegel sagen würde, das\nAn sich des Geldes.] . Die in ihr enthaltenen unterschiedenen Bestimmungen sind ver-\n hüllt, unentwickelt, abstrakt und daher nur durch einige Anstrengung der\n Abstraktionskraft auseinander- und festzuhalten. So viel ergiebt sich\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"16\", \"facs\": \"#f0035\"} --> aber auf den ersten Blick, dass die Form dieselbe bleibt, ob 20 Ellen\n Leinwand = 1 Rock oder 20 Ellen Leinwand = x Röcke [Note: Die wenigen Oekonomen, die sich, wie J. Bailey, mit der Analyse der\n Werthform beschäftigt haben, konnten zu keinem Resultat kommen, einmal, weil\n sie Werthform und Werth verwechseln, zweitens, weil sie, unter dem rohen Ein-\n fluss des praktischen Bürgers, von vorn herein ausschliesslich die quantitative Be-\n stimmtheit ins Auge fassen.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"1.68"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0069","text":"„The command of quantity .... constitutes\nvalue“. („Money and its Vicissitudes“. Lond. 1837, p. 11.) Verfasser:\n J. Bailey.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"1.69"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0070","text":"Leinwand kömmt auf die Welt in Gestalt eines Gebrauchswerths\n oder nützlichen Dings. Ihre steifleinene Körperlichkeit oder Natural-\n form ist daher nicht ihre Werthform, sondern deren grades Gegen-\n theil. Ihr eignes Werthsein zeigt sie zunächst dadurch, dass sie sich\n auf eine andre Waare, den Rock, als ihr Gleichesbezieht. Wäre\n sie nicht selbst Werth, so könnte sie sich nicht auf den Rock als Werth,\n als Ihresgleichen, beziehn. Qualitativ setzt sie sich den Rock\n gleich, indem sie sich auf ihn bezieht als Vergegenständlichung\n gleichartiger menschlicher Arbeit, d. h. ihrer eignen\n Werthsubstanz, und sie setzt sich nur einen Rock gleich statt\n x Röcke, weil sie nicht nur Werth überhaupt, sondern Werth von be-\n stimmter Grösse ist, ein Rock aber grade soviel Arbeit enthält als\n 20 Ellen Leinwand.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"1.70"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0071","text":"Durch diese Beziehung auf den Rock schlägt die\n Leinwand verschiedne Fliegen mit einer Klappe. Indem sie die andre\n Waare sich als Werth gleichsetzt, bezieht sie sich auf sich\n selbst als Werth. Indem sie sich auf sich selbst als Werth be-\n zieht, unterscheidet sie sich zugleich von sich selbst als Ge-\n brauchswerth. Indem sie ihre Werthgrösse — und Werthgrösse\n ist beides, Werth überhaupt und quantitativ gemessner Werth — im\n Rocke ausdrückt, giebt sie ihrem Werthsein eine von ihrem un-\n mittelbaren Dasein unterschiedne Werthform. Indem sie sich so als ein\n in sich selbst Differenzirtes darstellt, stellt sie sich erst wirklich als\n Waare dar — nützliches Ding, das zugleich Werth ist. Soweit die\n Leinwand Gebrauchswerth, ist sie ein selbstständiges Ding. Ihr\nWerth erscheint dagegen nur im Verhältniss zu andrer Waare,\n dem Rocke z.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"1.71"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0072","text":"B., ein Verhältniss, worin die Waarenart Rock ihr quali-\n tativ gleichgesetzt wird und daher in bestimmter Quantität\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"17\", \"facs\": \"#f0036\"} --> gleichgilt, sie ersetzt, mit ihr austauschbar ist. Eigne, vom Gebrauchs-\n werth unterschiedne Form erhält der Werth daher nur durch\n seine Darstellung als Tauschwerth.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"1.72"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0073","text":"Der Ausdruck des Leinwandwerths im Rocke prägt dem Rocke\n selbst eine neue Form auf. In der That, was besagt die Werthform\n der Leinwand? Dass der Rock mit ihr austauschbar ist. Wie er geht\n oder liegt, mit Haut und Haaren, in seiner Naturalform Rock be-\n sitzt er jetzt die Form unmittelbarer Austauschbarkeit mit\n andrer Waare, die Form eines austauschbaren Gebrauchswerths oder\nAequivalents. Die Bestimmung des Aequivalents enthält nicht nur,\n dass eine Waare Werth überhaupt ist, sondern dass sie in ihrer ding-\n lichen Gestalt, in ihrer Gebrauchsform, andrer Waare als Werth\n gilt und daher unmittelbar als Tauschwerth für die andre Waare\n da ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"1.73"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0074","text":"Als Werth besteht die Leinwand nur aus Arbeit, bildet eine durch-\n sichtig krystallisirte Arbeitsgallerte. In der Wirklichkeit ist dieser Kry-\n stall jedoch sehr trüb. Soweit Arbeit in ihm zu entdecken, und nicht\n jeder Waarenkörper zeigt die Spur der Arbeit, ist es nicht unterschiedslose\n menschliche Arbeit, sondern Weberei, Spinnerei u. s. w., die auch keines-\n wegs seine einzige Substanz bilden, vielmehr mit Naturstoffen verquickt\n sind. Um Leinwand als bloss dinglichen Ausdruck menschlicher Arbeit\n festzuhalten, muss man von allem absehn, was sie wirklich zum Ding\n macht. Gegenständlichkeit der menschlichen Arbeit, die selbst abstrakt\n ist, ohne weitere Qualität und Inhalt, ist nothwendig abstrakte Gegen-\n ständlichkeit, ein Gedankending. So wird das Flachsgewebe zum\n Hirngespinnst. Aber Waaren sind Sachen.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"1.74"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0075","text":"Was sie sind, müssen\n sie sachlich sein oder in ihren eignen sachlichen Beziehungen zeigen. In\n der Produktion der Leinwand ist ein bestimmtes Quantum menschlicher\n Arbeitskraft verausgabt worden. Ihr Werth ist der bloss gegenständ-\n liche Reflex der so verausgabten Arbeit, aber er reflektirt sich nicht\n in ihrem Körper. Er offenbart sich, erhält sinnlichen Ausdruck\n durch ihr Werthverhältniss zum Rock. Indem sie ihn als\n Werth sich gleichsetzt, während sie sich zugleich als Ge-\n brauchsgegenstand von ihm unterscheidet, wird der Rock\n die Erscheinungsform des Leinwand-Werths im Gegensatz zum\n [Running matter: I. 2] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"18\", \"facs\": \"#f0037\"} --> Leinwand-Körper, ihre Werthform im Unterschied von ihrer Na-\n turalform [Note: Man spricht desshalb vom Rockwerth der Leinwand, wenn man ihren\n Werth in Röcken, von ihrem Kornwerth, wenn man ihn in Korn darstellt\n u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"1.75"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0076","text":"Jeder solcher Ausdruck besagt, dass es ihr Werth ist, der in den Ge-\n brauchswerthen Rock, Korn u. s. w. erscheint.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"1.76"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0077","text":"In dem relativen Werthausdruck: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock\n oder x Leinwand ist y Rockwerth, gilt der Rock zwar nur als Werth\n oder Arbeitsgallerte, aber eben dadurch gilt die Arbeitsgallerte als Rock,\n der Rock als die Form, worin menschliche Arbeit gerinnt [Note: In gewisser Art gehts dem Menschen wie der Waare. Da er weder mit\n einem Spiegel auf die Welt kommt, noch als Fichtescher Philosoph: Ich bin Ich,\n bespiegelt sich der Mensch zuerst nur in einem andern Menschen. Erst durch die\n Beziehung auf den Menschen Paul als seinesgleichen, bezieht sich der Mensch\n Peter auf sich selbst als Mensch. Damit gilt ihm aber auch der Paul mit Haut\n und Haaren, in seiner paulinischen Leiblichkeit, als Erscheinungsform des genus\n Mensch.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"1.77"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0078","text":"Der Ge-\n brauchswerth Rock wird nur zur Erscheinungsform des Leinwand-Werths,\n weil sich die Leinwand auf das Rockmaterial als unmittelbare\n Materiatur abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit bezieht, also\n Arbeit gleicher Art wie die in ihr selbst vergegenständlichte. Der\n Gegenstand Rock gilt ihr als sinnlich handgreifliche Gegenständlichkeit\n gleichartiger menschlicher Arbeit, daher als Werth in Naturalform. Da\n sie als Werth gleichen Wesens mit dem Rock ist, wird die Naturalform\n Rock so zur Erscheinungsform ihres eignen Werths. Aber die im Ge-\n brauchswerth Rock dargestellte Arbeit ist nicht menschliche Arbeit\n schlechthin, sondern eine bestimmte, nützliche Arbeit, Schneiderarbeit.\n Menschliche Arbeit schlechthin, Verausgabung menschlicher Arbeitskraft,\n ist zwar jeder Bestimmung fähig, aber an und für sich unbestimmt.","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"1.78"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0079","text":"Ver-\n wirklichen, vergegenständlichen kann sie sich nur, sobald die menschliche\n Arbeitskraft in bestimmter Form verausgabt wird, als bestimmte\n Arbeit, denn nur der bestimmten Arbeit steht ein Naturstoff gegen-\n über, ein äusseres Material, worin sie sich vergegenständlicht. Bloss der\n Hegel’sche „Begriff“ bringt es fertig, sich ohne äussern Stoff zu objek-\n tiviren [Note: „Der Begriff, welcher zunächst nur subjektiv ist, schreitet, ohne dass es] .","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"1.79"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0080","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"19\", \"facs\": \"#f0038\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"1.80"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0081","text":"Die Leinwand kann sich nicht auf den Rock als Werth oder incar-\n nirte menschliche Arbeit beziehn, ohne sich auf Schneider-\n arbeit als die unmittelbare Verwirklichungsform mensch-\n licher Arbeit zu beziehen. Was jedoch die Leinwand am Gebrauchs-\n werth Rock interessirt, ist weder seine wollne Behäbigkeit, noch sein zu-\n geknöpftes Wesen, noch irgend eine andre nützliche Qualität, die ihn zum\n Gebrauchswerth stempelt. Er dient ihr nur dazu, ihre Werthgegenständ-\n lichkeit im Unterschied von ihrer steifleinenen Gebrauchsgegenständlichkeit\n darzustellen. Sie hätte denselben Zweck erreicht, wenn sie ihren Werth\n in Assa Fötida oder Poudrette oder Stiefelwichse ausgedrückt.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"1.81"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0082","text":"Die\nSchneiderarbeit gilt ihr daher ebenfalls nicht, sofern sie zweckmäs-\n sig produktive Thätigkeit, nützliche Arbeit, sondern nur sofern sie als\nbestimmte Arbeit Verwirklichungsform, Vergegenständ-\n lichungsweise menschlicher Arbeit überhaupt ist. Drückte\n die Leinwand ihren Werth statt im Rock in Stiefelwichse aus, so gälte ihr\n auch statt Schneidern Wichsen als die unmittelbare Verwirklichungsform\n abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit [Note: Sofern man nämlich populär die Bereitung der Wichse selbst Wichsen\n heisst.] . Erscheinungsform des Werths oder\n Aequivalent wird ein Gebrauchswerth oder Waarenkörper also nur da-\n durch, dass sich eine andere Waare auf die in ihm enthaltne konkrete,\n nützliche Arbeitsart als die unmittelbare Verwirklichungsform abstrakter\n menschlicher Arbeit bezieht.","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"1.82"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0083","text":"Wir stehn hier bei dem Springpunkt aller Schwierigkeiten, welche\n das Verständniss der Werthform hindern. Es ist relativ leicht, den\n Werth der Waare von ihrem Gebrauchswerth zu unterscheiden, oder die\n den Gebrauchswerth formende Arbeit von derselben Arbeit, so weit sie\n bloss als Verausgabung mensehlicher Arbeitskraft im Waarenwerth berech-\n net wird. Betrachtet man Waare oder Arbeit in der einen Form, so\n nicht in der andern und vice versa. Diese abstrakten Gegensätze fallen\n von selbst auseinander und sind daher leicht auseinander zu halten. An-\n [Note: dazu eines äusseren Materials oder Stoffs bedarf, seiner eignen Thätigkeit gemäss\n dazu fort, sich zu objektiviren.“ Hegel, „Logik“ p. 367 in der „Encyklo-\n pädie: Erster Theil.","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"1.83"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0084","text":"Berlin 1840.“] \n [Running matter: 2*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"20\", \"facs\": \"#f0039\"} --> ders mit der Werthform, die nur im Verhältniss von Waare zu Waare\n existirt. Der Gebrauchswerth oder Waarenkörper spielt hier eine neue\n Rolle. Er wird zur Erscheinungsform des Waarenwerths, also seines\n eignen Gegentheils. Ebenso wird die im Gebrauchswerth enthaltene kon-\n krete nützliche Arbeit zu ihrem eignen Gegentheil, zur blossen Verwirk-\n lichungsform abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit. Statt auseinanderzufal-\n len, reflektiren sich die gegensätzlichen Bestimmungen der Waare hier in\n einander. So befremdlich diess auf ersten Blick, erweist es sich bei wei-\n terem Nachdenken als nothwendig. Die Waare ist von Haus aus ein\nzwieschlächtig Ding, Gebrauchswerth und Werth, Produkt nütz-\n licher Arbeit und abstrakte Arbeitsgallerte.","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"1.84"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0085","text":"Um sich darzustellen als\n das was sie ist, muss sie daher ihre Form verdoppeln. Die Form\n eines Gebrauchswerths besitzt sie von Natur. Es ist ihre Naturalform.\n Werthform erwirbt sie erst im Umgang mit andren Waaren. Aber ihre\n Werthform muss selbst wieder gegenständliche Form sein. Die ein-\n zigen gegenständlichen Formen der Waaren sind ihre Gebrauchsgestalten,\n ihre Naturalformen. Da nun die Naturalform einer Waare, der Leinwand\n z. B., das grade Gegentheil ihrer Werthform ist, muss sie eine andre\n Naturalform, die Naturalform einer andern Waare zu ihrer\nWerthform machen. Was sie nicht unmittelbar für sich selbst, kann\n sie unmittelbar für andre Waare und daher auf einem Umweg für sich\n selbst thun.","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"1.85"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0086","text":"Sie kann ihren Werth nicht in ihrem eignen Körper oder in\n ihrem eignen Gebrauchswerth ausdrücken, aber sie kann sich auf einen\n andern Gebrauchswerth oder Waarenkörper als unmittelbares Werthdasein\n beziehn. Sie kann sich nicht zu der in ihr selbst, wohl aber zu der in\n andrer Waarenart enthaltenen konkreten Arbeit als blosser Verwirklichungs-\n form abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit verhalten. Sie braucht dazu nur die\n andre Waare sich als Aequivalent gleichzusetzen. Der Gebrauchs-\n werth einer Waare existirt überhaupt nur für eine andre Waare, soweit er\n in dieser Weise zur Erscheinungsform ihres Werths dient.","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"1.86"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0087","text":"Betrachtet\n man in dem einfachen relativen Werthausdrucke: x Waare A = y Waare\n B nur das quantitative Verhältniss, so findet man auch nur die oben\n entwickelten Gesetze über die Bewegung des relativen Werths, die alle\n darauf beruhn, dass die Werthgrösse der Waaren durch die zu ihrer Pro-\n duktion nothwendige Arbeitszeit bestimmt ist. Betrachtet man aber das\n Werthverhältniss der beiden Waaren nach seiner qualitativen Seite,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"21\", \"facs\": \"#f0040\"} --> so entdeckt man in jenem einfachen Werthausdruck das Geheimniss der\n Werthform und daher, in nuce, des Geldes [Note: Es ist kaum verwunderlich, dass die Oekonomen, ganz unter dem Einfluss\n stofflicher Interessen, den Formgehalt des relativen Werthausdrucks übersehn\n haben, wenn vor Hegel die Logiker von Profession sogar den Forminhalt der\n Urtheils- und Schlussparadigmen übersahen.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"1.87"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0088","text":"Unsre Analyse hat gezeigt, dass der relative Werthausdruck\n einer Waare zwei verschiedne Werthformen einschliesst.\n Die Leinwand drückt ihren Werth und ihre bestimmte Werthgrösse\n im Rock aus. Sie stellt ihren Werth dar im Werthverhältniss zu\n einer andern Waare, daher als Tauschwerth. Andrerseits die andre\n Waare, der Rock, worin sie ihren Werth relativ ausdrückt, erhält eben\n dadurch die Form eines mit ihr unmittelbar austauschbaren Gebrauchs-\n werths oder Aequivalents. Beide Formen, relative Werthform\n der einen Waare, Aequivalentform der andern, sind Formen des\nTauschwerths. Beide sind in der That nur Momente, wechsel-\n seitig durcheinander bedingte Bestimmungen, desselben relativen\n Werthausdrucks, aber polarisch vertheilt auf die zwei gleichgesetzten\nWaarenextreme.","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"1.88"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0089","text":"Quantitative Bestimmtheit ist nicht in der Aequivalentform\n einer Waare eingeschlossen. Das bestimmte Verhältniss z. B., worin\n Rock Aequivalent von Leinwand ist, entspringt nicht aus seiner Aequiva-\n lentform, der Form seiner unmittelbaren Austauschbarkeit\n mit der Leinwand, sondern aus der Bestimmung der Werthgrösse durch\n Arbeitszeit. Die Leinwand kann ihren eignen Werth nur in Röcken dar-\n stellen, indem sie sich auf ein bestimmtes Rockquantum als gegebenes\n Quantum krystallisirter menschlicher Arbeit bezieht. Aendert sich der\n Rockwerth, so ändert sich auch diese Beziehung. Damit sich aber der\n relative Werth der Leinwand ändere, muss er vorhanden sein, und er kann\n nur gebildet werden bei gegebenem Rockwerth.","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"1.89"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0090","text":"Ob die Leinwand ihren\n eignen Werth nun in 1, 2 oder x Röcken darstellt, hängt unter dieser\n Voraussetzung ganz von der Werthgrösse einer Elle Leinwand und der\n Ellenanzahl ab, deren Werth in Rockform dargestellt werden soll. Die\nWerthgrösse einer Waare kann sich nur im Gebrauchswerth einer\n andern Waare ausdrücken, als relativer Werth. Die Form eines\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"22\", \"facs\": \"#f0041\"} --> unmittelbar austauschbaren Gebrauchswerths oder Aequivalents erhält\n eine Waare dagegen umgekehrt nur als das Material, worin der Werth\n einer andern Waare ausgedrückt wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"1.90"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0091","text":"Diese Unterscheidung ist getrübt durch eine charakteristische Eigen-\n thümlichkeit des relativen Werthausdrucks in seiner einfachen oder ersten\n Form. Die Gleichung: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock, oder\n 20 Ellen Leinwand sind einen Rock werth, schliesst nämlich offenbar die\n identische Gleichung ein: 1 Rock = 20 Ellen Leinwand, oder\n 1 Rock ist 20 Ellen Leinwand werth. Der relative Werthausdruck der\n Leinwand, worin der Rock als Aequivalent figurirt, enthält also rück-\n bezüglich den relativen Werthausdruck des Rocks, worin die Leinwand\n als Aequivalent figurirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"1.91"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0092","text":"Obgleich beide Bestimmungen der Werthform oder beide Dar-\n stellungsweisen des Waarenwerths als Tauschwerth nur relativ\n sind, scheinen beide nicht in demselben Grad relativ. Im relativen\n Werth der Leinwand: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock, ist der Tausch-\n werth der Leinwand ausdrücklich als ihre Beziehung auf eine andre\n Waare dargestellt. Der Rock seinerseits ist zwar auch nur Aequiva-\n lent, so weit sich die Leinwand auf ihn als Erscheinungsform ihres eig-\n nen Werths und daher mit ihr unmittelbar Austauschbares bezieht. Nur\ninnerhalb dieser Beziehung ist er Aequivalent. Aber er verhält sich\n passiv. Er ergreift keine Initiative. Er findet sich in Beziehung, weil\n sich auf ihn bezogen wird. Der Charakter, der ihm aus dem Verhältniss\n mit der Leinwand erwächst, erscheint daher nicht als Resultat seiner Be-\n ziehung, sondern ohne sein Zuthun vorhanden. Noch mehr.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"1.92"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0093","text":"Die\nbestimmte Art und Weise, wie sich die Leinwand auf ihn bezieht,\n ist ganz dazu gemacht, es ihm „anzuthun“, wäre er auch noch so beschei-\n den und keineswegs das Produkt eines „tailor run mad with pride“. Die\n Leinwand bezieht sich nämlich auf den Rock als sinnlich existirende Ma-\n teriatur der menschlichen Arbeit in abstracto und daher als vorhandnen\n Werthkörper. Er ist diess nur, weil und sofern sich die Leinwand in\n dieser bestimmten Weise auf ihn bezieht. Sein Aequivalentsein\n ist so zu sagen nur eine Reflexionsbestimmung der Leinwand.\n Aber es scheint grade umgekehrt. Einerseits giebt er sich selbst nicht\n die Mühe sich zu beziehn. Andrerseits bezieht sich die Leinwand auf ihn,\n nicht um ihn zu etwas zu machen, sondern weil er ohne sie etwas ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"1.93"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0094","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"23\", \"facs\": \"#f0042\"} --> Das fertige Produkt der Beziehung der Leinwand auf den Rock, seine\n Aequivalentform, seine Bestimmtheit als unmittelbar austauschbarer Ge-\n brauchswerth, scheint ihm daher auch ausserhalb der Beziehung zur\n Leinwand dinglich anzugehören, ganz wie etwa seine Eigenschaft\n warm zu halten. In der ersten oder einfachen Form des relativen\n Werths: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock, ist dieser falsche Schein noch\n nicht befestigt, weil sie unmittelbar auch das Gegentheil aussagt,\n dass der Rock Aequivalent der Leinwand und dass jede der beiden Waa-\n ren diese Bestimmtheit nur besitzt, weil und sofern die andre sie zu ihrem\n relativen Werthausdruck macht [Note: Es ist mit solchen Reflexionsbestimmungen überhaupt ein eignes Ding.\n Dieser Mensch ist z. B. nur König, weil sich andre Menschen als Unterthanen zu\n ihm verhalten.","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"1.94"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0095","text":"Sie glauben umgekehrt Unterthanen zu sein, weil er König ist.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"1.95"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0096","text":"In der einfachen Form des relativen Werths oder dem Ausdrucke der\n Aequivalenz zweier Waaren, ist die Form entwicklung des Werths für\n beide Waaren gleichmässig, obgleich jedesmal in entgegenge-\n setzter Richtung. Der relative Werthausdruck ist ferner mit\n Bezug auf jede der beiden Waaren einheitlich, denn die Leinwand\n stellt ihren Werth nur in einer Waare dar, dem Rocke und vice versa,\n aber für beide Waaren ist dieser Werthausdruck doppelt, verschieden\n für jede derselben. Endlich ist jede der beiden Waaren nur Aequiva-\n lent für die andre einzelne Waarenart. also nur einzelnes Aequi-\n valent.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"1.96"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0097","text":"Solche Gleichung, wie 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock, oder\n zwanzig Ellen Leinwand sind einen Rock werth, drückt offenbar den\n Werth der Waare nur ganz beschränkt und einseitig aus. Vergleiche ich\n die Leinwand z. B., statt mit Röcken, mit andern Waaren, so erhalte ich\n auch andre relative Werthausdrücke, andre Gleichungen,\n wie 20 Ellen Leinwand = u Kaffee, 20 Ellen Leinwand = v Thee\n u. s. w. Die Leinwand hat eben so viele verschiedne relative\n Werthausdrücke, als es von ihr verschiedne Waaren giebt und die\n Zahl ihrer relativen Werthausdrücke wächst beständig mit der Zahl neu\n auftretender Waarenarten [Note: „The value of any commodity denoting its relation in exchange, we may\n speak of it as .... cornvalue, clothvalue, according to the commodity with which] .","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"1.97"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0098","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"24\", \"facs\": \"#f0043\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"1.98"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0099","text":"Die erste Form 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock gab zwei\n relative Ausdrücke für den Werth zweier Waaren. Diese zweite\n Form giebt für den Werth derselben Waare die bunteste Mosaik\n relativer Ausdrücke. Auch scheint weder für den Ausdruck der Werth-\ngrösse irgend etwas gewonnen, denn in 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock\n ist die Werthgrösse der Leinwand, die ja in jedem Ausdrucke dieselbe\n bleibt, eben so erschöpfend dargestellt als in 20 Ellen Leinwand = u Thee\n u. s. w., noch für die Formbestimmung des Aequivalents, denn in\n 20 Ellen Leinwand = u Kaffee u. s. w., sind Kaffee u. s. w. nur ein-\n zelne Aequivalente, ganz wie es der Rock war.","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"1.99"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0100","text":"Dennoch birgt diese zweite Form eine wesentliche Fortentwicklung.\n Es liegt darin nämlich nicht nur, dass die Leinwand ihren Werth zufällig\n bald in Rücken ausdrückt, bald in Kaffee u. s. w., sondern dass sie ihn\nsowohl in Röcken als in Kaffee u. s. w. ausdrückt, entweder in\ndieser Waare oder jener oder der dritten u. s. w. Die Weiterbestim-\n mung zeigt sich, sobald diese zweite oder entfaltete Form des rela-\n tiven Werthausdrucks in ihrem Zusammenhang dargestellt\n wird. Wir erhalten dann:","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"1.100"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0101","text":"II. Zweite oder entfaltete Form des relativen Werths:","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"1.101"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0102","text":"20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock oder = u Kaffee oder = v Thee oder\n = x Eisen oder = y Weizen oder = u. s. w. u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"1.102"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0103","text":"z Waare A = u Waare B oder = v Waare C oder = w Waare D\noder = x Waare E oder = y Waare F oder = u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"1.103"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0104","text":"Zunächst bildet offenbar die erste Form das Grundelement der\n [Note: it is compared; and then there are a thousand different kinds of value,\n as many kinds of value as there are commodities in existence, and all are equally\n real and equally nominal.“ („A Critical Dissertation on the Nature,\n Measure and Causes of Value: chiefly in reference to the writings\n of Mr. Ricardo and his followers. By the Author of Essays on the\n Formation etc. of Opinions. London 1825“, p. 39). S. Bailey, der\n Verfasser dieser anonymen Schrift, die ihrer Zeit viel Lärm in England machte,\n bildet sich ein durch diesen Hinweis auf die kunterbunten relativen Ausdrücke\n desselben Waaren-Werths alle Begriffsbestimmung des Werths vernichtet zu\n haben.","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"1.104"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0105","text":"Dass er übrigens, trotz eigner Bornirtheit, wunde Flecken der Ricardo’-\n schen Theorie sondirt hat, bewies die Gereiztheit, womit die Ricardo’sche Schule\n ihn angriff, z. B. in der Westminster Review.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"25\", \"facs\": \"#f0044\"} --> zweiten, denn letztere besteht aus vielen einfachen relativen Werthaus-\n drücken, wie 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock, 20 Ellen Leinwand =\n u Kaffee u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"1.105"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0106","text":"In der ersten Form: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock kann es\n zufällige Thatsache scheinen, dass diese zwei Waaren in diesem bestimm-\n ten quantitativen Verhältnisse austauschbar sind. In der\n zweiten Form leuchtet dagegen sofort ein von der zufälligen Erscheinung\n wesentlich unterschiedner und sie bestimmender Hintergrund durch. Der\n Werth der Leinwand bleibt gleich gross, ob in Rock oder Kaffee oder\n Eisen u. s. w. dargestellt, in zahllos verschiednen Waaren, den verschie-\n densten Besitzern angehörig. Das zufällige Verhältniss zweier individuel-\n ler Waarenbesitzer fällt fort. Es wird offenbar, dass nicht der Austausch\n die Werthgrösse der Waare, sondern umgekehrt die Werthgrösse der\n Waare ihre Austauschverhältnisse regulirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"1.106"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0107","text":"In dem Ausdruck: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock galt der\n Rock als Erscheinungsform der in der Leinwand vergegenständlichten\n Arbeit. So wurde die in der Leinwand enthaltene Arbeit der im Rock\n enthaltnen gleichgesetzt und daher als gleichartige menschliche Arbeit\n bestimmt. Indess trat diese Bestimmung nicht ausdrücklich hervor.\n Unmittelbar setzt die erste Form die in der Leinwand enthaltne Arbeit\n nur der Schneiderarbeit gleich. Anders die zweite Form. In der end-\n losen, stets verlängerbaren Reihe ihrer relativen Werthausdrücke bezieht\n sich die Leinwand auf alle möglichen Waarenkörper als blosse Erschei-\n nungsformen der in ihr selbst enthaltenen Arbeit. Hier ist der Leinwand-\nWerth daher erst wahrhaft dargestellt als Werth, d. h. Krystall\n menschlicher Arbeit überhaupt.","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"1.107"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0108","text":"Die zweite Form besteht aus einer Summe von lauter Gleichungen\n der ersten Form. Jede dieser Gleichungen, wie 20 Ellen Leinwand\n = 1 Rock schliesst aber auch die Rückbeziehung ein: 1 Rock = 20 El-\n len Leinwand, wo der Rock seinen Werth in der Leinwand und\n eben dadurch die Leinwand als Aequivalent darstellt. Da diess nun von\n jedem der zahllosen relativen Werthausdrücke der Leinwand gilt,\n erhalten wir:","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"1.108"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0109","text":"III. Dritte, umgekehrte oder rückbezogene zweite\n Form des relativen Werths:","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"1.109"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0110","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"26\", \"facs\": \"#f0045\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"1.110"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0111","text":"1 Rock = 20 Ellen Leinwand.","kind":"passage","sequence":111,"address":"1.111"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0112","text":"u Kaffee = 20 Ellen Leinwand.","kind":"passage","sequence":112,"address":"1.112"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0113","text":"v Thee = 20 Ellen Leinwand.","kind":"passage","sequence":113,"address":"1.113"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0114","text":"x Eisen = 20 Ellen Leinwand.","kind":"passage","sequence":114,"address":"1.114"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0115","text":"y Weizen = 20 Ellen Leinwand.","kind":"passage","sequence":115,"address":"1.115"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0116","text":"u. s. w. = 20 Ellen Leinwand.","kind":"passage","sequence":116,"address":"1.116"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0117","text":"Der relative Werthausdruck der Waaren kehrt hier zurück\n in seiner ursprünglichen Gestalt: 1 Rock = 20 Ellen Leinwand. Jedoch\n ist diese einfache Gleichung jetzt weiter entwickelt. Ursprünglich ent-\n hielt sie nur, dass der Rockwerth durch seinen Ausdruck in einer an-\n dern Waare eine vom Gebrauchswerth Rock oder dem Rock-\n körper selbst unterschiedne und unabhängige Form er-\n hält. Jetzt stellt dieselbe Form den Rock auch allen andern Waaren\n gegenüber als Werth dar und ist daher seine allgemein gültige Werth-\n form. Nicht nur der Rock, sondern Kaffee, Eisen, Weizen, kurz alle\n andern Waaren drücken ihren Werth jetzt im Material Leinwand\n aus. Alle stellen sich so einander als dieselbe Materiatur\n menschlicher Arbeit dar. Sie sind nur noch quantitativ ver-\n schieden, wesswegen 1 Rock, u Kaffee, x Eisen u. s. w., d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":117,"address":"1.117"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0118","text":"ver-\n schiedne Quanta dieser verschiednen Dinge = 20 Ellen Leinwand,\n gleich demselben Quantum vergegenständlichter menschlicher Arbeit.\n Durch ihren gemeinschaftlichen Werthausdruck im Material Leinwand\nunterscheiden sich also alle Waaren als Tauschwerthe von ihren\n eignen Gebrauchswerthen und beziehn sich zugleich auf einander\n als Werthgrössen, setzen sich qualitativ gleich und verglei-\n chen sich quantitativ. Erst in diesem einheitlichen relativen\n Werthausdruck erscheinen sie alle für einander als Werthe und erhält\n ihr Werth daher erst seine entsprechende Erscheinungsform als\n Tauschwerth. Im Unterschied zur entfalteten Form des rela-\n tiven Werths (Form II), die den Werth einer Waare im Umkreis aller\n andern Waaren darstellt, nennen wir diesen einheitlichen Werth-\n ausdruck die allgemeine relative Werthform.","kind":"passage","sequence":118,"address":"1.118"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0119","text":"In der Form II: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock oder = u Kaffee\noder = v Thee oder = x Eisen u. s. w., worin die Leinwand ihren\n relativen Werthausdruck entfaltet, bezieht sie sich auf jede einzelne\n Waare, Rock, Kaffee u. s. w. als ein besondres Aequivalent und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"27\", \"facs\": \"#f0046\"} --> auf alle zusammen als den Umkreis ihrer besondern Aequivalent-\n formen. Ihr gegenüber gilt keine einzelne Waarenart noch als Aequiva-\n lent schlechthin, wie im einzelnen Aequivalent, sondern nur als be-\n sondres Aequivalent, wovon das eine das andre ausschliesst. In der\n Form III, welche die rückbezogene zweite Form und also in ihr einge-\n schlossen ist, erscheint die Leinwand dagegen als die Gattungsform\n des Aequivalents für alle andern Waaren.","kind":"passage","sequence":119,"address":"1.119"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0120","text":"Es ist als ob neben und ausser\n Löwen, Tigern, Hasen und allen andern wirklichen Thieren, die gruppirt\n die verschiednen Geschlechter, Arten, Unterarten, Familien u. s. w. des\n Thierreichs bilden, auch noch das Thier existirte, die individuelle Incar-\n nation des ganzen Thierreichs. Ein solches Einzelne, das in sich selbst alle\n wirklich vorhandenen Arten derselben Sache einbegreift, ist ein Allgemei-\n nes, wie Thier, Gott u. s. w. Wie die Leinwand daher einzelnes\n Aequivalent wurde, dadurch dass sich eine andre Waare auf sie als\n Erscheinungsform des Werths bezog, so wird sie als allen Waaren gemein-\n schaftliche Erscheinungsform des Werths das allgemeine Aequiva-\n lent, allgemeiner Werthleib, allgemeine Materiatur der\n abstrakten menschlichen Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":120,"address":"1.120"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0121","text":"Die in ihr materialisirte be-\n sondre Arbeit gilt daher jetzt als allgemeine Verwirklichungs-\n form der menschlichen Arbeit, als allgemeine Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":121,"address":"1.121"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0122","text":"Bei der Darstellung des Werths der Waare A in der Waare B, wo-\n durch die Waare B einzelnes Aequivalent wird, war es gleichgül-\n tig, von welcher besondern Sorte die Waare B. Nur musste die Kör-\n perlichkeit der Waare B andrer Art sein als die der Waare A, daher\n auch Produkt andrer nützlicher Arbeit. Indem der Rock seinen\n Werth in Leinwand darstellte, bezog er sich auf Leinwand als die ver-\n wirklichte menschliche Arbeit, und eben dadurch auf Leine-\n weberei als die Verwirklichungsform der menschlichen\n Arbeit, aber die besondre Bestimmtheit, welche Leineweberei von\n andern Arbeitsarten unterscheidet, war durchaus gleichgültig. Sie\n musste nur andrer Art sein als die Schneiderarbeit und im übrigen eine\nbestimmte Arbeitsart. Anders sobald die Leinwand allgemeines\n Aequivalent wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":122,"address":"1.122"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0123","text":"Dieser Gebrauchswerth in seiner besondern\n Bestimmtheit, wodurch er Leinwand im Unterschied von allen andern\n Waarenarten, Kaffee, Eisen u. s. w., wird jetzt die allgemeine Werthform\n aller andern Waaren und daher allgemeines Aequivalent. Die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"28\", \"facs\": \"#f0047\"} --> in ihm dargestellte besondre nützliche Arbeitsart gilt daher jetzt als\nallgemeine Verwirklichungsform der menschlichen Ar-\n beit, als allgemeine Arbeit, grade soweit sie Arbeit von besondrer\n Bestimmtheit ist, Leineweberei im Unterschied nicht nur von Schnei-\n derarbeit, sondern von Kaffeebau, Minenarbeit und allen andern Arbeits-\narten. Umgekehrt gelten alle andren Arbeitsarten, im relativen\n Werthausdruck der Leinwand, des allgemeinen Aequivalents (Form\n II), nur noch als besondre Verwirklichungsformen der mensch-\n lichen Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":123,"address":"1.123"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0124","text":"Als Werthe sind die Waaren Ausdrücke derselben Einheit,\n der abstrakten menschlichen Arbeit. In der Form des Tauschwerths\n erscheinen sie einander als Werthe und beziehn sich auf einander\nals Werthe. Sie beziehn sich damit zugleich auf die abstrakte mensch-\n liche Arbeit als ihre gemeinsame gesellschaftliche Substanz.\n Ihr gesellschaftliches Verhältniss besteht ausschliesslich darin ein-\n ander als nur quantitativ verschiedne, aber qualitativ gleiche und daher\n durch einander ersetzbare und mit einander vertauschbare Ausdrücke dieser\n ihrer gesellschaftlichen Substanz zu gelten. Als nützliches Ding besitzt\n eine Waare gesellschaftliche Bestimmtheit, soweit sie Gebrauchswerth für\n andre ausser ihrem Besitzer ist, also gesellschaftliche Bedürfnisse befrie-\n digt.","kind":"passage","sequence":124,"address":"1.124"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0125","text":"Aber gleichgültig, auf wessen Bedürfnisse ihre nützlichen Eigen-\n schaften sie beziehn, sie wird durch dieselben immer nur auf mensch-\n liche Bedürfnisse bezogener Gegenstand, nicht Waare für\nandre Waaren. Nur was blosse Gebrauchsgegenstände in Waaren\n verwandelt, kann sie als Waaren auf einander beziehn und daher in\ngesellschaftlichen Rapport setzen. Es ist diess aber ihr Werth.\n Die Form, worin sie sich als Werthe, als menschliche Arbeitsgallerte\ngelten, ist daher ihre gesellschaftliche Form. Gesellschaft-\n liche Form der Waare und Werthform oder Form der Aus-\n tauschbarkeit sind also eins und dasselbe. Ist die Naturalform einer\n Waare zugleich Werthform, so besitzt sie die Form unmittelbarer\n Austauschbarkeit mit andern Waaren und daher unmittelbar\n gesellschaftliche Form.","kind":"passage","sequence":125,"address":"1.125"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0126","text":"Die einfache relative Werthform (Form I) 1 Rock =\n 20 Ellen Leinwand unterscheidet sich von der allgemeinen relati-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"29\", \"facs\": \"#f0048\"} --> ven Werthform 1 Rock = 20 Ellen Leinwand nur dadurch, dass\n diese Gleichung jetzt ein Glied der Reihe bildet","kind":"passage","sequence":126,"address":"1.126"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0127","text":"1 Rock = 20 Ellen Leinwand","kind":"passage","sequence":127,"address":"1.127"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0128","text":"u Kaffee = 20 Ellen Leinwand","kind":"passage","sequence":128,"address":"1.128"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0129","text":"v Thee = 20 Ellen Leinwand","kind":"passage","sequence":129,"address":"1.129"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0130","text":"u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":130,"address":"1.130"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0131","text":"Sie unterscheidet sich also in der That nur dadurch, dass die Leinwand\n aus einem einzelnen zum allgemeinen Aequivalent fortent-\n wickelt ist. Wenn also im einfachen relativen Werthausdrucke nicht\n die Waare, die ihre Werthgrösse ausdrückt, sondern die Waare,\nworin Werthgrösse ausgedrückt wird, die Form unmittelbarer\n Austauschbarkeit, Aequivalentform, also unmittelbar gesell-\n schaftliche Form erhält, so gilt dasselbe für den allgemeinen relativen\n Werthausdruck. Aber in der einfachen relativen Werthform ist dieser\n Unterschied nur noch formell und verschwindend.","kind":"passage","sequence":131,"address":"1.131"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0132","text":"Wenn in 1 Rock =\n 20 Ellen Leinwand der Rock seinen Werth relativ, nämlich in Leinwand\n ausdrückt und die Leinwand dadurch Aequivalentform erhält, so schliesst\n dieselbe Gleichung unmittelbar die Rückbeziehung ein: 20 Ellen Lein-\n wand = 1 Rock, worin der Rock die Aequivalentform erhält und der\n Werth der Leinwand relativ ausgedrückt wird. Diese gleichmässige und\n gegenseitige Entwicklung der Werthform beider Waaren als relativer\n Werth und als Aequivalent findet jetzt nicht länger statt. Wird die all-\n gemeine relative Werthform 1 Rock = 20 Ellen Leinwand, wo die Leinwand\nallgemeines Aequivalent, umgekehrt in 20 Ellen Leinwand =\n 1 Rock, so wird der Rock dadurch nicht allgemeines Aequivalent für alle\n andern Waaren, sondern nur ein besondres Aequivalent der Leinwand.\nAllgemein ist die relative Werthform des Rocks nur, weil sie zugleich\n die relative Werthform aller andern Waaren.","kind":"passage","sequence":132,"address":"1.132"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0133","text":"Was vom Rock, gilt vom\n Kaffee u. s. w. Es folgt daher, dass die allgemeine relative Werthform\n der Waaren sie selbst von der allgemeinen Aequivalentform ausschliesst.\n Umgekehrt ist eine Waare, wie Leinwand, sobald sie die allgemeine\n Aequivalentform besitzt, von der allgemeinen relativen Werthform ausge-\n schlossen. Die allgemeine, mit den andern Waaren einheitliche relativc\n Werthform der Leinwand wäre: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 20 Ellen Lein-\n wand. Diess ist aber eine Tautologie, welche die Werthgrösse dieser\n in allgemeiner Aequivalentform und daher in stets austauschbarer Form\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"30\", \"facs\": \"#f0049\"} --> befindlichen Waare nicht ausdrückt. Vielmehr wird die entfaltete\n relative Werthform: 20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock oder = u Kaffee\noder = v Thee oder = u. s. w. jetzt zum specifischen relativen\n Werthausdrucke des allgemeinen Aequivalents.","kind":"passage","sequence":133,"address":"1.133"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0134","text":"In dem allgemeinen relativen Werthausdruck der Waaren besitzt jede\n Waare, Rock, Kaffee, Thee u. s. w. eine von ihrer Naturalform ver-\n schiedne Werthform, nämlich die Form Leinwand. Und eben in dieser\n Form beziehn sie sich auf einander als Austauschbare und in quantitativ\n bestimmten Verhältnissen Austauschbare, denn wenn 1 Rock = 20 Ellen\n Leinwand, u Kaffee = 20 Ellen Leinwand u. s. w., so ist auch 1 Rock\n = u Kaffee u. s. w. Indem alle Waaren sich in einer und derselben\n Waare als Werthgrössen bespiegeln, wiederspiegeln sie sich wechselseitig\n als Werthgrössen. Aber die Naturalformen, die sie als Gebrauchsgegen-\n stände besitzen, gelten ihnen wechselseitig nur auf diesem Umweg, also\n nicht unmittelbar als Erscheinungsformen des Werths. Sowie sie unmittel-\n bar sind, sind sie daher nicht unmittelbar austauschbar.","kind":"passage","sequence":134,"address":"1.134"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0135","text":"Sie besitzen also\n nicht die Form unmittelbarer Austauschbarkeit für einander\n oder ihre gesellschaftlich gültige Form ist eine vermittelte.\n Umgekehrt. Indem alle andern Waaren auf Leinwand als Erscheinungs-\n form des Werths sich beziehen, wird die Naturalform der Leinwand die\nForm ihrer unmittelbaren Austauschbarkeit mit allen Waa-\n ren, daher unmittelbar ihre allgemein gesellschaftliche\n Form.","kind":"passage","sequence":135,"address":"1.135"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0136","text":"Eine Waare erhält nur die allgemeine Aequivalentform,\n weil und sofern sie allen andern Waaren zur Darstellung ihrer allge-\n meinen relativen, daher nicht unmittelbaren Werthform dient.\n Waaren müssen sich aber relative Werthform überhaupt geben, weil ihre\n Naturalformen nur ihre Gebrauchswerthformen, und sie müssen sich ein-\n heitliche, daher allgemeine relative Werthform geben, um sich alle als\n Werthe, als gleichartige Gallerten menschlicher Arbeit auf einander zu\n beziehen.","kind":"passage","sequence":136,"address":"1.136"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0137","text":"Eine Waare befindet sich daher nur in der Form unmittel-\n barer Austauschbarkeit mit allen andern Waaren und daher in unmittelbar\n gesellschaftlicher Form, weil und sofern alle andern Waaren sich\nnicht darin befinden, oder weil die Waare überhaupt sich von Haus aus\nnicht in unmittelbar austauschbarer oder gesellschaftlicher Form befindet,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"31\", \"facs\": \"#f0050\"} --> indem ihre unmittelbare Form die Form ihres Gebrauchswerths, nicht ihres\n Werthes.","kind":"passage","sequence":137,"address":"1.137"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0138","text":"Man sieht es der Form allgemeiner unmittelbarer Aus-\n tauschbarkeit in der That keineswegs an, dass sie eine gegensätz-\n liche Waarenform ist, von der Form nicht unmittelbarer Austausch-\n barkeit ebenso unzertrennlich, wie die Positivität eines Magnetpols von der\n Negativität des andern. Man kann sich daher einbilden, man könne allen\n Waaren zugleich den Stempel unmittelbarer Austauschbarkeit aufdrücken,\n wie man sich auch einbilden kann, man könne alle Arbeiter zu Kapita-\n listen machen.","kind":"passage","sequence":138,"address":"1.138"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0139","text":"In der That aber sind allgemeine relative Werth-\n form und allgemeine Aequivalentform die gegensätzlichen, sich\n wechselweis voraussetzenden und wechselweis abstossenden Pole dersel-\n ben gesellschaftlichen Form der Waaren [Note: Für den Kleinbürger, der in der Form der Waarenproduktion das nec plus\n ultra menschlicher Freiheit und individueller Unabhängigkeit erblickt, wäre es\n natürlich sehr wünschenswerth, zugleich der mit dieser Form verbundnen Miss-\n stände überhoben zu sein, namentlich auch der nicht unmittelbaren Aus-\n tauschbarkeit der Waaren. Die Ausmalung dieser Philisterutopie bildet Proud-\n hon’s Socialismus, der, wie ich anderswo gezeigt, nicht einmal das Verdienst der\n Originalität besitzt, vielmehr lange vor ihm von Bray, Gray und Andern weit bes-\n ser entwickelt wurde.","kind":"passage","sequence":139,"address":"1.139"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0140","text":"Diess verhindert solche Weisheit nicht, heutzutage unter\n dem Namen der „science“ in Frankreich zu grassiren. Nie hat eine Schule mehr\n als die Proudhon’sche mit dem Wort „science“ um sich geworfen, denn\n„wo Begriffe fehlen,\n Da stellt zur rechten Zeit ein Wort sich ein.“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":140,"address":"1.140"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0141","text":"Als unmittelbar gesellschaftliche Materiatur der\n Arbeit ist die Leinwand, das allgemeine Aequivalent, Materiatur\n unmittelbar gesellschaftlicher Arbeit, während die andern\n Waarenkörper, welche ihren Werth in Leinwand darstellen, Materiaturen\nnicht unmittelbar gesellschaftlicher Arbeiten sind.","kind":"passage","sequence":141,"address":"1.141"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0142","text":"In der That sind alle Gebrauchswerthe nur Waaren, weil Produkte\n von einander unabhängiger Privatarbeiten, Privatarbeiten,\n die jedoch als besondere, wenn auch verselbständigte, Glieder des natur-\n wüchsigen Systems der Theilung der Arbeit stofflich von einander\n abhängen. Sie hängen so gesellschaftlich zusammen grade durch ihre\nVerschiedenheit, ihre besondre Nützlichkeit. Eben dess-\n wegen produciren sie qualitativ verschiedne Gebrauchswerthe. Wenn\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"32\", \"facs\": \"#f0051\"} --> nicht, so würden diese Gebrauchswerthe nicht zu Waaren für einander.\n Andrerseits macht diese verschiedne nützliche Qualität Produkte noch\n nicht zu Waaren. Producirt eine bäuerliche Familie für ihren eignen\n Consum Rock und Leinwand und Weizen, so treten diese Dinge der Fa-\n milie als verschiedne Produkte ihrer Familienarbeit gegenüber, aber nicht\n sich selbst wechselseitig als Waaren.","kind":"passage","sequence":142,"address":"1.142"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143","text":"Wäre die Arbeit unmittelbar\n gesellschaftliche, d. h. gemeinsame Arbeit, so erhielten die Pro-\n dukte den unmittelbar gesellschaftlichen Charakter eines Gemeinprodukts\n für ihre Producenten, aber nicht den Charakter von Waaren für einander.\n Indess haben wir hier nicht weit zu suchen, worin die gesellschaftliche\n Form der in den Waaren enthaltenen und von einander unabhängigen\nPrivatarbeiten besteht. Sie ergab sich bereits aus der Analyse der\n Waare. Ihre gesellschaftliche Form ist ihre Beziehung auf einander als\ngleiche Arbeit, also, da die Gleichheit toto coelo verschiedner\n Arbeiten nur in einer Abstraktion von ihrer Ungleichheit be-\n stehen kann, ihre Beziehung auf einander als menschliche Arbeit\n überhaupt, Verausgabungen menschlicher Arbeitskraft,\n was alle menschlichen Arbeiten, welches immer ihr Inhalt und ihre Opera-\n tionsweise, in der That sind.","kind":"passage","sequence":143,"address":"1.143"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0144","text":"In jeder gesellschaftlichen Arbeitsform\n sind die Arbeiten der verschiednen Individuen auch als menschliche auf\n einander bezogen, aber hier gilt diese Beziehung selbst als die\nspecifisch gesellschaftliche Form der Arbeiten. Nun besitzt\n aber keine dieser Privatarbeiten in ihrer Naturalform diese specifisch ge-\n sellschaftliche Form abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit, so wenig wie die\n Waare in ihrer Naturalform die gesellschaftliche Form blosser Arbeits-\n gallerte, oder des Werthes, besitzt. Dadurch aber dass die Naturalform\n einer Waare, hier der Leinwand, allgemeine Aequivalentform wird, weil\n sich alle andern Waaren auf dieselbe als Erscheinungsform ihres eignen\n Werths beziehn, wird auch die Leinweberei zur allgemeinen Verwirk-\n lichungsform abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit oder zu Arbeit in unmittelbar\n gesellschaftlicher Form.","kind":"passage","sequence":144,"address":"1.144"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0145","text":"Der Massstab der „Gesellschaftlichkeit“ muss\n aus der Natur der jeder Produktionsweise eigenthümlichen Verhältnisse,\n nicht aus ihr fremden Vorstellungen entlehnt werden. Wie vorhin gezeigt\n ward, dass die Waare von Natur die unmittelbare Form allgemeiner Aus-\n tauschbarkeit ausschliesst und die allgemeine Aequivalentform daher nur\ngegensätzlich entwickeln kann, so gilt dasselbe für die in den Waaren\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"33\", \"facs\": \"#f0052\"} --> steckenden Privatarbeiten.","kind":"passage","sequence":145,"address":"1.145"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0146","text":"Da sie nicht unmittelbar gesellschaft-\n liche Arbeit sind, so ist erstens die gesellschaftliche Form eine\n von den Naturalformen der wirklichen nützlichen Arbeiten unterschiedne,\n ihnen fremde, und abstrakte Form, und zweitens erhalten alle Arten Pri-\n vatarbeit ihren gesellschaftlichen Charakter nur gegensätzlich,\n indem sie alle einer ausschliesslichen Art Privatarbeit, hier der Leinewebe-\n rei, gleichgesetzt werden. Dadurch wird letztere die unmittelbare\n und allgemeine Erscheinungsform abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit und so\n Arbeit in unmittelbar gesellschaftlicher Form. Sie stellt sich daher auch\n unmittelbar in einem gesellschaftlich geltenden und allgemein austausch-\n baren Produkt dar.","kind":"passage","sequence":146,"address":"1.146"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0147","text":"Der Schein, als ob die Aequivalentform einer Waare aus ihrer eignen\n dinglichen Natur entspringe, statt blosser Reflex der Beziehungen der an-\n dern Waaren zu sein, befestigt sich mit der Fortbildung des einzelnen\n Aequivalents zum allgemeinen, weil die gegensätzlichen Momente der\n Werthform sich nicht mehr gleichmässig für die auf einander bezog-\n nen Waaren entwickeln, weil die allgemeine Aequivalentform eine Waare\n als etwas ganz apartes von allen andern Waaren scheidet und endlich weil\n diese ihre Form in der That nicht mehr das Produkt der Beziehung irgend\n einer einzelnen andern Waare ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":147,"address":"1.147"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0148","text":"Indess ist auf unserm jetzigen Standpunkt das allgemeine Aequiva-\n lent noch keineswegs verknöchert. Wie wurde in der That die Leinwand\n in das allgemeine Aequivalent verwandelt? Dadurch, dass sie ihren Werth\n erst in einer einzelnen Waare (Form I), dann in allen andern Waaren der\n Reihe nach relativ darstellte (Form II), und so rückbezüglich alle\n andern Waaren in ihr ihre Werthe relativ darstellten (Form III). Der\n einfache relative Werthausdruck war der Keim, woraus sich die allgemeine\n Aequivalentform der Leinwand entwickelte. Innerhalb dieser Entwicklung\n ändert sie die Rolle. Sie beginnt damit, ihre Werthgrösse in einer\n andern Waare darzustellen und endet damit zum Material für den Werth-\n ausdruck aller andern Waaren zu dienen. Was von der Leinwand, gilt\n von jeder Waare.","kind":"passage","sequence":148,"address":"1.148"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0149","text":"In ihrem entfalteten relativen Werthausdrucke (Form\n II), der nur aus ihren vielen, einfachen Werthausdrücken besteht,\n figurirt die Leinwand noch nicht als allgemeines Aequivalent. Vielmehr\n bildet hier jeder andre Waarenkörper ihr Aequivalent, ist daher un-\n mittelbar austauschbar mit ihr und kann also die Stelle mit ihr wechseln.","kind":"passage","sequence":149,"address":"1.149"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0150","text":"[Running matter: I. 3] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"34\", \"facs\": \"#f0053\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":150,"address":"1.150"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0151","text":"Wir erhalten daher schliesslich:","kind":"passage","sequence":151,"address":"1.151"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0152","text":"Form IV:","kind":"passage","sequence":152,"address":"1.152"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0153","text":"20 Ellen Leinwand = 1 Rock oder = u Kaffee oder = v Thee oder\n = x Eisen oder = y Weizen oder = u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":153,"address":"1.153"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0154","text":"1 Rock = 20 Ellen Leinwand oder = u Kaffee oder = v Thee oder\n = x Eisen oder = y Weizen oder = u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":154,"address":"1.154"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0155","text":"u Kaffee = 20 Ellen Leinwand oder = 1 Rock oder = v Thee oder\n = x Eisen oder = y Weizen oder = u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":155,"address":"1.155"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0156","text":"v Thee = u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":156,"address":"1.156"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0157","text":"Aber jede dieser Gleichungen rückbezogen ergiebt Rock, Kaffee,\n Thee u. s. w. als allgemeines Aequivalent, daher den Werthausdruck in\n Rock, Kaffee, Thee u. s. w. als allgemeine relative Werthform aller andern\n Waaren. Die allgemeine Aequivalentform kommt immer nur einer Waare\n zu im Gegensatz zu allen andern Waaren; aber sie kommt jeder Waare\n im Gegensatz zu allen andern zu. Stellt aber jede Waare ihre eigne Na-\n turalform allen andern Waaren gegenüber als allgemeine Aequivalentform,\n so schliessen alle Waaren alle von der allgemeinen Aequivalentform aus\n und daher sich selbst von der gesellschaftlich gültigen Darstellung ihrer\n Werthgrössen.","kind":"passage","sequence":157,"address":"1.157"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0158","text":"Man sieht: die Analyse der Waare ergiebt alle wesentlichen\n Bestimmungen der Werthform und die Werthform selbst in ihren gegen-\n sätzlichen Momenten, die allgemeine relative Werthform, die\nallgemeine Aequivalentform, endlich die nie abschliessende\nReihe einfacher relativer Werthausdrücke, welche erst eine\n Durchgangsphase in der Entwicklung der Werthform bildet, um schliess-\n lich in die specifisch relative Werthform des allgemeinen\n Aequivalents umzuschlagen. Aber die Analyse der Waare ergab diese\n Formen als Waarenformen überhaupt, die also auch jeder Waare\n zukommen, nur gegensätzlich, so dass wenn die Waare A sich in der\neinen Formbestimmung befindet, die Waaren B, C u. s. w. ihr gegen-\n über die andere annehmen. Das entscheidend Wichtige aber war den\n inneren nothwendigen Zusammenhang zwischen Werthform, Werthsub-\n stanz und Werthgrösse zu entdecken, d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":158,"address":"1.158"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0159","text":"ideell ausgedrückt, zu\n beweisen, dass die Werthform aus dem Werthbegriff entspringt [Note: Es ist einer der Grundmängel der klassischen politischen Oekonomie, dass\n es ihr nie gelang, aus der Analyse der Waare und specieller des Waarenwerths] .","kind":"passage","sequence":159,"address":"1.159"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0160","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"35\", \"facs\": \"#f0054\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":160,"address":"1.160"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0161","text":"Eine Waare scheint auf den ersten Blick ein selbstverständliches,\n triviales Ding. Ihre Analyse ergiebt, dass sie ein sehr vertracktes Ding\n ist, voller metaphysischer Spitzfindigkeit und theologischer Mucken. Als\n blosser Gebrauchswerth ist sie ein sinnliches Ding, woran nichts My-\n steriöses, ob ich sie nun unter dem Gesichtspunkt betrachte, dass ihre\n Eigenschaften menschliche Bedürfnisse befriedigen oder dass sie erst als\nProdukt menschlicher Arbeit diese Eigenschaften erhält. Es liegt ab-\n solut nichts räthselhaftes darin, dass der Mensch durch seine Thätigkeit\n die Formen der Naturstoffe in einer ihm nützlichen Weise verändert.\n Die Form des Holzes z. B. wird verändert, wenn man aus ihm einen Tisch\n macht. Nichtsdestoweniger bleibt der Tisch Holz, ein ordinäres sinn-\n [Note: die Form des Werths, die ihn eben zum Tauschwerth macht, herauszufinden.","kind":"passage","sequence":161,"address":"1.161"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","text":"Grade in ihren besten Repräsentanten, wie A. Smith und Ricardo, behandelt sie\n die Werthform als etwas ganz Gleichgültiges oder der Natur der Waare selbst\n Aeusserliches. Der Grund ist nicht allein, dass die Analyse der Werthgrösse\n ihre Aufmerksamkeit ganz absorbirt. Er liegt tiefer. Die Werthform des\n Arbeitsprodukts ist die abstrakteste, aber auch allgemeinste Form der bür-\n gerlichen Produktionsweise, die hierdurch als eine besondre Art gesell-\n schaftlicher Produktionsweise und damit zugleich historisch charakterisirt\n wird. Versieht man sie daher für die ewige Naturform gesellschaftlicher Pro-\n duktion, so übersieht man nothwendig auch das Specifische der Werthform, also\n der Waarenform, weiter entwickelt der Geldform, Kapitalform u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":162,"address":"1.162"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0163","text":"Man findet daher bei Oekonomen, welche über das Mass der Werthgrösse durch\n Arbeitszeit durchaus übereinstimmen, die kunterbuntesten und widersprechendsten\n Vorstellungen von Geld, d. h. der fertigen Gestalt des allgemeinen Aequivalents.\n Diess tritt schlagend hervor z. B. bei der Behandlung des Bankwesens, wo mit den\n gemeinplätzlichen Definitionen des Geldes nicht mehr ausgereicht wird. Im Gegen-\n satz entsprang daher ein restaurirtes Merkantilsystem (Ganilh u. s. w.),\n welches im Werth nur die gesellschaftliche Form sieht oder vielmehr nur\n ihren substanzlosen Schein. — Um es ein für allemal zu bemerken, verstehe ich\n unter klassischer politischer Oekonomie alle Oekonomie seit W.","kind":"passage","sequence":163,"address":"1.163"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0164","text":"Petty,\n die den innern Zusammenhang der bürgerlichen Produktionsverhältnisse er-\n forscht, im Gegensatz zur Vulgärökonomie, die sich nur innerhalb des schein-\n baren Zusammenhangs herumtreibt, für eine plausible Verständlichmachung der\n so zu sagen gröbsten Phänomene und den bürgerlichen Hausbedarf das von der\n wissenschaftlichen Oekonomie längst gelieferte Material stets von neuem wieder-\n kaut, im Uebrigen aber sich darauf beschränkt, die banalen und selbstgefälligen\n Vorstellungen der bürgerlichen Produktionsagenten von ihrer eignen besten Welt\n zu systematisiren, pedantisiren und als ewige Wahrheiten zu proklamiren.] \n [Running matter: 3*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"36\", \"facs\": \"#f0055\"} --> liches Ding. Aber sobald er als Waare auftritt, verwandelt er sich in\n ein sinnlich übersinnliches Ding.","kind":"passage","sequence":164,"address":"1.164"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0165","text":"Er steht nicht nur mit seinen Füssen\n auf dem Boden, sondern er stellt sich allen andern Waaren gegenüber auf\n den Kopf und entwickelt aus seinem Holzkopf Grillen, viel wunderlicher,\n als wenn er aus freien Stücken zu tanzen begänne [Note: Man erinnert sich, dass China und die Tische zu tanzen anfingen, als alle\n übrige Welt still zu stehn schien — pour encourager les autres.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":165,"address":"1.165"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0166","text":"Der mystische Charakter der Waare entspringt also nicht aus ihrem\n Gebrauchswerth. Er entspringt ebensowenig aus den Werth bestimmun-\n gen, für sich selbst betrachtet. Denn erstens, wie verschieden die nütz-\n lichen Arbeiten oder produktiven Thätigkeiten sein mögen, es ist eine\nphysiologische Wahrheit, dass sie Funktionen eines specifisch\nmenschlichen Organismus im Unterschied von andern Organismen\n sind, und dass jede solche Funktion, welches immer ihr Inhalt und ihre\n Form, wesentlich Verausgabung von menschlichem Hirn, Nerv,\n Muskel, Sinnesorgan u. s. w. ist. Was zweitens der Bestimmung der\n Werthgrösse zu Grunde liegt, die Zeitdauer jener Verausgabung oder\n die Quantität der Arbeit, so ist die Quantität sogar sinnfällig von der\nQualität der Arbeit unterscheidbar.","kind":"passage","sequence":166,"address":"1.166"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0167","text":"In allen Zuständen musste die\n Arbeitszeit, welche die Produktion der Lebensmittel kostet, den Men-\n schen interessiren, obgleich nicht gleichmässig auf verschiednen Entwick-\n lungsstufen. Endlich, sobald die Menschen in irgend einer Weise für ein-\n ander arbeiten, erhält ihre Arbeit auch eine gesellschaftliche Form.","kind":"passage","sequence":167,"address":"1.167"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0168","text":"Nehmen wir den Robinson auf seiner Insel. Bescheiden, wie er von\n Haus aus ist, hat er doch verschiedenartige Bedürfnisse zu befriedigen und\n muss daher nützliche Arbeiten verschiedner Art verrichten,\n Werkzeuge machen, Möbel fabriciren, Lama zähmen, fischen, jagen u. s. w.\n Vom Beten u. dgl. sprechen wir hier nicht, da unser Robinson daran sein\n Vergnügen findet und derartige Thätigkeit als Erholung betrachtet. Trotz\n der Verschiedenheit seiner produktiven Funktionen weiss er, dass sie nur\n verschiedne Bethätigungsformen desselben Robinson, also nur verschiedne\n Weisen menschlicher Arbeit sind. Die Noth selbst zwingt ihn, seine\nZeit genau zwischen seinen verschiednen Funktionen zu vertheilen.","kind":"passage","sequence":168,"address":"1.168"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0169","text":"Ob\n die eine mehr, die andre weniger Raum in seiner Gesammtthätigkeit ein-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"37\", \"facs\": \"#f0056\"} --> nimmt, hängt ab von der grössern oder geringern Schwierigkeit, die zur\n Erzielung des bezweckten Nutzeffekts zu überwinden ist. Die Erfahrung\n lehrt ihm das und unser Robinson, der Uhr, Hauptbuch, Tinte und Feder\n aus dem Schiffbruch gerettet, beginnt als guter Engländer bald Buch über\n sich selbst zu führen. Sein Inventarium enthält ein Verzeichniss der Ge-\n brauchsgegenstände, die er besitzt, der verschiednen Verrichtungen,\n die zu ihrer Produktion erheischt sind, endlich der Arbeitszeit, die\n ihm bestimmte Quanta dieser verschiednen Produkte im Durchschnitt\n kosten. Alle Beziehungen zwischen Robinson und den Dingen, die seinen\n selbstgeschaffnen Reichthum bilden, sind hier so einfach und durchsichtig,\n dass selbst Herr M.","kind":"passage","sequence":169,"address":"1.169"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0170","text":"Wirth sie ohne besondre Geistesanstrengung verstehn\n dürfte. Und dennoch sind darin alle wesentlichen Bestimmungen des\nWerths enthalten.","kind":"passage","sequence":170,"address":"1.170"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0171","text":"Setzen wir nun an die Stelle Robinson’s einen Verein freier Menschen,\n die mit gemeinschaftlichen Produktionsmitteln arbeiten und ihre vielen in-\n dividuellen Arbeitskräfte selbstbewusst als eine gesellschaftliche Arbeits-\n kraft verausgaben. Alle Bestimmungen von Robinson’s Arbeit wieder-\n holen sich, nur gesellschaftlich, statt individuell. Ein wesent-\n licher Unterschied tritt jedoch ein. Alle Produkte Robinson’s waren sein\n ausschliesslich persönliches Produkt und daher unmittelbar Gebrauchs-\n gegenstände für ihn. Das Gesammtprodukt des Vereins ist ein gesell-\n schaftliches Produkt. Ein Theil dieses Produkts dient wieder als\n Produktionsmittel. Er bleibt gesellschaftlich. Aber ein anderer Theil\n wird als Lebensmittel von den Vereinsgliedern verzehrt. Er muss daher\n unter sie vertheilt werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":171,"address":"1.171"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0172","text":"Die Art dieser Vertheilung wird wech-\n seln mit der besondern Art des gesellschaftlichen Produktionsorganismus\n selbst und der entsprechenden geschichtlichen Entwicklungshöhe der Produ-\n zenten. Nur zur Parallele mit der Waarenproduktion setzen wir voraus,\n der Antheil jedes Produzenten an den Lebensmitteln sei bestimmt durch\n seine Arbeitszeit. Die Arbeitszeit würde also eine doppelte Rolle\n spielen. Ihre gesellschaftlich planmässige Vertheilung regelt die richtige\n Proportion der verschiednen Arbeitsfunktionen zu den verschiednen Be-\n dürfnissen. Andrerseits dient die Arbeitszeit zugleich als Mass des indi-\n viduellen Antheils des Produzenten an der Gemeinarbeit und daher auch\n an dem individuell verzehrbaren Theil des Gemeinprodukts.","kind":"passage","sequence":172,"address":"1.172"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0173","text":"Die gesell-\n schaftlichen Beziehungen der Menschen zu ihren Arbeiten und ihren Ar-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"38\", \"facs\": \"#f0057\"} --> beitsprodukten blieben hier durchsichtig einfach, in der Produktion sowohl\n als in der Distribution.","kind":"passage","sequence":173,"address":"1.173"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0174","text":"Woher also der räthselhafte Charakter des Arbeitsprodukts, sobald\n es die Form der Waare annimmt?","kind":"passage","sequence":174,"address":"1.174"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","text":"Wenn die Menschen ihre Produkte auf einander als Werthe beziehn,\n sofern diese Sachen für bloss sachliche Hüllen gleichartig mensch-\n licher Arbeit gelten, so liegt darin zugleich umgekehrt, dass ihre ver-\n schiednen Arbeiten nur als gleichartige menschliche Arbeit gelten in\nsachlicher Hülle. Sie beziehn ihre verschiednen Arbeiten auf ein-\n ander als menschliche Arbeit, indem sie ihre Produkte auf einander\n als Werthe beziehn. Die persönliche Beziehung ist versteckt durch\n die sachliche Form. Es steht daher dem Werth nicht auf der Stirn\n geschrieben, was er ist. Um ihre Produkte auf einander als Waaren zu\n beziehn, sind die Menschen gezwungen, ihre verschiednen Arbeiten abstrakt\n menschlicher Arbeit gleichzusetzen. Sie wissen das nicht, aber sie thun\n es, indem sie das materielle Ding auf die Abstraktion Werth reduciren.","kind":"passage","sequence":175,"address":"1.175"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0176","text":"Es ist diess eine naturwüchsige und daher bewusstlos instinktive Opera-\n tion ihres Hirns, die aus der besondern Weise ihrer materiellen Produktion\n und den Verhältnissen, worin diese Produktion sie versetzt, nothwendig\n herauswächst. Erst ist ihr Verhältniss praktisch da. Zweitens aber,\n weil sie Menschen sind, ist ihr Verhältniss als Verhältniss für\n sie da. Die Art, wie es für sie da ist, oder sich in ihrem Hirn reflektirt,\n entspringt aus der Natur des Verhältnisses selbst. Später suchen sie\n durch die Wissenschaft hinter das Geheimniss ihres eignen gesellschaft-\n lichen Produkts zu kommen, denn die Bestimmung eines Dings als Werth\n ist ihr Produkt, so gut wie die Sprache.","kind":"passage","sequence":176,"address":"1.176"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0177","text":"Was nun ferner die Werth-\n grösse betrifft, so werden die unabhängig von einander betriebenen, aber,\n weil Glieder der naturwüchsigen Theilung der Arbeit, allsei-\n tig von einander abhängigen Privatarbeiten dadurch fortwährend auf ihr\n gesellschaftlich proportionelles Mass reducirt, dass sich in den zufälligen\n und stets schwankenden Austauschverhältnissen ihrer Pro-\n dukte die zu deren Produktion gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit\n als regelndes Naturgesetz gewaltsam durchsetzt, wie etwa das Gesetz\n der Schwere, wenn einem das Haus über dem Kopf zusammenpurzelt [Note: „Was soll man von einem Gesetze denken, das sich nur durch periodische\n Revolutionen durchsetzen kann? Es ist eben ein Naturgesetz, das auf der] .","kind":"passage","sequence":177,"address":"1.177"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0178","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"39\", \"facs\": \"#f0058\"} --> Die Bestimmung der Werthgrösse durch die Arbeitszeit ist daher unter\n den erscheinenden Bewegungen der relativen Waarenwerthe verstecktes\n Geheimniss. Die eigne gesellschaftliche Bewegung der Produzenten be-\n sitzt für sie die Form einer Bewegung von Sachen, unter deren Controle\n sie stehn, statt sie zu controliren. Was nun endlich die Werthform\n betrifft, so ist es ja grade diese Form, welche die gesellschaftlichen Be-\n ziehungen der Privatarbeiter und daher die gesellschaftlichen Bestimmt-\n heiten der Privatarbeiten sachlich verschleiert, statt sie zu offen-\n baren. Wenn ich sage, Rock, Stiefel u. s. w. beziehn sich auf Leinwand\n als allgemeine Materiatur abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit, so springt die\n Verrücktheit dieses Ausdrucks ins Auge. Aber wenn die Produzenten\n von Rock, Stiefel u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":178,"address":"1.178"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0179","text":"diese Waaren auf die Leinwand als allgemei-\n nes Aequivalent beziehn, erscheint ihnen die gesellschaftliche Bezie-\n hung ihrer Privatarbeiten genau in dieser verrückten Form.","kind":"passage","sequence":179,"address":"1.179"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","text":"Derartige Formen bilden eben die Kategorien der bürgerlichen\n Oekonomie. Es sind gesellschaftlich gültige, also objektive Gedan-\n kenformen für Produktionsverhältnisse dieser historisch bestimm-\n ten gesellschaftlichen Produktionsweise.","kind":"passage","sequence":180,"address":"1.180"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0181","text":"Die Privatproduzenten treten erst in gesellschaftlichen Contakt ver-\n mittelst ihrer Privatprodukte, der Sachen. Die gesellschaftlichen Be-\n ziehungen ihrer Arbeiten sind und erscheinen daher nicht als unmit-\n telbar gesellschaftliche Verhältnisse der Personen in ihren Arbeiten, son-\n dern als sachliche Verhältnisse der Personen oder gesellschaft-\n liche Verhältnisse der Sachen. Die erste und allgemeinste Dar-\n stellung der Sache als eines gesellschaftlichen Dings ist aber die\n Verwandlung des Arbeitsprodukts in Waare.","kind":"passage","sequence":181,"address":"1.181"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","text":"Der Mysticismus der Waare entspringt also daraus, dass den Privat-\n produzenten die gesellschaftlichen Bestimmungen ihrer Privat-\n arbeiten als gesellschaftliche Naturbestimmtheiten der\n Arbeitsprodukte, dass die gesellschaftlichen Produktions-\n verhältnisse der Personen als gesellschaftliche Verhält-\n [Note: Bewusstlosigkeit der Betheiligten beruht.“ (Friedrich Engels:\n „Umrisse zu Einer Kritik der Nationalökonomie“, p. 103 in\n „Deutsch-Französische Jahrbücher, herausgegeben von Arnold\n Ruge und Karl Marx. Paris 1849.“)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"40\", \"facs\": \"#f0059\"} -->nisse der Sachen zu einander und zu den Personen erscheinen. Die\n Verhältnisse der Privatarbeiter zur gesellschaftlichen Gesammtarbeit ver-\n gegenständlichen sich ihnen gegenüber und existiren daher für sie\n in den Formen von Gegenständen.","kind":"passage","sequence":182,"address":"1.182"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0183","text":"Für eine Gesellschaft von\n Waarenproducenten, deren allgemein gesellschaftliches Produktionsver-\n hältniss darin besteht, sich zu ihren Produkten als Waaren, also als\nWerthen zu verhalten, und in dieser sachlichen Form ihre Privat-\n arbeiten auf einander zu beziehn als gleiche menschliche Arbeit,\n ist das Christenthum, mit seinem Kultus des abstrakten Menschen,\n namentlich in seiner bürgerlichen Entwicklung, dem Protestantismus, Deis-\n mus u. s. w., die entsprechendste Religionsform. In den altasiati-\n schen, antiken u. s. w. Produktionsweisen spielt die Verwandlung des\n Produkts in Waare, und daher das Dasein der Menschen als Waarenpro-\n duzenten, eine untergeordnete Rolle, die jedoch um so bedeutender wird,\n je mehr die Gemeinwesen in das Stadium ihres Untergangs treten.","kind":"passage","sequence":183,"address":"1.183"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0184","text":"Eigent-\n liche Handelsvölker existiren nur in den Intermundien der alten Welt, wie Epi-\n kurs Götter, oder wie Juden in den Poren der polnischen Gesellschaft. Jene\n alten gesellschaftlichen Produktionsorganismen sind ausserordentlich viel\n einfacher und durchsichtiger als der bürgerliche, aber sie beruhen entweder\n auf der Unreife des individuellen Menschen, der sich von der Nabelschnur\n des natürlichen Gattungszusammenhangs mit Andern noch nicht losgerissen\n hat, oder auf unmittelbaren Herrschafts- und Knechtschaftsverhältnissen.\n Sie sind bedingt durch eine niedrige Entwicklungsstufe der Produktivkräfte\n der Arbeit und entsprechend befangene Verhältnisse der Menschen inner-\n halb ihres materiellen Lebenserzeugungsprozesses, daher zu einander und\n zur Natur. Diese wirkliche Befangenheit spiegelt sich ideell wieder in\n den alten Natur- und Volksreligionen.","kind":"passage","sequence":184,"address":"1.184"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0185","text":"Der religiöse Wiederschein\n der wirklichen Welt kann nur verschwinden, sobald die Verhältnisse des\n praktischen Werkeltagslebens den Menschen tagtäglich durchsichtig ver-\n nünftige Beziehungen zu einander und zur Natur darstellen. Die Ver-\n hältnisse können sich aber nur als das darstellen, was sie sind. Die Ge-\n stalt des gesellschaftlichen Lebensprozesses, d. h. des materiellen Pro-\n duktionsprozesses, streift nur ihren mystischen Nebelschleier ab, sobald\n sie als Produkt frei vergesellschafteter Menschen unter deren bewusster\n planmässiger Controle steht. Dazu ist jedoch eine materielle Grundlage\n der Gesellschaft erheischt oder eine Reihe materieller Existenzbedingungen,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"41\", \"facs\": \"#f0060\"} --> welche selbst wieder das naturwüchsige Produkt einer langen und qual-\n vollen Entwicklungsgeschichte sind.","kind":"passage","sequence":185,"address":"1.185"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0186","text":"Die politische Oekonomie hat nun zwar, wenn auch unvollkommen [Note: Das Unzulängliche in Ricardo’s Analyse der Werthgrösse — und es\n ist die beste — wird man aus dem dritten und vierten Buch dieser Schrift ersehn.\n Was aber den Werth überhaupt betrifft, so unterscheidet die klassische politische\n Oekonomie nirgendwo ausdrücklich und mit klarem Bewusstsein Arbeit, die sich\n in Werth, von derselben Arbeit, soweit sie sich im Gebrauchswerth\n ihres Produkts darstellt. Sie macht natürlich den Unterschied thatsächlich, da\n sie die Arbeit das einemal quantitativ, das andremal qualitativ betrachtet. Aber\n es fällt ihr nicht ein, dass bloss quantitativer Unterschied der Arbeiten ihre\nqualitative Einheit oder Gleichheit voraussetzt, also ihre Reduktion auf\nabstrakt menschliche Arbeit. Ricardo z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":186,"address":"1.186"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0187","text":"erklärt sich einverstanden\n mit Destutt de Tracy, wenn dieser sagt: „As it is certain that our physical\n and moral faculties are alone our original riches, the employment of those faculties,\n labour of some kind, is our original treasure, and that it is always from this\n employment — that all those things are created which we call riches … It is\n certain too, that all those things only represent the labour which\n has created them, and if they have a value, or even two dis-\n tinctvalues, they can only derive them from that (the value) of the\n labour from which they emanate.“ (Ricardo: „The Principles of Pol.\n Econ. 3 ed. Lond. 1821“, p. 334). Wir deuten nur an, dass Ricardo dem\n Destutt seinen eignen tieferen Sinn unterschiebt.","kind":"passage","sequence":187,"address":"1.187"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0188","text":"Destutt sagt in der That zwar\n einerseits, dass alle Dinge, die den Reichthum bilden, „die Arbeit reprä-\n sentiren, die sie geschaffen hat“, aber andrerseits, dass sie ihre „zwei ver-\n schiedenen Werthe“ (Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth) vom „Werth der\n Arbeit“ erhalten. Er fällt damit in die Flachheit der Vulgärökonomie, die den\nWerth einer Waare (hier der Arbeit) voraussetzt, um dadurch hinterher den\n Werth der anderen Waaren zu bestimmen. Ricardo liest ihn so, dass sowohl\n im Gebrauchswerth als Tauschwerth sich Arbeit (nicht Werth der Arbeit) dar-\n stellt. Er selbst aber scheidet sowenig den zwieschlächtigen Charakter der\nArbeit, die doppelt dargestellt ist, dass er in dem ganzen Kapitel: „Value\n and Riches, Their Distinctive Properties“ sich mühselig mit den\n Trivialitäten eines J. B. Say herumschlagen muss.","kind":"passage","sequence":188,"address":"1.188"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0189","text":"Am Ende ist er daher auch\n ganz erstaunt, dass Destutt zwar mit ihm selbst über Arbeit als Werthquelle\n und dennoch andererseits mit Say über den Werthbegriff harmonire] ,\n Werth und Werthgrösse analysirt. Sie hat niemals auch nur die Frage\n gestellt, warum sich die Arbeit im Werth und das Mass der Arbeit\n durch ihre Zeitdauer in der Werthgrösse darstellt? Formen, denen es\n auf der Stirn geschrieben steht, dass sie einer Gesellschaftsformation an-\n gehören, worin der Produktionsprozess die Menschen, der Mensch noch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"42\", \"facs\": \"#f0061\"} --> nicht den Produktionsprozess bemeistert, gelten ihrem bürgerlichen Be-\n wusstsein für eben so selbstverständliche Naturnothwendigkeit als die pro-\n duktive Arbeit selbst.","kind":"passage","sequence":189,"address":"1.189"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0190","text":"Vorbürgerliche Formen des gesellschaftlichen Pro-\n duktionsorganismus werden daher von ihr behandelt, wie etwa von den\n Kirchenvätern vorchristliche Religionen [Note: „Les économistes ont une singulière manière de procéder. Il n’y a\n pour eux que deux sortes d’institution, celles de l’art et celles de la nature. Les\n institutions de la féodalité sont des institutions artificielles, celles de la bour-\n geoisie sont des institutions naturelles. Ils ressemblent en’ ceci aux théologiens,\n qui eux aussi établissent deux sortes de religion. Toute religion qui n’est pas la\n leur est une invention des hommes, tandis que leur propre religion est une émana-\n tion de dieu. — Ainsi il y a eu de l’histoire, mais il n’y en a plus.“ (Karl\n Marx: „Misère de la Philosophie. Réponse à la Philosophie\n de la Misère par M. Proudhon. 1847“, p. 113.)","kind":"passage","sequence":190,"address":"1.190"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0191","text":"Wahrhaft drollig ist\n Herr Bastiat, der sich einbildet, die alten Griechen und Römer hätten nur von\nRaub gelebt. Wenn man aber viele Jahrhunderte durch von Raub lebt, muss\n doch beständig etwas zu rauben da sein oder der Gegenstand des Raubes sich\n fortwährend reproduciren. Es scheint daher, dass auch Griechen und Römer einen\n Produktionsprozess hatten, also eine Oekonomie, welche ganz so die materielle\n Grundlage ihrer Welt bildete, wie die bürgerliche Oekonomie die der heutigen\n Welt. Oder meint Bastiat etwa, dass eine Produktionsweise, die auf der Sklaven-\n arbeit beruht, auf einem Raubsystem ruht? Er stellt sich dann auf gefähr-\n lichen Boden. Wenn ein Denkriese wie Aristoteles in seiner Würdigung\n der Sklavenarbeit irrte, warum sollte ein Zwergökonom, wie Bastiat, in\n seiner Würdigung der Lohnarbeit richtig gehn?","kind":"passage","sequence":191,"address":"1.191"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0192","text":"— Ich ergreife diese Gelegen-\n heit, um einen Einwand, der mir beim Erscheinen meiner Schrift „Zur Kritik\n der Pol. Oekonomie. 1859“ von einem deutsch-amerikanischen Blatte gemacht\n wurde, kurz abzuweisen. Es sagte, meine Ansicht, dass die bestimmte Produk-\n tionsweise und die ihr jedesmal entsprechenden Produktionsverhältnisse, kurz „die\n ökonomische Struktur der Gesellschaft die reale Basis sei, worauf sich ein juristi-\n scher und politischer Ueberbau erhebe, und welcher bestimmte gesellschaftliche\n Bewusstseinsformen entsprächen“, dass „die Produktionsweise des materiellen\n Lebens den socialen, politischen und geistigen Lebensprozess überhaupt bedinge“,\n — alles diess sei zwar richtig für die heutige Welt, wo die materiellen Interessen,\n aber nicht für das Mittelalter, wo der Katholicismus, und für Athen und Rom, wo] .","kind":"passage","sequence":192,"address":"1.192"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0193","text":"Wie sehr ein Theil der Oekonomen von dem der Waarenwelt ankle-\n benden Fetischismus oder dem gegenständlichen Schein der gesell-\n schaftlichen Arbeitsbestimmungen getäuscht wird, beweist u. a. der\n langweilig abgeschmackte Zank über die Rolle der Natur in der Bil-\n dung des Tauschwerths. Da Tauschwerth eine bestimmte gesellschaft-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"43\", \"facs\": \"#f0062\"} --> liche Manier ist, die auf ein Ding verwandte Arbeit auszudrücken, kann\n er nicht mehr Naturstoff enthalten als etwa der Wechselkurs.","kind":"passage","sequence":193,"address":"1.193"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0194","text":"Als allgemeinste und unentwickeltste Form der bürgerlichen Pro-\n duktion, welche desswegen auch schon in früheren Produktionsperioden\n erscheint, obgleich nicht in derselben herrschenden, also charakteristischen\n Weise, war die Waarenform noch relativ leicht zu durchschauen.\n Aber konkretere Formen, wie das Kapital z. B.? Der Fetischismus der\n klassischen Oekonomie wird hier handgreiflich.","kind":"passage","sequence":194,"address":"1.194"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0195","text":"Um jedoch nicht vorzugreifen, genüge hier noch ein Beispiel bezüg-\n lich der Waarenform selbst. Man hat gesehn, dass in der Beziehung von\n Waare auf Waare, z. B. von Stiefel auf Stiefelknecht, der Gebrauchswerth\n des Stiefelknechts, also die Nützlichkeit seiner wirklichen dinglichen\n Eigenschaften dem Stiefel durchaus gleichgültig ist. Nur als Erschei-\n nungsform ihres eignen Werths interessirt die Stiefelwaare der Stiefel-\n knecht. Könnten die Waaren also sprechen, so würden sie sagen, unser\n Gebrauchswerth mag den Menschen interessiren. Er kömmt uns nicht\n als Dingen zu. Was uns aber dinglich zukömmt, ist unser Werth.\n Unser eigner Verkehr als Waarendinge beweist das. Wir beziehn uns\n nur als Tauschwerthe auf einander. Man höre nun, wie der Oekonom aus\n der Waarenseele heraus spricht: „Werth (Tauschwerth) ist Eigen-\n schaft der Dinge, Reichthum (Gebrauchswerth) des Menschen.","kind":"passage","sequence":195,"address":"1.195"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0196","text":"Werth\n in diesem Sinn schliesst nothwendig Austausch ein, Reichthum nicht [Note: „Value is a property of things, riches of man. Value, in this\n sense, necessarily implies exchanges, riches do not.“ „Observations on\n some verbal Disputes in Pol. Econ., particularly relating\n to value and to offer and demand. Lond. 1821“, p. 16.] .“\n [Note: die Politik herrschten. Zunächst ist es befremdlich, dass Jemand vorauszusetzen\n beliebt, diese weltbekannten Redensarten über Mittelalter und antike Welt seien\n irgend Jemand unbekannt geblieben. So viel ist klar, dass das Mittelalter nicht\n vom Katholicismus und die antike Welt nicht von der Politik leben konnten.\n Die Art und Weise, wie sie ihr Leben gewannen, erklärt umgekehrt, warum dort\n die Politik, hier der Katholicismus ihre Rollen spielten. Es gehört übrigens wenig\n Bekanntschaft z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":196,"address":"1.196"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0197","text":"mit der Geschichte der römischen Republik dazu, um zu\n wissen, dass die Geschichte des Grundeigenthums ihre Geheimgeschichte bildet.\n Andererseits hat schon Don Quixote den Irrthum gebüsst, dass er die fahrende\n Ritterschaft mit allen ökonomischen Formen der Gesellschaft gleich verträglich\n wähnte.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"44\", \"facs\": \"#f0063\"} --> „Reichthum (Gebrauchswerth) ist ein Attribut des Menschen, Werth\n ein Attribut der Waaren. Ein Mensch oder ein Gemeinwesen ist\nreich; eine Perle oder Diamant ist werthvoll … Eine Perle oder\n Diamant hat Werth als Perle oder Diamant [Note: „Riches are the attribute of man, value is the attribute of commodities.\n A man or a community is rich, a pearl or a diamond is valuable … A pearl or\n a diamond is valuable as a pearl or diamond.“ S. Bailey l. c.\n p. 165.] .“ Bisher hat\n noch kein Chemiker Tauschwerth in Perle oder Diamant entdeckt.","kind":"passage","sequence":197,"address":"1.197"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0198","text":"Unsere\n Verfasser, die besondern Anspruch auf kritische Tiefe machen, finden aber,\n dass der Gebrauchswerth der Sachen unabhängig von ihren sachlichen\n Eigenschaften, dagegen ihr Tauschwerth ihnen als Sachen zukömmt.\n Was sie hierin bestätigt, ist der sonderbare Umstand, dass der Gebrauchs-\n werth der Dinge sich für den Menschen ohne Austausch realisirt,\n also im unmittelbaren Verhältniss zwischen Ding und Mensch, ihr\n Werth umgekehrt nur im Austausch, d. h. in einem gesell-\n schaftlichen Prozess. Wer erinnert sich hier nicht des guten\n Dogberry, der den Nachtwächter Seacoal belehrt: „Ein gut aussehender\n Mann zu sein, ist eine Gabe der Umstände, aber Lesen und Schreiben\n zu können, kömmt von Natur [Note: Der Verfasser der „Observations“ und S. Bailey beschuldigen\n Ricardo, er habe den Tauschwerth aus einem nur Relativen in etwas Ab-\n solutes verwandelt. Umgekehrt.","kind":"passage","sequence":198,"address":"1.198"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0199","text":"Er hat die Scheinrelativität, die\n diese Dinge, Diamant und Perlen z. B., als Tauschwerthe besitzen, auf das hinter\n dem Schein verborgene wahre Verhältniss reducirt, auf ihre Relativität\n als blosse Ausdrücke menschlicher Arbeit. Wenn die Ricardianer dem Bailey\n grob, aber nicht schlagend antworteten, so nur weil sie bei Ricardo selbst keinen\n Aufschluss über den inneren Zusammenhang zwischen Werth und Tausch-\n werth fanden.] “.","kind":"passage","sequence":199,"address":"1.199"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","text":"Die Waare ist unmittelbare Einheit von Gebrauchs-\n werth und Tauschwerth, also zweier Entgegengesetzten. Sie ist\n daher ein unmittelbarer Widerspruch. Dieser Widerspruch muss\n sich entwickeln, sobald sie nicht wie bisher analytisch bald unter dem\n Gesichtspunkt des Gebrauchswerths, bald unter dem Gesichtspunkt des\n Tauschwerths betrachtet, sondern als ein Ganzes wirklich auf andere\n Waaren bezogen wird. Die wirkliche Beziehung der Waaren aufein-\n ander ist aber ihr Austauschprozess.","kind":"passage","sequence":200,"address":"1.200"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0201","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"45\", \"facs\": \"#f0064\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":201,"address":"1.201"}],"sha256":"d593a5cf94e8c48530b9c4b0c3276965b0af631a707d082f435ff86c44eae9d8","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T201416814104Z_2d833d61","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1) Die Waare.","source_sha256":"d593a5cf94e8c48530b9c4b0c3276965b0af631a707d082f435ff86c44eae9d8","sha256":"e28848665187cce46bc7ed95eb0158130e68fb2ce61db3915b73f0565e1d0bae","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:14:16.814143+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0001","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"[1]\", \"facs\": \"#f0020\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"1.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0002","text":"## Book One.\n The Process of Production of Capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"1.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0003","text":"## Chapter One.\n Commodity and Money.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"1.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0004","text":"## 1) The Commodity.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"1.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","text":"The wealth of societies in which the capitalist mode of pro-\n duction prevails appears as an “immense collection of commodities” [Note: Karl Marx: “A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy. Ber-\n lin 1859”, p. 4.] ,\n the individual commodity as its elementary form. Our investigation\n therefore begins with the analysis of the commodity.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"1.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0006","text":"The commodity is, in the first place, an external object, a thing which\n through its properties satisfies human needs of some kind.\n The nature of these needs, whether they arise, for instance, from the stomach or from fancy,\n changes nothing in the matter [Note: “Desire implies want; it is the appetite of the mind, and as natural as\n hunger to the body . . . . the greatest number (of things) have their value from\n supplying the wants of the mind.” Nicholas Barbon: “A Discourse on\n coining the new money lighter, in answer to Mr. Locke’s Consi-\n derations etc. London 1696”, p. 2, 3.] . Nor is the question here\n how the thing satisfies the human need, whether directly as a means of subsistence,\n i. e. as an object of enjoyment, or by a detour, as a means of production.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"1.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0007","text":"Every useful thing, such as iron, paper, etc., is to be considered from a double\n [Running matter: I. 1]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"2\", \"facs\": \"#f0021\"} --> point of view, according to quality and quantity. Every\n such thing is a whole of many properties and can therefore be useful in dif-\n ferent respects. To discover these different respects and hence\n the manifold modes of use of things is an historical deed [Note: “Things have an intrinsick vertue (this is Barbon’s specific desig-\n nation for use-value), which in all places have the same vertue; as\n the loadstone to attract iron” (l. c. p. 16). The property of the magnet of attracting iron\n became useful only once magnetic polarity had been discovered by means of it.] . Thus the finding of social measures for the quan-\n tity of useful things is also an historical deed.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"1.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0008","text":"The diversity of commodity measures arises\n partly from the differing nature of the objects to be measured,\n partly from convention.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"1.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0009","text":"The usefulness of a thing for human life makes it a use-value [Note: “The natural worth of anything consists in its fitness to supply the\n necessities, or serve the conveniences of human life.” (John Locke: “Some\n Considerations on the Consequences of the Lowering of Interest.\n 1691” in “Works edit. Lond. 1777” V. II p. 28). In the 17th century we still\n frequently find among English writers “Worth” for use-value and\n “Value” for exchange-value, quite in the spirit of a language which likes to\n express the immediate thing in Germanic and the reflected thing in Romance\n terms.] . In brief, we call the useful thing\n itself, or the commodity-body, such as iron, wheat, diamond, etc.,\nuse-value, good, article. In considering use-\n values, quantitative definiteness is always presupposed, such as dozen\n watches, ell of linen, ton of iron, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"1.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0010","text":"The use-values of\n commodities furnish the material for a discipline of their own, commodity-\n lore [Note: In bourgeois society the fictio juris prevails that every person,\n as a purchaser of commodities, possesses an encyclopaedic knowledge of commodities.] . Use-value is realised only in use or\n consumption. Use-values form the material content\n of wealth, whatever its social form may be. In the social form\n we have to consider, they are at the same time the material bearers of — exchange-value.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"1.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011","text":"Exchange-value appears at first as the quantitative rela-\n tion, the proportion, in which use-values of one kind exchange for\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"3\", \"facs\": \"#f0022\"} --> use-values of another kind [Note: “La valeur consiste dans le rapport d’échange qui se trouve entre\n telle chose et telle autre, entre telle mesure d’une production et telle mesure d’une\n autre.” (Le Trosne: “De L’Intérêt Social”. Physiocrates. ed.\n Daire. Paris 1846. p. 889.)] , a relation which con-\n stantly changes with time and place. Exchange-value therefore seems something\n accidental and purely relative; an exchange-value intrinsic, immanent\n to the commodity (valeur intrinsèque) is thus a contradictio in adjecto  [Note: “Nothing can have an intrinsick value” (N. Barbon l. c. p. 16), or\n as Butler says:\n“The value of a thing\n Is just as much as it will bring.”] .\n Let us consider the matter more closely.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"1.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0012","text":"A single commodity, a quarter of wheat, for example, exchanges in the most\n various proportions with other articles. Nevertheless,\n its exchange-value remains unchanged, whether expressed in x blacking,\n y silk, z gold, etc. It must therefore be distinguishable from these its differ-\n ent modes of expression.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"1.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0013","text":"Let us take, further, two commodities, for example wheat and iron. Whatever\n their exchange relation, it can always be represented in an equation,\n in which a given quantity of wheat is equated with some quantity of iron,\n for example 1 quarter of wheat = a cwt. of iron. What does\n this equation signify? That the same value exists in two different\n things, in 1 quarter of wheat and likewise in a cwt. of iron. Both\n are therefore equal to a third thing, which in and for itself is neither the one\n nor the other. Each of the two, insofar as it is exchange-value, must there-\n fore be reducible to this third thing independently of the other.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"1.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0014","text":"Let a simple geometrical example illustrate this. In order to determine\n and compare the areas of all rectilinear figures,\n they are resolved into triangles. The triangle itself is reduced to an\n expression wholly different from its visible figure — half\n the product of its base and its height. Likewise, the exchange-values\n of commodities are to be reduced to a common element, of which they represent a greater\n or lesser amount.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"1.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0015","text":"That the substance of exchange-value is something wholly dif-\n ferent from and independent of the physically palpable existence of the commodity\n or of its existence as use-value\n [Running matter: 1*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"4\", \"facs\": \"#f0023\"} --> is shown at first glance by its exchange relation.\n It is characterised precisely by abstraction\n from use-value. Considered in respect of exchange-value, namely,\n one commodity is just as good as every other, provided only that it is present in the right\n proportion [Note: “One sort of wares are as good as another, if the value be equal. There\n is no difference or distinction in things of equal value … One hundred\n pounds worth of lead or iron, is of as great a value as one hundred pounds worth\n of silver and gold.” (N. Barbon l. c. p. 53 u. 7.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"1.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0016","text":"Independently of their exchange relation or of the form\n in which they appear as exchange-values, commodities are therefore\n in the first instance to be considered simply as values [Note: Whenever henceforth we use the word “value” without further qualification,\n exchange-value is always meant.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"1.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","text":"As objects of use or goods, commodities are corporeally\n different things. Their being value, by contrast, constitutes their unity.\n This unity arises not from nature, but from society.\n The common social substance which presents itself differently only in\n different use-values is — labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"1.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0018","text":"As values, commodities are nothing but crystallised labour.\n The unit of measure of labour itself is simple average\n labour, whose character indeed changes in different countries and epochs of\n culture, but is given in a given society.\n More complex labour counts only as intensified, or rather multi-\n plied, simple labour, so that, for example, a smaller quantity of complex\n labour equals a greater quantity of simple labour. How this\n reduction is regulated is immaterial here. That it constantly takes\n place is shown by experience. A commodity may be the product of the most complex\n labour. Its value equates it with the product of simple labour\n and therefore itself represents only a definite quantity of simple la-\n bour.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"1.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0019","text":"A use-value or good therefore has value only because\nlabour is objectified or materialised in it.\n How, then, do we measure the magnitude of its value? By the quantity of\n the “value-forming substance,” labour, contained in it. The quan-\n tity of labour itself is measured by its duration in time, and labour-\n time in turn possesses its standard in definite portions of time,\n such as hour, day, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"1.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0020","text":"It might appear that, if the value of a commodity is determined by the\n quantity of labour expended during its production, the lazier or more unskilful a man is,\n the more valuable his commodity, since he requires\n the more labour-time for its manufacture. But only socially\n necessary labour-time counts as value-\n forming. Socially necessary labour-time is labour-time required\n to produce any use-value under the given socially normal conditions of pro-\n duction and with the socially average degree of skill and intensity of labour. After\n the introduction of the power-loom in England, for example, perhaps half\n as much labour as before sufficed to transform a given quantity of yarn into fabric.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"1.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0021","text":"The English hand-loom weaver required for this transformation\n in fact as before the same labour-time, but the product of his\n individual labour-hour now represented only half a social\n labour-hour and therefore fell to half of its former\n value.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"1.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0022","text":"It is therefore only the quantity of socially neces-\n sary labour, or the labour-time socially necessary for the production\n of a use-value, that determines\n its magnitude of value. The individual commodity here counts throughout\n as the average specimen of its kind [Note: “Toutes les productions d’un même genre ne forment proprement qu’une\n masse, dont le prix se détermine en général et sans égard aux circonstances par-\n ticulières”. (Le Trosne l. c. p. 893.)] . Commodities in which equal\n quantities of labour are contained, or which can be produced in the same labour-time,\n therefore have the same magnitude of value.\n The value of one commodity is related to the value of every other commodity as\n the labour-time necessary for the production of the one is to that necessary for the pro-\n duction of the other.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"1.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0023","text":"“As values all\n commodities are only definite masses of congealed labour-time” [Note: K. Marx l. c. p. 6.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"1.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0024","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"6\", \"facs\": \"#f0025\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"1.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0025","text":"The magnitude of value of a commodity would therefore remain constant if the labour-time required for its production were constant. The latter, however, varies with every variation in the productive power of labour. The productive power of labour is determined by manifold circumstances, among others by the average degree of skill of the workers, the stage of development of science and of its technological applicability, the social combination of the process of production, the extent and efficacy of the means of production, and by natural conditions. The same quantity of labour is represented, for example, during a favourable season by 8 bushels of wheat, during an unfavourable one by only 4. The same quantity of labour yields more metals in rich than in poor mines, etc. Diamonds occur rarely in the earth's crust, and their discovery therefore costs, on average, much labour-time.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"1.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0026","text":"Consequently, in little volume they represent much labour. Jacob doubts whether gold has ever paid its full value. This applies still more to the diamond. According to Eschwege, in 1823 the total yield of the Brazilian diamond mines over eighty years had not yet attained the value of the average product, over a year and a half, of the Brazilian sugar or coffee plantations. With richer mines, the same quantity of labour would be represented in more diamonds, and their value would fall. If it succeeds in transforming coal into diamond with little labour, its value can fall below that of bricks. Generally: The greater the productive power of labour, the smaller the labour-time required for the production of an article, the smaller the mass of labour crystallised in it, the smaller its value.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"1.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0027","text":"Conversely, the smaller the productive power of labour, the greater the labour-time necessary for the production of an article, the greater its value. The magnitude of value of a commodity thus varies directly as the quantity, and inversely as the productive power, of the labour realised in it.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"1.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0028","text":"We now know the substance of value. It is labour. We know its measure of magnitude. It is labour-time. Its form, which stamps value precisely as exchange-value, remains to be analysed. Before doing so, however, the determinations already found must be developed somewhat more closely.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"1.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0029","text":"A thing can be a use-value without being an exchange-value.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"7\", \"facs\": \"#f0026\"} --> This is the case when its existence for man is not mediated by labour. Thus air, virgin soil, natural meadows, wild-growing timber, etc. A thing can be useful and the product of human labour without being a commodity. Whoever satisfies his own need by his product indeed creates use-value, but not commodity. In order to produce commodity, he must produce not only use-value, but use-value for others, social use-value. Finally, no thing can be value without being an object of use. If it is useless, then the labour contained in it is also useless, does not count as labour, and therefore forms no value.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"1.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0030","text":"Originally the commodity appeared to us as a twofold thing, use-value and exchange-value. On closer consideration it will be seen that the labour contained in the commodity is also twofold. This point, which was first critically developed by me [Note: l. c. p. 12, 13 and passim.], is the pivot upon which the understanding of political economy turns.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"1.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0031","text":"Let us take two commodities, for instance a coat and 10 ells of linen. Let the former have twice the value of the latter, so that, if 10 ells of linen = W, the coat = 2 W.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"1.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0032","text":"The coat is a use-value which satisfies a particular need. In order to bring it forth, a determinate kind of purposive productive activity is required. It is determined by purpose, mode of operation, object, means, and result. The labour whose usefulness thus presents itself in the use-value of its product, or in the fact that its product is a use-value, shall here, for the sake of simplification, be called simply useful labour. From this point of view it is always considered in relation to the useful effect, the production of which it intends.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"1.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0033","text":"As coat and linen are qualitatively different use-values, so the labours that mediate their existence are qualitatively different—tailoring and weaving. Were those things not qualitatively different use-values, and therefore products of qualitatively different useful labours, they could not at all confront one another as commodities. Coat is not exchanged for coat, the same use-value not for the same use-value.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"8\", \"facs\": \"#f0027\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"1.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0034","text":"In the totality of the various kinds of use-values, or commodity bodies, there appears a totality of useful labours equally manifold, differing by genus, species, family, subspecies, and variety—a social division of labour. It is a condition of existence of commodity production, although commodity production is not, conversely, a condition of existence of the social division of labour. In the ancient Indian community labour is socially divided without the products becoming commodities. Or, to take a closer example, in every factory labour is systematically divided, but this division is not mediated by the workers exchanging their individual products. Only products of independent and mutually independent private labours confront one another as commodities.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"1.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0035","text":"Thus we have seen: in the use-value of every commodity there is contained a determinate purposive productive activity or useful labour. Use-values cannot confront one another as commodities unless qualitatively different useful labours are contained in them. In a society whose products generally assume the form of commodity, i.e. in a society of commodity producers, this qualitative difference among useful labours, which are carried on independently of one another as the private businesses of independent producers, develops into a many-membered system, into a social division of labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"1.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0036","text":"For the rest, it is immaterial to the coat whether it is worn by the tailor or by the tailor's customer. In both cases it acts as use-value. Nor is the relation between the coat and the labour producing it in and for itself altered by the fact that tailoring becomes a distinct profession, an independent member of the social division of labour. Wherever the need for clothing compelled him, man tailored for millennia before a tailor emerged from a man. But the existence of coat, linen, and every element of material wealth not present in nature, always had to be mediated by a special purposive productive activity, which assimilates particular natural materials to particular human needs.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"9\", \"facs\": \"#f0028\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"1.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0037","text":"As the creator of use-values, as useful labour, labour is therefore a condition of human existence independent of all social forms, an eternal natural necessity for mediating the metabolism between man and nature, and hence human life.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"1.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0038","text":"The use-values coat, linen, etc., in short, commodity bodies, are combinations of two elements, natural material and labour. If one subtracts the total sum of all the different useful labours contained in coat, linen, etc., there always remains a material substrate which is present in nature without human intervention. In his production man can proceed only as nature itself does, i.e. only alter the forms of materials [Note: “All the phenomena of the universe, whether they are products of the hand of man, or of the universal laws of physics, give us no idea of actual creation, but only of a modification of matter.”","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"1.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0039","text":"“Bringing together and separating are the only elements that human ingenuity finds in analysing the idea of reproduction; and it is equally reproduction of value (use-value, although Verri here, in his polemic against the Physiocrats, himself does not know quite what sort of value he is speaking of) and of riches, whether earth, air, and water in the fields are transformed into grain, or whether by the hand of man the gluten of an insect is transformed into velvet, or some pieces of metal are arranged to form a repetition.” (Pietro Verri: “Meditazioni sulla Economia Politica” [“Meditations on Political Economy”] (first printed 1773), in Custodi's edition of the Italian economists, Parte Moderna, vol. XV p. 22.)]. Still more. In this labour of shaping itself, he is constantly assisted by natural forces.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"1.39","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0040","text":"Labour is therefore not the sole source of the use-values it produces, of material wealth. Labour is its father, as William Petty says, and the earth its mother.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"1.40","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0041","text":"Let us now pass from the commodity, insofar as it is an object of use, to commodity-value.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"1.41","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0042","text":"According to our assumption, the coat has twice the value of the linen. This, however, is only a quantitative difference, which does not yet concern us immediately. We therefore recall that, if the value of a coat is twice as great as that of 10 ells of linen, 20 ells of linen have the same magnitude of value as one coat.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"10\", \"facs\": \"#f0029\"} --> As values, coat and linen are things of the same substance, objective expressions of homogeneous labour. But tailoring and weaving are qualitatively different labours.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"1.42","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0043","text":"There are, however, states of society in which the same man alternately tailors and weaves, in which these two different modes of labour are therefore only modifications of the labour of the same individual and not yet distinct fixed functions of different individuals, just as the coat our tailor makes today and the trousers he makes tomorrow presuppose only variations of the same individual labour. Moreover, plain sight teaches that in our capitalist society, according to the changing direction of the demand for labour, a given portion of human labour is supplied alternately in the form of tailoring or in the form of weaving. This change of form of labour may not proceed without friction, but it must proceed.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"1.43","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0044","text":"If one abstracts from the determinacy of productive activity and hence from the useful character of labour, what remains of it is that it is an expenditure of human labour-power. Tailoring and weaving, although qualitatively different productive activities, are both productive expenditures of human brain, muscle, nerve, hand, etc., and in this sense both are human labour. They are only two different forms of expending human labour-power. To be sure, human labour-power itself must be more or less developed in order to be expended in this or that form. But the value of commodities represents human labour simply, expenditure of human labour-power in general.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"1.44","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0045","text":"Just as in bourgeois society a general or banker plays a great role, while man simply plays a very shabby one [Note: Cf. Hegel, “Philosophy of Right. Berlin 1840”, p. 250, § 190.], so it is here also with human labour. It is expenditure of simple labour-power, which every ordinary man possesses in his bodily organism without special development. Let the labour-power of a farm servant, for example, count as simple labour-power, and its expenditure therefore as simple labour or human labour without further embellishment, while tailoring counts as\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"11\", \"facs\": \"#f0030\"} --> expenditure of more highly developed labour-power.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"1.45","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0046","text":"While the working day of the farm servant is therefore represented, say, in the expression of value of ½ W, the working day of the tailor is represented in the expression of value of W [Note: The reader must note that the question here is not of the wage or value which the worker may receive for a working day, but of the commodity-value in which his working day is objectified. The category of wages does not yet exist at all at this stage of our exposition.]. This difference, however, is only quantitative. If the coat is the product of one working day of the tailor, it has the same value as the product of 2 working days of the farm servant. Thus tailoring always counts only as multiplied farm labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"1.46","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0047","text":"The different proportions in which different kinds of labour are reduced to simple labour as their unit of measure are established by a social process behind the backs of the producers, and therefore appear to them as given by custom. For the sake of simplification, in what follows every kind of labour-power counts for us directly as simple labour-power, whereby only the trouble of reduction is spared.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"1.47","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0048","text":"Thus, just as in the values of coat and linen abstraction is made from the difference of their use-values, so in the labour that represents these values abstraction is made from the difference of the useful forms in which it is at one time tailoring, at another weaving. Just as the use-values coat and linen are combinations of purposively determined productive activities with cloth and yarn, whereas the values coat and linen are merely homogeneous jellies of labour, so the labour contained in these values counts not through its productive relation to cloth and yarn, but only as expenditure of human labour-power.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"1.48","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0049","text":"The constituent elements of the use-values coat and linen are\n tailoring and weaving; by virtue of their different qualities they are\n the substance of coat-value and linen-value only insofar as\n abstraction is made from their particular quality and both possess the same\n quality, the quality of human labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"1.49","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0050","text":"But coat and linen are not only values in general, but rather\n values of determinate magnitude, and according to our assumption\n the coat is worth twice as much as 10 yards of linen. Whence this\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"12\", \"facs\": \"#f0031\"} --> difference in their magnitudes of value? From the fact that the linen contains only\n half as much labour as the coat, so that for the production of the lat-\n ter labour-power must be expended for twice as much time\n as for the production of the former.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"1.50","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0051","text":"Thus, if with reference to use-value the labour contained in the\n commodity counts only qualitatively, with reference to the magnitude of\n value it counts only quantitatively, after it has already been reduced to human\n labour without further quality. There it is a question of the\n how and what of labour, here of its how much, its duration in time. Since the\n magnitude of value of a commodity measures only the quantity of labour contained in it,\n commodities of a certain proportion must always be values of equal\n magnitude.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"1.51","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0052","text":"If the productive power, say, of all the useful labours required for the production of a coat\n remains unchanged, the magnitude of value of\n coats rises with their own quantity. If 1 coat represents x, 2 coats represent 2 x\n working days, and so on. But assume that the labour-time necessary for the production of a coat\n rises to twice its amount or falls by one half.\n In the first case one coat has as much value as previously two coats, in the lat-\n ter case two coats have only as much value as previously one, although\n in both cases one coat continues as before to render the same services and the\n useful labour contained in it continues as before to remain of the same quality.\n But the quantity of labour expended in its production has\n changed.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"1.52","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0053","text":"A greater quantity of use-value constitutes in and for itself greater\n material wealth, two coats more than one. With two\n coats one can clothe two people, with one coat only one per-\n son, and so on. Nevertheless, a simultaneous fall in its magnitude of value\n may correspond to the rising mass of material wealth. This\n contrary movement arises from the twofold character of labour. Productive power is\n naturally always the productive power of useful, concrete labour.\n It in fact expresses only the degree of effectiveness of purposively determined productive activity in a given period of time.\n Useful labour thus becomes a richer or poorer source of products\n in direct proportion to the rise or fall of its productive\n power. By contrast, a change in productive power does not affect at all\n the labour represented in value in and for itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"1.53","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0054","text":"Since productive power belongs to the\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"13\", \"facs\": \"#f0032\"} --> concrete useful form of labour, it can naturally no longer affect\n labour as soon as abstraction is made from its concrete useful form.\n The same labour is therefore always represented in the same periods of\n time by the same magnitude of value, however the pro-\n ductive power may change. But in the same period it supplies dif-\n ferent quantities of use-values, more when the productive\n power rises, less when it falls. In the former case it may happen\n that 2 coats contain less labour than formerly one.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"1.54","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0055","text":"The same change\n in productive power, which increases the fruitfulness of labour and hence the\n mass of use-values supplied by it, can therefore diminish the\n magnitude of value of the increased total mass itself,\n namely if it shortens the labour-time necessary for its production. Conversely,\n likewise.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"1.55","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056","text":"It follows from what has gone before that, although two dif-\n ferent kinds of labour are not lodged in the commodity, the same labour is determined differently\n and even oppositely, according as it is related to the use-\n value of the commodity as its product or to commodity-\n value as its mere objective expression.\n Just as the commodity must above all be an object of use in order to\n be value, so labour must above all be useful labour, purposively determined pro-\n ductive activity, in order to count as expenditure of human\n labour-power and hence as human labour simply\n speaking.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"1.56","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0057","text":"Since up to now only value-substance and magnitude of value have been determined, we now\n turn to the analysis of the form of value.","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"1.57","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0058","text":"Let us first return to the first form of appearance\n of commodity-value.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"1.58","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0059","text":"We take two quantities of commodities which cost the same amount of labour-time\n for their production, which therefore are magnitudes of equal value, and\n we have 40 yards of linen = 2 coats, or 40 yards of lin-\n en are worth two coats. We see that the value of the lin-\n en is expressed in a determinate quantity of coats. The\n value of a commodity, thus represented in the use-value of another\n commodity, is called its relative value.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"1.59","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0060","text":"The relative value of a commodity can change although its value\n remains constant. Conversely, its relative value can remain constant,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"14\", \"facs\": \"#f0033\"} --> although its value changes. The equation: 40 yards of linen\n = 2 coats, namely presupposes that both commodities cost the same amount of labour.\n But with every change in the productive power of the labours which bring them\n forth, the labour-time necessary for their production changes. Let us consider the influence of such changes upon relative\n value.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"1.60","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0061","text":"I. Let the value of linen change while coat-value remains con-\n stant. If the labour-time expended in the production of linen is dou-\n bled, perhaps as a consequence of increasing infertility of flax-\n bearing soil, its value doubles. Instead of 40 yards of lin-\n en = 2 coats, we would have: 40 yards of linen = 4 coats,\n since 2 coats now contain only half as much labour-time as 40 yards of lin-\n en. If, by contrast, the labour-time necessary for the production of linen\n decreases by one half, perhaps as a consequence of improved looms, then\n linen-value falls by one half. Accordingly now: 40 yards of\n linen = 1 coat. The relative value of commodity A, i.e.\n its value expressed in commodity B, thus rises and falls directly\n as the value of commodity A, the value of\n commodity B remaining unchanged.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"1.61","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0062","text":"II. Let the value of linen remain constant while coat-\n value changes. If under these circumstances the labour-time necessary for the pro-\n duction of the coat doubles, perhaps as a consequence of an unfavourable\n wool-shearing, then instead of 40 yards of linen = 2 coats we now have: 40 yar-\n ds of linen = 1 coat. If, by contrast, the value of the coat falls by\n one half, then 40 yards of linen = 4 coats. With the value of commodity A remaining unchanged,\n its relative value, expressed in commodity\n B, therefore falls or rises in inverse proportion to the\n change in value of B.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"1.62","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0063","text":"If one compares the different cases under I and II, it follows\n that the same change in relative value can arise from wholly op-\n posite causes. Thus from\n40 yards of linen = 2 coats there results 1) the equation 40 yards of\n linen = 4 coats, either because the value of linen\n doubles or because the value of the coats falls by one half, and 2) the\n equation 40 yards of linen = 1 coat, either because the value\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"15\", \"facs\": \"#f0034\"} --> of linen falls by one half or because the value of the coat rises to\n twice its amount.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"1.63","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0064","text":"III. The quantities of labour necessary for the production of linen and coat\n change simultaneously, in the same direction and in the same\n proportion. In this case, as before, 40 yards of linen =\n 2 coats, however their values may have changed. One discovers their\n change in value as soon as one compares them with a third commodity whose\n value remained constant. If the values of all commodities rose or fell simul-\n taneously and in the same proportion, their relative values\n would remain unchanged. Their real change in value would be evident from the fact\n that in the same labour-time a greater or smaller quantity of commodi-\n ties than before would now generally be supplied.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"1.64","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0065","text":"IV. The labour-times respectively necessary for the production of linen and coat,\n and hence their values, may change simultaneously in the same\n direction, but in unequal degree, or in opposite\n direction, and so on. The influence of all possible such combina-\n tions upon the relative value of a commodity follows simply from the ap-\n plication of cases I, II and III.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"1.65","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0066","text":"We have just investigated to what extent changes in the relative\n magnitude of value of a commodity, linen, reflect a change in its own\n magnitude of value, and have considered relative value at all\n only in its quantitative aspect. We now turn\n to its form. If relative value is the form of representation\n of value, the expression of the equivalence of two commodities, such as\n x commodity A = y commodity B or 20 yards of linen = 1 coat, is the sim-\n ple form of relative value.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"1.66","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0067","text":"I. First or simple form of relative value:\n 20 yards of linen = 1 coat. (x commodity A = y commodity B.)","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"1.67","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0068","text":"This form is somewhat difficult to analyse, because it is simple\n [Note: It is, as it were, the cell-form or, as Hegel would say, the\nin-itself of money.] . The distinct determinations contained in it are con-\n cealed, undeveloped, abstract, and therefore can only by some exertion of the\n power of abstraction be separated and held fast. So much is evident\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"16\", \"facs\": \"#f0035\"} --> at first glance, however, that the form remains the same whether 20 yards of\n linen = 1 coat or 20 yards of linen = x coats [Note: The few economists who, like J. Bailey, have concerned themselves with the analysis of the\n form of value, could arrive at no result, first because\n they confuse the form of value and value, secondly because, under the crude in-\n fluence of the practical citizen, they from the outset consider exclusively the quantitative de-\n termination.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"1.68","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0069","text":"“The command of quantity .... constitutes\nvalue”. (“Money and its Vicissitudes”. Lond. 1837, p. 11.) Author:\n J. Bailey.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"1.69","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0070","text":"Linen comes into the world in the form of a use-value\n or useful thing. Its stiff-linen corporeality or natural\n form is therefore not its value-form, but rather its direct op-\n posite. It initially shows its own being-value by relating itself\n to another commodity, the coat, as its equal. Were\n it not itself value, it could not relate to the coat as value,\n as its like. Qualitatively it equates itself with the coat\n by relating itself to it as objectification\n of homogeneous human labour, i.e. of its own\n value-substance, and it equates itself only with one coat instead\n of x coats because it is not only value in general but value of a de-\n terminate magnitude, while one coat contains precisely as much labour as\n 20 yards of linen.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"1.70","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0071","text":"Through this relation to the coat the\n linen kills several birds with one stone. In that it equates the other\n commodity with itself as value, it relates to itself\n as value. In that it relates to itself as value, it at the same time dis-\n tinguishes itself from itself as use-\n value. In that it expresses its magnitude of value—and magnitude of value\n is both value in general and quantitatively measured value—in the\n coat, it gives its being-value a value-form distinct from its im-\n mediate existence. In thus representing itself as something dif-\n ferentiated within itself, it first truly represents itself as\n commodity—useful thing which is at the same time value. Insofar as\n linen is use-value, it is an independent thing. Its\n value, on the other hand, appears only in relation to another commodity,\n the coat, e.g.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"1.71","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0072","text":"B., a relation in which the kind of commodity coat is qualita-\n tively equated with it and therefore, in a determinate quantity,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"17\", \"facs\": \"#f0036\"} --> counts as equal to it, replaces it, is exchangeable with it. Value therefore receives a form of its own, distinct from use-\n value, only through\n its representation as exchange-value.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"1.72","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0073","text":"The expression of the value of linen in the coat impresses upon the coat\n itself a new form. In fact, what does the value-form\n of linen say? That the coat is exchangeable with it. As it goes\n or lies, with skin and hair, in its natural form as coat, it now pos-\n sesses the form of immediate exchangeability with\n another commodity, the form of an exchangeable use-value or\n equivalent. The determination of the equivalent entails not only\n that a commodity is value in general, but that in its material\n form, in its use-form, it counts as value for another commodity\n and is therefore immediately present as exchange-value for the other\n commodity.","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"1.73","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0074","text":"As value, linen consists only of labour, constitutes a trans-\n parently crystallized jelly of labour. In reality, however, this crys-\n tal is very turbid. So far as labour is to be discovered in it, and not\n every commodity-body displays the trace of labour, it is not undifferentiated\n human labour, but weaving, spinning, etc., which by no means form\n its sole substance, but are rather intermingled with natural materials.\n In order to retain linen as a merely material expression of human labour,\n one must abstract from everything that really makes it into a thing.\n Objectivity of human labour, itself abstract, without further quality\n and content, is necessarily abstract objectivity, a thing of thought. Thus\n the flaxen fabric becomes a figment of the brain. But commodities are things.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"1.74","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0075","text":"What they are, they must\n be materially or display in their own material relations. In\n the production of linen a definite quantity of human\n labour-power has been expended. Its value is the merely objective\n reflection of the labour thus expended, but it is not reflected\n in its body. It reveals itself, receives sensuous expression\n through its value-relation to the coat. By equating itself with it\n as value, while at the same time distinguishing itself from it as a use-\n object, the coat becomes the form of appearance of linen-value, as opposed to\n the [Running matter: I. 2]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"18\", \"facs\": \"#f0037\"} --> linen-body, its value-form as distinct from its natural\n form [Note: Thus one speaks of the coat-value of linen when one represents its\n value in coats, of its grain-value when one represents it in grain,\n etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"1.75","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0076","text":"Every such expression says that it is its value which appears in the use-\n values coat, grain, etc.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"1.76","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0077","text":"In the relative expression of value: 20 ells of linen = 1 coat\n or x linen is y coat-value, the coat counts indeed only as value\n or jelly of labour, but precisely thereby the jelly of labour counts as coat,\n the coat as the form in which human labour congeals [Note: In a certain way it is the same with man as with the commodity. Since he comes\n into the world neither with a mirror nor as a Fichtean philosopher: I am I,\n man at first mirrors himself only in another man. Only through the\n relation to the man Paul as his equal does the man\n Peter relate himself to himself as man. But thereby Paul too, with skin\n and hair, in his Pauline corporeality, counts for him as the form of appearance of the genus\n man.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"1.77","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0078","text":"The use-\n value coat becomes the form of appearance of linen-value only\n because linen relates to the coat-material as the immediate\n materiality of abstract human labour, thus\n labour of the same kind as that objectified in itself. The\n object coat counts for it as sensuously palpable objectivity\n of homogeneous human labour, hence as value in natural form. Since\n it is, as value, of the same essence as the coat, the natural form\n coat thus becomes the form of appearance of its own value. But the labour represented in the use-\n value coat is not human labour pure and simple, but a definite,\n useful labour, tailoring labour. Human labour pure and simple, expenditure of human labour-power,\n is indeed capable of every determination, but in and for itself indeterminate.","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"1.78","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0079","text":"It can be actu-\n alized, objectified, only once human\n labour-power is expended in a definite form, as definite\n labour, for only definite labour has a natural material confronting\n it, an external material in which it objectifies itself. Only the\n Hegelian “Concept” succeeds in objectifying itself without external material [Note: “The Concept, which is at first only subjective, proceeds, without requiring] .","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"1.79","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0080","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"19\", \"facs\": \"#f0038\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"1.80","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0081","text":"Linen cannot relate to the coat as value or incar-\n nate human labour without relating to tailoring\n labour as the immediate form of actualization of human\n labour. Yet what interests linen in the use-\n value coat is neither its woollen comfortableness nor its buttoned\n nature, nor any other useful quality that stamps it as a\n use-value. It serves it only to represent its value-objectivity as distinct\n from its stiff-linen use-objectivity. It would have attained the same purpose\n had it expressed its value in asa foetida or poudrette or boot-blacking.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"1.81","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0082","text":"Tailoring\n labour therefore likewise counts for it not insofar as it is purposive-\n ly productive activity, useful labour, but only insofar as it is,\n as definite labour, a form of actualization, a mode of objecti-\n fication of human labour in general. If linen expressed its value in boot-blacking\n instead of in the coat, then, instead of tailoring, blacking too would count for it as the immediate form of actualization\n of abstract human labour [Note: Namely, insofar as one popularly calls the preparation of blacking itself “blacking”.] . Form of appearance of value or\n equivalent, then, does a use-value or commodity-body become only\n because another commodity relates to the concrete,\n useful kind of labour contained in it as the immediate actualization of abstract\n human labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"1.82","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0083","text":"Here we stand at the crucial point of all the difficulties that\n hinder the understanding of the value-form. It is relatively easy to distinguish\n the value of the commodity from its use-value, or the labour\n forming use-value from the same labour insofar as it is merely calculated\n as expenditure of human labour-power in commodity-value. One considers commodity or labour in one form,\n not in the other, and vice versa. These abstract opposites fall\n apart of themselves and are therefore easy to keep apart. Differ-\n [Note: external material or stuff, to objectify itself in accordance with its own activity.” Hegel, “Logic” p. 367 in the “Encyclo-\n paedia: First Part.","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"1.83","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0084","text":"Berlin 1840.”]\n [Running matter: 2*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"20\", \"facs\": \"#f0039\"} --> ent with the value-form, which exists only in the relation of commodity to commodity.\n Use-value or commodity-body here plays a new\n role. It becomes the form of appearance of commodity-value, thus of\n its own opposite. Likewise the concrete useful labour contained in use-value\n becomes its own opposite, the mere form of actualization of\n abstract human labour. Instead of falling apart,\n the opposed determinations of the commodity here reflect themselves in\n one another. However strange this may seem at first sight, on fur-\n ther reflection it proves necessary. The commodity is from the outset a\n twofold thing, use-value and value, product of useful\n labour and abstract jelly of labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"1.84","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0085","text":"In order to represent itself as\n what it is, it must therefore duplicate its form. It possesses\n the form of a use-value by nature. It is its natural form.\n It acquires value-form only in intercourse with other commodities. But its\n value-form must itself again be objective form. The only\n objective forms of commodities are their use-shapes,\n their natural forms. Since now the natural form of a commodity, of linen\n for example, is the very opposite of its value-form, it must make\n another natural form, the natural form of another commodity, into its\nvalue-form. What it cannot do immediately for itself, it\n can do immediately for another commodity and therefore, by a detour, for itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"1.85","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0086","text":"It cannot express its value in its own body or in\n its own use-value, but it can relate to another\n use-value or commodity-body as immediate existence of value.\n It cannot relate to the concrete labour contained in itself, but can relate to that contained\n in another kind of commodity as the mere form of actualization\n of abstract human labour. To do this it need only equate the\n other commodity with itself as equivalent. The use-\n value of one commodity exists at all for another commodity only insofar as it\n serves in this way as the form of appearance of its value.","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"1.86","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0087","text":"If, in the simple relative expression of value: x commodity A = y commodity\n B, one considers only the quantitative relation, one finds only the laws\n developed above concerning the movement of relative value, all of which\n rest upon the fact that the magnitude of value of commodities is determined\n by the labour-time necessary for their pro-\n duction. But if one considers the value-relation of the two commodities\n in its qualitative aspect,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"21\", \"facs\": \"#f0040\"} --> one discovers in that simple expression of value the secret of the\n value-form and therefore, in nuce, of money [Note: It is scarcely surprising that the economists, wholly under the influence\n of material interests, overlook the formal content of the relative expression of value,\n when before Hegel the professional logicians even overlooked the formal content of the\n paradigms of judgment and inference.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"1.87","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0088","text":"Our analysis has shown that the relative expression of value\n of a commodity includes two different forms of value.\n Linen expresses its value and its definite magnitude of value\n in the coat. It represents its value in the value-relation to\n another commodity, hence as exchange-value. On the other hand, the other\n commodity, the coat, in which it expresses its value relatively, receives\n precisely thereby the form of a use-\n value or equivalent immediately exchangeable with it. Both forms, the relative form of value\n of the one commodity, the equivalent form of the other, are forms of\nexchange-value. Both are in fact only moments, determinations mutually\n conditioned through one another, of the same relative\n expression of value, but distributed polarly over the two equated\ncommodity-extremes.","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"1.88","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0089","text":"Quantitative determinacy is not included in the equivalent form\n of a commodity. The definite relation, for example, in which the\n coat is equivalent of linen, does not arise from its equiva-\n lent form, the form of its immediate exchangeability\n with linen, but from the determination of magnitude of value by\n labour-time. Linen can represent its own value in coats only\n by relating itself to a definite quantity of coats as a given\n quantity of crystallized human labour. If the value of the coat changes,\n this relation changes too. But for the relative value of linen to change,\n it must be present, and it can only be formed given a coat-value.","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"1.89","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0090","text":"Whether linen\n now represents its own value in 1, 2, or x coats depends, under this\n presupposition, wholly on the magnitude of value of an ell of linen and on the\n number of ells whose value is to be represented in coat-form. The\nmagnitude of value of a commodity can express itself only in the use-value of\n another commodity, as relative value. Conversely, a commodity receives\n the form of a\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"22\", \"facs\": \"#f0041\"} --> use-value or equivalent immediately exchangeable only as the material\n in which the value of another commodity is expressed.","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"1.90","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0091","text":"This distinction is clouded by a characteristic peculiarity\n of the relative expression of value in its simple or first\n form. The equation: 20 ells of linen = 1 coat, or\n 20 ells of linen are worth one coat, evidently includes\n the identical equation: 1 coat = 20 ells of linen, or\n 1 coat is worth 20 ells of linen. The relative expression of value of\n linen, in which the coat figures as equivalent, thus contains in re-\n turn the relative expression of value of the coat, in which linen\n figures as equivalent.","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"1.91","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0092","text":"Although both determinations of the value-form or both modes of repre-\n senting commodity-value as exchange-value are only relative,\n both do not seem relative in the same degree. In the relative\n value of linen: 20 ells of linen = 1 coat, the exchange-\n value of linen is expressly represented as its relation to another\n commodity. The coat, for its part, is indeed also only equiva-\n lent insofar as linen relates to it as the form of appearance of its own\n value and hence as immediately exchangeable with it. Only\nwithin this relation is it equivalent. But it behaves\n passively. It takes no initiative. It finds itself in relation because\n relation is made to it. The character that accrues to it from the relation\n with linen therefore appears not as the result of its re-\n lation, but as present without its intervention. Still more.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"1.92","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0093","text":"The\nspecific manner in which the linen relates to him\nis quite made to “affect” him, however modest he may be\nand by no means the product of a “tailor run mad with pride.” The\nlinen relates, namely, to the coat as sensuously existing ma-\nterialization of human labour in the abstract and therefore as an existing\nbody of value. It is this only because and insofar as the linen relates to it\nin this specific manner. Its being an equivalent\nis, so to speak, only a reflective determination of the linen.\nBut the very reverse appears to be the case. On the one hand, it does not\nitself take the trouble to relate itself. On the other hand, the linen relates to it,\nnot in order to make it into something, but because it is something without it.","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"1.93","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0094","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"23\", \"facs\": \"#f0042\"} --> The finished product of the linen’s relation to the coat, its\nequivalent form, its determinacy as immediately exchangeable use-\nvalue, therefore seems to belong to it objectively outside its relation to\nthe linen, quite like, for instance, its property of\nkeeping warm. In the first or simple form of relative\nvalue: 20 yards of linen = 1 coat, this false appearance is not yet\nconfirmed, because it also immediately states the opposite,\nthat the coat is the equivalent of the linen and that each of the two com-\nmodities possesses this determinacy only because and insofar as the other makes it\nits expression of relative value [Note: With such reflective determinations generally, there is a peculiar matter.\nThis man, for example, is king only because other men behave as subjects\ntowards\nhim.","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"1.94","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0095","text":"Conversely, they believe themselves to be subjects because he is king.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"1.95","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0096","text":"In the simple form of relative value, or the expression of the\nequivalence of two commodities, the development of the form of value is\nuniform for both commodities, although each time in opposite di-\nrections. The relative expression of value is furthermore uniform\nwith reference to each of the two commodities, for the linen\nrepresents its value only in one commodity, the coat, and vice versa,\nbut for both commodities this expression of value is double, different\nfor each of them. Finally, each of the two commodities is an equiva-\nlent only for the other single kind of commodity, thus only an individual equi-\nvalent.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"1.96","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0097","text":"Such an equation as 20 yards of linen = 1 coat, or\ntwenty yards of linen are worth one coat, obviously expresses the\nvalue of the commodity only in a quite restricted and one-sided way. If I compare\nlinen, for example, instead of with coats, with other commodities, I obtain\nother relative expressions of value as well, other equations,\nsuch as 20 yards of linen = u coffee, 20 yards of linen = v tea\netc. Linen has just as many different relative\nexpressions of value as there are different commodities from it, and the\nnumber of its relative expressions of value constantly grows with the number of newly\nappearing kinds of commodities [Note: “The value of any commodity denoting its relation in exchange, we may\nspeak of it as .... cornvalue, clothvalue, according to the commodity with which] .","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"1.97","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0098","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"24\", \"facs\": \"#f0043\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"1.98","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0099","text":"The first form 20 yards of linen = 1 coat gave two\nrelative expressions for the value of two commodities. This second\nform gives, for the value of the same commodity, the most motley mosaic\nof relative expressions. Nor does anything seem to have been gained either\nfor the expression of the magnitude of value, for in 20 yards of linen = 1 coat\nthe magnitude of value of the linen, which indeed remains the same in every expression,\nis represented just as exhaustively as in 20 yards of linen = u tea\netc.; nor for the formal determination of the equivalent, for in\n20 yards of linen = u coffee etc., coffee etc. are only individual\nequivalents, just as the coat was.","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"1.99","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0100","text":"Nevertheless, this second form contains an essential further development.\nFor it implies not only that linen expresses its value accidentally\nnow in coats, now in coffee etc., but that it expresses it\nboth in coats and in coffee etc., either in\nthis commodity or that or the third etc. The further determi-\nnation becomes apparent as soon as this second or expanded form of the rela-\ntive expression of value is represented in its connexion.\nWe then obtain:","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"1.100","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0101","text":"II. Second or expanded form of relative value:","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"1.101","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0102","text":"20 yards of linen = 1 coat or = u coffee or = v tea or\n= x iron or = y wheat or = etc. etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"1.102","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0103","text":"z commodity A = u commodity B or = v commodity C or = w commodity D\nor = x commodity E or = y commodity F or = etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"1.103","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0104","text":"First of all, the first form evidently constitutes the basic element of\nthe\n[Note: it is compared; and then there are a thousand different kinds of value,\nas many kinds of value as there are commodities in existence, and all are equally\nreal and equally nominal.“ („A Critical Dissertation on the Nature,\nMeasure and Causes of Value: chiefly in reference to the writings\nof Mr. Ricardo and his followers. By the Author of Essays on the\nFormation etc. of Opinions. London 1825“, p. 39). S. Bailey, the\nauthor of this anonymous writing, which in its time made much stir in England,\nimagines that through this reference to the motley relative expressions\nof the same commodity-value he has destroyed every conceptual determination of value.","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"1.104","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0105","text":"That he, moreover, despite his own narrow-mindedness, sounded out weak points in Ricardian\ntheory was proved by the irritability with which the Ricardian school\nattacked him, for example in the Westminster Review.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"25\", \"facs\": \"#f0044\"} --> second, for the latter consists of many simple relative expressions of value,\nsuch as 20 yards of linen = 1 coat, 20 yards of linen =\nu coffee etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"1.105","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0106","text":"In the first form: 20 yards of linen = 1 coat, it may\nseem an accidental fact that these two commodities are exchangeable in this specific\nquantitative proportion. In the\nsecond form, by contrast, a background essentially distinct from the accidental appearance\nand determining it at once becomes evident. The\nvalue of the linen remains equally great, whether represented in coat or coffee or\niron etc., in innumerably different commodities belonging to the most diver-\nse possessors. The accidental relation of two individual\ncommodity owners disappears. It becomes apparent that it is not exchange\nthat regulates the magnitude of value of the commodity, but conversely the magnitude of value of\nthe commodity that regulates its exchange relations.","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"1.106","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0107","text":"In the expression: 20 yards of linen = 1 coat, the\ncoat counted as the form of appearance of the labour objectified\nin the linen. Thus the labour contained in the linen was equated with that\ncontained in the coat and hence determined as homogeneous human labour.\nYet this determination did not emerge explicitly.\nThe first form directly equates the labour contained in the linen\nonly with tailoring labour. The second form is different. In the end-\nless, ever-extendable series of its relative expressions of value,\nthe linen relates to all possible commodity bodies as mere forms of appear-\nance of the labour contained in itself. Here, therefore, the linen-\nvalue is for the first time truly represented as value, i.e. as a crystal\nof human labour in general.","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"1.107","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0108","text":"The second form consists of a sum of nothing but equations\nof the first form. But each of these equations, such as 20 yards of linen\n= 1 coat, also includes the reverse relation: 1 coat = 20 yards\nof linen, where the coat represents its value in linen and\nprecisely thereby linen as equivalent. Since this applies to\neach of the innumerable relative expressions of value of linen,\nwe obtain:","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"1.108","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0109","text":"III. Third, inverted or back-referred second\nform of relative value:","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"1.109","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0110","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"26\", \"facs\": \"#f0045\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"1.110","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0111","text":"1 coat = 20 yards of linen.","kind":"passage","sequence":111,"address":"1.111","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0112","text":"u coffee = 20 yards of linen.","kind":"passage","sequence":112,"address":"1.112","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0113","text":"v tea = 20 yards of linen.","kind":"passage","sequence":113,"address":"1.113","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0114","text":"x iron = 20 yards of linen.","kind":"passage","sequence":114,"address":"1.114","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0115","text":"y wheat = 20 yards of linen.","kind":"passage","sequence":115,"address":"1.115","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0116","text":"etc. = 20 yards of linen.","kind":"passage","sequence":116,"address":"1.116","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0117","text":"The relative expression of value of commodities here returns\n in its original form: 1 coat = 20 yards of linen. However,\n this simple equation is now further developed. Originally it con-\n tained only that the value of the coat, through its expression in an-\n other commodity, receives a form distinct from and independent of the use-value coat or the coat-\n body itself. Now the same form represents the coat as value also in relation to all other commodities and is therefore its universally valid form of value. Not only the coat, but coffee, iron, wheat, in short all\n other commodities now express their value in the material of linen.\n They thus present themselves to one another as the same materiality\n of human labour. They are only quantitatively dif-\n ferent, which is why 1 coat, u coffee, x iron, etc., i.e.","kind":"passage","sequence":117,"address":"1.117","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0118","text":"dif-\n ferent quantities of these different things = 20 yards of linen,\n equal to the same quantity of objectified human labour.\n Through their common expression of value in the material of linen,\nall commodities thus distinguish themselves as exchange-values from their\n own use-values and at the same time relate to one another\n as magnitudes of value, posit themselves as qualitatively equal and com-\n pare themselves quantitatively. Only in this unified relative\n expression of value do they all appear to one another as values, and only then does\n their value receive its corresponding phenomenal form as\n exchange-value. In distinction from the expanded form of rela-\n tive value (Form II), which represents the value of one commodity in the compass of all\n other commodities, we call this unified expression of value\n the general relative form of value.","kind":"passage","sequence":118,"address":"1.118","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0119","text":"In Form II: 20 yards of linen = 1 coat or = u coffee\nor = v tea or = x iron, etc., in which linen unfolds its\n relative expression of value, it relates to each individual\n commodity, coat, coffee, etc., as a particular equivalent and\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"27\", \"facs\": \"#f0046\"} --> to all of them together as the compass of its particular equivalent-\n forms. In relation to it no individual kind of commodity yet counts as an equiva-\n lent simply as such, as in the individual equivalent, but only as a par-\n ticular equivalent, one of which excludes the other. In\n Form III, which is the reflected-back second form and is therefore con-\n tained within it, linen, by contrast, appears as the generic form\n of the equivalent for all other commodities.","kind":"passage","sequence":119,"address":"1.119","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0120","text":"It is as though alongside and apart from\n lions, tigers, hares, and all the other actual animals, which when grouped\n form the different genera, species, subspecies, families, etc. of the\n animal kingdom, there also existed the animal, the individual incar-\n nation of the whole animal kingdom. Such a singular thing, which in itself\n comprises all actually existing species of the same thing, is a univer-\n sal, such as animal, God, etc. Just as linen therefore became an individual\n equivalent because another commodity related to it as an\n phenomenal form of value, so it becomes, as the form of value common\n to all commodities, the general equiva-\n lent, general value-body, general materiality of\n abstract human labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":120,"address":"1.120","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0121","text":"The particular labour materialized in it there-\n fore now counts as the general form of realization\n of human labour, as general labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":121,"address":"1.121","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0122","text":"In the representation of the value of commodity A in commodity B, by means of which\n commodity B becomes an individual equiva-\n lent, it was immaterial which particular sort commodity B was. Only the bodi-\n liness of commodity B had to be of a different kind from that of commodity A, and\n thus also the product of different useful labour. In representing its\n value in linen, the coat related to linen as realized\n human labour, and precisely thereby to linen-\n weaving as the form of realization of human\n labour, but the particular determinacy that distinguishes linen-weaving from\n other kinds of labour was entirely immaterial. It\n merely had to be of a different kind from tailoring and otherwise a\n definite kind of labour. It is different as soon as linen becomes the general\n equivalent.","kind":"passage","sequence":122,"address":"1.122","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0123","text":"This use-value in its particular\n determinacy, by which it is linen in distinction from all other\n kinds of commodities, coffee, iron, etc., now becomes the general form of value\n of all other commodities and hence the general equivalent. The\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"28\", \"facs\": \"#f0047\"} --> particular useful kind of labour represented in it now counts as the\n general form of realization of human la-\n bour, as general labour, precisely insofar as it is labour of particular\n determinacy, linen-weaving in distinction not only from tailor-\n ing, but from coffee cultivation, mining labour, and all other kinds of labour.\n Conversely, all other kinds of labour, in the relative\n expression of value of linen, the general equivalent (Form\n II), count only as particular forms of realization of human\n labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":123,"address":"1.123","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0124","text":"As values, commodities are expressions of the same unity,\n of abstract human labour. In the form of exchange-value\n they appear to one another as values and relate to one another\nas values. In doing so, they at the same time relate to abstract human\n labour as their common social substance.\n Their social relation consists exclusively in counting to one\n another as merely quantitatively different, but qualitatively equal and therefore\n mutually replaceable and exchangeable expressions of this\n their social substance. As a useful thing, a commodity possesses social\n determinacy insofar as it is use-value for others outside its owner, that is, satis-\n fies social needs.","kind":"passage","sequence":124,"address":"1.124","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0125","text":"But regardless of whose needs its useful proper-\n ties relate to, through them it is always only an object related to human\n needs, not a commodity for\nother commodities. Only that which transforms mere objects of use into commodities\n can relate them to one another as commodities and thus place them in\nsocial rapport. But this is their value.\n The form in which they count as values, as jellies of human labour,\nis therefore their social form. The social\n form of the commodity and the value-form or form of ex-\n changeability are thus one and the same. If the natural form of a\n commodity is at the same time value-form, it possesses the form of immediate\n exchangeability with other commodities and hence an immediately\n social form.","kind":"passage","sequence":125,"address":"1.125","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0126","text":"The simple relative form of value (Form I) 1 coat =\n 20 yards of linen differs from the general rela-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"29\", \"facs\": \"#f0048\"} --> tive form of value 1 coat = 20 yards of linen only in that\n this equation now forms one member of the series","kind":"passage","sequence":126,"address":"1.126","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0127","text":"1 coat = 20 yards of linen","kind":"passage","sequence":127,"address":"1.127","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0128","text":"u coffee = 20 yards of linen","kind":"passage","sequence":128,"address":"1.128","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0129","text":"v tea = 20 yards of linen","kind":"passage","sequence":129,"address":"1.129","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0130","text":"etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":130,"address":"1.130","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0131","text":"It thus differs in fact only in that linen\n has been further developed from an individual into a general equiva-\n lent. Thus, if in the simple relative expression of value it is not\n the commodity that expresses its magnitude of value, but the commodity\nin which magnitude of value is expressed, that receives the form of immediate\n exchangeability, the equivalent-form, and hence the immediately social\n form, the same holds for the general relative\n expression of value. But in the simple relative form of value this\n difference is still merely formal and evanescent.","kind":"passage","sequence":131,"address":"1.131","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0132","text":"When in 1 coat =\n 20 yards of linen the coat expresses its value relatively, namely in linen,\n and linen thereby receives equivalent-form, the same equation immediately\n includes the reverse relation: 20 yards of lin-\n en = 1 coat, in which the coat receives equivalent-form and the\n value of linen is relatively expressed. This uniform and reciprocal\n development of the value-form of both commodities as relative\n value and as equivalent no longer takes place now. If the gen-\n eral relative form of value 1 coat = 20 yards of linen, where linen is\n the general equivalent, is reversed into 20 yards of linen =\n 1 coat, the coat thereby becomes not a general equivalent for all\n other commodities, but only a particular equivalent of linen.\n The relative form of value of the coat is general only because it is at the same time\n the relative form of value of all other commodities.","kind":"passage","sequence":132,"address":"1.132","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0133","text":"What applies to the coat applies to\n coffee, etc. It therefore follows that the general relative form of value\n of commodities excludes them themselves from the general equivalent-form.\n Conversely, as soon as a commodity, such as linen, possesses the general\n equivalent-form, it is excluded from the general relative form of value.\n The general relative form of value of linen, unified with the other commodities, would be: 20 yards of linen = 20 yards of lin-\n en. But this is a tautology, which does not express the magnitude of value of this\n commodity, which is in general equivalent-form and therefore in a form\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"30\", \"facs\": \"#f0049\"} --> of constant exchangeability. Rather, the expanded\n relative form of value: 20 yards of linen = 1 coat or = u coffee\nor = v tea or = etc. now becomes the specific relative\n expression of value of the general equivalent.","kind":"passage","sequence":133,"address":"1.133","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0134","text":"In the general relative expression of value of commodities, every\n commodity, coat, coffee, tea, etc., possesses a form of value distinct from its natural form,\n namely, the form linen. And precisely in this\n form they relate to one another as exchangeable and exchangeable in quantitatively\n determinate proportions, for if 1 coat = 20 yards\n of linen, u coffee = 20 yards of linen, etc., then 1 coat\n = u coffee, etc. In that all commodities mirror themselves as magnitudes of value in one and the same\n commodity, they mutually reflect one another as magnitudes of value. But the natural forms which they possess as objects of use\n count for one another only by this detour, thus\n not immediately as phenomenal forms of value. As soon as they are immedi-\n ate, they are therefore not immediately exchangeable.","kind":"passage","sequence":134,"address":"1.134","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0135","text":"They therefore possess\n not the form of immediate exchangeability for one another,\n or their socially valid form is a mediated one.\n Conversely, in that all other commodities relate to linen as a phenomenal\n form of value, the natural form of linen becomes the\nform of its immediate exchangeability with all commodi-\n ties, and hence immediately its universally social\n form.","kind":"passage","sequence":135,"address":"1.135","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0136","text":"A commodity receives the general equivalent-form only\n because and insofar as it serves all other commodities for the representation of their gen-\n eral relative, and hence non-immediate, form of value.\n But commodities must give themselves relative form of value at all,\n because their natural forms are only their use-value forms, and they must give themselves a uni-\n fied, and hence general, relative form of value, in order to relate all of them to one another\n as values, as homogeneous jellies of human labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":136,"address":"1.136","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0137","text":"A commodity is therefore in the form of immedi-\n ate exchangeability with all other commodities, and hence in immediately\n social form, only because and insofar as all other commodities do\nnot find themselves in it, or because the commodity as such is from the outset\nnot in immediately exchangeable or social form,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"31\", \"facs\": \"#f0050\"} --> since its immediate form is the form of its use-value, not of its\n value.","kind":"passage","sequence":137,"address":"1.137","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0138","text":"One certainly cannot see from the form of general immediate ex-\n changeability that it is an opposed form of commodity, as insepar-\n able from the form of non-immediate exchange-\n ability as the positivity of one magnetic pole is from the\n negativity of the other. One may therefore imagine that one can stamp all\n commodities at once with the mark of immediate exchangeability,\n just as one may imagine that one can make all workers into capi-\n talists.","kind":"passage","sequence":138,"address":"1.138","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0139","text":"In fact, however, the general relative form of val-\n ue and the general equivalent-form are the opposed, mutually\n presupposing and mutually repelling poles of the same social form\n of commodities [Note: For the petty bourgeois, who sees in the form of commodity production the nec plus\n ultra of human freedom and individual independence, it would naturally be highly desirable\n to be rid at the same time of the inconveniences connected with this form, espe-\n cially also of the non-immediate ex-\n changeability of commodities. The elaboration of this philistine utopia constitutes Proud-\n hon’s socialism, which, as I have shown elsewhere, does not even possess the merit of\n originality, but was, long before him, developed far bet-\n ter by Bray, Gray, and others.","kind":"passage","sequence":139,"address":"1.139","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0140","text":"This does not prevent such wisdom from proliferating in France nowadays\n under the name of “science.” Never has a school brandished the word “science” more\n than the Proudhonian, for\n“where concepts are lacking,\n At the proper time a word steps in.”] .","kind":"passage","sequence":140,"address":"1.140","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0141","text":"As the directly social materialization of\n labour, linen is the universal equivalent, materialization\n of directly social labour, whereas the other\n commodity bodies, which represent their value in linen, are materializations\nof not directly social labours.","kind":"passage","sequence":141,"address":"1.141","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0142","text":"In fact, all use-values are commodities because they are products\n of private labours independent of one another, private labours\n which, however, as particular, albeit self-subsistent, members of the naturally\n grown system of the division of labour, materially depend on one another.\n They are thus socially connected precisely through their\n difference, their particular usefulness. For this very rea-\n son they produce qualitatively different use-values. If\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"32\", \"facs\": \"#f0051\"} --> not, these use-values would not become commodities for one another.\n On the other hand, this different useful quality does not yet make products\n into commodities. If a peasant family produces for its own\n consumption coat and linen and wheat, then these things confront the fa-\n mily as different products of its family labour, but not\n reciprocally as commodities to themselves.","kind":"passage","sequence":142,"address":"1.142","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143","text":"If labour were directly\n social, i.e. common labour, then the pro-\n ducts would receive the directly social character of a common product\n for their producers, but not the character of commodities for one another.\n Meanwhile, we need not look far here for what the social\n form of the private labours contained in commodities and independent of\none another consists in. It already emerged from the analysis of the\n commodity. Their social form is their relation to one another as\nequal labour, hence, since the equality of toto coelo different\n labours can consist only in an abstraction from their inequality,\n their relation to one another as human labour\n in general, expenditures of human labour-power,\n which all human labours, whatever their content and their mode of opera-\n tion, in fact are.","kind":"passage","sequence":143,"address":"1.143","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0144","text":"In every social form of labour\n the labours of the different individuals are also related to one another\n as human, but here this relation itself counts as the\nspecifically social form of the labours. Now, however,\n none of these private labours in their natural form possesses this specifically so-\n cial form of abstract human labour, any more than the\n commodity in its natural form possesses the social form of mere labour-\n jelly, or of value. But through the fact that the natural form\n of a commodity, here linen, becomes the universal equivalent form, because\n all other commodities relate to it as the form of appearance of their own\n value, weaving too becomes the universal form of reali-\n zation of abstract human labour, or labour in directly\n social form.","kind":"passage","sequence":144,"address":"1.144","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0145","text":"The standard of “sociality” must\n be derived from the nature of the relations peculiar to each mode of production,\n not from conceptions foreign to it. As was shown above,\n that the commodity by nature excludes the direct form of universal ex-\n changeability and can therefore develop the universal equivalent form only\nantithetically, the same holds for the private labours\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"33\", \"facs\": \"#f0052\"} --> contained in commodities.","kind":"passage","sequence":145,"address":"1.145","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0146","text":"Since they are not directly so-\n cial labour, first, the social form is a form distinct from\n the natural forms of the actual useful labours, foreign to\n them, and abstract; and second, all kinds of pri-\n vate labour receive their social character only antithetically,\n by all being equated to one exclusive kind of private labour, here linen-weav-\n ing. Thereby the latter becomes the direct\n and universal form of appearance of abstract human labour, and thus\n labour in directly social form. It therefore also presents itself\n directly in a socially valid and universally exchange-\n able product.","kind":"passage","sequence":146,"address":"1.146","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0147","text":"The appearance that the equivalent form of a commodity springs from its own\n material nature, instead of being merely a reflection of the relations of the oth-\n er commodities to it, becomes consolidated with the development of the individual\n equivalent into the universal one, because the antithetical moments of the\n value-form no longer develop uniformly for the commodities related\n to one another, because the universal equivalent form separates one commodity\n as something quite apart from all other commodities, and finally because\n this form of it is in fact no longer the product of the relation of any\n single other commodity.","kind":"passage","sequence":147,"address":"1.147","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0148","text":"Meanwhile, at our present standpoint the universal equiva-\n lent is by no means yet ossified. How was linen in fact\n transformed into the universal equivalent? By first representing its value\n in a single commodity (Form I), then in all other commodities successively\n (Form II), and thus, retroactively, all\n other commodities representing their values relatively in it (Form III). The\n simple relative expression of value was the germ from which the universal\n equivalent form of linen developed. Within this development\n it changes its role. It begins by representing its magnitude of\n value in another commodity, and ends by serving as the material for the value-\n expression of all other commodities. What holds of linen holds\n of every commodity.","kind":"passage","sequence":148,"address":"1.148","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0149","text":"In its developed relative expression of value (Form\n II), which consists only of its many simple expressions of value,\n linen does not yet figure as universal equivalent. Rather,\n here every other commodity body forms its equivalent, is therefore di-\n rectly exchangeable with it, and can thus exchange places with it.","kind":"passage","sequence":149,"address":"1.149","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0150","text":"[Running matter: I. 3]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"34\", \"facs\": \"#f0053\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":150,"address":"1.150","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0151","text":"Thus we finally obtain:","kind":"passage","sequence":151,"address":"1.151","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0152","text":"Form IV:","kind":"passage","sequence":152,"address":"1.152","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0153","text":"20 ells of linen = 1 coat or = u coffee or = v tea or\n = x iron or = y wheat or = etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":153,"address":"1.153","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0154","text":"1 coat = 20 ells of linen or = u coffee or = v tea or\n = x iron or = y wheat or = etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":154,"address":"1.154","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0155","text":"u coffee = 20 ells of linen or = 1 coat or = v tea or\n = x iron or = y wheat or = etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":155,"address":"1.155","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0156","text":"v tea = etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":156,"address":"1.156","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0157","text":"But each of these equations, read back, yields coat, coffee,\n tea, etc. as universal equivalent, hence the expression of value in\n coat, coffee, tea, etc. as the universal relative value-form of all other\n commodities. The universal equivalent form always belongs only to one commodity\n in opposition to all other commodities; but it belongs to every commodity\n in opposition to all others. Yet if every commodity sets its own na-\n tural form against all other commodities as universal equivalent form,\n then all commodities exclude all from the universal equivalent form, and\n therefore themselves from the socially valid representation of their\n magnitudes of value.","kind":"passage","sequence":157,"address":"1.157","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0158","text":"One sees: the analysis of the commodity yields all the essential\n determinations of the value-form and the value-form itself in its antitheti-\n cal moments, the universal relative value-form, the\nuniversal equivalent form, finally the never concluding\nseries of simple relative expressions of value, which first forms a\n transitional phase in the development of the value-form, in order finally\n to turn into the specifically relative value-form of the universal\n equivalent. But the analysis of the commodity yielded these\n forms as commodity-forms in general, which therefore also belong to every commodity,\n only antithetically, so that when commodity A is in the\none formal determination, commodities B, C, etc. assume the other in op-\n position to it. But the decisively important matter was to discover the\n inner necessary connection between value-form, value-sub-\n stance, and magnitude of value, i.e.","kind":"passage","sequence":158,"address":"1.158","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0159","text":"expressed ideally, to\n prove that the value-form springs from the concept of value [Note: It is one of the fundamental defects of classical political economy that\n it never succeeded, from the analysis of the commodity and more specifically of commodity-value] .","kind":"passage","sequence":159,"address":"1.159","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0160","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"35\", \"facs\": \"#f0054\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":160,"address":"1.160","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0161","text":"A commodity appears at first sight to be a self-evident,\n trivial thing. Its analysis yields that it is a very intricate thing,\n full of metaphysical subtleties and theological niceties. As a\n mere use-value it is a sensuous thing, in which there is nothing mys-\n terious, whether I consider it from the standpoint that its\n properties satisfy human needs, or that it receives these properties only as a\nproduct of human labour. There is ab-\n solutely nothing enigmatic in the fact that man by his activity\n changes the forms of natural materials in a manner useful to him.\n The form of wood, for example, is changed when one makes a table out of it.\n Nevertheless, the table remains wood, an ordinary sen-\n [Note: to discover the form of value, which precisely makes it exchange-value.","kind":"passage","sequence":161,"address":"1.161","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","text":"Precisely in its best representatives, such as A. Smith and Ricardo, it treats\n the value-form as something wholly indifferent or external to the nature of the commodity itself.\n The reason is not solely that the analysis of the magnitude of value\n entirely absorbs its attention. It lies deeper. The value-form of the\n product of labour is the most abstract, but also the most general form of the bour-\n geois mode of production, which is thereby characterized as a particular kind of so-\n cial mode of production and hence at the same time historically.\n If it is therefore regarded as the eternal natural form of social pro-\n duction, one necessarily also overlooks the specific character of the value-form, hence\n of the commodity-form, further developed, of the money-form, capital-form, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":162,"address":"1.162","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0163","text":"Thus among economists who entirely agree concerning the measure of the magnitude of value by\n labour-time, one finds the most motley and contradictory conceptions of money, i.e. of the finished\n shape of the universal equivalent. This emerges strikingly, for example, in the treatment of banking,\n where the commonplace definitions of money no longer suffice. In op-\n position, there therefore arose a restored mercantile system (Ganilh etc.),\n which sees in value only the social form, or rather only\n its unsubstantial appearance. — To note once and for all, by classical political\n economy I understand all economics since W.","kind":"passage","sequence":163,"address":"1.163","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0164","text":"Petty,\n which investigates the inner connection of bourgeois relations of production, in op-\n position to vulgar economy, which merely busies itself within the apparent connec-\n tion, incessantly rechews anew the material long since supplied by scientific\n economy for a plausible explanation of, so to speak, the crudest phenomena and for\n bourgeois domestic use, but otherwise confines itself to systematizing,\n pedanticizing, and proclaiming as eternal truths the banal and self-satisfied\n conceptions of the bourgeois agents of production concerning their own best world.]\n [Running matter: 3*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"36\", \"facs\": \"#f0055\"} --> suous thing. But as soon as it appears as a commodity, it transforms itself into\n a sensuously supersensuous thing.","kind":"passage","sequence":164,"address":"1.164","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0165","text":"Not only does it stand with its feet\n on the ground, but it sets itself on its head in relation to all other commodities, and develops whims from its wooden head, far more wonderful\n than if it were to begin dancing of its own accord [Note: One recalls that China and tables began\n to dance when all the rest of the world seemed to stand still — pour encourager les autres.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":165,"address":"1.165","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0166","text":"The mystical character of the commodity therefore does not arise from its\n use-value. It arises just as little from the value determinations,\n considered in themselves. For, first, however different the useful\n labours or productive activities may be, it is a\nphysiological truth that they are functions of a specifically\nhuman organism, as distinct from other organisms,\n and that every such function, whatever its content and its\n form, is essentially expenditure of human brain, nerve,\n muscle, sense-organ, etc. Secondly, as regards what underlies the\n determination of the magnitude of value, the duration of that expenditure,\n or the quantity of labour, quantity is even sensibly distinguishable from the\nquality of labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":166,"address":"1.166","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0167","text":"In all conditions the\n labour-time which the production of the means of subsistence costs must have interested mankind, although not equally at different stages of develop-\n ment. Finally, as soon as men work for one another in any way,\n their labour also acquires a social form.","kind":"passage","sequence":167,"address":"1.167","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0168","text":"Let us take Robinson on his island. Modest as he is by\n nature, he nevertheless has various needs to satisfy and\n must therefore perform useful labours of various kinds,\n make tools, manufacture furniture, tame llamas, fish, hunt, etc.\n We do not speak here of praying and the like, since our Robinson finds\n pleasure in it and regards activity of that kind as recreation. Despite\n the diversity of his productive functions, he knows that they are only\n different forms of activity of the same Robinson, hence only different\n modes of human labour. Necessity itself compels him to distribute his\ntime accurately among his different functions.","kind":"passage","sequence":168,"address":"1.168","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0169","text":"Whether\n one occupies more, the other less space in his total activity de-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"37\", \"facs\": \"#f0056\"} -->pends upon the greater or lesser difficulty to be overcome in\n attaining the intended useful effect. Experience teaches him this, and our Robinson, who has rescued clock, ledger, ink and pen\n from the shipwreck, soon begins, as a good Englishman, to keep accounts\n of himself. His inventory contains a list of the articles of use\n he possesses, of the different operations\n required for their production, and finally of the labour-time which\n definite quantities of these different products cost\n him on average. All relations between Robinson and the things which constitute\n his self-created wealth are here so simple and transparent\n that even Mr. M.","kind":"passage","sequence":169,"address":"1.169","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0170","text":"Wirth would probably understand them without special exertion\n of mind. And yet all the essential determinations of\nvalue are contained therein.","kind":"passage","sequence":170,"address":"1.170","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0171","text":"Now let us put, in Robinson’s place, an association of free persons,\n who work with common means of production and expend their many in-\n dividual labour-powers self-consciously as one social labour-\n power. All determinations of Robinson’s labour are re-\n peated, only socially instead of individually. One essential\n difference, however, emerges. All Robinson’s products were his\n exclusively personal product and hence immediately articles of\n use for him. The total product of the association is a social\n product. One part of this product serves in turn as\n means of production. It remains social. But another part\n is consumed as means of subsistence by the members of the association. It must therefore\n be distributed among them.","kind":"passage","sequence":171,"address":"1.171","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0172","text":"The manner of this distribution will vary\n with the particular kind of the social organism of production\n itself and with the corresponding historical stage of development of the pro-\n ducers. Solely for the parallel with commodity production, we assume\n that the share of every producer in the means of subsistence is determined by\n his labour-time. Labour-time would thus play a double role.\n Its socially planned distribution regulates the proper\n proportion of the different labour-functions to the different needs.\n On the other hand, labour-time serves at the same time as the measure of the indi-\n vidual producer’s share in the common labour and hence also\n in the individually consumable part of the common product.","kind":"passage","sequence":172,"address":"1.172","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0173","text":"The social\n relations of men to their labours and their la-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"38\", \"facs\": \"#f0057\"} -->bour-products would remain here transparently simple, both\n in production and in distribution.","kind":"passage","sequence":173,"address":"1.173","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0174","text":"Whence, then, the enigmatic character of the product of labour as soon\n as it assumes the form of a commodity?","kind":"passage","sequence":174,"address":"1.174","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","text":"When men relate their products to one another as values,\n insofar as these things count as merely objective envelopes of homogeneous human\n labour, this also implies conversely that their different labours\n count only as homogeneous human labour in\nobjective envelope. They relate their different labours to one an-\n other as human labour by relating their products to one another\n as values. The personal relation is concealed by\n the objective form. Thus it is not written on value’s forehead\n what it is. In order to relate their products to one another as commodities,\n men are compelled to equate their different labours with abstract\n human labour. They do not know it, but they do\n it by reducing the material thing to the abstraction value.","kind":"passage","sequence":175,"address":"1.175","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0176","text":"This is a spontaneously arising and therefore unconsciously instinctive opera-\n tion of their brain, which necessarily grows out of the particular mode of their material production\n and the relations into which this production places them.\n First their relation is present in practice. Secondly, however,\n because they are human beings, their relation is present as a relation\n for them. The manner in which it is present for them, or is reflected in their brains,\n arises from the nature of the relation itself. Later they seek,\n through science, to penetrate the secret of their own social\n product, for the determination of a thing as value\n is their product, just as language is.","kind":"passage","sequence":176,"address":"1.176","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0177","text":"Further, as regards the magnitude of\n value, the private labours carried on independently of one another but,\n as members of the spontaneously arisen division of labour, depend-\n ent on one another in all directions, are thereby constantly reduced to their\n socially proportional measure, in that in the accidental\n and ever fluctuating exchange-relations of their pro-\n ducts the labour-time socially necessary for their production\n forcibly asserts itself as a regulating natural law, much as the law\n of gravity does when the house collapses over one’s head [Note: “What is one to think of a law which can assert itself only through periodic\n revolutions? It is precisely a natural law which rests upon the] .","kind":"passage","sequence":177,"address":"1.177","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0178","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"39\", \"facs\": \"#f0058\"} -->The determination of the magnitude of value by labour-time is therefore a\n secret concealed beneath the apparent movements of relative commodity-values. The producers’\n own social movement takes for them the form of a movement of things,\n under whose control they stand, instead of controlling them. Finally, as regards\n the value-form, it is precisely this form which objectively conceals\n the social relations of the private labourers and therefore the social determi-\n nations of the private labours, instead of re-\n vealing them. If I say that coat, boots, etc. relate themselves to linen\n as the universal materiality of abstract human labour, the absurdity of\n this expression is obvious. But when the producers\n of coat, boots, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":178,"address":"1.178","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0179","text":"relate these commodities to linen as the general\n equivalent, the social relation of their private la-\n bours appears to them in precisely this absurd form.","kind":"passage","sequence":179,"address":"1.179","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0179"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","text":"Forms of this kind constitute the categories of bourgeois\n political economy. They are socially valid, hence objective forms\n of thought for the relations of production of this historically deter-\n minate social mode of production.","kind":"passage","sequence":180,"address":"1.180","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0181","text":"Private producers enter into social contact only through\n their private products, things. The social relations of\n their labours therefore are and appear not as imme-\n diately social relations of persons in their labours, but rather\n as objective relations of persons or social\n relations of things. The first and most general repre-\n sentation of the thing as a social thing is, however,\n the transformation of the product of labour into commodity.","kind":"passage","sequence":181,"address":"1.181","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0181"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","text":"The mysticism of the commodity therefore arises from the fact that, to the private\n producers, the social determinations of their private\n labours appear as social natural determinations of the\n products of labour, and that the social relations of pro-\n duction of persons appear as social rela-\n [Note: unconsciousness of the participants.” (Friedrich Engels:\n “Outlines of a Critique of Political Economy,” p. 103 in\n “German-French Yearbooks, edited by Arnold\n Ruge and Karl Marx. Paris 1849.”)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"40\", \"facs\": \"#f0059\"} -->tions of things to one another and to persons. The\n relations of the private labourers to the social total labour are objectified\n in opposition to them and therefore exist for them\n in the forms of objects.","kind":"passage","sequence":182,"address":"1.182","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0183","text":"For a society of\n commodity-producers, whose general social relation of production\n consists in relating themselves to their products as commodities, that is, as\nvalues, and in this objective form relating their private\n labours to one another as equal human labour,\n Christianity, with its cult of abstract man,\n especially in its bourgeois development, Protestantism, Deism,\n etc., is the most appropriate religious form. In the ancient Asiatic,\n antique, etc. modes of production, the transformation of the\n product into commodity, and hence the existence of men as commodity-pro-\n ducers, plays a subordinate role, one which becomes all the more significant,\n however, the more the communities enter the stage of their decline.","kind":"passage","sequence":183,"address":"1.183","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0183"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0184","text":"Actual\n trading peoples exist only in the intermundia of the ancient world, like Epi-\n curus’ gods, or like Jews in the pores of Polish society. Those\n ancient social organisms of production are extraordinarily much\n simpler and more transparent than the bourgeois one, but they rest either\n upon the immaturity of the individual man, who has not yet torn himself loose from the umbilical cord\n of the natural species-connection with others,\n or upon direct relations of domination and servitude.\n They are conditioned by a low stage of development of the productive powers\n of labour and correspondingly restricted relations of men within\n their process of producing material life, hence to one another and\n to nature. This real restrictedness is reflected ideally in\n the ancient religions of nature and of peoples.","kind":"passage","sequence":184,"address":"1.184","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0184"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0185","text":"The religious reflection\n of the real world can vanish only when the relations of\n practical everyday working life present to men day after day transparently rational\n relations to one another and to nature. But the rela-\n tions can present themselves only as what they are. The form\n of the social process of life, i.e. of the material process of pro-\n duction, strips off its mystical veil only\n when it stands as the product of freely associated men under their conscious\n planned control. For this, however, a material foundation\n of society is required, or a series of material conditions of existence,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"41\", \"facs\": \"#f0060\"} -->which themselves are again the spontaneously arising product of a long and pain-\n ful history of development.","kind":"passage","sequence":185,"address":"1.185","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0185"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0186","text":"Political economy has indeed, though incompletely [Note: The inadequacy in Ricardo’s analysis of the magnitude of value—and it\n is the best—will be seen from the third and fourth books of this work.\n But as regards value in general, classical political\n economy nowhere explicitly and with clear consciousness distinguishes labour which objectifies itself\n in value from the same labour insofar as it presents itself in the use-value\n of its product. Naturally, it in fact makes this distinction, since\n it considers labour at one time quantitatively, at another qualitatively. But\n it does not occur to it that a merely quantitative difference between labours\npresupposes their qualitative unity or equality, hence their reduction to\nabstract human labour. Ricardo, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":186,"address":"1.186","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0186"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0187","text":"declares himself in agreement\n with Destutt de Tracy when the latter says: “As it is certain that our physical\n and moral faculties are alone our original riches, the employment of those faculties,\n labour of some kind, is our original treasure, and that it is always from this\n employment — that all those things are created which we call riches … It is\n certain too, that all those things only represent the labour which\n has created them, and if they have a value, or even two dis-\ntinct values, they can only derive them from that (the value) of the\n labour from which they emanate.” (Ricardo: “The Principles of Pol.\n Econ. 3 ed. Lond. 1821,” p. 334). We merely indicate that Ricardo imputes\n to Destutt his own deeper meaning.","kind":"passage","sequence":187,"address":"1.187","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0187"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0188","text":"Destutt does indeed say,\n on the one hand, that all things constituting wealth “represent the labour\n that has created them”, but on the other hand that they receive their “two dif-\n ferent values” (use-value and exchange-value) from the “value of\n labour”. He thereby falls into the shallowness of vulgar economy, which presupposes the\n value of a commodity (here, labour) in order subsequently to determine by it the\n value of the other commodities. Ricardo reads him as saying that labour (not the value of labour) is repre-\n sented both in use-value and exchange-value. Yet he himself distinguishes the twofold character of\n labour, which is represented doubly, so little that throughout the entire chapter: “Value\n and Riches, Their Distinctive Properties” he has laboriously to\n wrestle with the trivialities of a J. B. Say.","kind":"passage","sequence":188,"address":"1.188","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0188"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0189","text":"At the end, therefore, he is also\n quite astonished that Destutt, while agreeing with himself concerning labour as the source of value,\n nevertheless agrees, on the other hand, with Say concerning the concept of value] ,\n value and magnitude of value. It has never even posed the question\n why labour represents itself in value, and the measure of labour\n by its duration in time in magnitude of value? Forms which bear it\n written on their foreheads that they belong to a social formation in\n which the production process masters men, rather than man\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"42\", \"facs\": \"#f0061\"} --> mastering the production process, count for its bourgeois con-\n sciousness as a necessity of nature just as self-evident as produc-\n tive labour itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":189,"address":"1.189","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0189"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0190","text":"Pre-bourgeois forms of the social pro-\n ductive organism are therefore treated by it much as the\n Church Fathers treated pre-Christian religions [Note: “Les économistes ont une singulière manière de procéder. Il n’y a\n pour eux que deux sortes d’institution, celles de l’art et celles de la nature. Les\n institutions de la féodalité sont des institutions artificielles, celles de la bour-\n geoisie sont des institutions naturelles. Ils ressemblent en’ ceci aux théologiens,\n qui eux aussi établissent deux sortes de religion. Toute religion qui n’est pas la\n leur est une invention des hommes, tandis que leur propre religion est une émana-\n tion de dieu. — Ainsi il y a eu de l’histoire, mais il n’y en a plus.“ (Karl\n Marx: „Misère de la Philosophie. Réponse à la Philosophie\n de la Misère par M. Proudhon. 1847“, p. 113.)","kind":"passage","sequence":190,"address":"1.190","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0190"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0191","text":"Truly comical is\n Monsieur Bastiat, who imagines that the ancient Greeks and Romans lived only by\n plunder. But if one lives by plunder for many centuries, there must\n constantly be something to plunder, or the object of plunder must\n continuously reproduce itself. It appears, therefore, that Greeks and Romans too had a\n process of production, hence an economy, which formed the material\n foundation of their world just as bourgeois economy does that of the present\n world. Or does Bastiat perhaps mean that a mode of production based on slave\n labour rests upon a system of plunder? He then places himself on dangerous\n ground. If a giant thinker like Aristotle erred in his appraisal\n of slave labour, why should a dwarf economist like Bastiat be\n correct in his appraisal of wage labour?","kind":"passage","sequence":191,"address":"1.191","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0191"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0192","text":"— I take this oppor-\n tunity briefly to dismiss an objection made to me, upon the appearance of my work “Zur Kritik\n der Pol. Oekonomie. 1859”, by a German-American newspaper.\n It said that my view that the determinate mode of pro-\n duction and the relations of production corresponding to it in each case, in short “the\n economic structure of society is the real basis upon which a juridical\n and political superstructure arises, and to which determinate social\n forms of consciousness correspond”, that “the mode of production of material\n life conditions the social, political, and intellectual life-process in general”,\n — all this is indeed correct for the present world, where material interests [rule],\n but not for the Middle Ages, where Catholicism, and for Athens and Rome, where] .","kind":"passage","sequence":192,"address":"1.192","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0192"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0193","text":"How greatly a portion of economists are deceived by the fetishism adher-\n ing to the world of commodities, or by the objective appearance of social\n determinations of labour, is proved, among other things, by the\n tediously absurd quarrel over the role of nature in the formation\n of exchange-value. Since exchange-value is a determinate social\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"43\", \"facs\": \"#f0062\"} --> manner of expressing labour expended upon a thing, it can contain no more\n natural material than, for example, the rate of exchange.","kind":"passage","sequence":193,"address":"1.193","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0193"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0194","text":"As the most general and least developed form of bourgeois pro-\n duction, which for that reason already appears in earlier periods of production,\n although not in the same dominant, hence characteristic\n manner, the commodity form was still relatively easy to see through.\n But more concrete forms, such as capital, for example? The fetishism of\n classical economy becomes palpable here.","kind":"passage","sequence":194,"address":"1.194","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0194"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0195","text":"Yet, in order not to anticipate, let one more example suffice here concern-\n ing the commodity form itself. We have seen that in the relation of\n commodity to commodity, for example of boot to boot-jack, the use-value\n of the boot-jack, thus the usefulness of its actual material\n properties, is utterly indifferent to the boot. The boot commodity is interested in the boot-\n jack only as the form of appearance of its own value. If commodities could\n therefore speak, they would say: our use-value may interest men.\n It does not accrue to us as things. But what does accrue to us\n materially is our value. Our own intercourse as commodity-things proves this. We relate\n to one another only as exchange-values. Now listen to the economist\n speaking from out of the commodity soul: “Value (exchange-value) is a prop-\n erty of things, wealth (use-value) of man.”","kind":"passage","sequence":195,"address":"1.195","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0195"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0196","text":"Value\n in this sense necessarily implies exchange, wealth does not [Note: “Value is a property of things, riches of man. Value, in this\n sense, necessarily implies exchanges, riches do not.“ „Observations on\n some verbal Disputes in Pol. Econ., particularly relating\n to value and to offer and demand. Lond. 1821“, p. 16.] .“\n [Note: politics ruled. First of all, it is strange that someone should see fit to assume\n that these world-famous sayings about the Middle Ages and the ancient world\n have remained unknown to anybody. So much is clear: the Middle Ages could not\n live on Catholicism, nor could the ancient world live on politics.\n The manner in which they gained their livelihood explains, conversely, why\n politics there and Catholicism here played their roles. Besides, little\n acquaintance, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":196,"address":"1.196","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0196"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0197","text":"with the history of the Roman Republic is required in order to\n know that the history of landed property forms its secret history.\n On the other hand, Don Quixote already paid the penalty for the error\n of believing errant knighthood to be equally compatible with all economic forms of society.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"44\", \"facs\": \"#f0063\"} --> “Wealth (use-value) is an attribute of man, value\n an attribute of commodities. A man or a community is\nrich; a pearl or a diamond is valuable … A pearl or\n a diamond has value as a pearl or a diamond [Note: “Riches are the attribute of man, value is the attribute of commodities.\n A man or a community is rich, a pearl or a diamond is valuable … A pearl or\n a diamond is valuable as a pearl or diamond.“ S. Bailey l. c.\n p. 165.] .“ To this day,\n no chemist has discovered exchange-value in pearl or diamond.","kind":"passage","sequence":197,"address":"1.197","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0197"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0198","text":"Our\n authors, who make a special claim to critical profundity, find, however,\n that the use-value of things is independent of their material\n properties, whereas their exchange-value accrues to them as things.\n What confirms them in this is the peculiar circumstance that the use-\n value of things is realised for man without exchange,\n thus in the immediate relation between thing and man, while their\n value, conversely, is realised only in exchange, i. e. in a social\n process. Who does not here recall good\n Dogberry, who instructs the watchman Seacoal: “To be a well-favoured\n man is the gift of fortune, but to be able to read and write comes by nature [Note: The author of the “Observations” and S. Bailey accuse\n Ricardo of having transformed exchange-value from something merely relative into something ab-\n solute. The opposite is the case.","kind":"passage","sequence":198,"address":"1.198","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0198"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0199","text":"He has reduced the apparent relativity which\n these things, diamond and pearls, for example, possess as exchange-values, to the true relation\n concealed behind the appearance, to their relativity\n as mere expressions of human labour. If the Ricardians answered Bailey\n coarsely, but not convincingly, it was only because they found in Ricardo himself no\n elucidation of the inner connection between value and exchange-\n value.] “.","kind":"passage","sequence":199,"address":"1.199","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0199"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","text":"The commodity is the immediate unity of use-\n value and exchange-value, hence of two opposites. It is\n therefore an immediate contradiction. This contradiction must\n develop as soon as it is not, as hitherto, considered analytically now from the\n standpoint of use-value, now from the standpoint of\n exchange-value, but is actually related as a whole to other\n commodities. But the actual relation of commodities to one an-\n other is their process of exchange.","kind":"passage","sequence":200,"address":"1.200","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0201","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"45\", \"facs\": \"#f0064\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":201,"address":"1.201","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0201"]}],"source_digest":"3469968421887f3d58666414bc5dd4f1c5a7629b099529c5e2bcee754e9919ee","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD","title":"2) Der Austauschprozess der Waaren.","order":2,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD","text":"Die Waaren können nicht selbst zu Markte gehn und sich nicht selbst\n austauschen. Wir müssen uns also nach ihren Hütern umsehn, den\nWaarenbesitzern. Die Waaren sind Dinge und daher widerstands-\n los gegen den Menschen. Wenn sie nicht willig, kann er Gewalt brau-\n chen, in andern Worten sie nehmen [Note: Im 12. durch seine Frömmigkeit so berufenen Jahrhundert kommen unter\n diesen Waaren oft sehr zarte Dinge vor. So zählt ein französischer Dichter jener\n Zeit unter den Waaren, die sich auf dem Markt von Landit einfanden, neben Klei-\n dungsstoffen, Schuhen, Leder, Ackergeräthen, Häuten u. s. w. auch „femmes\n folles de leur corps“ auf.] . Um diese Dinge als Waaren auf\n einander zu beziehn, müssen die Waarenhüter sich aufeinander als Per-\n sonen beziehn, deren Willen ein Dasein in jenen Dingen hat, sodass\n Jeder nur mit seinem Willen und dem Willen des andern, beide also nur\n mit ihrem gemeinschaftlichen Willen sich die fremde Waare aneignen, in-\n dem sie die eigne veräussern und die eigne veräussern, um sich die fremde\n anzueignen. Sie müssen sich daher wechselseitig als Privateigen-\n thümer anerkennen. Diess Rechtsverhältniss, dessen Form der\nVertrag ist, ob nun legal entwickelt oder nicht, ist nur das Willens-\n verhältniss, worin sich das ökonomische Verhältniss wiederspiegelt.\n Der Inhalt dieses Rechts- oder Willensverhältnisses ist durch\n das ökonomische Verhältniss selbst gegeben [Note: Proudhon schöpft erst sein Ideal der Gerechtigkeit, der justice\n éternelle, aus den der Waarenproduktion entsprechenden Rechtsverhältnissen, wo-\n durch, nebenbei bemerkt, auch der für alle Spiessbürger so tröstliche Beweis ge-\n liefert wird, dass die Form der Waarenproduktion ebenso ewig ist als die Ge-\n rechtigkeit. Dann umgekehrt will er die wirkliche Waarenproduktion und das ihr\n entsprechende wirkliche Recht diesem Ideal gemäss ummodeln. Was würde man von\n einem Chemiker denken, der, statt die wirklichen Gesetze des Stoffwechsels zu\n studiren, und auf Basis derselben bestimmte Aufgaben zu lösen, den Stoffwechsel\n durch die „ewigen Ideen“ der „naturalité“ und der „affinité“ ummodeln wollte?\n Weiss man etwa mehr über den „Wucher“, wenn man sagt, er widerspreche der\n „justice éternelle“ und der „équite éternelle“ und der „mutualité éternelle“ und\n andern vérités éternelles“, als die Kirchenväter wussten, wenn sie sagten, er] . Die Personen beziehn\n sich hier nur auf einander, indem sie gewisse Sachen als Waaren auf\n einander beziehn. Alle Bestimmungen dieser Beziehung sind also in der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"46\", \"facs\": \"#f0065\"} --> Bestimmung der Sache als Waare enthalten. Der eine Mensch existirt\n hier nur für den andern als Repräsentant von Waare und daher als Waa-\n renbesitzer. Wir werden überhaupt im Fortgang der Entwicklung\n finden, dass die ökonomischen Charaktermasken der Personen nur die Perso-\n nifikationen der ökonomischen Verhältnisse sind, als deren Träger sie sich\n gegenübertreten.\n\n\n\n\nWas den Waarenbesitzer namentlich von der Waare unterscheidet,\n ist der Umstand, dass ihr der Gebrauchswerth jeder andern Waare nur\n als Erscheinungsform ihres eignen Werths gilt. Geborner Leveller und\n Cyniker steht sie daher stets auf dem Sprung mit jeder andern Waare, sei\n selbe auch ausgestattet mit mehr Unannehmlichkeiten als Maritorne, nicht\n nur die Seele, sondern den Leib zu wechseln. Diesen der Waare man-\n gelnden Sinn für das Konkrete des Waarenkörpers ergänzt der Waaren-\n besitzer durch seine eignen fünf und mehr Sinne. Seine Waare hat für\n ihn keinen unmittelbaren Gebrauchswerth. Sonst führte er sie nicht zu\n Markt. Sie hat Gebrauchswerth für andre. Für ihn hat sie unmittel-\n bar nur den Gebrauchswerth Träger von Tauschwerth und so\nTauschmittel zu sein [Note: „Denn zweifach ist der Gebrauch jeden Guts. — Der eine ist dem Ding\n als solchem eigen, der andere nicht, wie einer Sandale zur Beschuhung zu\n dienen und austauschbar zu sein. Beides sind Gebrauchswerthe der Sandale,\n denn auch wer die Sandale mit dem ihm Mangelnden, z. B. der Nahrung aus-\n tauscht, benutzt die Sandale als Sandale. Aber nicht in ihrer natürlichen Ge-\n brauchsweise. Denn sie ist nicht da des Austausches wegen.“ (Aristoteles,\n de Rep. l. 1. c. 9.)] . Darum will er sie veräussern für Waare,\n deren Gebrauchswerth ihm genüge thut. Alle Waaren sind Nicht-\n Gebrauchswerthe für ihre Besitzer, Gebrauchswerthe\n für ihre Nicht-Besitzer. Sie müssen also allseitig die Hände\n wechseln. Aber dieser Händewechsel bildet ihren Austausch und ihr Aus-\n tausch bezieht sie als Werthe auf einander und realisirt sie als Werthe.\n Die Waaren müssen sich daher als Werthe realisiren, bevor sie\n sich als Gebrauchswerthe realisiren können.\n\n\n\n\nAndrerseits müssen sie sich als Gebrauchswerthe bewähren,\n bevorsie sich als Werthe realisiren können. Denn die auf\n [Note: widerspreche der „grâce éternelle“, der „foi éternelle“, der „volonté éternelle\n de dieu“?] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"47\", \"facs\": \"#f0066\"} --> sie verausgabte menschliche Arbeit zählt nur, soweit sie in nützlicher Form\n verausgabt und zwar nützliche Arbeit für andre ist. Ob sie andern nütz-\n lich, ihr Produkt daher fremde Bedürfnisse befriedigt, kann aber nur ihr\n Austausch beweisen.\n\n\n\n\nJeder Waarenbesitzer will seine Waare nur veräussern gegen andre\n Waare, deren Gebrauchswerth sein Bedürfniss befriedigt. Sofern ist der\n Austausch für ihn nur individueller Prozess. Andrerseits will er\n seine Waare als Werth realisiren, also in jeder ihm beliebigen andern\n Waare von demselben Werth, ob seine eigne Waare nun für den\n Besitzer der andern Waare Gebrauchswerth habe oder nicht. Sofern\n ist der Austausch für ihn allgemein gesellschaftlicher Prozess.\n Aber derselbe Prozess kann nicht gleichzeitig für alle Waarenbesitzer nur\n individuell und zugleich nur allgemein gesellschaftlich sein.\n\n\n\n\nSehn wir näher zu, so gilt jedem Waarenbesitzer jede fremde Waare\n als besondres Aequivalent seiner Waare, seine Waare daher als\nallgemeines Aequivalent aller andern Waaren. Da aber alle\n Waarenbesitzer dasselbe thun, ist keine Waare allgemeines Aequi-\n valent und besitzen die Waaren daher auch keine allgemeine relative\n Werthform, worin sie sich als Werthe gleichsetzen und als Werth-\n grössen vergleichen. Sie stehn sich daher überhaupt nicht gegenüber\n als Waaren, sondern nur als Produkte oder Gebrauchswerthe.\n\n\n\n\nIn ihrer Verlegenheit denken unsre Waarenbesitzer wie Faust. Im\n Anfang war die That. Sie haben daher schon gehandelt, bevor sie ge-\n dacht haben. Die Gesetze der Waarennatur bethätigen sich im Natur-\n instinkt der Waarenbesitzer. Sie können ihre Waaren nur als Werthe und\n darum nur als Waaren auf einander beziehn, indem sie dieselben gegen-\n sätzlich auf irgend eine andre Waare als allgemeines Aequi-\n valent beziehn. Das ergab die Analyse der Waare. Aber nur die\ngesellschaftliche That kann eine bestimmte Waare zum allge-\n meinen Aequivalent machen. Die gesellschaftliche Action aller\n andern Waaren schliesst daher eine bestimmte Waare aus, worin sie\n allseitig ihre Werthe darstellen. Dadurch wird die Naturalform dieser\n Waare gesellschaftlich gültige Aequivalentform. Allgemeines Aequi-\n valent zu sein wird durch den gesellschaftlichen Prozess zur speci-\n fisch gesellschaftlichen Funktion der ausgeschlossenen\n Waare. So wird sie — Geld. „Illi unum consilium habent et virtutem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"48\", \"facs\": \"#f0067\"} --> et potestatem suam bestiae tradunt. Et ne quis possit emere aut vendere,\n nisi qui habet characterem aut nomen bestiae, aut numerum nominis ejus.“\n (Apocalypse.)\n\n\n\n\nDer Geldkrystall ist nothwendiges Produkt des Austauschpro-\n zesses der Waaren. Der immanente Widerspruch der Waare als unmit-\n telbarer Einheit von Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth, als Produkt\n nützlicher Privatarbeit, die ein nur vereinzeltes Glied eines natur-\n wüchsigen Gesammtsystems der nützlichen Arbeiten oder der Theilung\n der Arbeit bildet, und als unmittelbar gesellschaftliche Ma-\n teriatur abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit — dieser Wider-\n spruch ruht und rastet nicht, bis er sich zur Verdopplung der\n Waare in Waare und Geld gestaltet hat. In demselben Masse da-\n her, worin sich die Verwandlung der Arbeitsprodukte in Waaren,\n vollzieht sich die Verwandlung von Waare in Geld [Note: Danach beurtheile man die Pfiffigkeit des kleinbürgerlichen Socialismus,\n der die Waarenproduktion verewigen und zugleich den „Gegensatz von Geld und\n Waare“, also das Geld selbst, denn es ist nur in diesem Gegensatze, ab-\n schaffen will. Ebensowohl könnte man den Papst abschaffen, und den Katho-\n licismus bestehn lassen. Das Nähere hierüber sieh in meiner Schrift: „Zur\n Kritik der Pol. Oekonomie“ p. 61 sq.] .\n\n\n\n\nDer unmittelbare Produktenaustausch hat einerseits die\n Form des einfachen relativen Werthausdrucks und hat sie andrerseits noch\n nicht. Jene Form war: x Waare A = y Waare B. Die Form des un-\n mittelbaren Produktenaustauschs ist: x Gebrauchsgegenstand A = y Ge-\n brauchsgegenstand B [Note: So lange noch nicht zwei verschiedene Gebrauchsgegenstände aus-\n getauscht, sondern, wie wir das bei Wilden oft finden, eine chaotische Masse von\n Dingen als Aequivalent für ein Drittes angeboten wird, steht der unmittelbare Pro-\n duktenaustausch selbst erst in seiner Vorhalle.] . Die Dinge A und B sind hier nicht Waaren\n vor dem Austausch, sondern werden es erst durch denselben. Die erste\n Weise, worin ein Gebrauchsgegenstand der Möglichkeit nach Tauschwerth\n ist, ist sein Dasein als Nicht-Gebrauchswerth, als die unmittel-\n baren Bedürfnisse seines Besitzers überschiessendes Quantum von Ge-\n brauchswerth. Dinge sind an und für sich dem Menschen äusserlich und\n daher veräusserlich. Damit diese Veräusserung wechselseitig,\n brauchen Menschen nur stillschweigend als Privateigenthümer jener ver-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"49\", \"facs\": \"#f0068\"} --> äusserlichen Dinge und eben dadurch als von einander unabhängige Per-\n sonen einander gegenübertreten. Solch ein Verhältniss wechselseitiger\n Fremdheit existirt jedoch nicht für die Glieder eines naturwüchsigen Ge-\n meinwesens, habe es nun die Form einer patriarchalischen Familie, einer\n altindischen Gemeinde, eines Inkastaates u. s. w. Der Waarenaustausch\n beginnt, wo die Gemeinwesen enden, an den Punkten ihres Contakts mit\n fremden Gemeinwesen oder Gliedern fremder Gemeinwesen. Sobald Dinge\n aber einmal im auswärtigen, werden sie auch rückschlagend im innern Ge-\n meinleben zu Waaren. Ihr quantitatives Austauschverhält-\n niss ist zunächst ganz zufällig. Austauschbar sind sie durch den\n Willensakt ihrer Besitzer sie wechselseitig zu veräussern. Sie erhal-\n ten daher die Form Austauschbarer, bevor sie als Werthe entwickelt\n sind. Indess setzt sich das Bedürfniss für fremde Gebrauchsgegenstände\n allmälig fest. Die beständige Wiederholung des Austauschs macht ihn zu\n einem regelmässigen gesellschaftlichen Prozess. Im Laufe der Zeit muss\n daher wenigstens ein Theil der Arbeitsprodukte absichtlich zum Behuf\n des Austauschs producirt werden. Von diesem Augenblick befestigt sich\n einerseits die Scheidung zwischen der Nützlichkeit der Dinge für den un-\n mittelbaren Bedarf und ihrer Nützlichkeit zum Austausch. Ihr Gebrauchs-\n werth scheidet sich von ihrem Tauschwerthe. Andrerseits wird das quan-\n titative Verhältniss, worin sie sich austauschen, von ihrer Produktion selbst\n abhängig. Die Gewohnheit fixirt sie als Werthgrössen.\n\n\n\n\nIm unmittelbaren Produktenaustausch ist jede Waare unmittelbar\n Tauschmittel für ihren Besitzer, Aequivalent für ihren Nichtbesitzer, jedoch\n nur so weit sie Gebrauchswerth für ihn. Der Tauschartikel erhält also\n noch keine von seinem eignen Gebrauchswerth oder dem individuellen\n Bedürfniss der Austauscher unabhängige Werthform. Die Nothwendig-\n keit dieser Form entwickelt sich mit der wachsenden Anzahl und Mannig-\n faltigkeit der in den Austauschprozess eintretenden Waaren. Die Aufgabe\n entspringt gleichzeitig mit den Mitteln ihrer Lösung. Ein Verkehr, wel-\n cher die Waarenbesitzer treibt, ihre eigenen Artikel mit verschiedenen\n andern Artikeln auszutauschen und daher zu vergleichen, findet niemals\n statt, ohne dass verschiedene Waaren von verschiedenen Waarenbesitzern\n innerhalb ihres Verkehrs mit einer und derselben dritten Waaren-\n art ausgetauscht und als Werthe verglichen werden. Solche dritte Waare,\n indem sie Aequivalent für verschiedene andere Waaren wird, erhält\n [Running matter: I. 4] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"50\", \"facs\": \"#f0069\"} --> unmittelbar, wenn auch in engen Grenzen, allgemeine oder gesellschaft-\n liche Aequivalentform. Diese allgemeine Aequivalentform entsteht und ver-\n geht mit dem augenblicklichen gesellschaftlichen Contakt, der sie ins\n Leben rief. Abwechselnd und flüchtig kommt sie dieser und jener Waare\n zu. Mit der Entwicklung des Waarenaustauschs heftet sie sich aber aus-\n schliesslich fest an besondere Waarenarten, oder krystallisirt zur\nGeldform. An welcher Waarenart sie kleben bleibt, ist zunächst zu-\n fällig. Jedoch entscheiden im Grossen und Ganzen zwei Umstände. Die\nGeldform heftet sich entweder an die wichtigsten Eintauschartikel\n aus der Fremde, welche in der That naturwüchsige Erscheinungsfor-\n men des Tauschwerths der einheimischen Produkte sind. Oder an den\n Gebrauchsgegenstand, welcher das Hauptelement des einheimischen ver-\n äusserlichen Besitzthums bildet, wie Vieh z. B. Nomadenvölker ent-\n wickeln zuerst die Geldform, weil all ihr Hab und Gut sich in beweg-\n licher, daher unmittelbar veräusserlicher Form befindet, und weil ihre\n Lebensweise sie beständig mit fremden Gemeinwesen in Contakt bringt,\n daher zum Produktenaustausch sollicitirt. Die Menschen haben oft den\n Menschen selbst in der Gestalt des Sklaven zum ursprünglichen Geldmate-\n rial gemacht, aber niemals den Grund und Boden. Solche Idee\n konnte nur in bereits ausgebildeter bürgerlicher Gesellschaft aufkommen.\n Sie datirt vom letzten Drittheil des 17. Jahrhunderts und ihre Ausfüh-\n rung, auf nationalem Massstab, wurde erst ein Jahrhundert später in der\n bürgerlichen Revolution der Franzosen versucht.\n\n\n\n\nIn demselben Verhältniss, worin der Waarenaustausch seine nur\n lokalen Bande sprengt, der Waarenwerth sich daher zur Materiatur\nmenschlicher Arbeit überhaupt ausweitet, geht die Geldform\n auf Waaren über, die von Natur zur gesellschaftlichen Funktion eines all-\n gemeinen Aequivalents taugen, auf die edlen Metalle.\n\n\n\n\nDass nun, „obgleich Gold und Silber nicht von Natur Geld, Geld\n von Natur Gold und Silber ist“ [Note: Karl Marx l. c. p. 135. „I metalli .... naturalmente mo-\n neta.“ (Galiani: „Della Moneta“ in Custodi’s Sammlung Parte\n Moderna, t. III, p. 72.)] , zeigt die Congruenz ihrer Natur-\n eigenschaften mit seinen Funktionen [Note: Das Nähere darüber in meiner eben citirten Schrift, Abschnitt: „Die\n edlen Metalle “.] . Bisher kennen wir aber nur\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"51\", \"facs\": \"#f0070\"} --> die eine Funktion des Geldes, als Erscheinungsform des Waarenwerths\n zu dienen oder als das Material, worin die Werthgrössen der Waaren\n sich gesellschaftlich ausdrücken. Adäquate Erscheinungsform von Werth\n oder Materiatur abstrakter und daher gleicher menschlicher Arbeit kann\n nur eine Materie sein, deren sämmtliche Exemplare dieselbe gleichförmige\n Qualität besitzen. Andrerseits, da der Unterschied der Werthgrössen\n rein quantitativ ist, verschiedne Quanta geronnener Arbeitszeit aus-\n drückt, muss die Geldwaare rein quantitativer Unterschiede fähig, also\n nach Willkühr theilbar und aus ihren Theilen wieder zusammensetzbar\n sein. Gold und Silber besitzen aber diese Eigenschaften von Natur.\n\n\n\n\nDer Gebrauchswerth der Geldwaare verdoppelt sich. Neben\n ihrem besondern Gebrauchswerth als Waare, wie Gold z. B. zum Aus-\n stopfen hohler Zähne, Rohmaterial von Luxusartikeln u. s. w. dient, er-\n hält sie einen formalen Gebrauchswerth, der aus ihren specifischen gesell-\n schaftlichen Funktionen entspringt.\n\n\n\n\nDa alle andern Waaren nur besondere Aequivalente des Geldes, das\n Geld ihr allgemeines Aequivalent, verhalten sie sich als besondre\n Waaren zum Geld als der allgemeinen Waare [Note: „Il danaro è la merce universale.“ (Verri l. c. p. 16.)] .\n\n\n\n\nMan hat gesehn, dass die Geldform nur der an einer Waare fest-\n haftende Reflex der Beziehungen aller andern Waaren. Dass Geld\nWaare ist [Note: „Silver and gold themselves, which we may call by the general name of\n Bullion, are … commodities … raising and falling in ‥ value … Bullion\n then may be reckoned to be of higher value, where the smaller weight will pur-\n chase the greater quantity of the product or manufacture of the country etc.“\n („A Discourse of the General Notions of Money, Trade, and\n Exchange, as they stand in relations to eachother. By a Mer-\n chant. Lond. 1695“, p. 7.) „Silver and gold, coined or uncoined, tho’ they\n are used for a measure of all other things, are no less a commodity than wine,\n oyl, tobacco, cloth or stuffs.“ („A Discourse concerning Trade, and\n that in particular of the East-Indies etc., London 1689“, p. 2.) „The\n stock and riches of the kingdom cannot properly be confined to money, nor ought\n gold and silver to be excluded from being merchandize.“ („The East\n India Trade a most Profitable Trade. London 1677“, p. 4.)] , ist also nur eine Entdeckung für den, der von seiner fer-\n tigen Gestalt ausgeht, um sie hinterher zu analysiren. Der Austausch-\n prozess giebt der Waare, die er in Geld verwandelt, nicht ihren Werth,\n [Running matter: 4*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"52\", \"facs\": \"#f0071\"} --> sondern ihre spezifische Werthform. Die Verwechslung beider Bestim-\n mungen verleitete dazu, den Werth von Gold und Silber für imaginär\n zu halten [Note: „L’oro e l’argento hanno valore come metalli anteriori all’ essere mo-\n neta.“ (Galiani l. c.) Locke sagt: „Die allgemeine Uebereinstimmung\n der Menschen legte dem Silber, wegen seiner Qualitäten, die es zum Geld ge-\n eignet machten, einen imaginären Werth bei.“ Dagegen Law: „Wie\n könnten verschiedene Nationen irgend einer Sache einen imaginären Werth\n geben … oder wie hätte sich dieser imaginäre Werth erhalten können?“ Wie\n wenig er selbst aber von der Sache verstand: „Das Silber tauschte sich aus nach\n dem Gebrauchswerth, den es hatte, also nach seinem wirklichen Werth;\n durch seine Bestimmung als Geld erhielt es einen zuschüssigen Werth (une\n valeur additionnelle).“ (Jean Law: „Considérations sur le numé-\n raire et le commerce“ in E. Daire’s Edit. der „Économistes\n Financiers du XVIII. siècle“ p. 470.)] . Weil Geld in bestimmten Funktionen durch blosse Zeichen\n seiner selbst ersetzt werden kann, entsprang der andere Irrthum, es sei\n ein blosses Zeichen. Andrerseits lag darin die Ahnung, dass die Geld-\n form des Dings ihm selbst äusserlich und blosse Erscheinungsform\n dahinter versteckter menschlicher Verhältnisse. In diesem Sinn wäre jede\n Waare ein Zeichen, weil als Werth nur sachliche Hülle der auf sie\n verausgabten menschlichen Arbeit [Note: „L’argent en (des denrées) est le signe.“ (V. de Forbonnais:\n „Eléments du Commerce. Nouv. Edit. Leyde 1766“, t. II, p. 143.)\n „Comme signe il est attiré par les denrées.“ (l. c. p. 155.) „L’argent est un\n signe d’une chose et la représente.“ (Montesquieu: „Esprit des Lois“.\n Oeuvres Lond. 1767, t. II, p. 2.) „L’argent n’est pas simple signe, car il\n est lui-même richesse; il ne représente pas les valeurs, il les équivaut.“\n (Le Trosne l. c. p. 910.) „Betrachtet man den Begriff des Werths, so\n wird die Sache selbst nur als ein Zeichen angesehn und sie gilt nicht\nals sie selber, sondern als was sie werth ist.“ (Hegel l. c. p. 100.) Lange\n vor den Oekonomen brachten die Juristen die Vorstellung von Geld als\n blossem Zeichen und dem nur imaginären Werth der edlen Metalle in Schwung,\n im Sykophantendienst der königlichen Gewalt, deren Münzverfälschungsrecht sie\n das ganze Mittelalter hindurch auf die Traditionen des römischen Kaiserreichs und\n die Geldbegriffe der Pandekten stützten. „Qu’aucun puisse ni doive faire doute“,\n sagt ihr gelehriger Schüler, Philipp von Valois, in einem Dekret von 1346, „que\n à nous et à notre majesté royale n’appartienne seulement … le mestier, le fait,\n l’état, la provision et toute l’ordonnance des monnaies, de donner tel cours, et\npour tel prix comme il nous plait et bon nous semble.“ Es\n war römisches Rechtsdogma, dass der Kaiser den Geldwerth dekretirt. Es] . Indem man aber die gesellschaft-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"53\", \"facs\": \"#f0072\"} --> lichen Charaktere, welche Sachen oder die sachlichen Charaktere, welche\ngesellschaftliche Bestimmungen der Arbeit auf Grundlage\n einer bestimmten Produktionsweise erhalten, für blosse Zeichen, erklärt\n man sie zugleich für willkührliches Reflexionsprodukt der Menschen. Es\n war diess beliebte Aufklärungsmanier des 18. Jahrhunderts, um den räth-\n selhaften Gestalten menschlicher Verhältnisse, deren Entstehungsprozess\n es noch nicht entziffern konnte, vorläufig wenigstens den Schein der Fremd-\n heit abzustreifen.\n\n\n\n\nEs ward vorhin bemerkt, dass die Aequivalentform einer Waare die\nquantitative Bestimmung ihrer Werthgrösse nicht einschliesst.\n Weiss man, dass Gold Geld, daher mit allen andern Waaren unmittelbar\n austauschbar ist, so weiss man desswegen nicht, wie viel z. B. 10 Pfund\n Gold werth sind. Wie jede Waare kann das Geld seine eigne Werth-\n grösse nur relativ in andern Waaren ausdrücken. Sein eigner Werth\n ist bestimmt durch die zu seiner Produktion erheischte Arbeitszeit und\n drückt sich in dem Quantum jeder andern Waare aus, worin gleichviel\n Arbeitszeit geronnen ist [Note: „If a man can bring to London an ounce of silver out of the earth in\n Peru, in the same time that he can produce a bushel of corn, then one is\n the natural price of the other; now if by reason of new and more easier mines a\n man can procure two ounces of silver as easily as he formerly did one, the corn will\n be as cheap at 10 shillings the bushel, as it was before at 5 shillings, caeteris\n paribus.“ William Petty: „A Treatise on Taxes and Contri-\n butions. Lond. 1667“, p. 31.] . Diese Festsetzung seiner relativen Werth-\n grösse findet statt an seiner Produktionsquelle in unmittelbarem Tausch-\n handel. Sobald es als Geld in den Austauschprozess eintritt, ist sein\n Werth bereits gegeben. Wenn es schon in den letzten Decennien des\n 17. Jahrhunderts weit überschrittener Anfang der Geldanalyse, zu wissen,\n dass Geld Waare ist, so aber auch nur der Anfang. Die Schwierigkeit\n [Note: war ausdrücklich verboten, das Geld als Waare zu behandeln. „Pecunias vero\n nulli emere fas erit, nam in usu publico constitutas oportet non esse mercem.“\n Gute Auseinandersetzung hierüber von G. F. Pagnini: „Saggio sopra\n il giusto pregio delle cose. 1751“, bei Custodi Parte Moderna,\n t. II. Namentlich im zweiten Theil der Schrift polemisirt Pagnini gegen die\n Herren Juristen.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"54\", \"facs\": \"#f0073\"} --> liegt nicht darin zu begreifen, dass Geld Waare, sondern wie, warum, wo-\n durch Waare Geld ist [Note: Nachdem Herr Professor Roscher uns belehrt: „Die falschen\n Definitionen von Geld lassen sich in zwei Hauptgruppen theilen: solche, die\n es für mehr, und solche, die es für weniger halten als eine Waare,“\n folgt ein kunterbunter Katalog von Schriften über das Geldwesen, wodurch auch\n nicht die entfernteste Einsicht in die wirkliche Geschichte der Theorie durch-\n schimmert, und dann die Moral: „Zu leugnen ist übrigens nicht, dass die meisten\n neueren Nationalökonomen die Eigenthümlichkeiten, welche das Geld von\n andern Waaren unterscheiden (also doch mehr oder weniger als\n Waare?) nicht genug im Auge behalten haben … Insofern ist die\n halbmercantilistische Reaktion von Ganilh etc. nicht ganz unbegründet.“\n (Wilhelm Roscher: „Die Grundlagen der Nationalökonomie.\n 3. Aufl. 1858“, p. 207—10.) Mehr — weniger — nicht genug — insofern —\n nicht ganz! Welche Begriffsbestimmungen! Und dergleichen eklektische Pro-\n fessoralfaselei tauft Herr Roscher bescheiden „die anatomisch-physiologische Me-\n thode“ der politischen Oekonomie! Eine Entdeckung ist ihm jedoch geschuldet,\n nämlich, dass Geld „eine angenehme Waare“ ist.] .\n\n\n\n\nWir sahen, wie schon in dem einfachsten Ausdruck des Tausch-\n werths: x Waare A = y Waare B, das Ding, worin die Werthgrösse\n eines andern Dings dargestellt wird, seine Aequivalentform unabhängig von\n dieser Beziehung als gesellschaftliche Natureigenschaft zu besitzen\n scheint. Wir verfolgten die Befestigung dieses falschen Scheins. Er ist\n vollendet, sobald die allgemeine Aequivalentform mit der Naturalform einer\n besondern Waarenart verwachsen oder zur Geldform krystallisirt ist.\n Eine Waare scheint nicht erst Geld zu werden, weil die andern Waaren\n allseitig ihre Werthe in ihr darstellen, sondern sie scheinen umgekehrt all-\n gemein ihre Werthe in ihr darzustellen, weil sie Geldist. Die vermit-\n telnde Bewegung verschwindet in ihrem eignen Resultat und lässt keine\n Spur zurück. Ohne ihr Zuthun finden die Waaren ihre eigne Werthge-\n stalt fertig vor als einen ausser und neben ihnen existirenden Waaren-\n körper. Diese Dinge, Gold und Silber, wie sie aus den Eingeweiden der\n Erde herauskommen, sind zugleich die unmittelbare Incarnation aller\n menschlichen Arbeit. Daher die Magie des Geldes. Das bloss atomi-\n stische Verhalten der Menschen in ihrem gesellschaftlichen Produk-\n tionsprozess und daher die von ihrer Controle und ihrem bewussten indi-\n viduellen Thun unabhängige, sachliche Gestalt ihrer eignen Produk-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"55\", \"facs\": \"#f0074\"} --> tionsverhältnisse erscheinen zunächst darin, dass ihre Arbeitsprodukte all-\n gemein die Waarenform annehmen. Das Räthsel des Geld-\n fetischs ist daher nur das sichtbar gewordne, die Augen blendende\nRäthsel des Waarenfetischs selbst.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0001","text":"Die Waaren können nicht selbst zu Markte gehn und sich nicht selbst\n austauschen. Wir müssen uns also nach ihren Hütern umsehn, den\nWaarenbesitzern. Die Waaren sind Dinge und daher widerstands-\n los gegen den Menschen. Wenn sie nicht willig, kann er Gewalt brau-\n chen, in andern Worten sie nehmen [Note: Im 12. durch seine Frömmigkeit so berufenen Jahrhundert kommen unter\n diesen Waaren oft sehr zarte Dinge vor. So zählt ein französischer Dichter jener\n Zeit unter den Waaren, die sich auf dem Markt von Landit einfanden, neben Klei-\n dungsstoffen, Schuhen, Leder, Ackergeräthen, Häuten u. s. w. auch „femmes\n folles de leur corps“ auf.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"2.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","text":"Um diese Dinge als Waaren auf\n einander zu beziehn, müssen die Waarenhüter sich aufeinander als Per-\n sonen beziehn, deren Willen ein Dasein in jenen Dingen hat, sodass\n Jeder nur mit seinem Willen und dem Willen des andern, beide also nur\n mit ihrem gemeinschaftlichen Willen sich die fremde Waare aneignen, in-\n dem sie die eigne veräussern und die eigne veräussern, um sich die fremde\n anzueignen. Sie müssen sich daher wechselseitig als Privateigen-\n thümer anerkennen. Diess Rechtsverhältniss, dessen Form der\nVertrag ist, ob nun legal entwickelt oder nicht, ist nur das Willens-\n verhältniss, worin sich das ökonomische Verhältniss wiederspiegelt.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"2.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","text":"Der Inhalt dieses Rechts- oder Willensverhältnisses ist durch\n das ökonomische Verhältniss selbst gegeben [Note: Proudhon schöpft erst sein Ideal der Gerechtigkeit, der justice\n éternelle, aus den der Waarenproduktion entsprechenden Rechtsverhältnissen, wo-\n durch, nebenbei bemerkt, auch der für alle Spiessbürger so tröstliche Beweis ge-\n liefert wird, dass die Form der Waarenproduktion ebenso ewig ist als die Ge-\n rechtigkeit. Dann umgekehrt will er die wirkliche Waarenproduktion und das ihr\n entsprechende wirkliche Recht diesem Ideal gemäss ummodeln. Was würde man von\n einem Chemiker denken, der, statt die wirklichen Gesetze des Stoffwechsels zu\n studiren, und auf Basis derselben bestimmte Aufgaben zu lösen, den Stoffwechsel\n durch die „ewigen Ideen“ der „naturalité“ und der „affinité“ ummodeln wollte?","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"2.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0004","text":"Weiss man etwa mehr über den „Wucher“, wenn man sagt, er widerspreche der\n „justice éternelle“ und der „équite éternelle“ und der „mutualité éternelle“ und\n andern vérités éternelles“, als die Kirchenväter wussten, wenn sie sagten, er] . Die Personen beziehn\n sich hier nur auf einander, indem sie gewisse Sachen als Waaren auf\n einander beziehn. Alle Bestimmungen dieser Beziehung sind also in der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"46\", \"facs\": \"#f0065\"} --> Bestimmung der Sache als Waare enthalten. Der eine Mensch existirt\n hier nur für den andern als Repräsentant von Waare und daher als Waa-\n renbesitzer. Wir werden überhaupt im Fortgang der Entwicklung\n finden, dass die ökonomischen Charaktermasken der Personen nur die Perso-\n nifikationen der ökonomischen Verhältnisse sind, als deren Träger sie sich\n gegenübertreten.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"2.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005","text":"Was den Waarenbesitzer namentlich von der Waare unterscheidet,\n ist der Umstand, dass ihr der Gebrauchswerth jeder andern Waare nur\n als Erscheinungsform ihres eignen Werths gilt. Geborner Leveller und\n Cyniker steht sie daher stets auf dem Sprung mit jeder andern Waare, sei\n selbe auch ausgestattet mit mehr Unannehmlichkeiten als Maritorne, nicht\n nur die Seele, sondern den Leib zu wechseln. Diesen der Waare man-\n gelnden Sinn für das Konkrete des Waarenkörpers ergänzt der Waaren-\n besitzer durch seine eignen fünf und mehr Sinne. Seine Waare hat für\n ihn keinen unmittelbaren Gebrauchswerth. Sonst führte er sie nicht zu\n Markt. Sie hat Gebrauchswerth für andre. Für ihn hat sie unmittel-\n bar nur den Gebrauchswerth Träger von Tauschwerth und so\nTauschmittel zu sein [Note: „Denn zweifach ist der Gebrauch jeden Guts.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"2.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","text":"— Der eine ist dem Ding\n als solchem eigen, der andere nicht, wie einer Sandale zur Beschuhung zu\n dienen und austauschbar zu sein. Beides sind Gebrauchswerthe der Sandale,\n denn auch wer die Sandale mit dem ihm Mangelnden, z. B. der Nahrung aus-\n tauscht, benutzt die Sandale als Sandale. Aber nicht in ihrer natürlichen Ge-\n brauchsweise. Denn sie ist nicht da des Austausches wegen.“ (Aristoteles,\n de Rep. l. 1. c. 9.)] . Darum will er sie veräussern für Waare,\n deren Gebrauchswerth ihm genüge thut. Alle Waaren sind Nicht-\n Gebrauchswerthe für ihre Besitzer, Gebrauchswerthe\n für ihre Nicht-Besitzer. Sie müssen also allseitig die Hände\n wechseln. Aber dieser Händewechsel bildet ihren Austausch und ihr Aus-\n tausch bezieht sie als Werthe auf einander und realisirt sie als Werthe.\n Die Waaren müssen sich daher als Werthe realisiren, bevor sie\n sich als Gebrauchswerthe realisiren können.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"2.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","text":"Andrerseits müssen sie sich als Gebrauchswerthe bewähren,\n bevorsie sich als Werthe realisiren können. Denn die auf\n [Note: widerspreche der „grâce éternelle“, der „foi éternelle“, der „volonté éternelle\n de dieu“?] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"47\", \"facs\": \"#f0066\"} --> sie verausgabte menschliche Arbeit zählt nur, soweit sie in nützlicher Form\n verausgabt und zwar nützliche Arbeit für andre ist. Ob sie andern nütz-\n lich, ihr Produkt daher fremde Bedürfnisse befriedigt, kann aber nur ihr\n Austausch beweisen.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"2.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","text":"Jeder Waarenbesitzer will seine Waare nur veräussern gegen andre\n Waare, deren Gebrauchswerth sein Bedürfniss befriedigt. Sofern ist der\n Austausch für ihn nur individueller Prozess. Andrerseits will er\n seine Waare als Werth realisiren, also in jeder ihm beliebigen andern\n Waare von demselben Werth, ob seine eigne Waare nun für den\n Besitzer der andern Waare Gebrauchswerth habe oder nicht. Sofern\n ist der Austausch für ihn allgemein gesellschaftlicher Prozess.\n Aber derselbe Prozess kann nicht gleichzeitig für alle Waarenbesitzer nur\n individuell und zugleich nur allgemein gesellschaftlich sein.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"2.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009","text":"Sehn wir näher zu, so gilt jedem Waarenbesitzer jede fremde Waare\n als besondres Aequivalent seiner Waare, seine Waare daher als\nallgemeines Aequivalent aller andern Waaren. Da aber alle\n Waarenbesitzer dasselbe thun, ist keine Waare allgemeines Aequi-\n valent und besitzen die Waaren daher auch keine allgemeine relative\n Werthform, worin sie sich als Werthe gleichsetzen und als Werth-\n grössen vergleichen. Sie stehn sich daher überhaupt nicht gegenüber\n als Waaren, sondern nur als Produkte oder Gebrauchswerthe.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"2.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","text":"In ihrer Verlegenheit denken unsre Waarenbesitzer wie Faust. Im\n Anfang war die That. Sie haben daher schon gehandelt, bevor sie ge-\n dacht haben. Die Gesetze der Waarennatur bethätigen sich im Natur-\n instinkt der Waarenbesitzer. Sie können ihre Waaren nur als Werthe und\n darum nur als Waaren auf einander beziehn, indem sie dieselben gegen-\n sätzlich auf irgend eine andre Waare als allgemeines Aequi-\n valent beziehn. Das ergab die Analyse der Waare. Aber nur die\ngesellschaftliche That kann eine bestimmte Waare zum allge-\n meinen Aequivalent machen. Die gesellschaftliche Action aller\n andern Waaren schliesst daher eine bestimmte Waare aus, worin sie\n allseitig ihre Werthe darstellen. Dadurch wird die Naturalform dieser\n Waare gesellschaftlich gültige Aequivalentform.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"2.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","text":"Allgemeines Aequi-\n valent zu sein wird durch den gesellschaftlichen Prozess zur speci-\n fisch gesellschaftlichen Funktion der ausgeschlossenen\n Waare. So wird sie — Geld. „Illi unum consilium habent et virtutem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"48\", \"facs\": \"#f0067\"} --> et potestatem suam bestiae tradunt. Et ne quis possit emere aut vendere,\n nisi qui habet characterem aut nomen bestiae, aut numerum nominis ejus.“\n (Apocalypse.)","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"2.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","text":"Der Geldkrystall ist nothwendiges Produkt des Austauschpro-\n zesses der Waaren. Der immanente Widerspruch der Waare als unmit-\n telbarer Einheit von Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth, als Produkt\n nützlicher Privatarbeit, die ein nur vereinzeltes Glied eines natur-\n wüchsigen Gesammtsystems der nützlichen Arbeiten oder der Theilung\n der Arbeit bildet, und als unmittelbar gesellschaftliche Ma-\n teriatur abstrakter menschlicher Arbeit — dieser Wider-\n spruch ruht und rastet nicht, bis er sich zur Verdopplung der\n Waare in Waare und Geld gestaltet hat.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"2.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0013","text":"In demselben Masse da-\n her, worin sich die Verwandlung der Arbeitsprodukte in Waaren,\n vollzieht sich die Verwandlung von Waare in Geld [Note: Danach beurtheile man die Pfiffigkeit des kleinbürgerlichen Socialismus,\n der die Waarenproduktion verewigen und zugleich den „Gegensatz von Geld und\n Waare“, also das Geld selbst, denn es ist nur in diesem Gegensatze, ab-\n schaffen will. Ebensowohl könnte man den Papst abschaffen, und den Katho-\n licismus bestehn lassen. Das Nähere hierüber sieh in meiner Schrift: „Zur\n Kritik der Pol. Oekonomie“ p. 61 sq.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"2.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0014","text":"Der unmittelbare Produktenaustausch hat einerseits die\n Form des einfachen relativen Werthausdrucks und hat sie andrerseits noch\n nicht. Jene Form war: x Waare A = y Waare B. Die Form des un-\n mittelbaren Produktenaustauschs ist: x Gebrauchsgegenstand A = y Ge-\n brauchsgegenstand B [Note: So lange noch nicht zwei verschiedene Gebrauchsgegenstände aus-\n getauscht, sondern, wie wir das bei Wilden oft finden, eine chaotische Masse von\n Dingen als Aequivalent für ein Drittes angeboten wird, steht der unmittelbare Pro-\n duktenaustausch selbst erst in seiner Vorhalle.] . Die Dinge A und B sind hier nicht Waaren\n vor dem Austausch, sondern werden es erst durch denselben.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"2.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0015","text":"Die erste\n Weise, worin ein Gebrauchsgegenstand der Möglichkeit nach Tauschwerth\n ist, ist sein Dasein als Nicht-Gebrauchswerth, als die unmittel-\n baren Bedürfnisse seines Besitzers überschiessendes Quantum von Ge-\n brauchswerth. Dinge sind an und für sich dem Menschen äusserlich und\n daher veräusserlich. Damit diese Veräusserung wechselseitig,\n brauchen Menschen nur stillschweigend als Privateigenthümer jener ver-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"49\", \"facs\": \"#f0068\"} --> äusserlichen Dinge und eben dadurch als von einander unabhängige Per-\n sonen einander gegenübertreten. Solch ein Verhältniss wechselseitiger\n Fremdheit existirt jedoch nicht für die Glieder eines naturwüchsigen Ge-\n meinwesens, habe es nun die Form einer patriarchalischen Familie, einer\n altindischen Gemeinde, eines Inkastaates u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"2.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016","text":"Der Waarenaustausch\n beginnt, wo die Gemeinwesen enden, an den Punkten ihres Contakts mit\n fremden Gemeinwesen oder Gliedern fremder Gemeinwesen. Sobald Dinge\n aber einmal im auswärtigen, werden sie auch rückschlagend im innern Ge-\n meinleben zu Waaren. Ihr quantitatives Austauschverhält-\n niss ist zunächst ganz zufällig. Austauschbar sind sie durch den\n Willensakt ihrer Besitzer sie wechselseitig zu veräussern. Sie erhal-\n ten daher die Form Austauschbarer, bevor sie als Werthe entwickelt\n sind. Indess setzt sich das Bedürfniss für fremde Gebrauchsgegenstände\n allmälig fest. Die beständige Wiederholung des Austauschs macht ihn zu\n einem regelmässigen gesellschaftlichen Prozess. Im Laufe der Zeit muss\n daher wenigstens ein Theil der Arbeitsprodukte absichtlich zum Behuf\n des Austauschs producirt werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"2.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0017","text":"Von diesem Augenblick befestigt sich\n einerseits die Scheidung zwischen der Nützlichkeit der Dinge für den un-\n mittelbaren Bedarf und ihrer Nützlichkeit zum Austausch. Ihr Gebrauchs-\n werth scheidet sich von ihrem Tauschwerthe. Andrerseits wird das quan-\n titative Verhältniss, worin sie sich austauschen, von ihrer Produktion selbst\n abhängig. Die Gewohnheit fixirt sie als Werthgrössen.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"2.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0018","text":"Im unmittelbaren Produktenaustausch ist jede Waare unmittelbar\n Tauschmittel für ihren Besitzer, Aequivalent für ihren Nichtbesitzer, jedoch\n nur so weit sie Gebrauchswerth für ihn. Der Tauschartikel erhält also\n noch keine von seinem eignen Gebrauchswerth oder dem individuellen\n Bedürfniss der Austauscher unabhängige Werthform. Die Nothwendig-\n keit dieser Form entwickelt sich mit der wachsenden Anzahl und Mannig-\n faltigkeit der in den Austauschprozess eintretenden Waaren. Die Aufgabe\n entspringt gleichzeitig mit den Mitteln ihrer Lösung. Ein Verkehr, wel-\n cher die Waarenbesitzer treibt, ihre eigenen Artikel mit verschiedenen\n andern Artikeln auszutauschen und daher zu vergleichen, findet niemals\n statt, ohne dass verschiedene Waaren von verschiedenen Waarenbesitzern\n innerhalb ihres Verkehrs mit einer und derselben dritten Waaren-\n art ausgetauscht und als Werthe verglichen werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"2.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","text":"Solche dritte Waare,\n indem sie Aequivalent für verschiedene andere Waaren wird, erhält\n [Running matter: I. 4] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"50\", \"facs\": \"#f0069\"} --> unmittelbar, wenn auch in engen Grenzen, allgemeine oder gesellschaft-\n liche Aequivalentform. Diese allgemeine Aequivalentform entsteht und ver-\n geht mit dem augenblicklichen gesellschaftlichen Contakt, der sie ins\n Leben rief. Abwechselnd und flüchtig kommt sie dieser und jener Waare\n zu. Mit der Entwicklung des Waarenaustauschs heftet sie sich aber aus-\n schliesslich fest an besondere Waarenarten, oder krystallisirt zur\nGeldform. An welcher Waarenart sie kleben bleibt, ist zunächst zu-\n fällig. Jedoch entscheiden im Grossen und Ganzen zwei Umstände.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"2.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0020","text":"Die\nGeldform heftet sich entweder an die wichtigsten Eintauschartikel\n aus der Fremde, welche in der That naturwüchsige Erscheinungsfor-\n men des Tauschwerths der einheimischen Produkte sind. Oder an den\n Gebrauchsgegenstand, welcher das Hauptelement des einheimischen ver-\n äusserlichen Besitzthums bildet, wie Vieh z. B. Nomadenvölker ent-\n wickeln zuerst die Geldform, weil all ihr Hab und Gut sich in beweg-\n licher, daher unmittelbar veräusserlicher Form befindet, und weil ihre\n Lebensweise sie beständig mit fremden Gemeinwesen in Contakt bringt,\n daher zum Produktenaustausch sollicitirt. Die Menschen haben oft den\n Menschen selbst in der Gestalt des Sklaven zum ursprünglichen Geldmate-\n rial gemacht, aber niemals den Grund und Boden. Solche Idee\n konnte nur in bereits ausgebildeter bürgerlicher Gesellschaft aufkommen.\n Sie datirt vom letzten Drittheil des 17.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"2.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0021","text":"Jahrhunderts und ihre Ausfüh-\n rung, auf nationalem Massstab, wurde erst ein Jahrhundert später in der\n bürgerlichen Revolution der Franzosen versucht.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"2.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022","text":"In demselben Verhältniss, worin der Waarenaustausch seine nur\n lokalen Bande sprengt, der Waarenwerth sich daher zur Materiatur\nmenschlicher Arbeit überhaupt ausweitet, geht die Geldform\n auf Waaren über, die von Natur zur gesellschaftlichen Funktion eines all-\n gemeinen Aequivalents taugen, auf die edlen Metalle.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"2.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0023","text":"Dass nun, „obgleich Gold und Silber nicht von Natur Geld, Geld\n von Natur Gold und Silber ist“ [Note: Karl Marx l. c. p. 135. „I metalli .... naturalmente mo-\n neta.“ (Galiani: „Della Moneta“ in Custodi’s Sammlung Parte\n Moderna, t. III, p. 72.)] , zeigt die Congruenz ihrer Natur-\n eigenschaften mit seinen Funktionen [Note: Das Nähere darüber in meiner eben citirten Schrift, Abschnitt: „Die\n edlen Metalle “.] . Bisher kennen wir aber nur\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"51\", \"facs\": \"#f0070\"} --> die eine Funktion des Geldes, als Erscheinungsform des Waarenwerths\n zu dienen oder als das Material, worin die Werthgrössen der Waaren\n sich gesellschaftlich ausdrücken. Adäquate Erscheinungsform von Werth\n oder Materiatur abstrakter und daher gleicher menschlicher Arbeit kann\n nur eine Materie sein, deren sämmtliche Exemplare dieselbe gleichförmige\n Qualität besitzen.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"2.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0024","text":"Andrerseits, da der Unterschied der Werthgrössen\n rein quantitativ ist, verschiedne Quanta geronnener Arbeitszeit aus-\n drückt, muss die Geldwaare rein quantitativer Unterschiede fähig, also\n nach Willkühr theilbar und aus ihren Theilen wieder zusammensetzbar\n sein. Gold und Silber besitzen aber diese Eigenschaften von Natur.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"2.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0025","text":"Der Gebrauchswerth der Geldwaare verdoppelt sich. Neben\n ihrem besondern Gebrauchswerth als Waare, wie Gold z. B. zum Aus-\n stopfen hohler Zähne, Rohmaterial von Luxusartikeln u. s. w. dient, er-\n hält sie einen formalen Gebrauchswerth, der aus ihren specifischen gesell-\n schaftlichen Funktionen entspringt.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"2.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0026","text":"Da alle andern Waaren nur besondere Aequivalente des Geldes, das\n Geld ihr allgemeines Aequivalent, verhalten sie sich als besondre\n Waaren zum Geld als der allgemeinen Waare [Note: „Il danaro è la merce universale.“ (Verri l. c. p. 16.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"2.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0027","text":"Man hat gesehn, dass die Geldform nur der an einer Waare fest-\n haftende Reflex der Beziehungen aller andern Waaren. Dass Geld\nWaare ist [Note: „Silver and gold themselves, which we may call by the general name of\n Bullion, are … commodities … raising and falling in ‥ value … Bullion\n then may be reckoned to be of higher value, where the smaller weight will pur-\n chase the greater quantity of the product or manufacture of the country etc.“\n („A Discourse of the General Notions of Money, Trade, and\n Exchange, as they stand in relations to eachother. By a Mer-\n chant. Lond. 1695“, p. 7.) „Silver and gold, coined or uncoined, tho’ they\n are used for a measure of all other things, are no less a commodity than wine,\n oyl, tobacco, cloth or stuffs.“ („A Discourse concerning Trade, and\n that in particular of the East-Indies etc., London 1689“, p. 2.)","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"2.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0028","text":"„The\n stock and riches of the kingdom cannot properly be confined to money, nor ought\n gold and silver to be excluded from being merchandize.“ („The East\n India Trade a most Profitable Trade. London 1677“, p. 4.)] , ist also nur eine Entdeckung für den, der von seiner fer-\n tigen Gestalt ausgeht, um sie hinterher zu analysiren. Der Austausch-\n prozess giebt der Waare, die er in Geld verwandelt, nicht ihren Werth,\n [Running matter: 4*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"52\", \"facs\": \"#f0071\"} --> sondern ihre spezifische Werthform. Die Verwechslung beider Bestim-\n mungen verleitete dazu, den Werth von Gold und Silber für imaginär\n zu halten [Note: „L’oro e l’argento hanno valore come metalli anteriori all’ essere mo-\n neta.“ (Galiani l. c.)","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"2.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0029","text":"Locke sagt: „Die allgemeine Uebereinstimmung\n der Menschen legte dem Silber, wegen seiner Qualitäten, die es zum Geld ge-\n eignet machten, einen imaginären Werth bei.“ Dagegen Law: „Wie\n könnten verschiedene Nationen irgend einer Sache einen imaginären Werth\n geben … oder wie hätte sich dieser imaginäre Werth erhalten können?“ Wie\n wenig er selbst aber von der Sache verstand: „Das Silber tauschte sich aus nach\n dem Gebrauchswerth, den es hatte, also nach seinem wirklichen Werth;\n durch seine Bestimmung als Geld erhielt es einen zuschüssigen Werth (une\n valeur additionnelle).“ (Jean Law: „Considérations sur le numé-\n raire et le commerce“ in E. Daire’s Edit. der „Économistes\n Financiers du XVIII. siècle“ p. 470.)] . Weil Geld in bestimmten Funktionen durch blosse Zeichen\n seiner selbst ersetzt werden kann, entsprang der andere Irrthum, es sei\n ein blosses Zeichen.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"2.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0030","text":"Andrerseits lag darin die Ahnung, dass die Geld-\n form des Dings ihm selbst äusserlich und blosse Erscheinungsform\n dahinter versteckter menschlicher Verhältnisse. In diesem Sinn wäre jede\n Waare ein Zeichen, weil als Werth nur sachliche Hülle der auf sie\n verausgabten menschlichen Arbeit [Note: „L’argent en (des denrées) est le signe.“ (V. de Forbonnais:\n „Eléments du Commerce. Nouv. Edit. Leyde 1766“, t. II, p. 143.)\n „Comme signe il est attiré par les denrées.“ (l. c. p. 155.) „L’argent est un\n signe d’une chose et la représente.“ (Montesquieu: „Esprit des Lois“.\n Oeuvres Lond. 1767, t. II, p. 2.) „L’argent n’est pas simple signe, car il\n est lui-même richesse; il ne représente pas les valeurs, il les équivaut.“\n (Le Trosne l. c. p. 910.)","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"2.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0031","text":"„Betrachtet man den Begriff des Werths, so\n wird die Sache selbst nur als ein Zeichen angesehn und sie gilt nicht\nals sie selber, sondern als was sie werth ist.“ (Hegel l. c. p. 100.) Lange\n vor den Oekonomen brachten die Juristen die Vorstellung von Geld als\n blossem Zeichen und dem nur imaginären Werth der edlen Metalle in Schwung,\n im Sykophantendienst der königlichen Gewalt, deren Münzverfälschungsrecht sie\n das ganze Mittelalter hindurch auf die Traditionen des römischen Kaiserreichs und\n die Geldbegriffe der Pandekten stützten.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"2.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0032","text":"„Qu’aucun puisse ni doive faire doute“,\n sagt ihr gelehriger Schüler, Philipp von Valois, in einem Dekret von 1346, „que\n à nous et à notre majesté royale n’appartienne seulement … le mestier, le fait,\n l’état, la provision et toute l’ordonnance des monnaies, de donner tel cours, et\npour tel prix comme il nous plait et bon nous semble.“ Es\n war römisches Rechtsdogma, dass der Kaiser den Geldwerth dekretirt. Es] . Indem man aber die gesellschaft-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"53\", \"facs\": \"#f0072\"} --> lichen Charaktere, welche Sachen oder die sachlichen Charaktere, welche\ngesellschaftliche Bestimmungen der Arbeit auf Grundlage\n einer bestimmten Produktionsweise erhalten, für blosse Zeichen, erklärt\n man sie zugleich für willkührliches Reflexionsprodukt der Menschen. Es\n war diess beliebte Aufklärungsmanier des 18.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"2.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0033","text":"Jahrhunderts, um den räth-\n selhaften Gestalten menschlicher Verhältnisse, deren Entstehungsprozess\n es noch nicht entziffern konnte, vorläufig wenigstens den Schein der Fremd-\n heit abzustreifen.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"2.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0034","text":"Es ward vorhin bemerkt, dass die Aequivalentform einer Waare die\nquantitative Bestimmung ihrer Werthgrösse nicht einschliesst.\n Weiss man, dass Gold Geld, daher mit allen andern Waaren unmittelbar\n austauschbar ist, so weiss man desswegen nicht, wie viel z. B. 10 Pfund\n Gold werth sind. Wie jede Waare kann das Geld seine eigne Werth-\n grösse nur relativ in andern Waaren ausdrücken.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"2.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0035","text":"Sein eigner Werth\n ist bestimmt durch die zu seiner Produktion erheischte Arbeitszeit und\n drückt sich in dem Quantum jeder andern Waare aus, worin gleichviel\n Arbeitszeit geronnen ist [Note: „If a man can bring to London an ounce of silver out of the earth in\n Peru, in the same time that he can produce a bushel of corn, then one is\n the natural price of the other; now if by reason of new and more easier mines a\n man can procure two ounces of silver as easily as he formerly did one, the corn will\n be as cheap at 10 shillings the bushel, as it was before at 5 shillings, caeteris\n paribus.“ William Petty: „A Treatise on Taxes and Contri-\n butions. Lond. 1667“, p. 31.] . Diese Festsetzung seiner relativen Werth-\n grösse findet statt an seiner Produktionsquelle in unmittelbarem Tausch-\n handel. Sobald es als Geld in den Austauschprozess eintritt, ist sein\n Werth bereits gegeben.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"2.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0036","text":"Wenn es schon in den letzten Decennien des\n 17. Jahrhunderts weit überschrittener Anfang der Geldanalyse, zu wissen,\n dass Geld Waare ist, so aber auch nur der Anfang. Die Schwierigkeit\n [Note: war ausdrücklich verboten, das Geld als Waare zu behandeln. „Pecunias vero\n nulli emere fas erit, nam in usu publico constitutas oportet non esse mercem.“\n Gute Auseinandersetzung hierüber von G. F. Pagnini: „Saggio sopra\n il giusto pregio delle cose. 1751“, bei Custodi Parte Moderna,\n t. II.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"2.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0037","text":"Namentlich im zweiten Theil der Schrift polemisirt Pagnini gegen die\n Herren Juristen.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"54\", \"facs\": \"#f0073\"} --> liegt nicht darin zu begreifen, dass Geld Waare, sondern wie, warum, wo-\n durch Waare Geld ist [Note: Nachdem Herr Professor Roscher uns belehrt: „Die falschen\n Definitionen von Geld lassen sich in zwei Hauptgruppen theilen: solche, die\n es für mehr, und solche, die es für weniger halten als eine Waare,“\n folgt ein kunterbunter Katalog von Schriften über das Geldwesen, wodurch auch\n nicht die entfernteste Einsicht in die wirkliche Geschichte der Theorie durch-\n schimmert, und dann die Moral: „Zu leugnen ist übrigens nicht, dass die meisten\n neueren Nationalökonomen die Eigenthümlichkeiten, welche das Geld von\n andern Waaren unterscheiden (also doch mehr oder weniger als\n Waare?)","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"2.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0038","text":"nicht genug im Auge behalten haben … Insofern ist die\n halbmercantilistische Reaktion von Ganilh etc. nicht ganz unbegründet.“\n (Wilhelm Roscher: „Die Grundlagen der Nationalökonomie.\n 3. Aufl. 1858“, p. 207—10.) Mehr — weniger — nicht genug — insofern —\n nicht ganz! Welche Begriffsbestimmungen! Und dergleichen eklektische Pro-\n fessoralfaselei tauft Herr Roscher bescheiden „die anatomisch-physiologische Me-\n thode“ der politischen Oekonomie! Eine Entdeckung ist ihm jedoch geschuldet,\n nämlich, dass Geld „eine angenehme Waare“ ist.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"2.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","text":"Wir sahen, wie schon in dem einfachsten Ausdruck des Tausch-\n werths: x Waare A = y Waare B, das Ding, worin die Werthgrösse\n eines andern Dings dargestellt wird, seine Aequivalentform unabhängig von\n dieser Beziehung als gesellschaftliche Natureigenschaft zu besitzen\n scheint. Wir verfolgten die Befestigung dieses falschen Scheins. Er ist\n vollendet, sobald die allgemeine Aequivalentform mit der Naturalform einer\n besondern Waarenart verwachsen oder zur Geldform krystallisirt ist.\n Eine Waare scheint nicht erst Geld zu werden, weil die andern Waaren\n allseitig ihre Werthe in ihr darstellen, sondern sie scheinen umgekehrt all-\n gemein ihre Werthe in ihr darzustellen, weil sie Geldist. Die vermit-\n telnde Bewegung verschwindet in ihrem eignen Resultat und lässt keine\n Spur zurück.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"2.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","text":"Ohne ihr Zuthun finden die Waaren ihre eigne Werthge-\n stalt fertig vor als einen ausser und neben ihnen existirenden Waaren-\n körper. Diese Dinge, Gold und Silber, wie sie aus den Eingeweiden der\n Erde herauskommen, sind zugleich die unmittelbare Incarnation aller\n menschlichen Arbeit. Daher die Magie des Geldes. Das bloss atomi-\n stische Verhalten der Menschen in ihrem gesellschaftlichen Produk-\n tionsprozess und daher die von ihrer Controle und ihrem bewussten indi-\n viduellen Thun unabhängige, sachliche Gestalt ihrer eignen Produk-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"55\", \"facs\": \"#f0074\"} --> tionsverhältnisse erscheinen zunächst darin, dass ihre Arbeitsprodukte all-\n gemein die Waarenform annehmen. Das Räthsel des Geld-\n fetischs ist daher nur das sichtbar gewordne, die Augen blendende\nRäthsel des Waarenfetischs selbst.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"2.40"}],"sha256":"8599c5b69191b0167675b25045f15ad573cb195d5063b3275240657461370195","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T201510426419Z_9f8a1cf3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2) Der Austauschprozess der Waaren.","source_sha256":"8599c5b69191b0167675b25045f15ad573cb195d5063b3275240657461370195","sha256":"a3d0520a8703fd9af930c67dcb82ba053314a36db177413e7fe05fb699d0f7f5","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:15:10.426432+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0001","text":"Commodities cannot themselves go to market and exchange themselves. We must therefore look about for their guardians, the commodity-owners. Commodities are things and hence defenceless against man. If they are not willing, he can use force, in other words, take them [Note: In the 12th century, so renowned for its piety, very delicate things often occur among these commodities. Thus a French poet of that time counts among the commodities that appeared at the market of Landit, alongside fabrics, shoes, leather, agricultural implements, hides, etc., also “femmes folles de leur corps”.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"2.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","text":"In order to relate these things to one another as commodities, the commodity-owners must relate to one another as persons whose will has an existence in those things, so that each appropriates the other's commodity only with his own will and the will of the other, thus both only through their common will, by alienating his own and alienating his own in order to appropriate the other's. They must therefore mutually recognize one another as private proprietors. This juridical relation, whose form is the contract, whether legally developed or not, is merely the relation of wills in which the economic relation is reflected.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"2.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","text":"The content of this juridical or relation of wills is given by the economic relation itself [Note: Proudhon first derives his ideal of justice, of justice éternelle, from the juridical relations corresponding to commodity production, whereby, incidentally, the proof so comforting to all philistines is also supplied, that the form of commodity production is as eternal as justice. Then conversely he wishes to remodel actual commodity production and the actual law corresponding to it in accordance with this ideal. What would one think of a chemist who, instead of studying the actual laws of metabolism and solving definite problems on their basis, wished to remodel metabolism through the “eternal ideas” of “naturalité” and “affinité”? ","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"2.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0004","text":"Does one know any more about “usury” when one says that it contradicts “justice éternelle” and “équite éternelle” and “mutualité éternelle” and other vérités éternelles”, than the Fathers of the Church knew when they said that it] . Persons here relate to one another only insofar as they relate certain things to one another as commodities. All determinations of this relation are therefore contained in the determination of the thing as commodity. The one person exists here for the other only as representative of commodity and hence as commodity-owner. We shall generally find, in the course of the development, that the economic character-masks of persons are merely the personifications of the economic relations, as whose bearers they confront one another.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"46\", \"facs\": \"#f0065\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"2.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005","text":"What distinguishes the commodity-owner from the commodity in particular is the circumstance that for it the use-value of every other commodity counts only as a form of appearance of its own value. A born leveller and cynic, it is therefore always ready to exchange not only its soul but its body with every other commodity, even if the latter is endowed with more unattractiveness than Maritorne. The commodity-owner supplements this sense, lacking in the commodity, for the concrete character of the commodity-body by means of his own five and more senses. His commodity has no immediate use-value for him. Otherwise he would not bring it to market. It has use-value for others. For him it immediately has only the use-value of being bearer of exchange-value and thus means of exchange [Note: “For the use of every good is twofold.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"2.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","text":"— The one is proper to the thing as such, the other is not, as in the case of a sandal, to serve for footwear and to be exchangeable. Both are use-values of the sandal, for he too who exchanges the sandal for what he lacks, e.g. food, uses the sandal as a sandal. But not in its natural mode of use. For it does not exist for the sake of exchange.” (Aristotle, de Rep. l. 1. c. 9.)] . Therefore he wishes to alienate it for a commodity whose use-value satisfies him. All commodities are non-use-values for their owners, use-values for their non-owners. They must therefore change hands on all sides. But this change of hands constitutes their exchange, and their exchange relates them to one another as values and realizes them as values. Commodities must therefore be realized as values before they can be realized as use-values.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"2.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","text":"On the other hand, they must prove themselves as use-values before they can be realized as values. For the human labour expended upon\n[Note: contradicts “grâce éternelle”, “foi éternelle”, “volonté éternelle de dieu”?]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"47\", \"facs\": \"#f0066\"} --> them counts only insofar as it is expended in useful form, namely as useful labour for others. But whether it is useful to others, hence whether its product satisfies the needs of others, can be proved only by its exchange.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"2.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","text":"Every commodity-owner wishes to alienate his commodity only in exchange for other commodity whose use-value satisfies his need. To that extent, exchange is for him only an individual process. On the other hand, he wishes to realize his commodity as value, that is, in every other commodity of the same value that pleases him, whether or not his own commodity has use-value for the owner of the other commodity. To that extent, exchange is for him a general social process. But the same process cannot simultaneously be merely individual for all commodity-owners and at the same time merely generally social.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"2.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009","text":"If we look more closely, every commodity-owner regards every other commodity as the particular equivalent of his commodity, and his commodity therefore as the general equivalent of all other commodities. But since all commodity-owners do the same, no commodity is a general equivalent, and commodities therefore also possess no general relative form of value in which they equate themselves as values and compare themselves as magnitudes of value. Thus they do not confront one another at all as commodities, but only as products or use-values.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"2.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","text":"In their perplexity our commodity-owners think like Faust. In the beginning was the deed. They have therefore already acted before they thought. The laws of commodity-nature assert themselves in the natural instinct of the commodity-owners. They can relate their commodities to one another as values, and hence only as commodities, only by relating them oppositely to some other commodity as general equivalent. That was yielded by the analysis of the commodity. But only the social deed can make a definite commodity into the general equivalent. The social action of all other commodities therefore excludes a definite commodity in which they represent their values on all sides. Thereby the natural form of this commodity becomes socially valid equivalent form.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"2.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","text":"Being general equivalent becomes, through the social process, the specifically social function of the excluded commodity. Thus it becomes—money. “Illi unum consilium habent et virtutem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"48\", \"facs\": \"#f0067\"} --> et potestatem suam bestiae tradunt. Et ne quis possit emere aut vendere, nisi qui habet characterem aut nomen bestiae, aut numerum nominis ejus.” (Apocalypse.)","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"2.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","text":"The money-crystal is a necessary product of the exchange-process of commodities. The immanent contradiction of the commodity as immediate unity of use-value and exchange-value, as product of useful private labour, which forms only an isolated member of a spontaneously arisen total system of useful labours or of the division of labour, and as immediately social materialization of abstract human labour—this contradiction neither rests nor ceases until it has formed itself into the duplication of commodity into commodity and money.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"2.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0013","text":"In the same measure, therefore, in which the transformation of products of labour into commodities takes place, the transformation of commodity into money takes place [Note: Judge accordingly the cleverness of petty-bourgeois socialism, which wishes to perpetuate commodity production and simultaneously abolish the “antithesis of money and commodity,” thus money itself, for it exists only in this antithesis. One might equally well abolish the Pope and let Catholicism remain. For more detail on this see my work: “Zur Kritik der Pol. Oekonomie” p. 61 sq.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"2.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0014","text":"Immediate exchange of products has, on the one hand, the form of the simple relative expression of value, and on the other hand does not yet have it. That form was: x commodity A = y commodity B. The form of immediate exchange of products is: x object of use A = y object of use B [Note: So long as two different objects of use have not yet been exchanged, but, as we often find among savages, a chaotic mass of things is offered as equivalent for a third, immediate exchange of products itself still stands only in its vestibule.] . Things A and B are not commodities here before exchange, but become such only through it.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"2.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0015","text":"The first manner in which an object of use is exchange-value in possibility is its existence as non-use-value, as a quantity of use-value exceeding the immediate needs of its owner. Things are in and for themselves external to man and hence alienable. For this alienation to be reciprocal, people need only tacitly confront one another as private proprietors of those alienable things and thereby precisely as persons independent of one another. Such a relation of mutual foreignness does not, however, exist for the members of a spontaneously arisen community, whether it has the form of a patriarchal family, an ancient Indian commune, an Inca state, etc.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"49\", \"facs\": \"#f0068\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"2.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016","text":"The exchange of commodities begins where communities end, at the points of their contact with foreign communities or members of foreign communities. But once things have become commodities in external relations, they also become such, by reaction, in the internal life of the community. Their quantitative exchange-relation is at first entirely accidental. They are exchangeable through the act of will of their owners to alienate them reciprocally. They therefore receive the form of exchangeables before they are developed as values. Meanwhile the need for foreign objects of use gradually becomes established. The constant repetition of exchange makes it a regular social process. In the course of time, therefore, at least a portion of the products of labour must be produced deliberately for the purpose of exchange.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"2.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0017","text":"From this moment, on the one hand, the separation between the usefulness of things for immediate need and their usefulness for exchange becomes fixed. Their use-value separates itself from their exchange-value. On the other hand, the quantitative relation in which they exchange becomes dependent upon their production itself. Habit fixes them as magnitudes of value.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"2.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0018","text":"In immediate exchange of products, every commodity is immediately a means of exchange for its owner, an equivalent for its non-owner, but only insofar as it is use-value for him. The article of exchange therefore does not yet receive a form of value independent of its own use-value or of the individual need of the exchangers. The necessity of this form develops with the growing number and diversity of the commodities entering into the exchange-process. The task arises simultaneously with the means of its solution. An intercourse which drives commodity-owners to exchange and hence compare their own articles with various other articles never takes place without different commodities of different commodity-owners being exchanged and compared as values, within their intercourse, with one and the same third kind of commodity.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"2.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","text":"Such a third commodity, by becoming equivalent for various other commodities, immediately receives, even if within narrow limits, general or social equivalent form. This general equivalent form arises and vanishes with the momentary social contact that called it into life. Alternately and fleetingly it falls to this or that commodity. But with the development of commodity-exchange it attaches itself exclusively to particular kinds of commodities, or crystallizes into the money-form. To which kind of commodity it remains attached is at first accidental. Yet, on the whole, two circumstances decide.\n[Running matter: I. 4]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"50\", \"facs\": \"#f0069\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"2.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0020","text":"The money-form attaches itself either to the most important articles of exchange from abroad, which are in fact spontaneously arisen forms of appearance of the exchange-value of domestic products. Or to the object of use which forms the chief element of domestic alienable property, such as cattle. Nomadic peoples, for example, first develop the money-form, because all their possessions are in movable, hence immediately alienable form, and because their mode of life constantly brings them into contact with foreign communities, thus soliciting them to exchange products. People have often made man himself, in the form of the slave, into the original money-material, but never land. Such an idea could arise only in already developed bourgeois society. It dates from the last third of the 17th","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"2.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0021","text":"century, and its execution, on a national scale, was first attempted a century later in the bourgeois revolution of the French.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"2.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022","text":"In the same proportion in which the exchange of commodities bursts its merely\n local bonds, and commodity-value therefore expands into the materialization\nof human labour in general, the money-form passes over\n to commodities which by nature are suited to the social function of a gen-\n eral equivalent, the precious metals.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"2.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0023","text":"That now, “although gold and silver are not by nature money, money\n is by nature gold and silver” [Note: Karl Marx l. c. p. 135. “I metalli .... naturalmente mo-\n neta.” (Galiani: “Della Moneta” in Custodi’s collection Parte\n Moderna, t. III, p. 72.)] , is shown by the congruence of their natural\nproperties with its functions [Note: For further details on this, see my work just cited, section: “The\n precious metals”.] . But hitherto we know only\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"51\", \"facs\": \"#f0070\"} --> the one function of money, to serve as the form of appearance of commodity-value\n or as the material in which the magnitudes of value of commodities\n express themselves socially. An adequate form of appearance of value\n or materialization of abstract and therefore equal human labour can\n only be a material all of whose specimens possess the same uniform\n quality.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"2.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0024","text":"On the other hand, since the difference between magnitudes of value\n is purely quantitative, expresses different quantities of congealed labour-time,\n the money-commodity must be capable of purely quantitative differences, thus\n divisible at will and capable of being recomposed from its parts.\n But gold and silver possess these properties by nature.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"2.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0025","text":"The use-value of the money-commodity is doubled. Alongside\n its particular use-value as a commodity, as gold, for example, serves for fill-\n ing hollow teeth, as raw material for luxury articles, etc., it re-\n ceives a formal use-value arising from its specific social\n functions.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"2.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0026","text":"Since all other commodities are only particular equivalents of money, while\n money is their general equivalent, they relate as particular\n commodities to money as the general commodity [Note: “Il danaro è la merce universale.” (Verri l. c. p. 16.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"2.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0027","text":"We have seen that the money-form is only the reflection adhering to one commodity\n of the relations of all other commodities. That money\nis a commodity [Note: “Silver and gold themselves, which we may call by the general name of\n Bullion, are … commodities … raising and falling in ‥ value … Bullion\n then may be reckoned to be of higher value, where the smaller weight will pur-\n chase the greater quantity of the product or manufacture of the country etc.”\n (“A Discourse of the General Notions of Money, Trade, and\n Exchange, as they stand in relations to eachother. By a Mer-\n chant. Lond. 1695”, p. 7.) “Silver and gold, coined or uncoined, tho’ they\n are used for a measure of all other things, are no less a commodity than wine,\n oyl, tobacco, cloth or stuffs.” (“A Discourse concerning Trade, and\n that in particular of the East-Indies etc., London 1689”, p. 2.)","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"2.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0028","text":"“The\n stock and riches of the kingdom cannot properly be confined to money, nor ought\n gold and silver to be excluded from being merchandize.” (“The East\n India Trade a most Profitable Trade. London 1677”, p. 4.)] , is therefore only a discovery for one who proceeds from its finished\n form in order subsequently to analyse it. The process of exchange\n does not give the commodity which it transforms into money its value,\n [Running matter: 4*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"52\", \"facs\": \"#f0071\"} --> but its specific form of value. The confusion of both determinations\n led to regarding the value of gold and silver as imaginary\n [Note: “L’oro e l’argento hanno valore come metalli anteriori all’ essere mo-\n neta.” (Galiani l. c.)","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"2.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0029","text":"Locke says: “The general agreement\n of men assigned to silver, on account of its qualities which made it suitable for money,\n an imaginary value.” Against this Law: “How\n could different nations give an imaginary value to any thing … or how\n could this imaginary value have maintained itself?” But how little he himself\n understood the matter: “Silver was exchanged according to the use-value it had,\n thus according to its real value;\n by its designation as money it received an additional value (une\n valeur additionnelle).” (Jean Law: “Considérations sur le numé-\n raire et le commerce” in E. Daire’s edition of the “Économistes\n Financiers du XVIII. siècle” p. 470.)] . Because money can in certain functions be replaced by mere signs\n of itself, the other error arose, that it is a mere\n sign.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"2.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0030","text":"On the other hand, this contained the intuition that the money-\n form of the thing is external to it itself and is a mere form of appearance\n of human relations hidden behind it. In this sense every\n commodity would be a sign, because as value it is only the material envelope\n of the human labour expended upon it [Note: “L’argent en (des denrées) est le signe.” (V. de Forbonnais:\n “Eléments du Commerce. Nouv. Edit. Leyde 1766”, t. II, p. 143.)\n “Comme signe il est attiré par les denrées.” (l. c. p. 155.) “L’argent est un\n signe d’une chose et la représente.” (Montesquieu: “Esprit des Lois”.\n Oeuvres Lond. 1767, t. II, p. 2.) “L’argent n’est pas simple signe, car il\n est lui-même richesse; il ne représente pas les valeurs, il les équivaut.”\n (Le Trosne l. c. p. 910.)","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"2.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0031","text":"“If one considers the concept of value,\n the thing itself is regarded only as a sign and it counts not\nas itself, but as what it is worth.” (Hegel l. c. p. 100.) Long\n before the economists, the jurists set the notion of money as a\n mere sign and of the merely imaginary value of the precious metals in motion,\n in the sycophantic service of royal power, whose right to debase coinage they\n throughout the entire Middle Ages based upon the traditions of the Roman Empire\n and the monetary concepts of the Pandects.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"2.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0032","text":"“That no one can or ought to doubt”,\n says their docile pupil, Philip of Valois, in a decree of 1346, “that\n to us and to our royal majesty belongs solely … the office, the matter,\n the status, the provision and the entire ordering of coinage, to give it such circulation, and\nat such price as pleases us and seems good to us.” It\n was a dogma of Roman law that the emperor decrees the value of money. It] . But in declaring the social\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"53\", \"facs\": \"#f0072\"} --> characters which things, or the material characters which\nsocial determinations of labour receive on the basis\n of a definite mode of production, to be mere signs,\n one simultaneously declares them to be arbitrary products of human reflection. This\n was the favourite Enlightenment manner of the 18th","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"2.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0033","text":"century, in order, at least provisionally,\n to strip from the enigmatic forms of human relations, whose process of\n emergence it could not yet decipher, the appearance of foreignness.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"2.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0034","text":"It was noted earlier that the equivalent-form of a commodity does not include\n the quantitative determination of its magnitude of value.\n If one knows that gold is money, hence directly exchangeable with all other commodities,\n one does not thereby know how much, for example, 10 pounds\n of gold are worth. Like every commodity, money can express its own magnitude\n of value only relatively in other commodities.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"2.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0035","text":"Its own value\n is determined by the labour-time required for its production and\n expresses itself in the quantity of every other commodity in which an equal amount\n of labour-time is congealed [Note: “If a man can bring to London an ounce of silver out of the earth in\n Peru, in the same time that he can produce a bushel of corn, then one is\n the natural price of the other; now if by reason of new and more easier mines a\n man can procure two ounces of silver as easily as he formerly did one, the corn will\n be as cheap at 10 shillings the bushel, as it was before at 5 shillings, caeteris\n paribus.” William Petty: “A Treatise on Taxes and Contri-\n butions. Lond. 1667”, p. 31.] . This determination of its relative magnitude\n of value takes place at its source of production in direct exchange.\n As soon as it enters the process of exchange as money, its\n value is already given.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"2.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0036","text":"If, already in the last decades of the\n 17th century, it was a beginning of the analysis of money, far surpassed long ago, to know\n that money is commodity, it was nevertheless only the beginning. The difficulty\n [Note: it was expressly forbidden to treat money as a commodity. “Pecunias vero\n nulli emere fas erit, nam in usu publico constitutas oportet non esse mercem.”\n Good discussion of this by G. F. Pagnini: “Saggio sopra\n il giusto pregio delle cose. 1751”, in Custodi Parte Moderna,\n t. II.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"2.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0037","text":"In particular, in the second part of the work Pagnini polemicizes against the\n gentlemen jurists.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"54\", \"facs\": \"#f0073\"} --> does not lie in understanding that money is commodity, but how, why, where-\n by commodity is money [Note: After Professor Roscher has instructed us: “The false\n definitions of money may be divided into two main groups: those which\n take it for more, and those which take it for less than a commodity,”\n there follows a motley catalogue of writings on monetary matters, through which\n not the remotest insight into the real history of the theory\n shines through, and then the moral: “It cannot otherwise be denied that most\n modern economists have not kept sufficiently in view the peculiarities\n which distinguish money from other commodities (thus nevertheless more or less than\n commodity?)","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"2.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0038","text":"not sufficiently in view … To this extent the\n semi-mercantilist reaction of Ganilh etc. is not entirely unfounded.”\n (Wilhelm Roscher: “Die Grundlagen der Nationalökonomie.\n 3. Aufl. 1858”, p. 207—10.) More — less — not enough — to this extent —\n not entirely! What determinations of concepts! And this sort of eclectic pro-\n fessorial nonsense Mr. Roscher modestly christens “the anatomical-physiological me-\n thod” of political economy! Yet one discovery is due to him,\n namely, that money is “an agreeable commodity”.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"2.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","text":"We saw how, already in the simplest expression of exchange-\n value: x commodity A = y commodity B, the thing in which the magnitude\n of value of another thing is represented appears to possess its equivalent-form\n independently of this relation as a social natural property.\n We traced the consolidation of this false appearance. It is\n complete as soon as the general equivalent-form has grown together with the\n natural form of a particular kind of commodity, or has crystallized into the money-form.\n A commodity does not seem first to become money because the other commodities\n represent their values in it on all sides; rather, conversely, they seem to represent their values\n generally in it because it is money. The mediat-\n ing movement disappears in its own result and leaves no\n trace behind.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"2.39","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","text":"Without any action on their part, commodities find their own form of value\n ready-made before them as a commodity-body existing outside and alongside\n them. These things, gold and silver, as they emerge from the bowels of\n the earth, are at the same time the immediate incarnation of all\n human labour. Hence the magic of money. The merely atomistic\n conduct of human beings in their social process of produc-\n tion, and hence the material form of their own relations of produc-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"55\", \"facs\": \"#f0074\"} --> tion, independent of their control and their conscious individual\n action, appear first of all in the fact that their products of labour generally\n assume the commodity-form. The riddle of the money-\n fetish is therefore only the riddle of the commodity-fetish itself become visible,\n dazzling the eyes.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"2.40","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040"]}],"source_digest":"6b2f66168caf5f3e98cf8c6101d48c298300ca7776db696e06bdc8f33baf47ea","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO","title":"3) Das Geld oder die Waarencirculation.","order":3,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO","text":"## A. Mass der Werthe.\n\n\n\n\nIch setze überall in dieser Schrift, der Vereinfachung halber, Gold\n als die Geldwaare voraus.\n\n\n\n\nDadurch dass sich die Waaren in Gold ihren allgemeinen relativen\n Werthausdruck geben, funktionirt das Gold ihnen gegenüber als Mass\n der Werthe. Die Waaren werden nicht durch das Geld commensurabel.\n Umgekehrt. Weil alle Waaren als Werthe vergegenständ-\n lichte menschliche Arbeit, daher an und für sich commensurabel\n sind, können sie sich alle in irgend einer dritten Waare messen und diese\n dadurch in ihr gemeinschaftliches Werthmass oder Geld verwandeln. Geld\n als Werthmass ist aber nothwendige Erscheinungsform des\nimmanenten Werthmasses der Waaren, der Arbeitszeit [Note: Die Frage, warum das Geld nicht unmittelbar die Arbeitszeit\n selbst repräsentirt, so dass z. B. eine Papiernote x Arbeitsstunden vorstellte,\n kömmt ganz einfach auf die Frage heraus, warum auf Grundlage der Waarenpro-\n duktion die Arbeitsprodukte sich als Waaren darstellen müssen, denn die Dar-\n stellung der Waare schliesst ihre Verdopplung in Waare und Geldwaare ein.\n Oder warum Privatarbeit nicht als unmittelbar gesellschaftliche Arbeit, als ihr\n Gegentheil, behandelt werden kann. Ich habe den seichten Utopismus eines\n „Arbeitsgelds“ auf Grundlage der Waarenproduktion anderswo ausführlicher er-\n örtert. (l. c. p. 61 sqq.) Hier sei noch bemerkt, dass z. B. das Owensche\n „Arbeitsgeld“ ebensowenig „Geld“ ist, wie etwa eine Theatermarke. Owen setzt\nunmittelbar vergesellschaftete Arbeit voraus, eine der Waaren-\n produktion diametral entgegengesetzte Produktionsform. Das Arbeitscertifikat kon-\n statirt nur den individuellen Antheil des Producenten an der Gemeinarbeit\n und seinen individuellen Anspruch auf den zur Konsumtion bestimmten Theil des\nGemeinprodukts. Aber es fällt Owen nicht ein, die Waarenproduktion einer-\n seits vorauszusetzen und dennoch andrerseits ihre nothwendigen Bedingungen\n durch Geldpfuschereien umgehn zu wollen.] .\n\n\n\n\nDer einfache relative Werthausdruck der Waaren in\nGeld — x Waare A = y Geldwaare — ist ihr Preis. In ihren Prei-\n sen erscheinen die Waaren erstens als Werthe, qualitativ Gleiche,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"56\", \"facs\": \"#f0075\"} -->Materiatur derselben Arbeit oder dieselbe Materiatur der\n Arbeit, Gold, zweitens als quantitativ bestimmte Werthgrös-\n sen, denn in der Proportion, worin sie gleich bestimmten Goldquanta,\n sind sie einander gleich oder stellen gleiche Arbeitsquanta vor. Andrer-\n seits wird der entfaltete relative Werthausdruck oder die end-\n lose Reihe relativer Werthausdrücke zur spezifisch relativen\n Werthform der Geldwaare. Diese Reihe ist aber jetzt schon ge-\n geben in den Waarenpreisen. Man lese die Quotationen eines Preiscou-\n rants rückwärts und man findet die Werthgrösse des Geldes in allen mög-\n lichen Waaren dargestellt. Die Reihe hat auch neuen Sinn erhalten.\n Das Gold, weil Geld, besitzt bereits in seiner Naturalform die allgemeine\n Aequivalentform oder die Form allgemeiner unmittelbarer Austauschbar-\n keit unabhängig von seinen relativen Werthausdrücken. Ihre Reihe stellt\n jetzt daher zugleich, ausser seiner Werthgrösse, die entfaltete Welt des\nstofflichen Reichthums oder der Gebrauchswerthe vor, worin es un-\n mittelbar umsetzbar ist. Geld hat dagegen keinen Preis. Um an\n dieser einheitlichen relativen Werthform der andern Waaren theilzu-\n nehmen, müsste es auf sich selbst als sein eignes Aequivalent bezogen\n werden.\n\n\n\n\nFür die Bewegung der Waarenpreise gelten die früher gegebnen\n Gesetze des einfachen relativen Werthausdrucks. Die Waarenpreise\n können nur allgemein steigen, bei gleichbleibendem Geldwerth, wenn die\n Waarenwerthe steigen, bei gleichbleibenden Waarenwerthen, wenn der\n Geldwerth fällt. Umgekehrt. Die Waarenpreise können nur allge-\n mein fallen, bei gleichbleibendem Geldwerth, wenn die Waarenwerthe\n fallen, bei gleichbleibenden Waarenwerthen, wenn der Geldwerth steigt.\n Es folgt daher keineswegs, dass steigender Geldwerth proportionelles Sin-\n ken der Waarenpreise und fallender Geldwerth proportionelles Steigen der\n Waarenpreise bedingt. Diess gilt nur für Waaren von unverändertem\n Werth. Solche Waaren z. B., deren Werth gleichmässig und gleichzeitig\n steigt mit dem Geldwerth, behalten dieselben Preise. Steigt ihr Werth\n langsamer oder rascher als der Geldwerth, so wird der Fall oder das Stei-\n gen ihrer Preise beschränkt durch die Differenz zwischen ihrer\n Werthbewegung und der des Geldes u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nDie preisbestimmte Waare hat doppelte Form, reelle und\nvorgestellte oder ideelle. Ihre wirkliche Gestalt ist die eines Ge-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"57\", \"facs\": \"#f0076\"} --> brauchsgegenstandes, eines Produkts konkreter nützlicher Ar-\n beit, z. B. Eisen. Ihre Werthgestalt, ihre Erscheinungsform als Mate-\n riatur eines bestimmten Quantums gleichartiger menschlicher\n Arbeit, ist ihr Preis, ein Quantum Gold. Aber Gold ist vom Eisen ver-\n schiednes Ding und in seinem Preise bezieht das Eisen sich selbst\n auf Gold als ein andres Ding, das jedoch ihm Werth-Gleiches ist.\n Der Preis oder die Geldform der Waare existirt nur in dieser gleichsetzen-\n den Beziehung, also so zu sagen nur in ihrem Kopfe, und ihr Besitzer\n muss seine Zunge in ihren Kopf stecken oder ihr Papierzettel anhängen,\n um ihren Preis für die Aussenwelt vorzustellen [Note: Der Wilde oder Halbwilde braucht die Zunge anders. Kapitain\nParry bemerkt z. B. von den Bewohnern an der Westküste der Baffinsbay:\n „In this case (beim Produktenaustausch) .... they licked it (the thing re-\n presented to them) twice to their tongues after which they seemed to consider the\n bargain satisfactorily concluded.“ Ebenso beleckte bei den östlichen Eskimos\n der Eintauscher jedesmal den Artikel beim Empfang desselben. Wenn die Zunge\n so im Norden als Organ der Aneignung, ist es kein Wunder, dass der Bauch\n im Süden als Organ des accumulirten Eigenthums gilt und der Kaffer den Reich-\n thum eines Mannes nach seinem Fettwanst schätzt. Die Kaffern sind grund-\n gescheute Kerle, denn während der britische „Board of Health“ in seinem\n Bericht von 1864 den Mangel eines grossen Theils der Arbeiterklasse an fettbil-\n denden Substanzen beklagt, machte ein Dr. Harvey, der jedoch nicht die Blut-\n circulation erfunden hat, in demselben Jahre sein Glück durch Puff-Recepte, die\n der Bourgeoisie und Aristokratie Fettüberflusseslast abzutreiben versprachen] . Die Form ihres\n Werths ist daher vorgestellte, ideelle Geldform im Unterschied zur hand-\n greiflich reellen Körperform ihres Gebrauchswerths. Da die Waaren so\n ihre Werthe nur ideell im Geld ausdrücken, drücken sie dieselben auch in\n nur vorgestelltem oder ideellem Geld aus. Mass der Werthe ist das\n Geld daher nur als vorgestelltes ideelles Geld. Jeder Waaren-\n besitzer weiss, dass er kein wirkliches Gold verbraucht, wenn er Waaren\n in Gold schätzt oder dem Waarenwerth die Form des Waarenpreises\n giebt. Obgleich nun das Geld als Werthmass nur ideell funktionirt,\n hängt der Preis dennoch ganz vom reellen Geldmaterial ab. Denn\n eine Waare, eine Tonne Eisen z. B., wird in ihrem Preise als Materiatur\n eines bestimmten Quantums Arbeit auf bestimmtes Quantum Geldmaterial\n als Materiatur desselben Quantums Arbeit bezogen, aber dasselbe\n Quantum Arbeit materialisirt sich in ganz verschiednen Quanta Gold, Sil-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"58\", \"facs\": \"#f0077\"} --> ber oder Kupfer. Der Werth einer Tonne Eisen erhält also ganz ver-\n schiedne Preisausdrücke, je nachdem Gold, Silber oder Kupfer als Werth-\n mass funktionirt.\n\n\n\n\nDie preisbestimmten Waaren stellen sich alle dar in der Form:\n a Waare A = x Gold; b Waare B = z Gold, c Waare C = y Gold u. s. w.,\n wo a, b, c bestimmte Masse der Waarenarten A, B, C, x, z, y be-\n stimmte Masse des Goldes. Die Waarenwerthe sind daher ver-\n wandelt in vorgestellte Goldquanta von verschiedner Grösse,\n also, trotz der wirren Buntheit der Waarenkörper, in gleichnamige\n Grössen, Goldgrössen. Verschiedne Goldquanta, weil gleichnamige\n Grössen, vergleichen und messen sich unter einander, indem sie auf ein\nfixirtes Quantum Gold als ihre Masseinheit bezogen werden. Diese\n Masseinheit selbst wird durch weitere Eintheilung in aliquote Theile zum\nMassstab fortentwickelt. Solche Massstäbe besassen Gold, Silber, Kupfer\n bereits in ihren Metallgewichten, bevor sie Geld wurden. Ihr vor-\n gefundener metallischer Gewichtmassstab dient daher ursprünglich auch\n stets in ihrer Geldfunktion.\n\n\n\n\nDie Geldnamen der Metallgewichte trennen sich jedoch nach und\n nach von ihren ursprünglichen Gewichtnamen aus verschiedenen\n Gründen, darunter historisch entscheidend: 1) Einführung fremden\n Geldes bei minder entwickelten Völkern, wie z. B. im alten Rom Silber-\n und Goldmünzen zuerst als ausländische Waaren circulirten. Die Namen\n dieses fremden Gelds sind von den einheimischen Gewichtnamen verschie-\n den. 2) Mit der Entwicklung des Reichthums wird das minder edle Metall\n durch das edlere aus der Funktion des Werthmasses verdrängt, Kupfer\n durch Silber, Silber durch Gold, so sehr diese Reihenfolge aller poetischen\n Chronologie widersprechen mag [Note: Sie ist übrigens auch nicht von allgemein historischer Gültigkeit.] . Pfund war nun z. B. Geldname\n für ein wirkliches Pfund Silber. Sobald Gold das Silber als Werthmass\n verdrängt, hängt sich derselbe Name vielleicht an \\frac{1}{15} u. s. w. Pfund\n Gold, je nach dem Werthverhältniss von Gold und Silber. Pfund als\n Geldname und als gewöhnlicher Gewichtname des Goldes sind jetzt ge-\n trennt. 3) Die Jahrhunderte lang fortgesetzte Geldfälschung der\n Fürsten, welche vom ursprünglichen Gewicht der Geldmünzen in der\n That nur den Namen zurückliess.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"59\", \"facs\": \"#f0078\"} -->\n\nDiese historischen Prozesse machen die Trennung des Geldnamens\n der Metallgewichte von ihrem gewöhnlichen Gewichtnamen zur Volksge-\n wohnheit. Bei definitiver Reglung des Geldmassstabs, die einerseits rein\n conventionell ist, andrerseits gesetzlicher Allgemeinheit und Zwangsgültig-\n keit bedarf, versteht es sich zuletzt von selbst, dass die Staatsautorität\n einen bestimmten Gewichttheil des edlen Metalls, z. B. eine Unze Gold, als\n Gewichteinheit fixirt und diese abtheilt in aliquote Theile, denen sie belie-\n bige legale Taufnamen beilegt, wie Pfund, Thaler u. s. w. Solcher ali-\n quote Theil, der dann als die eigentliche Masseinheit des Geldes gilt, wird\n weiter getheilt und untergetheilt in andre aliquote Theile, die ihrerseits\n gesetzliche Taufnamen erhalten, wie Shilling, Penny u. s. w. Nach wie\n vor bleiben bestimmte Metallgewichte Massstab des Metallgelds. Was sich\n geändert, ist Eintheilung und Namengebung.\n\n\n\n\nDie Waaren verwandeln Gold in das Mass der Werthe, indem\n sie allseitig ihre Werthe in ihm ausdrücken. So erhalten ihre Werth-\n grössen die Form der Preise oder vorgestellter Goldquanta.\n Diese Verwandlung von Werth in Preis einmal vollbracht, wird es tech-\n nisch nothwendig, das Mass der Werthe weiter zu bestimmen zum\nMassstab der Preise. Beide Funktionen sind durchaus verschieden.\n Als Massstab der Preise kann und muss ein bestimmtes Quantum Gold\nfixirt werden, grade wie der Massstab andrer gleichnamiger Grössen.\n Der Werthwechsel des Goldes ändert nichts am Werthverhältniss sei-\n ner verschiednen Gewichttheile unter einander und die gesetzlich fixirten\n Taufnamen dieser Theile ändern nichts an ihrem Gewicht. Der Massstab\n der Preise misst aber nur verschiedne Quanta Gold an einem fixirten Gold-\n quantum, nicht den Werth eines Goldquantums durch das Gewicht\n des andern.\n\n\n\n\nDer Werthwechsel des Goldes verhindert auch nicht seine Funk-\n tion als Werthmass. Er trifft alle Waaren gleichzeitig, lässt also,\n caeteris paribus, ihre wechselseitigen relativen Werthe unverändert,\n obgleich sie sich nun alle in höheren oder niedrigeren Goldpreisen als zu-\n vor ausdrücken.\n\n\n\n\nDie Waaren stellen ihre Werthe jetzt nicht nur gleichnamig als\n Gold, sondern in denselben gesellschaftlich gültigen Rechennamen des\n Goldmassstabs, wie Pfd. St. s. d. u. s. w., dar. Das Geld dient als Rechen-\n geld, so oft es gilt eine Sache als Werth und daher in Geldform zu fixiren.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"60\", \"facs\": \"#f0079\"} -->\n\nDer Name einer Sache ist ihrer Natur ganz äusserlich und da-\n her erlischt auch ihre Begriffsbestimmung in ihm. Ich weiss nichts vom\n Menschen, wenn ich weiss, dass ein Mensch Jakobus heisst. Ebenso ver-\n schwindet in den Geldnamen Pfund, Thaler, Frank, Dukat u. s. w.\n jede Spur des Werthverhältnisses. Die Wirre über den Geheimsinn\n dieser kabbalistischen Zeichen ist um so grösser als die Geldnamen zugleich\n den Werth der Waaren und aliquote Theile eines Goldgewichts,\n des Geldmassstabs, und das eine nur darstellen, weil das andre [Note: Vgl. „Theorieen von der Masseinheit des Geldes“ in „Zur Kritik\n der pol. Oekon. etc.“, p. 53 sqq. Die Phantasien über Erhöhung oder\n Erniedrigung des „Münzpreises“, die darin besteht, die gesetzlichen Geld-\n namen für gesetzlich fixirte Gewichttheile Gold oder Silber auf grössere oder\n kleinere Gewichttheile von Staatswegen zu übertragen und demgemäss auch etwa\n ¼ Unze Gold statt in 20 künftig in 40 sh. zu prägen — diese Phantasien, soweit\n sie nicht ungeschickte Finanzoperationen gegen Staats- und Privatgläubiger, son-\n dern ökonomische „Wunderkuren“ bezwecken, hat Petty so erschöpfend be-\n handelt in „Quantulumcumque concerning Money. To the Lord\n Marquis of Halifax. 1682“, dass schon seine unmittelbaren Nachfolger,\nSir Dudley North und John Locke, von Späteren gar nicht zu reden,\n ihn nur verflachen konnten. „If the wealth of a nation“, sagt er u. A., „could\n be decupled by a Proclamation, it were strange that such proclamations have not\n long since been made by our Governors.“ (l. c. p. 36.)] .\n Andrerseits ist es nothwendig, dass der Werth im Unterschied von den\n bunten Körpern der Waaren sich zu dieser begriffslos sachlichen, aber\n auch einfach gesellschaftlichen Form fortentwickle.\n\n\n\n\nDer Preis ist der Geldname der in der Waare vergegenständlich-\n ten Arbeitszeit. Die Aequivalenz der Waare und des Geldquantums,\n dem ihr Preis sie gleichsetzt, ist daher eine Tautologie [Note: „Ou bien, il faut consentir à dire qu’une valeur d’un million en\n argent vaut plus qu’une valeur égale en marchandises.“ (Le Trosne\n l. c. p. 922), also „qu’une valeur vaut plus qu’une valeur égale.“] , wie ja\n überhaupt der relative Werthausdruck einer Waare stets der\n Ausdruck der Aequivalenz zweier Waaren ist. Wenn aber der\n Preis als Exponent der Werthgrösse der Waare Exponent ihres Austausch-\n verhältnisses mit Geld ist, so folgt nicht umgekehrt, dass der Exponent\n ihres Austauschverhältnisses mit Geld nothwendig der Expo-\n nent ihrer Werthgrösse ist. Gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"61\", \"facs\": \"#f0080\"} --> von gleicher Grösse stelle sich in 1 Quarter Weizen und in 2 Pfd. St.\n (ungefähr ½ Unze Gold) dar. Die 2 Pfd. St. sind Geldausdrücke der\n Werthgrösse des Quarter Weizen, oder sein Preis. Erlauben nun die\n Umstände, ihn zu 3 Pfd. St., oder zwingen sie ihn zu 1 Pfd. St. zu notiren,\n so sind 1 Pfd. St. und 3 Pfd. St. als Ausdrücke der Werthgrösse des\n Weizens zu klein oder zu gross, aber sie sind dennoch Preise desselben,\n denn erstens sind sie seine Werthform, Geld, und zweitens Exponente\n seines Austauschverhältnisses mit Geld. Bei gleichbleibenden Produktions-\n bedingungen oder gleichbleibender Produktivkraft der Arbeit muss nach\n wie vor zur Reproduktion des Quarter Weizen gleich viel gesell-\n schaftliche Arbeitszeit verausgabt werden. Dieser Umstand\n hängt weder vom Willen des Weizenproduzenten noch der andern Waa-\n renbesitzer ab. Die Werthgrösse der Waare drückt also ein nothwen-\n diges, ihrem Bildungsprozess immanentes Verhältniss zur gesell-\n schaftlichen Arbeitszeit aus. Mit der Verwandlung der Werthgrösse in\n Preis erscheint diess nothwendige Verhältniss als Austauschverhältniss der\n Waare mit einer andern ausser ihr existirenden Waare. Diese Form\n kann aber ebensowohl die Werthgrösse der Waare als das zufällige Ver-\n hältniss ausdrücken, worin sie unter gegebnen Umständen veräusserlich\n ist. Die Möglichkeit quantitativer Incongruenz zwischen\n Preis und Werthgrösse, oder der Abweichung des Preises von der Werth-\n grösse, ist also in der Preisform selbst gegeben. Es ist diess kein\n Mangel dieser Form, sondern macht sie umgekehrt zur adäquaten Form\n einer Produktionsweise, worin sich die Regel nur als blindwirkendes\n Durchschnittsgesetz der Regellosigkeit durchsetzen kann.\n\n\n\n\nDie Preisform lässt jedoch nicht nur die Möglichkeit quanti-\n tativer Incongruenz zwischen Werthgrösse und Preis, d. h. zwischen\n der Werthgrösse und ihrem eignen Geldausdruck zu, sondern kann einen\nqualitativen Widerspruch beherbergen, so dass der Preis überhaupt\n aufhört, Werthausdruck zu sein, obgleich Geld nur die Werthform\n der Waaren ist. Dinge, die an und für sich keine Waaren sind, z. B. Ge-\n wissen, Ehre u. s. w., können ihren Besitzern gegen Geld veräusserlich\n sein und so durch ihren Preis die Waarenform erhalten. Ein Ding\n kann daher formell einen Preis haben, ohne einen Werth zu haben.\n Der Preisausdruck wird hier imaginär, wie gewisse Grössen der Ma-\n thematik oder das „unendliche Urtheil“ der Logik. Wo wir jedoch für\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"62\", \"facs\": \"#f0081\"} --> wesentliche Produktionsverhältnisse derartige imaginäre Preisform\n finden, wie z. B. Preis des Grund und Bodens, obgleich der Boden,\n weil keine menschliche Arbeit in ihm vergegenständlicht ist, auch keinen\n Werth hat, wird die tiefere Analyse unter der imaginären Form stets ein\n wirkliches Werthverhältniss oder von ihm abgeleitete Beziehung verbor-\n gen finden.\n\n\n\n\nDoch kehren wir zurück zum normalen Waarenpreis, der uns hier\n allein noch bekannt ist. Der Preis ist die nurideelle Werthgestalt\n der Waare. Er drückt also zugleich aus, dass sie noch nicht reelle\n Werthgestalt besitzt oder dass ihre Naturalform nicht ihre allgemeine\n Aequivalentform ist. Die ideelle Werthgestalt der Waare ist ferner\nPreis, d. h. nur vorgestellte oder ideelle Goldgestalt. Er\n drückt also aus, dass, um die Wirkung eines Tauschwerths oder allgemei-\n nen Aequivalents auf andre Waaren auszuüben, sie ihren natürlichen Leib\n abstreifen, sich aus nur vorgestelltem Gold in wirkliches Gold verwandeln\n muss, obgleich diese Transsubstantiation ihr „saurer“ ankommen mag als\n dem hegel’schen „Begriff“ der Uebergang aus der Nothwendigkeit in die\n Freiheit oder einem Hummer das Sprengen seiner Schale, oder dem Kir-\n chenvater Hieronymus das Abstreifen des alten Adam [Note: Wenn Hieronymus in seiner Jugend viel mit dem materiellen Fleisch zu\n ringen hatte, wie sein Wüstenkampf mit schönen Frauenbildern zeigt, so im Alter\n mit dem geistigen Fleisch. „Ich glaubte mich“, sagt er z. B., „im Geist vor dem\n Weltrichter. „Wer bist du?“ fragte eine Stimme. „Ich bin ein Christ.“ „Du\n lügst“, donnerte der Weltrichter. „Du bist nur ein Ciceronianer!“] . Neben ihrer\nreellen Gestalt, Eisen z. B., kann die Waare im Preis ideelle Werth-\n gestalt oder vorgestellte Goldgestalt besitzen, aber sie kann nicht zu-\n gleich wirklich Eisen und wirklich Gold sein. Für ihre Preisgebung ge-\n nügt es, vorgestelltes Gold ihr gleichzusetzen. Durch Gold ist sie\n zu ersetzen, damit sie ihrem Besitzer den Dienst eines allgemeinen\n Aequivalents leiste. Träte der Besitzer des Eisens z. B. dem Besitzer\n einer weltlustigen Waare gegenüber, und verwiese ihn auf den Eisen-\npreis, der Geldform sei, so würde der Weltlustige antworten, wie im\n Himmel der heilige Petrus dem Dante, der ihm die Glaubensformeln\n hergesagt:\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"63\", \"facs\": \"#f0082\"} -->„Assai bene è trascorsa\n D’esta moneta già la lega e’l peso,\n Ma dimmi se tu l’hai nella tua borsa.“\n\n\n\n\nDie Preisform schliesst die Veräusserlichkeit der Waaren gegen\n Geld und die Nothwendigkeit dieser Veräusserung ein. Die Preisbestim-\n mung der Waaren hat andrerseits eine im Austauschprozess befindliche\n Waare, das Gold, bereits zu Geld gemacht. Im ideellen Mass der\n Werthe lauert daher das harte Geld.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## B. Circulationsmittel.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## a) Die Metamorphose der Waaren.\n\n\n\n\nMan sah, dass der Austauschprozess der Waaren widersprechende und\n einander ausschliessende Beziehungen einschloss. Die Entwicklung der\n Waare, die wir eben betrachtet, hebt diese Widersprüche nicht auf, aber\n sie schafft die Form, worin sie sich bewegen können. Diess ist über-\n haupt die Methode, wodurch sich wirkliche Widersprüche lösen. Es ist\n z. B. ein Widerspruch, dass ein Körper beständig in einen andern fällt und\n eben so beständig von ihm weg flieht. Die Ellipse ist eine der Bewe-\n gungsformen, worin dieser Widerspruch sich eben so sehr verwirklicht\n als löst.\n\n\n\n\nSoweit der Austauschprozess der Waaren sie aus der Hand, worin sie\nNicht-Gebrauchswerthe, in die Hand überträgt, worin sie Ge-\n brauchswerthe, ist er gesellschaftlicher Stoffwechsel.\n Das Produkt einer nützlichen Arbeitsweise ersetzt das der andern. Ein-\n mal angelangt zur Stelle, wo sie als Gebrauchswerth gilt, dient die Waare\n als Gebrauchsgegenstand oder fällt in die Sphäre der Consumtion aus der\n Sphäre des Waarenaustauschs. Letztre allein interessirt uns hier. Wir\n haben also den ganzen Prozess nach der Formseite zu betrachten, also nur\n den Formwechsel oder die Metamorphose der Waaren, welche\n den gesellschaftlichen Stoffwechsel vermittelt.\n\n\n\n\nDie durchaus mangelhafte Auffassung dieses Formwechsels, der Funk-\n tionen des Geldes, der daraus entspringenden verschiednen Formbestimmt-\n heiten, die das Geld aus seinen verschiednen Funktionen schöpft, ist, abge-\n sehn von Unklarheit über den Werthbegriff selbst, dem Umstand geschul-\n det, dass jeder Formwechsel einer Waare sich im Austausch zweier\n Waaren, der Waare und der Geldwaare, darstellt. Hält man an diesem\nstofflichen Moment, dem Austausch von Waare und Gold, allein fest,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"64\", \"facs\": \"#f0083\"} --> so übersieht man grade, was man sehn soll, nämlich was sich mit der\nForm zuträgt. Man übersieht, dass die Bestimmung des Goldes als Geld\n bereits eine Formbestimmung ist, die ihm nicht als blosser Waare gehört,\n dass die andern Waaren sich in ihren Preisen selbst auf das Gold als ihre\neigne Geldgestalt beziehn, und dass es seinerseits nur die allgemeine\n unmittelbare Aequivalentform erhält, weil die Waaren überhaupt sich eine\n allgemeine relative Werthform geben müssen.\n\n\n\n\nDie Waaren gehn zunächst unvergoldet, unverzuckert, wie der Kamm\n ihnen gewachsen ist, den Austauschprozess ein. Er produzirt die Ver-\n dopplung der Waare in Waare und Geld, ein äusserer Gegen-\n satz, worin sie ihren immanenten Gegensatz von Gebrauchswerth und\n Tauschwerth darstellen. In diesem Gegensatz treten die Waaren als\n Gebrauchswerthe dem Geld als Tauschwerth gegenüber. An-\n drerseits sind beide Seiten des Gegensatzes Waaren, also Einheiten\n von Gebrauchswerth und Werth. Aber diese Einheit von Unter-\n schieden stellt sich auf jedem der beiden Pole umgekehrt dar und stellt\n dadurch zugleich deren Wechselbeziehung dar. Die Waare ist reell\n Gebrauchswerth, ihr Werthdasein erscheint nur ideell im Preis, der\n sie auf das gegenüberstehende Gold als ihre reelle Werthgestalt\n bezieht. Umgekehrt gilt das Goldmaterial nur als Werthmateriatur\n Geld. Es ist reell daher all gemeines Aequivalent, Tausch-\n werth. Sein Gebrauchswerth erscheint nur noch ideell in der\n Reihe der relativen Werthausdrücke, worin es sich auf die gegenüber-\n stehenden Waaren als den Umkreis seiner reellen Gebrauchs-\n gestalten bezieht. Diese gegensätzlichen Formen der Waaren sind\n die wirklichen Bewegungsformen ihres Austauschpro-\n zesses.\n\n\n\n\nBegleiten wir nun irgend einen Waarenbesitzer, unsern altbekannten\n Leinweber z. B., zur Scene des Austauschprozesses, dem Waarenmarkt.\n Seine Waare, 20 Ellen Leinwand, ist preisbestimmt. Ihr Preis ist\n 2 Pfd. St. Er tauscht sie aus gegen 2 Pfd. St., und, Mann von altem\n Schrot und Korn, tauscht die 2 Pfd. St. wieder aus gegen eine Familien-\n bibel vom selben Preis. Die Leinwand, für ihn nur Waare, Werthträger,\n wird entäussert gegen Gold, ihre Werthgestalt, und aus dieser Gestalt rück-\n veräussert gegen eine andere Waare, die Bibel, die aber als Gebrauchs-\n gegenstand in’s Weberhaus wandern und dort Erbauungsbedürfnisse befrie-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"65\", \"facs\": \"#f0084\"} --> digen soll. Der Austauschprozess der Waare vollzieht sich also in zwei\n entgegengesetzten und einander ergänzenden Metamor-\n phosen — Verwandlung der Waare in Geld und ihre Rück-\n verwandlung aus Geld in Waare [Note: „Ἐϰ δὲ τοῦ … πυϱὸς ἀνταμείβεσϑαι πάντα, φησὶν ὁ Ἡϱάϰλειτος,\n ϰαὶ πῦϱ ἁπάντων, ὥςπεϱ χϱυσοῦ χϱήματα ϰαὶ χϱημάτων χϱυσός.“\n (F. Lassalle: „Die Philosophie Herakleitos des Dunkeln. Ber-\n lin 1858“, Bd. I, p. 222.) Lassalle’s Note zu dieser Stelle, p. 224, n. 3, er-\n klärt das Geld unrichtig für blosses Werthzeichen.] . Die Momente der Waaren-\n metamorphose sind zugleich Händel des Waarenbesitzers — Verkauf,\n Austausch der Waare mit Geld, Kauf, Austausch des Gelds mit Waare,\n und Einheit beider Akte: Verkaufen um zu kaufen.\n\n\n\n\nBesieht sich der Leinweber nun das Endresultat des Handels, so be-\n sitzt er Bibel statt Leinwand, statt seiner ursprünglichen Waare eine\n andre vom selben Werth, aber verschiedner Nützlichkeit. In gleicher\n Weise eignet er sich seine verschiednen Lebens- und Produktionsmittel\n an. Von seinem Standpunkt vermittelt der ganze Prozess, die\n Geldwerdung der Leinwand und die Waarenwerdung des Geldes, Verkauf\n und Kauf, nur den Austausch seines Arbeitsprodukts mit fremdem Arbeits-\n produkt, den Produktenaustausch.\n\n\n\n\nDer Austauschprozess der Waare vollzieht sich also in folgendem\n Formwechsel:\nWaare — Geld — Waare.\n W — G — W.\n\n\n\n\nNach ihrem stofflichen Inhalt ist die Bewegung W — W, Austausch\n von Waare gegen Waare, Stoffwechsel der gesellschaftlichen Arbeit, in\n dessen Resultat der Prozess selbst erlischt.\n\n\n\n\nW — G. Erste Metamorphose der Waare oder Verkauf:\n Das Ueberspringen des Waarenwerths aus dem Waarenleib in den Gold-\n leib ist, wie ich es anderswo bezeichnet, der salto mortale der Waare.\n Misslingt er, so ist zwar nicht die Waare geprellt, wohl aber der Waaren-\n besitzer. Die gesellschaftliche Theilung der Arbeit macht seine Arbeit\n ebenso einseitig als seine Bedürfnisse vielseitig. Eben desswegen dient\n ihm sein Produkt nur als Tauschwerth, allgemeines Aequiva-\n lent. Allgemeine, gesellschaftlich gültige Aequivalentform erhält es\n [Running matter: I. 5] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"66\", \"facs\": \"#f0085\"} --> aber nur im Geld und das Geld befindet sich in fremder Tasche. Um es\n herauszuziehn, muss die Waare vor allem Gebrauchswerth für den\n Geldbesitzer sein, die auf sie verausgabte Arbeit also in gesellschaftlich\n nützlicher Form verausgabt sein oder sich als Glied der gesellschaft-\n lichen Theilung der Arbeit bewähren. Aber die Theilung der\n Arbeit ist ein naturwüchsiger Produktionsorganismus, dessen\n Fäden hinter dem Rücken der Waarenproducenten gewebt wurden und\n sich fortweben. Vielleicht ist die Waare Produkt einer neuen Arbeitsweise,\n die ein neu aufgekommenes Bedürfniss zu befriedigen vorgiebt oder auf\n eigne Faust ein Bedürfniss erst hervorrufen will. Gestern noch eine Funk-\n tion unter den vielen Funktionen eines und desselben Waarenproducenten,\n reisst sich eine besondre Arbeitsverrichtung heute vielleicht los von diesem\n Zusammenhang, verselbstständigt sich und schickt eben desswegen ihr\nTheilprodukt als selbstständige Waare zu Markt. Die Um-\n stände mögen reif oder unreif sein für diesen Scheidungsprozess. Das\n Produkt befriedigt heute ein gesellschaftliches Bedürfniss. Morgen wird\n es vielleicht ganz oder theilweise von einer ähnlichen Produktenart aus\n seinem Platze verdrängt. Ist auch die Arbeit, wie die unsres Leinwebers,\n patentirtes Glied der gesellschaftlichen Arbeitstheilung, so ist damit noch\n keineswegs der Gebrauchswerth grade seiner 20 Ellen Leinwand garan-\n tirt. Wenn das gesellschaftliche Bedürfniss für Leinwand, und es hat sein\n Mass, wie alles andre, bereits durch nebenbuhlerische Leinweber gesättigt\n ist, wird das Produkt unsres Freundes überschüssig, überflüssig und damit\n nutzlos. Einem geschenkten Gaul sieht man nicht ins Maul, aber er be-\n schreitet nicht den Markt, um Präsente zu machen. Gesetzt aber der Ge-\n brauchswerth seines Produkts bewähre sich und Geld werde daher ange-\n zogen von der Waare. Aber nun fragt sich’s, wie viel Geld? Die Ant-\n wort ist allerdings schon anticipirt im Preis der Waare, dem Exponenten\n ihrer Werthgrösse. Wir sehn ab von etwaigen rein subjektiven Rechen-\n fehlern des Waarenbesitzers, die auf dem Markt sofort objectiv corrigirt\n werden. Er soll auf sein Produkt nur den gesellschaftlich nothwendigen\n Durchschnitt von Arbeitszeit verausgabt haben. Der Preis der Waare ist\n also nur Geldname des in ihr vergegenständlichten Quantums gesellschaft-\n licher Arbeit. Aber ohne Erlaubniss und hinter dem Rücken unsres Lein-\n webers geriethen die altverbürgten Produktionsbedingungen der Leinweberei\n in Gährung. Was gestern zweifelsohne gesellschaftlich nothwendige Ar-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"67\", \"facs\": \"#f0086\"} --> beitszeit zur Produktion einer Elle Leinwand war, hört heute auf es zu\n sein, wie der Geldbesitzer eifrigst demonstrirt aus den Preisquotationen\n verschiedner Nebenbuhler unsres Freundes. Zu seinem Unglück giebt’s\n viele Weber auf der Welt. Gesetzt endlich jedes auf dem Markt vor-\n handne Stück Leinwand enthalte nur gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit. Trotzdem kann die Gesammtsumme dieser Stücke überflüssig ver-\n ausgabte Arbeitszeit enthalten. Vermag der Marktmagen das Gesammt-\n quantum Leinwand, zum Normalpreis von 2 Sh. per Elle, nicht zu ab-\n sorbiren, so beweist das, dass ein zu grosser Theil der gesellschaftlichen\n Gesammtarbeitszeit in der Form der Leinweberei verausgabt wurde. Die\n Wirkung ist dieselbe als hätte jeder einzelne Leinweber mehr als die ge-\n sellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit auf sein individuelles Produkt ver-\n wandt. Hier heisst’s: Mitgefangen, mitgehangen. Alle Leinwand auf\n dem Markt gilt nur als ein Handelsartikel, jedes Stück nur als aliquoter\n Theil. Und in der That ist der Werth jeder individuellen Elle ja auch\n nur die Materiatur desselben gesellschaftlich bestimmten Quantums gleich-\n artiger menschlicher Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nMan sieht, die Waare liebt das Geld, aber „the course of true love runs\n never smooth.“ Ebenso naturwüchsig zufällig, wie die qualitative, ist\n die quantitative Gliederung des gesellschaftlichen Produktionsorganis-\n mus, der seine membra disjecta im System der Theilung der Arbeit dar-\n stellt. Unsere Waarenbesitzer entdecken daher, dass dieselbe Theilung\n der Arbeit, die sie zu unabhängigen Privatproducenten, den\n gesellschaftlichen Produktionsprozess und ihre Verhältnisse in diesem Pro-\n zess von ihnen selbst unabhängig macht, dass die Unabhängig-\n keit der Personen von einander sich in einem System allseitiger sachlicher\n Abhängigkeit ergänzt.\n\n\n\n\nDie Theilung der Arbeit verwandelt das Arbeitsprodukt in Waare\n und macht dadurch seine Verwandlung in Geld nothwendig. Sie macht\n es zugleich zufällig, ob diese Transsubstantiation gelingt. Hier ist jedoch\n das Phänomen rein zu betrachten, sein normaler Vorgang also vorauszu-\n setzen. Wenn es übrigens überhaupt vorgeht, die Waare also nicht un-\n verkäuflich ist, findet stets ihr Formwechsel statt, obgleich abnormal\n in diesem Formwechsel Substanz — Werthgrösse — eingebüsst oder zuge-\n setzt werden mag.\n\n\n\n\nDem einen Waarenbesitzer ersetzt Gold seine Waare und dem an-\n [Running matter: 5*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"68\", \"facs\": \"#f0087\"} --> dern Waare sein Gold. Das sinnfällige Phänomen ist der Hände-\n oder Stellenwechsel von Waare und Gold, von 20 Ellen Leinwand\n und 2 Pfd. St., d. h. ihr Austausch. Aber womit tauscht sich die\n Waare aus? Mit ihrer eignen allgemeinen Werthgestalt. Und womit das\n Gold? Mit einer besondern Gestalt seines Gebrauchswerths. Warum\n tritt Gold der Leinwand als Geld gegenüber? Weil ihr Preis von\n 2 Pfd. St. oder ihr Geldname sie bereits auf Gold als Geld bezieht.\n Die Entäusserung der ursprünglichen Waarenform vollzieht sich durch\n die Veräusserung der Waare, d. h. in dem Augenblicke, wo ihr Ge-\n brauchswerth das in ihrem Preis nur vorgestellte Gold wirklich anzieht.\n Die Realisirung des Preises oder der nur ideellen Werthform der\n Waare ist daher zugleich umgekehrt Realisirung des nur ideellen Ge-\n brauchswerths des Geldes, die Verwandlung von Waare in Geld zugleich\n Verwandlung von Geld in Waare. Der eine Prozess ist zweiseitiger\n Prozess, vom Pol des Waarenbesitzers Verkauf, vom Gegenpol des\n Geldbesitzers Kauf. Oder Verkauf ist Kauf, W — G zugleich\n G — W [Note: „Toute vente est achat“ (Dr. Quesnay: „Dialogues sur le Com-\n merce et les Travaux des Artisans“. Physiocrates, ed. Daire,\n I. Partie, Paris 1846, p. 170), oder, wie Quesnay in seinen „Maximes\n Générales“ sagt: „Vendre est acheter.“] .\n\n\n\n\nWir kennen bisher kein andres ökonomisches Verhältniss der Men-\n schen zu einander ausser dem von Waarenbesitzern, ein Verhältniss, worin\n sie fremdes Arbeitsprodukt nur aneignen, indem sie eignes entfremden.\n Einem Waarenbesitzer kann der andre daher nur als Geldbesitzer gegenüber-\n treten, entweder weil sein Arbeitsprodukt von Natur die Geldform besitzt,\n also Geldmaterial ist, Gold u. s. w., oder weil seine eigne Waare sich bereits\n gehäutet und ihre ursprüngliche Gebrauchsform abgestreift hat. Um als Geld\n zu funktioniren, muss das Gold natürlich an irgend einem Punkt in den Waaren-\n markt eintreten. Dieser Punkt liegt an seiner Produktionsquelle, wo es sich\n als unmittelbares Arbeitsprodukt mit anderm Arbeitsprodukt von demselben\n Werth austauscht. Aber von diesem Augenblick funktionirt es nur als\n wirkliches Geld, weil es beständig realisirter Waarenpreis ist [Note: „Le prix d’une marchandise ne pouvant pas être payé que par le prix\n d’une autre marchandise.“ (Mercier de la Rivière: „L’Ordre na-] .\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"69\", \"facs\": \"#f0088\"} --> Abgesehn vom Austausch des Golds mit Waare an seiner Produktions-\n quelle, ist das Gold in der Hand jedes Waarenbesitzers die entäusserte Ge-\n stalt seiner veräusserten Waare, Produkt des Verkaufs oder der ersten\n Waarenmetamorphose W — G [Note: „Pour avoir cet argent, il faut avoir vendu.“ (l. c. p. 543.)] . Ideelles Geld oder Werth-\n mass wurde das Gold, weil alle Waaren ihre Werthe in ihm massen und es so\nvorstellungsweise zu ihrer entäusserten Gebrauchsgestalt oder Werth-\n gestalt machten. Reelles Geld wird es, weil die Waaren durch ihre\n allseitige Veräusserung es zu ihrer wirklich entäusserten oder verwandelten\n Gebrauchsgestalt und daher zu ihrer wirklichen Werthgestalt machen.\n In ihrer Werthgestalt streift die Waare jede Spur ihres naturwüchsigen\n Gebrauchswerths und der besondern nützlichen Arbeit ab, welcher sie den\n Ursprung verdankt, um sich in die gleichförmige gesellschaftliche Materiatur\n unterschiedsloser menschlicher Arbeit zu verpuppen. Man sieht dem Geld\n daher nicht an, welchen Schlags die in es verwandelte Waare. Eine sieht\n in ihrer Geldform grade aus wie die andre. Geld mag daher Dreck sein,\n obgleich Dreck nicht Geld ist. Wir wollen annehmen, dass die zwei\n Goldfüchse, wogegen unser Leineweber seine Waare veräussert, die ver-\n wandelte Gestalt eines Quarters Weizen sind. Der Verkauf der Leinwand,\n W — G, ist zugleich ihr Kauf, G — W. Aber als Verkauf der\n Leinwand beginnt dieser Prozess eine Bewegung, die mit seinem Gegen-\n theil endet, mit dem Kauf der Bibel; als Kauf der Leinwand\n endet er eine Bewegung, die mit seinem Gegentheil begann, mit dem\nVerkauf des Weizens. W — G (Leinwand — Geld), die erste\n Phase von W — G — W (Leinwand — Geld — Bibel), ist zugleich\n G — W (Geld — Leinwand), die letzte Phase einer andern Bewegung\n W — G — W (Weizen — Geld — Leinwand). Die erste Meta-\n morphose einer Waare, ihre Verwandlung aus der Waarenform in\n Geld, ist stets zugleich zweite entgegengesetzte Metamorphose\n einer andern Waare, ihre Rückverwandlung aus der Geldform in\n Waare [Note: Ausnahme, wie vorher bemerkt, bildet der Gold - resp. Silberproducent,\n der sein Produkt austauscht, ohne es vorher verkauft zu haben.] .\n\n\n\n\n [Note: turel et essentiel des sociétés politiques“. Physiocrates, ed.\n Daire, II. Partie, p. 554.)] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"70\", \"facs\": \"#f0089\"} -->\n\nG — W. Zweite oder Schlussmetamorphose der\n Waare. Kauf. — Weil die entäusserte Gestalt aller andern Waaren\n oder das Produkt ihrer allgemeinen Veräusserung, ist Geld die absolut\n veräusserliche Waare. Es liest alle Preise rückwärts und spiegelt\n sich so in allen Waarenleibern als dem hingebenden Material seiner eignen\n Waarenwerdung. Zugleich zeigen die Preise, die Liebesaugen, womit\n ihm die Waaren winken, die Schranke seiner Verwandlungsfähig-\n keit, nämlich seine eigne Quantität. Da die Waare in ihrer Geld-\n werdung verschwindet, sieht man dem Geld nicht an, wie es in die Hände\n seines Besitzers gelangt oder was in es verwandelt ist. Non olet, wessen\n Ursprungs auch immer. Als entäusserte Gestalt steht es den\n Waaren, ihm als der absolut veräusserlichen Waarengestalt\n steht die Waarenwelt gegenüber [Note: „Si l’argent représente, dans nos mains, les choses que nous pouvons\n désirer d’acheter, il y représente aussi les choses que nous avous vendues\n pour cet argent.“ (Mercier de la Rivière l. c. p. 586.)] .\n\n\n\n\nG — W, der Kauf ist zugleich Verkauf, W — G, die letzte Meta-\n morphose einer Waare daher zugleich die erste Metamorphose einer andern\n Waare. Für unsern Leinweber schliesst der Lebenslauf seiner Waare\n mit der Bibel, worin er die 2 Pfd. St. rückverwandelt hat. Aber der ur-\n sprüngliche Bibelbesitzer setzt die vom Leineweber gelösten 2 Pfd. St. in\n Kornbranntwein um, G — W, die Schlussphase von W — G — W (Lein-\n wand — Geld — Bibel) ist zugleich W — G, die erste Phase von W —\n G — W (Bibel — Geld — Kornbranntwein). Da der Waarenproduzent\n nur ein einseitiges Produkt liefert, verkauft er es oft in grösseren Mas-\n sen, während seine vielseitigen Bedürfnisse ihn zwingen, den realisirten\n Preis oder die gelöste Geldsumme beständig in zahlreiche Käufe zu zer-\n splittern. Ein Verkauf mündet daher in viele Käufe verschiedner\n Waaren. Die Schlussmetamorphose einer Waare bildet so eine Summe\n von ersten Metamorphosen andrer Waaren.\n\n\n\n\nBetrachten wir nun die Gesammtmetamorphose einer Waare,\n z. B. der Leinwand, so sehn wir zunächst, dass sie aus zwei entgegenge-\n setzten und einander ergänzenden Bewegungen besteht, W — G und G —\n W. Die zwei entgegengesetzten Wandlungen vollziehn sich in zwei ent-\n gegengesetzten gesellschaftlichen Prozessen des Waarenbesitzers und reflek-\n tiren sich in zwei entgegengesetzten ökonomischen Charakteren dessel-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"71\", \"facs\": \"#f0090\"} --> ben. Als Agent des Verkaufs wird er Verkäufer, als Agent des Kaufs\nKäufer. Wie aber in jeder Wandlung der Waare ihre beiden Formen,\n Waarenform und Geldform, gleichzeitig existiren, nur auf entgegengesetz-\n ten Polen, so steht demselben Waarenbesitzer als Verkäufer ein andrer\n Käufer und als Käufer ein andrer Verkäufer gegenüber. Wie dieselbe\n Waare die zwei umgekehrten Wandlungen successiv durchläuft, aus Waare\n Geld und aus Geld Waare wird, so wechselt derselbe Waarenbesitzer die\n Rollen von Verkäufer und Käufer. Es sind diess also keine festen, son-\n dern im Tauschprozess der Waaren beständig die Person wechselnden\n Charaktere.\n\n\n\n\nDie Gesammtmetamorphose einer Waare unterstellt, in ihrer einfach-\n sten Form, vier Extreme und drei Personae dramatis. Erst\n tritt der Waare das Geld als ihre entäusserte Gestalt gegenüber, die\n jenseits, in fremder Tasche, sachlich harte Realität besitzt. So tritt dem\n Waarenbesitzer ein Geldbesitzer gegenüber. Sobald die Waare nun in\n Geld verwandelt, wird letztres zu ihrer verschwindenden Aequi-\n valentform, deren Gebrauchswerth oder Inhalt diesseits in andern\n Waarenkörpern existirt. Als Endpunkt der ersten Waarenwandlung ist\n das Geld zugleich Ausgangspunkt der zweiten. So der Verkäufer im\n ersten Act Käufer im zweiten, wo ihm ein dritter Waarenbesitzer als Ver-\n käufer gegenübertritt [Note: „Il y a donc quatre termes et trois contractants, dont l’un intervient deux\n fois.“ (Le Trosne l. c. p. 908.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDie beiden umgekehrten Bewegungsphasen der Waarenmetamorphose\n bilden einen Kreislauf: Waarenform, Abstreifung der Waarenform,\n Rückkehr zur Waarenform. Allerdings ist die Waare selbst hier\n gegensätzlich bestimmt, am Ausgangspunkt Nicht-Gebrauchswerth, am\n Endpunkt Gebrauchswerth für ihren Besitzer, wie das Geld die Waare erst\n als festen Werthkrystall darstellt, um hinterher als ihre blosse Aequiva-\n lentform zu zerrinnen.\n\n\n\n\nDie zwei Metamorphosen, die den Kreislauf einer Waare, bilden\n zugleich die umgekehrten Theilmetamorphosen zweier andren Waa-\n ren. Dieselbe Waare (Leinwand) eröffnet die Reihe ihrer eignen Meta-\n morphosen und schliesst die Gesammtmetamorphose einer andern Waare\n (des Weizens). Während ihrer ersten Wandlung, dem Verkauf, spielt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"72\", \"facs\": \"#f0091\"} --> sie diese zwei Rollen in eigner Person. Als Goldchrysalide dagegen,\n worin sie selbst den Weg alles Fleisches wandert, endet sie zugleich die\n erste Metamorphose einer dritten Waare. Der Kreislauf, den die Meta-\n morphosenreihe jeder Waare bildet, verschlingt sich also unentwirrbar\n mit den Kreisläufen andrer Waaren. Der Gesammtprozess stellt sich dar\n als Waarencirculation.\n\n\n\n\nDie Waarencirculation ist nicht nur formell, sondern wesentlich vom\n unmittelbaren Produktenaustausch unterschieden. Man werfe nur einen\n Rückblick auf den Vorgang. Der Leineweber hat unbedingt Leinwand\n mit Bibel vertauscht, eigne Waare mit fremder. Aber diess Phänomen\n ist nur wahr für ihn. Der Bibelagent, der dem Kühlen Heisses vorzieht,\n dachte nicht daran, Leinwand für Bibel einzutauschen, wie der Leineweber\n nicht davon weiss, dass Weizen gegen seine Leinwand eingetauscht wor-\n den ist u. s. w. Die Waare des B ersetzt die Waare des A, aber A und\n B tauschen nicht wechselseitig ihre Waaren aus. Es kann in der\n That vorkommen, dass A und B wechselweis von einander kaufen, aber\n solche besondre Beziehung ist keineswegs durch die allgemeinen Verhält-\n nisse der Waarencirculation bedingt. Einerseits sieht man hier, wie der\n Waarenaustausch die individuellen und lokalen Schranken\n des unmittelbaren Produktenaustauschs durchbricht und den Stoffwechsel\n der menschlichen Arbeit entwickelt. Andrerseits entwickelt sich ein gan-\n zer Kreis von den handelnden Personen uncontrolirbarer, gesellschaftlicher\n Naturzusammenhänge. Der Weber kann nur Leinwand verkaufen, weil\n der Bauer Weizen, Heisssporn nur die Bibel, weil der Weber Leinwand,\n der Destillateur nur gebranntes Wasser, weil der andre das Wasser des\n ewigen Lebens bereits verkauft hat u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nDer Circulationsprozess erlischt desswegen auch nicht, wie der un-\n mittelbare Produktenaustausch, in dem Stellen - oder Händewechsel der\n Gebrauchswerthe. Das Geld verschwindet nicht, weil es schliesslich aus\n der Metamorphosenreihe einer Waare herausfällt. Es schlägt immer\n nieder auf eine durch die Waaren geräumte Circulationsstelle. Z. B. in\n der Gesammtmetamorphose der Leinwand: Leinwand — Geld —\n Bibel fällt erst die Leinwand aus der Circulation, Geld tritt an ihre\n Stelle, fällt dann die Bibel aus der Circulation, Geld tritt an ihre Stelle.\n Der Ersatz von Waare durch Waare lässt zugleich an dritter Hand die\n Geldwaare hängen. Die Circulation schwitzt beständig Geld aus.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"73\", \"facs\": \"#f0092\"} -->\n\nNichts kann alberner sein als das Dogma, die Waarencirculation be-\n dinge ein nothwendiges Gleichgewicht der Verkäufe und Käufe, weil jeder\n Verkauf Kauf und vice versa. Meint diess, dass die Zahl der wirklich\n vollzognen Verkäufe gleich derselben Zahl von Käufen, so ist es platte\n Tautologie. Aber es soll beweisen, dass der Verkäufer seinen eignen\n Käufer zu Markt führt. Verkauf und Kauf sind ein identischer Act\n als Wechselbeziehung zweier polarisch entgegengesetzter\n Personen, des Waarenbesitzers und des Geldbesitzers. Sie bilden\nzweipolarisch entgegengesetzte Akte als Handlungen dersel-\n ben Person. Die Identität von Verkauf und Kauf schliesst daher ein,\n dass die Waare nutzlos wird, wenn sie, in die alchymistische Retorte\n der Circulation geworfen, nicht als Geld herauskommt, nicht vom Waa-\n renbesitzer verkauft, also vom Geldbesitzer gekauft wird. Jene Identität\n enthält ferner, dass der Prozess, wenn er gelingt, einen Ruhepunkt, einen\n Lebensabschnitt der Waare bildet, der länger oder kürzer währen kann.\n Da die erste Metamorphose der Waare zugleich Verkauf und Kauf,\n ist dieser Theilprozess zugleich selbstständiger Prozess. Der Käufer hat\n die Waare, der Verkäufer hat das Geld, d. h. eine Waare, die circula-\n tionsfähige Form bewahrt, ob sie früher oder später wieder auf dem\n Markt erscheine. Keiner kann verkaufen, ohne dass ein Andrer kauft.\n Aber keiner braucht unmittelbar zu kaufen, weil er selbst verkauft hat.\n Die Circulation sprengt die zeitlichen, örtlichen und individuellen Schran-\n ken des Produktenaustauschs eben dadurch, dass sie die hier vorhandne\nunmittelbare Identität zwischen dem Austausch des eignen und\n dem Eintausch des fremden Arbeitsprodukts in den Gegensatz von\n Verkauf und Kauf spaltet. Dass die selbstständig einander gegenüber-\n tretenden Prozesse eine innere Einheit bilden, heisst eben so sehr,\n dass ihre innere Einheit sich in äusseren Gegensätzen bewegt.\n Geht die äusserliche Verselbstständigung der innerlich Unselbstständigen,\n weil einander ergänzenden, bis zu einem gewissen Punkt fort, so macht\n sich die Einheit gewaltsam geltend durch eine — Krise. Der der Waare\n immanente Gegensatz von Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth, von Privat-\n arbeit, die sich zugleich als unmittelbar gesellschaftliche Arbeit darstellen\n muss, von besondrer konkreter Arbeit, die zugleich nur als abstrakt all-\n gemeine Arbeit gilt, von Personificirung der Sache und Versachlichung der\n Personen — dieser immanente Widerspruch erhält in den Gegensätzen der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"74\", \"facs\": \"#f0093\"} --> Waarenmetamorphose seine entwickelten Bewegungsformen. Diese\n Formen schliessen daher die Möglichkeit, aber auch nur die Möglich-\n keit der Krisen ein. Die Entwicklung dieser Möglichkeit zur Wirklich-\n keit erfordert einen ganzen Umkreis von Verhältnissen, die vom Standpunkt\n der einfachen Waarencirculation noch gar nicht existiren [Note: Vergleiche meine Bemerkungen über James Mill: „Zur Kritik\n etc.“ p. 74—76. Zwei Punkte sind hier charakteristisch für die Methode der\n ökonomistischen Apologetik. Erstens die Identificirung von Waarencircula-\n tion und unmittelbarem Produktenaustausch durch einfache Abstraktion\n von ihren Unterschieden. Zweitens der Versuch, die Widersprüche des kapi-\n talistischen Produktionsprozesses wegzuleugnen, indem man die Ver-\n hältnisse seiner Produktionsagenten in die einfachen Beziehungen auflöst, die aus\n der Waarencirculation entspringen. Waarenproduktion und Waarencirculation\n sind aber Phänomene, die den verschiedensten Produktionsweisen angehören, wenn\n auch in verschiednem Umfang und Tragweite. Man weiss also noch nichts von\n der differentia specifica dieser Produktionsweisen und kann sie daher nicht beur-\n theilen, wenn man nur die ihnen gemeinschaftlichen, abstrakten Kategorien der\n Waarencirculation kennt. In keiner andern Wissenschaft ausser der politischen\n Oekonomie herrscht ähnliche Wichtigthuerei mit elementarischer Gemeinplätzlich-\n keit. Z. B. J. B. Say nimmt sich heraus, über die Krisen abzuurtheilen,\n weil er weiss, dass die Waare Produkt ist.] .\n\n\n\n\nAls Vermittler der Waarencirculation erhält das Geld die Funktion\n des Circulationsmittels.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## b) Der Umlauf des Geldes.\n\n\n\n\nDer Formwechsel, worin sich der Stoffwechsel der Arbeitsprodukte\n vollzieht, W — G — W, bedingt, dass derselbe Werth als Waare\n den Ausgangspunkt des Prozesses bildet und zu demselben Punkt zurück-\n kehrt als Waare. Diese Bewegung der Waaren ist daher Kreis-\n lauf. Andrerseits schliesst dieselbe Form den Kreislauf des Geldes aus.\n Ihr Resultat ist beständige Entfernung des Geldes von seinem\n Ausgangspunkt, nicht Rückkehr zu demselben. So lange der Verkäufer\n die verwandelte Gestalt seiner Waare festhält, das Geld, befindet sich die\n Waare im Stadium der ersten Metamorphose oder hat nur ihre erste Cir-\n culationshälfte zurückgelegt. Ist der Prozess, Verkaufen um zu kau-\n fen, vervollständigt, so ist auch das Geld wieder aus der Hand ihres\n ursprünglichen Besitzers entfernt. Allerdings, wenn der Leinweber, nach-\n dem er die Bibel gekauft, von neuem Leinwand verkauft, kehrt auch das\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"75\", \"facs\": \"#f0094\"} --> Geld in seine Hand zurück. Aber es kehrt nicht zurück durch den Circula-\n tionsprozess der ersten 20 Ellen Leinwand. Er hat es vielmehr aus den\n Händen des Leinwebers in die des Bibelverkäufers entfernt. Es kehrt nur\n zurück durch die Erneuerung oder Wiederholung desselben Circula-\n tionsprozesses für neue Waare, und endet hier wie dort mit demselben Re-\n sultat. Die dem Geld durch die Waarencirculation unmittelbar ertheilte\n Bewegungsform ist daher seine beständige Entfernung vom Ausgangspunkt,\n sein Lauf aus der Hand eines Waarenbesitzers in die eines andern, oder\nsein Umlauf (currency, cours de la monnaie).\n\n\n\n\nDer Umlauf des Geldes zeigt beständige, eintönige Wiederholung\ndesselben Prozesses. Die Waare steht stets auf Seite des Verkäufers,\n das Geld stets auf Seite des Käufers, als Kaufmittel. Es funktionirt\n als Kaufmittel, indem es den Preis der Waare realisirt. Indem es\n ihn realisirt, überträgt es die Waare aus der Hand des Verkäufers in die\n des Känfers, während es sich gleichzeitig aus der Hand des Käufers in die\n des Verkäufers entfernt, um denselben Prozess mit einer andern Waare zu\n wiederholen. Dass diese einseitige Form der Geldbewegung aus der dop-\n pelseitigen Formbewegung der Waare entspringt, ist verhüllt. Die Natur\n der Waarencirculation selbst erzeugt den entgegengesetzten Schein. Die\n erste Metamorphose der Waare ist nicht nur als Bewegung des Geldes,\n sondern als ihre eigne Bewegung sichtbar, aber ihre zweite Metamorphose\n ist nur als Bewegung des Geldes sichtbar. In ihrer ersten Circulations-\n hälfte wechselt die Waare den Platz mit dem Geld. Damit fällt zugleich\n ihre Gebrauchsgestalt aus der Circulation heraus, in die Consumtion [Note: Selbst wenn die Waare wieder und wieder verkauft wird, ein Phänomen,\n das hier noch nicht für uns existirt, fällt sie mit dem letzten definitiven Verkauf\n aus der Sphäre der Circulation in die der Consumtion, um hier als Lebensmittel\n oder als Produktionsmittel zu dienen.] .\n Ihre Werthgestalt oder Geldlarve tritt an ihre Stelle. Die zweite Circu-\n lationshälfte durchläuft sie nicht mehr in ihrer eignen Naturalhaut, sondern\n in ihrer Goldhaut. Die Continuität der Bewegung fällt damit ganz\n auf die Seite des Geldes und dieselbe Bewegung, die für die Waare zwei\n entgegengesetzte Prozesse einschliesst, schliesst als eigne Bewegung\n des Geldes stets denselben Prozess ein, seinen Stellenwechsel mit stets\n andrer Waare. Das Resultat der Waarencirculation, Ersatz von Waare\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"76\", \"facs\": \"#f0095\"} --> durch andre Waare, erscheint daher nicht durch ihren eignen Formwechsel\n vermittelt, sondern durch die Funktion des Geldes als Circulations-\n mittel, welches die an und für sich bewegungslosen Waaren circulirt,\n sie aus der Hand, worin sie Nicht-Gebrauchswerthe, in die Hand überträgt,\n worin sie Gebrauchswerthe, stets in entgegengesetzter Richtung zu seinem\n eignen Lauf. Es entfernt die Waaren beständig aus der Circulations-\n sphäre, indem es beständig an ihre Circulationsstelle tritt und sich damit\n von seinem eignen Ausgangspunkt entfernt. Obgleich daher die Geldbe-\n wegung nur Ausdruck der Waarencirculation, erscheint umgekehrt die\n Waarencirculation nur als Resultat der Geldbewegung [Note: „Il (l’argent) n’a d’autre mouvement que celui qui est imprimé par les pro-\n ductions.“ (Le Trosne l. c. p. 885.)] .\n\n\n\n\nAndrerseits kommt dem Geld nur die Funktion des Circulationsmittels\n zu, weil es die verselbstständigte Werthgestalt der Waaren ist. Seine Be-\n wegung als Circulationsmittel ist daher in der That nur ihre eigne Form-\n bewegung. Diese muss sich daher auch sinnlich im Umlauf des Geldes\n wiederspiegeln. Der doppelte Formwechsel der Waare spiegelt sich wie-\n der im zweimaligen Stellenwechsel desselben Geldstücks,\n wenn wir die Gesammtmetamorphose einer Waare, in der mehrmaligen\n Wiederholung seines Stellenwechsels, wenn wir die Ver-\n schlingung der zahllosen Metamorphosen in einander betrachten. Diesel-\n ben Geldstücke kommen als entäusserte Gestalt der Waare in die\n Hand des Verkäufers und verlassen sie als absolut veräusserliche\n Gestalt der Waare. Das Geld wirkt in derselben Weise als Kauf-\n mittel, jedesmal gegenüber andrer Waare, in beiden Prozessen. Aber ihr\n innerer Zusammenhang, oder die doppelte Formbestimmtheit, die es in bei-\n den Prozessen für dieselbe Waare hat, erscheint in der doppelten und\n gegensätzlichen Bewegung, die denselben Geldstücken aufgedrückt wird.\nDieselben 2 Pfd. St., die beim Verkauf der Leinwand aus der Tasche\n des Weizenbauers in die Tasche des Leinwebers einwandern, wandern von\n ihr aus beim Kauf der Bibel. Es ist doppelter Stellenwechsel, und die\n Leinwand oder ihren Repräsentanten als Centrum betrachtet, in entgegen-\n gesetzter Richtung, positiver bei Einnahme des Geldes, negativer bei sei-\n ner Ausgabe. Finden dagegen nur einseitige Waarenmetamorphosen\n statt, blosse Verkäufe oder blosse Käufe, wie man will, so wechselt das-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"77\", \"facs\": \"#f0096\"} --> selbe Geld auch nur einmal den Platz. Sein zweiter Stellenwechsel drückt\n stets die zweite Metamorphose der Waare aus, ihre Rückverwandlung aus\n Geld. Es versteht sich übrigens ganz von selbst, dass alles diess nur für\n die hier betrachtete Form der einfachen Waarencirculation gilt.\n\n\n\n\nJede Waare, bei ihrem ersten Schritt in die Circulation, ihrem ersten\n Formwechsel, fällt aus der Circulation heraus, während stets neue Waare\n in sie eintritt. Das Geld dagegen als Circulationsmittel haust beständig\n in der Circulationssphäre und treibt sich beständig in ihr um. Es ent-\n steht also die Frage, wie viel Geld diese Sphäre beständig absorbirt.\n\n\n\n\nIn einem Lande geht jeden Tag gleichzeitig, daher räumlich neben\n einander, eine ungeheure Anzahl einseitiger Waarenmetamorphosen vor, oder\n eine zahllose Masse zersplitterter Verkäufe, welche die von einander unab-\n hängigen Waarenbesitzer vollziehen. In ihren Preisen sind die Waaren\n bereits bestimmten vorgestellten Geldquantis gleichgesetzt. Da nun die\n hier betrachtete, unmittelbare Circulationsform Waare und Geld einander\n stets leiblich gegenüberstellt, die eine auf den Pol des Verkaufs, das andre\n auf den Gegenpol des Kaufs, ist die für den Circulationsprozess der Waaren-\n welt erheischte Masse von Circulationsmitteln bereits durch die Preis-\n summe der Waaren bestimmt. In der That stellt das Geld nur reell die\n in der Preissumme der Waaren bereits ideell ausgedrückte Goldsumme\n dar. Die Gleichheit dieser Summen versteht sich daher von selbst. Wir\n wissen jedoch, dass bei gleichbleibenden Werthen der Waaren ihre Preise\n mit dem Werthe des Goldes (des Geldmaterials) selbst wechseln, verhält-\n nissmässig steigen, wenn er fällt, und fallen, wenn er steigt. Ob die Preis-\n summe der Waaren so steige oder falle, die Masse des circulirenden Geldes\n muss gleichmässig steigen oder fallen. Der Wechsel in der Masse der\n Circulationsmittel entspringt hier allerdings aus dem Geld selbst, aber\nnicht aus seiner Funktion als Circulationsmittel, son-\n dern aus seiner Funktion als Werthmass. Der Preis der\n Waaren wechselt erst umgekehrt wie der Werth des Geldes\n und dann wechselt die Masse der Circulationsmittel direkt wie der Preis\n der Waaren. Ganz dasselbe Phänomen würde sich ereignen, wenn z. B.\n nicht der Werth des Goldes sänke, sondern Silber es als Werthmass er-\n setzte, oder nicht der Werth des Silbers stiege, sondern Gold es aus der\n Funktion des Werthmasses verdrängte. In dem einen Fall müsste mehr\n Silber circuliren als vorher Gold, in dem andern weniger Gold als vorher\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"78\", \"facs\": \"#f0097\"} --> Silber. In beiden Fällen hätte sich der Werth des Geldmaterials\n verändert, d. h. der Waare, die als Mass der Werthe funktionirt, da-\n her der Preisausdruck der Waarenwerthe, daher die Masse des circuliren-\n den Geldes, das zur Realisirung dieser Preise dient. Man hat gesehn,\n dass die Circulationssphäre der Waaren ein Loch hat, wodurch Gold (Sil-\n ber, kurz das Geldmaterial) in sie eintritt als Waare von gegebnem\n Werth. Dieser Werth ist vorausgesetzt bei der Funktion des Geldes\n als Werthmass, also bei der Preisbestimmung. Sinkt nun z. B. der Werth\n des Werthmasses selbst, so erscheint diess zunächst im Preiswechsel der\n Waaren, die unmittelbar an den Produktionsquellen der edlen Metalle mit\n ihnen als Waaren ausgetauscht werden. Namentlich in minder entwickel-\n ten Zuständen der bürgerlichen Gesellschaft wird ein grosser Theil der\n andern Waaren noch längere Zeit in dem nun illusorisch gewordnen, ver-\n alteten Werth des Werthmasses geschätzt werden. Indess inficirt eine\n Waare die andre durch ihr relatives Werthverhältniss zu derselben, die\n Gold- oder Silberpreise der Waaren gleichen sich allmählig aus in den\n durch ihre Werthe selbst bestimmten Proportionen, bis schliesslich alle\n Waarenwerthe dem neuen Werth des Geldmetalls entsprechend geschätzt\n werden. Dieser Ausgleichungsprozess ist begleitet von dem fortwähren-\n den Wachsthum der edlen Metalle, welche im Ersatz für die direkt mit\n ihnen ausgetauschten Waaren einströmen. In demselben Mass daher,\n worin die berichtigte Preisgebung der Waaren sich verallgemeinert, oder\n ihre Werthe dem neuen, gesunkenen und bis zu einem gewissen Punkt\n fortsinkenden Werth des Metalls gemäss geschätzt werden, ist auch bereits\n seine zu ihrer Realisirung nothwendige Mehrmasse vorhanden. Einseitige\n Beobachtung der Thatsachen, welche der Entdeckung der neuen Gold- und\n Silberquellen folgten, verleitete im 17. und namentlich im 18. Jahrhun-\n dert zum Trugschluss, die Waarenpreise seien gestiegen, weil mehr Gold\n und Silber als Circulationsmittel funktionirten. Im Folgenden wird der\n Werth des Goldes als gegeben vorausgesetzt, wie er in der That im\n Augenblick der Preisschätzung gegeben ist.\n\n\n\n\nUnter dieser Voraussetzung also ist die Masse der Circulationsmittel\n durch die zu realisirende Preissumme der Waaren bestimmt. Setzen\n wir nun ferner den Preis jeder Waarenart als gegeben voraus, so hängt\n die Preissumme der Waaren offenbar von der in Circulation befind-\n lichen Waarenmasse ab. Es gehört wenig Kopfbrechens dazu, um\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"79\", \"facs\": \"#f0098\"} --> zu begreifen, dass wenn 1 Quarter Weizen 2 Pfd. St., 100 Quarter 200\n Pfd. St., 200 Quarter 400 Pfd. St. u. s. w. kosten, mit der Masse des\n Weizens daher die Geldmasse wachsen muss, die beim Verkauf den Platz\n mit ihm wechselt.\n\n\n\n\nDie Waarenmasse als gegeben vorausgesetzt, fluthet die Masse\n des circulirenden Geldes auf und ab mit den Preisschwankungen der\n Waaren. Sie steigt und fällt, weil die Preissumme der Waaren in\n Folge ihres Preiswechsels zu- oder abnimmt. Dazu ist keineswegs nöthig,\n dass die Preise aller Waaren gleichzeitig steigen oder fallen. Die Preis-\n steigerung einer gewissen Anzahl leitender Artikel in dem einen, oder ihre\n Preissenkung in dem andern Fall, reicht hin, um die zu realisirende\nPreissumme aller circulirenden Waaren zu erhöhn oder zu senken,\n also auch mehr oder weniger Geld in Circulation zu setzen. Ob der Preis-\n wechsel der Waaren wirkliche Werthwechsel wiederspiegelt oder blosse\n Schwankungen der Marktpreise, die Wirkung auf die Masse der Circula-\n tionsmittel bleibt dieselbe.\n\n\n\n\nEs sei gegeben eine Anzahl zusammenhangsloser, gleichzeitiger und\n daher räumlich neben einander fallender Verkäufe oder Theilmetamor-\n phosen, z. B. von 2 Quarter Weizen, 20 Ellen Leinwand, 1 Bibel, 4 Gal-\n lons Kornbranntwein. Wenn der Preis jedes Artikels 2 Pfd. St., die zu\n realisirende Preissumme daher 8 Pfd. St., so muss eine Geldmasse von\n 8 Pfd. St. in die Circulation eingehn. Bilden dieselben Waaren dagegen\n Glieder der uns bekannten Metamorphosenreihe: 1 Quarter Weizen —\n 2 Pfd. St. — 20 Ellen Leinwand — 2 Pfd. St. — 1 Bibel — 2 Pfd. St.\n — 4 Gallons Kornbranntwein — 2 Pfd. St., so circuliren dieselben\n 2 Pfd. St. die verschiedenen Waaren der Reihe nach, indem sie deren\n Preise der Reihe nach, also auch die Preissumme von 8 Pfd. St. realisiren,\n um schliesslich in der Hand des Destillateurs auszuruhn. Sie vollbringen\nvier Umläufe. Dieser wiederholte Stellenwechsel derselben Geldstücke\n stellt den doppelten Formwechsel der Waare dar, ihre Bewegung durch\n zwei entgegengesetzte Circulationsstadien und die Verschlingung der Meta-\n morphosen verschiedner Waaren [Note: „Ce sont les productions qui le (l’argent) mettent en mouvement et le\n font circuler … La célérité de son mouvement (sc. de l’argent) supplée à sa\n quantité. Lorqu’il en est besoin, il ne fait que glisser d’une main dans l’autre sans\n s’arrêter un instant.“ (Le Trosne l. c. p. 915, 916.)] . Die gegensätzlichen und einander\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"80\", \"facs\": \"#f0099\"} --> ergänzenden Phasen, wodurch dieser Prozess verläuft, können nicht räum-\n lich neben einander fallen, sondern nur zeitlich auf einander folgen.\n Zeitabschnitte bilden daher das Mass seiner Dauer, oder die Anzahl der\n Umläufe derselben Geldstücke in gegebner Zeit misst die Geschwindig-\n keit des Geldumlaufs. Der Circulationsprozess jener vier Waaren\n dauere z. B. einen Tag. So ist die zu realisirende Preissumme: 8 Pfd. St.,\n die Anzahl der Umläufe desselben Geldstücks während des Tags: 4 und\n die Masse des circulirenden Geldes: 2 Pfd. St., oder für einen gegebnen\n Zeitabschnitt des Circulationsprozesses:\n\\frac{Preissumme der Waaren}{Umlaufsanzahl eines Geldstücks} = Masse des als Circulations-\n mittel funktionirenden Gel-\n des. Dies Gesetz gilt allgemein.\n Der Circulationsprozess eines Landes in einem gegebnen Zeitabschnitt um-\n fasst zwar einerseits viele zersplitterte, gleichzeitige und räumlich neben\n einander fallende Verkäufe (resp. Käufe) oder Theilmetamorphosen, worin\n dieselben Geldstücke nur einmal die Stelle wechseln oder nur einen Um-\n lauf vollziehn, andrerseits viele theils neben einander herlaufende, theils\n sich in einander verschlingende mehr oder minder gliederreiche Metamor-\n phosenreihen, worin dieselben Geldstücke mehr oder minder zahlreiche\n Umläufe zurücklegen. Die Gesammtzahl der Umläufe aller in Circula-\n tion befindlichen gleichnamigen Geldstücke ergiebt jedoch die\nDurchschnittsanzahl der Umläufe des einzelnen Geldstücks oder die\n Durchschnittsgeschwindigkeit des Geldumlaufs. Die Geldmasse, die bei\n Beginn z. B. des täglichen Circulationsprozesses in ihn hineingeworfen\n wird, ist natürlich bestimmt durch die Preissumme der gleichzeitig\n und räumlich neben einander circulirenden Waaren. Aber inner-\n halb des Prozesses wird ein Geldstück so zu sagen für das andre verant-\n wortlich gemacht. Beschleunigt das eine seine Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit,\n so erlahmt die des andern, oder es fliegt ganz aus der Circulationssphäre\n heraus, da diese nur eine Goldmasse absorbiren kann, welche multiplicirt\n mit der mittlern Umlaufsanzahl ihres einzelnen Elements gleich der zu\n realisirenden Preissumme ist. Wächst daher die Anzahl der Umläufe der\n Geldstücke, so nimmt ihre circulirende Masse ab. Nimmt die Anzahl\n ihrer Umläufe ab, so wächst ihre Masse. Weil die Masse des Geldes, die\n als Circulationsmittel funktioniren kann, bei gegebner Durchschnittsge-\n schwindigkeit gegeben ist, hat man daher z. B. nur eine bestimmte\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"81\", \"facs\": \"#f0100\"} --> Quantität von Ein-Pfund-Noten in die Cirkulation hinein zu werfen, um\n eben so viele Sovereigns hinaus zu werfen, ein allen Banken wohlbekanntes\n Kunststück.\n\n\n\n\nWie im Geldumlauf überhaupt nur der Circulationsprozess der\n Waaren erscheint, so in der Geschwindigkeit des Geldumlaufs die Ge-\n schwindigkeit ihres Formwechsels, das continuirliche Ineinandergreifen der\n Metamorphosenreihen, die Hast des Stoffwechsels, das rasche Verschwin-\n den der Waaren aus der Circulationssphäre und ihr ebenso rascher Ersatz\n durch neue Waaren. In der Geschwindigkeit des Geldumlaufs erscheint\n also die flüssige Einheit der entgegengesetzten und sich ergänzen-\n den Phasen, Verwandlung der Gebrauchsgestalt in Werthgestalt und Rück-\n verwandlung der Werthgestalt in Gebrauchsgestalt, oder der beiden Pro-\n zesse des Verkaufs und Kaufs. Umgekehrt erscheint in der Verlangsa-\n mung des Geldumlaufs die Trennung und gegensätzliche Ver-\n selbstständigung dieser Prozesse, die Stockung des Formwechsels\n und daher des Stoffwechsels. Woher diese Stockung entspringt, ist na-\n türlich der Circulation selbst nicht anzusehn. Sie zeigt nur das Phänomen\n selbst. Der populären Anschauung, welche mit verlangsamtem Geldumlauf\n das Geld minder häufig auf allen Punkten der Circulationsperipherie er-\n scheinen und verschwinden sieht, liegt es nah das Phänomen aus mangeln-\n der Quantität der Circulationsmittel zu deuten [Note: „Money being … the common measure of buying and selling, every\n body who has anything to sell, and cannot procure chapmen for it, is presently\n apt to think, that want of money in the kingdom, or country, is the\n cause why his goods do not go off; and so, want of money is the common cry;\n which is a great mistake … What do these people want, who cry out for\n money? … The Farmer complains ‥ he thinks that were more money in the\n country, he should have a price for his goods … Then it seems money is not his\n want, but a Price for his corn and cattle, which he would sell, but cannot …\n why cannot he get a price? … 1) Either there is too much corn and cattle in the\n country, so that most who come to market have need of selling, as he has, and\n few of buying: or, 2) There wants the usual vent abroad by Transportation . . . .\n Or, 3) The consumption fails, as when men, by reason of poverty, do not spend\n so much in their houses as formerly they did; wherefore it is not the increase of\n specifick money, which would at all advance the farmer’s goods, but the removal\n of any of these three causes, which do truly keep down the market ‥ The mer-\n chant and shopkeeper want money in the same manner, that is, they want a vent] .\n\n\n [Running matter: I. 6] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"82\", \"facs\": \"#f0101\"} -->\n\nDas Gesammtquantum des in jedem Zeitabschnitt als Circulations-\n mittel functionirenden Geldes ist also bestimmt einerseits durch die Preis-\n summe der circulirenden Waarenwelt, andrerseits durch den langsamern\n oder raschern Fluss ihrer gegensätzlichen Circulationsprozesse, von dem\n es abhängt, der wievielte Theil jener Preissumme durch dieselben\n Geldstücke realisirt werden kann. Die Preissumme der Waaren hängt\n aber ab sowohl von der Masse als den Preisen jeder Waarenart. Die\n drei Faktoren der Preisbewegung, der circulirenden Waaren-\n masse und endlich der Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit des Geldes\n können aber in verschiedner Richtung und verschiednen Verhältnissen\n wechseln, die zu realisirende Preissumme, daher die durch sie\n bedingte Masse der Circulationsmittel, also sehr zahlreiche Com-\n binationen untergehn. Wir zählen hier nur die in der Geschichte der\n Waarenpreise wichtigsten auf.\n\n\n\n\nBei gleichbleibenden Waarenpreisen kann die Masse der\n Circulationsmittel wachsen, weil die Masse der circulirenden Waaren zu-\n nimmt oder die Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit des Geldes abnimmt, oder beides\n zusammenwirkt. Die Masse der Circulationsmittel kann umgekehrt ab-\n nehmen mit abnehmender Waarenmasse oder zunehmender Circulations-\n geschwindigkeit.\n\n\n\n\nBei allgemein steigenden Waarenpreisen kann die Masse\n der Circulationsmittel gleichbleiben, wenn die Masse der circulirenden\n Waaren in demselben Verhältniss abnimmt, worin ihr Preis zunimmt, oder\n die Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit des Geldes eben so rasch zunimmt als die\n Preiserhöhung, während die cirkulirende Waarenmasse constant bleibt.\n [Note: for the goods they deal in, by reason that the markets fail … in a nation\n never thrives better, than when riches are tost from hand to hand.“ (Sir\n Dudley North: „Discourses upon Trade. Lond. 1691“, p. 11—15\n passim.) Herrenschwand’s Schwindeleien kommen alle darauf hinaus, dass\n die aus der Natur der Waare entspringenden und daher in der Waarencirculation\n erscheinenden Widersprüche durch Vermehrung der Circulationsmittel beseitigt\n werden können. Aus der Volksillusion, welche Stockungen des Produktions- und\n Circulationsprozesses einem Mangel an Circulationsmitteln zuschreibt, folgt übri-\n gens keineswegs umgekehrt, dass wirklicher Mangel an Circulationsmitteln, z. B.\n in Folge officieller Pfuschereien mit der „regulation of currency“, nicht seinerseits\n Stockungen hervorrufen kann.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"83\", \"facs\": \"#f0102\"} --> Die Masse der Circulationsmittel kann fallen, weil die Waarenmasse\n rascher ab- oder die Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit rascher zunimmt als die\n Preise.\n\n\n\n\nBei allgemein fallenden Waarenpreisen kann die Masse\n der Circulationsmittel gleichbleiben, wenn die Waarenmasse in demselben\n Verhältniss wächst, worin ihr Preis fällt, oder die Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit\n des Geldes in demselben Verhältniss abnimmt wie die Preise. Sie kann\n wachsen, wenn die Waarenmasse rascher wächst oder die Circulations-\n geschwindigkeit rascher abnimmt als die Waarenpreise fallen.\n\n\n\n\nDie Variationen der verschiedenen Faktoren können sich wechselsei-\n tig compensiren, so dass ihrer beständigen Unstätigkeit zum Trotz die zu\n realisirende Gesammtsumme der Waarenpreise constant bleibt, also auch\n die circulirende Geldmasse. Man findet daher, namentlich bei Betrach-\n tung etwas längerer Perioden, ein viel constanteres Durchschnitts-\n niveau der in jedem Lande circulirenden Geldmasse und,\n mit Ausnahme starker Perturbationen, die periodisch aus den Produktions-\n und Handelskrisen, seltner aus einem Wechsel im Geldwerth selbst ent-\n springen, viel geringere Abweichungen von diesem Durchschnittsniveau\n als man nach dem Augenschein erwarten sollte.\n\n\n\n\nDas Gesetz, dass die Quantität der Circulationsmittel bestimmt ist\n durch die Preissumme der circulirenden Waaren und die Durchschnitts-\n geschwindigkeit des Geldumlaufs [Note: „There is a certain measure, and proportion of money requisite to drive\n the trade of a nation, more or less than which, would prejudice the same. Just as\n there is a certain proportion of farthings necessary in a small retail Trade, to\n change silver money, and to even such reckonings as cannot be adjusted with the\n smallest silver pieces … Now as the proportion of the number of farthings re-\n quisite in commerce is to be taken from the number of people, the frequency of\n their exchanges; as also, and principally, from the value of the smallest\n silver pieces of money; so in like manner, the proportion of money (gold and\n silver species) requisite to our trade, is to be likewise taken from the frequency\n of commutations, and from the bigness of payments.“ (Wil-\n liam Petty: „A Treatise on Taxes and Contributions. Lond.\n 1667“, p. 17.) Die Hume’sche Theorie ward gegen J. Stenart u. A. ver-\n theidigt von A. Young in seiner „Political Arithmetic. Lond. 1774“,\n wo ein eignes Kapitel: „Prices depend on quantity of money“, p.] , kann auch so ausgedrückt werden,\n [Running matter: 6*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"84\", \"facs\": \"#f0103\"} --> dass bei gegebner Werthsumme der Waaren und gegebner Durchschnitts-\n geschwindigkeit ihrer Metamorphosen, die Quantität des umlaufenden\n Geldes oder des Geldmaterials von seinem eignen Werth abhängt.\n Die Illusion, dass umgekehrt die Waarenpreise durch die Masse der Circu-\n lationsmittel und letztre ihrerseits durch die Masse des in einem Lande\n befindlichen Geldmaterials bestimmt werden [Note: „The prices of things will certainly rise in every nation, as the gold and\n silver increase amongst the people; and, consequently, where the gold and silver\n decrease in any nation, the prices of all things must fall proportionably to such\n decrease of money.“ (Jacob Vanderlint: „Money answers all\n Things“. Lond. 1734, p. 5.) Nähere Vergleichung zwischen Vanderlint und\n Hume’s „Essays“ lässt mir nicht den geringsten Zweifel, dass Hume V.’s\n übrigens sehr bedeutende Schrift kannte und benutzte. Die Ansicht, dass die\n Masse der Circulationsmittel die Preise bestimmt, auch bei Barbon und noch viel\n älteren Schriftstellern. „No inconvenience“, sagt Vanderlint, „can arise by an\nunrestrained trade, but very great advantage … since, if the cash of\n the nation be decreased by it, which prohibitions are designed to prevent, those\n nations that get the cash will certainly find every thing advance in price, as the\n cash increases amongst them. And . . . . our manufactures and every thing else,\n will soon become so moderate as to turn the balance of trade in our favour, and\n thereby fetch the money back again.“ (l. c. p. 44.)] , wurzelt bei ihren ursprüng-\n lichen Vertretern in der abgeschmackten Hypothese, dass Waaren ohne\n Preis und Geld ohne Werth in den Circulationsprozess eingehn,\n [Note: 112 sqq. Ich bemerke „Zur Kritik etc. p. 149“: „Die Frage über die\nQuantität der circulirenden Münze beseitigt er (A. Smith) stillschweigend,\n indem er das Geld ganz falsch als blosse Waare behandelt.“ Diess gilt nur, so-\n weit A. Smith ex officio das Geld behandelt. Gelegentlich jedoch, z. B. in der\n Kritik der früheren Systeme der Pol. Oekon., spricht er das Richtige aus: „The\n quantity of coin in every country is regulated by the value of the commodities\n which are to be circulated by it . . . . The value of goods annually bought and\n sold in any country requires a certain quantity of money to circulate and distri-\n bute them to their proper consumers, and can give employment to no more. The\n channel of circulation necessarily draws to itself a sum sufficient to fill it, and\n never admits any more.“ (Wealth of Nations, l. IV. ch. I.) Aehnlich\n eröffnet A. Smith sein Werk ex officio mit einer Apotheose der Theilung der\n Arbeit. Hinterher, im letzten Buch über die Quellen des Staatseinkommens,\n reproducirt er gelegentlich A. Ferguson’s, seines Lehrers, Denunciation der Thei-\n lung der Arbeit.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"85\", \"facs\": \"#f0104\"} --> wo sich dann ein aliquoter Theil des Waarenbreis mit einem aliquoten\n Theil des Metallbergs austausche [Note: Dass jede einzelne Waarenart durch ihren Preis ein Element der\nPreissumme aller circulirenden Waaren bildet, ist selbstverständ-\n lich. Wie aber unter einander incommensurable Gebrauchswerthe sich\n en masse mit der in einem Land befindlichen Gold- oder Silbermasse austauschen\n sollen, ist völlig unbegreiflich. Verschwindelt man die Waarenwelt in eine ein-\n zige Gesammtwaare, wovon jede Waare nur einen aliquoten Theil bildet,\n so kommt das schöne Rechenexempel heraus: Gesammtwaare = x Ctr. Gold,\n Waare A = aliquoter Theil der Gesammtwaare = demselben aliquoten Theil von\n x Ctr. Gold. Diess ehrlich heraus bei Montesquieu: „Si l’on compare la masse\n de l’or et de l’argent qui est dans le monde, avec la somme des marchandises qui\n y sont, il est certain que chaque denrée ou marchandise, en particulier, pourra\n être comparée à une certaine portion de l’autre. Supposons qu’il n’y ait\n qu’une seule denrée ou marchandise dans le monde, ou qu’il\n n’y ait qu’une seule qui s’achète, et qu’elle se divise comme l’argent;\n cette partie de cette marchandise répondra à une partie de la masse de l’argent;\n la moitié du total de l’une à la moitié du total de l’autre etc. . . . l’établissement\n du prix des choses dépend toujours fondamentalement de la raison du total des\n choses au total des signes.“ (Montesquieu, l. c. t. III, p. 12, 13.) Ueber\n die Weiterentwicklung dieser Theorie durch Ricardo, seinen Schüler James Mill,\n Lord Overstone u. s. w. vgl. „Zur Kritik u. s. w.“ p. 140—146 u. p. 150\n seqq. Herr J. St. Mill versteht es, mit der ihm geläufigen eklektischen Logik,\n der Ansicht seines Vaters J. Mill und zugleich der entgegengesetzten zu sein.\n Vergleicht man den Text seines Compendiums: „Princ. of Pol. Econ.“\n mit der Vorrede (erste Ausgabe), worin er sich selbst als Adam Smith der Gegen-\n wart ankündet, so weiss man nicht, was mehr bewundern, die Naivetät des Mannes\n oder die des Publikums, das ihn auf Treu und Glauben in den Kauf nahm als\n Adam Smith, zu dem er sich etwa verhält wie General Williams Kars von Kars\n zum Herzog von Wellington. Die weder umfangreichen noch gehaltreichen Ori-\n ginalforschungen des Herrn J. St. Mill im Gebiet der Pol. Oek. findet man alle in\n Reih’ und Glied aufmarschirt in seinem 1844 erschienenen Schriftchen: „Some\n Unsettled Questions of Political Economy“. Locke spricht direkt\n den Zusammenhang zwischen der Werthlosigkeit von Gold und Silber und\n der Bestimmung ihres Werths durch Quantität aus. „Mankind\n having consented to put an imaginary value upon gold and silver … the\n intrinsick value, regarded in these metals, is nothing but the quantity.“\n „Some Considerations etc. 1691“. Works ed 1777, vol. II, p. 15.] .\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## c) Die Münze. Das Werthzeichen.\n\n\n\n\nAus der Funktion des Geldes als Circulationsmittel entspringt seine\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"86\", \"facs\": \"#f0105\"} --> Münzgestalt. Der in dem Preise oder Geldnamen der Waaren vorge-\n stellte Gewichtstheil Gold muss ihnen in der Circulation als gleichnamiges\n Goldstück oder Münze gegenübertreten. Wie die Feststellung des\n Massstabs der Preise, fällt das Geschäft der Münzung dem Staat anheim.\n In den verschiedenen Nationaluniformen, die Gold und Silber als Münzen\n tragen, auf dem Weltmarkt aber wieder ausziehn, erscheint die Scheidung\n zwischen den innern oder nationalen Sphären der Waarencirculation und\n ihrer allgemeinen Weltmarktssphäre.\n\n\n\n\nGoldmünze und Barrengold unterscheiden sich also von Haus aus nur\n durch die Figur, und das Gold ist beständig aus einer Form in die andre\n verwandelbar [Note: Es liegt natürlich ganz jenseits meines Zwecks, Details wie Schlagschatz\n u. dgl. zu behandeln. Gegenüber dem romantischen Sykophanten Adam Müller\n jedoch, der „die grossartige Liberalität“ bewundert, womit die „eng-\n lische Regierung unentgeldlich münzt“, folgendes Urtheil Sir Dud-\n ley North’s: „Silver and gold, like other commodities, have their ebbings and\n flowings. Upon the arrival of quantities from Spain … it is carried into the\n Tower, and coined. Not long after there will come a demand for bullion, to\n be exported again. If there is none, but all happens to be in coin, what then?\n Melt it down again; there’s no loss in it, for the coining costs the owner nothing.\n Thus the nation has been abused, and made to pay for the twisting of straw, for\n asses to eat. If the merchant (North war selbst einer der grössten Kaufleute zu\n Charles II. Zeit) had to pay the price of the coinage, he would not have sent his\n silver to the Tower without consideration; and coined money would always keep a\n value above uncoined silver.“ (North l. c. p. 18.)] . Der Weg aus der Münze ist aber zugleich der Gang\n zum Schmelztiegel. Im Umlauf verschleissen nämlich die Goldmünzen,\n die eine mehr, die andre weniger. Goldtitel und Goldsubstanz, Nominal-\n gehalt und Realgehalt beginnen ihren Scheidungsprozess. Gleichnamige\n Goldmünzen werden von ungleichem Werth, weil verschiednem Gewicht.\n Das Gold als Circulationsmittel weicht ab vom Gold als Massstab der\n Preise, und hört damit auch auf wirkliches Aequivalent der Waaren zu\n sein, deren Preise es realisirt. Die Geschichte dieser Wirren bildet die\n Münzgeschichte des Mittelalters und der Neuzeit bis ins 18. Jahrhundert.\n Die naturwüchsige Tendenz des Cirkulationsprozesses, das Goldsein der\n Münze in Goldschein oder die Münze in ein Symbol ihres officiellen Metall-\n gehalts zu verwandeln, ist selbst anerkannt durch die modernsten Gesetze\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"87\", \"facs\": \"#f0106\"} --> über den Grad des Metallverlustes, der ein Goldstück kursunfähig macht\n oder demonetisirt.\n\n\n\n\nWenn der Geldumlauf selbst den Realgehalt vom Nominalgehalt\n der Münze scheidet, ihr Metalldasein von ihrem funktionellen Dasein,\n so enthält er die Möglichkeit latent, das Metallgeld in seiner Münzfunktion\n durch Marken aus andrem Material oder Symbole zu ersetzen. Die tech-\n nischen Hindernisse der Münzung ganz diminutiver Gewichtstheile des\n Goldes, resp. Silbers, und der Umstand, dass niedrigere Metalle ursprüng-\n lich statt der edleren, Silber statt des Goldes, Kupfer statt des Silbers,\n zum Werthmass dienen und daher als Geld circuliren im Augenblick, wo\n das edlere Metall sie entthront, erklären historisch die Rolle von Silber-\n und Kupfermarken als Substitute der Goldmünze. Sie ersetzen das Gold\n in den Kreisen der Waarencirculation, worin die Münze am schnellsten\n circulirt und sich daher am schnellsten abnutzt, d. h. wo Käufe und Ver-\n käufe unaufhörlich im kleinsten Massstab erneuert werden. Um die Fest-\n setzung dieser Trabanten an der Stelle des Goldes selbst zu verhindern,\n werden gesetzlich die sehr niedrigen Proportionen bestimmt, worin sie\n allein an Zahlungsstatt für Gold angenommen werden müssen. Die be-\n sondern Kreise, worin die verschiednen Münzsorten umlaufen, laufen natür-\n lich in einander. Die Scheidemünze erscheint neben dem Gold zur Zah-\n lung von Bruchtheilen der kleinsten Goldmünze; das Gold tritt beständig\n in die Detailcirculation ein, wird aber durch Auswechslung mit Scheide-\n münze ebenso beständig herausgeworfen [Note: „If silver never exceed what is wanted for the smaller payments, it cannot\n be collected in sufficient quantities for the larger payments … the use of gold in\n the main payments necessarily implies also its use in the retail trade: those who\n have gold coin offering them for small purchases, and receiving with the commo-\n dity purchased a balance of silver in return; by which means the surplus of silver\n that would otherwise encumber the retail dealer, is drawn off and dispersed into\n general circulation. But if there is as much silver as will transact the small pay-\n ments independent of gold, the retail dealer must then receive silver for small pur-\n chases; and it must of necessity accumulate in his hands.“ (David Buchanan:\n „Inquiry into the Taxation and Commercial Policy of Great\n Britain. Edinburgh 1844“, p. 248, 249.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDer Metallgehalt der Silber- oder Kupfermarken ist willkührlich durch\n das Gesetz bestimmt. Im Umlauf verschleissen sie noch rascher als die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"88\", \"facs\": \"#f0107\"} --> Goldmünze. Ihre Münzfunktion wird daher faktisch durchaus unabhängig\n von ihrem Gewicht, d. h. von allem Werth. Das Münzdasein des Goldes\n scheidet sich völlig von seiner Werthsubstanz. Relativ werthlose Dinge,\nPapierzettel, können also an seiner Statt als Münze funktioniren. In\n den metallischen Geldmarken ist der rein symbolische Charakter noch eini-\n germassen versteckt. Im Papiergeld tritt er augenscheinlich hervor.\n Man sieht: ce n’est que le premier pas qui coûte.\n\n\n\n\nEs handelt sich hier nur von Staatspapiergeld mit Zwangs-\n kurs. Es wächst unmittelbar aus der metallischen Circulation heraus.\nCreditgeld unterstellt dagegen Verhältnisse, die uns vom Standpunkt\n der einfachen Waarencirculation noch durchaus unbekannt sind. Im Vor-\n beigehn sei jedoch bemerkt, dass, wie eigentliches Papiergeld aus der\n Funktion des Geldes als Circulationsmittel entspringt, das Cre-\n ditgeld in der Funktion des Geldes als Zahlungsmittel seine natur-\n wüchsige Wurzel besitzt [Note: Der Finanzmandarine Wan-mao-in liess sich beigehn, dem Sohn des\n Himmels ein Projekt zu unterbreiten, welches versteckt auf Verwandlung der chi-\n nesischen Reichsassignaten in convertible Banknoten hinzielte. Im Bericht des\n Assignaten-Comités von April 1854 erhält er gehörig den Kopf gewaschen. Ob\n er auch die obligate Tracht Bambushiebe erhielt, wird nicht gemeldet. „Das\n Comité“, lautet es am Schluss des Berichts, „hat sein Projekt aufmerksam er-\n wogen und findet, dass alles in ihm auf den Vortheil der Kaufleute ausgeht und\n nichts für die Krone vortheilhaft ist.“ („Arbeiten der Kaiserlich Russi-\n schen Gesandtschaft zu Peking über China. Aus dem Russi-\n schen von Dr. K. Abel und F. A. Mecklenburg. Erster Band.\n Berlin 1858“, p. 47 sq.) Ueber die beständige Entmetallung der\n Goldmünzen durch ihren Umlauf sagt ein „Governor“ der Bank of England als\n Zeuge vor dem „House of Lords’ Committee“ (über „Bankacts“): „Jedes Jahr\n wird eine frische Klasse von Souverainen (diess nicht politisch, sondern der So-\n vereign ist Name des Pfd. St.) zu leicht. Die Klasse, welche das eine Jahr als voll-\n wichtig passirt, verliert durch den Verschleiss hinreichend, um das nächste Jahr\n die Wagschale gegen sich zu drehn.“ (H. o. Lords’ Committee 1848,\n n. 429.)] .\n\n\n\n\nPapierzettel, denen Geldnamen, wie 1 Pfd. St., 5 Pfd. St. u. s. w.\n aufgedruckt sind, werden vom Staat äusserlich in den Circulationsprozess\n hineingeworfen. Soweit sie wirklich an der Stelle der gleichnamigen\n Goldsumme circuliren, spiegeln sich in ihrer Bewegung nur die Gesetze des\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"89\", \"facs\": \"#f0108\"} --> Geldumlaufs selbst wieder. Ein spezifisches Gesetz der Papiercirculation\n kann nur aus ihrem Repräsentationsverhältniss zum Gold entspringen. Und\n diess Gesetz ist einfach diess, dass die Ausgabe des Papiergelds auf die\n Quantität zu beschränken ist, worin das von ihm symbolisch dargestellte\n Gold (resp. Silber) wirklich circuliren müsste. Nun schwankt zwar das\n Goldquantum, welches die Circulationssphäre absorbiren kann, beständig\n über oder unter ein gewisses Durchschnittsniveau. Jedoch sinkt die\n Mass des circulirenden Mediums in einem gegebnen Land nie unter ein\n gewisses Minimum, das sich erfahrungsmässig feststellt. Dass diese\n Minimalmasse fortwährend ihre Bestandtheile wechselt, d. h. aus stets an-\n dern Goldstücken besteht, ändert natürlich nichts an ihrem Umfang und\n ihrem constanten Umtrieb in der Circulationssphäre. Sie kann daher durch\n Papiersymbole ersetzt werden. Werden dagegen heute alle Circulations-\n kanäle zum vollen Grad ihrer Geldabsorptionsfähigkeit mit Papiergeld ge-\n füllt, so können sie in Folge der Schwankungen der Waarencirculation\n morgen übervoll sein. Alles Mass geht verloren. Ueberschreitet aber\n das Papier sein Mass, d. h. die Quantität von Goldmünze gleicher Deno-\n mination, welche circuliren könnte, so stellt es, von der Gefahr allgemeiner\n Discreditirung abgesehn, innerhalb der Waarenwelt dennoch nur die durch\n ihre immanenten Gesetze bestimmte, also auch allein repräsentirbare Gold-\n quantität vor. Stellt die Papierzettelmasse z. B. je 2 Unzen Gold, statt\n je 1 Unze dar, so werden ihre Geldnamen faktisch, sage etwa von 1 Pfd.\n St. per ¼ Unze, zu Namen von ⅛ Unzen herabgesetzt. Die Wirkung\n ist dieselbe, als wäre das Gold in seiner Funktion als Mass der Preise\n verändert worden. Dieselben Werthe, die sich daher vorher im Preise von\n 1 Pfd. St., drücken sich jetzt im Preise von 2 Pfd. St. aus.\n\n\n\n\nDas Papiergeld ist Goldzeichen oder Geldzeichen. Sein Ver-\n hältniss zu den Waaren werthen besteht nur darin, dass sie ideell in den-\n selben Goldquanta ausgedrückt sind, welche vom Papier symbolisch sinn-\n lich dargestellt werden. Nur sofern das Papiergeld Goldquanta repräsen-\n tirt, die, wie alle andern Waarenquanta, auch Werthquanta, ist es\nWerthzeichen.\n\n\n\n\nEs fragt sich schliesslich, warum das Gold durch blosse werthlose\n Zeichen seiner selbst ersetzt werden kann? Es ist aber, wie man gesehn,\n nur so ersetzbar, soweit es in seiner Funktion als Münze oder Circulations-\n mittel isolirt oder verselbstständigt wird. Nun findet die Verselbststän-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"90\", \"facs\": \"#f0109\"} --> digung dieser Funktion zwar nicht für die einzelnen Goldmünzen statt,\n obgleich sie in dem Fortcirculiren verschlissener Goldstücke erscheint.\n Blosse Münze oder Circulationsmittel sind die Goldstücke grade nur so\n lang sie sich wirklich im Umlauf befinden. Was aber nicht für die ein-\n zelne Goldmünze, gilt für die vom Papiergeld ersetzbare Minimalmasse\n Gold. Sie haust beständig in der Circulationssphäre, funktionirt fortwäh-\n rend als Circulationsmittel und existirt daher ausschliesslich als Träger\n dieser Funktion. Ihre Bewegung stellt also nur das fortwährende Inein-\n anderumschlagen der entgegengesetzten Prozesse der Waarenmetamor-\n phose W — G — W dar, worin der Waare ihre Werthgestalt nur gegen-\n übertritt, um sofort wieder zu verschwinden. Die selbstständige\n Darstellung des Tauschwerths der Waare ist hier nur flüch-\n tiges Moment. Sofort wird sie wieder durch andere Waare ersetzt. Da-\n her genügt auch die bloss symbolische Existenz des Geldes in einem Pro-\n zess, der es beständig aus einer Hand in die andre entfernt. Sein funk-\n tionelles Dasein absorbirt so zu sagen sein materielles. Verschwindend\n objektivirter Reflex der Waarenpreise funktionirt es nur noch als Zeichen\n seiner selbst und kann daher auch durch Zeichen ersetzt werden [Note: Daraus, dass Gold und Silber als Münze oder in der ausschliesslichen\n Funktion als Circulationsmittel zu Zeichen ihrer selbst werden, leitet\nNicholas Barbon das Recht der Regierungen her „to raise money“, d. h.,\n z. B. einem Quantum Silber, das Groschen hiess, den Namen eines grössern\n Silberquantums wie Thaler zu geben, und so den Gläubigern Groschen statt Thaler\n zurückzuzahlen. „Money does wear and grow lighter by often telling over …\n It is the denomination and currency of the money that men regard in\n bargaining, and not the quantity of silver … ’Tis the publick authority upon the\n metal that makes it money.“ (N. Barbon l. c. p. 29, 30, 25.)] . Nur\n bedarf das Zeichen des Geldes seiner eignen objektiv gesellschaft-\n lichen Gültigkeit und diese erhält das Papiersymbol durch den\n Zwangskurs. Nur innerhalb der von den Grenzen eines Gemeinwesens\n umschriebnen oder innern Circulationssphäre gilt dieser Staatszwang, aber\n auch nur hier geht das Geld völlig auf in seine Funktion als Circulations-\n mittel oder Münze, und kann daher im Papiergeld eine von seiner Metall-\n substanz äusserlich getrennte und bloss funktionelle Existenzweise er-\n halten.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"91\", \"facs\": \"#f0110\"} -->\n\n\n\n## C. Geld.\n\n\n\n\nDie Waare, welche als Werthmass und daher auch, persönlich oder\n durch Stellvertreter, als Circulationsmittel funktionirt, ist Geld.\n Gold (resp. Silber) ist daher Geld. Als Geld funktionirt es, einerseits\n wo es in seiner goldnen (resp. silbernen) Leiblichkeit erscheinen muss,\n daher als Geldwaare, also weder bloss ideell, wie im Werthmass, noch\n repräsentationsfähig, wie im Circulationsmittel; andrerseits, wo seine Funk-\n tion, ob es selbe nun in eigner Person oder durch Stellvertreter vollziehe,\n es als alleinige Werthgestalt oder allein adäquates Dasein\n des Tauschwerths allen andern Waaren als blossen Gebrauchs-\n werthen gegenüber fixirt.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## a) Schatzbildung.\n\n\n\n\nDer continuirliche Kreislauf der zwei entgegengesetzten Waaren-\n metamorphosen oder der flüssige Umschlag von Verkauf und Kauf er-\n scheint im rastlosen Umlauf des Geldes oder seiner Funktion als per-\n petuum mobile der Circulation. Es wird immobilisirt, oder verwandelt sich,\n wie Boisguillebert sagt, aus meuble in immeuble, aus Münze\n in Geld, sobald die Metamorphosenreihe unterbrochen, der Verkauf\n nicht durch nachfolgenden Kauf ergänzt wird.\n\n\n\n\nMit der ersten Entwicklung der Waarencirculation selbst entwickelt\n sich die Nothwendigkeit und die Leidenschaft, das Produkt der ersten\n Metamorphose, die verwandelte Gestalt der Waare oder ihre Goldpuppe\n festzuhalten [Note: „Une richesse en argent n’est que … richesse en productions, con-\n verties en argent.“ (Mercier de la Rivière l. c. p. 557.) „Une\n valeur en productions n’a fait que changer de forme.“ (id. p. 486.)] . Waare wird verkauft, nicht um Waare zu kaufen, son-\n dern um Waarenform durch Geldform zu ersetzen. Aus blosser Vermitt-\n lung des Stoffwechsels wird dieser Formwechsel zum Selbstzweck. Die\nentäusserte Gestalt der Waare wird verhindert als ihre absolut ver-\n äusserliche Gestalt oder nur verschwindende Geldform zu funktioniren.\n Das Geld versteinert damit zum Schatz, und der Waarenverkäufer\n wird Schatzbildner.\n\n\n\n\nGrade in den Anfängen der Waarencirculation verwandelt sich nur\n der Ueberschuss an Gebrauchswerthen in Geld. Gold und Silber werden so\n von selbst zu gesellschaftlichen Ausdrücken des Ueberflusses oder des\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"92\", \"facs\": \"#f0111\"} --> Reichthums. Diese naive Form der Schatzbildung verewigt sich bei Völ-\n kern, wo der traditionellen und auf Selbstbedarf gerichteten Produktions-\n weise ein fest abgeschlossner Kreis von Bedürfnissen entspricht. So bei\n den Asiaten, namentlich den Indern. Vanderlint, der die Waaren-\n preise durch die Masse des in einem Land befindlichen Goldes und Silbers\n bestimmt wähnt, fragt sich, warum die indischen Waaren so wohlfeil?\n Antwort: Weil die Inder das Geld vergraben. Von 1602—1734, be-\n merkt er, vergruben sie 150 Millionen Pfd. St. Silber, die ursprünglich von\n Amerika nach Europa kamen [Note: ’Tis by this practice they keep all their goods and manufactures at such\n low rates.“ (Vanderlint l. c. p. 95, 96.)] . Von 1856—1866, also in 10 Jahren,\n exportirte England nach Indien und China (das nach China exportirte Me-\n tall fliesst grossentheils wieder nach Indien) 120 Millionen Pfd. St. in\n Silber, welches vorher gegen australisches Gold eingewechselt wurde.\n\n\n\n\nMit mehr entwickelter Waarenproduktion muss jeder Waarenpro-\n ducent sich den nexus rerum, das „gesellschaftliche Faustpfand“ sichern [Note: „Money is a pledge.“ (John Bellers: „Essays about the\n Poor, Manufactures, Trade, Plantations, and Immorality.\n Lond. 1699“, p. 13.)] .\n Seine Bedürfnisse erneuern sich unaufhörlich und gebieten unaufhörlichen\n Kauf fremder Waare, während Produktion und Verkauf seiner eignen\n Waare Zeit kosten und von Zufällen abhängen. Um zu kaufen, ohne zu\n verkaufen, muss er vorher verkauft haben, ohne zu kaufen. Diese Ope-\n ration, auf allgemeiner Stufenleiter ausgeführt, scheint sich selbst zu wider-\n sprechen. An ihren Produktionsquellen jedoch tauschen sich die edlen\n Metalle direkt mit andern Waaren aus. Es findet hier Verkauf (auf Seite\n der Waarenbesitzer) ohne Kauf (auf Seite der Gold- und Silberbesitzer)\n statt [Note: Kauf im kategorischen Sinn unterstellt nämlich Gold oder Silber schon\n als verwandelte Gestalt der Waare, oder als Produkt des Verkaufs.] . Und spätere Verkäufe ohne nachfolgende Käufe vermitteln bloss\n die weitere Vertheilung der edlen Metalle unter alle Waarenbesitzer. So\n entstehn auf allen Punkten des Verkehrs Gold- und Silberschätze vom\n verschiedensten Umfang. Mit der Möglichkeit, die Waare als Tausch-\n werth oder den Tauschwerth als Waare festzuhalten, erwacht die Goldgier.\n Mit der Ausdehnung der Waarencirculation wächst die Macht des Geldes,\n der stets schlagfertigen, absolut gesellschaftlichen Form des Reichthums.\n „Gold ist ein wunderbares Ding! Wer dasselbe besitzt, ist Herr von\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"93\", \"facs\": \"#f0112\"} --> allem, was er wünscht. Durch Gold kann man sogar Seelen in das Para-\n dies gelangen lassen.“ (Columbus, im Brief aus Jamaica, 1503). Da\n dem Geld nicht anzusehn, was in es verwandelt ist, verwandelt sich alles,\n Waare oder nicht, in Geld. Alles wird verkäuflich und kaufbar. Die\n Circulation wird die grosse gesellschaftliche Retorte, worin alles hinein-\n fliegt, um als Geldkrystall wieder herauszukommen. Dieser Alchymie wider-\n stehen nicht einmal Heiligenknochen und noch viel weniger minder grobe\n res sacrosanctae, extra commercium hominum [Note: Heinrich III., allerchristlichster König von Frankreich, raubt Klöstern\n u. s. w. ihre Reliquien, um sie zu versilbern. Man weiss, welche Rolle der Raub\n der delphischen Tempelschätze durch die Phokier in der griechischen Geschichte\n spielt. Dem Gott der Waaren dienten bei den Alten bekanntlich die Tempel zum\n Wohnsitz. Sie waren „heilige Banken“. Den Phöniziern, einem Handelsvolke par\n excellence, galt Geld als die entäusserte Gestalt aller Dinge. Es war daher in der\n Ordnung, dass die Jungfrauen, die sich an den Festen der Liebesgöttin den\n Fremden hingaben, das zum Lohn empfangene Geldstück der Göttin opferten.] . Wie im Geld aller\n qualitative Unterschied der Waaren ausgelöscht ist, löscht es seinerseits\n als radikaler Leveller alle Unterschiede aus [Note: „Gold! yellow, glittering precious gold!\n Thus much of this, will make black white; foul, fair;\n Wrong, right; base, noble; old, young; coward, valiant\n . . . . . . . . . . What this, you gods! Why this\n Will lug your priests and servants from your sides;\n Pluck stout men’s pillows from below their heads.\n This yellow slave\n Will knit and break religions; bless the accurs’d;\n Make the hoar leprosy ador’d; place thieves\n And give them title, knee and approbation,\n With senators of the bench; this is it,\n That makes the wappen’d widow wed again\n . . . . . . . . . . Come damned earth,\n Thou common whore of mankind.“\n (Shakespeare, Timon of Athens.)] . Das Geld ist aber selbst\n Waare, ein äusserlich Ding, das Privateigenthum eines Jeden werden kann.\n Die gesellschaftliche Macht wird so zur Privatmacht der Privatperson.\n Die antike Gesellschaft denuncirt es daher als die Scheidemünze ihrer\n ökonomischen und sittlichen Ordnung [Note: „Οὐδὲν γὰϱ ἀνϑϱώποισιν οἷον ἄϱγυϱος\n Καϰὸν νόμισμα ἔβλαστε· τοῦτο ϰαὶ πόλεις\n Ποϱϑεῖ, τόδ᾽ἄνδϱας ἐξανίστησιν δόμων.\n Τόδ᾽ ἐϰδιδάσϰει ϰαὶ παϱαλλάσσει φϱένας\n Χϱηστὰς πϱὸς αἰσχϱὰ ἀνϑϱώποις ἔχειν,\n Καὶ παντὸς ἔϱγου δυσσέβειαν εἰδέναι.“\n (Sophocles, Antigone.)] . Die moderne Gesellschaft, die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"94\", \"facs\": \"#f0113\"} --> schon in ihren Kinderjahren den Plutus an den Haaren aus den Eingewei-\n den der Erde herauszieht [Note: „Ἐλπιζούσης τῆς πλεονεξίας ἀνάξειν ἐϰ τῶν μυχῶν τῆς γῆς αὐτὸν τὸν\n Πλούτωνα.“ (Athen. Deipnos.)] , begrüsst im Goldgral die glänzende Incarna-\n tion ihres eigensten Lebensprinzips.\n\n\n\n\nDie Waare als Gebrauchswerth befriedigt ein besondres Bedürfniss\n und bildet ein besondres Element des stofflichen Reichthums. Aber der\nWerth der Waare misst den Grad ihrer Attraktionskraft auf alle Ele-\n mente des stofflichen Reichthums, daher den gesellschaftlichen\n Reichthum ihres Besitzers. Dem barbarisch einfachen Waarenbesitzer,\n selbst einem westeuropäischen Bauer, ist der Werth unzertrennlich von der\n Werthform, Vermehrung des Gold- und Silberschatzes daher Werthver-\n mehrung. Allerdings wechselt der Werth des Geldes, sei es in Folge sei-\n nes eignen Werthwechsels, sei es des Werthwechsels der Waaren. Diess\n verhindert aber einerseits nicht, dass 200 Unzen Gold nach wie vor mehr\n Werth enthalten als 100, 300 mehr als 200 u. s. w., noch andrerseits\n dass die metallne Naturalform dieses Dings die allgemeine Aequivalent-\n form aller Waaren bleibt, die unmittelbar gesellschaftliche Incarnation\n aller menschlichen Arbeit. Der Trieb der Schatzbildung ist von Natur\n masslos. Qualitativ oder seiner Form nach ist das Geld schranken-\n los, d. h. allgemeiner Repräsentant des stofflichen Reichthums, weil in\n jede Waare unmittelbar umsetzbar. Aber zugleich ist jede wirkliche Geld-\n summe quantitativ beschränkt, daher auch nur Kaufmittel von be-\n schränkter Wirkung. Dieser Widerspruch zwischen der quantitativen\n Schranke und der qualitativen Schrankenlosigkeit des Geldes treibt den\n Schatzbildner stets zurück zur Sisyphusarbeit der Accumulation.\n Es geht ihm wie dem Welteroberer, der mit jedem neuen Land nur eine\n neue Grenze erobert.\n\n\n\n\nUm das Gold als Geld festzuhalten und daher als Element der Schatz-\n bildung, muss es verhindert werden zu circuliren oder als Kaufmittel\n sich in Genussmittel aufzulösen. Der Schatzbildner opfert daher dem\n Goldfetisch seine Fleischeslust. Er macht Ernst mit dem Evangelium\n der Entsagung. Andrerseits kann er der Circulation nur in Geld entziehn,\n was er ihr in Waare giebt. Je mehr er producirt, desto mehr kann er\n verkaufen. Arbeitsamkeit, Sparsamkeit und Geiz bilden daher seine Kar-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"95\", \"facs\": \"#f0114\"} --> dinaltugenden, viel verkaufen, wenig kaufen, die Summe seiner politi-\n schen Oekonomie [Note: „Accrescere quanto più si può il numero de’ venditori d’ogni merce, di-\n minuire quanto più si può il numero dei compratori, questi sono i cardini sui quali\n si raggirano tutte le operazioni di economia politica.“ (Verri l. c. p. 52.)] .\n\n\n\n\nNeben der unmittelbaren Form des Schatzes läuft seine ästheti-\n sche Form, der Besitz von Gold- und Silberwaaren. Er wächst mit\n dem Reichthum der bürgerlichen Gesellschaft. „Soyons riches ou parais-\n sons riches.“ (Diderot.) Es bildet sich so theils ein stets ausgedehnterer\n Markt für Gold und Silber, unabhängig von ihren Geldfunktionen, theils\n eine latente Zufuhrquelle des Geldes, die namentlich in gesellschaftlichen\n Sturmperioden fliesst.\n\n\n\n\nDie Schatzbildung erfüllt verschiedne Funktionen in der Oekonomie\n der metallischen Circulation. Die nächste Funktion entspringt aus den\n Umlaufsbedingungen der Gold- oder Silbermünze. Man hat gesehn, wie\n mit den beständigen Schwankungen der Waarencirculation in Umfang,\n Preisen und Geschwindigkeit die Umlaufsmasse des Geldes rastlos ebbt\n und fluthet. Sie muss also der Contraktion und Expansion fähig sein.\n Bald muss Geld als Münze attrahirt, bald Münze als Geld repellirt werden.\n Damit die wirklich umlaufende Geldmasse dem Sättigungsgrad der Circu-\n lationssphäre stets entspreche, muss das in einem Lande befindliche Gold-\n oder Silberquantum grösser sein als das in Münzfunktion begriffene. Diese\n Bedingung wird erfüllt durch die Schatzform des Geldes. Die Schatz-\n reservoirs dienen zugleich als Abfuhr- und Zufuhrkanäle des cirkuliren-\n den Geldes, welches seine Umlaufskanäle daher nie überfüllt [Note: „There is required for carrying on the trade of the nation, a determinate\n sum of specifick Money, which varies, and is sometimes more, sometimes less, as\n the circumstances we are in require . . . . This ebbing and flowing of money,\n supplies and accommodates itself, without any aid of Politicians … The buckets\n work alternately; when money is scarce, bullion is coined; when bullion is scarce,\n money is melted.“ (Sir D. North l. c. p. 22.) John Stuart Mill,\n lange Zeit Beamter der ostindischen Compagnie, bestätigt, dass in Indien immer\n noch der Silberschmuck unmittelbar als Schatz functionirt. Die „silver ornaments\n are brought out and coined when there is a high rate of interest, and go back\n again when the rate of interest falls.“ (J. St. Mill’s Evidence. Repts on\n Bankacts 1857, n. 2084.) Nach einem parlamentarischen Document von\n 1864 über Gold- und Silber-Import und Export in Indien, überstieg 1863 der] .\n\n\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"96\", \"facs\": \"#f0115\"} -->\n\n\n\n## b) Zahlungsmittel.\n\n\n\n\nIn der bisher betrachteten unmittelbaren Form der Waarencirkulation\n war dieselbe Werthgrösse stets doppelt vorhanden, Waare auf dem einen Pol,\n Geld auf dem Gegenpol. Die Waarenbesitzer traten daher nur in Contakt als\n Repräsentanten wechselseitig vorhandner Aequivalente. Mit der Entwicklung\n der Waarencirkulation entwickeln sich jedoch Verhältnisse, wodurch die Ver-\n äusserung der Waare von der Realisirung ihres Preises zeitlich getrennt\n wird. Es genügt die einfachsten dieser Verhältnisse hier anzudeuten. Die\n eine Waarenart erheischt längere, die andere kürzere Zeitdauer zu ihrer\n Produktion. Die Produktion verschiedner Waaren ist an verschiedne\n Jahreszeiten geknüpft. Die eine Waare wird auf ihrem Marktplatz gebo-\n ren, die andre muss zu entferntem Markt reisen. Der eine Waarenbesitzer\n kann daher als Verkäufer auftreten, bevor der andre als Käufer. Bei\n steter Wiederkehr derselben Transactionen unter denselben Personen regeln\n sich die Verkaufsbedingungen der Waaren nach ihren Produktionsbedin-\n gungen. Der eine Waarenbesitzer verkauft vorhandne Waare, der andre\n kauft als blosser Repräsentant von Geld oder als Repräsentant von künf-\n tigem Gelde. Der Verkäufer wird Gläubiger, der Käufer Schuld-\n ner. Da die Metamorphose der Waare oder die Entwicklung ihrer\n Werthform sich hier verändert, erhält auch das Gold eine andre Funktion.\n Es wird Zahlungsmittel [Note: Luther unterscheidet zwischen Geld als Kaufmittel und Zahlungs-\n mittel. „Machest mir einen Zwilling aus dem Schadewacht, das ich hie nicht\nbezalen und dort nicht kauffen kann.“ (Martin Luther: „An die\n Pfarrherrn, wider den Wucher zu predigen. Wittenberg 1540.“)] .\n\n\n\n\nDer Charakter von Gläubiger oder Schuldner entspringt hier aus\n der einfachen Waarencirkulation. Ihre Formveränderung drückt dem\n Verkäufer und Käufer diese neuen Stempel auf. Zunächst also sind es\n ebenso verschwindende und wechselweis von denselben Cirkulationsagenten\n gespielte Rollen wie die von Verkäufer und Käufer. Jedoch sieht der\n Gegensatz jetzt von Haus aus minder gemüthlich aus und ist grösserer\n [Note: Import von Gold und Silber den Export um 19,367,764 Pfd. St.. In den letzten 8\n Jahren vor 1864 betrug der Excess des Imports über den Export der edlen Me-\n talle 109,652,917 Pfd. St. Während dieses Jahrhunderts wurden weit über\n 200,000,000 Pfd. St. in Indien gemünzt.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"97\", \"facs\": \"#f0116\"} --> Krystallisation fähig [Note: Ueber die Schuldner- und Gläubigerverhältnisse unter den englischen\n Handelsleuten Anfang des 18. Jahrhunderts: „Such a spirit of cruelty reigns\n here in England among the men of trade, that is not to be met with in any other\n society of men, nor in any other kingdom of the world.“ („An Essay on\n Credit and the Bankrupt Act. Lond. 1707“, p. 2.)] . Dieselben Charaktere können aber auch von\n der Waarencirculation unabhängig auftreten. Der Klassenkampf der an-\n tiken Welt z. B. bewegt sich hauptsächlich in der Form eines Kampfes\n zwischen Gläubiger und Schuldner, und endet in Rom mit dem Untergang\n des plebejischen Schuldners, der durch den Sklaven ersetzt wird. Im\n Mittelalter endet der Kampf mit dem Untergang des feudalen Schuldners,\n der seine politische Macht mit ihrer ökonomischen Basis einbüsst. Indess\n spiegelt die Geldform — und das Verhältniss von Gläubiger und Schuld-\n ner besitzt die Form eines Geldverhältnisses — hier nur den Antagonis-\n mus tiefer liegender ökonomischer Lebensbedingungen wieder.\n\n\n\n\nKehren wir zur Sphäre der Waarencirculation zurück. Die gleich-\n zeitige Erscheinung der Aequivalente Waare und Geld auf den beiden\n Polen des Verkaufsprozesses hat aufgehört. Das Geld funktionirt jetzt\n erstens als Werthmass in der Preisbestimmung der verkauften Waare.\n Ihr kontraktlich festgesetzter Preis misst die Obligation des Käufers, d. h.\n die Geldsumme, die er an bestimmtem Zeittermin schuldet. Es funktio-\n nirt zweitens als ideelles Kaufmittel. Obgleich es nur im Geld-\n versprechen des Käufers existirt, bewirkt es den Händewechsel der Waare.\n Erst am fälligen Zahlungstermin tritt das Zahlungsmittel wirklich in Circu-\n lation, d. h. geht aus der Hand des Käufers in die des Verkäufers über.\n Das Circulationsmittel verwandelte sich in Schatz, weil der Circulations-\n prozess mit der ersten Phase abbrach oder die verwandelte Gestalt der\n Waare der Circulation entzogen wurde. Das Zahlungsmittel tritt in\n die Circulation hinein, aber nachdem die Waare bereits aus ihr ausgetre-\n ten ist. Das Geld vermittelt nicht mehr den Prozess. Es schliesst ihn\n selbstständig ab, als absolutes Dasein des Tauschwerths oder allgemeine\n Waare. Der Verkäufer verwandelte Waare in Geld, um ein Bedürfniss\n durch das Geld zu befriedigen, der Schatzbildner, um die Waare in Geld-\n form zu präserviren, der schuldige Käufer, um zahlen zu können. Zahlt\n er nicht, so finden Zwangsverkäufe seiner Habe statt. Die Werthgestalt\n [Running matter: I. 7] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"98\", \"facs\": \"#f0117\"} --> der Waare, Geld, wird also jetzt zum Selbstzweck des Verkaufs\n durch eine den Verhältnissen des Circulationsprozesses selbst entspringende,\n gesellschaftliche Nothwendigkeit.\n\n\n\n\nDer Käufer verwandelt Geld zurück in Waare, bevor er Waare in\n Geld verwandelt hat, oder vollzieht die zweite Waarenmetamorphose vor\n der ersten. Die Waare des Verkäufers circulirt, realisirt ihren Preis aber\n nur in einem privatrechtlichen Titel auf Geld. Sie verwandelt sich in Ge-\n brauchswerth, bevor sie sich in Geld verwandelt hat. Die Vollziehung\n ihrer ersten Metamorphose folgt erst nachträglich.\n\n\n\n\nIn jedem bestimmten Zeitabschnitt des Circulationsprozesses reprä-\n sentiren die fälligen Obligationen die Preissumme der Waaren, deren\n Verkauf sie hervorrief. Die zur Realisirung dieser Preissumme nöthige\n Geldmasse hängt zunächst ab von der Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit\n der Zahlungsmittel. Sie ist bedingt durch zwei Umstände: die\nVerkettung der Verhältnisse von Gläubiger und Schuldner, so dass A,\n der Geld von seinem Schuldner B erhält, es an seinen Gläubiger C fort-\n zahlt u. s. w. — und die Zeitlänge zwischen den verschiednen Zah-\n lungsterminen. Die prozessirende Kette von Zahlungen oder nachträg-\n lichen ersten Metamorphosen unterscheidet sich wesentlich von der früher\n betrachteten Verschlingung der Metamorphosenreihen. Im Umlauf des\n Circulationsmittels wird der Zusammenhang zwischen Verkäufern und Käu-\n fern nicht nur ausgedrückt. Der Zusammenhang selbst entsteht erst\n in und mit dem Geldumlauf. Dagegen drückt die Bewegung des Zah-\n lungsmittels einen schon vor ihr fertig vorhandnen gesellschaftlichen Zu-\n sammenhang aus.\n\n\n\n\nGleichzeitigkeit und Nebeneinander der Verkäufe beschränken den Er-\n satz der Münzmasse durch Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit. Sie bilden umgekehrt\n einen neuen Hebel in der Oekonomie der Zahlungsmittel. Mit der Con-\n centration der Zahlungen an demselben Platz entwickeln sich naturwüch-\n sig eigne Anstalten und Methoden ihrer Ausgleichung. So z. B. die vire-\n ments im mittelaltrigen Lyon. Die Schuldforderungen von A an B, B an\n C, C an A u. s. w. brauchen bloss confrontirt zu werden, um sich wech-\n selseitig bis zu einem gewissen Belauf als positive und negative Grössen\n aufzuheben. So bleibt nur eine Schuldbilanz zu saldiren. Je massen-\n hafter die Concentration der Zahlungen, desto kleiner relativ die Bilanz,\n also die Masse der circulirenden Zahlungsmittel.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"99\", \"facs\": \"#f0118\"} -->\n\nDie Funktion des Geldes als Zahlungsmittel schliesst einen unvermit-\n telten Widerspruch ein. So weit sich die Zahlungen ausgleichen, funktio-\n nirt es nur ideell als Rechengeld oder Mass der Werthe. Soweit\n wirkliche Zahlung zu verrichten, tritt es nicht als Circulationsmittel auf,\n als nur verschwindende und vermittelnde Form des Stoffwechsels, sondern\n als die individuelle Incarnation der gesellschaftlichen Arbeit, selbstständiges\n Dasein des Tauschwerths, absolute Waare. Dieser Widerspruch eklatirt\n in dem Moment der Produktions- und Handelskrisen, der Geldkrise\n heisst [Note: Die Geldkrise, wie sie im Text bestimmt ist, als Phase jeder Krise,\n ist wohl zu unterscheiden von der besondern Krisenart, die man auch\n Geldkrise nennt, die aber ein ganz selbstständiges Phänomen bilden kann, so dass\n sie auf Industrie und Handel nur rückschlagend wirkt. Es sind diess Krisen,\n deren Bewegungscentrum das Geldkapital ist, und deren unmittelbare Sphäre daher\n auch die Sphäre der Haupt- und Staatsaktionen des Geldkapitals, Bank, Börse,\n Finanz.] . Sie ereignet sich nur, wo die prozessirende Kette der Zahlun-\n gen und ein künstliches System ihrer Ausgleichung völlig entwickelt sind.\n Mit allgemeineren Störungen dieses Mechanismus, woher sie immer ent-\n springen mögen, schlägt das Geld plötzlich und unvermittelt aus der nur\n ideellen Gestalt des Rechengeldes in hartes Geld um. Es wird unersetz-\n lich durch profane Waaren. Der Gebrauchswerth der Waare wird werth-\n los und ihr Werth verschwindet vor seiner eignen Werthform. Eben noch\n erklärte der Bürger in prosperitätstrunknem Aufklärungsdünkel das Geld\n für leeren Wahn. Nur die Waare ist Geld. Nur das Geld ist Waare!\n gellt’s jetzt über den Weltmarkt. Wie der Hirsch schreit nach frischem\n Wasser, so schreit seine Seele nach Geld, dem einzigen Reichthum [Note: „Dieses plötzliche Umschlagen aus dem Kreditsystem in das Monetar-\n system fügt den theoretischen Schrecken zum praktischen Panik: und die Circu-\n lationsagenten schaudern vor dem undurchdringlichen Geheimniss ihrer eignen\n Verhältnisse.“ (Karl Marx l. c. p. 126.) „The Poor stand still, because\n the Rich have no Money to employ them, though they have the same land and\n hands to provide victuals and cloaths, as ever they had; which is the true Riches\n of a Nation, and not the Money.“ (John Bellers: „Proposals forrais-\n ing a Colledge of Industry. Lond. 1696“, p. 3.)] .\n In der Krise wird der Gegensatz zwischen der Waare und ihrer Werthgestalt,\n dem Geld, bis zum absoluten Widerspruch gesteigert. Die Erscheinungs-\n form des Geldes ist hier daher auch gleichgültig. Die Geldhungersnoth\n [Running matter: 7*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"100\", \"facs\": \"#f0119\"} --> bleibt dieselbe, ob in Gold oder Creditgeld, Banknoten etwa, zu zah-\n len ist [Note: Wie solche Momente von den „amis du commerce“ ausgebeutet werden:\n „On one occasion (1839) an old grasping banker (der City) in his private room,\n raised the lid of the desk he sat over, and displayed to a friend rolls of banknotes,\n saying with intense glee there were 600,000 £ of them, they were held to\n make money tight, and would all be let out after three o’clock on the same\n day.“ („The Theory of the Exchanges. The Bank Charter Act\n of 1844. Lond. 1864“, p. 81.) Das halbofficielle Organ, „The Ob-\n server“, bemerkt am 24. April 1864: „Some very curious rumours are current\n of the means which have been resorted to in order to create a scarcity\n of Banknotes … Questionable at it would seem, to suppose that any trick\n of the kind would be adopted, the report has been so universal that it really\n deserves mention.“] .\n\n\n\n\nBetrachten wir nun die Gesammtsumme des in einem gegebenen\n Zeitabschnitt umlaufenden Geldes, so ist sie, bei gegebener Umlaufsge-\n schwindigkeit der Cirkulations- und Zahlungsmittel, gleich der Summe der\n zu realisirenden Waarenpreise plus der Summe der fälligen Zahlungen\n minus der sich ausgleichenden Zahlungen. Selbst Preise, Geschwindigkeit des\n Geldumlaufs, und Oekonomie der Zahlungen gegeben, decken sich daher\n nicht länger die während einer Periode, eines Tags z. B., umlaufende\n Geldmasse und cirkulirende Waarenmasse. Es läuft Geld um, das der\n Cirkulation längst entzogne Waaren repräsentirt. Es laufen Waaren um,\n deren Geldäquivalent erst in der Zukunft erscheint. Andrerseits sind die\n jeden Tag contrahirten und die denselben Tag fälligen Zahlungen\n durchaus incommensurable Grössen [Note: „The amount of sales or contracts entered upon during the course of any\n given day, will not affect the quantity of money afloat on that particular day,\n but, in the vast majority of cases, will resolve themselves into multifarious drafts\n upon the quantity of money which may be afloat at subsequent dates more or less\n distant … The bills granted or credits opened, to day, need have no resemblance\n whatever, either in quantity, amount or duration, to those granted or entered upon\n to-morrow or next day; nay, many of to-day’s bills, and credits, when due, fall\n in with a mass of liabilities whose origins traverse a range of antecedent dates al-\n together indefinite, bills at 12, 6, 3 months or 1 often aggregating together to\n swell the common liabilities of one particular day . . . .“ („The currency\n Question Reviewed; a letter to the Scotch people. By a Banker\n in England. Edinburgh 1845“, p. 29, 30 passim.)] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"101\", \"facs\": \"#f0120\"} -->\n\nDas Creditgeld entspringt unmittelbar aus der Funktion des Geldes\n als Zahlungsmittel, indem Schuldcertificate für die verkauften Waaren selbst\n wieder zur Uebertragung der Schuldforderungen cirkuliren. Andrerseits,\n wie sich das Creditwesen ausdehnt, so die Funktion des Geldes als Zahlungs-\n mittel. Als solches erhält es eigne Existenzformen, worin es die Sphäre der\n grossen Handelstransaktionen behaust, während die Gold- oder Silbermünze\n hauptsächlich in die Sphäre des Kleinhandels zurückgedrängt wird [Note: Als Beispiel, wie wenig reelles Geld in die eigentlichen Handelsoperationen\n eingeht, folgt hier das Schema eines der grössten Londoner Handelshäuser über\n seine jährlichen Geldeinnahmen und Zahlungen. Seine Transaktionen im Jahr\n 1856, die viele Millionen Pf. St. umfassen, sind auf den Massstab einer Million\n verkürzt.\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n (Report from the Select Committee on the Bankacts. July\n 1858, p. LXXI.)] .\n\n\n\n\nBei gewissem Höhegrad und Umfang der Waarenproduktion greift die\n Funktion des Geldes als Zahlungsmittel über die Sphäre der Waarencircu-\n lation hinaus. Es wird die allgemeine Waare der Contrakte [Note: „The Course of Trade being thus turned, from exchanging of goods for\n goods, or delivering and taking, to selling and paying, all the bargains\n . . . . are now stated upon the foot of a Price in Money.“ („An Essay upon\n Publick Credit. 3ed. Lond. 1710“, p. 8.)] .\n Renten, Steuern u. s. w. verwandeln sich aus Naturallieferungen in Geld-\n zahlungen. Wie sehr diese Umwandlung durch die Gesammtgestalt des\n Produktionsprozesses bedingt wird, beweist z. B. der zweimal gescheiterte\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"102\", \"facs\": \"#f0121\"} --> Versuch des römischen Kaiserreichs alle Abgaben in Geld zu erheben.\n Das ungeheure Elend des französischen Landvolks unter Ludwig XIV., das\n Boisguillebert, Marschall Vauban u. s. w. so beredt denunciren, war nicht\n nur der Steuerhöhe geschuldet, sondern auch der Verwandlung von Natu-\n ralsteuer in Geldsteuer [Note: „L’argent est devenu le bourreau de toutes les choses.“ Die Finanz-\n kunst ist das „alambic qui a fait évaporer une quantité effrayable de biens et de\n denrées pour faire ce fatal précis.“ „L’argent déclare la guerre à tout le genre\n humain.“ (Boisguillebert: „Dissertation sur la nature des\n richesses, de l’argent et des tributs“, edit. Daire, „Écono-\n mistes financiers. Paris 1843, t. I, p. 413, 419, 417.)] . Wenn andrerseits die Naturalform der Grund-\n rente, in Asien zugleich das Hauptelement der Staatssteuer, dort auf\n Produktionsverhältnissen beruht, welche sich mit der Unwandelbarkeit von\n Naturverhältnissen reproduciren, erhält jene Zahlungsform rückwirkend\n die alte Produktionsform. Sie bildet eines der Selbsterhaltungsgeheim-\n nisse des türkischen Reichs. Zieht der durch Europa aufoctroyirte aus-\n wärtige Handel in Japan die Verwandlung von Naturalrente in Geldrente\n nach sich, so ist es um seine musterhafte Agrikultur geschehn. Ihre engen\n ökonomischen Existenzbedingungen werden sich auflösen.\n\n\n\n\nIn jedem Land setzen sich gewisse allgemeine Zahlungster-\n mine fest. Sie beruhn theilweis, von andern Cirkelläufen der Reproduk-\n tion abgesehn, auf den an Wechsel der Jahreszeit gebundnen Naturbe-\n dingungen der Produktion. Sie regeln ebenso Zahlungen, die nicht direkt\n der Waarencirculation entspringen, wie Steuern, Renten u. s. w. Die\n Geldmasse, die zu diesen über die ganze Oberfläche der Gesellschaft zer-\n splitterten Zahlungen an gewissen Tagen des Jahres erheischt ist, verur-\n sacht periodische, aber ganz oberflächliche Perturbationen in der Oeko-\n nomie der Zahlungsmittel [Note: „Pfingstmontag 1824“, erzählt Herr Craig dem parlamentarischen Unter-\n suchungscomité von 1826, „war eine solche ungeheure Nachfrage für Banknoten\n in Edinburg, dass wir um 11 Uhr keine einzige Note mehr in unserm Verwahrsam\n hatten. Wir sandten der Reihe nach zu den verschiednen Banken um welche\n zu borgen, konnten aber keine erhalten, und viele Transaktionen konnten nur\n durch slips of paper berichtigt werden. Um 3 Uhr Nachmittags jedoch waren\n bereits sämmtliche Noten returnirt zu den Banken, von denen sie ausliefen. Sie\n hatten nur die Hände gewechselt.“ Obgleich die effektive Durchschnittscirculation\n der Banknoten in Schottland weniger als 3 Mill. Pf. St. beträgt, wird dennoch, an ver-] . Aus dem Gesetz über die Umlaufsgeschwin-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"103\", \"facs\": \"#f0122\"} --> digkeit der Zahlungsmittel folgt, dass für alle periodischen Zah-\n lungen, welches immer ihre Quelle, die nothwendige Masse der\n Zahlungsmittel in umgekehrtem Verhältniss zur Länge\n der Zahlungsperioden steht [Note: Auf die Frage „if there were occasion to raise 40 millions p. a., whether\n the same 6 millions (Gold) would suffice for such revolutions and circu-\n lations thereof as trade requires?“, antwortet Petty mit seiner gewöhnlichen\n Meisterschaft: „I answer yes: for the expense being 40 millions, if the revolu-\n tions were in such short circles, viz. weekly, as happens among\n poor artizans and labourers, who receive and pay every Saturday, then \\frac{40}{52} parts\n of 1 million of money would answer these ends; but if the circles be quar-\n terly, according to our custom of paying rent, and gathering taxes, then 10\n millions were requisite. Wherefore supposing payments in general to be of a\nmixed circle between one week and 12, then add 10 millions to \\frac{40}{52}, the\n half of the which will be 5½, so as if we have 5½ mill., we have enough.“\n (William Petty: „Political Anatomy of Ireland. 1672“, edit.\nLond. 1691, p. 13, 14.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Entwicklung des Geldes als Zahlungsmittel ernöthigt Geldaccu-\n mulationen für die Verfallstermine der geschuldeten Summen. Während\n die Schatzbildung als selbstständige Bereicherungsform verschwindet mit\n dem Fortschritt der bürgerlichen Gesellschaft, wächst sie umgekehrt mit\n demselben in der Form von Reservefonds der Zahlungs-\n mittel.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## c) Weltgeld.\n\n\n\n\nMit dem Austritt aus der innern Circulationssphäre streift das Geld\n die dort aufschiessenden Lokalformen vom Massstab der Preise, Münze,\n Scheidemünze und Werthzeichen wieder ab und fällt in die ursprüngliche\n Barrenform der edlen Metalle zurück. Im Welthandel entfalten die Waaren\n ihren Werth universell. Ihre selbstständige Werthgestalt tritt ihnen daher\n hier auch gegenüber als Weltgeld. Erst auf dem Weltmarkt funktio-\n [Note: schiednen Zahlungsterminen im Jahr, jede im Besitz der Banquiers befindliche\n Note, alles in allem ungefähr 7 Mill. Pf. St., in Aktivität gerufen. Bei diesen Ge-\n legenheiten haben die Noten eine einzige und specifische Funktion zu vollziehen\n und sobald sie vollzogen, fliessen sie zu den respektiven Banken zurück, von denen\n sie ausliefen.“ (John Fullarton: „Regulation of Currencies. 2nd\n ed. Lond. 1845“ p. 86 Nte.) Zum Verständniss ist hinzuzufügen, dass in\n Schottland nicht cheques, sondern nur Noten für die Deposits ausgegeben werden.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"104\", \"facs\": \"#f0123\"} --> nirt das Geld in vollem Umfang als die Waare, deren Naturalform zugleich\n unmittelbar gesellschaftliche Verwirklichungsform der menschlichen\n Arbeit in abstracto ist. Seine Daseinsweise wird seinem Begriff\n adäquat.\n\n\n\n\nIn der innern Cirkulationssphäre kann nur eine Waare zum Werth-\n mass und daher als Geld dienen. Auf dem Weltmarkt herrscht dop-\n peltes Werthmass, Gold und Silber [Note: Daher die Abgeschmacktheit jeder Gesetzgebung, die den Nationalbanken\n vorschreibt, nur das edle Metall aufzuschatzen, das im Innern des Landes als Geld\n funktionirt. Die so selbstgeschaffenen „holden Hindernisse“ der Bank von Eng-\n land z. B. sind bekannt. Ueber die grossen historischen Epochen des relativen\n Werthwechsels von Gold in Silber sieh Karl Marx l. c. p. 136 sq.] .\n\n\n\n\nDas Weltgeld funktionirt als allgemeines Zahlungsmittel,\n allgemeines Kaufmittel und absolut gesellschaftliche\n Materiatur des Reichthums überhaupt (universal wealth).\n Die Funktion als Zahlungsmittel, zur Ausgleichung internationaler\n Bilanzen, herrscht vor. Daher das Losungswort des Merkantilsystems —\nHandelsbilanz [Note: Die Gegner des Merkantilsystems, welches die Saldirung überschüssiger\n Handelsbilanz durch Gold und Silber als Zweck des Welthandels behandelte,\n verkannten ihrerseits durchaus die Funktion des Weltgeldes. Wie die falsche\n Auffassung der Gesetze, welche die Masse der Circulationsmittel regeln, sich in\n der falschen Auffassung der internationalen Bewegung der edlen Metalle nur\n wiederspiegelt, habe ich ausführlich an Ricardo nachgewiesen. (l. c. p. 150\n sqq.) Sein falsches Dogma: „An unfavourable balance of trade never arises but\n from a redundant currency … The exportation of the coin is caused by its\n cheapness, and is not the effect, but the cause of an unfavourable balance“ findet\n man daher schon bei Barbon: „The Balance of Trade, if there be one, is\nnot the cause of sending away the money out of a nation: but that\n proceeds from the difference of the value of Bullion in every\n country.“ (N. Barbon l. c. p. 59, 60.) MacCulloch in „The Litte-\n rature of Political Economy, a classified catalogue. Lond.\n 1845“ belobt Barbon für diese Anticipation, vermeidet aber wohlweislich die\n naiven Formen, worin bei B. die absurden Voraussetzungen des „currency prin-\n ciple“ noch erscheinen, auch nur zu erwähnen. Die Kritiklosigkeit und selbst Un-\n ehrlichkeit jenes Katalogs gipfeln in den Abschnitten über die Geschichte der Geld-\n theorie, weil Mac Culloch hier als Sykophant des Lord Overstone (ex-banker\n Loyd), den er „facile princeps argentariorum“ nennt, schwanzwedelt.] ! Zum internationalen Kaufmittel dienen Gold\n und Silber wesentlich, so oft das herkömmliche Gleichgewicht des Stoff-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"105\", \"facs\": \"#f0124\"} --> wechsels zwischen verschiednen Nationen plötzlich gestört wird. Endlich\n als absolut gesellschaftliche Materiatur des Reichthums, wo es sich weder\n um Kauf noch Zahlung handelt, sondern um Uebertragung des Reich-\n thums von einem Land zum andern, und wo diese Uebertragung in Waa-\n renform entweder durch die Conjunkturen des Waarenmarkts oder den\n zu erfüllenden Zweck selbst ausgeschlossen wird [Note: Z. B. bei Subsidien, Geldanleihn zur Kriegsführung oder zur Wiederauf-\n nahme der Baarzahlungen von Banken u. s. w. kann Werth grade in der Geldform\n erheischt sein.] .\n\n\n\n\nWie für seine innere Circulation, braucht jedes Land für die Welt-\n marktscirculation einen Reservefonds. Die Funktionen der Schätze ent-\n springen also theils aus der Funktion des Geldes als inneres Circulations-\n und Zahlungsmittel, theils seiner Funktion als Weltgeld. In der letz-\n teren Rolle ist stets die wirkliche Geldwaare, leibhaftes Gold und Sil-\n ber erheischt, wesswegen James Steuart Gold und Silber, im Unter-\n schied von ihren nur lokalen Stellvertretern, ausdrücklich als money\n of the world charakterisirt.\n\n\n\n\nDie Bewegung des Gold- und Silberstroms ist eine doppelte. Einer-\n seits wälzt er sich von seinen Quellen über den ganzen Weltmarkt, wo er\n von den verschiednen nationalen Circulationssphären in verschiednem Um-\n fang abgefangen wird, um in die inneren Umlaufskanäle einzugehn, ver-\n schlissene Gold- und Silbermünzen zu ersetzen, das Material von Luxus-\n waaren zu liefern und zu Schätzen zu erstarren [Note: „L’argent se partage entre les nations relativement au besoin qu’elles en\n ont … étant toujours attiré par les productions.“ (Le Trosne l. c. p. 916.)\n „The mines which are continually giving gold and silver, do give sufficient to\n supply such a needful balance to every nation.“ (J. Vanderlint l. c. p. 40.)] . Diese erste Bewegung\n ist vermittelt durch direkten Austausch der in Waare realisirten National-\n arbeiten mit der in edlen Metallen realishten Arbeit der Gold und Silber\n producirenden Länder. Andrerseits laufen Gold und Silber fortwährend\n hin und her zwischen den verschiednen nationalen Circulationssphären, eine\n Bewegung, die den unaufhörlichen Oscillationen des Wechselkurses\n folgt [Note: „Exchanges rise and fall every week, and at some particular times in the\n year run high against a nation, and at other times run as high on the contrary.“\n (N. Barbon l. c. p. 39.)] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"106\", \"facs\": \"#f0125\"} -->\n\nLänder entwickelter bürgerlicher Produktion beschränken die in Bank-\n reservoirs massenhaft concentrirten Schätze auf das zu ihren spezifischen\n Funktionen erheischte Minimum [Note: Diese verschiedenen Funktionen können in gefährlichen Konflikt gerathen,\n sobald die Funktion eines Conversionsfonds für Banknoten hinzutritt.] . Mit gewisser Ausnahme zeigt auf-\n fallendes Ueberfüllen der Schatzreservoirs über ihr Durchschnittsniveau\n Stockung der Waarencirculation an oder unterbrochenen Fluss der Waa-\n renmetamorphose [Note: „What money is more than of absolute necessity for a Home Trade, is\ndead stock, and brings no profit to that country it’s kept in, but as it is trans-\n ported in Trade, as well as imported.“ (John Bellers l. c. p. 12.) „What\n if we have too much coin? We may melt down the heaviest and turn it into the\n splendour of plate, vessels or ustensils of gold and silver; or send it out as a\n commodity, where the same is wanted or desired; or let it out at interest, where\n interest is high.“ (W. Petty: „Quantulumcunque“, p. 39.) „Money\n is but the fat of the Body Politick, whereof too much does as often hinder its\n agility, as too little makes it sick. . . . . . . . as fat lubricates the motion of\n the muscles, feeds in want of victuals, fills up uneven cavities, and beautifies the\n body; so doth money in the state quicken its action, feeds from abroad in time\n of dearth at home; evens accounts … and beautifies the whole, although“, iro-\n nisch abschliessend, „more especially the particular persons that\n have it in plenty.“ (W. Petty: „Political anatomy of Ireland“ p. 14.)] .","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0001","text":"## A. Mass der Werthe.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"3.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0002","text":"Ich setze überall in dieser Schrift, der Vereinfachung halber, Gold\n als die Geldwaare voraus.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"3.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0003","text":"Dadurch dass sich die Waaren in Gold ihren allgemeinen relativen\n Werthausdruck geben, funktionirt das Gold ihnen gegenüber als Mass\n der Werthe. Die Waaren werden nicht durch das Geld commensurabel.\n Umgekehrt. Weil alle Waaren als Werthe vergegenständ-\n lichte menschliche Arbeit, daher an und für sich commensurabel\n sind, können sie sich alle in irgend einer dritten Waare messen und diese\n dadurch in ihr gemeinschaftliches Werthmass oder Geld verwandeln. Geld\n als Werthmass ist aber nothwendige Erscheinungsform des\nimmanenten Werthmasses der Waaren, der Arbeitszeit [Note: Die Frage, warum das Geld nicht unmittelbar die Arbeitszeit\n selbst repräsentirt, so dass z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"3.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0004","text":"eine Papiernote x Arbeitsstunden vorstellte,\n kömmt ganz einfach auf die Frage heraus, warum auf Grundlage der Waarenpro-\n duktion die Arbeitsprodukte sich als Waaren darstellen müssen, denn die Dar-\n stellung der Waare schliesst ihre Verdopplung in Waare und Geldwaare ein.\n Oder warum Privatarbeit nicht als unmittelbar gesellschaftliche Arbeit, als ihr\n Gegentheil, behandelt werden kann. Ich habe den seichten Utopismus eines\n „Arbeitsgelds“ auf Grundlage der Waarenproduktion anderswo ausführlicher er-\n örtert. (l. c. p. 61 sqq.) Hier sei noch bemerkt, dass z. B. das Owensche\n „Arbeitsgeld“ ebensowenig „Geld“ ist, wie etwa eine Theatermarke. Owen setzt\nunmittelbar vergesellschaftete Arbeit voraus, eine der Waaren-\n produktion diametral entgegengesetzte Produktionsform.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"3.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0005","text":"Das Arbeitscertifikat kon-\n statirt nur den individuellen Antheil des Producenten an der Gemeinarbeit\n und seinen individuellen Anspruch auf den zur Konsumtion bestimmten Theil des\nGemeinprodukts. Aber es fällt Owen nicht ein, die Waarenproduktion einer-\n seits vorauszusetzen und dennoch andrerseits ihre nothwendigen Bedingungen\n durch Geldpfuschereien umgehn zu wollen.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"3.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0006","text":"Der einfache relative Werthausdruck der Waaren in\nGeld — x Waare A = y Geldwaare — ist ihr Preis. In ihren Prei-\n sen erscheinen die Waaren erstens als Werthe, qualitativ Gleiche,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"56\", \"facs\": \"#f0075\"} -->Materiatur derselben Arbeit oder dieselbe Materiatur der\n Arbeit, Gold, zweitens als quantitativ bestimmte Werthgrös-\n sen, denn in der Proportion, worin sie gleich bestimmten Goldquanta,\n sind sie einander gleich oder stellen gleiche Arbeitsquanta vor. Andrer-\n seits wird der entfaltete relative Werthausdruck oder die end-\n lose Reihe relativer Werthausdrücke zur spezifisch relativen\n Werthform der Geldwaare. Diese Reihe ist aber jetzt schon ge-\n geben in den Waarenpreisen. Man lese die Quotationen eines Preiscou-\n rants rückwärts und man findet die Werthgrösse des Geldes in allen mög-\n lichen Waaren dargestellt. Die Reihe hat auch neuen Sinn erhalten.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"3.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0007","text":"Das Gold, weil Geld, besitzt bereits in seiner Naturalform die allgemeine\n Aequivalentform oder die Form allgemeiner unmittelbarer Austauschbar-\n keit unabhängig von seinen relativen Werthausdrücken. Ihre Reihe stellt\n jetzt daher zugleich, ausser seiner Werthgrösse, die entfaltete Welt des\nstofflichen Reichthums oder der Gebrauchswerthe vor, worin es un-\n mittelbar umsetzbar ist. Geld hat dagegen keinen Preis. Um an\n dieser einheitlichen relativen Werthform der andern Waaren theilzu-\n nehmen, müsste es auf sich selbst als sein eignes Aequivalent bezogen\n werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"3.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0008","text":"Für die Bewegung der Waarenpreise gelten die früher gegebnen\n Gesetze des einfachen relativen Werthausdrucks. Die Waarenpreise\n können nur allgemein steigen, bei gleichbleibendem Geldwerth, wenn die\n Waarenwerthe steigen, bei gleichbleibenden Waarenwerthen, wenn der\n Geldwerth fällt. Umgekehrt. Die Waarenpreise können nur allge-\n mein fallen, bei gleichbleibendem Geldwerth, wenn die Waarenwerthe\n fallen, bei gleichbleibenden Waarenwerthen, wenn der Geldwerth steigt.\n Es folgt daher keineswegs, dass steigender Geldwerth proportionelles Sin-\n ken der Waarenpreise und fallender Geldwerth proportionelles Steigen der\n Waarenpreise bedingt. Diess gilt nur für Waaren von unverändertem\n Werth. Solche Waaren z. B., deren Werth gleichmässig und gleichzeitig\n steigt mit dem Geldwerth, behalten dieselben Preise.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"3.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0009","text":"Steigt ihr Werth\n langsamer oder rascher als der Geldwerth, so wird der Fall oder das Stei-\n gen ihrer Preise beschränkt durch die Differenz zwischen ihrer\n Werthbewegung und der des Geldes u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"3.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0010","text":"Die preisbestimmte Waare hat doppelte Form, reelle und\nvorgestellte oder ideelle. Ihre wirkliche Gestalt ist die eines Ge-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"57\", \"facs\": \"#f0076\"} --> brauchsgegenstandes, eines Produkts konkreter nützlicher Ar-\n beit, z. B. Eisen. Ihre Werthgestalt, ihre Erscheinungsform als Mate-\n riatur eines bestimmten Quantums gleichartiger menschlicher\n Arbeit, ist ihr Preis, ein Quantum Gold. Aber Gold ist vom Eisen ver-\n schiednes Ding und in seinem Preise bezieht das Eisen sich selbst\n auf Gold als ein andres Ding, das jedoch ihm Werth-Gleiches ist.\n Der Preis oder die Geldform der Waare existirt nur in dieser gleichsetzen-\n den Beziehung, also so zu sagen nur in ihrem Kopfe, und ihr Besitzer\n muss seine Zunge in ihren Kopf stecken oder ihr Papierzettel anhängen,\n um ihren Preis für die Aussenwelt vorzustellen [Note: Der Wilde oder Halbwilde braucht die Zunge anders.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"3.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0011","text":"Kapitain\nParry bemerkt z. B. von den Bewohnern an der Westküste der Baffinsbay:\n „In this case (beim Produktenaustausch) .... they licked it (the thing re-\n presented to them) twice to their tongues after which they seemed to consider the\n bargain satisfactorily concluded.“ Ebenso beleckte bei den östlichen Eskimos\n der Eintauscher jedesmal den Artikel beim Empfang desselben. Wenn die Zunge\n so im Norden als Organ der Aneignung, ist es kein Wunder, dass der Bauch\n im Süden als Organ des accumulirten Eigenthums gilt und der Kaffer den Reich-\n thum eines Mannes nach seinem Fettwanst schätzt. Die Kaffern sind grund-\n gescheute Kerle, denn während der britische „Board of Health“ in seinem\n Bericht von 1864 den Mangel eines grossen Theils der Arbeiterklasse an fettbil-\n denden Substanzen beklagt, machte ein Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"3.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0012","text":"Harvey, der jedoch nicht die Blut-\n circulation erfunden hat, in demselben Jahre sein Glück durch Puff-Recepte, die\n der Bourgeoisie und Aristokratie Fettüberflusseslast abzutreiben versprachen] . Die Form ihres\n Werths ist daher vorgestellte, ideelle Geldform im Unterschied zur hand-\n greiflich reellen Körperform ihres Gebrauchswerths. Da die Waaren so\n ihre Werthe nur ideell im Geld ausdrücken, drücken sie dieselben auch in\n nur vorgestelltem oder ideellem Geld aus. Mass der Werthe ist das\n Geld daher nur als vorgestelltes ideelles Geld. Jeder Waaren-\n besitzer weiss, dass er kein wirkliches Gold verbraucht, wenn er Waaren\n in Gold schätzt oder dem Waarenwerth die Form des Waarenpreises\n giebt. Obgleich nun das Geld als Werthmass nur ideell funktionirt,\n hängt der Preis dennoch ganz vom reellen Geldmaterial ab. Denn\n eine Waare, eine Tonne Eisen z.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"3.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0013","text":"B., wird in ihrem Preise als Materiatur\n eines bestimmten Quantums Arbeit auf bestimmtes Quantum Geldmaterial\n als Materiatur desselben Quantums Arbeit bezogen, aber dasselbe\n Quantum Arbeit materialisirt sich in ganz verschiednen Quanta Gold, Sil-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"58\", \"facs\": \"#f0077\"} --> ber oder Kupfer. Der Werth einer Tonne Eisen erhält also ganz ver-\n schiedne Preisausdrücke, je nachdem Gold, Silber oder Kupfer als Werth-\n mass funktionirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"3.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0014","text":"Die preisbestimmten Waaren stellen sich alle dar in der Form:\n a Waare A = x Gold; b Waare B = z Gold, c Waare C = y Gold u. s. w.,\n wo a, b, c bestimmte Masse der Waarenarten A, B, C, x, z, y be-\n stimmte Masse des Goldes. Die Waarenwerthe sind daher ver-\n wandelt in vorgestellte Goldquanta von verschiedner Grösse,\n also, trotz der wirren Buntheit der Waarenkörper, in gleichnamige\n Grössen, Goldgrössen. Verschiedne Goldquanta, weil gleichnamige\n Grössen, vergleichen und messen sich unter einander, indem sie auf ein\nfixirtes Quantum Gold als ihre Masseinheit bezogen werden. Diese\n Masseinheit selbst wird durch weitere Eintheilung in aliquote Theile zum\nMassstab fortentwickelt. Solche Massstäbe besassen Gold, Silber, Kupfer\n bereits in ihren Metallgewichten, bevor sie Geld wurden. Ihr vor-\n gefundener metallischer Gewichtmassstab dient daher ursprünglich auch\n stets in ihrer Geldfunktion.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"3.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0015","text":"Die Geldnamen der Metallgewichte trennen sich jedoch nach und\n nach von ihren ursprünglichen Gewichtnamen aus verschiedenen\n Gründen, darunter historisch entscheidend: 1) Einführung fremden\n Geldes bei minder entwickelten Völkern, wie z. B. im alten Rom Silber-\n und Goldmünzen zuerst als ausländische Waaren circulirten. Die Namen\n dieses fremden Gelds sind von den einheimischen Gewichtnamen verschie-\n den. 2) Mit der Entwicklung des Reichthums wird das minder edle Metall\n durch das edlere aus der Funktion des Werthmasses verdrängt, Kupfer\n durch Silber, Silber durch Gold, so sehr diese Reihenfolge aller poetischen\n Chronologie widersprechen mag [Note: Sie ist übrigens auch nicht von allgemein historischer Gültigkeit.] . Pfund war nun z. B. Geldname\n für ein wirkliches Pfund Silber. Sobald Gold das Silber als Werthmass\n verdrängt, hängt sich derselbe Name vielleicht an \\frac{1}{15} u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"3.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0016","text":"w. Pfund\n Gold, je nach dem Werthverhältniss von Gold und Silber. Pfund als\n Geldname und als gewöhnlicher Gewichtname des Goldes sind jetzt ge-\n trennt. 3) Die Jahrhunderte lang fortgesetzte Geldfälschung der\n Fürsten, welche vom ursprünglichen Gewicht der Geldmünzen in der\n That nur den Namen zurückliess.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"3.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0017","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"59\", \"facs\": \"#f0078\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"3.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0018","text":"Diese historischen Prozesse machen die Trennung des Geldnamens\n der Metallgewichte von ihrem gewöhnlichen Gewichtnamen zur Volksge-\n wohnheit. Bei definitiver Reglung des Geldmassstabs, die einerseits rein\n conventionell ist, andrerseits gesetzlicher Allgemeinheit und Zwangsgültig-\n keit bedarf, versteht es sich zuletzt von selbst, dass die Staatsautorität\n einen bestimmten Gewichttheil des edlen Metalls, z. B. eine Unze Gold, als\n Gewichteinheit fixirt und diese abtheilt in aliquote Theile, denen sie belie-\n bige legale Taufnamen beilegt, wie Pfund, Thaler u. s. w. Solcher ali-\n quote Theil, der dann als die eigentliche Masseinheit des Geldes gilt, wird\n weiter getheilt und untergetheilt in andre aliquote Theile, die ihrerseits\n gesetzliche Taufnamen erhalten, wie Shilling, Penny u. s. w. Nach wie\n vor bleiben bestimmte Metallgewichte Massstab des Metallgelds.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"3.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0019","text":"Was sich\n geändert, ist Eintheilung und Namengebung.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"3.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0020","text":"Die Waaren verwandeln Gold in das Mass der Werthe, indem\n sie allseitig ihre Werthe in ihm ausdrücken. So erhalten ihre Werth-\n grössen die Form der Preise oder vorgestellter Goldquanta.\n Diese Verwandlung von Werth in Preis einmal vollbracht, wird es tech-\n nisch nothwendig, das Mass der Werthe weiter zu bestimmen zum\nMassstab der Preise. Beide Funktionen sind durchaus verschieden.\n Als Massstab der Preise kann und muss ein bestimmtes Quantum Gold\nfixirt werden, grade wie der Massstab andrer gleichnamiger Grössen.\n Der Werthwechsel des Goldes ändert nichts am Werthverhältniss sei-\n ner verschiednen Gewichttheile unter einander und die gesetzlich fixirten\n Taufnamen dieser Theile ändern nichts an ihrem Gewicht. Der Massstab\n der Preise misst aber nur verschiedne Quanta Gold an einem fixirten Gold-\n quantum, nicht den Werth eines Goldquantums durch das Gewicht\n des andern.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"3.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0021","text":"Der Werthwechsel des Goldes verhindert auch nicht seine Funk-\n tion als Werthmass. Er trifft alle Waaren gleichzeitig, lässt also,\n caeteris paribus, ihre wechselseitigen relativen Werthe unverändert,\n obgleich sie sich nun alle in höheren oder niedrigeren Goldpreisen als zu-\n vor ausdrücken.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"3.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0022","text":"Die Waaren stellen ihre Werthe jetzt nicht nur gleichnamig als\n Gold, sondern in denselben gesellschaftlich gültigen Rechennamen des\n Goldmassstabs, wie Pfd. St. s. d. u. s. w., dar. Das Geld dient als Rechen-\n geld, so oft es gilt eine Sache als Werth und daher in Geldform zu fixiren.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"3.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0023","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"60\", \"facs\": \"#f0079\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"3.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0024","text":"Der Name einer Sache ist ihrer Natur ganz äusserlich und da-\n her erlischt auch ihre Begriffsbestimmung in ihm. Ich weiss nichts vom\n Menschen, wenn ich weiss, dass ein Mensch Jakobus heisst. Ebenso ver-\n schwindet in den Geldnamen Pfund, Thaler, Frank, Dukat u. s. w.\n jede Spur des Werthverhältnisses. Die Wirre über den Geheimsinn\n dieser kabbalistischen Zeichen ist um so grösser als die Geldnamen zugleich\n den Werth der Waaren und aliquote Theile eines Goldgewichts,\n des Geldmassstabs, und das eine nur darstellen, weil das andre [Note: Vgl. „Theorieen von der Masseinheit des Geldes“ in „Zur Kritik\n der pol. Oekon. etc.“, p. 53 sqq.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"3.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0025","text":"Die Phantasien über Erhöhung oder\n Erniedrigung des „Münzpreises“, die darin besteht, die gesetzlichen Geld-\n namen für gesetzlich fixirte Gewichttheile Gold oder Silber auf grössere oder\n kleinere Gewichttheile von Staatswegen zu übertragen und demgemäss auch etwa\n ¼ Unze Gold statt in 20 künftig in 40 sh. zu prägen — diese Phantasien, soweit\n sie nicht ungeschickte Finanzoperationen gegen Staats- und Privatgläubiger, son-\n dern ökonomische „Wunderkuren“ bezwecken, hat Petty so erschöpfend be-\n handelt in „Quantulumcumque concerning Money. To the Lord\n Marquis of Halifax. 1682“, dass schon seine unmittelbaren Nachfolger,\nSir Dudley North und John Locke, von Späteren gar nicht zu reden,\n ihn nur verflachen konnten. „If the wealth of a nation“, sagt er u.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"3.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0026","text":"A., „could\n be decupled by a Proclamation, it were strange that such proclamations have not\n long since been made by our Governors.“ (l. c. p. 36.)] .\n Andrerseits ist es nothwendig, dass der Werth im Unterschied von den\n bunten Körpern der Waaren sich zu dieser begriffslos sachlichen, aber\n auch einfach gesellschaftlichen Form fortentwickle.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"3.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0027","text":"Der Preis ist der Geldname der in der Waare vergegenständlich-\n ten Arbeitszeit. Die Aequivalenz der Waare und des Geldquantums,\n dem ihr Preis sie gleichsetzt, ist daher eine Tautologie [Note: „Ou bien, il faut consentir à dire qu’une valeur d’un million en\n argent vaut plus qu’une valeur égale en marchandises.“ (Le Trosne\n l. c. p. 922), also „qu’une valeur vaut plus qu’une valeur égale.“] , wie ja\n überhaupt der relative Werthausdruck einer Waare stets der\n Ausdruck der Aequivalenz zweier Waaren ist. Wenn aber der\n Preis als Exponent der Werthgrösse der Waare Exponent ihres Austausch-\n verhältnisses mit Geld ist, so folgt nicht umgekehrt, dass der Exponent\n ihres Austauschverhältnisses mit Geld nothwendig der Expo-\n nent ihrer Werthgrösse ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"3.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0028","text":"Gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"61\", \"facs\": \"#f0080\"} --> von gleicher Grösse stelle sich in 1 Quarter Weizen und in 2 Pfd. St.\n (ungefähr ½ Unze Gold) dar. Die 2 Pfd. St. sind Geldausdrücke der\n Werthgrösse des Quarter Weizen, oder sein Preis. Erlauben nun die\n Umstände, ihn zu 3 Pfd. St., oder zwingen sie ihn zu 1 Pfd. St. zu notiren,\n so sind 1 Pfd. St. und 3 Pfd. St. als Ausdrücke der Werthgrösse des\n Weizens zu klein oder zu gross, aber sie sind dennoch Preise desselben,\n denn erstens sind sie seine Werthform, Geld, und zweitens Exponente\n seines Austauschverhältnisses mit Geld. Bei gleichbleibenden Produktions-\n bedingungen oder gleichbleibender Produktivkraft der Arbeit muss nach\n wie vor zur Reproduktion des Quarter Weizen gleich viel gesell-\n schaftliche Arbeitszeit verausgabt werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"3.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0029","text":"Dieser Umstand\n hängt weder vom Willen des Weizenproduzenten noch der andern Waa-\n renbesitzer ab. Die Werthgrösse der Waare drückt also ein nothwen-\n diges, ihrem Bildungsprozess immanentes Verhältniss zur gesell-\n schaftlichen Arbeitszeit aus. Mit der Verwandlung der Werthgrösse in\n Preis erscheint diess nothwendige Verhältniss als Austauschverhältniss der\n Waare mit einer andern ausser ihr existirenden Waare. Diese Form\n kann aber ebensowohl die Werthgrösse der Waare als das zufällige Ver-\n hältniss ausdrücken, worin sie unter gegebnen Umständen veräusserlich\n ist. Die Möglichkeit quantitativer Incongruenz zwischen\n Preis und Werthgrösse, oder der Abweichung des Preises von der Werth-\n grösse, ist also in der Preisform selbst gegeben.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"3.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0030","text":"Es ist diess kein\n Mangel dieser Form, sondern macht sie umgekehrt zur adäquaten Form\n einer Produktionsweise, worin sich die Regel nur als blindwirkendes\n Durchschnittsgesetz der Regellosigkeit durchsetzen kann.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"3.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0031","text":"Die Preisform lässt jedoch nicht nur die Möglichkeit quanti-\n tativer Incongruenz zwischen Werthgrösse und Preis, d. h. zwischen\n der Werthgrösse und ihrem eignen Geldausdruck zu, sondern kann einen\nqualitativen Widerspruch beherbergen, so dass der Preis überhaupt\n aufhört, Werthausdruck zu sein, obgleich Geld nur die Werthform\n der Waaren ist. Dinge, die an und für sich keine Waaren sind, z. B. Ge-\n wissen, Ehre u. s. w., können ihren Besitzern gegen Geld veräusserlich\n sein und so durch ihren Preis die Waarenform erhalten. Ein Ding\n kann daher formell einen Preis haben, ohne einen Werth zu haben.\n Der Preisausdruck wird hier imaginär, wie gewisse Grössen der Ma-\n thematik oder das „unendliche Urtheil“ der Logik. Wo wir jedoch für\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"62\", \"facs\": \"#f0081\"} --> wesentliche Produktionsverhältnisse derartige imaginäre Preisform\n finden, wie z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"3.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0032","text":"Preis des Grund und Bodens, obgleich der Boden,\n weil keine menschliche Arbeit in ihm vergegenständlicht ist, auch keinen\n Werth hat, wird die tiefere Analyse unter der imaginären Form stets ein\n wirkliches Werthverhältniss oder von ihm abgeleitete Beziehung verbor-\n gen finden.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"3.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0033","text":"Doch kehren wir zurück zum normalen Waarenpreis, der uns hier\n allein noch bekannt ist. Der Preis ist die nurideelle Werthgestalt\n der Waare. Er drückt also zugleich aus, dass sie noch nicht reelle\n Werthgestalt besitzt oder dass ihre Naturalform nicht ihre allgemeine\n Aequivalentform ist. Die ideelle Werthgestalt der Waare ist ferner\nPreis, d. h. nur vorgestellte oder ideelle Goldgestalt.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"3.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0034","text":"Er\n drückt also aus, dass, um die Wirkung eines Tauschwerths oder allgemei-\n nen Aequivalents auf andre Waaren auszuüben, sie ihren natürlichen Leib\n abstreifen, sich aus nur vorgestelltem Gold in wirkliches Gold verwandeln\n muss, obgleich diese Transsubstantiation ihr „saurer“ ankommen mag als\n dem hegel’schen „Begriff“ der Uebergang aus der Nothwendigkeit in die\n Freiheit oder einem Hummer das Sprengen seiner Schale, oder dem Kir-\n chenvater Hieronymus das Abstreifen des alten Adam [Note: Wenn Hieronymus in seiner Jugend viel mit dem materiellen Fleisch zu\n ringen hatte, wie sein Wüstenkampf mit schönen Frauenbildern zeigt, so im Alter\n mit dem geistigen Fleisch. „Ich glaubte mich“, sagt er z. B., „im Geist vor dem\n Weltrichter. „Wer bist du?“ fragte eine Stimme. „Ich bin ein Christ.“ „Du\n lügst“, donnerte der Weltrichter. „Du bist nur ein Ciceronianer!“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"3.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0035","text":"Neben ihrer\nreellen Gestalt, Eisen z. B., kann die Waare im Preis ideelle Werth-\n gestalt oder vorgestellte Goldgestalt besitzen, aber sie kann nicht zu-\n gleich wirklich Eisen und wirklich Gold sein. Für ihre Preisgebung ge-\n nügt es, vorgestelltes Gold ihr gleichzusetzen. Durch Gold ist sie\n zu ersetzen, damit sie ihrem Besitzer den Dienst eines allgemeinen\n Aequivalents leiste. Träte der Besitzer des Eisens z. B. dem Besitzer\n einer weltlustigen Waare gegenüber, und verwiese ihn auf den Eisen-\npreis, der Geldform sei, so würde der Weltlustige antworten, wie im\n Himmel der heilige Petrus dem Dante, der ihm die Glaubensformeln\n hergesagt:\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"63\", \"facs\": \"#f0082\"} -->„Assai bene è trascorsa\n D’esta moneta già la lega e’l peso,\n Ma dimmi se tu l’hai nella tua borsa.“","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"3.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0036","text":"Die Preisform schliesst die Veräusserlichkeit der Waaren gegen\n Geld und die Nothwendigkeit dieser Veräusserung ein. Die Preisbestim-\n mung der Waaren hat andrerseits eine im Austauschprozess befindliche\n Waare, das Gold, bereits zu Geld gemacht. Im ideellen Mass der\n Werthe lauert daher das harte Geld.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"3.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0037","text":"## B. Circulationsmittel.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"3.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0038","text":"## a) Die Metamorphose der Waaren.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"3.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0039","text":"Man sah, dass der Austauschprozess der Waaren widersprechende und\n einander ausschliessende Beziehungen einschloss. Die Entwicklung der\n Waare, die wir eben betrachtet, hebt diese Widersprüche nicht auf, aber\n sie schafft die Form, worin sie sich bewegen können. Diess ist über-\n haupt die Methode, wodurch sich wirkliche Widersprüche lösen. Es ist\n z. B. ein Widerspruch, dass ein Körper beständig in einen andern fällt und\n eben so beständig von ihm weg flieht. Die Ellipse ist eine der Bewe-\n gungsformen, worin dieser Widerspruch sich eben so sehr verwirklicht\n als löst.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"3.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0040","text":"Soweit der Austauschprozess der Waaren sie aus der Hand, worin sie\nNicht-Gebrauchswerthe, in die Hand überträgt, worin sie Ge-\n brauchswerthe, ist er gesellschaftlicher Stoffwechsel.\n Das Produkt einer nützlichen Arbeitsweise ersetzt das der andern. Ein-\n mal angelangt zur Stelle, wo sie als Gebrauchswerth gilt, dient die Waare\n als Gebrauchsgegenstand oder fällt in die Sphäre der Consumtion aus der\n Sphäre des Waarenaustauschs. Letztre allein interessirt uns hier. Wir\n haben also den ganzen Prozess nach der Formseite zu betrachten, also nur\n den Formwechsel oder die Metamorphose der Waaren, welche\n den gesellschaftlichen Stoffwechsel vermittelt.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"3.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0041","text":"Die durchaus mangelhafte Auffassung dieses Formwechsels, der Funk-\n tionen des Geldes, der daraus entspringenden verschiednen Formbestimmt-\n heiten, die das Geld aus seinen verschiednen Funktionen schöpft, ist, abge-\n sehn von Unklarheit über den Werthbegriff selbst, dem Umstand geschul-\n det, dass jeder Formwechsel einer Waare sich im Austausch zweier\n Waaren, der Waare und der Geldwaare, darstellt. Hält man an diesem\nstofflichen Moment, dem Austausch von Waare und Gold, allein fest,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"64\", \"facs\": \"#f0083\"} --> so übersieht man grade, was man sehn soll, nämlich was sich mit der\nForm zuträgt.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"3.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0042","text":"Man übersieht, dass die Bestimmung des Goldes als Geld\n bereits eine Formbestimmung ist, die ihm nicht als blosser Waare gehört,\n dass die andern Waaren sich in ihren Preisen selbst auf das Gold als ihre\neigne Geldgestalt beziehn, und dass es seinerseits nur die allgemeine\n unmittelbare Aequivalentform erhält, weil die Waaren überhaupt sich eine\n allgemeine relative Werthform geben müssen.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"3.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0043","text":"Die Waaren gehn zunächst unvergoldet, unverzuckert, wie der Kamm\n ihnen gewachsen ist, den Austauschprozess ein. Er produzirt die Ver-\n dopplung der Waare in Waare und Geld, ein äusserer Gegen-\n satz, worin sie ihren immanenten Gegensatz von Gebrauchswerth und\n Tauschwerth darstellen. In diesem Gegensatz treten die Waaren als\n Gebrauchswerthe dem Geld als Tauschwerth gegenüber. An-\n drerseits sind beide Seiten des Gegensatzes Waaren, also Einheiten\n von Gebrauchswerth und Werth. Aber diese Einheit von Unter-\n schieden stellt sich auf jedem der beiden Pole umgekehrt dar und stellt\n dadurch zugleich deren Wechselbeziehung dar. Die Waare ist reell\n Gebrauchswerth, ihr Werthdasein erscheint nur ideell im Preis, der\n sie auf das gegenüberstehende Gold als ihre reelle Werthgestalt\n bezieht. Umgekehrt gilt das Goldmaterial nur als Werthmateriatur\n Geld.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"3.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0044","text":"Es ist reell daher all gemeines Aequivalent, Tausch-\n werth. Sein Gebrauchswerth erscheint nur noch ideell in der\n Reihe der relativen Werthausdrücke, worin es sich auf die gegenüber-\n stehenden Waaren als den Umkreis seiner reellen Gebrauchs-\n gestalten bezieht. Diese gegensätzlichen Formen der Waaren sind\n die wirklichen Bewegungsformen ihres Austauschpro-\n zesses.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"3.44"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0045","text":"Begleiten wir nun irgend einen Waarenbesitzer, unsern altbekannten\n Leinweber z. B., zur Scene des Austauschprozesses, dem Waarenmarkt.\n Seine Waare, 20 Ellen Leinwand, ist preisbestimmt. Ihr Preis ist\n 2 Pfd. St. Er tauscht sie aus gegen 2 Pfd. St., und, Mann von altem\n Schrot und Korn, tauscht die 2 Pfd. St. wieder aus gegen eine Familien-\n bibel vom selben Preis. Die Leinwand, für ihn nur Waare, Werthträger,\n wird entäussert gegen Gold, ihre Werthgestalt, und aus dieser Gestalt rück-\n veräussert gegen eine andere Waare, die Bibel, die aber als Gebrauchs-\n gegenstand in’s Weberhaus wandern und dort Erbauungsbedürfnisse befrie-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"65\", \"facs\": \"#f0084\"} --> digen soll.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"3.45"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0046","text":"Der Austauschprozess der Waare vollzieht sich also in zwei\n entgegengesetzten und einander ergänzenden Metamor-\n phosen — Verwandlung der Waare in Geld und ihre Rück-\n verwandlung aus Geld in Waare [Note: „Ἐϰ δὲ τοῦ … πυϱὸς ἀνταμείβεσϑαι πάντα, φησὶν ὁ Ἡϱάϰλειτος,\n ϰαὶ πῦϱ ἁπάντων, ὥςπεϱ χϱυσοῦ χϱήματα ϰαὶ χϱημάτων χϱυσός.“\n (F. Lassalle: „Die Philosophie Herakleitos des Dunkeln. Ber-\n lin 1858“, Bd. I, p. 222.) Lassalle’s Note zu dieser Stelle, p. 224, n. 3, er-\n klärt das Geld unrichtig für blosses Werthzeichen.] . Die Momente der Waaren-\n metamorphose sind zugleich Händel des Waarenbesitzers — Verkauf,\n Austausch der Waare mit Geld, Kauf, Austausch des Gelds mit Waare,\n und Einheit beider Akte: Verkaufen um zu kaufen.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"3.46"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0047","text":"Besieht sich der Leinweber nun das Endresultat des Handels, so be-\n sitzt er Bibel statt Leinwand, statt seiner ursprünglichen Waare eine\n andre vom selben Werth, aber verschiedner Nützlichkeit. In gleicher\n Weise eignet er sich seine verschiednen Lebens- und Produktionsmittel\n an. Von seinem Standpunkt vermittelt der ganze Prozess, die\n Geldwerdung der Leinwand und die Waarenwerdung des Geldes, Verkauf\n und Kauf, nur den Austausch seines Arbeitsprodukts mit fremdem Arbeits-\n produkt, den Produktenaustausch.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"3.47"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0048","text":"Der Austauschprozess der Waare vollzieht sich also in folgendem\n Formwechsel:\nWaare — Geld — Waare.\n W — G — W.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"3.48"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0049","text":"Nach ihrem stofflichen Inhalt ist die Bewegung W — W, Austausch\n von Waare gegen Waare, Stoffwechsel der gesellschaftlichen Arbeit, in\n dessen Resultat der Prozess selbst erlischt.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"3.49"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0050","text":"W — G. Erste Metamorphose der Waare oder Verkauf:\n Das Ueberspringen des Waarenwerths aus dem Waarenleib in den Gold-\n leib ist, wie ich es anderswo bezeichnet, der salto mortale der Waare.\n Misslingt er, so ist zwar nicht die Waare geprellt, wohl aber der Waaren-\n besitzer. Die gesellschaftliche Theilung der Arbeit macht seine Arbeit\n ebenso einseitig als seine Bedürfnisse vielseitig. Eben desswegen dient\n ihm sein Produkt nur als Tauschwerth, allgemeines Aequiva-\n lent. Allgemeine, gesellschaftlich gültige Aequivalentform erhält es\n [Running matter: I. 5] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"66\", \"facs\": \"#f0085\"} --> aber nur im Geld und das Geld befindet sich in fremder Tasche.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"3.50"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0051","text":"Um es\n herauszuziehn, muss die Waare vor allem Gebrauchswerth für den\n Geldbesitzer sein, die auf sie verausgabte Arbeit also in gesellschaftlich\n nützlicher Form verausgabt sein oder sich als Glied der gesellschaft-\n lichen Theilung der Arbeit bewähren. Aber die Theilung der\n Arbeit ist ein naturwüchsiger Produktionsorganismus, dessen\n Fäden hinter dem Rücken der Waarenproducenten gewebt wurden und\n sich fortweben. Vielleicht ist die Waare Produkt einer neuen Arbeitsweise,\n die ein neu aufgekommenes Bedürfniss zu befriedigen vorgiebt oder auf\n eigne Faust ein Bedürfniss erst hervorrufen will. Gestern noch eine Funk-\n tion unter den vielen Funktionen eines und desselben Waarenproducenten,\n reisst sich eine besondre Arbeitsverrichtung heute vielleicht los von diesem\n Zusammenhang, verselbstständigt sich und schickt eben desswegen ihr\nTheilprodukt als selbstständige Waare zu Markt.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"3.51"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0052","text":"Die Um-\n stände mögen reif oder unreif sein für diesen Scheidungsprozess. Das\n Produkt befriedigt heute ein gesellschaftliches Bedürfniss. Morgen wird\n es vielleicht ganz oder theilweise von einer ähnlichen Produktenart aus\n seinem Platze verdrängt. Ist auch die Arbeit, wie die unsres Leinwebers,\n patentirtes Glied der gesellschaftlichen Arbeitstheilung, so ist damit noch\n keineswegs der Gebrauchswerth grade seiner 20 Ellen Leinwand garan-\n tirt. Wenn das gesellschaftliche Bedürfniss für Leinwand, und es hat sein\n Mass, wie alles andre, bereits durch nebenbuhlerische Leinweber gesättigt\n ist, wird das Produkt unsres Freundes überschüssig, überflüssig und damit\n nutzlos. Einem geschenkten Gaul sieht man nicht ins Maul, aber er be-\n schreitet nicht den Markt, um Präsente zu machen.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"3.52"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0053","text":"Gesetzt aber der Ge-\n brauchswerth seines Produkts bewähre sich und Geld werde daher ange-\n zogen von der Waare. Aber nun fragt sich’s, wie viel Geld? Die Ant-\n wort ist allerdings schon anticipirt im Preis der Waare, dem Exponenten\n ihrer Werthgrösse. Wir sehn ab von etwaigen rein subjektiven Rechen-\n fehlern des Waarenbesitzers, die auf dem Markt sofort objectiv corrigirt\n werden. Er soll auf sein Produkt nur den gesellschaftlich nothwendigen\n Durchschnitt von Arbeitszeit verausgabt haben. Der Preis der Waare ist\n also nur Geldname des in ihr vergegenständlichten Quantums gesellschaft-\n licher Arbeit. Aber ohne Erlaubniss und hinter dem Rücken unsres Lein-\n webers geriethen die altverbürgten Produktionsbedingungen der Leinweberei\n in Gährung.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"3.53"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0054","text":"Was gestern zweifelsohne gesellschaftlich nothwendige Ar-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"67\", \"facs\": \"#f0086\"} --> beitszeit zur Produktion einer Elle Leinwand war, hört heute auf es zu\n sein, wie der Geldbesitzer eifrigst demonstrirt aus den Preisquotationen\n verschiedner Nebenbuhler unsres Freundes. Zu seinem Unglück giebt’s\n viele Weber auf der Welt. Gesetzt endlich jedes auf dem Markt vor-\n handne Stück Leinwand enthalte nur gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit. Trotzdem kann die Gesammtsumme dieser Stücke überflüssig ver-\n ausgabte Arbeitszeit enthalten. Vermag der Marktmagen das Gesammt-\n quantum Leinwand, zum Normalpreis von 2 Sh. per Elle, nicht zu ab-\n sorbiren, so beweist das, dass ein zu grosser Theil der gesellschaftlichen\n Gesammtarbeitszeit in der Form der Leinweberei verausgabt wurde.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"3.54"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0055","text":"Die\n Wirkung ist dieselbe als hätte jeder einzelne Leinweber mehr als die ge-\n sellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit auf sein individuelles Produkt ver-\n wandt. Hier heisst’s: Mitgefangen, mitgehangen. Alle Leinwand auf\n dem Markt gilt nur als ein Handelsartikel, jedes Stück nur als aliquoter\n Theil. Und in der That ist der Werth jeder individuellen Elle ja auch\n nur die Materiatur desselben gesellschaftlich bestimmten Quantums gleich-\n artiger menschlicher Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"3.55"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0056","text":"Man sieht, die Waare liebt das Geld, aber „the course of true love runs\n never smooth.“ Ebenso naturwüchsig zufällig, wie die qualitative, ist\n die quantitative Gliederung des gesellschaftlichen Produktionsorganis-\n mus, der seine membra disjecta im System der Theilung der Arbeit dar-\n stellt. Unsere Waarenbesitzer entdecken daher, dass dieselbe Theilung\n der Arbeit, die sie zu unabhängigen Privatproducenten, den\n gesellschaftlichen Produktionsprozess und ihre Verhältnisse in diesem Pro-\n zess von ihnen selbst unabhängig macht, dass die Unabhängig-\n keit der Personen von einander sich in einem System allseitiger sachlicher\n Abhängigkeit ergänzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"3.56"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0057","text":"Die Theilung der Arbeit verwandelt das Arbeitsprodukt in Waare\n und macht dadurch seine Verwandlung in Geld nothwendig. Sie macht\n es zugleich zufällig, ob diese Transsubstantiation gelingt. Hier ist jedoch\n das Phänomen rein zu betrachten, sein normaler Vorgang also vorauszu-\n setzen. Wenn es übrigens überhaupt vorgeht, die Waare also nicht un-\n verkäuflich ist, findet stets ihr Formwechsel statt, obgleich abnormal\n in diesem Formwechsel Substanz — Werthgrösse — eingebüsst oder zuge-\n setzt werden mag.","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"3.57"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0058","text":"Dem einen Waarenbesitzer ersetzt Gold seine Waare und dem an-\n [Running matter: 5*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"68\", \"facs\": \"#f0087\"} --> dern Waare sein Gold. Das sinnfällige Phänomen ist der Hände-\n oder Stellenwechsel von Waare und Gold, von 20 Ellen Leinwand\n und 2 Pfd. St., d. h. ihr Austausch. Aber womit tauscht sich die\n Waare aus? Mit ihrer eignen allgemeinen Werthgestalt. Und womit das\n Gold? Mit einer besondern Gestalt seines Gebrauchswerths. Warum\n tritt Gold der Leinwand als Geld gegenüber? Weil ihr Preis von\n 2 Pfd. St. oder ihr Geldname sie bereits auf Gold als Geld bezieht.\n Die Entäusserung der ursprünglichen Waarenform vollzieht sich durch\n die Veräusserung der Waare, d. h. in dem Augenblicke, wo ihr Ge-\n brauchswerth das in ihrem Preis nur vorgestellte Gold wirklich anzieht.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"3.58"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0059","text":"Die Realisirung des Preises oder der nur ideellen Werthform der\n Waare ist daher zugleich umgekehrt Realisirung des nur ideellen Ge-\n brauchswerths des Geldes, die Verwandlung von Waare in Geld zugleich\n Verwandlung von Geld in Waare. Der eine Prozess ist zweiseitiger\n Prozess, vom Pol des Waarenbesitzers Verkauf, vom Gegenpol des\n Geldbesitzers Kauf. Oder Verkauf ist Kauf, W — G zugleich\n G — W [Note: „Toute vente est achat“ (Dr. Quesnay: „Dialogues sur le Com-\n merce et les Travaux des Artisans“. Physiocrates, ed. Daire,\n I. Partie, Paris 1846, p. 170), oder, wie Quesnay in seinen „Maximes\n Générales“ sagt: „Vendre est acheter.“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"3.59"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0060","text":"Wir kennen bisher kein andres ökonomisches Verhältniss der Men-\n schen zu einander ausser dem von Waarenbesitzern, ein Verhältniss, worin\n sie fremdes Arbeitsprodukt nur aneignen, indem sie eignes entfremden.\n Einem Waarenbesitzer kann der andre daher nur als Geldbesitzer gegenüber-\n treten, entweder weil sein Arbeitsprodukt von Natur die Geldform besitzt,\n also Geldmaterial ist, Gold u. s. w., oder weil seine eigne Waare sich bereits\n gehäutet und ihre ursprüngliche Gebrauchsform abgestreift hat. Um als Geld\n zu funktioniren, muss das Gold natürlich an irgend einem Punkt in den Waaren-\n markt eintreten. Dieser Punkt liegt an seiner Produktionsquelle, wo es sich\n als unmittelbares Arbeitsprodukt mit anderm Arbeitsprodukt von demselben\n Werth austauscht.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"3.60"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0061","text":"Aber von diesem Augenblick funktionirt es nur als\n wirkliches Geld, weil es beständig realisirter Waarenpreis ist [Note: „Le prix d’une marchandise ne pouvant pas être payé que par le prix\n d’une autre marchandise.“ (Mercier de la Rivière: „L’Ordre na-] .\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"69\", \"facs\": \"#f0088\"} --> Abgesehn vom Austausch des Golds mit Waare an seiner Produktions-\n quelle, ist das Gold in der Hand jedes Waarenbesitzers die entäusserte Ge-\n stalt seiner veräusserten Waare, Produkt des Verkaufs oder der ersten\n Waarenmetamorphose W — G [Note: „Pour avoir cet argent, il faut avoir vendu.“ (l. c. p. 543.)] . Ideelles Geld oder Werth-\n mass wurde das Gold, weil alle Waaren ihre Werthe in ihm massen und es so\nvorstellungsweise zu ihrer entäusserten Gebrauchsgestalt oder Werth-\n gestalt machten.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"3.61"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0062","text":"Reelles Geld wird es, weil die Waaren durch ihre\n allseitige Veräusserung es zu ihrer wirklich entäusserten oder verwandelten\n Gebrauchsgestalt und daher zu ihrer wirklichen Werthgestalt machen.\n In ihrer Werthgestalt streift die Waare jede Spur ihres naturwüchsigen\n Gebrauchswerths und der besondern nützlichen Arbeit ab, welcher sie den\n Ursprung verdankt, um sich in die gleichförmige gesellschaftliche Materiatur\n unterschiedsloser menschlicher Arbeit zu verpuppen. Man sieht dem Geld\n daher nicht an, welchen Schlags die in es verwandelte Waare. Eine sieht\n in ihrer Geldform grade aus wie die andre. Geld mag daher Dreck sein,\n obgleich Dreck nicht Geld ist. Wir wollen annehmen, dass die zwei\n Goldfüchse, wogegen unser Leineweber seine Waare veräussert, die ver-\n wandelte Gestalt eines Quarters Weizen sind. Der Verkauf der Leinwand,\n W — G, ist zugleich ihr Kauf, G — W.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"3.62"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0063","text":"Aber als Verkauf der\n Leinwand beginnt dieser Prozess eine Bewegung, die mit seinem Gegen-\n theil endet, mit dem Kauf der Bibel; als Kauf der Leinwand\n endet er eine Bewegung, die mit seinem Gegentheil begann, mit dem\nVerkauf des Weizens. W — G (Leinwand — Geld), die erste\n Phase von W — G — W (Leinwand — Geld — Bibel), ist zugleich\n G — W (Geld — Leinwand), die letzte Phase einer andern Bewegung\n W — G — W (Weizen — Geld — Leinwand). Die erste Meta-\n morphose einer Waare, ihre Verwandlung aus der Waarenform in\n Geld, ist stets zugleich zweite entgegengesetzte Metamorphose\n einer andern Waare, ihre Rückverwandlung aus der Geldform in\n Waare [Note: Ausnahme, wie vorher bemerkt, bildet der Gold - resp. Silberproducent,\n der sein Produkt austauscht, ohne es vorher verkauft zu haben.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"3.63"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0064","text":"[Note: turel et essentiel des sociétés politiques“. Physiocrates, ed.\n Daire, II. Partie, p. 554.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"3.64"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0065","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"70\", \"facs\": \"#f0089\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"3.65"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0066","text":"G — W. Zweite oder Schlussmetamorphose der\n Waare. Kauf. — Weil die entäusserte Gestalt aller andern Waaren\n oder das Produkt ihrer allgemeinen Veräusserung, ist Geld die absolut\n veräusserliche Waare. Es liest alle Preise rückwärts und spiegelt\n sich so in allen Waarenleibern als dem hingebenden Material seiner eignen\n Waarenwerdung. Zugleich zeigen die Preise, die Liebesaugen, womit\n ihm die Waaren winken, die Schranke seiner Verwandlungsfähig-\n keit, nämlich seine eigne Quantität. Da die Waare in ihrer Geld-\n werdung verschwindet, sieht man dem Geld nicht an, wie es in die Hände\n seines Besitzers gelangt oder was in es verwandelt ist. Non olet, wessen\n Ursprungs auch immer.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"3.66"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0067","text":"Als entäusserte Gestalt steht es den\n Waaren, ihm als der absolut veräusserlichen Waarengestalt\n steht die Waarenwelt gegenüber [Note: „Si l’argent représente, dans nos mains, les choses que nous pouvons\n désirer d’acheter, il y représente aussi les choses que nous avous vendues\n pour cet argent.“ (Mercier de la Rivière l. c. p. 586.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"3.67"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0068","text":"G — W, der Kauf ist zugleich Verkauf, W — G, die letzte Meta-\n morphose einer Waare daher zugleich die erste Metamorphose einer andern\n Waare. Für unsern Leinweber schliesst der Lebenslauf seiner Waare\n mit der Bibel, worin er die 2 Pfd. St. rückverwandelt hat. Aber der ur-\n sprüngliche Bibelbesitzer setzt die vom Leineweber gelösten 2 Pfd. St. in\n Kornbranntwein um, G — W, die Schlussphase von W — G — W (Lein-\n wand — Geld — Bibel) ist zugleich W — G, die erste Phase von W —\n G — W (Bibel — Geld — Kornbranntwein). Da der Waarenproduzent\n nur ein einseitiges Produkt liefert, verkauft er es oft in grösseren Mas-\n sen, während seine vielseitigen Bedürfnisse ihn zwingen, den realisirten\n Preis oder die gelöste Geldsumme beständig in zahlreiche Käufe zu zer-\n splittern. Ein Verkauf mündet daher in viele Käufe verschiedner\n Waaren.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"3.68"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0069","text":"Die Schlussmetamorphose einer Waare bildet so eine Summe\n von ersten Metamorphosen andrer Waaren.","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"3.69"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0070","text":"Betrachten wir nun die Gesammtmetamorphose einer Waare,\n z. B. der Leinwand, so sehn wir zunächst, dass sie aus zwei entgegenge-\n setzten und einander ergänzenden Bewegungen besteht, W — G und G —\n W. Die zwei entgegengesetzten Wandlungen vollziehn sich in zwei ent-\n gegengesetzten gesellschaftlichen Prozessen des Waarenbesitzers und reflek-\n tiren sich in zwei entgegengesetzten ökonomischen Charakteren dessel-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"71\", \"facs\": \"#f0090\"} --> ben. Als Agent des Verkaufs wird er Verkäufer, als Agent des Kaufs\nKäufer. Wie aber in jeder Wandlung der Waare ihre beiden Formen,\n Waarenform und Geldform, gleichzeitig existiren, nur auf entgegengesetz-\n ten Polen, so steht demselben Waarenbesitzer als Verkäufer ein andrer\n Käufer und als Käufer ein andrer Verkäufer gegenüber.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"3.70"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0071","text":"Wie dieselbe\n Waare die zwei umgekehrten Wandlungen successiv durchläuft, aus Waare\n Geld und aus Geld Waare wird, so wechselt derselbe Waarenbesitzer die\n Rollen von Verkäufer und Käufer. Es sind diess also keine festen, son-\n dern im Tauschprozess der Waaren beständig die Person wechselnden\n Charaktere.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"3.71"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0072","text":"Die Gesammtmetamorphose einer Waare unterstellt, in ihrer einfach-\n sten Form, vier Extreme und drei Personae dramatis. Erst\n tritt der Waare das Geld als ihre entäusserte Gestalt gegenüber, die\n jenseits, in fremder Tasche, sachlich harte Realität besitzt. So tritt dem\n Waarenbesitzer ein Geldbesitzer gegenüber. Sobald die Waare nun in\n Geld verwandelt, wird letztres zu ihrer verschwindenden Aequi-\n valentform, deren Gebrauchswerth oder Inhalt diesseits in andern\n Waarenkörpern existirt. Als Endpunkt der ersten Waarenwandlung ist\n das Geld zugleich Ausgangspunkt der zweiten. So der Verkäufer im\n ersten Act Käufer im zweiten, wo ihm ein dritter Waarenbesitzer als Ver-\n käufer gegenübertritt [Note: „Il y a donc quatre termes et trois contractants, dont l’un intervient deux\n fois.“ (Le Trosne l. c. p. 908.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"3.72"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0073","text":"Die beiden umgekehrten Bewegungsphasen der Waarenmetamorphose\n bilden einen Kreislauf: Waarenform, Abstreifung der Waarenform,\n Rückkehr zur Waarenform. Allerdings ist die Waare selbst hier\n gegensätzlich bestimmt, am Ausgangspunkt Nicht-Gebrauchswerth, am\n Endpunkt Gebrauchswerth für ihren Besitzer, wie das Geld die Waare erst\n als festen Werthkrystall darstellt, um hinterher als ihre blosse Aequiva-\n lentform zu zerrinnen.","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"3.73"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0074","text":"Die zwei Metamorphosen, die den Kreislauf einer Waare, bilden\n zugleich die umgekehrten Theilmetamorphosen zweier andren Waa-\n ren. Dieselbe Waare (Leinwand) eröffnet die Reihe ihrer eignen Meta-\n morphosen und schliesst die Gesammtmetamorphose einer andern Waare\n (des Weizens). Während ihrer ersten Wandlung, dem Verkauf, spielt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"72\", \"facs\": \"#f0091\"} --> sie diese zwei Rollen in eigner Person. Als Goldchrysalide dagegen,\n worin sie selbst den Weg alles Fleisches wandert, endet sie zugleich die\n erste Metamorphose einer dritten Waare. Der Kreislauf, den die Meta-\n morphosenreihe jeder Waare bildet, verschlingt sich also unentwirrbar\n mit den Kreisläufen andrer Waaren. Der Gesammtprozess stellt sich dar\n als Waarencirculation.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"3.74"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0075","text":"Die Waarencirculation ist nicht nur formell, sondern wesentlich vom\n unmittelbaren Produktenaustausch unterschieden. Man werfe nur einen\n Rückblick auf den Vorgang. Der Leineweber hat unbedingt Leinwand\n mit Bibel vertauscht, eigne Waare mit fremder. Aber diess Phänomen\n ist nur wahr für ihn. Der Bibelagent, der dem Kühlen Heisses vorzieht,\n dachte nicht daran, Leinwand für Bibel einzutauschen, wie der Leineweber\n nicht davon weiss, dass Weizen gegen seine Leinwand eingetauscht wor-\n den ist u. s. w. Die Waare des B ersetzt die Waare des A, aber A und\n B tauschen nicht wechselseitig ihre Waaren aus. Es kann in der\n That vorkommen, dass A und B wechselweis von einander kaufen, aber\n solche besondre Beziehung ist keineswegs durch die allgemeinen Verhält-\n nisse der Waarencirculation bedingt.","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"3.75"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0076","text":"Einerseits sieht man hier, wie der\n Waarenaustausch die individuellen und lokalen Schranken\n des unmittelbaren Produktenaustauschs durchbricht und den Stoffwechsel\n der menschlichen Arbeit entwickelt. Andrerseits entwickelt sich ein gan-\n zer Kreis von den handelnden Personen uncontrolirbarer, gesellschaftlicher\n Naturzusammenhänge. Der Weber kann nur Leinwand verkaufen, weil\n der Bauer Weizen, Heisssporn nur die Bibel, weil der Weber Leinwand,\n der Destillateur nur gebranntes Wasser, weil der andre das Wasser des\n ewigen Lebens bereits verkauft hat u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"3.76"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0077","text":"Der Circulationsprozess erlischt desswegen auch nicht, wie der un-\n mittelbare Produktenaustausch, in dem Stellen - oder Händewechsel der\n Gebrauchswerthe. Das Geld verschwindet nicht, weil es schliesslich aus\n der Metamorphosenreihe einer Waare herausfällt. Es schlägt immer\n nieder auf eine durch die Waaren geräumte Circulationsstelle. Z. B. in\n der Gesammtmetamorphose der Leinwand: Leinwand — Geld —\n Bibel fällt erst die Leinwand aus der Circulation, Geld tritt an ihre\n Stelle, fällt dann die Bibel aus der Circulation, Geld tritt an ihre Stelle.\n Der Ersatz von Waare durch Waare lässt zugleich an dritter Hand die\n Geldwaare hängen. Die Circulation schwitzt beständig Geld aus.","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"3.77"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0078","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"73\", \"facs\": \"#f0092\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"3.78"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0079","text":"Nichts kann alberner sein als das Dogma, die Waarencirculation be-\n dinge ein nothwendiges Gleichgewicht der Verkäufe und Käufe, weil jeder\n Verkauf Kauf und vice versa. Meint diess, dass die Zahl der wirklich\n vollzognen Verkäufe gleich derselben Zahl von Käufen, so ist es platte\n Tautologie. Aber es soll beweisen, dass der Verkäufer seinen eignen\n Käufer zu Markt führt. Verkauf und Kauf sind ein identischer Act\n als Wechselbeziehung zweier polarisch entgegengesetzter\n Personen, des Waarenbesitzers und des Geldbesitzers. Sie bilden\nzweipolarisch entgegengesetzte Akte als Handlungen dersel-\n ben Person. Die Identität von Verkauf und Kauf schliesst daher ein,\n dass die Waare nutzlos wird, wenn sie, in die alchymistische Retorte\n der Circulation geworfen, nicht als Geld herauskommt, nicht vom Waa-\n renbesitzer verkauft, also vom Geldbesitzer gekauft wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"3.79"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0080","text":"Jene Identität\n enthält ferner, dass der Prozess, wenn er gelingt, einen Ruhepunkt, einen\n Lebensabschnitt der Waare bildet, der länger oder kürzer währen kann.\n Da die erste Metamorphose der Waare zugleich Verkauf und Kauf,\n ist dieser Theilprozess zugleich selbstständiger Prozess. Der Käufer hat\n die Waare, der Verkäufer hat das Geld, d. h. eine Waare, die circula-\n tionsfähige Form bewahrt, ob sie früher oder später wieder auf dem\n Markt erscheine. Keiner kann verkaufen, ohne dass ein Andrer kauft.\n Aber keiner braucht unmittelbar zu kaufen, weil er selbst verkauft hat.\n Die Circulation sprengt die zeitlichen, örtlichen und individuellen Schran-\n ken des Produktenaustauschs eben dadurch, dass sie die hier vorhandne\nunmittelbare Identität zwischen dem Austausch des eignen und\n dem Eintausch des fremden Arbeitsprodukts in den Gegensatz von\n Verkauf und Kauf spaltet.","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"3.80"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0081","text":"Dass die selbstständig einander gegenüber-\n tretenden Prozesse eine innere Einheit bilden, heisst eben so sehr,\n dass ihre innere Einheit sich in äusseren Gegensätzen bewegt.\n Geht die äusserliche Verselbstständigung der innerlich Unselbstständigen,\n weil einander ergänzenden, bis zu einem gewissen Punkt fort, so macht\n sich die Einheit gewaltsam geltend durch eine — Krise. Der der Waare\n immanente Gegensatz von Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth, von Privat-\n arbeit, die sich zugleich als unmittelbar gesellschaftliche Arbeit darstellen\n muss, von besondrer konkreter Arbeit, die zugleich nur als abstrakt all-\n gemeine Arbeit gilt, von Personificirung der Sache und Versachlichung der\n Personen — dieser immanente Widerspruch erhält in den Gegensätzen der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"74\", \"facs\": \"#f0093\"} --> Waarenmetamorphose seine entwickelten Bewegungsformen.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"3.81"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0082","text":"Diese\n Formen schliessen daher die Möglichkeit, aber auch nur die Möglich-\n keit der Krisen ein. Die Entwicklung dieser Möglichkeit zur Wirklich-\n keit erfordert einen ganzen Umkreis von Verhältnissen, die vom Standpunkt\n der einfachen Waarencirculation noch gar nicht existiren [Note: Vergleiche meine Bemerkungen über James Mill: „Zur Kritik\n etc.“ p. 74—76. Zwei Punkte sind hier charakteristisch für die Methode der\n ökonomistischen Apologetik. Erstens die Identificirung von Waarencircula-\n tion und unmittelbarem Produktenaustausch durch einfache Abstraktion\n von ihren Unterschieden. Zweitens der Versuch, die Widersprüche des kapi-\n talistischen Produktionsprozesses wegzuleugnen, indem man die Ver-\n hältnisse seiner Produktionsagenten in die einfachen Beziehungen auflöst, die aus\n der Waarencirculation entspringen.","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"3.82"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0083","text":"Waarenproduktion und Waarencirculation\n sind aber Phänomene, die den verschiedensten Produktionsweisen angehören, wenn\n auch in verschiednem Umfang und Tragweite. Man weiss also noch nichts von\n der differentia specifica dieser Produktionsweisen und kann sie daher nicht beur-\n theilen, wenn man nur die ihnen gemeinschaftlichen, abstrakten Kategorien der\n Waarencirculation kennt. In keiner andern Wissenschaft ausser der politischen\n Oekonomie herrscht ähnliche Wichtigthuerei mit elementarischer Gemeinplätzlich-\n keit. Z. B. J. B. Say nimmt sich heraus, über die Krisen abzuurtheilen,\n weil er weiss, dass die Waare Produkt ist.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"3.83"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0084","text":"Als Vermittler der Waarencirculation erhält das Geld die Funktion\n des Circulationsmittels.","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"3.84"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0085","text":"## b) Der Umlauf des Geldes.","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"3.85"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0086","text":"Der Formwechsel, worin sich der Stoffwechsel der Arbeitsprodukte\n vollzieht, W — G — W, bedingt, dass derselbe Werth als Waare\n den Ausgangspunkt des Prozesses bildet und zu demselben Punkt zurück-\n kehrt als Waare. Diese Bewegung der Waaren ist daher Kreis-\n lauf. Andrerseits schliesst dieselbe Form den Kreislauf des Geldes aus.\n Ihr Resultat ist beständige Entfernung des Geldes von seinem\n Ausgangspunkt, nicht Rückkehr zu demselben. So lange der Verkäufer\n die verwandelte Gestalt seiner Waare festhält, das Geld, befindet sich die\n Waare im Stadium der ersten Metamorphose oder hat nur ihre erste Cir-\n culationshälfte zurückgelegt. Ist der Prozess, Verkaufen um zu kau-\n fen, vervollständigt, so ist auch das Geld wieder aus der Hand ihres\n ursprünglichen Besitzers entfernt.","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"3.86"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0087","text":"Allerdings, wenn der Leinweber, nach-\n dem er die Bibel gekauft, von neuem Leinwand verkauft, kehrt auch das\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"75\", \"facs\": \"#f0094\"} --> Geld in seine Hand zurück. Aber es kehrt nicht zurück durch den Circula-\n tionsprozess der ersten 20 Ellen Leinwand. Er hat es vielmehr aus den\n Händen des Leinwebers in die des Bibelverkäufers entfernt. Es kehrt nur\n zurück durch die Erneuerung oder Wiederholung desselben Circula-\n tionsprozesses für neue Waare, und endet hier wie dort mit demselben Re-\n sultat. Die dem Geld durch die Waarencirculation unmittelbar ertheilte\n Bewegungsform ist daher seine beständige Entfernung vom Ausgangspunkt,\n sein Lauf aus der Hand eines Waarenbesitzers in die eines andern, oder\nsein Umlauf (currency, cours de la monnaie).","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"3.87"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0088","text":"Der Umlauf des Geldes zeigt beständige, eintönige Wiederholung\ndesselben Prozesses. Die Waare steht stets auf Seite des Verkäufers,\n das Geld stets auf Seite des Käufers, als Kaufmittel. Es funktionirt\n als Kaufmittel, indem es den Preis der Waare realisirt. Indem es\n ihn realisirt, überträgt es die Waare aus der Hand des Verkäufers in die\n des Känfers, während es sich gleichzeitig aus der Hand des Käufers in die\n des Verkäufers entfernt, um denselben Prozess mit einer andern Waare zu\n wiederholen. Dass diese einseitige Form der Geldbewegung aus der dop-\n pelseitigen Formbewegung der Waare entspringt, ist verhüllt. Die Natur\n der Waarencirculation selbst erzeugt den entgegengesetzten Schein. Die\n erste Metamorphose der Waare ist nicht nur als Bewegung des Geldes,\n sondern als ihre eigne Bewegung sichtbar, aber ihre zweite Metamorphose\n ist nur als Bewegung des Geldes sichtbar.","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"3.88"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0089","text":"In ihrer ersten Circulations-\n hälfte wechselt die Waare den Platz mit dem Geld. Damit fällt zugleich\n ihre Gebrauchsgestalt aus der Circulation heraus, in die Consumtion [Note: Selbst wenn die Waare wieder und wieder verkauft wird, ein Phänomen,\n das hier noch nicht für uns existirt, fällt sie mit dem letzten definitiven Verkauf\n aus der Sphäre der Circulation in die der Consumtion, um hier als Lebensmittel\n oder als Produktionsmittel zu dienen.] .\n Ihre Werthgestalt oder Geldlarve tritt an ihre Stelle. Die zweite Circu-\n lationshälfte durchläuft sie nicht mehr in ihrer eignen Naturalhaut, sondern\n in ihrer Goldhaut.","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"3.89"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0090","text":"Die Continuität der Bewegung fällt damit ganz\n auf die Seite des Geldes und dieselbe Bewegung, die für die Waare zwei\n entgegengesetzte Prozesse einschliesst, schliesst als eigne Bewegung\n des Geldes stets denselben Prozess ein, seinen Stellenwechsel mit stets\n andrer Waare. Das Resultat der Waarencirculation, Ersatz von Waare\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"76\", \"facs\": \"#f0095\"} --> durch andre Waare, erscheint daher nicht durch ihren eignen Formwechsel\n vermittelt, sondern durch die Funktion des Geldes als Circulations-\n mittel, welches die an und für sich bewegungslosen Waaren circulirt,\n sie aus der Hand, worin sie Nicht-Gebrauchswerthe, in die Hand überträgt,\n worin sie Gebrauchswerthe, stets in entgegengesetzter Richtung zu seinem\n eignen Lauf.","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"3.90"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0091","text":"Es entfernt die Waaren beständig aus der Circulations-\n sphäre, indem es beständig an ihre Circulationsstelle tritt und sich damit\n von seinem eignen Ausgangspunkt entfernt. Obgleich daher die Geldbe-\n wegung nur Ausdruck der Waarencirculation, erscheint umgekehrt die\n Waarencirculation nur als Resultat der Geldbewegung [Note: „Il (l’argent) n’a d’autre mouvement que celui qui est imprimé par les pro-\n ductions.“ (Le Trosne l. c. p. 885.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"3.91"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0092","text":"Andrerseits kommt dem Geld nur die Funktion des Circulationsmittels\n zu, weil es die verselbstständigte Werthgestalt der Waaren ist. Seine Be-\n wegung als Circulationsmittel ist daher in der That nur ihre eigne Form-\n bewegung. Diese muss sich daher auch sinnlich im Umlauf des Geldes\n wiederspiegeln. Der doppelte Formwechsel der Waare spiegelt sich wie-\n der im zweimaligen Stellenwechsel desselben Geldstücks,\n wenn wir die Gesammtmetamorphose einer Waare, in der mehrmaligen\n Wiederholung seines Stellenwechsels, wenn wir die Ver-\n schlingung der zahllosen Metamorphosen in einander betrachten. Diesel-\n ben Geldstücke kommen als entäusserte Gestalt der Waare in die\n Hand des Verkäufers und verlassen sie als absolut veräusserliche\n Gestalt der Waare. Das Geld wirkt in derselben Weise als Kauf-\n mittel, jedesmal gegenüber andrer Waare, in beiden Prozessen.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"3.92"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0093","text":"Aber ihr\n innerer Zusammenhang, oder die doppelte Formbestimmtheit, die es in bei-\n den Prozessen für dieselbe Waare hat, erscheint in der doppelten und\n gegensätzlichen Bewegung, die denselben Geldstücken aufgedrückt wird.\nDieselben 2 Pfd. St., die beim Verkauf der Leinwand aus der Tasche\n des Weizenbauers in die Tasche des Leinwebers einwandern, wandern von\n ihr aus beim Kauf der Bibel. Es ist doppelter Stellenwechsel, und die\n Leinwand oder ihren Repräsentanten als Centrum betrachtet, in entgegen-\n gesetzter Richtung, positiver bei Einnahme des Geldes, negativer bei sei-\n ner Ausgabe. Finden dagegen nur einseitige Waarenmetamorphosen\n statt, blosse Verkäufe oder blosse Käufe, wie man will, so wechselt das-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"77\", \"facs\": \"#f0096\"} --> selbe Geld auch nur einmal den Platz.","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"3.93"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0094","text":"Sein zweiter Stellenwechsel drückt\n stets die zweite Metamorphose der Waare aus, ihre Rückverwandlung aus\n Geld. Es versteht sich übrigens ganz von selbst, dass alles diess nur für\n die hier betrachtete Form der einfachen Waarencirculation gilt.","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"3.94"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0095","text":"Jede Waare, bei ihrem ersten Schritt in die Circulation, ihrem ersten\n Formwechsel, fällt aus der Circulation heraus, während stets neue Waare\n in sie eintritt. Das Geld dagegen als Circulationsmittel haust beständig\n in der Circulationssphäre und treibt sich beständig in ihr um. Es ent-\n steht also die Frage, wie viel Geld diese Sphäre beständig absorbirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"3.95"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0096","text":"In einem Lande geht jeden Tag gleichzeitig, daher räumlich neben\n einander, eine ungeheure Anzahl einseitiger Waarenmetamorphosen vor, oder\n eine zahllose Masse zersplitterter Verkäufe, welche die von einander unab-\n hängigen Waarenbesitzer vollziehen. In ihren Preisen sind die Waaren\n bereits bestimmten vorgestellten Geldquantis gleichgesetzt. Da nun die\n hier betrachtete, unmittelbare Circulationsform Waare und Geld einander\n stets leiblich gegenüberstellt, die eine auf den Pol des Verkaufs, das andre\n auf den Gegenpol des Kaufs, ist die für den Circulationsprozess der Waaren-\n welt erheischte Masse von Circulationsmitteln bereits durch die Preis-\n summe der Waaren bestimmt. In der That stellt das Geld nur reell die\n in der Preissumme der Waaren bereits ideell ausgedrückte Goldsumme\n dar. Die Gleichheit dieser Summen versteht sich daher von selbst.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"3.96"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0097","text":"Wir\n wissen jedoch, dass bei gleichbleibenden Werthen der Waaren ihre Preise\n mit dem Werthe des Goldes (des Geldmaterials) selbst wechseln, verhält-\n nissmässig steigen, wenn er fällt, und fallen, wenn er steigt. Ob die Preis-\n summe der Waaren so steige oder falle, die Masse des circulirenden Geldes\n muss gleichmässig steigen oder fallen. Der Wechsel in der Masse der\n Circulationsmittel entspringt hier allerdings aus dem Geld selbst, aber\nnicht aus seiner Funktion als Circulationsmittel, son-\n dern aus seiner Funktion als Werthmass. Der Preis der\n Waaren wechselt erst umgekehrt wie der Werth des Geldes\n und dann wechselt die Masse der Circulationsmittel direkt wie der Preis\n der Waaren. Ganz dasselbe Phänomen würde sich ereignen, wenn z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"3.97"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0098","text":"nicht der Werth des Goldes sänke, sondern Silber es als Werthmass er-\n setzte, oder nicht der Werth des Silbers stiege, sondern Gold es aus der\n Funktion des Werthmasses verdrängte. In dem einen Fall müsste mehr\n Silber circuliren als vorher Gold, in dem andern weniger Gold als vorher\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"78\", \"facs\": \"#f0097\"} --> Silber. In beiden Fällen hätte sich der Werth des Geldmaterials\n verändert, d. h. der Waare, die als Mass der Werthe funktionirt, da-\n her der Preisausdruck der Waarenwerthe, daher die Masse des circuliren-\n den Geldes, das zur Realisirung dieser Preise dient. Man hat gesehn,\n dass die Circulationssphäre der Waaren ein Loch hat, wodurch Gold (Sil-\n ber, kurz das Geldmaterial) in sie eintritt als Waare von gegebnem\n Werth. Dieser Werth ist vorausgesetzt bei der Funktion des Geldes\n als Werthmass, also bei der Preisbestimmung. Sinkt nun z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"3.98"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0099","text":"der Werth\n des Werthmasses selbst, so erscheint diess zunächst im Preiswechsel der\n Waaren, die unmittelbar an den Produktionsquellen der edlen Metalle mit\n ihnen als Waaren ausgetauscht werden. Namentlich in minder entwickel-\n ten Zuständen der bürgerlichen Gesellschaft wird ein grosser Theil der\n andern Waaren noch längere Zeit in dem nun illusorisch gewordnen, ver-\n alteten Werth des Werthmasses geschätzt werden. Indess inficirt eine\n Waare die andre durch ihr relatives Werthverhältniss zu derselben, die\n Gold- oder Silberpreise der Waaren gleichen sich allmählig aus in den\n durch ihre Werthe selbst bestimmten Proportionen, bis schliesslich alle\n Waarenwerthe dem neuen Werth des Geldmetalls entsprechend geschätzt\n werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"3.99"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0100","text":"Dieser Ausgleichungsprozess ist begleitet von dem fortwähren-\n den Wachsthum der edlen Metalle, welche im Ersatz für die direkt mit\n ihnen ausgetauschten Waaren einströmen. In demselben Mass daher,\n worin die berichtigte Preisgebung der Waaren sich verallgemeinert, oder\n ihre Werthe dem neuen, gesunkenen und bis zu einem gewissen Punkt\n fortsinkenden Werth des Metalls gemäss geschätzt werden, ist auch bereits\n seine zu ihrer Realisirung nothwendige Mehrmasse vorhanden. Einseitige\n Beobachtung der Thatsachen, welche der Entdeckung der neuen Gold- und\n Silberquellen folgten, verleitete im 17. und namentlich im 18. Jahrhun-\n dert zum Trugschluss, die Waarenpreise seien gestiegen, weil mehr Gold\n und Silber als Circulationsmittel funktionirten. Im Folgenden wird der\n Werth des Goldes als gegeben vorausgesetzt, wie er in der That im\n Augenblick der Preisschätzung gegeben ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"3.100"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0101","text":"Unter dieser Voraussetzung also ist die Masse der Circulationsmittel\n durch die zu realisirende Preissumme der Waaren bestimmt. Setzen\n wir nun ferner den Preis jeder Waarenart als gegeben voraus, so hängt\n die Preissumme der Waaren offenbar von der in Circulation befind-\n lichen Waarenmasse ab. Es gehört wenig Kopfbrechens dazu, um\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"79\", \"facs\": \"#f0098\"} --> zu begreifen, dass wenn 1 Quarter Weizen 2 Pfd. St., 100 Quarter 200\n Pfd. St., 200 Quarter 400 Pfd. St. u. s. w. kosten, mit der Masse des\n Weizens daher die Geldmasse wachsen muss, die beim Verkauf den Platz\n mit ihm wechselt.","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"3.101"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0102","text":"Die Waarenmasse als gegeben vorausgesetzt, fluthet die Masse\n des circulirenden Geldes auf und ab mit den Preisschwankungen der\n Waaren. Sie steigt und fällt, weil die Preissumme der Waaren in\n Folge ihres Preiswechsels zu- oder abnimmt. Dazu ist keineswegs nöthig,\n dass die Preise aller Waaren gleichzeitig steigen oder fallen. Die Preis-\n steigerung einer gewissen Anzahl leitender Artikel in dem einen, oder ihre\n Preissenkung in dem andern Fall, reicht hin, um die zu realisirende\nPreissumme aller circulirenden Waaren zu erhöhn oder zu senken,\n also auch mehr oder weniger Geld in Circulation zu setzen. Ob der Preis-\n wechsel der Waaren wirkliche Werthwechsel wiederspiegelt oder blosse\n Schwankungen der Marktpreise, die Wirkung auf die Masse der Circula-\n tionsmittel bleibt dieselbe.","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"3.102"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0103","text":"Es sei gegeben eine Anzahl zusammenhangsloser, gleichzeitiger und\n daher räumlich neben einander fallender Verkäufe oder Theilmetamor-\n phosen, z. B. von 2 Quarter Weizen, 20 Ellen Leinwand, 1 Bibel, 4 Gal-\n lons Kornbranntwein. Wenn der Preis jedes Artikels 2 Pfd. St., die zu\n realisirende Preissumme daher 8 Pfd. St., so muss eine Geldmasse von\n 8 Pfd. St. in die Circulation eingehn. Bilden dieselben Waaren dagegen\n Glieder der uns bekannten Metamorphosenreihe: 1 Quarter Weizen —\n 2 Pfd. St. — 20 Ellen Leinwand — 2 Pfd. St. — 1 Bibel — 2 Pfd. St.\n — 4 Gallons Kornbranntwein — 2 Pfd. St., so circuliren dieselben\n 2 Pfd. St. die verschiedenen Waaren der Reihe nach, indem sie deren\n Preise der Reihe nach, also auch die Preissumme von 8 Pfd. St. realisiren,\n um schliesslich in der Hand des Destillateurs auszuruhn. Sie vollbringen\nvier Umläufe.","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"3.103"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0104","text":"Dieser wiederholte Stellenwechsel derselben Geldstücke\n stellt den doppelten Formwechsel der Waare dar, ihre Bewegung durch\n zwei entgegengesetzte Circulationsstadien und die Verschlingung der Meta-\n morphosen verschiedner Waaren [Note: „Ce sont les productions qui le (l’argent) mettent en mouvement et le\n font circuler … La célérité de son mouvement (sc. de l’argent) supplée à sa\n quantité. Lorqu’il en est besoin, il ne fait que glisser d’une main dans l’autre sans\n s’arrêter un instant.“ (Le Trosne l. c. p. 915, 916.)] . Die gegensätzlichen und einander\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"80\", \"facs\": \"#f0099\"} --> ergänzenden Phasen, wodurch dieser Prozess verläuft, können nicht räum-\n lich neben einander fallen, sondern nur zeitlich auf einander folgen.","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"3.104"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0105","text":"Zeitabschnitte bilden daher das Mass seiner Dauer, oder die Anzahl der\n Umläufe derselben Geldstücke in gegebner Zeit misst die Geschwindig-\n keit des Geldumlaufs. Der Circulationsprozess jener vier Waaren\n dauere z. B. einen Tag. So ist die zu realisirende Preissumme: 8 Pfd. St.,\n die Anzahl der Umläufe desselben Geldstücks während des Tags: 4 und\n die Masse des circulirenden Geldes: 2 Pfd. St., oder für einen gegebnen\n Zeitabschnitt des Circulationsprozesses:\n\\frac{Preissumme der Waaren}{Umlaufsanzahl eines Geldstücks} = Masse des als Circulations-\n mittel funktionirenden Gel-\n des. Dies Gesetz gilt allgemein.\n Der Circulationsprozess eines Landes in einem gegebnen Zeitabschnitt um-\n fasst zwar einerseits viele zersplitterte, gleichzeitige und räumlich neben\n einander fallende Verkäufe (resp.","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"3.105"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0106","text":"Käufe) oder Theilmetamorphosen, worin\n dieselben Geldstücke nur einmal die Stelle wechseln oder nur einen Um-\n lauf vollziehn, andrerseits viele theils neben einander herlaufende, theils\n sich in einander verschlingende mehr oder minder gliederreiche Metamor-\n phosenreihen, worin dieselben Geldstücke mehr oder minder zahlreiche\n Umläufe zurücklegen. Die Gesammtzahl der Umläufe aller in Circula-\n tion befindlichen gleichnamigen Geldstücke ergiebt jedoch die\nDurchschnittsanzahl der Umläufe des einzelnen Geldstücks oder die\n Durchschnittsgeschwindigkeit des Geldumlaufs. Die Geldmasse, die bei\n Beginn z. B. des täglichen Circulationsprozesses in ihn hineingeworfen\n wird, ist natürlich bestimmt durch die Preissumme der gleichzeitig\n und räumlich neben einander circulirenden Waaren. Aber inner-\n halb des Prozesses wird ein Geldstück so zu sagen für das andre verant-\n wortlich gemacht.","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"3.106"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0107","text":"Beschleunigt das eine seine Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit,\n so erlahmt die des andern, oder es fliegt ganz aus der Circulationssphäre\n heraus, da diese nur eine Goldmasse absorbiren kann, welche multiplicirt\n mit der mittlern Umlaufsanzahl ihres einzelnen Elements gleich der zu\n realisirenden Preissumme ist. Wächst daher die Anzahl der Umläufe der\n Geldstücke, so nimmt ihre circulirende Masse ab. Nimmt die Anzahl\n ihrer Umläufe ab, so wächst ihre Masse. Weil die Masse des Geldes, die\n als Circulationsmittel funktioniren kann, bei gegebner Durchschnittsge-\n schwindigkeit gegeben ist, hat man daher z. B. nur eine bestimmte\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"81\", \"facs\": \"#f0100\"} --> Quantität von Ein-Pfund-Noten in die Cirkulation hinein zu werfen, um\n eben so viele Sovereigns hinaus zu werfen, ein allen Banken wohlbekanntes\n Kunststück.","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"3.107"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0108","text":"Wie im Geldumlauf überhaupt nur der Circulationsprozess der\n Waaren erscheint, so in der Geschwindigkeit des Geldumlaufs die Ge-\n schwindigkeit ihres Formwechsels, das continuirliche Ineinandergreifen der\n Metamorphosenreihen, die Hast des Stoffwechsels, das rasche Verschwin-\n den der Waaren aus der Circulationssphäre und ihr ebenso rascher Ersatz\n durch neue Waaren. In der Geschwindigkeit des Geldumlaufs erscheint\n also die flüssige Einheit der entgegengesetzten und sich ergänzen-\n den Phasen, Verwandlung der Gebrauchsgestalt in Werthgestalt und Rück-\n verwandlung der Werthgestalt in Gebrauchsgestalt, oder der beiden Pro-\n zesse des Verkaufs und Kaufs. Umgekehrt erscheint in der Verlangsa-\n mung des Geldumlaufs die Trennung und gegensätzliche Ver-\n selbstständigung dieser Prozesse, die Stockung des Formwechsels\n und daher des Stoffwechsels.","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"3.108"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0109","text":"Woher diese Stockung entspringt, ist na-\n türlich der Circulation selbst nicht anzusehn. Sie zeigt nur das Phänomen\n selbst. Der populären Anschauung, welche mit verlangsamtem Geldumlauf\n das Geld minder häufig auf allen Punkten der Circulationsperipherie er-\n scheinen und verschwinden sieht, liegt es nah das Phänomen aus mangeln-\n der Quantität der Circulationsmittel zu deuten [Note: „Money being … the common measure of buying and selling, every\n body who has anything to sell, and cannot procure chapmen for it, is presently\n apt to think, that want of money in the kingdom, or country, is the\n cause why his goods do not go off; and so, want of money is the common cry;\n which is a great mistake … What do these people want, who cry out for\n money?","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"3.109"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0110","text":"… The Farmer complains ‥ he thinks that were more money in the\n country, he should have a price for his goods … Then it seems money is not his\n want, but a Price for his corn and cattle, which he would sell, but cannot …\n why cannot he get a price? … 1) Either there is too much corn and cattle in the\n country, so that most who come to market have need of selling, as he has, and\n few of buying: or, 2) There wants the usual vent abroad by Transportation . . . .\n Or, 3) The consumption fails, as when men, by reason of poverty, do not spend\n so much in their houses as formerly they did; wherefore it is not the increase of\n specifick money, which would at all advance the farmer’s goods, but the removal\n of any of these three causes, which do truly keep down the market ‥ The mer-\n chant and shopkeeper want money in the same manner, that is, they want a vent] .","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"3.110"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0111","text":"[Running matter: I. 6] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"82\", \"facs\": \"#f0101\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":111,"address":"3.111"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0112","text":"Das Gesammtquantum des in jedem Zeitabschnitt als Circulations-\n mittel functionirenden Geldes ist also bestimmt einerseits durch die Preis-\n summe der circulirenden Waarenwelt, andrerseits durch den langsamern\n oder raschern Fluss ihrer gegensätzlichen Circulationsprozesse, von dem\n es abhängt, der wievielte Theil jener Preissumme durch dieselben\n Geldstücke realisirt werden kann. Die Preissumme der Waaren hängt\n aber ab sowohl von der Masse als den Preisen jeder Waarenart. Die\n drei Faktoren der Preisbewegung, der circulirenden Waaren-\n masse und endlich der Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit des Geldes\n können aber in verschiedner Richtung und verschiednen Verhältnissen\n wechseln, die zu realisirende Preissumme, daher die durch sie\n bedingte Masse der Circulationsmittel, also sehr zahlreiche Com-\n binationen untergehn. Wir zählen hier nur die in der Geschichte der\n Waarenpreise wichtigsten auf.","kind":"passage","sequence":112,"address":"3.112"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0113","text":"Bei gleichbleibenden Waarenpreisen kann die Masse der\n Circulationsmittel wachsen, weil die Masse der circulirenden Waaren zu-\n nimmt oder die Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit des Geldes abnimmt, oder beides\n zusammenwirkt. Die Masse der Circulationsmittel kann umgekehrt ab-\n nehmen mit abnehmender Waarenmasse oder zunehmender Circulations-\n geschwindigkeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":113,"address":"3.113"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0114","text":"Bei allgemein steigenden Waarenpreisen kann die Masse\n der Circulationsmittel gleichbleiben, wenn die Masse der circulirenden\n Waaren in demselben Verhältniss abnimmt, worin ihr Preis zunimmt, oder\n die Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit des Geldes eben so rasch zunimmt als die\n Preiserhöhung, während die cirkulirende Waarenmasse constant bleibt.\n [Note: for the goods they deal in, by reason that the markets fail … in a nation\n never thrives better, than when riches are tost from hand to hand.“ (Sir\n Dudley North: „Discourses upon Trade. Lond. 1691“, p. 11—15\n passim.) Herrenschwand’s Schwindeleien kommen alle darauf hinaus, dass\n die aus der Natur der Waare entspringenden und daher in der Waarencirculation\n erscheinenden Widersprüche durch Vermehrung der Circulationsmittel beseitigt\n werden können.","kind":"passage","sequence":114,"address":"3.114"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0115","text":"Aus der Volksillusion, welche Stockungen des Produktions- und\n Circulationsprozesses einem Mangel an Circulationsmitteln zuschreibt, folgt übri-\n gens keineswegs umgekehrt, dass wirklicher Mangel an Circulationsmitteln, z. B.\n in Folge officieller Pfuschereien mit der „regulation of currency“, nicht seinerseits\n Stockungen hervorrufen kann.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"83\", \"facs\": \"#f0102\"} --> Die Masse der Circulationsmittel kann fallen, weil die Waarenmasse\n rascher ab- oder die Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit rascher zunimmt als die\n Preise.","kind":"passage","sequence":115,"address":"3.115"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0116","text":"Bei allgemein fallenden Waarenpreisen kann die Masse\n der Circulationsmittel gleichbleiben, wenn die Waarenmasse in demselben\n Verhältniss wächst, worin ihr Preis fällt, oder die Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit\n des Geldes in demselben Verhältniss abnimmt wie die Preise. Sie kann\n wachsen, wenn die Waarenmasse rascher wächst oder die Circulations-\n geschwindigkeit rascher abnimmt als die Waarenpreise fallen.","kind":"passage","sequence":116,"address":"3.116"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0117","text":"Die Variationen der verschiedenen Faktoren können sich wechselsei-\n tig compensiren, so dass ihrer beständigen Unstätigkeit zum Trotz die zu\n realisirende Gesammtsumme der Waarenpreise constant bleibt, also auch\n die circulirende Geldmasse. Man findet daher, namentlich bei Betrach-\n tung etwas längerer Perioden, ein viel constanteres Durchschnitts-\n niveau der in jedem Lande circulirenden Geldmasse und,\n mit Ausnahme starker Perturbationen, die periodisch aus den Produktions-\n und Handelskrisen, seltner aus einem Wechsel im Geldwerth selbst ent-\n springen, viel geringere Abweichungen von diesem Durchschnittsniveau\n als man nach dem Augenschein erwarten sollte.","kind":"passage","sequence":117,"address":"3.117"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0118","text":"Das Gesetz, dass die Quantität der Circulationsmittel bestimmt ist\n durch die Preissumme der circulirenden Waaren und die Durchschnitts-\n geschwindigkeit des Geldumlaufs [Note: „There is a certain measure, and proportion of money requisite to drive\n the trade of a nation, more or less than which, would prejudice the same.","kind":"passage","sequence":118,"address":"3.118"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0119","text":"Just as\n there is a certain proportion of farthings necessary in a small retail Trade, to\n change silver money, and to even such reckonings as cannot be adjusted with the\n smallest silver pieces … Now as the proportion of the number of farthings re-\n quisite in commerce is to be taken from the number of people, the frequency of\n their exchanges; as also, and principally, from the value of the smallest\n silver pieces of money; so in like manner, the proportion of money (gold and\n silver species) requisite to our trade, is to be likewise taken from the frequency\n of commutations, and from the bigness of payments.“ (Wil-\n liam Petty: „A Treatise on Taxes and Contributions. Lond.\n 1667“, p. 17.) Die Hume’sche Theorie ward gegen J. Stenart u. A. ver-\n theidigt von A. Young in seiner „Political Arithmetic. Lond.","kind":"passage","sequence":119,"address":"3.119"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0120","text":"1774“,\n wo ein eignes Kapitel: „Prices depend on quantity of money“, p.] , kann auch so ausgedrückt werden,\n [Running matter: 6*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"84\", \"facs\": \"#f0103\"} --> dass bei gegebner Werthsumme der Waaren und gegebner Durchschnitts-\n geschwindigkeit ihrer Metamorphosen, die Quantität des umlaufenden\n Geldes oder des Geldmaterials von seinem eignen Werth abhängt.\n Die Illusion, dass umgekehrt die Waarenpreise durch die Masse der Circu-\n lationsmittel und letztre ihrerseits durch die Masse des in einem Lande\n befindlichen Geldmaterials bestimmt werden [Note: „The prices of things will certainly rise in every nation, as the gold and\n silver increase amongst the people; and, consequently, where the gold and silver\n decrease in any nation, the prices of all things must fall proportionably to such\n decrease of money.“ (Jacob Vanderlint: „Money answers all\n Things“. Lond.","kind":"passage","sequence":120,"address":"3.120"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0121","text":"1734, p. 5.) Nähere Vergleichung zwischen Vanderlint und\n Hume’s „Essays“ lässt mir nicht den geringsten Zweifel, dass Hume V.’s\n übrigens sehr bedeutende Schrift kannte und benutzte. Die Ansicht, dass die\n Masse der Circulationsmittel die Preise bestimmt, auch bei Barbon und noch viel\n älteren Schriftstellern. „No inconvenience“, sagt Vanderlint, „can arise by an\nunrestrained trade, but very great advantage … since, if the cash of\n the nation be decreased by it, which prohibitions are designed to prevent, those\n nations that get the cash will certainly find every thing advance in price, as the\n cash increases amongst them. And . . . . our manufactures and every thing else,\n will soon become so moderate as to turn the balance of trade in our favour, and\n thereby fetch the money back again.“ (l. c. p.","kind":"passage","sequence":121,"address":"3.121"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0122","text":"44.)] , wurzelt bei ihren ursprüng-\n lichen Vertretern in der abgeschmackten Hypothese, dass Waaren ohne\n Preis und Geld ohne Werth in den Circulationsprozess eingehn,\n [Note: 112 sqq. Ich bemerke „Zur Kritik etc. p. 149“: „Die Frage über die\nQuantität der circulirenden Münze beseitigt er (A. Smith) stillschweigend,\n indem er das Geld ganz falsch als blosse Waare behandelt.“ Diess gilt nur, so-\n weit A. Smith ex officio das Geld behandelt. Gelegentlich jedoch, z. B. in der\n Kritik der früheren Systeme der Pol. Oekon., spricht er das Richtige aus: „The\n quantity of coin in every country is regulated by the value of the commodities\n which are to be circulated by it . . . . The value of goods annually bought and\n sold in any country requires a certain quantity of money to circulate and distri-\n bute them to their proper consumers, and can give employment to no more.","kind":"passage","sequence":122,"address":"3.122"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0123","text":"The\n channel of circulation necessarily draws to itself a sum sufficient to fill it, and\n never admits any more.“ (Wealth of Nations, l. IV. ch. I.) Aehnlich\n eröffnet A. Smith sein Werk ex officio mit einer Apotheose der Theilung der\n Arbeit. Hinterher, im letzten Buch über die Quellen des Staatseinkommens,\n reproducirt er gelegentlich A. Ferguson’s, seines Lehrers, Denunciation der Thei-\n lung der Arbeit.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"85\", \"facs\": \"#f0104\"} --> wo sich dann ein aliquoter Theil des Waarenbreis mit einem aliquoten\n Theil des Metallbergs austausche [Note: Dass jede einzelne Waarenart durch ihren Preis ein Element der\nPreissumme aller circulirenden Waaren bildet, ist selbstverständ-\n lich. Wie aber unter einander incommensurable Gebrauchswerthe sich\n en masse mit der in einem Land befindlichen Gold- oder Silbermasse austauschen\n sollen, ist völlig unbegreiflich.","kind":"passage","sequence":123,"address":"3.123"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0124","text":"Verschwindelt man die Waarenwelt in eine ein-\n zige Gesammtwaare, wovon jede Waare nur einen aliquoten Theil bildet,\n so kommt das schöne Rechenexempel heraus: Gesammtwaare = x Ctr. Gold,\n Waare A = aliquoter Theil der Gesammtwaare = demselben aliquoten Theil von\n x Ctr. Gold. Diess ehrlich heraus bei Montesquieu: „Si l’on compare la masse\n de l’or et de l’argent qui est dans le monde, avec la somme des marchandises qui\n y sont, il est certain que chaque denrée ou marchandise, en particulier, pourra\n être comparée à une certaine portion de l’autre. Supposons qu’il n’y ait\n qu’une seule denrée ou marchandise dans le monde, ou qu’il\n n’y ait qu’une seule qui s’achète, et qu’elle se divise comme l’argent;\n cette partie de cette marchandise répondra à une partie de la masse de l’argent;\n la moitié du total de l’une à la moitié du total de l’autre etc. . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":124,"address":"3.124"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0125","text":"l’établissement\n du prix des choses dépend toujours fondamentalement de la raison du total des\n choses au total des signes.“ (Montesquieu, l. c. t. III, p. 12, 13.) Ueber\n die Weiterentwicklung dieser Theorie durch Ricardo, seinen Schüler James Mill,\n Lord Overstone u. s. w. vgl. „Zur Kritik u. s. w.“ p. 140—146 u. p. 150\n seqq. Herr J. St. Mill versteht es, mit der ihm geläufigen eklektischen Logik,\n der Ansicht seines Vaters J. Mill und zugleich der entgegengesetzten zu sein.\n Vergleicht man den Text seines Compendiums: „Princ. of Pol. Econ.“\n mit der Vorrede (erste Ausgabe), worin er sich selbst als Adam Smith der Gegen-\n wart ankündet, so weiss man nicht, was mehr bewundern, die Naivetät des Mannes\n oder die des Publikums, das ihn auf Treu und Glauben in den Kauf nahm als\n Adam Smith, zu dem er sich etwa verhält wie General Williams Kars von Kars\n zum Herzog von Wellington.","kind":"passage","sequence":125,"address":"3.125"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0126","text":"Die weder umfangreichen noch gehaltreichen Ori-\n ginalforschungen des Herrn J. St. Mill im Gebiet der Pol. Oek. findet man alle in\n Reih’ und Glied aufmarschirt in seinem 1844 erschienenen Schriftchen: „Some\n Unsettled Questions of Political Economy“. Locke spricht direkt\n den Zusammenhang zwischen der Werthlosigkeit von Gold und Silber und\n der Bestimmung ihres Werths durch Quantität aus. „Mankind\n having consented to put an imaginary value upon gold and silver … the\n intrinsick value, regarded in these metals, is nothing but the quantity.“\n „Some Considerations etc. 1691“. Works ed 1777, vol. II, p. 15.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":126,"address":"3.126"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0127","text":"## c) Die Münze. Das Werthzeichen.","kind":"passage","sequence":127,"address":"3.127"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0128","text":"Aus der Funktion des Geldes als Circulationsmittel entspringt seine\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"86\", \"facs\": \"#f0105\"} --> Münzgestalt. Der in dem Preise oder Geldnamen der Waaren vorge-\n stellte Gewichtstheil Gold muss ihnen in der Circulation als gleichnamiges\n Goldstück oder Münze gegenübertreten. Wie die Feststellung des\n Massstabs der Preise, fällt das Geschäft der Münzung dem Staat anheim.\n In den verschiedenen Nationaluniformen, die Gold und Silber als Münzen\n tragen, auf dem Weltmarkt aber wieder ausziehn, erscheint die Scheidung\n zwischen den innern oder nationalen Sphären der Waarencirculation und\n ihrer allgemeinen Weltmarktssphäre.","kind":"passage","sequence":128,"address":"3.128"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0129","text":"Goldmünze und Barrengold unterscheiden sich also von Haus aus nur\n durch die Figur, und das Gold ist beständig aus einer Form in die andre\n verwandelbar [Note: Es liegt natürlich ganz jenseits meines Zwecks, Details wie Schlagschatz\n u. dgl. zu behandeln. Gegenüber dem romantischen Sykophanten Adam Müller\n jedoch, der „die grossartige Liberalität“ bewundert, womit die „eng-\n lische Regierung unentgeldlich münzt“, folgendes Urtheil Sir Dud-\n ley North’s: „Silver and gold, like other commodities, have their ebbings and\n flowings. Upon the arrival of quantities from Spain … it is carried into the\n Tower, and coined. Not long after there will come a demand for bullion, to\n be exported again. If there is none, but all happens to be in coin, what then?\n Melt it down again; there’s no loss in it, for the coining costs the owner nothing.","kind":"passage","sequence":129,"address":"3.129"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0130","text":"Thus the nation has been abused, and made to pay for the twisting of straw, for\n asses to eat. If the merchant (North war selbst einer der grössten Kaufleute zu\n Charles II. Zeit) had to pay the price of the coinage, he would not have sent his\n silver to the Tower without consideration; and coined money would always keep a\n value above uncoined silver.“ (North l. c. p. 18.)] . Der Weg aus der Münze ist aber zugleich der Gang\n zum Schmelztiegel. Im Umlauf verschleissen nämlich die Goldmünzen,\n die eine mehr, die andre weniger. Goldtitel und Goldsubstanz, Nominal-\n gehalt und Realgehalt beginnen ihren Scheidungsprozess. Gleichnamige\n Goldmünzen werden von ungleichem Werth, weil verschiednem Gewicht.\n Das Gold als Circulationsmittel weicht ab vom Gold als Massstab der\n Preise, und hört damit auch auf wirkliches Aequivalent der Waaren zu\n sein, deren Preise es realisirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":130,"address":"3.130"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0131","text":"Die Geschichte dieser Wirren bildet die\n Münzgeschichte des Mittelalters und der Neuzeit bis ins 18. Jahrhundert.\n Die naturwüchsige Tendenz des Cirkulationsprozesses, das Goldsein der\n Münze in Goldschein oder die Münze in ein Symbol ihres officiellen Metall-\n gehalts zu verwandeln, ist selbst anerkannt durch die modernsten Gesetze\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"87\", \"facs\": \"#f0106\"} --> über den Grad des Metallverlustes, der ein Goldstück kursunfähig macht\n oder demonetisirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":131,"address":"3.131"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0132","text":"Wenn der Geldumlauf selbst den Realgehalt vom Nominalgehalt\n der Münze scheidet, ihr Metalldasein von ihrem funktionellen Dasein,\n so enthält er die Möglichkeit latent, das Metallgeld in seiner Münzfunktion\n durch Marken aus andrem Material oder Symbole zu ersetzen. Die tech-\n nischen Hindernisse der Münzung ganz diminutiver Gewichtstheile des\n Goldes, resp. Silbers, und der Umstand, dass niedrigere Metalle ursprüng-\n lich statt der edleren, Silber statt des Goldes, Kupfer statt des Silbers,\n zum Werthmass dienen und daher als Geld circuliren im Augenblick, wo\n das edlere Metall sie entthront, erklären historisch die Rolle von Silber-\n und Kupfermarken als Substitute der Goldmünze. Sie ersetzen das Gold\n in den Kreisen der Waarencirculation, worin die Münze am schnellsten\n circulirt und sich daher am schnellsten abnutzt, d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":132,"address":"3.132"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0133","text":"wo Käufe und Ver-\n käufe unaufhörlich im kleinsten Massstab erneuert werden. Um die Fest-\n setzung dieser Trabanten an der Stelle des Goldes selbst zu verhindern,\n werden gesetzlich die sehr niedrigen Proportionen bestimmt, worin sie\n allein an Zahlungsstatt für Gold angenommen werden müssen. Die be-\n sondern Kreise, worin die verschiednen Münzsorten umlaufen, laufen natür-\n lich in einander.","kind":"passage","sequence":133,"address":"3.133"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0134","text":"Die Scheidemünze erscheint neben dem Gold zur Zah-\n lung von Bruchtheilen der kleinsten Goldmünze; das Gold tritt beständig\n in die Detailcirculation ein, wird aber durch Auswechslung mit Scheide-\n münze ebenso beständig herausgeworfen [Note: „If silver never exceed what is wanted for the smaller payments, it cannot\n be collected in sufficient quantities for the larger payments … the use of gold in\n the main payments necessarily implies also its use in the retail trade: those who\n have gold coin offering them for small purchases, and receiving with the commo-\n dity purchased a balance of silver in return; by which means the surplus of silver\n that would otherwise encumber the retail dealer, is drawn off and dispersed into\n general circulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":134,"address":"3.134"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0135","text":"But if there is as much silver as will transact the small pay-\n ments independent of gold, the retail dealer must then receive silver for small pur-\n chases; and it must of necessity accumulate in his hands.“ (David Buchanan:\n „Inquiry into the Taxation and Commercial Policy of Great\n Britain. Edinburgh 1844“, p. 248, 249.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":135,"address":"3.135"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0136","text":"Der Metallgehalt der Silber- oder Kupfermarken ist willkührlich durch\n das Gesetz bestimmt. Im Umlauf verschleissen sie noch rascher als die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"88\", \"facs\": \"#f0107\"} --> Goldmünze. Ihre Münzfunktion wird daher faktisch durchaus unabhängig\n von ihrem Gewicht, d. h. von allem Werth. Das Münzdasein des Goldes\n scheidet sich völlig von seiner Werthsubstanz. Relativ werthlose Dinge,\nPapierzettel, können also an seiner Statt als Münze funktioniren. In\n den metallischen Geldmarken ist der rein symbolische Charakter noch eini-\n germassen versteckt. Im Papiergeld tritt er augenscheinlich hervor.\n Man sieht: ce n’est que le premier pas qui coûte.","kind":"passage","sequence":136,"address":"3.136"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0137","text":"Es handelt sich hier nur von Staatspapiergeld mit Zwangs-\n kurs. Es wächst unmittelbar aus der metallischen Circulation heraus.\nCreditgeld unterstellt dagegen Verhältnisse, die uns vom Standpunkt\n der einfachen Waarencirculation noch durchaus unbekannt sind. Im Vor-\n beigehn sei jedoch bemerkt, dass, wie eigentliches Papiergeld aus der\n Funktion des Geldes als Circulationsmittel entspringt, das Cre-\n ditgeld in der Funktion des Geldes als Zahlungsmittel seine natur-\n wüchsige Wurzel besitzt [Note: Der Finanzmandarine Wan-mao-in liess sich beigehn, dem Sohn des\n Himmels ein Projekt zu unterbreiten, welches versteckt auf Verwandlung der chi-\n nesischen Reichsassignaten in convertible Banknoten hinzielte. Im Bericht des\n Assignaten-Comités von April 1854 erhält er gehörig den Kopf gewaschen. Ob\n er auch die obligate Tracht Bambushiebe erhielt, wird nicht gemeldet.","kind":"passage","sequence":137,"address":"3.137"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0138","text":"„Das\n Comité“, lautet es am Schluss des Berichts, „hat sein Projekt aufmerksam er-\n wogen und findet, dass alles in ihm auf den Vortheil der Kaufleute ausgeht und\n nichts für die Krone vortheilhaft ist.“ („Arbeiten der Kaiserlich Russi-\n schen Gesandtschaft zu Peking über China. Aus dem Russi-\n schen von Dr. K. Abel und F. A. Mecklenburg. Erster Band.\n Berlin 1858“, p. 47 sq.) Ueber die beständige Entmetallung der\n Goldmünzen durch ihren Umlauf sagt ein „Governor“ der Bank of England als\n Zeuge vor dem „House of Lords’ Committee“ (über „Bankacts“): „Jedes Jahr\n wird eine frische Klasse von Souverainen (diess nicht politisch, sondern der So-\n vereign ist Name des Pfd. St.) zu leicht. Die Klasse, welche das eine Jahr als voll-\n wichtig passirt, verliert durch den Verschleiss hinreichend, um das nächste Jahr\n die Wagschale gegen sich zu drehn.“ (H. o. Lords’ Committee 1848,\n n. 429.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":138,"address":"3.138"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0139","text":"Papierzettel, denen Geldnamen, wie 1 Pfd. St., 5 Pfd. St. u. s. w.\n aufgedruckt sind, werden vom Staat äusserlich in den Circulationsprozess\n hineingeworfen. Soweit sie wirklich an der Stelle der gleichnamigen\n Goldsumme circuliren, spiegeln sich in ihrer Bewegung nur die Gesetze des\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"89\", \"facs\": \"#f0108\"} --> Geldumlaufs selbst wieder. Ein spezifisches Gesetz der Papiercirculation\n kann nur aus ihrem Repräsentationsverhältniss zum Gold entspringen. Und\n diess Gesetz ist einfach diess, dass die Ausgabe des Papiergelds auf die\n Quantität zu beschränken ist, worin das von ihm symbolisch dargestellte\n Gold (resp. Silber) wirklich circuliren müsste. Nun schwankt zwar das\n Goldquantum, welches die Circulationssphäre absorbiren kann, beständig\n über oder unter ein gewisses Durchschnittsniveau.","kind":"passage","sequence":139,"address":"3.139"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0140","text":"Jedoch sinkt die\n Mass des circulirenden Mediums in einem gegebnen Land nie unter ein\n gewisses Minimum, das sich erfahrungsmässig feststellt. Dass diese\n Minimalmasse fortwährend ihre Bestandtheile wechselt, d. h. aus stets an-\n dern Goldstücken besteht, ändert natürlich nichts an ihrem Umfang und\n ihrem constanten Umtrieb in der Circulationssphäre. Sie kann daher durch\n Papiersymbole ersetzt werden. Werden dagegen heute alle Circulations-\n kanäle zum vollen Grad ihrer Geldabsorptionsfähigkeit mit Papiergeld ge-\n füllt, so können sie in Folge der Schwankungen der Waarencirculation\n morgen übervoll sein. Alles Mass geht verloren. Ueberschreitet aber\n das Papier sein Mass, d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":140,"address":"3.140"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0141","text":"die Quantität von Goldmünze gleicher Deno-\n mination, welche circuliren könnte, so stellt es, von der Gefahr allgemeiner\n Discreditirung abgesehn, innerhalb der Waarenwelt dennoch nur die durch\n ihre immanenten Gesetze bestimmte, also auch allein repräsentirbare Gold-\n quantität vor. Stellt die Papierzettelmasse z. B. je 2 Unzen Gold, statt\n je 1 Unze dar, so werden ihre Geldnamen faktisch, sage etwa von 1 Pfd.\n St. per ¼ Unze, zu Namen von ⅛ Unzen herabgesetzt. Die Wirkung\n ist dieselbe, als wäre das Gold in seiner Funktion als Mass der Preise\n verändert worden. Dieselben Werthe, die sich daher vorher im Preise von\n 1 Pfd. St., drücken sich jetzt im Preise von 2 Pfd. St. aus.","kind":"passage","sequence":141,"address":"3.141"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0142","text":"Das Papiergeld ist Goldzeichen oder Geldzeichen. Sein Ver-\n hältniss zu den Waaren werthen besteht nur darin, dass sie ideell in den-\n selben Goldquanta ausgedrückt sind, welche vom Papier symbolisch sinn-\n lich dargestellt werden. Nur sofern das Papiergeld Goldquanta repräsen-\n tirt, die, wie alle andern Waarenquanta, auch Werthquanta, ist es\nWerthzeichen.","kind":"passage","sequence":142,"address":"3.142"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0143","text":"Es fragt sich schliesslich, warum das Gold durch blosse werthlose\n Zeichen seiner selbst ersetzt werden kann? Es ist aber, wie man gesehn,\n nur so ersetzbar, soweit es in seiner Funktion als Münze oder Circulations-\n mittel isolirt oder verselbstständigt wird. Nun findet die Verselbststän-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"90\", \"facs\": \"#f0109\"} --> digung dieser Funktion zwar nicht für die einzelnen Goldmünzen statt,\n obgleich sie in dem Fortcirculiren verschlissener Goldstücke erscheint.\n Blosse Münze oder Circulationsmittel sind die Goldstücke grade nur so\n lang sie sich wirklich im Umlauf befinden. Was aber nicht für die ein-\n zelne Goldmünze, gilt für die vom Papiergeld ersetzbare Minimalmasse\n Gold. Sie haust beständig in der Circulationssphäre, funktionirt fortwäh-\n rend als Circulationsmittel und existirt daher ausschliesslich als Träger\n dieser Funktion.","kind":"passage","sequence":143,"address":"3.143"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0144","text":"Ihre Bewegung stellt also nur das fortwährende Inein-\n anderumschlagen der entgegengesetzten Prozesse der Waarenmetamor-\n phose W — G — W dar, worin der Waare ihre Werthgestalt nur gegen-\n übertritt, um sofort wieder zu verschwinden. Die selbstständige\n Darstellung des Tauschwerths der Waare ist hier nur flüch-\n tiges Moment. Sofort wird sie wieder durch andere Waare ersetzt. Da-\n her genügt auch die bloss symbolische Existenz des Geldes in einem Pro-\n zess, der es beständig aus einer Hand in die andre entfernt. Sein funk-\n tionelles Dasein absorbirt so zu sagen sein materielles.","kind":"passage","sequence":144,"address":"3.144"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0145","text":"Verschwindend\n objektivirter Reflex der Waarenpreise funktionirt es nur noch als Zeichen\n seiner selbst und kann daher auch durch Zeichen ersetzt werden [Note: Daraus, dass Gold und Silber als Münze oder in der ausschliesslichen\n Funktion als Circulationsmittel zu Zeichen ihrer selbst werden, leitet\nNicholas Barbon das Recht der Regierungen her „to raise money“, d. h.,\n z. B. einem Quantum Silber, das Groschen hiess, den Namen eines grössern\n Silberquantums wie Thaler zu geben, und so den Gläubigern Groschen statt Thaler\n zurückzuzahlen. „Money does wear and grow lighter by often telling over …\n It is the denomination and currency of the money that men regard in\n bargaining, and not the quantity of silver … ’Tis the publick authority upon the\n metal that makes it money.“ (N. Barbon l. c. p. 29, 30, 25.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":145,"address":"3.145"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0146","text":"Nur\n bedarf das Zeichen des Geldes seiner eignen objektiv gesellschaft-\n lichen Gültigkeit und diese erhält das Papiersymbol durch den\n Zwangskurs. Nur innerhalb der von den Grenzen eines Gemeinwesens\n umschriebnen oder innern Circulationssphäre gilt dieser Staatszwang, aber\n auch nur hier geht das Geld völlig auf in seine Funktion als Circulations-\n mittel oder Münze, und kann daher im Papiergeld eine von seiner Metall-\n substanz äusserlich getrennte und bloss funktionelle Existenzweise er-\n halten.","kind":"passage","sequence":146,"address":"3.146"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0147","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"91\", \"facs\": \"#f0110\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":147,"address":"3.147"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0148","text":"## C. Geld.","kind":"passage","sequence":148,"address":"3.148"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0149","text":"Die Waare, welche als Werthmass und daher auch, persönlich oder\n durch Stellvertreter, als Circulationsmittel funktionirt, ist Geld.\n Gold (resp. Silber) ist daher Geld. Als Geld funktionirt es, einerseits\n wo es in seiner goldnen (resp. silbernen) Leiblichkeit erscheinen muss,\n daher als Geldwaare, also weder bloss ideell, wie im Werthmass, noch\n repräsentationsfähig, wie im Circulationsmittel; andrerseits, wo seine Funk-\n tion, ob es selbe nun in eigner Person oder durch Stellvertreter vollziehe,\n es als alleinige Werthgestalt oder allein adäquates Dasein\n des Tauschwerths allen andern Waaren als blossen Gebrauchs-\n werthen gegenüber fixirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":149,"address":"3.149"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0150","text":"## a) Schatzbildung.","kind":"passage","sequence":150,"address":"3.150"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0151","text":"Der continuirliche Kreislauf der zwei entgegengesetzten Waaren-\n metamorphosen oder der flüssige Umschlag von Verkauf und Kauf er-\n scheint im rastlosen Umlauf des Geldes oder seiner Funktion als per-\n petuum mobile der Circulation. Es wird immobilisirt, oder verwandelt sich,\n wie Boisguillebert sagt, aus meuble in immeuble, aus Münze\n in Geld, sobald die Metamorphosenreihe unterbrochen, der Verkauf\n nicht durch nachfolgenden Kauf ergänzt wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":151,"address":"3.151"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0152","text":"Mit der ersten Entwicklung der Waarencirculation selbst entwickelt\n sich die Nothwendigkeit und die Leidenschaft, das Produkt der ersten\n Metamorphose, die verwandelte Gestalt der Waare oder ihre Goldpuppe\n festzuhalten [Note: „Une richesse en argent n’est que … richesse en productions, con-\n verties en argent.“ (Mercier de la Rivière l. c. p. 557.) „Une\n valeur en productions n’a fait que changer de forme.“ (id. p. 486.)] . Waare wird verkauft, nicht um Waare zu kaufen, son-\n dern um Waarenform durch Geldform zu ersetzen. Aus blosser Vermitt-\n lung des Stoffwechsels wird dieser Formwechsel zum Selbstzweck. Die\nentäusserte Gestalt der Waare wird verhindert als ihre absolut ver-\n äusserliche Gestalt oder nur verschwindende Geldform zu funktioniren.\n Das Geld versteinert damit zum Schatz, und der Waarenverkäufer\n wird Schatzbildner.","kind":"passage","sequence":152,"address":"3.152"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0153","text":"Grade in den Anfängen der Waarencirculation verwandelt sich nur\n der Ueberschuss an Gebrauchswerthen in Geld. Gold und Silber werden so\n von selbst zu gesellschaftlichen Ausdrücken des Ueberflusses oder des\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"92\", \"facs\": \"#f0111\"} --> Reichthums. Diese naive Form der Schatzbildung verewigt sich bei Völ-\n kern, wo der traditionellen und auf Selbstbedarf gerichteten Produktions-\n weise ein fest abgeschlossner Kreis von Bedürfnissen entspricht. So bei\n den Asiaten, namentlich den Indern. Vanderlint, der die Waaren-\n preise durch die Masse des in einem Land befindlichen Goldes und Silbers\n bestimmt wähnt, fragt sich, warum die indischen Waaren so wohlfeil?\n Antwort: Weil die Inder das Geld vergraben. Von 1602—1734, be-\n merkt er, vergruben sie 150 Millionen Pfd. St.","kind":"passage","sequence":153,"address":"3.153"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0154","text":"Silber, die ursprünglich von\n Amerika nach Europa kamen [Note: ’Tis by this practice they keep all their goods and manufactures at such\n low rates.“ (Vanderlint l. c. p. 95, 96.)] . Von 1856—1866, also in 10 Jahren,\n exportirte England nach Indien und China (das nach China exportirte Me-\n tall fliesst grossentheils wieder nach Indien) 120 Millionen Pfd. St. in\n Silber, welches vorher gegen australisches Gold eingewechselt wurde.","kind":"passage","sequence":154,"address":"3.154"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0155","text":"Mit mehr entwickelter Waarenproduktion muss jeder Waarenpro-\n ducent sich den nexus rerum, das „gesellschaftliche Faustpfand“ sichern [Note: „Money is a pledge.“ (John Bellers: „Essays about the\n Poor, Manufactures, Trade, Plantations, and Immorality.\n Lond. 1699“, p. 13.)] .\n Seine Bedürfnisse erneuern sich unaufhörlich und gebieten unaufhörlichen\n Kauf fremder Waare, während Produktion und Verkauf seiner eignen\n Waare Zeit kosten und von Zufällen abhängen. Um zu kaufen, ohne zu\n verkaufen, muss er vorher verkauft haben, ohne zu kaufen. Diese Ope-\n ration, auf allgemeiner Stufenleiter ausgeführt, scheint sich selbst zu wider-\n sprechen. An ihren Produktionsquellen jedoch tauschen sich die edlen\n Metalle direkt mit andern Waaren aus.","kind":"passage","sequence":155,"address":"3.155"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0156","text":"Es findet hier Verkauf (auf Seite\n der Waarenbesitzer) ohne Kauf (auf Seite der Gold- und Silberbesitzer)\n statt [Note: Kauf im kategorischen Sinn unterstellt nämlich Gold oder Silber schon\n als verwandelte Gestalt der Waare, oder als Produkt des Verkaufs.] . Und spätere Verkäufe ohne nachfolgende Käufe vermitteln bloss\n die weitere Vertheilung der edlen Metalle unter alle Waarenbesitzer. So\n entstehn auf allen Punkten des Verkehrs Gold- und Silberschätze vom\n verschiedensten Umfang. Mit der Möglichkeit, die Waare als Tausch-\n werth oder den Tauschwerth als Waare festzuhalten, erwacht die Goldgier.\n Mit der Ausdehnung der Waarencirculation wächst die Macht des Geldes,\n der stets schlagfertigen, absolut gesellschaftlichen Form des Reichthums.\n „Gold ist ein wunderbares Ding! Wer dasselbe besitzt, ist Herr von\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"93\", \"facs\": \"#f0112\"} --> allem, was er wünscht.","kind":"passage","sequence":156,"address":"3.156"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0157","text":"Durch Gold kann man sogar Seelen in das Para-\n dies gelangen lassen.“ (Columbus, im Brief aus Jamaica, 1503). Da\n dem Geld nicht anzusehn, was in es verwandelt ist, verwandelt sich alles,\n Waare oder nicht, in Geld. Alles wird verkäuflich und kaufbar. Die\n Circulation wird die grosse gesellschaftliche Retorte, worin alles hinein-\n fliegt, um als Geldkrystall wieder herauszukommen. Dieser Alchymie wider-\n stehen nicht einmal Heiligenknochen und noch viel weniger minder grobe\n res sacrosanctae, extra commercium hominum [Note: Heinrich III., allerchristlichster König von Frankreich, raubt Klöstern\n u. s. w. ihre Reliquien, um sie zu versilbern. Man weiss, welche Rolle der Raub\n der delphischen Tempelschätze durch die Phokier in der griechischen Geschichte\n spielt. Dem Gott der Waaren dienten bei den Alten bekanntlich die Tempel zum\n Wohnsitz. Sie waren „heilige Banken“.","kind":"passage","sequence":157,"address":"3.157"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0158","text":"Den Phöniziern, einem Handelsvolke par\n excellence, galt Geld als die entäusserte Gestalt aller Dinge. Es war daher in der\n Ordnung, dass die Jungfrauen, die sich an den Festen der Liebesgöttin den\n Fremden hingaben, das zum Lohn empfangene Geldstück der Göttin opferten.] . Wie im Geld aller\n qualitative Unterschied der Waaren ausgelöscht ist, löscht es seinerseits\n als radikaler Leveller alle Unterschiede aus [Note: „Gold! yellow, glittering precious gold!\n Thus much of this, will make black white; foul, fair;\n Wrong, right; base, noble; old, young; coward, valiant\n . . . . . . . . . . What this, you gods! Why this\n Will lug your priests and servants from your sides;\n Pluck stout men’s pillows from below their heads.","kind":"passage","sequence":158,"address":"3.158"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0159","text":"This yellow slave\n Will knit and break religions; bless the accurs’d;\n Make the hoar leprosy ador’d; place thieves\n And give them title, knee and approbation,\n With senators of the bench; this is it,\n That makes the wappen’d widow wed again\n . . . . . . . . . . Come damned earth,\n Thou common whore of mankind.“\n (Shakespeare, Timon of Athens.)] . Das Geld ist aber selbst\n Waare, ein äusserlich Ding, das Privateigenthum eines Jeden werden kann.\n Die gesellschaftliche Macht wird so zur Privatmacht der Privatperson.\n Die antike Gesellschaft denuncirt es daher als die Scheidemünze ihrer\n ökonomischen und sittlichen Ordnung [Note: „Οὐδὲν γὰϱ ἀνϑϱώποισιν οἷον ἄϱγυϱος\n Καϰὸν νόμισμα ἔβλαστε· τοῦτο ϰαὶ πόλεις\n Ποϱϑεῖ, τόδ᾽ἄνδϱας ἐξανίστησιν δόμων.\n Τόδ᾽ ἐϰδιδάσϰει ϰαὶ παϱαλλάσσει φϱένας\n Χϱηστὰς πϱὸς αἰσχϱὰ ἀνϑϱώποις ἔχειν,\n Καὶ παντὸς ἔϱγου δυσσέβειαν εἰδέναι.“\n (Sophocles, Antigone.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":159,"address":"3.159"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0160","text":"Die moderne Gesellschaft, die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"94\", \"facs\": \"#f0113\"} --> schon in ihren Kinderjahren den Plutus an den Haaren aus den Eingewei-\n den der Erde herauszieht [Note: „Ἐλπιζούσης τῆς πλεονεξίας ἀνάξειν ἐϰ τῶν μυχῶν τῆς γῆς αὐτὸν τὸν\n Πλούτωνα.“ (Athen. Deipnos.)] , begrüsst im Goldgral die glänzende Incarna-\n tion ihres eigensten Lebensprinzips.","kind":"passage","sequence":160,"address":"3.160"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0161","text":"Die Waare als Gebrauchswerth befriedigt ein besondres Bedürfniss\n und bildet ein besondres Element des stofflichen Reichthums. Aber der\nWerth der Waare misst den Grad ihrer Attraktionskraft auf alle Ele-\n mente des stofflichen Reichthums, daher den gesellschaftlichen\n Reichthum ihres Besitzers. Dem barbarisch einfachen Waarenbesitzer,\n selbst einem westeuropäischen Bauer, ist der Werth unzertrennlich von der\n Werthform, Vermehrung des Gold- und Silberschatzes daher Werthver-\n mehrung. Allerdings wechselt der Werth des Geldes, sei es in Folge sei-\n nes eignen Werthwechsels, sei es des Werthwechsels der Waaren. Diess\n verhindert aber einerseits nicht, dass 200 Unzen Gold nach wie vor mehr\n Werth enthalten als 100, 300 mehr als 200 u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":161,"address":"3.161"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0162","text":"w., noch andrerseits\n dass die metallne Naturalform dieses Dings die allgemeine Aequivalent-\n form aller Waaren bleibt, die unmittelbar gesellschaftliche Incarnation\n aller menschlichen Arbeit. Der Trieb der Schatzbildung ist von Natur\n masslos. Qualitativ oder seiner Form nach ist das Geld schranken-\n los, d. h. allgemeiner Repräsentant des stofflichen Reichthums, weil in\n jede Waare unmittelbar umsetzbar. Aber zugleich ist jede wirkliche Geld-\n summe quantitativ beschränkt, daher auch nur Kaufmittel von be-\n schränkter Wirkung. Dieser Widerspruch zwischen der quantitativen\n Schranke und der qualitativen Schrankenlosigkeit des Geldes treibt den\n Schatzbildner stets zurück zur Sisyphusarbeit der Accumulation.\n Es geht ihm wie dem Welteroberer, der mit jedem neuen Land nur eine\n neue Grenze erobert.","kind":"passage","sequence":162,"address":"3.162"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0163","text":"Um das Gold als Geld festzuhalten und daher als Element der Schatz-\n bildung, muss es verhindert werden zu circuliren oder als Kaufmittel\n sich in Genussmittel aufzulösen. Der Schatzbildner opfert daher dem\n Goldfetisch seine Fleischeslust. Er macht Ernst mit dem Evangelium\n der Entsagung. Andrerseits kann er der Circulation nur in Geld entziehn,\n was er ihr in Waare giebt. Je mehr er producirt, desto mehr kann er\n verkaufen. Arbeitsamkeit, Sparsamkeit und Geiz bilden daher seine Kar-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"95\", \"facs\": \"#f0114\"} --> dinaltugenden, viel verkaufen, wenig kaufen, die Summe seiner politi-\n schen Oekonomie [Note: „Accrescere quanto più si può il numero de’ venditori d’ogni merce, di-\n minuire quanto più si può il numero dei compratori, questi sono i cardini sui quali\n si raggirano tutte le operazioni di economia politica.“ (Verri l. c. p. 52.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":163,"address":"3.163"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0164","text":"Neben der unmittelbaren Form des Schatzes läuft seine ästheti-\n sche Form, der Besitz von Gold- und Silberwaaren. Er wächst mit\n dem Reichthum der bürgerlichen Gesellschaft. „Soyons riches ou parais-\n sons riches.“ (Diderot.) Es bildet sich so theils ein stets ausgedehnterer\n Markt für Gold und Silber, unabhängig von ihren Geldfunktionen, theils\n eine latente Zufuhrquelle des Geldes, die namentlich in gesellschaftlichen\n Sturmperioden fliesst.","kind":"passage","sequence":164,"address":"3.164"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0165","text":"Die Schatzbildung erfüllt verschiedne Funktionen in der Oekonomie\n der metallischen Circulation. Die nächste Funktion entspringt aus den\n Umlaufsbedingungen der Gold- oder Silbermünze. Man hat gesehn, wie\n mit den beständigen Schwankungen der Waarencirculation in Umfang,\n Preisen und Geschwindigkeit die Umlaufsmasse des Geldes rastlos ebbt\n und fluthet. Sie muss also der Contraktion und Expansion fähig sein.\n Bald muss Geld als Münze attrahirt, bald Münze als Geld repellirt werden.\n Damit die wirklich umlaufende Geldmasse dem Sättigungsgrad der Circu-\n lationssphäre stets entspreche, muss das in einem Lande befindliche Gold-\n oder Silberquantum grösser sein als das in Münzfunktion begriffene. Diese\n Bedingung wird erfüllt durch die Schatzform des Geldes.","kind":"passage","sequence":165,"address":"3.165"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0166","text":"Die Schatz-\n reservoirs dienen zugleich als Abfuhr- und Zufuhrkanäle des cirkuliren-\n den Geldes, welches seine Umlaufskanäle daher nie überfüllt [Note: „There is required for carrying on the trade of the nation, a determinate\n sum of specifick Money, which varies, and is sometimes more, sometimes less, as\n the circumstances we are in require . . . . This ebbing and flowing of money,\n supplies and accommodates itself, without any aid of Politicians … The buckets\n work alternately; when money is scarce, bullion is coined; when bullion is scarce,\n money is melted.“ (Sir D. North l. c. p. 22.) John Stuart Mill,\n lange Zeit Beamter der ostindischen Compagnie, bestätigt, dass in Indien immer\n noch der Silberschmuck unmittelbar als Schatz functionirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":166,"address":"3.166"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0167","text":"Die „silver ornaments\n are brought out and coined when there is a high rate of interest, and go back\n again when the rate of interest falls.“ (J. St. Mill’s Evidence. Repts on\n Bankacts 1857, n. 2084.) Nach einem parlamentarischen Document von\n 1864 über Gold- und Silber-Import und Export in Indien, überstieg 1863 der] .","kind":"passage","sequence":167,"address":"3.167"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0168","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"96\", \"facs\": \"#f0115\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":168,"address":"3.168"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0169","text":"## b) Zahlungsmittel.","kind":"passage","sequence":169,"address":"3.169"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0170","text":"In der bisher betrachteten unmittelbaren Form der Waarencirkulation\n war dieselbe Werthgrösse stets doppelt vorhanden, Waare auf dem einen Pol,\n Geld auf dem Gegenpol. Die Waarenbesitzer traten daher nur in Contakt als\n Repräsentanten wechselseitig vorhandner Aequivalente. Mit der Entwicklung\n der Waarencirkulation entwickeln sich jedoch Verhältnisse, wodurch die Ver-\n äusserung der Waare von der Realisirung ihres Preises zeitlich getrennt\n wird. Es genügt die einfachsten dieser Verhältnisse hier anzudeuten. Die\n eine Waarenart erheischt längere, die andere kürzere Zeitdauer zu ihrer\n Produktion. Die Produktion verschiedner Waaren ist an verschiedne\n Jahreszeiten geknüpft. Die eine Waare wird auf ihrem Marktplatz gebo-\n ren, die andre muss zu entferntem Markt reisen. Der eine Waarenbesitzer\n kann daher als Verkäufer auftreten, bevor der andre als Käufer.","kind":"passage","sequence":170,"address":"3.170"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0171","text":"Bei\n steter Wiederkehr derselben Transactionen unter denselben Personen regeln\n sich die Verkaufsbedingungen der Waaren nach ihren Produktionsbedin-\n gungen. Der eine Waarenbesitzer verkauft vorhandne Waare, der andre\n kauft als blosser Repräsentant von Geld oder als Repräsentant von künf-\n tigem Gelde. Der Verkäufer wird Gläubiger, der Käufer Schuld-\n ner. Da die Metamorphose der Waare oder die Entwicklung ihrer\n Werthform sich hier verändert, erhält auch das Gold eine andre Funktion.\n Es wird Zahlungsmittel [Note: Luther unterscheidet zwischen Geld als Kaufmittel und Zahlungs-\n mittel. „Machest mir einen Zwilling aus dem Schadewacht, das ich hie nicht\nbezalen und dort nicht kauffen kann.“ (Martin Luther: „An die\n Pfarrherrn, wider den Wucher zu predigen. Wittenberg 1540.“)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":171,"address":"3.171"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0172","text":"Der Charakter von Gläubiger oder Schuldner entspringt hier aus\n der einfachen Waarencirkulation. Ihre Formveränderung drückt dem\n Verkäufer und Käufer diese neuen Stempel auf. Zunächst also sind es\n ebenso verschwindende und wechselweis von denselben Cirkulationsagenten\n gespielte Rollen wie die von Verkäufer und Käufer. Jedoch sieht der\n Gegensatz jetzt von Haus aus minder gemüthlich aus und ist grösserer\n [Note: Import von Gold und Silber den Export um 19,367,764 Pfd. St.. In den letzten 8\n Jahren vor 1864 betrug der Excess des Imports über den Export der edlen Me-\n talle 109,652,917 Pfd. St. Während dieses Jahrhunderts wurden weit über\n 200,000,000 Pfd. St. in Indien gemünzt.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"97\", \"facs\": \"#f0116\"} --> Krystallisation fähig [Note: Ueber die Schuldner- und Gläubigerverhältnisse unter den englischen\n Handelsleuten Anfang des 18.","kind":"passage","sequence":172,"address":"3.172"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0173","text":"Jahrhunderts: „Such a spirit of cruelty reigns\n here in England among the men of trade, that is not to be met with in any other\n society of men, nor in any other kingdom of the world.“ („An Essay on\n Credit and the Bankrupt Act. Lond. 1707“, p. 2.)] . Dieselben Charaktere können aber auch von\n der Waarencirculation unabhängig auftreten. Der Klassenkampf der an-\n tiken Welt z. B. bewegt sich hauptsächlich in der Form eines Kampfes\n zwischen Gläubiger und Schuldner, und endet in Rom mit dem Untergang\n des plebejischen Schuldners, der durch den Sklaven ersetzt wird. Im\n Mittelalter endet der Kampf mit dem Untergang des feudalen Schuldners,\n der seine politische Macht mit ihrer ökonomischen Basis einbüsst.","kind":"passage","sequence":173,"address":"3.173"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0174","text":"Indess\n spiegelt die Geldform — und das Verhältniss von Gläubiger und Schuld-\n ner besitzt die Form eines Geldverhältnisses — hier nur den Antagonis-\n mus tiefer liegender ökonomischer Lebensbedingungen wieder.","kind":"passage","sequence":174,"address":"3.174"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0175","text":"Kehren wir zur Sphäre der Waarencirculation zurück. Die gleich-\n zeitige Erscheinung der Aequivalente Waare und Geld auf den beiden\n Polen des Verkaufsprozesses hat aufgehört. Das Geld funktionirt jetzt\n erstens als Werthmass in der Preisbestimmung der verkauften Waare.\n Ihr kontraktlich festgesetzter Preis misst die Obligation des Käufers, d. h.\n die Geldsumme, die er an bestimmtem Zeittermin schuldet. Es funktio-\n nirt zweitens als ideelles Kaufmittel. Obgleich es nur im Geld-\n versprechen des Käufers existirt, bewirkt es den Händewechsel der Waare.\n Erst am fälligen Zahlungstermin tritt das Zahlungsmittel wirklich in Circu-\n lation, d. h. geht aus der Hand des Käufers in die des Verkäufers über.\n Das Circulationsmittel verwandelte sich in Schatz, weil der Circulations-\n prozess mit der ersten Phase abbrach oder die verwandelte Gestalt der\n Waare der Circulation entzogen wurde.","kind":"passage","sequence":175,"address":"3.175"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0176","text":"Das Zahlungsmittel tritt in\n die Circulation hinein, aber nachdem die Waare bereits aus ihr ausgetre-\n ten ist. Das Geld vermittelt nicht mehr den Prozess. Es schliesst ihn\n selbstständig ab, als absolutes Dasein des Tauschwerths oder allgemeine\n Waare. Der Verkäufer verwandelte Waare in Geld, um ein Bedürfniss\n durch das Geld zu befriedigen, der Schatzbildner, um die Waare in Geld-\n form zu präserviren, der schuldige Käufer, um zahlen zu können. Zahlt\n er nicht, so finden Zwangsverkäufe seiner Habe statt. Die Werthgestalt\n [Running matter: I. 7] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"98\", \"facs\": \"#f0117\"} --> der Waare, Geld, wird also jetzt zum Selbstzweck des Verkaufs\n durch eine den Verhältnissen des Circulationsprozesses selbst entspringende,\n gesellschaftliche Nothwendigkeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":176,"address":"3.176"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0177","text":"Der Käufer verwandelt Geld zurück in Waare, bevor er Waare in\n Geld verwandelt hat, oder vollzieht die zweite Waarenmetamorphose vor\n der ersten. Die Waare des Verkäufers circulirt, realisirt ihren Preis aber\n nur in einem privatrechtlichen Titel auf Geld. Sie verwandelt sich in Ge-\n brauchswerth, bevor sie sich in Geld verwandelt hat. Die Vollziehung\n ihrer ersten Metamorphose folgt erst nachträglich.","kind":"passage","sequence":177,"address":"3.177"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0178","text":"In jedem bestimmten Zeitabschnitt des Circulationsprozesses reprä-\n sentiren die fälligen Obligationen die Preissumme der Waaren, deren\n Verkauf sie hervorrief. Die zur Realisirung dieser Preissumme nöthige\n Geldmasse hängt zunächst ab von der Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit\n der Zahlungsmittel. Sie ist bedingt durch zwei Umstände: die\nVerkettung der Verhältnisse von Gläubiger und Schuldner, so dass A,\n der Geld von seinem Schuldner B erhält, es an seinen Gläubiger C fort-\n zahlt u. s. w. — und die Zeitlänge zwischen den verschiednen Zah-\n lungsterminen. Die prozessirende Kette von Zahlungen oder nachträg-\n lichen ersten Metamorphosen unterscheidet sich wesentlich von der früher\n betrachteten Verschlingung der Metamorphosenreihen. Im Umlauf des\n Circulationsmittels wird der Zusammenhang zwischen Verkäufern und Käu-\n fern nicht nur ausgedrückt.","kind":"passage","sequence":178,"address":"3.178"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0179","text":"Der Zusammenhang selbst entsteht erst\n in und mit dem Geldumlauf. Dagegen drückt die Bewegung des Zah-\n lungsmittels einen schon vor ihr fertig vorhandnen gesellschaftlichen Zu-\n sammenhang aus.","kind":"passage","sequence":179,"address":"3.179"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0180","text":"Gleichzeitigkeit und Nebeneinander der Verkäufe beschränken den Er-\n satz der Münzmasse durch Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit. Sie bilden umgekehrt\n einen neuen Hebel in der Oekonomie der Zahlungsmittel. Mit der Con-\n centration der Zahlungen an demselben Platz entwickeln sich naturwüch-\n sig eigne Anstalten und Methoden ihrer Ausgleichung. So z. B. die vire-\n ments im mittelaltrigen Lyon. Die Schuldforderungen von A an B, B an\n C, C an A u. s. w. brauchen bloss confrontirt zu werden, um sich wech-\n selseitig bis zu einem gewissen Belauf als positive und negative Grössen\n aufzuheben. So bleibt nur eine Schuldbilanz zu saldiren. Je massen-\n hafter die Concentration der Zahlungen, desto kleiner relativ die Bilanz,\n also die Masse der circulirenden Zahlungsmittel.","kind":"passage","sequence":180,"address":"3.180"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0181","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"99\", \"facs\": \"#f0118\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":181,"address":"3.181"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0182","text":"Die Funktion des Geldes als Zahlungsmittel schliesst einen unvermit-\n telten Widerspruch ein. So weit sich die Zahlungen ausgleichen, funktio-\n nirt es nur ideell als Rechengeld oder Mass der Werthe. Soweit\n wirkliche Zahlung zu verrichten, tritt es nicht als Circulationsmittel auf,\n als nur verschwindende und vermittelnde Form des Stoffwechsels, sondern\n als die individuelle Incarnation der gesellschaftlichen Arbeit, selbstständiges\n Dasein des Tauschwerths, absolute Waare. Dieser Widerspruch eklatirt\n in dem Moment der Produktions- und Handelskrisen, der Geldkrise\n heisst [Note: Die Geldkrise, wie sie im Text bestimmt ist, als Phase jeder Krise,\n ist wohl zu unterscheiden von der besondern Krisenart, die man auch\n Geldkrise nennt, die aber ein ganz selbstständiges Phänomen bilden kann, so dass\n sie auf Industrie und Handel nur rückschlagend wirkt.","kind":"passage","sequence":182,"address":"3.182"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0183","text":"Es sind diess Krisen,\n deren Bewegungscentrum das Geldkapital ist, und deren unmittelbare Sphäre daher\n auch die Sphäre der Haupt- und Staatsaktionen des Geldkapitals, Bank, Börse,\n Finanz.] . Sie ereignet sich nur, wo die prozessirende Kette der Zahlun-\n gen und ein künstliches System ihrer Ausgleichung völlig entwickelt sind.\n Mit allgemeineren Störungen dieses Mechanismus, woher sie immer ent-\n springen mögen, schlägt das Geld plötzlich und unvermittelt aus der nur\n ideellen Gestalt des Rechengeldes in hartes Geld um. Es wird unersetz-\n lich durch profane Waaren. Der Gebrauchswerth der Waare wird werth-\n los und ihr Werth verschwindet vor seiner eignen Werthform. Eben noch\n erklärte der Bürger in prosperitätstrunknem Aufklärungsdünkel das Geld\n für leeren Wahn. Nur die Waare ist Geld. Nur das Geld ist Waare!\n gellt’s jetzt über den Weltmarkt.","kind":"passage","sequence":183,"address":"3.183"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0184","text":"Wie der Hirsch schreit nach frischem\n Wasser, so schreit seine Seele nach Geld, dem einzigen Reichthum [Note: „Dieses plötzliche Umschlagen aus dem Kreditsystem in das Monetar-\n system fügt den theoretischen Schrecken zum praktischen Panik: und die Circu-\n lationsagenten schaudern vor dem undurchdringlichen Geheimniss ihrer eignen\n Verhältnisse.“ (Karl Marx l. c. p. 126.) „The Poor stand still, because\n the Rich have no Money to employ them, though they have the same land and\n hands to provide victuals and cloaths, as ever they had; which is the true Riches\n of a Nation, and not the Money.“ (John Bellers: „Proposals forrais-\n ing a Colledge of Industry. Lond. 1696“, p. 3.)] .\n In der Krise wird der Gegensatz zwischen der Waare und ihrer Werthgestalt,\n dem Geld, bis zum absoluten Widerspruch gesteigert. Die Erscheinungs-\n form des Geldes ist hier daher auch gleichgültig.","kind":"passage","sequence":184,"address":"3.184"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0185","text":"Die Geldhungersnoth\n [Running matter: 7*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"100\", \"facs\": \"#f0119\"} --> bleibt dieselbe, ob in Gold oder Creditgeld, Banknoten etwa, zu zah-\n len ist [Note: Wie solche Momente von den „amis du commerce“ ausgebeutet werden:\n „On one occasion (1839) an old grasping banker (der City) in his private room,\n raised the lid of the desk he sat over, and displayed to a friend rolls of banknotes,\n saying with intense glee there were 600,000 £ of them, they were held to\n make money tight, and would all be let out after three o’clock on the same\n day.“ („The Theory of the Exchanges. The Bank Charter Act\n of 1844. Lond. 1864“, p. 81.) Das halbofficielle Organ, „The Ob-\n server“, bemerkt am 24.","kind":"passage","sequence":185,"address":"3.185"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0186","text":"April 1864: „Some very curious rumours are current\n of the means which have been resorted to in order to create a scarcity\n of Banknotes … Questionable at it would seem, to suppose that any trick\n of the kind would be adopted, the report has been so universal that it really\n deserves mention.“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":186,"address":"3.186"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0187","text":"Betrachten wir nun die Gesammtsumme des in einem gegebenen\n Zeitabschnitt umlaufenden Geldes, so ist sie, bei gegebener Umlaufsge-\n schwindigkeit der Cirkulations- und Zahlungsmittel, gleich der Summe der\n zu realisirenden Waarenpreise plus der Summe der fälligen Zahlungen\n minus der sich ausgleichenden Zahlungen. Selbst Preise, Geschwindigkeit des\n Geldumlaufs, und Oekonomie der Zahlungen gegeben, decken sich daher\n nicht länger die während einer Periode, eines Tags z. B., umlaufende\n Geldmasse und cirkulirende Waarenmasse. Es läuft Geld um, das der\n Cirkulation längst entzogne Waaren repräsentirt. Es laufen Waaren um,\n deren Geldäquivalent erst in der Zukunft erscheint.","kind":"passage","sequence":187,"address":"3.187"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0188","text":"Andrerseits sind die\n jeden Tag contrahirten und die denselben Tag fälligen Zahlungen\n durchaus incommensurable Grössen [Note: „The amount of sales or contracts entered upon during the course of any\n given day, will not affect the quantity of money afloat on that particular day,\n but, in the vast majority of cases, will resolve themselves into multifarious drafts\n upon the quantity of money which may be afloat at subsequent dates more or less\n distant … The bills granted or credits opened, to day, need have no resemblance\n whatever, either in quantity, amount or duration, to those granted or entered upon\n to-morrow or next day; nay, many of to-day’s bills, and credits, when due, fall\n in with a mass of liabilities whose origins traverse a range of antecedent dates al-\n together indefinite, bills at 12, 6, 3 months or 1 often aggregating together to\n swell the common liabilities of one","kind":"passage","sequence":188,"address":"3.188"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0189","text":"particular day . . . .“ („The currency\n Question Reviewed; a letter to the Scotch people. By a Banker\n in England. Edinburgh 1845“, p. 29, 30 passim.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":189,"address":"3.189"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0190","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"101\", \"facs\": \"#f0120\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":190,"address":"3.190"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0191","text":"Das Creditgeld entspringt unmittelbar aus der Funktion des Geldes\n als Zahlungsmittel, indem Schuldcertificate für die verkauften Waaren selbst\n wieder zur Uebertragung der Schuldforderungen cirkuliren. Andrerseits,\n wie sich das Creditwesen ausdehnt, so die Funktion des Geldes als Zahlungs-\n mittel. Als solches erhält es eigne Existenzformen, worin es die Sphäre der\n grossen Handelstransaktionen behaust, während die Gold- oder Silbermünze\n hauptsächlich in die Sphäre des Kleinhandels zurückgedrängt wird [Note: Als Beispiel, wie wenig reelles Geld in die eigentlichen Handelsoperationen\n eingeht, folgt hier das Schema eines der grössten Londoner Handelshäuser über\n seine jährlichen Geldeinnahmen und Zahlungen. Seine Transaktionen im Jahr\n 1856, die viele Millionen Pf. St. umfassen, sind auf den Massstab einer Million\n verkürzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":191,"address":"3.191"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0192","text":"| \n\n\n\n (Report from the Select Committee on the Bankacts. July\n 1858, p. LXXI.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":192,"address":"3.192"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0193","text":"Bei gewissem Höhegrad und Umfang der Waarenproduktion greift die\n Funktion des Geldes als Zahlungsmittel über die Sphäre der Waarencircu-\n lation hinaus. Es wird die allgemeine Waare der Contrakte [Note: „The Course of Trade being thus turned, from exchanging of goods for\n goods, or delivering and taking, to selling and paying, all the bargains\n . . . . are now stated upon the foot of a Price in Money.“ („An Essay upon\n Publick Credit. 3ed. Lond. 1710“, p. 8.)] .\n Renten, Steuern u. s. w. verwandeln sich aus Naturallieferungen in Geld-\n zahlungen. Wie sehr diese Umwandlung durch die Gesammtgestalt des\n Produktionsprozesses bedingt wird, beweist z. B. der zweimal gescheiterte\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"102\", \"facs\": \"#f0121\"} --> Versuch des römischen Kaiserreichs alle Abgaben in Geld zu erheben.","kind":"passage","sequence":193,"address":"3.193"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0194","text":"Das ungeheure Elend des französischen Landvolks unter Ludwig XIV., das\n Boisguillebert, Marschall Vauban u. s. w. so beredt denunciren, war nicht\n nur der Steuerhöhe geschuldet, sondern auch der Verwandlung von Natu-\n ralsteuer in Geldsteuer [Note: „L’argent est devenu le bourreau de toutes les choses.“ Die Finanz-\n kunst ist das „alambic qui a fait évaporer une quantité effrayable de biens et de\n denrées pour faire ce fatal précis.“ „L’argent déclare la guerre à tout le genre\n humain.“ (Boisguillebert: „Dissertation sur la nature des\n richesses, de l’argent et des tributs“, edit. Daire, „Écono-\n mistes financiers. Paris 1843, t. I, p. 413, 419, 417.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":194,"address":"3.194"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0195","text":"Wenn andrerseits die Naturalform der Grund-\n rente, in Asien zugleich das Hauptelement der Staatssteuer, dort auf\n Produktionsverhältnissen beruht, welche sich mit der Unwandelbarkeit von\n Naturverhältnissen reproduciren, erhält jene Zahlungsform rückwirkend\n die alte Produktionsform. Sie bildet eines der Selbsterhaltungsgeheim-\n nisse des türkischen Reichs. Zieht der durch Europa aufoctroyirte aus-\n wärtige Handel in Japan die Verwandlung von Naturalrente in Geldrente\n nach sich, so ist es um seine musterhafte Agrikultur geschehn. Ihre engen\n ökonomischen Existenzbedingungen werden sich auflösen.","kind":"passage","sequence":195,"address":"3.195"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0196","text":"In jedem Land setzen sich gewisse allgemeine Zahlungster-\n mine fest. Sie beruhn theilweis, von andern Cirkelläufen der Reproduk-\n tion abgesehn, auf den an Wechsel der Jahreszeit gebundnen Naturbe-\n dingungen der Produktion. Sie regeln ebenso Zahlungen, die nicht direkt\n der Waarencirculation entspringen, wie Steuern, Renten u. s. w. Die\n Geldmasse, die zu diesen über die ganze Oberfläche der Gesellschaft zer-\n splitterten Zahlungen an gewissen Tagen des Jahres erheischt ist, verur-\n sacht periodische, aber ganz oberflächliche Perturbationen in der Oeko-\n nomie der Zahlungsmittel [Note: „Pfingstmontag 1824“, erzählt Herr Craig dem parlamentarischen Unter-\n suchungscomité von 1826, „war eine solche ungeheure Nachfrage für Banknoten\n in Edinburg, dass wir um 11 Uhr keine einzige Note mehr in unserm Verwahrsam\n hatten.","kind":"passage","sequence":196,"address":"3.196"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0197","text":"Wir sandten der Reihe nach zu den verschiednen Banken um welche\n zu borgen, konnten aber keine erhalten, und viele Transaktionen konnten nur\n durch slips of paper berichtigt werden. Um 3 Uhr Nachmittags jedoch waren\n bereits sämmtliche Noten returnirt zu den Banken, von denen sie ausliefen. Sie\n hatten nur die Hände gewechselt.“ Obgleich die effektive Durchschnittscirculation\n der Banknoten in Schottland weniger als 3 Mill. Pf. St. beträgt, wird dennoch, an ver-] . Aus dem Gesetz über die Umlaufsgeschwin-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"103\", \"facs\": \"#f0122\"} --> digkeit der Zahlungsmittel folgt, dass für alle periodischen Zah-\n lungen, welches immer ihre Quelle, die nothwendige Masse der\n Zahlungsmittel in umgekehrtem Verhältniss zur Länge\n der Zahlungsperioden steht [Note: Auf die Frage „if there were occasion to raise 40 millions p.","kind":"passage","sequence":197,"address":"3.197"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0198","text":"a., whether\n the same 6 millions (Gold) would suffice for such revolutions and circu-\n lations thereof as trade requires?“, antwortet Petty mit seiner gewöhnlichen\n Meisterschaft: „I answer yes: for the expense being 40 millions, if the revolu-\n tions were in such short circles, viz. weekly, as happens among\n poor artizans and labourers, who receive and pay every Saturday, then \\frac{40}{52} parts\n of 1 million of money would answer these ends; but if the circles be quar-\n terly, according to our custom of paying rent, and gathering taxes, then 10\n millions were requisite. Wherefore supposing payments in general to be of a\nmixed circle between one week and 12, then add 10 millions to \\frac{40}{52}, the\n half of the which will be 5½, so as if we have 5½ mill., we have enough.“\n (William Petty: „Political Anatomy of Ireland. 1672“, edit.\nLond. 1691, p. 13, 14.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":198,"address":"3.198"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0199","text":"Die Entwicklung des Geldes als Zahlungsmittel ernöthigt Geldaccu-\n mulationen für die Verfallstermine der geschuldeten Summen. Während\n die Schatzbildung als selbstständige Bereicherungsform verschwindet mit\n dem Fortschritt der bürgerlichen Gesellschaft, wächst sie umgekehrt mit\n demselben in der Form von Reservefonds der Zahlungs-\n mittel.","kind":"passage","sequence":199,"address":"3.199"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0200","text":"## c) Weltgeld.","kind":"passage","sequence":200,"address":"3.200"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0201","text":"Mit dem Austritt aus der innern Circulationssphäre streift das Geld\n die dort aufschiessenden Lokalformen vom Massstab der Preise, Münze,\n Scheidemünze und Werthzeichen wieder ab und fällt in die ursprüngliche\n Barrenform der edlen Metalle zurück. Im Welthandel entfalten die Waaren\n ihren Werth universell. Ihre selbstständige Werthgestalt tritt ihnen daher\n hier auch gegenüber als Weltgeld. Erst auf dem Weltmarkt funktio-\n [Note: schiednen Zahlungsterminen im Jahr, jede im Besitz der Banquiers befindliche\n Note, alles in allem ungefähr 7 Mill. Pf. St., in Aktivität gerufen. Bei diesen Ge-\n legenheiten haben die Noten eine einzige und specifische Funktion zu vollziehen\n und sobald sie vollzogen, fliessen sie zu den respektiven Banken zurück, von denen\n sie ausliefen.“ (John Fullarton: „Regulation of Currencies. 2nd\n ed. Lond. 1845“ p. 86 Nte.)","kind":"passage","sequence":201,"address":"3.201"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0202","text":"Zum Verständniss ist hinzuzufügen, dass in\n Schottland nicht cheques, sondern nur Noten für die Deposits ausgegeben werden.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"104\", \"facs\": \"#f0123\"} --> nirt das Geld in vollem Umfang als die Waare, deren Naturalform zugleich\n unmittelbar gesellschaftliche Verwirklichungsform der menschlichen\n Arbeit in abstracto ist. Seine Daseinsweise wird seinem Begriff\n adäquat.","kind":"passage","sequence":202,"address":"3.202"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0203","text":"In der innern Cirkulationssphäre kann nur eine Waare zum Werth-\n mass und daher als Geld dienen. Auf dem Weltmarkt herrscht dop-\n peltes Werthmass, Gold und Silber [Note: Daher die Abgeschmacktheit jeder Gesetzgebung, die den Nationalbanken\n vorschreibt, nur das edle Metall aufzuschatzen, das im Innern des Landes als Geld\n funktionirt. Die so selbstgeschaffenen „holden Hindernisse“ der Bank von Eng-\n land z. B. sind bekannt. Ueber die grossen historischen Epochen des relativen\n Werthwechsels von Gold in Silber sieh Karl Marx l. c. p. 136 sq.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":203,"address":"3.203"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0204","text":"Das Weltgeld funktionirt als allgemeines Zahlungsmittel,\n allgemeines Kaufmittel und absolut gesellschaftliche\n Materiatur des Reichthums überhaupt (universal wealth).\n Die Funktion als Zahlungsmittel, zur Ausgleichung internationaler\n Bilanzen, herrscht vor. Daher das Losungswort des Merkantilsystems —\nHandelsbilanz [Note: Die Gegner des Merkantilsystems, welches die Saldirung überschüssiger\n Handelsbilanz durch Gold und Silber als Zweck des Welthandels behandelte,\n verkannten ihrerseits durchaus die Funktion des Weltgeldes. Wie die falsche\n Auffassung der Gesetze, welche die Masse der Circulationsmittel regeln, sich in\n der falschen Auffassung der internationalen Bewegung der edlen Metalle nur\n wiederspiegelt, habe ich ausführlich an Ricardo nachgewiesen. (l. c. p. 150\n sqq.)","kind":"passage","sequence":204,"address":"3.204"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0205","text":"Sein falsches Dogma: „An unfavourable balance of trade never arises but\n from a redundant currency … The exportation of the coin is caused by its\n cheapness, and is not the effect, but the cause of an unfavourable balance“ findet\n man daher schon bei Barbon: „The Balance of Trade, if there be one, is\nnot the cause of sending away the money out of a nation: but that\n proceeds from the difference of the value of Bullion in every\n country.“ (N. Barbon l. c. p. 59, 60.) MacCulloch in „The Litte-\n rature of Political Economy, a classified catalogue. Lond.\n 1845“ belobt Barbon für diese Anticipation, vermeidet aber wohlweislich die\n naiven Formen, worin bei B. die absurden Voraussetzungen des „currency prin-\n ciple“ noch erscheinen, auch nur zu erwähnen.","kind":"passage","sequence":205,"address":"3.205"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0206","text":"Die Kritiklosigkeit und selbst Un-\n ehrlichkeit jenes Katalogs gipfeln in den Abschnitten über die Geschichte der Geld-\n theorie, weil Mac Culloch hier als Sykophant des Lord Overstone (ex-banker\n Loyd), den er „facile princeps argentariorum“ nennt, schwanzwedelt.] ! Zum internationalen Kaufmittel dienen Gold\n und Silber wesentlich, so oft das herkömmliche Gleichgewicht des Stoff-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"105\", \"facs\": \"#f0124\"} --> wechsels zwischen verschiednen Nationen plötzlich gestört wird. Endlich\n als absolut gesellschaftliche Materiatur des Reichthums, wo es sich weder\n um Kauf noch Zahlung handelt, sondern um Uebertragung des Reich-\n thums von einem Land zum andern, und wo diese Uebertragung in Waa-\n renform entweder durch die Conjunkturen des Waarenmarkts oder den\n zu erfüllenden Zweck selbst ausgeschlossen wird [Note: Z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":206,"address":"3.206"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0207","text":"bei Subsidien, Geldanleihn zur Kriegsführung oder zur Wiederauf-\n nahme der Baarzahlungen von Banken u. s. w. kann Werth grade in der Geldform\n erheischt sein.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":207,"address":"3.207"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0208","text":"Wie für seine innere Circulation, braucht jedes Land für die Welt-\n marktscirculation einen Reservefonds. Die Funktionen der Schätze ent-\n springen also theils aus der Funktion des Geldes als inneres Circulations-\n und Zahlungsmittel, theils seiner Funktion als Weltgeld. In der letz-\n teren Rolle ist stets die wirkliche Geldwaare, leibhaftes Gold und Sil-\n ber erheischt, wesswegen James Steuart Gold und Silber, im Unter-\n schied von ihren nur lokalen Stellvertretern, ausdrücklich als money\n of the world charakterisirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":208,"address":"3.208"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0209","text":"Die Bewegung des Gold- und Silberstroms ist eine doppelte. Einer-\n seits wälzt er sich von seinen Quellen über den ganzen Weltmarkt, wo er\n von den verschiednen nationalen Circulationssphären in verschiednem Um-\n fang abgefangen wird, um in die inneren Umlaufskanäle einzugehn, ver-\n schlissene Gold- und Silbermünzen zu ersetzen, das Material von Luxus-\n waaren zu liefern und zu Schätzen zu erstarren [Note: „L’argent se partage entre les nations relativement au besoin qu’elles en\n ont … étant toujours attiré par les productions.“ (Le Trosne l. c. p. 916.)\n „The mines which are continually giving gold and silver, do give sufficient to\n supply such a needful balance to every nation.“ (J. Vanderlint l. c. p. 40.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":209,"address":"3.209"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0210","text":"Diese erste Bewegung\n ist vermittelt durch direkten Austausch der in Waare realisirten National-\n arbeiten mit der in edlen Metallen realishten Arbeit der Gold und Silber\n producirenden Länder. Andrerseits laufen Gold und Silber fortwährend\n hin und her zwischen den verschiednen nationalen Circulationssphären, eine\n Bewegung, die den unaufhörlichen Oscillationen des Wechselkurses\n folgt [Note: „Exchanges rise and fall every week, and at some particular times in the\n year run high against a nation, and at other times run as high on the contrary.“\n (N. Barbon l. c. p. 39.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":210,"address":"3.210"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0211","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"106\", \"facs\": \"#f0125\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":211,"address":"3.211"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0212","text":"Länder entwickelter bürgerlicher Produktion beschränken die in Bank-\n reservoirs massenhaft concentrirten Schätze auf das zu ihren spezifischen\n Funktionen erheischte Minimum [Note: Diese verschiedenen Funktionen können in gefährlichen Konflikt gerathen,\n sobald die Funktion eines Conversionsfonds für Banknoten hinzutritt.] . Mit gewisser Ausnahme zeigt auf-\n fallendes Ueberfüllen der Schatzreservoirs über ihr Durchschnittsniveau\n Stockung der Waarencirculation an oder unterbrochenen Fluss der Waa-\n renmetamorphose [Note: „What money is more than of absolute necessity for a Home Trade, is\ndead stock, and brings no profit to that country it’s kept in, but as it is trans-\n ported in Trade, as well as imported.“ (John Bellers l. c. p. 12.) „What\n if we have too much coin?","kind":"passage","sequence":212,"address":"3.212"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0213","text":"We may melt down the heaviest and turn it into the\n splendour of plate, vessels or ustensils of gold and silver; or send it out as a\n commodity, where the same is wanted or desired; or let it out at interest, where\n interest is high.“ (W. Petty: „Quantulumcunque“, p. 39.) „Money\n is but the fat of the Body Politick, whereof too much does as often hinder its\n agility, as too little makes it sick. . . . . . . . as fat lubricates the motion of\n the muscles, feeds in want of victuals, fills up uneven cavities, and beautifies the\n body; so doth money in the state quicken its action, feeds from abroad in time\n of dearth at home; evens accounts … and beautifies the whole, although“, iro-\n nisch abschliessend, „more especially the particular persons that\n have it in plenty.“ (W. Petty: „Political anatomy of Ireland“ p. 14.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":213,"address":"3.213"}],"sha256":"74b04fcd4ce9bd59713d66a9adb364bc0e3cf8de9ced04cace65527cef86db55","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T201945608082Z_9179e5ea","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"3) Das Geld oder die Waarencirculation.","source_sha256":"74b04fcd4ce9bd59713d66a9adb364bc0e3cf8de9ced04cace65527cef86db55","sha256":"1cfa61b752af88a5c4ed8c4701400d219243e302e531965263aa9befe6f0d67b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:19:45.608100+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0001","text":"## A. Measure of Values.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"3.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0002","text":"Throughout this work, for the sake of simplification, I assume gold\n to be the money commodity.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"3.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0003","text":"By giving their general relative expression of value in gold, commodities\n cause gold to function, in relation to them, as the measure\n of values. Commodities are not rendered commensurable by money.\n On the contrary. Because all commodities, as values, are objectified\n human labour, and therefore commensurable in and for themselves,\n they can all measure themselves in some third commodity and thereby\n transform it into their common measure of value, or money. Money\n as measure of value is, however, the necessary phenomenal form of the\nimmanent measure of value of commodities, labour-time [Note: The question why money does not directly represent labour-time\n itself, so that, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"3.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0004","text":"a paper note represented x hours of labour,\n amounts quite simply to the question why, on the basis of commodity pro-\n duction, the products of labour must present themselves as commodities, for the repre-\n sentation of the commodity includes its duplication into commodity and money commodity.\n Or why private labour cannot be treated as directly social labour, as its\n opposite. I have discussed elsewhere in greater detail the shallow utopianism of\n “labour money” on the basis of commodity production.\n (l. c. p. 61 sqq.) Here it should merely be noted that, for example, Owen's\n “labour money” is no more “money” than a theatre token, say. Owen presupposes\nimmediately socialised labour, a form of production diametrically op-\n posed to commodity production.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"3.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0005","text":"The labour certificate merely certi-\n fies the producer's individual share in the common labour\n and his individual claim upon the portion of the common product intended for\nconsumption. But it never occurs to Owen to presuppose commodity production on the one\n hand and yet on the other to seek to evade its necessary conditions\n by monetary tinkering.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"3.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0006","text":"The simple relative expression of value of commodities in\nmoney — x commodity A = y money commodity — is their price. In their pri-\n ces commodities appear, firstly, as values, qualitatively equal,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"56\", \"facs\": \"#f0075\"} -->materialisations of the same labour, or the same materialisation of\n labour, gold; secondly, as quantitatively determinate magnitudes\n of value, for in the proportion in which they are equal to definite quantities of gold,\n they are equal to one another or represent equal quantities of labour. On the other\n hand, the developed relative expression of value, or the end-\n less series of relative expressions of value, becomes the specifically relative\n form of value of the money commodity. But this series is now already\n given in commodity prices. Read the quotations in a price cur-\n rent backwards, and one finds the magnitude of value of money represented in all pos-\n sible commodities. The series has also acquired a new meaning.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"3.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0007","text":"Gold, because it is money, already possesses in its natural form the general\n equivalent form, or the form of general immediate exchangeability,\n independently of its relative expressions of value. Its series now therefore\n represents, together with its magnitude of value, the developed world of\nmaterial wealth, or of use-values, into which it is immediately con-\n vertible. Money, on the other hand, has no price. In order to participate\n in this unitary relative form of value of the other commodities,\n it would have to be related to itself as its own equivalent.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"3.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0008","text":"For the movement of commodity prices, the laws previously given\n for the simple relative expression of value apply. Commodity prices\n can rise generally only, given an unchanged value of money, if commodity\n values rise; given unchanged commodity values, if the value of money falls.\n Conversely. Commodity prices can fall generally only, given an un-\n changed value of money, if commodity values fall; given unchanged commodity values,\n if the value of money rises. It by no means follows, therefore, that a rising value of\n money conditions a proportional fall in commodity prices, and a falling value of\n money a proportional rise in commodity prices. This holds only for commodities\n of unchanged value. Such commodities, for example, whose value rises\n uniformly and simultaneously with the value of money, retain the same prices.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"3.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0009","text":"If their value rises\n more slowly or more rapidly than the value of money, then the fall or rise\n in their prices is limited by the difference between their\n movement of value and that of money, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"3.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0010","text":"The commodity whose price is determined has a double form, real and\nrepresented or ideal. Its actual shape is that of an object of use,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"57\", \"facs\": \"#f0076\"} --> a product of concrete useful labour, e.g. iron. Its value-shape,\n its phenomenal form as materialisation of a definite quantity of homogeneous human\n labour, is its price, a quantity of gold. But gold is a thing different\n from iron, and in its price iron relates itself\n to gold as another thing which is, however, its equal in value.\n The price, or money-form, of the commodity exists only in this equation,\n thus, so to speak, only in its head, and its owner must put his tongue into\n its head or attach to it a paper label,\n in order to represent its price for the outer world [Note: The savage or semi-savage uses the tongue differently.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"3.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0011","text":"Captain\nParry remarks, for example, of the inhabitants on the west coast of Baffin Bay:\n “In this case (in the exchange of products) .... they licked it (the thing re-\n presented to them) twice to their tongues after which they seemed to consider the\n bargain satisfactorily concluded.” Similarly, among the eastern Eskimos,\n the exchanger each time licked the article upon receiving it. If the tongue\n is thus in the North the organ of appropriation, it is no wonder that the belly\n in the South counts as the organ of accumulated property, and that the Kaffir estimates a man's\n wealth by his fat paunch. The Kaffirs are exceedingly shrewd fellows,\n for while the British “Board of Health” in its report of 1864 lamented the lack of fat-forming\n substances among a large part of the working class, a Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"3.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0012","text":"Harvey, who, however, did not invent blood\n circulation, made his fortune in the same year by quack recipes which\n promised to rid bourgeoisie and aristocracy of the burden of an excess of fat] . The form of their\n value is therefore represented, ideal money-form, as distinct from the tangible real\n bodily form of their use-value. Since commodities thus express\n their values only ideally in money, they also express them in\n merely represented or ideal money. Money as measure of values is\n therefore only represented, ideal money. Every owner of commodities\n knows that he consumes no actual gold when he estimates commodities\n in gold or gives the value of a commodity the form of a commodity price.\n Although money now functions only ideally as measure of value,\n price nevertheless depends wholly upon the real money material. For\n a commodity, a ton of iron, e.g.,","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"3.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0013","text":"for instance, is related in its price, as the materialisation\n of a definite quantity of labour, to a definite quantity of money material\n as the materialisation of the same quantity of labour; but the same\n quantity of labour materialises itself in quite different quantities of gold, sil-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"58\", \"facs\": \"#f0077\"} --> ver, or copper. The value of a ton of iron thus receives quite dif-\n ferent expressions of price, according as gold, silver, or copper functions as the measure\n of value.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"3.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0014","text":"All commodities whose prices are determined represent themselves in the form:\n a commodity A = x gold; b commodity B = z gold, c commodity C = y gold etc.,\n where a, b, c are definite amounts of the kinds of commodities A, B, C, and x, z, y are defi-\n nite amounts of gold. The values of commodities are therefore trans-\n formed into represented quantities of gold of different magnitude,\n thus, despite the confused motley diversity of commodity bodies, into magnitudes\n of the same denomination, gold magnitudes. Different quantities of gold, because magnitudes\n of the same denomination, compare and measure themselves with one another by being related to a\nfixed quantity of gold as their unit of measure. This\n unit of measure itself is developed into a scale by further division into aliquot parts.\n Gold, silver, and copper already possessed such scales in their metallic weights\n before they became money. Their pre-existing metallic scale of weight\n therefore originally always serves also in their monetary function.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"3.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0015","text":"The money names of metallic weights, however, gradually separate\n from their original weight names for various\n reasons, among them historically decisive: 1) the introduction of foreign\n money among less developed peoples, as, for example, in ancient Rome silver\n and gold coins first circulated as foreign commodities. The names\n of this foreign money differ from the native weight names.\n 2) With the development of wealth, the less noble metal is displaced from the function\n of measure of value by the nobler one, copper\n by silver, silver by gold, however much this sequence may contradict all poetic\n chronology [Note: Besides, it is not of universal historical validity.] . The pound, for example, was then the money name\n for an actual pound of silver. As soon as gold displaces silver as measure of value,\n the same name perhaps attaches itself to \\frac{1}{15} etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"3.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0016","text":"of a pound\n of gold, according to the value relation of gold and silver. Pound as\n money name and as the ordinary weight name of gold are now sepa-\n rated. 3) The counterfeiting of money, continued over centuries, by\n princes, which in fact left of the original weight of the coins only the name.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"3.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0017","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"59\", \"facs\": \"#f0078\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"3.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0018","text":"These historical processes make the separation of the money name\n of metallic weights from their ordinary weight name a popular cus-\n tom. With the definitive regulation of the monetary scale, which is on the one hand purely\n conventional, and on the other requires legal universality and binding force,\n it finally becomes self-evident that the state authority\n fixes a definite weight-part of the precious metal, e.g. an ounce of gold, as\n the unit of weight and divides it into aliquot parts, to which it assigns arbi-\n trary legal baptismal names, such as pound, thaler, etc. Such an ali-\n quot part, which then counts as the actual unit of measure of money, is\n further divided and subdivided into other aliquot parts, which in turn\n receive legal baptismal names, such as shilling, penny, etc. As before,\n definite metallic weights remain the scale of metallic money.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"3.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0019","text":"What has\n changed is division and naming.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"3.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0020","text":"Commodities transform gold into the measure of values by\n expressing their values in it on all sides. Thus their magnitudes of value\n receive the form of prices or represented quantities of gold.\n Once this transformation of value into price has been accomplished, it becomes tech-\n nically necessary to determine the measure of values further into a\nscale of prices. The two functions are entirely distinct.\n As a scale of prices, a definite quantity of gold can and must be\nfixed, just as the scale of other magnitudes of the same denomination.\n The change in value of gold changes nothing in the value relation of its\n various weight-parts to one another, and the legally fixed baptismal\n names of these parts change nothing in their weight. The scale\n of prices, however, measures only different quantities of gold by a fixed quantity of gold,\n not the value of one quantity of gold by the weight\n of another.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"3.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0021","text":"The change in value of gold also does not prevent its func-\n tion as measure of value. It affects all commodities simultaneously and thus leaves,\n caeteris paribus, their reciprocal relative values unchanged,\n although they now all express themselves in higher or lower gold prices than be-\n fore.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"3.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0022","text":"Commodities now represent their values not merely as gold of the same denomination, but\n in the same socially valid accounting names of the gold\n scale, such as £ s. d. etc. Money serves as money of account\n whenever it is a matter of fixing a thing as value and therefore in money-form.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"3.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0023","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"60\", \"facs\": \"#f0079\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"3.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0024","text":"The name of a thing is entirely external to its nature, and there-\n fore its conceptual determination also vanishes in it. I know nothing of\n a human being if I know that a human being is called James. Likewise, in the money names\n pound, thaler, franc, ducat, etc., every trace of the value relation\n disappears. The confusion concerning the secret sense\n of these cabbalistic signs is all the greater because the money names simultaneously\n represent the value of commodities and aliquot parts of a weight of gold,\n the monetary scale, and represent the one only because they represent the other [Note: Cf. “Theories of the unit of measure of money” in “A Contribution\n to the Critique of Political Economy etc.”, p. 53 sqq.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"3.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0025","text":"The fantasies about raising or\n lowering the “coin price,” which consists in transferring the legal money-\n names for legally fixed weight portions of gold or silver to greater or\n smaller weight portions by state authority, and accordingly, for example,\n henceforth coining ¼ ounce of gold into 40 instead of 20 sh. — these fantasies, insofar\n as they do not aim at clumsy financial operations against state and private creditors, but rather at economic “miracle cures,” Petty treated so exhaustively in “Quantulumcumque concerning Money. To the Lord\n Marquis of Halifax. 1682,” that even his immediate successors,\nSir Dudley North and John Locke, not to speak of later writers,\n could only dilute him. “If the wealth of a nation,” he says, inter alia,","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"3.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0026","text":"A., “could\n be decupled by a Proclamation, it were strange that such proclamations have not\n long since been made by our Governors.” (l. c. p. 36.)] .\n On the other hand, it is necessary that value, as distinguished from the\n variegated bodies of commodities, should develop into this conceptless objective,\n but also simple social form.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"3.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0027","text":"Price is the money-name of the labour-time objectified\n in the commodity. The equivalence of the commodity and the quantity of money\n to which its price equates it is therefore a tautology [Note: “Ou bien, il faut consentir à dire qu’une valeur d’un million en\n argent vaut plus qu’une valeur égale en marchandises.” (Le Trosne\n l. c. p. 922), thus “qu’une valeur vaut plus qu’une valeur égale.”] , just as, indeed,\n the relative expression of value of a commodity is always the\n expression of the equivalence of two commodities. But if price, as exponent\n of the magnitude of value of the commodity, is the exponent of its exchange-\n relation with money, it does not follow conversely\n that the exponent of its exchange-relation with money is necessarily the expo-\n nent of its magnitude of value.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"3.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0028","text":"Socially necessary labour-time\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"61\", \"facs\": \"#f0080\"} --> of equal magnitude may be represented in 1 quarter of wheat and in £2\n (approximately ½ ounce of gold). The £2 are the money-expression of the\n magnitude of value of the quarter of wheat, or its price. If circumstances now\n permit it to be quoted at £3, or compel it to be quoted at £1,\n then £1 and £3, as expressions of the magnitude of value of\n wheat, are too small or too large, but they are nevertheless its prices,\n for, firstly, they are its value-form, money, and secondly, exponents\n of its exchange-relation with money. Given unchanged conditions of production\n or unchanged productive power of labour, the reproduction of the quarter of wheat\n still requires the expenditure of the same amount of social labour-time.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"3.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0029","text":"This circumstance\n depends neither on the will of the wheat producer nor on that of the other commo-\n dity owners. The magnitude of value of the commodity thus expresses a neces-\n sary relation, immanent in its process of formation, to social labour-time.\n With the transformation of the magnitude of value into\n price, this necessary relation appears as the exchange-relation of the\n commodity with another commodity existing outside it. This form,\n however, can express just as well the magnitude of value of the commodity as the accidental rela-\n tion in which it is alienable under given circumstances. The possibility of quantitative incongruence between\n price and magnitude of value, or of the divergence of price from magnitude of\n value, is therefore given in the price-form itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"3.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0030","text":"This is no\n defect of this form; on the contrary, it makes it the adequate form\n of a mode of production in which the rule can assert itself only as a blindly operating\n average law of lawlessness.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"3.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0031","text":"Yet the price-form does not merely allow for the possibility of quanti-\n tative incongruence between magnitude of value and price, i. e. between\n the magnitude of value and its own money-expression, but can harbour a\nqualitative contradiction, so that price ceases altogether\n to be an expression of value, although money is only the value-form\n of commodities. Things which are not in and for themselves commodities, e. g.\n conscience, honour, etc., may be alienable by their possessors for money,\n and thus receive the commodity-form through their price. A thing\n may therefore formally have a price without having a value.\n The expression of price is here imaginary, like certain magnitudes in mathe-\n matics or the “infinite judgment” of logic. Where, however, in the case of\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"62\", \"facs\": \"#f0081\"} --> essential relations of production we find such an imaginary price-form,\n as, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"3.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0032","text":"the price of land, although land,\n because no human labour is objectified in it, has no value either, deeper\n analysis will always find beneath the imaginary form a real relation of value\n or a relation derived from it.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"3.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0033","text":"Yet let us return to the normal commodity-price, which alone is as yet\n known to us here. Price is the merely ideal form of value\n of the commodity. It thus expresses at the same time that it does not yet\n possess a real form of value, or that its natural form is not its general\n equivalent-form. The ideal form of value of the commodity is further\nprice, i. e. merely imagined or ideal gold-form.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"3.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0034","text":"It\n thus expresses that, in order to exercise the effect of an exchange-value or general\n equivalent upon other commodities, it must cast off its natural body,\n transform itself from merely imagined gold into real gold,\n although this transubstantiation may come more “sourly” to it than\n the Hegelian “Concept” finds the transition from necessity to\n freedom, or a lobster the bursting of its shell, or the Church Father\n Jerome the casting off of the old Adam [Note: If Jerome in his youth had much to struggle with material flesh,\n as his desert struggle with images of beautiful women shows, then in old age he struggled\n with spiritual flesh. “I believed myself,” he says, for example, “in spirit before the\n Judge of the World. ‘Who are you?’ asked a voice. ‘I am a Christian.’ ‘You\n lie,’ thundered the Judge of the World. ‘You are only a Ciceronian!’”] .","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"3.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0035","text":"Alongside its\nreal form, iron for example, the commodity may possess in price an ideal form of\n value or imagined gold-form, but it cannot be at the same time\n really iron and really gold. For its pricing it is sufficient\n to equate imagined gold with it. It must be replaced by gold,\n in order that it may render its owner the service of a general\n equivalent. If, for example, the owner of iron confronted the owner\n of a worldly commodity, and referred him to the iron-\nprice, which was money-form, the worldly man would answer as,\n in Heaven, Saint Peter answered Dante, who had recited to him the formulas of faith:\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"63\", \"facs\": \"#f0082\"} -->“Assai bene è trascorsa\n D’esta moneta già la lega e’l peso,\n Ma dimmi se tu l’hai nella tua borsa.”","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"3.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0036","text":"The price-form includes the alienability of commodities for\n money and the necessity of this alienation. On the other hand, the price-determination\n of commodities has already made a commodity present in the exchange-process,\n gold, into money. Thus hard money lies in wait within the ideal measure\n of values.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"3.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0037","text":"## B. Means of circulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"3.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0038","text":"## a) The metamorphosis of commodities.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"3.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0039","text":"We saw that the exchange-process of commodities included contradictory\n and mutually exclusive relations. The development of the\n commodity, which we have just considered, does not abolish these contradictions, but\n it creates the form in which they can move. This is in general the method\n by which real contradictions are resolved. It is, for example, a contradiction\n that a body constantly falls into another and just as constantly flees from it.\n The ellipse is one of the forms of motion in which this contradiction is as much\n realized as resolved.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"3.39","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0040","text":"Insofar as the exchange-process of commodities transfers them from the hand\n in which they are non-use-values into the hand in which they are use-\n values, it is social metabolism.\n The product of one useful mode of labour replaces that of another.\n Once it has arrived at the place where it counts as a use-value, the commodity\n serves as an object of use or falls into the sphere of consumption from the\n sphere of commodity-exchange. The latter alone interests us here. We\n therefore have to consider the whole process from its formal side, that is, only\n the change of form or the metamorphosis of commodities,\n which mediates social metabolism.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"3.40","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0041","text":"The wholly defective conception of this change of form, of the func-\n tions of money, of the different determinations of form arising from it\n which money derives from its different functions, is, apart\n from lack of clarity about the concept of value itself, due to the circumstance\n that every change of form of a commodity presents itself in the exchange of two\n commodities, the commodity and the money-commodity. If one holds fast to this\nmaterial moment alone, the exchange of commodity and gold,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"64\", \"facs\": \"#f0083\"} --> one overlooks precisely what one ought to see, namely, what happens to the\nform.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"3.41","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0042","text":"One overlooks that the determination of gold as money\n is already a determination of form, which does not belong to it as a mere commodity,\n that the other commodities in their prices themselves relate to gold as their\nown money-form, and that gold, for its part, receives the general\n immediate equivalent-form only because commodities must in general give themselves\n a general relative form of value.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"3.42","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0043","text":"Commodities initially enter the exchange-process un-gilded, unsweetened,\n just as they have grown, as the comb has grown for them. It produces the dou-\n bling of the commodity into commodity and money, an external opposi-\n tion in which they represent their immanent opposition of use-value and\n exchange-value. In this opposition commodities confront money as\n use-values, and money as exchange-value. On the other hand,\n both sides of the opposition are commodities, hence unities\n of use-value and value. But this unity of differences presents itself\n inversely at each of the two poles and thereby at the same time presents\n their interrelation. The commodity is really a use-value; its existence as value\n appears only ideally in price, which relates it to the gold standing opposite\n as its real form of value. Conversely, the gold-material counts only as the\n materialization of value, money.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"3.43","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0044","text":"It is therefore really a general equivalent, exchange-\n value. Its use-value appears only ideally in the\n series of relative expressions of value in which it relates to the commodities standing opposite\n as the circumference of its real forms of use-\n value. These opposed forms of commodities are\n the real forms of motion of their exchange-pro-\n cess.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"3.44","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0045","text":"Let us now accompany some commodity-owner, our old acquaintance\n the weaver, for example, to the scene of the exchange-process, the commodity market.\n His commodity, 20 yards of linen, is price-determined. Its price is\n £2. He exchanges it for £2, and, a man of the old stamp,\n exchanges the £2 in turn for a family Bible of the same price. The linen,\n for him merely a commodity, a bearer of value, is alienated for gold, its\n form of value, and from this form re-alienated for another commodity,\n the Bible, which, however, is to travel as an article of use into the weaver’s house\n and there satisfy needs of edification.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"65\", \"facs\": \"#f0084\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"3.45","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0046","text":"The exchange-process of the commodity thus takes place in two\n opposed and mutually complementary metamor-\n phoses — transformation of the commodity into money and its trans-\n formation back from money into commodity [Note: “Ἐϰ δὲ τοῦ … πυϱὸς ἀνταμείβεσϑαι πάντα, φησὶν ὁ Ἡϱάϰλειτος,\n ϰαὶ πῦϱ ἁπάντων, ὥςπεϱ χϱυσοῦ χϱήματα ϰαὶ χϱημάτων χϱυσός.“\n (F. Lassalle: “The Philosophy of Heraclitus the Obscure.” Ber-\n lin 1858, Vol. I, p. 222.) Lassalle’s note on this passage, p. 224, n. 3, incorrectly explains money as a mere sign of value.] . The moments of commodity-\n metamorphosis are at the same time transactions of the commodity-owner — sale,\n exchange of commodity for money; purchase, exchange of money for commodity;\n and unity of both acts: selling in order to buy.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"3.46","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0047","text":"If the weaver now considers the final result of the transaction, he pos-\n sesses a Bible instead of linen, instead of his original commodity another\n of the same value but different usefulness. In the same way\n he appropriates his various means of life and production. From his standpoint,\n the whole process, the becoming-money of the linen and the becoming-\n commodity of the money, sale and purchase,\n merely mediates the exchange of his product of labour with another’s product of labour,\n the exchange of products.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"3.47","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0048","text":"The exchange-process of the commodity thus takes place in the following\n change of form:\nCommodity — Money — Commodity.\n C — M — C.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"3.48","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0049","text":"According to its material content, the movement is C — C, exchange\n of commodity for commodity, metabolism of social labour, in\n whose result the process itself is extinguished.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"3.49","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0050","text":"C — M. First metamorphosis of the commodity, or sale:\n The leaping of the commodity-value from the commodity-body into the gold-\n body is, as I have elsewhere called it, the commodity’s salto mortale.\n If it miscarries, it is indeed not the commodity that is cheated, but rather the commodity-\n owner. The social division of labour makes his labour\n as one-sided as it makes his needs many-sided. Precisely for this reason\n his product serves him only as exchange-value, general equiva-\n lent. General, socially valid equivalent-form it receives\n [Running matter: I. 5]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"66\", \"facs\": \"#f0085\"} --> only in money, however, and money is in another’s pocket.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"3.50","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0051","text":"In order to\n draw it out, the commodity must above all be use-value for the\n money-owner; the labour expended upon it must therefore have been expended in a socially\n useful form, or prove itself a member of the social\n division of labour. But the division of\n labour is a spontaneously evolved organism of production, whose\n threads were woven, and continue to be woven, behind the backs of the commodity-producers.\n Perhaps the commodity is the product of a new mode of labour\n that professes to satisfy a newly arisen need, or intends on\n its own initiative first to call forth a need. Yesterday still a func-\n tion among the many functions of one and the same commodity-producer,\n a particular labour-operation today perhaps tears itself loose from this\n connection, becomes independent, and precisely for that reason sends its\n part-product to market as an independent commodity.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"3.51","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0052","text":"The cir-\n cumstances may be ripe or unripe for this process of separation. The\n product today satisfies a social need. Tomorrow\n it may perhaps be displaced, wholly or in part, from its place by a similar kind of product.\n Even if the labour, like that of our weaver, is a\n duly certified member of the social division of labour, this by no means\n guarantees the use-value precisely of his 20 ells of linen.\n If the social need for linen—and it has its measure, like everything else—has already been satisfied\n by rival weavers, our friend’s product becomes superfluous, redundant, and thereby\n useless. One does not look a gift horse in the mouth, but it does not\n enter the market in order to make presents.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"3.52","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0053","text":"Suppose, however, that the use-\n value of his product proves itself, and money is therefore at-\n tracted by the commodity. But now the question is, how much money? The an-\n swer is indeed already anticipated in the price of the commodity, the exponent\n of its magnitude of value. We disregard any purely subjective errors of calcu-\n lation on the part of the commodity-owner, which are at once objectively corrected\n in the market. Let him have expended upon his product only the socially neces-\n sary average of labour-time. The price of the commodity is\n therefore only the money-name of the quantity of social labour objectified in it.\n But without permission and behind the back of our linen-\n weaver, the time-honoured conditions of production of weaving had begun to ferment.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"3.53","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0054","text":"What yesterday was unquestionably socially necessary la-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"67\", \"facs\": \"#f0086\"} --> bour-time for the production of an ell of linen, today ceases to\n be so, as the money-owner most zealously demonstrates from the price quotations\n of various rivals of our friend. To his misfortune there are\n many weavers in the world. Suppose, finally, that every piece of linen avail-\n able on the market contains only socially necessary labour-\n time. Nevertheless, the total sum of these pieces may contain superfluously ex-\n pended labour-time. If the market-stomach cannot ab-\n sorb the total quantity of linen at the normal price of 2 shillings per ell,\n this proves that too great a part of the total social labour-time\n was expended in the form of weaving.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"3.54","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0055","text":"The\n effect is the same as if every individual weaver had applied more than the so-\n cially necessary labour-time to his individual product.\n Here it is: in together, hanged together. All linen on\n the market counts only as one article of commerce, each piece only as an aliquot\n part. And in fact the value of every individual ell is\n only the materialization of the same socially determined quantity of homogeneous\n human labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"3.55","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0056","text":"One sees that the commodity loves money, but “the course of true love runs\n never smooth.” Just as spontaneously evolved and accidental as the qualitative is\n the quantitative articulation of the social organism of produc-\n tion, which represents its membra disjecta in the system of the division of labour.\n Our commodity-owners therefore discover that the same division\n of labour which makes them independent private producers, makes the\n social process of production and their relations within this pro-\n cess independent of themselves; that the independence\n of persons from one another is supplemented by a system of all-sided material\n dependence.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"3.56","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0057","text":"The division of labour transforms the product of labour into a commodity\n and thereby makes its transformation into money necessary. At the\n same time, it makes it accidental whether this transubstantiation succeeds.\n Here, however, the phenomenon is to be considered purely, and thus its normal course is to be presup-\n posed. If, moreover, it takes place at all, if the commodity is therefore not un-\n saleable, its change of form always takes place, although, abnormally,\n in this change of form substance — magnitude of value — may have been lost or\n added.","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"3.57","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0058","text":"To one commodity-owner gold replaces his commodity, and to the oth-\n [Running matter: 5*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"68\", \"facs\": \"#f0087\"} --> er commodity his gold. The perceptible phenomenon is the changing of hands\n or places of commodity and gold, of 20 ells of linen\n and 2 pounds sterling, i.e. their exchange. But for what does the\n commodity exchange itself? For its own general form of value. And for what does\n gold? For a particular form of its use-value. Why\n does gold confront linen as money? Because its price of\n 2 pounds sterling, or its money-name, already relates it to gold as money.\n The alienation of the original commodity-form is accomplished through\n the sale of the commodity, i.e. at the moment when its use-\n value really attracts the gold that is only ideally represented in its price.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"3.58","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0059","text":"The realization of the price, or of the merely ideal form of value of\n the commodity, is therefore at the same time, conversely, realization of the merely ideal use-\n value of money; the transformation of commodity into money is at the same time\n transformation of money into commodity. The one process is a two-sided\n process: from the pole of the commodity-owner, sale; from the counter-pole of the\n money-owner, purchase. Or sale is purchase, C — M at the same time\n M — C [Note: “Toute vente est achat” (Dr. Quesnay: “Dialogues sur le Com-\n merce et les Travaux des Artisans”. Physiocrates, ed. Daire,\n I. Partie, Paris 1846, p. 170), or, as Quesnay says in his “Maximes\n Générales”: “Vendre est acheter.”] .","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"3.59","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0060","text":"Thus far we know no other economic relation of men to one another\n than that of commodity-owners, a relation in which\n they appropriate another’s product of labour only by alienating their own.\n One commodity-owner can therefore confront another only as money-owner—\n either because his product of labour naturally possesses the money-form,\n and is thus money-material, gold, etc., or because his own commodity has already\n shed its skin and cast off its original use-form. In order to function as money,\n gold must of course enter the commodity-\n market at some point. This point lies at its source of production, where it\n exchanges itself as an immediate product of labour for another product of labour of the same\n value.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"3.60","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0061","text":"But from that moment it functions only as\n actual money, because it is constantly realized commodity-price [Note: “Le prix d’une marchandise ne pouvant pas être payé que par le prix\n d’une autre marchandise.” (Mercier de la Rivière: “L’Ordre na-] .\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"69\", \"facs\": \"#f0088\"} --> Apart from the exchange of gold for commodity at its source of production,\n gold in the hand of every commodity-owner is the alienated form of his al-\n ienated commodity, product of sale or of the first commodity-metamorphosis\n C — M [Note: “Pour avoir cet argent, il faut avoir vendu.” (l. c. p. 543.)] . Gold became\n ideal money or measure of value because all commodities measured their values in it and thereby\n ideally made it into their alienated use-form or form of\n value.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"3.61","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0062","text":"It becomes real money because the commodities, through their\n all-sided alienation, make it into their actually alienated or transformed\n use-form and hence into their actual form of value.\n In its value-form the commodity strips off every trace of its spontaneously evolved\n use-value and of the particular useful labour to which it owes\n its origin, in order to pupate into the uniform social materialization\n of undifferentiated human labour. One cannot therefore see from money\n what sort the commodity transformed into it was. One commodity looks\n in its money-form exactly like another. Money may therefore be dirt,\n although dirt is not money. Let us assume that the two gold sovereigns\n for which our linen-weaver alienates his commodity are the trans-\n formed form of a quarter of wheat. The sale of the linen,\n C — M, is at the same time its purchase, M — C.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"3.62","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0063","text":"But as sale of the\n linen this process begins a movement that ends with its oppo-\n site, with the purchase of the Bible; as purchase of the linen\n it ends a movement that began with its opposite, with the\n sale of wheat. C — M (linen — money), the first\n phase of C — M — C (linen — money — Bible), is at the same time\n M — C (money — linen), the final phase of another movement\n C — M — C (wheat — money — linen). The first meta-\n morphosis of a commodity, its transformation from commodity-form into\n money, is always at the same time the second, opposite metamorphosis\n of another commodity, its retransformation from money-form into\n commodity [Note: The exception, as previously remarked, is formed by the producer of gold or silver,\n who exchanges his product without having previously sold it.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"3.63","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0064","text":"[Note: turel et essentiel des sociétés politiques”. Physiocrates, ed.\n Daire, II. Partie, p. 554.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"3.64","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0065","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"70\", \"facs\": \"#f0089\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"3.65","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0066","text":"M — C. Second or concluding metamorphosis of the\n commodity. Purchase. — Because it is the alienated form of all other commodities,\n or the product of their universal alienation, money is the absolutely\n alienable commodity. It reads all prices backwards and thereby mirrors\n itself in all commodity-bodies as the yielding material of its own\n becoming-commodity. At the same time the prices, the amorous eyes\n with which the commodities beckon to it, show the limit of its capacity for transformation—\n namely, its own quantity. Since the commodity disappears in its becoming-\n money, one cannot see from money how it came into the hands\n of its possessor or what has been transformed into it. Non olet, whatever\n its origin.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"3.66","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0067","text":"As alienated form it stands opposed to the\n commodities; the world of commodities stands opposed to it as the absolutely alienable\n commodity-form [Note: “Si l’argent représente, dans nos mains, les choses que nous pouvons\n désirer d’acheter, il y représente aussi les choses que nous avous vendues\n pour cet argent.” (Mercier de la Rivière l. c. p. 586.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"3.67","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0068","text":"M — C, purchase, is at the same time sale, C — M; the final meta-\n morphosis of one commodity is therefore at the same time the first metamorphosis of another\n commodity. For our linen-weaver, the life-course of his commodity\n closes with the Bible, into which he has retransformed the 2 pounds sterling. But the orig-\n inal owner of the Bible converts the 2 pounds sterling received from\n the linen-weaver into grain-brandy, M — C; the concluding phase of C — M — C (lin-\n en — money — Bible) is at the same time C — M, the first phase of C —\n M — C (Bible — money — grain-brandy). Since the commodity-producer\n supplies only a one-sided product, he often sells it in larger quan-\n tities, while his many-sided needs compel him constantly to split the realized\n price, or the money-sum obtained, into numerous purchases. A sale\n therefore issues in many purchases of different commodities.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"3.68","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0069","text":"The concluding metamorphosis of a commodity thus forms a sum\n of first metamorphoses of other commodities.","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"3.69","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0070","text":"Let us now consider the total metamorphosis of a commodity,\n e.g. of linen. We see first that it consists of two opposite\n and mutually complementary movements, C — M and M —\n C. The two opposite transformations are accomplished in two op-\n posite social processes of the commodity-owner and are reflected in two opposite economic characters of the same\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"71\", \"facs\": \"#f0090\"} --> person. As agent of sale he becomes seller; as agent of purchase,\n buyer. But just as in every transformation of the commodity its two forms,\n commodity-form and money-form, simultaneously exist, only at opposite\n poles, so the same commodity-owner as seller confronts another\n buyer, and as buyer another seller.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"3.70","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0071","text":"Just as the same\n commodity successively passes through the two reversed transformations,\n becomes money from commodity and commodity from money, so the same commodity-owner changes the\n roles of seller and buyer. These are therefore not fixed charac-\n ters, but characters that constantly change persons in the exchange-process of\n commodities.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"3.71","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0072","text":"The total metamorphosis of a commodity presupposes, in its simplest form, four extremes and three dramatis personae. First, money confronts the commodity as its alienated form, possessing materially hard reality beyond it, in another’s pocket. Thus the commodity-owner is confronted by a money-owner. As soon as the commodity is transformed into money, the latter becomes its vanishing equivalent-form, whose use-value or content exists on this side in other commodity-bodies. As the endpoint of the first transformation of the commodity, money is at the same time the point of departure of the second. Thus the seller in the first act is buyer in the second, where a third commodity-owner confronts him as seller [Note: “There are therefore four terms and three contracting parties, one of whom intervenes twice.” (Le Trosne, l. c., p. 908.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"3.72","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0073","text":"The two inverse phases of movement of the commodity metamorphosis form a circuit: commodity-form, shedding of commodity-form, return to commodity-form. To be sure, the commodity itself is here determined oppositely: at the point of departure, non-use-value; at the endpoint, use-value for its owner, just as money first represents the commodity as a fixed crystal of value, in order afterwards to melt away as its mere equivalent-form.","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"3.73","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0074","text":"The two metamorphoses which form the circuit of one commodity are at the same time the inverse partial metamorphoses of two other commodities. The same commodity (linen) opens the series of its own metamorphoses and closes the total metamorphosis of another commodity (wheat). During its first transformation, the sale, it plays these two roles in its own person. <!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"72\", \"facs\": \"#f0091\"} --> As a gold chrysalis, on the other hand, in which it itself travels the way of all flesh, it at the same time ends the first metamorphosis of a third commodity. The circuit formed by the series of metamorphoses of each commodity thus becomes inextricably intertwined with the circuits of other commodities. The total process presents itself as commodity circulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"3.74","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0075","text":"Commodity circulation is distinguished not only formally, but essentially, from direct exchange of products. One need only cast a backward glance at the process. The linen-weaver has unconditionally exchanged linen for a Bible, his own commodity for another’s. But this phenomenon is true only for him. The Bible agent, who prefers hot to cool, had no thought of exchanging linen for Bibles, just as the linen-weaver does not know that wheat has been exchanged for his linen, and so on. B’s commodity replaces A’s commodity, but A and B do not reciprocally exchange their commodities. It may indeed happen that A and B buy from one another in turn, but such a particular relation is by no means conditioned by the general relations of commodity circulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"3.75","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0076","text":"On the one hand, one sees here how commodity exchange breaks through the individual and local barriers of direct exchange of products and develops the metabolism of human labour. On the other hand, a whole circle of social natural connexions develops which are uncontrollable by the acting persons. The weaver can sell linen only because the peasant sells wheat, Hotspur the Bible only because the weaver sells linen, the distiller only fiery water because the other has already sold the water of eternal life, and so on.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"3.76","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0077","text":"For this reason, too, the process of circulation does not expire, like direct exchange of products, in the changing of place—or of hands—of use-values. Money does not disappear because it finally drops out of the series of metamorphoses of a commodity. It always precipitates itself upon a place in circulation vacated by commodities. For example, in the total metamorphosis of linen: linen — money — Bible, first linen drops out of circulation, money takes its place; then the Bible drops out of circulation, money takes its place. The replacement of commodity by commodity simultaneously leaves the money-commodity hanging in a third hand. Circulation continually sweats out money.","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"3.77","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0078","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"73\", \"facs\": \"#f0092\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"3.78","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0079","text":"Nothing can be more absurd than the dogma that commodity circulation conditions a necessary equilibrium of sales and purchases, because every sale is a purchase and vice versa. If this means that the number of sales actually completed equals the same number of purchases, it is flat tautology. But it is meant to prove that the seller brings his own buyer to market. Sale and purchase are an identical act as the reciprocal relation of two polarly opposite persons, the commodity-owner and the money-owner. They form two polarly opposite acts as actions of the same person. The identity of sale and purchase therefore includes the fact that the commodity becomes useless if, thrown into the alchemical retort of circulation, it does not emerge as money, is not sold by the commodity-owner and thus bought by the money-owner.","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"3.79","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0080","text":"That identity further contains the fact that the process, if it succeeds, forms a resting-point, a section of the commodity’s life, which may last longer or shorter. Since the first metamorphosis of the commodity is at the same time sale and purchase, this partial process is at the same time an independent process. The buyer has the commodity, the seller has the money, i.e. a commodity which preserves the form capable of circulation, whether it reappears on the market sooner or later. No one can sell unless another buys. But no one need buy immediately because he has himself sold. Circulation bursts the temporal, local, and individual barriers of exchange of products precisely by splitting the immediate identity existing here between the exchange of one’s own labour-product and the acquisition by exchange of another’s labour-product into the antithesis of sale and purchase.","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"3.80","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0081","text":"That the processes which independently confront one another form an inner unity means just as much that their inner unity moves in external oppositions. If the external independence of that which is inwardly non-independent, because complementary to one another, proceeds to a certain point, the unity forcibly asserts itself through a — crisis. The contradiction immanent in the commodity between use-value and exchange-value, between private labour, which must at the same time represent itself as immediately social labour, between particular concrete labour, which at the same time counts only as abstract universal labour, between personification of the thing and reification of persons—this immanent contradiction receives its developed forms of motion in the oppositions of <!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"74\", \"facs\": \"#f0093\"} --> commodity metamorphosis.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"3.81","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0082","text":"These forms therefore include the possibility, but only the possibility, of crises. The development of this possibility into actuality requires a whole complex of relations which, from the standpoint of simple commodity circulation, do not yet exist at all [Note: Compare my remarks on James Mill: “A Contribution to the Critique etc.,” pp. 74—76. Two points are characteristic here of the method of economistic apologetics. First, the identification of commodity circulation and direct exchange of products by simple abstraction from their differences. Second, the attempt to deny away the contradictions of the capitalist process of production by resolving the relations of its agents of production into the simple relations which arise from","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"3.82","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0083","text":"commodity production and commodity circulation. But commodity production and commodity circulation are phenomena which belong to the most diverse modes of production, though in differing extent and scope. Thus one knows nothing as yet of the differentia specifica of these modes of production, and can therefore not judge them, if one knows only the abstract categories of commodity circulation common to them. In no other science than political economy does such pretentiousness with elementary commonplaces prevail. For example, J. B. Say ventures to pass judgment on crises because he knows that the commodity is a product.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"3.83","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0084","text":"As mediator of commodity circulation, money acquires the function of means of circulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"3.84","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0085","text":"## b) The circulation of money.","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"3.85","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0086","text":"The change of form in which the metabolism of labour-products takes place, C — M — C, conditions the same value, as commodity, to form the point of departure of the process and to return to the same point as commodity. This movement of commodities is therefore a circuit. On the other hand, the same form excludes the circuit of money. Its result is the continual removal of money from its point of departure, not return to it. So long as the seller retains the transformed shape of his commodity, the money, the commodity is in the stage of the first metamorphosis, or has completed only its first half of circulation. If the process, selling in order to buy, is completed, the money has also again been removed from the hand of its original possessor.","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"3.86","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0087","text":"Certainly, if the linen-weaver, after buying the Bible, sells linen anew, <!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"75\", \"facs\": \"#f0094\"} --> the money returns to his hand. But it does not return through the process of circulation of the first 20 ells of linen. Rather, he has removed it from the hands of the linen-buyer into those of the Bible-seller. It returns only through the renewal or repetition of the same process of circulation for new commodity, and ends here as there with the same result. The form of motion directly imparted to money by commodity circulation is therefore its continual removal from the point of departure, its course from the hand of one commodity-owner into that of another, or its currency (currency, cours de la monnaie).","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"3.87","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0088","text":"The currency of money displays continual, monotonous repetition of the same process. The commodity always stands on the side of the seller, money always on the side of the buyer, as means of purchase. It functions as means of purchase by realizing the price of the commodity. In realizing it, it transfers the commodity from the hand of the seller into that of the buyer, while simultaneously removing itself from the hand of the buyer into that of the seller, in order to repeat the same process with another commodity. That this one-sided form of the movement of money arises from the two-sided movement of form of the commodity is concealed. The nature of commodity circulation itself generates the opposite appearance. The first metamorphosis of the commodity is visible not only as a movement of money, but as its own movement; but its second metamorphosis is visible only as a movement of money.","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"3.88","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0089","text":"In its first half of circulation, the commodity changes places with money. At the same time, its use-form drops out of circulation into consumption [Note: Even if the commodity is sold again and again, a phenomenon which does not yet exist for us here, with the last definitive sale it drops out of the sphere of circulation into that of consumption, in order to serve here as means of subsistence or as means of production.] . Its value-form or money-mask takes its place. It no longer traverses the second half of circulation in its own natural skin, but in its gold skin.","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"3.89","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0090","text":"The continuity of movement thereby falls wholly on the side of money, and the same movement which includes two opposite processes for the commodity includes, as money’s own movement, always the same process, its change of place with ever different commodity. The result of commodity circulation, replacement of commodity by another commodity, thus appears mediated not through its own change of form, <!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"76\", \"facs\": \"#f0095\"} --> but through the function of money as means of circulation, which circulates the commodities in themselves motionless, transfers them from the hand in which they are non-use-values to the hand in which they are use-values, always in the opposite direction to its own course.","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"3.90","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0091","text":"It continually removes commodities from the sphere of circulation, by continually taking their place in circulation and thereby removing itself from its own point of departure. Although the movement of money is therefore only the expression of commodity circulation, conversely commodity circulation appears only as the result of the movement of money [Note: “It [money] has no movement other than that which is imparted by productions.” (Le Trosne, l. c., p. 885.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"3.91","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0092","text":"On the other hand, money has the function of means of circulation only because it is the independently existing value-form of commodities. Its movement as means of circulation is therefore in fact only their own movement of form. This must therefore also be sensuously reflected in the currency of money. The double change of form of the commodity is reflected in the twice-repeated change of place of the same piece of money, if we consider the total metamorphosis of one commodity; in the multiple repetition of its change of place, if we consider the intertwining of innumerable metamorphoses with one another. The same pieces of money come into the hand of the seller as the alienated form of the commodity and leave it as the absolutely alienable form of the commodity. Money acts in the same way as means of purchase, each time in relation to another commodity, in both processes.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"3.92","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0093","text":"But its\n inner connexion, or the double determination of form which it has in both\n processes for the same commodity, appears in the double and opposite\n movement impressed upon the same pieces of money.\nThe same £2 which, in the sale of the linen, migrate from the pocket\n of the wheat farmer into the pocket of the linen weaver, migrate from\n the latter in the purchase of the Bible. It is a double change of place, and,\n the linen or its representative being regarded as the centre, in opposite\n directions, positive in the receipt of the money, negative in its expendi-\n ture. If, on the other hand, only one-sided commodity metamorphoses\n take place, mere sales or mere purchases, as one will, then the same\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"77\", \"facs\": \"#f0096\"} --> money also changes place only once.","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"3.93","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0094","text":"Its second change of place\n always expresses the second metamorphosis of the commodity, its retrans-\n formation from money. It is, moreover, quite self-evident that all this\n applies only to the form of simple commodity circulation considered here.","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"3.94","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0095","text":"Every commodity, upon its first step into circulation, its first\n change of form, drops out of circulation, while fresh commodity continually\n enters it. Money, on the other hand, as a means of circulation, dwells\n constantly in the sphere of circulation and constantly moves about within it. There\n thus arises the question of how much money this sphere constantly absorbs.","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"3.95","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0096","text":"In a country, every day, simultaneously, and thus spatially side by\n side, an enormous number of one-sided commodity metamorphoses takes place, or\n an innumerable mass of scattered sales, which the commodity owners indepen-\n dent of one another carry out. In their prices, the commodities have\n already been equated with definite imagined quantities of money. Since the\n immediate form of circulation considered here always confronts commodity and\n money bodily with one another, the one at the pole of sale, the other\n at the opposite pole of purchase, the mass of means of circulation required\n for the process of circulation of the commodity world is already determined\n by the sum of the prices of the commodities. In fact, money merely represents\n in reality the sum of gold already ideally expressed in the sum of the prices\n of the commodities. The equality of these sums is therefore self-evident.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"3.96","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0097","text":"We\n know, however, that with the values of commodities remaining unchanged their prices\n change with the value of gold (the money material) itself, rising propor-\n tionately when it falls, and falling when it rises. Whether the sum of the\n prices of commodities rises or falls in this manner, the mass of circulating money\n must rise or fall correspondingly. The change in the mass of\n means of circulation admittedly arises here from money itself, but\nnot from its function as a means of circulation, but from\n its function as measure of value. The price of\n commodities changes first inversely with the value of money,\n and then the mass of means of circulation changes directly with the price\n of commodities. Exactly the same phenomenon would occur if, e.g.","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"3.97","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0098","text":"the value of gold did not fall, but silver re-\n placed it as measure of value, or if the value of silver did not rise, but\n gold displaced it from the function of measure of value. In the one case,\n more silver would have to circulate than gold did previously; in the other,\n less gold than silver did previously.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"78\", \"facs\": \"#f0097\"} --> In both cases the value of the money material\n would have changed, i.e. of the commodity which functions as measure\n of values, hence the price expression of commodity values, hence the mass\n of circulating money which serves for the realization of these prices. We have seen\n that the sphere of circulation of commodities has a hole through which gold (sil-\n ver, in short the money material) enters it as a commodity of given\n value. This value is presupposed in the function of money\n as measure of value, and thus in the determination of price. If now, e.g.","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"3.98","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0099","text":"the value\n of the measure of value itself falls, this appears first in the change of price of\n commodities which are exchanged with them as commodities directly at the\n sources of production of the precious metals. Especially in less developed\n conditions of bourgeois society, a great part of the other commodities will\n still for a longer time be estimated in the now illusory, obsolete value of\n the measure of value. Yet one commodity infects the other through its relative\n relation of value to it; the gold or silver prices of commodities gradually\n equalize themselves in the proportions determined by their values themselves, until finally\n all commodity values are estimated in accordance with the new value of the money metal.","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"3.99","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0100","text":"This process of equalization is accompanied by the continual\n growth of the precious metals, which flow in as replacement for the commodities\n exchanged directly with them. Therefore, in the same measure\n in which the corrected pricing of commodities becomes general,\n or their values are estimated in accordance with the new, fallen, and to a certain point\n still falling value of the metal, the additional mass necessary for their\n realization is already present. One-sided observation of the facts which followed\n the discovery of the new sources of gold and silver misled people in the 17th and especially\n in the 18th century into the false conclusion that commodity prices\n had risen because more gold and silver functioned as means of circulation. In what follows,\n the value of gold is presupposed as given, as it is in fact given at the\n moment of price estimation.","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"3.100","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0101","text":"Under this presupposition, then, the mass of means of circulation\n is determined by the sum of commodity prices to be realized. Let us\n further presuppose the price of each kind of commodity as given; then\n the sum of the prices of commodities obviously depends upon the mass of commodities\n in circulation. It takes little head-scratching to\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"79\", \"facs\": \"#f0098\"} --> understand that if 1 quarter of wheat costs £2, 100 quarters £200,\n 200 quarters £400, etc., then with the mass of\n wheat the mass of money must grow which changes place with it in the sale.","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"3.101","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0102","text":"The mass of commodities being presupposed as given, the mass\n of circulating money ebbs and flows with the fluctuations in the prices of\n commodities. It rises and falls because the sum of the prices of commodities\n increases or decreases as a consequence of their change of price. For this it is by no means necessary\n that the prices of all commodities should rise or fall simultaneously. The rise in price\n of a certain number of leading articles in the one case, or their\n fall in price in the other, suffices to raise or lower the sum of prices to be realized\n of all circulating commodities, and thus also to put more or less money\n into circulation. Whether the change in price of commodities reflects actual\n changes in value or mere fluctuations of market prices, the effect upon the mass of means of circula-\n tion remains the same.","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"3.102","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0103","text":"Let there be given a number of unconnected, simultaneous and\n thus spatially side-by-side sales or partial metamorphoses, e.g. of 2 quarters\n of wheat, 20 ells of linen, 1 Bible, 4 gal-\n lons of corn brandy. If the price of each article is £2, the sum of prices\n to be realized therefore £8, then a mass of money of\n £8 must enter circulation. If, on the other hand, the same commodities form\n links in the sequence of metamorphoses known to us: 1 quarter of wheat —\n £2 — 20 ells of linen — £2 — 1 Bible — £2\n — 4 gallons of corn brandy — £2, then the same\n £2 circulate through the various commodities one after another, realizing their\n prices one after another, and thus also the sum of prices of £8, in order finally\n to rest in the hand of the distiller. They accomplish\nfour circuits.","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"3.103","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0104","text":"This repeated change of place by the same pieces of money\n represents the double change of form of the commodity, its movement through\n two opposite stages of circulation, and the interlacing of the meta-\n morphoses of different commodities [Note: “It is the products which put it (money) into motion and\n make it circulate … The rapidity of its movement (sc. of money) makes up for its\n quantity. When it is needed, it merely glides from one hand to another without\n stopping for an instant.” (Le Trosne l. c. pp. 915, 916.)] . The opposite and mutually\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"80\", \"facs\": \"#f0099\"} --> complementary phases through which this process proceeds cannot occur spatially\n side by side, but can only follow one another in time.","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"3.104","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0105","text":"Intervals of time therefore form the measure of its duration, or the number of\n circuits of the same pieces of money in a given time measures the speed\n of monetary circulation. Let the process of circulation of those four commodities\n last, e.g., one day. Then the sum of prices to be realized is: £8;\n the number of circuits of the same piece of money during the day: 4; and\n the mass of circulating money: £2; or, for a given\n period of time of the process of circulation:\n\\frac{Sum of the prices of commodities}{Number of circuits of a piece of money} = Mass of money functioning as a means of circula-\n tion. This law applies generally.\n The process of circulation of a country in a given period of time com-\n prises, indeed, on the one hand many scattered, simultaneous and spatially side-\n by-side sales (respectively","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"3.105","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0106","text":"purchases) or partial metamorphoses, in\n which the same pieces of money change place only once or accomplish only one cir-\n cuit, and on the other hand many, partly running alongside one another, partly\n interlacing series of metamorphoses with a greater or lesser number of links,\n in which the same pieces of money complete a greater or lesser number\n of circuits. Yet the total number of circuits of all similarly denominated pieces of money in circula-\n tion yields the\naverage number of circuits of the individual piece of money, or the\n average speed of monetary circulation. The mass of money which, at the\n beginning, for example, of the daily process of circulation, is thrown into it\n is naturally determined by the sum of prices of the simultaneously\n and spatially side-by-side circulating commodities. But within\n the process one piece of money is, so to speak, made responsible for another.","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"3.106","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0107","text":"If one accelerates its speed of circulation,\n that of the other slackens, or it flies entirely out of the sphere of circulation,\n since this can absorb only a mass of gold which, multiplied\n by the average number of circuits of its individual element, is equal to\n the sum of prices to be realized. If therefore the number of circuits of\n the pieces of money grows, their circulating mass diminishes. If the number\n of their circuits diminishes, their mass grows. Because the mass of money which\n can function as a means of circulation is given at a given average speed,\n one need therefore, for example, only throw a definite\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"81\", \"facs\": \"#f0100\"} --> quantity of one-pound notes into circulation in order to throw out\n just as many sovereigns, a trick well known to all banks.","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"3.107","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0108","text":"As in monetary circulation in general only the process of circulation of\n commodities appears, so in the speed of monetary circulation appears the speed\n of their change of form, the continuous interlocking of the\n series of metamorphoses, the haste of metabolism, the rapid disappearance\n of commodities from the sphere of circulation and their equally rapid replacement\n by new commodities. In the speed of monetary circulation there thus appears\n the fluid unity of the opposite and mutually complementary\n phases, transformation of the form of use into the form of value and retrans-\n formation of the form of value into the form of use, or of the two pro-\n cesses of sale and purchase. Conversely, in the slowing down\n of monetary circulation appears the separation and opposite inde-\n pendentization of these processes, the stagnation of the change of form\n and hence of metabolism.","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"3.108","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0109","text":"Whence this stagnation arises cannot, of course,\n be seen from circulation itself. It shows only the phenomenon\n itself. To popular conception, which sees money, with slowed monetary circulation,\n appear and disappear less frequently at all points of the periphery of circulation,\n it readily suggests itself to interpret the phenomenon as arising from a lack\n of quantity of means of circulation [Note: “Money being … the common measure of buying and selling, every\n body who has anything to sell, and cannot procure chapmen for it, is presently\n apt to think, that want of money in the kingdom, or country, is the\n cause why his goods do not go off; and so, want of money is the common cry;\n which is a great mistake … What do these people want, who cry out for\n money?","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"3.109","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0110","text":"… The Farmer complains ‥ he thinks that were more money in the\n country, he should have a price for his goods … Then it seems money is not his\n want, but a Price for his corn and cattle, which he would sell, but cannot …\n why cannot he get a price? … 1) Either there is too much corn and cattle in the\n country, so that most who come to market have need of selling, as he has, and\n few of buying: or, 2) There wants the usual vent abroad by Transportation . . . .\n Or, 3) The consumption fails, as when men, by reason of poverty, do not spend\n so much in their houses as formerly they did; wherefore it is not the increase of\n specifick money, which would at all advance the farmer’s goods, but the removal\n of any of these three causes, which do truly keep down the market ‥ The mer-\n chant and shopkeeper want money in the same manner, that is, they want a vent] .","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"3.110","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0111","text":"[Running matter: I. 6]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"82\", \"facs\": \"#f0101\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":111,"address":"3.111","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0112","text":"The total quantity of money functioning as means of circulation in each period of time is thus determined, on the one hand, by the sum of prices of the circulating world of commodities, and, on the other, by the slower or more rapid flow of its opposite processes of circulation, upon which it depends what fraction of that sum of prices can be realized by the same pieces of money. But the sum of prices of commodities depends both on the mass and on the prices of each kind of commodity. The three factors, however, of the movement of prices, the mass of circulating commodities, and finally the velocity of circulation of money, can vary in different directions and in different proportions; the sum of prices to be realized, and hence the mass of means of circulation determined by it, can therefore undergo very numerous combinations. We enumerate here only those most important in the history of commodity prices.","kind":"passage","sequence":112,"address":"3.112","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0113","text":"With commodity prices remaining the same, the mass of means of circulation can increase because the mass of circulating commodities increases or the velocity of circulation of money decreases, or because both operate together. Conversely, the mass of means of circulation can decrease with a decreasing mass of commodities or an increasing velocity of circulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":113,"address":"3.113","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0114","text":"With commodity prices rising generally, the mass of means of circulation can remain the same if the mass of circulating commodities decreases in the same proportion in which their price increases, or if the velocity of circulation of money increases just as rapidly as the rise in prices, while the circulating mass of commodities remains constant.\n[Note: for the goods they deal in, by reason that the markets fail … in a nation\nnever thrives better, than when riches are tost from hand to hand.“ (Sir\nDudley North: „Discourses upon Trade. Lond. 1691“, p. 11—15\npassim.) All Herrenschwand’s swindles amount to the assertion that the contradictions arising from the nature of the commodity and hence appearing in commodity circulation can be abolished by an increase in the means of circulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":114,"address":"3.114","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0115","text":"From the popular illusion which attributes stagnations in the process of production and circulation to a lack of means of circulation, it by no means follows conversely, moreover, that an actual lack of means of circulation, e.g. as a consequence of official bungling with the “regulation of currency,” cannot in its turn bring about stagnations.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"83\", \"facs\": \"#f0102\"} --> The mass of means of circulation can fall because the mass of commodities decreases more rapidly, or the velocity of circulation increases more rapidly, than prices.","kind":"passage","sequence":115,"address":"3.115","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0116","text":"With commodity prices falling generally, the mass of means of circulation can remain the same if the mass of commodities grows in the same proportion in which their price falls, or if the velocity of circulation of money decreases in the same proportion as prices. It can increase if the mass of commodities grows more rapidly, or the velocity of circulation decreases more rapidly, than commodity prices fall.","kind":"passage","sequence":116,"address":"3.116","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0117","text":"The variations of the different factors can mutually compensate one another, so that, despite their continual instability, the total sum of commodity prices to be realized remains constant, and likewise the circulating mass of money. Hence one finds, especially when considering somewhat longer periods, a much more constant average level of the mass of money circulating in each country and, with the exception of violent perturbations which arise periodically from crises of production and trade, more rarely from a change in the value of money itself, much slighter deviations from this average level than one might expect from appearances.","kind":"passage","sequence":117,"address":"3.117","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0118","text":"The law that the quantity of means of circulation is determined by the sum of prices of circulating commodities and the average velocity of monetary circulation [Note: “There is a certain measure, and proportion of money requisite to drive\nthe trade of a nation, more or less than which, would prejudice the same.”","kind":"passage","sequence":118,"address":"3.118","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0119","text":"Just as\nthere is a certain proportion of farthings necessary in a small retail Trade, to\nchange silver money, and to even such reckonings as cannot be adjusted with the\nsmallest silver pieces … Now as the proportion of the number of farthings re-\nquisite in commerce is to be taken from the number of people, the frequency of\ntheir exchanges; as also, and principally, from the value of the smallest\nsilver pieces of money; so in like manner, the proportion of money (gold and\nsilver species) requisite to our trade, is to be likewise taken from the frequency\nof commutations, and from the bigness of payments.” (Wil-\nliam Petty: „A Treatise on Taxes and Contributions. Lond.\n1667“, p. 17.) Hume’s theory was defended against J. Stenart and others by A. Young in his “Political Arithmetic. Lond.","kind":"passage","sequence":119,"address":"3.119","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0120","text":"1774”,\nwhere there is a special chapter: “Prices depend on quantity of money”, p.] , can also be expressed thus,\n[Running matter: 6*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"84\", \"facs\": \"#f0103\"} --> that, given the sum of values of commodities and the given average velocity of their metamorphoses, the quantity of circulating money, or of money-material, depends on its own value.\nThe illusion that, conversely, commodity prices are determined by the mass of means of circulation, and the latter in turn by the mass of money-material present in a country [Note: “The prices of things will certainly rise in every nation, as the gold and\nsilver increase amongst the people; and, consequently, where the gold and silver\ndecrease in any nation, the prices of all things must fall proportionably to such\ndecrease of money.” (Jacob Vanderlint: „Money answers all\nThings“. Lond.","kind":"passage","sequence":120,"address":"3.120","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0121","text":"1734, p. 5.) A closer comparison between Vanderlint and Hume’s “Essays” leaves me not the slightest doubt that Hume knew and used V.’s otherwise very significant work. The view that the mass of means of circulation determines prices occurs also in Barbon and much older writers. “No inconvenience,” says Vanderlint, “can arise by an\nunrestrained trade, but very great advantage … since, if the cash of\nthe nation be decreased by it, which prohibitions are designed to prevent, those\nnations that get the cash will certainly find every thing advance in price, as the\ncash increases amongst them. And . . . . our manufactures and every thing else,\nwill soon become so moderate as to turn the balance of trade in our favour, and\nthereby fetch the money back again.” (l. c. p.","kind":"passage","sequence":121,"address":"3.121","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0122","text":"44.)] , is rooted, in its original representatives, in the absurd hypothesis that commodities without price and money without value enter into the process of circulation,\n[Note: 112 sqq. I remark in “Zur Kritik etc. p. 149”: “The question concerning the\nquantity of circulating coin is silently disposed of by him (A. Smith), in that he\ntreats money, quite falsely, as a mere commodity.” This applies only insofar as A. Smith treats money ex officio. On occasion, however, e.g. in the criticism of the earlier systems of political economy, he states what is correct: “The\nquantity of coin in every country is regulated by the value of the commodities\nwhich are to be circulated by it . . . . The value of goods annually bought and\nsold in any country requires a certain quantity of money to circulate and distri-\nbute them to their proper consumers, and can give employment to no more.","kind":"passage","sequence":122,"address":"3.122","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0123","text":"The\nchannel of circulation necessarily draws to itself a sum sufficient to fill it, and\nnever admits any more.” (Wealth of Nations, l. IV. ch. I.) Similarly, A. Smith opens his work ex officio with an apotheosis of the division of labour. Subsequently, in the last book on the sources of state revenue, he occasionally reproduces A. Ferguson’s, his teacher’s, denunciation of the division of labour.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"85\", \"facs\": \"#f0104\"} --> whereupon an aliquot part of the commodity price would exchange with an aliquot part of the mountain of metal [Note: That every individual kind of commodity, through its price, forms an element of the sum of prices of all circulating commodities, is self-evident. But how use-values incommensurable with one another are supposed to exchange en masse with the mass of gold or silver present in a country is wholly incomprehensible.","kind":"passage","sequence":123,"address":"3.123","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0124","text":"If one transforms the world of commodities into a single aggregate commodity, of which every commodity forms only an aliquot part, the fine arithmetical example results: aggregate commodity = x cwt. gold, commodity A = aliquot part of the aggregate commodity = the same aliquot part of x cwt. gold. Montesquieu states this candidly: “Si l’on compare la masse\n de l’or et de l’argent qui est dans le monde, avec la somme des marchandises qui\n y sont, il est certain que chaque denrée ou marchandise, en particulier, pourra\n être comparée à une certaine portion de l’autre. Supposons qu’il n’y ait\n qu’une seule denrée ou marchandise dans le monde, ou qu’il\n n’y ait qu’une seule qui s’achète, et qu’elle se divise comme l’argent;\n cette partie de cette marchandise répondra à une partie de la masse de l’argent;\n la moitié du total de l’une à la moitié du total de l’autre etc. . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":124,"address":"3.124","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0125","text":"l’établissement\ndu prix des choses dépend toujours fondamentalement de la raison du total des\nchoses au total des signes.” (Montesquieu, l. c. t. III, p. 12, 13.) On the further development of this theory by Ricardo, his pupil James Mill, Lord Overstone, etc., cf. “Zur Kritik etc.” pp. 140—146 and p. 150 seqq. Mr. J. St. Mill understands, with the eclectic logic familiar to him, how to hold the view of his father J. Mill and, at the same time, the opposite view. If one compares the text of his compendium, “Princ. of Pol. Econ.”, with the preface (first edition), in which he announces himself as the Adam Smith of the present age, one does not know what to admire more, the man’s naïveté or that of the public which took him, in good faith, as Adam Smith, to whom he stands in somewhat the same relation as General Williams Kars of Kars to the Duke of Wellington.","kind":"passage","sequence":125,"address":"3.125","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0126","text":"Mr. J. St. Mill’s original researches in the field of political economy, neither extensive nor substantial, are all found drawn up in rank and file in his little work published in 1844: “Some Unsettled Questions of Political Economy”. Locke directly expresses the connection between the valuelessness of gold and silver and the determination of their value by quantity. “Mankind\nhaving consented to put an imaginary value upon gold and silver … the\nintrinsick value, regarded in these metals, is nothing but the quantity.”\n“Some Considerations etc. 1691”. Works ed 1777, vol. II, p. 15.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":126,"address":"3.126","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0127","text":"## c) Coin. The token of value.","kind":"passage","sequence":127,"address":"3.127","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0128","text":"From the function of money as means of circulation arises its coin-form. The portion by weight of gold represented in the price, or money-name, of commodities must confront them in circulation as a gold piece, or coin, of the same name. Like the establishment of the standard of prices, the business of minting falls to the state. In the various national uniforms which gold and silver wear as coins, but take off again on the world market, there appears the separation between the internal or national spheres of commodity circulation and its universal sphere of the world market.","kind":"passage","sequence":128,"address":"3.128","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0129","text":"Gold coin and gold bullion thus differ from the outset only by their shape, and gold can constantly be transformed from one form into the other [Note: It naturally lies quite beyond my purpose to deal with details such as seigniorage and the like. Yet, against the romantic sycophant Adam Müller, who admires “the magnificent liberality” with which the “English government mints gratuitously,” the following judgment of Sir Dudley North: “Silver and gold, like other commodities, have their ebbings and\nflowings. Upon the arrival of quantities from Spain … it is carried into the\nTower, and coined. Not long after there will come a demand for bullion, to\nbe exported again. If there is none, but all happens to be in coin, what then?\nMelt it down again; there’s no loss in it, for the coining costs the owner nothing.","kind":"passage","sequence":129,"address":"3.129","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0130","text":"Thus the nation has been abused, and made to pay for the twisting of straw, for\nasses to eat. If the merchant (North himself was one of the greatest merchants in the time of Charles II) had to pay the price of the coinage, he would not have sent his\nsilver to the Tower without consideration; and coined money would always keep a\nvalue above uncoined silver.” (North l. c. p. 18.)] . But the path out of the mint is at the same time the course to the melting-pot. For in circulation gold coins wear down, one more, the other less. Gold title and gold substance, nominal content and real content, begin their process of separation. Gold coins of the same name become of unequal value because of their different weights. Gold as means of circulation diverges from gold as measure of prices, and thereby also ceases to be the real equivalent of the commodities whose prices it realizes.","kind":"passage","sequence":130,"address":"3.130","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0131","text":"The history of these disorders forms the monetary history of the Middle Ages and of modern times down to the eighteenth century. The spontaneous tendency of the process of circulation to transform the gold-being of coin into a gold semblance, or coin into a symbol of its official metal content, is itself recognized by the most modern laws\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"87\", \"facs\": \"#f0106\"} --> concerning the degree of loss of metal which renders a gold piece incapable of circulation, or demonetizes it.","kind":"passage","sequence":131,"address":"3.131","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0132","text":"If monetary circulation itself separates the real content from the nominal content\n of the coin, its metallic existence from its functional existence,\n it contains latently the possibility of replacing metallic money in its function\n as coin by tokens of another material or symbols. The tech-\n nical obstacles to the coining of quite diminutive fractional weights of\n gold, respectively silver, and the circumstance that lower metals originally,\n instead of the nobler ones, silver instead of gold, copper instead of silver,\n serve as the measure of value and therefore circulate as money at the moment when\n the nobler metal dethrones them, explain historically the role of silver-\n and copper tokens as substitutes for the gold coin. They replace gold\n in those circles of commodity circulation in which coin circulates most rapidly\n and therefore wears out most rapidly, i.e.","kind":"passage","sequence":132,"address":"3.132","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0133","text":"where purchases and sales are con-\n tinually renewed on the smallest scale. In order to prevent the establish-\n ment of these satellites in the place of gold itself,\n the very low proportions in which alone they must be accepted in payment\n for gold are legally determined. The special circles in which the different\n kinds of coin circulate naturally run into one another.","kind":"passage","sequence":133,"address":"3.133","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0134","text":"Token coin appears alongside gold for the pay-\n ment of fractions of the smallest gold coin; gold constantly\n enters into retail circulation, but is just as constantly thrown out again through exchange with token\n coin [Note: “If silver never exceed what is wanted for the smaller payments, it cannot\n be collected in sufficient quantities for the larger payments … the use of gold in\n the main payments necessarily implies also its use in the retail trade: those who\n have gold coin offering them for small purchases, and receiving with the commo-\n dity purchased a balance of silver in return; by which means the surplus of silver\n that would otherwise encumber the retail dealer, is drawn off and dispersed into\n general circulation.”","kind":"passage","sequence":134,"address":"3.134","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0135","text":"But if there is as much silver as will transact the small pay-\n ments independent of gold, the retail dealer must then receive silver for small pur-\n chases; and it must of necessity accumulate in his hands.” (David Buchanan:\n “Inquiry into the Taxation and Commercial Policy of Great\n Britain. Edinburgh 1844”, p. 248, 249.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":135,"address":"3.135","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0136","text":"The metallic content of silver or copper tokens is arbitrarily determined by\n law. In circulation they wear out even more rapidly than the\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"88\", \"facs\": \"#f0107\"} --> gold coin. Their function as coin thus becomes in fact wholly independent\n of their weight, i.e. of all value. The existence of gold as coin\n separates itself completely from its substance of value. Relatively valueless things,\npaper slips, can therefore function in its stead as coin. In\n metallic money tokens the purely symbolic character is still to some extent con-\n cealed. In paper money it emerges plainly.\n One sees: ce n’est que le premier pas qui coûte.","kind":"passage","sequence":136,"address":"3.136","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0137","text":"We are concerned here only with state paper money with compulsory\n currency. It grows directly out of metallic circulation.\nCredit money, on the other hand, presupposes relations\n which, from the standpoint of simple commodity circulation, are still wholly unknown to us. In pass-\n ing, however, it should be noted that, just as money proper made of paper arises from the\n function of money as a means of circulation, cre-\n dit money has its naturally grown root in the function of money as a means of pay-\n ment [Note: The financial mandarin Wan-mao-in allowed himself to submit to the Son of\n Heaven a project which covertly aimed at the transformation of the Chi-\n nese imperial assignats into convertible banknotes. In the report of the\n Assignat Committee of April 1854 he receives a proper washing of the head. Whether\n he also received the obligatory number of bamboo strokes is not reported.","kind":"passage","sequence":137,"address":"3.137","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0138","text":"“The\n Committee,” it says at the end of the report, “has carefully con-\n sidered his project and finds that everything in it is directed to the advantage of\n merchants and that nothing is advantageous to the Crown.” (“Works of the Imperial Rus-\n sian Legation at Peking on China. From the Rus-\n sian by Dr. K. Abel and F. A. Mecklenburg. First Volume.\n Berlin 1858”, p. 47 sq.) Concerning the continual demetallization of\n gold coins through their circulation, a “Governor” of the Bank of England says\n as a witness before the “House of Lords’ Committee” (on “Bank Acts”): “Every year\n a fresh class of sovereigns (this not politically, but the so-\n vereign is the name of the pound sterling) becomes too light. The class which in one year\n passes as full-weight loses through wear sufficiently to turn the scale\n against itself the next year.” (H. o. Lords’ Committee 1848,\n n. 429.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":138,"address":"3.138","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0139","text":"Paper slips on which money names such as £1, £5, etc.\n are printed are externally thrown by the state into the process of circulation.\n Insofar as they actually circulate in place of the gold sum bearing the same name,\n the laws of monetary circulation itself are merely reflected in their movement.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"89\", \"facs\": \"#f0108\"} --> A specific law of paper circulation\n can arise only from its relation of representation to gold. And\n this law is simply this: that the issue of paper money is to be restricted\n to the quantity in which the gold (respectively silver) symbolically represented by it\n would actually have to circulate. Now, the quantum of gold\n which the sphere of circulation can absorb constantly fluctuates\n above or below a certain average level.","kind":"passage","sequence":139,"address":"3.139","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0140","text":"However, the\n mass of the circulating medium in a given country never falls below\n a certain minimum, which is empirically established. That this\n minimum mass continually changes its components, i.e. consists of ever dif-\n ferent gold pieces, naturally changes nothing in its extent\n and its constant rotation in the sphere of circulation. It can therefore\n be replaced by paper symbols. If, on the other hand, today all channels of circulation\n are filled to the full degree of their money-absorbing capacity with paper money,\n then tomorrow they may be overfull in consequence of fluctuations in commodity circulation.\n All measure is lost. But if\n paper exceeds its measure, i.e.","kind":"passage","sequence":140,"address":"3.140","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0141","text":"the quantity of gold coin of equal deno-\n mination which could circulate, then, apart from the danger of general\n discrediting, within the world of commodities it nevertheless represents only the quantity of gold\n determined by its immanent laws, and thus alone capable of representation. If,\n for example, the mass of paper slips represents 2 ounces of gold each, instead\n of 1 ounce each, then their money names are in fact reduced, say, from\n £1 per ¼ ounce to names of ⅛ ounces. The effect\n is the same as if gold had been altered in its function as the measure of prices.\n The same values which were therefore previously expressed in the price of\n £1, now express themselves in the price of £2.","kind":"passage","sequence":141,"address":"3.141","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0142","text":"Paper money is a gold sign or money sign. Its rela-\n tion to commodity values consists only in the fact that they are ideally expressed in the same\n quanta of gold which are sensuously symbolically represented by paper. Only insofar as paper money repre-\n sents quanta of gold which, like all other commodity quanta, are also quanta of value, is it a\nsign of value.","kind":"passage","sequence":142,"address":"3.142","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0143","text":"Finally, the question is why gold can be replaced by mere valueless\n signs of itself? But, as we have seen, it is replaceable only\n insofar as it is isolated or made independent in its function as coin or means of circulation.\n Now, this making-independent of this function indeed does not take place for individual gold coins,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"90\", \"facs\": \"#f0109\"} --> although it appears in the continued circulation of worn gold pieces.\n Gold pieces are merely coin or means of circulation precisely only so\n long as they actually find themselves in circulation. But what does not hold for the indi-\n vidual gold coin holds for the minimum mass of gold replaceable by paper money.\n It dwells constantly in the sphere of circulation, functions continually\n as means of circulation, and therefore exists exclusively as bearer\n of this function.","kind":"passage","sequence":143,"address":"3.143","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0144","text":"Its movement thus represents only the continual trans-\n formation into one another of the opposed processes of commodity metamor-\n phosis C — M — C, in which the commodity's form of value confronts it\n only in order immediately to disappear again. The independent\n representation of the exchange-value of the commodity is here only a fleet-\n ing moment. It is immediately replaced again by another commodity. There-\n fore the merely symbolic existence of money also suffices in a process\n that continually removes it from one hand into the other. Its func-\n tional existence, so to speak, absorbs its material existence.","kind":"passage","sequence":144,"address":"3.144","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0145","text":"A vanishingly\n objectified reflection of commodity prices, it functions only as a sign\n of itself and can therefore also be replaced by signs [Note: From the fact that gold and silver, as coin or in the exclusive\n function of means of circulation, become signs of themselves,\nNicholas Barbon derives the right of governments “to raise money”, i.e.,\n for example, to give a quantum of silver called groats the name of a larger\n quantum of silver, such as thalers, and thus to repay creditors with groats instead of thalers.\n “Money does wear and grow lighter by often telling over …\n It is the denomination and currency of the money that men regard in\n bargaining, and not the quantity of silver … ’Tis the publick authority upon the\n metal that makes it money.” (N. Barbon l. c. p. 29, 30, 25.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":145,"address":"3.145","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0146","text":"Only,\n the sign of money requires its own objective social\n validity, and the paper symbol receives this through compulsory\n currency. Only within the internal sphere of circulation circumscribed\n by the boundaries of a community does this state compulsion apply, but\n only here too does money wholly merge into its function as means of circulation\n or coin, and can therefore in paper money obtain a mode of existence externally\n separated from its metallic substance and merely functional.","kind":"passage","sequence":146,"address":"3.146","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0147","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"91\", \"facs\": \"#f0110\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":147,"address":"3.147","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0148","text":"## C. Money.","kind":"passage","sequence":148,"address":"3.148","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0149","text":"The commodity which functions as measure of value and therefore also, personally or\n through representatives, as means of circulation, is money.\n Gold (respectively silver) is therefore money. As money it functions, on the one hand,\n where it must appear in its golden (respectively silver) corporeality,\n hence as money commodity, thus neither merely ideal, as in the measure of value,\n nor capable of representation, as in the means of circulation; on the other hand, where its func-\n tion, whether it performs the same in its own person or through representatives,\n fixes it as the sole form of value or the sole adequate existence\n of exchange-value in opposition to all other commodities as mere use-\n values.","kind":"passage","sequence":149,"address":"3.149","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0150","text":"## a) Hoard formation.","kind":"passage","sequence":150,"address":"3.150","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0151","text":"The continuous circuit of the two opposed commodity-\n metamorphoses, or the fluid turnover of sale and purchase, appears\n in the restless circulation of money or its function as the per-\n petuum mobile of circulation. It is immobilized, or transforms itself,\n as Boisguillebert says, from meuble into immeuble, from coin\n into money, as soon as the series of metamorphoses is interrupted, the sale\n not supplemented by a subsequent purchase.","kind":"passage","sequence":151,"address":"3.151","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0152","text":"With the first development of commodity circulation itself there develops\n the necessity and the passion to hold fast the product of the first\n metamorphosis, the transformed shape of the commodity or its gold pupa\n [Note: “Une richesse en argent n’est que … richesse en productions, con-\n verties en argent.” (Mercier de la Rivière l. c. p. 557.) “Une\n valeur en productions n’a fait que changer de forme.” (id. p. 486.)] . Commodity is sold, not in order to buy commodity, but\n in order to replace commodity form by money form. From a mere mediation\n of metabolism, this change of form becomes an end in itself. The alienated\n shape of the commodity is prevented from functioning as its absolutely alien-\n able shape or merely vanishing money form. Money thereby\n petrifies into a hoard, and the commodity seller becomes a hoard-former.","kind":"passage","sequence":152,"address":"3.152","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0153","text":"Precisely in the beginnings of commodity circulation only\n the surplus of use-values is transformed into money. Gold and silver thus\n become of themselves social expressions of surplus or\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"92\", \"facs\": \"#f0111\"} --> wealth. This naive form of hoard formation perpetuates itself among peo-\n ples in whom the traditional mode of production directed toward self-requirement\n corresponds to a firmly closed circle of needs. Thus among\n Asians, notably Indians. Vanderlint, who imagines commodity\n prices to be determined by the mass of gold and silver found in a country,\n asks himself why Indian commodities are so cheap?\n Answer: because Indians bury money. From 1602—1734, he re-\n marks, they buried 150 million pounds sterling","kind":"passage","sequence":153,"address":"3.153","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0154","text":"in silver, which originally\n came from America to Europe [Note: ’Tis by this practice they keep all their goods and manufactures at such\n low rates.” (Vanderlint l. c. p. 95, 96.)] . From 1856—1866, thus in 10 years,\n England exported to India and China (the metal exported to China for the most part flows back again to India) 120 million pounds sterling in\n silver, which had previously been exchanged for Australian gold.","kind":"passage","sequence":154,"address":"3.154","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0155","text":"With more developed commodity production, every commodity pro-\nducer must secure for himself the nexus rerum, the “social pledge” [Note: “Money is a pledge.” (John Bellers: “Essays about the\n Poor, Manufactures, Trade, Plantations, and Immorality.\n Lond. 1699”, p. 13.)] .\n His needs are renewed incessantly and command incessant\n purchase of others' commodities, while production and sale of his own\n commodity cost time and depend upon contingencies. In order to buy without\n selling, he must previously have sold without buying. This ope-\n ration, carried out on a general scale, seems to contradict itself. At\n their sources of production, however, the precious metals exchange directly\n with other commodities.","kind":"passage","sequence":155,"address":"3.155","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0156","text":"Here sale takes place (on the side\n of the commodity owners) without purchase (on the side of the gold and silver owners)\n [Note: Purchase in the categorical sense presupposes gold or silver already\n as the transformed form of the commodity, or as the product of sale.] . And subsequent sales without ensuing purchases merely mediate\n the further distribution of the precious metals among all commodity owners. Thus\n hoards of gold and silver of the most various extent arise at all\n points of circulation. With the possibility of holding fast the commodity as exchange-\n value, or exchange-value as commodity, the lust for gold awakens.\n With the expansion of commodity circulation grows the power of money,\n the ever ready, absolutely social form of wealth.\n “Gold is a wonderful thing! Whoever possesses it is master of\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"93\", \"facs\": \"#f0112\"} --> everything he desires.","kind":"passage","sequence":156,"address":"3.156","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0157","text":"By means of gold one can even get souls into Para-\ndise.” (Columbus, in the letter from Jamaica, 1503). Since one cannot see\n from money what has been transformed into it, everything,\n commodity or not, transforms itself into money. Everything becomes\n saleable and purchasable. Circulation becomes the great social retort,\n into which everything flies, in order to come out again as a money crystal. Not even\n saints' bones withstand this alchemy, and still less less crude\n res sacrosanctae, extra commercium hominum [Note: Henry III, most Christian King of France, robs monasteries\n etc. of their relics in order to turn them into silver. It is known what role the plunder\n of the Delphic temple treasures by the Phocians plays in Greek history.\n With the ancients, the temples notoriously served the god of commodities as\n dwelling-place. They were “holy banks.”","kind":"passage","sequence":157,"address":"3.157","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0158","text":"To the Phoenicians, a trading people par\n excellence, money counted as the alienated form of all things. It was therefore in the\n order of things that the maidens who gave themselves to strangers at the festivals\n of the goddess of love should offer to the goddess the coin received\n as recompense.] . Just as in money every qualitative difference among commodities\n is extinguished, so money in turn, as a radical leveller, extinguishes all distinctions [Note: “Gold! yellow, glittering precious gold!\n Thus much of this, will make black white; foul, fair;\n Wrong, right; base, noble; old, young; coward, valiant\n . . . . . . . . . . What this, you gods! Why this\n Will lug your priests and servants from your sides;\n Pluck stout men’s pillows from below their heads.","kind":"passage","sequence":158,"address":"3.158","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0159","text":"This yellow slave\n Will knit and break religions; bless the accurs’d;\n Make the hoar leprosy ador’d; place thieves\n And give them title, knee and approbation,\n With senators of the bench; this is it,\n That makes the wappen’d widow wed again\n . . . . . . . . . . Come damned earth,\n Thou common whore of mankind.”\n (Shakespeare, Timon of Athens.)] . But money is itself\n a commodity, an external thing, which can become the private property of anyone.\n Social power thus becomes the private power of the private individual.\n Ancient society therefore denounces it as the small change of its\n economic and moral order [Note: “Οὐδὲν γὰϱ ἀνϑϱώποισιν οἷον ἄϱγυϱος\n Καϰὸν νόμισμα ἔβλαστε· τοῦτο ϰαὶ πόλεις\n Ποϱϑεῖ, τόδ᾽ἄνδϱας ἐξανίστησιν δόμων.\n Τόδ᾽ ἐϰδιδάσϰει ϰαὶ παϱαλλάσσει φϱένας\n Χϱηστὰς πϱὸς αἰσχϱὰ ἀνϑϱώποις ἔχειν,\n Καὶ παντὸς ἔϱγου δυσσέβειαν εἰδέναι.”\n (Sophocles, Antigone.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":159,"address":"3.159","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0160","text":"Modern society, which\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"94\", \"facs\": \"#f0113\"} --> even in its childhood pulls Plutus by the hair out of the entrails\n of the earth [Note: “Ἐλπιζούσης τῆς πλεονεξίας ἀνάξειν ἐϰ τῶν μυχῶν τῆς γῆς αὐτὸν τὸν\n Πλούτωνα.” (Athen. Deipnos.)] , hails in the golden grail the shining incarna-\ntion of its own innermost principle of life.","kind":"passage","sequence":160,"address":"3.160","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0161","text":"The commodity as use-value satisfies a particular need\nand forms a particular element of material wealth. But the\nvalue of the commodity measures the degree of its attractive power upon all the ele-\nments of material wealth, hence the social\nwealth of its possessor. For the barbarously simple commodity owner,\neven for a Western European peasant, value is inseparable from the\nform of value; increase of the hoard of gold and silver is therefore increase of\nvalue. Certainly the value of money changes, whether as a consequence of a change\nin its own value or of a change in the value of commodities. This\nhowever does not prevent, on the one hand, 200 ounces of gold from continuing to\ncontain more value than 100, 300 more than 200, and so\non.","kind":"passage","sequence":161,"address":"3.161","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0162","text":"forth, nor, on the other hand,\ndoes it prevent the metallic natural form of this thing from remaining the general equivalent-\nform of all commodities, the immediately social incarnation\nof all human labour. The drive to hoard formation is by nature\nmeasureless. Qualitatively, or according to its form, money is without\nlimits, i.e. universal representative of material wealth, because it is\nimmediately convertible into every commodity. But at the same time every actual sum of\nmoney is quantitatively limited, and thus also only a means of purchase of lim-\nited effect. This contradiction between the quantitative\nlimit and the qualitative limitlessness of money continually drives the\nhoarder back to the Sisyphean labour of accumulation.\nIt is with him as with the world-conqueror, who with every new land conquers only a\nnew frontier.","kind":"passage","sequence":162,"address":"3.162","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0163","text":"In order to hold fast gold as money and thus as an element of hoard-\nformation, it must be prevented from circulating or from dissolving itself as a means\nof purchase into means of enjoyment. The hoarder therefore sacrifices his\nlust of the flesh to the gold fetish. He is serious about the gospel\nof renunciation. On the other hand, he can withdraw from circulation in money only\nwhat he gives to it in commodity. The more he produces, the more\nhe can sell. Diligence, thrift, and avarice therefore form his car-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"95\", \"facs\": \"#f0114\"} --> dinal virtues: to sell much, to buy little, the sum of his politi-\ncal economy [Note: “Accrescere quanto più si può il numero de’ venditori d’ogni merce, di-\nminuire quanto più si può il numero dei compratori, questi sono i cardini sui quali\nsi raggirano tutte le operazioni di economia politica.” (Verri l. c. p. 52.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":163,"address":"3.163","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0164","text":"Alongside the immediate form of the hoard runs its aestheti-\nc form, the possession of gold and silver articles. It grows with\nthe wealth of bourgeois society. “Soyons riches ou parais-\nsons riches.” (Diderot.) Thus there forms, partly, an ever more extensive\nmarket for gold and silver, independently of their money functions, and, partly,\na latent source of supply of money, which flows especially in periods of social\ntumult.","kind":"passage","sequence":164,"address":"3.164","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0165","text":"Hoard formation fulfils various functions in the economy\nof metallic circulation. The nearest function arises from the\nconditions of circulation of gold or silver coin. We have seen how,\nwith the constant fluctuations of commodity circulation in volume,\nprices, and velocity, the circulating mass of money incessantly ebbs\nand flows. It must therefore be capable of contraction and expansion.\nNow money must be attracted as coin, now coin repelled as money.\nIn order that the mass of money actually circulating may always correspond to the\ndegree of saturation of the sphere of circulation, the quantity of gold\nor silver present in a country must be greater than that engaged in coin\nfunction. This condition is fulfilled by the hoard form of money.","kind":"passage","sequence":165,"address":"3.165","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0166","text":"The hoard-\nreservoirs serve at the same time as channels of outflow and inflow for circulat-\ning money, which therefore never overfills its channels of circulation [Note: “There is required for carrying on the trade of the nation, a determinate\nsum of specifick Money, which varies, and is sometimes more, sometimes less, as\nthe circumstances we are in require . . . . This ebbing and flowing of money,\nsupplies and accommodates itself, without any aid of Politicians … The buckets\nwork alternately; when money is scarce, bullion is coined; when bullion is scarce,\nmoney is melted.” (Sir D. North l. c. p. 22.) John Stuart Mill,\nfor a long time an official of the East India Company, confirms that in India silver\nornaments still function directly as hoards.","kind":"passage","sequence":166,"address":"3.166","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0167","text":"The “silver ornaments\nare brought out and coined when there is a high rate of interest, and go back\nagain when the rate of interest falls.” (J. St. Mill’s Evidence. Repts on\nBankacts 1857, n. 2084.) According to a parliamentary document of\n1864 on gold and silver import and export in India, in 1863 the] .","kind":"passage","sequence":167,"address":"3.167","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0168","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"96\", \"facs\": \"#f0115\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":168,"address":"3.168","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0169","text":"## b) Means of payment.","kind":"passage","sequence":169,"address":"3.169","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0170","text":"In the immediate form of commodity circulation considered hitherto,\nthe same magnitude of value was always present twice, commodity at one pole,\nmoney at the opposite pole. Commodity owners therefore came into contact only\nas representatives of reciprocally existing equivalents. With the development\nof commodity circulation, however, relations develop through which the alien-\nation of the commodity is temporally separated from the realisation of its\nprice. It suffices here to indicate the simplest of these relations. One\nkind of commodity requires a longer, another a shorter period of time for its\nproduction. The production of different commodities is bound to different\nseasons. One commodity is born in its market-place, the other must travel\nto a distant market. One commodity owner can therefore appear as seller\nbefore the other as buyer.","kind":"passage","sequence":170,"address":"3.170","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0171","text":"With\nconstant recurrence of the same transactions among the same persons, the\nconditions of sale of commodities are regulated according to their conditions of produc-\ntion. One commodity owner sells commodity at hand, the other\nbuys as the mere representative of money or as the representative of future\nmoney. The seller becomes creditor, the buyer debt-\nor. Since the metamorphosis of the commodity, or the development of its\nform of value, here changes, gold too receives another function.\nIt becomes means of payment [Note: Luther distinguishes between money as a means of purchase and as a means of pay-\nment. “Machest mir einen Zwilling aus dem Schadewacht, das ich hie nicht\nbezalen und dort nicht kauffen kann.” (Martin Luther: “An die\nPfarrherrn, wider den Wucher zu predigen. Wittenberg 1540.”)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":171,"address":"3.171","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0172","text":"The character of creditor or debtor here arises out of\nsimple commodity circulation. Its change of form stamps these new\nmarks upon seller and buyer. At first, then, they are roles just as transient,\nand played alternately by the same agents of circulation, as those of seller and buyer.\nYet the opposition now looks less genial from the outset and is capable of greater\n[Note: Imports of gold and silver exceeded exports by 19,367,764 pounds sterling in 1863. In the last 8\nyears before 1864, the excess of imports over exports of precious met-\nals amounted to 109,652,917 pounds sterling. During this century, far more than\n200,000,000 pounds sterling were coined in India.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"97\", \"facs\": \"#f0116\"} --> crystallisation [Note: On the relations of debtor and creditor among English\nmen of trade at the beginning of the 18th","kind":"passage","sequence":172,"address":"3.172","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0173","text":"century: “Such a spirit of cruelty reigns\nhere in England among the men of trade, that is not to be met with in any other\nsociety of men, nor in any other kingdom of the world.” (“An Essay on\nCredit and the Bankrupt Act. Lond. 1707”, p. 2.)] . But the same characters can also\nappear independently of commodity circulation. The class struggle of the an-\ncient world, for example, moves chiefly in the form of a struggle\nbetween creditor and debtor, and ends in Rome with the downfall\nof the plebeian debtor, who is replaced by the slave. In the\nMiddle Ages the struggle ends with the downfall of the feudal debtor,\nwho forfeits his political power along with its economic basis.","kind":"passage","sequence":173,"address":"3.173","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0174","text":"Meanwhile,\nthe money form — and the relation of creditor and debt-\nor possesses the form of a money relation — here merely reflects the antagon-\nism of more deeply situated economic conditions of life.","kind":"passage","sequence":174,"address":"3.174","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0175","text":"Let us return to the sphere of commodity circulation. The simulta-\nneous appearance of the equivalents, commodity and money, at the two\npoles of the sale process has ceased. Money now functions,\nfirstly, as measure of value in the determination of the price of the commodity sold.\nIts contractually fixed price measures the buyer's obligation, i.e.\nthe sum of money which he owes at a definite date. Secondly, it func-\ntions as an ideal means of purchase. Although it exists only in the buyer's\npromise of money, it effects the change of hands of the commodity.\nOnly at the due date of payment does the means of payment actually enter circu-\nlation, i.e. pass from the hand of the buyer into that of the seller.\nThe means of circulation transformed itself into hoard because the process of circulation\nbroke off with the first phase, or because the transformed form of the\ncommodity was withdrawn from circulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":175,"address":"3.175","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0176","text":"The means of payment enters into\n circulation, but after the commodity has already withdrawn from it.\n Money no longer mediates the process. It concludes it\n independently, as the absolute existence of exchange-value or general\n commodity. The seller transformed commodity into money in order to satisfy\n a need by means of money, the hoarder, in order to preserve commodity in money-\n form, the indebted buyer, in order to be able to pay. If\n he does not pay, his possessions are compulsorily sold. The value-form\n [Running matter: I. 7]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"98\", \"facs\": \"#f0117\"} --> of the commodity, money, thus now becomes the end in itself of sale\n through a social necessity arising from the conditions of the process of\n circulation itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":176,"address":"3.176","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0177","text":"The buyer transforms money back into commodity before he has transformed commodity into\n money, or accomplishes the second commodity-metamorphosis before\n the first. The seller's commodity circulates, but realizes its price only\n in a title, valid in private law, to money. It transforms itself into use-\n value before it has transformed itself into money. The accomplishment\n of its first metamorphosis follows only subsequently.","kind":"passage","sequence":177,"address":"3.177","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0178","text":"In every definite period of the process of circulation, the due obli-\n gations represent the sum of prices of the commodities whose\n sale called them forth. The mass of money necessary for realizing this sum\n of prices depends in the first instance upon the velocity of circulation\n of the means of payment. It is conditioned by two circumstances: the\nconcatenation of the relations of creditor and debtor, so that A,\n who receives money from his debtor B, pays it over to his creditor C,\n and so on — and the length of time between the different terms of pay-\n ment. The processual chain of payments, or subsequent first metamor-\n phoses, differs essentially from the previously considered interlacing of\n the series of metamorphoses. In the circulation of the medium of circu-\n lation, the connection between sellers and buyers is not merely expressed.","kind":"passage","sequence":178,"address":"3.178","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0179","text":"The connection itself arises only\n in and with monetary circulation. By contrast, the movement of the means of pay-\n ment expresses a social connection already completely present before it.","kind":"passage","sequence":179,"address":"3.179","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0179"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0180","text":"The simultaneity and juxtaposition of sales limit the re-\n placement of the mass of coin by velocity of circulation. Conversely, they\n form a new lever in the economy of means of payment. With the con-\n centration of payments at the same place, special institutions and methods\n of their settlement develop spontaneously. Thus, for example, the vire-\n ments in medieval Lyons. The claims of A upon B, B upon\n C, C upon A, and so on, need only be compared in order mutually to\n cancel one another out, up to a certain amount, as positive and negative\n magnitudes. Thus only a balance of debts remains to be settled. The more\n massive the concentration of payments, the relatively smaller the balance,\n and hence the mass of circulating means of payment.","kind":"passage","sequence":180,"address":"3.180","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0180"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0181","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"99\", \"facs\": \"#f0118\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":181,"address":"3.181","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0181"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0182","text":"The function of money as means of payment includes an immediate\n contradiction. In so far as payments balance out, it functions\n only ideally as money of account or measure of values. In so far as\n actual payment is to be made, it appears not as medium of circulation,\n as merely vanishing and mediating form of metabolism, but\n as the individual incarnation of social labour, independent\n existence of exchange-value, absolute commodity. This contradiction erupts\n in the moment of production and commercial crises, called the money crisis\n [Note: The money crisis, as it is determined in the text, as a phase of every crisis,\n is to be clearly distinguished from the particular kind of crisis which is also\n called money crisis, but which can constitute a quite independent phenomenon, so that\n it acts only reactively upon industry and commerce.","kind":"passage","sequence":182,"address":"3.182","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0182"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0183","text":"These are crises\n whose centre of movement is money-capital, and whose immediate sphere is therefore\n also the sphere of the principal and state operations of money-capital, bank, stock exchange,\n finance.] . It occurs only where the processual chain of pay-\n ments and an artificial system for their settlement are fully developed.\n With more general disturbances of this mechanism, from whatever source\n they may spring, money suddenly and without mediation changes from the merely\n ideal form of money of account into hard money. It becomes irre-\n placeable by profane commodities. The use-value of the commodity becomes value-\n less, and its value vanishes before its own value-form. Just now the citizen,\n in enlightenment conceit intoxicated by prosperity, declared money\n an empty illusion. Only commodity is money. Only money is commodity!\n it now rings out across the world market.","kind":"passage","sequence":183,"address":"3.183","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0183"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0184","text":"As the stag cries out for fresh\n water, so its soul cries out for money, the sole wealth [Note: “This sudden transition from the credit system to the monetary\n system adds the theoretical terror to the practical panic: and the agents of circu-\n lation shudder before the impenetrable mystery of their own\n relations.” (Karl Marx l. c. p. 126.) “The Poor stand still, because\n the Rich have no Money to employ them, though they have the same land and\n hands to provide victuals and cloaths, as ever they had; which is the true Riches\n of a Nation, and not the Money.” (John Bellers: “Proposals for rais-\n ing a Colledge of Industry. Lond. 1696”, p. 3.)] .\n In the crisis, the opposition between the commodity and its value-form,\n money, is heightened into an absolute contradiction. The form of appearance\n of money is therefore immaterial here.","kind":"passage","sequence":184,"address":"3.184","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0184"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0185","text":"The money-famine\n [Running matter: 7*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"100\", \"facs\": \"#f0119\"} --> remains the same, whether payment is to be made in gold or credit-money,\n banknotes, for instance [Note: How such moments are exploited by the “amis du commerce”:\n “On one occasion (1839) an old grasping banker (of the City) in his private room,\n raised the lid of the desk he sat over, and displayed to a friend rolls of banknotes,\n saying with intense glee there were 600,000 £ of them, they were held to\n make money tight, and would all be let out after three o’clock on the same\n day.” (“The Theory of the Exchanges. The Bank Charter Act\n of 1844. Lond. 1864”, p. 81.) The semi-official organ, “The Ob-\n server”, remarks on the 24th","kind":"passage","sequence":185,"address":"3.185","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0185"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0186","text":"April 1864: “Some very curious rumours are current\n of the means which have been resorted to in order to create a scarcity\n of Banknotes … Questionable as it would seem, to suppose that any trick\n of the kind would be adopted, the report has been so universal that it really\n deserves mention.”] .","kind":"passage","sequence":186,"address":"3.186","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0186"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0187","text":"If we now consider the total sum of money circulating during a given\n period, it is, given the velocity of circulation of the means of circu-\n lation and payment, equal to the sum of the commodity prices\n to be realized plus the sum of payments due\n minus the payments which balance out. Even if prices, velocity of\n monetary circulation, and economy of payments are given, the mass of money\n circulating during a period, a day for example, and the mass of commodities\n circulating no longer coincide. Money circulates which represents commodities\n long withdrawn from circulation. Commodities circulate whose money-equivalent\n appears only in the future.","kind":"passage","sequence":187,"address":"3.187","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0187"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0188","text":"On the other hand, the\n payments contracted each day and those falling due on the same day\n are wholly incommensurable magnitudes [Note: “The amount of sales or contracts entered upon during the course of any\n given day, will not affect the quantity of money afloat on that particular day,\n but, in the vast majority of cases, will resolve themselves into multifarious drafts\n upon the quantity of money which may be afloat at subsequent dates more or less\n distant … The bills granted or credits opened, to day, need have no resemblance\n whatever, either in quantity, amount or duration, to those granted or entered upon\n to-morrow or next day; nay, many of to-day’s bills, and credits, when due, fall\n in with a mass of liabilities whose origins traverse a range of antecedent dates al-\n together indefinite, bills at 12, 6, 3 months or 1 often aggregating together to\n swell the common liabilities of one","kind":"passage","sequence":188,"address":"3.188","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0188"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0189","text":"particular day . . . .” (“The currency\n Question Reviewed; a letter to the Scotch people. By a Banker\n in England. Edinburgh 1845”, p. 29, 30 passim.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":189,"address":"3.189","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0189"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0190","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"101\", \"facs\": \"#f0120\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":190,"address":"3.190","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0190"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0191","text":"Credit-money springs directly from the function of money\n as means of payment, in that certificates of debt for the commodities sold themselves\n circulate anew for the transfer of claims. On the other hand,\n as the credit system expands, so does the function of money as means of pay-\n ment. As such, it receives forms of existence of its own, in which it inhabits\n the sphere of large commercial transactions, while gold or silver coin\n is thrust back mainly into the sphere of retail trade [Note: As an example of how little real money enters into commercial operations proper,\n the following is the scheme of one of the largest London commercial houses concerning\n its annual monetary receipts and payments. Its transactions in the year\n 1856, amounting to many millions sterling, have been reduced to the scale of one\n million.","kind":"passage","sequence":191,"address":"3.191","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0191"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0192","text":"|\n\n (Report from the Select Committee on the Bankacts. July\n 1858, p. LXXI.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":192,"address":"3.192","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0192"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0193","text":"At a certain degree of development and extent of commodity production, the\n function of money as means of payment extends beyond the sphere of commodity circu-\n lation. It becomes the general commodity of contracts [Note: “The Course of Trade being thus turned, from exchanging of goods for\n goods, or delivering and taking, to selling and paying, all the bargains\n . . . . are now stated upon the foot of a Price in Money.” (“An Essay upon\n Publick Credit. 3ed. Lond. 1710”, p. 8.)] .\n Rents, taxes, and so forth are transformed from payments in kind into money-\n payments. How much this transformation is conditioned by the total form of the\n process of production is shown, for example, by the twice-failed\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"102\", \"facs\": \"#f0121\"} --> attempt of the Roman Empire to levy all imposts in money.","kind":"passage","sequence":193,"address":"3.193","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0193"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0194","text":"The immense misery of the French rural population under Louis XIV, which\n Boisguillebert, Marshal Vauban, and so forth, denounce so eloquently, was due not\n only to the height of taxation, but also to the transformation of tax in kind\n into money tax [Note: “L’argent est devenu le bourreau de toutes les choses.” The financial\n art is the “alambic qui a fait évaporer une quantité effrayable de biens et de\n denrées pour faire ce fatal précis.” “L’argent déclare la guerre à tout le genre\n humain.” (Boisguillebert: “Dissertation sur la nature des\n richesses, de l’argent et des tributs”, edit. Daire, “Écono-\n mistes financiers. Paris 1843, t. I, p. 413, 419, 417.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":194,"address":"3.194","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0194"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0195","text":"If, on the other hand, the natural form of ground-\n rent, in Asia at the same time the chief element of state tax, rests there\n upon relations of production which reproduce themselves with the immutability of\n natural conditions, that form of payment reacts so as to preserve\n the old form of production. It forms one of the secrets of the self-preser-\n vation of the Turkish Empire. If the foreign trade imposed upon Japan\n by Europe entails the transformation of rent in kind into money-rent,\n then its exemplary agriculture is done for. Its narrow economic conditions\n of existence will dissolve.","kind":"passage","sequence":195,"address":"3.195","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0195"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0196","text":"In every country, certain general terms of payment become es-\n tablished. They rest partly, apart from other circuits of repro-\n duction, upon the natural conditions of production bound to the change of\n seasons. They likewise regulate payments which do not arise directly\n from commodity circulation, such as taxes, rents, and so forth. The\n mass of money required on certain days of the year for these payments dis-\n persed over the whole surface of society causes periodic, but quite superficial\n perturbations in the economy of means of payment [Note: “Whit Monday 1824,” Mr. Craig relates to the parliamentary investi-\n gative committee of 1826, “there was such an enormous demand for banknotes\n in Edinburgh that by 11 o’clock we did not have a single note left in our custody.","kind":"passage","sequence":196,"address":"3.196","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0196"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0197","text":"We sent successively to the various banks to borrow some,\n but could obtain none, and many transactions could be settled only\n by slips of paper. By 3 o’clock in the afternoon, however, all the notes had\n already returned to the banks from which they had issued. They had only\n changed hands.” Although the effective average circulation\n of banknotes in Scotland amounts to less than 3 million pounds sterling, nevertheless, at ver-] . From the law concerning the velocity of circu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"103\", \"facs\": \"#f0122\"} --> lation of means of payment it follows that, for all periodic pay-\n ments, whatever their source, the necessary mass of\n means of payment stands in inverse proportion to the length\n of the payment periods [Note: To the question “if there were occasion to raise 40 millions p.","kind":"passage","sequence":197,"address":"3.197","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0197"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0198","text":"a., whether\n the same 6 millions (Gold) would suffice for such revolutions and circu-\n lations thereof as trade requires?”, Petty answers with his customary\n mastery: “I answer yes: for the expense being 40 millions, if the revolu-\n tions were in such short circles, viz. weekly, as happens among\n poor artizans and labourers, who receive and pay every Saturday, then \\frac{40}{52} parts\n of 1 million of money would answer these ends; but if the circles be quar-\n terly, according to our custom of paying rent, and gathering taxes, then 10\n millions were requisite. Wherefore supposing payments in general to be of a\nmixed circle between one week and 12, then add 10 millions to \\frac{40}{52}, the\n half of the which will be 5½, so as if we have 5½ mill., we have enough.”\n (William Petty: “Political Anatomy of Ireland. 1672”, edit.\nLond. 1691, p. 13, 14.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":198,"address":"3.198","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0198"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0199","text":"The development of money as means of payment necessitates accumulations of money for the dates on which the sums owed fall due. While hoard formation as an independent form of enrichment disappears with the progress of bourgeois society, it grows conversely with the same progress in the form of reserve funds of means of payment.","kind":"passage","sequence":199,"address":"3.199","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0199"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0200","text":"## c) World Money.","kind":"passage","sequence":200,"address":"3.200","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0200"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0201","text":"On leaving the inner sphere of circulation, money casts off again the local forms there sprouting from it—standard of prices, coin, subsidiary coin, and token of value—and falls back into the original bullion form of the precious metals. In world trade commodities unfold their value universally. Their independent value-form therefore confronts them here also as world money. It is only on the world market that money func-\n[Note: different dates of payment during the year, every note in the possession of the bankers, altogether about 7 million pounds sterling, is called into activity. On these occasions the notes have one single and specific function to perform, and as soon as they have performed it, they flow back to the respective banks from which they issued.” (John Fullarton: “Regulation of Currencies. 2nd ed. Lond. 1845” p. 86 Nte.)","kind":"passage","sequence":201,"address":"3.201","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0201"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0202","text":"For understanding it must be added that in Scotland not cheques, but only notes, are issued for deposits.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"104\", \"facs\": \"#f0123\"} --> tions in its full extent as the commodity whose natural form is at the same time the immediate social form of realization of human labour in the abstract. Its mode of existence is adequate to its concept.","kind":"passage","sequence":202,"address":"3.202","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0202"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0203","text":"Within the inner sphere of circulation only one commodity can serve as measure of value and hence as money. On the world market a double measure of value prevails, gold and silver [Note: Hence the absurdity of all legislation which prescribes to national banks that they should hoard only the precious metal which functions as money within the country. The thus self-created “golden obstacles” of the Bank of England, for example, are well known. On the great historical epochs of the relative change in value of gold and silver see Karl Marx l. c. p. 136 sq.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":203,"address":"3.203","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0203"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0204","text":"World money functions as universal means of payment, universal means of purchase, and absolutely social materiality of wealth as such (universal wealth). The function as means of payment, for the settlement of international balances, predominates. Hence the watchword of the mercantile system—balance of trade [Note: The opponents of the mercantile system, which treated the settlement of an excess balance of trade by gold and silver as the purpose of world trade, for their part entirely misunderstood the function of world money. How the false conception of the laws regulating the quantity of means of circulation is merely reflected in the false conception of the international movement of the precious metals, I have demonstrated in detail with regard to Ricardo. (l. c. p. 150 sqq.)","kind":"passage","sequence":204,"address":"3.204","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0204"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0205","text":"Its false dogma: “An unfavourable balance of trade never arises but from a redundant currency … The exportation of the coin is caused by its cheapness, and is not the effect, but the cause of an unfavourable balance” is therefore already to be found in Barbon: “The Balance of Trade, if there be one, is not the cause of sending away the money out of a nation: but that proceeds from the difference of the value of Bullion in every country.” (N. Barbon l. c. p. 59, 60.) MacCulloch, in “The Literature of Political Economy, a classified catalogue. Lond. 1845,” praises Barbon for this anticipation, but wisely avoids even mentioning the naïve forms in which the absurd premises of the “currency principle” still appear in B.","kind":"passage","sequence":205,"address":"3.205","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0205"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0206","text":"The uncriticality and indeed dishonesty of that catalogue culminate in the sections on the history of monetary theory, because Mac Culloch there wags his tail as a sycophant of Lord Overstone (ex-banker Loyd), whom he calls “facile princeps argentariorum”.] ! Gold and silver serve essentially as international means of purchase whenever the customary equilibrium of material exchange between different nations is suddenly disturbed. Finally, as absolutely social materiality of wealth, where the question is neither purchase nor payment, but transfer of wealth from one country to another, and where this transfer in commodity form is excluded either by the conjunctures of the commodity market or by the purpose to be fulfilled itself [Note: E. g.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"105\", \"facs\": \"#f0124\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":206,"address":"3.206","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0206"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0207","text":"in the case of subsidies, money loans for the conduct of war, or for the resumption of cash payments by banks, etc., value may be required precisely in money form.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":207,"address":"3.207","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0207"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0208","text":"As for its internal circulation, every country requires a reserve fund for world-market circulation. The functions of hoards thus arise partly from the function of money as an internal means of circulation and payment, and partly from its function as world money. In the latter role the actual money commodity, corporeal gold and silver, is always required, which is why James Steuart expressly characterizes gold and silver, in distinction from their merely local representatives, as money of the world.","kind":"passage","sequence":208,"address":"3.208","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0208"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0209","text":"The movement of the stream of gold and silver is twofold. On the one hand, it rolls from its sources over the whole world market, where it is intercepted to varying extents by the different national spheres of circulation, in order to enter the internal channels of circulation, replace worn gold and silver coins, furnish the material for luxury commodities, and congeal into hoards [Note: “L’argent se partage entre les nations relativement au besoin qu’elles en ont … étant toujours attiré par les productions.” (Le Trosne l. c. p. 916.) “The mines which are continually giving gold and silver, do give sufficient to supply such a needful balance to every nation.” (J. Vanderlint l. c. p. 40.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":209,"address":"3.209","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0209"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0210","text":"This first movement is mediated by the direct exchange of the national labours realized in commodities for the labour realized in precious metals of the gold- and silver-producing countries. On the other hand, gold and silver run continuously back and forth between the different national spheres of circulation, a movement which follows the unceasing oscillations of the exchange rate [Note: “Exchanges rise and fall every week, and at some particular times in the year run high against a nation, and at other times run as high on the contrary.” (N. Barbon l. c. p. 39.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":210,"address":"3.210","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0210"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0211","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"106\", \"facs\": \"#f0125\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":211,"address":"3.211","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0211"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0212","text":"Countries of developed bourgeois production restrict the hoards massively concentrated in bank reservoirs to the minimum required for their specific functions [Note: These different functions can come into dangerous conflict as soon as the function of a conversion fund for banknotes is added.] . With certain exceptions, a conspicuous overfilling of the hoard reservoirs above their average level indicates stagnation of commodity circulation or an interrupted flow of commodity metamorphosis [Note: “What money is more than of absolute necessity for a Home Trade, is dead stock, and brings no profit to that country it’s kept in, but as it is transported in Trade, as well as imported.” (John Bellers l. c. p. 12.) “What if we have too much coin?","kind":"passage","sequence":212,"address":"3.212","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0212"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0213","text":"We may melt down the heaviest and turn it into the splendour of plate, vessels or ustensils of gold and silver; or send it out as a commodity, where the same is wanted or desired; or let it out at interest, where interest is high.” (W. Petty: “Quantulumcunque”, p. 39.) “Money is but the fat of the Body Politick, whereof too much does as often hinder its agility, as too little makes it sick. . . . . . . . as fat lubricates the motion of the muscles, feeds in want of victuals, fills up uneven cavities, and beautifies the body; so doth money in the state quicken its action, feeds from abroad in time of dearth at home; evens accounts … and beautifies the whole, although”, concluding ironically, “more especially the particular persons that have it in plenty.” (W. Petty: “Political anatomy of Ireland” p. 14.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":213,"address":"3.213","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0213"]}],"source_digest":"e09dc02c5d43875ef3a87ec024025b596a6771e575f35c049689aa73e82825ba","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV","title":"Zweites Kapitel. Die Verwandlung von Geld in Kapital.","order":4,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_ZKDV","text":"## Zweites Kapitel.\n Die Verwandlung von Geld in Kapital.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## 1) Die allgemeine Formel des Kapitals.\n\n\n\n\nDie Waarencirculation ist der Ausgangspunkt des Kapitals. Waaren-\n produktion, Waarencirculation und entwickelte Waarencirculation, Handel,\n bilden daher stets die historischen Voraussetzungen, unter denen\n das Kapital entsteht. Von der Schöpfung des modernen Welthandels und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"107\", \"facs\": \"#f0126\"} --> Weltmarkts im 16. Jahrhundert datirt die moderne Lebensgeschichte des\n Kapitals.\n\n\n\n\nSehn wir ab vom stofflichen Inhalt der Waarencirculation, vom Aus-\n tausch der verschiednen Gebrauchswerthe, und betrachten wir nur die öko-\n nomischen Formen, die dieser Prozess erzeugt, so finden wir als sein letz-\n tes Produkt das Geld. Diess letzte Produkt der Waarencirculation ist\ndie erste Erscheinungsform des Kapitals.\n\n\n\n\nHistorisch tritt das Kapital dem Grundeigenthum überall zunächst in\n der Form von Geld gegenüber, von Geldvermögen, Kaufmannskapital\n und Wucherkapital [Note: Der Gegensatz zwischen der auf persönlichen Knechtschafts- und Herr-\n schaftsverhältnissen beruhenden Macht des Grundeigenthums und der unpersön-\n lichen Macht des Geldes ist klar gefasst in den zwei französischen Sprichworten:\n „Nulle terre sans seigneur.“ „L’argent n’a pas de maître.“] . Jedoch bedarf es nicht des Rückblicks auf die\n Entstehungsgeschichte des Kapitals, um das Geld als seine erste Erschei-\n nungsform zu erkennen. Dieselbe Geschichte spielt täglich vor unsern\n Augen. Jedes neue Kapital betritt in erster Instanz die Bühne, d. h. den\n Markt, Waarenmarkt, Arbeitsmarkt, oder Geldmarkt, immer noch als Geld,\n Geld, das sich durch bestimmte Prozesse in Kapital verwandeln soll.\n\n\n\n\nGeld als Geld und Geld als Kapital unterscheiden sich zu-\n nächst nur durch ihre verschiedene Circulationsform.\n\n\n\n\nDie unmittelbare Form der Waarencirculation ist W — G — W,\n Verwandlung von Waare in Geld und Rückverwandlung von Geld in Waare,\nverkaufen um zu kaufen. Neben dieser Form finden wir aber eine\n zweite, spezifisch unterschiedene vor, die Form G — W — G, Verwand-\n lung von Geld in Waare und Rückverwandlung von Waare in Geld, kau-\n fen um zu verkaufen. Geld, das in seiner Bewegung diese letztre\n Circulationsform beschreibt, verwandelt sich in Kapital, wird Kapital und\nist schon an sich, d. h. seiner Bestimmung nach, Kapital.\n\n\n\n\nSehn wir uns die Circulation G — W — G näher an. Es ist ein\n Prozess, der gleich dem der einfachen Waarencirculation, zwei entgegen-\n gesetzte Phasen durchläuft und ihre Einheit bildet. In der ersten Phase,\n G — W, Kauf, wird das Geld in Waare verwandelt. In der zweiten\n Phase, W — G, Verkauf, wird die Waare in Geld rückverwandelt.\n Die Einheit beider Phasen aber, die Gesammtbewegung, stellt sich\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"108\", \"facs\": \"#f0127\"} --> dar als Austausch von Geld gegen Waare und Wiederaustausch dersel-\n ben Waare gegen Geld, Waare kaufen um sie zu verkaufen, oder, wenn\n man die formellen Unterschiede von Kauf und Verkauf übersieht, mit dem\n Geld Waare und mit der Waare Geld kaufen [Note: „Avec de l’argent on achète des marchandises, et avec des marchandises\n on achète de l’argent.“ (Mercier de la Rivière: „L’ordre naturel\n et essentiel des sociétés publiques“, p. 543.)] . Was aber das Resultat\n des Prozesses angeht, so erlischt er im Austausch von Geld gegen\n Geld, G — G. Wenn ich für 100 Pfd. St. 2000 Pfund Baum-\n wolle kaufe und die 2000 Pfund Baumwolle wieder für 110 Pfd. St. ver-\n kaufe, so habe ich schliesslich 100 Pfd. St. gegen 110 Pfd. St. ausge-\n tauscht, Geld gegen Geld.\n\n\n\n\nEs ist nun zwar augenscheinlich, dass der Circulationsprozess G —\n W — G abgeschmackt und inhaltslos wäre, wollte man vermittelst seines\n Umwegs denselben Geldwerth gegen denselben Geldwerth, also z. B.\n 100 Pfd. St. gegen 100 Pfd. St. austauschen. Ungleich einfacher und\n sichrer wäre die Methode des Schatzbildners, der die 100 Pfd. St. fest-\n hält, statt sie der Circulationsgefahr preiszugeben. Andrerseits, ob der\n Kaufmann die mit 100 Pfd. St. gekaufte Baumwolle wieder verkauft zu\n 110 Pfd. St., oder ob er sie zu 100 Pfd. St. und selbst zu 50 Pfd. St.\n losschlagen muss, unter allen Umständen hat sein Geld eine eigenthüm-\n liche und originelle Bewegung beschrieben, durchaus verschieden von der,\n die es in der einfachen Waarencirculation beschreibt, z. B. in der Hand\n des Bauern, der Korn verkauft und mit dem so gelösten Geld Kleider\n kauft. Es gilt also zunächst die Charakteristik der Formunterschiede\n zwischen den Kreisläufen G — W — G und W — G — W. Damit wird\n sich zugleich der inhaltliche Unterschied ergeben, der hinter diesen Form-\n unterschieden lauert.\n\n\n\n\nSehn wir zunächst, was beiden Formen gemeinsam.\n\n\n\n\nBeide Kreisläufe zerfallen in dieselben zwei entgegengesetzten Phasen,\n W — G, Verkauf, und G — W, Kauf.. Jede dieser Phasen für sich be-\n trachtet, ist kein Unterschied zu erkennen. Die den Prozess eingehenden\n Elemente sind in beiden Formen dieselben, Waare und Geld. In jedem\n Abschnitt der beiden Kreisläufe stehn sich dieselben ökonomischen Charak-\n termasken gegenüber, Käufer und Verkäufer. In beiden Prozessen treten\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"109\", \"facs\": \"#f0128\"} --> drei Contrahenten auf, so dass aber stets nur ein Contrahent abwechselnd\n als Käufer und Verkäufer figurirt, während von den beiden andern Con-\n trahenten der eine nur verkauft und der andre nur kauft. Beide Kreisläufe\n endlich sind Einheiten derselben entgegengesetzten Phasen.\n\n\n\n\nDer Formunterschied zwischen den Prozessen W — G — W und\n G — W — G springt erst in’s Auge, sobald man, statt der zwei Phasen,\n worin beide zerfallen, ihre Gesammtverläufe vergleicht. Was sie von vorn\n herein scheidet, ist die umgekehrte Reihenfolge derselben ent-\n gegengesetzten Circulationsphasen. Die einfache Waarencirculation be-\n ginnt mit dem Verkauf und endet mit dem Kauf, die Circulation des Gel-\n des als Kapital beginnt mit dem Kauf und endet mit dem Verkauf. Dort\n bildet die Waare, hier das Geld den Ausgangspunkt und Schlusspunkt\n der Bewegung. In der ersten Form funktionirt das Geld, in der andern\n umgekehrt die Waare als Mittler des Gesammtverlaufs.\n\n\n\n\nIn der Circulation W — G — W wird das Geld schliesslich in\n Waare verwandelt, die als Gebrauchswerth dient. Das Geld ist also defi-\n nitiv ausgegeben. In der umgekehrten Form G — W — G giebt der\n Käufer dagegen Geld aus, um als Verkäufer Geld einzunehmen. Er wirft\n Geld beim Kauf der Waare in die Circulation, um es ihr durch den Verkauf\n derselben Waare wieder zu entziehn. Er entlässt das Geld nur mit der\n hinterlistigen Absicht seiner wieder habhaft zu werden. Es wird daher\n nur vorgeschossen [Note: „When a thing is bought, in order to be sold again, the sum employed is\n called money advanced; when it is bought not to be sold, it may be said to\n be expended.“ (James Steuart: Works etc. edited by General\n Sir James Steuart, his son. Lond. 1801, v. I, p. 274.)] .\n\n\n\n\nIn der Form W — G — W wechselt dasselbe Geldstück\n zweimal die Stelle. Der Verkäufer erhält es vom Käufer und zahlt es\n weg an einen andern Verkäufer. Mit der Weggabe von Geld für Waare\n schliesst der Gesammtprozess ab, wie er mit der Einnahme von Geld für\n Waare begann. Umgekehrt in der Form G — W — G. Nicht das-\n selbe Geldstück, sondern dieselbe Waare wechselt hier zweimal\n die Stelle. Der Käufer erhält sie aus der Hand des Verkäufers und giebt\n sie weg in die Hand eines andern Käufers. Wie in der einfachen Waaren-\n circulation durch den zweimaligen Stellenwechsel dessel-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"110\", \"facs\": \"#f0129\"} --> ben Geldstücks sein definitives Uebergehn aus einer Hand\n in die andre bedingt ist, so hier durch den zweimaligen Stel-\n lenwechsel derselben Waare der Rückfluss des Geldes zu\n seinem ersten Ausgangspunkt.\n\n\n\n\nDer Rückfluss des Geldes zu seinem Ausgangspunkt\n hängt nicht davon ab, ob oder ob nicht die Waare theurer verkauft wird\n als sie gekauft war. Dieser Umstand beeinflusst nur die Grösse der rück-\n fliessenden Geldsumme. Das Phänomen des Rückflusses selbst findet\n statt, sobald die gekaufte Waare wieder verkauft, also der Kreislauf\n G — W — G vollständig beschrieben wird. Es ist diess also ein sinnlich\n wahrnehmbarer Unterschied zwischen der Circulation des Geldes als\n Kapital und seiner Circulation als blossem Geld.\n\n\n\n\nAllerdings kann auch in W — G — W Rückfluss des Geldes zu sei-\n nem Ausgangspunkt stattfinden, aber nur durch die Erneuerung oder Wie-\n derholung des Gesammtprozesses, nicht durch den Verlauf seiner eignen\n Momente. Wenn ich ein Quarter Korn verkaufe für 3 Pfd. St. und mit die-\n sen 3 Pfd. St. Kleider kaufe, sind die 3 Pfd. St. für mich definitiv veraus-\n gabt. Ich habe nichts mehr mit ihnen zu schaffen. Sie sind des Kleider-\n händlers. Verkaufe ich nun ein zweites Quarter Korn, so fliesst Geld zu\n mir zurück, aber nicht in Folge der ersten Transaktion, sondern nur in\n Folge ihrer Wiederholung. Es entfernt sich wieder von mir, sobald ich\n die zweite Transaktion zu Ende führe und von neuem kaufe. In der Cir-\n culation W — G — W hat also die Verausgabung des Geldes nichts mit\n seinem Rückfluss zu schaffen. In G — W — G dagegen ist der Rück-\n fluss des Geldes durch die Art seiner Verausgabung selbst bedingt. Ohne\n diesen Rückfluss ist die Operation missglückt oder der Prozess unter-\n brochen und noch nicht fertig, weil seine zweite Phase, der den Kauf er-\n gänzende und abschliessende Verkauf fehlt.\n\n\n\n\nDer Kreislauf W — G — W geht aus von dem Extrem einer Waare\n und schliesst ab mit dem Extrem einer andern Waare, die aus der Circulation\n heraus und der Consumtion anheim fällt. Consumtion, Befriedigung von Be-\n dürfnissen, mit einem Wort, Gebrauchswerth ist daher sein Endzweck.\n Der Kreislauf G — W — G geht dagegen aus von dem Extrem des Geldes\n und bewegt sich fort zu demselben Extrem als seinem Schluss. Sein treiben-\n des Motiv und bestimmender Zweck ist daher der Tauschwerth selbst.\n\n\n\n\nIn der einfachen Waarencirculation haben beide Extreme dieselbe\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"111\", \"facs\": \"#f0130\"} --> ökonomische Formbestimmtheit. Sie sind beide Waaren. Sie sind auch\n Waaren von derselben Werthgrösse. Aber sie sind zugleich qua-\n litativ verschiedne Gebrauchswerthe, z. B. Korn und Kleider.\n Der Produktenaustausch, der Wechsel der verschiednen Stoffe, worin sich\n die gesellschaftliche Arbeit darstellt, bildet hier den Inhalt der Bewegung.\n Anders in der Circulation G — W — G. Sie scheint auf den ersten\n Blick inhaltlos, weil tautologisch. Beide Extreme haben dieselbe öko-\n nomische Formbestimmtheit. Sie sind beide Geld. Sie unterscheiden\n sich auch nicht qualitativ als Gebrauchswerthe, denn Geld ist eben die\n verwandelte Gestalt der Waaren, worin ihre besondern Gebrauchswerthe\n ausgelöscht sind. Erst 100 Pfd. St. gegen Baumwolle und dann wieder\n dieselbe Baumwolle gegen 100 Pfd. St. austauschen, also auf einem Um-\n weg Geld gegen Geld, dasselbe gegen dasselbe, scheint eine ebenso zweck-\n lose als abgeschmackte Operation [Note: „On n’échange pas de l’argent contre de l’argent,“ ruft Mercier de la\n Rivière den Merkantilisten zu. (l. c. p. 486.) In einem Werke, welches ex\n professo vom „Handel“ und der „Spekulation“ handelt, liest man: „Aller\n Handel besteht im Austausch von Dingen verschiedner Art; und der Vortheil (für\n den Kaufmann?) entspringt eben aus dieser Verschiedenheit. Ein Pfund Brod\n gegen ein Pfund Brod austauschen, wäre ohne allen Vortheil … daher der vor-\n theilhafte Kontrast zwischen Handel und Spiel, welches nur Austausch\n von Geld gegen Geld ist.“ (Th. Corbet: „An Inquiry into\n the Causes and Modes of the Wealth of Individuals; or the\n Principles of Trade and Speculation explained. London. 1841“,\n p. 5.) Obgleich Corbet nicht sieht, dass G — G, Geld gegen Geld austauschen,\n die charakteristische Circulationsform, nicht nur des Handelskapitals, sondern\nallen Kapitals ist, giebt er wenigstens zu, dass diese Form einer Art des Handels,\n der Spekulation, mit dem Spiel gemein sei, aber dann kommt MacCulloch\n und findet, dass Kaufen um zu verkaufen Speculiren ist, und der Unter-\n schied zwischen Speculation und Handel also wegfällt. „Every transaction\n in which an individual buys produce in order to sell it again, is,\n in fact, a speculation.“ (Mac Culloch: A Dictionary practical\n etc. of Commerce. London 1847, p. 1056.) Ungleich naiver Pinto,\n der Pindar der Amsterdamer Börse: „Le commerce est un jeu (dieser\n Satz entlehnt aus Locke); et ce n’est pas avec des gueux qu’on peut gagner. Si\n l’on gagnait long temps en tout avec tous, il faudrait rendre de bon accord les\n plus grandes parties du profit, pour recommencer le jeu.“ (Pinto: Traité de\n la Circulation et du Credit. Amsterdam 1771, p. 231.)] . Eine Geldsumme kann sich von der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"112\", \"facs\": \"#f0131\"} --> andern Geldsumme überhaupt nur durch ihre Grösse unterscheiden. Der\n Prozess G — W — G schuldet seinen Inhalt daher keinem qualita-\n tiven Unterschied seiner Extreme, denn sie sind beide Geld, son-\n dern nur ihrer quantitativen Verschiedenheit. Schliesslich\n wird der Circulation mehr Geld entzogen als Anfangs hineingeworfen\n ward. Die zu 100 Pfd. St. gekaufte Baumwolle wird z. B. wieder ver-\n kauft zu 100 + 10 Pfd. St. oder 110 Pfd. St. Die vollständige Form\n dieses Prozesses ist daher G — W — G', wo G' = G + ∆ G, d. h.\n gleich der ursprünglich vorgeschossenen Geldsumme plus einem Incre-\n ment. Diess Increment oder den Ueberschuss über den ursprünglichen\n Werth nenne ich Mehrwerth (surplus value). Der ursprünglich\n vorgeschossene Werth erhält sich daher nicht nur in der Circulation, son-\n dern in ihr verändert er seine Werthgrösse, setzt einen Mehr-\n werth zu, oder verwerthet sich. Und diese Bewegung verwandelt\n ihn in Kapital.\n\n\n\n\nEs ist zwar auch möglich, dass in W — G — W die beiden Extreme\n W, W, z. B. Korn und Kleider, quantitativ verschiedne Werth-\n grössen sind. Der Bauer kann sein Korn über dem Werth verkauft\n oder die Kleider unter ihrem Werth gekauft haben. Er kann seinerseits\n vom Kleiderhändler geprellt werden. Solche Werthverschiedenheit bleibt\n jedoch für diese Circulationsform selbst rein zufällig. Sinn\n und Verstand verliert sie nicht schier, wie der Prozess G — W — G,\n wenn die beiden Extreme, Korn und Kleider z. B., Aequivalente sind.\n Ihr Gleichwerth ist hier vielmehr Bedingung des normalen Verlaufs.\n\n\n\n\nDie Wiederholung oder Erneurung des Verkaufs um zu kaufen\n findet, wie dieser Prozess selbst, Mass und Ziel an einem ausser ihm lie-\n genden Endzwecke, der Consumtion, der Befriedigung bestimmter Bedürf-\n nisse. Im Kauf für den Verkauf dagegen sind Anfang und Ende\n dasselbe, Geld, Tauschwerth, und schon dadurch ist die Bewegung\n endlos. Allerdings ist aus G, G + ∆ G geworden, aus den 100 Pfd.\n St., 100 + 10. Aber blos die Form betrachtet, sind 110 Pfd. St. das-\n selbe wie 100 Pfd. St., nämlich Geld. Und die Werthgrösse betrachtet,\n sind 110 Pfd. eine beschränkte Werthsumme wie 100 Pfd. St.\n Würden die 110 Pfd. St. als Geld verausgabt, so fielen sie aus ihrer\n Rolle. Sie hörten auf Kapital zu sein. Der Circulation entzogen, ver-\n steinern sie zum Schatz und kein Farthing wächst ihnen an, ob sie bis\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"113\", \"facs\": \"#f0132\"} --> zum jüngsten Tag fortlagern. Handelt es sich also einmal um die Werth-\n grösse als solche, um Verwerthung des Werths, so besteht das-\n selbe Bedürfniss für die Verwerthung von 110 Pfd. St. wie für die von\n 100 Pfd. St., da beide beschränkte Ausdrücke des Tauschwerths sind,\n beide also denselben Beruf haben sich dem Reichthum schlechthin durch\n Grössenausdehnung anzunähern. Allerdings unterscheidet sich für einen\n Augenblick der ursprünglich vorgeschossene Werth 100 Pfd. St. von dem\n in der Circulation ihm zuwachsenden Mehrwerth von 10 Pfd. St., aber\n dieser Unterschied zerfliesst sofort wieder. Es kommt am Ende des Pro-\n zesses nicht auf der einen Seite der Originalwerth von 100 Pfd. St. und\n auf der andern Seite der Mehrwerth von 10 Pfd. St. heraus. Was heraus-\n kommt ist Ein Werth von 110 Pfd. St., der sich ganz in derselben ent-\n sprechenden Form befindet, um den Verwerthungsprozess zu beginnen, wie\n die ursprünglichen 100 Pfd. St. Geld kommt am Ende der Bewegung\n wieder als ihr Anfang heraus [Note: „Das Kapital theilt sich … in das ursprüngliche Kapital und den Gewinn,\n den Zuwachs des Kapitals … obwohl die Praxis selbst diesen Gewinn sogleich\n wieder zum Kapital schlägt und mit diesem in Fluss setzt.“ (F. Engels: „Um-\n risse zu einer Kritik der Nationalökonomie“ in „Deutsch-\n Französische Jahrbücher, herausgegeben von Arnold Ruge und\n Karl Marx.“ Paris 1844, p. 99.)] . Wenn die einfache Waarencirculation\n daher im Gebrauchswerth eine ihr von aussen gesetzte Schranke\n hat, ist die Bewegung des Kapitals dagegen masslos, indem sie in ihrem\n Abschluss das Prinzip und den Trieb ihrer Wiedererneuerung findet, und\n ihr Ziel, die Verwerthung des Werths, am Ende des Prozesses\n eben so wenig erreicht ist als am Anfang [Note: Aristoteles stellt der Chrematistik die Oekonomik entgegen.\n Er geht von der Oekonomik aus. So weit sie Erwerbskunst, beschränkt sie sich\n auf die Verschaffung der zum Leben nothwendigen und für das Haus oder den\n Staat nützlichen Güter. „Der wahre Reichthum (ὁ ἀληϑινὸς πλοῦτος) besteht aus\n solchen Gebrauchswerthen; denn das zum guten Leben genügende Mass dieser\n Art von Besitz ist nicht unbegrenzt. Es giebt aber eine zweite Art der Er-\n werbskunst, die vorzugsweise und mit Recht Chrematistik heisst, in Folge\n deren keine Grenze des Reichthums und Besitzes zu existiren scheint. Der\nWaarenhandel (ἡ ϰαπηλιπὴ heisst wörtlich Kramhandel und Aristoteles nimmt] .\n\n\n\n\nAls bewusster Träger dieses Prozesses wird der Geldbesitzer Kapita-\n list. Seine Person, oder vielmehr seine Tasche, ist der Ausgangspunkt\n [Running matter: I. 8] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"114\", \"facs\": \"#f0133\"} --> und der Rückkehrpunkt des Geldes. Der objektive Inhalt jenes\n Prozesses — Verwerthung des Werths — ist sein subjektiver Zweck,\n und nur soweit wachsende Aneignung des abstrakten Reichthums das allein\n treibende Motiv seiner Operationen, funktionirt er als Kapitalist oder\n personificirtes, mit Willen und Bewusstsein begabtes Kapital. Der Ge-\n brauchswerth ist also nie als unmittelbarer Zweck des Kapitalisten zu\n behandeln [Note: „Commodities (hier im Sinn von Gebrauchswerthen) are not the termi-\n nating object of the trading capitalist … money is his terminating object.“ (Th.\n Chalmers: On Politic. Econ. etc. 2nd edit. Lond. 1832, p. 166.)] . Auch nicht der einzelne Gewinn, sondern nur die rastlose\n Bewegung des Gewinnens [Note: „Il mercante non conta quasi per niente il lucro fatto, ma mira sempre al\n futuro.“ (A. Genovesi: Lezioni di Economia Civile (1765). Aus-\n gabe der italienischen Oekonomen von Custodi, Parte Moderna, t. VIII,\n p. 139.)] . Dieser absolute Bereicherungstrieb, diese\n [Note: diese Form, weil in ihr der Gebrauchswerth vorherrscht) gehört von Natur nicht\n zur Chrematistik, denn hier bezieht sich der Austausch nur auf das für sie selbst\n (Käufer und Verkäufer) Nöthige.“ Daher, entwickelt er weiter, war auch die ur-\n sprüngliche Form des Waarenhandels der Tauschhandel, aber mit seiner Aus-\n dehnung entstand nothwendig das Geld. Mit der Erfindung des Geldes musste\n sich der Tauschhandel nothwendig zur ϰαπηλιϰὴ, zum Waarenhandel entwickeln,\n und dieser, im Widerspruch zu seiner ursprünglichen Tendenz, bildete sich zur\n Chrematistik aus, zur Kunst Geld zu machen. Die Chrematistik nun unterscheidet\n sich von der Oekonomik dadurch, dass „für sie die Circulation die Quelle des\n Reichthums ist (ποιητιϰὴ χϱημάτων … διὰ χϱημάτων διαβολῆς).\n Und um das Geld scheint sie sich zu drehen, denn das Geld ist der Anfang\n und das Ende dieser Art von Austausch (τὸ γάϱ νόμισμα στοιχεῖον\n ϰαὶ πέϱας τῆς ἀλλαγῆς ἐστίν). Daher ist auch der Reichthum, wie ihn die Chre-\n matistik anstrebt, unbegrenzt. Wie nämlich jede Kunst, der ihr Ziel nicht als\n Mittel, sondern als letzter Endzweck gilt, unbegrenzt in ihrem Streben ist, denn\n sie sucht sich ihm stets mehr zu nähern, während die Künste, die nur Mittel zum\n Zwecke verfolgen, nicht unbegrenzt sind, da der Zweck selbst ihnen die Grenze\n setzt, so giebt es auch für diese Chrematistik keine Schranke ihres Ziels, sondern\n ihr Ziel ist absolute Bereicherung. Die Oekonomik, nicht die Chrematistik hat\n eine Grenze . . . . die erstere bezweckt ein vom Gelde selbst Verschiednes, die\n andere seine Vermehrung . . . . Die Verwechslung beider Formen, die in einander\n überspielen, verursacht einige die Erhaltung und Vermehrung des Geldes ins Un-\n endliche als Endziel der Oekonomik zu betrachten.“ (Aristoteles: De Rep.\n edit. Bekker, lib. I, c. 8 und 9 passim.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"115\", \"facs\": \"#f0134\"} --> leidenschaftliche Jagd auf den Tauschwerth [Note: „Die unauslöschliche Leidenschaft für den Gewinn, die auri sacra fames\n bestimmt stets den Kapitalisten.“ (Mac Culloch: The Principles of Polit.\n Econ. London 1830, p. 163.) Diese Einsicht verhindert denselben Mac Cul-\n loch und Cons. natürlich nicht, in theoretischen Verlegenheiten, z. B. bei Behand-\n lung der Ueberproduktion, denselben Kapitalisten in einen guten Bürger zu ver-\n wandeln, dem es sich nur um den Gebrauchswerth handelt und der sogar einen\n wahren Wehrwolfsheisshunger für Stiefel, Hüte, Eier, Kattune und andre höchst\n familiäre Sorten von Gebrauchswerth entwickelt.]  ist dem Kapitalisten mit dem\n Schatzbildner gemein, aber während der Schatzbildner nur der verrückte\n Kapitalist, ist der Kapitalist der rationelle Schatzbildner. Die Unvergäng-\n lichkeit des Tauschwerths, die der Schatzbildner anstrebt, indem er das\n Geld vor der Circulation zu retten sucht [Note: „Σώζειν“ ist einer der charakteristischen Ausdrücke der Griechen für\n das Schatzbilden.] , erreicht der klügere Kapi-\n talist, indem er es stets von neuem der Circulation preisgiebt [Note: „Questo infinito che le cose non hanno in progresso, hanno in giro.“\n (Galiani.)] .\n\n\n\n\nIn der einfachen Circulation entwickeln die Waaren ihren Werth zu\n verschiednen ihrem Gebrauchswerth gegenübertretenden selbstständigen\n Formen, d. h. zu Geldformen, die den Austausch vermitteln und in seinem\n Endresultat verschwinden. In der Circulation G — W — G funktioniren\n beide, Waare und Geld, nur als verschiedne Existenzweisen\n des Werths selbst, das Geld seine allgemeine, die Waare seine be-\n sondre, so zu sagen nur verkleidete Existenzweise [Note: „Ce n’est pas la matière qui fait le capital, mais la valeur de ces ma-\n tières.“ (J. B. Say: Traité de L’Econ. Polit. 3ème ed. Paris 1817,\n t. I, p. 428.)] . Er geht beständig\n aus der einen Form in die andre über, ohne sich in dieser Bewegung zu\n verlieren und verwandelt sich so in ein automatisches, in sich selbst pro-\n zessirendes Subjekt. Fixirt man eine der besondern Erscheinungsformen,\n worin er sich abwechselnd darstellt, so erhält man die Erklärungen:\nKapitalist Geld, Kapitalist Waare [Note: „Currency (!) employed to productive purposes is capital.“ (Mac\n Leod: „The Theory and Practice of Banking. London 1855“, v. I,\n c. I.) „Capital is commodities.“ (James Mill: „Elements of Pol. Econ.\n Lond. 1821“, p. 74.)] . In der That aber wird\n [Running matter: 8*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"116\", \"facs\": \"#f0135\"} -->der Werth hier das Subjekt eines Prozesses, worin er unter\n dem beständigen Wechsel der Formen von Geld und Waare, seine Grösse\n selbst verändert, sich als Mehrwerth von sich selbst als ursprünglichem\n Werth abstösst, sich selbst verwerthet. Denn die Bewegung,\n worin er Mehrwerth zusetzt, ist seine eigne Bewegung, seine Verwerthung\n ist also Selbstverwerthung. Er hat die occulte Qualität erhalten,\n Werth zu setzen, weil er Werth ist. Er wirft lebendige Junge oder legt\n wenigstens goldne Eier.\n\n\n\n\nAls das übergreifende Subjekt eines solchen Prozesses, worin er\n Geldform und Waarenform bald annimmt, bald abstösst, sich aber in die-\n sem Wechsel erhält und ausreckt, bedarf der Werth natürlich vor allem\n einer selbsstständigen Form, wodurch seine Identität mit sich selbst konsta-\n tirt werden kann. Und diese Form besitzt er nur im Gelde. Diess\n bildet daher Ausgangspunkt und Schlusspunkt jeden Verwerthungspro-\n zesses. Er war 100 Pfd. St., er ist jetzt 110 Pfd. St. u. s. w. Aber das\n Geld selbst gilt hier nur als eine Form des Werths, denn er hat deren\nzwei. Und die Annahme der Waarenform bildet grade das vermit-\n telnde Moment seiner Bewegung. Das Geld tritt hier also nicht pole-\n misch gegen die Waare auf, wie in der Schatzbildung. Der Kapitalist\n weiss, dass alle Waaren, wie lumpig sie immer aussehn oder wie schlecht\n sie immer riechen mögen, im Glauben und in der Wahrheit Geld, inner-\n lich verschnittene Juden sind. G — G', geldheckendes Geld —\n (money which begets money lautet die Beschreibung des Kapitals\n im Munde seiner ersten Dolmetscher, der Mercantilisten), — ist in\n der That nur die unmittelbare Erscheinungsform des Werth setzenden\n Werths, des sich selbst verwerthenden Werths.\n\n\n\n\nWenn der Tauschwerth in der Waarencirculation höchstens zur selbst-\n ständigen Form gegenüber dem Gebrauchswerth der Waare heranreift, so stellt\n er sich hier plötzlich dar als eine prozessirende, sich selbst bewegende Sub-\n stanz, für welche Waare und Geld beide blosse Formen. Aber noch mehr. Statt\n Waarenverhältnisse darzustellen, tritt er jetzt so zu sagen in ein Privat-\n verhältniss zu sich selbst. Er unterscheidet sich als ursprüng-\n licher Werth von sich selbst als Mehrwerth, als Gott Vater von sich selbst\n als Gottsohn, und beide sind vom selben Alter, und bilden in der That nur\n eine Person, denn nur durch den Mehrwerth von 10 Pfd. St. werden die vorge-\n schossenen 100 Pfd. St. Kapital, und sobald sie diess geworden, sobald der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"117\", \"facs\": \"#f0136\"} --> Sohn, und durch den Sohn der Vater erzeugt, verschwindet ihr Unterschied\n wieder und sind beide Eins, 110 Pfd. St.\n\n\n\n\nDer Werth wird also prozessirender Werth, prozessiren-\n des Geld und als solches Kapital. Er kommt aus der Circulation\n her, geht wieder in sie ein, erhält und vervielfältigt sich in ihr, kehrt ver-\n grössert aus ihr zurück und beginnt denselben Kreislauf stets wieder von\n neuem [Note: „Kapital . . . . permanenter sich vervielfältigender Werth“. (Sis\n mondi: Nouveaux Principes del’Econ. Polit., t. I, p. 90.)] .\n\n\n\n\nKaufen um zu verkaufen, oder vollständiger, kaufen um\n theurer zu verkaufen, G — W — G', scheint zwar nur einer Art\n des Kapitals, dem Kaufmannskapital, eigenthümliche Form. Aber\n auch das industrielle Kapital ist Geld, das sich in Waare verwan-\n delt und durch den Verkauf der Waare in mehr Geld rückverwandelt.\n Akte, die etwa zwischen dem Kauf und dem Verkaufe, ausserhalb der Cir-\n culationssphäre, vorgehn, ändern nichts an dieser Form der Bewegung.\n In dem zinstragenden Kapital endlich stellt sich die Form\n G — W — G' abgekürzt dar, im Resultat ohne die Vermittlung, so zu\n sagen im Lapidarstyl, als G — G', Geld, das gleich mehr Geld, Werth,\n der grösser als er selbst ist.\n\n\n\n\nIn der That also ist G — W — G' die allgemeine Formel\n des Kapitals, wie es unmittelbar in der Circulationssphäre erscheint.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## b) Widersprüche der allgemeinen Formel.\n\n\n\n\nDie Circulationsform, worin sich das Geld zum Kapital entpuppt,\n widerspricht allen früher entwickelten Gesetzen über die Natur der Waare,\n des Werths, des Geldes und der Circulation selbst. Was sie von der ein-\n fachen Waarencirculation unterscheidet, ist die umgekehrte Reihen-\n folge derselben zwei entgegengesetzten Prozesse, Verkauf und Kauf. Und\n wie sollte dieser rein formelle Unterschied die Natur dieser Prozesse um-\n zaubern?\n\n\n\n\nNoch mehr. Diese Umkehrung existirt nur für einen der drei\n Geschäftsfreunde, die mit einander handeln. Als Kapitalist kaufe ich\n Waare von A und verkaufe sie wieder an B, während ich als einfacher\n Waarenbesitzer Waare an B verkaufe und dann Waare von A kaufe. Für\n die Geschäftsfreunde A und B existirt dieser Unterschied nicht. Sie treten\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"118\", \"facs\": \"#f0137\"} --> nur als Käufer oder Verkäufer von Waaren auf. Ich selbst stehe ihnen\n jedesmal als einfacher Geldbesitzer oder Waarenbesitzer, Käufer oder Ver-\n käufer, gegenüber, und zwar trete ich in beiden Reihenfolgen der einen\n Person nur als Käufer und der andern nur als Verkäufer gegenüber, der\n einen als nur Geld, der andern als nur Waare, keiner von beiden als Kapi-\n tal oder Kapitalist oder Repräsentant von irgend etwas, das mehr als Geld\n oder Waare wäre oder eine andere Wirkung ausser der des Geldes oder der\n Waare ausüben könnte. Für mich bilden Kauf von A und Verkauf an\n B eine Reihenfolge. Aber der Zusammenhang zwischen diesen beiden\n Akten existirt nur für mich. A scheert sich nicht um meine Transaktion\n mit B, und B nicht um meine Transaktion mit A. Wollte ich ihnen etwa\n das besondere Verdienst klar machen, das ich mir durch die Umkeh-\n rung der Reihenfolge erworben, so würden sie mir beweisen, dass ich\n mich in der Reihenfolge selbst irre und dass die Gesammttransaktion\n nicht mit einem Kauf begann und einem Verkauf endete, sondern umgekehrt\n mit einem Verkauf begann und mit einem Kauf abschloss. In der That,\n mein erster Akt, der Kauf, war von A’s Standpunkt ein Verkauf, und mein\n zweiter Akt, der Verkauf, war von B’s Standpunkt ein Kauf. Nicht zu-\n frieden damit, werden A und B erklären, dass die ganze Reihenfolge über-\n flüssig und Hokus Pokus war. A wird die Waare direkt an B verkaufen\n und B sie direkt von A kaufen. Damit verschrumpft die ganze Trans-\n aktion in einen einseitigen Akt der gewöhnlichen Waarencirculation,\n vom Standpunkt A’s blosser Verkauf und vom Standpunkt B’s blosser Kauf.\n Wir sind also durch die Umkehrung der Reihenfolge nicht über die Sphäre\n der einfachen Waarencirculation hinausgekommen und müssen\n vielmehr zusehn, ob sie ihrer Natur nach Verwerthung der in sie hinein-\n gehenden Werthe und daher Bildung von Mehrwerth gestattet.\n\n\n\n\nNehmen wir den Circulationsprozess in einer Form, worin er sich als\n blosser Waarenaustausch darstellt. Diess ist stets der Fall, wenn beide\n Waarenbesitzer Waaren von einander kaufen und die Bilanz ihrer wechsel-\n seitigen Geldforderungen sich am Zahlungstag ausgleicht. Das Geld dient\n hier als Rechengeld, um die Werthe der Waaren in ihren Preisen aus-\n zudrücken, tritt aber nicht den Waaren selbst dinglich gegenüber. So-\n weit es sich um den Gebrauchswerth handelt, ist es klar, dass beide\n Austauscher hier gewinnen können. Beide veräussern Waaren, die\n ihnen als Gebrauchswerth nutzlos, und erhalten Waaren, deren sie zum Ge-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"119\", \"facs\": \"#f0138\"} --> brauch bedürfen. Und dieser Nutzen mag nicht der einzige sein. A, der\n Wein verkauft und Getreide kauft, producirt vielleicht mehr Wein als Ge-\n treidebauer B in derselben Arbeitszeit produciren könnte und Getreide-\n bauer B in derselben Arbeitszeit mehr Getreide als Weinbauer A\n produciren könnte. A erhält also für denselben Tauschwerth mehr Ge-\n treide und B mehr Wein als wenn jeder von den beiden, ohne Austausch,\n Wein und Getreide für sich selbst produciren müsste. Mit Bezug auf den\nGebrauchswerth also kann gesagt werden, dass „der Austausch eine\n Transaktion ist, worin beide Seiten gewinnen [Note: „L’échange est une transaction admirable dans laquelle les deux contrac-\n tants gagnent — toujours (!).“ (Destutt de Tracy: Traité de la Volonté\n et de ses Effets. Paris 1826, p. 68.) Dasselbe Buch erschien später als\n „Traité de l’Ec. Pol.“] .“ Anders mit dem\nTauschwerth. „Ein Mann, der viel Wein und kein Getreide besitzt,\n handelt mit einem Mann, der viel Getreide und keinen Wein besitzt und\n zwischen ihnen wird ausgetauscht Weizen zum Werth von 50 gegen einen\n Werth von 50 in Wein. Dieser Austausch ist keine Vermehrung\n des Tauschwerths weder für den einen, noch für den andern; denn\n bereits vor dem Austausch besass jeder von ihnen einen Werth gleich dem,\n den er sich vermittelst dieser Operation verschafft hat [Note: Mercier de la Rivière l. c. p. 544.] .“ Es ändert\n nichts an der Sache, wenn das Geld als Circulationsmittel zwischen die\n Waaren tritt und die Akte des Kaufs und Verkaufs sinnlich auseinander-\n fallen [Note: „Que l’une de ces deux valeurs soit argent, ou qu’elles soient toutes deux\n marchandises usuelles, rien de plus indifférent en soi.“ (Mercier de la Ri-\n vière l. c. p. 543.)] . Der Werth der Waaren ist in ihren Preisen dargestellt,\n bevor sie in die Circulation treten, also Voraussetzung und nicht Resultat\n derselben [Note: „Ce ne sont pas les contractants qui prononcent sur la valeur; elle est\n decidée avant la convention.“ (Le Trosne p. 906.)] .\n\n\n\n\nAbstrakt betrachtet, d. h. abgesehn von Umständen, die nicht aus\n den immanenten Gesetzen der einfachen Waarencirculation hervorfliessen,\n geht ausser dem Ersatz eines Gebrauchswerths durch einen andern nichts\n in ihr vor als eine Metamorphose, ein blosser Formwechsel der\n Waare. Derselbe Tauschwerth, d. h. dasselbe Quantum vergegen-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"120\", \"facs\": \"#f0139\"} --> ständlichter gesellschaftlicher Arbeit, bleibt in der Hand desselben Waaren-\n besitzers abwechselnd in Gestalt seiner Waare, des Geldes, worin sie sich\n verwandelt, der Waare, worin sich diess Geld rückverwandelt. Dieser\n Formwechsel schliesst keine Aenderung der Werthgrösse ein. Der Wech-\n sel aber, den der Tauschwerth der Waare selbst in diesem Prozess erfährt,\n beschränkt sich auf einen Wechsel seiner Geldform. Er existirt erst\n als Preis der zum Verkauf angebotenen Waare, dann als dieselbe\n Geldsumme, die in diesem Preise ausgedrückt ist, endlich als der Preis\n einer äquivalenten Waare. Dieser Formwechsel schliesst an und für\n sich eben so wenig eine Aenderung der Werthgrösse ein, wie das\n Auswechseln einer Fünfpfundnote gegen Sovereigns, halbe Sovereigns und\n Schillinge. Sofern also die Cirkulation der Waare nur einen Form-\n wechsel ihres Tauschwerths bedingt, bedingt sie, wenn das Phänomen\n rein vorgeht, Austausch von Aequivalenten. Die Vulgäröko-\n nomie selbst, so wenig sie ahnt, was der Werth ist, unterstellt daher, so\n oft sie in ihrer Art das Phänomen rein betrachten will, dass Nachfrage\n und Zufuhr sich decken, d. h. dass ihre Wirkung überhaupt fortfällt.\n Wenn also mit Bezug auf den Gebrauchswerth beide Austau-\n scher gewinnen können, können sie nicht beide gewinnen an Tausch-\n werth. Hier heisst es vielmehr: „Wo Gleichheit ist, ist kein Gewinn [Note: „Dove è eguagità, non è lucro.“ (Galiani: Della Moneta in\nCustodi: Parte Moderna, t. IV, p. 244.)] .“\n Waaren können zwar zu Preisen verkauft werden, die von ihren Werthen\n abweichen, aber diese Abweichung erscheint als Verletzung des Gesetzes\n des Waarenaustausches [Note: „L’échange devient désavantageux pour l’une des parties, lorsque quelque\n chose étrangère vient diminuer ou exagérer le prix: alors l’égalité est blessée, mais\n la lésion procède de cette cause et non de l’échange.“ (Le Trosne l. c.\n p. 904.)] . In seiner reinen Gestalt ist er ein Austausch\n von Aequivalenten, also kein Mittel sich an Werth zu bereichern [Note: „L’échange est de sa nature un contrat d’égalité qui se fait de valeur pour\n valeur égale. Il n’est donc pas un moyen de s’enrichir, puisque l’on\n donne autant que l’on reçoit.“ (Le Trosne l. c. p. 903.)] .\n\n\n\n\nHinter den Versuchen, die Waarencirkulation als Quelle von\n Mehrwerth darzustellen, lauert daher meist ein quid pro quo, eine Ver-\n wechslung von Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth. So z. B. bei Con-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"121\", \"facs\": \"#f0140\"} --> dillac: „Es ist falsch, dass man im Waarenaustausch gleichen Werth\n gegen gleichen Werth austauscht. Umgekehrt. Jeder der beiden Con-\n trahenten giebt immer einen kleineren für einen grösseren Werth …\n Tauschte man in der That immer gleiche Werthe aus, so wäre kein\n Gewinn zu machen für irgend einen Contrahenten. Aber alle beide\n gewinnen oder sollten doch gewinnen. Warum? Der Werth der Dinge\n besteht bloss in ihrer Beziehung auf unsere Bedürfnisse. Was für\n den einen mehr, ist für den andern weniger, und umgekehrt. … Man\n setzt nicht voraus, dass wir unsrer Consumtion unentbehrliche Dinge zum\n Verkauf ausbieten. . . . Wir wollen eine uns nutzlose Sache weggeben, um\n eine uns nothwendige zu erhalten; wir wollen weniger für mehr geben …\n Es war natürlich zu urtheilen, dass man im Austausch gleichen Werth für\n gleichen Werth gebe, so oft jedes der ausgetauschten Dinge an Werth\n demselben Quantum Geld gleich war … Aber eine andre Betrachtung\n muss noch in die Rechnung eingehn; es fragt sich, ob wir beide einen\nUeberfluss gegen etwas Nothwendiges austauschen [Note: Condillac: „Le Commerce et le Gouvernement“ (1776).\nÉdit. Daire et Molinari in den „Mélanges d’Économie Politique.\n Paris 1847“, p. 267.] .“ Man sieht,\n wie Condillac nicht nur Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth durcheinan-\n der wirft, sondern wahrhaft kindlich einer Gesellschaft mit entwickelter\n Waarenproduktion einen Zustand unterschiebt, worin der Producent seine\n Subsistenzmittel selbst producirt und nur den Ueberschuss über den eignen\n Bedarf, den Ueberfluss, in die Cirkulation wirft [Note: Le Trosne antwortet daher seinem Freunde Condillac sehr richtig:\n „Dans la société formée il n’y a pas de surabondant en aucun genre.“ Zugleich\n neckt er ihn mit der Glosse, dass „wenn beide Austauscher gleich viel mehr für\n gleich viel weniger erhalten, sie beide gleich viel erhalten.“ Weil Condillac\n noch nicht die geringste Ahnung von der Natur des Tauschwerths besitzt, ist\n er der passende Gewährsmann des Herrn Prof. Wilhelm Roscher für seine\n eignen Kinderbegriffe. Sieh dessen: „Die Grundlagen der National-\n ökonomie. Dritte Auflage. 1858“.] . Dennoch wird\nCondillac’s Argument häufig bei modernen Oekonomen wiederholt, na-\n mentlich wenn es gilt, die entwickelte Gestalt des Waarenaustausches, den\nHandel, als produktiv von Mehrwerth darzustellen. „Der Handel“,\n heisst es z. B., „fügt den Produkten Werth zu, denn dieselben\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"122\", \"facs\": \"#f0141\"} --> Produkte haben mehr Werth in den Händen des Consumenten als in den\n Händen des Producenten und er muss daher wörtlich (strictly) als Pro-\n duktionsakt betrachtet werden [Note: S. P. Newman: Elements of Polit. Econ. Andover and\n New - York 1835, p. 85.] .“ Aber man zahlt die Waaren nicht\n doppelt, das einemal ihren Gebrauchswerth und das andremal ihren Tausch\n werth. Und wenn der Gebrauchswerth der Waare dem Käufer nützlicher\n als dem Verkäufer, ist ihre Geldform dem Verkäufer nützlicher als dem\n Käufer. Würde er sie sonst verkaufen? Und so könnte ebensowohl ge-\n sagt werden, dass der Käufer wörtlich (strictly) einen „Produktionsakt“\n vollbringt, indem er z. B. die Strümpfe des Kaufmanns in Geld ver-\n wandelt.\n\n\n\n\nWerden Waaren oder Waaren und Geld von gleichem Tauschwerth,\n also Aequivalente ausgetauscht, so zieht offenbar keiner mehr Werth\n aus der Cirkulation heraus als er in sie hineinwirft. Es findet dann keine\n Bildung von Mehrwerth Statt. In seiner reinen Form aber bedingt der\n Cirkulationsprozess der Waaren Austausch von Aequivalenten. Jedoch\n gehn die Dinge in der Wirklichkeit nicht rein zu. Unterstellen wir daher\nAustausch von Nicht-Aequivalenten.\n\n\n\n\nJedenfalls steht auf dem Waarenmarkt nur Waarenbesitzer dem Waa-\n renbesitzer gegenüber, und die Macht, die diese Personen über einander\n ausüben, ist nur die Macht ihrer Waaren. Die stoffliche Verschiedenheit\n der Waaren ist das stoffliche Motiv des Austauschs und macht die Waaren-\n besitzer wechselseitig von einander abhängig, indem keiner von ihnen den\n Gegenstand seines eignen Bedürfnisses und jeder von ihnen den Gegenstand\n des Bedürfnisses der Andern in seiner Hand hält. Ausser dieser stoff-\n lichen Verschiedenheit ihrer Gebrauchswerthe besteht nur noch ein Unter-\n schied unter den Waaren, der Unterschied zwischen ihrer Naturalform und\n ihrer verwandelten Form, zwischen Waare und Geld. Und so unter-\n scheiden sich die Waarenbesitzer nur als Verkäufer, Besitzer von Waare,\n und als Käufer, Besitzer von Geld.\n\n\n\n\nGesetzt nun, es sei durch irgend ein unerklärliches Privilegium dem Ver-\n käufer gegeben, die Waare über ihrem Werthe zu verkaufen, zu 110,\n wenn sie 100 werth ist, also mit einem nominellen Preisaufschlage von\n 10 %. Der Verkäufer kassirt also einen Mehrwerth von 10 ein. Aber\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"123\", \"facs\": \"#f0142\"} --> nachdem er Verkäufer war, wird er Käufer. Ein dritter Waarenbesitzer\n begegnet ihm jetzt als Verkäufer und geniesst seinerseits das Privilegium,\n die Waare 10 % zu theuer zu verkaufen. Unser Mann hat als Verkäufer\n 10 gewonnen, um als Käufer 10 zu verlieren [Note: „By the augmentation of the nominal value of the produce . . . . sellers\n not enriched … since what they gain as sellers, they precisely expend in the\n quality of buyers.“ („The Essential Principles of the Wealth of\n Nation etc. London 1797“, p. 66.)] . Das ganze kömmt in\n der That darauf hinaus, dass alle Waarenbesitzer ihre Waaren einander\n 10 % über dem Werth verkaufen, was ganz dasselbe ist, als ob sie die\n Waaren zu ihren Werthen verkauften. Ein solcher allgemeiner nomineller\nPreisaufschlag der Waaren bringt dieselbe Wirkung hervor, als ob\n die Waarenwerthe z. B. in Silber statt in Gold geschätzt würden. Die\n Geldnamen, d. h. die Preise der Waaren würden anschwellen, aber ihre\n Werthverhältnisse unverändert bleiben.\n\n\n\n\nUnterstellen wir umgekehrt, es sei das Privilegium des Käufers,\n die Waaren unter ihrem Werth zu kaufen. Hier ist es nicht einmal\n nöthig zu erinnern, dass der Käufer wieder Verkäufer wird. Er war\n Verkäufer, bevor er Käufer ward. Er hat bereits 10 % als Verkäufer\n verloren, bevor er 10 % als Käufer gewinnt [Note: „Si l’on est forcé de donner pour 18 livres une quantité de telle produc-\n tion qui en valait 24, lorsqu’on exployera ce même argent à acheter, on aura\n également pour 18 l. ce que l’on payait 24“. (Le Trosne l. c. p. 897.)] . Alles bleibt wieder beim\n Alten.\n\n\n\n\nDie Bildung von Mehrwerth, und daher die Verwandlung von Geld\n in Kapital, kann also weder dadurch erklärt werden, dass die Verkäufer\n die Waaren über ihrem Werthe verkaufen, noch dadurch, dass die Käufer\n sie unter ihrem Werthe kaufen [Note: „Chaque vendeur ne peut donc parvenir à renchérir habituellement ses\n marchandises, qu’en se soumettant aussi à payer habituellement plus cher les mar-\n chandises des autres vendeurs; et par la même raison, chaque consommateur ne\n peut payer habituellement moins cher ce qu’il achète, qu’en se soumettant aussi à\n une diminution semblable sur le prix des choses qu’il vend.“ (Mercier de la\n Rivière l. c. p. 555.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDas Problem wird in keiner Weise dadurch vereinfacht, dass man\n fremde Beziehungen einschmuggelt, also etwa mit Oberst Torrens sagt:\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"124\", \"facs\": \"#f0143\"} --> „Die effektive Nachfrage besteht in dem Vermögen und der Neigung (!)\n der Konsumenten, sei es durch unmittelbaren oder vermittelten Aus-\n tausch, für Waaren eine gewisse grössere Portion von allen Ingredienzien\n des Kapitals zu geben, als ihre Produktion kostet [Note: R. Torrens: „An Essay on the Production of Wealth.“\n London 1821, p. 349.] .“ In der Cirkula-\n tion stehn sich Produzenten und Konsumenten nur als Verkäufer und Käu-\n fer gegenüber. Behaupten, der Mehrwerth für den Produzenten entspringe\n daraus, dass die Konsumenten die Waare über dem Werth zahlen, heisst\n nur den einfachen Satz maskiren: Der Waarenbesitzer besitzt als Ver-\n käufer das Privilegium zu theuer zu verkaufen. Der Verkäufer hat die\n Waare selbst producirt oder vertritt ihren Produzenten, aber der Käufer\n hat nicht minder die in seinem Gelde dargestellte Waare selbst produzirt\n oder vertritt ihren Produzenten. Es steht also Produzent dem Produzen-\n ten gegenüber. Was sie unterscheidet, ist dass der eine kauft und der\n andre verkauft. Es bringt uns keinen Schritt weiter, dass der Waaren-\n besitzer unter dem Namen Produzent die Waare über ihrem Werthe\n verkauft und unter dem Namen Consument sie zu theuer zahlt [Note: „The idea of profits being paid by the consumers, is, assuredly, very\n absurd. Who are the consumers?“ (G. Ramsay: An Essay on the Dis-\n tribution of Wealth. Edinburgh 1836, p. 184.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDie consequenten Vertreter der Illusion, dass der Mehrwerth aus\n einem nominellen Preiszuschlag entspringt, oder aus dem Privilegium des\nVerkäufers die Waare zu theuer zu verkaufen, unterstellen daher eine\nKlasse, die nur kauft ohne zu verkaufen, also auch nur con-\n sumirt ohne zu produziren. Die Existenz einer solchen Klasse\n ist von unsrem bisher erreichten Standpunkt, dem der einfachen Cirkula-\n tion nach, unerklärlich. Aber greifen wir vor. Das Geld, womit eine\n solche Klasse beständig kauft, muss ihr beständig, ohne Austausch,\n umsonst, auf beliebige Rechts- und Gewaltstitel hin, von den Waaren-\n besitzern selbst zufliessen. Dieser Klasse die Waaren über dem Werth\n verkaufen, heisst nur, umsonst weggegebenes Geld sich zum Theil wieder\n zurückschwindeln [Note: „When a man is in want of demand, does Mr. Malthus recommend him\n to pay some other person to take off his goods?“, fragt ein entrüsteter Ricardianer] . So zahlten die kleinasiatischen Städte jährlichen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"125\", \"facs\": \"#f0144\"} --> Geldtribut an das alte Rom. Mit diesem Geld kaufte Rom Waaren von\n ihnen und kaufte sie zu theuer. Die Kleinasiaten prellten die Römer, in-\n dem sie den Eroberern einen Theil des Tributs wieder abluchsten auf dem\n Wege des Handels. Aber dennoch blieben die Kleinasiaten die Geprell-\n ten. Ihre Waaren wurden ihnen nach wie vor mit ihrem eignen Gelde ge-\n zahlt. Es ist diess keine Methode der Bereicherung oder der Bildung von\n Mehrwerth.\n\n\n\n\nHalten wir uns also innerhalb der Schranken des Waarenaustauschs,\n wo Verkäufer Käufer und Käufer Verkäufer sind. Unsere Verlegenheit\n stammt vielleicht daher, dass wir die Personen nur als personificirte\n Categorien, nicht individuell, gefasst haben.\n\n\n\n\nWaarenbesitzer A mag so pfiffig sein seine Collegen B oder C über’s\n Ohr zu hauen, während sie trotz des besten Willens die Revanche schuldig\n bleiben. A verkauft Wein zum Werth von 40 Pfd. St. an B und erwirbt\n im Austausch Getreide zum Werth von 50 Pfd. St. A hat seine 40 Pfd.\n St. in 50 Pfd. St. verwandelt, mehr Geld aus weniger Geld gemacht und\n seine Waare in Kapital verwandelt. Sehn wir näher zu. Vor dem Aus-\n tausch hatten wir für 40 Pfd. St. Wein in der Hand von A und für 50 Pfd.\n St. Getreide in der Hand von B, Gesammtwerth von 90 Pfd. St.\n Nach dem Austausch haben wir denselben Gesammtwerth von 90 Pfd. St.\n Der circulirende Werth hat sich um kein Atom vergrössert, seine Ver-\n theilung zwischen A und B hat sich verändert. Auf der einen Seite er-\n scheint als Mehrwerth, was auf der anderen Minderwerth ist, auf\n der einen Seite als Plus, was auf der andern als Minus. Derselbe Wech-\n sel hätte sich ereignet, wenn A, ohne die verhüllende Form des Austauschs,\n dem B 10 Pfd. St. direkt gestohlen hätte. Die Summe der circulirenden\n Werthe kann offenbar durch keinen Wechsel in ihrer Vertheilung vermehrt\n werden, so wenig wie ein Jude die Masse der edlen Metalle in einem Lande\n dadurch vermehrt, dass er einen Farthing aus der Zeit der Königin Anna\n [Note: den Malthus, der wie sein Schüler, der Pfaffe Chalmers, die Klasse von\n blossen Käufern oder Consumenten ökonomisch verherrlicht. Sieh: „An In-\n quiry into those principles respecting the Nature of Demand and\n the Necessity of Consumption, lately advocated by Mr. Malthus“\n etc. London 1821, p. 55.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"126\", \"facs\": \"#f0145\"} --> für eine Guinea verkauft. Die Gesammtheit der Kapitalistenklasse eines\n Landes kann sich nicht selbst übervortheilen [Note: Destutt de Tracy, obgleich, vielleicht weil Membre de l’Institut, war\n umgekehrter Ansicht. Die industriellen Kapitalisten, sagt er, machen dadurch\n ihre Profite, dass „sie alles theurer verkaufen als es gekostet hat zu produciren.\n Und an wen verkaufen sie? Erstens an einander.“ (l. c. p. 239.)] .\n\n\n\n\nMan mag sich also drehen und wenden wie man will, das Facit bleibt\n dasselbe. Werden Aequivalente ausgetauscht, so entsteht kein Mehrwerth,\n und werden Nicht-Aequivalente ausgetauscht, so entsteht auch kein Mehr-\n werth [Note: „L’échange qui se fait de deux valeurs égales n’augmente ni ne diminue\n la masse des valeurs subsistantes dans la société. L’échange de deux valeurs in-\n égales … ne change rien non plus à la somme des valeurs sociales, bien qu’il\n ajoute à la fortune de l’un ce qu’il ôte de la fortune de l’autre.“ (J. B. Say\n l. c. t. I, p. 434, 435.) Say, natürlich unbekümmert um die Consequenzen\n dieses Satzes, entlehnt ihn ziemlich wörtlich den Physiokraten. Die Art, wie er\n ihre zu seiner Zeit verschollenen Schriften zur Vermehrung seines eigenen\n „Werthes“ ausgebeutet hat, zeige folgendes Beispiel. Der „berühmteste“ Satz\n des Monsieur Say: „On n’achète des produits qu’avec des pro-\n duits“ (l. c. t. II, p. 438) lautet im physiokratischen Original: „Les pro-\n ductions ne se paient qu’avec des productions.“ (Le Trosne\n l. c. p. 899.)] . Die Circulation oder der Waarenaustausch schafft keinen\n Werth [Note: „Exchange confers no value at all upon products.“ (F. Wayland:\n The Elements of Pol. Econ. Boston 1853, p. 168.)] .\n\n\n\n\nMan versteht daher, warum in unserer Analyse der Grundform\n des Kapitals, der Form, worin es die ökonomische Organisation der\n modernen Gesellschaft bestimmt, seine populärsten und so zu sagen ante-\n diluvianischen Gestalten, Handelskapital und Wucherkapital,\n zunächst gänzlich unberücksichtigt bleiben.\n\n\n\n\nIm eigentlichen Handelskapital erscheint die Form G — W — G',\n kaufen um theuer zu verkaufen, am reinsten. Andrerseits geht seine ganze\n Bewegung innerhalb der Circulationssphäre vor. Da es aber\n unmöglich ist aus der Circulation selbst die Verwandlung von Geld in Kapi-\n tal, die Bildung von Mehrwerth zu erklären, erscheint das Handelskapital\n unmöglich, sobald Aequivalente ausgetauscht werden [Note: „Under the rule of invariable equivalents commerce would be im-\n possible.“ (G. Opdyke: A Treatise on Polit. Economy. New-] , daher nur\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"127\", \"facs\": \"#f0146\"} --> ableitbar aus der doppelseitigen Uebervortheilung der kaufenden\n und verkaufenden Waarenproducenten durch den sich parasitisch zwischen\n sie schiebenden Kaufmann. In diesem Sinn sagt Franklin: „Krieg\n ist Raub, Handel ist Prellerei [Note: Benjamin Franklin: Works, vol. II, edit. Sparks in:\n „Positions to be examined concerning National Wealth.“] :“ Soll die Verwer-\n thung des Handelskapitals nicht aus blosser Prellerei der Waaren-\n producenten erklärt werden, so gehört dazu eine lange Reihe von Mittel-\n gliedern, die hier, wo die Waarencirculation und ihre einfachen Momente\n unsere einzige Voraussetzung bilden, noch gänzlich fehlt.\n\n\n\n\nWas vom Handelskapital, gilt noch mehr vom Wucher-\n kapital. Im Handelskapital sind die Extreme, das Geld, das auf den\n Markt geworfen und das vermehrte Geld, das dem Markt entzogen wird,\n wenigstens vermittelt durch Kauf und Verkauf, durch die Bewegung\n der Circulation. Im Wucherkapital ist die Form G — W — G'\n abgekürzt auf die unvermittelten Extreme G — G', Geld, das sich gegen\n mehr Geld austauscht, eine der Natur des Geldes widersprechende und\n daher vom Standpunkt des Waarenaustauschs unerklärliche\n Form. Daher Aristoteles: „da die Chrematistik eine doppelte ist, die\n eine zum Handel, die andre zur Oekonomik gehörig, die letztere nothwendig\n und lobenswerth, die erstere auf die Circulation gegründet und mit Recht ge-\n tadelt, (denn sie beruht nicht auf der Natur, sondern auf wechselseitiger Prel-\n lerei), so ist der Wucher mit vollstem Rechte verhasst, weil das Geld selbst\n hier die Quelle des Erwerbs und nicht dazu gebraucht wird, wozu es erfunden\n ward. Denn für den Waarenaustausch entstand es, der Zins aber\n macht aus Geld mehr Geld. Daher auch sein Name (τόϰος Zins und\nGeborenes). Denn die Geborenen sind den Erzeugern ähnlich. Der\n Zins aber ist Geld von Geld, so dass von allen Erwerbszweigen dieser der\n naturwidrigste [Note: Arist. l. c. c. 10.] .“\n\n\n\n\nWie das Handelskapital werden wir das zinstragende\n Kapital im Verlauf unserer Untersuchung als abgeleitete Formen\n [Note: York 1851, p. 69.) „Dem Unterschiede zwischen Realwerth und Tauschwerth\n liegt eine Thatsache zum Grunde — nämlich dass der Werth einer Sache verschie-\n den ist von dem im Handel für sie gegebenen sogenannten Aequivalent, d. h.\n dass diess Aequivalent kein Aequivalent ist.“ (F. Engels l. c. p. 96.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"128\", \"facs\": \"#f0147\"} --> vorfinden und zugleich sehn, warum sie historisch vor dem Kapital\n in seiner modernen Grundform erscheinen.\n\n\n\n\nEs hat sich gezeigt, dass der Mehrwerth nicht aus der Circulation\n entspringen kann, bei seiner Bildung also etwas hinter ihrem Rücken\n vorgehn muss, das in ihr selbst unsichtbar ist [Note: „Profit, in the usual condition of the market, is not made by exchang-\n ing. Had it not existed before, neither could it after that transaction.“\n (Ramsay l. c. p. 184.)] . Kann aber der\n Mehrwerth anders woher entspringen als aus der Circulation? Die\n Circulation ist die Summe aller Wechselbeziehungen der Waarenbesitzer.\n Ausserhalb derselben steht der Waarenbesitzer nur noch in Beziehung zu\n seiner eignen Waare. Was ihren Werth [Note: Ich bemerke hier, dass wenn ich das Wort Werth in dieser Schrift ohne\n nähere Bezeichnung brauche, stets Tauschwerth zu verstehn ist.]  angeht, beschränkt sich das\n Verhältniss darauf, dass sie ein nach bestimmten gesellschaftlichen Gesetzen\n gemessenes Quantum seiner eignen Arbeit enthält. Diess Quantum Arbeit\n drückt sich aus in der Werthgrösse seiner Waare und da sich Werth-\n grösse in Rechengeld darstellt, in einem Preise von z. B. 10 Pfd. St.\n Aber seine Arbeit stellt sich nicht dar im Werthe der Waare und einem\n Ueberschuss über ihren eignen Werth, nicht in einem Preise von 10, der\n zugleich ein Preis von 11 ist, nicht in einem Werth, der grösser als\n er selbst ist. Der Waarenbesitzer kann durch seine Arbeit Werthe\n bilden, aber keine sich verwerthenden Werthe. Er kann den\n Werth einer Waare erhöhen, indem er vorhandnem Werth neuen Werth\n durch neue Arbeit zusetzt, z. B. aus Leder Stiefel macht. Derselbe\n Stoff hat jetzt mehr Werth, weil er ein grösseres Arbeitsquantum enthält.\n Der Stiefel hat daher mehr Werth als das Leder, aber der Werth des Leders\n ist geblieben, was er war. Er hat sich nicht verwerthet, nicht während\n der Stiefelfabrikation seinen ursprünglichen Werth um einen Mehrwerth be-\n reichert. Es ist also unmöglich, dass der Waarenproducent ausserhalb\n der Circulationssphäre, ohne mit andern Waarenbesitzern in Berührung\n zu treten, Werth verwerthe und daher Geld oder Waare in Kapital\n verwandle.\n\n\n\n\nKapital kann also nicht aus der Circulation entspringen und es kann\n eben so wenig aus der Circulation nicht entspringen. Es muss zugleich\n in ihr und nicht in ihr entspringen.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"129\", \"facs\": \"#f0148\"} -->\n\nEin doppeltes Resultat hat sich also ergeben.\n\n\n\n\nDie Verwandlung des Geldes in Kapital ist auf Grundlage dem Waaren-\n austausch immanenter Gesetze zu entwickeln, so dass der Austausch\n von Aequivalenten als Ausgangspunkt gilt [Note: Nach der gegebnen Auseinandersetzung versteht der Leser, dass diess nur\n heisst: Die Kapitalbildung muss möglich sein, auch wenn der Waarenpreis\ngleich dem Waarenwerth. Sie kann nicht aus der Abweichung der Waaren-\n preise von den Waarenwerthen erklärt werden. Weichen die Preise von den\n Werthen wirklich ab, so muss man sie erst auf die letztern reduciren, d. h. von\n diesem Umstand als einem zufälligen absehn, um das Phänomen der Kapitalbildung\n auf Grundlage des Waarenaustausches rein vor sich zu haben und in seiner Beob-\n achtung nicht durch störende und dem eigentlichen Verlauf fremde Nebenum-\n stände verwirrt zu werden. Man weiss übrigens, dass diese Reduktion keineswegs\n eine blos wissenschaftliche Procedur ist. Die beständigen Oscillationen der Markt-\n preise, ihr Steigen und Sinken, compensiren sich, heben sich wechselseitig auf und\n reduciren sich selbst zum Durchschnittspreis als ihrer inneren Regel. Diese\n bildet den Leitstern z. B. des Kaufmanns oder des Industriellen in jeder Unter-\n nehmung, die längeren Zeitraum umfasst. Er weiss also, dass eine längere Pe-\n riode im Ganzen betrachtet, die Waaren wirklich weder unter, noch über, son-\n dern zu ihrem Durchschnittspreis verkauft werden. Wäre interesseloses Denken\n also überhaupt sein Interesse, so müsste er sich das Problem der Kapitalbildung so\n stellen: Wie kann Kapital entstehn bei der Reglung der Preise durch den Durch-\n schnittspreis, d. h. in letzter Instanz durch den Werth der Waare? Ich sage\n „in letzter Instanz,“ weil die Durchschnittspreise nicht direkt mit den Werth-\n grössen der Waaren, wie A. Smith, Ricardo u. s. w. glauben, zusammenfallen.] . Unser nur noch als\n Kapitalistenraupe vorhandner Geldbesitzer muss die Waaren zu ihrem\n Werth kaufen, zu ihrem Werth verkaufen, und dennoch am Ende des Pro-\n zesses mehr Werth herausziehn als er hineinwarf. Seine Schmetterlings-\n entfaltung muss in der Cirkulationssphäre und muss nicht in der Cirkula-\n tionssphäre vorgehn. Diess sind die Bedingungen des Problems. Hie\n Rhodus, hic salta!\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## 3) Kauf und Verkauf der Arbeitskraft.\n\n\n\n\nDie Werthveränderung des Geldes, das sich in Kapital ver-\n wandeln soll, kann nicht an diesem Geld selbst vorgehn, denn als Kauf-\n mittel und als Zahlungsmittel realisirt es nur den Preis der Waare, die\n es kauft oder zahlt, während es, in seiner eignen Form verharrend, zum\n Petrefakt von gleichbleibender Werthgrösse erstarrt [Note: „In the form of money . . . . capital is productive of no profit.“\n (Ricardo: Princ. of Pol. Econ. p. 267.)] . Eben so wenig\n [Running matter: I. 9] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"130\", \"facs\": \"#f0149\"} --> kann die Veränderung aus dem zweiten Akt, dem Wiederverkauf der\n Waare, entspringen, denn dieser Akt verwandelt die Waare blos aus der\n Naturalform zurück in die Geldform. Die Veränderung muss sich also\n zutragen mit der Waare, die im ersten Akt G — W gekauft wird,\n aber nicht mit ihrem Tauschwerth, denn es werden Aequivalente aus-\n getauscht, die Waare wird zu ihrem Werthe bezahlt. Die Veränderung\n kann also erst entspringen aus ihrem Gebrauchswerth als solchem,\n d. h. aus ihrem Verbrauch. Um aus dem Verbrauch einer Waare\n Tauschwerth herauszuziehn, müsste unser Geldbesitzer so glücklich sein\ninnerhalb der Cirkulationssphäre, auf dem Markt, eine Waare\n zu entdecken, deren Gebrauchswerth selbst die eigenthümliche Be-\n schaffenheit besässe, Quelle von Tauschwerth zu sein, deren wirk-\n licher Verbrauch also selbst Vergegenständlichung von Arbeit\n wäre, daher Werthschöpfung. Und der Geldbesitzer findet auf dem\n Markt eine solche spezifische Waare vor — das Arbeitsvermögen\n oder die Arbeitskraft.\n\n\n\n\nUnter Arbeitskraft oder Arbeitsvermögen verstehn wir den\n Inbegriff der physischen und geistigen Fähigkeiten, die in der Leiblichkeit,\n der lebendigen Persönlichkeit eines Menschen existiren und die er in Be-\n wegung setzt, so oft er Gebrauchswerthe irgend einer Art producirt.\n\n\n\n\nDamit jedoch der Geldbesitzer die Arbeitskraft als Waare auf dem\n Markt vorfinde, müssen verschiedne Bedingungen erfüllt sein. Der Waaren-\n austausch schliesst an und für sich keine andern Abhängigkeitsver-\n hältnisse ein als die aus seiner eignen Natur entspringenden. Unter\n dieser Voraussetzung kann die Arbeitskraft als Waare nur auf dem\n Markt erscheinen, sofern und weil sie von ihrem eignen Besitzer,\n der Person, deren Arbeitskraft sie ist, als Waare feilgeboten oder ver-\n kauft wird. Damit ihr Besitzer sie als Waare verkaufe, muss er über sie\n verfügen können, also freier Eigenthümer seines Arbeitsvermögens,\n seiner Person sein [Note: In Realencyklopädien des klassischen Alterthums kann man den Unsinn\n lesen, dass in der antiken Welt das Kapital völlig entwickelt war „ausser dass\n der freie Arbeiter und das Creditwesen fehlten.“ Auch Herr Mommsen in seiner\n „Römischen Geschichte“ begeht ein quid pro quo über das andere.] . Er und der Geldbesitzer begegnen sich auf dem\n Markt und treten in Verhältniss zu einander als ebenbürtige Waaren-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"131\", \"facs\": \"#f0150\"} --> besitzer, nur dadurch unterschieden, dass der eine Käufer, der andere\n Verkäufer, beide also juristisch gleiche Personen sind. Die\n Fortdauer dieses Verhältnisses erheischt, dass der Eigenthümer der Arbeits-\n kraft sie stets nur für bestimmte Zeit verkaufe, denn verkauft er sie\n in Bausch und Bogen, ein für allemal, so verkauft er sich selbst, verwan-\n delt sich aus einem Freien in einen Sklaven, aus einem Waarenbesitzer\n in eine Waare. Er als Person muss sich beständig zu seiner Arbeits-\n kraft als seinem Eigenthum und daher seiner eignen Waare verhalten und\n das kann er nur, so weit er sie dem Käufer stets nur vorübergehend, für\n einen bestimmten Zeittermin, zur Verfügung stellt, zum Verbrauch über-\n lässt, also durch ihre Veräusserung nicht auf sein Eigenthum an ihr\n verzichtet [Note: Verschiedne Gesetzgebungen setzen daher ein Maximum für den Arbeits-\n kontrakt fest. Alle Gesetzbücher bei Völkern freier Arbeit regeln Kündigungs-\n bedingungen des Kontrakts. Bei verschiednen Völkern, namentlich in Mexiko\n (vor dem amerikanischen Bürgerkrieg auch in den von Mexiko losgerissenen Terri-\n torien, und der Sache nach bis zu Kusa’s Umwälzung in den Donauprovinzen) ist\n die Sklaverei unter der Form von Peonage versteckt. Durch Vorschüsse, die\n in Arbeit abzutragen, und sich von Generation zu Generation fortwälzen, wird nicht\n nur der einzelne Arbeiter, sondern seine Familie, thatsächlich das Eigenthum\n andrer Personen und ihrer Familien. Juarez hatte die Peonage abgeschafft.\n Der sogenannte Kaiser Maximilian führte sie wieder ein durch ein\n Dekret, das im Repräsentantenhaus zu Washington treffend als Dekret zur\n Wiedereinführung der Sklaverei in Mexiko denuncirt ward. „Von\n meinen besonderen körperlichen und geistigen Geschicklichkeiten und Möglich-\n keiten der Thätigkeit kann ich . . . . einen in der Zeit beschränkten Ge-\n brauch an einen Andern veräussern, weil sie nach dieser Beschränkung ein\n äusserliches Verhältniss zu meiner Totalität und Allgemeinheit erhalten.\n Durch die Veräusserung meiner ganzen durch die Arbeit konkreten\n Zeit und der Totalität meiner Produktion würde ich das Substantielle derselben,\n meine allgemeine Thätigkeit und Wirklichkeit, meine Persönlichkeit\n zum Eigenthum eines Andern machen.“ Hegel: „Philosophie\n des Rechts. Berlin 1840“, p. 104, § 67.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie zweite wesentliche Bedingung, damit der Geldbesitzer die Ar-\n beitskraft auf dem Markt als Waare vorfinde, ist die, dass ihr Be-\n sitzer, statt Waaren verkaufen zu können, worin sich seine Arbeit ver-\n gegenständlicht hat, vielmehr seine Arbeitskraft selbst, die nur in\n seiner lebendigen Leiblichkeit existirt, als Waare feilbieten muss.\n\n\n [Running matter: 9*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"132\", \"facs\": \"#f0151\"} -->\n\nDamit Jemand von seiner Arbeitskraft unterschiedne Waaren\n verkaufe, muss er natürlich Produktionsmittel besitzen, z. B. Roh-\n stoffe, Arbeitsinstrumente u. s. w. Er kann keine Stiefel machen ohne\n Leder. Er bedarf ausserdem Lebensmittel. Niemand kann von\n Produkten der Zukunft zehren, also auch nicht von Gebrauchswerthen,\n mit deren Produktion er noch nicht fertig, und wie am ersten Tag seiner\n Erscheinung auf der Weltbühne muss der Mensch noch jeden Tag konsu-\n miren, bevor und während er produzirt. Werden die Produkte als Waa-\n ren produzirt, so müssen sie verkauft werden, nachdem sie produ-\n zirt sind und können die Bedürfnisse des Produzenten erst nach dem Ver-\n kauf befriedigen. Zur Produktionszeit kömmt die für den Verkauf nöthige\n Zeit hinzu.\n\n\n\n\nZur Verwandlung von Geld in Kapital muss der Geldbesitzer also\nden freien Arbeiter auf dem Waarenmarkt vorfinden, frei in\n dem Doppelsinn, dass er als freie Person über seine Arbeitskraft als seine\n Waare verfügt, dass er andrerseits andre Waaren nicht zu verkaufen hat,\n los und ledig, frei ist von allen zur Verwirklichung seiner Arbeitskraft\n nöthigen Sachen.\n\n\n\n\nDie Frage, warum dieser freie Arbeiter ihm in der Cirkulations-\n sphäre gegenübertritt, interessirt den Geldbesitzer nicht, der den Arbeits-\n markt als eine besondre Abtheilung des Waarenmarkts vorfindet. Und\n einstweilen interessirt sie uns eben so wenig. Wir halten uns theoretisch\n an die Thatsache, wie der Geldbesitzer praktisch. Eins jedoch ist klar.\n Die Natur produzirt nicht auf der einen Seite Geld- oder Waarenbesitzer\n und auf der andern blosse Besitzer der eignen Arbeitskräfte. Diess Ver-\n hältniss ist kein naturgeschichtliches und eben so wenig ein ge-\n sellschaftliches, das allen Geschichtsperioden gemein wäre. Es\n ist offenbar selbst das Resultat einer vorhergegangnen historischen Ent-\n wicklung, das Produkt vieler ökonomischer Umwälzungen, des Untergangs\n einer ganzen Reihe älterer Formationen der gesellschaftlichen Pro-\n duktion.\n\n\n\n\nAuch die ökonomischen Kategorieen, die wir früher betrachtet, tra-\n gen ihre geschichtliche Spur. Im Dasein des Produkts als Waare sind\n bestimmte historische Bedingungen eingehüllt. Um Waare zu werden,\n darf das Produkt nicht als unmittelbares Subsistenzmittel\n für den Produzenten selbst produzirt werden. Hätten wir weiter geforscht:\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"133\", \"facs\": \"#f0152\"} --> Unter welchen Umständen nehmen alle oder nimmt auch nur die Mehr-\n zahl der Produkte die Form der Waare an, so hätte sich gefunden, dass\n diess nur auf Grundlage einer ganz spezifischen, der kapitalistischen\n Produktionsweise, geschieht. Eine solche Untersuchung lag jedoch\n der Analyse der Waare fern. Waarenproduktion und Waarencirkulation\n können stattfinden, obgleich die weit überwiegende Produktenmasse, un-\n mittelbar auf den Selbstbedarf gerichtet, sich nicht in Waare verwan-\n delt, der gesellschaftliche Produktionsprozess also noch lange nicht in sei-\n ner ganzen Breite und Tiefe vom Tauschwerth beherrscht ist. Die Dar-\n stellung des Produkts als Waare bedingt eine so weit entwickelte\nTheilung der Arbeit innerhalb der Gesellschaft, dass die\n Scheidung zwischen Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth, die im unmittel-\n baren Tauschhandel erst beginnt, bereits vollzogen ist. Eine\n solche Entwicklungsstufe ist aber den geschichtlich verschiedensten ökono-\n mischen Gesellschaftsformationen gemein.\n\n\n\n\nOder betrachten wir das Geld, so setzt es eine gewisse Höhe der Waa-\n rencirkulation voraus. Die besondern Geldformen, blosses Waaren-\n äquivalent, oder Cirkulationsmittel, oder Zahlungsmittel, Schatz und Weltgeld,\n deuten, je nach dem verschiednen Umfang und dem relativen Vorwiegen einer\n oder der andern Funktion, auf sehr verschiedne Stufen des gesellschaftlichen\n Produktionsprozesses. Dennoch genügt erfahrungsmässig eine relativ\n schwach entwickelte Waarencirkulation zur Bildung aller dieser Formen.\n Anders mit dem Kapital. Seine historischen Existenzbedingungen\n sind durchaus nicht da mit der Waaren- und Geldcirkulation. Es entsteht\n nur, wo der Besitzer von Produktions- und Lebensmitteln den freien\n Arbeiter als Verkäufer seiner Arbeitskraft auf dem Markt vorfindet,\n und diese eine historische Bedingung umschliesst eine Welt-\n geschichte. Das Kapital kündigt sich daher von vorn herein als eine\nEpoche des gesellschaftlichen Produktionsprozesses an.\n\n\n\n\nDiese eigenthümliche Waare, die Arbeitskraft, ist nun näher\n zu betrachten. Gleich allen andern Waaren besitzt sie einen Tausch-\n werth [Note: „The Value or Worth of a man, is as of all other things, his\n price: that is to say, so much as would be given for the use of his power.“\nTh. Hobbes: „Leviathan“ in Worksedit. Molesworth. London\n 1839—44, v. III, p. 76.] . Wie wird er bestimmt?\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"134\", \"facs\": \"#f0153\"} -->\n\nDer Werth der Arbeitskraft, gleich dem jeder andern Waare, ist be-\n stimmt durch die zur Produktion, also auch Reproduktion, dieses spezifi-\n schen Artikels nothwendige Arbeitszeit. Soweit sie Tausch-\n werth, repräsentirt die Arbeitskraft selbst nur ein bestimmtes Quantum in\n ihr vergegenständlichter gesellschaftlicher Durchschnittsarbeit.\n Die Arbeitskraft existirt nur als Anlage des lebendigen Individuums. Ihre\n Produktion setzt also seine Existenz voraus. Diese gegeben, besteht ihre\n Produktion in seiner Reproduktion oder Erhaltung. Zu seiner Erhaltung\n bedarf das lebendige Individuum einer gewissen Summe von Lebensmitteln.\n Die zur Produktion der Arbeitskraft nothwendige Arbeitszeit löst sich also\n auf in die zur Produktion dieser Lebensmittel nothwendige Arbeitszeit,\n oder der Werth der Arbeitskraft ist der Werth der zur Erhal-\n tung ihres Besitzers nothwendigen Lebensmittel. Die Arbeits-\n kraft verwirklicht sich jedoch nur durch ihre Aeusserung, bethätigt sich\n nur in der Arbeit. Durch ihre Bethätigung, die Arbeit, wird aber ein\n bestimmtes Quantum von menschlichem Muskel, Nerv, Hirn u. s. w. ver-\n ausgabt, das wieder ersetzt werden muss. Diese vermehrte Ausgabe be-\n dingt eine vermehrte Einnahme [Note: Der altrömische villicus, als Wirthschafter an der Spitze der Acker-\n bausklaven, empfing daher, „weil er leichtere Arbeit hat als die Knechte, knapperes\n Mass als diese.“ (Th. Mommsen: Röm. Geschichte 1856, p. 810.)] . Wenn der Eigenthümer der Arbeits-\n kraft heute gearbeitet hat, muss er denselben Prozess morgen unter den-\n selben Bedingungen von Kraft und Gesundheit wiederholen können. Die\n Summe der Lebensmittel muss also hinreichen, das arbeitende Individuum\n als arbeitendes Individuum in seinem normalen Lebenszustand zu erhalten.\n Die natürlichen Bedürfnisse selbst, wie Nahrung, Kleidung, Heizung,\n Wohnung u. s. w. sind verschieden je nach den klimatischen und andern\n natürlichen Eigenthümlichkeiten eines Landes. Andrerseits ist der Um-\n fang s. g. nothwendiger Lebensmittel, wie die Art ihrer Be-\n friedigung, selbst ein historisches Produkt und hängt daher gros-\n sentheils von der Kulturstufe eines Landes, unter anderm auch wesentlich\n davon ab, unter welchen Bedingungen, und daher mit welchen Gewohn-\n heiten und Lebensansprüchen die Klasse der freien Arbeiter sich gebildet\n hat [Note: In seiner Schrift: „Over population and its Remedy. London\n 1846“ giebt W. Th. Thornton hierzu interessante Belege.] . Im Gegensatz zu den andern Waaren enthält also die Werth-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"135\", \"facs\": \"#f0154\"} --> bestimmung der Arbeitskraft ein historisches und moralisches Element.\n Für ein bestimmtes Land, zu einer bestimmten Periode jedoch, ist der\n Durchschnitts-Umkreis der nothwendigen Lebensmittel gegeben.\n\n\n\n\nDer Eigenthümer der Arbeitskraft ist sterblich. Soll also seine Er-\n scheinung auf dem Markt eine kontinuirliche sein, wie die kontinuirliche\n Verwandlung von Geld in Kapital voraussetzt, so muss der Verkäufer der\n Arbeitskraft sich verewigen, „wie jedes lebendige Individuum sich ver-\n ewigt, durch Fortpflanzung [Note: Petty.] .“ Die durch Abnutzung und Tod\n dem Markt entzogenen Arbeitskräfte müssen zum allermindesten durch eine\n gleiche Zahl neuer Arbeitskräfte beständig ersetzt werden. Die Summe\n der zur Produktion der Arbeitskraft nothwendigen Lebensmittel schliesst\n also die Lebensmittel der Ersatzmänner ein, d. h. der Kinder der Arbei-\n ter, so dass sich diese Race eigenthümlicher Waarenbesitzer auf dem Waa-\n renmarkt verewigt [Note: „Its (labour’s) natural price … consists in such a quantity of necessaries,\n and comforts of life, as, from the nature of the climate, and the habits of the\n country, are necessary to support the labourer, and to enable him to rear such a\n family as may preserve, in the market, an undiminished supply of\n labours.“ R. Torrens: „An Essay on the External Corn Trade.\n London 1815“, p. 62. Das Wort Arbeit steht hier fälschlich für Arbeitskraft.] .\n\n\n\n\nUm die allgemein menschliche Natur so zu modificiren, dass sie Ge-\n schick und Fertigkeit in einem bestimmten Arbeitszweig erlangt, ent-\n wickelte und spezifische Arbeitskraft wird, bedarf es einer bestimmten\n Bildung oder Erziehung, welche ihrerseits eine grössere oder geringere\n Summe von Waarenäquivalenten kostet. Je nach dem mehr oder minder\n vermittelten Charakter der Arbeitskraft, sind ihre Bildungskosten ver-\n schieden. Diese Erlernungskosten, verschwindend klein für die gewöhn-\n liche Arbeitskraft, gehn also ein in den Umkreis der zu ihrer Produktion\n nothwendigen Waaren.\n\n\n\n\nDer Werth der Arbeitskraft löst sich auf in den Werth\n einer bestimmten Summe von Lebensmitteln. Er wech-\n selt daher auch mit dem Werth dieser Lebensmittel, d. h. der Grösse der\n zu ihrer Produktion erheischten Arbeitszeit.\n\n\n\n\nEin Theil der Lebensmittel, z. B. Nahrungsmittel, Heizungsmittel\n u. s. w., werden täglich neu verzehrt, und müssen täglich neu ersetzt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"136\", \"facs\": \"#f0155\"} --> werden. Andere Lebensmittel, wie Kleider, Möbel u. s. w., verbrauchen\n sich in längeren Zeiträumen, und sind daher nur in längeren Zeiträumen\n zu ersetzen. Waaren einer Art müssen täglich, andere wöchentlich, vier-\n teljährlich u. s. f. gekauft oder gezahlt werden. Wie sich die Summe\n dieser Ausgaben aber immer während eines Jahres z. B. vertheilen möge,\n sie muss gedeckt sein durch die Durchschnittseinnahme, Tag ein, Tag aus.\n Wäre die Masse der täglich zur Produktion der Arbeitskraft erheischten\n Waaren = A, die der wöchentlich erheischten = B, die der viertel-\n jährlich erheischten = C u. s. w., so wäre der tägliche Durchschnitt\n dieser Waaren \\frac{= 365 A + 52 B + 4 C +}{365} u. s. w. Gesetzt in dieser\n für den Durchschnitts-Tag nöthigen Waarenmasse steckten 6 Stunden\n gesellschaftlicher Arbeit, so vergegenständlicht sich in\n der Arbeitskraft täglich ein halber Tag gesellschaftlicher\n Durchschnittsarbeit, oder ein halber Arbeitstag ist zur täglichen\n Produktion der Arbeitskraft erheischt. Diess zu ihrer täglichen Produktion\n erheischte Arbeitsquantum bildet den Tageswerth der Arbeits-\n kraft, oder den Werth der täglich reproduzirten Arbeitskraft. Wenn\n sich ein halber Tag gesellschaftlicher Durchschnittsarbeit ebenfalls in einer\n Goldmasse von 3 sh. oder einem Thaler darstellt, so ist Ein Thaler\n der dem Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft entsprechende Preis. Bietet der\n Besitzer der Arbeitskraft sie feil für Einen Thaler täglich, so ist ihr Ver-\n kaufspreis gleich ihrem Werth, und, nach unsrer Voraussetzung,\nzahlt der auf Verwandlung seiner Thaler in Kapital erpichte Geldbesitzer\ndiesen Werth.\n\n\n\n\nDie letzte Grenze oder Minimalgrenze des Werths der Arbeits-\n kraft wird gebildet durch den Werth einer Waarenmasse, ohne deren täg-\n liche Zufuhr der Träger der Arbeitskraft, der Mensch, seinen Lebenspro-\n zess nicht erneuern kann, also durch den Werth der physisch un-\n entbehrlichen Lebensmittel. Sinkt der Preis der Arbeitskraft\nauf diess Minimum, so sinkt er unter ihren Werth, denn sie\n kann so nur in verkümmerter Form erhalten werden und sich ent-\n wickeln. Der Tauschwerth jeder Waare ist aber bestimmt durch die\n Arbeitszeit, erfordert um sie in normaler Güte zu liefern.\n\n\n\n\nEs ist eine ausserordentlich wohlfeile Sentimentalität, diese aus der Na-\n tur der Sache fliessende Werthbestimmung der Arbeitskraft grob\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"137\", \"facs\": \"#f0156\"} --> zu finden und etwa mit Rossi zu jammern: „Das Arbeitsvermögen (puis-\n sance de travail) begreifen, während man von den Subsistenzmitteln der\n Arbeit während des Produktionsprozesses abstrahirt, heisst ein Hirnge-\n spinnst (être de raison) begreifen. Wer Arbeit sagt, wer Arbeitsvermögen\n sagt, sagt zugleich Arbeiter und Subsistenzmittel, Arbeiter und Arbeits-\n lohn [Note: Rossi: „Cours d’Écon. Polit. Bruxelles 1842“, p. 370.] .“ Wer Arbeitsvermögen sagt, sagt nicht Arbeit, so wenig als\n wer Verdauungsvermögen sagt, Verdauen sagt. Zum letztern Prozess ist\n bekanntlich mehr als ein guter Magen erfordert. Wer Arbeitsvermögen\n sagt, abstrahirt nicht von den zu seiner Subsistenz nothwendigen Lebens-\n mitteln. Ihr Werth ist vielmehr ausgedrückt in seinem Werth. Wird es\n nicht verkauft, so nützt es dem Arbeiter nichts, so empfindet er es viel-\n mehr als eine grausame Naturnothwendigkeit, dass sein Arbeitsvermögen\n ein bestimmtes Quantum Subsistenzmittel zu seiner Produktion erheischt\n hat und stets wieder von neuem zu seiner Reproduktion erheischt. Er\n entdeckt dann mit Sismondi: „das Arbeitsvermögen . . . . ist\nNichts, wenn es nicht verkauft wird [Note: Sismondi: „Nouv. Princ. etc.“, t. I, p. 112.] .“\n\n\n\n\nDie eigenthümliche Natur dieser spezifischen Waare, der Arbeits-\n kraft, bringt es mit sich, dass mit der Abschliessung des Kontrakts zwischen\n Käufer und Verkäufer ihr Gebrauchswerth noch nicht wirklich in die\n Hand des Käufers übergegangen ist. Ihr Tauschwerth, gleich dem\n jeder andern Waare, war bestimmt, bevor sie in die Cirkulation trat, denn\n ein bestimmtes Quantum gesellschaftlicher Arbeit ward zur Produktion der\n Arbeitskraft verausgabt, aber ihr Gebrauchswerth besteht erst in der\n nachträglichen Kraftäusserung. Die Veräusserung der Kraft und ihre\n wirkliche Aeusserung, d. h. ihr Dasein als Gebrauchswerth, fallen daher\n der Zeit nach aus einander. Bei solchen Waaren aber, wo die formelle\n Veräusserung des Gebrauchswerths durch den Verkauf und seine wirkliche\n Ueberlassung an den Käufer der Zeit nach auseinander fallen, funktionirt\n das Geld des Käufers meist als Zahlungsmittel. In allen Ländern\n kapitalistischer Produktionsweise wird die Arbeitskraft erst gezahlt, nach-\n dem sie bereits während des im Kaufkontrakt festgesetzten Termins funk-\n tionirt hat, z. B. am Ende jeder Woche [Note: „All labour is paid, after it has ceased.“ („An Inquiry into those] . Ueberall schiesst daher der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"138\", \"facs\": \"#f0157\"} --> Arbeiter dem Kapitalisten den Gebrauchswerth der Arbeitskraft vor; er\n lässt sie vom Käufer konsumiren, bevor er ihren Preis bezahlt erhält,\n überall kreditirt daher der Arbeiter dem Kapitalisten. Dass diess\n Kreditiren kein leerer Wahn ist, zeigt nicht nur der gelegentliche Verlust des\n kreditirten Salairs beim Bankerott des Kapitalisten [Note: „L’ouvrier prête son industrie“, aber setzt Storch schlau hinzu: er\n „riskirt nichts“ ausser „de perdre son salaire … l’ouvrier ne transmet\nrien de matériel.“ (Storch: Cours d’Écon. Polit. Petersbourg\n 1815, t. II, p. 37.)] , sondern auch eine\n Reihe mehr nachhaltiger Wirkungen [Note: Ein Beispiel. In London existiren zweierlei Sorten von Bäckern, die\n „full priced“, die das Brod zu seinem vollen Werthe verkaufen, und die under-\n sellers, die es unter diesem Werth verkaufen. Letztere Klasse bildet über ¾\n der Gesammtzahl der Bäcker. (p. XXXII im „Report“ des Regierungskom-\n missairs H. S. Tremenheere über die „Grievances complained of by the\n journeymen bakers etc. London 1862.“) Diese undersellers verkaufen,\n fast ausnahmslos, Brod, das verfälscht ist durch Beimischung von Alaun, Seife,\n Perlasche, Kalk, Derbyshire Steinmehl und ähnlicher angenehmer, nahrhafter und\n gesunder Ingredienzien. (Sieh das oben citirte Blaubuch, ebenso den Bericht des\n „Committee of 1855 on the Adulteration of Bread“ und Dr. Hassall’s:\n „Adulterations Detected.“ 2nd edit. London 1862.) Sir John Gor-\n don erklärte vor dem Committee von 1855, dass „in Folge dieser Fälschungen\n der Arme, der von zwei Pfund Brod täglich lebt, jetzt nicht den vierten Theil des\n Nahrungsstoffes wirklich erhält, abgesehn von den schädlichen Wirkungen auf\n seine Gesundheit.“ Als Grund, „warum ein sehr grosser Theil der Arbeiterklasse“,\n obgleich wohlunterrichtet über die Fälschungen, dennoch Alaun, Steinmehl\n u. s. w. mit in den Kauf nimmt, führt Tremenheere (l. c. p. XLVIII) an, dass\n es für sie „ein Ding der Nothwendigkeit ist, von ihrem Bäcker oder dem chandler’s\n shop das Brod zu nehmen, wie man es ihnen zu geben beliebt.“ Da sie erst Ende\n der Arbeitswoche bezahlt werden, können sie auch „das während der Woche von\n ihren Familien verzehrte Brod erst Ende der Woche zahlen“ und fügt Tremen-\n heere mit Anführung der Zeugenaussagen hinzu: „es ist notorisch, dass\n mit solchen Mixturen bereitetes Brod express für diese Art Kunden ge-\n macht wird.“ („It is notorious that bread composed of those mixtures, is] . Indess ändert es an der Natur des\n [Note: Principles respecting the Nature of demand etc.“, p. 104. „Le\n crédit commercial a dû commencer au moment où l’ouvrier, premier artisan de la\n production, a pu, au moyen de ses économies, attendre le salaire de son travail\n jusqu’à la fin de la semaine, de la quinzaine, du mois, du trimestre etc.“ (Ch.\n Ganilh: Des Systèmes de l’Écon. Polit. 2èmeedit. Paris 1821,\n t. I, p. 150.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"139\", \"facs\": \"#f0158\"} --> Waarenaustauschs selbst nichts, ob das Geld als Kaufmittel oder als Zah-\n lungsmittel funktionirt. Der Preis der Arbeitskraft ist kontraktlich festgesetzt,\n obgleich er erst hinterher realisirt wird, wie der Miethpreis eines Hauses.\n Die Arbeitskraft ist verkauft, obgleich sie erst hinterher bezahlt wird.\n Für die reine Auffassung des Verhältnisses wird es daher nützlich sein,\n einstweilen stets vorauszusetzen, dass der Besitzer der Arbeitskraft\n mit ihrem Verkauf jedesmal auch sogleich den kontraktlich stipulirten Preis\n erhält.\n\n\n\n\nWir kennen nun die Art und Weise der Bestimmung des Tausch-\n werths, der dem Besitzer dieser eigenthümlichen Waare, der Arbeits-\n kraft, vom Geldbesitzer gezahlt wird. Der Gebrauchswerth, den\n letzterer seinerseits im Austausch erhält, zeigt sich erst im wirklichen\nVerbrauch, im Konsumtionsprozess der Arbeitskraft.\n Alle zu diesem Prozess nöthigen Dinge, wie Rohmaterial u. s. w., kauft der\n Geldbesitzer auf dem Waarenmarkt und zahlt sie zum vollen Preis. Der\nKonsumtionsprozess der Arbeitskraft ist zugleich der Pro-\n duktionsprozess von Waare und von Mehrwerth. Die Kon-\n [Note: made expressly for sale in this manner.“) „In vielen englischen Agrikultur-\n distrikten [aber noch mehr in schottischen] wird der Arbeitslohn vierzehntägig und\n selbst monatlich gezahlt. Mit diesen langen Zahlungsfristen muss der Agrikultur-\n arbeiter seine Waaren auf Kredit kaufen … Er hat höhere Preise zu zahlen und\n ist thatsächlich an die Boutique gebunden, die ihm pumpt. So kostet ihm z. B.\n zu Horningsham in Wilts, wo die Löhnung monatlich, dasselbe Mehl 2 sh. 4 d. per\n stone, das er sonstwo mit 1 sh. 10 d. zahlt.“ („Sixth Report“ on „Public\n Health“ by „The Medical Officer of the Privy Council etc. 1864“, p.\n 264.) „Die block printers von Paisley und Kilmarnock (Westschottland) er-\n zwangen 1853 durch einen Strike die Herabsetzung des Zahlungstermins von einem\n Monat auf 14 Tage.“ („Reports of the Inspectors of Factories for 31st\nOct. 1853“, p. 34.) Als eine weitere artige Entwicklung des Kredits, den der Ar-\n beiter dem Kapitalisten giebt, kann man die Methode vieler englischer Kohlenberg-\n werksbesitzer betrachten, wonach der Arbeiter erst Ende des Monats bezahlt wird,\n und in der Zwischenzeit Vorschüsse vom Kapitalisten erhält, oft in Waaren,\n die er über ihrem Marktpreis zahlen muss (Prucksystem). „It is a common\n practice with the coal masters to pay once a month, and advance cash to their\n workmen at the end of each intermediate week. The cash is given in the shop (näm-\n lich dem tommy shop oder dem Meister selbst gehörigen Kramladen); the men\n take it on one side and lay it out on the other.“ („Childrens’ Employmen\n Commission, III. Report. Lond. 1864“, p. 38, n. 192.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"140\", \"facs\": \"#f0159\"} --> sumtion der Arbeitskraft, gleich der Konsumtion jeder andern Waare,\n vollzieht sich ausserhalb des Markts oder der Cirkulations-\n sphäre. Diese geräuschvolle, auf der Oberfläche hausende und Aller\n Augen zugängliche Sphäre verlassen wir daher, zusammen mit Geldbe-\n sitzer und Besitzer der Arbeitskraft, um beiden nachzufolgen in die ver-\n borgne Stätte der Produktion, an deren Schwelle zu lesen steht:\nNo admittance except on business. Hier wird sich zeigen,\n nicht nur wie das Kapital producirt, sondern auch wie das\n Kapital selbst producirt wird. Das Geheimniss der Plus-\n macherei muss sich endlich enthüllen.\n\n\n\n\nDie Sphäre der Cirkulation oder des Waarenaus-\n tauschs, innerhalb deren Schranken Kauf und Verkauf der Arbeitskraft\n sich bewegt, war in der That ein wahres Eden der angebornen\n Menschenrechte. Was allein hier herrscht, ist Freiheit, Gleich-\n heit, Eigenthum, und Bentham. Freiheit! denn Käufer und\n Verkäufer einer Waare, z. B. der Arbeitskraft, sind nur durch ihren\nfreien Willen bestimmt. Sie kontrahiren als freie, rechtlich ebenbür-\n tige Personen. Der Kontrakt ist das freie Produkt, worin sich ihre\n Willen einen gemeinsamen Rechtsausdruck geben. Gleichheit!\n Denn sie beziehen sich nur als Waarenbesitzer auf einander und\n tauschen Aequivalent für Aequivalent. Eigenthum! Denn jeder ver-\n fügt nur über das Seine. Bentham! Denn jedem von den beiden ist\n es nur um sich zu thun. Die einzige Macht, die sie zusammen und in ein\n Verhältniss bringt, ist die ihres Eigennutzes, ihres Sondervortheils,\n ihrer Privatinteressen. Und eben weil so jeder nur für sich und\n keiner für den andern kehrt, vollbringen alle, in Folge einer prästabi-\n lirten Harmonie der Dinge, oder unter den Auspicien einer all-\n pfiffigen Vorsehung, nur das Werk ihres wechselseitigen Vortheils, des Ge-\n meinnutzens, des Gesammtinteresses.\n\n\n\n\nBeim Scheiden von dieser Sphäre der einfachen Cirkulation oder des\n Waarenaustauschs, woraus der Freihändler vulgaris Anschauungen, Be-\n griffe und Massstab für sein Urtheil über die Gesellschaft des Kapitals und\n der Lohnarbeit entlehnt, verwandelt sich, so scheint es, schon in etwas die\n Physiognomie unserer dramatis personae. Der ehemalige Geldbe-\n sitzer schreitet voran als Kapitalist, der Arbeitskraft-Besitzer folgt ihm\n nach als sein Arbeiter; der Eine bedeutungsvoll schmunzelnd und ge-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"141\", \"facs\": \"#f0160\"} --> schäftseifrig, der Andre scheu, widerstrebsam, wie Jemand, der seine eigne\n Haut zu Markt getragen und nun nichts andres zu erwarten hat als die\n — Gerberei.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0001","text":"## Zweites Kapitel.\n Die Verwandlung von Geld in Kapital.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0002","text":"## 1) Die allgemeine Formel des Kapitals.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0003","text":"Die Waarencirculation ist der Ausgangspunkt des Kapitals. Waaren-\n produktion, Waarencirculation und entwickelte Waarencirculation, Handel,\n bilden daher stets die historischen Voraussetzungen, unter denen\n das Kapital entsteht. Von der Schöpfung des modernen Welthandels und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"107\", \"facs\": \"#f0126\"} --> Weltmarkts im 16. Jahrhundert datirt die moderne Lebensgeschichte des\n Kapitals.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0004","text":"Sehn wir ab vom stofflichen Inhalt der Waarencirculation, vom Aus-\n tausch der verschiednen Gebrauchswerthe, und betrachten wir nur die öko-\n nomischen Formen, die dieser Prozess erzeugt, so finden wir als sein letz-\n tes Produkt das Geld. Diess letzte Produkt der Waarencirculation ist\ndie erste Erscheinungsform des Kapitals.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0005","text":"Historisch tritt das Kapital dem Grundeigenthum überall zunächst in\n der Form von Geld gegenüber, von Geldvermögen, Kaufmannskapital\n und Wucherkapital [Note: Der Gegensatz zwischen der auf persönlichen Knechtschafts- und Herr-\n schaftsverhältnissen beruhenden Macht des Grundeigenthums und der unpersön-\n lichen Macht des Geldes ist klar gefasst in den zwei französischen Sprichworten:\n „Nulle terre sans seigneur.“ „L’argent n’a pas de maître.“] . Jedoch bedarf es nicht des Rückblicks auf die\n Entstehungsgeschichte des Kapitals, um das Geld als seine erste Erschei-\n nungsform zu erkennen. Dieselbe Geschichte spielt täglich vor unsern\n Augen. Jedes neue Kapital betritt in erster Instanz die Bühne, d. h. den\n Markt, Waarenmarkt, Arbeitsmarkt, oder Geldmarkt, immer noch als Geld,\n Geld, das sich durch bestimmte Prozesse in Kapital verwandeln soll.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0006","text":"Geld als Geld und Geld als Kapital unterscheiden sich zu-\n nächst nur durch ihre verschiedene Circulationsform.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0007","text":"Die unmittelbare Form der Waarencirculation ist W — G — W,\n Verwandlung von Waare in Geld und Rückverwandlung von Geld in Waare,\nverkaufen um zu kaufen. Neben dieser Form finden wir aber eine\n zweite, spezifisch unterschiedene vor, die Form G — W — G, Verwand-\n lung von Geld in Waare und Rückverwandlung von Waare in Geld, kau-\n fen um zu verkaufen. Geld, das in seiner Bewegung diese letztre\n Circulationsform beschreibt, verwandelt sich in Kapital, wird Kapital und\nist schon an sich, d. h. seiner Bestimmung nach, Kapital.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0008","text":"Sehn wir uns die Circulation G — W — G näher an. Es ist ein\n Prozess, der gleich dem der einfachen Waarencirculation, zwei entgegen-\n gesetzte Phasen durchläuft und ihre Einheit bildet. In der ersten Phase,\n G — W, Kauf, wird das Geld in Waare verwandelt. In der zweiten\n Phase, W — G, Verkauf, wird die Waare in Geld rückverwandelt.\n Die Einheit beider Phasen aber, die Gesammtbewegung, stellt sich\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"108\", \"facs\": \"#f0127\"} --> dar als Austausch von Geld gegen Waare und Wiederaustausch dersel-\n ben Waare gegen Geld, Waare kaufen um sie zu verkaufen, oder, wenn\n man die formellen Unterschiede von Kauf und Verkauf übersieht, mit dem\n Geld Waare und mit der Waare Geld kaufen [Note: „Avec de l’argent on achète des marchandises, et avec des marchandises\n on achète de l’argent.“ (Mercier de la Rivière: „L’ordre naturel\n et essentiel des sociétés publiques“, p. 543.)] . Was aber das Resultat\n des Prozesses angeht, so erlischt er im Austausch von Geld gegen\n Geld, G — G. Wenn ich für 100 Pfd. St. 2000 Pfund Baum-\n wolle kaufe und die 2000 Pfund Baumwolle wieder für 110 Pfd. St. ver-\n kaufe, so habe ich schliesslich 100 Pfd. St. gegen 110 Pfd. St. ausge-\n tauscht, Geld gegen Geld.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0009","text":"Es ist nun zwar augenscheinlich, dass der Circulationsprozess G —\n W — G abgeschmackt und inhaltslos wäre, wollte man vermittelst seines\n Umwegs denselben Geldwerth gegen denselben Geldwerth, also z. B.\n 100 Pfd. St. gegen 100 Pfd. St. austauschen. Ungleich einfacher und\n sichrer wäre die Methode des Schatzbildners, der die 100 Pfd. St. fest-\n hält, statt sie der Circulationsgefahr preiszugeben. Andrerseits, ob der\n Kaufmann die mit 100 Pfd. St. gekaufte Baumwolle wieder verkauft zu\n 110 Pfd. St., oder ob er sie zu 100 Pfd. St. und selbst zu 50 Pfd. St.\n losschlagen muss, unter allen Umständen hat sein Geld eine eigenthüm-\n liche und originelle Bewegung beschrieben, durchaus verschieden von der,\n die es in der einfachen Waarencirculation beschreibt, z. B. in der Hand\n des Bauern, der Korn verkauft und mit dem so gelösten Geld Kleider\n kauft. Es gilt also zunächst die Charakteristik der Formunterschiede\n zwischen den Kreisläufen G — W — G und W — G — W. Damit wird\n sich zugleich der inhaltliche Unterschied ergeben, der hinter diesen Form-\n unterschieden lauert.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0010","text":"Sehn wir zunächst, was beiden Formen gemeinsam.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0011","text":"Beide Kreisläufe zerfallen in dieselben zwei entgegengesetzten Phasen,\n W — G, Verkauf, und G — W, Kauf.. Jede dieser Phasen für sich be-\n trachtet, ist kein Unterschied zu erkennen. Die den Prozess eingehenden\n Elemente sind in beiden Formen dieselben, Waare und Geld. In jedem\n Abschnitt der beiden Kreisläufe stehn sich dieselben ökonomischen Charak-\n termasken gegenüber, Käufer und Verkäufer. In beiden Prozessen treten\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"109\", \"facs\": \"#f0128\"} --> drei Contrahenten auf, so dass aber stets nur ein Contrahent abwechselnd\n als Käufer und Verkäufer figurirt, während von den beiden andern Con-\n trahenten der eine nur verkauft und der andre nur kauft. Beide Kreisläufe\n endlich sind Einheiten derselben entgegengesetzten Phasen.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0012","text":"Der Formunterschied zwischen den Prozessen W — G — W und\n G — W — G springt erst in’s Auge, sobald man, statt der zwei Phasen,\n worin beide zerfallen, ihre Gesammtverläufe vergleicht. Was sie von vorn\n herein scheidet, ist die umgekehrte Reihenfolge derselben ent-\n gegengesetzten Circulationsphasen. Die einfache Waarencirculation be-\n ginnt mit dem Verkauf und endet mit dem Kauf, die Circulation des Gel-\n des als Kapital beginnt mit dem Kauf und endet mit dem Verkauf. Dort\n bildet die Waare, hier das Geld den Ausgangspunkt und Schlusspunkt\n der Bewegung. In der ersten Form funktionirt das Geld, in der andern\n umgekehrt die Waare als Mittler des Gesammtverlaufs.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0013","text":"In der Circulation W — G — W wird das Geld schliesslich in\n Waare verwandelt, die als Gebrauchswerth dient. Das Geld ist also defi-\n nitiv ausgegeben. In der umgekehrten Form G — W — G giebt der\n Käufer dagegen Geld aus, um als Verkäufer Geld einzunehmen. Er wirft\n Geld beim Kauf der Waare in die Circulation, um es ihr durch den Verkauf\n derselben Waare wieder zu entziehn. Er entlässt das Geld nur mit der\n hinterlistigen Absicht seiner wieder habhaft zu werden. Es wird daher\n nur vorgeschossen [Note: „When a thing is bought, in order to be sold again, the sum employed is\n called money advanced; when it is bought not to be sold, it may be said to\n be expended.“ (James Steuart: Works etc. edited by General\n Sir James Steuart, his son. Lond. 1801, v. I, p. 274.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0014","text":"In der Form W — G — W wechselt dasselbe Geldstück\n zweimal die Stelle. Der Verkäufer erhält es vom Käufer und zahlt es\n weg an einen andern Verkäufer. Mit der Weggabe von Geld für Waare\n schliesst der Gesammtprozess ab, wie er mit der Einnahme von Geld für\n Waare begann. Umgekehrt in der Form G — W — G. Nicht das-\n selbe Geldstück, sondern dieselbe Waare wechselt hier zweimal\n die Stelle. Der Käufer erhält sie aus der Hand des Verkäufers und giebt\n sie weg in die Hand eines andern Käufers. Wie in der einfachen Waaren-\n circulation durch den zweimaligen Stellenwechsel dessel-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"110\", \"facs\": \"#f0129\"} --> ben Geldstücks sein definitives Uebergehn aus einer Hand\n in die andre bedingt ist, so hier durch den zweimaligen Stel-\n lenwechsel derselben Waare der Rückfluss des Geldes zu\n seinem ersten Ausgangspunkt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0015","text":"Der Rückfluss des Geldes zu seinem Ausgangspunkt\n hängt nicht davon ab, ob oder ob nicht die Waare theurer verkauft wird\n als sie gekauft war. Dieser Umstand beeinflusst nur die Grösse der rück-\n fliessenden Geldsumme. Das Phänomen des Rückflusses selbst findet\n statt, sobald die gekaufte Waare wieder verkauft, also der Kreislauf\n G — W — G vollständig beschrieben wird. Es ist diess also ein sinnlich\n wahrnehmbarer Unterschied zwischen der Circulation des Geldes als\n Kapital und seiner Circulation als blossem Geld.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0016","text":"Allerdings kann auch in W — G — W Rückfluss des Geldes zu sei-\n nem Ausgangspunkt stattfinden, aber nur durch die Erneuerung oder Wie-\n derholung des Gesammtprozesses, nicht durch den Verlauf seiner eignen\n Momente. Wenn ich ein Quarter Korn verkaufe für 3 Pfd. St. und mit die-\n sen 3 Pfd. St. Kleider kaufe, sind die 3 Pfd. St. für mich definitiv veraus-\n gabt. Ich habe nichts mehr mit ihnen zu schaffen. Sie sind des Kleider-\n händlers. Verkaufe ich nun ein zweites Quarter Korn, so fliesst Geld zu\n mir zurück, aber nicht in Folge der ersten Transaktion, sondern nur in\n Folge ihrer Wiederholung. Es entfernt sich wieder von mir, sobald ich\n die zweite Transaktion zu Ende führe und von neuem kaufe. In der Cir-\n culation W — G — W hat also die Verausgabung des Geldes nichts mit\n seinem Rückfluss zu schaffen. In G — W — G dagegen ist der Rück-\n fluss des Geldes durch die Art seiner Verausgabung selbst bedingt. Ohne\n diesen Rückfluss ist die Operation missglückt oder der Prozess unter-\n brochen und noch nicht fertig, weil seine zweite Phase, der den Kauf er-\n gänzende und abschliessende Verkauf fehlt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0017","text":"Der Kreislauf W — G — W geht aus von dem Extrem einer Waare\n und schliesst ab mit dem Extrem einer andern Waare, die aus der Circulation\n heraus und der Consumtion anheim fällt. Consumtion, Befriedigung von Be-\n dürfnissen, mit einem Wort, Gebrauchswerth ist daher sein Endzweck.\n Der Kreislauf G — W — G geht dagegen aus von dem Extrem des Geldes\n und bewegt sich fort zu demselben Extrem als seinem Schluss. Sein treiben-\n des Motiv und bestimmender Zweck ist daher der Tauschwerth selbst.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0018","text":"In der einfachen Waarencirculation haben beide Extreme dieselbe\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"111\", \"facs\": \"#f0130\"} --> ökonomische Formbestimmtheit. Sie sind beide Waaren. Sie sind auch\n Waaren von derselben Werthgrösse. Aber sie sind zugleich qua-\n litativ verschiedne Gebrauchswerthe, z. B. Korn und Kleider.\n Der Produktenaustausch, der Wechsel der verschiednen Stoffe, worin sich\n die gesellschaftliche Arbeit darstellt, bildet hier den Inhalt der Bewegung.\n Anders in der Circulation G — W — G. Sie scheint auf den ersten\n Blick inhaltlos, weil tautologisch. Beide Extreme haben dieselbe öko-\n nomische Formbestimmtheit. Sie sind beide Geld. Sie unterscheiden\n sich auch nicht qualitativ als Gebrauchswerthe, denn Geld ist eben die\n verwandelte Gestalt der Waaren, worin ihre besondern Gebrauchswerthe\n ausgelöscht sind. Erst 100 Pfd. St. gegen Baumwolle und dann wieder\n dieselbe Baumwolle gegen 100 Pfd. St. austauschen, also auf einem Um-\n weg Geld gegen Geld, dasselbe gegen dasselbe, scheint eine ebenso zweck-\n lose als abgeschmackte Operation [Note: „On n’échange pas de l’argent contre de l’argent,“ ruft Mercier de la\n Rivière den Merkantilisten zu. (l. c. p. 486.) In einem Werke, welches ex\n professo vom „Handel“ und der „Spekulation“ handelt, liest man: „Aller\n Handel besteht im Austausch von Dingen verschiedner Art; und der Vortheil (für\n den Kaufmann?) entspringt eben aus dieser Verschiedenheit. Ein Pfund Brod\n gegen ein Pfund Brod austauschen, wäre ohne allen Vortheil … daher der vor-\n theilhafte Kontrast zwischen Handel und Spiel, welches nur Austausch\n von Geld gegen Geld ist.“ (Th. Corbet: „An Inquiry into\n the Causes and Modes of the Wealth of Individuals; or the\n Principles of Trade and Speculation explained. London. 1841“,\n p. 5.) Obgleich Corbet nicht sieht, dass G — G, Geld gegen Geld austauschen,\n die charakteristische Circulationsform, nicht nur des Handelskapitals, sondern\nallen Kapitals ist, giebt er wenigstens zu, dass diese Form einer Art des Handels,\n der Spekulation, mit dem Spiel gemein sei, aber dann kommt MacCulloch\n und findet, dass Kaufen um zu verkaufen Speculiren ist, und der Unter-\n schied zwischen Speculation und Handel also wegfällt. „Every transaction\n in which an individual buys produce in order to sell it again, is,\n in fact, a speculation.“ (Mac Culloch: A Dictionary practical\n etc. of Commerce. London 1847, p. 1056.) Ungleich naiver Pinto,\n der Pindar der Amsterdamer Börse: „Le commerce est un jeu (dieser\n Satz entlehnt aus Locke); et ce n’est pas avec des gueux qu’on peut gagner. Si\n l’on gagnait long temps en tout avec tous, il faudrait rendre de bon accord les\n plus grandes parties du profit, pour recommencer le jeu.“ (Pinto: Traité de\n la Circulation et du Credit. Amsterdam 1771, p. 231.)] . Eine Geldsumme kann sich von der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"112\", \"facs\": \"#f0131\"} --> andern Geldsumme überhaupt nur durch ihre Grösse unterscheiden. Der\n Prozess G — W — G schuldet seinen Inhalt daher keinem qualita-\n tiven Unterschied seiner Extreme, denn sie sind beide Geld, son-\n dern nur ihrer quantitativen Verschiedenheit. Schliesslich\n wird der Circulation mehr Geld entzogen als Anfangs hineingeworfen\n ward. Die zu 100 Pfd. St. gekaufte Baumwolle wird z. B. wieder ver-\n kauft zu 100 + 10 Pfd. St. oder 110 Pfd. St. Die vollständige Form\n dieses Prozesses ist daher G — W — G', wo G' = G + ∆ G, d. h.\n gleich der ursprünglich vorgeschossenen Geldsumme plus einem Incre-\n ment. Diess Increment oder den Ueberschuss über den ursprünglichen\n Werth nenne ich Mehrwerth (surplus value). Der ursprünglich\n vorgeschossene Werth erhält sich daher nicht nur in der Circulation, son-\n dern in ihr verändert er seine Werthgrösse, setzt einen Mehr-\n werth zu, oder verwerthet sich. Und diese Bewegung verwandelt\n ihn in Kapital.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0019","text":"Es ist zwar auch möglich, dass in W — G — W die beiden Extreme\n W, W, z. B. Korn und Kleider, quantitativ verschiedne Werth-\n grössen sind. Der Bauer kann sein Korn über dem Werth verkauft\n oder die Kleider unter ihrem Werth gekauft haben. Er kann seinerseits\n vom Kleiderhändler geprellt werden. Solche Werthverschiedenheit bleibt\n jedoch für diese Circulationsform selbst rein zufällig. Sinn\n und Verstand verliert sie nicht schier, wie der Prozess G — W — G,\n wenn die beiden Extreme, Korn und Kleider z. B., Aequivalente sind.\n Ihr Gleichwerth ist hier vielmehr Bedingung des normalen Verlaufs.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0020","text":"Die Wiederholung oder Erneurung des Verkaufs um zu kaufen\n findet, wie dieser Prozess selbst, Mass und Ziel an einem ausser ihm lie-\n genden Endzwecke, der Consumtion, der Befriedigung bestimmter Bedürf-\n nisse. Im Kauf für den Verkauf dagegen sind Anfang und Ende\n dasselbe, Geld, Tauschwerth, und schon dadurch ist die Bewegung\n endlos. Allerdings ist aus G, G + ∆ G geworden, aus den 100 Pfd.\n St., 100 + 10. Aber blos die Form betrachtet, sind 110 Pfd. St. das-\n selbe wie 100 Pfd. St., nämlich Geld. Und die Werthgrösse betrachtet,\n sind 110 Pfd. eine beschränkte Werthsumme wie 100 Pfd. St.\n Würden die 110 Pfd. St. als Geld verausgabt, so fielen sie aus ihrer\n Rolle. Sie hörten auf Kapital zu sein. Der Circulation entzogen, ver-\n steinern sie zum Schatz und kein Farthing wächst ihnen an, ob sie bis\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"113\", \"facs\": \"#f0132\"} --> zum jüngsten Tag fortlagern. Handelt es sich also einmal um die Werth-\n grösse als solche, um Verwerthung des Werths, so besteht das-\n selbe Bedürfniss für die Verwerthung von 110 Pfd. St. wie für die von\n 100 Pfd. St., da beide beschränkte Ausdrücke des Tauschwerths sind,\n beide also denselben Beruf haben sich dem Reichthum schlechthin durch\n Grössenausdehnung anzunähern. Allerdings unterscheidet sich für einen\n Augenblick der ursprünglich vorgeschossene Werth 100 Pfd. St. von dem\n in der Circulation ihm zuwachsenden Mehrwerth von 10 Pfd. St., aber\n dieser Unterschied zerfliesst sofort wieder. Es kommt am Ende des Pro-\n zesses nicht auf der einen Seite der Originalwerth von 100 Pfd. St. und\n auf der andern Seite der Mehrwerth von 10 Pfd. St. heraus. Was heraus-\n kommt ist Ein Werth von 110 Pfd. St., der sich ganz in derselben ent-\n sprechenden Form befindet, um den Verwerthungsprozess zu beginnen, wie\n die ursprünglichen 100 Pfd. St. Geld kommt am Ende der Bewegung\n wieder als ihr Anfang heraus [Note: „Das Kapital theilt sich … in das ursprüngliche Kapital und den Gewinn,\n den Zuwachs des Kapitals … obwohl die Praxis selbst diesen Gewinn sogleich\n wieder zum Kapital schlägt und mit diesem in Fluss setzt.“ (F. Engels: „Um-\n risse zu einer Kritik der Nationalökonomie“ in „Deutsch-\n Französische Jahrbücher, herausgegeben von Arnold Ruge und\n Karl Marx.“ Paris 1844, p. 99.)] . Wenn die einfache Waarencirculation\n daher im Gebrauchswerth eine ihr von aussen gesetzte Schranke\n hat, ist die Bewegung des Kapitals dagegen masslos, indem sie in ihrem\n Abschluss das Prinzip und den Trieb ihrer Wiedererneuerung findet, und\n ihr Ziel, die Verwerthung des Werths, am Ende des Prozesses\n eben so wenig erreicht ist als am Anfang [Note: Aristoteles stellt der Chrematistik die Oekonomik entgegen.\n Er geht von der Oekonomik aus. So weit sie Erwerbskunst, beschränkt sie sich\n auf die Verschaffung der zum Leben nothwendigen und für das Haus oder den\n Staat nützlichen Güter. „Der wahre Reichthum (ὁ ἀληϑινὸς πλοῦτος) besteht aus\n solchen Gebrauchswerthen; denn das zum guten Leben genügende Mass dieser\n Art von Besitz ist nicht unbegrenzt. Es giebt aber eine zweite Art der Er-\n werbskunst, die vorzugsweise und mit Recht Chrematistik heisst, in Folge\n deren keine Grenze des Reichthums und Besitzes zu existiren scheint. Der\nWaarenhandel (ἡ ϰαπηλιπὴ heisst wörtlich Kramhandel und Aristoteles nimmt] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0021","text":"Als bewusster Träger dieses Prozesses wird der Geldbesitzer Kapita-\n list. Seine Person, oder vielmehr seine Tasche, ist der Ausgangspunkt\n [Running matter: I. 8] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"114\", \"facs\": \"#f0133\"} --> und der Rückkehrpunkt des Geldes. Der objektive Inhalt jenes\n Prozesses — Verwerthung des Werths — ist sein subjektiver Zweck,\n und nur soweit wachsende Aneignung des abstrakten Reichthums das allein\n treibende Motiv seiner Operationen, funktionirt er als Kapitalist oder\n personificirtes, mit Willen und Bewusstsein begabtes Kapital. Der Ge-\n brauchswerth ist also nie als unmittelbarer Zweck des Kapitalisten zu\n behandeln [Note: „Commodities (hier im Sinn von Gebrauchswerthen) are not the termi-\n nating object of the trading capitalist … money is his terminating object.“ (Th.\n Chalmers: On Politic. Econ. etc. 2nd edit. Lond. 1832, p. 166.)] . Auch nicht der einzelne Gewinn, sondern nur die rastlose\n Bewegung des Gewinnens [Note: „Il mercante non conta quasi per niente il lucro fatto, ma mira sempre al\n futuro.“ (A. Genovesi: Lezioni di Economia Civile (1765). Aus-\n gabe der italienischen Oekonomen von Custodi, Parte Moderna, t. VIII,\n p. 139.)] . Dieser absolute Bereicherungstrieb, diese\n [Note: diese Form, weil in ihr der Gebrauchswerth vorherrscht) gehört von Natur nicht\n zur Chrematistik, denn hier bezieht sich der Austausch nur auf das für sie selbst\n (Käufer und Verkäufer) Nöthige.“ Daher, entwickelt er weiter, war auch die ur-\n sprüngliche Form des Waarenhandels der Tauschhandel, aber mit seiner Aus-\n dehnung entstand nothwendig das Geld. Mit der Erfindung des Geldes musste\n sich der Tauschhandel nothwendig zur ϰαπηλιϰὴ, zum Waarenhandel entwickeln,\n und dieser, im Widerspruch zu seiner ursprünglichen Tendenz, bildete sich zur\n Chrematistik aus, zur Kunst Geld zu machen. Die Chrematistik nun unterscheidet\n sich von der Oekonomik dadurch, dass „für sie die Circulation die Quelle des\n Reichthums ist (ποιητιϰὴ χϱημάτων … διὰ χϱημάτων διαβολῆς).\n Und um das Geld scheint sie sich zu drehen, denn das Geld ist der Anfang\n und das Ende dieser Art von Austausch (τὸ γάϱ νόμισμα στοιχεῖον\n ϰαὶ πέϱας τῆς ἀλλαγῆς ἐστίν). Daher ist auch der Reichthum, wie ihn die Chre-\n matistik anstrebt, unbegrenzt. Wie nämlich jede Kunst, der ihr Ziel nicht als\n Mittel, sondern als letzter Endzweck gilt, unbegrenzt in ihrem Streben ist, denn\n sie sucht sich ihm stets mehr zu nähern, während die Künste, die nur Mittel zum\n Zwecke verfolgen, nicht unbegrenzt sind, da der Zweck selbst ihnen die Grenze\n setzt, so giebt es auch für diese Chrematistik keine Schranke ihres Ziels, sondern\n ihr Ziel ist absolute Bereicherung. Die Oekonomik, nicht die Chrematistik hat\n eine Grenze . . . . die erstere bezweckt ein vom Gelde selbst Verschiednes, die\n andere seine Vermehrung . . . . Die Verwechslung beider Formen, die in einander\n überspielen, verursacht einige die Erhaltung und Vermehrung des Geldes ins Un-\n endliche als Endziel der Oekonomik zu betrachten.“ (Aristoteles: De Rep.\n edit. Bekker, lib. I, c. 8 und 9 passim.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"115\", \"facs\": \"#f0134\"} --> leidenschaftliche Jagd auf den Tauschwerth [Note: „Die unauslöschliche Leidenschaft für den Gewinn, die auri sacra fames\n bestimmt stets den Kapitalisten.“ (Mac Culloch: The Principles of Polit.\n Econ. London 1830, p. 163.) Diese Einsicht verhindert denselben Mac Cul-\n loch und Cons. natürlich nicht, in theoretischen Verlegenheiten, z. B. bei Behand-\n lung der Ueberproduktion, denselben Kapitalisten in einen guten Bürger zu ver-\n wandeln, dem es sich nur um den Gebrauchswerth handelt und der sogar einen\n wahren Wehrwolfsheisshunger für Stiefel, Hüte, Eier, Kattune und andre höchst\n familiäre Sorten von Gebrauchswerth entwickelt.]  ist dem Kapitalisten mit dem\n Schatzbildner gemein, aber während der Schatzbildner nur der verrückte\n Kapitalist, ist der Kapitalist der rationelle Schatzbildner. Die Unvergäng-\n lichkeit des Tauschwerths, die der Schatzbildner anstrebt, indem er das\n Geld vor der Circulation zu retten sucht [Note: „Σώζειν“ ist einer der charakteristischen Ausdrücke der Griechen für\n das Schatzbilden.] , erreicht der klügere Kapi-\n talist, indem er es stets von neuem der Circulation preisgiebt [Note: „Questo infinito che le cose non hanno in progresso, hanno in giro.“\n (Galiani.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0022","text":"In der einfachen Circulation entwickeln die Waaren ihren Werth zu\n verschiednen ihrem Gebrauchswerth gegenübertretenden selbstständigen\n Formen, d. h. zu Geldformen, die den Austausch vermitteln und in seinem\n Endresultat verschwinden. In der Circulation G — W — G funktioniren\n beide, Waare und Geld, nur als verschiedne Existenzweisen\n des Werths selbst, das Geld seine allgemeine, die Waare seine be-\n sondre, so zu sagen nur verkleidete Existenzweise [Note: „Ce n’est pas la matière qui fait le capital, mais la valeur de ces ma-\n tières.“ (J. B. Say: Traité de L’Econ. Polit. 3ème ed. Paris 1817,\n t. I, p. 428.)] . Er geht beständig\n aus der einen Form in die andre über, ohne sich in dieser Bewegung zu\n verlieren und verwandelt sich so in ein automatisches, in sich selbst pro-\n zessirendes Subjekt. Fixirt man eine der besondern Erscheinungsformen,\n worin er sich abwechselnd darstellt, so erhält man die Erklärungen:\nKapitalist Geld, Kapitalist Waare [Note: „Currency (!) employed to productive purposes is capital.“ (Mac\n Leod: „The Theory and Practice of Banking. London 1855“, v. I,\n c. I.) „Capital is commodities.“ (James Mill: „Elements of Pol. Econ.\n Lond. 1821“, p. 74.)] . In der That aber wird\n [Running matter: 8*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"116\", \"facs\": \"#f0135\"} -->der Werth hier das Subjekt eines Prozesses, worin er unter\n dem beständigen Wechsel der Formen von Geld und Waare, seine Grösse\n selbst verändert, sich als Mehrwerth von sich selbst als ursprünglichem\n Werth abstösst, sich selbst verwerthet. Denn die Bewegung,\n worin er Mehrwerth zusetzt, ist seine eigne Bewegung, seine Verwerthung\n ist also Selbstverwerthung. Er hat die occulte Qualität erhalten,\n Werth zu setzen, weil er Werth ist. Er wirft lebendige Junge oder legt\n wenigstens goldne Eier.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0023","text":"Als das übergreifende Subjekt eines solchen Prozesses, worin er\n Geldform und Waarenform bald annimmt, bald abstösst, sich aber in die-\n sem Wechsel erhält und ausreckt, bedarf der Werth natürlich vor allem\n einer selbsstständigen Form, wodurch seine Identität mit sich selbst konsta-\n tirt werden kann. Und diese Form besitzt er nur im Gelde. Diess\n bildet daher Ausgangspunkt und Schlusspunkt jeden Verwerthungspro-\n zesses. Er war 100 Pfd. St., er ist jetzt 110 Pfd. St. u. s. w. Aber das\n Geld selbst gilt hier nur als eine Form des Werths, denn er hat deren\nzwei. Und die Annahme der Waarenform bildet grade das vermit-\n telnde Moment seiner Bewegung. Das Geld tritt hier also nicht pole-\n misch gegen die Waare auf, wie in der Schatzbildung. Der Kapitalist\n weiss, dass alle Waaren, wie lumpig sie immer aussehn oder wie schlecht\n sie immer riechen mögen, im Glauben und in der Wahrheit Geld, inner-\n lich verschnittene Juden sind. G — G', geldheckendes Geld —\n (money which begets money lautet die Beschreibung des Kapitals\n im Munde seiner ersten Dolmetscher, der Mercantilisten), — ist in\n der That nur die unmittelbare Erscheinungsform des Werth setzenden\n Werths, des sich selbst verwerthenden Werths.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0024","text":"Wenn der Tauschwerth in der Waarencirculation höchstens zur selbst-\n ständigen Form gegenüber dem Gebrauchswerth der Waare heranreift, so stellt\n er sich hier plötzlich dar als eine prozessirende, sich selbst bewegende Sub-\n stanz, für welche Waare und Geld beide blosse Formen. Aber noch mehr. Statt\n Waarenverhältnisse darzustellen, tritt er jetzt so zu sagen in ein Privat-\n verhältniss zu sich selbst. Er unterscheidet sich als ursprüng-\n licher Werth von sich selbst als Mehrwerth, als Gott Vater von sich selbst\n als Gottsohn, und beide sind vom selben Alter, und bilden in der That nur\n eine Person, denn nur durch den Mehrwerth von 10 Pfd. St. werden die vorge-\n schossenen 100 Pfd. St. Kapital, und sobald sie diess geworden, sobald der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"117\", \"facs\": \"#f0136\"} --> Sohn, und durch den Sohn der Vater erzeugt, verschwindet ihr Unterschied\n wieder und sind beide Eins, 110 Pfd. St.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0025","text":"Der Werth wird also prozessirender Werth, prozessiren-\n des Geld und als solches Kapital. Er kommt aus der Circulation\n her, geht wieder in sie ein, erhält und vervielfältigt sich in ihr, kehrt ver-\n grössert aus ihr zurück und beginnt denselben Kreislauf stets wieder von\n neuem [Note: „Kapital . . . . permanenter sich vervielfältigender Werth“. (Sis\n mondi: Nouveaux Principes del’Econ. Polit., t. I, p. 90.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0026","text":"Kaufen um zu verkaufen, oder vollständiger, kaufen um\n theurer zu verkaufen, G — W — G', scheint zwar nur einer Art\n des Kapitals, dem Kaufmannskapital, eigenthümliche Form. Aber\n auch das industrielle Kapital ist Geld, das sich in Waare verwan-\n delt und durch den Verkauf der Waare in mehr Geld rückverwandelt.\n Akte, die etwa zwischen dem Kauf und dem Verkaufe, ausserhalb der Cir-\n culationssphäre, vorgehn, ändern nichts an dieser Form der Bewegung.\n In dem zinstragenden Kapital endlich stellt sich die Form\n G — W — G' abgekürzt dar, im Resultat ohne die Vermittlung, so zu\n sagen im Lapidarstyl, als G — G', Geld, das gleich mehr Geld, Werth,\n der grösser als er selbst ist.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0027","text":"In der That also ist G — W — G' die allgemeine Formel\n des Kapitals, wie es unmittelbar in der Circulationssphäre erscheint.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0028","text":"## b) Widersprüche der allgemeinen Formel.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0029","text":"Die Circulationsform, worin sich das Geld zum Kapital entpuppt,\n widerspricht allen früher entwickelten Gesetzen über die Natur der Waare,\n des Werths, des Geldes und der Circulation selbst. Was sie von der ein-\n fachen Waarencirculation unterscheidet, ist die umgekehrte Reihen-\n folge derselben zwei entgegengesetzten Prozesse, Verkauf und Kauf. Und\n wie sollte dieser rein formelle Unterschied die Natur dieser Prozesse um-\n zaubern?","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0030","text":"Noch mehr. Diese Umkehrung existirt nur für einen der drei\n Geschäftsfreunde, die mit einander handeln. Als Kapitalist kaufe ich\n Waare von A und verkaufe sie wieder an B, während ich als einfacher\n Waarenbesitzer Waare an B verkaufe und dann Waare von A kaufe. Für\n die Geschäftsfreunde A und B existirt dieser Unterschied nicht. Sie treten\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"118\", \"facs\": \"#f0137\"} --> nur als Käufer oder Verkäufer von Waaren auf. Ich selbst stehe ihnen\n jedesmal als einfacher Geldbesitzer oder Waarenbesitzer, Käufer oder Ver-\n käufer, gegenüber, und zwar trete ich in beiden Reihenfolgen der einen\n Person nur als Käufer und der andern nur als Verkäufer gegenüber, der\n einen als nur Geld, der andern als nur Waare, keiner von beiden als Kapi-\n tal oder Kapitalist oder Repräsentant von irgend etwas, das mehr als Geld\n oder Waare wäre oder eine andere Wirkung ausser der des Geldes oder der\n Waare ausüben könnte. Für mich bilden Kauf von A und Verkauf an\n B eine Reihenfolge. Aber der Zusammenhang zwischen diesen beiden\n Akten existirt nur für mich. A scheert sich nicht um meine Transaktion\n mit B, und B nicht um meine Transaktion mit A. Wollte ich ihnen etwa\n das besondere Verdienst klar machen, das ich mir durch die Umkeh-\n rung der Reihenfolge erworben, so würden sie mir beweisen, dass ich\n mich in der Reihenfolge selbst irre und dass die Gesammttransaktion\n nicht mit einem Kauf begann und einem Verkauf endete, sondern umgekehrt\n mit einem Verkauf begann und mit einem Kauf abschloss. In der That,\n mein erster Akt, der Kauf, war von A’s Standpunkt ein Verkauf, und mein\n zweiter Akt, der Verkauf, war von B’s Standpunkt ein Kauf. Nicht zu-\n frieden damit, werden A und B erklären, dass die ganze Reihenfolge über-\n flüssig und Hokus Pokus war. A wird die Waare direkt an B verkaufen\n und B sie direkt von A kaufen. Damit verschrumpft die ganze Trans-\n aktion in einen einseitigen Akt der gewöhnlichen Waarencirculation,\n vom Standpunkt A’s blosser Verkauf und vom Standpunkt B’s blosser Kauf.\n Wir sind also durch die Umkehrung der Reihenfolge nicht über die Sphäre\n der einfachen Waarencirculation hinausgekommen und müssen\n vielmehr zusehn, ob sie ihrer Natur nach Verwerthung der in sie hinein-\n gehenden Werthe und daher Bildung von Mehrwerth gestattet.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0031","text":"Nehmen wir den Circulationsprozess in einer Form, worin er sich als\n blosser Waarenaustausch darstellt. Diess ist stets der Fall, wenn beide\n Waarenbesitzer Waaren von einander kaufen und die Bilanz ihrer wechsel-\n seitigen Geldforderungen sich am Zahlungstag ausgleicht. Das Geld dient\n hier als Rechengeld, um die Werthe der Waaren in ihren Preisen aus-\n zudrücken, tritt aber nicht den Waaren selbst dinglich gegenüber. So-\n weit es sich um den Gebrauchswerth handelt, ist es klar, dass beide\n Austauscher hier gewinnen können. Beide veräussern Waaren, die\n ihnen als Gebrauchswerth nutzlos, und erhalten Waaren, deren sie zum Ge-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"119\", \"facs\": \"#f0138\"} --> brauch bedürfen. Und dieser Nutzen mag nicht der einzige sein. A, der\n Wein verkauft und Getreide kauft, producirt vielleicht mehr Wein als Ge-\n treidebauer B in derselben Arbeitszeit produciren könnte und Getreide-\n bauer B in derselben Arbeitszeit mehr Getreide als Weinbauer A\n produciren könnte. A erhält also für denselben Tauschwerth mehr Ge-\n treide und B mehr Wein als wenn jeder von den beiden, ohne Austausch,\n Wein und Getreide für sich selbst produciren müsste. Mit Bezug auf den\nGebrauchswerth also kann gesagt werden, dass „der Austausch eine\n Transaktion ist, worin beide Seiten gewinnen [Note: „L’échange est une transaction admirable dans laquelle les deux contrac-\n tants gagnent — toujours (!).“ (Destutt de Tracy: Traité de la Volonté\n et de ses Effets. Paris 1826, p. 68.) Dasselbe Buch erschien später als\n „Traité de l’Ec. Pol.“] .“ Anders mit dem\nTauschwerth. „Ein Mann, der viel Wein und kein Getreide besitzt,\n handelt mit einem Mann, der viel Getreide und keinen Wein besitzt und\n zwischen ihnen wird ausgetauscht Weizen zum Werth von 50 gegen einen\n Werth von 50 in Wein. Dieser Austausch ist keine Vermehrung\n des Tauschwerths weder für den einen, noch für den andern; denn\n bereits vor dem Austausch besass jeder von ihnen einen Werth gleich dem,\n den er sich vermittelst dieser Operation verschafft hat [Note: Mercier de la Rivière l. c. p. 544.] .“ Es ändert\n nichts an der Sache, wenn das Geld als Circulationsmittel zwischen die\n Waaren tritt und die Akte des Kaufs und Verkaufs sinnlich auseinander-\n fallen [Note: „Que l’une de ces deux valeurs soit argent, ou qu’elles soient toutes deux\n marchandises usuelles, rien de plus indifférent en soi.“ (Mercier de la Ri-\n vière l. c. p. 543.)] . Der Werth der Waaren ist in ihren Preisen dargestellt,\n bevor sie in die Circulation treten, also Voraussetzung und nicht Resultat\n derselben [Note: „Ce ne sont pas les contractants qui prononcent sur la valeur; elle est\n decidée avant la convention.“ (Le Trosne p. 906.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0032","text":"Abstrakt betrachtet, d. h. abgesehn von Umständen, die nicht aus\n den immanenten Gesetzen der einfachen Waarencirculation hervorfliessen,\n geht ausser dem Ersatz eines Gebrauchswerths durch einen andern nichts\n in ihr vor als eine Metamorphose, ein blosser Formwechsel der\n Waare. Derselbe Tauschwerth, d. h. dasselbe Quantum vergegen-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"120\", \"facs\": \"#f0139\"} --> ständlichter gesellschaftlicher Arbeit, bleibt in der Hand desselben Waaren-\n besitzers abwechselnd in Gestalt seiner Waare, des Geldes, worin sie sich\n verwandelt, der Waare, worin sich diess Geld rückverwandelt. Dieser\n Formwechsel schliesst keine Aenderung der Werthgrösse ein. Der Wech-\n sel aber, den der Tauschwerth der Waare selbst in diesem Prozess erfährt,\n beschränkt sich auf einen Wechsel seiner Geldform. Er existirt erst\n als Preis der zum Verkauf angebotenen Waare, dann als dieselbe\n Geldsumme, die in diesem Preise ausgedrückt ist, endlich als der Preis\n einer äquivalenten Waare. Dieser Formwechsel schliesst an und für\n sich eben so wenig eine Aenderung der Werthgrösse ein, wie das\n Auswechseln einer Fünfpfundnote gegen Sovereigns, halbe Sovereigns und\n Schillinge. Sofern also die Cirkulation der Waare nur einen Form-\n wechsel ihres Tauschwerths bedingt, bedingt sie, wenn das Phänomen\n rein vorgeht, Austausch von Aequivalenten. Die Vulgäröko-\n nomie selbst, so wenig sie ahnt, was der Werth ist, unterstellt daher, so\n oft sie in ihrer Art das Phänomen rein betrachten will, dass Nachfrage\n und Zufuhr sich decken, d. h. dass ihre Wirkung überhaupt fortfällt.\n Wenn also mit Bezug auf den Gebrauchswerth beide Austau-\n scher gewinnen können, können sie nicht beide gewinnen an Tausch-\n werth. Hier heisst es vielmehr: „Wo Gleichheit ist, ist kein Gewinn [Note: „Dove è eguagità, non è lucro.“ (Galiani: Della Moneta in\nCustodi: Parte Moderna, t. IV, p. 244.)] .“\n Waaren können zwar zu Preisen verkauft werden, die von ihren Werthen\n abweichen, aber diese Abweichung erscheint als Verletzung des Gesetzes\n des Waarenaustausches [Note: „L’échange devient désavantageux pour l’une des parties, lorsque quelque\n chose étrangère vient diminuer ou exagérer le prix: alors l’égalité est blessée, mais\n la lésion procède de cette cause et non de l’échange.“ (Le Trosne l. c.\n p. 904.)] . In seiner reinen Gestalt ist er ein Austausch\n von Aequivalenten, also kein Mittel sich an Werth zu bereichern [Note: „L’échange est de sa nature un contrat d’égalité qui se fait de valeur pour\n valeur égale. Il n’est donc pas un moyen de s’enrichir, puisque l’on\n donne autant que l’on reçoit.“ (Le Trosne l. c. p. 903.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0033","text":"Hinter den Versuchen, die Waarencirkulation als Quelle von\n Mehrwerth darzustellen, lauert daher meist ein quid pro quo, eine Ver-\n wechslung von Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth. So z. B. bei Con-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"121\", \"facs\": \"#f0140\"} --> dillac: „Es ist falsch, dass man im Waarenaustausch gleichen Werth\n gegen gleichen Werth austauscht. Umgekehrt. Jeder der beiden Con-\n trahenten giebt immer einen kleineren für einen grösseren Werth …\n Tauschte man in der That immer gleiche Werthe aus, so wäre kein\n Gewinn zu machen für irgend einen Contrahenten. Aber alle beide\n gewinnen oder sollten doch gewinnen. Warum? Der Werth der Dinge\n besteht bloss in ihrer Beziehung auf unsere Bedürfnisse. Was für\n den einen mehr, ist für den andern weniger, und umgekehrt. … Man\n setzt nicht voraus, dass wir unsrer Consumtion unentbehrliche Dinge zum\n Verkauf ausbieten. . . . Wir wollen eine uns nutzlose Sache weggeben, um\n eine uns nothwendige zu erhalten; wir wollen weniger für mehr geben …\n Es war natürlich zu urtheilen, dass man im Austausch gleichen Werth für\n gleichen Werth gebe, so oft jedes der ausgetauschten Dinge an Werth\n demselben Quantum Geld gleich war … Aber eine andre Betrachtung\n muss noch in die Rechnung eingehn; es fragt sich, ob wir beide einen\nUeberfluss gegen etwas Nothwendiges austauschen [Note: Condillac: „Le Commerce et le Gouvernement“ (1776).\nÉdit. Daire et Molinari in den „Mélanges d’Économie Politique.\n Paris 1847“, p. 267.] .“ Man sieht,\n wie Condillac nicht nur Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth durcheinan-\n der wirft, sondern wahrhaft kindlich einer Gesellschaft mit entwickelter\n Waarenproduktion einen Zustand unterschiebt, worin der Producent seine\n Subsistenzmittel selbst producirt und nur den Ueberschuss über den eignen\n Bedarf, den Ueberfluss, in die Cirkulation wirft [Note: Le Trosne antwortet daher seinem Freunde Condillac sehr richtig:\n „Dans la société formée il n’y a pas de surabondant en aucun genre.“ Zugleich\n neckt er ihn mit der Glosse, dass „wenn beide Austauscher gleich viel mehr für\n gleich viel weniger erhalten, sie beide gleich viel erhalten.“ Weil Condillac\n noch nicht die geringste Ahnung von der Natur des Tauschwerths besitzt, ist\n er der passende Gewährsmann des Herrn Prof. Wilhelm Roscher für seine\n eignen Kinderbegriffe. Sieh dessen: „Die Grundlagen der National-\n ökonomie. Dritte Auflage. 1858“.] . Dennoch wird\nCondillac’s Argument häufig bei modernen Oekonomen wiederholt, na-\n mentlich wenn es gilt, die entwickelte Gestalt des Waarenaustausches, den\nHandel, als produktiv von Mehrwerth darzustellen. „Der Handel“,\n heisst es z. B., „fügt den Produkten Werth zu, denn dieselben\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"122\", \"facs\": \"#f0141\"} --> Produkte haben mehr Werth in den Händen des Consumenten als in den\n Händen des Producenten und er muss daher wörtlich (strictly) als Pro-\n duktionsakt betrachtet werden [Note: S. P. Newman: Elements of Polit. Econ. Andover and\n New - York 1835, p. 85.] .“ Aber man zahlt die Waaren nicht\n doppelt, das einemal ihren Gebrauchswerth und das andremal ihren Tausch\n werth. Und wenn der Gebrauchswerth der Waare dem Käufer nützlicher\n als dem Verkäufer, ist ihre Geldform dem Verkäufer nützlicher als dem\n Käufer. Würde er sie sonst verkaufen? Und so könnte ebensowohl ge-\n sagt werden, dass der Käufer wörtlich (strictly) einen „Produktionsakt“\n vollbringt, indem er z. B. die Strümpfe des Kaufmanns in Geld ver-\n wandelt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0034","text":"Werden Waaren oder Waaren und Geld von gleichem Tauschwerth,\n also Aequivalente ausgetauscht, so zieht offenbar keiner mehr Werth\n aus der Cirkulation heraus als er in sie hineinwirft. Es findet dann keine\n Bildung von Mehrwerth Statt. In seiner reinen Form aber bedingt der\n Cirkulationsprozess der Waaren Austausch von Aequivalenten. Jedoch\n gehn die Dinge in der Wirklichkeit nicht rein zu. Unterstellen wir daher\nAustausch von Nicht-Aequivalenten.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0035","text":"Jedenfalls steht auf dem Waarenmarkt nur Waarenbesitzer dem Waa-\n renbesitzer gegenüber, und die Macht, die diese Personen über einander\n ausüben, ist nur die Macht ihrer Waaren. Die stoffliche Verschiedenheit\n der Waaren ist das stoffliche Motiv des Austauschs und macht die Waaren-\n besitzer wechselseitig von einander abhängig, indem keiner von ihnen den\n Gegenstand seines eignen Bedürfnisses und jeder von ihnen den Gegenstand\n des Bedürfnisses der Andern in seiner Hand hält. Ausser dieser stoff-\n lichen Verschiedenheit ihrer Gebrauchswerthe besteht nur noch ein Unter-\n schied unter den Waaren, der Unterschied zwischen ihrer Naturalform und\n ihrer verwandelten Form, zwischen Waare und Geld. Und so unter-\n scheiden sich die Waarenbesitzer nur als Verkäufer, Besitzer von Waare,\n und als Käufer, Besitzer von Geld.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0036","text":"Gesetzt nun, es sei durch irgend ein unerklärliches Privilegium dem Ver-\n käufer gegeben, die Waare über ihrem Werthe zu verkaufen, zu 110,\n wenn sie 100 werth ist, also mit einem nominellen Preisaufschlage von\n 10 %. Der Verkäufer kassirt also einen Mehrwerth von 10 ein. Aber\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"123\", \"facs\": \"#f0142\"} --> nachdem er Verkäufer war, wird er Käufer. Ein dritter Waarenbesitzer\n begegnet ihm jetzt als Verkäufer und geniesst seinerseits das Privilegium,\n die Waare 10 % zu theuer zu verkaufen. Unser Mann hat als Verkäufer\n 10 gewonnen, um als Käufer 10 zu verlieren [Note: „By the augmentation of the nominal value of the produce . . . . sellers\n not enriched … since what they gain as sellers, they precisely expend in the\n quality of buyers.“ („The Essential Principles of the Wealth of\n Nation etc. London 1797“, p. 66.)] . Das ganze kömmt in\n der That darauf hinaus, dass alle Waarenbesitzer ihre Waaren einander\n 10 % über dem Werth verkaufen, was ganz dasselbe ist, als ob sie die\n Waaren zu ihren Werthen verkauften. Ein solcher allgemeiner nomineller\nPreisaufschlag der Waaren bringt dieselbe Wirkung hervor, als ob\n die Waarenwerthe z. B. in Silber statt in Gold geschätzt würden. Die\n Geldnamen, d. h. die Preise der Waaren würden anschwellen, aber ihre\n Werthverhältnisse unverändert bleiben.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0037","text":"Unterstellen wir umgekehrt, es sei das Privilegium des Käufers,\n die Waaren unter ihrem Werth zu kaufen. Hier ist es nicht einmal\n nöthig zu erinnern, dass der Käufer wieder Verkäufer wird. Er war\n Verkäufer, bevor er Käufer ward. Er hat bereits 10 % als Verkäufer\n verloren, bevor er 10 % als Käufer gewinnt [Note: „Si l’on est forcé de donner pour 18 livres une quantité de telle produc-\n tion qui en valait 24, lorsqu’on exployera ce même argent à acheter, on aura\n également pour 18 l. ce que l’on payait 24“. (Le Trosne l. c. p. 897.)] . Alles bleibt wieder beim\n Alten.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0038","text":"Die Bildung von Mehrwerth, und daher die Verwandlung von Geld\n in Kapital, kann also weder dadurch erklärt werden, dass die Verkäufer\n die Waaren über ihrem Werthe verkaufen, noch dadurch, dass die Käufer\n sie unter ihrem Werthe kaufen [Note: „Chaque vendeur ne peut donc parvenir à renchérir habituellement ses\n marchandises, qu’en se soumettant aussi à payer habituellement plus cher les mar-\n chandises des autres vendeurs; et par la même raison, chaque consommateur ne\n peut payer habituellement moins cher ce qu’il achète, qu’en se soumettant aussi à\n une diminution semblable sur le prix des choses qu’il vend.“ (Mercier de la\n Rivière l. c. p. 555.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0039","text":"Das Problem wird in keiner Weise dadurch vereinfacht, dass man\n fremde Beziehungen einschmuggelt, also etwa mit Oberst Torrens sagt:\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"124\", \"facs\": \"#f0143\"} --> „Die effektive Nachfrage besteht in dem Vermögen und der Neigung (!)\n der Konsumenten, sei es durch unmittelbaren oder vermittelten Aus-\n tausch, für Waaren eine gewisse grössere Portion von allen Ingredienzien\n des Kapitals zu geben, als ihre Produktion kostet [Note: R. Torrens: „An Essay on the Production of Wealth.“\n London 1821, p. 349.] .“ In der Cirkula-\n tion stehn sich Produzenten und Konsumenten nur als Verkäufer und Käu-\n fer gegenüber. Behaupten, der Mehrwerth für den Produzenten entspringe\n daraus, dass die Konsumenten die Waare über dem Werth zahlen, heisst\n nur den einfachen Satz maskiren: Der Waarenbesitzer besitzt als Ver-\n käufer das Privilegium zu theuer zu verkaufen. Der Verkäufer hat die\n Waare selbst producirt oder vertritt ihren Produzenten, aber der Käufer\n hat nicht minder die in seinem Gelde dargestellte Waare selbst produzirt\n oder vertritt ihren Produzenten. Es steht also Produzent dem Produzen-\n ten gegenüber. Was sie unterscheidet, ist dass der eine kauft und der\n andre verkauft. Es bringt uns keinen Schritt weiter, dass der Waaren-\n besitzer unter dem Namen Produzent die Waare über ihrem Werthe\n verkauft und unter dem Namen Consument sie zu theuer zahlt [Note: „The idea of profits being paid by the consumers, is, assuredly, very\n absurd. Who are the consumers?“ (G. Ramsay: An Essay on the Dis-\n tribution of Wealth. Edinburgh 1836, p. 184.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0040","text":"Die consequenten Vertreter der Illusion, dass der Mehrwerth aus\n einem nominellen Preiszuschlag entspringt, oder aus dem Privilegium des\nVerkäufers die Waare zu theuer zu verkaufen, unterstellen daher eine\nKlasse, die nur kauft ohne zu verkaufen, also auch nur con-\n sumirt ohne zu produziren. Die Existenz einer solchen Klasse\n ist von unsrem bisher erreichten Standpunkt, dem der einfachen Cirkula-\n tion nach, unerklärlich. Aber greifen wir vor. Das Geld, womit eine\n solche Klasse beständig kauft, muss ihr beständig, ohne Austausch,\n umsonst, auf beliebige Rechts- und Gewaltstitel hin, von den Waaren-\n besitzern selbst zufliessen. Dieser Klasse die Waaren über dem Werth\n verkaufen, heisst nur, umsonst weggegebenes Geld sich zum Theil wieder\n zurückschwindeln [Note: „When a man is in want of demand, does Mr. Malthus recommend him\n to pay some other person to take off his goods?“, fragt ein entrüsteter Ricardianer] . So zahlten die kleinasiatischen Städte jährlichen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"125\", \"facs\": \"#f0144\"} --> Geldtribut an das alte Rom. Mit diesem Geld kaufte Rom Waaren von\n ihnen und kaufte sie zu theuer. Die Kleinasiaten prellten die Römer, in-\n dem sie den Eroberern einen Theil des Tributs wieder abluchsten auf dem\n Wege des Handels. Aber dennoch blieben die Kleinasiaten die Geprell-\n ten. Ihre Waaren wurden ihnen nach wie vor mit ihrem eignen Gelde ge-\n zahlt. Es ist diess keine Methode der Bereicherung oder der Bildung von\n Mehrwerth.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0041","text":"Halten wir uns also innerhalb der Schranken des Waarenaustauschs,\n wo Verkäufer Käufer und Käufer Verkäufer sind. Unsere Verlegenheit\n stammt vielleicht daher, dass wir die Personen nur als personificirte\n Categorien, nicht individuell, gefasst haben.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0042","text":"Waarenbesitzer A mag so pfiffig sein seine Collegen B oder C über’s\n Ohr zu hauen, während sie trotz des besten Willens die Revanche schuldig\n bleiben. A verkauft Wein zum Werth von 40 Pfd. St. an B und erwirbt\n im Austausch Getreide zum Werth von 50 Pfd. St. A hat seine 40 Pfd.\n St. in 50 Pfd. St. verwandelt, mehr Geld aus weniger Geld gemacht und\n seine Waare in Kapital verwandelt. Sehn wir näher zu. Vor dem Aus-\n tausch hatten wir für 40 Pfd. St. Wein in der Hand von A und für 50 Pfd.\n St. Getreide in der Hand von B, Gesammtwerth von 90 Pfd. St.\n Nach dem Austausch haben wir denselben Gesammtwerth von 90 Pfd. St.\n Der circulirende Werth hat sich um kein Atom vergrössert, seine Ver-\n theilung zwischen A und B hat sich verändert. Auf der einen Seite er-\n scheint als Mehrwerth, was auf der anderen Minderwerth ist, auf\n der einen Seite als Plus, was auf der andern als Minus. Derselbe Wech-\n sel hätte sich ereignet, wenn A, ohne die verhüllende Form des Austauschs,\n dem B 10 Pfd. St. direkt gestohlen hätte. Die Summe der circulirenden\n Werthe kann offenbar durch keinen Wechsel in ihrer Vertheilung vermehrt\n werden, so wenig wie ein Jude die Masse der edlen Metalle in einem Lande\n dadurch vermehrt, dass er einen Farthing aus der Zeit der Königin Anna\n [Note: den Malthus, der wie sein Schüler, der Pfaffe Chalmers, die Klasse von\n blossen Käufern oder Consumenten ökonomisch verherrlicht. Sieh: „An In-\n quiry into those principles respecting the Nature of Demand and\n the Necessity of Consumption, lately advocated by Mr. Malthus“\n etc. London 1821, p. 55.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"126\", \"facs\": \"#f0145\"} --> für eine Guinea verkauft. Die Gesammtheit der Kapitalistenklasse eines\n Landes kann sich nicht selbst übervortheilen [Note: Destutt de Tracy, obgleich, vielleicht weil Membre de l’Institut, war\n umgekehrter Ansicht. Die industriellen Kapitalisten, sagt er, machen dadurch\n ihre Profite, dass „sie alles theurer verkaufen als es gekostet hat zu produciren.\n Und an wen verkaufen sie? Erstens an einander.“ (l. c. p. 239.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0043","text":"Man mag sich also drehen und wenden wie man will, das Facit bleibt\n dasselbe. Werden Aequivalente ausgetauscht, so entsteht kein Mehrwerth,\n und werden Nicht-Aequivalente ausgetauscht, so entsteht auch kein Mehr-\n werth [Note: „L’échange qui se fait de deux valeurs égales n’augmente ni ne diminue\n la masse des valeurs subsistantes dans la société. L’échange de deux valeurs in-\n égales … ne change rien non plus à la somme des valeurs sociales, bien qu’il\n ajoute à la fortune de l’un ce qu’il ôte de la fortune de l’autre.“ (J. B. Say\n l. c. t. I, p. 434, 435.) Say, natürlich unbekümmert um die Consequenzen\n dieses Satzes, entlehnt ihn ziemlich wörtlich den Physiokraten. Die Art, wie er\n ihre zu seiner Zeit verschollenen Schriften zur Vermehrung seines eigenen\n „Werthes“ ausgebeutet hat, zeige folgendes Beispiel. Der „berühmteste“ Satz\n des Monsieur Say: „On n’achète des produits qu’avec des pro-\n duits“ (l. c. t. II, p. 438) lautet im physiokratischen Original: „Les pro-\n ductions ne se paient qu’avec des productions.“ (Le Trosne\n l. c. p. 899.)] . Die Circulation oder der Waarenaustausch schafft keinen\n Werth [Note: „Exchange confers no value at all upon products.“ (F. Wayland:\n The Elements of Pol. Econ. Boston 1853, p. 168.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0044","text":"Man versteht daher, warum in unserer Analyse der Grundform\n des Kapitals, der Form, worin es die ökonomische Organisation der\n modernen Gesellschaft bestimmt, seine populärsten und so zu sagen ante-\n diluvianischen Gestalten, Handelskapital und Wucherkapital,\n zunächst gänzlich unberücksichtigt bleiben.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0045","text":"Im eigentlichen Handelskapital erscheint die Form G — W — G',\n kaufen um theuer zu verkaufen, am reinsten. Andrerseits geht seine ganze\n Bewegung innerhalb der Circulationssphäre vor. Da es aber\n unmöglich ist aus der Circulation selbst die Verwandlung von Geld in Kapi-\n tal, die Bildung von Mehrwerth zu erklären, erscheint das Handelskapital\n unmöglich, sobald Aequivalente ausgetauscht werden [Note: „Under the rule of invariable equivalents commerce would be im-\n possible.“ (G. Opdyke: A Treatise on Polit. Economy. New-] , daher nur\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"127\", \"facs\": \"#f0146\"} --> ableitbar aus der doppelseitigen Uebervortheilung der kaufenden\n und verkaufenden Waarenproducenten durch den sich parasitisch zwischen\n sie schiebenden Kaufmann. In diesem Sinn sagt Franklin: „Krieg\n ist Raub, Handel ist Prellerei [Note: Benjamin Franklin: Works, vol. II, edit. Sparks in:\n „Positions to be examined concerning National Wealth.“] :“ Soll die Verwer-\n thung des Handelskapitals nicht aus blosser Prellerei der Waaren-\n producenten erklärt werden, so gehört dazu eine lange Reihe von Mittel-\n gliedern, die hier, wo die Waarencirculation und ihre einfachen Momente\n unsere einzige Voraussetzung bilden, noch gänzlich fehlt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0046","text":"Was vom Handelskapital, gilt noch mehr vom Wucher-\n kapital. Im Handelskapital sind die Extreme, das Geld, das auf den\n Markt geworfen und das vermehrte Geld, das dem Markt entzogen wird,\n wenigstens vermittelt durch Kauf und Verkauf, durch die Bewegung\n der Circulation. Im Wucherkapital ist die Form G — W — G'\n abgekürzt auf die unvermittelten Extreme G — G', Geld, das sich gegen\n mehr Geld austauscht, eine der Natur des Geldes widersprechende und\n daher vom Standpunkt des Waarenaustauschs unerklärliche\n Form. Daher Aristoteles: „da die Chrematistik eine doppelte ist, die\n eine zum Handel, die andre zur Oekonomik gehörig, die letztere nothwendig\n und lobenswerth, die erstere auf die Circulation gegründet und mit Recht ge-\n tadelt, (denn sie beruht nicht auf der Natur, sondern auf wechselseitiger Prel-\n lerei), so ist der Wucher mit vollstem Rechte verhasst, weil das Geld selbst\n hier die Quelle des Erwerbs und nicht dazu gebraucht wird, wozu es erfunden\n ward. Denn für den Waarenaustausch entstand es, der Zins aber\n macht aus Geld mehr Geld. Daher auch sein Name (τόϰος Zins und\nGeborenes). Denn die Geborenen sind den Erzeugern ähnlich. Der\n Zins aber ist Geld von Geld, so dass von allen Erwerbszweigen dieser der\n naturwidrigste [Note: Arist. l. c. c. 10.] .“","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0047","text":"Wie das Handelskapital werden wir das zinstragende\n Kapital im Verlauf unserer Untersuchung als abgeleitete Formen\n [Note: York 1851, p. 69.) „Dem Unterschiede zwischen Realwerth und Tauschwerth\n liegt eine Thatsache zum Grunde — nämlich dass der Werth einer Sache verschie-\n den ist von dem im Handel für sie gegebenen sogenannten Aequivalent, d. h.\n dass diess Aequivalent kein Aequivalent ist.“ (F. Engels l. c. p. 96.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"128\", \"facs\": \"#f0147\"} --> vorfinden und zugleich sehn, warum sie historisch vor dem Kapital\n in seiner modernen Grundform erscheinen.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0048","text":"Es hat sich gezeigt, dass der Mehrwerth nicht aus der Circulation\n entspringen kann, bei seiner Bildung also etwas hinter ihrem Rücken\n vorgehn muss, das in ihr selbst unsichtbar ist [Note: „Profit, in the usual condition of the market, is not made by exchang-\n ing. Had it not existed before, neither could it after that transaction.“\n (Ramsay l. c. p. 184.)] . Kann aber der\n Mehrwerth anders woher entspringen als aus der Circulation? Die\n Circulation ist die Summe aller Wechselbeziehungen der Waarenbesitzer.\n Ausserhalb derselben steht der Waarenbesitzer nur noch in Beziehung zu\n seiner eignen Waare. Was ihren Werth [Note: Ich bemerke hier, dass wenn ich das Wort Werth in dieser Schrift ohne\n nähere Bezeichnung brauche, stets Tauschwerth zu verstehn ist.]  angeht, beschränkt sich das\n Verhältniss darauf, dass sie ein nach bestimmten gesellschaftlichen Gesetzen\n gemessenes Quantum seiner eignen Arbeit enthält. Diess Quantum Arbeit\n drückt sich aus in der Werthgrösse seiner Waare und da sich Werth-\n grösse in Rechengeld darstellt, in einem Preise von z. B. 10 Pfd. St.\n Aber seine Arbeit stellt sich nicht dar im Werthe der Waare und einem\n Ueberschuss über ihren eignen Werth, nicht in einem Preise von 10, der\n zugleich ein Preis von 11 ist, nicht in einem Werth, der grösser als\n er selbst ist. Der Waarenbesitzer kann durch seine Arbeit Werthe\n bilden, aber keine sich verwerthenden Werthe. Er kann den\n Werth einer Waare erhöhen, indem er vorhandnem Werth neuen Werth\n durch neue Arbeit zusetzt, z. B. aus Leder Stiefel macht. Derselbe\n Stoff hat jetzt mehr Werth, weil er ein grösseres Arbeitsquantum enthält.\n Der Stiefel hat daher mehr Werth als das Leder, aber der Werth des Leders\n ist geblieben, was er war. Er hat sich nicht verwerthet, nicht während\n der Stiefelfabrikation seinen ursprünglichen Werth um einen Mehrwerth be-\n reichert. Es ist also unmöglich, dass der Waarenproducent ausserhalb\n der Circulationssphäre, ohne mit andern Waarenbesitzern in Berührung\n zu treten, Werth verwerthe und daher Geld oder Waare in Kapital\n verwandle.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0049","text":"Kapital kann also nicht aus der Circulation entspringen und es kann\n eben so wenig aus der Circulation nicht entspringen. Es muss zugleich\n in ihr und nicht in ihr entspringen.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0050","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"129\", \"facs\": \"#f0148\"} -->","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0051","text":"Ein doppeltes Resultat hat sich also ergeben.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0052","text":"Die Verwandlung des Geldes in Kapital ist auf Grundlage dem Waaren-\n austausch immanenter Gesetze zu entwickeln, so dass der Austausch\n von Aequivalenten als Ausgangspunkt gilt [Note: Nach der gegebnen Auseinandersetzung versteht der Leser, dass diess nur\n heisst: Die Kapitalbildung muss möglich sein, auch wenn der Waarenpreis\ngleich dem Waarenwerth. Sie kann nicht aus der Abweichung der Waaren-\n preise von den Waarenwerthen erklärt werden. Weichen die Preise von den\n Werthen wirklich ab, so muss man sie erst auf die letztern reduciren, d. h. von\n diesem Umstand als einem zufälligen absehn, um das Phänomen der Kapitalbildung\n auf Grundlage des Waarenaustausches rein vor sich zu haben und in seiner Beob-\n achtung nicht durch störende und dem eigentlichen Verlauf fremde Nebenum-\n stände verwirrt zu werden. Man weiss übrigens, dass diese Reduktion keineswegs\n eine blos wissenschaftliche Procedur ist. Die beständigen Oscillationen der Markt-\n preise, ihr Steigen und Sinken, compensiren sich, heben sich wechselseitig auf und\n reduciren sich selbst zum Durchschnittspreis als ihrer inneren Regel. Diese\n bildet den Leitstern z. B. des Kaufmanns oder des Industriellen in jeder Unter-\n nehmung, die längeren Zeitraum umfasst. Er weiss also, dass eine längere Pe-\n riode im Ganzen betrachtet, die Waaren wirklich weder unter, noch über, son-\n dern zu ihrem Durchschnittspreis verkauft werden. Wäre interesseloses Denken\n also überhaupt sein Interesse, so müsste er sich das Problem der Kapitalbildung so\n stellen: Wie kann Kapital entstehn bei der Reglung der Preise durch den Durch-\n schnittspreis, d. h. in letzter Instanz durch den Werth der Waare? Ich sage\n „in letzter Instanz,“ weil die Durchschnittspreise nicht direkt mit den Werth-\n grössen der Waaren, wie A. Smith, Ricardo u. s. w. glauben, zusammenfallen.] . Unser nur noch als\n Kapitalistenraupe vorhandner Geldbesitzer muss die Waaren zu ihrem\n Werth kaufen, zu ihrem Werth verkaufen, und dennoch am Ende des Pro-\n zesses mehr Werth herausziehn als er hineinwarf. Seine Schmetterlings-\n entfaltung muss in der Cirkulationssphäre und muss nicht in der Cirkula-\n tionssphäre vorgehn. Diess sind die Bedingungen des Problems. Hie\n Rhodus, hic salta!","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0053","text":"## 3) Kauf und Verkauf der Arbeitskraft.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0054","text":"Die Werthveränderung des Geldes, das sich in Kapital ver-\n wandeln soll, kann nicht an diesem Geld selbst vorgehn, denn als Kauf-\n mittel und als Zahlungsmittel realisirt es nur den Preis der Waare, die\n es kauft oder zahlt, während es, in seiner eignen Form verharrend, zum\n Petrefakt von gleichbleibender Werthgrösse erstarrt [Note: „In the form of money . . . . capital is productive of no profit.“\n (Ricardo: Princ. of Pol. Econ. p. 267.)] . Eben so wenig\n [Running matter: I. 9] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"130\", \"facs\": \"#f0149\"} --> kann die Veränderung aus dem zweiten Akt, dem Wiederverkauf der\n Waare, entspringen, denn dieser Akt verwandelt die Waare blos aus der\n Naturalform zurück in die Geldform. Die Veränderung muss sich also\n zutragen mit der Waare, die im ersten Akt G — W gekauft wird,\n aber nicht mit ihrem Tauschwerth, denn es werden Aequivalente aus-\n getauscht, die Waare wird zu ihrem Werthe bezahlt. Die Veränderung\n kann also erst entspringen aus ihrem Gebrauchswerth als solchem,\n d. h. aus ihrem Verbrauch. Um aus dem Verbrauch einer Waare\n Tauschwerth herauszuziehn, müsste unser Geldbesitzer so glücklich sein\ninnerhalb der Cirkulationssphäre, auf dem Markt, eine Waare\n zu entdecken, deren Gebrauchswerth selbst die eigenthümliche Be-\n schaffenheit besässe, Quelle von Tauschwerth zu sein, deren wirk-\n licher Verbrauch also selbst Vergegenständlichung von Arbeit\n wäre, daher Werthschöpfung. Und der Geldbesitzer findet auf dem\n Markt eine solche spezifische Waare vor — das Arbeitsvermögen\n oder die Arbeitskraft.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0055","text":"Unter Arbeitskraft oder Arbeitsvermögen verstehn wir den\n Inbegriff der physischen und geistigen Fähigkeiten, die in der Leiblichkeit,\n der lebendigen Persönlichkeit eines Menschen existiren und die er in Be-\n wegung setzt, so oft er Gebrauchswerthe irgend einer Art producirt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0056","text":"Damit jedoch der Geldbesitzer die Arbeitskraft als Waare auf dem\n Markt vorfinde, müssen verschiedne Bedingungen erfüllt sein. Der Waaren-\n austausch schliesst an und für sich keine andern Abhängigkeitsver-\n hältnisse ein als die aus seiner eignen Natur entspringenden. Unter\n dieser Voraussetzung kann die Arbeitskraft als Waare nur auf dem\n Markt erscheinen, sofern und weil sie von ihrem eignen Besitzer,\n der Person, deren Arbeitskraft sie ist, als Waare feilgeboten oder ver-\n kauft wird. Damit ihr Besitzer sie als Waare verkaufe, muss er über sie\n verfügen können, also freier Eigenthümer seines Arbeitsvermögens,\n seiner Person sein [Note: In Realencyklopädien des klassischen Alterthums kann man den Unsinn\n lesen, dass in der antiken Welt das Kapital völlig entwickelt war „ausser dass\n der freie Arbeiter und das Creditwesen fehlten.“ Auch Herr Mommsen in seiner\n „Römischen Geschichte“ begeht ein quid pro quo über das andere.] . Er und der Geldbesitzer begegnen sich auf dem\n Markt und treten in Verhältniss zu einander als ebenbürtige Waaren-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"131\", \"facs\": \"#f0150\"} --> besitzer, nur dadurch unterschieden, dass der eine Käufer, der andere\n Verkäufer, beide also juristisch gleiche Personen sind. Die\n Fortdauer dieses Verhältnisses erheischt, dass der Eigenthümer der Arbeits-\n kraft sie stets nur für bestimmte Zeit verkaufe, denn verkauft er sie\n in Bausch und Bogen, ein für allemal, so verkauft er sich selbst, verwan-\n delt sich aus einem Freien in einen Sklaven, aus einem Waarenbesitzer\n in eine Waare. Er als Person muss sich beständig zu seiner Arbeits-\n kraft als seinem Eigenthum und daher seiner eignen Waare verhalten und\n das kann er nur, so weit er sie dem Käufer stets nur vorübergehend, für\n einen bestimmten Zeittermin, zur Verfügung stellt, zum Verbrauch über-\n lässt, also durch ihre Veräusserung nicht auf sein Eigenthum an ihr\n verzichtet [Note: Verschiedne Gesetzgebungen setzen daher ein Maximum für den Arbeits-\n kontrakt fest. Alle Gesetzbücher bei Völkern freier Arbeit regeln Kündigungs-\n bedingungen des Kontrakts. Bei verschiednen Völkern, namentlich in Mexiko\n (vor dem amerikanischen Bürgerkrieg auch in den von Mexiko losgerissenen Terri-\n torien, und der Sache nach bis zu Kusa’s Umwälzung in den Donauprovinzen) ist\n die Sklaverei unter der Form von Peonage versteckt. Durch Vorschüsse, die\n in Arbeit abzutragen, und sich von Generation zu Generation fortwälzen, wird nicht\n nur der einzelne Arbeiter, sondern seine Familie, thatsächlich das Eigenthum\n andrer Personen und ihrer Familien. Juarez hatte die Peonage abgeschafft.\n Der sogenannte Kaiser Maximilian führte sie wieder ein durch ein\n Dekret, das im Repräsentantenhaus zu Washington treffend als Dekret zur\n Wiedereinführung der Sklaverei in Mexiko denuncirt ward. „Von\n meinen besonderen körperlichen und geistigen Geschicklichkeiten und Möglich-\n keiten der Thätigkeit kann ich . . . . einen in der Zeit beschränkten Ge-\n brauch an einen Andern veräussern, weil sie nach dieser Beschränkung ein\n äusserliches Verhältniss zu meiner Totalität und Allgemeinheit erhalten.\n Durch die Veräusserung meiner ganzen durch die Arbeit konkreten\n Zeit und der Totalität meiner Produktion würde ich das Substantielle derselben,\n meine allgemeine Thätigkeit und Wirklichkeit, meine Persönlichkeit\n zum Eigenthum eines Andern machen.“ Hegel: „Philosophie\n des Rechts. Berlin 1840“, p. 104, § 67.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0057","text":"Die zweite wesentliche Bedingung, damit der Geldbesitzer die Ar-\n beitskraft auf dem Markt als Waare vorfinde, ist die, dass ihr Be-\n sitzer, statt Waaren verkaufen zu können, worin sich seine Arbeit ver-\n gegenständlicht hat, vielmehr seine Arbeitskraft selbst, die nur in\n seiner lebendigen Leiblichkeit existirt, als Waare feilbieten muss.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0058","text":" [Running matter: 9*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"132\", \"facs\": \"#f0151\"} -->","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0059","text":"Damit Jemand von seiner Arbeitskraft unterschiedne Waaren\n verkaufe, muss er natürlich Produktionsmittel besitzen, z. B. Roh-\n stoffe, Arbeitsinstrumente u. s. w. Er kann keine Stiefel machen ohne\n Leder. Er bedarf ausserdem Lebensmittel. Niemand kann von\n Produkten der Zukunft zehren, also auch nicht von Gebrauchswerthen,\n mit deren Produktion er noch nicht fertig, und wie am ersten Tag seiner\n Erscheinung auf der Weltbühne muss der Mensch noch jeden Tag konsu-\n miren, bevor und während er produzirt. Werden die Produkte als Waa-\n ren produzirt, so müssen sie verkauft werden, nachdem sie produ-\n zirt sind und können die Bedürfnisse des Produzenten erst nach dem Ver-\n kauf befriedigen. Zur Produktionszeit kömmt die für den Verkauf nöthige\n Zeit hinzu.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0060","text":"Zur Verwandlung von Geld in Kapital muss der Geldbesitzer also\nden freien Arbeiter auf dem Waarenmarkt vorfinden, frei in\n dem Doppelsinn, dass er als freie Person über seine Arbeitskraft als seine\n Waare verfügt, dass er andrerseits andre Waaren nicht zu verkaufen hat,\n los und ledig, frei ist von allen zur Verwirklichung seiner Arbeitskraft\n nöthigen Sachen.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0061","text":"Die Frage, warum dieser freie Arbeiter ihm in der Cirkulations-\n sphäre gegenübertritt, interessirt den Geldbesitzer nicht, der den Arbeits-\n markt als eine besondre Abtheilung des Waarenmarkts vorfindet. Und\n einstweilen interessirt sie uns eben so wenig. Wir halten uns theoretisch\n an die Thatsache, wie der Geldbesitzer praktisch. Eins jedoch ist klar.\n Die Natur produzirt nicht auf der einen Seite Geld- oder Waarenbesitzer\n und auf der andern blosse Besitzer der eignen Arbeitskräfte. Diess Ver-\n hältniss ist kein naturgeschichtliches und eben so wenig ein ge-\n sellschaftliches, das allen Geschichtsperioden gemein wäre. Es\n ist offenbar selbst das Resultat einer vorhergegangnen historischen Ent-\n wicklung, das Produkt vieler ökonomischer Umwälzungen, des Untergangs\n einer ganzen Reihe älterer Formationen der gesellschaftlichen Pro-\n duktion.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0062","text":"Auch die ökonomischen Kategorieen, die wir früher betrachtet, tra-\n gen ihre geschichtliche Spur. Im Dasein des Produkts als Waare sind\n bestimmte historische Bedingungen eingehüllt. Um Waare zu werden,\n darf das Produkt nicht als unmittelbares Subsistenzmittel\n für den Produzenten selbst produzirt werden. Hätten wir weiter geforscht:\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"133\", \"facs\": \"#f0152\"} --> Unter welchen Umständen nehmen alle oder nimmt auch nur die Mehr-\n zahl der Produkte die Form der Waare an, so hätte sich gefunden, dass\n diess nur auf Grundlage einer ganz spezifischen, der kapitalistischen\n Produktionsweise, geschieht. Eine solche Untersuchung lag jedoch\n der Analyse der Waare fern. Waarenproduktion und Waarencirkulation\n können stattfinden, obgleich die weit überwiegende Produktenmasse, un-\n mittelbar auf den Selbstbedarf gerichtet, sich nicht in Waare verwan-\n delt, der gesellschaftliche Produktionsprozess also noch lange nicht in sei-\n ner ganzen Breite und Tiefe vom Tauschwerth beherrscht ist. Die Dar-\n stellung des Produkts als Waare bedingt eine so weit entwickelte\nTheilung der Arbeit innerhalb der Gesellschaft, dass die\n Scheidung zwischen Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth, die im unmittel-\n baren Tauschhandel erst beginnt, bereits vollzogen ist. Eine\n solche Entwicklungsstufe ist aber den geschichtlich verschiedensten ökono-\n mischen Gesellschaftsformationen gemein.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0063","text":"Oder betrachten wir das Geld, so setzt es eine gewisse Höhe der Waa-\n rencirkulation voraus. Die besondern Geldformen, blosses Waaren-\n äquivalent, oder Cirkulationsmittel, oder Zahlungsmittel, Schatz und Weltgeld,\n deuten, je nach dem verschiednen Umfang und dem relativen Vorwiegen einer\n oder der andern Funktion, auf sehr verschiedne Stufen des gesellschaftlichen\n Produktionsprozesses. Dennoch genügt erfahrungsmässig eine relativ\n schwach entwickelte Waarencirkulation zur Bildung aller dieser Formen.\n Anders mit dem Kapital. Seine historischen Existenzbedingungen\n sind durchaus nicht da mit der Waaren- und Geldcirkulation. Es entsteht\n nur, wo der Besitzer von Produktions- und Lebensmitteln den freien\n Arbeiter als Verkäufer seiner Arbeitskraft auf dem Markt vorfindet,\n und diese eine historische Bedingung umschliesst eine Welt-\n geschichte. Das Kapital kündigt sich daher von vorn herein als eine\nEpoche des gesellschaftlichen Produktionsprozesses an.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0064","text":"Diese eigenthümliche Waare, die Arbeitskraft, ist nun näher\n zu betrachten. Gleich allen andern Waaren besitzt sie einen Tausch-\n werth [Note: „The Value or Worth of a man, is as of all other things, his\n price: that is to say, so much as would be given for the use of his power.“\nTh. Hobbes: „Leviathan“ in Worksedit. Molesworth. London\n 1839—44, v. III, p. 76.] . Wie wird er bestimmt?","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0065","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"134\", \"facs\": \"#f0153\"} -->","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0066","text":"Der Werth der Arbeitskraft, gleich dem jeder andern Waare, ist be-\n stimmt durch die zur Produktion, also auch Reproduktion, dieses spezifi-\n schen Artikels nothwendige Arbeitszeit. Soweit sie Tausch-\n werth, repräsentirt die Arbeitskraft selbst nur ein bestimmtes Quantum in\n ihr vergegenständlichter gesellschaftlicher Durchschnittsarbeit.\n Die Arbeitskraft existirt nur als Anlage des lebendigen Individuums. Ihre\n Produktion setzt also seine Existenz voraus. Diese gegeben, besteht ihre\n Produktion in seiner Reproduktion oder Erhaltung. Zu seiner Erhaltung\n bedarf das lebendige Individuum einer gewissen Summe von Lebensmitteln.\n Die zur Produktion der Arbeitskraft nothwendige Arbeitszeit löst sich also\n auf in die zur Produktion dieser Lebensmittel nothwendige Arbeitszeit,\n oder der Werth der Arbeitskraft ist der Werth der zur Erhal-\n tung ihres Besitzers nothwendigen Lebensmittel. Die Arbeits-\n kraft verwirklicht sich jedoch nur durch ihre Aeusserung, bethätigt sich\n nur in der Arbeit. Durch ihre Bethätigung, die Arbeit, wird aber ein\n bestimmtes Quantum von menschlichem Muskel, Nerv, Hirn u. s. w. ver-\n ausgabt, das wieder ersetzt werden muss. Diese vermehrte Ausgabe be-\n dingt eine vermehrte Einnahme [Note: Der altrömische villicus, als Wirthschafter an der Spitze der Acker-\n bausklaven, empfing daher, „weil er leichtere Arbeit hat als die Knechte, knapperes\n Mass als diese.“ (Th. Mommsen: Röm. Geschichte 1856, p. 810.)] . Wenn der Eigenthümer der Arbeits-\n kraft heute gearbeitet hat, muss er denselben Prozess morgen unter den-\n selben Bedingungen von Kraft und Gesundheit wiederholen können. Die\n Summe der Lebensmittel muss also hinreichen, das arbeitende Individuum\n als arbeitendes Individuum in seinem normalen Lebenszustand zu erhalten.\n Die natürlichen Bedürfnisse selbst, wie Nahrung, Kleidung, Heizung,\n Wohnung u. s. w. sind verschieden je nach den klimatischen und andern\n natürlichen Eigenthümlichkeiten eines Landes. Andrerseits ist der Um-\n fang s. g. nothwendiger Lebensmittel, wie die Art ihrer Be-\n friedigung, selbst ein historisches Produkt und hängt daher gros-\n sentheils von der Kulturstufe eines Landes, unter anderm auch wesentlich\n davon ab, unter welchen Bedingungen, und daher mit welchen Gewohn-\n heiten und Lebensansprüchen die Klasse der freien Arbeiter sich gebildet\n hat [Note: In seiner Schrift: „Over population and its Remedy. London\n 1846“ giebt W. Th. Thornton hierzu interessante Belege.] . Im Gegensatz zu den andern Waaren enthält also die Werth-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"135\", \"facs\": \"#f0154\"} --> bestimmung der Arbeitskraft ein historisches und moralisches Element.\n Für ein bestimmtes Land, zu einer bestimmten Periode jedoch, ist der\n Durchschnitts-Umkreis der nothwendigen Lebensmittel gegeben.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0067","text":"Der Eigenthümer der Arbeitskraft ist sterblich. Soll also seine Er-\n scheinung auf dem Markt eine kontinuirliche sein, wie die kontinuirliche\n Verwandlung von Geld in Kapital voraussetzt, so muss der Verkäufer der\n Arbeitskraft sich verewigen, „wie jedes lebendige Individuum sich ver-\n ewigt, durch Fortpflanzung [Note: Petty.] .“ Die durch Abnutzung und Tod\n dem Markt entzogenen Arbeitskräfte müssen zum allermindesten durch eine\n gleiche Zahl neuer Arbeitskräfte beständig ersetzt werden. Die Summe\n der zur Produktion der Arbeitskraft nothwendigen Lebensmittel schliesst\n also die Lebensmittel der Ersatzmänner ein, d. h. der Kinder der Arbei-\n ter, so dass sich diese Race eigenthümlicher Waarenbesitzer auf dem Waa-\n renmarkt verewigt [Note: „Its (labour’s) natural price … consists in such a quantity of necessaries,\n and comforts of life, as, from the nature of the climate, and the habits of the\n country, are necessary to support the labourer, and to enable him to rear such a\n family as may preserve, in the market, an undiminished supply of\n labours.“ R. Torrens: „An Essay on the External Corn Trade.\n London 1815“, p. 62. Das Wort Arbeit steht hier fälschlich für Arbeitskraft.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0068","text":"Um die allgemein menschliche Natur so zu modificiren, dass sie Ge-\n schick und Fertigkeit in einem bestimmten Arbeitszweig erlangt, ent-\n wickelte und spezifische Arbeitskraft wird, bedarf es einer bestimmten\n Bildung oder Erziehung, welche ihrerseits eine grössere oder geringere\n Summe von Waarenäquivalenten kostet. Je nach dem mehr oder minder\n vermittelten Charakter der Arbeitskraft, sind ihre Bildungskosten ver-\n schieden. Diese Erlernungskosten, verschwindend klein für die gewöhn-\n liche Arbeitskraft, gehn also ein in den Umkreis der zu ihrer Produktion\n nothwendigen Waaren.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0069","text":"Der Werth der Arbeitskraft löst sich auf in den Werth\n einer bestimmten Summe von Lebensmitteln. Er wech-\n selt daher auch mit dem Werth dieser Lebensmittel, d. h. der Grösse der\n zu ihrer Produktion erheischten Arbeitszeit.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0070","text":"Ein Theil der Lebensmittel, z. B. Nahrungsmittel, Heizungsmittel\n u. s. w., werden täglich neu verzehrt, und müssen täglich neu ersetzt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"136\", \"facs\": \"#f0155\"} --> werden. Andere Lebensmittel, wie Kleider, Möbel u. s. w., verbrauchen\n sich in längeren Zeiträumen, und sind daher nur in längeren Zeiträumen\n zu ersetzen. Waaren einer Art müssen täglich, andere wöchentlich, vier-\n teljährlich u. s. f. gekauft oder gezahlt werden. Wie sich die Summe\n dieser Ausgaben aber immer während eines Jahres z. B. vertheilen möge,\n sie muss gedeckt sein durch die Durchschnittseinnahme, Tag ein, Tag aus.\n Wäre die Masse der täglich zur Produktion der Arbeitskraft erheischten\n Waaren = A, die der wöchentlich erheischten = B, die der viertel-\n jährlich erheischten = C u. s. w., so wäre der tägliche Durchschnitt\n dieser Waaren \\frac{= 365 A + 52 B + 4 C +}{365} u. s. w. Gesetzt in dieser\n für den Durchschnitts-Tag nöthigen Waarenmasse steckten 6 Stunden\n gesellschaftlicher Arbeit, so vergegenständlicht sich in\n der Arbeitskraft täglich ein halber Tag gesellschaftlicher\n Durchschnittsarbeit, oder ein halber Arbeitstag ist zur täglichen\n Produktion der Arbeitskraft erheischt. Diess zu ihrer täglichen Produktion\n erheischte Arbeitsquantum bildet den Tageswerth der Arbeits-\n kraft, oder den Werth der täglich reproduzirten Arbeitskraft. Wenn\n sich ein halber Tag gesellschaftlicher Durchschnittsarbeit ebenfalls in einer\n Goldmasse von 3 sh. oder einem Thaler darstellt, so ist Ein Thaler\n der dem Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft entsprechende Preis. Bietet der\n Besitzer der Arbeitskraft sie feil für Einen Thaler täglich, so ist ihr Ver-\n kaufspreis gleich ihrem Werth, und, nach unsrer Voraussetzung,\nzahlt der auf Verwandlung seiner Thaler in Kapital erpichte Geldbesitzer\ndiesen Werth.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0071","text":"Die letzte Grenze oder Minimalgrenze des Werths der Arbeits-\n kraft wird gebildet durch den Werth einer Waarenmasse, ohne deren täg-\n liche Zufuhr der Träger der Arbeitskraft, der Mensch, seinen Lebenspro-\n zess nicht erneuern kann, also durch den Werth der physisch un-\n entbehrlichen Lebensmittel. Sinkt der Preis der Arbeitskraft\nauf diess Minimum, so sinkt er unter ihren Werth, denn sie\n kann so nur in verkümmerter Form erhalten werden und sich ent-\n wickeln. Der Tauschwerth jeder Waare ist aber bestimmt durch die\n Arbeitszeit, erfordert um sie in normaler Güte zu liefern.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0072","text":"Es ist eine ausserordentlich wohlfeile Sentimentalität, diese aus der Na-\n tur der Sache fliessende Werthbestimmung der Arbeitskraft grob\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"137\", \"facs\": \"#f0156\"} --> zu finden und etwa mit Rossi zu jammern: „Das Arbeitsvermögen (puis-\n sance de travail) begreifen, während man von den Subsistenzmitteln der\n Arbeit während des Produktionsprozesses abstrahirt, heisst ein Hirnge-\n spinnst (être de raison) begreifen. Wer Arbeit sagt, wer Arbeitsvermögen\n sagt, sagt zugleich Arbeiter und Subsistenzmittel, Arbeiter und Arbeits-\n lohn [Note: Rossi: „Cours d’Écon. Polit. Bruxelles 1842“, p. 370.] .“ Wer Arbeitsvermögen sagt, sagt nicht Arbeit, so wenig als\n wer Verdauungsvermögen sagt, Verdauen sagt. Zum letztern Prozess ist\n bekanntlich mehr als ein guter Magen erfordert. Wer Arbeitsvermögen\n sagt, abstrahirt nicht von den zu seiner Subsistenz nothwendigen Lebens-\n mitteln. Ihr Werth ist vielmehr ausgedrückt in seinem Werth. Wird es\n nicht verkauft, so nützt es dem Arbeiter nichts, so empfindet er es viel-\n mehr als eine grausame Naturnothwendigkeit, dass sein Arbeitsvermögen\n ein bestimmtes Quantum Subsistenzmittel zu seiner Produktion erheischt\n hat und stets wieder von neuem zu seiner Reproduktion erheischt. Er\n entdeckt dann mit Sismondi: „das Arbeitsvermögen . . . . ist\nNichts, wenn es nicht verkauft wird [Note: Sismondi: „Nouv. Princ. etc.“, t. I, p. 112.] .“","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0073","text":"Die eigenthümliche Natur dieser spezifischen Waare, der Arbeits-\n kraft, bringt es mit sich, dass mit der Abschliessung des Kontrakts zwischen\n Käufer und Verkäufer ihr Gebrauchswerth noch nicht wirklich in die\n Hand des Käufers übergegangen ist. Ihr Tauschwerth, gleich dem\n jeder andern Waare, war bestimmt, bevor sie in die Cirkulation trat, denn\n ein bestimmtes Quantum gesellschaftlicher Arbeit ward zur Produktion der\n Arbeitskraft verausgabt, aber ihr Gebrauchswerth besteht erst in der\n nachträglichen Kraftäusserung. Die Veräusserung der Kraft und ihre\n wirkliche Aeusserung, d. h. ihr Dasein als Gebrauchswerth, fallen daher\n der Zeit nach aus einander. Bei solchen Waaren aber, wo die formelle\n Veräusserung des Gebrauchswerths durch den Verkauf und seine wirkliche\n Ueberlassung an den Käufer der Zeit nach auseinander fallen, funktionirt\n das Geld des Käufers meist als Zahlungsmittel. In allen Ländern\n kapitalistischer Produktionsweise wird die Arbeitskraft erst gezahlt, nach-\n dem sie bereits während des im Kaufkontrakt festgesetzten Termins funk-\n tionirt hat, z. B. am Ende jeder Woche [Note: „All labour is paid, after it has ceased.“ („An Inquiry into those] . Ueberall schiesst daher der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"138\", \"facs\": \"#f0157\"} --> Arbeiter dem Kapitalisten den Gebrauchswerth der Arbeitskraft vor; er\n lässt sie vom Käufer konsumiren, bevor er ihren Preis bezahlt erhält,\n überall kreditirt daher der Arbeiter dem Kapitalisten. Dass diess\n Kreditiren kein leerer Wahn ist, zeigt nicht nur der gelegentliche Verlust des\n kreditirten Salairs beim Bankerott des Kapitalisten [Note: „L’ouvrier prête son industrie“, aber setzt Storch schlau hinzu: er\n „riskirt nichts“ ausser „de perdre son salaire … l’ouvrier ne transmet\nrien de matériel.“ (Storch: Cours d’Écon. Polit. Petersbourg\n 1815, t. II, p. 37.)] , sondern auch eine\n Reihe mehr nachhaltiger Wirkungen [Note: Ein Beispiel. In London existiren zweierlei Sorten von Bäckern, die\n „full priced“, die das Brod zu seinem vollen Werthe verkaufen, und die under-\n sellers, die es unter diesem Werth verkaufen. Letztere Klasse bildet über ¾\n der Gesammtzahl der Bäcker. (p. XXXII im „Report“ des Regierungskom-\n missairs H. S. Tremenheere über die „Grievances complained of by the\n journeymen bakers etc. London 1862.“) Diese undersellers verkaufen,\n fast ausnahmslos, Brod, das verfälscht ist durch Beimischung von Alaun, Seife,\n Perlasche, Kalk, Derbyshire Steinmehl und ähnlicher angenehmer, nahrhafter und\n gesunder Ingredienzien. (Sieh das oben citirte Blaubuch, ebenso den Bericht des\n „Committee of 1855 on the Adulteration of Bread“ und Dr. Hassall’s:\n „Adulterations Detected.“ 2nd edit. London 1862.) Sir John Gor-\n don erklärte vor dem Committee von 1855, dass „in Folge dieser Fälschungen\n der Arme, der von zwei Pfund Brod täglich lebt, jetzt nicht den vierten Theil des\n Nahrungsstoffes wirklich erhält, abgesehn von den schädlichen Wirkungen auf\n seine Gesundheit.“ Als Grund, „warum ein sehr grosser Theil der Arbeiterklasse“,\n obgleich wohlunterrichtet über die Fälschungen, dennoch Alaun, Steinmehl\n u. s. w. mit in den Kauf nimmt, führt Tremenheere (l. c. p. XLVIII) an, dass\n es für sie „ein Ding der Nothwendigkeit ist, von ihrem Bäcker oder dem chandler’s\n shop das Brod zu nehmen, wie man es ihnen zu geben beliebt.“ Da sie erst Ende\n der Arbeitswoche bezahlt werden, können sie auch „das während der Woche von\n ihren Familien verzehrte Brod erst Ende der Woche zahlen“ und fügt Tremen-\n heere mit Anführung der Zeugenaussagen hinzu: „es ist notorisch, dass\n mit solchen Mixturen bereitetes Brod express für diese Art Kunden ge-\n macht wird.“ („It is notorious that bread composed of those mixtures, is] . Indess ändert es an der Natur des\n [Note: Principles respecting the Nature of demand etc.“, p. 104. „Le\n crédit commercial a dû commencer au moment où l’ouvrier, premier artisan de la\n production, a pu, au moyen de ses économies, attendre le salaire de son travail\n jusqu’à la fin de la semaine, de la quinzaine, du mois, du trimestre etc.“ (Ch.\n Ganilh: Des Systèmes de l’Écon. Polit. 2èmeedit. Paris 1821,\n t. I, p. 150.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"139\", \"facs\": \"#f0158\"} --> Waarenaustauschs selbst nichts, ob das Geld als Kaufmittel oder als Zah-\n lungsmittel funktionirt. Der Preis der Arbeitskraft ist kontraktlich festgesetzt,\n obgleich er erst hinterher realisirt wird, wie der Miethpreis eines Hauses.\n Die Arbeitskraft ist verkauft, obgleich sie erst hinterher bezahlt wird.\n Für die reine Auffassung des Verhältnisses wird es daher nützlich sein,\n einstweilen stets vorauszusetzen, dass der Besitzer der Arbeitskraft\n mit ihrem Verkauf jedesmal auch sogleich den kontraktlich stipulirten Preis\n erhält.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0074","text":"Wir kennen nun die Art und Weise der Bestimmung des Tausch-\n werths, der dem Besitzer dieser eigenthümlichen Waare, der Arbeits-\n kraft, vom Geldbesitzer gezahlt wird. Der Gebrauchswerth, den\n letzterer seinerseits im Austausch erhält, zeigt sich erst im wirklichen\nVerbrauch, im Konsumtionsprozess der Arbeitskraft.\n Alle zu diesem Prozess nöthigen Dinge, wie Rohmaterial u. s. w., kauft der\n Geldbesitzer auf dem Waarenmarkt und zahlt sie zum vollen Preis. Der\nKonsumtionsprozess der Arbeitskraft ist zugleich der Pro-\n duktionsprozess von Waare und von Mehrwerth. Die Kon-\n [Note: made expressly for sale in this manner.“) „In vielen englischen Agrikultur-\n distrikten [aber noch mehr in schottischen] wird der Arbeitslohn vierzehntägig und\n selbst monatlich gezahlt. Mit diesen langen Zahlungsfristen muss der Agrikultur-\n arbeiter seine Waaren auf Kredit kaufen … Er hat höhere Preise zu zahlen und\n ist thatsächlich an die Boutique gebunden, die ihm pumpt. So kostet ihm z. B.\n zu Horningsham in Wilts, wo die Löhnung monatlich, dasselbe Mehl 2 sh. 4 d. per\n stone, das er sonstwo mit 1 sh. 10 d. zahlt.“ („Sixth Report“ on „Public\n Health“ by „The Medical Officer of the Privy Council etc. 1864“, p.\n 264.) „Die block printers von Paisley und Kilmarnock (Westschottland) er-\n zwangen 1853 durch einen Strike die Herabsetzung des Zahlungstermins von einem\n Monat auf 14 Tage.“ („Reports of the Inspectors of Factories for 31st\nOct. 1853“, p. 34.) Als eine weitere artige Entwicklung des Kredits, den der Ar-\n beiter dem Kapitalisten giebt, kann man die Methode vieler englischer Kohlenberg-\n werksbesitzer betrachten, wonach der Arbeiter erst Ende des Monats bezahlt wird,\n und in der Zwischenzeit Vorschüsse vom Kapitalisten erhält, oft in Waaren,\n die er über ihrem Marktpreis zahlen muss (Prucksystem). „It is a common\n practice with the coal masters to pay once a month, and advance cash to their\n workmen at the end of each intermediate week. The cash is given in the shop (näm-\n lich dem tommy shop oder dem Meister selbst gehörigen Kramladen); the men\n take it on one side and lay it out on the other.“ („Childrens’ Employmen\n Commission, III. Report. Lond. 1864“, p. 38, n. 192.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"140\", \"facs\": \"#f0159\"} --> sumtion der Arbeitskraft, gleich der Konsumtion jeder andern Waare,\n vollzieht sich ausserhalb des Markts oder der Cirkulations-\n sphäre. Diese geräuschvolle, auf der Oberfläche hausende und Aller\n Augen zugängliche Sphäre verlassen wir daher, zusammen mit Geldbe-\n sitzer und Besitzer der Arbeitskraft, um beiden nachzufolgen in die ver-\n borgne Stätte der Produktion, an deren Schwelle zu lesen steht:\nNo admittance except on business. Hier wird sich zeigen,\n nicht nur wie das Kapital producirt, sondern auch wie das\n Kapital selbst producirt wird. Das Geheimniss der Plus-\n macherei muss sich endlich enthüllen.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0075","text":"Die Sphäre der Cirkulation oder des Waarenaus-\n tauschs, innerhalb deren Schranken Kauf und Verkauf der Arbeitskraft\n sich bewegt, war in der That ein wahres Eden der angebornen\n Menschenrechte. Was allein hier herrscht, ist Freiheit, Gleich-\n heit, Eigenthum, und Bentham. Freiheit! denn Käufer und\n Verkäufer einer Waare, z. B. der Arbeitskraft, sind nur durch ihren\nfreien Willen bestimmt. Sie kontrahiren als freie, rechtlich ebenbür-\n tige Personen. Der Kontrakt ist das freie Produkt, worin sich ihre\n Willen einen gemeinsamen Rechtsausdruck geben. Gleichheit!\n Denn sie beziehen sich nur als Waarenbesitzer auf einander und\n tauschen Aequivalent für Aequivalent. Eigenthum! Denn jeder ver-\n fügt nur über das Seine. Bentham! Denn jedem von den beiden ist\n es nur um sich zu thun. Die einzige Macht, die sie zusammen und in ein\n Verhältniss bringt, ist die ihres Eigennutzes, ihres Sondervortheils,\n ihrer Privatinteressen. Und eben weil so jeder nur für sich und\n keiner für den andern kehrt, vollbringen alle, in Folge einer prästabi-\n lirten Harmonie der Dinge, oder unter den Auspicien einer all-\n pfiffigen Vorsehung, nur das Werk ihres wechselseitigen Vortheils, des Ge-\n meinnutzens, des Gesammtinteresses.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0076","text":"Beim Scheiden von dieser Sphäre der einfachen Cirkulation oder des\n Waarenaustauschs, woraus der Freihändler vulgaris Anschauungen, Be-\n griffe und Massstab für sein Urtheil über die Gesellschaft des Kapitals und\n der Lohnarbeit entlehnt, verwandelt sich, so scheint es, schon in etwas die\n Physiognomie unserer dramatis personae. Der ehemalige Geldbe-\n sitzer schreitet voran als Kapitalist, der Arbeitskraft-Besitzer folgt ihm\n nach als sein Arbeiter; der Eine bedeutungsvoll schmunzelnd und ge-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"141\", \"facs\": \"#f0160\"} --> schäftseifrig, der Andre scheu, widerstrebsam, wie Jemand, der seine eigne\n Haut zu Markt getragen und nun nichts andres zu erwarten hat als die\n — Gerberei.","kind":"display"}],"sha256":"5dadd7f19e0e0af64ee2c96acc18a020131fcafa74fcd749d3591a132e561055","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Zweites Kapitel. Die Verwandlung von Geld in Kapital.","source_sha256":"5dadd7f19e0e0af64ee2c96acc18a020131fcafa74fcd749d3591a132e561055","sha256":"07de6381c48d0cfdc4ecd5c80ccb2a5a2529f168392ad8df1358f117a9e490b4","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:22:42.296687+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0001","text":"## Chapter Two.\n The Transformation of Money into Capital.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0002","text":"## 1) The General Formula of Capital.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0003","text":"Commodity circulation is the starting-point of capital. Commodity\n production, commodity circulation, and developed commodity circulation, trade,\n therefore always constitute the historical preconditions under which\n capital arises. The modern life-history of capital dates from the creation of modern world trade and\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"107\", \"facs\": \"#f0126\"} --> the world market in the 16th century.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0004","text":"If we disregard the material content of commodity circulation, the ex-\n change of the various use-values, and consider only the economic forms\n which this process generates, we find money as its final product.\n This final product of commodity circulation is\nthe first form of appearance of capital.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0005","text":"Historically, capital everywhere first confronts landed property\n in the form of money, of monetary wealth, merchant's capital,\n and usurer's capital [Note: The antithesis between the power of landed property based on relations of personal servitude and lordship,\n and the impersonal power of money, is clearly grasped in the two French proverbs:\n “Nulle terre sans seigneur.” “L’argent n’a pas de maître.”] . Yet no retrospect upon the\n history of the emergence of capital is required in order to recognize money\n as its first form of appearance. The same history plays itself out daily before our\n eyes. Every new capital enters the stage in the first instance, i.e. the\n market—commodity market, labour market, or money market—still as money,\n money which is to transform itself into capital through definite processes.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0006","text":"Money as money and money as capital differ at first\n only by their different form of circulation.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0007","text":"The immediate form of commodity circulation is C — M — C,\n transformation of commodity into money and retransformation of money into commodity,\nselling in order to buy. Alongside this form, however, we find another,\n specifically distinct one, the form M — C — M, transfor-\n mation of money into commodity and retransformation of commodity into money, buy-\n ing in order to sell. Money which, in its movement, describes this latter\n form of circulation transforms itself into capital, becomes capital, and\nis already in itself, i.e. by its destination, capital.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0007"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0008","text":"Let us look more closely at the circulation M — C — M. It is a\n process which, like that of simple commodity circulation, passes through two opposed\n phases and forms their unity. In the first phase,\n M — C, purchase, money is transformed into commodity. In the second\n phase, C — M, sale, commodity is retransformed into money.\n But the unity of both phases, the total movement, presents itself\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"108\", \"facs\": \"#f0127\"} --> as exchange of money for commodity and re-exchange of the same\n commodity for money, buying commodity in order to sell it, or, if\n one disregards the formal distinctions between purchase and sale, buying\n commodity with money and money with commodity [Note: “Avec de l’argent on achète des marchandises, et avec des marchandises\n on achète de l’argent.” (Mercier de la Rivière: “L’ordre naturel\n et essentiel des sociétés publiques”, p. 543.)] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0008"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0009","text":"But as regards the result\n of the process, it terminates in the exchange of money for\n money, M — M. If I buy 2,000 pounds of cotton for £100\n and sell the 2,000 pounds of cotton again for £110, then in the end I have ex-\n changed £100 for £110, money for money.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0008"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0010","text":"Now it is indeed obvious that the process of circulation M —\n C — M would be absurd and devoid of content if one wished, by means of its\n detour, to exchange the same value in money for the same value in money, thus, for example,\n £100 for £100. Far simpler and\n safer would be the method of the hoard-maker, who holds fast to the £100\n instead of exposing it to the danger of circulation. On the other hand, whether the\n merchant sells the cotton bought for £100 again for\n £110, or whether he has to dispose of it for £100 or even £50,\n in all circumstances his money has described a peculiar\n and original movement, wholly different from that which\n it describes in simple commodity circulation, for example in the hand\n of the peasant who sells grain and buys clothes with the money thus obtained.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0009"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0011","text":"Thus, first of all, the differences of form\n between the circuits M — C — M and C — M — C must be characterized. Thereby\n the difference of content which lurks behind these differences\n of form will likewise emerge.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0010","text":"Let us first see what the two forms have in common.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0011","text":"Both circuits break down into the same two opposed phases,\n C — M, sale, and M — C, purchase. Considered in itself, each of these phases pre-\n sents no difference. The elements entering into the process\n are the same in both forms, commodity and money. In each\n section of the two circuits, the same economic character-\n masks confront one another, buyer and seller. In both processes there appear\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"109\", \"facs\": \"#f0128\"} --> three contracting parties, though only one contracting party always figures alternately\n as buyer and seller, while of the other two contracting parties\n one only sells and the other only buys. Finally, both circuits\n are unities of the same opposed phases.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0012","text":"The difference of form between the processes C — M — C and\n M — C — M only becomes apparent as soon as, instead of the two phases\n into which both break down, one compares their total courses. What divides them\n from the outset is the reversed sequence of the same op-\n posed phases of circulation. Simple commodity circulation be-\n gins with sale and ends with purchase; the circulation of money\n as capital begins with purchase and ends with sale. There,\n commodity, here money, forms the starting-point and final point\n of the movement. In the first form money functions, in the other,\n conversely, commodity, as mediator of the total course.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0013","text":"In the circulation C — M — C, money is ultimately transformed\n into commodity, which serves as use-value. Money is therefore defini-\n tively spent. In the reverse form M — C — M, by contrast, the\n buyer spends money in order to take in money as seller. In purchasing the commodity,\n he casts money into circulation in order to withdraw it from circulation again by the sale\n of the same commodity. He lets money go only with the\n sly intention of getting hold of it again. It is therefore only advanced [Note: “When a thing is bought, in order to be sold again, the sum employed is\n called money advanced; when it is bought not to be sold, it may be said to\n be expended.” (James Steuart: Works etc. edited by General\n Sir James Steuart, his son. Lond. 1801, v. I, p. 274.)] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0014","text":"In the form C — M — C, the same piece of money\n changes places twice. The seller receives it from the buyer and pays it\n away to another seller. The total process concludes with the giving away of money for commodity,\n as it began with the receipt of money for\n commodity. Conversely, in the form M — C — M. Not the\n same piece of money, but the same commodity here\n changes places twice. The buyer receives it from the hand of the seller and gives\n it away into the hand of another buyer. Just as in simple commodity\n circulation the twice-repeated change of place of the same\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"110\", \"facs\": \"#f0129\"} --> piece of money conditions its definitive passing from one hand\n into another, so here the twice-repeated change of place\n of the same commodity conditions the reflux of money to\n its first starting-point.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0015","text":"The reflux of money to its starting-point\n does not depend on whether or not the commodity is sold dearer than it was bought.\n This circumstance influences only the magnitude of the re-\n flowing sum of money. The phenomenon of reflux itself takes\n place as soon as the purchased commodity is sold again, that is, as soon as the circuit\n M — C — M is fully completed. This is thus a sensuously\n perceptible difference between the circulation of money as\n capital and its circulation as mere money.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0015"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0018","text":"To be sure, in C — M — C there can also be a reflux of money to its starting-point,\n but only through the renewal or repetition of the total process, not through the course of its own\n moments. If I sell a quarter of grain for £3 and buy clothes with these\n £3, the £3 are definitively expended for me. I have nothing more to do with them. They belong to the clothes-\n dealer. If I now sell a second quarter of grain, money flows back\n to me, but not as a consequence of the first transaction, but only as a consequence of\n its repetition. It moves away from me again as soon as I\n conclude the second transaction and buy anew. In the cir-\n culation C — M — C, therefore, the expenditure of money has nothing to do\n with its reflux.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0016"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0019","text":"In M — C — M, by contrast, the re-\n flux of money is conditioned by the manner of its expenditure itself. Without\n this reflux, the operation has miscarried or the process is inter-\n rupted and not yet complete, because its second phase, the sale completing\n and concluding the purchase, is absent.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0017","text":"The circuit C — M — C proceeds from the extreme of one commodity\n and concludes with the extreme of another commodity, which falls out of circulation\n and into consumption. Consumption, satisfaction of needs,\n in a word, use-value, is therefore its final purpose.\n The circuit M — C — M, by contrast, proceeds from the extreme of money\n and moves onward to the same extreme as its conclusion. Its driving\n motive and determining purpose is therefore exchange-value itself.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0017"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0021","text":"In simple commodity circulation, both extremes have the same\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"111\", \"facs\": \"#f0130\"} --> economic formal determination. They are both commodities. They are also\n commodities of the same magnitude of value. But they are at the same time quali-\n tatively different use-values, for example grain and clothes.\n The exchange of products, the change of the different materials in\n which social labour represents itself, here forms the content of the movement.\n It is otherwise in the circulation M — C — M. At first sight it seems\n devoid of content, because tautological. Both extremes have the same eco-\n nomic formal determination. They are both money. Nor do they differ\n qualitatively as use-values, for money is precisely the transformed shape of commodities,\n in which their particular use-values are effaced. First £100","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0018"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0022","text":"for cotton and then again\n the same cotton for £100, thus, by a detour, exchanging money for money, the same for the same, seems an operation just as purpose-\n less as it is absurd [Note: “On n’échange pas de l’argent contre de l’argent,” Mercier de la\n Rivière cries out to the mercantilists. (l. c. p. 486.) In a work which treats ex\n professo of “trade” and “speculation,” one reads: “All trade consists in the exchange of things of different kinds; and the advantage (for\n the merchant?) arises precisely from this difference. To exchange a pound of bread\n for a pound of bread would be without any advantage … hence the advan-\n tageous contrast between trade and gambling, which is only exchange\n of money for money.” (Th.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0018"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0023","text":"Corbet: “An Inquiry into\n the Causes and Modes of the Wealth of Individuals; or the\n Principles of Trade and Speculation explained. London. 1841”,\n p. 5.) Although Corbet does not see that M — M, exchange of money for money,\n is the characteristic form of circulation not only of commercial capital, but of\nall capital, he at least admits that this form is common to one kind of trade,\n speculation, and gambling; but then comes MacCulloch\n and finds that buying in order to sell is speculation, and that the differ-\n ence between speculation and trade thus disappears. “Every transaction\n in which an individual buys produce in order to sell it again, is,\n in fact, a speculation.” (Mac Culloch: A Dictionary practical\n etc. of Commerce. London 1847, p. 1056.)","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0018"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0024","text":"Far more naïve is Pinto,\n the Pindar of the Amsterdam exchange: “Le commerce est un jeu (this\n proposition borrowed from Locke); et ce n’est pas avec des gueux qu’on peut gagner. Si\n l’on gagnait long temps en tout avec tous, il faudrait rendre de bon accord les\n plus grandes parties du profit, pour recommencer le jeu.” (Pinto: Traité de\n la Circulation et du Credit. Amsterdam 1771, p. 231.)] . One sum of money can differ from another sum of money\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"112\", \"facs\": \"#f0131\"} --> only in its magnitude. The process M — C — M therefore owes its content not to any qualita-\n tive difference between its extremes, for both are money, but\n only to their quantitative difference. In the end,\n more money is withdrawn from circulation than was initially cast into it.\n The cotton bought for £100, for example, is sold again for\n £100 + £10.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0018"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0025","text":"or £110. The complete form\n of this process is therefore M — C — M', where M' = M + Δ M, i.e.\n equal to the sum of money originally advanced plus an incre-\n ment. This increment or the surplus over the original\n value I call surplus-value (surplus value). The value originally\n advanced therefore not only preserves itself in circulation, but\n in it alters its magnitude of value, adds a surplus-\n value, or valorizes itself. And this movement transforms\n it into capital.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0019","text":"It is indeed also possible that in C — M — C the two extremes\n C, C, for example corn and clothes, are quantitatively different magni-\n tudes of value. The farmer may have sold his corn above its value\n or bought the clothes below their value. He may in turn\n be cheated by the clothes dealer. Such differences in value remain,\n however, purely accidental for this form of circulation itself. It does\n not thereby lose sense and understanding, as does the process M — C — M,\n when the two extremes, corn and clothes for example, are equivalents.\n Their equivalence is here rather the condition of the normal course.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0019"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0027","text":"The repetition or renewal of selling in order to buy\n finds, like this process itself, its measure and aim in an end lying out-\n side it, consumption, the satisfaction of definite needs. In buying\n in order to sell, on the other hand, beginning and end are\n the same, money, exchange-value, and for that very reason the movement\n is endless. Certainly, M has become M + Δ M, £100 has become\n 100 + 10. But considered merely as to form, £110 are the same\n as £100, namely money. And considered as to the magnitude of value,\n £110 are a limited sum of value, like £100.\n If the £110 were spent as money, they would fall out of their\n role. They would cease to be capital. Withdrawn from circulation, they\n petrify into a hoard, and not a farthing accrues to them, even if they lie\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"113\", \"facs\": \"#f0132\"} --> there until Judgment Day.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0020"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0028","text":"If, then, it is a question of the magnitude of value\n as such, of the valorization of value, the same need exists for the\n valorization of £110 as for that of\n £100, since both are limited expressions of exchange-value,\n and thus both have the same vocation to approximate to wealth as such by\n expansion of magnitude. Certainly, for a moment the value originally\n advanced, £100, differs from the surplus-value of £10 accruing to it\n in circulation, but this difference immediately dissolves again. At the end\n of the process, what emerges is not, on the one hand, the original value\n of £100 and, on the other hand, the surplus-value of £10. What emerges\n is one value of £110.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0020"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0029","text":"which finds itself in precisely the same appropriate\n form in order to begin the process of valorization as\n the original £100. At the end of the movement, money\n again emerges as its beginning [Note: “Capital divides itself … into the original capital and profit,\n the increment of capital … although practice itself immediately adds this profit\n again to capital and sets it in motion with it.” (F. Engels: “Out-\n lines of a Critique of Political Economy” in “German-\n French Yearbooks, edited by Arnold Ruge and\n Karl Marx.” Paris 1844, p. 99.)] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0020"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0030","text":"If simple commodity circulation\n therefore has, in use-value, a barrier imposed upon it from\n outside, the movement of capital is on the contrary boundless, since in its\n conclusion it finds the principle and drive of its renewal, and\n its goal, the valorization of value, is just as little attained at the end of\n the process as at the beginning [Note: Aristotle opposes chrematistics to economics.\n He starts from economics. Insofar as it is an art of acquisition, it confines itself\n to the procurement of goods necessary for life and useful for the household or the\n state. “True wealth (ὁ ἀληϑινὸς πλοῦτος) consists of\n such use-values; for the measure of possession of this kind sufficient for the good\n life is not unlimited.”","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0020"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0031","text":"But there is a second kind of art of ac-\n quisition, which pre-eminently and rightly is called chrematistics, as a result\n of which no limit to wealth and possession seems to exist. The\ncommodity trade (ἡ ϰαπηλιπὴ literally means petty trade, and Aristotle assumes] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0020"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0032","text":"As the conscious bearer of this process, the money-owner becomes a capi-\n talist. His person, or rather his pocket, is the point of departure\n [Running matter: I. 8]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"114\", \"facs\": \"#f0133\"} --> and the point of return of money. The objective content of that\n process — valorization of value — is his subjective purpose,\n and only insofar as the growing appropriation of abstract wealth is the sole\n driving motive of his operations does he function as a capitalist, or as\n personified capital endowed with will and consciousness. Use-\n value is therefore never to be treated as the immediate aim of the capitalist\n [Note: “Commodities (here in the sense of use-values) are not the termi-\n nating object of the trading capitalist … money is his terminating object.” (Th.\n Chalmers: On Politic. Econ. etc. 2nd edit. Lond. 1832, p. 166.)] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0021"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0033","text":"Nor is individual profit, but only the restless\n movement of making profit [Note: “Il mercante non conta quasi per niente il lucro fatto, ma mira sempre al\n futuro.” (A. Genovesi: Lezioni di Economia Civile (1765). Edi-\n tion of the Italian economists by Custodi, Parte Moderna, t. VIII,\n p. 139.)] . This absolute drive to enrich oneself, this\n [Note: this form, because in it use-value predominates) does not by nature belong\n to chrematistics, for here exchange relates only to what is necessary for themselves\n (buyers and sellers). Hence, he further develops, the original form of commodity\n trade was also barter, but with its extension money necessarily arose.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0021"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0034","text":"With the invention of money,\n barter necessarily had to develop into ϰαπηλιϰὴ, into commodity trade,\n and this, in contradiction to its original tendency, developed into\n chrematistics, into the art of making money. Chrematistics now differs\n from economics in that “for it circulation is the source of\n wealth (ποιητιϰὴ χϱημάτων … διὰ χϱημάτων διαβολῆς).\n And it seems to revolve around money, for money is the beginning\n and the end of this kind of exchange (τὸ γάϱ νόμισμα στοιχεῖον\n ϰαὶ πέϱας τῆς ἀλλαγῆς ἐστίν). Hence wealth, as chre-\n matistics strives after it, is also unlimited.”","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0021"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0035","text":"For just as every art, for which its goal counts not as a\n means but as a final end, is unlimited in its striving, because\n it always seeks to come closer to it, whereas the arts which pursue only means to\n an end are not unlimited, since the end itself sets them their limit, so this\n chrematistics too has no barrier to its aim, but\n its aim is absolute enrichment. Economics, not chrematistics, has\n a limit . . . . the former aims at something different from money itself, the\n latter at its increase . . . . The confusion of the two forms, which pass over into\n each other, causes some to regard the preservation and multiplication of money to\n infinity as the final aim of economics.” (Aristotle: De Rep.\n edit. Bekker, lib. I, c.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0021"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0036","text":"8 and 9 passim.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"115\", \"facs\": \"#f0134\"} --> passionate hunt for exchange-value [Note: “The inextinguishable passion for profit, the auri sacra fames,\n always determines the capitalist.” (Mac Culloch: The Principles of Polit.\n Econ. London 1830, p. 163.) This insight naturally does not prevent Mac Cul-\n loch and Co., in theoretical embarrassments, e.g. in dealing with overpro-\n duction, from transforming the same capitalist into a good citizen, concerned only\n with use-value and even developing a veritable werewolf-like hunger for boots,\n hats, eggs, calicoes, and other most familiar kinds of use-value.]  is shared by the capitalist with the\n hoard-builder, but whereas the hoard-builder is only the mad\n capitalist, the capitalist is the rational hoard-builder.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0021"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0037","text":"The imperishabil-\n ity of exchange-value, which the hoard-builder seeks by attempting to\n rescue money from circulation [Note: “Σώζειν” is one of the characteristic expressions of the Greeks for\n hoard-building.] , is attained by the more astute capi-\n talist by continually surrendering it anew to circulation [Note: “Questo infinito che le cose non hanno in progresso, hanno in giro.”\n (Galiani.)] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0021"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0038","text":"In simple circulation, commodities develop their value into\n various independent forms confronting their use-value,\n i.e. into money-forms, which mediate exchange and disappear in its\n final result. In the circulation M — C — M, both commodity and money\n function only as different modes of existence\n of value itself, money as its general, commodity as its partic-\n ular, so to speak merely disguised mode of existence [Note: “Ce n’est pas la matière qui fait le capital, mais la valeur de ces ma-\n tières.” (J. B. Say: Traité de L’Econ. Polit. 3ème ed. Paris 1817,\n t. I, p. 428.)] . It constantly\n passes from one form into the other, without losing itself in this movement,\n and thus transforms itself into an automatic, self-processing sub-\n ject.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0022"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0039","text":"If one fixes one of the particular forms of appearance\n in which it alternately presents itself, one obtains the declarations:\ncapital is money, capital is commodity [Note: “Currency (!) employed to productive purposes is capital.” (Mac\n Leod: “The Theory and Practice of Banking. London 1855”, v. I,\n c. I.) “Capital is commodities.” (James Mill: “Elements of Pol. Econ.\n Lond. 1821”, p. 74.)] . But in fact\n [Running matter: 8*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"116\", \"facs\": \"#f0135\"} --> value here becomes the subject of a process in which, under\n the constant change of the forms of money and commodity, it changes\n its own magnitude, repels itself from itself as original\n value in the form of surplus-value, valorizes itself. For the movement\n in which it adds surplus-value is its own movement; its valorization\n is therefore self-valorization.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0022"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0040","text":"It has acquired the occult quality\n of setting value because it is value. It casts living offspring or at\n least lays golden eggs.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0022"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0041","text":"As the overriding subject of such a process, in which it now\n assumes, now casts off money-form and commodity-form, yet preserves\n and expands itself in this change, value naturally requires above all\n an independent form by which its identity with itself can be estab-\n lished. And it possesses this form only in money. This\n therefore forms the point of departure and the point of conclusion of every\n process of valorization. It was £100; it is now £110, and so\n on. But money itself counts here only as one form of value, for it has\n two of them. And the assumption of commodity-form constitutes precisely\n the mediating moment of its movement. Money therefore does not appear here in a pole-\n mical relation to commodity, as in hoard-building.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0023"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0042","text":"The capitalist\n knows that all commodities, however shabby they may look or however badly\n they may smell, are in faith and in truth money, inwardly circumcised\n Jews. M — M', money-breeding money —\n (money which begets money is the description of capital\n in the mouth of its first interpreters, the Mercantilists), — is in\n fact only the immediate form of appearance of value-setting\n value, of self-valorizing value.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0023"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0043","text":"If exchange-value in commodity circulation at most matures into an inde-\n pendent form over against the use-value of the commodity, it here suddenly presents\n itself as a self-processing, self-moving sub-\n stance, for which commodity and money are both mere forms. But more than\n that. Instead of representing commodity relations, it now enters, so to speak,\n into a private relation with itself. As original\n value, it distinguishes itself from itself as surplus-value, as God the Father\n from himself as God the Son, and both are of the same age and in fact\n constitute only one person, for only through the surplus-value of £10 do the £100\n advanced become","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0024"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0044","text":"capital, and as soon as they have become this, as soon as the\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"117\", \"facs\": \"#f0136\"} --> Son, and through the Son the Father, are generated, their distinction\n vanishes again and both are one, £110.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0025","text":"Value thus becomes self-processing value, self-process-\n ing money and, as such, capital. It comes out of circulation,\n enters it again, preserves and multiplies itself in it, returns from it en-\n larged, and always begins the same circuit anew [Note: “Capital . . . . permanently self-multiplying value”. (Sis\n mondi: Nouveaux Principes del’Econ. Polit., t. I, p. 90.)] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0026","text":"Buying in order to sell, or more completely, buying in order\n to sell more dearly, M — C — M', seems indeed to be a form peculiar\n only to one kind of capital, merchants’ capital. But industrial\n capital too is money that transforms itself into commodity\n and, through the sale of the commodity, transforms itself back into more\n money. Acts which may take place between purchase and sale, outside the\n sphere of circulation, alter nothing in this form of movement.\n In interest-bearing capital, finally, the form\n M — C — M' presents itself in abbreviated form, in the result without the\n mediation, so to speak in lapidary style, as M — M', money which is at\n once more money, value greater than itself.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0027","text":"Thus M — C — M' is in fact the general formula\n of capital, as it appears directly in the sphere of circulation.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0028","text":"## b) Contradictions of the general formula.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0029","text":"The form of circulation in which money reveals itself as capital\n contradicts all the laws previously developed concerning the nature of the commodity,\n of value, of money, and of circulation itself. What distinguishes it from simple\n commodity circulation is the reversed sequence of the same two opposed\n processes, sale and purchase. And how could this purely formal difference\n transform the nature of these processes by magic?","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0029"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0050","text":"More than that. This reversal exists only for one of the three\n business friends who deal with one another. As a capitalist I buy\n a commodity from A and sell it again to B, whereas as a mere\n commodity-owner I sell a commodity to B and then buy a commodity from A. For\n the business friends A and B, this difference does not exist. They appear\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"118\", \"facs\": \"#f0137\"} --> only as buyers or sellers of commodities.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0030"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0051","text":"I myself confront them\n each time as a mere owner of money or commodities, as buyer or seller;\n and indeed, in both sequences I confront the one person only as buyer\n and the other only as seller, the one as money alone, the other as commodity\n alone, neither of them as capital or capitalist or representative of anything\n that would be more than money or commodity, or could exert an effect other than\n that of money or commodity. For me, purchase from A and sale to\n B form a sequence. But the connection between these two\n acts exists only for me. A does not care about my transaction\n with B, nor B about my transaction with A.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0030"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0052","text":"Were I perhaps to wish\n to make clear to them the particular merit I acquired through the rever-\n sal of the sequence, they would prove to me that I was mistaken in\n the sequence itself and that the total transaction did not begin with a purchase\n and end with a sale, but conversely began with a sale and concluded with a purchase. In fact,\n my first act, the purchase, was from A’s standpoint a sale, and my\n second act, the sale, was from B’s standpoint a purchase. Not satis-\n fied with this, A and B will declare that the entire sequence was super-\n fluous and hocus-pocus. A will sell the commodity directly to B,\n and B will buy it directly from A. Therewith the whole trans-\n action shrivels into a one-sided act of ordinary commodity circulation,\n from A’s standpoint mere sale and from B’s standpoint mere purchase.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0030"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0053","text":"Thus, through the reversal of the sequence, we have not got beyond the sphere\n of simple commodity circulation, and must rather see whether, by its nature, it permits\n the valorization of the values entering into it and hence the formation\n of surplus-value.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0030"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0054","text":"Let us take the process of circulation in a form in which it presents itself as\n mere exchange of commodities. This is always the case when both\n commodity-owners buy commodities from one another, and the balance of their mutual\n monetary claims is settled on the day of payment. Money serves\n here as money of account, in order to express the values of commodities in their\n prices, but does not confront the commodities themselves as a thing. So\n far as use-value is concerned, it is clear that both exchangers\n may gain here. Both alienate commodities that are useless to\n them as use-values, and receive commodities which they require for their use.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"119\", \"facs\": \"#f0138\"} --> And this utility may not be the only one.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0031"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0055","text":"A, who\n sells wine and buys grain, perhaps produces more wine than grain-\n farmer B could produce in the same working time, and grain-\n farmer B more grain in the same working time than wine-grower A\n could produce. Thus A receives, for the same exchange-value, more grain,\n and B more wine, than if each of the two, without exchange,\n had to produce wine and grain for himself. With reference to\n use-value, therefore, it may be said that “exchange is a\n transaction in which both sides gain [Note: “L’échange est une transaction admirable dans laquelle les deux contrac-\n tants gagnent — toujours (!).” (Destutt de Tracy: Traité de la Volonté\n et de ses Effets. Paris 1826, p. 68.) The same book later appeared as\n “Traité de l’Ec. Pol.”] .” It is otherwise with\nexchange-value.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0031"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0056","text":"“A man who possesses much wine and no grain\n deals with a man who possesses much grain and no wine, and between them\n wheat to the value of 50 is exchanged for wine to a value of 50. This exchange is no increase\n of exchange-value either for the one or for the other; for\n already before the exchange each of them possessed a value equal to that\n which he procures for himself by means of this operation [Note: Mercier de la Rivière l. c. p. 544.] .” It changes\n nothing in the matter if money enters between the commodities as medium\n of circulation and the acts of purchase and sale sensuously fall apart [Note: “Que l’une de ces deux valeurs soit argent, ou qu’elles soient toutes deux\n marchandises usuelles, rien de plus indifférent en soi.” (Mercier de la Ri-\n vière l. c. p. 543.)] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0031"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0057","text":"The value of commodities is represented in their prices\n before they enter into circulation; it is thus the presupposition, not the result,\n of the latter [Note: “Ce ne sont pas les contractants qui prononcent sur la valeur; elle est\n decidée avant la convention.” (Le Trosne p. 906.)] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0031"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0058","text":"Considered abstractly, i.e. apart from circumstances that do not flow from\n the immanent laws of simple commodity circulation,\n nothing takes place in it, apart from the replacement of one use-value by another,\n but a metamorphosis, a mere change of form of the\n commodity. The same exchange-value, i.e. the same quantity of objectified\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"120\", \"facs\": \"#f0139\"} --> social labour, remains in the hands of the same commodity-\n owner alternately in the form of his commodity, of the money into\n which it is transformed, and of the commodity into which this money is retransformed.\n This change of form entails no alteration in the magnitude of value. The change,\n however, which the exchange-value of the commodity itself undergoes in this process,\n is confined to a change of its money-form.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0032"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0059","text":"It exists first\n as the price of the commodity offered for sale, then as the same\n sum of money that is expressed in this price, and finally as the price\n of an equivalent commodity. This change of form entails in and for\n itself just as little an alteration in the magnitude of value as the\n exchange of a five-pound note for sovereigns, half-sovereigns, and\n shillings. Insofar, then, as the circulation of commodities entails only a change\n of form in their exchange-value, it entails, where the phenomenon proceeds\n purely, exchange of equivalents. Even vulgar economy,\n however little it suspects what value is, therefore presupposes, whenever\n it wishes to consider the phenomenon purely in its fashion, that demand\n and supply coincide, i.e. that their effect ceases altogether.\n Thus, if with reference to use-value both exchangers\n can gain, they cannot both gain in exchange-\n value.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0032"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0060","text":"Here, rather, it holds: “Where there is equality, there is no profit [Note: “Dove è eguagità, non è lucro.” (Galiani: Della Moneta in\nCustodi: Parte Moderna, t. IV, p. 244.)] .”\n Commodities may indeed be sold at prices that diverge from their values,\n but this divergence appears as a violation of the law of commodity exchange [Note: “L’échange devient désavantageux pour l’une des parties, lorsque quelque\n chose étrangère vient diminuer ou exagérer le prix: alors l’égalité est blessée, mais\n la lésion procède de cette cause et non de l’échange.” (Le Trosne l. c.\n p. 904.)] . In its pure form, it is an exchange\n of equivalents, hence no means of enriching oneself in value [Note: “L’échange est de sa nature un contrat d’égalité qui se fait de valeur pour\n valeur égale. Il n’est donc pas un moyen de s’enrichir, puisque l’on\n donne autant que l’on reçoit.” (Le Trosne l.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0032"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0061","text":"c. p. 903.)] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0032"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0062","text":"Behind attempts to represent commodity circulation as a source of\n surplus-value there therefore usually lurks a quid pro quo, a con-\n fusion of use-value and exchange-value. Thus, for example, in Con-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"121\", \"facs\": \"#f0140\"} --> dillac: “It is false that in the exchange of commodities equal value\n is exchanged for equal value. On the contrary. Each of the two con-\n tracting parties always gives a smaller for a greater value …\n If, in fact, equal values were always exchanged, no profit\n could be made for either contracting party. But both gain,\n or at least ought to gain. Why? The value of things\n consists solely in their relation to our needs. What is more\n for one is less for the other, and conversely. … It is not\n assumed that we offer for sale things indispensable to our consumption. . . .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0033"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0063","text":"We wish to give away a thing useless to us in order\n to obtain one necessary to us; we wish to give less for more …\n It was natural to judge that in exchange one gives equal value for\n equal value, whenever each of the exchanged things was in value equal\n to the same quantity of money … But another consideration\n must still enter into the reckoning; the question is whether we both exchange a\nsurplus for something necessary [Note: Condillac: “Le Commerce et le Gouvernement” (1776).\nÉdit. Daire et Molinari in the “Mélanges d’Économie Politique.\n Paris 1847”, p.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0033"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0064","text":"267.] .” One sees\n how Condillac not only confounds use-value and exchange-value,\n but, in truly childish fashion, imputes to a society with developed\n commodity production a condition in which the producer produces his own\n means of subsistence and throws into circulation only the surplus over his own\n requirements, the excess [Note: Le Trosne therefore answers his friend Condillac quite correctly:\n “Dans la société formée il n’y a pas de surabondant en aucun genre.” At the\n same time he teases him with the gloss that “if both exchangers receive equally much more for\n equally much less, they both receive equally much.” Because Condillac\n does not yet possess the slightest notion of the nature of exchange-value,\n he is the fitting authority for Mr Professor Wilhelm Roscher’s own\n childish notions. See his: “The Foundations of National-\n Economy.”","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0033"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0065","text":"Third edition. 1858”.] . Yet\nCondillac’s argument is frequently repeated among modern economists, es-\n pecially when it is a question of representing the developed form of commodity exchange,\ntrade, as productive of surplus-value. “Trade,”\n it is said, for example, “adds value to products, since the same\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"122\", \"facs\": \"#f0141\"} --> products have more value in the hands of the consumer than in the\n hands of the producer, and it must therefore literally (strictly) be regarded as an act\n of production [Note: S. P. Newman: Elements of Polit. Econ. Andover and\n New - York 1835, p. 85.] .” But commodities are not paid for twice,\n once for their use-value and the other time for their exchange-\n value. And if the use-value of the commodity is more useful to the buyer\n than to the seller, its money-form is more useful to the seller than to the\n buyer.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0033"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0066","text":"Would he otherwise sell it? And thus it could equally well be\n said that the buyer literally (strictly) performs an “act of production”\n by, for example, transforming the merchant’s stockings into money.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0034","text":"If commodities, or commodities and money, of equal exchange-value,\n thus equivalents, are exchanged, evidently no one draws more value\n out of circulation than he throws into it. Then no formation of\n surplus-value takes place. But in its pure form the process of circulation of\n commodities entails exchange of equivalents. Yet things do not proceed purely\n in reality. Let us therefore suppose exchange of non-\n equivalents.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0035","text":"In any case, on the commodity market commodity-owner confronts com-\n modity-owner alone, and the power these persons exercise over one another\n is only the power of their commodities. The material difference\n between commodities is the material motive of exchange and makes commodity-\n owners mutually dependent on one another, inasmuch as none of them holds in his hand\n the object of his own need, while each holds the object of the need\n of the others. Apart from this material difference of their use-values,\n there exists only one further difference among commodities, the difference\n between their natural form and their transformed form, between commodity and money.\n And thus commodity-owners differ only as sellers, possessors of commodities,\n and as buyers, possessors of money.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0035"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0069","text":"Suppose now that by some inexplicable privilege the seller is granted the power\n to sell the commodity above its value, at 110\n when it is worth 100, thus with a nominal markup in price of\n 10%. The seller thus pockets a surplus-value of 10. But\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"123\", \"facs\": \"#f0142\"} --> after having been seller, he becomes buyer. A third commodity-owner\n now confronts him as seller and in his turn enjoys the privilege\n of selling the commodity 10% too dear. Our man has gained 10 as seller,\n only to lose 10 as buyer [Note: “By the augmentation of the nominal value of the produce . . . . sellers\n not enriched … since what they gain as sellers, they precisely expend in the\n quality of buyers.” (“The Essential Principles of the Wealth of\n Nation etc. London 1797”, p. 66.)] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0036"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0070","text":"The whole matter amounts in fact to this, that all commodity owners sell their commodities to one another 10% above their value, which is exactly the same as if they sold the commodities at their values. Such a general nominal rise in the prices of commodities produces the same effect as if, for example, commodity values were estimated in silver instead of gold. The money names, i.e. the prices of commodities, would swell, but their value relations would remain unchanged.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0037","text":"Let us assume conversely that it is the privilege of the buyer to buy commodities below their value. Here it is not even necessary to recall that the buyer again becomes a seller. He was a seller before he became a buyer. He has already lost 10% as a seller before he gains 10% as a buyer [Note: “If one is compelled to give for 18 livres a quantity of such a produc-\n tion that was worth 24, when one employs this same money to buy, one will\n likewise get for 18 l. what one paid 24 for.” (Le Trosne l. c. p. 897.)] . Everything again remains as before.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0038","text":"The formation of surplus-value, and hence the transformation of money into capital, can therefore be explained neither by sellers selling commodities above their value, nor by buyers buying them below their value [Note: “Each seller can therefore succeed in habitually raising the price of his\n commodities only by submitting also to habitually paying more dearly for the com-\n modities of other sellers; and for the same reason, each consumer can\n habitually pay less dearly for what he buys only by submitting also to\n a similar reduction in the price of the things he sells.” (Mercier de la\n Rivière l. c. p. 555.)] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0038"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0073","text":"The problem is in no way simplified by smuggling in foreign relations, thus, for instance, by saying with Colonel Torrens:\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"124\", \"facs\": \"#f0143\"} --> “Effective demand consists in the power and inclination (!) of consumers, whether by direct or indirect ex-\nchange, to give for commodities a certain greater portion of all the ingredients\nof capital than their production costs [Note: R. Torrens: “An Essay on the Production of Wealth.”\n London 1821, p. 349.] .” In circu-\nlation producers and consumers confront one another only as sellers and buy-\ners. To maintain that surplus-value for the producer arises from consumers paying for the commodity above its value means merely to disguise the simple proposition: the commodity owner possesses, as seller, the privilege of selling too dearly.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0039"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0074","text":"The seller has himself produced the commodity or represents its producer, but the buyer has no less himself produced the commodity represented in his money or represents its producer. Thus producer confronts produc-\ner. What distinguishes them is that one buys and the other sells. We are not advanced one step by the fact that the commodity owner, under the name of producer, sells the commodity above its value and, under the name of consumer, pays too dearly for it [Note: “The idea of profits being paid by the consumers, is, assuredly, very\n absurd. Who are the consumers?” (G. Ramsay: An Essay on the Dis-\n tribution of Wealth. Edinburgh 1836, p. 184.)] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0039"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0075","text":"The consistent representatives of the illusion that surplus-value arises from a nominal addition to price, or from the seller’s privilege of selling the commodity too dearly, therefore assume a class which only buys without selling, hence also only con-\nsumes without producing. The existence of such a class is inexplicable from the standpoint we have so far attained, that of simple circu-\nlation. But let us anticipate. The money with which such a class continually buys must continually flow to it from the commodity owners themselves, without exchange, gratuitously, on whatever legal titles and titles of force. To sell commodities to this class above their value means only to swindle back in part money that has been given away for nothing [Note: “When a man is in want of demand, does Mr.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0040"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0076","text":"Malthus recommend him\n to pay some other person to take off his goods?”, asks an indignant Ricardian] . Thus the cities of Asia Minor paid annual\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"125\", \"facs\": \"#f0144\"} --> money tribute to ancient Rome. With this money Rome bought commodities from them and bought them too dearly. The inhabitants of Asia Minor cheated the Romans by extracting from the conquerors, in the course of trade, a part of the tribute again. But nevertheless the inhabitants of Asia Minor remained the cheated ones. Their commodities were still paid for with their own money. This is no method of enrichment or of the formation of surplus-value.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0040"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0041","text":"Let us therefore keep within the bounds of commodity exchange, where sellers are buyers and buyers sellers. Our embarrassment perhaps stems from the fact that we have grasped the persons only as personified categories, not individually.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0041"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0078","text":"Commodity owner A may be clever enough to take in his colleagues B or C, while they, despite the best will, remain unable to retaliate. A sells wine to B to the value of 40 pounds sterling and acquires in exchange grain to the value of 50 pounds sterling. A has transformed his 40 pounds sterling into 50 pounds sterling, made more money out of less money, and transformed his commodity into capital. Let us look more closely. Before the ex-\nchange we had wine to the value of 40 pounds sterling in A’s hands and grain to the value of 50 pounds sterling in B’s hands, a total value of 90 pounds sterling. After the exchange we have the same total value of 90 pounds sterling. The circulating value has not increased by one atom; its dis-\ntribution between A and B has changed. On the one side what appears as surplus-value is on the other diminished value; on the one side as a plus, what on the other is a minus.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0042"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0079","text":"The same ex-\nchange would have taken place if A, without the disguising form of exchange, had directly stolen 10 pounds sterling from B. The sum of the circulating values can evidently be increased by no change in their distribution, any more than a Jew increases the mass of precious metals in a country by selling a farthing from the time of Queen Anne\n[Note: Malthus, who, like his pupil, the parson Chalmers, economically glorifies the class of\nmere buyers or consumers. See: “An In-\nquiry into those principles respecting the Nature of Demand and\n the Necessity of Consumption, lately advocated by Mr. Malthus”\n etc. London 1821, p. 55.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"126\", \"facs\": \"#f0145\"} --> for a guinea.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0042"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0080","text":"The capitalist class as a whole of a country cannot cheat itself [Note: Destutt de Tracy, although, perhaps because he was a Membre de l’Institut, was\nof the opposite opinion. Industrial capitalists, he says, make their profits by\n“selling everything more dearly than it cost to produce.\nAnd to whom do they sell? First, to one another.” (l. c. p. 239.)] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0042"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0081","text":"One may twist and turn as one will, the conclusion remains the same. If equivalents are exchanged, no surplus-value arises, and if non-equivalents are exchanged, no surplus-\nvalue arises either [Note: “The exchange of two equal values neither increases nor diminishes\n the mass of values existing in society. The exchange of two un-\nequal values … likewise changes nothing in the sum of social values, although it\nadds to the fortune of one what it takes from the fortune of the other.” (J. B. Say\nl. c. t. I, p. 434, 435.) Say, naturally unconcerned with the consequences\nof this proposition, borrows it almost verbatim from the Physiocrats. The following example\nwill show the manner in which he exploited their writings, forgotten in his time,\nto increase his own “value”.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0043"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0082","text":"Monsieur Say’s “most famous” proposition:\n“On n’achète des produits qu’avec des pro-\nduits” (l. c. t. II, p. 438) reads in the Physiocratic original: “Les pro-\nductions ne se paient qu’avec des productions.” (Le Trosne\nl. c. p. 899.)] . Circulation or commodity exchange creates no value [Note: “Exchange confers no value at all upon products.” (F. Wayland:\n The Elements of Pol. Econ. Boston 1853, p. 168.)] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0043"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0044","text":"One therefore understands why, in our analysis of the fundamental form of capital, the form in which it determines the economic organisation of modern society, its most popular and, so to speak, antediluvian forms, commercial capital and usury capital, at first remain wholly unconsidered.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0044"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0084","text":"In commercial capital proper, the form M — C — M', buying in order to sell dearer, appears in its purest form. On the other hand, its whole movement takes place within the sphere of circulation. But since it is impossible to explain from circulation itself the transformation of money into capi-\ntal, the formation of surplus-value, commercial capital appears impossible as soon as equivalents are exchanged [Note: “Under the rule of invariable equivalents commerce would be im-\npossible.” (G. Opdyke: A Treatise on Polit. Economy. New-] , and is therefore\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"127\", \"facs\": \"#f0146\"} --> derivable only from the two-sided cheating of the commodity producers who buy and sell by the merchant who parasitically pushes himself between them. In this sense Franklin says: “War\nis robbery, commerce is cheating [Note: Benjamin Franklin: Works, vol. II, edit.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0045"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0085","text":"Sparks in:\n“Positions to be examined concerning National Wealth.”] :” If the valorisation of commercial capital is not to be explained by mere cheating of commodity producers, a long series of intermediate links is required, which is still wholly absent here, where commodity circulation and its simple moments form our sole presupposition.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0045"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0086","text":"What applies to commercial capital applies still more to usury capi-\ntal. In commercial capital, the extremes, the money cast upon the market and the increased money withdrawn from the market, are at least mediated by purchase and sale, by the movement of circulation. In usury capital the form M — C — M'\nis abbreviated to the unmediated extremes M — M', money which exchanges itself for more money, a form contrary to the nature of money and therefore inexplicable from the standpoint of commodity exchange.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0046"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0087","text":"Hence Aristotle: “since chrematistics is twofold, one kind belonging to trade, the other to household management, the latter necessary and praiseworthy, the former based on circu-\nlation and rightly censured, (for it rests not on nature, but on mutual cheat-\ning), usury is with fullest right detested, because money itself is here the source of gain and is not used for that for which it was invented. For it arose for commodity exchange, but interest makes more money out of money. Hence also its name (τόϰος interest and offspring). For offspring are like their begetters. But interest is money from money, so that of all modes of acquisition this is the most contrary to nature [Note: Arist. l. c. c. 10.] .”","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0046"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0047","text":"Like commercial capital, we shall find interest-bearing capital in the course of our investigation as derived forms\n[Note: York 1851, p. 69.) “The distinction between real value and exchange value\nis based on a fact—namely, that the value of a thing differs from the so-called\n equivalent given for it in trade, i.e. that this equivalent is not an equivalent.” (F. Engels l. c. p. 96.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"128\", \"facs\": \"#f0147\"} --> and shall at the same time see why they appear historically before capital in its modern fundamental form.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0047"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0089","text":"It has been shown that surplus-value cannot arise from circulation, and that therefore, in its formation, something must take place behind its back which is itself invisible within it [Note: “Profit, in the usual condition of the market, is not made by exchang-\ning. Had it not existed before, neither could it after that transaction.”\n(Ramsay l. c. p. 184.)] . But can sur-\nplus-value arise from anywhere other than circulation? Circulation is the sum of all the relations of exchange among commodity owners. Outside it, the commodity owner stands only in relation to his own commodity.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0048"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0090","text":"As regards its value [Note: I remark here that when I use the word value in this work without\nfurther qualification, exchange-value is always to be understood.] , the relation is confined to the fact that it contains a quantum of his own labour measured according to definite social laws. This quantum of labour is expressed in the magnitude of value of his commodity and, since magnitude of value is represented in money of account, in a price of, for example, 10 pounds sterling. But his labour is not represented in the value of the commodity and in a surplus over its own value, not in a price of 10 which is at the same time a price of 11, not in a value greater than itself. Through his labour the commodity owner can form values, but not self-valorising values.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0048"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0091","text":"He can increase the value of a commodity by adding new value to existing value through new labour, for example, by making boots out of leather. The same material now has more value because it contains a greater quantum of labour. The boot therefore has more value than the leather, but the value of the leather has remained what it was. It has not valorised itself, has not enriched its original value by a surplus-value during the manufacture of the boot. It is therefore impossible for the commodity producer, outside the sphere of circulation, without coming into contact with other commodity owners, to valorise value and thus transform money or commodity into capital.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0048"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0049","text":"Capital can therefore not arise from circulation, and it can just as little not arise from circulation. It must arise simultaneously in it and not in it.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0049"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0050","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"129\", \"facs\": \"#f0148\"} -->","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0050"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0051","text":"A twofold result has thus emerged.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0051"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0095","text":"The transformation of money into capital is to be developed on the basis of laws immanent in commodity exchange, so that the exchange of equivalents counts as the point of departure [Note: After the exposition given, the reader understands that this only means: The formation of capital must be possible even if the commodity price equals the commodity value. It cannot be explained from the deviation of commodity prices from commodity values. If prices really deviate from values, then they must first be reduced to the latter, i.e. one must abstract from this circumstance as an accidental one, in order to have before oneself the phenomenon of capital formation purely on the basis of commodity exchange and not to be confused in observing it by disturbing subsidiary circumstances alien to the actual course.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0052"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0096","text":"Moreover, it is known that this reduction is by no means a merely scientific procedure. The constant oscillations of market prices, their rise and fall, compensate one another, mutually cancel one another, and reduce themselves to the average price as their inner rule. This forms the guiding star, for example, of the merchant or industrialist in every undertaking that embraces a longer period of time. He knows, therefore, that, viewed as a whole over a longer period, commodities are actually sold neither below nor above, but at their average price. If disinterested thinking were thus his interest at all, he would have to formulate the problem of capital formation as follows: How can capital arise when prices are regulated by the average price, i.e., in the last instance, by the value of the commodity?","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0052"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0097","text":"I say “in the last instance,” because average prices do not directly coincide with the magnitudes of value of commodities, as A. Smith, Ricardo, etc. believe.] . Our money-owner, who still exists only as a capitalist caterpillar, must buy commodities at their value, sell them at their value, and yet at the end of the process draw out more value than he threw in. His butterfly-like unfolding must proceed in the sphere of circulation and must not proceed in the sphere of circulation. These are the conditions of the problem. Hic Rhodus, hic salta!","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0052"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0053","text":"## 3) Purchase and sale of labour-power.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0053"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0099","text":"The change in value of the money which is to transform itself into capital cannot take place in this money itself, for as means of purchase and means of payment it merely realizes the price of the commodity which it buys or pays for, while, persisting in its own form, it congeals into a petrifaction of constant magnitude of value [Note: “In the form of money . . . . capital is productive of no profit.”\n(Ricardo: Princ. of Pol. Econ. p. 267.)] . Just as little\n[Running matter: I. 9]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"130\", \"facs\": \"#f0149\"} --> can the change arise from the second act, the resale of the commodity, for this act merely transforms the commodity back from the natural form into the money form.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0054"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0100","text":"The change must therefore take place with the commodity which is bought in the first act M — C, but not with its exchange-value, for equivalents are exchanged; the commodity is paid for at its value. The change can therefore arise only from its use-value as such, i.e., from its consumption. In order to draw exchange-value out of the consumption of a commodity, our money-owner would have to be fortunate enough to discover within the sphere of circulation, on the market, a commodity whose use-value itself possessed the peculiar property of being a source of exchange-value, whose actual consumption therefore itself would be objectification of labour, hence value-creation. And the money-owner finds such a specific commodity on the market—the capacity for labour or labour-power.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0054"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0055","text":"By labour-power or capacity for labour we understand the aggregate of the physical and mental capabilities which exist in the corporeality, the living personality, of a human being, and which he sets in motion whenever he produces use-values of any kind.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0055"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0102","text":"For the money-owner, however, to find labour-power as a commodity on the market, various conditions must be fulfilled. Commodity exchange as such includes no relations of dependence other than those arising from its own nature. Under this presupposition, labour-power can appear as a commodity on the market only insofar as and because it is offered for sale or sold as a commodity by its own owner, the person whose labour-power it is.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0056"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0103","text":"For its owner to sell it as a commodity, he must be able to dispose of it, and hence be the free owner of his capacity for labour, of his person [Note: In encyclopaedias of classical antiquity one may read the absurdity that in the ancient world capital was fully developed “except that the free worker and the credit system were lacking.” Mr. Mommsen, too, in his “Roman History,” commits one quid pro quo after another.] . He and the money-owner meet on the market and enter into relation with one another as equal commodity-owners, distinguished only in that one is buyer, the other seller, both therefore juridically equal persons.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0056"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0104","text":"The continuance of this relation requires that the owner of labour-power always sell it only for a definite time, for if he sells it wholesale, once and for all, he sells himself, transforms himself from a free man into a slave, from a commodity-owner into a commodity. As a person he must constantly relate to his labour-power as his property and hence as his own commodity, and he can do so only insofar as he always places it at the buyer's disposal only temporarily, for a definite term of time, leaves it to him for consumption, and thus, through its alienation, does not renounce his property in it [Note: Various legislations therefore fix a maximum for the labour contract. All law codes among peoples of free labour regulate conditions for terminating the contract.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0056"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0105","text":"Among various peoples, notably in Mexico (before the American Civil War also in the territories torn away from Mexico, and in fact until Kusa's revolution in the Danubian principalities), slavery is concealed under the form of peonage. Through advances to be worked off, which are carried forward from generation to generation, not only the individual worker but also his family becomes in fact the property of other persons and their families. Juarez had abolished peonage. The so-called Emperor Maximilian reintroduced it by a decree which in the House of Representatives at Washington was aptly denounced as a decree for the reintroduction of slavery in Mexico. “Of my particular bodily and mental skills and possibilities of activity I can . . . .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0056"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0106","text":"alienate to another a use limited in time, because after this limitation they retain an external relation to my totality and universality. By alienating my entire time made concrete through labour, and the totality of my production, I would make their substantial element, my universal activity and actuality, my personality, the property of another.” Hegel: “Philosophy of Right. Berlin 1840,” p. 104, § 67.] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0056"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0057","text":"The second essential condition for the money-owner to find labour-power on the market as a commodity is that its owner, instead of being able to sell commodities in which his labour has objectified itself, must rather offer for sale as a commodity his labour-power itself, which exists only in his living corporeality.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0057"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0058","text":"[Running matter: 9*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"132\", \"facs\": \"#f0151\"} -->","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0058"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0059","text":"For anyone to sell commodities distinct from his labour-power, he must naturally possess means of production, e.g. raw materials, instruments of labour, etc. He cannot make boots without leather. Besides this, he requires means of subsistence. No one can live on products of the future, and hence not on use-values whose production he has not yet completed; and, as on the first day of his appearance on the world-stage, man must still consume every day before and while he produces. If products are produced as commodities, they must be sold after they are produced and can satisfy the needs of the producer only after the sale. To the time of production is added the time necessary for sale.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0059"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0060","text":"For the transformation of money into capital, the money-owner must therefore find the free worker on the commodity market, free in the double sense that, as a free person, he disposes of his labour-power as his commodity, and that, on the other hand, he has no other commodities to sell, is free and unencumbered, free of all things necessary for the realization of his labour-power.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0060"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0061","text":"The question why this free worker confronts him in the sphere of circulation does not interest the money-owner, who finds the labour-market before him as a special department of the commodity market. And for the time being it interests us just as little. Theoretically we adhere to the fact, as the money-owner does practically. One thing, however, is clear. Nature does not produce on one side owners of money or commodities and on the other mere owners of their own labour-powers. This relation is not one of natural history, nor is it a social relation common to all periods of history. It is manifestly itself the result of a preceding historical development, the product of many economic revolutions, of the downfall of a whole series of older formations of social production.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0061"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0112","text":"The economic categories which we considered earlier also bear their historical trace. In the existence of the product as commodity, definite historical conditions are concealed. In order to become a commodity, the product must not be produced as an immediate means of subsistence for the producer himself. Had we inquired further:\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"133\", \"facs\": \"#f0152\"} --> Under what circumstances do all, or even only the majority, of products assume the form of commodity?—it would have been found that this occurs only on the basis of a quite specific, the capitalist, mode of production. Such an investigation, however, lay outside the analysis of the commodity.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0062"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0113","text":"Commodity production and commodity circulation can take place although the overwhelmingly greater mass of products, directed immediately towards self-supply, does not transform itself into commodity, and the social process of production is therefore still far from being dominated in its whole breadth and depth by exchange-value. The presentation of the product as commodity presupposes so developed a division of labour within society that the separation between use-value and exchange-value, which only begins in immediate barter, has already been accomplished. But such a stage of development is common to the most historically diverse economic formations of society.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0062"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0114","text":"Or consider money: it presupposes a certain level of commodity circulation. The particular money forms—mere commodity-equivalent, or medium of circulation, or means of payment, hoard, and world money—indicate, according to the differing scope and the relative predominance of one or the other function, very different stages of the social process of production. Yet experience shows that relatively weakly developed commodity circulation suffices for the formation of all these forms. It is otherwise with capital. Its historical conditions of existence are by no means present with commodity and money circulation. It arises only where the owner of means of production and subsistence finds the free worker as seller of his labour-power on the market, and this one historical condition encompasses a world-history.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0063"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0115","text":"Capital therefore announces itself from the very outset as an epoch of the social process of production.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0063"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0064","text":"This peculiar commodity, labour-power, must now be considered more closely. Like all other commodities, it possesses an exchange-value [Note: “The Value or Worth of a man, is as of all other things, his price: that is to say, so much as would be given for the use of his power.”\nTh. Hobbes: “Leviathan” in Works edit. Molesworth. London\n1839—44, v. III, p. 76.] . How is it determined?","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0064"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0065","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"134\", \"facs\": \"#f0153\"} -->","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0065"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0118","text":"The value of labour-power, like that of every other commodity, is de-\n termined by the labour-time necessary for the production, and hence also re-\n production, of this specific article. In so far as it is exchange-\n value, labour-power itself represents only a definite quantum of objectified\n social average labour.\n Labour-power exists only as the capacity of the living individual. Its\n production therefore presupposes his existence. Given this, its\n production consists in his reproduction or maintenance. For his maintenance\n the living individual requires a certain sum of means of subsistence.\n The labour-time necessary for the production of labour-power thus resolves\n itself into the labour-time necessary for the production of these means of subsistence,\n or the value of labour-power is the value of the means of subsistence neces-\n sary for the maintenance of its possessor.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0066"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0119","text":"Labour-\n power, however, realises itself only through its externalisation, is active\n only in labour. Through its activity, labour, however, a\n definite quantum of human muscle, nerve, brain, etc. is expended,\n which must be replaced. This increased expenditure entails an increased in-\n take [Note: The ancient Roman villicus, as steward at the head of the agricultural\n slaves, accordingly received, “because he has lighter work than the servants, a scantier\n allowance than they.” (Th. Mommsen: Roman History 1856, p. 810.)] . If the owner of labour-\n power has worked today, he must be able to repeat the same process tomorrow under the\n same conditions of strength and health. The sum of means of subsistence must therefore\n suffice to maintain the working individual as a working individual in his normal state of life.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0066"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0120","text":"Natural needs themselves, such as food, clothing, heating,\n housing, etc., differ according to the climatic and other\n natural peculiarities of a country. On the other hand, the extent\n of so-called necessary means of subsistence, as well as the manner of their satis-\n faction, is itself a historical product and therefore depends largely\n on the level of culture of a country, among other things essentially\n also on the conditions under which, and hence on the habits\n and requirements of life with which, the class of free labourers has formed\n itself [Note: In his work: “Over population and its Remedy. London\n 1846,” W. Th. Thornton gives interesting evidence on this point.] . In contrast to the other commodities, the determination of the value of\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"135\", \"facs\": \"#f0154\"} --> labour-power therefore contains a historical and moral element.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0066"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0121","text":"For a definite country, however, at a definite period, the average\n range of necessary means of subsistence is given.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0066"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0122","text":"The owner of labour-power is mortal. If, therefore, his ap-\n pearance on the market is to be continuous, as the continuous\n transformation of money into capital presupposes, then the seller of labour-\n power must perpetuate himself, “as every living individual perpetuates him-\n self, by procreation [Note: Petty.] .” The labour-powers withdrawn from\n the market by wear and death must at the very least be constantly replaced\n by an equal number of new labour-powers. The sum\n of means of subsistence necessary for the production of labour-power therefore includes\n the means of subsistence of the replacements, i.e.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0067"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0123","text":"of the children of the work-\n ers, so that this race of peculiar commodity-owners perpetuates itself on the com-\n modity market [Note: “Its (labour’s) natural price … consists in such a quantity of necessaries,\n and comforts of life, as, from the nature of the climate, and the habits of the\n country, are necessary to support the labourer, and to enable him to rear such a\n family as may preserve, in the market, an undiminished supply of\n labours.“ R. Torrens: “An Essay on the External Corn Trade.\n London 1815”, p. 62. The word labour here incorrectly stands for labour-power.] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0067"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0068","text":"In order so to modify general human nature that it acquires skill\n and dexterity in a particular branch of labour, becomes developed\n and specific labour-power, a definite education or training is required,\n which in turn costs a greater or lesser\n sum of commodity-equivalents. According to the more or less\n mediated character of labour-power, the costs of its education dif-\n fer. These costs of learning, vanishingly small in the case of ordinary\n labour-power, thus enter into the range of commodities necessary for its\n production.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0068"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0069","text":"The value of labour-power resolves itself into the value\n of a definite sum of means of subsistence. It therefore also varies\n with the value of these means of subsistence, i.e. with the magnitude of the\n labour-time required for their production.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0069"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0126","text":"One part of the means of subsistence, e.g. foodstuffs, means of heating,\n etc., is consumed afresh daily and must be replaced afresh daily\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"136\", \"facs\": \"#f0155\"} --> . Other means of subsistence, such as clothes, furniture, etc., wear out\n over longer periods and therefore need be replaced only at longer intervals.\n Commodities of one kind must be bought or paid for daily, others weekly, quar-\n terly, and so forth. However the sum\n of these expenditures may be distributed over the course of a year, for example,\n it must be covered by the average income, day in, day out.\n If the mass of commodities required daily for the production of labour-power\n = A, that required weekly = B, that required quar-\n terly = C, etc., then the daily average\n of these commodities would be \\frac{= 365 A + 52 B + 4 C +}{365} etc.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0070"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0127","text":"Suppose that this\n mass of commodities necessary for the average day contained 6 hours\n of social labour, then there would be objectified in\n labour-power daily half a day of social average labour,\n or half a working day is required for the daily production of labour-power.\n This quantum of labour required for its daily production constitutes the daily value of labour-\n power, or the value of the labour-power reproduced daily. If\n half a day of social average labour is likewise represented in a\n quantity of gold of 3 sh. or one thaler, then one thaler\n is the price corresponding to the daily value of labour-power.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0070"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0128","text":"If the\n possessor of labour-power offers it for sale for one thaler daily, then its sell-\n ing price is equal to its value and, according to our assumption,\nthe money-owner, eager to transform his thalers into capital,\npays this value.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0070"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0071","text":"The ultimate limit or minimum limit of the value of labour-\n power is formed by the value of a mass of commodities without whose daily\n supply the bearer of labour-power, the human being, cannot renew his life-pro-\n cess, hence by the value of the physically indis-\n pensable means of subsistence. If the price of labour-power\nfalls to this minimum, it falls below its value, for it\n can then be maintained and developed only in a stunted form.\n But the exchange-value of every commodity is determined by the\n labour-time required to supply it in normal quality.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0071"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0130","text":"It is extraordinarily cheap sentimentality to find this determination of the value of labour-power\n arising from the nature of the matter coarse,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"137\", \"facs\": \"#f0156\"} --> and, like Rossi, perhaps to lament: “To conceive labour-power (puis-\n sance de travail) while abstracting from the means of subsistence of\n labour during the production process is to conceive a figment (être de raison).\n Whoever says labour, whoever says labour-power, at the same time says labourer\n and means of subsistence, labourer and wages [Note: Rossi: “Cours d’Écon. Polit. Bruxelles 1842”, p. 370.] .” Whoever says labour-power does not say labour, any more than\n whoever says digestive power says digestion. The latter process,\n as is well known, requires more than a good stomach.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0072"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0131","text":"Whoever says labour-power\n does not abstract from the means of subsistence necessary for its maintenance.\n Their value is rather expressed in its value. If it is not sold,\n it is of no use to the labourer; rather, he feels it as a cruel\n natural necessity that his labour-power has required a definite quantum of means of subsistence\n for its production and always requires it anew for its reproduction. He\n then discovers with Sismondi: “labour-power . . . . is\nNothing if it is not sold [Note: Sismondi: “Nouv. Princ. etc.”, t. I, p. 112.] .”","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0072"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0132","text":"The peculiar nature of this specific commodity, labour-\n power, entails that, with the conclusion of the contract between\n buyer and seller, its use-value has not yet actually passed into\n the hands of the buyer. Its exchange-value, like that\n of every other commodity, was determined before it entered circulation, for\n a definite quantum of social labour was expended for the production of\n labour-power; but its use-value consists only in the subsequent exertion of power.\n The alienation of the power and its actual\n exertion, i.e. its existence as use-value, therefore fall apart in time.\n But in the case of commodities where the formal\n alienation of the use-value through sale and its actual\n transfer to the buyer fall apart in time, the buyer’s money generally functions\n as means of payment.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0073"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0133","text":"In all countries\n of capitalist mode of production, labour-power is paid for only after\n it has already functioned during the term stipulated in the contract of purchase,\n e.g. at the end of every week [Note: “All labour is paid, after it has ceased.” (“An Inquiry into those] . Everywhere, therefore, the\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"138\", \"facs\": \"#f0157\"} --> labourer advances to the capitalist the use-value of labour-power;\n he lets it be consumed by the buyer before receiving payment of its price;\n everywhere, therefore, the labourer gives credit to the capitalist.\n That this giving of credit is no empty fancy is shown not only by the occasional loss of\n the credited wage in the bankruptcy of the capitalist [Note: “L’ouvrier prête son industrie”, but Storch shrewdly adds: he\n “risks nothing” except “de perdre son salaire … l’ouvrier ne transmet\nrien de matériel.” (Storch: Cours d’Écon.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0073"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0134","text":"Polit. Petersburg\n 1815, t. II, p. 37.)] , but also by a\n series of more lasting effects [Note: One example. In London there exist two sorts of bakers, the\n “full priced”, who sell bread at its full value, and the under-\n sellers, who sell it below this value. The latter class makes up over ¾\n of the total number of bakers. (p. XXXII in the “Report” of the government com-\n missioner H. S. Tremenheere on the “Grievances complained of by the\n journeymen bakers etc. London 1862.”) These undersellers sell,\n almost without exception, bread adulterated by the addition of alum, soap,\n pearl ash, lime, Derbyshire stone meal, and similar pleasant, nourishing and\n healthy ingredients. (See the Blue Book cited above, likewise the report of the\n “Committee of 1855 on the Adulteration of Bread” and Dr. Hassall’s:\n “Adulterations Detected.” 2nd edit. London 1862.)","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0073"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0135","text":"Sir John Gor-\n don declared before the Committee of 1855 that “as a consequence of these adulterations\n the poor man, who lives on two pounds of bread daily, now does not actually receive a quarter of\n the nutritive substance, apart from the harmful effects upon\n his health.” As the reason “why a very large part of the working class,”\n although well informed about the adulterations, nevertheless takes alum, stone meal,\n etc. into the bargain, Tremenheere (l. c. p.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0073"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0136","text":"XLVIII) states that\n it is for them “a matter of necessity to take their bread from their baker or the chandler’s\n shop as they please to give it to them.” Since they are paid only at the end\n of the working week, they can also “pay for the bread consumed by\n their families during the week only at the end of the week,” and Tremen-\n heere adds, citing the depositions of witnesses: “it is notorious that\n bread prepared with such mixtures is made expressly for this kind of cus-\n tomer.” (“It is notorious that bread composed of those mixtures, is] . Yet this does not alter the nature of the\n [Note: Principles respecting the Nature of demand etc.”, p. 104. “Le\n crédit commercial a dû commencer au moment où l’ouvrier, premier artisan de la\n production, a pu, au moyen de ses économies, attendre le salaire de son travail\n jusqu’à la fin de la semaine, de la quinzaine, du mois, du trimestre etc.” (Ch.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0073"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0137","text":"Ganilh: Des Systèmes de l’Écon. Polit. 2èmeedit. Paris 1821,\n t. I, p. 150.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"139\", \"facs\": \"#f0158\"} --> commodity exchange itself, whether money functions as means of purchase or as means of pay-\n ment. The price of labour-power is fixed by contract,\n although it is realised only afterwards, like the rent of a house.\n Labour-power is sold, although it is paid for only afterwards.\n For a pure conception of the relation it will therefore be useful,\n for the time being always to assume that the possessor of labour-power,\n with each sale of it, also immediately receives the contractually stipulated price.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0073"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0138","text":"We now know the manner of determining the exchange-\nvalue which is paid by the money-owner to the owner of this peculiar commodity, labour-\npower. The use-value which the former in turn receives in exchange appears only in the actual\nconsumption, in the consumption-process of labour-power.\n The money-owner buys on the commodity market all things necessary to this process, such as raw material, etc., and pays for them at their full price. The\nconsumption-process of labour-power is at the same time the pro-\nduction-process of commodity and of surplus-value. The con-\n[Note: made expressly for sale in this manner.”) “In many English agricultural\n districts [but still more in Scottish ones] wages are paid fortnightly and\n even monthly.”","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0074"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0139","text":"With these long terms of payment the agricultural\n labourer has to buy his commodities on credit … He has to pay higher prices and\n is in fact tied to the shop which gives him credit. Thus, for example,\n at Horningsham in Wilts, where payment is monthly, the same flour costs him 2 sh. 4 d. per\n stone which elsewhere he pays for at 1 sh. 10 d.” (“Sixth Report” on “Public\n Health” by “The Medical Officer of the Privy Council etc. 1864”, p.\n 264.) “The block printers of Paisley and Kilmarnock (West Scotland) com-\npelled in 1853, by means of a strike, the reduction of the term of payment from one\n month to 14 days.” (“Reports of the Inspectors of Factories for 31st\nOct. 1853”, p. 34.)","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0074"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0140","text":"As a further pretty development of the credit which the la-\nbourer gives to the capitalist, one may regard the method of many English coal-mine\n owners, according to which the labourer is paid only at the end of the month,\n and in the meantime receives advances from the capitalist, often in commodities,\n for which he must pay above their market price (the truck system). “It is a common\n practice with the coal masters to pay once a month, and advance cash to their\n workmen at the end of each intermediate week. The cash is given in the shop (that\n is, the tommy shop or the grocer’s shop belonging to the master himself); the men\n take it on one side and lay it out on the other.” (“Childrens’ Employmen\n Commission, III. Report. Lond. 1864”, p. 38, n.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0074"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0141","text":"192.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"140\", \"facs\": \"#f0159\"} --> sumption of labour-power, like the consumption of every other commodity,\ntakes place outside the market or the sphere of circu-\nlation. We therefore leave this noisy sphere, dwelling upon the surface and accessible to all\n eyes, together with the money-owner and the owner of labour-power, in order to follow both into the hid-\nden abode of production, upon whose threshold stands written:\nNo admittance except on business. Here it will be shown,\nnot only how capital produces, but also how capital itself\nis produced. The secret of surplus-value-\nmaking must at last disclose itself.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0074"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0142","text":"The sphere of circulation or of commodity ex-\nchange, within whose bounds the purchase and sale of labour-power\nmoves, was in fact a veritable Eden of the innate\nrights of man. What alone reigns here is Freedom, Equal-\nity, Property, and Bentham. Freedom! for buyer and\nseller of a commodity, for example of labour-power, are determined only by their\nfree will. They contract as free, legally equal\npersons. The contract is the free product in which their\nwills give themselves a common legal expression. Equality!\nFor they relate to one another only as owners of commodities and\nexchange equivalent for equivalent. Property! For each dis-\nposes only of what is his own. Bentham! For each of the two is\nconcerned only with himself.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0075"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202242296669Z_f74dc862:DISPLAY-0143","text":"The sole power which brings them together and into a\nrelation is that of their self-interest, their particular advantage,\ntheir private interests. And precisely because each looks only to himself and\nnone to the other, all accomplish, as a consequence of a pre-estab-\nlished harmony of things, or under the auspices of an all-\nclever Providence, only the work of their mutual advantage, of the com-\nmon utility, of the general interest.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0075"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0076","text":"On leaving this sphere of simple circulation or of\ncommodity exchange, from which the vulgar free-trader derives his views, con-\ncepts, and standard for his judgment upon the society of capital and\nwage-labour, the physiognomy of our dramatis personae is transformed, it seems,\nalready somewhat. The former money-own-\ner advances as capitalist, the owner of labour-power follows him\nas his labourer; the one significantly smirking and full of busi-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"141\", \"facs\": \"#f0160\"} --> ness zeal, the other timid and reluctant, like someone who has carried his own\n skin to market and now has nothing else to expect but\n— the tannery.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_ZKDV:DISPLAY-0076"]}],"source_digest":"69d5efedcead43d0306e0b98f2e7e16caac5bc696df74385ebb94f624b6c6551","source_language_code":"de"}]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV","title":"1) Arbeitsprozess und Verwerthungsprozess.","order":5,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_1AUV","text":"## Drittes Kapitel.\n Die Produktion des absoluten Mehrwerths.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## 1) Arbeitsprozess und Verwerthungsprozess.\n\n\n\n\nDer Gebrauch der Arbeitskraft ist die Arbeit selbst. Der Käu-\n fer der Arbeitskraft konsumirt sie, indem er ihren Verkäufer arbeiten\n lässt. Letztrer wird hierdurch actu sich bethätigende Arbeitskraft, Ar-\n beiter, was er früher nur potentia war. Um seine Arbeit in Waaren\n darzustellen, muss er sie vor allem in Gebrauchswerthen darstellen,\nSachen, die zur Befriedigung von Bedürfnissen irgend einer Art dienen.\n Es ist also ein besondrer Gebrauchswerth, ein bestimmter Artikel, den\n der Kapitalist vom Arbeiter anfertigen lässt. Die Produktion von\n Gebrauchswerthen, oder Gütern, ändert ihre allgemeine Na-\n tur nicht dadurch, dass sie für den Kapitalisten und unter seiner Kontrole\n vorgeht. Der Arbeitsprozess ist daher zunächst in seinen abstrak-\n ten Momenten, unabhängig von jeder bestimmten gesellschaft-\n lichen Form zu betrachten.\n\n\n\n\nDer Arbeitsprozess ist zunächst ein Prozess zwischen dem Menschen\n und der Natur, ein Prozess, worin er seinen Stoffwechsel mit der Natur\n durch seine eigne That vermittelt, regelt und kontrolirt. Der Mensch tritt\n dem Naturstoff selbst als eine Naturmacht gegenüber. Die seiner Leiblich-\n keit angehörigen Naturkräfte, Arme und Beine, Kopf und Hand, setzt er in\n Bewegung, um sich den Naturstoff in einer für sein eignes Leben brauch-\n baren Form zu assimiliren. Indem er durch diese Bewegung auf die Natur\n ausser ihm wirkt und sie verändert, verändert er zugleich seine eigne Natur.\n Er entwickelt die in ihr schlummernden Potenzen und unterwirft das Spiel\n ihrer Kräfte seiner eignen Botmässigkeit. Wir haben es hier nicht mit\n den ersten thierartig instinktmässigen Formen des Arbeitsprozesses zu\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"142\", \"facs\": \"#f0161\"} --> thun. Dem Zustand, worin der Arbeiter als Verkäufer seiner eignen Arbeits-\n kraft auf dem Waarenmarkt auftritt, ist in urzeitlichen Hintergrund der Zu-\n stand entrückt, worin der menschliche Arbeitsprozess seine erste instinktartige\n Form noch nicht abgestreift hatte. Wir unterstellen den Arbeitsprozess\n in einer Form, worin er dem Menschen ausschliesslich angehört. Eine\n Spinne verrichtet Operationen, die denen des Webers ähneln, und eine\n Biene beschämt durch den Bau ihrer Wachszellen manchen menschlichen\n Baumeister. Was aber von vorn herein den schlechtesten Baumeister vor\n der besten Biene auszeichnet, ist, dass er die Zelle in seinem Kopf ge-\n baut hat, bevor er sie in Wachs baut. Am Ende des Arbeitsprozesses\n kommt ein Resultat heraus, das beim Beginn desselben schon in der Vor-\n stellung des Arbeiters, also schon ideell vorhanden war. Nicht\n dass er nur eine Formveränderung des Natürlichen bewirkt, verwirk-\n licht er im Natürlichen zugleich seinen Zweck, den er weiss, der\n die Art und Weise seines Thuns als Gesetz bestimmt und dem er seinen\n Willen unterordnen muss. Und diese Unterordnung ist kein vereinzelter\n Akt. Ausser der Anstrengung der Organe, die arbeiten, ist der zweck-\n gemässe Wille, der sich als Aufmerksamkeit äussert, für die\n ganze Dauer des Arbeitsprozesses erheischt, und um so mehr, je weniger\n die Arbeit durch ihren eignen Inhalt und ihre Art und Weise der Ausfüh-\n rung den Arbeiter mit sich fortreisst, je weniger er sie daher als Spiel\n seiner eignen körperlichen und geistigen Kräfte geniesst.\n\n\n\n\nDie einfachen Momente des Arbeitsprozesses sind die zweck-\n mässige Thätigkeit oder die Arbeit selbst, ihr Gegen-\n stand und ihr Mittel.\n\n\n\n\nDie Erde (worunter ökonomisch auch das Wasser einbegriffen),\n wie sie den Menschen ursprünglich mit Proviant, fertigen Lebensmitteln\n ausrüstet [Note: „The earth’s spontaneous productions being in small quantity, and quite\n independent of man, appear as it were, to be furnished by nature, in the same way\n as a small sum is given to a young man, in order to put him in a way of industry,\n and of making his fortune.“ (James Steuart: Principles of Polit.\n Econ. edit. Dublin 1770, v. I, p. 116.)] , findet sich ohne sein Zuthun als der allgemeine Gegen-\n stand der menschlichen Arbeit vor. Alle Dinge, welche die Arbeit nur\n von ihrem unmittelbaren Zusammenhang mit dem Erdganzen loslöst, sind\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"143\", \"facs\": \"#f0162\"} --> von Natur vorgefundne Arbeitsgegenstände. So der Fisch, der von seinem\n Lebenselement, dem Wasser, getrennt, gefangen wird, das Holz, das im\n Urwald gefällt, das Erz, das aus seiner Ader losgebrochen wird. Ist der\n Arbeitsgegenstand dagegen selbst schon sozusagen durch frühere\n Arbeit filtrirt, so nennen wir ihn Rohmaterial. Z. B. das bereits\n losgebrochene Erz, das nun ausgewaschen wird. Alles Rohmaterial ist\n Arbeitsgegenstand, aber nicht jeder Arbeitsgegenstand ist Rohmaterial.\n Rohmaterial ist der Arbeitsgegenstand nur, sobald er bereits eine durch\n Arbeit vermittelte Veränderung erfahren hat.\n\n\n\n\nDas Arbeitsmittel ist ein Ding oder ein Komplex von Dingen,\n die der Arbeiter zwischen sich und den Arbeitsgegenstand schiebt und die\n ihm als Leiter seiner Thätigkeit auf diesen Gegenstand dienen. Er be-\n nutzt die mechanischen, physischen, chemischen Eigenschaften der Dinge,\n um sie als Machtmittel auf andre Dinge, seinem Zweck gemäss,\n wirken zu lassen [Note: „Die Vernunft ist eben so listig als mächtig. Die List besteht über-\n haupt in der vermittelnden Thätigkeit, welche, indem sie die Objekte ihrer eigenen\n Natur gemäss auf einander einwirken und sich an einander abarbeiten lässt, ohne\n sich unmittelbar in diesen Prozess einzumischen, gleichwohl nur ihren Zweck zur\n Ausführung bringt.“ (Hegel: Encyklopädie. Erster Theil: Die Logik.\n Berlin 1840, p. 382.)] . Der Gegenstand, dessen sich der Arbeiter un-\n mittelbar bemächtigt — abgesehn von der Ergreifung fertiger Lebens-\n mittel, der Früchte z. B., wobei seine eigenen Leibesorgane allein als\n Arbeitsmittel dienen — ist nicht der Arbeitsgegenstand, sondern das\n Arbeitsmittel. So wird das Natürliche selbst zum Organ seiner Thätig-\n keit, ein Organ, das er seinen eigenen Leibesorganen hinzufügt, seine\n natürliche Gestalt verlängernd, trotz der Bibel. Wie die Erde seine ur-\n sprüngliche Proviantkammer, ist sie sein ursprüngliches Arsenal von\n Arbeitsmitteln. Sie liefert ihm z. B. den Stein, womit er wirft, reibt,\n drückt, schneidet u. s. w. Die Erde selbst ist ein Arbeitsmittel, setzt\n jedoch zu ihrem Dienst als Arbeitsmittel in der Agrikultur wieder eine\n ganze Reihe andrer Arbeitsmittel und eine schon relativ hohe Entwicklung\n der Arbeitskraft voraus [Note: In der sonst elenden Schrift: „Théorie de l’Écon. Polit. Paris\n 1815“, zählt Ganilh den Physiokraten gegenüber treffend die grosse Reihe von\n Arbeitsprozessen auf, welche Voraussetzungen der eigentlichen Agrikultur\n bilden.] . Sobald überhaupt der Arbeitsprozess nur\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"144\", \"facs\": \"#f0163\"} --> einigermassen entwickelt ist, bedarf er bereits bearbeiteter Arbeitsmittel.\n In den ältesten Menschenhöhlen finden wir Steinwerkzeuge und Steinwaffen.\n Neben bearbeitetem Stein und Holz spielt im Anfang der Menschenge-\n schichte das gezähmte, also selbst schon durch Arbeit veränderte, ge-\n züchtete Thier die Hauptrolle als Arbeitsmittel [Note: In den oben citirten „Réflexions“ entwickelt Turgot gut die Wich-\n tigkeit des gezähmten Thiers für die Anfänge der Kultur.] . Der Gebrauch und\n die Schöpfung von Arbeitsmitteln, obgleich im Keim schon gewissen Thier-\n arten eigen, charakterisiren den spezifisch menschlichen Arbeits-\n prozess und Franklin definirt daher den Menschen als „a tool-\n making animal“, ein Werkzeuge fabrizirendes Thier. Dieselbe\n Wichtigkeit, die der Bau von Knochenreliquien für die Erkenntniss der\n Organisation untergegangner Thiergeschlechter, haben Reliquien von Ar-\n beitsmitteln für die Beurtheilung untergegangner ökonomischer Ge-\n sellschaftsformationen. Nicht was gemacht wird, sondern wie, mit\n welchen Arbeitsmitteln gemacht wird, unterscheidet die ökonomischen\n Epochen [Note: Von allen Waaren sind eigentliche Luxuswaaren die unbedeutendsten\n für die technologische Vergleichung verschiedner Produktionsepochen.] . Die Arbeitsmittel sind nicht nur der Gradmesser der Ent-\n wicklung der menschlichen Arbeitskraft, sondern auch der Index der ge-\n sellschaftlichen Verhältnisse, unter denen gearbeitet wird. Unter den\n Arbeitsmitteln selbst bieten die mechanischen Arbeitsmittel,\n deren Gesammtheit man das Knochen- und Muskelsystem der\n Produktion nennen kann, viel entscheidendere Charaktermerkmale\n einer gesellschaftlichen Produktionsepoche, als solche Arbeitsmittel, die\n nur zu Behältern des Arbeitsgegenstands dienen, und deren Gesammtheit\n ganz allgemein als das Gefässsystem der Produktion bezeichnet\n werden kann, wie z. B. Röhren, Fässer, Körbe, Krüge u. s. w. Erst in\n der chemischen Fabrikation spielen sie eine bedeutungsvolle Rolle.\n\n\n\n\nIm weiteren Sinn zählt der Arbeitsprozess unter seine Mittel ausser\n den Dingen, welche die Wirkung der Arbeit auf ihren Gegenstand ver-\n mitteln, und daher in einer oder der andern Weise als Leiter der Thätig-\n keit dienen, alle gegenständlichen Bedingungen, die überhaupt\n erheischt sind, damit der ganze Prozess vorgehe. Sie gehn nicht direkt\n in den Arbeitsprozess ein, aber der Arbeitsprozess kann ohne sie gar nicht\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"145\", \"facs\": \"#f0164\"} --> oder nur unvollkommen vorgehn. Das allgemeine Arbeitsmittel dieser Art\n ist wieder die Erde selbst, denn sie giebt dem Arbeiter den locus\n standi und seinem Prozess den Wirkungsraum (field of em-\n ployment). Durch die Arbeit schon vermittelte Arbeitsmittel dieser\n Art sind z. B. Arbeitsgebäude, Kanäle, Strassen u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nDer Arbeitsprozess ist also ein Prozess, worin die Thätigkeit\n des Menschen durch das Arbeitsmittel eine von vorn herein bezweckte Ver-\n änderung im Arbeitsgegenstand bewirkt. Dieser Prozess erlischt im\nProdukt. Sein Produkt ist ein Gebrauchswerth, ein durch Form-\n veränderung menschlichen Bedürfnissen assimilirter Naturstoff. Durch\n den Prozess hat sich die Arbeit mit ihrem Gegenstand verbunden. Die\n Arbeit ist vergegenständlicht und der Gegenstand ist verarbeitet. Was\n auf Seiten des Arbeiters in der Form der Unruhe erschien, erscheint nun als\n ruhende Eigenschaft, in der Form des Seins, auf Seiten des Produkts. Er\n hat gesponnen und das Produkt ist ein Gespinnst.\n\n\n\n\nBetrachtet man den ganzen Prozess vom Standpunkt seines Resultats,\ndes Produkts, so erscheinen beide, Arbeitsmittel und Arbeits-\n gegenstand, als Produktionsmittel [Note: Es scheint paradox z. B. den Fisch, der noch nicht gefangen ist, ein Pro-\n duktionsmittel für den Fischfang zu nennen. Bisher ist aber noch nicht die\n Kunst erfunden, Fische in Gewässern zu fangen, in denen sie sich nicht vorfinden.]  und die Arbeit selbst als\nproduktive Arbeit [Note: Diese Bestimmung produktiver Arbeit, wie sie sich vom Stand-\n punkt des einfachen Arbeitsprozesses ergiebt, reicht keineswegs hin für den kapi-\n talistischen Produktionsprozess.] .\n\n\n\n\nWenn ein Gebrauchswerth als Produkt aus dem Arbeitsprozess\n herauskommt, gehn andre Gebrauchswerthe, selbst Produkte früherer\n Arbeitsprozesse, als Produktionsmittel in den Prozess ein. Der-\n selbe Gebrauchswerth, der das Produkt eines Arbeitsprozesses, bildet das\n Produktionsmittel eines andern. Produkte sind daher nicht nur Resul-\n tat, sondern zugleich Bedingung des Arbeitsprozesses.\n\n\n\n\nMit Ausnahme der extraktiven Industrie, die ihren Arbeits-\n gegenstand von Natur vorfindet, wie der Bergbau, die Jagd, der Fischfang\n u. s. w., (der Ackerbau nur, soweit er in erster Instanz die jungfräuliche\n Erde selbst aufbricht), behandeln alle Industriezweige einen Gegen-\n stand, der Rohmaterial, d. h. bereits durch die Arbeit filtrirter\n [Running matter: I. 10] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"146\", \"facs\": \"#f0165\"} --> Arbeitsgegenstand, selbst schon Produkt eines früheren Arbeitsprozesses\n ist. So z. B. der Samen in der Agrikultur. Thiere und Pflanzen, die\n man als Naturprodukte zu betrachten pflegt, sind nicht nur Produkte viel-\n leicht der Arbeit vom vorigen Jahr, sondern, in ihren jetzigen Formen,\n Produkte einer durch viele Generationen, unter menschlicher Kontrole,\n vermittelst menschlicher Arbeit, fortgesetzten Umwandlung. Was aber\n die Arbeitsmittel insbesondere betrifft, so zeigt ihre ungeheure Mehr-\n zahl dem oberflächlichsten Blick die Spur vergangner Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nDas Rohmaterial kann die Hauptsubstanz eines Produkts bilden, oder\nnur als Hilfsstoff in seine Bildung eingehn. Der Hilfsstoff wird vom\n Arbeitsmittel konsumirt, wie Kohle von einer Dampfmaschine,\n Oel vom Rade, Heu von einem Zugpferd, oder dem Rohmaterial zu-\n gesetzt, um eine stoffliche Veränderung darin zu bewirken, wie Chlor\n zur ungebleichten Leinwand, Kohle zum Eisen, Farbe zur Wolle, oder er\n unterstützt die Verrichtung der Arbeit selbst, wie z. B. zur Beleuchtung\n und Heizung des Arbeitslokals verwandte Stoffe. Der Unterschied zwischen\n Hauptstoff und Hilfsstoff verschwimmt in der eigentlich chemischen Fabri-\n kation, weil keines der angewandten Rohmaterialien als die Substanz des\n Produkts wieder erscheint [Note: Storch unterscheidet das eigentliche Rohmaterial als „matière“ von\n den Hilfsstoffen als „matériaux“: Cherbuliez bezeichnet die Hilfsstoffe als\n „matières instrumentales.“] .\n\n\n\n\nDa jedes Ding vielerlei Eigenschaften besitzt und daher verschiedner\n Nutzanwendung fähig ist, kann dasselbe Produkt das Rohmaterial sehr\n verschiedner Arbeitsprozesse bilden. Korn z. B. ist Rohmaterial für\n Müller, Stärkefabrikant, Destillateur, Viehzüchter u. s. w. Es wird Roh-\n material seiner eignen Produktion als Samen. So geht die Kohle als\n Produkt aus der Minenindustrie hervor und als Produktionsmittel in sie ein.\n\n\n\n\nDasselbe Produkt mag in demselben Arbeitsprozess als Arbeitsmittel\n und Rohmaterial dienen. Bei der Viehmast z. B., wo das Vieh, das be-\n arbeitete Rohmaterial, zugleich Mittel der Düngerbereitung ist.\n\n\n\n\nEin Produkt kann in einer für die Konsumtion fertigen Form\n existiren und dennoch von neuem zum Rohmaterial eines andern Produkts\n werden, wie die Traube zum Rohmaterial des Weins. Oder der Arbeits-\n prozess entlässt sein Produkt in Formen, worin es nur als Rohmaterial\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"147\", \"facs\": \"#f0166\"} --> eines nachfolgenden Arbeitsprozesses benutzt werden kann. Rohmaterial\n in diesem Zustand heisst Halbfabrikat und hiesse besser Stufen-\n fabrikat, wie z. B. Baumwolle, Faden, Garn u. s. w. Obgleich selbst\n schon Produkt, mag das ursprüngliche Rohmaterial eine ganze Staffel ver-\n schiedner Arbeitsprozesse zu durchlaufen haben, worin es in stets ver-\n änderter Gestalt stets von neuem als Rohmaterial funktionirt bis zum letzten\n Arbeitsprozess, der es als fertiges Lebensmittel oder fertiges Ar-\n beitsmittel von sich abstösst.\n\n\n\n\nMan sieht: ob ein Gebrauchswerth als Rohmaterial,\n Arbeitsmittel oder Produkt erscheint, hängt ganz und gar ab von\n seiner bestimmten Funktion im Arbeitsprozesse, von der\n Stelle, die er in ihm einnimmt, und mit dem Wechsel dieser Stelle\n wechseln jene Bestimmungen.\n\n\n\n\nDurch ihren Eintritt als Produktionsmittel in neue Arbeits-\n prozesse verlieren Produkte daher den Charakter des Produkts. Sie\n funktioniren nur noch als gegenständliche Faktoren der lebendigen Arbeit.\n Der Spinner bezieht sich auf die Spindel nur als Mittel, womit, und auf\n den Flachs nur als Gegenstand, den er spinnt. Allerdings kann man nicht\n spinnen ohne Spinnmaterial und Spindel. Das Vorhandensein dieser Pro-\n dukte ist daher vorausgesetzt beim Beginn des wirklichen Spinnprozesses.\nIn diesem Prozess selbst aber ist es eben so gleichgültig, dass\n Flachs und Spindel Produkte vergangner Arbeit sind, wie es im\n wirklichen Ernährungsprozess gleichgültig ist, dass Brod das Produkt\n der vergangnen Arbeiten von Bauer, Müller, Bäcker u. s. w. Umgekehrt.\n Machen Produktionsmittel im Arbeitsprozess ihren Charakter als Produkte\n vergangner Arbeit geltend, so durch ihre Mängel. Ein Messer, das nicht\n schneidet, Garn, das beständig zerreisst u. s. w., erinnern lebhaft an Messer-\n schmied A und Garnwichser E. Im gelungenen Produkt ist die Ver-\n mittlung seiner Gebrauchseigenschaften durch vergangne Arbeit ausge-\n löscht.\n\n\n\n\nEine Maschine, die nicht im Arbeitsprozess dient, ist nutzlos. Ausser-\n dem verfällt sie der zerstörenden Gewalt des natürlichen Stoffwechsels.\n Das Eisen verrostet, das Holz verfault. Garn, das nicht verwebt oder\n verstrickt wird, ist verdorbene Baumwolle. Die lebendige Arbeit muss\n diese Dinge ergreifen, sie von den Todten erwecken, sie aus nur möglichen\n in wirkliche und wirkende Gebrauchswerthe verwandeln. Vom Feuer der\n [Running matter: 10*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"148\", \"facs\": \"#f0167\"} --> Arbeit beleckt, als Leiber derselben angeeignet, zu ihren begriffs - und\n berufsmässigen Funktionen im Prozess begeistet, werden sie zwar auch\n verzehrt, aber zweckvoll, als Bildungselemente neuer Gebrauchswerthe.\n\n\n\n\nWenn vorhandne Produkte nicht nur die Resultate, sondern\n auch die Existenzbedingungen des Arbeitsprozesses sind, ist\n andrerseits ihr Hineinwerfen in den Arbeitsprozess, also ihr Kontakt\n mit lebendiger Arbeit, das einzige Mittel um diese Produkte\n vergangner Arbeit als Gebrauchswerthe zu erhalten und zu\n verwirklichen. Der Arbeitsprozess resultirt überhaupt nur in Ge-\n brauchswerth, so weit sein Produkt fähig, als Lebensmittel in\n die individuelle Konsumtion oder als Produktionsmittel in neuen\n Arbeitsprozess einzugehn.\n\n\n\n\nIm Arbeitsprozess werden seine stofflichen Elemente, sein Gegenstand\n und sein Mittel, als Gebrauchswerthe verbraucht. Die Arbeit ver-\n speist sie. Der Arbeitsprozess ist also Konsumtionsprozess. Es\n unterscheidet diese produktive Konsumtion von der indivi-\n duellen Konsumtion, dass letztere die Produkte als Lebensmittel\n des lebendigen Individuums, jene sie als Lebensmittel der Arbeit,\n der sich bethätigenden Arbeitskraft, verzehrt. Das Produkt der indivi-\n duellen Konsumtion ist daher der Konsument selbst, das Resultat\n der produktiven Konsumtion ein von dem Konsumenten unterschiednes\nProdukt.\n\n\n\n\nSofern Arbeitsmittel und Arbeitsgegenstand selbst schon Produkte\n sind, Resultate vergangner Arbeitsprozesse, ist der Arbeitsprozess ein Pro-\n zess, worin Produkte verzehrt werden um Produkte zu\n schaffen oder worin Produkte als Produktionsmittel von\n Produkten dienen. Wie der Arbeitsprozess aber ursprünglich nur\n zwischen dem Menschen und der ohne sein Zuthun vorhandenen Erde\n vorgeht, dienen in ihm immer noch solche Produktionsmittel, die von\n Natur vorhanden, keine Verbindung von Naturstoff und menschlicher\n Arbeit darstellen.\n\n\n\n\nDer Arbeitsprozess, wie wir ihn in seinen einfachen und ab-\n strakten Momenten dargestellt haben, ist zweckmässige Thätigkeit zur\n Herstellung von Gebrauchswerthen, Aneignung des Natürlichen für mensch-\n liche Bedürfnisse, allgemeine Bedingung des Stoffwechsels zwischen Mensch\n und Natur, ewige Naturbedingung des menschlichen Lebens und daher\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"149\", \"facs\": \"#f0168\"} --> unabhängig von jeder Form dieses Lebens, vielmehr allen seinen Gesell-\n schaftsformen gleich gemeinsam. Wir hatten daher bei unsrer Darstellung\n nicht nöthig, den Arbeiter im Verhältniss zu andern Arbeitern darzustellen.\n Der Mensch und seine Arbeit auf der einen, die Natur und ihre Stoffe auf\n der andern Seite, genügten. So wenig man dem Weizen anschmeckt, wer\n ihn gebaut hat, so wenig sieht man diesem Prozess an, unter welchen Be-\n dingungen er vorgeht, ob unter der brutalen Peitsche des Sklavenaufsehers\n oder unter dem ängstlichen Auge des Kapitalisten, ob Cincinnatus ihn ver-\n richtet in der Bestellung seiner paar jugera, oder der Wilde, der mit einem\n Stein eine Bestie erlegt [Note: Aus diesem höchst logischen Grund entdeckt wohl Oberst Torrens in\n dem Stein des Wilden — den Ursprung des Kapitals. „In dem ersten\nStein, den der Wilde auf die Bestie wirft, die er verfolgt, in dem ersten Stock,\n den er ergreift, um die Frucht niederzuziehn, die er nicht mit den Händen fassen\n kann, sehn wir die Aneignung eines Artikels zum Zweck der Erwerbung eines andern\nund entdecken so — den Ursprung des Kapitals.“ (R. Torrens: An\n Essay on the Production of Wealth etc. p. 79.) Aus jenem ersten Stock\n ist wahrscheinlich auch zu erklären, warum stock im Englischen synonym mit\nKapital ist.] .\n\n\n\n\nKehren wir zu unserem Kapitalisten in spe zurück. Wir ver-\n liessen ihn, nachdem er auf dem Waarenmarkt alle zu einem Arbeitsprozess\n nothwendigen Faktoren gekauft hatte, die gegenständlichen Fak-\n toren in den Produktionsmitteln, den subjektiven Fak-\n tor in der Arbeitskraft. Die bestimmte Art dieser Produktions-\n mittel und dieser Arbeitskraft richtet sich nach der Art des Arbeitsprozesses,\n wofür sie bestimmt sind, ob Stiefel, Garn u. s. w. gemacht werden sollen.\n Unser Kapitalist setzt sich also daran, die von ihm gekaufte Waare, die\n Arbeitskraft, zu konsumiren, d. h. den Träger der Arbeitskraft, den\n Arbeiter die Produktionsmittel durch seine Arbeit konsumiren zu\n lassen. Die allgemeine Natur des Arbeitsprozesses ändert sich\n natürlich nicht dadurch, dass der Arbeiter ihn für den Kapitalisten, statt\n für sich selbst verrichtet. Aber auch die bestimmte Art und\n Weise wie Stiefel gemacht oder Garn gesponnen wird, kann sich zu-\n nächst nicht ändern durch die Dazwischenkunft des Kapitalisten. Er muss\n die Arbeitskraft zunächst nehmen, wie er sie auf dem Markt vorfindet, also\n auch ihre Arbeit, wie sie in einer Periode entsprang, wo es noch keine\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"150\", \"facs\": \"#f0169\"} --> Kapitalisten gab. Die Verwandlung der Produktionsweise\n selbst durch die Unterordnung der Arbeit unter das Kapital kann sich erst\n später ereignen und ist daher erst später zu betrachten.\n\n\n\n\nDer Arbeitsprozess, wie er als Konsumtionsprozess der\n Arbeitskraft durch den Kapitalisten vorgeht, zeigt nur zwei\n eigenthümliche Phänomene.\n\n\n\n\nDer Arbeiter arbeitet unter der Kontrole des Kapita-\n listen, dem seine Arbeit gehört. Der Kapitalist passt auf, dass die\n Arbeit ordentlich verrichtet wird und dass die Produktionsmittel zweckge-\n mäss verwandt werden, also kein Rohmaterial vergeudet und das Arbeits-\n instrument geschont, d. h. nur so weit zerstört wird, als unzertrennlich ist\n von seinem Gebrauch in der Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nZweitens aber: das Produkt ist Eigenthum des Kapita-\n listen, nicht des unmittelbaren Produzenten, des Arbeiters. Der Kapi-\n talist zahlt z. B. den Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft. Ihr Ge-\n brauch, wie der jeder andern Waare, z. B. eines Pferdes, das er für einen\n Tag gemiethet, gehört ihm also für den Tag. Dem Käufer der Waare\n gehört der Gebrauch der Waare, und der Besitzer der Arbeitskraft giebt\n in der That nur den von ihm verkauften Gebrauchswerth, indem er seine\n Arbeit giebt. Von dem Augenblicke, wo er in die Werkstätte des Ka-\n pitalisten trat, gehörte der Gebrauchswerth seiner Arbeitskraft, also\n ihr Gebrauch, die Arbeit, dem Kapitalisten. Der Kapitalist hat durch\n den Kauf der Arbeitskraft die Arbeit selbst als lebendigen Gäh-\n rungsstoff den todten von ihm gleichfalls besessenen Bildungselementen des\n Produkts einverleibt. Von seinem Standpunkt ist der Arbeits-\n prozess nur die Konsumtion der von ihm gekauften Waare Arbeits-\n kraft, die er jedoch nur konsumiren kann, indem er ihr Produktions-\n mittel zusetzt. Der Arbeitsprozess ist ein Prozess zwischen Dingen,\n die der Kapitalist gekauft hat, zwischen ihm gehörigen\n Dingen. Das Produkt dieses Prozesses gehört ihm daher\n ganz eben so sehr als das Produkt des Gährungsprozesses in seinem\n Weinkeller [Note: „Die Produkte sind appropriirt, bevor sie in Kapital verwandelt werden;\n diese Verwandlung entzieht sie nicht jener Appropriation.“ (Cherbuliez:\n Riche ou Pauvre, edit. Paris 1841, p. 53, 54.) „Indem der Proletarier] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"151\", \"facs\": \"#f0170\"} -->\n\nDas Produkt — das Eigenthum des Kapitalisten — ist ein Ge-\n brauchswerth, Garn, Stiefel u. s. w. Aber obgleich Stiefel z. B. ge-\n wissermassen die Basis des gesellschaftlichen Fortschritts bilden und unser\n Kapitalist ein entschiedener Fortschrittsmann ist, fabrizirt er die Stiefel\n nicht ihrer selbst wegen. Der Gebrauchswerth ist überhaupt nicht\n das Ding „qu’on aime pour lui même“ in der Waarenpro-\n duktion. Gebrauchswerthe werden hier überhaupt nur produzirt, weil\n und sofern sie materielles Substrat, Träger des Tausch-\n werths sind. Und unserem Kapitalisten handelt es sich um zweierlei.\nErstens will er einen Gebrauchswerth produziren, der einen Tausch-\n werth hat, einen zum Verkauf bestimmten Artikel, eine Waare. Und\n zweitens will er eine Waare produziren, deren Werth höher als die\nWerthsumme der zu ihrer Produktion erheischten Waa-\n ren, der Produktionsmittel und der Arbeitskraft, für die er sein gutes Geld\n auf dem Waarenmarkt vorschoss. Er will nicht nur einen Ge-\n brauchswerth produziren, sondern eine Waare, nicht nur Ge-\n brauchswerth, sondern Tauschwerth, und nicht nur Werth, sondern auch\nMehrwerth.\n\n\n\n\nIn der That, da es sich hier um Waarenproduktion handelt,\n haben wir bisher offenbar nur eine Seite des Prozesses betrachtet.\n Wie die Waare selbst Einheit von Gebrauchswerth und Tausch-\n werth, muss ihr Produktionsprozess Einheit von Arbeits-\n prozess und Werthbildungsprozess sein.\n\n\n\n\n [Note: seine Arbeit gegen ein bestimmtes Quantum Lebensmittel (approvisionnement)\n verkauft, verzichtet er vollständig auf jeden Antheil am Produkt. Die Appro-\n priation der Produkte bleibt dieselbe wie vorher; sie ist in keiner Weise durch die\n erwähnte Konvention verändert. Das Produkt gehört ausschliesslich dem Kapi-\n talisten, der die Rohstoffe und das Approvisionnement geliefert hat. Es ist diess\n eine strenge Konsequenz des Gesetzes der Appropriation, dessen Fundamental-\n prinzip umgekehrt das ausschliessliche Eigenthumsrecht jedes Arbeiters an\n seinem Produkte war.“ (l. c. p. 58.) James Mill: Elements of Pol.\n Econ. etc. p. 75: „Wenn die Arbeiter für Arbeitslohn arbeiten, ist der Kapi-\n talist Eigenthümer nicht nur des Kapitals (meint hier die Produktionsmittel),\n sondern auch der Arbeit (of labour also). Wenn man das, was für Arbeitslohn\n gezahlt wird, wie diess gebräuchlich, in den Begriff Kapital einschliesst, ist es\nabgeschmackt von der Arbeit getrennt vom Kapital zu sprechen.\n Das Wort Kapital in diesem Sinn schliesst beides ein, Kapital und Arbeit.“] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"152\", \"facs\": \"#f0171\"} -->\n\nBetrachten wir den Produktionsprozess also jetzt auch als\nWerthbildungsprozess.\n\n\n\n\nWir wissen, dass der Werth jeder Waare bestimmt ist durch das\n Quantum der in ihrem Gebrauchswerth materialisirten Arbeit,\n durch die zu ihrer Produktion gesellschaftlich nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit. Diess gilt auch für das Produkt, das sich unsrem Kapi-\n talisten als Resultat des Arbeitsprozesses ergab. Es ist also zunächst die\n in diesem Produkt vergegenständlichte Arbeit zu be-\n rechnen.\n\n\n\n\nEs sei z. B. Garn.\n\n\n\n\nZur Herstellung des Garns war zuerst sein Rohmaterial nöthig,\n z. B. 10 Pfund Baumwolle. Was der Werth der Baumwolle, ist nicht\n erst zu untersuchen, denn der Kapitalist hat sie auf dem Markt zu ihrem\n Werth, z. B. zu 10 sh. gekauft. In dem Preise der Baumwolle ist die\n zu ihrer Produktion erheischte Arbeit schon als allgemeine gesellschaftliche\n Arbeit dargestellt. Wir wollen ferner annehmen, dass die in der Verar-\n beitung der Baumwolle verzehrte Spindelmasse, die uns alle an-\n deren aufgewandten Arbeitsmittel repräsentirt, einen Werth von 2 sh. be-\n sitzt. Ist eine Goldmasse von 12 sh. das Produkt von 24 Arbeitsstunden\n oder zwei Arbeitstagen, so folgt zunächst, dass im Garn zwei Arbeitstage\n vergegenständlicht sind.\n\n\n\n\nDer Umstand, dass die Baumwolle ihre Form verändert hat und die\n aufgezehrte Spindelmasse ganz verschwunden ist, darf nicht beirren. Nach\n dem allgemeinen Werthgesetz sind z. B. 10 lbs. Garn ein Aequivalent für 10\n lbs. Baumwolle und ¼ Spindel, wenn der Werth von 40 lbs. Garn = dem\n Werth von 40 lbs. Baumwolle + dem Werth einer ganzen Spindel, d. h.\n wenn dieselbe Arbeitszeit erfordert ist um beide Seiten dieser\n Gleichung zu produziren. In diesem Fall stellt sich dieselbe Arbeits-\n zeit das einemal in dem Gebrauchswerth Garn, das andremal in den Ge-\n brauchswerthen Baumwolle und Spindel dar. Der Tauschwerth ist also\n gleichgültig dagegen, ob er in Garn, Spindel oder Baumwolle erscheint.\n Dass Spindel und Baumwolle, statt ruhig neben einander zu liegen, im\n Spinnprozesse eine Verbindung eingehn, welche die Form ihres Gebrauchs-\n werths verändert, sie in Garn verwandelt, berührt ihren Tausch-\n werth eben so wenig, als wenn sie durch einfachen Austausch gegen\n ein Aequivalent von Garn umgesetzt worden wären.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"153\", \"facs\": \"#f0172\"} -->\n\nDie zur Produktion der Baumwolle erheischte Arbeitszeit ist Theil\n der zur Produktion des Garns, dessen Rohmaterial sie bildet, erheischten\n Arbeitszeit und deshalb im Garn enthalten. Ebenso verhält es sich mit\n der Arbeitszeit, die zur Produktion der Spindelmasse erheischt ist, ohne\n deren Verschleiss oder Konsum die Baumwolle nicht versponnen werden\n kann [Note: „Not only the labour applied immediately to commodities affect their\n value, but the labour also which is bestowed on the implements, tools, and build-\n ings with which such labour is assisted.“ Ricardo l. c. p. 16.] .\n\n\n\n\nSo weit also der Werth des Garns, die zu seiner Herstellung er-\n heischte Arbeitszeit, in Betracht kommt, können die verschied-\n nen besondern, der Zeit und dem Raum nach getrennten Ar-\n beitsprozesse, die durchlaufen werden müssen, um die Baumwolle zu\n produziren, die vernutzte Spindelmasse zu produziren, endlich aus Baum-\n wolle und Spindel Garn zu machen, als verschiedne successive Phasen\neines und desselben Arbeitsprozesses betrachtet werden.\n Alle im Garn enthaltne Arbeit ist vergangne Arbeit. Dass die zur\n Produktion seiner Bildungselemente erheischte Arbeitszeit früher ver-\n gangen war, im Plusquamperfectum steht, dagegen die zum Schlussprozess,\n dem Spinnen, unmittelbar verwandte Arbeit dem Präsens näher, im Per-\n fectum steht, ist ein durchaus gleichgültiger Umstand. Ist eine bestimmte\n Masse Arbeit, z. B. von 30 Arbeitstagen, zum Bau eines Hauses nöthig,\n so ändert es nichts am Gesammtquantum der dem Hause einverleib-\n ten Arbeitszeit, dass der 30. Arbeitstag 29 Tage später in die Produktion\n einging als der erste Arbeitstag. Und so kann die im Arbeitsmaterial\n und Arbeitsmittel enthaltene Arbeitszeit ganz so betrachtet werden, als\n wäre sie nur in einem früheren Stadium des Spinnprozesses verausgabt\n worden als das zuletzt in der Form des Spinnens verausgabte Arbeits-\n quantum.\n\n\n\n\nDie Werthe der Produktionsmittel, der Baumwolle und der Spindel,\n ausgedrückt in dem Preise von 12 sh., bilden also Bestandtheile des Garn-\n werths, oder des Werths des Produkts.\n\n\n\n\nNur müssen zwei Bedingungen erfüllt sein. Einmal müssen Baum-\n wolle und Spindel wirklich zur Produktion eines Gebrauchswerths\n gedient haben. Es muss in unserm Fall Garn aus ihnen geworden sein.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"154\", \"facs\": \"#f0173\"} -->Welcher Gebrauchswerth ihn trägt, ist dem Tauschwerth gleichgültig,\n aber ein Gebrauchswerth muss ihn tragen. Zweitens ist voraus-\n gesetzt, dass nur die unter den gegebenen gesellschaftlichen Pro-\n duktionsbedingungen nothwendige Arbeitszeit verwandt worden\n ist. Wäre also nur 1 Pfund Baumwolle nöthig, um 1 Pfund Garn zu\n spinnen, so darf nur 1 Pfund Baumwolle verzehrt sein in der Bildung von\n 1 Pfund Garn. Ebenso verhält es sich mit der Spindel. Hätte der Kapi-\n talist die Phantasie mit goldenen, statt mit eisernen Spindeln spinnen zu\n lassen, so zählte im Garnwerth dennoch nur die gesellschaftlich\n nothwendige Arbeitszeit, d. h. die zur Produktion der eisernen\n Spindelmasse nothwendige Arbeitszeit.\n\n\n\n\nWir kennen jetzt den Werththeil, den die Produktionsmittel,\n Baumwolle und Spindel, im Garnwerth bilden. Er ist gleich 12 sh.,\n oder die Materiatur von zwei Arbeitstagen. Es handelt sich also nun um\n den Werththeil, welchen die Arbeit des Spinners selbst der Baum-\n wolle zusetzt.\n\n\n\n\nWir haben diese Arbeit jetzt von einem ganz anderen Gesichtspunkte\n zu betrachten, als während des Arbeitsprozesses. Dort handelte es\n sich um die zweckmässige Thätigkeit, Baumwolle in Garn zu verwandeln.\n Je zweckmässiger die Arbeit, desto besser das Garn, alle andern Umstände\n als gleichbleibend vorausgesetzt. Die Arbeit des Spinners war spezifisch\n verschieden von andern produktiven Arbeiten, und diese Verschiedenheit\n offenbarte sich subjektiv und objektiv, im besondern Zweck des Spinnens,\n seiner besondern Operationsweise, der besondern Natur seiner Produktions-\n mittel, dem spezifischen Gebrauchswerth seines Produkts. Baumwolle und\n Spindel dienen als Lebensmittel der Spinnarbeit, aber man kann\n mit ihnen keine gezogenen Kanonen machen. Sofern die Arbeit des\n Spinners dagegen werthbildend ist, d. h. Quelle von Tausch-\n werth, ist sie durchaus nicht verschieden von der Arbeit des Kanonenboh-\n rers, oder, was uns hier näher liegt, von den Arbeiten, die in den Produk-\n tionsmitteln des Garns verwirklicht sind, den Arbeiten des Baumwollpflanzers\n und des Spindelmachers. Nur wegen dieser Identität können Baum-\n wollpflanzen, Spindelmachen und Spinnen blos quantitativ verschiedene\n Theile desselben Gesammtwerths, des Garnwerths, bilden. Es\n handelt sich hier nicht mehr um die Qualität, die Beschaffenheit und\n den Inhalt der Arbeit, sondern nur noch um ihre Quantität. Diese\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"155\", \"facs\": \"#f0174\"} --> ist einfach zu zählen. Wir nehmen an, dass die Spinnarbeit einfache\n Arbeit, gesellschaftliche Durchschnittsarbeit ist. Man wird später sehn,\n dass die gegentheilige Annahme nichts an der Sache ändern würde.\n\n\n\n\nWährend des Arbeitsprozesses setzt sich die Arbeit beständig aus der\n Form der Unruhe in die des Seins, aus der Form der Bewegung in die der\n Gegenständlichkeit um. Am Ende einer Stunde ist die Spinnbewegung\n in einem gewissen Quantum Garn dargestellt, also ein bestimmtes Quan-\n tum Arbeit, eine Arbeitsstunde in der Baumwolle vergegen-\n ständlicht. Wir sagen Arbeitsstunde, denn die Spinnarbeit gilt hier\n nur, so weit sie Verausgabung von Arbeitskraft, nicht so weit sie die\n spezifische Arbeit des Spinnens ist.\n\n\n\n\nEs ist nun entscheidend wichtig, dass während der Dauer des Pro-\n zesses, d. h. der Verwandlung von Baumwolle in Garn, nur die gesell-\n schaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit verzehrt wird. Müssen\n unter normalen, d. h. durchschnittlichen gesellschaftlichen Produk-\n tionsbedingungen, a Pfund Baumwolle während einer Arbeitsstunde in\n b Pfund Garn verwandelt sein, so gilt nur der Arbeitstag als Arbeitstag\n von 12 Stunden, der 12 × a Pfund Baumwolle in 12 × b Pfund Garn\n verwandelt. Denn nur die gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit zählt\n als werthbildend.\n\n\n\n\nRohmaterial und Produkt erscheinen hier in einem ganz ande-\n ren Licht als vom Standpunkt des eigentlichen Arbeitsprozesses. Das\n Rohmaterial gilt hier nur als Aufsauger eines bestimmten\n Quantums Arbeit. Durch diese Aufsaugung verwandelt es sich in\n der That in Garn, weil ihm Spinnarbeit zugesetzt wurde. Aber das\n Produkt, das Garn, ist jetzt nur Gradmesser der von der Baumwolle ein-\n gesaugten Arbeit. Wird in einer Stunde 1⅔ lbs. Baumwolle versponnen\n oder in 1⅔ lbs. Garn verwandelt, so zeigen 10 lbs. Garn 6 eingesaugte\n Arbeitsstunden an. Bestimmte und erfahrungsmässig festge-\n stellte Quanta Produkt stellen jetzt nichts dar als bestimmte Quanta\n Arbeit, bestimmte Masse festgeronnener Arbeitszeit. Sie sind nur noch\nMateriatur von einer Stunde, zwei Stunden, einem Tag gesell-\n schaftlicher Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nDass die Arbeit grade Spinnarbeit, ihr Material Baumwolle und ihr Pro-\n dukt Garn, wird hier eben so gleichgültig, als dass der Arbeitsgegen-\n stand selbst schon Produkt, also Rohmaterial ist. Wäre der Arbei-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"156\", \"facs\": \"#f0175\"} --> ter, statt in der Spinnerei, in der Kohlengrube beschäftigt, so wäre der\n Arbeitsgegenstand, die Kohle, von Natur vorhanden. Dennoch stellte ein\n bestimmtes Quantum aus dem Bett losgebrochener Kohle, z. B. ein Cent-\n ner, ein bestimmtes Quantum aufgesaugter Arbeit dar.\n\n\n\n\nBeim Verkauf der Arbeitskraft ward unterstellt, dass ihr Ta-\n geswerth = 3 sh., und in den letztern 6 Arbeitsstunden verkörpert sind,\n diess Arbeitsquantum also erheischt ist, um die Durchschnittssumme der\n täglichen Lebensmittel des Arbeiters zu produziren. Verwandelt unser\n Spinner nun während einer Arbeitsstunde 1⅔ lbs. Baumwolle in 1⅔ lbs.\n Garn [Note: Die Zahlen hier sind ganz willkührlich.] , so in 6 Stunden 10 lbs. Baumwolle in 10 lbs. Garn. Während\n der Dauer des Spinnprozesses saugt die Baumwolle also 6 Arbeitsstunden\n ein. Dieselbe Arbeitszeit stellt sich in einem Goldquantum von 3 sh. dar.\n Der Baumwolle wurde also durch das Spinnen selbst ein Werth von 3 sh.\n zugesetzt.\n\n\n\n\nSehn wir uns nun den Gesammtwerth des Produkts, der\n 10 lbs. Garn, an. In ihnen sind 2½ Arbeitstage vergegenständlicht,\n 2 Tage enthalten in Baumwolle und Spindelmasse, ½ Tag Arbeit einge-\n saugt während des Spinnprozesses. Dieselbe Arbeitszeit stellt sich in\n einer Goldmasse von 15 sh. dar. Der dem Werth der 10 lbs. Garn adä-\n quate Preis beträgt also 15 sh., der Preis eines lb. Garn 1 sh. 6 d.\n\n\n\n\nUnser Kapitalist stutzt. Der Werth des Produkts ist gleich\n dem Werth des vorgeschossenen Kapitals. Der vorge-\n schossene Werth hat sich nicht verwerthet, keinen Mehrwerth\n erzeugt, Geld sich also nicht in Kapital verwandelt. Der Preis der\n 10 lbs. Garn ist 15 sh. und 15 sh. wurden verausgabt auf dem Waarenmarkt\n für die Bildungselemente des Produkts oder, was dasselbe, die\nFaktoren des Arbeitsprozesses, 10 sh. für Baumwolle, 2 sh.\n für die verzehrte Spindelmasse, und 3 sh. für Arbeitskraft. Der aufge-\n schwollene Werth des Garns hilft nichts, denn sein Werth ist\n nur die Summe der früher auf Baumwolle, Spindel und Arbeitskraft ver-\n theilten Werthe, und aus einer solchen blossen Addition vor-\n handner Werthe kann nun und nimmermehr ein Mehrwerth ent-\n springen [Note: Diess ist der Fundamentalsatz, worauf die Lehre der Physiokraten von] . Diese Werthe sind jetzt alle auf ein Ding konzentrirt, aber\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"157\", \"facs\": \"#f0176\"} --> so waren sie in der Geldsumme von 15 sh., bevor diese sich durch drei\n Waarenkäufe zersplitterte.\n\n\n\n\nAn und für sich ist diess Resultat nicht befremdlich. Der Werth\n eines lb. Garn ist 1 sh. 6 d. und für 10 lbs. Garn müsste unser Kapitalist\n daher auf dem Waarenmarkt 15 sh. zahlen. Ob er sich sein Privathaus\n fertig auf dem Markt kauft, oder es selbst bauen lässt, keine dieser Ope-\n rationen wird das im Erwerb des Hauses ausgelegte Geld vermehren.\n\n\n\n\nDer Kapitalist, der in der Vulgärökonomie Bescheid weiss, sagt viel-\n leicht, er habe sein Geld mit der Absicht vorgeschossen, mehr Geld\n daraus zu machen. Der Weg zur Hölle ist jedoch mit guten Ab-\n sichten gepflastert und er konnte eben so gut der Absicht sein, Geld zu\n machen, ohne zu produziren [Note: So z. B. entzog er 1844—47 Theil seines Kapitals dem produktiven Ge-\n schäft, um es in Eisenbahnaktien zu verspekuliren. So, zur Zeit des amerikani-\n schen Bürgerkriegs, schloss er die Fabrik und warf den Fabrikarbeiter auf’s Pflaster,\n um auf der Liverpooler Baumwollbörse zu spielen.] . Er droht. Man werde ihn nicht wieder\n ertappen. Künftig werde er die Waare fertig auf dem Markt kaufen,\n statt sie selbst zu fabriziren. Wenn aber alle seine Brüder Kapitalisten\n dessgleichen thun, wo soll er Waare auf dem Markt finden? Und Geld\n kann er nicht essen. Er katechisirt. Man soll seine Abstinenz be-\n denken. Er konnte seine 15 sh. verprassen. Statt dessen hat er sie\nproduktiv konsumirt und Garn daraus gemacht. Aber dafür ist er\n ja im Besitz von Garn statt von Gewissensbissen. Er muss bei Leibe\n nicht in die Rolle des Schatzbildners zurückfallen, der uns zeigte,\n was bei der Ascetik herauskommt. Ausserdem, wo nichts ist, hat der\n Kaiser sein Recht verloren. Welches immer das Verdienst seiner Ent-\n sagung, es ist nichts da, um sie extra zu zahlen, da der Werth des\n Produkts, der aus dem Prozess herauskommt, nur gleich der Summe der\n [Note: der Unproduktivität aller nicht agrikolen Arbeit beruht und er ist unumstösslich\n für den Oekonomen — von Fach. „Cette façon d’imputer à une seule chose la\n valeur de plusieurs autres (par exemple au lin la consommation du tisserand),\n d’appliquer, pour ainsi dire, couche sur couche, plusieurs valeurs sur une\n seule, fait que celle-ci grossit d’autant . . . . Le terme d’addition peint très-\n bien la manière dont se forme le prix des ouvrages de main d’oeuvre; ce\n prix n’est qu’un total de plusieurs valeurs consommées et additionnées ensemble;\n or, additionner n’est pas multiplier.“ (Mercier de la Rivière l. c.\n p. 599.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"158\", \"facs\": \"#f0177\"} --> hineingeworfenen Waarenwerthe. Er beruhige sich also dabei, dass Tu-\n gend der Tugend Lohn. Statt dessen wird er zudringlich. Das Garn\n ist ihm unnütz. Er hat es für den Verkauf produzirt. So verkaufe er\n es, oder, noch einfacher, produzire in Zukunft nur Dinge für seinen\n eignen Bedarf, ein Rezept, das ihm bereits sein Hausarzt Mac Culloch\n als probates Mittel gegen die Epidemie der Ueberproduktion verschrieben\n hat. Er stellt sich trutzig auf die Hinterbeine. Sollte der Arbeiter\n mit seinen eignen Gliedmassen in der blauen Luft Arbeitsgebilde schaffen,\n Waaren produziren? Gab er ihm nicht den Stoff, womit und worin er\n allein seine Arbeit verleiblichen kann? Da nun der grösste Theil der Ge-\n sellschaft aus solchen Habenichtsen besteht, hat er nicht der Gesellschaft\n durch seine Produktionsmittel, seine Baumwolle und seine Spindel, einen\n unermesslichen Dienst erwiesen, nicht dem Arbeiter selbst, den er oben-\n drein noch mit Lebensmitteln versah? Und soll er den Dienst nicht be-\n rechnen? Hat der Arbeiter ihm aber nicht den Gegendienst erwiesen,\n Baumwolle und Spindel in Garn zu verwandeln? Ausserdem handelt es\n sich hier nicht um Dienste [Note: „Las du rhümen, schmücken und putzen . . . . Wer aber mehr oder\n besseres nimpt (als er giebt), das ist Wucher, und heisst, nicht Dienst, son-\n dern Schaden gethan seinem Nehesten, als mit stelen und rauben geschicht. Es\n ist nicht alles Dienst und wolgethan dem Nehesten, was man heisst, Dienst und\n wolgethan. Denn eine Ehebrecherin und Ehebrecher thun einander grossen Dienst\n und wolgefallen. Ein Reuter thut einem Mordbrenner grossen reuterdienst, das\n er im hilfft, auff der strassen rauben, Land und Leute bevehden. Die Papisten\n thun den unsern grossen Dienst, das sie nicht alle ertrenken, verbrennen, ermor-\n den, im Gefengniss verfaulen lassen, sondern lassen doch etliche leben, und ver-\n jagen sie, oder nemen jenen was sie haben. Der Teuffel thut selber seinen Die-\n nern grossen, unermesslichen Dienst . . . . Summa, die Welt ist voll grosser,\n trefflicher, teglicher Dienst und wolthaten.“ (Martin Luther: An die\n Pfarherrn, wider den Wucher zu predigen etc. Wittenberg\n 1540.)] . Ein Dienst ist nichts als die nützliche\n Wirkung eines Gebrauchswerths, sei es der Waare, sei es der Ar-\n beit [Note: Ich bemerke darüber in „Zur Kritik der Pol. Oek.“ p. 14 u. a.:\n „Man begreift, welchen „Dienst“ die Kategorie „Dienst“ (service) einer\n Sorte Oekonomen wie J. B. Say und F. Bastiat leisten muss.“] . Hier aber gilt’s den Tauschwerth. Er zahlte dem Arbeiter\n den Werth von 3 sh. Der Arbeiter gab ihm ein exaktes Aequivalent\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"159\", \"facs\": \"#f0178\"} --> zurück in dem der Baumwolle zugesetzten Werth von 3 sh., Werth für\n Werth. Unser Freund, eben noch so kapitalübermüthig, nimmt plötzlich\n die anspruchslose Haltung seines eignen Arbeiters an. Hat er nicht selbst\n gearbeitet? nicht die Arbeit der Ueberwachung, der Oberaufsicht über\n den Spinner verrichtet? Bildet diese seine Arbeit nicht auch Werth? Sein\n eigner overlooker und sein manager zucken die Achseln. Unterdess hat\n er aber bereits mit heitrem Lächeln seine alte Physiognomie wieder an-\n genommen. Er foppte uns mit der ganzen Litanei. Er giebt keinen\n Deut darum. Er überlässt diese und ähnliche faule Ausflüchte und hohle\n Flausen den dafür eigens bezahlten Professoren der politischen Oekonomie.\n Er selbst ist ein praktischer Mann, der zwar nicht immer bedenkt, was er\n ausserhalb des Geschäfts sagt, aber stets weiss, was er im Geschäft thut.\n\n\n\n\nSehn wir näher zu. Der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft be-\n trug 3 sh., weil in ihr selbst ein halber Arbeitstag vergegenständlicht\n ist, d. h. weil die täglich zur Produktion der Arbeitskraft nöthigen Lebens-\n mittel einen halben Arbeitstag kosten. Aber die vergangene Arbeit, die\n in der Arbeitskraft steckt, und die lebendige Arbeit, die sie leisten kann,\n ihre täglichen Erhaltungskosten und ihre tägliche Verausgabung, sind zwei\n ganz verschiedne Grössen. Die erstere bestimmt ihren Tauschwerth, die\n andere bildet ihren Gebrauchswerth. Dass ein halber Arbeitstag\n nöthig, um ihn während 24 Stunden am Leben zu erhalten, hindert den\n Arbeiter keineswegs einen ganzen Tag zu arbeiten. Der Werth\n der Arbeitskraft und ihre Verwerthung im Arbeitsprozess sind also\n zwei verschiedne Grössen. Diese Werthdifferenz hatte der Kapita-\n list im Auge, als er die Arbeitskraft kaufte. Ihre nützliche Eigenschaft,\n Garn oder Stiefel zu machen, war nur eine conditio sine qua, weil Arbeit\n in nützlicher Form verausgabt werden muss, um Werth zu bilden. Was aber\n entschied, war der spezifische Gebrauchswerth dieser Waare,\n Quelle von Tauschwerth zu sein und von mehr Tauschwerth als sie selbst\n hat. Diess ist der spezifische Dienst, den der Kapitalist von ihr er-\n wartet. Und er verfährt dabei den ewigen Gesetzen des Waarenaus-\n tausches gemäss. In der That, der Verkäufer der Arbeitskraft, wie der\n Verkäufer jeder andern Waare, realisirtihren Tauschwerth und\nveräussert ihren Gebrauchswerth. Er kann den einen nicht\n erhalten, ohne den andern wegzugeben. Der Gebrauchswerth der Arbeits-\n kraft, die Arbeit selbst, gehört eben so wenig ihrem Verkäufer, wie der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"160\", \"facs\": \"#f0179\"} --> Gebrauchswerth des verkauften Oels dem Oelhändler. Der Geldbesitzer\n hat den Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft gezahlt; ihm gehört daher ihr\n Gebrauch während des Tages, die tagelange Arbeit. Der\n Umstand, dass die tägliche Erhaltung der Arbeitskraft nur einen halben\n Arbeitstag kostet, obgleich die Arbeitskraft einen ganzen Tag wirken, arbei-\n ten kann, dass daher der Werth, den ihr Gebrauch während eines Tags\n schafft, doppelt so gross ist als ihr eigner Tageswerth, ist ein besondres\n Glück für den Käufer, aber durchaus kein Unrecht gegen den Verkäufer.\n\n\n\n\nUnser Kapitalist hat den Casus vorgesehn. Der Arbeiter findet da-\n her in der Werkstätte die nöthigen Produktionsmittel nicht nur für einen\n sechsstündigen, sondern für einen zwölfstündigen Arbeitsprozess. Saugten\n 10 lbs. Baumwolle 6 Arbeitsstunden ein und verwandelten sich in 10 lbs.\n Garn, so werden 20 lbs. Baumwolle 12 Arbeitsstunden einsaugen und in 20\n lbs. Garn verwandelt. Betrachten wir das Produkt des verlänger-\n ten Arbeitsprozesses. In den 20 lbs. Garn sind jetzt 5 Arbeits-\n tage vergegenständlicht, 4 in der verzehrten Baumwoll- und Spindel-\n masse, 1 von der Baumwolle eingesaugt während des Spinnprozesses.\n Der Goldausdruck von 5 Arbeitstagen ist aber 30 sh. oder 1 Pfd. St. 10 sh.\n Diess also der Preis der 20 lbs. Garn. Das Pfund Garn kostet nach wie\n vor 1 sh. 6 d. Aber die Werthsumme der in den Prozess geworfenen\n Waaren betrug 27 sh. Der Werth des Garns beträgt 30 sh. Der Werth\n des Produkts ist um ⅓ gewachsen über den zu seiner Produktion vor-\n geschossenen Werth. So haben sich 27 sh. in 30 sh. verwandelt. Sie\n haben einen Mehrwerth von 3 sh. gesetzt. Das Kunststück ist end-\n lich gelungen. Geld ist in Kapital verwandelt.\n\n\n\n\nAlle Bedingungen des Problems sind gelöst und die Gesetze des\n Waarenaustausches in keiner Weise verletzt. Aequivalent wurde gegen\n Aequivalent ausgetauscht. Der Kapitalist zahlte als Käufer jede Waare\n zu ihrem Werth, Baumwolle, Spindelmasse, Arbeitskraft. Er that dann,\n was jeder andre Käufer von Waaren thut. Er konsumirte ihren Ge-\n brauchswerth. Der Konsumtionsprozess der Arbeitskraft,\n der zugleich Produktionsprozess der Waare, ergab ein Produkt\n von 20 lbs. Garn mit einem Werth von 30 sh. Der Kapitalist kehrt nun\n zum Markt zurück und verkauft Waare, nachdem er Waare gekauft hat.\n Er verkauft das Pfund Garn zu 1 sh. 6 d., keinen Deut über oder unter\n seinem Werth. Und doch zieht er 3 sh. mehr aus der Cirkulation her-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"161\", \"facs\": \"#f0180\"} --> aus als er ursprünglich in sie hineinwarf. Dieser ganze Verlauf, die Ver-\n wandlung seines Geldes in Kapital, geht in der Cirkulationssphäre vor und\n geht nicht in ihr vor. Durch die Vermittlung der Cirkulation, weil\n bedingt durch den Kauf der Arbeitskraft auf dem Waarenmarkt.\nNicht in der Cirkulation, denn diese leitet nur den Verwerthungs-\n prozess ein, der sich in der Produktionssphäre zuträgt. Und\n so ist „tout pour le mieux dans le meilleur des mondes possibles.“\n\n\n\n\nIndem der Kapitalist Geld in Waaren verwandelt, die als Stoffbildner\n eines neuen Produkts oder als Faktoren des Arbeitsprozesses dienen, in-\n dem er ihrer todten Gegenständlichkeit lebendige Arbeitskraft einverleibt,\n verwandelt er Werth, vergangne, vergegenständlichte, todte Arbeit in\nKapital, sich selbst verwerthenden Werth, ein beseeltes Unge-\n heuer, das zu „arbeiten“ beginnt, als hätt’ es Lieb’ im Leibe.\n\n\n\n\nVergleichen wir nun Werthbildungsprozess und Verwer-\n thungsprozess, so ist der Verwerthungsprozess nichts als ein über\n einen gewissen Punkt hinaus verlängerter Werthbildungsprozess.\n Dauert der letztre nur bis zu dem Punkt, wo der vom Kapital ge-\n zahlte Werth der Arbeitskraft durch ein neues Aequivalent ersetzt\n ist, so ist er einfacher Werthbildungsprozess. Dauert der Werthbildungs-\n prozess über diesen Punkt hinaus, so wird er Verwerthungsprozess.\n\n\n\n\nVergleichen wir ferner den Werthbildungsprozess mit dem\nArbeitsprozess, so besteht der letztere in der wirklichen Arbeit,\n die Gebrauchswerthe produzirt. Die Bewegung wird hier quali-\n tativ betrachtet, in ihrer besondern Art und Weise, nach Zweck und In-\n halt. Derselbe Arbeitsprozess stellt sich im Werthbildungs-\n prozess nur von seiner quantitativen Seite dar. Es handelt sich\n nur noch um die Zeit, welche die Arbeit zu ihrer Operation braucht,\n oder um die Dauer, während deren die Arbeitskraft verausgabt wird. Hier\n gelten auch die Waaren, die in den Arbeitsprozess eingehn, nicht mehr als\n funktionell bestimmte, stoffliche Faktoren der zweckmässig wirkenden Ar-\n beitskraft. Sie zählen nur noch als bestimmte Quanta vergegenständ-\n lichter Arbeit. Ob in den Produktionsmitteln enthalten oder durch die\n Arbeitskraft zugesetzt, die Arbeit zählt nur noch nach ihrem Zeitmass.\n Sie beträgt so viel Stunden, Tage u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nSie zählt jedoch nur, soweit die zur Produktion des Gebrauchswerths\n verbrauchte Zeit gesellschaftlich nothwendig ist. Es umfasst\n [Running matter: I. 11] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"162\", \"facs\": \"#f0181\"} --> diess Verschiednes. Die Arbeitskraft muss unter normalen Bedingungen\n funktioniren. Ist die Spinnmaschine das gesellschaftlich herrschende\n Arbeitsmittel für die Spinnerei, so darf dem Arbeiter nicht ein Spinnrad\n in die Hand gegeben werden. Statt Baumwolle von normaler Güte muss\n er nicht Schund erhalten, der jeden Augenblick reisst. In beiden Fällen\n würde er mehr als die gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit zur Produk-\n tion eines Pfundes Garn verbrauchen, diese überschüssige Zeit aber nicht\n Werth oder Geld bilden. Der normale Charakter der gegenständlichen\n Arbeitsfaktoren hängt jedoch nicht vom Arbeiter, sondern vom Kapitalisten\n ab. Fernere Bedingung ist der normale Charakter der Arbeits-\n kraft selbst. In dem Fach, worin sie verwandt wird, muss sie das herr-\n schende Durchschnittsmass von Geschick, Fertigkeit und Raschheit besitzen.\n Aber unser Kapitalist kaufte auf dem Arbeitsmarkt Arbeitskraft von nor-\n maler Güte. Diese Kraft muss in dem gewöhnlichen Durchschnittsmass\n der Anstrengung, mit dem gesellschaftlich üblichen Grad von Inten-\n sivität verausgabt werden. Darüber wacht der Kapitalist eben so\n ängstlich, als dass keine Zeit ohne Arbeit vergeudet wird. Er hat die\n Arbeitskraft für bestimmte Zeitfrist gekauft. Er hält darauf das Seine zu\n haben. Er will nicht bestohlen sein. Endlich — und hierfür hat der-\n selbe Herr einen eignen code pénal — darf kein zweckwidriger Consum\n von Rohmaterial und Arbeitsmitteln stattfinden, weil vergeudetes Material\n oder Arbeitsmittel überflüssig verausgabte Quanta vergegenständlichter\n Arbeit darstellen, also nicht zählen und nicht in das Produkt der Werthbil-\n dung eingehn [Note: Diess ist einer der Umstände, die auf Sklaverei gegründete Produktion\n vertheuern. Der Arbeiter soll sich hier, nach dem treffenden Ausdruck der Alten,\n nur als instrumentum vocale von dem Thier als instrumentum semi-\n vocale und dem todten Arbeitszeug als instrumentum mutum unterscheiden.\n Er selbst aber lässt Thier und Arbeitszeug fühlen, dass er nicht Ihresgleichen,\n sondern ein Mensch ist. Er verschafft sich das Selbstgefühl seines Unterschieds\n von ihnen, indem er sie misshandelt und con amore verwüstet. Es gilt daher als\n ökonomisches Princip in dieser Produktionsweise nur die rohesten, schwerfällig-\n sten, aber grade wegen ihrer unbehülflichen Plumpheit schwer zu ruinirenden\n Arbeitsinstrumente anzuwenden. Bis zum Ausbruch des Bürgerkriegs fand man\n daher in den am Meerbusen von Mexiko liegenden Sklavenstaaten Pflüge altchine-\n sischer Konstruktion, die den Boden aufwühlen wie ein Schwein oder Maulwurf,\n aber ihn nicht spalten und wenden. Vgl. J. C. Cairns: „The Slave Power.] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"163\", \"facs\": \"#f0182\"} -->\n\nMan sieht: der früher aus der Analyse der Waare gewonnene\n Unterschied zwischen der Arbeit, soweit sie Gebrauchswerth, und der-\n selben Arbeit, soweit sie Tauschwerth schafft, hat sich jetzt als Unterschei-\n dung der verschiednen Seiten des Produktionsprozesses dargestellt.\n\n\n\n\nAls Einheit von Arbeitsprozess und Werthbildungs-\n prozess ist der Produktionsprozess Produktionsprozess von\n Waaren; als Einheit von Arbeitsprozess und Verwer-\n thungsprozess ist er kapitalistischer Produktionspro-\n zess, kapitalistische Form der Waarenproduktion.\n\n\n\n\nEs wurde früher bemerkt, dass es für den Verwerthungsprozess\n durchaus gleichgültig, ob die vom Kapitalisten angeeignete Arbeit ein-\n fache, gesellschaftliche Durchschnittsarbeit, oder kom-\n plizirtere Arbeit, Arbeit von höherem spezifischem Ge-\n wicht ist. Die Arbeit, die als höhere, komplizirtere Arbeit gegenüber\n der gesellschaftlichen Durchschnittsarbeit gilt, ist die Aeusserung\n einer Arbeitskraft, worin höhere Bildungskosten eingehn, deren\n Produktion mehr Arbeitszeit kostet und die daher einen höheren Tauschwerth\n hat als die einfache Arbeitskraft. Ist der Werth dieser Kraft höher, so\n äussert sie sich aber auch in höherer Arbeit und vergegenständlicht sich\n [Note: London 1862“, p. 46 sqq. In seinem „Sea Board Slave States“ er-\n zählt Olmstedt u. A.: „I am here shewn tools that no man in his senses, with\n us, would allow a labourer, for whom he was paying wages, to be encumbered with;\n and the excessive weight and clumsiness of which, I would judge, would make\n work at least ten per cent greater than with those ordinarily used with us. And\n I am assured that, in the careless and clumsy way they must be used by the slaves,\n anything lighter or less rude could not be furnished them with good economy, and\n that such tools as we constantly give our labourers, and find our profit in giving\n them, would not last out a day in a Virginia cornfield — much lighter and more free\n from stones though it be than ours. So, too, when I ask why mules are so uni-\n versally substituted for horses on the farm, the first reason given, and confessedly\n the most conclusive one, is that horses cannot bear the treatment that they always\nmust get from the negroes; horses are always soon foundered or crippled by them,\n while mules will bear cudgelling, or lose a meal or two now and then, and not be\n materially injured, and they do not take cold or get sick, if neglected or overwork-\n ed. But I do not need to go further than to the window of the room in which\n I am writing, to see at almost any time, treatment of cattle that would insure\n the immediate discharge of the driver by almost any farmer owning them in the\n North.“] \n [Running matter: 11*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"164\", \"facs\": \"#f0183\"} --> daher, in denselben Zeiträumen, in verhältnissmässig höheren\n Werthen. Welches jedoch immer der Gradunterschied zwischen Spinnar-\n beit und Juwelierarbeit, die Portion Arbeit, wodurch der Juwelenarbeiter nur\n den Werth seiner eignen Arbeitskraft ersetzt, unterscheidet sich quali-\n tativ in keiner Weise von der zusätzlichen Portion Arbeit, wodurch er\n Mehrwerth schafft. Nach wie vor kommt der Mehrwerth nur heraus durch\n einen quantitativen Ueberschuss von Arbeit, durch die ver-\n längerte Dauer desselben Arbeitsprozesses, in dem einen\n Fall Prozess der Garnproduktion, in dem andern Fall Prozess der Juwelen-\n produktion [Note: Der Unterschied zwischen höherer und einfacher, „skilled“ und „unskilled\n labour“, beruht zum Theil auf blossen Illusionen, oder wenigstens Unter-\n schieden, die längst aufgehört haben reell zu sein und nur noch in traditioneller\n Convention fortleben; zum Theil auf der hilfsloseren Lage gewisser Schichten der\n Arbeiterklasse, die ihnen minder als andren erlaubt, den Werth ihrer Arbeits-\n kraft zu ertrotzen. Zufällige Umstände spielen dabei so grosse Rolle, dass die-\n selben Arbeitsarten den Platz wechseln. Wo z. B. die physische Substanz der\n Arbeiterklasse abgeschwächt und relativ erschöpft ist, wie in allen Ländern ent-\n wickelter kapitalistischer Produktion, verkehren sich im Allgemeinen brutale Ar-\n beiten, die viel Muskelkraft erfordern, in höhere gegenüber viel feineren Arbeiten,\n die auf die Stufe einfacher Arbeit herabsinken, wie z. B. die Arbeit eines brick-\n layer (Maurer) in England eine viel höhere Stufe einnimmt, als die eines Damast-\n wirkers. Auf der andern Seite figurirt die Arbeit eines fustian cutters (Baumwoll-\n sammtscheerers), obgleich sie viel körperliche Anstrengung kostet und obendrein\n sehr ungesund ist, als „einfache“ Arbeit. Uebrigens muss man sich nicht ein-\n bilden, dass die sogenannte „skilled labour“ einen quantitativ bedeutenden Umfang\n in der Nationalarbeit einnimmt. Laing, vor kurzem noch Schatzkanzler von\n Indien, rechnet, dass in England (und Wales) die Existenz von 11 Millionen auf\n einfacher Arbeit beruht. Nach Abzug einer Million von Aristokraten und einer\n andern Million Paupers, Vagabunden, Verbrecher, Prostituirte u. s. w. von den\n 18 Millionen der Bevölkerungszahl, zur Zeit seiner Schrift, bleiben 4 Millionen\n Mittelklasse mit Einschluss kleinerer Rentner, Beamten, Schriftsteller, Künstler,\n Schulmeister u. s. w. Um diese 4 Millionen herauszubekommen, zählt er zum\narbeitenden Theil der Mittelklasse, ausser Banquiers u. s. w., alle besser\n bezahlten „Fabrikarbeiter“! Auch die bricklayers fehlen nicht unter den\n „potenzirten Arbeitern.“ Bleiben ihm dann die besagten 11 Millionen.“ (S.\nLaing: National Distress etc. London 1844.) „The great class,\n who have nothing to give for food but ordinary labour, are the great\n bulk of the people.“ (James Mill in Art. „Colony.“ Supple-\n ment of the Encyclop. Brit. 1831.)] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"165\", \"facs\": \"#f0184\"} -->\n\nAndrerseits muss in jedem Werthbildungsprozess die höhere Arbeit\n stets auf gesellschaftliche Durchschnittsarbeit reducirt werden, z. B. ein\n Tag höherer Arbeit auf x Tage einfacher Arbeit [Note: „Where reference is made to labour as a measure of value, it necessarily\n implies labour of one particular kind. . . . the proportion which\n the other kinds bear to it being easily ascertained.“ („Outlines of Polit.\n Economy. London 1832“, p. 22, 23.)] . Man erspart also\n eine überflüssige Operation und vereinfacht die Analyse durch die Annahme,\n dass der vom Kapital verwandte Arbeiter einfache gesellschaftliche Durch-\n schnittsarbeit verrichtet.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0001","text":"## Drittes Kapitel.\n Die Produktion des absoluten Mehrwerths.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"5.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0002","text":"## 1) Arbeitsprozess und Verwerthungsprozess.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"5.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0003","text":"Der Gebrauch der Arbeitskraft ist die Arbeit selbst. Der Käu-\n fer der Arbeitskraft konsumirt sie, indem er ihren Verkäufer arbeiten\n lässt. Letztrer wird hierdurch actu sich bethätigende Arbeitskraft, Ar-\n beiter, was er früher nur potentia war. Um seine Arbeit in Waaren\n darzustellen, muss er sie vor allem in Gebrauchswerthen darstellen,\nSachen, die zur Befriedigung von Bedürfnissen irgend einer Art dienen.\n Es ist also ein besondrer Gebrauchswerth, ein bestimmter Artikel, den\n der Kapitalist vom Arbeiter anfertigen lässt. Die Produktion von\n Gebrauchswerthen, oder Gütern, ändert ihre allgemeine Na-\n tur nicht dadurch, dass sie für den Kapitalisten und unter seiner Kontrole\n vorgeht. Der Arbeitsprozess ist daher zunächst in seinen abstrak-\n ten Momenten, unabhängig von jeder bestimmten gesellschaft-\n lichen Form zu betrachten.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"5.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0004","text":"Der Arbeitsprozess ist zunächst ein Prozess zwischen dem Menschen\n und der Natur, ein Prozess, worin er seinen Stoffwechsel mit der Natur\n durch seine eigne That vermittelt, regelt und kontrolirt. Der Mensch tritt\n dem Naturstoff selbst als eine Naturmacht gegenüber. Die seiner Leiblich-\n keit angehörigen Naturkräfte, Arme und Beine, Kopf und Hand, setzt er in\n Bewegung, um sich den Naturstoff in einer für sein eignes Leben brauch-\n baren Form zu assimiliren. Indem er durch diese Bewegung auf die Natur\n ausser ihm wirkt und sie verändert, verändert er zugleich seine eigne Natur.\n Er entwickelt die in ihr schlummernden Potenzen und unterwirft das Spiel\n ihrer Kräfte seiner eignen Botmässigkeit. Wir haben es hier nicht mit\n den ersten thierartig instinktmässigen Formen des Arbeitsprozesses zu\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"142\", \"facs\": \"#f0161\"} --> thun.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"5.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0005","text":"Dem Zustand, worin der Arbeiter als Verkäufer seiner eignen Arbeits-\n kraft auf dem Waarenmarkt auftritt, ist in urzeitlichen Hintergrund der Zu-\n stand entrückt, worin der menschliche Arbeitsprozess seine erste instinktartige\n Form noch nicht abgestreift hatte. Wir unterstellen den Arbeitsprozess\n in einer Form, worin er dem Menschen ausschliesslich angehört. Eine\n Spinne verrichtet Operationen, die denen des Webers ähneln, und eine\n Biene beschämt durch den Bau ihrer Wachszellen manchen menschlichen\n Baumeister. Was aber von vorn herein den schlechtesten Baumeister vor\n der besten Biene auszeichnet, ist, dass er die Zelle in seinem Kopf ge-\n baut hat, bevor er sie in Wachs baut. Am Ende des Arbeitsprozesses\n kommt ein Resultat heraus, das beim Beginn desselben schon in der Vor-\n stellung des Arbeiters, also schon ideell vorhanden war.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"5.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0006","text":"Nicht\n dass er nur eine Formveränderung des Natürlichen bewirkt, verwirk-\n licht er im Natürlichen zugleich seinen Zweck, den er weiss, der\n die Art und Weise seines Thuns als Gesetz bestimmt und dem er seinen\n Willen unterordnen muss. Und diese Unterordnung ist kein vereinzelter\n Akt. Ausser der Anstrengung der Organe, die arbeiten, ist der zweck-\n gemässe Wille, der sich als Aufmerksamkeit äussert, für die\n ganze Dauer des Arbeitsprozesses erheischt, und um so mehr, je weniger\n die Arbeit durch ihren eignen Inhalt und ihre Art und Weise der Ausfüh-\n rung den Arbeiter mit sich fortreisst, je weniger er sie daher als Spiel\n seiner eignen körperlichen und geistigen Kräfte geniesst.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"5.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0007","text":"Die einfachen Momente des Arbeitsprozesses sind die zweck-\n mässige Thätigkeit oder die Arbeit selbst, ihr Gegen-\n stand und ihr Mittel.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"5.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0008","text":"Die Erde (worunter ökonomisch auch das Wasser einbegriffen),\n wie sie den Menschen ursprünglich mit Proviant, fertigen Lebensmitteln\n ausrüstet [Note: „The earth’s spontaneous productions being in small quantity, and quite\n independent of man, appear as it were, to be furnished by nature, in the same way\n as a small sum is given to a young man, in order to put him in a way of industry,\n and of making his fortune.“ (James Steuart: Principles of Polit.\n Econ. edit. Dublin 1770, v. I, p. 116.)] , findet sich ohne sein Zuthun als der allgemeine Gegen-\n stand der menschlichen Arbeit vor. Alle Dinge, welche die Arbeit nur\n von ihrem unmittelbaren Zusammenhang mit dem Erdganzen loslöst, sind\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"143\", \"facs\": \"#f0162\"} --> von Natur vorgefundne Arbeitsgegenstände.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"5.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0009","text":"So der Fisch, der von seinem\n Lebenselement, dem Wasser, getrennt, gefangen wird, das Holz, das im\n Urwald gefällt, das Erz, das aus seiner Ader losgebrochen wird. Ist der\n Arbeitsgegenstand dagegen selbst schon sozusagen durch frühere\n Arbeit filtrirt, so nennen wir ihn Rohmaterial. Z. B. das bereits\n losgebrochene Erz, das nun ausgewaschen wird. Alles Rohmaterial ist\n Arbeitsgegenstand, aber nicht jeder Arbeitsgegenstand ist Rohmaterial.\n Rohmaterial ist der Arbeitsgegenstand nur, sobald er bereits eine durch\n Arbeit vermittelte Veränderung erfahren hat.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"5.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0010","text":"Das Arbeitsmittel ist ein Ding oder ein Komplex von Dingen,\n die der Arbeiter zwischen sich und den Arbeitsgegenstand schiebt und die\n ihm als Leiter seiner Thätigkeit auf diesen Gegenstand dienen. Er be-\n nutzt die mechanischen, physischen, chemischen Eigenschaften der Dinge,\n um sie als Machtmittel auf andre Dinge, seinem Zweck gemäss,\n wirken zu lassen [Note: „Die Vernunft ist eben so listig als mächtig. Die List besteht über-\n haupt in der vermittelnden Thätigkeit, welche, indem sie die Objekte ihrer eigenen\n Natur gemäss auf einander einwirken und sich an einander abarbeiten lässt, ohne\n sich unmittelbar in diesen Prozess einzumischen, gleichwohl nur ihren Zweck zur\n Ausführung bringt.“ (Hegel: Encyklopädie. Erster Theil: Die Logik.\n Berlin 1840, p. 382.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"5.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0011","text":"Der Gegenstand, dessen sich der Arbeiter un-\n mittelbar bemächtigt — abgesehn von der Ergreifung fertiger Lebens-\n mittel, der Früchte z. B., wobei seine eigenen Leibesorgane allein als\n Arbeitsmittel dienen — ist nicht der Arbeitsgegenstand, sondern das\n Arbeitsmittel. So wird das Natürliche selbst zum Organ seiner Thätig-\n keit, ein Organ, das er seinen eigenen Leibesorganen hinzufügt, seine\n natürliche Gestalt verlängernd, trotz der Bibel. Wie die Erde seine ur-\n sprüngliche Proviantkammer, ist sie sein ursprüngliches Arsenal von\n Arbeitsmitteln. Sie liefert ihm z. B. den Stein, womit er wirft, reibt,\n drückt, schneidet u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"5.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0012","text":"Die Erde selbst ist ein Arbeitsmittel, setzt\n jedoch zu ihrem Dienst als Arbeitsmittel in der Agrikultur wieder eine\n ganze Reihe andrer Arbeitsmittel und eine schon relativ hohe Entwicklung\n der Arbeitskraft voraus [Note: In der sonst elenden Schrift: „Théorie de l’Écon. Polit. Paris\n 1815“, zählt Ganilh den Physiokraten gegenüber treffend die grosse Reihe von\n Arbeitsprozessen auf, welche Voraussetzungen der eigentlichen Agrikultur\n bilden.] . Sobald überhaupt der Arbeitsprozess nur\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"144\", \"facs\": \"#f0163\"} --> einigermassen entwickelt ist, bedarf er bereits bearbeiteter Arbeitsmittel.\n In den ältesten Menschenhöhlen finden wir Steinwerkzeuge und Steinwaffen.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"5.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0013","text":"Neben bearbeitetem Stein und Holz spielt im Anfang der Menschenge-\n schichte das gezähmte, also selbst schon durch Arbeit veränderte, ge-\n züchtete Thier die Hauptrolle als Arbeitsmittel [Note: In den oben citirten „Réflexions“ entwickelt Turgot gut die Wich-\n tigkeit des gezähmten Thiers für die Anfänge der Kultur.] . Der Gebrauch und\n die Schöpfung von Arbeitsmitteln, obgleich im Keim schon gewissen Thier-\n arten eigen, charakterisiren den spezifisch menschlichen Arbeits-\n prozess und Franklin definirt daher den Menschen als „a tool-\n making animal“, ein Werkzeuge fabrizirendes Thier. Dieselbe\n Wichtigkeit, die der Bau von Knochenreliquien für die Erkenntniss der\n Organisation untergegangner Thiergeschlechter, haben Reliquien von Ar-\n beitsmitteln für die Beurtheilung untergegangner ökonomischer Ge-\n sellschaftsformationen.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"5.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0014","text":"Nicht was gemacht wird, sondern wie, mit\n welchen Arbeitsmitteln gemacht wird, unterscheidet die ökonomischen\n Epochen [Note: Von allen Waaren sind eigentliche Luxuswaaren die unbedeutendsten\n für die technologische Vergleichung verschiedner Produktionsepochen.] . Die Arbeitsmittel sind nicht nur der Gradmesser der Ent-\n wicklung der menschlichen Arbeitskraft, sondern auch der Index der ge-\n sellschaftlichen Verhältnisse, unter denen gearbeitet wird. Unter den\n Arbeitsmitteln selbst bieten die mechanischen Arbeitsmittel,\n deren Gesammtheit man das Knochen- und Muskelsystem der\n Produktion nennen kann, viel entscheidendere Charaktermerkmale\n einer gesellschaftlichen Produktionsepoche, als solche Arbeitsmittel, die\n nur zu Behältern des Arbeitsgegenstands dienen, und deren Gesammtheit\n ganz allgemein als das Gefässsystem der Produktion bezeichnet\n werden kann, wie z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"5.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0015","text":"Röhren, Fässer, Körbe, Krüge u. s. w. Erst in\n der chemischen Fabrikation spielen sie eine bedeutungsvolle Rolle.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"5.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0016","text":"Im weiteren Sinn zählt der Arbeitsprozess unter seine Mittel ausser\n den Dingen, welche die Wirkung der Arbeit auf ihren Gegenstand ver-\n mitteln, und daher in einer oder der andern Weise als Leiter der Thätig-\n keit dienen, alle gegenständlichen Bedingungen, die überhaupt\n erheischt sind, damit der ganze Prozess vorgehe. Sie gehn nicht direkt\n in den Arbeitsprozess ein, aber der Arbeitsprozess kann ohne sie gar nicht\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"145\", \"facs\": \"#f0164\"} --> oder nur unvollkommen vorgehn. Das allgemeine Arbeitsmittel dieser Art\n ist wieder die Erde selbst, denn sie giebt dem Arbeiter den locus\n standi und seinem Prozess den Wirkungsraum (field of em-\n ployment). Durch die Arbeit schon vermittelte Arbeitsmittel dieser\n Art sind z. B. Arbeitsgebäude, Kanäle, Strassen u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"5.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0017","text":"Der Arbeitsprozess ist also ein Prozess, worin die Thätigkeit\n des Menschen durch das Arbeitsmittel eine von vorn herein bezweckte Ver-\n änderung im Arbeitsgegenstand bewirkt. Dieser Prozess erlischt im\nProdukt. Sein Produkt ist ein Gebrauchswerth, ein durch Form-\n veränderung menschlichen Bedürfnissen assimilirter Naturstoff. Durch\n den Prozess hat sich die Arbeit mit ihrem Gegenstand verbunden. Die\n Arbeit ist vergegenständlicht und der Gegenstand ist verarbeitet. Was\n auf Seiten des Arbeiters in der Form der Unruhe erschien, erscheint nun als\n ruhende Eigenschaft, in der Form des Seins, auf Seiten des Produkts. Er\n hat gesponnen und das Produkt ist ein Gespinnst.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"5.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0018","text":"Betrachtet man den ganzen Prozess vom Standpunkt seines Resultats,\ndes Produkts, so erscheinen beide, Arbeitsmittel und Arbeits-\n gegenstand, als Produktionsmittel [Note: Es scheint paradox z. B. den Fisch, der noch nicht gefangen ist, ein Pro-\n duktionsmittel für den Fischfang zu nennen. Bisher ist aber noch nicht die\n Kunst erfunden, Fische in Gewässern zu fangen, in denen sie sich nicht vorfinden.]  und die Arbeit selbst als\nproduktive Arbeit [Note: Diese Bestimmung produktiver Arbeit, wie sie sich vom Stand-\n punkt des einfachen Arbeitsprozesses ergiebt, reicht keineswegs hin für den kapi-\n talistischen Produktionsprozess.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"5.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0019","text":"Wenn ein Gebrauchswerth als Produkt aus dem Arbeitsprozess\n herauskommt, gehn andre Gebrauchswerthe, selbst Produkte früherer\n Arbeitsprozesse, als Produktionsmittel in den Prozess ein. Der-\n selbe Gebrauchswerth, der das Produkt eines Arbeitsprozesses, bildet das\n Produktionsmittel eines andern. Produkte sind daher nicht nur Resul-\n tat, sondern zugleich Bedingung des Arbeitsprozesses.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"5.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0020","text":"Mit Ausnahme der extraktiven Industrie, die ihren Arbeits-\n gegenstand von Natur vorfindet, wie der Bergbau, die Jagd, der Fischfang\n u. s. w., (der Ackerbau nur, soweit er in erster Instanz die jungfräuliche\n Erde selbst aufbricht), behandeln alle Industriezweige einen Gegen-\n stand, der Rohmaterial, d. h. bereits durch die Arbeit filtrirter\n [Running matter: I. 10] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"146\", \"facs\": \"#f0165\"} --> Arbeitsgegenstand, selbst schon Produkt eines früheren Arbeitsprozesses\n ist. So z. B. der Samen in der Agrikultur. Thiere und Pflanzen, die\n man als Naturprodukte zu betrachten pflegt, sind nicht nur Produkte viel-\n leicht der Arbeit vom vorigen Jahr, sondern, in ihren jetzigen Formen,\n Produkte einer durch viele Generationen, unter menschlicher Kontrole,\n vermittelst menschlicher Arbeit, fortgesetzten Umwandlung.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"5.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0021","text":"Was aber\n die Arbeitsmittel insbesondere betrifft, so zeigt ihre ungeheure Mehr-\n zahl dem oberflächlichsten Blick die Spur vergangner Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"5.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0022","text":"Das Rohmaterial kann die Hauptsubstanz eines Produkts bilden, oder\nnur als Hilfsstoff in seine Bildung eingehn. Der Hilfsstoff wird vom\n Arbeitsmittel konsumirt, wie Kohle von einer Dampfmaschine,\n Oel vom Rade, Heu von einem Zugpferd, oder dem Rohmaterial zu-\n gesetzt, um eine stoffliche Veränderung darin zu bewirken, wie Chlor\n zur ungebleichten Leinwand, Kohle zum Eisen, Farbe zur Wolle, oder er\n unterstützt die Verrichtung der Arbeit selbst, wie z. B. zur Beleuchtung\n und Heizung des Arbeitslokals verwandte Stoffe.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"5.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0023","text":"Der Unterschied zwischen\n Hauptstoff und Hilfsstoff verschwimmt in der eigentlich chemischen Fabri-\n kation, weil keines der angewandten Rohmaterialien als die Substanz des\n Produkts wieder erscheint [Note: Storch unterscheidet das eigentliche Rohmaterial als „matière“ von\n den Hilfsstoffen als „matériaux“: Cherbuliez bezeichnet die Hilfsstoffe als\n „matières instrumentales.“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"5.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0024","text":"Da jedes Ding vielerlei Eigenschaften besitzt und daher verschiedner\n Nutzanwendung fähig ist, kann dasselbe Produkt das Rohmaterial sehr\n verschiedner Arbeitsprozesse bilden. Korn z. B. ist Rohmaterial für\n Müller, Stärkefabrikant, Destillateur, Viehzüchter u. s. w. Es wird Roh-\n material seiner eignen Produktion als Samen. So geht die Kohle als\n Produkt aus der Minenindustrie hervor und als Produktionsmittel in sie ein.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"5.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0025","text":"Dasselbe Produkt mag in demselben Arbeitsprozess als Arbeitsmittel\n und Rohmaterial dienen. Bei der Viehmast z. B., wo das Vieh, das be-\n arbeitete Rohmaterial, zugleich Mittel der Düngerbereitung ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"5.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0026","text":"Ein Produkt kann in einer für die Konsumtion fertigen Form\n existiren und dennoch von neuem zum Rohmaterial eines andern Produkts\n werden, wie die Traube zum Rohmaterial des Weins. Oder der Arbeits-\n prozess entlässt sein Produkt in Formen, worin es nur als Rohmaterial\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"147\", \"facs\": \"#f0166\"} --> eines nachfolgenden Arbeitsprozesses benutzt werden kann. Rohmaterial\n in diesem Zustand heisst Halbfabrikat und hiesse besser Stufen-\n fabrikat, wie z. B. Baumwolle, Faden, Garn u. s. w. Obgleich selbst\n schon Produkt, mag das ursprüngliche Rohmaterial eine ganze Staffel ver-\n schiedner Arbeitsprozesse zu durchlaufen haben, worin es in stets ver-\n änderter Gestalt stets von neuem als Rohmaterial funktionirt bis zum letzten\n Arbeitsprozess, der es als fertiges Lebensmittel oder fertiges Ar-\n beitsmittel von sich abstösst.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"5.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0027","text":"Man sieht: ob ein Gebrauchswerth als Rohmaterial,\n Arbeitsmittel oder Produkt erscheint, hängt ganz und gar ab von\n seiner bestimmten Funktion im Arbeitsprozesse, von der\n Stelle, die er in ihm einnimmt, und mit dem Wechsel dieser Stelle\n wechseln jene Bestimmungen.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"5.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0028","text":"Durch ihren Eintritt als Produktionsmittel in neue Arbeits-\n prozesse verlieren Produkte daher den Charakter des Produkts. Sie\n funktioniren nur noch als gegenständliche Faktoren der lebendigen Arbeit.\n Der Spinner bezieht sich auf die Spindel nur als Mittel, womit, und auf\n den Flachs nur als Gegenstand, den er spinnt. Allerdings kann man nicht\n spinnen ohne Spinnmaterial und Spindel. Das Vorhandensein dieser Pro-\n dukte ist daher vorausgesetzt beim Beginn des wirklichen Spinnprozesses.\nIn diesem Prozess selbst aber ist es eben so gleichgültig, dass\n Flachs und Spindel Produkte vergangner Arbeit sind, wie es im\n wirklichen Ernährungsprozess gleichgültig ist, dass Brod das Produkt\n der vergangnen Arbeiten von Bauer, Müller, Bäcker u. s. w. Umgekehrt.\n Machen Produktionsmittel im Arbeitsprozess ihren Charakter als Produkte\n vergangner Arbeit geltend, so durch ihre Mängel.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"5.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0029","text":"Ein Messer, das nicht\n schneidet, Garn, das beständig zerreisst u. s. w., erinnern lebhaft an Messer-\n schmied A und Garnwichser E. Im gelungenen Produkt ist die Ver-\n mittlung seiner Gebrauchseigenschaften durch vergangne Arbeit ausge-\n löscht.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"5.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0030","text":"Eine Maschine, die nicht im Arbeitsprozess dient, ist nutzlos. Ausser-\n dem verfällt sie der zerstörenden Gewalt des natürlichen Stoffwechsels.\n Das Eisen verrostet, das Holz verfault. Garn, das nicht verwebt oder\n verstrickt wird, ist verdorbene Baumwolle. Die lebendige Arbeit muss\n diese Dinge ergreifen, sie von den Todten erwecken, sie aus nur möglichen\n in wirkliche und wirkende Gebrauchswerthe verwandeln. Vom Feuer der\n [Running matter: 10*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"148\", \"facs\": \"#f0167\"} --> Arbeit beleckt, als Leiber derselben angeeignet, zu ihren begriffs - und\n berufsmässigen Funktionen im Prozess begeistet, werden sie zwar auch\n verzehrt, aber zweckvoll, als Bildungselemente neuer Gebrauchswerthe.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"5.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0031","text":"Wenn vorhandne Produkte nicht nur die Resultate, sondern\n auch die Existenzbedingungen des Arbeitsprozesses sind, ist\n andrerseits ihr Hineinwerfen in den Arbeitsprozess, also ihr Kontakt\n mit lebendiger Arbeit, das einzige Mittel um diese Produkte\n vergangner Arbeit als Gebrauchswerthe zu erhalten und zu\n verwirklichen. Der Arbeitsprozess resultirt überhaupt nur in Ge-\n brauchswerth, so weit sein Produkt fähig, als Lebensmittel in\n die individuelle Konsumtion oder als Produktionsmittel in neuen\n Arbeitsprozess einzugehn.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"5.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0032","text":"Im Arbeitsprozess werden seine stofflichen Elemente, sein Gegenstand\n und sein Mittel, als Gebrauchswerthe verbraucht. Die Arbeit ver-\n speist sie. Der Arbeitsprozess ist also Konsumtionsprozess. Es\n unterscheidet diese produktive Konsumtion von der indivi-\n duellen Konsumtion, dass letztere die Produkte als Lebensmittel\n des lebendigen Individuums, jene sie als Lebensmittel der Arbeit,\n der sich bethätigenden Arbeitskraft, verzehrt. Das Produkt der indivi-\n duellen Konsumtion ist daher der Konsument selbst, das Resultat\n der produktiven Konsumtion ein von dem Konsumenten unterschiednes\nProdukt.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"5.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0033","text":"Sofern Arbeitsmittel und Arbeitsgegenstand selbst schon Produkte\n sind, Resultate vergangner Arbeitsprozesse, ist der Arbeitsprozess ein Pro-\n zess, worin Produkte verzehrt werden um Produkte zu\n schaffen oder worin Produkte als Produktionsmittel von\n Produkten dienen. Wie der Arbeitsprozess aber ursprünglich nur\n zwischen dem Menschen und der ohne sein Zuthun vorhandenen Erde\n vorgeht, dienen in ihm immer noch solche Produktionsmittel, die von\n Natur vorhanden, keine Verbindung von Naturstoff und menschlicher\n Arbeit darstellen.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"5.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0034","text":"Der Arbeitsprozess, wie wir ihn in seinen einfachen und ab-\n strakten Momenten dargestellt haben, ist zweckmässige Thätigkeit zur\n Herstellung von Gebrauchswerthen, Aneignung des Natürlichen für mensch-\n liche Bedürfnisse, allgemeine Bedingung des Stoffwechsels zwischen Mensch\n und Natur, ewige Naturbedingung des menschlichen Lebens und daher\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"149\", \"facs\": \"#f0168\"} --> unabhängig von jeder Form dieses Lebens, vielmehr allen seinen Gesell-\n schaftsformen gleich gemeinsam. Wir hatten daher bei unsrer Darstellung\n nicht nöthig, den Arbeiter im Verhältniss zu andern Arbeitern darzustellen.\n Der Mensch und seine Arbeit auf der einen, die Natur und ihre Stoffe auf\n der andern Seite, genügten.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"5.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0035","text":"So wenig man dem Weizen anschmeckt, wer\n ihn gebaut hat, so wenig sieht man diesem Prozess an, unter welchen Be-\n dingungen er vorgeht, ob unter der brutalen Peitsche des Sklavenaufsehers\n oder unter dem ängstlichen Auge des Kapitalisten, ob Cincinnatus ihn ver-\n richtet in der Bestellung seiner paar jugera, oder der Wilde, der mit einem\n Stein eine Bestie erlegt [Note: Aus diesem höchst logischen Grund entdeckt wohl Oberst Torrens in\n dem Stein des Wilden — den Ursprung des Kapitals. „In dem ersten\nStein, den der Wilde auf die Bestie wirft, die er verfolgt, in dem ersten Stock,\n den er ergreift, um die Frucht niederzuziehn, die er nicht mit den Händen fassen\n kann, sehn wir die Aneignung eines Artikels zum Zweck der Erwerbung eines andern\nund entdecken so — den Ursprung des Kapitals.“ (R. Torrens: An\n Essay on the Production of Wealth etc. p. 79.)","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"5.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0036","text":"Aus jenem ersten Stock\n ist wahrscheinlich auch zu erklären, warum stock im Englischen synonym mit\nKapital ist.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"5.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0037","text":"Kehren wir zu unserem Kapitalisten in spe zurück. Wir ver-\n liessen ihn, nachdem er auf dem Waarenmarkt alle zu einem Arbeitsprozess\n nothwendigen Faktoren gekauft hatte, die gegenständlichen Fak-\n toren in den Produktionsmitteln, den subjektiven Fak-\n tor in der Arbeitskraft. Die bestimmte Art dieser Produktions-\n mittel und dieser Arbeitskraft richtet sich nach der Art des Arbeitsprozesses,\n wofür sie bestimmt sind, ob Stiefel, Garn u. s. w. gemacht werden sollen.\n Unser Kapitalist setzt sich also daran, die von ihm gekaufte Waare, die\n Arbeitskraft, zu konsumiren, d. h. den Träger der Arbeitskraft, den\n Arbeiter die Produktionsmittel durch seine Arbeit konsumiren zu\n lassen. Die allgemeine Natur des Arbeitsprozesses ändert sich\n natürlich nicht dadurch, dass der Arbeiter ihn für den Kapitalisten, statt\n für sich selbst verrichtet.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"5.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0038","text":"Aber auch die bestimmte Art und\n Weise wie Stiefel gemacht oder Garn gesponnen wird, kann sich zu-\n nächst nicht ändern durch die Dazwischenkunft des Kapitalisten. Er muss\n die Arbeitskraft zunächst nehmen, wie er sie auf dem Markt vorfindet, also\n auch ihre Arbeit, wie sie in einer Periode entsprang, wo es noch keine\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"150\", \"facs\": \"#f0169\"} --> Kapitalisten gab. Die Verwandlung der Produktionsweise\n selbst durch die Unterordnung der Arbeit unter das Kapital kann sich erst\n später ereignen und ist daher erst später zu betrachten.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"5.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0039","text":"Der Arbeitsprozess, wie er als Konsumtionsprozess der\n Arbeitskraft durch den Kapitalisten vorgeht, zeigt nur zwei\n eigenthümliche Phänomene.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"5.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0040","text":"Der Arbeiter arbeitet unter der Kontrole des Kapita-\n listen, dem seine Arbeit gehört. Der Kapitalist passt auf, dass die\n Arbeit ordentlich verrichtet wird und dass die Produktionsmittel zweckge-\n mäss verwandt werden, also kein Rohmaterial vergeudet und das Arbeits-\n instrument geschont, d. h. nur so weit zerstört wird, als unzertrennlich ist\n von seinem Gebrauch in der Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"5.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0041","text":"Zweitens aber: das Produkt ist Eigenthum des Kapita-\n listen, nicht des unmittelbaren Produzenten, des Arbeiters. Der Kapi-\n talist zahlt z. B. den Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft. Ihr Ge-\n brauch, wie der jeder andern Waare, z. B. eines Pferdes, das er für einen\n Tag gemiethet, gehört ihm also für den Tag. Dem Käufer der Waare\n gehört der Gebrauch der Waare, und der Besitzer der Arbeitskraft giebt\n in der That nur den von ihm verkauften Gebrauchswerth, indem er seine\n Arbeit giebt. Von dem Augenblicke, wo er in die Werkstätte des Ka-\n pitalisten trat, gehörte der Gebrauchswerth seiner Arbeitskraft, also\n ihr Gebrauch, die Arbeit, dem Kapitalisten. Der Kapitalist hat durch\n den Kauf der Arbeitskraft die Arbeit selbst als lebendigen Gäh-\n rungsstoff den todten von ihm gleichfalls besessenen Bildungselementen des\n Produkts einverleibt.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"5.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0042","text":"Von seinem Standpunkt ist der Arbeits-\n prozess nur die Konsumtion der von ihm gekauften Waare Arbeits-\n kraft, die er jedoch nur konsumiren kann, indem er ihr Produktions-\n mittel zusetzt. Der Arbeitsprozess ist ein Prozess zwischen Dingen,\n die der Kapitalist gekauft hat, zwischen ihm gehörigen\n Dingen. Das Produkt dieses Prozesses gehört ihm daher\n ganz eben so sehr als das Produkt des Gährungsprozesses in seinem\n Weinkeller [Note: „Die Produkte sind appropriirt, bevor sie in Kapital verwandelt werden;\n diese Verwandlung entzieht sie nicht jener Appropriation.“ (Cherbuliez:\n Riche ou Pauvre, edit. Paris 1841, p. 53, 54.) „Indem der Proletarier] .","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"5.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0043","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"151\", \"facs\": \"#f0170\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"5.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0044","text":"Das Produkt — das Eigenthum des Kapitalisten — ist ein Ge-\n brauchswerth, Garn, Stiefel u. s. w. Aber obgleich Stiefel z. B. ge-\n wissermassen die Basis des gesellschaftlichen Fortschritts bilden und unser\n Kapitalist ein entschiedener Fortschrittsmann ist, fabrizirt er die Stiefel\n nicht ihrer selbst wegen. Der Gebrauchswerth ist überhaupt nicht\n das Ding „qu’on aime pour lui même“ in der Waarenpro-\n duktion. Gebrauchswerthe werden hier überhaupt nur produzirt, weil\n und sofern sie materielles Substrat, Träger des Tausch-\n werths sind. Und unserem Kapitalisten handelt es sich um zweierlei.\nErstens will er einen Gebrauchswerth produziren, der einen Tausch-\n werth hat, einen zum Verkauf bestimmten Artikel, eine Waare.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"5.44"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0045","text":"Und\n zweitens will er eine Waare produziren, deren Werth höher als die\nWerthsumme der zu ihrer Produktion erheischten Waa-\n ren, der Produktionsmittel und der Arbeitskraft, für die er sein gutes Geld\n auf dem Waarenmarkt vorschoss. Er will nicht nur einen Ge-\n brauchswerth produziren, sondern eine Waare, nicht nur Ge-\n brauchswerth, sondern Tauschwerth, und nicht nur Werth, sondern auch\nMehrwerth.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"5.45"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0046","text":"In der That, da es sich hier um Waarenproduktion handelt,\n haben wir bisher offenbar nur eine Seite des Prozesses betrachtet.\n Wie die Waare selbst Einheit von Gebrauchswerth und Tausch-\n werth, muss ihr Produktionsprozess Einheit von Arbeits-\n prozess und Werthbildungsprozess sein.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"5.46"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0047","text":"[Note: seine Arbeit gegen ein bestimmtes Quantum Lebensmittel (approvisionnement)\n verkauft, verzichtet er vollständig auf jeden Antheil am Produkt. Die Appro-\n priation der Produkte bleibt dieselbe wie vorher; sie ist in keiner Weise durch die\n erwähnte Konvention verändert. Das Produkt gehört ausschliesslich dem Kapi-\n talisten, der die Rohstoffe und das Approvisionnement geliefert hat. Es ist diess\n eine strenge Konsequenz des Gesetzes der Appropriation, dessen Fundamental-\n prinzip umgekehrt das ausschliessliche Eigenthumsrecht jedes Arbeiters an\n seinem Produkte war.“ (l. c. p. 58.) James Mill: Elements of Pol.\n Econ. etc. p. 75: „Wenn die Arbeiter für Arbeitslohn arbeiten, ist der Kapi-\n talist Eigenthümer nicht nur des Kapitals (meint hier die Produktionsmittel),\n sondern auch der Arbeit (of labour also).","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"5.47"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0048","text":"Wenn man das, was für Arbeitslohn\n gezahlt wird, wie diess gebräuchlich, in den Begriff Kapital einschliesst, ist es\nabgeschmackt von der Arbeit getrennt vom Kapital zu sprechen.\n Das Wort Kapital in diesem Sinn schliesst beides ein, Kapital und Arbeit.“]","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"5.48"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0049","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"152\", \"facs\": \"#f0171\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"5.49"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0050","text":"Betrachten wir den Produktionsprozess also jetzt auch als\nWerthbildungsprozess.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"5.50"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0051","text":"Wir wissen, dass der Werth jeder Waare bestimmt ist durch das\n Quantum der in ihrem Gebrauchswerth materialisirten Arbeit,\n durch die zu ihrer Produktion gesellschaftlich nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit. Diess gilt auch für das Produkt, das sich unsrem Kapi-\n talisten als Resultat des Arbeitsprozesses ergab. Es ist also zunächst die\n in diesem Produkt vergegenständlichte Arbeit zu be-\n rechnen.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"5.51"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0052","text":"Es sei z. B. Garn.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"5.52"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0053","text":"Zur Herstellung des Garns war zuerst sein Rohmaterial nöthig,\n z. B. 10 Pfund Baumwolle. Was der Werth der Baumwolle, ist nicht\n erst zu untersuchen, denn der Kapitalist hat sie auf dem Markt zu ihrem\n Werth, z. B. zu 10 sh. gekauft. In dem Preise der Baumwolle ist die\n zu ihrer Produktion erheischte Arbeit schon als allgemeine gesellschaftliche\n Arbeit dargestellt. Wir wollen ferner annehmen, dass die in der Verar-\n beitung der Baumwolle verzehrte Spindelmasse, die uns alle an-\n deren aufgewandten Arbeitsmittel repräsentirt, einen Werth von 2 sh. be-\n sitzt. Ist eine Goldmasse von 12 sh. das Produkt von 24 Arbeitsstunden\n oder zwei Arbeitstagen, so folgt zunächst, dass im Garn zwei Arbeitstage\n vergegenständlicht sind.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"5.53"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0054","text":"Der Umstand, dass die Baumwolle ihre Form verändert hat und die\n aufgezehrte Spindelmasse ganz verschwunden ist, darf nicht beirren. Nach\n dem allgemeinen Werthgesetz sind z. B. 10 lbs. Garn ein Aequivalent für 10\n lbs. Baumwolle und ¼ Spindel, wenn der Werth von 40 lbs. Garn = dem\n Werth von 40 lbs. Baumwolle + dem Werth einer ganzen Spindel, d. h.\n wenn dieselbe Arbeitszeit erfordert ist um beide Seiten dieser\n Gleichung zu produziren. In diesem Fall stellt sich dieselbe Arbeits-\n zeit das einemal in dem Gebrauchswerth Garn, das andremal in den Ge-\n brauchswerthen Baumwolle und Spindel dar. Der Tauschwerth ist also\n gleichgültig dagegen, ob er in Garn, Spindel oder Baumwolle erscheint.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"5.54"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0055","text":"Dass Spindel und Baumwolle, statt ruhig neben einander zu liegen, im\n Spinnprozesse eine Verbindung eingehn, welche die Form ihres Gebrauchs-\n werths verändert, sie in Garn verwandelt, berührt ihren Tausch-\n werth eben so wenig, als wenn sie durch einfachen Austausch gegen\n ein Aequivalent von Garn umgesetzt worden wären.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"5.55"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0056","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"153\", \"facs\": \"#f0172\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"5.56"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0057","text":"Die zur Produktion der Baumwolle erheischte Arbeitszeit ist Theil\n der zur Produktion des Garns, dessen Rohmaterial sie bildet, erheischten\n Arbeitszeit und deshalb im Garn enthalten. Ebenso verhält es sich mit\n der Arbeitszeit, die zur Produktion der Spindelmasse erheischt ist, ohne\n deren Verschleiss oder Konsum die Baumwolle nicht versponnen werden\n kann [Note: „Not only the labour applied immediately to commodities affect their\n value, but the labour also which is bestowed on the implements, tools, and build-\n ings with which such labour is assisted.“ Ricardo l. c. p. 16.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"5.57"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0058","text":"So weit also der Werth des Garns, die zu seiner Herstellung er-\n heischte Arbeitszeit, in Betracht kommt, können die verschied-\n nen besondern, der Zeit und dem Raum nach getrennten Ar-\n beitsprozesse, die durchlaufen werden müssen, um die Baumwolle zu\n produziren, die vernutzte Spindelmasse zu produziren, endlich aus Baum-\n wolle und Spindel Garn zu machen, als verschiedne successive Phasen\neines und desselben Arbeitsprozesses betrachtet werden.\n Alle im Garn enthaltne Arbeit ist vergangne Arbeit. Dass die zur\n Produktion seiner Bildungselemente erheischte Arbeitszeit früher ver-\n gangen war, im Plusquamperfectum steht, dagegen die zum Schlussprozess,\n dem Spinnen, unmittelbar verwandte Arbeit dem Präsens näher, im Per-\n fectum steht, ist ein durchaus gleichgültiger Umstand. Ist eine bestimmte\n Masse Arbeit, z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"5.58"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0059","text":"von 30 Arbeitstagen, zum Bau eines Hauses nöthig,\n so ändert es nichts am Gesammtquantum der dem Hause einverleib-\n ten Arbeitszeit, dass der 30. Arbeitstag 29 Tage später in die Produktion\n einging als der erste Arbeitstag. Und so kann die im Arbeitsmaterial\n und Arbeitsmittel enthaltene Arbeitszeit ganz so betrachtet werden, als\n wäre sie nur in einem früheren Stadium des Spinnprozesses verausgabt\n worden als das zuletzt in der Form des Spinnens verausgabte Arbeits-\n quantum.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"5.59"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0060","text":"Die Werthe der Produktionsmittel, der Baumwolle und der Spindel,\n ausgedrückt in dem Preise von 12 sh., bilden also Bestandtheile des Garn-\n werths, oder des Werths des Produkts.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"5.60"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0061","text":"Nur müssen zwei Bedingungen erfüllt sein. Einmal müssen Baum-\n wolle und Spindel wirklich zur Produktion eines Gebrauchswerths\n gedient haben. Es muss in unserm Fall Garn aus ihnen geworden sein.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"154\", \"facs\": \"#f0173\"} -->Welcher Gebrauchswerth ihn trägt, ist dem Tauschwerth gleichgültig,\n aber ein Gebrauchswerth muss ihn tragen. Zweitens ist voraus-\n gesetzt, dass nur die unter den gegebenen gesellschaftlichen Pro-\n duktionsbedingungen nothwendige Arbeitszeit verwandt worden\n ist. Wäre also nur 1 Pfund Baumwolle nöthig, um 1 Pfund Garn zu\n spinnen, so darf nur 1 Pfund Baumwolle verzehrt sein in der Bildung von\n 1 Pfund Garn. Ebenso verhält es sich mit der Spindel. Hätte der Kapi-\n talist die Phantasie mit goldenen, statt mit eisernen Spindeln spinnen zu\n lassen, so zählte im Garnwerth dennoch nur die gesellschaftlich\n nothwendige Arbeitszeit, d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"5.61"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0062","text":"die zur Produktion der eisernen\n Spindelmasse nothwendige Arbeitszeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"5.62"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0063","text":"Wir kennen jetzt den Werththeil, den die Produktionsmittel,\n Baumwolle und Spindel, im Garnwerth bilden. Er ist gleich 12 sh.,\n oder die Materiatur von zwei Arbeitstagen. Es handelt sich also nun um\n den Werththeil, welchen die Arbeit des Spinners selbst der Baum-\n wolle zusetzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"5.63"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0064","text":"Wir haben diese Arbeit jetzt von einem ganz anderen Gesichtspunkte\n zu betrachten, als während des Arbeitsprozesses. Dort handelte es\n sich um die zweckmässige Thätigkeit, Baumwolle in Garn zu verwandeln.\n Je zweckmässiger die Arbeit, desto besser das Garn, alle andern Umstände\n als gleichbleibend vorausgesetzt. Die Arbeit des Spinners war spezifisch\n verschieden von andern produktiven Arbeiten, und diese Verschiedenheit\n offenbarte sich subjektiv und objektiv, im besondern Zweck des Spinnens,\n seiner besondern Operationsweise, der besondern Natur seiner Produktions-\n mittel, dem spezifischen Gebrauchswerth seines Produkts. Baumwolle und\n Spindel dienen als Lebensmittel der Spinnarbeit, aber man kann\n mit ihnen keine gezogenen Kanonen machen. Sofern die Arbeit des\n Spinners dagegen werthbildend ist, d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"5.64"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0065","text":"Quelle von Tausch-\n werth, ist sie durchaus nicht verschieden von der Arbeit des Kanonenboh-\n rers, oder, was uns hier näher liegt, von den Arbeiten, die in den Produk-\n tionsmitteln des Garns verwirklicht sind, den Arbeiten des Baumwollpflanzers\n und des Spindelmachers. Nur wegen dieser Identität können Baum-\n wollpflanzen, Spindelmachen und Spinnen blos quantitativ verschiedene\n Theile desselben Gesammtwerths, des Garnwerths, bilden. Es\n handelt sich hier nicht mehr um die Qualität, die Beschaffenheit und\n den Inhalt der Arbeit, sondern nur noch um ihre Quantität. Diese\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"155\", \"facs\": \"#f0174\"} --> ist einfach zu zählen. Wir nehmen an, dass die Spinnarbeit einfache\n Arbeit, gesellschaftliche Durchschnittsarbeit ist. Man wird später sehn,\n dass die gegentheilige Annahme nichts an der Sache ändern würde.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"5.65"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0066","text":"Während des Arbeitsprozesses setzt sich die Arbeit beständig aus der\n Form der Unruhe in die des Seins, aus der Form der Bewegung in die der\n Gegenständlichkeit um. Am Ende einer Stunde ist die Spinnbewegung\n in einem gewissen Quantum Garn dargestellt, also ein bestimmtes Quan-\n tum Arbeit, eine Arbeitsstunde in der Baumwolle vergegen-\n ständlicht. Wir sagen Arbeitsstunde, denn die Spinnarbeit gilt hier\n nur, so weit sie Verausgabung von Arbeitskraft, nicht so weit sie die\n spezifische Arbeit des Spinnens ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"5.66"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0067","text":"Es ist nun entscheidend wichtig, dass während der Dauer des Pro-\n zesses, d. h. der Verwandlung von Baumwolle in Garn, nur die gesell-\n schaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit verzehrt wird. Müssen\n unter normalen, d. h. durchschnittlichen gesellschaftlichen Produk-\n tionsbedingungen, a Pfund Baumwolle während einer Arbeitsstunde in\n b Pfund Garn verwandelt sein, so gilt nur der Arbeitstag als Arbeitstag\n von 12 Stunden, der 12 × a Pfund Baumwolle in 12 × b Pfund Garn\n verwandelt. Denn nur die gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit zählt\n als werthbildend.","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"5.67"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0068","text":"Rohmaterial und Produkt erscheinen hier in einem ganz ande-\n ren Licht als vom Standpunkt des eigentlichen Arbeitsprozesses. Das\n Rohmaterial gilt hier nur als Aufsauger eines bestimmten\n Quantums Arbeit. Durch diese Aufsaugung verwandelt es sich in\n der That in Garn, weil ihm Spinnarbeit zugesetzt wurde. Aber das\n Produkt, das Garn, ist jetzt nur Gradmesser der von der Baumwolle ein-\n gesaugten Arbeit. Wird in einer Stunde 1⅔ lbs. Baumwolle versponnen\n oder in 1⅔ lbs. Garn verwandelt, so zeigen 10 lbs. Garn 6 eingesaugte\n Arbeitsstunden an. Bestimmte und erfahrungsmässig festge-\n stellte Quanta Produkt stellen jetzt nichts dar als bestimmte Quanta\n Arbeit, bestimmte Masse festgeronnener Arbeitszeit. Sie sind nur noch\nMateriatur von einer Stunde, zwei Stunden, einem Tag gesell-\n schaftlicher Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"5.68"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0069","text":"Dass die Arbeit grade Spinnarbeit, ihr Material Baumwolle und ihr Pro-\n dukt Garn, wird hier eben so gleichgültig, als dass der Arbeitsgegen-\n stand selbst schon Produkt, also Rohmaterial ist. Wäre der Arbei-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"156\", \"facs\": \"#f0175\"} --> ter, statt in der Spinnerei, in der Kohlengrube beschäftigt, so wäre der\n Arbeitsgegenstand, die Kohle, von Natur vorhanden. Dennoch stellte ein\n bestimmtes Quantum aus dem Bett losgebrochener Kohle, z. B. ein Cent-\n ner, ein bestimmtes Quantum aufgesaugter Arbeit dar.","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"5.69"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0070","text":"Beim Verkauf der Arbeitskraft ward unterstellt, dass ihr Ta-\n geswerth = 3 sh., und in den letztern 6 Arbeitsstunden verkörpert sind,\n diess Arbeitsquantum also erheischt ist, um die Durchschnittssumme der\n täglichen Lebensmittel des Arbeiters zu produziren. Verwandelt unser\n Spinner nun während einer Arbeitsstunde 1⅔ lbs. Baumwolle in 1⅔ lbs.\n Garn [Note: Die Zahlen hier sind ganz willkührlich.] , so in 6 Stunden 10 lbs. Baumwolle in 10 lbs. Garn. Während\n der Dauer des Spinnprozesses saugt die Baumwolle also 6 Arbeitsstunden\n ein. Dieselbe Arbeitszeit stellt sich in einem Goldquantum von 3 sh. dar.\n Der Baumwolle wurde also durch das Spinnen selbst ein Werth von 3 sh.\n zugesetzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"5.70"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0071","text":"Sehn wir uns nun den Gesammtwerth des Produkts, der\n 10 lbs. Garn, an. In ihnen sind 2½ Arbeitstage vergegenständlicht,\n 2 Tage enthalten in Baumwolle und Spindelmasse, ½ Tag Arbeit einge-\n saugt während des Spinnprozesses. Dieselbe Arbeitszeit stellt sich in\n einer Goldmasse von 15 sh. dar. Der dem Werth der 10 lbs. Garn adä-\n quate Preis beträgt also 15 sh., der Preis eines lb. Garn 1 sh. 6 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"5.71"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0072","text":"Unser Kapitalist stutzt. Der Werth des Produkts ist gleich\n dem Werth des vorgeschossenen Kapitals. Der vorge-\n schossene Werth hat sich nicht verwerthet, keinen Mehrwerth\n erzeugt, Geld sich also nicht in Kapital verwandelt. Der Preis der\n 10 lbs. Garn ist 15 sh. und 15 sh. wurden verausgabt auf dem Waarenmarkt\n für die Bildungselemente des Produkts oder, was dasselbe, die\nFaktoren des Arbeitsprozesses, 10 sh. für Baumwolle, 2 sh.\n für die verzehrte Spindelmasse, und 3 sh. für Arbeitskraft. Der aufge-\n schwollene Werth des Garns hilft nichts, denn sein Werth ist\n nur die Summe der früher auf Baumwolle, Spindel und Arbeitskraft ver-\n theilten Werthe, und aus einer solchen blossen Addition vor-\n handner Werthe kann nun und nimmermehr ein Mehrwerth ent-\n springen [Note: Diess ist der Fundamentalsatz, worauf die Lehre der Physiokraten von] .","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"5.72"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0073","text":"Diese Werthe sind jetzt alle auf ein Ding konzentrirt, aber\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"157\", \"facs\": \"#f0176\"} --> so waren sie in der Geldsumme von 15 sh., bevor diese sich durch drei\n Waarenkäufe zersplitterte.","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"5.73"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0074","text":"An und für sich ist diess Resultat nicht befremdlich. Der Werth\n eines lb. Garn ist 1 sh. 6 d. und für 10 lbs. Garn müsste unser Kapitalist\n daher auf dem Waarenmarkt 15 sh. zahlen. Ob er sich sein Privathaus\n fertig auf dem Markt kauft, oder es selbst bauen lässt, keine dieser Ope-\n rationen wird das im Erwerb des Hauses ausgelegte Geld vermehren.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"5.74"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0075","text":"Der Kapitalist, der in der Vulgärökonomie Bescheid weiss, sagt viel-\n leicht, er habe sein Geld mit der Absicht vorgeschossen, mehr Geld\n daraus zu machen. Der Weg zur Hölle ist jedoch mit guten Ab-\n sichten gepflastert und er konnte eben so gut der Absicht sein, Geld zu\n machen, ohne zu produziren [Note: So z. B. entzog er 1844—47 Theil seines Kapitals dem produktiven Ge-\n schäft, um es in Eisenbahnaktien zu verspekuliren. So, zur Zeit des amerikani-\n schen Bürgerkriegs, schloss er die Fabrik und warf den Fabrikarbeiter auf’s Pflaster,\n um auf der Liverpooler Baumwollbörse zu spielen.] . Er droht. Man werde ihn nicht wieder\n ertappen. Künftig werde er die Waare fertig auf dem Markt kaufen,\n statt sie selbst zu fabriziren. Wenn aber alle seine Brüder Kapitalisten\n dessgleichen thun, wo soll er Waare auf dem Markt finden? Und Geld\n kann er nicht essen. Er katechisirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"5.75"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0076","text":"Man soll seine Abstinenz be-\n denken. Er konnte seine 15 sh. verprassen. Statt dessen hat er sie\nproduktiv konsumirt und Garn daraus gemacht. Aber dafür ist er\n ja im Besitz von Garn statt von Gewissensbissen. Er muss bei Leibe\n nicht in die Rolle des Schatzbildners zurückfallen, der uns zeigte,\n was bei der Ascetik herauskommt. Ausserdem, wo nichts ist, hat der\n Kaiser sein Recht verloren. Welches immer das Verdienst seiner Ent-\n sagung, es ist nichts da, um sie extra zu zahlen, da der Werth des\n Produkts, der aus dem Prozess herauskommt, nur gleich der Summe der\n [Note: der Unproduktivität aller nicht agrikolen Arbeit beruht und er ist unumstösslich\n für den Oekonomen — von Fach.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"5.76"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0077","text":"„Cette façon d’imputer à une seule chose la\n valeur de plusieurs autres (par exemple au lin la consommation du tisserand),\n d’appliquer, pour ainsi dire, couche sur couche, plusieurs valeurs sur une\n seule, fait que celle-ci grossit d’autant . . . . Le terme d’addition peint très-\n bien la manière dont se forme le prix des ouvrages de main d’oeuvre; ce\n prix n’est qu’un total de plusieurs valeurs consommées et additionnées ensemble;\n or, additionner n’est pas multiplier.“ (Mercier de la Rivière l. c.\n p. 599.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"158\", \"facs\": \"#f0177\"} --> hineingeworfenen Waarenwerthe. Er beruhige sich also dabei, dass Tu-\n gend der Tugend Lohn. Statt dessen wird er zudringlich. Das Garn\n ist ihm unnütz. Er hat es für den Verkauf produzirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"5.77"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0078","text":"So verkaufe er\n es, oder, noch einfacher, produzire in Zukunft nur Dinge für seinen\n eignen Bedarf, ein Rezept, das ihm bereits sein Hausarzt Mac Culloch\n als probates Mittel gegen die Epidemie der Ueberproduktion verschrieben\n hat. Er stellt sich trutzig auf die Hinterbeine. Sollte der Arbeiter\n mit seinen eignen Gliedmassen in der blauen Luft Arbeitsgebilde schaffen,\n Waaren produziren? Gab er ihm nicht den Stoff, womit und worin er\n allein seine Arbeit verleiblichen kann? Da nun der grösste Theil der Ge-\n sellschaft aus solchen Habenichtsen besteht, hat er nicht der Gesellschaft\n durch seine Produktionsmittel, seine Baumwolle und seine Spindel, einen\n unermesslichen Dienst erwiesen, nicht dem Arbeiter selbst, den er oben-\n drein noch mit Lebensmitteln versah? Und soll er den Dienst nicht be-\n rechnen?","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"5.78"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0079","text":"Hat der Arbeiter ihm aber nicht den Gegendienst erwiesen,\n Baumwolle und Spindel in Garn zu verwandeln? Ausserdem handelt es\n sich hier nicht um Dienste [Note: „Las du rhümen, schmücken und putzen . . . . Wer aber mehr oder\n besseres nimpt (als er giebt), das ist Wucher, und heisst, nicht Dienst, son-\n dern Schaden gethan seinem Nehesten, als mit stelen und rauben geschicht. Es\n ist nicht alles Dienst und wolgethan dem Nehesten, was man heisst, Dienst und\n wolgethan. Denn eine Ehebrecherin und Ehebrecher thun einander grossen Dienst\n und wolgefallen. Ein Reuter thut einem Mordbrenner grossen reuterdienst, das\n er im hilfft, auff der strassen rauben, Land und Leute bevehden.","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"5.79"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0080","text":"Die Papisten\n thun den unsern grossen Dienst, das sie nicht alle ertrenken, verbrennen, ermor-\n den, im Gefengniss verfaulen lassen, sondern lassen doch etliche leben, und ver-\n jagen sie, oder nemen jenen was sie haben. Der Teuffel thut selber seinen Die-\n nern grossen, unermesslichen Dienst . . . . Summa, die Welt ist voll grosser,\n trefflicher, teglicher Dienst und wolthaten.“ (Martin Luther: An die\n Pfarherrn, wider den Wucher zu predigen etc. Wittenberg\n 1540.)] . Ein Dienst ist nichts als die nützliche\n Wirkung eines Gebrauchswerths, sei es der Waare, sei es der Ar-\n beit [Note: Ich bemerke darüber in „Zur Kritik der Pol. Oek.“ p. 14 u. a.:\n „Man begreift, welchen „Dienst“ die Kategorie „Dienst“ (service) einer\n Sorte Oekonomen wie J. B. Say und F. Bastiat leisten muss.“] . Hier aber gilt’s den Tauschwerth. Er zahlte dem Arbeiter\n den Werth von 3 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"5.80"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0081","text":"Der Arbeiter gab ihm ein exaktes Aequivalent\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"159\", \"facs\": \"#f0178\"} --> zurück in dem der Baumwolle zugesetzten Werth von 3 sh., Werth für\n Werth. Unser Freund, eben noch so kapitalübermüthig, nimmt plötzlich\n die anspruchslose Haltung seines eignen Arbeiters an. Hat er nicht selbst\n gearbeitet? nicht die Arbeit der Ueberwachung, der Oberaufsicht über\n den Spinner verrichtet? Bildet diese seine Arbeit nicht auch Werth? Sein\n eigner overlooker und sein manager zucken die Achseln. Unterdess hat\n er aber bereits mit heitrem Lächeln seine alte Physiognomie wieder an-\n genommen. Er foppte uns mit der ganzen Litanei. Er giebt keinen\n Deut darum. Er überlässt diese und ähnliche faule Ausflüchte und hohle\n Flausen den dafür eigens bezahlten Professoren der politischen Oekonomie.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"5.81"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0082","text":"Er selbst ist ein praktischer Mann, der zwar nicht immer bedenkt, was er\n ausserhalb des Geschäfts sagt, aber stets weiss, was er im Geschäft thut.","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"5.82"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0083","text":"Sehn wir näher zu. Der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft be-\n trug 3 sh., weil in ihr selbst ein halber Arbeitstag vergegenständlicht\n ist, d. h. weil die täglich zur Produktion der Arbeitskraft nöthigen Lebens-\n mittel einen halben Arbeitstag kosten. Aber die vergangene Arbeit, die\n in der Arbeitskraft steckt, und die lebendige Arbeit, die sie leisten kann,\n ihre täglichen Erhaltungskosten und ihre tägliche Verausgabung, sind zwei\n ganz verschiedne Grössen. Die erstere bestimmt ihren Tauschwerth, die\n andere bildet ihren Gebrauchswerth. Dass ein halber Arbeitstag\n nöthig, um ihn während 24 Stunden am Leben zu erhalten, hindert den\n Arbeiter keineswegs einen ganzen Tag zu arbeiten. Der Werth\n der Arbeitskraft und ihre Verwerthung im Arbeitsprozess sind also\n zwei verschiedne Grössen. Diese Werthdifferenz hatte der Kapita-\n list im Auge, als er die Arbeitskraft kaufte.","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"5.83"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0084","text":"Ihre nützliche Eigenschaft,\n Garn oder Stiefel zu machen, war nur eine conditio sine qua, weil Arbeit\n in nützlicher Form verausgabt werden muss, um Werth zu bilden. Was aber\n entschied, war der spezifische Gebrauchswerth dieser Waare,\n Quelle von Tauschwerth zu sein und von mehr Tauschwerth als sie selbst\n hat. Diess ist der spezifische Dienst, den der Kapitalist von ihr er-\n wartet. Und er verfährt dabei den ewigen Gesetzen des Waarenaus-\n tausches gemäss. In der That, der Verkäufer der Arbeitskraft, wie der\n Verkäufer jeder andern Waare, realisirtihren Tauschwerth und\nveräussert ihren Gebrauchswerth. Er kann den einen nicht\n erhalten, ohne den andern wegzugeben. Der Gebrauchswerth der Arbeits-\n kraft, die Arbeit selbst, gehört eben so wenig ihrem Verkäufer, wie der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"160\", \"facs\": \"#f0179\"} --> Gebrauchswerth des verkauften Oels dem Oelhändler.","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"5.84"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0085","text":"Der Geldbesitzer\n hat den Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft gezahlt; ihm gehört daher ihr\n Gebrauch während des Tages, die tagelange Arbeit. Der\n Umstand, dass die tägliche Erhaltung der Arbeitskraft nur einen halben\n Arbeitstag kostet, obgleich die Arbeitskraft einen ganzen Tag wirken, arbei-\n ten kann, dass daher der Werth, den ihr Gebrauch während eines Tags\n schafft, doppelt so gross ist als ihr eigner Tageswerth, ist ein besondres\n Glück für den Käufer, aber durchaus kein Unrecht gegen den Verkäufer.","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"5.85"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0086","text":"Unser Kapitalist hat den Casus vorgesehn. Der Arbeiter findet da-\n her in der Werkstätte die nöthigen Produktionsmittel nicht nur für einen\n sechsstündigen, sondern für einen zwölfstündigen Arbeitsprozess. Saugten\n 10 lbs. Baumwolle 6 Arbeitsstunden ein und verwandelten sich in 10 lbs.\n Garn, so werden 20 lbs. Baumwolle 12 Arbeitsstunden einsaugen und in 20\n lbs. Garn verwandelt. Betrachten wir das Produkt des verlänger-\n ten Arbeitsprozesses. In den 20 lbs. Garn sind jetzt 5 Arbeits-\n tage vergegenständlicht, 4 in der verzehrten Baumwoll- und Spindel-\n masse, 1 von der Baumwolle eingesaugt während des Spinnprozesses.\n Der Goldausdruck von 5 Arbeitstagen ist aber 30 sh. oder 1 Pfd. St. 10 sh.\n Diess also der Preis der 20 lbs. Garn. Das Pfund Garn kostet nach wie\n vor 1 sh. 6 d. Aber die Werthsumme der in den Prozess geworfenen\n Waaren betrug 27 sh. Der Werth des Garns beträgt 30 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"5.86"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0087","text":"Der Werth\n des Produkts ist um ⅓ gewachsen über den zu seiner Produktion vor-\n geschossenen Werth. So haben sich 27 sh. in 30 sh. verwandelt. Sie\n haben einen Mehrwerth von 3 sh. gesetzt. Das Kunststück ist end-\n lich gelungen. Geld ist in Kapital verwandelt.","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"5.87"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0088","text":"Alle Bedingungen des Problems sind gelöst und die Gesetze des\n Waarenaustausches in keiner Weise verletzt. Aequivalent wurde gegen\n Aequivalent ausgetauscht. Der Kapitalist zahlte als Käufer jede Waare\n zu ihrem Werth, Baumwolle, Spindelmasse, Arbeitskraft. Er that dann,\n was jeder andre Käufer von Waaren thut. Er konsumirte ihren Ge-\n brauchswerth. Der Konsumtionsprozess der Arbeitskraft,\n der zugleich Produktionsprozess der Waare, ergab ein Produkt\n von 20 lbs. Garn mit einem Werth von 30 sh. Der Kapitalist kehrt nun\n zum Markt zurück und verkauft Waare, nachdem er Waare gekauft hat.\n Er verkauft das Pfund Garn zu 1 sh. 6 d., keinen Deut über oder unter\n seinem Werth. Und doch zieht er 3 sh. mehr aus der Cirkulation her-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"161\", \"facs\": \"#f0180\"} --> aus als er ursprünglich in sie hineinwarf.","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"5.88"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0089","text":"Dieser ganze Verlauf, die Ver-\n wandlung seines Geldes in Kapital, geht in der Cirkulationssphäre vor und\n geht nicht in ihr vor. Durch die Vermittlung der Cirkulation, weil\n bedingt durch den Kauf der Arbeitskraft auf dem Waarenmarkt.\nNicht in der Cirkulation, denn diese leitet nur den Verwerthungs-\n prozess ein, der sich in der Produktionssphäre zuträgt. Und\n so ist „tout pour le mieux dans le meilleur des mondes possibles.“","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"5.89"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0090","text":"Indem der Kapitalist Geld in Waaren verwandelt, die als Stoffbildner\n eines neuen Produkts oder als Faktoren des Arbeitsprozesses dienen, in-\n dem er ihrer todten Gegenständlichkeit lebendige Arbeitskraft einverleibt,\n verwandelt er Werth, vergangne, vergegenständlichte, todte Arbeit in\nKapital, sich selbst verwerthenden Werth, ein beseeltes Unge-\n heuer, das zu „arbeiten“ beginnt, als hätt’ es Lieb’ im Leibe.","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"5.90"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0091","text":"Vergleichen wir nun Werthbildungsprozess und Verwer-\n thungsprozess, so ist der Verwerthungsprozess nichts als ein über\n einen gewissen Punkt hinaus verlängerter Werthbildungsprozess.\n Dauert der letztre nur bis zu dem Punkt, wo der vom Kapital ge-\n zahlte Werth der Arbeitskraft durch ein neues Aequivalent ersetzt\n ist, so ist er einfacher Werthbildungsprozess. Dauert der Werthbildungs-\n prozess über diesen Punkt hinaus, so wird er Verwerthungsprozess.","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"5.91"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0092","text":"Vergleichen wir ferner den Werthbildungsprozess mit dem\nArbeitsprozess, so besteht der letztere in der wirklichen Arbeit,\n die Gebrauchswerthe produzirt. Die Bewegung wird hier quali-\n tativ betrachtet, in ihrer besondern Art und Weise, nach Zweck und In-\n halt. Derselbe Arbeitsprozess stellt sich im Werthbildungs-\n prozess nur von seiner quantitativen Seite dar. Es handelt sich\n nur noch um die Zeit, welche die Arbeit zu ihrer Operation braucht,\n oder um die Dauer, während deren die Arbeitskraft verausgabt wird. Hier\n gelten auch die Waaren, die in den Arbeitsprozess eingehn, nicht mehr als\n funktionell bestimmte, stoffliche Faktoren der zweckmässig wirkenden Ar-\n beitskraft. Sie zählen nur noch als bestimmte Quanta vergegenständ-\n lichter Arbeit. Ob in den Produktionsmitteln enthalten oder durch die\n Arbeitskraft zugesetzt, die Arbeit zählt nur noch nach ihrem Zeitmass.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"5.92"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0093","text":"Sie beträgt so viel Stunden, Tage u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"5.93"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0094","text":"Sie zählt jedoch nur, soweit die zur Produktion des Gebrauchswerths\n verbrauchte Zeit gesellschaftlich nothwendig ist. Es umfasst\n [Running matter: I. 11] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"162\", \"facs\": \"#f0181\"} --> diess Verschiednes. Die Arbeitskraft muss unter normalen Bedingungen\n funktioniren. Ist die Spinnmaschine das gesellschaftlich herrschende\n Arbeitsmittel für die Spinnerei, so darf dem Arbeiter nicht ein Spinnrad\n in die Hand gegeben werden. Statt Baumwolle von normaler Güte muss\n er nicht Schund erhalten, der jeden Augenblick reisst. In beiden Fällen\n würde er mehr als die gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit zur Produk-\n tion eines Pfundes Garn verbrauchen, diese überschüssige Zeit aber nicht\n Werth oder Geld bilden. Der normale Charakter der gegenständlichen\n Arbeitsfaktoren hängt jedoch nicht vom Arbeiter, sondern vom Kapitalisten\n ab.","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"5.94"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0095","text":"Fernere Bedingung ist der normale Charakter der Arbeits-\n kraft selbst. In dem Fach, worin sie verwandt wird, muss sie das herr-\n schende Durchschnittsmass von Geschick, Fertigkeit und Raschheit besitzen.\n Aber unser Kapitalist kaufte auf dem Arbeitsmarkt Arbeitskraft von nor-\n maler Güte. Diese Kraft muss in dem gewöhnlichen Durchschnittsmass\n der Anstrengung, mit dem gesellschaftlich üblichen Grad von Inten-\n sivität verausgabt werden. Darüber wacht der Kapitalist eben so\n ängstlich, als dass keine Zeit ohne Arbeit vergeudet wird. Er hat die\n Arbeitskraft für bestimmte Zeitfrist gekauft. Er hält darauf das Seine zu\n haben. Er will nicht bestohlen sein.","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"5.95"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0096","text":"Endlich — und hierfür hat der-\n selbe Herr einen eignen code pénal — darf kein zweckwidriger Consum\n von Rohmaterial und Arbeitsmitteln stattfinden, weil vergeudetes Material\n oder Arbeitsmittel überflüssig verausgabte Quanta vergegenständlichter\n Arbeit darstellen, also nicht zählen und nicht in das Produkt der Werthbil-\n dung eingehn [Note: Diess ist einer der Umstände, die auf Sklaverei gegründete Produktion\n vertheuern. Der Arbeiter soll sich hier, nach dem treffenden Ausdruck der Alten,\n nur als instrumentum vocale von dem Thier als instrumentum semi-\n vocale und dem todten Arbeitszeug als instrumentum mutum unterscheiden.\n Er selbst aber lässt Thier und Arbeitszeug fühlen, dass er nicht Ihresgleichen,\n sondern ein Mensch ist. Er verschafft sich das Selbstgefühl seines Unterschieds\n von ihnen, indem er sie misshandelt und con amore verwüstet.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"5.96"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0097","text":"Es gilt daher als\n ökonomisches Princip in dieser Produktionsweise nur die rohesten, schwerfällig-\n sten, aber grade wegen ihrer unbehülflichen Plumpheit schwer zu ruinirenden\n Arbeitsinstrumente anzuwenden. Bis zum Ausbruch des Bürgerkriegs fand man\n daher in den am Meerbusen von Mexiko liegenden Sklavenstaaten Pflüge altchine-\n sischer Konstruktion, die den Boden aufwühlen wie ein Schwein oder Maulwurf,\n aber ihn nicht spalten und wenden. Vgl. J. C. Cairns: „The Slave Power.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"5.97"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0098","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"163\", \"facs\": \"#f0182\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"5.98"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0099","text":"Man sieht: der früher aus der Analyse der Waare gewonnene\n Unterschied zwischen der Arbeit, soweit sie Gebrauchswerth, und der-\n selben Arbeit, soweit sie Tauschwerth schafft, hat sich jetzt als Unterschei-\n dung der verschiednen Seiten des Produktionsprozesses dargestellt.","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"5.99"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0100","text":"Als Einheit von Arbeitsprozess und Werthbildungs-\n prozess ist der Produktionsprozess Produktionsprozess von\n Waaren; als Einheit von Arbeitsprozess und Verwer-\n thungsprozess ist er kapitalistischer Produktionspro-\n zess, kapitalistische Form der Waarenproduktion.","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"5.100"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0101","text":"Es wurde früher bemerkt, dass es für den Verwerthungsprozess\n durchaus gleichgültig, ob die vom Kapitalisten angeeignete Arbeit ein-\n fache, gesellschaftliche Durchschnittsarbeit, oder kom-\n plizirtere Arbeit, Arbeit von höherem spezifischem Ge-\n wicht ist. Die Arbeit, die als höhere, komplizirtere Arbeit gegenüber\n der gesellschaftlichen Durchschnittsarbeit gilt, ist die Aeusserung\n einer Arbeitskraft, worin höhere Bildungskosten eingehn, deren\n Produktion mehr Arbeitszeit kostet und die daher einen höheren Tauschwerth\n hat als die einfache Arbeitskraft. Ist der Werth dieser Kraft höher, so\n äussert sie sich aber auch in höherer Arbeit und vergegenständlicht sich\n [Note: London 1862“, p. 46 sqq. In seinem „Sea Board Slave States“ er-\n zählt Olmstedt u.","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"5.101"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0102","text":"A.: „I am here shewn tools that no man in his senses, with\n us, would allow a labourer, for whom he was paying wages, to be encumbered with;\n and the excessive weight and clumsiness of which, I would judge, would make\n work at least ten per cent greater than with those ordinarily used with us. And\n I am assured that, in the careless and clumsy way they must be used by the slaves,\n anything lighter or less rude could not be furnished them with good economy, and\n that such tools as we constantly give our labourers, and find our profit in giving\n them, would not last out a day in a Virginia cornfield — much lighter and more free\n from stones though it be than ours.","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"5.102"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0103","text":"So, too, when I ask why mules are so uni-\n versally substituted for horses on the farm, the first reason given, and confessedly\n the most conclusive one, is that horses cannot bear the treatment that they always\nmust get from the negroes; horses are always soon foundered or crippled by them,\n while mules will bear cudgelling, or lose a meal or two now and then, and not be\n materially injured, and they do not take cold or get sick, if neglected or overwork-\n ed. But I do not need to go further than to the window of the room in which\n I am writing, to see at almost any time, treatment of cattle that would insure\n the immediate discharge of the driver by almost any farmer owning them in the\n North.“] \n [Running matter: 11*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"164\", \"facs\": \"#f0183\"} --> daher, in denselben Zeiträumen, in verhältnissmässig höheren\n Werthen.","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"5.103"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0104","text":"Welches jedoch immer der Gradunterschied zwischen Spinnar-\n beit und Juwelierarbeit, die Portion Arbeit, wodurch der Juwelenarbeiter nur\n den Werth seiner eignen Arbeitskraft ersetzt, unterscheidet sich quali-\n tativ in keiner Weise von der zusätzlichen Portion Arbeit, wodurch er\n Mehrwerth schafft.","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"5.104"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0105","text":"Nach wie vor kommt der Mehrwerth nur heraus durch\n einen quantitativen Ueberschuss von Arbeit, durch die ver-\n längerte Dauer desselben Arbeitsprozesses, in dem einen\n Fall Prozess der Garnproduktion, in dem andern Fall Prozess der Juwelen-\n produktion [Note: Der Unterschied zwischen höherer und einfacher, „skilled“ und „unskilled\n labour“, beruht zum Theil auf blossen Illusionen, oder wenigstens Unter-\n schieden, die längst aufgehört haben reell zu sein und nur noch in traditioneller\n Convention fortleben; zum Theil auf der hilfsloseren Lage gewisser Schichten der\n Arbeiterklasse, die ihnen minder als andren erlaubt, den Werth ihrer Arbeits-\n kraft zu ertrotzen. Zufällige Umstände spielen dabei so grosse Rolle, dass die-\n selben Arbeitsarten den Platz wechseln. Wo z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"5.105"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0106","text":"die physische Substanz der\n Arbeiterklasse abgeschwächt und relativ erschöpft ist, wie in allen Ländern ent-\n wickelter kapitalistischer Produktion, verkehren sich im Allgemeinen brutale Ar-\n beiten, die viel Muskelkraft erfordern, in höhere gegenüber viel feineren Arbeiten,\n die auf die Stufe einfacher Arbeit herabsinken, wie z. B. die Arbeit eines brick-\n layer (Maurer) in England eine viel höhere Stufe einnimmt, als die eines Damast-\n wirkers. Auf der andern Seite figurirt die Arbeit eines fustian cutters (Baumwoll-\n sammtscheerers), obgleich sie viel körperliche Anstrengung kostet und obendrein\n sehr ungesund ist, als „einfache“ Arbeit. Uebrigens muss man sich nicht ein-\n bilden, dass die sogenannte „skilled labour“ einen quantitativ bedeutenden Umfang\n in der Nationalarbeit einnimmt.","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"5.106"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0107","text":"Laing, vor kurzem noch Schatzkanzler von\n Indien, rechnet, dass in England (und Wales) die Existenz von 11 Millionen auf\n einfacher Arbeit beruht. Nach Abzug einer Million von Aristokraten und einer\n andern Million Paupers, Vagabunden, Verbrecher, Prostituirte u. s. w. von den\n 18 Millionen der Bevölkerungszahl, zur Zeit seiner Schrift, bleiben 4 Millionen\n Mittelklasse mit Einschluss kleinerer Rentner, Beamten, Schriftsteller, Künstler,\n Schulmeister u. s. w. Um diese 4 Millionen herauszubekommen, zählt er zum\narbeitenden Theil der Mittelklasse, ausser Banquiers u. s. w., alle besser\n bezahlten „Fabrikarbeiter“! Auch die bricklayers fehlen nicht unter den\n „potenzirten Arbeitern.“ Bleiben ihm dann die besagten 11 Millionen.“ (S.\nLaing: National Distress etc. London 1844.)","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"5.107"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0108","text":"„The great class,\n who have nothing to give for food but ordinary labour, are the great\n bulk of the people.“ (James Mill in Art. „Colony.“ Supple-\n ment of the Encyclop. Brit. 1831.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"5.108"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0109","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"165\", \"facs\": \"#f0184\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"5.109"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0110","text":"Andrerseits muss in jedem Werthbildungsprozess die höhere Arbeit\n stets auf gesellschaftliche Durchschnittsarbeit reducirt werden, z. B. ein\n Tag höherer Arbeit auf x Tage einfacher Arbeit [Note: „Where reference is made to labour as a measure of value, it necessarily\n implies labour of one particular kind. . . . the proportion which\n the other kinds bear to it being easily ascertained.“ („Outlines of Polit.\n Economy. London 1832“, p. 22, 23.)] . Man erspart also\n eine überflüssige Operation und vereinfacht die Analyse durch die Annahme,\n dass der vom Kapital verwandte Arbeiter einfache gesellschaftliche Durch-\n schnittsarbeit verrichtet.","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"5.110"}],"sha256":"46c8ac018e78a097fdbe7cb85480ea5d7543ebbe13bad430e3a8bcfc7db91ad4","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202453136719Z_edbe955a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1) Arbeitsprozess und Verwerthungsprozess.","source_sha256":"46c8ac018e78a097fdbe7cb85480ea5d7543ebbe13bad430e3a8bcfc7db91ad4","sha256":"55903e871cc55a00bd419f4d8195f842c80cb13bd351a844baa4710394f8f502","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:24:53.136743+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0001","text":"## Third Chapter.\n The Production of Absolute Surplus-Value.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"5.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0002","text":"## 1) Labour-Process and Valorisation-Process.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"5.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0003","text":"The use of labour-power is labour itself. The purchaser of labour-\n power consumes it by causing its seller to work. The latter thereby becomes\n labour-power actually setting itself in motion, a labourer, which he was\n previously only potentially. In order to represent his labour in commodities,\n he must above all represent it in use-values, things which serve for the\n satisfaction of needs of some kind. Thus it is a particular use-value, a\n definite article, which the capitalist has the labourer produce. The production\n of use-values, or goods, does not alter its general na-\n ture by taking place for the capitalist and under his control. The labour-\n process must therefore first be considered in its abstract mo-\n ments, independently of every definite social form.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"5.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0004","text":"The labour-process is first of all a process between man\n and nature, a process in which he mediates, regulates, and controls his\n metabolism with nature through his own action. Man confronts natural material\n itself as a natural force. The natural forces belonging to his corporeality,\n arms and legs, head and hand, he sets in motion, in order to assimilate\n natural material to himself in a form useful for his own life. In acting upon\n nature outside him through this movement and changing it, he simultaneously\n changes his own nature. He develops the potentialities slumbering within it\n and subjects the play of its forces to his own dominion. Here we are not dealing\n with the first animal-like instinctive forms of the labour-process.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"142\", \"facs\": \"#f0161\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"5.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0005","text":"The condition in which the labourer appears on the commodity-market\n as seller of his own labour-power has receded into the primeval background\n behind the condition in which the human labour-process had not yet cast off\n its first instinct-like form. We presuppose the labour-process\n in a form in which it belongs exclusively to man. A\n spider performs operations which resemble those of the weaver, and a\n bee puts many a human architect to shame by the construction of its wax cells.\n But what distinguishes the worst architect from the best bee from the outset\n is that he has built the cell in his head be-\n fore he builds it in wax. At the end of the labour-process\n a result emerges which was already present at its beginning in the labourer's\n conception, hence already ideally.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"5.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0006","text":"Not merely\n does he effect a change of form in the natural; he simultaneously realises\n in the natural his purpose, which he knows, which\n determines the mode and manner of his doing as law and to which he must\n subordinate his will. And this subordination is no isolated\n act. Besides the exertion of the organs which labour, the purposive will,\n which manifests itself as attention, is required for the\n whole duration of the labour-process, and all the more so the less\n labour, through its own content and its mode of execution,\n carries the labourer away with it, the less therefore he enjoys it as the play\n of his own physical and mental powers.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"5.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0007","text":"The simple moments of the labour-process are purposive\n activity or labour itself, its ob-\n ject and its means.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"5.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0008","text":"The earth (which economically includes water),\n as it originally equips man with provisions, ready-made means of subsistence\n [Note: “The earth’s spontaneous productions being in small quantity, and quite\n independent of man, appear as it were, to be furnished by nature, in the same way\n as a small sum is given to a young man, in order to put him in a way of industry,\n and of making his fortune.” (James Steuart: Principles of Polit.\n Econ. edit. Dublin 1770, v. I, p. 116.)] , is found without his intervention as the general ob-\n ject of human labour. All things which labour merely detaches from their\n immediate connection with the earth as a whole are\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"143\", \"facs\": \"#f0162\"} --> objects of labour found in nature.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"5.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0009","text":"Such are the fish which is\n caught, separated from its element of life, the water; the wood felled in the\n primeval forest; the ore broken loose from its vein. If, on the other hand,\n the object of labour has itself already, so to speak, been filtered\n through previous labour, we call it raw material. For example, the ore\n already broken loose which is now washed. All raw material is\n an object of labour, but not every object of labour is raw material.\n An object of labour is raw material only when it has already undergone a\n change mediated by labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"5.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0010","text":"The means of labour is a thing or a complex of things\n which the labourer interposes between himself and the object of labour and which\n serve him as conductors of his activity upon this object. He em-\n ploys the mechanical, physical, chemical properties of things,\n in order to make them act as means of power upon other things, in accordance\n with his purpose [Note: “Reason is as cunning as it is powerful. Cunning consists in general in the mediating activity\n which, by allowing objects to act upon one another according to their own\n nature and to wear one another out, without directly interfering in this process,\n nevertheless brings only its purpose to execution.” (Hegel: Encyklopädie. Erster Theil: Die Logik.\n Berlin 1840, p. 382.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"5.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0011","text":"The object of which the labourer im-\n mediately takes possession — apart from the appropriation of ready-made means of\n subsistence, fruits for example, in which his own bodily organs alone serve as\n means of labour — is not the object of labour, but the\n means of labour. Thus the natural itself becomes an organ of his activ-\n ity, an organ which he adds to his own bodily organs, extending his\n natural shape, despite the Bible. As the earth is his original\n storehouse of provisions, so it is his original arsenal of\n means of labour. It supplies him, for example, with the stone with which he\n throws, rubs, presses, cuts, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"5.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0012","text":"The earth itself is a means of labour, but\n its service as a means of labour in agriculture in turn presupposes\n a whole series of other means of labour and a relatively high development\n of labour-power [Note: In the otherwise wretched work: “Théorie de l’Écon. Polit. Paris\n 1815,” Ganilh aptly enumerates, in opposition to the Physiocrats, the great series of\n labour-processes which constitute presuppositions of agriculture proper.] . As soon as the labour-process is at all\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"144\", \"facs\": \"#f0163\"} --> somewhat developed, it already requires worked means of labour.\n In the oldest human caves we find stone tools and stone weapons.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"5.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0013","text":"Alongside worked stone and wood, at the beginning of human his-\n tory the tamed, thus itself already altered by labour, bred animal plays\n the principal role as means of labour [Note: In the above-cited “Réflexions,” Turgot well expounds the impor-\n tance of the tamed animal for the beginnings of culture.] . The use and\n creation of means of labour, although in germ already peculiar to certain animal\n species, characterise the specifically human labour-\n process, and Franklin therefore defines man as “a tool-\n making animal,” an animal manufacturing tools. The same\n significance which the structure of fossil bones has for the knowledge of the\n organisation of extinct animal species, relics of means of la-\n bour have for the assessment of extinct economic forms of\n society.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"5.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0014","text":"Not what is made, but how, with\n what means of labour it is made, distinguishes the economic\n epochs [Note: Of all commodities, proper luxury commodities are the least significant\n for the technological comparison of different epochs of production.] . The means of labour are not only the measure of the de-\n velopment of human labour-power, but also the index of the so-\n cial relations under which labour is performed. Among the\n means of labour themselves, the mechanical means of labour,\n the totality of which may be called the bone and muscular system of\n production, offer far more decisive characteristic marks\n of a social epoch of production than those means of labour which\n serve only as receptacles for the object of labour, and the totality of which\n may quite generally be described as the vascular system of\n production, such as, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"5.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0015","text":"pipes, barrels, baskets, jars, etc. Only in\n chemical manufacture do they play a significant role.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"5.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0016","text":"In the wider sense, the labour-process counts among its means, apart\n from the things which mediate the effect of labour upon its object and therefore\n serve in one way or another as conductors of activity, all the objective\n conditions which are in general required for the whole process to proceed.\n They do not enter directly into the labour-process, but the labour-process\n cannot proceed at all without them, or only imperfectly\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"145\", \"facs\": \"#f0164\"} --> . The general means of labour of this kind is again the earth itself,\n for it gives the labourer the locus\n standi and his process its field of action (field of em-\n ployment). Means of labour of this kind already mediated by labour\n are, for example, work-buildings, canals, roads, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"5.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0017","text":"The labour-process is therefore a process in which the activity\n of man, by means of the means of labour, effects a change in the object of labour\n intended from the outset. This process is extinguished in the\n product. Its product is a use-value, natural material assimilated to human\n needs by change of form. Through the process, labour has connected itself\n with its object. Labour is objectified and the object is worked up. What\n appeared on the side of the labourer in the form of unrest now appears as\n a resting property, in the form of being, on the side of the product. He\n has spun, and the product is a yarn.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"5.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0018","text":"If one considers the whole process from the standpoint of its result,\nthe product, both means of labour and ob-\n ject of labour appear as means of production [Note: It seems paradoxical, for example, to call the fish which has not yet been caught a means of pro-\n duction for fishing. But the art has not yet been invented of catching fish in waters\n where they are not to be found.]  and labour itself as\nproductive labour [Note: This determination of productive labour, as it results from the stand-\n point of the simple labour-process, is by no means sufficient for the capitalist\n process of production.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"5.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0019","text":"When a use-value emerges from the labour-process as product,\nother use-values, themselves products of earlier labour-processes, enter\n the process as means of production. The same use-value which\n is the product of one labour-process forms the means of\n production of another. Products are therefore not merely the result,\n but at the same time the condition of the labour-process.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"5.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0020","text":"With the exception of extractive industry, which finds its object\n of labour in nature, such as mining, hunting, fishing,\n etc. (agriculture only insofar as in the first instance it breaks up virgin\n earth itself), all branches of industry treat an ob-\n ject which is raw material, i.e. an object of labour already filtered\n through labour,\n [Running matter: I. 10]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"146\", \"facs\": \"#f0165\"} --> itself already the product of an earlier labour-process.\n Thus, for example, seed in agriculture. Animals and plants, which one is\n accustomed to regard as products of nature, are not only perhaps products of\n last year's labour, but, in their present forms,\n products of a transformation continued through many generations, under human\n control, by means of human labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"5.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0021","text":"But as regards\n the means of labour in particular, their enormous ma-\n jority show to the most superficial glance the trace of past labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"5.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0022","text":"Raw material may form the principal substance of a product, or\nenter into its formation only as an auxiliary material. The auxiliary material\n is consumed by the means of labour, as coal by a steam-engine,\n oil by a wheel, hay by a draught-horse, or is added to the raw material\n in order to effect a material change in it, as chlorine\n to unbleached linen, coal to iron, dye to wool; or it\n supports the performance of labour itself, as, for example, substances used\n for lighting and heating the workplace.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"5.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0023","text":"The distinction between\n principal material and auxiliary material becomes blurred in chemical manufac-\n ture proper, because none of the raw materials employed reappears as the sub-\n stance of the product [Note: Storch distinguishes raw material proper as “matière” from\n auxiliary materials as “matériaux”: Cherbuliez designates auxiliary materials as\n “matières instrumentales.”] .","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"5.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0024","text":"Since every thing possesses manifold properties and is therefore capable of\n different useful applications, the same product can form the raw material of\n very different labour-processes. Grain, for example, is raw material for the\n miller, starch manufacturer, distiller, cattle-breeder, etc. It becomes raw\n material of its own production as seed. Thus coal emerges as a\n product from mining industry and enters it as a means of production.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"5.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0025","text":"The same product may serve in the same labour-process as means of labour\n and raw material. In the fattening of cattle, for example, where the cattle, the worked-up raw material, are at the same time a means of producing manure.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"5.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0026","text":"A product may exist in a form ready for consumption\n and nevertheless become anew the raw material of another product,\n as grapes become the raw material of wine. Or the labour-\n process discharges its product in forms in which it can be used only as the raw material\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"147\", \"facs\": \"#f0166\"} --> of a subsequent labour-process. Raw material\n in this condition is called a semi-finished product and would better be called a stage-\n product, as, for example, cotton, thread, yarn, etc. Although itself\n already a product, the original raw material may have to pass through a whole series of dif-\n ferent labour-processes, in which, in ever-altered form, it constantly\n functions anew as raw material, down to the final\n labour-process, which casts it off as a finished article of consumption or a finished means\n of labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"5.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0027","text":"One sees: whether a use-value appears as raw material,\n means of labour, or product depends entirely and altogether upon\n its determinate function in the labour-process, upon the\n place which it occupies in it, and with the change of this place\n those determinations change.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"5.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0028","text":"By their entry as means of production into new labour-\n processes, products therefore lose the character of product. They\n now function only as objective factors of living labour.\n The spinner relates to the spindle only as the means with which, and to\n the flax only as the object which, he spins. To be sure, one cannot\n spin without spinning material and spindle. The existence of these pro-\n ducts is therefore presupposed at the beginning of the actual spinning-process.\nBut in this process itself it is just as indifferent that\n flax and spindle are products of past labour as it is in the\n actual process of nourishment indifferent that bread is the product\n of the past labours of farmer, miller, baker, etc. Conversely.\n If means of production assert in the labour-process their character as products\n of past labour, they do so through their defects.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"5.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0029","text":"A knife that does not\n cut, yarn that continually breaks, etc., vividly recall knife-\n smith A and yarn-dresser E. In the successful product, the mediation\n of its use-properties by past labour is effaced.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"5.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0030","text":"A machine which does not serve in the labour-process is useless. More-\n over, it succumbs to the destructive force of natural metabolism.\n Iron rusts, wood rots. Yarn which is not woven or\n knitted is spoiled cotton. Living labour must seize upon\n these things, awaken them from the dead, transform them from merely possible\n into actual and effective use-values. Licked by the fire of\n [Running matter: 10*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"148\", \"facs\": \"#f0167\"} --> labour, appropriated as bodies thereof, animated for their conceptual - and\n vocational functions in the process, they are indeed also\n consumed, but purposively, as elements for the formation of new use-values.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"5.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0031","text":"If existing products are not only the results, but\n also the conditions of existence of the labour-process, then,\n on the other hand, their being thrown into the labour-process, thus their contact\n with living labour, is the sole means of preserving and\n actualizing these products of past labour as use-values. The labour-process in general results only in use-\n value, insofar as its product is capable of entering, as an article of consumption,\n into individual consumption, or, as a means of production, into a new\n labour-process.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"5.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0032","text":"In the labour-process its material elements, its object\n and its means, are consumed as use-values. Labour de-\n vours them. The labour-process is therefore a process of consumption. This\n productive consumption differs from indi-\n vidual consumption in that the latter consumes products as means of subsistence\n of the living individual, the former consumes them as means of subsistence of labour,\n of labour-power in action. The product of indivi-\n dual consumption is therefore the consumer himself, the result\n of productive consumption a product distinct from the\nconsumer.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"5.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0033","text":"Insofar as means of labour and objects of labour are themselves already products,\n results of past labour-processes, the labour-process is a pro-\n cess in which products are consumed in order to create products,\n or in which products serve as means of production of\n products. But as the labour-process originally proceeds only\n between man and the earth existing without his intervention,\n means of production still serve in it which are present by\n nature and do not represent any combination of natural material and human\n labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"5.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0034","text":"The labour-process, as we have presented it in its simple and ab-\n stract moments, is purposive activity for the production of use-values,\n appropriation of the natural for human needs, the general condition of the metabolism between man\n and nature, the eternal natural condition of human life and therefore\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"149\", \"facs\": \"#f0168\"} --> independent of every form of this life, rather equally common to all its social\n forms. In our presentation, therefore, we had no need\n to represent the worker in relation to other workers.\n Man and his labour on the one side, nature and its materials on\n the other, sufficed.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"5.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0035","text":"Just as one cannot taste from wheat who\n has grown it, so one cannot see from this process under what con-\n ditions it proceeds, whether under the brutal whip of the slave overseer\n or under the anxious eye of the capitalist, whether Cincinnatus per-\n forms it in cultivating his few jugera, or the savage who slays a beast with a\n stone [Note: For this highly logical reason Colonel Torrens probably discovers in\n the stone of the savage — the origin of capital. “In the first\nstone which the savage throws at the beast he pursues, in the first stick\nwhich he grasps in order to pull down the fruit which he cannot reach with his hands,\nwe see the appropriation of one article for the purpose of acquiring another\nand thus discover — the origin of capital.” (R. Torrens: An\n Essay on the Production of Wealth etc. p. 79.)","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"5.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0036","text":"From that first stick\n it is probably also to be explained why stock is synonymous with\ncapital in English.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"5.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0037","text":"Let us return to our capitalist in spe. We had left\n him after he had bought on the commodity market all the factors necessary for a labour-process,\n the objective fac-\n tors in the means of production, the subjective fac-\n tor in labour-power. The determinate kind of these means of production\n and of this labour-power is governed by the kind of labour-process\n for which they are intended, whether boots, yarn, etc. are to be made.\n Our capitalist therefore sets about consuming the commodity bought by him,\n labour-power, i.e. making the bearer of labour-power, the\n worker, consume the means of production through his labour. The general nature of the labour-process\n naturally does not change because the worker performs it for the capitalist instead\n of for himself.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"5.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0038","text":"But even the determinate manner and\n way in which boots are made or yarn spun cannot at first change\n through the intervention of the capitalist. He must first take\n labour-power as he finds it on the market, and therefore also its labour as it arose in a period\n when there were as yet no\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"150\", \"facs\": \"#f0169\"} --> capitalists. The transformation of the mode of production\n itself through the subordination of labour to capital can occur only\n later and is therefore to be considered only later.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"5.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0039","text":"The labour-process, as it proceeds as a process of consumption of\n labour-power by the capitalist, displays only two\n peculiar phenomena.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"5.39","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0040","text":"The worker labours under the control of the capi-\n talist, to whom his labour belongs. The capitalist sees to it that the\n labour is properly performed and that the means of production are used purposively,\n so that no raw material is wasted and the instrument of labour is spared,\n i.e. destroyed only insofar as is inseparable\n from its use in labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"5.40","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0041","text":"Secondly, however: the product is the property of the capi-\n talist, not of the immediate producer, the worker. The capi-\n talist pays, for example, the daily value of labour-power. Its use,\n like that of every other commodity, for example a horse which he has hired for a\n day, therefore belongs to him for the day. The buyer of the commodity\n owns the use of the commodity, and the possessor of labour-power in fact gives\n only the use-value sold by him, in that he gives his\n labour. From the moment when he entered the capitalist's workshop,\n the use-value of his labour-power, hence\n its use, labour, belonged to the capitalist. By purchasing labour-power,\n the capitalist has incorporated labour itself as living fermenting\n matter into the dead elements of the product, likewise possessed by him.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"5.41","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0042","text":"From his standpoint the labour-\n process is only the consumption of the commodity labour-\n power which he has bought, but which he can consume only by adding means of production\n to it. The labour-process is a process between things\n which the capitalist has bought, between things\n belonging to him. The product of this process therefore belongs\n to him quite as much as the product of the fermentation process in his\n wine cellar [Note: “The products are appropriated before they are transformed into capital;\nthis transformation does not withdraw them from that appropriation.” (Cherbuliez:\nRiche ou Pauvre, edit. Paris 1841, p. 53, 54.) “In that the proletarian] .","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"5.42","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0043","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"151\", \"facs\": \"#f0170\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"5.43","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0044","text":"The product — the property of the capitalist — is a use-\n value, yarn, boots, etc. But although boots, for example, in a certain\n measure form the basis of social progress, and our capitalist is a decided man\n of progress, he does not manufacture boots for their own sake. Use-value is in general not\n the thing “qu’on aime pour lui même” in commodity pro-\n duction. Use-values are here produced at all only because\n and insofar as they are the material substratum, the bearers of exchange-\n value. And our capitalist is concerned with two things.\nFirst, he wants to produce a use-value which has exchange-\n value, an article intended for sale, a commodity.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"5.44","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0045","text":"And\n secondly he wants to produce a commodity whose value is higher than the\nsum of the values of the commodities required for its pro-\n duction, the means of production and labour-power, for which he advanced his good money\n on the commodity market. He wants not merely to pro-\n duce a use-value, but a commodity, not merely use-\n value, but exchange-value, and not merely value, but also\nsurplus-value.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"5.45","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0046","text":"In fact, since commodity production is at issue here,\n we have hitherto evidently considered only one side of the process.\n Just as the commodity itself is a unity of use-value and exchange-\n value, its process of production must be a unity of labour-\n process and process of value-formation.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"5.46","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0047","text":"[Note: sells his labour in exchange for a determinate quantity of means of subsistence (approvisionnement),\n he completely renounces every share in the product. The appro-\n priation of the products remains the same as before; it is in no way altered by the\n convention mentioned. The product belongs exclusively to the capi-\n talist who has supplied the raw materials and the approvisionnement. This\n is a strict consequence of the law of appropriation, whose fundamental\n principle was, conversely, the exclusive right of property of every worker in\n his product.” (l. c. p. 58.) James Mill: Elements of Pol.\n Econ. etc. p. 75: “When workers labour for wages, the capi-\n talist is proprietor not only of the capital (here meaning the means of production),\n but also of the labour (of labour also).","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"5.47","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0048","text":"If what is paid as wages\n is included, as is customary, in the concept of capital, it is\nabsurd to speak of labour as separate from capital.\n The word capital in this sense includes both, capital and labour.”]","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"5.48","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0049","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"152\", \"facs\": \"#f0171\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"5.49","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0050","text":"Let us now consider the production process also as a\nvalue-formation process.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"5.50","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0051","text":"We know that the value of every commodity is determined by the\n quantum of labour materialised in its use-value,\n by the labour-time socially necessary for its production. This applies also to the product which presented itself to our capi-\n talist as the result of the labour process. Thus, first of all, the\n labour objectified in this product is to be calcu-\n lated.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"5.51","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0052","text":"Let it be, e.g., yarn.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"5.52","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0053","text":"For the manufacture of the yarn, its raw material was first necessary,\n e.g. 10 pounds of cotton. What the value of the cotton is need not\n first be investigated, for the capitalist bought it on the market at its\n value, e.g. for 10 sh. In the price of the cotton, the labour\n required for its production is already represented as general social\n labour. Let us further assume that the spindle material consumed in pro-\n cessing the cotton, which represents to us all the other expended\n means of labour, possesses a value of 2 sh. If a quantity of gold of 12 sh. is the product of 24 labour-hours\n or two working days, it follows first of all that two working days\n are objectified in the yarn.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"5.53","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0054","text":"The circumstance that the cotton has altered its form and that the\n consumed spindle material has disappeared altogether must not mislead. According\n to the general law of value, e.g. 10 lbs. of yarn are an equivalent for 10\n lbs. of cotton and ¼ spindle, if the value of 40 lbs. of yarn = the\n value of 40 lbs. of cotton + the value of a whole spindle, i.e.\n if the same labour-time is required to produce both sides of this\n equation. In this case, the same labour-\n time presents itself on one occasion in the use-value yarn, on the other in the use-\n values cotton and spindle. Exchange-value is therefore\n indifferent whether it appears in yarn, spindle, or cotton.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"5.54","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0055","text":"That spindle and cotton, instead of lying quietly alongside one another, enter\n into a connection in the spinning process which alters the form of their use-\n value, transforms them into yarn, affects their exchange-\n value just as little as if they had been converted by simple exchange\n into an equivalent of yarn.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"5.55","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0056","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"153\", \"facs\": \"#f0172\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"5.56","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0057","text":"The labour-time required for the production of the cotton is a part\n of the labour-time required for the production of the yarn, whose raw material\n it forms, and is therefore contained in the yarn. The same holds\n for the labour-time required for the production of the spindle material, without\n whose wear or consumption the cotton cannot be spun [Note: “Not only the labour applied immediately to commodities affect their\n value, but the labour also which is bestowed on the implements, tools, and build-\n ings with which such labour is assisted.” Ricardo l. c. p. 16.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"5.57","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0058","text":"In so far, therefore, as the value of the yarn, the labour-time re-\n quired for its manufacture, is concerned, the differ-\n ent particular labour processes, separated in time and space, which\n must be gone through in order to produce the cotton, to\n produce the consumed spindle material, finally to make yarn out of cot-\n ton and spindle, may be regarded as different successive phases\nof one and the same labour process.\n All labour contained in the yarn is past labour. That the labour-time\n required for the production of its constituent elements had passed earlier,\n stands in the pluperfect, whereas the labour directly applied to the final\n process, spinning, stands nearer to the present, in the per-\n fect, is an entirely indifferent circumstance. If a definite\n mass of labour, e.g.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"5.58","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0059","text":"of 30 working days, is necessary for the construction of a house,\n it alters nothing in the total quantum of labour-time incorporated\n in the house that the 30th working day entered production 29 days\n later than the first working day. And thus the labour-time contained\n in the labour material and means of labour may be regarded quite as\n if it had been expended only at an earlier stage of the spinning process\n than the quantum of labour expended last in the form of spinning.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"5.59","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0060","text":"The values of the means of production, of the cotton and the spindle,\n expressed in the price of 12 sh., therefore form constituent parts of the yarn-\n value, or of the value of the product.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"5.60","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0061","text":"Only two conditions must be fulfilled. First, cotton\n and spindle must actually have served for the production of a use-value.\n In our case, yarn must have been made from them.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"154\", \"facs\": \"#f0173\"} -->What use-value bears it is indifferent to exchange-value,\n but it must be borne by a use-value. Secondly, it is presup-\n posed that only the labour-time necessary under the given social con-\n ditions of production has been employed.\n If, therefore, only 1 pound of cotton were necessary to spin 1 pound of yarn,\n only 1 pound of cotton may have been consumed in the formation of\n 1 pound of yarn. The same applies to the spindle. If the capi-\n talist had the fancy to have spinning done with golden instead of iron\n spindles, then nevertheless only the socially\n necessary labour-time would count in the yarn-value, i.e.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"5.61","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0062","text":"the labour-time necessary for the production of the iron\n spindle material.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"5.62","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0063","text":"We now know the portion of value which the means of production,\n cotton and spindle, form in the yarn-value. It is equal to 12 sh.,\n or the materialisation of two working days. It is now therefore a question of\n the portion of value which the labour of the spinner himself adds to the cot-\n ton.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"5.63","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0064","text":"We must now consider this labour from a quite different point of view\n than during the labour process. There it was a question\n of purposive activity, of transforming cotton into yarn.\n The more purposive the labour, the better the yarn, all other circumstances\n being assumed equal. The labour of the spinner was specifically\n different from other productive labours, and this difference\n manifested itself subjectively and objectively, in the particular purpose of spinning,\n its particular mode of operation, the particular nature of its means of production,\n the specific use-value of its product. Cotton and\n spindle serve as means of subsistence for spinning labour, but\n no rifled cannon can be made with them. In so far as the labour of\n the spinner is value-forming, i.e.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"5.64","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0065","text":"a source of exchange-\n value, it is in no way different from the labour of the cannon-\n borer, or, what lies nearer to us here, from the labours realised in the means of pro-\n duction of the yarn, the labours of the cotton planter\n and of the spindle maker. Only because of this identity can cotton-\n planting, spindle-making, and spinning form merely quantitatively different\n parts of the same total value, the yarn-value. Here\n it is no longer a question of the quality, the character and\n content of labour, but only of its quantity. This\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"155\", \"facs\": \"#f0174\"} --> is simply to be counted. We assume that spinning labour is simple\n labour, average social labour. It will later be seen\n that the contrary assumption would alter nothing in the matter.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"5.65","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0066","text":"During the labour process, labour constantly converts itself from the\n form of unrest into that of being, from the form of motion into that of\n objectivity. At the end of an hour, the spinning motion\n is represented in a certain quantum of yarn, thus a determinate quan-\n tum of labour, one labour-hour, is objectified in the cot-\n ton. We say labour-hour, for spinning labour counts here\n only in so far as it is expenditure of labour-power, not in so far as it is\n the specific labour of spinning.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"5.66","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0067","text":"It is now decisively important that during the duration of the pro-\n cess, i.e. the transformation of cotton into yarn, only socially\n necessary labour-time is consumed.\n If, under normal, i.e. average social conditions of pro-\n duction, a pounds of cotton must be transformed during one labour-hour into\n b pounds of yarn, then only that working day counts as a working day\n of 12 hours which transforms 12 × a pounds of cotton into 12 × b pounds\n of yarn. For only socially necessary labour-time counts\n as value-forming.","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"5.67","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0068","text":"Raw material and product appear here in a quite differ-\n ent light than from the standpoint of the labour process proper. The\n raw material counts here only as absorber of a determinate\n quantum of labour. Through this absorption it is in fact transformed\n into yarn, because spinning labour has been added to it. But the\n product, the yarn, is now only the measure of the labour ab-\n sorbed by the cotton. If in one hour 1⅔ lbs. of cotton are spun\n or transformed into 1⅔ lbs. of yarn, then 10 lbs. of yarn indicate 6 absorbed\n labour-hours. Definite and experientially estab-\n lished quanta of product now represent nothing but determinate quanta\n of labour, determinate masses of congealed labour-time. They are only\nmaterialisation of one hour, two hours, one day of social\n labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"5.68","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0069","text":"That the labour is precisely spinning labour, its material cotton and its pro-\n duct yarn, is here just as indifferent as that the object of labour\n itself is already a product, thus raw material. If the work-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"156\", \"facs\": \"#f0175\"} --> er were employed in the coal mine instead of in the spinning mill, the\n object of labour, coal, would be present by nature. Nevertheless, a\n determinate quantum of coal broken loose from the seam, e.g. a hundred-\n weight, would represent a determinate quantum of absorbed labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"5.69","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0070","text":"In the sale of labour-power it was assumed that its daily\n value = 3 sh., and that 6 labour-hours are embodied in the latter,\n that this quantum of labour is therefore required in order to produce the\n average sum of the worker's daily means of subsistence. If our\n spinner now transforms 1⅔ lbs. of cotton into 1⅔ lbs. of yarn during one labour-hour [Note: The figures here are quite arbitrary.] , then in 6 hours\n 10 lbs. of cotton into 10 lbs. of yarn. During the duration of the spinning process,\n the cotton thus absorbs 6 labour-hours.\n The same labour-time presents itself in a quantity of gold of 3 sh.\n Thus a value of 3 sh. has been added to the cotton by spinning\n itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"5.70","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0071","text":"Let us now look at the total value of the product, the\n 10 lbs. of yarn. In them 2½ working days are objectified,\n 2 days contained in cotton and spindle material, ½ day of labour ab-\n sorbed during the spinning process. The same labour-time presents itself in\n a mass of gold of 15 sh. The price adequate to the value of the 10 lbs. of yarn\n is therefore 15 sh., the price of a lb. of yarn 1 sh. 6 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"5.71","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0072","text":"Our capitalist is taken aback. The value of the product is equal\n to the value of the capital advanced. The ad-\n vanced value has not valorised itself, has generated no surplus-value,\n money has therefore not transformed itself into capital. The price of the\n 10 lbs. of yarn is 15 sh. and 15 sh. were expended on the commodity market\n for the constituent elements of the product or, what is the same,\nthe factors of the labour process, 10 sh. for cotton, 2 sh.\n for the consumed spindle material, and 3 sh. for labour-power. The\n swollen value of the yarn is of no help, for its value is\n only the sum of the values previously distributed among cotton, spindle, and labour-\n power, and from such a mere addition of pre-\n existing values surplus-value can never arise [Note: This is the fundamental proposition on which the doctrine of the Physiocrats rests] .","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"5.72","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0073","text":"These values are now all concentrated in one thing, but\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"157\", \"facs\": \"#f0176\"} --> they were dispersed in the sum of money of 15 sh. before the latter split up through three\n commodity purchases.","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"5.73","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0074","text":"In and for itself this result is not surprising. The value\n of a lb. of yarn is 1 sh. 6 d., and for 10 lbs. of yarn our capitalist\n would therefore have to pay 15 sh. in the commodity market. Whether he buys\n his private house ready-made on the market, or has it built himself, neither of these oper-\n ations will increase the money laid out in the acquisition of the house.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"5.74","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0075","text":"The capitalist, who is versed in vulgar economics, says per-\n haps that he advanced his money with the intention of making more money\n out of it. The road to hell, however, is paved with good in-\n tentions, and he might just as well intend to make money without produc-\n ing [Note: Thus, for example, in 1844—47 he withdrew part of his capital from productive busi-\n ness in order to speculate in railway shares. Thus, during the American Civil\n War, he shut the factory and threw the factory worker onto the pavement,\n in order to gamble on the Liverpool cotton exchange.] . He threatens that no one shall catch him\n again. In future he will buy the commodity ready-made on the market,\n instead of manufacturing it himself. But if all his brother capitalists\n do likewise, where is he to find commodities on the market? And money\n he cannot eat. He catechizes.","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"5.75","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0076","text":"One ought to bear his abstinence in mind. He could have squandered\n his 15 sh. Instead, he consumed them productively and made yarn out of them.\n But in return he is in possession of yarn instead of pangs of conscience.\n He must by no means fall back into the role of the hoard-maker,\n who showed us what comes of asceticism. Besides, where there is nothing,\n the Emperor has lost his right. Whatever the merit of his renun-\n ciation, there is nothing there to pay him extra for it, since the value of\n the product that emerges from the process is only equal to the sum of the\n [Note: the unproductiveness of all non-agricultural labour rests, and it is irrefutable\n for the economist — by profession.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"5.76","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0077","text":"“This way of imputing to a single thing the\n value of several others (for example, to flax the consumption of the weaver),\n of applying, so to speak, layer upon layer, several values to one\n single thing, makes the latter grow accordingly . . . . The term addition depicts very\n well the manner in which the price of works of labour is formed; this\n price is only a total of several values consumed and added together;\n now, to add is not to multiply.” (Mercier de la Rivière, l. c.\n p. 599.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"158\", \"facs\": \"#f0177\"} --> commodity values thrown into it. Let him therefore console himself with the fact that vir-\n tue is virtue’s reward. Instead, he becomes importunate. The yarn\n is useless to him. He produced it for sale.","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"5.77","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0078","text":"Then let him sell\n it, or, more simply still, in future produce only things for his\n own needs, a recipe which his physician Mac Culloch has already prescribed him\n as a proven remedy against the epidemic of overproduction.\n He defiantly rears up on his hind legs. Could the worker, with his own\n limbs, create formations of labour in the blue air, produce commodi-\n ties? Did he not give him the material with and in which alone he could\n embody his labour? Since now the greater part of so-\n ciety consists of such have-nots, has he not rendered society,\n by means of his means of production, his cotton and his spindle, an\n immeasurable service, not to mention the worker himself, whom he moreover\n supplied with food? And should he not charge for the ser-\n vice?","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"5.78","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0079","text":"But has not the worker rendered him the counter-service\n of transforming cotton and spindle into yarn? Besides, the matter here is\n not one of services [Note: “Let them boast, adorn and deck themselves out . . . . But whoever takes more or\n better (than he gives), that is usury, and is called, not service, but rather\n injury done to his neighbour, as happens through stealing and robbing. Not\n everything is service and well-doing to one’s neighbour that people call service and\n well-doing. For an adulteress and an adulterer render each other great service\n and pleasure. A horseman renders a great rider’s service to an arsonist-murderer,\n in helping him to rob on the roads and to wage feud against land and people.","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"5.79","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0080","text":"The Papists\n render ours a great service by not drowning, burning, murdering,\n letting them rot in prison, but leaving at least some alive, and ban-\n ishing them, or taking from them what they have. The Devil himself renders his ser-\n vants a great, immeasurable service . . . . In sum, the world is full of great,\n excellent, daily services and good deeds.” (Martin Luther: To the\n Parish Priests, to preach against usury etc. Wittenberg\n 1540.)] . A service is nothing but the useful\n effect of a use-value, whether of the commodity or of la-\n bour [Note: On this I remark, among other things, in “A Contribution to the Critique of Pol. Econ.” p. 14:\n “One understands what ‘service’ the category ‘service’ renders to a\n kind of economists such as J. B. Say and F. Bastiat.”] . But here exchange-value is at issue. He paid the worker\n the value of 3 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"5.80","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0081","text":"The worker gave him an exact equivalent\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"159\", \"facs\": \"#f0178\"} --> back in the value of 3 sh. added to the cotton, value for\n value. Our friend, just now so capitalistically overbearing, suddenly\n assumes the modest bearing of his own worker. Has he not himself\n worked? has he not performed the labour of supervision, of overseeing\n the spinner? Does not this labour of his also create value? His\n own overlooker and his manager shrug their shoulders. Meanwhile,\n however, with a cheerful smile he has already resumed his old physi-\n ognomy. He was fooling us with the whole litany. He does not care\n a farthing for it. He leaves these and similar stale evasions and hollow\n phrases to the professors of political economy specially paid for that purpose.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"5.81","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0082","text":"He himself is a practical man, who does not always reflect on what he\n says outside business, but always knows what he is doing in business.","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"5.82","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0083","text":"Let us look more closely. The daily value of labour-power amounted\n to 3 sh., because a half working day is itself objectified in it,\n i.e. because the means of subsistence necessary daily for the production of labour-\n power cost half a working day. But the past labour contained in labour-\n power, and the living labour it can perform, its daily maintenance costs\n and its daily expenditure, are two quite different magnitudes. The former\n determines its exchange-value, the latter constitutes its use-value.\n That half a working day is necessary to keep him alive for 24 hours\n does not in the least prevent the worker from working a whole day.\n The value of labour-power and its valorization in the labour process\n are therefore two different magnitudes. The capital-\n ist had this difference in value in mind when he bought labour-power.","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"5.83","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0084","text":"Its useful property,\n that of making yarn or boots, was only a conditio sine qua, because labour\n must be expended in useful form in order to create value. But what was\n decisive was the specific use-value of this commodity,\n that it should be a source of exchange-value, and of more exchange-value than it itself\n has. This is the specific service the capitalist expects\n from it. And in so doing he proceeds in accordance with the eternal laws of commodity ex-\n change. In fact, the seller of labour-power, like the\n seller of every other commodity, realizes its exchange-value and\n alienates its use-value. He cannot retain the one without\n giving up the other. The use-value of labour-\n power, labour itself, belongs just as little to its seller as the\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"160\", \"facs\": \"#f0179\"} --> use-value of sold oil belongs to the oil-dealer.","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"5.84","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0085","text":"The money-owner\n has paid the daily value of labour-power; its use during the day,\n the day-long labour, therefore belongs to him. The circumstance\n that the daily maintenance of labour-power costs only half a working day,\n although labour-power can act, can work, for a whole day, and that hence\n the value which its use creates during one day is twice as great as its\n own daily value, is a particular good fortune for the buyer, but not at all\n an injustice to the seller.","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"5.85","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0086","text":"Our capitalist foresaw the case. The worker therefore finds in the\n workshop the necessary means of production not merely for a six-hour,\n but for a twelve-hour labour process. If 10 lbs. of cotton absorbed 6 labour hours\n and were transformed into 10 lbs. of yarn, then 20 lbs. of cotton will absorb 12\n labour hours and be transformed into 20 lbs. of yarn. Let us consider the product of the ex-\n tended labour process. In the 20 lbs. of yarn there are now 5 working\n days objectified, 4 in the consumed mass of cotton and spindle,\n 1 absorbed by the cotton during the spinning process.\n But the gold expression of 5 working days is 30 sh. or £1 10 sh.\n This, then, is the price of the 20 lbs. of yarn. The pound of yarn costs, as before,\n 1 sh. 6 d. But the sum of values of the commodities thrown into the\n process amounted to 27 sh. The value of the yarn amounts to 30 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"5.86","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0087","text":"The value\n of the product has increased by ⅓ over the value advanced for its pro-\n duction. Thus 27 sh. have been transformed into 30 sh. They have set\n a surplus-value of 3 sh. The trick has at last suc-\n ceeded. Money has been transformed into capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"5.87","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0088","text":"All the conditions of the problem are solved and the laws of\n commodity exchange have in no way been violated. Equivalent was exchanged for\n equivalent. As buyer, the capitalist paid for every commodity at its value,\n cotton, spindle mass, labour-power. He then did\n what every other buyer of commodities does. He consumed their use-\n value. The process of consumption of labour-power,\n which is at the same time the process of production of the commodity,\n yielded a product of 20 lbs. of yarn with a value of 30 sh. The capitalist now returns\n to the market and sells commodity, after having bought commodity.\n He sells the pound of yarn at 1 sh. 6 d., not a farthing above or below\n its value. And yet he draws 3 sh. more out of circulation\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"161\", \"facs\": \"#f0180\"} --> than he originally threw into it.","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"5.88","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0089","text":"This entire course, the trans-\n formation of his money into capital, takes place in the sphere of circulation and\n does not take place in it. Through the mediation of circulation, because\n conditioned by the purchase of labour-power on the commodity market.\nNot in circulation, because this merely initiates the valorization\n process, which takes place in the sphere of production. And\n thus “all is for the best in the best of all possible worlds.”","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"5.89","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0090","text":"By transforming money into commodities, which serve as the material elements\n of a new product or as factors of the labour process, by incorpo-\n rating living labour-power into their dead objectivity, the capitalist transforms\n value, past, objectified, dead labour into\ncapital, self-valorizing value, an animated mon-\n ster which begins to “work” as though it had love in its belly.","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"5.90","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0091","text":"If we now compare the value-formation process and the valor-\n ization process, the valorization process is nothing but a value-\n formation process prolonged beyond a certain point.\n If the latter lasts only up to the point at which the value of labour-power\n paid by capital is replaced by a new equivalent,\n then it is a simple value-formation process. If the value-formation\n process lasts beyond this point, it becomes a valorization process.","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"5.91","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0092","text":"If, further, we compare the value-formation process with the\nlabour process, the latter consists in the actual labour\n that produces use-values. The movement is here considered quali-\n tatively, in its particular mode, according to purpose and con-\n tent. The same labour process presents itself in the value-formation\n process only from its quantitative side. It is now concerned\n only with the time which labour requires for its operation,\n or with the duration for which labour-power is expended. Here,\n too, the commodities entering into the labour process no longer count as\n functionally determined, material factors of purposively active labour-\n power. They count only as definite quantities of objectified\n labour. Whether contained in the means of production or added by\n labour-power, labour counts only according to its measure of time.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"5.92","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0093","text":"It amounts to so many hours, days, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"5.93","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0094","text":"It counts, however, only insofar as the time consumed in producing the use-value\n is socially necessary.\n [Running matter: I. 11]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"162\", \"facs\": \"#f0181\"} --> This comprises various things. Labour-power must\n function under normal conditions. If the spinning machine is the socially prevailing\n instrument of labour for spinning, the worker must not be given a spinning wheel\n in his hand. Instead of cotton of normal quality, he must not receive\n rubbish that breaks at every moment. In both cases,\n he would consume more than the socially necessary labour-time for the produc-\n tion of a pound of yarn, but this surplus time would not create\n value or money. The normal character of the objective factors of labour,\n however, depends not on the worker, but on the capitalist.","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"5.94","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0095","text":"A further condition is the normal character of labour-\npower itself. In the sphere in which it is employed, it must possess the pre-\nvailing average measure of skill, dexterity, and rapidity.\nBut our capitalist bought labour-power of normal quality on the labour market. This power must be expended in the ordinary average measure\nof exertion, with the socially customary degree of inten-\nsity. The capitalist watches over this just as anxiously\nas over ensuring that no time is wasted without labour. He has bought\nlabour-power for a definite period of time. He insists on having what is\nhis. He does not wish to be cheated.","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"5.95","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0096","text":"Finally—and for this the same gentleman has a penal code\nof his own—no inappropriate consumption of raw material\nand means of labour must take place, because wasted material\nor means of labour represent superfluously expended quanta of objectified\nlabour, and therefore do not count and do not enter into the product of value-for-\nmation [Note: This is one of the circumstances that makes production based on slavery\nmore expensive. Here, in the apt expression of the ancients, the worker is supposed\nto differ from the animal as instrumentum semi-\nvocale and from the dead instrument of labour as instrumentum mutum\nonly as instrumentum vocale.\nBut he himself makes animal and instrument of labour feel that he is not their equal,\nbut a human being. He procures for himself the self-feeling of his difference\nfrom them by maltreating them and devastating them con amore.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"5.96","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0097","text":"It therefore counts as\nan economic principle in this mode of production to employ only the crudest, most unwieldy-\ninstruments of labour, but precisely because of their clumsy awkwardness difficult\nto ruin. Until the outbreak of the Civil War, therefore, in the slave states lying on\nthe Gulf of Mexico one found ploughs of ancient Chinese construction,\nwhich root up the soil like a pig or a mole, but do not split and turn it over. Cf. J. C. Cairns: “The Slave Power.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"5.97","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0098","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"163\", \"facs\": \"#f0182\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"5.98","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0099","text":"It is evident: the distinction previously obtained from the analysis of the commodity\nbetween labour insofar as it creates use-value, and the\nsame labour insofar as it creates exchange-value, has now presented itself as the distinc-\ntion between the different aspects of the process of production.","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"5.99","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0100","text":"As the unity of the labour process and the process of value-for-\nmation, the process of production is a process of production of\ncommodities; as the unity of the labour process and the valorisation\nprocess, it is a capitalist process of produc-\ntion, the capitalist form of commodity production.","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"5.100","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0101","text":"It was remarked earlier that for the valorisation process it is\nentirely immaterial whether the labour appropriated by the capitalist is sim-\nple, social average labour, or more com-\nplicated labour, labour of higher specific gra-\nvity. The labour which counts as higher, more complicated labour in relation\nto social average labour is the manifestation\nof a labour-power into which higher costs of education enter, whose\nproduction costs more labour-time, and which therefore has a higher exchange-value\nthan simple labour-power. But if the value of this power is higher,\nit also manifests itself in higher labour and objectifies itself\n[Note: London 1862”, p. 46 sqq. In his “Sea Board Slave States” Olmstedt re-\ncounts inter alia:","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"5.101","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0102","text":"A.: “I am here shewn tools that no man in his senses, with\nus, would allow a labourer, for whom he was paying wages, to be encumbered with;\nand the excessive weight and clumsiness of which, I would judge, would make\nwork at least ten per cent greater than with those ordinarily used with us. And\nI am assured that, in the careless and clumsy way they must be used by the slaves,\nanything lighter or less rude could not be furnished them with good economy, and\nthat such tools as we constantly give our labourers, and find our profit in giving\nthem, would not last out a day in a Virginia cornfield — much lighter and more free\nfrom stones though it be than ours.”","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"5.102","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0103","text":"“So, too, when I ask why mules are so uni-\nversally substituted for horses on the farm, the first reason given, and confessedly\nthe most conclusive one, is that horses cannot bear the treatment that they always\nmust get from the negroes; horses are always soon foundered or crippled by them,\nwhile mules will bear cudgelling, or lose a meal or two now and then, and not be\nmaterially injured, and they do not take cold or get sick, if neglected or overwork-\ned. But I do not need to go further than to the window of the room in which\nI am writing, to see at almost any time, treatment of cattle that would insure\nthe immediate discharge of the driver by almost any farmer owning them in the\nNorth.“]\n[Running matter: 11*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"164\", \"facs\": \"#f0183\"} --> therefore, in the same periods of time, in proportionately higher\nvalues.","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"5.103","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0104","text":"Whatever, however, the difference in degree between spinning-\nlabour and the labour of the jeweller, the portion of labour by which the jeweller merely\nreplaces the value of his own labour-power differs quali-\ntatively in no way from the additional portion of labour by which he\ncreates surplus-value.","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"5.104","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0105","text":"As before, surplus-value results only through\na quantitative excess of labour, through the pro-\nlonged duration of the same labour process, in the one\ncase the process of yarn production, in the the other case the process of jewellery-\nproduction [Note: The difference between higher and simple, “skilled” and “unskilled\nlabour”, rests in part upon mere illusions, or at least upon distinc-\ntions which have long ceased to be real and survive only in traditional\nconvention; in part upon the more helpless position of certain strata of the\nworking class, which permits them less than others to extort the value of their labour-\npower. Accidental circumstances play so great a role in this that the\nsame kinds of labour exchange places. Where, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"5.105","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0106","text":"the physical substance of\nthe working class is weakened and relatively exhausted, as in all countries of devel-\noped capitalist production, brutal kinds of labour requiring much muscular power\nare generally transformed into higher labour in relation to much finer kinds of labour,\nwhich sink to the level of simple labour; thus, for example, the labour of a brick-\nlayer (bricklayer) in England occupies a much higher level than that of a damask-\nweaver. On the other hand, the labour of a fustian cutter (cotton-\nvelvet shearer), although it costs much bodily exertion and is moreover\nvery unhealthy, figures as “simple” labour. Incidentally, one must not imagine\nthat so-called “skilled labour” occupies a quantitatively significant extent\nin national labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"5.106","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0107","text":"Laing, until recently Chancellor of\nthe Exchequer of India, calculates that in England (and Wales) the existence of 11 million rests\nupon simple labour. After deducting one million aristocrats and another\nmillion paupers, vagabonds, criminals, prostitutes, etc. from the\n18 million of the population at the time of his writing, there remain 4 million\nmiddle class, including lesser annuitants, officials, writers, artists,\nschoolmasters, etc. In order to obtain these 4 million, he counts among the\nworking part of the middle class, apart from bankers etc., all better-\npaid “factory workers”! The bricklayers too are not absent among the\n“potentiated workers.” There then remain for him the said 11 million.” (S.\nLaing: National Distress etc. London 1844.)","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"5.107","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0108","text":"“The great class,\nwho have nothing to give for food but ordinary labour, are the great\nbulk of the people.” (James Mill in Art. “Colony.” Supple-\nment of the Encyclop. Brit. 1831.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"5.108","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0109","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"165\", \"facs\": \"#f0184\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"5.109","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0110","text":"On the other hand, in every process of value-formation higher labour\nmust always be reduced to social average labour, e.g. one\nday of higher labour to x days of simple labour [Note: “Where reference is made to labour as a measure of value, it necessarily\nimplies labour of one particular kind. . . . the proportion which\nthe other kinds bear to it being easily ascertained.” (“Outlines of Polit.\nEconomy. London 1832”, p. 22, 23.)] . One therefore saves\na superfluous operation and simplifies the analysis by assuming\nthat the worker employed by capital performs simple social average\nlabour.","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"5.110","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1AUV:P0110"]}],"source_digest":"d6e512820654f400c1466f56fb472e1e670c6e3089ec56d9d2b0dbd86f29ad77","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU","title":"2) Constantes Kapital und variables Kapital.","order":6,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_2CKU","text":"Die verschiedenen Faktoren des Arbeitsprozesses nehmen verschied-\n nen Antheil an der Bildung des Produkten-Werths.\n\n\n\n\nDer Arbeiter setzt dem Arbeitsgegenstand neuen Tauschwerth\n zu durch Zusatz eines bestimmten Quantums von Arbeit, abge-\n sehn vom bestimmten Inhalt, Zweck und technologischen Charakter seiner\n Arbeit. Andrerseits finden wir die Werthe der verzehrten Produktions-\n mittel wieder als Bestandtheile des Produkten-Werths, z. B.\n die Werthe von Baumwolle und Spindel im Garnwerth. Der Werth der\n Produktionsmittel wird also erhalten durch seine Uebertragung\n auf das Produkt. Diess Uebertragen geschieht während der Verwand-\n lung der Produktionsmittel in Produkt, im Arbeitsprozess. Es ist ver-\n mittelt durch die Arbeit. Aber wie?\n\n\n\n\nDer Arbeiter arbeitet nicht doppelt in derselben Zeit, nicht ein-\n mal um der Baumwolle durch seine Arbeit einen Werth zuzusetzen, und das\n andremal um ihren alten Werth zu erhalten, oder, was dasselbe, um den\n Werth der Baumwolle, die er verarbeitet, und der Spindel, womit er\n arbeitet, auf das Produkt, das Garn, zu übertragen. Sondern durch\n blosses Zusetzen von neuem Werth erhält er den alten Werth. Da\n aber der Zusatz von neuem Werth zum Arbeitsgegenstand und die Erhal-\n tung der alten Werthe im Produkt zwei ganz verschiedne Resultate sind,\n die der Arbeiter in derselben Zeit hervorbringt, obgleich er nur\n einmal in derselben Zeit arbeitet, kann diese Doppelseitigkeit\n des Resultats offenbar nur aus der Doppelseitigkeit seiner\n Arbeit selbst erklärt werden. In demselben Zeitpunkt muss sie in\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"166\", \"facs\": \"#f0185\"} --> einer Eigenschaft Werth schaffen und in einer andern Eigenschaft Werth\n erhalten oder übertragen.\n\n\n\n\nWie setzt jeder Arbeiter Arbeitszeit und daher Werth zu? Immer nur\n in der Form seiner eigenthümlich produktiven Arbeitsweise. Der Spinner\n setzt nur Arbeitszeit zu, indem er spinnt, der Weber, indem er webt, der\n Schmidt, indem er schmiedet. Durch die zweckbestimmte Form\n aber, worin sie Arbeit überhaupt zusetzen und daher Neuwerth,\n durch das Spinnen, Weben, Schmieden werden die Produktionsmittel,\n Baumwolle und Spindel, Garn und Webstuhl, Eisen und Amboss, zu Bil-\n dungselementen eines Produkts, eines neuen Gebrauchswerths [Note: „Labour gives a new creation for one extinguished.“ (An Essay on\n the Polit. Econ. of Nations. London 1821, p. 13.)] .\n Die alte Form ihres Gebrauchswerths vergeht, aber nur um in einer\n neuen Form von Gebrauchswerth aufzugehn. Bei Betrachtung des\n Werthbildungsprozesses ergab sich aber, dass so weit ein Gebrauchswerth\n zweckgemäss vernutzt wird zur Produktion eines neuen Gebrauchswerths,\n die zur Herstellung des vernutzten Gebrauchswerths nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit einen Theil der zur Herstellung des neuen Gebrauchswerths nothwen-\n digen Arbeitszeit bildet, also Arbeitszeit ist, die vom vernutzten Produk-\n tionsmittel auf das neue Produkt übertragen wird. Der Arbeiter erhält\n also die Werthe der vernutzten Produktionsmittel oder überträgt sie als\n Werthbestandtheile auf das Produkt, nicht durch sein Zusetzen von\n Arbeit überhaupt, sondern durch den besondren nützlichen\n Charakter, durch die spezifisch produktive Form dieser zu-\n sätzlichen Arbeit. Als solche zweckgemässe produktive Thätigkeit, Spin-\n nen, Weben, Schmieden, erweckt die Arbeit durch ihren blossen Kon-\n takt die Produktionsmittel von den Todten, begeistet sie zu Faktoren des\n Arbeitsprozesses und verbindet sich mit ihnen zu Produkten.\n\n\n\n\nWäre die spezifische produktive Arbeit des Arbeiters nicht Spin-\n nen, so würde er die Baumwolle nicht in Garn verwandeln, also auch die\n Werthe von Baumwolle und Spindel nicht auf das Garn übertragen. Wech-\n selt dagegen derselbe Arbeiter das Metier und wird Tischler, so wird er\n nach wie vor durch einen Arbeitstag seinem Material Werth zusetzen.\n Er setzt ihn also zu nicht durch seine Arbeit, soweit sie Spinnarbeit\n oder Tischlerarbeit, sondern so weit sie abstrakte, gesell-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"167\", \"facs\": \"#f0186\"} --> schaftliche Arbeit überhaupt, und er setzt eine bestimmte Werth-\n grösse zu, nicht weil seine Arbeit einen besondern nützlichen Inhalt hat,\n sondern weil sie eine bestimmte Zeit dauert. In ihrer abstrakten\n allgemeinen Eigenschaft also, als Verausgabung menschlicher Arbeits-\n kraft, setzt die Arbeit des Spinners den Werthen von Baumwolle und Spin-\n del Neuwerth zu, und in ihrer konkreten, besondern, nütz-\n lichen Eigenschaft als Spinnprozess, überträgt sie den Werth dieser\n Produktionsmittel auf das Produkt und erhält so ihren Werth im Pro-\n dukt. Daher die Doppelseitigkeit ihres Resultats in demselben\n Zeitpunkt.\n\n\n\n\nDurch das bloss quantitative Zusetzen von Arbeit wird neuer\n Werth zugesetzt, durch die Qualität der zugesetzten Ar-\n beit werden die alten Werthe der Produktionsmittel im Produkt erhalten.\n Diese doppelseitige Wirkung derselben Arbeit in Folge ihres doppelsei-\n tigen Charakters zeigt sich handgreiflich an verschiednen Erscheinungen.\n\n\n\n\nNimm an, irgend eine Erfindung befähige den Spinner in 6 Stunden\n so viel Baumwolle zu verspinnen wie früher in 36 Stunden. Als zweck-\n mässig-nützliche, produktive Thätigkeit hat seine Arbeit ihre Kraft\n versechsfacht. Ihr Produkt ist ein sechsfaches, 36 statt 6 lbs. Garn.\n Aber die 36 Pfund Baumwolle saugen jetzt nur so viel Arbeitszeit ein als\n früher 6 Pfund. Ein Sechstel weniger neuer Arbeit wird ihnen zugesetzt\n als mit der alten Methode, daher nur noch ein Sechstel des früheren Werths.\n Andrerseits existirt jetzt der sechsfache Werth von Baumwolle im\n Produkt, den 36 Pfund Garn. In den 6 Spinnstunden wird ein sechsmal\n grösserer Werth von Rohmaterial erhalten und auf das Produkt über-\n tragen, obgleich demselben Rohmaterial ein sechsmal kleinerer Neu-\n werth zugesetzt wird. Diess zeigt, wie die Eigenschaft, worin die\n Arbeit während desselben untheilbaren Prozesses Werthe erhält, wesent-\n lich unterschieden ist von der Eigenschaft, worin sie Werth schafft. Je\n mehr nothwendige Arbeitszeit während der Spinnoperation auf dasselbe\n Quantum Baumwolle geht, desto grösser der Neuwerth, der\n der Baumwolle zugesetzt wird, aber je mehr Pfunde Baumwolle in der-\n selben Arbeitszeit versponnen werden, desto grösser der alte\n Werth, der im Produkt erhalten wird.\n\n\n\n\nNimm umgekehrt an, die Produktivität der Spinnarbeit bleibe un-\n verändert, der Spinner brauche also nach wie vor gleich viel Zeit, um ein\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"168\", \"facs\": \"#f0187\"} --> Pfund Baumwolle in Garn zu verwandeln. Aber der Tauschwerth\n der Baumwolle selbst wechsle, ein Pfund Baumwolle steige oder falle um\n das Sechsfache seines Preises. In beiden Fällen fährt der Spinner fort\ndemselben Quantum Baumwolle dieselbe Arbeitszeit zuzu-\n setzen, also denselben Werth und in beiden Fällen produzirt er in\n gleicher Zeit gleich viel Garn. Dennoch ist der Werth, den er von der\n Baumwolle auf das Garn, das Produkt, überträgt, das einemal sechsmal\n kleiner, das andremal sechsmal grösser als zuvor. Ebenso wenn die Ar-\n beitsmittel sich vertheuern oder verwohlfeilen, aber stets denselben Dienst\n im Arbeitsprozess leisten.\n\n\n\n\nBleiben die technologischen Bedingungen des Spinnprozesses unver-\n ändert und geht gleichfalls kein Werthwechsel mit seinen Produk-\n tionsmitteln vor, so verbraucht der Spinner nach wie vor in gleichen Ar-\n beitszeiten gleiche Quanta Rohmaterial und Maschinerie von gleichbleiben-\n den Werthen. Der Werth, den er im Produkt erhält, steht dann in\n direktem Verhältniss zu dem Neuwerth, den er zusetzt. In zwei\n Wochen setzt er zweimal mehr Arbeit zu als in einer Woche, also zwei-\n mal mehr Werth, und zugleich vernutzt er zweimal mehr Material von\n zweimal mehr Werth, und verschleisst zweimal mehr Maschinerie von zwei-\n mal mehr Werth, erhält also im Produkt von zwei Wochen zweimal mehr\n Werth als im Produkt einer Woche. Unter gegebnen gleichbleibenden\n Produktionsbedingungen erhält der Arbeiter um so mehr Werth, je mehr\n Werth er zusetzt, aber er erhält nicht mehr Werth, weil er mehr Werth\n zusetzt, sondern weil er ihn unter gleichbleibenden und von seiner\n eignen Arbeit unabhängigen Bedingungen zusetzt.\n\n\n\n\nAllerdings kann in einem relativen, wenn auch nicht absoluten\n Sinn gesagt werden, dass der Arheiter stets in derselben Propor-\n tion alte Werthe erhält, worin er Neuwerth zusetzt. Ob die Baum-\n wolle von 1 sh. auf 2 sh. steige oder auf 6 d. falle, er erhält in dem\n Produkt einer Stunde stets nur halb so viel Baumwollwerth, wie der auch\n wechsle, als in dem Produkt von zwei Stunden. Wechselt ferner die\nProduktivität seiner eignen Arbeit, sie steige oder falle, so wird er\n z. B. in einer Arbeitsstunde mehr oder weniger Baumwolle verspinnen als\n früher, und dem entsprechend mehr oder weniger Baumwollwerth im\n Produkt einer Arbeitsstunde erhalten. Mit alle dem wird er in\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"169\", \"facs\": \"#f0188\"} --> zwei Arbeitsstunden zweimal mehr Werth erhalten als in einer Arbeits-\n stunde.\n\n\n\n\nWerth, von seiner nur symbolischen Darstellung im Werth-\n zeichen abgesehn, existirt nur in einem Gebrauchswerth, einem\n Ding. (Der Mensch selbst, als blosses Dasein von Arbeitskraft betrachtet,\n ist ein Naturgegenstand, ein Ding, wenn auch lebendiges, selbstbewusstes\n Ding, und die Arbeit selbst ist dingliche Aeusserung dieser Kraft.)\n Geht daher der Gebrauchswerth verloren, so geht auch der Tauschwerth\n verloren. Die Produktionsmittel verlieren mit ihrem Gebrauchswerth nicht\n zugleich ihren Tauschwerth, weil sie durch den Arbeitsprozess die ursprüng-\n liche Gestalt ihres Gebrauchswerths in der That nur verlieren, um im Pro-\n dukt die Gestalt eines andern Gebrauchswerths zu gewinnen. So wichtig\n es aber für den Tauschwerth ist in irgend einem Gebrauchswerth zu existi-\n ren, so gleichgültig ist es für ihn, in welchem er existirt, wie die Meta-\n morphose der Waare zeigt. Es folgt hieraus, dass im Arbeitsprozess\n Werth vom Produktionsmittel auf das Produkt nur übergeht, so weit das\n Produktionsmittel mit seinem selbstständigen Gebrauchswerth auch seinen\n Tauschwerth verliert. Es giebt nur den Werth an das Produkt ab,\n den es als Produktionsmittel verliert. Die gegenständlichen Fak-\n toren des Arbeitsprozesses verhalten sich aber in dieser Hinsicht\n verschieden.\n\n\n\n\nDie Kohle, womit die Maschine geheizt wird, verschwindet spurlos,\n ebenso der Talg, womit man die Axe des Rades schmiert u. s. w. Farben\n und andre Hilfsstoffe verschwinden, zeigen sich aber in den Eigenschaften\n des Produkts. Das Rohmaterial bildet die Substanz des Produkts, hat\n aber seine Form verändert. Rohmaterial und Hilfsstoffe verlieren also die\n selbstständige Gestalt, womit sie in den Arbeitsprozess als Gebrauchs-\n werthe eintraten. Anders mit den eigentlichen Arbeitsmitteln.\n Ein Instrument, eine Maschine, ein Fabrikgebäude, ein Gefäss u. s. w.\n dienen im Arbeitsprozess nur, so lange sie ihre ursprüngliche Gestalt bewah-\n ren und morgen wieder in eben derselben Form in den Arbeitsprozess ein-\n gehn, wie gestern. Wie sie während ihres Lebens, des Arbeitsprozesses,\n ihre selbstständige Gestalt dem Produkt gegenüber bewahren, so auch nach\n ihrem Tode. Die Leichen von Maschinen, Werkzeugen, Arbeitsgebäuden\n u. s. w. existiren immer noch selbstständig getrennt von den Produkten,\n die sie bilden halfen. Betrachten wir nun die ganze Periode, während\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"170\", \"facs\": \"#f0189\"} --> deren ein solches Arbeitsmittel dient, von dem Tag seines Eintritts in die\n Werkstätte bis zum Tage seiner Verbannung in die Rumpelkammer, so ist\n während dieser Periode sein Gebrauchswerth von der Arbeit vollständig\n verzehrt worden, und sein Tauschwerth daher vollständig auf das Produkt\n übergegangen. Hat eine Spinnmaschine z. B. in 10 Jahren ausgelebt, so\n ist während des zehnjährigen Arbeitsprozesses ihr Gesammtwerth auf das\n zehnjährige Produkt übergegangen. Die Lebensperiode eines Arbeitsmit-\n tels umfängt also eine grössere oder kleinere Anzahl stets von neuem\n mit ihm wiederholter Arbeitsprozesse. Und es geht dem Arbeitsmittel\n wie dem Menschen. Jeder Mensch stirbt täglich um 24 Stunden ab. Man\n sieht aber keinem Menschen genau an, wie viel Tage er bereits verstorben\n ist. Diess verhindert Lebensversicherungsgesellschaften jedoch nicht, aus\n dem Durchschnittsleben der Menschen sehr sichre, und was noch viel mehr\n ist, sehr profitliche Schlüsse zu ziehn. So mit dem Arbeitsmittel. Man\n weiss aus der Erfahrung, wie lang ein Arbeitsmittel, z. B. eine Maschine\n von gewisser Art, durchschnittlich vorhält. Gesetzt sein Gebrauchswerth\n im Arbeitsprozess daure nur 6 Tage. So verliert es im Durchschnitt\n jeden Arbeitstag ⅙ seines Gebrauchswerths und giebt daher ⅙ seines\n Tauschwerths an das tägliche Produkt ab. In dieser Art wird der Ver-\n schleiss aller Arbeitsmittel berechnet, also z. B. ihr täglicher\n Verlust an Gebrauchswerth, und die ihm entsprechende tägliche Abgabe\n von Tauschwerth an das Produkt.\n\n\n\n\nEs zeigt sich hier schlagend, dass ein Produktionsmittel nie mehr\n Werth an das Produkt abgiebt, als es selbst im Arbeitsprozess durch Ver-\n nichtung seines eignen Gebrauchswerths verliert. Hätte es keinen Tausch-\n werth zu verlieren, d. h. wäre es nicht selbst Produkt menschlicher Arbeit,\n so würde es keinen Tauschwerth an das Produkt abgeben. Es diente als\n Bildner von Gebrauchswerth, ohne als Bildner von Tauschwerth zu dienen.\n Diess ist daher der Fall mit allen Produktionsmitteln, die von Natur, ohne\n menschliches Zuthun, vorhanden sind, mit Erde, Wind, Wasser, dem Eisen\n in der Erzader, dem Holze des Urwaldes u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nEin andres interessantes Phänomen tritt uns hier entgegen. Eine\n Maschine sei z. B. 1000 Pfd. St. werth und schleisse sich in 1000 Tagen\n ab. In diesem Falle geht täglich \\frac{1}{1000} des Werths der Maschine von ihr\n selbst auf ihr tägliches Produkt über, aber, obgleich mit abnehmender\n Lebenskraft, wirkt die Maschine stets ganz im Arbeitsprozess. Es zeigt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"171\", \"facs\": \"#f0190\"} --> sich hier also, dass ein Faktor des Arbeitsprozesses, ein Pro-\n duktionsmittel, ganz in den Arbeitsprozess, aber nur stück-\n weis in den Verwerthungsprozess eingeht. Der Unterschied\n von Arbeitsprozess und Verwerthungsprozess reflektirt sich hier an den\ngegenständlichen Faktoren, indem dasselbe Produktionsmittel\n als Element des Arbeitsprozesses ganz und als Element der\nWerthbildung nur stückweis in demselben Produktionsprozess\n zählt [Note: Es handelt sich hier nicht um Reparaturarbeiten der Arbeits-\n mittel, Maschinen, Baulichkeiten u. s. w. Eine Maschine, die reparirt wird,\n funktionirt nicht als Arbeitsmittel, sondern als Arbeitsmaterial. Es\n wird nicht mit ihr gearbeitet, sondern sie selbst wird bearbeitet, um ihren Ge-\n brauchswerth zu flicken. Solche Reparaturarbeiten kann man für unsern Zweck\n immer eingeschlossen denken in die zur Produktion des Arbeitsmittels erheischte\n Arbeit. Im Text handelt es sich um den Verschleiss, den kein Doktor\n kuriren kann und der allmählig den Tod herbeiführt, um „that kind of wear which\n cannot be repaired from time to time, and which, in the case of a knife, would\n ultimately reduce it to a state in which the cutler would say of it, it is not worth\n a new blade.“ Man hat im Text gesehn, dass eine Maschine z. B. ganz in jeden\n einzelnen Arbeitsprozess, aber nur stückweise in den gleichzeitigen Ver-\n werthungsprozess eingeht. Danach zu beurtheilen die folgende Begriffsverwechs-\n lung: „Mr. Ricardo speaks of the portion of the labour of the engineer in making\n stocking machines“ als z. B. enthalten in dem Werth von ein paar Strümpfen.\n „Yet the total labour that produced each single pair of stockings …\n includes the whole labour of the engineer, not a portion; for one\n machine makes many pairs, and none of those pairs could have been done\n without any part of the machine.“ (Observations on certain verbal\n disputes in Pol. Econ., particularly relating to Value, and\n to demand and supply. London 1821, p. 54.) Der Verfasser, ein un-\n gemein selbstgefälliger „wiseacre“, hat mit seiner Konfusion und daher seiner Po-\n lemik nur so weit Recht, als weder Ricardo noch irgend ein andrer Oekonom, vor\n oder nach ihm, die beiden Seiten der Arbeit genau geschieden,\n daher noch weniger ihre verschiedne Wirkung auf die Werthbildung analysirt hat.] .\n\n\n\n\nAndrerseits kann umgekehrt ein Produktionsmittel ganz in den Ver-\n werthungsprozess eingehn, obgleich nur stückweis in den Arbeitsprozess.\n Nimm an, beim Verspinnen der Baumwolle fielen täglich auf 115 Pfund\n 15 Pfunde ab, die kein Garn, sondern nur devil’s dust bilden. Dennoch,\n wenn dieser Abfall von 15 % normal, von der Durchschnitts-Verarbeitung\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"172\", \"facs\": \"#f0191\"} --> der Baumwolle unzertrennlich ist, geht der Werth der 15 lbs. Baumwolle,\n die kein Element des Garns, ganz eben so sehr in seinen Werth ein, wie\n der Werth der 100 lbs., die seine Substanz bilden. Der Gebrauchswerth\n von 15 lbs. Baumwolle muss verstauben, um 100 lbs. Garn zu machen.\n Der Untergang dieser Baumwolle ist also eine Produktionsbedingung\n des Garns. Eben desswegen giebt sie ihren Tauschwerth an das Garn\n ab. Diess gilt von allen Exkrementen des Arbeitsprozesses,\n in dem Grad wenigstens, worin diese Exkremente nicht wieder neue Pro-\n duktionsmittel und daher neue selbstständige Gebrauchswerthe bilden. So\n sieht man in den grossen Maschinenfabriken zu Manchester Berge von Eisen-\n abfällen, durch cyklopische Maschinen gleich Hobelspähnen abgeschält,\n am Abend auf grossen Wagen aus der Fabrik in die Eisengiesserei wandern,\n um den andern Tag wieder als massives Eisen aus der Eisengiesserei in\n die Fabrik zurückzuwandern.\n\n\n\n\nNur soweit Produktionsmittel während des Arbeitsprozesses Tausch-\n werth in der Gestalt ihrer alten Gebrauchswerthe verlieren, übertra-\n gen sie Tauschwerth auf die neue Gestalt des Produkts. Das Maxi-\n mum des Werthverlustes, den sie im Arbeitsprozess erleiden kön-\n nen, ist aber beschränkt durch ihre ursprüngliche Werth-\n grösse, womit sie in den Arbeitsprozess eintreten, oder durch die zu\n ihrer eignen Produktion erheischte Arbeitszeit. Produktionsmittel\n können dem Produkt daher nie mehr Werth zusetzen, als\n sie unabhängig vom Arbeitsprozess, dem sie dienen,\n besitzen. Wie nützlich ein Arbeitsmaterial, eine Maschine, ein Pro-\n duktionsmittel, wenn es 150 Pfd. St., sage 500 Arbeitstage, kostet, setzt\n es dem Gesammtprodukt, zu dessen Bildung es dient, nie mehr als\n 150 Pfd. St. zu. Sein Werth ist bestimmt nicht durch den Arbeitspro-\n zess, worin es als Produktionsmittel eingeht, sondern durch den Arbeits-\n prozess, woraus es als Produkt herauskommt. In dem Arbeitsprozess\n dient es nur als Gebrauchswerth, als Ding mit nützlichen\n Eigenschaften, und gäbe daher keinen Tauschwerth an das Produkt\n ab, hätte es nicht Tauschwerth besessen vor seinem Eintritt in den\n Prozess [Note: Man begreift daher die Abgeschmacktheit des faden J. B. Say, der\n den Mehrwerth (Zins, Profit, Rente) aus den „services productives“] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"173\", \"facs\": \"#f0192\"} -->\n\nIndem die produktive Arbeit Produktionsmittel in Bildungselemente\n eines neuen Produkts verwandelt, geht mit deren Tauschwerth eine Seelen-\n wanderung vor. Er geht aus dem verzehrten Leib in den neu gestalteten\n Leib über. Aber diese Seelenwanderung ereignet sich gleichsam hinter\n dem Rücken der wirklichen Arbeit. Der Arbeiter kann neue Arbeit\n nicht zusetzen, also nicht neuen Werth schaffen, ohne alte Werthe\n zu erhalten, denn er muss die Arbeit immer in bestimmter nützlicher\n Form zusetzen, und er kann sie nicht in nützlicher Form zusetzen, ohne\n Produkte zu Produktionsmitteln eines neuen Produkts zu machen, und da-\n durch ihren Werth auf das neue Produkt zu übertragen. Es ist also eine\nNaturgabe der sich bethätigenden Arbeitskraft, der lebendigen Arbeit,\nWerth zu erhalten, indem sie Werth zusetzt, eine Natur-\n gabe, die dem Arbeiter nichts kostet, aber dem Kapitalisten viel ein-\n bringt, die Erhaltung des vorhandnen Kapitalwerths [Note: „Of all the instruments of the farmer’s trade, the labour of man …\n is that on which he is most to rely for the re-payment of his capital.] .\n [Note: ableiten will, welche die Produktionsmittel, Erde, Instrumente, Leder u. s. w.\n durch ihre Gebrauchswerthe im Arbeitsprozesse leisten. Herr Wilhelm\n Roscher, der es nicht leicht lässt, artige apologetische Einfälle schwarz auf\n weiss zu registriren, ruft aus: „Sehr richtig bemerkt J. B. Say, Traité,\n t. I. ch. 4: „der durch eine Oelmühle nach Abzug aller Kosten hervorge-\n brachte Werth sei doch etwas Neues, von der Arbeit, wodurch die Oelmühle\n selbst geschaffen worden, wesentlich verschiedenes.“ (l. c. p. 82 Note.) Sehr\n richtig! Das von der Oelmühle hervorgebrachte „Oel“ ist etwas sehr Ver-\n schiednes von der Arbeit, welche der Bau der Mühle kostet. Und unter\n „Werth“ versteht Herr Roscher solches Zeug wie „Oel“, da „Oel“\n Werth hat, „in der Natur“ aber sich Steinöl vorfindet, wenn auch relativ nicht\n „sehr viel“, worauf wohl seine andre Bemerkung abzielt: „Tauschwerthe\n bringt sie (die Natur!) fast gar nicht hervor.“ Es geht der Roscher’schen Natur\n mit dem Tauschwerth, wie der thörichten Jungfrau mit dem Kind, das nur „ganz\n klein war.“ Derselbe „Gelehrte“ („savant sérieux“) bemerkt noch bei oben er-\n wähnter Gelegenheit: „Die Schule Ricardo’s pflegt auch das Kapital unter den\n Begriff Arbeit zu subsumiren als „aufgesparte Arbeit.“ Diess ist ungeschickt\n (!), weil (!) ja (!) der Kapital besitzer (!) doch (!) mehr (!) gethan hat als die\nblosse (?!) Hervorbringung (??) und (??) Erhaltung desselben (wessel-\n bigen?): eben (?!?) die Enthaltung vom eignen Genusse, wofür er z. B. (!!!)\n Zinsen verlangt.“ (l. c.) Wie „geschickt“! diese „anatomisch-phy-\n siologische Methode“ der politischen Oekonomie, die aus blossem „Ver-\n langen“ ja doch eben „Werth“ entwickelt.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"174\", \"facs\": \"#f0193\"} --> So lange das Geschäft flott geht, ist der Kapitalist zu sehr in die Plus-\n macherei vertieft, um diese Gratisgabe der Arbeit zu sehn. Gewaltsame\n Unterbrechungen des Arbeitsprozesses, Krisen, machen sie ihm empfind-\n lich bemerksam [Note: In der Times vom 26. Nov. 1862 jammert ein Fabrikant, dessen\n Spinnerei 800 Arbeiter beschäftigt, und wöchentlich im Durchschnitt 150 Ballen\n ostindischer oder ungefähr 130 Ballen amerikanischer Baumwolle verzehrt, dem\n Publikum die jährlichen Stillstandskosten seiner Fabrik vor. Er schlägt\n sie auf 6000 Pfd. St. an. Unter diesen Unkosten befinden sich viele Posten, die\n uns hier nichts angehn, wie Grundrente, Steuern, Versicherungsprämien, Salaire\n für jährlich engagirte Arbeiter, manager, Buchhalter, Ingenieur u. s. w. Dann\n aber berechnet er für 150 Pfd. St. Kohlen, um die Fabrik von Zeit zu Zeit zu wärmen\n und die Dampfmaschine gelegentlich in Gang zu setzen, ausserdem Salaire für\n Arbeiter, die durch gelegentliche Arbeit die Maschinerie „flüssig“ erhalten. End-\n lich 1200 Pfd. St. für Verschlechterung der Maschinerie, da „the weather and the\n natural principle of decay do not suspend their operations because the steam-\n engine ceases to revolve.“ Er bemerkt ausdrücklich, diese Summe von 1200 Pfd. St.\n sei so gering angeschlagen, weil sich die Maschinerie bereits in sehr abgenutztem\n Zustand befinde.] .\n\n\n\n\nWas überhaupt im Arbeitsprozess an den Produktionsmitteln verzehrt\n wird, ist ihr Gebrauchswerth, dessen Konsumtion durch die Arbeit Pro-\n dukte bildet. Ihr Tauschwerth wird in der That nicht konsumirt [Note: „Productive Consumption: where the consumption of a commo-\n dity is a part of the process of production … In these instances\nthere is no consumption of value.“ S. P. Newman l. c. p. 296.] ,\n kann also auch nicht reproduzirt werden. Er wird erhalten,\n aber nicht weil eine Operation mit ihm selbst im Arbeitsprozess vorgeht,\n sondern weil der Gebrauchswerth, worin er ursprünglich existirt, zwar\n verschwindet, aber nur in einem anderen Gebrauchswerth verschwindet.\n Der Tauschwerth der Produktionsmittel erscheint daher wieder\n im Werth des Produkts, aber er wird, genau gesprochen, nicht repro-\n duzirt. Was produzirt wird, ist der neue Gebrauchswerth, worin der\n alte Tauschwerth wieder erscheint [Note: In einem nordamerikanischen Kompendium, das vielleicht 20 Auflagen er-] .\n\n\n\n\n [Note: The other two — the working stock of the cattle, and the … carts, ploughs,\n spades, and so forth, without a given portion of the first, are nothing at all.“\n (Edmund Burke: Thoughts and Details on Scarcity, origi-\n nally presented to the R. Hon. W. Pitt in the Month of No-\n vember 1795, edit. London 1800“, p. 10.] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"175\", \"facs\": \"#f0194\"} -->\n\nAnders mit dem subjektiven Faktor des Arbeitsprozesses, der\n sich bethätigenden Arbeitskraft. Während die Arbeit durch ihre zweck-\n mässige Form den Werth der Produktionsmittel auf das Produkt über-\n trägt und erhält, bildet jedes Moment ihrer Bewegung zusätzlichen\n Werth, Neuwerth. Gesetzt der Produktionsprozess breche ab\n beim Punkt, wo der Arbeiter ein Aequivalent für den Werth\n seiner eignen Arbeitskraft produzirt, durch sechsstündige Ar-\n beit z. B. einen Werth von 3 sh. zugesetzt hat. Dieser Werth bildet den\nUeberschuss des Produktenwerthes über seine dem Werth der\n Produktionsmittel geschuldeten Bestandtheile. Es ist der einzige\n Originalwerth, der innerhalb dieses Prozesses entstand,\n der einzige Werththeil des Produkts, der durch den Prozess\n selbst produzirt ist. Allerdings ersetzt er nur das vom Kapitalisten\n beim Kauf der Arbeitskraft vorgeschossene, vom Arbeiter selbst in Lebens-\n mitteln verausgabte Geld. Mit Bezug auf die verausgabten 3 sh. erscheint\n der Neuwerth von 3 sh. nur als Reproduktion. Aber er ist wirk-\n lich reproduzirt, nicht nur scheinbar, wie der Werth der Pro-\n duktionsmittel. Der Ersatz eines Werths durch den andern\n ist hier vermittelt durch neue Werthschöpfung.\n\n\n\n\nWir wissen jedoch bereits, dass der Arbeitsprozess über den Punkt\n hinaus fortdauert, wo ein blosses Aequivalent für den Werth der\n [Note: lebt hat, liest man: „It matters not in what form capital reappears.“ Nach einer\n redseligen Aufzählung aller möglichen Produktionsingredienzen, deren Werth im\n Produkt wieder erscheint, heisst’s schliesslich: „The various kinds of food,\n clothing, and shelter, necessary for the existence and comfort of the human being,\n are also changed. They are consumed from time to time, and their value re-\n appears, in that new vigour imparted to his body and mind, forming fresh\n capital, to be employed again in the work of production.“ (F. Weyland l. c.\n p. 31, 32.) Von allen andern Wunderlichkeiten abgesehn, ist es z. B. nicht der\n Preis des Brodes, der in der erneuten Kraft wieder erscheint, sondern seine\n blutbildenden Substanzen. Was dagegen als Werth der Kraft wiedererscheint,\n sind nicht die Lebensmittel, sondern ihr Werth. Dieselben Lebensmittel,\n wenn sie nur die Hälfte kosten, produziren ganz eben so viel Muskel. Knochen\n u. s. w., kurz dieselbe Kraft, aber nicht Kraft vom selben Werth. Diess Umsetzen\n von „Werth“ in „Kraft“ und die ganze pharisäische Unbestimmtheit verstecken\n den allerdings vergeblichen Versuch aus blossem Wiedererscheinen\n vorgeschossener Werthe einen Mehrwerth herauszudrechseln.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"176\", \"facs\": \"#f0195\"} --> Arbeitskraft reproduzirt und dem Arbeitsgegenstand zugesetzt wäre. Statt\n der 6 Stunden, die hierzu genügten, währt der Prozess z. B. 12 Stunden.\n Durch die Bethätigung der Arbeitskraft wird also nicht nur ihr eigner\n Werth reproduzirt, sondern ein überschüssiger Werth produzirt. Dieser\nMehrwerth bildet den Ueberschuss des Produktenwerthes\n über den Werth der verzehrten Produktbildner, d. h. der Pro-\n duktionsmittel und der Arbeitskraft.\n\n\n\n\nIndem wir die verschiedenen Rollen dargestellt, welche die verschie-\n denen Faktoren des Arbeitsprozesses in der Bildung des Produkten-\n werths spielen, haben wir in der That die Funktionen der ver-\n schiedenen Bestandtheile des Kapitals in seinem eignen\n Verwerthungsprozess charakterisirt. Der Ueberschuss des Ge-\n sammtwerths des Produkts über die Werthsumme seiner Bildungselemente\n ist der Ueberschuss des verwertheten Kapitals über den ur-\n sprünglich vorgeschossenen Kapitalwerth. Produktions-\n mittel auf der einen Seite, Arbeitskraft auf der andern, sind nur die ver-\n schiednen Existenzformen, die der ursprüngliche Kapitalwerth bei Abstrei-\n fung seiner Geldform und seiner Verwandlung in die Faktoren des Arbeits-\n prozesses annahm.\n\n\n\n\nDer Theil des Kapitals also, der sich in Produktionsmittel,\n d. h. in Rohmaterial, Hilfsstoffe und Arbeitsmittel umsetzt, verän-\n dert seine Werthgrösse nicht im Produktionsprozesse. Ich\n nenne ihn daher konstanten Kapitaltheil, oder kürzer: kon-\n stantes Kapital.\n\n\n\n\nDer in Arbeitskraft umgesetzte Theil des Kapitals verändert\n dagegen seinen Werth im Produktionsprozesse. Er reproduzirt sein\n eignes Aequivalent und einen Ueberschuss darüber, Mehrwerth, der\n selbst wechseln, grösser oder kleiner sein kann. Aus einer konstanten\n Grösse verwandelt sich dieser Theil des Kapitals fortwährend in eine\n variable. Ich nenne ihn daher variablen Kapitaltheil, oder kür-\n zer: variables Kapital. Dieselben Kapitalbestandtheile,\n die sich vom Standpunkt des Arbeitsprozesses als objektive\n und subjektive Faktoren, als Produktionsmittel und Arbeitskraft unter-\n scheiden, unterscheiden sich vom Standpunkt des Verwerthungs-\n prozesses als konstantes Kapital und variables Kapital.\n\n\n\n\nDer Begriff des konstanten Kapitals schliesst eine Werthrevo-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"177\", \"facs\": \"#f0196\"} --> lution seiner Bestandtheile in keiner Weise aus. Nimm an, das Pfund\n Baumwolle koste heute 6 d. und steige morgen, in Folge eines Ausfalls\n der Baumwollerndte, auf 1 sh. Die alte Baumwolle, die fortfährt verar-\n beitet zu werden, ist zum Werth von 6 d. gekauft, aber sie fügt dem Pro-\n dukt jetzt einen Werththeil von 1 sh. zu. Und die bereits versponnene,\n vielleicht schon als Garn auf dem Markt cirkulirende Baumwolle, fügt dem\n Produkt ebenfalls das Doppelte ihres ursprünglichen Werthes zu. Man\n sieht jedoch, dass diese Werthwechsel unabhängig sind von der Ver-\n werthung der Baumwolle im Spinnprozess selbst. Wäre die\n alte Baumwolle noch gar nicht in den Arbeitsprozess eingegangen, so\n könnte sie jetzt zu 1 sh. statt zu 6 d. wieder verkauft werden. Um-\n gekehrt: Je weniger Arbeitsprozesse sie noch durchlaufen hat,\n desto sichrer ist diess Resultat. Es ist daher Gesetz der Spekulation bei\n solchen Werthrevolutionen auf das Rohmaterial in seiner mindest verarbei-\n teten Form zu spekuliren, also eher auf Garn als auf Gewebe und eher auf\n die Baumwolle selbst als auf das Garn. Die Werthänderung ent-\n springt hier in dem Prozesse, der Baumwolle produzirt, nicht in dem Pro-\n zesse, worin sie als Produktionsmittel und daher als konstantes Kapi-\n tal funktionirt. Der Werth einer Waare ist zwar bestimmt durch das\n Quantum der in ihr enthaltenen Arbeit, aber diess Quantum ist gesell-\n schaftlich bestimmt. Hat sich die gesellschaftlich zu ihrer Produktion\n erheischte Arbeitszeit verändert — und dasselbe Quantum Baum-\n wolle z. B. stellt in ungünstigen Erndten grösseres Quantum\n Arbeit dar, als in günstigen — so findet eine Rückwirkung auf die\n alte Waare statt, die immer nur als einzelnes Exemplar ihrer Gattung\n gilt [Note: „Toutes les productions d’un même genre ne forment proprement qu’une\n masse, dont le prix se détermine en général et sans égard aux circonstances\n particulières.“ (Le Trosne l. c. p. 893.)] , deren Werth stets durch gesellschaftlich nothwendige,\n also auch stets unter gegenwärtigen gesellschaftlichen Be-\n dingungen nothwendige Arbeit gemessen wird.\n\n\n\n\nWie der Werth des Rohmaterials, mag der Werth bereits im Pro-\n duktionsprozess dienender Arbeitsmittel, der Maschinerie u. s. w.,\n wechseln, also auch der Werththeil, den sie dem Produkt abgeben. Wird\n z. B. in Folge einer neuen Erfindung Maschinerie derselben Art mit ver-\n minderter Ausgabe von Arbeit reproduzirt, so entwerthet die alte Maschi-\n [Running matter: I. 12] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"178\", \"facs\": \"#f0197\"} --> nerie mehr oder minder und überträgt daher auch verhältnissmässig weniger\n Werth auf das Produkt. Aber auch hier entspringt der Werthwechsel\nausserhalb des Produktionsprozesses, worin die Maschine als Produk-\n tionsmittel funktionirt. In diesem Prozess giebt sie nie mehr Werth ab\n als sie unabhängig von diesem Prozess besitzt.\n\n\n\n\nWie ein Wechsel im Werthe der Produktionsmittel, ob auch rück-\n wirkend nach ihrem bereits erfolgten Eintritt in den Prozess, ihren Cha-\n rakter als konstantes Kapital nicht verändert, ebenso wenig berührt\n ein Wechsel im Verhältnisse von konstantem und variab-\n lem Kapital ihren begrifflichen Unterschied. Die technologischen Be-\n dingungen des Arbeitsprozesses mögen z. B. so umgestaltet werden, dass\n wo früher 10 Arbeiter mit 10 Werkzeugen von geringem Werth eine ver-\n hältnissmässig kleine Masse von Rohmaterial verarbeiteten, jetzt 1 Arbei-\n ter mit einer theuren Maschine das hundertfache Rohmaterial verarbeitet.\n In diesem Falle wäre das konstante Kapital, d. h. die Werthmasse\n der angewandten Produktionsmittel, sehr gewachsen, und der variable\n Theil des Kapitals, der in Arbeitskraft vorgeschossene, sehr gefallen.\n Dieser Wechsel ändert jedoch nur das Grössenverhältniss zwischen\n konstantem und variablem Kapital, oder die Proportion, worin das Ge-\n sammtkapital in konstante und variable Bestandtheile zerfällt, be-\n rührt dagegen nicht den Unterschied von konstant und variabel.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0001","text":"Die verschiedenen Faktoren des Arbeitsprozesses nehmen verschied-\n nen Antheil an der Bildung des Produkten-Werths.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"6.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0002","text":"Der Arbeiter setzt dem Arbeitsgegenstand neuen Tauschwerth\n zu durch Zusatz eines bestimmten Quantums von Arbeit, abge-\n sehn vom bestimmten Inhalt, Zweck und technologischen Charakter seiner\n Arbeit. Andrerseits finden wir die Werthe der verzehrten Produktions-\n mittel wieder als Bestandtheile des Produkten-Werths, z. B.\n die Werthe von Baumwolle und Spindel im Garnwerth. Der Werth der\n Produktionsmittel wird also erhalten durch seine Uebertragung\n auf das Produkt. Diess Uebertragen geschieht während der Verwand-\n lung der Produktionsmittel in Produkt, im Arbeitsprozess. Es ist ver-\n mittelt durch die Arbeit. Aber wie?","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"6.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0003","text":"Der Arbeiter arbeitet nicht doppelt in derselben Zeit, nicht ein-\n mal um der Baumwolle durch seine Arbeit einen Werth zuzusetzen, und das\n andremal um ihren alten Werth zu erhalten, oder, was dasselbe, um den\n Werth der Baumwolle, die er verarbeitet, und der Spindel, womit er\n arbeitet, auf das Produkt, das Garn, zu übertragen. Sondern durch\n blosses Zusetzen von neuem Werth erhält er den alten Werth. Da\n aber der Zusatz von neuem Werth zum Arbeitsgegenstand und die Erhal-\n tung der alten Werthe im Produkt zwei ganz verschiedne Resultate sind,\n die der Arbeiter in derselben Zeit hervorbringt, obgleich er nur\n einmal in derselben Zeit arbeitet, kann diese Doppelseitigkeit\n des Resultats offenbar nur aus der Doppelseitigkeit seiner\n Arbeit selbst erklärt werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"6.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0004","text":"In demselben Zeitpunkt muss sie in\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"166\", \"facs\": \"#f0185\"} --> einer Eigenschaft Werth schaffen und in einer andern Eigenschaft Werth\n erhalten oder übertragen.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"6.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0005","text":"Wie setzt jeder Arbeiter Arbeitszeit und daher Werth zu? Immer nur\n in der Form seiner eigenthümlich produktiven Arbeitsweise. Der Spinner\n setzt nur Arbeitszeit zu, indem er spinnt, der Weber, indem er webt, der\n Schmidt, indem er schmiedet. Durch die zweckbestimmte Form\n aber, worin sie Arbeit überhaupt zusetzen und daher Neuwerth,\n durch das Spinnen, Weben, Schmieden werden die Produktionsmittel,\n Baumwolle und Spindel, Garn und Webstuhl, Eisen und Amboss, zu Bil-\n dungselementen eines Produkts, eines neuen Gebrauchswerths [Note: „Labour gives a new creation for one extinguished.“ (An Essay on\n the Polit. Econ. of Nations. London 1821, p. 13.)] .\n Die alte Form ihres Gebrauchswerths vergeht, aber nur um in einer\n neuen Form von Gebrauchswerth aufzugehn.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"6.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0006","text":"Bei Betrachtung des\n Werthbildungsprozesses ergab sich aber, dass so weit ein Gebrauchswerth\n zweckgemäss vernutzt wird zur Produktion eines neuen Gebrauchswerths,\n die zur Herstellung des vernutzten Gebrauchswerths nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit einen Theil der zur Herstellung des neuen Gebrauchswerths nothwen-\n digen Arbeitszeit bildet, also Arbeitszeit ist, die vom vernutzten Produk-\n tionsmittel auf das neue Produkt übertragen wird. Der Arbeiter erhält\n also die Werthe der vernutzten Produktionsmittel oder überträgt sie als\n Werthbestandtheile auf das Produkt, nicht durch sein Zusetzen von\n Arbeit überhaupt, sondern durch den besondren nützlichen\n Charakter, durch die spezifisch produktive Form dieser zu-\n sätzlichen Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"6.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0007","text":"Als solche zweckgemässe produktive Thätigkeit, Spin-\n nen, Weben, Schmieden, erweckt die Arbeit durch ihren blossen Kon-\n takt die Produktionsmittel von den Todten, begeistet sie zu Faktoren des\n Arbeitsprozesses und verbindet sich mit ihnen zu Produkten.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"6.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0008","text":"Wäre die spezifische produktive Arbeit des Arbeiters nicht Spin-\n nen, so würde er die Baumwolle nicht in Garn verwandeln, also auch die\n Werthe von Baumwolle und Spindel nicht auf das Garn übertragen. Wech-\n selt dagegen derselbe Arbeiter das Metier und wird Tischler, so wird er\n nach wie vor durch einen Arbeitstag seinem Material Werth zusetzen.\n Er setzt ihn also zu nicht durch seine Arbeit, soweit sie Spinnarbeit\n oder Tischlerarbeit, sondern so weit sie abstrakte, gesell-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"167\", \"facs\": \"#f0186\"} --> schaftliche Arbeit überhaupt, und er setzt eine bestimmte Werth-\n grösse zu, nicht weil seine Arbeit einen besondern nützlichen Inhalt hat,\n sondern weil sie eine bestimmte Zeit dauert.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"6.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0009","text":"In ihrer abstrakten\n allgemeinen Eigenschaft also, als Verausgabung menschlicher Arbeits-\n kraft, setzt die Arbeit des Spinners den Werthen von Baumwolle und Spin-\n del Neuwerth zu, und in ihrer konkreten, besondern, nütz-\n lichen Eigenschaft als Spinnprozess, überträgt sie den Werth dieser\n Produktionsmittel auf das Produkt und erhält so ihren Werth im Pro-\n dukt. Daher die Doppelseitigkeit ihres Resultats in demselben\n Zeitpunkt.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"6.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0010","text":"Durch das bloss quantitative Zusetzen von Arbeit wird neuer\n Werth zugesetzt, durch die Qualität der zugesetzten Ar-\n beit werden die alten Werthe der Produktionsmittel im Produkt erhalten.\n Diese doppelseitige Wirkung derselben Arbeit in Folge ihres doppelsei-\n tigen Charakters zeigt sich handgreiflich an verschiednen Erscheinungen.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"6.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0011","text":"Nimm an, irgend eine Erfindung befähige den Spinner in 6 Stunden\n so viel Baumwolle zu verspinnen wie früher in 36 Stunden. Als zweck-\n mässig-nützliche, produktive Thätigkeit hat seine Arbeit ihre Kraft\n versechsfacht. Ihr Produkt ist ein sechsfaches, 36 statt 6 lbs. Garn.\n Aber die 36 Pfund Baumwolle saugen jetzt nur so viel Arbeitszeit ein als\n früher 6 Pfund. Ein Sechstel weniger neuer Arbeit wird ihnen zugesetzt\n als mit der alten Methode, daher nur noch ein Sechstel des früheren Werths.\n Andrerseits existirt jetzt der sechsfache Werth von Baumwolle im\n Produkt, den 36 Pfund Garn. In den 6 Spinnstunden wird ein sechsmal\n grösserer Werth von Rohmaterial erhalten und auf das Produkt über-\n tragen, obgleich demselben Rohmaterial ein sechsmal kleinerer Neu-\n werth zugesetzt wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"6.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0012","text":"Diess zeigt, wie die Eigenschaft, worin die\n Arbeit während desselben untheilbaren Prozesses Werthe erhält, wesent-\n lich unterschieden ist von der Eigenschaft, worin sie Werth schafft. Je\n mehr nothwendige Arbeitszeit während der Spinnoperation auf dasselbe\n Quantum Baumwolle geht, desto grösser der Neuwerth, der\n der Baumwolle zugesetzt wird, aber je mehr Pfunde Baumwolle in der-\n selben Arbeitszeit versponnen werden, desto grösser der alte\n Werth, der im Produkt erhalten wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"6.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0013","text":"Nimm umgekehrt an, die Produktivität der Spinnarbeit bleibe un-\n verändert, der Spinner brauche also nach wie vor gleich viel Zeit, um ein\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"168\", \"facs\": \"#f0187\"} --> Pfund Baumwolle in Garn zu verwandeln. Aber der Tauschwerth\n der Baumwolle selbst wechsle, ein Pfund Baumwolle steige oder falle um\n das Sechsfache seines Preises. In beiden Fällen fährt der Spinner fort\ndemselben Quantum Baumwolle dieselbe Arbeitszeit zuzu-\n setzen, also denselben Werth und in beiden Fällen produzirt er in\n gleicher Zeit gleich viel Garn. Dennoch ist der Werth, den er von der\n Baumwolle auf das Garn, das Produkt, überträgt, das einemal sechsmal\n kleiner, das andremal sechsmal grösser als zuvor. Ebenso wenn die Ar-\n beitsmittel sich vertheuern oder verwohlfeilen, aber stets denselben Dienst\n im Arbeitsprozess leisten.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"6.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0014","text":"Bleiben die technologischen Bedingungen des Spinnprozesses unver-\n ändert und geht gleichfalls kein Werthwechsel mit seinen Produk-\n tionsmitteln vor, so verbraucht der Spinner nach wie vor in gleichen Ar-\n beitszeiten gleiche Quanta Rohmaterial und Maschinerie von gleichbleiben-\n den Werthen. Der Werth, den er im Produkt erhält, steht dann in\n direktem Verhältniss zu dem Neuwerth, den er zusetzt. In zwei\n Wochen setzt er zweimal mehr Arbeit zu als in einer Woche, also zwei-\n mal mehr Werth, und zugleich vernutzt er zweimal mehr Material von\n zweimal mehr Werth, und verschleisst zweimal mehr Maschinerie von zwei-\n mal mehr Werth, erhält also im Produkt von zwei Wochen zweimal mehr\n Werth als im Produkt einer Woche.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"6.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0015","text":"Unter gegebnen gleichbleibenden\n Produktionsbedingungen erhält der Arbeiter um so mehr Werth, je mehr\n Werth er zusetzt, aber er erhält nicht mehr Werth, weil er mehr Werth\n zusetzt, sondern weil er ihn unter gleichbleibenden und von seiner\n eignen Arbeit unabhängigen Bedingungen zusetzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"6.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0016","text":"Allerdings kann in einem relativen, wenn auch nicht absoluten\n Sinn gesagt werden, dass der Arheiter stets in derselben Propor-\n tion alte Werthe erhält, worin er Neuwerth zusetzt. Ob die Baum-\n wolle von 1 sh. auf 2 sh. steige oder auf 6 d. falle, er erhält in dem\n Produkt einer Stunde stets nur halb so viel Baumwollwerth, wie der auch\n wechsle, als in dem Produkt von zwei Stunden. Wechselt ferner die\nProduktivität seiner eignen Arbeit, sie steige oder falle, so wird er\n z. B. in einer Arbeitsstunde mehr oder weniger Baumwolle verspinnen als\n früher, und dem entsprechend mehr oder weniger Baumwollwerth im\n Produkt einer Arbeitsstunde erhalten. Mit alle dem wird er in\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"169\", \"facs\": \"#f0188\"} --> zwei Arbeitsstunden zweimal mehr Werth erhalten als in einer Arbeits-\n stunde.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"6.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0017","text":"Werth, von seiner nur symbolischen Darstellung im Werth-\n zeichen abgesehn, existirt nur in einem Gebrauchswerth, einem\n Ding. (Der Mensch selbst, als blosses Dasein von Arbeitskraft betrachtet,\n ist ein Naturgegenstand, ein Ding, wenn auch lebendiges, selbstbewusstes\n Ding, und die Arbeit selbst ist dingliche Aeusserung dieser Kraft.)\n Geht daher der Gebrauchswerth verloren, so geht auch der Tauschwerth\n verloren. Die Produktionsmittel verlieren mit ihrem Gebrauchswerth nicht\n zugleich ihren Tauschwerth, weil sie durch den Arbeitsprozess die ursprüng-\n liche Gestalt ihres Gebrauchswerths in der That nur verlieren, um im Pro-\n dukt die Gestalt eines andern Gebrauchswerths zu gewinnen. So wichtig\n es aber für den Tauschwerth ist in irgend einem Gebrauchswerth zu existi-\n ren, so gleichgültig ist es für ihn, in welchem er existirt, wie die Meta-\n morphose der Waare zeigt.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"6.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0018","text":"Es folgt hieraus, dass im Arbeitsprozess\n Werth vom Produktionsmittel auf das Produkt nur übergeht, so weit das\n Produktionsmittel mit seinem selbstständigen Gebrauchswerth auch seinen\n Tauschwerth verliert. Es giebt nur den Werth an das Produkt ab,\n den es als Produktionsmittel verliert. Die gegenständlichen Fak-\n toren des Arbeitsprozesses verhalten sich aber in dieser Hinsicht\n verschieden.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"6.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0019","text":"Die Kohle, womit die Maschine geheizt wird, verschwindet spurlos,\n ebenso der Talg, womit man die Axe des Rades schmiert u. s. w. Farben\n und andre Hilfsstoffe verschwinden, zeigen sich aber in den Eigenschaften\n des Produkts. Das Rohmaterial bildet die Substanz des Produkts, hat\n aber seine Form verändert. Rohmaterial und Hilfsstoffe verlieren also die\n selbstständige Gestalt, womit sie in den Arbeitsprozess als Gebrauchs-\n werthe eintraten. Anders mit den eigentlichen Arbeitsmitteln.\n Ein Instrument, eine Maschine, ein Fabrikgebäude, ein Gefäss u. s. w.\n dienen im Arbeitsprozess nur, so lange sie ihre ursprüngliche Gestalt bewah-\n ren und morgen wieder in eben derselben Form in den Arbeitsprozess ein-\n gehn, wie gestern. Wie sie während ihres Lebens, des Arbeitsprozesses,\n ihre selbstständige Gestalt dem Produkt gegenüber bewahren, so auch nach\n ihrem Tode.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"6.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0020","text":"Die Leichen von Maschinen, Werkzeugen, Arbeitsgebäuden\n u. s. w. existiren immer noch selbstständig getrennt von den Produkten,\n die sie bilden halfen. Betrachten wir nun die ganze Periode, während\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"170\", \"facs\": \"#f0189\"} --> deren ein solches Arbeitsmittel dient, von dem Tag seines Eintritts in die\n Werkstätte bis zum Tage seiner Verbannung in die Rumpelkammer, so ist\n während dieser Periode sein Gebrauchswerth von der Arbeit vollständig\n verzehrt worden, und sein Tauschwerth daher vollständig auf das Produkt\n übergegangen. Hat eine Spinnmaschine z. B. in 10 Jahren ausgelebt, so\n ist während des zehnjährigen Arbeitsprozesses ihr Gesammtwerth auf das\n zehnjährige Produkt übergegangen. Die Lebensperiode eines Arbeitsmit-\n tels umfängt also eine grössere oder kleinere Anzahl stets von neuem\n mit ihm wiederholter Arbeitsprozesse.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"6.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0021","text":"Und es geht dem Arbeitsmittel\n wie dem Menschen. Jeder Mensch stirbt täglich um 24 Stunden ab. Man\n sieht aber keinem Menschen genau an, wie viel Tage er bereits verstorben\n ist. Diess verhindert Lebensversicherungsgesellschaften jedoch nicht, aus\n dem Durchschnittsleben der Menschen sehr sichre, und was noch viel mehr\n ist, sehr profitliche Schlüsse zu ziehn. So mit dem Arbeitsmittel. Man\n weiss aus der Erfahrung, wie lang ein Arbeitsmittel, z. B. eine Maschine\n von gewisser Art, durchschnittlich vorhält. Gesetzt sein Gebrauchswerth\n im Arbeitsprozess daure nur 6 Tage. So verliert es im Durchschnitt\n jeden Arbeitstag ⅙ seines Gebrauchswerths und giebt daher ⅙ seines\n Tauschwerths an das tägliche Produkt ab. In dieser Art wird der Ver-\n schleiss aller Arbeitsmittel berechnet, also z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"6.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0022","text":"ihr täglicher\n Verlust an Gebrauchswerth, und die ihm entsprechende tägliche Abgabe\n von Tauschwerth an das Produkt.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"6.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0023","text":"Es zeigt sich hier schlagend, dass ein Produktionsmittel nie mehr\n Werth an das Produkt abgiebt, als es selbst im Arbeitsprozess durch Ver-\n nichtung seines eignen Gebrauchswerths verliert. Hätte es keinen Tausch-\n werth zu verlieren, d. h. wäre es nicht selbst Produkt menschlicher Arbeit,\n so würde es keinen Tauschwerth an das Produkt abgeben. Es diente als\n Bildner von Gebrauchswerth, ohne als Bildner von Tauschwerth zu dienen.\n Diess ist daher der Fall mit allen Produktionsmitteln, die von Natur, ohne\n menschliches Zuthun, vorhanden sind, mit Erde, Wind, Wasser, dem Eisen\n in der Erzader, dem Holze des Urwaldes u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"6.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0024","text":"Ein andres interessantes Phänomen tritt uns hier entgegen. Eine\n Maschine sei z. B. 1000 Pfd. St. werth und schleisse sich in 1000 Tagen\n ab. In diesem Falle geht täglich \\frac{1}{1000} des Werths der Maschine von ihr\n selbst auf ihr tägliches Produkt über, aber, obgleich mit abnehmender\n Lebenskraft, wirkt die Maschine stets ganz im Arbeitsprozess. Es zeigt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"171\", \"facs\": \"#f0190\"} --> sich hier also, dass ein Faktor des Arbeitsprozesses, ein Pro-\n duktionsmittel, ganz in den Arbeitsprozess, aber nur stück-\n weis in den Verwerthungsprozess eingeht.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"6.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0025","text":"Der Unterschied\n von Arbeitsprozess und Verwerthungsprozess reflektirt sich hier an den\ngegenständlichen Faktoren, indem dasselbe Produktionsmittel\n als Element des Arbeitsprozesses ganz und als Element der\nWerthbildung nur stückweis in demselben Produktionsprozess\n zählt [Note: Es handelt sich hier nicht um Reparaturarbeiten der Arbeits-\n mittel, Maschinen, Baulichkeiten u. s. w. Eine Maschine, die reparirt wird,\n funktionirt nicht als Arbeitsmittel, sondern als Arbeitsmaterial. Es\n wird nicht mit ihr gearbeitet, sondern sie selbst wird bearbeitet, um ihren Ge-\n brauchswerth zu flicken. Solche Reparaturarbeiten kann man für unsern Zweck\n immer eingeschlossen denken in die zur Produktion des Arbeitsmittels erheischte\n Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"6.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0026","text":"Im Text handelt es sich um den Verschleiss, den kein Doktor\n kuriren kann und der allmählig den Tod herbeiführt, um „that kind of wear which\n cannot be repaired from time to time, and which, in the case of a knife, would\n ultimately reduce it to a state in which the cutler would say of it, it is not worth\n a new blade.“ Man hat im Text gesehn, dass eine Maschine z. B. ganz in jeden\n einzelnen Arbeitsprozess, aber nur stückweise in den gleichzeitigen Ver-\n werthungsprozess eingeht. Danach zu beurtheilen die folgende Begriffsverwechs-\n lung: „Mr. Ricardo speaks of the portion of the labour of the engineer in making\n stocking machines“ als z. B. enthalten in dem Werth von ein paar Strümpfen.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"6.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0027","text":"„Yet the total labour that produced each single pair of stockings …\n includes the whole labour of the engineer, not a portion; for one\n machine makes many pairs, and none of those pairs could have been done\n without any part of the machine.“ (Observations on certain verbal\n disputes in Pol. Econ., particularly relating to Value, and\n to demand and supply. London 1821, p. 54.) Der Verfasser, ein un-\n gemein selbstgefälliger „wiseacre“, hat mit seiner Konfusion und daher seiner Po-\n lemik nur so weit Recht, als weder Ricardo noch irgend ein andrer Oekonom, vor\n oder nach ihm, die beiden Seiten der Arbeit genau geschieden,\n daher noch weniger ihre verschiedne Wirkung auf die Werthbildung analysirt hat.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"6.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0028","text":"Andrerseits kann umgekehrt ein Produktionsmittel ganz in den Ver-\n werthungsprozess eingehn, obgleich nur stückweis in den Arbeitsprozess.\n Nimm an, beim Verspinnen der Baumwolle fielen täglich auf 115 Pfund\n 15 Pfunde ab, die kein Garn, sondern nur devil’s dust bilden. Dennoch,\n wenn dieser Abfall von 15 % normal, von der Durchschnitts-Verarbeitung\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"172\", \"facs\": \"#f0191\"} --> der Baumwolle unzertrennlich ist, geht der Werth der 15 lbs. Baumwolle,\n die kein Element des Garns, ganz eben so sehr in seinen Werth ein, wie\n der Werth der 100 lbs., die seine Substanz bilden. Der Gebrauchswerth\n von 15 lbs. Baumwolle muss verstauben, um 100 lbs. Garn zu machen.\n Der Untergang dieser Baumwolle ist also eine Produktionsbedingung\n des Garns. Eben desswegen giebt sie ihren Tauschwerth an das Garn\n ab.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"6.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0029","text":"Diess gilt von allen Exkrementen des Arbeitsprozesses,\n in dem Grad wenigstens, worin diese Exkremente nicht wieder neue Pro-\n duktionsmittel und daher neue selbstständige Gebrauchswerthe bilden. So\n sieht man in den grossen Maschinenfabriken zu Manchester Berge von Eisen-\n abfällen, durch cyklopische Maschinen gleich Hobelspähnen abgeschält,\n am Abend auf grossen Wagen aus der Fabrik in die Eisengiesserei wandern,\n um den andern Tag wieder als massives Eisen aus der Eisengiesserei in\n die Fabrik zurückzuwandern.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"6.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0030","text":"Nur soweit Produktionsmittel während des Arbeitsprozesses Tausch-\n werth in der Gestalt ihrer alten Gebrauchswerthe verlieren, übertra-\n gen sie Tauschwerth auf die neue Gestalt des Produkts. Das Maxi-\n mum des Werthverlustes, den sie im Arbeitsprozess erleiden kön-\n nen, ist aber beschränkt durch ihre ursprüngliche Werth-\n grösse, womit sie in den Arbeitsprozess eintreten, oder durch die zu\n ihrer eignen Produktion erheischte Arbeitszeit. Produktionsmittel\n können dem Produkt daher nie mehr Werth zusetzen, als\n sie unabhängig vom Arbeitsprozess, dem sie dienen,\n besitzen. Wie nützlich ein Arbeitsmaterial, eine Maschine, ein Pro-\n duktionsmittel, wenn es 150 Pfd. St., sage 500 Arbeitstage, kostet, setzt\n es dem Gesammtprodukt, zu dessen Bildung es dient, nie mehr als\n 150 Pfd. St. zu.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"6.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0031","text":"Sein Werth ist bestimmt nicht durch den Arbeitspro-\n zess, worin es als Produktionsmittel eingeht, sondern durch den Arbeits-\n prozess, woraus es als Produkt herauskommt. In dem Arbeitsprozess\n dient es nur als Gebrauchswerth, als Ding mit nützlichen\n Eigenschaften, und gäbe daher keinen Tauschwerth an das Produkt\n ab, hätte es nicht Tauschwerth besessen vor seinem Eintritt in den\n Prozess [Note: Man begreift daher die Abgeschmacktheit des faden J. B. Say, der\n den Mehrwerth (Zins, Profit, Rente) aus den „services productives“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"6.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0032","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"173\", \"facs\": \"#f0192\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"6.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0033","text":"Indem die produktive Arbeit Produktionsmittel in Bildungselemente\n eines neuen Produkts verwandelt, geht mit deren Tauschwerth eine Seelen-\n wanderung vor. Er geht aus dem verzehrten Leib in den neu gestalteten\n Leib über. Aber diese Seelenwanderung ereignet sich gleichsam hinter\n dem Rücken der wirklichen Arbeit. Der Arbeiter kann neue Arbeit\n nicht zusetzen, also nicht neuen Werth schaffen, ohne alte Werthe\n zu erhalten, denn er muss die Arbeit immer in bestimmter nützlicher\n Form zusetzen, und er kann sie nicht in nützlicher Form zusetzen, ohne\n Produkte zu Produktionsmitteln eines neuen Produkts zu machen, und da-\n durch ihren Werth auf das neue Produkt zu übertragen.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"6.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0034","text":"Es ist also eine\nNaturgabe der sich bethätigenden Arbeitskraft, der lebendigen Arbeit,\nWerth zu erhalten, indem sie Werth zusetzt, eine Natur-\n gabe, die dem Arbeiter nichts kostet, aber dem Kapitalisten viel ein-\n bringt, die Erhaltung des vorhandnen Kapitalwerths [Note: „Of all the instruments of the farmer’s trade, the labour of man …\n is that on which he is most to rely for the re-payment of his capital.] .\n [Note: ableiten will, welche die Produktionsmittel, Erde, Instrumente, Leder u. s. w.\n durch ihre Gebrauchswerthe im Arbeitsprozesse leisten. Herr Wilhelm\n Roscher, der es nicht leicht lässt, artige apologetische Einfälle schwarz auf\n weiss zu registriren, ruft aus: „Sehr richtig bemerkt J. B. Say, Traité,\n t. I. ch.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"6.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0035","text":"4: „der durch eine Oelmühle nach Abzug aller Kosten hervorge-\n brachte Werth sei doch etwas Neues, von der Arbeit, wodurch die Oelmühle\n selbst geschaffen worden, wesentlich verschiedenes.“ (l. c. p. 82 Note.) Sehr\n richtig! Das von der Oelmühle hervorgebrachte „Oel“ ist etwas sehr Ver-\n schiednes von der Arbeit, welche der Bau der Mühle kostet. Und unter\n „Werth“ versteht Herr Roscher solches Zeug wie „Oel“, da „Oel“\n Werth hat, „in der Natur“ aber sich Steinöl vorfindet, wenn auch relativ nicht\n „sehr viel“, worauf wohl seine andre Bemerkung abzielt: „Tauschwerthe\n bringt sie (die Natur!)","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"6.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0036","text":"fast gar nicht hervor.“ Es geht der Roscher’schen Natur\n mit dem Tauschwerth, wie der thörichten Jungfrau mit dem Kind, das nur „ganz\n klein war.“ Derselbe „Gelehrte“ („savant sérieux“) bemerkt noch bei oben er-\n wähnter Gelegenheit: „Die Schule Ricardo’s pflegt auch das Kapital unter den\n Begriff Arbeit zu subsumiren als „aufgesparte Arbeit.“ Diess ist ungeschickt\n (!), weil (!) ja (!) der Kapital besitzer (!) doch (!) mehr (!) gethan hat als die\nblosse (?!) Hervorbringung (??) und (??) Erhaltung desselben (wessel-\n bigen?): eben (?!?) die Enthaltung vom eignen Genusse, wofür er z. B. (!!!)\n Zinsen verlangt.“ (l. c.) Wie „geschickt“!","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"6.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0037","text":"diese „anatomisch-phy-\n siologische Methode“ der politischen Oekonomie, die aus blossem „Ver-\n langen“ ja doch eben „Werth“ entwickelt.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"174\", \"facs\": \"#f0193\"} --> So lange das Geschäft flott geht, ist der Kapitalist zu sehr in die Plus-\n macherei vertieft, um diese Gratisgabe der Arbeit zu sehn. Gewaltsame\n Unterbrechungen des Arbeitsprozesses, Krisen, machen sie ihm empfind-\n lich bemerksam [Note: In der Times vom 26. Nov. 1862 jammert ein Fabrikant, dessen\n Spinnerei 800 Arbeiter beschäftigt, und wöchentlich im Durchschnitt 150 Ballen\n ostindischer oder ungefähr 130 Ballen amerikanischer Baumwolle verzehrt, dem\n Publikum die jährlichen Stillstandskosten seiner Fabrik vor. Er schlägt\n sie auf 6000 Pfd. St. an.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"6.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0038","text":"Unter diesen Unkosten befinden sich viele Posten, die\n uns hier nichts angehn, wie Grundrente, Steuern, Versicherungsprämien, Salaire\n für jährlich engagirte Arbeiter, manager, Buchhalter, Ingenieur u. s. w. Dann\n aber berechnet er für 150 Pfd. St. Kohlen, um die Fabrik von Zeit zu Zeit zu wärmen\n und die Dampfmaschine gelegentlich in Gang zu setzen, ausserdem Salaire für\n Arbeiter, die durch gelegentliche Arbeit die Maschinerie „flüssig“ erhalten. End-\n lich 1200 Pfd. St. für Verschlechterung der Maschinerie, da „the weather and the\n natural principle of decay do not suspend their operations because the steam-\n engine ceases to revolve.“ Er bemerkt ausdrücklich, diese Summe von 1200 Pfd. St.\n sei so gering angeschlagen, weil sich die Maschinerie bereits in sehr abgenutztem\n Zustand befinde.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"6.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0039","text":"Was überhaupt im Arbeitsprozess an den Produktionsmitteln verzehrt\n wird, ist ihr Gebrauchswerth, dessen Konsumtion durch die Arbeit Pro-\n dukte bildet. Ihr Tauschwerth wird in der That nicht konsumirt [Note: „Productive Consumption: where the consumption of a commo-\n dity is a part of the process of production … In these instances\nthere is no consumption of value.“ S. P. Newman l. c. p. 296.] ,\n kann also auch nicht reproduzirt werden. Er wird erhalten,\n aber nicht weil eine Operation mit ihm selbst im Arbeitsprozess vorgeht,\n sondern weil der Gebrauchswerth, worin er ursprünglich existirt, zwar\n verschwindet, aber nur in einem anderen Gebrauchswerth verschwindet.\n Der Tauschwerth der Produktionsmittel erscheint daher wieder\n im Werth des Produkts, aber er wird, genau gesprochen, nicht repro-\n duzirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"6.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0040","text":"Was produzirt wird, ist der neue Gebrauchswerth, worin der\n alte Tauschwerth wieder erscheint [Note: In einem nordamerikanischen Kompendium, das vielleicht 20 Auflagen er-] .","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"6.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0041","text":"[Note: The other two — the working stock of the cattle, and the … carts, ploughs,\n spades, and so forth, without a given portion of the first, are nothing at all.“\n (Edmund Burke: Thoughts and Details on Scarcity, origi-\n nally presented to the R. Hon. W. Pitt in the Month of No-\n vember 1795, edit. London 1800“, p. 10.]","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"6.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0042","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"175\", \"facs\": \"#f0194\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"6.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0043","text":"Anders mit dem subjektiven Faktor des Arbeitsprozesses, der\n sich bethätigenden Arbeitskraft. Während die Arbeit durch ihre zweck-\n mässige Form den Werth der Produktionsmittel auf das Produkt über-\n trägt und erhält, bildet jedes Moment ihrer Bewegung zusätzlichen\n Werth, Neuwerth. Gesetzt der Produktionsprozess breche ab\n beim Punkt, wo der Arbeiter ein Aequivalent für den Werth\n seiner eignen Arbeitskraft produzirt, durch sechsstündige Ar-\n beit z. B. einen Werth von 3 sh. zugesetzt hat. Dieser Werth bildet den\nUeberschuss des Produktenwerthes über seine dem Werth der\n Produktionsmittel geschuldeten Bestandtheile. Es ist der einzige\n Originalwerth, der innerhalb dieses Prozesses entstand,\n der einzige Werththeil des Produkts, der durch den Prozess\n selbst produzirt ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"6.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0044","text":"Allerdings ersetzt er nur das vom Kapitalisten\n beim Kauf der Arbeitskraft vorgeschossene, vom Arbeiter selbst in Lebens-\n mitteln verausgabte Geld. Mit Bezug auf die verausgabten 3 sh. erscheint\n der Neuwerth von 3 sh. nur als Reproduktion. Aber er ist wirk-\n lich reproduzirt, nicht nur scheinbar, wie der Werth der Pro-\n duktionsmittel. Der Ersatz eines Werths durch den andern\n ist hier vermittelt durch neue Werthschöpfung.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"6.44"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0045","text":"Wir wissen jedoch bereits, dass der Arbeitsprozess über den Punkt\n hinaus fortdauert, wo ein blosses Aequivalent für den Werth der\n [Note: lebt hat, liest man: „It matters not in what form capital reappears.“ Nach einer\n redseligen Aufzählung aller möglichen Produktionsingredienzen, deren Werth im\n Produkt wieder erscheint, heisst’s schliesslich: „The various kinds of food,\n clothing, and shelter, necessary for the existence and comfort of the human being,\n are also changed. They are consumed from time to time, and their value re-\n appears, in that new vigour imparted to his body and mind, forming fresh\n capital, to be employed again in the work of production.“ (F. Weyland l. c.\n p. 31, 32.) Von allen andern Wunderlichkeiten abgesehn, ist es z. B. nicht der\n Preis des Brodes, der in der erneuten Kraft wieder erscheint, sondern seine\n blutbildenden Substanzen.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"6.45"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0046","text":"Was dagegen als Werth der Kraft wiedererscheint,\n sind nicht die Lebensmittel, sondern ihr Werth. Dieselben Lebensmittel,\n wenn sie nur die Hälfte kosten, produziren ganz eben so viel Muskel. Knochen\n u. s. w., kurz dieselbe Kraft, aber nicht Kraft vom selben Werth. Diess Umsetzen\n von „Werth“ in „Kraft“ und die ganze pharisäische Unbestimmtheit verstecken\n den allerdings vergeblichen Versuch aus blossem Wiedererscheinen\n vorgeschossener Werthe einen Mehrwerth herauszudrechseln.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"176\", \"facs\": \"#f0195\"} --> Arbeitskraft reproduzirt und dem Arbeitsgegenstand zugesetzt wäre. Statt\n der 6 Stunden, die hierzu genügten, währt der Prozess z. B. 12 Stunden.\n Durch die Bethätigung der Arbeitskraft wird also nicht nur ihr eigner\n Werth reproduzirt, sondern ein überschüssiger Werth produzirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"6.46"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0047","text":"Dieser\nMehrwerth bildet den Ueberschuss des Produktenwerthes\n über den Werth der verzehrten Produktbildner, d. h. der Pro-\n duktionsmittel und der Arbeitskraft.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"6.47"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0048","text":"Indem wir die verschiedenen Rollen dargestellt, welche die verschie-\n denen Faktoren des Arbeitsprozesses in der Bildung des Produkten-\n werths spielen, haben wir in der That die Funktionen der ver-\n schiedenen Bestandtheile des Kapitals in seinem eignen\n Verwerthungsprozess charakterisirt. Der Ueberschuss des Ge-\n sammtwerths des Produkts über die Werthsumme seiner Bildungselemente\n ist der Ueberschuss des verwertheten Kapitals über den ur-\n sprünglich vorgeschossenen Kapitalwerth. Produktions-\n mittel auf der einen Seite, Arbeitskraft auf der andern, sind nur die ver-\n schiednen Existenzformen, die der ursprüngliche Kapitalwerth bei Abstrei-\n fung seiner Geldform und seiner Verwandlung in die Faktoren des Arbeits-\n prozesses annahm.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"6.48"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0049","text":"Der Theil des Kapitals also, der sich in Produktionsmittel,\n d. h. in Rohmaterial, Hilfsstoffe und Arbeitsmittel umsetzt, verän-\n dert seine Werthgrösse nicht im Produktionsprozesse. Ich\n nenne ihn daher konstanten Kapitaltheil, oder kürzer: kon-\n stantes Kapital.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"6.49"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0050","text":"Der in Arbeitskraft umgesetzte Theil des Kapitals verändert\n dagegen seinen Werth im Produktionsprozesse. Er reproduzirt sein\n eignes Aequivalent und einen Ueberschuss darüber, Mehrwerth, der\n selbst wechseln, grösser oder kleiner sein kann. Aus einer konstanten\n Grösse verwandelt sich dieser Theil des Kapitals fortwährend in eine\n variable. Ich nenne ihn daher variablen Kapitaltheil, oder kür-\n zer: variables Kapital. Dieselben Kapitalbestandtheile,\n die sich vom Standpunkt des Arbeitsprozesses als objektive\n und subjektive Faktoren, als Produktionsmittel und Arbeitskraft unter-\n scheiden, unterscheiden sich vom Standpunkt des Verwerthungs-\n prozesses als konstantes Kapital und variables Kapital.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"6.50"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0051","text":"Der Begriff des konstanten Kapitals schliesst eine Werthrevo-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"177\", \"facs\": \"#f0196\"} --> lution seiner Bestandtheile in keiner Weise aus. Nimm an, das Pfund\n Baumwolle koste heute 6 d. und steige morgen, in Folge eines Ausfalls\n der Baumwollerndte, auf 1 sh. Die alte Baumwolle, die fortfährt verar-\n beitet zu werden, ist zum Werth von 6 d. gekauft, aber sie fügt dem Pro-\n dukt jetzt einen Werththeil von 1 sh. zu. Und die bereits versponnene,\n vielleicht schon als Garn auf dem Markt cirkulirende Baumwolle, fügt dem\n Produkt ebenfalls das Doppelte ihres ursprünglichen Werthes zu. Man\n sieht jedoch, dass diese Werthwechsel unabhängig sind von der Ver-\n werthung der Baumwolle im Spinnprozess selbst. Wäre die\n alte Baumwolle noch gar nicht in den Arbeitsprozess eingegangen, so\n könnte sie jetzt zu 1 sh. statt zu 6 d. wieder verkauft werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"6.51"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0052","text":"Um-\n gekehrt: Je weniger Arbeitsprozesse sie noch durchlaufen hat,\n desto sichrer ist diess Resultat. Es ist daher Gesetz der Spekulation bei\n solchen Werthrevolutionen auf das Rohmaterial in seiner mindest verarbei-\n teten Form zu spekuliren, also eher auf Garn als auf Gewebe und eher auf\n die Baumwolle selbst als auf das Garn. Die Werthänderung ent-\n springt hier in dem Prozesse, der Baumwolle produzirt, nicht in dem Pro-\n zesse, worin sie als Produktionsmittel und daher als konstantes Kapi-\n tal funktionirt. Der Werth einer Waare ist zwar bestimmt durch das\n Quantum der in ihr enthaltenen Arbeit, aber diess Quantum ist gesell-\n schaftlich bestimmt. Hat sich die gesellschaftlich zu ihrer Produktion\n erheischte Arbeitszeit verändert — und dasselbe Quantum Baum-\n wolle z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"6.52"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0053","text":"stellt in ungünstigen Erndten grösseres Quantum\n Arbeit dar, als in günstigen — so findet eine Rückwirkung auf die\n alte Waare statt, die immer nur als einzelnes Exemplar ihrer Gattung\n gilt [Note: „Toutes les productions d’un même genre ne forment proprement qu’une\n masse, dont le prix se détermine en général et sans égard aux circonstances\n particulières.“ (Le Trosne l. c. p. 893.)] , deren Werth stets durch gesellschaftlich nothwendige,\n also auch stets unter gegenwärtigen gesellschaftlichen Be-\n dingungen nothwendige Arbeit gemessen wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"6.53"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0054","text":"Wie der Werth des Rohmaterials, mag der Werth bereits im Pro-\n duktionsprozess dienender Arbeitsmittel, der Maschinerie u. s. w.,\n wechseln, also auch der Werththeil, den sie dem Produkt abgeben. Wird\n z. B. in Folge einer neuen Erfindung Maschinerie derselben Art mit ver-\n minderter Ausgabe von Arbeit reproduzirt, so entwerthet die alte Maschi-\n [Running matter: I. 12] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"178\", \"facs\": \"#f0197\"} --> nerie mehr oder minder und überträgt daher auch verhältnissmässig weniger\n Werth auf das Produkt. Aber auch hier entspringt der Werthwechsel\nausserhalb des Produktionsprozesses, worin die Maschine als Produk-\n tionsmittel funktionirt. In diesem Prozess giebt sie nie mehr Werth ab\n als sie unabhängig von diesem Prozess besitzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"6.54"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0055","text":"Wie ein Wechsel im Werthe der Produktionsmittel, ob auch rück-\n wirkend nach ihrem bereits erfolgten Eintritt in den Prozess, ihren Cha-\n rakter als konstantes Kapital nicht verändert, ebenso wenig berührt\n ein Wechsel im Verhältnisse von konstantem und variab-\n lem Kapital ihren begrifflichen Unterschied. Die technologischen Be-\n dingungen des Arbeitsprozesses mögen z. B. so umgestaltet werden, dass\n wo früher 10 Arbeiter mit 10 Werkzeugen von geringem Werth eine ver-\n hältnissmässig kleine Masse von Rohmaterial verarbeiteten, jetzt 1 Arbei-\n ter mit einer theuren Maschine das hundertfache Rohmaterial verarbeitet.\n In diesem Falle wäre das konstante Kapital, d. h. die Werthmasse\n der angewandten Produktionsmittel, sehr gewachsen, und der variable\n Theil des Kapitals, der in Arbeitskraft vorgeschossene, sehr gefallen.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"6.55"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0056","text":"Dieser Wechsel ändert jedoch nur das Grössenverhältniss zwischen\n konstantem und variablem Kapital, oder die Proportion, worin das Ge-\n sammtkapital in konstante und variable Bestandtheile zerfällt, be-\n rührt dagegen nicht den Unterschied von konstant und variabel.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"6.56"}],"sha256":"f5fa90020b59924e6fd71bc1bbcbcfe670e18a5fcaab7b89ec63610393ad47b4","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202556784845Z_0e21c65e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2) Constantes Kapital und variables Kapital.","source_sha256":"f5fa90020b59924e6fd71bc1bbcbcfe670e18a5fcaab7b89ec63610393ad47b4","sha256":"b8a15cf66e1bd550ae549756ca3339cc48d98913a13153bf9b885451d52a5919","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:25:56.784878+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0001","text":"The various factors of the labour process take different shares in the formation of the product’s value.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"6.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0002","text":"The worker adds new exchange-value to the object of labour by adding a determinate quantum of labour, apart from the determinate content, purpose, and technological character of his labour. On the other hand, we find the values of the consumed means of production again as components of the product’s value, e.g. the values of cotton and spindle in the value of yarn. The value of the means of production is therefore preserved by its transfer to the product. This transfer takes place during the transformation of the means of production into product, in the labour process. It is mediated by labour. But how?","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"6.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0003","text":"The worker does not work twice in the same time, once in order to add value to the cotton by his labour, and the second time in order to preserve its old value, or, what is the same thing, in order to transfer to the product, the yarn, the value of the cotton which he processes and of the spindle with which he works. Rather, by the mere addition of new value he preserves the old value. But since the addition of new value to the object of labour and the preservation of the old values in the product are two quite different results which the worker produces in the same time, although he works only once in the same time, this two-sidedness of the result can evidently be explained only from the two-sidedness of his labour itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"6.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0004","text":"At the same moment it must, in\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"166\", \"facs\": \"#f0185\"} --> one capacity create value and in another capacity preserve or transfer value.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"6.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0005","text":"How does every worker add labour-time, and hence value? Always only in the form of his peculiar productive mode of labour. The spinner adds labour-time by spinning, the weaver by weaving, the smith by forging. But through the purpose-determined form in which they add labour in general and hence new value, through spinning, weaving, forging, the means of production—cotton and spindle, yarn and loom, iron and anvil—become elements in the formation of a product, of a new use-value [Note: “Labour gives a new creation for one extinguished.” (An Essay on\n the Polit. Econ. of Nations. London 1821, p. 13.)] .\nThe old form of their use-value passes away, but only in order to arise in a new form of use-value.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"6.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0006","text":"But in considering the process of value formation, it emerged that insofar as a use-value is purposefully consumed for the production of a new use-value, the labour-time necessary for the production of the consumed use-value forms a part of the labour-time necessary for the production of the new use-value, and is therefore labour-time that is transferred from the consumed means of production to the new product. The worker thus preserves the values of the consumed means of production, or transfers them to the product as components of value, not by his addition of labour in general, but by the particular useful character, by the specifically productive form of this additional labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"6.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0007","text":"As such purposefully productive activity—spinning, weaving, forging—labour, by its mere contact, awakens the means of production from the dead, inspires them into factors of the labour process, and combines with them into products.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"6.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0008","text":"If the worker’s specifically productive labour were not spinning, he would not transform cotton into yarn, and therefore would not transfer the values of cotton and spindle to the yarn. If, on the other hand, the same worker changes his trade and becomes a joiner, he will, as before, add value to his material by means of a working day. He therefore adds it not through his labour insofar as it is spinning labour or joinery labour, but insofar as it is abstract, social labour in general, and he adds a determinate magnitude of value, not because his labour has a particular useful content, but because it lasts for a determinate time.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"167\", \"facs\": \"#f0186\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"6.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0009","text":"Thus, in its abstract, general capacity, as expenditure of human labour-power, the labour of the spinner adds new value to the values of cotton and spindle; and in its concrete, particular, useful capacity as a spinning process, it transfers the value of these means of production to the product and thus preserves their value in the product. Hence the two-sidedness of its result at the same moment.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"6.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0010","text":"Through the merely quantitative addition of labour, new value is added; through the quality of the added labour, the old values of the means of production are preserved in the product. This two-sided effect of the same labour, in consequence of its two-sided character, manifests itself palpably in various phenomena.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"6.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0011","text":"Suppose that some invention enables the spinner to spin as much cotton in 6 hours as formerly in 36 hours. As purposive-useful, productive activity, his labour has sextupled its force. Its product is sixfold: 36 instead of 6 lbs. of yarn. But the 36 pounds of cotton now absorb only as much labour-time as formerly 6 pounds. One-sixth less new labour is added to them than by the old method, hence only one-sixth of the former value. On the other hand, there now exists in the product, the 36 pounds of yarn, six times the value of cotton. In the 6 spinning hours, a value of raw material six times greater is preserved and transferred to the product, although a new value six times smaller is added to the same raw material.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"6.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0012","text":"This shows how the capacity in which labour preserves values during the same indivisible process is essentially distinct from the capacity in which it creates value. The more necessary labour-time is expended upon the same quantity of cotton during the spinning operation, the greater the new value added to the cotton; but the more pounds of cotton are spun in the same labour-time, the greater the old value preserved in the product.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"6.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0013","text":"Suppose, conversely, that the productivity of spinning labour remains unchanged, so that the spinner still requires the same amount of time to transform a\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"168\", \"facs\": \"#f0187\"} --> pound of cotton into yarn. But let the exchange-value of the cotton itself change: a pound of cotton rises or falls by six times its price. In both cases the spinner continues to add the same labour-time to the same quantity of cotton, hence the same value, and in both cases he produces the same quantity of yarn in the same time. Nevertheless, the value which he transfers from the cotton to the yarn, the product, is in the one case six times smaller, in the other six times greater than before. Likewise, when the means of labour become dearer or cheaper, but always render the same service in the labour process.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"6.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0014","text":"If the technological conditions of the spinning process remain unchanged, and likewise no change of value occurs in its means of production, then the spinner continues to consume, in equal labour-times, equal quantities of raw material and machinery of constant values. The value which he preserves in the product then stands in direct proportion to the new value which he adds. In two weeks he adds twice as much labour as in one week, hence twice as much value, and at the same time consumes twice as much material of twice as much value, and wears out twice as much machinery of twice as much value; thus, in the product of two weeks, he preserves twice as much value as in the product of one week.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"6.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0015","text":"Under given constant conditions of production, the worker preserves so much the more value the more value he adds; but he does not preserve more value because he adds more value, but because he adds it under constant conditions independent of his own labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"6.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0016","text":"To be sure, in a relative, though not absolute, sense it can be said that the worker always preserves old values in the same proportion in which he adds new value. Whether cotton rises from 1 sh. to 2 sh. or falls to 6 d., in the product of one hour he always preserves only half as much cotton value, however it may vary, as in the product of two hours. Further, if the productivity of his own labour changes, if it rises or falls, he will, for example, spin more or less cotton in one labour-hour than before, and correspondingly preserve more or less cotton value in the product of one labour-hour. With all this, in\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"169\", \"facs\": \"#f0188\"} --> two labour-hours he will preserve twice as much value as in one labour-hour.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"6.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0017","text":"Value, apart from its merely symbolic representation in the sign of value, exists only in a use-value, a thing. (Man himself, considered as mere existence of labour-power, is a natural object, a thing, albeit a living, self-conscious thing, and labour itself is the thingly manifestation of this power.) If, therefore, the use-value is lost, exchange-value is lost as well. The means of production do not lose their exchange-value together with their use-value, because through the labour process they in fact lose the original shape of their use-value only in order to acquire in the product the shape of another use-value. But however important it is for exchange-value to exist in some use-value, it is equally immaterial to it in which one it exists, as the metamorphosis of the commodity shows.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"6.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0018","text":"It follows from this that, in the labour process, value passes from the means of production to the product only insofar as the means of production loses its exchange-value along with its independent use-value. It gives up to the product only the value that it loses as means of production. But the objective factors of the labour process behave differently in this respect.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"6.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0019","text":"The coal with which the machine is heated disappears without trace, as does the tallow with which the axle of the wheel is lubricated, etc. Colours and other auxiliary materials disappear, but show themselves in the properties of the product. The raw material forms the substance of the product, but has changed its form. Raw material and auxiliary materials thus lose the independent shape in which they entered the labour process as use-values. It is otherwise with the means of labour proper. An instrument, a machine, a factory building, a vessel, etc., serve in the labour process only so long as they retain their original shape and re-enter the labour process tomorrow in exactly the same form as yesterday. As they retain their independent shape in relation to the product during their life, the labour process, so also after their death.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"6.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0020","text":"The corpses of machines, tools, work buildings, etc., still exist independently, separate from the products which they helped to form. Now let us consider the entire period during which such a means of labour serves, from the day of its entry into the workshop until the day of its banishment to the lumber room; then, during this period, its use-value has been completely consumed by labour, and its exchange-value has therefore passed completely to the product. If, for example, a spinning machine has worn out in 10 years, then during the ten-year labour process its total value has passed to the ten-year product. The life period of a means of labour thus embraces a greater or lesser number of labour processes repeatedly renewed with it.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"170\", \"facs\": \"#f0189\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"6.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0021","text":"And the means of labour fares like man. Every man dies off daily by 24 hours. But one cannot tell exactly from any man how many days he has already died. This does not, however, prevent life-insurance companies from drawing very sure, and what is much more, very profitable conclusions from the average life of men. So it is with the means of labour. Experience tells us how long a means of labour, e.g. a machine of a certain kind, lasts on average. Suppose that its use-value in the labour process lasts only 6 days. Then on average it loses ⅙ of its use-value every working day and therefore gives up ⅙ of its exchange-value to the daily product. In this way the wear of all means of labour is calculated, thus, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"6.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0022","text":"their daily loss of use-value, and the corresponding daily surrender of exchange-value to the product.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"6.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0023","text":"Here it strikingly appears that a means of production never gives up more value to the product than it itself loses in the labour process through the destruction of its own use-value. If it had no exchange-value to lose, i.e. if it were not itself a product of human labour, it would give up no exchange-value to the product. It would serve as a former of use-value without serving as a former of exchange-value. This is therefore the case with all means of production present by nature, without human intervention: with earth, wind, water, the iron in the ore vein, the wood of the primeval forest, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"6.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0024","text":"Another interesting phenomenon confronts us here. Let a\n machine be worth, for example, £1000 and wear out in 1000 days.\n In this case \\frac{1}{1000} of the value of the machine passes daily from the machine\n itself to its daily product, but, although with diminishing life-force,\n the machine always acts in its entirety in the labour process. It thus\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"171\", \"facs\": \"#f0190\"} --> appears here that one factor of the labour process, a means of pro-\n duction, enters the labour process wholly, but enters the process of\n valorisation only piece-\n meal.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"6.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0025","text":"The difference\n between the labour process and the process of valorisation is reflected here in the\nobjective factors, in that the same means of production\n counts as an element of the labour process wholly and as an element of\nvalue-formation only piecemeal in the same process of production\n [Note: This does not concern here repair work on the means of labour,\n machines, buildings, etc. A machine which is being repaired does not\n function as a means of labour, but as material of labour. Work is\n not done with it, but it is itself worked upon in order to mend its use-\n value. For our purpose, such repair work may always be conceived as included\n in the labour required for the production of the means of labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"6.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0026","text":"The text concerns wear and tear which no doctor\n can cure and which gradually brings about death, that “kind of wear which\n cannot be repaired from time to time, and which, in the case of a knife, would\n ultimately reduce it to a state in which the cutler would say of it, it is not worth\n a new blade.” One has seen in the text that a machine, for example, enters\n wholly into each individual labour process, but only piecemeal into the simultaneous pro-\n cess of valorisation. Judge accordingly the following confusion of concepts:\n “Mr. Ricardo speaks of the portion of the labour of the engineer in making\n stocking machines” as, for example, contained in the value of a pair of stockings.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"6.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0027","text":"“Yet the total labour that produced each single pair of stockings …\n includes the whole labour of the engineer, not a portion; for one\n machine makes many pairs, and none of those pairs could have been done\n without any part of the machine.” (Observations on certain verbal\n disputes in Pol. Econ., particularly relating to Value, and\n to demand and supply. London 1821, p. 54.) The author, an un-\n commonly self-satisfied “wiseacre,” is right with his confusion and hence his po-\n lemic only insofar as neither Ricardo nor any other economist, before\n or after him, has exactly distinguished the two aspects of labour,\n and still less analysed their different effect upon value-formation.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"6.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0028","text":"Conversely, however, a means of production can enter wholly into the pro-\n cess of valorisation, although only piecemeal into the labour process.\n Assume that, in the spinning of cotton, 15 pounds out of every 115 pounds\n fall away daily, forming no yarn but only devil’s dust. Nevertheless,\n if this waste of 15% is normal, inseparable from the average processing\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"172\", \"facs\": \"#f0191\"} --> of cotton, the value of the 15 lbs. of cotton, which form no element of the yarn,\n enters wholly just as much into its value as does\n the value of the 100 lbs. which form its substance. The use-value\n of 15 lbs. of cotton must go to dust in order to make 100 lbs. of yarn.\n The destruction of this cotton is therefore a condition of production\n of the yarn. For that very reason it yields up its exchange-value to the yarn.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"6.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0029","text":"This applies to all excrements of the labour process,\n at least to the extent that these excrements do not again form new means of pro-\n duction and hence new independent use-values. Thus one sees\n in the great machine factories of Manchester mountains of iron-\n waste, shaved off like plane-shavings by cyclopean machines,\n travelling in large wagons from the factory to the iron foundry in the evening,\n in order to return the following day from the iron foundry to\n the factory as massive iron again.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"6.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0030","text":"Only insofar as means of production lose exchange-\n value during the labour process in the form of their old use-values do they trans-\n fer exchange-value to the new form of the product. But the maxi-\n mum loss of value which they can suffer in the labour process\n is limited by their original magni-\n tude of value with which they enter the labour process, or by the labour-time\n required for their own production. Means of production can therefore never\n add more value to the product than\n they possess independently of the labour process\n which they serve. However useful a material of labour, a machine, a means of pro-\n duction may be, if it costs £150, say 500 working days, it never adds more\n than £150 to the total product whose formation it serves.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"6.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0031","text":"Its value is determined not by the labour pro-\n cess into which it enters as a means of production, but by the labour-\n process from which it emerges as a product. In the labour process\n it serves only as use-value, as a thing with useful\n properties, and would therefore yield no exchange-value to the product\n if it had not possessed exchange-value before its entry into the\n process [Note: One therefore understands the absurdity of the vapid J. B. Say, who\n derives surplus-value (interest, profit, rent) from the “services productives”] .","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"6.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0032","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"173\", \"facs\": \"#f0192\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"6.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0033","text":"As productive labour transforms means of production into elements of formation\n of a new product, a transmigration of souls takes place with their exchange-\n value. It passes from the consumed body into the newly formed\n body. But this transmigration of souls occurs, as it were, behind\n the back of actual labour. The worker cannot add new labour,\n hence cannot create new value, without preserving old values,\n because he must always add labour in a determinate useful\n form, and he cannot add it in useful form without making\n products into means of production of a new product, and there-\n by transferring their value to the new product.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"6.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0034","text":"It is therefore a\nnatural gift of labour-power in action, of living labour,\nto preserve value while it adds value, a natural\n gift which costs the worker nothing, but brings the capitalist much,\n the preservation of the existing capital-value [Note: “Of all the instruments of the farmer’s trade, the labour of man …\n is that on which he is most to rely for the re-payment of his capital.] .\n [Note: which he wishes to derive from the “productive services”\n rendered by the means of production, land, instruments, leather, etc.\n through their use-values in the labour process. Mr Wilhelm\n Roscher, who does not readily refrain from putting pretty apologetic notions on paper,\n exclaims: “Very correctly observes J. B. Say, Traité,\n t. I. ch.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"6.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0035","text":"4: ‘the value produced by an oil-mill after deduction of all costs\n is nevertheless something new, essentially different from the labour by which the oil-mill\n itself was created.’” (l. c. p. 82 note.) Quite correct!\n The “oil” produced by the oil-mill is something very dif-\n ferent from the labour which the building of the mill costs. And by\n “value” Mr Roscher understands stuff such as “oil,” since “oil”\n has value, while petroleum is found “in nature,” though not relatively\n “very much,” to which his other remark probably alludes: “Exchange-values\n it (nature!)","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"6.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0036","text":"produces almost none at all.” Roscher’s nature\n fares with exchange-value as the foolish virgin does with the child, which was only “quite\n small.” The same “scholar” (“savant sérieux”) further remarks on the occasion mentioned above:\n “Ricardo’s school is also wont to subsume capital under the\n concept of labour as ‘saved-up labour.’ This is unskilful\n (!), because (!) the owner of capital (!) has after all (!) done more (!) than the\nmere (?!) production (??) and (??) preservation of the same (of the self-\n same?): namely (?!?) abstinence from his own enjoyment, for which he, for example (!!!),\n demands interest.” (l. c.) How “skilful”!","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"6.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0037","text":"this “anatomico-physio-\n logical method” of political economy, which indeed develops “value” from mere “de-\n sire.”]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"174\", \"facs\": \"#f0193\"} --> So long as business is brisk, the capitalist is too deeply absorbed in money-\n making to see this gratuitous gift of labour. Violent\n interruptions of the labour process, crises, make him feel-\n ingly aware of it [Note: In The Times of 26 November 1862, a manufacturer whose\n spinning mill employs 800 workers and consumes weekly on average 150 bales\n of East Indian or approximately 130 bales of American cotton, laments to the\n public the annual costs of the stoppage of his factory. He estimates\n them at £6000.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"6.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0038","text":"Among these costs are many items which\n do not concern us here, such as ground rent, taxes, insurance premiums, salaries\n for annually engaged workers, managers, bookkeepers, engineers, etc. But then\n he calculates £150 for coal to warm the factory from time to time\n and occasionally set the steam-engine going, in addition wages for\n workers who, through occasional work, keep the machinery “liquid.” Finally,\n £1200 for deterioration of the machinery, since “the weather and the\n natural principle of decay do not suspend their operations because the steam-\n engine ceases to revolve.” He expressly observes that this sum of £1200\n is estimated so low because the machinery is already in a very worn-out\n condition.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"6.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0039","text":"What is consumed in the labour process by the means of production in general\n is their use-value, the consumption of which by labour forms pro-\n ducts. Their exchange-value is in fact not consumed [Note: “Productive Consumption: where the consumption of a commo-\n dity is a part of the process of production … In these instances\nthere is no consumption of value.” S. P. Newman l. c. p. 296.] ,\n and therefore cannot be reproduced either. It is preserved,\n but not because an operation takes place upon it itself in the labour process,\n but because the use-value in which it originally exists indeed\n disappears, but disappears only in another use-value.\n The exchange-value of the means of production therefore reappears\n in the value of the product, but, strictly speaking, it is not repro-\n duced.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"6.39","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0040","text":"What is produced is the new use-value in which the\n old exchange-value reappears [Note: In a North American compendium, which perhaps has had 20 editions-] .","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"6.40","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0041","text":"[Note: The other two — the working stock of the cattle, and the … carts, ploughs,\n spades, and so forth, without a given portion of the first, are nothing at all.”\n (Edmund Burke: Thoughts and Details on Scarcity, origi-\n nally presented to the R. Hon. W. Pitt in the Month of No-\n vember 1795, edit. London 1800“, p. 10.]","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"6.41","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0042","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"175\", \"facs\": \"#f0194\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"6.42","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0043","text":"It is otherwise with the subjective factor of the labour process,\n labour-power in action. While labour, through its purposive\n form, transfers and preserves the value of the means of production in the product,\n every moment of its movement forms additional\n value, new value. Suppose that the process of production breaks off\n at the point where the worker has produced an equivalent for the value\n of his own labour-power, where, through six hours’ labour, he has, for example,\n added a value of 3 sh. This value forms the\nexcess of the value of the product over its component parts\n owed to the value of the means of production. It is the only\n original value which arose within this process,\n the only portion of value of the product which is produced by\n the process itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"6.43","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0044","text":"To be sure, it merely replaces the money\n advanced by the capitalist in the purchase of labour-power, and expended by the worker himself on means of subsistence.\n With reference to the expended 3 sh., the new value of 3 sh. appears\n only as reproduction. But it is really reproduced,\n not merely apparently, as is the value of the means of pro-\n duction. The replacement of one value by the other\n is here mediated by new creation of value.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"6.44","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0045","text":"However, we already know that the labour process continues beyond the point\n at which a mere equivalent for the value of\n [Note: has lived, one reads: “It matters not in what form capital reappears.” After a\n verbose enumeration of all possible ingredients of production whose value\n reappears in the product, it finally says: “The various kinds of food,\n clothing, and shelter, necessary for the existence and comfort of the human being,\n are also changed. They are consumed from time to time, and their value re-\n appears, in that new vigour imparted to his body and mind, forming fresh\n capital, to be employed again in the work of production.” (F. Weyland l. c.\n p. 31, 32.) Apart from all the other absurdities, it is, for example, not the\n price of bread that reappears in renewed strength, but its\n blood-forming substances.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"6.45","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0046","text":"What, on the other hand, reappears as the value of force\n is not the means of subsistence, but their value. The same means of subsistence,\n if they cost only half as much, produce exactly as much muscle, bone,\n etc., in short, the same force, but not force of the same value. This transmutation\n of “value” into “force,” and the entire Pharisaical indeterminacy, conceal\n the admittedly futile attempt to extract surplus-value from the mere reappearance\n of advanced values.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"176\", \"facs\": \"#f0195\"} --> labour-power is reproduced and added to the object of labour. Instead\n of the 6 hours which sufficed for this, the process lasts, for example, 12 hours.\n Through the exercise of labour-power, therefore, not only is its own\n value reproduced, but a surplus-value is produced.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"6.46","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0047","text":"This\nsurplus-value forms the excess of the value of the product\n over the value of the product-forming elements consumed, i.e. of the means\n of production and labour-power.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"6.47","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0048","text":"In presenting the different roles which the differ-\n ent factors of the labour-process play in the formation of the value of the\n product, we have in fact characterised the functions of the dif-\n ferent components of capital in its own process of self-\n expansion. The excess of the total value of the product over the sum of the values of\n its elements of formation is the excess of the valorised capital over the orig-\n inally advanced capital-value. Means of produc-\n tion on the one hand, labour-power on the other, are only the differ-\n ent forms of existence which the original capital-value assumed in casting off its\n money-form and transforming itself into the factors of the labour-\n process.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"6.48","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0049","text":"The part of capital, therefore, which is transformed into means of production,\n i.e. into raw material, auxiliary materials, and instruments of labour, does not alter\n its magnitude of value in the process of production. I\n therefore call it the constant part of capital, or, more briefly: con-\n stant capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"6.49","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0050","text":"The part of capital transformed into labour-power, on the other hand, alters\n its value in the process of production. It reproduces its\n own equivalent and an excess over it, surplus-value, which may\n itself vary, being greater or smaller. From a constant\n magnitude this part of capital is continually transformed into a\n variable one. I therefore call it the variable part of capital, or, more briefly:\n variable capital. The same components of capital\n which, from the standpoint of the labour-process, are distinguished as objective\n and subjective factors, as means of production and labour-power, are distin-\n guished, from the standpoint of the process of valorisation,\n as constant capital and variable capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"6.50","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0051","text":"The concept of constant capital in no way excludes a revolution in value\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"177\", \"facs\": \"#f0196\"} --> of its components. Suppose that a pound of\n cotton costs 6 d. today and rises tomorrow, as a consequence of a failure\n of the cotton harvest, to 1 sh. The old cotton which continues to be pro-\n cessed was bought at a value of 6 d., but now adds a portion of value of\n 1 sh. to the product. And the cotton already spun, perhaps already circulating\n on the market as yarn, likewise adds to the product twice its original value. Yet\n it is evident that these changes in value are independent of the valor-\n isation of the cotton in the spinning-process itself. If the\n old cotton had not yet entered the labour-process at all, it\n could now be resold for 1 sh. instead of 6 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"6.51","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0052","text":"Con-\n versely: the fewer labour-processes it has yet passed through,\n the more certain is this result. It is therefore a law of speculation, in\n such revolutions in value, to speculate on raw material in its least processed\n form, thus rather on yarn than on woven fabric and rather on\n cotton itself than on yarn. The change in value here ori-\n ginates in the process which produces cotton, not in the pro-\n cess in which it functions as a means of production and therefore as constant capi-\n tal. The value of a commodity is indeed determined by the\n quantity of labour contained in it, but this quantity is socially\n determined. If the labour-time socially required for its production has\n changed—and the same quantity of cot-\n ton, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"6.52","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0053","text":"represents a greater quantity of\n labour in unfavourable harvests than in favourable ones—then there is a repercussion upon the\n old commodity, which always counts only as an individual specimen of its genus\n [Note: “Toutes les productions d’un même genre ne forment proprement qu’une\n masse, dont le prix se détermine en général et sans égard aux circonstances\n particulières.” (Le Trosne l. c. p. 893.)] , whose value is always measured by socially necessary,\n thus also by labour necessary under present social con-\n ditions.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"6.53","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0054","text":"Like the value of raw material, the value of instruments of labour already serving in the pro-\n cess of production, machinery, etc., may change, and hence also the portion of value which they yield to\n the product. If, for example, as a consequence of a new invention, machinery of the same kind is re-\n produced with a diminished expenditure of labour, the old machi-\n [Running matter: I. 12]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"178\", \"facs\": \"#f0197\"} --> nery is depreciated more or less and therefore transfers proportionately less\n value to the product. But here too the change in value originates\noutside the process of production in which the machine functions as a means of produc-\n tion. In this process it never yields more value than it possesses independently\n of this process.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"6.54","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0055","text":"Just as a change in the value of the means of production, even if it acts retro-\n actively after their entry into the process has already taken place, does not alter\n their character as constant capital, so likewise a change in the ratio of constant and variab-\n le capital does not affect their conceptual distinction. The technological con-\n ditions of the labour-process may, for example, be so transformed that,\n where formerly 10 workers with 10 tools of slight value processed a rela-\n tively small mass of raw material, now 1 work-\n er with an expensive machine processes a hundredfold quantity of raw material.\n In this case constant capital, i.e. the mass of value\n of the means of production employed, would have grown greatly, and the variable\n part of capital, that advanced in labour-power, would have fallen greatly.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"6.55","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0056","text":"This change, however, alters only the relation of magnitude between\n constant and variable capital, or the proportion in which total\n capital is divided into constant and variable components; it does\n not, however, affect the distinction between constant and variable.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"6.56","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2CKU:P0056"]}],"source_digest":"ce0ca9ea7826ee93c00433faeedf8297b680d14eb402272047e0446b82398eba","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD","title":"3) Die Rate des Mehrwerths.","order":7,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_3DRD","text":"Der Mehrwerth, den das vorgeschossene Kapital C im Produk-\n tionsprozess erzeugt hat, oder die Verwerthung des vorgeschossenen\nKapitalwerths C stellt sich zunächst dar als Ueberschuss des\n Werths des Produkts über die Werthsumme seiner Pro-\n duktionselemente.\n\n\n\n\nDas Kapital C zerfällt in zwei Theile, eine Geldsumme c, die für\n Produktionsmittel, und eine andere Geldsumme v, die für Arbeitskraft\n verausgabt wird; c stellt den in konstantes, v den in vari-\n ables Kapital verwandelten Werththeil vor. Ursprünglich ist also\n C = c + v, z. B. das vorgeschossene Kapital von 500 Pfd. St. =\n Pfd. St. +  Pfd. St. Am Ende des Prozesses kommt ein Produkt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"179\", \"facs\": \"#f0198\"} --> heraus, dessen Werth =  + m, wo m der Mehrwerth, z. B.\n. Das ursprüngliche Kapital C hat sich in C'ver-\n wandelt, aus 500 Pfd. St. in 590 Pfd. St. Die Differenz zwischen beiden ist\n = m, einem Mehrwerth von 90. Da der Werth der Produktions-\n elemente gleich dem Werth des vorgeschossenen Kapitals,\n so ist es in der That eine Tautologie, dass der Ueberschuss des Produkten-\n werths über den Werth seiner Produktionselemente gleich der Verwerthung\n des vorgeschossenen Kapitals oder gleich dem produzirten Mehrwerth.\n\n\n\n\nIndess erfordert diese Tautologie eine nähere Bestimmung. Der mit\n dem Produktenwerth verglichene Werth seiner Produktionselemente ist der\n Werth der in seiner Bildung aufgezehrten Produktionselemente. Nun\n haben wir aber gesehn, dass ein Theil des angewandten konstan-\n ten Kapitals, der aus Arbeitsmitteln bestehende, seinen Werth nur\n bruchweis an das Produkt abgiebt, während eine andere Portion desselben\n in seiner alten Existenzform fortdauert. Da der letztere Theil keine\n Rolle im Werthbildungsprozess spielt, abstrahiren wir hier\n ganz und gar von ihm. Sein Hineinziehn in die Rechnung würde nichts\n ändern. Nimm an, c = 410 l. bestehe aus Rohmaterial zu 312 l.,\n Hilfsstoffen zu 44 l. und im Prozess verschleissender Maschinerie\n von 54 l., der Werth der wirklich angewandten Maschinerie betrage\n aber 1054 l. Als vorgeschossen zur Erzeugung des Produkten-\n werths berechnen wir nur den Werth von 54 l., den die Maschinerie durch\n ihre Funktion verliert und daher an das Produkt abgiebt. Rechneten\n wir die 1000 Pfd. St. mit, die in ihrer alten Form fortexistiren als Dampf-\n maschine u. s. w., so müssten wir sie auf beiden Seiten mitrechnen, auf\n Seite des vorgeschossenen Werths und auf Seite des Produktenwerths [Note: „If we reckon the value of the fixed capital employed as a part of the\n advances, we must reckon the remaining value of such capital at the end of the\n year as a part of the annual returns.“ (Malthus: „Princ. of Pol. Econ.\n 2nd. ed London 1836“, p. 269.)] ,\n und erhielten so resp. 1500 Pfd. St. und 1590 Pfd. St. Die Differenz\n oder der Mehrwerth wäre nach wie vor 90 Pfd. St. Unter dem zur\nWerthproduktion vorgeschossenen konstanten Kapital verstehn wir\n [Running matter: 12*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"180\", \"facs\": \"#f0199\"} --> daher, wo das Gegentheil nicht aus dem Zusammenhang erhellt, stets nur\n den Werth der in der Produktion verzehrten Produktionsmittel.\n\n\n\n\nDiess vorausgesetzt, kehren wir zurück zur Formel C = c + v,\n die sich in C' =  + m und eben dadurch C in C' verwandelt.\n Man weiss, dass der Werth des konstanten Kapitals im Produkt nur\nwieder erscheint. Das im Prozess wirklich neu erzeugte Werthpro-\n dukt ist also verschieden von dem aus dem Prozess erhaltenen Produkten-\n werth, daher nicht, wie es auf den ersten Blick scheint,  + m oder\n, sondern v + m oder , nicht 590 l.,\n sondern 180 l. Wäre c, das konstante Kapital, = 0, in anderen Wor-\n ten, gäbe es Industriezweige, worin der Kapitalist keine produzirten Pro-\n duktionsmittel, weder Rohmaterial, noch Hilfsstoffe, noch Arbeitsinstru-\n mente, sondern nur von Natur vorhandne Stoffe und Arbeitskraft an-\n zuwenden hätte, so wäre kein konstanter Werththeil auf das Produkt zu\n übertragen. Dieses Element des Produktenwerths, in unsrem Beispiel\n 410 Pfd. St., fiele fort, aber das Werthprodukt von 180 Pfd. St., welches\n 90 Pfd. St. Mehrwerth enthält, bliebe ganz ebenso gross als ob c die grösste\n Werthsumme darstellte. Wir hätten C =  = v, und C', das ver-\n werthete Kapital, = v + m, C' — C nach wie vor = m. Wäre um-\n gekehrt m = o, in anderen Worten, hätte die Arbeitskraft, deren Werth\n im variablen Kapital vorgeschossen wird, nur ein Aequivalent produzirt, so\n C = c + v und C' (der Produktenwerth) =  + o, daher C = C'.\n Das vorgeschossene Kapital hätte sich nicht verwerthet.\n\n\n\n\nWir wissen in der That bereits, dass der Mehrwerth blos Folge\n der Werthveränderung ist, die mit v, dem in Arbeitskraft umgesetz-\n ten Kapitaltheil vorgeht, dass also v + m = v + ∆ v (v plus In-\n crement von v) ist. Aber die wirkliche Werthveränderung\n und das Verhältniss, worin sich der Werth ändert, werden dadurch\n verdunkelt, dass in Folge des Wachsens seines variirenden Be-\n standtheils auch das vorgeschossene Gesammtkapital\n wächst. Es war 500 und es ist am Ende des Prozesses 590. Die\n reine Analyse des Prozesses erheischt also von dem Theil des Produkten-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"181\", \"facs\": \"#f0200\"} --> werths, worin nur konstanter Kapitalwerth wieder erscheint, ganz zu ab-\n strahiren, also das konstante Kapital c = o zu setzen, und damit ein Ge-\n setz der Mathematik anzuwenden, wo sie mit variablen und konstanten\n Grössen operirt, und die konstante Grösse nur durch Addition oder Sub-\n traktion mit der variablen verbunden ist [Note: „Constant quantities connected to variable by the operations of Addition\n or Substraction disappear by the process of differentiation.“ (J. Hind: „Diffe-\n rential Calculus. Cambridge 1831“ p. 126.) In der That, die Grössen-\nveränderung einer konstanten Grösse ist Nichts. Daher das Gesetz des\n Differentialkalkuls: Differential einer konstanten Grösse = 0.] .\n\n\n\n\nEine andre Schwierigkeit entspringt aus der ursprünglichen\n Form des variablen Kapitals. So im obigen Beispiel ist C' = 410 l.\n konstantes Kapital + 90 l. variables Kapital + 90 l. Mehrwerth. Neun-\n zig Pfd. St. sind aber eine gegebne, also konstante Grösse und es scheint\n daher ungereimt sie als variable Grösse zu behandeln. Aber  oder 90 l.\n variables Kapital ist hier in der That nur ein Symbol für den Pro-\n zess, den dieser Werth durchläuft. Der im Ankauf der Ar-\n beitskraft vorgeschossene Kapitaltheil ist bestimmtes Quantum\n vergegenständlichter Arbeit, also konstante Werthgrösse,\n wie der Werth der gekauften Arbeitskraft. Im Produktionsprozess selbst\n aber tritt an die Stelle der vorgeschossenen 90 Pfd. St. die sich bethäti-\n gende Arbeitskraft, an die Stelle todter lebendige Arbeit, an die Stelle\n einer ruhenden eine fliessende Grösse, an die Stelle einer konstanten eine\n variable. Das Resultat ist die Reproduktion von v plus Increment von v.\n Vom Standpunkt der kapitalistischen Produktion ist dieser ganze Verlauf\nSelbstbewegung des in Arbeitskraft umgesetzten, ursprünglich kon-\n stanten Werths. Ihm wird der Prozess und sein Resultat zu gut ge-\n schrieben. Erscheint die Formel 90 l. variables Kapital oder sich\n verwerthender Werth daher widerspruchsvoll, so drückt sie nur einen der\n kapitalistischen Produktion immanenten Widerspruch aus.\n\n\n\n\nDie Gleichsetzung des konstanten Kapitals mit o befremdet auf den\n ersten Blick. Indess vollzieht man sie beständig im Alltagsleben. Will\n Jemand z. B. Englands Gewinn an der Baumwollindustrie berechnen, so\n zieht er vor allem den an die Vereinigten Staaten, Indien, Aegypten u. s. w.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"182\", \"facs\": \"#f0201\"} --> gezahlten Baumwoll preis ab; d. h. er setzt im Produktenwerth nur wie-\n dererscheinenden Kapitalwerth = o.\n\n\n\n\nAllerdings hat das Verhältniss des Mehrwerths, nicht nur zum Kapi-\n taltheil, woraus er unmittelbar entspringt und dessen Werthverände-\n rung er darstellt, sondern auch zum vorgeschossenen Gesammtkapi-\n tal seine grosse ökonomische Bedeutung. Wir behandeln diess Verhält-\n niss daher ausführlich im dritten Buch. Um einen Theil des Kapitals\n durch seinen Umsatz in Arbeitskraft zu verwerthen, muss ein andrer Theil\n des Kapitals in Produktionsmittel verwandelt werden. Damit das variable\n Kapital funktionire, muss konstantes Kapital in entsprechenden Propor-\n tionen, je nach dem bestimmten technologischen Charakter des Arbeits-\n prozesses, vorgeschossen werden. Der Umstand jedoch, dass man\n zu einem chemischen Prozess Retorten und andre Gefässe braucht, verhindert\n nicht bei der Analyse von der Retorte selbst zu abstrahiren. Sofern Werth-\n schöpfung und Werthveränderung für sich selbst, d. h. rein betrachtet wer-\n den, liefern die Produktionsmittel, diese stofflichen Gestalten des konstanten\n Kapitals, nur den Stoff, worin sich die flüssige, werthbildende Kraft fixiren\n kann. Die Natur dieses Stoffes ist daher auch gleichgültig, ob Baumwolle\n oder Eisen. Auch der Werth dieses Stoffes ist gleichgültig. Er muss nur\n in hinreichender Masse vorhanden sein, um das während des Produktions-\n prozesses zu verausgabende Arbeitsquantum einsaugen zu können. Diese\n Masse gegeben, mag ihr Werth steigen, oder fallen, oder sie mag werthlos\n sein, wie Erde und Meer, der Prozess der Werthschöpfung und Werthver-\n änderung wird nicht davon berührt.\n\n\n\n\nWir setzen also zunächst den konstanten Kapitaltheil gleich Null.\n Das vorgeschossene Kapital reduzirt sich daher von c + v auf v, und der\n Produktenwerth  + m auf das Werthprodukt . Gegeben\n das Werthprodukt = 180 Pfd. St., worin sich die während der ganzen\n Dauer des Produktionsprozesses fliessende Arbeit darstellt, so haben wir\n den Werth des variablen Kapitals = 90 Pfd. St. abzuziehn, um den\n Mehrwerth = 90 Pfd. St. zu erhalten. Die Zahl 90 Pfd. St. = m drückt\n hier die absolute Grösse des produzirten Mehrwerths aus. Seine\nproportionelle Grösse aber, also das Verhältniss, worin das va-\n riable Kapital sich verwerthet hat, ist offenbar bestimmt durch das Ver-\n hältniss des Mehrwerths zum variablen Kapital, oder ist\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"183\", \"facs\": \"#f0202\"} --> ausgedrückt in \\frac{m}{v}. Im obigen Beispiel also in \\frac{90}{90} = 100 %. Diese\n verhältnissmässige Verwerthung des variablen Kapitals, oder die verhält-\n nissmässige Grösse des Mehrwerths, nenne ich Rate des Mehr-\n werths [Note: In derselben Weise, wie der Engländer „rate of profit“, „rate of inte-\n rest“ u. s. w. braucht. Man wird aus Buch III sehen, dass die Profitrate leicht\n zu begreifen, sobald man die Gesetze des Mehrwerths kennt. Auf dem um-\n gekehrten Weg begreift man ni l’un, ni l’autre.] .\n\n\n\n\nWir haben gesehn, dass der Arbeiter während eines Abschnitts\n des Arbeitsprozesses nur den Werth seiner Arbeitskraft produ-\n zirt, d. h. den Werth seiner nothwendigen Lebensmittel. Da er in\n einem auf gesellschaftlicher Theilung der Arbeit beruhenden Zustand pro-\n duzirt, produzirt er seine Lebensmittel nicht direkt, sondern, in Form einer\n besondern Waare, des Garns z. B., einen Werth gleich dem Werth\n seiner Lebensmittel, oder dem Geld, womit er sie kauft. Der\nTheil seines Arbeitstags, den er hierzu verbraucht, ist grösser\n oder kleiner, je nach dem Werth seiner durchschnittlichen täglichen\n Lebensmittel, also der zu ihrer Produktion erheischten durchschnittli-\n chen täglichen Arbeitszeit. Stellt die Werthgrösse dieser täglichen Lebens-\n mittel im Durchschnitt 6 vergegenständlichte Arbeitsstunden dar, so muss\n der Arbeiter im Durchschnitt täglich 6 Stunden arbeiten, um sie zu produ-\n ziren. Arbeitete er nicht für den Kapitalisten, sondern als unabhängiger\n Produzent, so müsste er, unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen, nach\n wie vor im Durchschnitt denselben aliquoten Theil des Tags\n arbeiten, um den Werth seiner Arbeitskraft zu produziren, und\n dadurch die zu seiner eignen Erhaltung oder beständigen Reproduktion\n nöthigen Lebensmittel zu gewinnen. Da er aber in dem Theil des Ar-\n beitstags, worin er den Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft, sage 3 sh., produ-\n zirt, nur ein Aequivalent für ihren vom Kapitalisten bereits gezahlten\n Werth produzirt, also durch den neu geschaffnen Werth nur den vorge-\n schossenen variablen Kapitalwerth ersetzt, erscheint diese Pro-\n duktion von Werth als blosse Reproduktion. Den Theil des Arbeits-\n tags also, worin diese Reproduktion vorgeht, nenne ich nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit, die während derselben verausgabte Arbeit nothwen_\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"184\", \"facs\": \"#f0203\"} --> dige Arbeit [Note: Wir haben bisher in dieser Schrift das Wort „nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit“ angewandt für die zur Produktion einer Waare überhaupt gesellschaftlich\n nothwendige Arbeitszeit. Wir brauchen es von jetzt ab auch für die zur Produk-\n tion der spezifischen Waare Arbeitskraft nothwendige Arbeitszeit. Der\n Gebrauch derselben termini technici in verschiednem Sinn ist misslich, aber in\n keiner Wissenschaft ganz zu vermeiden. Man vergleiche z. B. die höheren und\n niederen Theile der Mathematik.] . Nothwendig für den Arbeiter, weil unabhängig von\n der gesellschaftlichen Form seiner Arbeit. Nothwendig für das Kapital\n und seine Welt, weil das beständige Dasein des Arbeiters ihre Basis.\n\n\n\n\nDie zweite Periode des Arbeitsprozesses, die der Arbeiter über die\n Grenzen der nothwendigen Arbeit hinausschanzt, kostet ihm zwar Arbeit,\n Verausgabung von Arbeitskraft, bildet aber keinen Werth für ihn. Sie\n bildet Mehrwerth, der den Kapitalisten mit allem Reiz einer Schöpfung\n aus Nichts anlacht. Diesen Theil des Arbeitstags nenne ich Surplus-\n arbeitszeit, und die in ihr verausgabte Arbeit: Mehrarbeit (surplus\n labour). So entscheidend es für die Erkenntniss des Werths überhaupt,\n ihn als blosse Gerinnung von Arbeitszeit, als bloss vergegenständ-\n lichte Arbeit, so entscheidend für die Erkenntniss des Mehrwerths,\n ihn als blosse Gerinnung von Surplusarbeitszeit, als bloss\nvergegenständlichte Mehrarbeit zu begreifen. Nur die Form,\n worin diese Mehrarbeit dem unmittelbaren Produzenten, dem Arbeiter, ab-\n gepresst wird, unterscheidet die ökonomischen Gesellschaftsformationen,\n z. B. die Gesellschaft der Sklaverei von der der Lohnarbeit [Note: Mit wahrhaft Gottsched’scher Genialität entdeckt Herr Wilhelm\n Thucydides Roscher, dass wenn die Bildung von Mehrwerth oder\nMehrprodukt, und die damit verbundene Accumulation, heurigen Tags der\n „Sparsamkeit“ des Kapitalisten geschuldet, der dafür „z. B. Zins verlangt“,\n dagegen „auf den niedrigsten Kulturstufen … die Schwächeren von den Stär-\n keren zur Sparsamkeit gezwungen werden.“ (l. c. p. 78.) Zur Ersparung von\n Arbeit? oder nicht vorhandner überschüssiger Produkte? Neben\n wirklicher Ignoranz ist es apologetische Scheu vor gewissenhafter Analyse des\n Werths und Mehrwerths, und etwa verfänglich-polizeiwidrigem Resultat, die einen\n Roscher und Cons. zwingt, die mehr oder minder plausiblen Rechtfertigungs-\n gründe des Kapitalisten für seine Aneignung vorhandner Mehrwerthe\n zum Entstehungsgrund des Mehrwerths selbst zu stempeln.] .\n\n\n\n\nDa der Werth des variablen Kapitals = Werth der von ihm gekauf-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"185\", \"facs\": \"#f0204\"} --> ten Arbeitskraft, da der Werth dieser Arbeitskraft den nothwendigen\n Theil des Arbeitstags bestimmt, der Mehrwerth seinerseits aber bestimmt\n ist durch den überschüssigen Theil des Arbeitstags, so folgt: Der Mehr-\n werth verhält sich zum variablen Kapital, wie die Mehr-\n arbeit zur nothwendigen, oder die Rate des Mehrwerths\n\\frac{m}{v} = \\frac{Mehrarbeit}{Nothwendige Arbeit}. Beide Proportionen stellen dasselbe Verhält-\n niss in verschiedner Form dar, das einemal in der Form vergegenständ-\n lichter, das andremal in der Form flüssiger Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nDie Rate des Mehrwerths ist daher der exakte Ausdruck für\n den Exploitationsgrad der Arbeitskraft durch das Ka-\n pital oder des Arbeiters durch den Kapitalisten.\n\n\n\n\nNach unsrer Annahme war der Werth des Produkts = \n + , das vorgeschossene Kapital = 500 l. Da der Mehrwerth 90 und\n das vorgeschossene Kapital 500, würde man nach der gewöhnlichen Art\n der Berechnung herausbekommen, dass die Rate des Mehrwerths (die man\n mit der Profitrate verwechselt) = 18 %, eine Verhältnisszahl, deren\n Niedrigkeit Herrn Carey und andre Harmoniker rühren möchte. In der\n That aber ist die Rate des Mehrwerths nicht = \\frac{m}{C} oder \\frac{m}{c + v}, sondern = \\frac{m}{v},\n also nicht \\frac{90}{500}, sondern \\frac{90}{90} = 100 %, mehr als das Fünffache des\nscheinbaren Exploitationsgrads. Obgleich wir nun im gegebnen Fall\n die absolute Grösse des Arbeitstags nicht kennen, auch nicht\n die Periode des Arbeitsprozesses (Tag, Woche u. s. w.), endlich nicht\n die Anzahl der Arbeiter, die das variable Kapital von 90 l. gleichzeitig in\n Bewegung setzt, zeigt uns die Rate des Mehrwerths \\frac{m}{v} durch ihre Konver-\n tibilität in \\frac{Mehrarbeit}{Nothwendige Arbeit} genau das Verhältniss der zwei Bestand-\n theile des Arbeitstags zu einander. Es ist 100 %. Also arbeitete der\n Arbeiter die eine Hälfte des Tags für sich und die andre für den Ka-\n pitalisten.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"186\", \"facs\": \"#f0205\"} -->\n\nDie Methode zur Berechnung der Rate des Mehrwerths ist also kurz-\n gefasst diese: Wir nehmen den ganzen Produktenwerth und setzen\n den darin nur wiedererscheinenden constanten Kapitalwerth\n gleich Null. Die übrigbleibende Werthsumme ist das einzige im Bildungs-\n prozess der Waare wirklich erzeugte Werthprodukt. Ist der Mehrwerth\n gegeben, so ziehn wir ihn von diesem Werthprodukt ab, um das variable\n Kapital zu finden. Umgekehrt, wenn letzteres gegeben und wir den\n Mehrwerth suchen. Sind beide gegeben, so ist nur noch die Schlussopera-\n tion zu verrichten, das Verhältniss des Mehrwerths zum variablen Kapital,\n\\frac{m}{v}, zu berechnen.\n\n\n\n\nSo einfach die Methode, scheint es doch passend, den Leser in die\n ihr zu Grunde liegende und ihm ungewohnte Anschauungsweise durch\n einige Beispiele einzuexerciren.\n\n\n\n\nZunächst ein Beispiel aus der Spinnindustrie. Die Data gehören\n dem Jahre 1860. Für unsren Zweck gleichgültige Umstände sind unter-\n drückt. Eine Fabrik konsumirte wöchentlich 11,500 lbs. Baumwolle,\n wovon 1500 Abfall. Zu 7 d. das Pfund Baumwolle, beträgt das Roh-\n material daher 336 Pfd. St. Sie setzte 10000 Spindeln in Bewegung, zu\n 1 Pfd. St. per Spindel = 10000 Pfd. St., wovon der jährliche Verschleiss,\n zu 12½ %, 1250 Pfd. St., für die Woche also 24 Pfd. St.; der wöchent-\n liche Verschleiss der Dampfmaschine 20 Pfd. St.; die wöchentliche Aus-\n gabe für Hilfsstoffe, Kohle, Oel u. s. w. 40 Pfd. St. Der wöchentliche\n Arbeitslohn betrug 70 Pfd. St. und der Verkaufspreis des lb. Garn 1⅒ sh.,\n also der 10000 lbs. Garn wöchentlich 550 Pfd. St. Der constante\n Werththeil des Kapitals beträgt also 420 Pfd. St. Wir setzen ihn = 0,\n da er in der wöchentlichen Werthbildung nicht mitspielt. Das wirkliche\nwöchentliche Werthprodukt, das übrig bleibt, also = 130 Pfd. St.\n Wir ziehn davon das an die Arbeiter gezahlte variable Kapital von\n 70 Pfd. St. ab, bleibt Mehrwerth von 60 Pfd. St. Die Rate des\n Mehrwerths, \\frac{m}{v}, \\frac{60}{70}, also ungefähr 86 %. Diese Prozentzahl drückt\n den Exploitationsgrad der Arbeitskraft oder den Verwerthungsgrad des\n variablen Kapitals aus. Nehmen wir an, dass 10 Stunden in jener Fabrik\n im täglichen Durchschnitt gearbeitet ward, so betrug die nothwendige\n Arbeit ungefähr 5\\frac{5}{13}, und die Mehrarbeit 4\\frac{8}{13} Stunden.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"187\", \"facs\": \"#f0206\"} -->\n\nJacob giebt für das Jahr 1815, bei Annahme eines Weizenpreises\n von 80 sh. per Quarter, und eines Durchschnittsertrags von 22 Bushels\n per acre, so dass der acre 11 Pfd. St. einbringt, folgende durch vorherige\n Kompensation verschiedner Posten sehr mangelhafte, aber für unsern\n Zweck genügende Rechnung.\n\n\n\n\nWerthproduktion per acre.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSamen (Weizen) 1 Pfd. St. 9 sh.\n\n\n\n\nDünger 2 Pfd. St. 10 sh.\n\n\n\n\nArbeitslohn 3 Pfd. St. 10 sh.\n\n\n\n\nSumma: 7 Pfd. St. 9 sh.\n\n\n\n\nZehnten, Rates, Taxes 1 Pfd. St. 1 sh.\n\n\n\n\nRente 1 Pfd. St. 8 sh.\n\n\n\n\nPächter’s Profit u. Zins 1 Pfd. St. 2 sh.\n\n\n\n\nSumma: 3 Pfd. St. 11 sh.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDer Mehrwerth, stets unter der Voraussetzung, dass Preis des\n Produkts = seinem Werth, wird hier unter die verschiedenen Rubriken\n Profit, Zins, Zehnten u. s. w. vertheilt. Diese Rubriken sind hier gleich-\n gültig. Wir addiren sie zusammen und erhalten einen Mehrwerth von\n 3 l. 11 sh. Die 3 l. 19 sh. für Samen und Dünger setzen wir\n als constanten Kapitaltheil gleich Null. Bleibt vorgeschossenes va-\n riables Kapital von 3 l. 10 sh., an dessen Stelle ein Aequivalent von\nproduzirt worden ist. Also beträgt\n\\frac{m}{v} = \\frac{3 l. 11 sh.}{3 l. 10 sh.} mehr als 100 %. Der Arbeiter verwendet mehr als\n die Hälfte seines Arbeitstags zur Produktion eines Mehrwerths, den ver-\n schiedne Personen auf verschiedne Vorwände hin unter sich vertheilen [Note: Die gegebnen Rechnungen gelten nur als Illustration. Es wird nämlich\n unterstellt, dass die Preise = den Werthen. Man wird in Buch III sehn, dass\n diese Gleichsetzung, selbst für die Durchschnittspreise, sich nicht in dieser\n einfachen Weise macht.] .\n\n\n\n\nDer Mehrwerth stellt sich dar in einem Mehrprodukt (sur-\n plusproduce).\n\n\n\n\nWir nahmen vorher an, dass der zwölfstündige Arbeitstag des Spin-\n ners aus 6 Stunden nothwendiger Arbeit und 6 Stunden Mehr-\n arbeit besteht, 20 lbs. Baumwolle in 20 lbs. Garn verwandelt, und ihnen\n einen Werth von 6 sh. zusetzt, dass ferner 1 lb. Baumwolle 1 sh. und die\n während des ganzen Prozesses verzehrten Arbeitsmittel 4 sh. kosten, also\n Werth des Gesammtprodukts 30 sh. und der eines lb. Garn 1 sh. 6 d.\n\n\n\n\nJedes Pfund Garn stellt denselben Gebrauchswerth dar. Jedes ist\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"188\", \"facs\": \"#f0207\"} --> das Produkt der Verbindung desselben Rohmaterials Baumwolle mit der-\n selben produktiven Arbeit, Spinnen, vermittelt durch dieselben Arbeits-\n mittel. Auch der Werth jedes einzelnen Pfundes Garn zeigt dieselbe\n Zusammensetzung, 1 sh. für Baumwolle, 2⅖ d. für verbrauchte\n Arbeitsmittel, 1⅘ d., worin nothwendige Arbeit, und 1⅘ d., worin Mehr-\n arbeit verleiblicht ist.\n\n\n\n\nVereinzelt für sich oder als aliquote Theile des Gesammtprodukts\n betrachtet, bestimmte Quanta Garn bleiben stets Gebilde derselben\n produktiven Arbeit, des Spinnens. Unter einem andern Gesichts-\n punkt verändert sich dagegen die Stellung des Theilprodukts ganz\n und gar, je nachdem es selbstständig oder im Zusammenhang mit dem Ge-\n sammtprodukt, als Theilprodukt oder als Produkttheil betrach-\n tet wird.\n\n\n\n\nEin Pfund Garn kostet 1 sh. 6 d., und, wir sehn vom Abfall ab, das\n in ihm versponnene lb. Baumwolle 1 sh., also ⅔ seines Werths. Also\n sind ⅔ lb. Garn = 1 lb. Baumwolle und 13⅓ lbs. Garn = 20 lbs.\n Baumwolle. In den 13⅓ lbs. Garn stecken zwar nur 13⅓ lbs. Baum-\n wolle zum Werth von 13⅓ sh., aber ihr zusätzlicher Werth von 6⅔ sh.\n bildet ein Aequivalent für die in den überschüssigen 6⅔ lbs. Garn ver-\n sponnene Baumwolle. Die 13⅓ lbs. Garn stellen also alle im Ge-\n sammtprodukt von 20 lbs. Garn versponnene Baumwolle vor, das\n Rohmaterial des Gesammtprodukts, aber auch weiter nichts. Es ist als\n ob den andern 6⅔ lbs. Garn die Wolle ausgerupft und alle Wolle des\n Gesammtprodukts in 13⅓ lbs. Garn zusammengepresst wäre. Dagegen\n sind die in den 13⅓ lbs. Garn enthaltene Spinnarbeit, und der Werth-\n theil, den die verbrauchten Arbeitsmittel zusetzen, aus ihnen selbst ent-\n fernt und auf den neben ihnen liegenden Produkttheil von 6⅔ lbs. Garn\n übertragen. In derselben Weise kann ein Theil dieser übrigbleibenden\n 6⅔ lbs. Garn — nämlich 2⅔ lbs. — wieder als blosse Darstellung der\n im Gesammtprodukt vernützten Arbeitsmittel zum Werth von\n 4 sh. gefasst werden. Acht Zehntel des Gesammtprodukts, oder 16 lbs.\n Garn, obgleich leiblich, als Gebrauchswerth betrachtet, als Garn,\n eben so sehr Gebilde der Spinnarbeit als die restirenden \\frac{2}{10} des Produkts,\n 4 lbs. Garn, enthalten daher in diesem Zusammenhang keine Spinnarbeit,\n keine während des Spinnprozesses selbst eingesaugte Arbeit. Es ist als\n ob sie sich ohne Spinnen in Garn verwandelt und als wäre ihre Garngestalt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"189\", \"facs\": \"#f0208\"} --> reiner Lug und Trug. In der That, wenn der Kapitalist sie verkauft zu\n 24 sh. und damit seine Produktionsmittel zurückkauft, zeigt sich, dass die\n 16 lbs. Garn nur verkleidete Baumwolle, Spindel, Kohle u. s. w. sind.\n Die übrigbleibenden 4 lbs. Garn enthalten jetzt ihrerseits kein Atom von\n Rohmaterial und Arbeitsmittel. Was davon in ihnen steckte, ward bereits\n ausgeweidet und den ersten 16 lbs. Garn einverleibt. Die 4 lbs. Garn\n enthalten daher kein Atom der in der Produktion von Baumwolle, Maschi-\n nerie, Kohle u. s. w. verausgabten Arbeit. Ihr Werth von 6 sh. ist reine\n Materiatur der vom Spinner selbst verausgabten 12 Arbeitsstunden. Die\n im Produkt von 20 lbs. Garn verkörperte Spinnarbeit ist jetzt konzentrirt\n auf 4 lbs. Garn, auf ⅕ des Produkts. Es ist als ob der Spinner diess\n Gespinnst von 4 lbs. in der Luft gewirket oder in Baumwolle und mit\n Spindeln, die, ohne Zuthat menschlicher Arbeit, von Natur vorhanden,\n dem Produkt keinen Werth zusetzen. Von diesen 4 lbs. Garn endlich\n verkörpert eine Hälfte bloss nothwendige Arbeit von 6 Stunden, die\n andere — die letzten 2 lbs. Garn — bloss Mehrarbeit. Nur dieser\n letzte Theil des Gesammtprodukts bildet das Mehrprodukt.\n\n\n\n\nDas Gesammtprodukt von 20 lbs. Garn kann also folgendermassen\n zerfällt werden:\nGesammtprodukt von 20 lbs. Garn, werth 30 sh.\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nVon dem Gesammtprodukt von 20 lbs. Garn vertreten 16 lbs. oder ⅘\n nur constantes Kapital, dagegen bloss ⅕ oder 4 lbs. die im Spinnprozess\n selbst verausgabte Arbeit. Und dennoch bilden die 20 lbs. Garn das Pro-\n dukt der zwölfstündigen Spinnarbeit. Hier erscheint wieder der Unter-\n schied von Arbeitsprozess und Verwerthungsprozess. Es wiederholt sich\n das bereits Bekannte, dass der Werth des Produkts des Arbeitstags,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"190\", \"facs\": \"#f0209\"} --> d. h. der Produktenwerth, grösser ist als das tägliche Werthpro-\n dukt. So ist der Werth der täglich produzirten 20 lbs. Garn = 30 sh.,\n aber ihr während des Tags produzirter Werththeil nur = 6 sh. Die eben\n gegebne Darstellung unterscheidet sich von der früheren dadurch, dass\n die funktionell oder begrifflich unterschiednen Bestand-\n theile des Produktenwerths in proportionellen Theilen\n des Produkts selbst ausgedrückt werden.\n\n\n\n\nDiese Zerfällung des Produkts — des Resultats des\n Produktionsprozesses — in ein Quantum Produkt, das nur die in den\nProduktionsmitteln enthaltene Arbeit, ein andres Quantum, das\n nur die im Produktionsprozess zugesetzte nothwendige Arbeit,\n und ein letztes Quantum Produkt, das nur die im selben Prozess zuge-\n setzte Mehrarbeit darstellt, ist eben so einfach als wichtig, wie ihre\n spätere Anwendung auf verwickelte und noch ungelöste Probleme zei-\n gen wird.\n\n\n\n\nWir betrachteten eben das Gesammtprodukt als fertiges Resultat des\n zwölfstündigen Arbeitstags. Wir können es aber auch in seinem Entste-\n hungsprozess begleiten, und dennoch die Theilprodukte als funktionell\n unterschiedne Produktentheile darstellen.\n\n\n\n\nDer Spinner produzirt in 12 Stunden 20 lbs. Garn, daher in einer\n Stunde 1⅔ und in 8 Stunden 13⅓ lbs., also ein Theilprodukt vom Ge-\n sammtwerth der Baumwolle, die während des ganzen Arbeitstags\n versponnen wird. In derselben Art und Weise ist das Theilprodukt der\n folgenden Stunde und 36 Minuten = 2⅔ lbs. Garn und stellt daher den\n Werth der während der 12 Arbeitsstunden vernutzten Produktions-\n mittel dar. Ebenso produzirt der Spinner in der folgenden Stunde und\n 12 Minuten 2 lbs. Garn = 3 sh., ein Produktenwerth gleich dem ganzen\n Werthprodukt, das er in 6 Stunden nothwendiger Arbeit\n schafft. Endlich produzirt er in den letzten \\frac{5}{4} Stunden ebenfalls 2 lbs.\n Garn, deren Werth gleich dem durch seine halbtägige Mehrarbeit\n erzeugten Mehrwerth. Diese Art Berechnung dient dem englischen\n Fabrikanten zum Hausgebrauch und er wird z. B. sagen, dass er in den\n ersten 8 Stunden oder ⅔ des Arbeitstags seine Baumwolle herausschlägt\n u. s. w. Man sieht, die Formel ist richtig, in der That die erste Formel,\n übersetzt aus dem Raum, wo die Theile des Produkts fertig neben ein-\n ander liegen, in die Zeit, wo sie auf einander folgen. Die Formel kann\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"191\", \"facs\": \"#f0210\"} --> aber auch von sehr barbarischen Vorstellungen begleitet sein, namentlich\n in Köpfen, die eben so praktisch im Verwerthungsprozess interessirt sind,\n als sie ein Interesse haben, ihn theoretisch misszuverstehen. So kann\n sich eingebildet werden, dass unser Spinner z. B. in den ersten 8 Stunden\n seines Arbeitstags den Werth der Baumwolle, in der folgenden Stunde\n und 36 Minuten den Werth der verzehrten Arbeitsmittel, in der folgenden\n Stunde und 12 Minuten den Werth des Arbeitslohns produzirt oder er-\n setzt, und nur die vielberühmte „letzte Stunde“ dem Fabrikanten\n zur Produktion von Mehrwerth widmet. Dem Spinner wird so das dop-\n pelte Wunder aufgebürdet, Baumwolle, Spindel, Dampfmaschine, Kohle,\n Oel u. s. w. in demselben Augenblick zu produziren, wo er mit ihnen\n spinnt, und aus Einem Arbeitstag von gegebnem Intensivitätsgrad fünf\n solcher Tage zu machen. In unserm Fall nämlich erfordert die Produk-\n tion des Rohmaterials und der Arbeitsmittel 4 zwölfstündige Arbeitstage\n und ihre Verwandlung in Garn einen andern zwölfstündigen Arbeitstag.\n Dass die Raubgier solche Wunder glaubt und nie den doktrinären\n Sykophanten misst, der sie beweist, zeige folgendes Beispiel.\n\n\n\n\nAn einem schönen Morgen des Jahres 1836 wurde der wegen seiner\n ökonomischen Wissenschaft und seines „schönen Styls“ berufene Nassau\n W. Senior, gewissermassen der Clauren unter den englischen Oekono-\n men, von Oxford nach Manchester citirt, um statt in Oxford politische\n Oekonomie zu lehren, sie in Manchester zu lernen. Die Fabrikanten er-\n kiesten ihn zum Preisfechter gegen den neulich erlassenen Factory\n Act und die darüber noch hinausstrebende Zehnstundenagitation. Mit\n gewohntem praktischen Scharfsinn hatten sie erkannt, dass der Herr Pro-\n fessor „wanted a good deal of finishing“. Sie verschrieben ihn daher\n nach Manchester. Der Herr Professor seinerseits hat die zu Manchester\n von den Fabrikanten erhaltene Lektion stylisirt in dem Pamphlet: „Let-\n ters on the Factory Act, as it affects the cotton manu-\n facture. London 1837.“ Hier kann man u. a. folgendes Erbau-\n liche lesen:\n\n\n\n\n„Unter dem gegenwärtigen Gesetz kann keine Fabrik, die Personen\nunter 18 Jahren beschäftigt, länger als 11½ Stunden täglich\n arbeiten, d. h. 12 Stunden während der ersten 5 Tage und 9 Stunden\n am Sonnabend. Die folgende Analyse (!) zeigt nun, dass in einer sol-\n chen Fabrik der ganze Reingewinn von der letzten Stunde\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"192\", \"facs\": \"#f0211\"} --> abgeleitet ist. Ein Fabrikant legt 100,000 Pfd. St. aus — 80,000 Pfd. St.\n in Fabrikgebäude und Maschinen, 20,000 in Rohmaterial und Arbeits-\n lohn. Das jährliche Einkommen der Fabrik, vorausgesetzt das Kapital\n schlage jährlich einmal um, und der Bruttogewinn betrage 15 %,\n muss sich auf Waaren zum Werth von 115,000 Pfd. St. belaufen …\n Von diesen 115,000 Pfd. St. produzirt jede der 23 halben Ar-\n beitsstunden täglich\\frac{5}{115} oder \\frac{1}{23}. Von diesen \\frac{23}{23} Arbeitsstunden,\n die das Ganze der 115,000 Pfd. St. bilden (constituting the whole 115,000\n Pd. St.), ersetzen\\frac{20}{23}, d. h. 100,000 von den 115,000, nur das Ka-\n pital; \\frac{1}{23} oder 5000 Pfd. St. von den 15,000 Brutto-Gewinn (!)\n ersetzen die Abnutzung der Fabrik und Maschinerie. Die übrigblei-\n benden \\frac{2}{23}, die beiden letzten halben Stunden jeden Tags produziren\n den Reingewinn von 10 %. Wenn daher bei gleichbleibenden\n Preisen die Fabrik 13 Stunden statt 11½ arbeiten dürfte, so würde, mit\n einer Zulage von ungefähr 2600 Pfd. St. zum cirkulirenden Kapital, der\n Reingewinn mehr als verdoppelt werden. Andrerseits wenn die Arbeits-\n stunden täglich um 1 Stunde reducirt würden, würde der Reingewinn\n verschwinden, wenn um 1½ Stunden, auch der Bruttogewinn [Note: Senior l. c. p. 12, 13. Wir gehn auf die für unsern Zweck gleich-\n gültigen Curiosa nicht ein, z. B. die Behauptung, dass die Fabrikanten den Ersatz\n der verschlissenen Maschinerie u. s. w., also eines Kapitalbestandtheils,\n zum Gewinn, Brutto oder Netto, schmutzig oder rein, rechnen. Auch nicht\n auf die Richtigkeit oder Falschheit der Zahlenangaben. Dass sie nicht mehr werth\n sind als die sogenannte „Analyse“, bewies Leonhard Horner in: „A\nLetter to Mr. Senior etc. Lond. 1837.“ Leonhard Horner, einer der\n Factory Inquiry Commissioners von 1833, und Fabrikinspektor, in der That\nFabrikcensor, bis 1859, hat unsterbliche Verdienste um die englische Ar-\n beiterklasse gewonnen. Ausser mit den erbitterten Fabrikanten führte er einen\n lebenslangen Kampf mit den Ministern, für die es ungleich wichtiger war, „die\n Stimmen“ der Fabrikherrn im Unterhaus als die Arbeitsstunden der\n „Hände“ in der Fabrik zu zählen.] !“\n\n\n\n\nUnd das nennt der Herr Professor eine „Analyse!“ Glaubte er\n den Fabrikantenjammer, dass die Arbeiter die beste Zeit des Tags in\n der Produktion, daher der Reproduktion oder dem Ersatz des Werths\n von Baulichkeiten, Maschinen, Baumwolle, Kohle u. s. w. vergeuden, so\n war jede Analyse überflüssig. Er hatte einfach zu antworten: Meine\n Herren! Wenn Ihr 10 Stunden arbeiten lasst statt 11½, wird, unter\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"193\", \"facs\": \"#f0212\"} --> sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen, der tägliche Verzehr von Baumwolle,\n Maschinerie u. s. w. um 1½ Stunden abnehmen. Ihr gewinnt also grade\n so viel als Ihr verliert. Eure Arbeiter werden in Zukunft 1½ Stunden\n weniger für Reproduktion oder Ersatz des vorgeschossenen Kapitalwerths\n vergeuden. Glaubte er ihnen nicht aufs Wort, sondern hielt als Sachver-\n ständiger eine Analyse für nöthig, so musste er vor allem, in einer Frage,\n die sich ausschliesslich um das Verhältniss des Reingewinns zur Grösse\n des Arbeitstags dreht, die Herren Fabrikanten ersuchen, Maschinerie und\n Fabrikgebäude, Rohmaterial und Arbeit nicht kunterbunt durcheinander\n zu wirren, sondern gefälligst das in Fabrikgebäude, Maschinerie, Roh-\n material u. s. w. enthaltene constante Kapital auf die eine, das in\n Arbeitslohn vorgeschossene Kapital auf die andere Seite zu\n stellen. Ergab sich dann etwa, dass nach der Fabrikantenrechnung der\n Arbeiter in \\frac{2}{23} des Arbeitstags, oder in einer Stunde, den Arbeitslohn\n reproduzirt oder ersetzt, so hatte der Analytiker fortzufahren:\n\n\n\n\nNach Eurer Angabe produzirt der Arbeiter in der vorletzten Stunde\n seinen Arbeitslohn und in der letzten Euren Mehrwerth oder den Rein-\n gewinn. Da er in gleichen Zeiträumen gleiche Werthe produzirt, hat das\n Produkt der vorletzten Stunde denselben Werth wie das der letzten. Er\n produzirt ferner nur Werth, so weit er Arbeit verausgabt, und das Quan-\n tum seiner Arbeit ist gemessen durch seine Arbeitszeit. Diese be-\n trägt nach Eurer Angabe 11½ Stunden per Tag. Einen Theil dieser\n 11½ Stunden verbraucht er zur Produktion oder zum Ersatz seines\n Arbeitslohns, den andern zur Produktion Eures Reingewinns. Weiter\n thut er nichts während des Arbeitstags. Da aber, nach Angabe, sein Lohn\n und der von ihm gelieferte Mehrwerth gleich grosse Werthe sind, pro-\n duzirt er offenbar seinen Arbeitslohn in 5¾ Stunden und Euren Rein-\n gewinn in andern 5¾ Stunden. Da ferner der Werth des zwei-\n stündigen Garnprodukts gleich der Werthsumme seines Arbeits-\n lohns plus Eures Reingewinns ist, muss dieser Garnwerth durch 11½\n Arbeitsstunden gemessen sein, das Produkt der vorletzten Stunde durch\n 5¾ Arbeitsstunden, das der letzten ditto. Wir kommen jetzt zu einem\n häklichen Punkt. Also aufgepasst! Die vorletzte Arbeitsstunde ist eine\n gewöhnliche Arbeitsstunde wie die erste. Ni plus, ni moins. Wie kann\n der Spinner daher in Einer Arbeitsstunde einen Garnwerth produ-\n ziren, der 5¾ Arbeitsstunden darstellt? Er verrichtet in der That\n [Running matter: I. 13] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"194\", \"facs\": \"#f0213\"} --> kein solches Wunder. Was er in Einer Arbeitsstunde an Gebrauchs-\n werth produzirt, ist ein bestimmtes Quantum Garn. Der\nWerth dieses Garns ist gemessen durch 5¾ Arbeitsstunden, wovon 4¾\n ohne sein Zuthun in den stündlich verzehrten Produktionsmitteln,\n Baumwolle, Maschinerie u. s. w. stecken, \\frac{4}{4} oder eine Stunde von ihm\n selbst zugesetzt ist. Da also sein Arbeitslohn in 5¾ Stunden produzirt\n wird und in dem Garnprodukt Einer Spinnstunde ebenfalls 5¾\n Stunden stecken, ist es durchaus keine Hexerei, dass das Werthpro-\n duktseiner 5¾ Spinnstunden gleich dem Produkten-\n werth Einer Spinnstunde. Ihr seid aber durchaus auf dem Holz-\n weg, wenn Ihr meint, er verliere ein einziges Zeitatom seines Arbeitstags\n mit der Reproduktion oder dem „Ersatz“ der Werthe von Baumwolle,\n Maschinerie u. s. w. Dadurch dass seine Arbeit aus Baumwolle und Spin-\n del Garn macht, dadurch dass er spinnt, geht der Werth von Baum-\n wolle und Spindel von selbst auf das Garn über. Es ist diess der\nQualität seiner Arbeit geschuldet, nicht ihrer Quantität. Allerdings\n wird er in einer Stunde mehr Baumwollwerth u. s. w. auf Garn übertragen\n als in ½ Stunde, aber nur weil er in 1 Stunde mehr Baumwolle verspinnt\n als in ½. Ihr begreift also: Euer Ausdruck, der Arbeiter produzirt in\n der vorletzten Stunde den Werth seines Arbeitslohns und in der letz-\n ten den Reingewinn, heisst weiter nichts, als dass in dem Garnpro-\n dukt von zwei Stunden seines Arbeitstags, ob sie vorn oder\n hinten stehen, 11½ Arbeitsstunden verkörpert sind, grade so viel Stun-\n den als sein ganzer Arbeitstag zählt. Und der Ausdruck, dass er\n in den ersten 5¾ Stunden seinen Arbeitslohn und in den letzten\n 5¾ Stunden Euren Reingewinn produzirt, heisst wieder nichts, als\n dass Ihr die ersten 5¾ Stunden zahlt und die letzten 5¾ Stunden\n nicht zahlt. Ich spreche von Zahlung der Arbeit, statt der Arbeits-\n kraft, um Euren slang zu reden. Vergleicht Ihr Herren nun das Verhält-\n niss der Arbeitszeit, die Ihr zahlt, zur Arbeitszeit, die Ihr nicht zahlt, so\n werdet Ihr finden, dass es halber Tag zu halbem Tag ist, also 100 %,\n was allerdings ein artiger Prozentsatz. Es unterliegt auch nicht dem ge-\n ringsten Zweifel, dass wenn Ihr Eure „Hände“ statt 11½ Stunden 13\n schanzen lasst, und, was Euch so ähnlich sieht, wie ein Ei dem andern,\n die überschüssigen 1½ Stunden zur blossen Mehrarbeit schlagt, letztere\n von 5¾ Stunden auf 7¼ Stunden wachsen wird, die Rate des Mehr-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"195\", \"facs\": \"#f0214\"} --> werths daher von 100 % auf 126\\frac{2}{23} %. Dagegen seid Ihr gar zu\n tolle Sanguiniker, wenn Ihr hofft, sie werde durch den Zusatz von 1½\n Stunden von 100 auf 200 % und gar auf mehr als 200 % steigen, d. h.\n sich „mehr als verdoppeln“. Andrerseits — des Menschen Herz ist ein\n wunderlich Ding, namentlich wenn der Mensch sein Herz im Beutel trägt,\n — seid Ihr gar zu verrückte Pessimisten, wenn Ihr fürchtet, mit der Re-\n duktion des Arbeitstags von 11½ auf 10½ Stunden werde Euer ganzer\n Reingewinn in die Brüche gehn. Bei Leibe nicht. Alle andern Umstände\n als gleichbleibend vorausgesetzt, wird die Mehrarbeit von 5¾ auf 4¾\n Stunden fallen, was immer noch eine ganz erkleckliche Rate des Mehr-\n werths giebt, nämlich 82\\frac{14}{23} %. Die verhängnissvolle „letzte\n Stunde“ aber, von der Ihr mehr gefabelt habt als die Chiliasten vom\n Weltuntergang, ist „all bosh“. Ihr Verlust wird weder Euch den „Rein-\n gewinn“ noch den von Euch verarbeiteten Kindern beiderlei Geschlechts\n die „Seelenreinheit“ kosten [Note: Wenn Senior bewies, dass an „der letzten Arbeitsstunde“ der\nReingewinn der Fabrikanten, die Existenz der englischen Baumwollindustrie,\n Englands Weltmarktgrösse hing, bewies dahinwiederum Dr. Andrew Ure in\n den Kauf, dass wenn man Fabrikkinder und junge Personen unter 18 Jahren,\n statt sie volle 12 Stunden in der warmen und reinen Moralluft der Fabrikstube ab-\n zuschanzen, „eine Stunde“ früher in die gemüthskalte und frivole Aussenwelt ver-\n stosse, Müssiggang und Laster sie um ihr Seelenheil prellen müssten. Seit 1848 wer-\n den die Fabrikinspektoren nicht müde, in ihren halbjährigen „Reports“ die Fabri-\n kanten mit „der letzten“, „der verhängnissvollen Stunde“ zu necken.\n So sagt Herr Howell, in seinem Fabrikbericht vom 31. Mai 1855: „Wäre die\n folgende scharfsinnige Berechnung (er citirt Senior) richtig, so hätte jede Baum-\n wollfabrik im Ver. Königreich seit 1850 mit Verlust gearbeitet.“ (Reports of\n the Insp. of Fact. for the half year ending 30th April 1855“ p.\n 19, 20.) Als im Jahr 1848 die Zehnstundenbill durchs Parlament ging, octroyirten\n die Fabrikanten einigen Normalarbeitern in den ländlichen Flachsspinnereien, die\n zwischen den Grafschaften Dorset und Somerset zerstreut liegen, eine Gegenpeti-\n tion, worin es u. A. heisst: „Eure Bittsteller, Eltern, glauben, dass eine zusätz-\n liche Mussestunde weiter keinen Erfolg haben kann, als ihre Kinder zu demorali-\n siren, denn Müssiggang ist allen Lasters Anfang.“ Der Fabrikbericht vom\n 31. Oktober 1848 bemerkt hierzu: „Die Atmosphäre der Flachsspinnereien, worin\n die Kinder dieser tugendhaft - zärtlichen Eltern arbeiten, ist geschwängert mit so\n unzähligen Staub - und Faserpartikeln des Rohmaterials, dass es ausserordentlich\n unangenehm ist, auch nur 10 Minuten in den Spinnstuben zuzubringen, denn ihr\n könnt das nicht ohne die peinlichste Empfindung, indem Auge, Ohr, Nasenlöcher] . Wenn einmal Euer „letztes Stünd-\n [Running matter: 13*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"196\", \"facs\": \"#f0215\"} -->lein“ wirklich schlägt, denkt an den Professor von Oxford. Und nun:\n In einer bessern Welt wünsch’ ich mir mehr von Eurem werthen Umgang.\n Adio [Note: Indess hatte der Herr Professor doch etwas bei seinem Manchester Ausflug\n profitirt! In den „Letters on the Factory Act“ hängt der ganze Rein-\n gewinn, „Profit“ und „Zins“ und sogar ‚something more‘ an einer\n unbezahlten Arbeitsstunde des Arbeiters! Ein Jahr zuvor, in seinen] ! ‥ Das Signal der von Senior 1836 entdeckten „letzten\n Stunde“ ward am 15. April 1848, polemisch gegen das Zehnstunden-\n gesetz, von James Wilson, einem der ökonomischen Hauptmandarine,\n im „London Economist“ von neuem geblasen.\n\n\n\n\n [Note: und Mund sich sofort füllen mit Flachsstaubwolken, vor denen kein Entrinnen ist.\n Die Arbeit selbst erheischt, wegen der Fieberhast der Maschinerie, rastlosen Auf-\n wand von Geschick und Bewegung, unter der Kontrole nie ermüdender Aufmerk-\n samkeit, und es scheint etwas hart, Eltern den Ausdruck „Faullenzerei“ auf ihre\n eigenen Kinder anwenden zu lassen, die, nach Abzug der Essenszeit, 10 volle\n Stunden an solche Beschäftigung, in einer solchen Atmosphäre, geschmiedet sind\n … Diese Kinder arbeiten länger als die Ackerknechte in den Nachbardörfern…\n Solch liebloses Gekohl über „Müssiggang und Laster“ muss als der reinste cant\n und die schamloseste Heuchelei gebrandmarkt werden … Der Theil des Publi-\n kums, der vor ungefähr zwölf Jahren auffuhr über die Zuversicht, womit man\n öffentlich und ganz ernsthaft proklamirte, unter der Sanction hoher Autorität, dass\n der ganze „Reingewinn“ des Fabrikanten aus „der letzten Stunde“\n Arbeit fliesse, und die Reduktion des Arbeitstags um eine Stunde den Reingewinn\n vernichten müsse; dieser Theil des Publikums, sagen wir, wird kaum seinen Augen\n trauen, wenn er nun findet, dass die Original - Entdeckung über die Tugenden der\n „letzten Stunde“ seitdem soweit verbessert worden ist „Moral“ und „Profit“\n gleichmässig einzuschliessen; so dass wenn die Dauer der Kinderarbeit auf volle\n 10 Stunden reducirt würde, ihre Moral zugleich mit dem Nettogewinn ihrer\n Anwender flöten ginge, beide abhängig von dieser letzten, dieser fatalen Stunde.“\n („Repts of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1848“, p. 101.) Derselbe\n Fabrikbericht giebt dann Proben von der „Moral“ und „Tugend“ dieser Herrn\n Fabrikanten, von den Schlichen, Pfiffen, Lockungen, Drohmitteln, Fälschungen\n u. s. w., die sie anwandten, um dergleichen Petitionen von wenigen ganz verwahr-\n losten Arbeitern unterzeichnen zu machen, um sie dann als Petitionen eines ganzen\n Industriezweigs, ganzer Grafschaften, dem Parlament aufzubinden. — Höchst cha-\n rakteristisch bleibt es für den heutigen Stand der ökonomischen sogenannten\n „Wissenschaft“, dass weder Senior selbst, der später zu seiner Ehre\n energisch für die Fabrikgesetzgebung auftrat, noch seine ursprünglichen und\n späteren Widersacher, die Trugschlüsse der „Originalentdeckung“ aufzulösen\n wussten. Sie appellirten an die thatsächliche Erfahrung. Das why und wherefore\n blieb Mysterium.] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"197\", \"facs\": \"#f0216\"} -->\n\nWie die Rate des Mehrwerths bestimmt ist durch das Verhält-\n niss des Mehrwerths, nicht zur Gesammtsumme des vorgeschossenen Kapi-\n tals, sondern zu seinem in Arbeitskraft ausgelegten, variablen Bestand-\n theil, so ist die Höhe des Mehrprodukts bestimmt, nicht durch\n sein Verhältniss zum Rest des Gesammtprodukts, sondern ausschliesslich\n zum Produkttheil, worin sich die nothwendige Arbeit darstellt. Wie die\n Produktion von Mehrwerth der bestimmende Zweck der kapitalistischen\n Produktion, so misst nicht die absolute Masse des Produkts, sondern allein\n die des Mehrprodukts, den Höhegrad des Reichthums [Note: „Für ein Individuum mit einem Kapital von 20,000 Pfd. St., dessen Pro-\n fite 2000 Pfd. St. jährlich betragen, wäre es ein durchaus gleichgültig Ding, ob\n sein Kapital 100 oder 1000 Arbeiter beschäftigt, ob die produzirten Waaren sich\n zu 10,000 oder 20,000 Pfd. St. verkaufen, immer vorausgesetzt, dass seine Profite\n in allen Fällen nicht unter 2000 Pfd. St. fallen. Ist das reale Interesse einer\n Nation nicht dasselbe? Vorausgesetzt ihr reales Nettoeinkommen, ihre Renten und\n Profite bleiben dieselben, so ist es nicht von der geringsten Wichtigkeit, ob die\n Nation aus 10 oder 12 Millionen Einwohnern besteht.“ (Ric. l. c. p. 416.)\n Lange vor Ricardo sagte der Fanatiker des Mehrprodukts, Arthur Young, ein\n übrigens schwatzschweifiger, kritikloser Schriftsteller, dessen Ruf in umgekehrtem\n Verhältniss zu seinem Verdienst steht, u. A.: „Von welchem Nutzen würde in\n einem modernen Königreich eine ganze Provinz sein, deren Boden in altrömischer\n Manier, von kleinen, unabhängigen Bauern, meinetwegen noch so gut bebaut\n würde? Von welchem Zwecke, ausser dem einzigen, Menschen zu erzeugen („the\n mere purpose of breeding men“), was an und für sich gar keinen Zweck hat“\n („is a most useless purpose“). Arthur Young: „Political Arith-\n metic etc. London 1774“, p. 47.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Summe der nothwendigen Arbeit und der Mehr-\n arbeit, der Zeit, worin der Arbeiter nur den Werth seiner Arbeitskraft\n reproduzirt, und der Zeit, worin er Mehrwerth produzirt, bestimmt die ab-\n solute Grösse seiner Arbeitszeit — den Arbeitstag (work-\n ing day).\n\n\n\n\n [Note: zum Gemeinbesten Oxforder Studenten und gebildeter Philister verfassten „Out-\n lines of Political Economy“ hatte er noch gegenüber Ricardo’s Werth-\n bestimmung durch die Arbeitszeit „entdeckt“, dass der Profit aus der Arbeit\n des Kapitalisten und der Zins aus seiner Ascetik, seiner „Absti-\n nenz“, herstamme. Die Flause selbst war alt, aber das Wort „Abstinenz“\n neu. Herr Roscher hat es richtig durch „Enthaltung“ verdeutscht. Seine\n minder mit Latein beschlagenen Compatrioten, Wirthe, Schulzen und andre\n Michels, haben es in „Entsagung“ vermöncht.] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"198\", \"facs\": \"#f0217\"} -->","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0001","text":"Der Mehrwerth, den das vorgeschossene Kapital C im Produk-\n tionsprozess erzeugt hat, oder die Verwerthung des vorgeschossenen\nKapitalwerths C stellt sich zunächst dar als Ueberschuss des\n Werths des Produkts über die Werthsumme seiner Pro-\n duktionselemente.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"7.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0002","text":"Das Kapital C zerfällt in zwei Theile, eine Geldsumme c, die für\n Produktionsmittel, und eine andere Geldsumme v, die für Arbeitskraft\n verausgabt wird; c stellt den in konstantes, v den in vari-\n ables Kapital verwandelten Werththeil vor. Ursprünglich ist also\n C = c + v, z. B. das vorgeschossene Kapital von 500 Pfd. St. =\n Pfd. St. +  Pfd. St. Am Ende des Prozesses kommt ein Produkt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"179\", \"facs\": \"#f0198\"} --> heraus, dessen Werth =  + m, wo m der Mehrwerth, z. B.\n. Das ursprüngliche Kapital C hat sich in C'ver-\n wandelt, aus 500 Pfd. St. in 590 Pfd. St. Die Differenz zwischen beiden ist\n = m, einem Mehrwerth von 90.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"7.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0003","text":"Da der Werth der Produktions-\n elemente gleich dem Werth des vorgeschossenen Kapitals,\n so ist es in der That eine Tautologie, dass der Ueberschuss des Produkten-\n werths über den Werth seiner Produktionselemente gleich der Verwerthung\n des vorgeschossenen Kapitals oder gleich dem produzirten Mehrwerth.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"7.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0004","text":"Indess erfordert diese Tautologie eine nähere Bestimmung. Der mit\n dem Produktenwerth verglichene Werth seiner Produktionselemente ist der\n Werth der in seiner Bildung aufgezehrten Produktionselemente. Nun\n haben wir aber gesehn, dass ein Theil des angewandten konstan-\n ten Kapitals, der aus Arbeitsmitteln bestehende, seinen Werth nur\n bruchweis an das Produkt abgiebt, während eine andere Portion desselben\n in seiner alten Existenzform fortdauert. Da der letztere Theil keine\n Rolle im Werthbildungsprozess spielt, abstrahiren wir hier\n ganz und gar von ihm. Sein Hineinziehn in die Rechnung würde nichts\n ändern. Nimm an, c = 410 l. bestehe aus Rohmaterial zu 312 l.,\n Hilfsstoffen zu 44 l. und im Prozess verschleissender Maschinerie\n von 54 l., der Werth der wirklich angewandten Maschinerie betrage\n aber 1054 l.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"7.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0005","text":"Als vorgeschossen zur Erzeugung des Produkten-\n werths berechnen wir nur den Werth von 54 l., den die Maschinerie durch\n ihre Funktion verliert und daher an das Produkt abgiebt. Rechneten\n wir die 1000 Pfd. St. mit, die in ihrer alten Form fortexistiren als Dampf-\n maschine u. s. w., so müssten wir sie auf beiden Seiten mitrechnen, auf\n Seite des vorgeschossenen Werths und auf Seite des Produktenwerths [Note: „If we reckon the value of the fixed capital employed as a part of the\n advances, we must reckon the remaining value of such capital at the end of the\n year as a part of the annual returns.“ (Malthus: „Princ. of Pol. Econ.\n 2nd. ed London 1836“, p. 269.)] ,\n und erhielten so resp. 1500 Pfd. St. und 1590 Pfd. St. Die Differenz\n oder der Mehrwerth wäre nach wie vor 90 Pfd. St.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"7.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0006","text":"Unter dem zur\nWerthproduktion vorgeschossenen konstanten Kapital verstehn wir\n [Running matter: 12*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"180\", \"facs\": \"#f0199\"} --> daher, wo das Gegentheil nicht aus dem Zusammenhang erhellt, stets nur\n den Werth der in der Produktion verzehrten Produktionsmittel.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"7.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0007","text":"Diess vorausgesetzt, kehren wir zurück zur Formel C = c + v,\n die sich in C' =  + m und eben dadurch C in C' verwandelt.\n Man weiss, dass der Werth des konstanten Kapitals im Produkt nur\nwieder erscheint. Das im Prozess wirklich neu erzeugte Werthpro-\n dukt ist also verschieden von dem aus dem Prozess erhaltenen Produkten-\n werth, daher nicht, wie es auf den ersten Blick scheint,  + m oder\n, sondern v + m oder , nicht 590 l.,\n sondern 180 l. Wäre c, das konstante Kapital, = 0, in anderen Wor-\n ten, gäbe es Industriezweige, worin der Kapitalist keine produzirten Pro-\n duktionsmittel, weder Rohmaterial, noch Hilfsstoffe, noch Arbeitsinstru-\n mente, sondern nur von Natur vorhandne Stoffe und Arbeitskraft an-\n zuwenden hätte, so wäre kein konstanter Werththeil auf das Produkt zu\n übertragen. Dieses Element des Produktenwerths, in unsrem Beispiel\n 410 Pfd.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"7.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0008","text":"St., fiele fort, aber das Werthprodukt von 180 Pfd. St., welches\n 90 Pfd. St. Mehrwerth enthält, bliebe ganz ebenso gross als ob c die grösste\n Werthsumme darstellte. Wir hätten C =  = v, und C', das ver-\n werthete Kapital, = v + m, C' — C nach wie vor = m. Wäre um-\n gekehrt m = o, in anderen Worten, hätte die Arbeitskraft, deren Werth\n im variablen Kapital vorgeschossen wird, nur ein Aequivalent produzirt, so\n C = c + v und C' (der Produktenwerth) =  + o, daher C = C'.\n Das vorgeschossene Kapital hätte sich nicht verwerthet.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"7.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0009","text":"Wir wissen in der That bereits, dass der Mehrwerth blos Folge\n der Werthveränderung ist, die mit v, dem in Arbeitskraft umgesetz-\n ten Kapitaltheil vorgeht, dass also v + m = v + ∆ v (v plus In-\n crement von v) ist. Aber die wirkliche Werthveränderung\n und das Verhältniss, worin sich der Werth ändert, werden dadurch\n verdunkelt, dass in Folge des Wachsens seines variirenden Be-\n standtheils auch das vorgeschossene Gesammtkapital\n wächst. Es war 500 und es ist am Ende des Prozesses 590.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"7.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0010","text":"Die\n reine Analyse des Prozesses erheischt also von dem Theil des Produkten-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"181\", \"facs\": \"#f0200\"} --> werths, worin nur konstanter Kapitalwerth wieder erscheint, ganz zu ab-\n strahiren, also das konstante Kapital c = o zu setzen, und damit ein Ge-\n setz der Mathematik anzuwenden, wo sie mit variablen und konstanten\n Grössen operirt, und die konstante Grösse nur durch Addition oder Sub-\n traktion mit der variablen verbunden ist [Note: „Constant quantities connected to variable by the operations of Addition\n or Substraction disappear by the process of differentiation.“ (J. Hind: „Diffe-\n rential Calculus. Cambridge 1831“ p. 126.) In der That, die Grössen-\nveränderung einer konstanten Grösse ist Nichts. Daher das Gesetz des\n Differentialkalkuls: Differential einer konstanten Grösse = 0.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"7.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0011","text":"Eine andre Schwierigkeit entspringt aus der ursprünglichen\n Form des variablen Kapitals. So im obigen Beispiel ist C' = 410 l.\n konstantes Kapital + 90 l. variables Kapital + 90 l. Mehrwerth. Neun-\n zig Pfd. St. sind aber eine gegebne, also konstante Grösse und es scheint\n daher ungereimt sie als variable Grösse zu behandeln. Aber  oder 90 l.\n variables Kapital ist hier in der That nur ein Symbol für den Pro-\n zess, den dieser Werth durchläuft. Der im Ankauf der Ar-\n beitskraft vorgeschossene Kapitaltheil ist bestimmtes Quantum\n vergegenständlichter Arbeit, also konstante Werthgrösse,\n wie der Werth der gekauften Arbeitskraft. Im Produktionsprozess selbst\n aber tritt an die Stelle der vorgeschossenen 90 Pfd. St.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"7.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0012","text":"die sich bethäti-\n gende Arbeitskraft, an die Stelle todter lebendige Arbeit, an die Stelle\n einer ruhenden eine fliessende Grösse, an die Stelle einer konstanten eine\n variable. Das Resultat ist die Reproduktion von v plus Increment von v.\n Vom Standpunkt der kapitalistischen Produktion ist dieser ganze Verlauf\nSelbstbewegung des in Arbeitskraft umgesetzten, ursprünglich kon-\n stanten Werths. Ihm wird der Prozess und sein Resultat zu gut ge-\n schrieben. Erscheint die Formel 90 l. variables Kapital oder sich\n verwerthender Werth daher widerspruchsvoll, so drückt sie nur einen der\n kapitalistischen Produktion immanenten Widerspruch aus.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"7.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0013","text":"Die Gleichsetzung des konstanten Kapitals mit o befremdet auf den\n ersten Blick. Indess vollzieht man sie beständig im Alltagsleben. Will\n Jemand z. B. Englands Gewinn an der Baumwollindustrie berechnen, so\n zieht er vor allem den an die Vereinigten Staaten, Indien, Aegypten u. s. w.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"182\", \"facs\": \"#f0201\"} --> gezahlten Baumwoll preis ab; d. h. er setzt im Produktenwerth nur wie-\n dererscheinenden Kapitalwerth = o.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"7.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0014","text":"Allerdings hat das Verhältniss des Mehrwerths, nicht nur zum Kapi-\n taltheil, woraus er unmittelbar entspringt und dessen Werthverände-\n rung er darstellt, sondern auch zum vorgeschossenen Gesammtkapi-\n tal seine grosse ökonomische Bedeutung. Wir behandeln diess Verhält-\n niss daher ausführlich im dritten Buch. Um einen Theil des Kapitals\n durch seinen Umsatz in Arbeitskraft zu verwerthen, muss ein andrer Theil\n des Kapitals in Produktionsmittel verwandelt werden. Damit das variable\n Kapital funktionire, muss konstantes Kapital in entsprechenden Propor-\n tionen, je nach dem bestimmten technologischen Charakter des Arbeits-\n prozesses, vorgeschossen werden. Der Umstand jedoch, dass man\n zu einem chemischen Prozess Retorten und andre Gefässe braucht, verhindert\n nicht bei der Analyse von der Retorte selbst zu abstrahiren. Sofern Werth-\n schöpfung und Werthveränderung für sich selbst, d.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"7.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0015","text":"h. rein betrachtet wer-\n den, liefern die Produktionsmittel, diese stofflichen Gestalten des konstanten\n Kapitals, nur den Stoff, worin sich die flüssige, werthbildende Kraft fixiren\n kann. Die Natur dieses Stoffes ist daher auch gleichgültig, ob Baumwolle\n oder Eisen. Auch der Werth dieses Stoffes ist gleichgültig. Er muss nur\n in hinreichender Masse vorhanden sein, um das während des Produktions-\n prozesses zu verausgabende Arbeitsquantum einsaugen zu können. Diese\n Masse gegeben, mag ihr Werth steigen, oder fallen, oder sie mag werthlos\n sein, wie Erde und Meer, der Prozess der Werthschöpfung und Werthver-\n änderung wird nicht davon berührt.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"7.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0016","text":"Wir setzen also zunächst den konstanten Kapitaltheil gleich Null.\n Das vorgeschossene Kapital reduzirt sich daher von c + v auf v, und der\n Produktenwerth  + m auf das Werthprodukt . Gegeben\n das Werthprodukt = 180 Pfd. St., worin sich die während der ganzen\n Dauer des Produktionsprozesses fliessende Arbeit darstellt, so haben wir\n den Werth des variablen Kapitals = 90 Pfd. St. abzuziehn, um den\n Mehrwerth = 90 Pfd. St. zu erhalten. Die Zahl 90 Pfd. St. = m drückt\n hier die absolute Grösse des produzirten Mehrwerths aus. Seine\nproportionelle Grösse aber, also das Verhältniss, worin das va-\n riable Kapital sich verwerthet hat, ist offenbar bestimmt durch das Ver-\n hältniss des Mehrwerths zum variablen Kapital, oder ist\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"183\", \"facs\": \"#f0202\"} --> ausgedrückt in \\frac{m}{v}. Im obigen Beispiel also in \\frac{90}{90} = 100 %.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"7.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0017","text":"Diese\n verhältnissmässige Verwerthung des variablen Kapitals, oder die verhält-\n nissmässige Grösse des Mehrwerths, nenne ich Rate des Mehr-\n werths [Note: In derselben Weise, wie der Engländer „rate of profit“, „rate of inte-\n rest“ u. s. w. braucht. Man wird aus Buch III sehen, dass die Profitrate leicht\n zu begreifen, sobald man die Gesetze des Mehrwerths kennt. Auf dem um-\n gekehrten Weg begreift man ni l’un, ni l’autre.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"7.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0018","text":"Wir haben gesehn, dass der Arbeiter während eines Abschnitts\n des Arbeitsprozesses nur den Werth seiner Arbeitskraft produ-\n zirt, d. h. den Werth seiner nothwendigen Lebensmittel. Da er in\n einem auf gesellschaftlicher Theilung der Arbeit beruhenden Zustand pro-\n duzirt, produzirt er seine Lebensmittel nicht direkt, sondern, in Form einer\n besondern Waare, des Garns z. B., einen Werth gleich dem Werth\n seiner Lebensmittel, oder dem Geld, womit er sie kauft. Der\nTheil seines Arbeitstags, den er hierzu verbraucht, ist grösser\n oder kleiner, je nach dem Werth seiner durchschnittlichen täglichen\n Lebensmittel, also der zu ihrer Produktion erheischten durchschnittli-\n chen täglichen Arbeitszeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"7.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0019","text":"Stellt die Werthgrösse dieser täglichen Lebens-\n mittel im Durchschnitt 6 vergegenständlichte Arbeitsstunden dar, so muss\n der Arbeiter im Durchschnitt täglich 6 Stunden arbeiten, um sie zu produ-\n ziren. Arbeitete er nicht für den Kapitalisten, sondern als unabhängiger\n Produzent, so müsste er, unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen, nach\n wie vor im Durchschnitt denselben aliquoten Theil des Tags\n arbeiten, um den Werth seiner Arbeitskraft zu produziren, und\n dadurch die zu seiner eignen Erhaltung oder beständigen Reproduktion\n nöthigen Lebensmittel zu gewinnen.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"7.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0020","text":"Da er aber in dem Theil des Ar-\n beitstags, worin er den Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft, sage 3 sh., produ-\n zirt, nur ein Aequivalent für ihren vom Kapitalisten bereits gezahlten\n Werth produzirt, also durch den neu geschaffnen Werth nur den vorge-\n schossenen variablen Kapitalwerth ersetzt, erscheint diese Pro-\n duktion von Werth als blosse Reproduktion. Den Theil des Arbeits-\n tags also, worin diese Reproduktion vorgeht, nenne ich nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit, die während derselben verausgabte Arbeit nothwen_\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"184\", \"facs\": \"#f0203\"} --> dige Arbeit [Note: Wir haben bisher in dieser Schrift das Wort „nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit“ angewandt für die zur Produktion einer Waare überhaupt gesellschaftlich\n nothwendige Arbeitszeit. Wir brauchen es von jetzt ab auch für die zur Produk-\n tion der spezifischen Waare Arbeitskraft nothwendige Arbeitszeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"7.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0021","text":"Der\n Gebrauch derselben termini technici in verschiednem Sinn ist misslich, aber in\n keiner Wissenschaft ganz zu vermeiden. Man vergleiche z. B. die höheren und\n niederen Theile der Mathematik.] . Nothwendig für den Arbeiter, weil unabhängig von\n der gesellschaftlichen Form seiner Arbeit. Nothwendig für das Kapital\n und seine Welt, weil das beständige Dasein des Arbeiters ihre Basis.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"7.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0022","text":"Die zweite Periode des Arbeitsprozesses, die der Arbeiter über die\n Grenzen der nothwendigen Arbeit hinausschanzt, kostet ihm zwar Arbeit,\n Verausgabung von Arbeitskraft, bildet aber keinen Werth für ihn. Sie\n bildet Mehrwerth, der den Kapitalisten mit allem Reiz einer Schöpfung\n aus Nichts anlacht. Diesen Theil des Arbeitstags nenne ich Surplus-\n arbeitszeit, und die in ihr verausgabte Arbeit: Mehrarbeit (surplus\n labour). So entscheidend es für die Erkenntniss des Werths überhaupt,\n ihn als blosse Gerinnung von Arbeitszeit, als bloss vergegenständ-\n lichte Arbeit, so entscheidend für die Erkenntniss des Mehrwerths,\n ihn als blosse Gerinnung von Surplusarbeitszeit, als bloss\nvergegenständlichte Mehrarbeit zu begreifen. Nur die Form,\n worin diese Mehrarbeit dem unmittelbaren Produzenten, dem Arbeiter, ab-\n gepresst wird, unterscheidet die ökonomischen Gesellschaftsformationen,\n z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"7.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0023","text":"die Gesellschaft der Sklaverei von der der Lohnarbeit [Note: Mit wahrhaft Gottsched’scher Genialität entdeckt Herr Wilhelm\n Thucydides Roscher, dass wenn die Bildung von Mehrwerth oder\nMehrprodukt, und die damit verbundene Accumulation, heurigen Tags der\n „Sparsamkeit“ des Kapitalisten geschuldet, der dafür „z. B. Zins verlangt“,\n dagegen „auf den niedrigsten Kulturstufen … die Schwächeren von den Stär-\n keren zur Sparsamkeit gezwungen werden.“ (l. c. p. 78.) Zur Ersparung von\n Arbeit? oder nicht vorhandner überschüssiger Produkte? Neben\n wirklicher Ignoranz ist es apologetische Scheu vor gewissenhafter Analyse des\n Werths und Mehrwerths, und etwa verfänglich-polizeiwidrigem Resultat, die einen\n Roscher und Cons.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"7.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0024","text":"zwingt, die mehr oder minder plausiblen Rechtfertigungs-\n gründe des Kapitalisten für seine Aneignung vorhandner Mehrwerthe\n zum Entstehungsgrund des Mehrwerths selbst zu stempeln.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"7.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0025","text":"Da der Werth des variablen Kapitals = Werth der von ihm gekauf-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"185\", \"facs\": \"#f0204\"} --> ten Arbeitskraft, da der Werth dieser Arbeitskraft den nothwendigen\n Theil des Arbeitstags bestimmt, der Mehrwerth seinerseits aber bestimmt\n ist durch den überschüssigen Theil des Arbeitstags, so folgt: Der Mehr-\n werth verhält sich zum variablen Kapital, wie die Mehr-\n arbeit zur nothwendigen, oder die Rate des Mehrwerths\n\\frac{m}{v} = \\frac{Mehrarbeit}{Nothwendige Arbeit}. Beide Proportionen stellen dasselbe Verhält-\n niss in verschiedner Form dar, das einemal in der Form vergegenständ-\n lichter, das andremal in der Form flüssiger Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"7.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0026","text":"Die Rate des Mehrwerths ist daher der exakte Ausdruck für\n den Exploitationsgrad der Arbeitskraft durch das Ka-\n pital oder des Arbeiters durch den Kapitalisten.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"7.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0027","text":"Nach unsrer Annahme war der Werth des Produkts = \n + , das vorgeschossene Kapital = 500 l. Da der Mehrwerth 90 und\n das vorgeschossene Kapital 500, würde man nach der gewöhnlichen Art\n der Berechnung herausbekommen, dass die Rate des Mehrwerths (die man\n mit der Profitrate verwechselt) = 18 %, eine Verhältnisszahl, deren\n Niedrigkeit Herrn Carey und andre Harmoniker rühren möchte. In der\n That aber ist die Rate des Mehrwerths nicht = \\frac{m}{C} oder \\frac{m}{c + v}, sondern = \\frac{m}{v},\n also nicht \\frac{90}{500}, sondern \\frac{90}{90} = 100 %, mehr als das Fünffache des\nscheinbaren Exploitationsgrads. Obgleich wir nun im gegebnen Fall\n die absolute Grösse des Arbeitstags nicht kennen, auch nicht\n die Periode des Arbeitsprozesses (Tag, Woche u. s. w.), endlich nicht\n die Anzahl der Arbeiter, die das variable Kapital von 90 l.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"7.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0028","text":"gleichzeitig in\n Bewegung setzt, zeigt uns die Rate des Mehrwerths \\frac{m}{v} durch ihre Konver-\n tibilität in \\frac{Mehrarbeit}{Nothwendige Arbeit} genau das Verhältniss der zwei Bestand-\n theile des Arbeitstags zu einander. Es ist 100 %. Also arbeitete der\n Arbeiter die eine Hälfte des Tags für sich und die andre für den Ka-\n pitalisten.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"7.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0029","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"186\", \"facs\": \"#f0205\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"7.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0030","text":"Die Methode zur Berechnung der Rate des Mehrwerths ist also kurz-\n gefasst diese: Wir nehmen den ganzen Produktenwerth und setzen\n den darin nur wiedererscheinenden constanten Kapitalwerth\n gleich Null. Die übrigbleibende Werthsumme ist das einzige im Bildungs-\n prozess der Waare wirklich erzeugte Werthprodukt. Ist der Mehrwerth\n gegeben, so ziehn wir ihn von diesem Werthprodukt ab, um das variable\n Kapital zu finden. Umgekehrt, wenn letzteres gegeben und wir den\n Mehrwerth suchen. Sind beide gegeben, so ist nur noch die Schlussopera-\n tion zu verrichten, das Verhältniss des Mehrwerths zum variablen Kapital,\n\\frac{m}{v}, zu berechnen.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"7.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0031","text":"So einfach die Methode, scheint es doch passend, den Leser in die\n ihr zu Grunde liegende und ihm ungewohnte Anschauungsweise durch\n einige Beispiele einzuexerciren.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"7.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0032","text":"Zunächst ein Beispiel aus der Spinnindustrie. Die Data gehören\n dem Jahre 1860. Für unsren Zweck gleichgültige Umstände sind unter-\n drückt. Eine Fabrik konsumirte wöchentlich 11,500 lbs. Baumwolle,\n wovon 1500 Abfall. Zu 7 d. das Pfund Baumwolle, beträgt das Roh-\n material daher 336 Pfd. St. Sie setzte 10000 Spindeln in Bewegung, zu\n 1 Pfd. St. per Spindel = 10000 Pfd. St., wovon der jährliche Verschleiss,\n zu 12½ %, 1250 Pfd. St., für die Woche also 24 Pfd. St.; der wöchent-\n liche Verschleiss der Dampfmaschine 20 Pfd. St.; die wöchentliche Aus-\n gabe für Hilfsstoffe, Kohle, Oel u. s. w. 40 Pfd. St. Der wöchentliche\n Arbeitslohn betrug 70 Pfd. St. und der Verkaufspreis des lb. Garn 1⅒ sh.,\n also der 10000 lbs. Garn wöchentlich 550 Pfd. St. Der constante\n Werththeil des Kapitals beträgt also 420 Pfd. St. Wir setzen ihn = 0,\n da er in der wöchentlichen Werthbildung nicht mitspielt.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"7.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0033","text":"Das wirkliche\nwöchentliche Werthprodukt, das übrig bleibt, also = 130 Pfd. St.\n Wir ziehn davon das an die Arbeiter gezahlte variable Kapital von\n 70 Pfd. St. ab, bleibt Mehrwerth von 60 Pfd. St. Die Rate des\n Mehrwerths, \\frac{m}{v}, \\frac{60}{70}, also ungefähr 86 %. Diese Prozentzahl drückt\n den Exploitationsgrad der Arbeitskraft oder den Verwerthungsgrad des\n variablen Kapitals aus. Nehmen wir an, dass 10 Stunden in jener Fabrik\n im täglichen Durchschnitt gearbeitet ward, so betrug die nothwendige\n Arbeit ungefähr 5\\frac{5}{13}, und die Mehrarbeit 4\\frac{8}{13} Stunden.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"7.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0034","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"187\", \"facs\": \"#f0206\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"7.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0035","text":"Jacob giebt für das Jahr 1815, bei Annahme eines Weizenpreises\n von 80 sh. per Quarter, und eines Durchschnittsertrags von 22 Bushels\n per acre, so dass der acre 11 Pfd. St. einbringt, folgende durch vorherige\n Kompensation verschiedner Posten sehr mangelhafte, aber für unsern\n Zweck genügende Rechnung.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"7.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0036","text":"Werthproduktion per acre.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"7.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0037","text":"Samen (Weizen) 1 Pfd. St. 9 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"7.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0038","text":"Dünger 2 Pfd. St. 10 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"7.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0039","text":"Arbeitslohn 3 Pfd. St. 10 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"7.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0040","text":"Summa: 7 Pfd. St. 9 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"7.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0041","text":"Zehnten, Rates, Taxes 1 Pfd. St. 1 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"7.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0042","text":"Rente 1 Pfd. St. 8 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"7.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0043","text":"Pächter’s Profit u. Zins 1 Pfd. St. 2 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"7.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0044","text":"Summa: 3 Pfd. St. 11 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"7.44"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0045","text":"Der Mehrwerth, stets unter der Voraussetzung, dass Preis des\n Produkts = seinem Werth, wird hier unter die verschiedenen Rubriken\n Profit, Zins, Zehnten u. s. w. vertheilt. Diese Rubriken sind hier gleich-\n gültig. Wir addiren sie zusammen und erhalten einen Mehrwerth von\n 3 l. 11 sh. Die 3 l. 19 sh. für Samen und Dünger setzen wir\n als constanten Kapitaltheil gleich Null. Bleibt vorgeschossenes va-\n riables Kapital von 3 l. 10 sh., an dessen Stelle ein Aequivalent von\nproduzirt worden ist. Also beträgt\n\\frac{m}{v} = \\frac{3 l. 11 sh.}{3 l. 10 sh.} mehr als 100 %. Der Arbeiter verwendet mehr als\n die Hälfte seines Arbeitstags zur Produktion eines Mehrwerths, den ver-\n schiedne Personen auf verschiedne Vorwände hin unter sich vertheilen [Note: Die gegebnen Rechnungen gelten nur als Illustration. Es wird nämlich\n unterstellt, dass die Preise = den Werthen.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"7.45"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0046","text":"Man wird in Buch III sehn, dass\n diese Gleichsetzung, selbst für die Durchschnittspreise, sich nicht in dieser\n einfachen Weise macht.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"7.46"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0047","text":"Der Mehrwerth stellt sich dar in einem Mehrprodukt (sur-\n plusproduce).","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"7.47"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0048","text":"Wir nahmen vorher an, dass der zwölfstündige Arbeitstag des Spin-\n ners aus 6 Stunden nothwendiger Arbeit und 6 Stunden Mehr-\n arbeit besteht, 20 lbs. Baumwolle in 20 lbs. Garn verwandelt, und ihnen\n einen Werth von 6 sh. zusetzt, dass ferner 1 lb. Baumwolle 1 sh. und die\n während des ganzen Prozesses verzehrten Arbeitsmittel 4 sh. kosten, also\n Werth des Gesammtprodukts 30 sh. und der eines lb. Garn 1 sh. 6 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"7.48"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0049","text":"Jedes Pfund Garn stellt denselben Gebrauchswerth dar. Jedes ist\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"188\", \"facs\": \"#f0207\"} --> das Produkt der Verbindung desselben Rohmaterials Baumwolle mit der-\n selben produktiven Arbeit, Spinnen, vermittelt durch dieselben Arbeits-\n mittel. Auch der Werth jedes einzelnen Pfundes Garn zeigt dieselbe\n Zusammensetzung, 1 sh. für Baumwolle, 2⅖ d. für verbrauchte\n Arbeitsmittel, 1⅘ d., worin nothwendige Arbeit, und 1⅘ d., worin Mehr-\n arbeit verleiblicht ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"7.49"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0050","text":"Vereinzelt für sich oder als aliquote Theile des Gesammtprodukts\n betrachtet, bestimmte Quanta Garn bleiben stets Gebilde derselben\n produktiven Arbeit, des Spinnens. Unter einem andern Gesichts-\n punkt verändert sich dagegen die Stellung des Theilprodukts ganz\n und gar, je nachdem es selbstständig oder im Zusammenhang mit dem Ge-\n sammtprodukt, als Theilprodukt oder als Produkttheil betrach-\n tet wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"7.50"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0051","text":"Ein Pfund Garn kostet 1 sh. 6 d., und, wir sehn vom Abfall ab, das\n in ihm versponnene lb. Baumwolle 1 sh., also ⅔ seines Werths. Also\n sind ⅔ lb. Garn = 1 lb. Baumwolle und 13⅓ lbs. Garn = 20 lbs.\n Baumwolle. In den 13⅓ lbs. Garn stecken zwar nur 13⅓ lbs. Baum-\n wolle zum Werth von 13⅓ sh., aber ihr zusätzlicher Werth von 6⅔ sh.\n bildet ein Aequivalent für die in den überschüssigen 6⅔ lbs. Garn ver-\n sponnene Baumwolle. Die 13⅓ lbs. Garn stellen also alle im Ge-\n sammtprodukt von 20 lbs. Garn versponnene Baumwolle vor, das\n Rohmaterial des Gesammtprodukts, aber auch weiter nichts. Es ist als\n ob den andern 6⅔ lbs. Garn die Wolle ausgerupft und alle Wolle des\n Gesammtprodukts in 13⅓ lbs. Garn zusammengepresst wäre. Dagegen\n sind die in den 13⅓ lbs.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"7.51"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0052","text":"Garn enthaltene Spinnarbeit, und der Werth-\n theil, den die verbrauchten Arbeitsmittel zusetzen, aus ihnen selbst ent-\n fernt und auf den neben ihnen liegenden Produkttheil von 6⅔ lbs. Garn\n übertragen. In derselben Weise kann ein Theil dieser übrigbleibenden\n 6⅔ lbs. Garn — nämlich 2⅔ lbs. — wieder als blosse Darstellung der\n im Gesammtprodukt vernützten Arbeitsmittel zum Werth von\n 4 sh. gefasst werden. Acht Zehntel des Gesammtprodukts, oder 16 lbs.\n Garn, obgleich leiblich, als Gebrauchswerth betrachtet, als Garn,\n eben so sehr Gebilde der Spinnarbeit als die restirenden \\frac{2}{10} des Produkts,\n 4 lbs. Garn, enthalten daher in diesem Zusammenhang keine Spinnarbeit,\n keine während des Spinnprozesses selbst eingesaugte Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"7.52"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0053","text":"Es ist als\n ob sie sich ohne Spinnen in Garn verwandelt und als wäre ihre Garngestalt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"189\", \"facs\": \"#f0208\"} --> reiner Lug und Trug. In der That, wenn der Kapitalist sie verkauft zu\n 24 sh. und damit seine Produktionsmittel zurückkauft, zeigt sich, dass die\n 16 lbs. Garn nur verkleidete Baumwolle, Spindel, Kohle u. s. w. sind.\n Die übrigbleibenden 4 lbs. Garn enthalten jetzt ihrerseits kein Atom von\n Rohmaterial und Arbeitsmittel. Was davon in ihnen steckte, ward bereits\n ausgeweidet und den ersten 16 lbs. Garn einverleibt. Die 4 lbs. Garn\n enthalten daher kein Atom der in der Produktion von Baumwolle, Maschi-\n nerie, Kohle u. s. w. verausgabten Arbeit. Ihr Werth von 6 sh. ist reine\n Materiatur der vom Spinner selbst verausgabten 12 Arbeitsstunden. Die\n im Produkt von 20 lbs. Garn verkörperte Spinnarbeit ist jetzt konzentrirt\n auf 4 lbs.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"7.53"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0054","text":"Garn, auf ⅕ des Produkts. Es ist als ob der Spinner diess\n Gespinnst von 4 lbs. in der Luft gewirket oder in Baumwolle und mit\n Spindeln, die, ohne Zuthat menschlicher Arbeit, von Natur vorhanden,\n dem Produkt keinen Werth zusetzen. Von diesen 4 lbs. Garn endlich\n verkörpert eine Hälfte bloss nothwendige Arbeit von 6 Stunden, die\n andere — die letzten 2 lbs. Garn — bloss Mehrarbeit. Nur dieser\n letzte Theil des Gesammtprodukts bildet das Mehrprodukt.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"7.54"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0055","text":"Das Gesammtprodukt von 20 lbs. Garn kann also folgendermassen\n zerfällt werden:\nGesammtprodukt von 20 lbs. Garn, werth 30 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"7.55"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0056","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nVon dem Gesammtprodukt von 20 lbs. Garn vertreten 16 lbs. oder ⅘\n nur constantes Kapital, dagegen bloss ⅕ oder 4 lbs. die im Spinnprozess\n selbst verausgabte Arbeit. Und dennoch bilden die 20 lbs. Garn das Pro-\n dukt der zwölfstündigen Spinnarbeit. Hier erscheint wieder der Unter-\n schied von Arbeitsprozess und Verwerthungsprozess. Es wiederholt sich\n das bereits Bekannte, dass der Werth des Produkts des Arbeitstags,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"190\", \"facs\": \"#f0209\"} --> d. h. der Produktenwerth, grösser ist als das tägliche Werthpro-\n dukt. So ist der Werth der täglich produzirten 20 lbs. Garn = 30 sh.,\n aber ihr während des Tags produzirter Werththeil nur = 6 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"7.56"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0057","text":"Die eben\n gegebne Darstellung unterscheidet sich von der früheren dadurch, dass\n die funktionell oder begrifflich unterschiednen Bestand-\n theile des Produktenwerths in proportionellen Theilen\n des Produkts selbst ausgedrückt werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"7.57"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0058","text":"Diese Zerfällung des Produkts — des Resultats des\n Produktionsprozesses — in ein Quantum Produkt, das nur die in den\nProduktionsmitteln enthaltene Arbeit, ein andres Quantum, das\n nur die im Produktionsprozess zugesetzte nothwendige Arbeit,\n und ein letztes Quantum Produkt, das nur die im selben Prozess zuge-\n setzte Mehrarbeit darstellt, ist eben so einfach als wichtig, wie ihre\n spätere Anwendung auf verwickelte und noch ungelöste Probleme zei-\n gen wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"7.58"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0059","text":"Wir betrachteten eben das Gesammtprodukt als fertiges Resultat des\n zwölfstündigen Arbeitstags. Wir können es aber auch in seinem Entste-\n hungsprozess begleiten, und dennoch die Theilprodukte als funktionell\n unterschiedne Produktentheile darstellen.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"7.59"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0060","text":"Der Spinner produzirt in 12 Stunden 20 lbs. Garn, daher in einer\n Stunde 1⅔ und in 8 Stunden 13⅓ lbs., also ein Theilprodukt vom Ge-\n sammtwerth der Baumwolle, die während des ganzen Arbeitstags\n versponnen wird. In derselben Art und Weise ist das Theilprodukt der\n folgenden Stunde und 36 Minuten = 2⅔ lbs. Garn und stellt daher den\n Werth der während der 12 Arbeitsstunden vernutzten Produktions-\n mittel dar. Ebenso produzirt der Spinner in der folgenden Stunde und\n 12 Minuten 2 lbs. Garn = 3 sh., ein Produktenwerth gleich dem ganzen\n Werthprodukt, das er in 6 Stunden nothwendiger Arbeit\n schafft. Endlich produzirt er in den letzten \\frac{5}{4} Stunden ebenfalls 2 lbs.\n Garn, deren Werth gleich dem durch seine halbtägige Mehrarbeit\n erzeugten Mehrwerth. Diese Art Berechnung dient dem englischen\n Fabrikanten zum Hausgebrauch und er wird z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"7.60"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0061","text":"sagen, dass er in den\n ersten 8 Stunden oder ⅔ des Arbeitstags seine Baumwolle herausschlägt\n u. s. w. Man sieht, die Formel ist richtig, in der That die erste Formel,\n übersetzt aus dem Raum, wo die Theile des Produkts fertig neben ein-\n ander liegen, in die Zeit, wo sie auf einander folgen. Die Formel kann\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"191\", \"facs\": \"#f0210\"} --> aber auch von sehr barbarischen Vorstellungen begleitet sein, namentlich\n in Köpfen, die eben so praktisch im Verwerthungsprozess interessirt sind,\n als sie ein Interesse haben, ihn theoretisch misszuverstehen. So kann\n sich eingebildet werden, dass unser Spinner z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"7.61"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0062","text":"in den ersten 8 Stunden\n seines Arbeitstags den Werth der Baumwolle, in der folgenden Stunde\n und 36 Minuten den Werth der verzehrten Arbeitsmittel, in der folgenden\n Stunde und 12 Minuten den Werth des Arbeitslohns produzirt oder er-\n setzt, und nur die vielberühmte „letzte Stunde“ dem Fabrikanten\n zur Produktion von Mehrwerth widmet. Dem Spinner wird so das dop-\n pelte Wunder aufgebürdet, Baumwolle, Spindel, Dampfmaschine, Kohle,\n Oel u. s. w. in demselben Augenblick zu produziren, wo er mit ihnen\n spinnt, und aus Einem Arbeitstag von gegebnem Intensivitätsgrad fünf\n solcher Tage zu machen. In unserm Fall nämlich erfordert die Produk-\n tion des Rohmaterials und der Arbeitsmittel 4 zwölfstündige Arbeitstage\n und ihre Verwandlung in Garn einen andern zwölfstündigen Arbeitstag.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"7.62"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0063","text":"Dass die Raubgier solche Wunder glaubt und nie den doktrinären\n Sykophanten misst, der sie beweist, zeige folgendes Beispiel.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"7.63"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0064","text":"An einem schönen Morgen des Jahres 1836 wurde der wegen seiner\n ökonomischen Wissenschaft und seines „schönen Styls“ berufene Nassau\n W. Senior, gewissermassen der Clauren unter den englischen Oekono-\n men, von Oxford nach Manchester citirt, um statt in Oxford politische\n Oekonomie zu lehren, sie in Manchester zu lernen. Die Fabrikanten er-\n kiesten ihn zum Preisfechter gegen den neulich erlassenen Factory\n Act und die darüber noch hinausstrebende Zehnstundenagitation. Mit\n gewohntem praktischen Scharfsinn hatten sie erkannt, dass der Herr Pro-\n fessor „wanted a good deal of finishing“. Sie verschrieben ihn daher\n nach Manchester. Der Herr Professor seinerseits hat die zu Manchester\n von den Fabrikanten erhaltene Lektion stylisirt in dem Pamphlet: „Let-\n ters on the Factory Act, as it affects the cotton manu-\n facture. London 1837.“ Hier kann man u. a. folgendes Erbau-\n liche lesen:","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"7.64"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0065","text":"„Unter dem gegenwärtigen Gesetz kann keine Fabrik, die Personen\nunter 18 Jahren beschäftigt, länger als 11½ Stunden täglich\n arbeiten, d. h. 12 Stunden während der ersten 5 Tage und 9 Stunden\n am Sonnabend. Die folgende Analyse (!) zeigt nun, dass in einer sol-\n chen Fabrik der ganze Reingewinn von der letzten Stunde\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"192\", \"facs\": \"#f0211\"} --> abgeleitet ist. Ein Fabrikant legt 100,000 Pfd. St. aus — 80,000 Pfd. St.\n in Fabrikgebäude und Maschinen, 20,000 in Rohmaterial und Arbeits-\n lohn. Das jährliche Einkommen der Fabrik, vorausgesetzt das Kapital\n schlage jährlich einmal um, und der Bruttogewinn betrage 15 %,\n muss sich auf Waaren zum Werth von 115,000 Pfd. St. belaufen …\n Von diesen 115,000 Pfd. St. produzirt jede der 23 halben Ar-\n beitsstunden täglich\\frac{5}{115} oder \\frac{1}{23}.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"7.65"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0066","text":"Von diesen \\frac{23}{23} Arbeitsstunden,\n die das Ganze der 115,000 Pfd. St. bilden (constituting the whole 115,000\n Pd. St.), ersetzen\\frac{20}{23}, d. h. 100,000 von den 115,000, nur das Ka-\n pital; \\frac{1}{23} oder 5000 Pfd. St. von den 15,000 Brutto-Gewinn (!)\n ersetzen die Abnutzung der Fabrik und Maschinerie. Die übrigblei-\n benden \\frac{2}{23}, die beiden letzten halben Stunden jeden Tags produziren\n den Reingewinn von 10 %. Wenn daher bei gleichbleibenden\n Preisen die Fabrik 13 Stunden statt 11½ arbeiten dürfte, so würde, mit\n einer Zulage von ungefähr 2600 Pfd. St. zum cirkulirenden Kapital, der\n Reingewinn mehr als verdoppelt werden. Andrerseits wenn die Arbeits-\n stunden täglich um 1 Stunde reducirt würden, würde der Reingewinn\n verschwinden, wenn um 1½ Stunden, auch der Bruttogewinn [Note: Senior l. c. p. 12, 13.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"7.66"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0067","text":"Wir gehn auf die für unsern Zweck gleich-\n gültigen Curiosa nicht ein, z. B. die Behauptung, dass die Fabrikanten den Ersatz\n der verschlissenen Maschinerie u. s. w., also eines Kapitalbestandtheils,\n zum Gewinn, Brutto oder Netto, schmutzig oder rein, rechnen. Auch nicht\n auf die Richtigkeit oder Falschheit der Zahlenangaben. Dass sie nicht mehr werth\n sind als die sogenannte „Analyse“, bewies Leonhard Horner in: „A\nLetter to Mr. Senior etc. Lond. 1837.“ Leonhard Horner, einer der\n Factory Inquiry Commissioners von 1833, und Fabrikinspektor, in der That\nFabrikcensor, bis 1859, hat unsterbliche Verdienste um die englische Ar-\n beiterklasse gewonnen. Ausser mit den erbitterten Fabrikanten führte er einen\n lebenslangen Kampf mit den Ministern, für die es ungleich wichtiger war, „die\n Stimmen“ der Fabrikherrn im Unterhaus als die Arbeitsstunden der\n „Hände“ in der Fabrik zu zählen.] !“","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"7.67"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0068","text":"Und das nennt der Herr Professor eine „Analyse!“ Glaubte er\n den Fabrikantenjammer, dass die Arbeiter die beste Zeit des Tags in\n der Produktion, daher der Reproduktion oder dem Ersatz des Werths\n von Baulichkeiten, Maschinen, Baumwolle, Kohle u. s. w. vergeuden, so\n war jede Analyse überflüssig. Er hatte einfach zu antworten: Meine\n Herren! Wenn Ihr 10 Stunden arbeiten lasst statt 11½, wird, unter\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"193\", \"facs\": \"#f0212\"} --> sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen, der tägliche Verzehr von Baumwolle,\n Maschinerie u. s. w. um 1½ Stunden abnehmen. Ihr gewinnt also grade\n so viel als Ihr verliert. Eure Arbeiter werden in Zukunft 1½ Stunden\n weniger für Reproduktion oder Ersatz des vorgeschossenen Kapitalwerths\n vergeuden.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"7.68"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0069","text":"Glaubte er ihnen nicht aufs Wort, sondern hielt als Sachver-\n ständiger eine Analyse für nöthig, so musste er vor allem, in einer Frage,\n die sich ausschliesslich um das Verhältniss des Reingewinns zur Grösse\n des Arbeitstags dreht, die Herren Fabrikanten ersuchen, Maschinerie und\n Fabrikgebäude, Rohmaterial und Arbeit nicht kunterbunt durcheinander\n zu wirren, sondern gefälligst das in Fabrikgebäude, Maschinerie, Roh-\n material u. s. w. enthaltene constante Kapital auf die eine, das in\n Arbeitslohn vorgeschossene Kapital auf die andere Seite zu\n stellen. Ergab sich dann etwa, dass nach der Fabrikantenrechnung der\n Arbeiter in \\frac{2}{23} des Arbeitstags, oder in einer Stunde, den Arbeitslohn\n reproduzirt oder ersetzt, so hatte der Analytiker fortzufahren:","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"7.69"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0070","text":"Nach Eurer Angabe produzirt der Arbeiter in der vorletzten Stunde\n seinen Arbeitslohn und in der letzten Euren Mehrwerth oder den Rein-\n gewinn. Da er in gleichen Zeiträumen gleiche Werthe produzirt, hat das\n Produkt der vorletzten Stunde denselben Werth wie das der letzten. Er\n produzirt ferner nur Werth, so weit er Arbeit verausgabt, und das Quan-\n tum seiner Arbeit ist gemessen durch seine Arbeitszeit. Diese be-\n trägt nach Eurer Angabe 11½ Stunden per Tag. Einen Theil dieser\n 11½ Stunden verbraucht er zur Produktion oder zum Ersatz seines\n Arbeitslohns, den andern zur Produktion Eures Reingewinns. Weiter\n thut er nichts während des Arbeitstags. Da aber, nach Angabe, sein Lohn\n und der von ihm gelieferte Mehrwerth gleich grosse Werthe sind, pro-\n duzirt er offenbar seinen Arbeitslohn in 5¾ Stunden und Euren Rein-\n gewinn in andern 5¾ Stunden.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"7.70"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0071","text":"Da ferner der Werth des zwei-\n stündigen Garnprodukts gleich der Werthsumme seines Arbeits-\n lohns plus Eures Reingewinns ist, muss dieser Garnwerth durch 11½\n Arbeitsstunden gemessen sein, das Produkt der vorletzten Stunde durch\n 5¾ Arbeitsstunden, das der letzten ditto. Wir kommen jetzt zu einem\n häklichen Punkt. Also aufgepasst! Die vorletzte Arbeitsstunde ist eine\n gewöhnliche Arbeitsstunde wie die erste. Ni plus, ni moins. Wie kann\n der Spinner daher in Einer Arbeitsstunde einen Garnwerth produ-\n ziren, der 5¾ Arbeitsstunden darstellt? Er verrichtet in der That\n [Running matter: I. 13] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"194\", \"facs\": \"#f0213\"} --> kein solches Wunder. Was er in Einer Arbeitsstunde an Gebrauchs-\n werth produzirt, ist ein bestimmtes Quantum Garn.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"7.71"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0072","text":"Der\nWerth dieses Garns ist gemessen durch 5¾ Arbeitsstunden, wovon 4¾\n ohne sein Zuthun in den stündlich verzehrten Produktionsmitteln,\n Baumwolle, Maschinerie u. s. w. stecken, \\frac{4}{4} oder eine Stunde von ihm\n selbst zugesetzt ist. Da also sein Arbeitslohn in 5¾ Stunden produzirt\n wird und in dem Garnprodukt Einer Spinnstunde ebenfalls 5¾\n Stunden stecken, ist es durchaus keine Hexerei, dass das Werthpro-\n duktseiner 5¾ Spinnstunden gleich dem Produkten-\n werth Einer Spinnstunde. Ihr seid aber durchaus auf dem Holz-\n weg, wenn Ihr meint, er verliere ein einziges Zeitatom seines Arbeitstags\n mit der Reproduktion oder dem „Ersatz“ der Werthe von Baumwolle,\n Maschinerie u. s. w. Dadurch dass seine Arbeit aus Baumwolle und Spin-\n del Garn macht, dadurch dass er spinnt, geht der Werth von Baum-\n wolle und Spindel von selbst auf das Garn über.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"7.72"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0073","text":"Es ist diess der\nQualität seiner Arbeit geschuldet, nicht ihrer Quantität. Allerdings\n wird er in einer Stunde mehr Baumwollwerth u. s. w. auf Garn übertragen\n als in ½ Stunde, aber nur weil er in 1 Stunde mehr Baumwolle verspinnt\n als in ½. Ihr begreift also: Euer Ausdruck, der Arbeiter produzirt in\n der vorletzten Stunde den Werth seines Arbeitslohns und in der letz-\n ten den Reingewinn, heisst weiter nichts, als dass in dem Garnpro-\n dukt von zwei Stunden seines Arbeitstags, ob sie vorn oder\n hinten stehen, 11½ Arbeitsstunden verkörpert sind, grade so viel Stun-\n den als sein ganzer Arbeitstag zählt. Und der Ausdruck, dass er\n in den ersten 5¾ Stunden seinen Arbeitslohn und in den letzten\n 5¾ Stunden Euren Reingewinn produzirt, heisst wieder nichts, als\n dass Ihr die ersten 5¾ Stunden zahlt und die letzten 5¾ Stunden\n nicht zahlt.","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"7.73"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0074","text":"Ich spreche von Zahlung der Arbeit, statt der Arbeits-\n kraft, um Euren slang zu reden. Vergleicht Ihr Herren nun das Verhält-\n niss der Arbeitszeit, die Ihr zahlt, zur Arbeitszeit, die Ihr nicht zahlt, so\n werdet Ihr finden, dass es halber Tag zu halbem Tag ist, also 100 %,\n was allerdings ein artiger Prozentsatz. Es unterliegt auch nicht dem ge-\n ringsten Zweifel, dass wenn Ihr Eure „Hände“ statt 11½ Stunden 13\n schanzen lasst, und, was Euch so ähnlich sieht, wie ein Ei dem andern,\n die überschüssigen 1½ Stunden zur blossen Mehrarbeit schlagt, letztere\n von 5¾ Stunden auf 7¼ Stunden wachsen wird, die Rate des Mehr-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"195\", \"facs\": \"#f0214\"} --> werths daher von 100 % auf 126\\frac{2}{23} %. Dagegen seid Ihr gar zu\n tolle Sanguiniker, wenn Ihr hofft, sie werde durch den Zusatz von 1½\n Stunden von 100 auf 200 % und gar auf mehr als 200 % steigen, d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"7.74"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0075","text":"sich „mehr als verdoppeln“. Andrerseits — des Menschen Herz ist ein\n wunderlich Ding, namentlich wenn der Mensch sein Herz im Beutel trägt,\n — seid Ihr gar zu verrückte Pessimisten, wenn Ihr fürchtet, mit der Re-\n duktion des Arbeitstags von 11½ auf 10½ Stunden werde Euer ganzer\n Reingewinn in die Brüche gehn. Bei Leibe nicht. Alle andern Umstände\n als gleichbleibend vorausgesetzt, wird die Mehrarbeit von 5¾ auf 4¾\n Stunden fallen, was immer noch eine ganz erkleckliche Rate des Mehr-\n werths giebt, nämlich 82\\frac{14}{23} %. Die verhängnissvolle „letzte\n Stunde“ aber, von der Ihr mehr gefabelt habt als die Chiliasten vom\n Weltuntergang, ist „all bosh“.","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"7.75"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0076","text":"Ihr Verlust wird weder Euch den „Rein-\n gewinn“ noch den von Euch verarbeiteten Kindern beiderlei Geschlechts\n die „Seelenreinheit“ kosten [Note: Wenn Senior bewies, dass an „der letzten Arbeitsstunde“ der\nReingewinn der Fabrikanten, die Existenz der englischen Baumwollindustrie,\n Englands Weltmarktgrösse hing, bewies dahinwiederum Dr. Andrew Ure in\n den Kauf, dass wenn man Fabrikkinder und junge Personen unter 18 Jahren,\n statt sie volle 12 Stunden in der warmen und reinen Moralluft der Fabrikstube ab-\n zuschanzen, „eine Stunde“ früher in die gemüthskalte und frivole Aussenwelt ver-\n stosse, Müssiggang und Laster sie um ihr Seelenheil prellen müssten. Seit 1848 wer-\n den die Fabrikinspektoren nicht müde, in ihren halbjährigen „Reports“ die Fabri-\n kanten mit „der letzten“, „der verhängnissvollen Stunde“ zu necken.\n So sagt Herr Howell, in seinem Fabrikbericht vom 31.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"7.76"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0077","text":"Mai 1855: „Wäre die\n folgende scharfsinnige Berechnung (er citirt Senior) richtig, so hätte jede Baum-\n wollfabrik im Ver. Königreich seit 1850 mit Verlust gearbeitet.“ (Reports of\n the Insp. of Fact. for the half year ending 30th April 1855“ p.\n 19, 20.) Als im Jahr 1848 die Zehnstundenbill durchs Parlament ging, octroyirten\n die Fabrikanten einigen Normalarbeitern in den ländlichen Flachsspinnereien, die\n zwischen den Grafschaften Dorset und Somerset zerstreut liegen, eine Gegenpeti-\n tion, worin es u. A. heisst: „Eure Bittsteller, Eltern, glauben, dass eine zusätz-\n liche Mussestunde weiter keinen Erfolg haben kann, als ihre Kinder zu demorali-\n siren, denn Müssiggang ist allen Lasters Anfang.“ Der Fabrikbericht vom\n 31.","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"7.77"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0078","text":"Oktober 1848 bemerkt hierzu: „Die Atmosphäre der Flachsspinnereien, worin\n die Kinder dieser tugendhaft - zärtlichen Eltern arbeiten, ist geschwängert mit so\n unzähligen Staub - und Faserpartikeln des Rohmaterials, dass es ausserordentlich\n unangenehm ist, auch nur 10 Minuten in den Spinnstuben zuzubringen, denn ihr\n könnt das nicht ohne die peinlichste Empfindung, indem Auge, Ohr, Nasenlöcher] . Wenn einmal Euer „letztes Stünd-\n [Running matter: 13*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"196\", \"facs\": \"#f0215\"} -->lein“ wirklich schlägt, denkt an den Professor von Oxford. Und nun:\n In einer bessern Welt wünsch’ ich mir mehr von Eurem werthen Umgang.\n Adio [Note: Indess hatte der Herr Professor doch etwas bei seinem Manchester Ausflug\n profitirt!","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"7.78"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0079","text":"In den „Letters on the Factory Act“ hängt der ganze Rein-\n gewinn, „Profit“ und „Zins“ und sogar ‚something more‘ an einer\n unbezahlten Arbeitsstunde des Arbeiters! Ein Jahr zuvor, in seinen] ! ‥ Das Signal der von Senior 1836 entdeckten „letzten\n Stunde“ ward am 15. April 1848, polemisch gegen das Zehnstunden-\n gesetz, von James Wilson, einem der ökonomischen Hauptmandarine,\n im „London Economist“ von neuem geblasen.","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"7.79"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0080","text":"[Note: und Mund sich sofort füllen mit Flachsstaubwolken, vor denen kein Entrinnen ist.\n Die Arbeit selbst erheischt, wegen der Fieberhast der Maschinerie, rastlosen Auf-\n wand von Geschick und Bewegung, unter der Kontrole nie ermüdender Aufmerk-\n samkeit, und es scheint etwas hart, Eltern den Ausdruck „Faullenzerei“ auf ihre\n eigenen Kinder anwenden zu lassen, die, nach Abzug der Essenszeit, 10 volle\n Stunden an solche Beschäftigung, in einer solchen Atmosphäre, geschmiedet sind\n … Diese Kinder arbeiten länger als die Ackerknechte in den Nachbardörfern…\n Solch liebloses Gekohl über „Müssiggang und Laster“ muss als der reinste cant\n und die schamloseste Heuchelei gebrandmarkt werden … Der Theil des Publi-\n kums, der vor ungefähr zwölf Jahren auffuhr über die Zuversicht, womit man\n öffentlich und ganz ernsthaft proklamirte, unter der Sanction hoher Autorität, dass\n der ganze „Reingewinn“","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"7.80"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0081","text":"des Fabrikanten aus „der letzten Stunde“\n Arbeit fliesse, und die Reduktion des Arbeitstags um eine Stunde den Reingewinn\n vernichten müsse; dieser Theil des Publikums, sagen wir, wird kaum seinen Augen\n trauen, wenn er nun findet, dass die Original - Entdeckung über die Tugenden der\n „letzten Stunde“ seitdem soweit verbessert worden ist „Moral“ und „Profit“\n gleichmässig einzuschliessen; so dass wenn die Dauer der Kinderarbeit auf volle\n 10 Stunden reducirt würde, ihre Moral zugleich mit dem Nettogewinn ihrer\n Anwender flöten ginge, beide abhängig von dieser letzten, dieser fatalen Stunde.“\n („Repts of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1848“, p. 101.) Derselbe\n Fabrikbericht giebt dann Proben von der „Moral“ und „Tugend“ dieser Herrn\n Fabrikanten, von den Schlichen, Pfiffen, Lockungen, Drohmitteln, Fälschungen\n u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"7.81"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0082","text":"w., die sie anwandten, um dergleichen Petitionen von wenigen ganz verwahr-\n losten Arbeitern unterzeichnen zu machen, um sie dann als Petitionen eines ganzen\n Industriezweigs, ganzer Grafschaften, dem Parlament aufzubinden. — Höchst cha-\n rakteristisch bleibt es für den heutigen Stand der ökonomischen sogenannten\n „Wissenschaft“, dass weder Senior selbst, der später zu seiner Ehre\n energisch für die Fabrikgesetzgebung auftrat, noch seine ursprünglichen und\n späteren Widersacher, die Trugschlüsse der „Originalentdeckung“ aufzulösen\n wussten. Sie appellirten an die thatsächliche Erfahrung. Das why und wherefore\n blieb Mysterium.]","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"7.82"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0083","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"197\", \"facs\": \"#f0216\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"7.83"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0084","text":"Wie die Rate des Mehrwerths bestimmt ist durch das Verhält-\n niss des Mehrwerths, nicht zur Gesammtsumme des vorgeschossenen Kapi-\n tals, sondern zu seinem in Arbeitskraft ausgelegten, variablen Bestand-\n theil, so ist die Höhe des Mehrprodukts bestimmt, nicht durch\n sein Verhältniss zum Rest des Gesammtprodukts, sondern ausschliesslich\n zum Produkttheil, worin sich die nothwendige Arbeit darstellt. Wie die\n Produktion von Mehrwerth der bestimmende Zweck der kapitalistischen\n Produktion, so misst nicht die absolute Masse des Produkts, sondern allein\n die des Mehrprodukts, den Höhegrad des Reichthums [Note: „Für ein Individuum mit einem Kapital von 20,000 Pfd. St., dessen Pro-\n fite 2000 Pfd. St. jährlich betragen, wäre es ein durchaus gleichgültig Ding, ob\n sein Kapital 100 oder 1000 Arbeiter beschäftigt, ob die produzirten Waaren sich\n zu 10,000 oder 20,000 Pfd. St.","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"7.84"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0085","text":"verkaufen, immer vorausgesetzt, dass seine Profite\n in allen Fällen nicht unter 2000 Pfd. St. fallen. Ist das reale Interesse einer\n Nation nicht dasselbe? Vorausgesetzt ihr reales Nettoeinkommen, ihre Renten und\n Profite bleiben dieselben, so ist es nicht von der geringsten Wichtigkeit, ob die\n Nation aus 10 oder 12 Millionen Einwohnern besteht.“ (Ric. l. c. p. 416.)\n Lange vor Ricardo sagte der Fanatiker des Mehrprodukts, Arthur Young, ein\n übrigens schwatzschweifiger, kritikloser Schriftsteller, dessen Ruf in umgekehrtem\n Verhältniss zu seinem Verdienst steht, u. A.: „Von welchem Nutzen würde in\n einem modernen Königreich eine ganze Provinz sein, deren Boden in altrömischer\n Manier, von kleinen, unabhängigen Bauern, meinetwegen noch so gut bebaut\n würde?","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"7.85"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0086","text":"Von welchem Zwecke, ausser dem einzigen, Menschen zu erzeugen („the\n mere purpose of breeding men“), was an und für sich gar keinen Zweck hat“\n („is a most useless purpose“). Arthur Young: „Political Arith-\n metic etc. London 1774“, p. 47.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"7.86"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0087","text":"Die Summe der nothwendigen Arbeit und der Mehr-\n arbeit, der Zeit, worin der Arbeiter nur den Werth seiner Arbeitskraft\n reproduzirt, und der Zeit, worin er Mehrwerth produzirt, bestimmt die ab-\n solute Grösse seiner Arbeitszeit — den Arbeitstag (work-\n ing day).","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"7.87"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0088","text":"[Note: zum Gemeinbesten Oxforder Studenten und gebildeter Philister verfassten „Out-\n lines of Political Economy“ hatte er noch gegenüber Ricardo’s Werth-\n bestimmung durch die Arbeitszeit „entdeckt“, dass der Profit aus der Arbeit\n des Kapitalisten und der Zins aus seiner Ascetik, seiner „Absti-\n nenz“, herstamme. Die Flause selbst war alt, aber das Wort „Abstinenz“\n neu. Herr Roscher hat es richtig durch „Enthaltung“ verdeutscht. Seine\n minder mit Latein beschlagenen Compatrioten, Wirthe, Schulzen und andre\n Michels, haben es in „Entsagung“ vermöncht.]","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"7.88"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0089","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"198\", \"facs\": \"#f0217\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"7.89"}],"sha256":"1bb61143dc7d57cded9958b95589e3240358ae05ba19b7fa5aa99873f08b0c53","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T202810089164Z_ff2a8b17","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"3) Die Rate des Mehrwerths.","source_sha256":"1bb61143dc7d57cded9958b95589e3240358ae05ba19b7fa5aa99873f08b0c53","sha256":"b1b8ff18e50bca147b379344639b41cdcb0c19b1950742106e1a7edf19039da8","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:28:10.089178+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0001","text":"The surplus-value which the advanced capital C has generated in the process of production, or the valorisation of the advanced capital-value C, presents itself at first as the excess of the value of the product over the sum of the values of its elements of production.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"7.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0002","text":"Capital C falls into two parts, one sum of money c, which is expended on means of production, and another sum of money v, which is expended on labour-power; c represents the portion of value transformed into constant, v that transformed into variable capital. Originally, therefore, C = c + v, e.g. the advanced capital of £500 = £ + £. At the end of the process there emerges a product whose value = + m, where m is the surplus-value, e.g. . The original capital C has been transformed into C', from £500 into £590. The difference between the two is = m, a surplus-value of £90.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"179\", \"facs\": \"#f0198\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"7.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0003","text":"Since the value of the elements of production is equal to the value of the advanced capital, it is indeed a tautology that the excess of the value of the product over the value of its elements of production is equal to the valorisation of the advanced capital, or equal to the surplus-value produced.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"7.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0004","text":"Nevertheless, this tautology requires a more precise determination. The value of its elements of production compared with the value of the product is the value of the elements of production consumed in its formation. Now we have seen that a part of the applied constant capital, consisting of instruments of labour, gives up its value to the product only piecemeal, while another portion of it continues to exist in its old form. Since the latter part plays no role in the process of value-formation, we abstract from it altogether here. Its inclusion in the calculation would alter nothing. Assume that c = £410 consists of raw material worth £312, auxiliary materials worth £44, and machinery worn out in the process worth £54, but that the value of the machinery actually employed amounts to £1,054.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"7.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0005","text":"As advanced for the production of the value of the product, we calculate only the value of £54 which the machinery loses through its function and therefore gives up to the product. If we counted in the £1,000 which continue to exist in their old form as steam-engine, etc., we should have to count them on both sides, on the side of the advanced value and on the side of the value of the product [Note: “If we reckon the value of the fixed capital employed as a part of the\n advances, we must reckon the remaining value of such capital at the end of the\n year as a part of the annual returns.” (Malthus: “Princ. of Pol. Econ.\n 2nd. ed London 1836”, p. 269.)] , and would thus obtain respectively £1,500 and £1,590. The difference, or the surplus-value, would still be £90.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"7.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0006","text":"By the constant capital advanced for value-production we therefore understand, wherever the contrary is not evident from the context, always only the value of the means of production consumed in production.\n [Running matter: 12*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"180\", \"facs\": \"#f0199\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"7.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0007","text":"This presupposed, let us return to the formula C = c + v, which is transformed into C' = + m and thereby transforms C into C'. It is known that the value of the constant capital merely reappears in the product. The value-product actually newly generated in the process is therefore distinct from the value of the product obtained from the process; it is therefore not, as it seems at first sight, + m or , but v + m or , not £590, but £180. If c, the constant capital, were = 0, in other words, if there were branches of industry in which the capitalist had to employ no produced means of production, neither raw material nor auxiliary materials nor instruments of labour, but only materials existing in nature and labour-power, then no constant portion of value would be transferred to the product. This element of the value of the product, £410 in our example","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"7.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0008","text":"sterling, would disappear, but the value-product of £180, which contains £90 of surplus-value, would remain just as great as if c represented the largest sum of value. We should have C = = v, and C', the valorised capital, = v + m, C' — C still = m. If, conversely, m = 0, in other words, if labour-power, whose value is advanced in variable capital, had produced only an equivalent, then C = c + v and C' (the value of the product) = + 0, hence C = C'. The advanced capital would not have valorised itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"7.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0009","text":"We already know, in fact, that surplus-value is merely the consequence of the change in value which takes place with v, the part of capital converted into labour-power, that therefore v + m = v + ∆ v (v plus increment of v). But the actual change in value and the ratio in which value changes are obscured by the fact that, as a result of the growth of its variable constituent, the total advanced capital also grows. It was £500 and at the end of the process it is £590.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"7.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0010","text":"The pure analysis of the process therefore requires us to abstract altogether from the part of the value of the product in which only constant capital-value reappears, thus to set the constant capital c = 0, and thereby to apply a law of mathematics, where it operates with variable and constant magnitudes, and the constant magnitude is connected with the variable only by addition or subtraction [Note: “Constant quantities connected to variable by the operations of Addition\n or Substraction disappear by the process of differentiation.” (J. Hind: “Diffe-\n rential Calculus. Cambridge 1831” p. 126.) In fact, the change in magnitude of a constant magnitude is Nothing. Hence the law of differential calculus: differential of a constant magnitude = 0.] .\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"181\", \"facs\": \"#f0200\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"7.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0011","text":"Another difficulty arises from the original form of variable capital. Thus, in the above example C' = £410 constant capital + £90 variable capital + £90 surplus-value. But £90 is a given, hence constant, magnitude, and it therefore seems absurd to treat it as a variable magnitude. But or £90 of variable capital here is in fact only a symbol for the process which this value undergoes. The part of capital advanced in the purchase of labour-power is a definite quantum of objectified labour, hence a constant magnitude of value, just like the value of the labour-power bought. But in the process of production the advanced £90 is replaced by","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"7.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0012","text":"the labour-power in action; living labour replaces dead labour, a flowing magnitude replaces a resting one, a variable magnitude replaces a constant one. The result is the reproduction of v plus increment of v. From the standpoint of capitalist production, this entire course is the self-movement of the originally constant value converted into labour-power. The process and its result are credited to it. If the formula £90 variable capital, or self-valorising value, therefore appears contradictory, it only expresses a contradiction immanent in capitalist production.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"7.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0013","text":"The equation of constant capital with 0 is startling at first sight. Yet one performs it constantly in everyday life. If, for example, someone wishes to calculate England's gain from the cotton industry, he first of all deducts the price of cotton paid to the United States, India, Egypt, etc.; that is, he sets the capital-value merely reappearing in the value of the product = 0.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"182\", \"facs\": \"#f0201\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"7.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0014","text":"Certainly, the relation of surplus-value not only to the part of capital from which it immediately springs and whose change in value it represents, but also to the total advanced capital, has great economic significance. We shall therefore treat this relation in detail in the third book. In order to valorise one part of capital by its conversion into labour-power, another part of capital must be transformed into means of production. For variable capital to function, constant capital must be advanced in appropriate proportions, according to the specific technological character of the labour-process. The circumstance, however, that one needs retorts and other vessels for a chemical process does not prevent one from abstracting from the retort itself in analysis. In so far as value-creation and change in value are considered by themselves, i.e. purely, the means of production, these material forms of constant capital, provide only the material in which the fluid, value-forming force can fix itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"7.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0015","text":"The nature of this material is therefore also indifferent, whether cotton or iron. The value of this material is likewise indifferent. It need only be present in sufficient mass to be able to absorb the quantity of labour to be expended during the process of production. Given this mass, its value may rise or fall, or it may be valueless, like earth and sea; the process of value-creation and change in value is not affected by this.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"7.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0016","text":"Thus we first set the constant part of capital equal to zero. The advanced capital is thereby reduced from c + v to v, and the value of the product + m to the value-product . Given the value-product = £180, in which the labour flowing throughout the whole duration of the process of production is represented, we have to deduct the value of the variable capital = £90 in order to obtain the surplus-value = £90. The number £90 = m here expresses the absolute magnitude of the surplus-value produced. But its proportional magnitude, i.e. the ratio in which the variable capital has valorised itself, is evidently determined by the ratio of surplus-value to variable capital, or is expressed in \\frac{m}{v}. Thus, in the above example, in \\frac{90}{90} = 100 %.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"183\", \"facs\": \"#f0202\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"7.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0017","text":"This proportional valorisation of variable capital, or the proportional magnitude of surplus-value, I call the rate of surplus-value [Note: In the same way as the Englishman uses “rate of profit”, “rate of interest”, etc. It will be seen from Book III that the rate of profit is easy to understand once one knows the laws of surplus-value. By the reverse route one understands ni l’un, ni l’autre.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"7.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0018","text":"We have seen that during one section of the labour-process the worker produces only the value of his labour-power, i.e. the value of his necessary means of subsistence. Since he produces in a condition based upon the social division of labour, he does not produce his means of subsistence directly, but in the form of a particular commodity, yarn, for example, a value equal to the value of his means of subsistence, or to the money with which he buys them. The part of his working day which he spends on this is greater or smaller according to the value of his average daily means of subsistence, hence according to the average daily labour-time required for their production.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"7.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0019","text":"If the magnitude of value of these daily means of subsistence represents, on average, 6 objectified hours of labour, then the worker must work, on average, 6 hours daily in order to produce them. If he did not work for the capitalist, but as an independent producer, then, other circumstances remaining the same, he would still have to work, on average, the same aliquot part of the day in order to produce the value of his labour-power and thereby to obtain the means of subsistence necessary for his own maintenance or continuous reproduction.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"7.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0020","text":"But since, in the part of the working day in which he produces the daily value of labour-power, say 3 sh., he produces only an equivalent for the value already paid for it by the capitalist, and thus, by the newly created value, merely replaces the advanced variable capital-value, this production of value appears as mere reproduction. I therefore call the part of the working day in which this reproduction takes place necessary labour-time, and the labour expended during it necessary labour [Note: Hitherto in this work we have applied the word “necessary labour-time” to the labour-time socially necessary for the production of a commodity in general. From now on, we also use it for the labour-time necessary for the production of the specific commodity labour-power.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"184\", \"facs\": \"#f0203\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"7.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0021","text":"The use of the same technical terms in different senses is inconvenient, but cannot be entirely avoided in any science. Compare, for example, the higher and lower parts of mathematics.] . Necessary for the worker, because independent of the social form of his labour. Necessary for capital and its world, because the worker's continuous existence is their basis.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"7.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0022","text":"The second period of the labour-process, which the worker toils through beyond the limits of necessary labour, does indeed cost him labour, expenditure of labour-power, but it forms no value for him. It forms surplus-value, which smiles upon the capitalist with all the charms of a creation out of nothing. This part of the working day I call surplus-labour-time, and the labour expended in it: surplus-labour (surplus labour). As decisive as it is for the knowledge of value in general to grasp it as mere congealed labour-time, as merely objectified labour, so decisive is it for the knowledge of surplus-value to grasp it as mere congealed surplus-labour-time, as merely objectified surplus-labour. Only the form in which this surplus-labour is squeezed out of the immediate producer, the worker, distinguishes the economic formations of society, e.g.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"7.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0023","text":"the society of slavery from that of wage-labour [Note: With truly Gottschedian ingenuity, Mr Wilhelm\n Thucydides Roscher discovers that, whereas nowadays the formation of surplus-value or\nsurplus-product, and the accumulation connected with it, are owed to the\n “thrift” of the capitalist, who for it “e.g. demands interest”,\n by contrast, “at the lowest stages of culture … the weaker are compelled to\n thrift by the stronger.” (l. c. p. 78.) In order to save\n labour? or non-existent surplus products? Alongside\n genuine ignorance, it is apologetic timidity before a conscientious analysis of\n value and surplus-value, and a perhaps incriminatingly police-illicit result, that a\n Roscher and Co.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"7.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0024","text":"compels [them] to stamp the more or less plausible grounds of justification\n of the capitalist for his appropriation of existing surplus-values\n as the ground of origin of surplus-value itself.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"7.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0025","text":"Since the value of variable capital = the value of the labour-power purchased\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"185\", \"facs\": \"#f0204\"} --> by it, since the value of this labour-power determines the necessary\n part of the working day, while surplus-value, for its part, is determined\n by the surplus part of the working day, it follows: surplus-\n value is related to variable capital as surplus-\n labour to necessary labour, or the rate of surplus-value\n\\frac{m}{v} = \\frac{Surplus-labour}{Necessary Labour}. Both proportions represent the same relation\n ship in different form, once in the form of objectified,\n the other time in the form of fluid labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"7.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0026","text":"The rate of surplus-value is therefore the exact expression for\n the degree of exploitation of labour-power by capital\n or of the worker by the capitalist.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"7.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0027","text":"According to our assumption, the value of the product =\n + , the capital advanced = 500 l. Since the surplus-value is 90 and\n the capital advanced 500, according to the usual method\n of calculation one would obtain that the rate of surplus-value (which is\n confused with the rate of profit) = 18%, a ratio whose\n lowliness might move Mr Carey and other harmonisers. In\n fact, however, the rate of surplus-value is not = \\frac{m}{C} or \\frac{m}{c + v}, but = \\frac{m}{v},\n thus not \\frac{90}{500}, but \\frac{90}{90} = 100%, more than five times the\napparent degree of exploitation. Although, in the given case,\n we do not know the absolute magnitude of the working day, nor\n the period of the labour-process (day, week, etc.), nor finally\n the number of workers which the variable capital of 90 l.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"7.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0028","text":"simultaneously sets\n in motion, the rate of surplus-value \\frac{m}{v}, through its convertibility into \\frac{Surplus-labour}{Necessary Labour}, shows us exactly the relation of the two component\n parts of the working day to one another. It is 100%. Thus the\n worker worked one half of the day for himself and the other for the capi-\n talist.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"7.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0029","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"186\", \"facs\": \"#f0205\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"7.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0030","text":"The method for calculating the rate of surplus-value is thus, briefly\n stated, this: We take the whole value of the product and set\n the constant capital-value that merely reappears in it\n equal to zero. The remaining sum of value is the sole value-product actually\n produced in the process of formation of the commodity. If the surplus-value\n is given, we subtract it from this value-product in order to find the variable\n capital. Conversely, if the latter is given and we seek the\n surplus-value. If both are given, only the final operation remains to be performed,\n to calculate the ratio of surplus-value to variable capital,\n\\frac{m}{v}.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"7.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0031","text":"However simple the method, it nevertheless seems appropriate to train the reader\n in the mode of apprehension underlying it and unfamiliar to him\n by means of some examples.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"7.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0032","text":"First, an example from the spinning industry. The data belong\n to the year 1860. Circumstances irrelevant for our purpose have been\n omitted. A factory consumed weekly 11,500 lbs. of cotton,\n of which 1500 were waste. At 7 d. per pound of cotton, the raw\n material thus amounts to 336 pounds sterling. It set 10,000 spindles in motion, at\n 1 pound sterling per spindle = 10,000 pounds sterling, of which the annual wear and tear,\n at 12½%, amounted to 1250 pounds sterling, thus 24 pounds sterling per week; the weekly\n wear and tear of the steam-engine, 20 pounds sterling; the weekly expenditure\n for auxiliary materials, coal, oil, etc., 40 pounds sterling. The weekly\n wage amounted to 70 pounds sterling and the selling price of the lb. of yarn was 1⅒ sh.,\n thus that of the 10,000 lbs. of yarn weekly, 550 pounds sterling. The constant\n value-part of capital therefore amounts to 420 pounds sterling. We set it = 0,\n since it plays no part in the weekly formation of value.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"7.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0033","text":"The actual\nweekly value-product which remains is therefore = 130 pounds sterling.\n From this we subtract the variable capital of 70 pounds sterling paid to\n the workers; there remains surplus-value of 60 pounds sterling. The rate of\n surplus-value, \\frac{m}{v}, \\frac{60}{70}, is therefore approximately 86%. This percentage expresses\n the degree of exploitation of labour-power or the degree of valorisation of\n variable capital. If we assume that 10 hours were worked in that factory\n on the daily average, necessary labour amounted to approximately 5\\frac{5}{13},\n and surplus-labour to 4\\frac{8}{13} hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"7.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0034","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"187\", \"facs\": \"#f0206\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"7.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0035","text":"For the year 1815, assuming a wheat price\n of 80 sh. per quarter, and an average yield of 22 bushels\n per acre, so that the acre yields 11 pounds sterling, Jacob gives the following\n calculation, very defective owing to the prior compensation of various\n items, but sufficient for our purpose.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"7.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0036","text":"Value production per acre.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"7.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0037","text":"Seed (wheat) 1 pound sterling 9 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"7.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0038","text":"Manure 2 pounds sterling 10 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"7.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0039","text":"Wages 3 pounds sterling 10 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"7.39","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0040","text":"Total: 7 pounds sterling 9 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"7.40","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0041","text":"Tithes, Rates, Taxes 1 pound sterling 1 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"7.41","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0042","text":"Rent 1 pound sterling 8 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"7.42","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0043","text":"Farmer’s profit and interest 1 pound sterling 2 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"7.43","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0044","text":"Total: 3 pounds sterling 11 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"7.44","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0045","text":"Surplus-value, always on the assumption that the price of the\n product = its value, is here distributed among the various headings\n profit, interest, tithes, etc. These headings are here indifferent.\n We add them together and obtain a surplus-value of\n 3 l. 11 sh. We set the 3 l. 19 sh. for seed and manure\n equal to zero as the constant capital-part. There remains advanced va-\n riable capital of 3 l. 10 sh., in place of which an equivalent of\nhas been produced. Thus\n\\frac{m}{v} = \\frac{3 l. 11 sh.}{3 l. 10 sh.}, more than 100%. The worker devotes more than\n half of his working day to the production of a surplus-value which various\n persons distribute among themselves under various pretexts [Note: The calculations given serve only as illustration. For it is namely\n assumed that prices = values.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"7.45","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0046","text":"In Book III we shall see that\n this equation, even for average prices, is not made in this\n simple manner.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"7.46","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0047","text":"Surplus-value presents itself in a surplus product (sur-\n plusproduce).","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"7.47","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0048","text":"We previously assumed that the spinner’s twelve-hour working day consists of 6 hours of necessary labour and 6 hours of surplus-\nlabour, that it transforms 20 lbs. of cotton into 20 lbs. of yarn and adds\nvalue to them amounting to 6 sh., that furthermore 1 lb. of cotton costs 1 sh. and the\nmeans of labour consumed during the whole process cost 4 sh., thus\nthe value of the total product 30 sh. and that of a lb. of yarn 1 sh. 6 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"7.48","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0049","text":"Every pound of yarn represents the same use-value. Each is\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"188\", \"facs\": \"#f0207\"} --> the product of the combination of the same raw material, cotton, with the\nsame productive labour, spinning, mediated by the same means of\nlabour. The value of each individual pound of yarn likewise displays the same\ncomposition: 1 sh. for cotton, 2⅖ d. for consumed\nmeans of labour, 1⅘ d., in which necessary labour, and 1⅘ d., in which surplus-\nlabour is embodied.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"7.49","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0050","text":"Considered separately in itself or as aliquot parts of the total product,\ndeterminate quantities of yarn remain always formations of the same\nproductive labour, of spinning. From another point of\nview, however, the position of the partial product changes wholly\nand entirely, according as it is considered independently or in connection with the total\nproduct, as a partial product or as a product-part.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"7.50","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0051","text":"One pound of yarn costs 1 sh. 6 d., and, disregarding waste, the\nlb. of cotton spun in it costs 1 sh., thus ⅔ of its value. Hence\n⅔ lb. of yarn = 1 lb. of cotton and 13⅓ lbs. of yarn = 20 lbs. of\ncotton. The 13⅓ lbs. of yarn do indeed contain only 13⅓ lbs. of cot-\nton to the value of 13⅓ sh., but their additional value of 6⅔ sh.\nforms an equivalent for the cotton spun in the surplus 6⅔ lbs. of yarn.\nThe 13⅓ lbs. of yarn thus represent all the cotton spun in the total\nproduct of 20 lbs. of yarn, the raw material of the total product,\nbut nothing further. It is as though the wool had been plucked out of the other\n6⅔ lbs. of yarn and all the wool of the total product compressed together into 13⅓ lbs. of yarn. On the other hand,\nthe spinning labour contained in the 13⅓ lbs.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"7.51","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0052","text":"of yarn, and the value-\npart added by the consumed means of labour, are themselves re-\nmoved from them and transferred to the product-part of 6⅔ lbs. of yarn\nlying beside them. In the same way, a part of these remaining\n6⅔ lbs. of yarn—namely 2⅔ lbs.—can again be conceived as the mere representation of the\nmeans of labour consumed in the total product, to the value of\n4 sh. Eight tenths of the total product, or 16 lbs.\nof yarn, although bodily, considered as use-value, as yarn,\njust as much formations of spinning labour as the remaining \\frac{2}{10} of the product,\n4 lbs. of yarn, therefore in this connection contain no spinning labour,\nno labour absorbed during the spinning process itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"7.52","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0053","text":"It is as\nthough they had transformed themselves into yarn without spinning, and as though their yarn-form\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"189\", \"facs\": \"#f0208\"} --> were pure sham and deception. In fact, when the capitalist sells them for\n24 sh. and therewith buys back his means of production, it appears that the\n16 lbs. of yarn are only cotton, spindle, coal etc. in disguise.\nThe remaining 4 lbs. of yarn now in their turn contain not an atom of\nraw material and means of labour. What of these was contained in them had\nalready been hollowed out and incorporated into the first 16 lbs. of yarn. The 4 lbs. of yarn\ntherefore contain not an atom of the labour expended in the production of cotton, machin-\nery, coal etc. Their value of 6 sh. is pure materialization of the 12 hours of labour expended by the spinner himself. The\nspinning labour embodied in the product of 20 lbs. of yarn is now concentrated\nin 4 lbs.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"7.53","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0054","text":"of yarn, in ⅕ of the product. It is as though the spinner had\nwoven this spun yarn of 4 lbs. in the air, or in cotton and with\nspindles which, existing by nature without the addition of human labour,\nadd no value to the product. Of these 4 lbs. of yarn, finally,\none half embodies only necessary labour of 6 hours, the\nother—the last 2 lbs. of yarn—only surplus-labour. Only this\nlast part of the total product forms the surplus product.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"7.54","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0055","text":"The total product of 20 lbs. of yarn can therefore be broken down as follows:\nTotal product of 20 lbs. of yarn, worth 30 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"7.55","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0056","text":"|\n\nOf the total product of 20 lbs. of yarn, 16 lbs. or ⅘ represent\nonly constant capital, while only ⅕ or 4 lbs. represent the labour\nexpended in the spinning process itself. And yet the 20 lbs. of yarn form the pro-\nduct of twelve hours of spinning labour. Here there again appears the dis-\ntinction between labour process and valorization process. What is already known is repeated,\nnamely that the value of the product of the working day,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"190\", \"facs\": \"#f0209\"} --> i.e. the product-value, is greater than the daily value-pro-\nduct. Thus the value of the 20 lbs. of yarn produced daily = 30 sh.,\nbut its part of value produced during the day only = 6 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"7.56","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0057","text":"The representation just\ngiven differs from the earlier one in that the functionally or conceptually distinct con-\nstituent parts of product-value are expressed in proportional parts\nof the product itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"7.57","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0058","text":"This decomposition of the product—the result of the\nproduction process—into a quantity of product that represents only the labour\ncontained in the means of production, another quantity that\nrepresents only the necessary labour added in the production process,\nand a final quantity of product that represents only the surplus-\nlabour added in the same process, is as simple as it is important, as its\nlater application to complicated and still unsolved problems will show.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"7.58","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0059","text":"We have just considered the total product as the finished result of the\ntwelve-hour working day. But we can also accompany it in its process of\nformation, and yet represent the partial products as functionally\ndistinct parts of the product.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"7.59","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0060","text":"The spinner produces 20 lbs. of yarn in 12 hours, hence in one\nhour 1⅔ and in 8 hours 13⅓ lbs., thus a partial product equal to the total\nvalue of the cotton spun during the whole working day.\nIn the same way, the partial product of the following hour and 36 minutes\n= 2⅔ lbs. of yarn and therefore represents the value of the means of production\nconsumed during the 12 hours of labour. Likewise, in the following hour and\n12 minutes the spinner produces 2 lbs. of yarn = 3 sh., a product-value equal to the whole\nvalue-product which he creates in 6 hours of necessary labour.\nFinally, in the last \\frac{5}{4} hours he likewise produces 2 lbs.\nof yarn, whose value is equal to the surplus-value produced by his half-day’s\nsurplus-labour. This kind of calculation serves the English\nmanufacturer for domestic use, and he will, e.g.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"7.60","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0061","text":"say that in the\nfirst 8 hours or ⅔ of the working day he gets back his cotton\netc. One sees that the formula is correct, indeed the first formula,\ntranslated from space, where the parts of the product lie finished alongside one\nanother, into time, where they follow one another. The formula can\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"191\", \"facs\": \"#f0210\"} --> however also be accompanied by very barbarous notions, particularly\nin heads which are as practically interested in the valorization process\nas they have an interest in theoretically misunderstanding it. Thus one may imagine\nthat our spinner, e.g.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"7.61","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0062","text":"in the first 8 hours\nof his working day produces or replaces the value of the cotton, in the following hour\nand 36 minutes the value of the consumed means of labour, in the following\nhour and 12 minutes the value of wages, and devotes only the much-celebrated “last hour” to the manufacturer\nfor the production of surplus-value. The spinner is thus saddled with the dou-\nble miracle of producing cotton, spindle, steam-engine, coal,\noil etc. at the same moment at which he spins with them, and of making five\nsuch working days out of one working day of a given degree of intensity. In our case,\nnamely, the production of the raw material and the means of labour requires 4 twelve-hour working days\nand their transformation into yarn another twelve-hour working day.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"7.62","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0063","text":"That rapacity believes in such miracles and never lacks a doctrinaire\nsycophant to prove them, the following example will show.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"7.63","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0064","text":"On a fine morning in the year 1836, Nassau\nW. Senior, renowned for his economic science and his “fine style,” a sort of Clauren among English econo-\nmists, was summoned from Oxford to Manchester, in order, instead of teaching political\n economy in Oxford, to learn it in Manchester. The manufacturers chose\nhim as their prize-fighter against the Factory\nAct recently passed and the Ten Hours agitation which reached still further beyond it. With\ncustomary practical acumen they had recognized that Mr. Pro-\nfessor “wanted a good deal of finishing”. They therefore sent for him to\nManchester. Mr. Professor, for his part, styled the lesson received from the manufacturers\nin Manchester in the pamphlet: “Let-\nters on the Factory Act, as it affects the cotton manu-\nfacture. London 1837.” Here one may read, among other things, the following edi-\nfying matter:","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"7.64","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0065","text":"“Under the present law, no factory employing persons\nunder 18 years of age can work more than 11½ hours daily,\ni.e. 12 hours during the first 5 days and 9 hours\non Saturday. The following analysis (!) now shows that in such a fac-\ntory the whole net profit is derived from the last hour.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"192\", \"facs\": \"#f0211\"} --> A manufacturer lays out £100,000—£80,000\nin factory buildings and machinery, £20,000 in raw material and wages.\nThe annual income of the factory, assuming that the capital turns over once annually and gross profit amounts to 15 %,\nmust amount to commodities to the value of £115,000 …\nOf these £115,000, each of the 23 half working\nhours daily produces \\frac{5}{115} or \\frac{1}{23}.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"7.65","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0066","text":"Of these \\frac{23}{23} working hours,\nwhich constitute the whole £115,000 (constituting the whole 115,000\nPd. St.), \\frac{20}{23}, i.e. 100,000 of the 115,000, merely replace capi-\ntal; \\frac{1}{23} or £5000 of the £15,000 gross profit (!) replace\nthe wear and tear of the factory and machinery. The remaining\n\\frac{2}{23}, the last two half-hours of each day, produce\nthe net profit of 10 %. If therefore, with prices remaining the same, the factory were permitted to work 13 hours instead of 11½, then, with\nan addition of approximately £2600 to circulating capital, the\nnet profit would be more than doubled. Conversely, if the working\nhours were reduced daily by 1 hour, net profit would disappear;\nif by 1½ hours, gross profit as well [Note: Senior l. c. p. 12, 13.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"7.66","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0067","text":"We shall not enter into the curiosities irrele-\nvant to our purpose, e.g. the assertion that the manufacturers count the replacement\nof worn-out machinery etc., thus of a constituent part of capital,\nas profit, gross or net, dirty or pure. Nor shall we enter into the correctness or incorrectness of the numerical statements. That they are worth no more\nthan the so-called “analysis,” Leonhard Horner proved in: “A\nLetter to Mr. Senior etc. Lond. 1837.” Leonhard Horner, one of the\nFactory Inquiry Commissioners of 1833, and factory inspector, indeed factory censor, until 1859,\nwon immortal merits for the English working class. Apart from the embittered manufacturers, he waged a\nlifelong struggle with the ministers, for whom it was incomparably more important to count “the votes” of the factory masters\nin the House of Commons than the working hours of the\n“hands” in the factory.] !”","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"7.67","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0068","text":"And Mr. Professor calls that an “analysis!” If he believed\nthe manufacturers’ lament that the workers waste the best time of the day\nin the production, hence the reproduction or replacement, of the value\nof buildings, machinery, cotton, coal etc., then every analysis was superfluous. He\nhad simply to answer: Gentlemen! If you have them work 10 hours instead of 11½, then, with\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"193\", \"facs\": \"#f0212\"} --> other circumstances remaining the same, the daily consumption of cotton,\nmachinery etc. will decrease by 1½ hours. You therefore gain exactly\nas much as you lose. Your workers will in future waste 1½ hours\nless on the reproduction or replacement of the capital-value advanced.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"7.68","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0069","text":"If he did not believe them at their word, but as an expert\nconsidered an analysis necessary, then above all, in a question\nwhich turns exclusively upon the relation of net profit to the magnitude\nof the working day, he had to request the gentlemen manufacturers not to muddle together machinery and\nfactory buildings, raw material and labour, but kindly to\nplace the constant capital contained in factory buildings, machinery, raw\nmaterial etc. on one side, the capital advanced in\nwages on the other side. If it then emerged, say, according to the manufacturers’ calculation,\nthat the worker reproduces or replaces wages in \\frac{2}{23} of the working day,\nor in one hour, then the analyst had to continue:","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"7.69","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0070","text":"According to your statement, the worker produces his wages in the penultimate hour\n and your surplus-value, or net profit, in the last. Since he produces equal values in equal periods of time, the\n product of the penultimate hour has the same value as that of the last. He\n further produces value only insofar as he expends labour, and the quan-\n tity of his labour is measured by his labour-time. According to your statement,\n this amounts to 11½ hours per day. One part of these\n 11½ hours he uses for the production or replacement of his\n wages, the other for the production of your net profit. He does nothing else\n during the working day. But since, according to the statement, his wage\n and the surplus-value supplied by him are values of equal magnitude, he ob-\n viously produces his wages in 5¾ hours and your net\n profit in another 5¾ hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"7.70","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0071","text":"Since, furthermore, the value of the two-\n hour yarn product is equal to the sum of the values of his wages\n plus your net profit, this yarn-value must be measured by 11½\n hours of labour, the product of the penultimate hour by\n 5¾ hours of labour, that of the last likewise. We now come to a\n ticklish point. So pay attention! The penultimate working hour is an\n ordinary working hour, like the first. Ni plus, ni moins. How, then, can\n the spinner produce in One working hour a yarn-value repre-\n senting 5¾ working hours? In fact, he performs\n [Running matter: I. 13]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"194\", \"facs\": \"#f0213\"} --> no such miracle. What he produces in One working hour in use-\n value is a definite quantity of yarn.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"7.71","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0072","text":"The\nvalue of this yarn is measured by 5¾ working hours, of which 4¾\n are contained, without any action on his part, in the means of production\n consumed hourly—cotton, machinery, etc.; \\frac{4}{4}, or one hour, is added by him-\n self. Since, then, his wages are produced in 5¾ hours and the yarn-product of One spinning hour likewise contains 5¾\n hours, it is by no means witchcraft that the value-pro-\n duct of his 5¾ spinning hours is equal to the product-\n value of One spinning hour. But you are entirely on the wrong\n track if you imagine that he loses a single atom of time from his working day\n in the reproduction or “replacement” of the values of cotton,\n machinery, etc. Because his labour makes yarn out of cotton and spin-\n dle, because he spins, the value of cotton and spindle passes of itself\n to the yarn.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"7.72","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0073","text":"This is owing to the\nquality of his labour, not to its quantity. It is true\n that in one hour he transfers more cotton-value, etc., to yarn\n than in ½ hour, but only because in 1 hour he spins more cotton\n than in ½ hour. You therefore understand: your expression that the worker produces in\n the penultimate hour the value of his wages and in the last\n the net profit, means nothing further than that in the yarn-pro-\n duct of two hours of his working day, whether they stand at the beginning or\n at the end, 11½ working hours are embodied, precisely as many hours\n as his entire working day numbers. And the expression that he\n produces his wages in the first 5¾ hours and your net profit in the last\n 5¾ hours, again means nothing other than that you pay for the first 5¾ hours\n and do not pay for the last 5¾ hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"7.73","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0074","text":"I speak of payment for labour, instead of labour-\n power, in order to speak your slang. Now, gentlemen, if you compare the ratio\n of the labour-time you pay for to the labour-time you do not pay for, you\n will find that it is half a day to half a day, hence 100%,\n which is certainly a pretty percentage. Nor is there the least doubt\n that if you make your “hands” slave away for 13 hours instead of 11½,\n and, as one egg resembles another, reckon the surplus 1½ hours as mere surplus-labour,\n the latter will grow from 5¾ hours to 7¼ hours, and the rate of sur-\n plus-value therefore from 100% to 126\\frac{2}{23}%. On the other hand, you are far too\n wildly sanguine if you hope that, through the addition of 1½\n hours, it will rise from 100% to 200%, and indeed to more than 200%, i.e.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"7.74","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0075","text":"“more than double.” On the other hand—the human heart is a\n strange thing, especially when a man carries his heart in his purse—\n you are far too madly pessimistic if you fear that, with the re-\n duction of the working day from 11½ to 10½ hours, your entire\n net profit will go to pieces. Not at all. All other circumstances\n being assumed to remain the same, surplus-labour will fall from 5¾ to 4¾\n hours, which still gives a quite considerable rate of sur-\n plus-value, namely 82\\frac{14}{23}%. But the fateful “last\n hour,” about which you have fabulated more than the Chiliasts about\n the end of the world, is “all bosh.”","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"7.75","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0076","text":"Its loss will cost neither you your “net\n profit” nor the children of both sexes worked by you their “purity of soul” [Note: While Senior proved that the manufacturers’ “last working hour”\nwas the condition upon which the net profit of the manufacturers, the existence of the English cotton industry,\n and England’s greatness in the world market depended, Dr Andrew Ure, conversely, proved in\n a bargain that if factory children and young persons under 18, instead of being made to slave away for a full 12 hours in the warm and pure moral air of the factory room,\n were thrust “one hour” earlier into the cold-hearted and frivolous outer world,\n idleness and vice would have to rob them of their salvation. Since 1848 the factory inspectors\n have not tired, in their half-yearly “Reports,” of teasing the manufacturers with “the last,” “the fateful hour.”\n Thus Mr Howell says, in his factory report of 31","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"7.76","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0077","text":"May 1855: “If the\n following ingenious calculation (he cites Senior) were correct, every cotton-\n factory in the United Kingdom would have operated at a loss since 1850.” (Reports of\n the Insp. of Fact. for the half year ending 30th April 1855, pp.\n 19, 20.) When, in the year 1848, the Ten Hours Bill passed through Parliament, the\n manufacturers induced some ordinary workers in the rural flax-spinning mills scattered\n between the counties of Dorset and Somerset to submit a counter-petition,\n which says, inter alia: “Your petitioners, parents, believe that an additional\n hour of leisure can have no other effect than to demorali-\n se their children, for idleness is the beginning of all vice.” The factory report of\n 31","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"7.77","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0078","text":"October 1848 remarks upon this: “The atmosphere of the flax-spinning mills in which\n the children of these virtuously tender parents work is saturated with such\n innumerable particles of dust and fibre from the raw material that it is extraordinarily\n unpleasant to spend even 10 minutes in the spinning rooms, for you cannot do so\n without the most painful sensation, as eye, ear, nostrils] . Once your “last little\n [Running matter: 13*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"196\", \"facs\": \"#f0215\"} -->hour” really strikes, think of the Professor of Oxford. And now:\n In a better world I wish for myself more of your esteemed company.\n Adio [Note: Yet the professor had profited somewhat from his Manchester excursion!","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"7.78","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0079","text":"In the “Letters on the Factory Act,” the entire net-\nprofit, “profit” and “interest,” and even ‘something more,’ depends upon an\nunpaid hour of the worker’s labour! A year earlier, in his] ! … The signal of the “last\n hour” discovered by Senior in 1836 was sounded anew on 15 April 1848,\n polemically against the Ten Hours Act, by James Wilson, one of the chief mandarins\n of economics, in the “London Economist.”","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"7.79","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0080","text":"[Note: and mouth immediately fill with clouds of flax dust, from which there is no escape.\n The work itself demands, because of the feverish haste of the machinery, an unceasing ex-\n penditure of skill and movement, under the control of unwearying attention; and it seems somewhat harsh to allow parents to apply the expression “idling” to their\n own children, who, after deduction of meal-times, are chained for a full\n 10 hours to such employment, in such an atmosphere\n … These children work longer than the farm labourers in the neighbouring villages…\n Such unfeeling twaddle about “idleness and vice” must be branded as the purest cant\n and the most shameless hypocrisy … That part of the pub-\n lic which, about twelve years ago, rose up against the assurance with which it was\n publicly and in all seriousness proclaimed, under the sanction of high authority, that\n the whole “net profit”","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"7.80","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0081","text":"of the manufacturer flowed from the “last hour”\n of labour, and that the reduction of the working day by one hour had to destroy net profit—\n that part of the public, we say, will scarcely believe its eyes when it now finds\n that the original discovery concerning the virtues of the “last hour” has since been so\n far improved as to include “morality” and “profit” equally; so that if the duration of children’s labour were reduced to a full\n 10 hours, their morality, together with the net profit of those who employ them,\n would go to the devil, both dependent on this last, this fatal hour.”\n (“Repts of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1848,” p. 101.) The same\n factory report then gives examples of the “morality” and “virtue” of these gentlemen\n manufacturers, of the tricks, whistlings, enticements, threats, falsifications,\n etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"7.81","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0082","text":"which they employed in order to have petitions of this sort signed by a few quite de-\n praved workers, and then to palm them off on Parliament as petitions of a whole\n branch of industry, of entire counties. — It remains highly charac-\n teristic of the present state of the so-called economic “science” that neither\n Senior himself, who later, to his credit, vigorously came out in favour of factory legislation,\n nor his original and later opponents, knew how to resolve\n the fallacies of the “original discovery.” They appealed to actual experience. The why and wherefore\n remained a mystery.]","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"7.82","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0083","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"197\", \"facs\": \"#f0216\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"7.83","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0084","text":"Just as the rate of surplus-value is determined by the ratio\n of surplus-value, not to the total sum of the advanced capi-\n tal, but to its variable component laid out in labour-power,\n so the magnitude of the surplus-product is determined, not by\n its ratio to the remainder of the total product, but exclusively\n by its ratio to the part of the product in which necessary labour is represented. Just as the\n production of surplus-value is the determining purpose of capitalist\n production, so it is not the absolute mass of the product, but only\n that of the surplus-product, which measures the degree of wealth [Note: “For an individual with a capital of £20,000, whose pro-\nfits amount to £2,000 annually, it would be a matter of complete indifference whether\nhis capital employed 100 or 1,000 workers, whether the commodities produced sold\nfor £10,000 or £20,000,","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"7.84","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0085","text":"provided always that his profits\nin all cases did not fall below £2,000. Is not the real interest of a\nnation the same? Provided its real net income, its rents and\nprofits remain the same, is it of the least importance whether the\nnation consists of 10 or 12 million inhabitants?” (Ric. l. c. p. 416.)\nLong before Ricardo, the fanatic of the surplus-product, Arthur Young, an otherwise\nlong-winded, uncritical writer whose reputation stands in inverse proportion to his merit, said,\ninter alia: “What use would an entire province be in a modern kingdom if its soil, in the manner of ancient Rome, were cultivated by small, independent farmers, however well, if you please?","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"7.85","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0086","text":"For what purpose, apart from the sole one of producing human beings (‘the\nmere purpose of breeding men’), which in and for itself has no purpose at all”\n(‘is a most useless purpose’). Arthur Young: “Political Arith-\nmetic etc. London 1774,” p. 47.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"7.86","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0087","text":"The sum of necessary labour and surplus-\n labour, the time in which the worker merely reproduces the value of his labour-power\n and the time in which he produces surplus-value, determines the ab-\n solute magnitude of his labour-time—the working day (work-\n ing day).","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"7.87","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0088","text":"[Note: in the “Out-\nlines of Political Economy,” composed for the common good of Oxford students and educated philistines, he had, in opposition to Ricardo’s determination of value\nby labour-time, “discovered” that profit derives from the labour\nof the capitalist and interest from his asceticism, his “absti-\nnence.” The notion itself was old, but the word “abstinence”\nnew. Mr Roscher has correctly rendered it as “Enthaltung” [forbearance]. His\ncompatriots less versed in Latin—innkeepers, village headmen, and other\nMichels—have monkified it into “Entsagung” [renunciation].]","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"7.88","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0089","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"198\", \"facs\": \"#f0217\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"7.89","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DRD:P0089"]}],"source_digest":"3d9629e471f893decaf15b3d96cc38639d31ebd03ddf51b89c8a9c13c85da4e5","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR","title":"4) Der Arbeitstag. · reading part 1/2","order":8,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_4DAR","text":"## 4) Der Arbeitstag.\n\n\n\n\nWir gingen von der Voraussetzung aus, dass die Arbeitskraft zu\n ihrem Werthe gekauft und verkauft wird. Ihr Werth, wie der jeder\n andern Waare, wird bestimmt durch die zu ihrer Produktion nöthige Ar-\n beitszeit. Erheischt also die Durchschnittssumme der täglichen Lebens-\n mittel des Arbeiters zu ihrer Produktion 6 Stunden täglich, so muss er\n im Durchschnitt 6 Stunden per Tag arbeiten, um seine Arbeitskraft täglich\n zu produziren oder den in ihrem Verkauf erhaltenen Werth zu repro-\n duziren. Der nothwendige Theil seines Arbeitstages beträgt\n dann 6 Stunden, und ist daher, unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen,\n eine gegebene Grösse. Aber damit ist die Grösse des Arbeits-\n tags selbst noch nicht gegeben.\n\n\n\n\nNehmen wir an, die Linie a--------------b stelle die Dauer oder Länge\n der noth wendigen Arbeitszeit vor, sage 6 Stunden. Je nachdem\n die Arbeit über a b um 1, 3 oder 6 Stunden u. s. w. verlängert wird, er-\n halten wir die 3 verschiedenen Linien:\n\n\n[Figure in source: ]  die drei verschiedne Arbeitstage von 7, 9 und 12 Stunden vorstellen. Die\n Verlängerungslinie b c stellt die Länge der Surplusarbeitszeit vor. Da der\n Arbeitstag = a b + b c oder a c ist, variirt er mit der variablen Grösse\n b c. Da uns a b gegeben ist, kann das Verhältniss von b c zu a b\n stets gemessen werden. Es beträgt in Arbeitstag I ⅙, in Arbeitstag II\n\\frac{3}{6}, und in Arbeitstag III \\frac{6}{6} von a b. Da ferner die Proportion\n\\frac{Surplusarbeitszeit}{Nothwendige Arbeitszeit} die Rate des Mehrwerths bestimmt, ist letztere\n gegeben durch jenes Verhältniss. Sie beträgt in den drei verschiedenen\n Arbeitstagen respektive 16⅔, 50 und 100 %. Umgekehrt würde die\nRate des Mehrwerths allein uns nicht die Grösse des Arbeits-\n tags geben. Wäre sie z. B. gleich 100 %, so könnte der Arbeitstag\n 8, 10, 12stündig u. s. w. sein. Sie würde anzeigen, dass die zwei Be-\n standtheile des Arbeitstags, nothwendige Arbeit und Mehrarbeit, gleich\n gross sind, aber nicht wie gross jeder dieser Theile.\n\n\n\n\nDer Arbeitstag ist also keine constante, sondern eine variable\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"199\", \"facs\": \"#f0218\"} --> Grösse. Einer seiner Theile ist zwar bestimmt durch die zur bestän-\n digen Reproduktion des Arbeiters selbst erheischte Arbeitszeit, aber seine\n Gesammtgrösse wechselt mit der Länge oder Dauer der Mehrarbeit. Der\n Arbeitstag ist daher bestimmbar, aber an und für sich unbestimmt [Note: „A day’s labour is vague, it may be long or short.“ „An Essay on\n Trade and Commerce, containing Observations on Taxation etc.\nLondon 1770“, p. 73.] .\n\n\n\n\nObgleich nun der Arbeitstag keine feste, sondern eine fliessende Grösse\n ist, kann er andrerseits nur innerhalb gewisser Schranken vari-\n iren. Seine Minimalschranke ist jedoch unbestimmbar. Allerdings,\n setzen wir die Verlängerungslinie b c, oder die Mehrarbeit, = O, so er-\n halten wir eine Minimalschranke, den Theil des Tages nämlich, den der\n Arbeiter nothwendig zu seiner Selbsterhaltung arbeiten muss. Auf Grund-\n lage der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise kann die nothwendige Arbeit\n aber immer nur einen Theil seines Arbeitstages bilden, der Arbeitstag\n sich also nie auf diess Minimum verkürzen. Dagegen besitzt der Arbeitstag\n eine Maximalschranke. Er kann über eine gewisse Grenze hinaus\n nicht verlängert werden. Diese Maximalschranke ist doppelt bestimmt.\n Einmal durch die physische Schranke der Arbeitskraft. Ein\n Mensch kann während des natürlichen Tags von 24 Stunden nur ein be-\n stimmtes Quantum Lebenskraft verausgaben und das Mass dieser Kraft-\n verausgabung bildet ein Mass für seine physisch mögliche Arbeitszeit. So\n kann ein Pferd Tag aus, Tag ein, nur 8 Stunden arbeiten. Während\n eines Theils des Tags muss die Kraft ruhen, schlafen, während eines an-\n dern Theils hat der Mensch andere physische Bedürfnisse zu befriedigen,\n sich zu nähren, reinigen, kleiden u. s. w. Ausser dieser rein physi-\n schen Schranke stösst die Verlängerung des Arbeitstags auf mora-\n lische Schranken. Der Arbeiter braucht Zeit zur Befriedigung gei-\n stiger und sozialer Bedürfnisse, deren Umfang und Zahl durch den allge-\n meinen Kulturzustand bestimmt sind. Die Variation des Arbeitstags be-\n wegt sich daher innerhalb absoluter physischer und mehr oder minder\n relativer sozialer Schranken. Beide Schranken sind aber sehr elastischer\n Natur und erlauben den grössten Spielraum. So finden wir Arbeitstage\n von 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 18 und mehr Stunden, also von der verschie-\n densten Länge.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"200\", \"facs\": \"#f0219\"} -->\n\nDer Kapitalist hat die Arbeitskraft zu ihrem Tageswerth gekauft.\n Ihm gehört ihr Gebrauchswerth während eines Arbeitstags. Er hat\n also das Recht erlangt, den Arbeiter während eines Tags für sich arbei-\n ten zu lassen. Aber was ist ein Arbeitstag [Note: Diese Frage ist unendlich wichtiger als die berühmte Frage Sir Robert\n Peel’s an die Birminghamer Handelskammer: „What is a pound?“, eine\n Frage, die nur gestellt werden konnte, weil Peel über die Natur des Geldes eben\n so unklar war als die „little shilling men“ von Birmingham.] ? Jedenfalls weni-\n ger als ein natürlicher Lebenstag. Um wie viel? Der Kapitalist hat\n seine eigne Ansicht über diess ultima Thule, die nothwendige\n Schranke des Arbeitstags. Als Kapitalist ist er nur personifizirtes\n Kapital. Seine Seele ist die Kapitalseele. Das Kapital hat aber einen\n einzigen Lebenstrieb, den Trieb, sich zu verwerthen, Mehrwerth zu schaf-\n fen, mit seinem constanten Theil, den Produktionsmitteln, die grösst-\n mögliche Masse Mehrarbeit einzusaugen [Note: „Es ist die Aufgabe des Kapitalisten mit dem verausgabten Kapi-\n tal die grösstmögliche Summe Arbeit herauszuschlagen.“ („D’obtenir du\n capital dépensé la plus forte somme de travail possible.“) J. G.\nCourcelle-Seneuil: „Traité théorique et pratique des entre-\n prises industrielles. 2ème édit. Paris 1857“, p. 63.] . Das Kapital ist verstorbene\n Arbeit, die sich nur vampyrmässig belebt durch Einsaugung lebendiger\n Arbeit und um so mehr lebt, je mehr sie davon einsaugt. Die Zeit, wäh-\n rend deren der Arbeiter arbeitet, ist die Zeit, während deren der Kapita-\n list die von ihm gekaufte Arbeitskraft consumirt [Note: „An Hour’s Labour lost in a day is a prodigious injury to a commercial\n state.“ „There is a very great consumption of luxuries among the\n labouring poor of this kingdom; particularly among the manufacturing populace:\n by which they also consume their time, the most fatal of consump-\n tions.“ An Essay on Trade and Commerce etc. p. 47 u. 153.] . Consumirt der\n Arbeiter seine disponible Zeit für sich selbst, so bestiehlt er den Kapita-\n listen [Note: „Si le manouvrier libre prend un instant de repos, l’économie sordide qui\n le suit des yeux avec inquiétude, prétend qu’il la vole.“ N. Linguet: „Théo-\n rie des Lois Civiles etc. London 1767“, t. II, p. 466.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDer Kapitalist beruft sich also auf das Gesetz des Waaren-\n austauschs. Er, wie jeder andre Käufer, sucht den grösstmöglichen\n Nutzen aus dem Gebrauchswerth seiner Waare herauszuschlagen. Plötz-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"201\", \"facs\": \"#f0220\"} --> lich aber erhebt sich die Stimme des Arbeiters, die im Sturm und Drang\n des Produktionsprozesses verstummt war:\n\n\n\n\nDie Waare, die ich dir verkauft habe, unterscheidet sich von dem\n andern Waarenpöbel dadurch, dass ihr Gebrauch Werth schafft und\n grösseren Werth als sie selbst kostet. Diess war der Grund, warum du\n sie kauftest. Was auf deiner Seite als Verwerthung von Kapital erscheint,\n ist auf meiner Seite überschüssige Verausgabung von Arbeitskraft. Du\n und ich kennen auf dem Marktplatz nur ein Gesetz, das des Waarenaus-\n tauschs. Und der Consum der Waare gehört nicht dem Verkäufer, der sie\n veräussert, sondern dem Käufer, der sie erwirbt. Dir gehört daher der Ge-\n brauch meiner täglichen Arbeitskraft. Aber vermittelst ihres täglichen\n Verkaufspreises muss ich sie täglich reproduziren und daher von neuem ver-\n kaufen können. Abgesehen von dem natürlichen Verschleiss durch Alter\n u. s. w., muss ich fähig sein, morgen mit demselben Normalzustand von\n Kraft, Gesundheit und Frische zu arbeiten, wie heute. Du predigst\n mir beständig das Evangelium der „Sparsamkeit“ und „Enthaltung“.\n Nun gut! Ich will wie ein vernünftiger, sparsamer Wirth mein ein-\n ziges Vermögen, die Arbeitskraft, haushalten und mich jeder tollen\n Verschwendung derselben enthalten. Ich will täglich nur so viel von\n ihr flüssig machen, in Bewegung, in Arbeit umsetzen, als sich mit ihrer\n Normaldauer und gesunden Entwicklung verträgt. Durch massloses Ver-\n längern des Arbeitstages kannst du in Einem Tage ein grösseres Quantum\n meiner Arbeitskraft flüssig machen, als ich in drei Tagen ersetzen kann.\n Was du so an Arbeit gewinnst, verliere ich an Arbeitssubstanz. Die Be-\n nutzung meiner Arbeitskraft und die Beraubung derselben sind ganz\n verschiedne Dinge. Wenn die Durchschnittsperiode, die ein Durchschnitts-\n arbeiter bei vernünftigem Arbeitsmass leben kann, 30 Jahre beträgt, ist\n der Werth meiner Arbeitskraft, den du mir einen Tag in den andern zahlst,\n\\frac{1}{365 × 30} oder \\frac{1}{10950} ihres Gesammtwerths. Consumirst du sie aber\n in 10 Jahren, so zahlst du mir nur \\frac{1}{3650} oder nur ⅓ ihres Werths täg-\n lich und bestiehlst mich daher täglich um ⅔ des Werths meiner Waare.\n Du zahlst mir eintägige Arbeitskraft, wo du dreitägige verbrauchst. Das\n ist wider unsern Vertrag und das Gesetz des Waarenaustauschs. Ich ver-\n lange also einen Arbeitstag von normaler Länge und ich verlange ihn\n ohne Appell an dein Herz, denn in Geldsachen hört die Gemüthlichkeit\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"202\", \"facs\": \"#f0221\"} --> auf. Du magst ein Musterbürger sein, vielleicht Mitglied des Vereins zur\n Abschaffung der Thierquälerei und obendrein im Geruch der Heiligkeit\n stehen, aber dem Ding, das du mir gegenüber repräsentirst, schlägt kein\n Herz in seiner Brust. Was darin zu pochen scheint, ist mein eigner\n Herzschlag. Ich verlange den Normalarbeitstag, weil ich den\nWerth meiner Waare verlange, wie jeder andre Verkäufer [Note: Während des grossen Strike der London builders, 1860—61, zur Reduk-\n tion des Arbeitstags auf 9 Stunden, veröffentlichte ihr Comité eine Erklärung, die\n halb und halb auf das Plaidoyer unsres Arbeiters hinausläuft. Die Erklärung\n spielt nicht ohne Ironie darauf an, dass der Profitwüthigste der „building masters“\n — ein gewisser Sir M. Peto — im „Geruch der Heiligkeit“ stehe.] .\n\n\n\n\nMan sieht: von ganz elastischen Schranken abgesehn, ergiebt sich aus\n der Natur des Waarenaustauschs selbst keine Grenze des Arbeitstags, also\n keine Grenze der Mehrarbeit. Der Kapitalist behauptet daher nur sein Recht\n als Käufer, wenn er den Arbeitstag so lange als möglich und wo möglich aus\n Einem Arbeitstag zwei zu machen sucht. Andrerseits schliesst die spezifische\n Natur der verkauften Waare eine Schranke ihres Consums durch den Käufer\n ein, und der Arbeiter behauptet daher nur sein Recht als Verkäufer, wenn er\n den Arbeitstag auf eine bestimmte Normalgrösse beschränken will. Es findet\n hier also eine Antinomie statt, Recht wider Recht, beide gleichmässig\n durch das Gesetz des Waarenaustauschs besiegelt. Zwischen gleichen\n Rechten entscheidet die Gewalt. Und so stellt sich in der Geschichte\n der kapitalistischen Produktion die Normirung des Arbeitstags\n als Kampf um die Schranken des Arbeitstags dar — ein\n Kampf zwischen dem Gesammtkapitalisten, d. h. der Klasse der Kapi-\n talisten, und dem Gesammtarbeiter, oder der Arbeiterklasse.\n\n\n\n\nDas Kapital, wie bereits bemerkt, hat die Mehrarbeit nicht er-\n funden. Ueberall, wo ein Theil der Gesellschaft das Monopol der Produk-\n tionsmittel besitzt, muss der Arbeiter, frei oder unfrei, der zu seiner Selbst-\n erhaltung nothwendigen Arbeitszeit überschüssige Arbeitszeit zusetzen,\n um die Lebensmittel für die Eigner der Produktionsmittel zu produziren [Note: „Those who labour . . . . in reality feed both the pensioners called\n therich, and themselves.“ (Edmund Burke l. c. p. 2.)] ,\n ob dieser Eigenthümer nun ein atheniensischer ϰαλος ϰἀγαϑός, ein\n etruskischer Theokrat, civis romanus, normännischer Baron, amerikanischer\n Sklavenhalter, walachischer Bojar, moderner Landlord oder Kapitalist\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"203\", \"facs\": \"#f0222\"} --> ist [Note: Sehr naiv bemerkt Niebuhr in seiner „Römischen Geschichte“:\n „Man kann sich nicht verhehlen, dass Werke wie die etruskischen, die in ihren\n Trümmern erstaunen, in kleinen (!) Staaten Frohnherrn und Knechte vor-\n aussetzen.“ Viel tiefer sagte Sismondi, dass „Brüsseler Spitzen“ Lohnherrn\n und Lohndiener voraussetzen.] . Indess ist klar, dass wenn in einer ökonomischen Gesellschafts-\n formation nicht der Tauschwerth, sondern der Gebrauchswerth\n des Produkts vorwiegt, die Mehrarbeit durch einen engeren oder weiteren\n Kreis von Bedürfnissen beschränkt, aber kein schrankenloses Be-\n dürfniss nach Mehrarbeit durch den Charakter der Pro-\n duktion selbst gegeben ist. Wo wir im Alterthume scheinbare Ab-\n weichungen von diesem Gesetz finden, bilden sie in der That seinen direk-\n testen Beweis. Am entsetzlichsten z. B. zeigt sich hier die Ueberarbeit,\n wo der Tauschwerth in seiner selbstständigen Gestalt als Geld produzirt\n wird, in der Produktion von Gold und Silber. Gewaltsames zu Tod arbei-\n ten ist hier die offizielle Form der Ueberarbeit. Man lese nur den Diodo-\n rus Siculus [Note: „Man kann diese Unglücklichen (in den Goldbergwerken zwischen\n Aegypten, Aethiopien und Arabien), die nicht einmal ihren Körper reinlich halten,\n noch ihre Blösse decken können, nicht ansehen, ohne ihr jammervolles Schick-\n sal zu beklagen. Denn da findet keine Nachsicht und keine Schonung statt für\n Kranke, für Gebrechliche, für Greise, für die weibliche Schwachheit. Alle müssen,\n durch Schläge gezwungen, fortarbeiten, bis der Tod ihren Qualen und ihrer Noth\n ein Ende macht.“ Diod. Sic. Historische Bibliothek, Buch 3, c. 13.] . Wenn jedoch Völker, bei denen sich die Produktion noch\n in den niedrigeren Formen der Sklavenarbeit, Frohnarbeit u. s. w. bewegt,\n mitten in einem durch die kapitalistische Produktionsweise beherrschten\n Weltmarkt stehn, der den Verkauf ihrer Produkte ins Ausland zum vor-\n wiegenden Interesse entwickelt, wird den barbarischen Greueln der Skla-\n verei, Leibeigenschaft u. s. w. der civilisirte Greuel der Ueberarbeit auf-\n gepfropft. Daher bewahrte die Negerarbeit in den südlichen Staaten der\n amerikanischen Union einen gemässigt patriarchalischen Charakter, so\n lange die Produktion hauptsächlich auf den unmittelbaren Selbstbedarf ge-\n richtet war. In dem Masse aber wie der Export der Baumwolle das\n Lebensinteresse jener Staaten wurde, wurde die Ueberarbeitung des Negers,\n hier und da die Consumtion seines Lebens in sieben Arbeitsjahren, Faktor\n eines berechneten und berechnenden Systems. Es galt nicht mehr eine\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"204\", \"facs\": \"#f0223\"} --> gewisse Masse nützlicher Produkte aus ihm gewinnen. Es galt nun\nder Produktion des Mehrwerths selbst. Aehnlich mit der\nFrohnarbeit, z. B. in den Donaufürstenthümern.\n\n\n\n\nDie Vergleichung des Heisshungers nach Mehrarbeit in den Donau-\n fürstenthümern mit demselben Heisshunger in englischen Fabriken bietet\n ein besondres Interesse, weil die Mehrarbeit in der Frohnarbeit\n eine selbstständige, sinnlich wahrnehmbare Form besitzt.\n\n\n\n\nGesetzt der Arbeitstag zähle 6 Stunden nothwendiger Arbeit und 6\n Stunden Mehrarbeit. So liefert der freie Arbeiter dem Kapitalisten wöchent-\n lich 6 × 6, oder 36 Stunden Mehrarbeit. Es ist dasselbe, als ob er 3\n Tage in der Woche für sich und 3 Tage in der Woche umsonst für den\n Kapitalisten arbeite. Aber diess ist nicht sichtbar. Mehrarbeit und noth-\n wendige Arbeit verschwimmen in einander. Ich kann daher dasselbe Ver-\n hältniss z. B. auch so ausdrücken, dass der Arbeiter in jeder Minute 30\n Sekunden für sich und 30 für den Kapitalisten arbeitet u. s. w. Anders\n mit der Frohnarbeit. Die nothwendige Arbeit, die der walachische\n Bauer z. B. zu seiner Selbsterhaltung verrichtet, ist räumlich getrennt\n von seiner Mehrarbeit für den Bojaren. Die eine verrichtet er auf seinem\n eignen Felde, die andre auf dem herrschaftlichen Gut. Beide Theile der\n Arbeitszeit existiren daher selbstständig neben einander. Die Mehr-\n arbeit ist als Frohnarbeit genau abgeschieden von der nothwendigen\n Arbeit. An dem quantitativen Verhältniss von Mebrarbeit und noth-\n wendiger Arbeit ändert diese verschiedne Erscheinungsform offenbar nichts.\n Drei Tage Mehrarbeit in der Woche bleiben drei Tage Arbeit, die kein\n Aequivalent für den Arbeiter selbst bildet, ob sie Frohnarbeit heisse oder\n Lohnarbeit. Bei dem Kapitalisten jedoch erscheint der Heisshunger nach\n Mehrarbeit im Drang zu massloser Verlängerung des Arbeits-\n tags, bei dem Bojaren einfacher in unmittelbarer Jagd auf Frohntage [Note: Das Nachfolgende bezieht sich auf die Zustände der rumänischen Provin-\n zen, wie sie sich vor der Umwälzung seit dem Krimkrieg gestaltet hatten.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Frohnarbeit war in den Donaufürstenthümern verknüpft mit Na-\n turalrenten und sonstigem Zubehör von Leibeigenschaft, bildete aber den\n entscheidenden Tribut an die herrschende Klasse. Wo diess der Fall,\n entsprang die Frohnarbeit selten aus der Leibeigenschaft, sondern die\n Leibeigenschaft meist umgekehrt aus der Frohnarbeit. So in den rumäni-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"205\", \"facs\": \"#f0224\"} --> schen Provinzen. Ihre ursprüngliche Produktionsweise war auf Gemein-\n eigenthum gegründet, aber nicht auf Gemeineigenthum in slavischer oder\n gar indischer Form. Ein Theil der Ländereien wurde als freies Privat-\n eigenthum von den Mitgliedern der Gemeinde selbstständig bewirthschaftet,\n ein anderer Theil — der ager publicus — gemeinsam von ihnen bestellt.\n Die Produkte dieser gemeinsamen Arbeit dienten theils als Reservefonds\n gegen Missernten und andere Zufälle, theils als Staatsschatz zur Deckung\n für die Kosten von Krieg, Religion und andere Gemeindeausgaben. Im\n Laufe der Zeit usurpirten kriegerische und kirchliche Würdenträger mit\n dem Gemeineigenthum die Leistungen für dasselbe. Die Arbeit der freien\n Bauern auf ihrem Gemeindeland verwandelte sich in Frohnarbeit\n für die Diebe des Gemeindelandes. Damit entwickelten sich zugleich\n Leibeigenschafts-Verhältnisse, jedoch nur thatsächlich, nicht gesetzlich,\n bis das weltbefreiende Russland unter dem Vorwand, die Leibeigenschaft\n abzuschaffen, sie zum Gesetz erhob. Der Kodex der Frohnarbeit,\n den der russische General Kisseleff 1831 proklamirte, war natürlich\n von den Bojaren selbst diktirt. Russland eroberte so mit einem Schlag\n diese Magnaten der Donaufürstenthümer und den Beifallsklatsch des libe-\n ralen Cretinismus von ganz Europa.\n\n\n\n\nNach dem „Règlement organique“, so heisst jener Kodex der\n Frohnarbeit, schuldet jeder walachische Bauer, ausser einer Masse detail-\n lirter Naturalabgaben, dem s. g. Grundeigenthümer 1) 12 Arbeitstage\n überhaupt, 2) einen Tag Feldarbeit und 3) einen Tag Holzfuhre. Summa\n Summarum 14 Tage im Jahre. Mit tiefer Einsicht in die politische Oeko-\n nomie wird jedoch der Arbeitstag nicht in seinem ordinären Sinn genom-\n men, sondern der zur Herstellung eines täglichen Durchschnittsprodukts\nnothwendige Arbeitstag, aber das tägliche Durchschnittsprodukt ist\n pfiffiger Weise so bestimmt, dass kein Cyklope in 24 Stunden damit fertig\n würde. In den dürren Worten echt russischer Ironie erklärt daher das\n „Règlement“ selbst, unter 12 Arbeitstagen sei das Produkt einer Hand-\n arbeit von 36 Tagen zu verstehn, unter einem Tag Feldarbeit drei Tage,\n und unter einem Tag Holzfuhr ebenfalls das Dreifache. Summa: 42 Frohn-\n tage. Es kommt aber hinzu die s. g. Jobagie, Dienstleistungen, die\n dem Grundherrn für ausserordentliche Produktionsbedürfnisse gebühren.\n Eine bestimmtes jährliches Quantum der Mannschaft, je nach der Anzahl\n der Dorfbevölkerung, ist zur Jobagie verfehmt. Diese zusätzliche Frohn-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"206\", \"facs\": \"#f0225\"} --> arbeit wird für jeden walachischen Bauer auf 14 Tage geschätzt. So\n beträgt die vorgeschriebene Frohnarbeit 56 Arbeitstage jährlich. Das\n Ackerbaujahr zählt aber in der Walachei wegen des schlechten Klima’s\n nur 210 Tage, wovon 40 für Sonn- und Feiertage, 30 durchschnittlich\n für Unwetter, zusammen 70 Tage ausfallen. Bleiben 140 Arbeitstage.\n Das Verhältniss der Frohnarbeit zur nothwendigen Arbeit, \\frac{56}{84}, oder\n 66⅔ %, drückt eine viel kleinere Rate des Mehrwerths aus als die,\n welche die Arbeit des englischen Agrikultur- oder Fabrikarbeiters regu-\n lirt. Diess ist jedoch nur die gesetzlich vorgeschriebene Frohnarbeit.\n Und in noch „liberalerem“ Geist als die englische Fabrikgesetzgebung hat\n das „Règlement organique“ seine eigne Umgehung zu erleichtern gewusst.\n Nachdem es aus 12 Tagen 54 gemacht, wird das nominelle Tagwerk jedes\n der 54 Frohntage wieder so bestimmt, dass eine Zubusse auf die folgenden\n Tage fallen muss. In einem Tag z. B. soll eine Landstrecke ausgegätet\n werden, die zu dieser Operation, namentlich auf den Maispflanzungen,\n doppelt so viel Zeit erheischt. Das gesetzliche Tagwerk für einzelne\n Agrikulturarbeiten ist so auslegbar, dass der Tag im Monat Mai anfängt\n und im Monat Oktober aufhört. Für die Moldau sind die Bestimmun-\n gen noch härter. „Die zwölf Frohntage des Règlement organique“,\n rief ein siegtrunkener Bojar, „belaufen sich auf 365 Tage im Jahr!“ [Note: Weitere Details findet man in: „E. Regnault: Histoire poli-\n tique et sociale des Principautés Danubiennes. Paris 1855.“] \n\n\n\n\nWar das Règlement organique der Donaufürstenthümer ein\npositiver Ausdruck des Heisshungers nach Mehrarbeit, den jeder\n Paragraph legalisirt, so sind die englischen Factory Acts nega-\n tive Ausdrücke desselben Heisshungers. Diese Gesetze treten dem\n Drang des Kapitals nach massloser Aussaugung der Arbeitskraft durch\ngewaltsame Beschränkung des Arbeitstags von Staats-\n wegen entgegen, und zwar von Seiten eines Staats, den Kapitalist und\n Landlord beherrschen. Von einer täglich bedrohlicher anschwellenden Ar-\n beiterbewegung abgesehn, diktirte dieselbe Nothwendigkeit die Beschrän-\n kung der Fabrikarbeit, welche den Guano auf die englischen Felder aus-\n goss. Dieselbe blinde Raubgier, die in dem einen Fall die Erde erschöpft,\n hatte in dem andern die Lebenskraft der Nation an der Wurzel ergriffen.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"207\", \"facs\": \"#f0226\"} --> Periodische Epidemieen sprachen hier eben so deutlich als das abneh-\n mende Soldatenmass in Deutschland und Frankreich [Note: „Im Allgemeinen spricht innerhalb gewisser Grenzen für das Gedeihen\n organischer Wesen das Ueberschreiten des Mittelmasses ihrer Art. Für den\n Menschen verkleinert sich sein Körpermass, wenn sein Gedeihen beeinträchtigt\n ist, sei es durch physische oder sociale Verhältnisse. In allen europäischen Län-\n dern, wo Konscription besteht, hat seit Einführung derselben das mittlere Körper-\n mass der erwachsenen Männer und im Ganzen ihre Tauglichkeit zum Kriegsdienst\n abgenommen. Vor der Revolution (1789) war das Maximum für den Infanterist\n in Frankreich 165 Centimeter; 1818 (Gesetz vom 10. März) 157, nach dem Gesetz\n vom 21. März 1832, 156 Centimeter; durchschnittlich in Frankreich wegen\n mangelnder Grösse und Gebrechen über die Hälfte ausgemustert. Das Militair-\n mass war in Sachsen 1780: 178 Centimeter, jetzt 155. In Preussen ist es 157.\n Nach Angabe in der Bayrischen Zeitung vom 9. Mai 1862 von Dr. Meyer, stellt\n sich nach einem 9jährigen Durchschnitt heraus, dass in Preussen von 1000 Kon-\n scribirten 716 untauglich zum Militairdienst, 317 wegen Mindermass und 399\n wegen Gebrechen . . . . Berlin konnte 1858 sein Kontingent an Ersatz-Mann-\n schaft nicht stellen, es fehlten 156 Mann.“ (J. v. Liebig: „Die Chemie\n in ihrer Anwendung auf Agrikultur und Physiologie. 1862.\n 7te Auflage.“ Band I, p. 117, 118.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDer jetzt regulirende Factory Act von 1850 erlaubt für den\n durchschnittlichen Wochentag 10 Stunden, nämlich für die ersten 5 Wochen-\n tage 12 Stunden, von 6 Uhr Morgens bis 6 Uhr Abends, wovon aber\n ½ Stunde für Frühstück und eine Stunde für Mittagsessen gesetzlich ab-\n gehn, also 10½ Arbeitsstunden bleiben, und 8 Stunden für den Samstag,\n von 6 Uhr Morgens bis 2 Uhr Nachmittags, wovon ½ Stunde für Früh-\n stück abgeht. Bleiben 60 Arbeitsstunden, 10½ für die ersten 5 Wochen-\n tage, 7½ für den letzten Wochentag [Note: Die Geschichte des Fabrikakts von 1850 folgt im Verlauf dieses Kapitels.] . Es sind eigne Wächter des\n Gesetzes bestellt, die dem Ministerium des Innern direkt untergeordneten\nFabrikinspektoren, deren Berichte halbjährig von Parlamentswe-\n gen veröffentlicht werden. Sie liefern also eine fortlaufende und offizielle\n Statistik über den Kapitalistenheisshunger nach Mehrarbeit.\n\n\n\n\nHören wir einen Augenblick die Fabrikinspektoren [Note: Auf die Periode vom Beginn der grossen Industrie in England bis 1845\n gehe ich nur hier und da  [Editorial alternatives: sic = eiu; corr = ein] , und verweise den Leser darüber auf:\n „Die Lage der arbeitenden Klasse in England. Von Friedrich\n Engels. Leipzig 1845.“ Wie tief Engelsden Geistder kapitalistischen Pro-] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"208\", \"facs\": \"#f0227\"} -->\n\n„Der betrügerische Fabrikant beginnt die Arbeit eine Viertelstunde,\n manchmal früher, manchmal später, vor 6 Uhr Morgens, und schliesst sie\n eine Viertelstunde, manchmal früher, manchmal später, nach 6 Uhr Nach-\n mittags. Er nimmt 5 Minuten weg vom Anfang und Ende der nominell\n für das Frühstück anberaumten halben Stunde, und knappt 10 Minuten ab\n zu Anfang und Ende der für Mittagsessen anberaumten Stunde. Samstag\n arbeitet er eine Viertelstunde, manchmal mehr, manchmal weniger, nach\n 2 Uhr Nachmittags. So beträgt sein Gewinn:\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOder 5 Stunden 40 Minuten wöchentlich, was mit 50 Arbeitswochen\n multiplicirt, nach Abzug von 2 Wochen für Feiertage oder gelegentliche\n Unterbrechungen, 27 Arbeitstage giebt [Note: „Suggestions etc. by Mr. L. Horner, Inspector of Factories“,\n im: „Factory Regulation’s Act. Ordered by the House of Com-\n mons to be printed 9. Aug. 1859“, p. 4, 5.] .“\n\n\n\n\n [Note: duktionsweise begriff, zeigen die Factory Reports, Reports on Mines\n u. s. w., die seit 1845 erschienen sind, und wie bewunderungswürdig er die Zustände\n im Detail malte, zeigt der oberflächlichste Vergleich seiner Schrift mit den 18 bis 20\n Jahre später veröffentlichten officiellen Reports der „Children’s Employment\n Commission.“ (1863—67.) Diese handeln nämlich von Industriezweigen,\n worin die Fabrikgesetzgebung bis 1862 noch nicht eingeführt war, zum Theil noch\n nicht eingeführt ist. Hier wurde also den von Engels geschilderten Zuständen\n mehr oder minder grosse Aenderung nicht von aussen aufgeherrscht. Meine Bei-\n spiele entlehne ich hauptsächlich der Freihandelsperiode nach 1848, jener paradisi-\n schen Zeit, wovon eben so grossmäulige als wissenschaftlich verwahrloste Frei-\n handelshausirburschen den Deutschen so fabelhaft viel vorfauchen. — Uebrigens\n figurirt England hier nur im Vordergrund, weil es die kapitalistische Produktion\n klassisch repräsentirt und allein eine officiell fortlaufende Statistik der behandelten\n Gegenstände besitzt.] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"209\", \"facs\": \"#f0228\"} -->\n\n„Wird der Arbeitstag täglich 5 Minuten über die Normaldaner ver-\n längert, so giebt das 2½ Produktionstage im Jahr. [Note: „Reports of the Insp. of Fact. for the half year ended 1856“,\n p. 34.] “ „Eine zusätz-\n liche Stunde täglich, dadurch gewonnen, dass bald hier ein Stückchen Zeit\n erhascht wird, bald dort ein anderes Stückchen, macht aus den 12 Mona-\n ten des Jahrs 13“ [Note: „Reports etc. 30th April 1858“, p. 9.] .\n\n\n\n\nKrisen, worin die Produktion unterbrochen und nur „kurze Zeit“, nur\n während einiger Tage in der Woche, gearbeitet wird, ändern natürlich nichts\n an dem Trieb nach Verlängerung des Arbeitstags. Je weniger Geschäfte\n gemacht werden, desto grösser soll der Gewinn auf das gemachte Geschäft\n sein. Je weniger Zeit gearbeitet werden kann, desto mehr Surplusarbeits-\n zeit soll gearbeitet werden. So berichten die Fabrikinspektoren über die\n Periode der Krise von 1857 — 1858:\n\n\n\n\n„Man mag es für eine Inkonsequenz halten, dass irgendwelche Ueber-\n arbeit zu einer Zeit stattfinde, wo der Handel so schlecht geht, aber sein\n schlechter Zustand spornt rücksichtslose Leute zu Ueberschreitungen; sie\n sichern sich so einen Extraprofit …“ „Zur selben Zeit“, sagt Leon-\n hard Horner, „wo 122 Fabriken in meinem Distrikt ganz aufgegeben\n sind, 143 still stehn und alle andern kurze Zeit arbeiten, wird die Ueber-\n arbeit über die gesetzlich bestimmte Zeit fortgesetzt [Note: „Reports etc.“ l. c. p. 43.] “. „Obgleich“,\n sagt Herr Howell, „in den meisten Fabriken des schlechten Geschäfts-\n stands wegen nur halbe Zeit gearbeitet wird, erhalte ich nach wie vor\ndieselbe Anzahl von Klagen, dass eine halbe Stunde oder ¾ Stun-\n den täglich den Arbeitern weggeschnappt (snatched) werden durch Ein-\n griffe in die ihnen gesetzlich gesicherten Fristen für Mahlzeit und Erho-\n lung [Note: „Reports etc.“ l. c. p. 25.] “.\n\n\n\n\nDasselbe Phänomen wiederholt sich auf kleinerer Stufenleiter wäh-\n rend der furchtbaren Baumwollkrise von 1861 bis 1865 [Note: „Reports etc. for half year ending 30th April 1861.“ Sieh Ap-\n pendix N. 2; „Reports etc. 31st Octob. 1862“, p. 7, 52, 53. Die Ueber-\n schreitungen werden wieder zahlreicher mit dem letzten Halbjahr 1863. Vgl.\n „Reports etc. ending 31st Oct. 1863“, p. 7.] .\n\n\n [Running matter: I. 14] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"210\", \"facs\": \"#f0229\"} -->\n\n„Es wird zuweilen vorgeschützt, wenn wir Arbeiter während der\n Speisestunden oder sonst zu ungesetzlicher Zeit am Werk ertappen, dass\nsie die Fabrik durchaus nicht verlassen wollen, und dass\n es des Zwangs bedarf, um ihre Arbeit (Reinigen der Maschinen u. s. w.)\n zu unterbrechen, namentlich Samstag Nachmittags. Aber wenn die\n „Hände“ nach Stillsetzung der Maschinerie in der Fabrik bleiben, geschieht\n es nur, weil ihnen zwischen 6 Uhr Morgens und 6 Uhr Abends, in den ge-\n setzlich bestimmten Arbeitsstunden, keine Frist zur Verrichtung solcher\n Geschäfte gestattet worden ist [Note: „Reports etc. 31st Oct. 1860“, p. 23. Mit welchem Fanatismus,\n nach gerichtlichen Aussagen der Fabrikanten, ihre Fabrikhände sich jeder Unter-\n brechung der Fabrikarbeit widersetzen, zeige folgendes Kuriosum: Anfang Juni\n 1836 gingen den Magistrates von Dewsbury (Yorkshire) Denunciationen zu, wo-\n nach die Eigner von 8 grossen Fabriken in der Nähe von Batley den Fabrikakt ver-\n letzt hätten. Ein Theil dieser Herren war angeklagt, 5 Knaben zwischen 12 und\n 15 Jahren von 6 Uhr Morgen des Freitags bis 4 Uhr Nachmittag des folgenden\n Samstags abgearbeitet zu haben, ohne irgend eine Erholung zu gestatten, ausser\n für Mahlzeiten und Eine Stunde Schlaf um Mitternacht. Und diese\n Kinder hatten die rastlose, 30stündige Arbeit zu verrichten in dem „shoddy-hole“,\n wie die Höhle heisst, worin Wollenlumpen aufgerissen werden und wo ein Luft-\n meer von Staub, Abfällen u. s. w. selbst den erwachsenen Arbeiter zwingt, den\n Mund beständig mit Schnupftüchern zu verbinden, zum Schutz seiner Lungen! Die\n Herren Angeklagten versicherten an Eidesstatt — als Quäker waren sie zu scru-\n pulös religiöse Männer einen Eid zu leisten, — sie hätten in ihrer grossen Barm-\n herzigkeit den elenden Kindern 4 Stunden Schlaf erlaubt, aber die Starrköpfe von\n Kindern wollten durchaus nicht zu Bett gehn! Die Herrn Quäker wurden zu 20\n Pfd. St. Geldbusse verurtheilt. Dryden ahnte diese Quäker:\n\n\n\n\n„Fox full fraught in seeming sanctity,\n\n\n\n\nThat feared an oath, but like the devil would lie,\n\n\n\n\nThat look’d like Lent, and had the holy leer,\n\n\n\n\nAnd durst not sin! before he said his prayer!“\n\n\n\n] “.\n\n\n\n\n„Der durch Ueberarbeit über die gesetzliche Zeit zu machende Extra-\n profit scheint für viele Fabrikanten eine zu grosse Versuchung, um ihr\n widerstehen zu können. Sie rechnen auf die Chance nicht ausgefunden\n zu werden und berechnen, dass selbst im Fall der Entdeckung die Gering-\n fügigkeit der Geldstrafen und Gerichtskosten ihnen immer noch eine Ge-\n winnbilanz sichert [Note: „Rep. etc. 31. Oct. 1856“ p. 34.] “. „Wo die zusätzliche Zeit durch Multipli-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"211\", \"facs\": \"#f0230\"} --> kation kleiner Diebstähle („a multiplication of small\n thefts“) im Laufe des Tages gewonnen wird, stehn den Inspektoren fast\n unüberwindliche Schwierigkeiten der Beweisführung im Weg [Note: l. c. p. 35.] “. Diese\n „kleinen Diebstähle“ des Kapitals an der Mahlzeit und Erholungs-\n zeit der Arbeiter bezeichnen die Fabrikinspektoren auch als „petty pilfer-\n ings of minutes“, Mausereien von Minuten [Note: l. c. p. 48.] , „snatching a few minutes“,\n Wegschnappen von Minuten [Note: l. c.] , oder wie die Arbeiter es technisch heissen,\n „nibbling and cribbling at meal times [Note: l. c.] “.\n\n\n\n\nMan sieht, in dieser Atmosphäre ist die Bildung des Mehr-\n werths durch die Mehrarbeit kein Geheimniss. „Wenn Sie mir\n erlauben,“ sagte mir ein sehr respektabler Fabrikherr, „täglich nur\n 10 Minuten Ueberzeit arbeiten zu lassen, stecken Sie jährlich 1000\n Pfd. St. in meine Tasche [Note: l. c. p. 48.] “. „Zeitatome sind die Elemente\n des Gewinns [Note: „Moments are the elements of profit.“ „Rep. of the Insp.\n etc. 30th April 1860“, p. 56.] “.\n\n\n\n\nNichts ist in dieser Hinsicht charakteristischer als die Bezeichnung\n der Arbeiter, die volle Zeit arbeiten, durch „full times“ und die der Kin-\n der unter 13 Jahren, die nur 6 Stunden arbeiten dürfen, als „half-\n times [Note: Der Ausdruck hat offizielles Bürgerrecht, wie in der Fabrik, so in den\n Fabrikberichten.] “. Der Arbeiter ist hier nichts mehr als personificirte Arbeits-\n zeit. Alle individuellen Unterschiede lösen sich auf in die von „Voll-\n zeitler“ und „Halbzeitler“.\n\n\n\n\nDen Trieb nach Verlängerung des Arbeitstags, den Wehr-\n wolfsheisshunger für Mehrarbeit, beobachteten wir bisher auf einem Gebiet,\n wo masslose Ausschreitungen, nicht übergipfelt, so sagt ein bürgerlicher\n englischer Oekonom, von den Grausamkeiten der Spanier gegen die Roth-\n häute Amerika’s [Note: „The cupidity of mill owners, whose cruelties in pursuits of gain, have\n hardly been exceeded by those perpetrated by the Spaniards on the conquest of] , das Kapital endlich an die Kette gesetzlicher\n [Running matter: 14*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"212\", \"facs\": \"#f0231\"} -->Regulation gelegt haben. Werfen wir jetzt den Blick auf einige Produk-\n tionszweige, wo die Aussaugung der Arbeitskraft entweder noch heute fessel-\nfrei ist oder es gestern noch war.\n\n\n\n\n„Herr Broughton, ein County Magistrate, erklärte als Präsident eines\n Meetings, abgehalten in der Stadthalle von Nottingham, am 1 4. Januar\n 1860, dass in dem mit der Spitzenfabrikation beschäftigten\n Theile der städtischen Bevölkerung ein der übrigen civilisirten Welt un-\n bekannter Grad von Leid und Entbehrung vorherrscht . . . . Um 2, 3,\n 4 Uhr des Morgens werden Kinder von 9—10 Jahren ihren schmutzigen\n Betten entrissen und gezwungen, für die nackte Subsistenz bis 10, 11, 12\n Uhr Nachts zu arbeiten, während ihre Glieder wegschwinden, ihre Gestalt\n zusammenschrumpft, ihre Gesichtszüge abstumpfen und ihr menschliches\n Wesen ganz und gar in einem steinähnlichen Torpor erstarrt, dessen blos-\n ser Anblick schauderhaft ist. Wir sind nicht überrascht, dass Herr Mal-\n let und andere Fabrikanten auftraten, um Protest gegen jede Dis-\n kussion einzulegen . . . . Das System, wie der Rev. Mr. Montagu\n Valpu es beschrieb, ist ein System unbeschränkter Sklaverei,\n Sklaverei in socialer, physischer, moralischer und intellektueller Beziehung.\n … Was soll man denken von einer Stadt, die ein öffentliches Meeting ab-\n hält, um zu petitioniren, dass die Arbeitszeit für Männer\n täglich auf 18 Stunden beschränkt werden solle! . . . .\n Wir deklamiren gegen die virginischen und karolinischen Pflanzer. Ist\n jedoch ihr Negermarkt, mit allen Schrecken der Peitsche und dem Schacher\n in Menschenfleisch, abscheulicher als diese langsame Menschenabschlach-\n tung, die vor sich geht, damit Schleier und Kragen zum Vortheil von\n Kapitalisten fabrizirt werden [Note: London Daily Telegraph vom 14. Januar 1860.] ?“\n\n\n\n\nDie Töpferei (Pottery) von Staffordshire hat während der\n letzten 22 Jahre den Gegenstand drei parlamentarischer Untersuchungen\n [Note: America, in the pursuit of gold.“ John Wade: „History of the Middle\n and Working Classes. 3d ed. Lond. 1835,“ p. 114. Der theoretische\n Theil dieses Buchs, eine Art Grundriss der politischen Oekonomie, enthält für\n seine Zeit einiges Originelle, z. B. über Handelskrisen. Der historische Theil\n leidet an schamlosem Plagiarismus aus Sir M. Eden’s: „History of the\n Poor. London 1799.“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"213\", \"facs\": \"#f0232\"} --> gebildet. Die Resultate sind niedergelegt im Bericht des Herrn\nScriven von 1841 an die „Children’s Employment Commissioners“,\n im Bericht des Dr. Greenhow von 1860, veröffentlicht auf Befehl\n des ärztlichen Beamten des Privy Council (Public Health, 3d Re-\n port, I, 102—113), endlich im Bericht des Herrn Longe von 1863,\n in „First Report of the Children’s Employment Commis-\n sion“ vom 13. Juni 1863. Für meine Aufgabe genügt ein kurzer Auszug\n aus den Zeugenaussagen der verarbeiteten Kinder, in den Berichten von 1860\n und 1863. Von den Kindern mag man auf die Erwachsenen schliessen, na-\n mentlich Mädchen und Frauen, und zwar in einem Industriezweig, neben\n dem Baumwollspinnerei u. d. g. als ein sehr angenehmes und gesundes\n Geschäft erscheint [Note: Vgl. Engels „Lage etc.“ p. 249—51.] .\n\n\n\n\nWilhelm Wood, neunjährig, „war 7 Jahre 10 Monate alt, als er\n zu arbeiten begann.“ Er „ran moulds“ (trug die fertig geformte\n Waare in die Trockenstube, um nachher die leere Form zurückzubringen)\n von Anfang an. Er kommt jeden Tag in der Woche um 6 Uhr Morgens\n und hört auf ungefähr 9 Uhr Abends. „Ich arbeite bis 9 Uhr Abends\n jeden Tag in der Woche. So z. B. während der letzten 7—8 Wochen.“\n Also fünfzehnstündige Arbeit für ein siebenjähriges Kind! J. Murray,\n ein zwölfjähriger Knabe, sagt aus: „I run moulds and turn jigger (drehe\n das Rad). Ich komme um 6 Uhr, manchmal um 4 Uhr Morgens. Ich\n habe während der ganzen letzten Nacht bis diesen Morgen 8 Uhr gearbei-\n tet. Ich war nicht im Bett seit der letzten Nacht. Ausser mir arbeiteten\n 8 oder 9 andre Knaben die letzte Nacht durch. Alle ausser Einem sind\n diesen Morgen wieder gekommen. Ich bekomme wöchentlich 3 sh. 6 d.\n (1 Thaler 5 Groschen). Ich bekomme nicht mehr, wenn ich\n die ganze Nacht durcharbeite. Ich habe in der letzten Woche\n zwei Nächte durchgearbeitet.“ Fernyhough, ein zehnjähriger\n Knabe: „Ich habe nicht immer eine ganze Stunde für das Mittagsessen;\n oft nur eine halbe Stunde; jeden Donnerstag, Freitag und Samstag [Note: „Children’s Employment Commission. First Report etc.\n 1863“, Appendix p. 16, 19, 18.] .“\n\n\n\n\nDr. Greenhow erklärt die Lebenszeit in den Töpferdistrikten von\nStoke-upon-Trent und Wolstanton für ausserordentlich kurz.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"214\", \"facs\": \"#f0233\"} --> Obgleich im Distrikt Stoke nur 30.6 %, und in Wolstanton nur 30.4 % der\n männlichen Bevölkerung über 20 Jahre in den Töpfereien beschäftigt\n sind, fällt im ersten Distrikt mehr als die Hälfte der Todesfälle\n an Brustkrankheiten unter Männern über 20 Jahren, und im letzteren Distrikt\n ungefähr ⅖, auf Töpfer. Dr. Boothroyd, praktischer Arzt zu Han-\n ley, sagt aus: „Jede successive Generation der Töpfer ist zwerghafter\n und schwächer als die vorhergehende.“ Ebenso ein anderer Arzt, Herr\n Mc Bean: „Seit ich vor 25 Jahren meine Praxis unter den Töpfern be-\n gann, hat sich die auffallende Entartung dieser Klasse fortschreitend in\n Abnahme von Gestalt und Gewicht gezeigt.“ Diese Aussagen sind dem\n Bericht des Dr. Greenhow von 1860 entnommen [Note: „Public Health. 3d Report etc.“, p. 102, 104, 105.] .\n\n\n\n\nAus dem Bericht der Kommissäre von 1863 Folgendes: Dr. J. T.\n Arledge, Oberarzt des North Staffordshire Krankenhauses, sagt: „Als eine\n Klasse repräsentiren die Töpfer, Männer und Frauen … eine entartete\n Bevölkerung, physisch und moralisch. Sie sind in der Regel verzwergt,\n schlecht gebaut, und oft an der Brust verwachsen. Sie altern vorzeitig\n und sind kurzlebig; phlegmatisch und blutlos, verrathen sie die Schwäche\n ihrer Konstitution durch hartnäckige Anfälle von Dyspepsie, Leber- und\n Nierenstörungen und Rheumatismus. Vor allem aber sind sie Brustkrank-\n heiten unterworfen, der Pneumonie, Phthisis, Bronchitis und dem Asthma.\n Eine Form des letztern ist ihnen eigenthümlich und bekannt unter dem\n Namen des Töpfer-Asthma oder der Töpfer-Schwindsucht. Skrophulose,\n die Mandeln, Knochen oder andre Körpertheile angreift, ist eine Krank-\n heit von mehr als zwei Dritttheilen der Töpfer … Dass die Entartung\n (Degenerescence) der Bevölkerung dieses Distrikts nicht noch viel grösser\n ist, verdankt sie ausschliesslich der Rekrutirung aus den umliegenden\n Landdistrikten und den Zwischenheirathen mit gesundern Racen.“ Herr\nCharles Pearson, vor kurzem noch House Surgeon derselben Kran-\n kenanstalt, schreibt in einem Briefe an den Kommissär Longe u. a.: „Ich\n kann nur aus persönlicher Beobachtung, nicht statistisch sprechen, aber\n ich stehe nicht an zu versichern, dass meine Empörung wieder und wieder\n aufkochte bei dem Anblick dieser armen Kinder, deren Gesundheit geopfert\n wurde, um der Habgier ihrer Eltern und Arbeitsgeber zu fröhnen.“ Er\n zählt die Ursachen der Töpferkrankheiten auf und schliesst sie culminirend\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"215\", \"facs\": \"#f0234\"} --> ab mit „Long Hours“ („langen Arbeitsstunden“). Der Kommissions-\n bericht hofft, dass „eine Manufaktur von so hervorragender Stellung\n in den Augen der Welt nicht lange mehr den Makel tragen wird, dass ihr\n grosser Erfolg begleitet ist von physischer Entartung, vielverzweigten\n körperlichen Leiden, und frühem Tode der Arbeiterbevölkerung, durch\n deren Arbeit und Geschick so grosse Resultate erzielt worden sind [Note: „Children’s Employm. Commission. 1863“, p. 24, 22 u. XI.] “.\n Was von den Töpfereien in England, gilt von denen in Schottland [Note: l. c. p. XLVII.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Manufaktur von Schwefelhölzern datirt von 1833,\n von der Entdeckung, den Phosphor auf die Zündruthe selbst anzubringen.\n Seit 1845 hat sie sich rasch in England entwickelt und von den dicht-\n bevölkertsten Theilen Londons namentlich auch nach Manchester, Bir-\n mingham, Liverpool, Bristol, Norwich, Newcastle, Glasgow verbreitet, mit\n ihr die Mundsperre, die ein Wiener Arzt schon 1845 als eigenthümliche\n Krankheit der Schwefelholzmacher entdeckte. Die Hälfte der Arbeiter\n sind Kinder unter 13 und junge Personen unter 18 Jahren. Die Manu-\n faktur ist wegen ihrer Ungesundheit und Widerwärtigkeit so verrufen,\n dass nur der verkommenste Theil der Arbeiterklasse, halbverhungerte\n Wittwen u. s. w., Kinder für sie hergiebt, „zerlumpte, halb verhungerte,\n ganz verwahrloste und unerzogne Kinder [Note: l. c. p. LIV.] .“ Von den Zeugen, die\n Kommissär White (1863) verhörte, waren 270 unter 18 J., 40 unter\n 10 J., 10 nur 8 und 5 nur 6 Jahre alt. Wechsel des Arbeitstags von\n 12 auf 14 und 15 Stunden, Nachtarbeit, unregelmässige Mahlzeiten, meist\n in den Arbeitsräumen selbst, die vom Phosphor verpestet sind [Note: l. c. p. LIV.] . Dante\n würde in dieser Manufaktur seine grausamsten Höllenphantasien übertrof-\n fen finden.\n\n\n\n\nIn der Tapetenfabrik werden die gröberen Sorten mit Maschinen,\n die feineren mit der Hand (block printing) gedruckt. Die lebhaftesten Ge-\n schäftsmonate fallen zwischen Anfang Oktober und Ende April. Wäh-\n rend dieser Periode dauert diese Arbeit häufig und fast ohne Unter-\n brechung von 6 Uhr Vormittags bis 10 Uhr Abends und tiefer in die\n Nacht.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"216\", \"facs\": \"#f0235\"} -->\n\nJ. Leach sagt aus: „Letzten Winter (1862) blieben von 19 Mäd-\n chen 6 weg in Folge durch Ueberarbeitung zugezogener Krankheiten.\n Um sie wach zu halten, muss ich sie anschreien.“ W. Duffy: „Die\n Kinder konnten oft vor Müdigkeit die Augen nicht aufhalten, in der That,\n wir selbst können es oft kaum.“ J. Lightbourne: „Ich bin 13 Jahre\n alt … Wir arbeiteten letzten Winter bis 9 Uhr Abends und den Winter\n vorher bis 10 Uhr. Ich pflegte letzten Winter fast jeden Abend vom\n Schmerz wunder Füsse zu schreien.“ G. Apsden: „Diesen meinen\n Jungen pflegte ich, als er 7 Jahre alt war, auf meinem Rücken hin und\n her über den Schnee zu tragen, und er pflegte 16 Stunden zu arbeiten!\n … Ich habe oft nieder gekniet, um ihn zu füttern, während er an der\n Maschine stand, denn er durfte sie nicht verlassen, oder\n stillsetzen.“ Smith, der geschäftsführende Associé einer Manchester-\n Fabrik: „Wir (er meint seine „Hände,“ die für „uns“ arbeiten) arbeiten\n ohne Unterbrechung für Mahlzeiten, so dass die Tagesarbeit von 10½\n Stunden um 4½ Uhr Nachmittags fertig ist, und alles Spätere ist Ueber-\n zeit [Note: Diess ist nicht in unsrem Sinn der Surplusarbeitszeit zu nehmen.\n Diese Herrn betrachten die 10½ stündige Arbeit als Normalarbeitstag, der\n also auch die normale Mehrarbeit einschliesst. Dann beginnt „die Ueberzeit,“\n die etwas besser bezahlt wird. Man wird bei einer spätern Gelegenheit sehn, dass\n die Verwendung der Arbeitskraft während des sogenannten Normaltages unter\n dem Werthe bezahlt wird, so dass die „Ueberzeit“ ein blosser Kapitalistenpfiff\n ist, um mehr „Mehrarbeit“ auszupressen, was es übrigens selbst dann bleibt, wenn\n die während des „Normaltages“ verwandte Arbeitskraft wirklich voll bezahlt\n wird.] . (Ob dieser Herr Smith wohl keine Mahlzeit während 10½\n Stunden zu sich nimmt?) Wir (derselbe Smith) hören selten auf vor 6\n Uhr Abends (er meint mit der Konsumtion „unserer“ Arbeitskraftsmaschi-\n nen), so dass wir (iterum Crispinus) in der That das ganze Jahr durch\n Ueberzeit arbeiten … Die Kinder und Erwachsenen (152 Kinder und\n junge Personen unter 18 Jahren und 140 Erwachsene) haben gleich-\n mässig während der letzten 18 Monate im Durchschnitt allermin-\n destens 7 Tage und 5 Stunden in der Woche gearbeitet, oder\n 78½ Stunden wöchentlich. Für die 6 Wochen endend am 2. Mai dieses\n Jahres (1863) war der Durchschnitt höher — 8 Tage oder 84 Stun-\n den in der Woche!“ Doch fügt derselbe Herr Smith, der dem plu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"217\", \"facs\": \"#f0236\"} --> ralis majestatis so sehr ergeben ist, schmunzelnd hinzu: „Maschinenarbeit\n ist leicht.“ Und so sagen die Anwender des Block Printing: „Handar-\n beit ist gesunder als Maschinenarbeit.“ Im Ganzen erklären sich die\n Herrn Fabrikanten mit Entrüstung gegen den Vorschlag, „die Maschi-\n nen wenigstens während der Mahlzeiten still zu setzen“.\n „Ein Gesetz,“ sagt Herr Otley, der manager einer Tapetenfabrik in\n Borough, das „Arbeitsstunden von 6 Uhr Morgens bis 9 Uhr Abends er-\n laubte, würde uns (!) sehr wohl zusagen, aber die Stunden des Factory\n Act von 6 Uhr Morgens bis 6 Uhr Abends passen uns (!) nicht …\n Unsere Maschine wird während des Mittagessens (welche Gross-\n muth!) still gesetzt. Das Stillsetzen verursacht keinen nennenswerthen\n Verlust an Papier und Farbe. „Aber,“ fügt er sympathetisch hinzu,\n „ich kann verstehn, dass der damit verbundene Verlust\n nicht geliebt wird.“ Der Kommissionsbericht meint naiv, die Furcht\n einiger „leitender Firmen“ Zeit, d. h. Aneignungszeit fremder Arbeitszeit,\n und dadurch „Profit zu verlieren,“ sei kein „hinreichender Grund,“ um\n Kinder unter 13 und junge Personen unter 18 Jahren während 12 — 16\n Stunden ihr Mittagsmahl „verlieren zu lassen“, oder es ihnen zuzusetzen,\n wie man der Dampfmaschine Kohle und Wasser, der Wolle Seife, dem Rad\n Oel u. s. w. zusetzt, — während des Produktionsprozesses\n selbst, als blossen Hilfsstoff des Arbeitsmittels [Note: l. c. Appendix p. 123, 124, 125, 140 u. LIV.] .\n\n\n\n\nKein Industriezweig in England — (wir sehn von dem erst neuer-\n dings sich bahnbrechenden Maschinenbrod ab) — hat so alterthümliche,\n ja, wie man aus den Dichtern der römischen Kaiserzeit ersehn kann, vor-\n christliche Produktionsweise bis heute beibehalten, als die Bäckerei.\n Aber das Kapital, wie früher bemerkt, ist zunächst gleichgültig gegen\n den technologischen Charakter des Arbeitsprozesses, dessen es sich be-\n mächtigt. Es nimmt ihn zunächst, wie es ihn vorfindet.\n\n\n\n\nDie unglaubliche Brodverfälschung, namentlich in London, wurde\n zuerst enthüllt durch das Comité des Unterhauses „über die Verfäl-\n schung von Nahrungsmitteln“ (1855—56) und Dr. Hassall’s\n Schrift: „Adulterations detected [Note: Alaun, fein gerieben, oder mit Salz gemischt, ist ein normaler Handels-\n artikel, der den bezeichnenden Namen „baker’s stuff“ führt.] “. Die Folge dieser Enthül-\n lungen war das Gesetz vom 6. August 1860: „for preventing the adulte-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"218\", \"facs\": \"#f0237\"} --> ration of articles of food and drink,“ ein wirkungsloses Gesetz, da es\n natürlich die höchste Delikatesse gegen jeden free-trader beobachtet, der\n sich vornimmt durch Kauf und Verkauf gefälschter Waaren „to turn an\n honest penny [Note: Russ ist bekanntlich eine sehr energische Form des Kohlenstoffs und\n bildet ein Düngmittel, das kapitalistische Schornsteinfeger an englische Pächter\n verkaufen. Es ereignete sich nun 1862 ein Prozess, worin der britische „Juryman“\n entscheiden sollte, ob Russ, welchem ohne Wissen des Käufers 90 % Staub und\n Sand beigemischt sind, „wirklicher“ Russ im „commerciellen“ Sinn oder „ge-\n fälschter“ Russ im „gesetzlichen“ Sinn sei. Die „amis du commerce“ entschie-\n den, es sei „wirklicher“ commercieller Russ, und wiesen den klagenden Pächter\n ab, der noch obendrein die Prozesskosten zu zahlen hatte.] .“ Das Comité selbst formulirte mehr oder minder naiv\n seine Ueberzeugung, dass Freihandel wesentlich den Handel mit gefälsch-\n ten, oder wie der Engländer es witzig nennt, „sophisticirten Stoffen“ be-\n deute. In der That, diese Art „Sophistik“ versteht es besser als\nProtagoras schwarz aus weiss und weiss aus schwarz zu machen, und\n besser als die Eleaten den blossen Schein alles Realen ad oculos zu\n demonstriren [Note: Der französische Chemiker Chevallier, in einer Abhandlung über die\n „sophistications“ der Waaren, zählt unter 600 und einigen Artikeln, die er Revue\n passiren lässt, für viele derselben 10, 20, 30 verschiedne Methoden der Fälschung\n auf. Er fügt hinzu, er kenne nicht alle Methoden und erwähne nicht alle, die er\n kenne. Für den Zucker giebt er 6 Fälschungsarten, 9 für das Olivenöl, 10 für\n die Butter, 12 für das Salz, 19 für die Milch, 20 für das Brod, 23 für den Brannt-\n wein, 24 für Mehl, 28 für Chokolade, 30 für Wein, 32 für Kaffee etc.] .\n\n\n\n\nJedenfalls hatte das Comité die Augen des Publikums auf sein „täg-\n liches Brod“ und damit auf die Bäckerei gelenkt. Gleichzeitig er-\n scholl in öffentlichen Meetings und Petitionen an das Parlament der Schrei\n der Londoner Bäckergesellen über Ueberarbeitung u. s. w. Der\n Schrei wurde so dringend, dass Herr H. S. Tremenheere, auch Mitglied der\n mehrerwähnten Kommission von 1863, zum königlichen Untersuchungs-\n kommissär bestallt wurde. Sein Bericht [Note: „Report etc. relating to the Grievances complained of by\n the Journeymen Bakers etc. London 1862“ und „Second Report\n etc. London 1863.“] , sammt Zeugenaussagen,\n regte das Publikum auf, nicht sein Herz, sondern seinen Magen. Der\n bibelfeste Engländer wusste zwar, dass der Mensch, wenn nicht durch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"219\", \"facs\": \"#f0238\"} -->Gnadenwahl Kapitalist oder Landlord oder Sinekurist, dazu be-\n rufen ist, sein Brod im Schweisse seines Angesichts zu essen, aber er\n wusste nicht, dass er in seinem Brode täglich ein gewisses Quantum\nMenschenschweiss essen muss, getränkt mit Eiterbeulenauslee-\n rung, Spinnweb, schwarzen Käfer-Leichnamen und fauler deutscher Hefe,\n abgesehn von Alaun, Sandstein und sonstigen angenehmen mineralischen\n Ingredienzen. Ohne alle Rücksicht auf Seine Heiligkeit, den „Free-\n trade“, wurde daher die anhero „freie“ Bäckerei der Aufsicht von Staats-\n inspektoren unterworfen (Ende der Parlamentssitzung 1863) und durch\n denselben Parlamentsakt die Arbeitszeit von 9 Uhr Abends bis 5 Uhr Mor-\n gens für Bäckergesellen unter 18 Jahren verboten. Die letztere Klausel\n spricht Bände über die Ueberarbeitung in diesem uns so altväterisch an-\n heimelnden Geschäftszweig.\n\n\n\n\n„Die Arbeit eines Londoner Bäckergesellen beginnt in der Regel\n um 11 Uhr Nachts. Zu dieser Stunde macht er den Teig, ein sehr müh-\n samer Prozess, der ½ bis ¾ Stunden währt, je nach der Grösse des Ge-\n bäcks und seiner Feinheit. Er legt sich dann nieder auf das Kneetbrett,\n das zugleich als Deckel des Trogs dient, worin der Teig gemacht wird,\n und schläft ein paar Stunden mit einem Mehlsack unter dem Kopf und\n einem andern Mehlsack auf dem Leib. Dann beginnt eine rasche und un-\n unterbrochene Arbeit von 4 Stunden, Werfen, Wägen, Formen, in den\n Ofen schieben, aus dem Ofen holen u. s. w. des Teiges. Die Temperatur\n eines Backhauses beträgt von 75 bis 90 Grad und in den kleinen Back-\n häusern eher mehr als weniger. Wenn das Geschäft Brod, Wecken u. s. w.\n zu machen vollbracht ist, beginnt die Vertheilung des Brods; und ein be-\n trächtlicher Theil der Taglöhner, nachdem er die beschriebene harte Nacht-\n arbeit vollbracht, trägt während des Tags das Brod in Körben, oder\n schiebt es in Karren, von Haus zu Haus und operirt dazwischen auch manch-\n mal im Backhaus. Je nach der Jahreszeit und dem Umfang des Geschäfts\n endet die Arbeit zwischen 1 und 6 Uhr Nachmittags, während ein andrer\n Theil der Gesellen bis spät um Mitternacht im Backhaus beschäftigt ist [Note: l. c. First Report etc. p. XL.] “.\n „Während der Londoner Saison beginnen die Gesellen der Bäcker zu\n „vollen“ Brodpreisen im Westend regelmässig um 11 Uhr Nachts, und\n sind mit dem Brodbacken, unterbrochen durch einen oder zwei oft sehr\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"220\", \"facs\": \"#f0239\"} --> kurze Zwischenräume, bis 8 Uhr des nächsten Morgens beschäftigt. Sie\n werden dann bis 4, 5, 6, ja 7 Uhr zur Brodherumträgerei vernutzt oder\n manchmal mit Biscuitbacken im Backhaus. Nach vollbrachtem Werk ge-\n niessen sie einen Schlaf von 6, oft nur von 5 und 4 Stunden. Freitags\n beginnt die Arbeit stets früher, sage Abends 10 Uhr und dauert ohne Unter-\n lass, sei es in der Zubereitung, sei es in der Colportirung des Brods, bis\n den folgenden Samstag Abend 8 Uhr, aber meist bis 4 oder 5 Uhr in Sonn-\n tag Nacht hinein. Auch in den vornehmen Bäckereien, die das Brod zum\n „vollen Preise“ verkaufen, muss wieder 4—5 Stunden am Sonntag vorbe-\n reitende Arbeit für den nächsten Tag verrichtet werden … Die Bäcker-\n gesellen der „underselling masters“ (die das Brod unter dem\n vollen Preise verkaufen), und diese betragen, wie früher bemerkt, über ¾\n der Londoner Bäcker, haben noch längere Arbeitsstunden, aber ihre Arbeit\n ist fast ganz auf das Backhaus beschränkt, da ihre Meister, die Lieferung\n an kleine Kramläden ausgenommen, nur in der eignen Boutique verkaufen.\n Gegen „Ende“ der Woche, d. h. am Donnerstag beginnt hier die Arbeit\n um 10 Uhr in der Nacht und dauert bis tief in Sonntag Nacht hinein [Note: l. c. p. LXXI.] .“\n\n\n\n\nVon den „underselling masters“ begreift selbst der bürgerliche\n Standpunkt: „die unbezahlte Arbeit der Gesellen (the unpaid la-\n bour of the men) bildet die Grundlage ihrer Konkurrenz“ [Note: George Read: „The History of Baking. London 1848,“\n p. 16.] . Und der „full pri-\n ced baker“ denunzirt seine „underselling“ Konkurrenten der Untersuchungs-\n kommission als Diebe fremder Arbeit und Fälscher. „Sie reussiren nur\n durch den Betrug des Publikums und dadurch dass sie 18 Stunden aus\n ihren Gesellen für einen Lohn von 12 Stunden herausschlagen [Note: Report (First) etc. Evidence. Aussage des „full priced baker“\n Cheeseman p. 108. Dass der Lohn für 12 Stunden den vielleicht nur 6stündigen\n Werth der Arbeitskraft ausdrückt, und 6 Stunden daher „unbezahlt“ sind von den\n 12, ahnt der „full priced“ Mr. Cheeseman schwerlich.] .“\n\n\n\n\nDie Brodfälschung und die Bildung einer Bäckerklasse, die das Brod\nunter dem vollen Preise verkauft, entwickelten sich in England seit An-\n fang des 18. Jahrhunderts, sobald der Zunftcharakter des Gewerbs verfiel\n und der Kapitalist in der Gestalt von Müller oder Mehlfaktor hinter\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"221\", \"facs\": \"#f0240\"} --> den nominellen Bäckermeister trat [Note: George Read l. c. Ende des 17. und Anfangs des 18. Jahrhunderts\n wurden die in alle möglichen Gewerbe sich eindrängenden Factors (Agenten) noch\n officiell als „Public Nuisances“ denuncirt. So erliess z. B. die Grand Jury, bei\n der vierteljährigen Friedensrichtersitzung in der Grafschaft Somerset, ein „present-\n ment“ an das Unterhaus, worin es u. a. heisst: „that these factors of Plackwell\n Hall are a Publick Nuisance and Prejudice to the Clothing Trade, and ought to be\n put down as a Nuisance.“ („The Case of our English Wool etc. Lon-\n don 1685“, p. 6, 7.)] . Damit war die Grundlage zur\n kapitalistischen Produktion, zur masslosen Verlängerung des Arbeitstages\n und Nachtarbeit gelegt, obgleich letztere selbst in London erst 1824 ernst-\n haft Fuss fasste [Note: First Report etc. p. VIII.] .\n\n\n\n\nMan wird nach dem Vorhergehenden verstehn, dass der Kommis-\n sionsbericht die Bäckergesellen zu den kurzlebigen Arbeitern zählt, die,\n nachdem sie der unter allen Theilen der Arbeiterklasse normalen Kinder-\n decimation glücklich entwischt sind, selten das 42. Lebensjahr erreichen.\n Nichts desto weniger ist das Bäckergewerb stets mit Kandidaten über-\n füllt. Die Zufuhrquellen dieser „Arbeitskräfte“ für London sind Schott-\n land, die westlichen Agrikulturdistrikte Englands und — Deutsch-\n land.\n\n\n\n\nIn den Jahren 1858—1860 organisirten die Bäckergesellen in Ir-\n land auf ihre eigenen Kosten grosse Meetings zur Agitation gegen die\n Nachtarbeit und das Arbeiten an Sonntagen. Das Publikum, z. B. auf\n dem Maimeeting zu Dublin, 1860, ergriff, der zündenden Natur des Irlän-\n ders gemäss, überall lebhaft Partei für sie. Ausschliessliche Tagesarbeit\n wurde durch diese Bewegung in der That erfolgreich durchgesetzt zu\n Wexford, Kilkenny, Clonmel, Waterford u. s. w. „Zu Limerick, wo die\n Qualen der Lohngesellen bekanntermassen alles Mass überstiegen, schei-\n terte die Bewegung an der Opposition der Bäckermeister, namentlich der\n Bäcker-Müller. Das Beispiel Limerick’s führte zum Rückschritt in Ennis\n und Tipperary. Zu Cork, wo der öffentliche Unwille sich in der lebhaf-\n testen Form kundgab, vereitelten die Meister die Bewegung durch den\n Gebrauch ihrer Macht die Gesellen an die Luft zu setzen. Zu Dublin lei-\n steten die Meister den entschiedensten Widerstand und zwangen durch Ver-\n folgung der Gesellen, die an der Spitze der Agitation standen, den Rest\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"222\", \"facs\": \"#f0241\"} --> zum Nachgeben, zur Fügung in die Nacht- und Sonntagsarbeit [Note: „Report of Committee on the Baking Trade in Ireland\n for 1861.“] .“\n Die Kommission der in Irland bis an die Zähne gewaffneten englischen\n Regierung remonstrirt leichenbitterlich gegen die unerbittlichen Bäckermei-\n ster von Dublin, Limerick, Cork u. s. w.: „Das Comité glaubt, dass die Ar-\n beitsstunden durch Naturgesetze beschränkt sind, die nicht ungestraft\n verletzt werden. Indem die Meister durch die Drohung sie fortzujagen,\n ihre Arbeiter zur Verletzung ihrer religiösen Ueberzeugung, zum Unge-\n horsam gegen das Landesgesetz und die Verachtung der öffentlichen Mei-\n nung zwingen,“ (diess letztere bezieht sich alles auf die Sonntagsarbeit),\n „setzen sie böses Blut zwischen Kapital und Arbeit und geben ein Bei-\n spiel, gefährlich für Religion, Moralität und öffentliche Ordnung … Das\n Comité glaubt, dass die Verlängerung des Arbeitstags über 12 Stunden\n ein usurpatorischer Eingriff in das häusliche und Privatleben des\n Arbeiters ist und so zu unheilvollen moralischen Resultaten führt, durch\nEinmischung in die Häuslichkeit eines Mannes und die\n Erfüllung seiner Familienpflichten als Sohn, Bruder, Gatte und\n Vater. Arbeit über 12 Stunden hat die Tendenz die Gesundheit des Ar-\n beiters zu untergraben, führt zu vorzeitiger Alterung und frühem\n Tod und daher zum Unglück der Arbeiterfamilien, die der Vorsorge und\n der Stütze des Familienhaupts grade im nothwendigsten Augenblick be-\n raubt werden“ („are deprived“) [Note: l. c.] .\n\n\n\n\nWir waren eben in Irland. Auf der andern Seite des Kanals, in\nSchottland, denuncirt der Ackerbauarbeiter, der Mann des\n Pfluges, seine 13—14stündige Arbeit, im rauhsten Klima, mit vierstün-\n diger Zusatzarbeit für den Sonntag, (in diesem Lande der Sabbat-Hei-\n ligen!) [Note: Oeffentliches Meeting der Agrikulturarbeiter in Lass-\n wade, bei Glasgow, vom 5. Jan. 1866. (Sieh „Workman’s Advocate“\n vom 13. Jan. 1866.) Die Bildung, seit Ende 1865, einer Trade’s Union unter\n den Agrikulturarbeitern, zunächst in Schottland, ist ein historisches Ereigniss.] , während vor einer Londoner Grand Jury gleichzeitig drei Eisen-\n bahnarbeiter stehn, ein Personencondukteur, ein Lokomotivenführer und\n ein Signalgeber. Ein grosses Eisenbahnunglück hat Hunderte von Passa-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"223\", \"facs\": \"#f0242\"} --> gieren in die andere Welt expedirt. Die Nachlässigkeit der Eisen-\n bahnarbeiter ist die Ursache des Unglücks. Sie erklären vor den Ge-\n schworenen einstimmig, vor 10 bis 12 Jahren habe ihre Arbeit nur 8\n Stunden täglich gedauert. Während der letzten 5—6 Jahre habe man sie\n auf 14, 18 und 20 Stunden aufgeschraubt und bei besonders lebhaftem Zu-\n drang der Reiselustigen, wie in den Perioden der Excursion-trains, währe\n sie oft ununterbrochen 40—50 Stunden. Sie seien gewöhnliche Men-\n schen und keine Cyklopen. Auf einem gegebnen Punkt versage ihre Ar-\n beitskraft. Torpor ergreife sie. Ihr Hirn höre auf zu denken und ihr\n Auge zu sehn. Der ganz und gar „respectable British Juryman“ antwortet\n durch ein Verdikt, das sie wegen „manslaughter“ (Todtschlag) vor die\n nächsten Assisen schickt, und in einem milden Anhang den frommen\n Wunsch äussert, die Herren Kapitalmagnaten der Eisenbahn möchten doch\n in Zukunft verschwenderischer im Ankauf der nöthigen Anzahl von „Ar-\n beitskräften“ und „enthaltsamer“ oder „entsagender“ oder\n „sparsamer“ in der Aussaugung der bezahlten Arbeitskraft sein [Note: „Reynold’s Paper“ vom 20. Jan. 1866. Woche für Woche bringt\n dasselbe Wochenblatt gleich darauf, unter den „sensational leadings: „Fearful\n and fatal accidents“ „Appalling tragedies“ u. s. w., eine ganze Liste neuer Eisen-\n bahnkatastrophen. Darauf antwortet ein Arbeiter von der North Staffordlinie:\n „Jedermann kennt die Folgen, wenn die Aufmerksamkeit von Lokomotivenführer\n und Heizer einen Augenblick erlahmt. Und wie ist es anders möglich bei mass-] !\n\n\n\n\nAus dem buntscheckigen Haufen der Arbeiter von allen Professionen,\n Altern, Geschlechtern, die eifriger auf uns andrängen als die Seelen der\n Erschlagenen auf den Odysseus, und denen man, ohne die Blaubücher\n unter ihren Armen, auf den ersten Blick die Ueberarbeit ansieht, greifen\n wir noch zwei Figuren heraus, deren frappanter Kontrast beweist, dass\n vor dem Kapital alle Menschen gleich sind, — eine Putzmacherin\n und einen Grobschmidt.\n\n\n\n\nIn den letzten Wochen von Juni 1863 brachten alle Londoner\n Tagesblätter einen Paragraph mit dem „sensational“ Aushängeschild:\n „Death from simple Overwork“ (Tod von einfacher Ueber-\n arbeit). Es handelte sich um den Tod der Putzmacherin Mary Anne\n Walkley, zwanzigjährig, beschäftigt in einer sehr respektablen Hofputz-\n manufaktur, exploitirt von einer Dame mit dem gemüthlichen Namen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"224\", \"facs\": \"#f0243\"} -->Elise. Die alte oft erzählte Geschichte ward nun neuentdeckt [Note: Vgl. F. Engels l. c. p. 253, 254.] , dass\n diese Mädchen durchschnittlich 16½ Stunden, während der Saison aber\n oft 30 Stunden ununterbrochen arbeiten, indem ihre versagende „Arbeits-\n kraft“ durch gelegentliche Zufuhr von Sherry, Portwein oder Kaffee flüssig\n erhalten wird. Und es war grade die Höhe der Saison. Es galt die\n Prachtkleider edler Ladies für den Huldigungsball bei der frisch importir-\n ten Prinzessin von Wales im Umsehn fertig zu zaubern. Mary Anne\n Walkley hatte 26½ Stunden ohne Unterlass gearbeitet zusammen mit 60\n andern Mädchen, je 30 in einem Zimmer, das kaum ⅓ der nöthigen\n Kubikzoll Luft gewährte, während sie Nachts zwei zu zwei Ein Bett theil-\n ten in einem der Sticklöcher, worin Ein Schlafzimmer durch verschiedne\n Bretterwände abgepfercht ist [Note: Dr. Letheby, beim Board of Health funktionirender Arzt, er-\n klärte damals: „Das Minimum für jeden Erwachsnen in einem Schlafzimmer\n sollte 300 Kubikfuss und in einem Wohnzimmer 500 Kubikfuss Luft sein.“\n Dr. Richardson, Oberarzt eines Londoner Hospitals: „Nätherinnen aller\n Art, Putzmacherinnen, Kleidermacherinnen und gewöhnliche Näherinnen, leiden\n an dreifachem Elend — Ueberarbeit, Luftmangel und Mangel an Nahrung oder\n Mangel an Verdauung. Im Ganzen passt diese Art Arbeit für Weiber, besser unter\n allen Umständen als für Männer. Aber das Unheil des Geschäfts ist, dass es,\n namentlich in der Hauptstadt, von einigen 26 Kapitalisten monopolisirt wird, die\n durch Machtmittel, welche dem Kapital entspringen, (that spring from capital)\nOekonomie aus der Arbeit herauszwingen, (force economy out of labour; er\n meint, Auslagen ökonomisiren durch Verschwendung der Arbeitskraft). Diese] . Und diess war eine der besseren Putz-\n [Note: loser Verlängerung der Arbeit, im rauhsten Wetter, ohne Pause und Erholung?\n Nehmt als ein Beispiel, wie es täglich vorkommt, folgenden Fall. Letzten Montag\n begann ein Heizer sehr früh Morgens sein Tageswerk. Er endete es nach 14\n Stunden, 50 Minuten. Bevor er auch nur die Zeit hatte, seinen Thee zu nehmen,\n wurde er von neuem an die Arbeit gerufen. Er hatte also 29 Stunden, 15 Mi-\n nuten ununterbrochen durchzuschanzen. Der Rest seines Wochenwerks wurde\n aufgemacht wie folgt: Mittwoch, 15 Stunden; Donnerstag 15 Stunden, 35 Mi-\n nuten; Freitag 14½ Stunden; Sonnabend 14 Stunden, 10 Minuten; zusammen\n für die Woche 88 Stunden, 40 Minuten. Und nun denkt euch sein Erstaunen, als\n er nur Zahlung für 6 Arbeitstage erhielt. Der Mann war ein Neuling und fragte,\n was man unter einem Tagewerk verstehe. Antwort: 13 Stunden, also 78 Stunden\n per Woche. Aber wie mit der Zahlung für die überschüssigen 10 Stunden, 40\n Minuten? Nach langem Hader erhielt er eine Vergütung von 10 d. (noch nicht\n 10 Silbergroschen).“ l. c. Nr. vom 4. Februar, 1866.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"225\", \"facs\": \"#f0244\"} --> machereien Londons. Mary Anne Walkley erkrankte am Freitag und\n starb am Sonntag, ohne, zum Erstaunen von Frau Elise, auch nur vorher\n das letzte Putzstück fertig zu machen. Der zu spät ans Sterbebett geru-\n fene Arzt, Herr Keys, bezeugte vor der Coroner’s Jury in dürren\n Worten: „Mary Anne Walkley sei gestorben an langen Arbeits-\n stunden in einem überfüllten Arbeitszimmer und überengem schlecht-\n ventilirten Schlafgemache.“ Um dem Arzt eine Lektion in guter Lebens-\n art zu geben, erklärte dagegen die „Coroner’s Jury“: „Die Hingeschie-\n dene sei gestorben an der Apoplexie, aber es sei Grund zu\nfürchten, dass ihr Tod durch Ueberarbeit in einer überfüllten Werk-\n statt u. s. w. beschleunigt worden sei.“ Unsere „weissen Skla-\n ven,“ rief der Morning Star, das Organ der Freihandelsherrn Cobden\n und Bright, „unsere weissen Sklaven werden in das Grab hin-\n eingearbeitet und verderben und sterben ohne Sang und Klang [Note: Morning Star, 23. Juni 1863. Die Times benutzte den Vorfall\n zur Vertheidigung der amerikanischen Sklavenhalter gegen Bright u. s. w. „Sehr\n viele von uns“, sagt sie, „meinen, dass so lange wir unsre eignen jungen Frauen-\n zimmer zu Tode arbeiten, mit der Geissel des Hungers statt dem Knall der Peitsche,\n wir kaum das Recht haben, Feuer und Schwert auf Familien zu hetzen, die als\n Sklavenhalter geboren waren und ihre Sklaven mindestens gut nähren und mässig\n arbeiten lassen.“ (Times, Juli 2, 1863.) In derselben Weise kanzelte der\nStandard, ein Toryblatt, den Rev. Newman Hall ab: „er excommunicire die\n Sklavenhalter, bete aber mit den braven Leuten, die Kutscher und Omnibusführer\n von London u. s. w. nur 16 Stunden täglich für einen Hundelohn arbeiten liessen“.] “.\n\n\n\n\n [Note: Macht wird im Bereich der ganzen Klasse von Arbeiterinnen gefühlt. Kann eine\n Kleidermacherin einen kleinen Kreis von Kunden gewinnen, so zwingt die Kon-\n kurrenz sie, sich zu Hause todt zu arbeiten, um ihn zu erhalten, und mit derselben\n Ueberarbeit muss sie nothwendig ihre Gehülfinnen heimsuchen. Misslingt ihr\n Geschäft oder kann sie sich nicht selbstständig etabliren, so wendet sie sich an ein\n Etablissement, wo die Arbeit nicht geringer, aber die Zahlung sicher ist. So ge-\n stellt wird sie eine reine Sklavin, hin und her geschleudert von jeder Flu-\n thung der Gesellschaft; bald zu Hause in einem kleinen Zimmer verhungernd,\n oder nahe so; dann wieder von 24 Stunden 15, 16, ja 18 Stunden beschäftigt in\n kaum erträglicher Luft, und mit einer Nahrung, die, selbst wenn gut, wegen Ab-\n wesenheit reiner Luft nicht verdaut werden kann. Von diesen Opfern lebt die\n Schwindsucht, welche nichts als eine Luftkrankheit ist.“ (Dr. Richardson:\n „Death from simple Overwork“ in „Social Science Review“,\n Juliheft, 1863.)] \n\n\n [Running matter: I. 15] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"226\", \"facs\": \"#f0245\"} -->\n\n„Zu Tod arbeiten ist die Tagesordnung, nicht nur in der Werk-\n stätte der Putzmacherinnen, sondern in tausend Plätzen, ja an jedem Platz,\n wo das Geschäft im Zug ist … Lasst uns den Grobschmidt als Bei-\n spiel nehmen. Wenn man den Dichtern glauben darf, giebt es keinen so\n lebenskräftigen, lustigen Mann als den Grobschmidt. Er erhebt sich früh\n und schlägt Funken vor der Sonne; er isst und trinkt und schläft wie\n kein andrer Mensch. Rein physisch betrachtet, befindet er sich, bei mäs-\n siger Arbeit, in der That in einer der besten menschlichen Stellungen.\n Aber wir folgen ihm in die Stadt, und sehn die Arbeitslast, die auf den\n starken Mann gewälzt wird, und welchen Rang nimmt er ein in den Sterb-\n lichkeitslisten unsres Landes? Zu Marylebone (einem der grössten Stadt-\n viertel Londons) sterben Grobschmidte in dem Verhältniss von 31 per\n 1000 jährlich, oder 11 über der Durchschnittssterblichkeit erwachsener\n Männer in England. Die Beschäftigung, eine fast instinktive Kunst der\n Menschheit, an und für sich tadellos, wird durch blosse Uebertreibung der\n Arbeit, der Zerstörer des Mannes. Er kann so viel Hammerschläge täg-\n lich schlagen, so viel Schritte gehn, so viel Athemzüge holen, so viel Werk\n verrichten, und durchschnittlich sage 50 Jahre leben. Man zwingt ihn\n so viel mehr Schläge zu schlagen, so viel mehr Schritte zu gehn, so viel\n öfter des Tags zu athmen, und alles zusammen seine Lebensaus-\n gabe täglich um ein Viertel zu vermehren. Er macht den\n Versuch, und das Resultat ist, dass er für eine beschränkte Periode ein\n Viertel mehr Werk verrichtet und im 37. Jahr statt im 50. stirbt [Note: Dr. Richardson. l. c.] “.\n\n\n\n\nDas konstante Kapital, die Produktionsmittel, sind, vom Standpunkt\n des Verwerthungsprozesses betrachtet, nur da, um Arbeit, und\n [Note: Endlich sprach das Orakel, Herr Thomas Carlyle, von dem ich schon\n 1850 drucken liess: „Zum Teufel ist der Genius, der Kultus ist geblieben.“\n In einer kurzen Parabel reducirt er das einzig grossartige Ereigniss der Zeitge-\n schichte, den amerikanischen Bürgerkrieg, darauf, dass der Peter vom Norden dem\n Paul vom Süden mit aller Gewalt den Hirnschädel einschlagen will, weil der Peter\n vom Norden seinen Arbeiter „täglich“ und der Paul vom Süden ihn für „Leb-\n zeit miethet.“ (Macmillian’s Magazine. Ilias Americana in\n nuce. Augustheft 1863.) So ist endlich die Schaumblase der Torysympathie für\n den städtischen, bei Leibe nicht den ländlichen! — Lohnarbeiter ge-\n platzt. Der Kern heisst — Sklaverei!] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"227\", \"facs\": \"#f0246\"} --> mit jedem Tropfen Arbeit ein proportionelles Quantum Mehr-\n arbeit einzusaugen. So weit sie das nicht thun, bildet ihre blosse\n Existenz einen negativen Verlust für den Kapitalisten, denn sie\n repräsentiren während der Zeit, wo sie brach liegen, nutzlosen Kapital-\n vorschuss, und dieser Verlust wird positiv, sobald sie während der In-\n tervalle des Arbeitsprozesses zusätzliche Auslagen erheischen, um sie für\n den Wiederbeginn des Werks im Gang zu erhalten. Die Verlänge-\n rungdes Arbeitstags über die Grenzen des natürlichen\n Tags in die Nacht hinein wirkt nur als Palliativ, stillt nur an-\n nähernd den Vampyrdurst nach lebendigem Arbeitsblut. Arbeit während\naller 24 Stunden des Tags anzueignen, ist daher der immanente Trieb\n der kapitalistischen Produktion. Da diess aber physisch unmöglich, wür-\n den dieselben Arbeitskräfte Tag und Nacht fortwährend ausgesaugt,\n so bedarf es, zur Ueberwindung des physischen Hindernisses, der Ab-\n wechslung zwischen den bei Tag und bei Nacht verspeisten Arbeits-\n kräften, eine Abwechslung, die verschiedne Methoden zulässt, z. B. so ge-\n ordnet sein kann, dass ein Theil des Arbeiterpersonals eine Woche Tagdienst,\n Nachtdienst die andre Woche versieht u. s. w. Man weiss, dass dieses Ablö-\n sungssystem, diese Wechselwirthschaft, in der vollblütigen Jugend-\n periode der englischen Baumwollindustrie u. s. w. vorherrschte, und u. a.\n gegenwärtig in den Baumwollspinnereien von Moskau blüht. Als Sy-\n stem existirt dieser 24stündige Produktionsprozess heute noch in vielen\n bis jetzt „freien“ Industriezweigen Grossbritaniens, u. a. in den Hoch-\n öfen, Schmieden, Walzwerken und andern Metallmanufakturen von Eng-\n land, Wales und Schottland. Der Arbeitsprozess umfasst hier ausser den\n 24 Stunden der 6 Werkeltage grossentheils auch die 24 Stunden des\n Sonntags. Die Arbeiter bestehn aus Männern und Weibern, Erwachsenen\n und Kindern beiderlei Geschlechts. Das Alter der Kinder und jungen Per-\n sonen durchläuft alle Zwischenstufen vom 8. (in einigen Fällen vom 6.)\n bis zum 18. Jahr [Note: „Children’s Employment Commission.“ Third Report.\n Lond. 1864, p. IV, V, VII.] . In einigen Branchen arbeiten auch die Mädchen\n und Weiber des Nachts zusammen mit dem männlichen Personal [Note: „Both in Staffordshire and in South Wales young girls and women are\n employed on the pit banks and on the coke heaps, not only by day, but also by] .\n\n\n [Running matter: 15*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"228\", \"facs\": \"#f0247\"} -->\n\nVon den allgemeinen schädlichen Wirkungen der Nachtarbeit abge-\n sehn [Note: „Es scheint natürlich,“ bemerkte ein Stahlfabrikant, der Kinder zur\n Nachtarbeit verwendet, „dass die Jungen, die Nachts arbeiten, bei Tag nicht\n schlafen und keine ordentliche Ruhe finden können, sondern rastlos am nächsten\n Tag herumlaufen.“ l. c. Fourth Rep. 63, p. XIII. Ueber die Wichtigkeit des\nSonnenlichts zur Erhaltung und Entwicklung des Körpers bemerkt ein Arzt\n u. a.: „Licht wirkt auch direkt auf die Gewebe des Leibes, denen es Härte und\n Elasticität giebt. Die Muskeln von Thieren, denen man das normale Quantum\n Licht vorenthält, werden schwammig und unelastisch, die Nervenkraft verliert\n ihren Ton durch Mangel an Stimulirung, und die Ausarbeitung von allem was im\n Wachsthum begriffen ist, wird verkümmert … Im Fall von Kindern ist beständiger\n Zutritt von reichlichem Tageslicht und der direkten Sonnenstrahlen während eines\n Theils des Tags durchaus wesentlich für die Gesundheit. Licht hilft die Speisen\n zu gutem plastischem Blut verarbeiten und härtet die Fiber, nachdem sie gebildet\n ist. Es wirkt ebenso als Reizmittel auf die Sehorgane und ruft hierdurch grössere\n Thätigkeit in verschiednen Hirnfunktionen hervor.“ Herr W. Strange, Oberarzt\n des Worcester „General Hospital“, aus dessen Schrift über „Gesundheit“\n (1864) diese Stelle entlehnt ist, schreibt in einem Brief an einen der Untersuchungs-\n kommissaire, Herrn White: „Ich habe früher in Lancashire Gelegenheit gehabt,\n die Wirkungen der Nachtarbeit auf Fabrikkinder zu beobachten, und im Wider-\n spruch zu der beliebten Versicherung einiger Arbeitsgeber, er-\n kläre ich mit Entschiedenheit, dass die Gesundheit der Kinder schwer davon\n leidet.“ (l. c. 284, p. 55.) Dass solche Dinge überhaupt den Gegenstand ernst-\n hafter Kontroversen bilden, zeigt am besten, wie die kapitalistische Produktion\n auf die „Gehirnfunktionen“ der Kapitalisten und ihrer retainers wirkt.] , bietet die ununterbrochene, vierundzwanzigstündige Dauer des\n Produktionsprozesses höchst willkommene Gelegenheit die Grenze des nomi-\n nellen Arbeitstags zu überschreiten. Z. B. in den vorhin erwähnten,\n sehr anstrengenden Industriezweigen beträgt der officielle Arbeitstag für\n jeden Arbeiter meist 12 Stunden, Nachtstunden oder Tagesstunden.\n Aber die Ueberarbeit über diese Grenze hinaus ist in vielen Fällen, um\n die Worte des englischen officiellen Berichts zu brauchen, „wirklich\n [Note: night. This practice has been often noticed in Reports presented to Parliament,\n as being attended with great and notorious evils. These females, employed with\n the men, hardly distinguished from them in their dress, and begrimed with dirt\n and smoke, are exposed to the deterioration of character arising from the loss of\n self-respect which can hardly fail to follow from their unfeminine occupation.“\n l. c. 194, p. XXVI. Vgl. Fourth Report (1865) 61, p. XIII. Ebenso in\nGlasfabriken.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"229\", \"facs\": \"#f0248\"} -->schauderhaft“ („truly fearful“) [Note: l. c. 57, p. XII.] . „Kein menschliches Gemüth,“\n heisst es, „kann die Arbeitmasse, die nach den Zeugenaussagen durch\n Knaben von 9 bis 12 Jahren verrichtet wird, überdenken, ohne unwider-\n stehlich zum Schlusse zu kommen, dass dieser Machtmissbrauch der\n Eltern und Arbeitgeber nicht länger erlaubt werden darf [Note: l. c. (4th Rep. 1865) 58, p. XII.] .“\n\n\n\n\n„Die Methode Knaben überhaupt abwechselnd Tag und Nacht arbei-\n ten zu lassen, führt, sowohl während des Geschäftsdrangs als während\n des gewöhnlichen Verlaufs der Dinge, zu schmählicher Verlängerung des\n Arbeitstags. Diese Verlängerung ist in vielen Fällen nicht nurgrau-\n sam, sondern gradezu unglaublich. Es kann nicht fehlen, dass\n aus einer oder der andern Ursache ein Ablösungsknabe hier und da weg-\n bleibt. Einer oder mehrere der anwesenden Knaben, die ihren Arbeitstag\n bereits vollbracht, müssen dann den Ausfall gut machen. Diess System\n ist so allgemein bekannt, dass der manager eines Walzwerks, auf meine\n Frage, wie die Stelle der abwesenden Ersatzknaben ausgefüllt würde, ant-\n wortete: Ich weiss wohl, dass Sie das eben so gut wissen als ich, und er\n nahm keinen Anstand die Thatsache zu gestehn [Note: l. c.] .“\n\n\n\n\n„In einem Walzwerke, wo der nominelle Arbeitstag für den einzelnen\n Arbeiter 11½ Stunden war, arbeitete ein Junge 4 Nächte jede Woche\n bis mindestens 8½ Uhr Abends des nächsten Tags und diess während der\n 6 Monate, wo er engagirt war.“ „Ein Andrer arbeitete im Alter von\n 9 Jahren manchmal drei zwölfstündige Ablösungstermine\n nacheinander, und im Alter von 10 Jahren, zwei Tage und zwei\n Nächte nach einander.“ „Ein Dritter, jetzt 10 Jahre, arbeitete\n von Morgens 6 Uhr bis 12 Uhr in die Nacht während drei Nächten und\n bis 9 Uhr Abends während der andren Nächte.“ „Ein Vierter, jetzt 13\n Jahre, arbeitete von 6 Uhr Nachmittags bis den andern Tag 12 Uhr Mit-\n tags, während einer ganzen Woche, und manchmal drei Ablösungstermine\n nach einander, von Montag Morgen bis Dienstag Nacht.“ „Ein Fünfter,\n jetzt 12 Jahre, arbeitete in einer Eisengiesserei zu Stavely von 6 Uhr\n Morgens bis 12 Uhr Nachts während 14 Tagen, ist unfähig es länger zu\n thun.“ „George Allinsworth, neunjährig: „Ich kam hierhin\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"230\", \"facs\": \"#f0249\"} --> letzten Freitag. Nächsten Tag hatten wir um 3 Uhr Morgens anzufangen.\n Ich blieb daher die ganze Nacht hier. Wohne 5 Meilen von hier. Schlief\n auf der Flur mit einem Schurzfell unter mir und einer kleinen Jacke über\n mir. Die zwei andern Tage war ich hier um 6 Uhr Morgens. Ja! diess\n ist ein heisser Platz! Bevor ich herkam, arbeitete ich ebenfalls\nwährend eines ganzen Jahres in einem Hochofen. Es war ein\n sehr grosses Werk, auf dem Lande. Begann auch Samstag Morgens um\n 3 Uhr, aber ich konnte wenigstens nach Hause schlafen gehn, weil es nah\n war. An andern Tagen fing ich 6 Uhr Morgens an und endete 6 oder\n 7 Uhr Abends u. s. w. [Note: l. c. p. XIII. Die Bildungsstufe dieser „Arbeitskräfte“ muss natürlich\n so sein, wie sie in folgenden Dialogen mit einem der Untersuchungskommissäre\n erscheint! „Jeremias Haynes, 12 Jahre alt: ‥ Viermal vier ist acht, aber\n vier Vierer (4 fours) sind 16 … Ein König ist ihm der alles Geld und Gold\n hat. (A king is him that has all the money and gold.) Wir haben einen König,\n man sagt, er ist eine Königin, sie nennen sie Prinzessin Alexandra. Man sagt,\n sie heirathete der Königin Sohn. Eine Prinzessin ist ein Mann.“ Wm. Turner,\n zwölfjährig: „Lebe nicht in England. Denke, es giebt solch ein Land, wusste\n nichts davon zuvor.“ John Morris, 14jährig: „Habe sagen hören, dass Gott\n die Welt gemacht und dass alles Volk ersoff, ausser einem; habe gehört, dass\n einer ein kleiner Vogel war.“ William Smith, fünfzehnjährig: „Gott machte\n den Mann; der Mann machte das Weib.“ Edward Taylor, fünfzehnjährig:\n „Weiss nichts von London.“ Henry Matthewman, siebzehnjährig: „Geh’\n manchmal in die Kirche … Ein Name, worüber sie predigten, war ein gewisser\n Jesus Christ, aber ich kann keine andren Namen nennen, und ich kann auch nichts\n über ihn sagen. Er wurde nicht gemordet, sondern starb wie andre Leute. Er\n war nicht so wie andre Leute in gewisser Art, weil er religiös war in gewisser Art,\n und andere ist es nicht.“ (He was not the same as other people in some ways,\n because he was religious in some ways, and others is n’t.“ (l. c. 74, p. XV.) „Der\n Teufel ist eine gute Person. Ich weiss nicht, wo er lebt. Christus war ein schlechter\n Kerl.“ („The devil is a good person. I don’t know where he lives.“ „Christ\n was a wicked man.“) „Diess Mädchen (10 Jahre) buchstabirt God Dog und\n kannte den Namen der Königin nicht.“ („Ch. Empl. Comm. V. Rep. 1866,\n p. 55, n. 278.) Dasselbe System, das in den erwähnten Metallmanufakturen,\n herrscht in den Glas- und Papierfabriken. In den Papierfabriken, wo\n das Papier mit Maschinen gemacht wird, ist Nachtarbeit die Regel für alle\n Prozesse ausser dem der Lumpensortirung. In einigen Fällen wird die Nacht-\n arbeit, vermittelst Ablösungen, unaufhörlich die ganze Woche durch fortgesetzt,\n gewöhnlich von Sonntag Nacht bis 12 Uhr Nacht des folgenden Samstags. Die\n Mannschaft, die sich an der Tagesreihe befindet, arbeitet 5 Tage von 12 und einen] .“\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"231\", \"facs\": \"#f0250\"} -->\n\nLasst uns nun hören, wie das Kapital selbst diess Vierundzwanzig-\n Stundensystem auffasst. Die Uebertreibungen des Systems, seinen Missbrauch\n zur „grausamen und unglaublichen“ Verlängerung des Arbeitstags, über-\n geht es natürlich mit Stillschweigen. Es spricht nur von dem System in\n seiner „normalen“ Form.\n\n\n\n\n„Die Herren Naylor und Vickers, Stahlfabrikanten, die zwischen\n 600 und 700 Personen anwenden, und darunter nur 10 % unter 18 Jah-\n ren, und hiervon wieder nur 20 Knaben zum Nachtpersonal, äussern sich\n wie folgt: „Die Knaben leiden durchaus nicht von der Hitze. Die Tem-\n peratur ist wahrscheinlich 86° bis 90°. . . . . In den Hammer- und\n Walzwerken arbeiten die Hände Tag und Nacht ablösungsweise, aber\n dahingegen ist auch alles andre Werk Tagwerk, von 6 Uhr Morgens bis\n 6 Uhr Abends. In der Schmiede wird von 12 Uhr bis 12 Uhr gearbeitet.\nEinige Hände arbeiten fortwährend des Nachts ohne\n Wechsel zwischen Tag- und Nachtzeit . . . . Wir finden nicht,\n dass Tag- oder Nachtarbeit irgend einen Unterschied in der Gesundheit\n (der Herren Naylor und Vickers?) macht, und wahrscheinlich schla-\n [Note: von 18 Stunden, und die der Nachtreihe 5 Nächte von 12 Stunden und eine von 6\n Stunden, in jeder Woche. In andern Fällen arbeitet jede Reihe 24 Stunden, die\n eine nach der andern, an Wechseltagen. Eine Reihe arbeitet 6 Stunden am Mon-\n tag, und 18 am Samstag, um 24 Stunden vollzumachen. In andern Fällen ist ein\n Zwischensystem eingeführt, worin alle an der Papiermacher-Maschinerie Ange-\n stellten jeden Tag in der Woche 15—16 Stunden arbeiten. Diess System, sagt\n Untersuchungskommissär Lord, scheint alle Uebel der Zwölfstunden- und der\n Vierundzwanzigstunden-Ablösung zu vereinigen. Kinder unter 13 Jahren, junge\n Personen unter 18 und Weiber arbeiten unter diesem Nachtsystem. Manchmal,\n in dem Zwölfstundensystem, mussten sie, wegen Ausbleiben der Ablöser, die\n doppelte Reihe von 24 Stunden arbeiten. Zeugenaussagen beweisen, dass\n Knaben und Mädchen sehr oft Ueberzeit arbeiten, die sich nicht selten zu 24,\n ja 36 Stunden ununterbrochner Arbeit ausdehnt. In dem „kontinuirlichen und\n unveränderlichen“ Prozess der Glasirräume findet man Mädchen von 12 Jahren,\n die den ganzen Monat durch täglich 14 Stunden arbeiten, „ohne irgend eine regel-\n mässige Erholung oder Unterbrechung ausser zwei, höchstens drei halbstündigen\n Ausfällen für Mahlzeiten.“ In einigen Fabriken, wo man die reguläre Nacht-\n arbeit ganz aufgegeben, wird entsetzlich viel Ueberzeit gearbeitet und „diess häufig\n in den schmutzigsten, heissesten und monotonsten Prozessen.“ („Children’s\n Employment Commission. Report IV, 1865,“ p. XXXVIII und\n XXXIX.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"232\", \"facs\": \"#f0251\"} --> fen Leute besser, wenn sie dieselbe Ruheperiode geniessen, als wenn\n sie wechselt . . . . Ungefähr zwanzig Knaben unter 18 Jahren arbeiten\n mit der Nachtmannschaft . . . . Wir könntens nicht recht thun (not well do)\n ohne die Nachtarbeit von Jungen unter 18 Jahren. Unser Einwurf ist —\ndie Vermehrung der Produktionskosten . . . . Geschickte\n Hände und Häupter von Departements sind schwer zu haben, aber Jungens\n kriegt man so viel man will . . . . Natürlich, Anbetrachts der geringen\n Proportion von Jungen, die wir verwenden, wären Beschränkungen\n der Nachtarbeit von wenig Wichtigkeit oder Interesse für\n uns [Note: Fourth Report etc. 1865, 79, p. XVI.] .“\n\n\n\n\n„Herr J. Ellis, von der Firma der Herren John Brown et Co.,\n Stahl- und Eisenwerke, die 3000 Männer und Jungen anwenden, und zwar\n für Theil der schweren Stahl- und Eisenarbeit „Tag und Nacht, in Ablö-\n sungen“, erklärt, dass in den schweren Stahlwerken einer oder zwei Jungen\n auf zwei Männer kommen. Ihr Geschäft zählt 500 Jungen unter 18 Jahren\n und davon ungefähr ⅓, oder 170, unter 13 Jahren. Mit Bezug auf\n die vorgeschlagne Gesetzänderung meint Herr Ellis: „Ich glaube nicht,\n dass es sehr tadelhaft (very objectionable) wäre, keine Person unter\n 18 Jahren über 12 Stunden aus den 24 arbeiten zu lassen. Aber ich\n glaube nicht, dass man irgend eine Linie ziehen kann für die Entbehr-\n lichkeit von Jungen über 12 Jahren für die Nachtarbeit. Wir würden\nsogar eher ein Gesetz annehmen, keine Jungen unter 13 Jahren oder\nselbst so hoch wie 14 Jahre, überhaupt zu verwenden, als ein Ver-\n bot die Jungen, die wir einmal haben, während der Nacht zu brauchen.\n Die Jungen, die in der Tagesreihe, müssen wechselweis auch in der Nachtreihe\n arbeiten, weil die Männer nicht unaufhörlich Nachtarbeit verrichten kön-\n nen; es würde ihre Gesundheit ruiniren. Wir glauben jedoch, dass Nacht-\n arbeit, wenn die Woche dafür wechselt, keinen Schaden thut. (Die Her-\n ren Naylor und Vickers glaubten, übereinstimmend mit dem Besten\n ihres Geschäfts, umgekehrt, dass statt der fortwährenden, grade die perio-\n disch wechselnde Nachtarbeit möglicher Weise Schaden anrichten kann.)\n Wir finden die Leute, die die alternirende Nachtarbeit verrichten, grade\n so gesund als die, die nur am Tage arbeiten . . . . Unsere Einwürfe\n gegen die Nichtanwendung von Jungen unter 18 Jahren zur Nachtarbeit\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"233\", \"facs\": \"#f0252\"} --> würden gemacht werden von wegen Vermehrung der Auslage, aber diess\n ist auch der einzige Grund. (Wie cynisch naiv!) Wir glauben, dass diese\n Vermehrung grösser wäre, als das Geschäft (the trade) mit schuldiger\n Rücksicht auf seine erfolgreiche Ausführung billiger Weise tragen könnte.\n (As the trade with dueregard to etc. could fairly bear! Welche brei-\n mäulige Phraseologie!) Arbeit ist hier rar, und könnte unzureichend wer-\n den unter einer solchen Regulation“ (d. h. Ellis, Brown et Co. könnten\n in die fatale Verlegenheit kommen den Werth der Arbeitskraft voll\n zahlen zu müssen) [Note: l. c. 80.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie „Cyklops Stahl- und Eisenwerke“ der Herren Cammell et Co.\n werden auf derselben grossen Stufenleiter ausgeführt, wie die des besag-\n ten John Brown et Co. Der geschäftsführende Direktor hatte dem Regie-\n rungskommissär White seine Zeugenaussage schriftlich eingehändigt, fand\n es aber später passend, das zur Revision ihm wieder zurückgestellte Manu-\n script zu unterschlagen. Jedoch Herr White hat ein nachhaltiges Gedächt-\n niss. Er erinnert sich ganz genau, dass für diese Herrn Cyklopen das\n Verbot der Nachtarbeit von Kindern und jungen Personen „ein Ding der\n Unmöglichkeit; es wäre dasselbe, als setzte man ihre Werke still“, und\n dennoch zählt ihr Geschäft wenig mehr als 6 % Jungen unter 18 und nur\n 1 % unter 13 Jahren [Note: l. c. 82.] !\n\n\n\n\nUeber denselben Gegenstand erklärt Herr E. F. Sanderson, von\n der Firma Sanderson, Bros. et Co., Stahl-Walz- und Schmiedewerke, in\n Attercliffe: „Grosse Schwierigkeiten würden entspringen aus dem Verbot\n Jungen unter 18 Jahren des Nachts arbeiten zu lassen, die Hauptschwie-\n rigkeit aus der Vermehrung der Kosten, welche ein Ersatz der Knaben-\n arbeit durch Männerarbeit nothwendig nach sich zöge. Wie viel das\n betragen würde, kann ich nicht sagen, aber wahrscheinlich wäre es nicht\n so viel, dass der Fabrikant den Stahlpreis erhöhen könnte, und folglich\n fiele der Verlust auf ihn, da die Männer (welch querköpfig Volk!) na-\n türlich weigern würden, ihn zu tragen.“ Herr Sanderson weiss nicht,\n wie viel er den Kindern zahlt, aber „vielleicht beträgt es 4 bis 5 sh. per\n Kopf die Woche … Die Knabenarbeit ist von einer Art, wofür im All-\n gemeinen („generally,“ natürlich nicht immer „im Besondern“) die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"234\", \"facs\": \"#f0253\"} --> Kraft der Jungens grade ausreicht, und folglich würde kein\n Gewinn aus der grössern Kraft der Männer fliessen, um\n den Verlust zu kompensire, oder doch nur in den wenigen Fäl-\n len, wo das Metall sehr schwer ist. Die Männer würden es auch minder\n lieben keine Knaben unter sich zu haben, da Männer minder gehorsam\n sind. Ausserdem müssen die Jungen jung anfangen, um das Geschäft zu\n lernen. Die Beschränkung der Jungen auf blosse Tagesarbeit würde die-\n sen Zweck nicht erfüllen.“ Und warum nicht? Warum können Jungen\n ihr Handwerk nicht bei Tag lernen? Deinen Grund? „Weil dadurch die\n Männer, die in Wechselwochen bald den Tag, bald die Nacht arbeiten,\n von den Jungen ihrer Reihe während derselben Zeit getrennt, halb den\n Profit verlieren würden, den sie aus ihnen herausschlagen. Die Anleitung,\n die sie den Jungen geben, wird nämlich als Theil des Arbeitslohnes dieser\n Jungen berechnet und befähigt die Männer daher die Jungenarbeit wohl-\n feiler zu bekommen. Jeder Mann würde seinen halben Profit verlieren.\n (In andern Worten, die Herren Sanderson müssten einen Theil des Arbeits-\n lohnes der erwachsenen Männer aus eigner Tasche, statt mit der Nachtarbeit\n der Jungen zahlen. Der Profit der Herren Sanderson würde bei\n dieser Gelegenheit etwas fallen und diess ist der Sanderson’sche\n gute Grund, warum Jungen ihr Handwerk nicht bei Tag lernen\n können [Note: „In unserer reflexionsreichen und raisonnirenden Zeit muss es Einer noch\n nicht weit gebracht haben, der nicht für Alles, auch das Schlechteste und Ver-\n kehrteste, einen guten Grund anzugeben weiss. Alles, was in der Welt ver-\n dorben worden ist, das ist aus guten Gründen verdorben worden.“ (Hegel\n l. c. p. 249.)] .) Ausserdem würde diess reguläre Nachtarbeit auf die\n Männer werfen, die nun von den Jungen abgelöst werden, und sie würden\n das nicht aushalten. Kurz und gut, die Schwierigkeiten wären\n so gross, dass sie wahrscheinlich zur gänzlichen Unterdrückung\n der Nachtarbeit führen würden.“ „Was die Produktion von Stahl\n selbst angeht,“ sagt E. F. Sanderson, „würde es nicht den geringsten\n Unterschied machen, aber!“ Aber die Herren Sanderson haben mehr\n zu thun als Stahl zu machen. Die Stahlmacherei ist blosser Vorwand der\n Plusmacherei. Die Schmelzöfen, Walzwerke u.s. w., die Baulichkeiten, die\n Maschinerie, das Eisen, die Kohle u. s. w. haben mehr zu thun als sich\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"235\", \"facs\": \"#f0254\"} --> in Stahl zu verwandeln. Sie sind da, um Mehrarbeit einzusaugen und\n saugen natürlich mehr davon in 24 Stunden als in 12 Stunden ein. Sie\n geben in der That von Gottes und Rechtswegen den Sandersons eine An-\n weisung auf die Arbeitszeit einer gewissen Anzahl von Händen für volle\n 24 Stunden des Tags, und verlieren ihren Kapitalcharakter, sind\n daher reiner Verlust für die Sandersons, sobald ihre Funktion der\n Arbeitseinsaugung unterbrochen wird. „Aber dann wäre da der Ver-\n lust so viel kostspieliger Maschinerie, welche die halbe Zeit brach läge,\n und für eine solche Produktenmasse, wie wir fähig sind, sie bei dem\ngegenwärtigen System zu leisten, müssten wir Räumlichkeiten und\n Maschinenwerke verdoppeln, was die Auslage verdoppeln würde.“ Aber\n warum sollen grade diese Sandersons ein Privilegium der Arbeitsausbeutung\n vor den andern Kapitalisten geniessen, die nur bei Tag arbeiten lassen\n dürfen und deren Baulichkeiten, Maschinerie, Rohmaterial daher bei Nacht\n „brach“ liegen? „Es ist wahr“, antwortet E. F. Sanderson im Namen\n aller Sandersons, „es ist wahr, dass dieser Verlust von brachliegender\n Maschinerie alle Manufakturen trifft, worin nur bei Tag gearbeitet wird.\nAber der Gebrauch der Schmelzöfen würde in unserem Fall einen Extra-\n verlust verursachen. Hält man sie im Gang, so wird Brennmaterial ver-\n wüstet, (statt dass jetzt das Lebensmaterial der Arbeiter verwüstet wird)\n und hält man sie nicht im Gang, so setzt das Zeitverlust im Wiederan-\n legen des Feuers und zur Gewinnung des nöthigen Hitzegrads (während\n der Verlust, selbst Achtjähriger, an Schlafzeit Gewinn von Arbeitszeit für\n die Sandersonsippe) und die Oefen selbst würden vom Temperaturwechsel\n leiden“ (während doch dieselbigen Oefen nichts leiden vom Tag- und\n Nachtwechsel der Arbeit) [Note: l. c. p. 85. Auf ähnliches zartes Bedenken der Herrn Glasfabri-\n kanten, dass „regelmässige Mahlzeiten“ der Kinder „unmöglich“\n sind, weil dadurch ein bestimmtes „Quantum Hitze“, das die Oefen aus-\n strahlen, „reiner Verlust“ für sie wäre oder „verwüstet“ würde, antwortet\n Untersuchungskommissär White, durchaus nicht gerührt, gleich Ure, Senior\n etc., und ihren schmalen deutschen Nachkläffern, wie Roscher etc., von der\n „Enthaltsamkeit,“ „Entsagung“ und „Sparsamkeit“ der Kapitalisten in Veraus-\n gabung ihres Geldes, und ihrer Timur-Tamerlanschen „Verschwendung“ von\nLeben andrer Menschen, wie folgt: „Ein gewisses Quantum Hitze\n mag über das jetzige Mass hinaus verwüstet werden, in Folge von Sicherung] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"236\", \"facs\": \"#f0255\"} -->\n\n„Was ist ein Arbeitstag?“ Wie gross ist die Zeit, während deren\n das Kapital die Arbeitskraft, deren Tageswerth es zahlt, konsumiren darf?\n Wie weit kann der Arbeitstag verlängert werden über die zur Repro-\n duktion der Arbeitskraft selbst nothwendige Arbeitszeit? Auf diese Fragen,\n man hat es gesehn, antwortet das Kapital: der Arbeitstag zählt täg-\n lich volle 24 Stunden nach Abzug der wenigen Ruhestunden, ohne\n welche die Arbeitskraft ihren erneuerten Dienst absolut versagt. Es ver-\n steht sich zunächst von selbst, dass der Arbeiter seinen ganzen Lebenstag\n durch nichts ist ausser Arbeitskraft, dass daher alle seine dis-\n ponible Zeit von Natur und Rechtswegen Arbeitszeit ist, also der\nSelbstverwerthung des Kapitals angehört. Zeit zu menschlicher\n Bildung, zu geistiger Entwicklung, zur Erfüllung sozialer Funktionen, zu\n [Note: regulärer Mahlzeiten, aber selbst in Geldwerth ist es nichts verglichen\n mit der Verwüstung von Lebenskraft („the waste of animal power“),\n die jetzt dem Königreich daraus erwächst, dass in den Glashütten beschäftigte und\n im Wachsthum begriffene Kinder keine Ruhezeit finden, ihre Speisen bequem ein-\n zunehmen und zu verdauen.“ (l. c. p. XLV.) Und das im „Fortschrittsjahr“\n 1865! Abgesehn von der Kraftausgabe im Heben und Tragen, marschirt ein\n solches Kind in den Hütten, die Flaschen und Flintglas machen, während der\n kontinuirlichen Verrichtung seiner Arbeit, 15 bis 20 (englische) Meilen in 6\n Stunden! Und die Arbeit dauert oft 14 bis 15 Stunden! In vielen dieser Glas-\n hütten herrscht, wie in den Spinnereien von Moskau, das System sechsstündi-\n ger Ablösungen. „Während der Arbeitszeit der Woche sind sechs Stunden\n die äusserste ununterbrochne Rastperiode, und davon geht ab die Zeit zur und\n von der Fabrik zu gehn, Waschen, Kleiden, Speisen, was alles Zeit kostet. So\n bleibt in der That nur die kürzeste Ruhezeit. Keine Zeit für Spiel und frische\n Luft, ausser auf Kosten des Schlafes, so unentbehrlich für Kinder, die in solch\n heisser Atmosphäre solch anstrengendes Werk verrichten . . . . Selbst der kurze\n Schlaf ist dadurch unterbrochen, dass das Kind sich selbst wecken muss bei Nacht,\n oder bei Tag vom Aussenlärm geweckt wird.“ Herr White giebt Fälle, wo ein\n Junge 36 Stunden nach einander arbeitete; andere, wo Knaben von 12 Jahren\n bis zwei Uhr Nachts schanzen und dann in der Hütte schlafen bis 5 Uhr\n Morgens (3 Stunden!), um das Tagewerk von neuem zu beginnen! „Die Masse\n Arbeit,“ sagen die Redakteure des allgemeinen Berichts, Tremenheere und Tufnell,\n „die Knaben, Mädchen und Weiber im Lauf ihres täglichen oder nächtlichen Ar-\n beitsbanns („spell of labour“) verrichten, ist fabelhaft.“ (l. c. XLIII\n und XLIV.) Unterdess wankt vielleicht, eines Abends späte, das „entsagungs-\nvolle“ Glaskapital, Portweinduslig, aus dem Klub nach Haus, idiotisch vor sich\n hersummend: „Britons never, never shall be slaves!“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"237\", \"facs\": \"#f0256\"} --> geselligem Verkehr, zum freien Spiel der physischen und geistigen Leibes-\n kräfte, selbst die Feierzeit des Sonntags — und wäre es im Lande der\n Sabbathheiligen [Note: In England z. B. wird immer noch hier und da auf dem Lande ein Arbeiter zu\n Gefängnissstrafe verurtheilt wegen Entheiligung des Sabbaths durch Arbeit auf dem\n Gärtchen vor seinem Hause. Derselbe Arbeiter wird wegen Kontraktbruches be-\n straft, bleibt er des Sonntags, sei es selbst aus religiösen Mucken, vom Metall-,\n Papier- oder Glaswerk weg. Das orthodoxe Parlament hat kein Ohr für Sabbath-\n entheiligung, wenn sie im „Verwerthungsprozess“ des Kapitals vorgeht. In einer\n Denkschrift (August 1863) der Londoner journeymen fishmongers and\n poulterers, worin die Abschaffung der Sonntagsarbeit verlangt\n wird, heisst es, dass ihre Arbeit während der ersten 6 Wochentage durchschnittlich\n 15 Stunden täglich dauert und am Sonntag 8—10 Stunden. Man entnimmt zu-\n gleich aus dieser Denkschrift, dass es namentlich die kitzliche Gourmandise der\naristokratischen Mucker von Exeter Hall ist, die diese „Sonntagsarbeit“\n ermuthigt. Diese „Heiligen“, so eifrig „in cute curanda“, bewähren ihr Christen-\n thum durch die Ergebung, womit sie die Ueberarbeit, die Entbehrungen und\n den Hunger dritter Personen ertragen.„ Obsequium ventris istis (den Arbeitern)\n perniciosius est.“]  — reiner Firlefanz! Aber in seinem masslos blin-\n den Trieb, seinem Wehrwolfs-Heisshunger nach Mehrarbeit überrennt das\n Kapital nicht nur die moralischen, sondern auch die rein-\n physischen Maximalschranken des Arbeitstags. Es usur-\n pirt die Zeit für Wachsthum, Entwicklung und gesunde Erhaltung des\n Körpers. Es raubt die Zeit, erheischt zum Verzehr von freier Luft und\n Sonnenlicht. Es knickert ab an der Mahlzeit und einverleibt sie, wo mög-\n lich, dem Produktionsprozess selbst, so dass dem Arbeiter als blossem Pro-\n duktionsmittel Speisen zugesetzt werden, wie dem Dampfkessel Kohle\n und der Maschinerie Talg oder Oel. Den gesunden Schlaf zur Sammlung,\n Erneurung und Erfrischung der Lebenskraft reduzirt es auf so viel Stun-\n den dumpfen Torpor als die Wiederbelebung eines absolut erschöpften\n Organismus unentbehrlich macht. Statt dass die normale Erhaltung\n der Arbeitskraft hier die Schranke des Arbeitstags, bestimmt umgekehrt\n die grösste täglich mögliche Verausgabung der Arbeitskraft, wie krankhaft\n gewaltsam und peinlich auch immer, die Schranke für die Rastzeit des\n Arbeiters. Das Kapital fragt nicht nach der Lebensdauer der Ar-\n beitskraft. Was es interessirt, ist einzig und allein das Maximum\n von Arbeitskraft, das in einem Arbeitstag flüssig gemacht werden kann.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"238\", \"facs\": \"#f0257\"} --> Es erreicht diess Ziel durch Verkürzung der Dauer der Arbeits-\n kraft, wie ein habgieriger Landwirth gesteigerten Bodenertrag durch\nBeraubung der Bodenfruchtbarkeit erreicht.\n\n\n\n\nDie kapitalistische Produktion, die wesentlich Produktion von Mehr-\n werth, Einsaugung von Mehrarbeit ist, produzirt also mit der Verlängerung\n des Arbeitstags nicht nur die Verkümmerung der menschlichen Arbeits-\n kraft, die sie ihrer normalen moralischen und physischen Entwicklungs-\n und Bethätigungsbedingungen beraubt. Sie produzirt die vorzei-\n tige Erschöpfung und Abtödtung der Arbeitskraft selbst [Note: „We have given in our previous reports the statements of several ex-\n perienced manufacturers to the effect that over-hours . . . . certainly tend prema-\n turely to exhaust the working power of the men.“ l. c. 64, p. XIII.] .\n Sie verlängert die Produktionszeit des Arbeiters während eines gege-\n benen Termins durch Verkürzung seiner Lebenszeit.\n\n\n\n\nDer Werth der Arbeitskraft schliesst aber den Werth der\n Waaren ein, welche zur Reproduktion des Arbeiters oder zur Fortpflan-\n zung der Arbeiterklasse erheischt sind. Wenn also die naturwidrige Ver-\n längerung des Arbeitstags, die das Kapital in seinem masslosen Trieb nach\n Selbstverwerthung nothwendig anstrebt, die Lebensperiode der einzelnen\n Arbeiter und damit die Dauer ihrer Arbeitskraft verkürzt, wird rascherer\n Ersatz der Verschlissenen nöthig, also das Eingehn grösserer Verschleiss-\n kosten in die Reproduktion der Arbeitskraft, ganz wie der täglich zu repro-\n duzirende Werththeil einer Maschine um so grösser ist, in je kürzerer Zeit\n sie verschleisst. Das Kapital scheint daher durch sein eignes Interesse\n auf einen Normal-Arbeitstag hingewiesen.\n\n\n\n\nDer Sklavenhalter kauft seinen Arbeiter, wie er sein Pferd kauft.\n Mit dem Sklaven verliert er ein Kapital, das durch neue Auslage auf dem\n Sklavenmarkt ersetzt werden muss. Aber „die Reisfelder von Georgien\n und die Sümpfe des Mississippi mögen fatalistisch zerstörend auf die\n menschliche Constitution wirken; dennoch ist diese Verwüstung von\n menschlichem Leben nicht so gross, dass sie nicht gut gemacht werden\n könnte aus den strotzenden Gehegen von Virginien und Kentucky. Oeko-\n nomische Rücksichten, die eine Art Sicherheit für die menschliche Behand-\n lung des Sklaven bieten könnten, sofern sie das Interesse des Herrn mit der\n Erhaltung des Sklaven identifiziren, verwandeln sich, nach Einführung des\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"239\", \"facs\": \"#f0258\"} --> Sklavenhandels, umgekehrt in Gründe der extremsten Zugrunderichtung\n des Sklaven, denn sobald sein Platz einmal durch Zufuhr aus fremden\n Negergehegen ausgefüllt werden kann, wird die Dauer seines\n Lebens minder wichtig als dessen Produktivität, so lange\n es dauert. Es ist daher eine Maxime der Sklavenwirthschaft in Län-\n dern der Sklaveneinfuhr, dass die wirksamste Oekonomie darin besteht, die\n grösstmöglichste Masse Leistung in möglichst kurzer Zeit dem Menschen-\n vieh (human chattle) auszupressen. Es ist in tropischer Kultur, wo die\n jährlichen Profite oft dem Gesammtkapital der Pflanzungen gleich sind,\n dass Negerleben am rücksichtslosesten geopfert wird. Es ist die Agri-\n kultur Westindiens, seit Jahrhunderten die Wiege fabelhaften Reichthums,\n die Millionen der afrikanischen Race verschlungen hat. Es ist heut zu\n Tage in Cuba, dessen Revenüen nach Millionen zählen, und dessen Pflan-\n zer Fürsten sind, wo wir bei der Sklavenklasse, ausser der gröbsten Nah-\n rung, der erschöpfendsten und unablässigsten Plackerei, einen grossen Theil\ndurch die langsame Tortur von Ueberarbeit und Mangel\n an Schlaf und Erholung jährlich direkt zerstört sehn“ [Note: Cairns l. c. p. 110, 111.] .\n\n\n\n\nMutato nomine de te fabula narratur! Lies statt Sklavenhandel\n Arbeitsmarkt, statt Kentucky und Virginien Irland und die Agrikulturdi-\n strikte von England, Schottland und Wales, statt Afrika Deutschland! Wir\n hörten, wie die Ueberarbeit mit den Bäckern in London aufräumt, und den-\n noch ist der Londoner Arbeitsmarkt stets überfüllt mit deutschen und\n anderen Todeskandidaten für die Bäckerei. Die Töpferei, wie wir sahen,\n ist einer der kurzlebigsten Industriezweige. Fehlt es desswegen an\n Töpfern? Josiah Wedgwood, der Erfinder der modernen Töpferei,\n von Haus selbst ein gewöhnlicher Arbeiter, erklärte 1785 vor dem Hause\n der Gemeinen, dass die ganze Manufaktur 15 bis 20,000 Personen be-\n schäftige [Note: John Ward: „History of the Borough of Stoke-upon-\n Trent. London 1843“, p. 42.] . Im Jahr 1861 betrug die Bevölkerung allein der städti-\n schen Sitze dieser Industrie in Grossbritanien 101,302. „Die Baumwoll-\n industrie zählt 90 Jahre . . . . In drei Generationen der englischen Race\n hat sie neun Generationen von Baumwollarbeitern verspeist [Note: Ferrand’s Rede im „House of Commons“ vom 27. April 1863.] “. Aller-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"240\", \"facs\": \"#f0259\"} --> dings, in einzelnen Epochen fieberhaften Aufschwungs zeigte der Arbeits-\n markt bedenkliche Lücken. So z. B. 1834. Aber die Herrn Fabrikanten\n schlugen nun den Poor Law Commissioners vor die „Uebervölkerung“\n der Ackerbaudistrikte nach dem Norden zu schicken, mit der Erklärung,\n dass die Fabrikanten sie absorbiren und konsumiren\n würden [Note: „That the manufacturers would absorb it and use it up. Those were\n the very words used by the cotton manufacturers.“ l. c.] . Diess waren ihre eigensten Worte. „Agenten wurden zu\n Manchester bestallt mit Einwilligung der Poor Law Commissioners. Agri-\n kulturarbeiterlisten wurden ausgefertigt und diesen Agenten übermacht.\n Die Fabrikanten liefen in die Büreaus, und nachdem sie, was ihnen passte,\n ausgewählt, wurden die Familien vom Süden Englands verschickt. Diese\n Menschenpackete wurden geliefert mit Etiquetten gleich so viel Güter-\n ballen, auf Kanal und Lastwagen, — einige strolchten zu Fusse nach und\n viele irrten verloren und halbverhungert in den Manufakturdistrikten um-\n her. Diess entwickelte sich zu einem wahren Handelszweig. Das Haus\n der Gemeinen wird es kaum glauben. Dieser regelmässige Handel, die-\n ser Schacher in Menschenfleisch, dauerte fort, und diese Leute wurden ge-\n kauft und verkauft von den Manchester Agenten an die Manchester Fabri-\n kanten, ganz so regelmässig wie Neger an die Baumwollpflanzer der süd-\n lichen Staaten . . . . Das Jahr 1860 bezeichnet das Zenith der Baum-\n wollindustrie. Es fehlte wieder an Händen. Die Fabrikanten wandten\n sich wieder an die Fleischagenten und diese durchstöberten die Dünen von\n Dorset, die Hügel von Devon und die Ebenen von Wilts, aber die Ueber-\n völkerung war bereits verspeist. Der „Bury Guardian“ jammerte,\n dass 10,000 zusätzliche Hände nach Abschluss des englisch-französischen\n Handelsvertrags absorbirt werden könnten und bald an 30 oder 40,000\n mehr nöthig sein würden. Nachdem die Agenten und Subagenten des\n Fleischhandels die Agrikulturdistrikte 1860 ziemlich resultatlos durchge-\n fegt, wandte sich eine Fabrikantendeputation an Herrn Villiers, den\n Präsidenten des Poor Law Board, um wieder wie früher Zufuhr der Armen-\n und Waisenkinder aus den Workhouses erlaubt zu erhalten [Note: l. c. Villiers, trotz bestem Willen, war „gesetzlich“ in der Lage das\n Fabrikantenanliegen abschlagen zu müssen. Die Herrn erreichten jedoch ihre\n Zwecke durch die Willfährigkeit der lokalen Armenverwaltungen. Herr A. Red-\n grave, Fabrikinspektor, versichert, dass diessmal das System, wonach die Waisen] .“\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"241\", \"facs\": \"#f0260\"} -->\n\nWas die Erfahrung dem Kapitalisten im Allgemeinen zeigt, ist\n eine beständige Uebervölkerung, d. h. Uebervölkerung im Verhältniss\n [Note: und Pauper’s Kinder „gesetzlich“ als apprentices (Lehrlinge) gelten, „nicht\n begleitet war von den alten Missständen“ — (über diese„Missstände“ vgl. Engels\n l. c.) —, obgleich allerdings in einem Fall „Missbrauch mit dem System getrieben\n worden ist, in Bezug auf eine Anzahl Mädchen und junge Weiber, die von den\n Agrikulturdistrikten Schottlands nach Lancashire und Cheshire gebracht wurden.“\n In diesem „System“ schliesst der Fabrikant einen Kontrakt mit den Behörden der\n Armenhäuser für bestimmte Perioden. Er nährt, kleidet und logirt die Kinder und\n giebt ihnen einen kleinen Zuschuss in Geld. Sonderbar klingt folgende Bemer-\n kung des Herrn Redgrave, namentlich wenn man bedenkt, dass selbst unter den\n Prosperitätsjahren der englischen Baumwollindustrie das Jahr 1860 einzig da-\n steht und die Arbeitslöhne ausserdem hoch standen, weil die ausserordentliche\n Arbeitsnachfrage auf Entvölkerung in Irland stiess, auf beispiellose Aus-\n wanderung von englischen und schottischen Agrikulturdistrikten nach Austra-\n lien und Amerika, auf positive Abnahme der Bevölkerung in einigen\n englischen Agrikulturdistrikten in Folge theils glücklich erzielten Bruchs der\n Lebenskraft, theils des früheren Abschöpfens der disponiblen Bevölkerung durch\n die Händler in Menschenfleisch. Und trotz alledem sagt Herr Redgrave:\n „Diese Art Arbeit (der Armenhauskinder) wird jedoch nur gesucht, wenn keine\n andere gefunden werden kann, denn es ist theure Arbeit (high-priced labour).\n Der gewöhnliche Arbeitslohn für einen Jungen von 13 Jahren ist ungefähr 4 sh.\n wöchentlich; aber 50 oder 100 solcher Jungen logiren, kleiden, nähren, mit ärzt-\n licher Hilfsleistung und passender Oberaufsicht versehn, und ihnen obendrein eine\n kleine Zubusse in Geld geben, ist unthubar für 4 sh. per Kopf wöchentlich.“\n („Rep. of the Insp. of Factories for 30th April 1860“ p. 27.) Herr Red-\n grave vergisst zu sagen, wie der Arbeiter selbst diess alles seinen Jungen für ihre\n 4 sh. Arbeitslohn leisten kann, wenn es der Fabrikant nicht kann für 50 oder 100\n Jungen, die gemeinsam logirt, beköstigt und beaufsichtigt werden. Zur Ab-\n wehr falscher Schlussfolgerungen aus dem Text muss ich hier noch bemerken, dass\ndie englische Baumwollindustrie, seit ihrer Unterwerfung unter den\n Factory Act von 1850 mit seiner Reglung der Arbeitszeit u. s. w., als die eng-\n lische Musterindustrie betrachtet werden muss. Der englische Baumwoll-\n arbeiter steht in jeder Hinsicht höher als sein kontinentaler Schicksalsgenosse.\n „Der preussische Fabrikarbeiter arbeitet mindestens 10 Stunden mehr per Woche als\n sein englischer Rivale, und wenn er an seinem eignen Webstuhl zu Hause beschäftigt\n wird, fällt selbst diese Schranke seiner zusätzlichen Arbeitsstunden weg.“ („Rep.\n of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1855“ p. 103.) Der obenerwähnte Fabrikinspektor\n Redgrave reiste nach der Industrieausstellung von 1851 auf dem Kontinent, spe-\n ziell in Frankreich und Preussen, um die dortigen Fabrikzustände zu untersuchen.\n Er sagt von dem preussischen Fabrikarbeiter: „Er erhält einen Lohn ausreichend] \n [Running matter: I. 16] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"242\", \"facs\": \"#f0261\"} --> zum augenblicklichen Verwerthungsbedüfniss des Kapitals, obgleich sie\n aus verkümmerten, schnell hinlebenden, sich rasch verdrängenden, so zu\n sagen unreif gepflückten Menschengenerationen ihren Strom bildet [Note: „Die Ueberarbeiteten sterben mit befremdlicher Raschheit; aber die\n Plätze derer, die untergehn, sind sofort wieder ausgefüllt, und ein häufiger\n Wechsel der Personen bringt keine Aenderung auf der Bühne hervor.“\n „England and America. London 1833“, t. I, 55. (Verfasser E. G. Wake-\n field.)] .\n Allerdings zeigt die Erfahrung dem verständigen Beobachter auf der an-\n dern Seite, wie rasch und tief die kapitalistische Produktion, die, geschicht-\n lich gesprochen, kaum von gestern datirt, die Volkskraft an der Lebens-\n wurzel ergriffen hat, wie die Degeneration der industriellen Bevölkerung\n nur durch beständige Absorption naturwüchsiger Lebenselemente vom\n Lande verlangsamt wird, und wie selbst die ländlichen Arbeiter, trotz\n freier Luft und des unter ihnen so allmächtig waltenden principle of na-\n tural selection, das nur die kräftigsten Individuen aufkommen lässt, schon\n abzuleben beginnen [Note: Siehe: „Public Health. Sixth Report of the Medical\n Officer of the Privy Council. 1863.“ Veröffentlicht London,\n 1864. Dieser Report handelt namentlich von den Agrikulturarbeitern. „Man hat\n die Grafschaft Sutherland als eine sehr verbesserte Grafschaft dargestellt, aber eine\n neuerliche Untersuchung hat entdeckt, dass hier, in Distrikten einst so berühmt\n wegen schöner Männer und tapferer Soldaten, die Einwohner degenerirt sind zu\n einer mageren und verkümmerten Raçe. In den gesundesten Lagen, auf Hügel-\n abhängen im Angesicht des Meers, sind die Gesichter ihrer Kinder so dünn und\n blass, wie sie nur in der faulen Atmosphäre einer Londoner Winkelgasse sein\n können.“ (Thornton l. c. p. 74, 75.) Sie gleichen in der That den 30,000\n „gallant Highlanders,“ die Glasgow in seinen wynds und closes mit Prostituirten\n und Dieben zusammenbettet.] . Das Kapital, das so „gute Gründe“ hat, die\n Leiden der es umgebenden Arbeitergeneration zu läugnen, wird in seiner\n praktischen Bewegung durch die Aussicht auf zukünftige Verfaulung der\n Menschheit und schliesslich doch unaufhaltsame Entvölkerung so wenig und\n so viel bestimmt als durch den möglichen Fall der Erde in die Sonne. In\n jeder Aktienschwindelei weiss jeder, dass das Unwetter einmal einschlagen\n muss, aber jeder hofft, dass es das Haupt seines Nächsten trifft, nachdem\n [Note: zur Verschaffung einfacher Kost und der wenigen Comforts, woran er gewöhnt und\n womit er zufrieden ist . . . . Er lebt schlechter und arbeitet härter als sein eng-\n lischer Rivale.“ („Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1853“ p. 85.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"243\", \"facs\": \"#f0262\"} --> er selbst den Goldregen aufgefangen und in Sicherheit gebracht hat.\nAprès moile déluge! ist der Wahlruf jedes Kapitalisten und jeder Ka-\n pitalistennation. Das Kapital ist daher rücksichtslos gegen Gesundheit und\n Lebensdauer des Arbeiters, wo es nicht durch die Gesellschaft\n zur Rücksicht gezwungen wird [Note: „Obgleich die Gesundheit der Bevölkerung ein so wichtiges Element des\n nationalen Kapitals ist, fürchten wir gestehn zu müssen, dass die Kapitalisten\n durchaus nicht bei der Hand sind, diesen Schatz zu erhalten und werth zu achten.\n Die Rücksicht auf die Gesundheit der Arbeiter wurde den Fabrikanten aufge-\n zwungen.“ („Times“, October 1861.) „Die Männer des West Riding wurden\n die Tuchmacher der Menschheit, die Gesundheit des Arbeitervolks wurde ge-\n opfert, und in ein paar Generationen wäre die Raçe degenerirt, aber eine Reaktion\n trat ein. Die Stunden der Kinderarbeit wurden beschränkt u. s. w.“ („Report\n of the Registrar General for October 1861.“)] . Der Klage über physische\n und geistige Verkümmerung, vorzeitigen Tod, Tortur der Ueberarbeit, ant-\n wortet es: Sollte diese Qual uns quälen, da sie unsre Lust (den Profit)\n vermehrt? Im Grossen und Ganzen hängt diess aber auch nicht vom\n guten oder bösen Willen des einzelnen Kapitalisten ab. Die freie Kon-\n kurrenz macht die immanenten Gesetze der kapitali-\n stischen Produktion dem einzelnen Kapitalisten gegenüber als\näusserliches Zwangsgesetz geltend [Note: Wir finden daher z. B., dass Anfang 1863 26 Firmen, welche ausge-\n dehnte Töpfereien in Staffordshire besitzen, darunter auch J. Wedgwood und\n Söhne, in einer Denkschrift „um gewaltsame Einmischung des Staats“\npetitioniren. Die „Konkurrenz mit andern Kapitalisten“ erlaube ihnen keine\n „freiwillige“ Beschränkung der Arbeitszeit der Kinder u. s. w. „So sehr wir\n daher die oben erwähnten Uebel beklagen, würde es unmöglich sein, sie durch\nirgend eine Art Uebereinkunft unter den Fabrikanten zu verhin-\n dern . . . . In Anbetracht aller dieser Punkte, sind wir zur Ueberzeugung gelangt,\n dass ein Zwangsgesetz nöthig ist.“ Children’s Emp. Comm. Rep. I,\n 1863, p. 322.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Festsetzung eines normalen Arbeitstags ist das\nResultat eines vielhundertjährigen Kampfes zwischen\n Kapitalist und Arbeiter. Doch zeigt die Geschichte dieses Kampfes\n zwei entgegengesetzte Strömungen. Man vergleiche z. B. die englische\n Fabrikgesetzgebung unserer Zeit mit den englischen Arbeitsstatuten vom 14.\n [Running matter: 16*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"244\", \"facs\": \"#f0263\"} --> bis tief in die Mitte des 18. Jahrhunderts [Note: Diese Arbeiterstatute, die man gleichzeitig auch in Frankreich, den\n Niederlanden u. s. w. findet, wurden in England erst 1813 formell aufgehoben,\n nachdem sie längst von den Produktionsverhältnissen beseitigt waren.] . Während das moderne Fabrik-\n gesetz den Arbeitstag gewaltsam abkürzt, suchen ihn jene Statute gewalt-\n sam zu verlängern. Allerdings erscheinen die Ansprüche des Ka-\n pitals im Embryozustand, wo es erst wird, also noch nicht durch blosse Ge-\n walt der ökonomischen Verhältnisse, sondern auch durch Hilfe der Staats-\n macht sein Einsaugungsrecht eines genügenden Quantums Mehrarbeit sichert,\n ganz und gar bescheiden, vergleicht man sie mit den Konzessionen,\n die es in seinem Mannesalter knurrend und widerstrebig machen muss.\n Es kostet Jahrhunderte, bis der „freie“ Arbeiter, in Folge entwickelter\n kapitalistischer Produktionsweise, sich freiwillig dazu versteht, d. h.\ngesellschaftlich gezwungen ist, für den Preis seiner gewohnheits-\n mässigen Lebensmittel seine ganze aktive Lebenszeit, ja seine\n Arbeitsfähigkeit selbst, seine Erstgeburt für ein Gericht Linsen zu ver-\n kaufen. Es ist daher natürlich, dass die Verlängerung des Ar-\n beitstags, die das Kapital von Mitte des 14. bis Ende des 17. Jahr-\n hunderts staatsgewaltig den volljährigen Arbeitern aufzu-\n dringen sucht, ungefähr mit der Schranke der Arbeitszeit zu-\n sammenfällt, die in der zweiten Hälfte des 19. Jahrhunderts der Verwand-\n lung von Kinderblut in Kapital hier und da von Staatswegen gezogen\n wird. Was heute, z. B. im Staate Massachussetts, bis jüngst dem\n freisten Staate der nordamerikanischen Republik, als Staatsschranke der\n Arbeit von Kindern unter 12 Jahren proklamirt ist, war in England noch\nMitte des 17. Jahrhunderts der normale Arbeitstag vollblütiger\n Handwerker, robuster Ackerknechte und riesenhafter Grobschmidte [Note: „No child under the age of 12 years shall be employed in any manu-\n facturing establishment more than 10 hours in one day.“ „General Sta-\n tutes of Massachussetts. 63, ch. 12. (Die Ordonnanzen sind erlassen\n von 1836—1858.) „Labour performed during a period of 10 hours on any day\n in all cotton, woollen, silk, paper, glass, and flax factories, or in manufactories\n of iron and brass, shall be considered a legal day’s labour. And be it enacted,\n that hereafter no minor engaged in any factory shall be holden or required to\n work more than 10 hours in any day, or 60 hours in any week; and that hereafter\n no minor shall be admitted as a worker under the age of 10 years in any factory] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"245\", \"facs\": \"#f0264\"} -->\n\nDas erste „Statute of Labourers“ (23 Eduard III. 1349) fand\n seinen unmittelbaren Vorwand (nicht seine Ursache, denn die Gesetz-\n gebung dieser Art dauert Jahrhunderte fort ohne den Vorwand) in der grossen\n Pest, welche die Bevölkerung dezimirte, so dass, wie ein Tory Schriftsteller\n sagt, „die Schwierigkeit Arbeiter zu raisonablen Preisen (d. h. zu\n Preisen, die ihren Anwendern ein raisonables Quantum Mehrarbeit liessen)\n an die Arbeit zu setzen, in der That unerträglich wurde [Note: „Sophisms of Free Trade. 7th edit. Lond. 1850“, p. 205.\n Derselbe Tory giebt übrigens zu: „Parlamentsakte, die die Arbeitslöhne gegen\n die Arbeiter zu Gunsten der Arbeitsanwender regulirten, währten für die lange\n Periode von 464 Jahren. Die Bevölkerung wuchs. Diese Gesetze wurden nun\n überflüssig und lästig.“ (l. c. p. 206.)] “. Raisonable\n Arbeitslöhne wurden daher zwangsgesetzlich diktirt, ebenso wie die Grenze\n des Arbeitstags. Der letztere Punkt, der uns hier allein interessirt, ist\n wiederholt in dem Statut von 1496 (unter Henry VIII). Der Arbeitstag\n für alle Handwerker (artificers) und Ackerbauarbeiter vom\n März bis September sollte damals, was jedoch nie durchgesetzt wurde,\n dauern von 5 Uhr Morgens bis zwischen 7 und 8 Uhr Abends, aber die\n Stunden für Mahlzeiten betragen 1 Stunde für Frühstück, 1½ Stunden für\n Mittagsessen, und ½ Stunde für Vieruhrenbrod, also grade doppelt so\n viel als nach dem jetzt gültigen Fabrikakt [Note: J. Wade bemerkt mit Recht in Bezug auf diess Statut: „Aus dem Statut\n von 1496 geht hervor, dass die Nahrung als Aequivalent für ⅓ des Einkommens\n eines Handwerkers und ⅔ des Einkommens eines Agrikulturarbeiters galt, und\n diess zeigt eine grössere Stufe von Unabhängigkeit unter den Arbeitern an als jetzt\n vorherrscht, wo die Nahrung der Arbeiter in Agrikultur und Manufaktur ein viel\n höheres Verhältniss zu ihren Löhnen bildet.“ (J. Wade l. c. p. 24, 25 u. 577.)\n Die Meinung als sei diese Differenz etwa der Differenz im Preisverhältniss zwischen\n Nahrungsmitteln und Kleidungsstücken, jetzt und damals, geschuldet, widerlegt\n der oberflächlichste Blick auf: „Chronicon Pretiosum etc. By Bishop\n Fletwood. 1st edit. London 1707. 2nd edit. London 1745.“] . Im Winter sollte ge-\n [Note: within this state.“ „State of New Jersey. An act to limit the hours\n of labour etc., 61 und 2. (Gesetz vom 11. März 1855.) „No minor who\n has attained the age of 12 years, and is under the age of 15 years, shall be em-\n ployed in any manufacturing establishment more than 11 hours in any one day,\nnor before 5 o’clock in the morning, nor after 7½ in the evening.“\n „Revised Statutes of the State of Rhode Island etc. ch. 39, §. 23,\n 1st July 1857.“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"246\", \"facs\": \"#f0265\"} --> arbeitet werden von 5 Uhr Morgens bis zum Dunkeln, mit denselben Unter-\n brechungen. Ein Statut der Elisabeth von 1562 für alle Arbei-\n ter, „gedungen für Lohn per Tag oder Woche“, lässt die Länge des Ar-\n beitstags unberührt, sucht aber die Zwischenräume zu beschränken auf\n 2½ Stunden für den Sommer und 2 für den Winter. Das Mittagsessen\n soll nur eine Stunde dauern und „der Nachmittagsschlaf von ½ Stunde“\n nur zwischen Mitte Mai und Mitte August erlaubt sein. Für jede Stunde\n Abwesenheit soll 1 d. (etwa 10 Pfennige) vom Lohn abgehen. In der\n Praxis jedoch war das Verhältniss den Arbeitern viel günstiger als im\n Statutenbuch. Der Vater der politischen Oekonomie und gewissermassen\n der Erfinder der Statistik, William Petty, sagt in einer Schrift, die er\n im letzten Drittel des 17. Jahrhunderts veröffentlichte: „Arbeiter (labou-\n ring men, eigentlich damals Ackerbauarbeiter) arbeiten 10 Stunden\n täglich und nehmen wöchentlich 20 Mahlzeiten ein, nämlich an Arbeits-\n tagen täglich drei und an Sonntagen zwei; woraus man klärlich sieht, dass,\n wenn sie an Freitag Abenden fasten wollten und in anderthalb Stun-\n den zu Mittag speisen wollten, während sie jetzt zu dieser Mahlzeit\n zwei Stunden brauchen, von 11 bis 1 Uhr Morgens, wenn sie also\n\\frac{1}{20} mehr arbeiteten und \\frac{1}{20} weniger verzehrten, das Zehntel der oben\n erwähnten Steuer aufbringbar wäre [Note: „W. Petty: Political Anatomy of Ireland. 1672. edit.\n 1691“, p. 10.] “. Hatte Dr. Andrew Ure nicht\n Recht, die Zwölfstundenbill von 1833 als Rückgang in die Zeiten der Fin-\n sterniss zu verschreien? Allerdings gelten die in den Statuten und von\n Petty erwähnten Bestimmungen auch für „apprentices“ (Lehrlinge).\n Wie es aber noch Ende des 17. Jahrhunderts mit der Kinderarbeit\n stand, ersieht man aus folgender Klage: „Unsere Jugend, hier in Eng-\n land, treibt gar nichts bis zu der Zeit wo sie Lehrlinge werden, und dann\n brauchen sie natürlich lange Zeit — sieben Jahre — um sich zu vollkom-\n menen Handwerkern zu bilden“. Deutschland wird dagegen gerühmt, weil\n dort die Kinder von der Wiege auf wenigstens zu „ein bischen Beschäf-\n tigung erzogen werden“ [Note: „A Discussion on the Necessity of Encouraging Mecha-\n nick Industry. London 1689“, p. 13. Macaulay, der die englische\n Geschichte im Whig- und Bourgeoisinteresse zurechtgefälscht hat, deklamirt wie] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"247\", \"facs\": \"#f0266\"} -->\n\nNoch während des grössten Theils des 18. Jahrhunderts, bis\n zur Epoche der grossen Industrie, war es dem Kapital in England nicht\n gelungen, durch Zahlung des wöchentlichen Werths der Arbeitskraft sich\n der ganzen Wochenarbeit des Arbeiters, mit Ausnahme je-\n doch des Agrikulturarbeiters, zu bemächtigen. Der Umstand,\n dass sie eine ganze Woche mit dem Lohn von 4 Tagen leben konnten,\n schien den Arbeitern kein hinreichender Grund, auch die andren zwei Tage\n für den Kapitalisten zu arbeiten. Eine Seite der englischen Oekonomen\n denuncirte im Dienst des Kapitals diesen Eigensinn auf’s Wüthendste, eine\n andre Seite vertheidigte die Arbeiter. Hören wir z. B. die Polemik zwi-\n schen Postlethwaite, dessen Handels-Diktionnair damals denselben\n Ruf genoss wie heut zu Tage ähnliche Schriften von Mac Culloch und Mac\n [Note: folgt: „Die Praxis, Kinder vorzeitig an die Arbeit zu setzen, herrschte im 17.\n Jahrhundert in einem für den damaligen Zustand der Industrie fast unglaublichen\n Grad vor. Zu Norwich, dem Hauptsitz der Wollenindustrie, wurde ein Kind von\n 6 Jahren für arbeitsfähig gehalten. Verschiedene Schriftsteller jener Zeit, und\n darunter manche, die als ausserordentlich wohlgesinnt betrachtet wurden, erwähnen\n mit „Exultation“ (Entzücken) die Thatsache, dass in dieser Stadt allein Knaben\n und Mädchen einen Reichthum schaffen, der über ihren eignen Unterhalt hinaus\n 12,000 Pfd. St. in einem Jahr betrug. Je genauer wir die Geschichte der Ver-\n gangenheit untersuchen, desto mehr Grund finden wir die Ansicht derer zu ver-\n werfen, die unser Zeitalter für fruchtbar an neuen socialen Uebeln halten. Das\n was neu ist, ist die Intelligenz, die die Uebel entdeckt, und die Humanität, die sie\n heilt.“ („History of England“, v. I, p. 419.) Macaulay hätte weiter\n berichten können, dass „ausserordentlich wohlgesinnte“ amis du commerce\n im 17. Jahrhundert mit „Exultation“ erzählen, wie in einem Armenhaus in Holland\n ein Kind von 4 Jahren beschäftigt wurde, und dass diess Beispiel der „vertue\n mise en pratique“ in allen Schriften von Humanitairen à la Macaulay Muster\n passirt bis zur Zeit A. Smith’s. Es ist richtig, dass mit dem Aufkommen der\n Manufaktur im Unterschied zum Handwerk sich Spuren der Kinderexploi-\n tation zeigen. Die Tendenz des Kapitals ist unverkennbar, aber die Thatsachen\n selbst stehn noch so vereinzelt, wie die Erscheinung zweiköpfiger Kinder. Sie\n wurden daher „mit Exultation“, als besonders merkwürdig und bewundernswerth,\n von ahnungsvollen „amis du commerce“ für Mit- und Nachwelt aufgezeich-\n net und zur Nachahmung empfohlen. Derselbe schottische Sykophant und Schön-\n redner Macaulay sagt: „man höre heute nur von Rückschritt und sehe nur Fort-\n schritt“. Was für Augen und namentlich was für Ohren!] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"248\", \"facs\": \"#f0267\"} --> Gregor, und dem früher citirten Verfasser des „Essay on Trade\n and Commerce“ [Note: Unter den Anklägern der Arbeiter ist der grimmigste der im Text er-\n wähnte anonyme Verfasser von: „An Essay on Trade and Commerce,\n containing Observations on Taxation etc. London 1770“. Schon\n früher in seiner Schrift: „Considerations on Taxes. London 1765.“\n Auch Polonius Arthur Young, der unsägliche statistische Schwätzer, folgt\n in derselben Linie. Unter den Vertheidigern der Arbeiter stehn oben an: Jacob\n Vanderlint in: „Money answers all things. London 1734“, Rev.\n Nathaniel Forster, D. D. in: „An Enquiry into the Causes of the\n Present Price of Provisions. London 1766“, Dr. Price, und nament-\n lich auch Postlethwaite, sowohl in einem Supplement zu seinem „Uni-\n versal Dictionary of Trade and Commerce“ als in: „Great Bri-\n tain’s Commercial Interest explained and improved. 2nd edit.\n London 1755“. Die Thatsachen selbst findet man bei vielen andern gleichzei-\n tigen Schriftstellern konstatirt, u. a. bei Josiah Tucker.] .\n\n\n\n\nPostlethwaite sagt u. a.: „Ich kann diese wenigen Bemerkun-\n gen nicht abschliessen, ohne Notiz zu nehmen von der trivialen Redens-\n art in dem Munde zu vieler, dass wenn der Arbeiter (industrious poor)\n in 5 Tagen genug erhalten kann um zu leben, er nicht volle 6 Tage ar-\n beiten will. Daher schliessen sie auf die Nothwendigkeit, selbst die\n nothwendigen Lebensmittel durch Steuern oder irgend welche andre Mittel\n zu vertheuern, um den Handwerker und Manufakturarbeiter zu unausge-\n setzter sechstägiger Arbeit in der Woche zu zwingen. Ich muss um\n die Erlaubniss bitten, andrer Meinung zu sein als diese grossen Politiker,\n welche für die beständige Sklaverei der Arbeiterbevölke-\n rung dieses Königreichs („the perpetual slavery of the working people“)\n die Lanze einlegen; sie vergessen das Sprichwort „all work and no\n play“ (nur Arbeit, und kein Spiel, macht dumm). Brüsten sich die\n Engländer nicht mit der Genialität und Gewandtheit ihrer Handwerker und\n Manufakturarbeiter, die bisher den britischen Waaren allgemeinen Kredit\n und Ruf verschafft haben? Welchem Umstand war diess geschuldet?\n Wahrscheinlich keinem andern als der Art und Weise, wie unser Arbeits-\n volk, eigenlaunig, sich zu zerstreuen weiss. Wären sie gezwungen das\n ganze Jahr durchzuarbeiten, alle sechs Tage in der Woche, in\n steter Wiederholung desselben Werkes, würde das nicht ihre Genialität ab-\n stumpfen, und sie dumm-träg statt munter und gewandt machen; und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"249\", \"facs\": \"#f0268\"} --> würden unsere Arbeiter in Folge solcher ewigen Sklaverei ihren\n Ruf nicht verlieren statt erhalten? Welche Art Kunstgeschick könnten wir\n erwarten von solch hart geplackten Thieren? (hard driven ani-\n mals) . . . . Viele von ihnen verrichten soviel Arbeit in 4 Tagen als ein\n Franzose in 5 oder 6. Aber wenn Engländer ewige Schanzarbeiter sein\n sollen, so steht zu fürchten, dass sie noch unter die Franzosen entarten\n (degenerate) werden. Wenn unser Volk wegen seiner Tapferkeit im\n Krieg berühmt ist, sagen wir nicht, dass diess einerseits dem guten eng-\n lischen Roastbeef und Pudding in seinem Leibe, andrerseits nicht minder\n unsrem konstitutionellen Geiste der Freiheit geschuldet ist? Und warum\n sollte die grössere Genialität, Energie und Gewandtheit unserer Handwer-\n ker und Manufakturarbeiter nicht der Freiheit geschuldet sein, womit sie\n sich in ihrer eignen Art und Weise zerstreuen? Ich hoffe, sie werden\n nie weder diese Privilegien verlieren, noch das gute Le-\n ben, woraus ihre Arbeitstüchtigkeit und ihr Muth gleichmässig her-\n stammen“! [Note: Postlethwaite l. c. „First Preliminary Discourse“, p. 14.] \n\n\n\n\nDarauf antwortet der Verfasser des „Essay on Trade and Com-\n merce“:\n\n\n\n\n„Wenn es für eine göttliche Einrichtung gilt, den siebenten Tag\n der Woche zu feiern, so schliesst diess ein, dass die andern Wochentage\n der Arbeit (er meint dem Kapital, wie man gleich sehn wird) angehö-\n ren, und es kann nicht grausam gescholten werden, diess Gebot Gottes zu\n erzwingen . . . . . Dass die Menschheit im Allgemeinen von Natur zur\n Bequemlichkeit und Trägheit neigt, davon machen wir die fatale Erfah-\n rung im Betragen unsres Manufakturpöbels, der durchschnittlich\n nicht über 4 Tage die Wochearbeitet, ausser im Fall einer\n Theurung der Lebensmittel . . . . Gesetzt ein Bushel Weizen repräsentire\n alle Lebensmittel des Arbeiters, koste 5 sh., und der Arbeiter verdiene\n einen Shilling täglich durch seine Arbeit. Dann braucht er bloss 5 Tage\n in der Woche zu arbeiten; nur 4, wenn der Bushel 4 sh. beträgt . . . .\n Da aber der Arbeitslohn in diesem Königreich viel höher steht, verglichen\n mit dem Preise der Lebensmittel, so besitzt der Manufakturarbeiter, der\n 4 Tage arbeitet, einen Geldüberschuss, womit er während des Rests der\n Woche müssig lebt . . . . Ich hoffe, ich habe genug gesagt, um klar zu\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"250\", \"facs\": \"#f0269\"} --> machen, dass mässiges Arbeiten von 6 Tagen in der Woche\n keine Sklaverei ist. Unsere Agrikulturarbeiter thun diess\n und, allem Anschein nach, sind sie die Glücklichsten unter den Arbeitern\n (labouring poor) [Note: „An Essay etc.“ Er selbst erzählt p. 96, worin schon 1770 „das\n Glück“ der englischen Agrikulturarbeiter bestand. „Ihre Arbeitskräfte\n („their working powers“) sind stets auf das Aeusserste angespannt („on the\n stretch“); sie können nicht schlechter leben als sie thun („they cannot live cheaper\n than they do“), noch härter arbeiten“ („nor work harder“).] , aber die Holländer thun es in den Manufak-\n turen und scheinen ein sehr glückliches Volk. Die Franzosen thun\n es, so weit nicht die vielen Feiertage dazwischen kommen [Note: Der Protestantismus spielt schon durch seine Verwandlung fast aller\n traditionellen Feiertage in Werktage eine wichtige Rolle in der Genesis des\n Kapitals.]  . . . .\n Aber unser Manufakturpöbel hat sich die fixe Idee in den Kopf gesetzt,\n dass sie als Engländer durch das Recht der Geburt das Privilegium be-\n sitzen, freier und unabhängiger zu sein als die Arbeiter in irgend\n einem andern Lande von Europa. Nun, diese Idee, so weit sie auf die\n Tapferkeit unserer Soldaten einwirkt, mag von einigem Nutzen sein; aber\n je weniger die Manufakturarbeiter davon haben, desto besser für sie selbst\n und den Staat. Arbeiter sollten sich nie für unabhängig von ihren\n Vorgesetzten („independent of their superiors“) halten . . . . Es ist\n ausserordentlich gefährlich, mobs in einem kommerziellen Staat wie dem\n unsrigen zu encouragiren, wo vielleicht 7 Theile von den 8 der Gesammt-\n bevölkerung Leute mit wenig oder keinem Eigenthum sind [Note: „An Essay etc.“ p. 15—57 passim.]  . . . . .\n Die Kur wird nicht vollständig sein, bis unsre industriellen Armen\n sich bescheiden, 6 Tage für dieselbe Summe zu arbeiten,\n die sie nun in 4 Tagen verdienen“ [Note: l. c. p. 69. Jacob Vanderlint erklärte schon 1734, das Geheimniss\n der Kapitalistenklage über die Faullenzerei des Arbeitervolks sei einfach, dass sie\n für denselben Lohn 6 statt 4 Arbeitstage beanspruchten.] . Zu diesem Zwecke, wie\n zur „Ausrottung der Faullenzerei, Ausschweifung und romantischen Frei-\n heitsduselei“, ditto „zur Minderung der Armentaxe, Förderung des Gei-\n stes der Industrie und Herabdrückung des Arbeitspreises in\n den Manufakturen“, schlägt unser treuer Eckart des Kapitals das\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"251\", \"facs\": \"#f0270\"} --> probate Mittel vor, solche Arbeiter, die der öffentlichen Wohlthätigkeit\n anheimfallen, in einem Wort, paupers, einzusperren in ein „ideales\n Arbeitshaus“ (an ideal Workhouse). „Ein solches Haus muss\n zu einem Hause des Schreckens (House of Terror) gemacht\n werden [Note: l. c. p. 260: „Such ideal workhouse must be made an „House\n of Terror“, and not an asylum for the poor, where they are to be plentifully\n fed, warmly and decently clothed, and where they do but little work.“] . In diesem „Hause des Schreckens“, diesem „Ideal\n von einem Workhouse“, soll gearbeitet werden 14 Stunden täglich\n mit Einbegriff jedoch der passenden Mahlzeiten, so dass volle 12 Ar-\n beitsstunden übrig bleiben“ [Note: „In this ideal workhouse the poor shall work 14 hours in a day, allowing\n proper time for meals, in such manner that there shall remain 12 hours of neat\n labour.“ (l. c.) „Die Franzosen“, sagt er, „lachen über unsre enthusiastischen\n Ideen von Freiheit.“ (l. c. p. 78.)] .\n\n\n\n\nZwölf Arbeitsstunden täglich im „Workhaus-Ideal“,\n im Hause des Schreckens von 1770! Drei und sechzig Jahre\n später, 1833, als das englische Parlament in vier Fabrikzweigen den\n Arbeitstag für Kinder von 13 bis 18 Jahren auf zwölf volle Arbeits-\n stunden herabsetzte, schien der jüngste Tag der englischen In-\n dustrie angebrochen! 1852, als L. Bonaparte bürgerlich Fuss zu fassen\n suchte durch Rütteln am gesetzlichen Arbeitstag, schrie das französische\n Arbeitervolk aus einem Munde: „Das Gesetz, das den Arbeitstag\n auf 12 Stunden verkürzt, ist das einzige Gut, das uns von der\n Gesetzgebung der Republik blieb“ [Note: „They especially objected to work beyond the 12 hours per day, because\n the law which fixed those hours is the only good which remains to them of the\n legislation of the Republic.“ (Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31. Octob. 1856,\n p. 80.) Das französische Zwölfstundengesetz vom 5. September 1850, eine ver-\n bürgerlichte Ausgabe des Dekrets der provisorischen Regierung vom 2. März 1848,\n erstreckt sich auf alle Ateliers ohne Unterschied. Vor diesem Gesetz\n war der Arbeitstag in Frankreich unbeschränkt. Er währte in den Fabriken 14,\n 15 und mehr Stunden. Siehe „Des classes ouvrières en France, pen-\n dant l’année 1848. Par M. Blanqui.“ Herr Blanqui, der Oekonom,\n nicht der Revolutionär, war von Regierungswegen mit der Enquête über die Ar-\n beiterzustände betraut.] ! In Zürich ist die Arbeit von\n Kindern über 10 Jahren auf 12 Stunden beschränkt; im Aargau\n wurde 1862 die Arbeit von Kindern zwischen 13 und 16 Jahren von\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"252\", \"facs\": \"#f0271\"} --> 12½ auf 12 Stunden reducirt, in Oestreich 1860 für Kin-\n der zwischen 14 und 16 Jahren ditto auf 12 Stunden [Note: Lobenswerth wären die Verordnungen der Herrn v. d. Heydt und Man-\n teuffel vom 18. August 1853 und 12. August 1854, wenn sie — ausgeführt würden.\nBelgien bewährt sich auch mit Bezug auf die Regulation des Arbeitstags als\nbürgerlicher Musterstaat. Lord Howard de Welden, englischer Bevoll-\n mächtigter in Brüssel, berichtet dem Foreign Office d. d. 12. Mai 1862: „Der\n Minister Rogier erklärte mir, dass weder ein allgemeines Gesetz noch Lokalregu-\n lationen die Kinderarbeit irgendwie beschränken; dass die Regierung sich während\n der letzten 3 Jahre in jeder Sitzung mit dem Gedanken trug, den Kammern ein\n Gesetz über den Gegenstand vorzulegen, dass sie aber stets ein unüberwindliches\n Hinderniss fand an der eifersüchtigen Angst gegen irgend welche Gesetzgebung\nim Widerspruch mit dem Prinzip vollkommener Freiheit der\n Arbeit“!] . Welch\n ein „Fortschritt seit 1770“ würde Macaulay „mit Exultation“\n aufjauchzen!\n\n\n\n\nDas „Haus des Schreckens“ für Paupers, wovon die Kapital-\n seele 1770 noch träumte, erhob sich wenige Jahre später als riesiges\n „Arbeitshaus“ für die Manufakturarbeiter selbst. Es hiess Fabrik.\n Und diessmal erblasste das Ideal vor der Wirklichkeit.\n\n\n\n\nNachdem das Kapital Jahrhunderte gebraucht, um den Arbeits-\n tag bis zu seinen normalen Maximalgrenzen und dann über diese\n hinaus, bis zu den Grenzen des natürlichen Tags von 12\n Stunden zu verlängern [Note: „Es ist sicher sehr bedauerlich, dass irgend eine Klasse von Personen\n 12 Stunden täglich sich abplacken muss. Rechnet man die Mahlzeiten zu und die\n Zeit um zu und von der Werkstatt zu gehn, so beträgt diess in der That 14 von\n den 24 Tagesstunden … Abgesehn von der Gesundheit, wird Niemand, ich hoffe,\n anstehn zuzugeben, dass vom moralischen Gesichtspunkt eine so gänzliche Ab-\n sorption der Zeit der arbeitenden Klassen, ohne Unterlass, vom frühen Alter von\n 13 Jahren, und in den „freien“ Industriezweigen selbst von viel früherem Alter an,\n ausserordentlich schädlich und ein furchtbares Uebel ist. Im Interesse der öffent-\n lichen Moral, für die Aufziehung einer tüchtigen Bevölkerung, und um der grossen\n Masse des Volks einen vernünftigen Lebensgenuss zu verschaffen, muss darauf ge-\n drungen werden, dass in allen Geschäftszweigen ein Theil jedes Arbeitstags reser-\n virt werde für Erholung und Musse.“ (Leonhard Horner in: „Insp. of\n Fact. Reports. 31st Dec. 1841.“)] , erfolgte nun, seit der Geburt der gros-\n sen Industrie im letzten Drittel des 18. Jahrhunderts, eine lawinenartig\n gewaltsame und masslose Ueberstürzung. Jede Schranke von Sitte und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"253\", \"facs\": \"#f0272\"} --> Natur, Alter und Geschlecht, Tag und Nacht, wurde zertrümmert. Selbst\n die Begriffe von Tag und Nacht, bäuerlich einfach in den alten Statuten,\n verschwammen so sehr, dass ein englischer Richter noch 1860 wahrhaft\n talmudistischen Scharfsinn aufbieten musste, um „urtheilskräftig“ zu er-\n klären, was Tag und Nacht sei [Note: Siehe „Judgment of Mr. J. H. Otwey, Belfast, Hilary\n Sessions, 1860.“] . Das Kapital feierte seine Orgien.\n\n\n\n\nSobald die vom Produktionslarm übertölpelte Arbeiterklasse wieder\n einigermassen zur Besinnung kam, begann ihr Widerstand, zunächst im\n Geburtsland der grossen Industrie, in England. Während drei Decennien\n jedoch blieben die von ihr ertrotzten Konzessionen rein nominell. Das\n Parlament erliess 5 Arbeits-Akte von 1802 bis 1833, war aber so schlau\n keinen Pfennig für ihre zwangsmässige Ausführung, das nöthige Beamten-\n personal u. s. w. zu votiren [Note: Sehr charakteristisch ist es für das Régime Louis Philippe’s, des roi\n bourgeois, dass das einzige unter ihm erlassene Fabrikgesetz vom 22. März 1841\nniemals durchgeführt worden ist. Und diess Gesetz betrifft nur Kinder-\n arbeit. Es setzt 8 Stunden für Kinder zwischen 8 und 12, 12 Stunden für Kinder\n zwischen 12 und 16 Jahren u. s. w. fest, mit vielen Ausnahmen, welche die Nacht-\n arbeit selbst für Achtjährige erlauben. Ueberwachung und Erzwingung des Gesetzes\n blieben in einem Lande, wo jede Maus polizeilich administrirt wird, dem guten\n Willen der „amis du commerce“ überlassen. Erst seit 1853 ist das Gesetz in einem\n einzigen Département, dem Département du Nord, durch Anstellung eines bezahl-\n ten Regierungsinspektors, wirklich durchgeführt worden. Nicht minder charakte-\n ristisch für die Entwicklung der französischen Gesellschaft überhaupt ist es, dass\n Louis Philippe’s Gesetz bis zur Revolution von 1848 einzig dastand in der alles\n umspinnenden französischen Gesetzfabrik!] . Sie blieben ein todter Buchstabe. „Die\n Thatsache ist, dass vor dem Akt von 1833 Kinder und junge Per-\n sonen abgearbeitet wurden („were worked“) die ganze Nacht, den ganzen\n Tag, oder beide ad libitum“ [Note: „Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 30th April 1860“, p. 51.] .\n\n\n\n\nErst seit dem Fabrikakt von 1833 — umfassend Baumwoll-,\n Wolle-, Flachs- und Seidenfabriken — datirt für die moderne Industrie ein\nNormalarbeitstag. Nichts charakterisirt den Geist des Kapi-\n tals besser als die Geschichte der englischen Fabrikgesetzgebung von\n 1833 bis 1864!\n\n\n\n\nDas Gesetz von 1833 erklärt, „der gewöhnliche Fabrik-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"254\", \"facs\": \"#f0273\"} --> Arbeitstag solle beginnen um halb 6 Uhr Morgens und enden halb 9 Uhr\n Abends, und innerhalb dieser Schranken, einer Periode von 15\n Stunden, solle es gesetzlich sein junge Personen (d. h. Personen zwi-\n schen 13 und 18 Jahren) zu irgend einer Zeit des Tages anzuwen-\n den, immer vorausgesetzt, dass keine individuelle junge Person\n mehr als 12 Stunden innerhalb Eines Tags arbeite, mit\n Ausnahme gewisser speziell vorgesehner Fälle“. Die 6. Sektion des Akts\n bestimmt, „dass im Laufe jedes Tags jeder solchen Person von beschränk-\n ter Arbeitszeit mindestens 1½ Stunden für Mahlzeiten eingeräumt werden\n sollen“. Die Anwendung von Kindern unter 9 Jahren, mit später\n zu erwähnender Ausnahme, ward verboten, die Arbeit der Kinder von\n 9 bis 13 Jahren auf 8 Stunden täglich beschränkt. Nacht-\n arbeit, d. h. nach diesem Gesetz, Arbeit zwischen halb 9 Uhr Abends\n und halb 6 Uhr Morgens, ward verboten für alle Personen zwischen 9 und\n 18 Jahren.\n\n\n\n\nDie Gesetzgeber waren so weit entfernt, die Freiheit des Kapi-\n tals in Aussaugung der erwachsenen Arbeitskraft, oder, wie sie es nann-\n ten, „die Freiheit der Arbeit“ antasten zu wollen, dass sie ein\n eignes System ausheckten, um solcher haarsträubenden Konsequenz des\n Fabrikakts vorzubeugen.\n\n\n\n\n„Das grosse Uebel des Fabriksystems, wie es gegenwärtig einge-\n richtet ist“, heisst es im ersten Bericht des Centralraths der Kommission\n vom 25. Juni 1833, „besteht darin, dass es die Nothwendigkeit schafft, die\n Kinderarbeit zur äussersten Länge des Arbeitstags der Erwachsenen aus-\n zudehnen. Das einzige Heilmittel für diess Uebel, ohne Beschrän-\n kung der Arbeit der Erwachsenen, woraus ein Uebel ent-\n springen würde grösser als das, dem vorgebeugt werden\n soll, scheint der Plan doppelte Reihen von Kindern zu verwenden“. Unter\n dem Namen Relaissystem („System of Relays“; Relay heisst\n im Englischen wie im Französischen: das Wechseln der Postpferde auf\n verschiedenen Stationen) wurde daher dieser „Plan“ ausgeführt, so dass\n z. B. von halb 6 Uhr Morgens bis halb zwei Uhr Nachmittags eine Reihe\n von Kindern zwischen 9 und 13 Jahren, von halb zwei Uhr Nachmittags\n bis halb 9 Uhr Abend eine andre Reihe vorgespannt wird u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nZur Belohnung dafür, dass die Herrn Fabrikanten alle während der\n letzten 22 Jahre erlassnen Gesetze über Kinderarbeit aufs frechste ignorirt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"255\", \"facs\": \"#f0274\"} --> hatten, ward ihnen jetzt aber auch die Pille vergoldet. Das Parlament\n bestimmte, dass nach dem 1. März 1834 kein Kind unter 11 Jah-\n ren, nach dem 1. März 1835 kein Kind unter 12 Jahren, und nach\n dem 1. März 1836 kein Kind unter 13 Jahren über 8 Stunden in\n einer Fabrik arbeiten solle! Dieser für das „Kapital“ so schonungsvolle\n „Liberalismus“ war um so anerkennenswerther als Dr. Farre, Sir A. Car-\n lisle, Sir B. Brodie, Sir C. Bell, Mr. Guthrie u. s. w., kurz die bedeutend-\n sten physicians und surgeons London’s in ihren Zeugenaussagen vor\n dem Unterhaus erklärt hatten, dass periculum in mora! Dr. Farre\n drückte sich noch etwas gröber dahin aus: „Gesetzgebung ist gleich noth-\n wendig für die Prävention des Tods in jeder Form, worin er\n vorzeitig angethan werden kann, und sicher dieser (der Fabrikmodus)\n muss als eine der grausamsten Methoden ihn anzuthun betrachtet\n werden [Note: „Legislation is equally necessary for the prevention of death, in any\n form in which it can be prematurely inflicted, and certainly this must be viewed\n as a most cruel mode of inflicting it.“] “. Dasselbe „reformirte“ Parlament, das aus Zartsinn für die\n Herrn Fabrikanten Kinder unter 13 Jahren noch Jahre lang in die Hölle\n 72stündiger Fabrikarbeit per Woche festbannte, verbot dagegen in dem\nEmancipationsakt, der auch die Freiheit tropfenweise eingab,\n von vorn herein den Pflanzern irgend einen Negersklaven länger\n als 45 Stunden per Woche abzuarbeiten!\n\n\n\n\nAber keineswegs gesühnt, eröffnete das Kapital jetzt eine mehrjäh-\n rige und geräuschvolle Agitation. Sie drehte sich hauptsächlich um das\n Alter der Kategorien, die unter dem Namen Kinder auf 8stün-\n dige Arbeit beschränkt und einem gewissen Schulzwang unterworfen wor-\n den waren. Nach der kapitalistischen Anthropologie hörte das Kindes-\n alter im 10., oder, wenn es hoch ging, im 11. Jahre auf. Je näher der\n Termin der vollen Ausführung des Fabrikakts, das verhängnissvolle Jahr\n 1836 rückte, um so wilder raste der Fabrikantenmob. Es gelang ihm in\n der That, die Regierung so weit einzuschüchtern, dass diese 1835 den\n Termin des Kindesalters von 13 auf 12 Jahre herabzusetzen vorschlug.\n Indess wuchs die pressure from without drohend an. Der Muth\n versagte dem Unterhause. Es verweigerte Dreizehnjährige länger als 8\n Stunden täglich unter das Juggernautrad des Kapitals zu werfen und der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"256\", \"facs\": \"#f0275\"} --> Akt von 1833 trat in volle Wirkung. Er blieb unverändert bis\n Juni 1844.\n\n\n\n\nWährend des Decenniums, worin er erst theilweise, dann ganz die\n Fabrikarbeit regulirte, strotzen die officiellen Berichte der Fabrikinspek-\n toren von Klagen über die Unmöglichkeit seiner Ausführung. Da das\n Gesetz von 1833 es nämlich den Herrn vom Kapital freistellte in der\n fünfzehnstündigen Periode von halb 6 Uhr Morgens bis halb 9 Uhr Abends\n jede „junge Person“ und „jedes Kind“ zu irgend beliebiger Zeit die\n zwölf-, respektive 8stündige Arbeit beginnen, unterbrechen, enden zu las-\n sen, und ebenso den verschiednen Personen verschiedne Stunden\n der Mahlzeiten anzuweisen, fanden die Herrn bald ein neues „Re-\n laissystem“ aus, wonach die Arbeitspferde nicht nach bestimmter Zeit\n Stationen wechselten, sondern an wechselnden Stationen stets wieder von\n neuem vorgespannt wurden. Wir verweilen nicht weiter bei der Schön-\n heit dieses Systems, da wir später darauf zurückkommen müssen. So viel\n ist aber auf den ersten Blick klar, dass es den ganzen Fabrikakt, nicht nur\n seinem Geist, sondern auch seinem Buchstaben nach aufhob. Wie sollten\n die Fabrikinspektoren, bei dieser komplizirten Buchführung über jedes ein-\n zelne Kind und jede junge Person, die gesetzlich bestimmte Arbeitszeit und\n die Gewährung der gesetzlichen Mahlzeiten erzwingen? In einem grossen\n Theil der Fabriken blühte der alte brutale Unfug bald wieder ungestraft\n auf. In einer Zusammenkunft mit dem Minister des Innern (1844) wiesen\n die Fabrikinspektoren die Unmöglichkeit aller Kontrole unter dem neu-\n ausgeheckten Relaissystem nach [Note: „Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31. October 1849“, p. 6.] . Unterdess hatten sich aber die Um-\n stände sehr geändert. Die Fabrikarbeiter, namentlich seit 1838, hatten\n die Zehnstundenbill zu ihrem ökonomischen, wie die Charter zu\n ihrem politischen Wahlruf gemacht. Ein Theil der Fabrikanten selbst,\n der den Fabrikbetrieb dem Ak von 1833 gemäss geregelt hatte, überwarf\n das Parlament mit Denkschriften über die unsittliche „Konkurrenz“ der\n „falschen Brüder“, denen grössere Frechheit oder glücklichere Lokalum-\n stände den Gesetzesbruch erlaubten. Zudem, wie sehr immerhin der ein-\n zelne Fabrikant der alten Raubgier den Zügel frei schiessen lassen mochte,\n die Wortführer und politischen Leiter der Fabrikantenklasse geboten eine\n veränderte Haltung und veränderte Sprache gegenüber den Arbeitern. Sie\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"257\", \"facs\": \"#f0276\"} --> hatten den Feldzug zur Abschaffung der Korngesetze eröffnet\n und bedurften der Hilfe der Arbeiter zum Siege! Sie versprachen daher\n nicht nur Verdopplung des Laibes Brod, sondern Annahme der Zehn-\n stundenbill unter dem tausendjährigen Reich des Free Trade [Note: „Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1848“, p. 98.] . Sie\n durften also um so weniger eine Massregel bekämpfen, die nur den Akt\n von 1833 zur Wahrheit machen sollte. In ihrem heiligsten Interesse, der\nGrundrente, bedroht, donnerten endlich die Tories entrüstet philanthro-\n pisch über die „infamen Praktiken“ [Note: Uebrigens braucht Leonhard Horner den Ausdruck „nefarious prac-\n tices“ officiell. („Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31st October 1859“, p. 7.)]  ihrer Feinde.\n\n\n\n\nSo kam der zusätzliche Fabrikakt vom 7. Juni 1844 zu\n Stande. Er trat am 10. September 1844 in Wirkung. Er gruppirt eine\n neue Kategorie von Arbeitern unter die Beschützten, nämlich die Frauen-\n zimmer über 18 Jahre. Sie wurden in jeder Rücksicht den jungen\n Personen gleichgesetzt, ihre Arbeitszeit auf 12 Stunden beschränkt,\n Nachtarbeit ihnen untersagt u. s. w. Zum erstenmal sah sich die Gesetz-\n gebung also gezwungen auch die Arbeit Volljähriger direkt und officiell\n zu kontroliren. In dem Fabrikbericht von 1844—45 heisst es ironisch:\n „Es ist kein einziger Fall zu unsrer Kenntniss gekommen, wo erwachsne\n Weiber sich über diesen Eingriff in ihre Rechte beschwert hät-\n ten“ [Note: „Rep. etc. for 30th Sept. 1844“, p. 15.] . Die Arbeit von Kindern unter 13 Jahren wurde auf 6½ und,\n unter gewissen Bedingungen, 7 Stunden täglich reducirt [Note: Der Akt erlaubt Kinder 10 Stunden anzuwenden, wenn sie nicht Tag\n nach Tag, sondern nur einen Tag über den andern arbeiten. Im Ganzen blieb\n diese Klausel wirkungslos.] .\n\n\n\n\nUm die Missbräuche des „falschen Relaissystems“ zu besei-\n tigen, traf das Gesetz u. a. folgende wichtige Detailbestimmungen: „Der\n Arbeitstag für Kinder und junge Personen ist von der Zeit an zu zählen,\n wo irgend ein Kind oder junge Person des Morgens in der Fabrik\n zu arbeiten anfängt.“ So dass wenn A z. B. um 8 Uhr Morgens die Ar-\n beit beginnt, und B um 10 Uhr, der Arbeitstag dennoch für B zur selben\n Stunde enden muss wie für A. „Der Anfang des Arbeitstags soll angezeigt\n werden durch eine öffentliche Uhr, z. B. die nächste Eisenbahnuhr, wo-\n nach die Fabrikglocke zu richten. Der Fabrikant hat eine grossgedruckte\n [Running matter: I. 17] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"258\", \"facs\": \"#f0277\"} --> Notiz in der Fabrik aufzuhängen, worin Anfang, Ende, Pausen des Arbeits-\n tags angegeben sind. Kinder, die ihre Arbeit des Vormittags vor 12 Uhr\n beginnen, dürfen nicht wieder nach 1 Uhr Mittags verwandt werden. Die\n Nachmittagsreihe muss also aus andern Kindern bestehn als die Vormit-\n tagsreihe. Die 1½ Stunden für Mahlzeit müssen allen beschützten\n Arbeitern zu denselben Tagesperioden eingeräumt werden, eine\n Stunde wenigstens vor 3 Uhr Nachmittags. Kein Kind oder junge Person\n darf länger als 5 Stunden vor 1 Uhr Mittags verwandt werden ohne eine\n mindestens halbstündige Pause für Mahlzeit. Kein Kind, junge Person,\n oder Frauenzimmer darf während irgend einer Mahlzeit in einer Fabrikstube\n bleiben, worin irgend ein Arbeitsprozess vorgeht u. s. w.“\n\n\n\n\nMan hat gesehn: Diese minutiösen Bestimmungen, welche die Periode,\n Grenzen, Pausen der Arbeit so militärisch uniform nach dem Glockenschlag\n regeln, waren keineswegs Produkte parlamentarischer Hirnweberei. Sie\n entwickelten sich allmählig aus den Verhältnissen heraus, als Naturge-\n setze der modernen Produktionsweise. Ihre Formulirung, officielle An-\n erkennung und staatliche Proklamation waren Ergebniss langwieriger\n Klassenkämpfe. Eine ihrer nächsten Folgen war, dass die Praxis auch\n den Arbeitstag der erwachsnen männlichen Fabrikarbeiter\n denselben Schranken unterwarf, da in den meisten Produktionsprozessen\n die Cooperation der Kinder, jungen Personen und Frauenzimmer unentbehr-\n lich. Im Grossen und Ganzen galt daher während der Periode von\n 1844—47 der zwölfstündige Arbeitstag allgemein und uniform in allen\n der Fabrikgesetzgebung unterworfenen Industriezweigen.\n\n\n\n\nDie Fabrikanten erlaubten diesen „Fortschritt“ jedoch nicht ohne\n einen kompensirenden „Rückschritt“. Auf ihren Antrieb reducirte das\n Unterhaus das Minimalalter der zu verarbeitenden Kinder von 9 Jah-\n ren auf 8, zur Sicherung der dem Kapital von Gott und Rechtswegen\n geschuldeten „additionellen Fabrikkinderzufuhr“ [Note: „As a reduction in their hours of work would cause a large number (of\n children) to be employed, it was thought that the additional supply of\n children from eight to nine years of age, would meet the increased\n demand.“ (l. c. p. 13.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Jahre 1846—47 machen Epoche in der ökonomischen Ge-\n schichte Englands. Widerruf der Korngesetze, die Einfuhrzölle auf\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"259\", \"facs\": \"#f0278\"} --> Baumwolle und andre Rohmaterialien abgeschafft, der Freihandel zum Leit-\n stern der Gesetzgebung erklärt! Kurz das tausendjährige Reich brach\n an. Andrerseits erreichten in denselben Jahren Chartistenbewegung und\n Zehnstundenagitation ihren Höhe unkt. Sie fanden Bundesgenossen an\n den racheschnaubenden Tories. Trotz des fanatischen Widerstands des\n wortbrüchigen Freihandelsheers mit Bright und Cobden an der Spitze,\n ging die so lang erstrebte Zehnstundenbill durch das Parla-\n ment. —\n\n\n\n\nDer neue Fabrikakt vom 8. Juni 1847 setzte fest, dass am ersten\n Juli 1847 eine vorläufige Verkürzung des Arbeitstags der „jungen\n Personen“ (von 13 bis zu 18 Jahren) und aller Arbeiterinnen auf\n 11 Stunden, am 1. Mai 1848 aber die definitive Beschränkung auf 10\n Stunden eintreten solle. Im übrigen war der Akt nur ein amen-\n dirender Zusatz der Gesetze von 1833 und 1844.\n\n\n\n\nDas Kapital unternahm zunächst einen vorläufigen Feldzug, dessen\n Ziel, die volle Ausführung des Akts am 1. Mai 1848 zu verhindern.\n Und zwar sollten die Arbeiter selbst, angeblich durch die Erfahrung ge-\n witzigt, ihr eignes Werk wieder zerstören helfen. Der Augenblick war\n geschickt gewählt. „Man muss sich erinnern, dass, in Folge der furcht-\n baren Krise von 1846—47, grosses Leid unter den Fabrikarbeitern vor-\n herrschte, da viele Fabriken nur für kurze Zeit gearbeitet, andre ganz still\n gestanden hatten. Eine beträchtliche Anzahl der Arbeiter befand sich\n daher in drückendster Lage, viele in Schulden. Man konnte daher mit\n ziemlicher Gewissheit annehmen, dass sie die längere Arbeitszeit vorziehn\n würden, um die vergangenen Verluste gut zu machen, vielleicht Schulden\n abzuzahlen, oder ihre Möbel aus dem Pfandhaus zu nehmen, oder ver-\n kaufte Habseligkeiten zu ersetzen, oder neue Kleidungsstücke sich selbst\n und ihren Familien zu verschaffen“ [Note: „Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1848“, p. 16.] . Die Herrn Fabrikanten suchten\n die natürliche Wirkung dieser Umstände zu steigern durch eine allge-\n meine Lohnherabsetzung von 10 %. Diess geschah so zu sagen\n zur Einweihungsfeier der neuen Freihandelsära. Dann folgte weitere\n Herabsetzung um 8⅓ %, sobald der Arbeitstag auf 11, und um das Dop-\n pelte, sobald er definitiv auf 10 Stunden verkürzt wurde. Wo es daher\n irgendwie die Verhältnisse zuliessen, fand eine Lohnherabsetzung von wenig-\n [Running matter: 17*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"260\", \"facs\": \"#f0279\"} --> stens 25 % statt [Note: „Ich fand, dass Leute, die 10 sh. wöchentlich erhalten hatten, 1 sh. ab-\n gezogen erhielten auf Rechnung der allgemeinen Lohnherabsetzung von 10 %, und\n von den restirenden 9 sh. weiteren Abzug von 1 sh. 6 d. erlitten für die Zeitver-\n kürzung, zusammen 2 sh. 6 d., und trotz alledem hielt die Mehrzahl fest an der\n Zehnstundenbill.“ (l. c.)] . Unter so günstig vorbereiteten Chancen begann man\n die Agitation unter den Arbeitern zum Repeal des Akts von 1847. Kein\n Mittel des Betrugs, der Verführung und der Drohung wurde dabei ver-\n schmäht, aber alles umsonst. Von dem halben Dutzend Petitionen, worin\n die Arbeiter klagen mussten über „ihre Unterdrückung durch den Akt.“\n erklärten die Bittsteller selbst, bei mündlichem Verhör, ihre Unterschriften\n seien abgenöthigt worden. „Sie seien unterdrückt, aber von Jemand an-\n ders als dem Fabrikakt“ [Note: „Als ich die Petition unterzeichnete, erklärte ich zugleich, ich thue da-\n mit etwas Schlechtes. — Warum habt Ihr sie denn unterzeichnet? — Weil man\n mich im Weigerungsfall auf das Pflaster geworfen hätte.“ Der Bittsteller fühlte\n sich in der That „unterdrückt“, aber nicht grade durch den Fabrikakt.“ (l. c.\n p. 102.)] . Wenn es aber den Fabrikanten nicht ge-\n lang, die Arbeiter in ihrem Sinn sprechen zu machen, schrieen sie selbst\n nur um so lauter in Presse und Parlament im Namen der Arbeiter.\n Sie denuncirten die Fabrikinspektoren als eine Art Konventskommissäre,\n die ihrer Weltverbesserungsgrille den unglücklichen Arbeiter unbarmherzig\n aufopferten. Auch diess Manöver schlug fehl. Fabrikinspektor Leon-\n hard Horner stellte in eigner Person und durch seine Unterinspektoren\n zahlreiche Zeugenverhöre in den Fabriken Lancashire’s an. Ungefähr 70 %\n der verhörten Arbeiter erklärten sich für 10 Stunden, eine viel geringere\n Prozentzahl für 11 und eine ganz unbedeutende Minorität für die alten 12\n Stunden [Note: p. 17 l. c. In Herrn Horner’s Distrikt wurden so 10,270 erwachsene\n männliche Arbeiter in 181 Fabriken verhört. Man findet ihre Aussagen im\nAppendix des Fabrikreports für das Halbjahr endend October 1848. Diese\n Zeugenverhöre bieten auch in andrer Beziehung schätzbares Material.] .\n\n\n\n\nEin andres „gütliches“ Manöver war die erwachsnen männ-\n lichen Arbeiter 12 bis 15 Stunden arbeiten zu lassen und dann\ndiese Thatsache für den besten Ausdruck der proletarischen Herzens-\n wünsche zu erklären. Aber der „unbarmherzige“ Fabrikinspektor Leon-\n hard Horner war wieder an Ort und Stelle. Die meisten „Ueberstündigen“\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"261\", \"facs\": \"#f0280\"} --> sagten aus, „sie würden es bei weitem vorziehn 10 Stunden für geringern\n Arbeitslohn zu arbeiten, aber sie hätten keine Wahl; so viele von ihnen\n seien arbeitslos, so viele Spinner gezwungen als blosse piecers zu arbeiten,\n dass wenn sie die längere Arbeitszeit verweigerten, andre sofort ihre Stellen\n einnehmen würden, so dass die Frage so für sie stehe: entweder die längere\n Zeit arbeiten oder auf dem Pflaster liegen“ [Note: l. c. Siehe die von Leonhard Horner selbst gesammelten Aussagen\n No. 69, 70, 71, 72, 92, 93 und die von Subinspektor A. gesammelten No. 51,\n 52, 58, 59, 60, 62, 70 des „Appendix.“ Ein Fabrikant schenkte selbst klaren\n Wein ein. Siehe No. 14 nach No. 265 l. c.] .\n\n\n\n\nDer vorläufige Feldzug des Kapitals war missglückt und das Zehn-\n stundengesetz trat am 1. Mai 1848 in Kraft. Unterdess hatte\n jedoch das Fiasko der Chartistenpartei, deren Führer eingekerkert und deren\n Organisation zersprengt, bereits das Selbstvertrauen der englischen Arbeiter-\n klasse erschüttert. Bald darauf vereinigte die Pariser Juniinsurrektion und\n ihre blutige Erstickung, wie im kontinentalen Europa, so in England, alle\n Fraktionen der herrschenden Klassen, Grundeigenthümer und Kapitalisten,\n Börsenwölfe und Krämer, Protektionisten und Freihändler, Regierung und\n Opposition, Pfaffen und Freigeister, junge Huren und alte Nonnen, unter\n dem gemeinschaftlichen Ruf zur Rettung des Eigenthums, der Religion,\n der Familie, der Gesellschaft! Die Arbeiterklasse wurde überall verfehmt,\n in den Bann gethan, unter das „loi des suspects“ gestellt. Die\n Herrn Fabrikanten brauchten sich also nicht länger zu geniren. Sie\n brachen in offne Revolte aus, nicht nur wider das Zehnstundengesetz,\n sondern die ganze Gesetzgebung, welche seit 1833 die „freie“ Aussaugung\n der Arbeitskraft einigermassen zu zügeln suchte. Es war eine Proslavery\n Rebellion in Miniatur, während mehr als zwei Jahren durchgeführt mit\n cynischer Rücksichtslosigkeit, mit terroristischer Energie, beide um so wohl-\n feiler als der rebellische Kapitalist nichts riskirte ausser der Haut seiner\n Arbeiter.\n\n\n\n\nZum Verständniss des Nachfolgenden muss man sich erinnern, dass\n die Fabrikakte von 1833, 1844 und 1847 alle drei in Rechtskraft, so\n weit der eine nicht den andern amendirt; dass keiner derselben den Ar-\n beitstag des männlichen Arbeiters über 18 Jahren beschränkt,\n und dass seit 1833 die fünfzehnstündige Periode von halb 6 Uhr Mor-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"262\", \"facs\": \"#f0281\"} --> gens bis halb 9 Uhr Abends der gesetzliche „Tag“ blieb, innerhalb des-\n sen erst die zwölf-, später die 10stündige Arbeit der jungen Personen und\n Frauenzimmer unter den vorgeschriebnen Bedingungen zu verrichten war.\n\n\n\n\nDie Fabrikanten begannen hier und da mit Entlassung eines Theils,\n manchmal der Hälfte, der von ihnen beschäftigten jungen Personen und\n Arbeiterinnen, und stellten dagegen die fast verschollene Nachtarbeit\n unter den erwachsnen männlichen Arbeitern wieder her. Das Zehnstun-\n dengesetz, riefen sie, lasse ihnen keine andre Alternative [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st October 1848“, p. 133, 134.] !\n\n\n\n\nDer zweite Schritt bezog sich auf die gesetzlichen Pausen für Mahl-\n zeiten. Hören wir die Fabrikinspektoren. „Seit der Beschränkung\n der Arbeitsstunden auf 10, behaupten die Fabrikanten, obgleich sie prak-\n tisch ihre Ansicht noch nicht bis zur letzten Konsequenz durchführen, dass\n wenn sie z. B. von 9 Uhr Morgens bis 7 Uhr Abends arbeiten lassen, sie\n den gesetzlichen Vorschriften genug thun, indem sie eine Stunde für\n Mahlzeit vor 9 Uhr Morgens und eine halbe Stunde nach 7 Uhr\n Abends, also 1½ Stunden für Mahlzeiten geben. In einigen Fällen er-\n lauben sie jetzt eine halbe Stunde für Mittagsessen, bestehn aber zugleich\n darauf, sie seien durchaus nicht verpflichtet, irgend einen Theil der 1½\n Stunden im Lauf des zehnstündigen Arbeitstags einzuräumen“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 30th April 1848“, p. 47.] . Die\n Herrn Fabrikanten behaupteten also, die peinlich genauen Bestimmungen\n des Akts von 1844 über Mahlzeiten gäben den Arbeitern nur die Er-\n laubniss, vor ihrem Eintritt in die Fabrik und nach ihrem Austritt\n aus der Fabrik, also bei sich zu Hause, zu essen und zu trinken! Und\n warum sollten die Arbeiter auch nicht vor 9 Uhr Morgens ihr Mittagsessen\n einnehmen? Die Kronjuristen entschieden jedoch, dass die vorgeschrie-\n benen Mahlzeiten „in Pausen während des wirklichen Arbeits-\n tags gegeben werden müssen und dass es ungesetzlich 10 Stunden nach\n einander von 9 Uhr Morgens bis 7 Uhr Abends ohne Unterbrechung arbei-\n ten zu lassen“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1848“, p. 130.] .\n\n\n\n\nNach diesen gemüthlichen Demonstrationen leitete das Kapital seine\nRevolte ein durch einen Schritt, der dem Buchstaben des Gesetzes\n von 1844 entsprach, also legal war.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"263\", \"facs\": \"#f0282\"} -->\n\nDas Gesetz von 1844 verbot allerdings, Kinder von 8 bis 13 Jahren,\n die vor 12 Uhr Vormittags beschäftigt würden, wieder nach 1 Uhr\n Mittags zu beschäftigen. Aber es regelte in keiner Weise die 6½stün-\n dige Arbeit der Kinder, deren Arbeitszeit um 12 Uhr Vormittags\n oder später begann! Achtjährige Kinder konnten daher, wenn sie die\n Arbeit um 12 Uhr Vormittags begannen, von 12 bis 1 Uhr verwandt werden,\n 1 Stunde; von 2 Uhr bis 4 Uhr Nachmittags, 2 Stunden; und von 5 Uhr bis\n halb 9 Uhr Abends, 3½ Stunden; alles in allem die gesetzlichen 6½\n Stunden! Oder noch besser. Um ihre Verwendung der Arbeit er-\n wachsner männlicher Arbeiter bis halb 9 Uhr Abends\n anzupassen, brauchten ihnen die Fabrikanten kein Werk zu geben vor 2\n Uhr Nachmittags, und konnten sie dann ununterbrochen in der Fabrik hal-\n ten bis halb 9 Uhr Abends! „Und es wird jetzt ausdrücklich zugestanden,\n dass neuerdings, in Folge der Fabrikantengier, ihre Maschinerie länger\n als 10 Stunden laufen zu lassen, sich die Praxis in England eingeschlichen\n hat, acht- bis dreizehnjährige Kinder beiderlei Geschlechts, nach Entfer-\n nung aller jungen Personen und Weiber aus der Fabrik, allein mit den\n erwachsnen Männern, bis halb 9 Uhr Abends arbeiten zu las-\n sen“ [Note: „Reports etc.“ l. c. p. 42.] . Arbeiter und Fabrikinspektoren protestirten aus hygienischen\n und moralischen Gründen. Aber das Kapital antwortete:\n„Meine Thaten auf mein Haupt! Mein Recht verlang’ ich!\n Die Busse und Verpfändung meines Scheins!“\n\n\n\n\nIn der That waren, nach statistischer Vorlage an das Unterhaus vom\n 26. Juli 1850, trotz aller Proteste, am 15. Juli 1850 3742 Kinder in 275\n Fabriken dieser „Praxis“ unterworfen [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1850“, p. 5, 6.] . Noch nicht genug! Das\n Luchsauge des Kapitals entdeckte, dass der Akt von 1844 fünfstündige\n Arbeit des Vormittags nicht ohne Pause von wenigstens 30 Minuten\n für Erfrischung erlaubt, aber nichts der Art für die Nachmittagsarbeit vor-\n schreibt. Es verlangte und ertrotzte daher den Genuss, achtjährige Ar-\n beiterkinder unausgesetzt von 2 bis halb 9 Uhr Abends nicht nur schan-\n zen, sondern auch hungern zu lassen!\n„Ja, die Brust.\n So sagt der Schein“ [Note: Die Natur des Kapitals bleibt dieselbe, in seinen unentwickelten, wie in] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"264\", \"facs\": \"#f0283\"} -->\n\nDiess Shylocksche Festklammern am Buchstaben des Gesetzes von\n 1844, soweit es die Kinderarbeit regelt, sollte jedoch nur die offne\n Revolte gegen dasselbe Gesetz vermitteln, so weit es die Arbeit von\n „jungen Personen und Frauenzimmern“ regelt. Man erin-\n nert sich, dass die Abschaffung des „falschen Relaissystems“\n Hauptzweck und Hauptinhalt jenes Gesetzes bildet. Die Fabrikanten er-\n öffneten ihre Revolte mit der einfachen Erklärung, die Sektionen des Akts\n von 1844, welche beliebigen Niessbrauch der jungen Personen und Frauen-\n zimmer in beliebigen kürzeren Abschnitten des fünfzehnstündigen Fabrik-\n tags verbieten, seien „vergleichungsweise harmlos (comparati-\n vely harmless) gewesen, so lange die Arbeitszeit auf 12 Stunden einge-\n schränkt war. Unter dem Zehnstundengesetz seien sie eine unerträgliche\nUnbill“ (hard ship) [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1848“, p. 133.] . Sie zeigten daher den Inspektoren in der\n kühlsten Weise an, dass sie sich über den Buchstaben des Gesetzes\n hinwegsetzen und das alte System auf eigne Faust wieder einführen wür-\n den [Note: So unter andern Philanthrop Ashworth in einem quäkerhaft widrigen\n Brief an Leonhard Horner.] . Es geschehe im Interesse der übelberathnen Arbeiter selbst,\n „um ihnen höhere Löhne zahlen zu können.“ „Es sei der einzig mög-\n liche Plan, um unter dem Zehnstundengesetz die industrielle Suprematie\n Grossbritanniens zu erhalten“ [Note: l. c. p. 134.] . „Es möge etwas schwer sein Un-\n regelmässigkeiten unter dem Relaissystem zu entdecken, aber was heisse\n das? (what of that?) Soll das grosse Fabrikinteresse dieses Landes als ein\n [Note: seinen entwickelten Formen. In dem Gesetzbuch, das der Einfluss der Sklaven-\n halter, kurz vor Ausbruch des amerikanischen Bürgerkriegs, dem Territorium von\n New - Mexico aufoctroyirte, heisst es: der Arbeiter, so weit der Kapitalist\n seine Arbeitskraft gekauft hat, „ist sein (des Kapitalisten) Geld.“ („The\n labourer is his (the capitalist’s) money.“) Dieselbe Anschauung herrschte bei\n den römischen Patriziern. Das Geld, das sie dem plebejischen Schuldner vor-\n geschossen, hatte sich, vermittelst der Lebensmittel, in Fleisch und Blut des\n Schuldners verwandelt. Diess „Fleisch und Blut“ war daher „ihr Geld.“ Daher\n das Shylocksche Gesetz der 10 Tafeln! Linguet’s Hypothese, dass die patri-\n zischen Gläubiger von Zeit zu Zeit jenseits der Tiber Festschmäuse in gekochtem\n Schuldnerfleisch veranstalteten, bleibe ebenso dahingestellt, wie Daumer’s Hypo-\n these über das christliche Abendmahl.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"265\", \"facs\": \"#f0284\"} --> sekundäres Ding behandelt werden, um den Fabrik-Inspektoren und Sub-\n inspektoren ein bischen mehr Mühe (some little trouble) zu sparen“ [Note: l. c. p. 140.] ?\n\n\n\n\nAlle diese Flausen halfen natürlich nichts. Die Fabrikinspektoren\n schritten gerichtlich ein. Bald aber überschüttete eine solche Staubwolke\n von Fabrikantenpetitionen den Minister des Innern, Sir George Grey,\n dass er in einem Cirkular vom 5. August 1848 die Inspektoren anwies,\n „im Allgemeinen nicht einzuschreiten wegen Verletzung des Buchsta-\n bens des Akts, so oft das Relaissystem nicht erwiesnermassen missbraucht\n werde, um junge Personen und Frauenzimmer über 10 Stunden arbeiten zu\n lassen.“ Hierauf erlaubte Fabrikinspektor J. Stuart das sogenannte Ab-\n lösungssystem während der fünfzehnstündigen Periode des Fabriktags in\n ganz Schottland, wo es bald wieder in alter Weise aufblühte. Die\n englischen Fabrikinspektoren dagegen erklärten, der Minister besitze keine\n diktatorische Gewalt zur Suspension der Gesetze, und fuhren mit gericht-\n licher Procedur wider die Proslavery Rebellen fort.\n\n\n\n\nWozu jedoch alle Citation vor’s Gericht, sobald die Gerichte, die\ncounty magistrates [Note: Diese „county magistrates“, die „great unpaid“ wie W. Cobbett sie\n nennt, sind eine Art unbezahlter Friedensrichter, aus den Honoratioren der Graf-\n schaften gebildet. Sie bilden in der That die Patrimonialgerichte der\n herrschenden Klassen.] , freisprachen? In diesen Gerichten sassen\n die Herrn Fabrikanten über sich selbst zu Gericht. Ein Beispiel. Ein\n gewisser Eskrigge, Baumwollspinner von der Firma Kershaw, Leese\n et Co., hatte dem Fabrikinspektor seines Distrikts das Schema eines für\n seine Fabrik bestimmten Relaissystems vorgelegt. Abschlägig beschieden,\n verhielt er sich zunächst passiv. Wenige Monate später stand ein Indi-\n viduum Namens Robinson, ebenfalls Baumwollspinner, und wenn nicht der\n Freitag, so jedenfalls der Verwandte des Eskrigge, vor den Borough Jus-\n tices zu Stockport, wegen Einführung des identischen, von Eskrigge\n ausgeheckten Relaisplans. Es sassen 4 Richter, darunter 3 Baumwollspin-\n ner, an ihrer Spitze derselbe unvermeidliche Eskrigge. Eskrigge sprach\n den Robinson frei, und erklärte nun, was dem Robinson recht, sei dem\n Eskrigge billig. Auf seine eigne rechtskräftige Entscheidung gestützt,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"266\", \"facs\": \"#f0285\"} --> führte er sofort das System in seiner eignen Fabrik ein [Note: „Reports etc. for 30th April 1849“, p. 21, 22. Vgl. ähnliche\n Beispiele ibid. p. 4, 5.] . Allerdings\n war schon die Zusammensetzung dieser Gerichte offne Verletzung des Ge-\n setzes [Note: Durch 1 und 2 Wm. IV. c. 24, s. 10, bekannt als Sir John Hobhouse’s\n Factory Act, wird verboten, dass irgend ein Besitzer einer Baumwollspinnerei oder\n Weberei, oder Vater, Sohn und Bruder eines solchen Besitzers in Fragen, die den\n Factory Act betreffen, als Friedensrichter funktioniren.] . „Diese Art gerichtlicher Farcen“, ruft Inspektor Howell aus,\n „schreien nach einem Heilmittel . . . . entweder passt das Gesetz diesen\n Urtheilssprüchen an oder lasst es verwalten durch ein minder fehlbares\n Tribunal, das seine Entscheidungen dem Gesetz anpasst . . . . In allen\n solchen Fällen, wie sehnt man sich nach einem bezahlten Richter“ [Note: l. c.] !\n\n\n\n\nDie Kronjuristen erklärten die Fabrikanten-Interpretation des Akts\n von 1844 für abgeschmackt, aber die Gesellschaftsretter liessen sich nicht\n beirren. „Nachdem ich“, berichtet Leonhard Horner, „durch 10 Verfol-\n gungen in 7 verschiednen Gerichtsbezirken versucht habe das Gesetz zu\n erzwingen und nur in einem Fall von den Magistraten unterstützt wurde,\n halte ich weitere Verfolgung wegen Umgehung des Gesetzes für nutzlos.\n Der Theil des Akts, der verfasst wurde, um Uniformität in den Arbeits-\n stunden zu schaffen, existirt nicht mehr in Lancashire. Auch besitze ich\n mit meinen Unteragenten durchaus kein Mittel uns zu versichern, dass Fabri-\n ken, wo das sog. Relaissystem herrscht, junge Personen und Frauenzimmer\n nicht über 10 Stunden beschäftigen . . . . Ende April 1849 arbeiten\n schon 118 Fabriken in meinem Distrikt nach dieser Methode und ihre\n Anzahl nimmt in der letzten Zeit reissend zu. Im Allgemeinen arbeiten\n sie jetzt 13½ Stunden, von 6 Uhr Morgens bis halb 8 Uhr Abends; in\n einigen Fällen 15 Stunden, von halb 6 Uhr Morgens bis halb 9 Uhr\n Abends“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 30th April 1849“, p. 5.] . Schon December 1848 besass Leonhard Horner eine Liste\n von 65 Fabrikanten und 29 Fabrikaufsehern, die einstimmig erklärten,\n kein System der Oberaufsicht könne unter diesem Relaissystem die exten-\n sivste Ueberarbeit verhindern [Note: „Rep. etc. for 31st Oct. 1849“, p. 6.] . Bald wurden dieselben Kinder und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"267\", \"facs\": \"#f0286\"} --> jungen Personen aus der Spinnstube in die Webstube u. s. w., bald, wäh-\n rend 15 Stunden, aus einer Fabrik in die andre geschoben (shifted) [Note: „Rep. etc. for 30th April 1849“, p. 21.] .\n Wie ein System kontroliren, „welches das Wort Ablösung missbraucht,\n um die Hände in endloser Mannichfaltigkeit wie Karten durcheinander zu\n mischen und die Stunden der Arbeit und der Rast für die verschiednen\n Individuen täglich so zu verschieben, dass ein und dasselbe vollständige\n Assortiment von Händen niemals an demselben Platz zur selben Zeit zu-\n sammenarbeitet“ [Note: „Rep. etc. 1. Dec. 1848“, p. 95.] !\n\n\n\n\nAber ganz abgesehn von wirklicher Ueberarbeitung, war diess sog.\nRelaissystem eine Ausgeburt der Kapitalphantasie, wie sie Fou-\n rier in seinen genialsten Skizzen der „courtes séances“ nie übertroffen\n hat, nur dass die Attraktion der Arbeit verwandelt war in die Attraktion\n des Kapitals. Man sehe sich jene Fabrikantenschemas an, welche die\n gute Presse pries als Muster von dem, „was ein vernünftiger Grad von\n Sorgfalt und Methode ausrichten kann“ („what a reasonable degree of\n care and method can accomplish“). Das Arbeiterpersonal wurde manch-\n mal in 12 bis 14 Kategorien vertheilt, die selbst wieder ihre Bestand-\n theile beständig wechselten. Während der fünfzehnstündigen Periode des\n Fabriktags zog das Kapital den Arbeiter jetzt für 30 Minuten, jetzt für\n eine Stunde an, und stiess ihn dann wieder ab, um ihn von neuem in die\n Fabrik zu ziehn und aus der Fabrik zu stossen, ihn hin und her hetzend\n in zerstreuten Zeitfetzen, ohne je den Halt auf ihn zu verlieren, bis die\n zehnstündige Arbeit vollgemacht. Wie auf der Bühne hatten dieselben\n Personen abwechselnd in den verschiednen Scenen der verschiednen Akte\n aufzutreten. Aber wie ein Schauspieler während der ganzen Dauer des\n Dramas der Bühne gehört, so gehörten die Arbeiter jetzt während 15 Stun-\n den der Fabrik, nicht eingerechnet die Zeit, um von und zu ihr zu gehn.\n Die Stunden der Rast verwandelten sich so in Stunden erzwungenen Müssig-\n gangs, welche den jungen Arbeiter in die Kneipe und die junge Arbei-\n terin in das Bordell trieben. Bei jedem neuen Einfall, den der Kapi-\n talist täglich ausheckte, um seine Maschinerie ohne Vermehrung des\n Arbeiterpersonals 12 oder 15 Stunden im Gang zu halten, hatte der\n Arbeiter bald in diesem Stück Zeitabfall, bald in jenem seine Mahlzeit ein-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"268\", \"facs\": \"#f0287\"} --> zuschlucken. Zur Zeit der Zehnstundenagitation schrien die Fabrikanten,\n das Arbeiterpack petitionire, in der Erwartung, zwölfstündigen Arbeitslohn\n für zehnstündige Arbeit zu erhalten. Sie hatten jetzt die Medaille umge-\n kehrt. Sie zahlten 10stündigen Arbeitslohn für zwölf- und fünfzehnstün-\n dige Verfügung über die Arbeitskräfte [Note: Siehe „Reports etc. for 30th April 1849“, p. 6 und die weitläufige\n Auseinandersetzung des „shifting system“ durch die Fabrikinspektoren Howell\n und Saunders in „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1848.“ Siehe auch die Petition\n der Geistlichkeit von Ashton und Nachbarschaft, Frühling 1849, an die Königin.\n gegen das „shift system.“] ! Diess war des Pudels Kern,\n diess die Fabrikantenausgabe des Zehnstundengesetzes! Es waren die-\n selben salbungsvollen, Menschenliebe triefenden Freihändler, die den Ar-\n beitern 10 volle Jahre, während der Anticornlaw-Agitation, auf Heller\n und Pfennig vorgerechnet, dass bei freier Korneinfuhr eine zehnstündige\n Arbeit, mit den Mitteln der englischen Industrie, vollständig genüge, um\n die Kapitalisten zu bereichern [Note: Vgl. z. B. „The Factory Question and the Ten Hours Bill.\n By R. H. Greg. 1837.“] .\n\n\n\n\nDie zweijährige Kapitalrevolte wurde endlich gekrönt durch den Ur-\n theilsspruch eines der vier höchsten Gerichtshöfe von England, des Court\n of Exchequer, der in einem vor ihn gebrachten Fall am 8. Februar\n 1850 entschied, dass die Fabrikanten zwar wider den Sinn des Akts von\n 1844 handelten, dieser Akt selbst aber gewisse Worte enthalte, die ihn\n sinnlos machten. „Mit dieser Entscheidung war das Zehnstundengesetz\n abgeschafft“ [Note: F. Engels: „Die englische Zehnstundenbill“ (in der von\n mir herausgegebenen: „Neuen Rh. Zeitung. Politisch-ökonomische\n Revue. Aprilheft 1850“, p. 13). Derselbe „hohe“ Gerichtshof entdeckte\n ebenfalls während des amerikanischen Bürgerkriegs eine Wortschraube, die das\n Gesetz gegen Ausrüstung von Piratenschiffen in’s direkte Gegentheil verkehrt.] . Eine Masse Fabrikanten, die bisher noch das Relais-\n system für junge Personen und Arbeiterinnen gescheut, griffen nun mit\n beiden Händen zu [Note: „Rep. etc. for 30th April 1850.“] .\n\n\n\n\nMit diesem scheinbar definitiven Sieg des Kapitals trat aber sofort\n ein Umschlag ein. Die Arbeiter hatten bisher passiven, obgleich unbeug-\n samen und täglich erneuten Widerstand geleistet. Sie protestirten jetzt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"269\", \"facs\": \"#f0288\"} --> in laut drohenden Meetings in Lancashire und Yorkshire. Das angebliche\n Zehnstundengesetz sei also blosser Humbug, parlamentarische Prellerei,\n und habe nie existirt! Die Fabrikinspektoren warnten dringend die Re-\n gierung, der Klassenantagonismus sei zu einer unglaublichen Höhe ge-\n spannt. Ein Theil der Fabrikanten selbst murrte: „durch die wider-\n sprechenden Entscheidungen der Magistrate herrsche ein ganz abnormaler\n und anarchischer Zustand. Ein andres Gesetz gelte in Yorkshire, ein\n andres in Lancashire, ein andres Gesetz in einer Pfarrei von Lancashire, ein\n andres in ihrer unmittelbaren Nachbarschaft. Der Fabrikant in grossen\n Städten könne das Gesetz umgehn, der in Landflecken finde nicht das\n nöthige Personal für das Relaissystem und noch minder zur Verschiebung\n der Arbeiter aus einer Fabrik in die andre u. s. w.“ Und gleiche Ex-\n ploitation der Arbeitskraft ist das erste Menschenrecht des Kapitals.\n\n\n\n\nUnter diesen Umständen kam es zu einem Kompromiss zwi-\n schen Fabrikanten und Arbeitern, der in dem neuen zusätz-\n lichen Fabrikakt vom 5. August 1850 parlamentarisch versiegelt\n ist. Für „junge Personen und Frauenzimmer“ wurde der\n Arbeitstag in den ersten 5 Wochentagen von 10 auf 10½ Stunden erhöht,\n für den Samstag auf 7½ Stunden beschränkt. Die Arbeit muss in der\n Periode von 6 Uhr Morgens bis 6 Uhr Abends vorgehn [Note: Im Winter kann die Periode zwischen 7 Uhr Morgens und 7 Uhr Abends\n an die Stelle treten.] , mit 1½stün-\n digen Pausen für Mahlzeiten, die gleichzeitig und gemäss den Bestim-\n mungen von 1844 einzuräumen sind u. s. w. Damit war dem Relais-\n system ein für allemal ein Ende gemacht [Note: „The present law (of 1850) was a compromise whereby the employed\n surrendered the benefit of the Ten Hours’ Act for the advantage of one\n uniform period for the commencement and termination of the\n labour of those whose labour is restricted.“ („Reports etc. for 30th\n April 1852“, p. 14.)] . Für die Kinderarbeit blieb\n das Gesetz von 1844 in Kraft.\n\n\n\n\nEine Fabrikantenkategorie sicherte sich diessmal, wie früher, beson-\n dere Seigneurialrechte auf Proletarierkinder. Es waren diess die Sei-\n denfabrikanten. Im Jahr 1833 hatten sie drohend geheult, „wenn\n man ihnen die Freiheit raube, Kinder jeden Alters täglich\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"270\", \"facs\": \"#f0289\"} --> 10 Stunden abzurackern, setze man ihre Fabriken still“ (if the liberty\n of working children of any age for 10 hours a day was\n taken away, it would stop their works). Es sei ihnen unmöglich, eine\n hinreichende Anzahl von Kindern über 13 Jahren zu kaufen. Sie\n erpressten das gewünschte Privilegium. Der Vorwand stellte sich bei\n späterer Untersuchung als baare Lüge heraus [Note: „Reports etc. for 30th Sept. 1844“, p. 13.] , was sie jedoch nicht\n verhinderte, während eines Decenniums aus dem Blut kleiner Kinder, die\n zur Verrichtung ihrer Arbeit auf Stühle gestellt werden mussten, täglich\n 10 Stunden Seide zu spinnen [Note: l. c.] . Der Akt von 1844 „beraubte“ sie\n zwar der „Freiheit“, Kinder unter 11 Jahren länger als 6½ Stun-\n den, sicherte ihnen dagegen das Privilegium, Kinder zwischen 11\n und 13 Jahren 10 Stunden täglich zu verarbeiten, und kassirte den für\n andere Fabrikkinder vorgeschriebenen Schulzwang. Diessmal der\n Vorwand: „Die Delikatesse des Gewebes erheische eine Fin-\n gerzartheit, die nur durch frühen Eintritt in die Fabrik zu sichern“ [Note: „The delicate texture of the fabric in which they were employed requir-\n ing a lightness of touch, only to be acquired by their early introduction to these\n factories.“ (l. c. p. 20.)] .\n Der delikaten Finger wegen wurden die Kinder ganz geschlachtet, wie\n Hornvieh in Südrussland wegen Haut und Talg. Endlich, 1850, wurde\n das 1844 eingeräumte Privilegium auf die Departements der Seidenzwir-\n nerei und Seidenhaspelei beschränkt, hier aber, zum Schadenersatz des\n seiner „Freiheit“ beraubten Kapitals, die Arbeitszeit für Kinder von 11\n bis 13 Jahren von 10 auf 10½ Stunden erhöht. Vorwand: „Die Arbeit\n sei leichter in Seidenfabriken als in den andern Fabriken und in keiner\n Weise so nachtheilig für die Gesundheit“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1861“, p. 26.] . Offizielle ärztliche\n Untersuchung bewies hinterher, dass umgekehrt „die durchschnittliche\n Sterblichkeitsrate in den Seidendistrikten ausnahmsweise hoch und\n unter dem weiblichen Theil der Bevölkerung selbst höher ist als in den\n Baumwolldistrikten von Lancashire“ [Note: l. c. p. 27. Im Allgemeinen hat sich die dem Fabrikgesetz unterworfene\n Arbeiterbevölkerung physisch sehr verbessert. Alle ärztlichen Zeugnisse stimmen\n darin überein und eigne persönliche Anschauung zu verschiednen Perioden hat] . Trotz des halbjährlich wieder-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"271\", \"facs\": \"#f0290\"} --> holten Protests der Fabrikinspektoren, dauert der Unfug bis zur Stunde\n fort [Note: Man weiss, wie widerstrebend die englischen „Freihändler“ dem Schutz-\n zoll für Seidenmanufaktur entsagten. Statt des Schutzes gegen französische Ein-\n fuhr dient nun die Schutzlosigkeit englischer Fabrikkinder.] .\n\n\n\n\nDas Gesetz von 1850 verwandelte nur für „junge Personen und\n Frauenzimmer“ die fünfzehnstündige Periode von halb 6 Uhr Morgens bis\n halb 9 Uhr Abends in die zwölfstündige Periode von 6 Uhr Morgens bis\n 6 Uhr Abends. Also nicht für Kinder, die immer noch eine halbe\n Stunde vor Beginn und 2½ Stunden nach Schluss dieser Periode ver-\n werthbar blieben, wenn auch die Gesammtdauer ihrer Arbeit 6½ Stunden\n nicht überschreiten durfte. Während der Diskussion des Gesetzes wurde\n dem Parlament von den Fabrikinspektoren eine Statistik über die infamen\n Missbräuche jener Anomalie unterbreitet. Jedoch umsonst. Im Hinter-\n grund lauerte die Absicht, den Arbeitstag der erwachsnen Arbeiter mit\n [Note: mich davon überzeugt. Dennoch, und abgesehn von der ungeheuren Sterblich-\n keitsrate der Kinder in den ersten Lebensjahren, zeigen die officiellen Berichte des\n Dr. Greenhow den ungünstigen Gesundheitsstand der Fabrikdistrikte, verglichen\n mit „Agrikulturdistrikten von normaler Gesundheit“. Zum Beweis u. a. folgende\n Tabelle aus seinem Bericht von 1861:\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n] <!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"272\", \"facs\": \"#f0291\"} --> Beihilfe der Kinder in Prosperitätsjahren wieder auf 15 Stunden zu\n schrauben. Die Erfahrung der folgenden 3 Jahre zeigte, dass solcher\n Versuch am Widerstand der erwachsnen männlichen Arbeiter scheitern\n müsse [Note: „Reports etc. for 30th April 1853“, p. 31.] . Der Akt von 1850 wurde daher 1853 endlich ergänzt\n durch das Verbot, „Kinder des Morgens vor und des Abends nach den\n jungen Personen und Frauenzimmern zu verwenden“. Von nun an regelte,\n mit wenigen Ausnahmen, der Fabrikakt von 1850 in den ihm unterworfe-\n nen Industriezweigen den Arbeitstag aller Arbeiter [Note: Während der Zenithjahre der englischen Baumwollindustrie, 1859 und\n 1860, versuchten einige Fabrikanten durch die Lockangel hoher Arbeitslöhne für\n Extrazeit die erwachsenen männlichen Spinner u. s. w. zur Verlängerung\n des Arbeitstags zu bestimmen. Die Hand-Mule Spinners und Self-Actor Minders\n machten dem Experiment ein Ende durch eine Denkschrift an ihre Anwender,\n worin es u. a. heisst: „Grad herausgesprochen, unser Leben ist uns zur Last,\n und so lange wir fast 2 Tage die Woche (20 Stunden) länger an die Fabrik ge-\n kettet sind als die anderen Arbeiter, fühlen wir uns gleich Heloten im Lande\n und werfen uns selbst vor, ein System zu verewigen, das uns selbst und unsre\n Nachkommen physisch und moralisch beschädigt . . . . Daher geben wir hiermit\n respektvolle Notiz, dass wir von Neujahrstag an keine Minute mehr als 60 Stunden\n wöchentlich, von 6 Uhr zu 6 Uhr, mit Abzug der gesetzlichen Pausen von 1½\n Stunden, arbeiten werden.“ („Reports etc. for 30th April 1860“, p. 30.)] . Seit dem Er-\n lass des ersten Fabrikakts war jetzt ein halbes Jahrhundert ver-\n flossen [Note: Ueber die Mittel, die die Fassung dieses Gesetzes für seinen Bruch ge-\n währt, cf. den Parliamentary Return: „Factory Regulations Acts“\n (6. August 1859) und darin Leonhard Horner’s „Suggestions for Amend-\n ing the Factory Acts to enable the Inspectors to prevent Illegal\n Working, now become very prevalent.“] .\n\n\n\n\nUeber ihre ursprüngliche Sphäre griff die Fabrikgesetzgebung\n zuerst hinaus durch den ‚Printwork’s Act‘ (Gesetz über Kattundrucke-\n reien u. s. w.) von 1845. Die Unlust, womit das Kapital diese neue\n „Extravaganz“ zuliess, spricht aus jeder Zeile des Acts! Er beschränkt\n den Arbeitstag für Kinder von 8—13 Jahren und für Frauenzimmer auf\n16 Stunden zwischen 6 Uhr Morgens und 10 Uhr Abends, ohne irgend\n eine gesetzliche Pause für Mahlzeiten. Er erlaubt männliche Arbeiter\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"273\", \"facs\": \"#f0292\"} --> über 13 Jahre Tag und Nacht hindurch beliebig abzuarbeiten [Note: „Kinder von 8 Jahren und drüber sind in der That von 6 Uhr Morgens\n bis 9 Uhr Abends während des letzten Halbjahrs (1857) in meinem Distrikt abge\n rackert worden.“ („Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1857“, p. 39.)] . Er\n ist ein parlamentarischer Abort [Note: „The Printwork’s Act is admitted to be a failure, both with reference to\n its educational and protective provisions.“ („Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1862“,\n p. 52.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDennoch hatte das Prinzip gesiegt mit seinem Sieg in den grossen\n Industriezweigen, welche das eigenste Geschöpf der modernen Produktions-\n weise. Ihre wundervolle Entwicklung von 1853—1860, Hand in Hand\n mit der physischen und moralischen Wiedergeburt der Fabrikarbeiter.\n schlug das blödeste Auge. Die Fabrikanten selbst, denen die gesetzliche\n Schranke und Regel des Arbeitstags durch halbhundertjährigen Bürger-\n krieg Schritt für Schritt abgetrotzt, wiesen prahlend auf den Kontrast mit\n den noch „freien“ Exploitationsgebieten hin [Note: So z. B. E. Potter in Brief an Times vom 24. März 1863. Die\nTimes erinnert ihn an die Fabrikantenrevolte gegen das Zehnstundengesetz.] . Die Pharisäer der\n „politischen Oekonomie“ proklamirten nun die Einsicht in die Noth-\n wendigkeit eines gesetzlich geregelten Arbeitstags als charakteristische\n Neuerrungenschaft ihrer „Wissenschaft“ [Note: So u. a. Herr W. Newmarch, Mitarbeiter an und Herausgeber von\nTooke’s: „History of Prices“. Ist es wissenschaftlicher Fort-\n schritt, der öffentlichen Meinung feige Concessionen zu machen?] . Man versteht leicht, dass\n nachdem sich die Fabrikmagnaten in das Unvermeidliche gefügt und mit\n ihm ausgesöhnt, die Widerstandskraft des Kapitals graduell abschwachte,\n während zugleich die Angriffskraft der Arbeiterklasse wuchs mit der Zahl\n ihrer Verbündeten in den nicht unmittelbar interessirten Gesellschafts-\n schichten. Daher vergleichungsweis rascher Fortschritt seit 1860.\n\n\n\n\nDie Färbereien und Bleichereien [Note: Der 1860 erlassene Akt über Bleichereien und Färbereien bestimmt, dass\n der Arbeitstag am 1. August 1861 vorläufig auf 12, am 1. August 1862 definitiv\n auf 10 Stunden, d. h. 10½ für Werkeltage und 7½ für Samstage herabgesetzt\n werde. Als nun das böse Jahr 1862 anbrach, wiederholte sich die alte Farce.\n Die Herrn Fabrikanten petitionirten das Parlament, nur noch für ein einziges Jahr\n länger die zwölfstündige Beschäftigung von jungen Personen und Frauenzimmern\n zu dulden . . . . „Beim gegenwärtigen Zustand des Geschäfts (zur Zeit der Baum-\n wollnoth) sei es ein grosser Vortheil für die Arbeiter, wenn man ihnen erlaube]  wurden 1860, die Spitzenfabri-\n [Running matter: I. 18] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"274\", \"facs\": \"#f0293\"} --> ken und Strumpfwirkereien 1861 dem Fabrikakt von 1850 unterworfen.\n In Folge des ersten Berichts der „Kommission über die Be-\n schäftigung der Kinder“ (1863) theilten dasselbe Schicksal die\n Manufaktur aller Erdenwaaren (nicht nur Töpfereien), der Schwe-\n felhölzer, Zündhütchen, Patronen, Tapetenfabrik, Baumwollsammt-Schee-\n rerei (fustian cutting) und zahlreiche Prozesse, die unter dem Ausdruck\n „finishing“ (letzte Appretur) zusammengefasst sind. Im Jahre 1863 wur-\n den die „Bleicherei in offener Luft“ [Note: Die „Bleicher in offner Luft“ hatten sich dem Gesetz von 1860 über\n „Bleicherei“ durch die Lüge entzogen, dass sie keine Weiber des Nachts ver-\n arbeiteten. Die Lüge wurde von den Fabrikinspektoren aufgedeckt, zugleich aber\n das Parlament durch Arbeiterpetitionen seiner wiesenduftigkühlen Vorstellungen\n von „Bleicherei in offner Luft“ beraubt. In dieser Luftbleicherei werden Trocken-\n zimmer von 90 bis 100 Grad Fahrenheit angewandt, worin hauptsächlich Mädchen\n arbeiten. „Cooling“ (Abkühlung) ist der technische Ausdruck für ihr gelegent-\n liches Entrinnen aus dem Trockenzimmer in die freie Luft. „Fünfzehn Mädchen\n in den Trockenzimmern. Hitze von 80 zu 90° für Leinwand, von 100° und mehr\n für Cambrics. Zwölf Mädchen bügeln und legen auf (die Cambrics etc.) in einem\n kleinen Zimmer von ungefähr 10 Quadratfuss, in der Mitte ein enggeschlossener\n Ofen. Die Mädchen stehn rund um den Ofen herum, der eine schreckliche Gluth\n ausstrahlt und die Cambrics rasch für die Bügelerinnen trocknet. Die Stunden-\n zahl für diese Hände ist unbeschränkt. Wenn geschäftig, arbeiten sie bis 9 oder\n 12 Uhr Nachts viele Tage hintereinander.“ (Reports etc. for 31st Oct.\n 1862“, p. 56.) Ein Arzt erklärt: „Für die Abkühlung sind keine besondren\n Stunden erlaubt, aber wenn die Temperatur zu unerträglich wird, oder die Hände\n der Arbeiterinnen sich vom Schweiss beschmutzen, ist ihnen gestattet, ein paar]  und die Bäckerei\n [Note: 12 Stunden täglich zu arbeiten und so viel Arbeitslohn als möglich herauszu-\n schlagen … Es war bereits gelungen, eine Bill in diesem Sinn ins Unterhaus zu\n bringen. Sie fiel vor der Agitation der Arbeiter in den Bleichereien Schottlands.“\n („Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1862“, p. 14, 15.) So geschlagen von den Arbeitern\n selbst, in deren Namen es zu sprechen vorgab, entdeckte das Kapital nun, mit Hilfe\n juristischer Brillen, dass der Akt von 1860, gleich allen Parlamentsakten zum\n „Schutz der Arbeit“, in sinnverwirrten Wortschraubungen abgefasst, einen Vor-\n wand gebe, die „calenderers“ und „finishers“ von seiner Wirkung auszu-\n schliessen. Die englische Jurisdiktion, stets getreuer Knecht des Kapitals,\n sanktionirte durch den Hof der „Common Pleas“ die Rabulisterei. „Es hat\n grosse Unzufriedenheit unter den Arbeitern erregt und ist sehr bedauerlich, dass\n die klare Absicht der Gesetzgebung auf Vorwand einer mangelhaften Wortdefinition\n vereitelt wird.“ (l. c. p. 18.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"275\", \"facs\": \"#f0294\"} --> unter eigne Akte gestellt, wovon der erste u. a. die Arbeit von Kindern,\n jungen Personen und Weibern zur Nachtzeit (von 8 Uhr Abends bis 6 Uhr\n Morgens) und der zweite die Anwendung von Bäckergesellen unter 18 Jah-\n ren zwischen 9 Uhr Abends und 5 Uhr Morgens verbietet. Auf die spä-\n teren Vorschläge der erwähnten Kommission, welche, mit Ausnahme des\n Ackerbaus, der Minen und des Transportwesens, alle wichtigern englischen\n Industriezweige der „Freiheit“ zu berauben drohen, kommen wir zurück.\n\n\n\n\nDer Leser erinnert sich, dass die Produktion von Mehrwerth\n oder die Extraktion von Mehrarbeit den spezifischen Inhalt und\n Zweck der kapitalistischen Produktion bildet, abgesehn von\n einer jeden aus der Unterordnung der Arbeit unter das Kapital etwa ent-\n springenden Umgestaltung der Produktionsweise selbst. Er\n erinnert sich, dass auf dem bisher entwickelten Standpunkt nur der selbst-\n ständige und daher gesetzlich mündige Arbeiter als Waaren-\n [Note: Minuten fortzugehn … Meine Erfahrung in der Behandlung der Krankheiten\n dieser Arbeiterinnen zwingt mich zu konstatiren, dass ihr Gesundheits-\n zustand tief unter dem der Baumwollarbeiterinnen steht (und\n das Kapital hatte sie in seinen Bittschriften an das Parlament als Rembrandisch\nübergesund gemalt!). Ihre auffallendsten Krankheiten sind Phthisis, Bron-\n chitis, Uterinkrankheiten, Hysterie in der scheusslichsten Form und Rheumatis-\n mus. Alle diese entspringen, wie ich glaube, direkt oder indirekt, aus der über-\n hitzten Luft ihrer Arbeitszimmer und dem Mangel genügender comfortabler Klei-\n dung, um sie beim Nachhausegehn während der Wintermonate vor der kaltfeuchten\n Atmosphäre zu schützen.“ l. c. p. 56, 57. Die Fabrikinspektoren bemerken\n über das den jovialen „Bleichern in offner Luft“ nachträglich abgetrotzte Gesetz\n von 1863: „Dieser Akt hat nicht nur verfehlt den Arbeitern den Schutz zu ge-\n währen, den er zu gewähren scheint . . . . er ist so formulirt, dass der Schutz erst\n eintritt, sobald man Kinder und Frauenzimmer nach 8 Uhr Abends an der Arbeit\n ertappt, und selbst dann ist die vorgeschriebene Beweismethode so verklausulirt,\n dass Bestrafung kaum erfolgen kann.“ (l. c. p. 52.) „Als ein Akt mit humanen\n und auf Erziehung gerichteten Zwecken ist er ganz und gar verfehlt. Man wird\n es doch kaum human nennen, Weibern und Kindern zu erlauben, oder, was\n auf dasselbe hinauskommt, sie zu zwingen, 14 Stunden täglich,\n mit oder ohne Mahlzeiten, wie es sich treffen mag, und vielleicht noch längere\n Stunden zu arbeiten, ohne Schranke mit Bezug auf das Alter, ohne Unterschied\n des Geschlechts, und ohne Rücksicht auf die gesellschaftlichen Gewohnheiten der\n Familien der Nachbarschaft, worin die Bleichwerke liegen.“ („Reports etc.\nfor 30th April 1863“, p. 40.)] \n [Running matter: 18*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"276\", \"facs\": \"#f0295\"} --> verkäufer mit dem Kapitalisten kontrahirt. Wenn in unsrer historischen\n Skizze also einerseits die moderne Industrie eine Hauptrolle spielt\n andrerseits die Arbeit physisch und rechtlich Unmündige\n so galt uns die eine nur als besondre Sphäre, die andre nur als besonders\n schlagendes Beispiel der Arbeitsaussaugung. Ohne jedoch der spätern\n Entwicklung vorzugreifen, folgt aus dem blossen Zusammenhang der ge-\n schichtlichen Thatsachen:\n\n\n\n\nErstens: In den durch Wasser, Dampf und Maschinerie zunächst\n revolutionirten Industrien, in diesen ersten Schöpfungen der modernen Pro-\n duktionsweise, den Baumwolle-, Wolle-, Flachs-, Seide-Spinnereien und\n Webereien wird der Trieb des Kapitals nach mass- und rücksichtsloser\nVerlängerung des Arbeitstags zuerst befriedigt. Die veränderte\n materielle Produktionsweise und die ihr entsprechend veränderten socialen\n Verhältnisse der Produzenten [Note: „The conduct of each of these classes (capitalists and workmen) has\n been the result of the relative situation in which they have been placed.“\n („Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1848“, p. 112.)]  schaffen erst die masslose Ausschreitung\n und rufen dann im Gegensatz die gesellschaftliche Kontrole hervor, welche\n den Arbeitstag mit seinen Pausen gesetzlich beschränkt, regulirt und uni-\n formirt. Diese Kontrole erscheint daher während der ersten Hälfte des\n 19. Jahrhunderts bloss als Ausnahmsgesetzgebung [Note: „The employments placed under restriction were connected with the\n manufacture of textile fabrics by the aid of steam or water power. There were\n two conditions to which an employment must be subject to cause it to be inspected,\n viz. the use of steam or water power, and the manufacture of certain specified\n fibres.“ („Reports etc. for 31. October 1864“, p. 8.)] . Sobald sie\n das Urgebiet der neuen Produktionsweise erobert hatte, fand sich, dass unter\n dess nicht nur viele andre Produktionszweige in das eigentliche Fa-\n brikregime eingetreten, sondern dass Manufakturen mit mehr\n oder minder verjährter Betriebsweise, wie Töpfereien, Glasereien u. s. w.,\n dass altmodische Handwerke, wie die Bäckerei, und endlich selbst\n die zerstreute s. g. Hausarbeit, wie Nägelmacherei u. s. w. [Note: Ueber den Zustand dieser sogenannten häuslichen Industrie äusserst\n reichhaltiges Material in den letzten Berichten der „Children’s Employment\n Commission“.] , seit\n lange der kapitalistischen Exploitation eben so sehr verfallen waren als\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"277\", \"facs\": \"#f0296\"} --> die Fabrik. Die Gesetzgebung ward daher gezwungen, ihren Ausnahms-\n charakter allmählig abzustreifen, oder, wo sie römisch kasuistisch verfährt,\n wie in England, irgend ein Haus, worin man arbeitet, nach Belieben für\n eine Fabrik (factory) zu erklären [Note: „The Acts of last Session (1864) … embrace a diversity of occupations\n the customs in which differ greatly, and the use of mechanical power to give\n motion to machinery is no longer one of the elements necessary, as formerly, to\n constitute in legal phrase a Factory.“ („Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1864“,\n p. 8.)] .","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0001","text":"## 4) Der Arbeitstag.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"8.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0002","text":"Wir gingen von der Voraussetzung aus, dass die Arbeitskraft zu\n ihrem Werthe gekauft und verkauft wird. Ihr Werth, wie der jeder\n andern Waare, wird bestimmt durch die zu ihrer Produktion nöthige Ar-\n beitszeit. Erheischt also die Durchschnittssumme der täglichen Lebens-\n mittel des Arbeiters zu ihrer Produktion 6 Stunden täglich, so muss er\n im Durchschnitt 6 Stunden per Tag arbeiten, um seine Arbeitskraft täglich\n zu produziren oder den in ihrem Verkauf erhaltenen Werth zu repro-\n duziren. Der nothwendige Theil seines Arbeitstages beträgt\n dann 6 Stunden, und ist daher, unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen,\n eine gegebene Grösse. Aber damit ist die Grösse des Arbeits-\n tags selbst noch nicht gegeben.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"8.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0003","text":"Nehmen wir an, die Linie a--------------b stelle die Dauer oder Länge\n der noth wendigen Arbeitszeit vor, sage 6 Stunden. Je nachdem\n die Arbeit über a b um 1, 3 oder 6 Stunden u. s. w. verlängert wird, er-\n halten wir die 3 verschiedenen Linien:","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"8.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0004","text":"[Figure in source: ]  die drei verschiedne Arbeitstage von 7, 9 und 12 Stunden vorstellen. Die\n Verlängerungslinie b c stellt die Länge der Surplusarbeitszeit vor. Da der\n Arbeitstag = a b + b c oder a c ist, variirt er mit der variablen Grösse\n b c. Da uns a b gegeben ist, kann das Verhältniss von b c zu a b\n stets gemessen werden. Es beträgt in Arbeitstag I ⅙, in Arbeitstag II\n\\frac{3}{6}, und in Arbeitstag III \\frac{6}{6} von a b. Da ferner die Proportion\n\\frac{Surplusarbeitszeit}{Nothwendige Arbeitszeit} die Rate des Mehrwerths bestimmt, ist letztere\n gegeben durch jenes Verhältniss. Sie beträgt in den drei verschiedenen\n Arbeitstagen respektive 16⅔, 50 und 100 %. Umgekehrt würde die\nRate des Mehrwerths allein uns nicht die Grösse des Arbeits-\n tags geben. Wäre sie z. B. gleich 100 %, so könnte der Arbeitstag\n 8, 10, 12stündig u. s. w. sein.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"8.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0005","text":"Sie würde anzeigen, dass die zwei Be-\n standtheile des Arbeitstags, nothwendige Arbeit und Mehrarbeit, gleich\n gross sind, aber nicht wie gross jeder dieser Theile.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"8.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0006","text":"Der Arbeitstag ist also keine constante, sondern eine variable\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"199\", \"facs\": \"#f0218\"} --> Grösse. Einer seiner Theile ist zwar bestimmt durch die zur bestän-\n digen Reproduktion des Arbeiters selbst erheischte Arbeitszeit, aber seine\n Gesammtgrösse wechselt mit der Länge oder Dauer der Mehrarbeit. Der\n Arbeitstag ist daher bestimmbar, aber an und für sich unbestimmt [Note: „A day’s labour is vague, it may be long or short.“ „An Essay on\n Trade and Commerce, containing Observations on Taxation etc.\nLondon 1770“, p. 73.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"8.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0007","text":"Obgleich nun der Arbeitstag keine feste, sondern eine fliessende Grösse\n ist, kann er andrerseits nur innerhalb gewisser Schranken vari-\n iren. Seine Minimalschranke ist jedoch unbestimmbar. Allerdings,\n setzen wir die Verlängerungslinie b c, oder die Mehrarbeit, = O, so er-\n halten wir eine Minimalschranke, den Theil des Tages nämlich, den der\n Arbeiter nothwendig zu seiner Selbsterhaltung arbeiten muss. Auf Grund-\n lage der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise kann die nothwendige Arbeit\n aber immer nur einen Theil seines Arbeitstages bilden, der Arbeitstag\n sich also nie auf diess Minimum verkürzen. Dagegen besitzt der Arbeitstag\n eine Maximalschranke. Er kann über eine gewisse Grenze hinaus\n nicht verlängert werden. Diese Maximalschranke ist doppelt bestimmt.\n Einmal durch die physische Schranke der Arbeitskraft.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"8.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0008","text":"Ein\n Mensch kann während des natürlichen Tags von 24 Stunden nur ein be-\n stimmtes Quantum Lebenskraft verausgaben und das Mass dieser Kraft-\n verausgabung bildet ein Mass für seine physisch mögliche Arbeitszeit. So\n kann ein Pferd Tag aus, Tag ein, nur 8 Stunden arbeiten. Während\n eines Theils des Tags muss die Kraft ruhen, schlafen, während eines an-\n dern Theils hat der Mensch andere physische Bedürfnisse zu befriedigen,\n sich zu nähren, reinigen, kleiden u. s. w. Ausser dieser rein physi-\n schen Schranke stösst die Verlängerung des Arbeitstags auf mora-\n lische Schranken. Der Arbeiter braucht Zeit zur Befriedigung gei-\n stiger und sozialer Bedürfnisse, deren Umfang und Zahl durch den allge-\n meinen Kulturzustand bestimmt sind. Die Variation des Arbeitstags be-\n wegt sich daher innerhalb absoluter physischer und mehr oder minder\n relativer sozialer Schranken.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"8.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0009","text":"Beide Schranken sind aber sehr elastischer\n Natur und erlauben den grössten Spielraum. So finden wir Arbeitstage\n von 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 18 und mehr Stunden, also von der verschie-\n densten Länge.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"8.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0010","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"200\", \"facs\": \"#f0219\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"8.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0011","text":"Der Kapitalist hat die Arbeitskraft zu ihrem Tageswerth gekauft.\n Ihm gehört ihr Gebrauchswerth während eines Arbeitstags. Er hat\n also das Recht erlangt, den Arbeiter während eines Tags für sich arbei-\n ten zu lassen. Aber was ist ein Arbeitstag [Note: Diese Frage ist unendlich wichtiger als die berühmte Frage Sir Robert\n Peel’s an die Birminghamer Handelskammer: „What is a pound?“, eine\n Frage, die nur gestellt werden konnte, weil Peel über die Natur des Geldes eben\n so unklar war als die „little shilling men“ von Birmingham.] ? Jedenfalls weni-\n ger als ein natürlicher Lebenstag. Um wie viel? Der Kapitalist hat\n seine eigne Ansicht über diess ultima Thule, die nothwendige\n Schranke des Arbeitstags. Als Kapitalist ist er nur personifizirtes\n Kapital. Seine Seele ist die Kapitalseele.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"8.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0012","text":"Das Kapital hat aber einen\n einzigen Lebenstrieb, den Trieb, sich zu verwerthen, Mehrwerth zu schaf-\n fen, mit seinem constanten Theil, den Produktionsmitteln, die grösst-\n mögliche Masse Mehrarbeit einzusaugen [Note: „Es ist die Aufgabe des Kapitalisten mit dem verausgabten Kapi-\n tal die grösstmögliche Summe Arbeit herauszuschlagen.“ („D’obtenir du\n capital dépensé la plus forte somme de travail possible.“) J. G.\nCourcelle-Seneuil: „Traité théorique et pratique des entre-\n prises industrielles. 2ème édit. Paris 1857“, p. 63.] . Das Kapital ist verstorbene\n Arbeit, die sich nur vampyrmässig belebt durch Einsaugung lebendiger\n Arbeit und um so mehr lebt, je mehr sie davon einsaugt.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"8.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0013","text":"Die Zeit, wäh-\n rend deren der Arbeiter arbeitet, ist die Zeit, während deren der Kapita-\n list die von ihm gekaufte Arbeitskraft consumirt [Note: „An Hour’s Labour lost in a day is a prodigious injury to a commercial\n state.“ „There is a very great consumption of luxuries among the\n labouring poor of this kingdom; particularly among the manufacturing populace:\n by which they also consume their time, the most fatal of consump-\n tions.“ An Essay on Trade and Commerce etc. p. 47 u. 153.] . Consumirt der\n Arbeiter seine disponible Zeit für sich selbst, so bestiehlt er den Kapita-\n listen [Note: „Si le manouvrier libre prend un instant de repos, l’économie sordide qui\n le suit des yeux avec inquiétude, prétend qu’il la vole.“ N. Linguet: „Théo-\n rie des Lois Civiles etc. London 1767“, t. II, p. 466.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"8.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0014","text":"Der Kapitalist beruft sich also auf das Gesetz des Waaren-\n austauschs. Er, wie jeder andre Käufer, sucht den grösstmöglichen\n Nutzen aus dem Gebrauchswerth seiner Waare herauszuschlagen. Plötz-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"201\", \"facs\": \"#f0220\"} --> lich aber erhebt sich die Stimme des Arbeiters, die im Sturm und Drang\n des Produktionsprozesses verstummt war:","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"8.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0015","text":"Die Waare, die ich dir verkauft habe, unterscheidet sich von dem\n andern Waarenpöbel dadurch, dass ihr Gebrauch Werth schafft und\n grösseren Werth als sie selbst kostet. Diess war der Grund, warum du\n sie kauftest. Was auf deiner Seite als Verwerthung von Kapital erscheint,\n ist auf meiner Seite überschüssige Verausgabung von Arbeitskraft. Du\n und ich kennen auf dem Marktplatz nur ein Gesetz, das des Waarenaus-\n tauschs. Und der Consum der Waare gehört nicht dem Verkäufer, der sie\n veräussert, sondern dem Käufer, der sie erwirbt. Dir gehört daher der Ge-\n brauch meiner täglichen Arbeitskraft. Aber vermittelst ihres täglichen\n Verkaufspreises muss ich sie täglich reproduziren und daher von neuem ver-\n kaufen können. Abgesehen von dem natürlichen Verschleiss durch Alter\n u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"8.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0016","text":"w., muss ich fähig sein, morgen mit demselben Normalzustand von\n Kraft, Gesundheit und Frische zu arbeiten, wie heute. Du predigst\n mir beständig das Evangelium der „Sparsamkeit“ und „Enthaltung“.\n Nun gut! Ich will wie ein vernünftiger, sparsamer Wirth mein ein-\n ziges Vermögen, die Arbeitskraft, haushalten und mich jeder tollen\n Verschwendung derselben enthalten. Ich will täglich nur so viel von\n ihr flüssig machen, in Bewegung, in Arbeit umsetzen, als sich mit ihrer\n Normaldauer und gesunden Entwicklung verträgt. Durch massloses Ver-\n längern des Arbeitstages kannst du in Einem Tage ein grösseres Quantum\n meiner Arbeitskraft flüssig machen, als ich in drei Tagen ersetzen kann.\n Was du so an Arbeit gewinnst, verliere ich an Arbeitssubstanz. Die Be-\n nutzung meiner Arbeitskraft und die Beraubung derselben sind ganz\n verschiedne Dinge.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"8.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0017","text":"Wenn die Durchschnittsperiode, die ein Durchschnitts-\n arbeiter bei vernünftigem Arbeitsmass leben kann, 30 Jahre beträgt, ist\n der Werth meiner Arbeitskraft, den du mir einen Tag in den andern zahlst,\n\\frac{1}{365 × 30} oder \\frac{1}{10950} ihres Gesammtwerths. Consumirst du sie aber\n in 10 Jahren, so zahlst du mir nur \\frac{1}{3650} oder nur ⅓ ihres Werths täg-\n lich und bestiehlst mich daher täglich um ⅔ des Werths meiner Waare.\n Du zahlst mir eintägige Arbeitskraft, wo du dreitägige verbrauchst. Das\n ist wider unsern Vertrag und das Gesetz des Waarenaustauschs. Ich ver-\n lange also einen Arbeitstag von normaler Länge und ich verlange ihn\n ohne Appell an dein Herz, denn in Geldsachen hört die Gemüthlichkeit\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"202\", \"facs\": \"#f0221\"} --> auf.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"8.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0018","text":"Du magst ein Musterbürger sein, vielleicht Mitglied des Vereins zur\n Abschaffung der Thierquälerei und obendrein im Geruch der Heiligkeit\n stehen, aber dem Ding, das du mir gegenüber repräsentirst, schlägt kein\n Herz in seiner Brust. Was darin zu pochen scheint, ist mein eigner\n Herzschlag. Ich verlange den Normalarbeitstag, weil ich den\nWerth meiner Waare verlange, wie jeder andre Verkäufer [Note: Während des grossen Strike der London builders, 1860—61, zur Reduk-\n tion des Arbeitstags auf 9 Stunden, veröffentlichte ihr Comité eine Erklärung, die\n halb und halb auf das Plaidoyer unsres Arbeiters hinausläuft. Die Erklärung\n spielt nicht ohne Ironie darauf an, dass der Profitwüthigste der „building masters“\n — ein gewisser Sir M. Peto — im „Geruch der Heiligkeit“ stehe.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"8.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0019","text":"Man sieht: von ganz elastischen Schranken abgesehn, ergiebt sich aus\n der Natur des Waarenaustauschs selbst keine Grenze des Arbeitstags, also\n keine Grenze der Mehrarbeit. Der Kapitalist behauptet daher nur sein Recht\n als Käufer, wenn er den Arbeitstag so lange als möglich und wo möglich aus\n Einem Arbeitstag zwei zu machen sucht. Andrerseits schliesst die spezifische\n Natur der verkauften Waare eine Schranke ihres Consums durch den Käufer\n ein, und der Arbeiter behauptet daher nur sein Recht als Verkäufer, wenn er\n den Arbeitstag auf eine bestimmte Normalgrösse beschränken will. Es findet\n hier also eine Antinomie statt, Recht wider Recht, beide gleichmässig\n durch das Gesetz des Waarenaustauschs besiegelt. Zwischen gleichen\n Rechten entscheidet die Gewalt.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"8.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0020","text":"Und so stellt sich in der Geschichte\n der kapitalistischen Produktion die Normirung des Arbeitstags\n als Kampf um die Schranken des Arbeitstags dar — ein\n Kampf zwischen dem Gesammtkapitalisten, d. h. der Klasse der Kapi-\n talisten, und dem Gesammtarbeiter, oder der Arbeiterklasse.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"8.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0021","text":"Das Kapital, wie bereits bemerkt, hat die Mehrarbeit nicht er-\n funden. Ueberall, wo ein Theil der Gesellschaft das Monopol der Produk-\n tionsmittel besitzt, muss der Arbeiter, frei oder unfrei, der zu seiner Selbst-\n erhaltung nothwendigen Arbeitszeit überschüssige Arbeitszeit zusetzen,\n um die Lebensmittel für die Eigner der Produktionsmittel zu produziren [Note: „Those who labour . . . . in reality feed both the pensioners called\n therich, and themselves.“ (Edmund Burke l. c. p.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"8.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0022","text":"2.)] ,\n ob dieser Eigenthümer nun ein atheniensischer ϰαλος ϰἀγαϑός, ein\n etruskischer Theokrat, civis romanus, normännischer Baron, amerikanischer\n Sklavenhalter, walachischer Bojar, moderner Landlord oder Kapitalist\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"203\", \"facs\": \"#f0222\"} --> ist [Note: Sehr naiv bemerkt Niebuhr in seiner „Römischen Geschichte“:\n „Man kann sich nicht verhehlen, dass Werke wie die etruskischen, die in ihren\n Trümmern erstaunen, in kleinen (!) Staaten Frohnherrn und Knechte vor-\n aussetzen.“ Viel tiefer sagte Sismondi, dass „Brüsseler Spitzen“ Lohnherrn\n und Lohndiener voraussetzen.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"8.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0023","text":"Indess ist klar, dass wenn in einer ökonomischen Gesellschafts-\n formation nicht der Tauschwerth, sondern der Gebrauchswerth\n des Produkts vorwiegt, die Mehrarbeit durch einen engeren oder weiteren\n Kreis von Bedürfnissen beschränkt, aber kein schrankenloses Be-\n dürfniss nach Mehrarbeit durch den Charakter der Pro-\n duktion selbst gegeben ist. Wo wir im Alterthume scheinbare Ab-\n weichungen von diesem Gesetz finden, bilden sie in der That seinen direk-\n testen Beweis. Am entsetzlichsten z. B. zeigt sich hier die Ueberarbeit,\n wo der Tauschwerth in seiner selbstständigen Gestalt als Geld produzirt\n wird, in der Produktion von Gold und Silber. Gewaltsames zu Tod arbei-\n ten ist hier die offizielle Form der Ueberarbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"8.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0024","text":"Man lese nur den Diodo-\n rus Siculus [Note: „Man kann diese Unglücklichen (in den Goldbergwerken zwischen\n Aegypten, Aethiopien und Arabien), die nicht einmal ihren Körper reinlich halten,\n noch ihre Blösse decken können, nicht ansehen, ohne ihr jammervolles Schick-\n sal zu beklagen. Denn da findet keine Nachsicht und keine Schonung statt für\n Kranke, für Gebrechliche, für Greise, für die weibliche Schwachheit. Alle müssen,\n durch Schläge gezwungen, fortarbeiten, bis der Tod ihren Qualen und ihrer Noth\n ein Ende macht.“ Diod. Sic. Historische Bibliothek, Buch 3, c. 13.] . Wenn jedoch Völker, bei denen sich die Produktion noch\n in den niedrigeren Formen der Sklavenarbeit, Frohnarbeit u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"8.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0025","text":"bewegt,\n mitten in einem durch die kapitalistische Produktionsweise beherrschten\n Weltmarkt stehn, der den Verkauf ihrer Produkte ins Ausland zum vor-\n wiegenden Interesse entwickelt, wird den barbarischen Greueln der Skla-\n verei, Leibeigenschaft u. s. w. der civilisirte Greuel der Ueberarbeit auf-\n gepfropft. Daher bewahrte die Negerarbeit in den südlichen Staaten der\n amerikanischen Union einen gemässigt patriarchalischen Charakter, so\n lange die Produktion hauptsächlich auf den unmittelbaren Selbstbedarf ge-\n richtet war. In dem Masse aber wie der Export der Baumwolle das\n Lebensinteresse jener Staaten wurde, wurde die Ueberarbeitung des Negers,\n hier und da die Consumtion seines Lebens in sieben Arbeitsjahren, Faktor\n eines berechneten und berechnenden Systems.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"8.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0026","text":"Es galt nicht mehr eine\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"204\", \"facs\": \"#f0223\"} --> gewisse Masse nützlicher Produkte aus ihm gewinnen. Es galt nun\nder Produktion des Mehrwerths selbst. Aehnlich mit der\nFrohnarbeit, z. B. in den Donaufürstenthümern.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"8.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0027","text":"Die Vergleichung des Heisshungers nach Mehrarbeit in den Donau-\n fürstenthümern mit demselben Heisshunger in englischen Fabriken bietet\n ein besondres Interesse, weil die Mehrarbeit in der Frohnarbeit\n eine selbstständige, sinnlich wahrnehmbare Form besitzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"8.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0028","text":"Gesetzt der Arbeitstag zähle 6 Stunden nothwendiger Arbeit und 6\n Stunden Mehrarbeit. So liefert der freie Arbeiter dem Kapitalisten wöchent-\n lich 6 × 6, oder 36 Stunden Mehrarbeit. Es ist dasselbe, als ob er 3\n Tage in der Woche für sich und 3 Tage in der Woche umsonst für den\n Kapitalisten arbeite. Aber diess ist nicht sichtbar. Mehrarbeit und noth-\n wendige Arbeit verschwimmen in einander. Ich kann daher dasselbe Ver-\n hältniss z. B. auch so ausdrücken, dass der Arbeiter in jeder Minute 30\n Sekunden für sich und 30 für den Kapitalisten arbeitet u. s. w. Anders\n mit der Frohnarbeit. Die nothwendige Arbeit, die der walachische\n Bauer z. B. zu seiner Selbsterhaltung verrichtet, ist räumlich getrennt\n von seiner Mehrarbeit für den Bojaren. Die eine verrichtet er auf seinem\n eignen Felde, die andre auf dem herrschaftlichen Gut.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"8.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0029","text":"Beide Theile der\n Arbeitszeit existiren daher selbstständig neben einander. Die Mehr-\n arbeit ist als Frohnarbeit genau abgeschieden von der nothwendigen\n Arbeit. An dem quantitativen Verhältniss von Mebrarbeit und noth-\n wendiger Arbeit ändert diese verschiedne Erscheinungsform offenbar nichts.\n Drei Tage Mehrarbeit in der Woche bleiben drei Tage Arbeit, die kein\n Aequivalent für den Arbeiter selbst bildet, ob sie Frohnarbeit heisse oder\n Lohnarbeit. Bei dem Kapitalisten jedoch erscheint der Heisshunger nach\n Mehrarbeit im Drang zu massloser Verlängerung des Arbeits-\n tags, bei dem Bojaren einfacher in unmittelbarer Jagd auf Frohntage [Note: Das Nachfolgende bezieht sich auf die Zustände der rumänischen Provin-\n zen, wie sie sich vor der Umwälzung seit dem Krimkrieg gestaltet hatten.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"8.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0030","text":"Die Frohnarbeit war in den Donaufürstenthümern verknüpft mit Na-\n turalrenten und sonstigem Zubehör von Leibeigenschaft, bildete aber den\n entscheidenden Tribut an die herrschende Klasse. Wo diess der Fall,\n entsprang die Frohnarbeit selten aus der Leibeigenschaft, sondern die\n Leibeigenschaft meist umgekehrt aus der Frohnarbeit. So in den rumäni-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"205\", \"facs\": \"#f0224\"} --> schen Provinzen. Ihre ursprüngliche Produktionsweise war auf Gemein-\n eigenthum gegründet, aber nicht auf Gemeineigenthum in slavischer oder\n gar indischer Form. Ein Theil der Ländereien wurde als freies Privat-\n eigenthum von den Mitgliedern der Gemeinde selbstständig bewirthschaftet,\n ein anderer Theil — der ager publicus — gemeinsam von ihnen bestellt.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"8.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0031","text":"Die Produkte dieser gemeinsamen Arbeit dienten theils als Reservefonds\n gegen Missernten und andere Zufälle, theils als Staatsschatz zur Deckung\n für die Kosten von Krieg, Religion und andere Gemeindeausgaben. Im\n Laufe der Zeit usurpirten kriegerische und kirchliche Würdenträger mit\n dem Gemeineigenthum die Leistungen für dasselbe. Die Arbeit der freien\n Bauern auf ihrem Gemeindeland verwandelte sich in Frohnarbeit\n für die Diebe des Gemeindelandes. Damit entwickelten sich zugleich\n Leibeigenschafts-Verhältnisse, jedoch nur thatsächlich, nicht gesetzlich,\n bis das weltbefreiende Russland unter dem Vorwand, die Leibeigenschaft\n abzuschaffen, sie zum Gesetz erhob. Der Kodex der Frohnarbeit,\n den der russische General Kisseleff 1831 proklamirte, war natürlich\n von den Bojaren selbst diktirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"8.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0032","text":"Russland eroberte so mit einem Schlag\n diese Magnaten der Donaufürstenthümer und den Beifallsklatsch des libe-\n ralen Cretinismus von ganz Europa.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"8.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0033","text":"Nach dem „Règlement organique“, so heisst jener Kodex der\n Frohnarbeit, schuldet jeder walachische Bauer, ausser einer Masse detail-\n lirter Naturalabgaben, dem s. g. Grundeigenthümer 1) 12 Arbeitstage\n überhaupt, 2) einen Tag Feldarbeit und 3) einen Tag Holzfuhre. Summa\n Summarum 14 Tage im Jahre. Mit tiefer Einsicht in die politische Oeko-\n nomie wird jedoch der Arbeitstag nicht in seinem ordinären Sinn genom-\n men, sondern der zur Herstellung eines täglichen Durchschnittsprodukts\nnothwendige Arbeitstag, aber das tägliche Durchschnittsprodukt ist\n pfiffiger Weise so bestimmt, dass kein Cyklope in 24 Stunden damit fertig\n würde. In den dürren Worten echt russischer Ironie erklärt daher das\n „Règlement“ selbst, unter 12 Arbeitstagen sei das Produkt einer Hand-\n arbeit von 36 Tagen zu verstehn, unter einem Tag Feldarbeit drei Tage,\n und unter einem Tag Holzfuhr ebenfalls das Dreifache.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"8.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0034","text":"Summa: 42 Frohn-\n tage. Es kommt aber hinzu die s. g. Jobagie, Dienstleistungen, die\n dem Grundherrn für ausserordentliche Produktionsbedürfnisse gebühren.\n Eine bestimmtes jährliches Quantum der Mannschaft, je nach der Anzahl\n der Dorfbevölkerung, ist zur Jobagie verfehmt. Diese zusätzliche Frohn-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"206\", \"facs\": \"#f0225\"} --> arbeit wird für jeden walachischen Bauer auf 14 Tage geschätzt. So\n beträgt die vorgeschriebene Frohnarbeit 56 Arbeitstage jährlich. Das\n Ackerbaujahr zählt aber in der Walachei wegen des schlechten Klima’s\n nur 210 Tage, wovon 40 für Sonn- und Feiertage, 30 durchschnittlich\n für Unwetter, zusammen 70 Tage ausfallen. Bleiben 140 Arbeitstage.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"8.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0035","text":"Das Verhältniss der Frohnarbeit zur nothwendigen Arbeit, \\frac{56}{84}, oder\n 66⅔ %, drückt eine viel kleinere Rate des Mehrwerths aus als die,\n welche die Arbeit des englischen Agrikultur- oder Fabrikarbeiters regu-\n lirt. Diess ist jedoch nur die gesetzlich vorgeschriebene Frohnarbeit.\n Und in noch „liberalerem“ Geist als die englische Fabrikgesetzgebung hat\n das „Règlement organique“ seine eigne Umgehung zu erleichtern gewusst.\n Nachdem es aus 12 Tagen 54 gemacht, wird das nominelle Tagwerk jedes\n der 54 Frohntage wieder so bestimmt, dass eine Zubusse auf die folgenden\n Tage fallen muss. In einem Tag z. B. soll eine Landstrecke ausgegätet\n werden, die zu dieser Operation, namentlich auf den Maispflanzungen,\n doppelt so viel Zeit erheischt. Das gesetzliche Tagwerk für einzelne\n Agrikulturarbeiten ist so auslegbar, dass der Tag im Monat Mai anfängt\n und im Monat Oktober aufhört.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"8.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0036","text":"Für die Moldau sind die Bestimmun-\n gen noch härter. „Die zwölf Frohntage des Règlement organique“,\n rief ein siegtrunkener Bojar, „belaufen sich auf 365 Tage im Jahr!“ [Note: Weitere Details findet man in: „E. Regnault: Histoire poli-\n tique et sociale des Principautés Danubiennes. Paris 1855.“]","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"8.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0037","text":"War das Règlement organique der Donaufürstenthümer ein\npositiver Ausdruck des Heisshungers nach Mehrarbeit, den jeder\n Paragraph legalisirt, so sind die englischen Factory Acts nega-\n tive Ausdrücke desselben Heisshungers. Diese Gesetze treten dem\n Drang des Kapitals nach massloser Aussaugung der Arbeitskraft durch\ngewaltsame Beschränkung des Arbeitstags von Staats-\n wegen entgegen, und zwar von Seiten eines Staats, den Kapitalist und\n Landlord beherrschen. Von einer täglich bedrohlicher anschwellenden Ar-\n beiterbewegung abgesehn, diktirte dieselbe Nothwendigkeit die Beschrän-\n kung der Fabrikarbeit, welche den Guano auf die englischen Felder aus-\n goss. Dieselbe blinde Raubgier, die in dem einen Fall die Erde erschöpft,\n hatte in dem andern die Lebenskraft der Nation an der Wurzel ergriffen.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"8.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0038","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"207\", \"facs\": \"#f0226\"} --> Periodische Epidemieen sprachen hier eben so deutlich als das abneh-\n mende Soldatenmass in Deutschland und Frankreich [Note: „Im Allgemeinen spricht innerhalb gewisser Grenzen für das Gedeihen\n organischer Wesen das Ueberschreiten des Mittelmasses ihrer Art. Für den\n Menschen verkleinert sich sein Körpermass, wenn sein Gedeihen beeinträchtigt\n ist, sei es durch physische oder sociale Verhältnisse. In allen europäischen Län-\n dern, wo Konscription besteht, hat seit Einführung derselben das mittlere Körper-\n mass der erwachsenen Männer und im Ganzen ihre Tauglichkeit zum Kriegsdienst\n abgenommen. Vor der Revolution (1789) war das Maximum für den Infanterist\n in Frankreich 165 Centimeter; 1818 (Gesetz vom 10. März) 157, nach dem Gesetz\n vom 21.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"8.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0039","text":"März 1832, 156 Centimeter; durchschnittlich in Frankreich wegen\n mangelnder Grösse und Gebrechen über die Hälfte ausgemustert. Das Militair-\n mass war in Sachsen 1780: 178 Centimeter, jetzt 155. In Preussen ist es 157.\n Nach Angabe in der Bayrischen Zeitung vom 9. Mai 1862 von Dr. Meyer, stellt\n sich nach einem 9jährigen Durchschnitt heraus, dass in Preussen von 1000 Kon-\n scribirten 716 untauglich zum Militairdienst, 317 wegen Mindermass und 399\n wegen Gebrechen . . . . Berlin konnte 1858 sein Kontingent an Ersatz-Mann-\n schaft nicht stellen, es fehlten 156 Mann.“ (J. v. Liebig: „Die Chemie\n in ihrer Anwendung auf Agrikultur und Physiologie. 1862.\n 7te Auflage.“ Band I, p. 117, 118.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"8.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0040","text":"Der jetzt regulirende Factory Act von 1850 erlaubt für den\n durchschnittlichen Wochentag 10 Stunden, nämlich für die ersten 5 Wochen-\n tage 12 Stunden, von 6 Uhr Morgens bis 6 Uhr Abends, wovon aber\n ½ Stunde für Frühstück und eine Stunde für Mittagsessen gesetzlich ab-\n gehn, also 10½ Arbeitsstunden bleiben, und 8 Stunden für den Samstag,\n von 6 Uhr Morgens bis 2 Uhr Nachmittags, wovon ½ Stunde für Früh-\n stück abgeht. Bleiben 60 Arbeitsstunden, 10½ für die ersten 5 Wochen-\n tage, 7½ für den letzten Wochentag [Note: Die Geschichte des Fabrikakts von 1850 folgt im Verlauf dieses Kapitels.] . Es sind eigne Wächter des\n Gesetzes bestellt, die dem Ministerium des Innern direkt untergeordneten\nFabrikinspektoren, deren Berichte halbjährig von Parlamentswe-\n gen veröffentlicht werden. Sie liefern also eine fortlaufende und offizielle\n Statistik über den Kapitalistenheisshunger nach Mehrarbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"8.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0041","text":"Hören wir einen Augenblick die Fabrikinspektoren [Note: Auf die Periode vom Beginn der grossen Industrie in England bis 1845\n gehe ich nur hier und da  [Editorial alternatives: sic = eiu; corr = ein] , und verweise den Leser darüber auf:\n „Die Lage der arbeitenden Klasse in England. Von Friedrich\n Engels. Leipzig 1845.“ Wie tief Engelsden Geistder kapitalistischen Pro-] .","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"8.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0042","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"208\", \"facs\": \"#f0227\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"8.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0043","text":"„Der betrügerische Fabrikant beginnt die Arbeit eine Viertelstunde,\n manchmal früher, manchmal später, vor 6 Uhr Morgens, und schliesst sie\n eine Viertelstunde, manchmal früher, manchmal später, nach 6 Uhr Nach-\n mittags. Er nimmt 5 Minuten weg vom Anfang und Ende der nominell\n für das Frühstück anberaumten halben Stunde, und knappt 10 Minuten ab\n zu Anfang und Ende der für Mittagsessen anberaumten Stunde. Samstag\n arbeitet er eine Viertelstunde, manchmal mehr, manchmal weniger, nach\n 2 Uhr Nachmittags. So beträgt sein Gewinn:","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"8.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0044","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n |","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"8.44"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0045","text":"Oder 5 Stunden 40 Minuten wöchentlich, was mit 50 Arbeitswochen\n multiplicirt, nach Abzug von 2 Wochen für Feiertage oder gelegentliche\n Unterbrechungen, 27 Arbeitstage giebt [Note: „Suggestions etc. by Mr. L. Horner, Inspector of Factories“,\n im: „Factory Regulation’s Act. Ordered by the House of Com-\n mons to be printed 9. Aug. 1859“, p. 4, 5.] .“","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"8.45"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0046","text":"[Note: duktionsweise begriff, zeigen die Factory Reports, Reports on Mines\n u. s. w., die seit 1845 erschienen sind, und wie bewunderungswürdig er die Zustände\n im Detail malte, zeigt der oberflächlichste Vergleich seiner Schrift mit den 18 bis 20\n Jahre später veröffentlichten officiellen Reports der „Children’s Employment\n Commission.“ (1863—67.) Diese handeln nämlich von Industriezweigen,\n worin die Fabrikgesetzgebung bis 1862 noch nicht eingeführt war, zum Theil noch\n nicht eingeführt ist. Hier wurde also den von Engels geschilderten Zuständen\n mehr oder minder grosse Aenderung nicht von aussen aufgeherrscht. Meine Bei-\n spiele entlehne ich hauptsächlich der Freihandelsperiode nach 1848, jener paradisi-\n schen Zeit, wovon eben so grossmäulige als wissenschaftlich verwahrloste Frei-\n handelshausirburschen den Deutschen so fabelhaft viel vorfauchen.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"8.46"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0047","text":"— Uebrigens\n figurirt England hier nur im Vordergrund, weil es die kapitalistische Produktion\n klassisch repräsentirt und allein eine officiell fortlaufende Statistik der behandelten\n Gegenstände besitzt.]","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"8.47"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0048","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"209\", \"facs\": \"#f0228\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"8.48"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0049","text":"„Wird der Arbeitstag täglich 5 Minuten über die Normaldaner ver-\n längert, so giebt das 2½ Produktionstage im Jahr. [Note: „Reports of the Insp. of Fact. for the half year ended 1856“,\n p. 34.] “ „Eine zusätz-\n liche Stunde täglich, dadurch gewonnen, dass bald hier ein Stückchen Zeit\n erhascht wird, bald dort ein anderes Stückchen, macht aus den 12 Mona-\n ten des Jahrs 13“ [Note: „Reports etc. 30th April 1858“, p. 9.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"8.49"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0050","text":"Krisen, worin die Produktion unterbrochen und nur „kurze Zeit“, nur\n während einiger Tage in der Woche, gearbeitet wird, ändern natürlich nichts\n an dem Trieb nach Verlängerung des Arbeitstags. Je weniger Geschäfte\n gemacht werden, desto grösser soll der Gewinn auf das gemachte Geschäft\n sein. Je weniger Zeit gearbeitet werden kann, desto mehr Surplusarbeits-\n zeit soll gearbeitet werden. So berichten die Fabrikinspektoren über die\n Periode der Krise von 1857 — 1858:","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"8.50"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0051","text":"„Man mag es für eine Inkonsequenz halten, dass irgendwelche Ueber-\n arbeit zu einer Zeit stattfinde, wo der Handel so schlecht geht, aber sein\n schlechter Zustand spornt rücksichtslose Leute zu Ueberschreitungen; sie\n sichern sich so einen Extraprofit …“ „Zur selben Zeit“, sagt Leon-\n hard Horner, „wo 122 Fabriken in meinem Distrikt ganz aufgegeben\n sind, 143 still stehn und alle andern kurze Zeit arbeiten, wird die Ueber-\n arbeit über die gesetzlich bestimmte Zeit fortgesetzt [Note: „Reports etc.“ l. c. p. 43.] “.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"8.51"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0052","text":"„Obgleich“,\n sagt Herr Howell, „in den meisten Fabriken des schlechten Geschäfts-\n stands wegen nur halbe Zeit gearbeitet wird, erhalte ich nach wie vor\ndieselbe Anzahl von Klagen, dass eine halbe Stunde oder ¾ Stun-\n den täglich den Arbeitern weggeschnappt (snatched) werden durch Ein-\n griffe in die ihnen gesetzlich gesicherten Fristen für Mahlzeit und Erho-\n lung [Note: „Reports etc.“ l. c. p. 25.] “.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"8.52"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0053","text":"Dasselbe Phänomen wiederholt sich auf kleinerer Stufenleiter wäh-\n rend der furchtbaren Baumwollkrise von 1861 bis 1865 [Note: „Reports etc. for half year ending 30th April 1861.“ Sieh Ap-\n pendix N. 2; „Reports etc. 31st Octob. 1862“, p. 7, 52, 53. Die Ueber-\n schreitungen werden wieder zahlreicher mit dem letzten Halbjahr 1863. Vgl.\n „Reports etc. ending 31st Oct. 1863“, p. 7.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"8.53"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0054","text":"[Running matter: I. 14] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"210\", \"facs\": \"#f0229\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"8.54"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0055","text":"„Es wird zuweilen vorgeschützt, wenn wir Arbeiter während der\n Speisestunden oder sonst zu ungesetzlicher Zeit am Werk ertappen, dass\nsie die Fabrik durchaus nicht verlassen wollen, und dass\n es des Zwangs bedarf, um ihre Arbeit (Reinigen der Maschinen u. s. w.)\n zu unterbrechen, namentlich Samstag Nachmittags. Aber wenn die\n „Hände“ nach Stillsetzung der Maschinerie in der Fabrik bleiben, geschieht\n es nur, weil ihnen zwischen 6 Uhr Morgens und 6 Uhr Abends, in den ge-\n setzlich bestimmten Arbeitsstunden, keine Frist zur Verrichtung solcher\n Geschäfte gestattet worden ist [Note: „Reports etc. 31st Oct. 1860“, p. 23.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"8.55"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0056","text":"Mit welchem Fanatismus,\n nach gerichtlichen Aussagen der Fabrikanten, ihre Fabrikhände sich jeder Unter-\n brechung der Fabrikarbeit widersetzen, zeige folgendes Kuriosum: Anfang Juni\n 1836 gingen den Magistrates von Dewsbury (Yorkshire) Denunciationen zu, wo-\n nach die Eigner von 8 grossen Fabriken in der Nähe von Batley den Fabrikakt ver-\n letzt hätten. Ein Theil dieser Herren war angeklagt, 5 Knaben zwischen 12 und\n 15 Jahren von 6 Uhr Morgen des Freitags bis 4 Uhr Nachmittag des folgenden\n Samstags abgearbeitet zu haben, ohne irgend eine Erholung zu gestatten, ausser\n für Mahlzeiten und Eine Stunde Schlaf um Mitternacht. Und diese\n Kinder hatten die rastlose, 30stündige Arbeit zu verrichten in dem „shoddy-hole“,\n wie die Höhle heisst, worin Wollenlumpen aufgerissen werden und wo ein Luft-\n meer von Staub, Abfällen u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"8.56"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0057","text":"selbst den erwachsenen Arbeiter zwingt, den\n Mund beständig mit Schnupftüchern zu verbinden, zum Schutz seiner Lungen! Die\n Herren Angeklagten versicherten an Eidesstatt — als Quäker waren sie zu scru-\n pulös religiöse Männer einen Eid zu leisten, — sie hätten in ihrer grossen Barm-\n herzigkeit den elenden Kindern 4 Stunden Schlaf erlaubt, aber die Starrköpfe von\n Kindern wollten durchaus nicht zu Bett gehn! Die Herrn Quäker wurden zu 20\n Pfd. St. Geldbusse verurtheilt. Dryden ahnte diese Quäker:","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"8.57"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0058","text":"„Fox full fraught in seeming sanctity,","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"8.58"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0059","text":"That feared an oath, but like the devil would lie,","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"8.59"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0060","text":"That look’d like Lent, and had the holy leer,","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"8.60"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0061","text":"And durst not sin! before he said his prayer!“","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"8.61"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0062","text":"] “.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"8.62"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0063","text":"„Der durch Ueberarbeit über die gesetzliche Zeit zu machende Extra-\n profit scheint für viele Fabrikanten eine zu grosse Versuchung, um ihr\n widerstehen zu können. Sie rechnen auf die Chance nicht ausgefunden\n zu werden und berechnen, dass selbst im Fall der Entdeckung die Gering-\n fügigkeit der Geldstrafen und Gerichtskosten ihnen immer noch eine Ge-\n winnbilanz sichert [Note: „Rep. etc. 31. Oct. 1856“ p. 34.] “. „Wo die zusätzliche Zeit durch Multipli-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"211\", \"facs\": \"#f0230\"} --> kation kleiner Diebstähle („a multiplication of small\n thefts“) im Laufe des Tages gewonnen wird, stehn den Inspektoren fast\n unüberwindliche Schwierigkeiten der Beweisführung im Weg [Note: l. c. p. 35.] “.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"8.63"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0064","text":"Diese\n „kleinen Diebstähle“ des Kapitals an der Mahlzeit und Erholungs-\n zeit der Arbeiter bezeichnen die Fabrikinspektoren auch als „petty pilfer-\n ings of minutes“, Mausereien von Minuten [Note: l. c. p. 48.] , „snatching a few minutes“,\n Wegschnappen von Minuten [Note: l. c.] , oder wie die Arbeiter es technisch heissen,\n „nibbling and cribbling at meal times [Note: l. c.] “.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"8.64"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0065","text":"Man sieht, in dieser Atmosphäre ist die Bildung des Mehr-\n werths durch die Mehrarbeit kein Geheimniss. „Wenn Sie mir\n erlauben,“ sagte mir ein sehr respektabler Fabrikherr, „täglich nur\n 10 Minuten Ueberzeit arbeiten zu lassen, stecken Sie jährlich 1000\n Pfd. St. in meine Tasche [Note: l. c. p. 48.] “. „Zeitatome sind die Elemente\n des Gewinns [Note: „Moments are the elements of profit.“ „Rep. of the Insp.\n etc. 30th April 1860“, p. 56.] “.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"8.65"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0066","text":"Nichts ist in dieser Hinsicht charakteristischer als die Bezeichnung\n der Arbeiter, die volle Zeit arbeiten, durch „full times“ und die der Kin-\n der unter 13 Jahren, die nur 6 Stunden arbeiten dürfen, als „half-\n times [Note: Der Ausdruck hat offizielles Bürgerrecht, wie in der Fabrik, so in den\n Fabrikberichten.] “. Der Arbeiter ist hier nichts mehr als personificirte Arbeits-\n zeit. Alle individuellen Unterschiede lösen sich auf in die von „Voll-\n zeitler“ und „Halbzeitler“.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"8.66"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0067","text":"Den Trieb nach Verlängerung des Arbeitstags, den Wehr-\n wolfsheisshunger für Mehrarbeit, beobachteten wir bisher auf einem Gebiet,\n wo masslose Ausschreitungen, nicht übergipfelt, so sagt ein bürgerlicher\n englischer Oekonom, von den Grausamkeiten der Spanier gegen die Roth-\n häute Amerika’s [Note: „The cupidity of mill owners, whose cruelties in pursuits of gain, have\n hardly been exceeded by those perpetrated by the Spaniards on the conquest of] , das Kapital endlich an die Kette gesetzlicher\n [Running matter: 14*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"212\", \"facs\": \"#f0231\"} -->Regulation gelegt haben. Werfen wir jetzt den Blick auf einige Produk-\n tionszweige, wo die Aussaugung der Arbeitskraft entweder noch heute fessel-\nfrei ist oder es gestern noch war.","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"8.67"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0068","text":"„Herr Broughton, ein County Magistrate, erklärte als Präsident eines\n Meetings, abgehalten in der Stadthalle von Nottingham, am 1 4. Januar\n 1860, dass in dem mit der Spitzenfabrikation beschäftigten\n Theile der städtischen Bevölkerung ein der übrigen civilisirten Welt un-\n bekannter Grad von Leid und Entbehrung vorherrscht . . . . Um 2, 3,\n 4 Uhr des Morgens werden Kinder von 9—10 Jahren ihren schmutzigen\n Betten entrissen und gezwungen, für die nackte Subsistenz bis 10, 11, 12\n Uhr Nachts zu arbeiten, während ihre Glieder wegschwinden, ihre Gestalt\n zusammenschrumpft, ihre Gesichtszüge abstumpfen und ihr menschliches\n Wesen ganz und gar in einem steinähnlichen Torpor erstarrt, dessen blos-\n ser Anblick schauderhaft ist. Wir sind nicht überrascht, dass Herr Mal-\n let und andere Fabrikanten auftraten, um Protest gegen jede Dis-\n kussion einzulegen . . . . Das System, wie der Rev. Mr.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"8.68"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0069","text":"Montagu\n Valpu es beschrieb, ist ein System unbeschränkter Sklaverei,\n Sklaverei in socialer, physischer, moralischer und intellektueller Beziehung.\n … Was soll man denken von einer Stadt, die ein öffentliches Meeting ab-\n hält, um zu petitioniren, dass die Arbeitszeit für Männer\n täglich auf 18 Stunden beschränkt werden solle! . . . .\n Wir deklamiren gegen die virginischen und karolinischen Pflanzer. Ist\n jedoch ihr Negermarkt, mit allen Schrecken der Peitsche und dem Schacher\n in Menschenfleisch, abscheulicher als diese langsame Menschenabschlach-\n tung, die vor sich geht, damit Schleier und Kragen zum Vortheil von\n Kapitalisten fabrizirt werden [Note: London Daily Telegraph vom 14. Januar 1860.] ?“","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"8.69"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0070","text":"Die Töpferei (Pottery) von Staffordshire hat während der\n letzten 22 Jahre den Gegenstand drei parlamentarischer Untersuchungen\n [Note: America, in the pursuit of gold.“ John Wade: „History of the Middle\n and Working Classes. 3d ed. Lond. 1835,“ p. 114. Der theoretische\n Theil dieses Buchs, eine Art Grundriss der politischen Oekonomie, enthält für\n seine Zeit einiges Originelle, z. B. über Handelskrisen. Der historische Theil\n leidet an schamlosem Plagiarismus aus Sir M. Eden’s: „History of the\n Poor. London 1799.“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"213\", \"facs\": \"#f0232\"} --> gebildet. Die Resultate sind niedergelegt im Bericht des Herrn\nScriven von 1841 an die „Children’s Employment Commissioners“,\n im Bericht des Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"8.70"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0071","text":"Greenhow von 1860, veröffentlicht auf Befehl\n des ärztlichen Beamten des Privy Council (Public Health, 3d Re-\n port, I, 102—113), endlich im Bericht des Herrn Longe von 1863,\n in „First Report of the Children’s Employment Commis-\n sion“ vom 13. Juni 1863. Für meine Aufgabe genügt ein kurzer Auszug\n aus den Zeugenaussagen der verarbeiteten Kinder, in den Berichten von 1860\n und 1863. Von den Kindern mag man auf die Erwachsenen schliessen, na-\n mentlich Mädchen und Frauen, und zwar in einem Industriezweig, neben\n dem Baumwollspinnerei u. d. g. als ein sehr angenehmes und gesundes\n Geschäft erscheint [Note: Vgl. Engels „Lage etc.“ p. 249—51.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"8.71"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0072","text":"Wilhelm Wood, neunjährig, „war 7 Jahre 10 Monate alt, als er\n zu arbeiten begann.“ Er „ran moulds“ (trug die fertig geformte\n Waare in die Trockenstube, um nachher die leere Form zurückzubringen)\n von Anfang an. Er kommt jeden Tag in der Woche um 6 Uhr Morgens\n und hört auf ungefähr 9 Uhr Abends. „Ich arbeite bis 9 Uhr Abends\n jeden Tag in der Woche. So z. B. während der letzten 7—8 Wochen.“\n Also fünfzehnstündige Arbeit für ein siebenjähriges Kind! J. Murray,\n ein zwölfjähriger Knabe, sagt aus: „I run moulds and turn jigger (drehe\n das Rad). Ich komme um 6 Uhr, manchmal um 4 Uhr Morgens. Ich\n habe während der ganzen letzten Nacht bis diesen Morgen 8 Uhr gearbei-\n tet. Ich war nicht im Bett seit der letzten Nacht. Ausser mir arbeiteten\n 8 oder 9 andre Knaben die letzte Nacht durch. Alle ausser Einem sind\n diesen Morgen wieder gekommen. Ich bekomme wöchentlich 3 sh. 6 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"8.72"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0073","text":"(1 Thaler 5 Groschen). Ich bekomme nicht mehr, wenn ich\n die ganze Nacht durcharbeite. Ich habe in der letzten Woche\n zwei Nächte durchgearbeitet.“ Fernyhough, ein zehnjähriger\n Knabe: „Ich habe nicht immer eine ganze Stunde für das Mittagsessen;\n oft nur eine halbe Stunde; jeden Donnerstag, Freitag und Samstag [Note: „Children’s Employment Commission. First Report etc.\n 1863“, Appendix p. 16, 19, 18.] .“","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"8.73"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0074","text":"Dr. Greenhow erklärt die Lebenszeit in den Töpferdistrikten von\nStoke-upon-Trent und Wolstanton für ausserordentlich kurz.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"214\", \"facs\": \"#f0233\"} --> Obgleich im Distrikt Stoke nur 30.6 %, und in Wolstanton nur 30.4 % der\n männlichen Bevölkerung über 20 Jahre in den Töpfereien beschäftigt\n sind, fällt im ersten Distrikt mehr als die Hälfte der Todesfälle\n an Brustkrankheiten unter Männern über 20 Jahren, und im letzteren Distrikt\n ungefähr ⅖, auf Töpfer. Dr. Boothroyd, praktischer Arzt zu Han-\n ley, sagt aus: „Jede successive Generation der Töpfer ist zwerghafter\n und schwächer als die vorhergehende.“ Ebenso ein anderer Arzt, Herr\n Mc Bean: „Seit ich vor 25 Jahren meine Praxis unter den Töpfern be-\n gann, hat sich die auffallende Entartung dieser Klasse fortschreitend in\n Abnahme von Gestalt und Gewicht gezeigt.“ Diese Aussagen sind dem\n Bericht des Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"8.74"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0075","text":"Greenhow von 1860 entnommen [Note: „Public Health. 3d Report etc.“, p. 102, 104, 105.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"8.75"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0076","text":"Aus dem Bericht der Kommissäre von 1863 Folgendes: Dr. J. T.\n Arledge, Oberarzt des North Staffordshire Krankenhauses, sagt: „Als eine\n Klasse repräsentiren die Töpfer, Männer und Frauen … eine entartete\n Bevölkerung, physisch und moralisch. Sie sind in der Regel verzwergt,\n schlecht gebaut, und oft an der Brust verwachsen. Sie altern vorzeitig\n und sind kurzlebig; phlegmatisch und blutlos, verrathen sie die Schwäche\n ihrer Konstitution durch hartnäckige Anfälle von Dyspepsie, Leber- und\n Nierenstörungen und Rheumatismus. Vor allem aber sind sie Brustkrank-\n heiten unterworfen, der Pneumonie, Phthisis, Bronchitis und dem Asthma.\n Eine Form des letztern ist ihnen eigenthümlich und bekannt unter dem\n Namen des Töpfer-Asthma oder der Töpfer-Schwindsucht.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"8.76"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0077","text":"Skrophulose,\n die Mandeln, Knochen oder andre Körpertheile angreift, ist eine Krank-\n heit von mehr als zwei Dritttheilen der Töpfer … Dass die Entartung\n (Degenerescence) der Bevölkerung dieses Distrikts nicht noch viel grösser\n ist, verdankt sie ausschliesslich der Rekrutirung aus den umliegenden\n Landdistrikten und den Zwischenheirathen mit gesundern Racen.“ Herr\nCharles Pearson, vor kurzem noch House Surgeon derselben Kran-\n kenanstalt, schreibt in einem Briefe an den Kommissär Longe u.","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"8.77"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0078","text":"a.: „Ich\n kann nur aus persönlicher Beobachtung, nicht statistisch sprechen, aber\n ich stehe nicht an zu versichern, dass meine Empörung wieder und wieder\n aufkochte bei dem Anblick dieser armen Kinder, deren Gesundheit geopfert\n wurde, um der Habgier ihrer Eltern und Arbeitsgeber zu fröhnen.“ Er\n zählt die Ursachen der Töpferkrankheiten auf und schliesst sie culminirend\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"215\", \"facs\": \"#f0234\"} --> ab mit „Long Hours“ („langen Arbeitsstunden“). Der Kommissions-\n bericht hofft, dass „eine Manufaktur von so hervorragender Stellung\n in den Augen der Welt nicht lange mehr den Makel tragen wird, dass ihr\n grosser Erfolg begleitet ist von physischer Entartung, vielverzweigten\n körperlichen Leiden, und frühem Tode der Arbeiterbevölkerung, durch\n deren Arbeit und Geschick so grosse Resultate erzielt worden sind [Note: „Children’s Employm. Commission. 1863“, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"8.78"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0079","text":"24, 22 u. XI.] “.\n Was von den Töpfereien in England, gilt von denen in Schottland [Note: l. c. p. XLVII.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"8.79"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0080","text":"Die Manufaktur von Schwefelhölzern datirt von 1833,\n von der Entdeckung, den Phosphor auf die Zündruthe selbst anzubringen.\n Seit 1845 hat sie sich rasch in England entwickelt und von den dicht-\n bevölkertsten Theilen Londons namentlich auch nach Manchester, Bir-\n mingham, Liverpool, Bristol, Norwich, Newcastle, Glasgow verbreitet, mit\n ihr die Mundsperre, die ein Wiener Arzt schon 1845 als eigenthümliche\n Krankheit der Schwefelholzmacher entdeckte. Die Hälfte der Arbeiter\n sind Kinder unter 13 und junge Personen unter 18 Jahren. Die Manu-\n faktur ist wegen ihrer Ungesundheit und Widerwärtigkeit so verrufen,\n dass nur der verkommenste Theil der Arbeiterklasse, halbverhungerte\n Wittwen u. s. w., Kinder für sie hergiebt, „zerlumpte, halb verhungerte,\n ganz verwahrloste und unerzogne Kinder [Note: l. c. p.","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"8.80"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0081","text":"LIV.] .“ Von den Zeugen, die\n Kommissär White (1863) verhörte, waren 270 unter 18 J., 40 unter\n 10 J., 10 nur 8 und 5 nur 6 Jahre alt. Wechsel des Arbeitstags von\n 12 auf 14 und 15 Stunden, Nachtarbeit, unregelmässige Mahlzeiten, meist\n in den Arbeitsräumen selbst, die vom Phosphor verpestet sind [Note: l. c. p. LIV.] . Dante\n würde in dieser Manufaktur seine grausamsten Höllenphantasien übertrof-\n fen finden.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"8.81"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0082","text":"In der Tapetenfabrik werden die gröberen Sorten mit Maschinen,\n die feineren mit der Hand (block printing) gedruckt. Die lebhaftesten Ge-\n schäftsmonate fallen zwischen Anfang Oktober und Ende April. Wäh-\n rend dieser Periode dauert diese Arbeit häufig und fast ohne Unter-\n brechung von 6 Uhr Vormittags bis 10 Uhr Abends und tiefer in die\n Nacht.","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"8.82"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0083","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"216\", \"facs\": \"#f0235\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"8.83"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0084","text":"J. Leach sagt aus: „Letzten Winter (1862) blieben von 19 Mäd-\n chen 6 weg in Folge durch Ueberarbeitung zugezogener Krankheiten.\n Um sie wach zu halten, muss ich sie anschreien.“ W. Duffy: „Die\n Kinder konnten oft vor Müdigkeit die Augen nicht aufhalten, in der That,\n wir selbst können es oft kaum.“ J. Lightbourne: „Ich bin 13 Jahre\n alt … Wir arbeiteten letzten Winter bis 9 Uhr Abends und den Winter\n vorher bis 10 Uhr. Ich pflegte letzten Winter fast jeden Abend vom\n Schmerz wunder Füsse zu schreien.“ G. Apsden: „Diesen meinen\n Jungen pflegte ich, als er 7 Jahre alt war, auf meinem Rücken hin und\n her über den Schnee zu tragen, und er pflegte 16 Stunden zu arbeiten!","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"8.84"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0085","text":"… Ich habe oft nieder gekniet, um ihn zu füttern, während er an der\n Maschine stand, denn er durfte sie nicht verlassen, oder\n stillsetzen.“ Smith, der geschäftsführende Associé einer Manchester-\n Fabrik: „Wir (er meint seine „Hände,“ die für „uns“ arbeiten) arbeiten\n ohne Unterbrechung für Mahlzeiten, so dass die Tagesarbeit von 10½\n Stunden um 4½ Uhr Nachmittags fertig ist, und alles Spätere ist Ueber-\n zeit [Note: Diess ist nicht in unsrem Sinn der Surplusarbeitszeit zu nehmen.\n Diese Herrn betrachten die 10½ stündige Arbeit als Normalarbeitstag, der\n also auch die normale Mehrarbeit einschliesst. Dann beginnt „die Ueberzeit,“\n die etwas besser bezahlt wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"8.85"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0086","text":"Man wird bei einer spätern Gelegenheit sehn, dass\n die Verwendung der Arbeitskraft während des sogenannten Normaltages unter\n dem Werthe bezahlt wird, so dass die „Ueberzeit“ ein blosser Kapitalistenpfiff\n ist, um mehr „Mehrarbeit“ auszupressen, was es übrigens selbst dann bleibt, wenn\n die während des „Normaltages“ verwandte Arbeitskraft wirklich voll bezahlt\n wird.] . (Ob dieser Herr Smith wohl keine Mahlzeit während 10½\n Stunden zu sich nimmt?)","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"8.86"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0087","text":"Wir (derselbe Smith) hören selten auf vor 6\n Uhr Abends (er meint mit der Konsumtion „unserer“ Arbeitskraftsmaschi-\n nen), so dass wir (iterum Crispinus) in der That das ganze Jahr durch\n Ueberzeit arbeiten … Die Kinder und Erwachsenen (152 Kinder und\n junge Personen unter 18 Jahren und 140 Erwachsene) haben gleich-\n mässig während der letzten 18 Monate im Durchschnitt allermin-\n destens 7 Tage und 5 Stunden in der Woche gearbeitet, oder\n 78½ Stunden wöchentlich. Für die 6 Wochen endend am 2.","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"8.87"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0088","text":"Mai dieses\n Jahres (1863) war der Durchschnitt höher — 8 Tage oder 84 Stun-\n den in der Woche!“ Doch fügt derselbe Herr Smith, der dem plu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"217\", \"facs\": \"#f0236\"} --> ralis majestatis so sehr ergeben ist, schmunzelnd hinzu: „Maschinenarbeit\n ist leicht.“ Und so sagen die Anwender des Block Printing: „Handar-\n beit ist gesunder als Maschinenarbeit.“ Im Ganzen erklären sich die\n Herrn Fabrikanten mit Entrüstung gegen den Vorschlag, „die Maschi-\n nen wenigstens während der Mahlzeiten still zu setzen“.\n „Ein Gesetz,“ sagt Herr Otley, der manager einer Tapetenfabrik in\n Borough, das „Arbeitsstunden von 6 Uhr Morgens bis 9 Uhr Abends er-\n laubte, würde uns (!) sehr wohl zusagen, aber die Stunden des Factory\n Act von 6 Uhr Morgens bis 6 Uhr Abends passen uns (!) nicht …\n Unsere Maschine wird während des Mittagessens (welche Gross-\n muth!) still gesetzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"8.88"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0089","text":"Das Stillsetzen verursacht keinen nennenswerthen\n Verlust an Papier und Farbe. „Aber,“ fügt er sympathetisch hinzu,\n „ich kann verstehn, dass der damit verbundene Verlust\n nicht geliebt wird.“ Der Kommissionsbericht meint naiv, die Furcht\n einiger „leitender Firmen“ Zeit, d. h. Aneignungszeit fremder Arbeitszeit,\n und dadurch „Profit zu verlieren,“ sei kein „hinreichender Grund,“ um\n Kinder unter 13 und junge Personen unter 18 Jahren während 12 — 16\n Stunden ihr Mittagsmahl „verlieren zu lassen“, oder es ihnen zuzusetzen,\n wie man der Dampfmaschine Kohle und Wasser, der Wolle Seife, dem Rad\n Oel u. s. w. zusetzt, — während des Produktionsprozesses\n selbst, als blossen Hilfsstoff des Arbeitsmittels [Note: l. c. Appendix p. 123, 124, 125, 140 u. LIV.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"8.89"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0090","text":"Kein Industriezweig in England — (wir sehn von dem erst neuer-\n dings sich bahnbrechenden Maschinenbrod ab) — hat so alterthümliche,\n ja, wie man aus den Dichtern der römischen Kaiserzeit ersehn kann, vor-\n christliche Produktionsweise bis heute beibehalten, als die Bäckerei.\n Aber das Kapital, wie früher bemerkt, ist zunächst gleichgültig gegen\n den technologischen Charakter des Arbeitsprozesses, dessen es sich be-\n mächtigt. Es nimmt ihn zunächst, wie es ihn vorfindet.","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"8.90"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0091","text":"Die unglaubliche Brodverfälschung, namentlich in London, wurde\n zuerst enthüllt durch das Comité des Unterhauses „über die Verfäl-\n schung von Nahrungsmitteln“ (1855—56) und Dr. Hassall’s\n Schrift: „Adulterations detected [Note: Alaun, fein gerieben, oder mit Salz gemischt, ist ein normaler Handels-\n artikel, der den bezeichnenden Namen „baker’s stuff“ führt.] “. Die Folge dieser Enthül-\n lungen war das Gesetz vom 6.","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"8.91"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0092","text":"August 1860: „for preventing the adulte-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"218\", \"facs\": \"#f0237\"} --> ration of articles of food and drink,“ ein wirkungsloses Gesetz, da es\n natürlich die höchste Delikatesse gegen jeden free-trader beobachtet, der\n sich vornimmt durch Kauf und Verkauf gefälschter Waaren „to turn an\n honest penny [Note: Russ ist bekanntlich eine sehr energische Form des Kohlenstoffs und\n bildet ein Düngmittel, das kapitalistische Schornsteinfeger an englische Pächter\n verkaufen. Es ereignete sich nun 1862 ein Prozess, worin der britische „Juryman“\n entscheiden sollte, ob Russ, welchem ohne Wissen des Käufers 90 % Staub und\n Sand beigemischt sind, „wirklicher“ Russ im „commerciellen“ Sinn oder „ge-\n fälschter“ Russ im „gesetzlichen“ Sinn sei.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"8.92"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0093","text":"Die „amis du commerce“ entschie-\n den, es sei „wirklicher“ commercieller Russ, und wiesen den klagenden Pächter\n ab, der noch obendrein die Prozesskosten zu zahlen hatte.] .“ Das Comité selbst formulirte mehr oder minder naiv\n seine Ueberzeugung, dass Freihandel wesentlich den Handel mit gefälsch-\n ten, oder wie der Engländer es witzig nennt, „sophisticirten Stoffen“ be-\n deute. In der That, diese Art „Sophistik“ versteht es besser als\nProtagoras schwarz aus weiss und weiss aus schwarz zu machen, und\n besser als die Eleaten den blossen Schein alles Realen ad oculos zu\n demonstriren [Note: Der französische Chemiker Chevallier, in einer Abhandlung über die\n „sophistications“ der Waaren, zählt unter 600 und einigen Artikeln, die er Revue\n passiren lässt, für viele derselben 10, 20, 30 verschiedne Methoden der Fälschung\n auf.","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"8.93"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0094","text":"Er fügt hinzu, er kenne nicht alle Methoden und erwähne nicht alle, die er\n kenne. Für den Zucker giebt er 6 Fälschungsarten, 9 für das Olivenöl, 10 für\n die Butter, 12 für das Salz, 19 für die Milch, 20 für das Brod, 23 für den Brannt-\n wein, 24 für Mehl, 28 für Chokolade, 30 für Wein, 32 für Kaffee etc.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"8.94"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0095","text":"Jedenfalls hatte das Comité die Augen des Publikums auf sein „täg-\n liches Brod“ und damit auf die Bäckerei gelenkt. Gleichzeitig er-\n scholl in öffentlichen Meetings und Petitionen an das Parlament der Schrei\n der Londoner Bäckergesellen über Ueberarbeitung u. s. w. Der\n Schrei wurde so dringend, dass Herr H. S. Tremenheere, auch Mitglied der\n mehrerwähnten Kommission von 1863, zum königlichen Untersuchungs-\n kommissär bestallt wurde. Sein Bericht [Note: „Report etc. relating to the Grievances complained of by\n the Journeymen Bakers etc. London 1862“ und „Second Report\n etc. London 1863.“] , sammt Zeugenaussagen,\n regte das Publikum auf, nicht sein Herz, sondern seinen Magen.","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"8.95"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0096","text":"Der\n bibelfeste Engländer wusste zwar, dass der Mensch, wenn nicht durch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"219\", \"facs\": \"#f0238\"} -->Gnadenwahl Kapitalist oder Landlord oder Sinekurist, dazu be-\n rufen ist, sein Brod im Schweisse seines Angesichts zu essen, aber er\n wusste nicht, dass er in seinem Brode täglich ein gewisses Quantum\nMenschenschweiss essen muss, getränkt mit Eiterbeulenauslee-\n rung, Spinnweb, schwarzen Käfer-Leichnamen und fauler deutscher Hefe,\n abgesehn von Alaun, Sandstein und sonstigen angenehmen mineralischen\n Ingredienzen. Ohne alle Rücksicht auf Seine Heiligkeit, den „Free-\n trade“, wurde daher die anhero „freie“ Bäckerei der Aufsicht von Staats-\n inspektoren unterworfen (Ende der Parlamentssitzung 1863) und durch\n denselben Parlamentsakt die Arbeitszeit von 9 Uhr Abends bis 5 Uhr Mor-\n gens für Bäckergesellen unter 18 Jahren verboten.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"8.96"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0097","text":"Die letztere Klausel\n spricht Bände über die Ueberarbeitung in diesem uns so altväterisch an-\n heimelnden Geschäftszweig.","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"8.97"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0098","text":"„Die Arbeit eines Londoner Bäckergesellen beginnt in der Regel\n um 11 Uhr Nachts. Zu dieser Stunde macht er den Teig, ein sehr müh-\n samer Prozess, der ½ bis ¾ Stunden währt, je nach der Grösse des Ge-\n bäcks und seiner Feinheit. Er legt sich dann nieder auf das Kneetbrett,\n das zugleich als Deckel des Trogs dient, worin der Teig gemacht wird,\n und schläft ein paar Stunden mit einem Mehlsack unter dem Kopf und\n einem andern Mehlsack auf dem Leib. Dann beginnt eine rasche und un-\n unterbrochene Arbeit von 4 Stunden, Werfen, Wägen, Formen, in den\n Ofen schieben, aus dem Ofen holen u. s. w. des Teiges. Die Temperatur\n eines Backhauses beträgt von 75 bis 90 Grad und in den kleinen Back-\n häusern eher mehr als weniger. Wenn das Geschäft Brod, Wecken u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"8.98"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0099","text":"zu machen vollbracht ist, beginnt die Vertheilung des Brods; und ein be-\n trächtlicher Theil der Taglöhner, nachdem er die beschriebene harte Nacht-\n arbeit vollbracht, trägt während des Tags das Brod in Körben, oder\n schiebt es in Karren, von Haus zu Haus und operirt dazwischen auch manch-\n mal im Backhaus. Je nach der Jahreszeit und dem Umfang des Geschäfts\n endet die Arbeit zwischen 1 und 6 Uhr Nachmittags, während ein andrer\n Theil der Gesellen bis spät um Mitternacht im Backhaus beschäftigt ist [Note: l. c. First Report etc. p. XL.] “.\n „Während der Londoner Saison beginnen die Gesellen der Bäcker zu\n „vollen“ Brodpreisen im Westend regelmässig um 11 Uhr Nachts, und\n sind mit dem Brodbacken, unterbrochen durch einen oder zwei oft sehr\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"220\", \"facs\": \"#f0239\"} --> kurze Zwischenräume, bis 8 Uhr des nächsten Morgens beschäftigt.","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"8.99"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0100","text":"Sie\n werden dann bis 4, 5, 6, ja 7 Uhr zur Brodherumträgerei vernutzt oder\n manchmal mit Biscuitbacken im Backhaus. Nach vollbrachtem Werk ge-\n niessen sie einen Schlaf von 6, oft nur von 5 und 4 Stunden. Freitags\n beginnt die Arbeit stets früher, sage Abends 10 Uhr und dauert ohne Unter-\n lass, sei es in der Zubereitung, sei es in der Colportirung des Brods, bis\n den folgenden Samstag Abend 8 Uhr, aber meist bis 4 oder 5 Uhr in Sonn-\n tag Nacht hinein.","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"8.100"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0101","text":"Auch in den vornehmen Bäckereien, die das Brod zum\n „vollen Preise“ verkaufen, muss wieder 4—5 Stunden am Sonntag vorbe-\n reitende Arbeit für den nächsten Tag verrichtet werden … Die Bäcker-\n gesellen der „underselling masters“ (die das Brod unter dem\n vollen Preise verkaufen), und diese betragen, wie früher bemerkt, über ¾\n der Londoner Bäcker, haben noch längere Arbeitsstunden, aber ihre Arbeit\n ist fast ganz auf das Backhaus beschränkt, da ihre Meister, die Lieferung\n an kleine Kramläden ausgenommen, nur in der eignen Boutique verkaufen.\n Gegen „Ende“ der Woche, d. h. am Donnerstag beginnt hier die Arbeit\n um 10 Uhr in der Nacht und dauert bis tief in Sonntag Nacht hinein [Note: l. c. p. LXXI.] .“","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"8.101"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0102","text":"Von den „underselling masters“ begreift selbst der bürgerliche\n Standpunkt: „die unbezahlte Arbeit der Gesellen (the unpaid la-\n bour of the men) bildet die Grundlage ihrer Konkurrenz“ [Note: George Read: „The History of Baking. London 1848,“\n p. 16.] . Und der „full pri-\n ced baker“ denunzirt seine „underselling“ Konkurrenten der Untersuchungs-\n kommission als Diebe fremder Arbeit und Fälscher. „Sie reussiren nur\n durch den Betrug des Publikums und dadurch dass sie 18 Stunden aus\n ihren Gesellen für einen Lohn von 12 Stunden herausschlagen [Note: Report (First) etc. Evidence. Aussage des „full priced baker“\n Cheeseman p. 108. Dass der Lohn für 12 Stunden den vielleicht nur 6stündigen\n Werth der Arbeitskraft ausdrückt, und 6 Stunden daher „unbezahlt“ sind von den\n 12, ahnt der „full priced“ Mr. Cheeseman schwerlich.] .“","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"8.102"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0103","text":"Die Brodfälschung und die Bildung einer Bäckerklasse, die das Brod\nunter dem vollen Preise verkauft, entwickelten sich in England seit An-\n fang des 18. Jahrhunderts, sobald der Zunftcharakter des Gewerbs verfiel\n und der Kapitalist in der Gestalt von Müller oder Mehlfaktor hinter\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"221\", \"facs\": \"#f0240\"} --> den nominellen Bäckermeister trat [Note: George Read l. c. Ende des 17. und Anfangs des 18. Jahrhunderts\n wurden die in alle möglichen Gewerbe sich eindrängenden Factors (Agenten) noch\n officiell als „Public Nuisances“ denuncirt. So erliess z. B. die Grand Jury, bei\n der vierteljährigen Friedensrichtersitzung in der Grafschaft Somerset, ein „present-\n ment“ an das Unterhaus, worin es u. a.","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"8.103"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0104","text":"heisst: „that these factors of Plackwell\n Hall are a Publick Nuisance and Prejudice to the Clothing Trade, and ought to be\n put down as a Nuisance.“ („The Case of our English Wool etc. Lon-\n don 1685“, p. 6, 7.)] . Damit war die Grundlage zur\n kapitalistischen Produktion, zur masslosen Verlängerung des Arbeitstages\n und Nachtarbeit gelegt, obgleich letztere selbst in London erst 1824 ernst-\n haft Fuss fasste [Note: First Report etc. p. VIII.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"8.104"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0105","text":"Man wird nach dem Vorhergehenden verstehn, dass der Kommis-\n sionsbericht die Bäckergesellen zu den kurzlebigen Arbeitern zählt, die,\n nachdem sie der unter allen Theilen der Arbeiterklasse normalen Kinder-\n decimation glücklich entwischt sind, selten das 42. Lebensjahr erreichen.\n Nichts desto weniger ist das Bäckergewerb stets mit Kandidaten über-\n füllt. Die Zufuhrquellen dieser „Arbeitskräfte“ für London sind Schott-\n land, die westlichen Agrikulturdistrikte Englands und — Deutsch-\n land.","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"8.105"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0106","text":"In den Jahren 1858—1860 organisirten die Bäckergesellen in Ir-\n land auf ihre eigenen Kosten grosse Meetings zur Agitation gegen die\n Nachtarbeit und das Arbeiten an Sonntagen. Das Publikum, z. B. auf\n dem Maimeeting zu Dublin, 1860, ergriff, der zündenden Natur des Irlän-\n ders gemäss, überall lebhaft Partei für sie. Ausschliessliche Tagesarbeit\n wurde durch diese Bewegung in der That erfolgreich durchgesetzt zu\n Wexford, Kilkenny, Clonmel, Waterford u. s. w. „Zu Limerick, wo die\n Qualen der Lohngesellen bekanntermassen alles Mass überstiegen, schei-\n terte die Bewegung an der Opposition der Bäckermeister, namentlich der\n Bäcker-Müller. Das Beispiel Limerick’s führte zum Rückschritt in Ennis\n und Tipperary.","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"8.106"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0107","text":"Zu Cork, wo der öffentliche Unwille sich in der lebhaf-\n testen Form kundgab, vereitelten die Meister die Bewegung durch den\n Gebrauch ihrer Macht die Gesellen an die Luft zu setzen. Zu Dublin lei-\n steten die Meister den entschiedensten Widerstand und zwangen durch Ver-\n folgung der Gesellen, die an der Spitze der Agitation standen, den Rest\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"222\", \"facs\": \"#f0241\"} --> zum Nachgeben, zur Fügung in die Nacht- und Sonntagsarbeit [Note: „Report of Committee on the Baking Trade in Ireland\n for 1861.“] .“\n Die Kommission der in Irland bis an die Zähne gewaffneten englischen\n Regierung remonstrirt leichenbitterlich gegen die unerbittlichen Bäckermei-\n ster von Dublin, Limerick, Cork u. s. w.: „Das Comité glaubt, dass die Ar-\n beitsstunden durch Naturgesetze beschränkt sind, die nicht ungestraft\n verletzt werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"8.107"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0108","text":"Indem die Meister durch die Drohung sie fortzujagen,\n ihre Arbeiter zur Verletzung ihrer religiösen Ueberzeugung, zum Unge-\n horsam gegen das Landesgesetz und die Verachtung der öffentlichen Mei-\n nung zwingen,“ (diess letztere bezieht sich alles auf die Sonntagsarbeit),\n „setzen sie böses Blut zwischen Kapital und Arbeit und geben ein Bei-\n spiel, gefährlich für Religion, Moralität und öffentliche Ordnung … Das\n Comité glaubt, dass die Verlängerung des Arbeitstags über 12 Stunden\n ein usurpatorischer Eingriff in das häusliche und Privatleben des\n Arbeiters ist und so zu unheilvollen moralischen Resultaten führt, durch\nEinmischung in die Häuslichkeit eines Mannes und die\n Erfüllung seiner Familienpflichten als Sohn, Bruder, Gatte und\n Vater.","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"8.108"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0109","text":"Arbeit über 12 Stunden hat die Tendenz die Gesundheit des Ar-\n beiters zu untergraben, führt zu vorzeitiger Alterung und frühem\n Tod und daher zum Unglück der Arbeiterfamilien, die der Vorsorge und\n der Stütze des Familienhaupts grade im nothwendigsten Augenblick be-\n raubt werden“ („are deprived“) [Note: l. c.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"8.109"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0110","text":"Wir waren eben in Irland. Auf der andern Seite des Kanals, in\nSchottland, denuncirt der Ackerbauarbeiter, der Mann des\n Pfluges, seine 13—14stündige Arbeit, im rauhsten Klima, mit vierstün-\n diger Zusatzarbeit für den Sonntag, (in diesem Lande der Sabbat-Hei-\n ligen!) [Note: Oeffentliches Meeting der Agrikulturarbeiter in Lass-\n wade, bei Glasgow, vom 5. Jan. 1866. (Sieh „Workman’s Advocate“\n vom 13. Jan. 1866.) Die Bildung, seit Ende 1865, einer Trade’s Union unter\n den Agrikulturarbeitern, zunächst in Schottland, ist ein historisches Ereigniss.] , während vor einer Londoner Grand Jury gleichzeitig drei Eisen-\n bahnarbeiter stehn, ein Personencondukteur, ein Lokomotivenführer und\n ein Signalgeber. Ein grosses Eisenbahnunglück hat Hunderte von Passa-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"223\", \"facs\": \"#f0242\"} --> gieren in die andere Welt expedirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"8.110"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0111","text":"Die Nachlässigkeit der Eisen-\n bahnarbeiter ist die Ursache des Unglücks. Sie erklären vor den Ge-\n schworenen einstimmig, vor 10 bis 12 Jahren habe ihre Arbeit nur 8\n Stunden täglich gedauert. Während der letzten 5—6 Jahre habe man sie\n auf 14, 18 und 20 Stunden aufgeschraubt und bei besonders lebhaftem Zu-\n drang der Reiselustigen, wie in den Perioden der Excursion-trains, währe\n sie oft ununterbrochen 40—50 Stunden. Sie seien gewöhnliche Men-\n schen und keine Cyklopen. Auf einem gegebnen Punkt versage ihre Ar-\n beitskraft. Torpor ergreife sie. Ihr Hirn höre auf zu denken und ihr\n Auge zu sehn.","kind":"passage","sequence":111,"address":"8.111"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0112","text":"Der ganz und gar „respectable British Juryman“ antwortet\n durch ein Verdikt, das sie wegen „manslaughter“ (Todtschlag) vor die\n nächsten Assisen schickt, und in einem milden Anhang den frommen\n Wunsch äussert, die Herren Kapitalmagnaten der Eisenbahn möchten doch\n in Zukunft verschwenderischer im Ankauf der nöthigen Anzahl von „Ar-\n beitskräften“ und „enthaltsamer“ oder „entsagender“ oder\n „sparsamer“ in der Aussaugung der bezahlten Arbeitskraft sein [Note: „Reynold’s Paper“ vom 20. Jan. 1866. Woche für Woche bringt\n dasselbe Wochenblatt gleich darauf, unter den „sensational leadings: „Fearful\n and fatal accidents“ „Appalling tragedies“ u. s. w., eine ganze Liste neuer Eisen-\n bahnkatastrophen. Darauf antwortet ein Arbeiter von der North Staffordlinie:\n „Jedermann kennt die Folgen, wenn die Aufmerksamkeit von Lokomotivenführer\n und Heizer einen Augenblick erlahmt.","kind":"passage","sequence":112,"address":"8.112"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0113","text":"Und wie ist es anders möglich bei mass-] !","kind":"passage","sequence":113,"address":"8.113"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0114","text":"Aus dem buntscheckigen Haufen der Arbeiter von allen Professionen,\n Altern, Geschlechtern, die eifriger auf uns andrängen als die Seelen der\n Erschlagenen auf den Odysseus, und denen man, ohne die Blaubücher\n unter ihren Armen, auf den ersten Blick die Ueberarbeit ansieht, greifen\n wir noch zwei Figuren heraus, deren frappanter Kontrast beweist, dass\n vor dem Kapital alle Menschen gleich sind, — eine Putzmacherin\n und einen Grobschmidt.","kind":"passage","sequence":114,"address":"8.114"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0115","text":"In den letzten Wochen von Juni 1863 brachten alle Londoner\n Tagesblätter einen Paragraph mit dem „sensational“ Aushängeschild:\n „Death from simple Overwork“ (Tod von einfacher Ueber-\n arbeit). Es handelte sich um den Tod der Putzmacherin Mary Anne\n Walkley, zwanzigjährig, beschäftigt in einer sehr respektablen Hofputz-\n manufaktur, exploitirt von einer Dame mit dem gemüthlichen Namen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"224\", \"facs\": \"#f0243\"} -->Elise. Die alte oft erzählte Geschichte ward nun neuentdeckt [Note: Vgl. F. Engels l. c. p. 253, 254.] , dass\n diese Mädchen durchschnittlich 16½ Stunden, während der Saison aber\n oft 30 Stunden ununterbrochen arbeiten, indem ihre versagende „Arbeits-\n kraft“ durch gelegentliche Zufuhr von Sherry, Portwein oder Kaffee flüssig\n erhalten wird. Und es war grade die Höhe der Saison.","kind":"passage","sequence":115,"address":"8.115"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0116","text":"Es galt die\n Prachtkleider edler Ladies für den Huldigungsball bei der frisch importir-\n ten Prinzessin von Wales im Umsehn fertig zu zaubern. Mary Anne\n Walkley hatte 26½ Stunden ohne Unterlass gearbeitet zusammen mit 60\n andern Mädchen, je 30 in einem Zimmer, das kaum ⅓ der nöthigen\n Kubikzoll Luft gewährte, während sie Nachts zwei zu zwei Ein Bett theil-\n ten in einem der Sticklöcher, worin Ein Schlafzimmer durch verschiedne\n Bretterwände abgepfercht ist [Note: Dr. Letheby, beim Board of Health funktionirender Arzt, er-\n klärte damals: „Das Minimum für jeden Erwachsnen in einem Schlafzimmer\n sollte 300 Kubikfuss und in einem Wohnzimmer 500 Kubikfuss Luft sein.“\n Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":116,"address":"8.116"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0117","text":"Richardson, Oberarzt eines Londoner Hospitals: „Nätherinnen aller\n Art, Putzmacherinnen, Kleidermacherinnen und gewöhnliche Näherinnen, leiden\n an dreifachem Elend — Ueberarbeit, Luftmangel und Mangel an Nahrung oder\n Mangel an Verdauung. Im Ganzen passt diese Art Arbeit für Weiber, besser unter\n allen Umständen als für Männer. Aber das Unheil des Geschäfts ist, dass es,\n namentlich in der Hauptstadt, von einigen 26 Kapitalisten monopolisirt wird, die\n durch Machtmittel, welche dem Kapital entspringen, (that spring from capital)\nOekonomie aus der Arbeit herauszwingen, (force economy out of labour; er\n meint, Auslagen ökonomisiren durch Verschwendung der Arbeitskraft). Diese] . Und diess war eine der besseren Putz-\n [Note: loser Verlängerung der Arbeit, im rauhsten Wetter, ohne Pause und Erholung?\n Nehmt als ein Beispiel, wie es täglich vorkommt, folgenden Fall.","kind":"passage","sequence":117,"address":"8.117"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0118","text":"Letzten Montag\n begann ein Heizer sehr früh Morgens sein Tageswerk. Er endete es nach 14\n Stunden, 50 Minuten. Bevor er auch nur die Zeit hatte, seinen Thee zu nehmen,\n wurde er von neuem an die Arbeit gerufen. Er hatte also 29 Stunden, 15 Mi-\n nuten ununterbrochen durchzuschanzen. Der Rest seines Wochenwerks wurde\n aufgemacht wie folgt: Mittwoch, 15 Stunden; Donnerstag 15 Stunden, 35 Mi-\n nuten; Freitag 14½ Stunden; Sonnabend 14 Stunden, 10 Minuten; zusammen\n für die Woche 88 Stunden, 40 Minuten. Und nun denkt euch sein Erstaunen, als\n er nur Zahlung für 6 Arbeitstage erhielt. Der Mann war ein Neuling und fragte,\n was man unter einem Tagewerk verstehe. Antwort: 13 Stunden, also 78 Stunden\n per Woche. Aber wie mit der Zahlung für die überschüssigen 10 Stunden, 40\n Minuten? Nach langem Hader erhielt er eine Vergütung von 10 d. (noch nicht\n 10 Silbergroschen).“ l. c. Nr. vom 4.","kind":"passage","sequence":118,"address":"8.118"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0119","text":"Februar, 1866.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"225\", \"facs\": \"#f0244\"} --> machereien Londons. Mary Anne Walkley erkrankte am Freitag und\n starb am Sonntag, ohne, zum Erstaunen von Frau Elise, auch nur vorher\n das letzte Putzstück fertig zu machen. Der zu spät ans Sterbebett geru-\n fene Arzt, Herr Keys, bezeugte vor der Coroner’s Jury in dürren\n Worten: „Mary Anne Walkley sei gestorben an langen Arbeits-\n stunden in einem überfüllten Arbeitszimmer und überengem schlecht-\n ventilirten Schlafgemache.“ Um dem Arzt eine Lektion in guter Lebens-\n art zu geben, erklärte dagegen die „Coroner’s Jury“: „Die Hingeschie-\n dene sei gestorben an der Apoplexie, aber es sei Grund zu\nfürchten, dass ihr Tod durch Ueberarbeit in einer überfüllten Werk-\n statt u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":119,"address":"8.119"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0120","text":"beschleunigt worden sei.“ Unsere „weissen Skla-\n ven,“ rief der Morning Star, das Organ der Freihandelsherrn Cobden\n und Bright, „unsere weissen Sklaven werden in das Grab hin-\n eingearbeitet und verderben und sterben ohne Sang und Klang [Note: Morning Star, 23. Juni 1863. Die Times benutzte den Vorfall\n zur Vertheidigung der amerikanischen Sklavenhalter gegen Bright u. s. w. „Sehr\n viele von uns“, sagt sie, „meinen, dass so lange wir unsre eignen jungen Frauen-\n zimmer zu Tode arbeiten, mit der Geissel des Hungers statt dem Knall der Peitsche,\n wir kaum das Recht haben, Feuer und Schwert auf Familien zu hetzen, die als\n Sklavenhalter geboren waren und ihre Sklaven mindestens gut nähren und mässig\n arbeiten lassen.“ (Times, Juli 2, 1863.) In derselben Weise kanzelte der\nStandard, ein Toryblatt, den Rev.","kind":"passage","sequence":120,"address":"8.120"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0121","text":"Newman Hall ab: „er excommunicire die\n Sklavenhalter, bete aber mit den braven Leuten, die Kutscher und Omnibusführer\n von London u. s. w. nur 16 Stunden täglich für einen Hundelohn arbeiten liessen“.] “.","kind":"passage","sequence":121,"address":"8.121"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0122","text":"[Note: Macht wird im Bereich der ganzen Klasse von Arbeiterinnen gefühlt. Kann eine\n Kleidermacherin einen kleinen Kreis von Kunden gewinnen, so zwingt die Kon-\n kurrenz sie, sich zu Hause todt zu arbeiten, um ihn zu erhalten, und mit derselben\n Ueberarbeit muss sie nothwendig ihre Gehülfinnen heimsuchen. Misslingt ihr\n Geschäft oder kann sie sich nicht selbstständig etabliren, so wendet sie sich an ein\n Etablissement, wo die Arbeit nicht geringer, aber die Zahlung sicher ist. So ge-\n stellt wird sie eine reine Sklavin, hin und her geschleudert von jeder Flu-\n thung der Gesellschaft; bald zu Hause in einem kleinen Zimmer verhungernd,\n oder nahe so; dann wieder von 24 Stunden 15, 16, ja 18 Stunden beschäftigt in\n kaum erträglicher Luft, und mit einer Nahrung, die, selbst wenn gut, wegen Ab-\n wesenheit reiner Luft nicht verdaut werden kann.","kind":"passage","sequence":122,"address":"8.122"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0123","text":"Von diesen Opfern lebt die\n Schwindsucht, welche nichts als eine Luftkrankheit ist.“ (Dr. Richardson:\n „Death from simple Overwork“ in „Social Science Review“,\n Juliheft, 1863.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":123,"address":"8.123"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0124","text":"[Running matter: I. 15] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"226\", \"facs\": \"#f0245\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":124,"address":"8.124"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0125","text":"„Zu Tod arbeiten ist die Tagesordnung, nicht nur in der Werk-\n stätte der Putzmacherinnen, sondern in tausend Plätzen, ja an jedem Platz,\n wo das Geschäft im Zug ist … Lasst uns den Grobschmidt als Bei-\n spiel nehmen. Wenn man den Dichtern glauben darf, giebt es keinen so\n lebenskräftigen, lustigen Mann als den Grobschmidt. Er erhebt sich früh\n und schlägt Funken vor der Sonne; er isst und trinkt und schläft wie\n kein andrer Mensch. Rein physisch betrachtet, befindet er sich, bei mäs-\n siger Arbeit, in der That in einer der besten menschlichen Stellungen.\n Aber wir folgen ihm in die Stadt, und sehn die Arbeitslast, die auf den\n starken Mann gewälzt wird, und welchen Rang nimmt er ein in den Sterb-\n lichkeitslisten unsres Landes?","kind":"passage","sequence":125,"address":"8.125"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0126","text":"Zu Marylebone (einem der grössten Stadt-\n viertel Londons) sterben Grobschmidte in dem Verhältniss von 31 per\n 1000 jährlich, oder 11 über der Durchschnittssterblichkeit erwachsener\n Männer in England. Die Beschäftigung, eine fast instinktive Kunst der\n Menschheit, an und für sich tadellos, wird durch blosse Uebertreibung der\n Arbeit, der Zerstörer des Mannes. Er kann so viel Hammerschläge täg-\n lich schlagen, so viel Schritte gehn, so viel Athemzüge holen, so viel Werk\n verrichten, und durchschnittlich sage 50 Jahre leben. Man zwingt ihn\n so viel mehr Schläge zu schlagen, so viel mehr Schritte zu gehn, so viel\n öfter des Tags zu athmen, und alles zusammen seine Lebensaus-\n gabe täglich um ein Viertel zu vermehren. Er macht den\n Versuch, und das Resultat ist, dass er für eine beschränkte Periode ein\n Viertel mehr Werk verrichtet und im 37. Jahr statt im 50. stirbt [Note: Dr. Richardson.","kind":"passage","sequence":126,"address":"8.126"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0127","text":"l. c.] “.","kind":"passage","sequence":127,"address":"8.127"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0128","text":"Das konstante Kapital, die Produktionsmittel, sind, vom Standpunkt\n des Verwerthungsprozesses betrachtet, nur da, um Arbeit, und\n [Note: Endlich sprach das Orakel, Herr Thomas Carlyle, von dem ich schon\n 1850 drucken liess: „Zum Teufel ist der Genius, der Kultus ist geblieben.“\n In einer kurzen Parabel reducirt er das einzig grossartige Ereigniss der Zeitge-\n schichte, den amerikanischen Bürgerkrieg, darauf, dass der Peter vom Norden dem\n Paul vom Süden mit aller Gewalt den Hirnschädel einschlagen will, weil der Peter\n vom Norden seinen Arbeiter „täglich“ und der Paul vom Süden ihn für „Leb-\n zeit miethet.“ (Macmillian’s Magazine. Ilias Americana in\n nuce. Augustheft 1863.) So ist endlich die Schaumblase der Torysympathie für\n den städtischen, bei Leibe nicht den ländlichen! — Lohnarbeiter ge-\n platzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":128,"address":"8.128"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0129","text":"Der Kern heisst — Sklaverei!] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"227\", \"facs\": \"#f0246\"} --> mit jedem Tropfen Arbeit ein proportionelles Quantum Mehr-\n arbeit einzusaugen. So weit sie das nicht thun, bildet ihre blosse\n Existenz einen negativen Verlust für den Kapitalisten, denn sie\n repräsentiren während der Zeit, wo sie brach liegen, nutzlosen Kapital-\n vorschuss, und dieser Verlust wird positiv, sobald sie während der In-\n tervalle des Arbeitsprozesses zusätzliche Auslagen erheischen, um sie für\n den Wiederbeginn des Werks im Gang zu erhalten. Die Verlänge-\n rungdes Arbeitstags über die Grenzen des natürlichen\n Tags in die Nacht hinein wirkt nur als Palliativ, stillt nur an-\n nähernd den Vampyrdurst nach lebendigem Arbeitsblut. Arbeit während\naller 24 Stunden des Tags anzueignen, ist daher der immanente Trieb\n der kapitalistischen Produktion.","kind":"passage","sequence":129,"address":"8.129"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0130","text":"Da diess aber physisch unmöglich, wür-\n den dieselben Arbeitskräfte Tag und Nacht fortwährend ausgesaugt,\n so bedarf es, zur Ueberwindung des physischen Hindernisses, der Ab-\n wechslung zwischen den bei Tag und bei Nacht verspeisten Arbeits-\n kräften, eine Abwechslung, die verschiedne Methoden zulässt, z. B. so ge-\n ordnet sein kann, dass ein Theil des Arbeiterpersonals eine Woche Tagdienst,\n Nachtdienst die andre Woche versieht u. s. w. Man weiss, dass dieses Ablö-\n sungssystem, diese Wechselwirthschaft, in der vollblütigen Jugend-\n periode der englischen Baumwollindustrie u. s. w. vorherrschte, und u. a.\n gegenwärtig in den Baumwollspinnereien von Moskau blüht. Als Sy-\n stem existirt dieser 24stündige Produktionsprozess heute noch in vielen\n bis jetzt „freien“ Industriezweigen Grossbritaniens, u. a.","kind":"passage","sequence":130,"address":"8.130"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0131","text":"in den Hoch-\n öfen, Schmieden, Walzwerken und andern Metallmanufakturen von Eng-\n land, Wales und Schottland. Der Arbeitsprozess umfasst hier ausser den\n 24 Stunden der 6 Werkeltage grossentheils auch die 24 Stunden des\n Sonntags. Die Arbeiter bestehn aus Männern und Weibern, Erwachsenen\n und Kindern beiderlei Geschlechts. Das Alter der Kinder und jungen Per-\n sonen durchläuft alle Zwischenstufen vom 8. (in einigen Fällen vom 6.)\n bis zum 18. Jahr [Note: „Children’s Employment Commission.“ Third Report.\n Lond. 1864, p. IV, V, VII.] . In einigen Branchen arbeiten auch die Mädchen\n und Weiber des Nachts zusammen mit dem männlichen Personal [Note: „Both in Staffordshire and in South Wales young girls and women are\n employed on the pit banks and on the coke heaps, not only by day, but also by] .","kind":"passage","sequence":131,"address":"8.131"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0132","text":"[Running matter: 15*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"228\", \"facs\": \"#f0247\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":132,"address":"8.132"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0133","text":"Von den allgemeinen schädlichen Wirkungen der Nachtarbeit abge-\n sehn [Note: „Es scheint natürlich,“ bemerkte ein Stahlfabrikant, der Kinder zur\n Nachtarbeit verwendet, „dass die Jungen, die Nachts arbeiten, bei Tag nicht\n schlafen und keine ordentliche Ruhe finden können, sondern rastlos am nächsten\n Tag herumlaufen.“ l. c. Fourth Rep. 63, p. XIII. Ueber die Wichtigkeit des\nSonnenlichts zur Erhaltung und Entwicklung des Körpers bemerkt ein Arzt\n u. a.: „Licht wirkt auch direkt auf die Gewebe des Leibes, denen es Härte und\n Elasticität giebt.","kind":"passage","sequence":133,"address":"8.133"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0134","text":"Die Muskeln von Thieren, denen man das normale Quantum\n Licht vorenthält, werden schwammig und unelastisch, die Nervenkraft verliert\n ihren Ton durch Mangel an Stimulirung, und die Ausarbeitung von allem was im\n Wachsthum begriffen ist, wird verkümmert … Im Fall von Kindern ist beständiger\n Zutritt von reichlichem Tageslicht und der direkten Sonnenstrahlen während eines\n Theils des Tags durchaus wesentlich für die Gesundheit. Licht hilft die Speisen\n zu gutem plastischem Blut verarbeiten und härtet die Fiber, nachdem sie gebildet\n ist. Es wirkt ebenso als Reizmittel auf die Sehorgane und ruft hierdurch grössere\n Thätigkeit in verschiednen Hirnfunktionen hervor.“ Herr W.","kind":"passage","sequence":134,"address":"8.134"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0135","text":"Strange, Oberarzt\n des Worcester „General Hospital“, aus dessen Schrift über „Gesundheit“\n (1864) diese Stelle entlehnt ist, schreibt in einem Brief an einen der Untersuchungs-\n kommissaire, Herrn White: „Ich habe früher in Lancashire Gelegenheit gehabt,\n die Wirkungen der Nachtarbeit auf Fabrikkinder zu beobachten, und im Wider-\n spruch zu der beliebten Versicherung einiger Arbeitsgeber, er-\n kläre ich mit Entschiedenheit, dass die Gesundheit der Kinder schwer davon\n leidet.“ (l. c. 284, p. 55.) Dass solche Dinge überhaupt den Gegenstand ernst-\n hafter Kontroversen bilden, zeigt am besten, wie die kapitalistische Produktion\n auf die „Gehirnfunktionen“ der Kapitalisten und ihrer retainers wirkt.] , bietet die ununterbrochene, vierundzwanzigstündige Dauer des\n Produktionsprozesses höchst willkommene Gelegenheit die Grenze des nomi-\n nellen Arbeitstags zu überschreiten. Z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":135,"address":"8.135"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0136","text":"in den vorhin erwähnten,\n sehr anstrengenden Industriezweigen beträgt der officielle Arbeitstag für\n jeden Arbeiter meist 12 Stunden, Nachtstunden oder Tagesstunden.\n Aber die Ueberarbeit über diese Grenze hinaus ist in vielen Fällen, um\n die Worte des englischen officiellen Berichts zu brauchen, „wirklich\n [Note: night. This practice has been often noticed in Reports presented to Parliament,\n as being attended with great and notorious evils. These females, employed with\n the men, hardly distinguished from them in their dress, and begrimed with dirt\n and smoke, are exposed to the deterioration of character arising from the loss of\n self-respect which can hardly fail to follow from their unfeminine occupation.“\n l. c. 194, p. XXVI. Vgl. Fourth Report (1865) 61, p. XIII. Ebenso in\nGlasfabriken.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"229\", \"facs\": \"#f0248\"} -->schauderhaft“ („truly fearful“) [Note: l.","kind":"passage","sequence":136,"address":"8.136"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0137","text":"c. 57, p. XII.] . „Kein menschliches Gemüth,“\n heisst es, „kann die Arbeitmasse, die nach den Zeugenaussagen durch\n Knaben von 9 bis 12 Jahren verrichtet wird, überdenken, ohne unwider-\n stehlich zum Schlusse zu kommen, dass dieser Machtmissbrauch der\n Eltern und Arbeitgeber nicht länger erlaubt werden darf [Note: l. c. (4th Rep. 1865) 58, p. XII.] .“","kind":"passage","sequence":137,"address":"8.137"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0138","text":"„Die Methode Knaben überhaupt abwechselnd Tag und Nacht arbei-\n ten zu lassen, führt, sowohl während des Geschäftsdrangs als während\n des gewöhnlichen Verlaufs der Dinge, zu schmählicher Verlängerung des\n Arbeitstags. Diese Verlängerung ist in vielen Fällen nicht nurgrau-\n sam, sondern gradezu unglaublich. Es kann nicht fehlen, dass\n aus einer oder der andern Ursache ein Ablösungsknabe hier und da weg-\n bleibt. Einer oder mehrere der anwesenden Knaben, die ihren Arbeitstag\n bereits vollbracht, müssen dann den Ausfall gut machen. Diess System\n ist so allgemein bekannt, dass der manager eines Walzwerks, auf meine\n Frage, wie die Stelle der abwesenden Ersatzknaben ausgefüllt würde, ant-\n wortete: Ich weiss wohl, dass Sie das eben so gut wissen als ich, und er\n nahm keinen Anstand die Thatsache zu gestehn [Note: l. c.] .“","kind":"passage","sequence":138,"address":"8.138"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0139","text":"„In einem Walzwerke, wo der nominelle Arbeitstag für den einzelnen\n Arbeiter 11½ Stunden war, arbeitete ein Junge 4 Nächte jede Woche\n bis mindestens 8½ Uhr Abends des nächsten Tags und diess während der\n 6 Monate, wo er engagirt war.“ „Ein Andrer arbeitete im Alter von\n 9 Jahren manchmal drei zwölfstündige Ablösungstermine\n nacheinander, und im Alter von 10 Jahren, zwei Tage und zwei\n Nächte nach einander.“ „Ein Dritter, jetzt 10 Jahre, arbeitete\n von Morgens 6 Uhr bis 12 Uhr in die Nacht während drei Nächten und\n bis 9 Uhr Abends während der andren Nächte.“ „Ein Vierter, jetzt 13\n Jahre, arbeitete von 6 Uhr Nachmittags bis den andern Tag 12 Uhr Mit-\n tags, während einer ganzen Woche, und manchmal drei Ablösungstermine\n nach einander, von Montag Morgen bis Dienstag Nacht.“ „Ein Fünfter,\n jetzt 12 Jahre, arbeitete in einer Eisengiesserei zu Stavely von 6 Uhr\n Morgens bis 12 Uhr Nachts","kind":"passage","sequence":139,"address":"8.139"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0140","text":"während 14 Tagen, ist unfähig es länger zu\n thun.“ „George Allinsworth, neunjährig: „Ich kam hierhin\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"230\", \"facs\": \"#f0249\"} --> letzten Freitag. Nächsten Tag hatten wir um 3 Uhr Morgens anzufangen.\n Ich blieb daher die ganze Nacht hier. Wohne 5 Meilen von hier. Schlief\n auf der Flur mit einem Schurzfell unter mir und einer kleinen Jacke über\n mir. Die zwei andern Tage war ich hier um 6 Uhr Morgens. Ja! diess\n ist ein heisser Platz! Bevor ich herkam, arbeitete ich ebenfalls\nwährend eines ganzen Jahres in einem Hochofen. Es war ein\n sehr grosses Werk, auf dem Lande. Begann auch Samstag Morgens um\n 3 Uhr, aber ich konnte wenigstens nach Hause schlafen gehn, weil es nah\n war. An andern Tagen fing ich 6 Uhr Morgens an und endete 6 oder\n 7 Uhr Abends u. s. w. [Note: l. c. p. XIII.","kind":"passage","sequence":140,"address":"8.140"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0141","text":"Die Bildungsstufe dieser „Arbeitskräfte“ muss natürlich\n so sein, wie sie in folgenden Dialogen mit einem der Untersuchungskommissäre\n erscheint! „Jeremias Haynes, 12 Jahre alt: ‥ Viermal vier ist acht, aber\n vier Vierer (4 fours) sind 16 … Ein König ist ihm der alles Geld und Gold\n hat. (A king is him that has all the money and gold.) Wir haben einen König,\n man sagt, er ist eine Königin, sie nennen sie Prinzessin Alexandra. Man sagt,\n sie heirathete der Königin Sohn. Eine Prinzessin ist ein Mann.“ Wm. Turner,\n zwölfjährig: „Lebe nicht in England.","kind":"passage","sequence":141,"address":"8.141"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0142","text":"Denke, es giebt solch ein Land, wusste\n nichts davon zuvor.“ John Morris, 14jährig: „Habe sagen hören, dass Gott\n die Welt gemacht und dass alles Volk ersoff, ausser einem; habe gehört, dass\n einer ein kleiner Vogel war.“ William Smith, fünfzehnjährig: „Gott machte\n den Mann; der Mann machte das Weib.“ Edward Taylor, fünfzehnjährig:\n „Weiss nichts von London.“ Henry Matthewman, siebzehnjährig: „Geh’\n manchmal in die Kirche … Ein Name, worüber sie predigten, war ein gewisser\n Jesus Christ, aber ich kann keine andren Namen nennen, und ich kann auch nichts\n über ihn sagen. Er wurde nicht gemordet, sondern starb wie andre Leute. Er\n war nicht so wie andre Leute in gewisser Art, weil er religiös war in gewisser Art,\n und andere ist es nicht.“ (He was not the same as other people in some ways,\n because he was religious in some ways, and others is n’t.“ (l. c. 74, p. XV.)","kind":"passage","sequence":142,"address":"8.142"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0143","text":"„Der\n Teufel ist eine gute Person. Ich weiss nicht, wo er lebt. Christus war ein schlechter\n Kerl.“ („The devil is a good person. I don’t know where he lives.“ „Christ\n was a wicked man.“) „Diess Mädchen (10 Jahre) buchstabirt God Dog und\n kannte den Namen der Königin nicht.“ („Ch. Empl. Comm. V. Rep. 1866,\n p. 55, n. 278.) Dasselbe System, das in den erwähnten Metallmanufakturen,\n herrscht in den Glas- und Papierfabriken. In den Papierfabriken, wo\n das Papier mit Maschinen gemacht wird, ist Nachtarbeit die Regel für alle\n Prozesse ausser dem der Lumpensortirung. In einigen Fällen wird die Nacht-\n arbeit, vermittelst Ablösungen, unaufhörlich die ganze Woche durch fortgesetzt,\n gewöhnlich von Sonntag Nacht bis 12 Uhr Nacht des folgenden Samstags. Die\n Mannschaft, die sich an der Tagesreihe befindet, arbeitet 5 Tage von 12 und einen] .“","kind":"passage","sequence":143,"address":"8.143"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0144","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"231\", \"facs\": \"#f0250\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":144,"address":"8.144"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0145","text":"Lasst uns nun hören, wie das Kapital selbst diess Vierundzwanzig-\n Stundensystem auffasst. Die Uebertreibungen des Systems, seinen Missbrauch\n zur „grausamen und unglaublichen“ Verlängerung des Arbeitstags, über-\n geht es natürlich mit Stillschweigen. Es spricht nur von dem System in\n seiner „normalen“ Form.","kind":"passage","sequence":145,"address":"8.145"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0146","text":"„Die Herren Naylor und Vickers, Stahlfabrikanten, die zwischen\n 600 und 700 Personen anwenden, und darunter nur 10 % unter 18 Jah-\n ren, und hiervon wieder nur 20 Knaben zum Nachtpersonal, äussern sich\n wie folgt: „Die Knaben leiden durchaus nicht von der Hitze. Die Tem-\n peratur ist wahrscheinlich 86° bis 90°. . . . . In den Hammer- und\n Walzwerken arbeiten die Hände Tag und Nacht ablösungsweise, aber\n dahingegen ist auch alles andre Werk Tagwerk, von 6 Uhr Morgens bis\n 6 Uhr Abends. In der Schmiede wird von 12 Uhr bis 12 Uhr gearbeitet.\nEinige Hände arbeiten fortwährend des Nachts ohne\n Wechsel zwischen Tag- und Nachtzeit . . . . Wir finden nicht,\n dass Tag- oder Nachtarbeit irgend einen Unterschied in der Gesundheit\n (der Herren Naylor und Vickers?)","kind":"passage","sequence":146,"address":"8.146"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0147","text":"macht, und wahrscheinlich schla-\n [Note: von 18 Stunden, und die der Nachtreihe 5 Nächte von 12 Stunden und eine von 6\n Stunden, in jeder Woche. In andern Fällen arbeitet jede Reihe 24 Stunden, die\n eine nach der andern, an Wechseltagen. Eine Reihe arbeitet 6 Stunden am Mon-\n tag, und 18 am Samstag, um 24 Stunden vollzumachen. In andern Fällen ist ein\n Zwischensystem eingeführt, worin alle an der Papiermacher-Maschinerie Ange-\n stellten jeden Tag in der Woche 15—16 Stunden arbeiten. Diess System, sagt\n Untersuchungskommissär Lord, scheint alle Uebel der Zwölfstunden- und der\n Vierundzwanzigstunden-Ablösung zu vereinigen. Kinder unter 13 Jahren, junge\n Personen unter 18 und Weiber arbeiten unter diesem Nachtsystem. Manchmal,\n in dem Zwölfstundensystem, mussten sie, wegen Ausbleiben der Ablöser, die\n doppelte Reihe von 24 Stunden arbeiten.","kind":"passage","sequence":147,"address":"8.147"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0148","text":"Zeugenaussagen beweisen, dass\n Knaben und Mädchen sehr oft Ueberzeit arbeiten, die sich nicht selten zu 24,\n ja 36 Stunden ununterbrochner Arbeit ausdehnt. In dem „kontinuirlichen und\n unveränderlichen“ Prozess der Glasirräume findet man Mädchen von 12 Jahren,\n die den ganzen Monat durch täglich 14 Stunden arbeiten, „ohne irgend eine regel-\n mässige Erholung oder Unterbrechung ausser zwei, höchstens drei halbstündigen\n Ausfällen für Mahlzeiten.“ In einigen Fabriken, wo man die reguläre Nacht-\n arbeit ganz aufgegeben, wird entsetzlich viel Ueberzeit gearbeitet und „diess häufig\n in den schmutzigsten, heissesten und monotonsten Prozessen.“ („Children’s\n Employment Commission. Report IV, 1865,“ p. XXXVIII und\n XXXIX.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"232\", \"facs\": \"#f0251\"} --> fen Leute besser, wenn sie dieselbe Ruheperiode geniessen, als wenn\n sie wechselt . . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":148,"address":"8.148"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0149","text":"Ungefähr zwanzig Knaben unter 18 Jahren arbeiten\n mit der Nachtmannschaft . . . . Wir könntens nicht recht thun (not well do)\n ohne die Nachtarbeit von Jungen unter 18 Jahren. Unser Einwurf ist —\ndie Vermehrung der Produktionskosten . . . . Geschickte\n Hände und Häupter von Departements sind schwer zu haben, aber Jungens\n kriegt man so viel man will . . . . Natürlich, Anbetrachts der geringen\n Proportion von Jungen, die wir verwenden, wären Beschränkungen\n der Nachtarbeit von wenig Wichtigkeit oder Interesse für\n uns [Note: Fourth Report etc. 1865, 79, p. XVI.] .“","kind":"passage","sequence":149,"address":"8.149"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0150","text":"„Herr J. Ellis, von der Firma der Herren John Brown et Co.,\n Stahl- und Eisenwerke, die 3000 Männer und Jungen anwenden, und zwar\n für Theil der schweren Stahl- und Eisenarbeit „Tag und Nacht, in Ablö-\n sungen“, erklärt, dass in den schweren Stahlwerken einer oder zwei Jungen\n auf zwei Männer kommen. Ihr Geschäft zählt 500 Jungen unter 18 Jahren\n und davon ungefähr ⅓, oder 170, unter 13 Jahren. Mit Bezug auf\n die vorgeschlagne Gesetzänderung meint Herr Ellis: „Ich glaube nicht,\n dass es sehr tadelhaft (very objectionable) wäre, keine Person unter\n 18 Jahren über 12 Stunden aus den 24 arbeiten zu lassen. Aber ich\n glaube nicht, dass man irgend eine Linie ziehen kann für die Entbehr-\n lichkeit von Jungen über 12 Jahren für die Nachtarbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":150,"address":"8.150"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0151","text":"Wir würden\nsogar eher ein Gesetz annehmen, keine Jungen unter 13 Jahren oder\nselbst so hoch wie 14 Jahre, überhaupt zu verwenden, als ein Ver-\n bot die Jungen, die wir einmal haben, während der Nacht zu brauchen.\n Die Jungen, die in der Tagesreihe, müssen wechselweis auch in der Nachtreihe\n arbeiten, weil die Männer nicht unaufhörlich Nachtarbeit verrichten kön-\n nen; es würde ihre Gesundheit ruiniren. Wir glauben jedoch, dass Nacht-\n arbeit, wenn die Woche dafür wechselt, keinen Schaden thut. (Die Her-\n ren Naylor und Vickers glaubten, übereinstimmend mit dem Besten\n ihres Geschäfts, umgekehrt, dass statt der fortwährenden, grade die perio-\n disch wechselnde Nachtarbeit möglicher Weise Schaden anrichten kann.)\n Wir finden die Leute, die die alternirende Nachtarbeit verrichten, grade\n so gesund als die, die nur am Tage arbeiten . . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":151,"address":"8.151"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0152","text":"Unsere Einwürfe\n gegen die Nichtanwendung von Jungen unter 18 Jahren zur Nachtarbeit\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"233\", \"facs\": \"#f0252\"} --> würden gemacht werden von wegen Vermehrung der Auslage, aber diess\n ist auch der einzige Grund. (Wie cynisch naiv!) Wir glauben, dass diese\n Vermehrung grösser wäre, als das Geschäft (the trade) mit schuldiger\n Rücksicht auf seine erfolgreiche Ausführung billiger Weise tragen könnte.\n (As the trade with dueregard to etc. could fairly bear! Welche brei-\n mäulige Phraseologie!) Arbeit ist hier rar, und könnte unzureichend wer-\n den unter einer solchen Regulation“ (d. h. Ellis, Brown et Co. könnten\n in die fatale Verlegenheit kommen den Werth der Arbeitskraft voll\n zahlen zu müssen) [Note: l. c. 80.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":152,"address":"8.152"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0153","text":"Die „Cyklops Stahl- und Eisenwerke“ der Herren Cammell et Co.\n werden auf derselben grossen Stufenleiter ausgeführt, wie die des besag-\n ten John Brown et Co. Der geschäftsführende Direktor hatte dem Regie-\n rungskommissär White seine Zeugenaussage schriftlich eingehändigt, fand\n es aber später passend, das zur Revision ihm wieder zurückgestellte Manu-\n script zu unterschlagen. Jedoch Herr White hat ein nachhaltiges Gedächt-\n niss. Er erinnert sich ganz genau, dass für diese Herrn Cyklopen das\n Verbot der Nachtarbeit von Kindern und jungen Personen „ein Ding der\n Unmöglichkeit; es wäre dasselbe, als setzte man ihre Werke still“, und\n dennoch zählt ihr Geschäft wenig mehr als 6 % Jungen unter 18 und nur\n 1 % unter 13 Jahren [Note: l. c. 82.] !","kind":"passage","sequence":153,"address":"8.153"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0154","text":"Ueber denselben Gegenstand erklärt Herr E. F. Sanderson, von\n der Firma Sanderson, Bros. et Co., Stahl-Walz- und Schmiedewerke, in\n Attercliffe: „Grosse Schwierigkeiten würden entspringen aus dem Verbot\n Jungen unter 18 Jahren des Nachts arbeiten zu lassen, die Hauptschwie-\n rigkeit aus der Vermehrung der Kosten, welche ein Ersatz der Knaben-\n arbeit durch Männerarbeit nothwendig nach sich zöge. Wie viel das\n betragen würde, kann ich nicht sagen, aber wahrscheinlich wäre es nicht\n so viel, dass der Fabrikant den Stahlpreis erhöhen könnte, und folglich\n fiele der Verlust auf ihn, da die Männer (welch querköpfig Volk!) na-\n türlich weigern würden, ihn zu tragen.“ Herr Sanderson weiss nicht,\n wie viel er den Kindern zahlt, aber „vielleicht beträgt es 4 bis 5 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":154,"address":"8.154"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0155","text":"per\n Kopf die Woche … Die Knabenarbeit ist von einer Art, wofür im All-\n gemeinen („generally,“ natürlich nicht immer „im Besondern“) die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"234\", \"facs\": \"#f0253\"} --> Kraft der Jungens grade ausreicht, und folglich würde kein\n Gewinn aus der grössern Kraft der Männer fliessen, um\n den Verlust zu kompensire, oder doch nur in den wenigen Fäl-\n len, wo das Metall sehr schwer ist. Die Männer würden es auch minder\n lieben keine Knaben unter sich zu haben, da Männer minder gehorsam\n sind. Ausserdem müssen die Jungen jung anfangen, um das Geschäft zu\n lernen. Die Beschränkung der Jungen auf blosse Tagesarbeit würde die-\n sen Zweck nicht erfüllen.“ Und warum nicht? Warum können Jungen\n ihr Handwerk nicht bei Tag lernen? Deinen Grund?","kind":"passage","sequence":155,"address":"8.155"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0156","text":"„Weil dadurch die\n Männer, die in Wechselwochen bald den Tag, bald die Nacht arbeiten,\n von den Jungen ihrer Reihe während derselben Zeit getrennt, halb den\n Profit verlieren würden, den sie aus ihnen herausschlagen. Die Anleitung,\n die sie den Jungen geben, wird nämlich als Theil des Arbeitslohnes dieser\n Jungen berechnet und befähigt die Männer daher die Jungenarbeit wohl-\n feiler zu bekommen. Jeder Mann würde seinen halben Profit verlieren.\n (In andern Worten, die Herren Sanderson müssten einen Theil des Arbeits-\n lohnes der erwachsenen Männer aus eigner Tasche, statt mit der Nachtarbeit\n der Jungen zahlen.","kind":"passage","sequence":156,"address":"8.156"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0157","text":"Der Profit der Herren Sanderson würde bei\n dieser Gelegenheit etwas fallen und diess ist der Sanderson’sche\n gute Grund, warum Jungen ihr Handwerk nicht bei Tag lernen\n können [Note: „In unserer reflexionsreichen und raisonnirenden Zeit muss es Einer noch\n nicht weit gebracht haben, der nicht für Alles, auch das Schlechteste und Ver-\n kehrteste, einen guten Grund anzugeben weiss. Alles, was in der Welt ver-\n dorben worden ist, das ist aus guten Gründen verdorben worden.“ (Hegel\n l. c. p. 249.)] .) Ausserdem würde diess reguläre Nachtarbeit auf die\n Männer werfen, die nun von den Jungen abgelöst werden, und sie würden\n das nicht aushalten. Kurz und gut, die Schwierigkeiten wären\n so gross, dass sie wahrscheinlich zur gänzlichen Unterdrückung\n der Nachtarbeit führen würden.“ „Was die Produktion von Stahl\n selbst angeht,“ sagt E. F.","kind":"passage","sequence":157,"address":"8.157"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0158","text":"Sanderson, „würde es nicht den geringsten\n Unterschied machen, aber!“ Aber die Herren Sanderson haben mehr\n zu thun als Stahl zu machen. Die Stahlmacherei ist blosser Vorwand der\n Plusmacherei. Die Schmelzöfen, Walzwerke u.s. w., die Baulichkeiten, die\n Maschinerie, das Eisen, die Kohle u. s. w. haben mehr zu thun als sich\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"235\", \"facs\": \"#f0254\"} --> in Stahl zu verwandeln. Sie sind da, um Mehrarbeit einzusaugen und\n saugen natürlich mehr davon in 24 Stunden als in 12 Stunden ein. Sie\n geben in der That von Gottes und Rechtswegen den Sandersons eine An-\n weisung auf die Arbeitszeit einer gewissen Anzahl von Händen für volle\n 24 Stunden des Tags, und verlieren ihren Kapitalcharakter, sind\n daher reiner Verlust für die Sandersons, sobald ihre Funktion der\n Arbeitseinsaugung unterbrochen wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":158,"address":"8.158"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0159","text":"„Aber dann wäre da der Ver-\n lust so viel kostspieliger Maschinerie, welche die halbe Zeit brach läge,\n und für eine solche Produktenmasse, wie wir fähig sind, sie bei dem\ngegenwärtigen System zu leisten, müssten wir Räumlichkeiten und\n Maschinenwerke verdoppeln, was die Auslage verdoppeln würde.“ Aber\n warum sollen grade diese Sandersons ein Privilegium der Arbeitsausbeutung\n vor den andern Kapitalisten geniessen, die nur bei Tag arbeiten lassen\n dürfen und deren Baulichkeiten, Maschinerie, Rohmaterial daher bei Nacht\n „brach“ liegen? „Es ist wahr“, antwortet E. F. Sanderson im Namen\n aller Sandersons, „es ist wahr, dass dieser Verlust von brachliegender\n Maschinerie alle Manufakturen trifft, worin nur bei Tag gearbeitet wird.\nAber der Gebrauch der Schmelzöfen würde in unserem Fall einen Extra-\n verlust verursachen.","kind":"passage","sequence":159,"address":"8.159"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0160","text":"Hält man sie im Gang, so wird Brennmaterial ver-\n wüstet, (statt dass jetzt das Lebensmaterial der Arbeiter verwüstet wird)\n und hält man sie nicht im Gang, so setzt das Zeitverlust im Wiederan-\n legen des Feuers und zur Gewinnung des nöthigen Hitzegrads (während\n der Verlust, selbst Achtjähriger, an Schlafzeit Gewinn von Arbeitszeit für\n die Sandersonsippe) und die Oefen selbst würden vom Temperaturwechsel\n leiden“ (während doch dieselbigen Oefen nichts leiden vom Tag- und\n Nachtwechsel der Arbeit) [Note: l. c. p. 85.","kind":"passage","sequence":160,"address":"8.160"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0161","text":"Auf ähnliches zartes Bedenken der Herrn Glasfabri-\n kanten, dass „regelmässige Mahlzeiten“ der Kinder „unmöglich“\n sind, weil dadurch ein bestimmtes „Quantum Hitze“, das die Oefen aus-\n strahlen, „reiner Verlust“ für sie wäre oder „verwüstet“ würde, antwortet\n Untersuchungskommissär White, durchaus nicht gerührt, gleich Ure, Senior\n etc., und ihren schmalen deutschen Nachkläffern, wie Roscher etc., von der\n „Enthaltsamkeit,“ „Entsagung“ und „Sparsamkeit“ der Kapitalisten in Veraus-\n gabung ihres Geldes, und ihrer Timur-Tamerlanschen „Verschwendung“ von\nLeben andrer Menschen, wie folgt: „Ein gewisses Quantum Hitze\n mag über das jetzige Mass hinaus verwüstet werden, in Folge von Sicherung] .","kind":"passage","sequence":161,"address":"8.161"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0162","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"236\", \"facs\": \"#f0255\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":162,"address":"8.162"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0163","text":"„Was ist ein Arbeitstag?“ Wie gross ist die Zeit, während deren\n das Kapital die Arbeitskraft, deren Tageswerth es zahlt, konsumiren darf?\n Wie weit kann der Arbeitstag verlängert werden über die zur Repro-\n duktion der Arbeitskraft selbst nothwendige Arbeitszeit? Auf diese Fragen,\n man hat es gesehn, antwortet das Kapital: der Arbeitstag zählt täg-\n lich volle 24 Stunden nach Abzug der wenigen Ruhestunden, ohne\n welche die Arbeitskraft ihren erneuerten Dienst absolut versagt. Es ver-\n steht sich zunächst von selbst, dass der Arbeiter seinen ganzen Lebenstag\n durch nichts ist ausser Arbeitskraft, dass daher alle seine dis-\n ponible Zeit von Natur und Rechtswegen Arbeitszeit ist, also der\nSelbstverwerthung des Kapitals angehört.","kind":"passage","sequence":163,"address":"8.163"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0164","text":"Zeit zu menschlicher\n Bildung, zu geistiger Entwicklung, zur Erfüllung sozialer Funktionen, zu\n [Note: regulärer Mahlzeiten, aber selbst in Geldwerth ist es nichts verglichen\n mit der Verwüstung von Lebenskraft („the waste of animal power“),\n die jetzt dem Königreich daraus erwächst, dass in den Glashütten beschäftigte und\n im Wachsthum begriffene Kinder keine Ruhezeit finden, ihre Speisen bequem ein-\n zunehmen und zu verdauen.“ (l. c. p. XLV.) Und das im „Fortschrittsjahr“\n 1865! Abgesehn von der Kraftausgabe im Heben und Tragen, marschirt ein\n solches Kind in den Hütten, die Flaschen und Flintglas machen, während der\n kontinuirlichen Verrichtung seiner Arbeit, 15 bis 20 (englische) Meilen in 6\n Stunden! Und die Arbeit dauert oft 14 bis 15 Stunden! In vielen dieser Glas-\n hütten herrscht, wie in den Spinnereien von Moskau, das System sechsstündi-\n ger Ablösungen.","kind":"passage","sequence":164,"address":"8.164"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0165","text":"„Während der Arbeitszeit der Woche sind sechs Stunden\n die äusserste ununterbrochne Rastperiode, und davon geht ab die Zeit zur und\n von der Fabrik zu gehn, Waschen, Kleiden, Speisen, was alles Zeit kostet. So\n bleibt in der That nur die kürzeste Ruhezeit. Keine Zeit für Spiel und frische\n Luft, ausser auf Kosten des Schlafes, so unentbehrlich für Kinder, die in solch\n heisser Atmosphäre solch anstrengendes Werk verrichten . . . . Selbst der kurze\n Schlaf ist dadurch unterbrochen, dass das Kind sich selbst wecken muss bei Nacht,\n oder bei Tag vom Aussenlärm geweckt wird.“ Herr White giebt Fälle, wo ein\n Junge 36 Stunden nach einander arbeitete; andere, wo Knaben von 12 Jahren\n bis zwei Uhr Nachts schanzen und dann in der Hütte schlafen bis 5 Uhr\n Morgens (3 Stunden!), um das Tagewerk von neuem zu beginnen!","kind":"passage","sequence":165,"address":"8.165"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0166","text":"„Die Masse\n Arbeit,“ sagen die Redakteure des allgemeinen Berichts, Tremenheere und Tufnell,\n „die Knaben, Mädchen und Weiber im Lauf ihres täglichen oder nächtlichen Ar-\n beitsbanns („spell of labour“) verrichten, ist fabelhaft.“ (l. c. XLIII\n und XLIV.) Unterdess wankt vielleicht, eines Abends späte, das „entsagungs-\nvolle“ Glaskapital, Portweinduslig, aus dem Klub nach Haus, idiotisch vor sich\n hersummend: „Britons never, never shall be slaves!“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"237\", \"facs\": \"#f0256\"} --> geselligem Verkehr, zum freien Spiel der physischen und geistigen Leibes-\n kräfte, selbst die Feierzeit des Sonntags — und wäre es im Lande der\n Sabbathheiligen [Note: In England z. B. wird immer noch hier und da auf dem Lande ein Arbeiter zu\n Gefängnissstrafe verurtheilt wegen Entheiligung des Sabbaths durch Arbeit auf dem\n Gärtchen vor seinem Hause.","kind":"passage","sequence":166,"address":"8.166"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0167","text":"Derselbe Arbeiter wird wegen Kontraktbruches be-\n straft, bleibt er des Sonntags, sei es selbst aus religiösen Mucken, vom Metall-,\n Papier- oder Glaswerk weg. Das orthodoxe Parlament hat kein Ohr für Sabbath-\n entheiligung, wenn sie im „Verwerthungsprozess“ des Kapitals vorgeht. In einer\n Denkschrift (August 1863) der Londoner journeymen fishmongers and\n poulterers, worin die Abschaffung der Sonntagsarbeit verlangt\n wird, heisst es, dass ihre Arbeit während der ersten 6 Wochentage durchschnittlich\n 15 Stunden täglich dauert und am Sonntag 8—10 Stunden. Man entnimmt zu-\n gleich aus dieser Denkschrift, dass es namentlich die kitzliche Gourmandise der\naristokratischen Mucker von Exeter Hall ist, die diese „Sonntagsarbeit“\n ermuthigt.","kind":"passage","sequence":167,"address":"8.167"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0168","text":"Diese „Heiligen“, so eifrig „in cute curanda“, bewähren ihr Christen-\n thum durch die Ergebung, womit sie die Ueberarbeit, die Entbehrungen und\n den Hunger dritter Personen ertragen.„ Obsequium ventris istis (den Arbeitern)\n perniciosius est.“]  — reiner Firlefanz! Aber in seinem masslos blin-\n den Trieb, seinem Wehrwolfs-Heisshunger nach Mehrarbeit überrennt das\n Kapital nicht nur die moralischen, sondern auch die rein-\n physischen Maximalschranken des Arbeitstags. Es usur-\n pirt die Zeit für Wachsthum, Entwicklung und gesunde Erhaltung des\n Körpers. Es raubt die Zeit, erheischt zum Verzehr von freier Luft und\n Sonnenlicht. Es knickert ab an der Mahlzeit und einverleibt sie, wo mög-\n lich, dem Produktionsprozess selbst, so dass dem Arbeiter als blossem Pro-\n duktionsmittel Speisen zugesetzt werden, wie dem Dampfkessel Kohle\n und der Maschinerie Talg oder Oel.","kind":"passage","sequence":168,"address":"8.168"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0169","text":"Den gesunden Schlaf zur Sammlung,\n Erneurung und Erfrischung der Lebenskraft reduzirt es auf so viel Stun-\n den dumpfen Torpor als die Wiederbelebung eines absolut erschöpften\n Organismus unentbehrlich macht. Statt dass die normale Erhaltung\n der Arbeitskraft hier die Schranke des Arbeitstags, bestimmt umgekehrt\n die grösste täglich mögliche Verausgabung der Arbeitskraft, wie krankhaft\n gewaltsam und peinlich auch immer, die Schranke für die Rastzeit des\n Arbeiters. Das Kapital fragt nicht nach der Lebensdauer der Ar-\n beitskraft. Was es interessirt, ist einzig und allein das Maximum\n von Arbeitskraft, das in einem Arbeitstag flüssig gemacht werden kann.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"238\", \"facs\": \"#f0257\"} --> Es erreicht diess Ziel durch Verkürzung der Dauer der Arbeits-\n kraft, wie ein habgieriger Landwirth gesteigerten Bodenertrag durch\nBeraubung der Bodenfruchtbarkeit erreicht.","kind":"passage","sequence":169,"address":"8.169"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0170","text":"Die kapitalistische Produktion, die wesentlich Produktion von Mehr-\n werth, Einsaugung von Mehrarbeit ist, produzirt also mit der Verlängerung\n des Arbeitstags nicht nur die Verkümmerung der menschlichen Arbeits-\n kraft, die sie ihrer normalen moralischen und physischen Entwicklungs-\n und Bethätigungsbedingungen beraubt. Sie produzirt die vorzei-\n tige Erschöpfung und Abtödtung der Arbeitskraft selbst [Note: „We have given in our previous reports the statements of several ex-\n perienced manufacturers to the effect that over-hours . . . . certainly tend prema-\n turely to exhaust the working power of the men.“ l. c. 64, p. XIII.] .\n Sie verlängert die Produktionszeit des Arbeiters während eines gege-\n benen Termins durch Verkürzung seiner Lebenszeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":170,"address":"8.170"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0171","text":"Der Werth der Arbeitskraft schliesst aber den Werth der\n Waaren ein, welche zur Reproduktion des Arbeiters oder zur Fortpflan-\n zung der Arbeiterklasse erheischt sind. Wenn also die naturwidrige Ver-\n längerung des Arbeitstags, die das Kapital in seinem masslosen Trieb nach\n Selbstverwerthung nothwendig anstrebt, die Lebensperiode der einzelnen\n Arbeiter und damit die Dauer ihrer Arbeitskraft verkürzt, wird rascherer\n Ersatz der Verschlissenen nöthig, also das Eingehn grösserer Verschleiss-\n kosten in die Reproduktion der Arbeitskraft, ganz wie der täglich zu repro-\n duzirende Werththeil einer Maschine um so grösser ist, in je kürzerer Zeit\n sie verschleisst. Das Kapital scheint daher durch sein eignes Interesse\n auf einen Normal-Arbeitstag hingewiesen.","kind":"passage","sequence":171,"address":"8.171"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0172","text":"Der Sklavenhalter kauft seinen Arbeiter, wie er sein Pferd kauft.\n Mit dem Sklaven verliert er ein Kapital, das durch neue Auslage auf dem\n Sklavenmarkt ersetzt werden muss. Aber „die Reisfelder von Georgien\n und die Sümpfe des Mississippi mögen fatalistisch zerstörend auf die\n menschliche Constitution wirken; dennoch ist diese Verwüstung von\n menschlichem Leben nicht so gross, dass sie nicht gut gemacht werden\n könnte aus den strotzenden Gehegen von Virginien und Kentucky.","kind":"passage","sequence":172,"address":"8.172"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0173","text":"Oeko-\n nomische Rücksichten, die eine Art Sicherheit für die menschliche Behand-\n lung des Sklaven bieten könnten, sofern sie das Interesse des Herrn mit der\n Erhaltung des Sklaven identifiziren, verwandeln sich, nach Einführung des\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"239\", \"facs\": \"#f0258\"} --> Sklavenhandels, umgekehrt in Gründe der extremsten Zugrunderichtung\n des Sklaven, denn sobald sein Platz einmal durch Zufuhr aus fremden\n Negergehegen ausgefüllt werden kann, wird die Dauer seines\n Lebens minder wichtig als dessen Produktivität, so lange\n es dauert. Es ist daher eine Maxime der Sklavenwirthschaft in Län-\n dern der Sklaveneinfuhr, dass die wirksamste Oekonomie darin besteht, die\n grösstmöglichste Masse Leistung in möglichst kurzer Zeit dem Menschen-\n vieh (human chattle) auszupressen.","kind":"passage","sequence":173,"address":"8.173"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0174","text":"Es ist in tropischer Kultur, wo die\n jährlichen Profite oft dem Gesammtkapital der Pflanzungen gleich sind,\n dass Negerleben am rücksichtslosesten geopfert wird. Es ist die Agri-\n kultur Westindiens, seit Jahrhunderten die Wiege fabelhaften Reichthums,\n die Millionen der afrikanischen Race verschlungen hat. Es ist heut zu\n Tage in Cuba, dessen Revenüen nach Millionen zählen, und dessen Pflan-\n zer Fürsten sind, wo wir bei der Sklavenklasse, ausser der gröbsten Nah-\n rung, der erschöpfendsten und unablässigsten Plackerei, einen grossen Theil\ndurch die langsame Tortur von Ueberarbeit und Mangel\n an Schlaf und Erholung jährlich direkt zerstört sehn“ [Note: Cairns l. c. p. 110, 111.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":174,"address":"8.174"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0175","text":"Mutato nomine de te fabula narratur! Lies statt Sklavenhandel\n Arbeitsmarkt, statt Kentucky und Virginien Irland und die Agrikulturdi-\n strikte von England, Schottland und Wales, statt Afrika Deutschland! Wir\n hörten, wie die Ueberarbeit mit den Bäckern in London aufräumt, und den-\n noch ist der Londoner Arbeitsmarkt stets überfüllt mit deutschen und\n anderen Todeskandidaten für die Bäckerei. Die Töpferei, wie wir sahen,\n ist einer der kurzlebigsten Industriezweige. Fehlt es desswegen an\n Töpfern? Josiah Wedgwood, der Erfinder der modernen Töpferei,\n von Haus selbst ein gewöhnlicher Arbeiter, erklärte 1785 vor dem Hause\n der Gemeinen, dass die ganze Manufaktur 15 bis 20,000 Personen be-\n schäftige [Note: John Ward: „History of the Borough of Stoke-upon-\n Trent. London 1843“, p. 42.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":175,"address":"8.175"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0176","text":"Im Jahr 1861 betrug die Bevölkerung allein der städti-\n schen Sitze dieser Industrie in Grossbritanien 101,302. „Die Baumwoll-\n industrie zählt 90 Jahre . . . . In drei Generationen der englischen Race\n hat sie neun Generationen von Baumwollarbeitern verspeist [Note: Ferrand’s Rede im „House of Commons“ vom 27. April 1863.] “. Aller-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"240\", \"facs\": \"#f0259\"} --> dings, in einzelnen Epochen fieberhaften Aufschwungs zeigte der Arbeits-\n markt bedenkliche Lücken. So z. B. 1834. Aber die Herrn Fabrikanten\n schlugen nun den Poor Law Commissioners vor die „Uebervölkerung“\n der Ackerbaudistrikte nach dem Norden zu schicken, mit der Erklärung,\n dass die Fabrikanten sie absorbiren und konsumiren\n würden [Note: „That the manufacturers would absorb it and use it up. Those were\n the very words used by the cotton manufacturers.“ l. c.] . Diess waren ihre eigensten Worte.","kind":"passage","sequence":176,"address":"8.176"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0177","text":"„Agenten wurden zu\n Manchester bestallt mit Einwilligung der Poor Law Commissioners. Agri-\n kulturarbeiterlisten wurden ausgefertigt und diesen Agenten übermacht.\n Die Fabrikanten liefen in die Büreaus, und nachdem sie, was ihnen passte,\n ausgewählt, wurden die Familien vom Süden Englands verschickt. Diese\n Menschenpackete wurden geliefert mit Etiquetten gleich so viel Güter-\n ballen, auf Kanal und Lastwagen, — einige strolchten zu Fusse nach und\n viele irrten verloren und halbverhungert in den Manufakturdistrikten um-\n her. Diess entwickelte sich zu einem wahren Handelszweig. Das Haus\n der Gemeinen wird es kaum glauben. Dieser regelmässige Handel, die-\n ser Schacher in Menschenfleisch, dauerte fort, und diese Leute wurden ge-\n kauft und verkauft von den Manchester Agenten an die Manchester Fabri-\n kanten, ganz so regelmässig wie Neger an die Baumwollpflanzer der süd-\n lichen Staaten .","kind":"passage","sequence":177,"address":"8.177"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0178","text":". . . Das Jahr 1860 bezeichnet das Zenith der Baum-\n wollindustrie. Es fehlte wieder an Händen. Die Fabrikanten wandten\n sich wieder an die Fleischagenten und diese durchstöberten die Dünen von\n Dorset, die Hügel von Devon und die Ebenen von Wilts, aber die Ueber-\n völkerung war bereits verspeist. Der „Bury Guardian“ jammerte,\n dass 10,000 zusätzliche Hände nach Abschluss des englisch-französischen\n Handelsvertrags absorbirt werden könnten und bald an 30 oder 40,000\n mehr nöthig sein würden. Nachdem die Agenten und Subagenten des\n Fleischhandels die Agrikulturdistrikte 1860 ziemlich resultatlos durchge-\n fegt, wandte sich eine Fabrikantendeputation an Herrn Villiers, den\n Präsidenten des Poor Law Board, um wieder wie früher Zufuhr der Armen-\n und Waisenkinder aus den Workhouses erlaubt zu erhalten [Note: l. c.","kind":"passage","sequence":178,"address":"8.178"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0179","text":"Villiers, trotz bestem Willen, war „gesetzlich“ in der Lage das\n Fabrikantenanliegen abschlagen zu müssen. Die Herrn erreichten jedoch ihre\n Zwecke durch die Willfährigkeit der lokalen Armenverwaltungen. Herr A. Red-\n grave, Fabrikinspektor, versichert, dass diessmal das System, wonach die Waisen] .“","kind":"passage","sequence":179,"address":"8.179"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0180","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"241\", \"facs\": \"#f0260\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":180,"address":"8.180"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0181","text":"Was die Erfahrung dem Kapitalisten im Allgemeinen zeigt, ist\n eine beständige Uebervölkerung, d. h. Uebervölkerung im Verhältniss\n [Note: und Pauper’s Kinder „gesetzlich“ als apprentices (Lehrlinge) gelten, „nicht\n begleitet war von den alten Missständen“ — (über diese„Missstände“ vgl. Engels\n l. c.) —, obgleich allerdings in einem Fall „Missbrauch mit dem System getrieben\n worden ist, in Bezug auf eine Anzahl Mädchen und junge Weiber, die von den\n Agrikulturdistrikten Schottlands nach Lancashire und Cheshire gebracht wurden.“\n In diesem „System“ schliesst der Fabrikant einen Kontrakt mit den Behörden der\n Armenhäuser für bestimmte Perioden. Er nährt, kleidet und logirt die Kinder und\n giebt ihnen einen kleinen Zuschuss in Geld.","kind":"passage","sequence":181,"address":"8.181"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0182","text":"Sonderbar klingt folgende Bemer-\n kung des Herrn Redgrave, namentlich wenn man bedenkt, dass selbst unter den\n Prosperitätsjahren der englischen Baumwollindustrie das Jahr 1860 einzig da-\n steht und die Arbeitslöhne ausserdem hoch standen, weil die ausserordentliche\n Arbeitsnachfrage auf Entvölkerung in Irland stiess, auf beispiellose Aus-\n wanderung von englischen und schottischen Agrikulturdistrikten nach Austra-\n lien und Amerika, auf positive Abnahme der Bevölkerung in einigen\n englischen Agrikulturdistrikten in Folge theils glücklich erzielten Bruchs der\n Lebenskraft, theils des früheren Abschöpfens der disponiblen Bevölkerung durch\n die Händler in Menschenfleisch. Und trotz alledem sagt Herr Redgrave:\n „Diese Art Arbeit (der Armenhauskinder) wird jedoch nur gesucht, wenn keine\n andere gefunden werden kann, denn es ist theure Arbeit (high-priced labour).","kind":"passage","sequence":182,"address":"8.182"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0183","text":"Der gewöhnliche Arbeitslohn für einen Jungen von 13 Jahren ist ungefähr 4 sh.\n wöchentlich; aber 50 oder 100 solcher Jungen logiren, kleiden, nähren, mit ärzt-\n licher Hilfsleistung und passender Oberaufsicht versehn, und ihnen obendrein eine\n kleine Zubusse in Geld geben, ist unthubar für 4 sh. per Kopf wöchentlich.“\n („Rep. of the Insp. of Factories for 30th April 1860“ p. 27.) Herr Red-\n grave vergisst zu sagen, wie der Arbeiter selbst diess alles seinen Jungen für ihre\n 4 sh. Arbeitslohn leisten kann, wenn es der Fabrikant nicht kann für 50 oder 100\n Jungen, die gemeinsam logirt, beköstigt und beaufsichtigt werden. Zur Ab-\n wehr falscher Schlussfolgerungen aus dem Text muss ich hier noch bemerken, dass\ndie englische Baumwollindustrie, seit ihrer Unterwerfung unter den\n Factory Act von 1850 mit seiner Reglung der Arbeitszeit u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":183,"address":"8.183"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0184","text":"w., als die eng-\n lische Musterindustrie betrachtet werden muss. Der englische Baumwoll-\n arbeiter steht in jeder Hinsicht höher als sein kontinentaler Schicksalsgenosse.\n „Der preussische Fabrikarbeiter arbeitet mindestens 10 Stunden mehr per Woche als\n sein englischer Rivale, und wenn er an seinem eignen Webstuhl zu Hause beschäftigt\n wird, fällt selbst diese Schranke seiner zusätzlichen Arbeitsstunden weg.“ („Rep.\n of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1855“ p. 103.) Der obenerwähnte Fabrikinspektor\n Redgrave reiste nach der Industrieausstellung von 1851 auf dem Kontinent, spe-\n ziell in Frankreich und Preussen, um die dortigen Fabrikzustände zu untersuchen.\n Er sagt von dem preussischen Fabrikarbeiter: „Er erhält einen Lohn ausreichend] \n [Running matter: I.","kind":"passage","sequence":184,"address":"8.184"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0185","text":"16] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"242\", \"facs\": \"#f0261\"} --> zum augenblicklichen Verwerthungsbedüfniss des Kapitals, obgleich sie\n aus verkümmerten, schnell hinlebenden, sich rasch verdrängenden, so zu\n sagen unreif gepflückten Menschengenerationen ihren Strom bildet [Note: „Die Ueberarbeiteten sterben mit befremdlicher Raschheit; aber die\n Plätze derer, die untergehn, sind sofort wieder ausgefüllt, und ein häufiger\n Wechsel der Personen bringt keine Aenderung auf der Bühne hervor.“\n „England and America. London 1833“, t. I, 55. (Verfasser E. G. Wake-\n field.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":185,"address":"8.185"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0186","text":"Allerdings zeigt die Erfahrung dem verständigen Beobachter auf der an-\n dern Seite, wie rasch und tief die kapitalistische Produktion, die, geschicht-\n lich gesprochen, kaum von gestern datirt, die Volkskraft an der Lebens-\n wurzel ergriffen hat, wie die Degeneration der industriellen Bevölkerung\n nur durch beständige Absorption naturwüchsiger Lebenselemente vom\n Lande verlangsamt wird, und wie selbst die ländlichen Arbeiter, trotz\n freier Luft und des unter ihnen so allmächtig waltenden principle of na-\n tural selection, das nur die kräftigsten Individuen aufkommen lässt, schon\n abzuleben beginnen [Note: Siehe: „Public Health. Sixth Report of the Medical\n Officer of the Privy Council. 1863.“ Veröffentlicht London,\n 1864. Dieser Report handelt namentlich von den Agrikulturarbeitern.","kind":"passage","sequence":186,"address":"8.186"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0187","text":"„Man hat\n die Grafschaft Sutherland als eine sehr verbesserte Grafschaft dargestellt, aber eine\n neuerliche Untersuchung hat entdeckt, dass hier, in Distrikten einst so berühmt\n wegen schöner Männer und tapferer Soldaten, die Einwohner degenerirt sind zu\n einer mageren und verkümmerten Raçe. In den gesundesten Lagen, auf Hügel-\n abhängen im Angesicht des Meers, sind die Gesichter ihrer Kinder so dünn und\n blass, wie sie nur in der faulen Atmosphäre einer Londoner Winkelgasse sein\n können.“ (Thornton l. c. p. 74, 75.) Sie gleichen in der That den 30,000\n „gallant Highlanders,“ die Glasgow in seinen wynds und closes mit Prostituirten\n und Dieben zusammenbettet.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":187,"address":"8.187"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0188","text":"Das Kapital, das so „gute Gründe“ hat, die\n Leiden der es umgebenden Arbeitergeneration zu läugnen, wird in seiner\n praktischen Bewegung durch die Aussicht auf zukünftige Verfaulung der\n Menschheit und schliesslich doch unaufhaltsame Entvölkerung so wenig und\n so viel bestimmt als durch den möglichen Fall der Erde in die Sonne. In\n jeder Aktienschwindelei weiss jeder, dass das Unwetter einmal einschlagen\n muss, aber jeder hofft, dass es das Haupt seines Nächsten trifft, nachdem\n [Note: zur Verschaffung einfacher Kost und der wenigen Comforts, woran er gewöhnt und\n womit er zufrieden ist . . . . Er lebt schlechter und arbeitet härter als sein eng-\n lischer Rivale.“ („Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1853“ p. 85.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"243\", \"facs\": \"#f0262\"} --> er selbst den Goldregen aufgefangen und in Sicherheit gebracht hat.\nAprès moile déluge!","kind":"passage","sequence":188,"address":"8.188"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0189","text":"ist der Wahlruf jedes Kapitalisten und jeder Ka-\n pitalistennation. Das Kapital ist daher rücksichtslos gegen Gesundheit und\n Lebensdauer des Arbeiters, wo es nicht durch die Gesellschaft\n zur Rücksicht gezwungen wird [Note: „Obgleich die Gesundheit der Bevölkerung ein so wichtiges Element des\n nationalen Kapitals ist, fürchten wir gestehn zu müssen, dass die Kapitalisten\n durchaus nicht bei der Hand sind, diesen Schatz zu erhalten und werth zu achten.\n Die Rücksicht auf die Gesundheit der Arbeiter wurde den Fabrikanten aufge-\n zwungen.“ („Times“, October 1861.) „Die Männer des West Riding wurden\n die Tuchmacher der Menschheit, die Gesundheit des Arbeitervolks wurde ge-\n opfert, und in ein paar Generationen wäre die Raçe degenerirt, aber eine Reaktion\n trat ein. Die Stunden der Kinderarbeit wurden beschränkt u. s. w.“ („Report\n of the Registrar General for October 1861.“)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":189,"address":"8.189"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0190","text":"Der Klage über physische\n und geistige Verkümmerung, vorzeitigen Tod, Tortur der Ueberarbeit, ant-\n wortet es: Sollte diese Qual uns quälen, da sie unsre Lust (den Profit)\n vermehrt? Im Grossen und Ganzen hängt diess aber auch nicht vom\n guten oder bösen Willen des einzelnen Kapitalisten ab. Die freie Kon-\n kurrenz macht die immanenten Gesetze der kapitali-\n stischen Produktion dem einzelnen Kapitalisten gegenüber als\näusserliches Zwangsgesetz geltend [Note: Wir finden daher z. B., dass Anfang 1863 26 Firmen, welche ausge-\n dehnte Töpfereien in Staffordshire besitzen, darunter auch J. Wedgwood und\n Söhne, in einer Denkschrift „um gewaltsame Einmischung des Staats“\npetitioniren. Die „Konkurrenz mit andern Kapitalisten“ erlaube ihnen keine\n „freiwillige“ Beschränkung der Arbeitszeit der Kinder u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":190,"address":"8.190"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0191","text":"„So sehr wir\n daher die oben erwähnten Uebel beklagen, würde es unmöglich sein, sie durch\nirgend eine Art Uebereinkunft unter den Fabrikanten zu verhin-\n dern . . . . In Anbetracht aller dieser Punkte, sind wir zur Ueberzeugung gelangt,\n dass ein Zwangsgesetz nöthig ist.“ Children’s Emp. Comm. Rep. I,\n 1863, p. 322.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":191,"address":"8.191"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0192","text":"Die Festsetzung eines normalen Arbeitstags ist das\nResultat eines vielhundertjährigen Kampfes zwischen\n Kapitalist und Arbeiter. Doch zeigt die Geschichte dieses Kampfes\n zwei entgegengesetzte Strömungen. Man vergleiche z. B. die englische\n Fabrikgesetzgebung unserer Zeit mit den englischen Arbeitsstatuten vom 14.\n [Running matter: 16*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"244\", \"facs\": \"#f0263\"} --> bis tief in die Mitte des 18. Jahrhunderts [Note: Diese Arbeiterstatute, die man gleichzeitig auch in Frankreich, den\n Niederlanden u. s. w. findet, wurden in England erst 1813 formell aufgehoben,\n nachdem sie längst von den Produktionsverhältnissen beseitigt waren.] . Während das moderne Fabrik-\n gesetz den Arbeitstag gewaltsam abkürzt, suchen ihn jene Statute gewalt-\n sam zu verlängern.","kind":"passage","sequence":192,"address":"8.192"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0193","text":"Allerdings erscheinen die Ansprüche des Ka-\n pitals im Embryozustand, wo es erst wird, also noch nicht durch blosse Ge-\n walt der ökonomischen Verhältnisse, sondern auch durch Hilfe der Staats-\n macht sein Einsaugungsrecht eines genügenden Quantums Mehrarbeit sichert,\n ganz und gar bescheiden, vergleicht man sie mit den Konzessionen,\n die es in seinem Mannesalter knurrend und widerstrebig machen muss.\n Es kostet Jahrhunderte, bis der „freie“ Arbeiter, in Folge entwickelter\n kapitalistischer Produktionsweise, sich freiwillig dazu versteht, d. h.\ngesellschaftlich gezwungen ist, für den Preis seiner gewohnheits-\n mässigen Lebensmittel seine ganze aktive Lebenszeit, ja seine\n Arbeitsfähigkeit selbst, seine Erstgeburt für ein Gericht Linsen zu ver-\n kaufen. Es ist daher natürlich, dass die Verlängerung des Ar-\n beitstags, die das Kapital von Mitte des 14. bis Ende des 17.","kind":"passage","sequence":193,"address":"8.193"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0194","text":"Jahr-\n hunderts staatsgewaltig den volljährigen Arbeitern aufzu-\n dringen sucht, ungefähr mit der Schranke der Arbeitszeit zu-\n sammenfällt, die in der zweiten Hälfte des 19. Jahrhunderts der Verwand-\n lung von Kinderblut in Kapital hier und da von Staatswegen gezogen\n wird. Was heute, z. B. im Staate Massachussetts, bis jüngst dem\n freisten Staate der nordamerikanischen Republik, als Staatsschranke der\n Arbeit von Kindern unter 12 Jahren proklamirt ist, war in England noch\nMitte des 17. Jahrhunderts der normale Arbeitstag vollblütiger\n Handwerker, robuster Ackerknechte und riesenhafter Grobschmidte [Note: „No child under the age of 12 years shall be employed in any manu-\n facturing establishment more than 10 hours in one day.“ „General Sta-\n tutes of Massachussetts. 63, ch. 12. (Die Ordonnanzen sind erlassen\n von 1836—1858.)","kind":"passage","sequence":194,"address":"8.194"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0195","text":"„Labour performed during a period of 10 hours on any day\n in all cotton, woollen, silk, paper, glass, and flax factories, or in manufactories\n of iron and brass, shall be considered a legal day’s labour. And be it enacted,\n that hereafter no minor engaged in any factory shall be holden or required to\n work more than 10 hours in any day, or 60 hours in any week; and that hereafter\n no minor shall be admitted as a worker under the age of 10 years in any factory] .","kind":"passage","sequence":195,"address":"8.195"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0196","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"245\", \"facs\": \"#f0264\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":196,"address":"8.196"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0197","text":"Das erste „Statute of Labourers“ (23 Eduard III. 1349) fand\n seinen unmittelbaren Vorwand (nicht seine Ursache, denn die Gesetz-\n gebung dieser Art dauert Jahrhunderte fort ohne den Vorwand) in der grossen\n Pest, welche die Bevölkerung dezimirte, so dass, wie ein Tory Schriftsteller\n sagt, „die Schwierigkeit Arbeiter zu raisonablen Preisen (d. h. zu\n Preisen, die ihren Anwendern ein raisonables Quantum Mehrarbeit liessen)\n an die Arbeit zu setzen, in der That unerträglich wurde [Note: „Sophisms of Free Trade. 7th edit. Lond. 1850“, p. 205.\n Derselbe Tory giebt übrigens zu: „Parlamentsakte, die die Arbeitslöhne gegen\n die Arbeiter zu Gunsten der Arbeitsanwender regulirten, währten für die lange\n Periode von 464 Jahren. Die Bevölkerung wuchs. Diese Gesetze wurden nun\n überflüssig und lästig.“ (l. c. p. 206.)] “.","kind":"passage","sequence":197,"address":"8.197"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0198","text":"Raisonable\n Arbeitslöhne wurden daher zwangsgesetzlich diktirt, ebenso wie die Grenze\n des Arbeitstags. Der letztere Punkt, der uns hier allein interessirt, ist\n wiederholt in dem Statut von 1496 (unter Henry VIII). Der Arbeitstag\n für alle Handwerker (artificers) und Ackerbauarbeiter vom\n März bis September sollte damals, was jedoch nie durchgesetzt wurde,\n dauern von 5 Uhr Morgens bis zwischen 7 und 8 Uhr Abends, aber die\n Stunden für Mahlzeiten betragen 1 Stunde für Frühstück, 1½ Stunden für\n Mittagsessen, und ½ Stunde für Vieruhrenbrod, also grade doppelt so\n viel als nach dem jetzt gültigen Fabrikakt [Note: J.","kind":"passage","sequence":198,"address":"8.198"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0199","text":"Wade bemerkt mit Recht in Bezug auf diess Statut: „Aus dem Statut\n von 1496 geht hervor, dass die Nahrung als Aequivalent für ⅓ des Einkommens\n eines Handwerkers und ⅔ des Einkommens eines Agrikulturarbeiters galt, und\n diess zeigt eine grössere Stufe von Unabhängigkeit unter den Arbeitern an als jetzt\n vorherrscht, wo die Nahrung der Arbeiter in Agrikultur und Manufaktur ein viel\n höheres Verhältniss zu ihren Löhnen bildet.“ (J. Wade l. c. p. 24, 25 u. 577.)\n Die Meinung als sei diese Differenz etwa der Differenz im Preisverhältniss zwischen\n Nahrungsmitteln und Kleidungsstücken, jetzt und damals, geschuldet, widerlegt\n der oberflächlichste Blick auf: „Chronicon Pretiosum etc. By Bishop\n Fletwood. 1st edit. London 1707. 2nd edit. London 1745.“] . Im Winter sollte ge-\n [Note: within this state.“ „State of New Jersey. An act to limit the hours\n of labour etc., 61 und 2. (Gesetz vom 11.","kind":"passage","sequence":199,"address":"8.199"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0200","text":"März 1855.) „No minor who\n has attained the age of 12 years, and is under the age of 15 years, shall be em-\n ployed in any manufacturing establishment more than 11 hours in any one day,\nnor before 5 o’clock in the morning, nor after 7½ in the evening.“\n „Revised Statutes of the State of Rhode Island etc. ch. 39, §. 23,\n 1st July 1857.“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"246\", \"facs\": \"#f0265\"} --> arbeitet werden von 5 Uhr Morgens bis zum Dunkeln, mit denselben Unter-\n brechungen. Ein Statut der Elisabeth von 1562 für alle Arbei-\n ter, „gedungen für Lohn per Tag oder Woche“, lässt die Länge des Ar-\n beitstags unberührt, sucht aber die Zwischenräume zu beschränken auf\n 2½ Stunden für den Sommer und 2 für den Winter. Das Mittagsessen\n soll nur eine Stunde dauern und „der Nachmittagsschlaf von ½ Stunde“\n nur zwischen Mitte Mai und Mitte August erlaubt sein. Für jede Stunde\n Abwesenheit soll 1 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":200,"address":"8.200"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0201","text":"(etwa 10 Pfennige) vom Lohn abgehen. In der\n Praxis jedoch war das Verhältniss den Arbeitern viel günstiger als im\n Statutenbuch. Der Vater der politischen Oekonomie und gewissermassen\n der Erfinder der Statistik, William Petty, sagt in einer Schrift, die er\n im letzten Drittel des 17. Jahrhunderts veröffentlichte: „Arbeiter (labou-\n ring men, eigentlich damals Ackerbauarbeiter) arbeiten 10 Stunden\n täglich und nehmen wöchentlich 20 Mahlzeiten ein, nämlich an Arbeits-\n tagen täglich drei und an Sonntagen zwei; woraus man klärlich sieht, dass,\n wenn sie an Freitag Abenden fasten wollten und in anderthalb Stun-\n den zu Mittag speisen wollten, während sie jetzt zu dieser Mahlzeit\n zwei Stunden brauchen, von 11 bis 1 Uhr Morgens, wenn sie also\n\\frac{1}{20} mehr arbeiteten und \\frac{1}{20} weniger verzehrten, das Zehntel der oben\n erwähnten Steuer aufbringbar wäre [Note: „W.","kind":"passage","sequence":201,"address":"8.201"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0202","text":"Petty: Political Anatomy of Ireland. 1672. edit.\n 1691“, p. 10.] “. Hatte Dr. Andrew Ure nicht\n Recht, die Zwölfstundenbill von 1833 als Rückgang in die Zeiten der Fin-\n sterniss zu verschreien? Allerdings gelten die in den Statuten und von\n Petty erwähnten Bestimmungen auch für „apprentices“ (Lehrlinge).\n Wie es aber noch Ende des 17. Jahrhunderts mit der Kinderarbeit\n stand, ersieht man aus folgender Klage: „Unsere Jugend, hier in Eng-\n land, treibt gar nichts bis zu der Zeit wo sie Lehrlinge werden, und dann\n brauchen sie natürlich lange Zeit — sieben Jahre — um sich zu vollkom-\n menen Handwerkern zu bilden“. Deutschland wird dagegen gerühmt, weil\n dort die Kinder von der Wiege auf wenigstens zu „ein bischen Beschäf-\n tigung erzogen werden“ [Note: „A Discussion on the Necessity of Encouraging Mecha-\n nick Industry. London 1689“, p. 13.","kind":"passage","sequence":202,"address":"8.202"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0203","text":"Macaulay, der die englische\n Geschichte im Whig- und Bourgeoisinteresse zurechtgefälscht hat, deklamirt wie] .","kind":"passage","sequence":203,"address":"8.203"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0204","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"247\", \"facs\": \"#f0266\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":204,"address":"8.204"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0205","text":"Noch während des grössten Theils des 18. Jahrhunderts, bis\n zur Epoche der grossen Industrie, war es dem Kapital in England nicht\n gelungen, durch Zahlung des wöchentlichen Werths der Arbeitskraft sich\n der ganzen Wochenarbeit des Arbeiters, mit Ausnahme je-\n doch des Agrikulturarbeiters, zu bemächtigen. Der Umstand,\n dass sie eine ganze Woche mit dem Lohn von 4 Tagen leben konnten,\n schien den Arbeitern kein hinreichender Grund, auch die andren zwei Tage\n für den Kapitalisten zu arbeiten. Eine Seite der englischen Oekonomen\n denuncirte im Dienst des Kapitals diesen Eigensinn auf’s Wüthendste, eine\n andre Seite vertheidigte die Arbeiter. Hören wir z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":205,"address":"8.205"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0206","text":"die Polemik zwi-\n schen Postlethwaite, dessen Handels-Diktionnair damals denselben\n Ruf genoss wie heut zu Tage ähnliche Schriften von Mac Culloch und Mac\n [Note: folgt: „Die Praxis, Kinder vorzeitig an die Arbeit zu setzen, herrschte im 17.\n Jahrhundert in einem für den damaligen Zustand der Industrie fast unglaublichen\n Grad vor. Zu Norwich, dem Hauptsitz der Wollenindustrie, wurde ein Kind von\n 6 Jahren für arbeitsfähig gehalten. Verschiedene Schriftsteller jener Zeit, und\n darunter manche, die als ausserordentlich wohlgesinnt betrachtet wurden, erwähnen\n mit „Exultation“ (Entzücken) die Thatsache, dass in dieser Stadt allein Knaben\n und Mädchen einen Reichthum schaffen, der über ihren eignen Unterhalt hinaus\n 12,000 Pfd. St. in einem Jahr betrug.","kind":"passage","sequence":206,"address":"8.206"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0207","text":"Je genauer wir die Geschichte der Ver-\n gangenheit untersuchen, desto mehr Grund finden wir die Ansicht derer zu ver-\n werfen, die unser Zeitalter für fruchtbar an neuen socialen Uebeln halten. Das\n was neu ist, ist die Intelligenz, die die Uebel entdeckt, und die Humanität, die sie\n heilt.“ („History of England“, v. I, p. 419.) Macaulay hätte weiter\n berichten können, dass „ausserordentlich wohlgesinnte“ amis du commerce\n im 17. Jahrhundert mit „Exultation“ erzählen, wie in einem Armenhaus in Holland\n ein Kind von 4 Jahren beschäftigt wurde, und dass diess Beispiel der „vertue\n mise en pratique“ in allen Schriften von Humanitairen à la Macaulay Muster\n passirt bis zur Zeit A. Smith’s. Es ist richtig, dass mit dem Aufkommen der\n Manufaktur im Unterschied zum Handwerk sich Spuren der Kinderexploi-\n tation zeigen.","kind":"passage","sequence":207,"address":"8.207"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0208","text":"Die Tendenz des Kapitals ist unverkennbar, aber die Thatsachen\n selbst stehn noch so vereinzelt, wie die Erscheinung zweiköpfiger Kinder. Sie\n wurden daher „mit Exultation“, als besonders merkwürdig und bewundernswerth,\n von ahnungsvollen „amis du commerce“ für Mit- und Nachwelt aufgezeich-\n net und zur Nachahmung empfohlen. Derselbe schottische Sykophant und Schön-\n redner Macaulay sagt: „man höre heute nur von Rückschritt und sehe nur Fort-\n schritt“. Was für Augen und namentlich was für Ohren!] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"248\", \"facs\": \"#f0267\"} --> Gregor, und dem früher citirten Verfasser des „Essay on Trade\n and Commerce“ [Note: Unter den Anklägern der Arbeiter ist der grimmigste der im Text er-\n wähnte anonyme Verfasser von: „An Essay on Trade and Commerce,\n containing Observations on Taxation etc. London 1770“. Schon\n früher in seiner Schrift: „Considerations on Taxes.","kind":"passage","sequence":208,"address":"8.208"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0209","text":"London 1765.“\n Auch Polonius Arthur Young, der unsägliche statistische Schwätzer, folgt\n in derselben Linie. Unter den Vertheidigern der Arbeiter stehn oben an: Jacob\n Vanderlint in: „Money answers all things. London 1734“, Rev.\n Nathaniel Forster, D. D. in: „An Enquiry into the Causes of the\n Present Price of Provisions. London 1766“, Dr. Price, und nament-\n lich auch Postlethwaite, sowohl in einem Supplement zu seinem „Uni-\n versal Dictionary of Trade and Commerce“ als in: „Great Bri-\n tain’s Commercial Interest explained and improved. 2nd edit.\n London 1755“. Die Thatsachen selbst findet man bei vielen andern gleichzei-\n tigen Schriftstellern konstatirt, u. a. bei Josiah Tucker.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":209,"address":"8.209"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0210","text":"Postlethwaite sagt u. a.: „Ich kann diese wenigen Bemerkun-\n gen nicht abschliessen, ohne Notiz zu nehmen von der trivialen Redens-\n art in dem Munde zu vieler, dass wenn der Arbeiter (industrious poor)\n in 5 Tagen genug erhalten kann um zu leben, er nicht volle 6 Tage ar-\n beiten will. Daher schliessen sie auf die Nothwendigkeit, selbst die\n nothwendigen Lebensmittel durch Steuern oder irgend welche andre Mittel\n zu vertheuern, um den Handwerker und Manufakturarbeiter zu unausge-\n setzter sechstägiger Arbeit in der Woche zu zwingen. Ich muss um\n die Erlaubniss bitten, andrer Meinung zu sein als diese grossen Politiker,\n welche für die beständige Sklaverei der Arbeiterbevölke-\n rung dieses Königreichs („the perpetual slavery of the working people“)\n die Lanze einlegen; sie vergessen das Sprichwort „all work and no\n play“ (nur Arbeit, und kein Spiel, macht dumm).","kind":"passage","sequence":210,"address":"8.210"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0211","text":"Brüsten sich die\n Engländer nicht mit der Genialität und Gewandtheit ihrer Handwerker und\n Manufakturarbeiter, die bisher den britischen Waaren allgemeinen Kredit\n und Ruf verschafft haben? Welchem Umstand war diess geschuldet?\n Wahrscheinlich keinem andern als der Art und Weise, wie unser Arbeits-\n volk, eigenlaunig, sich zu zerstreuen weiss. Wären sie gezwungen das\n ganze Jahr durchzuarbeiten, alle sechs Tage in der Woche, in\n steter Wiederholung desselben Werkes, würde das nicht ihre Genialität ab-\n stumpfen, und sie dumm-träg statt munter und gewandt machen; und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"249\", \"facs\": \"#f0268\"} --> würden unsere Arbeiter in Folge solcher ewigen Sklaverei ihren\n Ruf nicht verlieren statt erhalten? Welche Art Kunstgeschick könnten wir\n erwarten von solch hart geplackten Thieren? (hard driven ani-\n mals) . . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":211,"address":"8.211"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0212","text":"Viele von ihnen verrichten soviel Arbeit in 4 Tagen als ein\n Franzose in 5 oder 6. Aber wenn Engländer ewige Schanzarbeiter sein\n sollen, so steht zu fürchten, dass sie noch unter die Franzosen entarten\n (degenerate) werden. Wenn unser Volk wegen seiner Tapferkeit im\n Krieg berühmt ist, sagen wir nicht, dass diess einerseits dem guten eng-\n lischen Roastbeef und Pudding in seinem Leibe, andrerseits nicht minder\n unsrem konstitutionellen Geiste der Freiheit geschuldet ist? Und warum\n sollte die grössere Genialität, Energie und Gewandtheit unserer Handwer-\n ker und Manufakturarbeiter nicht der Freiheit geschuldet sein, womit sie\n sich in ihrer eignen Art und Weise zerstreuen? Ich hoffe, sie werden\n nie weder diese Privilegien verlieren, noch das gute Le-\n ben, woraus ihre Arbeitstüchtigkeit und ihr Muth gleichmässig her-\n stammen“! [Note: Postlethwaite l. c.","kind":"passage","sequence":212,"address":"8.212"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0213","text":"„First Preliminary Discourse“, p. 14.]","kind":"passage","sequence":213,"address":"8.213"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0214","text":"Darauf antwortet der Verfasser des „Essay on Trade and Com-\n merce“:","kind":"passage","sequence":214,"address":"8.214"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0215","text":"„Wenn es für eine göttliche Einrichtung gilt, den siebenten Tag\n der Woche zu feiern, so schliesst diess ein, dass die andern Wochentage\n der Arbeit (er meint dem Kapital, wie man gleich sehn wird) angehö-\n ren, und es kann nicht grausam gescholten werden, diess Gebot Gottes zu\n erzwingen . . . . . Dass die Menschheit im Allgemeinen von Natur zur\n Bequemlichkeit und Trägheit neigt, davon machen wir die fatale Erfah-\n rung im Betragen unsres Manufakturpöbels, der durchschnittlich\n nicht über 4 Tage die Wochearbeitet, ausser im Fall einer\n Theurung der Lebensmittel . . . . Gesetzt ein Bushel Weizen repräsentire\n alle Lebensmittel des Arbeiters, koste 5 sh., und der Arbeiter verdiene\n einen Shilling täglich durch seine Arbeit. Dann braucht er bloss 5 Tage\n in der Woche zu arbeiten; nur 4, wenn der Bushel 4 sh. beträgt . . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":215,"address":"8.215"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0216","text":"Da aber der Arbeitslohn in diesem Königreich viel höher steht, verglichen\n mit dem Preise der Lebensmittel, so besitzt der Manufakturarbeiter, der\n 4 Tage arbeitet, einen Geldüberschuss, womit er während des Rests der\n Woche müssig lebt . . . . Ich hoffe, ich habe genug gesagt, um klar zu\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"250\", \"facs\": \"#f0269\"} --> machen, dass mässiges Arbeiten von 6 Tagen in der Woche\n keine Sklaverei ist. Unsere Agrikulturarbeiter thun diess\n und, allem Anschein nach, sind sie die Glücklichsten unter den Arbeitern\n (labouring poor) [Note: „An Essay etc.“ Er selbst erzählt p. 96, worin schon 1770 „das\n Glück“ der englischen Agrikulturarbeiter bestand.","kind":"passage","sequence":216,"address":"8.216"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0217","text":"„Ihre Arbeitskräfte\n („their working powers“) sind stets auf das Aeusserste angespannt („on the\n stretch“); sie können nicht schlechter leben als sie thun („they cannot live cheaper\n than they do“), noch härter arbeiten“ („nor work harder“).] , aber die Holländer thun es in den Manufak-\n turen und scheinen ein sehr glückliches Volk. Die Franzosen thun\n es, so weit nicht die vielen Feiertage dazwischen kommen [Note: Der Protestantismus spielt schon durch seine Verwandlung fast aller\n traditionellen Feiertage in Werktage eine wichtige Rolle in der Genesis des\n Kapitals.]  . . . .\n Aber unser Manufakturpöbel hat sich die fixe Idee in den Kopf gesetzt,\n dass sie als Engländer durch das Recht der Geburt das Privilegium be-\n sitzen, freier und unabhängiger zu sein als die Arbeiter in irgend\n einem andern Lande von Europa.","kind":"passage","sequence":217,"address":"8.217"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0218","text":"Nun, diese Idee, so weit sie auf die\n Tapferkeit unserer Soldaten einwirkt, mag von einigem Nutzen sein; aber\n je weniger die Manufakturarbeiter davon haben, desto besser für sie selbst\n und den Staat. Arbeiter sollten sich nie für unabhängig von ihren\n Vorgesetzten („independent of their superiors“) halten . . . . Es ist\n ausserordentlich gefährlich, mobs in einem kommerziellen Staat wie dem\n unsrigen zu encouragiren, wo vielleicht 7 Theile von den 8 der Gesammt-\n bevölkerung Leute mit wenig oder keinem Eigenthum sind [Note: „An Essay etc.“ p. 15—57 passim.]  . . . . .\n Die Kur wird nicht vollständig sein, bis unsre industriellen Armen\n sich bescheiden, 6 Tage für dieselbe Summe zu arbeiten,\n die sie nun in 4 Tagen verdienen“ [Note: l. c. p. 69.","kind":"passage","sequence":218,"address":"8.218"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0219","text":"Jacob Vanderlint erklärte schon 1734, das Geheimniss\n der Kapitalistenklage über die Faullenzerei des Arbeitervolks sei einfach, dass sie\n für denselben Lohn 6 statt 4 Arbeitstage beanspruchten.] . Zu diesem Zwecke, wie\n zur „Ausrottung der Faullenzerei, Ausschweifung und romantischen Frei-\n heitsduselei“, ditto „zur Minderung der Armentaxe, Förderung des Gei-\n stes der Industrie und Herabdrückung des Arbeitspreises in\n den Manufakturen“, schlägt unser treuer Eckart des Kapitals das\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"251\", \"facs\": \"#f0270\"} --> probate Mittel vor, solche Arbeiter, die der öffentlichen Wohlthätigkeit\n anheimfallen, in einem Wort, paupers, einzusperren in ein „ideales\n Arbeitshaus“ (an ideal Workhouse). „Ein solches Haus muss\n zu einem Hause des Schreckens (House of Terror) gemacht\n werden [Note: l. c. p.","kind":"passage","sequence":219,"address":"8.219"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0220","text":"260: „Such ideal workhouse must be made an „House\n of Terror“, and not an asylum for the poor, where they are to be plentifully\n fed, warmly and decently clothed, and where they do but little work.“] . In diesem „Hause des Schreckens“, diesem „Ideal\n von einem Workhouse“, soll gearbeitet werden 14 Stunden täglich\n mit Einbegriff jedoch der passenden Mahlzeiten, so dass volle 12 Ar-\n beitsstunden übrig bleiben“ [Note: „In this ideal workhouse the poor shall work 14 hours in a day, allowing\n proper time for meals, in such manner that there shall remain 12 hours of neat\n labour.“ (l. c.) „Die Franzosen“, sagt er, „lachen über unsre enthusiastischen\n Ideen von Freiheit.“ (l. c. p. 78.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":220,"address":"8.220"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0221","text":"Zwölf Arbeitsstunden täglich im „Workhaus-Ideal“,\n im Hause des Schreckens von 1770! Drei und sechzig Jahre\n später, 1833, als das englische Parlament in vier Fabrikzweigen den\n Arbeitstag für Kinder von 13 bis 18 Jahren auf zwölf volle Arbeits-\n stunden herabsetzte, schien der jüngste Tag der englischen In-\n dustrie angebrochen! 1852, als L. Bonaparte bürgerlich Fuss zu fassen\n suchte durch Rütteln am gesetzlichen Arbeitstag, schrie das französische\n Arbeitervolk aus einem Munde: „Das Gesetz, das den Arbeitstag\n auf 12 Stunden verkürzt, ist das einzige Gut, das uns von der\n Gesetzgebung der Republik blieb“ [Note: „They especially objected to work beyond the 12 hours per day, because\n the law which fixed those hours is the only good which remains to them of the\n legislation of the Republic.“ (Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31. Octob. 1856,\n p. 80.) Das französische Zwölfstundengesetz vom 5.","kind":"passage","sequence":221,"address":"8.221"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0222","text":"September 1850, eine ver-\n bürgerlichte Ausgabe des Dekrets der provisorischen Regierung vom 2. März 1848,\n erstreckt sich auf alle Ateliers ohne Unterschied. Vor diesem Gesetz\n war der Arbeitstag in Frankreich unbeschränkt. Er währte in den Fabriken 14,\n 15 und mehr Stunden. Siehe „Des classes ouvrières en France, pen-\n dant l’année 1848. Par M. Blanqui.“ Herr Blanqui, der Oekonom,\n nicht der Revolutionär, war von Regierungswegen mit der Enquête über die Ar-\n beiterzustände betraut.] ! In Zürich ist die Arbeit von\n Kindern über 10 Jahren auf 12 Stunden beschränkt; im Aargau\n wurde 1862 die Arbeit von Kindern zwischen 13 und 16 Jahren von\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"252\", \"facs\": \"#f0271\"} --> 12½ auf 12 Stunden reducirt, in Oestreich 1860 für Kin-\n der zwischen 14 und 16 Jahren ditto auf 12 Stunden [Note: Lobenswerth wären die Verordnungen der Herrn v. d. Heydt und Man-\n teuffel vom 18.","kind":"passage","sequence":222,"address":"8.222"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0223","text":"August 1853 und 12. August 1854, wenn sie — ausgeführt würden.\nBelgien bewährt sich auch mit Bezug auf die Regulation des Arbeitstags als\nbürgerlicher Musterstaat. Lord Howard de Welden, englischer Bevoll-\n mächtigter in Brüssel, berichtet dem Foreign Office d. d. 12. Mai 1862: „Der\n Minister Rogier erklärte mir, dass weder ein allgemeines Gesetz noch Lokalregu-\n lationen die Kinderarbeit irgendwie beschränken; dass die Regierung sich während\n der letzten 3 Jahre in jeder Sitzung mit dem Gedanken trug, den Kammern ein\n Gesetz über den Gegenstand vorzulegen, dass sie aber stets ein unüberwindliches\n Hinderniss fand an der eifersüchtigen Angst gegen irgend welche Gesetzgebung\nim Widerspruch mit dem Prinzip vollkommener Freiheit der\n Arbeit“!] . Welch\n ein „Fortschritt seit 1770“ würde Macaulay „mit Exultation“\n aufjauchzen!","kind":"passage","sequence":223,"address":"8.223"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0224","text":"Das „Haus des Schreckens“ für Paupers, wovon die Kapital-\n seele 1770 noch träumte, erhob sich wenige Jahre später als riesiges\n „Arbeitshaus“ für die Manufakturarbeiter selbst. Es hiess Fabrik.\n Und diessmal erblasste das Ideal vor der Wirklichkeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":224,"address":"8.224"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0225","text":"Nachdem das Kapital Jahrhunderte gebraucht, um den Arbeits-\n tag bis zu seinen normalen Maximalgrenzen und dann über diese\n hinaus, bis zu den Grenzen des natürlichen Tags von 12\n Stunden zu verlängern [Note: „Es ist sicher sehr bedauerlich, dass irgend eine Klasse von Personen\n 12 Stunden täglich sich abplacken muss. Rechnet man die Mahlzeiten zu und die\n Zeit um zu und von der Werkstatt zu gehn, so beträgt diess in der That 14 von\n den 24 Tagesstunden … Abgesehn von der Gesundheit, wird Niemand, ich hoffe,\n anstehn zuzugeben, dass vom moralischen Gesichtspunkt eine so gänzliche Ab-\n sorption der Zeit der arbeitenden Klassen, ohne Unterlass, vom frühen Alter von\n 13 Jahren, und in den „freien“ Industriezweigen selbst von viel früherem Alter an,\n ausserordentlich schädlich und ein furchtbares Uebel ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":225,"address":"8.225"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0226","text":"Im Interesse der öffent-\n lichen Moral, für die Aufziehung einer tüchtigen Bevölkerung, und um der grossen\n Masse des Volks einen vernünftigen Lebensgenuss zu verschaffen, muss darauf ge-\n drungen werden, dass in allen Geschäftszweigen ein Theil jedes Arbeitstags reser-\n virt werde für Erholung und Musse.“ (Leonhard Horner in: „Insp. of\n Fact. Reports. 31st Dec. 1841.“)] , erfolgte nun, seit der Geburt der gros-\n sen Industrie im letzten Drittel des 18. Jahrhunderts, eine lawinenartig\n gewaltsame und masslose Ueberstürzung. Jede Schranke von Sitte und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"253\", \"facs\": \"#f0272\"} --> Natur, Alter und Geschlecht, Tag und Nacht, wurde zertrümmert.","kind":"passage","sequence":226,"address":"8.226"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0227","text":"Selbst\n die Begriffe von Tag und Nacht, bäuerlich einfach in den alten Statuten,\n verschwammen so sehr, dass ein englischer Richter noch 1860 wahrhaft\n talmudistischen Scharfsinn aufbieten musste, um „urtheilskräftig“ zu er-\n klären, was Tag und Nacht sei [Note: Siehe „Judgment of Mr. J. H. Otwey, Belfast, Hilary\n Sessions, 1860.“] . Das Kapital feierte seine Orgien.","kind":"passage","sequence":227,"address":"8.227"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0228","text":"Sobald die vom Produktionslarm übertölpelte Arbeiterklasse wieder\n einigermassen zur Besinnung kam, begann ihr Widerstand, zunächst im\n Geburtsland der grossen Industrie, in England. Während drei Decennien\n jedoch blieben die von ihr ertrotzten Konzessionen rein nominell. Das\n Parlament erliess 5 Arbeits-Akte von 1802 bis 1833, war aber so schlau\n keinen Pfennig für ihre zwangsmässige Ausführung, das nöthige Beamten-\n personal u. s. w. zu votiren [Note: Sehr charakteristisch ist es für das Régime Louis Philippe’s, des roi\n bourgeois, dass das einzige unter ihm erlassene Fabrikgesetz vom 22. März 1841\nniemals durchgeführt worden ist. Und diess Gesetz betrifft nur Kinder-\n arbeit. Es setzt 8 Stunden für Kinder zwischen 8 und 12, 12 Stunden für Kinder\n zwischen 12 und 16 Jahren u. s. w. fest, mit vielen Ausnahmen, welche die Nacht-\n arbeit selbst für Achtjährige erlauben.","kind":"passage","sequence":228,"address":"8.228"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0229","text":"Ueberwachung und Erzwingung des Gesetzes\n blieben in einem Lande, wo jede Maus polizeilich administrirt wird, dem guten\n Willen der „amis du commerce“ überlassen. Erst seit 1853 ist das Gesetz in einem\n einzigen Département, dem Département du Nord, durch Anstellung eines bezahl-\n ten Regierungsinspektors, wirklich durchgeführt worden. Nicht minder charakte-\n ristisch für die Entwicklung der französischen Gesellschaft überhaupt ist es, dass\n Louis Philippe’s Gesetz bis zur Revolution von 1848 einzig dastand in der alles\n umspinnenden französischen Gesetzfabrik!] . Sie blieben ein todter Buchstabe. „Die\n Thatsache ist, dass vor dem Akt von 1833 Kinder und junge Per-\n sonen abgearbeitet wurden („were worked“) die ganze Nacht, den ganzen\n Tag, oder beide ad libitum“ [Note: „Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 30th April 1860“, p. 51.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":229,"address":"8.229"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0230","text":"Erst seit dem Fabrikakt von 1833 — umfassend Baumwoll-,\n Wolle-, Flachs- und Seidenfabriken — datirt für die moderne Industrie ein\nNormalarbeitstag. Nichts charakterisirt den Geist des Kapi-\n tals besser als die Geschichte der englischen Fabrikgesetzgebung von\n 1833 bis 1864!","kind":"passage","sequence":230,"address":"8.230"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0231","text":"Das Gesetz von 1833 erklärt, „der gewöhnliche Fabrik-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"254\", \"facs\": \"#f0273\"} --> Arbeitstag solle beginnen um halb 6 Uhr Morgens und enden halb 9 Uhr\n Abends, und innerhalb dieser Schranken, einer Periode von 15\n Stunden, solle es gesetzlich sein junge Personen (d. h. Personen zwi-\n schen 13 und 18 Jahren) zu irgend einer Zeit des Tages anzuwen-\n den, immer vorausgesetzt, dass keine individuelle junge Person\n mehr als 12 Stunden innerhalb Eines Tags arbeite, mit\n Ausnahme gewisser speziell vorgesehner Fälle“. Die 6. Sektion des Akts\n bestimmt, „dass im Laufe jedes Tags jeder solchen Person von beschränk-\n ter Arbeitszeit mindestens 1½ Stunden für Mahlzeiten eingeräumt werden\n sollen“. Die Anwendung von Kindern unter 9 Jahren, mit später\n zu erwähnender Ausnahme, ward verboten, die Arbeit der Kinder von\n 9 bis 13 Jahren auf 8 Stunden täglich beschränkt.","kind":"passage","sequence":231,"address":"8.231"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0232","text":"Nacht-\n arbeit, d. h. nach diesem Gesetz, Arbeit zwischen halb 9 Uhr Abends\n und halb 6 Uhr Morgens, ward verboten für alle Personen zwischen 9 und\n 18 Jahren.","kind":"passage","sequence":232,"address":"8.232"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0233","text":"Die Gesetzgeber waren so weit entfernt, die Freiheit des Kapi-\n tals in Aussaugung der erwachsenen Arbeitskraft, oder, wie sie es nann-\n ten, „die Freiheit der Arbeit“ antasten zu wollen, dass sie ein\n eignes System ausheckten, um solcher haarsträubenden Konsequenz des\n Fabrikakts vorzubeugen.","kind":"passage","sequence":233,"address":"8.233"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0234","text":"„Das grosse Uebel des Fabriksystems, wie es gegenwärtig einge-\n richtet ist“, heisst es im ersten Bericht des Centralraths der Kommission\n vom 25. Juni 1833, „besteht darin, dass es die Nothwendigkeit schafft, die\n Kinderarbeit zur äussersten Länge des Arbeitstags der Erwachsenen aus-\n zudehnen. Das einzige Heilmittel für diess Uebel, ohne Beschrän-\n kung der Arbeit der Erwachsenen, woraus ein Uebel ent-\n springen würde grösser als das, dem vorgebeugt werden\n soll, scheint der Plan doppelte Reihen von Kindern zu verwenden“. Unter\n dem Namen Relaissystem („System of Relays“; Relay heisst\n im Englischen wie im Französischen: das Wechseln der Postpferde auf\n verschiedenen Stationen) wurde daher dieser „Plan“ ausgeführt, so dass\n z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":234,"address":"8.234"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0235","text":"von halb 6 Uhr Morgens bis halb zwei Uhr Nachmittags eine Reihe\n von Kindern zwischen 9 und 13 Jahren, von halb zwei Uhr Nachmittags\n bis halb 9 Uhr Abend eine andre Reihe vorgespannt wird u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":235,"address":"8.235"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0236","text":"Zur Belohnung dafür, dass die Herrn Fabrikanten alle während der\n letzten 22 Jahre erlassnen Gesetze über Kinderarbeit aufs frechste ignorirt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"255\", \"facs\": \"#f0274\"} --> hatten, ward ihnen jetzt aber auch die Pille vergoldet. Das Parlament\n bestimmte, dass nach dem 1. März 1834 kein Kind unter 11 Jah-\n ren, nach dem 1. März 1835 kein Kind unter 12 Jahren, und nach\n dem 1. März 1836 kein Kind unter 13 Jahren über 8 Stunden in\n einer Fabrik arbeiten solle! Dieser für das „Kapital“ so schonungsvolle\n „Liberalismus“ war um so anerkennenswerther als Dr. Farre, Sir A. Car-\n lisle, Sir B. Brodie, Sir C. Bell, Mr. Guthrie u. s. w., kurz die bedeutend-\n sten physicians und surgeons London’s in ihren Zeugenaussagen vor\n dem Unterhaus erklärt hatten, dass periculum in mora! Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":236,"address":"8.236"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0237","text":"Farre\n drückte sich noch etwas gröber dahin aus: „Gesetzgebung ist gleich noth-\n wendig für die Prävention des Tods in jeder Form, worin er\n vorzeitig angethan werden kann, und sicher dieser (der Fabrikmodus)\n muss als eine der grausamsten Methoden ihn anzuthun betrachtet\n werden [Note: „Legislation is equally necessary for the prevention of death, in any\n form in which it can be prematurely inflicted, and certainly this must be viewed\n as a most cruel mode of inflicting it.“] “. Dasselbe „reformirte“ Parlament, das aus Zartsinn für die\n Herrn Fabrikanten Kinder unter 13 Jahren noch Jahre lang in die Hölle\n 72stündiger Fabrikarbeit per Woche festbannte, verbot dagegen in dem\nEmancipationsakt, der auch die Freiheit tropfenweise eingab,\n von vorn herein den Pflanzern irgend einen Negersklaven länger\n als 45 Stunden per Woche abzuarbeiten!","kind":"passage","sequence":237,"address":"8.237"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0238","text":"Aber keineswegs gesühnt, eröffnete das Kapital jetzt eine mehrjäh-\n rige und geräuschvolle Agitation. Sie drehte sich hauptsächlich um das\n Alter der Kategorien, die unter dem Namen Kinder auf 8stün-\n dige Arbeit beschränkt und einem gewissen Schulzwang unterworfen wor-\n den waren. Nach der kapitalistischen Anthropologie hörte das Kindes-\n alter im 10., oder, wenn es hoch ging, im 11. Jahre auf. Je näher der\n Termin der vollen Ausführung des Fabrikakts, das verhängnissvolle Jahr\n 1836 rückte, um so wilder raste der Fabrikantenmob. Es gelang ihm in\n der That, die Regierung so weit einzuschüchtern, dass diese 1835 den\n Termin des Kindesalters von 13 auf 12 Jahre herabzusetzen vorschlug.\n Indess wuchs die pressure from without drohend an. Der Muth\n versagte dem Unterhause.","kind":"passage","sequence":238,"address":"8.238"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0239","text":"Es verweigerte Dreizehnjährige länger als 8\n Stunden täglich unter das Juggernautrad des Kapitals zu werfen und der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"256\", \"facs\": \"#f0275\"} --> Akt von 1833 trat in volle Wirkung. Er blieb unverändert bis\n Juni 1844.","kind":"passage","sequence":239,"address":"8.239"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0240","text":"Während des Decenniums, worin er erst theilweise, dann ganz die\n Fabrikarbeit regulirte, strotzen die officiellen Berichte der Fabrikinspek-\n toren von Klagen über die Unmöglichkeit seiner Ausführung. Da das\n Gesetz von 1833 es nämlich den Herrn vom Kapital freistellte in der\n fünfzehnstündigen Periode von halb 6 Uhr Morgens bis halb 9 Uhr Abends\n jede „junge Person“ und „jedes Kind“ zu irgend beliebiger Zeit die\n zwölf-, respektive 8stündige Arbeit beginnen, unterbrechen, enden zu las-\n sen, und ebenso den verschiednen Personen verschiedne Stunden\n der Mahlzeiten anzuweisen, fanden die Herrn bald ein neues „Re-\n laissystem“ aus, wonach die Arbeitspferde nicht nach bestimmter Zeit\n Stationen wechselten, sondern an wechselnden Stationen stets wieder von\n neuem vorgespannt wurden. Wir verweilen nicht weiter bei der Schön-\n heit dieses Systems, da wir später darauf zurückkommen müssen.","kind":"passage","sequence":240,"address":"8.240"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0241","text":"So viel\n ist aber auf den ersten Blick klar, dass es den ganzen Fabrikakt, nicht nur\n seinem Geist, sondern auch seinem Buchstaben nach aufhob. Wie sollten\n die Fabrikinspektoren, bei dieser komplizirten Buchführung über jedes ein-\n zelne Kind und jede junge Person, die gesetzlich bestimmte Arbeitszeit und\n die Gewährung der gesetzlichen Mahlzeiten erzwingen? In einem grossen\n Theil der Fabriken blühte der alte brutale Unfug bald wieder ungestraft\n auf. In einer Zusammenkunft mit dem Minister des Innern (1844) wiesen\n die Fabrikinspektoren die Unmöglichkeit aller Kontrole unter dem neu-\n ausgeheckten Relaissystem nach [Note: „Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31. October 1849“, p. 6.] . Unterdess hatten sich aber die Um-\n stände sehr geändert. Die Fabrikarbeiter, namentlich seit 1838, hatten\n die Zehnstundenbill zu ihrem ökonomischen, wie die Charter zu\n ihrem politischen Wahlruf gemacht.","kind":"passage","sequence":241,"address":"8.241"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0242","text":"Ein Theil der Fabrikanten selbst,\n der den Fabrikbetrieb dem Ak von 1833 gemäss geregelt hatte, überwarf\n das Parlament mit Denkschriften über die unsittliche „Konkurrenz“ der\n „falschen Brüder“, denen grössere Frechheit oder glücklichere Lokalum-\n stände den Gesetzesbruch erlaubten. Zudem, wie sehr immerhin der ein-\n zelne Fabrikant der alten Raubgier den Zügel frei schiessen lassen mochte,\n die Wortführer und politischen Leiter der Fabrikantenklasse geboten eine\n veränderte Haltung und veränderte Sprache gegenüber den Arbeitern. Sie\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"257\", \"facs\": \"#f0276\"} --> hatten den Feldzug zur Abschaffung der Korngesetze eröffnet\n und bedurften der Hilfe der Arbeiter zum Siege! Sie versprachen daher\n nicht nur Verdopplung des Laibes Brod, sondern Annahme der Zehn-\n stundenbill unter dem tausendjährigen Reich des Free Trade [Note: „Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct.","kind":"passage","sequence":242,"address":"8.242"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0243","text":"1848“, p. 98.] . Sie\n durften also um so weniger eine Massregel bekämpfen, die nur den Akt\n von 1833 zur Wahrheit machen sollte. In ihrem heiligsten Interesse, der\nGrundrente, bedroht, donnerten endlich die Tories entrüstet philanthro-\n pisch über die „infamen Praktiken“ [Note: Uebrigens braucht Leonhard Horner den Ausdruck „nefarious prac-\n tices“ officiell. („Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31st October 1859“, p. 7.)]  ihrer Feinde.","kind":"passage","sequence":243,"address":"8.243"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0244","text":"So kam der zusätzliche Fabrikakt vom 7. Juni 1844 zu\n Stande. Er trat am 10. September 1844 in Wirkung. Er gruppirt eine\n neue Kategorie von Arbeitern unter die Beschützten, nämlich die Frauen-\n zimmer über 18 Jahre. Sie wurden in jeder Rücksicht den jungen\n Personen gleichgesetzt, ihre Arbeitszeit auf 12 Stunden beschränkt,\n Nachtarbeit ihnen untersagt u. s. w. Zum erstenmal sah sich die Gesetz-\n gebung also gezwungen auch die Arbeit Volljähriger direkt und officiell\n zu kontroliren. In dem Fabrikbericht von 1844—45 heisst es ironisch:\n „Es ist kein einziger Fall zu unsrer Kenntniss gekommen, wo erwachsne\n Weiber sich über diesen Eingriff in ihre Rechte beschwert hät-\n ten“ [Note: „Rep. etc. for 30th Sept. 1844“, p. 15.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":244,"address":"8.244"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0245","text":"Die Arbeit von Kindern unter 13 Jahren wurde auf 6½ und,\n unter gewissen Bedingungen, 7 Stunden täglich reducirt [Note: Der Akt erlaubt Kinder 10 Stunden anzuwenden, wenn sie nicht Tag\n nach Tag, sondern nur einen Tag über den andern arbeiten. Im Ganzen blieb\n diese Klausel wirkungslos.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":245,"address":"8.245"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0246","text":"Um die Missbräuche des „falschen Relaissystems“ zu besei-\n tigen, traf das Gesetz u. a. folgende wichtige Detailbestimmungen: „Der\n Arbeitstag für Kinder und junge Personen ist von der Zeit an zu zählen,\n wo irgend ein Kind oder junge Person des Morgens in der Fabrik\n zu arbeiten anfängt.“ So dass wenn A z. B. um 8 Uhr Morgens die Ar-\n beit beginnt, und B um 10 Uhr, der Arbeitstag dennoch für B zur selben\n Stunde enden muss wie für A. „Der Anfang des Arbeitstags soll angezeigt\n werden durch eine öffentliche Uhr, z. B. die nächste Eisenbahnuhr, wo-\n nach die Fabrikglocke zu richten. Der Fabrikant hat eine grossgedruckte\n [Running matter: I. 17] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"258\", \"facs\": \"#f0277\"} --> Notiz in der Fabrik aufzuhängen, worin Anfang, Ende, Pausen des Arbeits-\n tags angegeben sind.","kind":"passage","sequence":246,"address":"8.246"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0247","text":"Kinder, die ihre Arbeit des Vormittags vor 12 Uhr\n beginnen, dürfen nicht wieder nach 1 Uhr Mittags verwandt werden. Die\n Nachmittagsreihe muss also aus andern Kindern bestehn als die Vormit-\n tagsreihe. Die 1½ Stunden für Mahlzeit müssen allen beschützten\n Arbeitern zu denselben Tagesperioden eingeräumt werden, eine\n Stunde wenigstens vor 3 Uhr Nachmittags. Kein Kind oder junge Person\n darf länger als 5 Stunden vor 1 Uhr Mittags verwandt werden ohne eine\n mindestens halbstündige Pause für Mahlzeit. Kein Kind, junge Person,\n oder Frauenzimmer darf während irgend einer Mahlzeit in einer Fabrikstube\n bleiben, worin irgend ein Arbeitsprozess vorgeht u. s. w.“","kind":"passage","sequence":247,"address":"8.247"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0248","text":"Man hat gesehn: Diese minutiösen Bestimmungen, welche die Periode,\n Grenzen, Pausen der Arbeit so militärisch uniform nach dem Glockenschlag\n regeln, waren keineswegs Produkte parlamentarischer Hirnweberei. Sie\n entwickelten sich allmählig aus den Verhältnissen heraus, als Naturge-\n setze der modernen Produktionsweise. Ihre Formulirung, officielle An-\n erkennung und staatliche Proklamation waren Ergebniss langwieriger\n Klassenkämpfe. Eine ihrer nächsten Folgen war, dass die Praxis auch\n den Arbeitstag der erwachsnen männlichen Fabrikarbeiter\n denselben Schranken unterwarf, da in den meisten Produktionsprozessen\n die Cooperation der Kinder, jungen Personen und Frauenzimmer unentbehr-\n lich. Im Grossen und Ganzen galt daher während der Periode von\n 1844—47 der zwölfstündige Arbeitstag allgemein und uniform in allen\n der Fabrikgesetzgebung unterworfenen Industriezweigen.","kind":"passage","sequence":248,"address":"8.248"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0249","text":"Die Fabrikanten erlaubten diesen „Fortschritt“ jedoch nicht ohne\n einen kompensirenden „Rückschritt“. Auf ihren Antrieb reducirte das\n Unterhaus das Minimalalter der zu verarbeitenden Kinder von 9 Jah-\n ren auf 8, zur Sicherung der dem Kapital von Gott und Rechtswegen\n geschuldeten „additionellen Fabrikkinderzufuhr“ [Note: „As a reduction in their hours of work would cause a large number (of\n children) to be employed, it was thought that the additional supply of\n children from eight to nine years of age, would meet the increased\n demand.“ (l. c. p. 13.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":249,"address":"8.249"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0250","text":"Die Jahre 1846—47 machen Epoche in der ökonomischen Ge-\n schichte Englands. Widerruf der Korngesetze, die Einfuhrzölle auf\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"259\", \"facs\": \"#f0278\"} --> Baumwolle und andre Rohmaterialien abgeschafft, der Freihandel zum Leit-\n stern der Gesetzgebung erklärt! Kurz das tausendjährige Reich brach\n an. Andrerseits erreichten in denselben Jahren Chartistenbewegung und\n Zehnstundenagitation ihren Höhe unkt. Sie fanden Bundesgenossen an\n den racheschnaubenden Tories. Trotz des fanatischen Widerstands des\n wortbrüchigen Freihandelsheers mit Bright und Cobden an der Spitze,\n ging die so lang erstrebte Zehnstundenbill durch das Parla-\n ment. —","kind":"passage","sequence":250,"address":"8.250"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0251","text":"Der neue Fabrikakt vom 8. Juni 1847 setzte fest, dass am ersten\n Juli 1847 eine vorläufige Verkürzung des Arbeitstags der „jungen\n Personen“ (von 13 bis zu 18 Jahren) und aller Arbeiterinnen auf\n 11 Stunden, am 1. Mai 1848 aber die definitive Beschränkung auf 10\n Stunden eintreten solle. Im übrigen war der Akt nur ein amen-\n dirender Zusatz der Gesetze von 1833 und 1844.","kind":"passage","sequence":251,"address":"8.251"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0252","text":"Das Kapital unternahm zunächst einen vorläufigen Feldzug, dessen\n Ziel, die volle Ausführung des Akts am 1. Mai 1848 zu verhindern.\n Und zwar sollten die Arbeiter selbst, angeblich durch die Erfahrung ge-\n witzigt, ihr eignes Werk wieder zerstören helfen. Der Augenblick war\n geschickt gewählt. „Man muss sich erinnern, dass, in Folge der furcht-\n baren Krise von 1846—47, grosses Leid unter den Fabrikarbeitern vor-\n herrschte, da viele Fabriken nur für kurze Zeit gearbeitet, andre ganz still\n gestanden hatten. Eine beträchtliche Anzahl der Arbeiter befand sich\n daher in drückendster Lage, viele in Schulden.","kind":"passage","sequence":252,"address":"8.252"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0253","text":"Man konnte daher mit\n ziemlicher Gewissheit annehmen, dass sie die längere Arbeitszeit vorziehn\n würden, um die vergangenen Verluste gut zu machen, vielleicht Schulden\n abzuzahlen, oder ihre Möbel aus dem Pfandhaus zu nehmen, oder ver-\n kaufte Habseligkeiten zu ersetzen, oder neue Kleidungsstücke sich selbst\n und ihren Familien zu verschaffen“ [Note: „Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1848“, p. 16.] . Die Herrn Fabrikanten suchten\n die natürliche Wirkung dieser Umstände zu steigern durch eine allge-\n meine Lohnherabsetzung von 10 %. Diess geschah so zu sagen\n zur Einweihungsfeier der neuen Freihandelsära. Dann folgte weitere\n Herabsetzung um 8⅓ %, sobald der Arbeitstag auf 11, und um das Dop-\n pelte, sobald er definitiv auf 10 Stunden verkürzt wurde.","kind":"passage","sequence":253,"address":"8.253"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0254","text":"Wo es daher\n irgendwie die Verhältnisse zuliessen, fand eine Lohnherabsetzung von wenig-\n [Running matter: 17*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"260\", \"facs\": \"#f0279\"} --> stens 25 % statt [Note: „Ich fand, dass Leute, die 10 sh. wöchentlich erhalten hatten, 1 sh. ab-\n gezogen erhielten auf Rechnung der allgemeinen Lohnherabsetzung von 10 %, und\n von den restirenden 9 sh. weiteren Abzug von 1 sh. 6 d. erlitten für die Zeitver-\n kürzung, zusammen 2 sh. 6 d., und trotz alledem hielt die Mehrzahl fest an der\n Zehnstundenbill.“ (l. c.)] . Unter so günstig vorbereiteten Chancen begann man\n die Agitation unter den Arbeitern zum Repeal des Akts von 1847. Kein\n Mittel des Betrugs, der Verführung und der Drohung wurde dabei ver-\n schmäht, aber alles umsonst.","kind":"passage","sequence":254,"address":"8.254"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0255","text":"Von dem halben Dutzend Petitionen, worin\n die Arbeiter klagen mussten über „ihre Unterdrückung durch den Akt.“\n erklärten die Bittsteller selbst, bei mündlichem Verhör, ihre Unterschriften\n seien abgenöthigt worden. „Sie seien unterdrückt, aber von Jemand an-\n ders als dem Fabrikakt“ [Note: „Als ich die Petition unterzeichnete, erklärte ich zugleich, ich thue da-\n mit etwas Schlechtes. — Warum habt Ihr sie denn unterzeichnet? — Weil man\n mich im Weigerungsfall auf das Pflaster geworfen hätte.“ Der Bittsteller fühlte\n sich in der That „unterdrückt“, aber nicht grade durch den Fabrikakt.“ (l. c.\n p. 102.)] . Wenn es aber den Fabrikanten nicht ge-\n lang, die Arbeiter in ihrem Sinn sprechen zu machen, schrieen sie selbst\n nur um so lauter in Presse und Parlament im Namen der Arbeiter.","kind":"passage","sequence":255,"address":"8.255"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0256","text":"Sie denuncirten die Fabrikinspektoren als eine Art Konventskommissäre,\n die ihrer Weltverbesserungsgrille den unglücklichen Arbeiter unbarmherzig\n aufopferten. Auch diess Manöver schlug fehl. Fabrikinspektor Leon-\n hard Horner stellte in eigner Person und durch seine Unterinspektoren\n zahlreiche Zeugenverhöre in den Fabriken Lancashire’s an. Ungefähr 70 %\n der verhörten Arbeiter erklärten sich für 10 Stunden, eine viel geringere\n Prozentzahl für 11 und eine ganz unbedeutende Minorität für die alten 12\n Stunden [Note: p. 17 l. c. In Herrn Horner’s Distrikt wurden so 10,270 erwachsene\n männliche Arbeiter in 181 Fabriken verhört. Man findet ihre Aussagen im\nAppendix des Fabrikreports für das Halbjahr endend October 1848. Diese\n Zeugenverhöre bieten auch in andrer Beziehung schätzbares Material.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":256,"address":"8.256"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0257","text":"Ein andres „gütliches“ Manöver war die erwachsnen männ-\n lichen Arbeiter 12 bis 15 Stunden arbeiten zu lassen und dann\ndiese Thatsache für den besten Ausdruck der proletarischen Herzens-\n wünsche zu erklären. Aber der „unbarmherzige“ Fabrikinspektor Leon-\n hard Horner war wieder an Ort und Stelle. Die meisten „Ueberstündigen“\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"261\", \"facs\": \"#f0280\"} --> sagten aus, „sie würden es bei weitem vorziehn 10 Stunden für geringern\n Arbeitslohn zu arbeiten, aber sie hätten keine Wahl; so viele von ihnen\n seien arbeitslos, so viele Spinner gezwungen als blosse piecers zu arbeiten,\n dass wenn sie die längere Arbeitszeit verweigerten, andre sofort ihre Stellen\n einnehmen würden, so dass die Frage so für sie stehe: entweder die längere\n Zeit arbeiten oder auf dem Pflaster liegen“ [Note: l. c. Siehe die von Leonhard Horner selbst gesammelten Aussagen\n No.","kind":"passage","sequence":257,"address":"8.257"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0258","text":"69, 70, 71, 72, 92, 93 und die von Subinspektor A. gesammelten No. 51,\n 52, 58, 59, 60, 62, 70 des „Appendix.“ Ein Fabrikant schenkte selbst klaren\n Wein ein. Siehe No. 14 nach No. 265 l. c.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":258,"address":"8.258"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0259","text":"Der vorläufige Feldzug des Kapitals war missglückt und das Zehn-\n stundengesetz trat am 1. Mai 1848 in Kraft. Unterdess hatte\n jedoch das Fiasko der Chartistenpartei, deren Führer eingekerkert und deren\n Organisation zersprengt, bereits das Selbstvertrauen der englischen Arbeiter-\n klasse erschüttert. Bald darauf vereinigte die Pariser Juniinsurrektion und\n ihre blutige Erstickung, wie im kontinentalen Europa, so in England, alle\n Fraktionen der herrschenden Klassen, Grundeigenthümer und Kapitalisten,\n Börsenwölfe und Krämer, Protektionisten und Freihändler, Regierung und\n Opposition, Pfaffen und Freigeister, junge Huren und alte Nonnen, unter\n dem gemeinschaftlichen Ruf zur Rettung des Eigenthums, der Religion,\n der Familie, der Gesellschaft! Die Arbeiterklasse wurde überall verfehmt,\n in den Bann gethan, unter das „loi des suspects“ gestellt.","kind":"passage","sequence":259,"address":"8.259"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0260","text":"Die\n Herrn Fabrikanten brauchten sich also nicht länger zu geniren. Sie\n brachen in offne Revolte aus, nicht nur wider das Zehnstundengesetz,\n sondern die ganze Gesetzgebung, welche seit 1833 die „freie“ Aussaugung\n der Arbeitskraft einigermassen zu zügeln suchte. Es war eine Proslavery\n Rebellion in Miniatur, während mehr als zwei Jahren durchgeführt mit\n cynischer Rücksichtslosigkeit, mit terroristischer Energie, beide um so wohl-\n feiler als der rebellische Kapitalist nichts riskirte ausser der Haut seiner\n Arbeiter.","kind":"passage","sequence":260,"address":"8.260"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0261","text":"Zum Verständniss des Nachfolgenden muss man sich erinnern, dass\n die Fabrikakte von 1833, 1844 und 1847 alle drei in Rechtskraft, so\n weit der eine nicht den andern amendirt; dass keiner derselben den Ar-\n beitstag des männlichen Arbeiters über 18 Jahren beschränkt,\n und dass seit 1833 die fünfzehnstündige Periode von halb 6 Uhr Mor-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"262\", \"facs\": \"#f0281\"} --> gens bis halb 9 Uhr Abends der gesetzliche „Tag“ blieb, innerhalb des-\n sen erst die zwölf-, später die 10stündige Arbeit der jungen Personen und\n Frauenzimmer unter den vorgeschriebnen Bedingungen zu verrichten war.","kind":"passage","sequence":261,"address":"8.261"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0262","text":"Die Fabrikanten begannen hier und da mit Entlassung eines Theils,\n manchmal der Hälfte, der von ihnen beschäftigten jungen Personen und\n Arbeiterinnen, und stellten dagegen die fast verschollene Nachtarbeit\n unter den erwachsnen männlichen Arbeitern wieder her. Das Zehnstun-\n dengesetz, riefen sie, lasse ihnen keine andre Alternative [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st October 1848“, p. 133, 134.] !","kind":"passage","sequence":262,"address":"8.262"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0263","text":"Der zweite Schritt bezog sich auf die gesetzlichen Pausen für Mahl-\n zeiten. Hören wir die Fabrikinspektoren. „Seit der Beschränkung\n der Arbeitsstunden auf 10, behaupten die Fabrikanten, obgleich sie prak-\n tisch ihre Ansicht noch nicht bis zur letzten Konsequenz durchführen, dass\n wenn sie z. B. von 9 Uhr Morgens bis 7 Uhr Abends arbeiten lassen, sie\n den gesetzlichen Vorschriften genug thun, indem sie eine Stunde für\n Mahlzeit vor 9 Uhr Morgens und eine halbe Stunde nach 7 Uhr\n Abends, also 1½ Stunden für Mahlzeiten geben. In einigen Fällen er-\n lauben sie jetzt eine halbe Stunde für Mittagsessen, bestehn aber zugleich\n darauf, sie seien durchaus nicht verpflichtet, irgend einen Theil der 1½\n Stunden im Lauf des zehnstündigen Arbeitstags einzuräumen“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 30th April 1848“, p. 47.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":263,"address":"8.263"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0264","text":"Die\n Herrn Fabrikanten behaupteten also, die peinlich genauen Bestimmungen\n des Akts von 1844 über Mahlzeiten gäben den Arbeitern nur die Er-\n laubniss, vor ihrem Eintritt in die Fabrik und nach ihrem Austritt\n aus der Fabrik, also bei sich zu Hause, zu essen und zu trinken! Und\n warum sollten die Arbeiter auch nicht vor 9 Uhr Morgens ihr Mittagsessen\n einnehmen? Die Kronjuristen entschieden jedoch, dass die vorgeschrie-\n benen Mahlzeiten „in Pausen während des wirklichen Arbeits-\n tags gegeben werden müssen und dass es ungesetzlich 10 Stunden nach\n einander von 9 Uhr Morgens bis 7 Uhr Abends ohne Unterbrechung arbei-\n ten zu lassen“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1848“, p. 130.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":264,"address":"8.264"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0265","text":"Nach diesen gemüthlichen Demonstrationen leitete das Kapital seine\nRevolte ein durch einen Schritt, der dem Buchstaben des Gesetzes\n von 1844 entsprach, also legal war.","kind":"passage","sequence":265,"address":"8.265"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0266","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"263\", \"facs\": \"#f0282\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":266,"address":"8.266"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0267","text":"Das Gesetz von 1844 verbot allerdings, Kinder von 8 bis 13 Jahren,\n die vor 12 Uhr Vormittags beschäftigt würden, wieder nach 1 Uhr\n Mittags zu beschäftigen. Aber es regelte in keiner Weise die 6½stün-\n dige Arbeit der Kinder, deren Arbeitszeit um 12 Uhr Vormittags\n oder später begann! Achtjährige Kinder konnten daher, wenn sie die\n Arbeit um 12 Uhr Vormittags begannen, von 12 bis 1 Uhr verwandt werden,\n 1 Stunde; von 2 Uhr bis 4 Uhr Nachmittags, 2 Stunden; und von 5 Uhr bis\n halb 9 Uhr Abends, 3½ Stunden; alles in allem die gesetzlichen 6½\n Stunden! Oder noch besser. Um ihre Verwendung der Arbeit er-\n wachsner männlicher Arbeiter bis halb 9 Uhr Abends\n anzupassen, brauchten ihnen die Fabrikanten kein Werk zu geben vor 2\n Uhr Nachmittags, und konnten sie dann ununterbrochen in der Fabrik hal-\n ten bis halb 9 Uhr Abends!","kind":"passage","sequence":267,"address":"8.267"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0268","text":"„Und es wird jetzt ausdrücklich zugestanden,\n dass neuerdings, in Folge der Fabrikantengier, ihre Maschinerie länger\n als 10 Stunden laufen zu lassen, sich die Praxis in England eingeschlichen\n hat, acht- bis dreizehnjährige Kinder beiderlei Geschlechts, nach Entfer-\n nung aller jungen Personen und Weiber aus der Fabrik, allein mit den\n erwachsnen Männern, bis halb 9 Uhr Abends arbeiten zu las-\n sen“ [Note: „Reports etc.“ l. c. p. 42.] . Arbeiter und Fabrikinspektoren protestirten aus hygienischen\n und moralischen Gründen. Aber das Kapital antwortete:\n„Meine Thaten auf mein Haupt! Mein Recht verlang’ ich!\n Die Busse und Verpfändung meines Scheins!“","kind":"passage","sequence":268,"address":"8.268"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0269","text":"In der That waren, nach statistischer Vorlage an das Unterhaus vom\n 26. Juli 1850, trotz aller Proteste, am 15. Juli 1850 3742 Kinder in 275\n Fabriken dieser „Praxis“ unterworfen [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1850“, p. 5, 6.] . Noch nicht genug! Das\n Luchsauge des Kapitals entdeckte, dass der Akt von 1844 fünfstündige\n Arbeit des Vormittags nicht ohne Pause von wenigstens 30 Minuten\n für Erfrischung erlaubt, aber nichts der Art für die Nachmittagsarbeit vor-\n schreibt. Es verlangte und ertrotzte daher den Genuss, achtjährige Ar-\n beiterkinder unausgesetzt von 2 bis halb 9 Uhr Abends nicht nur schan-\n zen, sondern auch hungern zu lassen!\n„Ja, die Brust.\n So sagt der Schein“ [Note: Die Natur des Kapitals bleibt dieselbe, in seinen unentwickelten, wie in] .","kind":"passage","sequence":269,"address":"8.269"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0270","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"264\", \"facs\": \"#f0283\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":270,"address":"8.270"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0271","text":"Diess Shylocksche Festklammern am Buchstaben des Gesetzes von\n 1844, soweit es die Kinderarbeit regelt, sollte jedoch nur die offne\n Revolte gegen dasselbe Gesetz vermitteln, so weit es die Arbeit von\n „jungen Personen und Frauenzimmern“ regelt. Man erin-\n nert sich, dass die Abschaffung des „falschen Relaissystems“\n Hauptzweck und Hauptinhalt jenes Gesetzes bildet. Die Fabrikanten er-\n öffneten ihre Revolte mit der einfachen Erklärung, die Sektionen des Akts\n von 1844, welche beliebigen Niessbrauch der jungen Personen und Frauen-\n zimmer in beliebigen kürzeren Abschnitten des fünfzehnstündigen Fabrik-\n tags verbieten, seien „vergleichungsweise harmlos (comparati-\n vely harmless) gewesen, so lange die Arbeitszeit auf 12 Stunden einge-\n schränkt war. Unter dem Zehnstundengesetz seien sie eine unerträgliche\nUnbill“ (hard ship) [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1848“, p. 133.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":271,"address":"8.271"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0272","text":"Sie zeigten daher den Inspektoren in der\n kühlsten Weise an, dass sie sich über den Buchstaben des Gesetzes\n hinwegsetzen und das alte System auf eigne Faust wieder einführen wür-\n den [Note: So unter andern Philanthrop Ashworth in einem quäkerhaft widrigen\n Brief an Leonhard Horner.] . Es geschehe im Interesse der übelberathnen Arbeiter selbst,\n „um ihnen höhere Löhne zahlen zu können.“ „Es sei der einzig mög-\n liche Plan, um unter dem Zehnstundengesetz die industrielle Suprematie\n Grossbritanniens zu erhalten“ [Note: l. c. p. 134.] . „Es möge etwas schwer sein Un-\n regelmässigkeiten unter dem Relaissystem zu entdecken, aber was heisse\n das? (what of that?) Soll das grosse Fabrikinteresse dieses Landes als ein\n [Note: seinen entwickelten Formen.","kind":"passage","sequence":272,"address":"8.272"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0273","text":"In dem Gesetzbuch, das der Einfluss der Sklaven-\n halter, kurz vor Ausbruch des amerikanischen Bürgerkriegs, dem Territorium von\n New - Mexico aufoctroyirte, heisst es: der Arbeiter, so weit der Kapitalist\n seine Arbeitskraft gekauft hat, „ist sein (des Kapitalisten) Geld.“ („The\n labourer is his (the capitalist’s) money.“) Dieselbe Anschauung herrschte bei\n den römischen Patriziern. Das Geld, das sie dem plebejischen Schuldner vor-\n geschossen, hatte sich, vermittelst der Lebensmittel, in Fleisch und Blut des\n Schuldners verwandelt. Diess „Fleisch und Blut“ war daher „ihr Geld.“ Daher\n das Shylocksche Gesetz der 10 Tafeln!","kind":"passage","sequence":273,"address":"8.273"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0274","text":"Linguet’s Hypothese, dass die patri-\n zischen Gläubiger von Zeit zu Zeit jenseits der Tiber Festschmäuse in gekochtem\n Schuldnerfleisch veranstalteten, bleibe ebenso dahingestellt, wie Daumer’s Hypo-\n these über das christliche Abendmahl.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"265\", \"facs\": \"#f0284\"} --> sekundäres Ding behandelt werden, um den Fabrik-Inspektoren und Sub-\n inspektoren ein bischen mehr Mühe (some little trouble) zu sparen“ [Note: l. c. p. 140.] ?","kind":"passage","sequence":274,"address":"8.274"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0275","text":"Alle diese Flausen halfen natürlich nichts. Die Fabrikinspektoren\n schritten gerichtlich ein. Bald aber überschüttete eine solche Staubwolke\n von Fabrikantenpetitionen den Minister des Innern, Sir George Grey,\n dass er in einem Cirkular vom 5. August 1848 die Inspektoren anwies,\n „im Allgemeinen nicht einzuschreiten wegen Verletzung des Buchsta-\n bens des Akts, so oft das Relaissystem nicht erwiesnermassen missbraucht\n werde, um junge Personen und Frauenzimmer über 10 Stunden arbeiten zu\n lassen.“ Hierauf erlaubte Fabrikinspektor J. Stuart das sogenannte Ab-\n lösungssystem während der fünfzehnstündigen Periode des Fabriktags in\n ganz Schottland, wo es bald wieder in alter Weise aufblühte.","kind":"passage","sequence":275,"address":"8.275"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0276","text":"Die\n englischen Fabrikinspektoren dagegen erklärten, der Minister besitze keine\n diktatorische Gewalt zur Suspension der Gesetze, und fuhren mit gericht-\n licher Procedur wider die Proslavery Rebellen fort.","kind":"passage","sequence":276,"address":"8.276"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0277","text":"Wozu jedoch alle Citation vor’s Gericht, sobald die Gerichte, die\ncounty magistrates [Note: Diese „county magistrates“, die „great unpaid“ wie W. Cobbett sie\n nennt, sind eine Art unbezahlter Friedensrichter, aus den Honoratioren der Graf-\n schaften gebildet. Sie bilden in der That die Patrimonialgerichte der\n herrschenden Klassen.] , freisprachen? In diesen Gerichten sassen\n die Herrn Fabrikanten über sich selbst zu Gericht. Ein Beispiel. Ein\n gewisser Eskrigge, Baumwollspinner von der Firma Kershaw, Leese\n et Co., hatte dem Fabrikinspektor seines Distrikts das Schema eines für\n seine Fabrik bestimmten Relaissystems vorgelegt. Abschlägig beschieden,\n verhielt er sich zunächst passiv.","kind":"passage","sequence":277,"address":"8.277"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0278","text":"Wenige Monate später stand ein Indi-\n viduum Namens Robinson, ebenfalls Baumwollspinner, und wenn nicht der\n Freitag, so jedenfalls der Verwandte des Eskrigge, vor den Borough Jus-\n tices zu Stockport, wegen Einführung des identischen, von Eskrigge\n ausgeheckten Relaisplans. Es sassen 4 Richter, darunter 3 Baumwollspin-\n ner, an ihrer Spitze derselbe unvermeidliche Eskrigge. Eskrigge sprach\n den Robinson frei, und erklärte nun, was dem Robinson recht, sei dem\n Eskrigge billig. Auf seine eigne rechtskräftige Entscheidung gestützt,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"266\", \"facs\": \"#f0285\"} --> führte er sofort das System in seiner eignen Fabrik ein [Note: „Reports etc. for 30th April 1849“, p. 21, 22. Vgl. ähnliche\n Beispiele ibid. p. 4, 5.] . Allerdings\n war schon die Zusammensetzung dieser Gerichte offne Verletzung des Ge-\n setzes [Note: Durch 1 und 2 Wm. IV. c. 24, s.","kind":"passage","sequence":278,"address":"8.278"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0279","text":"10, bekannt als Sir John Hobhouse’s\n Factory Act, wird verboten, dass irgend ein Besitzer einer Baumwollspinnerei oder\n Weberei, oder Vater, Sohn und Bruder eines solchen Besitzers in Fragen, die den\n Factory Act betreffen, als Friedensrichter funktioniren.] . „Diese Art gerichtlicher Farcen“, ruft Inspektor Howell aus,\n „schreien nach einem Heilmittel . . . . entweder passt das Gesetz diesen\n Urtheilssprüchen an oder lasst es verwalten durch ein minder fehlbares\n Tribunal, das seine Entscheidungen dem Gesetz anpasst . . . . In allen\n solchen Fällen, wie sehnt man sich nach einem bezahlten Richter“ [Note: l. c.] !","kind":"passage","sequence":279,"address":"8.279"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0280","text":"Die Kronjuristen erklärten die Fabrikanten-Interpretation des Akts\n von 1844 für abgeschmackt, aber die Gesellschaftsretter liessen sich nicht\n beirren. „Nachdem ich“, berichtet Leonhard Horner, „durch 10 Verfol-\n gungen in 7 verschiednen Gerichtsbezirken versucht habe das Gesetz zu\n erzwingen und nur in einem Fall von den Magistraten unterstützt wurde,\n halte ich weitere Verfolgung wegen Umgehung des Gesetzes für nutzlos.\n Der Theil des Akts, der verfasst wurde, um Uniformität in den Arbeits-\n stunden zu schaffen, existirt nicht mehr in Lancashire. Auch besitze ich\n mit meinen Unteragenten durchaus kein Mittel uns zu versichern, dass Fabri-\n ken, wo das sog. Relaissystem herrscht, junge Personen und Frauenzimmer\n nicht über 10 Stunden beschäftigen . . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":280,"address":"8.280"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0281","text":"Ende April 1849 arbeiten\n schon 118 Fabriken in meinem Distrikt nach dieser Methode und ihre\n Anzahl nimmt in der letzten Zeit reissend zu. Im Allgemeinen arbeiten\n sie jetzt 13½ Stunden, von 6 Uhr Morgens bis halb 8 Uhr Abends; in\n einigen Fällen 15 Stunden, von halb 6 Uhr Morgens bis halb 9 Uhr\n Abends“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 30th April 1849“, p. 5.] . Schon December 1848 besass Leonhard Horner eine Liste\n von 65 Fabrikanten und 29 Fabrikaufsehern, die einstimmig erklärten,\n kein System der Oberaufsicht könne unter diesem Relaissystem die exten-\n sivste Ueberarbeit verhindern [Note: „Rep. etc. for 31st Oct. 1849“, p. 6.] . Bald wurden dieselben Kinder und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"267\", \"facs\": \"#f0286\"} --> jungen Personen aus der Spinnstube in die Webstube u. s. w., bald, wäh-\n rend 15 Stunden, aus einer Fabrik in die andre geschoben (shifted) [Note: „Rep. etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":281,"address":"8.281"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0282","text":"for 30th April 1849“, p. 21.] .\n Wie ein System kontroliren, „welches das Wort Ablösung missbraucht,\n um die Hände in endloser Mannichfaltigkeit wie Karten durcheinander zu\n mischen und die Stunden der Arbeit und der Rast für die verschiednen\n Individuen täglich so zu verschieben, dass ein und dasselbe vollständige\n Assortiment von Händen niemals an demselben Platz zur selben Zeit zu-\n sammenarbeitet“ [Note: „Rep. etc. 1. Dec. 1848“, p. 95.] !","kind":"passage","sequence":282,"address":"8.282"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0283","text":"Aber ganz abgesehn von wirklicher Ueberarbeitung, war diess sog.\nRelaissystem eine Ausgeburt der Kapitalphantasie, wie sie Fou-\n rier in seinen genialsten Skizzen der „courtes séances“ nie übertroffen\n hat, nur dass die Attraktion der Arbeit verwandelt war in die Attraktion\n des Kapitals. Man sehe sich jene Fabrikantenschemas an, welche die\n gute Presse pries als Muster von dem, „was ein vernünftiger Grad von\n Sorgfalt und Methode ausrichten kann“ („what a reasonable degree of\n care and method can accomplish“). Das Arbeiterpersonal wurde manch-\n mal in 12 bis 14 Kategorien vertheilt, die selbst wieder ihre Bestand-\n theile beständig wechselten.","kind":"passage","sequence":283,"address":"8.283"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0284","text":"Während der fünfzehnstündigen Periode des\n Fabriktags zog das Kapital den Arbeiter jetzt für 30 Minuten, jetzt für\n eine Stunde an, und stiess ihn dann wieder ab, um ihn von neuem in die\n Fabrik zu ziehn und aus der Fabrik zu stossen, ihn hin und her hetzend\n in zerstreuten Zeitfetzen, ohne je den Halt auf ihn zu verlieren, bis die\n zehnstündige Arbeit vollgemacht. Wie auf der Bühne hatten dieselben\n Personen abwechselnd in den verschiednen Scenen der verschiednen Akte\n aufzutreten. Aber wie ein Schauspieler während der ganzen Dauer des\n Dramas der Bühne gehört, so gehörten die Arbeiter jetzt während 15 Stun-\n den der Fabrik, nicht eingerechnet die Zeit, um von und zu ihr zu gehn.\n Die Stunden der Rast verwandelten sich so in Stunden erzwungenen Müssig-\n gangs, welche den jungen Arbeiter in die Kneipe und die junge Arbei-\n terin in das Bordell trieben.","kind":"passage","sequence":284,"address":"8.284"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0285","text":"Bei jedem neuen Einfall, den der Kapi-\n talist täglich ausheckte, um seine Maschinerie ohne Vermehrung des\n Arbeiterpersonals 12 oder 15 Stunden im Gang zu halten, hatte der\n Arbeiter bald in diesem Stück Zeitabfall, bald in jenem seine Mahlzeit ein-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"268\", \"facs\": \"#f0287\"} --> zuschlucken. Zur Zeit der Zehnstundenagitation schrien die Fabrikanten,\n das Arbeiterpack petitionire, in der Erwartung, zwölfstündigen Arbeitslohn\n für zehnstündige Arbeit zu erhalten. Sie hatten jetzt die Medaille umge-\n kehrt. Sie zahlten 10stündigen Arbeitslohn für zwölf- und fünfzehnstün-\n dige Verfügung über die Arbeitskräfte [Note: Siehe „Reports etc. for 30th April 1849“, p. 6 und die weitläufige\n Auseinandersetzung des „shifting system“ durch die Fabrikinspektoren Howell\n und Saunders in „Reports etc. for 31st Oct.","kind":"passage","sequence":285,"address":"8.285"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0286","text":"1848.“ Siehe auch die Petition\n der Geistlichkeit von Ashton und Nachbarschaft, Frühling 1849, an die Königin.\n gegen das „shift system.“] ! Diess war des Pudels Kern,\n diess die Fabrikantenausgabe des Zehnstundengesetzes! Es waren die-\n selben salbungsvollen, Menschenliebe triefenden Freihändler, die den Ar-\n beitern 10 volle Jahre, während der Anticornlaw-Agitation, auf Heller\n und Pfennig vorgerechnet, dass bei freier Korneinfuhr eine zehnstündige\n Arbeit, mit den Mitteln der englischen Industrie, vollständig genüge, um\n die Kapitalisten zu bereichern [Note: Vgl. z. B. „The Factory Question and the Ten Hours Bill.\n By R. H. Greg. 1837.“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":286,"address":"8.286"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0287","text":"Die zweijährige Kapitalrevolte wurde endlich gekrönt durch den Ur-\n theilsspruch eines der vier höchsten Gerichtshöfe von England, des Court\n of Exchequer, der in einem vor ihn gebrachten Fall am 8. Februar\n 1850 entschied, dass die Fabrikanten zwar wider den Sinn des Akts von\n 1844 handelten, dieser Akt selbst aber gewisse Worte enthalte, die ihn\n sinnlos machten. „Mit dieser Entscheidung war das Zehnstundengesetz\n abgeschafft“ [Note: F. Engels: „Die englische Zehnstundenbill“ (in der von\n mir herausgegebenen: „Neuen Rh. Zeitung. Politisch-ökonomische\n Revue. Aprilheft 1850“, p. 13). Derselbe „hohe“ Gerichtshof entdeckte\n ebenfalls während des amerikanischen Bürgerkriegs eine Wortschraube, die das\n Gesetz gegen Ausrüstung von Piratenschiffen in’s direkte Gegentheil verkehrt.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":287,"address":"8.287"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0288","text":"Eine Masse Fabrikanten, die bisher noch das Relais-\n system für junge Personen und Arbeiterinnen gescheut, griffen nun mit\n beiden Händen zu [Note: „Rep. etc. for 30th April 1850.“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":288,"address":"8.288"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0289","text":"Mit diesem scheinbar definitiven Sieg des Kapitals trat aber sofort\n ein Umschlag ein. Die Arbeiter hatten bisher passiven, obgleich unbeug-\n samen und täglich erneuten Widerstand geleistet. Sie protestirten jetzt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"269\", \"facs\": \"#f0288\"} --> in laut drohenden Meetings in Lancashire und Yorkshire. Das angebliche\n Zehnstundengesetz sei also blosser Humbug, parlamentarische Prellerei,\n und habe nie existirt! Die Fabrikinspektoren warnten dringend die Re-\n gierung, der Klassenantagonismus sei zu einer unglaublichen Höhe ge-\n spannt. Ein Theil der Fabrikanten selbst murrte: „durch die wider-\n sprechenden Entscheidungen der Magistrate herrsche ein ganz abnormaler\n und anarchischer Zustand. Ein andres Gesetz gelte in Yorkshire, ein\n andres in Lancashire, ein andres Gesetz in einer Pfarrei von Lancashire, ein\n andres in ihrer unmittelbaren Nachbarschaft.","kind":"passage","sequence":289,"address":"8.289"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0290","text":"Der Fabrikant in grossen\n Städten könne das Gesetz umgehn, der in Landflecken finde nicht das\n nöthige Personal für das Relaissystem und noch minder zur Verschiebung\n der Arbeiter aus einer Fabrik in die andre u. s. w.“ Und gleiche Ex-\n ploitation der Arbeitskraft ist das erste Menschenrecht des Kapitals.","kind":"passage","sequence":290,"address":"8.290"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0291","text":"Unter diesen Umständen kam es zu einem Kompromiss zwi-\n schen Fabrikanten und Arbeitern, der in dem neuen zusätz-\n lichen Fabrikakt vom 5. August 1850 parlamentarisch versiegelt\n ist. Für „junge Personen und Frauenzimmer“ wurde der\n Arbeitstag in den ersten 5 Wochentagen von 10 auf 10½ Stunden erhöht,\n für den Samstag auf 7½ Stunden beschränkt. Die Arbeit muss in der\n Periode von 6 Uhr Morgens bis 6 Uhr Abends vorgehn [Note: Im Winter kann die Periode zwischen 7 Uhr Morgens und 7 Uhr Abends\n an die Stelle treten.] , mit 1½stün-\n digen Pausen für Mahlzeiten, die gleichzeitig und gemäss den Bestim-\n mungen von 1844 einzuräumen sind u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":291,"address":"8.291"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0292","text":"Damit war dem Relais-\n system ein für allemal ein Ende gemacht [Note: „The present law (of 1850) was a compromise whereby the employed\n surrendered the benefit of the Ten Hours’ Act for the advantage of one\n uniform period for the commencement and termination of the\n labour of those whose labour is restricted.“ („Reports etc. for 30th\n April 1852“, p. 14.)] . Für die Kinderarbeit blieb\n das Gesetz von 1844 in Kraft.","kind":"passage","sequence":292,"address":"8.292"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0293","text":"Eine Fabrikantenkategorie sicherte sich diessmal, wie früher, beson-\n dere Seigneurialrechte auf Proletarierkinder. Es waren diess die Sei-\n denfabrikanten. Im Jahr 1833 hatten sie drohend geheult, „wenn\n man ihnen die Freiheit raube, Kinder jeden Alters täglich\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"270\", \"facs\": \"#f0289\"} --> 10 Stunden abzurackern, setze man ihre Fabriken still“ (if the liberty\n of working children of any age for 10 hours a day was\n taken away, it would stop their works). Es sei ihnen unmöglich, eine\n hinreichende Anzahl von Kindern über 13 Jahren zu kaufen. Sie\n erpressten das gewünschte Privilegium. Der Vorwand stellte sich bei\n späterer Untersuchung als baare Lüge heraus [Note: „Reports etc. for 30th Sept. 1844“, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":293,"address":"8.293"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0294","text":"13.] , was sie jedoch nicht\n verhinderte, während eines Decenniums aus dem Blut kleiner Kinder, die\n zur Verrichtung ihrer Arbeit auf Stühle gestellt werden mussten, täglich\n 10 Stunden Seide zu spinnen [Note: l. c.] . Der Akt von 1844 „beraubte“ sie\n zwar der „Freiheit“, Kinder unter 11 Jahren länger als 6½ Stun-\n den, sicherte ihnen dagegen das Privilegium, Kinder zwischen 11\n und 13 Jahren 10 Stunden täglich zu verarbeiten, und kassirte den für\n andere Fabrikkinder vorgeschriebenen Schulzwang. Diessmal der\n Vorwand: „Die Delikatesse des Gewebes erheische eine Fin-\n gerzartheit, die nur durch frühen Eintritt in die Fabrik zu sichern“ [Note: „The delicate texture of the fabric in which they were employed requir-\n ing a lightness of touch, only to be acquired by their early introduction to these\n factories.“ (l. c. p. 20.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":294,"address":"8.294"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0295","text":"Der delikaten Finger wegen wurden die Kinder ganz geschlachtet, wie\n Hornvieh in Südrussland wegen Haut und Talg. Endlich, 1850, wurde\n das 1844 eingeräumte Privilegium auf die Departements der Seidenzwir-\n nerei und Seidenhaspelei beschränkt, hier aber, zum Schadenersatz des\n seiner „Freiheit“ beraubten Kapitals, die Arbeitszeit für Kinder von 11\n bis 13 Jahren von 10 auf 10½ Stunden erhöht. Vorwand: „Die Arbeit\n sei leichter in Seidenfabriken als in den andern Fabriken und in keiner\n Weise so nachtheilig für die Gesundheit“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1861“, p. 26.] . Offizielle ärztliche\n Untersuchung bewies hinterher, dass umgekehrt „die durchschnittliche\n Sterblichkeitsrate in den Seidendistrikten ausnahmsweise hoch und\n unter dem weiblichen Theil der Bevölkerung selbst höher ist als in den\n Baumwolldistrikten von Lancashire“ [Note: l. c. p. 27.","kind":"passage","sequence":295,"address":"8.295"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0296","text":"Im Allgemeinen hat sich die dem Fabrikgesetz unterworfene\n Arbeiterbevölkerung physisch sehr verbessert. Alle ärztlichen Zeugnisse stimmen\n darin überein und eigne persönliche Anschauung zu verschiednen Perioden hat] . Trotz des halbjährlich wieder-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"271\", \"facs\": \"#f0290\"} --> holten Protests der Fabrikinspektoren, dauert der Unfug bis zur Stunde\n fort [Note: Man weiss, wie widerstrebend die englischen „Freihändler“ dem Schutz-\n zoll für Seidenmanufaktur entsagten. Statt des Schutzes gegen französische Ein-\n fuhr dient nun die Schutzlosigkeit englischer Fabrikkinder.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":296,"address":"8.296"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0297","text":"Das Gesetz von 1850 verwandelte nur für „junge Personen und\n Frauenzimmer“ die fünfzehnstündige Periode von halb 6 Uhr Morgens bis\n halb 9 Uhr Abends in die zwölfstündige Periode von 6 Uhr Morgens bis\n 6 Uhr Abends. Also nicht für Kinder, die immer noch eine halbe\n Stunde vor Beginn und 2½ Stunden nach Schluss dieser Periode ver-\n werthbar blieben, wenn auch die Gesammtdauer ihrer Arbeit 6½ Stunden\n nicht überschreiten durfte. Während der Diskussion des Gesetzes wurde\n dem Parlament von den Fabrikinspektoren eine Statistik über die infamen\n Missbräuche jener Anomalie unterbreitet. Jedoch umsonst. Im Hinter-\n grund lauerte die Absicht, den Arbeitstag der erwachsnen Arbeiter mit\n [Note: mich davon überzeugt. Dennoch, und abgesehn von der ungeheuren Sterblich-\n keitsrate der Kinder in den ersten Lebensjahren, zeigen die officiellen Berichte des\n Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":297,"address":"8.297"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0298","text":"Greenhow den ungünstigen Gesundheitsstand der Fabrikdistrikte, verglichen\n mit „Agrikulturdistrikten von normaler Gesundheit“. Zum Beweis u. a. folgende\n Tabelle aus seinem Bericht von 1861:","kind":"passage","sequence":298,"address":"8.298"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0299","text":"| \n\n\n\n] <!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"272\", \"facs\": \"#f0291\"} --> Beihilfe der Kinder in Prosperitätsjahren wieder auf 15 Stunden zu\n schrauben. Die Erfahrung der folgenden 3 Jahre zeigte, dass solcher\n Versuch am Widerstand der erwachsnen männlichen Arbeiter scheitern\n müsse [Note: „Reports etc. for 30th April 1853“, p. 31.] . Der Akt von 1850 wurde daher 1853 endlich ergänzt\n durch das Verbot, „Kinder des Morgens vor und des Abends nach den\n jungen Personen und Frauenzimmern zu verwenden“. Von nun an regelte,\n mit wenigen Ausnahmen, der Fabrikakt von 1850 in den ihm unterworfe-\n nen Industriezweigen den Arbeitstag aller Arbeiter [Note: Während der Zenithjahre der englischen Baumwollindustrie, 1859 und\n 1860, versuchten einige Fabrikanten durch die Lockangel hoher Arbeitslöhne für\n Extrazeit die erwachsenen männlichen Spinner u. s. w. zur Verlängerung\n des Arbeitstags zu bestimmen.","kind":"passage","sequence":299,"address":"8.299"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0300","text":"Die Hand-Mule Spinners und Self-Actor Minders\n machten dem Experiment ein Ende durch eine Denkschrift an ihre Anwender,\n worin es u. a. heisst: „Grad herausgesprochen, unser Leben ist uns zur Last,\n und so lange wir fast 2 Tage die Woche (20 Stunden) länger an die Fabrik ge-\n kettet sind als die anderen Arbeiter, fühlen wir uns gleich Heloten im Lande\n und werfen uns selbst vor, ein System zu verewigen, das uns selbst und unsre\n Nachkommen physisch und moralisch beschädigt . . . . Daher geben wir hiermit\n respektvolle Notiz, dass wir von Neujahrstag an keine Minute mehr als 60 Stunden\n wöchentlich, von 6 Uhr zu 6 Uhr, mit Abzug der gesetzlichen Pausen von 1½\n Stunden, arbeiten werden.“ („Reports etc. for 30th April 1860“, p. 30.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":300,"address":"8.300"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0301","text":"Seit dem Er-\n lass des ersten Fabrikakts war jetzt ein halbes Jahrhundert ver-\n flossen [Note: Ueber die Mittel, die die Fassung dieses Gesetzes für seinen Bruch ge-\n währt, cf. den Parliamentary Return: „Factory Regulations Acts“\n (6. August 1859) und darin Leonhard Horner’s „Suggestions for Amend-\n ing the Factory Acts to enable the Inspectors to prevent Illegal\n Working, now become very prevalent.“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":301,"address":"8.301"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0302","text":"Ueber ihre ursprüngliche Sphäre griff die Fabrikgesetzgebung\n zuerst hinaus durch den ‚Printwork’s Act‘ (Gesetz über Kattundrucke-\n reien u. s. w.) von 1845. Die Unlust, womit das Kapital diese neue\n „Extravaganz“ zuliess, spricht aus jeder Zeile des Acts! Er beschränkt\n den Arbeitstag für Kinder von 8—13 Jahren und für Frauenzimmer auf\n16 Stunden zwischen 6 Uhr Morgens und 10 Uhr Abends, ohne irgend\n eine gesetzliche Pause für Mahlzeiten. Er erlaubt männliche Arbeiter\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"273\", \"facs\": \"#f0292\"} --> über 13 Jahre Tag und Nacht hindurch beliebig abzuarbeiten [Note: „Kinder von 8 Jahren und drüber sind in der That von 6 Uhr Morgens\n bis 9 Uhr Abends während des letzten Halbjahrs (1857) in meinem Distrikt abge\n rackert worden.“ („Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1857“, p. 39.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":302,"address":"8.302"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0303","text":"Er\n ist ein parlamentarischer Abort [Note: „The Printwork’s Act is admitted to be a failure, both with reference to\n its educational and protective provisions.“ („Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1862“,\n p. 52.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":303,"address":"8.303"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0304","text":"Dennoch hatte das Prinzip gesiegt mit seinem Sieg in den grossen\n Industriezweigen, welche das eigenste Geschöpf der modernen Produktions-\n weise. Ihre wundervolle Entwicklung von 1853—1860, Hand in Hand\n mit der physischen und moralischen Wiedergeburt der Fabrikarbeiter.\n schlug das blödeste Auge. Die Fabrikanten selbst, denen die gesetzliche\n Schranke und Regel des Arbeitstags durch halbhundertjährigen Bürger-\n krieg Schritt für Schritt abgetrotzt, wiesen prahlend auf den Kontrast mit\n den noch „freien“ Exploitationsgebieten hin [Note: So z. B. E. Potter in Brief an Times vom 24. März 1863. Die\nTimes erinnert ihn an die Fabrikantenrevolte gegen das Zehnstundengesetz.] . Die Pharisäer der\n „politischen Oekonomie“ proklamirten nun die Einsicht in die Noth-\n wendigkeit eines gesetzlich geregelten Arbeitstags als charakteristische\n Neuerrungenschaft ihrer „Wissenschaft“ [Note: So u. a.","kind":"passage","sequence":304,"address":"8.304"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0305","text":"Herr W. Newmarch, Mitarbeiter an und Herausgeber von\nTooke’s: „History of Prices“. Ist es wissenschaftlicher Fort-\n schritt, der öffentlichen Meinung feige Concessionen zu machen?] . Man versteht leicht, dass\n nachdem sich die Fabrikmagnaten in das Unvermeidliche gefügt und mit\n ihm ausgesöhnt, die Widerstandskraft des Kapitals graduell abschwachte,\n während zugleich die Angriffskraft der Arbeiterklasse wuchs mit der Zahl\n ihrer Verbündeten in den nicht unmittelbar interessirten Gesellschafts-\n schichten. Daher vergleichungsweis rascher Fortschritt seit 1860.","kind":"passage","sequence":305,"address":"8.305"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0306","text":"Die Färbereien und Bleichereien [Note: Der 1860 erlassene Akt über Bleichereien und Färbereien bestimmt, dass\n der Arbeitstag am 1. August 1861 vorläufig auf 12, am 1. August 1862 definitiv\n auf 10 Stunden, d. h. 10½ für Werkeltage und 7½ für Samstage herabgesetzt\n werde. Als nun das böse Jahr 1862 anbrach, wiederholte sich die alte Farce.\n Die Herrn Fabrikanten petitionirten das Parlament, nur noch für ein einziges Jahr\n länger die zwölfstündige Beschäftigung von jungen Personen und Frauenzimmern\n zu dulden . . . . „Beim gegenwärtigen Zustand des Geschäfts (zur Zeit der Baum-\n wollnoth) sei es ein grosser Vortheil für die Arbeiter, wenn man ihnen erlaube]  wurden 1860, die Spitzenfabri-\n [Running matter: I. 18] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"274\", \"facs\": \"#f0293\"} --> ken und Strumpfwirkereien 1861 dem Fabrikakt von 1850 unterworfen.","kind":"passage","sequence":306,"address":"8.306"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0307","text":"In Folge des ersten Berichts der „Kommission über die Be-\n schäftigung der Kinder“ (1863) theilten dasselbe Schicksal die\n Manufaktur aller Erdenwaaren (nicht nur Töpfereien), der Schwe-\n felhölzer, Zündhütchen, Patronen, Tapetenfabrik, Baumwollsammt-Schee-\n rerei (fustian cutting) und zahlreiche Prozesse, die unter dem Ausdruck\n „finishing“ (letzte Appretur) zusammengefasst sind. Im Jahre 1863 wur-\n den die „Bleicherei in offener Luft“ [Note: Die „Bleicher in offner Luft“ hatten sich dem Gesetz von 1860 über\n „Bleicherei“ durch die Lüge entzogen, dass sie keine Weiber des Nachts ver-\n arbeiteten. Die Lüge wurde von den Fabrikinspektoren aufgedeckt, zugleich aber\n das Parlament durch Arbeiterpetitionen seiner wiesenduftigkühlen Vorstellungen\n von „Bleicherei in offner Luft“ beraubt.","kind":"passage","sequence":307,"address":"8.307"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0308","text":"In dieser Luftbleicherei werden Trocken-\n zimmer von 90 bis 100 Grad Fahrenheit angewandt, worin hauptsächlich Mädchen\n arbeiten. „Cooling“ (Abkühlung) ist der technische Ausdruck für ihr gelegent-\n liches Entrinnen aus dem Trockenzimmer in die freie Luft. „Fünfzehn Mädchen\n in den Trockenzimmern. Hitze von 80 zu 90° für Leinwand, von 100° und mehr\n für Cambrics. Zwölf Mädchen bügeln und legen auf (die Cambrics etc.) in einem\n kleinen Zimmer von ungefähr 10 Quadratfuss, in der Mitte ein enggeschlossener\n Ofen. Die Mädchen stehn rund um den Ofen herum, der eine schreckliche Gluth\n ausstrahlt und die Cambrics rasch für die Bügelerinnen trocknet. Die Stunden-\n zahl für diese Hände ist unbeschränkt. Wenn geschäftig, arbeiten sie bis 9 oder\n 12 Uhr Nachts viele Tage hintereinander.“ (Reports etc. for 31st Oct.\n 1862“, p. 56.)","kind":"passage","sequence":308,"address":"8.308"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0309","text":"Ein Arzt erklärt: „Für die Abkühlung sind keine besondren\n Stunden erlaubt, aber wenn die Temperatur zu unerträglich wird, oder die Hände\n der Arbeiterinnen sich vom Schweiss beschmutzen, ist ihnen gestattet, ein paar]  und die Bäckerei\n [Note: 12 Stunden täglich zu arbeiten und so viel Arbeitslohn als möglich herauszu-\n schlagen … Es war bereits gelungen, eine Bill in diesem Sinn ins Unterhaus zu\n bringen. Sie fiel vor der Agitation der Arbeiter in den Bleichereien Schottlands.“\n („Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1862“, p. 14, 15.) So geschlagen von den Arbeitern\n selbst, in deren Namen es zu sprechen vorgab, entdeckte das Kapital nun, mit Hilfe\n juristischer Brillen, dass der Akt von 1860, gleich allen Parlamentsakten zum\n „Schutz der Arbeit“, in sinnverwirrten Wortschraubungen abgefasst, einen Vor-\n wand gebe, die „calenderers“ und „finishers“ von seiner Wirkung auszu-\n schliessen.","kind":"passage","sequence":309,"address":"8.309"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0310","text":"Die englische Jurisdiktion, stets getreuer Knecht des Kapitals,\n sanktionirte durch den Hof der „Common Pleas“ die Rabulisterei. „Es hat\n grosse Unzufriedenheit unter den Arbeitern erregt und ist sehr bedauerlich, dass\n die klare Absicht der Gesetzgebung auf Vorwand einer mangelhaften Wortdefinition\n vereitelt wird.“ (l. c. p. 18.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"275\", \"facs\": \"#f0294\"} --> unter eigne Akte gestellt, wovon der erste u. a. die Arbeit von Kindern,\n jungen Personen und Weibern zur Nachtzeit (von 8 Uhr Abends bis 6 Uhr\n Morgens) und der zweite die Anwendung von Bäckergesellen unter 18 Jah-\n ren zwischen 9 Uhr Abends und 5 Uhr Morgens verbietet. Auf die spä-\n teren Vorschläge der erwähnten Kommission, welche, mit Ausnahme des\n Ackerbaus, der Minen und des Transportwesens, alle wichtigern englischen\n Industriezweige der „Freiheit“ zu berauben drohen, kommen wir zurück.","kind":"passage","sequence":310,"address":"8.310"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0311","text":"Der Leser erinnert sich, dass die Produktion von Mehrwerth\n oder die Extraktion von Mehrarbeit den spezifischen Inhalt und\n Zweck der kapitalistischen Produktion bildet, abgesehn von\n einer jeden aus der Unterordnung der Arbeit unter das Kapital etwa ent-\n springenden Umgestaltung der Produktionsweise selbst. Er\n erinnert sich, dass auf dem bisher entwickelten Standpunkt nur der selbst-\n ständige und daher gesetzlich mündige Arbeiter als Waaren-\n [Note: Minuten fortzugehn … Meine Erfahrung in der Behandlung der Krankheiten\n dieser Arbeiterinnen zwingt mich zu konstatiren, dass ihr Gesundheits-\n zustand tief unter dem der Baumwollarbeiterinnen steht (und\n das Kapital hatte sie in seinen Bittschriften an das Parlament als Rembrandisch\nübergesund gemalt!).","kind":"passage","sequence":311,"address":"8.311"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0312","text":"Ihre auffallendsten Krankheiten sind Phthisis, Bron-\n chitis, Uterinkrankheiten, Hysterie in der scheusslichsten Form und Rheumatis-\n mus. Alle diese entspringen, wie ich glaube, direkt oder indirekt, aus der über-\n hitzten Luft ihrer Arbeitszimmer und dem Mangel genügender comfortabler Klei-\n dung, um sie beim Nachhausegehn während der Wintermonate vor der kaltfeuchten\n Atmosphäre zu schützen.“ l. c. p. 56, 57. Die Fabrikinspektoren bemerken\n über das den jovialen „Bleichern in offner Luft“ nachträglich abgetrotzte Gesetz\n von 1863: „Dieser Akt hat nicht nur verfehlt den Arbeitern den Schutz zu ge-\n währen, den er zu gewähren scheint . . . . er ist so formulirt, dass der Schutz erst\n eintritt, sobald man Kinder und Frauenzimmer nach 8 Uhr Abends an der Arbeit\n ertappt, und selbst dann ist die vorgeschriebene Beweismethode so verklausulirt,\n dass Bestrafung kaum erfolgen kann.“ (l. c.","kind":"passage","sequence":312,"address":"8.312"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0313","text":"p. 52.) „Als ein Akt mit humanen\n und auf Erziehung gerichteten Zwecken ist er ganz und gar verfehlt. Man wird\n es doch kaum human nennen, Weibern und Kindern zu erlauben, oder, was\n auf dasselbe hinauskommt, sie zu zwingen, 14 Stunden täglich,\n mit oder ohne Mahlzeiten, wie es sich treffen mag, und vielleicht noch längere\n Stunden zu arbeiten, ohne Schranke mit Bezug auf das Alter, ohne Unterschied\n des Geschlechts, und ohne Rücksicht auf die gesellschaftlichen Gewohnheiten der\n Familien der Nachbarschaft, worin die Bleichwerke liegen.“ („Reports etc.\nfor 30th April 1863“, p. 40.)] \n [Running matter: 18*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"276\", \"facs\": \"#f0295\"} --> verkäufer mit dem Kapitalisten kontrahirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":313,"address":"8.313"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0314","text":"Wenn in unsrer historischen\n Skizze also einerseits die moderne Industrie eine Hauptrolle spielt\n andrerseits die Arbeit physisch und rechtlich Unmündige\n so galt uns die eine nur als besondre Sphäre, die andre nur als besonders\n schlagendes Beispiel der Arbeitsaussaugung. Ohne jedoch der spätern\n Entwicklung vorzugreifen, folgt aus dem blossen Zusammenhang der ge-\n schichtlichen Thatsachen:","kind":"passage","sequence":314,"address":"8.314"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0315","text":"Erstens: In den durch Wasser, Dampf und Maschinerie zunächst\n revolutionirten Industrien, in diesen ersten Schöpfungen der modernen Pro-\n duktionsweise, den Baumwolle-, Wolle-, Flachs-, Seide-Spinnereien und\n Webereien wird der Trieb des Kapitals nach mass- und rücksichtsloser\nVerlängerung des Arbeitstags zuerst befriedigt. Die veränderte\n materielle Produktionsweise und die ihr entsprechend veränderten socialen\n Verhältnisse der Produzenten [Note: „The conduct of each of these classes (capitalists and workmen) has\n been the result of the relative situation in which they have been placed.“\n („Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1848“, p. 112.)]  schaffen erst die masslose Ausschreitung\n und rufen dann im Gegensatz die gesellschaftliche Kontrole hervor, welche\n den Arbeitstag mit seinen Pausen gesetzlich beschränkt, regulirt und uni-\n formirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":315,"address":"8.315"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0316","text":"Diese Kontrole erscheint daher während der ersten Hälfte des\n 19. Jahrhunderts bloss als Ausnahmsgesetzgebung [Note: „The employments placed under restriction were connected with the\n manufacture of textile fabrics by the aid of steam or water power. There were\n two conditions to which an employment must be subject to cause it to be inspected,\n viz. the use of steam or water power, and the manufacture of certain specified\n fibres.“ („Reports etc. for 31. October 1864“, p. 8.)] . Sobald sie\n das Urgebiet der neuen Produktionsweise erobert hatte, fand sich, dass unter\n dess nicht nur viele andre Produktionszweige in das eigentliche Fa-\n brikregime eingetreten, sondern dass Manufakturen mit mehr\n oder minder verjährter Betriebsweise, wie Töpfereien, Glasereien u. s. w.,\n dass altmodische Handwerke, wie die Bäckerei, und endlich selbst\n die zerstreute s. g. Hausarbeit, wie Nägelmacherei u.","kind":"passage","sequence":316,"address":"8.316"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0317","text":"s. w. [Note: Ueber den Zustand dieser sogenannten häuslichen Industrie äusserst\n reichhaltiges Material in den letzten Berichten der „Children’s Employment\n Commission“.] , seit\n lange der kapitalistischen Exploitation eben so sehr verfallen waren als\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"277\", \"facs\": \"#f0296\"} --> die Fabrik. Die Gesetzgebung ward daher gezwungen, ihren Ausnahms-\n charakter allmählig abzustreifen, oder, wo sie römisch kasuistisch verfährt,\n wie in England, irgend ein Haus, worin man arbeitet, nach Belieben für\n eine Fabrik (factory) zu erklären [Note: „The Acts of last Session (1864) … embrace a diversity of occupations\n the customs in which differ greatly, and the use of mechanical power to give\n motion to machinery is no longer one of the elements necessary, as formerly, to\n constitute in legal phrase a Factory.“ („Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1864“,\n p. 8.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":317,"address":"8.317"}],"sha256":"b67b55d270aac7ffb814d20a1a4db11da9abbc0cbddf1d57b62694f971650df5","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T203538592820Z_3c9f6b84","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"4) Der Arbeitstag. · reading part 1/2","source_sha256":"b67b55d270aac7ffb814d20a1a4db11da9abbc0cbddf1d57b62694f971650df5","sha256":"d82f56adb1e841c4919271c53429e72a33a68bc112e020d417549588c67746ba","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:35:38.592836+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0001","text":"## 4) The Working Day.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"8.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0002","text":"We proceeded from the assumption that labour-power is bought and sold at its value. Its value, like that of every other commodity, is determined by the labour-time necessary for its production. Thus, if the average sum of the worker’s daily means of subsistence requires 6 hours daily for its production, he must, on average, work 6 hours per day in order to produce his labour-power daily, or to reproduce the value received in its sale. The necessary part of his working day then amounts to 6 hours and is therefore, other circumstances remaining the same, a given magnitude. But thereby the magnitude of the working day itself is not yet given.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"8.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0003","text":"Let us assume that the line a--------------b represents the duration or length of necessary labour-time, say 6 hours. According as labour is prolonged beyond a b by 1, 3, or 6 hours, etc., we obtain the 3 different lines:","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"8.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0004","text":"[Figure in source: ]  represent the three different working days of 7, 9, and 12 hours. The line of prolongation b c represents the length of surplus-labour time. Since the working day = a b + b c or a c, it varies with the variable magnitude b c. Since a b is given to us, the ratio of b c to a b can always be measured. It amounts, in working day I, to ⅙, in working day II to\n\\frac{3}{6}, and in working day III to \\frac{6}{6} of a b. Since, furthermore, the proportion\n\\frac{Surplus labour-time}{Necessary labour-time} determines the rate of surplus-value, the latter is given by that ratio. In the three different working days it amounts respectively to 16⅔, 50, and 100 %. Conversely, the rate of surplus-value alone would not give us the magnitude of the working day. Were it, for example, equal to 100 %, the working day might be of 8, 10, 12 hours, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"8.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0005","text":"It would indicate that the two constituent parts of the working day, necessary labour and surplus labour, are of equal magnitude, but not how large each of these parts is.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"8.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0006","text":"The working day is therefore not a constant but a variable\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"199\", \"facs\": \"#f0218\"} --> magnitude. One of its parts is indeed determined by the labour-time required for the constant reproduction of the worker himself, but its total magnitude changes with the length or duration of surplus labour. The working day is therefore determinable, but in and for itself indeterminate [Note: “A day’s labour is vague, it may be long or short.” “An Essay on\nTrade and Commerce, containing Observations on Taxation etc.\nLondon 1770”, p. 73.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"8.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0007","text":"Although the working day is thus not a fixed but a fluid magnitude, it can, on the other hand, vary only within certain limits. Its minimum limit, however, cannot be determined. Certainly, if we set the line of prolongation b c, or surplus labour, = O, we obtain a minimum limit, namely the part of the day which the worker must necessarily work for his self-preservation. On the basis of the capitalist mode of production, however, necessary labour can always form only a part of his working day; the working day can therefore never be shortened to this minimum. By contrast, the working day possesses a maximum limit. It cannot be prolonged beyond a certain boundary. This maximum limit is determined in two ways. Firstly, by the physical limit of labour-power.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"8.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0008","text":"A human being can, during the natural day of 24 hours, expend only a definite quantum of vital force, and the measure of this expenditure of force forms a measure of his physically possible labour-time. Thus a horse can, day in, day out, work only 8 hours. During one part of the day force must rest, sleep; during another part the human being has other physical needs to satisfy, to nourish himself, cleanse himself, clothe himself, etc. Besides this purely physical limit, the prolongation of the working day encounters moral limits. The worker needs time for the satisfaction of intellectual and social needs, whose extent and number are determined by the general state of culture. The variation of the working day therefore moves within absolute physical and more or less relative social limits.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"8.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0009","text":"Both limits, however, are of a very elastic nature and allow the greatest scope. Thus we find working days of 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, and more hours, thus of the most various lengths.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"8.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0010","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"200\", \"facs\": \"#f0219\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"8.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0011","text":"The capitalist has bought labour-power at its daily value. Its use-value during one working day belongs to him. He has thus acquired the right to have the worker work for him during one day. But what is a working day [Note: This question is infinitely more important than Sir Robert Peel’s famous question to the Birmingham Chamber of Commerce: “What is a pound?”, a question which could only be asked because Peel was as unclear about the nature of money as the “little shilling men” of Birmingham.] ? In any case, less than a natural day of life. By how much? The capitalist has his own view of this ultima Thule, the necessary limit of the working day. As capitalist, he is only personified capital. His soul is the soul of capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"8.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0012","text":"Capital, however, has a single driving force of life, the drive to valorize itself, to create surplus-value, to suck up with its constant part, the means of production, the greatest possible mass of surplus labour [Note: “It is the task of the capitalist to extract from the capital expended the greatest possible sum of labour.” (“D’obtenir du\ncapital dépensé la plus forte somme de travail possible.”) J. G.\nCourcelle-Seneuil: “Traité théorique et pratique des entre-\nprises industrielles. 2ème édit. Paris 1857”, p. 63.] . Capital is dead labour which revives, vampire-like, only by sucking up living labour, and lives the more, the more of it it sucks up.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"8.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0013","text":"The time during which the worker works is the time during which the capitalist consumes the labour-power purchased by him [Note: “An Hour’s Labour lost in a day is a prodigious injury to a commercial\nstate.” “There is a very great consumption of luxuries among the\nlabouring poor of this kingdom; particularly among the manufacturing populace:\nby which they also consume their time, the most fatal of consump-\ntions.” An Essay on Trade and Commerce etc. p. 47 u. 153.] . If the worker consumes his disposable time for himself, he robs the capitalist [Note: “Si le manouvrier libre prend un instant de repos, l’économie sordide qui\nle suit des yeux avec inquiétude, prétend qu’il la vole.” N. Linguet: “Théo-\nrie des Lois Civiles etc. London 1767”, t. II, p. 466.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"8.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0014","text":"The capitalist therefore invokes the law of commodity exchange. He, like every other buyer, seeks to extract the greatest possible benefit from the use-value of his commodity. Suddenly, however, the voice of the worker rises, a voice which had been silenced in the stress and turmoil of the production process:","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"8.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0015","text":"The commodity which I have sold you differs from the other commodity rabble in that its use creates value and costs more value than it itself does. That was the reason why you bought it. What appears on your side as the valorization of capital is on my side the excessive expenditure of labour-power. You and I know only one law in the marketplace, that of commodity exchange. And the consumption of the commodity belongs not to the seller who alienates it, but to the buyer who acquires it. Therefore, the use of my daily labour-power belongs to you. But by means of its daily sale-price I must be able to reproduce it daily and thus be able to sell it anew. Apart from the natural wear and tear through age, etc.,","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"8.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0016","text":"I must be capable tomorrow of working with the same normal condition of strength, health, and freshness as today. You constantly preach to me the gospel of “economy” and “abstinence.” Very well! Like a rational, thrifty husbandman, I shall husband my sole possession, labour-power, and refrain from every mad squandering of it. Each day I shall make fluid, set in motion, convert into labour, only as much of it as is compatible with its normal duration and healthy development. By immoderately prolonging the working day, you can make fluid in one day a greater quantum of my labour-power than I can replace in three days. What you thus gain in labour, I lose in labour substance. The use of my labour-power and its robbery are entirely different things.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"8.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0017","text":"If the average period for which an average worker can live with a rational measure of labour amounts to 30 years, the value of my labour-power, which you pay me day after day, is\n\\frac{1}{365 × 30} or \\frac{1}{10950} of its total value. But if you consume it in 10 years, you pay me only \\frac{1}{3650}, or only ⅓ of its value daily, and therefore rob me daily of ⅔ of the value of my commodity. You pay me for one day’s labour-power where you consume that of three days. That is contrary to our contract and to the law of commodity exchange. I therefore demand a working day of normal length, and I demand it without appeal to your heart, for in money matters all sentimentality\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"202\", \"facs\": \"#f0221\"} --> ceases.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"8.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0018","text":"You may be a model citizen, perhaps a member of the society for the abolition of cruelty to animals, and moreover stand in the odour of sanctity, but the thing which you represent in relation to me has no heart beating in its breast. What seems to throb within it is my own heartbeat. I demand the normal working day because I demand the value of my commodity, like every other seller [Note: During the great strike of the London builders, 1860—61, for the reduction of the working day to 9 hours, their committee published a declaration which more or less amounts to the plea of our worker. The declaration points out, not without irony, that the most profit-greedy of the “building masters”—a certain Sir M. Peto—stands in the “odour of sanctity”.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"8.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0019","text":"We see: apart from quite elastic limits, the nature of commodity exchange itself yields no limit to the working day, hence no limit to surplus labour. The capitalist therefore merely asserts his right as buyer when he seeks to make the working day as long as possible and, where possible, to make two working days out of one. On the other hand, the specific nature of the commodity sold includes a limit to its consumption by the buyer, and the worker therefore merely asserts his right as seller when he wishes to restrict the working day to a definite normal magnitude. Thus an antinomy takes place here, right against right, both alike sealed by the law of commodity exchange. Between equal rights force decides.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"8.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0020","text":"And thus, in the history of capitalist production, the regulation of the working day presents itself as a struggle over the limits of the working day—a struggle between the aggregate capitalist, i.e. the class of capitalists, and the aggregate worker, or the working class.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"8.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0021","text":"Capital, as already noted, did not invent surplus labour. Wherever one part of society possesses the monopoly of the means of production, the worker, free or unfree, must add labour-time in excess of that necessary for his self-preservation, in order to produce the means of subsistence for the owners of the means of production [Note: “Those who labour . . . . in reality feed both the pensioners called\nthe rich, and themselves.” (Edmund Burke l. c. p.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"8.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0022","text":"2.)] , whether this owner be an Athenian ϰαλος ϰἀγαϑός, an Etruscan theocrat, civis romanus, Norman baron, American slaveholder, Wallachian boyar, modern landlord, or capitalist\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"203\", \"facs\": \"#f0222\"} --> [Note: Niebuhr remarks very naively in his “Roman History”: “One cannot conceal from oneself that works such as the Etruscan ones, which astonish in their ruins, presuppose corvée lords and serfs in small (!) states.” Sismondi said much more profoundly that “Brussels lace” presupposes wage masters and wage servants.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"8.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0023","text":"It is clear, however, that if, in an economic social formation, not the exchange-value but the use-value of the product predominates, surplus labour is restricted by a narrower or wider circle of needs, but no boundless need for surplus labour is given by the character of production itself. Where in antiquity we find apparent deviations from this law, they in fact constitute its most direct proof. Here, for example, overwork manifests itself most terribly where exchange-value is produced in its independent form as money, in the production of gold and silver. Violently working to death is here the official form of overwork.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"8.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0024","text":"One need only read Diodorus Siculus [Note: “One cannot look upon these unfortunates (in the gold mines between Egypt, Ethiopia, and Arabia), who are unable even to keep their bodies clean or cover their nakedness, without lamenting their miserable fate. For there is no indulgence and no mercy for the sick, for the infirm, for the aged, for female weakness. All must, compelled by blows, continue working until death puts an end to their torments and their misery.” Diod. Sic. Historical Library, Book 3, c. 13.] . But when peoples among whom production is still in the lower forms of slave labour, corvée labour, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"8.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0025","text":"moved,\n standing in the midst of a world market dominated by the capitalist mode of production, which develops the sale of their products abroad into the predominant interest, the barbarous horror of slavery, serfdom, etc., is grafted with the civilized horror of overwork. Hence Negro labour in the southern states of the American Union retained a moderately patriarchal character so long as production was chiefly directed towards immediate self-requirement. But in proportion as the export of cotton became the vital interest of those states, the overworking of the Negro, here and there the consumption of his life in seven years of labour, became a factor of a calculated and calculating system.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"8.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0026","text":"It was no longer a question of extracting a\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"204\", \"facs\": \"#f0223\"} --> certain mass of useful products from him. It was now a question\nof the production of surplus value itself. Similarly with\ncorvée labour, e.g. in the Danubian Principalities.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"8.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0027","text":"The comparison of the hot hunger for surplus labour in the Danubian\n Principalities with the same hot hunger in English factories offers\n a special interest, because surplus labour in corvée labour\n possesses an independent, sensibly perceptible form.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"8.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0028","text":"Let the working day comprise 6 hours of necessary labour and 6\n hours of surplus labour. The free worker thus supplies the capitalist weekly\n 6 × 6, or 36 hours of surplus labour. It is the same as if he worked 3\n days in the week for himself and 3 days in the week gratis for the\n capitalist. But this is not visible. Surplus labour and necessary\n labour merge into one another. I can therefore express the same rela-\n tion, for example, by saying that in every minute the worker works 30\n seconds for himself and 30 for the capitalist, etc. It is otherwise\n with corvée labour. The necessary labour which the Wallachian\n peasant, for example, performs for his own maintenance is spatially separated\n from his surplus labour for the Boyar. He performs the one on his\n own field, the other on the seigniorial estate.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"8.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0029","text":"Both parts of\n working time therefore exist independently alongside one another. Surplus\n labour, as corvée labour, is precisely separated from necessary\n labour. This different form of appearance evidently alters nothing in the\n quantitative relation of surplus labour and necessary labour.\n Three days of surplus labour in the week remain three days of labour\n which forms no equivalent for the worker himself, whether it be called\n corvée labour or wage labour. With the capitalist, however, the hot hunger\n for surplus labour appears in the urge towards boundless prolongation\n of the working day; with the Boyar, more simply in the immediate hunt\n for corvée days [Note: What follows refers to conditions in the Romanian prov-\n inces as they had taken shape before the transformation since the Crimean War.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"8.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0030","text":"Corvée labour in the Danubian Principalities was connected with pay-\n ments in kind and other appurtenances of serfdom, but constituted the\n decisive tribute to the ruling class. Where this is the case,\n corvée labour rarely arose out of serfdom; rather, conversely, serfdom\n mostly arose out of corvée labour. Thus in the Romanian\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"205\", \"facs\": \"#f0224\"} --> provinces. Their original mode of production was founded upon common\n property, but not upon common property in Slavic or indeed Indian form.\n One part of the lands was independently cultivated as free private\n property by the members of the community themselves; another part—the\n ager publicus—was cultivated jointly by them.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"8.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0031","text":"The products of this common labour served partly as a reserve fund\n against crop failures and other contingencies, partly as a public treasury to cover\n the costs of war, religion, and other communal expenditures. In the\n course of time, warlike and ecclesiastical dignitaries usurped, along\n with the common property, the services owing to it. The labour of the free\n peasants on their communal land was transformed into corvée labour\n for the thieves of the communal land. At the same time, relations\n of serfdom developed, though only in fact, not by law,\n until world-liberating Russia, under the pretext of abolishing serfdom,\n raised it into law. The code of corvée labour\n proclaimed by the Russian General Kisseleff in 1831 was naturally\n dictated by the Boyars themselves.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"8.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0032","text":"Russia thus conquered at one stroke\n these magnates of the Danubian Principalities and the applause of liberal\n cretinism throughout Europe.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"8.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0033","text":"According to the “Règlement organique,” as that code of\n corvée labour is called, every Wallachian peasant owes, apart from a mass of detailed\n payments in kind, to the so-called landed proprietor 1) 12 working days\n generally, 2) one day of field labour, and 3) one day of carting wood. Summa\n summarum, 14 days a year. With profound insight into political economy,\n however, the working day is not taken in its ordinary sense,\n but as the working day necessary for the production of an average daily product;\nbut the average daily product is cunningly determined in such a way\n that no Cyclops could get through with it in 24 hours. In the dry words\n of genuine Russian irony, the “Règlement” itself therefore declares that\n by 12 working days is to be understood the product of 36 days of manual\n labour, by one day of field labour three days, and by one day of carting\n wood likewise three times as much.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"8.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0034","text":"Sum total: 42 corvée\n days. But there is added the so-called Jobagie, services\n due to the landowner for extraordinary needs of production.\n A fixed annual quota of men, according to the number\n of the village population, is assigned to Jobagie. This additional corvée\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"206\", \"facs\": \"#f0225\"} --> labour is estimated at 14 days for every Wallachian peasant. Thus\n the prescribed corvée labour amounts to 56 working days annually. But\n owing to the bad climate, the agricultural year in Wallachia\n counts only 210 days, of which 40 are lost to Sundays and holidays, 30 on average\n to bad weather, in all 70 days. There remain 140 working days.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"8.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0035","text":"The ratio of corvée labour to necessary labour, \\frac{56}{84}, or\n 66⅔ %, expresses a much smaller rate of surplus value than that\n which regulates the labour of the English agricultural or factory worker.\n This, however, is only the legally prescribed corvée labour.\n And in a still more “liberal” spirit than English factory legislation,\n the “Règlement organique” has known how to facilitate its own evasion.\n After making 54 out of 12 days, it again determines the nominal day’s work of each\n of the 54 corvée days in such a way that a supplement must fall upon the following\n days. In one day, for example, a tract of land is to be sown\n which, for this operation, especially in maize plantations,\n demands twice as much time. The legal day’s work for individual\n agricultural labours is so interpretable that the day begins in the month of May\n and ends in the month of October.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"8.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0036","text":"For Moldavia the provisions are still harsher. “The twelve\n corvée days of the Règlement organique,” cried a Boyar intoxicated with victory,\n “amount to 365 days in the year!” [Note: Further details may be found in: “E. Regnault: Histoire poli-\n tique et sociale des Principautés Danubiennes. Paris 1855.”]","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"8.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0037","text":"If the Règlement organique of the Danubian Principalities is a\npositive expression of the hot hunger for surplus labour, which each\n paragraph legalizes, the English Factory Acts are nega-\n tive expressions of the same hot hunger. These laws counteract\n the drive of capital towards boundless sucking-out of labour-power through\nforcible restriction of the working day by the state,\n and indeed by a state ruled by capitalist and landlord. Apart\n from a workers’ movement swelling daily in a threatening manner, the restriction\n of factory labour was dictated by the same necessity which poured\n guano upon the English fields. The same blind rapacity which in the one\n case exhausted the earth had in the other seized the vital force of the nation\n at its root.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"8.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0038","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"207\", \"facs\": \"#f0226\"} --> Periodic epidemics spoke here just as distinctly as the diminish-\n ing military measure in Germany and France [Note: “In general, within certain limits, the exceeding of the average measure\n of their species testifies to the flourishing of organic beings. In the case of man,\n his bodily measure diminishes when his flourishing is impaired,\n whether by physical or social conditions. In all European coun-\n tries where conscription exists, since its introduction the average bodily measure\n of adult men, and in general their fitness for military service,\n has decreased. Before the Revolution (1789), the maximum for an infantryman\n in France was 165 centimetres; in 1818 (law of 10 March) 157, after the law\n of the 21st","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"8.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0039","text":"March 1832, 156 centimetres; on average in France, owing to\n insufficient height and infirmities, more than half are rejected. The military\n measure in Saxony was in 1780: 178 centimetres, now 155. In Prussia it is 157.\n According to information in the Bayrische Zeitung of 9 May 1862 by Dr. Meyer,\n a 9-year average shows that in Prussia, out of 1000 con-\n scripts, 716 are unfit for military service, 317 owing to insufficient height and 399\n owing to infirmities . . . . In 1858 Berlin could not furnish its contingent of replacement\n men; it lacked 156 men.” (J. v. Liebig: “Die Chemie\n in ihrer Anwendung auf Agrikultur und Physiologie. 1862.\n 7th edition.” Vol. I, p. 117, 118.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"8.39","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0040","text":"The Factory Act of 1850 now in force permits for the\n average weekday 10 hours, namely, for the first 5 week-\n days 12 hours, from 6 o’clock in the morning until 6 o’clock in the evening, from which\n ½ hour for breakfast and one hour for dinner are legally de-\n ducted, leaving 10½ working hours; and 8 hours for Saturday,\n from 6 o’clock in the morning to 2 o’clock in the afternoon, from which ½ hour for break-\n fast is deducted. There remain 60 working hours, 10½ for the first 5 week-\n days, 7½ for the last weekday [Note: The history of the Factory Act of 1850 follows in the course of this chapter.] . Special guardians of\n the law have been appointed, the factory inspectors directly subordinate to the\nHome Office, whose reports are published semi-annually by order\n of Parliament. They thus provide a continuous and official\n statistic of the capitalist hot hunger for surplus labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"8.40","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0041","text":"Let us hear the factory inspectors for a moment [Note: Concerning the period from the beginning of large-scale industry in England until 1845,\n I touch upon it only here and there  [Editorial alternatives: sic = eiu; corr = ein] , and refer the reader on this subject to:\n “The Condition of the Working Class in England. By Friedrich\n Engels. Leipzig 1845.” How profoundly Engels grasped the spirit of the capitalist mode of pro-] .","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"8.41","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0042","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"208\", \"facs\": \"#f0227\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"8.42","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0043","text":"“The fraudulent factory owner begins work a quarter of an hour,\n sometimes earlier, sometimes later, before 6 o’clock in the morning, and closes it\n a quarter of an hour, sometimes earlier, sometimes later, after 6 o’clock in the after-\n noon. He takes 5 minutes from the beginning and end of the half-hour nominally\n allotted for breakfast, and docks 10 minutes\n at the beginning and end of the hour allotted for dinner. On Saturday\n he works a quarter of an hour, sometimes more, sometimes less, after\n 2 o’clock in the afternoon. Thus his gain amounts to:","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"8.43","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0044","text":"|\n\n |","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"8.44","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0045","text":"Or 5 hours 40 minutes weekly, which, multiplied by 50 working weeks,\n after deduction of 2 weeks for holidays or occasional\n interruptions, gives 27 working days [Note: “Suggestions etc. by Mr. L. Horner, Inspector of Factories”,\n in: “Factory Regulation’s Act. Ordered by the House of Com-\n mons to be printed 9. Aug. 1859”, p. 4, 5.] .”","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"8.45","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0046","text":"[Note: duction, as shown by the Factory Reports, Reports on Mines\n etc., which have appeared since 1845; and how admirably he depicted conditions\n in detail is shown by the most superficial comparison of his work with the official Reports\n of the “Children’s Employment Commission” published 18 to 20\n years later. (1863—67.) These deal with branches of industry\n in which factory legislation had not yet been introduced by 1862, and in part\n is still not introduced. Here, therefore, the conditions described by Engels\n were not subjected to more or less great alteration from outside. My ex-\n amples I borrow chiefly from the free-trade period after 1848, that paradisi-\n cal time, of which free-trade pedlars, as loud-mouthed as they are scientifically neglected,\n prate so fabulously much to the Germans.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"8.46","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0047","text":"— Moreover,\n England figures here only in the foreground because it represents capitalist production\n classically and alone possesses a continuous official statistic of the subjects dealt with.]","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"8.47","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0048","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"209\", \"facs\": \"#f0228\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"8.48","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0049","text":"“If the working day is lengthened daily by 5 minutes beyond its normal duration, this yields 2½ days of production in the year. [Note: ‘Reports of the Insp. of Fact. for the half year ended 1856’,\n p. 34.]” “An addi-\n tional hour daily, gained by snatching a bit of time here, another bit there, makes 13 months out of the 12 months of the year” [Note: ‘Reports etc. 30th April 1858’, p. 9.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"8.49","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0050","text":"Crises, during which production is interrupted and only “short time,” only a few days in the week, is worked, naturally alter nothing in the drive to lengthen the working day. The less business is done, the greater should be the profit on the business done. The less time can be worked, the more surplus labour-time should be worked. Thus the factory inspectors report on the period of crisis of 1857—1858:","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"8.50","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0051","text":"“One may consider it an inconsistency that any overwork should take place at a time when trade is so bad, but its bad condition incites ruthless people to transgressions; they thus secure an extra profit …” “At the same time,” says Leon-\n hard Horner, “when 122 factories in my district have been entirely given up, 143 stand still and all the others are working short time, the overwork beyond the legally prescribed time is continued [Note: ‘Reports etc.’ l. c. p. 43.]”.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"8.51","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0052","text":"“Although,”\n says Mr. Howell, “in most factories, on account of the bad state of business, only half-time is worked, I receive, as before, the same number of complaints that half an hour or ¾ of an hour daily are snatched from the workers by encroachments upon the periods legally secured to them for meals and recreation [Note: ‘Reports etc.’ l. c. p. 25.]”.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"8.52","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0053","text":"The same phenomenon recurs on a smaller scale during the terrible cotton crisis of 1861 to 1865 [Note: ‘Reports etc. for half year ending 30th April 1861.’ See Ap-\n pendix N. 2; ‘Reports etc. 31st Octob. 1862’, p. 7, 52, 53. The trans-\n gressions become more numerous again with the last half-year of 1863. Cf.\n ‘Reports etc. ending 31st Oct. 1863’, p. 7.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"8.53","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0054","text":"[Running matter: I. 14]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"210\", \"facs\": \"#f0229\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"8.54","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0055","text":"“It is sometimes alleged, when we catch workers at work during meal-times or at other unlawful times, that they absolutely do not wish to leave the factory, and that compulsion is needed to interrupt their work (cleaning the machinery, etc.), especially on Saturday afternoons. But if the ‘hands’ remain in the factory after the machinery has been stopped, this happens only because no time has been allowed them between 6 o’clock in the morning and 6 o’clock in the evening, in the legally prescribed working hours, for performing such tasks [Note: ‘Reports etc. 31st Oct. 1860’, p. 23.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"8.55","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0056","text":"With what fanaticism,\n according to the judicial statements of the manufacturers, their factory hands resist every interruption of factory labour, the following curiosity shows: At the beginning of June 1836, denunciations reached the Magistrates of Dewsbury (Yorkshire), according to which the owners of 8 large factories near Batley had violated the Factory Act. Some of these gentlemen were accused of having worked 5 boys between 12 and 15 years of age from 6 o’clock on Friday morning until 4 o’clock on the afternoon of the following Saturday, without allowing any recreation, except for meals and one hour’s sleep at midnight. And these children had to perform the incessant, 30-hour labour in the “shoddy-hole,” as the den is called in which woollen rags are torn up and where a sea of dust, waste, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"8.56","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0057","text":"itself compels even the adult worker continually to tie up his mouth with handkerchiefs, to protect his lungs! The accused gentlemen assured on oath—being Quakers, they were men too scrupulously religious to take an oath—that in their great compassion they had allowed the wretched children 4 hours’ sleep, but the pig-headed children absolutely would not go to bed! The Quaker gentlemen were sentenced to a fine of 20 pounds sterling. Dryden foresaw these Quakers:","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"8.57","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0058","text":"“Fox full fraught in seeming sanctity,","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"8.58","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0059","text":"That feared an oath, but like the devil would lie,","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"8.59","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0060","text":"That look’d like Lent, and had the holy leer,","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"8.60","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0061","text":"And durst not sin! before he said his prayer!”","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"8.61","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0062","text":"]”.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"8.62","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0063","text":"“The extra profit to be made by overwork beyond the legal time seems to many manufacturers too great a temptation for them to be able to resist it. They count on the chance of not being found out and calculate that, even in the event of discovery, the smallness of the fines and court costs still secures them a balance of profit [Note: ‘Rep. etc. 31. Oct. 1856’ p. 34.]”. “Where the additional time is gained through the multipli-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"211\", \"facs\": \"#f0230\"} -->cation of small thefts (‘a multiplication of small\n thefts’) in the course of the day, the inspectors encounter almost insuperable difficulties of proof [Note: l. c. p. 35.]”.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"8.63","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0064","text":"These\n “small thefts” by capital from the workers’ meal-time and recreation-time are also described by the factory inspectors as “petty pilfer-\n ings of minutes,” pilferings of minutes [Note: l. c. p. 48.] , “snatching a few minutes,”\n snatching away minutes [Note: l. c.] , or, as the workers technically call it,\n “nibbling and cribbling at meal times [Note: l. c.]”.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"8.64","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0065","text":"One sees that, in this atmosphere, the formation of surplus-\n value by surplus labour is no secret. “If you allow me,” a very respectable factory proprietor said to me, “to have only 10 minutes of overtime worked daily, you put £1,000 annually into my pocket [Note: l. c. p. 48.]”. “Atoms of time are the elements of profit [Note: ‘Moments are the elements of profit.’ ‘Rep. of the Insp.\n etc. 30th April 1860’, p. 56.]”.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"8.65","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0066","text":"Nothing is more characteristic in this respect than the designation of workers who work full time as “full times,” and that of children under 13 years of age, who may work only 6 hours, as “half-\n times [Note: The expression has official citizenship, both in the factory and in the\n factory reports.]”. The worker here is nothing more than personified labour-\n time. All individual distinctions are dissolved into those of “full-\n timers” and “half-timers”.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"8.66","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0067","text":"The drive to lengthen the working day, the werewolf-like hunger for surplus labour, we have hitherto observed in a field where immoderate excesses, unsurpassed, so says a bourgeois English economist, by the cruelties of the Spaniards against the Red Indians of America [Note: ‘The cupidity of mill owners, whose cruelties in pursuits of gain, have\n hardly been exceeded by those perpetrated by the Spaniards on the conquest of] , finally placed capital in the chains of legal\n [Running matter: 14*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"212\", \"facs\": \"#f0231\"} -->regulation. Let us now cast a glance at some branches of production where the sucking dry of labour-power is either still unshackled today or was so only yesterday.","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"8.67","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0068","text":"“Mr. Broughton, a County Magistrate, declared as chairman of a meeting held in the town hall of Nottingham on 14 January 1860 that among the part of the town’s population occupied in lace manufacture there prevails a degree of suffering and deprivation unknown to the rest of the civilized world. . . . At 2, 3, 4 o’clock in the morning, children of 9—10 years are torn from their dirty beds and compelled to work for bare subsistence until 10, 11, 12 o’clock at night, while their limbs waste away, their figures shrink, their features become dull, and their human being entirely stiffens into a stone-like torpor, the mere sight of which is horrifying. We are not surprised that Mr. Mal-\n let and other manufacturers came forward to protest against any dis-\n cussion . . . . The system, as the Rev. Mr.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"8.68","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0069","text":"Montagu\n Valpu described it, is a system of unlimited slavery,\n slavery in social, physical, moral, and intellectual respects.\n … What is one to think of a town that holds a public meeting in order to petition that the working time for men should be restricted to 18 hours daily! . . . .\n We declaim against the Virginian and Carolinian planters. Yet is their Negro market, with all the horrors of the whip and the trafficking in human flesh, more abhorrent than this slow human slaughter which proceeds so that veils and collars may be manufactured for the profit of capitalists [Note: London Daily Telegraph of 14 January 1860.]?”","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"8.69","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0070","text":"The pottery industry (Pottery) of Staffordshire has, during the last 22 years, formed the subject of three parliamentary investigations [Note: America, in the pursuit of gold.’ John Wade: ‘History of the Middle\n and Working Classes. 3d ed. Lond. 1835,’ p. 114. The theoretical\n part of this book, a kind of outline of political economy, contains for\n its time some original material, e.g. on commercial crises. The historical part\n suffers from shameless plagiarism from Sir M. Eden’s: ‘History of the\n Poor. London 1799.’]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"213\", \"facs\": \"#f0232\"} -->. The results are set down in the report of Mr.\nScriven of 1841 to the ‘Children’s Employment Commissioners’,\n in the report of Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"8.70","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0071","text":"Greenhow of 1860, published by order\n of the medical officer of the Privy Council (Public Health, 3d Re-\n port, I, 102—113), finally in the report of Mr. Longe of 1863,\n in the ‘First Report of the Children’s Employment Commis-\n sion’ of 13 June 1863. For my purpose, a brief extract from the testimony of the exploited children in the reports of 1860 and 1863 suffices. From the children one may infer the condition of the adults, especially girls and women, and indeed in a branch of industry beside which cotton spinning and the like appear as a very pleasant and healthy occupation [Note: Cf. Engels, ‘Condition etc.’ pp. 249—51.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"8.71","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0072","text":"William Wood, nine years old, “was 7 years and 10 months old when he began to work.” He “ran moulds” (carried the finished moulded ware into the drying room, subsequently to bring back the empty mould) from the beginning. He comes every day of the week at 6 o’clock in the morning and stops at about 9 o’clock in the evening. “I work until 9 o’clock at night every day of the week. Thus, for example, during the last 7—8 weeks.” Thus fifteen-hour labour for a seven-year-old child! J. Murray, a twelve-year-old boy, testifies: “I run moulds and turn jigger (turn the wheel). I come at 6 o’clock, sometimes at 4 o’clock in the morning. I worked throughout the whole of last night until 8 o’clock this morning. I have not been in bed since last night. Besides me, 8 or 9 other boys worked through the last night. All except one came back again this morning. I receive 3 sh. 6 d. weekly.”","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"8.72","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0073","text":"(1 thaler 5 groschen). I get no more if I work through the entire night. Last week I worked through two nights.” Fernyhough, a boy of ten: “I do not always get a full hour for dinner; often only half an hour; every Thursday, Friday and Saturday [Note: “Children’s Employment Commission. First Report etc. 1863”, Appendix pp. 16, 19, 18.] .”","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"8.73","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0074","text":"Dr. Greenhow declares the lifespan in the pottery districts of Stoke-upon-Trent and Wolstanton to be extraordinarily short.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"214\", \"facs\": \"#f0233\"} --> Although in the district of Stoke only 30.6%, and in Wolstanton only 30.4%, of the male population over 20 are employed in the potteries, in the former district more than half of the deaths from chest diseases among men over 20, and in the latter approximately ⅖, fall upon potters. Dr. Boothroyd, a practising physician in Hanley, testifies: “Each successive generation of potters is more dwarfish and weaker than the preceding one.” Likewise another physician, Mr. McBean: “Since I began my practice among the potters 25 years ago, the striking degeneration of this class has progressively shown itself in diminished stature and weight.” These statements are taken from Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"8.74","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0075","text":"Greenhow’s report of 1860 [Note: “Public Health. 3d Report etc.”, pp. 102, 104, 105.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"8.75","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0076","text":"The following from the report of the commissioners of 1863: Dr. J. T. Arledge, senior physician of the North Staffordshire Hospital, says: “As a class, the potters, men and women … represent a degenerate population, physically and morally. They are as a rule dwarfish, ill-built, and often deformed in the chest. They age prematurely and are short-lived; phlegmatic and bloodless, they betray the weakness of their constitution through obstinate attacks of dyspepsia, disorders of the liver and kidneys, and rheumatism. Above all, however, they are subject to diseases of the chest—to pneumonia, phthisis, bronchitis, and asthma. One form of the latter is peculiar to them and is known under the name of potters’ asthma or potters’ consumption.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"8.76","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0077","text":"Scrofula, which attacks the glands, bones, or other parts of the body, is a disease of more than two thirds of the potters … That the degeneration (Degenerescence) of the population of this district is not yet much greater is owing exclusively to recruitment from the surrounding rural districts and to intermarriage with healthier races.” Mr. Charles Pearson, until recently House Surgeon at the same hospital, writes in a letter to Commissioner Longe, inter alia:","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"8.77","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0078","text":"“I can speak only from personal observation, not statistically, but I do not hesitate to affirm that my indignation has repeatedly boiled over at the sight of these poor children, whose health was sacrificed in order to indulge the greed of their parents and employers.” He enumerates the causes of the potters’ diseases and concludes, as their culmination,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"215\", \"facs\": \"#f0234\"} --> with “Long Hours” (“long working hours”). The commission’s report hopes that “a manufacture holding so eminent a position in the eyes of the world will not much longer bear the stigma that its great success is accompanied by physical degeneration, multifarious bodily sufferings, and early death among the working population, by whose labour and skill such great results have been achieved [Note: “Children’s Employm. Commission. 1863”, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"8.78","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0079","text":"24, 22 and XI.] ”.\n What applies to the potteries in England applies to those in Scotland [Note: l. c. p. XLVII.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"8.79","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0080","text":"The manufacture of matches dates from 1833, from the discovery of applying phosphorus to the matchstick itself. Since 1845 it has developed rapidly in England and has spread from the most densely populated parts of London, notably also to Manchester, Birmingham, Liverpool, Bristol, Norwich, Newcastle, and Glasgow; with it, jaw disease, which a Viennese physician discovered as a peculiar disease of match-makers as early as 1845. Half the workers are children under 13 and young persons under 18 years of age. On account of its unhealthiness and repulsiveness, the manufacture is so disreputable that only the most degraded portion of the working class, half-starved widows, etc., supplies children for it—“ragged, half-starved, wholly neglected and uneducated children [Note: l. c. p.","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"8.80","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0081","text":"LIV.] .” Of the witnesses whom Commissioner White examined (1863), 270 were under 18 years, 40 under 10, 10 only 8, and 5 only 6 years old. Alternation of the working day from 12 to 14 and 15 hours, night-work, irregular meals, mostly in the workrooms themselves, which are pestilential with phosphorus [Note: l. c. p. LIV.] . Dante would have found his cruelest fantasies of hell surpassed in this manufacture.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"8.81","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0082","text":"In wallpaper manufacture, the coarser kinds are printed by machines, the finer by hand (block printing). The busiest months fall between the beginning of October and the end of April. During this period the work frequently continues, almost without interruption, from 6 o’clock in the morning until 10 o’clock at night and far into the night.","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"8.82","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0083","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"216\", \"facs\": \"#f0235\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"8.83","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0084","text":"J. Leach testifies: “Last winter (1862), 6 out of 19 girls stayed away owing to illnesses contracted through overwork. In order to keep them awake, I have to shout at them.” W. Duffy: “The children often could not keep their eyes open from fatigue; indeed, we ourselves can often scarcely do so.” J. Lightbourne: “I am 13 years old … Last winter we worked until 9 o’clock in the evening, and the winter before until 10 o’clock. Last winter I used almost every evening to cry out from the pain of sore feet.” G. Apsden: “When this boy of mine was 7 years old, I used to carry him back and forth over the snow on my back, and he used to work 16 hours!”","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"8.84","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0085","text":"… I have often knelt down to feed him while he stood at the machine, for he was not permitted to leave it, or to stop it.” Smith, the managing partner of a Manchester factory: “We (he means his ‘hands,’ who work for ‘us’) work without interruption for meals, so that the day’s work of 10½ hours is finished at 4½ in the afternoon, and everything later is overtime [Note: This is not to be understood in our sense of surplus labour-time. These gentlemen regard the 10½-hour labour as the normal working day, which therefore also includes normal surplus labour. Then ‘overtime’ begins, which is paid somewhat better.","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"8.85","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0086","text":"On a later occasion it will be seen that the employment of labour-power during the so-called normal day is paid below its value, so that ‘overtime’ is a mere capitalist’s device for squeezing out more ‘surplus labour’; which, moreover, it remains even if the labour-power employed during the ‘normal day’ is really paid in full.] . (Whether this Mr. Smith himself takes no meal during 10½ hours?)","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"8.86","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0087","text":"We (the same Smith) seldom stop before 6 o’clock in the evening (he means with the consumption of ‘our’ labour-power machines), so that we (iterum Crispinus) in fact work overtime throughout the whole year … The children and adults (152 children and young persons under 18 years and 140 adults) have alike, during the last 18 months, worked on average at the very least 7 days and 5 hours per week, or 78½ hours weekly. For the 6 weeks ending on 2","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"8.87","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0088","text":"May of this year (1863), the average was higher—8 days or 84 hours per week!” Yet the same Mr. Smith, who is so devoted to the pluralis majestatis, adds with a smirk:\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"217\", \"facs\": \"#f0236\"} --> “Machine work is easy.” And so the employers of block printing say: “Hand labour is healthier than machine labour.” On the whole, the gentlemen manufacturers indignantly declare themselves against the proposal “to stop the machines at least during meals.”\n“An Act,” says Mr. Otley, the manager of a wallpaper factory in Borough, “which allowed working hours from 6 o’clock in the morning until 9 o’clock in the evening would suit us (!) very well, but the Factory Act hours, from 6 o’clock in the morning until 6 o’clock in the evening, do not suit us (!) … Our machine is stopped during dinner (what magnanimity!).”","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"8.88","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0089","text":"Stopping it causes no appreciable loss of paper and colour. “But,” he adds sympathetically, “I can understand that the loss connected with it is not liked.” The commission’s report naively thinks that the fear of some “leading firms” of losing time, i.e. time for appropriating others’ labour-time, and thereby “profit,” is not a “sufficient reason” for allowing children under 13 and young persons under 18 to “lose” their midday meal during 12–16 hours, or for giving it to them as coal and water are given to the steam-engine, soap to wool, oil to the wheel, etc.—during the production process itself, as a mere auxiliary material of the means of labour [Note: l. c. Appendix pp. 123, 124, 125, 140 and LIV.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"8.89","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0090","text":"No branch of industry in England—(leaving aside machine-made bread, which has only recently begun to make its way)—has retained down to the present day such an archaic, indeed, as can be seen from the poets of the Roman imperial period, pre-Christian mode of production as baking. But capital, as previously remarked, is at first indifferent to the technological character of the labour-process of which it takes possession. It initially takes it as it finds it.","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"8.90","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0091","text":"The incredible adulteration of bread, especially in London, was first exposed by the House of Commons Committee “on the Adulteration of Food” (1855–56) and Dr. Hassall’s work: “Adulterations detected [Note: Alum, finely ground, or mixed with salt, is a regular article of commerce, bearing the characteristic name of “baker’s stuff”.] ”. The consequence of these revelations was the Act of 6","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"8.91","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0092","text":"August 1860: “for preventing the adulte-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"218\", \"facs\": \"#f0237\"} --> ration of articles of food and drink,” an ineffective Act, since it naturally observes the utmost delicacy towards every free-trader who intends, by buying and selling adulterated wares, “to turn an honest penny [Note: Soot is known to be a very energetic form of carbon and constitutes a fertiliser which capitalist chimney-sweeps sell to English tenants. Now in 1862 there occurred a lawsuit in which the British “Juryman” was to decide whether soot to which 90% dust and sand had been added without the buyer’s knowledge was “genuine” soot in the “commercial” sense or “adulterated” soot in the “legal” sense.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"8.92","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0093","text":"The “amis du commerce” decided that it was “genuine” commercial soot, and dismissed the complaining tenant, who moreover had to pay the costs of the suit.] .” The Committee itself formulated, more or less naively, its conviction that free trade essentially means trade in adulterated, or, as the Englishman wittily calls them, “sophisticated materials.” Indeed, this kind of “sophistry” understands better than Protagoras how to make black out of white and white out of black, and better than the Eleatics how to demonstrate ad oculos the mere appearance of all reality [Note: The French chemist Chevallier, in a treatise on the “sophistications” of commodities, enumerates among 600-odd articles which he reviews, for many of them 10, 20, 30 different methods of adulteration.","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"8.93","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0094","text":"He adds that he does not know all the methods and does not mention all those he knows. For sugar he gives 6 modes of adulteration, 9 for olive oil, 10 for butter, 12 for salt, 19 for milk, 20 for bread, 23 for brandy, 24 for flour, 28 for chocolate, 30 for wine, 32 for coffee, etc.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"8.94","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0095","text":"In any case, the Committee had directed the public’s eyes to its “daily bread,” and thereby to baking. At the same time, at public meetings and in petitions to Parliament, the cry of the London journeymen bakers over overwork, etc. was heard. The cry became so urgent that Mr. H. S. Tremenheere, also a member of the repeatedly mentioned Commission of 1863, was appointed royal investigating commissioner. His report [Note: “Report etc. relating to the Grievances complained of by the Journeymen Bakers etc. London 1862” and “Second Report etc. London 1863.”] , together with the witnesses’ testimony, agitated the public—not its heart, but its stomach.","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"8.95","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0096","text":"The Bible-steeped Englishman knew indeed that man, unless called by election of grace to be a capitalist, landlord, or sinecurist, is called upon to eat his bread in the sweat of his brow; but he did not know that every day in his bread he must eat a certain quantum of human sweat, saturated with discharge from pus-filled boils, cobwebs, black beetle corpses, and rotten German yeast, apart from alum, sandstone, and other agreeable mineral ingredients. Without any regard for His Holiness, “Free Trade,” baking, hitherto “free,” was therefore subjected to the supervision of state inspectors (at the end of the parliamentary session of 1863), and by the same Act of Parliament work from 9 o’clock in the evening until 5 o’clock in the morning was prohibited for journeymen bakers under 18 years of age.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"8.96","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0097","text":"The latter clause\n speaks volumes about the revision in this branch of business, which seems so old-fashioned and homely to us.","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"8.97","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0098","text":"“The work of a London baker’s journeyman generally begins\n at 11 o’clock at night. At this hour he makes the dough, a very labo-\n rious process, lasting ½ to ¾ of an hour, according to the size of the pas-\n try and its fineness. He then lies down upon the kneading-board,\n which also serves as the lid of the trough in which the dough is made,\n and sleeps for a couple of hours with a sack of flour under his head and\n another sack of flour upon his body. Then begins a rapid and unin-\n terrupted labour of 4 hours: throwing, weighing, shaping, pushing into\n the oven, taking out of the oven, etc., the dough. The temperature\n of a bakehouse ranges from 75 to 90 degrees, and in the small bake-\n houses is rather greater than less. When the business of making bread,\n rolls, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"8.98","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0099","text":"is accomplished, the distribution of the bread begins; and a con-\n siderable part of the day-labourers, after completing the hard night-\n work described, carries the bread during the day in baskets, or\n pushes it in carts, from house to house, and in between sometimes also oper-\n ates in the bakehouse. According to the season and the extent of the business,\n work ends between 1 and 6 o’clock in the afternoon, while another\n part of the journeymen is occupied in the bakehouse until late at midnight [Note: l. c. First Report etc. p. XL.] ”.\n “During the London season, the journeymen of bakers selling at\n ‘full’ bread prices in the West End regularly begin at 11 o’clock at night, and\n are occupied with bread-baking, interrupted by one or two often very\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"220\", \"facs\": \"#f0239\"} --> short intervals, until 8 o’clock the next morning.”","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"8.99","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0100","text":"They\n are then employed until 4, 5, 6, indeed 7 o’clock in carrying bread about,\n or sometimes in baking biscuits in the bakehouse. Once the work is accom-\n plished, they enjoy sleep for 6, often only 5 and 4 hours. On Fridays\n work always begins earlier, say at 10 o’clock in the evening, and continues with-\n out interruption, whether in the preparation or in the hawking of bread, until\n the following Saturday evening at 8 o’clock, but usually until 4 or 5 o’clock into Sun-\n day night.”","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"8.100","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0101","text":"Even in the fashionable bakeries, which sell bread at\n the ‘full price’, 4—5 hours of preparatory work for the next day must again\n be performed on Sunday … The baker’s journeymen of the ‘underselling masters’ (who sell bread below the\n full price), and these amount, as remarked earlier, to more than ¾\n of the London bakers, have still longer working hours, but their work\n is almost entirely confined to the bakehouse, since their masters, delivery\n to small shops excepted, sell only in their own boutique.\n Towards the ‘end’ of the week, i.e. on Thursday, work begins here\n at 10 o’clock at night and lasts until deep into Sunday night [Note: l. c. p. LXXI.] .”","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"8.101","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0102","text":"Even the bourgeois standpoint comprehends of the ‘underselling masters’:\n “the unpaid labour of the men forms the basis of their competition” [Note: George Read: “The History of Baking. London 1848,”\n p. 16.] . And the ‘full pri-\n ced baker’ denounces his ‘underselling’ competitors to the commission of in-\n quiry as thieves of others’ labour and falsifiers. “They succeed only\n through the deception of the public and by extracting 18 hours from\n their journeymen for a wage of 12 hours [Note: Report (First) etc. Evidence. Statement of the ‘full priced baker’\n Cheeseman p. 108. That the wage for 12 hours expresses the perhaps only 6-hour\n value of labour-power, and that 6 hours are therefore ‘unpaid’ out of the\n 12, scarcely dawns upon the ‘full priced’ Mr. Cheeseman.] .”","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"8.102","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0103","text":"The adulteration of bread and the formation of a class of bakers who sell bread\nbelow the full price developed in England from the be-\n ginning of the 18th century, as soon as the guild character of the trade decayed\n and the capitalist, in the form of miller or flour-factor, stepped behind\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"221\", \"facs\": \"#f0240\"} --> the nominal master baker [Note: George Read l. c. At the end of the 17th and beginning of the 18th century\n the factors (agents) forcing their way into all possible trades were still\n officially denounced as ‘Public Nuisances’. Thus, for example, the Grand Jury at\n the quarterly session of justices of the peace in the county of Somerset issued a ‘present-\n ment’ to the House of Commons, in which, among other things,","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"8.103","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0104","text":"it says: “that these factors of Plackwell\n Hall are a Publick Nuisance and Prejudice to the Clothing Trade, and ought to be\n put down as a Nuisance.” (“The Case of our English Wool etc. Lon-\n don 1685”, p. 6, 7.)] . Thus the foundation was laid for\n capitalist production, for the boundless prolongation of the working day\n and night work, although the latter itself gained a serious foothold in London only in 1824 [Note: First Report etc. p. VIII.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"8.104","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0105","text":"From what has preceded, it will be understood that the Commis-\n sion’s report counts baker’s journeymen among the short-lived workers who,\n after luckily escaping the child-decimation normal among all sections of the working\n class, rarely reach the age of 42.\n Nevertheless, the baking trade is always overcrowded with candidates. The sources\n supplying these ‘labour-powers’ for London are Scot-\n land, the western agricultural districts of England and — Germa-\n ny.","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"8.105","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0106","text":"In the years 1858—1860, the baker’s journeymen in Ire-\n land organized, at their own expense, large meetings to agitate against\n night work and work on Sundays. The public, for example at\n the May meeting in Dublin in 1860, everywhere warmly took their side, in accordance\n with the inflammable nature of the Irishman. Exclusive day work\n was in fact successfully enforced by this movement in\n Wexford, Kilkenny, Clonmel, Waterford, etc. “At Limerick, where the\n sufferings of the wage-labourers notoriously exceeded all measure, the move-\n ment foundered on the opposition of the master bakers, especially the\n baker-millers. The example of Limerick led to regression in Ennis\n and Tipperary.”","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"8.106","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0107","text":"At Cork, where public indignation manifested itself in the liveli-\n est form, the masters frustrated the movement by using\n their power to turn the journeymen out. At Dublin, the masters offered the\n most determined resistance and, by persecuting the journeymen who stood\n at the head of the agitation, forced the rest\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"222\", \"facs\": \"#f0241\"} --> to yield, to submit to night and Sunday work [Note: “Report of Committee on the Baking Trade in Ireland\n for 1861.”] .”\n The Commission of the English government, armed to the teeth in Ireland,\n remonstrates mournfully against the inexorable master bakers\n of Dublin, Limerick, Cork, etc.: “The Committee believes that working\n hours are limited by laws of nature which cannot be violated with impunity.","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"8.107","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0108","text":"By forcing their workers, through the threat of dismissing them,\n to violate their religious convictions, to disobey the law of the land\n and to despise public opinion,” (the latter all refers to Sunday work),\n “they sow bad blood between capital and labour and give an ex-\n ample dangerous to religion, morality, and public order … The\n Committee believes that the prolongation of the working day beyond 12 hours\n is a usurpatory encroachment upon the domestic and private life of the\n worker and thus leads to disastrous moral results, through\ninterference with a man’s domesticity and the\n fulfilment of his family duties as son, brother, husband, and\n father.","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"8.108","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0109","text":"Work beyond 12 hours tends to undermine the health of the wor-\n ker, leads to premature ageing and early\n death, and hence to the misfortune of working families, who are deprived\n of the care and support of the head of the family precisely at the most necessary moment” (“are deprived”) [Note: l. c.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"8.109","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0110","text":"We have just been in Ireland. On the other side of the Channel, in\nScotland, the agricultural labourer, the man of the\n plough, denounces his 13—14 hours’ work, in the harshest climate, with four hours’\n additional work on Sunday (in this land of Sabbath-saints!) [Note: Public meeting of agricultural labourers in Lass-\n wade, near Glasgow, 5 Jan. 1866. (See “Workman’s Advocate”\n of 13 Jan. 1866.) The formation, since the end of 1865, of a Trade Union among\n agricultural labourers, initially in Scotland, is an historical event.] , while before a London Grand Jury three railway\n workers simultaneously stand: a passenger guard, a locomotive driver, and\n a signalman. A great railway disaster has dispatched hundreds of passen-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"223\", \"facs\": \"#f0242\"} --> gers into the other world.","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"8.110","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0111","text":"The negligence of the railway\n workers is the cause of the accident. They declare unanimously before the\n jurors that, 10 to 12 years ago, their work had lasted only 8\n hours daily. During the last 5—6 years it had been screwed up\n to 14, 18, and 20 hours; and with a particularly lively influx of travellers,\n as in the periods of excursion trains, it had often lasted uninterruptedly for\n 40—50 hours. They were ordinary human beings and not Cyclopes. At\n a given point their labour-power failed. Torpor seized them.\n Their brain ceased to think and their eye to see.","kind":"passage","sequence":111,"address":"8.111","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0112","text":"The altogether ‘respectable British Juryman’ answers\n with a verdict sending them before the next assizes for ‘manslaughter’ (homicide),\n and in a mild appendix expresses the pious wish that the gentlemen capital-magnates\n of the railways might in future be more lavish in purchasing the requisite number of ‘la-\n bour-powers’ and more ‘abstemious’, or ‘self-denying’, or\n ‘economical’ in the exploitation of paid labour-power [Note: “Reynold’s Paper” of 20 Jan. 1866. Week after week,\n the same weekly paper immediately brings, under the ‘sensational leadings’: ‘Fearful\n and fatal accidents’, ‘Appalling tragedies’, etc., a whole list of new railway\n catastrophes. To this a worker from the North Stafford line replies:\n “Everyone knows the consequences when the attention of locomotive driver\n and stoker slackens for a moment.”","kind":"passage","sequence":112,"address":"8.112","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0113","text":"And how is it otherwise possible with bound-] !","kind":"passage","sequence":113,"address":"8.113","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0114","text":"From the motley multitude of workers of every profession,\n age, and sex, who crowd upon us more eagerly than the souls of the slain upon\n Odysseus, and upon whom, even without the Blue Books under their arms, one sees\n overwork at first glance, we shall pick out two more figures, whose\n striking contrast proves that before capital all men are equal, — a milliner\n and a blacksmith.","kind":"passage","sequence":114,"address":"8.114","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0115","text":"In the last weeks of June 1863, all the London\n daily newspapers carried a paragraph with the ‘sensational’ heading:\n “Death from simple Overwork” (death from simple over-\n work). It concerned the death of the milliner Mary Anne\n Walkley, twenty years old, employed in a very respectable court millinery\n manufactory, exploited by a lady bearing the genial name\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"224\", \"facs\": \"#f0243\"} --> Elise. The old, often-told story was now rediscovered [Note: Cf. F. Engels l. c. p. 253, 254.] , namely that\n these girls work on average 16½ hours, but during the season often\n 30 hours uninterruptedly, their failing ‘labour-\n power’ being kept fluid by occasional supplies of sherry, port wine, or coffee.\n And it was precisely the height of the season.","kind":"passage","sequence":115,"address":"8.115","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0116","text":"It was necessary to conjure up\n the splendid dresses of noble ladies for the homage ball of the newly impor-\n ted Princess of Wales in no time. Mary Anne\n Walkley had worked 26½ hours without interruption together with 60\n other girls, 30 in each room, which afforded scarcely ⅓ of the necessary\n cubic inches of air, while at night they shared one bed two by two\n in one of the stifling holes in which a bedroom is penned off by various\n board partitions [Note: Dr. Letheby, physician then functioning at the Board of Health, de-\n clared: “The minimum for every adult in a bedroom\n should be 300 cubic feet, and in a living room 500 cubic feet of air.”\n Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":116,"address":"8.116","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0117","text":"Richardson, chief physician of a London hospital: “Sewers of every\n kind, milliners, dressmakers, and ordinary seamstresses, suffer from\n threefold misery — overwork, lack of air, and lack of nourishment or\n lack of digestion. On the whole, this kind of work suits women, under all\n circumstances, better than men. But the evil of the business is that it,\n especially in the capital, is monopolized by some 26 capitalists, who,\n through means of power arising from capital (that spring from capital),\nforce economy out of labour (force economy out of labour; he\n means, economize outlays through the waste of labour-power). This] . And this was one of the better millinery-\n [Note: boundless prolongation of work, in the harshest weather, without pause and recuperation?\n Take as an example, as occurs daily, the following case.”]","kind":"passage","sequence":117,"address":"8.117","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0118","text":"Last Monday\n a stoker began his day's work very early in the morning. He ended it after 14\n hours, 50 minutes. Before he had even time to take his tea,\n he was called back to work. He thus had to slave away uninterruptedly for 29 hours, 15 mi-\n nutes. The remainder of his week's work was made up\n as follows: Wednesday, 15 hours; Thursday 15 hours, 35 mi-\n nutes; Friday 14½ hours; Saturday 14 hours, 10 minutes; altogether\n for the week 88 hours, 40 minutes. And now imagine his astonishment when\n he received payment only for 6 working days. The man was a newcomer and asked\n what was understood by a day's work. Answer: 13 hours, thus 78 hours\n per week. But what about payment for the surplus 10 hours, 40\n minutes? After long wrangling he received a remuneration of 10 d. (not yet\n 10 silver groschen).” l. c. No. of the 4th.","kind":"passage","sequence":118,"address":"8.118","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0119","text":"February, 1866.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"225\", \"facs\": \"#f0244\"} --> dressmaking establishments of London. Mary Anne Walkley fell ill on Friday and\n died on Sunday, without, to the astonishment of Mrs. Elise, even having first\n finished the last piece of finery. The doctor, Mr. Keys, called too late to the deathbed,\n testified before the Coroner’s Jury in dry\n words: “Mary Anne Walkley died from long working-\n hours in an overcrowded workroom and an excessively cramped, badly-\n ventilated bedroom.” In order to give the doctor a lesson in good manners,\n the “Coroner’s Jury,” on the other hand, declared: “The deceased\n had died of apoplexy, but there was reason to\nfear that her death had been accelerated by overwork in an overcrowded work-\n shop, etc.”","kind":"passage","sequence":119,"address":"8.119","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0120","text":"had been accelerated.” “Our ‘white sla-\n ves,’” cried the Morning Star, the organ of the free-trade gentlemen Cobden\n and Bright, “our white slaves are worked into the grave\n and perish and die without song or sound [Note: Morning Star, 23 June 1863. The Times used the incident\n to defend the American slaveholders against Bright etc. “Very\n many of us,” it says, “think that so long as we work our own young women\n to death, with the lash of hunger instead of the crack of the whip,\n we hardly have the right to set fire and sword upon families\n who were born slaveholders and at least feed their slaves well and make them work moderately.” (Times, 2 July 1863.) In the same manner the\nStandard, a Tory paper, rebuked the Rev.","kind":"passage","sequence":120,"address":"8.120","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0121","text":"Newman Hall: “he excommunicates the\n slaveholders, but prays with the worthy people who allow the coachmen and omnibus drivers\n of London etc. to work 16 hours daily for a dog's wage”.] ”.","kind":"passage","sequence":121,"address":"8.121","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0122","text":"[Note: The power is felt throughout the entire class of women workers. If a\n dressmaker can gain a small circle of customers, competition compels her\n to work herself to death at home in order to retain it, and with the same\n overwork she must necessarily afflict her assistants. If her\n business fails or if she cannot establish herself independently, she turns to an\n establishment where the work is no less, but payment is certain. Thus situated,\n she becomes a pure slave, tossed to and fro by every fluc-\n tuation of society; now starving at home in a little room,\n or nearly so; then again employed 15, 16, indeed 18 hours out of 24,\n in scarcely tolerable air, and with food which, even when good, cannot be digested\n because of the absence of pure air.","kind":"passage","sequence":122,"address":"8.122","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0123","text":"Consumption lives on these victims,\n being nothing but a disease of the air.” (Dr. Richardson:\n “Death from simple Overwork” in the “Social Science Review”,\n July issue, 1863.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":123,"address":"8.123","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0124","text":"[Running matter: I. 15]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"226\", \"facs\": \"#f0245\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":124,"address":"8.124","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0125","text":"“Working to death is the order of the day, not only in the work-\n shop of the milliners, but in a thousand places, indeed in every place\n where business is brisk … Let us take the blacksmith as an ex-\n ample. If poets are to be believed, there is no man so\n vigorous and merry as the blacksmith. He rises early\n and strikes sparks before the sun; he eats and drinks and sleeps like\n no other man. Considered purely physically, with mod-\n erate work, he is indeed in one of the best human conditions.\n But let us follow him into the city, and see the burden of labour\n that is heaped upon the strong man, and what rank does he occupy in the mortality-\n lists of our country?”","kind":"passage","sequence":125,"address":"8.125","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0126","text":"In Marylebone (one of the largest urban\n districts of London) blacksmiths die at the rate of 31 per\n 1000 annually, or 11 above the average mortality of adult\n men in England. The occupation, an almost instinctive art of\n humanity, faultless in and for itself, becomes, through mere exaggeration of\n labour, the destroyer of the man. He can strike so many hammer-blows daily,\n take so many steps, draw so many breaths, perform so much work,\n and live, on average, say 50 years. He is compelled to strike so many more\n blows, take so many more steps, breathe so much more often\n in the day, and altogether to increase his life-expenditure\n daily by a quarter. He makes the attempt, and the result is\n that for a limited period he performs a quarter more work and dies in his 37th year\n instead of his 50th [Note: Dr. Richardson.","kind":"passage","sequence":126,"address":"8.126","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0127","text":"l. c.] ”.","kind":"passage","sequence":127,"address":"8.127","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0128","text":"Constant capital, the means of production, considered from the standpoint\n of the process of valorisation, exist only in order to absorb labour and,\n [Note: At last the oracle, Mr. Thomas Carlyle, of whom I had already\n printed in 1850: “The genius has gone to the devil; the cult remains.”\n In a short parable he reduces the sole great event of contemporary his-\n tory, the American Civil War, to the fact that Peter of the North wants by all\n means to smash in the skull of Paul of the South, because Peter\n of the North “hires” his worker “daily” and Paul of the South for “life-\n time.” (Macmillian’s Magazine. Ilias Americana in\n nuce. August issue 1863.) Thus at last the soap-bubble of Tory sympathy for\n the urban, by no means the rural! — wage-worker has burst.","kind":"passage","sequence":128,"address":"8.128","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0129","text":"The kernel is called — slavery!]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"227\", \"facs\": \"#f0246\"} --> to suck in, with every drop of labour, a proportional quantum of surplus-\n labour. So far as they do not do this, their mere\n existence constitutes a negative loss for the capitalist, because they\n represent, during the time they lie idle, an unproductive advance of capital,\n and this loss becomes positive as soon as they require additional outlays during the in-\n tervals of the labour process in order to keep them in running order for\n the resumption of work. The prolongation of the working day beyond the limits of the natural\n day into the night acts only as a palliative, only approximately\n assuages the vampire-thirst for living labour-blood. To appropriate labour during\nall 24 hours of the day is therefore the immanent drive\n of capitalist production.","kind":"passage","sequence":129,"address":"8.129","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0130","text":"But since it is physically impossible for the\n same labour-powers to be continuously exhausted day and night,\n overcoming the physical obstacle requires alternation between the labour-\n powers consumed by day and those consumed by night,\n an alternation that permits different methods; it can, for example, be so ar-\n ranged that one part of the working personnel performs day service one week,\n night service the other week, etc. It is known that this relay-\n system, this alternating economy, prevailed in the full-blooded youthful\n period of the English cotton industry etc., and among other places\n flourishes at present in the cotton-spinning mills of Moscow. As a sys-\n tem, this 24-hour process of production still exists today in many hitherto\n “free” branches of industry in Great Britain, among others","kind":"passage","sequence":130,"address":"8.130","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0131","text":"in the blast-\n furnaces, forges, rolling mills, and other metal manufactures of Eng-\n land, Wales, and Scotland. Here the labour process, besides the\n 24 hours of the 6 working days, largely also encompasses the 24 hours\n of Sunday. The workers consist of men and women, adults\n and children of both sexes. The ages of the children and young per-\n sons run through all intermediate stages from 8 (in some cases from 6)\n to the 18th year [Note: “Children’s Employment Commission.” Third Report.\n Lond. 1864, p. IV, V, VII.] . In some branches girls\n and women also work at night together with the male personnel [Note: “Both in Staffordshire and in South Wales young girls and women are\n employed on the pit banks and on the coke heaps, not only by day, but also by] .","kind":"passage","sequence":131,"address":"8.131","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0132","text":"[Running matter: 15*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"228\", \"facs\": \"#f0247\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":132,"address":"8.132","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0133","text":"Apart from the general harmful effects of night work [Note: “It seems natural,” remarked a steel manufacturer who employs children for\n night work, “that the boys who work at night do not sleep during the day\n and find no proper rest, but run about restlessly the next\n day.” l. c. Fourth Rep. 63, p. XIII. On the importance of\nsunlight for the preservation and development of the body, a physician remarks\n among other things: “Light acts also directly upon the tissues of the body, to which it gives hardness and\n elasticity.","kind":"passage","sequence":133,"address":"8.133","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0134","text":"The muscles of animals deprived of the normal quantum\n of light become spongy and inelastic, nervous force loses\n its tone for want of stimulation, and the development of everything that is in\n growth is stunted … In the case of children, constant\n access to abundant daylight and direct rays of the sun during a\n part of the day is absolutely essential for health. Light helps to process food\n into good plastic blood and hardens the fibre after it has been formed.\n It acts likewise as a stimulant upon the organs of sight and thereby calls forth greater\n activity in various functions of the brain.” Mr. W.","kind":"passage","sequence":134,"address":"8.134","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0135","text":"Strange, chief physician\n of the Worcester “General Hospital,” from whose writing on “Health”\n (1864) this passage is taken, writes in a letter to one of the investigating\n commissioners, Mr. White: “I formerly had occasion in Lancashire to observe\n the effects of night work upon factory children, and contrary to the popular assurance of some employers,\n I declare decidedly that the children’s health suffers severely from it.” (l. c. 284, p. 55.) That such matters can at all form the subject of serious\n controversies best shows how capitalist production acts upon the “brain functions” of\n capitalists and their retainers.] , the uninterrupted, twenty-four-hour duration of the\n process of production offers a most welcome opportunity to overstep the limit of the nomi-\n nal working day. For example,","kind":"passage","sequence":135,"address":"8.135","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0136","text":"in the aforementioned,\n very strenuous branches of industry, the official working day for\n each worker is generally 12 hours, night hours or day hours.\n But overwork beyond this limit is in many cases, to use\n the words of the English official report, “truly\n [Note: night. This practice has been often noticed in Reports presented to Parliament,\n as being attended with great and notorious evils. These females, employed with\n the men, hardly distinguished from them in their dress, and begrimed with dirt\n and smoke, are exposed to the deterioration of character arising from the loss of\n self-respect which can hardly fail to follow from their unfeminine occupation.”\n l. c. 194, p. XXVI. Cf. Fourth Report (1865) 61, p. XIII. Likewise in\nGlass factories.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"229\", \"facs\": \"#f0248\"} -->fearful” (“truly fearful”) [Note: l.","kind":"passage","sequence":136,"address":"8.136","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0137","text":"c. 57, p. XII.] . “No human mind,”\n it says, “can contemplate the mass of work which, according to the testimony,\n is performed by boys of 9 to 12 years, without irresistibly\n coming to the conclusion that this abuse of power by parents and employers\n must no longer be permitted [Note: l. c. (4th Rep. 1865) 58, p. XII.] .”","kind":"passage","sequence":137,"address":"8.137","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0138","text":"“The method of allowing boys in general to work alternately by day and night\n leads, both during the pressure of business and during\n the ordinary course of things, to shameful prolongation of the\n working day. In many cases this prolongation is not merely cruel,\n but downright incredible. It cannot fail that,\n from one cause or another, a relief boy is absent here and there.\n One or more of the boys present, who have already completed their working day,\n must then make good the deficiency. This system\n is so generally known that the manager of a rolling mill, when I asked\n how the place of absent replacement boys would be filled, an-\n swered: I know well that you know this just as well as I do, and he\n did not hesitate to admit the fact [Note: l. c.] .”","kind":"passage","sequence":138,"address":"8.138","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0139","text":"“In a rolling mill, where the nominal working day for the individual\n worker was 11½ hours, a boy worked 4 nights every week\n until at least 8½ o’clock in the evening of the following day, and this during the\n 6 months for which he was engaged.” “Another, at the age of\n 9 years, sometimes worked three twelve-hour relief shifts\n consecutively, and at the age of 10, two days and two\n nights consecutively.” “A third, now 10 years old, worked\n from 6 o’clock in the morning until 12 o’clock at night for three nights and\n until 9 o’clock in the evening during the other nights.” “A fourth, now 13\n years old, worked from 6 o’clock in the afternoon until 12 o’clock\n midday the next day, during an entire week, and sometimes three relief shifts\n consecutively, from Monday morning until Tuesday night.” “A fifth,\n now 12 years old, worked in an iron foundry at Stavely from 6 o’clock\n in the morning until 12 o’clock at night","kind":"passage","sequence":139,"address":"8.139","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0140","text":"for 14 days, is incapable of doing it longer.” “George Allinsworth, nine years old: “I came here\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"230\", \"facs\": \"#f0249\"} --> last Friday. The next day we had to begin at 3 o’clock in the morning.\n I therefore stayed here the whole night. I live 5 miles from here. Slept\n on the floor with an apron under me and a small jacket over\n me. The two other days I was here at 6 o’clock in the morning. Yes! this\n is a hot place! Before I came here, I likewise worked\nfor a whole year in a blast-furnace. It was a\n very large work, in the country. There, too, I began on Saturday morning at\n 3 o’clock, but I could at least go home to sleep, because it was near\n by. On other days I began at 6 o’clock in the morning and ended at 6 or\n 7 o’clock in the evening, etc. [Note: l. c. p. XIII.","kind":"passage","sequence":140,"address":"8.140","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0141","text":"The educational level of these “labour-powers” must naturally\n be such as it appears in the following dialogues with one of the investigating commissioners!\n “Jeremias Haynes, 12 years old: ‥ Four times four is eight, but\n four fours (4 fours) are 16 … A king is him that has all the money and gold.\n (A king is him that has all the money and gold.) We have a king,\n they say he is a queen, they call her Princess Alexandra. They say\n she married the queen’s son. A princess is a man.” Wm. Turner,\n twelve years old: “Do not live in England.","kind":"passage","sequence":141,"address":"8.141","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0142","text":"Think there is such a country, knew\n nothing of it before.” John Morris, 14 years old: “Have heard say that God\n made the world and that all the people were drowned, except one; have heard that\n one was a little bird.” William Smith, fifteen years old: “God made\n the man; the man made the woman.” Edward Taylor, fifteen years old:\n “Know nothing of London.” Henry Matthewman, seventeen years old: “Go\n sometimes to church … A name they preached about was a certain\n Jesus Christ, but I cannot name any others, and I cannot say anything\n about him either. He was not murdered, but died like other people. He\n was not like other people in a certain way, because he was religious in a certain way,\n and others is n’t.” (He was not the same as other people in some ways,\n because he was religious in some ways, and others is n’t.“ (l. c. 74, p. XV.)","kind":"passage","sequence":142,"address":"8.142","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0143","text":"“The\n Devil is a good person. I do not know where he lives. Christ was a bad\n fellow.” (“The devil is a good person. I don’t know where he lives.” “Christ\n was a wicked man.”) “This girl (10 years old) spells God Dog and\n did not know the name of the Queen.” (“Ch. Empl. Comm. V. Rep. 1866,\n p. 55, n. 278.) The same system which prevails in the metal manufactures\n mentioned prevails in the glass and paper factories. In the paper factories, where\n paper is made by machinery, night-work is the rule for all\n processes except that of rag-sorting. In some cases night-\nwork, by means of relays, is continued uninterruptedly throughout the whole week,\n usually from Sunday night until 12 o’clock at night on the following Saturday. The\n crew on the day shift works 5 days of 12 and one] .”","kind":"passage","sequence":143,"address":"8.143","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0144","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"231\", \"facs\": \"#f0250\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":144,"address":"8.144","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0145","text":"Let us now hear how capital itself conceives this twenty-four-\nhour system. The exaggerations of the system, its abuse for the “cruel and\n incredible” prolongation of the working day, it naturally\n passes over in silence. It speaks only of the system in\n its “normal” form.","kind":"passage","sequence":145,"address":"8.145","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0146","text":"“Messrs. Naylor and Vickers, steel manufacturers, who employ between\n 600 and 700 persons, and among these only 10% under 18 years of\n age, and of these again only 20 boys in the night personnel, express themselves\n as follows: ‘The boys do not suffer at all from the heat. The tem-\n perature is probably 86° to 90°. . . . . In the hammer and\n rolling mills the hands work by relays day and night, but,\n on the other hand, all the other work is day-work, from 6 o’clock in the morning to\n 6 o’clock in the evening. In the smithy work is carried on from 12 o’clock to 12 o’clock.\nSome hands work continuously at night without\n alternation between day and night time . . . . We find not\n that day- or night-work makes any difference to the health\n(of Messrs. Naylor and Vickers?)","kind":"passage","sequence":146,"address":"8.146","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0147","text":"makes, and probably they sleep\n [Note: of 18 hours, and that of the night shift 5 nights of 12 hours and one of 6\n hours, in each week. In other cases each shift works 24 hours, one after the other,\n on alternate days. One shift works 6 hours on Monday, and 18 on Saturday,\n in order to make up 24 hours. In other cases an intermediate system is introduced,\n in which all those employed at the paper-making machinery work 15—16 hours\n every day of the week. This system, says investigating commissioner Lord, appears\n to unite all the evils of the twelve-hour and the twenty-four-hour relay systems. Children\n under 13 years, young persons under 18, and women work under this night system. Sometimes,\n in the twelve-hour system, they had, owing to the absence of the relievers, to work the\n double shift of 24 hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":147,"address":"8.147","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0148","text":"Testimonies prove that\n boys and girls very often work overtime, which not infrequently extends to 24,\n indeed 36 hours of uninterrupted labour. In the “continuous and\n unchanging” process of the glazing rooms, one finds girls of 12 years\n who work 14 hours daily throughout the whole month, “without any regular\n rest or interruption except two, at most three half-hour breaks\n for meals.” In some factories where regular night-\nwork has been entirely abandoned, a frightful amount of overtime is worked, and “this frequently\n in the dirtiest, hottest, and most monotonous processes.” (“Children’s\n Employment Commission. Report IV, 1865,” p. XXXVIII and\n XXXIX.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"232\", \"facs\": \"#f0251\"} --> better if they enjoy the same period of rest than if it\n alternates . . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":148,"address":"8.148","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0149","text":"About twenty boys under 18 years of age work\n with the night crew . . . . We could not properly do it (not well do)\n without the night-work of boys under 18. Our objection is—the\n increase in the cost of production . . . . Skilled\n hands and heads of departments are difficult to obtain, but boys\n can be got as many as one wants . . . . Naturally, in view of the small\n proportion of boys that we employ, restrictions\n on night-work would be of little importance or interest for\n us [Note: Fourth Report etc. 1865, 79, p. XVI.] .”","kind":"passage","sequence":149,"address":"8.149","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0150","text":"“Mr. J. Ellis, of the firm of Messrs. John Brown et Co.,\n steel and iron works, who employ 3,000 men and boys, namely\n for part of the heavy steel and iron work ‘day and night, in relays,’\n declares that in the heavy steel works there are one or two boys\n to every two men. Their business counts 500 boys under 18 years of age,\n and of these about ⅓, or 170, under 13 years. With reference to\n the proposed alteration of the law, Mr. Ellis thinks: ‘I do not believe\n that it would be very objectionable (very objectionable) not to\n let any person under 18 years work more than 12 hours out of the 24.\n But I do not think that any line can be drawn as to the dispens-\n ability of boys over 12 years for night-work.","kind":"passage","sequence":150,"address":"8.150","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0151","text":"We would\nrather accept a law not to employ boys under 13 years, or\neven as high as 14 years, at all, than a prohi-\nbition on using during the night the boys whom we already have.\n The boys who are in the day shift must also work alternately in the night shift,\n because the men cannot perform night-work continuously;\n it would ruin their health. We believe, however, that night-\nwork, when the week alternates for it, does no harm. (Messrs.\n Naylor and Vickers believed, in agreement with the best interests\n of their business, the reverse: that, instead of continuous, precisely periodically\n alternating night-work might possibly do harm.)\n We find the people who perform alternating night-work exactly\n as healthy as those who work only by day . . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":151,"address":"8.151","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0152","text":"Our objections\n to the non-employment of boys under 18 years for night-work\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"233\", \"facs\": \"#f0252\"} --> would be made on account of the increase of expense, but this\n is also the only reason. (How cynically naïve!) We believe that this\n increase would be greater than the business (the trade), with due\n regard to its successful execution, could fairly bear.\n (As the trade with due regard to etc. could fairly bear! What broad-\nmouthed phraseology!) Labour is scarce here, and might become insufficient\n under such a regulation” (i.e. Ellis, Brown et Co. might come\n into the fatal predicament of having to pay the value of labour-power in full)\n [Note: l. c. 80.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":152,"address":"8.152","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0153","text":"The “Cyclops Steel and Iron Works” of Messrs. Cammell et Co.\n are carried on upon the same large scale as those of the said\n John Brown et Co. The managing director had submitted his testimony\n in writing to the government commissioner White, but later found it\n suitable to suppress the manuscript returned to him for revision. Yet Mr.\n White has a tenacious memory. He remembers quite exactly that for these\n gentlemen Cyclopes the prohibition of night-work by children and young persons was\n “a thing of impossibility; it would be the same as stopping their works,”\n and yet their business counts little more than 6% boys under 18, and only\n 1% under 13 years [Note: l. c. 82.] !","kind":"passage","sequence":153,"address":"8.153","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0154","text":"On the same subject Mr. E. F. Sanderson, of\n the firm Sanderson, Bros. et Co., steel rolling and forging works, in\n Attercliffe, declares: “Great difficulties would arise from prohibiting\n boys under 18 years from working at night; the chief diffi-\n culty from the increase in costs which replacing boys’\n labour by men’s labour would necessarily entail. How much that\n would amount to I cannot say, but it would probably not be\n so much that the manufacturer could raise the price of steel, and consequently\n the loss would fall upon him, since the men (what a perverse people!) would\n naturally refuse to bear it.” Mr. Sanderson does not know how much\n he pays the children, but “perhaps it amounts to 4 to 5 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":154,"address":"8.154","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0155","text":"per\n head per week … Boys’ labour is of a kind for which in gen-\n eral (“generally,” naturally not always “in particular”)\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"234\", \"facs\": \"#f0253\"} --> the strength of the boys is just sufficient, and consequently no\n profit would flow from the greater strength of the men in order\n to compensate for the loss, or only in the few cases\n where the metal is very heavy. The men would also like it less to have no\n boys under them, since men are less obedient. Moreover, the boys must\n begin young in order to learn the business. Restricting the boys to mere\n day-work would not fulfil this purpose.” And why not? Why can boys\n not learn their trade by day? Your reason?","kind":"passage","sequence":155,"address":"8.155","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0156","text":"“Because in that case the\n men, who work in alternating weeks now by day, now by night,\n would be separated from the boys of their shift during the same time,\n and would lose half the profit which they get out of them. For the instruction\n they give the boys is reckoned as part of the wages of these\n boys and therefore enables the men to obtain the boys’ labour more cheaply.\n Every man would lose half his profit.\n (In other words, Messrs. Sanderson would have to pay a part of the wages\n of the adult men out of their own pockets, instead of with the night-work\n of the boys.","kind":"passage","sequence":156,"address":"8.156","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0157","text":"The profit of Messrs. Sanderson would on\n this occasion fall somewhat, and this is Sanderson’s\n good reason why boys cannot learn their trade by day\n [Note: “In our reflective and reasoning age, a man must not have got very far who\n does not know how to give a good reason for everything, even for the worst and most\n perverse. Everything that has been corrupted in the world has been\n corrupted for good reasons.” (Hegel\n l. c. p. 249.)] .) Besides, this would throw regular night-work upon the\n men, who are now relieved by the boys, and they would not endure\n it. In short, the difficulties would be\n so great that they would probably lead to the complete suppression\n of night-work.” “As regards the production of steel\n itself,” says E. F.","kind":"passage","sequence":157,"address":"8.157","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0158","text":"Sanderson, “it would not make the slightest\n difference, but!” But Messrs. Sanderson have more to do than make\n steel. Steel-making is merely a pretext for profit-making. The\n melting furnaces, rolling mills, etc., the buildings, the\n machinery, the iron, the coal, etc., have more to do than transform themselves\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"235\", \"facs\": \"#f0254\"} --> into steel. They are there to absorb surplus-labour, and naturally\n absorb more of it in 24 hours than in 12. They do indeed give the Sandersons,\n by God’s and right’s warrant, an order upon the labour-time of a certain\n number of hands for the full 24 hours of the day, and lose their\n capital character, and are therefore a pure loss to the Sandersons,\n as soon as their function of absorbing labour is interrupted.","kind":"passage","sequence":158,"address":"8.158","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0159","text":"“But then there would be the loss of so much costly machinery, which would lie idle half the time, and for such a mass of products as we are capable of turning out under the present system, we should have to double our premises and machinery, which would double the outlay.” But why should precisely these Sandersons enjoy a privilege of exploiting labour over the other capitalists, who are permitted to have work done only by day and whose buildings, machinery, and raw material therefore lie “idle” at night? “It is true,” E. F. Sanderson replies on behalf of all the Sandersons, “it is true that this loss from idle machinery affects all manufactures in which work is done only by day. But in our case the use of the smelting furnaces would cause an extra loss.”","kind":"passage","sequence":159,"address":"8.159","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0160","text":"If they are kept going, fuel is wasted (instead of, as now, the workers’ material of life being wasted); and if they are not kept going, this entails loss of time in rekindling the fire and in attaining the necessary degree of heat (while the loss, even for eight-year-olds, of sleeping time is gain of working time for the Sanderson clan); and the furnaces themselves would suffer from the change of temperature” (while those same furnaces suffer nothing from the change between day and night work) [Note: l. c. p. 85.","kind":"passage","sequence":160,"address":"8.160","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0161","text":"To similar tender concern on the part of the gentlemen glass manufacturers, that “regular meals” for the children are “impossible,” because thereby a definite “quantity of heat” radiated by the furnaces would be “a pure loss” to them or would be “wasted,” Commissioner of Inquiry White replies, entirely unmoved, like Ure, Senior, etc., and their petty German echoes, such as Roscher, etc., by the “abstinence,” “renunciation,” and “thrift” of the capitalists in the expenditure of their money, and by their Timur-Tamerlane-like “wastefulness” of the lives of other human beings, as follows: “A certain quantity of heat may be wasted beyond the present measure, as a result of securing] .","kind":"passage","sequence":161,"address":"8.161","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0162","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"236\", \"facs\": \"#f0255\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":162,"address":"8.162","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0163","text":"“What is a working day?” How great is the period during which capital may consume the labour-power whose daily value it pays? How far may the working day be extended beyond the labour-time necessary for the reproduction of labour-power itself? To these questions, as we have seen, capital replies: the working day daily comprises a full 24 hours, after deduction of the few hours of rest without which labour-power absolutely refuses to renew its service. In the first place, it goes without saying that the worker, throughout his entire life-day, is nothing but labour-power, that therefore all his disposable time is by nature and by right labour-time, and thus belongs to the self-valorization of capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":163,"address":"8.163","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0164","text":"Time for human education, for intellectual development, for the fulfilment of social functions, for [Note: regular meals, but even in monetary value it is nothing compared with the waste of vital power (“the waste of animal power”) which now accrues to the Kingdom from the fact that children employed in glassworks and still growing find no period of rest in which conveniently to take and digest their food.” (l. c. p. XLV.) And this in the “year of progress” 1865! Apart from the expenditure of strength in lifting and carrying, such a child, in the works making bottles and flint glass, walks 15 to 20 (English) miles in 6 hours during the continuous performance of its work! And the work often lasts 14 to 15 hours! In many of these glassworks, as in the spinning mills of Moscow, the system of six-hour relays prevails.","kind":"passage","sequence":164,"address":"8.164","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0165","text":"“During the working time of the week six hours are the utmost uninterrupted period of rest, and from this must be deducted the time to go to and from the factory, washing, dressing, eating, all of which takes time. Thus in fact only the very shortest period of rest remains. No time for play and fresh air, except at the expense of sleep, so indispensable for children performing such strenuous work in such a hot atmosphere . . . . Even the brief sleep is interrupted by the fact that the child must wake itself at night, or is awakened by outside noise during the day.” Mr. White gives cases where a boy worked 36 hours consecutively; others where boys of 12 toiled until two o’clock at night and then slept in the works until 5 o’clock in the morning (3 hours!), in order to begin the day’s work anew!","kind":"passage","sequence":165,"address":"8.165","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0166","text":"“The mass of labour,” say the editors of the general report, Tremenheere and Tufnell, “which boys, girls, and women perform in the course of their daily or nightly spell of labour, is fabulous.” (l. c. XLIII and XLIV.) Meanwhile, perhaps late one evening, the “self-denying” glass capital, tipsy with port, staggers home from the club, idiotically humming to itself: “Britons never, never shall be slaves!”]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"237\", \"facs\": \"#f0256\"} --> social intercourse, for the free play of the physical and intellectual vital powers, even the time for Sunday celebration—and were it in the land of Sabbath-holy ones [Note: In England, for example, here and there in the countryside a worker is still sentenced to imprisonment for desecration of the Sabbath by working in the little garden in front of his house.","kind":"passage","sequence":166,"address":"8.166","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0167","text":"The same worker is punished for breach of contract if on Sunday, even out of religious whims, he stays away from the metalworks, paper mill, or glassworks. The orthodox Parliament has no ear for desecration of the Sabbath when it takes place in capital’s “valorization process.” A memorandum (August 1863) of the London journeymen fishmongers and poulterers, demanding the abolition of Sunday work, states that their work lasts, during the first 6 days of the week, on average 15 hours daily, and on Sunday 8–10 hours. At the same time, it may be gathered from this memorandum that it is particularly the ticklish gourmandise of the aristocratic hypocrites of Exeter Hall that encourages this “Sunday work.”","kind":"passage","sequence":167,"address":"8.167","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0168","text":"These “saints,” so zealous “in cute curanda,” prove their Christianity by the resignation with which they endure the overwork, privation, and hunger of third persons. “Obsequium ventris istis (the workers) perniciosius est.”] — pure nonsense! But in its immeasurably blind drive, its werewolf-like hunger for surplus-labour, capital overleaps not only the moral but also the purely physical maximum limits of the working day. It usurps the time for the growth, development, and healthy maintenance of the body. It steals the time required for the consumption of fresh air and sunlight. It stints on meal-time and, where possible, incorporates it into the production process itself, so that food is added to the worker, as a mere means of production, like coal to the steam boiler and tallow or oil to the machinery.","kind":"passage","sequence":168,"address":"8.168","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0169","text":"It reduces healthy sleep, for the gathering, renewal, and refreshment of vital power, to as many hours of dull torpor as are indispensable for reviving an absolutely exhausted organism. Instead of the normal preservation of labour-power determining the limit of the working day, the greatest daily possible expenditure of labour-power, however morbidly violent and painful it may be, determines, conversely, the limit of the worker’s period of rest. Capital does not ask about the duration of life of labour-power. What interests it is solely and exclusively the maximum of labour-power that can be made fluid in a working day.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"238\", \"facs\": \"#f0257\"} --> It attains this end by shortening the duration of labour-power, just as an avaricious farmer attains an increased yield from the soil by robbing it of its fertility.","kind":"passage","sequence":169,"address":"8.169","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0170","text":"Capitalist production, which is essentially production of surplus-value, absorption of surplus-labour, thus produces, through the prolongation of the working day, not only the atrophy of human labour-power, which it deprives of its normal moral and physical conditions of development and activity. It produces the premature exhaustion and destruction of labour-power itself [Note: “We have given in our previous reports the statements of several experienced manufacturers to the effect that over-hours . . . . certainly tend prematurely to exhaust the working power of the men.” l. c. 64, p. XIII.] . It prolongs the worker’s production time during a given term by shortening his life-time.","kind":"passage","sequence":170,"address":"8.170","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0171","text":"But the value of labour-power includes the value of the commodities required for the reproduction of the worker or for the propagation of the working class. If, therefore, the unnatural prolongation of the working day, which capital necessarily pursues in its boundless drive for self-valorization, shortens the life span of the individual workers and with it the duration of their labour-power, more rapid replacement of those worn out becomes necessary, hence the incurring of greater wear-and-tear costs in the reproduction of labour-power, just as the portion of value of a machine to be reproduced daily is so much greater the more rapidly it wears out. Capital thus seems to be directed, by its own interest, toward a normal working day.","kind":"passage","sequence":171,"address":"8.171","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0172","text":"The slaveholder buys his worker as he buys his horse. With the slave he loses capital which must be replaced by a new outlay on the slave market. But “the rice fields of Georgia and the swamps of the Mississippi may act fatally destructively upon the human constitution; nevertheless this devastation of human life is not so great that it could not be made good from the teeming enclosures of Virginia and Kentucky.”","kind":"passage","sequence":172,"address":"8.172","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0173","text":"Economic considerations, which might offer a kind of security for the humane treatment of the slave insofar as they identify the master’s interest with the preservation of the slave, are transformed, after the introduction of the slave trade, conversely into grounds for the most extreme destruction of the slave, for as soon as his place can be filled by supplies from foreign Negro enclosures, the duration of his life becomes less important than his productivity, so long as it lasts.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"239\", \"facs\": \"#f0258\"} --> It is therefore a maxim of slave economy in countries importing slaves that the most effective economy consists in squeezing the greatest possible mass of performance out of the human cattle (human chattle) in the shortest possible time.","kind":"passage","sequence":173,"address":"8.173","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0174","text":"It is in tropical cultivation, where annual profits often equal the total capital of the plantations, that Negro lives are sacrificed most ruthlessly. It is the agriculture of the West Indies, for centuries the cradle of fabulous wealth, that has swallowed up millions of the African race. It is today in Cuba, whose revenues amount to millions and whose planters are princes, where we see among the slave class, besides the coarsest nourishment and the most exhausting and unremitting drudgery, a great part directly destroyed every year by the slow torture of overwork and lack of sleep and recreation” [Note: Cairns l. c. p. 110, 111.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":174,"address":"8.174","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0175","text":"Mutato nomine de te fabula narratur! Read, instead of slave trade, labour market; instead of Kentucky and Virginia, Ireland and the agricultural districts of England, Scotland, and Wales; instead of Africa, Germany! We heard how overwork clears away the bakers in London, and yet the London labour market is always overfilled with German and other candidates for death in baking. Pottery, as we saw, is one of the shortest-lived branches of industry. Does it therefore lack potters? Josiah Wedgwood, the inventor of modern pottery, himself originally an ordinary worker, declared in 1785 before the House of Commons that the entire manufacture employed 15 to 20,000 persons [Note: John Ward: “History of the Borough of Stoke-upon-Trent. London 1843”, p. 42.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":175,"address":"8.175","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0176","text":"In 1861 the population of the urban seats of this industry in Great Britain alone amounted to 101,302. “The cotton industry is 90 years old . . . . In three generations of the English race it has devoured nine generations of cotton workers [Note: Ferrand’s speech in the “House of Commons” of 27 April 1863.] ”. To be sure, in individual epochs of feverish upswing the labour market showed alarming gaps. Thus, for example, in 1834. But the gentlemen manufacturers now proposed to the Poor Law Commissioners that the “surplus population” of the agricultural districts be sent north, declaring that the manufacturers would absorb and consume them [Note: “That the manufacturers would absorb it and use it up. Those were the very words used by the cotton manufacturers.” l. c.] . These were their very own words.","kind":"passage","sequence":176,"address":"8.176","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0177","text":"“Agents were ordered to Manchester with the consent of the Poor Law Commissioners. Lists of agricultural labourers were drawn up and handed over to these agents. The manufacturers ran to the bureaux, and after they had selected what suited them, the families were dispatched from the south of England. These packages of human beings were delivered with labels like so many bales of goods, by canal and wagon,—some wandered on foot, and many lost their way and roamed half-starved about the manufacturing districts. This developed into a genuine branch of trade. The House of Commons will scarcely believe it. This regular trade, this trafficking in human flesh, continued, and these people were bought and sold by the Manchester agents to the Manchester manufacturers as regularly as Negroes to the cotton planters of the Southern States .","kind":"passage","sequence":177,"address":"8.177","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0178","text":". . . The year 1860 marks the zenith of the cotton industry. Once again there was a shortage of hands. The manufacturers again turned to the flesh-agents, and these ransacked the dunes of Dorset, the hills of Devon, and the plains of Wilts, but the surplus population had already been devoured. The “Bury Guardian” lamented that 10,000 additional hands could be absorbed after the conclusion of the Anglo-French commercial treaty, and that before long some 30 or 40,000 more would be needed. After the agents and sub-agents of the flesh trade had swept through the agricultural districts in 1860 with rather fruitless results, a deputation of manufacturers turned to Mr Villiers, the President of the Poor Law Board, in order to obtain permission once again, as formerly, to receive supplies of poor and orphan children from the workhouses [Note: l. c.","kind":"passage","sequence":178,"address":"8.178","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0179","text":"Villiers, despite the best will, was “legally” obliged to reject the manufacturers’ request. The gentlemen nevertheless achieved their ends through the compliance of the local poor-law administrations. Mr A. Redgrave, factory inspector, assures us that this time the system under which the orphans] .”","kind":"passage","sequence":179,"address":"8.179","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0179"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0180","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"241\", \"facs\": \"#f0260\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":180,"address":"8.180","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0180"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0181","text":"What experience shows the capitalist in general is a constant surplus population, i.e. surplus population in relation [Note: and paupers’ children are “legally” regarded as apprentices, “was not accompanied by the old abuses”—(on these “abuses” see Engels, l. c.)—although admittedly in one case “the system was abused in regard to a number of girls and young women who were brought from the agricultural districts of Scotland to Lancashire and Cheshire.” Under this “system” the manufacturer concludes a contract with the authorities of the poorhouses for specified periods. He feeds, clothes, and lodges the children and gives them a small monetary allowance.","kind":"passage","sequence":181,"address":"8.181","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0181"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0182","text":"The following remark by Mr Redgrave sounds strange, especially when one considers that, even among the years of prosperity of the English cotton industry, the year 1860 stands alone, and that wages were moreover high, because the extraordinary demand for labour encountered depopulation in Ireland, unparalleled emigration from English and Scottish agricultural districts to Australia and America, and an actual decline of population in some English agricultural districts, partly as a consequence of successfully breaking down vital power, partly owing to the earlier draining-off of the available population by the dealers in human flesh. And despite all this Mr Redgrave says: “This kind of labour (that of poorhouse children) is, however, sought only when no other can be found, for it is high-priced labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":182,"address":"8.182","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0182"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0183","text":"The ordinary wage for a boy of 13 is about 4 sh. weekly; but to lodge, clothe, feed, provide with medical assistance and proper supervision 50 or 100 such boys, and in addition to give them a small monetary allowance, is impracticable for 4 sh. per head weekly.” (“Rep. of the Insp. of Factories for 30th April 1860” p. 27.) Mr Redgrave forgets to say how the worker himself can provide all this for his boys out of their 4 sh. wages, if the manufacturer cannot do so for 50 or 100 boys who are lodged, fed, and supervised together. To ward off false conclusions from the text, I must further observe here that the English cotton industry, since its subjection to the Factory Act of 1850 with its regulation of working time, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":183,"address":"8.183","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0183"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0184","text":"etc., must be regarded as the English model industry. The English cotton worker stands in every respect above his continental companion in fate. “The Prussian factory worker works at least 10 hours more per week than his English rival, and if he is employed at his own loom at home, even this limit to his additional working hours falls away.” (“Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1855” p. 103.) The above-mentioned factory inspector Redgrave travelled on the Continent after the industrial exhibition of 1851, especially in France and Prussia, in order to investigate factory conditions there. He says of the Prussian factory worker: “He receives a wage sufficient]\n[Running matter: I.","kind":"passage","sequence":184,"address":"8.184","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0184"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0185","text":"16]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"242\", \"facs\": \"#f0261\"} --> for the momentary needs of the valorization of capital, although it forms its stream from stunted, short-lived, rapidly displaced, so to speak prematurely plucked generations of human beings [Note: “The overworked die with strange rapidity; but the places of those who perish are at once filled again, and a frequent change of persons produces no alteration on the stage.” “England and America. London 1833”, t. I, 55. (Author E. G. Wakefield.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":185,"address":"8.185","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0185"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0186","text":"To be sure, experience shows the intelligent observer, on the other hand, how rapidly and deeply capitalist production, which historically speaking scarcely dates from yesterday, has seized the vital root of the people’s strength; how the degeneration of the industrial population is retarded only by constant absorption of naturally grown elements of life from the countryside; and how even rural labourers, despite fresh air and the principle of natural selection, which exercises such all-powerful sway among them and allows only the strongest individuals to thrive, are already beginning to die out [Note: See: “Public Health. Sixth Report of the Medical Officer of the Privy Council. 1863.” Published London, 1864. This Report deals especially with agricultural labourers.","kind":"passage","sequence":186,"address":"8.186","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0186"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0187","text":"“The county of Sutherland has been represented as a greatly improved county, but a recent inquiry has discovered that here, in districts once so famous for handsome men and brave soldiers, the inhabitants have degenerated into a thin and stunted race. In the healthiest situations, on hill-slopes in view of the sea, the faces of their children are as thin and pale as they can only be in the foul atmosphere of a London alley.” (Thornton l. c. pp. 74, 75.) They indeed resemble the 30,000 “gallant Highlanders” whom Glasgow beds together with prostitutes and thieves in its wynds and closes.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":187,"address":"8.187","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0187"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0188","text":"Capital, which has such “good reasons” for denying the sufferings of the generation of workers surrounding it, is in its practical movement determined as little and as much by the prospect of the future putrefaction of humanity and, finally, unavoidable depopulation, as by the possible fall of the earth into the sun. In every stock-exchange swindle everyone knows that the storm must strike one day, but everyone hopes that it will fall upon his neighbour’s head after [Note: to procure simple fare and the few comforts to which he is accustomed and with which he is content . . . . He lives worse and works harder than his English rival.” (“Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1853” p. 85.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"243\", \"facs\": \"#f0262\"} --> he himself has caught the rain of gold and brought it to safety.\nAfter me, the deluge!","kind":"passage","sequence":188,"address":"8.188","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0188"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0189","text":"is the watchword of every capitalist and every capitalist nation. Capital is therefore ruthless against the health and length of life of the worker, wherever it is not compelled by society to show consideration [Note: “Although the health of the population is such an important element of national capital, we fear we must admit that the capitalists are by no means ready to preserve and value this treasure. Consideration for the health of the workers had to be forced upon the manufacturers.” (“Times”, October 1861.) “The men of the West Riding became the cloth-makers of humanity; the health of the working people was sacrificed, and in a few generations the race would have degenerated, but a reaction set in. The hours of children’s labour were restricted, etc.” (“Report of the Registrar General for October 1861.”)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":189,"address":"8.189","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0189"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0190","text":"To the complaint of physical and mental stunting, premature death, the torture of overwork, it replies: Should this agony torment us, since it increases our pleasure (profit)? But, on the whole, this does not depend either on the good or bad will of the individual capitalist. Free competition causes the immanent laws of capitalist production to assert themselves upon the individual capitalist as an external coercive law [Note: Thus we find, for example, that at the beginning of 1863, 26 firms which possess extensive potteries in Staffordshire, among them J. Wedgwood and Sons, petitioned in a memorandum “for forcible intervention by the State.” Competition with other capitalists did not permit them any “voluntary” restriction of the working time of children, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":190,"address":"8.190","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0190"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0191","text":"“However much we may therefore deplore the evils mentioned above, it would be impossible to prevent them by any kind of agreement among the manufacturers . . . . In view of all these points, we have come to the conviction that a coercive law is necessary.” Children’s Emp. Comm. Rep. I, 1863, p. 322.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":191,"address":"8.191","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0191"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0192","text":"The establishment of a normal working day is the result of a struggle lasting many hundreds of years between capitalist and worker. Yet the history of this struggle displays two opposite currents. Compare, for example, the English factory legislation of our time with the English labour statutes from the 14th [Running matter: 16*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"244\", \"facs\": \"#f0263\"} --> to far into the middle of the 18th century [Note: These labour statutes, which are also found at the same time in France, the Netherlands, etc., were formally repealed in England only in 1813, after they had long since been abolished by the conditions of production.] . Whereas modern factory legislation forcibly shortens the working day, those statutes seek forcibly to prolong it.","kind":"passage","sequence":192,"address":"8.192","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0192"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0193","text":"Certainly the claims of capital in its embryonic state, when it is only becoming, and therefore secures its right of absorption to a sufficient quantity of surplus labour not merely through the brute force of economic conditions but also with the aid of state power, appear altogether modest when compared with the concessions which it must make, growling and reluctantly, in its manhood. It takes centuries before the “free” worker, as a result of the developed capitalist mode of production, voluntarily consents to it, i.e. is socially compelled, to sell for the price of his customary means of subsistence his entire active lifetime, indeed his very capacity for labour, his birthright for a mess of pottage. It is therefore natural that the prolongation of the working day, which capital seeks to impose by state force upon adult workers from the middle of the 14th to the end of the 17th","kind":"passage","sequence":193,"address":"8.193","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0193"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0194","text":"centuries, approximately coincides with the limit to working time which, in the second half of the 19th century, is drawn here and there by the state against the transformation of children’s blood into capital. What is today, for example in the State of Massachusetts, until recently the freest state of the North American Republic, proclaimed as the state limit upon the labour of children under 12 years of age, was still in England in the middle of the 17th century the normal working day of full-blooded craftsmen, robust farm servants, and gigantic blacksmiths [Note: “No child under the age of 12 years shall be employed in any manufacturing establishment more than 10 hours in one day.” “General Statutes of Massachusetts. 63, ch. 12. (The ordinances were enacted from 1836—1858.)","kind":"passage","sequence":194,"address":"8.194","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0194"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0195","text":"“Labour performed during a period of 10 hours on any day in all cotton, woollen, silk, paper, glass, and flax factories, or in manufactories of iron and brass, shall be considered a legal day’s labour. And be it enacted, that hereafter no minor engaged in any factory shall be holden or required to work more than 10 hours in any day, or 60 hours in any week; and that hereafter no minor shall be admitted as a worker under the age of 10 years in any factory] .","kind":"passage","sequence":195,"address":"8.195","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0195"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0196","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"245\", \"facs\": \"#f0264\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":196,"address":"8.196","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0196"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0197","text":"The first “Statute of Labourers” (23 Edward III, 1349) found its immediate pretext (not its cause, for legislation of this kind continues for centuries without the pretext) in the great plague, which decimated the population, so that, as a Tory writer says, “the difficulty of putting labourers to work at reasonable prices (i.e. at prices which left their employers a reasonable quantity of surplus labour) became in truth unbearable [Note: “Sophisms of Free Trade. 7th edit. Lond. 1850”, p. 205. This same Tory further admits: “Acts of Parliament which regulated wages against the workers in favour of the employers of labour lasted for the long period of 464 years. The population increased. These laws now became superfluous and burdensome.” (l. c. p. 206.)] ”.","kind":"passage","sequence":197,"address":"8.197","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0197"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0198","text":"Reasonable wages were therefore dictated by coercive law, as was the limit of the working day. The latter point, which alone interests us here, is repeated in the statute of 1496 (under Henry VIII). The working day for all artificers and agricultural labourers from March to September was then to last, though this was never enforced, from 5 o’clock in the morning until between 7 and 8 o’clock in the evening; but the hours for meals amounted to 1 hour for breakfast, 1½ hours for dinner, and ½ hour for four o’clock bread, thus exactly twice as much as under the Factory Act now in force [Note: J.","kind":"passage","sequence":198,"address":"8.198","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0198"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0199","text":"Wade rightly observes with reference to this statute: “The statute\n of 1496 shows that food was considered the equivalent of ⅓ of the income\n of an artisan and ⅔ of the income of an agricultural labourer, and\n this indicates a greater degree of independence among the labourers than now\n prevails, when the food of labourers in agriculture and manufacture forms a much\n higher proportion of their wages.” (J. Wade l. c. pp. 24, 25 and 577.)\n The opinion that this difference was perhaps due to the difference in the ratio of prices between\n foodstuffs and articles of clothing, now and then, is refuted by\n the most superficial glance at: “Chronicon Pretiosum etc. By Bishop\n Fletwood. 1st edit. London 1707. 2nd edit. London 1745.”] . In winter there was to be-\n [Note: within this state.” “State of New Jersey. An act to limit the hours\n of labour etc., 61 and 2. (Act of 11th","kind":"passage","sequence":199,"address":"8.199","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0199"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0200","text":"March 1855.) “No minor who\n has attained the age of 12 years, and is under the age of 15 years, shall be em-\n ployed in any manufacturing establishment more than 11 hours in any one day,\nnor before 5 o’clock in the morning, nor after 7½ in the evening.”\n “Revised Statutes of the State of Rhode Island etc. ch. 39, §. 23,\n 1st July 1857.”]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"246\", \"facs\": \"#f0265\"} --> worked from 5 o’clock in the morning until dark, with the same inter-\n ruptions. A statute of Elizabeth of 1562 for all work-\n ers “hired for wages by the day or week” leaves the length of the working day untouched,\n but seeks to restrict the intervals to\n 2½ hours in summer and 2 in winter. The midday meal\n is to last only one hour and “the afternoon sleep of ½ hour”\n is permitted only between the middle of May and the middle of August. For every hour\n of absence 1 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":200,"address":"8.200","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0200"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0201","text":"(about 10 pfennigs) was to be deducted from wages. In\n practice, however, the relation was much more favourable to the workers than in\n the statute book. The father of political economy and, to a certain extent,\n the inventor of statistics, William Petty, says in a work which he\n published in the last third of the 17th century: “Labour-\n ing men, properly speaking at that time agricultural labourers, work 10 hours\n daily and eat 20 meals weekly, namely three daily on working\n days and two on Sundays; from which one clearly sees that,\n if they wished to fast on Friday evenings and to dine in an hour and a half,\n whereas they now require two hours for this meal,\n from 11 to 1 o’clock in the morning, if therefore they\n worked \\frac{1}{20} more and consumed \\frac{1}{20} less, a tenth of the above-\n mentioned tax would be raisable [Note: “W.","kind":"passage","sequence":201,"address":"8.201","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0201"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0202","text":"Petty: Political Anatomy of Ireland. 1672. edit.\n 1691”, p. 10.] ”. Was Dr Andrew Ure not\n right to cry out against the Twelve Hours Bill of 1833 as a relapse into the times of dark-\n ness? To be sure, the provisions mentioned in the statutes and by\n Petty apply also to “apprentices”.\n But the state of child labour even at the end of the 17th century\n may be seen from the following complaint: “Our youth, here in Eng-\n land, do absolutely nothing until the time when they become apprentices, and then\n they naturally require a long time—seven years—to become perfect\n artisans”. Germany, by contrast, is praised because\n there children are brought up from the cradle onwards at least to “a little occu-\n pation” [Note: “A Discussion on the Necessity of Encouraging Mecha-\n nick Industry. London 1689”, p. 13.","kind":"passage","sequence":202,"address":"8.202","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0202"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0203","text":"Macaulay, who has falsified English\n history to suit Whig and bourgeois interests, declaims as follows] .","kind":"passage","sequence":203,"address":"8.203","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0203"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0204","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"247\", \"facs\": \"#f0266\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":204,"address":"8.204","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0204"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0205","text":"Throughout the greater part of the 18th century, until\n the epoch of large-scale industry, capital in England had not\n succeeded, by paying the weekly value of labour-power, in appropriating to itself\n the worker’s entire week’s labour, with the excep-\n tion, however, of the agricultural labourer. The circumstance\n that they could live an entire week on the wage of 4 days\n did not seem to the workers a sufficient reason to work the other two days\n as well for the capitalist. One section of the English economists,\n in the service of capital, denounced this obstinacy in the most furious terms; an-\n other section defended the workers. Let us hear, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":205,"address":"8.205","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0205"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0206","text":"the controversy be-\n tween Postlethwaite, whose commercial dictionary at that time enjoyed the same\n reputation as similar writings by Mac Culloch and Mac\n [Note: follows: “The practice of setting children prematurely to work prevailed in the 17th\n century to a degree almost incredible for the condition of industry at that time. At\n Norwich, the chief seat of the woollen industry, a child of\n 6 years was considered capable of work. Various writers of that time, and\n among them some who were considered extraordinarily well disposed, mention\n with ‘Exultation’ the fact that in this city alone boys\n and girls created a wealth which, over and above their own maintenance,\n amounted to £12,000 in one year.","kind":"passage","sequence":206,"address":"8.206","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0206"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0207","text":"The more closely we investigate the history of the past,\n the more reason we find to reject the view of those who regard our age as\n fruitful in new social evils. What is new is the intelligence which discovers\n the evils, and the humanity which cures them.” (“History of England”,\n v. I, p. 419.) Macaulay might further have\n reported that “extraordinarily well disposed” amis du commerce\n in the 17th century relate with “Exultation” how in a poorhouse in Holland\n a child of 4 years was employed, and that this example of “vertue\n mise en pratique” passes as a model in all the writings of humanitarians\n à la Macaulay down to the time of A. Smith. It is true that with the rise of\n manufacture, as distinct from handicraft, traces of child exploita-\n tion appear.","kind":"passage","sequence":207,"address":"8.207","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0207"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0208","text":"The tendency of capital is unmistakable, but the facts\n themselves still stand as isolated as the phenomenon of two-headed children.\n They were therefore recorded “with Exultation”, as especially remarkable and admirable,\n by prescient “amis du commerce” for contemporaries and posterity,\n and recommended for imitation. The same Scottish sycophant and smooth-\n tongued orator Macaulay says: “today one hears only of retrogression and sees only pro-\n gress”. What eyes, and above all what ears!]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"248\", \"facs\": \"#f0267\"} --> Gregor, and the previously cited author of the “Essay on Trade\n and Commerce” [Note: Among the accusers of the workers, the fiercest is the anonymous author mentioned in the text\n of: “An Essay on Trade and Commerce,\n containing Observations on Taxation etc. London 1770”. Already\n earlier, in his work: “Considerations on Taxes.","kind":"passage","sequence":208,"address":"8.208","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0208"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0209","text":"London 1765.”\n Polonius Arthur Young, the unspeakable statistical windbag, follows\n in the same line. Among the defenders of the workers, foremost are: Jacob\n Vanderlint in: “Money answers all things. London 1734”, Rev.\n Nathaniel Forster, D. D. in: “An Enquiry into the Causes of the\n Present Price of Provisions. London 1766”, Dr Price, and espe-\n cially also Postlethwaite, both in a supplement to his “Uni-\n versal Dictionary of Trade and Commerce” and in: “Great Bri-\n tain’s Commercial Interest explained and improved. 2nd edit.\n London 1755”. The facts themselves are attested by many other contem-\n porary writers, among others by Josiah Tucker.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":209,"address":"8.209","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0209"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0210","text":"Among other things, Postlethwaite says: “I cannot conclude these few observa-\n tions without taking notice of the trite saying in the mouths\n of too many, that if the labourer (industrious poor)\n can obtain enough to live on in 5 days, he will not work a full 6 days.\n Hence they infer the necessity of making even the necessaries of life\n dearer by taxes or by any other means,\n in order to compel the artisan and manufacturing worker to unremit-\n ting six-day labour in the week. I must beg leave to be of another opinion\n than these great politicians, who take up the lance for\n the perpetual slavery of the working popula-\n tion of this kingdom (“the perpetual slavery of the working people”);\n they forget the proverb ‘all work and no\n play’ (only work, and no play, makes dull).","kind":"passage","sequence":210,"address":"8.210","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0210"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0211","text":"Do not the\n English pride themselves on the genius and skill of their artisans and\n manufacturing workers, which have hitherto secured general credit\n and reputation for British wares? To what circumstance was this due?\n Probably to none other than the manner in which our working-\n people, following their own inclination, know how to amuse themselves. If they were compelled to work throughout\n the whole year, all six days of the week,\n in constant repetition of the same work, would this not blunt their genius\n and make them stupidly indolent instead of lively and skilful;\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"249\", \"facs\": \"#f0268\"} --> and would not our workers, as a consequence of such perpetual slavery,\n lose their reputation instead of maintaining it? What kind of artistic skill could we\n expect from such hard-driven animals? (hard driven ani-\n mals) . . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":211,"address":"8.211","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0211"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0212","text":"Many of them perform as much work in 4 days as a\n Frenchman in 5 or 6. But if Englishmen are to be perpetual drudges,\n there is reason to fear that they will degenerate even below the French\n (degenerate). If our people are renowned for their bravery in\n war, do we not say that this is due, on the one hand, to good Eng-\n lish roast beef and pudding in their bellies, and, on the other, no less\n to our constitutional spirit of liberty? And why should not the greater\n genius, energy, and skill of our artisans and manufacturing workers be due to\n the freedom with which they amuse themselves in their own fashion?\n I hope they will never lose either these privileges\n or the good liv-\n ing from which their capacity for work and their courage alike pro-\n ceed”! [Note: Postlethwaite l. c.","kind":"passage","sequence":212,"address":"8.212","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0212"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0213","text":"“First Preliminary Discourse”, p. 14.]","kind":"passage","sequence":213,"address":"8.213","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0213"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0214","text":"To this the author of the “Essay on Trade and Com-\n merce” replies:","kind":"passage","sequence":214,"address":"8.214","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0214"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0215","text":"“If it is held to be a divine ordinance to celebrate the seventh day\n of the week, this implies that the other days of the week belong to\n labour (he means to capital, as will immediately be seen), and it cannot\n be denounced as cruel to enforce this commandment of God . . . . . Since mankind\n in general naturally inclines to ease and idleness, we have the fatal experi-\n ence of this in the conduct of our manufacturing rabble, who on average\n do not work more than 4 days a week, except in the event of a\n dearness of foodstuffs . . . . Let a bushel of wheat represent\n all the worker’s food, cost 5 sh., and let the worker earn\n one shilling daily by his labour. Then he need work only 5 days\n in the week; only 4 if the bushel costs 4 sh. . . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":215,"address":"8.215","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0215"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0216","text":"But since wages in this kingdom stand much higher, compared\n with the price of foodstuffs, the manufacturing worker who\n works 4 days possesses a monetary surplus with which he lives idly during the remainder of\n the week . . . . I hope I have said enough to make it clear that\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"250\", \"facs\": \"#f0269\"} --> moderate labour for 6 days in the week\n is no slavery. Our agricultural labourers do this\n and, to all appearances, they are the happiest among the workers\n (labouring poor) [Note: “An Essay etc.” He himself relates on p. 96 what “the\n happiness” of the English agricultural labourers already consisted in in 1770.","kind":"passage","sequence":216,"address":"8.216","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0216"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0217","text":"“Their working powers\n are always on the utmost stretch (‘on the\n stretch’); they cannot live cheaper than they do (‘they cannot live cheaper\n than they do’), nor work harder” (‘nor work harder’).] , but the Dutch do it in manufac-\n tures and seem a very happy people. The French do\n it, insofar as the many holidays do not intervene [Note: Protestantism already plays an important role in the genesis of\n capital through its transformation of almost all traditional holidays into working days.]  . . . .\n But our manufacturing rabble has got the fixed idea into its head\n that, as Englishmen, by right of birth they possess the privilege\n of being freer and more independent than the workers in any\n other country in Europe.","kind":"passage","sequence":217,"address":"8.217","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0217"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0218","text":"Now, this idea, insofar as it\n affects the bravery of our soldiers, may be of some use; but\n the less the manufacturing workers have of it, the better for themselves\n and the state. Workers should never regard themselves as independent\n of their superiors (‘independent of their superiors’) . . . . It is\n extraordinarily dangerous to encourage mobs in a commercial state such as\n ours, where perhaps 7 parts out of 8 of the total popula-\n tion are people with little or no property [Note: “An Essay etc.” pp. 15—57 passim.]  . . . . .\n The cure will not be complete until our industrial poor\n consent to work 6 days for the same sum\n that they now earn in 4 days” [Note: l. c. p. 69.]}","kind":"passage","sequence":218,"address":"8.218","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0218"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0219","text":"As early as 1734 Jacob Vanderlint declared that the secret of the capitalists’ complaint about the laziness of the working people was simply that they demanded 6 instead of 4 working days for the same wage.] . For this purpose, as for the “eradication of idleness, dissipation, and romantic delusions of freedom,” likewise “for reducing the poor-rate, fostering the spirit of industry, and depressing the price of labour in the manufactures,” our faithful Eckart of capital proposes the tried and tested means of imprisoning in an “ideal workhouse” (an ideal Workhouse) such workers as fall upon public charity, in a word, paupers. “Such a house must be made a House of Terror (House of Terror) [Note: l. c. p.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"251\", \"facs\": \"#f0270\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":219,"address":"8.219","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0219"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0220","text":"260: “Such ideal workhouse must be made an ‘House\nof Terror’, and not an asylum for the poor, where they are to be plentifully\nfed, warmly and decently clothed, and where they do but little work.”] . In this “House of Terror,” this “ideal\nof a workhouse,” work is to be done for 14 hours daily, including, however, the proper meal-times, so that a full 12 working hours remain” [Note: “In this ideal workhouse the poor shall work 14 hours in a day, allowing\nproper time for meals, in such manner that there shall remain 12 hours of neat\nlabour.” (l. c.) “The French,” he says, “laugh at our enthusiastic\nideas of liberty.” (l. c. p. 78.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":220,"address":"8.220","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0220"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0221","text":"Twelve working hours daily in the “workhouse ideal,”\nin the House of Terror of 1770! Sixty-three years\nlater, in 1833, when the English Parliament reduced the working day for children from 13 to 18 years old to twelve full working\nhours in four branches of industry, the Day of Judgment of English industry seemed to have dawned! In 1852, when L. Bonaparte sought to gain a civil foothold by shaking the legal working day, the French working people cried with one voice: “The law which shortens the working day\nto 12 hours is the only good that has remained to us from the\nlegislation of the Republic” [Note: “They especially objected to work beyond the 12 hours per day, because\nthe law which fixed those hours is the only good which remains to them of the\nlegislation of the Republic.” (Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31. Octob. 1856,\np. 80.) The French twelve-hour law of 5.","kind":"passage","sequence":221,"address":"8.221","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0221"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0222","text":"September 1850, a bourgeoisified edition of the decree of the provisional government of 2 March 1848,\nextends to all workshops without distinction. Before this law,\nthe working day in France was unlimited. It lasted 14,\n15, and more hours in the factories. See “Des classes ouvrières en France, pen-\ndant l’année 1848. Par M. Blanqui.” Monsieur Blanqui, the economist,\nnot the revolutionary, was entrusted by the government with the inquiry into the condition of the workers.] ! In Zurich the labour of\nchildren over 10 years old is restricted to 12 hours; in Aargau,\nin 1862, the labour of children between 13 and 16 years old was\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"252\", \"facs\": \"#f0271\"} --> reduced from 12½ to 12 hours; in Austria, in 1860, that of children between 14 and 16 years old likewise to 12 hours [Note: The ordinances of Messrs. von der Heydt and Man-\nteuffel of 18.","kind":"passage","sequence":222,"address":"8.222","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0222"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0223","text":"August 1853 and 12 August 1854 would be praiseworthy, if they were—carried out.\nBelgium likewise proves itself, with regard to the regulation of the working day, a bourgeois model state. Lord Howard de Welden, English plenipotentiary in Brussels, reports to the Foreign Office, dated 12 May 1862: “Minister Rogier declared to me that neither a general law nor local regulations restrict child labour in any way; that during the last 3 years the government had entertained, at every session, the idea of submitting to the Chambers a law on the subject, but that it had always found an insuperable obstacle in the jealous fear of any legislation\nin contradiction with the principle of complete freedom of\nlabour”!] . What “progress since 1770” Macaulay would have cheered “with exultation”!","kind":"passage","sequence":223,"address":"8.223","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0223"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0224","text":"The “House of Terror” for paupers, of which the soul of capital still dreamt in 1770, rose a few years later as a gigantic\n“workhouse” for the manufacturing workers themselves. It was called the factory.\nAnd this time the ideal paled before reality.","kind":"passage","sequence":224,"address":"8.224","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0224"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0225","text":"After capital had required centuries to extend the working\nday to its normal maximum limits and then beyond these,\nto the limits of the natural day of 12\nhours [Note: “It is certainly much to be regretted that any class of persons\nshould work themselves out 12 hours daily. If meals and the time required to go\nto and from the workshop be added, this amounts in fact to 14 of the 24 hours of\nthe day … Apart from health, no one, I hope, will hesitate to admit that, from the\nmoral point of view, so complete an absorption of the time of the working classes,\nwithout interruption, from the early age of 13 years, and in the ‘free’ branches of\nindustry even from a much earlier age, is extraordinarily harmful and a terrible evil.","kind":"passage","sequence":225,"address":"8.225","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0225"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0226","text":"In the interest of public morality, for the rearing of a vigorous population, and in order to secure for the great mass of the people a rational enjoyment of life, it must be insisted that in all branches of business a part of every working day be reserved for recreation and leisure.” (Leonhard Horner in: “Insp. of\nFact. Reports. 31st Dec. 1841.”)] , there now followed, since the birth of large-scale industry in the last third of the 18th century, an avalanche-like,\nviolent and immoderate precipitate rush. Every barrier of custom and\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"253\", \"facs\": \"#f0272\"} --> nature, age and sex, day and night, was shattered.","kind":"passage","sequence":226,"address":"8.226","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0226"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0227","text":"Even\nthe concepts of day and night, peasantly simple in the old statutes,\nbecame so blurred that as late as 1860 an English judge had to display truly Talmudic acuteness in order to declare “with judicial competence” what day and night were [Note: See “Judgment of Mr. J. H. Otwey, Belfast, Hilary\nSessions, 1860.”] . Capital celebrated its orgies.","kind":"passage","sequence":227,"address":"8.227","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0227"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0228","text":"As soon as the working class, bamboozled by the clamour of production, came somewhat to its senses again, its resistance began, first in the birthplace of large-scale industry, in England. For three decades, however, the concessions wrested by it remained purely nominal. Parliament enacted 5 Labour Acts from 1802 to 1833, but was cunning enough not to vote a penny for their compulsory enforcement, the necessary official personnel, etc. [Note: It is very characteristic of the régime of Louis Philippe, the roi\nbourgeois, that the only factory law enacted under him, of 22 March 1841,\nwas never carried out. And this law concerns only child\nlabour. It fixes 8 hours for children between 8 and 12, 12 hours for children\nbetween 12 and 16 years, etc., with many exceptions that permit night\nlabour even for eight-year-olds.","kind":"passage","sequence":228,"address":"8.228","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0228"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0229","text":"Supervision and enforcement of the law\nwere left, in a country where every mouse is administered by the police, to the good\nwill of the “amis du commerce.” Only since 1853 has the law been actually carried out in one single Département, the Département du Nord, through the appointment of a paid government inspector. No less characteristic of the development of French society in general is the fact that Louis Philippe’s law stood alone until the Revolution of 1848 in the all-entangling French law-factory!] . They remained a dead letter. “The\nfact is that before the Act of 1833 children and young persons were worked the whole night, the whole day, or both ad libitum” [Note: “Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 30th April 1860”, p. 51.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":229,"address":"8.229","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0229"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0230","text":"Only from the Factory Act of 1833—embracing cotton,\nwool, flax, and silk factories—does a normal working day date for modern industry. Nothing characterizes the spirit of capital better than the history of English factory legislation from\n1833 to 1864!","kind":"passage","sequence":230,"address":"8.230","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0230"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0231","text":"The law of 1833 declares that “the ordinary factory\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"254\", \"facs\": \"#f0273\"} --> working day shall begin at half past 5 in the morning and end at half past 8 in the evening, and within these limits, a period of 15\nhours, it shall be lawful to employ young persons (i.e. persons between 13 and 18 years of age) at any time of day, always provided that no individual young person works more than 12 hours within one day, with the exception of certain specially provided cases.” Section 6 of the Act stipulates “that in the course of every day at least 1½ hours for meals shall be allowed to every such person of restricted working time.” The employment of children under 9 years old, with an exception to be mentioned later, was prohibited; the labour of children from 9 to 13 years old was restricted to 8 hours daily.","kind":"passage","sequence":231,"address":"8.231","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0231"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0232","text":"Night\nlabour, that is, according to this law, labour between half past 8 in the evening and half past 5 in the morning, was prohibited for all persons between 9 and 18 years old.","kind":"passage","sequence":232,"address":"8.232","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0232"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0233","text":"The legislators were so far from wishing to infringe the freedom of capital in exploiting adult labour-power, or, as they called it, “the freedom of labour,” that they hatched a special system in order to forestall such a hair-raising consequence of the Factory Act.","kind":"passage","sequence":233,"address":"8.233","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0233"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0234","text":"“The great evil of the factory system, as it is at present constituted,” says the first report of the Central Board of the Commission of 25 June 1833, “consists in its creating the necessity of extending child labour to the utmost length of the working day of adults. The only remedy for this evil, without restricting the labour of adults, from which an evil would spring greater than that which is to be prevented, appears to be the plan of employing double sets of children.” Under the name of the relay system (“System of Relays”; relay means, in English as in French, the changing of post-horses at different stations), this “plan” was therefore carried out, so that, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":234,"address":"8.234","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0234"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0235","text":"from half past 5 in the morning until half past one in the afternoon one set of children between 9 and 13 years old, and from half past one in the afternoon until half past 8 in the evening another set, are harnessed up, and so forth.","kind":"passage","sequence":235,"address":"8.235","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0235"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0236","text":"As a reward for the fact that Messrs. the factory owners had most brazenly ignored all the laws on child labour enacted during the last 22 years,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"255\", \"facs\": \"#f0274\"} --> the pill was now gilded for them as well. Parliament decreed that after 1 March 1834 no child under 11 years old, after 1 March 1835 no child under 12 years old, and after 1 March 1836 no child under 13 years old should work more than 8 hours in a factory! This “liberalism,” so considerate towards “capital,” was all the more praiseworthy because Dr. Farre, Sir A. Carlisle, Sir B. Brodie, Sir C. Bell, Mr. Guthrie, etc.—in short, the most eminent physicians and surgeons of London—had declared in their testimony before the House of Commons that periculum in mora! Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":236,"address":"8.236","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0236"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0237","text":"Farre\nexpressed himself somewhat more coarsely: “Legislation is equally necessary for the prevention of death in every form in which it can be prematurely inflicted, and certainly this (the factory mode) must be regarded as one of the cruellest methods of inflicting it [Note: “Legislation is equally necessary for the prevention of death, in any\nform in which it can be prematurely inflicted, and certainly this must be viewed\nas a most cruel mode of inflicting it.”] ”. The same “reformed” Parliament, which out of tenderness for Messrs. the factory owners still bound children under 13 for years to the hell of 72 hours of factory labour per week, on the other hand, in the Emancipation Act, which also administered freedom drop by drop, from the outset prohibited the planters from working any Negro slave for more than 45 hours per week!","kind":"passage","sequence":237,"address":"8.237","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0237"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0238","text":"But far from being chastened, capital now opened a noisy agitation lasting several years. It turned chiefly on the age of the categories that, under the name of children, had been restricted to 8-hour labour and subjected to a certain compulsory schooling. According to capitalist anthropology, childhood ceased in the 10th, or, at the latest, the 11th year. The nearer the date of the full execution of the Factory Act, the fateful year 1836, approached, the more wildly the factory-owner mob raged. It did in fact succeed in intimidating the government to such an extent that in 1835 it proposed lowering the limit of childhood from 13 to 12 years. Meanwhile the pressure from without grew threateningly. Courage failed the House of Commons.","kind":"passage","sequence":238,"address":"8.238","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0238"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0239","text":"It refused to throw thirteen-year-olds for more than 8 hours daily beneath the Juggernaut wheel of capital, and the\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"256\", \"facs\": \"#f0275\"} --> Act of 1833 came fully into force. It remained unchanged until June 1844.","kind":"passage","sequence":239,"address":"8.239","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0239"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0240","text":"During the decade in which it first partially, then wholly, regulated\n factory labour, the official reports of the factory inspectors bristle with complaints about the impossibility of its enforcement. For the\n Act of 1833 left the gentlemen of capital free, in the fifteen-hour period\n from half past 5 in the morning to half past 8 in the evening,\n to make every “young person” and “every child” begin, interrupt, and end\n twelve- or respectively 8-hour labour at any time whatever, and likewise\n to assign different persons different hours\n for meals; the gentlemen soon devised a new “relay system,”\n whereby the work-horses did not change stations after a fixed time,\n but were always harnessed anew at changing stations. We shall not linger further over the beauty\n of this system, since we must return to it later.","kind":"passage","sequence":240,"address":"8.240","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0240"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0241","text":"But\n it is clear at first sight that it abolished the entire Factory Act, not merely\n according to its spirit, but also according to its letter. How were\n the factory inspectors, with this complicated bookkeeping concerning every single\n child and every young person, to enforce the legally determined working time and\n the granting of the statutory meals? In a great\n part of the factories the old brutal mischief soon flourished again with impunity.\n At a meeting with the Minister of the Interior (1844),\n the factory inspectors demonstrated the impossibility of all control under the newly\n hatched relay system [Note: “Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31. October 1849”, p. 6.] . In the meantime, however, circumstances had changed greatly. The factory workers, especially\n since 1838, had made the Ten Hours’ Bill their economic, just as the Charter\n was their political battle-cry.","kind":"passage","sequence":241,"address":"8.241","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0241"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0242","text":"One section of the factory owners themselves,\n which had regulated factory operation in accordance with the Act of 1833, inundated\n Parliament with memoranda about the immoral “competition” of the\n “false brethren,” whom greater audacity or more favourable local circumstances\n permitted to break the law. Moreover, however much the individual\n factory owner might wish to give free rein to the old rapacity,\n the spokesmen and political leaders of the factory-owning class commanded\n an altered attitude and altered language towards the workers. They\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"257\", \"facs\": \"#f0276\"} --> had opened the campaign for the repeal of the Corn Laws\n and required the workers’ assistance for victory! They therefore promised\n not only a doubling of the loaf of bread, but the adoption of the Ten\n Hours’ Bill under the millennium of Free Trade [Note: “Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct.","kind":"passage","sequence":242,"address":"8.242","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0242"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0243","text":"1848”, p. 98.] . They\n were therefore all the less able to oppose a measure that was merely\n to make the Act of 1833 a reality. In their holiest interest, ground\n rent, the Tories finally thundered, indignantly philanthropic, against the “infamous practices” [Note: Incidentally, Leonhard Horner uses the expression “nefarious prac-\n tices” officially. (“Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31st October 1859”, p. 7.)]  of their enemies.","kind":"passage","sequence":243,"address":"8.243","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0243"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0244","text":"Thus the additional Factory Act of 7 June 1844 came\n into being. It came into effect on 10 September 1844. It grouped a\n new category of workers among those protected, namely females\n over 18 years of age. In every respect they were placed on an equality with young\n persons, their working time limited to 12 hours,\n night-work forbidden to them, etc. For the first time legislation\n thus found itself compelled directly and officially to control the labour of adults as well. In the factory report of 1844–45 it is said ironically:\n “Not a single case has come to our knowledge in which adult\n women have complained of this infringement of their rights” [Note: “Rep. etc. for 30th Sept. 1844”, p. 15.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":244,"address":"8.244","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0244"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0245","text":"The labour of children under 13 years of age was reduced to 6½ and,\n under certain conditions, 7 hours daily [Note: The Act permits children to be employed for 10 hours if they do not work day\n after day, but only every other day. On the whole,\n this clause remained ineffective.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":245,"address":"8.245","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0245"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0246","text":"In order to eliminate the abuses of the “false relay system,”\n the law made, among others, the following important detailed provisions: “The\n working day for children and young persons is to be counted from the time\n at which any child or young person begins to work in the factory\n in the morning.” Thus, if A, for example, begins work at 8 in the morning, and B at 10,\n the working day must nevertheless end for B at the same\n hour as for A. “The beginning of the working day is to be indicated\n by a public clock, e.g. the nearest railway clock, by which\n the factory bell is to be regulated. The factory owner must post a large-print\n [Running matter: I. 17]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"258\", \"facs\": \"#f0277\"} --> notice in the factory stating the beginning, end, and pauses of the working\n day.","kind":"passage","sequence":246,"address":"8.246","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0246"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0247","text":"Children who begin their work in the morning before 12 o’clock\n may not be employed again after 1 o’clock in the afternoon. The\n afternoon set must therefore consist of children other than the morning\n set. The 1½ hours for meals must be granted to all protected\n workers at the same periods of the day,\n at least one hour before 3 o’clock in the afternoon. No child or young person\n may be employed for longer than 5 hours before 1 o’clock in the afternoon without a\n pause of at least half an hour for a meal. No child, young person,\n or female may remain during any meal in a factory room\n in which any work process is taking place, etc.”","kind":"passage","sequence":247,"address":"8.247","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0247"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0248","text":"We have seen: these minute provisions, which regulate the period,\n limits, and pauses of labour so militarily uniformly by the stroke of the bell,\n were by no means products of parliamentary brain-weaving. They\n developed gradually out of circumstances, as natural laws\n of the modern mode of production. Their formulation, official recognition,\n and state proclamation were the result of protracted\n class struggles. One of their immediate consequences was that practice also\n subjected the working day of adult male factory workers\n to the same restrictions, since in most processes of production\n the co-operation of children, young persons, and females is indispensable.\n Hence, on the whole, during the period from\n 1844–47, the twelve-hour working day applied generally and uniformly in all\n branches of industry subject to factory legislation.","kind":"passage","sequence":248,"address":"8.248","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0248"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0249","text":"The factory owners did not, however, permit this “advance” without\n a compensating “retrogression.” At their instigation the\n House of Commons reduced the minimum age of children to be worked from 9 years\n to 8, in order to secure the “additional supply of factory children” owed\n to capital by God and by right [Note: “As a reduction in their hours of work would cause a large number (of\n children) to be employed, it was thought that the additional supply of\n children from eight to nine years of age, would meet the increased\n demand.” (l. c. p. 13.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":249,"address":"8.249","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0249"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0250","text":"The years 1846–47 mark an epoch in the economic history\n of England. Repeal of the Corn Laws, import duties on\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"259\", \"facs\": \"#f0278\"} --> cotton and other raw materials abolished, free trade declared the guiding\n star of legislation! In short, the millennium had dawned. On the other hand,\n in the same years the Chartist movement and the agitation for ten hours reached their\n culminating point. They found allies in the revenge-breathing Tories. Despite the fanatical resistance of the\n faithless army of free trade, with Bright and Cobden at its head,\n the Ten Hours’ Bill, so long sought after, passed through Parlia-\n ment. —","kind":"passage","sequence":250,"address":"8.250","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0250"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0251","text":"The new Factory Act of 8 June 1847 stipulated that on the first\n of July 1847 there should be a provisional shortening of the working day of “young\n persons” (from 13 up to 18 years) and of all women workers to\n 11 hours, but that on 1 May 1848 the definitive limitation to 10\n hours should come into force. In other respects, the Act was merely an amen-\n ding addition to the laws of 1833 and 1844.","kind":"passage","sequence":251,"address":"8.251","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0251"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0252","text":"Capital at first undertook a preliminary campaign, whose\n aim was to prevent the full enforcement of the Act on 1 May 1848.\n And the workers themselves, allegedly made wiser by experience,\n were to help destroy their own work again. The moment was\n skilfully chosen. “It must be remembered that, as a result of the ter-\n rible crisis of 1846–47, great suffering prevailed among the factory workers,\n since many factories had worked only for a short time, others had stood\n wholly idle. A considerable number of workers was therefore in\n the most oppressive situation, many in debt.","kind":"passage","sequence":252,"address":"8.252","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0252"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0253","text":"It could therefore\n be assumed with considerable certainty that they would prefer the longer working time\n in order to make up for past losses, perhaps to pay debts,\n or to take their furniture out of the pawnshop, or to replace\n possessions that had been sold, or to procure new articles of clothing for themselves\n and their families” [Note: “Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1848”, p. 16.] . The gentlemen factory owners sought\n to increase the natural effect of these circumstances through a general\n reduction of wages by 10 %. This occurred, so to speak,\n as the inauguration ceremony of the new era of free trade. Then followed a further\n reduction of 8⅓ %, when the working day was reduced to 11, and double\n that once it was definitively shortened to 10 hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":253,"address":"8.253","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0253"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0254","text":"Wherever, therefore,\n circumstances in any way permitted, there occurred a wage reduction of at least\n [Running matter: 17*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"260\", \"facs\": \"#f0279\"} --> 25 % [Note: “I found that persons who had received 10 sh. weekly had 1 sh. de-\n ducted on account of the general wage reduction of 10%, and a further\n deduction of 1 sh. 6 d. from the remaining 9 sh. for the reduction of time,\n in all 2 sh. 6 d., and despite all this the majority held fast to the\n Ten Hours’ Bill.” (l. c.)] . With opportunities so favourably prepared, they began\n the agitation among the workers for repeal of the Act of 1847. No\n means of deception, seduction, and threat was disdained,\n but all in vain.","kind":"passage","sequence":254,"address":"8.254","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0254"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0255","text":"Of the half-dozen petitions in which\n the workers had to complain of “their oppression by the Act,”\n the petitioners themselves declared, when orally examined, that their signatures\n had been extorted. “They were oppressed, but by someone other\n than the Factory Act” [Note: “When I signed the petition, I stated at the same time that I was doing some-\n thing bad. — Why then did you sign it? — Because I would have been thrown\n onto the pavement if I had refused.” The petitioner did indeed feel himself “oppressed,”\n but not precisely by the Factory Act.” (l. c.\n p. 102.)] . But if the factory owners did not suc-\n ceed in making the workers speak in their sense, they themselves cried\n all the louder in press and Parliament in the name of the workers.","kind":"passage","sequence":255,"address":"8.255","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0255"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0256","text":"They denounced the factory inspectors as a kind of Convention commissioners,\n who mercilessly sacrificed the unhappy worker to their mania for improving the world.\n This manoeuvre too failed. Factory Inspector Leon-\n hard Horner conducted numerous examinations of witnesses in person and through\n his sub-inspectors in the factories of Lancashire. About 70 %\n of the workers examined declared for 10 hours, a much smaller\n percentage for 11, and a wholly insignificant minority for the old 12\n hours [Note: p. 17 l. c. In Mr Horner’s district, 10,270 adult\n male workers in 181 factories were thus examined. Their statements are found in the\nAppendix to the factory report for the half-year ending October 1848. These\n examinations of witnesses offer valuable material in other respects as well.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":256,"address":"8.256","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0256"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0257","text":"Another “amicable” manoeuvre was to make the adult male\n workers labour 12 to 15 hours and then\nto declare this fact the best expression of proletarian heartfelt\n wishes. But the “merciless” factory inspector Leon-\n hard Horner was again on the spot. Most of those “working overtime”\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"261\", \"facs\": \"#f0280\"} --> testified that “they would very much prefer to work 10 hours for lower\n wages, but they had no choice; so many of them\n were unemployed, so many spinners compelled to work as mere piecers,\n that if they refused the longer working time, others would immediately take their places,\n so that the question stood thus for them: either work the longer\n time or lie on the pavement” [Note: l. c. See the statements collected by Leonhard Horner himself,\n Nos.","kind":"passage","sequence":257,"address":"8.257","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0257"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0258","text":"69, 70, 71, 72, 92, 93 and those collected by Sub-inspector A., Nos. 51,\n 52, 58, 59, 60, 62, 70 of the “Appendix.” One factory owner himself poured clear\n wine. See No. 14 after No. 265 l. c.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":258,"address":"8.258","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0258"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0259","text":"Capital’s preliminary campaign had failed, and the Ten\n Hours’ Act came into force on 1 May 1848. In the meantime,\n however, the fiasco of the Chartist party, whose leaders were imprisoned and whose\n organisation was shattered, had already shaken the self-confidence of the English working\n class. Soon afterwards the Paris June insurrection and\n its bloody suppression, as in continental Europe, so in England, united all\n factions of the ruling classes, landowners and capitalists,\n stock-exchange wolves and shopkeepers, protectionists and free traders, government and\n opposition, priests and free-thinkers, young whores and old nuns,\n under the common cry for the salvation of property, religion,\n the family, society! The working class was everywhere proscribed,\n placed under the ban, subjected to the “loi des suspects”.","kind":"passage","sequence":259,"address":"8.259","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0259"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0260","text":"The gentlemen manufacturers therefore no longer needed to feel embarrassed. They broke out into open revolt, not only against the Ten Hours Act, but against the whole legislation which, since 1833, had sought to curb to some extent the “free” sucking dry of labour-power. It was a pro-slavery rebellion in miniature, carried on for more than two years with cynical ruthlessness and terrorist energy, all the more cheaply because the rebellious capitalist risked nothing but the skin of his workers.","kind":"passage","sequence":260,"address":"8.260","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0260"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0261","text":"In order to understand what follows, one must remember that the Factory Acts of 1833, 1844, and 1847 were all three in force, insofar as one did not amend another; that none of them restricted the working day of the male worker above 18 years of age, and that since 1833 the fifteen-hour period from half past 5 in the morning to half past 8 in the evening remained the legal “day,” within which first twelve-hour, later ten-hour, labour by young persons and women had to be performed under the prescribed conditions.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"262\", \"facs\": \"#f0281\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":261,"address":"8.261","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0261"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0262","text":"Here and there the manufacturers began by dismissing a part, sometimes half, of the young persons and women workers employed by them, and in exchange restored the almost obsolete night-work among adult male workers. The Ten Hours Act, they cried, left them no other alternative [Note: “Reports etc. for 31st October 1848”, p. 133, 134.] !","kind":"passage","sequence":262,"address":"8.262","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0262"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0263","text":"The second step concerned the statutory intervals for meals. Let us hear the factory inspectors. “Since the restriction of working hours to 10, the manufacturers maintain, although they have not yet in practice carried their view through to its ultimate consequence, that if, for example, they have work done from 9 o’clock in the morning until 7 o’clock in the evening, they comply sufficiently with the legal provisions by giving one hour for meals before 9 o’clock in the morning and half an hour after 7 o’clock in the evening, thus 1½ hours for meals. In some cases they now allow half an hour for dinner, but at the same time insist that they are under absolutely no obligation to grant any part of the 1½ hours during the course of the ten-hour working day” [Note: “Reports etc. for 30th April 1848”, p. 47.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":263,"address":"8.263","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0263"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0264","text":"The gentlemen manufacturers thus maintained that the painfully precise provisions of the Act of 1844 concerning meals merely gave the workers permission to eat and drink before entering the factory and after leaving the factory, that is, at home! And why should the workers not take their dinner before 9 o’clock in the morning? The Crown lawyers, however, decided that the prescribed meals “must be given in intervals during the actual working day and that it is unlawful to have work done for 10 hours consecutively from 9 o’clock in the morning until 7 o’clock in the evening without interruption” [Note: “Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1848”, p. 130.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":264,"address":"8.264","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0264"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0265","text":"After these genial demonstrations, capital initiated its revolt by a step that corresponded to the letter of the law of 1844, and was therefore legal.","kind":"passage","sequence":265,"address":"8.265","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0265"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0266","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"263\", \"facs\": \"#f0282\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":266,"address":"8.266","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0266"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0267","text":"The Act of 1844 indeed forbade children from 8 to 13 years of age, who were employed before 12 o’clock noon, to be employed again after 1 o’clock in the afternoon. But it in no way regulated the 6½ hours’ work of children whose working time began at 12 o’clock noon or later! Eight-year-old children could therefore, if they began work at 12 o’clock noon, be employed from 12 to 1 o’clock, 1 hour; from 2 to 4 o’clock in the afternoon, 2 hours; and from 5 to half past 8 in the evening, 3½ hours; altogether the statutory 6½ hours! Or, better still. In order to adapt their employment to the work of adult male workers until half past 8 in the evening, the manufacturers needed to give them no work before 2 o’clock in the afternoon, and could then keep them in the factory uninterruptedly until half past 8 in the evening!","kind":"passage","sequence":267,"address":"8.267","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0267"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0268","text":"“And it is now expressly admitted that recently, as a consequence of the manufacturers’ greed to have their machinery run for longer than 10 hours, the practice has crept into England of having children of both sexes from eight to thirteen years, after all young persons and women have been removed from the factory, work alone with the adult men until half past 8 in the evening” [Note: “Reports etc.” l. c. p. 42.] . Workers and factory inspectors protested on hygienic and moral grounds. But capital replied:\n“My deeds upon my head! I demand my right!\nThe penalty and forfeiture of my bond!”","kind":"passage","sequence":268,"address":"8.268","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0268"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0269","text":"In fact, according to a statistical return submitted to the House of Commons on 26 July 1850, in spite of all protests, on 15 July 1850 3742 children in 275 factories were subjected to this “practice” [Note: “Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1850”, p. 5, 6.] . Still not enough! The lynx-eye of capital discovered that the Act of 1844 permitted five hours’ work in the morning not without an interval of at least 30 minutes for refreshment, but prescribed nothing of the kind for afternoon work. It therefore demanded and extorted the pleasure of having eight-year-old working children not only toil but also go hungry without interruption from 2 o’clock until half past 8 in the evening!\n“Yes, the breast.\nThus says the bond” [Note: The nature of capital remains the same, in its undeveloped as in] .","kind":"passage","sequence":269,"address":"8.269","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0269"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0270","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"264\", \"facs\": \"#f0283\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":270,"address":"8.270","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0270"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0271","text":"This Shylock-like clinging to the letter of the law of 1844, insofar as it regulates child labour, was, however, meant only to mediate the open revolt against the same law insofar as it regulates the labour of “young persons and women.” It will be remembered that abolition of the “false relay system” forms the chief purpose and chief content of that law. The manufacturers opened their revolt with the simple declaration that the sections of the Act of 1844 which forbid the arbitrary usufruct of young persons and women in arbitrary shorter sections of the fifteen-hour factory day had been “comparatively harmless (comparatively harmless) so long as working time was restricted to 12 hours. Under the Ten Hours Act they were an intolerable hardship” [Note: “Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1848”, p. 133.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":271,"address":"8.271","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0271"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0272","text":"They therefore informed the inspectors in the coolest manner that they would set themselves above the letter of the law and reintroduce the old system on their own authority [Note: Thus, among others, philanthropist Ashworth in a Quakerishly offensive letter to Leonard Horner.] . This was done in the interest of the ill-advised workers themselves, “in order to be able to pay them higher wages.” “It was the only possible plan for maintaining the industrial supremacy of Great Britain under the Ten Hours Act” [Note: l. c. p. 134.] . “It might be somewhat difficult to detect irregularities under the relay system, but what of that? (what of that?) Is the great factory interest of this country to be treated as a [Note: its developed forms.","kind":"passage","sequence":272,"address":"8.272","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0272"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0273","text":"In the code of law which the influence of the slaveholders, shortly before the outbreak of the American Civil War, imposed upon the territory of New Mexico, it is said: the worker, insofar as the capitalist has bought his labour-power, “is his (the capitalist’s) money.” (“The labourer is his (the capitalist’s) money.”) The same view prevailed among the Roman patricians. The money which they had advanced to the plebeian debtor had, by means of the means of subsistence, transformed itself into the debtor’s flesh and blood. This “flesh and blood” was therefore “their money.” Hence the Shylock-like law of the Twelve Tables!","kind":"passage","sequence":273,"address":"8.273","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0273"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0274","text":"Linguet’s hypothesis that the patrician creditors from time to time held feasts beyond the Tiber upon cooked debtor’s flesh is to remain just as undecided as Daumer’s hypothesis concerning the Christian Eucharist.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"265\", \"facs\": \"#f0284\"} --> secondary thing, in order to save the factory inspectors and sub-inspectors a little more trouble (some little trouble)” [Note: l. c. p. 140.] ?","kind":"passage","sequence":274,"address":"8.274","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0274"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0275","text":"All this nonsense naturally helped nothing. The factory inspectors proceeded judicially. But soon such a cloud of dust from manufacturers’ petitions overwhelmed the Minister of the Interior, Sir George Grey, that in a circular of 5 August 1848 he instructed the inspectors “in general not to intervene because of violations of the letter of the Act, whenever the relay system is not demonstrably abused in order to have young persons and women work more than 10 hours.” Thereupon Factory Inspector J. Stuart permitted the so-called relay system during the fifteen-hour period of the factory day throughout Scotland, where it soon flourished again in the old manner.","kind":"passage","sequence":275,"address":"8.275","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0275"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0276","text":"The English factory inspectors, on the other hand, declared that the minister possessed no dictatorial power to suspend the laws, and continued judicial procedure against the pro-slavery rebels.","kind":"passage","sequence":276,"address":"8.276","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0276"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0277","text":"But what use was any summons before the courts when the courts, the county magistrates [Note: These “county magistrates,” the “great unpaid,” as W. Cobbett calls them, are a kind of unpaid justices of the peace, formed from the notables of the counties. They are in fact the patrimonial courts of the ruling classes.] , acquitted? In these courts the gentlemen manufacturers sat in judgment upon themselves. An example. A certain Eskrigge, cotton spinner of the firm Kershaw, Leese et Co., had submitted to the factory inspector of his district the scheme of a relay system intended for his factory. Receiving a negative reply, he at first remained passive.","kind":"passage","sequence":277,"address":"8.277","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0277"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0278","text":"A few months later an individual named Robinson, likewise a cotton spinner, and, if not the Friday, at any rate the relative of Eskrigge, appeared before the Borough Justices at Stockport for introducing the identical relay plan devised by Eskrigge. Four judges sat there, among them three cotton spinners, at their head the same inevitable Eskrigge. Eskrigge acquitted Robinson, and now declared that what was right for Robinson was fair for Eskrigge. Resting upon his own legally valid decision, he immediately introduced the system in his own factory [Note: “Reports etc. for 30th April 1849”, p. 21, 22. Cf. similar examples ibid. p. 4, 5.] . Indeed, the very composition of these courts was an open violation of the law [Note: By 1 and 2 Wm. IV. c. 24, s.","kind":"passage","sequence":278,"address":"8.278","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0278"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0279","text":"10, known as Sir John Hobhouse’s Factory Act, it is forbidden that any owner of a cotton spinning mill or weaving mill, or father, son, and brother of such an owner, should function as a justice of the peace in questions concerning the Factory Act.] . “This sort of judicial farce,” cries Inspector Howell, “calls for a remedy . . . . either adapt the law to these judgments or have it administered by a less fallible tribunal, which adapts its decisions to the law . . . . In all such cases, how one longs for a paid judge” [Note: l. c.] !","kind":"passage","sequence":279,"address":"8.279","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0279"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0280","text":"The Crown lawyers declared the manufacturers’ interpretation of the Act of 1844 absurd, but the saviours of society would not be put off. “After I,” reports Leonard Horner, “have attempted through 10 prosecutions in 7 different judicial districts to enforce the law and was supported by the magistrates in only one case, I consider further prosecutions for evasion of the law useless. The part of the Act that was drafted in order to create uniformity in working hours no longer exists in Lancashire. Nor do I, together with my sub-agents, possess any means whatever of assuring ourselves that factories in which the so-called relay system prevails do not employ young persons and women for more than 10 hours . . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":280,"address":"8.280","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0280"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0281","text":"By the end of April 1849, 118 factories in my district were already working by this method, and their number has increased rapidly of late. In general, they now work 13½ hours, from 6 o’clock in the morning until half past 7 in the evening; in some cases 15 hours, from half past 5 in the morning until half past 8 in the evening” [Note: “Reports etc. for 30th April 1849”, p. 5.] . As early as December 1848 Leonard Horner possessed a list of 65 manufacturers and 29 factory overseers who unanimously declared that no system of supervision could, under this relay system, prevent the most extensive overwork [Note: “Rep. etc. for 31st Oct. 1849”, p. 6.] . Soon the same children and\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"267\", \"facs\": \"#f0286\"} --> young persons were shifted from the spinning room to the weaving room, etc., and soon, during 15 hours, from one factory to another (shifted) [Note: “Rep. etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":281,"address":"8.281","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0281"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0282","text":"for 30th April 1849”, p. 21.] . How is one to control a system “which abuses the word relay in order to shuffle the hands together in endless variety like cards, and to shift the hours of work and rest for the different individuals daily in such a way that one and the same complete assortment of hands never works together in the same place at the same time” [Note: “Rep. etc. 1. Dec. 1848”, p. 95.] !","kind":"passage","sequence":282,"address":"8.282","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0282"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0283","text":"But quite apart from actual overwork, this so-called\nrelay system was an offspring of capitalist fantasy, such as Fou-\nrier never surpassed in his most ingenious sketches of the “courtes séances,”\nexcept that the attraction of labour had been transformed into the attraction\nof capital. Let one look at those manufacturers’ schemes, which the\ngood press praised as models of “what a reasonable degree of\ncare and method can accomplish.” The body of workers was some-\ntimes divided into 12 to 14 categories, whose constituent parts in turn\nwere constantly changing.","kind":"passage","sequence":283,"address":"8.283","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0283"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0284","text":"During the fifteen-hour period of\nthe factory day, capital now drew the worker in for 30 minutes, now for\nan hour, and then thrust him out again, in order to draw him anew into the\nfactory and thrust him out of the factory, hounding him to and fro\nin scattered fragments of time, never losing its hold upon him until the\nten hours’ work had been completed. As on the stage, the same\npersons had to appear in turn in the various scenes of the various acts.\nBut just as an actor belongs to the stage throughout the whole duration of\nthe drama, so the workers now belonged to the factory for 15 hours,\nnot counting the time for going to and from it. The hours of rest were thus\ntransformed into hours of enforced idleness, which drove the young worker\nto the public house and the young female worker to the brothel.","kind":"passage","sequence":284,"address":"8.284","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0284"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0285","text":"With every new notion which the capi-\ntalist daily hatched in order to keep his machinery going for 12 or 15\nhours without increasing the personnel of workers, the\nworker had now in this scrap of time, now in that, to gulp down his meal.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"268\", \"facs\": \"#f0287\"} --> At the time of the Ten Hours’ agitation, the manufacturers cried out that\nthe workers’ rabble were petitioning in the expectation of receiving twelve\nhours’ wages for ten hours’ work. They had now turned the medal\nround. They paid ten hours’ wages for twelve- and fifteen-hour\ndisposal over labour-power [Note: See “Reports etc. for 30th April 1849,” p. 6, and the detailed\nexposition of the “shifting system” by the factory inspectors Howell\nand Saunders in “Reports etc. for 31st Oct.","kind":"passage","sequence":285,"address":"8.285","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0285"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0286","text":"1848.” See also the petition\nof the clergy of Ashton and neighbourhood, spring 1849, to the Queen,\nagainst the “shift system.”] ! This was the crux of the matter,\nthis the manufacturers’ version of the Ten Hours Act! They were the\nsame unctuous free-traders, dripping with philanthropy, who for 10 full\nyears, during the Anti-Corn-Law agitation, had calculated to the workers\nto the last farthing that, with free importation of corn, ten hours’\nlabour, given the means of English industry, entirely sufficed to enrich\nthe capitalists [Note: Compare, e.g., “The Factory Question and the Ten Hours Bill.\nBy R. H. Greg. 1837.”] .","kind":"passage","sequence":286,"address":"8.286","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0286"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0287","text":"The two-year revolt of capital was at last crowned by the judg-\nment of one of the four highest courts of England, the Court\nof Exchequer, which, in a case brought before it, decided on 8 February\n1850 that the manufacturers, though acting contrary to the meaning of the Act of\n1844, yet that Act itself contained certain words which rendered it\nmeaningless. “By this decision the Ten Hours Act\nwas abolished” [Note: F. Engels: “The English Ten Hours Bill” (in the\n“Neue Rh. Zeitung. Politisch-ökonomische\nRevue. April issue 1850,” p. 13, edited by\nme). The same “high” court likewise discovered, during the American Civil War,\na verbal screw which turned the law against the fitting-out of pirate ships into its\ndirect opposite.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":287,"address":"8.287","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0287"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0288","text":"A mass of manufacturers who had hitherto still shunned the relay\nsystem for young persons and female workers now seized upon it with\nboth hands [Note: “Rep. etc. for 30th April 1850.”] .","kind":"passage","sequence":288,"address":"8.288","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0288"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0289","text":"With this apparently definitive victory of capital, however, a reversal\nimmediately set in. Until then the workers had offered passive, though inflex-\nible and daily renewed resistance. They now protested in loudly threatening\nmeetings in Lancashire and Yorkshire. The alleged\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"269\", \"facs\": \"#f0288\"} --> Ten Hours Act, then, was mere humbug, parliamentary swindling,\nand had never existed! The factory inspectors urgently warned the gov-\nernment that class antagonism was strained to an incredible height. A\npart of the manufacturers themselves grumbled: “owing to the contra-\ndictory decisions of the magistrates, an altogether abnormal\nand anarchic condition prevails. One law applies in Yorkshire, another\nin Lancashire, another law in one parish of Lancashire, another in its\nimmediate neighbourhood.”","kind":"passage","sequence":289,"address":"8.289","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0289"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0290","text":"The manufacturer in large\ncities can evade the law; that in rural spots cannot find the necessary\npersonnel for the relay system and still less for shifting the workers\nfrom one factory to another, etc.” And equal exploitation of labour-power\nis capital’s first human right.","kind":"passage","sequence":290,"address":"8.290","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0290"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0291","text":"Under these circumstances a compromise was reached be-\ntween manufacturers and workers, which was given parliamentary\nseal in the new supplementary Factory Act of 5 August 1850.\nFor “young persons and females,” the working day was increased\non the first 5 weekdays from 10 to 10½ hours, and limited\non Saturday to 7½ hours. Work must proceed during the\nperiod from 6 o’clock in the morning to 6 o’clock in the evening [Note: In winter, the period between 7 o’clock in the morning and 7 o’clock in the evening\nmay take its place.] , with pauses of\n1½ hours for meals, to be granted simultaneously and in accordance with\nthe provisions of 1844, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":291,"address":"8.291","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0291"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0292","text":"This put an end to the relay\nsystem once and for all [Note: “The present law (of 1850) was a compromise whereby the employed\nsurrendered the benefit of the Ten Hours’ Act for the advantage of one\nuniform period for the commencement and termination of the\nlabour of those whose labour is restricted.” (“Reports etc. for 30th\nApril 1852,” p. 14.)] . For child labour,\nthe law of 1844 remained in force.","kind":"passage","sequence":292,"address":"8.292","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0292"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0293","text":"This time, as before, one category of manufacturers secured special\nseigneurial rights over proletarian children. These were the silk\nmanufacturers. In 1833 they had howled threateningly that “if\nthe liberty of working children of any age for 10 hours a day\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"270\", \"facs\": \"#f0289\"} --> was taken away, it would stop their works.” It was impossible for\nthem to purchase a sufficient number of children over 13. They\nextorted the desired privilege. The pretext proved, upon later\ninvestigation, to be a bare lie [Note: “Reports etc. for 30th Sept. 1844,” p.","kind":"passage","sequence":293,"address":"8.293","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0293"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0294","text":"13.] , which did not prevent them, for a decade, from spinning silk\ndaily for 10 hours out of the blood of little children, who had to be set\non chairs in order to perform their work [Note: l. c.] . The Act of 1844 indeed “deprived” them\nof the “liberty” of working children under 11 for more than 6½\nhours, but on the other hand secured them the privilege of working\nchildren between 11 and 13 for 10 hours daily, and annulled the compulsory\nschooling prescribed for other factory children. This time the\npretext was: “the delicacy of the fabric requires a delicacy\nof finger which can only be secured by early entry into the factory” [Note: “The delicate texture of the fabric in which they were employed requir-\ning a lightness of touch, only to be acquired by their early introduction to these\nfactories.” (l. c. p. 20.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":294,"address":"8.294","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0294"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0295","text":"For the sake of delicate fingers, the children were slaughtered outright, as\ncattle are in Southern Russia for the sake of hide and tallow. Finally, in\n1850, the privilege granted in 1844 was restricted to the departments of silk\ntwisting and silk winding, but here, as compensation to capital deprived\nof its “liberty,” the working time for children from 11\nto 13 years was increased from 10 to 10½ hours. Pretext: “The work\nis lighter in silk factories than in the other factories and in no way so\ninjurious to health” [Note: “Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1861,” p. 26.] . Official medical\ninvestigation subsequently proved that, conversely, “the average\nmortality rate in the silk districts is exceptionally high and\namong the female part of the population itself higher than in the\ncotton districts of Lancashire” [Note: l. c. p. 27.","kind":"passage","sequence":295,"address":"8.295","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0295"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0296","text":"In general, the working population subject to the Factory Act has\nphysically improved greatly. All medical testimonies agree on this point,\nand personal observation of my own at different periods has] . Despite the half-yearly repeated\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"271\", \"facs\": \"#f0290\"} --> protest of the factory inspectors, the abuse continues to this hour\n[Note: It is known how reluctantly the English “free-traders” renounced the protective\ntariff for silk manufacture. In place of protection against French imports,\nthe defencelessness of English factory children now serves as protection.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":296,"address":"8.296","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0296"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0297","text":"The law of 1850 transformed, only for “young persons and\nfemales,” the fifteen-hour period from half past 5 in the morning to\nhalf past 8 in the evening into the twelve-hour period from 6 o’clock in the\nmorning to 6 o’clock in the evening. Thus not for children, who still\nremained exploitable for half an hour before the beginning and 2½ hours\nafter the close of this period, although the total duration of their labour\nwas not permitted to exceed 6½ hours. During the discussion of the law,\nthe factory inspectors submitted to Parliament statistics concerning the infamous\nabuses of that anomaly. But in vain. In the background lurked the intention\nto screw up the working day of adult workers, with\n[Note: convinced me of it. Nevertheless, and apart from the enormous mortality\nrate of children in the first years of life, the official reports of Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":297,"address":"8.297","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0297"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0298","text":"Greenhow show the unfavourable state of health in the factory districts, compared\nwith “agricultural districts of normal health.” As proof, among other things, the following\ntable from his report of 1861:","kind":"passage","sequence":298,"address":"8.298","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0298"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0299","text":"|\n\n] <!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"272\", \"facs\": \"#f0291\"} --> the aid of children, once more to 15 hours in years of prosperity.\nThe experience of the following 3 years showed that such an attempt\nmust fail against the resistance of the adult male workers [Note: “Reports etc. for 30th April 1853,” p. 31.] . The Act of 1850 was therefore at last supplemented\nin 1853 by the prohibition “of employing children in the morning before\nand in the evening after the young persons and females.” From then on,\nwith few exceptions, the Factory Act of 1850 regulated, in the branches\nof industry subject to it, the working day of all workers [Note: During the zenith years of the English cotton industry, 1859 and\n1860, some manufacturers attempted, by the lure of high wages for\novertime, to induce the adult male spinners, etc., to prolong\nthe working day.","kind":"passage","sequence":299,"address":"8.299","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0299"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0300","text":"The hand-mule spinners and self-actor minders\nput an end to the experiment by a memorandum to their employers,\nwhich says, among other things: “To speak plainly, our life is a burden to us,\nand so long as we are chained to the factory almost 2 days a week (20 hours)\nlonger than the other workers, we feel like Helots in the land\nand reproach ourselves for perpetuating a system that physically and morally injures\nourselves and our descendants . . . . We therefore hereby give\nrespectful notice that, from New Year’s Day onward, we shall work not a minute more\nthan 60 hours weekly, from 6 o’clock to 6 o’clock, less the statutory pauses of 1½\nhours.” (“Reports etc. for 30th April 1860,” p. 30.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":300,"address":"8.300","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0300"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0301","text":"Since the promul-\ngation of the first Factory Act, half a century had now\nelapsed [Note: On the means which the wording of this law affords for its violation,\ncf. the Parliamentary Return: “Factory Regulations Acts”\n(6 August 1859) and therein Leonard Horner’s “Suggestions for Amend-\ning the Factory Acts to enable the Inspectors to prevent Illegal\nWorking, now become very prevalent.”] .","kind":"passage","sequence":301,"address":"8.301","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0301"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0302","text":"Factory legislation first extended beyond its original sphere\nthrough the ‘Printwork’s Act’ (law concerning calico printing\nworks, etc.) of 1845. The reluctance with which capital permitted this new\n“extravagance” is evident in every line of the Act! It restricts\nthe working day for children from 8—13 years and for females to\n16 hours between 6 o’clock in the morning and 10 o’clock in the evening, without any\nlegal pause for meals. It permits male workers\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"273\", \"facs\": \"#f0292\"} --> over 13 to be worked arbitrarily day and night without interruption [Note: “Children of 8 years and upwards have in fact been worked from 6 o’clock in the morning\nto 9 o’clock in the evening during the last half-year (1857) in my district.”\n(“Reports etc. for 31 Oct. 1857,” p. 39.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":302,"address":"8.302","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0302"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0303","text":"It\nis a parliamentary abortion [Note: “The Printwork’s Act is admitted to be a failure, both with reference to\nits educational and protective provisions.” (“Reports etc. for 31 Oct. 1862,”\np. 52.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":303,"address":"8.303","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0303"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0304","text":"Nevertheless, the principle had triumphed with its victory in the great\n branches of industry, which are the most characteristic creation of the modern mode of production.\n Their wonderful development from 1853—1860, hand in hand\n with the physical and moral rebirth of the factory workers,\n struck the dullest eye. The manufacturers themselves, from whom the legal\n barrier and rule of the working day had been wrested step by step through half a century of civil\n war, vauntingly pointed to the contrast with\n the still “free” fields of exploitation [Note: Thus, for example, E. Potter in a letter to the Times of 24 March 1863. The\nTimes reminds him of the manufacturers’ revolt against the Ten Hours Act.] . The Pharisees of\n “political economy” now proclaimed insight into the necessity\n of a legally regulated working day as a characteristic\n new achievement of their “science” [Note: Thus, among others,","kind":"passage","sequence":304,"address":"8.304","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0304"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0305","text":"Mr W. Newmarch, contributor to and editor of\nTooke’s: “History of Prices”. Is it scientific progress to make cowardly concessions\n to public opinion?] . It is readily understood that,\n after the factory magnates had submitted to the inevitable and reconciled themselves\n to it, the power of resistance of capital gradually weakened,\n while at the same time the power of attack of the working class grew with the number\n of its allies in those strata of society not directly interested.\n Hence comparatively rapid progress since 1860.","kind":"passage","sequence":305,"address":"8.305","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0305"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0306","text":"The dye-works and bleaching-works [Note: The Act concerning bleaching-works and dye-works enacted in 1860 provides that\n the working day shall be reduced, provisionally on 1 August 1861 to 12, and definitively\n on 1 August 1862 to 10 hours, i.e. 10½ for weekdays and 7½ for Saturdays.\n When the evil year 1862 then arrived, the old farce was repeated.\n The gentlemen manufacturers petitioned Parliament to permit, for only one single year\n longer, the twelve-hour employment of young persons and women . . . . “In the present state of business (at the time of the cotton\n famine) it would be a great advantage for the workers if they were permitted]  were subjected in 1860, the lace factories\n [Running matter: I. 18]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"274\", \"facs\": \"#f0293\"} --> and hosiery manufacture in 1861, to the Factory Act of 1850.","kind":"passage","sequence":306,"address":"8.306","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0306"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0307","text":"As a consequence of the first report of the “Commission on the Employment\n of Children” (1863), the manufacture of all earthenware (not merely potteries),\n matches, percussion caps, cartridges, wallpaper factories, cotton-velvet shearing\n (fustian cutting), and numerous processes\n comprised under the term “finishing” (final dressing), shared the same fate.\n In the year 1863 “bleaching in the open air” [Note: The “bleachers in the open air” had evaded the Act of 1860 concerning\n “bleaching-works” by the lie that they did not employ women at night.\n The lie was exposed by the factory inspectors, while at the same time Parliament was deprived,\n through workers’ petitions, of its meadow-fragrant, cool notions\n of “bleaching in the open air.”","kind":"passage","sequence":307,"address":"8.307","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0307"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0308","text":"In this open-air bleaching,\n drying rooms of 90 to 100 degrees Fahrenheit are used, in which chiefly girls\n work. “Cooling” is the technical expression for their occasional\n escape from the drying room into the open air. “Fifteen girls\n in the drying rooms. Heat from 80 to 90° for linen, from 100° and more\n for cambrics. Twelve girls iron and lay out (the cambrics etc.) in a\n small room of approximately 10 square feet, with a tightly enclosed\n stove in the middle. The girls stand around the stove, which radiates a dreadful glow\n and rapidly dries the cambrics for the ironers. The number of hours\n for these hands is unrestricted. When busy, they work until 9 or\n 12 o’clock at night for many days in succession.” (Reports etc. for 31st Oct.\n 1862”, p. 56.)","kind":"passage","sequence":308,"address":"8.308","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0308"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0309","text":"A doctor declares: “No special hours are allowed for\n cooling, but when the temperature becomes too unbearable, or the hands\n of the women workers become soiled with sweat, they are permitted a few]  and baking\n [Note: to continue for minutes … My experience in treating the diseases of these women workers\n compels me to state that their state of health is far below that of the cotton women workers (and\n capital had painted them, in its petitions to Parliament, as Rembrandtishly\n over-healthy!).\n It had already succeeded in bringing a Bill in this sense before the House of Commons. It fell\n before the agitation of the workers in the bleaching-works of Scotland.”\n (“Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1862”, pp. 14, 15.) Thus defeated by the workers\n themselves, in whose name it pretended to speak, capital now discovered, with the help\n of juridical spectacles, that the Act of 1860, like all Parliamentary Acts for the\n “protection of labour”, was drafted in mind-confusing verbal convolutions, which afforded\n a pretext for excluding the “calenderers” and “finishers” from its operation.","kind":"passage","sequence":309,"address":"8.309","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0309"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0310","text":"English jurisprudence, ever the faithful servant of capital,\n sanctioned the quibbling through the Court of “Common Pleas”. “It has\n aroused great dissatisfaction among the workers, and it is very regrettable that\n the clear intention of the legislation should be frustrated on the pretext of a defective definition\n of words.” (l. c. p. 18.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"275\", \"facs\": \"#f0294\"} --> were placed under separate Acts, the first of which, among other things, prohibits the work of children,\n young persons, and women at night (from 8 o’clock in the evening until 6 o’clock\n in the morning), and the second the employment of bakers under 18 years\n of age between 9 o’clock in the evening and 5 o’clock in the morning. We shall return\n to the later proposals of the aforesaid Commission, which threaten, with the exception of\n agriculture, mining, and transport, to deprive all the more important English\n branches of industry of “freedom”.","kind":"passage","sequence":310,"address":"8.310","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0310"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0311","text":"The reader will recall that the production of surplus-value,\n or the extraction of surplus-labour, forms the specific content and\n purpose of capitalist production, apart from\n every transformation of the mode of production itself that may perhaps arise from the subordination\n of labour to capital. He will recall that, from the standpoint hitherto developed, only the independent\n and hence legally adult worker contracts as a commodity-\n [Note: minutes … My experience in treating the diseases of these women workers\n compels me to state that their state of health is far below that of the cotton women workers (and\n capital had painted them, in its petitions to Parliament, as Rembrandtishly\n over-healthy!).","kind":"passage","sequence":311,"address":"8.311","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0311"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0312","text":"Their most conspicuous diseases are phthisis, bron-\n chitis, uterine diseases, hysteria in its most dreadful form, and rheumatism.\n All these arise, I believe, directly or indirectly, from the overheated\n air of their workrooms and the lack of sufficiently comfortable clothing\n to protect them, when going home during the winter months, from the cold damp\n atmosphere.” l. c. pp. 56, 57. The factory inspectors remark\n concerning the Act of 1863 subsequently wrested from the jovial “bleachers in the open air”:\n “This Act has not only failed to afford workers the protection\n which it appears to afford . . . . it is so worded that protection only\n begins as soon as children and women are caught at work after 8 o’clock in the evening,\n and even then the prescribed method of proof is so entangled,\n that punishment can scarcely follow.” (l. c.","kind":"passage","sequence":312,"address":"8.312","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0312"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0313","text":"p. 52.) “As an Act with humane\n and educational purposes, it is wholly a failure. One can scarcely call it humane\n to allow women and children, or, which amounts to the same thing,\n to compel them, to work 14 hours daily,\n with or without meals, as it may happen, and perhaps even longer\n hours, without limit with regard to age, without distinction of sex,\n and without regard to the social customs of the families in whose neighbourhood\n the bleaching works are situated.” (“Reports etc.\nfor 30th April 1863”, p. 40.)]\n [Running matter: 18*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"276\", \"facs\": \"#f0295\"} --> seller with the capitalist.","kind":"passage","sequence":313,"address":"8.313","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0313"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0314","text":"If, therefore, in our historical\n sketch modern industry plays a principal role on the one hand\n and labour by those physically and legally immature on the other,\n the former was for us only a particular sphere, the latter only an especially\n striking example of the sucking out of labour. Without, however, anticipating\n later development, the mere connexion of the historical facts entails:","kind":"passage","sequence":314,"address":"8.314","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0314"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0315","text":"Firstly: In the industries first revolutionised by water, steam, and machinery,\n in these first creations of the modern mode of production,\n the cotton-, wool-, flax-, and silk-spinning and weaving mills, the drive of capital toward the boundless and ruthless\n prolongation of the working day is first satisfied. The altered\n material mode of production and the correspondingly altered social\n relations of the producers [Note: “The conduct of each of these classes (capitalists and workmen) has\n been the result of the relative situation in which they have been placed.”\n (“Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1848”, p. 112.)]  first create the boundless excess\n and then, in opposition, call forth social control, which legally limits, regulates, and uni-\n formises the working day with its pauses.","kind":"passage","sequence":315,"address":"8.315","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0315"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0316","text":"This control therefore appears during the first half of the\n 19th century merely as exceptional legislation [Note: “The employments placed under restriction were connected with the\n manufacture of textile fabrics by the aid of steam or water power. There were\n two conditions to which an employment must be subject to cause it to be inspected,\n viz. the use of steam or water power, and the manufacture of certain specified\n fibres.” (“Reports etc. for 31. October 1864”, p. 8.)] . As soon as it\n had conquered the original territory of the new mode of production, it was found that,\n meanwhile, not only had many other branches of production entered into the proper factory\n regime, but also that manufactures with a more\n or less antiquated mode of operation, such as potteries, glassworks, etc.,\n that old-fashioned crafts, such as baking, and finally even\n dispersed so-called domestic labour, such as nail-making, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":316,"address":"8.316","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0316"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0317","text":"[Note: Extremely abundant material on the condition of this so-called domestic industry\n is to be found in the latest reports of the “Children’s Employment\n Commission”.] , had long\n been just as much subject to capitalist exploitation as\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"277\", \"facs\": \"#f0296\"} --> the factory. Legislation was therefore compelled gradually to cast off its exceptional\n character, or, where it proceeds in Roman casuistic fashion,\n as in England, to declare at will any house in which work is done a\n factory [Note: “The Acts of last Session (1864) … embrace a diversity of occupations\n the customs in which differ greatly, and the use of mechanical power to give\n motion to machinery is no longer one of the elements necessary, as formerly, to\n constitute in legal phrase a Factory.” (“Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1864”,\n p. 8.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":317,"address":"8.317","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4DAR:P0317"]}],"source_digest":"d349850d7fa32553295c364253ab8866b4fc3122bcd4ec7ed0adbeba60217378","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2","title":"4) Der Arbeitstag. · reading part 2/2","order":9,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_4D_2","text":"Zweitens: Die Geschichte der Reglung des Arbeitstags in einigen\n Produktionsweisen, in andern der noch fortdauernde Kampf um diese\n Reglung, beweisen handgreiflich, dass der vereinzelte Arbeiter, der\n Arbeiter als „freier“ Verkäufer seiner Arbeitskraft, auf gewisser Reife-\n stufe der kapitalistischen Produktion, widerstandslos unterliegt. Die\n Schöpfung eines Normal-Arbeitstags ist daher das Produkt eines langwie-\n rigen, mehr oder minder versteckten Bürgerkriegs zwischen der Kapita-\n listenklasse und der Arbeiterklasse. Wie der Kampf eröffnet wird im\n Umkreis der modernen Industrie, so spielt er zuerst in ihrem Heimaths-\n land, England [Note: Belgien, das Paradies des kontinentalen Liberalismus, zeigt auch keine\n Spur dieser Bewegung. Selbst in seinen Kohlengruben und Metallminen\n werden Arbeiter beider Geschlechter und von jeder Altersstufe mit vollkommener\n „Freiheit“ für jede Zeitdauer und Zeitperiode konsumirt. Auf je 1000\n darin beschäftigte Personen kommen 733 Männer, 88 Weiber, 135 Jungen und 44\n Mädchen unter 16 Jahren; in den Hochöfen u. s. w. auf je 1000 668 Männer, 149\n Weiber, 98 Jungen und 85 Mädchen unter 16 Jahren. Kömmt nun noch hinzu\n niedriger Arbeitslohn für enorme Ausbeutung reifer und unreifer Arbeitskräfte, im\n Tagesdurchschnitt 2 sh. 8 d. für Männer, 1 sh. 8 d. für Weiber, 1 sh. 2½ d. für\n Jungen. Dafür hat Belgien aber auch 1863, verglichen mit 1850, Quantum und\n Werth seiner Ausfuhr von Kohlen, Eisen u. s. w. ziemlich verdoppelt.] . Die englischen Fabrikarbeiter waren die Preis-\n fechter nicht nur der englischen, sondern der modernen Arbeiterklasse\n überhaupt, wie auch ihre Theoretiker der Theorie des Kapitals zuerst den\n Fehdehandschuh hinwarfen [Note: Als Robert Owen kurz nach dem ersten Decennium dieses Jahrhunderts\n die Nothwendigkeit einer Beschränkung des Arbeitstags nicht nur theoretisch ver-\n trat, sondern den Zehnstundentag wirklich in seine Fabrik zu New-Lanark ein-\n führte, ward das als kommunistische Utopie verlacht, ganz so wie seine „Ver-\n bindung von produktiver Arbeit mit Erziehung der Kinder“, ganz wie die von ihm] . Der Fabrikphilosoph Ure denunzirt es\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"278\", \"facs\": \"#f0297\"} --> daher als unauslöschliche Schmach der englischen Arbeiterklasse, dass sie\n „die Sklaverei der Fabrikakte“ auf ihre Fahne schrieb gegenüber\n dem Kapital, das männlich für „vollkommene Freiheit der Ar-\n beit“ stritt [Note: Ure fzs. Uebers. „Philosophie des Manufactures. Paris\n 1836,“ t. II, p. 39, 40, 67, 77 etc.] .\n\n\n\n\nFrankreich hinkt langsam hinter England her. Es bedarf der\nFebruarrevolution zur Geburt des Zwölfstundengesetzes [Note: In dem Compte Rendu des „Internationalen Statistischen\n Kongresses zu Paris, 1855“, heisst es u. a.: „Das französische Gesetz, das\n die Dauer der täglichen Arbeit in Fabriken und Werkstätten auf 12 Stunden be-\n schränkt, begrenzt diese Arbeit nicht innerhalb bestimmter fixer Stunden\n (Zeitperioden), indem nur für die Kinderarbeit die Periode zwischen 5 Uhr Vor-\n mittags und 9 Uhr Abends vorgeschrieben ist. Daher bedient sich ein Theil der\n Fabrikanten des Rechts, welches ihnen diess verhängnissvolle Schweigen giebt.\n um Tag aus Tag ein, vielleicht mit Ausnahme der Sonntage, ohne Unter-\n brechung arbeiten zu lassen. Sie wenden dazu zwei verschiedne Arbeiterreihen\n an, von denen keine mehr als 12 Stunden in der Werkstätte zubringt, aber das\n Werk des Etablissements dauert Tag und Nacht. Das Gesetz ist befriedigt, aber\n ist es die Humanität ebenfalls?“ Ausser dem „zerstörenden Einfluss der Nacht-\n arbeit auf den menschlichen Organismus“, wird auch „der fatale Einfluss der\n nächtlichen Association beider Geschlechter in denselben trüb erleuchteten Werk-\n stätten“ betont.] ,\n das viel mangelhafter ist als sein englisches Original. Trotzdem macht\n die französische revolutionäre Methode auch ihre eigenthümlichen Vorzüge\n geltend. Mit einem Schlag diktirt sie allen Ateliers und Fabriken ohne\n Unterschied dieselbe Schranke des Arbeitstags, während die\n englische Gesetzgebung bald an diesem Punkt, bald an jenem, dem Druck\n der Verhältnisse widerwillig weicht und auf dem besten Weg ist, einen\n neuen juristischen Rattenkönig auszubrüten [Note: „Z. B. in meinem Distrikt, in denselben Fabrikbaulichkeiten, ist der-\n selbe Fabrikant Bleicher und Färber unter dem „Bleicherei- und Färberei Akt“,\n Drucker unter dem „Print Works Act“ und finisher unter dem „Fabrikakt“ . . . .\n (Report of Mr. Redgrave in „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1861“, p. 20.)\n Nach Aufzählung der verschiednen Bestimmungen dieser Akte und der daher fol-] . Andrerseits proklamirt\n [Note: ins Leben gerufenen Cooperationsgeschäfte der Arbeiter. Heutzutage ist die erste\n Utopie Fabrikgesetz, die zweite figurirt als officielle Phrase in allen „Fac-\n tory Acts“ und die dritte dient sogar schon zum Deckmantel reaktionärer\n Schwindeleien.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"279\", \"facs\": \"#f0298\"} --> das französische Gesetz prinzipiell, was in England nur im Namen von\n Kindern, Unmündigen und Frauenzimmern erkämpft und erst neuerdings\n als allgemeines Recht beansprucht wird [Note: So getrauen sich endlich die Fabrikinspektoren zu sagen: „These ob-\n jections (des Kapitals gegen legale Beschränkung der Arbeitszeit) must succumb\n before the broad principle of the rights of labour … there is a time when the\n master’s right in his workman’s labour ceases and his time becomes his own, even\n if there was no exhaustion in the question.“ („Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1862“,\n p. 54.)] .\n\n\n\n\nIn den Vereinigten Staaten von Nordamerika blieb jede\n selbstständige Arbeiterbewegung gelähmt, so lange die Sklaverei einen\n Theil der Republik verunstaltete. Die Arbeit in weisser Haut kann sich\n dort nicht emancipiren, wo sie in schwarzer Haut gebrandmarkt wird.\n Aber aus dem Tod der Sklaverei entspross sofort ein neu verjüngtes\n Leben. Die erste Frucht des Bürgerkriegs war die Achtstunden-\n agitation, mit den Siebenmeilenstiefeln der Lokomotive vom atlanti-\n schen bis zum stillen Ocean ausschreitend, von Neuengland bis nach Kali-\n fornien. Der allgemeine Arbeiterkongress zu Baltimore (16.\n August 1866) erklärt: „Das erste und grosse Erheischniss der Gegen-\n wart, um die Arbeit dieses Landes von der kapitalistischen Sklaverei zu\n befreien, ist der Erlass eines Gesetzes, wodurch 8 Stunden den\n Normal-Arbeitstag in allen Staaten der amerikanischen Union bilden\n sollen. Wir sind entschlossen alle unsre Macht aufzubieten, bis diess\n glorreiche Resultat erreicht ist“ [Note: „Wir, die Arbeiter von Dunkirk, erklären, dass die unter dem jetzigen\n System erheischte Länge der Arbeitszeit zu gross ist und dem Arbeiter keine Zeit\n für Erholung und Entwickelung lässt, ihn vielmehr auf einen Zustand der\n Knechtschaft herabdrückt, der wenig besser als die Sklaverei ist\n („a condition of servitude but little better than slavery“). Desshalb beschlossen,\n dass 8 Stunden für einen Arbeitstag genügen und legal als genügend anerkannt\n werden müssen; dass wir zu unsrem Beistand die Presse anrufen, den gewaltigen\n Hebel … und alle, die diesen Beistand versagen, als Feinde der Arbeitsreform\n und Arbeiterrechte betrachten.“ (Beschlüsse der Arbeiter zu Dunkirk,\n Staat New-York, 1866.)] . Gleichzeitig (Anfang September\n [Note: genden Komplikation, sagt Herr Redgrave: „Man sieht, wie schwer es sein muss\n die Vollziehung dieser 3 Parlamentsakte zu sichern, wenn der Fabrikeigner das\n Gesetz zu umgehn beliebt.“ Was aber den Herrn Juristen dadurch gesichert ist,\n sind Prozesse.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"280\", \"facs\": \"#f0299\"} --> 1866) beschloss der „Internationale Arbeiterkongress“ zu\n Genf auf Vorschlag der Londoner Centralbehörde: „Wir erklären die Be-\n schränkung des Arbeitstags für eine vorläufige Bedingung, ohne\n welche alle andern Bestrebungen nach Emancipation scheitern müssen …\n Wir schlagen 8 Arbeitsstunden als legale Schranke des Ar-\n beitstags vor“.\n\n\n\n\nSo besiegelt die auf beiden Seiten des atlantischen Meers instinktiv\n aus den Produktionsverhältnissen selbst erwachsne Arbeiterbewegung den\n Ausspruch des englischen Fabrikinspektors R. J. Saunders: „Weitere\n Schritte zur Reform der Gesellschaft sind niemals mit irgend einer Aus-\n sicht auf Erfolg durchzuführen, wenn nicht zuvor der Arbeitstag beschränkt\n und seine vorgeschriebene Schranke strikt erzwungen wird“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1848“, p. 112.] .\n\n\n\n\nMan muss gestehn, dass unser Arbeiter anders aus dem Produktions-\n prozess herauskömmt als er in ihn eintrat. Auf dem Markt trat er als Be-\n sitzer der Waare „Arbeitskraft“ andern Waarenbesitzern gegenüber,\n Waarenbesitzer dem Waarenbesitzer. Der Kontrakt, wodurch er dem\n Kapitalisten seine Arbeitskraft verkaufte, schien durch den freien Willen\n von Verkäufer und Käufer vereinbartes Produkt. Nach geschlossnem\n Handel wird entdeckt, dass er „kein freier Agent“ war, dass die\n Zeit, wofür es ihm freisteht seine Arbeitskraft zu verkaufen, die Zeit ist,\n wofür er gezwungen ist sie zu verkaufen [Note: „These proceedings (die Manöver des Kapitals z. B. 1848—50) have\n afforded, moreover, incontrovertible proof of the fallacy of the asser-\n tion so often advanced, that operatives need no protection, but may be\nconsidered as free agents in the disposal of the only property\n they possess, the labour of their hands, and the sweat of their brows.“\n („Reports etc. for 30. April 1850“, p. 45). „Free Labour, if so it\n may be termed, even in a free country, requires the strong arm of the law to\n protect it.“ („Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1864“, p. 34.) „To permit,\n which is tantamount to compelling . . . . to work 14 hours a day with\n or without meals etc.“ („Reports etc. for 30. April 1863“, p. 40.)] , dass in der That sein\n Sauger nicht loslässt, „so lange noch ein Muskel, eine Sehne, ein Tropfen\n Bluts auszubeuten“ [Note: Friedrich Engels l. c. p. 5.] . Zum „Schutze“ gegen die Schlange ihrer Qualen\n müssen die Arbeiter ihre Köpfe zusammenrotten und als Klasse ein\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"281\", \"facs\": \"#f0300\"} --> Staatsgesetz erzwingen, ein übermächtiges gesellschaftliches Hin-\n derniss, das sie selbst verhindert, durch freiwilligen Kontrakt\n mit dem Kapital sich und ihre Generation in Tod und Sklaverei zu\n verkaufen [Note: Die Zehnstundenbill hat in den ihr unterworfenen Industriezweigen „die\n Arbeiter vor gänzlicher Degeneration gerettet und ihren physischen\n Zustand beschützt.“ („Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1859“, p. 47—52.) „Das\n Kapital (in den Fabriken) kann niemals die Maschinerie in Bewegung halten über\n eine begrenzte Zeitperiode ohne die beschäftigten Arbeiter an ihrer Gesundheit und\n ihrer Moral zu beschädigen; und sie sind nicht in einer Lage, sich\n selbst zu schützen.“ l. c. p. 8.] . An die Stelle des prunkvollen Katalogs der „unver-\n äusserlichen Menschenrechte“ tritt die bescheidne Magna Charta eines\n gesetzlich beschränkten Arbeitstags, die „endlich klar macht, wann die\n Zeit, die der Arbeiter verkauft, endet, und wann die ihm\n selbst gehörige Zeit beginnt“ [Note: „A still greater boon is, the distinetion at least made clear between\n the worker’s own time and his master’s. The worker knows now when\n that which he sells is ended, and when his own begins, and, by\n possessing a sure foreknowledge of this, is enabled to pre-arrange his own minutes\n for his own purposes.“ (l. c. p. 52.) „By making them masters of their own time,\n they (die Fabrikgesetze) have given them a moral energy which is directing them to\n the eventual possession of political power.“ (l. c. p. 47.) Mit verhaltner Ironie, und\n in sehr vorsichtigen Ausdrücken, deuten die Fabrikinspektoren an, dass das jetzige\n Zehnstundengesetz auch den Kapitalisten einigermassen von seiner naturwüchsigen\n Brutalität als blosser Verkörperung des Kapitals befreit und ihm Zeit zu einiger\n „Bildung“ gegeben habe. Vorher „the master had no time for anything but\nmoney: the servant had no time for anything but labour.“ l. c. p. 48.] . Quantum mutatus ab illo!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0001","text":"Zweitens: Die Geschichte der Reglung des Arbeitstags in einigen\n Produktionsweisen, in andern der noch fortdauernde Kampf um diese\n Reglung, beweisen handgreiflich, dass der vereinzelte Arbeiter, der\n Arbeiter als „freier“ Verkäufer seiner Arbeitskraft, auf gewisser Reife-\n stufe der kapitalistischen Produktion, widerstandslos unterliegt. Die\n Schöpfung eines Normal-Arbeitstags ist daher das Produkt eines langwie-\n rigen, mehr oder minder versteckten Bürgerkriegs zwischen der Kapita-\n listenklasse und der Arbeiterklasse. Wie der Kampf eröffnet wird im\n Umkreis der modernen Industrie, so spielt er zuerst in ihrem Heimaths-\n land, England [Note: Belgien, das Paradies des kontinentalen Liberalismus, zeigt auch keine\n Spur dieser Bewegung.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"9.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0002","text":"Selbst in seinen Kohlengruben und Metallminen\n werden Arbeiter beider Geschlechter und von jeder Altersstufe mit vollkommener\n „Freiheit“ für jede Zeitdauer und Zeitperiode konsumirt. Auf je 1000\n darin beschäftigte Personen kommen 733 Männer, 88 Weiber, 135 Jungen und 44\n Mädchen unter 16 Jahren; in den Hochöfen u. s. w. auf je 1000 668 Männer, 149\n Weiber, 98 Jungen und 85 Mädchen unter 16 Jahren. Kömmt nun noch hinzu\n niedriger Arbeitslohn für enorme Ausbeutung reifer und unreifer Arbeitskräfte, im\n Tagesdurchschnitt 2 sh. 8 d. für Männer, 1 sh. 8 d. für Weiber, 1 sh. 2½ d. für\n Jungen. Dafür hat Belgien aber auch 1863, verglichen mit 1850, Quantum und\n Werth seiner Ausfuhr von Kohlen, Eisen u. s. w. ziemlich verdoppelt.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"9.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0003","text":"Die englischen Fabrikarbeiter waren die Preis-\n fechter nicht nur der englischen, sondern der modernen Arbeiterklasse\n überhaupt, wie auch ihre Theoretiker der Theorie des Kapitals zuerst den\n Fehdehandschuh hinwarfen [Note: Als Robert Owen kurz nach dem ersten Decennium dieses Jahrhunderts\n die Nothwendigkeit einer Beschränkung des Arbeitstags nicht nur theoretisch ver-\n trat, sondern den Zehnstundentag wirklich in seine Fabrik zu New-Lanark ein-\n führte, ward das als kommunistische Utopie verlacht, ganz so wie seine „Ver-\n bindung von produktiver Arbeit mit Erziehung der Kinder“, ganz wie die von ihm] .","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"9.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0004","text":"Der Fabrikphilosoph Ure denunzirt es\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"278\", \"facs\": \"#f0297\"} --> daher als unauslöschliche Schmach der englischen Arbeiterklasse, dass sie\n „die Sklaverei der Fabrikakte“ auf ihre Fahne schrieb gegenüber\n dem Kapital, das männlich für „vollkommene Freiheit der Ar-\n beit“ stritt [Note: Ure fzs. Uebers. „Philosophie des Manufactures. Paris\n 1836,“ t. II, p. 39, 40, 67, 77 etc.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"9.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0005","text":"Frankreich hinkt langsam hinter England her. Es bedarf der\nFebruarrevolution zur Geburt des Zwölfstundengesetzes [Note: In dem Compte Rendu des „Internationalen Statistischen\n Kongresses zu Paris, 1855“, heisst es u. a.: „Das französische Gesetz, das\n die Dauer der täglichen Arbeit in Fabriken und Werkstätten auf 12 Stunden be-\n schränkt, begrenzt diese Arbeit nicht innerhalb bestimmter fixer Stunden\n (Zeitperioden), indem nur für die Kinderarbeit die Periode zwischen 5 Uhr Vor-\n mittags und 9 Uhr Abends vorgeschrieben ist. Daher bedient sich ein Theil der\n Fabrikanten des Rechts, welches ihnen diess verhängnissvolle Schweigen giebt.\n um Tag aus Tag ein, vielleicht mit Ausnahme der Sonntage, ohne Unter-\n brechung arbeiten zu lassen.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"9.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0006","text":"Sie wenden dazu zwei verschiedne Arbeiterreihen\n an, von denen keine mehr als 12 Stunden in der Werkstätte zubringt, aber das\n Werk des Etablissements dauert Tag und Nacht. Das Gesetz ist befriedigt, aber\n ist es die Humanität ebenfalls?“ Ausser dem „zerstörenden Einfluss der Nacht-\n arbeit auf den menschlichen Organismus“, wird auch „der fatale Einfluss der\n nächtlichen Association beider Geschlechter in denselben trüb erleuchteten Werk-\n stätten“ betont.] ,\n das viel mangelhafter ist als sein englisches Original. Trotzdem macht\n die französische revolutionäre Methode auch ihre eigenthümlichen Vorzüge\n geltend.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"9.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0007","text":"Mit einem Schlag diktirt sie allen Ateliers und Fabriken ohne\n Unterschied dieselbe Schranke des Arbeitstags, während die\n englische Gesetzgebung bald an diesem Punkt, bald an jenem, dem Druck\n der Verhältnisse widerwillig weicht und auf dem besten Weg ist, einen\n neuen juristischen Rattenkönig auszubrüten [Note: „Z. B. in meinem Distrikt, in denselben Fabrikbaulichkeiten, ist der-\n selbe Fabrikant Bleicher und Färber unter dem „Bleicherei- und Färberei Akt“,\n Drucker unter dem „Print Works Act“ und finisher unter dem „Fabrikakt“ . . . .\n (Report of Mr. Redgrave in „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1861“, p. 20.)\n Nach Aufzählung der verschiednen Bestimmungen dieser Akte und der daher fol-] . Andrerseits proklamirt\n [Note: ins Leben gerufenen Cooperationsgeschäfte der Arbeiter.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"9.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0008","text":"Heutzutage ist die erste\n Utopie Fabrikgesetz, die zweite figurirt als officielle Phrase in allen „Fac-\n tory Acts“ und die dritte dient sogar schon zum Deckmantel reaktionärer\n Schwindeleien.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"279\", \"facs\": \"#f0298\"} --> das französische Gesetz prinzipiell, was in England nur im Namen von\n Kindern, Unmündigen und Frauenzimmern erkämpft und erst neuerdings\n als allgemeines Recht beansprucht wird [Note: So getrauen sich endlich die Fabrikinspektoren zu sagen: „These ob-\n jections (des Kapitals gegen legale Beschränkung der Arbeitszeit) must succumb\n before the broad principle of the rights of labour … there is a time when the\n master’s right in his workman’s labour ceases and his time becomes his own, even\n if there was no exhaustion in the question.“ („Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1862“,\n p. 54.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"9.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0009","text":"In den Vereinigten Staaten von Nordamerika blieb jede\n selbstständige Arbeiterbewegung gelähmt, so lange die Sklaverei einen\n Theil der Republik verunstaltete. Die Arbeit in weisser Haut kann sich\n dort nicht emancipiren, wo sie in schwarzer Haut gebrandmarkt wird.\n Aber aus dem Tod der Sklaverei entspross sofort ein neu verjüngtes\n Leben. Die erste Frucht des Bürgerkriegs war die Achtstunden-\n agitation, mit den Siebenmeilenstiefeln der Lokomotive vom atlanti-\n schen bis zum stillen Ocean ausschreitend, von Neuengland bis nach Kali-\n fornien. Der allgemeine Arbeiterkongress zu Baltimore (16.\n August 1866) erklärt: „Das erste und grosse Erheischniss der Gegen-\n wart, um die Arbeit dieses Landes von der kapitalistischen Sklaverei zu\n befreien, ist der Erlass eines Gesetzes, wodurch 8 Stunden den\n Normal-Arbeitstag in allen Staaten der amerikanischen Union bilden\n sollen.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"9.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0010","text":"Wir sind entschlossen alle unsre Macht aufzubieten, bis diess\n glorreiche Resultat erreicht ist“ [Note: „Wir, die Arbeiter von Dunkirk, erklären, dass die unter dem jetzigen\n System erheischte Länge der Arbeitszeit zu gross ist und dem Arbeiter keine Zeit\n für Erholung und Entwickelung lässt, ihn vielmehr auf einen Zustand der\n Knechtschaft herabdrückt, der wenig besser als die Sklaverei ist\n („a condition of servitude but little better than slavery“). Desshalb beschlossen,\n dass 8 Stunden für einen Arbeitstag genügen und legal als genügend anerkannt\n werden müssen; dass wir zu unsrem Beistand die Presse anrufen, den gewaltigen\n Hebel … und alle, die diesen Beistand versagen, als Feinde der Arbeitsreform\n und Arbeiterrechte betrachten.“ (Beschlüsse der Arbeiter zu Dunkirk,\n Staat New-York, 1866.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"9.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0011","text":"Gleichzeitig (Anfang September\n [Note: genden Komplikation, sagt Herr Redgrave: „Man sieht, wie schwer es sein muss\n die Vollziehung dieser 3 Parlamentsakte zu sichern, wenn der Fabrikeigner das\n Gesetz zu umgehn beliebt.“ Was aber den Herrn Juristen dadurch gesichert ist,\n sind Prozesse.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"280\", \"facs\": \"#f0299\"} --> 1866) beschloss der „Internationale Arbeiterkongress“ zu\n Genf auf Vorschlag der Londoner Centralbehörde: „Wir erklären die Be-\n schränkung des Arbeitstags für eine vorläufige Bedingung, ohne\n welche alle andern Bestrebungen nach Emancipation scheitern müssen …\n Wir schlagen 8 Arbeitsstunden als legale Schranke des Ar-\n beitstags vor“.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"9.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0012","text":"So besiegelt die auf beiden Seiten des atlantischen Meers instinktiv\n aus den Produktionsverhältnissen selbst erwachsne Arbeiterbewegung den\n Ausspruch des englischen Fabrikinspektors R. J. Saunders: „Weitere\n Schritte zur Reform der Gesellschaft sind niemals mit irgend einer Aus-\n sicht auf Erfolg durchzuführen, wenn nicht zuvor der Arbeitstag beschränkt\n und seine vorgeschriebene Schranke strikt erzwungen wird“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1848“, p. 112.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"9.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0013","text":"Man muss gestehn, dass unser Arbeiter anders aus dem Produktions-\n prozess herauskömmt als er in ihn eintrat. Auf dem Markt trat er als Be-\n sitzer der Waare „Arbeitskraft“ andern Waarenbesitzern gegenüber,\n Waarenbesitzer dem Waarenbesitzer. Der Kontrakt, wodurch er dem\n Kapitalisten seine Arbeitskraft verkaufte, schien durch den freien Willen\n von Verkäufer und Käufer vereinbartes Produkt. Nach geschlossnem\n Handel wird entdeckt, dass er „kein freier Agent“ war, dass die\n Zeit, wofür es ihm freisteht seine Arbeitskraft zu verkaufen, die Zeit ist,\n wofür er gezwungen ist sie zu verkaufen [Note: „These proceedings (die Manöver des Kapitals z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"9.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0014","text":"1848—50) have\n afforded, moreover, incontrovertible proof of the fallacy of the asser-\n tion so often advanced, that operatives need no protection, but may be\nconsidered as free agents in the disposal of the only property\n they possess, the labour of their hands, and the sweat of their brows.“\n („Reports etc. for 30. April 1850“, p. 45). „Free Labour, if so it\n may be termed, even in a free country, requires the strong arm of the law to\n protect it.“ („Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1864“, p. 34.) „To permit,\n which is tantamount to compelling . . . . to work 14 hours a day with\n or without meals etc.“ („Reports etc. for 30. April 1863“, p. 40.)] , dass in der That sein\n Sauger nicht loslässt, „so lange noch ein Muskel, eine Sehne, ein Tropfen\n Bluts auszubeuten“ [Note: Friedrich Engels l. c. p. 5.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"9.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0015","text":"Zum „Schutze“ gegen die Schlange ihrer Qualen\n müssen die Arbeiter ihre Köpfe zusammenrotten und als Klasse ein\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"281\", \"facs\": \"#f0300\"} --> Staatsgesetz erzwingen, ein übermächtiges gesellschaftliches Hin-\n derniss, das sie selbst verhindert, durch freiwilligen Kontrakt\n mit dem Kapital sich und ihre Generation in Tod und Sklaverei zu\n verkaufen [Note: Die Zehnstundenbill hat in den ihr unterworfenen Industriezweigen „die\n Arbeiter vor gänzlicher Degeneration gerettet und ihren physischen\n Zustand beschützt.“ („Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1859“, p. 47—52.) „Das\n Kapital (in den Fabriken) kann niemals die Maschinerie in Bewegung halten über\n eine begrenzte Zeitperiode ohne die beschäftigten Arbeiter an ihrer Gesundheit und\n ihrer Moral zu beschädigen; und sie sind nicht in einer Lage, sich\n selbst zu schützen.“ l. c. p. 8.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"9.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0016","text":"An die Stelle des prunkvollen Katalogs der „unver-\n äusserlichen Menschenrechte“ tritt die bescheidne Magna Charta eines\n gesetzlich beschränkten Arbeitstags, die „endlich klar macht, wann die\n Zeit, die der Arbeiter verkauft, endet, und wann die ihm\n selbst gehörige Zeit beginnt“ [Note: „A still greater boon is, the distinetion at least made clear between\n the worker’s own time and his master’s. The worker knows now when\n that which he sells is ended, and when his own begins, and, by\n possessing a sure foreknowledge of this, is enabled to pre-arrange his own minutes\n for his own purposes.“ (l. c. p. 52.) „By making them masters of their own time,\n they (die Fabrikgesetze) have given them a moral energy which is directing them to\n the eventual possession of political power.“ (l. c. p. 47.)","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"9.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0017","text":"Mit verhaltner Ironie, und\n in sehr vorsichtigen Ausdrücken, deuten die Fabrikinspektoren an, dass das jetzige\n Zehnstundengesetz auch den Kapitalisten einigermassen von seiner naturwüchsigen\n Brutalität als blosser Verkörperung des Kapitals befreit und ihm Zeit zu einiger\n „Bildung“ gegeben habe. Vorher „the master had no time for anything but\nmoney: the servant had no time for anything but labour.“ l. c. p. 48.] . Quantum mutatus ab illo!","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"9.17"}],"sha256":"4455fbdfe92a3d63efa5e124d08fd72513039f879dc36cd0baa6a95834f7b299","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T203604602217Z_a8a5391f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"4) Der Arbeitstag. · reading part 2/2","source_sha256":"4455fbdfe92a3d63efa5e124d08fd72513039f879dc36cd0baa6a95834f7b299","sha256":"266442a45d5f8ff6ea778bcd7a5b60376a424d1663c93839796f460a37558435","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:36:04.602248+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0001","text":"Secondly: The history of the regulation of the working day in some\n modes of production, in others the struggle still continuing over this\n regulation, proves palpably that the isolated worker, the worker as a\n “free” seller of his labour-power, at a certain stage of maturity\n of capitalist production, succumbs without resistance. The\n creation of a normal working day is therefore the product of a protracted,\n more or less concealed civil war between the capitalist\n class and the working class. As the struggle is opened in the\n sphere of modern industry, so it first takes place in its native\n land, England [Note: Belgium, the paradise of continental liberalism, likewise displays no\n trace of this movement.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"9.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0002","text":"Even in its coal mines and metal mines\n workers of both sexes and of every age are consumed with perfect\n “freedom” for every duration and period of time. For every 1000\n persons employed therein there are 733 men, 88 women, 135 boys and 44\n girls under 16 years of age; in blast furnaces, etc., for every 1000, 668 men, 149\n women, 98 boys and 85 girls under 16 years of age. Added to this\n are low wages for the enormous exploitation of mature and immature labour-powers, on\n daily average 2 sh. 8 d. for men, 1 sh. 8 d. for women, 1 sh. 2½ d. for\n boys. In return, however, Belgium has, in 1863 as compared with 1850, rather\n doubled the quantity and value of its exports of coal, iron, etc.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"9.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0003","text":"The English factory workers were the champions\n not only of the English, but of the modern working class\n in general, just as their theoreticians were the first to throw down the\n gauntlet to the theory of capital [Note: When Robert Owen, shortly after the first decade of this century,\n not only advanced theoretically the necessity of a limitation of the working day,\n but actually introduced the ten-hour day in his factory at New Lanark,\n this was ridiculed as a communist utopia, quite as were his “combination\n of productive labour with the education of children,” quite as were the by him] .","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"9.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0004","text":"The factory philosopher Ure therefore denounces it\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"278\", \"facs\": \"#f0297\"} --> as an indelible disgrace to the English working class that it\n inscribed “the slavery of the Factory Acts” upon its banner, in opposition\n to capital, which struggled manfully for “complete freedom of la-\n bour” [Note: Ure, French trans., “Philosophie des Manufactures. Paris\n 1836,” t. II, pp. 39, 40, 67, 77 etc.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"9.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0005","text":"France limps slowly behind England. It required the\nFebruary Revolution for the birth of the Twelve-Hour Law [Note: In the Compte Rendu of the “International Statistical\n Congress at Paris, 1855,” it says, among other things: “The French law which\n limits the duration of daily labour in factories and workshops to 12 hours,\n does not delimit this labour within definite fixed hours\n (periods of time), since only for child labour is the period between 5 o’clock in the\n morning and 9 o’clock in the evening prescribed. Hence a part of the\n manufacturers avail themselves of the right which this fateful silence gives them\n to have work carried on day in, day out, perhaps excepting Sundays, without inter-\n ruption.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"9.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0006","text":"For this purpose they employ two different shifts of workers,\n neither of which spends more than 12 hours in the workshop, but the\n work of the establishment lasts day and night. The law is satisfied, but\n is humanity likewise?” Besides the “destructive influence of night-\n work upon the human organism,” there is also stressed “the fatal influence of the\n nocturnal association of the two sexes in the same dimly lighted work-\n shops.”] ,\n which is far more defective than its English original. Nevertheless,\n the French revolutionary method also asserts its peculiar advantages.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"9.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0007","text":"At one stroke it dictates to all workshops and factories without\n distinction the same limit of the working day, whereas English\n legislation now at this point, now at that, reluctantly yields to\n the pressure of circumstances and is well on the way to hatching a\n new juridical rat-king [Note: “For example, in my district, in the same factory buildings, the same\n manufacturer is bleacher and dyer under the ‘Bleaching and Dyeing Act,’\n printer under the ‘Print Works Act,’ and finisher under the ‘Factory Act’ . . . .\n (Report of Mr. Redgrave in ‘Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1861,’ p. 20.)\n After enumerating the various provisions of these Acts and the conse-] . On the other hand it proclaims\n [Note: workers’ co-operative enterprises called into being.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"9.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0008","text":"Nowadays the first\n utopia is Factory Law, the second figures as an official phrase in all “Fac-\n tory Acts,” and the third already serves even as a cloak for reactionary\n swindles.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"279\", \"facs\": \"#f0298\"} --> the French law proclaims in principle what in England has been won only\n in the name of children, minors, and females, and has only recently\n been claimed as a general right [Note: Thus the factory inspectors finally dare to say: “These ob-\n jections (of capital against legal limitation of working time) must succumb\n before the broad principle of the rights of labour … there is a time when the\n master’s right in his workman’s labour ceases and his time becomes his own, even\n if there was no exhaustion in the question.” (“Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1862,”\n p. 54.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"9.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0009","text":"In the United States of North America every\n independent workers’ movement remained paralysed so long as slavery disfigured\n one part of the Republic. Labour in a white skin cannot emancipate itself\n where it is branded in a black skin.\n But out of the death of slavery there immediately sprang a newly rejuvenated\n life. The first fruit of the Civil War was the Eight-Hour\n agitation, striding with the seven-league boots of the locomotive from the Atlan-\n tic to the Pacific Ocean, from New England to Cali-\n fornia. The general workers’ congress at Baltimore (16\n August 1866) declares: “The first and great requirement of the pres-\n ent, in order to free the labour of this country from capitalist slavery,\n is the enactment of a law whereby 8 hours shall constitute the\n normal working day in all States of the American Union.\n","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"9.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0010","text":"We are resolved to exert all our power until this\n glorious result is attained” [Note: “We, the workers of Dunkirk, declare that the length of working time\n required under the present system is too great and leaves the worker no time\n for rest and development, but rather reduces him to a condition of\n servitude little better than slavery\n (‘a condition of servitude but little better than slavery’). We therefore resolved\n that 8 hours suffice for one working day and must be legally recognised as\n sufficient; that we call upon the press, that mighty\n lever … to assist us, and regard all who refuse this assistance as enemies of labour reform\n and workers’ rights.” (Resolutions of the workers at Dunkirk,\n State of New York, 1866.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"9.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0011","text":"At the same time (beginning of September\n [Note: quent complication, Mr. Redgrave says: “One sees how difficult it must be\n to secure the execution of these 3 Acts of Parliament if the factory owner is pleased\n to evade the law.” What is thereby secured for the gentlemen jurists,\n however, are lawsuits.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"280\", \"facs\": \"#f0299\"} --> 1866) the “International Workers’ Congress” at\n Geneva resolved, upon the proposal of the London Central Council: “We declare the limi-\n tation of the working day to be a preliminary condition without\n which all other endeavours after emancipation must fail …\n We propose 8 working hours as the legal limit of the working\n day”.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"9.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0012","text":"Thus the workers’ movement, growing instinctively out of the relations of production\n themselves on both sides of the Atlantic Ocean, seals the\n pronouncement of the English factory inspector R. J. Saunders: “Further\n steps toward the reform of society can never be carried out with any pro-\n spect of success unless the working day is first limited\n and its prescribed limit strictly enforced” [Note: “Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1848,” p. 112.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"9.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0013","text":"It must be admitted that our worker comes out of the process of production\n differently from how he entered it. In the market he confronted other\n commodity owners as the possessor of the commodity “labour-power,”\n commodity owner confronting commodity owner. The contract by which he\n sold his labour-power to the capitalist appeared to be a product agreed upon\n by the free will of seller and buyer. Once the bargain is concluded, it is\n discovered that he “was no free agent,” that the\n time for which he is free to sell his labour-power is the time\n for which he is compelled to sell it [Note: “These proceedings (the manoeuvres of capital, e.g.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"9.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0014","text":"1848—50) have\n afforded, moreover, incontrovertible proof of the fallacy of the asser-\n tion so often advanced, that operatives need no protection, but may be\nconsidered as free agents in the disposal of the only property\n they possess, the labour of their hands, and the sweat of their brows.”\n (“Reports etc. for 30. April 1850,” p. 45). “Free Labour, if so it\n may be termed, even in a free country, requires the strong arm of the law to\n protect it.” (“Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1864,” p. 34.) “To permit,\n which is tantamount to compelling . . . . to work 14 hours a day with\n or without meals etc.” (“Reports etc. for 30. April 1863,” p. 40.)] , that in fact his\n sucker does not let go, “so long as there is still a muscle, a sinew, a drop\n of blood to exploit” [Note: Friedrich Engels l. c. p. 5.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"9.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0015","text":"For “protection” against the serpent of their torments,\n the workers must put their heads together and, as a class, compel a\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"281\", \"facs\": \"#f0300\"} --> state law, an overpowering social obstacle, which itself prevents\n them, by voluntary contract with capital, from selling themselves and their\n generation into death and slavery [Note: The Ten Hours Bill has, in the branches of industry subject to it, “saved\n the workers from complete degeneration and protected their physical\n condition.” (“Reports etc. for 31. Oct. 1859,” pp. 47—52.) “Capital\n (in the factories) can never keep machinery in motion beyond\n a limited period of time without damaging the health and\n morality of the workers employed; and they are not in a position to\n protect themselves.” l. c. p. 8.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"9.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0016","text":"In place of the pompous catalogue of “inalien-\n able human rights” comes the modest Magna Charta of a\n legally limited working day, which “finally makes clear when the\n time that the worker sells ends, and when the time belonging\n to himself begins” [Note: “A still greater boon is, the distinetion at least made clear between\n the worker’s own time and his master’s. The worker knows now when\n that which he sells is ended, and when his own begins, and, by\n possessing a sure foreknowledge of this, is enabled to pre-arrange his own minutes\n for his own purposes.” (l. c. p. 52.) “By making them masters of their own time,\n they (the Factory Acts) have given them a moral energy which is directing them to\n the eventual possession of political power.” (l. c. p. 47.)","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"9.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0017","text":"With restrained irony, and\n in very cautious expressions, the factory inspectors indicate that the present\n Ten Hours Law has also somewhat freed the capitalist from his naturally arisen\n brutality as the mere embodiment of capital and has given him time for some\n “education.” Formerly “the master had no time for anything but\nmoney: the servant had no time for anything but labour.” l. c. p. 48.] . Quantum mutatus ab illo!","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"9.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4D_2:P0017"]}],"source_digest":"648129aa0bb54176923c726f3f2334c93efd19904011faab9fc109f7e10f7fe9","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM","title":"5) Rate und Masse des Mehrwerths.","order":10,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_5RUM","text":"Wie bisher, wird in diesem Paragraph der Werth der Arbeits-\n kraft, also der zur Reproduktion oder Erhaltung der Arbeitskraft noth-\n wendige Theil des Arbeitstags, als gegebne, constante\n Grösse unterstellt.\n\n\n\n\nDiess also vorausgesetzt, ist mit der Rate zugleich die Masse des\n Mehrwerths gegeben, die der einzelne Arbeiter dem Kapitalisten in\n bestimmter Zeitperiode liefert. Beträgt z. B. die nothwendige Arbeit täglich\n 6 Stunden, ausgedrückt in einem Goldquantum von 3 sh. = 1 Thaler, so\n ist 1 Thaler der Tageswerth einer Arbeitskraft, oder der im Ankauf\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"282\", \"facs\": \"#f0301\"} -->einer Arbeitskraft vorgeschossene Kapitalwerth. Ist ferner die Rate\n des Mehrwerths 100 %, so producirt diess variable Kapital von\n 1 Thaler eine Masse Mehrwerth von 1 Thaler, oder der Arbeiter liefert\n täglich eine Masse Mehrarbeit von 6 Stunden.\n\n\n\n\nDas variable Kapital ist aber der Geldausdruck für den Ge-\n sammtwerth aller Arbeitskräfte, die der Kapitalist gleichzeitig\n in einem bestimmten Produktionsprozess verwendet. Ist der Tageswerth\neiner Arbeitskraft 1 Thaler, so ist also ein Kapital von 100 Thalern vor-\n zuschiessen um 100, und von n Thalern, um n Arbeitskräfte täglich zu\n exploitiren. Der Werth des vorgeschossenen variablen Ka-\n pitals ist also gleich dem Durchschnittswerth einer Arbeits-\n kraft multiplicirt mit der Anzahl der verwandten Arbeitskräfte. Bei\n gegebnem Werth der Arbeitskraft wechselt also Werthumfang\n oder Grösse des variablen Kapitals mit der Masse der ange-\n eigneten Arbeitskräfte oder der Anzahl der gleichzeitig beschäftigten\n Arbeiter.\n\n\n\n\nProduzirt ein variables Kapital von 1 Thaler, der Tageswerth einer\n Arbeitskraft, einen täglichen Mehrwerth von 1 Thaler, so ein variables\n Kapital von 100 Thalern einen täglichen Mehrwerth von 100, und eins von\n n Thalern einen täglichen Mehrwerth von 1 Thaler × n. Die Masse\n des producirten Mehrwerths ist also gleich dem Mehrwerth, den\n der Arbeitstag des einzelnen Arbeiters liefert, multiplicirt mit der Anzahl\n der angewandten Arbeiter. Da aber ferner die Masse Mehrwerth, die der\n einzelne Arbeiter producirt, bei gegebenem Werth der Arbeitskraft, durch\n die Rate des Mehrwerths bestimmt ist, so folgt, unter derselben Vor-\n aussetzung: Die Masse des von einem gegebnen variablen\n Kapital producirten Mehrwerths ist gleich der Grösse\n des vorgeschossenen variablen Kapitals multiplicirt mit\n der Rate des Mehrwerths oder ist bestimmt durch das zusammen-\n gesetzte Verhältniss zwischen der Anzahl der gleichzeitig ex-\n ploitirten Arbeitskräfte und dem Exploitationsgrad der\n einzelnen Arbeitskraft.\n\n\n\n\nIn der Produktion einer bestimmten Masse Mehrwerth\n kann daher die Abnahme des einen Faktors durch Zunahme\n des andern ersetzt werden. Wird das variable Kapital vermindert und\n gleichzeitig in demselben Verhältniss die Rate des Mehrwerths\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"283\", \"facs\": \"#f0302\"} --> erhöht, so bleibt die Masse des producirten Mehrwerths un-\n verändert. Muss unter den früheren Annahmen der Kapitalist 100 Tha-\n ler vorschiessen, um 100 Arbeiter täglich zu exploitiren, und beträgt die\n Rate des Mehrwerths 50 %, so wirft diess variable Kapital von 100 einen\n Mehrwerth von 50 ab, oder von 100 × 3 Arbeitsstunden. Wird die\n Rate des Mehrwerths verdoppelt, oder der Arbeitstag statt von 6 zu 9,\n von 6 zu 12 Stunden verlängert, so wirft das um die Hälfte verminderte\n variable Kapital von 50 Thalern ebenfalls einen Mehrwerth von 50 Thalern\n ab oder von 50 × 6 Arbeitsstunden. Verminderung des variablen Kapi-\n tals ist also ausgleichbar durch proportionelle Erhöhung im Exploitations-\n grad der Arbeitskraft oder Abnahme in der Anzahl der beschäftigten Ar-\n beiter durch proportionelle Verlängerung des Arbeitstags. Innerhalb ge-\n wisser Grenzen wird die vom Kapital erpressbare Zufuhr der Arbeit\n also unabhängig von der Arbeiterzufuhr [Note: Diess Elementargesetz scheint den Herren von der Vulgärökonomie un-\n bekannt, die, umgekehrte Archimedes, in der Bestimmung der Marktpreise der\n Arbeit durch Nachfrage und Zufuhr den Punkt gefunden zu haben glauben, nicht\n um die Welt aus den Angeln zu heben, sondern um sie stillzusetzen.] . Umgekehrt lässt Ab-\n nahmein der Rate des Mehrwerths die Masse des produ-\n cirten Mehrwerths unverändert, wenn proportionell die Grösse des\n variablen Kapitals oder die Anzahl der beschäftigten Arbeiter wächst.\n\n\n\n\nIndess hat der Ersatz von Arbeiteranzahl oder Grösse des variablen\n Kapitals durch gesteigerte Rate des Mehrwerths oder Verlängerung des\n Arbeitstags unüberspringbare absolute Schranken. Welches immer der\nWerth der Arbeitskraft sei, ob daher die zur Erhaltung des Arbei-\n ters nothwendige Arbeitszeit 2 oder 10 Stunden betrage, der Ge-\n sammtwerth, den ein Arbeiter, Tag aus Tag ein, produciren kann, ist\n immer kleiner als der Werth, worin sich 24 Arbeitsstunden vergegen-\n ständlichen, kleiner als 12 sh. oder 4 Thaler, wenn diess der Geldausdruck\n der 24 vergegenständlichten Arbeitsstunden. Unter unsrer früheren An-\n nahme, wonach täglich 6 Arbeitsstunden erheischt, um die Arbeitskraft\n selbst zu reproduciren oder den in ihrem Ankauf vorgeschossenen Kapital-\n werth zu ersetzen, producirt ein variables Kapital von 500 Thalern, das\n täglich 500 Arbeiter zur Mehrwerthsrate von 100 % oder mit zwölfstün-\n digem Arbeitstag verwendet, täglich einen Mehrwerth von 500 Thalern\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"284\", \"facs\": \"#f0303\"} --> oder 6 × 500 Arbeitsstunden. Ein Kapital von 100 Thalern, das 100\n Arbeiter täglich verwendet zur Mehrwerthsrate von 200 % oder mit 18stün-\n digem Arbeitstag, producirt nur eine Mehrwerths masse von 200 Tha-\n lern oder 18 × 100 Arbeitsstunden. Und sein gesammtes Werthpro-\n dukt, Aequivalent des vorgeschossenen Kapitals plus Mehrwerth, kann\n Tag aus Tag ein, niemals die Summe von 400 Thalern oder 24 × 100\n Arbeitsstunden erreichen. Die absolute Schranke des durch-\n schnittlichen Arbeitstags, der von Natur immer kleiner ist als\n 24 Stunden, bildet eine absolute Schranke für den Ersatz von\n variablem Kapital durch gesteigerte Rate des Mehr-\n werths oder von exploitirter Arbeiteranzahl durch er-\n höhten Exploitationsgrad der Arbeitskraft. Diess hand-\n greifliche Gesetz ist wichtig zur Erklärung vieler Erscheinungen, entsprin-\n gend aus der später zu entwickelnden Tendenz des Kapitals, die von ihm\n beschäftigte Arbeiteranzahl oder seinen variablen in Arbeitskraft umgesetz-\n ten Bestandtheil auf die Minimalschranke zu reduciren, im Wider-\n spruch zu seiner andern Tendenz, die möglichst grosse Masse von\n Mehrwerth zu produciren. Umgekehrt. Nimmt die Masse der ver-\n wandten Arbeitskräfte zu, oder die Grösse des variablen Kapitals, aber\n langsamer als die Rate des Mehrwerths abnimmt, so sinkt die Masse\n des producirten Mehrwerths.\n\n\n\n\nEin drittes Gesetz ergiebt sich aus der Bestimmung der Masse des\n producirten Mehrwerths durch die zwei Faktoren, Rate\n des Mehrwerths und Grösse des vorgeschossenen variablen Kapitals. Die\n Rate des Mehrwerths oder den Exploitationsgrad der Arbeitskraft, und den\n Werth der Arbeitskraft oder die Grösse der nothwendigen Arbeits-\n zeit gegeben, ist es selbstverständlich, dass je grösser das variable\n Kapital, desto grösser die Masse des producirten Werths\n und Mehrwerths. Ist die Grenze des Arbeitstags gegeben,\n ebenso die Grenze seines nothwendigen Bestandtheils, so hängt\n die Masse von Werth und Mehrwerth, die ein einzelner Kapitalist produ-\n cirt, offenbar ausschliesslich ab von der Masse Arbeit, die er in Be-\n wegung setzt. Diese aber hängt, unter den gegebnen Annahmen, ab von\n der Masse Arbeitskraft oder der Arbeiteranzahl, die er\n exploitirt, und diese Anzahl ihrerseits ist bestimmt durch die Grösse\n des von ihm vorgeschossenen variablen Kapitals. Bei gegebner\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"285\", \"facs\": \"#f0304\"} --> Rate des Mehrwerths und gegebnem Werth der Arbeits-\n kraft verhalten sich also die Massen des producirten\n Mehrwerths direkt wie die Grössen der vorgeschossenen\n variablen Kapitale. Nun weiss man aber, dass der Kapitalist sein Kapi-\n tal in zwei Theile theilt. Einen Theil legt er aus in Produktionsmitteln.\n Diess ist der constante Theil seines Kapitals. Den andern Theil setzt er um\n in lebendige Arbeitskraft. Dieser Theil bildet sein variables Kapital.\n Auf Basis derselben Produktionsweise findet in verschiednen\n Produktionssphären verschiedne Theilung des Kapitals in constan-\n ten und variablen Bestandtheil statt. Innerhalb derselben Produk-\n tionssphäre wechselt diess Verhältniss mit wechselnder technolo-\n gischer Grundlage und gesellschaftlicher Kombination des Produktions-\n prozesses. Wie aber ein gegebnes Kapital immer zerfalle in con-\n stanten und variablen Bestandtheil, ob der letztere sich zum ersteren ver-\n halte wie 1 : 2, 1 : 10, oder 1 : x, das eben aufgestellte Gesetz wird nicht\n davon berührt, da, früherer Analyse gemäss, der Werth des constanten\n Kapitals im Produktenwerth zwar wiedererscheint, aber nicht in das neu-\n gebildete Werthprodukt eingeht. Um 1000 Spinner zu verwenden, sind\n natürlich mehr Rohmaterialien, Spindeln u. s. w. erheischt, als um 100 zu\n verwenden. Der Werth dieser zuzusetzenden Produktionsmittel aber mag\n steigen, fallen, unverändert bleiben, gross oder klein sein, er bleibt ohne\n irgend einen Einfluss auf den Verwerthungsprozess der sie bewe-\n genden Arbeitskräfte. Das oben konstatirte Gesetz nimmt also die allge-\n meinere Form an: Die von verschiedenen Kapitalien pro-\n ducirten Massen von Werth und Mehrwerth verhalten\n sich, bei gegebnem Werth und gleich grossem Exploita-\n tionsgrad der Arbeitskraft, direkt wie die Grössen der\n variablen Bestandtheile dieser Kapitale, d. h. ihrer in\n lebendige Arbeitskraft umgesetzten Bestandtheile.\n\n\n\n\nDiess Gesetz widerspricht offenbar aller auf den Augenschein\n gegründeten Erfahrung. Jedermann weiss, dass ein Baumwollspinner, der,\n die Prozenttheile des angewandten Gesammtkapitals berechnet, relativ\n viel constantes und wenig variables Kapital anwendet, desswegen kei-\n nen kleineren Gewinn oder Mehrwerth erbeutet als ein Bäcker, der relativ\n viel variables und wenig constantes Kapital in Bewegung setzt. Zur\n Lösung dieses schcinbaren Widerspruchs bedarf es noch vieler Mittelglieder,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"286\", \"facs\": \"#f0305\"} --> wie es vom Standpunkt der elementaren Algebra vieler Mittelglieder bedarf,\n um zu verstehn, dass \\frac{0}{0} eine wirkliche Grösse darstellen kann. Obgleich\n sie das Gesetz nie formulirt hat, hängt die klassische Oekonomie\n instinktiv daran fest, weil es eine nothwendige Konsequenz des Werth-\n gesetzes überhaupt ist. Sie sucht es durch gewaltsame Abstraktion vor\n den Widersprüchen der Erscheinung zu retten. Man wird später [Note: Näheres darüber im „Vierten Buch“.] \n sehn, wie die Ricardo’sche Schule an diesem Stein des Anstosses ge-\n stolpert ist. Die Vulgärökonomie, die „wirklich auch nichts gelernt hat“,\n pocht hier, wie überall auf den Schein gegen das Gesetz der Erscheinung.\n Sie glaubt im Gegensatz zu Spinoza, dass „die Unwissenheit ein hin-\n reichender Grund ist“.\n\n\n\n\nDie Arbeit, die das Gesammtkapital einer Gesellschaft täglich in Be-\n wegung setzt, kann als ein einziger Arbeitstag betrachtet werden.\n Ist z. B. die Zahl der Arbeiter eine Million und beträgt der Durchschnitts-\n Arbeitstag eines Arbeiters 10 Stunden, so besteht der gesellschaft-\n liche Arbeitstag aus 10 Millionen Stunden. Bei gegebner Länge\n dieses Arbeitstags, seien seine Grenzen physisch oder social gezogen, kann\n die Masse des Mehrwerths nur vermehrt werden durch Vermehrung\n der Arbeiteranzahl, d. h. der Arbeiterbevölkerung. Das Wachs-\n thum der Bevölkerung bildet hier die mathematische Grenze für Produk-\n tion des Mehrwerths durch das gesellschaftliche Gesammtkapital. Umge-\n kehrt. Bei gegebner Grösse der Bevölkerung wird diese Grenze gebildet\ndurch die mögliche Verlängerung des Arbeitstags [Note: „The labour, that is the economic time of society, is a given\n portion, say ten hours a day of a million of people or ten millions hours . . . .\n Capital has its boundary of increase. The boundary may, at any given period,\n be attained in the actual extent of economic time employed.“ („An Essay on\n the Political Economy of Nations. London 1821“, p. 48, 49.)] .\n Man wird im folgenden Kapital sehn, dass diess Gesetz nur für die bisher\n behandelte Form des Mehrwerths gilt.\n\n\n\n\nAus der bisherigen Betrachtung der Produktion des Mehrwerths er-\n giebt sich, dass nicht jede beliebige Geld- oder Werthsumme in Kapital\n verwandelt werden kann, sondern zu dieser Verwandlung vielmehr ein be-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"287\", \"facs\": \"#f0306\"} --> stimmtes Minimum von Geld oder Tauschwerth in der Hand des ein-\n zelnen Geld- oder Waarenbesitzers vorausgesetzt ist. Das Minimum\n von variablem Kapital ist der Kostenpreis einer einzelnen Arbeits-\n kraft, die das ganze Jahr durch, Tag aus Tag ein, zur Gewinnung von\n Mehrwerth vernutzt wird. Wäre dieser Arbeiter im Besitz seiner eignen\n Produktionsmittel, und begnügte er sich als Arbeiter zu leben, so genügte\n ihm die zur Reproduktion seiner Lebensmittel nothwendige Arbeitszeit,\n sage von 8 Stunden täglich. Er bedürfte also auch nur Produktions-\n mittel für 8 Arbeitsstunden. Der Kapitalist dagegen, der ihn ausser die-\n sen 8 Stunden sage 4 Stunden Mehrarbeit verrichten lässt, bedarf einer\n zusätzlichen Geldsumme zur Beschaffung der zusätzlichen Produktions-\n mittel. Unter unserer Annahme jedoch müsste er schon zwei Arbeiter\n anwenden, um von dem täglich angeeigneten Mehrwerth wie ein Arbeiter\n leben, d. h. die nothwendigen Bedürfnisse befriedigen zu können. In die-\n sem Fall wäre blosser Lebensunterhalt der Zweck seiner Produktion, nicht\n Vermehrung des Reichthums, und das letztre ist unterstellt bei der kapita-\n listischen Produktion. Damit er nur doppelt so gut lebe wie ein gewöhn-\n licher Arbeiter, und die Hälfte des producirten Mehrwerths in Kapital zu-\n rückverwandle, müsste er zugleich mit der Arbeiterzahl das Minimum\n des vorgeschossenen Kapitals um das Achtfache steigern. Allerdings kann\n er selbst, gleich seinem Arbeiter, unmittelbar Hand im Produktionsprozesse\n anlegen, aber ist dann auch nur ein Mittelding zwischen Kapitalist und\n Arbeiter, ein „kleiner Meister“. Ein gewisser Höhegrad der kapi-\n talistischen Produktion bedingt, dass der Kapitalist die ganze Zeit, während\n deren er als Kapitalist, d. h. als personificirtes Kapital funktionirt, zur\n Aneignung und daher Kontrole fremder Arbeit, und zum Verkauf der Pro-\n dukte dieser Arbeit verwenden könne [Note: „The farmer cannot rely on his own labour; and if he does, I will main-\n tain that he is a loser by it. His employment should be, a general attention to\n the whole: his thrasher must be watched, or he will soon lose his wages in corn\n not thrashed out; his mowers, reapers etc. must be looked after; he must con-\n stantly go round his fences; he must see there is no neglect; which would be the\n case if he was confined to any one spot.“ „An Enquiry into the Connec-\n tion between the Price of Provisions, and the Size of Farms etc.\nBy a Farmer. London 1773“, p. 12. Diese Schrift ist sehr interessant.] . Die Verwandlung des Hand-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"288\", \"facs\": \"#f0307\"} --> werksmeisters in den Kapitalisten suchte die zünftige Industrie des Mittel-\n alters dadurch gewaltsam zu verhindern, dass sie die Arbeiteranzahl, die ein\n einzelner Meister beschäftigen durfte, auf ein sehr geringes Maximum be-\n schränkte. Der Geld- oder Waarenbesitzer verwandelt sich erst wirklich in\n einen Kapitalisten, wo die für die Produktion vorgeschossene Minimal-\n summe weit über dem mittelaltrigen Maximum steht. Hier, wie in der\n Naturwissenschaft, bewährt sich die Richtigkeit des von Hegel in seiner\n Logik entdeckten Gesetzes, dass bloss quantitative Veränderungen\n auf einem gewissen Punkt in qualitative Unterschiede umschlagen [Note: Die in der modernen Chemie angewandte, von Laurent und Ger-\n hardt angebahnte, von Prof. Würtz zu Paris zuerst wissenschaftlich entwickelte\nMolekulartheorie beruht auf keinem anderen Gesetze.] .\n\n\n\n\nDas Minimum der Werthsumme, worüber der einzelne Geld-\n oder Waarenbesitzer verfügen muss, um sich in einen Kapitalisten zu ent-\n puppen, wechselt auf verschiedenen Entwicklungsstufen der kapitalistischen\n Produktion und ist, bei gegebner Entwicklungsstufe, verschieden in ver-\n schiedenen Produktionssphären, je nach ihren besondern technologischen\n Bedingungen. Gewisse Produktionssphären erheischen schon in den An-\n fängen der kapitalistischen Produktion ein Minimum von Kapital,\n das sich noch nicht in der Hand einzelner Individuen vorfindet. Diess\n veranlasst theils Staatssubsidien an solche Private, wie in Frankreich zur\n Zeit Colberts und wie in manchen deutschen Staaten bis in unsre Epoche\n hinein, theils die Bildung von Gesellschaften mit gesetzlichem Monopol für\n den Betrieb gewisser Industrie- und Handelszweige [Note: „Die Gesellschaft Monopolia“ nennt Martin Luther derartige In-\n stitute.] , — die Vorläufer\n der modernen Aktiengesellschaften.\n\n\n\n\n [Note: Man kann darin die Genesis des „capitalist farmer“ oder „merchant\n farmer“, wie er ausdrücklich genannt wird, studiren und seiner Selbstverherr-\n lichung gegenüber dem „small farmer“, dem es wesentlich um die Subsistenz zu\n thun ist, zuhören. „Die Kapitalistenklasse wird zuerst theilweise und schliesslich\n ganz und gar entbunden von der Nothwendigkeit der Handarbeit.“ („Text-\n book of Lectures on the Polit. Economy of Nations. By the Rev.\n Mr. Richard Jones. Hertford 1852.“ Lecture III.)] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"289\", \"facs\": \"#f0308\"} -->\n\nWir halten uns nicht beim Detail der Veränderungen auf, die das\n Verhältniss von Kapitalist und Lohnarbeiter im Verlaufe des Produktions-\n prozesses erfuhr, also auch nicht bei den weiteren Fortbestimmungen des\n Kapitals selbst. Nur wenige Hauptpunkte seien hier betont.\n\n\n\n\nInnerhalb des Produktionsprozesses entwickelte sich das Kapital\n zum Kommando über die Arbeit, d. h. über die sich bethätigende\n Arbeitskraft oder den Arbeiter selbst. Das personificirte Kapital,\n der Kapitalist, passt auf, dass der Arbeiter sein Werk ordentlich und mit\n dem gehörigen Grad von Intensivität verrichte.\n\n\n\n\nDas Kapital entwickelte sich ferner zu einem Zwangsverhält-\n niss, welches die Arbeiterklasse zwingt mehr Arbeit zu verrichten als der\n enge Umkreis ihrer eignen Lebensbedürfnisse vorschrieb. Und als Pro-\n duzent fremder Arbeitsamkeit, als Auspumper von Mehrarbeit und Exploi-\n teur von Arbeitskraft, übergipfelt es an Energie, Masslosigkeit und Wirk-\n samkeit alle früheren auf direkter Zwangsarbeit beruhenden Pro-\n duktionssysteme.\n\n\n\n\nDas Kapital ordnet sich zunächst die Arbeit unter mit den gegebenen\n technologischen Bedingungen, worin es sie historisch vorfindet. Es ver-\n ändert daher nicht unmittelbar die Produktionsweise. Die Produktion von\n Mehrwerth in der bisher betrachteten Form, durch einfache Verlängerung des\n Arbeitstags, erschien daher von jedem Wechsel der Produktionsweise selbst\n unabhängig. Sie war in der altmodischen Bäckerei nicht minder wirksam\n als in der modernen Baumwollspinnerei. Betrachteten wir den Produk-\n tionsprozess daher bloss unter dem Gesichtspunkt des Arbeitsprozesses,\n so verhielt sich der Arbeiter zu den Produktionsmitteln nicht als Kapital,\n sondern als blossem Mittel und Material seiner zweckmässigen produktiven\n Thätigkeit. In einer Gerberei z. B. behandelt er die Felle als seinen blossen\n Arbeitsgegenstand. Es ist nicht der Kapitalist, dem er das Fell gerbt.\n Anders, sobald wir den Produktionsprozess unter dem Gesichtspunkt des\nVerwerthungsprozesses betrachteten. Die Produktionsmittel ver-\n wandelten sich sofort in Mittel zur Einsaugung fremder Arbeit. Es ist nicht\n mehr der Arbeiter, der die Produktionsmittel anwendet, son-\n dern es sind die Produktionsmittel, die den Arbeiter an-\n wenden. Statt von ihm als stoffliche Elemente seiner produktiven Thä-\n tigkeit verzehrt zu werden, verzehren sie ihn als Ferment ihres eignen\n Lebensprozesses, und der Lebensprozess des Kapitals besteht nur in seiner\n [Running matter: I. 19] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"290\", \"facs\": \"#f0309\"} --> Bewegung als sich selbst verwerthender Werth. Schmelzöfen\n und Arbeitsgebäude, die des Nachts ruhn und keine lebendige Arbeit ein-\n saugen, sind „reiner Verlust“ („a mere loss“) für den Kapitalisten. Da-\n rum konstituiren Schmelzöfen und Arbeitsgebäude einen „Anspruch auf\n die Nachtarbeit“ der Arbeitskräfte. Die blosse Verwandlung des Geldes\n in gegenständliche Faktoren des Produktionsprozesses, in Produktions-\n mittel, verwandelt letztre in Rechtstitel und Zwangstitel auf\n fremde Arbeit und Mehrarbeit. Wie diese der kapitalistischen Produktion\n eigenthümliche und sie charakterisirende Verkehrung, ja Verrückung des\n Verhältnisses von todter und lebendiger Arbeit, von Werth und werth-\n schöpferischer Kraft, sich im Bewusstsein der Kapitalistenköpfe abspie-\n gelt, zeige schliesslich noch ein Beispiel. Während der englischen Fabri-\n kantenrevolte von 1848—50 schrieb „der Chef der Leinen- und Baum-\n wollspinnerei zu Paisley, einer der ältesten und respektabelsten Firmen\n von Westschottland, der Herrn Carlile, Söhne und Co., deren Kompagnie\n seit 1752 besteht und Generation nach Generation von derselben Familie\n geführt wird“, — dieser äusserst intelligente Gentleman also schrieb in die\n „Glasgow Daily Mail“ vom 25. April 1849 einen Brief [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 30th April 1849“, p. 59.]  unter dem\n Titel: „das Relaissystem“, worin u. a. folgende grotesk naive Stelle\n unterläuft: „Lasst uns nun die Uebel betrachten, die aus einer Reduktion\n der Arbeitszeit von 12 auf 10 Stunden fliessen . . . . Sie „belaufen“ sich\n auf die allerernsthafteste Beschädigung der Aussichten und des Eigen-\n thums des Fabrikanten. Arbeitete er (d. h. seine „Hände“) 12 Stunden\n und wird er auf 10 beschränkt, dann schrumpfen je 12 Maschinen oder\n Spindeln seines Etablissements auf 10 zusammen, („then every 12 ma-\n chines or spindles, in his establishment, shrink to 10“), und wollte er\n seine Fabrik verkaufen, so würden sie nur als 10 gewerthschätzt werden,\n so dass so ein sechster Theil vom Werth einer jeden Fabrik im ganzen\n Lande abgezogen würde“ [Note: l. c. p. 60. Fabrikinspektor Stuart, selbst Schotte, und im Gegensatz\n zu den englischen Fabrikinspektoren, ganz in kapitalistischer Denkart befangen,\n bemerkt ausdrücklich, dieser Brief, den er seinem Bericht einverleibt, „sei die aller-\n nützlichste Mittheilung, die irgend einer der Fabrikanten, welche das Relaissystem\n anwenden, gemacht, und ganz besonders darauf berechnet, die Vorurtheile und\n Skrupel gegen jenes System zu beseitigen.“] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"291\", \"facs\": \"#f0310\"} -->\n\nDiesem erbangestammten Kapitalhirn von Westschottland ver-\n schwimmt der Werth der Produktionsmittel, Spindeln u. s. w., so sehr\n mit ihrer Kapitaleigenschaft, sich selbst zu verwerthen, oder täg-\n lich ein bestimmtes Quantum fremder Gratisarbeit einzuschlucken, dass\n der Chef des Hauses Carlile und Co. in der That wähnt, beim Verkauf\n seiner Fabrik werde ihm nicht nur der Werth der Spindeln gezahlt, sondern\n obendrein ihre Verwerthung, nicht nur die Arbeit, die in ihnen steckt und zur\n Produktion von Spindeln derselben Art nöthig ist, sondern auch die Mehr-\n arbeit, die sie täglich aus den braven Westschotten von Paisley auspumpen,\n und eben desshalb, meint er, schrumpfe mit der Kontraktion des Arbeits-\n tags um zwei Stunden der Verkaufspreis von je 12 Spinnmaschinen auf\n den von je 10 zusammen!","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0001","text":"Wie bisher, wird in diesem Paragraph der Werth der Arbeits-\n kraft, also der zur Reproduktion oder Erhaltung der Arbeitskraft noth-\n wendige Theil des Arbeitstags, als gegebne, constante\n Grösse unterstellt.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"10.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0002","text":"Diess also vorausgesetzt, ist mit der Rate zugleich die Masse des\n Mehrwerths gegeben, die der einzelne Arbeiter dem Kapitalisten in\n bestimmter Zeitperiode liefert. Beträgt z. B. die nothwendige Arbeit täglich\n 6 Stunden, ausgedrückt in einem Goldquantum von 3 sh. = 1 Thaler, so\n ist 1 Thaler der Tageswerth einer Arbeitskraft, oder der im Ankauf\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"282\", \"facs\": \"#f0301\"} -->einer Arbeitskraft vorgeschossene Kapitalwerth. Ist ferner die Rate\n des Mehrwerths 100 %, so producirt diess variable Kapital von\n 1 Thaler eine Masse Mehrwerth von 1 Thaler, oder der Arbeiter liefert\n täglich eine Masse Mehrarbeit von 6 Stunden.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"10.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0003","text":"Das variable Kapital ist aber der Geldausdruck für den Ge-\n sammtwerth aller Arbeitskräfte, die der Kapitalist gleichzeitig\n in einem bestimmten Produktionsprozess verwendet. Ist der Tageswerth\neiner Arbeitskraft 1 Thaler, so ist also ein Kapital von 100 Thalern vor-\n zuschiessen um 100, und von n Thalern, um n Arbeitskräfte täglich zu\n exploitiren. Der Werth des vorgeschossenen variablen Ka-\n pitals ist also gleich dem Durchschnittswerth einer Arbeits-\n kraft multiplicirt mit der Anzahl der verwandten Arbeitskräfte. Bei\n gegebnem Werth der Arbeitskraft wechselt also Werthumfang\n oder Grösse des variablen Kapitals mit der Masse der ange-\n eigneten Arbeitskräfte oder der Anzahl der gleichzeitig beschäftigten\n Arbeiter.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"10.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0004","text":"Produzirt ein variables Kapital von 1 Thaler, der Tageswerth einer\n Arbeitskraft, einen täglichen Mehrwerth von 1 Thaler, so ein variables\n Kapital von 100 Thalern einen täglichen Mehrwerth von 100, und eins von\n n Thalern einen täglichen Mehrwerth von 1 Thaler × n. Die Masse\n des producirten Mehrwerths ist also gleich dem Mehrwerth, den\n der Arbeitstag des einzelnen Arbeiters liefert, multiplicirt mit der Anzahl\n der angewandten Arbeiter.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"10.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0005","text":"Da aber ferner die Masse Mehrwerth, die der\n einzelne Arbeiter producirt, bei gegebenem Werth der Arbeitskraft, durch\n die Rate des Mehrwerths bestimmt ist, so folgt, unter derselben Vor-\n aussetzung: Die Masse des von einem gegebnen variablen\n Kapital producirten Mehrwerths ist gleich der Grösse\n des vorgeschossenen variablen Kapitals multiplicirt mit\n der Rate des Mehrwerths oder ist bestimmt durch das zusammen-\n gesetzte Verhältniss zwischen der Anzahl der gleichzeitig ex-\n ploitirten Arbeitskräfte und dem Exploitationsgrad der\n einzelnen Arbeitskraft.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"10.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0006","text":"In der Produktion einer bestimmten Masse Mehrwerth\n kann daher die Abnahme des einen Faktors durch Zunahme\n des andern ersetzt werden. Wird das variable Kapital vermindert und\n gleichzeitig in demselben Verhältniss die Rate des Mehrwerths\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"283\", \"facs\": \"#f0302\"} --> erhöht, so bleibt die Masse des producirten Mehrwerths un-\n verändert. Muss unter den früheren Annahmen der Kapitalist 100 Tha-\n ler vorschiessen, um 100 Arbeiter täglich zu exploitiren, und beträgt die\n Rate des Mehrwerths 50 %, so wirft diess variable Kapital von 100 einen\n Mehrwerth von 50 ab, oder von 100 × 3 Arbeitsstunden. Wird die\n Rate des Mehrwerths verdoppelt, oder der Arbeitstag statt von 6 zu 9,\n von 6 zu 12 Stunden verlängert, so wirft das um die Hälfte verminderte\n variable Kapital von 50 Thalern ebenfalls einen Mehrwerth von 50 Thalern\n ab oder von 50 × 6 Arbeitsstunden.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"10.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0007","text":"Verminderung des variablen Kapi-\n tals ist also ausgleichbar durch proportionelle Erhöhung im Exploitations-\n grad der Arbeitskraft oder Abnahme in der Anzahl der beschäftigten Ar-\n beiter durch proportionelle Verlängerung des Arbeitstags. Innerhalb ge-\n wisser Grenzen wird die vom Kapital erpressbare Zufuhr der Arbeit\n also unabhängig von der Arbeiterzufuhr [Note: Diess Elementargesetz scheint den Herren von der Vulgärökonomie un-\n bekannt, die, umgekehrte Archimedes, in der Bestimmung der Marktpreise der\n Arbeit durch Nachfrage und Zufuhr den Punkt gefunden zu haben glauben, nicht\n um die Welt aus den Angeln zu heben, sondern um sie stillzusetzen.] . Umgekehrt lässt Ab-\n nahmein der Rate des Mehrwerths die Masse des produ-\n cirten Mehrwerths unverändert, wenn proportionell die Grösse des\n variablen Kapitals oder die Anzahl der beschäftigten Arbeiter wächst.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"10.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0008","text":"Indess hat der Ersatz von Arbeiteranzahl oder Grösse des variablen\n Kapitals durch gesteigerte Rate des Mehrwerths oder Verlängerung des\n Arbeitstags unüberspringbare absolute Schranken. Welches immer der\nWerth der Arbeitskraft sei, ob daher die zur Erhaltung des Arbei-\n ters nothwendige Arbeitszeit 2 oder 10 Stunden betrage, der Ge-\n sammtwerth, den ein Arbeiter, Tag aus Tag ein, produciren kann, ist\n immer kleiner als der Werth, worin sich 24 Arbeitsstunden vergegen-\n ständlichen, kleiner als 12 sh. oder 4 Thaler, wenn diess der Geldausdruck\n der 24 vergegenständlichten Arbeitsstunden.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"10.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0009","text":"Unter unsrer früheren An-\n nahme, wonach täglich 6 Arbeitsstunden erheischt, um die Arbeitskraft\n selbst zu reproduciren oder den in ihrem Ankauf vorgeschossenen Kapital-\n werth zu ersetzen, producirt ein variables Kapital von 500 Thalern, das\n täglich 500 Arbeiter zur Mehrwerthsrate von 100 % oder mit zwölfstün-\n digem Arbeitstag verwendet, täglich einen Mehrwerth von 500 Thalern\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"284\", \"facs\": \"#f0303\"} --> oder 6 × 500 Arbeitsstunden. Ein Kapital von 100 Thalern, das 100\n Arbeiter täglich verwendet zur Mehrwerthsrate von 200 % oder mit 18stün-\n digem Arbeitstag, producirt nur eine Mehrwerths masse von 200 Tha-\n lern oder 18 × 100 Arbeitsstunden. Und sein gesammtes Werthpro-\n dukt, Aequivalent des vorgeschossenen Kapitals plus Mehrwerth, kann\n Tag aus Tag ein, niemals die Summe von 400 Thalern oder 24 × 100\n Arbeitsstunden erreichen.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"10.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0010","text":"Die absolute Schranke des durch-\n schnittlichen Arbeitstags, der von Natur immer kleiner ist als\n 24 Stunden, bildet eine absolute Schranke für den Ersatz von\n variablem Kapital durch gesteigerte Rate des Mehr-\n werths oder von exploitirter Arbeiteranzahl durch er-\n höhten Exploitationsgrad der Arbeitskraft. Diess hand-\n greifliche Gesetz ist wichtig zur Erklärung vieler Erscheinungen, entsprin-\n gend aus der später zu entwickelnden Tendenz des Kapitals, die von ihm\n beschäftigte Arbeiteranzahl oder seinen variablen in Arbeitskraft umgesetz-\n ten Bestandtheil auf die Minimalschranke zu reduciren, im Wider-\n spruch zu seiner andern Tendenz, die möglichst grosse Masse von\n Mehrwerth zu produciren. Umgekehrt.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"10.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0011","text":"Nimmt die Masse der ver-\n wandten Arbeitskräfte zu, oder die Grösse des variablen Kapitals, aber\n langsamer als die Rate des Mehrwerths abnimmt, so sinkt die Masse\n des producirten Mehrwerths.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"10.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0012","text":"Ein drittes Gesetz ergiebt sich aus der Bestimmung der Masse des\n producirten Mehrwerths durch die zwei Faktoren, Rate\n des Mehrwerths und Grösse des vorgeschossenen variablen Kapitals. Die\n Rate des Mehrwerths oder den Exploitationsgrad der Arbeitskraft, und den\n Werth der Arbeitskraft oder die Grösse der nothwendigen Arbeits-\n zeit gegeben, ist es selbstverständlich, dass je grösser das variable\n Kapital, desto grösser die Masse des producirten Werths\n und Mehrwerths. Ist die Grenze des Arbeitstags gegeben,\n ebenso die Grenze seines nothwendigen Bestandtheils, so hängt\n die Masse von Werth und Mehrwerth, die ein einzelner Kapitalist produ-\n cirt, offenbar ausschliesslich ab von der Masse Arbeit, die er in Be-\n wegung setzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"10.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0013","text":"Diese aber hängt, unter den gegebnen Annahmen, ab von\n der Masse Arbeitskraft oder der Arbeiteranzahl, die er\n exploitirt, und diese Anzahl ihrerseits ist bestimmt durch die Grösse\n des von ihm vorgeschossenen variablen Kapitals. Bei gegebner\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"285\", \"facs\": \"#f0304\"} --> Rate des Mehrwerths und gegebnem Werth der Arbeits-\n kraft verhalten sich also die Massen des producirten\n Mehrwerths direkt wie die Grössen der vorgeschossenen\n variablen Kapitale. Nun weiss man aber, dass der Kapitalist sein Kapi-\n tal in zwei Theile theilt. Einen Theil legt er aus in Produktionsmitteln.\n Diess ist der constante Theil seines Kapitals. Den andern Theil setzt er um\n in lebendige Arbeitskraft. Dieser Theil bildet sein variables Kapital.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"10.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0014","text":"Auf Basis derselben Produktionsweise findet in verschiednen\n Produktionssphären verschiedne Theilung des Kapitals in constan-\n ten und variablen Bestandtheil statt. Innerhalb derselben Produk-\n tionssphäre wechselt diess Verhältniss mit wechselnder technolo-\n gischer Grundlage und gesellschaftlicher Kombination des Produktions-\n prozesses. Wie aber ein gegebnes Kapital immer zerfalle in con-\n stanten und variablen Bestandtheil, ob der letztere sich zum ersteren ver-\n halte wie 1 : 2, 1 : 10, oder 1 : x, das eben aufgestellte Gesetz wird nicht\n davon berührt, da, früherer Analyse gemäss, der Werth des constanten\n Kapitals im Produktenwerth zwar wiedererscheint, aber nicht in das neu-\n gebildete Werthprodukt eingeht. Um 1000 Spinner zu verwenden, sind\n natürlich mehr Rohmaterialien, Spindeln u. s. w. erheischt, als um 100 zu\n verwenden.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"10.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0015","text":"Der Werth dieser zuzusetzenden Produktionsmittel aber mag\n steigen, fallen, unverändert bleiben, gross oder klein sein, er bleibt ohne\n irgend einen Einfluss auf den Verwerthungsprozess der sie bewe-\n genden Arbeitskräfte. Das oben konstatirte Gesetz nimmt also die allge-\n meinere Form an: Die von verschiedenen Kapitalien pro-\n ducirten Massen von Werth und Mehrwerth verhalten\n sich, bei gegebnem Werth und gleich grossem Exploita-\n tionsgrad der Arbeitskraft, direkt wie die Grössen der\n variablen Bestandtheile dieser Kapitale, d. h. ihrer in\n lebendige Arbeitskraft umgesetzten Bestandtheile.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"10.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0016","text":"Diess Gesetz widerspricht offenbar aller auf den Augenschein\n gegründeten Erfahrung. Jedermann weiss, dass ein Baumwollspinner, der,\n die Prozenttheile des angewandten Gesammtkapitals berechnet, relativ\n viel constantes und wenig variables Kapital anwendet, desswegen kei-\n nen kleineren Gewinn oder Mehrwerth erbeutet als ein Bäcker, der relativ\n viel variables und wenig constantes Kapital in Bewegung setzt. Zur\n Lösung dieses schcinbaren Widerspruchs bedarf es noch vieler Mittelglieder,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"286\", \"facs\": \"#f0305\"} --> wie es vom Standpunkt der elementaren Algebra vieler Mittelglieder bedarf,\n um zu verstehn, dass \\frac{0}{0} eine wirkliche Grösse darstellen kann. Obgleich\n sie das Gesetz nie formulirt hat, hängt die klassische Oekonomie\n instinktiv daran fest, weil es eine nothwendige Konsequenz des Werth-\n gesetzes überhaupt ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"10.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0017","text":"Sie sucht es durch gewaltsame Abstraktion vor\n den Widersprüchen der Erscheinung zu retten. Man wird später [Note: Näheres darüber im „Vierten Buch“.] \n sehn, wie die Ricardo’sche Schule an diesem Stein des Anstosses ge-\n stolpert ist. Die Vulgärökonomie, die „wirklich auch nichts gelernt hat“,\n pocht hier, wie überall auf den Schein gegen das Gesetz der Erscheinung.\n Sie glaubt im Gegensatz zu Spinoza, dass „die Unwissenheit ein hin-\n reichender Grund ist“.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"10.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0018","text":"Die Arbeit, die das Gesammtkapital einer Gesellschaft täglich in Be-\n wegung setzt, kann als ein einziger Arbeitstag betrachtet werden.\n Ist z. B. die Zahl der Arbeiter eine Million und beträgt der Durchschnitts-\n Arbeitstag eines Arbeiters 10 Stunden, so besteht der gesellschaft-\n liche Arbeitstag aus 10 Millionen Stunden. Bei gegebner Länge\n dieses Arbeitstags, seien seine Grenzen physisch oder social gezogen, kann\n die Masse des Mehrwerths nur vermehrt werden durch Vermehrung\n der Arbeiteranzahl, d. h. der Arbeiterbevölkerung. Das Wachs-\n thum der Bevölkerung bildet hier die mathematische Grenze für Produk-\n tion des Mehrwerths durch das gesellschaftliche Gesammtkapital. Umge-\n kehrt.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"10.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0019","text":"Bei gegebner Grösse der Bevölkerung wird diese Grenze gebildet\ndurch die mögliche Verlängerung des Arbeitstags [Note: „The labour, that is the economic time of society, is a given\n portion, say ten hours a day of a million of people or ten millions hours . . . .\n Capital has its boundary of increase. The boundary may, at any given period,\n be attained in the actual extent of economic time employed.“ („An Essay on\n the Political Economy of Nations. London 1821“, p. 48, 49.)] .\n Man wird im folgenden Kapital sehn, dass diess Gesetz nur für die bisher\n behandelte Form des Mehrwerths gilt.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"10.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0020","text":"Aus der bisherigen Betrachtung der Produktion des Mehrwerths er-\n giebt sich, dass nicht jede beliebige Geld- oder Werthsumme in Kapital\n verwandelt werden kann, sondern zu dieser Verwandlung vielmehr ein be-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"287\", \"facs\": \"#f0306\"} --> stimmtes Minimum von Geld oder Tauschwerth in der Hand des ein-\n zelnen Geld- oder Waarenbesitzers vorausgesetzt ist. Das Minimum\n von variablem Kapital ist der Kostenpreis einer einzelnen Arbeits-\n kraft, die das ganze Jahr durch, Tag aus Tag ein, zur Gewinnung von\n Mehrwerth vernutzt wird. Wäre dieser Arbeiter im Besitz seiner eignen\n Produktionsmittel, und begnügte er sich als Arbeiter zu leben, so genügte\n ihm die zur Reproduktion seiner Lebensmittel nothwendige Arbeitszeit,\n sage von 8 Stunden täglich. Er bedürfte also auch nur Produktions-\n mittel für 8 Arbeitsstunden.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"10.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0021","text":"Der Kapitalist dagegen, der ihn ausser die-\n sen 8 Stunden sage 4 Stunden Mehrarbeit verrichten lässt, bedarf einer\n zusätzlichen Geldsumme zur Beschaffung der zusätzlichen Produktions-\n mittel. Unter unserer Annahme jedoch müsste er schon zwei Arbeiter\n anwenden, um von dem täglich angeeigneten Mehrwerth wie ein Arbeiter\n leben, d. h. die nothwendigen Bedürfnisse befriedigen zu können. In die-\n sem Fall wäre blosser Lebensunterhalt der Zweck seiner Produktion, nicht\n Vermehrung des Reichthums, und das letztre ist unterstellt bei der kapita-\n listischen Produktion. Damit er nur doppelt so gut lebe wie ein gewöhn-\n licher Arbeiter, und die Hälfte des producirten Mehrwerths in Kapital zu-\n rückverwandle, müsste er zugleich mit der Arbeiterzahl das Minimum\n des vorgeschossenen Kapitals um das Achtfache steigern.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"10.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0022","text":"Allerdings kann\n er selbst, gleich seinem Arbeiter, unmittelbar Hand im Produktionsprozesse\n anlegen, aber ist dann auch nur ein Mittelding zwischen Kapitalist und\n Arbeiter, ein „kleiner Meister“. Ein gewisser Höhegrad der kapi-\n talistischen Produktion bedingt, dass der Kapitalist die ganze Zeit, während\n deren er als Kapitalist, d. h. als personificirtes Kapital funktionirt, zur\n Aneignung und daher Kontrole fremder Arbeit, und zum Verkauf der Pro-\n dukte dieser Arbeit verwenden könne [Note: „The farmer cannot rely on his own labour; and if he does, I will main-\n tain that he is a loser by it. His employment should be, a general attention to\n the whole: his thrasher must be watched, or he will soon lose his wages in corn\n not thrashed out; his mowers, reapers etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"10.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0023","text":"must be looked after; he must con-\n stantly go round his fences; he must see there is no neglect; which would be the\n case if he was confined to any one spot.“ „An Enquiry into the Connec-\n tion between the Price of Provisions, and the Size of Farms etc.\nBy a Farmer. London 1773“, p. 12. Diese Schrift ist sehr interessant.] . Die Verwandlung des Hand-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"288\", \"facs\": \"#f0307\"} --> werksmeisters in den Kapitalisten suchte die zünftige Industrie des Mittel-\n alters dadurch gewaltsam zu verhindern, dass sie die Arbeiteranzahl, die ein\n einzelner Meister beschäftigen durfte, auf ein sehr geringes Maximum be-\n schränkte. Der Geld- oder Waarenbesitzer verwandelt sich erst wirklich in\n einen Kapitalisten, wo die für die Produktion vorgeschossene Minimal-\n summe weit über dem mittelaltrigen Maximum steht.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"10.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0024","text":"Hier, wie in der\n Naturwissenschaft, bewährt sich die Richtigkeit des von Hegel in seiner\n Logik entdeckten Gesetzes, dass bloss quantitative Veränderungen\n auf einem gewissen Punkt in qualitative Unterschiede umschlagen [Note: Die in der modernen Chemie angewandte, von Laurent und Ger-\n hardt angebahnte, von Prof. Würtz zu Paris zuerst wissenschaftlich entwickelte\nMolekulartheorie beruht auf keinem anderen Gesetze.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"10.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0025","text":"Das Minimum der Werthsumme, worüber der einzelne Geld-\n oder Waarenbesitzer verfügen muss, um sich in einen Kapitalisten zu ent-\n puppen, wechselt auf verschiedenen Entwicklungsstufen der kapitalistischen\n Produktion und ist, bei gegebner Entwicklungsstufe, verschieden in ver-\n schiedenen Produktionssphären, je nach ihren besondern technologischen\n Bedingungen. Gewisse Produktionssphären erheischen schon in den An-\n fängen der kapitalistischen Produktion ein Minimum von Kapital,\n das sich noch nicht in der Hand einzelner Individuen vorfindet.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"10.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0026","text":"Diess\n veranlasst theils Staatssubsidien an solche Private, wie in Frankreich zur\n Zeit Colberts und wie in manchen deutschen Staaten bis in unsre Epoche\n hinein, theils die Bildung von Gesellschaften mit gesetzlichem Monopol für\n den Betrieb gewisser Industrie- und Handelszweige [Note: „Die Gesellschaft Monopolia“ nennt Martin Luther derartige In-\n stitute.] , — die Vorläufer\n der modernen Aktiengesellschaften.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"10.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0027","text":"[Note: Man kann darin die Genesis des „capitalist farmer“ oder „merchant\n farmer“, wie er ausdrücklich genannt wird, studiren und seiner Selbstverherr-\n lichung gegenüber dem „small farmer“, dem es wesentlich um die Subsistenz zu\n thun ist, zuhören. „Die Kapitalistenklasse wird zuerst theilweise und schliesslich\n ganz und gar entbunden von der Nothwendigkeit der Handarbeit.“ („Text-\n book of Lectures on the Polit. Economy of Nations. By the Rev.\n Mr. Richard Jones. Hertford 1852.“ Lecture III.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"10.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0028","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"289\", \"facs\": \"#f0308\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"10.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0029","text":"Wir halten uns nicht beim Detail der Veränderungen auf, die das\n Verhältniss von Kapitalist und Lohnarbeiter im Verlaufe des Produktions-\n prozesses erfuhr, also auch nicht bei den weiteren Fortbestimmungen des\n Kapitals selbst. Nur wenige Hauptpunkte seien hier betont.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"10.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0030","text":"Innerhalb des Produktionsprozesses entwickelte sich das Kapital\n zum Kommando über die Arbeit, d. h. über die sich bethätigende\n Arbeitskraft oder den Arbeiter selbst. Das personificirte Kapital,\n der Kapitalist, passt auf, dass der Arbeiter sein Werk ordentlich und mit\n dem gehörigen Grad von Intensivität verrichte.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"10.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0031","text":"Das Kapital entwickelte sich ferner zu einem Zwangsverhält-\n niss, welches die Arbeiterklasse zwingt mehr Arbeit zu verrichten als der\n enge Umkreis ihrer eignen Lebensbedürfnisse vorschrieb. Und als Pro-\n duzent fremder Arbeitsamkeit, als Auspumper von Mehrarbeit und Exploi-\n teur von Arbeitskraft, übergipfelt es an Energie, Masslosigkeit und Wirk-\n samkeit alle früheren auf direkter Zwangsarbeit beruhenden Pro-\n duktionssysteme.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"10.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0032","text":"Das Kapital ordnet sich zunächst die Arbeit unter mit den gegebenen\n technologischen Bedingungen, worin es sie historisch vorfindet. Es ver-\n ändert daher nicht unmittelbar die Produktionsweise. Die Produktion von\n Mehrwerth in der bisher betrachteten Form, durch einfache Verlängerung des\n Arbeitstags, erschien daher von jedem Wechsel der Produktionsweise selbst\n unabhängig. Sie war in der altmodischen Bäckerei nicht minder wirksam\n als in der modernen Baumwollspinnerei. Betrachteten wir den Produk-\n tionsprozess daher bloss unter dem Gesichtspunkt des Arbeitsprozesses,\n so verhielt sich der Arbeiter zu den Produktionsmitteln nicht als Kapital,\n sondern als blossem Mittel und Material seiner zweckmässigen produktiven\n Thätigkeit. In einer Gerberei z. B. behandelt er die Felle als seinen blossen\n Arbeitsgegenstand. Es ist nicht der Kapitalist, dem er das Fell gerbt.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"10.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0033","text":"Anders, sobald wir den Produktionsprozess unter dem Gesichtspunkt des\nVerwerthungsprozesses betrachteten. Die Produktionsmittel ver-\n wandelten sich sofort in Mittel zur Einsaugung fremder Arbeit. Es ist nicht\n mehr der Arbeiter, der die Produktionsmittel anwendet, son-\n dern es sind die Produktionsmittel, die den Arbeiter an-\n wenden. Statt von ihm als stoffliche Elemente seiner produktiven Thä-\n tigkeit verzehrt zu werden, verzehren sie ihn als Ferment ihres eignen\n Lebensprozesses, und der Lebensprozess des Kapitals besteht nur in seiner\n [Running matter: I. 19] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"290\", \"facs\": \"#f0309\"} --> Bewegung als sich selbst verwerthender Werth. Schmelzöfen\n und Arbeitsgebäude, die des Nachts ruhn und keine lebendige Arbeit ein-\n saugen, sind „reiner Verlust“ („a mere loss“) für den Kapitalisten.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"10.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0034","text":"Da-\n rum konstituiren Schmelzöfen und Arbeitsgebäude einen „Anspruch auf\n die Nachtarbeit“ der Arbeitskräfte. Die blosse Verwandlung des Geldes\n in gegenständliche Faktoren des Produktionsprozesses, in Produktions-\n mittel, verwandelt letztre in Rechtstitel und Zwangstitel auf\n fremde Arbeit und Mehrarbeit. Wie diese der kapitalistischen Produktion\n eigenthümliche und sie charakterisirende Verkehrung, ja Verrückung des\n Verhältnisses von todter und lebendiger Arbeit, von Werth und werth-\n schöpferischer Kraft, sich im Bewusstsein der Kapitalistenköpfe abspie-\n gelt, zeige schliesslich noch ein Beispiel.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"10.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0035","text":"Während der englischen Fabri-\n kantenrevolte von 1848—50 schrieb „der Chef der Leinen- und Baum-\n wollspinnerei zu Paisley, einer der ältesten und respektabelsten Firmen\n von Westschottland, der Herrn Carlile, Söhne und Co., deren Kompagnie\n seit 1752 besteht und Generation nach Generation von derselben Familie\n geführt wird“, — dieser äusserst intelligente Gentleman also schrieb in die\n „Glasgow Daily Mail“ vom 25. April 1849 einen Brief [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 30th April 1849“, p. 59.]  unter dem\n Titel: „das Relaissystem“, worin u. a. folgende grotesk naive Stelle\n unterläuft: „Lasst uns nun die Uebel betrachten, die aus einer Reduktion\n der Arbeitszeit von 12 auf 10 Stunden fliessen . . . . Sie „belaufen“ sich\n auf die allerernsthafteste Beschädigung der Aussichten und des Eigen-\n thums des Fabrikanten. Arbeitete er (d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"10.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0036","text":"seine „Hände“) 12 Stunden\n und wird er auf 10 beschränkt, dann schrumpfen je 12 Maschinen oder\n Spindeln seines Etablissements auf 10 zusammen, („then every 12 ma-\n chines or spindles, in his establishment, shrink to 10“), und wollte er\n seine Fabrik verkaufen, so würden sie nur als 10 gewerthschätzt werden,\n so dass so ein sechster Theil vom Werth einer jeden Fabrik im ganzen\n Lande abgezogen würde“ [Note: l. c. p. 60. Fabrikinspektor Stuart, selbst Schotte, und im Gegensatz\n zu den englischen Fabrikinspektoren, ganz in kapitalistischer Denkart befangen,\n bemerkt ausdrücklich, dieser Brief, den er seinem Bericht einverleibt, „sei die aller-\n nützlichste Mittheilung, die irgend einer der Fabrikanten, welche das Relaissystem\n anwenden, gemacht, und ganz besonders darauf berechnet, die Vorurtheile und\n Skrupel gegen jenes System zu beseitigen.“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"10.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0037","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"291\", \"facs\": \"#f0310\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"10.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0038","text":"Diesem erbangestammten Kapitalhirn von Westschottland ver-\n schwimmt der Werth der Produktionsmittel, Spindeln u. s. w., so sehr\n mit ihrer Kapitaleigenschaft, sich selbst zu verwerthen, oder täg-\n lich ein bestimmtes Quantum fremder Gratisarbeit einzuschlucken, dass\n der Chef des Hauses Carlile und Co. in der That wähnt, beim Verkauf\n seiner Fabrik werde ihm nicht nur der Werth der Spindeln gezahlt, sondern\n obendrein ihre Verwerthung, nicht nur die Arbeit, die in ihnen steckt und zur\n Produktion von Spindeln derselben Art nöthig ist, sondern auch die Mehr-\n arbeit, die sie täglich aus den braven Westschotten von Paisley auspumpen,\n und eben desshalb, meint er, schrumpfe mit der Kontraktion des Arbeits-\n tags um zwei Stunden der Verkaufspreis von je 12 Spinnmaschinen auf\n den von je 10 zusammen!","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"10.38"}],"sha256":"6ad90b6c47ab1bc275e33aaefcc4e34208c0a61ae1d0593124c106baa00a6fa0","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T203653477777Z_ac5f16c7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"5) Rate und Masse des Mehrwerths.","source_sha256":"6ad90b6c47ab1bc275e33aaefcc4e34208c0a61ae1d0593124c106baa00a6fa0","sha256":"fa3df80e82ca6bbaf17c6b4af5d567d38565d958357a8566fbee29aa8783fe2e","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:36:53.477795+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0001","text":"As hitherto, in this paragraph the value of labour-\npower, that is, the portion of the working day necessary for the reproduction or maintenance of labour-power, is assumed to be a given, constant\nmagnitude.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"10.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0002","text":"This being assumed, the mass of\nsurplus-value which the individual worker supplies to the capitalist in\na definite period of time is given together with the rate. If, for example, necessary labour amounts daily to\n6 hours, expressed in a quantity of gold of 3 sh. = 1 thaler, then\n1 thaler is the daily value of a labour-power, or the capital-value\nadvanced in the purchase\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"282\", \"facs\": \"#f0301\"} -->of a labour-power. If, further, the rate\nof surplus-value is 100%, then this variable capital of\n1 thaler produces a mass of surplus-value of 1 thaler, or the worker supplies\ndaily a mass of surplus-labour of 6 hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"10.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0003","text":"But variable capital is the money-expression for the aggre-\ngate value of all the labour-powers which the capitalist employs simultaneously\nin a definite process of production. If the daily value\nof one labour-power is 1 thaler, then a capital of 100 thalers must be ad-\nvanced in order to exploit 100, and of n thalers in order to\nexploit n labour-powers daily. The value of the advanced variable capi-\ntal is therefore equal to the average value of a labour-\npower multiplied by the number of labour-powers employed. Given\nthe value of labour-power, therefore, the extent of value\nor magnitude of the variable capital varies with the mass of labour-\npowers appropriated or the number of workers employed simultaneously.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"10.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0004","text":"If a variable capital of 1 thaler, the daily value of one\nlabour-power, produces a daily surplus-value of 1 thaler, then a variable\ncapital of 100 thalers produces a daily surplus-value of 100, and one of\nn thalers a daily surplus-value of 1 thaler × n. The mass\nof surplus-value produced is therefore equal to the surplus-value which\nthe working day of the individual worker supplies, multiplied by the number\nof workers employed.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"10.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0005","text":"But since, further, the mass of surplus-value which the\nindividual worker produces is determined, given the value of labour-power, by\nthe rate of surplus-value, it follows, under the same pre-\nsupposition: The mass of surplus-value produced by a given variable\ncapital is equal to the magnitude\nof the advanced variable capital multiplied by\nthe rate of surplus-value, or is determined by the composite\nratio between the number of labour-powers simultaneously ex-\nploited and the degree of exploitation of the\nindividual labour-power.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"10.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0006","text":"In the production of a definite mass of surplus-value,\ntherefore, the diminution of one factor can be compensated\nby the increase of the other. If the variable capital is diminished and\nat the same time the rate of surplus-value\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"283\", \"facs\": \"#f0302\"} --> increased in the same proportion, the mass of surplus-value produced remains un-\nchanged. If, under the earlier assumptions, the capitalist must advance 100 tha-\nlers in order to exploit 100 workers daily, and if the\nrate of surplus-value amounts to 50%, then this variable capital of 100\nyields a surplus-value of 50, or of 100 × 3 working hours. If the\nrate of surplus-value is doubled, or the working day lengthened from 6 to 9,\nfrom 6 to 12 hours, then the variable capital diminished by one half,\nof 50 thalers, likewise yields a surplus-value of 50 thalers,\nor of 50 × 6 working hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"10.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0007","text":"Diminution of variable capi-\ntal is therefore compensable by a proportional increase in the degree of exploitation\nof labour-power, or diminution in the number of workers employed by a\nproportional prolongation of the working day. Within cer-\ntain limits, the supply of labour extortable by capital is therefore\nindependent of the supply of workers [Note: This elementary law appears to be un-\nknown to the gentlemen of vulgar economy, who, as inverted Archimedes, believe\nthey have found in the determination of the market prices of\nlabour by demand and supply the point, not in order to lift the world from its hinges,\nbut in order to bring it to a standstill.] . Conversely, a de-\ncrease in the rate of surplus-value leaves the mass of surplus-\nvalue produced unchanged if, proportionally, the magnitude of\nthe variable capital or the number of workers employed increases.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"10.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0008","text":"Meanwhile, the replacement of the number of workers or magnitude of variable\ncapital by an increased rate of surplus-value or prolongation of the\nworking day has absolute barriers that cannot be overleapt. Whatever\nthe value of labour-power may be, whether therefore the labour-\ntime necessary for the maintenance of the work-\ner amounts to 2 or 10 hours, the aggregate value which a worker,\nday in, day out, can produce is always smaller than the value in which\n24 working hours are embodied, smaller than 12 sh. or 4 thalers, if that is\nthe money-expression of the 24 embodied working hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"10.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0009","text":"Under our earlier pre-\nsupposition, according to which 6 working hours are required daily in order to reproduce labour-power\nitself or to replace the capital-\nvalue advanced in its purchase, a variable capital of 500 thalers, which\nemploys daily 500 workers at a rate of surplus-value of 100%, or with a twelve-hour\nworking day, produces daily a surplus-value of 500 thalers\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"284\", \"facs\": \"#f0303\"} --> or 6 × 500 working hours. A capital of 100 thalers, which employs 100\nworkers daily at a rate of surplus-value of 200%, or with an 18-hour\nworking day, produces only a mass of surplus-value of 200 tha-\nlers, or 18 × 100 working hours. And its aggregate value-pro-\nduct, equivalent to the advanced capital plus surplus-value, can,\nday in, day out, never attain the sum of 400 thalers, or 24 × 100\nworking hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"10.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0010","text":"The absolute barrier of the aver-\nage working day, which by nature is always smaller than\n24 hours, forms an absolute barrier to the replacement of\nvariable capital by an increased rate of surplus-\nvalue, or of the number of workers exploited by an in-\ncreased degree of exploitation of labour-power. This pal-\npable law is important for the explanation of many phenomena arising\nfrom the tendency of capital, to be developed later, to reduce the number of\nworkers employed by it, or its component part converted into labour-power,\nto the minimum barrier, in contradic-\ntion to its other tendency, to produce the greatest possible mass of\nsurplus-value. Conversely.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"10.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0011","text":"If the mass of labour-\npowers employed, or the magnitude of the variable capital, increases, but\nmore slowly than the rate of surplus-value decreases, then the mass\nof surplus-value produced declines.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"10.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0012","text":"A third law follows from the determination of the mass of\nsurplus-value produced by the two factors, rate\nof surplus-value and magnitude of the advanced variable capital. Given the\nrate of surplus-value or the degree of exploitation of labour-power, and the\nvalue of labour-power or the magnitude of necessary labour-\ntime, it is self-evident that the greater the variable\ncapital, the greater the mass of value produced\nand of surplus-value. If the limit of the working day is given,\nand likewise the limit of its necessary component part,\nthen the mass of value and surplus-value which an individual capitalist pro-\nduces evidently depends exclusively upon the mass of labour which he sets\nin motion.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"10.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0013","text":"But this, under the given assumptions, depends upon\nthe mass of labour-power or the number of workers which he\nexploits, and this number in turn is determined by the magnitude\nof the variable capital advanced by him. Given\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"285\", \"facs\": \"#f0304\"} --> the rate of surplus-value and the value of labour-\npower, therefore, the masses of surplus-value pro-\nduced are directly proportional to the magnitudes of the advanced\nvariable capitals. Now it is known, however, that the capitalist divides his capi-\ntal into two parts. One part he lays out in means of production.\nThis is the constant part of his capital. The other part he converts\ninto living labour-power. This part forms his variable capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"10.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0014","text":"On the basis of the same mode of production, in different\nspheres of production different division of capital into constant\nand variable component parts takes place. Within the same sphere of produc-\ntion this ratio varies with varying technologi-\ncal foundation and social combination of the process of production.\nBut however a given capital may always be divided into con-\nstant and variable component parts, whether the latter stands in relation to the former\nas 1 : 2, 1 : 10, or 1 : x, the law just established is not\naffected thereby, since, according to earlier analysis, the value of constant\ncapital indeed reappears in the value of the product, but does not enter into the newly\nformed value-product. In order to employ 1000 spinners,\nmore raw materials, spindles, &c. are naturally required than in order to\nemploy 100.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"10.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0015","text":"But the value of these additional means of production may\nrise, fall, remain unchanged, be great or small, it remains without\nany influence upon the valorisation process of the labour-powers setting them\nin motion. The law established above thus assumes the more\ngeneral form: The masses of value and surplus-value pro-\nduced by different capitals are, given the value and an equal degree of exploita-\ntion of labour-power, directly proportional to the magnitudes of the\nvariable components of these capitals, i.e. of their components\nconverted into living labour-power.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"10.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0016","text":"This law manifestly contradicts all experience founded on\nappearance. Everyone knows that a cotton spinner who, calculating\nthe percentages of the total capital employed, applies relatively\na great deal of constant and little variable capital, does not on that account\nreap a smaller profit or surplus-value than a baker who sets relatively\na great deal of variable and little constant capital in motion. For the\nsolution of this apparent contradiction many intermediate links are still needed,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"286\", \"facs\": \"#f0305\"} --> just as, from the standpoint of elementary algebra, many intermediate links are needed\nto understand that \\frac{0}{0} can represent a real magnitude. Although\nit has never formulated the law, classical political economy\ninstinctively holds fast to it, because it is a necessary consequence of the law of\nvalue in general.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"10.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0017","text":"By violent abstraction it seeks to rescue it from\nthe contradictions of appearance. It will later [Note: More on this in the “Fourth Book”.]\nbe seen how the Ricardian school has stumbled over this stumbling-\nblock. Vulgar economy, which “has really learned nothing either,”\nhere, as everywhere, insists upon appearance against the law of appearance.\nIn contrast to Spinoza, it believes that “ignorance is a suffi-\ncient reason”.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"10.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0018","text":"The labour which the aggregate capital of a society sets in mo-\ntion daily can be regarded as a single working day.\nIf, for example, the number of workers is one million and the average\nworking day of one worker amounts to 10 hours, then the social\nworking day consists of 10 million hours. Given the length\nof this working day, whether its limits be drawn physically or socially, the\nmass of surplus-value can be increased only by increasing\nthe number of workers, i.e. the working population. The growth\nof the population here forms the mathematical limit for the produc-\ntion of surplus-value by the social aggregate capital. Conversely.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"10.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0019","text":"Given the magnitude of the population, this limit is formed\nby the possible prolongation of the working day [Note: “The labour, that is the economic time of society, is a given\n portion, say ten hours a day of a million of people or ten millions hours . . . .\n Capital has its boundary of increase. The boundary may, at any given period,\n be attained in the actual extent of economic time employed.“ (“An Essay on\n the Political Economy of Nations. London 1821“, p. 48, 49.)] .\nIt will be seen in the following chapter that this law applies only to the form\nof surplus-value hitherto considered.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"10.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0020","text":"From the consideration hitherto made of the production of surplus-value it fol-\nlows that not every arbitrary sum of money or value can be transformed\ninto capital, but that rather a de-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"287\", \"facs\": \"#f0306\"} --> finite minimum of money or exchange-value in the hand of the indi-\nvidual owner of money or commodities is presupposed for this transformation. The minimum\nof variable capital is the cost price of a single labour-\npower, which is used up throughout the whole year, day in, day out, for the acquisition of\nsurplus-value. If this worker were in possession of his own\nmeans of production, and contented himself with living as a worker, the labour-time\nnecessary for the reproduction of his means of subsistence,\nsay 8 hours daily, would suffice for him. He would therefore require only means of produc-\ntion for 8 working hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"10.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0021","text":"The capitalist, on the other hand, who makes him perform,\nbeyond these 8 hours, say 4 hours of surplus-labour, requires an\nadditional sum of money to procure the additional means of produc-\ntion. Under our assumption, however, he would already have to employ two\nworkers in order to be able to live like a worker from the surplus-value appropriated\ndaily, i.e. to be able to satisfy his necessary needs. In this\ncase mere subsistence would be the aim of his production, not increase\nof wealth, and the latter is presupposed in capitalist produc-\ntion. In order that he may live only twice as well as an ordinary\nworker, and reconvert one half of the surplus-value produced into capi-\ntal, he would have to increase, together with the number of workers, the minimum\nof the capital advanced eightfold.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"10.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0022","text":"However, like his worker, he may himself directly set hand to the production process, but he is then only an intermediate being between capitalist and worker, a “small master.” A certain degree of capitalist production presupposes that the capitalist can devote the whole time during which he functions as capitalist, i.e. as personified capital, to the appropriation and hence control of others’ labour, and to the sale of the products of this labour [Note: “The farmer cannot rely on his own labour; and if he does, I will main-\ntain that he is a loser by it. His employment should be, a general attention to\nthe whole: his thrasher must be watched, or he will soon lose his wages in corn\nnot thrashed out; his mowers, reapers etc.”","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"10.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0023","text":"must be looked after; he must con-\nstantly go round his fences; he must see there is no neglect; which would be the\ncase if he was confined to any one spot.“ „An Enquiry into the Connec-\ntion between the Price of Provisions, and the Size of Farms etc.\nBy a Farmer. London 1773“, p. 12. This writing is very interesting.] . The transformation of the handi-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"288\", \"facs\": \"#f0307\"} -->craft master into the capitalist was forcibly prevented by the guild industry of the Middle\nAges by its restricting the number of workers which an individual master was permitted to employ to a very small maximum. The owner of money or commodities is only really transformed into a capitalist where the minimum sum advanced for production stands far above the medieval maximum.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"10.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0024","text":"Here, as in\nnatural science, the correctness of the law discovered by Hegel in his\nLogic proves itself: that merely quantitative changes, at a certain point, pass over into qualitative distinctions [Note: The molecular theory applied in modern chemistry, initiated by Laurent and Ger-\nhardt, and first developed scientifically by Professor Würtz in Paris,\nrests upon no other law.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"10.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0025","text":"The minimum amount of value over which the individual owner of money\nor commodities must dispose in order to emerge as a capitalist varies at different stages of development of capitalist production and, at a given stage of development, differs in different spheres of production, according to their particular technological conditions. Certain spheres of production already require, at the beginnings of capitalist production, a minimum of capital which is not yet found in the hands of individual persons.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"10.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0026","text":"This\noccasionally gives rise to state subsidies to such private persons, as in France in the time of Colbert and as in many German states down into our epoch, and occasionally to the formation of companies with a legal monopoly for carrying on certain branches of industry and trade [Note: Martin Luther calls institutions of this sort “the society Monopolia.”] , — the forerunners\nof modern joint-stock companies.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"10.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0027","text":"[Note: One may study therein the genesis of the “capitalist farmer” or “merchant\nfarmer,” as he is expressly called, and listen to his self-glorification in contrast to\nthe “small farmer,” whose concern is essentially subsistence. “The capitalist class\nis first partially and finally wholly released from the necessity of manual labour.” (“Text-\nbook of Lectures on the Polit. Economy of Nations. By the Rev.\nMr. Richard Jones. Hertford 1852.” Lecture III.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"10.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0028","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"289\", \"facs\": \"#f0308\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"10.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0029","text":"We shall not dwell on the details of the changes which the relation between capitalist and wage-worker underwent in the course of the production process, and thus neither on the further determinations of capital itself. Only a few main points shall be emphasized here.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"10.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0030","text":"Within the production process, capital developed into command over labour, i.e. over labour-power in action, or over the worker himself. Personified capital, the capitalist, takes care that the worker performs his work properly and with the requisite degree of intensity.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"10.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0031","text":"Capital developed further into a relation of compulsion which forces the working class to perform more labour than the narrow circle of its own necessities of life prescribed. And, as producer of alien labour-activity, as extractor of surplus labour and exploiter of labour-power, it surpasses in energy, excessiveness, and effectiveness all earlier systems of production based on direct forced labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"10.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0032","text":"Capital initially subsumes labour under itself with the given technological conditions in which it finds it historically. It therefore does not immediately alter the mode of production. The production of surplus-value in the form hitherto considered, through the simple prolongation of the working day, therefore appeared independent of every change in the mode of production itself. It was no less effective in the old-fashioned bakery than in modern cotton spinning. If, therefore, we considered the production process merely from the standpoint of the labour process, the worker related to the means of production not as capital, but as mere means and material of his purposive productive activity. In a tannery, for example, he treats the hides as his mere object of labour. It is not for the capitalist that he tans the hide.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"10.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0033","text":"It was otherwise as soon as we considered the production process from the standpoint of the valorization process. The means of production immediately transformed themselves into means for sucking in alien labour. It is no longer the worker who employs the means of production, but the means of production which employ the worker. Instead of being consumed by him as material elements of his productive activity, they consume him as the ferment of their own life-process, and the life-process of capital consists only in its\n[Running matter: I. 19]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"290\", \"facs\": \"#f0309\"} --> movement as value valorizing itself. Smelting furnaces\nand workshop buildings which rest at night and suck in no living labour are “a mere loss” for the capitalist.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"10.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0034","text":"For this\nreason, smelting furnaces and workshop buildings constitute a “claim to the night labour” of labour-powers. The mere transformation of money into objective factors of the production process, into means of production, transforms the latter into legal titles and titles of compulsion upon alien labour and surplus labour. Finally, let one more example show how this inversion, indeed derangement, of the relation of dead and living labour, of value and value-creating force, peculiar to and characteristic of capitalist production, is reflected in the consciousness of capitalist heads.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"10.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0035","text":"During the English manufacturers’ revolt of 1848–50, “the head of the linen and cotton spinning mill at Paisley, one of the oldest and most respectable firms of West Scotland, Messrs. Carlile, Sons and Co., whose company has existed since 1752 and has been conducted by the same family generation after generation,” — this exceedingly intelligent gentleman, then, wrote a letter in the “Glasgow Daily Mail” of 25 April 1849 [Note: “Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 30th April 1849”, p. 59.] under the\ntitle: “the relay system,” in which, among other things, the following grotesquely naive passage occurs: “Let us now consider the evils which flow from a reduction of working time from 12 to 10 hours . . . . They ‘amount’ to the most serious injury to the prospects and property of the manufacturer. If he (i.e.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"10.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0036","text":"his “hands”) worked 12 hours\nand he is restricted to 10, then every 12 machines or spindles in his establishment shrink to 10, (“then every 12 ma-\nchines or spindles, in his establishment, shrink to 10”), and if he wished to sell his factory, they would be valued only as 10, so that a sixth part would thus be deducted from the value of every factory throughout the country” [Note: l. c. p. 60. Factory Inspector Stuart, himself a Scot, and, in contrast\nto the English factory inspectors, wholly caught up in the capitalist mode of thought,\nexpressly remarks that this letter, which he incorporates in his report, “is the most\nuseful communication which any of the manufacturers using the relay system have\nmade, and is particularly calculated to remove prejudices and scruples against that\nsystem.”] .","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"10.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0037","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"291\", \"facs\": \"#f0310\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"10.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0038","text":"In this capital-brain of West Scotland, inherited from father to son, the value of the means of production, spindles, etc., is so merged with their capitalist property of valorizing themselves, or of daily swallowing up a definite quantity of alien unpaid labour, that the head of the house of Carlile and Co. actually imagines that, upon selling his factory, he will be paid not only the value of the spindles, but in addition their valorization; not only the labour embodied in them and necessary for the production of spindles of the same kind, but also the surplus labour which they daily pump out of the worthy West Scots of Paisley; and it is precisely for this reason, he thinks, that with the contraction of the working day by two hours the selling price of every 12 spinning machines shrinks to that of every 10 together!","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"10.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_5RUM:P0038"]}],"source_digest":"1a26ac5eea4972685637be1d4f3953df34222211c7d4bd1e5e5be3963fe77529","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR","title":"1) Begriff des relativen Mehrwerths.","order":11,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_1BDR","text":"## Viertes Kapitel.\n Die Produktion des relativen Mehrwerths.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## 1) Begriff des relativen Mehrwerths.\n\n\n\n\nDer Theil des Arbeitstags, der bloss ein Aequivalent für den\n vom Kapital gezahlten Werth der Arbeitskraft produzirt, galt uns bisher\n als constante Grösse, was er in der That ist unter gegebnen Pro-\n duktionsbedingungen, auf einer vorhandnen ökonomischen Entwicklungs-\n stufe der Gesellschaft. Ueber diese seine nothwendige Arbeitszeit\n hinaus konnte der Arbeiter 2, 3, 4, 6 u. s. w. Stunden arbeiten. Von\n der Grösse dieser Verlängerung hingen Rate des Mehr-\n werths und Grösse des Arbeitstags ab. War die nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit constant, so dagegen der Gesammtarbeitstag variabel. Unter-\n stelle jetzt einen Arbeitstag, dessen Grösse und dessen Theilung\n in nothwendige Arbeit und Mehrarbeit gegeben sind. Die Linie a c\n a-------------b---c stelle z. B. einen zwölfstündigen Arbeitstag vor,\n das Stück a b 10 Stunden nothwendige Arbeit, das Stück b c 2 Stunden\n Mehrarbeit. Wie kann nun die Produktion von Mehrwerth vergrössert,\n d. h. die Mehrarbeit verlängert werden, ohne jede weitere Verlängerung\n oder unabhängig von jeder weiteren Verlängerung von a c?\n\n\n [Running matter: 19*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"292\", \"facs\": \"#f0311\"} -->\n\nTrotz gegebner Grenzen des Arbeitstags a c scheint b c verlängerbar,\n wenn nicht durch Ausdehnung über seinen Endpunkt c, der zugleich der\n Endpunkt des Arbeitstags a c ist, so durch Verschiebung seines Anfangs-\n punkts b in entgegengesetzter Richtung nach a hin. Nimm an, b' b\n a-------------b'--b----c sei gleich der Hälfte von b c oder gleich einer\n Arbeitsstunde. Wird nun in dem zwölfstündigen Arbeitstag a c der Punkt\n b nach b' verrückt, so dehnt sich b c aus zu b' c, die Mehrarbeit wächst\n um die Hälfte, von 2 auf 3 Stunden, obgleich der Arbeitstag nach wie vor\n nur 12 Stunden zählt. Diese Ausdehnung der Mehrarbeit von b c auf\n b' c, von 2 auf 3 Stunden, ist aber offenbar unmöglich ohne gleichzeitige\n Zusammenziehung der nothwendigen Arbeit von a b auf a b', von 10 auf\n 9 Stunden. Der Verlängerung der Mehrarbeit entspräche die\nVerkürzung der nothwendigen Arbeit, oder ein Theil der\n Arbeitszeit, die der Arbeiter bisher in der That für sich selbst verbraucht,\n verwandelt sich in Arbeitszeit für den Kapitalisten. Was verändert,\n wäre nicht die Länge des Arbeitstags, sondern seine Theilung\n in nothwendige Arbeit und Mehrarbeit.\n\n\n\n\nAndrerseits ist die Grösse der Mehrarbeit offenbar selbst ge-\n geben mit gegebner Grösse des Arbeitstags und gegebnem\n Werth der Arbeitskraft. Der Werth der Arbeitskraft, d. h. die zu ihrer\n Produktion erheischte Arbeitszeit, bestimmt die zur Reproduktion ihres\n Werths nothwendige Arbeitszeit. Stellt sich eine Arbeitsstunde in einem\n Goldquantum von einem halben Shilling oder 6 d. dar, und beträgt der\n Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft 5 sh., so muss der Arbeiter täglich 10 Stun-\n den arbeiten, um den ihm vom Kapital gezahlten Tageswerth seiner Arbeits-\n kraft zu ersetzen oder ein Aequivalent für den Werth seiner nothwendigen\n täglichen Lebensmittel zu produziren. Mit dem Werth dieser Lebensmittel\n ist der Werth seiner Arbeitskraft [Note: Der Werth des täglichen Durchschnittslohns ist bestimmt durch das, was\n der Arbeiter braucht „so as to live, labeur, and generate.“ (William Petty:\n „Political Anatomy of Ireland“ 1672, p. 64.) „The Price of Labour\n is always constituted of the price of necessaries.“ Der Arbeiter erhält nicht den\n entsprechenden Lohn „whenever . . . . the labouring man’s wages will not,\nsuitably to his low rank and station, as a labouring man, support such a family\n as is often the lot of many of them to have.“ (J. Vanderlint l. c. p. 19.)\n „Le simple ouvrier, qui n’a que ses bras et son industrie, n’a rien qu’autant qu’il] , mit dem Werth seiner Arbeitskraft ist\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"293\", \"facs\": \"#f0312\"} --> die Grösse seiner nothwendigen Arbeitszeit gegeben. Die\nGrösse der Mehrarbeit aber wird erhalten durch Subtraktion\n der nothwendigen Arbeitszeit vom Gesammtarbeitstag.\n Zehn Stunden subtrahirt von zwölf lassen zwei, und es ist nicht abzusehn,\n wie die Mehrarbeit unter den gegebnen Bedingungen über zwei Stunden\n hinaus verlängert werden kann. Allerdings mag der Kapitalist statt 5 sh.\n dem Arbeiter nur 4 sh. 6 d. oder noch weniger zahlen. Zur Reproduktion\n dieses Werths von 4 sh. 6 d. würden 9 Arbeitsstunden genügen, von dem\n zwölfstündigen Arbeitstag daher 3 statt 2 Stunden der Mehrarbeit anheim-\n fallen und der Mehrwerth selbst von 1 sh. auf 1 sh. 6 d. steigen. Diess\n Resultat wäre jedoch nur erzielt durch Herabdrückung des Lohns\n des Arbeiters unter den Werth seiner Arbeitskraft. Mit\n den 4 sh. 6 d., die er in 9 Stunden producirt, verfügt er über ⅒ weniger\n Lebensmittel als vorher und so findet nur eine verkümmerte Reproduk-\n tion seiner Arbeitskraft statt. Die Mehrarbeit würde hier nur verlängert\n durch Ueberschreitung ihrer normalen Grenzen, ihre Domäne nur ausge-\n dehnt durch usurpatorischen Abbruch von der Domäne der nothwendigen\n Arbeitszeit. Trotz der wichtigen Rolle, welche diese Methode in der wirk-\n lichen Bewegung des Arbeitslohnes spielt, ist sie hier ausgeschlossen durch\n die Voraussetzung, dass die Waaren, also auch die Arbeitskraft, zu ihrem\n vollen Werth gekauft und verkauft werden. Diess einmal unterstellt, kann\n die zur Produktion der Arbeitskraft oder zur Reproduktion ihres Werths noth-\n wendige Arbeitszeit nicht abnehmen, weil der Lohn des Arbeiters\nunter den Werth seiner Arbeitskraft, sondern nur weil dieser Werth\n selbst sinkt. Bei gegebner Länge des Arbeitstags muss die Verlänge-\n rung der Mehrarbeit aus der Verkürzung der nothwendigen Arbeitszeit ent-\n springen, nicht umgekehrt die Verkürzung der nothwendigen Arbeitszeit\n aus der Verlängerung der Mehrarbeit. In unsrem Beispiel muss der\n Werth der Arbeitskraft wirklich um ⅒ sinken, damit die nothwendige\n [Note: parvient à vendre à d’autres sa peine … En tout genre de travail il doit arriver\n et il arrive en effet, que le salaire de l’ouvrier se borne à ce qui lui est nécessaire\n pour lui procurer la subsistance.“ (Turgot: „Réflexions sur la For-\n mation et la Distribution des Richesses“ (1766). Oeuvres ed.\n Daire, t. I, p. 10.) „The price of the necessaries of life is, in fact, the cost\n of producing labour.“ (Malthus: „Inquiry into etc. Rent.“ Lond.\n 1815, p. 48 Note.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"294\", \"facs\": \"#f0313\"} --> Arbeitszeit um ⅒ abnehme, von 10 auf 9 Stunden, und daher die\n Mehrarbeit sich von 2 auf 3 Stunden verlängere.\n\n\n\n\nEine solche Senkung des Werths der Arbeitskraft um ⅒ bedingt\n aber ihrerseits, dass dieselbe Masse Lebensmittel, die früher in\n 10, jetzt in 9 Stunden producirt wird. Diess ist jedoch unmöglich\n ohne eine Erhöhung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit. Mit\n gegebnen Mitteln kann ein Schuster z. B. ein Paar Stiefel in einem Ar-\n beitstag von 12 Stunden machen. Soll er in derselben Zeit zwei Paar\n Stiefel machen, so muss sich die Produktivkraft seiner Arbeit verdoppeln,\n und sie kann sich nicht verdoppeln ohne eine Aenderung in seinen Ar-\n beitsmitteln oder seiner Arbeitsmethode oder beiden zugleich. Es muss\n daher eine Revolution in den Produktionsbedingungen seiner\n Arbeit, d. h. in seiner Produktionsweise und daher im Arbeitsprozess\n selbst eintreten. Unter Erhöhung der Produktivkraft der Ar-\n beit verstehn wir hier überhaupt eine Veränderung im Arbeits-\n prozess, wodurch die zur Produktion einer Waare gesellschaftlich er-\n heischte Arbeitszeit verkürzt wird, ein kleineres Quantum Arbeit also die\nKraft erwirbt ein grösseres Quantum Gebrauchswerth zu produciren [Note: „Quando si perfezionano le arti, che no è altero che la scoperta di nuove\n vie, onde si possa compiere una manufattura con meno gente o (che è lo\n stesso) in minor tempo di prima.“ Galiani l. c. p. 159. „L’économie\n sur les frais de production ne peut être autre chose que l’économie sur la quan-\n tité de travail employé pour produire.“ Sismondi: Études etc. t. I. p. 22.)] .\n Während also bei der Produktion des Mehrwerths in der bisher betrach-\n teten Form die Produktionsweise als gegeben unterstellt war, ge-\n nügt es für die Produktion von Mehrwerth durch Verwandlung noth-\n wendiger Arbeit in Mehrarbeit keineswegs, dass das Kapital sich des Ar-\n beitsprozesses in seiner historisch überlieferten oder vorhandenen\n Gestalt bemächtigt und nur seine Dauer verlängert. Es muss die tech-\n nologischen und gesellschaftlichen Bedingungen des Arbeitsprozesses, also\n die Produktionsweise selbst umwälzen, um die Produktivkraft\n der Arbeit zu erhöhn, durch die Erhöhung der Produktivkraft der Ar-\n beit den Werth der Arbeitskraft zu senken und so den zur Repro-\n duktion dieses Werths nothwendigen Theil des Arbeitstages zu ver-\n kürzen.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"295\", \"facs\": \"#f0314\"} -->\n\nDurch Verlängerung des Arbeitstags producirten Mehrwerth\n nenne ich absoluten Mehrwerth; den Mehrwerth dagegen, der aus\nVerkürzung der nothwendigen Arbeitszeit und entsprechender Ver-\n änderung im Grössenverhältniss der beiden Bestandtheile des Ar-\n beitstags entspringt, — relativen Mehrwerth.\n\n\n\n\nUm den Werth der Arbeitskraft zu senken, muss die Steige-\n rung der Produktivkraft Industriezweige ergreifen, deren Pro-\n dukte den Werth der Arbeitskraft bestimmen, also entweder dem Umkreis\n der gewohnheitsmässigen Lebensmittel angehören oder sie ersetzen können.\n Der Werth einer Waare ist aber nicht nur bestimmt durch das Quantum der\n Arbeit, welche ihr die letzte Form giebt, sondern ebensowohl durch die in\n ihren Produktionsmitteln enthaltene Arbeitsmasse. Z. B. der Werth\n eines Stiefels nicht nur durch die Schusterarbeit, sondern auch durch den\n Werth von Leder, Pech, Draht u. s. w. Steigerung der Produktivkraft und\n entsprechende Verwohlfeilerung der Waaren in den Industrien,\n welche die stofflichen Elemente des constanten Kapitals, die Arbeits-\n mittel und das Arbeitsmaterial, zur Erzeugung der nothwendigen Lebens-\n mittel liefern, senken also ebenfalls den Werth der Arbeitskraft. In Pro-\n duktionszweigen dagegen, die weder nothwendige Lebensmittel liefern, noch\n Produktionsmittel zu ihrer Herstellung, lässt die erhöhte Produktivkraft\n den Werth der Arbeitskraft unberührt.\n\n\n\n\nDie verwohlfeilerte Waare senkt natürlich den Werth der Arbeits-\n kraft nur pro tanto, d. h. nur im Verhältniss, worin sie in die Repro-\n duktion der Arbeitskraft eingeht. Hemden z. B. sind ein nothwendiges\n Lebensmittel, aber nur eins von vielen. Ihre Verwohlfeilerung vermin-\n dert nur die Ausgabe des Arbeiters für Hemden. Die Gesammtsumme\n der nothwendigen Lebensmittel besteht zwar nur aus einzelnen Waaren,\n lauter Produkten besondrer Industrien, und der Werth jeder besondern\n Waare bildet immer nur einen aliquoten Theil vom Werth der Arbeitskraft.\n Die Gesammtabnahme dieses Werths, und daher der zu seiner Reproduk-\n tion nothwendigen Arbeitszeit, ist jedoch gleich der Summe der Verkür-\n zungen der nothwendigen Arbeitszeit in allen jenen besondren Produktions-\n zweigen. Wir behandeln diess allgemeine Resultat hier so, als wäre es\nunmittelbares Resultat und unmittelbarer Zweck in jedem\n einzelnen Fall, obgleich die Sache anders erscheint. Wenn ein einzel-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"296\", \"facs\": \"#f0315\"} --> ner Kapitalist durch Steigerung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit z. B. Hem-\n den verwohlfeilert, schwebt ihm keineswegs nothwendig der Zweck vor,\n den Werth der Arbeitskraft und daher die nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit pro tanto zu senken, aber nur soweit er schliesslich zu diesem Re-\n sultat beiträgt, trägt er bei zur Erhöhung der allgemeinen Rate des\n Mehrwerths [Note: „Wenn der Fabrikant durch Verbesserung der Maschinerie seine Produkte\n verdoppelt . . . . gewinnt er (schliesslich) bloss, sofern er dadurch befähigt wird,\n den Arbeiter wohlfeiler zu kleiden u. s. w. und so ein kleinerer Theil des Gesammt-\n ertrags auf den Arbeiter fällt.“ (Ramsay l. c. p. 168.)] . Die allgemeinen und nothwendigen Tendenzen des\n Kapitals sind zu unterscheiden von ihren Erscheinungsformen.\n\n\n\n\nDie Darstellung der Art und Weise, wie die immanenten Ge-\n setze der kapitalistischen Produktion in der äussern Bewe-\n gung der Kapitale erscheinen, sich als Zwangsgesetze der Kon-\n kurrenz geltend machen, und daher als treibende Motive dem indivi-\n duellen Kapitalisten zum Bewusstsein kommen, fällt ausserhalb der\n Schranken dieser Schrift. Wissenschaftliche Analyse der Konkurrenz ist\n überhaupt nur möglich, sobald die innere Natur des Kapitals begriffen ist.\n Ganz wie die scheinbare Bewegung der Himmelskörper nur dem verständ-\n lich, der ihre wirkliche, aber sinnlich nicht wahrnehmbare Bewegung\n kennt. Dennoch ist zum Verständniss der Produktion des relativen Mehr-\n werths, und bloss auf Grundlage der bereits gewonnenen Resultate, Folgendes\n zu bemerken.\n\n\n\n\nStellt sich eine Arbeitsstunde in einem Goldquantum von 6 d. oder\n ½sh. dar, so wird in zwölfstündigem Arbeitstag ein Werth von 6 sh. pro-\n ducirt. Gesetzt mit der gegebnen Produktivkraft der Arbeit würden 12 Stück\n Waaren in diesen 12 Arbeitsstunden verfertigt. Der Werth der in jedem Stück\n vernutzten Produktionsmittel, Rohmaterial u. s. w., sei 6 d. Unter diesen\n Umständen kostet die einzelne Waare 1 sh., nämlich 6 d. für den Werth\n der Produktionsmittel, 6 d. für den in ihrer Verarbeitung neu zugesetzten\n Werth. Es gelinge nun einem Kapitalisten die Produktivkraft der Arbeit\n zu verdoppeln und daher 24 Stück dieser Waarenart statt 12 in dem zwölf-\n stündigen Arbeitstag zu produciren. Bei unverändertem Werth der Produk-\n tionsmittel sinkt der Werth der einzelnen Waare jetzt auf 9 d., nämlich 6 d.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"297\", \"facs\": \"#f0316\"} --> für den Werth der Produktionsmittel, 3 d. für den durch die letzte Arbeit\n neu zugesetzten Werth. Trotz der verdoppelten Produktivkraft schafft der\n Arbeitstag nach wie vor nur einen Neuwerth von 6 sh. Aber dieser Werth\n vertheilt sich auf doppelt so viel Produkte. Auf jedes einzelne Produkt\n fällt daher nur noch \\frac{1}{24} dieses Gesammtwerths statt früher \\frac{1}{12}, 3 d.\n statt 6 d., oder, was dasselbe ist, den Produktionsmitteln wird bei ihrer\n Verwandlung in Produkt, jedes Stück berechnet, jetzt nur noch eine halbe\n statt wie früher eine ganze Arbeitsstunde zugesetzt. Der individuelle\n Werth dieser Waare steht nun unter ihrem gesellschaftlichen\n Werth, d. h. sie kostet weniger Arbeitszeit als der grosse Haufen der-\n selben Artikel, producirt unter den gesellschaftlichen Durchschnittsbe-\n dingungen. Das Stück kostet im Durchschnitt 1 sh. oder stellt 2 Stunden\n gesellschaftlicher Arbeit dar; mit der veränderten Produktionsweise kostet\n es nur 9 d. oder enthält nur 1½ Arbeitsstunden. Der wirkliche Werth\n einer Waare ist aber nicht durch ihren individuellen, sondern durch\n ihren gesellschaftlichen Werth bestimmt, d. h. nicht durch die Ar-\n beitszeit, die sie im einzelnen Fall dem Produzenten thatsächlich kostet,\n sondern durch die gesellschaftlich zu ihrer Produktion erheischte Arbeitszeit.\n Verkauft also der Kapitalist, der die neue Methode anwendet, seine Waare\n zu ihrem gesellschaftlichen Werth von 1 sh., so verkauft er sie 3 d. über\n ihrem individuellen Werth und realisirt so einen Extra-Mehrwerth von 3 d.\n Andrerseits stellt sich aber der zwölfstündige Arbeitstag jetzt für ihn in\n 24 Stück Waare dar statt früher in 12. Um also das Produkt eines\n Arbeitstags zu verkaufen, bedarf er doppelten Absatzes oder eines zwei-\n fach grösseren Markts. Unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen erobern\n seine Waaren nur grösseren Marktraum durch Kontraktion ihrer Preise.\n Er wird sie daher über ihrem individuellen, aber unter ihrem gesell-\n schaftlichen Werth verkaufen, sage zu 10 d. das Stück. So schlägt\n er an jedem einzelnen Stück immer noch einen Extra-Mehrwerth von 1 d. her-\n aus. Diese Steigerung des Mehrwerths findet für ihn statt, ob oder ob nicht\n seine Waare dem Umkreis der nothwendigen Lebensmittel angehört und\n daher bestimmend in den allgemeinen Werth der Arbeitskraft eingeht.\n Vom letztren Umstand abgesehn, existirt also für jeden einzelnen Kapita-\n listen das Motiv die Waare durch erhöhte Produktivkraft der Arbeit zu\n verwohlfeilern.\n\n\n\n\nIndess entspringt selbst in diesem Fall die gesteigerte Produktion von\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"298\", \"facs\": \"#f0317\"} --> Mehrwerth aus der Verkürzung der nothwendigen Arbeitszeit und ent-\n sprechender Verlängerung der Mehrarbeit [Note: „A man’s profit does not depend upon his command of the produce of\n other men’s labour, but upon his command of labour itself. If he can\n sell his goods at a higher price, while his workmen’s wages remain unaltered, he\n is clearly benefited … A smaller proportion of what he produces is sufficient to\n put that labour into motion, and a larger proportion consequently remains for\n himself.“ („Outlines of Polit. Econ.“ Lond. 1832, p. 49, 50.)] . Die nothwendige Arbeitszeit\n betrug 10 Stunden oder der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft 5 sh., die Mehr-\n arbeit 2 Stunden, der täglich producirte Mehrwerth daher 1 sh. Unser Kapi-\n talist producirt aber jetzt 24 Stück, die er zu 10 d. per Stück oder zusammen\n zu 20 sh. verkauft. Da der Werth der Produktionsmittel gleich 12 sh.,\n ersetzen 14⅖ Stück Waare nur das vorgeschossene constante Kapital.\n Der zwölfstündige Arbeitstag stellt sich in den übrigbleibenden 9⅗ Stück\n dar. Da der Preis der Arbeitskraft = 5 sh., stellt sich im Produkt von\n 6 Stück die nothwendige Arbeitszeit dar und in 3⅗ Stück die Mehrarbeit.\n Die nothwendige Arbeitszeit beträgt jetzt weniger als ⅔, die Mehrarbeit\n mehr als ⅓ des Arbeitstags, während unter den gesellschaftlichen Durch-\n schnittsbedingungen die nothwendige Arbeit ⅚ und die Mehrarbeit nur\n ⅙ des Arbeitstags einnimmt. Dasselbe Resultat erhält man so: Der\nProduktenwerth des zwölfstündigen Arbeitstags ist 20 sh. Davon\n gehören 12 sh. dem nur wieder erscheinenden Werth der Produktions-\n mittel. Bleiben also 8 sh. als Geldausdruck des Werths, worin sich der\n Arbeitstag darstellt. Dieser Geldausdruck ist höher als der Geldausdruck\n der gesellschaftlichen Durchschnittsarbeit von derselben Sorte, wovon sich\n 12 Stunden nur in 6 sh. ausdrücken. Die Arbeit von ausnahms-\n weiser Produktivkraft wirkt als potenzirte Arbeit oder schafft in\n gleichen Zeiträumen höhere Werthe als die gesellschaftliche Durchschnitts-\n arbeit derselben Art. Aber unser Kapitalist zahlt nach wie vor nur 5 sh.\n für den Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft. Der Arbeiter bedarf daher statt\n früher 10, jetzt weniger als 8 Stunden zur Reproduktion dieses Werths.\n Seine Mehrarbeit wächst daher von 2 Stunden auf mehr als 4, der von\n ihm producirte Mehrwerth von 1 sh. auf 3 sh. 6 d. Der Kapitalist, der\n die verbesserte Produktionsweise anwendet, eignet sich daher einen grös-\n seren Theil des Arbeitstags für Mehrarbeit an, als die übrigen Kapita-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"299\", \"facs\": \"#f0318\"} --> listen in demselben Geschäft. Er thut im Einzelnen, was das Kapital\n bei der Produktion des relativen Mehrwerths im Grossen und Ganzen thut.\n Andrerseits aber verschwindet jener Extra-Mehrwerth, sobald die neue\n Produktionsweise sich verallgemeinert und damit die Differenz zwischen\n dem individuellen Werth der wohlfeiler producirten Waaren und\n ihrem gesellschaftlichen Werth verschwindet. Dasselbe Gesetz\n der Werthbestimmung durch die Arbeitszeit, das dem Kapitalisten mit der\n neuen Methode in der Form fühlbar wird, dass er seine Waare unter\n ihrem gesellschaftlichen Werth verkaufen muss, treibt seine Mitbewerber\n als Zwangsgesetz der Konkurrenz zur Einführung der neuen Produk-\n tionsweise [Note: „If my neighbour by doing much with little labour, can sell cheap, I must\n contrive to sell as cheap as he. So that every art, trade, or engine, doing work\n with labour of fewer hands, and consequently cheaper, begets in others a kind of\n necessity and emulation, either of using the same art, trade, or engine, or of in-\n venting something like it, that every man may be upon the square, that no man\n may be able to undersell his neighbour.“ („The Advantages of the East-\n India Trade to England. Lond. 1720“, p. 67.)] . Die allgemeine Rate des Mehrwerths wird also\n durch den ganzen Prozess schliesslich nur berührt, wenn die Erhöhung\n der Produktivkraft der Arbeit Produktionszweige ergriffen, also Waaren\n verwohlfeilert hat, die in den Kreis der nothwendigen Lebensmittel ein-\n gehn, daher Elemente des Werths der Arbeitskraft bilden.\n\n\n\n\nDer Werth der Waaren steht in umgekehrtem Verhält-\n niss zur Produktivkraft der Arbeit. Ebenso, weil durch\n Waarenwerthe bestimmt, der Werth der Arbeitskraft. Dagegen\n steht der relative Mehrwerth in direktem Verhältniss zur\n Produktivkraft der Arbeit. Er steigt mit steigender und fällt mit fallender\n Produktivkraft. Ein gesellschaftlicher Durchschnittsarbeitstag von 12\n Stunden, Geldwerth als gleichbleibend vorausgesetzt, producirt stets das-\n selbe Werthprodukt von 6 sh., wie diese Werthsumme sich immer vertheile\n zwischen Aequivalent für den Werth der Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth.\n Fällt aber in Folge gesteigerter Produktivkraft der Werth der täglichen\n Lebensmittel und daher der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft von 5 sh. auf\n 3 sh., so wächst der Mehrwerth von 1 sh. auf 3 sh. Um den Werth der\n Arbeitskraft zu reproduciren, waren 10 und sind jetzt nur noch 6 Ar-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"300\", \"facs\": \"#f0319\"} --> beitsstunden nöthig. Vier Arbeitsstunden sind frei geworden und können\n der Domäne der Mehrarbeit annexirt werden. Es ist daher der imma-\n nente Trieb und die beständige Tendenz des Kapitals die Produktivkraft\n der Arbeit zu steigern, um die Waare und durch die Verwohlfeile-\n rung der Waare den Arbeiter selbst zu verwohlfeilern [Note: „In whatever proportion the expences of a labourer are diminished, in the\n same proportion will his wages be diminished, if the restraints upon industry are\n at the same time taken off.“ („Considerations concerning taking off\n the Bounty on Corn exported etc. Lond. 1752“, p. 7.) „The\n interest of trade requires, that corn and all provisions should be as cheap as\n possible; for whatever makes them dear, must make labour dear also … in all\n countries, where industry is not restrained, the price of provisions must affect the\n Price of Labour. This will always be diminished when the necessaries of life\n grow cheaper.“ (l. c. p. 3.) „Wages are decreased in the same proportion\n as the powers of production increase. Machinery, it is true, cheapens\n the necessaries of life, but it also cheapens the labourer too.“ („A\n Prize Essay on the comparative merits of Competition and Co-\n operation. Lond. 1834“, p. 27.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDer absolute Werth der Waare ist dem Kapitalisten, der sie\n producirt, an und für sich gleichgültig. Ihn interessirt nur der in ihr\n steckende und im Verkauf realisirbare Mehrwerth. Realisirung von Mehr-\n werth schliesst von selbst Ersatz des vorgeschossenen Werths ein. Da\n nun der relative Mehrwerth in direktem Verhältniss zur Entwicklung der\n Produktivkraft der Arbeit wächst, während der Werth der Waaren in um-\n gekehrtem Verhältniss zur selben Entwicklung fällt, da also derselbe iden-\n tische Prozess die Waaren verwohlfeilert und den in ihnen enthaltenen\n Mehrwerth steigert, löst sich das Räthsel, dass der Kapitalist, dem es nur\n um die Produktion von Tauschwerth zu thun ist, den Tauschwerth der Waa-\n ren beständig zu senken strebt, ein Widerspruch, womit einer der Gründer\n der politischen Oekonomie, Dr. Quesnay, seine Gegner quälte und wor-\n auf sie ihm die Antwort schuldig blieben. „Ihr gebt zu“, sagt Quesnay,\n „dass je mehr man, ohne Nachtheil für die Produktion, Kosten oder kost-\n spielige Arbeiten in der Fabrikation industrieller Produkte ersparen kann,\n desto vortheilhafter diese Ersparung, weil sie den Preis des Machwerks\n vermindert. Und trotzdem glaubt ihr, dass die Produktion des Reich-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"301\", \"facs\": \"#f0320\"} --> thums, der aus den Arbeiten der Industriellen resultirt, in der Vermehrung\n des Tauschwerths ihres Machwerks besteht“ [Note: „Ils conviennent que plus on peut, sans préjudice, épargner de frais ou de\n travaux dispendieux dans la fabrication des ouvrages des artisans, plus cette épargne\n est profitable par la diminution des prix de ces ouvrages. Cependant ils croient\n que la production de richesse qui résulte des travaux des artisans consiste dans\n l’augmentation de la valeur vénale de leurs ouvrages.“ (Quesnay: „Dia-\n logues sur le Commerce et sur les Travaux des Artisans“, p. 188,\n 189.)] .\n\n\n\n\nOekonomie der Arbeit durch Entwicklung der Produktivkraft der\n Arbeit [Note: „Ces spéculateurs si économes du travail des ouvriers qu’il faudrait qu’ils\n payassent.“ (J. N. Bidault: „Du Monopole qui s’établit dans les\n arts industrielles et le commerce. Paris 1828“, p. 13.) „The\n employer will be always on the stretch to economise time and labour.“ (Dugald\n Stewart: Works ed. by Sir W. Hamilton. Edinburgh, v. III, 1855,\n „Lectures on Polit. Econ.“, p. 318.) „Their (the capitalists’) interest is\n that the productive powers of the labourers they employ should be the greatest\n possible. On promoting that power their attention is fixed and almost exclusively\n fixed.“ (R. Jones l. c. Lecture IlI.)]  bezweckt in der kapitalistischen Produktion also durchaus nicht\nVerkürzung des Arbeitstags. Sie bezweckt nur Verkürzung der für Pro-\n duktion eines bestimmten Waarenquantums nothwendigen Arbeits-\n zeit. Dass der Arbeiter bei gesteigerter Produktivkraft seiner Arbeit in einer\n Stunde z. B. 10 mal mehr Waare als früher producirt, also für jedes Stück\n Waare 10 mal weniger Arbeitszeit braucht, verhindert durchaus nicht, ihn nach\n wie vor 12 Stunden arbeiten und in den 12 Stunden 1200 statt früher\n 120 Stück produciren zu lassen. Ja sein Arbeitstag mag gleichzeitig\n verlängert werden, so dass er jetzt in 14 Stunden 1400 Stück producirt\n u. s. w. Man kann daher bei Oekonomen vom Schlag eines Mac Cul-\n loch, Ure, Senior und tutti quanti auf einer Seite lesen, dass der Arbeiter\n dem Kapital für die Entwicklung der Produktivkräfte Dank schuldet, weil sie\n die nothwendige Arbeitszeit verkürzt, und auf der nächsten Seite, dass\n er diesen Dank beweisen muss, indem er statt 10 künftig 15 Stunden\n arbeitet. Die Entwicklung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit, innerhalb der\n kapitalistischen Produktion, bezweckt den Theil des Arbeitstags,\n den der Arbeiter für sich selbst arbeiten muss, zu verkürzen,\n um grade dadurch den andern Theil des Arbeitstags, den er\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"302\", \"facs\": \"#f0321\"} -->für den Kapitalisten umsonst arbeiten kann, zu verlän-\n gern. Wie weit diess Resultat auch ohne Verwohlfeilerung der Waaren\n erreichbar, wird sich zeigen in den besondern Produktionsmetho-\n den des relativen Mehrwerths, zu deren Betrachtung wir jetzt\n übergehn.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0001","text":"## Viertes Kapitel.\n Die Produktion des relativen Mehrwerths.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"11.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0002","text":"## 1) Begriff des relativen Mehrwerths.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"11.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0003","text":"Der Theil des Arbeitstags, der bloss ein Aequivalent für den\n vom Kapital gezahlten Werth der Arbeitskraft produzirt, galt uns bisher\n als constante Grösse, was er in der That ist unter gegebnen Pro-\n duktionsbedingungen, auf einer vorhandnen ökonomischen Entwicklungs-\n stufe der Gesellschaft. Ueber diese seine nothwendige Arbeitszeit\n hinaus konnte der Arbeiter 2, 3, 4, 6 u. s. w. Stunden arbeiten. Von\n der Grösse dieser Verlängerung hingen Rate des Mehr-\n werths und Grösse des Arbeitstags ab. War die nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit constant, so dagegen der Gesammtarbeitstag variabel. Unter-\n stelle jetzt einen Arbeitstag, dessen Grösse und dessen Theilung\n in nothwendige Arbeit und Mehrarbeit gegeben sind. Die Linie a c\n a-------------b---c stelle z. B. einen zwölfstündigen Arbeitstag vor,\n das Stück a b 10 Stunden nothwendige Arbeit, das Stück b c 2 Stunden\n Mehrarbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"11.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0004","text":"Wie kann nun die Produktion von Mehrwerth vergrössert,\n d. h. die Mehrarbeit verlängert werden, ohne jede weitere Verlängerung\n oder unabhängig von jeder weiteren Verlängerung von a c?","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"11.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0005","text":"[Running matter: 19*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"292\", \"facs\": \"#f0311\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"11.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0006","text":"Trotz gegebner Grenzen des Arbeitstags a c scheint b c verlängerbar,\n wenn nicht durch Ausdehnung über seinen Endpunkt c, der zugleich der\n Endpunkt des Arbeitstags a c ist, so durch Verschiebung seines Anfangs-\n punkts b in entgegengesetzter Richtung nach a hin. Nimm an, b' b\n a-------------b'--b----c sei gleich der Hälfte von b c oder gleich einer\n Arbeitsstunde. Wird nun in dem zwölfstündigen Arbeitstag a c der Punkt\n b nach b' verrückt, so dehnt sich b c aus zu b' c, die Mehrarbeit wächst\n um die Hälfte, von 2 auf 3 Stunden, obgleich der Arbeitstag nach wie vor\n nur 12 Stunden zählt. Diese Ausdehnung der Mehrarbeit von b c auf\n b' c, von 2 auf 3 Stunden, ist aber offenbar unmöglich ohne gleichzeitige\n Zusammenziehung der nothwendigen Arbeit von a b auf a b', von 10 auf\n 9 Stunden.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"11.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0007","text":"Der Verlängerung der Mehrarbeit entspräche die\nVerkürzung der nothwendigen Arbeit, oder ein Theil der\n Arbeitszeit, die der Arbeiter bisher in der That für sich selbst verbraucht,\n verwandelt sich in Arbeitszeit für den Kapitalisten. Was verändert,\n wäre nicht die Länge des Arbeitstags, sondern seine Theilung\n in nothwendige Arbeit und Mehrarbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"11.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0008","text":"Andrerseits ist die Grösse der Mehrarbeit offenbar selbst ge-\n geben mit gegebner Grösse des Arbeitstags und gegebnem\n Werth der Arbeitskraft. Der Werth der Arbeitskraft, d. h. die zu ihrer\n Produktion erheischte Arbeitszeit, bestimmt die zur Reproduktion ihres\n Werths nothwendige Arbeitszeit. Stellt sich eine Arbeitsstunde in einem\n Goldquantum von einem halben Shilling oder 6 d. dar, und beträgt der\n Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft 5 sh., so muss der Arbeiter täglich 10 Stun-\n den arbeiten, um den ihm vom Kapital gezahlten Tageswerth seiner Arbeits-\n kraft zu ersetzen oder ein Aequivalent für den Werth seiner nothwendigen\n täglichen Lebensmittel zu produziren.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"11.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0009","text":"Mit dem Werth dieser Lebensmittel\n ist der Werth seiner Arbeitskraft [Note: Der Werth des täglichen Durchschnittslohns ist bestimmt durch das, was\n der Arbeiter braucht „so as to live, labeur, and generate.“ (William Petty:\n „Political Anatomy of Ireland“ 1672, p. 64.) „The Price of Labour\n is always constituted of the price of necessaries.“ Der Arbeiter erhält nicht den\n entsprechenden Lohn „whenever . . . . the labouring man’s wages will not,\nsuitably to his low rank and station, as a labouring man, support such a family\n as is often the lot of many of them to have.“ (J. Vanderlint l. c. p. 19.)\n „Le simple ouvrier, qui n’a que ses bras et son industrie, n’a rien qu’autant qu’il] , mit dem Werth seiner Arbeitskraft ist\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"293\", \"facs\": \"#f0312\"} --> die Grösse seiner nothwendigen Arbeitszeit gegeben.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"11.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0010","text":"Die\nGrösse der Mehrarbeit aber wird erhalten durch Subtraktion\n der nothwendigen Arbeitszeit vom Gesammtarbeitstag.\n Zehn Stunden subtrahirt von zwölf lassen zwei, und es ist nicht abzusehn,\n wie die Mehrarbeit unter den gegebnen Bedingungen über zwei Stunden\n hinaus verlängert werden kann. Allerdings mag der Kapitalist statt 5 sh.\n dem Arbeiter nur 4 sh. 6 d. oder noch weniger zahlen. Zur Reproduktion\n dieses Werths von 4 sh. 6 d. würden 9 Arbeitsstunden genügen, von dem\n zwölfstündigen Arbeitstag daher 3 statt 2 Stunden der Mehrarbeit anheim-\n fallen und der Mehrwerth selbst von 1 sh. auf 1 sh. 6 d. steigen. Diess\n Resultat wäre jedoch nur erzielt durch Herabdrückung des Lohns\n des Arbeiters unter den Werth seiner Arbeitskraft. Mit\n den 4 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"11.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0011","text":"6 d., die er in 9 Stunden producirt, verfügt er über ⅒ weniger\n Lebensmittel als vorher und so findet nur eine verkümmerte Reproduk-\n tion seiner Arbeitskraft statt. Die Mehrarbeit würde hier nur verlängert\n durch Ueberschreitung ihrer normalen Grenzen, ihre Domäne nur ausge-\n dehnt durch usurpatorischen Abbruch von der Domäne der nothwendigen\n Arbeitszeit. Trotz der wichtigen Rolle, welche diese Methode in der wirk-\n lichen Bewegung des Arbeitslohnes spielt, ist sie hier ausgeschlossen durch\n die Voraussetzung, dass die Waaren, also auch die Arbeitskraft, zu ihrem\n vollen Werth gekauft und verkauft werden. Diess einmal unterstellt, kann\n die zur Produktion der Arbeitskraft oder zur Reproduktion ihres Werths noth-\n wendige Arbeitszeit nicht abnehmen, weil der Lohn des Arbeiters\nunter den Werth seiner Arbeitskraft, sondern nur weil dieser Werth\n selbst sinkt.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"11.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0012","text":"Bei gegebner Länge des Arbeitstags muss die Verlänge-\n rung der Mehrarbeit aus der Verkürzung der nothwendigen Arbeitszeit ent-\n springen, nicht umgekehrt die Verkürzung der nothwendigen Arbeitszeit\n aus der Verlängerung der Mehrarbeit. In unsrem Beispiel muss der\n Werth der Arbeitskraft wirklich um ⅒ sinken, damit die nothwendige\n [Note: parvient à vendre à d’autres sa peine … En tout genre de travail il doit arriver\n et il arrive en effet, que le salaire de l’ouvrier se borne à ce qui lui est nécessaire\n pour lui procurer la subsistance.“ (Turgot: „Réflexions sur la For-\n mation et la Distribution des Richesses“ (1766). Oeuvres ed.\n Daire, t. I, p. 10.) „The price of the necessaries of life is, in fact, the cost\n of producing labour.“ (Malthus: „Inquiry into etc. Rent.“ Lond.\n 1815, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"11.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0013","text":"48 Note.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"294\", \"facs\": \"#f0313\"} --> Arbeitszeit um ⅒ abnehme, von 10 auf 9 Stunden, und daher die\n Mehrarbeit sich von 2 auf 3 Stunden verlängere.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"11.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0014","text":"Eine solche Senkung des Werths der Arbeitskraft um ⅒ bedingt\n aber ihrerseits, dass dieselbe Masse Lebensmittel, die früher in\n 10, jetzt in 9 Stunden producirt wird. Diess ist jedoch unmöglich\n ohne eine Erhöhung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit. Mit\n gegebnen Mitteln kann ein Schuster z. B. ein Paar Stiefel in einem Ar-\n beitstag von 12 Stunden machen. Soll er in derselben Zeit zwei Paar\n Stiefel machen, so muss sich die Produktivkraft seiner Arbeit verdoppeln,\n und sie kann sich nicht verdoppeln ohne eine Aenderung in seinen Ar-\n beitsmitteln oder seiner Arbeitsmethode oder beiden zugleich. Es muss\n daher eine Revolution in den Produktionsbedingungen seiner\n Arbeit, d. h. in seiner Produktionsweise und daher im Arbeitsprozess\n selbst eintreten.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"11.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0015","text":"Unter Erhöhung der Produktivkraft der Ar-\n beit verstehn wir hier überhaupt eine Veränderung im Arbeits-\n prozess, wodurch die zur Produktion einer Waare gesellschaftlich er-\n heischte Arbeitszeit verkürzt wird, ein kleineres Quantum Arbeit also die\nKraft erwirbt ein grösseres Quantum Gebrauchswerth zu produciren [Note: „Quando si perfezionano le arti, che no è altero che la scoperta di nuove\n vie, onde si possa compiere una manufattura con meno gente o (che è lo\n stesso) in minor tempo di prima.“ Galiani l. c. p. 159. „L’économie\n sur les frais de production ne peut être autre chose que l’économie sur la quan-\n tité de travail employé pour produire.“ Sismondi: Études etc. t. I. p. 22.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"11.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0016","text":"Während also bei der Produktion des Mehrwerths in der bisher betrach-\n teten Form die Produktionsweise als gegeben unterstellt war, ge-\n nügt es für die Produktion von Mehrwerth durch Verwandlung noth-\n wendiger Arbeit in Mehrarbeit keineswegs, dass das Kapital sich des Ar-\n beitsprozesses in seiner historisch überlieferten oder vorhandenen\n Gestalt bemächtigt und nur seine Dauer verlängert. Es muss die tech-\n nologischen und gesellschaftlichen Bedingungen des Arbeitsprozesses, also\n die Produktionsweise selbst umwälzen, um die Produktivkraft\n der Arbeit zu erhöhn, durch die Erhöhung der Produktivkraft der Ar-\n beit den Werth der Arbeitskraft zu senken und so den zur Repro-\n duktion dieses Werths nothwendigen Theil des Arbeitstages zu ver-\n kürzen.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"11.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0017","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"295\", \"facs\": \"#f0314\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"11.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0018","text":"Durch Verlängerung des Arbeitstags producirten Mehrwerth\n nenne ich absoluten Mehrwerth; den Mehrwerth dagegen, der aus\nVerkürzung der nothwendigen Arbeitszeit und entsprechender Ver-\n änderung im Grössenverhältniss der beiden Bestandtheile des Ar-\n beitstags entspringt, — relativen Mehrwerth.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"11.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0019","text":"Um den Werth der Arbeitskraft zu senken, muss die Steige-\n rung der Produktivkraft Industriezweige ergreifen, deren Pro-\n dukte den Werth der Arbeitskraft bestimmen, also entweder dem Umkreis\n der gewohnheitsmässigen Lebensmittel angehören oder sie ersetzen können.\n Der Werth einer Waare ist aber nicht nur bestimmt durch das Quantum der\n Arbeit, welche ihr die letzte Form giebt, sondern ebensowohl durch die in\n ihren Produktionsmitteln enthaltene Arbeitsmasse. Z. B. der Werth\n eines Stiefels nicht nur durch die Schusterarbeit, sondern auch durch den\n Werth von Leder, Pech, Draht u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"11.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0020","text":"Steigerung der Produktivkraft und\n entsprechende Verwohlfeilerung der Waaren in den Industrien,\n welche die stofflichen Elemente des constanten Kapitals, die Arbeits-\n mittel und das Arbeitsmaterial, zur Erzeugung der nothwendigen Lebens-\n mittel liefern, senken also ebenfalls den Werth der Arbeitskraft. In Pro-\n duktionszweigen dagegen, die weder nothwendige Lebensmittel liefern, noch\n Produktionsmittel zu ihrer Herstellung, lässt die erhöhte Produktivkraft\n den Werth der Arbeitskraft unberührt.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"11.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0021","text":"Die verwohlfeilerte Waare senkt natürlich den Werth der Arbeits-\n kraft nur pro tanto, d. h. nur im Verhältniss, worin sie in die Repro-\n duktion der Arbeitskraft eingeht. Hemden z. B. sind ein nothwendiges\n Lebensmittel, aber nur eins von vielen. Ihre Verwohlfeilerung vermin-\n dert nur die Ausgabe des Arbeiters für Hemden. Die Gesammtsumme\n der nothwendigen Lebensmittel besteht zwar nur aus einzelnen Waaren,\n lauter Produkten besondrer Industrien, und der Werth jeder besondern\n Waare bildet immer nur einen aliquoten Theil vom Werth der Arbeitskraft.\n Die Gesammtabnahme dieses Werths, und daher der zu seiner Reproduk-\n tion nothwendigen Arbeitszeit, ist jedoch gleich der Summe der Verkür-\n zungen der nothwendigen Arbeitszeit in allen jenen besondren Produktions-\n zweigen.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"11.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0022","text":"Wir behandeln diess allgemeine Resultat hier so, als wäre es\nunmittelbares Resultat und unmittelbarer Zweck in jedem\n einzelnen Fall, obgleich die Sache anders erscheint. Wenn ein einzel-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"296\", \"facs\": \"#f0315\"} --> ner Kapitalist durch Steigerung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit z. B. Hem-\n den verwohlfeilert, schwebt ihm keineswegs nothwendig der Zweck vor,\n den Werth der Arbeitskraft und daher die nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit pro tanto zu senken, aber nur soweit er schliesslich zu diesem Re-\n sultat beiträgt, trägt er bei zur Erhöhung der allgemeinen Rate des\n Mehrwerths [Note: „Wenn der Fabrikant durch Verbesserung der Maschinerie seine Produkte\n verdoppelt . . . . gewinnt er (schliesslich) bloss, sofern er dadurch befähigt wird,\n den Arbeiter wohlfeiler zu kleiden u. s. w. und so ein kleinerer Theil des Gesammt-\n ertrags auf den Arbeiter fällt.“ (Ramsay l. c.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"11.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0023","text":"p. 168.)] . Die allgemeinen und nothwendigen Tendenzen des\n Kapitals sind zu unterscheiden von ihren Erscheinungsformen.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"11.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0024","text":"Die Darstellung der Art und Weise, wie die immanenten Ge-\n setze der kapitalistischen Produktion in der äussern Bewe-\n gung der Kapitale erscheinen, sich als Zwangsgesetze der Kon-\n kurrenz geltend machen, und daher als treibende Motive dem indivi-\n duellen Kapitalisten zum Bewusstsein kommen, fällt ausserhalb der\n Schranken dieser Schrift. Wissenschaftliche Analyse der Konkurrenz ist\n überhaupt nur möglich, sobald die innere Natur des Kapitals begriffen ist.\n Ganz wie die scheinbare Bewegung der Himmelskörper nur dem verständ-\n lich, der ihre wirkliche, aber sinnlich nicht wahrnehmbare Bewegung\n kennt. Dennoch ist zum Verständniss der Produktion des relativen Mehr-\n werths, und bloss auf Grundlage der bereits gewonnenen Resultate, Folgendes\n zu bemerken.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"11.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0025","text":"Stellt sich eine Arbeitsstunde in einem Goldquantum von 6 d. oder\n ½sh. dar, so wird in zwölfstündigem Arbeitstag ein Werth von 6 sh. pro-\n ducirt. Gesetzt mit der gegebnen Produktivkraft der Arbeit würden 12 Stück\n Waaren in diesen 12 Arbeitsstunden verfertigt. Der Werth der in jedem Stück\n vernutzten Produktionsmittel, Rohmaterial u. s. w., sei 6 d. Unter diesen\n Umständen kostet die einzelne Waare 1 sh., nämlich 6 d. für den Werth\n der Produktionsmittel, 6 d. für den in ihrer Verarbeitung neu zugesetzten\n Werth. Es gelinge nun einem Kapitalisten die Produktivkraft der Arbeit\n zu verdoppeln und daher 24 Stück dieser Waarenart statt 12 in dem zwölf-\n stündigen Arbeitstag zu produciren. Bei unverändertem Werth der Produk-\n tionsmittel sinkt der Werth der einzelnen Waare jetzt auf 9 d., nämlich 6 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"11.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0026","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"297\", \"facs\": \"#f0316\"} --> für den Werth der Produktionsmittel, 3 d. für den durch die letzte Arbeit\n neu zugesetzten Werth. Trotz der verdoppelten Produktivkraft schafft der\n Arbeitstag nach wie vor nur einen Neuwerth von 6 sh. Aber dieser Werth\n vertheilt sich auf doppelt so viel Produkte. Auf jedes einzelne Produkt\n fällt daher nur noch \\frac{1}{24} dieses Gesammtwerths statt früher \\frac{1}{12}, 3 d.\n statt 6 d., oder, was dasselbe ist, den Produktionsmitteln wird bei ihrer\n Verwandlung in Produkt, jedes Stück berechnet, jetzt nur noch eine halbe\n statt wie früher eine ganze Arbeitsstunde zugesetzt. Der individuelle\n Werth dieser Waare steht nun unter ihrem gesellschaftlichen\n Werth, d. h. sie kostet weniger Arbeitszeit als der grosse Haufen der-\n selben Artikel, producirt unter den gesellschaftlichen Durchschnittsbe-\n dingungen.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"11.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0027","text":"Das Stück kostet im Durchschnitt 1 sh. oder stellt 2 Stunden\n gesellschaftlicher Arbeit dar; mit der veränderten Produktionsweise kostet\n es nur 9 d. oder enthält nur 1½ Arbeitsstunden. Der wirkliche Werth\n einer Waare ist aber nicht durch ihren individuellen, sondern durch\n ihren gesellschaftlichen Werth bestimmt, d. h. nicht durch die Ar-\n beitszeit, die sie im einzelnen Fall dem Produzenten thatsächlich kostet,\n sondern durch die gesellschaftlich zu ihrer Produktion erheischte Arbeitszeit.\n Verkauft also der Kapitalist, der die neue Methode anwendet, seine Waare\n zu ihrem gesellschaftlichen Werth von 1 sh., so verkauft er sie 3 d. über\n ihrem individuellen Werth und realisirt so einen Extra-Mehrwerth von 3 d.\n Andrerseits stellt sich aber der zwölfstündige Arbeitstag jetzt für ihn in\n 24 Stück Waare dar statt früher in 12.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"11.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0028","text":"Um also das Produkt eines\n Arbeitstags zu verkaufen, bedarf er doppelten Absatzes oder eines zwei-\n fach grösseren Markts. Unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen erobern\n seine Waaren nur grösseren Marktraum durch Kontraktion ihrer Preise.\n Er wird sie daher über ihrem individuellen, aber unter ihrem gesell-\n schaftlichen Werth verkaufen, sage zu 10 d. das Stück. So schlägt\n er an jedem einzelnen Stück immer noch einen Extra-Mehrwerth von 1 d. her-\n aus. Diese Steigerung des Mehrwerths findet für ihn statt, ob oder ob nicht\n seine Waare dem Umkreis der nothwendigen Lebensmittel angehört und\n daher bestimmend in den allgemeinen Werth der Arbeitskraft eingeht.\n Vom letztren Umstand abgesehn, existirt also für jeden einzelnen Kapita-\n listen das Motiv die Waare durch erhöhte Produktivkraft der Arbeit zu\n verwohlfeilern.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"11.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0029","text":"Indess entspringt selbst in diesem Fall die gesteigerte Produktion von\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"298\", \"facs\": \"#f0317\"} --> Mehrwerth aus der Verkürzung der nothwendigen Arbeitszeit und ent-\n sprechender Verlängerung der Mehrarbeit [Note: „A man’s profit does not depend upon his command of the produce of\n other men’s labour, but upon his command of labour itself. If he can\n sell his goods at a higher price, while his workmen’s wages remain unaltered, he\n is clearly benefited … A smaller proportion of what he produces is sufficient to\n put that labour into motion, and a larger proportion consequently remains for\n himself.“ („Outlines of Polit. Econ.“ Lond. 1832, p. 49, 50.)] . Die nothwendige Arbeitszeit\n betrug 10 Stunden oder der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft 5 sh., die Mehr-\n arbeit 2 Stunden, der täglich producirte Mehrwerth daher 1 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"11.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0030","text":"Unser Kapi-\n talist producirt aber jetzt 24 Stück, die er zu 10 d. per Stück oder zusammen\n zu 20 sh. verkauft. Da der Werth der Produktionsmittel gleich 12 sh.,\n ersetzen 14⅖ Stück Waare nur das vorgeschossene constante Kapital.\n Der zwölfstündige Arbeitstag stellt sich in den übrigbleibenden 9⅗ Stück\n dar. Da der Preis der Arbeitskraft = 5 sh., stellt sich im Produkt von\n 6 Stück die nothwendige Arbeitszeit dar und in 3⅗ Stück die Mehrarbeit.\n Die nothwendige Arbeitszeit beträgt jetzt weniger als ⅔, die Mehrarbeit\n mehr als ⅓ des Arbeitstags, während unter den gesellschaftlichen Durch-\n schnittsbedingungen die nothwendige Arbeit ⅚ und die Mehrarbeit nur\n ⅙ des Arbeitstags einnimmt. Dasselbe Resultat erhält man so: Der\nProduktenwerth des zwölfstündigen Arbeitstags ist 20 sh. Davon\n gehören 12 sh. dem nur wieder erscheinenden Werth der Produktions-\n mittel. Bleiben also 8 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"11.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0031","text":"als Geldausdruck des Werths, worin sich der\n Arbeitstag darstellt. Dieser Geldausdruck ist höher als der Geldausdruck\n der gesellschaftlichen Durchschnittsarbeit von derselben Sorte, wovon sich\n 12 Stunden nur in 6 sh. ausdrücken. Die Arbeit von ausnahms-\n weiser Produktivkraft wirkt als potenzirte Arbeit oder schafft in\n gleichen Zeiträumen höhere Werthe als die gesellschaftliche Durchschnitts-\n arbeit derselben Art. Aber unser Kapitalist zahlt nach wie vor nur 5 sh.\n für den Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft. Der Arbeiter bedarf daher statt\n früher 10, jetzt weniger als 8 Stunden zur Reproduktion dieses Werths.\n Seine Mehrarbeit wächst daher von 2 Stunden auf mehr als 4, der von\n ihm producirte Mehrwerth von 1 sh. auf 3 sh. 6 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"11.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0032","text":"Der Kapitalist, der\n die verbesserte Produktionsweise anwendet, eignet sich daher einen grös-\n seren Theil des Arbeitstags für Mehrarbeit an, als die übrigen Kapita-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"299\", \"facs\": \"#f0318\"} --> listen in demselben Geschäft. Er thut im Einzelnen, was das Kapital\n bei der Produktion des relativen Mehrwerths im Grossen und Ganzen thut.\n Andrerseits aber verschwindet jener Extra-Mehrwerth, sobald die neue\n Produktionsweise sich verallgemeinert und damit die Differenz zwischen\n dem individuellen Werth der wohlfeiler producirten Waaren und\n ihrem gesellschaftlichen Werth verschwindet.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"11.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0033","text":"Dasselbe Gesetz\n der Werthbestimmung durch die Arbeitszeit, das dem Kapitalisten mit der\n neuen Methode in der Form fühlbar wird, dass er seine Waare unter\n ihrem gesellschaftlichen Werth verkaufen muss, treibt seine Mitbewerber\n als Zwangsgesetz der Konkurrenz zur Einführung der neuen Produk-\n tionsweise [Note: „If my neighbour by doing much with little labour, can sell cheap, I must\n contrive to sell as cheap as he. So that every art, trade, or engine, doing work\n with labour of fewer hands, and consequently cheaper, begets in others a kind of\n necessity and emulation, either of using the same art, trade, or engine, or of in-\n venting something like it, that every man may be upon the square, that no man\n may be able to undersell his neighbour.“ („The Advantages of the East-\n India Trade to England. Lond. 1720“, p. 67.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"11.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0034","text":"Die allgemeine Rate des Mehrwerths wird also\n durch den ganzen Prozess schliesslich nur berührt, wenn die Erhöhung\n der Produktivkraft der Arbeit Produktionszweige ergriffen, also Waaren\n verwohlfeilert hat, die in den Kreis der nothwendigen Lebensmittel ein-\n gehn, daher Elemente des Werths der Arbeitskraft bilden.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"11.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0035","text":"Der Werth der Waaren steht in umgekehrtem Verhält-\n niss zur Produktivkraft der Arbeit. Ebenso, weil durch\n Waarenwerthe bestimmt, der Werth der Arbeitskraft. Dagegen\n steht der relative Mehrwerth in direktem Verhältniss zur\n Produktivkraft der Arbeit. Er steigt mit steigender und fällt mit fallender\n Produktivkraft. Ein gesellschaftlicher Durchschnittsarbeitstag von 12\n Stunden, Geldwerth als gleichbleibend vorausgesetzt, producirt stets das-\n selbe Werthprodukt von 6 sh., wie diese Werthsumme sich immer vertheile\n zwischen Aequivalent für den Werth der Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth.\n Fällt aber in Folge gesteigerter Produktivkraft der Werth der täglichen\n Lebensmittel und daher der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft von 5 sh. auf\n 3 sh., so wächst der Mehrwerth von 1 sh. auf 3 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"11.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0036","text":"Um den Werth der\n Arbeitskraft zu reproduciren, waren 10 und sind jetzt nur noch 6 Ar-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"300\", \"facs\": \"#f0319\"} --> beitsstunden nöthig. Vier Arbeitsstunden sind frei geworden und können\n der Domäne der Mehrarbeit annexirt werden. Es ist daher der imma-\n nente Trieb und die beständige Tendenz des Kapitals die Produktivkraft\n der Arbeit zu steigern, um die Waare und durch die Verwohlfeile-\n rung der Waare den Arbeiter selbst zu verwohlfeilern [Note: „In whatever proportion the expences of a labourer are diminished, in the\n same proportion will his wages be diminished, if the restraints upon industry are\n at the same time taken off.“ („Considerations concerning taking off\n the Bounty on Corn exported etc. Lond. 1752“, p. 7.)","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"11.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0037","text":"„The\n interest of trade requires, that corn and all provisions should be as cheap as\n possible; for whatever makes them dear, must make labour dear also … in all\n countries, where industry is not restrained, the price of provisions must affect the\n Price of Labour. This will always be diminished when the necessaries of life\n grow cheaper.“ (l. c. p. 3.) „Wages are decreased in the same proportion\n as the powers of production increase. Machinery, it is true, cheapens\n the necessaries of life, but it also cheapens the labourer too.“ („A\n Prize Essay on the comparative merits of Competition and Co-\n operation. Lond. 1834“, p. 27.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"11.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0038","text":"Der absolute Werth der Waare ist dem Kapitalisten, der sie\n producirt, an und für sich gleichgültig. Ihn interessirt nur der in ihr\n steckende und im Verkauf realisirbare Mehrwerth. Realisirung von Mehr-\n werth schliesst von selbst Ersatz des vorgeschossenen Werths ein. Da\n nun der relative Mehrwerth in direktem Verhältniss zur Entwicklung der\n Produktivkraft der Arbeit wächst, während der Werth der Waaren in um-\n gekehrtem Verhältniss zur selben Entwicklung fällt, da also derselbe iden-\n tische Prozess die Waaren verwohlfeilert und den in ihnen enthaltenen\n Mehrwerth steigert, löst sich das Räthsel, dass der Kapitalist, dem es nur\n um die Produktion von Tauschwerth zu thun ist, den Tauschwerth der Waa-\n ren beständig zu senken strebt, ein Widerspruch, womit einer der Gründer\n der politischen Oekonomie, Dr. Quesnay, seine Gegner quälte und wor-\n auf sie ihm die Antwort schuldig blieben.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"11.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0039","text":"„Ihr gebt zu“, sagt Quesnay,\n „dass je mehr man, ohne Nachtheil für die Produktion, Kosten oder kost-\n spielige Arbeiten in der Fabrikation industrieller Produkte ersparen kann,\n desto vortheilhafter diese Ersparung, weil sie den Preis des Machwerks\n vermindert. Und trotzdem glaubt ihr, dass die Produktion des Reich-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"301\", \"facs\": \"#f0320\"} --> thums, der aus den Arbeiten der Industriellen resultirt, in der Vermehrung\n des Tauschwerths ihres Machwerks besteht“ [Note: „Ils conviennent que plus on peut, sans préjudice, épargner de frais ou de\n travaux dispendieux dans la fabrication des ouvrages des artisans, plus cette épargne\n est profitable par la diminution des prix de ces ouvrages.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"11.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0040","text":"Cependant ils croient\n que la production de richesse qui résulte des travaux des artisans consiste dans\n l’augmentation de la valeur vénale de leurs ouvrages.“ (Quesnay: „Dia-\n logues sur le Commerce et sur les Travaux des Artisans“, p. 188,\n 189.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"11.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0041","text":"Oekonomie der Arbeit durch Entwicklung der Produktivkraft der\n Arbeit [Note: „Ces spéculateurs si économes du travail des ouvriers qu’il faudrait qu’ils\n payassent.“ (J. N. Bidault: „Du Monopole qui s’établit dans les\n arts industrielles et le commerce. Paris 1828“, p. 13.) „The\n employer will be always on the stretch to economise time and labour.“ (Dugald\n Stewart: Works ed. by Sir W. Hamilton. Edinburgh, v. III, 1855,\n „Lectures on Polit. Econ.“, p. 318.) „Their (the capitalists’) interest is\n that the productive powers of the labourers they employ should be the greatest\n possible. On promoting that power their attention is fixed and almost exclusively\n fixed.“ (R. Jones l. c. Lecture IlI.)]  bezweckt in der kapitalistischen Produktion also durchaus nicht\nVerkürzung des Arbeitstags.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"11.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0042","text":"Sie bezweckt nur Verkürzung der für Pro-\n duktion eines bestimmten Waarenquantums nothwendigen Arbeits-\n zeit. Dass der Arbeiter bei gesteigerter Produktivkraft seiner Arbeit in einer\n Stunde z. B. 10 mal mehr Waare als früher producirt, also für jedes Stück\n Waare 10 mal weniger Arbeitszeit braucht, verhindert durchaus nicht, ihn nach\n wie vor 12 Stunden arbeiten und in den 12 Stunden 1200 statt früher\n 120 Stück produciren zu lassen. Ja sein Arbeitstag mag gleichzeitig\n verlängert werden, so dass er jetzt in 14 Stunden 1400 Stück producirt\n u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"11.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0043","text":"Man kann daher bei Oekonomen vom Schlag eines Mac Cul-\n loch, Ure, Senior und tutti quanti auf einer Seite lesen, dass der Arbeiter\n dem Kapital für die Entwicklung der Produktivkräfte Dank schuldet, weil sie\n die nothwendige Arbeitszeit verkürzt, und auf der nächsten Seite, dass\n er diesen Dank beweisen muss, indem er statt 10 künftig 15 Stunden\n arbeitet. Die Entwicklung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit, innerhalb der\n kapitalistischen Produktion, bezweckt den Theil des Arbeitstags,\n den der Arbeiter für sich selbst arbeiten muss, zu verkürzen,\n um grade dadurch den andern Theil des Arbeitstags, den er\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"302\", \"facs\": \"#f0321\"} -->für den Kapitalisten umsonst arbeiten kann, zu verlän-\n gern.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"11.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0044","text":"Wie weit diess Resultat auch ohne Verwohlfeilerung der Waaren\n erreichbar, wird sich zeigen in den besondern Produktionsmetho-\n den des relativen Mehrwerths, zu deren Betrachtung wir jetzt\n übergehn.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"11.44"}],"sha256":"e293beb7a990374cca924120da50c3e87011b6d9e058ed755d162ca4a6d8d8a7","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T203746115306Z_ee5d8c69","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1) Begriff des relativen Mehrwerths.","source_sha256":"e293beb7a990374cca924120da50c3e87011b6d9e058ed755d162ca4a6d8d8a7","sha256":"e53dae2d48d576fcdf53f2d3235591d7126eb224b09fc51fea082bbbf6ee7178","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:37:46.115326+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0001","text":"## Fourth Chapter.\n The Production of Relative Surplus-Value.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"11.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0002","text":"## 1) Concept of Relative Surplus-Value.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"11.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0003","text":"The part of the working day which merely produces an equivalent for the\n value of labour-power paid by capital counted for us hitherto\n as a constant magnitude, which indeed it is under given con-\n ditions of production, at a given economic stage of development\n of society. Beyond this his necessary labour-time\n the worker could work 2, 3, 4, 6, etc. hours. The rate of surplus-\n value and the magnitude of the working day depended on the magnitude\n of this prolongation. While necessary\n labour-time was constant, the total working day was variable. Assume now\n a working day whose magnitude and whose division\n into necessary labour and surplus-labour are given. Let the line a c\n a-------------b---c represent, for example, a twelve-hour working day,\n the segment a b 10 hours of necessary labour, the segment b c 2 hours\n of surplus-labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"11.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0004","text":"How, then, can the production of surplus-value be increased,\n i.e. surplus-labour be prolonged, without any further prolongation\n or independently of any further prolongation of a c?","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"11.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0005","text":"[Running matter: 19*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"292\", \"facs\": \"#f0311\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"11.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0006","text":"Despite the given limits of the working day a c, b c seems capable of being prolonged,\n if not by extension beyond its end-point c, which is at the same time the\n end-point of the working day a c, then by shifting its starting-\n point b in the opposite direction towards a. Assume that b' b\n a-------------b'--b----c is equal to half of b c, or equal to one\n hour of labour. If now in the twelve-hour working day a c the point\n b is moved to b', then b c extends to b' c, surplus-labour increases\n by half, from 2 to 3 hours, although the working day still\n amounts to only 12 hours. This extension of surplus-labour from b c to\n b' c, from 2 to 3 hours, is however plainly impossible without a simultaneous\n contraction of necessary labour from a b to a b', from 10 to\n 9 hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"11.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0007","text":"The prolongation of surplus-labour would correspond to the\nshortening of necessary labour, or a part of the\n labour-time which the worker hitherto in fact expends for himself\n is transformed into labour-time for the capitalist. What would change\n would not be the length of the working day, but its division\n into necessary labour and surplus-labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"11.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0008","text":"On the other hand, the magnitude of surplus-labour itself is plainly\n given with a given magnitude of the working day and a given\n value of labour-power. The value of labour-power, i.e. the labour-time\n required for its production, determines the labour-time necessary for the\n reproduction of its value. If one hour of labour is represented in a quantity\n of gold of half a shilling or 6 d., and if the daily value of labour-power amounts\n to 5 sh., then the worker must work 10 hours daily in order to replace\n the daily value of his labour-power paid to him by capital,\n or to produce an equivalent for the value of his necessary daily means\n of subsistence.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"11.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0009","text":"With the value of these means of subsistence\n the value of his labour-power [Note: The value of the daily average wage is determined by what\n the worker needs “so as to live, labeur, and generate.” (William Petty:\n “Political Anatomy of Ireland” 1672, p. 64.) “The Price of Labour\n is always constituted of the price of necessaries.” The worker does not receive the\n corresponding wage “whenever . . . . the labouring man’s wages will not,\nsuitably to his low rank and station, as a labouring man, support such a family\n as is often the lot of many of them to have.” (J. Vanderlint l. c. p. 19.)\n “The simple worker, who has only his arms and his industry, has nothing except insofar as he] , with the value of his labour-power\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"293\", \"facs\": \"#f0312\"} --> the magnitude of his necessary labour-time is given.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"11.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0010","text":"The\nmagnitude of surplus-labour, however, is obtained by subtracting\n necessary labour-time from the total working day.\n Ten hours subtracted from twelve leave two, and it is not apparent\n how, under the given conditions, surplus-labour can be prolonged beyond\n two hours. Certainly, the capitalist may pay the worker only 4 sh. 6 d. instead of 5 sh.\n or still less. For the reproduction of this value of 4 sh. 6 d., 9 hours of labour\n would suffice; of the twelve-hour working day, therefore, 3 instead of 2 hours\n would fall to surplus-labour, and surplus-value itself would rise\n from 1 sh. to 1 sh. 6 d. This result, however, would only have been attained\n by depressing the worker's wage below the value of his labour-power. With\n the 4 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"11.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0011","text":"6 d. which he produces in 9 hours, he commands ⅒ less\n means of subsistence than before, and thus only a stunted reproduc-\n tion of his labour-power takes place. Surplus-labour would here be prolonged\n only by transgressing its normal limits, its domain extended\n only by usurpatory encroachment upon the domain of necessary\n labour-time. Despite the important role which this method plays in the actual\n movement of wages, it is excluded here by the assumption\n that commodities, and therefore also labour-power, are bought and sold at\n their full value. Once this is assumed, the labour-time necessary for the production\n of labour-power or for the reproduction of its value cannot diminish because the worker's wage\nfalls below the value of his labour-power, but only because this value\n itself falls.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"11.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0012","text":"Given the length of the working day, the prolongation\n of surplus-labour must spring from the shortening of necessary labour-time,\n not conversely the shortening of necessary labour-time\n from the prolongation of surplus-labour. In our example, the\n value of labour-power must really fall by ⅒, in order that necessary\n [Note: succeeds in selling his pains to others … In every kind of labour it must happen\n and does in fact happen, that the worker’s wage is limited to what is necessary\n to procure subsistence for him.” (Turgot: “Reflections on the For-\n mation and Distribution of Wealth” (1766). Works ed.\n Daire, vol. I, p. 10.) “The price of the necessaries of life is, in fact, the cost\n of producing labour.” (Malthus: “Inquiry into etc. Rent.” Lond.\n 1815, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"11.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0013","text":"48 Note.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"294\", \"facs\": \"#f0313\"} --> labour-time may decrease by ⅒, from 10 to 9 hours, and\n surplus-labour therefore be prolonged from 2 to 3 hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"11.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0014","text":"Such a reduction in the value of labour-power by ⅒, however, itself presupposes\n that the same mass of means of subsistence which was formerly\n produced in 10, is now produced in 9 hours. This is, however, impossible\n without an increase in the productive power of labour. With\n given means, a shoemaker can, for example, make one pair of boots in a working\n day of 12 hours. If he is to make two pairs of boots in the same time,\n the productive power of his labour must double,\n and it cannot double without a change in his means of labour\n or his method of labour, or both at once. A revolution must therefore\n take place in the conditions of production of his\n labour, i.e. in his mode of production and hence in the labour-process\n itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"11.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0015","text":"By increase in the productive power of la-\n bour we understand here, generally, a change in the labour-\n process whereby the labour-time socially required for the production of a commodity\n is shortened, so that a smaller quantity of labour\n acquires the power to produce a larger quantity of use-value [Note: “When the arts are perfected, which is nothing other than the discovery of new\n ways whereby a manufacture may be completed with fewer people or (which is the\n same) in less time than before.” Galiani l. c. p. 159. “Economy\n in the costs of production can be nothing other than economy in the quan-\n tity of labour employed to produce.” Sismondi: Studies etc. vol. I. p. 22.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"11.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0016","text":"While, therefore, in the production of surplus-value in the form considered hitherto,\n the mode of production was assumed to be given, it by no means\n suffices, for the production of surplus-value by the transformation of necessary\n labour into surplus-labour, that capital takes possession of the la-\n bour-process in its historically inherited or existing\n form and merely prolongs its duration. It must revolutionise the tech-\n nological and social conditions of the labour-process, thus\n the mode of production itself, in order to increase the productive power\n of labour, by increasing the productive power of la-\n bour to lower the value of labour-power, and thus to shorten\n the part of the working day necessary for the reproduction\n of this value.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"11.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0017","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"295\", \"facs\": \"#f0314\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"11.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0018","text":"I call surplus-value produced by prolongation of the working day\n absolute surplus-value; on the other hand, the surplus-value which arises from\nshortening of necessary labour-time and the corresponding alteration\n in the ratio of magnitude of the two components of the working\n day, relative surplus-value.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"11.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0019","text":"In order to lower the value of labour-power, the increase in\n productivity must affect branches of industry whose products\n determine the value of labour-power, and thus either belong to the sphere\n of customary means of subsistence or can replace them.\n But the value of a commodity is determined not only by the quantity of\n labour which gives it its final form, but equally by the mass of labour\n contained in its means of production. For example, the value\n of a boot is determined not only by shoemakers' labour, but also by the\n value of leather, pitch, thread, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"11.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0020","text":"Increase in productivity and\n corresponding cheapening of commodities in the industries\n which supply the material elements of constant capital, the means\n of labour and the material of labour, for the production of necessary means\n of subsistence, thus likewise lower the value of labour-power. In branches\n of production, on the other hand, which supply neither necessary means of subsistence nor\n means of production for their manufacture, increased productivity\n leaves the value of labour-power untouched.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"11.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0021","text":"The cheapened commodity naturally lowers the value of labour-\n power only pro tanto, i.e. only in the proportion\n in which it enters into the reproduction of labour-power. Shirts, for example,\n are a necessary means of subsistence, but only one among many. Their cheapening dimin-\n ishes only the worker's expenditure on shirts. The total sum\n of necessary means of subsistence consists, indeed, only of individual commodities,\n all products of particular industries, and the value of each particular\n commodity always forms only an aliquot part of the value of labour-power.\n The total decrease of this value, and hence of the labour-time necessary for\n its reproduction, is, however, equal to the sum of the shorten-\n ings of necessary labour-time in all those particular branches of production.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"11.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0022","text":"We treat this general result here as though it were the\nimmediate result and immediate purpose in every\n individual case, although matters appear otherwise. If an individual\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"296\", \"facs\": \"#f0315\"} --> capitalist, by increasing the productive power of labour, for example cheapens shirts,\n the purpose of lowering the value of labour-power and hence necessary labour-\n time pro tanto does not necessarily hover before him; but only insofar as he ultimately contributes to this re-\n sult does he contribute to the increase of the general rate of\n surplus-value [Note: “When the manufacturer, by improving machinery, doubles his products\n . . . . he gains (ultimately) only insofar as this enables him\n to clothe the worker more cheaply, etc., and thus a smaller part of the total\n product falls to the worker.” (Ramsay l. c.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"11.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0023","text":"p. 168.)] . The general and necessary tendencies of\n capital are to be distinguished from their forms of appearance.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"11.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0024","text":"The exposition of the manner in which the immanent laws\n of capitalist production appear in the external move-\n ment of capitals, assert themselves as coercive laws of com-\n petition, and therefore come to the consciousness of the individual\n capitalist as driving motives, falls outside the limits of this work.\n Scientific analysis of competition is in general possible only once the inner\n nature of capital has been grasped. Just as the apparent movement of heavenly\n bodies is intelligible only to one who knows their real, though sensuously imperceptible,\n movement. Nevertheless, for the understanding of the production of relative surplus-\n value, and solely on the basis of the results already obtained,\n the following is to be noted.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"11.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0025","text":"If one hour of labour is represented in a quantity of gold of 6 d. or\n½ sh., then in a twelve-hour working day a value of 6 sh. is pro-\nduced. Suppose that, with the given productive power of labour, 12 articles\nof commodities are manufactured in these 12 hours of labour. Let the value\nof the means of production used up in each article, raw material, etc., be\n6 d. Under these circumstances the individual commodity costs 1 sh., namely\n6 d. for the value of the means of production, 6 d. for the value newly added\nin its processing. Now let a capitalist succeed in doubling the productive\npower of labour and thus in producing 24 articles of this kind of commodity\ninstead of 12 in the twelve-\nhour working day. With the value of the means of pro-\nduction unchanged, the value of the individual commodity now falls to 9 d., namely 6 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"11.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0026","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"297\", \"facs\": \"#f0316\"} --> for the value of the means of production, 3 d. for the value newly\nadded by the last labour. Despite the doubled productive power, the\nworking day still creates only a new value of 6 sh. But this value\nis distributed over twice as many products. Each individual product\ntherefore receives only \\frac{1}{24} of this total value, instead of formerly \\frac{1}{12}, 3 d.\ninstead of 6 d.; or, what amounts to the same thing, in their\ntransformation into product the means of production now have added to them,\ncalculated per article, only half an hour of labour instead of, as formerly,\na whole hour of labour. The individual value of this commodity\nnow stands below its social\nvalue, i.e. it costs less labour-time than the great mass of the\nsame articles, produced under the social average condi-\ntions.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"11.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0027","text":"The article costs on average 1 sh., or represents 2 hours of\nsocial labour; with the altered mode of production it costs\nonly 9 d., or contains only 1½ hours of labour. The actual value\nof a commodity, however, is determined not by its individual, but by\nits social value, i.e. not by the labour-\ntime which it actually costs the producer in the individual case,\nbut by the labour-time socially required for its production.\nIf, therefore, the capitalist who applies the new method sells his commodity\nat its social value of 1 sh., he sells it 3 d. above\nits individual value and thus realizes an extra surplus-value of 3 d.\nOn the other hand, the twelve-hour working day is now represented for him\nin 24 articles of commodity instead of formerly in 12.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"11.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0028","text":"In order, therefore, to sell the product of one\nworking day, he requires double sales or a two-\nfold larger market. Other circumstances remaining the same, his\ncommodities conquer a larger market-space only by contraction of their prices.\nHe will therefore sell them above their individual, but below their social\nvalue, say at 10 d. per article. Thus from each individual article\nhe still strikes out an extra surplus-value of 1 d.\nThis increase of surplus-value takes place for him whether or not\nhis commodity belongs to the sphere of necessary means of subsistence and\ntherefore enters determinatively into the general value of labour-power.\nApart from the latter circumstance, then, there exists for every individual capita-\nlist the motive to cheapen the commodity through increased productive power\nof labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"11.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0029","text":"Yet even in this case the increased production of\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"298\", \"facs\": \"#f0317\"} --> surplus-value springs from the shortening of necessary labour-time and the cor-\nresponding prolongation of surplus-labour [Note: “A man’s profit does not depend upon his command of the produce of\nother men’s labour, but upon his command of labour itself. If he can\nsell his goods at a higher price, while his workmen’s wages remain unaltered, he\nis clearly benefited … A smaller proportion of what he produces is sufficient to\nput that labour into motion, and a larger proportion consequently remains for\nhimself.” (“Outlines of Polit. Econ.” Lond. 1832, p. 49, 50.)] . Necessary labour-time\namounted to 10 hours, or the daily value of labour-power to 5 sh.; surplus-\nlabour to 2 hours, and the surplus-value produced daily therefore to 1 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"11.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0030","text":"But our capi-\ntalist now produces 24 articles, which he sells at 10 d. per article, or together\nat 20 sh. Since the value of the means of production equals 12 sh.,\n14⅖ articles of commodity replace only the advanced constant capital.\nThe twelve-hour working day is represented in the remaining 9⅗ articles.\nSince the price of labour-power = 5 sh., necessary labour-time is represented\nin the product of 6 articles, and surplus-labour in 3⅗ articles.\nNecessary labour-time now amounts to less than ⅔, surplus-labour to\nmore than ⅓ of the working day, whereas under the social average condi-\ntions necessary labour occupies ⅚ and surplus-labour only\n⅙ of the working day. The same result is obtained thus: The\nproduct-value of the twelve-hour working day is 20 sh. Of this,\n12 sh. belong to the value of the means of production which merely reappears.\nThere thus remain 8 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"11.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0031","text":"as the monetary expression of the value in which the\nworking day is represented. This monetary expression is higher than the monetary expression\nof social average labour of the same sort, of which\n12 hours are expressed only in 6 sh. Labour of excep-\ntional productive power acts as potentiated labour, or creates\nin equal periods of time higher values than the social average-\nlabour of the same kind. But our capitalist still pays only 5 sh.\nfor the daily value of labour-power. The worker therefore requires, instead\nof formerly 10, now less than 8 hours for the reproduction of this value.\nHis surplus-labour thus grows from 2 hours to more than 4, and the\nsurplus-value produced by him from 1 sh. to 3 sh. 6 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"11.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0032","text":"The capitalist who\napplies the improved mode of production therefore appropriates a greater\npart of the working day for surplus-labour than the other capita-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"299\", \"facs\": \"#f0318\"} --> lists in the same business. He does individually what capital\ndoes on the whole and on a large scale in the production of relative\nsurplus-value. On the other hand, that extra surplus-value disappears as soon\nas the new mode of production becomes general, and with it the difference between\nthe individual value of the more cheaply produced commodities and\ntheir social value disappears.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"11.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0033","text":"The same law\nof the determination of value by labour-time, which makes itself felt to the\ncapitalist with the new method in the form that he must sell his commodity\nbelow its social value, drives his competitors, as the coercive law of competition,\nto introduce the new mode of produc-\ntion [Note: “If my neighbour by doing much with little labour, can sell cheap, I must\ncontrive to sell as cheap as he. So that every art, trade, or engine, doing work\nwith labour of fewer hands, and consequently cheaper, begets in others a kind of\nnecessity and emulation, either of using the same art, trade, or engine, or of in-\nventing something like it, that every man may be upon the square, that no man\nmay be able to undersell his neighbour.” (“The Advantages of the East-\nIndia Trade to England. Lond. 1720”, p. 67.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"11.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0034","text":"The general rate of surplus-value is therefore\nultimately affected by the entire process only when the increase\nin the productive power of labour has seized branches of production, thus\ncheapening commodities which enter into the sphere of necessary means of subsistence,\nand therefore form elements of the value of labour-power.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"11.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0035","text":"The value of commodities stands in inverse propor-\ntion to the productive power of labour. Likewise, because it is determined by\ncommodity-values, does the value of labour-power. By contrast,\nrelative surplus-value stands in direct proportion to\nthe productive power of labour. It rises with rising and falls with falling\nproductive power. A social average working day of 12\nhours, assuming money-value to remain constant, always produces the same\nvalue-product of 6 sh., however this sum of value may be distributed\nbetween equivalent for the value of labour-power and surplus-value.\nBut if, as a consequence of increased productive power, the value of daily\nmeans of subsistence, and therefore the daily value of labour-power, falls from 5 sh. to\n3 sh., surplus-value grows from 1 sh. to 3 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"11.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0036","text":"In order to reproduce the value of\nlabour-power, 10, and now only 6, hours of la-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"300\", \"facs\": \"#f0319\"} --> bour are necessary. Four hours of labour have been set free and can\nbe annexed to the domain of surplus-labour. It is therefore the immanent\nimpulse and constant tendency of capital to increase the productive power\nof labour, in order to cheapen the commodity and, through the cheapen-\ning of the commodity, the worker himself [Note: “In whatever proportion the expences of a labourer are diminished, in the\nsame proportion will his wages be diminished, if the restraints upon industry are\nat the same time taken off.” (“Considerations concerning taking off\nthe Bounty on Corn exported etc. Lond. 1752”, p. 7.)","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"11.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0037","text":"“The\ninterest of trade requires, that corn and all provisions should be as cheap as\npossible; for whatever makes them dear, must make labour dear also … in all\ncountries, where industry is not restrained, the price of provisions must affect the\nPrice of Labour. This will always be diminished when the necessaries of life\ngrow cheaper.” (l. c. p. 3.) “Wages are decreased in the same proportion\nas the powers of production increase. Machinery, it is true, cheapens\nthe necessaries of life, but it also cheapens the labourer too.” (“A\nPrize Essay on the comparative merits of Competition and Co-\noperation. Lond. 1834”, p. 27.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"11.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0038","text":"The absolute value of the commodity is in and for itself\nindifferent to the capitalist who produces it. He is interested only in\nthe surplus-value contained in it and realizable in its sale. Realization of sur-\nplus-value includes of itself replacement of the advanced value. Since,\nthen, relative surplus-value grows in direct proportion to the development of\nthe productive power of labour, while the value of commodities falls in in-\nverse proportion to the same development, since therefore the same iden-\ntical process cheapens commodities and increases the surplus-value contained\nin them, the riddle is resolved that the capitalist, whose sole concern is the\nproduction of exchange-value, constantly strives to lower the exchange-value of com-\nmodities, a contradiction with which one of the founders\nof political economy, Dr Quesnay, tormented his opponents, and to which\nthey remained owing him the answer.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"11.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0039","text":"“You admit,” says Quesnay,\n“that the more one can, without detriment to production, save costs or cost-\nly labours in the manufacture of industrial products,\nthe more advantageous this saving, because it diminishes the price of the manufactured article. And nevertheless you believe that the production of wealth\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"301\", \"facs\": \"#f0320\"} --> which results from the labours of industrial workers consists in the increase\nof the exchange-value of their manufactured article” [Note: “Ils conviennent que plus on peut, sans préjudice, épargner de frais ou de\ntravaux dispendieux dans la fabrication des ouvrages des artisans, plus cette épargne\nest profitable par la diminution des prix de ces ouvrages.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"11.39","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0040","text":"Cependant ils croient\nque la production de richesse qui résulte des travaux des artisans consiste dans\nl’augmentation de la valeur vénale de leurs ouvrages.” (Quesnay: “Dia-\nlogues sur le Commerce et sur les Travaux des Artisans”, p. 188,\n189.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"11.40","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0041","text":"Economy of labour through development of the productive power of\nlabour [Note: “Ces spéculateurs si économes du travail des ouvriers qu’il faudrait qu’ils\npayassent.” (J. N. Bidault: “Du Monopole qui s’établit dans les\narts industrielles et le commerce. Paris 1828”, p. 13.) “The\nemployer will be always on the stretch to economise time and labour.” (Dugald\nStewart: Works ed. by Sir W. Hamilton. Edinburgh, v. III, 1855,\n“Lectures on Polit. Econ.”, p. 318.) “Their (the capitalists’) interest is\nthat the productive powers of the labourers they employ should be the greatest\npossible. On promoting that power their attention is fixed and almost exclusively\nfixed.” (R. Jones l. c. Lecture IlI.)]  in capitalist production therefore by no means aims at\na shortening of the working day.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"11.41","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0042","text":"It aims only at a shortening of the labour-\ntime necessary for the production of a determinate quantity of commodities.\nThat the worker, with increased productive power of his labour, for example,\nproduces 10 times more commodity in one hour than formerly, and thus requires\n10 times less labour-time for each article of commodity, does not at all prevent\nhis being made to work, as before, 12 hours and in the 12 hours to produce 1200\narticles instead of formerly 120. Indeed, his working day may simultaneously\nbe extended, so that he now produces 1400 articles in 14 hours,\netc.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"11.42","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0043","text":"One may therefore read, in economists of the stamp of Mac Cul-\nloch, Ure, Senior and tutti quanti, on one page that the worker\nowes capital thanks for the development of the productive powers, because it\nshortens necessary labour-time, and on the next page that\nhe must prove this gratitude by working 15 hours in the future instead of 10.\nThe development of the productive power of labour, within capitalist\nproduction, aims to shorten the part of the working day\nwhich the worker must work for himself,\nin order precisely thereby to lengthen the other part of the working day, which he\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"302\", \"facs\": \"#f0321\"} -->can work gratis for the capitalist.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"11.43","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0044","text":"How far this result may be attainable even without the cheapening of commodities,\nwill be shown in the particular methods of production\nof relative surplus-value, to whose consideration we now\nproceed.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"11.44","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_1BDR:P0044"]}],"source_digest":"0a94b28068a74bdd6130c0f40c3b9221cd1401348b37ca274d15bdb2e830f390","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO","title":"2) Cooperation.","order":12,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_2COO","text":"Die kapitalistische Produktion, wie wir sahen, beginnt in der That erst,\n wo dasselbe individuelle Kapital eine grössere Anzahl Arbeiter gleichzeitig\n beschäftigt, der Arbeitsprozess also seinen Umfang erweitert und Produkt\n auf grösserer quantitativer Stufenleiter liefert. Nur wo die Arbeiter-\n anzahl hinreicht, damit die von ihr producirte Masse von Mehrwerth den\n Arbeitsanwender selbst von der Arbeit entbinde, wird der letztere vollbür-\n tiger Kapitalist. Das Wirken einer grössern Arbeiteranzahl zur selben Zeit,\n in demselben Raum (oder, wenn man will, auf demselben Arbeitsfeld), zur\n Produktion derselben Waarensorte, unter dem Kommando desselben Kapita-\n listen, bildet daher historisch und begrifflich den Ausgangspunkt der\n kapitalistischen Produktion. Mit Bezug auf die Produktions-\n weise selbst unterscheidet sich z. B. die Manufaktur in ihren An-\n fängen kaum anders von der zünftigen Handwerksindustrie als durch die\n grössere Zahl der gleichzeitig von demselben Kapital beschäftigten Arbei-\n ter. Die Werkstatt des Zunftmeisters ist nur erweitert.\n\n\n\n\nDer Unterschied ist also zunächst bloss quantitativ. Man sah,\n dass die Masse des Mehrwerths, welche ein gegebnes Kapital produ-\n cirt, gleich dem Mehrwerth, den der einzelne Arbeiter liefert, multiplicirt mit\n der Anzahl der gleichzeitig beschäftigten Arbeiter. Diese Anzahl ändert\n an und für sich nichts an der Rate des Mehrwerths oder dem Ex-\n ploitationsgrad der Arbeitskraft. Was aber die Produktion von Waaren-\n werth überhaupt betrifft, so scheint für sie selbst jede qualitative Ver-\n änderung des Arbeitsprozesses gleichgültig. Es folgt diess aus der Natur des\n Tauschwerths, der nichts ist als ein bestimmtes Quantum vergegen-\n ständlichter Arbeit. Vergegenständlicht sich ein zwölfstündiger Arbeits-\n tag in 6 sh., so 1200 solcher Arbeitstage in 6 sh. × 1200. In dem\n einen Fall haben sich 12 × 1200, in dem andern 12 Arbeitsstunden den\n Produkten einverleibt. In der Werthproduktion zählen Viele immer nur\n als viele Einzelne. Für die Werthproduktion macht es also keinen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"303\", \"facs\": \"#f0322\"} -->qualitativen Unterschied, ob 1200 Arbeiter vereinzelt oder ob sie\n unter dem Kommando desselben Kapitals vereint produciren.\n\n\n\n\nIndess findet doch innerhalb gewisser Grenzen eine Modifikation statt.\n Die im Werth vergegenständlichte Arbeit ist Arbeit von gesellschaft-\n licher Durchschnittsqualität und so ist der Werth der Arbeitskraft der\n Werth durchschnittlicher Arbeitskraft. Eine Durchschnittsgrösse existirt\n aber immer nur als Durchschnitt vieler verschiedner Grössenindividuen der-\n selben Art. In jedem Industriezweig weicht der individuelle Arbeiter,\n Peter oder Paul, mehr oder minder vom Durchschnittsarbeiter ab. Diese\n individuellen Abweichungen oder was man mathematisch „Irrthümer“\n nennt, kompensiren sich und verschwinden, sobald man eine grössere An-\n zahl Arbeiter zusammennimmt. Der berühmte Sophist und Sykophant\nEdmund Burke will aus seinen praktischen Erfahrungen als Pächter\n sogar wissen, dass schon „für ein so geringes Peloton“ wie 5 Acker-\n knechte aller individuelle Unterschied der Arbeit verschwindet, also die\n ersten besten im Mannesalter befindlichen fünf englischen Ackerknechte\n zusammengenommen in derselben Zeit grad so viel Arbeit verrichten als\n beliebige andre fünf englische Ackerknechte [Note: „Unquestionably, there is a great deal of difference between the value of\n one man’s labour and that of another, from strength, dexterity and honest appli-\n cation. But I am quite sure, from my best observation, that any given five\n men will in their total, afford a proportion of labour equal to any other five\n within the periods of life I have stated; that is, that among such five men there\n will be one possessing all the qualifications of a good workman, one bad, and the\n other three middling, and approximating to the first and the last. So that in so\n small a platoon as that of even five, you will find the full complement of all that\n five men can earn.“ (E. Burke l. c. p. 16.) cf. Quételet über das Durch-\n schnittsindividuum.] . Die von ihm angegebne\n Zahl ist hier gleichgültig, aber es ist klar, dass der Gesammtarbeitstag\n einer grössern Anzahl gleichzeitig beschäftigter Arbeiter an und für sich\n ein Tag gesellschaftlicher Durchschnittsarbeit ist. Der\n Arbeitstag des Einzelnen sei z. B. zwölfstündig. So bildet der Arbeitstag\n von 12 gleichzeitig beschäftigten Arbeitern einen Gesammtarbeitstag von\n 144 Stunden, und obgleich die Arbeit eines Jeden des Dutzend mehr oder\n minder von der gesellschaftlichen Durchschnittsarbeit abweichen, der Ein-\n zelne daher etwas mehr oder weniger Zeit zu derselben Verrichtung brau-\n chen mag, besitzt der Arbeitstag jedes Einzelnen als ein Zwölftel des Ge-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"304\", \"facs\": \"#f0323\"} --> sammtarbeitstags von 144 Stunden die gesellschaftliche Durchschnittsqualität.\n Für den Kapitalisten aber, der ein Dutzend beschäftigt, existirt der Arbeitstag\n als Gesammtarbeitstag des Dutzend. Der Arbeitstag jedes Einzelnen existirt\n als aliquoter Theil des Gesammtarbeitstags, ganz unabhängig davon, ob\n die Zwölf einander in die Hand arbeiten oder ob der ganze Zusammenhang\n ihrer Arbeiten nur darin besteht, dass sie für denselben Kapitalisten ar-\n beiten. Werden dagegen von den 12 Arbeitern je zwei von einem klei-\n nen Meister beschäftigt, so wird es zufällig, ob jeder einzelne Meister die-\n selbe Werthmasse producirt und daher die allgemeine Rate des Mehrwerths\n realisirt. Es fänden individuelle Abweichungen statt. Verbrauchte ein\n Arbeiter bedeutend mehr Zeit in der Produktion einer Waare als gesell-\n schaftlich erheischt ist, wiche die für ihn individuell nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit bedeutend ab von der gesellschaftlich nothwendigen oder der Durch-\n schnitts-Arbeitszeit, so gälte seine Arbeit nicht als Durchschnittsarbeit,\n seine Arbeitskraft nicht als durchschnittliche Arbeitskraft. Sie verkaufte sich\n gar nicht oder nur unter dem Durchschnittswerth der Arbeitskraft. Ein\n bestimmtes Minimum der Arbeitsfertigkeit ist also vorausgesetzt, und wir\n werden später sehn, dass die kapitalistische Produktion Mittel findet, diess\n Minimum zu messen. Nichts desto weniger weicht das Minimum vom\n Durchschnitt ab, obgleich auf der andern Seite der Durchschnittswerth\n der Arbeitskraft gezahlt werden muss. Von den sechs Kleinmeistern\n würde der eine daher mehr, der andre weniger als die allgemeine Rate\n des Mehrwerths herausschlagen. Die Ungleichheiten würden sich für die\n Gesellschaft kompensiren, aber nicht für den einzelnen Meister. Das Ge-\n setz der Verwerthung überhaupt realisirt sich also für den einzelnen\n Produzenten erst vollständig, sobald er als Kapitalist producirt, viele Ar-\n beiter gleichzeitig anwendet, also von vorn herein gesellschaftliche\n Durchschnittsarbeit in Bewegung setzt [Note: Herr Professor Roscher will entdeckt haben, dass eine Nähmamsell, die\n während zwei Tagen von der Frau Professorin beschäftigt wird, mehr Arbeit liefert,\n als zwei Nähmamsellen, welche die Frau Professorin am selben Tage beschäftigt.\n Der Herr Professor stelle seine Beobachtungen über den kapitalistischen Produk-\n tionsprozess nicht in der Kinderstube an, und nicht unter Umständen, worin die\n Hauptperson fehlt, der Kapitalist.] .\n\n\n\n\nAuch bei gleichbleibender Arbeitsweise bewirkt die gleich-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"305\", \"facs\": \"#f0324\"} --> zeitige Anwendung einer grösseren Arbeiteranzahl eine Revolution in den\n gegenständlichen Bedingungen des Arbeitsprozesses.\n Die Baulichkeiten, worin die Vielen arbeiten, die Lager für Rohmaterial,\n halbfertige Waaren u. s. w., die Gefässe, Instrumente, Apparate u. s. w.,\n die von vielen gleichzeitig oder abwechselnd gebraucht werden können,\n kurz ein Theil der Produktionsmittel wird jetzt gemeinsam\n im Arbeitsprozess konsumirt. Einerseits wird der Tauschwerth\n von Waaren, also auch von Produktionsmitteln, durchaus nicht erhöht\n durch irgend welche erhöhte Ausbeutung ihres Gebrauchswerths.\n Andrerseits wächst zwar der Massstab der gemeinsam gebrauchten Pro-\n duktionsmittel. Ein Zimmer, worin 20 Weber mit ihren 20 Webstühlen\n arbeiten, muss weiter gestreckt sein als das Zimmer eines unabhängigen\n Webers mit zwei Gesellen. Aber die Produktion einer Werkstatt für 20\n Personen kostet weniger Arbeit als die von 10 Werkstätten für je zwei Per-\n sonen, und so wächst überhaupt der Werth massenweise koncentrirter\n und gemeinsamer Produktionsmittel nicht verhältnissmässig mit ihrem\n Umfang und ihrem Nutzeffekt. Gemeinsam vernutzte Produktionsmittel\n geben geringeren Werthbestandtheil an das einzelne Produkt ab, theils weil\n der Gesammtwerth, den sie abgeben, sich gleichzeitig auf eine grössere\n Produktenmasse vertheilt, theils weil sie in Folge ihres vergrösserten Um-\n fangs zwar mit absolut grösserem, aber, im Verhältniss zu ihrem Wir-\n kungskreis, mit relativ kleinerem Werth als vereinzelte Produktionsmittel\n in den Produktionsprozess eintreten. Damit sinkt ein Werthbestand-\n theil, welcher constantes Kapital ersetzt, also, proportionell zur Grösse dieses\n Bestandtheils, auch der Gesammtwerth der Waare. Die Wirkung ist die-\n selbe, als hätte sich die Produktivkraft der Arbeit vermehrt in solchen\n Industriezweigen, die Produktionsmittel liefern. Diese Oekonomie in\n der Anwendung der Produktionsmittel entspringt nur aus\n ihrem gemeinsamen Konsum im Arbeitsprozess Vieler.\n Und sie erhalten diesen Charakter als Bedingungen gesellschaftli-\n cher Arbeit oder gesellschaftliche Bedingungen der Ar-\n beit im Unterschied von den zersplitterten und relativ theureren Pro-\n duktionsbedingungen vereinzelter selbstständiger Arbeiter oder Kleinmei-\n ster, selbst wenn die Vielen nur räumlich zusammen, nicht mit einander\n arbeiten. Ein Theil der Arbeitsmittel erwirbt diesen gesellschaft-\n lichen Charakter, bevor ihn der Arbeitsprozess selbst erwirbt.\n\n\n [Running matter: I. 20] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"306\", \"facs\": \"#f0325\"} -->\n\nDie Oekonomie der Produktionsmittel ist überhaupt von\n doppeltem Gesichtspunkt zu betrachten. Das einemal, so weit sie Waa-\n ren verwohlfeilert und dadurch den Werth der Arbeitskraft senkt.\n Das andremal, so weit sie das Verhältniss des Mehrwerths zum\n vorgeschossnen Gesammtkapital, d. h. zur Werthsumme\n seiner constanten und variablen Bestandtheile, verändert. Der letztere\n Punkt wird erst im dritten Buch dieses Werks erörtert, wohin wir des Zu-\n sammenhangs wegen auch manches schon hierher gehörige verweisen.\n Einerseits gebietet der Gang der Analyse diese Zerreissung des Gegenstands.\n Andrerseits entspricht sie dem Geist der kapitalistischen Produktion. Da\n nämlich die Arbeitsbedingungen hier dem Arbeiter selbststäudig gegenüber-\n treten, erscheint auch ihre Oekonomie als eine besondere Operation, die\n ihn nichts angeht und daher getrennt ist von den Methoden, wodurch die\n Produktivität der vom Kapital konsumirten Arbeitskraft erhöht wird.\n\n\n\n\nDie Form der Arbeit Vieler, die in demselben Produk-\n tionsprozess oder in verschiedenen, aber zusammenhängenden Pro-\n duktionsprozessen, planmässig neben und mit einander arbeiten, heisst\nCooperation [Note: „Concours de forces.“ (Destutt de Tracy l. c. p. 78.)] .\n\n\n\n\nWie die Angriffskraft einer Kavalerieschwadron oder die Widerstands-\n kraft eines Infanterieregiments wesentlich verschieden ist von der Summe\n der vereinzelten Angriffs- und Widerstandskräfte, welche jeder Kavalerist\n und Infanterist für sich entwickeln könnte, so die mechanische\n Kraftsumme vereinzelter Arbeiter von der mechanischen Kraftpotenz,\n die sich entwickelt, wenn viele Hände gleichzeitig in derselben\n ungetheilten Operation zusammenwirken, z. B. wenn es gilt eine\n Last zu heben, eine Kurbel zu drehn oder einen Widerstand aus dem Weg\n zu räumen [Note: „There are numerous operations of so simple a kind as not to admit a\n division into parts, which cannot be performed without the cooperation of many\n pairs of hands. For instance the lifting of a large tree on a wain … every\n thing in short, which cannot be done unless a great many pairs of hands help each\n other in the same undivided employment, and at the same time.“ (E. G. Wake-\n field: „A View of the Art of Colonization. Lond. 1849“, p. 168.)] . Die Wirkung der kombinirten Arbeit könnte hier von der\n vereinzelten gar nicht oder nur in viel längeren Zeiträumen oder nur auf\n einem Zwergmassstab hervorgebracht werden. Es handelt sich hier nicht\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"307\", \"facs\": \"#f0326\"} --> nur um Erhöhung der individuellen Produktivkraft durch die Cooperation,\n sondern um die Schöpfung einer Produktivkraft, die an und für sich Mas-\n senkraft ist [Note: „As one man cannot, and 10 men must strain, to lift a tun of weight,\n yet one hundred men can do it only by the strength of a finger of each of them.“\n (John Bellers: „Proposals for raising a colledge of industry.\n Lond. 1696“, p. 21.)] .\n\n\n\n\nAbgesehn von der neuen mechanischen Kraftpotenz, die aus der Ver-\n schmelzung vieler Kräfte in eine Gesammtkraft entspringt, erzeugt bei\n den meisten produktiven Arbeiten der blosse gesellschaftliche\n Kontakt einen Wetteifer und eine eigne Erregung der Lebensgei-\n ster (animal spirit), welche die individuelle Leistungsfähigkeit der Ein-\n zelnen erhöhen, so dass ein Dutzend Personen zusammen in einem gleich-\n zeitigen Arbeitstag von 144 Stunden ein viel grösseres Gesammtprodukt\n liefern als zwölf vereinzelte Arbeiter, von denen jeder 12 Stunden, oder\n als ein Arbeiter, der 12 Tage nach einander arbeitet [Note: „There is also“ (wenn dieselbe Arbeiterzahl von einem Pächter auf\n 300, statt von 10 Pächtern auf je 30 acres angewandt wird) „an advantage in the\n proportion of servants, which will not easily be understood but by practical men;\n for it is natural to say, as 1 is to 4, so are 3:12: but this will not hold good in\n practice; for in harvest-time and many other operations which require that kind\n of despatch, by the throwing many hands together, the work is better,\n and more expeditiously done: f. i., in harvest, 2 drivers, 2 loaders, 2 pitchers,\n 2 rakers, and the rest at the rick, or in the barn, will despatch double the work,\n that the same number of hands would do, if divided into different gangs, on\n different farms.“ („An Enquiry into the Connection between the\n present price of provisions and the size of farms. By a Farmer.\n Lond. 1773“, p. 7, 8)] . Diess rührt daher,\n dass der Mensch von Natur, wenn nicht, wie Aristoteles meint, ein politi-\n sches [Note: Aristoteles’ Definition ist eigentlich die, dass der Mensch von Natur Stadt-\n bürger. Sie ist ebenso charakteristisch für das klassische Alterthum, als Franklin’s\n Definition, dass der Mensch von Natur Instrumentenmacher, für das Yankeethum.] , jedenfalls ein gesellschaftliches Thier ist.\n\n\n\n\nObgleich Viele Dasselbe oder Gleichartiges gleichzeitig mit\n einander verrichten, kann die individuelle Arbeit eines Jeden dennoch als\n Theil der Gesammtarbeit verschiedne Phasen des Arbeitspro-\n zesses selbst darstellen, die der Arbeitsgegenstand, in Folge der Coo-\n [Running matter: 20*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"308\", \"facs\": \"#f0327\"} --> peration, rascher durchläuft. Z. B., wenn Maurer eine Reihe von Händen\n bilden, um Bausteine vom Fuss eines Gestells bis zu seiner Spitze zu be-\n fördern, thut jeder von ihnen dasselbe, aber dennoch bilden die einzelnen\n Verrichtungen continuirliche Theile einer Gesammtverrichtung, besondre\n Phasen, die jeder Baustein im Arbeitsprozess durchlaufen muss, und wo-\n durch ihn etwa die 24 Hände des Gesammtarbeiters rascher befördern,\n als die zwei Hände jedes einzelnen Arbeiters, der das Gerüst auf- und ab-\n stiege [Note: „On doit encore remarquer que cette division partielle du travail peut se\n faire quand même les ouvriers sont occupés d’une même bésogne. Des maçons par\n exemple, occupés de faire passer de mains en mains des briques à un échafaudage\n supérieur, font tous la même bésogne, et pourtant il existe parmi eux une espèce\n de division de travail, qui consiste en ce que chacun d’eux fait passer la brique par\n un espace donné, et que tous ensemble la font parvenir beaucoup plus prompte-\n ment à l’endroit marqué qu’ils ne feraient si chacun d’eux portait sa brique sépa-\n rement jusqu’à l’échafaudage supérieur.“ (F. Skarbek: „Théorie des\n richesses sociales. 2ème ed. Paris 1840,“ t. I, p. 97, 98.)] . Der Arbeitsgegenstand durchläuft denselben\n Raum in kürzerer Zeit. Andrerseits findet Kombination der Arbeit\n statt, wenn ein Bau z. B. von verschiednen Seiten gleichzeitig ange-\n griffen wird, obgleich die Cooperirenden Dasselbe oder Gleicharti-\n ges thun. Der kombinirte Arbeitstag von 144 Stunden, der den Arbeits-\n gegenstand vielseitig im Raum angreift, weil der kombinirte Arbeiter\n oder Gesammtarbeiter vorn und hinten Augen und Hände hat und\n in gewissem Grad Allgegenwart besitzt, fördert das Gesammtprodukt\n rascher als 12 zwölfstündige Arbeitstage mehr oder minder vereinzelter\n Arbeiter, die ihr Werk einseitiger angreifen müssen. In derselben\n Zeit reifen verschiedne Raumtheile des Produkts.\n\n\n\n\nWir betonten hier, dass die Vielen, die einander ergänzen, Das-\n selbe oder Gleichartiges thun, weil diese einfache Form der\n Cooperation, wie man später sehn wird, auch in ihrer ausgebildetsten\n Gestalt eine grosse Rolle spielt. Ist der Arbeitsprozess komplicirt,\n so erlaubt die blosse Masse der Zusammenarbeitenden die verschiede-\n nen Operationen unter verschiedne Hände zu vertheilen, daher gleich-\n zeitig zu verrichten, und dadurch die zur Herstellung des Gesammtpro-\n dukts nöthige Arbeitszeit zu verkürzen [Note: „Est-il question d’exécuter un travail compliqué? plusieurs choses doivent] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"309\", \"facs\": \"#f0328\"} -->\n\nIn vielen Produktionssphären giebt es kritische Momente, d. h.\n durch die Natur des Arbeitsprozesses selbst bestimmte Zeitepochen, wäh-\n rend deren bestimmte Arbeitsresultate erzielt werden müssen. Soll z. B.\n eine Heerde Schafe geschoren oder eine Morgenanzahl Kornland gemäht\n und geherbstet werden, so hängt Quantität und Qualität des Produkts da-\n von ab, dass die Operation zu einer gewissen Zeit begonnen und zu einer\n gewissen Zeit beendet wird. Der Zeitraum, den der Arbeitsprozess\n einnehmen darf, ist hier vorgeschrieben, wie etwa beim Häringsfang. Der\n Einzelne kann aus einem Tag nur einen Arbeitstag herausschnei-\n den, sage von 12 Stunden, aber die Cooperation von 100 z. B. erwei-\n tert einen zwölfstündigen Tag zu einem Arbeitstag von\n 1200 Stunden. Die Kürze der Arbeitsfrist wird kompensirt durch\n die Grösse der Arbeitsmasse, die im entscheidenden Augenblick auf das\n Produktionsfeld geworfen wird. Die rechtzeitige Wirkung hängt\n hier ab von der gleichzeitigen Anwendung vieler kombinirten Ar-\n beitstage, der Umfang des Nutzeffekts von der Arbeiteranzahl,\n die jedoch stets kleiner bleibt als die Anzahl der Arbeiter, die vereinzelt\n in demselben Zeitraum denselben Wirkungsraum ausfüllen würden [Note: „The doing of it (der Arbeit in der Agrikultur) at the critical junc-\n ture. is of so much the greater consequence.“ (An Inquiry into the Con-\n nection between the present price etc., p. 9.) „In der Agrikultur giebt\n es keinen wichtigeren Faktor, als den Faktor der Zeit.“ (Liebig: „Ueber\n Theorie und Praxis in der Landwirthschaft. 1856“, p. 23.)] .\n Es ist der Mangel dieser Cooperation, wodurch im Westen der Vereinigten\n Staaten eine Masse Korn, und in den Theilen Ostindiens, wo englische\n Herrschaft das alte Gemeinwesen zerstört hat, eine Masse Baumwolle jähr-\n lich verwüstet wird [Note: „The next evil is one which one would scarcely expect to find in a coun-\n try which exports more labour than any other in the world, with the exception\n perhaps of China and England — the impossibility of procuring a sufficient number\n of hands to clean the cotton. The consequence of this is that large quantities of\n the crop are left unpicked, while another portion is gathered from the ground,] .\n\n\n\n\n [Note: être fait simultanément. L’un en fait une pendant que l’autre en fait une autre,\n et tous contribuent à l’effet qu’un seul homme n’aurait pu produire. L’un rame\n pendant que l’autre tient le gouvernail, et qu’un troisième jette le filet ou har-\n ponne la poison, et la pêche a un succès impossible sans ce concours.“ (Destutt\n de Tracy l. c.)] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"310\", \"facs\": \"#f0329\"} -->\n\nAuf der einen Seite erweitert die Cooperation die Raumsphäre\n der Arbeit und wird daher für gewisse Arbeitsprozesse schon durch die\n räumliche Continuität des Arbeitsgegenstandes erheischt, wie bei Trocken-\n legung von Land, Eindämmung, Bewässerung, Kanal-, Strassen-, Eisen-\n bahnbauten u. s. w. Andrerseits erlaubt sie, verhältnissmässig zur Stu-\n fenleiter der Produktion, räumliche Kontraktion des Produk-\n tionsgebiets. Diese Beschränkung der Raumsphäre der Arbeit bei\n gleichzeitiger Ausdehnung ihrer Wirkungssphäre, wodurch eine Masse fal-\n scher Kosten (faux frais) erspart werden, entspringt aus der Konglome-\n ration der Arbeiter, dem Zusammenrücken verschiedner Arbeitsprozesse,\n und der Koncentration der Produktionsmittel [Note: „In the progress of culture all, and perhaps more than all the capital and\n labour which once loosely occupied 500 acres, are now concentrated for the more\n complete tillage of 100.“ Obgleich „relatively to the amount of capital and labour\n employed, space is concentrated, it is an enlarged sphere of production,\n as compared to the sphere of production formerly occupied or worked upon by\n one single, independent agent of production.“ (R. Jones: „On Rent.\n Lond. 1831“, p. 191, 199.)] .\n\n\n\n\nVerglichen mit einer gleich grossen Summe vereinzelter individueller\n Arbeitstage, producirt der kombinirte Arbeitstag grössere Massen von Ge-\n brauchswerth und vermindert daher die zur Produktion eines bestimmten\n Nutzeffekts nöthige Arbeitszeit. Ob er im gegebenen Fall diese gestei-\n gerte Produktivkraft erhält, weil er die mechanische Kraftpotenz der\n Arbeit erhöht, oder ihre räumliche Wirkungssphäre ausdehnt, oder das räum-\n liche Produktionsfeld im Verhältniss zur Stufenleiter der Produktion kon-\n trahirt, oder im kritischen Moment viel Arbeit in wenig Zeit flüssig macht,\n oder den Wetteifer der Einzelnen erregt und ihre Lebensgeister spannt,\n oder den gleichartigen Verrichtungen Vieler den Stempel der Konti-\n nuität und Vielseitigkeit aufdrückt, oder verschiedene Operationen\ngleichzeitig verrichtet, oder die Produktionsmittel durch ihren gemein-\n schaftlichen Gebrauch ökonomisirt, oder der individuellen Arbeit den Cha-\n [Note: when it has fallen, and is of course discoloured and partially rotted, so that for\n want of labour at the proper season the cultivator is actually forced to\n submit to the loss of a large part of that crop for which England is so anxiously\n looking.“ (Bengal Hurcuru. Bi-Monthly Overland Summary of\n News. 22nd July 1861.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"311\", \"facs\": \"#f0330\"} --> rakter gesellschaftlicher Durchschnittsarbeit verleiht, unter allen Umstän-\n den ist die spezifische Produktivkraft des kombinirten Arbeits-\n tags gesellschaftliche Produktivkraft der Arbeit oder Pro-\n duktivkraft gesellschaftlicher Arbeit. Sie entspringt aus\n der Cooperation selbst. Im planmässigen Zusammenwirken mit Andern\n streift der Arbeiter seine individuellen Schranken ab und entwickelt sein\n Gattungsvermögen [Note: „La forza di ciascuno uomo è minima, ma la riniunione delle minime\n forze forma una forza totale maggiore anche della somma delle forze medesime\n fino a che le forze per essere riunite possono diminuere il tempo ed accrescere lo\n spazio della loro azione.“ (G. R. Carli l. c. t. XV, p. 176 Note.)] .\n\n\n\n\nWenn Arbeiter überhaupt nicht unmittelbar zusammenwirken können,\n ohne zusammen zu sein, ihre Konglomeration auf bestimmtem Raum daher\n Bedingung ihrer Cooperation ist, können Lohnarbeiter nicht coope-\n riren, ohne dass dasselbe Kapital, derselbe Kapitalist sie\n gleichzeitig anwendet, also ihre Arbeitskräfte gleichzeitig kauft. Der Ge-\n sammtwerth dieser Arbeitskräfte, oder die Lohnsumme der\n Arbeiter für den Tag, die Woche u. s. w., muss daher in der Tasche des\n Kapitalisten vereint sein, bevor die Arbeitskräfte selbst im Produktions-\n prozess vereint werden. Zahlung von 300 Arbeitern auf einmal, auch nur\n für einen Tag, bedingt mehr Kapitalauslage als Zahlung weniger Arbeiter\n Woche für Woche während des ganzen Jahrs. Die Anzahl der cooperirenden\n Arbeiter, oder die Stufenleiter der Cooperation, hängt also zunächst ab von\n der Grösse des Kapitals, das der einzelne Kapitalist im Ankauf von Arbeits-\n kraft auslegen kann, d. h. von dem Umfang, worin je ein Kapi-\n talist über die Lebensmittel vieler Arbeiter verfügt.\n\n\n\n\nUnd wie mit dem variablen, verhält es sich mit dem constan-\n ten Kapital. Die Auslage für Rohmaterial z. B. ist 30 mal grösser für\n den einen Kapitalisten, der 300, als für jeden der 30 Kapitalisten, der je\n 10 Arbeiter beschäftigt. Werthumfang und Stoffmasse der gemeinsam\n benutzten Arbeitsmittel wachsen zwar nicht in demselben Mass wie die\n beschäftigte Arbeiteranzahl, aber sie wachsen beträchtlich. Koncen-\n tration grösserer Massen von Produktionsmitteln in der\n Hand einzelner Kapitalisten ist also materielle Bedingung für\n die Cooperation von Lohnarbeitern, und der Umfang der Cooperation, oder\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"312\", \"facs\": \"#f0331\"} --> die Stufenleiter der Produktion, hängt ab vom Umfang dieser Koncen-\n tration.\n\n\n\n\nUrsprünglich erschien eine gewisse Minimalgrösse des Kapi-\n tals in der Hand des einzelnen Arbeitsanwenders nothwendig, damit die\n Anzahl der gleichzeitig ausgebeuteten Arbeiter, daher die Masse des\n producirten Mehrwerths hinreiche, ihn selbst von der Handarbeit zu entbin-\n den, aus einem Kleinmeister in einen Kapitalisten zu verwandeln und so das\n Kapitalverhältniss formell herzustellen. Sie erscheint jetzt als mate-\n rielle Bedingung für die Verwandlung vieler zersplitterter und von\n einander unabhängiger individueller Arbeitsprozesse in einen kombinirten\n gesellschaftlichen Arbeitsprozess.\n\n\n\n\nEbenso erschien ursprünglich das Kommando des Kapitals über die\n Arbeit nur als formelle Folge davon, dass der Arbeiter, statt für sich,\n für den Kapitalisten und daher unter dem Kapitalisten arbeitet. Mit\n der Cooperation vieler Lohnarbeiter entwickelt sich das Kommando des\n Kapitals zum Erheischniss für die Ausführung des Arbeitsprozesses selbst,\n zu einer wirklichen Produktionsbedingung. Der Befehl des Kapitalisten\n auf dem Produktionsfeld wird jetzt so unentbehrlich wie der Befehl des\n Generals auf dem Schlachtfeld.\n\n\n\n\nAlle unmittelbar gesellschaftliche oder gemeinschaftliche Arbeit auf\n grösserem Massstab braucht mehr oder minder eine Direktion, welche die\n Harmonie der individuellen Thätigkeiten vermittelt und die allgemei-\n nen Funktionen vollzieht, die aus der Bewegung des produktiven\n Gesammtkörpers im Unterschied von der Bewegung seiner selbstständigen\n Organe entspringen. Ein einzelner Violinspieler dirigirt sich selbst, ein\n Orchester bedarf des Musikdirektors. Diese Funktion der Leitung, Ueber-\n wachung und Vermittlung wird zur Funktion des Kapitals, sobald\n die ihm untergeordnete Arbeit cooperativ wird. Als spezifische Funktion\n des Kapitals erhält die Funktion der Leitung spezifische Charaktermale.\n\n\n\n\nZunächst ist das treibende Motiv und der bestimmende Zweck des\n kapitalistischen Produktionsprozesses möglichst grosse Selbstverwer-\n thung des Kapitals [Note: „Profits … is the sole end of trade.“ (J. Vanderlint l. c.\n p. 11.)] , d. h. möglichst grosse Produktion von Mehr-\n werth, also möglichst grosse Ausbeutung der Arbeitskraft durch den Kapi-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"313\", \"facs\": \"#f0332\"} --> talisten. Mit der Masse der gleichzeitig beschäftigten Arbeiter wächst\n ihr Widerstand und damit nothwendig der Druck des Kapitals zur Be-\n wältigung dieses Widerstands. Die Leitung des Kapitalisten ist nicht\n nur eine aus der Natur des gesellschaftlichen Arbeitsprozesses ent-\n springende und ihm angehörige besondere Funktion, sie ist zugleich\nFunktion der Ausbeutung eines gesellschaftlichen Ar-\n beitsprozesses und daher bedingt durch den unvermeidlichen Anta-\n gonismus zwischen dem Ausbeuter und dem Rohmaterial seiner Ausbeu-\n tung. Ebenso wächst mit dem Umfang der Produktionsmittel, die dem\n Lohnarbeiter als fremdes Eigenthum gegenüberstehn, die Nothwendigkeit\n der Kontrole über deren sachgemässe Verwendung [Note: Ein englisches Philisterblatt, der Spectator vom 3. June 1866, be-\n richtet, dass nach Einführung einer Art von Kompagniegeschäft zwischen Kapi-\n talist und Arbeitern in der „wirework company of Manchester“: „the first result\n was a sudden decrease in waste, the men not seeing why they should waste their\n own property any more than any other masters, and waste is perhaps, next to bad\n debts, the greatest source of manufacturing loss.“ Dasselbe Blatt entdeckt als\n Grundmangel der Rochdale cooperative experiments: „They showed that asso-\n ciations of workmen could manage shops, mills, and almost all forms of industry\n with success, and they immensely improved the condition of the men, but then\n they did not leave a clear place for masters.“ („Sie bewiesen, dass\n Arbeiterassociationen Boutiquen, Fabriken und beinahe alle Formen der Industrie\n mit Erfolg handhaben können, und sie verbesserten ausserordentlich die Lage der\n Leute selbst, aber!, aber dann liessen sie keinen sichtbaren Platz für Kapitalisten\n offen.“ Quelle horreur!)] . Die Cooperation\n der Lohnarbeiter ist ferner blosse Wirkung des Kapitals, das sie gleich-\n zeitig anwendet. Der Zusammenhang ihrer Funktionen und ihre Einheit\n als produktiver Gesammtkörper liegen ausser ihnen, im Kapital, das sie\n zusammenbringt und zusammenhält. Der Zusammenhang ihrer Arbeiten tritt\n ihnen daher ideell als Plan des Kapitalisten, ihre eigne Einheit praktisch\n als seine Autorität gegenüber, die Macht des fremden Willens, der\n ihr Thun seinem Zweck unterwirft. Wenn daher die kapitalistische Lei-\n tung dem Inhalt nach zwieschlächtig, wegen der Zwieschlächtigkeit des\n zu leitenden Produktionsprozesses selbst, da er einerseits gesellschaftlicher\n Arbeitsprozess zur Herstellung eines Produkts, andrerseits Verwerthungs-\n prozess des Kapitals ist, so ist sie der Form nach despotisch. Mit der\n Entwicklung der Cooperation auf grösserem Massstab entwickelt dieser\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"314\", \"facs\": \"#f0333\"} --> Despotismus seine eigenthümlichen Formen. Wie der Kapitalist zunächst\n entbunden wird von der Handarbeit, sobald sein Kapital jene Minimalgrösse\n erreicht hat, womit die eigentlich kapitalistische Produktion erst beginnt, so\n tritt er jetzt die Funktion unmittelbarer und fortwährender Beaufsichtigung\n der einzelnen Arbeiter und Arbeitergruppen selbst wieder ab an eine be-\n sondre Sorte von Lohnarbeitern. Wie eine Armee militärischer, bedarf eine\n unter dem Kommando desselben Kapitals zusammenwirkende Arbeitermasse\n industrieller Oberofficiere (Dirigenten, managers) und Unterofficiere (Ar-\n beitsaufseher, foremen, overlookers, contre-maîtres), die während des Ar-\n beitsprozesses selbst im Namen des Kapitals kommandiren. Die Arbeit\n der Oberaufsicht befestigt sich zu ihrer ausschliesslichen Funktion. Bei\n Vergleichung der Produktionsweise unabhängiger Bauern oder selbststän-\n diger Handwerker mit der auf Sklaverei beruhenden Plantagenwirthschaft,\n zählt der politische Oekonom diese Arbeit der Oberaufsicht zu\n den faux frais de production [Note: Nachdem Prof. Cairnes die „superintendence of labour“ als einen\n Hauptcharakter der Sklavenproduktion in den südlichen Staaten von Nordamerika\n dargestellt hat, fährt er fort: „The peasant proprietor (des Nordens) appropria-\n ting the whole produce of his soil, needs no other stimulus to exertion. Super-\n intendence is here completely dispensed with.“ (Cairnes l. c. p. 48, 49.)] . Bei Betrachtung der kapitalistischen\n Produktionsweise identificirt er dagegen die Funktion der Leitung, soweit\n sie aus der Natur des gemeinschaftlichen Arbeitsprozesses entspringt, mit\n derselben Funktion, soweit sie durch den kapitalistischen und daher anta-\n gonistischen Charakter dieses Prozesses bedingt wird [Note: Sir James Steuart, überhaupt ausgezeichnet durch offnes Auge für\n die charakteristisch - gesellschaftlichen Unterschiede verschiedner Produktions-\n weisen, bemerkt: „Pourquoi l’industrie des particuliers est-elle ruinée par des\n grandes entreprises en manufactures, si ce n’est parce que celles-ci se rapprochent\n davantage à la simplicité du régime des esclaves?“ („Princ. of Pol. Econ.“\n Fzs. Uebers. Paris 1789, t. I, p. 308, 309.)] . Der Kapitalist\n ist nicht Kapitalist, weil er industrieller Leiter ist, sondern er wird indu-\n strieller Befehlshaber, weil er Kapitalist ist. Der Oberbefehl in der In-\n dustrie wird Attribut des Kapitals, wie zur Feudalzeit der Oberbefehl in\n Krieg und Gericht Attribut des Grundeigenthums war [Note: Auguste Comte und seine Schule könnten daher in derselben Art\n die ewige Nothwendigkeit von Feudalherrn beweisen, worin sie diess für die Kapi-\n talisten thun. Bei näherer Analyse der „Philosophie Positive“ entdeckt man] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"315\", \"facs\": \"#f0334\"} -->\n\nEigenthümer seiner Arbeitskraft ist der Arbeiter, so lange er als\n Verkäufer derselben mit dem Kapitalisten marktet, und er kann nur ver-\n kaufen, was er besitzt, seine individuelle, vereinzelte Arbeitskraft. Diess\n Verhältniss wird in keiner Weise dadurch verändert, dass der Kapitalist\n 100 Arbeitskräfte statt einer kauft oder mit 100 von einander unab-\n hängigen Arbeitern Kontrakte schliesst statt mit einem einzelnen. Er\n kann die 100 Arbeiter anwenden ohne sie cooperiren zu lassen. Der\n Kapitalist zahlt daher den Werth der 100 selbstständigen Arbeitskräfte,\n aber er zahlt nicht die kombinirte Arbeitskraft der Hundert. Als unab-\n hängige Personen sind die Arbeiter Vereinzelte, die in ein Verhält-\n niss zu demselben Kapital, aber nicht zu einander treten. Ihre Coopera-\n tion beginnt erst im Arbeitsprozess, aber im Arbeitsprozess haben sie be-\n reits aufgehört sich selbst zu gehören. Mit dem Eintritt in denselben sind\n sie dem Kapital einverleibt. Als Cooperirende, als Glieder eines werk-\n thätigen Organismus, sind sie selbst nur eine besondre Existenzweise des\n Kapitals. Die Produktivkraft, die der Arbeiter als gesellschaft-\n licher Arbeiter entwickelt, ist daher Produktivkraft des Kapi-\n tals. Die gesellschaftliche Produktivkraft der Arbeit ent-\n wickelt sich unentgeldlich, sobald die Arbeiter unter bestimmte Bedingungen\n gestellt sind, und das Kapital stellt sie unter diese Bedingungen. Weil\n die gesellschaftliche Produktivkraft der Arbeit dem Kapi-\n tal nichts kostet, weil sie andrerseits nicht von dem Arbeiter entwickelt\n wird, bevor seine Arbeit selbst dem Kapital gehört, erscheint sie als Pro-\n duktivkraft, die das Kapital von Natur besitzt, als seine immanente\n Produktivkraft.\n\n\n\n\nKolossal zeigt sich die Wirkung der einfachen Cooperation in den\n Riesenwerken der alten Asiaten, Aegypter, Etrusker u. s. w. „Es ge-\n schah in vergangnen Zeiten, dass diese asiatischen Staaten nach Bestrei-\n tung ihrer Civil- und Militairausgaben, sich im Besitz eines Ueberschusses\n von Lebensmitteln befanden, die sie für Werke der Pracht und des Nutzens\n verausgaben konnten. Ihr Kommando über die Hände und Arme fast der\n [Note: überhaupt, dass sie trotz aller scheinbaren Freigeisterei mit ihren Wurzeln tief in\n katholischer Erde steckt. Die Methode encyklopädischer Zusammenfassung gab\n dem A. Comte den Umlauf in Frankreich. Verglichen mit Hegel’s „Encyklo-\n pädie“, die ungefähr 1½ Decennien früher erschien, ist die Comte’sche Kom\n bination eine Schülerarbeit von bloss lokaler Bedeutung.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"316\", \"facs\": \"#f0335\"} --> ganzen nicht ackerbauenden Bevölkerung und die ausschliessliche Ver-\n fügung des Monarchen und der Priesterschaft über jenen Ueberschuss boten\n ihnen die Mittel zur Errichtung jener mächtigen Monumente, womit sie\n das Land erfüllten … In der Bewegung der kolossalen Statuen und der\n enormen Massen, deren Transport Staunen erregt, wurde fast nur mensch-\n liche Arbeit verschwenderisch angewandt. Die Zahl der Arbeiter\n und die Koncentration ihrer Mühen genügte. So sehn wir\n mächtige Korallenriffe aus den Tiefen des Oceaus zu Inseln anschwellen\n und festes Land bilden, obgleich jeder individuelle Ablagerer (depositary)\n winzig, schwach und verächtlich ist. Die nicht ackerbauenden Arbeiter\n einer asiatischen Monarchie haben ausser ihren individuellen körperlichen\n Bemühungen wenig zum Werk zu bringen, aber ihre Zahl ist ihre Kraft,\n und die Macht der Direktion über diese Massen gab jenen Riesenwerken\n den Ursprung. Es war die Koncentration der Revenüen, wovon die Ar-\n beiter leben, in eine Hand oder wenige Hände, welche solche Unternehmungen\n möglich machte“ [Note: R. Jones: Textbook of Lectures etc., p. 77, 78. Die altassy-\n rischen, ägyptischen u. s. w. Sammlungen in London und andern europäischen\n Hauptstädten machen uns zu Augenzeugen jener cooperativen Arbeitsprozesse.] . Diese Macht asiatischer und ägyptischer Könige\n oder etruskischer Theokraten u. s. w. ist in der modernen Gesellschaft\n auf den Kapitalisten übergegangen, ob er nun als vereinzelter Kapitalist\n auftritt, oder, wie bei Aktiengesellschaften, als kombinirter Kapitalist.\n\n\n\n\nDie Cooperation im Arbeitsprozess, wie wir sie in den Kulturan-\n fängen der Menschheit, bei Jägervölkern [Note: Linguet in seiner „Théorie des Lois civiles“ hat vielleicht\n nicht Unrecht, wenn er die Jagd für die erste Form der Cooperation, und\nMenschenjagd (Krieg) für eine der ersten Formen der Jagd erklärt.]  oder etwa in der Agrikultur\n indischer Gemeinwesen vorherrschend finden, beruht einerseits auf dem\nGemeineigenthum an den Produktionsbedingungen, an-\n drerseits darauf, dass das einzelne Individuum sich von der Nabelschnur\n des Stammes oder des Gemeinwesens noch ebensowenig losgerissen hat,\n wie das Bienenindividuum vom Bienenstock. Beides unterscheidet sie\n von der kapitalistischen Cooperation. Die sporadische Anwendung der\n Cooperation auf grossem Massstab in der antiken Welt, dem Mittelalter\n und den modernen Kolonien, beruht auf unmittelbaren Herrschafts-\n und Knechtschafts-Verhältnissen, zumeist auf der Sklaverei. Die kapita-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"317\", \"facs\": \"#f0336\"} --> listische Form setzt dagegen von vornherein den freien Lohnarbeiter vor-\n aus, der seine Arbeitskraft dem Kapital verkauft. Historisch jedoch ent-\n wickelt sie sich im Gegensatz zur Bauernwirthschaft und zum unab-\n hängigen Handwerksbetrieb, ob dieser zünftige Form besitze oder nicht [Note: Die kleine Bauernwirthschaft und der unabhängige Handwerksbetrieb,\n die beide theils die Basis der feudalen Produktionsweise bilden, theils nach deren\n Auflösung neben dem kapitalistischen Betrieb erscheinen, bilden zugleich die\n ökonomische Grundlage der klassischen Gemeinwesen zu ihrer besten Zeit, nach-\n dem sich das ursprünglich orientalische Gemeineigenthum aufgelöst, und bevor\n sich die Sklaverei der Produktion ernsthaft bemächtigt hat.] .\n Ihnen gegenüber erscheint die kapitalistische Cooperation nicht als eine\n besondre historische Form der Cooperation, sondern die\n Cooperation selbst als eine dem kapitalistischen Produk-\n tionsprozess eigenthümliche und ihn spezifisch unter-\n scheidende historische Form.\n\n\n\n\nWie die durch die Cooperation entwickelte gesellschaftliche\n Produktivkraft der Arbeit als Produktivkraft des Kapi-\n tals erscheint, so die Cooperation selbst als eine spezifische\n Form des kapitalistischen Produktionsprozesses im\n Gegensatz zum Produktionsprozess vereinzelter unabhängiger Arbeiter oder\n auch Kleinmeister. Es ist die erste Aenderung, welche der wirk-\n liche Arbeitsprozess durch seine Subsumtion unter das Kapital\n erfährt. Diese Aenderung geht naturwüchsig vor sich. Ihre Voraus-\n setzung, gleichzeitige Beschäftigung einer grösseren Anzahl von Lohnarbei-\n tern in demselben Arbeitsprozess, bildet den Ausgangspunkt der kapitalisti-\n schen Produktion. Dieser fällt mit dem Dasein des Kapitals selbst zusam-\n men. Wenn sich die kapitalistische Produktionsweise daher einerseits als\nhistorische Nothwendigkeit für die Verwandlung des Arbeitsprozesses\n in einen gesellschaftlichen Prozess darstellt, so andrerseits diese gesellschaft-\n liche Form des Arbeitsprozesses als eine vom Kapital angewandte Me-\n thode, um ihn durch Steigerung seiner Produktivkraft profitlicher aus-\n zubeuten.\n\n\n\n\nIn ihrer bisher betrachteten einfachen Gestalt fällt die Cooperation\n zusammen mit der Produktion auf grösserer Stufenleiter,\n bildet aber keine feste, charakteristische Form einer besondern Ent-\n wicklungs epoche der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise. Höchstens er-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"318\", \"facs\": \"#f0337\"} --> scheint sie annähernd so in den noch handwerksmässigen Anfängen der\n Manufaktur [Note: „Whether the united skill, industry and emulation of many together on\n the same work be not the way to advance it? And whether it had been otherwise\n possible for England, to have carried on her Woollen Manufacture to so great a\n perfection?“ (Berkeley: „The Querist.“ Lond. 1750, p. 521.)]  und in jener Art grosser Agrikultur, welche der Manufaktur-\n periode entspricht, und sich wesentlich nur durch die Masse der gleich-\n zeitig angewandten Arbeiter und den Umfang der koncentrirten Produk-\n tionsmittel von der Bauernwirthschaft unterscheidet. Die einfache Coope-\n ration ist stets noch vorherrschende Form solcher Produktionszweige, worin\n das Kapital auf grosser Stufenleiter operirt, ohne dass Theilung der\n Arbeit oder Maschinerie eine bedeutende Rolle spielte.\n\n\n\n\nDie Cooperation bleibt die Grundform der kapitalistischen\n Produktionsweise, obgleich ihre einfache Gestalt selbst als be-\n sondere Form neben ihren weiter entwickelten Formen erscheint.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0001","text":"Die kapitalistische Produktion, wie wir sahen, beginnt in der That erst,\n wo dasselbe individuelle Kapital eine grössere Anzahl Arbeiter gleichzeitig\n beschäftigt, der Arbeitsprozess also seinen Umfang erweitert und Produkt\n auf grösserer quantitativer Stufenleiter liefert. Nur wo die Arbeiter-\n anzahl hinreicht, damit die von ihr producirte Masse von Mehrwerth den\n Arbeitsanwender selbst von der Arbeit entbinde, wird der letztere vollbür-\n tiger Kapitalist. Das Wirken einer grössern Arbeiteranzahl zur selben Zeit,\n in demselben Raum (oder, wenn man will, auf demselben Arbeitsfeld), zur\n Produktion derselben Waarensorte, unter dem Kommando desselben Kapita-\n listen, bildet daher historisch und begrifflich den Ausgangspunkt der\n kapitalistischen Produktion. Mit Bezug auf die Produktions-\n weise selbst unterscheidet sich z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"12.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0002","text":"die Manufaktur in ihren An-\n fängen kaum anders von der zünftigen Handwerksindustrie als durch die\n grössere Zahl der gleichzeitig von demselben Kapital beschäftigten Arbei-\n ter. Die Werkstatt des Zunftmeisters ist nur erweitert.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"12.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0003","text":"Der Unterschied ist also zunächst bloss quantitativ. Man sah,\n dass die Masse des Mehrwerths, welche ein gegebnes Kapital produ-\n cirt, gleich dem Mehrwerth, den der einzelne Arbeiter liefert, multiplicirt mit\n der Anzahl der gleichzeitig beschäftigten Arbeiter. Diese Anzahl ändert\n an und für sich nichts an der Rate des Mehrwerths oder dem Ex-\n ploitationsgrad der Arbeitskraft. Was aber die Produktion von Waaren-\n werth überhaupt betrifft, so scheint für sie selbst jede qualitative Ver-\n änderung des Arbeitsprozesses gleichgültig. Es folgt diess aus der Natur des\n Tauschwerths, der nichts ist als ein bestimmtes Quantum vergegen-\n ständlichter Arbeit. Vergegenständlicht sich ein zwölfstündiger Arbeits-\n tag in 6 sh., so 1200 solcher Arbeitstage in 6 sh. × 1200. In dem\n einen Fall haben sich 12 × 1200, in dem andern 12 Arbeitsstunden den\n Produkten einverleibt.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"12.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0004","text":"In der Werthproduktion zählen Viele immer nur\n als viele Einzelne. Für die Werthproduktion macht es also keinen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"303\", \"facs\": \"#f0322\"} -->qualitativen Unterschied, ob 1200 Arbeiter vereinzelt oder ob sie\n unter dem Kommando desselben Kapitals vereint produciren.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"12.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0005","text":"Indess findet doch innerhalb gewisser Grenzen eine Modifikation statt.\n Die im Werth vergegenständlichte Arbeit ist Arbeit von gesellschaft-\n licher Durchschnittsqualität und so ist der Werth der Arbeitskraft der\n Werth durchschnittlicher Arbeitskraft. Eine Durchschnittsgrösse existirt\n aber immer nur als Durchschnitt vieler verschiedner Grössenindividuen der-\n selben Art. In jedem Industriezweig weicht der individuelle Arbeiter,\n Peter oder Paul, mehr oder minder vom Durchschnittsarbeiter ab. Diese\n individuellen Abweichungen oder was man mathematisch „Irrthümer“\n nennt, kompensiren sich und verschwinden, sobald man eine grössere An-\n zahl Arbeiter zusammennimmt.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"12.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0006","text":"Der berühmte Sophist und Sykophant\nEdmund Burke will aus seinen praktischen Erfahrungen als Pächter\n sogar wissen, dass schon „für ein so geringes Peloton“ wie 5 Acker-\n knechte aller individuelle Unterschied der Arbeit verschwindet, also die\n ersten besten im Mannesalter befindlichen fünf englischen Ackerknechte\n zusammengenommen in derselben Zeit grad so viel Arbeit verrichten als\n beliebige andre fünf englische Ackerknechte [Note: „Unquestionably, there is a great deal of difference between the value of\n one man’s labour and that of another, from strength, dexterity and honest appli-\n cation.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"12.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0007","text":"But I am quite sure, from my best observation, that any given five\n men will in their total, afford a proportion of labour equal to any other five\n within the periods of life I have stated; that is, that among such five men there\n will be one possessing all the qualifications of a good workman, one bad, and the\n other three middling, and approximating to the first and the last. So that in so\n small a platoon as that of even five, you will find the full complement of all that\n five men can earn.“ (E. Burke l. c. p. 16.) cf. Quételet über das Durch-\n schnittsindividuum.] . Die von ihm angegebne\n Zahl ist hier gleichgültig, aber es ist klar, dass der Gesammtarbeitstag\n einer grössern Anzahl gleichzeitig beschäftigter Arbeiter an und für sich\n ein Tag gesellschaftlicher Durchschnittsarbeit ist. Der\n Arbeitstag des Einzelnen sei z. B. zwölfstündig.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"12.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0008","text":"So bildet der Arbeitstag\n von 12 gleichzeitig beschäftigten Arbeitern einen Gesammtarbeitstag von\n 144 Stunden, und obgleich die Arbeit eines Jeden des Dutzend mehr oder\n minder von der gesellschaftlichen Durchschnittsarbeit abweichen, der Ein-\n zelne daher etwas mehr oder weniger Zeit zu derselben Verrichtung brau-\n chen mag, besitzt der Arbeitstag jedes Einzelnen als ein Zwölftel des Ge-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"304\", \"facs\": \"#f0323\"} --> sammtarbeitstags von 144 Stunden die gesellschaftliche Durchschnittsqualität.\n Für den Kapitalisten aber, der ein Dutzend beschäftigt, existirt der Arbeitstag\n als Gesammtarbeitstag des Dutzend.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"12.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0009","text":"Der Arbeitstag jedes Einzelnen existirt\n als aliquoter Theil des Gesammtarbeitstags, ganz unabhängig davon, ob\n die Zwölf einander in die Hand arbeiten oder ob der ganze Zusammenhang\n ihrer Arbeiten nur darin besteht, dass sie für denselben Kapitalisten ar-\n beiten. Werden dagegen von den 12 Arbeitern je zwei von einem klei-\n nen Meister beschäftigt, so wird es zufällig, ob jeder einzelne Meister die-\n selbe Werthmasse producirt und daher die allgemeine Rate des Mehrwerths\n realisirt. Es fänden individuelle Abweichungen statt.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"12.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0010","text":"Verbrauchte ein\n Arbeiter bedeutend mehr Zeit in der Produktion einer Waare als gesell-\n schaftlich erheischt ist, wiche die für ihn individuell nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit bedeutend ab von der gesellschaftlich nothwendigen oder der Durch-\n schnitts-Arbeitszeit, so gälte seine Arbeit nicht als Durchschnittsarbeit,\n seine Arbeitskraft nicht als durchschnittliche Arbeitskraft. Sie verkaufte sich\n gar nicht oder nur unter dem Durchschnittswerth der Arbeitskraft. Ein\n bestimmtes Minimum der Arbeitsfertigkeit ist also vorausgesetzt, und wir\n werden später sehn, dass die kapitalistische Produktion Mittel findet, diess\n Minimum zu messen. Nichts desto weniger weicht das Minimum vom\n Durchschnitt ab, obgleich auf der andern Seite der Durchschnittswerth\n der Arbeitskraft gezahlt werden muss.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"12.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0011","text":"Von den sechs Kleinmeistern\n würde der eine daher mehr, der andre weniger als die allgemeine Rate\n des Mehrwerths herausschlagen. Die Ungleichheiten würden sich für die\n Gesellschaft kompensiren, aber nicht für den einzelnen Meister. Das Ge-\n setz der Verwerthung überhaupt realisirt sich also für den einzelnen\n Produzenten erst vollständig, sobald er als Kapitalist producirt, viele Ar-\n beiter gleichzeitig anwendet, also von vorn herein gesellschaftliche\n Durchschnittsarbeit in Bewegung setzt [Note: Herr Professor Roscher will entdeckt haben, dass eine Nähmamsell, die\n während zwei Tagen von der Frau Professorin beschäftigt wird, mehr Arbeit liefert,\n als zwei Nähmamsellen, welche die Frau Professorin am selben Tage beschäftigt.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"12.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0012","text":"Der Herr Professor stelle seine Beobachtungen über den kapitalistischen Produk-\n tionsprozess nicht in der Kinderstube an, und nicht unter Umständen, worin die\n Hauptperson fehlt, der Kapitalist.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"12.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0013","text":"Auch bei gleichbleibender Arbeitsweise bewirkt die gleich-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"305\", \"facs\": \"#f0324\"} --> zeitige Anwendung einer grösseren Arbeiteranzahl eine Revolution in den\n gegenständlichen Bedingungen des Arbeitsprozesses.\n Die Baulichkeiten, worin die Vielen arbeiten, die Lager für Rohmaterial,\n halbfertige Waaren u. s. w., die Gefässe, Instrumente, Apparate u. s. w.,\n die von vielen gleichzeitig oder abwechselnd gebraucht werden können,\n kurz ein Theil der Produktionsmittel wird jetzt gemeinsam\n im Arbeitsprozess konsumirt. Einerseits wird der Tauschwerth\n von Waaren, also auch von Produktionsmitteln, durchaus nicht erhöht\n durch irgend welche erhöhte Ausbeutung ihres Gebrauchswerths.\n Andrerseits wächst zwar der Massstab der gemeinsam gebrauchten Pro-\n duktionsmittel.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"12.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0014","text":"Ein Zimmer, worin 20 Weber mit ihren 20 Webstühlen\n arbeiten, muss weiter gestreckt sein als das Zimmer eines unabhängigen\n Webers mit zwei Gesellen. Aber die Produktion einer Werkstatt für 20\n Personen kostet weniger Arbeit als die von 10 Werkstätten für je zwei Per-\n sonen, und so wächst überhaupt der Werth massenweise koncentrirter\n und gemeinsamer Produktionsmittel nicht verhältnissmässig mit ihrem\n Umfang und ihrem Nutzeffekt. Gemeinsam vernutzte Produktionsmittel\n geben geringeren Werthbestandtheil an das einzelne Produkt ab, theils weil\n der Gesammtwerth, den sie abgeben, sich gleichzeitig auf eine grössere\n Produktenmasse vertheilt, theils weil sie in Folge ihres vergrösserten Um-\n fangs zwar mit absolut grösserem, aber, im Verhältniss zu ihrem Wir-\n kungskreis, mit relativ kleinerem Werth als vereinzelte Produktionsmittel\n in den Produktionsprozess eintreten.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"12.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0015","text":"Damit sinkt ein Werthbestand-\n theil, welcher constantes Kapital ersetzt, also, proportionell zur Grösse dieses\n Bestandtheils, auch der Gesammtwerth der Waare. Die Wirkung ist die-\n selbe, als hätte sich die Produktivkraft der Arbeit vermehrt in solchen\n Industriezweigen, die Produktionsmittel liefern. Diese Oekonomie in\n der Anwendung der Produktionsmittel entspringt nur aus\n ihrem gemeinsamen Konsum im Arbeitsprozess Vieler.\n Und sie erhalten diesen Charakter als Bedingungen gesellschaftli-\n cher Arbeit oder gesellschaftliche Bedingungen der Ar-\n beit im Unterschied von den zersplitterten und relativ theureren Pro-\n duktionsbedingungen vereinzelter selbstständiger Arbeiter oder Kleinmei-\n ster, selbst wenn die Vielen nur räumlich zusammen, nicht mit einander\n arbeiten.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"12.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0016","text":"Ein Theil der Arbeitsmittel erwirbt diesen gesellschaft-\n lichen Charakter, bevor ihn der Arbeitsprozess selbst erwirbt.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"12.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0017","text":"[Running matter: I. 20] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"306\", \"facs\": \"#f0325\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"12.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0018","text":"Die Oekonomie der Produktionsmittel ist überhaupt von\n doppeltem Gesichtspunkt zu betrachten. Das einemal, so weit sie Waa-\n ren verwohlfeilert und dadurch den Werth der Arbeitskraft senkt.\n Das andremal, so weit sie das Verhältniss des Mehrwerths zum\n vorgeschossnen Gesammtkapital, d. h. zur Werthsumme\n seiner constanten und variablen Bestandtheile, verändert. Der letztere\n Punkt wird erst im dritten Buch dieses Werks erörtert, wohin wir des Zu-\n sammenhangs wegen auch manches schon hierher gehörige verweisen.\n Einerseits gebietet der Gang der Analyse diese Zerreissung des Gegenstands.\n Andrerseits entspricht sie dem Geist der kapitalistischen Produktion.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"12.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0019","text":"Da\n nämlich die Arbeitsbedingungen hier dem Arbeiter selbststäudig gegenüber-\n treten, erscheint auch ihre Oekonomie als eine besondere Operation, die\n ihn nichts angeht und daher getrennt ist von den Methoden, wodurch die\n Produktivität der vom Kapital konsumirten Arbeitskraft erhöht wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"12.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0020","text":"Die Form der Arbeit Vieler, die in demselben Produk-\n tionsprozess oder in verschiedenen, aber zusammenhängenden Pro-\n duktionsprozessen, planmässig neben und mit einander arbeiten, heisst\nCooperation [Note: „Concours de forces.“ (Destutt de Tracy l. c. p. 78.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"12.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0021","text":"Wie die Angriffskraft einer Kavalerieschwadron oder die Widerstands-\n kraft eines Infanterieregiments wesentlich verschieden ist von der Summe\n der vereinzelten Angriffs- und Widerstandskräfte, welche jeder Kavalerist\n und Infanterist für sich entwickeln könnte, so die mechanische\n Kraftsumme vereinzelter Arbeiter von der mechanischen Kraftpotenz,\n die sich entwickelt, wenn viele Hände gleichzeitig in derselben\n ungetheilten Operation zusammenwirken, z. B. wenn es gilt eine\n Last zu heben, eine Kurbel zu drehn oder einen Widerstand aus dem Weg\n zu räumen [Note: „There are numerous operations of so simple a kind as not to admit a\n division into parts, which cannot be performed without the cooperation of many\n pairs of hands.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"12.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0022","text":"For instance the lifting of a large tree on a wain … every\n thing in short, which cannot be done unless a great many pairs of hands help each\n other in the same undivided employment, and at the same time.“ (E. G. Wake-\n field: „A View of the Art of Colonization. Lond. 1849“, p. 168.)] . Die Wirkung der kombinirten Arbeit könnte hier von der\n vereinzelten gar nicht oder nur in viel längeren Zeiträumen oder nur auf\n einem Zwergmassstab hervorgebracht werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"12.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0023","text":"Es handelt sich hier nicht\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"307\", \"facs\": \"#f0326\"} --> nur um Erhöhung der individuellen Produktivkraft durch die Cooperation,\n sondern um die Schöpfung einer Produktivkraft, die an und für sich Mas-\n senkraft ist [Note: „As one man cannot, and 10 men must strain, to lift a tun of weight,\n yet one hundred men can do it only by the strength of a finger of each of them.“\n (John Bellers: „Proposals for raising a colledge of industry.\n Lond. 1696“, p. 21.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"12.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0024","text":"Abgesehn von der neuen mechanischen Kraftpotenz, die aus der Ver-\n schmelzung vieler Kräfte in eine Gesammtkraft entspringt, erzeugt bei\n den meisten produktiven Arbeiten der blosse gesellschaftliche\n Kontakt einen Wetteifer und eine eigne Erregung der Lebensgei-\n ster (animal spirit), welche die individuelle Leistungsfähigkeit der Ein-\n zelnen erhöhen, so dass ein Dutzend Personen zusammen in einem gleich-\n zeitigen Arbeitstag von 144 Stunden ein viel grösseres Gesammtprodukt\n liefern als zwölf vereinzelte Arbeiter, von denen jeder 12 Stunden, oder\n als ein Arbeiter, der 12 Tage nach einander arbeitet [Note: „There is also“ (wenn dieselbe Arbeiterzahl von einem Pächter auf\n 300, statt von 10 Pächtern auf je 30 acres angewandt wird) „an advantage in the\n proportion of servants, which will not easily be understood but by practical men;\n for it is natural to say, as 1 is to 4, so are","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"12.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0025","text":"3:12: but this will not hold good in\n practice; for in harvest-time and many other operations which require that kind\n of despatch, by the throwing many hands together, the work is better,\n and more expeditiously done: f. i., in harvest, 2 drivers, 2 loaders, 2 pitchers,\n 2 rakers, and the rest at the rick, or in the barn, will despatch double the work,\n that the same number of hands would do, if divided into different gangs, on\n different farms.“ („An Enquiry into the Connection between the\n present price of provisions and the size of farms. By a Farmer.\n Lond. 1773“, p. 7, 8)] . Diess rührt daher,\n dass der Mensch von Natur, wenn nicht, wie Aristoteles meint, ein politi-\n sches [Note: Aristoteles’ Definition ist eigentlich die, dass der Mensch von Natur Stadt-\n bürger.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"12.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0026","text":"Sie ist ebenso charakteristisch für das klassische Alterthum, als Franklin’s\n Definition, dass der Mensch von Natur Instrumentenmacher, für das Yankeethum.] , jedenfalls ein gesellschaftliches Thier ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"12.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0027","text":"Obgleich Viele Dasselbe oder Gleichartiges gleichzeitig mit\n einander verrichten, kann die individuelle Arbeit eines Jeden dennoch als\n Theil der Gesammtarbeit verschiedne Phasen des Arbeitspro-\n zesses selbst darstellen, die der Arbeitsgegenstand, in Folge der Coo-\n [Running matter: 20*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"308\", \"facs\": \"#f0327\"} --> peration, rascher durchläuft. Z.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"12.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0028","text":"B., wenn Maurer eine Reihe von Händen\n bilden, um Bausteine vom Fuss eines Gestells bis zu seiner Spitze zu be-\n fördern, thut jeder von ihnen dasselbe, aber dennoch bilden die einzelnen\n Verrichtungen continuirliche Theile einer Gesammtverrichtung, besondre\n Phasen, die jeder Baustein im Arbeitsprozess durchlaufen muss, und wo-\n durch ihn etwa die 24 Hände des Gesammtarbeiters rascher befördern,\n als die zwei Hände jedes einzelnen Arbeiters, der das Gerüst auf- und ab-\n stiege [Note: „On doit encore remarquer que cette division partielle du travail peut se\n faire quand même les ouvriers sont occupés d’une même bésogne.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"12.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0029","text":"Des maçons par\n exemple, occupés de faire passer de mains en mains des briques à un échafaudage\n supérieur, font tous la même bésogne, et pourtant il existe parmi eux une espèce\n de division de travail, qui consiste en ce que chacun d’eux fait passer la brique par\n un espace donné, et que tous ensemble la font parvenir beaucoup plus prompte-\n ment à l’endroit marqué qu’ils ne feraient si chacun d’eux portait sa brique sépa-\n rement jusqu’à l’échafaudage supérieur.“ (F. Skarbek: „Théorie des\n richesses sociales. 2ème ed. Paris 1840,“ t. I, p. 97, 98.)] . Der Arbeitsgegenstand durchläuft denselben\n Raum in kürzerer Zeit. Andrerseits findet Kombination der Arbeit\n statt, wenn ein Bau z. B. von verschiednen Seiten gleichzeitig ange-\n griffen wird, obgleich die Cooperirenden Dasselbe oder Gleicharti-\n ges thun.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"12.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0030","text":"Der kombinirte Arbeitstag von 144 Stunden, der den Arbeits-\n gegenstand vielseitig im Raum angreift, weil der kombinirte Arbeiter\n oder Gesammtarbeiter vorn und hinten Augen und Hände hat und\n in gewissem Grad Allgegenwart besitzt, fördert das Gesammtprodukt\n rascher als 12 zwölfstündige Arbeitstage mehr oder minder vereinzelter\n Arbeiter, die ihr Werk einseitiger angreifen müssen. In derselben\n Zeit reifen verschiedne Raumtheile des Produkts.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"12.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0031","text":"Wir betonten hier, dass die Vielen, die einander ergänzen, Das-\n selbe oder Gleichartiges thun, weil diese einfache Form der\n Cooperation, wie man später sehn wird, auch in ihrer ausgebildetsten\n Gestalt eine grosse Rolle spielt. Ist der Arbeitsprozess komplicirt,\n so erlaubt die blosse Masse der Zusammenarbeitenden die verschiede-\n nen Operationen unter verschiedne Hände zu vertheilen, daher gleich-\n zeitig zu verrichten, und dadurch die zur Herstellung des Gesammtpro-\n dukts nöthige Arbeitszeit zu verkürzen [Note: „Est-il question d’exécuter un travail compliqué? plusieurs choses doivent] .","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"12.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0032","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"309\", \"facs\": \"#f0328\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"12.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0033","text":"In vielen Produktionssphären giebt es kritische Momente, d. h.\n durch die Natur des Arbeitsprozesses selbst bestimmte Zeitepochen, wäh-\n rend deren bestimmte Arbeitsresultate erzielt werden müssen. Soll z. B.\n eine Heerde Schafe geschoren oder eine Morgenanzahl Kornland gemäht\n und geherbstet werden, so hängt Quantität und Qualität des Produkts da-\n von ab, dass die Operation zu einer gewissen Zeit begonnen und zu einer\n gewissen Zeit beendet wird. Der Zeitraum, den der Arbeitsprozess\n einnehmen darf, ist hier vorgeschrieben, wie etwa beim Häringsfang. Der\n Einzelne kann aus einem Tag nur einen Arbeitstag herausschnei-\n den, sage von 12 Stunden, aber die Cooperation von 100 z. B. erwei-\n tert einen zwölfstündigen Tag zu einem Arbeitstag von\n 1200 Stunden.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"12.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0034","text":"Die Kürze der Arbeitsfrist wird kompensirt durch\n die Grösse der Arbeitsmasse, die im entscheidenden Augenblick auf das\n Produktionsfeld geworfen wird. Die rechtzeitige Wirkung hängt\n hier ab von der gleichzeitigen Anwendung vieler kombinirten Ar-\n beitstage, der Umfang des Nutzeffekts von der Arbeiteranzahl,\n die jedoch stets kleiner bleibt als die Anzahl der Arbeiter, die vereinzelt\n in demselben Zeitraum denselben Wirkungsraum ausfüllen würden [Note: „The doing of it (der Arbeit in der Agrikultur) at the critical junc-\n ture. is of so much the greater consequence.“ (An Inquiry into the Con-\n nection between the present price etc., p. 9.) „In der Agrikultur giebt\n es keinen wichtigeren Faktor, als den Faktor der Zeit.“ (Liebig: „Ueber\n Theorie und Praxis in der Landwirthschaft. 1856“, p. 23.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"12.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0035","text":"Es ist der Mangel dieser Cooperation, wodurch im Westen der Vereinigten\n Staaten eine Masse Korn, und in den Theilen Ostindiens, wo englische\n Herrschaft das alte Gemeinwesen zerstört hat, eine Masse Baumwolle jähr-\n lich verwüstet wird [Note: „The next evil is one which one would scarcely expect to find in a coun-\n try which exports more labour than any other in the world, with the exception\n perhaps of China and England — the impossibility of procuring a sufficient number\n of hands to clean the cotton. The consequence of this is that large quantities of\n the crop are left unpicked, while another portion is gathered from the ground,] .","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"12.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0036","text":"[Note: être fait simultanément. L’un en fait une pendant que l’autre en fait une autre,\n et tous contribuent à l’effet qu’un seul homme n’aurait pu produire. L’un rame\n pendant que l’autre tient le gouvernail, et qu’un troisième jette le filet ou har-\n ponne la poison, et la pêche a un succès impossible sans ce concours.“ (Destutt\n de Tracy l. c.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"12.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0037","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"310\", \"facs\": \"#f0329\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"12.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0038","text":"Auf der einen Seite erweitert die Cooperation die Raumsphäre\n der Arbeit und wird daher für gewisse Arbeitsprozesse schon durch die\n räumliche Continuität des Arbeitsgegenstandes erheischt, wie bei Trocken-\n legung von Land, Eindämmung, Bewässerung, Kanal-, Strassen-, Eisen-\n bahnbauten u. s. w. Andrerseits erlaubt sie, verhältnissmässig zur Stu-\n fenleiter der Produktion, räumliche Kontraktion des Produk-\n tionsgebiets.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"12.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0039","text":"Diese Beschränkung der Raumsphäre der Arbeit bei\n gleichzeitiger Ausdehnung ihrer Wirkungssphäre, wodurch eine Masse fal-\n scher Kosten (faux frais) erspart werden, entspringt aus der Konglome-\n ration der Arbeiter, dem Zusammenrücken verschiedner Arbeitsprozesse,\n und der Koncentration der Produktionsmittel [Note: „In the progress of culture all, and perhaps more than all the capital and\n labour which once loosely occupied 500 acres, are now concentrated for the more\n complete tillage of 100.“ Obgleich „relatively to the amount of capital and labour\n employed, space is concentrated, it is an enlarged sphere of production,\n as compared to the sphere of production formerly occupied or worked upon by\n one single, independent agent of production.“ (R. Jones: „On Rent.\n Lond. 1831“, p. 191, 199.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"12.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0040","text":"Verglichen mit einer gleich grossen Summe vereinzelter individueller\n Arbeitstage, producirt der kombinirte Arbeitstag grössere Massen von Ge-\n brauchswerth und vermindert daher die zur Produktion eines bestimmten\n Nutzeffekts nöthige Arbeitszeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"12.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0041","text":"Ob er im gegebenen Fall diese gestei-\n gerte Produktivkraft erhält, weil er die mechanische Kraftpotenz der\n Arbeit erhöht, oder ihre räumliche Wirkungssphäre ausdehnt, oder das räum-\n liche Produktionsfeld im Verhältniss zur Stufenleiter der Produktion kon-\n trahirt, oder im kritischen Moment viel Arbeit in wenig Zeit flüssig macht,\n oder den Wetteifer der Einzelnen erregt und ihre Lebensgeister spannt,\n oder den gleichartigen Verrichtungen Vieler den Stempel der Konti-\n nuität und Vielseitigkeit aufdrückt, oder verschiedene Operationen\ngleichzeitig verrichtet, oder die Produktionsmittel durch ihren gemein-\n schaftlichen Gebrauch ökonomisirt, oder der individuellen Arbeit den Cha-\n [Note: when it has fallen, and is of course discoloured and partially rotted, so that for\n want of labour at the proper season the cultivator is actually forced to\n submit to the loss of a large part of that","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"12.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0042","text":"crop for which England is so anxiously\n looking.“ (Bengal Hurcuru. Bi-Monthly Overland Summary of\n News. 22nd July 1861.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"311\", \"facs\": \"#f0330\"} --> rakter gesellschaftlicher Durchschnittsarbeit verleiht, unter allen Umstän-\n den ist die spezifische Produktivkraft des kombinirten Arbeits-\n tags gesellschaftliche Produktivkraft der Arbeit oder Pro-\n duktivkraft gesellschaftlicher Arbeit. Sie entspringt aus\n der Cooperation selbst. Im planmässigen Zusammenwirken mit Andern\n streift der Arbeiter seine individuellen Schranken ab und entwickelt sein\n Gattungsvermögen [Note: „La forza di ciascuno uomo è minima, ma la riniunione delle minime\n forze forma una forza totale maggiore anche della somma delle forze medesime\n fino a che le forze per essere riunite possono diminuere il tempo ed accrescere lo\n spazio della loro azione.“ (G. R. Carli l. c. t. XV, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"12.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0043","text":"176 Note.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"12.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0044","text":"Wenn Arbeiter überhaupt nicht unmittelbar zusammenwirken können,\n ohne zusammen zu sein, ihre Konglomeration auf bestimmtem Raum daher\n Bedingung ihrer Cooperation ist, können Lohnarbeiter nicht coope-\n riren, ohne dass dasselbe Kapital, derselbe Kapitalist sie\n gleichzeitig anwendet, also ihre Arbeitskräfte gleichzeitig kauft. Der Ge-\n sammtwerth dieser Arbeitskräfte, oder die Lohnsumme der\n Arbeiter für den Tag, die Woche u. s. w., muss daher in der Tasche des\n Kapitalisten vereint sein, bevor die Arbeitskräfte selbst im Produktions-\n prozess vereint werden. Zahlung von 300 Arbeitern auf einmal, auch nur\n für einen Tag, bedingt mehr Kapitalauslage als Zahlung weniger Arbeiter\n Woche für Woche während des ganzen Jahrs.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"12.44"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0045","text":"Die Anzahl der cooperirenden\n Arbeiter, oder die Stufenleiter der Cooperation, hängt also zunächst ab von\n der Grösse des Kapitals, das der einzelne Kapitalist im Ankauf von Arbeits-\n kraft auslegen kann, d. h. von dem Umfang, worin je ein Kapi-\n talist über die Lebensmittel vieler Arbeiter verfügt.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"12.45"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0046","text":"Und wie mit dem variablen, verhält es sich mit dem constan-\n ten Kapital. Die Auslage für Rohmaterial z. B. ist 30 mal grösser für\n den einen Kapitalisten, der 300, als für jeden der 30 Kapitalisten, der je\n 10 Arbeiter beschäftigt. Werthumfang und Stoffmasse der gemeinsam\n benutzten Arbeitsmittel wachsen zwar nicht in demselben Mass wie die\n beschäftigte Arbeiteranzahl, aber sie wachsen beträchtlich. Koncen-\n tration grösserer Massen von Produktionsmitteln in der\n Hand einzelner Kapitalisten ist also materielle Bedingung für\n die Cooperation von Lohnarbeitern, und der Umfang der Cooperation, oder\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"312\", \"facs\": \"#f0331\"} --> die Stufenleiter der Produktion, hängt ab vom Umfang dieser Koncen-\n tration.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"12.46"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0047","text":"Ursprünglich erschien eine gewisse Minimalgrösse des Kapi-\n tals in der Hand des einzelnen Arbeitsanwenders nothwendig, damit die\n Anzahl der gleichzeitig ausgebeuteten Arbeiter, daher die Masse des\n producirten Mehrwerths hinreiche, ihn selbst von der Handarbeit zu entbin-\n den, aus einem Kleinmeister in einen Kapitalisten zu verwandeln und so das\n Kapitalverhältniss formell herzustellen. Sie erscheint jetzt als mate-\n rielle Bedingung für die Verwandlung vieler zersplitterter und von\n einander unabhängiger individueller Arbeitsprozesse in einen kombinirten\n gesellschaftlichen Arbeitsprozess.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"12.47"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0048","text":"Ebenso erschien ursprünglich das Kommando des Kapitals über die\n Arbeit nur als formelle Folge davon, dass der Arbeiter, statt für sich,\n für den Kapitalisten und daher unter dem Kapitalisten arbeitet. Mit\n der Cooperation vieler Lohnarbeiter entwickelt sich das Kommando des\n Kapitals zum Erheischniss für die Ausführung des Arbeitsprozesses selbst,\n zu einer wirklichen Produktionsbedingung. Der Befehl des Kapitalisten\n auf dem Produktionsfeld wird jetzt so unentbehrlich wie der Befehl des\n Generals auf dem Schlachtfeld.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"12.48"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0049","text":"Alle unmittelbar gesellschaftliche oder gemeinschaftliche Arbeit auf\n grösserem Massstab braucht mehr oder minder eine Direktion, welche die\n Harmonie der individuellen Thätigkeiten vermittelt und die allgemei-\n nen Funktionen vollzieht, die aus der Bewegung des produktiven\n Gesammtkörpers im Unterschied von der Bewegung seiner selbstständigen\n Organe entspringen. Ein einzelner Violinspieler dirigirt sich selbst, ein\n Orchester bedarf des Musikdirektors. Diese Funktion der Leitung, Ueber-\n wachung und Vermittlung wird zur Funktion des Kapitals, sobald\n die ihm untergeordnete Arbeit cooperativ wird. Als spezifische Funktion\n des Kapitals erhält die Funktion der Leitung spezifische Charaktermale.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"12.49"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0050","text":"Zunächst ist das treibende Motiv und der bestimmende Zweck des\n kapitalistischen Produktionsprozesses möglichst grosse Selbstverwer-\n thung des Kapitals [Note: „Profits … is the sole end of trade.“ (J. Vanderlint l. c.\n p. 11.)] , d. h. möglichst grosse Produktion von Mehr-\n werth, also möglichst grosse Ausbeutung der Arbeitskraft durch den Kapi-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"313\", \"facs\": \"#f0332\"} --> talisten. Mit der Masse der gleichzeitig beschäftigten Arbeiter wächst\n ihr Widerstand und damit nothwendig der Druck des Kapitals zur Be-\n wältigung dieses Widerstands.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"12.50"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0051","text":"Die Leitung des Kapitalisten ist nicht\n nur eine aus der Natur des gesellschaftlichen Arbeitsprozesses ent-\n springende und ihm angehörige besondere Funktion, sie ist zugleich\nFunktion der Ausbeutung eines gesellschaftlichen Ar-\n beitsprozesses und daher bedingt durch den unvermeidlichen Anta-\n gonismus zwischen dem Ausbeuter und dem Rohmaterial seiner Ausbeu-\n tung. Ebenso wächst mit dem Umfang der Produktionsmittel, die dem\n Lohnarbeiter als fremdes Eigenthum gegenüberstehn, die Nothwendigkeit\n der Kontrole über deren sachgemässe Verwendung [Note: Ein englisches Philisterblatt, der Spectator vom 3.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"12.51"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0052","text":"June 1866, be-\n richtet, dass nach Einführung einer Art von Kompagniegeschäft zwischen Kapi-\n talist und Arbeitern in der „wirework company of Manchester“: „the first result\n was a sudden decrease in waste, the men not seeing why they should waste their\n own property any more than any other masters, and waste is perhaps, next to bad\n debts, the greatest source of manufacturing loss.“ Dasselbe Blatt entdeckt als\n Grundmangel der Rochdale cooperative experiments: „They showed that asso-\n ciations of workmen could manage shops, mills, and almost all forms of industry\n with success, and they immensely improved the condition of the men, but then\n they did not leave a clear place for masters.“ („Sie bewiesen, dass\n Arbeiterassociationen Boutiquen, Fabriken und beinahe alle Formen der Industrie\n mit Erfolg handhaben können, und sie verbesserten ausserordentlich die Lage der\n Leute selbst,","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"12.52"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0053","text":"aber!, aber dann liessen sie keinen sichtbaren Platz für Kapitalisten\n offen.“ Quelle horreur!)] . Die Cooperation\n der Lohnarbeiter ist ferner blosse Wirkung des Kapitals, das sie gleich-\n zeitig anwendet. Der Zusammenhang ihrer Funktionen und ihre Einheit\n als produktiver Gesammtkörper liegen ausser ihnen, im Kapital, das sie\n zusammenbringt und zusammenhält. Der Zusammenhang ihrer Arbeiten tritt\n ihnen daher ideell als Plan des Kapitalisten, ihre eigne Einheit praktisch\n als seine Autorität gegenüber, die Macht des fremden Willens, der\n ihr Thun seinem Zweck unterwirft.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"12.53"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0054","text":"Wenn daher die kapitalistische Lei-\n tung dem Inhalt nach zwieschlächtig, wegen der Zwieschlächtigkeit des\n zu leitenden Produktionsprozesses selbst, da er einerseits gesellschaftlicher\n Arbeitsprozess zur Herstellung eines Produkts, andrerseits Verwerthungs-\n prozess des Kapitals ist, so ist sie der Form nach despotisch. Mit der\n Entwicklung der Cooperation auf grösserem Massstab entwickelt dieser\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"314\", \"facs\": \"#f0333\"} --> Despotismus seine eigenthümlichen Formen. Wie der Kapitalist zunächst\n entbunden wird von der Handarbeit, sobald sein Kapital jene Minimalgrösse\n erreicht hat, womit die eigentlich kapitalistische Produktion erst beginnt, so\n tritt er jetzt die Funktion unmittelbarer und fortwährender Beaufsichtigung\n der einzelnen Arbeiter und Arbeitergruppen selbst wieder ab an eine be-\n sondre Sorte von Lohnarbeitern.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"12.54"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0055","text":"Wie eine Armee militärischer, bedarf eine\n unter dem Kommando desselben Kapitals zusammenwirkende Arbeitermasse\n industrieller Oberofficiere (Dirigenten, managers) und Unterofficiere (Ar-\n beitsaufseher, foremen, overlookers, contre-maîtres), die während des Ar-\n beitsprozesses selbst im Namen des Kapitals kommandiren. Die Arbeit\n der Oberaufsicht befestigt sich zu ihrer ausschliesslichen Funktion. Bei\n Vergleichung der Produktionsweise unabhängiger Bauern oder selbststän-\n diger Handwerker mit der auf Sklaverei beruhenden Plantagenwirthschaft,\n zählt der politische Oekonom diese Arbeit der Oberaufsicht zu\n den faux frais de production [Note: Nachdem Prof.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"12.55"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0056","text":"Cairnes die „superintendence of labour“ als einen\n Hauptcharakter der Sklavenproduktion in den südlichen Staaten von Nordamerika\n dargestellt hat, fährt er fort: „The peasant proprietor (des Nordens) appropria-\n ting the whole produce of his soil, needs no other stimulus to exertion. Super-\n intendence is here completely dispensed with.“ (Cairnes l. c. p. 48, 49.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"12.56"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0057","text":"Bei Betrachtung der kapitalistischen\n Produktionsweise identificirt er dagegen die Funktion der Leitung, soweit\n sie aus der Natur des gemeinschaftlichen Arbeitsprozesses entspringt, mit\n derselben Funktion, soweit sie durch den kapitalistischen und daher anta-\n gonistischen Charakter dieses Prozesses bedingt wird [Note: Sir James Steuart, überhaupt ausgezeichnet durch offnes Auge für\n die charakteristisch - gesellschaftlichen Unterschiede verschiedner Produktions-\n weisen, bemerkt: „Pourquoi l’industrie des particuliers est-elle ruinée par des\n grandes entreprises en manufactures, si ce n’est parce que celles-ci se rapprochent\n davantage à la simplicité du régime des esclaves?“ („Princ. of Pol. Econ.“\n Fzs. Uebers. Paris 1789, t. I, p. 308, 309.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"12.57"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0058","text":"Der Kapitalist\n ist nicht Kapitalist, weil er industrieller Leiter ist, sondern er wird indu-\n strieller Befehlshaber, weil er Kapitalist ist. Der Oberbefehl in der In-\n dustrie wird Attribut des Kapitals, wie zur Feudalzeit der Oberbefehl in\n Krieg und Gericht Attribut des Grundeigenthums war [Note: Auguste Comte und seine Schule könnten daher in derselben Art\n die ewige Nothwendigkeit von Feudalherrn beweisen, worin sie diess für die Kapi-\n talisten thun. Bei näherer Analyse der „Philosophie Positive“ entdeckt man] .","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"12.58"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0059","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"315\", \"facs\": \"#f0334\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"12.59"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0060","text":"Eigenthümer seiner Arbeitskraft ist der Arbeiter, so lange er als\n Verkäufer derselben mit dem Kapitalisten marktet, und er kann nur ver-\n kaufen, was er besitzt, seine individuelle, vereinzelte Arbeitskraft. Diess\n Verhältniss wird in keiner Weise dadurch verändert, dass der Kapitalist\n 100 Arbeitskräfte statt einer kauft oder mit 100 von einander unab-\n hängigen Arbeitern Kontrakte schliesst statt mit einem einzelnen. Er\n kann die 100 Arbeiter anwenden ohne sie cooperiren zu lassen. Der\n Kapitalist zahlt daher den Werth der 100 selbstständigen Arbeitskräfte,\n aber er zahlt nicht die kombinirte Arbeitskraft der Hundert. Als unab-\n hängige Personen sind die Arbeiter Vereinzelte, die in ein Verhält-\n niss zu demselben Kapital, aber nicht zu einander treten. Ihre Coopera-\n tion beginnt erst im Arbeitsprozess, aber im Arbeitsprozess haben sie be-\n reits aufgehört sich selbst zu gehören.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"12.60"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0061","text":"Mit dem Eintritt in denselben sind\n sie dem Kapital einverleibt. Als Cooperirende, als Glieder eines werk-\n thätigen Organismus, sind sie selbst nur eine besondre Existenzweise des\n Kapitals. Die Produktivkraft, die der Arbeiter als gesellschaft-\n licher Arbeiter entwickelt, ist daher Produktivkraft des Kapi-\n tals. Die gesellschaftliche Produktivkraft der Arbeit ent-\n wickelt sich unentgeldlich, sobald die Arbeiter unter bestimmte Bedingungen\n gestellt sind, und das Kapital stellt sie unter diese Bedingungen. Weil\n die gesellschaftliche Produktivkraft der Arbeit dem Kapi-\n tal nichts kostet, weil sie andrerseits nicht von dem Arbeiter entwickelt\n wird, bevor seine Arbeit selbst dem Kapital gehört, erscheint sie als Pro-\n duktivkraft, die das Kapital von Natur besitzt, als seine immanente\n Produktivkraft.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"12.61"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0062","text":"Kolossal zeigt sich die Wirkung der einfachen Cooperation in den\n Riesenwerken der alten Asiaten, Aegypter, Etrusker u. s. w. „Es ge-\n schah in vergangnen Zeiten, dass diese asiatischen Staaten nach Bestrei-\n tung ihrer Civil- und Militairausgaben, sich im Besitz eines Ueberschusses\n von Lebensmitteln befanden, die sie für Werke der Pracht und des Nutzens\n verausgaben konnten. Ihr Kommando über die Hände und Arme fast der\n [Note: überhaupt, dass sie trotz aller scheinbaren Freigeisterei mit ihren Wurzeln tief in\n katholischer Erde steckt. Die Methode encyklopädischer Zusammenfassung gab\n dem A. Comte den Umlauf in Frankreich.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"12.62"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0063","text":"Verglichen mit Hegel’s „Encyklo-\n pädie“, die ungefähr 1½ Decennien früher erschien, ist die Comte’sche Kom\n bination eine Schülerarbeit von bloss lokaler Bedeutung.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"316\", \"facs\": \"#f0335\"} --> ganzen nicht ackerbauenden Bevölkerung und die ausschliessliche Ver-\n fügung des Monarchen und der Priesterschaft über jenen Ueberschuss boten\n ihnen die Mittel zur Errichtung jener mächtigen Monumente, womit sie\n das Land erfüllten … In der Bewegung der kolossalen Statuen und der\n enormen Massen, deren Transport Staunen erregt, wurde fast nur mensch-\n liche Arbeit verschwenderisch angewandt. Die Zahl der Arbeiter\n und die Koncentration ihrer Mühen genügte. So sehn wir\n mächtige Korallenriffe aus den Tiefen des Oceaus zu Inseln anschwellen\n und festes Land bilden, obgleich jeder individuelle Ablagerer (depositary)\n winzig, schwach und verächtlich ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"12.63"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0064","text":"Die nicht ackerbauenden Arbeiter\n einer asiatischen Monarchie haben ausser ihren individuellen körperlichen\n Bemühungen wenig zum Werk zu bringen, aber ihre Zahl ist ihre Kraft,\n und die Macht der Direktion über diese Massen gab jenen Riesenwerken\n den Ursprung. Es war die Koncentration der Revenüen, wovon die Ar-\n beiter leben, in eine Hand oder wenige Hände, welche solche Unternehmungen\n möglich machte“ [Note: R. Jones: Textbook of Lectures etc., p. 77, 78. Die altassy-\n rischen, ägyptischen u. s. w. Sammlungen in London und andern europäischen\n Hauptstädten machen uns zu Augenzeugen jener cooperativen Arbeitsprozesse.] . Diese Macht asiatischer und ägyptischer Könige\n oder etruskischer Theokraten u. s. w. ist in der modernen Gesellschaft\n auf den Kapitalisten übergegangen, ob er nun als vereinzelter Kapitalist\n auftritt, oder, wie bei Aktiengesellschaften, als kombinirter Kapitalist.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"12.64"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0065","text":"Die Cooperation im Arbeitsprozess, wie wir sie in den Kulturan-\n fängen der Menschheit, bei Jägervölkern [Note: Linguet in seiner „Théorie des Lois civiles“ hat vielleicht\n nicht Unrecht, wenn er die Jagd für die erste Form der Cooperation, und\nMenschenjagd (Krieg) für eine der ersten Formen der Jagd erklärt.]  oder etwa in der Agrikultur\n indischer Gemeinwesen vorherrschend finden, beruht einerseits auf dem\nGemeineigenthum an den Produktionsbedingungen, an-\n drerseits darauf, dass das einzelne Individuum sich von der Nabelschnur\n des Stammes oder des Gemeinwesens noch ebensowenig losgerissen hat,\n wie das Bienenindividuum vom Bienenstock. Beides unterscheidet sie\n von der kapitalistischen Cooperation.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"12.65"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0066","text":"Die sporadische Anwendung der\n Cooperation auf grossem Massstab in der antiken Welt, dem Mittelalter\n und den modernen Kolonien, beruht auf unmittelbaren Herrschafts-\n und Knechtschafts-Verhältnissen, zumeist auf der Sklaverei. Die kapita-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"317\", \"facs\": \"#f0336\"} --> listische Form setzt dagegen von vornherein den freien Lohnarbeiter vor-\n aus, der seine Arbeitskraft dem Kapital verkauft.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"12.66"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0067","text":"Historisch jedoch ent-\n wickelt sie sich im Gegensatz zur Bauernwirthschaft und zum unab-\n hängigen Handwerksbetrieb, ob dieser zünftige Form besitze oder nicht [Note: Die kleine Bauernwirthschaft und der unabhängige Handwerksbetrieb,\n die beide theils die Basis der feudalen Produktionsweise bilden, theils nach deren\n Auflösung neben dem kapitalistischen Betrieb erscheinen, bilden zugleich die\n ökonomische Grundlage der klassischen Gemeinwesen zu ihrer besten Zeit, nach-\n dem sich das ursprünglich orientalische Gemeineigenthum aufgelöst, und bevor\n sich die Sklaverei der Produktion ernsthaft bemächtigt hat.] .\n Ihnen gegenüber erscheint die kapitalistische Cooperation nicht als eine\n besondre historische Form der Cooperation, sondern die\n Cooperation selbst als eine dem kapitalistischen Produk-\n tionsprozess eigenthümliche und ihn spezifisch unter-\n scheidende historische Form.","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"12.67"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0068","text":"Wie die durch die Cooperation entwickelte gesellschaftliche\n Produktivkraft der Arbeit als Produktivkraft des Kapi-\n tals erscheint, so die Cooperation selbst als eine spezifische\n Form des kapitalistischen Produktionsprozesses im\n Gegensatz zum Produktionsprozess vereinzelter unabhängiger Arbeiter oder\n auch Kleinmeister. Es ist die erste Aenderung, welche der wirk-\n liche Arbeitsprozess durch seine Subsumtion unter das Kapital\n erfährt. Diese Aenderung geht naturwüchsig vor sich. Ihre Voraus-\n setzung, gleichzeitige Beschäftigung einer grösseren Anzahl von Lohnarbei-\n tern in demselben Arbeitsprozess, bildet den Ausgangspunkt der kapitalisti-\n schen Produktion. Dieser fällt mit dem Dasein des Kapitals selbst zusam-\n men.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"12.68"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0069","text":"Wenn sich die kapitalistische Produktionsweise daher einerseits als\nhistorische Nothwendigkeit für die Verwandlung des Arbeitsprozesses\n in einen gesellschaftlichen Prozess darstellt, so andrerseits diese gesellschaft-\n liche Form des Arbeitsprozesses als eine vom Kapital angewandte Me-\n thode, um ihn durch Steigerung seiner Produktivkraft profitlicher aus-\n zubeuten.","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"12.69"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0070","text":"In ihrer bisher betrachteten einfachen Gestalt fällt die Cooperation\n zusammen mit der Produktion auf grösserer Stufenleiter,\n bildet aber keine feste, charakteristische Form einer besondern Ent-\n wicklungs epoche der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise. Höchstens er-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"318\", \"facs\": \"#f0337\"} --> scheint sie annähernd so in den noch handwerksmässigen Anfängen der\n Manufaktur [Note: „Whether the united skill, industry and emulation of many together on\n the same work be not the way to advance it? And whether it had been otherwise\n possible for England, to have carried on her Woollen Manufacture to so great a\n perfection?“ (Berkeley: „The Querist.“ Lond. 1750, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"12.70"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0071","text":"521.)]  und in jener Art grosser Agrikultur, welche der Manufaktur-\n periode entspricht, und sich wesentlich nur durch die Masse der gleich-\n zeitig angewandten Arbeiter und den Umfang der koncentrirten Produk-\n tionsmittel von der Bauernwirthschaft unterscheidet. Die einfache Coope-\n ration ist stets noch vorherrschende Form solcher Produktionszweige, worin\n das Kapital auf grosser Stufenleiter operirt, ohne dass Theilung der\n Arbeit oder Maschinerie eine bedeutende Rolle spielte.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"12.71"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0072","text":"Die Cooperation bleibt die Grundform der kapitalistischen\n Produktionsweise, obgleich ihre einfache Gestalt selbst als be-\n sondere Form neben ihren weiter entwickelten Formen erscheint.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"12.72"}],"sha256":"f610a992d1299dd6cc5880cf1c43356269145b7a116e20e96a6250b39b05957f","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T203905687870Z_57797dd0","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2) Cooperation.","source_sha256":"f610a992d1299dd6cc5880cf1c43356269145b7a116e20e96a6250b39b05957f","sha256":"553cf8abb0423ff796d06944eb6fc2ff6fd30dda53091d4886c08d2a8c34c789","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:39:05.687883+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0001","text":"Capitalist production, as we saw, in fact begins only\n where the same individual capital simultaneously employs a larger number\n of workers, where the labour-process thus expands its scale and delivers product\n on a larger quantitative scale. Only where the number of workers\n suffices to liberate the employer of labour himself from labour by means of\n the mass of surplus-value produced by it, does the latter become a full-\n fledged capitalist. The activity of a larger number of workers at the same time,\n in the same space (or, if one will, in the same field of labour), for the\n production of the same kind of commodity, under the command of the same capi-\n talist, therefore forms historically and conceptually the starting-point of\n capitalist production. With reference to the mode of production\n itself, e.g.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"12.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0002","text":"manufacture in its begin-\n nings differs scarcely otherwise from guild handicraft industry than by the\n greater number of workers simultaneously employed by the same capital.\n The workshop of the guild-master is merely enlarged.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"12.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0003","text":"The difference, then, is at first merely quantitative. We saw\n that the mass of surplus-value which a given capital pro-\n duces equals the surplus-value delivered by the individual worker, multiplied by\n the number of simultaneously employed workers. This number changes\n nothing in and for itself in the rate of surplus-value or in the de-\n gree of exploitation of labour-power. But as regards the production of commodity-\n value in general, every qualitative change in the labour-process itself appears\n indifferent to it. This follows from the nature of exchange-value,\n which is nothing but a definite quantum of objectified labour. If a twelve-hour working-\n day is objectified in 6 sh., then 1200 such working-days are objectified in 6 sh. × 1200. In the\n one case 12 × 1200, in the other 12 labour-hours have been\n incorporated in the products.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"12.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0004","text":"In the production of value, many always count only\n as many individuals. For value-production, then, it makes no\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"303\", \"facs\": \"#f0322\"} -->qualitative difference whether 1200 workers produce in isolation or\n united under the command of the same capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"12.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0005","text":"Nevertheless, within certain limits a modification does take place.\n The labour objectified in value is labour of average social\n quality, and thus the value of labour-power is the value of\n average labour-power. But an average magnitude always exists only as the average of many different individual magnitudes of the\n same kind. In every branch of industry, the individual worker,\n Peter or Paul, deviates more or less from the average worker. These\n individual deviations, or what are mathematically called “errors,”\n compensate one another and disappear as soon as one takes together a larger num-\n ber of workers.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"12.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0006","text":"The celebrated sophist and sycophant\nEdmund Burke professes to know from his practical experience as a farmer\n even that already “in so small a platoon” as 5 farm-\n labourers every individual difference of labour vanishes, so that the\n first five English agricultural labourers of working age that come to hand,\n taken together, perform in the same time exactly as much labour as\n any other five English agricultural labourers [Note: “Unquestionably, there is a great deal of difference between the value of\n one man’s labour and that of another, from strength, dexterity and honest appli-\n cation.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"12.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0007","text":"But I am quite sure, from my best observation, that any given five\n men will in their total, afford a proportion of labour equal to any other five\n within the periods of life I have stated; that is, that among such five men there\n will be one possessing all the qualifications of a good workman, one bad, and the\n other three middling, and approximating to the first and the last. So that in so\n small a platoon as that of even five, you will find the full complement of all that\n five men can earn.“ (E. Burke l. c. p. 16.) cf. Quételet on the aver-\n age individual.] . The number indicated by him\n is immaterial here, but it is clear that the aggregate working-day\n of a larger number of simultaneously employed workers is in and for itself\n a day of average social labour. The\n working-day of the individual may, e.g., be twelve hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"12.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0008","text":"Thus the working-day\n of 12 simultaneously employed workers forms an aggregate working-day of\n 144 hours, and although the labour of each of the dozen deviates more or\n less from average social labour, and the indi-\n vidual may therefore require somewhat more or less time for the same operation,\n the working-day of each individual, as a twelfth part of the aggre-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"304\", \"facs\": \"#f0323\"} -->gate working-day of 144 hours, possesses the quality of average social labour.\n For the capitalist, however, who employs a dozen, the working-day\n exists as the aggregate working-day of the dozen.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"12.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0009","text":"The working-day of each individual exists\n as an aliquot part of the aggregate working-day, quite independently of whether\n the twelve work hand in hand with one another or whether the whole connection\n of their labours consists only in their working for the same capitalist. If, on the other hand, of the 12 workers two each are employed by a small\n master, it becomes accidental whether each individual master pro-\n duces the same mass of value and thus realizes the general rate of surplus-value.\n Individual deviations would occur.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"12.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0010","text":"If a\n worker consumed considerably more time in the production of a commodity than is socially required,\n if the labour-time individually necessary for him deviated considerably from the socially necessary or the aver-\n age labour-time, his labour would not count as average labour,\n nor his labour-power as average labour-power. It would not sell at all\n or only below the average value of labour-power. A definite minimum of skill in labour is therefore presupposed, and we\n shall later see that capitalist production finds means of measuring this\n minimum. Nevertheless, the minimum deviates from the\n average, although on the other hand the average value\n of labour-power must be paid.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"12.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0011","text":"Of the six small masters,\n one would therefore extract more, another less, than the general rate\n of surplus-value. The inequalities would compensate themselves for society,\n but not for the individual master. Thus the law of valorization in general\n realizes itself completely for the individual producer only\n once he produces as a capitalist, employs many workers simultaneously,\n and thus from the outset sets average social labour\n in motion [Note: Professor Roscher claims to have discovered that a seamstress who\n is employed for two days by the professor’s wife delivers more labour\n than two seamstresses whom the professor’s wife employs on the same day.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"12.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0012","text":"The professor should not make his observations on the capitalist process of pro-\n duction in the nursery, nor under circumstances in which the\n principal personage, the capitalist, is absent.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"12.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0013","text":"Even where the mode of labour remains unchanged, the simul-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"305\", \"facs\": \"#f0324\"} -->taneous employment of a larger number of workers effects a revolution in the\n objective conditions of the labour-process.\n The buildings in which the many work, the stores for raw materials,\n semi-finished commodities, etc., the vessels, instruments, apparatuses, etc.,\n which can be used by many simultaneously or alternately,\n in short, a part of the means of production is now consumed\n jointly in the labour-process. On the one hand, the exchange-value\n of commodities, and hence also of means of production, is in no way increased\n by any increased exploitation of their use-value.\n On the other hand, the scale of jointly used means of pro-\n duction grows.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"12.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0014","text":"A room in which 20 weavers work with their 20 looms\n must be more extensive than the room of an independent\n weaver with two journeymen. But the production of one workshop for 20\n persons costs less labour than that of 10 workshops for two per-\n sons each, and thus in general the value of means of production concentrated\n and used jointly en masse does not grow proportionally with their\n scale and their useful effect. Means of production used jointly\n transfer a smaller component of value to the individual product, partly because\n the total value they transfer is distributed simultaneously over a larger\n mass of products, partly because, as a consequence of their enlarged scale,\n they enter the process of production with a value that is indeed absolutely\n greater, but relatively smaller, in relation to their sphere of\n operation, than do isolated means of production.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"12.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0015","text":"Thereby a component of value\n that replaces constant capital falls, and thus, proportionately to the magnitude of this\n component, also the total value of the commodity. The effect is the\n same as if the productive power of labour had increased in those\n branches of industry which supply means of production. This economy in\n the application of the means of production springs only from\n their joint consumption in the labour-process of many.\n And they acquire this character as conditions of social labour\n or social conditions of la-\n bour, in distinction from the dispersed and relatively more expensive condi-\n tions of production of isolated independent workers or small mas-\n ters, even where the many work only alongside one another spatially, and not with one\n another.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"12.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0016","text":"A part of the instruments of labour acquires this social\n character before the labour-process itself acquires it.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"12.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0017","text":"[Running matter: I. 20]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"306\", \"facs\": \"#f0325\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"12.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0018","text":"The economy of means of production must in general be considered\n from a twofold point of view. First, insofar as it cheapens com-\n modities and thereby lowers the value of labour-power.\n Secondly, insofar as it alters the relation of surplus-value to\n the advanced total capital, i.e. to the sum of value\n of its constant and variable components. The latter\n point will be discussed only in the third book of this work, to which, for the sake of coherence,\n we also refer some matters already belonging here.\n On the one hand, the course of analysis commands this tearing apart of the subject.\n On the other hand, it corresponds to the spirit of capitalist production.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"12.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0019","text":"For\n since here the conditions of labour confront the worker independently,\n their economy too appears as a special operation which does not concern\n him and is therefore separate from the methods whereby the\n productivity of the labour-power consumed by capital is increased.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"12.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0020","text":"The form of labour of many, who in the same process of pro-\n duction or in different but interconnected processes of pro-\n duction work according to plan alongside and with one another, is called\nco-operation [Note: “Concours de forces.” (Destutt de Tracy l. c. p. 78.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"12.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0021","text":"Just as the force of attack of a squadron of cavalry or the force of resistance\n of a regiment of infantry is essentially different from the sum\n of the isolated forces of attack and resistance which each cavalryman\n and infantryman could develop by himself, so is the mechanical\n sum of force of isolated workers different from the mechanical potential force\n which develops when many hands co-operate simultaneously\n in the same undivided operation, e.g. when it is a matter of lifting\n a load, turning a crank, or clearing an obstacle out of the way [Note: “There are numerous operations of so simple a kind as not to admit a\n division into parts, which cannot be performed without the cooperation of many\n pairs of hands.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"12.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0022","text":"For instance the lifting of a large tree on a wain … every\n thing in short, which cannot be done unless a great many pairs of hands help each\n other in the same undivided employment, and at the same time.“ (E. G. Wake-\n field: „A View of the Art of Colonization. Lond. 1849“, p. 168.)] . The effect of combined labour could here not be produced at all by\n isolated labour, or only over much longer periods of time, or only on\n a dwarf scale.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"12.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0023","text":"Here it is not\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"307\", \"facs\": \"#f0326\"} --> merely a matter of increasing individual productive power through co-operation,\n but of creating a productive power which is in and for itself mass-\n power [Note: “As one man cannot, and 10 men must strain, to lift a tun of weight,\n yet one hundred men can do it only by the strength of a finger of each of them.“\n (John Bellers: „Proposals for raising a colledge of industry.\n Lond. 1696“, p. 21.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"12.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0024","text":"Apart from the new mechanical potential force which springs from the fusion\n of many forces into one aggregate force, in most productive labours\n mere social contact produces a rivalry and a peculiar excitation of the vital spi-\n rits (animal spirit), which heighten the individual capacity for performance of the indi-\n viduals, so that a dozen persons together in a simultaneous\n working-day of 144 hours deliver a much greater aggregate product\n than twelve isolated workers, each of whom works 12 hours, or\n than one worker who works 12 days in succession [Note: “There is also“ (when the same number of workers is employed by one farmer on\n 300, instead of by 10 farmers on 30 acres each) “an advantage in the\n proportion of servants, which will not easily be understood but by practical men;\n for it is natural to say, as 1 is to 4, so are","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"12.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0025","text":"3:12: but this will not hold good in\n practice; for in harvest-time and many other operations which require that kind\n of despatch, by the throwing many hands together, the work is better,\n and more expeditiously done: f. i., in harvest, 2 drivers, 2 loaders, 2 pitchers,\n 2 rakers, and the rest at the rick, or in the barn, will despatch double the work,\n that the same number of hands would do, if divided into different gangs, on\n different farms.“ („An Enquiry into the Connection between the\n present price of provisions and the size of farms. By a Farmer.\n Lond. 1773“, p. 7, 8)] . This arises from the fact\n that man is by nature, if not, as Aristotle thinks, a politi-\n cal [Note: Aristotle’s definition is properly that man is by nature a city-\n citizen.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"12.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0026","text":"It is as characteristic of classical antiquity as Franklin’s\n definition that man is by nature a maker of instruments is of Yankeeism.] , at all events a social animal.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"12.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0027","text":"Although many perform the same or similar things simultaneously\n together, the individual labour of each may nevertheless, as\n part of the total labour, itself represent different phases of the labour pro-\n cess, which the object of labour, in consequence of coo-\n [Running matter: 20*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"308\", \"facs\": \"#f0327\"} -->peration, passes through more rapidly. E.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"12.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0028","text":"g., when masons form a series of hands\n in order to convey building-stones from the foot of a scaffold to its top,\n each of them does the same thing, but nevertheless the individual\n operations form continuous parts of a total operation, special\n phases which each building-stone must pass through in the labour process, and by\n which it is conveyed more rapidly by, say, the 24 hands of the total labourer\n than by the two hands of each individual labourer, who would ascend and de-\n scend the scaffold [Note: „On doit encore remarquer que cette division partielle du travail peut se\n faire quand même les ouvriers sont occupés d’une même bésogne.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"12.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0029","text":"Des maçons par\n exemple, occupés de faire passer de mains en mains des briques à un échafaudage\n supérieur, font tous la même bésogne, et pourtant il existe parmi eux une espèce\n de division de travail, qui consiste en ce que chacun d’eux fait passer la brique par\n un espace donné, et que tous ensemble la font parvenir beaucoup plus prompte-\n ment à l’endroit marqué qu’ils ne feraient si chacun d’eux portait sa brique sépa-\n rement jusqu’à l’échafaudage supérieur.“ (F. Skarbek: „Théorie des\n richesses sociales. 2ème ed. Paris 1840,“ t. I, p. 97, 98.)] . The object of labour passes through the same\n space in a shorter time. On the other hand, combination of labour\n takes place when a building, e. g., is attacked simultaneously from differ-\n ent sides, although the cooperators do the same or similar\n things.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"12.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0030","text":"The combined working day of 144 hours, which attacks the object of labour\n on many sides in space, because the combined labourer\n or total labourer has eyes and hands in front and behind and\n possesses omnipresence to a certain degree, advances the total product\n more rapidly than 12 twelve-hour working days of more or less isolated\n labourers, who must attack their work more one-sidedly. In the same\n time different spatial parts of the product mature.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"12.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0031","text":"We emphasized here that the many who complement one another do the\n same or similar things, because this simple form of\n cooperation, as will be seen later, also plays a great role in its most developed\n form. If the labour process is complicated, the mere mass of those working\n together permits the different operations to be distributed among different\n hands, hence to be performed simultaneously, and thereby to shorten the labour time\n necessary for the production of the total product [Note: „Est-il question d’exécuter un travail compliqué? plusieurs choses doivent] .","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"12.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0032","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"309\", \"facs\": \"#f0328\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"12.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0033","text":"In many spheres of production there are critical moments, i. e.\n epochs of time determined by the nature of the labour process itself, during\n which definite results of labour must be achieved. If, e. g.,\n a flock of sheep is to be shorn or a number of acres of corn-land mown\n and harvested, the quantity and quality of the product de-\n pend upon the operation being begun at a certain time and ended at a\n certain time. The period of time which the labour process\n may occupy is here prescribed, as, for instance, in herring-fishing. The\n individual can cut out of one day only one working day,\n say of 12 hours, but the cooperation of 100, e. g., expands a twelve-hour day\n into a working day of 1200 hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"12.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0034","text":"The shortness of the labour period is compensated for\n by the magnitude of the mass of labour thrown upon the field of\n production at the decisive moment. The timely effect\n here depends upon the simultaneous application of many combined working\n days; the extent of the useful effect upon the number of labourers,\n which, however, always remains smaller than the number of labourers who,\n in isolation, would fill the same field of action in the same period [Note: „The doing of it (der Arbeit in der Agrikultur) at the critical junc-\n ture. is of so much the greater consequence.“ (An Inquiry into the Con-\n nection between the present price etc., p. 9.) „In agriculture there is\n no more important factor than the factor of time.“ (Liebig: „On\n Theory and Practice in Agriculture. 1856“, p. 23.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"12.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0035","text":"It is through the lack of this cooperation that, in the West of the United\n States, a mass of corn, and in the parts of the East Indies where English\n rule has destroyed the old community, a mass of cotton is annually\n ruined [Note: „The next evil is one which one would scarcely expect to find in a coun-\n try which exports more labour than any other in the world, with the exception\n perhaps of China and England — the impossibility of procuring a sufficient number\n of hands to clean the cotton. The consequence of this is that large quantities of\n the crop are left unpicked, while another portion is gathered from the ground,] .","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"12.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0036","text":"[Note: être fait simultanément. L’un en fait une pendant que l’autre en fait une autre,\n et tous contribuent à l’effet qu’un seul homme n’aurait pu produire. L’un rame\n pendant que l’autre tient le gouvernail, et qu’un troisième jette le filet ou har-\n ponne la poison, et la pêche a un succès impossible sans ce concours.“ (Destutt\n de Tracy l. c.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"12.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0037","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"310\", \"facs\": \"#f0329\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"12.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0038","text":"On the one hand, cooperation enlarges the spatial sphere\n of labour and is therefore required for certain labour processes by the\n spatial continuity of the object of labour itself, as in drainage of land,\n embankment, irrigation, canal-, road-, railway-construction, etc.\n On the other hand, in proportion to the scale of production, it permits\n spatial contraction of the field of production.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"12.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0039","text":"This restriction of the spatial sphere of labour with\n simultaneous expansion of its sphere of action, by which a mass of false\n costs (faux frais) is saved, arises from the conglome-\n ration of labourers, the bringing together of different labour processes,\n and the concentration of the means of production [Note: „In the progress of culture all, and perhaps more than all the capital and\n labour which once loosely occupied 500 acres, are now concentrated for the more\n complete tillage of 100.“ Obgleich „relatively to the amount of capital and labour\n employed, space is concentrated, it is an enlarged sphere of production,\n as compared to the sphere of production formerly occupied or worked upon by\n one single, independent agent of production.“ (R. Jones: „On Rent.\n Lond. 1831“, p. 191, 199.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"12.39","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0040","text":"Compared with an equally large sum of isolated individual\n working days, the combined working day produces larger masses of use-\n value and therefore diminishes the labour time necessary for the production\n of a definite useful effect.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"12.40","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0041","text":"Whether, in a given case, it obtains this increased productive\n power because it increases the mechanical force-potential of\n labour, or extends its spatial sphere of action, or con-\n tracts the spatial field of production in proportion to the scale of production,\n or sets much labour in motion in little time at the critical moment,\n or excites the emulation of individuals and strains their vital spirits,\n or stamps upon the similar operations of many the stamp of conti-\n nuity and versatility, or performs different operations\nsimultaneously, or economizes the means of production by their common\n use, or imparts to individual labour the charac-\n [Note: when it has fallen, and is of course discoloured and partially rotted, so that for\n want of labour at the proper season the cultivator is actually forced to\n submit to the loss of a large part of that","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"12.41","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0042","text":"crop for which England is so anxiously\n looking.“ (Bengal Hurcuru. Bi-Monthly Overland Summary of\n News. 22nd July 1861.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"311\", \"facs\": \"#f0330\"} -->ter of socially average labour, in all circum-\n stances the specific productive power of the combined working\n day is the social productive power of labour or the pro-\n ductive power of social labour. It springs from\n cooperation itself. In planned cooperation with others,\n the labourer casts off his individual limitations and develops his capacities as a species [Note: „La forza di ciascuno uomo è minima, ma la riniunione delle minime\n forze forma una forza totale maggiore anche della somma delle forze medesime\n fino a che le forze per essere riunite possono diminuere il tempo ed accrescere lo\n spazio della loro azione.“ (G. R. Carli l. c. t. XV, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"12.42","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0043","text":"176 Note.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"12.43","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0044","text":"If labourers cannot in general cooperate directly without being together,\n their conglomeration in a definite space being therefore a condition\n of their cooperation, wage-labourers cannot cooperate\n unless the same capital, the same capitalist, employs them\n simultaneously, and therefore purchases their labour-powers simultaneously. The total\n value of these labour-powers, or the aggregate wages of the\n labourers for the day, the week, etc., must therefore be united in the capitalist’s\n pocket before the labour-powers themselves are united in the process of\n production. Payment of 300 labourers at once, even if only\n for one day, requires a greater outlay of capital than payment of fewer labourers\n week after week throughout the whole year.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"12.44","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0045","text":"The number of cooperating\n labourers, or the scale of cooperation, thus depends in the first instance upon\n the magnitude of the capital which the individual capitalist can lay out in purchasing\n labour-power, i. e. upon the extent to which each capi-\n talist commands the means of subsistence of many labourers.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"12.45","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0046","text":"And as with variable, so it is with constant\n capital. The outlay for raw material, e. g., is 30 times greater for\n the one capitalist who employs 300 labourers than for each of the 30 capitalists who\n employ 10 labourers apiece. The magnitude of value and the mass of material of the\n jointly used means of labour certainly do not grow in the same measure as the\n number of labourers employed, but they grow considerably. Concen-\n tration of larger masses of means of production in the\n hand of individual capitalists is thus a material condition for\n the cooperation of wage-labourers, and the extent of cooperation, or\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"312\", \"facs\": \"#f0331\"} --> the scale of production, depends upon the extent of this concen-\n tration.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"12.46","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0047","text":"Originally, a certain minimum magnitude of capi-\n tal in the hand of the individual employer of labour appeared necessary in order that\n the number of labourers simultaneously exploited, and therefore the mass of\n surplus-value produced, might suffice to release him himself from manual labour,\n transform him from a small master into a capitalist, and thus formally establish the\n capital relation. It now appears as a mate-\n rial condition for the transformation of many fragmented and mutually\n independent individual labour processes into a combined\n social labour process.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"12.47","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0048","text":"Likewise, originally, the command of capital over\n labour appeared only as the formal consequence of the fact that the labourer, instead of working for himself,\n works for the capitalist and therefore under the capitalist. With\n the cooperation of many wage-labourers, the command of\n capital develops into a requirement for the execution of the labour process itself,\n into a real condition of production. The command of the capitalist\n on the field of production now becomes as indispensable as the command of the\n general on the field of battle.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"12.48","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0049","text":"All directly social or communal labour on a larger scale requires, more or less, a direction which mediates the harmony of the individual activities and performs the general functions that arise from the movement of the productive collective body, as distinct from the movement of its independent organs. A single violin player conducts himself; an orchestra requires a musical director. This function of management, supervision, and mediation becomes a function of capital as soon as the labour subordinated to it becomes co-operative. As a specific function of capital, the function of management acquires specific characteristics.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"12.49","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0050","text":"First of all, the driving motive and determining purpose of the capitalist process of production is the greatest possible self-expansion of capital [Note: “Profits … is the sole end of trade.” (J. Vanderlint l. c.\n p. 11.)] , i.e. the greatest possible production of surplus-value, hence the greatest possible exploitation of labour-power by the capitalist. With the mass of workers employed simultaneously, their resistance grows, and with it necessarily the pressure of capital to overcome this resistance.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"12.50","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0051","text":"The capitalist’s management is not merely a particular function arising from and belonging to the nature of the social labour process; it is at the same time a function of the exploitation of a social labour process and is therefore conditioned by the unavoidable antagonism between the exploiter and the raw material of his exploitation. Likewise, with the scale of the means of production confronting the wage-worker as alien property, grows the necessity of controlling their appropriate use [Note: An English Philistine paper, the Spectator of 3","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"12.51","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0052","text":"June 1866, reports that after the introduction of a kind of company business between capitalist and workers in the “wirework company of Manchester”: “the first result was a sudden decrease in waste, the men not seeing why they should waste their own property any more than any other masters, and waste is perhaps, next to bad debts, the greatest source of manufacturing loss.” The same paper discovers the fundamental defect of the Rochdale cooperative experiments: “They showed that associations of workmen could manage shops, mills, and almost all forms of industry with success, and they immensely improved the condition of the men, but then they did not leave a clear place for masters.” (“They proved that workers’ associations can manage shops, factories, and almost all forms of industry successfully, and they improved extraordinarily the condition of the people themselves,","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"12.52","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0053","text":"but!, but then they left no visible place open for capitalists.” Quelle horreur!)] . The co-operation of wage-workers is, furthermore, merely the effect of capital, which employs them simultaneously. The connection of their functions and their unity as a productive collective body lie outside them, in capital, which brings and holds them together. The connection of their labours thus confronts them ideally as the capitalist’s plan, their own unity practically as his authority, the power of an alien will that subjects their activity to its purpose.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"12.53","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0054","text":"If, therefore, capitalist management is twofold in content, owing to the twofold character of the production process itself to be managed, which is on the one hand a social labour process for the production of a product, and on the other a process of the valorisation of capital, it is despotic in form. With the development of co-operation on a larger scale, this despotism develops its peculiar forms. Just as the capitalist is at first released from manual labour as soon as his capital has attained that minimum magnitude at which production proper to capitalism first begins, so he now in turn relinquishes the function of direct and continuous supervision of the individual workers and groups of workers to a particular sort of wage-workers.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"12.54","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0055","text":"Like a military army, a mass of workers co-operating under the command of the same capital requires industrial superior officers (directors, managers) and non-commissioned officers (labour overseers, foremen, overlookers, contre-maîtres), who command in the name of capital during the labour process itself. The labour of supervision consolidates itself as their exclusive function. In comparing the mode of production of independent peasants or self-employed craftsmen with plantation economy based on slavery, the political economist counts this labour of supervision among the faux frais de production [Note: After Prof.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"12.55","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0056","text":"Cairnes has represented the “superintendence of labour” as a principal characteristic of slave production in the southern states of North America, he continues: “The peasant proprietor (of the North) appropriating the whole produce of his soil, needs no other stimulus to exertion. Superintendence is here completely dispensed with.” (Cairnes l. c. p. 48, 49.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"12.56","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0057","text":"In considering the capitalist mode of production, he, on the other hand, identifies the function of management, insofar as it arises from the nature of the communal labour process, with the same function insofar as it is conditioned by the capitalist and hence antagonistic character of this process [Note: Sir James Steuart, generally distinguished by an open eye for the characteristic-social differences of different modes of production, observes: “Pourquoi l’industrie des particuliers est-elle ruinée par des grandes entreprises en manufactures, si ce n’est parce que celles-ci se rapprochent davantage à la simplicité du régime des esclaves?” (“Princ. of Pol. Econ.”\nFrench trans. Paris 1789, vol. I, pp. 308, 309.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"12.57","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0058","text":"The capitalist is not a capitalist because he is an industrial manager; rather, he becomes an industrial commander because he is a capitalist. Supreme command in industry becomes an attribute of capital, just as in feudal times supreme command in war and judicature was an attribute of landed property [Note: Auguste Comte and his school could therefore prove, in the same manner, the eternal necessity of feudal lords, in which they do this for capitalists. Upon closer analysis of the “Philosophie Positive” one discovers] .","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"12.58","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0059","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"315\", \"facs\": \"#f0334\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"12.59","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0060","text":"The worker is the owner of his labour-power so long as he bargains with the capitalist as its seller, and he can sell only what he possesses, his individual, isolated labour-power. This relation is in no way altered by the capitalist’s buying 100 labour-powers instead of one, or contracting with 100 workers independent of one another instead of with a single worker. He can employ the 100 workers without allowing them to co-operate. The capitalist therefore pays the value of the 100 independent labour-powers, but he does not pay for the combined labour-power of the hundred. As independent persons, the workers are isolated individuals, who enter into a relation with the same capital, but not with one another. Their co-operation begins only in the labour process, but in the labour process they have already ceased to belong to themselves.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"12.60","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0061","text":"Upon entering it they are incorporated into capital. As co-operators, as members of a working organism, they themselves are only a particular mode of existence of capital. The productive power which the worker develops as a social worker is therefore productive power of capital. The social productive power of labour develops gratis as soon as the workers are placed under definite conditions, and capital places them under these conditions. Because the social productive power of labour costs capital nothing, while on the other hand it is not developed by the worker before his labour itself belongs to capital, it appears as a productive power which capital possesses by nature, as its immanent productive power.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"12.61","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0062","text":"The effect of simple co-operation is displayed colossally in the gigantic works of the ancient Asians, Egyptians, Etruscans, etc. “It occurred in past times that these Asiatic states, after defraying their civil and military expenditures, found themselves in possession of a surplus of foodstuffs which they could expend on works of splendour and utility. Their command over the hands and arms of almost the entire non-agricultural population and the exclusive disposal by the monarch and priesthood of that surplus afforded them the means for erecting those mighty monuments with which they filled the land … In moving the colossal statues and the enormous masses whose transport excites astonishment, almost only human labour was lavishly employed. The number of the workers and the concentration of their efforts sufficed. Thus we see mighty coral reefs swell up from the depths of the ocean into islands and form solid land, although each individual depositor (depositary) is tiny, weak, and contemptible.” [Note: generally, that despite all apparent freethinking it is deeply rooted in Catholic soil. The method of encyclopaedic compilation gave A. Comte currency in France.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"12.62","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0063","text":"Compared with Hegel’s “Encyclopaedia,” which appeared about 1½ decades earlier, Comte’s combination is a pupil’s work of merely local significance.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"316\", \"facs\": \"#f0335\"} --> The non-agricultural workers of an Asian monarchy have little to contribute to the work apart from their individual bodily exertions, but their number is their strength, and the power of management over these masses gave rise to those gigantic works. “It was the concentration of the revenues upon which the workers live in one hand or a few hands that made such undertakings possible” [Note: R. Jones: Textbook of Lectures etc., pp. 77, 78. The ancient Assyrian, Egyptian, etc. collections in London and other European capitals make us eyewitnesses of those co-operative labour processes.] . This power of Asian and Egyptian kings or Etruscan theocracies, etc. has passed in modern society to the capitalist, whether he appears as an isolated capitalist or, as in joint-stock companies, as a combined capitalist.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"12.63","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0064","text":"The non-agricultural workers of an Asian monarchy have little to contribute to the work apart from their individual bodily exertions, but their number is their strength, and the power of management over these masses gave rise to those gigantic works. “It was the concentration of the revenues upon which the workers live in one hand or a few hands that made such undertakings possible” [Note: R. Jones: Textbook of Lectures etc., pp. 77, 78. The ancient Assyrian, Egyptian, etc. collections in London and other European capitals make us eyewitnesses of those co-operative labour processes.] . This power of Asian and Egyptian kings or Etruscan theocracies, etc. has passed in modern society to the capitalist, whether he appears as an isolated capitalist or, as in joint-stock companies, as a combined capitalist.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"12.64","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0065","text":"Co-operation in the labour process, as we find it prevailing at the beginnings of human cultivation, among hunting peoples [Note: Linguet, in his “Théorie des Lois civiles,” is perhaps not wrong when he declares hunting to be the first form of co-operation, and the hunting of men (war) to be one of the first forms of hunting.] or, for example, in the agriculture of Indian communities, rests, on the one hand, on common property in the conditions of production, and, on the other, on the fact that the individual has as yet torn himself away from the umbilical cord of the tribe or community no more than the individual bee from the beehive. Both distinguish it from capitalist co-operation.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"12.65","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0066","text":"The sporadic application of co-operation on a large scale in the ancient world, the Middle Ages, and the modern colonies rests on direct relations of domination and servitude, mostly on slavery. The capitalist form, by contrast, presupposes from the outset the free wage-worker, who sells his labour-power to capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"12.66","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0067","text":"Historically, however, it develops in opposition to peasant economy and independent handicraft enterprise, whether or not the latter possesses guild form [Note: Small peasant economy and independent handicraft enterprise, both of which partly form the basis of the feudal mode of production, and after its dissolution appear alongside capitalist enterprise, also form the economic foundation of the classical communities in their best time, after the originally Oriental common property had dissolved and before slavery had seriously taken possession of production.] . In opposition to these, capitalist co-operation appears not as a particular historical form of co-operation, but co-operation itself appears as a historical form peculiar to the capitalist process of production and specifically distinguishing it.","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"12.67","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0068","text":"Just as the social productive power of labour developed by co-operation appears as the productive power of capital, so co-operation itself appears as a specific form of the capitalist process of production, in contrast to the process of production of isolated independent workers or also small masters. It is the first alteration which the actual labour process undergoes through its subsumption under capital. This alteration takes place spontaneously. Its presupposition, the simultaneous employment of a larger number of wage-workers in the same labour process, forms the starting-point of capitalist production. This coincides with the existence of capital itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"12.68","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0069","text":"If, therefore, the capitalist mode of production presents itself on the one hand as a historical necessity for the transformation of the labour process into a social process, then, on the other hand, this social form of the labour process presents itself as a method applied by capital in order to exploit it more profitably by increasing its productive power.","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"12.69","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0070","text":"In its hitherto considered simple form, cooperation coincides with production on a larger scale, but does not constitute a fixed, characteristic form of a particular epoch of development of the capitalist mode of production. At most it appears approximately thus in the still handicraft-like beginnings of manufacture [Note: “Whether the united skill, industry and emulation of many together on the same work be not the way to advance it? And whether it had been otherwise possible for England, to have carried on her Woollen Manufacture to so great a perfection?” (Berkeley: “The Querist.” Lond. 1750, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"12.70","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0071","text":"521.)] and in that kind of large-scale agriculture which corresponds to the manufacturing period, and which differs essentially from peasant economy only through the mass of workers simultaneously employed and the extent of the concentrated means of production. Simple cooperation is still always the predominant form of such branches of production in which capital operates on a large scale, without division of labour or machinery playing a significant role.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"12.71","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0072","text":"Cooperation remains the basic form of the capitalist mode of production, although its simple form itself appears as a particular form alongside its more developed forms.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"12.72","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_2COO:P0072"]}],"source_digest":"9b1acee13d855d23f8d34feb3aa38e0105869252ca33938fc0875f3c0ff8f79d","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA","title":"3) Theilung der Arbeit und Manufaktur.","order":13,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_3TDA","text":"Die auf Theilung der Arbeit beruhende Cooperation schafft sich ihre\n klassische Gestalt in der Manufaktur. Als charakteristische Form des\n kapitalistischen Produktionsprozesses herrscht sie vor während der eigent-\n lichen Manufakturperiode, die, rauh angeschlagen, von Mitte des\n 16. Jahrhunderts bis zum letzten Dritttheil des achtzehnten währt.\n\n\n\n\nDie Manufaktur entspringt auf doppelte Weise.\n\n\n\n\nEntweder werden Arbeiter von verschiedenartigen, selbstständi-\n gen Handwerken, durch deren Hände ein Produkt bis zu seiner letzten Reife\n laufen muss, in eine Werkstatt unter dem Kommando desselben Kapitalisten\nvereinigt. Z. B. eine Kutsche war das Gesammtprodukt der Arbeiten einer\n grossen Anzahl unabhängiger Handwerker, wie Stellmacher, Sattler, Schnei-\n der, Schlosser, Gürtler, Drechsler, Posamentirer, Glaser, Maler, Lackirer,\n Vergolder u. s. w. In der Kutschenmanufaktur wurden alle diese ver-\n schiednen Handwerker in einem Arbeitshaus vereinigt, um einander gleich-\n zeitig in die Hand zu arbeiten. Man kann eine Kutsche zwar nicht vergolden,\n bevor sie gemacht ist. Werden aber viele Kutschen gleichzeitig gemacht, so\n kann ein Theil beständig vergoldet werden, während ein andrer Theil eine\n frühere Phase des Produktionsprozesses durchläuft. Soweit stehn wir\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"319\", \"facs\": \"#f0338\"} --> noch auf dem Boden der einfachen Cooperation, die ihr Material an Men-\n schen und Dingen vorfindet. Indess tritt sehr bald eine wesentliche\n Veränderung ein. Der Schneider, Schlosser, Gürtler u. s. w., der nur im\n Kutschenmachen beschäftigt ist, verliert nach und nach mit der Gewohn-\n heit auch die Fähigkeit, sein altes Handwerk in seiner ganzen Ausdehnung\n zu betreiben. Andrerseits erhält sein vereinseitigtes Thun jetzt die zweck-\n mässigste Form für die verengte Wirkungssphäre. Ursprünglich erschien\n die Kutschenmanufaktur als eine Kombination selbstständiger\n Handwerke. Sie wird allmälig Theilung der Kutschenpro-\n duktion in ihre verschiednen Sonderoperationen, wovon\n jede einzelne zur ausschliesslichen Funktion eines Arbeiters krystallisirt\n und deren Gesammtheit vom Verein dieser Theilarbeiter ver-\n richtet wird. Ebenso entstand die Tuchmanufaktur, und eine ganze\n Reihe andrer Manufakturen, aus der Kombination verschiedner Hand-\n werke unter dem Kommando desselben Kapitals [Note: Um ein mehr modernes Beispiel dieser Bildungsart der Manufaktur anzu-\n führen, folgendes Citat: Die Seidenspinnerei und Weberei von Lyon und Nimes\n „est toute patriarcale; elle emploie beaucoup de femmes et d’enfants, mais sans\n les épuiser ni les corrompre; elle les laisse dans leurs belles vallées de la Drôme,\n du Var, de l’Isère, de Vaucluse, pour y élever des vers et divider leurs cocons;\n jamais elle n’entre dans une véritable fabrique. Pour être aussi bien observé …\n le principe de la division du travail, s’y revêt d’un caractère spécial. Il y a bien\n des divideuses, des moulineurs, des teinturieurs, des encolleurs, puis des tisse-\n rands; mais ils ne sont pas réunis dans un même établissement, ne dépendent pas\n d’un même maître; tous ils sont indépendants.“ (A. Blanqui: „Cours\n d’Econ. Industrielle. Recueilli par A. Blaise. Paris (1838—39)“,\n p. 44—80 passim.) Seit Blanqui diess schrieb, sind die verschiednen unab-\n hängigen Arbeiter zum Theil in Fabriken vereinigt worden.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Manufaktur entspringt aber auch auf entgegengesetztem\n Wege. Es werden viele Handwerker, die Dasselbe oder Gleich-\n artiges thun, z. B. Papier oder Typen oder Nadeln machen, von dem-\n selben Kapital gleichzeitig in derselben Werkstatt beschäf-\n tigt. Es ist diess Cooperation in der einfachsten Form. Jeder dieser\n Handwerker (vielleicht mit einem oder zwei Gesellen) macht die ganze\n Waare und vollbringt also die verschiednen zu ihrer Herstellung erheisch-\n ten Operationen der Reihe nach. Er arbeitet in seiner alten handwerks-\n mässigen Weise fort. Indess veranlassen bald äussere Umstände die Kon-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"320\", \"facs\": \"#f0339\"} --> centration der Arbeiter in demselben Raum und die Gleichzeitigkeit ihrer\n Arbeiten anders zu vernutzen. Es soll z. B. ein grösseres Quantum fer-\n tiger Waare in einer bestimmten Zeitfrist geliefert werden. Die Arbeit\n wird daher vertheilt. Statt die verschiedenen Operationen von dem-\n selben Handwerker in einer zeitlichen Reihefolge verrichten zu lassen,\n werden sie von einander losgelöst, isolirt, räumlich neben einander gestellt,\n jede derselben einem andern Handwerker zugewiesen und alle zusammen\n von den Cooperirenden gleichzeitig ausgeführt. Diese zufällige Verthei-\n lung wiederholt sich, zeigt ihre eigenthümlichen Vortheile, und verknö-\n chert nach und nach zur systematischen Theilung der Arbeit. Aus\n dem individuellen Produkt eines selbstständigen Handwerkers, der\n vielerlei thut, verwandelt sich die Waare in das gesellschaftliche\n Produkt eines Vereins von Handwerkern, von denen jeder fortwährend nur\n eine und dieselbe Theiloperation verrichtet. Dieselben Operationen, die\n in einander flossen als successive Verrichtungen des deutschen zünftigen\n Papiermachers, verselbstständigten sich in der holländischen Papiermanufak-\n tur zu nebeneinander laufenden Theiloperationen vieler cooperirenden Ar-\n beiter. Der zünftige Nadler von Nürnberg bildet das Grundelement der eng-\n lischen Nadelmanufaktur. Während aber jener eine Nadler eine Reihe von\n vielleicht 20 Operationen nach einander durchlief, verrichteten hier bald 20\n Nadler neben einander, jeder nur eine der 20 Operationen, die in Folge von\n Erfahrungen noch viel weiter gespaltet, isolirt, und zu ausschliesslichen\n Funktionen einzelner Arbeiter verselbstständigt wurden.\n\n\n\n\nDie Ursprungsweise der Manufaktur, ihre Herausbildung aus dem\n Handwerk, ist also zwieschlächtig. Einerseits geht sie von der Kom-\n bination verschiedenartiger, selbstständiger Handwerke aus, die\n bis zu dem Punkt verunselbstständigt und vereinseitigt werden,\n wo sie nur noch einander ergänzende Theiloperationen im Produktions-\n prozess einer und derselben Waare bilden. Andrerseits geht sie von der\nCooperation gleichartiger Handwerker aus, zersetzt dasselbe\n individuelle Handwerk in seine verschiednen besondern Operationen, und\n isolirt und verselbstständigt diese bis zu dem Punkt, wo jede der-\n selben zur ausschliesslichen Funktion eines besondern Arbeiters wird. Einer-\n seits führt daher die Manufaktur Theilung der Arbeit in einen Produk-\n tionsprozess ein oder entwickelt sie weiter, andrerseits kombinirt sie früher\n geschiedene Handwerke. Welches aber immer ihr besondrer Ausgangs-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"321\", \"facs\": \"#f0340\"} --> punkt, ihre Schlussgestalt ist dieselbe — ein Produktionsmecha-\n nismus, dessen Organe Menschen sind.\n\n\n\n\nZum richtigen Verständniss der Theilung der Arbeit in der Manufak-\n tur ist es wesentlich folgende Punkte festzuhalten: Zunächst fällt die\nAnalyse des Produktionsprozesses in seine besondern\n Phasen hier ganz und gar zusammen mit der Zersetzung einer\n handwerksmässigen Thätigkeit in ihre verschiedenen\n Theiloperationen. Zusammengesetzt oder einfach, die Verrichtung\n bleibt handwerksmässig und daher abhängig von Kraft, Geschick,\n Schnelle, Sicherheit des Einzelarbeiters in Handhabung seines Instruments.\n Das Handwerk bleibt die Basis. Diese enge technologische Basis\n schliesst wirklich wissenschaftliche Analyse des Produktionsprozesses aus,\n da jeder Theilprozess, den das Produkt untergeht, als handwerksmässige\n Theilarbeit ausführbar sein muss. Eben weil das handwerksmässige Ge-\n schick so die Grundlage des Produktionsprozesses bleibt, wird jeder Ar-\n beiter ausschliesslich einer Theilfunktion angeeignet und seine Arbeits-\n kraft in das lebenslängliche Organ dieser Theilfunktion verwandelt. End-\n lich ist diese Theilung der Arbeit eine besondere Art der Coopera-\n tion, und manche ihrer Vortheile entspringen aus dem allgemeinen Wesen,\n nicht aus dieser besonderen Form der Cooperation.\n\n\n\n\nGehn wir nun näher auf das Einzelne ein, so ist zunächst klar, dass\n ein Arbeiter, der lebenslang eine und dieselbe einfache Operation verrichtet,\n seinen ganzen Körper in ihr automatisch einseitiges Organ verwandelt und\n daher weniger Zeit dazu verbraucht als der Handwerker, der eine ganze\n Reihe von Operationen abwechselnd ausführt. Der kombinirte Ge-\n sammtarbeiter, der den lebendigen Mechanismus der Manufaktur\n bildet, besteht aber aus lauter solchen einseitigen Theilarbeitern. Im Ver-\n gleich zum selbstständigen Handwerk wird daher mehr in weniger Zeit\n producirt oder die Produktivkraft der Arbeit gesteigert [Note: „The more any manufacture of much variety shall be distributed and\n assigned to different artists, the same must needs be better done and with greater\n expedition, with less loss of time and labour.“ („The Advantages of the\n East India Trade. Lond. 1720“, p. 71.)] . Auch vervoll-\n kommnet sich die Methode der Theilarbeit, nachdem sie zur ausschliess-\n lichen Funktion einer Person verselbstständigt ist. Die stete Wieder-\n [Running matter: I. 21] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"322\", \"facs\": \"#f0341\"} --> holung desselben beschränkten Thuns und die Koncentration der Aufmerk-\n samkeit auf diess Beschränkte lehren erfahrungsmässig den bezweckten\n Nutzeffekt mit geringstem Kraftaufwand erreichen. Da aber immer ver-\n schiedne Arbeitergenerationen gleichzeitig zusammenleben und in densel-\n ben Manufakturen zusammenwirken, befestigen, häufen und übertragen\n sich bald die so gewonnenen technischen Kunstgriffe [Note: „Easy labour is transmitted skill.“ (Th. Hodgskin l. c. p. 125.)] . Die Manu-\n faktur producirt in der That die Virtuosität des Detailarbeiters, indem sie\n die naturwüchsige Sonderung der Gewerbe, die sie in der Gesellschaft vor-\n fand, im Innern der Werkstatt reproducirt und systematisch zum Extrem\n treibt. Andrerseits entspricht ihre Verwandlung der Theilarbeit in den\n Lebensberuf eines Menschen dem Trieb früherer Gesellschaften, die Ge-\n werbe erblich zu machen, sie in Kasten zu versteinern, oder, wo be-\n stimmte historische Bedingungen dem Kastenwesen widersprechende Varia-\n bilität des Individuums erzeugen, die Arbeitssonderung wenigstens in\nZünfte zu verknöchern. Die Entstehung dieser Kasten und Zünfte folgt\n demselben Naturgesetz, das die Sonderung von Pflanzen und Thieren in Arten\n und Unterarten regelt, nur dass auf einem gewissen Entwicklungsgrad die\n Erblichkeit der Kasten oder die Ausschliesslichkeit der Zünfte als ge-\n sellschaftliches Gesetz dekretirt wird [Note: „Auch die Künste sind … in Aegypten zu dem gehörigen Grad von\n Vollkommenheit gediehn. Denn in diesem Lande allein dürfen die Handwerker\n durchaus nicht in die Geschäfte einer andern Bürgerklasse eingreifen, sondern\n bloss den nach dem Gesetz ihrem Stamme erblich zugehörigen Beruf treiben …\n Bei andern Völkern findet man, dass die Gewerbsleute ihre Aufmerksamkeit auf\n zu viele Gegenstände vertheilen … Bald versuchen sie es mit dem Landbau, bald\n lassen sie sich in Handelsgeschäfte ein, bald befassen sie sich mit zwei oder drei\n Künsten zugleich. In Freistaaten laufen sie meist in die Volksversammlungen …\n In Aegypten dagegen verfällt jeder Handwerker in schwere Strafen, wenn er sich\n in Staatsgeschäfte mischt, oder mehrere Künste zugleich treibt. So kann nichts\n ihren Berußfleiss stören … Zudem, wie sie von ihren Vorfahren viele Regeln\n haben, sind sie eifrig darauf bedacht, noch neue Vortheile aufzufinden.“ (Dio-\n dorus Siculus: Historische Bibliothek l. I, c. 74.)] . „Die Musline von\n Dakka sind an Feinheit, die Kattune und andre Zeuge von Koromandel\n an Pracht und Dauerhaftigkeit der Farben, niemals übertroffen worden.\n Und dennoch werden sie producirt ohne Kapital, Maschinerie, Theilung\n der Arbeit, oder irgend eins der andern Mittel, die der Fabrikation in\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"323\", \"facs\": \"#f0342\"} --> Europa so viele Vortheile bieten. Der Weber ist ein vereinzeltes Indivi-\n duum, der das Gewebe auf Bestellung eines Kunden verfertigt und mit\n einem Webstuhl von der einfachsten Konstruktion, manchmal nur beste-\n hend aus hölzernen Stangen, die roh zusammengefügt sind. Er besitzt\n selbst keinen Apparat zum Aufziehn der Kette, der Webstuhl muss daher\n in seiner ganzen Länge ausgestreckt bleiben und wird so unförmlich und\n weit, dass er keinen Raum findet in der Hütte des Producenten, der seine\n Arbeit daher in freier Luft verrichten muss, wo sie durch jede Wetter-\n änderung unterbrochen wird“ [Note: „Historical and descriptive Account of Brit. India etc. by\n Hugh Murray, James Wilson etc.“ Edinburgh 1832, v. II, p. 449.\n Der indische Webstuhl ist hochschäftig, d. h. die Kette ist vertikal aufgespannt.] . Es ist nur das von Generation auf\n Generation gehäufte und von Vater auf Sohn vererbte Sondergeschick,\n das dem Hindu wie der Spinne diese Virtuosität verleiht. Und dennoch\n verrichtet ein solcher indischer Weber sehr komplicirte Arbeit, verglichen\n mit der Mehrzahl der Manufakturarbeiter.\n\n\n\n\nEin Handwerker, der die verschiedenen Theilprozesse in der Pro-\n duktion eines Machwerks nach einander ausführt, muss bald den Platz,\n bald die Instrumente wechseln. Der Uebergang von einer Operation zur\n andern unterbricht den Fluss seiner Arbeit und bildet gewissermassen\nPoren in seinem Arbeitstag. Diese Poren verdichten, sobald er den\n ganzen Tag ein und dieselbe Operation kontinuirlich verrichtet oder sie\n verschwinden in dem Masse, wie der Wechsel seiner Operationen abnimmt.\n Die gesteigerte Produktivität ist hier entweder der zunehmenden Ausgabe\n von Arbeitskraft in einem gegebnen Zeitraum geschuldet, also wach-\n sender Intensivität der Arbeit, oder einer Abnahme des\n unproduktiven Verzehrs von Arbeitskraft. Der Ueberschuss\n von Kraftaufwand nämlich, den jeder Uebergang aus der Ruhe in die Be-\n wegung erheischt, kompensirt sich bei längerer Fortdauer der einmal er-\n reichten Normalgeschwindigkeit. Andrerseits zerstört die Kontinuität\n gleichförmiger Arbeit die Spann- und Schwungkraft der Lebensgeister,\n die im Wechsel der Thätigkeit selbst ihre Erholung und ihren Reiz\n finden.\n\n\n\n\nDie Produktivität der Arbeit hängt nicht nur von der Virtuosität\n des Arbeiters ab, sondern auch von der Vollkommenheit seiner Werk-\n [Running matter: 21*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"324\", \"facs\": \"#f0343\"} -->zeuge. Werkzeuge derselben Art, wie Schneide-, Bohr-, Stoss-, Schlag-\n instrumente u. s. w. werden in verschiedenen Arbeitsprozessen gebraucht,\n und in demselben Arbeitsprozess dient dasselbe Instrument zu verschiednen\n Verrichtungen. Sobald jedoch die verschiednen Operationen eines Ar-\n beitsprozesses von einander losgelöst sind und jede Theiloperation in der\n Hand des Theilarbeiters eine möglichst entsprechende und daher ausschliess-\n liche Form erhält, werden Veränderungen der vorher zu verschiednen\n Zwecken dienenden Werkzeuge nothwendig. Die Richtung ihres Form-\n wechsels ergiebt sich aus der Erfahrung der besondern Schwierigkeiten,\n welche die unveränderte Form in den Weg legt. Die Differen-\n zirung der Arbeitsinstrumente, wodurch Instrumente derselben Art be-\n sondre feste Formen für jede besondre Nutzanwendung erhalten, und ihre\nSpezialisirung, wodurch jedes solches Sonderinstrument nur in der\n Hand spezifischer Theilarbeiter in seinem ganzen Umfang wirkt, charak-\n terisiren die Manufaktur. Zu Birmingham allein producirt man etwa 500\n Varietäten von Hämmern, wovon jeder nicht nur für einen besondern Pro-\n duktionsprozess, sondern eine Anzahl Varietäten oft nur für verschiedne\n Operationen in demselben Prozess dient. Die Manufakturperiode verein-\n facht, verbessert und vermannigfacht die Arbeitswerkzeuge durch deren\n Anpassung an die ausschliesslichen Sonderfunktionen der Theilarbeiter [Note: Darwin bemerkt in seinem Epoche machenden Werk über „die Ent-\n stehung der Arten“ mit Bezug auf die natürlichen Organe der Pflanzen und\n Thiere: „So lange ein und dasselbe Organ verschiedene Arbeiten zu verrichten\n hat, lässt sich ein Grund für seine Veränderlichkeit vielleicht darin finden, dass\n natürliche Züchtung jede kleine Abweichung der Form weniger sorgfältig erhält\n oder unterdrückt, als wenn dasselbe Organ nur zu einem besondern Zwecke allein\n bestimmt wäre. So mögen Messer, welche allerlei Dinge zu schneiden bestimmt\n sind, im Ganzen so ziemlich von einerlei Form sein, während ein nur zu einerlei\n Gebrauch bestimmtes Werkzeug für jeden andern Gebrauch auch eine andre Form\n haben muss.“] .\n Sie schafft damit zugleich eine der materiellen Bedingungen der\n Maschinerie, die aus einer Kombination einfacher Instrumente besteht.\n\n\n\n\nDer Detailarbeiter und sein Instrument bilden die einfachen Ele-\n mente der Manufaktur. Wenden wir uns jetzt zu ihrem Gesammt-\n mechanismus.\n\n\n\n\nDie Gliederung der Manufaktur besitzt zwei Grundformen, die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"325\", \"facs\": \"#f0344\"} --> trotz gelegentlicher Verschlingung zwei wesentlich verschiedne Arten bil-\n den und namentlich auch bei der späteren Verwandlung der Manufaktur\n in die maschinenartig betriebene, grosse Industrie eine ganz verschiedne\n Rolle spielen. Dieser Doppelcharakter entspringt aus der Natur des\n Machwerks selbst. Es wird entweder gebildet durch bloss mechanische\n Zusammensetzung selbstständiger Theilprodukte oder verdankt seine fer-\n tige Gestalt einer Reihenfolge zusammenhängender Prozesse und Manipu-\n lationen.\n\n\n\n\nEine Lokomotive z. B. besteht aus mehr als 5000 selbstständigen\n Theilen. Sie kann jedoch nicht als Beispiel der ersten Art der eigent-\n lichen Manufaktur gelten, weil sie ein Gebilde der grossen Industrie ist.\n Wohl aber die Uhr, an welcher auch William Petty die manufaktur-\n mässige Theilung der Arbeit veranschaulicht. Aus dem individuellen\n Werk eines Nürnberger Handwerkers verwandelte sich die Uhr in das ge-\n sellschaftliche Produkt einer Unzahl von Theilarbeitern, wie Rohwerk-\n macher, Uhrfedermacher, Zifferblattmacher, Spiralfedermacher, Steinloch-\n und Rubinhebelmacher, Zeigermacher, Gehäusemacher, Schraubenmacher,\n Vergolder, mit vielen Unterabtheilungen, wie z. B. Räderfabrikant\n (Messing- und Stahlräder wieder geschieden), Triebmacher, Zeigerwerk-\n macher, acheveur de pignon (befestigt die Räder auf den Trieben, polirt\n die facettes u. s. w.), Zapfenmacher, planteur de finissage (setzt verschiedne\n Räder und Triebe in das Werk), finisseur de barillet (lässt Zähne ein-\n schneiden, macht die Löcher zur richtigen Weite, härtet Stellung und Ge-\n sperr), Hemmungmacher, bei der Cylinderhemmung wieder Cylindermacher,\n Steigradmacher, Unruhemacher, Raquettemacher (das Rückwerk, woran\n die Uhr regulirt wird), planteur d’échappement (eigentliche Hemmung-\n macher); dann der repasseur de barillet (macht das Federhaus und\n Stellung ganz fertig), Stahlpolirer, Räderpolirer, Schraubenpolirer, Zahlen-\n maler, Blattmacher (schmelzen das Email auf das Kupfer), fabricant\n de pendants (macht bloss die Bügel des Gehäuses), finisseur de charnière\n (steckt das Messingstift in die Mitte des Gehäuses u. s. w.), faiseur de\n secret (macht die Federn im Gehäuse, die den Deckel aufspringen machen),\n graveur, ciliceur, polisseur de boite u. s. w., u. s. w., endlich der re-\n passeur, der die ganze Uhr zusammensetzt und sie gehend abliefert. Nur\n wenige Theile der Uhr laufen durch verschiedne Hände und alle diese\n membra disjecta sammeln sich erst in der Hand, die sie schliesslich in ein\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"326\", \"facs\": \"#f0345\"} --> mechanisches Ganze verbindet. Diess äusserliche Verhältniss des fertigen\n Produkts zu seinen verschiedenartigen Elementen lässt hier, wie bei ähn-\n lichem Machwerk, die Kombination der Theilarbeiter in derselben\n Werkstatt zufällig. Die Theilarbeiten können selbst wieder als von ein-\n ander unabhängige Handwerke betrieben werden, wie im Kanton Waadt\n und Neufchatel, während in Genf z. B. grosse Uhrenmanufakturen bestehn,\n d. h. unmittelbare Cooperation der Theilarbeiter unter dem Kommando\neines Kapitals stattfindet. Auch im letztren Fall werden Zifferblatt, Feder\n und Gehäuse selten in der Manufaktur selbst verfertigt. Der kombinirte\n manufakturmässige Betrieb ist hier nur unter ausnahmsweisen Verhält-\n nissen profitlich, weil die Konkurrenz unter den Arbeitern, die zu Hause\n arbeiten wollen, am grössten ist, die Zersplitterung der Produktion in eine\n Masse heterogener Prozesse wenig Verwendung gemeinschaftlicher Arbeits-\n mittel erlaubt, und der Kapitalist bei der zerstreuten Fabrikation die Aus-\n lage für Arbeitsgebäude u. s. w. erspart [Note: Genf hat im Jahr 1854 80,000 Uhren producirt, noch nicht ein Fünftheil\n der Uhrenproduktion des Kanton Neufchatel. Chaux-de-Fonds, das man als eine\n einzige Uhrenmanufaktur betrachten kann, liefert allein jährlich doppelt so viel als\n Genf. Von 1850—61 lieferte Genf 750,000 Uhren. Siehe: „Report from\n Geneva on the Watch Trade“ in „Reports by H. M’s. Secretaries\n of Embassy and Legation on the Manufactures, Commerce etc.\n No. 6. 1863.“ Wenn die Zusammenhangslosigkeit der Prozesse, worin die Pro-\n duktion nur zusammengesetzter Machwerke zerfällt, an und für sich die\n Verwandlung solcher Manufakturen in den Maschinenbetrieb der grossen Industrie\n sehr erschwert, kommen bei der Uhr noch zwei andre Hindernisse hinzu, die Klein-\n heit und Delikatesse ihrer Elemente, und ihr Luxuscharakter, daher ihre Varietät,\n so dass z. B. in den besten Londoner Häusern das ganze Jahr hindurch kaum ein\n Dutzend Uhren gemacht werden, die sich ähnlich sehn. Die Uhrenfabrik von\n Vacheron et Constantin, die mit Erfolg Maschinerie anwendet, liefert auch höch-\n stens 3—4 verschiedne Varietäten von Grösse und Form.] . Indess ist auch das Ver-\n hältniss dieser Detailarbeiter, die zu Hause, aber für einen Kapitalisten\n (Fabrikant, établisseur) arbeiten, ganz und gar verschieden von dem des\n selbstständigen Handwerkers, der für seine eignen Kunden arbeitet [Note: In der Uhrmacherei, diesem klassischen Beispiel der heterogenen Manu-\n faktur, kann man sehr genau die oben erwähnte, aus der Zersetzung der handwerks-\n mässigen Thätigkeit entspringende Differenzirung und Spezialisirung\n der Arbeitsinstrumente studiren.] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"327\", \"facs\": \"#f0346\"} -->\n\nDie zweite Art der Manufaktur, ihre vollendete Form, producirt\n Machwerke, die zusammenhängende Entwicklungsphasen, eine Reihenfolge\n von Stufenprozessen durchlaufen, wie z. B. der Draht in der Nähnadel-\n manufaktur die Hände von 72 und selbst 92 spezifischen Theilarbeitern\n durchläuft.\n\n\n\n\nSoweit solche Manufaktur ursprünglich zerstreute Handwerke kom-\n binirt, vermindert sie die räumliche Trennung zwischen den beson-\n dern Produktionsphasen des Machwerks. Die Zeit seines Uebergangs aus\n einem Stadium in das andre wird verkürzt, ebenso die Arbeit, welche diese\n Uebergänge vermittelt [Note: „In so close a cohabitation of the People, the carriage must needs be\n less.“ („The Advantages of the East India Trade“, p. 166.)] . Im Vergleich zum Handwerk wird so Produk-\n tivkraft gewonnen, und zwar entspringt dieser Gewinn aus dem allge-\n meinen cooperativen Charakter der Manufaktur. Andrerseits\n bedingt ihr eigenthümliches Prinzip der Theilung der Arbeit eine Iso-\n lirung der verschiednen Produktionsphasen, die als eben so viele hand-\n werksmässige Theilarbeiten gegen einander verselbstständigt sind. Die\n Herstellung und Erhaltung des Zusammenhangs zwischen den isolirten\n Funktionen ernöthigt beständigen Transport des Machwerks aus einer\n Hand in die andre und aus einem Prozess in den andern. Vom Stand-\n punkt der grossen Industrie tritt diess als eine charakteristische,\n kostspielige und dem Prinzip der Manufaktur immanente Beschränkt-\n heit hervor [Note: „The isolation of the different stages of manufacture consequent upon\n the employment of manual labour adds immensely to the cost of production, the\n loss mainly arising from the mere removals from one process to another.“ („The\n Industry of Nations. Lond. 1855“, Part II, p. 200.)] .\n\n\n\n\nBetrachtet man ein bestimmtes Quantum Rohmaterial, z. B. von\n Lumpen in der Papiermanufaktur oder von Draht in der Nadelmanufak-\n tur, so durchläuft es in den Händen der verschiednen Theilarbeiter eine\n zeitliche Stufenfolge von Produktionsphasen bis zu seiner Schlussgestalt.\n Betrachtet man dagegen die Werkstatt als einen Gesammtmechanismus,\n so befindet sich das Rohmaterial gleichzeitig in allen seinen Produk-\n tionsphasen auf einmal. Mit einem Theil seiner vielen instrumentbewaff-\n neten Hände zieht der aus den Detailarbeitern kombinirte Gesammtarbeiter\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"328\", \"facs\": \"#f0347\"} --> den Draht, während er gleichzeitig mit andern Händen und Werkzeugen\n ihn streckt, mit andern schneidet, spitzt u. s. w. Aus einem zeitlichen\n Nacheinander sind die verschiednen Stufenprozesse in ein räumliches\n Nebeneinander verwandelt. Daher Lieferung von mehr fertiger Waare\n in demselben Zeitraum [Note: „It (the division of labour) produces also an economy of time, by separat-\n ing the work into its different branches, all of which may be carried on into exe-\n cution at the same moment … By carrying on all the different processes at once,\n which an individual must have executed separately, it becomes possible to produce\n a multitude of pins for instance completely finished in the same time as a single\n pin might have been either cut or pointed.“ (Dugald Stewart l. c. p. 319.)] . Jene Gleichzeitigkeit entspringt zwar aus der\n allgemeinen cooperativen Form des Gesammtprozesses, aber die Manufak-\n tur findet nicht nur die Bedingungen der Cooperation vor, sondern schafft\n sie theilweise erst durch die Zerlegung der handwerksmässigen Thätigkeit.\n Andrerseits erreicht sie diese gesellschaftliche Organisation des Arbeits-\n prozesses nur durch Festschmieden desselben Arbeiters an dasselbe Detail.\n\n\n\n\nDa das Theilprodukt jedes Theilarbeiters zugleich nur eine besondre\n Entwicklungsstufe desselben Machwerks ist, liefert jeder Arbeiter oder\n jede Arbeitergruppe der andern ihr Rohmaterial. Das Arbeitsresultat des\n einen bildet den Ausgangspunkt für die Arbeit des andern. Der eine Ar-\n beiter beschäftigt daher hier unmittelbar den andern. Die nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit zur Erreichung des bezweckten Nutzeffekts in jedem Theilpro-\n zess wird erfahrungsmässig festgestellt und der Gesammtmechanis-\n mus der Manufaktur beruht auf der Voraussetzung, dass in ge-\n gebner Arbeitszeit ein gegebnes Resultat erzielt wird. Nur\n unter dieser Voraussetzung können die verschiednen, einander ergänzenden\n Arbeitsprozesse ununterbrochen, gleichzeitig und räumlich neben einander\n fortgehn. Es ist klar, dass diese unmittelbare Abhängigkeit\n der Arbeiten und daher der Arbeiter von einander jeden Einzelnen zwingt,\n nur die nothwendige Zeit zu seiner Funktion zu verwenden, und so eine\n ganz andere Kontinuität, Gleichförmigkeit, Regelmässigkeit, Ordnung [Note: „The more variety of artists to every manufacture … the greater the\n order and regularity of every work, the same must needs be done in less time, the\n labour must be less.“ („The Advantages etc.“ p. 68.)] \n und namentlich auch Intensivität der Arbeit erzeugt wird als im\n unabhängigen Handwerk oder selbst der einfachen Cooperation. Dass\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"329\", \"facs\": \"#f0348\"} --> auf eine Waare nur die zu ihrer Herstellung gesellschaftlich noth-\n wendige Arbeitszeit verwandt wird, erscheint bei der Waarenpro-\n duktion überhaupt als äusserer Zwang der Konkurrenz, weil, oberflächlich\n ausgedrückt, jeder einzelne Producent die Waare zu ihrem Marktpreis\n verkaufen muss. Lieferung von gegebnem Produktenquantum in gegebner\n Arbeitszeit wird dagegen hier in der Manufaktur technologisches\n Gesetz des Produktionsprozesses selbst [Note: Indess erreicht der manufakturmässige Betrieb diess Resultat in vielen\n Zweigen nur unvollkommen, weil er die allgemeinen chemischen und physischen\n Bedingungen des Produktionsprozesses nicht mit Sicherheit zu kontroliren weiss.] .\n\n\n\n\nVerschiedne Operationen bedürfen jedoch ungleicher Zeitlängen\n und liefern daher in gleichen Zeiträumen ungleiche Quanta von Theilproduk-\n ten. Soll also derselbe Arbeiter Tag aus Tag ein stets nur dieselbe Operation\n verrichten, so müssen für verschiedne Operationen verschiedne Ver-\n hältnisszahlen von Arbeitern verwandt werden, z. B. 4 Giesser\n und 2 Abbrecher auf einen Frottirer in einer Typenmanufaktur, wo der\n Giesser stündlich 2000 Typen giesst, der Abbrecher 4000 abbricht und\n der Frottirer 8000 blank reibt. Hier kehrt das Prinzip der Cooperation\n in seiner einfachsten Form zurück, gleichzeitige Beschäftigung Vieler, die\nGleichartiges thun, aber jetzt als Ausdruck eines organischen Ver-\n hältnisses. Die manufakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit vereinfacht und\n vermannigfacht also nicht nur die qualitativ unterschiednen Organe des\n gesellschaftlichen Gesammtarbeiters, sondern schafft auch ein mathematisch\n festes Verhältniss für den quantitativen Umfang dieser Organe, d. h.\n für die relative Arbeiteranzahl oder relative Grösse der Arbeiter-\n gruppen in jeder Sonderfunktion. Sie entwickelt mit der qualitati-\n ven Gliederung die quantitative Regel und Proportio-\n nalität des gesellschaftlichen Arbeitsprozesses.\n\n\n\n\nIst die passendste Verhältnisszahl der verschiednen Gruppen von\n Theilarbeitern erfahrungsmässig festgesetzt für eine bestimmte Stufenleiter\n der Produktion, so kann man diese Stufenleiter nur ausdehnen, indem man\n ein Multipel jeder besondern Arbeitergruppe verwendet [Note: „Wenn die Erfahrung, je nach der besondren Natur der Produkte jeder\n Manufaktur, sowohl die vortheilhafteste Art die Fabrikation in Theiloperationen\n zu spalten, als auch die für sie nöthige Arbeiterzahl kennen gelehrt hat, werden] . Es\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"330\", \"facs\": \"#f0349\"} --> kommt hinzu, dass dasselbe Individuum gewisse Arbeiten eben so gut auf\n grösserer als kleinerer Staffel ausführt, z. B. die Arbeit der Oberaufsicht,\n den Transport der Theilprodukte aus einer Produktionsphase in die andre\n u. s. w. Die Verselbstständigung dieser Funktionen, oder ihre Zuweisung\n an besondere Arbeiter, wird also erst vortheilhaft mit Vergrösserung der\n beschäftigten Arbeiterzahl, aber diese Vergrösserung muss sofort alle\n Gruppen proportionell ergreifen.\n\n\n\n\nDie einzelne Gruppe, als eine Anzahl von Arbeitern, die die-\n selbe Theilfunktion verrichten, besteht aus homogenen Elementen\n und bildet ein einfaches Organ des Gesammtmechanismus. In verschied-\n nen Manufakturen jedoch ist die Gruppe selbst ein gegliederter Ar-\n beitskörper, während der Gesammtmechanismus durch die Wieder-\n holung oder Vervielfältigung dieser produktiven Elementarorganismen ge-\n bildet wird. Nehmen wir z. B. die Manufaktur von Glasflaschen. Sie\n zerfällt in drei wesentlich unterschiedne Phasen. Erstens die vorbe-\n reitende Phase, wie Bereitung der Glaskomposition, Mengung von\n Sand, Kalk u. s. w. und Schmelzung dieser Komposition zu einer flüssigen\n Glasmasse [Note: In England ist der Schmelzofen getrennt vom Glasofen, an dem das Glas\n verarbeitet wird, in Belgien z. B. dient derselbe Ofen zu beiden Prozessen.] . In dieser ersten Phase sind verschiedne Theilarbeiter be-\n schäftigt, ebenso in der Schlussphase, der Entfernung der Flaschen\n aus den Trockenöfen, ihrer Sortirung, Verpackung u. s. w. Zwischen\n beiden Phasen steht in der Mitte die eigentliche Glasmacherei oder\n Verarbeitung der flüssigen Glasmasse. An demselben Munde eines Glas-\n ofens arbeitet eine Gruppe, die in England das „hole“ (Loch) heisst, und\n aus einem bottle maker oder finisher, einem blower, einem gatherer, einem\n putter up oder whetter off und einem taker in zusammengesetzt ist. Diese\n fünf Theilarbeiter bilden eben so viele Sonderorgane eines einzigen Arbeits-\n körpers, der nur als Einheit, also nur durch unmittelbare Cooperation der\n Fünf wirken kann. Fehlt ein Glied des fünftheiligen Körpers, so ist er\n paralysirt. Derselbe Glasofen hat aber verschiedne Oeffnungen, in Eng-\n [Note: alle Etablissements, die kein exaktes Multiplum dieser Zahl anwenden, mit weniger\n Oekonomie fabriziren . . . . Diess ist eine der Ursachen der kolossalen Ausdeh-\n nung industrieller Etablissements.“ (Ch. Babbage: „On the Economy of\n Machinery.“ 2nd ed. Lond. 1832, ch. XX.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"331\", \"facs\": \"#f0350\"} --> land z. B. 4—6, deren jede einen irdenen Schmelztiegel mit flüssigem\n Glas birgt, und wovon jede eine eigne Arbeitergruppe von derselben fünf-\n gliedrigen Form beschäftigt. Die Gliederung jeder einzelnen Gruppe be-\n ruht hier unmittelbar auf der Theilung der Arbeit, während das Band\n zwischen den verschiednen gleichartigen Gruppen einfache Cooperation\n ist, die eins der Produktionsmittel, hier den Glasofen, durch gemeinsamen\n Konsum ökonomischer verbraucht. Ein solcher Glasofen mit seinen 4—6\n Gruppen bildet eine Glashütte, und eine Glasmanufaktur umfasst eine\n Mehrzahl solcher Hütten, zugleich mit den Vorrichtungen und Arbeitern\n für die einleitenden und abschliessenden Produktionsphasen.\n\n\n\n\nEndlich kann die Manufaktur, wie sie theilweis aus der Kombination\n verschiedner Handwerke entspringt, sich zu einer Kombination verschiedner\n Manufakturen entwickeln. Die grösseren englischen Glashütten z. B. fabri-\n ciren ihre irdenen Schmelztiegel selbst, weil von deren Güte das Gelingen\n oder Misslingen des Produkts wesentlich abhängt. Die Manufaktur eines\n Produktionsmittels wird hier mit der Manufaktur des Produkts verbunden.\n Umgekehrt kann die Manufaktur des Produkts verbunden werden mit Ma-\n nufakturen, worin es selbst wieder als Rohmaterial dient, oder mit deren\n Produkten es später zusammengesetzt wird. So findet man z. B. die\n Manufaktur von Flintglas kombinirt mit der Glasschleiferei und der Gelb-\n giesserei, letztere für die metallische Einfassung mannigfacher Glasartikel.\n Die verschiednen kombinirten Manufakturen bilden dann mehr oder minder\n räumlich getrennte Departemente einer Gesammtmanufaktur, zugleich von\n einander unabhängige Produktionsprozesse, jeder mit eigner Theilung der\n Arbeit. Trotz mancher technischer und ökonomischer Vortheile, welche\n die kombinirte Manufaktur bietet, gewinnt sie, auf eigner Grundlage,\n keine wirklich technologische Einheit. Diese entsteht erst bei ihrer Ver-\n wandlung in den maschinenmässigen Betrieb.\n\n\n\n\nDie Manufakturperiode, welche Verminderung der zur Waarenpro-\n duktion nothwendigen Arbeitszeit bald als bewusstes Prinzip aus-\n spricht [Note: Man kann diess unter andern ersehn aus den Schriften von W. Petty, John\n Bellers, Andrew Yarranton, „The Advantages of the East India Trade“\n und J. Vanderlint.] , entwickelt sporadisch auch den Gebrauch von Maschinen,\n namentlich für gewisse einfache erste Prozesse, die massenhaft\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"332\", \"facs\": \"#f0351\"} --> und mit grossem Kraftaufwand auszuführen sind. So wird z. B. bald in\n der Papiermanufaktur das Zermalmen der Lumpen durch Papier-\n mühlen und in der Metallfabrik das Zerstossen der Erze durch sogenannte\nPochmühlen verrichtet [Note: Noch gegen Ende des 16. Jahrhunderts bedient sich Frankreich der\n Mörser und Siebe zum Pochen und Waschen der Erze.] . Die elementarische Form aller Maschinerie\n hatte das römische Kaiserreich überliefert in der Wassermühle [Note: Die ganze Entwicklungsgeschichte der Maschinerie lässt sich verfolgen an\n der Geschichte der Getreidemühlen. Die Fabrik heisst im Englischen immer\n noch mill. In deutschen technologischen Schriften aus den ersten Decennien\n des 19. Jahrhunderts findet man noch den Ausdruck Mühle nicht nur für alle mit\n Naturkräften getriebne Maschinerie, sondern selbst für alle Manufakturen, die\n maschinenartige Apparate anwenden.] . Die\n Handwerksperiode vermachte die grossen Erfindungen des Kompasses,\n des Pulvers, der Buchdruckerei und der automatischen Uhr. Im\n Grossen und Ganzen jedoch spielt die Maschinerie jene Nebenrolle, die\nAdam Smith ihr neben der Theilung der Arbeit anweist [Note: Wie man aus dem Vierten Buch dieser Schrift näher sehn wird, hat\n A. Smith keinen einzigen neuen Satz über die Theilung der Arbeit aufgestellt.\n Was ihn aber als den zusammenfassenden politischen Oekonomen der Manu-\n fakturperiode charakterisirt, ist der Accent, den er auf die Theilung der\n Arbeit legt. Die untergeordnete Rolle, die er der Maschinerie anweist, rief im\n Beginn der grossen Industrie Lauderdale’s, in einer weiter entwickelten\n Epoche Ure’s Polemik hervor. A. Smith verwechselt auch die Differenzirung\n der Instrumente, wobei die Theilarbeiter der Manufaktur selbst sehr thätig\n waren, mit der Maschinenerfindung. Es sind nicht Manufakturarbeiter,\n sondern Gelehrte, Handwerker, selbst Bauern (Brindley) u. s. w., die hier eine\n Rolle spielen.] .\n Sehr wichtig wurde die sporadische Anwendung der Maschinerie im\n 17. Jahrhundert, weil sie den grossen Mathematikern jener Zeit prak-\n tische Anhaltspunkte und Reizmittel zur Schöpfung der modernen Mechanik\n darbot.\n\n\n\n\nDie spezifische Maschinerie der Manufakturperiode\n bleibt der aus vielen Theilarbeitern kombinirte Gesammtarbeiter\n selbst. Die verschiednen Operationen, die der Producent einer Waare ab-\n wechselnd verrichtet und die sich im Ganzen seines Arbeitsprozesses ver-\n schlingen, nehmen ihn verschiedenartig in Anspruch. In der einen muss\n er mehr Kraft entwickeln, in der andern mehr Gewandtheit, in der dritten\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"333\", \"facs\": \"#f0352\"} --> mehr geistige Aufmerksamkeit u. s. w., und dasselbe Individuum besitzt\n diese Eigenschaften nicht in gleichem Grad. Nach der Trennung, Ver-\n selbstständigung und Isolirung der verschiednen Operationen werden die\n Arbeiter ihren vorwiegenden Eigenschaften gemäss getheilt, klassificirt und\n gruppirt. Bilden ihre Naturbesonderheiten die Grundlage, worauf sich\n die Theilung der Arbeit pfropft, so entwickelt die Manufaktur, einmal ein-\n geführt, Arbeitskräfte, die von Natur nur zu einseitiger Sonderfunktion\n taugen. Der Gesammtarbeiter besitzt jetzt alle produktiven Eigen-\n schaften in gleich hohem Grad der Virtuosität und verausgabt sie zugleich\n auf’s ökonomischste, indem er alle seine Organe, individualisirt in besondern\n Arbeitern oder Arbeitergruppen, ausschliesslich zu ihren spezifischen Funk-\n tionen verwendet [Note: „Indem man das Machwerk in mehrere verschiedne Operationen theilt,\n deren jede verschiedne Grade von Gewandtheit und Kraft erheischt, kann der\n Manufakturherr sich genau das jeder Operation entsprechende Quantum von Kraft\n und Gewandtheit verschaffen. Wäre dagegen das ganze Werk von einem Arbeiter\n zu verrichten, so müsste dasselbe Individuum genug Gewandtheit für die delika-\n testen und genug Kraft für die mühseligsten Operationen besitzen.“ (Ch. Bab-\n bage l. c. ch. XIX.)] . Die Einseitigkeit und selbst die Unvollkommenheit\n des Theilarbeiters werden zu seiner Vollkommenheit als Glied des Gesammt-\n arbeiters [Note: Z. B. einseitige Muskelentwicklung, Knochenverkrümmung u. s. w.] . Die Gewohnheit einer einseitigen Funktion verwandelt ihn\n in ihr naturgemäss sicher wirkendes Organ, während der Zusammenhang\n des Gesammtmechanismus ihn zwingt, mit der Regelmässigkeit eines Ma-\n schinentheils zu wirken [Note: Sehr richtig antwortet Herr Wm. Marschall, der general manager einer\n Glasmanufaktur, auf die Frage des Untersuchungskommissärs, wie die Arbeitsam-\n keit unter den beschäftigten Jungen aufrecht erhalten werde: „They cannot well\n neglect their work; when they once begin, they must go on; they are just\n the same as parts of a machine.“ („Child. Empl. Comm. Fourth\n Report“ 1865, p. 247.)] . Da die verschiednen Funktionen des Ge-\n sammtarbeiters einfacher oder zusammengesetzter, niedriger oder höher,\n erheischen seine Organe, die individuellen Arbeitskräfte, sehr verschiedne\n Grade der Ausbildung und besitzen daher sehr verschiedne Werthe. Die\n Manufaktur entwickelt also eine Hierarchie der Arbeitskräfte,\n der eine Stufenleiter der Arbeitslöhne entspricht. Wird einerseits der in-\n dividuelle Arbeiter einer einseitigen Funktion angeeignet und lebens-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"334\", \"facs\": \"#f0353\"} --> lang annexirt, so werden eben so sehr die verschiednen Arbeitsver-\n richtungen jener Hierarchie der natürlichen und erworbnen Geschicklich-\n keiten angepasst [Note: Dr. Ure in seiner Apotheose der grossen Industrie fühlt die eigen-\n thümlichen Charaktere der Manufaktur schärfer heraus als frühere Oekonomen,\n die nicht sein polemisches Interesse hatten, und selbst als seine Zeitgenossen, z. B.\nBabbage, der ihm zwar durchaus überlegen ist als gelehrter Mathematiker und\n Mechaniker, aber dennoch die grosse Industrie eigentlich nur vom Standpunkt der\n Manufaktur auffasst. Ure bemerkt: „Die Aneignung der Arbeiter an\n jede Sonderoperation bildet das Wesen der Vertheilung der Arbeiten.“ Andrer-\n seits bezeichnet er diese Vertheilung als „Anpassung der Arbeiten an die\n verschiednen individuellen Fähigkeiten“ und charakterisirt endlich das ganze\n Manufaktursystem als „ein System von Gradationen nach dem Rang der Geschick-\n lichkeit“, als „eine Theilung der Arbeit nach den verschiednen Graden des Ge-\n schicks“ u. s. w. Ure l. c. t. I, p. 28—35 passim.] . Jeder Produktionsprozess bedingt indess ge-\n wisse einfache Hanthierungen, deren jeder Mensch, wie er geht und steht,\n fähig ist. Auch sie werden jetzt von ihrem flüssigen Zusammenhang mit\n den inhaltvolleren Momenten der Thätigkeit losgelöst und zu ausschliess-\n lichen Funktionen verknöchert. Die Manufaktur erzeugt daher in jedem\n Handwerk, das sie ergreift, eine Klasse sogenannter ungeschickter\n Arbeiter, die der Handwerksbetrieb streng ausschloss. Wenn sie die\n durchaus vereinseitigte Spezialität auf Kosten des ganzen Arbeitsvermögens\n zur Virtuosität entwickelt, beginnt sie auch schon den Mangel aller Ent-\n wicklung zu einer Spezialität zu machen. Neben die hierarchische Ab-\n stufung tritt die einfache Scheidung der Arbeiter in geschickte und\nungeschickte. Für letztere fallen die Erlernungskosten ganz weg,\n für erstere sinken sie, im Vergleich zum Handwerker, in Folge vereinfachter\n Funktion. In beiden Fällen sinkt der Werth der Arbeitskraft [Note: „Un ouvrier, en se perfectionnant par la pratique sur un seul et même\n point, devient … moins coûteux.“ (l. c. p. 28.)] . Aus-\n nahme findet statt, soweit die Zersetzung des Arbeitsprozesses neue zu-\n sammenfassende Funktionen erzeugt, die im Handwerksbetrieb gar nicht\n oder nicht in demselben Umfang vorkamen. Die relative Entwerthung\n der Arbeitskraft, die aus dem Wegfall oder der Verminderung der\n Erlernungskosten entspringt, schliesst unmittelbar höhere Verwer-\n thung des Kapitals ein, denn alles, was die zur Reproduktion der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"335\", \"facs\": \"#f0354\"} --> Arbeitskraft nothwendige Zeit verkürzt, verlängert die Domaine der Mehr-\n arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nWir betrachteten erst den Ursprung der Manufaktur aus der Coope-\n ration, dann ihre einfachen Elemente, den Theilarbeiter und sein Werk-\n zeug, endlich ihren Gesammtmechanismus. Wir berühren jetzt kurz das\n Verhältniss zwischen der manufakturmässigen Theilung der Arbeit und\n der gesellschaftlichen Theilung der Arbeit, welche die allgemeine\n Grundlage aller Waarenproduktion bildet.\n\n\n\n\nHält man nur die Arbeit selbst im Auge, so kann man die\n Trennung der gesellschaftlichen Produktion in ihre grossen Gattungen,\n wie Agrikultur, Industrie u. s. w., als Theilung der Arbeit im All-\n gemeinen, die Sonderung dieser Produktionsgattungen in Arten und\n Unterarten als Theilung der Arbeit im Besondern, und die\n Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb einer Werkstatt als Theilung der Ar-\n beit im Einzelnen bezeichnen [Note: „Die Theilung der Arbeit geht von der Trennung der verschiedenartigsten\n Professionen fort bis zu jener Theilung, wo mehrere Arbeiter sich in die Anferti-\n gung eines und desselben Produkts theilen, wie in der Manufaktur.“ (Storch:\n „Cours d’Econ. Pol.“ Pariser Ausgabe, t. I, p. 173.) „Nous rencontrons\n chez les peuples parvenus à un certain degré de civilisation trois genres de divi-\n sions d’industrie: la première, que nous nommons générale, amène la dis-\n tinction des producteurs en agriculteurs, manufacturiers et commerçans, elle se\n rapporte aux trois principales branches d’industrie nationale; la seconde, qu’on\n pourrait appeler spéciale, est la division de chaque genre d’industrie en espèces\n . . . . la troisième division d’industrie, celle enfin qu’on devrait qualifier de\ndivision de la besogne ou de travail proprement dit, est celle qui s’établit\n dans les arts et les métiers séparés … qui s’établit dans la plupart des manufac-\n tures et des ateliers.“ (Skarbek l. c. p. 84, 86.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb der Gesellschaft, und\n die entsprechende Beschränkung der Individuen auf besondre Berufs-\n sphären, entwickelt sich, wie die Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb der Manu-\n faktur, von entgegengesetzten Ausgangspunkten. Innerhalb einer Familie,\n weiter entwickelt eines Stammes, entspringt eine naturwüchsige Theilung\n der Arbeit aus den Geschlechts- und Altersverschiedenheiten, also auf rein\nphysiologischer Grundlage, die mit der Ausdehnung des Ge-\n meinwesens, der Zunahme der Bevölkerung, und namentlich dem Konflikt\n zwischen verschiednen Stämmen und der Unterjochung eines Stamms durch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"336\", \"facs\": \"#f0355\"} --> den andern ihr Material ausweitet. Andrerseits, wie ich früher bemerkt,\n entspringt der Produktenaustausch an den Punkten, wo verschiedne Fa-\n milien, Stämme, Gemeinwesen in Kontakt kommen, denn nicht Privatper-\n sonen, sondern Familien, Stämme u. s. w. treten sich in den Anfängen der\n Kultur selbstständig gegenüber. Verschiedne Gemeinwesen finden ver-\n schiedne Produktionsmittel und verschiedne Lebensmittel in ihrer Natur-\n umgebung vor. Ihre Produktionsweise, Lebensweise und Produkte sind\n daher verschieden. Es ist diese naturwüchsige Verschiedenheit, die bei\n dem Kontakt der Gemeinwesen den Austausch der wechselseitigen Produkte\n und daher die allmälige Verwandlung dieser Produkte in Waaren hervorruft.\n Der Austausch schafft nicht den Unterschied der Produktionssphären,\n sondern bezieht die unterschiednen auf einander und verwandelt sie so in\n mehr oder minder von einander abhängige Zweige einer gesellschaft-\n lichen Gesammtproduktion. Hier entsteht die gesellschaftliche Theilung\n der Arbeit durch den Austausch ursprünglich verschiedner, aber\n gegen einander selbstständiger Produktionssphären. Dort, wo die phy-\n siologische Theilung der Arbeit den Ausgangspunkt bildet, lösen sich\n die besondern Organe eines unmittelbar zusammengehörigen Ganzen von\n einander ab, zersetzen sich, zu welchem Zersetzungsprozess der Waaren-\n austausch mit fremden Gemeinwesen den Hauptanstoss giebt, und verselbst-\n ständigen sich bis zu dem Punkt, wo der Zusammenhang der verschiednen\n Arbeiten durch den Austausch der Produkte als Waaren vermittelt wird.\n Es ist in dem einen Fall Verunselbstständigung der früher Selbstständigen,\n in dem andern Verselbstständigung der früher Unselbstständigen.\n\n\n\n\nDie Grundlage aller entwickelten und durch Waarenaustausch ver-\n mittelten Theilung der Arbeit ist die Scheidung von Stadt und\n Land [Note: Sir James Steuart hat diesen Punktam besten behandelt. Wie wenig\n sein Werk, welches zehn Jahre vor dem „Wealth of Nations“ erschien, heut zu\n Tage bekannt ist, sieht man u. a. daraus, dass die Bewunderer des Malthus nicht\n einmal wissen, dass dieser in der ersten Ausgabe seiner Schrift über die „Popu-\n lation“, vom rein deklamatorischen Theil abgesehn, neben den Pfaffen Wallace\n und Townsend fast nur den Steuart abschreibt.] . Man kann sagen, dass die ganze ökonomische Geschichte der\n Gesellschaft sich in der Bewegung dieses Gegensatzes resümirt, auf den\n wir jedoch hier nicht weiter eingehn.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"337\", \"facs\": \"#f0356\"} -->\n\nWie für die Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb der Manufaktur eine ge-\n wisse Anzahl gleichzeitig angewandter Arbeiter die materielle Voraus-\n setzung bildet, so für die Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb der Gesellschaft\ndie Grösse der Bevölkerung und ihre Dichtigkeit, die hier\n an die Stelle der Agglomeration in derselben Werkstatt tritt [Note: „There is a certain density of population which is convenient, both for\n social intercourse, and for that combination of powers by which the produce of\n labour is increased.“ (James Mill l. c. p. 50.) „As the number of labourers\n increases, the productive power of society augments in the compound ratio of that\n increase, multiplied by the effects of the division of labour.“ (Th. Hodgskin\n l. c. p. 125, 126.)] . Indess\n ist diese Dichtigkeit etwas relatives. Ein relativ spärlich bevölkertes\n Land mit entwickelten Kommunikationsmitteln besitzt eine dichtere Be-\n völkerung als ein mehr bevölkertes Land mit unentwickelten Kommuni-\n kationsmitteln, und in dieser Art sind z. B. die nördlichen Staaten der\n amerikanischen Union dichter bevölkert als Indien [Note: In Folge der grossen Baumwollnachfrage seit 1861 wurde in einigen,\n sonst zahlreich bevölkerten Distrikten Ostindiens die Baumwollproduktion auf\n Kosten der Reisproduktion ausgedehnt. Es entstand daher partielle Hungersnoth,\n weil wegen mangelnder Kommunikationsmittel und daher mangelnden physischen\n Zusammenhangs der Reisausfall in einem Distrikt nicht durch Zufuhr aus andern\n Distrikten ausgeglichen werden konnte.] .\n\n\n\n\nDa Waarenproduktion und Waarencirkulation die allgemeine Vor-\n aussetzung der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise, erheischt manufaktur-\n mässige Theilung der Arbeit eine schon bis zu gewissem Entwicklungs-\n grad gereifte Theilung der Arbeit im Innern der Gesellschaft. Umgekehrt\n entwickelt und vervielfältigt die manufakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit\n rückwirkend jene gesellschaftliche Theilung der Arbeit. Mit der Diffe-\n renzirung der Arbeitsinstrumente differenziren sich mehr und mehr die Ge-\n werbe, welche diese Instrumente produciren [Note: So bildete die Fabrikation der Weberschiffchen schon während des 17.\n Jahrhunderts einen besondern Industriezweig in Holland.] . Ergreift der manufaktur-\n mässige Betrieb ein Gewerb, das bisher als Haupt- oder Nebengewerb mit\n andern zusammenhing und von demselben Producenten ausgeführt wurde,\n so findet sofort Scheidung und gegenseitige Verselbstständigung statt. Er-\n greift er eine besondre Produktionsstufe einer Waare, so verwandeln\n sich ihre verschiednen Produktionsstufen in verschiedne unabhängige Ge-\n [Running matter: I. 22] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"338\", \"facs\": \"#f0357\"} --> werbe. Es ward bereits angedeutet, dass wo das Machwerk ein bloss\n mechanisch zusammengesetztes Ganze von Theilprodukten, die Theilarbei-\n ten sich selbst wieder zu eignen Handwerken verselbstständigen können.\n Um die Theilung der Arbeit vollkommner innerhalb einer Manufaktur aus-\n zuführen, wird derselbe Produktionszweig, je nach der Verschiedenheit\n seiner Rohstoffe oder den verschiednen Formen, die derselbe Rohstoff er-\n halten kann, in verschiedne zum Theil ganz neue Manufakturen gespaltet.\n So wurden bereits in der ersten Hälfte des 18. Jahrhunderts in Frankreich\n allein über 100 verschiedenartige Seidenzeuge gewebt, und in Avignon\n z. B. war es Gesetz, dass „jeder Lehrling sich immer nur einer Fabrika-\n tionsart widmen und nicht die Verfertigung mehrerer Zeugarten zugleich\n lernen durfte.“ Die territoriale Theilung der Arbeit, welche\n besondre Produktionszweige an besondre Distrikte eines Landes bannt,\n erhält neuen Anstoss durch den manufakturmässigen Betrieb, der alle Be-\n sonderheiten ausbeutet [Note: „Whether the Woollen Manufacture of England is not divided into\n several parts or branches appropriated to particular places, where they are only or\n principally manufactured; fine clothes in Somersetshire, coarse in Yorkshire, long\n ells at Exeter, soies at Sandbury, crapes at Norwich, linseys at Kendal, blankets\n at Whitney, and so forth!“ (Berkeley: „The Querist“ 1750, p. 520.)] . Reiches Material zur Theilung der Arbeit\n innerhalb der Gesellschaft liefert der Manufakturperiode die Erweiterung\n des Weltmarkts und das Kolonialsystem, die zum Umkreis ihrer allgemei-\n nen Existenzbedingungen gehören. Es ist hier nicht der Ort weiter nach-\n zuweisen, wie sie neben der ökonomischen jede andre Sphäre der Gesell-\n schaft inficirt und überall die Grundlage zu jener Ausbildung des Fach-\n wesens, der Specialitäten, und zu einer Parcellirung des Menschen legt, die\n schon A. Ferguson, den Lehrer A. Smiths, in den Ausruf ausbrechen\n liess: „Wir sind ganze Nationen von Heloten, und es giebt keine Freien\n unter uns“ [Note: A. Ferguson: „History of Civil Society“, Part IV, ch. II.] .\n\n\n\n\nTrotz der zahlreichen Analogien jedoch und der Zusammenhänge\n zwischen der Theilung der Arbeit im Innern der Gesellschaft und der\n Theilung innerhalb einer Werkstatt, sind beide nicht nur graduell, son-\n dern wesentlich unterschieden. Am schlagendsten scheint die Ana-\n logie unstreitig, wo ein inneres Band verschiedne Geschäftszweige ver-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"339\", \"facs\": \"#f0358\"} --> schlingt. Der Viehzüchter z. B. producirt Häute, der Gerber verwandelt\n die Häute in Leder, der Schuster das Leder in Stiefel. Jeder producirt\n hier ein Stufenprodukt und die letzte fertige Gestalt ist das kombinirte\n Produkt ihrer Sonderarbeiten. Es kommen hinzu die mannigfachen Ar-\n beitszweige, die dem Viehzüchter, Gerber, Schuster Produktionsmittel\n liefern. Man kann sich nun mit A. Smith einbilden, diese gesellschaft-\n liche Theilung der Arbeit unterscheide sich von der manufakturmässigen\nnur subjektiv, nämlich für den Beobachter, der hier die mannigfachen\n Theilarbeiten auf einen Blick räumlich zusammensieht, während dort ihre\n Zerstreuung über grosse Flächen und die grosse Zahl der in jedem Son-\n derzweig Beschäftigten den Zusammenhang verdunklen [Note: In den eigentlichen Manufakturen, sagt er, scheint die Theilung der\n Arbeit grösser, weil „those employed in every different branch of the work can\n often be collected into the same workhouse, and placed at once under the view of\n the spectator. In those great manufactures (!), on the contrary, which are\n destined to supply the great wants of the great body of the people, every different\n branch of the work employs so great a number of workmen, that it is impossible\n to collect them all into the same workhouse … the division is not near so ob-\n vious.“ (A. Smith: „Wealth of Nations“, b. I, ch. I.) Der berühmte\n Passus in demselben Kapitel, der mit den Worten beginnt: „Observe the acco-\n modation of the most common artificer or day labourer in a civilized and thriving\n country etc.“ und dann weiter ausmalt, wie zahllos mannigfaltige Gewerbe zur\n Befriedigung der Bedürfnisse eines gewöhnlichen Arbeiters zusammenwirken, ist\n ziemlich wörtlich kopirt aus B. deMandeville’s Remarks zu seiner: „The\n Fable of the Bees, or Private Vices, Publick Benefits.“ (Erste\n Ausgabe ohne Remarks 1706, mit den Remarks 1714.)] . Was aber\n stellt den Zusammenhang her zwischen den unabhängigen Arbeiten von\n Viehzüchter, Gerber, Schuster? Das Dasein ihrer respektiven Produkte als\nWaaren. Was charakterisirt dagegen die manufakturmässige Theilung\n der Arbeit? Dass der Theilarbeiter keine Waare producirt [Note: „There is no longer anything which we can call the natural reward of\n individual labonr. Each labourer produces only some part of a whole, and each\n part, having no value or utility of itself, there is nothing on which the labourer\n can seize, and say: it is my product, this I will keep for myself.“ („Labour\n defended against the claims of Capital. Lond. 1825“, p. 25.) Der\n Verfasser dieser vorzüglichen Schrift ist der früher citirte Th. Hodgskin.] . Erst\n das gemeinsame Produkt der Theilarbeiter verwandelt sich in Waare.\n [Running matter: 22*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"340\", \"facs\": \"#f0359\"} --> Die Theilung der Arbeit im Innern der Gesellschaft ist vermittelt durch den\n Kauf und Verkauf der Produkte verschiedner Arbeitszweige, der Zusammen-\n hang der Theilarbeiten in der Manufaktur durch den Verkauf verschiedner Ar-\n beitskräfte an denselben Kapitalisten, der sie als kombinirte Arbeitskraft ver-\n wendet. Die manufakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit unterstellt Koncen-\n tration der Produktionsmittel in der Hand eines Kapitalisten, die gesell-\n schaftliche Theilung der Arbeit Zersplitterung der Produktionsmittel unter\n viele von einander unabhängige Waarenproducenten. Statt dass in der Manu-\n faktur das eherne Gesetz der Verhältnisszahl oder Proportionalität bestimmte\n Arbeitermassen unter bestimmte Funktionen subsumirt, treiben Zufall und\n Willkühr ihr buntes Spiel in der Vertheilung der Waarenproducenten und\n ihrer Produktionsmittel unter die verschiednen gesellschaftlichen Arbeits-\n zweige. Zwar suchen sich die verschiednen Produktionssphären bestän-\n dig ins Gleichgewicht zu setzen, indem einerseits jeder Waarenproducent\n einen Gebrauchswerth produciren, also ein besondres gesellschaftliches Be-\n dürfniss befriedigen muss, der Umfang dieser Bedürfnisse aber quantitativ\n verschieden ist und ein innres Band die verschiedenen Bedürfnissmassen zu\n einem naturwüchsigen System verkettet; indem andrerseits das Werth-\n gesetz der Waaren bestimmt, wie viel die Gesellschaft von ihrer ganzen\n disponiblen Arbeitszeit auf die Produktion jeder besondern Waarenart ver-\n ausgaben kann. Aber diese beständige Tendenz der verschiednen Pro-\n duktionssphären sich ins Gleichgewicht zu setzen, bethätigt sich nur als\n Reaktion gegen die beständige Aufhebung dieses Gleichgewichts. Bei\n der Theilung der Arbeit im Innern der Werkstatt beherrscht die Ver-\n hältnisszahl die jeder Sonderfunktion zugewiesne Arbeitermasse a priori\n als bewusste und planmässig befolgte Regel; bei der Theilung der Arbeit\n im Innern der Gesellschaft wirkt sie nur a posteriori als innere,\n stumme, im Barometerwechsel der Marktpreise wahrnehmbare, die regel-\n lose Willkühr der Waarenproducenten überwältigende Naturnothwendig-\n keit. Die manufakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit unterstellt die unbe-\n dingte Autorität des Kapitalisten über Menschen, die blosse Glieder eines\n ihm gehörigen Gesammtmechanismus bilden; die gesellschaftliche Thei-\n lung der Arbeit stellt unabhängige Waarenproducenten einander gegen-\n über, die keine andere Autorität anerkennen als die der Konkurrenz,\n den Zwang, den der Druck ihrer wechselseitigen Interessen auf sie\n ausübt, wie auch im Thierreich das bellum omnium contra omnes die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"341\", \"facs\": \"#f0360\"} --> Existenzbedingungen aller Arten mehr oder minder erhält. Dasselbe bür-\n gerliche Bewusstsein, das die manufakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit,\n die lebenslängliche Annexation des Arbeiters an eine Detailverrichtung und\n die unbedingte Unterordnung der Theilarbeiter unter das Kapital, als eine\n Organisation der Arbeit feiert, welche ihre Produktivkraft steigere, denun-\n cirt daher eben so laut jede bewusste gesellschaftliche Kontrole und Reg-\n lung des gesellschaftlichen Produktionsprozesses als einen Eingriff in die\n unverletzlichen Eigenthumsrechte, Freiheit und sich selbst bestimmende\n „Genialität“ des individuellen Kapitalisten. Es ist sehr charakteristisch,\n dass die begeisterten Apologeten des Fabriksystems nichts ärgeres gegen\n jede allgemeine Organisation der gesellschaftlichen Arbeit zu sagen wis-\n sen, als dass sie die ganze Gesellschaft in eine Fabrik verwandeln würde.\n\n\n\n\nWenn die Anarchie der gesellschaftlichen und die Despotie der\nmanufakturmässigen Arbeitstheilung einander in der Gesellschaft\n der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise bedingen, bieten dagegen frühere\n Gesellschaftsformen, worin die Besonderung der Gewerbe sich naturwüchsig\n entwickelt, dann krystallisirt und endlich gesetzlich befestigt, einer-\n seits das Bild einer plan- und autoritätsmässigen Organisation der\n gesellschaftlichen Arbeit, während sie anderseits die Theilung der Arbeit\n innerhalb der Werkstatt ganz ausschliessen, oder nur auf einem Zwerg-\n massstab, oder nur sporadisch und zufällig entwickeln [Note: „On peut … établir en règle générale, que moins l’autorité préside à\n la division du travail dans l’intérieur de la société, plus la division du travail se\n développe dans l’intérieur de l’atélier, et plus elle y est soumise à l’autorité d’un\n seul. Ainsi l’autorité dans l’atélier et celle dans la société, par rapport à la\n division du travail, sont en raison inverse l’une de l’autre.“ (Karl Marx\n l. c. p. 130, 131.)] .\n\n\n\n\nJene uralterthümlichen, kleinen indischen Gemeinwesen z. B., die\n zum Theil noch fortexistiren, beruhn auf gemeinschaftlichem Besitz des\n Grund und Bodens, auf unmittelbarer Verbindung von Agrikultur und\n Handwerk, und auf einer festen Theilung der Arbeit, die bei Anlage neuer\n Gemeinwesen als gegebner Plan und Grundriss dient. Sie bilden sich\n selbst genügende Produktionsganze, deren Produktionsgebiet von 100 bis\n auf einige 1000 Acres wechselt. Die Hauptmasse der Produkte wird für\n den unmittelbaren Selbstbedarf der Gemeinde producirt, nicht als Waare,\n und die Produktion selbst ist daher unabhängig von der durch Waaren-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"342\", \"facs\": \"#f0361\"} --> austausch vermittelten Theilung der Arbeit im Grossen und Ganzen der\n indischen Gesellschaft. Nur der Ueberschuss der Produkte verwandelt\n sich in Waare, zum Theil selbst wieder erst in der Hand des Staats,\n dem ein bestimmtes Quantum seit undenklichen Zeiten als Naturalrente zu-\n fliesst. In verschiedenen Theilen Indiens besitzt diess Gemeinwesen ver-\n schiedne herrschende Formen. In der einfachsten Form bebaut die Ge-\n meinde das Land gemeinschaftlich und vertheilt seine Produkte unter ihre\n Glieder, während jede Familie Spinnen, Weben u. s. w. als häusliches\n Nebengewerb treibt. Neben dieser gleichartig beschäftigten Masse finden\n wir den „Haupteinwohner“, Richter, Polizei und Steuereinnehmer in\n einer Person; den Buchhalter, der die Rechnung über den Ackerbau führt\n und alles darauf Bezügliche katastrirt und registrirt; einen dritten Beam-\n ten, der Verbrecher verfolgt und fremde Reisende beschützt und von einem\n Dorf zum andern geleitet; den Grenzmann, der die Grenzen der Ge-\n meinde gegen die Nachbargemeinden bewacht; den Wasseraufseher,\n der das Wasser aus den gemeinschaftlichen Wasserbehältern zu Ackerbau-\n zwecken vertheilt; den Braminen, der die Funktionen des religiösen\n Kultus verrichtet; den Schulmeister, der die Gemeindekinder im Sand\n schreiben und lesen lehrt; den Kalenderbraminen, der als Astrolog\n die Zeiten für Saat, Ernte und die guten oder bösen Stunden für alle be-\n sondern Ackerbauarbeiten angiebt; einen Schmidt und einen Zimmer-\n mann, welche alle Ackerbauwerkzeuge verfertigen und ausbessern; den\nTöpfer, der alle Gefässe für das Dorf macht; den Barbier, den\nWäscher für die Reinigung der Kleider, den Silberschmidt, hier\n und da den Poeten, der in einigen Gemeinden den Silberschmidt, in\n andern den Schulmeister ersetzt. Diess Dutzend Personen wird auf\n Kosten der ganzen Gemeinde erhalten. Wächst die Bevölkerung, so\n wird eine neue Gemeinde nach dem Muster der alten auf unbebautem Bo-\n den angesiedelt. Den Gesammtmechanismus der Gemeinde betrachtet,\n findet sich hier planmässige Theilung der Arbeit, aber ihre manufaktur-\n mässige Theilung ist unmöglich, indem der Markt für Schmidt, Zimmer-\n mann u. s. w. unverändert bleibt und höchstens, je nach dem Grössen-\n unterschied der Dörfer, statt eines Schmidts, Töpfers u. s. w. ihrer zwei\n oder drei vorkommen [Note: Lieut. Col. Mark Wilks: „Historical Sketches of the\n South of India. Lond. 1810—17“, v. I, p. 118—20. Eine gute Zu-] . Das Gesetz, das die Theilung der Gemeinde-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"343\", \"facs\": \"#f0362\"} --> arbeit regelt, wirkt hier mit der unverbrüchlichen Autorität eines Natur-\n gesetzes, während jeder besondre Handwerker, wie Schmidt u. s. w., nach\n überlieferter Art, aber selbstständig, und ohne Anerkennung irgend einer\n Autorität in seiner Werkstatt, alle zu seinem Fach gehörigen Operationen\n verrichtet. Der einfache produktive Organismus dieser selbstgenügenden\n Gemeinwesen, die sich beständig in derselben Form reproduciren, und,\n wenn zufällig zerstört, an demselben Ort, mit demselben Namen, wieder\n aufbauen [Note: „Under this simple form … the inhabitants of the country have lived\n since time immemorial. The boundaries of the villages have been but seldom\n altered; and though the villages themselves have been sometimes injured, and\n even desolated by war, famine, and disease, the same name, the same limits, the\n same interests, and even the same families, have continued for ages. The inhabi-\n tants give themselves no trouble about the breaking up and division of kingdoms;\n while the village remains entire, they care not to what power it is transferred or\n to what sovereign it devolves; its internal economy remains unchanged.“ (Th.\n Stafford Raffles, late Lieut. Gov. of Java: „The History of Java.\n Lond. 1817“, v. II, p. 285, 286.)] , liefert den Schlüssel zum Geheimniss der Unveränder-\n lichkeit asiatischer Gesellschaften, so auffallend kontrastirt durch\n die beständige Auflösung und Neubildung asiatischer Staaten und rast-\n losen Dynastenwechsel. Die Struktur der ökonomischen Grundelemente\n der Gesellschaft bleibt von den Stürmen der politischen Wolkenregion un-\n berührt.\n\n\n\n\nDie Zunftgesetze, wie schon früher bemerkt, verhinderten planmässig,\n durch äusserste Beschränkung des Maximums der Gesellenzahl, die ein\n einzelner Zunftmeister beschäftigen durfte, seine Verwandlung in einen Ka-\n pitalisten. Ebenso konnte er Gesellen nur beschäftigen in dem ausschliess-\n lichen Handwerk, worin er selbst Meister war. Die Zunft wehrte eifer-\n süchtig jeden Uebergriff des Kaufmannskapitals ab, der einzig freien\n Form des Kapitals, die ihr gegenüberstand. Der Kaufmann konnte alle\n Waaren kaufen, nur nicht die Arbeit als Waare. Er war nur geduldet\n als Verleger der Handwerksprodukte. Riefen äussere Umstände eine\n fortschreitende Theilung der Arbeit hervor, so zerspalteten sich bestehende\n Zünfte in Unterarten oder lagerten sich neue Zünfte neben die alten hin,\n [Note: sammenstellung der verschiednen Formen des indischen Gemeinwesens findet man\n in George Campbell’s: „Modern India. London 1852.“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"344\", \"facs\": \"#f0363\"} --> jedoch ohne Zusammenfassung verschiedner Handwerke in eine Werkstatt.\n Die Zunftorganisation, so sehr ihre Besonderung, Isolirung und Ausbildung\n der Gewerbe zu den materiellen Existenzbedingungen der Manufaktur-\n periode gehören, schloss daher die manufakturmässige Theilung der Ar-\n beit aus. Im Grossen und Ganzen blieben der Arbeiter und seine Pro-\n duktionsmittel mit einander verbunden, wie die Schnecke mit dem\n Schneckenhaus, und so fehlte die erste Grundlage der Manufaktur, die Ver-\n selbstständigung der Produktionsmittel als Kapital gegenüber dem\n Arbeiter.\n\n\n\n\nWährend die Theilung der Arbeit im Ganzen einer Gesellschaft, ob\n vermittelt oder unvermittelt durch den Waarenaustausch, den verschieden-\n artigsten ökonomischen Gesellschaftsformationen angehört, ist die manu-\n fakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit eine ganz spezifische Schöpfung\n der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise.\n\n\n\n\nEine grössere Arbeiteranzahl unter dem Kommando desselben\n Kapitals bildet den naturwüchsigen Ausgangspunkt, wie der Coopera-\n tion überhaupt, so der Manufaktur. Umgekehrt entwickelt die manufak-\n turmässige Theilung der Arbeit das Wachsthum der angewandten Arbeiter-\n zahl zur technologischen Nothwendigkeit. Das Arbeiterminimum,\n das ein einzelner Kapitalist anwenden muss, ist ihm jetzt durch die vor-\n handene Theilung der Arbeit vorgeschrieben. Andrerseits sind die Vor-\n theile weiterer Theilung bedingt durch weitere Vermehrung der Arbeiter-\n anzahl, die nur noch in Multiplen ausführbar. Mit dem variablen\n muss aber auch der constante Bestandtheil des Kapitals wachsen, neben\n dem Umfang der gemeinsamen Produktionsbedingungen, wie Baulichkeiten,\n Oefen u. s. w., namentlich auch und viel rascher als die Arbeiteranzahl,\n das Rohmaterial. Seine Masse, verzehrt in gegebner Zeit durch ge-\n gebnes Arbeitsquantum, nimmt in demselben Verhältniss zu wie die Pro-\n duktivkraft der Arbeit in Folge ihrer Theilung. Wachsender Mini-\n malumfang von Kapital in der Hand der einzelnen Kapitalisten,\n oder wachsende Verwandlung der gesellschaftlichen\n Lebensmittel und Produktionsmittel in Kapital ist also\n ein aus dem technologischen Charakter der Manufaktur entspringendes\n Gesetz [Note: „Es genügt nicht, dass das zur Unterabtheilung der Handwerke nöthige\nKapital (sollte heissen, die dazu nöthigen Lebens- und Produktionsmittel) sich] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"345\", \"facs\": \"#f0364\"} -->\n\nWie in der einfachen Cooperation ist in der Manufaktur der\n funktionirende Arbeitskörper eine Existenzform des Kapitals.\n Der aus vielen individuellen Theilarbeitern zusammengesetzte gesellschaft-\n liche Produktionsmechanismus gehört dem Kapitalisten. Die aus der\n Kombination der Arbeiten entspringende Produktivkraft erscheint daher\n als Produktivkraft des Kapitals. Die eigentliche Manufaktur un-\n terwirft nicht nur den früher selbstständigen Arbeiter dem Kommando und\n der Disciplin des Kapitals, sondern schafft überdem eine hierarchische\n Gliederung unter den Arbeitern selbst. Während die einfache Coopera-\n tion die Arbeitsweise der Einzelnen im Grossen und Ganzen unverändert\n lässt, revolutionirt die Manufaktur sie von Grund aus und ergreift die in-\n dividuelle Arbeitskraft an ihrer Wurzel. Sie verkrüppelt den Arbeiter in\n eine Abnormität, indem sie sein Detailgeschick treibhausmässig fördert\n durch Unterdrückung einer Welt von produktiven Trieben und Anlagen,\n wie man in den La Plata Staaten ein ganzes Thier abschlachtet, um sein\n Fell oder seinen Talg zu erbeuten. Die besondren Theilarbeiten werden\n nicht nur unter verschiedne Individuen vertheilt, sondern das Individuum\n selbst wird getheilt, in das automatische Triebwerk einer Theilarbeit ver-\n wandelt [Note: Dugald Stewart nennt die Manufakturarbeiter „living automatons …\n employed in the details of the work.“ (l. c. p. 318.)]  und die abgeschmackte Fabel des Menenius Agrippa verwirk-\n licht, die einen Menschen als blosses Fragment seines eignen Körpers dar-\n stellt [Note: Bei den Korallen bildet jedes Individuum in der That den Magen für die\n ganze Gruppe. Es führt ihr aber Nahrungsstoff zu, statt wie der römische\n l’atricier ihn wegzuführen.] . Wenn der Arbeiter ursprünglich seine Arbeitskraft an das\n Kapital verkauft, weil ihm die materiellen Produktionsbedin-\n [Note: in der Gesellschaft vorhanden vorfinde; es ist ausserdem nöthig, dass es in den\n Händen der Unternehmer in hinreichend beträchtlichen Massen accumulirt sei, um\n sie zur Arbeit auf grosser Stufenleiter zu befähigen … Je mehr die Theilung zu-\n nimmt, erheischt die beständige Beschäftigung einer selben Zahl von Arbeitern\n immer beträchtlicheres Kapital in Werkzeugen, Rohstoffen u. s. w.“ (Storch:\n „Cours d’Écon. Polit.“ Pariser Ausg. t. I, p. 250, 251.) „La concen-\n tration des instrumens de production et la division du travail sont aussi inséparables\n l’une de l’autre que le sont, dans le régime politique, la concentration des pouvoirs\n publics et la division des intérêts privés.“ (Karl Marx l. c. p. 134.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"346\", \"facs\": \"#f0365\"} -->gungen einer Waare fehlen, existirt seine individuelle Arbeits-\n kraft jetzt überhaupt nur noch, wann und sofern sie an das Kapital ver-\n kauft wird. Sie funktionirt nur noch in einem Zusammenhang, der erst\nnach ihrem Verkauf, in der Werkstatt des Kapitalisten, existirt. Seiner\n natürlichen Beschaffenheit nach verunfähigt, etwas Selbstständiges zu\n machen, entwickelt der Manufakturarbeiter produktive Thätigkeit nur noch\n als Zubehör zur Werkstatt des Kapitalisten [Note: „L’ouvrier qui porte dans ses bras tout un métier, peut aller partout\n exercer son industrie et trouver des moyens de subsister: l’autre (der Manufaktur-\n arbeiter) n’est qu’un accessoire qui, séparé de ses confrères, n’a plus ni capa-\n cité, ni indépendance, et qui se trouve forcé d’accepter la loi, qu’on juge à propos\n de lui imposer.“ (Storch l. c. Petersb. édit. 1815, t. I, p. 204.)] . Wie dem auserwähl-\n ten Volk auf der Stirn geschrieben stand, dass es das Eigenthum Jehovas,\n so drückt die Theilung der Arbeit dem Manufakturarbeiter einen Stempel\n auf, der ihn zum Eigenthum des Kapitals brandmarkt.\n\n\n\n\nDie Kenntnisse, die Einsicht und der Wille, die der selbstständige\n Bauer oder Handwerker, wenn auch auf kleinem Massstab entwickelt, wie\n der Wilde alle Kunst des Kriegs als persönliche List ausübt, sind jetzt nur\n noch für das Ganze der Werkstatt erheischt. Die geistigen Potenzen der\n Produktion erweitern ihren Massstab auf der einen Seite, weil sie auf vielen\n Seiten verschwinden. Was die Theilarbeiter verlieren, koncentrirt\n sich ihnen gegenüber im Kapital [Note: A. Ferguson l. c. Fzs. Uebers. 1783, t. II, p. 135, 136. „L’un\n peut avoir gagné ce que l’autre a perdu.“] . Es ist ein Produkt der manufaktur-\n mässigen Theilung der Arbeit ihnen die geistigen Potenzen des mate-\n riellen Produktionsprozesses als fremdes Eigenthum und sie beherr-\n schende Macht gegenüberzustellen. Dieser Scheidungsprozess be-\n ginnt in der einfachen Cooperation, wo der Kapitalist den einzelnen\n Arbeitern gegenüber die Einheit und den Willen des gesellschaftlichen Ar-\n beitskörpers vertritt. Er entwickelt sich in der Manufaktur, die den Ar-\n beiter zum Theilarbeiter verstümmelt. Er vollendet sich in der grossen\n Industrie, welche die Wissenschaft als selbstständige Produktions-\n potenz von der Arbeit trennt und in den Dienst des Kapitals presst [Note: „Der Mann des Wissens und der produktive Arbeiter sind weit von ein-\n ander getrennt, und die Wissenschaft, statt in der Hand des Arbeiters seine eignen\n Produktivkräfte für ihn selbst zu vermehren, hat sich fast überall ihm gegenüber-] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"347\", \"facs\": \"#f0366\"} -->\n\nIn der Manufaktur ist die Bereicherung des Gesammtarbeiters, und\n daher des Kapitals, an gesellschaftlicher Produktivkraft bedingt durch die\n Verarmung des Arbeiters an individuellen Produktivkräften. „Die Unwissen-\n heit ist die Mutter der Industrie wie des Aberglaubens. Nachdenken und\n Einbildungskraft sind dem Irrthum unterworfen; aber die Gewohnheit den\n Fuss oder die Hand zu bewegen hängt weder von dem einen, noch von\n der andern ab. So könnte man sagen, dass mit Bezug auf Manufakturen\n ihre Vollkommenheit darin besteht, sich des Geistes entschlagen zu können,\n in der Art, dass die Werkstatt als eine Maschine betrachtet wer-\n den kann, deren Theile Menschen sind“ [Note: A. Ferguson l. c. p. 134, 135.] . In der That\n wandten einige Manufakturen in der Mitte des 18. Jahrhunderts für gewisse\n einfache Operationen, welche aber Fabrikgeheimnisse bildeten, mit Vorliebe\n halbe Idioten an [Note: J. D. Tuckett: „A History of the Past and Present State\n of the Labouring Population. Lond. 1846“, v. I, p. 149.] .\n\n\n\n\n„Der Geist der grossen Mehrzahl der Menschen“, sagt A. Smith,\n „entwickelt sich nothwendig aus und an ihren Alltagsverrichtungen. Ein\n Mensch, der sein ganzes Leben in der Verrichtung weniger einfacher Ope-\n rationen verausgabt … hat keine Gelegenheit seinen Verstand zu üben …\n Er wird im Allgemeinen so stupid und unwissend, wie es für eine mensch-\n liche Kreatur möglich ist.“ Nachdem Smith den Stumpfsinn des Theil-\n arbeiters geschildert, fährt er fort: „Die Einförmigkeit seines stationären\n Lebens verdirbt natürlich auch den Muth seines Geistes … Sie zerstört\n selbst die Energie seines Körpers und verunfähigt ihn, seine Kraft schwung-\n haft und ausdauernd anzuwenden ausser in der Detailbeschäftigung,\n wozu er herangezogen ist. Sein Geschick in seinem besondern Gewerke\n scheint so erworben auf Kosten seiner intellektuellen, socialen und kriege-\n rischen Tugenden. Aber in jeder industriellen und civilisirten Gesellschaft\n ist diess der Zustand, worin der arbeitende Arme (the labouring poor),\n d. h. die grosse Masse des Volks nothwendig verfallen muss“ [Note: A. Smith: „Wealth of Nations.“ B. V, ch. I, art. II. Als\n Schüler A. Ferguson’s, der die nachtheiligen Folgen der Theilung der Arbeit ent-] . Um\n [Note: gestellt . . . . Kenntniss wird ein Instrument, fähig von der Arbeit getrennt und\n ihr entgegengesetzt zu werden.“ (W. Thompson: „An Inquiry into the\n Principles of the Distribution of Wealth. Lond. 1824“, p. 274.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"348\", \"facs\": \"#f0367\"} --> die aus der Theilung der Arbeit entspringende völlige Verkümmerung der\n Volksmasse zu verhindern, empfiehlt A. Smith Volksunterricht von Staats-\n wegen, wenn auch in vorsichtig homöopathischen Dosen. Konsequent\n polemisirt dagegen sein französischer Uebersetzer und Kommentator,\n G. Garnier, der sich unter dem ersten französischen Kaiserthum natur-\n gemäss zum Senator entpuppte. Volksunterricht verstosse wider die\n ersten Gesetze der Theilung der Arbeit und mit demselben „proscribire\n man unser ganzes Gesellschaftssystem“. „Wie alle andern\n Theilungen der Arbeit“, sagt er, „wird die zwischen Handarbeit und Ver-\n standesarbeit [Note: Ferguson sagt bereits: „l’art de penser, dans un période où tout est sé-\n paré, peut lui-même former un métier à part.“]  ausgesprochner und entschiedner im Masse wie die Ge-\n sellschaft (er wendet richtig diesen Ausdruck an für das Kapital, das\n Grundeigenthum und ihren Staat) reicher wird. Gleich jeder andern ist\n diese Theilung der Arbeit eine Wirkung vergangner und eine Ursache\n künftiger Fortschritte … Darf die Regierung denn dieser Theilung der\n Arbeit entgegenwirken und sie in ihrem naturgemässen Gang aufhalten?\n Darf sie einen Theil der Staatseinnahme zum Versuch verwenden, zwei\n Klassen von Arbeit, die ihre Theilung und Trennung erstreben, zu verwir-\n ren und zu vermischen“? [Note: G. Garnier, t. V seiner Uebersetzung, p. 2—5.] \n\n\n\n\nEine gewisse geistige und körperliche Verkrüppelung ist unzertrenn-\n lich selbst von der Theilung der Arbeit im Ganzen und Grossen der Gesell-\n schaft. Da aber die Manufakturperiode diese gesellschaftliche Zerspal-\n tung der Arbeitszweige viel weiter führt, andrerseits erst mit der ihr eigen-\n thümlichen Theilung das Individuum an seiner Lebenswurzel ergreift,\n [Note: wickelt hatte, war A. Smith über diesen Punkt  [Editorial alternatives: sic = dnrchaus; corr = durchaus]  klar. Im Eingang seines\n Werks, wo die Theilung der Arbeit ex professo gefeiert wird, deutet er sie nur\n vorübergehend als Quelle der gesellschaftlichen Ungleichheiten an. Erst im 5. Buch\n über das Staatseinkommen reproducirt er Ferguson. Ich habe in „Misère de\n la Philosophie“ das Nöthige über das historische Verhältniss von Ferguson,\n A. Smith, Lemontey und Say in ihrer Kritik der Theilung der Arbeit gegeben und\n dort auch zuerst die manufakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit als spezifische\n Form der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise dargestellt. (l. c. p.\n 122 sq.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"349\", \"facs\": \"#f0368\"} --> liefert sie auch zuerst das Material und den Anstoss zur industriellen\n Pathologie [Note: Ramazzini, Professor der praktischen Medizin zu Padua, veröffent-\n lichte 1713 sein Werk: „De morbis artificum“, 1781 in’s Französische\n übersetzt, wieder abgedruckt 1841 in der „Encyclopédie des Sciences\n Médicales. 7me Dis. Auteurs Classiques.“ Die Periode der grossen In-\n dustrie hat seinen Katalog der Arbeiterkrankheiten natürlich sehr vermehrt. Siehe u.a.\nHygiène physique et morale de l’ouvrier dans les grandes villes\n en général, et dans la ville de Lyon en particulier. Par le Dr.\n A. L. Fonterel. Paris 1858“ und „Die Krankheiten, welche ver-\n schiedenen Ständen, Altern und Geschlechtern eigenthümlich\n sind. 6 Bände. Ulm 1860.“ Im Jahr 1854 ernannte die Society of\n Arts eine Untersuchungskommission über industrielle Pathologie. Die Liste der\n von dieser Kommission gesammelten Dokumente findet man im Katalog des\n „Twickenham Economic Museum.“ Sehr wichtig auch die officiellen\n Reports des „Board of Health“.] .\n\n\n\n\n„Einen Menschen unterabtheilen, heisst ihn hinrichten, wenn er das\n Todesurtheil verdient, ihn meuchelmorden, wenn er es nicht verdient. Die\n Unterabtheilung der Arbeit ist der Meuchelmord eines Volks“ [Note: „To subdivide a man is to execute him, if he deserves the sentence, to\n assassinate him if he does not … The subdivision of labour is the assassination\n of a people.“ (D. Urquhart: „Familiar Words. London 1855“,\n p. 119.) Hegel hatte sehr ketzerische Ansichten über die Theilung der Arbeit.\n „Unter gebildeten Menschen kann man zunächst solche verstehn, die Alles machen\n können, was Andere thun“, sagt er in seiner Rechtsphilosophie.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie auf Theilung der Arbeit beruhende Cooperation oder die Manu-\n faktur ist in ihren Anfängen ein naturwüchsiges Gebild der kapitalistischen\n Produktion. Sobald sie einige Konsistenz und Breite des Daseins gewon-\n nen, wird sie zur bewussten, planmässigen und systematischen Form der\n kapitalistischen Produktionsweise. Die Geschichte der eigentlichen Manu-\n faktur zeigt, wie die ihr eigenthümliche Theilung der Arbeit zunächst erfah-\n rungsmässig, gleichsam hinter dem Rücken der handelnden Personen, die\n sachgemässen Formen gewinnt, dann aber, gleich dem zünftigen Hand-\n werke, die einmal gefundene Form traditionell festzuhalten strebt und in\n einzelnen Fällen Jahrhundertlang festhält. Aendert sich diese Form, so,\n ausser in Nebendingen, immer nur in Folge einer Revolution der Arbeits-\n instrumente. Die moderne Manufaktur — ich spreche hier nicht von der\n auf Maschinerie beruhenden grossen Industrie — findet entweder, wie\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"350\", \"facs\": \"#f0369\"} --> z. B. die Kleidermanufaktur, in den grossen Städten, wo sie entsteht,\n die disjecta membra poetae bereits fertig vor und hat sie nur aus ihrer Zer-\n streuung zu sammeln, oder das Prinzip der Theilung liegt auf flacher Hand,\n indem einfach die verschiednen Verrichtungen der handwerksmässigen\n Produktion (z. B. beim Buchbinden) besondern Arbeitern ausschliesslich\n angeeignet werden. Es kostet noch keine Woche Erfahrung in solchen\n Fällen die Verhältnisszahl zwischen den für jede Funktion nöthigen Hän-\n den zu finden [Note: Der gemüthliche Glaube an das Erfindungsgenie, das der einzelne Kapi-\n talist in der Theilung der Arbeit a priori ausübe, findet sich nur noch bei deutschen\n Professoren, wie Herrn Roscher z. B., der dem Kapitalisten, aus dessen Jupiter-\n haupt die Theilung der Arbeit fertig hervorspringt, zum Dank „diverse Arbeits-\n löhne“ widmet. Die grössere oder geringere Anwendung der Theilung der\n Arbeit hängt von der Länge der Börse, nicht von der Grösse des Genies ab.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie manufakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit schafft durch Ana-\n lyse der handwerksmässigen Thätigkeit, Spezificirung der Arbeitsinstru-\n mente, Bildung der Theilarbeiter, ihre Gruppirung und Kombination in\n einem Gesammtmechanismus, die qualitative Gliederung und die quantita-\n tive Proportionalität gesellschaftlicher Produktionsprozesse, also eine be-\n stimmte Organisation gesellschaftlicher Arbeit und ent-\n wickelt damit zugleich neue, gesellschaftliche Produktivkraft der Arbeit.\n Als spezifisch kapitalistische Form des gesellschaftlichen Produk-\n tionsprozesses — und auf den vorgefundnen Grundlagen konnte sie sich\n nicht anders als in der kapitalistischen Form entwickeln — ist sie\n nur eine besondre Methode relativen Mehrwerth zu erzeugen oder\n die Selbstverwerthung des Kapitals — was man gesell-\n schaftlichen Reichthum, „Wealth of Nations“ u. s. w. nennt\n — auf Kosten der Arbeiter zu erhöhn. Sie entwickelt die gesellschaft-\n liche Produktivkraft der Arbeit nicht nur für den Kapitalisten, statt für\n den Arbeiter, sondern durch die Verkrüpplung des individuellen Arbeiters.\n Sie producirt neue Bedingungen der Herrschaft des Kapitals über die Ar-\n beit. Wenn sie daher einerseits als historischer Fortschritt und nothwen-\n diges Entwicklungsmoment im ökonomischen Bildungsprozess der Gesell-\n schaft erscheint, so andrerseits als ein Mittel civilisirter und raffinirter\n Exploitation.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"351\", \"facs\": \"#f0370\"} -->\n\nDie politische Oekonomie, die als eigne Wissenschaft erst in der Manu-\n fakturperiode aufkommt, betrachtet die gesellschaftliche Theilung der\n Arbeit überhaupt nur vom Standpunkt der manufakturmässigen Thei-\n lung der Arbeit [Note: Die älteren Schriftsteller wie Petty, „Advantages of the East India Trade“\n etc., fixiren mehr denn A. Smith die manufakturmässige Theilung als kapitali-\n stische Form der Produktion.] , als Mittel mit demselben Quantum Arbeit mehr Waare\n zu produciren, daher die Waaren zu verwohlfeilern und die Accumulation des\n Kapitals zu beschleunigen. Im strengsten Gegensatz zu dieser Accentuirung\n der Quantität und des Tauschwerths halten sich die Schriftsteller\n des klassischen Alterthums ausschliesslich an Qualität und Ge-\n brauchswerth [Note: Ausnahme unter den Modernen bilden einige Schriftsteller des 18. Jahr-\n hunderts, die in Bezug auf Theilung der Arbeit fast nur den Alten nachsprechen,\n wie Beccaria und James Harris. So Beccaria: „Ciascuno prova coll’ espe-\n rienza, che applicando la mano e l’ingegno sempre allo stesso genere di opere e di\n produtte, egli più facili, più abbondanti e migliori ne traca resultati, di quello che\n se ciascuno isolatamente le cose tutte a se necessarie soltanto facesse .... Divi-\n dendosi in tal maniera per la comune e privata utilità gli uomini in varie classe e\n condizioni.“ (Cesare Beccaria: „Elementi di Econ. Publica“, ed.\nCustodi, Part. Moderna t. XI, p. 28.) James Harris, später Earl of\n Malmesbury, berühmt durch die „Diaries“ über seine Gesandtschaft in Peters-\n burg, sagt selbst in einer Note zu seinem Dialogue concerning Hap-\n piness. Lond. 1741, später wieder abgedruckt in „Three Treatises etc.\n 3. ed. Lond. 1772“: „The whole argument, to prove society natural (nämlich\n durch die „division of employments“) is taken from the second book of Plato’s\n republic.“] . In Folge der Scheidung der gesellschaftlichen\n Produktionszweige werden die Waaren besser gemacht, die verschiedenen\n Triebe und Talente der Menschen wählen sich entsprechende Wirkungs-\n sphären [Note: So in der Odyssee XIV, 228: „Ἄλλος γάϱ τ᾽ ἄλλοισιν ἀνὴϱ ἐπιτέϱπε-\n ται ἔϱγοις“ und Archilochus beim Sextus Empiricus: „ἄλλος ἄλλῳ ἐπ᾽ ἔϱγῳ\n ϰαϱδίην ἰαίνεται.“]  und ohne Beschränkung ist nirgendwo Bedeutendes zu lei-\n sten [Note: „Πολλ᾽ ἠπίστατο ἔϱγα, ϰαϰῶς δ᾽ ἠπίστατο πάντα.“ Indess, so sehr sich\n der Athenienser als Waarenproducent dem Spartaner überlegen fühlte, weil dieser\n im Krieg wohl über Menschen, nicht aber über Geld verfügen könne, wie\n Thucydides den Pericles sagen lässt in der Rede, worin er die Athenienser zum\n peloponnesischen Krieg aufstachelt: „σώμασί τε ἑτοιμότεϱοι οἱ αὐτουϱγοὶ τῶν] . Also Produkt und Producent werden verbessert durch die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"352\", \"facs\": \"#f0371\"} --> Theilung der Arbeit. Wird gelegentlich auch das Wachsthum der Pro-\n duktenmasse erwähnt, so nur mit Bezug auf die grössere Fülle des Ge-\n brauchswerths. Es wird mit keiner Silbe des Tauschwerths, der\nVerwohlfeilerung der Waaren gedacht. Dieser Standpunkt des Ge-\n brauchswerths herrscht sowohl bei Plato [Note: Plato entwickelt die Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb des Gemeinwesens aus\n der Vielseitigkeit der Bedürfnisse und der Einseitigkeit der Anlagen der Indivi-\n duen. Hauptgesichtspunkt bei ihm, dass der Arbeiter sich nach dem Werk richten\n müsse, nicht das Werk nach dem Arbeiter, was unvermeidlich, wenn er verschiedne\n Künste zugleich, also eine oder die andere als Nebenwerk treibe. „Οὐ γὰϱ\n ἐϑέλει τὸ πϱαττόμενον τὴν τοῦ πϱάττοντος σχολὴν πεϱιμένειν, ἀλλ᾽ ἀνάγϰη τὸν\n πϱάττοντα τῷ πϱαττομένῳ ἐπαϰολουϑεῖν μὴ ἐν παϱέϱγου μέϱει. Ἀνάγϰη. Ἐϰ\n δὴ τούτων πλείω τε ἕϰαστα γίγνεται ϰαὶ ϰάλλιον ϰαὶ ῥᾷον, ὅταν εἷς ἓν ϰατὰ\n φύσιν ϰαὶ ἐν ϰαιϱῷ, σχολὴν τῶν ἄλλων ἄγων, πϱάττῃ.“ (Rep. l. 2. ed. Baiter,\n Orelli etc.) Aehnlich bei Thucydides l. c. c. 42: „Das Seewesen ist eine\n Kunst so sehr wie irgend etwas anderes und kann nicht bei etwa vorkommenden\n Fällen als Nebenwerk betrieben werden, sondern vielmehr nichts andres neben\n ihm als Nebenwerk.“ Muss das Werk, sagt Plato, auf den Arbeiter warten, so\n wird oft der kritische Zeitpunkt der Produktion verpasst und das Machwerk ver-\n dorben, „ἔϱγου ϰαιϱὸν διόλλυται.“ Dieselbe platonische Idee findet man wieder\n im Protest der englischen Bleichereibesitzer gegen die Klausel des Fabrikakts, die\n eine bestimmte Essstunde für alle Arbeiter festsetzt. Ihr Geschäft könne sich nicht\n nach den Arbeitern richten, denn „in the various operations of singeing, washing,\n bleaching, mangling, calendering, and dyeing, none of them can be stopped at a\n given moment without risk of damage … to enforce the same dinner hour for\n all the work people might occasionally subject valuable goods to the risk of\n danger for incomplete operations.“ Le platonisme où va-t-il se nicher!] , der die Theilung der Ar-\n beit als Grundlage der gesellschaftlichen Scheidung der Stände behandelt,\n als bei Xenophon [Note: Xenophon erzählt, es sei nicht nur ehrenvoll Speisen von der Tafel\n des Perserkönigs zu erhalten, sondern diese Speisen seien auch viel schmackhafter\n als andere. „Und diess ist nichts Wunderbares, denn wie die übrigen Künste in\n den grossen Städten besonders vervollkommnet sind, ebenso werden die könig-\n lichen Speisen ganz eigens zubereitet. Denn in den kleinen Städten macht\n Derselbe Bettstelle, Thüre, Pflug, Tisch; oft baut er obendrein noch Häuser und] , der mit seinem charakteristisch bürgerlichen In-\n [Note: ἀνϑϱώπων ἢ χϱήμασι πολεμεῖν“ (Thuc. l. I, c. 41), blieb ihnen auch in der ma-\n teriellen Produktion die αὐταϱϰεία, die der Theilung der Arbeit gegenüber-\n steht, das Ideal, „παϱ᾽ ὧν γὰϱ τὸ εὖ, παϱὰ τούτων ϰαὶ τὸ αὔταϱϰες.“ Man muss\n dabei erwägen, dass es noch zur Zeit des Sturzes der 30 Tyrannen keine 5000\n Athener ohne Grundeigenthum gab.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"353\", \"facs\": \"#f0372\"} --> stinkt schon der Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb einer Werkstatt näher\n rückt. Plato’s Republik, soweit in ihr die Theilung der Arbeit als das\n gestaltende Prinzip des Staats entwickelt wird, ist nur atheniensische\n Idealisirung des ägyptischen Kastenwesens, wie Aegypten\n als industrielles Musterland auch andern seiner Zeitgenossen gilt, z. B.\n dem Isokrates [Note: „Er (Busiris) theilte Alle in besondere Kasten … befahl, dass immer\n die Nämlichen die gleichen Geschäfte treiben sollten, weil er wusste, dass die,\n welche mit ihren Beschäftigungen wechseln, in keinem Geschäft gründlich werden;\n die aber, welche beständig bei denselben Beschäftigungen bleiben, jedes aufs\n vollendetste zu Stande bringen. Wirklich werden wir auch finden, dass sie in\n Beziehung auf Künste und Gewerbe ihre Rivalen mehr übertroffen haben als sonst\n der Meister den Stümper und in Beziehung auf die Einrichtung, wodurch sie die\n Künigsherrschaft und übrige Staatsverfassung erhalten, so vortrefflich sind, dass\n die berühmtesten Philosophen, welche darüber zu sprechen unternehmen, die\n Staatsverfassung Aegyptens vor andern lobten.“ (Isocr. Busiris, c. 8.)] , und diese Bedeutung selbst noch für die Griechen der\n römischen Kaiserzeit behielt [Note: cf. Diod. Sic.] .\n\n\n\n\nWährend der eigentlichen Manufakturperiode, d. h. der Periode, wo-\n rin die Manufaktur die herrschende Form der kapitalistischen Produktions-\n weise, stösst die volle Ausführung ihrer eigenen Tendenzen auf vielseitige\n Hindernisse. Obgleich sie, wie wir sahen, neben der hierarchischen Glie-\n derung der Arbeiter eine einfache Scheidung zwischen geschickten und\n ungeschickten Arbeitern schafft, bleibt die Zahl der letztern durch den\n überwiegenden Einfluss der erstern sehr beschränkt. Obgleich sie die\n Sonderoperationen dem verschiednen Grad von Reife, Kraft und Entwick-\n [Note: ist zufrieden, wenn er selbst so eine für seinen Unterhalt ausreichende Kundschaft\n findet. Es ist rein unmöglich, dass ein Mensch, der so vielerlei treibt, alles gut\n mache. In den grossen Städten aber, wo jeder Einzelne viele Käufer\n findet, genügt auch ein Handwerk, um seinen Mann zu nähren. Ja oft gehört\n dazu nicht einmal ein ganzes Handwerk, sondern der eine macht Mannsschuhe,\n der andere Weiberschuhe. Hier und da lebt einer bloss vom Nähen, der andere\n vom Zuschneiden der Schuhe; der eine schneidet bloss Kleider zu, der andere setzt\n die Stücke nur zusammen. Nothwendig ist es nun, dass der Verrichter der ein-\n fachsten Arbelt sie unbedingt auch am besten macht. Ebenso steht’s mit der\n Kochkunst.“ (Xen. Cyrop. l. VIII, c. 2.) Die zu erzielende Güte des Ge-\n brauchswerths wird hier ausschliesslich fixirt, obgleich schon Xenophon die\n Stufenleiter der Arbeitstheilung vom Umfang des Markts abhängig weiss.] \n [Running matter: I. 23] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"354\", \"facs\": \"#f0373\"} --> lung ihrer lehendigen Arbeitsorgane anpasst und daher zu produktiver\n Ausbeutung von Weibern und Kindern drängt, scheitert diese Tendenz im\n Grossen und Ganzen an den Gewohnheiten und dem Widerstand der männ-\n lichen Arbeiter. Obgleich die Zersetzung der handwerksmässigen Thätig-\n keit die Bildungskosten und daher den Werth der Arbeiter senkt, bleibt\n für schwierigere Detailarbeit eine längere Erlernungszeit nöthig und wird\n auch da, wo sie vom Ueberfluss, eifersüchtig von den Arbeitern aufrecht\n erhalten. Wir finden z. B. in England die laws of apprenticeship mit\n ihrer siebenjährigen Lernzeit bis zum Ende der Manufakturperiode in Voll-\n kraft und erst von der grossen Industrie über Haufen geworfen. Da das\nHandwerksgeschick die Grundlage der Manufaktur bleibt, und der in ihr\n funktionirende Gesammtmechanismus kein von den Arbeitern selbst unab-\n hängiges objektives Skelett besitzt, ringt das Kapital beständig mit\n der Insubordination der Arbeiter. „Die Schwäche der menschlichen Na-\n tur,“ ruft Freund Ure aus, „ist so gross, dass der Arbeiter; je geschickter,\n desto eigenwilliger und schwieriger zu behandeln wird, und folglich dem\n Gesammtmechanismus durch seine rappelköpfigen Capricen schweren\n Schaden zufügt“ [Note: Ure l. c. t. I, p. 31.] . Durch die ganze Manufakturperiode läuft daher die\n Klage über den Disciplinmangel der Arbeiter [Note: Das im Text Gesagte gilt viel mehr für England als für Frankreich und\n mehr für Frankreich als Holland.] . Und hätten wir nicht\n die Zeugnisse gleichzeitiger Schriftsteller, die einfachen Thatsachen, dass\n es vom 16. Jahrhundert bis zur Epoche der grossen Industrie dem Kapital\n misslingt, sich der ganzen disponiblen Arbeitszeit der Manufakturarbeiter\n zu bemächtigen, dass die Manufakturen kurzlebig sind und mit der Ein-\n oder Auswanderung der Arbeiter ihren Sitz in dem einen Land verlassen\n und in dem andern aufschlagen, würden Bibliotheken sprechen. „Ord-\n nung muss auf die eine oder die andre Weise gestiftet werden“, ruft 1770\n der wiederholt citirte Verfasser des „Essay on Trade and Commerce“.\nOrdnung, hallt es 66 Jahre später zurück aus dem Mund des Dr. Andrew\n Ure, „Ordnung“ fehlte in der auf „dem scholastischen Dogma der Theilung\n der Arbeit“ beruhenden Manufaktur, und „Arkwright schuf die\n Ordnung.“\n\n\n\n\nZugleich konnte die Manufaktur die gesellschaftliche Produktion weder\n in ihrem ganzen Umfang ergreifen, noch in ihrer Tiefe umwälzen. Sie\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"355\", \"facs\": \"#f0374\"} --> gipfelte als ökonomisches Kunstwerk auf der breiten Grundlage des städti-\n schen Handwerks und der ländlich häuslichen Nebenindustrie. Ihre eigne\n enge technologische Basis trat auf einem gewissen Entwicklungsgrad mit\n den von ihr selbst geschaffenen Produktionsbedürfnissen in Widerspruch.\n\n\n\n\nEins ihrer vollendetsten Gebilde war die Werkstatt zur Produktion der\nArbeitsinstrumente selbst, und namentlich auch der bereits angewand-\n ten komplicirteren mechanischen Apparate. „Ein solches Atelier“, sagt\n Ure, „bot dem Auge die Theilung der Arbeit in ihren mannigfachen Abstu-\n fungen. Bohrer, Meisel, Drechselbank hatten jede ihre eignen Arbeiter,\n hierarchisch gegliedert nach dem Grad ihrer Geschicklichkeit.“ Diess\n Produkt der manufakturmässigen Theilung der Arbeit producirte seiner-\n seits — Maschinen. Sie heben die handwerksmässige Thätigkeit als\n das regelnde Princip der gesellschaftlichen Produktion auf. So wird einer-\n seits der technologische Grund der lebenslangen Annexation des Arbeiters\n an eine Theilfunktion weggeräumt. Andrerseits fallen die Schranken, welche\n dasselbe Princip der Herrschaft des Kapitals noch auferlegte.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0001","text":"Die auf Theilung der Arbeit beruhende Cooperation schafft sich ihre\n klassische Gestalt in der Manufaktur. Als charakteristische Form des\n kapitalistischen Produktionsprozesses herrscht sie vor während der eigent-\n lichen Manufakturperiode, die, rauh angeschlagen, von Mitte des\n 16. Jahrhunderts bis zum letzten Dritttheil des achtzehnten währt.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"13.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0002","text":"Die Manufaktur entspringt auf doppelte Weise.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"13.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0003","text":"Entweder werden Arbeiter von verschiedenartigen, selbstständi-\n gen Handwerken, durch deren Hände ein Produkt bis zu seiner letzten Reife\n laufen muss, in eine Werkstatt unter dem Kommando desselben Kapitalisten\nvereinigt. Z. B. eine Kutsche war das Gesammtprodukt der Arbeiten einer\n grossen Anzahl unabhängiger Handwerker, wie Stellmacher, Sattler, Schnei-\n der, Schlosser, Gürtler, Drechsler, Posamentirer, Glaser, Maler, Lackirer,\n Vergolder u. s. w. In der Kutschenmanufaktur wurden alle diese ver-\n schiednen Handwerker in einem Arbeitshaus vereinigt, um einander gleich-\n zeitig in die Hand zu arbeiten. Man kann eine Kutsche zwar nicht vergolden,\n bevor sie gemacht ist. Werden aber viele Kutschen gleichzeitig gemacht, so\n kann ein Theil beständig vergoldet werden, während ein andrer Theil eine\n frühere Phase des Produktionsprozesses durchläuft.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"13.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0004","text":"Soweit stehn wir\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"319\", \"facs\": \"#f0338\"} --> noch auf dem Boden der einfachen Cooperation, die ihr Material an Men-\n schen und Dingen vorfindet. Indess tritt sehr bald eine wesentliche\n Veränderung ein. Der Schneider, Schlosser, Gürtler u. s. w., der nur im\n Kutschenmachen beschäftigt ist, verliert nach und nach mit der Gewohn-\n heit auch die Fähigkeit, sein altes Handwerk in seiner ganzen Ausdehnung\n zu betreiben. Andrerseits erhält sein vereinseitigtes Thun jetzt die zweck-\n mässigste Form für die verengte Wirkungssphäre. Ursprünglich erschien\n die Kutschenmanufaktur als eine Kombination selbstständiger\n Handwerke.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"13.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0005","text":"Sie wird allmälig Theilung der Kutschenpro-\n duktion in ihre verschiednen Sonderoperationen, wovon\n jede einzelne zur ausschliesslichen Funktion eines Arbeiters krystallisirt\n und deren Gesammtheit vom Verein dieser Theilarbeiter ver-\n richtet wird. Ebenso entstand die Tuchmanufaktur, und eine ganze\n Reihe andrer Manufakturen, aus der Kombination verschiedner Hand-\n werke unter dem Kommando desselben Kapitals [Note: Um ein mehr modernes Beispiel dieser Bildungsart der Manufaktur anzu-\n führen, folgendes Citat: Die Seidenspinnerei und Weberei von Lyon und Nimes\n „est toute patriarcale; elle emploie beaucoup de femmes et d’enfants, mais sans\n les épuiser ni les corrompre; elle les laisse dans leurs belles vallées de la Drôme,\n du Var, de l’Isère, de Vaucluse, pour y élever des vers et divider leurs cocons;\n jamais elle n’entre dans une véritable fabrique.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"13.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0006","text":"Pour être aussi bien observé …\n le principe de la division du travail, s’y revêt d’un caractère spécial. Il y a bien\n des divideuses, des moulineurs, des teinturieurs, des encolleurs, puis des tisse-\n rands; mais ils ne sont pas réunis dans un même établissement, ne dépendent pas\n d’un même maître; tous ils sont indépendants.“ (A. Blanqui: „Cours\n d’Econ. Industrielle. Recueilli par A. Blaise. Paris (1838—39)“,\n p. 44—80 passim.) Seit Blanqui diess schrieb, sind die verschiednen unab-\n hängigen Arbeiter zum Theil in Fabriken vereinigt worden.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"13.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0007","text":"Die Manufaktur entspringt aber auch auf entgegengesetztem\n Wege. Es werden viele Handwerker, die Dasselbe oder Gleich-\n artiges thun, z. B. Papier oder Typen oder Nadeln machen, von dem-\n selben Kapital gleichzeitig in derselben Werkstatt beschäf-\n tigt. Es ist diess Cooperation in der einfachsten Form. Jeder dieser\n Handwerker (vielleicht mit einem oder zwei Gesellen) macht die ganze\n Waare und vollbringt also die verschiednen zu ihrer Herstellung erheisch-\n ten Operationen der Reihe nach. Er arbeitet in seiner alten handwerks-\n mässigen Weise fort. Indess veranlassen bald äussere Umstände die Kon-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"320\", \"facs\": \"#f0339\"} --> centration der Arbeiter in demselben Raum und die Gleichzeitigkeit ihrer\n Arbeiten anders zu vernutzen. Es soll z. B. ein grösseres Quantum fer-\n tiger Waare in einer bestimmten Zeitfrist geliefert werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"13.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0008","text":"Die Arbeit\n wird daher vertheilt. Statt die verschiedenen Operationen von dem-\n selben Handwerker in einer zeitlichen Reihefolge verrichten zu lassen,\n werden sie von einander losgelöst, isolirt, räumlich neben einander gestellt,\n jede derselben einem andern Handwerker zugewiesen und alle zusammen\n von den Cooperirenden gleichzeitig ausgeführt. Diese zufällige Verthei-\n lung wiederholt sich, zeigt ihre eigenthümlichen Vortheile, und verknö-\n chert nach und nach zur systematischen Theilung der Arbeit. Aus\n dem individuellen Produkt eines selbstständigen Handwerkers, der\n vielerlei thut, verwandelt sich die Waare in das gesellschaftliche\n Produkt eines Vereins von Handwerkern, von denen jeder fortwährend nur\n eine und dieselbe Theiloperation verrichtet.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"13.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0009","text":"Dieselben Operationen, die\n in einander flossen als successive Verrichtungen des deutschen zünftigen\n Papiermachers, verselbstständigten sich in der holländischen Papiermanufak-\n tur zu nebeneinander laufenden Theiloperationen vieler cooperirenden Ar-\n beiter. Der zünftige Nadler von Nürnberg bildet das Grundelement der eng-\n lischen Nadelmanufaktur. Während aber jener eine Nadler eine Reihe von\n vielleicht 20 Operationen nach einander durchlief, verrichteten hier bald 20\n Nadler neben einander, jeder nur eine der 20 Operationen, die in Folge von\n Erfahrungen noch viel weiter gespaltet, isolirt, und zu ausschliesslichen\n Funktionen einzelner Arbeiter verselbstständigt wurden.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"13.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0010","text":"Die Ursprungsweise der Manufaktur, ihre Herausbildung aus dem\n Handwerk, ist also zwieschlächtig. Einerseits geht sie von der Kom-\n bination verschiedenartiger, selbstständiger Handwerke aus, die\n bis zu dem Punkt verunselbstständigt und vereinseitigt werden,\n wo sie nur noch einander ergänzende Theiloperationen im Produktions-\n prozess einer und derselben Waare bilden. Andrerseits geht sie von der\nCooperation gleichartiger Handwerker aus, zersetzt dasselbe\n individuelle Handwerk in seine verschiednen besondern Operationen, und\n isolirt und verselbstständigt diese bis zu dem Punkt, wo jede der-\n selben zur ausschliesslichen Funktion eines besondern Arbeiters wird. Einer-\n seits führt daher die Manufaktur Theilung der Arbeit in einen Produk-\n tionsprozess ein oder entwickelt sie weiter, andrerseits kombinirt sie früher\n geschiedene Handwerke.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"13.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0011","text":"Welches aber immer ihr besondrer Ausgangs-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"321\", \"facs\": \"#f0340\"} --> punkt, ihre Schlussgestalt ist dieselbe — ein Produktionsmecha-\n nismus, dessen Organe Menschen sind.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"13.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0012","text":"Zum richtigen Verständniss der Theilung der Arbeit in der Manufak-\n tur ist es wesentlich folgende Punkte festzuhalten: Zunächst fällt die\nAnalyse des Produktionsprozesses in seine besondern\n Phasen hier ganz und gar zusammen mit der Zersetzung einer\n handwerksmässigen Thätigkeit in ihre verschiedenen\n Theiloperationen. Zusammengesetzt oder einfach, die Verrichtung\n bleibt handwerksmässig und daher abhängig von Kraft, Geschick,\n Schnelle, Sicherheit des Einzelarbeiters in Handhabung seines Instruments.\n Das Handwerk bleibt die Basis. Diese enge technologische Basis\n schliesst wirklich wissenschaftliche Analyse des Produktionsprozesses aus,\n da jeder Theilprozess, den das Produkt untergeht, als handwerksmässige\n Theilarbeit ausführbar sein muss.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"13.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0013","text":"Eben weil das handwerksmässige Ge-\n schick so die Grundlage des Produktionsprozesses bleibt, wird jeder Ar-\n beiter ausschliesslich einer Theilfunktion angeeignet und seine Arbeits-\n kraft in das lebenslängliche Organ dieser Theilfunktion verwandelt. End-\n lich ist diese Theilung der Arbeit eine besondere Art der Coopera-\n tion, und manche ihrer Vortheile entspringen aus dem allgemeinen Wesen,\n nicht aus dieser besonderen Form der Cooperation.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"13.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0014","text":"Gehn wir nun näher auf das Einzelne ein, so ist zunächst klar, dass\n ein Arbeiter, der lebenslang eine und dieselbe einfache Operation verrichtet,\n seinen ganzen Körper in ihr automatisch einseitiges Organ verwandelt und\n daher weniger Zeit dazu verbraucht als der Handwerker, der eine ganze\n Reihe von Operationen abwechselnd ausführt. Der kombinirte Ge-\n sammtarbeiter, der den lebendigen Mechanismus der Manufaktur\n bildet, besteht aber aus lauter solchen einseitigen Theilarbeitern. Im Ver-\n gleich zum selbstständigen Handwerk wird daher mehr in weniger Zeit\n producirt oder die Produktivkraft der Arbeit gesteigert [Note: „The more any manufacture of much variety shall be distributed and\n assigned to different artists, the same must needs be better done and with greater\n expedition, with less loss of time and labour.“ („The Advantages of the\n East India Trade. Lond. 1720“, p. 71.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"13.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0015","text":"Auch vervoll-\n kommnet sich die Methode der Theilarbeit, nachdem sie zur ausschliess-\n lichen Funktion einer Person verselbstständigt ist. Die stete Wieder-\n [Running matter: I. 21] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"322\", \"facs\": \"#f0341\"} --> holung desselben beschränkten Thuns und die Koncentration der Aufmerk-\n samkeit auf diess Beschränkte lehren erfahrungsmässig den bezweckten\n Nutzeffekt mit geringstem Kraftaufwand erreichen. Da aber immer ver-\n schiedne Arbeitergenerationen gleichzeitig zusammenleben und in densel-\n ben Manufakturen zusammenwirken, befestigen, häufen und übertragen\n sich bald die so gewonnenen technischen Kunstgriffe [Note: „Easy labour is transmitted skill.“ (Th. Hodgskin l. c. p. 125.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"13.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0016","text":"Die Manu-\n faktur producirt in der That die Virtuosität des Detailarbeiters, indem sie\n die naturwüchsige Sonderung der Gewerbe, die sie in der Gesellschaft vor-\n fand, im Innern der Werkstatt reproducirt und systematisch zum Extrem\n treibt. Andrerseits entspricht ihre Verwandlung der Theilarbeit in den\n Lebensberuf eines Menschen dem Trieb früherer Gesellschaften, die Ge-\n werbe erblich zu machen, sie in Kasten zu versteinern, oder, wo be-\n stimmte historische Bedingungen dem Kastenwesen widersprechende Varia-\n bilität des Individuums erzeugen, die Arbeitssonderung wenigstens in\nZünfte zu verknöchern.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"13.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0017","text":"Die Entstehung dieser Kasten und Zünfte folgt\n demselben Naturgesetz, das die Sonderung von Pflanzen und Thieren in Arten\n und Unterarten regelt, nur dass auf einem gewissen Entwicklungsgrad die\n Erblichkeit der Kasten oder die Ausschliesslichkeit der Zünfte als ge-\n sellschaftliches Gesetz dekretirt wird [Note: „Auch die Künste sind … in Aegypten zu dem gehörigen Grad von\n Vollkommenheit gediehn. Denn in diesem Lande allein dürfen die Handwerker\n durchaus nicht in die Geschäfte einer andern Bürgerklasse eingreifen, sondern\n bloss den nach dem Gesetz ihrem Stamme erblich zugehörigen Beruf treiben …\n Bei andern Völkern findet man, dass die Gewerbsleute ihre Aufmerksamkeit auf\n zu viele Gegenstände vertheilen … Bald versuchen sie es mit dem Landbau, bald\n lassen sie sich in Handelsgeschäfte ein, bald befassen sie sich mit zwei oder drei\n Künsten zugleich.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"13.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0018","text":"In Freistaaten laufen sie meist in die Volksversammlungen …\n In Aegypten dagegen verfällt jeder Handwerker in schwere Strafen, wenn er sich\n in Staatsgeschäfte mischt, oder mehrere Künste zugleich treibt. So kann nichts\n ihren Berußfleiss stören … Zudem, wie sie von ihren Vorfahren viele Regeln\n haben, sind sie eifrig darauf bedacht, noch neue Vortheile aufzufinden.“ (Dio-\n dorus Siculus: Historische Bibliothek l. I, c. 74.)] . „Die Musline von\n Dakka sind an Feinheit, die Kattune und andre Zeuge von Koromandel\n an Pracht und Dauerhaftigkeit der Farben, niemals übertroffen worden.\n Und dennoch werden sie producirt ohne Kapital, Maschinerie, Theilung\n der Arbeit, oder irgend eins der andern Mittel, die der Fabrikation in\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"323\", \"facs\": \"#f0342\"} --> Europa so viele Vortheile bieten.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"13.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0019","text":"Der Weber ist ein vereinzeltes Indivi-\n duum, der das Gewebe auf Bestellung eines Kunden verfertigt und mit\n einem Webstuhl von der einfachsten Konstruktion, manchmal nur beste-\n hend aus hölzernen Stangen, die roh zusammengefügt sind. Er besitzt\n selbst keinen Apparat zum Aufziehn der Kette, der Webstuhl muss daher\n in seiner ganzen Länge ausgestreckt bleiben und wird so unförmlich und\n weit, dass er keinen Raum findet in der Hütte des Producenten, der seine\n Arbeit daher in freier Luft verrichten muss, wo sie durch jede Wetter-\n änderung unterbrochen wird“ [Note: „Historical and descriptive Account of Brit. India etc. by\n Hugh Murray, James Wilson etc.“ Edinburgh 1832, v. II, p. 449.\n Der indische Webstuhl ist hochschäftig, d. h. die Kette ist vertikal aufgespannt.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"13.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0020","text":"Es ist nur das von Generation auf\n Generation gehäufte und von Vater auf Sohn vererbte Sondergeschick,\n das dem Hindu wie der Spinne diese Virtuosität verleiht. Und dennoch\n verrichtet ein solcher indischer Weber sehr komplicirte Arbeit, verglichen\n mit der Mehrzahl der Manufakturarbeiter.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"13.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0021","text":"Ein Handwerker, der die verschiedenen Theilprozesse in der Pro-\n duktion eines Machwerks nach einander ausführt, muss bald den Platz,\n bald die Instrumente wechseln. Der Uebergang von einer Operation zur\n andern unterbricht den Fluss seiner Arbeit und bildet gewissermassen\nPoren in seinem Arbeitstag. Diese Poren verdichten, sobald er den\n ganzen Tag ein und dieselbe Operation kontinuirlich verrichtet oder sie\n verschwinden in dem Masse, wie der Wechsel seiner Operationen abnimmt.\n Die gesteigerte Produktivität ist hier entweder der zunehmenden Ausgabe\n von Arbeitskraft in einem gegebnen Zeitraum geschuldet, also wach-\n sender Intensivität der Arbeit, oder einer Abnahme des\n unproduktiven Verzehrs von Arbeitskraft.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"13.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0022","text":"Der Ueberschuss\n von Kraftaufwand nämlich, den jeder Uebergang aus der Ruhe in die Be-\n wegung erheischt, kompensirt sich bei längerer Fortdauer der einmal er-\n reichten Normalgeschwindigkeit. Andrerseits zerstört die Kontinuität\n gleichförmiger Arbeit die Spann- und Schwungkraft der Lebensgeister,\n die im Wechsel der Thätigkeit selbst ihre Erholung und ihren Reiz\n finden.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"13.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0023","text":"Die Produktivität der Arbeit hängt nicht nur von der Virtuosität\n des Arbeiters ab, sondern auch von der Vollkommenheit seiner Werk-\n [Running matter: 21*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"324\", \"facs\": \"#f0343\"} -->zeuge. Werkzeuge derselben Art, wie Schneide-, Bohr-, Stoss-, Schlag-\n instrumente u. s. w. werden in verschiedenen Arbeitsprozessen gebraucht,\n und in demselben Arbeitsprozess dient dasselbe Instrument zu verschiednen\n Verrichtungen. Sobald jedoch die verschiednen Operationen eines Ar-\n beitsprozesses von einander losgelöst sind und jede Theiloperation in der\n Hand des Theilarbeiters eine möglichst entsprechende und daher ausschliess-\n liche Form erhält, werden Veränderungen der vorher zu verschiednen\n Zwecken dienenden Werkzeuge nothwendig.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"13.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0024","text":"Die Richtung ihres Form-\n wechsels ergiebt sich aus der Erfahrung der besondern Schwierigkeiten,\n welche die unveränderte Form in den Weg legt. Die Differen-\n zirung der Arbeitsinstrumente, wodurch Instrumente derselben Art be-\n sondre feste Formen für jede besondre Nutzanwendung erhalten, und ihre\nSpezialisirung, wodurch jedes solches Sonderinstrument nur in der\n Hand spezifischer Theilarbeiter in seinem ganzen Umfang wirkt, charak-\n terisiren die Manufaktur. Zu Birmingham allein producirt man etwa 500\n Varietäten von Hämmern, wovon jeder nicht nur für einen besondern Pro-\n duktionsprozess, sondern eine Anzahl Varietäten oft nur für verschiedne\n Operationen in demselben Prozess dient.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"13.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0025","text":"Die Manufakturperiode verein-\n facht, verbessert und vermannigfacht die Arbeitswerkzeuge durch deren\n Anpassung an die ausschliesslichen Sonderfunktionen der Theilarbeiter [Note: Darwin bemerkt in seinem Epoche machenden Werk über „die Ent-\n stehung der Arten“ mit Bezug auf die natürlichen Organe der Pflanzen und\n Thiere: „So lange ein und dasselbe Organ verschiedene Arbeiten zu verrichten\n hat, lässt sich ein Grund für seine Veränderlichkeit vielleicht darin finden, dass\n natürliche Züchtung jede kleine Abweichung der Form weniger sorgfältig erhält\n oder unterdrückt, als wenn dasselbe Organ nur zu einem besondern Zwecke allein\n bestimmt wäre. So mögen Messer, welche allerlei Dinge zu schneiden bestimmt\n sind, im Ganzen so ziemlich von einerlei Form sein, während ein nur zu einerlei\n Gebrauch bestimmtes Werkzeug für jeden andern Gebrauch auch eine andre Form\n haben muss.“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"13.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0026","text":"Sie schafft damit zugleich eine der materiellen Bedingungen der\n Maschinerie, die aus einer Kombination einfacher Instrumente besteht.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"13.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0027","text":"Der Detailarbeiter und sein Instrument bilden die einfachen Ele-\n mente der Manufaktur. Wenden wir uns jetzt zu ihrem Gesammt-\n mechanismus.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"13.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0028","text":"Die Gliederung der Manufaktur besitzt zwei Grundformen, die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"325\", \"facs\": \"#f0344\"} --> trotz gelegentlicher Verschlingung zwei wesentlich verschiedne Arten bil-\n den und namentlich auch bei der späteren Verwandlung der Manufaktur\n in die maschinenartig betriebene, grosse Industrie eine ganz verschiedne\n Rolle spielen. Dieser Doppelcharakter entspringt aus der Natur des\n Machwerks selbst. Es wird entweder gebildet durch bloss mechanische\n Zusammensetzung selbstständiger Theilprodukte oder verdankt seine fer-\n tige Gestalt einer Reihenfolge zusammenhängender Prozesse und Manipu-\n lationen.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"13.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0029","text":"Eine Lokomotive z. B. besteht aus mehr als 5000 selbstständigen\n Theilen. Sie kann jedoch nicht als Beispiel der ersten Art der eigent-\n lichen Manufaktur gelten, weil sie ein Gebilde der grossen Industrie ist.\n Wohl aber die Uhr, an welcher auch William Petty die manufaktur-\n mässige Theilung der Arbeit veranschaulicht. Aus dem individuellen\n Werk eines Nürnberger Handwerkers verwandelte sich die Uhr in das ge-\n sellschaftliche Produkt einer Unzahl von Theilarbeitern, wie Rohwerk-\n macher, Uhrfedermacher, Zifferblattmacher, Spiralfedermacher, Steinloch-\n und Rubinhebelmacher, Zeigermacher, Gehäusemacher, Schraubenmacher,\n Vergolder, mit vielen Unterabtheilungen, wie z. B. Räderfabrikant\n (Messing- und Stahlräder wieder geschieden), Triebmacher, Zeigerwerk-\n macher, acheveur de pignon (befestigt die Räder auf den Trieben, polirt\n die facettes u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"13.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0030","text":"w.), Zapfenmacher, planteur de finissage (setzt verschiedne\n Räder und Triebe in das Werk), finisseur de barillet (lässt Zähne ein-\n schneiden, macht die Löcher zur richtigen Weite, härtet Stellung und Ge-\n sperr), Hemmungmacher, bei der Cylinderhemmung wieder Cylindermacher,\n Steigradmacher, Unruhemacher, Raquettemacher (das Rückwerk, woran\n die Uhr regulirt wird), planteur d’échappement (eigentliche Hemmung-\n macher); dann der repasseur de barillet (macht das Federhaus und\n Stellung ganz fertig), Stahlpolirer, Räderpolirer, Schraubenpolirer, Zahlen-\n maler, Blattmacher (schmelzen das Email auf das Kupfer), fabricant\n de pendants (macht bloss die Bügel des Gehäuses), finisseur de charnière\n (steckt das Messingstift in die Mitte des Gehäuses u. s. w.), faiseur de\n secret (macht die Federn im Gehäuse, die den Deckel aufspringen machen),\n graveur, ciliceur, polisseur de boite u. s. w., u.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"13.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0031","text":"s. w., endlich der re-\n passeur, der die ganze Uhr zusammensetzt und sie gehend abliefert. Nur\n wenige Theile der Uhr laufen durch verschiedne Hände und alle diese\n membra disjecta sammeln sich erst in der Hand, die sie schliesslich in ein\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"326\", \"facs\": \"#f0345\"} --> mechanisches Ganze verbindet. Diess äusserliche Verhältniss des fertigen\n Produkts zu seinen verschiedenartigen Elementen lässt hier, wie bei ähn-\n lichem Machwerk, die Kombination der Theilarbeiter in derselben\n Werkstatt zufällig. Die Theilarbeiten können selbst wieder als von ein-\n ander unabhängige Handwerke betrieben werden, wie im Kanton Waadt\n und Neufchatel, während in Genf z. B. grosse Uhrenmanufakturen bestehn,\n d. h. unmittelbare Cooperation der Theilarbeiter unter dem Kommando\neines Kapitals stattfindet.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"13.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0032","text":"Auch im letztren Fall werden Zifferblatt, Feder\n und Gehäuse selten in der Manufaktur selbst verfertigt. Der kombinirte\n manufakturmässige Betrieb ist hier nur unter ausnahmsweisen Verhält-\n nissen profitlich, weil die Konkurrenz unter den Arbeitern, die zu Hause\n arbeiten wollen, am grössten ist, die Zersplitterung der Produktion in eine\n Masse heterogener Prozesse wenig Verwendung gemeinschaftlicher Arbeits-\n mittel erlaubt, und der Kapitalist bei der zerstreuten Fabrikation die Aus-\n lage für Arbeitsgebäude u. s. w. erspart [Note: Genf hat im Jahr 1854 80,000 Uhren producirt, noch nicht ein Fünftheil\n der Uhrenproduktion des Kanton Neufchatel. Chaux-de-Fonds, das man als eine\n einzige Uhrenmanufaktur betrachten kann, liefert allein jährlich doppelt so viel als\n Genf. Von 1850—61 lieferte Genf 750,000 Uhren. Siehe: „Report from\n Geneva on the Watch Trade“ in „Reports by H. M’s.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"13.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0033","text":"Secretaries\n of Embassy and Legation on the Manufactures, Commerce etc.\n No. 6. 1863.“ Wenn die Zusammenhangslosigkeit der Prozesse, worin die Pro-\n duktion nur zusammengesetzter Machwerke zerfällt, an und für sich die\n Verwandlung solcher Manufakturen in den Maschinenbetrieb der grossen Industrie\n sehr erschwert, kommen bei der Uhr noch zwei andre Hindernisse hinzu, die Klein-\n heit und Delikatesse ihrer Elemente, und ihr Luxuscharakter, daher ihre Varietät,\n so dass z. B. in den besten Londoner Häusern das ganze Jahr hindurch kaum ein\n Dutzend Uhren gemacht werden, die sich ähnlich sehn. Die Uhrenfabrik von\n Vacheron et Constantin, die mit Erfolg Maschinerie anwendet, liefert auch höch-\n stens 3—4 verschiedne Varietäten von Grösse und Form.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"13.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0034","text":"Indess ist auch das Ver-\n hältniss dieser Detailarbeiter, die zu Hause, aber für einen Kapitalisten\n (Fabrikant, établisseur) arbeiten, ganz und gar verschieden von dem des\n selbstständigen Handwerkers, der für seine eignen Kunden arbeitet [Note: In der Uhrmacherei, diesem klassischen Beispiel der heterogenen Manu-\n faktur, kann man sehr genau die oben erwähnte, aus der Zersetzung der handwerks-\n mässigen Thätigkeit entspringende Differenzirung und Spezialisirung\n der Arbeitsinstrumente studiren.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"13.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0035","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"327\", \"facs\": \"#f0346\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"13.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0036","text":"Die zweite Art der Manufaktur, ihre vollendete Form, producirt\n Machwerke, die zusammenhängende Entwicklungsphasen, eine Reihenfolge\n von Stufenprozessen durchlaufen, wie z. B. der Draht in der Nähnadel-\n manufaktur die Hände von 72 und selbst 92 spezifischen Theilarbeitern\n durchläuft.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"13.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0037","text":"Soweit solche Manufaktur ursprünglich zerstreute Handwerke kom-\n binirt, vermindert sie die räumliche Trennung zwischen den beson-\n dern Produktionsphasen des Machwerks. Die Zeit seines Uebergangs aus\n einem Stadium in das andre wird verkürzt, ebenso die Arbeit, welche diese\n Uebergänge vermittelt [Note: „In so close a cohabitation of the People, the carriage must needs be\n less.“ („The Advantages of the East India Trade“, p. 166.)] . Im Vergleich zum Handwerk wird so Produk-\n tivkraft gewonnen, und zwar entspringt dieser Gewinn aus dem allge-\n meinen cooperativen Charakter der Manufaktur. Andrerseits\n bedingt ihr eigenthümliches Prinzip der Theilung der Arbeit eine Iso-\n lirung der verschiednen Produktionsphasen, die als eben so viele hand-\n werksmässige Theilarbeiten gegen einander verselbstständigt sind.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"13.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0038","text":"Die\n Herstellung und Erhaltung des Zusammenhangs zwischen den isolirten\n Funktionen ernöthigt beständigen Transport des Machwerks aus einer\n Hand in die andre und aus einem Prozess in den andern. Vom Stand-\n punkt der grossen Industrie tritt diess als eine charakteristische,\n kostspielige und dem Prinzip der Manufaktur immanente Beschränkt-\n heit hervor [Note: „The isolation of the different stages of manufacture consequent upon\n the employment of manual labour adds immensely to the cost of production, the\n loss mainly arising from the mere removals from one process to another.“ („The\n Industry of Nations. Lond. 1855“, Part II, p. 200.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"13.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0039","text":"Betrachtet man ein bestimmtes Quantum Rohmaterial, z. B. von\n Lumpen in der Papiermanufaktur oder von Draht in der Nadelmanufak-\n tur, so durchläuft es in den Händen der verschiednen Theilarbeiter eine\n zeitliche Stufenfolge von Produktionsphasen bis zu seiner Schlussgestalt.\n Betrachtet man dagegen die Werkstatt als einen Gesammtmechanismus,\n so befindet sich das Rohmaterial gleichzeitig in allen seinen Produk-\n tionsphasen auf einmal. Mit einem Theil seiner vielen instrumentbewaff-\n neten Hände zieht der aus den Detailarbeitern kombinirte Gesammtarbeiter\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"328\", \"facs\": \"#f0347\"} --> den Draht, während er gleichzeitig mit andern Händen und Werkzeugen\n ihn streckt, mit andern schneidet, spitzt u. s. w. Aus einem zeitlichen\n Nacheinander sind die verschiednen Stufenprozesse in ein räumliches\n Nebeneinander verwandelt.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"13.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0040","text":"Daher Lieferung von mehr fertiger Waare\n in demselben Zeitraum [Note: „It (the division of labour) produces also an economy of time, by separat-\n ing the work into its different branches, all of which may be carried on into exe-\n cution at the same moment … By carrying on all the different processes at once,\n which an individual must have executed separately, it becomes possible to produce\n a multitude of pins for instance completely finished in the same time as a single\n pin might have been either cut or pointed.“ (Dugald Stewart l. c. p. 319.)] . Jene Gleichzeitigkeit entspringt zwar aus der\n allgemeinen cooperativen Form des Gesammtprozesses, aber die Manufak-\n tur findet nicht nur die Bedingungen der Cooperation vor, sondern schafft\n sie theilweise erst durch die Zerlegung der handwerksmässigen Thätigkeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"13.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0041","text":"Andrerseits erreicht sie diese gesellschaftliche Organisation des Arbeits-\n prozesses nur durch Festschmieden desselben Arbeiters an dasselbe Detail.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"13.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0042","text":"Da das Theilprodukt jedes Theilarbeiters zugleich nur eine besondre\n Entwicklungsstufe desselben Machwerks ist, liefert jeder Arbeiter oder\n jede Arbeitergruppe der andern ihr Rohmaterial. Das Arbeitsresultat des\n einen bildet den Ausgangspunkt für die Arbeit des andern. Der eine Ar-\n beiter beschäftigt daher hier unmittelbar den andern. Die nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit zur Erreichung des bezweckten Nutzeffekts in jedem Theilpro-\n zess wird erfahrungsmässig festgestellt und der Gesammtmechanis-\n mus der Manufaktur beruht auf der Voraussetzung, dass in ge-\n gebner Arbeitszeit ein gegebnes Resultat erzielt wird. Nur\n unter dieser Voraussetzung können die verschiednen, einander ergänzenden\n Arbeitsprozesse ununterbrochen, gleichzeitig und räumlich neben einander\n fortgehn.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"13.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0043","text":"Es ist klar, dass diese unmittelbare Abhängigkeit\n der Arbeiten und daher der Arbeiter von einander jeden Einzelnen zwingt,\n nur die nothwendige Zeit zu seiner Funktion zu verwenden, und so eine\n ganz andere Kontinuität, Gleichförmigkeit, Regelmässigkeit, Ordnung [Note: „The more variety of artists to every manufacture … the greater the\n order and regularity of every work, the same must needs be done in less time, the\n labour must be less.“ („The Advantages etc.“ p. 68.)] \n und namentlich auch Intensivität der Arbeit erzeugt wird als im\n unabhängigen Handwerk oder selbst der einfachen Cooperation.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"13.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0044","text":"Dass\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"329\", \"facs\": \"#f0348\"} --> auf eine Waare nur die zu ihrer Herstellung gesellschaftlich noth-\n wendige Arbeitszeit verwandt wird, erscheint bei der Waarenpro-\n duktion überhaupt als äusserer Zwang der Konkurrenz, weil, oberflächlich\n ausgedrückt, jeder einzelne Producent die Waare zu ihrem Marktpreis\n verkaufen muss. Lieferung von gegebnem Produktenquantum in gegebner\n Arbeitszeit wird dagegen hier in der Manufaktur technologisches\n Gesetz des Produktionsprozesses selbst [Note: Indess erreicht der manufakturmässige Betrieb diess Resultat in vielen\n Zweigen nur unvollkommen, weil er die allgemeinen chemischen und physischen\n Bedingungen des Produktionsprozesses nicht mit Sicherheit zu kontroliren weiss.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"13.44"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0045","text":"Verschiedne Operationen bedürfen jedoch ungleicher Zeitlängen\n und liefern daher in gleichen Zeiträumen ungleiche Quanta von Theilproduk-\n ten. Soll also derselbe Arbeiter Tag aus Tag ein stets nur dieselbe Operation\n verrichten, so müssen für verschiedne Operationen verschiedne Ver-\n hältnisszahlen von Arbeitern verwandt werden, z. B. 4 Giesser\n und 2 Abbrecher auf einen Frottirer in einer Typenmanufaktur, wo der\n Giesser stündlich 2000 Typen giesst, der Abbrecher 4000 abbricht und\n der Frottirer 8000 blank reibt. Hier kehrt das Prinzip der Cooperation\n in seiner einfachsten Form zurück, gleichzeitige Beschäftigung Vieler, die\nGleichartiges thun, aber jetzt als Ausdruck eines organischen Ver-\n hältnisses.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"13.45"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0046","text":"Die manufakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit vereinfacht und\n vermannigfacht also nicht nur die qualitativ unterschiednen Organe des\n gesellschaftlichen Gesammtarbeiters, sondern schafft auch ein mathematisch\n festes Verhältniss für den quantitativen Umfang dieser Organe, d. h.\n für die relative Arbeiteranzahl oder relative Grösse der Arbeiter-\n gruppen in jeder Sonderfunktion. Sie entwickelt mit der qualitati-\n ven Gliederung die quantitative Regel und Proportio-\n nalität des gesellschaftlichen Arbeitsprozesses.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"13.46"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0047","text":"Ist die passendste Verhältnisszahl der verschiednen Gruppen von\n Theilarbeitern erfahrungsmässig festgesetzt für eine bestimmte Stufenleiter\n der Produktion, so kann man diese Stufenleiter nur ausdehnen, indem man\n ein Multipel jeder besondern Arbeitergruppe verwendet [Note: „Wenn die Erfahrung, je nach der besondren Natur der Produkte jeder\n Manufaktur, sowohl die vortheilhafteste Art die Fabrikation in Theiloperationen\n zu spalten, als auch die für sie nöthige Arbeiterzahl kennen gelehrt hat, werden] . Es\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"330\", \"facs\": \"#f0349\"} --> kommt hinzu, dass dasselbe Individuum gewisse Arbeiten eben so gut auf\n grösserer als kleinerer Staffel ausführt, z. B. die Arbeit der Oberaufsicht,\n den Transport der Theilprodukte aus einer Produktionsphase in die andre\n u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"13.47"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0048","text":"Die Verselbstständigung dieser Funktionen, oder ihre Zuweisung\n an besondere Arbeiter, wird also erst vortheilhaft mit Vergrösserung der\n beschäftigten Arbeiterzahl, aber diese Vergrösserung muss sofort alle\n Gruppen proportionell ergreifen.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"13.48"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0049","text":"Die einzelne Gruppe, als eine Anzahl von Arbeitern, die die-\n selbe Theilfunktion verrichten, besteht aus homogenen Elementen\n und bildet ein einfaches Organ des Gesammtmechanismus. In verschied-\n nen Manufakturen jedoch ist die Gruppe selbst ein gegliederter Ar-\n beitskörper, während der Gesammtmechanismus durch die Wieder-\n holung oder Vervielfältigung dieser produktiven Elementarorganismen ge-\n bildet wird. Nehmen wir z. B. die Manufaktur von Glasflaschen. Sie\n zerfällt in drei wesentlich unterschiedne Phasen. Erstens die vorbe-\n reitende Phase, wie Bereitung der Glaskomposition, Mengung von\n Sand, Kalk u. s. w. und Schmelzung dieser Komposition zu einer flüssigen\n Glasmasse [Note: In England ist der Schmelzofen getrennt vom Glasofen, an dem das Glas\n verarbeitet wird, in Belgien z. B. dient derselbe Ofen zu beiden Prozessen.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"13.49"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0050","text":"In dieser ersten Phase sind verschiedne Theilarbeiter be-\n schäftigt, ebenso in der Schlussphase, der Entfernung der Flaschen\n aus den Trockenöfen, ihrer Sortirung, Verpackung u. s. w. Zwischen\n beiden Phasen steht in der Mitte die eigentliche Glasmacherei oder\n Verarbeitung der flüssigen Glasmasse. An demselben Munde eines Glas-\n ofens arbeitet eine Gruppe, die in England das „hole“ (Loch) heisst, und\n aus einem bottle maker oder finisher, einem blower, einem gatherer, einem\n putter up oder whetter off und einem taker in zusammengesetzt ist. Diese\n fünf Theilarbeiter bilden eben so viele Sonderorgane eines einzigen Arbeits-\n körpers, der nur als Einheit, also nur durch unmittelbare Cooperation der\n Fünf wirken kann. Fehlt ein Glied des fünftheiligen Körpers, so ist er\n paralysirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"13.50"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0051","text":"Derselbe Glasofen hat aber verschiedne Oeffnungen, in Eng-\n [Note: alle Etablissements, die kein exaktes Multiplum dieser Zahl anwenden, mit weniger\n Oekonomie fabriziren . . . . Diess ist eine der Ursachen der kolossalen Ausdeh-\n nung industrieller Etablissements.“ (Ch. Babbage: „On the Economy of\n Machinery.“ 2nd ed. Lond. 1832, ch. XX.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"331\", \"facs\": \"#f0350\"} --> land z. B. 4—6, deren jede einen irdenen Schmelztiegel mit flüssigem\n Glas birgt, und wovon jede eine eigne Arbeitergruppe von derselben fünf-\n gliedrigen Form beschäftigt. Die Gliederung jeder einzelnen Gruppe be-\n ruht hier unmittelbar auf der Theilung der Arbeit, während das Band\n zwischen den verschiednen gleichartigen Gruppen einfache Cooperation\n ist, die eins der Produktionsmittel, hier den Glasofen, durch gemeinsamen\n Konsum ökonomischer verbraucht.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"13.51"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0052","text":"Ein solcher Glasofen mit seinen 4—6\n Gruppen bildet eine Glashütte, und eine Glasmanufaktur umfasst eine\n Mehrzahl solcher Hütten, zugleich mit den Vorrichtungen und Arbeitern\n für die einleitenden und abschliessenden Produktionsphasen.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"13.52"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0053","text":"Endlich kann die Manufaktur, wie sie theilweis aus der Kombination\n verschiedner Handwerke entspringt, sich zu einer Kombination verschiedner\n Manufakturen entwickeln. Die grösseren englischen Glashütten z. B. fabri-\n ciren ihre irdenen Schmelztiegel selbst, weil von deren Güte das Gelingen\n oder Misslingen des Produkts wesentlich abhängt. Die Manufaktur eines\n Produktionsmittels wird hier mit der Manufaktur des Produkts verbunden.\n Umgekehrt kann die Manufaktur des Produkts verbunden werden mit Ma-\n nufakturen, worin es selbst wieder als Rohmaterial dient, oder mit deren\n Produkten es später zusammengesetzt wird. So findet man z. B. die\n Manufaktur von Flintglas kombinirt mit der Glasschleiferei und der Gelb-\n giesserei, letztere für die metallische Einfassung mannigfacher Glasartikel.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"13.53"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0054","text":"Die verschiednen kombinirten Manufakturen bilden dann mehr oder minder\n räumlich getrennte Departemente einer Gesammtmanufaktur, zugleich von\n einander unabhängige Produktionsprozesse, jeder mit eigner Theilung der\n Arbeit. Trotz mancher technischer und ökonomischer Vortheile, welche\n die kombinirte Manufaktur bietet, gewinnt sie, auf eigner Grundlage,\n keine wirklich technologische Einheit. Diese entsteht erst bei ihrer Ver-\n wandlung in den maschinenmässigen Betrieb.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"13.54"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0055","text":"Die Manufakturperiode, welche Verminderung der zur Waarenpro-\n duktion nothwendigen Arbeitszeit bald als bewusstes Prinzip aus-\n spricht [Note: Man kann diess unter andern ersehn aus den Schriften von W. Petty, John\n Bellers, Andrew Yarranton, „The Advantages of the East India Trade“\n und J. Vanderlint.] , entwickelt sporadisch auch den Gebrauch von Maschinen,\n namentlich für gewisse einfache erste Prozesse, die massenhaft\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"332\", \"facs\": \"#f0351\"} --> und mit grossem Kraftaufwand auszuführen sind. So wird z. B. bald in\n der Papiermanufaktur das Zermalmen der Lumpen durch Papier-\n mühlen und in der Metallfabrik das Zerstossen der Erze durch sogenannte\nPochmühlen verrichtet [Note: Noch gegen Ende des 16. Jahrhunderts bedient sich Frankreich der\n Mörser und Siebe zum Pochen und Waschen der Erze.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"13.55"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0056","text":"Die elementarische Form aller Maschinerie\n hatte das römische Kaiserreich überliefert in der Wassermühle [Note: Die ganze Entwicklungsgeschichte der Maschinerie lässt sich verfolgen an\n der Geschichte der Getreidemühlen. Die Fabrik heisst im Englischen immer\n noch mill. In deutschen technologischen Schriften aus den ersten Decennien\n des 19. Jahrhunderts findet man noch den Ausdruck Mühle nicht nur für alle mit\n Naturkräften getriebne Maschinerie, sondern selbst für alle Manufakturen, die\n maschinenartige Apparate anwenden.] . Die\n Handwerksperiode vermachte die grossen Erfindungen des Kompasses,\n des Pulvers, der Buchdruckerei und der automatischen Uhr. Im\n Grossen und Ganzen jedoch spielt die Maschinerie jene Nebenrolle, die\nAdam Smith ihr neben der Theilung der Arbeit anweist [Note: Wie man aus dem Vierten Buch dieser Schrift näher sehn wird, hat\n A.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"13.56"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0057","text":"Smith keinen einzigen neuen Satz über die Theilung der Arbeit aufgestellt.\n Was ihn aber als den zusammenfassenden politischen Oekonomen der Manu-\n fakturperiode charakterisirt, ist der Accent, den er auf die Theilung der\n Arbeit legt. Die untergeordnete Rolle, die er der Maschinerie anweist, rief im\n Beginn der grossen Industrie Lauderdale’s, in einer weiter entwickelten\n Epoche Ure’s Polemik hervor. A. Smith verwechselt auch die Differenzirung\n der Instrumente, wobei die Theilarbeiter der Manufaktur selbst sehr thätig\n waren, mit der Maschinenerfindung. Es sind nicht Manufakturarbeiter,\n sondern Gelehrte, Handwerker, selbst Bauern (Brindley) u. s. w., die hier eine\n Rolle spielen.] .\n Sehr wichtig wurde die sporadische Anwendung der Maschinerie im\n 17.","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"13.57"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0058","text":"Jahrhundert, weil sie den grossen Mathematikern jener Zeit prak-\n tische Anhaltspunkte und Reizmittel zur Schöpfung der modernen Mechanik\n darbot.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"13.58"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0059","text":"Die spezifische Maschinerie der Manufakturperiode\n bleibt der aus vielen Theilarbeitern kombinirte Gesammtarbeiter\n selbst. Die verschiednen Operationen, die der Producent einer Waare ab-\n wechselnd verrichtet und die sich im Ganzen seines Arbeitsprozesses ver-\n schlingen, nehmen ihn verschiedenartig in Anspruch. In der einen muss\n er mehr Kraft entwickeln, in der andern mehr Gewandtheit, in der dritten\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"333\", \"facs\": \"#f0352\"} --> mehr geistige Aufmerksamkeit u. s. w., und dasselbe Individuum besitzt\n diese Eigenschaften nicht in gleichem Grad. Nach der Trennung, Ver-\n selbstständigung und Isolirung der verschiednen Operationen werden die\n Arbeiter ihren vorwiegenden Eigenschaften gemäss getheilt, klassificirt und\n gruppirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"13.59"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0060","text":"Bilden ihre Naturbesonderheiten die Grundlage, worauf sich\n die Theilung der Arbeit pfropft, so entwickelt die Manufaktur, einmal ein-\n geführt, Arbeitskräfte, die von Natur nur zu einseitiger Sonderfunktion\n taugen. Der Gesammtarbeiter besitzt jetzt alle produktiven Eigen-\n schaften in gleich hohem Grad der Virtuosität und verausgabt sie zugleich\n auf’s ökonomischste, indem er alle seine Organe, individualisirt in besondern\n Arbeitern oder Arbeitergruppen, ausschliesslich zu ihren spezifischen Funk-\n tionen verwendet [Note: „Indem man das Machwerk in mehrere verschiedne Operationen theilt,\n deren jede verschiedne Grade von Gewandtheit und Kraft erheischt, kann der\n Manufakturherr sich genau das jeder Operation entsprechende Quantum von Kraft\n und Gewandtheit verschaffen.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"13.60"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0061","text":"Wäre dagegen das ganze Werk von einem Arbeiter\n zu verrichten, so müsste dasselbe Individuum genug Gewandtheit für die delika-\n testen und genug Kraft für die mühseligsten Operationen besitzen.“ (Ch. Bab-\n bage l. c. ch. XIX.)] . Die Einseitigkeit und selbst die Unvollkommenheit\n des Theilarbeiters werden zu seiner Vollkommenheit als Glied des Gesammt-\n arbeiters [Note: Z. B. einseitige Muskelentwicklung, Knochenverkrümmung u. s. w.] . Die Gewohnheit einer einseitigen Funktion verwandelt ihn\n in ihr naturgemäss sicher wirkendes Organ, während der Zusammenhang\n des Gesammtmechanismus ihn zwingt, mit der Regelmässigkeit eines Ma-\n schinentheils zu wirken [Note: Sehr richtig antwortet Herr Wm.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"13.61"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0062","text":"Marschall, der general manager einer\n Glasmanufaktur, auf die Frage des Untersuchungskommissärs, wie die Arbeitsam-\n keit unter den beschäftigten Jungen aufrecht erhalten werde: „They cannot well\n neglect their work; when they once begin, they must go on; they are just\n the same as parts of a machine.“ („Child. Empl. Comm. Fourth\n Report“ 1865, p. 247.)] . Da die verschiednen Funktionen des Ge-\n sammtarbeiters einfacher oder zusammengesetzter, niedriger oder höher,\n erheischen seine Organe, die individuellen Arbeitskräfte, sehr verschiedne\n Grade der Ausbildung und besitzen daher sehr verschiedne Werthe. Die\n Manufaktur entwickelt also eine Hierarchie der Arbeitskräfte,\n der eine Stufenleiter der Arbeitslöhne entspricht.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"13.62"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0063","text":"Wird einerseits der in-\n dividuelle Arbeiter einer einseitigen Funktion angeeignet und lebens-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"334\", \"facs\": \"#f0353\"} --> lang annexirt, so werden eben so sehr die verschiednen Arbeitsver-\n richtungen jener Hierarchie der natürlichen und erworbnen Geschicklich-\n keiten angepasst [Note: Dr. Ure in seiner Apotheose der grossen Industrie fühlt die eigen-\n thümlichen Charaktere der Manufaktur schärfer heraus als frühere Oekonomen,\n die nicht sein polemisches Interesse hatten, und selbst als seine Zeitgenossen, z. B.\nBabbage, der ihm zwar durchaus überlegen ist als gelehrter Mathematiker und\n Mechaniker, aber dennoch die grosse Industrie eigentlich nur vom Standpunkt der\n Manufaktur auffasst.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"13.63"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0064","text":"Ure bemerkt: „Die Aneignung der Arbeiter an\n jede Sonderoperation bildet das Wesen der Vertheilung der Arbeiten.“ Andrer-\n seits bezeichnet er diese Vertheilung als „Anpassung der Arbeiten an die\n verschiednen individuellen Fähigkeiten“ und charakterisirt endlich das ganze\n Manufaktursystem als „ein System von Gradationen nach dem Rang der Geschick-\n lichkeit“, als „eine Theilung der Arbeit nach den verschiednen Graden des Ge-\n schicks“ u. s. w. Ure l. c. t. I, p. 28—35 passim.] . Jeder Produktionsprozess bedingt indess ge-\n wisse einfache Hanthierungen, deren jeder Mensch, wie er geht und steht,\n fähig ist. Auch sie werden jetzt von ihrem flüssigen Zusammenhang mit\n den inhaltvolleren Momenten der Thätigkeit losgelöst und zu ausschliess-\n lichen Funktionen verknöchert.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"13.64"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0065","text":"Die Manufaktur erzeugt daher in jedem\n Handwerk, das sie ergreift, eine Klasse sogenannter ungeschickter\n Arbeiter, die der Handwerksbetrieb streng ausschloss. Wenn sie die\n durchaus vereinseitigte Spezialität auf Kosten des ganzen Arbeitsvermögens\n zur Virtuosität entwickelt, beginnt sie auch schon den Mangel aller Ent-\n wicklung zu einer Spezialität zu machen. Neben die hierarchische Ab-\n stufung tritt die einfache Scheidung der Arbeiter in geschickte und\nungeschickte. Für letztere fallen die Erlernungskosten ganz weg,\n für erstere sinken sie, im Vergleich zum Handwerker, in Folge vereinfachter\n Funktion. In beiden Fällen sinkt der Werth der Arbeitskraft [Note: „Un ouvrier, en se perfectionnant par la pratique sur un seul et même\n point, devient … moins coûteux.“ (l. c. p. 28.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"13.65"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0066","text":"Aus-\n nahme findet statt, soweit die Zersetzung des Arbeitsprozesses neue zu-\n sammenfassende Funktionen erzeugt, die im Handwerksbetrieb gar nicht\n oder nicht in demselben Umfang vorkamen. Die relative Entwerthung\n der Arbeitskraft, die aus dem Wegfall oder der Verminderung der\n Erlernungskosten entspringt, schliesst unmittelbar höhere Verwer-\n thung des Kapitals ein, denn alles, was die zur Reproduktion der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"335\", \"facs\": \"#f0354\"} --> Arbeitskraft nothwendige Zeit verkürzt, verlängert die Domaine der Mehr-\n arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"13.66"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0067","text":"Wir betrachteten erst den Ursprung der Manufaktur aus der Coope-\n ration, dann ihre einfachen Elemente, den Theilarbeiter und sein Werk-\n zeug, endlich ihren Gesammtmechanismus. Wir berühren jetzt kurz das\n Verhältniss zwischen der manufakturmässigen Theilung der Arbeit und\n der gesellschaftlichen Theilung der Arbeit, welche die allgemeine\n Grundlage aller Waarenproduktion bildet.","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"13.67"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0068","text":"Hält man nur die Arbeit selbst im Auge, so kann man die\n Trennung der gesellschaftlichen Produktion in ihre grossen Gattungen,\n wie Agrikultur, Industrie u. s. w., als Theilung der Arbeit im All-\n gemeinen, die Sonderung dieser Produktionsgattungen in Arten und\n Unterarten als Theilung der Arbeit im Besondern, und die\n Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb einer Werkstatt als Theilung der Ar-\n beit im Einzelnen bezeichnen [Note: „Die Theilung der Arbeit geht von der Trennung der verschiedenartigsten\n Professionen fort bis zu jener Theilung, wo mehrere Arbeiter sich in die Anferti-\n gung eines und desselben Produkts theilen, wie in der Manufaktur.“ (Storch:\n „Cours d’Econ. Pol.“ Pariser Ausgabe, t. I, p. 173.)","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"13.68"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0069","text":"„Nous rencontrons\n chez les peuples parvenus à un certain degré de civilisation trois genres de divi-\n sions d’industrie: la première, que nous nommons générale, amène la dis-\n tinction des producteurs en agriculteurs, manufacturiers et commerçans, elle se\n rapporte aux trois principales branches d’industrie nationale; la seconde, qu’on\n pourrait appeler spéciale, est la division de chaque genre d’industrie en espèces\n . . . . la troisième division d’industrie, celle enfin qu’on devrait qualifier de\ndivision de la besogne ou de travail proprement dit, est celle qui s’établit\n dans les arts et les métiers séparés … qui s’établit dans la plupart des manufac-\n tures et des ateliers.“ (Skarbek l. c. p. 84, 86.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"13.69"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0070","text":"Die Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb der Gesellschaft, und\n die entsprechende Beschränkung der Individuen auf besondre Berufs-\n sphären, entwickelt sich, wie die Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb der Manu-\n faktur, von entgegengesetzten Ausgangspunkten. Innerhalb einer Familie,\n weiter entwickelt eines Stammes, entspringt eine naturwüchsige Theilung\n der Arbeit aus den Geschlechts- und Altersverschiedenheiten, also auf rein\nphysiologischer Grundlage, die mit der Ausdehnung des Ge-\n meinwesens, der Zunahme der Bevölkerung, und namentlich dem Konflikt\n zwischen verschiednen Stämmen und der Unterjochung eines Stamms durch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"336\", \"facs\": \"#f0355\"} --> den andern ihr Material ausweitet.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"13.70"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0071","text":"Andrerseits, wie ich früher bemerkt,\n entspringt der Produktenaustausch an den Punkten, wo verschiedne Fa-\n milien, Stämme, Gemeinwesen in Kontakt kommen, denn nicht Privatper-\n sonen, sondern Familien, Stämme u. s. w. treten sich in den Anfängen der\n Kultur selbstständig gegenüber. Verschiedne Gemeinwesen finden ver-\n schiedne Produktionsmittel und verschiedne Lebensmittel in ihrer Natur-\n umgebung vor. Ihre Produktionsweise, Lebensweise und Produkte sind\n daher verschieden. Es ist diese naturwüchsige Verschiedenheit, die bei\n dem Kontakt der Gemeinwesen den Austausch der wechselseitigen Produkte\n und daher die allmälige Verwandlung dieser Produkte in Waaren hervorruft.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"13.71"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0072","text":"Der Austausch schafft nicht den Unterschied der Produktionssphären,\n sondern bezieht die unterschiednen auf einander und verwandelt sie so in\n mehr oder minder von einander abhängige Zweige einer gesellschaft-\n lichen Gesammtproduktion. Hier entsteht die gesellschaftliche Theilung\n der Arbeit durch den Austausch ursprünglich verschiedner, aber\n gegen einander selbstständiger Produktionssphären. Dort, wo die phy-\n siologische Theilung der Arbeit den Ausgangspunkt bildet, lösen sich\n die besondern Organe eines unmittelbar zusammengehörigen Ganzen von\n einander ab, zersetzen sich, zu welchem Zersetzungsprozess der Waaren-\n austausch mit fremden Gemeinwesen den Hauptanstoss giebt, und verselbst-\n ständigen sich bis zu dem Punkt, wo der Zusammenhang der verschiednen\n Arbeiten durch den Austausch der Produkte als Waaren vermittelt wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"13.72"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0073","text":"Es ist in dem einen Fall Verunselbstständigung der früher Selbstständigen,\n in dem andern Verselbstständigung der früher Unselbstständigen.","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"13.73"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0074","text":"Die Grundlage aller entwickelten und durch Waarenaustausch ver-\n mittelten Theilung der Arbeit ist die Scheidung von Stadt und\n Land [Note: Sir James Steuart hat diesen Punktam besten behandelt. Wie wenig\n sein Werk, welches zehn Jahre vor dem „Wealth of Nations“ erschien, heut zu\n Tage bekannt ist, sieht man u. a. daraus, dass die Bewunderer des Malthus nicht\n einmal wissen, dass dieser in der ersten Ausgabe seiner Schrift über die „Popu-\n lation“, vom rein deklamatorischen Theil abgesehn, neben den Pfaffen Wallace\n und Townsend fast nur den Steuart abschreibt.] . Man kann sagen, dass die ganze ökonomische Geschichte der\n Gesellschaft sich in der Bewegung dieses Gegensatzes resümirt, auf den\n wir jedoch hier nicht weiter eingehn.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"13.74"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0075","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"337\", \"facs\": \"#f0356\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"13.75"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0076","text":"Wie für die Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb der Manufaktur eine ge-\n wisse Anzahl gleichzeitig angewandter Arbeiter die materielle Voraus-\n setzung bildet, so für die Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb der Gesellschaft\ndie Grösse der Bevölkerung und ihre Dichtigkeit, die hier\n an die Stelle der Agglomeration in derselben Werkstatt tritt [Note: „There is a certain density of population which is convenient, both for\n social intercourse, and for that combination of powers by which the produce of\n labour is increased.“ (James Mill l. c. p. 50.) „As the number of labourers\n increases, the productive power of society augments in the compound ratio of that\n increase, multiplied by the effects of the division of labour.“ (Th. Hodgskin\n l. c. p. 125, 126.)] . Indess\n ist diese Dichtigkeit etwas relatives.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"13.76"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0077","text":"Ein relativ spärlich bevölkertes\n Land mit entwickelten Kommunikationsmitteln besitzt eine dichtere Be-\n völkerung als ein mehr bevölkertes Land mit unentwickelten Kommuni-\n kationsmitteln, und in dieser Art sind z. B. die nördlichen Staaten der\n amerikanischen Union dichter bevölkert als Indien [Note: In Folge der grossen Baumwollnachfrage seit 1861 wurde in einigen,\n sonst zahlreich bevölkerten Distrikten Ostindiens die Baumwollproduktion auf\n Kosten der Reisproduktion ausgedehnt. Es entstand daher partielle Hungersnoth,\n weil wegen mangelnder Kommunikationsmittel und daher mangelnden physischen\n Zusammenhangs der Reisausfall in einem Distrikt nicht durch Zufuhr aus andern\n Distrikten ausgeglichen werden konnte.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"13.77"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0078","text":"Da Waarenproduktion und Waarencirkulation die allgemeine Vor-\n aussetzung der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise, erheischt manufaktur-\n mässige Theilung der Arbeit eine schon bis zu gewissem Entwicklungs-\n grad gereifte Theilung der Arbeit im Innern der Gesellschaft. Umgekehrt\n entwickelt und vervielfältigt die manufakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit\n rückwirkend jene gesellschaftliche Theilung der Arbeit. Mit der Diffe-\n renzirung der Arbeitsinstrumente differenziren sich mehr und mehr die Ge-\n werbe, welche diese Instrumente produciren [Note: So bildete die Fabrikation der Weberschiffchen schon während des 17.\n Jahrhunderts einen besondern Industriezweig in Holland.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"13.78"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0079","text":"Ergreift der manufaktur-\n mässige Betrieb ein Gewerb, das bisher als Haupt- oder Nebengewerb mit\n andern zusammenhing und von demselben Producenten ausgeführt wurde,\n so findet sofort Scheidung und gegenseitige Verselbstständigung statt. Er-\n greift er eine besondre Produktionsstufe einer Waare, so verwandeln\n sich ihre verschiednen Produktionsstufen in verschiedne unabhängige Ge-\n [Running matter: I. 22] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"338\", \"facs\": \"#f0357\"} --> werbe. Es ward bereits angedeutet, dass wo das Machwerk ein bloss\n mechanisch zusammengesetztes Ganze von Theilprodukten, die Theilarbei-\n ten sich selbst wieder zu eignen Handwerken verselbstständigen können.","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"13.79"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0080","text":"Um die Theilung der Arbeit vollkommner innerhalb einer Manufaktur aus-\n zuführen, wird derselbe Produktionszweig, je nach der Verschiedenheit\n seiner Rohstoffe oder den verschiednen Formen, die derselbe Rohstoff er-\n halten kann, in verschiedne zum Theil ganz neue Manufakturen gespaltet.\n So wurden bereits in der ersten Hälfte des 18. Jahrhunderts in Frankreich\n allein über 100 verschiedenartige Seidenzeuge gewebt, und in Avignon\n z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"13.80"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0081","text":"war es Gesetz, dass „jeder Lehrling sich immer nur einer Fabrika-\n tionsart widmen und nicht die Verfertigung mehrerer Zeugarten zugleich\n lernen durfte.“ Die territoriale Theilung der Arbeit, welche\n besondre Produktionszweige an besondre Distrikte eines Landes bannt,\n erhält neuen Anstoss durch den manufakturmässigen Betrieb, der alle Be-\n sonderheiten ausbeutet [Note: „Whether the Woollen Manufacture of England is not divided into\n several parts or branches appropriated to particular places, where they are only or\n principally manufactured; fine clothes in Somersetshire, coarse in Yorkshire, long\n ells at Exeter, soies at Sandbury, crapes at Norwich, linseys at Kendal, blankets\n at Whitney, and so forth!“ (Berkeley: „The Querist“ 1750, p. 520.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"13.81"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0082","text":"Reiches Material zur Theilung der Arbeit\n innerhalb der Gesellschaft liefert der Manufakturperiode die Erweiterung\n des Weltmarkts und das Kolonialsystem, die zum Umkreis ihrer allgemei-\n nen Existenzbedingungen gehören. Es ist hier nicht der Ort weiter nach-\n zuweisen, wie sie neben der ökonomischen jede andre Sphäre der Gesell-\n schaft inficirt und überall die Grundlage zu jener Ausbildung des Fach-\n wesens, der Specialitäten, und zu einer Parcellirung des Menschen legt, die\n schon A. Ferguson, den Lehrer A. Smiths, in den Ausruf ausbrechen\n liess: „Wir sind ganze Nationen von Heloten, und es giebt keine Freien\n unter uns“ [Note: A. Ferguson: „History of Civil Society“, Part IV, ch. II.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"13.82"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0083","text":"Trotz der zahlreichen Analogien jedoch und der Zusammenhänge\n zwischen der Theilung der Arbeit im Innern der Gesellschaft und der\n Theilung innerhalb einer Werkstatt, sind beide nicht nur graduell, son-\n dern wesentlich unterschieden. Am schlagendsten scheint die Ana-\n logie unstreitig, wo ein inneres Band verschiedne Geschäftszweige ver-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"339\", \"facs\": \"#f0358\"} --> schlingt. Der Viehzüchter z. B. producirt Häute, der Gerber verwandelt\n die Häute in Leder, der Schuster das Leder in Stiefel. Jeder producirt\n hier ein Stufenprodukt und die letzte fertige Gestalt ist das kombinirte\n Produkt ihrer Sonderarbeiten. Es kommen hinzu die mannigfachen Ar-\n beitszweige, die dem Viehzüchter, Gerber, Schuster Produktionsmittel\n liefern. Man kann sich nun mit A.","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"13.83"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0084","text":"Smith einbilden, diese gesellschaft-\n liche Theilung der Arbeit unterscheide sich von der manufakturmässigen\nnur subjektiv, nämlich für den Beobachter, der hier die mannigfachen\n Theilarbeiten auf einen Blick räumlich zusammensieht, während dort ihre\n Zerstreuung über grosse Flächen und die grosse Zahl der in jedem Son-\n derzweig Beschäftigten den Zusammenhang verdunklen [Note: In den eigentlichen Manufakturen, sagt er, scheint die Theilung der\n Arbeit grösser, weil „those employed in every different branch of the work can\n often be collected into the same workhouse, and placed at once under the view of\n the spectator.","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"13.84"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0085","text":"In those great manufactures (!), on the contrary, which are\n destined to supply the great wants of the great body of the people, every different\n branch of the work employs so great a number of workmen, that it is impossible\n to collect them all into the same workhouse … the division is not near so ob-\n vious.“ (A. Smith: „Wealth of Nations“, b. I, ch. I.) Der berühmte\n Passus in demselben Kapitel, der mit den Worten beginnt: „Observe the acco-\n modation of the most common artificer or day labourer in a civilized and thriving\n country etc.“ und dann weiter ausmalt, wie zahllos mannigfaltige Gewerbe zur\n Befriedigung der Bedürfnisse eines gewöhnlichen Arbeiters zusammenwirken, ist\n ziemlich wörtlich kopirt aus B. deMandeville’s Remarks zu seiner: „The\n Fable of the Bees, or Private Vices, Publick Benefits.“ (Erste\n Ausgabe ohne Remarks 1706, mit den Remarks 1714.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"13.85"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0086","text":"Was aber\n stellt den Zusammenhang her zwischen den unabhängigen Arbeiten von\n Viehzüchter, Gerber, Schuster? Das Dasein ihrer respektiven Produkte als\nWaaren. Was charakterisirt dagegen die manufakturmässige Theilung\n der Arbeit? Dass der Theilarbeiter keine Waare producirt [Note: „There is no longer anything which we can call the natural reward of\n individual labonr. Each labourer produces only some part of a whole, and each\n part, having no value or utility of itself, there is nothing on which the labourer\n can seize, and say: it is my product, this I will keep for myself.“ („Labour\n defended against the claims of Capital. Lond. 1825“, p. 25.) Der\n Verfasser dieser vorzüglichen Schrift ist der früher citirte Th. Hodgskin.] . Erst\n das gemeinsame Produkt der Theilarbeiter verwandelt sich in Waare.","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"13.86"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0087","text":"[Running matter: 22*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"340\", \"facs\": \"#f0359\"} --> Die Theilung der Arbeit im Innern der Gesellschaft ist vermittelt durch den\n Kauf und Verkauf der Produkte verschiedner Arbeitszweige, der Zusammen-\n hang der Theilarbeiten in der Manufaktur durch den Verkauf verschiedner Ar-\n beitskräfte an denselben Kapitalisten, der sie als kombinirte Arbeitskraft ver-\n wendet. Die manufakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit unterstellt Koncen-\n tration der Produktionsmittel in der Hand eines Kapitalisten, die gesell-\n schaftliche Theilung der Arbeit Zersplitterung der Produktionsmittel unter\n viele von einander unabhängige Waarenproducenten.","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"13.87"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0088","text":"Statt dass in der Manu-\n faktur das eherne Gesetz der Verhältnisszahl oder Proportionalität bestimmte\n Arbeitermassen unter bestimmte Funktionen subsumirt, treiben Zufall und\n Willkühr ihr buntes Spiel in der Vertheilung der Waarenproducenten und\n ihrer Produktionsmittel unter die verschiednen gesellschaftlichen Arbeits-\n zweige.","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"13.88"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0089","text":"Zwar suchen sich die verschiednen Produktionssphären bestän-\n dig ins Gleichgewicht zu setzen, indem einerseits jeder Waarenproducent\n einen Gebrauchswerth produciren, also ein besondres gesellschaftliches Be-\n dürfniss befriedigen muss, der Umfang dieser Bedürfnisse aber quantitativ\n verschieden ist und ein innres Band die verschiedenen Bedürfnissmassen zu\n einem naturwüchsigen System verkettet; indem andrerseits das Werth-\n gesetz der Waaren bestimmt, wie viel die Gesellschaft von ihrer ganzen\n disponiblen Arbeitszeit auf die Produktion jeder besondern Waarenart ver-\n ausgaben kann. Aber diese beständige Tendenz der verschiednen Pro-\n duktionssphären sich ins Gleichgewicht zu setzen, bethätigt sich nur als\n Reaktion gegen die beständige Aufhebung dieses Gleichgewichts.","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"13.89"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0090","text":"Bei\n der Theilung der Arbeit im Innern der Werkstatt beherrscht die Ver-\n hältnisszahl die jeder Sonderfunktion zugewiesne Arbeitermasse a priori\n als bewusste und planmässig befolgte Regel; bei der Theilung der Arbeit\n im Innern der Gesellschaft wirkt sie nur a posteriori als innere,\n stumme, im Barometerwechsel der Marktpreise wahrnehmbare, die regel-\n lose Willkühr der Waarenproducenten überwältigende Naturnothwendig-\n keit.","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"13.90"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0091","text":"Die manufakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit unterstellt die unbe-\n dingte Autorität des Kapitalisten über Menschen, die blosse Glieder eines\n ihm gehörigen Gesammtmechanismus bilden; die gesellschaftliche Thei-\n lung der Arbeit stellt unabhängige Waarenproducenten einander gegen-\n über, die keine andere Autorität anerkennen als die der Konkurrenz,\n den Zwang, den der Druck ihrer wechselseitigen Interessen auf sie\n ausübt, wie auch im Thierreich das bellum omnium contra omnes die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"341\", \"facs\": \"#f0360\"} --> Existenzbedingungen aller Arten mehr oder minder erhält.","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"13.91"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0092","text":"Dasselbe bür-\n gerliche Bewusstsein, das die manufakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit,\n die lebenslängliche Annexation des Arbeiters an eine Detailverrichtung und\n die unbedingte Unterordnung der Theilarbeiter unter das Kapital, als eine\n Organisation der Arbeit feiert, welche ihre Produktivkraft steigere, denun-\n cirt daher eben so laut jede bewusste gesellschaftliche Kontrole und Reg-\n lung des gesellschaftlichen Produktionsprozesses als einen Eingriff in die\n unverletzlichen Eigenthumsrechte, Freiheit und sich selbst bestimmende\n „Genialität“ des individuellen Kapitalisten. Es ist sehr charakteristisch,\n dass die begeisterten Apologeten des Fabriksystems nichts ärgeres gegen\n jede allgemeine Organisation der gesellschaftlichen Arbeit zu sagen wis-\n sen, als dass sie die ganze Gesellschaft in eine Fabrik verwandeln würde.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"13.92"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0093","text":"Wenn die Anarchie der gesellschaftlichen und die Despotie der\nmanufakturmässigen Arbeitstheilung einander in der Gesellschaft\n der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise bedingen, bieten dagegen frühere\n Gesellschaftsformen, worin die Besonderung der Gewerbe sich naturwüchsig\n entwickelt, dann krystallisirt und endlich gesetzlich befestigt, einer-\n seits das Bild einer plan- und autoritätsmässigen Organisation der\n gesellschaftlichen Arbeit, während sie anderseits die Theilung der Arbeit\n innerhalb der Werkstatt ganz ausschliessen, oder nur auf einem Zwerg-\n massstab, oder nur sporadisch und zufällig entwickeln [Note: „On peut … établir en règle générale, que moins l’autorité préside à\n la division du travail dans l’intérieur de la société, plus la division du travail se\n développe dans l’intérieur de l’atélier, et plus elle y est soumise à l’autorité d’un\n seul.","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"13.93"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0094","text":"Ainsi l’autorité dans l’atélier et celle dans la société, par rapport à la\n division du travail, sont en raison inverse l’une de l’autre.“ (Karl Marx\n l. c. p. 130, 131.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"13.94"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0095","text":"Jene uralterthümlichen, kleinen indischen Gemeinwesen z. B., die\n zum Theil noch fortexistiren, beruhn auf gemeinschaftlichem Besitz des\n Grund und Bodens, auf unmittelbarer Verbindung von Agrikultur und\n Handwerk, und auf einer festen Theilung der Arbeit, die bei Anlage neuer\n Gemeinwesen als gegebner Plan und Grundriss dient. Sie bilden sich\n selbst genügende Produktionsganze, deren Produktionsgebiet von 100 bis\n auf einige 1000 Acres wechselt. Die Hauptmasse der Produkte wird für\n den unmittelbaren Selbstbedarf der Gemeinde producirt, nicht als Waare,\n und die Produktion selbst ist daher unabhängig von der durch Waaren-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"342\", \"facs\": \"#f0361\"} --> austausch vermittelten Theilung der Arbeit im Grossen und Ganzen der\n indischen Gesellschaft.","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"13.95"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0096","text":"Nur der Ueberschuss der Produkte verwandelt\n sich in Waare, zum Theil selbst wieder erst in der Hand des Staats,\n dem ein bestimmtes Quantum seit undenklichen Zeiten als Naturalrente zu-\n fliesst. In verschiedenen Theilen Indiens besitzt diess Gemeinwesen ver-\n schiedne herrschende Formen. In der einfachsten Form bebaut die Ge-\n meinde das Land gemeinschaftlich und vertheilt seine Produkte unter ihre\n Glieder, während jede Familie Spinnen, Weben u. s. w. als häusliches\n Nebengewerb treibt.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"13.96"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0097","text":"Neben dieser gleichartig beschäftigten Masse finden\n wir den „Haupteinwohner“, Richter, Polizei und Steuereinnehmer in\n einer Person; den Buchhalter, der die Rechnung über den Ackerbau führt\n und alles darauf Bezügliche katastrirt und registrirt; einen dritten Beam-\n ten, der Verbrecher verfolgt und fremde Reisende beschützt und von einem\n Dorf zum andern geleitet; den Grenzmann, der die Grenzen der Ge-\n meinde gegen die Nachbargemeinden bewacht; den Wasseraufseher,\n der das Wasser aus den gemeinschaftlichen Wasserbehältern zu Ackerbau-\n zwecken vertheilt; den Braminen, der die Funktionen des religiösen\n Kultus verrichtet; den Schulmeister, der die Gemeindekinder im Sand\n schreiben und lesen lehrt; den Kalenderbraminen, der als Astrolog\n die Zeiten für Saat, Ernte und die guten oder bösen Stunden für alle be-\n sondern Ackerbauarbeiten angiebt; einen Schmidt und einen Zimmer-\n mann,","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"13.97"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0098","text":"welche alle Ackerbauwerkzeuge verfertigen und ausbessern; den\nTöpfer, der alle Gefässe für das Dorf macht; den Barbier, den\nWäscher für die Reinigung der Kleider, den Silberschmidt, hier\n und da den Poeten, der in einigen Gemeinden den Silberschmidt, in\n andern den Schulmeister ersetzt. Diess Dutzend Personen wird auf\n Kosten der ganzen Gemeinde erhalten. Wächst die Bevölkerung, so\n wird eine neue Gemeinde nach dem Muster der alten auf unbebautem Bo-\n den angesiedelt. Den Gesammtmechanismus der Gemeinde betrachtet,\n findet sich hier planmässige Theilung der Arbeit, aber ihre manufaktur-\n mässige Theilung ist unmöglich, indem der Markt für Schmidt, Zimmer-\n mann u. s. w. unverändert bleibt und höchstens, je nach dem Grössen-\n unterschied der Dörfer, statt eines Schmidts, Töpfers u. s. w. ihrer zwei\n oder drei vorkommen [Note: Lieut. Col.","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"13.98"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0099","text":"Mark Wilks: „Historical Sketches of the\n South of India. Lond. 1810—17“, v. I, p. 118—20. Eine gute Zu-] . Das Gesetz, das die Theilung der Gemeinde-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"343\", \"facs\": \"#f0362\"} --> arbeit regelt, wirkt hier mit der unverbrüchlichen Autorität eines Natur-\n gesetzes, während jeder besondre Handwerker, wie Schmidt u. s. w., nach\n überlieferter Art, aber selbstständig, und ohne Anerkennung irgend einer\n Autorität in seiner Werkstatt, alle zu seinem Fach gehörigen Operationen\n verrichtet. Der einfache produktive Organismus dieser selbstgenügenden\n Gemeinwesen, die sich beständig in derselben Form reproduciren, und,\n wenn zufällig zerstört, an demselben Ort, mit demselben Namen, wieder\n aufbauen [Note: „Under this simple form … the inhabitants of the country have lived\n since time immemorial.","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"13.99"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0100","text":"The boundaries of the villages have been but seldom\n altered; and though the villages themselves have been sometimes injured, and\n even desolated by war, famine, and disease, the same name, the same limits, the\n same interests, and even the same families, have continued for ages. The inhabi-\n tants give themselves no trouble about the breaking up and division of kingdoms;\n while the village remains entire, they care not to what power it is transferred or\n to what sovereign it devolves; its internal economy remains unchanged.“ (Th.\n Stafford Raffles, late Lieut. Gov. of Java: „The History of Java.\n Lond. 1817“, v. II, p. 285, 286.)] , liefert den Schlüssel zum Geheimniss der Unveränder-\n lichkeit asiatischer Gesellschaften, so auffallend kontrastirt durch\n die beständige Auflösung und Neubildung asiatischer Staaten und rast-\n losen Dynastenwechsel.","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"13.100"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0101","text":"Die Struktur der ökonomischen Grundelemente\n der Gesellschaft bleibt von den Stürmen der politischen Wolkenregion un-\n berührt.","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"13.101"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0102","text":"Die Zunftgesetze, wie schon früher bemerkt, verhinderten planmässig,\n durch äusserste Beschränkung des Maximums der Gesellenzahl, die ein\n einzelner Zunftmeister beschäftigen durfte, seine Verwandlung in einen Ka-\n pitalisten. Ebenso konnte er Gesellen nur beschäftigen in dem ausschliess-\n lichen Handwerk, worin er selbst Meister war. Die Zunft wehrte eifer-\n süchtig jeden Uebergriff des Kaufmannskapitals ab, der einzig freien\n Form des Kapitals, die ihr gegenüberstand. Der Kaufmann konnte alle\n Waaren kaufen, nur nicht die Arbeit als Waare. Er war nur geduldet\n als Verleger der Handwerksprodukte.","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"13.102"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0103","text":"Riefen äussere Umstände eine\n fortschreitende Theilung der Arbeit hervor, so zerspalteten sich bestehende\n Zünfte in Unterarten oder lagerten sich neue Zünfte neben die alten hin,\n [Note: sammenstellung der verschiednen Formen des indischen Gemeinwesens findet man\n in George Campbell’s: „Modern India. London 1852.“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"344\", \"facs\": \"#f0363\"} --> jedoch ohne Zusammenfassung verschiedner Handwerke in eine Werkstatt.\n Die Zunftorganisation, so sehr ihre Besonderung, Isolirung und Ausbildung\n der Gewerbe zu den materiellen Existenzbedingungen der Manufaktur-\n periode gehören, schloss daher die manufakturmässige Theilung der Ar-\n beit aus.","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"13.103"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0104","text":"Im Grossen und Ganzen blieben der Arbeiter und seine Pro-\n duktionsmittel mit einander verbunden, wie die Schnecke mit dem\n Schneckenhaus, und so fehlte die erste Grundlage der Manufaktur, die Ver-\n selbstständigung der Produktionsmittel als Kapital gegenüber dem\n Arbeiter.","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"13.104"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0105","text":"Während die Theilung der Arbeit im Ganzen einer Gesellschaft, ob\n vermittelt oder unvermittelt durch den Waarenaustausch, den verschieden-\n artigsten ökonomischen Gesellschaftsformationen angehört, ist die manu-\n fakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit eine ganz spezifische Schöpfung\n der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise.","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"13.105"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0106","text":"Eine grössere Arbeiteranzahl unter dem Kommando desselben\n Kapitals bildet den naturwüchsigen Ausgangspunkt, wie der Coopera-\n tion überhaupt, so der Manufaktur. Umgekehrt entwickelt die manufak-\n turmässige Theilung der Arbeit das Wachsthum der angewandten Arbeiter-\n zahl zur technologischen Nothwendigkeit. Das Arbeiterminimum,\n das ein einzelner Kapitalist anwenden muss, ist ihm jetzt durch die vor-\n handene Theilung der Arbeit vorgeschrieben. Andrerseits sind die Vor-\n theile weiterer Theilung bedingt durch weitere Vermehrung der Arbeiter-\n anzahl, die nur noch in Multiplen ausführbar. Mit dem variablen\n muss aber auch der constante Bestandtheil des Kapitals wachsen, neben\n dem Umfang der gemeinsamen Produktionsbedingungen, wie Baulichkeiten,\n Oefen u. s. w., namentlich auch und viel rascher als die Arbeiteranzahl,\n das Rohmaterial.","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"13.106"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0107","text":"Seine Masse, verzehrt in gegebner Zeit durch ge-\n gebnes Arbeitsquantum, nimmt in demselben Verhältniss zu wie die Pro-\n duktivkraft der Arbeit in Folge ihrer Theilung. Wachsender Mini-\n malumfang von Kapital in der Hand der einzelnen Kapitalisten,\n oder wachsende Verwandlung der gesellschaftlichen\n Lebensmittel und Produktionsmittel in Kapital ist also\n ein aus dem technologischen Charakter der Manufaktur entspringendes\n Gesetz [Note: „Es genügt nicht, dass das zur Unterabtheilung der Handwerke nöthige\nKapital (sollte heissen, die dazu nöthigen Lebens- und Produktionsmittel) sich] .","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"13.107"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0108","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"345\", \"facs\": \"#f0364\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"13.108"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0109","text":"Wie in der einfachen Cooperation ist in der Manufaktur der\n funktionirende Arbeitskörper eine Existenzform des Kapitals.\n Der aus vielen individuellen Theilarbeitern zusammengesetzte gesellschaft-\n liche Produktionsmechanismus gehört dem Kapitalisten. Die aus der\n Kombination der Arbeiten entspringende Produktivkraft erscheint daher\n als Produktivkraft des Kapitals. Die eigentliche Manufaktur un-\n terwirft nicht nur den früher selbstständigen Arbeiter dem Kommando und\n der Disciplin des Kapitals, sondern schafft überdem eine hierarchische\n Gliederung unter den Arbeitern selbst. Während die einfache Coopera-\n tion die Arbeitsweise der Einzelnen im Grossen und Ganzen unverändert\n lässt, revolutionirt die Manufaktur sie von Grund aus und ergreift die in-\n dividuelle Arbeitskraft an ihrer Wurzel.","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"13.109"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0110","text":"Sie verkrüppelt den Arbeiter in\n eine Abnormität, indem sie sein Detailgeschick treibhausmässig fördert\n durch Unterdrückung einer Welt von produktiven Trieben und Anlagen,\n wie man in den La Plata Staaten ein ganzes Thier abschlachtet, um sein\n Fell oder seinen Talg zu erbeuten. Die besondren Theilarbeiten werden\n nicht nur unter verschiedne Individuen vertheilt, sondern das Individuum\n selbst wird getheilt, in das automatische Triebwerk einer Theilarbeit ver-\n wandelt [Note: Dugald Stewart nennt die Manufakturarbeiter „living automatons …\n employed in the details of the work.“ (l. c. p. 318.)]  und die abgeschmackte Fabel des Menenius Agrippa verwirk-\n licht, die einen Menschen als blosses Fragment seines eignen Körpers dar-\n stellt [Note: Bei den Korallen bildet jedes Individuum in der That den Magen für die\n ganze Gruppe.","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"13.110"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0111","text":"Es führt ihr aber Nahrungsstoff zu, statt wie der römische\n l’atricier ihn wegzuführen.] . Wenn der Arbeiter ursprünglich seine Arbeitskraft an das\n Kapital verkauft, weil ihm die materiellen Produktionsbedin-\n [Note: in der Gesellschaft vorhanden vorfinde; es ist ausserdem nöthig, dass es in den\n Händen der Unternehmer in hinreichend beträchtlichen Massen accumulirt sei, um\n sie zur Arbeit auf grosser Stufenleiter zu befähigen … Je mehr die Theilung zu-\n nimmt, erheischt die beständige Beschäftigung einer selben Zahl von Arbeitern\n immer beträchtlicheres Kapital in Werkzeugen, Rohstoffen u. s. w.“ (Storch:\n „Cours d’Écon. Polit.“ Pariser Ausg. t. I, p. 250, 251.)","kind":"passage","sequence":111,"address":"13.111"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0112","text":"„La concen-\n tration des instrumens de production et la division du travail sont aussi inséparables\n l’une de l’autre que le sont, dans le régime politique, la concentration des pouvoirs\n publics et la division des intérêts privés.“ (Karl Marx l. c. p. 134.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"346\", \"facs\": \"#f0365\"} -->gungen einer Waare fehlen, existirt seine individuelle Arbeits-\n kraft jetzt überhaupt nur noch, wann und sofern sie an das Kapital ver-\n kauft wird. Sie funktionirt nur noch in einem Zusammenhang, der erst\nnach ihrem Verkauf, in der Werkstatt des Kapitalisten, existirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":112,"address":"13.112"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0113","text":"Seiner\n natürlichen Beschaffenheit nach verunfähigt, etwas Selbstständiges zu\n machen, entwickelt der Manufakturarbeiter produktive Thätigkeit nur noch\n als Zubehör zur Werkstatt des Kapitalisten [Note: „L’ouvrier qui porte dans ses bras tout un métier, peut aller partout\n exercer son industrie et trouver des moyens de subsister: l’autre (der Manufaktur-\n arbeiter) n’est qu’un accessoire qui, séparé de ses confrères, n’a plus ni capa-\n cité, ni indépendance, et qui se trouve forcé d’accepter la loi, qu’on juge à propos\n de lui imposer.“ (Storch l. c. Petersb. édit. 1815, t. I, p. 204.)] . Wie dem auserwähl-\n ten Volk auf der Stirn geschrieben stand, dass es das Eigenthum Jehovas,\n so drückt die Theilung der Arbeit dem Manufakturarbeiter einen Stempel\n auf, der ihn zum Eigenthum des Kapitals brandmarkt.","kind":"passage","sequence":113,"address":"13.113"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0114","text":"Die Kenntnisse, die Einsicht und der Wille, die der selbstständige\n Bauer oder Handwerker, wenn auch auf kleinem Massstab entwickelt, wie\n der Wilde alle Kunst des Kriegs als persönliche List ausübt, sind jetzt nur\n noch für das Ganze der Werkstatt erheischt. Die geistigen Potenzen der\n Produktion erweitern ihren Massstab auf der einen Seite, weil sie auf vielen\n Seiten verschwinden. Was die Theilarbeiter verlieren, koncentrirt\n sich ihnen gegenüber im Kapital [Note: A. Ferguson l. c. Fzs. Uebers. 1783, t. II, p. 135, 136. „L’un\n peut avoir gagné ce que l’autre a perdu.“] . Es ist ein Produkt der manufaktur-\n mässigen Theilung der Arbeit ihnen die geistigen Potenzen des mate-\n riellen Produktionsprozesses als fremdes Eigenthum und sie beherr-\n schende Macht gegenüberzustellen.","kind":"passage","sequence":114,"address":"13.114"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0115","text":"Dieser Scheidungsprozess be-\n ginnt in der einfachen Cooperation, wo der Kapitalist den einzelnen\n Arbeitern gegenüber die Einheit und den Willen des gesellschaftlichen Ar-\n beitskörpers vertritt. Er entwickelt sich in der Manufaktur, die den Ar-\n beiter zum Theilarbeiter verstümmelt. Er vollendet sich in der grossen\n Industrie, welche die Wissenschaft als selbstständige Produktions-\n potenz von der Arbeit trennt und in den Dienst des Kapitals presst [Note: „Der Mann des Wissens und der produktive Arbeiter sind weit von ein-\n ander getrennt, und die Wissenschaft, statt in der Hand des Arbeiters seine eignen\n Produktivkräfte für ihn selbst zu vermehren, hat sich fast überall ihm gegenüber-] .","kind":"passage","sequence":115,"address":"13.115"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0116","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"347\", \"facs\": \"#f0366\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":116,"address":"13.116"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0117","text":"In der Manufaktur ist die Bereicherung des Gesammtarbeiters, und\n daher des Kapitals, an gesellschaftlicher Produktivkraft bedingt durch die\n Verarmung des Arbeiters an individuellen Produktivkräften. „Die Unwissen-\n heit ist die Mutter der Industrie wie des Aberglaubens. Nachdenken und\n Einbildungskraft sind dem Irrthum unterworfen; aber die Gewohnheit den\n Fuss oder die Hand zu bewegen hängt weder von dem einen, noch von\n der andern ab. So könnte man sagen, dass mit Bezug auf Manufakturen\n ihre Vollkommenheit darin besteht, sich des Geistes entschlagen zu können,\n in der Art, dass die Werkstatt als eine Maschine betrachtet wer-\n den kann, deren Theile Menschen sind“ [Note: A. Ferguson l. c. p. 134, 135.] . In der That\n wandten einige Manufakturen in der Mitte des 18.","kind":"passage","sequence":117,"address":"13.117"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0118","text":"Jahrhunderts für gewisse\n einfache Operationen, welche aber Fabrikgeheimnisse bildeten, mit Vorliebe\n halbe Idioten an [Note: J. D. Tuckett: „A History of the Past and Present State\n of the Labouring Population. Lond. 1846“, v. I, p. 149.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":118,"address":"13.118"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0119","text":"„Der Geist der grossen Mehrzahl der Menschen“, sagt A. Smith,\n „entwickelt sich nothwendig aus und an ihren Alltagsverrichtungen. Ein\n Mensch, der sein ganzes Leben in der Verrichtung weniger einfacher Ope-\n rationen verausgabt … hat keine Gelegenheit seinen Verstand zu üben …\n Er wird im Allgemeinen so stupid und unwissend, wie es für eine mensch-\n liche Kreatur möglich ist.“ Nachdem Smith den Stumpfsinn des Theil-\n arbeiters geschildert, fährt er fort: „Die Einförmigkeit seines stationären\n Lebens verdirbt natürlich auch den Muth seines Geistes … Sie zerstört\n selbst die Energie seines Körpers und verunfähigt ihn, seine Kraft schwung-\n haft und ausdauernd anzuwenden ausser in der Detailbeschäftigung,\n wozu er herangezogen ist. Sein Geschick in seinem besondern Gewerke\n scheint so erworben auf Kosten seiner intellektuellen, socialen und kriege-\n rischen Tugenden.","kind":"passage","sequence":119,"address":"13.119"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0120","text":"Aber in jeder industriellen und civilisirten Gesellschaft\n ist diess der Zustand, worin der arbeitende Arme (the labouring poor),\n d. h. die grosse Masse des Volks nothwendig verfallen muss“ [Note: A. Smith: „Wealth of Nations.“ B. V, ch. I, art. II. Als\n Schüler A. Ferguson’s, der die nachtheiligen Folgen der Theilung der Arbeit ent-] . Um\n [Note: gestellt . . . . Kenntniss wird ein Instrument, fähig von der Arbeit getrennt und\n ihr entgegengesetzt zu werden.“ (W. Thompson: „An Inquiry into the\n Principles of the Distribution of Wealth. Lond. 1824“, p. 274.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"348\", \"facs\": \"#f0367\"} --> die aus der Theilung der Arbeit entspringende völlige Verkümmerung der\n Volksmasse zu verhindern, empfiehlt A. Smith Volksunterricht von Staats-\n wegen, wenn auch in vorsichtig homöopathischen Dosen.","kind":"passage","sequence":120,"address":"13.120"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0121","text":"Konsequent\n polemisirt dagegen sein französischer Uebersetzer und Kommentator,\n G. Garnier, der sich unter dem ersten französischen Kaiserthum natur-\n gemäss zum Senator entpuppte. Volksunterricht verstosse wider die\n ersten Gesetze der Theilung der Arbeit und mit demselben „proscribire\n man unser ganzes Gesellschaftssystem“. „Wie alle andern\n Theilungen der Arbeit“, sagt er, „wird die zwischen Handarbeit und Ver-\n standesarbeit [Note: Ferguson sagt bereits: „l’art de penser, dans un période où tout est sé-\n paré, peut lui-même former un métier à part.“]  ausgesprochner und entschiedner im Masse wie die Ge-\n sellschaft (er wendet richtig diesen Ausdruck an für das Kapital, das\n Grundeigenthum und ihren Staat) reicher wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":121,"address":"13.121"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0122","text":"Gleich jeder andern ist\n diese Theilung der Arbeit eine Wirkung vergangner und eine Ursache\n künftiger Fortschritte … Darf die Regierung denn dieser Theilung der\n Arbeit entgegenwirken und sie in ihrem naturgemässen Gang aufhalten?\n Darf sie einen Theil der Staatseinnahme zum Versuch verwenden, zwei\n Klassen von Arbeit, die ihre Theilung und Trennung erstreben, zu verwir-\n ren und zu vermischen“? [Note: G. Garnier, t. V seiner Uebersetzung, p. 2—5.]","kind":"passage","sequence":122,"address":"13.122"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0123","text":"Eine gewisse geistige und körperliche Verkrüppelung ist unzertrenn-\n lich selbst von der Theilung der Arbeit im Ganzen und Grossen der Gesell-\n schaft. Da aber die Manufakturperiode diese gesellschaftliche Zerspal-\n tung der Arbeitszweige viel weiter führt, andrerseits erst mit der ihr eigen-\n thümlichen Theilung das Individuum an seiner Lebenswurzel ergreift,\n [Note: wickelt hatte, war A. Smith über diesen Punkt  [Editorial alternatives: sic = dnrchaus; corr = durchaus]  klar. Im Eingang seines\n Werks, wo die Theilung der Arbeit ex professo gefeiert wird, deutet er sie nur\n vorübergehend als Quelle der gesellschaftlichen Ungleichheiten an. Erst im 5. Buch\n über das Staatseinkommen reproducirt er Ferguson. Ich habe in „Misère de\n la Philosophie“ das Nöthige über das historische Verhältniss von Ferguson,\n A.","kind":"passage","sequence":123,"address":"13.123"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0124","text":"Smith, Lemontey und Say in ihrer Kritik der Theilung der Arbeit gegeben und\n dort auch zuerst die manufakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit als spezifische\n Form der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise dargestellt. (l. c. p.\n 122 sq.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"349\", \"facs\": \"#f0368\"} --> liefert sie auch zuerst das Material und den Anstoss zur industriellen\n Pathologie [Note: Ramazzini, Professor der praktischen Medizin zu Padua, veröffent-\n lichte 1713 sein Werk: „De morbis artificum“, 1781 in’s Französische\n übersetzt, wieder abgedruckt 1841 in der „Encyclopédie des Sciences\n Médicales. 7me Dis. Auteurs Classiques.“ Die Periode der grossen In-\n dustrie hat seinen Katalog der Arbeiterkrankheiten natürlich sehr vermehrt. Siehe u.a.\nHygiène physique et morale de l’ouvrier dans les grandes villes\n en général, et dans la ville de Lyon en particulier. Par le Dr.\n A. L. Fonterel.","kind":"passage","sequence":124,"address":"13.124"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0125","text":"Paris 1858“ und „Die Krankheiten, welche ver-\n schiedenen Ständen, Altern und Geschlechtern eigenthümlich\n sind. 6 Bände. Ulm 1860.“ Im Jahr 1854 ernannte die Society of\n Arts eine Untersuchungskommission über industrielle Pathologie. Die Liste der\n von dieser Kommission gesammelten Dokumente findet man im Katalog des\n „Twickenham Economic Museum.“ Sehr wichtig auch die officiellen\n Reports des „Board of Health“.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":125,"address":"13.125"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0126","text":"„Einen Menschen unterabtheilen, heisst ihn hinrichten, wenn er das\n Todesurtheil verdient, ihn meuchelmorden, wenn er es nicht verdient. Die\n Unterabtheilung der Arbeit ist der Meuchelmord eines Volks“ [Note: „To subdivide a man is to execute him, if he deserves the sentence, to\n assassinate him if he does not … The subdivision of labour is the assassination\n of a people.“ (D. Urquhart: „Familiar Words. London 1855“,\n p. 119.) Hegel hatte sehr ketzerische Ansichten über die Theilung der Arbeit.\n „Unter gebildeten Menschen kann man zunächst solche verstehn, die Alles machen\n können, was Andere thun“, sagt er in seiner Rechtsphilosophie.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":126,"address":"13.126"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0127","text":"Die auf Theilung der Arbeit beruhende Cooperation oder die Manu-\n faktur ist in ihren Anfängen ein naturwüchsiges Gebild der kapitalistischen\n Produktion. Sobald sie einige Konsistenz und Breite des Daseins gewon-\n nen, wird sie zur bewussten, planmässigen und systematischen Form der\n kapitalistischen Produktionsweise. Die Geschichte der eigentlichen Manu-\n faktur zeigt, wie die ihr eigenthümliche Theilung der Arbeit zunächst erfah-\n rungsmässig, gleichsam hinter dem Rücken der handelnden Personen, die\n sachgemässen Formen gewinnt, dann aber, gleich dem zünftigen Hand-\n werke, die einmal gefundene Form traditionell festzuhalten strebt und in\n einzelnen Fällen Jahrhundertlang festhält. Aendert sich diese Form, so,\n ausser in Nebendingen, immer nur in Folge einer Revolution der Arbeits-\n instrumente.","kind":"passage","sequence":127,"address":"13.127"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0128","text":"Die moderne Manufaktur — ich spreche hier nicht von der\n auf Maschinerie beruhenden grossen Industrie — findet entweder, wie\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"350\", \"facs\": \"#f0369\"} --> z. B. die Kleidermanufaktur, in den grossen Städten, wo sie entsteht,\n die disjecta membra poetae bereits fertig vor und hat sie nur aus ihrer Zer-\n streuung zu sammeln, oder das Prinzip der Theilung liegt auf flacher Hand,\n indem einfach die verschiednen Verrichtungen der handwerksmässigen\n Produktion (z. B. beim Buchbinden) besondern Arbeitern ausschliesslich\n angeeignet werden. Es kostet noch keine Woche Erfahrung in solchen\n Fällen die Verhältnisszahl zwischen den für jede Funktion nöthigen Hän-\n den zu finden [Note: Der gemüthliche Glaube an das Erfindungsgenie, das der einzelne Kapi-\n talist in der Theilung der Arbeit a priori ausübe, findet sich nur noch bei deutschen\n Professoren, wie Herrn Roscher z.","kind":"passage","sequence":128,"address":"13.128"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0129","text":"B., der dem Kapitalisten, aus dessen Jupiter-\n haupt die Theilung der Arbeit fertig hervorspringt, zum Dank „diverse Arbeits-\n löhne“ widmet. Die grössere oder geringere Anwendung der Theilung der\n Arbeit hängt von der Länge der Börse, nicht von der Grösse des Genies ab.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":129,"address":"13.129"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0130","text":"Die manufakturmässige Theilung der Arbeit schafft durch Ana-\n lyse der handwerksmässigen Thätigkeit, Spezificirung der Arbeitsinstru-\n mente, Bildung der Theilarbeiter, ihre Gruppirung und Kombination in\n einem Gesammtmechanismus, die qualitative Gliederung und die quantita-\n tive Proportionalität gesellschaftlicher Produktionsprozesse, also eine be-\n stimmte Organisation gesellschaftlicher Arbeit und ent-\n wickelt damit zugleich neue, gesellschaftliche Produktivkraft der Arbeit.\n Als spezifisch kapitalistische Form des gesellschaftlichen Produk-\n tionsprozesses — und auf den vorgefundnen Grundlagen konnte sie sich\n nicht anders als in der kapitalistischen Form entwickeln — ist sie\n nur eine besondre Methode relativen Mehrwerth zu erzeugen oder\n die Selbstverwerthung des Kapitals — was man gesell-\n schaftlichen Reichthum, „Wealth of Nations“ u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":130,"address":"13.130"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0131","text":"nennt\n — auf Kosten der Arbeiter zu erhöhn. Sie entwickelt die gesellschaft-\n liche Produktivkraft der Arbeit nicht nur für den Kapitalisten, statt für\n den Arbeiter, sondern durch die Verkrüpplung des individuellen Arbeiters.\n Sie producirt neue Bedingungen der Herrschaft des Kapitals über die Ar-\n beit. Wenn sie daher einerseits als historischer Fortschritt und nothwen-\n diges Entwicklungsmoment im ökonomischen Bildungsprozess der Gesell-\n schaft erscheint, so andrerseits als ein Mittel civilisirter und raffinirter\n Exploitation.","kind":"passage","sequence":131,"address":"13.131"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0132","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"351\", \"facs\": \"#f0370\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":132,"address":"13.132"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0133","text":"Die politische Oekonomie, die als eigne Wissenschaft erst in der Manu-\n fakturperiode aufkommt, betrachtet die gesellschaftliche Theilung der\n Arbeit überhaupt nur vom Standpunkt der manufakturmässigen Thei-\n lung der Arbeit [Note: Die älteren Schriftsteller wie Petty, „Advantages of the East India Trade“\n etc., fixiren mehr denn A. Smith die manufakturmässige Theilung als kapitali-\n stische Form der Produktion.] , als Mittel mit demselben Quantum Arbeit mehr Waare\n zu produciren, daher die Waaren zu verwohlfeilern und die Accumulation des\n Kapitals zu beschleunigen. Im strengsten Gegensatz zu dieser Accentuirung\n der Quantität und des Tauschwerths halten sich die Schriftsteller\n des klassischen Alterthums ausschliesslich an Qualität und Ge-\n brauchswerth [Note: Ausnahme unter den Modernen bilden einige Schriftsteller des 18.","kind":"passage","sequence":133,"address":"13.133"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0134","text":"Jahr-\n hunderts, die in Bezug auf Theilung der Arbeit fast nur den Alten nachsprechen,\n wie Beccaria und James Harris. So Beccaria: „Ciascuno prova coll’ espe-\n rienza, che applicando la mano e l’ingegno sempre allo stesso genere di opere e di\n produtte, egli più facili, più abbondanti e migliori ne traca resultati, di quello che\n se ciascuno isolatamente le cose tutte a se necessarie soltanto facesse .... Divi-\n dendosi in tal maniera per la comune e privata utilità gli uomini in varie classe e\n condizioni.“ (Cesare Beccaria: „Elementi di Econ. Publica“, ed.\nCustodi, Part. Moderna t. XI, p. 28.) James Harris, später Earl of\n Malmesbury, berühmt durch die „Diaries“ über seine Gesandtschaft in Peters-\n burg, sagt selbst in einer Note zu seinem Dialogue concerning Hap-\n piness. Lond. 1741, später wieder abgedruckt in „Three Treatises etc.\n 3. ed. Lond.","kind":"passage","sequence":134,"address":"13.134"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0135","text":"1772“: „The whole argument, to prove society natural (nämlich\n durch die „division of employments“) is taken from the second book of Plato’s\n republic.“] . In Folge der Scheidung der gesellschaftlichen\n Produktionszweige werden die Waaren besser gemacht, die verschiedenen\n Triebe und Talente der Menschen wählen sich entsprechende Wirkungs-\n sphären [Note: So in der Odyssee XIV, 228: „Ἄλλος γάϱ τ᾽ ἄλλοισιν ἀνὴϱ ἐπιτέϱπε-\n ται ἔϱγοις“ und Archilochus beim Sextus Empiricus: „ἄλλος ἄλλῳ ἐπ᾽ ἔϱγῳ\n ϰαϱδίην ἰαίνεται.“]  und ohne Beschränkung ist nirgendwo Bedeutendes zu lei-\n sten [Note: „Πολλ᾽ ἠπίστατο ἔϱγα, ϰαϰῶς δ᾽ ἠπίστατο πάντα.“ Indess, so sehr sich\n der Athenienser als Waarenproducent dem Spartaner überlegen fühlte, weil dieser\n im Krieg wohl über Menschen, nicht aber über Geld verfügen könne, wie\n Thucydides den Pericles sagen lässt in der Rede, worin er die Athenienser zum","kind":"passage","sequence":135,"address":"13.135"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0136","text":"peloponnesischen Krieg aufstachelt: „σώμασί τε ἑτοιμότεϱοι οἱ αὐτουϱγοὶ τῶν] . Also Produkt und Producent werden verbessert durch die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"352\", \"facs\": \"#f0371\"} --> Theilung der Arbeit. Wird gelegentlich auch das Wachsthum der Pro-\n duktenmasse erwähnt, so nur mit Bezug auf die grössere Fülle des Ge-\n brauchswerths. Es wird mit keiner Silbe des Tauschwerths, der\nVerwohlfeilerung der Waaren gedacht. Dieser Standpunkt des Ge-\n brauchswerths herrscht sowohl bei Plato [Note: Plato entwickelt die Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb des Gemeinwesens aus\n der Vielseitigkeit der Bedürfnisse und der Einseitigkeit der Anlagen der Indivi-\n duen. Hauptgesichtspunkt bei ihm, dass der Arbeiter sich nach dem Werk richten\n müsse, nicht das Werk nach dem Arbeiter, was unvermeidlich, wenn er verschiedne\n Künste zugleich, also eine oder die andere als Nebenwerk treibe.","kind":"passage","sequence":136,"address":"13.136"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0137","text":"„Οὐ γὰϱ\n ἐϑέλει τὸ πϱαττόμενον τὴν τοῦ πϱάττοντος σχολὴν πεϱιμένειν, ἀλλ᾽ ἀνάγϰη τὸν\n πϱάττοντα τῷ πϱαττομένῳ ἐπαϰολουϑεῖν μὴ ἐν παϱέϱγου μέϱει. Ἀνάγϰη. Ἐϰ\n δὴ τούτων πλείω τε ἕϰαστα γίγνεται ϰαὶ ϰάλλιον ϰαὶ ῥᾷον, ὅταν εἷς ἓν ϰατὰ\n φύσιν ϰαὶ ἐν ϰαιϱῷ, σχολὴν τῶν ἄλλων ἄγων, πϱάττῃ.“ (Rep. l. 2. ed. Baiter,\n Orelli etc.) Aehnlich bei Thucydides l. c. c.","kind":"passage","sequence":137,"address":"13.137"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0138","text":"42: „Das Seewesen ist eine\n Kunst so sehr wie irgend etwas anderes und kann nicht bei etwa vorkommenden\n Fällen als Nebenwerk betrieben werden, sondern vielmehr nichts andres neben\n ihm als Nebenwerk.“ Muss das Werk, sagt Plato, auf den Arbeiter warten, so\n wird oft der kritische Zeitpunkt der Produktion verpasst und das Machwerk ver-\n dorben, „ἔϱγου ϰαιϱὸν διόλλυται.“ Dieselbe platonische Idee findet man wieder\n im Protest der englischen Bleichereibesitzer gegen die Klausel des Fabrikakts, die\n eine bestimmte Essstunde für alle Arbeiter festsetzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":138,"address":"13.138"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0139","text":"Ihr Geschäft könne sich nicht\n nach den Arbeitern richten, denn „in the various operations of singeing, washing,\n bleaching, mangling, calendering, and dyeing, none of them can be stopped at a\n given moment without risk of damage … to enforce the same dinner hour for\n all the work people might occasionally subject valuable goods to the risk of\n danger for incomplete operations.“ Le platonisme où va-t-il se nicher!] , der die Theilung der Ar-\n beit als Grundlage der gesellschaftlichen Scheidung der Stände behandelt,\n als bei Xenophon [Note: Xenophon erzählt, es sei nicht nur ehrenvoll Speisen von der Tafel\n des Perserkönigs zu erhalten, sondern diese Speisen seien auch viel schmackhafter\n als andere. „Und diess ist nichts Wunderbares, denn wie die übrigen Künste in\n den grossen Städten besonders vervollkommnet sind, ebenso werden die könig-\n lichen Speisen ganz eigens zubereitet.","kind":"passage","sequence":139,"address":"13.139"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0140","text":"Denn in den kleinen Städten macht\n Derselbe Bettstelle, Thüre, Pflug, Tisch; oft baut er obendrein noch Häuser und] , der mit seinem charakteristisch bürgerlichen In-\n [Note: ἀνϑϱώπων ἢ χϱήμασι πολεμεῖν“ (Thuc. l. I, c. 41), blieb ihnen auch in der ma-\n teriellen Produktion die αὐταϱϰεία, die der Theilung der Arbeit gegenüber-\n steht, das Ideal, „παϱ᾽ ὧν γὰϱ τὸ εὖ, παϱὰ τούτων ϰαὶ τὸ αὔταϱϰες.“ Man muss\n dabei erwägen, dass es noch zur Zeit des Sturzes der 30 Tyrannen keine 5000\n Athener ohne Grundeigenthum gab.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"353\", \"facs\": \"#f0372\"} --> stinkt schon der Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb einer Werkstatt näher\n rückt.","kind":"passage","sequence":140,"address":"13.140"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0141","text":"Plato’s Republik, soweit in ihr die Theilung der Arbeit als das\n gestaltende Prinzip des Staats entwickelt wird, ist nur atheniensische\n Idealisirung des ägyptischen Kastenwesens, wie Aegypten\n als industrielles Musterland auch andern seiner Zeitgenossen gilt, z. B.\n dem Isokrates [Note: „Er (Busiris) theilte Alle in besondere Kasten … befahl, dass immer\n die Nämlichen die gleichen Geschäfte treiben sollten, weil er wusste, dass die,\n welche mit ihren Beschäftigungen wechseln, in keinem Geschäft gründlich werden;\n die aber, welche beständig bei denselben Beschäftigungen bleiben, jedes aufs\n vollendetste zu Stande bringen.","kind":"passage","sequence":141,"address":"13.141"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0142","text":"Wirklich werden wir auch finden, dass sie in\n Beziehung auf Künste und Gewerbe ihre Rivalen mehr übertroffen haben als sonst\n der Meister den Stümper und in Beziehung auf die Einrichtung, wodurch sie die\n Künigsherrschaft und übrige Staatsverfassung erhalten, so vortrefflich sind, dass\n die berühmtesten Philosophen, welche darüber zu sprechen unternehmen, die\n Staatsverfassung Aegyptens vor andern lobten.“ (Isocr. Busiris, c. 8.)] , und diese Bedeutung selbst noch für die Griechen der\n römischen Kaiserzeit behielt [Note: cf. Diod. Sic.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":142,"address":"13.142"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0143","text":"Während der eigentlichen Manufakturperiode, d. h. der Periode, wo-\n rin die Manufaktur die herrschende Form der kapitalistischen Produktions-\n weise, stösst die volle Ausführung ihrer eigenen Tendenzen auf vielseitige\n Hindernisse. Obgleich sie, wie wir sahen, neben der hierarchischen Glie-\n derung der Arbeiter eine einfache Scheidung zwischen geschickten und\n ungeschickten Arbeitern schafft, bleibt die Zahl der letztern durch den\n überwiegenden Einfluss der erstern sehr beschränkt. Obgleich sie die\n Sonderoperationen dem verschiednen Grad von Reife, Kraft und Entwick-\n [Note: ist zufrieden, wenn er selbst so eine für seinen Unterhalt ausreichende Kundschaft\n findet. Es ist rein unmöglich, dass ein Mensch, der so vielerlei treibt, alles gut\n mache. In den grossen Städten aber, wo jeder Einzelne viele Käufer\n findet, genügt auch ein Handwerk, um seinen Mann zu nähren.","kind":"passage","sequence":143,"address":"13.143"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0144","text":"Ja oft gehört\n dazu nicht einmal ein ganzes Handwerk, sondern der eine macht Mannsschuhe,\n der andere Weiberschuhe. Hier und da lebt einer bloss vom Nähen, der andere\n vom Zuschneiden der Schuhe; der eine schneidet bloss Kleider zu, der andere setzt\n die Stücke nur zusammen. Nothwendig ist es nun, dass der Verrichter der ein-\n fachsten Arbelt sie unbedingt auch am besten macht. Ebenso steht’s mit der\n Kochkunst.“ (Xen. Cyrop. l. VIII, c. 2.) Die zu erzielende Güte des Ge-\n brauchswerths wird hier ausschliesslich fixirt, obgleich schon Xenophon die\n Stufenleiter der Arbeitstheilung vom Umfang des Markts abhängig weiss.] \n [Running matter: I.","kind":"passage","sequence":144,"address":"13.144"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0145","text":"23] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"354\", \"facs\": \"#f0373\"} --> lung ihrer lehendigen Arbeitsorgane anpasst und daher zu produktiver\n Ausbeutung von Weibern und Kindern drängt, scheitert diese Tendenz im\n Grossen und Ganzen an den Gewohnheiten und dem Widerstand der männ-\n lichen Arbeiter. Obgleich die Zersetzung der handwerksmässigen Thätig-\n keit die Bildungskosten und daher den Werth der Arbeiter senkt, bleibt\n für schwierigere Detailarbeit eine längere Erlernungszeit nöthig und wird\n auch da, wo sie vom Ueberfluss, eifersüchtig von den Arbeitern aufrecht\n erhalten. Wir finden z. B. in England die laws of apprenticeship mit\n ihrer siebenjährigen Lernzeit bis zum Ende der Manufakturperiode in Voll-\n kraft und erst von der grossen Industrie über Haufen geworfen.","kind":"passage","sequence":145,"address":"13.145"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0146","text":"Da das\nHandwerksgeschick die Grundlage der Manufaktur bleibt, und der in ihr\n funktionirende Gesammtmechanismus kein von den Arbeitern selbst unab-\n hängiges objektives Skelett besitzt, ringt das Kapital beständig mit\n der Insubordination der Arbeiter. „Die Schwäche der menschlichen Na-\n tur,“ ruft Freund Ure aus, „ist so gross, dass der Arbeiter; je geschickter,\n desto eigenwilliger und schwieriger zu behandeln wird, und folglich dem\n Gesammtmechanismus durch seine rappelköpfigen Capricen schweren\n Schaden zufügt“ [Note: Ure l. c. t. I, p. 31.] . Durch die ganze Manufakturperiode läuft daher die\n Klage über den Disciplinmangel der Arbeiter [Note: Das im Text Gesagte gilt viel mehr für England als für Frankreich und\n mehr für Frankreich als Holland.] . Und hätten wir nicht\n die Zeugnisse gleichzeitiger Schriftsteller, die einfachen Thatsachen, dass\n es vom 16.","kind":"passage","sequence":146,"address":"13.146"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0147","text":"Jahrhundert bis zur Epoche der grossen Industrie dem Kapital\n misslingt, sich der ganzen disponiblen Arbeitszeit der Manufakturarbeiter\n zu bemächtigen, dass die Manufakturen kurzlebig sind und mit der Ein-\n oder Auswanderung der Arbeiter ihren Sitz in dem einen Land verlassen\n und in dem andern aufschlagen, würden Bibliotheken sprechen. „Ord-\n nung muss auf die eine oder die andre Weise gestiftet werden“, ruft 1770\n der wiederholt citirte Verfasser des „Essay on Trade and Commerce“.\nOrdnung, hallt es 66 Jahre später zurück aus dem Mund des Dr. Andrew\n Ure, „Ordnung“ fehlte in der auf „dem scholastischen Dogma der Theilung\n der Arbeit“ beruhenden Manufaktur, und „Arkwright schuf die\n Ordnung.“","kind":"passage","sequence":147,"address":"13.147"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0148","text":"Zugleich konnte die Manufaktur die gesellschaftliche Produktion weder\n in ihrem ganzen Umfang ergreifen, noch in ihrer Tiefe umwälzen. Sie\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"355\", \"facs\": \"#f0374\"} --> gipfelte als ökonomisches Kunstwerk auf der breiten Grundlage des städti-\n schen Handwerks und der ländlich häuslichen Nebenindustrie. Ihre eigne\n enge technologische Basis trat auf einem gewissen Entwicklungsgrad mit\n den von ihr selbst geschaffenen Produktionsbedürfnissen in Widerspruch.","kind":"passage","sequence":148,"address":"13.148"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0149","text":"Eins ihrer vollendetsten Gebilde war die Werkstatt zur Produktion der\nArbeitsinstrumente selbst, und namentlich auch der bereits angewand-\n ten komplicirteren mechanischen Apparate. „Ein solches Atelier“, sagt\n Ure, „bot dem Auge die Theilung der Arbeit in ihren mannigfachen Abstu-\n fungen. Bohrer, Meisel, Drechselbank hatten jede ihre eignen Arbeiter,\n hierarchisch gegliedert nach dem Grad ihrer Geschicklichkeit.“ Diess\n Produkt der manufakturmässigen Theilung der Arbeit producirte seiner-\n seits — Maschinen. Sie heben die handwerksmässige Thätigkeit als\n das regelnde Princip der gesellschaftlichen Produktion auf. So wird einer-\n seits der technologische Grund der lebenslangen Annexation des Arbeiters\n an eine Theilfunktion weggeräumt. Andrerseits fallen die Schranken, welche\n dasselbe Princip der Herrschaft des Kapitals noch auferlegte.","kind":"passage","sequence":149,"address":"13.149"}],"sha256":"ebda89bacb6038e33696fabac97a0bc24118f2f820797c769c2d4cf528a129bf","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T204218780260Z_531e5dce","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"3) Theilung der Arbeit und Manufaktur.","source_sha256":"ebda89bacb6038e33696fabac97a0bc24118f2f820797c769c2d4cf528a129bf","sha256":"385798f6a3482a50fa096d09a140c6db6c377ee6fe4be78f2d645ff6f984b79b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:42:18.780272+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0001","text":"Cooperation based on division of labour assumes its\n classical form in manufacture. As the characteristic form of the\n capitalist process of production, it predominates during the actual\n period of manufacture, which, roughly speaking, lasts from the middle of\n the sixteenth century to the last third of the eighteenth.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"13.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0002","text":"Manufacture arises in a twofold way.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"13.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0003","text":"Either workers of various independent\n handicrafts, through whose hands a product must pass until its final maturity,\n are united in one workshop under the command of the same capitalist.\n For example, a carriage was the aggregate product of the labours of a\n large number of independent craftsmen, such as wheelwrights, saddlers, tailors,\n locksmiths, girdlers, turners, passementerie-makers, glaziers, painters, varnishers,\n gilders, etc. In the carriage manufactory all these different\n craftsmen were united in one workhouse in order to work into one another's hands\n simultaneously. A carriage cannot, indeed, be gilded before it is made. But if\n many carriages are made simultaneously, one part can constantly be gilded while\n another part passes through an earlier phase of the process of production.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"13.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0004","text":"So far we stand\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"319\", \"facs\": \"#f0338\"} --> still on the ground of simple cooperation, which finds its material in human\n beings and things. Meanwhile, a substantial alteration very soon\n occurs. The tailor, locksmith, girdler, etc., who is occupied only in\n carriage-making, gradually loses, together with the habit, the capacity to\n practise his old handicraft in its whole extent. On the other hand, his one-sided\n activity now receives the most appropriate form for its narrowed sphere of action.\n Originally the carriage manufactory appeared as a combination of independent\n handicrafts.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"13.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0005","text":"It gradually becomes division of carriage pro-\n duction into its various separate operations, each one of which\n crystallises into the exclusive function of one worker, while their totality is per-\n formed by the association of these detail workers. In the same way the\n cloth manufactory, and a whole series of other manufactures, arose from the\n combination of different handicrafts under the command of the same capital [Note: To cite a more modern example of this mode of formation of manufacture,\n the following quotation: The spinning and weaving of silk in Lyons and Nîmes\n “is wholly patriarchal; it employs many women and children, but without\n exhausting or corrupting them; it leaves them in their beautiful valleys of the Drôme,\n the Var, the Isère, and Vaucluse, there to rear silkworms and unwind their cocoons;\n it never enters a real factory.”","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"13.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0006","text":"To be equally well observed …\n the principle of division of labour there assumes a special character. There are indeed\n winders, throwsters, dyers, sizers, and then weavers; but they are not united in\n one establishment, do not depend upon one master; all of them are independent.” (A. Blanqui: “Course\n of Industrial Economics. Collected by A. Blaise. Paris (1838–39),”\n pp. 44–80 passim.) Since Blanqui wrote this, the various indepen-\n dent workers have in part been united in factories.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"13.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0007","text":"But manufacture also arises by the opposite\n route. Many craftsmen, who do the same or similar things,\n for example make paper or type or needles, are employed by the\n same capital simultaneously in the same workshop.\n This is cooperation in its simplest form. Each of these craftsmen\n (perhaps with one or two journeymen) makes the whole commodity and thus\n performs in succession the various operations required for its production.\n He continues to work in his old handicraft manner. Yet external circumstances\n soon induce the concentration of the workers in the same space and the simultaneity of their\n labours to be utilised differently. For example, a larger quantity of finished\n commodities is to be delivered within a definite period of time.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"13.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0008","text":"Labour\n is therefore divided. Instead of having the various operations performed by the\n same craftsman in a temporal sequence, they are detached from one another,\n isolated, placed side by side in space, each assigned to another craftsman,\n and all performed simultaneously by the cooperators. This accidental divi-\n sion repeats itself, displays its peculiar advantages, and gradually ossifies\n into systematic division of labour. From the individual product of an\n independent craftsman, who does many things, the commodity is transformed into the social\n product of an association of craftsmen, each of whom constantly performs only\n one and the same partial operation.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"13.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0009","text":"The same operations which\n merged into one another as successive acts of the German guild paper-maker\n became independent in the Dutch paper manufactory as parallel partial operations of many cooperating work-\n ers. The guild needle-maker of Nuremberg forms the basic element of the Eng-\n lish needle manufactory. But whereas that one needle-maker successively passed through\n a series of perhaps 20 operations, here 20 needle-makers soon performed them side by side,\n each only one of the 20 operations, which, as a result of\n experience, were divided, isolated, and made independent still further,\n as the exclusive functions of individual workers.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"13.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0010","text":"The mode of origin of manufacture, its formation out of\n handicraft, is therefore twofold. On the one hand, it proceeds from the com-\n bination of various, independent handicrafts, which are deprived of independence and made one-sided\n up to the point at which they form only mutually supplementary partial operations in the process of production\n of one and the same commodity. On the other hand, it proceeds from the\ncooperation of craftsmen of the same kind, decomposes the same\n individual handicraft into its various particular operations, and\n isolates and makes these independent up to the point at which each one\n becomes the exclusive function of a particular worker. On the one\n hand, therefore, manufacture introduces division of labour into a process of pro-\n duction or develops it further; on the other hand, it combines previously\n separated handicrafts.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"13.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0011","text":"But whatever its particular point of departure,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"321\", \"facs\": \"#f0340\"} --> its final form is the same—a mechanism of production,\n whose organs are human beings.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"13.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0012","text":"For a correct understanding of division of labour in manufac-\n ture it is essential to keep the following points in view: First, the\nanalysis of the process of production into its particular\n phases here coincides completely with the decomposition of a\n handicraft activity into its various partial operations.\n Whether composite or simple, the operation remains handicraft-like and is therefore dependent\n on the strength, skill, speed, and surety of the individual worker in handling his instrument.\n Handicraft remains the basis. This narrow technological basis\n excludes a truly scientific analysis of the process of production, since every\n partial process undergone by the product must be capable of execution as handicraft\n partial labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"13.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0013","text":"Precisely because handicraft skill thus\n remains the foundation of the process of production, each worker is appropriated exclusively\n to a partial function and his labour-power transformed into the lifelong organ\n of this partial function. Finally, this division of labour is a particular kind of coopera-\n tion, and some of its advantages arise from the general nature,\n not from this particular form, of cooperation.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"13.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0014","text":"If we now consider the details more closely, it is first clear that\n a worker who performs one and the same simple operation throughout his life\n transforms his entire body into its automatically one-sided organ and therefore\n spends less time upon it than does the craftsman who alternately performs a whole\n series of operations. But the combined aggregate worker,\n who forms the living mechanism of manufacture, consists entirely of such\n one-sided detail workers. In comparison with independent handicraft,\n therefore, more is produced in less time, or the productive power of labour\n is increased [Note: “The more any manufacture of much variety shall be distributed and\n assigned to different artists, the same must needs be better done and with greater\n expedition, with less loss of time and labour.” (“The Advantages of the\n East India Trade. Lond. 1720,” p. 71.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"13.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0015","text":"The method of partial labour is likewise per-\n fected after it has become independent as the exclusive function of a person. The constant repeti-\n [Running matter: I. 21]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"322\", \"facs\": \"#f0341\"} --> tion of the same restricted activity and the concentration of attention upon this\n restriction teach, by experience, how to attain the intended useful effect with the least\n expenditure of force. But since different generations of workers always live simultaneously\n and cooperate in the same manufactures, the technical artifices thus acquired\n soon become fixed, accumulated, and transmitted [Note: “Easy labour is transmitted skill.” (Th. Hodgskin l. c. p. 125.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"13.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0016","text":"Manufacture\n in fact produces the virtuosity of the detail worker by reproducing within\n the workshop the naturally grown differentiation of trades which it found in society,\n and systematically driving it to the extreme. On the other hand, its transformation of partial labour into the\n lifelong calling of a human being corresponds to the tendency of earlier societies to make occupations\n hereditary, to petrify them into castes, or, where definite historical conditions generate a vari-\n ability of the individual contrary to the caste system, at least to ossify the division of labour\n into guilds.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"13.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0017","text":"The emergence of these castes and guilds follows\n the same natural law that regulates the differentiation of plants and animals into species\n and subspecies, except that at a certain degree of development the\n heredity of castes or the exclusiveness of guilds is decreed as a social law [Note: “The arts also … in Egypt have attained the proper degree of\n perfection. For in this country alone craftsmen are absolutely forbidden to\n interfere in the affairs of another class of citizens, and may pursue only\n the occupation which by law belongs hereditarily to their stock …\n Among other peoples one finds that craftsmen distribute their attention among\n too many objects … Now they try agriculture, now they engage in commercial\n business, now they occupy themselves with two or three arts at once.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"13.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0018","text":"In free states they mostly run to the popular assemblies …\n In Egypt, on the contrary, every craftsman incurs severe punishments if he meddles\n in affairs of state or practises several arts at once. Thus nothing can disturb\n their industry in their calling … Moreover, as they have many rules from their\n forefathers, they are zealously intent upon discovering still new advantages.” (Dio-\n dorus Siculus: Historical Library bk. I, ch. 74.)] . “The muslins of\n Dacca have never been surpassed in fineness, the calicoes and other fabrics of Coromandel\n in splendour and durability of colour.\n And yet they are produced without capital, machinery, division\n of labour, or any of the other means which afford manufacture in\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"323\", \"facs\": \"#f0342\"} --> Europe so many advantages.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"13.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0019","text":"The weaver is an isolated indivi-\n dual, who manufactures the fabric to a customer's order and with\n a loom of the simplest construction, sometimes consisting only\n of wooden poles roughly joined together. He possesses no apparatus himself for stretching the warp;\n the loom must therefore remain extended throughout its entire length and becomes so unwieldy and\n broad that it finds no room in the producer's hut, who must therefore\n perform his work in the open air, where it is interrupted by every change of\n weather” [Note: “Historical and descriptive Account of Brit. India etc. by\n Hugh Murray, James Wilson etc.” Edinburgh 1832, vol. II, p. 449.\n The Indian loom is a high-warp loom, i.e. the warp is stretched vertically.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"13.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0020","text":"It is only the specialised skill accumulated from generation to\n generation and inherited from father to son that imparts to the Hindu, as to the spider,\n this virtuosity. And yet such an Indian weaver performs very complicated work,\n compared with the majority of manufacture workers.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"13.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0021","text":"A craftsman who successively performs the various partial processes in the pro-\n duction of an article must now change place, now change instruments. The transition\n from one operation to another interrupts the flow of his labour and forms, as it were,\npores in his working day. These pores close up as soon as he continuously\n performs one and the same operation all day, or they disappear to the extent\n that the alternation of his operations diminishes. The increased productivity here is due either\n to the increasing expenditure of labour-power in a given period of time, that is, to growing\n intensity of labour, or to a diminution of the\n unproductive consumption of labour-power.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"13.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0022","text":"The excess\n expenditure of force, namely, which every transition from rest to motion requires,\n is compensated by the longer continuance of the once attained normal speed. On the other hand,\n the continuity of uniform labour destroys the tension and elasticity of the vital spirits,\n which find their recreation and their charm in the alternation of activity\n itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"13.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0023","text":"The productivity of labour depends not only on the virtuosity\n of the worker, but also on the perfection of his imple-\n [Running matter: 21*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"324\", \"facs\": \"#f0343\"} -->ments. Tools of the same kind, such as cutting, boring, thrusting, striking\n instruments, etc., are used in different labour processes,\n and in the same labour process the same instrument serves for different\n operations. As soon, however, as the different operations of a labour\n process are detached from one another and each partial operation receives, in the\n hand of the detail worker, a form as corresponding as possible and hence exclu-\n sive, alterations of the tools that previously served different\n purposes become necessary.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"13.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0024","text":"The direction of their change\n of form follows from experience of the particular difficulties\n which the unaltered form places in the way. The differen-\n tiation of instruments of labour, through which instruments of the same kind re-\n ceive special fixed forms for each particular useful application, and their\nspecialisation, through which each such special instrument operates in its\n full extent only in the hand of specific detail workers, charac-\n terise manufacture. In Birmingham alone, some 500\n varieties of hammers are produced, each of which serves not only for a particular pro-\n duction process, but a number of varieties often serve only for different\n operations in the same process.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"13.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0025","text":"The manufacturing period simpli-\n fies, improves, and multiplies the instruments of labour by adapting them\n to the exclusive special functions of the detail workers [Note: Darwin observes in his epoch-making work on “the Origin\n of Species,” with reference to the natural organs of plants and\n animals: “So long as one and the same organ has to perform different functions,\n a ground for its variability may perhaps be found in the fact that\n natural selection preserves or suppresses every slight deviation of form less\n carefully than when the same organ were destined for one particular purpose alone.\n Thus knives intended to cut all sorts of things may, on the whole, be pretty much\n of one form, whereas a tool intended for only one kind\n of use must have another form for every other use.”] .","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"13.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0026","text":"Thereby it simultaneously creates one of the material conditions of\n machinery, which consists of a combination of simple instruments.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"13.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0027","text":"The detail worker and his instrument form the simple ele-\n ments of manufacture. Let us now turn to its overall\n mechanism.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"13.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0028","text":"The articulation of manufacture possesses two basic forms, which,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"325\", \"facs\": \"#f0344\"} --> despite occasional interweaving, constitute two essentially different kinds\n and in particular also play a quite different role in the later transformation of manufacture\n into large-scale industry driven by machinery. This dual character arises from the nature of\n the product itself. It is formed either by the merely mechanical\n composition of independent partial products, or owes its finished\n form to a succession of connected processes and manipu-\n lations.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"13.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0029","text":"A locomotive, for example, consists of more than 5,000 independent\n parts. Yet it cannot count as an example of the first kind of genuine\n manufacture, because it is a creation of large-scale industry.\n The watch, however, can, and William Petty too illustrates manufac-\n turing division of labour by it. From the individual\n work of a Nuremberg craftsman, the watch was transformed into the social\n product of a countless number of detail workers, such as makers of rough movements,\n mainspring makers, dial makers, hairspring makers, jewel-hole-\n and ruby-lever makers, hand makers, case makers, screw makers,\n gilders, with many subdivisions, such as wheel manufacturers\n (brass and steel wheels again separated), pinion makers, handwork-\n makers, acheveur de pignon (fastens the wheels to the pinions, polishes\n the facettes, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"13.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0030","text":"etc.), pivot makers, planteur de finissage (sets various\n wheels and pinions in the movement), finisseur de barillet (has teeth\n cut, makes the holes of the proper width, hardens the plate and the\n click), escapement maker, in the case of the cylinder escapement again cylinder maker,\n escape-wheel maker, balance maker, raquette maker (the regulator by\n which the watch is adjusted), planteur d’échappement (the actual escapement-\n maker); then the repasseur de barillet (completes the barrel and\n plate), steel polisher, wheel polisher, screw polisher, numeral-\n painter, dial maker (fuses the enamel onto the copper), fabricant\n de pendants (makes only the bows of the case), finisseur de charnière\n (inserts the brass pin into the middle of the case, etc.), faiseur de\n secret (makes the springs in the case that make the lid spring open),\n engraver, ciliceur, polisseur de boite, etc., etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"13.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0031","text":"and finally the re-\n passeur, who assembles the entire watch and delivers it running. Only\n few parts of the watch pass through different hands, and all these\n membra disjecta are gathered only in the hand that finally joins them into a\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"326\", \"facs\": \"#f0345\"} --> mechanical whole. This external relation of the finished\n product to its various elements makes here, as in similar\n products, the combination of the detail workers in the same\n workshop accidental. The partial labours can themselves again be carried on as\n crafts independent of one another, as in the cantons of Vaud\n and Neuchâtel, while in Geneva, for example, large watch manufactories exist,\n i.e. direct cooperation of the detail workers under the command\nof a capital takes place.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"13.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0032","text":"Even in the latter case, dial, spring\n and case are seldom manufactured in the manufactory itself. The combined\n manufacturing enterprise is profitable here only under exceptional condi-\n tions, because competition among workers who wish to work at home\n is greatest, the fragmentation of production into a\n mass of heterogeneous processes permits little use of common means of labour,\n and the capitalist saves the outlay on work buildings, etc. in dispersed manufacture [Note: In 1854 Geneva produced 80,000 watches, not even a fifth of\n the watch production of the canton of Neuchâtel. Chaux-de-Fonds, which may be\n regarded as a single watch manufactory, alone delivers annually twice as many as\n Geneva. From 1850—61 Geneva delivered 750,000 watches. See: “Report from\n Geneva on the Watch Trade” in “Reports by H. M’s.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"13.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0033","text":"Secretaries\n of Embassy and Legation on the Manufactures, Commerce etc.\n No. 6. 1863.” If the disconnectedness of the processes into which the pro-\n duction of merely composite products falls makes in and of itself the\n transformation of such manufactories into the machine operation of large-scale industry\n very difficult, in the case of the watch there are two further obstacles: the smallness\n and delicacy of its elements, and its character as a luxury article, hence its variety,\n so that, for example, in the best London houses scarcely a\n dozen watches are made throughout the year which look alike. The watch factory of\n Vacheron et Constantin, which successfully employs machinery, also delivers at\n most only 3—4 different varieties of size and form.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"13.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0034","text":"Meanwhile, the relation of these detail\n workers, who work at home but for a capitalist\n (manufacturer, établisseur), is wholly different from that of the\n independent craftsman, who works for his own customers [Note: In watchmaking, this classical example of heterogeneous manu-\n facture, one can study very exactly the above-mentioned differentiation and specialisation\n of the instruments of labour arising from the decomposition of craft\n activity.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"13.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0035","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"327\", \"facs\": \"#f0346\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"13.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0036","text":"The second kind of manufacture, its perfected form, produces\n products which pass through connected developmental phases, a succession\n of stage-processes, as, for example, wire in the sewing-needle\n manufactory passes through the hands of 72 and even 92 specific detail workers.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"13.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0037","text":"Insofar as such manufacture originally combines dispersed crafts,\n it diminishes the spatial separation between the par-\n ticular phases of production of the product. The time of its transition from\n one stage to another is shortened, as is the labour that mediates these\n transitions [Note: “In so close a cohabitation of the People, the carriage must needs be\n less.” (“The Advantages of the East India Trade”, p. 166.)] . Compared with craft, productive\n power is thus gained, and indeed this gain arises from the general\n cooperative character of manufacture. On the other hand,\n its peculiar principle of division of labour entails an iso-\n lation of the different phases of production, which are rendered independent in relation to one another\n as so many craft-like partial labours.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"13.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0038","text":"The\n production and maintenance of the connection between the isolated\n functions necessitates the continual transport of the product from one\n hand to another and from one process to another. From the stand-\n point of large-scale industry, this appears as a characteristic,\n costly, and immanent limi-\n tation of the principle of manufacture [Note: “The isolation of the different stages of manufacture consequent upon\n the employment of manual labour adds immensely to the cost of production, the\n loss mainly arising from the mere removals from one process to another.” (“The\n Industry of Nations. Lond. 1855”, Part II, p. 200.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"13.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0039","text":"If one considers a definite quantity of raw material, for example\n rags in paper manufacture or wire in needle manufac-\n ture, it passes through a temporal succession of phases of production in the hands of the\n different detail workers, until it reaches its final form.\n If, on the other hand, one considers the workshop as an overall mechanism,\n then the raw material is simultaneously in all its phases of pro-\n duction at once. With one part of its many instrument-armed\n hands, the collective worker combined from the detail workers draws\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"328\", \"facs\": \"#f0347\"} --> the wire, while at the same time with other hands and tools\n he stretches it, with others cuts it, points it, etc. From a temporal\n succession, the different stage-processes have been transformed into a spatial\n juxtaposition.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"13.39","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0040","text":"Hence the delivery of more finished commodities\n in the same period of time [Note: “It (the division of labour) produces also an economy of time, by separat-\n ing the work into its different branches, all of which may be carried on into exe-\n cution at the same moment … By carrying on all the different processes at once,\n which an individual must have executed separately, it becomes possible to produce\n a multitude of pins for instance completely finished in the same time as a single\n pin might have been either cut or pointed.” (Dugald Stewart l. c. p. 319.)] . That simultaneity does indeed arise from the\n general cooperative form of the overall process, but manufacture not only\n finds the conditions of cooperation already present; it partly creates them in the first place\n through the decomposition of craft activity.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"13.40","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0041","text":"On the other hand, it attains this social organisation of the labour-\n process only by forging the same worker fast to the same detail.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"13.41","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0042","text":"Since the partial product of every detail worker is at the same time only a particular\n stage of development of the same product, each worker or\n each group of workers supplies the other with its raw material. The labour result of\n one forms the point of departure for the labour of the other. One worker\n therefore here directly employs the other. The necessary\n labour time for attaining the intended useful effect in every partial pro-\n cess is established by experience, and the overall mecha-\n nism of manufacture rests on the assumption that, in a given\n labour time, a given result is achieved. Only\n on this assumption can the different, mutually complementary\n labour processes proceed uninterruptedly, simultaneously, and spatially alongside one another.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"13.42","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0043","text":"It is clear that this direct dependence\n of the labours, and hence of the workers, upon one another compels every individual\n to use only the necessary time for his function, and thus a\n wholly different continuity, uniformity, regularity, order [Note: “The more variety of artists to every manufacture … the greater the\n order and regularity of every work, the same must needs be done in less time, the\n labour must be less.” (“The Advantages etc.” p. 68.)]\n and especially also intensity of labour is produced than in\n independent craft or even simple cooperation.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"13.43","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0044","text":"That\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"329\", \"facs\": \"#f0348\"} --> only the socially necessary labour time required for its production\n is expended upon a commodity appears in commodity pro-\n duction in general as the external compulsion of competition, because, superficially\n expressed, every individual producer must sell the commodity at its\n market price. Here in manufacture, by contrast, the delivery of a given quantity of products in a given\n labour time becomes a technological\n law of the production process itself [Note: Yet manufacturing enterprise attains this result in many\n branches only imperfectly, because it does not know how to control the general chemical and physical\n conditions of the production process with certainty.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"13.44","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0045","text":"Different operations, however, require unequal lengths of time\n and therefore yield unequal quantities of partial products in equal periods of time. If, then, the same worker is to perform always only the same operation day in, day out, different proportional numbers of workers must be employed for different operations, e.g. 4 casters\n and 2 breakers-off to one rubber in a type manufactory, where the\n caster casts 2,000 types hourly, the breaker-off breaks off 4,000, and\n the rubber polishes 8,000. Here the principle of cooperation returns\n in its simplest form, the simultaneous employment of many who do\n the same thing, but now as the expression of an organic relation.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"13.45","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0046","text":"The manufacturing division of labour thus not only simplifies and\n multiplies the qualitatively different organs of the social collective worker, but also creates a mathematically fixed relation for the quantitative extent of these organs, i.e.\n for the relative number of workers or relative size of the worker\n groups in each special function. Along with the qualitative articulation, it develops the quantitative rule and proportionality of the social labour process.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"13.46","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0047","text":"If the most appropriate proportional number of the different groups of\n partial workers has been empirically established for a particular scale\n of production, this scale can be extended only by employing a multiple of each particular group of workers [Note: “When experience, according to the particular nature of the products of each\n manufactory, has taught both the most advantageous way of dividing manufacture into partial operations\n and also the number of workers necessary for it, [then] . It\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"330\", \"facs\": \"#f0349\"} --> must be added that the same individual performs certain tasks just as well on\n a larger as on a smaller scale, e.g. the work of supervision,\n the transport of the partial products from one phase of production to another,\n etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"13.47","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0048","text":"The rendering independent of these functions, or their assignment\n to particular workers, therefore becomes advantageous only with an increase in the number\n of workers employed, but this increase must at once affect all\n groups proportionately.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"13.48","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0049","text":"The individual group, as a number of workers performing the same\n partial function, consists of homogeneous elements\n and forms a simple organ of the collective mechanism. In different\n manufactories, however, the group itself is an articulated labouring body, while the collective mechanism is formed by the repetition or multiplication of these productive elementary organisms. Let us take, for example, the manufactory of glass bottles. It\n falls into three essentially distinct phases. First, the preparatory phase, such as the preparation of the glass composition, the mixing of\n sand, lime, etc., and the melting of this composition into a liquid\n glass mass [Note: In England the melting furnace is separate from the glass furnace at which the glass\n is worked; in Belgium, for example, the same furnace serves both processes.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"13.49","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0050","text":"In this first phase different partial workers are employed,\n likewise in the final phase, the removal of the bottles\n from the drying ovens, their sorting, packing, etc. Between\n the two phases, in the middle, stands glass-making proper, or\n the working of the liquid glass mass. At the same mouth of a glass\n furnace works a group which in England is called the “hole,” and\n is composed of a bottle maker or finisher, a blower, a gatherer, a\n putter up or whetter off, and a taker in. These five partial workers form just as many special organs of a single labouring body,\n which can operate only as a unity, hence only through the immediate cooperation of the\n five. If one member of the five-part body is lacking, it is\n paralysed.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"13.50","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0051","text":"But the same glass furnace has different openings, in Eng-\n [Note: all establishments which do not employ an exact multiple of this number manufacture with less\n economy . . . . This is one of the causes of the colossal expansion\n of industrial establishments.” (Ch. Babbage: “On the Economy of\n Machinery.” 2nd ed. Lond. 1832, ch. XX.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"331\", \"facs\": \"#f0350\"} --> land, for example, 4–6, each of which contains an earthen melting-pot with liquid\n glass, and each of which employs its own worker-group of the same five-\n membered form. The articulation of each individual group rests here directly upon the division of labour, while the bond\n between the different homogeneous groups is simple cooperation,\n which consumes one of the means of production, here the glass furnace, more economically through common\n consumption.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"13.51","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0052","text":"Such a glass furnace with its 4–6\n groups constitutes a glassworks, and a glass manufactory comprises a\n plurality of such works, together with the apparatuses and workers\n for the introductory and concluding phases of production.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"13.52","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0053","text":"Finally, the manufactory, just as it partly arises from the combination\n of different crafts, can develop into a combination of different\n manufactories. The larger English glassworks, for example, manu-\n facture their earthen melting-pots themselves, because the success\n or failure of the product essentially depends on their quality. Here the manufactory of a means of production is combined with the manufactory of the product.\n Conversely, the manufactory of the product can be combined with manu-\n factories in which it itself again serves as raw material, or with whose\n products it is later combined. Thus one finds, for example, the\n manufactory of flint glass combined with glass-cutting and brass-\n founding, the latter for the metallic mounting of diverse glass articles.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"13.53","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0054","text":"The different combined manufactories then form more or less\n spatially separate departments of an overall manufactory, and at the same time production processes\n independent of one another, each with its own division of\n labour. Despite many technical and economic advantages\n offered by the combined manufactory, it gains, on its own basis,\n no genuinely technological unity. This arises only upon its trans-\n formation into machine production.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"13.54","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0055","text":"The manufacturing period, which soon expressly enunciates the reduction of the labour-time necessary for commodity production as a conscious principle [Note: This may be seen, among other things, from the writings of W. Petty, John\n Bellers, Andrew Yarranton, “The Advantages of the East India Trade,”\n and J. Vanderlint.] , also develops sporadically the use of machinery,\n especially for certain simple initial processes, which are to be performed on a massive scale\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"332\", \"facs\": \"#f0351\"} --> and with great expenditure of force. Thus, for example, in\n paper manufacture the grinding up of rags is soon performed by paper-\n mills, and in metal works the crushing of ores by so-called\n stamping mills [Note: Even towards the end of the 16th century France still uses mortars and\n sieves for stamping and washing ores.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"13.55","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0056","text":"The elementary form of all machinery\n had been handed down by the Roman Empire in the watermill [Note: The entire history of the development of machinery can be traced in\n the history of grain mills. In English, the factory is still called a\n mill. In German technological writings from the first decades\n of the 19th century, one still finds the expression Mühle not only for all machinery driven by\n natural forces, but even for all manufactories that employ\n machine-like apparatuses.] . The\n handicraft period bequeathed the great inventions of the compass,\n gunpowder, printing, and the automatic clock. On the\n whole, however, machinery plays the subordinate role which\n Adam Smith assigns to it alongside the division of labour [Note: As will be seen more closely from Book Four of this work,\n A.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"13.56","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0057","text":"Smith did not establish a single new proposition concerning the division of labour.\n But what characterises him as the summarising political economist of the manufacturing period is the emphasis he places on the division of\n labour. The subordinate role he assigns to machinery provoked, at the beginning of large-scale industry, Lauderdale’s, and in a more developed\n epoch Ure’s, polemic. A. Smith also confuses the differentiation\n of instruments, in which the partial workers of manufacture themselves were very active, with the invention of machines. It is not manufacturing workers,\n but scholars, craftsmen, even peasants (Brindley), etc., who play a role here.] .\n The sporadic application of machinery became very important in the\n 17th","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"13.57","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0058","text":"century, because it offered the great mathematicians of that time practical points of support and incentives for the creation of modern mechanics.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"13.58","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0059","text":"The specific machinery of the manufacturing period\n remains the collective worker himself, combined out of many partial workers.\n The different operations which the producer of a commodity performs in alternation and which intertwine in the totality of his labour process,\n make different demands upon him. In one he must\n develop more strength, in another more dexterity, in the third\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"333\", \"facs\": \"#f0352\"} --> more mental attention, etc., and the same individual does not possess\n these qualities in the same degree. Following the separation, rendering independent, and isolation of the different operations,\n the workers are divided, classified, and\n grouped according to their predominant qualities.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"13.59","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0060","text":"If their natural peculiarities form the basis upon which\n the division of labour is grafted, manufacture, once intro-\n duced, develops labour-powers which by nature are fit only for one-sided special functions. The collective worker now possesses all productive qualities in an equally high degree of virtuosity and at the same time expends them in the most economical manner, by employing all his organs, individualised in particular\n workers or groups of workers, exclusively for their specific func-\n tions [Note: “By dividing the work into several different operations,\n each of which demands different degrees of dexterity and strength, the\n manufacturer can procure exactly the quantity of strength\n and dexterity corresponding to each operation.”","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"13.60","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0061","text":"“If, on the other hand, the whole work had\n to be performed by one worker, the same individual would have to possess enough dexterity for the most delicate and enough strength for the most laborious operations.” (Ch. Bab-\n bage l. c. ch. XIX.)] . The one-sidedness and even the imperfection\n of the partial worker become his perfection as a member of the collective\n worker [Note: E.g. one-sided muscular development, distortion of bones, etc.] . The habit of a one-sided function transforms him\n into its naturally sure-working organ, while the connection of the collective mechanism compels him to function with the regularity of a machine part [Note: Mr Wm.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"13.61","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0062","text":"Marschall, the general manager of a\n glass manufactory, answers very correctly to the question of the investigating commissioner as to how industriousness is maintained among the boys employed: “They cannot well\n neglect their work; when they once begin, they must go on; they are just\n the same as parts of a machine.” (“Child. Empl. Comm. Fourth\n Report” 1865, p. 247.)] . Since the different functions of the collective\n worker are simpler or more complex, lower or higher,\n his organs, the individual labour-powers, require very different\n degrees of training and therefore possess very different values. Manufacture thus develops a hierarchy of labour-powers,\n corresponding to which is a scale of wages.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"13.62","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0063","text":"If, on the one hand, the indi-\n vidual worker is appropriated to a one-sided function and annexed for\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"334\", \"facs\": \"#f0353\"} --> life, then, just as much, the different labour operations are adapted to that hierarchy of natural and acquired skills [Note: Dr. Ure, in his apotheosis of large-scale industry, brings out the distinctive\n characteristics of manufacture more sharply than earlier economists,\n who did not have his polemical interest, and even more sharply than his contemporaries, e.g.\n Babbage, who, though decidedly superior to him as a learned mathematician and\n mechanic, nevertheless conceives large-scale industry essentially only from the standpoint of\n manufacture.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"13.63","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0064","text":"Ure remarks: “The appropriation of workers to\n each special operation constitutes the essence of the distribution of labour.” On the other hand,\n he describes this distribution as “the adaptation of labour to the\n different individual abilities,” and finally characterises the entire\n manufacturing system as “a system of gradations according to the rank of skill,” as “a division of labour according to the different degrees of skill,” etc. Ure l. c. vol. I, pp. 28–35 passim.] . Every production process, however, requires certain simple manipulations, of which every human being, as he goes and stands,\n is capable. They too are now detached from their fluid connection with\n the more contentful moments of activity and ossified into exclu-\n sive functions.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"13.64","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0065","text":"Manufacture therefore produces, in every craft that it takes hold of, a class of so-called unskilled workers, whom the handicraft establishment strictly excluded. While it develops the wholly one-sided speciality, at the expense of the entire capacity for labour, into virtuosity, it also begins to make the lack of all development into a speciality. Alongside the hierarchical gradation appears the simple division of workers into skilled and unskilled. For the latter, the costs of apprenticeship disappear altogether; for the former, they fall, in comparison with the handicraftsman, as a consequence of the simplified function. In both cases the value of labour-power falls [Note: “A worker, by perfecting himself through practice in one and the same point, becomes … less costly.” (l. c. p. 28.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"13.65","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0066","text":"An exception occurs insofar as the decomposition of the labour process produces new combining functions, which did not occur at all in the handicraft establishment, or not to the same extent. The relative depreciation of labour-power, arising from the abolition or reduction of the costs of apprenticeship, directly entails a higher valorisation of capital, for everything that shortens the time necessary for the reproduction of\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"335\", \"facs\": \"#f0354\"} --> labour-power lengthens the domain of surplus-labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"13.66","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0067","text":"We first considered the origin of manufacture from co-operation, then its simple elements, the detail worker and his tool, and finally its total mechanism. We now briefly touch upon the relation between the manufacturing division of labour and the social division of labour, which forms the general basis of all commodity production.","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"13.67","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0068","text":"If one considers labour itself alone, one may designate the separation of social production into its great genera, such as agriculture, industry, etc., as division of labour in general; the differentiation of these branches of production into species and subspecies as division of labour in particular; and the division of labour within a workshop as division of labour in the singular [Note: “The division of labour proceeds from the separation of the most diverse professions to that division in which several workers share in the manufacture of one and the same product, as in manufacture.” (Storch: “Cours d’Econ. Pol.” Paris edition, t. I, p. 173.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"13.68","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0069","text":"“Among peoples who have reached a certain degree of civilisation, we encounter three kinds of divisions of industry: the first, which we call general, brings about the distinction of producers into agriculturists, manufacturers, and merchants; it relates to the three principal branches of national industry; the second, which might be called special, is the division of each kind of industry into species . . . . the third division of industry, finally, which should be termed the division of tasks or labour properly speaking, is that which is established in the separate arts and trades … which is established in most manufactures and workshops.” (Skarbek l. c. p. 84, 86.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"13.69","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0070","text":"The division of labour within society, and the corresponding restriction of individuals to particular occupational spheres, develops, like the division of labour within manufacture, from opposite points of departure. Within a family, and, more developed, within a tribe, a natural division of labour arises from differences of sex and age, thus on a purely physiological basis; with the expansion of the community, the increase of population, and in particular the conflict between different tribes and the subjugation of one tribe by\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"336\", \"facs\": \"#f0355\"} --> another, it extends its material.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"13.70","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0071","text":"On the other hand, as I remarked earlier, exchange of products arises at the points where different families, tribes, communities come into contact, for at the beginnings of culture it is not private persons but families, tribes, etc., that confront one another independently. Different communities find different means of production and different means of subsistence in their natural environment. Their mode of production, mode of life, and products are therefore different. It is this natural difference which, when communities come into contact, brings forth the exchange of their reciprocal products and therefore the gradual transformation of these products into commodities.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"13.71","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0072","text":"Exchange does not create the difference between the spheres of production, but brings the different ones into relation with one another and thus transforms them into branches, more or less dependent on one another, of a social total production. Here the social division of labour arises through the exchange of originally different but mutually independent spheres of production. There, where the physiological division of labour forms the point of departure, the particular organs of an immediately coherent whole detach themselves from one another, decompose—a process of decomposition to which the exchange of commodities with foreign communities gives the principal impetus—and become independent up to the point at which the connection between the different labours is mediated by the exchange of products as commodities.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"13.72","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0073","text":"In the one case, it is the loss of independence of what was formerly independent; in the other, the acquisition of independence by what was formerly dependent.","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"13.73","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0074","text":"The basis of every developed division of labour mediated by commodity exchange is the separation of town and country [Note: Sir James Steuart has treated this point best. How little his work, which appeared ten years before the “Wealth of Nations,” is known nowadays may be seen, among other things, from the fact that the admirers of Malthus do not even know that, in the first edition of his writing on “Population,” apart from the purely declamatory part, he copied almost exclusively Steuart, besides the parsons Wallace and Townsend.] . One may say that the entire economic history of society is summarised in the movement of this opposition, which, however, we shall not pursue further here.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"13.74","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0075","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"337\", \"facs\": \"#f0356\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"13.75","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0076","text":"Just as a certain number of workers employed simultaneously forms the material prerequisite for the division of labour within manufacture, so, for the division of labour within society, does the size and density of the population, which here takes the place of agglomeration in the same workshop [Note: “There is a certain density of population which is convenient, both for social intercourse, and for that combination of powers by which the produce of labour is increased.” (James Mill l. c. p. 50.) “As the number of labourers increases, the productive power of society augments in the compound ratio of that increase, multiplied by the effects of the division of labour.” (Th. Hodgskin l. c. p. 125, 126.)] . Yet this density is something relative.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"13.76","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0077","text":"A relatively sparsely populated country with developed means of communication possesses a denser population than a more populous country with undeveloped means of communication; and in this sense, for example, the northern states of the American Union are more densely populated than India [Note: As a consequence of the great demand for cotton since 1861, cotton production was extended in some otherwise densely populated districts of East India at the expense of rice production. Partial famine consequently arose, because, owing to the lack of means of communication and hence to the lack of physical connection, the failure of the rice crop in one district could not be compensated by supplies from other districts.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"13.77","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0078","text":"Since commodity production and commodity circulation are the general prerequisite of the capitalist mode of production, the manufacturing division of labour requires a division of labour within society already matured to a certain degree of development. Conversely, the manufacturing division of labour develops and multiplies that social division of labour reactively. With the differentiation of instruments of labour, the trades which produce these instruments become increasingly differentiated [Note: Thus the manufacture of weavers’ shuttles already formed a particular branch of industry in Holland during the seventeenth century.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"13.78","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0079","text":"If the manufacturing establishment takes hold of a trade which previously was connected with others as a principal or subsidiary trade and was carried on by the same producer, separation and reciprocal independence immediately take place. If it takes hold of a particular stage of production of a commodity, its different stages of production are transformed into different independent trades. It has already been indicated that where the article is a whole composed merely mechanically of partial products, the detail labours can themselves again become independent as distinct handicrafts.\n[Running matter: I. 22]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"338\", \"facs\": \"#f0357\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"13.79","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0080","text":"In order to carry out the division of labour more completely within a manufacture, the same branch of production is split into different, in part wholly new, manufactures, according to the difference of its raw materials or the different forms which the same raw material may receive. Thus, already in the first half of the eighteenth century, more than 100 different kinds of silk fabrics were woven in France alone, and in Avignon, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"13.80","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0081","text":"it was law that “each apprentice must always devote himself to only one kind of manufacture and was not permitted to learn the making of several kinds of fabric at once.” The territorial division of labour, which confines particular branches of production to particular districts of a country, receives a new impetus from the manufacturing establishment, which exploits every peculiarity [Note: “Whether the Woollen Manufacture of England is not divided into several parts or branches appropriated to particular places, where they are only or principally manufactured; fine clothes in Somersetshire, coarse in Yorkshire, long ells at Exeter, soies at Sandbury, crapes at Norwich, linseys at Kendal, blankets at Whitney, and so forth!” (Berkeley: “The Querist” 1750, p. 520.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"13.81","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0082","text":"The expansion of the world market and the colonial system, which belong to the circle of its general conditions of existence, furnish the manufacture period with rich material for the division of labour within society. This is not the place to show further how, alongside the economic sphere, they infect every other sphere of society and everywhere lay the foundation for that development of professional specialisation, of specialities, and for a parcelisation of the human being, which already caused A. Ferguson, the teacher of A. Smith, to exclaim: “We are whole nations of Helots, and there are no free men among us” [Note: A. Ferguson: “History of Civil Society”, Part IV, ch. II.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"13.82","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0083","text":"Despite the numerous analogies and connections between the division of labour within society and the division within a workshop, the two differ not merely in degree but essentially. The analogy appears most striking, unquestionably, where an internal bond entwines different branches of business. The cattle breeder, for example, produces hides; the tanner transforms the hides into leather; the shoemaker transforms the leather into boots. Each here produces an intermediate product, and the final finished form is the combined product of their particular labours. In addition, there are the various branches of labour that furnish the cattle breeder, tanner, and shoemaker with means of production. One may now imagine with A.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"339\", \"facs\": \"#f0358\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"13.83","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0084","text":"Smith that this social division of labour differs from the manufacturing division only subjectively, namely for the observer, who here sees the various detail labours spatially together at a glance, whereas there their dispersion over large areas and the great number employed in each particular branch obscure the connection [Note: In manufactures proper, he says, the division of labour appears greater because “those employed in every different branch of the work can often be collected into the same workhouse, and placed at once under the view of the spectator.”","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"13.84","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0085","text":"“In those great manufactures (!), on the contrary, which are destined to supply the great wants of the great body of the people, every different branch of the work employs so great a number of workmen, that it is impossible to collect them all into the same workhouse … the division is not near so obvious.” (A. Smith: “Wealth of Nations”, b. I, ch. I.) The famous passage in the same chapter, which begins with the words: “Observe the accommodation of the most common artificer or day labourer in a civilized and thriving country etc.” and then proceeds to depict how innumerable and diverse trades co-operate to satisfy the needs of an ordinary worker, is copied almost verbatim from B. de Mandeville’s Remarks to his: “The Fable of the Bees, or Private Vices, Publick Benefits.” (First edition without Remarks 1706, with the Remarks 1714.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"13.85","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0086","text":"But what establishes the connection between the independent labours of cattle-breeder, tanner, shoemaker? The existence of their respective products as commodities. What, on the other hand, characterises the manufacturing division of labour? That the detail labourer produces no commodity [Note: “There is no longer anything which we can call the natural reward of\n individual labonr. Each labourer produces only some part of a whole, and each\n part, having no value or utility of itself, there is nothing on which the labourer\n can seize, and say: it is my product, this I will keep for myself.” (“Labour\n defended against the claims of Capital. Lond. 1825”, p. 25.) The author of this excellent work is the previously cited Th. Hodgskin.] . Only the collective product of the detail labourers is transformed into a commodity.","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"13.86","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0087","text":"[Running matter: 22*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"340\", \"facs\": \"#f0359\"} --> The division of labour within society is mediated by the purchase and sale of the products of different branches of labour; the connection of the partial labours in manufacture by the sale of different labour-powers to the same capitalist, who employs them as combined labour-power. The manufacturing division of labour presupposes concentration of the means of production in the hand of one capitalist; the social division of labour, fragmentation of the means of production among many commodity producers independent of one another.","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"13.87","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0088","text":"Instead of the brazen law of numerical proportion or proportionality subsuming definite masses of workers under definite functions in manufacture, chance and arbitrariness play their motley game in the distribution of commodity producers and their means of production among the different social branches of labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"13.88","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0089","text":"It is true that the different spheres of production constantly seek to establish themselves in equilibrium, since, on the one hand, every commodity producer must produce a use-value, and thus satisfy a particular social need, while the extent of these needs differs quantitatively and an inner bond links the different masses of needs into a naturally evolved system; and since, on the other hand, the law of value of commodities determines how much of its total available labour-time society can expend on the production of each particular kind of commodity. But this constant tendency of the different spheres of production to establish themselves in equilibrium asserts itself only as a reaction against the constant abolition of this equilibrium.","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"13.89","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0090","text":"In the division of labour within the workshop, numerical proportion governs a priori, as a consciously and systematically followed rule, the mass of workers assigned to each special function; in the division of labour within society, it operates only a posteriori as an inner, mute natural necessity, perceptible in the barometric fluctuations of market prices, overpowering the lawless arbitrariness of commodity producers.","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"13.90","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0091","text":"The manufacturing division of labour presupposes the unconditional authority of the capitalist over human beings who form mere members of an aggregate mechanism belonging to him; the social division of labour confronts independent commodity producers with one another, who recognise no other authority than that of competition, the compulsion exercised upon them by the pressure of their reciprocal interests, just as in the animal kingdom the bellum omnium contra omnes more or less preserves the conditions of existence of all species.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"341\", \"facs\": \"#f0360\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"13.91","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0092","text":"The same bourgeois consciousness which celebrates the manufacturing division of labour, the lifelong annexation of the worker to a detail operation, and the unconditional subordination of the detail labourers to capital, as an organisation of labour that increases its productive power, denounces just as loudly every conscious social control and regulation of the social process of production as an encroachment upon the inviolable rights of property, freedom, and the self-determining “genius” of the individual capitalist. It is highly characteristic that the enthusiastic apologists of the factory system know nothing worse to say against every general organisation of social labour than that it would transform the whole of society into a factory.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"13.92","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0093","text":"While the anarchy of the social and the despotism of the manufacturing division of labour condition one another in the society of the capitalist mode of production, earlier forms of society, on the contrary, in which the differentiation of trades develops naturally, then crystallises and finally becomes legally fixed, offer, on the one hand, the picture of a systematic and authority-governed organisation of social labour, while, on the other hand, they wholly exclude the division of labour within the workshop, or develop it only on a dwarfish scale, or only sporadically and accidentally [Note: “On peut … établir en règle générale, que moins l’autorité préside à\n la division du travail dans l’intérieur de la société, plus la division du travail se\n développe dans l’intérieur de l’atélier, et plus elle y est soumise à l’autorité d’un\n seul.”","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"13.93","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0094","text":"Thus authority in the workshop and that in society, with respect to the division of labour, are in inverse ratio to one another.” (Karl Marx, l. c. pp. 130, 131.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"13.94","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0095","text":"Those very ancient, small Indian communities, for example, some of which still continue to exist, rest upon common possession of the soil, upon the direct connection of agriculture and handicraft, and upon a fixed division of labour, which serves as a given plan and ground-plan when new communities are founded. They form self-sufficient wholes of production, whose productive territory varies from 100 to several 1000 acres. The principal mass of products is produced for the immediate self-requirement of the community, not as commodities, and production itself is therefore independent of the division of labour, mediated by commodity exchange, in the society of India as a whole.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"342\", \"facs\": \"#f0361\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"13.95","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0096","text":"Only the surplus of products is transformed into commodities, in part even only in the hand of the state, to which a definite quantum has flowed from time immemorial as rent in kind. In different parts of India this community has different prevailing forms. In the simplest form, the community cultivates the land in common and distributes its products among its members, while each family pursues spinning, weaving, etc., as a domestic subsidiary occupation.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"13.96","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0097","text":"Alongside this uniformly occupied mass we find the “chief inhabitant,” judge, police officer, and tax collector in one person; the bookkeeper, who keeps the accounts of agriculture and cadasters and registers everything relating to it; a third official, who pursues criminals and protects foreign travellers and escorts them from one village to another; the boundary-man, who guards the boundaries of the community against neighbouring communities; the water overseer, who distributes water from the common reservoirs for agricultural purposes; the Brahmin, who performs the functions of religious worship; the schoolmaster, who teaches the children of the community to write and read in the sand; the calendar Brahmin, who as astrologer indicates the times for sowing, harvesting, and the good or bad hours for all particular agricultural labours; a smith and a carpenter,","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"13.97","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0098","text":"who make and repair all agricultural implements; the potter, who makes all vessels for the village; the barber, the washerman for cleaning clothes, the silversmith, here and there the poet, who in some communities replaces the silversmith, in others the schoolmaster. These dozen persons are maintained at the expense of the whole community. If the population grows, a new community is settled on uncultivated soil after the model of the old one. Considered as an aggregate mechanism, the community displays here a systematic division of labour, but its manufacturing division is impossible, since the market for smith, carpenter, etc., remains unchanged and, at most, according to the difference in size of the villages, two or three smiths, potters, etc., occur instead of one [Note: Lieut. Col.","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"13.98","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0099","text":"Mark Wilks: “Historical Sketches of the\n South of India. Lond. 1810—17”, vol. I, pp. 118—20. A good compi-] . The law regulating the division of communal labour operates here with the inviolable authority of a natural law, while every particular craftsman, such as smith, etc., performs all operations belonging to his trade in his workshop in the traditional manner, but independently, and without recognising any authority whatsoever. The simple productive organism of these self-sufficient communities, which constantly reproduce themselves in the same form and, when accidentally destroyed, rebuild themselves in the same place, under the same name [Note: “Under this simple form … the inhabitants of the country have lived\n since time immemorial.”","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"13.99","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0100","text":"The boundaries of the villages have been but seldom\n altered; and though the villages themselves have been sometimes injured, and\n even desolated by war, famine, and disease, the same name, the same limits, the\n same interests, and even the same families, have continued for ages. The inhabi-\n tants give themselves no trouble about the breaking up and division of kingdoms;\n while the village remains entire, they care not to what power it is transferred or\n to what sovereign it devolves; its internal economy remains unchanged.” (Th.\n Stafford Raffles, late Lieut. Gov. of Java: “The History of Java.\n Lond. 1817”, vol. II, pp. 285, 286.)] , provides the key to the secret of the immutability of Asian societies, so strikingly contrasted by the constant dissolution and re-formation of Asian states and the restless change of dynasties.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"343\", \"facs\": \"#f0362\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"13.100","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0101","text":"The structure of the economic basic elements of society remains untouched by the storms of the political cloud region.","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"13.101","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0102","text":"The guild laws, as previously remarked, systematically prevented, by the most extreme limitation of the maximum number of journeymen that an individual guild-master might employ, his transformation into a capitalist. Likewise, he could employ journeymen only in the exclusive craft in which he himself was master. The guild jealously warded off every encroachment of merchant capital, the sole free form of capital that confronted it. The merchant could buy all commodities, but not labour as a commodity. He was only tolerated as a putting-out merchant of handicraft products.","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"13.102","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0103","text":"If external circumstances called forth a progressive division of labour, existing guilds split into sub-species or new guilds were placed alongside the old, [Note: A good compilation of the different forms of the Indian community is to be found\n in George Campbell’s: “Modern India. London 1852.”]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"344\", \"facs\": \"#f0363\"} --> but without combining different crafts in one workshop. The guild organisation, however much its differentiation, isolation, and development of trades belong to the material conditions of existence of the manufacturing period, therefore excluded the manufacturing division of labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"13.103","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0104","text":"By and large, the worker and his means of production remained united with one another, like the snail with its shell, and thus the first basis of manufacture was lacking, the autonomisation of the means of production as capital in opposition to the worker.","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"13.104","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0105","text":"While the division of labour in society as a whole, whether mediated or unmediated by commodity exchange, belongs to the most diverse economic formations of society, the manufacturing division of labour is an entirely specific creation of the capitalist mode of production.","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"13.105","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0106","text":"A larger number of workers under the command of the same capital forms the naturally evolved starting point both of co-operation in general and of manufacture. Conversely, the manufacturing division of labour develops the growth in the number of workers employed into a technological necessity. The minimum number of workers that an individual capitalist must employ is now prescribed to him by the existing division of labour. On the other hand, the advantages of further division are conditioned by a further increase in the number of workers, which can now be carried out only in multiples. But together with the variable component, the constant component of capital must also grow: alongside the extent of the common conditions of production, such as buildings, furnaces, etc., especially too, and much more rapidly than the number of workers, the raw material.","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"13.106","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0107","text":"Its mass, consumed in a given time by a given quantity of labour, increases in the same proportion as the productive power of labour in consequence of its division. A growing minimum magnitude of capital in the hands of the individual capitalists, or a growing transformation of the social means of subsistence and means of production into capital, is therefore a law arising from the technological character of manufacture [Note: “It is not sufficient that the capital necessary for the subdivision of crafts (should read: the means of subsistence and means of production necessary for this) be] .","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"13.107","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0108","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"345\", \"facs\": \"#f0364\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"13.108","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0109","text":"As in simple cooperation, in manufacture the functioning labouring body is a form of existence of capital. The social mechanism of production composed of many individual detail labourers belongs to the capitalist. The productive power arising from the combination of labours therefore appears as the productive power of capital. Manufacture proper not only subjects the formerly independent worker to the command and discipline of capital, but moreover creates a hierarchical articulation among the workers themselves. While simple cooperation leaves the mode of working of the individuals, by and large, unchanged, manufacture revolutionises it from the ground up and seizes individual labour-power at its root.","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"13.109","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0110","text":"It cripples the worker into an abnormality by forcing his detail dexterity in a hothouse manner through the suppression of a world of productive impulses and aptitudes, just as in the La Plata States a whole animal is slaughtered in order to obtain its hide or its tallow. The particular detail labours are not only distributed among different individuals, but the individual himself is divided, transformed into the automatic driving mechanism of a detail operation [Note: Dugald Stewart calls the manufacture workers “living automatons … employed in the details of the work.” (l. c. p. 318.)] and the absurd fable of Menenius Agrippa is realised, which represents a human being as a mere fragment of his own body [Note: Among corals, each individual does in fact form the stomach for the whole group.","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"13.110","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0111","text":"But it supplies it with nutritive matter, instead of carrying it away like the Roman l’atricier.] . If the worker originally sells his labour-power to capital because the material conditions of production of a commodity are lacking to him,\n[Note: is found available in society; it is also necessary that it should be accumulated in the hands of the entrepreneurs in sufficiently considerable masses to enable them to work on a large scale … The more division increases, the constant employment of the same number of workers requires ever more considerable capital in tools, raw materials, etc.” (Storch: “Cours d’Écon. Polit.” Paris ed. t. I, p. 250, 251.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":111,"address":"13.111","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0112","text":"“The concentration of the instruments of production and the division of labour are as inseparable from one another as, in the political regime, are the concentration of public powers and the division of private interests.” (Karl Marx l. c. p. 134.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"346\", \"facs\": \"#f0365\"} -->his individual labour-power now exists at all only when and insofar as it is sold to capital. It functions only in a connection which exists only after its sale, in the workshop of the capitalist.","kind":"passage","sequence":112,"address":"13.112","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0113","text":"By his natural constitution rendered incapable of making anything independent, the manufacture worker henceforth develops productive activity only as an accessory to the workshop of the capitalist [Note: “The worker who carries an entire trade in his arms can go anywhere to exercise his industry and find means of subsistence: the other (the manufacture worker) is only an accessory who, separated from his fellows, no longer has either capacity or independence, and who finds himself compelled to accept the law which it is thought fit to impose upon him.” (Storch l. c. Petersburg ed. 1815, t. I, p. 204.)] . Just as it was written upon the forehead of the chosen people that it was the property of Jehovah, so the division of labour stamps the manufacture worker with a mark that brands him as the property of capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":113,"address":"13.113","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0114","text":"The knowledge, insight, and will which the independent peasant or handicraftsman develops, even if only on a small scale, just as the savage practises all the art of war as personal cunning, are now required only for the whole of the workshop. The intellectual powers of production extend their scale on one side because they vanish on many sides. What the detail labourers lose is concentrated in capital confronting them [Note: A. Ferguson l. c. French trans. 1783, t. II, p. 135, 136. “One may have gained what the other has lost.”] . It is a product of the manufacturing division of labour to set the intellectual powers of the material process of production before them as alien property and as a power dominating them.","kind":"passage","sequence":114,"address":"13.114","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0115","text":"This process of separation begins in simple cooperation, where the capitalist represents, as against the individual workers, the unity and the will of the social labouring body. It develops in manufacture, which mutilates the worker into a detail labourer. It is completed in large-scale industry, which separates science from labour as an independent productive power and presses it into the service of capital [Note: “The man of knowledge and the productive worker are far separated from one another, and science, instead of increasing in the worker’s hand his own productive powers for himself, has almost everywhere confronted him—] .","kind":"passage","sequence":115,"address":"13.115","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0116","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"347\", \"facs\": \"#f0366\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":116,"address":"13.116","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0117","text":"In manufacture, the enrichment of the collective worker, and therefore of capital, in social productive power is conditioned by the impoverishment of the worker in individual productive powers. “Ignorance is the mother of industry as well as of superstition. Reflection and imagination are subject to error; but the habit of moving the foot or the hand depends neither on the one nor on the other. Thus one might say, with reference to manufactures, that their perfection consists in being able to dispense with the mind, in such a way that the workshop can be regarded as a machine whose parts are human beings” [Note: A. Ferguson l. c. p. 134, 135.] . In fact, in the middle of the 18th","kind":"passage","sequence":117,"address":"13.117","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0118","text":"century some manufactures preferred to employ half-idiots for certain simple operations which, however, constituted factory secrets [Note: J. D. Tuckett: “A History of the Past and Present State of the Labouring Population. Lond. 1846”, v. I, p. 149.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":118,"address":"13.118","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0119","text":"“The mind of the great majority of men,” says A. Smith, “necessarily develops out of and through their everyday occupations. A man who spends his whole life performing a few simple operations … has no opportunity to exercise his understanding … He generally becomes as stupid and ignorant as it is possible for a human creature to become.” After Smith has described the dullness of the detail labourer, he continues: “The uniformity of his stationary life naturally corrupts also the courage of his mind … It destroys even the energy of his body and renders him incapable of applying his strength vigorously and perseveringly except in the detail occupation for which he has been brought up. His skill in his particular trade thus seems to have been acquired at the expense of his intellectual, social, and military virtues.","kind":"passage","sequence":119,"address":"13.119","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0120","text":"But in every industrial and civilised society this is the condition into which the labouring poor, i.e. the great mass of the people, must necessarily fall” [Note: A. Smith: “Wealth of Nations.” B. V, ch. I, art. II. As a pupil of A. Ferguson, who had developed the detrimental consequences of the division of labour—] . In order\n[Note: placed . . . . Knowledge becomes an instrument, capable of being separated from labour and set against it.” (W. Thompson: “An Inquiry into the Principles of the Distribution of Wealth. Lond. 1824”, p. 274.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"348\", \"facs\": \"#f0367\"} -->to prevent the complete crippling of the mass of the people arising from the division of labour, A. Smith recommends public education at the expense of the state, though only in cautiously homeopathic doses.","kind":"passage","sequence":120,"address":"13.120","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0121","text":"His French translator and commentator, G. Garnier, who naturally emerged as a senator under the First French Empire, consequently polemicises against this. Public education violates the first laws of the division of labour, and with it “one would proscribe our whole social system”. “Like all other divisions of labour,” he says, “that between manual labour and intellectual labour [Note: Ferguson already says: “l’art de penser, dans un période où tout est séparé, peut lui-même former un métier à part.”] becomes more pronounced and more decided in proportion as society (he rightly uses this expression for capital, landed property, and their state) becomes richer.","kind":"passage","sequence":121,"address":"13.121","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0122","text":"Like every other, this division of labour is an effect of past and a cause of future progress … May the government then act against this division of labour and halt it in its natural course? May it use a part of the state revenue for the attempt to confuse and intermingle two classes of labour which strive for their division and separation” [Note: G. Garnier, t. V of his translation, p. 2—5.]","kind":"passage","sequence":122,"address":"13.122","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0123","text":"A certain intellectual and bodily crippling is inseparable even from the division of labour in the whole and on a large scale of society. But since the manufacturing period carries this social splitting-up of branches of labour much further, while, on the other hand, only with the division peculiar to it does it seize the individual at the root of his life,\n[Note: developed, A. Smith was [Editorial alternatives: sic = throughly; corr = thoroughly] clear on this point. At the beginning of his work, where the division of labour is celebrated ex professo, he indicates it only in passing as a source of social inequalities. Only in Book 5, on state revenue, does he reproduce Ferguson. In “Misère de la Philosophie” I have given what is necessary concerning the historical relation of Ferguson, A.","kind":"passage","sequence":123,"address":"13.123","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0124","text":"Smith, Lemontey, and Say in their critique of the division of labour, and there also first presented the manufacturing division of labour as a specific form of the capitalist mode of production. (l. c. p. 122 sq.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"349\", \"facs\": \"#f0368\"} -->it also first supplies the material and the impetus for industrial pathology [Note: Ramazzini, professor of practical medicine at Padua, published in 1713 his work: “De morbis artificum”, translated into French in 1781, reprinted in 1841 in the “Encyclopédie des Sciences Médicales. 7me Dis. Auteurs Classiques.” The period of large-scale industry has naturally greatly augmented his catalogue of workers’ diseases. See among others:\nHygiène physique et morale de l’ouvrier dans les grandes villes\n en général, et dans la ville de Lyon en particulier. Par le Dr.\n A. L. Fonterel.","kind":"passage","sequence":124,"address":"13.124","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0125","text":"Paris 1858” and “The diseases peculiar to different estates, ages, and sexes. 6 volumes. Ulm 1860.” In the year 1854 the Society of Arts appointed a commission of inquiry into industrial pathology. The list of documents collected by this commission is to be found in the catalogue of the “Twickenham Economic Museum.” Also very important are the official Reports of the “Board of Health”.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":125,"address":"13.125","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0126","text":"“To subdivide a man is to execute him, if he deserves the sentence; to assassinate him, if he does not deserve it. The subdivision of labour is the assassination of a people” [Note: “To subdivide a man is to execute him, if he deserves the sentence, to assassinate him if he does not … The subdivision of labour is the assassination of a people.” (D. Urquhart: “Familiar Words. London 1855”, p. 119.) Hegel had very heretical views about the division of labour. “Among educated people one may first understand those who can do everything that others do,” he says in his Philosophy of Right.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":126,"address":"13.126","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0127","text":"Cooperation based on division of labour, or manufacture, is in its beginnings a naturally grown formation of capitalist production. As soon as it has acquired some consistency and breadth of existence, it becomes a conscious, planned, and systematic form of the capitalist mode of production. The history of manufacture proper shows how the division of labour peculiar to it initially acquires its appropriate forms empirically, as it were behind the backs of the persons acting, but then, like guild handicraft, strives to hold fast traditionally to the form once found, and in individual cases holds fast to it for centuries. If this form changes, then, except in minor matters, always only as a consequence of a revolution in the instruments of labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":127,"address":"13.127","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0128","text":"Modern manufacture—I am not speaking here of large-scale industry based on machinery—either finds, as, for example, clothing manufacture does in the large towns where it arises,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"350\", \"facs\": \"#f0369\"} -->the disjecta membra poetae already ready-made and has only to collect them from their dispersion, or the principle of division lies close at hand, in that the various operations of handicraft production (for example, in bookbinding) are simply appropriated exclusively by particular workers. In such cases, not even a week’s experience is required to find the proportional number of hands necessary for each function [Note: The cosy belief in the inventive genius which the individual capitalist exercises a priori in the division of labour is now found only among German professors, such as Mr. Roscher, for","kind":"passage","sequence":128,"address":"13.128","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0129","text":"example, who dedicates “diverse wages” in thanks to the capitalist, from whose Jupiter-head the division of labour springs forth fully formed. The greater or lesser application of the division of labour depends on the length of the purse, not on the magnitude of genius.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":129,"address":"13.129","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0130","text":"The manufacturing division of labour creates, through the analysis of handicraft activity, the specification of the instruments of labour, the formation of the detail labourers, their grouping and combination in an overall mechanism, the qualitative articulation and the quantitative proportionality of social processes of production, thus a definite organisation of social labour, and thereby simultaneously develops a new, social productive power of labour. As the specifically capitalist form of the social process of production—and upon the foundations it found, it could develop only in the capitalist form—it is only a particular method of producing relative surplus-value, or of increasing the self-valorisation of capital—what is called social wealth, “Wealth of Nations,” etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":130,"address":"13.130","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0131","text":"—at the expense of the labourers. It develops the social productive power of labour not only for the capitalist, instead of for the labourer, but through the crippling of the individual labourer. It produces new conditions for the domination of capital over labour. If, therefore, on the one hand it appears as historical progress and a necessary moment of development in the economic formative process of society, it appears, on the other hand, as a means of civilised and refined exploitation.","kind":"passage","sequence":131,"address":"13.131","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0132","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"351\", \"facs\": \"#f0370\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":132,"address":"13.132","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0133","text":"Political economy, which emerges as an independent science only in the manufacturing period, considers the social division of labour in general only from the standpoint of the manufacturing division of labour [Note: The older writers, such as Petty, “Advantages of the East India Trade,” etc., fix the manufacturing division more than A. Smith as the capitalist form of production.], as a means of producing more commodities with the same quantity of labour, hence of cheapening commodities and accelerating the accumulation of capital. In the strictest contrast to this accentuation of quantity and exchange-value, the writers of classical antiquity adhere exclusively to quality and use-value [Note: Among the moderns, an exception is formed by several writers of the 18th","kind":"passage","sequence":133,"address":"13.133","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0134","text":"century, who with regard to the division of labour almost merely repeat the ancients, such as Beccaria and James Harris. Thus Beccaria: “Ciascuno prova coll’ esperienza, che applicando la mano e l’ingegno sempre allo stesso genere di opere e di produtte, egli più facili, più abbondanti e migliori ne traca resultati, di quello che se ciascuno isolatamente le cose tutte a se necessarie soltanto facesse .... Dividendosi in tal maniera per la comune e privata utilità gli uomini in varie classe e condizioni.” (Cesare Beccaria: “Elementi di Econ. Publica,” ed. Custodi, Part. Moderna t. XI, p. 28.) James Harris, later Earl of Malmesbury, famous for the “Diaries” concerning his embassy to Petersburg, himself says in a note to his Dialogue concerning Happiness. Lond. 1741, later reprinted in “Three Treatises etc. 3rd ed. Lond.","kind":"passage","sequence":134,"address":"13.134","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0135","text":"1772”: “The whole argument, to prove society natural (namely through the ‘division of employments’) is taken from the second book of Plato’s republic.”]. As a consequence of the separation of the social branches of production, commodities are made better, the various drives and talents of men choose spheres of activity corresponding to them [Note: Thus in the Odyssey XIV, 228: “Ἄλλος γάϱ τ᾽ ἄλλοισιν ἀνὴϱ ἐπιτέϱπεται ἔϱγοις” and Archilochus in Sextus Empiricus: “ἄλλος ἄλλῳ ἐπ᾽ ἔϱγῳ ϰαϱδίην ἰαίνεται.”], and without restriction nothing significant can be accomplished anywhere [Note: “Πολλ᾽ ἠπίστατο ἔϱγα, ϰαϰῶς δ᾽ ἠπίστατο πάντα.” Yet, however superior the Athenian felt as a commodity producer to the Spartan, because the latter could indeed dispose of men in war, but not of money, as Thucydides makes Pericles say in the speech in which he incites the Athenians to the","kind":"passage","sequence":135,"address":"13.135","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0136","text":"Peloponnesian war: “σώμασί τε ἑτοιμότεϱοι οἱ αὐτουϱγοὶ τῶν]. Thus product and producer are improved through the\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"352\", \"facs\": \"#f0371\"} --> division of labour. If the growth of the mass of products is occasionally mentioned, it is only with reference to the greater abundance of use-value. Not a syllable is said of exchange-value, of the cheapening of commodities. This standpoint of use-value prevails both with Plato [Note: Plato develops the division of labour within the commonwealth from the multiplicity of needs and the one-sidedness of the aptitudes of individuals. His main point is that the labourer must conform to the work, not the work to the labourer, which is unavoidable if he pursues different arts simultaneously, thus one or another as a subsidiary occupation.","kind":"passage","sequence":136,"address":"13.136","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0137","text":"“Οὐ γὰϱ ἐϑέλει τὸ πϱαττόμενον τὴν τοῦ πϱάττοντος σχολὴν πεϱιμένειν, ἀλλ᾽ ἀνάγϰη τὸν πϱάττοντα τῷ πϱαττομένῳ ἐπαϰολουϑεῖν μὴ ἐν παϱέϱγου μέϱει. Ἀνάγϰη. Ἐϰ δὴ τούτων πλείω τε ἕϰαστα γίγνεται ϰαὶ ϰάλλιον ϰαὶ ῥᾷον, ὅταν εἷς ἓν ϰατὰ φύσιν ϰαὶ ἐν ϰαιϱῷ, σχολὴν τῶν ἄλλων ἄγων, πϱάττῃ.” (Rep. l. 2. ed. Baiter, Orelli etc.) Similarly in Thucydides l. c. c.","kind":"passage","sequence":137,"address":"13.137","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0138","text":"42: “Seamanship is an art just as much as anything else and cannot be pursued as a subsidiary occupation in cases that may arise, but rather no other thing can be pursued alongside it as a subsidiary occupation.” If the work must wait, says Plato, for the labourer, the critical moment of production is often missed and the work spoiled, “ἔϱγου ϰαιϱὸν διόλλυται.” The same Platonic idea is found again in the protest of the English bleaching-factory owners against the clause of the Factory Act that fixes a definite meal-time for all labourers.","kind":"passage","sequence":138,"address":"13.138","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0139","text":"Their business cannot be made to conform to the labourers, for “in the various operations of singeing, washing, bleaching, mangling, calendering, and dyeing, none of them can be stopped at a given moment without risk of damage … to enforce the same dinner hour for all the work people might occasionally subject valuable goods to the risk of danger for incomplete operations.” Le platonisme où va-t-il se nicher!] as with Xenophon [Note: Xenophon relates that it is not only honourable to receive food from the table of the Persian king, but that this food is also much more savoury than other food. “And this is nothing wonderful, for just as the other arts are especially perfected in the great cities, so royal foods too are prepared in a quite special manner.","kind":"passage","sequence":139,"address":"13.139","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0140","text":"For in the small cities the same man makes bedstead, door, plough, table; often he additionally builds houses and] who, with his characteristically bourgeois instinct, comes closer to the division of labour within a workshop.\n[Note: ἀνϑϱώπων ἢ χϱήμασι πολεμεῖν“ (Thuc. l. I, c. 41), there also remained for them in material production αὐταϱϰεία, which stands opposed to the division of labour, as the ideal, “παϱ᾽ ὧν γὰϱ τὸ εὖ, παϱὰ τούτων ϰαὶ τὸ αὔταϱϰες.” It must be considered in this connection that even at the time of the overthrow of the 30 Tyrants there were not 5,000 Athenians without landed property.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"353\", \"facs\": \"#f0372\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":140,"address":"13.140","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0141","text":"Plato’s Republic, insofar as the division of labour is developed in it as the formative principle of the state, is only an Athenian idealisation of the Egyptian caste system, just as Egypt counts as the model industrial country also for other contemporaries of his, e.g. for Isocrates [Note: “He (Busiris) divided all into separate castes … commanded that the same persons should always conduct the same businesses, because he knew that those who change their occupations become thoroughly proficient in no business; but those who remain constantly at the same occupations bring each one to the highest perfection.","kind":"passage","sequence":141,"address":"13.141","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0142","text":"Indeed, we shall also find that in relation to arts and crafts they surpassed their rivals more than otherwise the master surpasses the bungler; and in relation to the arrangement by which they preserved kingship and the rest of their constitution, they are so excellent that the most renowned philosophers who undertake to speak of them praised the constitution of Egypt above others.” (Isocr. Busiris, c. 8.)], and retained this significance even for the Greeks of the Roman imperial period [Note: cf. Diod. Sic.].","kind":"passage","sequence":142,"address":"13.142","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0143","text":"During the manufacturing period proper, i.e. the period in which manufacture is the dominant form of the capitalist mode of production, the full execution of its own tendencies encounters manifold obstacles. Although, as we have seen, alongside the hierarchical articulation of the labourers it creates a simple separation between skilled and unskilled labourers, the number of the latter remains very restricted by the preponderant influence of the former. Although it adapts the special operations to the different degrees of maturity, strength, and development [Note: is content if he himself finds such a clientele sufficient for his maintenance. It is purely impossible for a man who does so many different things to do everything well. In the great cities, however, where every individual finds many buyers, even one trade is sufficient to support its man.","kind":"passage","sequence":143,"address":"13.143","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0144","text":"Indeed, it often does not even require a whole trade: one makes men’s shoes, another women’s shoes. Here and there one lives solely by sewing, another by cutting shoes; one merely cuts out garments, another only joins the pieces together. Now it is necessary that the performer of the simplest labour should also do it unconditionally best. The same holds for cookery.” (Xen. Cyrop. l. VIII, c. 2.) The quality of use-value to be achieved is here fixed exclusively, although Xenophon already knows the scale of the division of labour to depend on the extent of the market.]\n[Running matter: I.","kind":"passage","sequence":144,"address":"13.144","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0145","text":"23]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"354\", \"facs\": \"#f0373\"} --> of their living organs of labour, and therefore presses towards the productive exploitation of women and children, this tendency, by and large, fails because of the habits and resistance of the male labourers. Although the decomposition of handicraft activity lowers the costs of training and therefore the value of the labourers, a longer period of apprenticeship remains necessary for more difficult detail work and is maintained even where it is superfluous, jealously by the labourers. Thus, for example, in England we find the laws of apprenticeship with their seven-year period of training in full force until the end of the manufacturing period, and only cast overboard by large-scale industry.","kind":"passage","sequence":145,"address":"13.145","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0146","text":"Since handicraft skill remains the foundation of manufacture, and the overall mechanism functioning within it possesses no objective skeleton independent of the labourers themselves, capital continually struggles with the insubordination of the labourers. “The weakness of human nature,” exclaims Friend Ure, “is so great that the more skilled the labourer is, the more self-willed and difficult to manage he becomes, and consequently, through his mulish caprices, inflicts grave damage upon the overall mechanism” [Note: Ure l. c. t. I, p. 31.]. Throughout the whole manufacturing period there thus runs the complaint about the labourers’ lack of discipline [Note: What is said in the text applies much more to England than to France, and more to France than to Holland.]. And if we did not have the testimony of contemporary writers, the simple facts that from the 16th","kind":"passage","sequence":146,"address":"13.146","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0147","text":"century down to the epoch of large-scale industry capital fails to take possession of the entire available working-time of manufacturing labourers, that manufactures are short-lived and abandon their seat in one country with the immigration or emigration of the labourers and establish it in another, would speak libraries. “Order must be established in one way or another,” exclaims in 1770 the repeatedly cited author of the “Essay on Trade and Commerce.” Order, it echoes back 66 years later from the mouth of Dr Andrew Ure, “order” was lacking in the manufacture based upon “the scholastic dogma of the division of labour,” and “Arkwright created order.”","kind":"passage","sequence":147,"address":"13.147","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0148","text":"At the same time, manufacture could neither seize social production throughout its whole extent nor revolutionise it in its depth. It\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"355\", \"facs\": \"#f0374\"} --> culminated as an economic work of art upon the broad foundation of urban handicraft and rural domestic subsidiary industry. At a certain degree of development, its own narrow technological basis came into contradiction with the needs of production it had itself created.","kind":"passage","sequence":148,"address":"13.148","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0149","text":"One of its most perfected creations was the workshop for the production of the instruments of labour themselves, and in particular also of the more complicated mechanical apparatuses already in use. “Such a workshop,” says Ure, “offered the eye the division of labour in its manifold gradations. Drill, chisel, lathe each had its own labourers, hierarchically arranged according to the degree of their skill.” This product of the manufacturing division of labour in turn produced—machines. They abolish handicraft activity as the regulating principle of social production. Thus, on the one hand, the technological ground for the life-long annexation of the labourer to a partial function is removed. On the other hand, the barriers which this same principle still imposed upon the domination of capital fall away.","kind":"passage","sequence":149,"address":"13.149","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3TDA:P0149"]}],"source_digest":"94499864662d937f54e64a329bc423b842c392e61074a818b944331d25b7c5e7","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG","title":"4) Maschinerie und grosse Industrie. · reading part 1/2","order":14,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_4MUG","text":"## 4) Maschinerie und grosse Industrie.\n\n\n\n\nJohn Stuart Mill sagt in seinen „Principien der politischen\n Oekonomie“: „Es ist fraglich, ob alle bisher gemachten mechanischen\n Erfindungen die Tagesmühe irgend eines menschlichen Wesens erleichtert\n haben“ [Note: „It is questionable, if all the mechanical inventions yet made have\n lightened the day’s toil of any human being.“ Mill hätte sagen sollen „of any\n human being not fed by other people’s labour“, denn die Maschinerie hat un-\n streitig die Zahl der vornehmen Müssiggänger sehr vermehrt.] . Solches ist jedoch auch keineswegs der Zweck der kapita-\n listisch verwandten Maschinerie. Gleich jeder andern Entwicklung der\n Produktivkraft der Arbeit soll sie Waaren verwohlfeilern und den Theil\n des Arbeitstags, den der Arbeiter für sich selbst braucht, verkürzen,\n um den andern Theil seines Arbeitstags, den er dem Kapitalisten umsonst\n giebt, zu verlängern. Sie ist Mittel zur Produktion von Mehr-\n werth.\n\n\n\n\nDie Umwälzung der Produktionsweise geht in der Manufaktur von der\nArbeitskraft aus, in der grossen Industrie vom Arbeitsmittel. Es\n ist also zunächst zu untersuchen, wodurch sich das Arbeitsmittel aus einem\n Werkzeug in eine Maschine verwandelt, oder wodurch sich die Maschine\n vom Handwerksinstrument unterscheidet. Es handelt sich hier nur um\n [Running matter: 23*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"356\", \"facs\": \"#f0375\"} --> grosse, allgemeine Charakterzüge, denn abstrakt strenge Grenzlinien schei-\n den ebensowenig die Epochen der Gesellschafts- wie die der Erdge-\n schichte.\n\n\n\n\nMathematiker und Mechaniker — und man findet diess hier und da\n von englischen Oekonomen wiederholt — erklären das Werkzeug für eine\n einfache Maschine und die Maschine für ein zusammengesetztes Werkzeug.\n Nach ihnen findet hier also kein wesentlicher Unterschied statt. In die-\n sem Sinn heissen sogar die einfachen mechanischen Potenzen, wie Hebel,\n schiefe Ebne, Schraube, Keil u. s. w. Maschinen [Note: Sieh z. B. „Hutton’s Course of Mathematics“.] . Diess ist richtig\n vom mathematischen Standpunkt, denn jede Maschine besteht aus jenen\n einfachen Potenzen, wie immer verkleidet und kombinirt. Vom ökono-\n mischen Standpunkt taugt die Erklärung nichts, denn ihr fehlt das Ent-\n scheidende, das historische Element. Andrerseits sucht man den Unter-\n schied zwischen Werkzeug und Maschine darin, dass beim Werkzeug\n der Mensch die Bewegungskraft, bei der Maschine eine von der mensch-\n lichen verschiedne Naturkraft, wie Thier, Wasser, Wind u. s. w. [Note: „Von diesem Gesichtspunkt aus lässt sich denn auch eine scharfe Grenze\n zwischen Werkzeug und Maschine ziehen: Spaten, Hammer, Meisel u. s. w.,\n Hebel- und Schraubenwerke, für welche, mögen sie übrigens noch so künstlich\n sein, der Mensch die bewegende Kraft ist … diess alles fällt unter den Begriff\n des Werkzeugs; während der Pflug mit der ihn bewegenden Thierkraft, Wind-\n u. s. w. Mühlen zu den Maschinen zu zählen sind.“ (Wilhelm Schulz:\n „Die Bewegung der Produktion. Zürich 1843“, p. 38.) Eine in\n mancher Hinsicht lobenswerthe Schrift.] \n Danach wäre ein mit Ochsen bespannter Pflug, der den verschiedensten\n Produktionsepochen angehört, eine Maschine, Claussen’s Circular Loom, der,\n von der Hand eines einzigen Arbeiters bewegt, 96,000 Maschen in einer\n Minute verfertigt, ein blosses Werkzeug. Ja, derselbe loom wäre Werk-\n zeug, wenn mit der Hand, und Maschine, wenn mit Dampf bewegt. Da\n die Anwendung von Thierkraft eine der ältesten Erfindungen der Mensch-\n heit, ginge in der That die Maschinenproduktion der Handwerksproduktion\n voraus. Als John Wyalt 1735 seine Spinnmaschine und mit ihr die\n industrielle Revolution des 18. Jahrhunderts ankündigte, erwähnte er mit\n keinem Wort, dass statt eines Menschen ein Esel die Maschine treibe, und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"357\", \"facs\": \"#f0376\"} --> dennoch fiel diese Rolle dem Esel zu. Eine Maschine, „um ohne\n Finger zu spinnen,“ lautete sein Programm [Note: Schon vor ihm wurden, wenn auch sehr unvollkommene, Maschinen zum\n Vorspinnen angewandt, wahrscheinlich zuerst in Italien. Eine kritische Ge-\n schichte der Technologie würde überhaupt nachweisen, wie wenig irgend\n eine Erfindung des 18. Jahrhunderts einem einzelnen Individuum gehört. Bisher\n existirt kein solches Werk. Darwin hat das Interesse auf die Geschichte der\n natürlichen Technologie gelenkt, d. h. auf die Bildung der Pflanzen- und Thier-\n organe als Produktionsinstrumente für das Leben der Pflanzen und Thiere. Ver-\n dient die Bildungsgeschichte der produktiven Organe des Gesellschaftsmenschen,\n der materiellen Basis jeder besondern Gesellschallsorganisation, nicht gleiche Auf-\n merksamkeit? Und wäre sie nicht leichter zu liefern, da, wie Vico sagt, die\n Menschengeschichte sich dadurch von der Naturgeschichte unterscheidet, dass wir\n die eine gemacht und die andere nicht gemacht haben? Die Technologie enthüllt\n das aktive Verhalten des Menschen zur Natur, den unmittelbaren Produktionspro-\n zess seines Lebens, damit auch seiner gesellschaftlichen Lebensverhältnisse und der\n ihnen entquellenden geistigen Vorstellungen. Selbst alle Religionsgeschichte, die\n von dieser materiellen Basis abstrahirt, ist — unkritisch. Es ist in der That viel\n leichter durch Analyse den irdischen Kern der religiösen Nebelbildungen zu finden,\n als umgekehrt aus den jedesmaligen wirklichen Lebensverhältnissen ihre verhim-\n melten Formen zu entwickeln. Die letztere ist die einzig materialistische und\n daher wissenschaftliche Methode. Die Mängel des abstrakt naturwissenschaftlichen\n Materialismus, der den geschichtlichen Prozess ausschliesst, ersieht man\n schon aus den abstrakten und ideologischen Vorstellungen seiner Wortführer, so\n bald sie sich über ihre Specialität hinauswagen.] .\n\n\n\n\nAlle entwickelte Maschinerie besteht aus drei wesentlich verschie-\n denen Theilen, der Bewegungsmaschine, dem Transmissions-\n mechanismus, endlich der Werkzeugmaschine oder Arbeits-\n maschine. Die Bewegungsmaschine wirkt als Triebkraft des ganzen\n Mechanismus. Sie erzeugt ihre eigne Bewegungskraft, wie die Dampf-\n maschine, kalorische Maschine, elektro-magnetische Maschine u. s. w., oder\n sie empfängt den Anstoss von einer Naturkraft ausser ihr, wie das Wasser-\n rad vom Wassergefäll, der Windflügel vom Wind u. s. w. Der Trans-\n missionsmechanismus, zusammengesetzt aus Schwungrädern, Treibwellen,\n Zahnrädern, Kreiselrädern, Schäften, Schnüren, Riemen, Zwischengeschirr\n und Vorgelege der verschiedensten Art, regelt die Bewegung, verwandelt,\n wo es nöthig, ihre Form, z. B. aus einer perpendikulären in eine kreis-\n förmige, vertheilt und überträgt sie auf die Werkzeugmaschinerie. Beide\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"358\", \"facs\": \"#f0377\"} --> Theile des Mechanismus sind nur vorhanden, um der Werkzeugmaschine\n die Bewegung mitzutheilen, wodurch sie den Arbeitsgegenstand anpackt\n und zweckgemäss verändert. Dieser Theil der Maschinerie, die Werk-\n zeugmaschine ist es, wovon die industrielle Revolution im 18. Jahr-\n hundert ausgeht. Sie bildet noch jeden Tag von neuem den Ausgangs-\n punkt, so oft Handwerksbetrieb oder Manufakturbetrieb in Maschinenbe-\n trieb übergeht.\n\n\n\n\nSehn wir uns nun die Werkzeugmaschine oder eigentliche\nArbeitsmaschine näher an, so erscheinen im Grossen und Ganzen,\n wenn auch oft in sehr modificirter Form, die Apparate und Werkzeuge\n wieder, womit der Handwerker oder Manufakturarbeiter arbeitet, aber\n statt als Werkzeuge des Menschen jetzt als Werkzeuge eines Mechanismus\n oder als mechanische Werkzeuge. Entweder ist die ganze Maschine nur\n eine mehr oder minder veränderte mechanische Ausgabe des alten Hand-\n werksinstruments, wie bei dem mechanischen Webstuhl [Note: Namentlich in der ursprünglichen Form des mechanischen Webstuhls er-\n kennt man den alten Webstuhl auf den ersten Blick wieder. Wesentlich verändert\n erscheint er in seiner modernen Form.] , oder die am\n Gerüst der Arbeitsmaschine angebrachten thätigen Organe sind alte Be-\n kannte, wie Spindeln bei der Spinnmaschine, Nadeln beim Strumpfwirker-\n stuhl, Sägeblätter bei der Sägemaschine, Messer bei der Zerhackmaschine\n u. s. w. Der Unterschied dieser Werkzeuge von dem eigentlichen\n Körper der Arbeitsmaschine erstreckt sich bis auf ihre Geburt. Sie wer-\n den nämlich immer noch grossentheils handwerksmässig oder manufaktur-\n mässig producirt und später erst an den maschinenmässig producirten\n Körper der Arbeitsmaschine befestigt [Note: Erst seit ungefähr 15 Jahren wird ein stets wachsender Theil der Werk-\n zeuge der Arbeitsmaschinen maschinenmässig in England fabricirt, obgleich nicht\n von denselben Fabrikanten, welche die Maschinen selbst machen. Maschinen zur\n Fabrikation solcher mechanischen Werkzeuge sind z. B. die automatic bobbin-making\n engine, card-setting engine, Maschinen zum Machen der Weberlitzen, Maschinen\n zum Schmieden von mule und throstle Spindeln.] . Die Werkzeugmaschine ist\n also ein Mechanismus, der, nach Mittheilung der entsprechenden Bewe-\n gung, mit seinen Werkzeugen dieselben Operationen verrichtet, welche früher\n der Arbeiter mit ähnlichen Werkzeugen verrichtete. Ob die Triebkraft\n nun vom Menschen ausgeht oder selbst wieder von einer Maschine, ändert\n am Wesen der Sache nichts. Nach Uebertragung des eigentlichen Werk-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"359\", \"facs\": \"#f0378\"} --> zeugs vom Menschen auf einen Mechanismus, tritt eine Maschine an die\n Stelle eines blossen Werkzeugs. Der Unterschied springt sofort ins\n Auge, auch wenn der Mensch selbst noch der erste Motor bleibt. Die\nAnzahl von Arbeitsinstrumenten, womit er gleichzeitig wirken\n kann, ist durch die Anzahl seiner natürlichen Produktionsinstrumente, sei-\n ner eignen körperlichen Organe, beschränkt. Man versuchte in Deutsch-\n land erst einen Spinner zwei Spinnräder treten, ihn also gleichzeitig mit\n zwei Händen und zwei Füssen arbeiten zu lassen. Diess war zu an-\n strengend. Später erfand man ein Tret-Spinnrad mit zwei Spindeln, aber\n die Spinnvirtuosen, die zwei Fäden gleichzeitig spinnen konnten, waren\n fast so selten als zweiköpfige Menschen. Die Jenny spinnt dagegen von\n vorn herein mit 12—18 Spindeln, der Strumpfwirkerstuhl strickt mit viel\n 1000 Nadeln auf einmal u. s. w. Die Anzahl der Werkzeuge, womit\n dieselbe Werkzeugmaschine gleichzeitig spielt, ist von vorn herein eman-\n cipirt von der organischen Schranke, wodurch das Handwerkszeug eines\n Arbeiters beengt wird.\n\n\n\n\nAn vielem Handwerkszeug besitzt der Unterschied zwischen dem\n Menschen als blosser Triebkraft und als Arbeiter mit dem eigentlichen Opera-\n teur eine sinnlich besonderte Existenz. Z. B. beim Spinnrad wirkt der Fuss\n nur als Triebkraft, während die Hand, die an der Spindel arbeitet,\n zupft und dreht, die eigentliche Spinnoperation verrichtet. Grade diesen\n letzten Theil des Handwerksinstruments ergreift die industrielle Revolution\n zuerst und überlässt dem Menschen, neben der neuen Arbeit die Maschine\n mit seinem Auge zu überwachen und ihre Irrthümer mit seiner Hand zu\n verbessern, zunächst noch die rein mechanische Rolle der Triebkraft.\n Werkzeuge dagegen, auf die der Mensch von vorn herein nur als einfache\n Triebkraft wirkt, wie z. B. beim Drehn der Kurbel einer Mühle [Note: Moses von Aegypten sagt: „Du sollst dem Ochsen, der drischt, nicht\n das Maul verbinden.“ Die christlich germanischen Philanthropen legten dagegen\n dem Leibeignen, den sie als Triebkraft zum Mahlen verwandten, eine grosse höl-\n zerne Scheibe um den Hals, damit er kein Mehl mit der Hand zum Mund bringen könne.] , bei\n Pumpen, beim Auf- und Abbewegen der Arme eines Blasbalgs, beim\n Stossen eines Mörsers u. s. w., rufen zwar zuerst die Anwendung von\n Thieren, Wasser, Wind [Note: Theils Mangel an lebendigem Wassergefäll, theils Kampf gegen sonstigen\n Wasserüberfluss zwangen die Holländer zur Anwendung des Winds als Triebkraft.]  als Bewegungskräften hervor. Sie recken sich,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"360\", \"facs\": \"#f0379\"} --> theilweise innerhalb, sporadisch schon lange vor der Manufakturperiode,\n zu Maschinen, aber sie revolutioniren die Produktionsweise nicht. Dass\n sie selbst in ihrer handwerksmässigen Form bereits Maschinen sind, zeigt\n sich in der Periode der grossen Industrie. Die Pumpen z. B., womit die\n Holländer 1836—37 den See von Harlem auspumpten, waren nach dem\n Princip gewöhnlicher Pumpen konstruirt, nur dass cyklopische Dampf-\n maschinen statt der Menschenhände ihre Kolben trieben. Der gewöhn-\n liche und sehr unvollkommene Blasbalg des Grobschmidts wird noch zu-\n weilen in England durch blosse Verbindung seines Arms mit einer Dampf-\n maschine in eine mechanische Luftpumpe verwandelt. Die Dampfmaschine\n selbst, wie sie Ende des 17. Jahrhunderts, während der Manufakturperiode,\n erfunden ward und bis zum Anfang der 80er Jahre des 18. Jahrhunderts\n fortexistirte [Note: Sie wurde zwar schon sehr verbessert durch Watt’s erste, sogenannte\n einfach wirkende Dampfmaschine, blieb aber in dieser Form blosse Hebemaschine\n für Wasser und Salzsoole. Man kann nicht einmal sagen, dass die Dampfmaschine\n in dieser ersten Form wissenschaftlich bedeutenden Anstoss gab.] , rief keine industrielle Revolution hervor. Es war viel-\n mehr umgekehrt die Schöpfung der Werkzeugmaschinen, welche die revo-\n lutionirte Dampfmaschine nothwendig und darum möglich machte. Sobald\n der Mensch, statt mit dem Werkzeug auf den Arbeitsgegenstand, nur noch\n als Triebkraft auf eine Werkzeugmaschine wirkt, wird die Verkleidung der\n Triebkraft in menschliche Muskel zufällig und kann Wind, Wasser,\n Dampf u. s. w. an die Stelle treten. Diess schliesst natürlich nicht aus,\n dass solcher Wechsel oft grosse technische Aenderungen des ursprünglich\n für menschliche Triebkraft allein konstruirten Mechanismus bedingt. Heut-\n zutage werden alle Maschinen, die sich erst Bahn brechen müssen, wie\n Nähmaschinen, Brodbereitungsmaschinen u. s. w., wenn sie den kleinen Mass-\n stab nicht von vorn herein durch ihre Bestimmung ausschliessen, für mensch-\n liche und rein mechanische Triebkraft zugleich konstruirt.\n\n\n\n\n [Note: Die Windmühle selbst erhielten sie aus Deutschland, wo diese Erfindung einen\n artigen Kampf zwischen Adel, Pfaffen und Kaiser hervorrief, wem denn von den\n drei der Wind „gehöre“. Luft macht eigen, hiess es in Deutschland, während\n der Wind Holland frei machte. Was er hier eigen machte, war nicht den Hollän-\n der, sondern den Grund und Boden für den Holländer. Noch 1836 wurden\n 12,000 Windmühlen von 6000 Pferdekraft in Holland verwandt, um zwei Dritt-\n theile des Lands vor Rückverwandlung in Morast zu schützen.] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"361\", \"facs\": \"#f0380\"} -->\n\nDie Maschine, wovon die industrielle Revolution ausgeht, ersetzt den\n Arbeiter, der ein einzelnes Werkzeug handhabt, durch einen Mechanismus,\n der mit einer Masse derselben oder gleichartiger Werkzeuge auf einmal\n operirt, und von einer einzigen Triebkraft, welches immer ihre Form, be-\n wegt wird [Note: „Die Vereinigung aller dieser einfachen Instrumente, durch einen einzigen\n Motor in Bewegung gesetzt, bildet eine Maschine.“ (Babbage l. c.)] . Hier haben wir die Maschine, aber erst als einfaches\n Element der maschinenmässigen Produktion.\n\n\n\n\nDie Erweiterung des Umfangs der Arbeitsmaschine und der Zahl\n ihrer gleichzeitig operirenden Werkzeuge bedingt einen massenhafteren Be-\n wegungsmechanismus, und dieser Mechanismus, zur Ueberwältigung seines\n eignen Widerstands, eine mächtigere Triebkraft als die menschliche, ab-\n gesehn davon, dass der Mensch ein sehr unvollkommenes Produktionsin-\n strument gleichförmiger und kontinuirlicher Bewegung ist. Vorausgesetzt,\n dass er nur noch als einfache Triebkraft wirkt, also an die Stelle seines\n Werkzeugs eine Werkzeugmaschine getreten ist, können Naturkräfte ihn\n jetzt auch als Triebkraft ersetzen. Von allen aus der Manufakturperiode\n überlieferten grossen Bewegungskräften war die Pferdekraft die schlech-\n teste, theils weil ein Pferd seinen eignen Kopf hat, theils wegen seiner\n Kostspieligkeit und des beschränkten Umfangs, worin es in Fabriken allein\n anwendbar ist [Note: John C. Morton verlas Januar 1861 in der Society of Arts einen\n Aufsatz über „die in der Agrikultur angewandten Kräfte“. Es heisst darin u. a.:\n „Jede Verbesserung, welche die Gleichförmigkeit des Grund und Bodens fördert,\n macht die Dampfmaschine zur Erzeugung rein mechanischer Kraft anwendbarer\n … Pferdekraft wird erheischt, wo krumme Hecken und andere Hindernisse\n gleichförmige Aktion verhindern. Diese Hindernisse schwinden täglich mehr. In\n Operationen, die mehr Ausübung des Willens und weniger wirkliche Kraft erfor-\n dern, ist die durch den menschlichen Geist von Minute zu Minute gelenkte Kraft,\n also Menschenkraft, allein anwendbar.“ Herr Morton reducirt dann Dampfkraft,\n Pferdekraft und Menschenkraft auf die bei Dampfmaschinen gewöhnliche Mass-\n einheit, nämlich die Kraft 33,000 Pfund in der Minute um einen Fuss zu heben\n oder zu ziehen, und berechnet die Kost einer Dampfpferdekraft bei der Dampf-\n maschine auf 3 d., und beim Pferde auf 5½ d. per Stunde. Ferner kann das\n Pferd, bei voller Erhaltung seiner Gesundheit, nur 8 Stunden täglich angewandt\n werden. Durch Dampfkraft können mindestens 3 von je 7 Pferden auf bebautem\n Land während des ganzen Jahrs erspart werden, zu einem Kostenpreis, nicht\n grösser als dem der entlassenen Pferde während der 3 oder 4 Monate, wo sie] . Dennoch wurde das Pferd häufig während der Kinder-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"362\", \"facs\": \"#f0381\"} --> zeit der grossen Industrie angewandt, wie, ausser dem Jammer gleichzei-\n tiger Agronomen, schon der bis heute überlieferte Ausdruck der mecha-\n nischen Kraft in Pferdekraft bezeugt. Der Wind war zu unstät und unkon-\n trolirbar, und die Anwendung der Wasserkraft überwog ausserdem in Eng-\n land, dem Geburtsland der grossen Industrie, schon während der Manufak-\n turperiode. Man hatte bereits im 17. Jahrhundert versucht, zwei Läufer\n und also auch zwei Mahlgänge mit einem Wasserrad in Bewegung zu\n setzen. Der geschwollne Umfang des Transmissionsmechanismus gerieth\n aber jetzt in Konflikt mit der nun unzureichenden Wasserkraft und diess\n ist einer der Umstände, der zur genaueren Untersuchung der Friktionsge-\n setze trieb. Ebenso führte das ungleichförmige Wirken der Bewegungs-\n kraft bei Mühlen, die durch Stossen und Ziehen mit Schwengeln in Bewe-\n gung gesetzt wurden, auf die Theorie und Anwendung des Schwung-\n rads [Note: Faulhebr 1625, De Cous 1688.] , das später eine so wichtige Rolle in der grossen Industrie spielt.\n In dieser Art entwickelte die Manufakturperiode die ersten wissenschaft-\n lichen und technologischen Elemente der grossen Industrie. Ark-\n wright’s Throstlespinnerei wurde von vorn herein mit Wasser getrieben.\n Indess war auch der Gebrauch der Wasserkraft als herrschender Trieb-\n kraft mit erschwerenden Umständen verbunden. Sie konnte nicht beliebig\n erhöht und ihrem Mangel nicht abgeholfen werden, sie versagte zuweilen\n und war vor allem rein lokaler Natur [Note: Die moderne Erfindung der Turbinen befreit die industrielle Ausbeu-\n tung der Wasserkraft von vielen früheren Schranken.] . Erst mit Watt’s zweiter,\n s. g. doppelt wirkender Dampfmaschine war ein erster Motor\n gefunden, der seine Bewegungskraft selbst aus der Verspeisung von Koh-\n len und Wasser erzeugt, dessen Kraftpotenz ganz unter menschlicher Kon-\n trole steht, der mobil und ein Mittel der Lokomotion, städtisch, und nicht\n gleich dem Wasserrad ländlich, die Koncentration der Produktion in\n Städten erlaubt, statt sie wie das Wasserrad über das Land zu zer-\n [Note: allein wirklich vernutzt werden. In den Agrikulturoperationen, worin die Dampf-\n kraft angewandt werden kann, verbessert sie endlich, verglichen mit der Pferde-\n kraft, die Qualität des Machwerks. Um das Werk der Dampfmaschine zu ver-\n richten, müssten 66 Arbeiter per Stunde zu 15 sh., und um das der Pferde zu ver-\n richten, 32 Mann zu 8 sh. per Stunde angewandt werden.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"363\", \"facs\": \"#f0382\"} --> streuen [Note: „In the early days of textile manufactures, the locality of the factory de-\n pended upon the existence of a stream having a sufficient fall to turn a water\n wheel; and, although the establishment of the water-mills was the\n commencement of the breaking up of the domestic system of\n manufacture, yet the mills necessarily situated upon streams, and frequently\n at considerable distances the one from the other, formed part of a rural rather\n than an urban system; and it was not until the introduction of the steam-\n power as a substitute for the stream, that factories were congregated in towns and\n localities where the coal and water required for the production of steam were\n found in sufficient quantities. The steam-engine is the parent of manufacturing\n towns.“ (A. Redgrave in „Reports of the Insp. of Fact. 30. April\n 1860“. p. 36.)] , universell in seiner technologischen Anwendung, in seiner Resi-\n denz verhältnissmässig wenig durch lokale Umstände bedingt. Das grosse\n Genie Watt’s zeigte sich in der Specifikation des Patents, das er April\n 1784 nahm, und worin seine Dampfmaschine nicht als eine Erfindung zu\n besondern Zwecken, sondern als allgemeiner Agent der grossen\n Industrie geschildert wird. Er deutet hier Anwendungen an, wovon\n manche, wie z. B. der Dampfhammer, mehr als ein halbes Jahrhundert\n später erst eingeführt wurden. Jedoch bezweifelte er die Anwendbarkeit\n der Dampfmaschine auf Seeschifffahrt. Seine Nachfolger, Boulton und\n Watt, stellten 1851 die kolossalste Dampfmaschine für Ocean steamers auf\n der Londoner Industrieausstellung aus.\n\n\n\n\nNachdem erst die Werkzeuge aus Werkzeugen des menschlichen Orga-\n nismus in Werkzeuge eines mechanischen Apparats, der Werkzeugmaschine,\n verwandelt, erhielt nun auch die Bewegungsmaschine eine selbstständige, von\n den Schranken menschlicher Kraft völlig emancipirte Form. Damit sinkt\n die einzelne Werkzeugmaschine, die wir bisher betrachtet, zu einem blossen\nElement der maschinenmässigen Produktion herab. Eine Bewegungs-\n maschine konnte jetzt viele Arbeitsmaschinen gleichzeitig treiben. Mit\n der Anzahl der gleichzeitig bewegten Arbeitsmaschinen wächst die Bewe-\n gungsmaschine und dehnt sich der Transmissionsmechanismus zu einem\n weitläufigen Apparat aus.\n\n\n\n\nEs ist nun zweierlei zu unterscheiden, Cooperation vieler\n gleichartiger Maschinen und Maschinensystem.\n\n\n\n\nIn dem einen Fall wird das ganze Machwerk von derselben\n Arbeitsmaschine verrichtet. Sie führt alle die verschiedenen Operationen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"364\", \"facs\": \"#f0383\"} --> aus, welche ein Handwerker mit seinem Werkzeng, z. B. der Weber mit\n seinem Webstuhl verrichtete, oder welche Handwerker mit verschiednen\n Werkzeugen, sei es selbstständig oder als Glieder einer Manufaktur, der\n Reihe nach ausführten [Note: Vom Standpunkt der manufakturmässigen Theilung war Weben keine\n einfache, sondern vielmehr eine komplicirte handwerksmässige Arbeit, und so ist\n der mechanische Webstuhl eine Maschine, die sehr Mannigfaltiges verrichtet. Es\n ist überhaupt eine falsche Vorstellung, dass die moderne Maschinerie sich ur-\n sprünglich solcher Operationen bemächtigt, welche die manufakturmässige Thei-\n lung der Arbeit vereinfacht hatte. Spinnen und Weben wurden während der\n Manufakturperiode in neue Arten gesondert und ihre Werkzeuge verbessert und\n variirt, aber der Arbeitsprozess selbst, in keiner Weise getheilt, blieb handwerks-\n mässig. Es ist nicht die Arbeit, sondern das Arbeitsmittel, wovon die Maschine\n ausgeht.] . Z. B. in der modernen Manufaktur von Enve-\n loppes faltete ein Arbeiter das Papier mit dem Falzbein, ein andrer legte\n den Gummi auf, ein dritter schlug die Klappe um, auf welche die Devise\n aufgedrückt wird, ein vierter bossirte die Devise u. s. w. und bei jeder\n dieser Theiloperationen musste jede einzelne Enveloppe die Hände wech-\n seln. Eine einzige Enveloppemaschine verrichtet alle diese Operationen\n auf einen Schlag und macht 3000 und mehr Enveloppen in einer Stunde.\n Eine auf der Londoner Industrieausstellung von 1862 ausgestellte ameri-\n kanische Maschine zur Bereitung von Papiertuten schneidet das Papier,\n kleistert, faltet und vollendet 300 Stück per Minute. Der innerhalb der\n Manufaktur getheilte und in einer Reihenfolge ausgeführte Gesammtprozess\n wird hier von einer Arbeitsmaschine vollbracht, die durch Kombination\n verschiedner Werkzeuge wirkt. Ob nun eine solche Arbeitsmaschine nur\n mechanische Wiedergeburt eines komplicirteren Handwerkszeugs sei, oder\n Kombination verschiedenartiger manufakturmässig partikularisirter ein-\n facher Instrumente, in der Fabrik, der neuen Form der auf Maschi-\n nenbetrieb gegründeten Werkstatt, erscheint jedesmal die ein-\n fache Cooperation wieder, zunächst, wir sehn hier vom Arbeiter\n ab, als räumliche Konglomeration gleichartiger und\n gleichzeitig zusammenwirkender Arbeitsmaschinen.\n So wird eine Webfabrik durch das Nebeneinander vieler mechanischen\n Webstühle und eine Nähfabrik durch das Nebeneinander vieler\n Nähmaschinen in demselben Arbeitsgebäude gebildet. Aber es exi-\n stirt hier eine technologische Einheit, indem die vielen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"365\", \"facs\": \"#f0384\"} --> gleichartigen Arbeitsmaschinen gleichzeitig und gleichmässig ihren Impuls\n empfangen vom Herzschlag des gemeinsamen ersten Motors, auf sie über-\n tragen durch den Transmissionsmechanismus, der ihnen auch theilweis ge-\n meinsam ist, indem sich nur besondere Ausläufe davon für jede einzelne\n Werkzeugmaschine verästeln. Ganz wie viele Werkzenge die Organe\neiner Arbeitsmaschine, bilden viele Arbeitsmaschinen jetzt nur noch viele\ngleichartige Organe desselben Bewegungsmechanismus.\n\n\n\n\nEin eigentliches Maschinensystem tritt aber erst an die Stelle\n der einzelnen selbstständigen Maschine, wo der Arbeits-\n gegenstand eine zusammenhängende Reihe verschiedner Stufenprozesse,\n ausgeführt von einer Kette verschiedenartiger, aber mit einander\n kombinirter Werkzeugsmaschinen, durchläuft. Hier erscheint die der Ma-\n nufaktur eigenthümliche Cooperation durch Theilung der Arbeit wieder,\n aber jetzt als Kombination von Theilarbeitsmaschinen. Die\n specifischen Werkzeuge der verschiednen Theilarbeiter, in der Wollmanu-\n faktur z. B. von Wollschläger, Wollkämmer, Wollscheerer, Wollspinner\n u. s. w., verwandeln sich jetzt in die Werkzeuge spezificirter Arbeits-\n maschinen, von denen jede ein besondres Organ für eine besondre Funk-\n tion im System des kombinirten Werkzeugsmechanismus bildet. Die\n Manufaktur selbst liefert dem Maschinensystem in den Zweigen, worin es\n zuerst eingeführt wird, im Grossen und Ganzen die naturwüchsige\n Grundlage der Theilung und daher der Organisation des Produktionspro-\n zesses [Note: Vor der Epoche der grossen Industrie war die Wollmanufaktur die herr-\n schende Manufaktur Englands. In ihr wurden daher während der ersten Hälfte\n des 18. Jahrhunderts die meisten Experimente gemacht. Der Baumwolle, deren\n mechanische Verarbeitung minder mühvolle Vorbereitungen erfordert als die\n Schafwolle, kamen die an der letztern gemachten Erfahrungen zu gut, wie später\n umgekehrt die mechanische Wollindustrie sich auf Grundlage der mechanischen\n Baumwollspinnerei und -Weberei entwickelt. Einzelne Elemente der Wollmanu-\n faktur sind erst seit den letzten Decennien dem Fabriksystem einverleibt worden,\n z. B. das Wollkämmen. „The application of power to the process of combing\n wool … extensively in operation since the introduction of the „combing\n machine“, especially Lister’s . . . . undoubtedly had the effect of throwing a very\n large number of men out of work. Wool was formerly combed by hand, most\n frequently in the cottage of the comber. It is now very generally combed in the\n factory, and handlabour is superseded, except in some particular kinds of work,] . Indess tritt sofort ein wesentlicher Unterschied ein. Bei der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"366\", \"facs\": \"#f0385\"} --> Manufaktur muss jeder besondre Theilprozess von Arbeitern, einzeln oder\n in Gruppen, mit ihrem Handwerkszeug ausführbar sein. Wird der Arbei-\n ter dem Prozess angeeignet, so ist aber auch vorher der Prozess dem Arbeiter\n angepasst. Diess subjektive Princip der Theilung fällt weg für die maschi-\n nenartige Produktion. Der Gesammtprozess wird hier objektiv, an und\n für sich betrachtet, in seine konstituirenden Phasen analysirt, und das Pro-\n blem jeden Theilprozess auszuführen und die verschiednen Theilprozesse zu\n verbinden, durch technische Anwendung der Mechanik, Chemie u. s. w.\n gelöst [Note: „Le principe du système automatique est donc … de remplacer la\n division du travail parmi les artisans par l’analyse du procédé dans ses principes\n constituans.“ (Ure l. c. t. I, p. 30.)] , wobei natürlich nach wie vor die theoretische Konception durch\n gehäufte praktische Erfahrung auf grosser Stufenleiter vervollkommnet\n werden muss. Jede Theilmaschine liefert der zunächst folgenden ihr Roh-\n material, und da sie alle gleichzeitig wirken, befindet sich das Produkt\n eben so fortwährend auf den verschiednen Stufen seines Bildungsprozesses,\n wie im Uebergang aus einer Produktionsphase in die andre. Wie in\n der Manufaktur die unmittelbare Cooperation der Theilarbeiter bestimmte\nVerhältnisszahlen zwischen den besondern Arbeitergruppen schafft,\n so in dem gegliederten Maschinensystem die beständige Beschäftigung der\n Theilmaschinen durch einander ein bestimmtes Verhältniss zwischen\n ihrer Anzahl, ihrem Umfang und ihrer Geschwindigkeit. Die kombinirte\n Arbeitsmaschine, jetzt ein gegliedertes System von verschiedenartigen\n einzelnen Arbeitsmaschinen und von Gruppen derselben, ist um so voll-\n kommner, je kontinuirlicher ihr Gesammtprozess, d. h. mit je weniger\n Unterbrechung das Rohmaterial von seiner ersten Phase zu seiner letzten über-\n geht, je mehr also der Mechanismus selbst, statt der Menschenhand, es von\n einer Produktionsphase in die andre fördert. Wenn daher in der Manu-\n faktur die Isolirung der Sonderprozesse ein durch die Theilung der\n Arbeit selbst gegebnes Princip ist, so in der entwickelten Fabrik die\nKontinuität der Sonderprozesse.\n\n\n\n\n [Note: in which hand-combed wool is still preferred. Many of the handcombers found\n employment in the factories, but the produce of the handcomber bears so small a\n proportion to that of the machine, that the employment of a very large number of\n combers has passed away.“ („Rep. of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct.\n 1856“, p. 16.)] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"367\", \"facs\": \"#f0386\"} -->\n\nEin System der Maschinerie, beruhe es nun auf blosser Cooperation\ngleichartiger Arbeitsmaschinen, wie in der Weberei, oder auf einer\n Kombination verschiedenartiger, wie in der Spinnerei, bildet an und\n für sich einen grossen Automaten, sobald es von einem sich selbst\n bewegenden ersten Motor getrieben wird. Indess kann das ganze System\n z. B. von der Dampfmaschine getrieben werden, obgleich einzelne Werkzeug-\n maschinen entweder für gewisse Bewegungen noch den Arbeiter brauchen,\n wie die zum Einfahren der Mule nöthige Bewegung vor der Einführung\n der selfacting mule und immer noch bei Feinspinnerei, oder indem be-\n stimmte Theile der Maschine zur Verrichtung ihres Werks gleich einem\n Werkzeug vom Arbeiter gelenkt werden müssen, wie beim Maschinenbau\n vor der Verwandlung des slide rest (ein Drehapparat) in einen selfactor.\n Sobald die Arbeitsmaschine alle zur Bearbeitung des Rohstoffs nöthigen\n Bewegungen ohne menschliche Beihilfe verrichtet und nur noch mensch-\n licher Nachhilfe bedarf, haben wir ein automatisches System der\n Maschinerie, das indess beständiger Ausarbeitung im Detail fähig ist, wie\n z. B. ein Apparat, der die Spinnmaschine von selbst still setzt, sobald ein\n einzelner Faden reisst, und der selfacting stop, der den verbesserten\n Dampfwebstuhl still setzt, sobald der Spule des Weberschiffs der Ein-\n schlagsfaden ausgeht, ganz moderne Erfindungen sind. Als ein Beispiel\n sowohl der Kontinuität der Produktion als der Durchführung des automa-\n tischen Princips kann die moderne Papierfabrik gelten. An der Papier-\n produktion kann überhaupt der Unterschied verschiedner Produktions-\n weisen, auf Basis verschiedner Produktionsmittel, wie der Zusammenhang\n der gesellschaftlichen Produktionsverhältnisse mit dieser Produktionsweise,\n im Einzelnen vortheilhaft studirt werden, da uns die ältere deutsche\n Papiermacherei Muster der handwerksmässigen Produktion, Holland im\n 17. und Frankreich im 18. Jahrhundert Muster der eigentlichen Manufak-\n tur, und das moderne England Muster der automatischen Fabrikation in\n diesem Zweig liefern, ausserdem in China und Indien noch zwei ver-\n schiedne uraltasiatische Formen derselben Industrie existiren.\n\n\n\n\nAls gegliedertes System automatischer Arbeitsmaschinen, die ihre Be-\n wegung durch Transmissionsmaschinerie von einem centralen Automa-\n ten empfangen, besitzt der Maschinenbetrieb seine entwickeltste Gestalt.\n An die Stelle der einzelnen Maschine tritt hier ein mechanisches Ungeheuer,\n dessen Leib ganze Fabrikgebäude füllt, und dessen dämonische Kraft, erst\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"368\", \"facs\": \"#f0387\"} --> versteckt durch die fast feierlich gemessne Bewegung seiner Riesenglieder,\n im fieberhaft tollen Wirbeltanz seiner zahllosen eigentlichen Arbeits-\n organe ausbricht.\n\n\n\n\nEs gab Mules, Dampfmaschinen u. s. w., bevor es Arbeiter gab,\n deren ausschliessliches Geschäft es war, Dampfmaschinen, Mules u. s. w.\n zu machen, ganz wie der Mensch Kleider trug, bevor es Schneider gab.\n Die Erfindungen von Vauconson, Arkwright, Watt u. s. w. waren jedoch nur\n ausführbar, weil jene Erfinder ein von der Manufakturperiode fertig ge-\n liefertes und beträchtliches Quantum geschickter mechanischer Arbeiter\n vorfanden. Ein Theil dieser Arbeiter bestand aus selbstständigen Hand-\n werkern verschiedner Profession, ein andrer Theil war in Manufakturen ver-\n einigt, worin, wie früher erwähnt, die Theilung der Arbeit mit besondrer\n Strenge durchgeführt. Mit der Zunahme der Erfindungen und der wach-\n senden Nachfrage nach den neu erfundnen Maschinen entwickelte sich\n mehr und mehr einerseits die Sonderung der Maschinenfabrikation in man-\n nichfaltige selbstständige Zweige, andrerseits die Theilung der Arbeit im\n Innern der maschinenbauenden Manufakturen. Wir erblicken hier also\n die Manufaktur als unmittelbare technologische Grundlage der gros-\n sen Industrie. Jene producirte die Maschinerie, womit diese, in den\n Produktionssphären, die sie zunächst ergriff, den handwerks- und manu-\n fakturmässigen Betrieb aufhob. Der Maschinenbetrieb erhob sich also\nnaturwüchsig auf einer ihm unangemessnen materiellen\n Grundlage. Auf einem gewissen Entwicklungsgrad musste er diese\n erst fertig vorgefundne und dann in ihrer alten Form weiter ausgearbeitete\n Grundlage selbst revolutioniren und sich eine seiner eignen Produktions-\n weise entsprechende neue Basis schaffen. Wie die einzelne Maschine\n zwergmässig bleibt, so lange sie nur durch Menschen bewegt wird, wie\n das Maschinensystem sich nicht frei entwickeln konnte, bevor an die Stelle\n der vorgefundnen Triebkräfte, Thier, Wind und selbst Wasser, die\n Dampfmaschine trat, ebenso war die grosse Industrie in ihrer ganzen Ent-\n wicklung gelähmt, so lange ihr charakteristisches Produktionsmittel, die\n Maschine selbst, seine Existenz persönlicher Kraft und persönlichem Ge-\n schick verdankte, also abhing von der Muskelentwicklung, der Schärfe des\n Blicks, und der Virtuosität der Hand, womit der Theilarbeiter in der Ma-\n nufaktur und der Handwerker ausserhalb derselben ihr Zwerginstrument\n führten. Abgesehn von der Vertheurung der Maschinen in Folge ihrer\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"369\", \"facs\": \"#f0388\"} --> Ursprungsweise — und dieser Umstand beherrscht das Kapital als be-\n wusstes Motiv — blieb so die Ausdehnung der bereits maschinenmässig\n betriebenen Industrie und das Eindringen der Maschinerie in neue Produk-\n tionszweige rein bedingt durch das Wachsthum einer Arbeiterkategorie, die\n wegen der halbkünstlerischen Natur ihres Geschäfts nur allmälig und nicht\n sprungweis vermehrt werden konnte. Aber auf einer gewissen Entwick-\n lungsstufe gerieth die grosse Industrie auch technologisch in Wider-\n streit mit ihrer handwerks- und manufakturmässigen Unterlage. Die Aus-\n reckung des Umfangs der Bewegungsmaschinen, des Transmissionsmecha-\n nismus und der Werkzeugmaschinen, die grössere Komplikation, Mannich-\n faltigkeit und erforderte Regelmässigkeit ihrer Bestandtheile, im Masse wie\n die Werkzeugmaschine sich von dem handwerksmässigen Model, das ihren\n Bau ursprünglich beherrscht, losriss, und eine freie, nur durch ihre mecha-\n nische Aufgabe bestimmte Gestalt erhielt [Note: Der mechanische Webstuhl in seiner ersten Form besteht hauptsächlich\n aus Holz, der verbesserte, moderne aus Eisen. Wie sehr im Anfang die alte Form\n des Produktionsmittels seine neue Form beherrscht, zeigt u. a. die oberflächlichste\n Vergleichung des modernen Dampfwebstuhls mit dem alten, der modernen Blas-\n instrumente in Eisengiessereien mit der ersten unbehilflichen mechanischen Wieder-\n geburt des gewöhnlichen Blasbalgs, und vielleicht schlagender als alles andre eine\n vor der Erfindung der jetzigen Lokomotiven versuchte Lokomotive, die in der That\n zwei Füsse hatte, welche sie abwechselnd wie ein Pferd aufhob. Erst nach weiterer\n Entwicklung der Mechanik und gehäufter praktischer Erfahrung wird die Form\n gänzlich durch das mechanische Princip bestimmt und daher gänzlich emancipirt\n von der überlieferten Körperform des Werkzeugs, das sich zur Maschine entpuppt.] , die Ausbildung des automa-\n tischen Systems und die stets unvermeidlichere Anwendung von schwer zu\n bewältigendem Material, z. B. Eisen statt Holz — die Lösung aller dieser\n naturwüchsig entspringenden Aufgaben stiess überall auf die persönlichen\n Schranken, die auch das in der Manufaktur kombinirte Arbeiterpersonal nur\n dem Grad, nicht dem Wesen nach durchbricht. Maschinen z. B. wie die\n moderne Druckerpresse, der moderne Dampfwebstuhl und die moderne\n Kardirmaschine konnten nicht von der Manufaktur geliefert werden.\n\n\n\n\nDie Umwälzung der Produktionsweise in einer Sphäre der Industrie\n bedingt ihre Umwälzung in der andern. Es gilt diess zunächst für solche\n Industriezweige, welche, trotz der Isolation durch die gesellschaft-\n liche Theilung der Arbeit, so dass jeder derselben eine selbstständige\n [Running matter: I. 24] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"370\", \"facs\": \"#f0389\"} --> Waare producirt, sich dennoch als Phasen eines Gesammtprozesses ver-\n schlingen. So machte die Maschinenspinnerei Maschinenweberei nöthig\n und beide zusammen die mechanisch-chemische Revolution der Bleicherei,\n Druckerei und Färberei, wie andrerseits die Revolution in der Baumwoll-\n spinnerei die Erfindung des gin zur Trennung der Baumwollfaser vom\n Samen hervorrief, womit erst die Baumwollproduktion auf dem nun erheisch-\n ten grossen Massstab möglich ward [Note: Des Yankee Eli Whitney cottongin war bis zur neusten Zeit im\n wesentlichen weniger verändert worden, als irgend eine andre Maschine des 18.\n Jahrhunderts. Erst in den letzten Decennien hat ein andrer Amerikaner, Herr\n Emery von Albany, New-York, Whitney’s Maschine durch eine ebenso einfache als\n wirksame Verbesserung antiquirt.] . Die Revolution in der Pro-\n duktionsweise der Industrie und Agrikultur ernöthigte namentlich aber\n auch eine Revolution in den allgemeinen Bedingungen des gesell-\n schaftlichen Produktionsprozesses, d. h. den Kommunikations- und\n Transportmitteln. Wie die Kommunikations- und Transportmittel\n einer Gesellschaft, deren Pivôt, um mich eines Ausdrucks Fourier’s zu\n bedienen, die kleine Agrikultur mit ihrer häuslichen Nebenindustrie und das\n städtische Handwerk waren, den Produktionsbedürfnissen der Manufaktur-\n periode mit ihrer erweiterten Theilung der gesellschaftlichen Arbeit, ihrer\n Koncentration von Arbeitsmitteln und Arbeitern, und ihren Kolonialmärk-\n ten durchaus nicht genügen konnten, daher auch in der That umgewälzt\n wurden, so verwandelten sich die von der Manufakturperiode überlieferten\n Transport- und Kommunikationsmittel bald in unerträgliche Hemmschuhe\n für die grosse Industrie mit ihrer fieberhaften Geschwindigkeit der Produk-\n tion, ihrer massenhaften Stufenleiter, ihrem beständigen Werfen von Ka-\n pital- und Arbeitermassen aus einer Produktionssphäre in die andre,\n und ihren neugeschaffenen weltmarktlichen Zusammenhängen. Neben\n einem ganz umgewälzten Segelschiffbau, wurde das Kommunikations- und\n Transportwesen daher allmälig durch ein System von Flussdampfschiffen,\n Eisenbahnen, oceanischen Dampfschiffen und Telegraphen der Produktions-\n weise der grossen Industrie angeeignet. Die furchtbaren Eisenmassen\n aber, die jetzt zu schmieden, zu schweissen, zu schneiden, zu bohren und\n zu formen waren, erforderten ihrerseits cyklopische Maschinen, deren\n Schöpfung der manufakturmässige Maschinenbau versagte.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"371\", \"facs\": \"#f0390\"} -->\n\nDie grosse Industrie musste sich also ihres charakteristischen Produk-\n tionsmittels, der Maschine selbst bemächtigen, um Maschinen durch\n Maschinen zu produciren. So erst schuf sie sich eine adäquate\n technologische Unterlage und stellte sich auf ihre eignen Füsse. Mit dem\n wachsenden Maschinenbetrieb in den ersten Decennien des 19. Jahrhunderts\nbemächtigte sich die Maschinerie in der That allmälig der Fabri-\n kation der Werkzeugmaschinen, während erst in den letztverflosse-\n nen Decennien ungeheurer Eisenbahnbau und oceanische Dampfschifffahrt\n die in der Konstruktion von ersten Motoren angewandten cyklo-\n pischen Maschinen ins Leben riefen.\n\n\n\n\nDie wesentlichste Produktionsbedingung für die Fabrikation von\n Maschinen durch Maschinen war eine jeder Kraftpotenz fähige und doch\n zugleich ganz kontrolirbare Bewegungsmaschine. Sie existirte bereits\n in der Dampfmaschine. Aber es galt zugleich die für die einzelnen Ma-\n schinentheile nöthigen streng geometrischen Formen wie Linie, Ebene, Kreis,\n Cylinder, Kegel und Kugel maschinenmässig zu produciren. Diess Pro-\n blem löste Henry Maudsley im ersten Decennium des 19. Jahrhun-\n derts durch die Erfindung des slide-rest, der bald automatisch ge-\n macht und in modificirter Form von der Drechselbank, wofür er zuerst\n bestimmt war, auf andre Konstruktionsmaschinen übertragen wurde.\n Diese mechanische Vorrichtung ersetzt nicht irgend ein besondres Werk-\n zeug, sondern die menschliche Hand selbst, die eine bestimmte Form\n hervorbringt durch Vorhalten, Anpassen und Richtung der Schärfe von\n Schneideinstrumenten u. s. w. gegen oder über das Arbeitsmaterial, z. B.\n Eisen. So gelang es die geometrischen Formen der einzelnen Maschinen-\n theile „mit einem Grad von Leichtigkeit, Genauigkeit und Raschheit zu\n produciren, den keine gehäufte Erfahrung der Hand des geschicktesten\n Arbeiters verleihen konnte“ [Note: „The Industry of Nations. Lond. 1855,“ Part II, p. 239.\n Es heisst eben daselbst: „Simple and outwardly unimportant as this appendage\n to lathes may appear, it is not, we believe, averring too much to state, that its in-\n fluence in improving and extending the use of machinery has been as great as that\n produced by Watt’s improvements of the steam-engine itself. Its introduction went] .\n\n\n\n\nBetrachten wir nun in der zum Maschinenbau angewandten Ma-\n schinerie den Theil derselben, der die eigentliche Werkzeugmaschine\n [Running matter: 24*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"372\", \"facs\": \"#f0391\"} --> bildet, so erscheint das handwerksmässige Instrument wieder, aber in\n cyklopischem Umfang. Der Operateur der Bohrmaschine z. B. ist\n ein ungeheurer Bohrer, der durch eine Dampfmaschine getrieben wird und\n ohne den umgekehrt die Cylinder grosser Dampfmaschinen und hydrau-\n lischer Pressen nicht producirt werden könnten. Die mechanische Drechsel-\n bank ist die cyklopische Wiedergeburt der gewöhnlichen Fussdrechselbank,\n die Hobelmaschine ein eiserner Zimmermann, der mit denselben Werkzeugen\n in Eisen arbeitet, womit der Zimmermann in Holz; das Werkzeug, welches\n in den Londoner Schiffswerften das Furnirwerk schneidet, ist ein riesen-\n artiges Rasirmesser, das Werkzeug der Scheermaschine, welche Eisen\n schneidet, wie die Schneiderscheere Tuch, eine Monsterscheere, und der\n Dampfhammer operirt mit einem gewöhnlichen Hammerkopf, aber von\n solchem Gewicht, dass Thor selbst ihn nicht schwingen könnte [Note: Eine dieser Maschinen in London zum Schmieden von paddle-wheel shafts\n führt den Namen „Thor“. Sie schmiedet einen Schaft von 16½ Tonnen Gewicht\n mit derselben Leichtigkeit, wie der Schmied ein Hufeisen.] . Einer\n dieser Dampfhämmer z. B., die eine Erfindung von Nasmyth sind, wiegt\n über 6 Tonnen und stürzt mit einem perpendikulären Fall von 7 Fuss auf\n einen Amboss von 36 Tonnen Gewicht. Er pulverisirt spielend einen\n Granitblock, und ist nicht minder fähig einen Nagel in weiches Holz mit\n einer Aufeinanderfolge leiser Schläge einzutreiben [Note: Die in Holz arbeitenden Maschinen, die auch auf kleinem Massstab an-\n gewandt werden können, sind meist amerikanische Erfindung.] .\n\n\n\n\nDas Arbeitsmittel erhält in der Maschinerie eine materielle Existenz-\n weise, welche Ersetzung der Menschenkraft durch Naturkräfte und erfah-\n rungsmässiger Routine durch bewusste Anwendung der Naturwissenschaft\n bedingt. In der Manufaktur ist die Gliederung des gesellschaftlichen Ar-\n beitsprozesses rein subjektiv, Kombination von Theilarbeitern;\n im Maschinensystem schafft die grosse Industrie einen ganz objektiven\n Produktionsorganismus, den der Arbeiter als fertige materielle Produktions-\n bedingung vorfindet. In der einfachen Cooperation und selbst in der\n durch Theilung der Arbeit spezificirten erscheint die Verdrängung des ver-\n einzelten Arbeiters durch den vergesellschafteten immer noch\n mehr oder minder zufällig. Die Maschinerie, mit einigen später zu er-\n [Note: at once to perfect all machinery, to cheapen it, and to stimulate invention and\n improvement.“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"373\", \"facs\": \"#f0392\"} --> wähnenden Ausnahmen, funktionirt nur in der Hand unmittelbar vergesell-\n schafteter oder gemeinsamer Arbeit. Der cooperative Charakter\n des Arbeitsprozesses wird jetzt also durch die Natur des Arbeitsmit-\n tels selbst diktirte technologische Nothwendigkeit.\n\n\n\n\nMan sah, dass die aus der Cooperation und der Theilung der Arbeit\n entspringenden Produktivkräfte dem Kapital nichts kosten. Sie sind Na-\n turkräfte der gesellschaftlichen Arbeit. Naturkräfte, wie\n Dampf, Wasser u. s. w., die zu produktiven Prozessen angeeignet werden,\n kosten ebenfalls nichts. Wie aber der Mensch eine Lunge zum Athmen\n braucht, braucht er ein „Gebild von Menschenhand,“ um Naturkräfte produk-\n tiv zu konsumiren. Ein Wasserrad ist nöthig, um die Bewegungskraft des\n Wassers, eine Dampfmaschine, um die Elasticität des Dampfs auszubeuten.\n Wie mit den Naturkräften, verhält es sich mit der Wissenschaft. Einmal\n entdeckt, kostet das Gesetz über die Abweichung der Magnetnadel im\n Wirkungskreise eines elektrischen Stroms oder über Erzeugung von Magne-\n tismus im Eisen, um das ein elektrischer Strom kreist, keinen Deut [Note: Die Wissenschaft kostet dem Kapitalisten überhaupt „Nichts“, was ihn\n durchaus nicht hindert, sie zu exploitiren. Die „fremde“ Wissenschaft wird dem\n Kapital einverleibt, wie die fremde Arbeit. „Kapitalistische“ Aneignung und\n „persönliche“ Aneignung, sei es von Wissenschaft, sei es von materiellem Reich-\n thum, sind aber ganz und gar disparate Dinge. Dr. Ure selbst bejammert die\n grobe Unbekanntschaft seiner lieben, Maschinenexploitirenden Fabrikanten mit\n der Mechanik und Liebig weiss von der haarsträubenden Unwissenheit der eng-\n lischen chemischen Fabrikanten in der Chemie zu erzählen.] .\n Aber zur Ausbeutung dieser Gesetze für Telegraphie u. s. w. bedarf es\n eines sehr kostspieligen und weitläufigen Apparats. Durch die Maschine\n wird, wie wir sahen, das Werkzeug nicht verdrängt. Aus einem\n Zwerg-Werkzeug des menschlichen Organismus reckt es sich in Um-\n fang und Zahl zum Werkzeug eines vom Menschen geschaffenen Mechanis-\n mus. Statt mit dem Handwerkszeug, lässt das Kapital den Arbeiter jetzt\n mit einer Maschine arbeiten, die ihre Werkzeuge selbst führt. Wenn es\n daher auf den ersten Blick klar ist, dass die grosse Industrie durch Einver-\n leibung ungeheurer Naturkräfte und der Naturwissenschaft in den Produk-\n tionsprozess die Produktivität der Arbeit ausserordentlich steigern muss, ist\n es keineswegs eben so klar, dass diese gesteigerte Produktivkraft nicht durch\n vermehrte Arbeitsausgabe auf der andern Seite erkauft wird. Gleich jedem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"374\", \"facs\": \"#f0393\"} --> andern Bestandtheil des constanten Kapitals, schafft die Maschinerie\n keinen Werth, giebt aber ihren eignen Werth an das Produkt ab, zu des-\n sen Erzeugung sie dient. Soweit sie Werth hat und daher Werth auf das\n Produkt überträgt, bildet sie einen Werthbestandtheil desselben. Statt\n es zu verwohlfeilern, vertheuert sie es im Verhältniss zu ihrem\n eignen Werth. Und es ist handgreiflich, dass Maschine und systematisch\n entwickelte Maschinerie, das charakteristische Arbeitsmittel der grossen\n Industrie, unverhältnissmässig an Werth schwillt, verglichen mit den Ar-\n beitsmitteln des Handwerks- und Manufakturbetriebs.\n\n\n\n\nEs ist nun zunächst zu bemerken, dass die Maschinerie stets\n ganz in den Arbeitsprozess und immer nur theilweis in den Ver-\n werthungsprozess eingeht. Sie setzt dem Produkt nie mehr Werth\n zu als sie im Durchschnitt durch ihre tägliche Abnutzung verliert. Es\n findet also grosse Differenz statt zwischen dem Maschinenwerth und dem\n Werththeil der Maschine, der im täglichen Produkt derselben wiederer-\n scheint. Es findet eine grosse Differenz statt zwischen der Maschine\n als werthbildendem und als produktbildendem Element. Je\n grösser die Periode, während welcher dieselbe Maschinerie wiederholt in\n demselben Arbeitsprozess dient, desto grösser jene Differenz. Allerdings\n haben wir gesehn, dass jedes eigentliche Arbeitsmittel oder Produk-\n tionsinstrument immer ganz in den Arbeitsprozess und stets nur stückweis,\n im Verhältniss zu seinem täglichen Durchschnittsverschleiss, in den Verwer-\n thungsprozess eingeht. Diese Differenz jedoch zwischen der Benutzung\n und der Abnutzung des Arbeitsmittels, zwischen dem Dienst, den es in der\n Waarenproduktion leistet, und der Vertheurung der Waare, die es durch\n Uebertragen seines Werths auf dieselbe hervorbringt, ist viel grösser bei\n der Maschinerie als bei dem Werkzeug, weil sie, aus dauerhafterem Ma-\n terial gebaut, eine längere Lebensperiode besitzt, weil ihre Anwendung im\n Arbeitsprozess, durch streng wissenschaftliche Gesetze geregelt, grössere\n Oekonomie in der Verausgabung ihrer Bestandtheile und ihrer Konsum-\n tionsmittel ermöglicht, und endlich, weil ihr Produktionsfeld unverhält-\n nissmässig grösser ist als das des Werkzeugs. Ziehn wir von beiden,\n von Maschinerie und Werkzeug, ihre tägliche Durchschnittskost ab, oder\n den Werthbestandtheil, den sie durch täglichen Durchschnittsverschleiss\n und den Konsum von Hilfsstoffen, wie Oel, Kohlen u. s. w. dem Produkt\n zusetzen, so wirken sie umsonst, ganz wie ohne Zuthun menschlicher\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"375\", \"facs\": \"#f0394\"} --> Arbeit vorhandne Naturkräfte. Um so grösser der produktive Wirkungs-\n umfang der Maschinerie als der des Werkzeugs, um so grösser ist der Um-\n fang ihres unentgeldlichen Dienstes verglichen mit dem des Werk-\n zeugs. Erst in der grossen Industrie lernt der Mensch das Produkt seiner\n vergangnen, bereits vergegenständlichten Arbeit auf grossem Massstab\n gleich einer Naturkraft umsonst wirken zu lassen [Note: Ricardo fasst diese, übrigens von ihm ebensowenig wie der allgemeine\n Unterschied zwischen Arbeitsprozess und Verwerthungsprozess entwickelte Wir-\n kung der Maschinen manchmal so vorzugsweise ins Auge, dass er gelegentlich den\n Werthbestandtheil vergisst, den Maschinen an das Produkt abgeben, und sie ganz\n und gar mit den Naturkräften zusammenwirft. So z. B. „Adam Smith nowhere\n undervalues the services which the natural agents and machinery perform for us,\n but he very justly distinguishes the nature of the value which they add to commo-\n dities .... as they perform their work gratuitously, the assistance which\n they afford us, adds nothing to value in exchange.“ (Ric. l. c. p. 336, 337.)\n Ricardo’s Bemerkung ist natürlich richtig gegen J. B. Say, der sich vorfaselt,\n die Maschinen leisteten den „Dienst“ Werthzuschaffen, der einen Theil des\n Profits der Kapitalisten bilde.] .\n\n\n\n\nEs ergab sich bei Betrachtung der Cooperation und Manufaktur, dass\n gewisse allgemeine Produktionsbedingungen, wie Baulichkeiten u. s. w.,\n im Vergleich mit den zersplitterten Produktionsbedingungen vereinzelter Ar-\n beiter durch den gemeinsamen Konsum ökonomisirt werden, daher das\n Produkt weniger vertheuern. Bei der Maschinerie wird nicht nur der\n Körper einer Arbeitsmaschine von ihren vielen Werkzeugen, sondern die-\n selbe Bewegungsmaschine nebst einem Theil des Transmissionsmechanis-\n mus von vielen Arbeitsmaschinen gemeinsam verbraucht.\n\n\n\n\nGegeben die Differenz zwischen dem Werth der Maschinerie und dem\n von ihr auf das Tagesprodukt übertragenen Werththeil, hängt der Grad,\n worin dieser Werththeil das Produkt vertheuert, zunächst vom Umfang\n des Produkts ab, gleichsam von seiner Oberfläche. Herr Baynes\n aus Blackburn schätzt in einer 1858 veröffentlichten Vorlesung, dass\n „jede reale mechanische Pferdekraft 450 selfacting Mulespindeln nebst\n Vorgeschirr treibt oder 200 Throstlespindeln oder 15 Webstühle für 40\n inch cloth nebst den Vorrichtungen zum Aufziehn der Kette, Schlichten\n u. s. w.“ Es ist im ersten Fall das Tagesprodukt von 450 Mulespin-\n deln, im zweiten von 200 Throstlespindeln, im dritten von 15 mecha-\n nischen Webstühlen, worüber sich die täglichen Kosten einer Dampf-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"376\", \"facs\": \"#f0395\"} --> pferdekraft und der Verschleiss der von ihr in Bewegung gesetzten Ma-\n schinerie vertheilen, so dass hierdurch auf eine Unze Garn oder eine Elle\n Geweb nur ein winziger Werththeil übertragen wird. Ebenso im obigen\n Beispiel mit dem Dampfhammer. Da sich sein täglicher Verschleiss,\n Kohlenkonsum u. s. w. vertheilen auf die furchtbaren Eisenmassen, die er\n täglich hämmert, hängt sich jedem Centner Eisen nur ein geringer Werth-\n theil an, der sehr gross wäre, sollte das cyklopische Instrument kleine\n Nägel eintreiben.\n\n\n\n\nDen Wirkungskreis der Arbeitsmaschine, also die Anzahl ihrer\n Werkzeuge, oder, wo es sich um Kraft handelt, deren Umfang gegeben,\n wird die Produktenmasse von der Geschwindigkeit abhängen, womit\n sie operirt, also z. B. von der Geschwindigkeit, womit sich die Spindel\n dreht, oder der Anzahl Schläge, die der Hammer in einer Minute austheilt.\n Manche jener kolossalen Hämmer geben 70 Schläge, Ryder’s Schmiede-\n patentmaschine, die Dampfhämmer in kleineren Dimensionen zum Schmie-\n den von Spindeln anwendet, 700 Schläge in einer Minute.\n\n\n\n\nDie Proportion gegeben, worin die Maschinerie Werth auf das Pro-\n dukt überträgt, hängt die Grösse dieses Werththeils von ihrer eignen\n Werthgrösse ab [Note: Der in kapitalistischen Vorstellungen befangene Leser vermisst hier natür-\n lich den „Zins“, den die Maschine, pro rata ihres Kapitalwerthes, dem Produkt\n zusetzt. Es ist jedoch leicht einzusehn, dass die Maschine, da sie so wenig als\n irgend ein andrer Bestandtheil des constanten Kapitals Neuwerth erzeugt,\n keinen solchen unter dem Namen „Zins“ zusetzen kann. Es ist ferner klar, dass\n hier, wo es sich um die Produktion des Mehrwerths handelt, kein Theil\n desselben unter dem Namen „Zins“ a priori vorausgesetzt werden kann. Die\n kapitalistische Rechnungsweise, die prima facie abgeschmackt und den Ge-\n setzen der Werthbildung widersprechend scheint, findet im dritten Buch dieser\n Schrift ihre Erklärung.] . Je weniger Arbeit sie selbst enthält, desto\n weniger Werth setzt sie dem Produkt zu. Je weniger Werth bildend,\n desto produktiver ist sie und desto mehr nähert sich ihr Dienst dem der\n Naturkräfte. Die Produktion der Maschinerie durch Ma-\n schinerie verringert aber ihren Werth, verhältnissmässig zu ihrer\n Ausdehnung und Wirkung.\n\n\n\n\nEine vergleichende Analyse der Preise handwerks- oder manufaktur-\n mässig producirter Waaren und der Preise derselben Waaren als Maschi-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"377\", \"facs\": \"#f0396\"} --> nenprodukt ergiebt im Allgemeinen das Resultat, dass beim Maschinenpro-\n dukt der dem Arbeitsmittel geschuldete Werthbestandtheil rela-\n tiv wächst, aber absolut abnimmt. Das heisst, seine absolute\n Grösse nimmt ab, aber seine Grösse im Verhältniss zum Gesammtwerth\n des Produkts, z. B. eines Pfundes Garn, nimmt zu [Note: Dieser von der Maschine zugesetzte Werthbestandtheil fällt absolut und\n relativ, wo sie Pferde verdrängt, überhaupt Arbeitsthiere, die nur als Bewegungs-\n kraft, nicht als Stoffwechselmaschinen benutzt werden. Nebenbei bemerkt, Des-\n cartes mit seiner Definition der Thiere als blosser Maschinen sieht mit den Augen\n der Manufakturperiode im Unterschied zum Mittelalter, dem das Thier als Ge-\n hilfe des Menschen galt, wie später wieder dem Herrn v. Haller in seiner\n „Restauration der Staatswissenschaften“. Dass Descartes ebenso wie Baco eine\n veränderte Gestalt der Produktion und praktische Beherrschung der Natur durch\n den Menschen als Resultat der veränderten Denkmethode betrachtete, zeigt sein\n „Discours dela Méthode“, wo es u. a. heisst: „il est possible (durch die\n von ihm in die Philosophie eingeführte Methode) de parvenir à des connaissances\n fort utiles à la vie, et qu’au lieu de cette philosophie spéculative qu’on enseigne\n dans les écoles, on en peut trouver une pratique, par laquelle, connaissant la force\n et les actions du feu, de l’eau, de l’air, des astres, et de tous les autres corps qui\n nous environnent, aussi distinctement que nous connaissons les divers métiers de nos\n artisans, nous les pourrions employer en même façon à tous les usages auxquels ils\n sont propres, et ainsi nous rendre comme maîtres et possesseurs de la\n nature“, und so „contribuer au perfectionnement de la vie humaine.“\n In der Vorrede zu Sir Dudley North’s „Discourses upon Trade“\n (1691) heisst es, die Methode des Descartes, auf die politische Oekonomie ange-\n wandt, habe sie von alten Mährchen und abergläubigen Vorstellungen über Geld,\n Handel u. s. w. zu befreien angefangen. Im Durchschnitt schliessen sich jedoch\n die englischen Oekonomen der früheren Zeit an Baco und Hobbes als ihre Philo-\n sophen an, während Locke später der „Philosoph“ der politischen Oekonomie\nϰατ᾽ ἐξοχὴν für England, Frankreich und Italien ward.] .\n\n\n\n\nEs ist klar, dass blosses Deplacement der Arbeit stattfindet, also die\n Gesammtsumme der zur Produktion einer Waare erheischten Arbeit nicht\n vermindert oder die Produktivkraft der Arbeit nicht vermehrt wird, wenn\n die Produktion einer Maschine so viel Arbeit kostet als ihre Anwendung\n erspart. Die Differenz jedoch zwischen der Arbeit, die sie kostet, und\n der Arbeit, die sie erspart, oder der Grad ihrer Produktivität hängt offen-\n bar nicht ab von der Differenz zwischen ihrem eigenen Werth und dem\n Werth des von ihr ersetzten Werkzeugs. Die Differenz dauert so lange\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"378\", \"facs\": \"#f0397\"} --> als die Arbeitskost der Maschine und daher der von ihr dem Produkt zu-\n gesetzte Werththeil kleiner bleiben als der Werth, den der Arbeiter mit\n seinem Werkzeug dem Arbeitsgegenstand zusetzen würde. Die Produk-\n tivität der Maschine misst sich daher an dem Grad, worin sie\n menschliche Arbeitskraft ersetzt. Nach Herrn Baynes kom-\n men auf 450 Mulespindeln nebst Vormaschinerie, die von einer Dampf-\n pferdekraft getrieben werden, 2½ Arbeiter [Note: Nach einem Jahresbericht der Handelskammer zu Essen (October 1863)\n produzirte 1862 die Krupp’sche Gussstahlfabrik mittelst 161 Schmelz-, Glüh- und\n Cementöfen, 32 Dampfmaschinen (im Jahr 1800 war das ungefähr die Gesammt-\n zahl der in Manchester angewandten Dampfmaschinen) und 14 Dampfhäm-\n mern, welche zusammen 1236 Pferdekraft repräsentiren, 49 Schmiedeessen, 203\n Werkzeugmaschinen und circa 2400 Arbeitern 13 Millionen Pfund Gussstahl. Hier\n noch nicht 2 Arbeiter auf 1 Pferdekraft.]  und werden mit jeder self-\n acting mule spindle bei zehnstündigem Arbeitstag 13 Unzen Garn (Durch-\n schnittsnumer), also wöchentlich 365⅝ lbs. Garn von 2½ Arbeitern\n gesponnen. Bei ihrer Verwandlung in Garn absorbiren ungefähr 366\n Pfund Baumwolle (wir sehn der Vereinfachung halber vom Abfall ab) also\n nur 150 Arbeitsstunden oder 15 zehnstündige Arbeitstage, während mit\n dem Spinnrad, wenn der Handspinner 13 Unzen Garn in 60 Stunden lie-\n fert, dasselbe Quantum Baumwolle 2700 Arbeitstage von 10 Stunden oder\n 27,000 Arbeitsstunden absorbiren würde [Note: Babbage berechnet, dass in Java im Spinnprozess 117 % der Baum-\n wolle auf ihren ursprünglichen Werth zugesetzt wird, ein Werthzusatz, der fast nur\n aus der Spinnarbeit entspringt. Zur selben Zeit (1832) betrug in England der Ge-\n sammtwerth, den Maschinerie und Arbeit der Baumwolle bei der Feinspinnerei zu-\n setzten, ungefähr 33 % auf den Werth des Rohmaterials. („On the Economy\n of Machinery“, p. 214.)] . Wo die alte Methode des\n blockprinting oder der Handkattundruckerei durch Maschinendruck ver-\n drängt ist, druckt eine einzige Maschine mit dem Beistand eines Mannes\n oder Jungen so viel vierfarbigen Kattun in einer Stunde als früher 200\n Männer [Note: Beim Maschinendruck ausserdem Farbe erspart.] . Bevor Eli Whitney 1793 den cottongin erfand, kostete die\n Trennung eines Pfundes Baumwolle vom Samen einen Durchschnittsar-\n beitstag. In Folge seiner Erfindung konnten täglich 100 lbs. Baumwolle\n von einer Negerin gewonnen werden und die Wirksamkeit des gin ward\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"379\", \"facs\": \"#f0398\"} --> seit seiner ersten Einführung noch bedeutend erhöht. Die Baumwollfaser,\n früher zu 50 cents per Pfund producirt, wird später mit grösserem Profit,\n d. h. mit Einschluss von mehr unbezahlter Arbeit, zu 10 cents verkauft.\n In Indien wird zur Trennung der Faser vom Samen ein halbmaschinen-\n artiges Instrument, die Churka, angewandt, womit ein Mann und eine Frau\n 28 lbs. täglich reinigen können. Mit der von Dr. Forbes vor einigen\n Jahren erfundenen Churka produciren 1 Mann und 1 Junge täglich 250 lbs.;\n wo Ochsen, Dampf oder Wasser als Triebkräfte gebraucht werden, sind\n nur wenige Jungen und Mädchen als feeders (Handlanger des Materials\n für die Maschine) erheischt. Sechszehn dieser Maschinen, mit Ochsen\n getrieben, verrichten täglich das frühere Durchschnitts-Tagewerk von 750\n Leuten [Note: Vgl. Paper read by Dr. Watson, Reporter on Products to\n the Government of India, before the Society of Arts, 17. April\n 1860.] .\n\n\n\n\nWie bereits erwähnt, verrichtet die Dampfmaschine, beim Dampf-\n pflug z. B., in einer Stunde zu 3 d. oder ¼ sh. so viel Werk als 66 Men-\n schen zu 15 sh. per Stunde. Ich komme auf diess Beispiel zurück gegen\n eine falsche Vorstellung. Die 15 sh. sind nämlich keineswegs der\n Ausdruck der während einer Stunde von den 66 Menschen zuge-\n fügten Arbeit. War das Verhältniss von Mehrarbeit zu nothwendiger\n Arbeit 100 %, so producirten diese 66 Arbeiter per Stunde einen Werth\n von 30 sh., obgleich sich nur 33 Stunden in einem Aequivalent für sie\n selbst, d. h. im Arbeitslohn von 15 sh. darstellen. Gesetzt also, eine Ma-\n schine koste eben so viel als z. B. der Jahreslohn von 150 durch sie ver-\n drängten Arbeitern, sage 3000 Pfd. St., so sind 3000 Pfd. St. keineswegs\n der Geldausdruck der Gesammtarbeit, welche die 150 Arbeiter lieferten und\n dem Arbeitsgegenstand zusetzten, sondern nur des Theils ihrer Jah-\n resarbeit, der sich in einem Aequivalent für sie selbst, vulgo Arbeits-\n lohn, darstellte. Ist dagegen der Werth der Maschine 3000 Pfd. St.,\n so sind diese 3000 Pfd. St. der Geldausdruck aller zu ihrer Produktion\n verausgabten Arbeit, in welchem Verhältniss immer diese Arbeit Arbeits-\n lohn für den Arbeiter und Mehrwerth  [Editorial alternatives: sic = fur; corr = für]  den Kapitalisten bilde. Kostet die\n Maschine also ebensoviel als die von ihr ersetzte Arbeitskraft, so ist die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"380\", \"facs\": \"#f0399\"} --> in ihr selbst vergegenständlichte Arbeit stets viel kleiner als die von ihr\n ersetzte lebendige Arbeit [Note: „The mute agents (die Maschinen) are always the produce of much less\n labour than that which they displace, even when they are of the same money\n value.“ (Ricardo l. c. p. 40.)] .\n\n\n\n\nAusschliesslich als Mittel zur Verwohlfeilerung des Produkts\n betrachtet, ist die Grenze für den Gebrauch der Maschinerie darin gegeben,\n dass ihre eigne Produktion weniger Arbeit kostet als ihre Anwendung Ar-\n beit ersetzt. Für das Kapital jedoch drückt sich diese Grenze enger\n aus. Da es nicht die angewandte Arbeit zahlt, sondern den Werth\n der angewandten Arbeitskraft, wird ihm der Maschinengebrauch begrenzt\n durch die Differenz zwischen dem Maschinenwerth und dem\n Werth der von ihr ersetzten Arbeitskraft. Da die Theilung\n des Arbeitstags in nothwendige Arbeit und Mehrarbeit, oder, populär aus-\n gedrückt, in bezahlte Arbeit und unbezahlte Arbeit, sowohl in verschie-\n denen Ländern als in demselben Lande zu verschiedenen Perioden oder in\n verschiedenen Geschäftszweigen während derselben Periode sehr verschie-\n den sein kann, da ferner der wirkliche Lohn des Arbeiters bald unter den\n Werth seiner Arbeitskraft sinkt, bald über ihn steigt, kann die Diffe-\n renz zwischen dem Preise der Maschinerie und dem\n Preise der von ihr zu ersetzenden Arbeitskraft sehr\nvariiren, wenn auch die Differenz zwischen dem zur Pro-\n duktion der Maschine nöthigen Arbeitsquantum und\n dem Gesammtquantum der von ihr ersetzten Arbeit die-\n selbe bleibt. Es ist aber nur die erste Differenz, welche die Kost\n der Waare für den Kapitalisten selbst bestimmt und ihn durch die\n Zwangsgesetze der Konkurrenz beeinflusst. Es werden daher heute Ma-\n schinen in England erfunden, die nur in Nordamerika angewandt werden,\n wie Deutschland im 16. und 17. Jahrhundert Maschinen erfand, die nur\n Holland anwandte, und wie manche französische Erfindung des 18. Jahr-\n hunderts nur in England ausgebeutet ward. Die Maschine selbst produ-\n cirt in älter entwickelten Ländern durch ihre Anwendung auf einige Ge-\n schäftszweige in anderen Zweigen solchen Arbeitsüberfluss (redundancy\n of labour, sagt Ricardo), dass hier der Fall des Arbeitslohns unter den\n Werth der Arbeitskraft den Gebrauch der Maschinerie verhindert und\n ihn vom Standpunkt des Kapitals, dessen Gewinn ohnehin aus der Ver-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"381\", \"facs\": \"#f0400\"} --> minderung nicht der angewandten, sondern der bezahlten Arbeit\n entspringt, überflüssig, oft unmöglich macht. In einigen Zweigen der\n englischen Wollmanufaktur ist während der letzten Jahre die Kinder-\n arbeit sehr vermindert, hier und da fast verdrängt worden. Warum? Der\n Fabrikakt ernöthigte eine doppelte Kinderreihe, von denen je eine 6,\n die andere 4 Stunden, oder jede nur 5 Stunden arbeitet. Die Aeltern\n wollten aber die half-times (Halbzeitler) nicht wohlfeiler verkaufen als früher\n die full-times (Vollzeitler). Daher Ersetzung der half-times durch\n Maschinerie [Note: „Employers of labour would not unnecessarily retain two sets of children\n under thirteen … In fact one class of manufacturers, the spinners of woollen\n yarn, now rarely employ children under thirteen years of ages, i. e. half-times.\n They have introduced improved and new machinery of various kinds, which alto-\n gether supersedes the employment of children (d. h. unter 13 J.); f. i.: I will\n mention one process as an illustration of this diminution in the number of children,\n wherein, by the addition of an apparatus, called a piecing machine, to existing\n machines, the work of six or four half-times, according to the peculiarity of each\n machine, can be performed by one young person (über 13 J.) … the half-time\n system“ stimulirte „the invention of the piecing machine.“ (Reports of Insp.\n of Fact. for 31. Oct. 1858.)] . Vor dem Verbot der Arbeit von Weibern und Kindern\n (unter 10 Jahren) in Minen, fand das Kapital die Methode, nackte Weiber\n und Mädchen, oft mit Männern zusammengebunden, in Kohlen- und an-\n dern Minen zu vernutzen, so übereinstimmend mit seinem Moralkodex und\n namentlich auch seinem Hauptbuch, dass erst nach dem Verbot Maschinerie\n sie für einige Funktionen ersetzte. Die Yankees haben Maschinen zum\n Steinklopfen erfunden. Die Engländer wenden sie nicht an, weil der\n „Elende“ („wretch“ ist Kunstausdruck der englischen politischen\n Oekonomie für den Agrikulturarbeiter), der diese Arbeit verrichtet, einen\n so geringen Theil seiner Arbeit bezahlt erhält, dass Maschinerie die Pro-\n duktion für den Kapitalisten vertheuern würde [Note: „Machinery … can frequently not be employed until labour (er\n meint wages) rises.“ (Ricardo l. c. p. 479.)] . In England\n werden gelegentlich statt der Pferde immer noch Weiber zum Ziehn\n u. s. w. bei den Kanalbauten verwandt [Note: Sieh: „Report of the Social Science Congress at Edin-\n burgh. October 1863.“] , weil die zur Produktion\n von Pferden und Maschinen erheischte Arbeit ein mathematisch ge-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"382\", \"facs\": \"#f0401\"} --> gebnes Quantum, die zur Erhaltung von Weibern der Surpluspopulation\n dagegen unter aller Berechnung steht. Man findet daher nirgendwo scham-\n losere Verschwendung von Menschenkraft für Lumpereien als grade in\n England, dem Land der Maschinen.\n\n\n\n\nDen Ausgangspunkt der grossen Industrie bildet, wie gezeigt, die\n Revolution des Arbeitsmittels, und das umgewälzte Arbeitsmittel erhält\n seine meist entwickelte Gestalt im gegliederten Maschinensystem der Fabrik.\n Bevor wir zusehn, wie diesem objektiven Organismus Menschenmaterial\n einverleibt wird, betrachten wir einige allgemeine Rückwirkungen jener\n Revolution auf den Arbeiter selbst.\n\n\n\n\nSofern die Maschinerie Muskelkraft entbehrlich macht, wird sie zum\nMittel Arbeiter ohne Muskelkraft oder von unreifer Körperent-\n wicklung, aber grösserer Geschmeidigkeit der Glieder anzuwenden.\nWeiber- und Kinderarbeit war daher das erste Wort der kapi-\n talistischen Anwendung der Maschinerie! Diess gewaltige Ersatz-\n mittel von Arbeit und Arbeitern verwandelte sich damit sofort in ein Mittel\ndie Zahl der Lohnarbeiter zu vermehren durch Einrollirung\n aller Mitglieder der Arbeiterfamilie, ohne Unterschied von Geschlecht und\n Alter, unter die unmittelbare Botmässigkeit des Kapitals. Die Zwangs-\n arbeit für den Kapitalisten usurpirte nicht nur die Stelle des Kinderspiels,\n sondern auch der freien Arbeit im häuslichen Kreis, innerhalb sittlicher\n Schranke, für die Familie selbst [Note: Dr. Edward Smith wurde während der den amerikanischen Bürger-\n krieg begleitenden Baumwollkrise von der englischen Regierung nach Lancashire,\n Cheshire u. s. w. geschickt, zur Berichterstattung über den Gesundheitszustand der\n Baumwollarbeiter. Er berichtet u. a.: Hygienisch habe die Krise, abgesehn von\n der Verbannung der Arbeiter aus der Fabrikatmosphäre, vielerlei andre Vortheile.\n Die Arbeiterfrauen fänden jetzt die nöthige Musse, ihren Kindern die Brust zu\n reichen, statt sie mit Godfrey’s Cordial zu vergiften. Sie hätten die Zeit ge-\n wonnen, kochen zu lernen. Unglücklicher Weise fiel diese Kochkunst in einen\n Augenblick, wo sie nichts zu essen hatten. Aber man sieht, wie das Kapital die für\n die Konsumtion nöthige Familienarbeit usurpirt hat zu seiner Selbstverwerthung.\n Ebenso wurde die Krise benutzt, um in eignen Schulen die Töchter der Arbeiter\nnähen zu lehren. Eine amerikanische Revolution und eine Weltkrise erheischt,\n damit die Arbeitermädchen, die für die ganze Welt spinnen, nähen lernen!] .\n\n\n\n\nDer Werth der Arbeitskraft war bestimmt nicht nur durch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"383\", \"facs\": \"#f0402\"} --> die zur Erhaltung des individuellen erwachsnen Arbeiters, sondern durch\n die zur Erhaltung der Arbeitsfamilie nöthige Arbeitszeit. Indem die\n Maschinerie alle Glieder der Arbeiterfamilie auf den Arbeitsmarkt wirft,\n vertheilt sie den Werth der Arbeitskraft des Mannes über seine ganze Fa-\n milie. Sie entwerthet daher seine Arbeitskraft. Der Ankauf der in\n 4 Arbeitskräfte z. B. parcellirten Familie kostet vielleicht mehr als früher\n der Ankauf der Arbeitskraft des Familienhaupts, aber dafür treten 4 Ar-\n beitstage an die Stelle von Einem, und ihr Preis fällt im Verhältniss zum\n Ueberschuss der Mehrarbeit der Vier über die Mehrarbeit des Einen. Vier\n müssen nun nicht nur Arbeit, sondern Mehrarbeit für das Kapital liefern,\n damit eine Familie lebe. So erweitert die Maschinerie von vorn herein\n mit dem menschlichen Exploitationsmaterial, dem eigensten\n Ausbeutungsfeld des Kapitals [Note: „The numerical increase of labourers has been great, through the grow-\n ing substitution of female for male, and above all of childish for adult, labour.\n Three girls of 13, at wages from of 6 sh. to 8 sh. a week, have replaced the one\n man of mature age, of wages varying from 18 sh. to 45 sh.“ (Th. de Quin-\n cey: „The Logic of Politic. Econ. Lond. 1845“, Note zu p. 147.)\n Da gewisse Funktionen der Familie, z. B. Warten und Säugen der Kinder u. s. w.,\n nicht ganz unterdrückt werden können, müssen die vom Kapital konfiscirten Fa-\n milienmütter mehr oder minder Stellvertreter dingen. Die Arbeiten, welche der\n Familienkonsum erheischt, wie Nähen, Flicken u. s. w., müssen durch Kauf fer-\n tiger Waaren ersetzt werden. Der verminderten Ausgabe von häuslicher Arbeit ent-\n spricht also vermehrte Geldausgabe. Die Produktionskosten der Arbeiterfamilie\n wachsen daher und balanciren die Mehreinnahme. Es kommt hinzu, dass Oeko-\n nomie und Zweckmässigkeit in Vernutzung und Bereitung der Lebensmittel\n unmöglich werden. Ueber diese von der officiellen politischen Oekonomie ver-\n heimlichten Thatsachen findet man reichliches Material in den „Reports“ der\n Fabrikinspektoren, der „Children’s Employment Commission“ und\n namentlich auch des „Board of Health“.] , zugleich den Exploitationsgrad.\n\n\n\n\nSie revolutionirt eben so von Grund aus die formelle Vermittlung des\n Kapitalverhältnisses, den Kontrakt zwischen Arbeiter und Kapitalist.\n Auf Grundlage des Waarenaustausches war es erste Voraussetzung, dass\n sich Kapitalist und Arbeiter als freie Personen, als unabhängige Waa-\n renbesitzer, der eine Besitzer von Geld und Produktionsmitteln, der andre\n Besitzer von Arbeitskraft, gegenübertraten. Aber jetzt kauft das Kapital\n Unmündige oder Halbmündige. Der Arbeiter verkaufte früher seine eigne\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"384\", \"facs\": \"#f0403\"} --> Arbeitskraft, worüber er als formell freie Person verfügte. Er verkauft\n jetzt Weib und Kind. Er wird Sklavenhändler [Note: Im Kontrast zur grossen Thatsache, dass die Beschränkung der Weiber-\n und Kinderarbeit in den englischen Fabriken von den erwachsnen männlichen Ar-\n beitern dem Kapital aberobert wurde, findet man noch in den jüngsten Berichten\n der „Children’s Employment Commission“ wahrhaft empörende und\n durchaus sklavenhändlerische Züge der Arbeitereltern mit Bezug auf den Kinder-\n schacher. Der kapitalistische Pharisäer aber, wie man aus denselben „Reports“\n sehn kann, denuncirt diese von ihm selbst geschaffene, verewigte und exploitirte\n Bestialität, die er sonst „Freiheit der Arbeit“ tauft. „Infant labour has been\n called into aid … even to work for their own daily bread. Withoutstrength to endure\n such disproportionate toil, without instruction to guide their future life, they have\n been thrown into a situation physically and morally polluted. The Jewish historian\n has remarked upon the overthrow of Jerusalem by Titus, that is was no wonder\n it should have been destroyed, with such a signal destruction, when an inhuman\n mother sacrificed her own offspring to satisfy the cravings of absolute hunger.“\n „Public Economy Concentrated. Carlisle 1833“, p. 56.] . Die Nachfrage\n nach Kinderarbeit gleicht oft auch in der Form der Nachfrage nach Neger-\n sklaven, wie man sie in amerikanischen Zeitungsinseraten zu lesen gewohnt\n war. „Meine Aufmerksamkeit“, sagt z. B. ein englischer Fabrikinspektor,\n „wurde gelenkt auf eine Annonce in dem Lokalblatt einer der bedeutend-\n sten Manufakturstädte meines Distrikts, wovon Folgendes die Kopie: Ge-\n braucht 12 bis 20 Jungen, nicht jünger als was für 13\n Jahre passiren kann. Lohn 4 sh. per Woche. Anzufragen etc.“ [Note: A. Redgrave in „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31. Oct.\n 1858“, p. 40, 41.] \n Der unterstrichne Passus bezieht sich darauf, dass nach dem Factory Act\nKinder unter 13 Jahren nur 6 Stunden arbeiten dürfen. Ein amt-\n lich qualificirter Arzt (certifying surgeon) muss das Alter bescheinigen.\n Der Fabrikant verlangt also Jungen, die so aussehn, als ob sie schon\n dreizehnjährig. Die manchmal sprungweise Abnahme in der Anzahl der\n von Fabrikanten beschäftigten Kinder unter 13 Jahren, überraschend in\n der englischen Statistik der letzten 20 Jahre, war, nach Aussage der\n Fabrikinspektoren selbst, grossentheils das Werk von certifying sur-\n geons, welche das Kindesalter der Exploitationslust der Kapitalisten und\n dem Schacherbedürfniss der Eltern gemäss verschoben. In dem berüch-\n tigten Londoner Distrikt von Bethnal Green wird jeden Montag und Dien-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"385\", \"facs\": \"#f0404\"} --> stag Morgen offner Markt gehalten, worin Kinder beiderlei Geschlechts\n vom 9. Jahre an sich selbst an die Londoner Seidenmanufakturen\n vermiethen. „Die gewöhnlichen Bedingungen sind 1 sh. 8 d. die Woche\n (die den Eltern gehören) und 2 d. für mich selbst nebst Thee.“ Die\n Kontrakte gelten nur für die Woche. Die Scenen und die Sprache wäh-\n rend der Dauer dieses Markts sind wahrhaft empörend [Note: „Children’s Employment Commission. V. Report. Lon-\n don 1866“, p. 81, n. 31.] . Es kömmt\n immer noch in England vor, dass Weiber „Jungen vom Workhouse neh-\n men und sie jedem beliebigen Käufer für 2 sh. 6 d. wöchentlich ver-\n miethen“ [Note: „Child. Employm. Comm. III. Report. Lond. 1864“, p. 53,\n n. 15.] . Trotz der Gesetzgebung werden immer noch mindestens\n 2000 Jungen in Grossbritannien als lebendige Schornsteinfegmaschinen (ob-\n gleich Maschinen zu ihrem Ersatz existiren) von ihren eigenen Eltern ver-\n kauft [Note: l. c. V. Report, p. XXII, n. 137.] . Die von der Maschinerie bewirkte Revolution im Rechts-\n verhältniss zwischen Käufer und Verkäufer der Arbeitskraft, so dass die\n ganze Transaktion selbst den Schein eines Kontrakts zwischen freien\n Personen verliert, bot dem englischen Parlament später den juristischen\nEntschuldigungsgrund für Staatseinmischung in das Fabrikwesen.\n So oft das Fabrikgesetz die Kinderarbeit in bisher unangefochtnen Indu-\n striezweigen auf 6 Stunden beschränkt, ertönt stets neu der Fabrikanten-\n jammer: ein Theil der Eltern entziehe die Kinder nun der gemassregelten\n Industrie, um sie in solche zu verkaufen, wo noch „Freiheit der Ar-\n beit“ herrscht, d. h. wo Kinder unter 13 Jahren gezwungen wer-\n den wie Erwachsene zu arbeiten, also auch theurer loszuschlagen sind.\n Da aber das Kapital von Natur ein leveller ist, d. h. in allen Produk-\n tionssphären Gleichheit der Exploitationsbedingungen der Arbeit als\n sein angebornes Menschenrecht verlangt, wird die legale Beschränkung der\n Kinderarbeit in einem Industriezweig Ursache ihrer Beschränkung in dem\n andern.\n\n\n\n\nBereits früher wurde der physische Verderb der Kinder und\n jungen Personen angedeutet, wie der Arbeiterweiber, welche die Maschi-\n nerie erst direkt, in den auf ihrer Grundlage aufschiessenden Fabriken,\n [Running matter: I. 25] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"386\", \"facs\": \"#f0405\"} --> und dann indirekt in allen übrigen Industriezweigen der Ex-\n ploitation des Kapitals unterwirft. Hier verweilen wir daher nur bei einem\n Punkt, der ungeheuren Sterblichkeit von Arbeiterkindern\n in ihren ersten Lebensjahren. In England giebt es 16 Regi-\n strations-Distrikte, wo im jährlichen Durchschnitt auf 100,000 lebende\n Kinder unter einem Jahr nur 9000 Todesfälle (in einem Distrikt nur\n 7,047) kommen, in 24 Distrikten über 10,000, aber unter 11,000, in\n 39 Distrikten über 11,000, aber unter 12,000, in 48 Distrikten über\n 12,000, aber unter 13,000, in 22 Distrikten über 20,000, in 25 Distrik-\n ten über 21,000, in 17 über 22,000, in 11 über 23,000, in Hoo, Wol-\n verhampton, Ashton-under-Lyne und Preston über 24,000, in Notting-\n ham, Stockport und Bradford über 25,000, in Wisbeach 26,000, und in\n Manchester 26,125 [Note: „Sixth Report“ des „Board of Health. Lond. 1864“, p. 34.] . Wie eine officielle ärztliche Untersuchung im\n Jahre 1861 nachwies, sind, von Lokalumständen abgesehn, die hohen\n Sterblichkeitsraten vorzugsweise der ausserhäuslichen Beschäf-\n tigung der Mütter geschuldet und der daher entspringenden Vernach-\n lässigung und Misshandlung der Kinder, u. a. unpassender Nahrung, Mangel\n an Nahrung, Fütterung mit Opiaten u. s. w., dazu die unnatürliche Entfrem-\n dung der Mütter gegen ihre Kinder, im Gefolge davon absichtliche Aushunge-\n rung und Vergiftung [Note: „It (the inquiry of 1861) . . . . showed, moreover, that while, with the\n described circumstances, infants perish under the neglect and mismanagement\n which their mothers’ occupations imply, the mothers become to a grievous extent\n denaturalized towards their offspring — commonly not troubling themselves much\n at the death, and even sometimes … taking direct measures to ensure it.“ (l. c.)] . In solchen Agrikulturdistrikten, „wo ein Minimum\n weiblicher Beschäftigung existirt, ist dagegen die Sterblichkeitsrate am\n niedrigsten“ [Note: l. c. p. 454.] . Die Untersuchungskommission von 1861 ergab jedoch\n das unerwartete Resultat, dass in einigen an der Nordsee gelegnen rein\n ackerbauenden Distrikten die Sterblichkeitsrate von Kindern unter einem\n Jahr fast die der verrufensten Fabrikdistrikte erreicht. Dr. Julian\n Hunter wurde daher vom „Board of Health“ beauftragt, diess Phäno-\n men an Ort und Stelle zu erforschen. Sein Bericht ist dem „VI. Report\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"387\", \"facs\": \"#f0406\"} --> of the Board of Health“ einverleibt [Note: l. c. p. 454—463. „Report by Dr. Henry Julian Hunter on\nthe excessive mortality of infants in some rural districts of\n England.“] . Man hatte bisher vermuthet,\n Malaria und andre niedrig gelegnen und sumpfigen Landstrichen eigenthüm-\n liche Krankheiten decimirten die Kinder. Die Untersuchung ergab das\n grade Gegentheil, nämlich, „dass dieselbe Ursache, welche die Ma-\n laria vertrieb, nämlich die Verwandlung des Bodens aus Morast im Winter\n und dürftiger Weide im Sommer in fruchtbares Kornland, die ausserordent-\n liche Todesrate der Säuglinge schuf“ [Note: l. c. p. 35 u. p. 455, 456.] . Die 70 ärztlichen Praktiker,\n die Dr. Hunter in jenen Distrikten verhörte, waren „wunderbar einstim-\n mig“ über diesen Punkt. Mit der Revolution der Bodenkultur wurde nämlich\ndas industrielle System eingeführt. „Verheirathete Weiber, die\n in Banden mit Mädchen und Jungen zusammenarbeiten, werden dem\n Pächter von einem Manne, welcher der „Gangmeister“ heisst und die\n Banden im Ganzen miethet, für eine bestimmte Summe zur Verfügung ge-\n stellt. Diese Banden wandern oft viele Meilen von ihren Dörfern weg,\n man trifft sie Morgens und Abends auf den Landstrassen, die Weiber be-\n kleidet mit kurzen Unterröcken und entsprechenden Röcken und Stiefeln\n und manchmal Hosen, sehr kräftig und gesund von Aussehn, aber verdor-\n ben durch gewohnheitsmässige Liederlichkeit und rücksichtslos gegen die\n unheilvollen Folgen, welche ihre Vorliebe für diese thätige und unab-\n hängige Lebensart auf ihre Sprösslinge wälzt, die zu Haus verküm-\n mern“ [Note: l. c. p. 456.] . Alle Phänomene der Fabrikdistrikte reproduciren sich hier,\n in noch höherem Grad versteckter Kindermord und Behandlung der Kin-\n der mit Opiaten [Note: Wie in den englischen Fabrikdistrikten, so dehnt sich auch in den Agri-\n kulturdistrikten der Opiumkonsum unter den erwachsnen Arbeitern und\n Arbeiterinnen täglich aus. „To push the sale of opiate … is the great aim of\n some enterprising wholesale merchants. By druggists it is considered the lead-\n ingarticle.“ (l. c. p. 459.) Säuglinge, die Opiate empfingen, „verrumpelten\n in kleine alte Männchen oder verschrumpften zu kleinen Affen.“ (l. c. p. 460.)\n Man sieht, wie Indien und China sich an England rächen.] . „Meine Kenntniss der von ihr erzeugten Uebel“,\n sagt Dr. Simon, der ärztliche Beamte des englischen Privy Council\n [Running matter: 25*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"388\", \"facs\": \"#f0407\"} --> und Redacteur en chef der Berichte des „Board of Health“, „muss\n den tiefen Abscheu entschuldigen, womit ich jede umfassende indu-\n strielle Beschäftigung erwachsner Weiber betrachte“ [Note: l. c. p. 37.] . „Es wird“,\n ruft Fabrikinspektor R. Baker in einem officiellen Bericht aus, „es wird\n in der That ein Glück für die Manufakturdistrikte Englands sein, wenn\n jeder verheiratheten Frau, die Familie hat, verboten wird in irgend\n einer Fabrik zu arbeiten“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 59. Dieser\n Fabrikinspektor war früher Arzt.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie aus der kapitalistischen Exploitation der Weiber- und Kinder-\n arbeit entspringende moralische Verkümmerung ist von F. En-\n gels in seiner „Lage der arbeitenden Klassen Englands“\n und von andern Schriftstellern so erschöpfend dargestellt worden, dass ich\n hier nur daran erinnere. Die intellektuelle Verödung aber, künstlich\n producirt durch die Verwandlung unreifer Menschen in blosse Maschinen\n zum Fabrikat von Mehrwerth, und sehr zu unterscheiden von jener natur-\n wüchsigen Unwissenheit, welche den Geist in Brache legt ohne Verderb\n seiner Entwicklungsfähigkeit, seiner natürlichen Frucht-\n barkeit selbst, zwang endlich sogar das englische Parlament in allen\n dem Fabrikgesetz unterworfenen Industrien den Elementarunterricht zur\ngesetzlichen Bedingung für den „produktiven“ Verbrauch von Kin-\n dern unter 14 Jahren zu machen. Der Geist der kapitalistischen Pro-\n duktion leuchtet hell aus der liederlichen Redaktion der s. g. Erzie-\n hungsklauseln der Fabrikakte, aus dem Mangel administrativer Ma-\n schinerie, wodurch dieser Zwangsunterricht grossentheils wieder illusorisch\n wird, aus der Fabrikantenopposition selbst gegen diess Unterrichtsgesetz\n und ihren praktischen Kniffen und Schlichen zu seiner Umgehung. „Die\n Gesetzgebung allein ist zu tadeln, weil sie ein Truggesetz (delusive law)\n erlassen hat, das unter dem Schein für die Erziehung der Kinder zu sor-\n gen, keine einzige Bestimmung enthält, wodurch dieser vorgeschützte\n Zweck gesichert werden kann. Es bestimmt nichts, ausser dass die Kinder\n für eine bestimmte Stundenzahl (3 Stunden) per Tag innerhalb der vier\n Wände eines Platzes, Schule benamst, eingeschlossen werden sollen, und dass\n der Anwender des Kindes hierüber wöchentlich ein Certifikat von einer Person\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"389\", \"facs\": \"#f0408\"} --> erhalten muss, die sich als Schullehrer oder Schullehrerin mit ihrem\n Namen unterzeichnet“ [Note: Leonhard Horner in „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 30th\n June 1857“, p. 17.] . Vor dem Erlass des amendirten Fabrikakts von\n 1844 waren Schulbesuchscertifikate nicht selten, die von Schulmeister oder\n Schulmeisterin mit einem Kreuz unterzeichnet wurden, da letztre selbst nicht\n schreiben konnten. „Beim Besuch, den ich einer solchen Certifikate aus-\n stellenden Schule abstattete, war ich so betroffen von der Unwissenheit\n des Schulmeisters, dass ich zu ihm sagte: ‘Bitte, mein Herr, können Sie\n lesen?‘ Seine Antwort war: ‘Ih jeh, Ebbes (summat)‘. Zu seiner Recht-\n fertigung fügte er hinzu: ‘Jedenfalls stehe ich vor meinen Schülern‘. Wäh-\n rend der Vorbereitung des Akts von 1844 denuncirten die Fabrikinspektoren\n den schmählichen Zustand der Plätze, Schulen benamst, deren Certifikate\n sie als zu Gesetz vollgültig zulassen mussten. Alles was sie durchsetzten,\n war, dass seit 1844 die Zahlen im Schulcertifikat in der Handschrift des\n Schulmeisters ausgefüllt, ditto sein Vor- und Zuname von ihm selbst unter-\n schrieben sein müssen“ [Note: id. in „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1855“,\n p. 18, 19.] . Sir John Kincaid, Fabrikinspektor\n für Schottland, erzählt von ähnlichen amtlichen Erfahrungen. „Die erste\n Schule, die wir besuchten, wurde von einer Mrs. Ann Killin gehalten.\n Auf meine Aufforderung, ihren Namen zu buchstabiren, machte sie gleich\n einen Schnitzer, indem sie mit dem Buchstaben C begann, aber sich sofort\n korrigirend sagte, ihr Name fange mit K an. Bei Ansicht ihrer Unter-\n schrift in den Schulcertifikatbüchern bemerkte ich jedoch, dass sie ihn ver-\n schiedenartig buchstabirte, während die Handschrift keinen Zweifel über\n ihre Lehrunfähigkeit liess. Auch gab sie selbst zu, sie könne das Regi-\n ster nicht führen … In einer zweiten Schule fand ich das Schulzimmer\n 15 Fuss lang und 10 Fuss breit und zählte in diesem Raum 75 Kinder,\n die etwas unverständliches herquiekten“ [Note: Sir John Kincaid in „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st\n Oct. 1858“, p. 31, 32.] . „Es sind jedoch nicht nur\n solche Jammerhöhlen, worin die Kinder Schulcertifikate, aber keinen\n Unterricht erhalten, denn in vielen Schulen, wo der Lehrer kompetent ist,\n scheitern seine Bemühungen fast ganz an dem sinnverwirrenden Knäuel\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"390\", \"facs\": \"#f0409\"} --> von Kindern aller Alter, aufwärts von Dreijährigen. Sein Auskommen,\n elend im besten Fall, hängt ganz von der Zahl der Pence ab, empfangen\n von der grössten Anzahl Kinder, die es möglich ist in ein Zimmer zu\n stopfen. Dazu kommt spärliche Schulmöblirung, Mangel an Büchern und\n andrem Lehrmaterial, und die niederschlagende Wirkung einer benauten\n und ekelhaften Luft auf die armen Kinder selbst. Ich war in vielen sol-\n chen Schulen, wo ich ganze Reihen Kinder sah, die absolut nichts thaten;\n und diess wird als Schulbesuch bescheinigt, und solche Kinder figuriren in\n der officiellen Statistik als erzogen (educated)“ [Note: Leonhard Horner in „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1856“,\n p. 17, 18.] . In Schottland suchen\n die Fabrikanten dem Schulbesuch unterworfne Kinder möglichst auszu-\n schliessen. „Diess genügt, um die grosse Missgunst der Fabri-\n kanten gegen die Erziehungsklauseln zu beweisen“ [Note: id. l. c. p. 66.] .\n Grotesk-entsetzlich erscheint diess in den Kattun- u. s. w. Drucke-\n reien, die durch ein eignes Fabrikgesetz geregelt sind. Nach den Be-\n stimmungen des Gesetzes „muss jedes Kind, bevor es in einer solchen\n Druckerei beschäftigt wird, Schule besucht haben für mindestens 30 Tage\n und nicht weniger als 150 Stunden während der 6 Monate, die dem ersten\n Tag seiner Beschäftigung unmittelbar vorhergehn. Während der Fort-\n dauer seiner Beschäftigung in der Druckerei muss es Schule besuchen\n ebenfalls für eine Periode von 30 Tagen und 150 Stunden während jeder\n Wechselperiode von 6 Monaten … Der Schulbesuch muss zwischen\n 8 Uhr Morgens und 6 Uhr Nachmittags stattfinden. Kein Besuch von\n weniger als 2½ oder mehr als 5 Stunden an demselben Tag soll als Theil\n der 150 Stunden gezählt werden. Unter gewöhnlichen Umständen besu-\n chen die Kinder die Schule Vormittags und Nachmittags für 30 Tage,\n 5 Stunden per Tag, und nach Ablauf der 30 Tage, wenn die statuten-\n mässige Gesammtsumme von 150 Stunden erreicht ist, wenn sie, in ihrer\n eignen Sprache zu reden, ihr Buch abgemacht haben, kehren sie zur\n Druckerei zurück, wo sie wieder 6 Monate bleiben, bis ein andrer Ab-\n schlagstermin des Schulbesuchs fällig wird, und dann bleiben sie wieder\n in der Schule, bis das Buch wieder abgemacht ist . . . . Sehr viele Jungen,\n welche die Schule während der vorschriftsmässigen 150 Stunden besucht,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"391\", \"facs\": \"#f0410\"} --> sind bei ihrer Rückkehr aus dem sechsmonatlichen Aufenthalt in der\n Druckerei grade so weit wie im Anfang … Sie haben natürlich alles wie-\n der verloren, was sie durch den früheren Schulbesuch gewonnen hatten.\n In andern Kattundruckereien wird der Schulbesuch ganz und gar abhängig\n gemacht von den Geschäftsbedürfnissen der Fabrik. Die erforderliche\n Stundenzahl wird vollgemacht während jeder sechsmonatlichen Periode durch\n Abschlagszahlungen von 3 bis 5 Stunden auf einmal, die vielleicht über 6 Mo-\n nate zerstreut sind. Z. B. an einem Tage wird die Schule besucht von\n 8 bis 11 Uhr Morgens, an einem andern Tag von 1 bis 4 Uhr Nachmit-\n tags, und nachdem das Kind dann wieder für eine Reihe Tage weggeblie-\n ben, kömmt es plötzlich wieder von 3 bis 6 Uhr Nachmittags; dann er-\n scheint es vielleicht für 3 oder 4 Tage hinter einander, oder für eine Woche,\n verschwindet dann wieder für 3 Wochen oder einen ganzen Monat und\n kehrt zurück an einigen Abfallstagen für einige Sparstunden, wenn seine\n Anwender seiner zufällig nicht bedürfen; und so wird das Kind so zu sa-\n gen hin und her gepufft (buffeted) von der Schule in die Fabrik, von der\n Fabrik in die Schule, bis die Summe der 150 Stunden abgezählt ist“ [Note: A. Redgrave in „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 30th June\n 1857“, p. 41, 42. In den englischen Industriezweigen, wo der eigentliche Fabrik-\n akt (nicht der zuletzt im Text angeführte Print Works’ Act) seit längerer Zeit\n herrscht, sind die Hindernisse gegen die Erziehungsklauseln in den letzten Jahren\n einigermassen überwältigt worden. In den nicht dem Fabrikgesetz unterworfenen\n Industrien herrschen noch sehr die Ansichten des Glasfabrikanten J. Geddes, der\n den Untersuchungskommissär White dahin belehrt: „So viel ich sehn kann, ist das\n grössere Quantum Erziehung, welches ein Theil der Arbeiterklasse seit den letzten\n Jahren genoss, vom Uebel. Es ist gefährlich, indem es sie zu unabhängig macht.“\n („Children’s Empl. Commission. IV. Report. London 1865“,\n p. 253.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDurch den überwiegenden Zusatz von Kindern und Weibern zum kom-\n binirten Arbeitspersonal bricht die Maschinerie endlich den Widerstand,\n den der männliche Arbeiter in der Manufaktur der Despotie des Kapitals\n noch entgegensetzte [Note: „Herr E., ein Fabrikant, unterrichtete mich, dass er ausschliesslich\n Weiber bei seinen mechanischen Webstühlen beschäftigt; er gebe verheirathe-\n ten Weibern den Vorzug, besonders solchen mit Familie zu Hause, die von ihnen\n für den Unterhalt abhängen; sie sind viel aufmerksamer und gelehriger als unver-] .\n\n\n\n\nWenn die Maschinerie das gewaltigste Mittel ist, die Produktivität der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"392\", \"facs\": \"#f0411\"} --> Arbeit zu steigern, d. h. die zur Produktion einer Waare nöthige Arbeits-\n zeit zu verkürzen, wird sie als Träger des Kapitals, zunächst\n in den unmittelbar von ihr ergriffenen Industrien, zum gewaltigsten Mittel\n den Arbeitstag über jede naturgemässe Schranke hinaus zu verlän-\n gern. Sie schafft einerseits neue Bedingungen, welche das\n Kapital befähigen, dieser seiner beständigen Tendenz die Zügel frei\n schiessen zu lassen, andrerseits neue Motive zur Wetzung seines Heiss-\n hungers nach fremder Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nZunächst verselbstständigt sich in der Maschinerie die Bewegung und\n Werkthätigkeit des Arbeitsmittels gegenüber dem Arbeiter. Es\n wird an und für sich ein industrielles Perpetuum mobile, das ununterbrochen\n fortproduciren würde, stiesse es nicht auf gewisse Naturschranken in seinen\n menschlichen Gehilfen, ihre Körperschwäche und ihren Eigenwillen. Als\nKapital, und als solches besitzt der Automat im Kapitalisten Bewusst-\n sein und Willen, ist es daher mit dem Trieb begeistet, die widerstrebende,\n aber elastische menschliche Naturschranke auf den Minimalwiderstand ein-\n zuzwängen [Note: „Since the general introduction of expensive machinery, human nature\n has been forced far beyond its average strength.“ (Robert Owen: „Ob-\n servations on the effects of the manufacturing system. 2nd ed.\n Lond. 1817.“)] . Dieser ist ohnehin vermindert durch die scheinbare Leich-\n tigkeit der Arbeit an der Maschine und das füg- und biegsamere Weiber-\n und Kinderelement [Note: Die Engländer, die gern die erste empirische Erscheinungsform einer\n Sache als ihren Grund betrachten, geben oft den grossen herodischen Kinderraub,\n den das Kapital in den Anfängen des Fabriksystems an den Armen- und Waisen-\n häusern verübte und wodurch es sich ein ganz willenloses Menschenmaterial\n einverleibte, als Grund der langen Arbeitszeit in den Fabriken an. So z. B.\nFielden, selbst englischer Fabrikant: „It is evident that the long hours of work\n were brought about by the circumstance of so great a number of destitute children\n being supplied from different parts of the country, that the masters were indepen-] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Produktivität der Maschinerie steht, wie wir sahen, in umgekehr-\n [Note: heirathete, und zur äussersten Anstrengung ihrer Kräfte gezwungen, um die noth-\n wendigen Lebensmittel beizuschaffen. So werden die Tugenden, die eigenthüm-\n lichen Tugenden des weiblichen Charakters zu seinem Schaden verkehrt, — so\n wird alles Sittliche und Zarte ihrer Natur zum Mittel ihrer Sklaverei und ihres\n Leidens gemacht.“ („Ten Hours’ Factory Bill. The Speech of Lord\n Ashley. Lond. 1844“, p. 20.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"393\", \"facs\": \"#f0412\"} --> tem Verhältniss zur Grösse des von ihr auf das Machwerk übertragenen\n Werthbestandtheils. Je länger die Periode, worin sie funktionirt, desto\n grösser die Produktenmasse, worüber sich der von ihr zugesetzte Werth\n vertheilt, und desto kleiner der Werththeil, den sie der einzelnen Waare zu-\n fügt. Die aktive Lebensperiode der Maschinerie ist aber offenbar bestimmt\n durch die Länge des Arbeitstags oder die Dauer des täglichen Ar-\n beitsprozesses, multiplicirt mit der Anzahl Tage, worin er sich wie-\n derholt.\n\n\n\n\nDer Maschinenverschleiss entspricht keineswegs exakt mathematisch\n ihrer Benutzungszeit. Und selbst diess vorausgesetzt, umfasst eine Ma-\n schine, die während 7½ Jahren täglich 16 Stunden dient, eine ebenso\n grosse Produktionsperiode und setzt dem Gesammtprodukt nicht mehr\n Werth zu als dieselbe Maschine, die während 15 Jahren nur 8 Stunden\n täglich dient. Im erstern Fall aber wäre der Maschinenwerth doppelt so\n rasch reproducirt als im letztern und der Kapitalist hätte vermittelst der-\n selben in 7½ Jahren so viel Mehrarbeit eingeschluckt als sonst in 15.\n\n\n\n\nDer materielle Verschleiss der Maschine ist doppelt. Der eine ent-\n springt aus ihrem Gebrauch, wie Geldstücke durch Cirkulation verschleis-\n sen, der andre aus ihrem Nichtgebrauch, wie ein unthätig Schwert in der\n Scheide verrostet. Es ist diess ihr Verzehr durch die Elemente. Der\n Verschleiss erster Art steht mehr oder minder in direktem Verhältniss,\n der letztere, zu gewissem Grad, in umgekehrtem Verhältniss zu ihrem\n Gebrauch [Note: „Le domage que cause l’inaction des machines à des pièces de metal\n mobiles et délicates.“ (Ure l. c. t. II, p. 8.)] .\n\n\n\n\nNeben dem materiellen unterliegt die Maschine aber auch einem so\n zu sagen moralischen Verschleiss. Sie verliert Tauschwerth im\n [Note: dent of the hands, and that, having once established the custom by means of the\n miserable materials they had procured in this way, they could impose it on their\n neighbours with the greater facility.“ (J. Fielden: „The Curse of the\n Factory System. Lond. 1836.“) Mit Bezug auf Weiberarbeit sagt Fabrik-\n inspektor Saunders im Fabrikbericht von 1844: „Unter den Arbeiterinnen giebt\n es Frauen, die hinter einander für viele Wochen, mit Ausfall nur weniger Tage,\n von 6 Uhr Morgens bis 12 Uhr Nachts beschäftigt werden, mit weniger als 2 Stun-\n den für Mahlzeiten, so dass ihnen für 5 Tage in der Woche von den 24 Tages-\n stunden nur 6 bleiben, um von und nach Haus zu gehn und im Bett auszuruhn.“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"394\", \"facs\": \"#f0413\"} --> Masse, worin entweder Maschinen derselben Konstruktion wohlfeiler reprodu-\n cirt werden können oder bessere Maschinen konkurrirend neben sie treten [Note: Der schon früher erwähnte „Manchester Spinner“ (Times, 26. Nov.\n 1862) zählt unter den Kosten der Maschinerie auf: „It (nämlich die „allowance\n for deterioration of machinery“) is also intended to cover the loss which is con-\n stantly arising from the superseding of machines before they are worn out by\n others of a new and better construction.“] .\n In beiden Fällen ist ihr Werth, so jung und lebenskräftig sie sonst noch sein\n mag, nicht mehr bestimmt durch die thatsächlich in ihr selbst vergegen-\n ständlichte, sondern durch die zu ihrer eignen Reproduktion oder zur Repro-\n duktion der besseren Maschine nothwendige Arbeitszeit. Sie ist daher mehr\n oder minder entwerthet. Je kürzer die Periode, worin ihr Gesammt-\n werth reproducirt wird, desto geringer die Gefahr des moralischen Ver-\n schleisses, und je länger der Arbeitstag, um so kürzer jene Periode.\n Bei der ersten Einführung der Maschinerie in irgend einen Produktions-\n zweig folgen Schlag auf Schlag neue Methoden zu ihrer wohlfeileren Re-\n produktion [Note: „Man schätzt im Grossen, dass eine einzige Maschine nach einem neuen\n Model zu konstruiren fünfmal so viel kostet, als die Rekonstruktion derselben\n Maschine nach demselben Model.“ (Babbage l. c. p. 349.)] , und Verbesserungen, die nicht nur einzelne Theile oder\n Apparate, sondern ihre ganze Konstruktion ergreifen. In ihrer ersten\n Lebensperiode wirkt daher diess besondre Motiv zur Verlängerung\n des Arbeitstags am akutesten [Note: „Seit einigen Jahren sind so bedeutende und zahlreiche Verbesserungen\n in der Tüllfabrikation gemacht worden, dass eine gut erhaltene Maschine zum ur-\n sprünglichen Kostenpreis von 1200 Pfd. St. einige Jahre später zu 60 Pfd. St.\n verkauft wurde . . . . Die Verbesserungen folgten sich mit solcher Geschwindig-\n keit, dass Maschinen unvollendet in der Hand ihrer Bauer blieben, weil sie durch\n glücklichere Erfindungen bereits veraltet waren.“ In dieser Sturm- und Drang-\n periode dehnten daher die Tüllfabrikanten bald die ursprüngliche Arbeitszeit von\n 8 Stunden mit doppelter Mannschaft auf 24 Stunden aus. (l. c. p. 377, 378\n u. 279.)] .\n\n\n\n\nUnter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen und bei gegebnem Arbeitstag\n erheischt Exploitation verdoppelter Arbeiteranzahl ebensowohl Ver-\n dopplung des in Maschinerie und Baulichkeiten ausgelegten Theils des\n constanten Kapitals als des in Rohmaterial, Hilfsstoffen u. s. w. ausgelegten.\n Mit verlängertem Arbeitstag dehnt sich die Stufenleiter der Pro-\n duktion, während der in Maschinerie und Baulichkeiten ausgelegte Kapi-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"395\", \"facs\": \"#f0414\"} --> taltheil unverändert bleibt [Note: „It is self-evident, that, amid the ebbings and flowings of the market,\n and the alternate expansions and contractions of demand, occasions will con-\n stantly recur, in which the manufacturer may employ additional floating capital\n without employing additional fixed capital … if additional quantities of raw\n material can be worked up without incurring an additional expence for buildings\n and machinery.“ (R. Torrens: „On Wages and Combination. Lond.\n 1834“, p. 63.)] . Nicht nur der Mehrwerth wächst daher, son-\n dern die zur Erbeutung desselben nothwendigen Auslagen nehmen ab.\n Zwar findet diess auch sonst mehr oder minder bei aller Verlängerung des Ar-\n beitstags statt, fällt aber hier entscheidender ins Gewicht, weil der in Arbeits-\n mittel verwandelte Kapitaltheil überhaupt mehr ins Gewicht fällt [Note: Der im Text erwähnte Umstand ist nur der Vollständigkeit wegen er-\n wähnt, da ich erst im dritten Buch die Profitrate, d. h. das Verhältniss des\n Mehrwerths zum vorgeschossenen Gesammtkapital, behandle.] . Die\n Entwicklung des Maschinenbetriebs bindet nämlich einen stets wachsenden\n Bestandtheil des Kapitals in eine Form, worin es einerseits fortwährend\nverwerthbar ist, andrerseits Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth ver-\n liert, sobald sein Kontakt mit der lebendigen Arbeit unterbrochen wird.\n „Wenn“, belehrte Herr Ashworth, ein englischer Baumwollmagnat,\n den Professor Nassau W. Senior, „wenn ein Ackersmann seinen Spaten\n niederlegt, macht er für diese Periode ein Kapital von 18 d. nutzlos.\n Wenn einer von unsern Leuten (d. h. den Fabrikarbeitern) die Fabrik ver-\n lässt, macht er ein Kapital nutzlos, das 100000 Pfd. St.\n gekostet hat“ [Note: „When a labourer“, said Mr. Ashworth, „lays down his spade, he\n renders useless, for that period, a capital worth 18 d. When one of our people\n leaves the mill, he renders useless a capital that has cost 100,000 Pd. St.\n (Senior: „Letters on the Factory Act. Lond. 1837“, p. 13, 14.)] . Man denke nur! Ein Kapital, das 100000 Pfd. St. ge-\n kostet hat, auch nur für einen Augenblick „nutzlos“ zu machen! Es ist\n in der That himmelschreiend, dass einer unsrer Leute überhaupt jemals\n die Fabrik verlässt! Der wachsende Umfang der Maschinerie macht, wie\n der von Ashworth belehrte Senior einsieht, eine stets wachsende Verlän-\n gerung des Arbeitstags „wünschenswerth“ [Note: „The great proportion of fixed to circulating capital … makes long\n hours of work desirable.“ Mit dem wachsenden Umfang der Maschinerie\n u. s. w. „the motives to long hours of work will become greater, as the only\n means by which a large proportion of fixed capital can be made profitable.“ (l. c.] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"396\", \"facs\": \"#f0415\"} -->\n\nDie Maschine producirt relativen Mehrwerth, nicht nur indem sie\n die Arbeitskraft direkt entwerthet und dieselbe indirekt durch Verwohl-\n feilerung der in ihre Reproduktion eingehenden Waaren verwohlfeilert,\n sondern auch, indem sie bei ihrer ersten sporadischen Einführung die vom\n Maschinenbesitzer verwandte Arbeit in potenzirte Arbeit verwandelt, den\n gesellschaftlichen Werth des Maschinenprodukts über seinen individuellen\n Werth erhöht und den Kapitalisten so befähigt mit geringerem Werththeil\n des Tagesprodukts den Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft zu ersetzen. Wäh-\n rend dieser Uebergangsperiode, worin der Maschinenbetrieb eine Art Mo-\n nopol bleibt, sind daher die Gewinne ausserordentlich und der Kapitalist\n sucht „diese erste Zeit der jungen Liebe“ gründlichst auszubeuten durch\n möglichste Verlängerung des Arbeitstags. Die Grösse des Ge-\n winns wetzt den Heisshunger nach mehr Gewinn.\n\n\n\n\nMit der Verallgemeinerung der Maschinerie im selben Produktions-\n zweig sinkt der gesellschaftliche Werth des Maschinenprodukts auf seinen in-\n dividuellen Werth und macht sich das Gesetz geltend, dass der Mehrwerth\n nicht aus den Arbeitskräften entspringt, welche der\n Kapitalist durch die Maschine ersetzt hat, sondern\n umgekehrt aus den Arbeitskräften, welche er an ihr\n beschäftigt. Der Mehrwerth entspringt nur aus dem variab-\n len Theil des Kapitals, und wir sahen, dass die Masse des\n Mehrwerths durch zwei Faktoren bestimmt ist, die Rate des Mehr-\n werths und die Anzahl der gleichzeitig beschäftigten Ar-\n beiter. Bei gegebner Länge des Arbeitstags wird die Rate des Mehr-\n werths bestimmt durch das Verhältniss, worin der Arbeitstag in nothwen-\n dige Arbeit und Mehrarbeit zerfällt. Die Anzahl der gleichzeitig beschäf-\n tigten Arbeiter hängt ihrerseits ab von dem Verhältniss des variablen Ka-\n pitaltheils zum constanten. Es ist nun klar, dass wie er immer durch\n Steigerung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit die Mehrarbeit auf Kosten der\n [Note: p. 11—13.) „Es giebt verschiedne Auslagen bei einer Fabrik, welche constant\n bleiben, ob die Fabrik mehr oder weniger Zeit arbeite, z. B. Rente für die Bau-\n lichkeiten, lokale und allgemeine Steuern, Feuerversicherung, Arbeitslohn für ver-\n schiedne permanente Arbeiter, Verschlechterung der Maschinerie nebst verschied-\n nen andren Lasten, deren Proportion zum Profit im selben Verhältniss wächst, wie\n der Umfang der Produktion zunimmt.“ („Reports of the Insp. of Fact.\n for 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 19.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"397\", \"facs\": \"#f0416\"} --> nothwendigen Arbeit ausdehne, der Maschinenbetrieb diess Resultat nur\n hervorbringt, indem er die Anzahl der von einem gegebnen Kapital\n beschäftigten Arbeiter vermindert. Er verwandelt einen Theil des\n Kapitals, der früher variabel war, d. h. sich in lebendige Arbeitskraft\n umsetzte, in Maschinerie, also in constantes Kapital, das keinen Mehr-\n werth producirt. Es ist unmöglich z. B. aus zwei Arbeitern so viel Mehr-\n werth auszupressen als aus 24. Wenn jeder der 24 Arbeiter auf 12\n Stunden nur eine Stunde Mehrarbeit liefert, liefern sie zusammen 24 Stun-\n den Mehrarbeit, während die Gesammtarbeit der zwei Arbeiter\n nur 24 Stunden beträgt. Es liegt also in der Anwendung der Maschine-\n rie zur Produktion von Mehrwerth ein immanenter Widerspruch,\n indem sie von den beiden Faktoren des Mehrwerths, den ein Kapital von\n gegebner Grösse liefert, den einen Faktor, die Rate des Mehrwerths,\n nur dadurch vergrössert, dass sie den andern Faktor, die Arbeiterzahl,\nverkleinert. Dieser immanente Widerspruch tritt hervor, sobald mit der\n Verallgemeinerung der Maschinerie in einem Industriezweig der Werth der\n maschinenmässig producirten Waare zum regelnden gesellschaftlichen\n Werth aller Waaren derselben Art wird, und es ist dieser Widerspruch, der\n wiederum das Kapital, ohne dass es sich dessen bewusst wäre [Note: Warum dieser immanente Widerspruch dem einzelnen Kapitalisten und\n daher auch der in seinen Anschauungen befangenen politischen Oekonomie nicht\n zum Bewusstsein kommt, wird man aus den ersten Kapiteln des dritten Buchs\n ersehn.] , zur ge-\n waltsamsten Verlängerung des Arbeitstags treibt, um die Ab-\n nahme in der verhältnissmässigen Anzahl der exploitirten Arbeiter\n durch Zunahme nicht nur der relativen, sondern auch der absoluten\n Mehrarbeit zu kompensiren.\n\n\n\n\nWenn also die kapitalistische Anwendung der Maschinerie\n einerseits neue mächtige Motive zur masslosen Verlängerung des Arbeitstags\n schafft und die Arbeitsweise selbst wie den Charakter des gesell-\n schaftlichen Arbeitskörpers in einer Art umwälzt, die den Wider-\n stand gegen diese Tendenz bricht, producirt sie andrerseits, theils durch Ein-\n rollirung dem Kapital früher unzugänglicher Schichten der Arbeiterklasse,\n theils durch Freisetzung der von der Maschine verdrängten Arbeiter, eine\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"398\", \"facs\": \"#f0417\"} -->überflüssige Arbeiterpopulation [Note: Es ist eins der grossen Verdienste Ricardo’s, die Maschinerie nicht nur\n als Produktionsmittel von Waaren, sondern auch von „redundant population“\n begriffen zu haben.] , die sich das Gesetz vom\n Kapital diktiren lassen muss. Daher das merkwürdige Phänomen in der\n Geschichte der modernen Industrie, dass die Maschine alle sittlichen und\n natürlichen Schranken des Arbeitstags über den Haufen wirft. Daher das\n ökonomische Paradoxon, dass das gewaltigste Mittel zur Verkür-\n zung der Arbeitszeit in das unfehlbarste Mittel umschlägt alle\n Lebenszeit des Arbeiters und seiner Familie in disponible Ar-\n beitszeit für die Verwerthung des Kapitals zu verwandeln. „Wenn“,\n träumte Aristoteles, der grösste Denker des Alterthums, „wenn jedes\n Werkzeug auf Geheiss, oder auch vorausahnend, das ihm zukommende\n Werk verrichten könnte, wie des Dädalus Kunstwerke sich von selbst be-\n wegten, oder die Dreifüsse des Hephästos aus eignem Antrieb an die heilige\n Arbeit gingen, wenn so die Weberschiffe von selbst webten,\n so bedürfte es weder für den Werkmeister der Gehilfen, noch für die Herrn\n der Sklaven“ [Note: F. Biese: „Die Philosophie des Aristoteles.“ Zweiter\n Band. Berl. 1842, p. 408.] . Und Antiparos, ein griechischer Dichter aus der\n Zeit des Cicero, begrüsste die Erfindung der Wassermühle zum Mah-\n len des Getreides, diese Elementarform aller produktiven Maschinerie, als\n Befreierin der Sklavinnen und Herstellerin des goldnen Zeitalters! [Note: Ich gebe hier die Stolbergsche Uebersetzung des Gedichts, weil es ganz\n so wie die früheren Citate über Theilung der Arbeit den Gegensatz der antiken\n Anschauung zur modernen charakterisirt.\n\n\n\n\n„Schonet der mahlenden Hand, o Müllerinnen, und schlafet\n\n\n\n\nSanft! es verkünde der Hahn euch den Morgen umsonst!\n\n\n\n\nDäo hat die Arbeit der Mädchen den Nymphen befohlen,\n\n\n\n\nUnd itzt hüpfen sie leicht über die Räder dahin,\n\n\n\n\nDass die erschütterten Achsen mit ihren Speichen sich wälzen,\n\n\n\n\nUnd im Kreise die Last drehen des wälzenden Steins.\n\n\n\n\nLasst uns leben das Leben der Väter, und lasst uns der Gaben\n\n\n\n\nArbeitslos uns freuen, welche die Göttin uns schenkt.“\n\n\n\n\n(„Gedichte aus dem Griechischen übersetzt von Christian\n Graf zu Stolberg. Hamburg 1782.“)] \n „Die Heiden, ja die Heiden!“ Sie begriffen, wie der gescheidte Bastiat\n entdeckt hat, und schon vor ihm der noch klügere Mac Culloch, nichts\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"399\", \"facs\": \"#f0418\"} --> von politischer Oekonomie und Christent um. Sie begriffen u. a. nicht,\n dass die Maschine das probateste Mittel zur Verlängerung des Arbeitstags\n ist. Sie entschuldigten etwa die Sklaverei des Einen als Mittel zur vollen\n menschlichen Entwicklung des Andern. Aber Sklaverei der Massen pre-\n digen, um einige rohe oder halbgebildete Parvenüs zu „eminent spinners“,\n „extensive sausage makers“ und „influential shoe black dealers“ zu machen,\n dazu fehlte ihnen das specifisch christliche Organ.\n\n\n\n\nDie masslose Verlängerung des Arbeitstags, welche die Ma-\n schinerie in der Hand des Kapitals producirt, führt, wie wir sahen, später eine\nReaktion der in ihrer Lebenswurzel bedrohten Gesellschaft herbei, und\n damit einen gesetzlich beschränkten Normal-Arbeitstag.\n Auf Grundlage des letztern entwickelt sich ein Phänomen, das uns schon\n früher begegnete, zu entscheidender Wichtigkeit — nämlich die Inten-\n sifikation der Arbeit. Bei der Analyse des absoluten Mehrwerths\n handelte es sich zunächst um die extensive Grösse der Arbeit, wäh-\n rend der Grad ihrer Intensivität als gegeben vorausgesetzt war. Wir ha-\n ben jetzt den Umschlag dieser extensiven in eine intensive oder\nGradgrösse zu betrachten.\n\n\n\n\nEs ist selbstverständlich, dass mit dem Fortschritt des Maschinen-\n wesens und der gehäuften Erfahrung einer eignen Klasse von Maschinen-\n arbeitern die Geschwindigkeit und damit die Intensivität der Arbeit natur-\n wüchsig zunehmen. So geht in England während eines halben Jahr-\n hunderts die Verlängerung des Arbeitstags Hand in Hand mit\n der wachsenden Intensivität der Fabrikarbeit. Indess begreift\n man, dass bei einer Arbeit, wo es sich nicht um vorübergehende Paroxys-\n men handelt, sondern um Tag aus, Tag ein wiederholte, regelmässige\n Gleichförmigkeit, ein Knotenpunkt eintreten muss, wo Ausdehnung des\n Arbeitstags und Intensivität der Arbeit einander ausschliessen, so dass die\n Verlängerung des Arbeitstags nur mit schwächerem Intensivitätsgrad der\n Arbeit und umgekehrt ein erhöhter Intensivitätsgrad nur mit Verkürzung\n des Arbeitstags verträglich bleibt. Sobald die allmälig anschwellende Em-\n pörung der Arbeiterklasse den Staat zwang, die Arbeitszeit gewaltsam zu\n verkürzen und zunächst der eigentlichen Fabrik einen Normal-Arbeitstag zu\n diktiren, von diesem Augenblick also, wo gesteigerte Produktion von Mehr-\n werth durch Verlängerung des Arbeitstags ein für allemal abge-\n schnitten war, warf sich das Kapital mit aller Macht und vollem Bewusst-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"400\", \"facs\": \"#f0419\"} --> sein auf die Produktion von relativem Mehrwerth durch beschleu-\n nigte Entwicklung des Maschinensystems. Gleichzeitig tritt eine Aende-\n rung in dem Charakter des relativen Mehrwerths ein. Im Allgemeinen\n besteht die Produktionsmethode des relativen Mehrwerths darin, durch\n gesteigerte Produktivkraft der Arbeit den Arbeiter zu befähigen mit der-\n selben Arbeitsausgabe in derselben Zeit mehr zu produciren.\nDieselbe Arbeitszeit setzt nach wie vor dem Gesammtprodukt\ndenselben Werth zu, obgleich dieser unveränderte Tauschwerth sich\n jetzt in mehr Gebrauchswerthen darstellt und daher der Werth der ein-\n zelnen Waare sinkt. Anders jedoch, sobald die gewaltsame Verkür-\n zung des Arbeitstags mit dem ungeheuren Anstoss, den sie der\nEntwicklung der Produktivkraft und der Oekonomisirung\n der Produktionsbedingungen giebt, zugleich vergrösserte\n Arbeitsausgabe in derselben Zeit, erhöhte Anspannung der\n Arbeitskraft, dichtere Ausfüllung der Poren der Arbeitszeit, d. h. Konden-\n sation der Arbeit dem Arbeiter zu einem Grad aufzwingt, der nur inner-\n halb des verkürzten Arbeitstags erreichbar ist. Diese Zusam-\n menpressung einer grösseren Masse Arbeit in eine gegebene Zeitperiode\n zählt jetzt als was sie ist, als grösseres Arbeitsquantum. Neben\n das Mass der Arbeitszeit als „ausgedehnter Grösse“ tritt jetzt das Mass\n ihres Verdichtungsgrads [Note: Es finden natürlich überhaupt Unterschiede in der Intensivität der Arbei-\n ten verschiedner Produktionszweige statt. Diese kompensiren sich,\n wie schon A. Smith gezeigt hat, zum Theil durch jeder Arbeitsart eigne Nebenum-\n stände. Einwirkung auf die Arbeitszeit als Werthmass findet aber auch hier nur\n statt, soweit intensive und extensive Grösse sich als entgegengesetzte und\n einander ausschliessende Ausdrücke desselben Arbeitsquantums darstellen.] . Die intensivere Stunde des zehnstün-\n digen Arbeitstags enthält jetzt so viel oder mehr Arbeit, d. h. veraus-\n gabte Arbeitskraft als die porösere Stunde des zwölfstündigen Arbeits-\n tags. Ihr Produkt hat daher so viel oder mehr Werth als das der poröseren\n 1⅕ Stunden. Abgesehn von der Erhöhung des relativen Mehrwerths\n durch die gesteigerte Produktivkraft der Arbeit, liefern jetzt z. B. 3⅓\n Stunden Mehrarbeit auf 6⅔ Stunden nothwendiger Arbeit dem Kapitali-\n sten dieselbe Werthmasse wie vorher 4 Stunden Mehrarbeit auf 8 Stun-\n den nothwendiger Arbeit.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"401\", \"facs\": \"#f0420\"} -->\n\nEs fragt sich nun, wie wird die Arbeit intensificirt?\n\n\n\n\nDie erste Wirkung des verkürzten Arbeitstags beruht auf\n dem selbstverständlichen Gesetz, dass die Wirkungsfähigkeit der Arbeits-\n kraft im umgekehrten Verhältniss zu ihrer Wirkungszeit steht. Es wird daher,\n innerhalb gewisser Grenzen, am Grad der Kraftäusserung gewonnen, was\n an ihrer Dauer verloren geht. Dass der Arbeiter aber auch wirklich mehr\n Arbeitskraft flüssig macht, dafür sorgt das Kapital durch die Methode\n der Zahlung [Note: Namentlich durch den Stücklohn, eine Form, die im nächsten Kapitel\n entwickelt wird.] . In Manufakturen, der Töpferei z. B., wo die Maschinerie\n keine oder unbedeutende Rolle spielt, hat die Einführung des Fabrikge-\n setzes schlagend bewiesen, dass blosse Verkürzung des Arbeitstags\n die Regelmässigkeit, Gleichförmigkeit, Ordnung, Kontinuität und Energie der\n Arbeit wundervoll erhöht [Note: Sieh „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1865.“] . Diese Wirkung schien jedoch zweifelhaft in der\neigentlichen Fabrik, weil die Abhängigkeit des Arbeiters von der\n kontinuirlichen und gleichförmigen Bewegung der Maschine hier längst die\n strengste Disciplin geschaffen hatte. Als daher 1844 die Herabsetzung des\n Arbeitstags unter 12 Stunden verhandelt ward, erklärten die Fabrikanten fast\n einstimmig, „ihre Aufseher passten in den verschiednen Arbeitsräumen auf,\n dass die Hände keine Zeit verlören“, „der Grad der Wachsamkeit und\n Aufmerksamkeit auf Seiten der Arbeiter („the extent of vigilance and atten-\n tion on the part of the workmen“) sei kaum steigerungsfähig“, und alle\n anderen Umstände, wie Gang der Maschinerie u. s. w. als gleichblei-\n bend vorausgesetzt, „sei es daher Unsinn in wohlgeführten Fabriken von\n der gesteigerten Aufmerksamkeit u. s. w. der Arbeiter irgend ein erkleck-\n liches Resultat zu erwarten“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 1844 and the quarter ending\n 30th April 1845“, p. 20, 21.] . Diese Behauptung ward durch Ex-\n perimente widerlegt. Herr R. Gardner liess in seinen zwei grossen\n Fabriken zu Preston vom 20. April 1844 an statt 12 nur noch 11 Stunden\n per Tag arbeiten. Nach ungefähr Jahresfrist ergab sich das Resultat,\n dass „dasselbe Quantum Produkt zur selben Kost erhalten ward, und\n sämmtliche Arbeiter in 11 Stunden eben so viel Arbeitslohn verdienten,\n [Running matter: I. 26] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"402\", \"facs\": \"#f0421\"} --> wie früher in 12“ [Note: l. c. p. 19. Da der Stücklohn derselbe blieb, hing die Höhe des Wochen-\n lohns vom Quantum des Produkts ab.] . Ich übergehe hier die Experimente in den Spinn-\n und Kardirräumen, weil sie mit Zunahme in der Geschwindigkeit der Ma-\n schinerie (um 2 %) verbunden waren. In dem Webedepartement dagegen,\n wo zudem sehr verschiedene Sorten leichter, figurenhaltiger Phanta-\n sieartikel gewebt wurden, fand durchaus keine Aenderung in den objek-\n tiven Produktionsbedingungen statt. Das Resultat war: „Vom 6. Januar\n bis 20. April 1844, mit zwölfstündigem Arbeitstag, wöchentlicher Durch-\n schnittslohn jedes Arbeiters 10 sh. 1½ d., vom 20. April bis 29. Juni\n 1844, mit elfstündigem Arbeitstag, wöchentlicher Durchschnittslohn 10 sh.\n 3½ d.“ [Note: l. c. p. 20.] . Es wurde hier in 11 Stunden mehr producirt als früher in\n 12, ausschliesslich in Folge grösserer gleichmässiger Ausdauer der Arbei-\n ter und Oekonomie ihrer Zeit. Während sie denselben Lohn empfingen\n und 1 Stunde freie Zeit gewannen, erhielt der Kapitalist dieselbe Pro-\n duktenmasse und sparte Verausgabung von Kohle, Gas u. s. w. für eine\n Stunde. Aehnliche Experimente wurden mit gleichem Erfolg in den Fa-\n briken der Herren Horrocks und Jacson ausgeführt [Note: l. c. p. 21. Das moralische Element spielte bedeutende Rolle in den\n oben erwähnten Experimenten. „We“, erklärten die Arbeiter dem Fabrikinspek-\n tor, „we work with more spirit, we have the reward ever before us of getting\n away sooner at night, and one active and cheerful spirit pervades the whole mill,\n from the youngest piecer to the oldest hand, and we can greatly help each\n other.“ (l. c.)] .\n\n\n\n\nSobald die Verkürzung des Arbeitstags, welche zunächst die\nsubjektive Bedingung der Kondensation der Arbeit schafft, nämlich die\n Fähigkeit des Arbeiters mehr Kraft in gegebner Zeit flüssig zu machen,\nzwangsgesetzlich wird, wandelt sich die Maschine in der Hand des Ka-\n pitals zum objektiven und systematisch angewandten Mit-\n tel mehr Arbeit in derselben Zeit zu erpressen. Es geschieht diess in\n doppelter Weise, durch die erhöhte Geschwindigkeit der Ma-\n schinen und den erweiterten Umfang der von demselben Arbeiter\n zu überwachenden Maschinerie oder seines Arbeitsfeldes. Verbesserte\n Konstruktion der Maschinerie ist theils nothwendig zur Ausübung des\n grösseren Drucks auf den Arbeiter, theils begleitet sie von selbst die Inten-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"403\", \"facs\": \"#f0422\"} --> sifikation der Arbeit, weil die Schranke des Arbeitstags den Kapitalisten\n zu strengstem Haushalt der Produktionskosten zwingt. Die Verbesserung\n der Dampfmaschine erhöht die Anzahl ihrer Kolbenschläge in einer Minute\n und erlaubt zugleich, durch grössere Oekonomie der Kraft, einen umfang-\n reicheren Mechanismus mit demselben Motor zu treiben, bei gleichbleiben-\n dem oder selbst fallendem Kohlenverzehr. Die Verbesserung des Trans-\n missionsmechanismus vermindert die Friktion und, was die moderne\n Maschinerie so augenfällig vor der älteren auszeichnet, reducirt Durch-\n messer und Gewicht der grossen und kleinen Wellenbäume auf ein stets\n fallendes Minimum. Die Verbesserungen der Arbeitsmaschinerie endlich\nvermindern bei erhöhter Geschwindigkeit und ausgedehnterer Wirkung\n ihren Umfang, wie beim modernen Dampfwebstuhl, oder vergrössern mit\n dem Rumpf Umfang und Zahl der von ihr geführten Werkzeuge, wie bei der\n Spinnmaschine, oder vermehren die Beweglichkeit dieser Werkzeuge durch\n unscheinbare Detailveränderung, wie durch solche bei der selfacting mule\n vor etwa 10 Jahren die Geschwindigkeit der Spindeln um ⅕ gesteigert wurde.\n\n\n\n\nDie Verkürzung des Arbeitstags auf 12 Stunden datirt in Eng-\n land von 1832. Schon 1836 erklärte ein englischer Fabrikant: „Ver-\n glichen mit früher ist die Arbeit, die in den Fabriken zu verrichten, sehr\n gewachsen, in Folge der grösseren Aufmerksamkeit und Thätigkeit, welche\n die bedeutend vermehrte Geschwindigkeit der Maschinerie vom Arbeiter\n erheischt“ [Note: John Fielden l. c. p. 32.] . Im Jahr 1844 machte Lord Ashley, jetzt Graf Shaftes-\n bury, folgende dokumentarisch belegte Aufstellungen im Hause der Gemeinen:\n\n\n\n\n„Die Arbeit der in den Fabrikprozessen Beschäftigten ist jetzt drei-\n mal so gross, als bei der Einführung solcher Operationen. Die Maschinerie\n hat zweifelsohne ein Werk verrichtet, welches die Sehnen und Muskeln von\n Millionen Menschen ersetzt, aber sie hat auch erstaunlich (prodigiously)\n die Arbeit der durch ihre furchtbare Bewegung beherrschten Menschen\n vermehrt. . . . Die Arbeit einem Paar Mules während 12 Stunden auf- und\n abzufolgen, zum Spinnen von Garn No. 40, bedang im Jahre 1815 die\n Nothwendigkeit einer Reise von 8 Meilen. Im Jahre 1812 betrug die im\n Gefolge eines Mulepaars, zum Spinnen derselben Nummer, während 12\n Stunden zu durchreisende Distanz 20 Meilen und oft mehr. Im Jahre\n 1825 hatte der Spinner während 12 Stunden 820 Auszüge an jeder Mule\n [Running matter: 26*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"404\", \"facs\": \"#f0423\"} --> zu machen, was eine Gesammtsumme von 1640 für 12 Stunden ergab.\n Im Jahre 1832 hatte der Spinner während seines zwölfstündigen Arbeits-\n tags an jeder Mule 2,200 Auszüge zu machen, zusammen 4,400, im Jahre\n 1844 an jeder Mule 2,400, zusammen 4,800; und in einigen Fällen ist die\n erheischte Arbeitsmasse (amount of labour) noch grösser....Ich habe hier\n ein andres Dokument von 1842 in der Hand, worin nachgewiesen wird,\n dass die Arbeit progressiv zunimmt, nicht nur, weil eine grössere\n Entfernung zu durchreisen ist, sondern weil die Quantität der producirten\n Waaren sich vermehrt, während die Hände proportionell abnehmen; und\n ferner, weil nun oft eine schlechtere Baumwolle gesponnen wird, die mehr\n Arbeit erfordert. . . . Im Kardirraum hat auch grosse Zunahme der Arbeit\n stattgefunden. Eine Person thut jetzt die Arbeit, die früher zwischen\n zwei vertheilt war. . . . In der Weberei, worin eine grosse Anzahl Per-\n sonen, meist weiblichen Geschlechts beschäftigt ist, ist die Arbeit während\n der letzten Jahre um volle 10 % gewachsen, in Folge der vermehrten\n Geschwindigkeit der Maschinerie. Im Jahre 1838 war die Zahl der\n hanks, die wöchentlich gesponnen wurde, 18,000, im Jahre 1843 belief\n sie sich auf 21,000. Im Jahr 1819 war die Zahl der picks beim Dampf-\n webestuhl 60 per Minute, im Jahre 1842 betrug sie 140, was einen\n grossen Zuwachs von Arbeit anzeigt“ [Note: Lord Ashley l. c. p. 6—9 passim.] .\n\n\n\n\nAngesichts dieser merkwürdigen Intensivität, welche die Arbeit unter\n der Herrschaft des Zwölfstundengesetzes bereits 1844 erreicht hatte, schien\n damals die Erklärung der englischen Fabrikanten berechtigt: jeder weitere\n Fortschritt in dieser Richtung sei unmöglich, daher jede weitere Abnahme\n der Arbeitszeit identisch mit Abnahme der Produktion. Die scheinbare\n Richtigkeit ihres Raisonnements wird am besten bewiesen durch folgende\n gleichzeitige Aeusserung ihres rastlosen Censors, des Fabrikinspektors\nLeonhard Horner:\n\n\n\n\n„Da die producirte Quantität hauptsächlich geregelt wird durch die\n Geschwindigkeit der Maschinerie, muss es das Interesse des Fabrikanten\n sein, sie mit dem äussersten Geschwindigkeitsgrad zu treiben, der mit fol-\n genden Bedingungen vereinbar ist: Bewahrung der Maschinerie vor zu\n raschem Verderb, Erhaltung der Qualität des fabricirten Artikels, und\n Fähigkeit des Arbeiters der Bewegung zu folgen ohne grössere Anstren-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"405\", \"facs\": \"#f0424\"} --> gung als er kontinuirlich leisten kann. Es ereignet sich oft, dass der Fabri-\n kant in seiner Hast die Bewegung zu sehr beschleunigt. Brüche und\n schlechtes Machwerk wiegen dann die Geschwindigkeit mehr als auf und\n er ist gezwungen den Gang der Maschinerie zu mässigen. Ich schloss\n daher, da ein aktiver und einsichtsvoller Fabrikant das sichre Maximum\n ausfindet, dass es unmöglich ist in 11 Stunden so viel zu produciren als\n in 12. Ich nahm ausserdem an, dass der per Stücklohn bezahlte Arbeiter\n sich auf’s äusserste anstrengt, soweit er denselben Arbeitsgrad kontinuir-\n lich aushalten kann“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 1845“, p. 20.] . Horner schloss daher, trotz der Experimente\n von Gardner u. s. w., dass eine weitere Herabsetzung des Ar-\n beitstags unter 12 Stunden die Quantität des Produkts\n vermindern müsse [Note: l. c. p. 22.] . Er selbst citirt 10 Jahre später sein Be-\n denken von 1845 zum Beweis, wie wenig er damals noch die Elastici-\n tät der Maschinerie und der menschlichen Arbeitskraft, die beide\n gleichmässig durch die zwangsweise Verkürzung des Ar-\n beitstags aufs Höchste gespannt werden, begriffen habe.\n\n\n\n\nKommen wir nun zur Periode nach 1847, seit Einführung des\nZehnstundengesetzes in die englischen Baumwoll-, Woll-, Seiden-\n und Flachsfabriken.\n\n\n\n\n„Die Geschwindigkeit der Spindeln ist auf Throstles um 500, auf\n Mules um 1000 Drehungen in einer Minute gewachsen, d. h. die Geschwin-\n digkeit der Throstlespindel, die 1839 4500 Drehungen in einer Minute\n zählte, beträgt nun (1862) 5000, und die der Mulespindel, die 5000\n zählte, beträgt jetzt 6000 in der Minute; diess beläuft sich im ersten Fall\n auf ⅒ und im zweiten auf ⅕ zusätzlicher Geschwindigkeit“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 62.] . Jos.\n Nasmyth, der berühmte Civilingenieur von Paticroft, bei Manchester,\n setzte 1852 in einem Brief an Leonhard Horner die von 1848—1852\n gemachten Verbesserungen in der Dampfmaschine auseinander. Nachdem\n er bemerkt, dass die Dampfpferdekraft, in der officiellen Fabrikstatistik\n fortwährend geschätzt nach ihrer Wirkung im Jahr 1828 [Note: Diess hat sich geändert mit dem „Parliamentary Return“ von 1862.\n Hier tritt die wirkliche Dampfpferdekraft der modernen Dampfmaschinen und\n Wasserräder an die Stelle der nominellen. Auch sind die Dublirspindeln nicht] , nur noch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"406\", \"facs\": \"#f0425\"} -->nominell ist und nur als Index der wirklichen Kraft dienen kann, sagt\n er u. a.: „Es unterliegt keinem Zweifel, dass Dampfmaschinerie von dem-\n selben Gewicht, oft dieselben identischen Maschinen, an denen nur die\n modernen Verbesserungen angebracht sind, im Durchschnitt 50 % mehr\n Werk als früher verrichten, und dass in vielen Fällen dieselben identischen\n Dampfmaschinen, die in den Tagen der beschränkten Geschwindigkeit von\n 220 Fuss per Minute 50 Pferdekraft lieferten, heute, mit vermindertem\n Kohlenkonsum über 100 liefern. . . . Die moderne Dampfmaschine von\n derselben nominellen Pferdekraft wird mit viel grösserer Gewalt als früher\n getrieben, in Folge der Verbesserungen in ihrer Konstruktion, vermindertem\n Umfang und Bau der Dampfkessel u. s. w. … Obgleich daher die-\n selbe Händezahl wie früher im Verhältniss zur nominellen Pferde-\n kraft beschäftigt wird, werden weniger Hände verwandt im Ver-\n hältniss zur Arbeitsmaschinerie“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1856“, p. 11.] . Im Jahr 1850 ver-\n wandten die Fabriken des Vereinigten Königreichs 134, 217 nominelle Pferde-\n kraft zur Bewegung von 25,638,716 Spindeln und 301,495 Webstühlen.\n Im Jahr 1856 betrug die Zahl der Spindeln und Webstühle respective\n 33,503,580 und 369,205. Wäre die erheischte Pferdekraft dieselbe\n geblieben wie 1850, so waren 1856: 175,000 Pferdekraft nöthig. Sie\n betrug aber nach dem officiellen Ausweis nur 161,435, also über 10,000\n Pferdekraft weniger, als wenn man nach der Basis von 1850 rechnet [Note: l c. p. 14, 15.] .\n „Die durch den letzten Return von 1856 (officielle Statistik) festgestellten\n Thatsachen sind, dass das Fabriksystem reissend rasch um sich greift,\ndie Zahl der Hände im Verhältniss zur Maschinerie ab-\n genommen hat, die Dampfmaschine durch Oekonomie der Kraft und\n andre Methoden ein grösseres Maschinengewicht treibt, und ein vermehrtes\n Quantum Machwerk erzielt wird in Folge verbesserter Arbeitsmaschinen,\n veränderter Methoden der Fabrikation, erhöhter Geschwindigkeit der Ma-\n [Note: mehr zusammengeworfen mit den eigentlichen Spinnspindeln (wie in den „Re-\n turns“ von 1839, 1850 und 1856); ferner ist für die Wollfabriken die Zahl der\n „gigs“ hinzugefügt, Scheidung eingeführt zwischen Jute- und Hanffabriken einer-\n seits, Flachsfabriken andrerseits, endlich zum erstenmal die Strumpfwirkerei in den\n Bericht aufgenommen.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"407\", \"facs\": \"#f0426\"} --> schinerie und vieler andrer Ursachen“ [Note: l. c. p. 20.] . „Die grossen in Maschinen\n jeder Art eingeführten Verbesserungen haben deren Produktivkraft sehr\n gesteigert. Ohne allen Zweifel gab die Verkürzung des Arbeits-\n tags … den Stachel zu diesen Verbesserungen. Letztere und die in-\n tensivere Anstrengung des Arbeiters bewirkten, dass wenig-\n stens eben so viel Machwerk in dem (um zwei Stunden oder ⅙)\nverkürzten Arbeitstag als früher während des längeren\n geliefert wird“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1858“, p. 9, 10. Vgl. „Reports etc.\nfor 30th April 1860“, p. 30 sqq.] .\n\n\n\n\nWie die Bereicherung der Fabrikanten mit der intensiveren Ausbeu-\n tung der Arbeitskraft zunahm, beweist schon der eine Umstand, dass das\n durchschnittliche proportionelle Wachsthum der englischen Baumwollen-\n u. s. w.-Fabriken von 1838 bis 1850 32 %, von 1850 bis 1856 dagegen\n 86 % jährlich betrug.\n\n\n\n\nSo gross in den 8 Jahren 1848 bis 1856, unter der Herrschaft des\n zehnstündigen Arbeitstags, der Fortschritt der englischen Industrie, wurde\n er wieder weit überflügelt in der folgenden sechsjährigen Periode von 1856\n bis 1862. In der Seidenfabrik z. B. 1856: Spindeln 1,093,799,\n 1862: 1,388,544; 1856: Webstühle 9,260 und 1862: 10,709. Da-\n gegen 1856: Arbeiteranzahl 56,131 und 1862: 52,429. Diess ergiebt\nZunahme der Spindelzahl 26.9 % und der Webstühle 15.6 % mit\ngleichzeitiger Abnahme der Arbeiteranzahl um 7 %. Im\n Jahre 1850 wurden in der Worsted-Fabrik angewandt 875,830 Spin-\n deln, 1856: 1,324,549 (Zunahme von 51.2 %) und 1862: 1,289,172\n (Abnahme von 2.7 %). Zählt man aber die Dublirspindeln ab, die in der\n Aufzählung für das Jahr 1856 figuriren, aber nicht für 1862, so blieb die\n Anzahl der Spindeln seit 1856 ziemlich stationär. Dagegen ward seit\n 1850 in vielen Fällen die Geschwindigkeit der Spindeln und Webstühle\nverdoppelt. Die Zahl der Dampfwebstühle in der Worsted-Fabrik\n1850: 32,617, 1856: 38,956 und 1862: 43,048. Es waren\n dabei beschäftigt 1850: 79,737 Personen, 1856: 87,794 und 1862:\n 86,063, aber davon Kinder unter 14 Jahren 1850: 9,956,\n1856: 11,228 und 1862: 13,178. Trotz sehr vermehrter Anzahl\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"408\", \"facs\": \"#f0427\"} --> der Webstühle, 1862 verglichen mit 1856, nahm also die Gesammt-\n zahl der beschäftigten Arbeiter ab, die der exploitirten Kinder zu [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 100 u. 130.] .\n\n\n\n\nAm 27. April 1863 erklärte das Parlamentsglied Ferrand im\n Unterhause: „Arbeiterdelegirte von 16 Distrikten von Lancashire und\n Cheshire, in deren Auftrag ich spreche, haben mir mitgetheilt, dass die\n Arbeit in den Fabriken in Folge der Verbesserung in der Maschinerie\n beständig wachse. Statt dass früher eine Person mit Gehilfen zwei\n Webstühle bediente, bedient sie jetzt drei ohne Gehilfen und es ist gar\n nichts ungewöhnliches, dass eine Person ihrer vier bedient u. s. w.\nZwölf Stunden Arbeit, wie aus den mitgetheilten Thatsachen her-\n vorgeht, werden jetzt in weniger als 10 Arbeitsstunden ge-\n presst. Es ist daher selbstverständlich, in welchem ungeheuren Umfang\n die Mühen der Fabrikarbeiter sich seit den letzten Jahren vermehrt\n haben“ [Note: Mit dem modernen Dampfwebstuhl fabricirt ein Weber jetzt in 60 Stunden\n per Woche auf 2 Stühlen 26 Stück einer gewissen Art von bestimmter Länge und\n Breite, wovon er auf dem alten Dampfwebstuhl nur 4 fabriciren konnte. Die Web-\n kosten eines solchen Stücks waren schon Anfang der 1850er Jahre von 2 sh. 9 d.\n auf 5⅛ d. gefallen.] .\n\n\n\n\nObgleich daher die Fabrikinspektoren die günstigen Resultate der\n Fabrikgesetze von 1844 und 1850 unermüdlich und mit vollem Recht\n lobpreisen, gestehen sie doch, dass die Verkürzung des Arbeitstags bereits\n eine die Gesundheit der Arbeiter, also die Arbeitskraft selbst zer-\n störende Kondensation der Arbeit hervorgerufen habe. „In den meisten\n Baumwoll-, Worsted- und Seidenfabriken scheint der erschöpfende Zustand\n von Aufregung, nöthig für die Arbeit an der Maschinerie, deren Bewegung\n in den letzten Jahren so ausserordentlich beschleunigt worden ist, eine der\n Ursachen des Ueberschusses der Sterblichkeit an Lungenkrankheiten, den\n Dr. Greenhow in seinem jüngsten bewundernswerthen Bericht nachge-\n wiesen hat“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1861“, p. 25, 26.] . Es unterliegt nicht dem geringsten Zweifel, dass die\n Tendenz des Kapitals, sobald ihm Verlängerung des Arbeitstags ein für\n allemal durch das Gesetz abgeschnitten ist, sich durch systematische Steige-\n rung des Intensivitätsgrads der Arbeit gütlich zu thun und jede Ver-\n besserung der Maschinerie in ein Mittel zu grösserer Aussaugung der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"409\", \"facs\": \"#f0428\"} --> Arbeitskraft zu verkehren, bald wieder zu einem Wendepunkt treiben muss,\n wo abermalige Abnahme der Arbeitsstunden unvermeidlich wird [Note: Die Achtstundenagitation hat jetzt (1867) in Lancashire unter den Fabrik-\n arbeitern begonnen.] . Andrer-\n seits überflügelt der Sturmmarsch der englischen Industrie von 1848 bis\n zur Gegenwart, d. h. während der Periode des zehnstündigen\n Arbeitstags, noch weit mehr die Zeit von 1833 bis 1847, d. h. die\nPeriode des zwölfstündigen Arbeitstags, als letztre das halbe\n Jahrhundert seit Einführung des Fabriksystems, d. h. die Periode des\n unbeschränkten Arbeitstags [Note: Folgende wenige Zahlen zeigen den Fortschritt der eigentlichen „Facto-\n ries“ im U. Kingd. seit 1848:\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"410\", \"facs\": \"#f0429\"} -->\n\nWir betrachteten im Beginn dieses Kapitels den Leib der Fabrik,\n die Gliederung des Maschinensystems. Wir sahen dann, wie die Maschi-\n nerie das menschliche Exploitationsmaterial des Kapitals vermehrt durch\n Aneignung der Weiber- und Kinderarbeit, wie sie die ganze Lebenszeit\n des Arbeiters confiscirt durch masslose Ausdehnung des Arbeitstags, und\n wie ihr Fortschritt, der ein ungeheuer wachsendes Produkt in stets kürzerer\n Zeit zu liefern erlaubt, endlich zum Mittel umschlägt in jedem Zeitmo-\n ment mehr Arbeit flüssig zu machen oder die Arbeitskraft\n stets intensiver auszubeuten. Wir wenden uns nun zum Fa-\n brikganzen, und zwar in seiner ausgebildetsten Gestalt.\n\n\n\n\nDr. Ure, der Pindar der automatischen Fabrik, beschreibt sie einer-\n seits als „Cooperation verschiedener Klassen von Arbeitern, erwachs-\n nen und nicht erwachsnen, die mit Gewandtheit und Fleiss ein System\n produktiver Maschinerie überwachen, das ununterbrochen durch eine Cen-\n tralkraft (den ersten Motor) in Thätigkeit gesetzt wird“, andrerseits als\n „einen ungeheuren Automaten, zusammengesetzt aus zahllosen\n mechanischen und selbstbewussten Organen, die im Einverständniss und\n ohne Unterbrechung wirken, um einen und denselben Gegenstand zu pro-\n duciren, so dass alle diese Organe einer Bewegungskraft untergeord-\n net sind, die sich von selbst bewegt.“ Diese beiden Ausdrücke sind\n keineswegs identisch. In dem einen erscheint der kombinirte Gesammt-\n arbeiter oder gesellschaftliche Arbeitskörper als übergreifendes Subjekt\n und der mechanische Automat als Objekt; in dem andern ist der Automat\n selbst das Subjekt und die Arbeiter sind seinen bewusstlosen Organen nur\n [Note: (Sieh die Blaubücher: „Statistical Abstract for the U. Kingd.“\n Nr. 8 und Nr. 13. Lond. 1861 und 1866.)\n In Lancashire vermehrten sich die Fabriken zwischen 1839 und 1850 nur um\n 4 %, zwischen 1850 und 1856 um 19 %, zwischen 1856 und 1862 um 33 %, wäh-\n rend in beiden elfjährigen Perioden die Zahl der beschäftigten Personen absolut\n zunahm, relativ fiel. Cf. Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1862, p. 63.\n In Lancashire herrscht die Baumwollfabrik vor. Welchen proportionellen Raum\n sie aber in der Fabrikation von Garn und Gewebe überhaupt einnimmt, sieht man\n daraus, dass auf sie allein von allen derartigen Fabriken in England, Wales,\n Schottland und Irland 45.2 % fallen, von allen Spindeln des U. Kingd. 83.3 %, von\n allen Dampfwebstühlen 81.4 %, von aller sie bewegenden Dampfpferdekraft 72.6 %\n und von der Gesammtzahl der beschäftigten Personen 58.2 %. (l. c. p. 62, 63.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"411\", \"facs\": \"#f0430\"} --> als bewusste Organe beigeordnet und mit den mechanischen Organen\n der centralen Bewegungskraft untergeordnet. Der erstere Ausdruck\n gilt von jeder möglichen Anwendung der Maschinerie im Grossen, der\n andre charakterisirt ihre kapitalistische Anwendung und daher\ndas moderne Fabriksystem. Ure liebt es daher auch die Central-\n maschine, von der die Bewegung ausgeht, nicht nur als Automat, son-\n dern als Autokrat darzustellen. „In diesen grossen Werkstätten ver-\n sammelt die wohlthätige Macht des Dampfes ihre Myriaden von Unter-\n thanen um sich“ [Note: Ure l. c. t. I, p. 19, 20.] .\n\n\n\n\nMit dem Arbeitswerkzeug geht auch die Virtuosität in seiner Führung\n vom Arbeiter auf die Maschine über. Die Leistungsfähigkeit des Werkzeugs\n ist emancipirt von den persönlichen Schranken menschlicher Arbeitskraft.\n Damit ist die technologische Grundlage aufgehoben, wo-\n rauf die Theilung der Arbeit in der Manufaktur beruht.\n An die Stelle der sie charakterisirenden Hierarchie der spezialisirten\n Arbeiter tritt daher in der automatischen Fabrik die Tendenz der\n Gleichmachung oder Nivellirung der Arbeiten, welche die\n Gehilfen der Maschinerie zu verrichten haben [Note: l. c. p. 31. Vgl. Karl Marx l. c. p. 140, 141.] , an die Stelle der künst-\n lich erzeugten Unterschiede der Theilarbeiter treten vorwiegend die natür-\n lichen Unterschiede des Alters und Geschlechts.\n\n\n\n\nSoweit in der automatischen Fabrik die Theilung der Arbeit\n wiedererscheint, ist sie zunächst Vertheilung von Arbeitern\n unter die spezialisirten Maschinen, und von Arbeitermassen,\n die jedoch keine kombinirten Gruppen bilden, unter die verschiedenen Depar-\n tements der Fabrik, wo sie an neben einander gereihten gleichartigen Werk-\n zeugmaschinen arbeiten, also nur einfache Cooperation unter ihnen\n stattfindet. Die kombinirte Gruppe der Manufaktur ist ersetzt durch den\n Zusammenhang des Hauptarbeiters mit wenigen Gehilfen. Die wesent-\n liche Scheidung ist die von Arbeitern, die wirklich an den Werkzeugma-\n schinen beschäftigt sind (es kommen hiezu einige Arbeiter zur Bewachung,\n resp. Fütterung der Bewegungsmaschine) und von blossen Hand-\n langern (fast ausschliesslich Kinder) dieser Maschinenarbeiter. Zu\n den Handlangern zählen mehr oder minder alle „feeders“ (die den Ma-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"412\", \"facs\": \"#f0431\"} --> schinen bloss Arbeitsstoff darreichen). Neben diese Hauptklassen tritt\n ein numerisch unbedeutendes Personal, das mit der Kontrole der gesamm-\n ten Maschinerie und ihrer beständigen Reparatur beschäftigt ist, wie In-\n genieure, Mechaniker, Schreiner u. s. w. Es ist eine höhere, theils wissen-\n schaftlich gebildete, theils handwerksmässige Arbeiterklasse, ausserhalb\n des Kreises der Fabrikarbeiter und ihnen nur aggregirt [Note: Es ist charakteristisch für die Absicht des statistischen Betrugs, die auch\n sonst noch im Detail nachweisbar wäre, wenn die englische Fabrikgesetzgebung\n die zuletzt im Text erwähnten Arbeiter ausdrücklich als Nicht-Fabrikarbei-\n ter von ihrem Wirkungskreis ausschliesst, andrerseits die vom Parlament ver-\n öffentlichten „Returns“ ebenso ausdrücklich nicht nur Ingenieure, Mechaniker\n u. s. w., sondern auch Fabrikdirigenten, Commis, Ausläufer, Lageraufseher, Ver-\n packer u. s. w., kurz alle Leute, mit Ausschluss des Fabrikeigenthümers selbst,\n in die Kategorie der Fabrikarbeiter einschliesst.] . Diese Thei-\n lung der Arbeit ist rein technologisch.\n\n\n\n\nAlle Arbeit an der Maschine erfordert frühzeitigen Einbruch des Ar-\n beiters, damit er seine eigne Bewegung der gleichförmig kontinuirlichen\n Bewegung eines Automaten anpassen lerne. Soweit die Gesammtmaschi-\n nerie selbst ein System verschiedenartiger, gleichzeitig wirkender\n und kombinirter Maschinen bildet, erfordert die auf ihr beruhende Coope-\n ration nicht minder Vertheilung besondrer Arbeiter unter die beson-\n derten Maschinen. Aber der Maschinenbetrieb hebt die Nothwendigkeit auf\n diese Vertheilung manufakturmässig zu befestigen durch fortwährende\n Aneignung derselben Arbeiter an dieselbe Funktion [Note: Ure giebt diess zu. Er sagt, dass die Arbeiter „im Nothfall“ nach dem\n Willen des Dirigenten von einer Maschine zur andern versetzt werden können und\n ruft triumphirend aus: „Dergleichen Wechsel steht im offnen Widerspruch mit der\n alten Routine, die die Arbeit theilt und dem einen Arbeiter die Aufgabe zuweist,\n den Kopf einer Stecknadel zu façonniren, dem andern ihre Spitze zu schleifen.“\n Er hätte sich vielmehr fragen sollen, warum diese „alte Routine“ in der auto-\n matischen Fabrik nur „im Nothfall“ verlassen wird?] . Da die Ge-\n sammtbewegung der Fabrik nicht vom Arbeiter ausgeht, sondern von\n der Maschine, kann fortwährender Personenwechsel stattfinden ohne\n Unterbrechung des Arbeitsprozesses. Den schlagendsten Beweis hierzu\n liefert das während der englischen Fabrikantenrevolte von 1848—50 ins\n Werk gesetzte Relaissystem. Die Geschwindigkeit endlich, womit die\n Arbeit an der Maschine im jugendlichen Alter erlernt wird, beseitigt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"413\", \"facs\": \"#f0432\"} --> ebenso die Nothwendigkeit, eine besondre Klasse Arbeiter ausschliesslich\n zu Maschinenarbeitern zu machen [Note: Wenn Noth an Mann ist, wie z. B. während des amerikanischen Bürger-\n kriegs, wird der Fabrikarbeiter ausnahmsweis vom Bourgeois zu den gröbsten Ar-\n beiten, wie Strassenbau u. s. w. verwandt. Die englischen „atéliers nationaux“\n des Jahres 1862 sq. für die beschäftigungslosen Baumwollarbeiter unterschieden\n sich dadurch von den französischen von 1848, dass in diesen der Arbeiter auf\n Kosten des Staats unproduktive Arbeiten, in jenen zum Vortheil des Bourgeois\n produktive städtische Arbeiten und zwar wohlfeiler als die regelmässigen Ar-\n beiter, mit denen er so in Konkurrenz geworfen ward, zu verrichten hatte. „The\n physical appearance of the cotton operatives is unquestionably improved. This\n I attribute … as to the men, to outdoor labour on public works.“ (Es\n handelt sich hier von den Preston-Fabrikarbeitern, die am „Preston Moor“ be-\n schäftigt wurden.) „Rep. of Insp. of Fact. Oct. 1865“, p. 59.)] . Die Dienste der blossen Hand-\n langer aber sind in der Fabrik grossentheils durch Maschinen ersetzbar [Note: Beispiel: Die verschiednen mechanischen Apparate, die zum Ersatz von\n Kinderarbeit seit dem Gesetz von 1844 in der Wollfabrik eingeführt wurden. So-\n bald die Kinder der Herrn Fabrikanten selbst ihre „Schule“ als Handlanger der\n Fabrik durchzumachen haben, wird diess fast noch unangebaute Gebiet der Mecha-\n nik bald einen merkwürdigen Aufschwung nehmen.] ,\n theils erlauben sie andrerseits wegen ihrer völligen Einfachheit raschen und\n beständigen Wechsel der mit dieser Plackerei belasteten Personen.\n\n\n\n\nObgleich nun die Maschinerie das alte System der Theilung der Ar-\n beit technologisch über den Haufen wirft, schleppt es sich zunächst als\n Tradition der Manufaktur gewohnheitsmässig in der Fabrik fort, um dann\nsystematisch vom Kapital als Exploitationsmittel der Arbeitskraft in\n noch ekelhafterer Form reproducirt und befestigt zu werden. Aus der lebens-\n langen Specialität ein Theilwerkzeug zu führen, wird die lebenslange\n Specialität einer Theilmaschine zu dienen. Die Maschinerie wird miss-\n braucht, um den Arbeiter selbst von Kindesbeinen in den Theil einer Theil-\n maschine zu verwandeln [Note: Man würdigt daher den fabelhaften Einfall Proudhon’s, der die\n Maschinerie nicht als Synthese von Arbeitsmitteln, sondern als Synthese von Theil-\n arbeiten für die Arbeiter selbst — „konstruirt“.] . Nicht nur werden so die zu seiner eignen\n Reproduktion nöthigen Kosten bedeutend vermindert, sondern zugleich\n seine hilflose Abhängigkeit vom Fabrikganzen, also vom Kapitalisten, voll-\n endet. Hier wie überall muss man unterscheiden zwischen der grössern\n Produktivität, die der Entwicklung des gesellschaftlichen Produktions-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"414\", \"facs\": \"#f0433\"} --> prozesses, und der grössern Produktivität, die seiner kapitalistischen Aus-\n beutung geschuldet ist.\n\n\n\n\nIn Manufaktur und Handwerk bedient sich der Arbeiter des Werk-\n zeugs, in der Fabrik dient er der Maschine. Dort geht von ihm die Be-\n wegung des Arbeitsmittels aus, dessen Bewegung er hier zu folgen hat.\n In der Manufaktur bilden die Arbeiter Glieder eines lebendigen Mechanis-\n mus. In der Fabrik existirt ein todter Mechanismus unabhängig von ihnen\n und sie werden ihm als lebendige Anhängsel einverleibt. „Der trübselige\n Schlendrian einer endlosen Arbeitsqual, worin derselbe mechanische Pro-\n zess immer wieder durchgemacht wird, gleicht der Arbeit des Sisyphus;\n die Last der Arbeit, gleich dem Felsen, fällt immer wieder auf den abge-\n matteten Arbeiter zurück“ [Note: F. Engels l. c. p. 217. Selbst ein ganz ordinärer, optimistischer Frei-\n händler, Herr Molinari, bemerkt: „Un homme s’use plus vite en surveillant,\n quinze heurs par jour l’évolution uniforme d’un mécanisme, qu’en exerçant dans\n le même espace de temps, sa force physique. Ce travail de surveillance, qui\n servirait peut-être d’utile gymnastique à l’intelligence, s’il n’était pas trop prolongé,\n détruit à la longue, par son excès, et l’intelligence et le corps même.“ (G. de\n Molinari: „Études Économiques.“ Paris 1846.)] . Während die Maschinenarbeit das Ner-\n vensystem aufs äusserste angreift, unterdrückt sie das vielseitige Spiel der\n Muskeln und konfiscirt alle freie körperliche und geistige Thätigkeit [Note: F. Engels l. c. p. 216.] .\n Selbst die Erleichterung der Arbeit wird zum Mittel der Tortur, indem die\n Maschine nicht den Arbeiter von der Arbeit befreit, sondern seine Arbeit\n vom Inhalt. Aller kapitalistischen Produktion, soweit sie nicht nur Ar-\n beitsprozess, sondern zugleich Verwerthungsprozess des Ka-\n pitals, ist es gemeinsam, dass nicht der Arbeiter die Arbeitsbedingung,\n sondern umgekehrt die Arbeitsbedingung den Arbeiter anwendet, aber erst\n mit der Maschinerie erhält diese Verkehrung technologisch hand-\n greifliche Wirklichkeit. Durch seine Verwandlung in einen Auto-\n maten tritt das Arbeitsmittel während des Arbeitsprozesses selbst dem\n Arbeiter als Kapital gegenüber, als todte Arbeit, welche die lebendige\n Arbeitskraft beherrscht und aussaugt. Die Scheidung der geistigen\n Potenzen des Produktionsprozesses von der Handarbeit und die Ver-\n wandlung derselben in Mächte des Kapitals über die Arbeit\n vollendet sich, wie bereits früher angedeutet, in der auf Grundlage der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"415\", \"facs\": \"#f0434\"} --> Maschinerie aufgebauten grossen Industrie. Das Detailgeschick des indi-\n viduellen, entleerten Maschinenarbeiters verschwindet als ein winzig Neben-\n ding vor der Wissenschaft, den ungeheuren Naturkräften und der gesell-\n schaftlichen Massenarbeit, die im Maschinensystem verkörpert sind und mit\n ihm die Macht „des Meisters“ bilden. Dieser Meister, in dessen Hirn\n die Maschinerie und sein Monopol an derselben unzertrennlich verwachsen\n sind, ruft daher in Kollisionsfällen den „Händen“ verächtlich zu: „Die\n Fabrikarbeiter sollten in heilsamer Erinnerung halten, dass ihre Arbeit in\n der That eine sehr niedrige Sorte geschickter Arbeit ist; dass keine leich-\n ter aneigenbar und in Anbetracht ihrer Qualität besser belohnt ist, dass\n keine durch kurze Unterweisung des mindest Erfahrnen in so kurzer Zeit\n und in solchem Ueberfluss zugeführt werden kann. Des Meisters\n Maschinerie spielt in der That eine viel wichtigere Rolle in dem Ge-\n schäft der Produktion als die Arbeit und das Geschick des Arbei-\n ters, die eine Erziehung von 6 Monaten lehren und jeder Bauernknecht\n lernen kann“ [Note: „The factory operatives should keep in wholesome remembrance the\n fact that theirs is really a low species of skilled labour; and that there is none\n which is more easily acquired or of its quality more amply remunerated, or which,\n by a short training of the least expert can be more quickly as well as abundantly\n acquired . . . . The master’s machinery really plays a far more im-\n portant part in the business of production than the labour and\n the skill of the operative, which six months’ education can teach, and a\n common labourer can learn.“ („The Master Spinners’ and Manufac-\n turers’ Defence Fund. Report of the Committee. Manchester\n 1854“, p. 17.) Man wird später sehn, dass der „Master“ aus einem andern Loch\n pfeift, sobald er mit Verlust seiner „lebendigen“ Automaten bedroht ist.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie technologische Unterordnung des Arbeiters unter den gleichför-\n migen Gang des Arbeitsmittels und die eigenthümliche Zusammensetzung\n des Arbeitskörpers aus Individuen beider Geschlechter und aller Alters-\n stufen schafft eine kasernenmässige Disciplin, die sich zum vollständigen\nFabrikregime ausbildet und die schon früher erwähnte Arbeit\n der Oberaufsicht, damit zugleich die Theilung der Arbeiter\n in Handarbeiter und Arbeitsaufseher, gemeine Industriesoldaten und In-\n dustrieunterofficiere, völlig entwickelt. „Die Hauptschwierigkeit in der auto-\n matischen Fabrik bestand in der nothwendigen Disciplin, um die Menschen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"416\", \"facs\": \"#f0435\"} --> auf ihre unregelmässigen Gewohnheiten in der Arbeit verzichten zu machen,\n und sie zu identificiren mit der unveränderlichen Regel-\n mässigkeit des grossen Automaten. Aber einen den Bedürf-\n nissen und der Geschwindigkeit des automatischen Systems entsprechen-\n den Disciplinarcodex zu erfinden und mit Erfolg auszuführen, war ein Unter-\n nehmen des Herkules würdig, das ist das edle Werk Arkwright’s!\n Selbst heut zu Tage, wo das System in seiner ganzen Vollendung organi-\n sirt ist, ist es fast unmöglich unter den Arbeitern, die das Alter der\n Mannbarkeit zurückgelegt haben, nützliche Gehilfen für das automatische\n System zu finden“ [Note: Ure l. c. p. 22, 23. Wer Arkwright’s Lebensgeschichte kennt, wird\n das Wort „edel“ diesem genialen Barbier nie an den Kopf werfen. Von allen\n grossen Erfindern des 18. Jahrhunderts war er unstreitig der grösste Dieb fremder\n Erfindungen und der gemeinste Kerl.] . Der Fabrikcodex, worin das Kapital seine Auto-\n kratie über seine Arbeiter, ohne die sonst vom Bürgerthum so beliebte\n Theilung der Gewalten und das noch beliebtere Repräsentativsystem, pri-\n vatgesetzlich und eigenherrlich formulirt, ist nur die kapitalistische\n Karrikatur der gesellschaftlichen Reglung des Arbeits-\n prozesses, welche mit der Cooperation auf grosser Stufenleiter und der\n Anwendung gemeinsamer Arbeitsmittel, wie namentlich der Maschinerie,\n nöthig wird. An die Stelle der Peitsche des Sklaventreibers tritt das Straf-\n buch des Aufsehers. Alle Strafen lösen sich natürlich auf in Geldstrafen\n und Lohnabzüge und der gesetzgeberische Scharfsinn der Fabrik-Lykurge\n macht ihnen die Verletzung ihrer Gesetze wo möglich noch einbringlicher\n als deren Befolgung [Note: „Die Sklaverei, in der die Bourgeoisie das Proletariat gefesselt hält,\n kommt nirgends deutlicher ans Tageslicht, als im Fabriksystem. Hier hört alle\n Freiheit rechtlich und faktisch auf. Der Arbeiter muss Morgens um halb 6 in der\n Fabrik sein; kommt er ein paar Minuten zu spät, so wird er gestraft, kommt er\n 10 Minuten zu spät, so wird er gar nicht hereingelassen, bis das Frühstück vorüber\n ist, und verliert einen Vierteltag am Lohn. Er muss auf Kommando essen, trinken\n und schlafen … Die despotische Glocke ruft ihn vom Bette, ruft ihn vom Früh-\n stück und Mittagstisch. Und wie geht es nun gar erst in der Fabrik? Hier ist\n der Fabrikant absoluter Gesetzgeber. Er erlässt Fabrikregulationen, wie er Lust\n hat; er ändert und macht Zusätze zu seinem Codex, wie es ihm beliebt; und wenn\n er das tollste Zeug hineinsetzt, so sagen doch die Gerichte zum Arbeiter: Da ihr\n unter diesen Kontrakt euch freiwillig begeben habt, jetzt müsst ihr ihn auch be-] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"417\", \"facs\": \"#f0436\"} -->\n\nWir deuten nur hin auf die materiellen Bedingungen, unter denen\n die Fabrikarbeit verrichtet wird. Alle Sinnesorgane werden gleichmässig\n [Note: folgen … Diese Arbeiter sind dazu verdammt, vom neunten Jahr bis zu ihrem\n Tod unter der geistigen und körperlichen Fuchtel zu leben.“ (F. Engels l. c.\n p. 217 sq.) Was „die Gerichte sagen“, will ich an zwei Beispielen erläutern. Der\n eine Fall spielt in Sheffield, Ende 1866. Dort hatte sich ein Arbeiter für 2\n Jahre in eine Metallfabrik verdingt. In Folge eines Zwistes mit dem Fabrikanten\n verliess er die Fabrik und erklärte unter keinen Umständen mehr für ihn arbeiten\n zu wollen. Wurde wegen Kontraktsbruch verklagt, zu zwei Monaten Geldbusse\n verurtheilt. (Bricht der Fabrikant den Kontrakt, so kann er nur civiliter verklagt\n werden und riskirt nur eine Geldbusse.) Nach Absitzen der zwei Monate stellt\n derselbe Fabrikant ihm Ladung zu, dem alten Kontrakt gemäss in die Fabrik zu-\n rückzukehren. Arbeiter erklärt, Nein. Den Kontraktsbruch habe er bereits ab-\n gebüsst. Fabrikant verklagt von neuem, Gericht verurtheilt von neuem, obgleich\n Einer der Richter, Mr. Shee, diess öffentlich als juristische Ungeheuerlichkeit\n denuncirt, wonach ein Mann sein ganzes Leben durch periodisch für dasselbe\n identische Vergehn, resp. Verbrechen, wieder und wieder bestraft werden könne.\n Diess Urtheil wurde gefällt nicht von dem „Great Unpaid“, provinzialen Dog-\n berries, sondern zu London, von einem der nöchsten Gerichtshöfe. — Der zweite\n Fall spielt in Wiltshire, Ende November 1863. Ungefähr 30 Dampfstuhl-\n weberinnen, in der Beschäftigung eines gewissen Harrupp, Tuchfabrikant von\n Leower’s Mill, Westbury Leigh, machten einen strike, weil dieser selbe Harrupp\n die angenehme Gewohnheit hatte, ihnen für Verspätung am Morgen Lohnabzug\n zu machen, und zwar 6 d. für 2 Minuten, 1 sh. für 3 Minuten, und 1 sh. 6 d. für\n 10 Minuten. Diess macht, bei 9 sh. per Stunde, 4 Pfd. St. 10 sh. per Tag, wäh-\n rend ihr Durchschnittslohn im Jahr nie über 10 bis 12 sh. wöchentlich steigt.\n Harrupp hat ebenfalls einen Jungen bestallt, um die Fabrikstunde zu blasen, was er\n selber manchmal vor 6 Morgens thut, und wenn die Hände nicht grade da sind, sobald\n er aufhört, werden die Thore geschlossen und die draussen in Geldbusse genommen;\n und da keine Uhr im Gebäude, sind die unglücklichen Hände in der Gewalt des\n von Harrupp inspirirten jugendlichen Zeitwächters. Die im „strike“ begriffenen\n Hände, Familienmütter und Mädchen, erklärten, sie wollten wieder ans Werk\n gehn, wenn der Zeitwächter durch eine Uhr ersetzt, und ein rationellerer Straftarif\n eingeführt würde. Harrupp citirte 19 Weiber und Mädchen vor die Magistrate,\n wegen Kontraktsbruch. Sie wurden verurtheilt zu je 6 d. Strafe und 2 sh. 6 d.\n Kosten, unter lauter Entrüstung des Auditoriums. Harrupp wurde vom Gericht\n weg von einer zischenden Volksmasse begleitet. — Eine Lieblingsoperation der\n Fabrikanten ist, die Arbeiter durch Lohnabzüge für die Fehler des ihnen gelieferten\n Materials zu züchtigen. Diese Methode rief 1866 allgemeinen strike in den eng-\n lischen Töpferdistrikten hervor. Die Berichte der „Ch. Employm.\n Commiss.“ (1863—1866) geben Fälle, wo der Arbeiter, statt Lohn zu erhalten,] \n [Running matter: I. 27] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"418\", \"facs\": \"#f0437\"} --> verletzt durch die künstlich gesteigerte Temperatur, die mit Abfällen des\n Rohmaterials geschwängerte Atmosphäre, den betäubenden Lärm u. s. w.,\n abgesehn von der Lebensgefahr unter dicht gehäufter Maschinerie, die mit\n der Regelmässigkeit der Jahreszeiten ihre industriellen Schlachtbülletins\n producirt. Die Oekonomisirung der gesellschaftlichen\n Produktionsmittel, erst im Fabriksystem treibhausmässig gereift,\n wird in der Hand des Kapitals zugleich zum systematischen Raub\n an den Lebensbedingungen des Arbeiters während der\n Arbeit, wie an Raum, Luft, Licht und persönlichen Schutzmitteln wider\n die lebensgefährlichen oder gesundheitswidrigen Umstände des Produk-\n tionsprozesses, von Vorrichtungen zur Bequemlichkeit des Arbeiters gar\n nicht zu sprechen [Note: Im Ersten Kapitel des Dritten Buchs werde ich berichten über einen\n jüngster Zeit angehörigen Feldzug der englischen Fabrikanten gegen die Klauseln\n des Fabrikakts zum Schutz der Gliedmassen der „Hände“ vor lebensgefährlicher\n Maschinerie. Hier genüge ein Citat aus einem officiellen Bericht des Fabrik-\n inspektor Leonhard Horner: „Ich habe Fabrikanten mit unentschuldbarer\n Frivolität von einigen der Unglücksfälle sprechen hören, z. B. der Verlust eines\n Fingers sei eine Kleinigkeit. Das Leben und die Aussichten eines Arbeiters hängen\n so sehr von seinen Fingern ab, dass ein solcher Verlust ein äusserst ernstes Ereig-\n niss für ihn ist. Wenn ich solch gedankenlos Geschwätz höre, stelle ich die Frage:\n Unterstellt, Sie brauchten einen zusätzlichen Arbeiter, und ihrer zwei meldeten] . Nennt Fourier mit Unrecht die Fabriken „gemäs-\n [Note: durch seine Arbeit, und vermittelst des Strafreglements, noch obendrein „Schuld-\n ner“ seines erlauchten „Master“ wird. Erbauliche Züge über den Lohnabzugs-\n Scharfsinn der Fabrikautokraten lieferte auch die jüngste Baumwollkrise. „Ich\n hatte selbst“, sagt Fabrikinspektor R. Baker, „vor kurzem gerichtliche Verfol-\n gung wider einen Baumwollfabrikanten einzuleiten, weil er in diesen schweren und\n qualvollen Zeitläuften 10 d. von einigen der von ihm beschäftigten „jungen“\n (mehr als dreizehnjährigen) Arbeiter abzog für das ärztliche Alterscertifikat, das\n ihm nur 6 d. kostet, und wofür das Gesetz nur einen Abzug von 3 d., das Her-\n kommen gar keinen erlaubt … Ein andrer Fabrikant, um denselben Zweck ohne\n Konflikt mit dem Gesetz zu erreichen, belastet jedes der armen Kinder, die für ihn\n arbeiten, mit einem Shilling als Sportel für Erlernung der Kunst und des Myste-\n riums zu spinnen, sobald das ärztliche Zeugniss sie reif für diese Beschäftigung er-\n klärt. Es existiren also Unterströmungen, die man kennen muss, um solche\n ausserordentliche Phänomene, wie strikes zu Zeiten wie die gegenwärtige (es\n handelt sich um einen strike in der Fabrik zu Darwen, Juni 1863, unter den Maschi-\n nenwebern) zu begreifen.“ Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 30th April\n 1863. (Die Fabrikberichte gehn immer weiter als ihr officielles Datum.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"419\", \"facs\": \"#f0438\"} --> sigte Bagnos“ [Note: In den Fabriken, die am längsten dem Fabrikakt, mit der Zwangsbe-\n schränkung der Arbeitszeit und seinen sonstigen Regulationen unterworfen sind,\n sind manche frühere Missstände verschwunden. Die Verbesserung der Maschinerie\n selbst erheischt auf einem gewissen Punkt eine „verbesserte Konstruktion\n der Fabrikgebäude“, die den Arbeitern zu gut kommt. (cf. Reports etc.\nfor 31st Oct. 1863, p. 109.)] ? — Der Kampf zwischen Kapitalist und Lohnarbeiter be-\n ginnt mit dem Kapitalverhältniss selbst. Er tobt fort während der ganzen\n Manufakturperiode [Note: Sieh u. a.: John Houghton: „Husbandry and Trade improv-\n ed. Lond. 1727“, „The Advantages of the East India Trade 1720“,\nJohn Bellers l. c. „The masters and the men are unhappily in a perpetual\n war with each other. The invariable object of the former is to get their work done\n as cheap as possibly; and they do not fail to employ every artifice to this purpose,\n whilst the latter are equally attentive to every occasion of distressing their masters\n into a compliance with higher demands.“ „An Inquiry into the causes\n of the Present High Prices of Provisions.“ (Verf. Rev. Mr. Na-\n thaniel Forster, ganz auf Seite der Arbeiter.)] . Erst seit der Einführung der Maschinerie\n bekämpft der Arbeiter das Arbeitsmittel selbst, die materielle Exi-\n stenzweise des Kapitals. Er revoltirt gegen diese bestimmte Form\n des Produktionsmittels als die materielle Grundlage der kapi-\n talistischen Produktionsweise.\n\n\n\n\nZiemlich ganz Europa erlebte während des 17. Jahrhunderts Ar-\n beiterrevolten gegen die s. g. Bandmühle (auch Schnurmühle oder\n Mühlenstuhl genannt), eine Maschine zum Weben von Bändern und Bor-\n ten [Note: Die Bandmühle ward in Deutschland erfunden. Der italienische Abbé\n Lancellotti in einer Schrift, die 1636 zu Venedig erschien, erzählt: „Anton\n Müller aus Danzig habe vor ungefähr 50 Jahren (L. schrieb 1579) eine sehr künst-\n liche Maschine in Danzig gesehn, die 4—6 Gewebe auf einmal verfertigte; weil\n der Stadtrath aber besorgt habe, diese Erfindung möchte eine Masse Arbeiter zu\n Bettlern machen, so habe er die Erfindung unterdrückt und den Erfinder heimlich] . Ende des ersten Dritttheils des 17. Jahrhunderts erlag eine\n [Note: sich, beide in jeder andern Hinsicht gleich tüchtig, aber der Eine ohne Daumen\n oder Vorfinger, welchen würden Sie wählen? Sie zögerten nie einen Augenblick\n für den Vollfingrigen zu entscheiden. . . . Diese Herrn Fabrikanten haben falsche\n Vorurtheile gegen was sie pseudo-philanthropische Gesetzgebung\n nennen.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1855.“) Diese\n Herrn Fabrikanten sind „gescheidte Leut’“ und schwärmten nicht umsonst für die\n Sklavenhalter-Rebellion!] \n [Running matter: 27*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"420\", \"facs\": \"#f0439\"} --> Windsägemühle, von einem Holländer in der Nähe Londons angelegt, vor\n Pöbelexcessen. Noch Anfang des 18. Jahrhunderts überwanden durch\n Wasser getriebne Sägemaschinen in England nur mühsam den parlamen-\n tarisch unterstützten Volkswiderstand. Als Everet 1758 die erste vom\n Wasser getriebene Maschine zum Wollscheeren erbaut hatte, wurde sie\n von 100,000 ausser Arbeit gesetzten Menschen in Brand gesteckt. Gegen\n die scribbling mills und Kardirmaschinen Arkwright’s petitionirten 50,000\n Arbeiter, die bisher vom Wollkratzen gelebt, beim Parlament. Die mas-\n senhafte Zerstörung von Maschinen in den englischen Manufaktur-\n distrikten während der ersten 15 Jahre des 19. Jahrhunderts, namentlich\n in Folge der Ausbeutung des Dampfwebstuhls, bot, unter dem Namen\n der Ludditenbewegung, der Antijakobiner-Regierung eines Sid-\n mouth, Castlereagh u. s. w. den Vorwand zu reaktionärsten Gewalt-\n schritten. Es bedarf Zeit und Erfahrung, bevor der Arbeiter die Maschi-\n nerie von ihrer kapitalistischen Anwendung unterscheiden und\n [Note: ersticken oder ersäufen lassen.“ In Leyden wurde dieselbe Maschine zuerst 1629\n angewandt. Die Emeuten der Bortenwirker zwangen den Magistrat erst zu ihrem\n Verbot; durch verschiedne Verordnungen von 1623, 1639 u. s. w. von Seiten der\n Generalstaaten sollte ihr Gebrauch beschränkt werden, endlich erlaubt, unter ge-\n wissen Bedingungen, durch Verordnung vom 15. December 1661. „In hac urbe“,\n sagt Boxhorn („Inst. Pol. 1663“) von der Einführung der Bandmühle in\n Leyden, „ante hos viginti circiter annos instrumentum quidam invenerunt texto-\n rium, quo solus quis plus panni et facilius conficere poterat, quam plures\n aequali tempore. Hinc turbae ortae et querulae textorum, tandemque usus\n hujus instrumenti a magistratu prohibitus est.“ Dieselbe Maschine ward 1676 in\n Köln verboten, während ihre Einführung in England gleichzeitige Arbeiterunruhen\n hervorrief. Durch kaiserliches Edikt vom 19. Februar 1685 wurde ihr Gebrauch\n in ganz Deutschland untersagt. In Hamburg wurde sie öffentlich auf Befehl des\n Magistrats verbrannt. Karl VI. erneuerte 9. Februar 1719 das Edikt von 1685\n und Chursachsen erlaubte ihren öffentlichen Gebrauch erst 1765. Diese Maschine,\n die so viel Lärm in der Welt gemacht hat, war in der That Vorläufer der Spinn-\n und Webmaschinen, also der industriellen Revolution des 18. Jahrhunderts. Sie\n befähigte einen in der Weberei ganz unerfahrenen Jungen durch blosses Ab- und\n Zustossen einer Treibstange den ganzen Stuhl mit allen seinen Schützen in Be-\n wegung zu setzen und lieferte, in ihrer verbesserten Form, 40—50 Stück auf\n einmal.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"421\", \"facs\": \"#f0440\"} --> daher seine Angriffe vom materiellen Produktionsmittel selbst auf\n dessen gesellschaftliche Exploitationsform übertragen lernt [Note: In altmodischen Manufakturen wiederholt sich noch heut zuweilen die\n rohe Form der Arbeiterempörung gegen die Maschinerie. So z. B. im file grind-\n ing zu Sheffield 1865.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Kämpfe um den Arbeitslohn innerhalb der Manufaktur setzen die\n Manufaktur voraus und sind keineswegs gegen ihre Existenz gerichtet.\n So weit die Bildung der Manufakturen bekämpft wird, geschieht es von\n den Zunftmeistern und privilegirten Städten, nicht von den Lohnarbeitern.\n Bei Schriftstellern der Manufakturperiode wird die Theilung der Ar-\n beit daher vorherrschend als Mittel aufgefasst virtuell Arbeiter zu er-\n setzen, aber nicht wirklich Arbeiter zu verdrängen. Dieser Unterschied\n ist selbstverständlich. Sagt man z. B., es würden 100 Millionen Menschen\n in England erheischt sein um mit dem alten Spinnrad die Baumwolle zu\n verspinnen, die jetzt von 500,000 mit der Maschine versponnen wird, so\n heisst das natürlich nicht, dass die Maschine den Platz dieser Millionen,\n die nie existirt haben, einnahm. Es heisst nur, dass viele Millionen Arbeiter\n erheischt wären, um die Spinnmaschinerie zu ersetzen. Sagt man\n dagegen, dass der Dampfwebstuhl in England 800,000 Weber auf das\n Pflaster warf, so spricht man nicht von existirender Maschinerie, die durch\n eine bestimmte Arbeiterzahl ersetzt werden müsste, sondern von einer exi-\n stirenden Arbeiterzahl, die faktisch durch Maschinerie ersetzt oder ver-\n drängt worden ist. Während der Manufakturperiode blieb der handwerks-\n mässige Betrieb, wenn auch zerlegt, die Grundlage. Die neuen Kolonialmärkte\n konnten durch die relativ schwache Anzahl der vom Mittelalter überliefer-\n ten städtischen Arbeiter nicht befriedigt werden und die eigentlichen Ma-\n nufakturen öffneten zugleich dem mit Auflösung der Feudalität von Grund\n und Boden verjagten Landvolke neue Produktionsgebiete. Damals trat also\n an der Theilung der Arbeit und der Cooperation in den Werkstätten mehr\n die positive Seite hervor, dass sie beschäftigte Arbeiter produktiver\n machen [Note: Sir James Steuart fasst auch die Wirkung der Maschinerie noch ganz\n in diesem Sinn. „Je considère donc les machines comme des moyens d’augmenter\n (virtuellement) le nombre des gens industrieux qu’on n’est pas obligé de nourrir\n … En quoi l’effet d’une machine differe-t-il de celui de nouveaux habitants?“] . Cooperation und Kombination der Arbeitsmittel in den Hän-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"422\", \"facs\": \"#f0441\"} --> den Weniger rufen, auf die Agrikultur angewandt, zwar grosse, plötzliche\n und gewaltsame Revolutionen der Produktionsweise und daher der\n Lebensbedingungen und Beschäftigungsmittel der Landbevölkerung her-\n vor, die zum Theil der Periode der grossen Industrie lang vorhergehn.\n Aber dieser Kampf spielt ursprünglich mehr zwischen grossen und kleinen\n Landeigenthümern als zwischen Kapital und Lohnarbeit; andrerseits, so-\n weit Arbeiter durch Arbeitsmittel, Schafe, Pferde u. s. w. verdrängt wer-\n den, bilden unmittelbare Gewaltakte hier in erster Instanz die Voraus-\n setzung der industriellen Revolution. Erst werden die Arbeiter vom\n Grund und Boden verjagt, und dann kommen die Schafe. Der Landdieb-\n stahl auf grosser Stufenleiter, wie in England, schafft der grossen Agri-\n kultur erst ihr Anwendungsfeld. In ihren Anfängen hat diese Umwäl-\n zung der Agrikultur daher mehr den Schein einer politischen Re-\n volution.\n\n\n\n\nAls Maschine wird das Arbeitsmittel sofort zum Konkurrenten\n des Arbeiters selbst [Note: „Machinery and Labour are in constant competition.“ (Ricardo l. c.\n p. 479.)] . Die Selbstverwerthung des Kapitals durch die\n Maschine steht im direkten Verhältniss zur Arbeiterzahl, deren Existenz-\n bedingungen sie vernichtet. Das ganze System der kapitalistischen Pro-\n duktion beruht darauf, dass der Arbeiter seine Arbeitskraft als Waare ver-\n kauft. Die Theilung der Arbeit vereinseitigt diese Arbeitskraft zum ganz\n partikularisirten Geschick ein Theilwerkzeug zu führen. Sobald die Füh-\n rung des Werkzeugs der Maschine anheimfällt, erlischt mit dem Gebrauchs-\n werth der Tauschwerth der Arbeitskraft. Der Arbeiter wird unverkäuf-\n lich, wie ausser Kurs gesetztes Papiergeld. Der Theil der Arbeiterklasse, den\n [Note: (Fzs. Uebers. t. I, l. I, ch. XIX.) Viel naiver Petty, der sagt, dass sie die\n „Polygamie“ ersetze. Dieser Gesichtspunkt passt höchstens für einige Theile\n der Ver. Staaten. Dagegen: „Machinery can seldom be used with success to\n abridge the labour of an individual; more time would be lost in its construction\n than could be saved by its application. It is only really useful when it acts on\n great masses, when a single machine can assist the work of thousands. It is\n accordingly in the most populous countries, where there are most idle men, that it\n is most abundant … It is not called into use by a scarcity of men, but\n by the facility with which they can be brought to work in\n masses.“ (Piercy Ravenstone: Thoughts on the Funding\n System and its Effects. Lond. 1824, p. 45.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"423\", \"facs\": \"#f0442\"} --> die Maschinerie so in überflüssige, d. h. nicht länger zur Selbst-\n verwerthung des Kapitals unmittelbar nothwendige Bevölke-\n rung verwandelt, geht einerseits unter in dem ungleichen Kampf des alten\n handwerksmässigen und manufakturmässigen Betriebs wider den maschinen-\n mässigen, überfluthet andrerseits alle leichter zugänglichen Industriezweige,\n überfüllt den Arbeitsmarkt und senkt daher den Preis der Arbeitskraft unter\n ihren Werth. Ein grosser Trost für die pauperisirten Arbeiter soll sein, dass\n ihre Leiden theils nur „zeitlich“ („a temporary inconvenience“), theils\n dass die Maschinerie sich nur allmälig eines ganzen Produktionsfelds be-\n mächtigt, wodurch Umfang und Intensivität ihrer vernichtenden Wirkung\n gebrochen werde. Der eine Trost schlägt den andern. Wo die Maschine\n allmälig ein Produktionsfeld ergreift, producirt sie chronisches Elend in der\n mit ihr konkurrirenden Arbeiterschichte. Wo der Uebergang rasch, wirkt sie\n massenhaft und akut. Die Weltgeschichte bietet kein entsetzlicheres Schau-\n spiel als den allmäligen, über Decennien verschleppten, endlich 1838 besie-\n gelten Untergang der englischen Handbaumwollweber. Viele von ihnen star-\n ben am Hungertod, viele vegetirten lange mit ihren Familien auf 2½ d.\n taglich [Note: Die Konkurrenz zwischen Handgeweb und Maschinengeweb wurde in\n England, vor der Einführung des Armengesetzes von 1833, dadurch verlängert,\n dass man die tief unter das Minimum gefallenen Löhne durch Pfarreiunterstützung\n ergänzte. „The Rev. Mr. Turner was in 1827 rector of Wilmstone, in Che-\n shire, a manufacturing district. The questions of the Committee of Emigration,\n and Mr. Turner’s answers show how the competition of human labour is maintained\n against machinery. Question: ‚Has not the use of the power-loom superseded\n the use of the hand-loom?‘ Answer: ‚Undoubtedly; it would have superseded\n them much more than it has done, if the hand-loom weavers were not enabled to\n submit to a reduction of wages.‘ Question: ‚But in submitting he has accepted\n wages which are insufficient to support him, and looks to parochial contribution\n as the remainder of his support?‘ Answer: ‚Yes, and in fact the competition\n between the hand-loom and the power-loom is maintened out of the poor-rates.‘\n Thus degrading pauperism or expatriation, is the benefit which the industrious\n receive from the introduction of machinery, to be reduced from the respectable\n and in some degree independent mechanic, to the cringing wretch who lives on\n the debasing bread of charity. This they call a temporary inconvenience.“\n („A Prize Essay on the comparative merits of Competition and\n Cooperation. Lond. 1834“, p. 29.)] . Akut dagegen wirkte die englische Baumwollmaschinerie auf\n Ostindien, dessen Generalgouverneur 1834—35 konstatirte: „Das Elend\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"424\", \"facs\": \"#f0443\"} --> findet kaum eine Parallele in der Geschichte des Handels. Die Kno-\n chen der Baumwollweber bleichen die Ebenen von In-\n dien.“ Allerdings, sofern diese Weber das Zeitliche segneten, bereitete\n ihnen die Maschine nur „zeitliche Missstände“. Uebrigens ist die „zeit-\n liche“ Wirkung der Maschinerie permanent, indem sie beständig neue\n Produktionsgebiete ergreift. Die verselbstständigte und entfremdete Gestalt,\n welche die kapitalistische Produktionsweise überhaupt den Arbeitsbedin-\n gungen und dem Arbeitsprodukt gegenüber dem Arbeiter giebt, entwickelt\n sich also mit der Maschinerie zum vollständigen Gegensatz [Note: „The same cause which may increase the revenue of the coun-\n try (d. h., wie Ricardo an derselben Stelle erläutert, the revenues of land-\n lords and capitalists, deren Wealth, ökonomisch betrachtet, überhaupt =\n Wealth of the Nation) may at the same time render the population redun-\n dant and deteriorate the condition of the labourer.“ (Ricardo\n l. c. p. 469.) „Der beständige Zweck und die Tendenz jeder Vervollkommnung\n des Mechanismus ist in der That sich der Arbeit des Menschen ganz zu entschlagen\n oder ihren Preis zu vermindern durch Substitution von Weiber- und Kinder-\n arbeit für die der erwachsnen männlichen Arbeiter, oder roher Arbeiter für ge-\n schickte.“ (Ure l. c. t. I, p. 35.)] . Daher\n mit ihr zum erstenmal die brutale Revolte des Arbeiters gegen das Arbeits-\n mittel.\n\n\n\n\nDas Arbeitsmittel erschlägt den Arbeiter. Dieser\n direkte Gegensatz erscheint allerdings am handgreiflichsten, so oft neu\n eingeführte Maschinerie konkurrirt mit überliefertem Handwerks- oder\n Manufakturbetrieb. Aber innerhalb der grossen Industrie selbst wirkt\n fortwährende Verbesserung der Maschinerie und Entwicklung des automa-\n tischen Systems analog „Der beständige Zweck verbesserter Maschinerie\n ist die Handarbeit zu vermindern oder einen Ring in der Produktionskette\n der Fabrik durch Substitution eiserner für menschliche Apparate zu voll-\n enden“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1858“, p. 43.] . „Die Anwendung von Dampf- und Wasserkraft auf Maschi-\n nerie, die bisher mit der Hand bewegt wurde, ist das Ereigniss jeden Ta-\n ges … Die kleineren Verbesserungen in der Maschinerie, welche Oekonomie\n der Bewegungskraft, Verbesserung des Machwerks, vermehrte Produktion\n in derselben Zeit oder Verdrängung eines Kindes, eines Frauenzimmers\n oder eines Mannes bezwecken, sind constant, und obgleich scheinbar\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"425\", \"facs\": \"#f0444\"} --> nicht von grossem Gewicht, haben sie dennoch wichtige Resultate“ [Note: „Reports etc. 31. Oct. 1856“, p. 15.] .\n „Ueberall, wo eine Operation viel Geschick und eine sichre Hand verlangt,\n entzieht man sie so schnell als möglich den Armen des zu geschickten\n und oft zu Unregelmässigkeiten aller Art geneigten Arbeiters, um einen\n besondern Mechanismus damit zu betrauen, der so gut geregelt ist, dass\n ein Kind ihn überwachen kann“ [Note: Ure l. c. t. I, p. 29. „Der grosse Vortheil der im Ziegelbacken an-\n gewandten Maschinerie besteht darin, den Anwender ganz und gar von geschickten\n Arbeitern unabhängig zu machen.“ („Ch. Empl. Comm. V. Report. Lond.\n 1866“, p. 180, n. 46.)] . „Im automatischen System wird\ndas Talent des Arbeiters progressiv verdrängt“ [Note: Ure l. c. p. 30.] . „Die\n Verbesserung der Maschinerie erfordert nicht nur Verminderung in der\n Anzahl der beschäftigten erwachsnen Arbeiter zur Erzielung eines bestimm-\n ten Resultats, sondern sie substituirt eine Klasse von Individuen einer an-\n dern Klasse, eine minder geschickte einer geschickteren, Kinder den Er-\n wachsnen, Frauen den Männern. Alle diese Wechsel verursachen be-\n ständige Fluktuationen in der Rate des Arbeitslohns“ [Note: l. c. t. II, p. 67.] .\n „Die Maschinerie wirft unaufhörlich Erwachsne aus der Fa-\n brik heraus“ [Note: l. c.] . Die ausserordentliche Elasticität des Ma-\n schinenwesens in Folge gehäufter praktischer Erfahrung, des schon\n vorhandnen Umfangs mechanischer Mittel, und des beständigen Fortschritts\n der Technologie, zeigte uns sein Sturmmarsch unter dem Druck eines ver-\n kürzten Arbeitstags. Aber wer hätte 1860, im Zenithjahr der englischen\n Baumwollindustrie, die galoppirenden Verbesserungen der Maschinerie und\n die entsprechende Deplacirung von Handarbeit geahnt, welche die drei fol-\n genden Jahre unter dem Stachel des amerikanischen Bürger-\n kriegs hervorriefen? Von den offiziellen Anführungen der englischen Fabrik-\n inspektoren über diesen Punkt genügen hier ein paar Beispiele. Ein Manchester\n Fabrikant erklärt: „Statt 75 Kardirmaschinen brauchen wir jetzt nur 12,\n welche dieselbe Quantität von ebenso guter, wenn nicht besserer Qualität\n liefern … Die Ersparung an Arbeitslohn beträgt 10 Pfd. St. wöchent-\n lich, die an Baumwollabfall 10 %.“ In einer Manchester Feinspinnerei\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"426\", \"facs\": \"#f0445\"} --> wurde „vermittelst beschleunigter Bewegung und Einführung verschiedner\n self-acting Prozesse in einem Departement ¼, in einem über ½ des Ar-\n beiterpersonals beseitigt, während die Kämmmaschine an der Stelle der\n zweiten Kardirmaschine die Zahl der früher im Kardirraum beschäftigten\n Hände sehr vermindert hat.“ Eine andre Spinnfabrik schätzt ihre allge-\n meine Ersparung von „Händen“ auf 10 %. Die Herren Gilmore,\n Spinner zu Manchester, erklären: „In unsrem blowing Departement\n schätzen wir die in Folge neuer Maschinerie gemachte Ersparung an Hän-\n den und Arbeitslohn auf ein volles Drittel … in dem jack frame und draw-\n ing frame room ungefähr ⅓ weniger in Auslage und Händen; im Spinn-\n raum ungefähr ⅓ weniger in Auslage. Aber das ist nicht alles; wenn\n unser Garn jetzt zum Weber geht, ist es so sehr verbessert durch die An-\n wendung der neuen Maschinerie, dass sie mehr und besseres Gewebe\n als mit dem alten Maschinengarn produciren“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1863“, p. 108 sqq.] . Fabrikinspektor\n A. Redgrave bemerkt hierzu: „Die Verminderung der Arbeiter bei gestei-\n gerter Produktion schreitet rasch vorwärts; in den Wollfabriken begann\n kürzlich eine neue Reduktion der Hände, und sie dauert fort; vor wenigen\n Tagen sagte mir ein Schulmeister, der bei Rochdale wohnt, die grosse Ab-\n nahme in den Mädchenschulen sei nicht nur dem Druck der Krise ge-\n schuldet, sondern auch den Aenderungen in der Maschinerie der Wollfabrik,\n in Folge deren eine Durchschnittsreduktion von 70 Halbzeitlern statt-\n gefunden“ [Note: l. c. p. 109. Die rasche Verbesserung der Maschinerie während der\n Baumwollkrise erlaubte den englischen Fabrikanten gleich nach Beendigung des\n amerikanischen Bürgerkriegs im Umsehn den Weltmarkt wieder zu überfüllen.\n Die Gewebe wurden schon während der letzten 6 Monate von 1866 fast unverkäuf-\n lich. Damit fing die Konsignation der Waaren nach China und Indien an, was\n den „glut“ natürlich noch intensiver machte. Anfang 1867 nahmen die Fabri-\n kanten zu ihrem gewöhnlichen Ausfluchtsmittel Zuflucht, Herabsetzung des Arbeits-\n lohns um 5 %. Die Arbeiter widersetzten sich und erklärten, theoretisch ganz\n richtig, das einzige Heilmittel sei, kurze Zeit, 4 Tage per Woche, zu arbeiten.\n Nach längerem Sträuben mussten die selbsternannten Industriekapitäne sich hierzu\n entschliessen, an einigen Stellen mit, an andern ohne Lohnherabsetzung\n um 5 %.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Maschinerie wirkt jedoch nicht nur als übermächtiger Konkur-\n rent, stets auf dem Sprung den Lohnarbeiter „überflüssig“ zu machen.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"427\", \"facs\": \"#f0446\"} --> Als ihm feindliche Potenz wird sie laut und tendenziell vom Kapital\n proklamirt und gehandhabt. Sie wird das machtvollste Kriegs-\n mittel zur Niederschlagung der periodischen Arbeiteraufstände, strikes\n u. s. w. wider die Autokratie des Kapitals [Note: „Das Verhältniss zwischen Meistern und Händen in den Flint- und\n Flaschenglas-Blasereien ist ein chronischer strike.“ Daher der Aufschwung der\n Manufaktur des gepressten Glases, wo die Hauptoperationen durch Maschinerie\n ausgeführt werden. Eine Firma bei Newcastle, die früher jährlich 350,000 ge-\n blasnes Flintglas producirte, producirt jetzt statt dessen 3,000,500 Pfund ge-\n presstes Glas. („Ch. Empl. Comm. IV. Rep. 1865“, p. 262, 263.)] . Nach Gaskell\n war gleich die Dampfmaschine ein Antagonist der „Menschenkraft“, der\n den Kapitalisten befähigte die steigenden Ansprüche der Arbeiter nieder-\n zuschmettern, die das beginnende Fabriksystem zur Krise zu treiben droh-\n ten [Note: Gaskell: „The Manufacturing Population of England.\n Lond. 1833“, p. 3, 4.] . Man könnte eine ganze Geschichte der Erfindungen seit 1830\n schreiben, die bloss als Kriegsmittel des Kapitals wider Arbeiteremeuten\n ins Leben traten. Wir erinnern vor allem an die selfacting mule, weil\n sie eine neue Epoche des automatischen Systems eröffnet [Note: Einige sehr bedeutende Anwendungen von Maschinen zum Maschinenbau\n erfand Herr Fairbairn in Folge von strikes in seiner eignen Maschinenfabrik.] . Ure sagt\n von einer Maschine zum Farbendruck in den Kattundruckereien: „Endlich\n suchten sich die Kapitalisten von dieser unerträglichen Sklaverei\n (nämlich den ihnen lästigen Kontraktsbedingungen der Arbeiter) zu be-\n freien, indem sie die Hilfsquellen der Wissenschaft anriefen, und bald wa-\n ren sie reintegrirt in ihre legitimen Rechte, die des Kopfes\n über die andern Körpertheile.“ Er sagt von einer Erfindung zum Ketten-\n schlichten, deren unmittelbarer Anlass ein strike: „Die Horde der Unzu-\n friednen, die sich hinter den alten Linien der Theilung der Arbeit unbesiegbar\n verschanzt wähnte, sah sich so in die Flanke genommen und ihre Vertheidi-\n gungsmittel vernichtet durch die moderne Taktik der Maschinisten.\n Sie mussten sich auf Gnade und Ungnade ergeben.“ Er sagt von\n der Erfindung der selfácting mule: „Sie war berufen die Ordnung unter\n den industriellen Klassen wieder herzustellen … Diese Erfindung bestä-\n tigt die von uns bereits entwickelte Doktrin, dass das Ka-\n pital, indem es die Wissenschaft in seinen Dienst presst,\n stets die rebellische Hand der Industrie zum Gehorsam\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"428\", \"facs\": \"#f0447\"} --> zwingt“ [Note: Ure l. c. t. II, p. 141, 142, 140] . Obgleich Ure’s Schrift vor 30 Jahren erschien, also zur\n Zeit eines relativ noch schwach entwickelten Fabriksystems, bleibt sie der\n klassische Ausdruck des Fabrikgeists, nicht nur wegen ihres\noffenherzigen Cynismus, sondern auch wegen der Naivetät, womit\n er die gedankenlosen Widersprüche des Kapitalhirns ausplaudert. Nach-\n dem er z. B. die „Doktrin“ entwickelt, dass das Kapital mit Hilfe der von\n ihm in Sold genommenen Wissenschaft „stets die rebellische Hand der In-\n dustrie zum Gehorsam zwingt“, entrüstet er sich darüber, „dass man von\n gewisser Seite die mechanisch-physische Wissenschaft anklagt, sich dem\n Despotismus reicher Kapitalisten zu leihen und zum Unterdrückungsmittel\n der armen Klassen herzugeben.“ Nachdem er weit und breit gepredigt,\n wie vortheilhaft rasche Entwicklung der Maschinerie den Arbeitern, warnt\n er sie, dass sie durch ihre Widersetzlichkeit, Strikes u. s. w., die Ent-\n wicklung der Maschinerie beschleunigen. „Derartige Re-\n volten“, sagt er, „zeigen die menschliche Verblendung in ihrem verächt-\n lichsten Charakter, dem Charakter eines Menschen, der sich zu sei-\n nem eignen Henker macht.“ Wenige Seiten vorher heisst es um-\n gekehrt: „Ohne die Kollisionen und heftigen Unterbrechungen, verursacht\n durch die irrigen Ansichten der Arbeiter, hätte sich das Fabrik-\n system noch viel rascher entwickelt und viel nützlicher für alle\n interessirten Parteien.“ Dann ruft er wieder aus: „Zum Glück für die\n Bevölkerung der Fabrikstädte Grossbritaniens finden die Verbesserungen\n in der Mechanik nur allmälig statt.“ „Mit Unrecht“, sagt er, „klagt man\n die Maschinen an, dass sie den Arbeitslohn der Erwachsnen vermindern,\n indem sie einen Theil derselben deplaciren, wodurch ihre Anzahl das Be-\n dürfniss nach Arbeit übersteigt. Aber es findet vermehrte Anwendung\n der Kinderarbeit statt und der Gewinn der Erwachsnen ist dadurch um so\n beträchtlicher.“ Derselbe Trostspender vertheidigt andrerseits die Niedrig-\n keit der Kinderlöhne damit, dass „sie die Aeltern abhalten ihre Kinder\n zu früh in die Fabriken zu schicken.“ Sein ganzes Buch ist eine Apo-\n logie des unbeschränkten Arbeitstags und es erinnert seine liberale Seele\n an die dunkelsten Zeiten des Mittelalters, wenn die Gesetzgebung verbietet\n Kinder von 13 Jahren mehr als 12 Stunden per Tag abzurackern. Diess\n hält ihn nicht ab die Fabrikarbeiter zu einem Dankgebet an die Vorsehung\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"429\", \"facs\": \"#f0448\"} --> aufzufordern, die ihnen durch die Maschinerie „die Musse verschafft habe\n über ihre unsterblichen Interessen nachzudenken“ [Note: l. c. und p. 68, 143, 5, 6.] .\n\n\n\n\nEine ganze Reihe bürgerlicher Oekonomen, wie James Mill, Mac Cul-\n loch, Torrens, Senior, J. St. Mill u. s. w., behauptet, dass alle Maschinerie,\n die Arbeiter verdrängt, stets gleichzeitig und nothwendig ein adäquates\n Kapital zur Beschäftigung derselben identischen Arbeiter freisetzt [Note: Ricardo theilte ursprünglich diese Ansicht, widerrief sie aber später\n ausdrücklich mit seiner charakteristischen wissenschaftlichen Unbefangenheit und\n Wahrheitsliebe. Sieh l. c. ch. XXXI. „On Machinery“.] .\n\n\n\n\nMan unterstelle, ein Kapitalist wende 100 Arbeiter an z. B. in einer\n Tapetenmanufaktur, den Mann zu 30 Pfd. St. jährlich. Das von ihm\n jährlich ausgelegte variable Kapital beträgt also 3000 Pfd. St. Er\n entlasse 50 Arbeiter und beschäftige die übrigbleibenden 50 mit einer\n Maschinerie, die ihm 1500 Pfd. St. kostet. Der Vereinfachung halber\n wird von Baulichkeiten, Kohlen u. s. w. abgesehn. Man nimmt ferner an,\n das jährlich verzehrte Rohmaterial koste nach wie vor 3000 Pfd. St. [Note: Nb., ich gebe die Illustration ganz in der Weise der obengenannten\n Oekonomen.] .\n Ist durch diese Metamorphose irgend ein Kapital „freigesetzt“? In der\n alten Betriebsweise bestand die ausgelegte Gesammtsumme von 6000 Pfd. St.\n halb aus constantem und halb aus variablem Kapital. Sie besteht jetzt\n aus 4500 Pfd. St. (3000 Pfd. St. für Rohmaterial und 1500 Pfd. St.\n für Maschinerie) constantem und 1500 Pfd. St. variablem Kapital.\n Statt der Hälfte bildet der variable oder in lebendige Arbeitskraft umge-\n setzte Kapitaltheil nur noch ¼ des Gesammtkapitals. Statt der Frei-\n setzung findet hier Bindung von Kapital in einer Form statt, worin es\n aufhört sich gegen Arbeitskraft auszutauschen, d. h. Verwandlung von\n variablem in constantes Kapital. Das Kapital von 6000 Pfd. St. kann,\n unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen, jetzt niemals mehr als 50 Arbeiter\n beschäftigen. Mit jeder Verbesserung der Maschinerie beschäftigt es we-\n niger. Kostete die neu eingeführte Maschinerie weniger als die Summe\n der von ihr verdrängten Arbeitskraft und Arbeitswerkzeuge, also z. B.\n statt 1500 nur 1000 Pfd. St., so würde ein variables Kapital von 1000\n Pfd. St. in constantes verwandelt oder gebunden, während ein Kapital von\n 500 Pfd. St. freigesetzt würde. Letzteres, denselben Jahreslohn unter-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"430\", \"facs\": \"#f0449\"} --> stellt, bildet einen Beschäftigungsfonds für ungefähr 16 Arbeiter, während\n 50 entlassen sind, ja für viel weniger als 16 Arbeiter, da die 500 Pfd. St.\n zu ihrer Verwandlung in Kapital wieder zum Theil in constantes Kapital\n verwandelt werden müssen, also auch nur zum Theil in Arbeitskraft um-\n gesetzt werden können.\n\n\n\n\nIn der That meinen jene Apologeten auch nicht diese Art Freisetzung\n von Kapital. Sie meinen die Lebensmittel der freigesetzten\n Arbeiter. Es kann nicht geläugnet werden, dass im obigen Fall z. B.\n die Maschinerie nicht nur 50 Arbeiter freisetzt und dadurch „disponibel“\n macht, sondern zugleich ihren Zusammenhang mit Lebensmitteln zum\n Werth von 1500 Pfd. St. aufhebt und so diese Lebensmittel „freisetzt“.\n Die einfache und keineswegs neue Thatsache, dass die Maschinerie\n den Arbeiter von Lebensmitteln freisetzt, lautet also ökono-\n misch, dass die Maschinerie Lebensmittel für den Arbeiter\n freisetzt oder in Kapital zu seiner Anwendung verwandelt. Man sieht,\n es kommt alles auf die Ausdrucksweise an. Nominibus mollire licet mala.\n\n\n\n\nDie Lebensmittel zum Betrag von 1500 Pfd. St. standen den ent-\n lassnen Arbeitern niemals als Kapital gegenüber. Was ihnen als Ka-\n pital gegenüberstand, waren die jetzt in Maschinerie verwandel-\n ten 1500 Pfd. St. Näher betrachtet repräsentirten diese 1500 Pfd. St.\n nur einen Theil der vermittelst der entlassnen 50 Arbeiter jährlich produ-\n cirten Tapeten, die sie in Geldform statt in natura von ihrem Anwender zum\n Lohn erhielten. Mit den in 1500 Pfd. St. verwandelten Tapeten kauften sie\n Lebensmittel zu demselben Betrag. Diese existirten für sie daher nicht als\n Kapital, sondern als Waaren, und sie selbst existirten für diese Waaren\n nicht als Lohnarbeiter, sondern als Käufer. Der Umstand, dass die Ma-\n schinerie sie von Kaufmitteln „freigesetzt“ hat, verwandelt sie aus Käufern\n in Nicht-Käufer. Daher verminderte Nachfrage für jene Waaren. Voilà tout.\n Wird diese verminderte Nachfrage nicht durch vermehrte Nachfrage von an-\n derer Seite kompensirt, so sinkt der Marktpreis der Waaren. Dauert diess\n länger und in grössrem Umfange, so erfolgt ein Deplacement der in der Pro-\n duktion jener Waaren beschäftigten Arbeiter. Ein Theil des Kapitals,\n das früher nothwendige Lebensmittel producirte, wird in anderer Form\n reproducirt. Während des Falls der Marktpreise und des Deplacements\n von Kapital, werden auch die in der Produktion der nothwendigen Lebens-\n mittel beschäftigten Arbeiter von einem Theil ihres Lohns „freigesetzt“.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"431\", \"facs\": \"#f0450\"} --> Statt also zu beweisen, dass die Maschinerie durch die Freisetzung der\n Arbeiter von Lebensmitteln letztre gleichzeitig in Kapital zur Anwendung\n der erstern verwandelt, beweist der Herr Apologet mit dem probaten Ge-\n setz von Nachfrage und Zufuhr umgekehrt, dass die Maschinerie nicht nur\n in dem Produktionszweig, worin sie eingeführt, sondern auch in den Pro-\n duktionszweigen, worin sie nicht eingeführt wird, Arbeiter aufs Pflaster\n wirft.\n\n\n\n\nAusser der guten Absicht der Vertuschung liegt jener abgeschmack-\n ten Kompensationstheorie zu Grunde, erstens, dass die Maschinerie früher\n gebundne Arbeitskraft freisetzt, und falls zuschüssiges Kapital\n nach Anlage drängt, ihm mit der disponiblen Arbeitskraft gleich-\n zeitig disponibel gemachte Lebensmittel zur Verfügung stellt. Aber die\n Maschinerie deplacirt nicht nur die zunächst „überzählig“ gemachten,\n sondern zugleich den neuen Menschenstrom, der jedem Industriezweig sein\n Kontingent zum regelmässigen Ersatz und Wachsthum liefert. Diese Er-\n satzmannschaft wird neu vertheilt und in andern Arbeitszweigen absorbirt,\n während die ursprünglichen Opfer grossentheils in der Uebergangs-\n periode verkommen und verkümmern. Zudem ist ihre Arbeitskraft\n durch die Theilung der Arbeit so vereinseitigt, dass sie nur in wenigen und\n daher beständig überfüllten niedrigen Arbeitszweigen Zugang finden [Note: Ein Ricardianer bemerkt hierüber gegen die Fadaisen J. B. Say’s:\n „Bei entwickelter Theilung der Arbeit ist das Geschick der Arbeiter nur in dem\n besondern Zweig anwendbar, worin sie aufgebracht wurden; sie selbst sind\n eine Art von Maschinen. Es hilft daher absolut nichts papageimässig zu\n plappern, dass die Dinge eine Tendenz haben, ihr Niveau zu finden. Wir müssen\n um uns schauen und sehn, dass sie für lange Zeit ihr Niveau nicht finden\n können; dass wenn sie es finden, das Niveau niedriger steht als beim Aus-\n gang des Prozesses.“ („An Inquiry into those Principles respecting\n the Nature of Demand etc. Lond. 1821“, p. 72.)] .\n Zweitens aber wird die unzweifelhafte Thatsache ausgesprochen, dass die\n Maschinerie an sich nicht verantwortlich ist für die „Freisetzung“\n der Arbeiter von Lebensmitteln. Sie verwohlfeilert und vermehrt das\n Produkt in dem Zweig, den sie ergreift, und lässt die in andern Industrie-\n zweigen producirte Lebensmittel-Masse zunächst unverändert. Nach wie\n vor ihrer Einführung besitzt die Gesellschaft also gleichviel oder mehr\n Lebensmittel für die deplacirten Arbeiter, ganz abgesehen von dem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"432\", \"facs\": \"#f0451\"} --> enormen Theil des jährlichen Produkts, der von Nichtarbeitern vergeudet\n wird. Und diess ist die Pointe der ökonomistischen Apologetik! Die von\n der kapitalistischen Anwendung der Maschinerie untrennbaren\n Widersprüche und Antagonismen existiren nicht, weil sie nicht\n aus der Maschinerie selbst erwachsen, sondern aus ihrer kapi-\n talistischen Anwendung! Da also die Maschinerie an sich betrachtet\n die Arbeitszeit verkürzt, während sie kapitalistisch angewandt den Arbeits-\n tag verlängert, an sich die Arbeit erleichtert, kapitalistisch angewandt ihre\n Intensivität steigert, an sich ein Sieg des Menschen über die Naturkraft ist,\n kapitalistisch angewandt den Menschen durch die Naturkraft unterjocht,\n an sich den Reichthum des Producenten vermehrt, kapitalistisch angewandt\n ihn verpaupert u. s. w., erklärt der bürgerliche Oekonom einfach, das An-\n sichbetrachten der Maschinerie beweise haarscharf, dass alle jene\n handgreiflichen Widersprüche blosser Schein der gemeinen Wirklichkeit,\n aber an sich, also auch in der Theorie, gar nicht vorhanden sind.\n Er spart sich so alles weitere Kopfbrechen und bürdet seinem Gegner\n obendrein die Dummheit auf, nicht die kapitalistische Anwen-\n dung der Maschinerie zu bekämpfen, sondern die Maschinerie\n selbst [Note: Ein Virtuose in diesem anmasslichen Cretinismus ist u. a. Mac Cul-\n loch. „Wenn es vortheilhaft ist“, sagt er z. B. mit der affektirten Naivetät eines\n Kindes von 8 Jahren, „das Geschick des Arbeiters mehr und mehr zu entwickeln,\n so dass er fähig wird ein stets wachsendes Waarenquantum mit demselben oder\n geringerem Arbeitsquantum zu produciren, so muss es auch vortheilhaft sein, dass\ner sich solcher Maschinerie zu seinem Beistande bediene als ihn\n am wirksamsten in der Erreichung dieses Resultats unterstützt.“ (Mac\n Culloch: „Princ. of Pol. Econ. Lond. 1830“, p. 166.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDa jedes Maschinenprodukt, z. B. eine Elle Maschinengeweb, wohl-\n feiler ist als das von ihm verdrängte gleichartige Handprodukt, folgt als\n absolutes Gesetz: Bleibt das Gesammtquantum des maschinen-\n mässig producirten Artikels gleich dem Gesammtquantum des\n von ihm ersetzten handwerks- oder manufakturmässig producirten Artikels,\nso vermindert sich die Gesammtsumme der angewandten\n Arbeit. Die etwa zur Produktion der Arbeitsmittel selbst, der Maschi-\n nerie, Kohle u. s. w., erheischte Arbeitszunahme muss kleiner sein\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"433\", \"facs\": \"#f0452\"} --> als die durch Anwendung der Maschinerie bewirkte Arbeitsabnahme.\n Das Maschinenprodukt wäre sonst eben so theuer oder theurer als das\n Handprodukt. Statt aber gleich zu bleiben, wächst thatsächlich die Ge-\n sammtmasse des von einer verminderten Arbeiteranzahl pro-\n ducirten Maschinenartikels weit über die Gesammtmasse des verdrängten\n Handwerksartikels. Gesetzt 400,000 Ellen Maschinengeweb würden von\n weniger Arbeitern producirt als 100,000 Ellen Handgeweb. In dem ver-\n vierfachten Produkt steckt viermal mehr Rohmaterial. Die Produktion\n des Rohmaterials muss also vervierfacht werden. Was aber die verzehr-\n ten Arbeitsmittel, wie Baulichkeiten, Kohlen, Maschinen u. s. w. betrifft,\n so ändert sich die Grenze, innerhalb deren die zu ihrer Produktion er-\n heischte zusätzliche Arbeit wachsen kann, mit der Differenz\n zwischen der Masse des Maschinenprodukts und der Masse des von der-\n selben Arbeiterzahl herstellbaren Handprodukts.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0001","text":"## 4) Maschinerie und grosse Industrie.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0002","text":"John Stuart Mill sagt in seinen „Principien der politischen\n Oekonomie“: „Es ist fraglich, ob alle bisher gemachten mechanischen\n Erfindungen die Tagesmühe irgend eines menschlichen Wesens erleichtert\n haben“ [Note: „It is questionable, if all the mechanical inventions yet made have\n lightened the day’s toil of any human being.“ Mill hätte sagen sollen „of any\n human being not fed by other people’s labour“, denn die Maschinerie hat un-\n streitig die Zahl der vornehmen Müssiggänger sehr vermehrt.] . Solches ist jedoch auch keineswegs der Zweck der kapita-\n listisch verwandten Maschinerie. Gleich jeder andern Entwicklung der\n Produktivkraft der Arbeit soll sie Waaren verwohlfeilern und den Theil\n des Arbeitstags, den der Arbeiter für sich selbst braucht, verkürzen,\n um den andern Theil seines Arbeitstags, den er dem Kapitalisten umsonst\n giebt, zu verlängern. Sie ist Mittel zur Produktion von Mehr-\n werth.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0003","text":"Die Umwälzung der Produktionsweise geht in der Manufaktur von der\nArbeitskraft aus, in der grossen Industrie vom Arbeitsmittel. Es\n ist also zunächst zu untersuchen, wodurch sich das Arbeitsmittel aus einem\n Werkzeug in eine Maschine verwandelt, oder wodurch sich die Maschine\n vom Handwerksinstrument unterscheidet. Es handelt sich hier nur um\n [Running matter: 23*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"356\", \"facs\": \"#f0375\"} --> grosse, allgemeine Charakterzüge, denn abstrakt strenge Grenzlinien schei-\n den ebensowenig die Epochen der Gesellschafts- wie die der Erdge-\n schichte.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0004","text":"Mathematiker und Mechaniker — und man findet diess hier und da\n von englischen Oekonomen wiederholt — erklären das Werkzeug für eine\n einfache Maschine und die Maschine für ein zusammengesetztes Werkzeug.\n Nach ihnen findet hier also kein wesentlicher Unterschied statt. In die-\n sem Sinn heissen sogar die einfachen mechanischen Potenzen, wie Hebel,\n schiefe Ebne, Schraube, Keil u. s. w. Maschinen [Note: Sieh z. B. „Hutton’s Course of Mathematics“.] . Diess ist richtig\n vom mathematischen Standpunkt, denn jede Maschine besteht aus jenen\n einfachen Potenzen, wie immer verkleidet und kombinirt. Vom ökono-\n mischen Standpunkt taugt die Erklärung nichts, denn ihr fehlt das Ent-\n scheidende, das historische Element. Andrerseits sucht man den Unter-\n schied zwischen Werkzeug und Maschine darin, dass beim Werkzeug\n der Mensch die Bewegungskraft, bei der Maschine eine von der mensch-\n lichen verschiedne Naturkraft, wie Thier, Wasser, Wind u. s. w. [Note: „Von diesem Gesichtspunkt aus lässt sich denn auch eine scharfe Grenze\n zwischen Werkzeug und Maschine ziehen: Spaten, Hammer, Meisel u. s. w.,\n Hebel- und Schraubenwerke, für welche, mögen sie übrigens noch so künstlich\n sein, der Mensch die bewegende Kraft ist … diess alles fällt unter den Begriff\n des Werkzeugs; während der Pflug mit der ihn bewegenden Thierkraft, Wind-\n u. s. w. Mühlen zu den Maschinen zu zählen sind.“ (Wilhelm Schulz:\n „Die Bewegung der Produktion. Zürich 1843“, p. 38.) Eine in\n mancher Hinsicht lobenswerthe Schrift.] \n Danach wäre ein mit Ochsen bespannter Pflug, der den verschiedensten\n Produktionsepochen angehört, eine Maschine, Claussen’s Circular Loom, der,\n von der Hand eines einzigen Arbeiters bewegt, 96,000 Maschen in einer\n Minute verfertigt, ein blosses Werkzeug. Ja, derselbe loom wäre Werk-\n zeug, wenn mit der Hand, und Maschine, wenn mit Dampf bewegt. Da\n die Anwendung von Thierkraft eine der ältesten Erfindungen der Mensch-\n heit, ginge in der That die Maschinenproduktion der Handwerksproduktion\n voraus. Als John Wyalt 1735 seine Spinnmaschine und mit ihr die\n industrielle Revolution des 18. Jahrhunderts ankündigte, erwähnte er mit\n keinem Wort, dass statt eines Menschen ein Esel die Maschine treibe, und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"357\", \"facs\": \"#f0376\"} --> dennoch fiel diese Rolle dem Esel zu. Eine Maschine, „um ohne\n Finger zu spinnen,“ lautete sein Programm [Note: Schon vor ihm wurden, wenn auch sehr unvollkommene, Maschinen zum\n Vorspinnen angewandt, wahrscheinlich zuerst in Italien. Eine kritische Ge-\n schichte der Technologie würde überhaupt nachweisen, wie wenig irgend\n eine Erfindung des 18. Jahrhunderts einem einzelnen Individuum gehört. Bisher\n existirt kein solches Werk. Darwin hat das Interesse auf die Geschichte der\n natürlichen Technologie gelenkt, d. h. auf die Bildung der Pflanzen- und Thier-\n organe als Produktionsinstrumente für das Leben der Pflanzen und Thiere. Ver-\n dient die Bildungsgeschichte der produktiven Organe des Gesellschaftsmenschen,\n der materiellen Basis jeder besondern Gesellschallsorganisation, nicht gleiche Auf-\n merksamkeit? Und wäre sie nicht leichter zu liefern, da, wie Vico sagt, die\n Menschengeschichte sich dadurch von der Naturgeschichte unterscheidet, dass wir\n die eine gemacht und die andere nicht gemacht haben? Die Technologie enthüllt\n das aktive Verhalten des Menschen zur Natur, den unmittelbaren Produktionspro-\n zess seines Lebens, damit auch seiner gesellschaftlichen Lebensverhältnisse und der\n ihnen entquellenden geistigen Vorstellungen. Selbst alle Religionsgeschichte, die\n von dieser materiellen Basis abstrahirt, ist — unkritisch. Es ist in der That viel\n leichter durch Analyse den irdischen Kern der religiösen Nebelbildungen zu finden,\n als umgekehrt aus den jedesmaligen wirklichen Lebensverhältnissen ihre verhim-\n melten Formen zu entwickeln. Die letztere ist die einzig materialistische und\n daher wissenschaftliche Methode. Die Mängel des abstrakt naturwissenschaftlichen\n Materialismus, der den geschichtlichen Prozess ausschliesst, ersieht man\n schon aus den abstrakten und ideologischen Vorstellungen seiner Wortführer, so\n bald sie sich über ihre Specialität hinauswagen.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0005","text":"Alle entwickelte Maschinerie besteht aus drei wesentlich verschie-\n denen Theilen, der Bewegungsmaschine, dem Transmissions-\n mechanismus, endlich der Werkzeugmaschine oder Arbeits-\n maschine. Die Bewegungsmaschine wirkt als Triebkraft des ganzen\n Mechanismus. Sie erzeugt ihre eigne Bewegungskraft, wie die Dampf-\n maschine, kalorische Maschine, elektro-magnetische Maschine u. s. w., oder\n sie empfängt den Anstoss von einer Naturkraft ausser ihr, wie das Wasser-\n rad vom Wassergefäll, der Windflügel vom Wind u. s. w. Der Trans-\n missionsmechanismus, zusammengesetzt aus Schwungrädern, Treibwellen,\n Zahnrädern, Kreiselrädern, Schäften, Schnüren, Riemen, Zwischengeschirr\n und Vorgelege der verschiedensten Art, regelt die Bewegung, verwandelt,\n wo es nöthig, ihre Form, z. B. aus einer perpendikulären in eine kreis-\n förmige, vertheilt und überträgt sie auf die Werkzeugmaschinerie. Beide\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"358\", \"facs\": \"#f0377\"} --> Theile des Mechanismus sind nur vorhanden, um der Werkzeugmaschine\n die Bewegung mitzutheilen, wodurch sie den Arbeitsgegenstand anpackt\n und zweckgemäss verändert. Dieser Theil der Maschinerie, die Werk-\n zeugmaschine ist es, wovon die industrielle Revolution im 18. Jahr-\n hundert ausgeht. Sie bildet noch jeden Tag von neuem den Ausgangs-\n punkt, so oft Handwerksbetrieb oder Manufakturbetrieb in Maschinenbe-\n trieb übergeht.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0006","text":"Sehn wir uns nun die Werkzeugmaschine oder eigentliche\nArbeitsmaschine näher an, so erscheinen im Grossen und Ganzen,\n wenn auch oft in sehr modificirter Form, die Apparate und Werkzeuge\n wieder, womit der Handwerker oder Manufakturarbeiter arbeitet, aber\n statt als Werkzeuge des Menschen jetzt als Werkzeuge eines Mechanismus\n oder als mechanische Werkzeuge. Entweder ist die ganze Maschine nur\n eine mehr oder minder veränderte mechanische Ausgabe des alten Hand-\n werksinstruments, wie bei dem mechanischen Webstuhl [Note: Namentlich in der ursprünglichen Form des mechanischen Webstuhls er-\n kennt man den alten Webstuhl auf den ersten Blick wieder. Wesentlich verändert\n erscheint er in seiner modernen Form.] , oder die am\n Gerüst der Arbeitsmaschine angebrachten thätigen Organe sind alte Be-\n kannte, wie Spindeln bei der Spinnmaschine, Nadeln beim Strumpfwirker-\n stuhl, Sägeblätter bei der Sägemaschine, Messer bei der Zerhackmaschine\n u. s. w. Der Unterschied dieser Werkzeuge von dem eigentlichen\n Körper der Arbeitsmaschine erstreckt sich bis auf ihre Geburt. Sie wer-\n den nämlich immer noch grossentheils handwerksmässig oder manufaktur-\n mässig producirt und später erst an den maschinenmässig producirten\n Körper der Arbeitsmaschine befestigt [Note: Erst seit ungefähr 15 Jahren wird ein stets wachsender Theil der Werk-\n zeuge der Arbeitsmaschinen maschinenmässig in England fabricirt, obgleich nicht\n von denselben Fabrikanten, welche die Maschinen selbst machen. Maschinen zur\n Fabrikation solcher mechanischen Werkzeuge sind z. B. die automatic bobbin-making\n engine, card-setting engine, Maschinen zum Machen der Weberlitzen, Maschinen\n zum Schmieden von mule und throstle Spindeln.] . Die Werkzeugmaschine ist\n also ein Mechanismus, der, nach Mittheilung der entsprechenden Bewe-\n gung, mit seinen Werkzeugen dieselben Operationen verrichtet, welche früher\n der Arbeiter mit ähnlichen Werkzeugen verrichtete. Ob die Triebkraft\n nun vom Menschen ausgeht oder selbst wieder von einer Maschine, ändert\n am Wesen der Sache nichts. Nach Uebertragung des eigentlichen Werk-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"359\", \"facs\": \"#f0378\"} --> zeugs vom Menschen auf einen Mechanismus, tritt eine Maschine an die\n Stelle eines blossen Werkzeugs. Der Unterschied springt sofort ins\n Auge, auch wenn der Mensch selbst noch der erste Motor bleibt. Die\nAnzahl von Arbeitsinstrumenten, womit er gleichzeitig wirken\n kann, ist durch die Anzahl seiner natürlichen Produktionsinstrumente, sei-\n ner eignen körperlichen Organe, beschränkt. Man versuchte in Deutsch-\n land erst einen Spinner zwei Spinnräder treten, ihn also gleichzeitig mit\n zwei Händen und zwei Füssen arbeiten zu lassen. Diess war zu an-\n strengend. Später erfand man ein Tret-Spinnrad mit zwei Spindeln, aber\n die Spinnvirtuosen, die zwei Fäden gleichzeitig spinnen konnten, waren\n fast so selten als zweiköpfige Menschen. Die Jenny spinnt dagegen von\n vorn herein mit 12—18 Spindeln, der Strumpfwirkerstuhl strickt mit viel\n 1000 Nadeln auf einmal u. s. w. Die Anzahl der Werkzeuge, womit\n dieselbe Werkzeugmaschine gleichzeitig spielt, ist von vorn herein eman-\n cipirt von der organischen Schranke, wodurch das Handwerkszeug eines\n Arbeiters beengt wird.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0007","text":"An vielem Handwerkszeug besitzt der Unterschied zwischen dem\n Menschen als blosser Triebkraft und als Arbeiter mit dem eigentlichen Opera-\n teur eine sinnlich besonderte Existenz. Z. B. beim Spinnrad wirkt der Fuss\n nur als Triebkraft, während die Hand, die an der Spindel arbeitet,\n zupft und dreht, die eigentliche Spinnoperation verrichtet. Grade diesen\n letzten Theil des Handwerksinstruments ergreift die industrielle Revolution\n zuerst und überlässt dem Menschen, neben der neuen Arbeit die Maschine\n mit seinem Auge zu überwachen und ihre Irrthümer mit seiner Hand zu\n verbessern, zunächst noch die rein mechanische Rolle der Triebkraft.\n Werkzeuge dagegen, auf die der Mensch von vorn herein nur als einfache\n Triebkraft wirkt, wie z. B. beim Drehn der Kurbel einer Mühle [Note: Moses von Aegypten sagt: „Du sollst dem Ochsen, der drischt, nicht\n das Maul verbinden.“ Die christlich germanischen Philanthropen legten dagegen\n dem Leibeignen, den sie als Triebkraft zum Mahlen verwandten, eine grosse höl-\n zerne Scheibe um den Hals, damit er kein Mehl mit der Hand zum Mund bringen könne.] , bei\n Pumpen, beim Auf- und Abbewegen der Arme eines Blasbalgs, beim\n Stossen eines Mörsers u. s. w., rufen zwar zuerst die Anwendung von\n Thieren, Wasser, Wind [Note: Theils Mangel an lebendigem Wassergefäll, theils Kampf gegen sonstigen\n Wasserüberfluss zwangen die Holländer zur Anwendung des Winds als Triebkraft.]  als Bewegungskräften hervor. Sie recken sich,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"360\", \"facs\": \"#f0379\"} --> theilweise innerhalb, sporadisch schon lange vor der Manufakturperiode,\n zu Maschinen, aber sie revolutioniren die Produktionsweise nicht. Dass\n sie selbst in ihrer handwerksmässigen Form bereits Maschinen sind, zeigt\n sich in der Periode der grossen Industrie. Die Pumpen z. B., womit die\n Holländer 1836—37 den See von Harlem auspumpten, waren nach dem\n Princip gewöhnlicher Pumpen konstruirt, nur dass cyklopische Dampf-\n maschinen statt der Menschenhände ihre Kolben trieben. Der gewöhn-\n liche und sehr unvollkommene Blasbalg des Grobschmidts wird noch zu-\n weilen in England durch blosse Verbindung seines Arms mit einer Dampf-\n maschine in eine mechanische Luftpumpe verwandelt. Die Dampfmaschine\n selbst, wie sie Ende des 17. Jahrhunderts, während der Manufakturperiode,\n erfunden ward und bis zum Anfang der 80er Jahre des 18. Jahrhunderts\n fortexistirte [Note: Sie wurde zwar schon sehr verbessert durch Watt’s erste, sogenannte\n einfach wirkende Dampfmaschine, blieb aber in dieser Form blosse Hebemaschine\n für Wasser und Salzsoole. Man kann nicht einmal sagen, dass die Dampfmaschine\n in dieser ersten Form wissenschaftlich bedeutenden Anstoss gab.] , rief keine industrielle Revolution hervor. Es war viel-\n mehr umgekehrt die Schöpfung der Werkzeugmaschinen, welche die revo-\n lutionirte Dampfmaschine nothwendig und darum möglich machte. Sobald\n der Mensch, statt mit dem Werkzeug auf den Arbeitsgegenstand, nur noch\n als Triebkraft auf eine Werkzeugmaschine wirkt, wird die Verkleidung der\n Triebkraft in menschliche Muskel zufällig und kann Wind, Wasser,\n Dampf u. s. w. an die Stelle treten. Diess schliesst natürlich nicht aus,\n dass solcher Wechsel oft grosse technische Aenderungen des ursprünglich\n für menschliche Triebkraft allein konstruirten Mechanismus bedingt. Heut-\n zutage werden alle Maschinen, die sich erst Bahn brechen müssen, wie\n Nähmaschinen, Brodbereitungsmaschinen u. s. w., wenn sie den kleinen Mass-\n stab nicht von vorn herein durch ihre Bestimmung ausschliessen, für mensch-\n liche und rein mechanische Triebkraft zugleich konstruirt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0008","text":" [Note: Die Windmühle selbst erhielten sie aus Deutschland, wo diese Erfindung einen\n artigen Kampf zwischen Adel, Pfaffen und Kaiser hervorrief, wem denn von den\n drei der Wind „gehöre“. Luft macht eigen, hiess es in Deutschland, während\n der Wind Holland frei machte. Was er hier eigen machte, war nicht den Hollän-\n der, sondern den Grund und Boden für den Holländer. Noch 1836 wurden\n 12,000 Windmühlen von 6000 Pferdekraft in Holland verwandt, um zwei Dritt-\n theile des Lands vor Rückverwandlung in Morast zu schützen.] ","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0009","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"361\", \"facs\": \"#f0380\"} -->","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0010","text":"Die Maschine, wovon die industrielle Revolution ausgeht, ersetzt den\n Arbeiter, der ein einzelnes Werkzeug handhabt, durch einen Mechanismus,\n der mit einer Masse derselben oder gleichartiger Werkzeuge auf einmal\n operirt, und von einer einzigen Triebkraft, welches immer ihre Form, be-\n wegt wird [Note: „Die Vereinigung aller dieser einfachen Instrumente, durch einen einzigen\n Motor in Bewegung gesetzt, bildet eine Maschine.“ (Babbage l. c.)] . Hier haben wir die Maschine, aber erst als einfaches\n Element der maschinenmässigen Produktion.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0011","text":"Die Erweiterung des Umfangs der Arbeitsmaschine und der Zahl\n ihrer gleichzeitig operirenden Werkzeuge bedingt einen massenhafteren Be-\n wegungsmechanismus, und dieser Mechanismus, zur Ueberwältigung seines\n eignen Widerstands, eine mächtigere Triebkraft als die menschliche, ab-\n gesehn davon, dass der Mensch ein sehr unvollkommenes Produktionsin-\n strument gleichförmiger und kontinuirlicher Bewegung ist. Vorausgesetzt,\n dass er nur noch als einfache Triebkraft wirkt, also an die Stelle seines\n Werkzeugs eine Werkzeugmaschine getreten ist, können Naturkräfte ihn\n jetzt auch als Triebkraft ersetzen. Von allen aus der Manufakturperiode\n überlieferten grossen Bewegungskräften war die Pferdekraft die schlech-\n teste, theils weil ein Pferd seinen eignen Kopf hat, theils wegen seiner\n Kostspieligkeit und des beschränkten Umfangs, worin es in Fabriken allein\n anwendbar ist [Note: John C. Morton verlas Januar 1861 in der Society of Arts einen\n Aufsatz über „die in der Agrikultur angewandten Kräfte“. Es heisst darin u. a.:\n „Jede Verbesserung, welche die Gleichförmigkeit des Grund und Bodens fördert,\n macht die Dampfmaschine zur Erzeugung rein mechanischer Kraft anwendbarer\n … Pferdekraft wird erheischt, wo krumme Hecken und andere Hindernisse\n gleichförmige Aktion verhindern. Diese Hindernisse schwinden täglich mehr. In\n Operationen, die mehr Ausübung des Willens und weniger wirkliche Kraft erfor-\n dern, ist die durch den menschlichen Geist von Minute zu Minute gelenkte Kraft,\n also Menschenkraft, allein anwendbar.“ Herr Morton reducirt dann Dampfkraft,\n Pferdekraft und Menschenkraft auf die bei Dampfmaschinen gewöhnliche Mass-\n einheit, nämlich die Kraft 33,000 Pfund in der Minute um einen Fuss zu heben\n oder zu ziehen, und berechnet die Kost einer Dampfpferdekraft bei der Dampf-\n maschine auf 3 d., und beim Pferde auf 5½ d. per Stunde. Ferner kann das\n Pferd, bei voller Erhaltung seiner Gesundheit, nur 8 Stunden täglich angewandt\n werden. Durch Dampfkraft können mindestens 3 von je 7 Pferden auf bebautem\n Land während des ganzen Jahrs erspart werden, zu einem Kostenpreis, nicht\n grösser als dem der entlassenen Pferde während der 3 oder 4 Monate, wo sie] . Dennoch wurde das Pferd häufig während der Kinder-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"362\", \"facs\": \"#f0381\"} --> zeit der grossen Industrie angewandt, wie, ausser dem Jammer gleichzei-\n tiger Agronomen, schon der bis heute überlieferte Ausdruck der mecha-\n nischen Kraft in Pferdekraft bezeugt. Der Wind war zu unstät und unkon-\n trolirbar, und die Anwendung der Wasserkraft überwog ausserdem in Eng-\n land, dem Geburtsland der grossen Industrie, schon während der Manufak-\n turperiode. Man hatte bereits im 17. Jahrhundert versucht, zwei Läufer\n und also auch zwei Mahlgänge mit einem Wasserrad in Bewegung zu\n setzen. Der geschwollne Umfang des Transmissionsmechanismus gerieth\n aber jetzt in Konflikt mit der nun unzureichenden Wasserkraft und diess\n ist einer der Umstände, der zur genaueren Untersuchung der Friktionsge-\n setze trieb. Ebenso führte das ungleichförmige Wirken der Bewegungs-\n kraft bei Mühlen, die durch Stossen und Ziehen mit Schwengeln in Bewe-\n gung gesetzt wurden, auf die Theorie und Anwendung des Schwung-\n rads [Note: Faulhebr 1625, De Cous 1688.] , das später eine so wichtige Rolle in der grossen Industrie spielt.\n In dieser Art entwickelte die Manufakturperiode die ersten wissenschaft-\n lichen und technologischen Elemente der grossen Industrie. Ark-\n wright’s Throstlespinnerei wurde von vorn herein mit Wasser getrieben.\n Indess war auch der Gebrauch der Wasserkraft als herrschender Trieb-\n kraft mit erschwerenden Umständen verbunden. Sie konnte nicht beliebig\n erhöht und ihrem Mangel nicht abgeholfen werden, sie versagte zuweilen\n und war vor allem rein lokaler Natur [Note: Die moderne Erfindung der Turbinen befreit die industrielle Ausbeu-\n tung der Wasserkraft von vielen früheren Schranken.] . Erst mit Watt’s zweiter,\n s. g. doppelt wirkender Dampfmaschine war ein erster Motor\n gefunden, der seine Bewegungskraft selbst aus der Verspeisung von Koh-\n len und Wasser erzeugt, dessen Kraftpotenz ganz unter menschlicher Kon-\n trole steht, der mobil und ein Mittel der Lokomotion, städtisch, und nicht\n gleich dem Wasserrad ländlich, die Koncentration der Produktion in\n Städten erlaubt, statt sie wie das Wasserrad über das Land zu zer-\n [Note: allein wirklich vernutzt werden. In den Agrikulturoperationen, worin die Dampf-\n kraft angewandt werden kann, verbessert sie endlich, verglichen mit der Pferde-\n kraft, die Qualität des Machwerks. Um das Werk der Dampfmaschine zu ver-\n richten, müssten 66 Arbeiter per Stunde zu 15 sh., und um das der Pferde zu ver-\n richten, 32 Mann zu 8 sh. per Stunde angewandt werden.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"363\", \"facs\": \"#f0382\"} --> streuen [Note: „In the early days of textile manufactures, the locality of the factory de-\n pended upon the existence of a stream having a sufficient fall to turn a water\n wheel; and, although the establishment of the water-mills was the\n commencement of the breaking up of the domestic system of\n manufacture, yet the mills necessarily situated upon streams, and frequently\n at considerable distances the one from the other, formed part of a rural rather\n than an urban system; and it was not until the introduction of the steam-\n power as a substitute for the stream, that factories were congregated in towns and\n localities where the coal and water required for the production of steam were\n found in sufficient quantities. The steam-engine is the parent of manufacturing\n towns.“ (A. Redgrave in „Reports of the Insp. of Fact. 30. April\n 1860“. p. 36.)] , universell in seiner technologischen Anwendung, in seiner Resi-\n denz verhältnissmässig wenig durch lokale Umstände bedingt. Das grosse\n Genie Watt’s zeigte sich in der Specifikation des Patents, das er April\n 1784 nahm, und worin seine Dampfmaschine nicht als eine Erfindung zu\n besondern Zwecken, sondern als allgemeiner Agent der grossen\n Industrie geschildert wird. Er deutet hier Anwendungen an, wovon\n manche, wie z. B. der Dampfhammer, mehr als ein halbes Jahrhundert\n später erst eingeführt wurden. Jedoch bezweifelte er die Anwendbarkeit\n der Dampfmaschine auf Seeschifffahrt. Seine Nachfolger, Boulton und\n Watt, stellten 1851 die kolossalste Dampfmaschine für Ocean steamers auf\n der Londoner Industrieausstellung aus.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0012","text":"Nachdem erst die Werkzeuge aus Werkzeugen des menschlichen Orga-\n nismus in Werkzeuge eines mechanischen Apparats, der Werkzeugmaschine,\n verwandelt, erhielt nun auch die Bewegungsmaschine eine selbstständige, von\n den Schranken menschlicher Kraft völlig emancipirte Form. Damit sinkt\n die einzelne Werkzeugmaschine, die wir bisher betrachtet, zu einem blossen\nElement der maschinenmässigen Produktion herab. Eine Bewegungs-\n maschine konnte jetzt viele Arbeitsmaschinen gleichzeitig treiben. Mit\n der Anzahl der gleichzeitig bewegten Arbeitsmaschinen wächst die Bewe-\n gungsmaschine und dehnt sich der Transmissionsmechanismus zu einem\n weitläufigen Apparat aus.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0013","text":"Es ist nun zweierlei zu unterscheiden, Cooperation vieler\n gleichartiger Maschinen und Maschinensystem.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0014","text":"In dem einen Fall wird das ganze Machwerk von derselben\n Arbeitsmaschine verrichtet. Sie führt alle die verschiedenen Operationen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"364\", \"facs\": \"#f0383\"} --> aus, welche ein Handwerker mit seinem Werkzeng, z. B. der Weber mit\n seinem Webstuhl verrichtete, oder welche Handwerker mit verschiednen\n Werkzeugen, sei es selbstständig oder als Glieder einer Manufaktur, der\n Reihe nach ausführten [Note: Vom Standpunkt der manufakturmässigen Theilung war Weben keine\n einfache, sondern vielmehr eine komplicirte handwerksmässige Arbeit, und so ist\n der mechanische Webstuhl eine Maschine, die sehr Mannigfaltiges verrichtet. Es\n ist überhaupt eine falsche Vorstellung, dass die moderne Maschinerie sich ur-\n sprünglich solcher Operationen bemächtigt, welche die manufakturmässige Thei-\n lung der Arbeit vereinfacht hatte. Spinnen und Weben wurden während der\n Manufakturperiode in neue Arten gesondert und ihre Werkzeuge verbessert und\n variirt, aber der Arbeitsprozess selbst, in keiner Weise getheilt, blieb handwerks-\n mässig. Es ist nicht die Arbeit, sondern das Arbeitsmittel, wovon die Maschine\n ausgeht.] . Z. B. in der modernen Manufaktur von Enve-\n loppes faltete ein Arbeiter das Papier mit dem Falzbein, ein andrer legte\n den Gummi auf, ein dritter schlug die Klappe um, auf welche die Devise\n aufgedrückt wird, ein vierter bossirte die Devise u. s. w. und bei jeder\n dieser Theiloperationen musste jede einzelne Enveloppe die Hände wech-\n seln. Eine einzige Enveloppemaschine verrichtet alle diese Operationen\n auf einen Schlag und macht 3000 und mehr Enveloppen in einer Stunde.\n Eine auf der Londoner Industrieausstellung von 1862 ausgestellte ameri-\n kanische Maschine zur Bereitung von Papiertuten schneidet das Papier,\n kleistert, faltet und vollendet 300 Stück per Minute. Der innerhalb der\n Manufaktur getheilte und in einer Reihenfolge ausgeführte Gesammtprozess\n wird hier von einer Arbeitsmaschine vollbracht, die durch Kombination\n verschiedner Werkzeuge wirkt. Ob nun eine solche Arbeitsmaschine nur\n mechanische Wiedergeburt eines komplicirteren Handwerkszeugs sei, oder\n Kombination verschiedenartiger manufakturmässig partikularisirter ein-\n facher Instrumente, in der Fabrik, der neuen Form der auf Maschi-\n nenbetrieb gegründeten Werkstatt, erscheint jedesmal die ein-\n fache Cooperation wieder, zunächst, wir sehn hier vom Arbeiter\n ab, als räumliche Konglomeration gleichartiger und\n gleichzeitig zusammenwirkender Arbeitsmaschinen.\n So wird eine Webfabrik durch das Nebeneinander vieler mechanischen\n Webstühle und eine Nähfabrik durch das Nebeneinander vieler\n Nähmaschinen in demselben Arbeitsgebäude gebildet. Aber es exi-\n stirt hier eine technologische Einheit, indem die vielen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"365\", \"facs\": \"#f0384\"} --> gleichartigen Arbeitsmaschinen gleichzeitig und gleichmässig ihren Impuls\n empfangen vom Herzschlag des gemeinsamen ersten Motors, auf sie über-\n tragen durch den Transmissionsmechanismus, der ihnen auch theilweis ge-\n meinsam ist, indem sich nur besondere Ausläufe davon für jede einzelne\n Werkzeugmaschine verästeln. Ganz wie viele Werkzenge die Organe\neiner Arbeitsmaschine, bilden viele Arbeitsmaschinen jetzt nur noch viele\ngleichartige Organe desselben Bewegungsmechanismus.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0015","text":"Ein eigentliches Maschinensystem tritt aber erst an die Stelle\n der einzelnen selbstständigen Maschine, wo der Arbeits-\n gegenstand eine zusammenhängende Reihe verschiedner Stufenprozesse,\n ausgeführt von einer Kette verschiedenartiger, aber mit einander\n kombinirter Werkzeugsmaschinen, durchläuft. Hier erscheint die der Ma-\n nufaktur eigenthümliche Cooperation durch Theilung der Arbeit wieder,\n aber jetzt als Kombination von Theilarbeitsmaschinen. Die\n specifischen Werkzeuge der verschiednen Theilarbeiter, in der Wollmanu-\n faktur z. B. von Wollschläger, Wollkämmer, Wollscheerer, Wollspinner\n u. s. w., verwandeln sich jetzt in die Werkzeuge spezificirter Arbeits-\n maschinen, von denen jede ein besondres Organ für eine besondre Funk-\n tion im System des kombinirten Werkzeugsmechanismus bildet. Die\n Manufaktur selbst liefert dem Maschinensystem in den Zweigen, worin es\n zuerst eingeführt wird, im Grossen und Ganzen die naturwüchsige\n Grundlage der Theilung und daher der Organisation des Produktionspro-\n zesses [Note: Vor der Epoche der grossen Industrie war die Wollmanufaktur die herr-\n schende Manufaktur Englands. In ihr wurden daher während der ersten Hälfte\n des 18. Jahrhunderts die meisten Experimente gemacht. Der Baumwolle, deren\n mechanische Verarbeitung minder mühvolle Vorbereitungen erfordert als die\n Schafwolle, kamen die an der letztern gemachten Erfahrungen zu gut, wie später\n umgekehrt die mechanische Wollindustrie sich auf Grundlage der mechanischen\n Baumwollspinnerei und -Weberei entwickelt. Einzelne Elemente der Wollmanu-\n faktur sind erst seit den letzten Decennien dem Fabriksystem einverleibt worden,\n z. B. das Wollkämmen. „The application of power to the process of combing\n wool … extensively in operation since the introduction of the „combing\n machine“, especially Lister’s . . . . undoubtedly had the effect of throwing a very\n large number of men out of work. Wool was formerly combed by hand, most\n frequently in the cottage of the comber. It is now very generally combed in the\n factory, and handlabour is superseded, except in some particular kinds of work,] . Indess tritt sofort ein wesentlicher Unterschied ein. Bei der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"366\", \"facs\": \"#f0385\"} --> Manufaktur muss jeder besondre Theilprozess von Arbeitern, einzeln oder\n in Gruppen, mit ihrem Handwerkszeug ausführbar sein. Wird der Arbei-\n ter dem Prozess angeeignet, so ist aber auch vorher der Prozess dem Arbeiter\n angepasst. Diess subjektive Princip der Theilung fällt weg für die maschi-\n nenartige Produktion. Der Gesammtprozess wird hier objektiv, an und\n für sich betrachtet, in seine konstituirenden Phasen analysirt, und das Pro-\n blem jeden Theilprozess auszuführen und die verschiednen Theilprozesse zu\n verbinden, durch technische Anwendung der Mechanik, Chemie u. s. w.\n gelöst [Note: „Le principe du système automatique est donc … de remplacer la\n division du travail parmi les artisans par l’analyse du procédé dans ses principes\n constituans.“ (Ure l. c. t. I, p. 30.)] , wobei natürlich nach wie vor die theoretische Konception durch\n gehäufte praktische Erfahrung auf grosser Stufenleiter vervollkommnet\n werden muss. Jede Theilmaschine liefert der zunächst folgenden ihr Roh-\n material, und da sie alle gleichzeitig wirken, befindet sich das Produkt\n eben so fortwährend auf den verschiednen Stufen seines Bildungsprozesses,\n wie im Uebergang aus einer Produktionsphase in die andre. Wie in\n der Manufaktur die unmittelbare Cooperation der Theilarbeiter bestimmte\nVerhältnisszahlen zwischen den besondern Arbeitergruppen schafft,\n so in dem gegliederten Maschinensystem die beständige Beschäftigung der\n Theilmaschinen durch einander ein bestimmtes Verhältniss zwischen\n ihrer Anzahl, ihrem Umfang und ihrer Geschwindigkeit. Die kombinirte\n Arbeitsmaschine, jetzt ein gegliedertes System von verschiedenartigen\n einzelnen Arbeitsmaschinen und von Gruppen derselben, ist um so voll-\n kommner, je kontinuirlicher ihr Gesammtprozess, d. h. mit je weniger\n Unterbrechung das Rohmaterial von seiner ersten Phase zu seiner letzten über-\n geht, je mehr also der Mechanismus selbst, statt der Menschenhand, es von\n einer Produktionsphase in die andre fördert. Wenn daher in der Manu-\n faktur die Isolirung der Sonderprozesse ein durch die Theilung der\n Arbeit selbst gegebnes Princip ist, so in der entwickelten Fabrik die\nKontinuität der Sonderprozesse.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0016","text":" [Note: in which hand-combed wool is still preferred. Many of the handcombers found\n employment in the factories, but the produce of the handcomber bears so small a\n proportion to that of the machine, that the employment of a very large number of\n combers has passed away.“ („Rep. of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct.\n 1856“, p. 16.)] ","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0017","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"367\", \"facs\": \"#f0386\"} -->","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0018","text":"Ein System der Maschinerie, beruhe es nun auf blosser Cooperation\ngleichartiger Arbeitsmaschinen, wie in der Weberei, oder auf einer\n Kombination verschiedenartiger, wie in der Spinnerei, bildet an und\n für sich einen grossen Automaten, sobald es von einem sich selbst\n bewegenden ersten Motor getrieben wird. Indess kann das ganze System\n z. B. von der Dampfmaschine getrieben werden, obgleich einzelne Werkzeug-\n maschinen entweder für gewisse Bewegungen noch den Arbeiter brauchen,\n wie die zum Einfahren der Mule nöthige Bewegung vor der Einführung\n der selfacting mule und immer noch bei Feinspinnerei, oder indem be-\n stimmte Theile der Maschine zur Verrichtung ihres Werks gleich einem\n Werkzeug vom Arbeiter gelenkt werden müssen, wie beim Maschinenbau\n vor der Verwandlung des slide rest (ein Drehapparat) in einen selfactor.\n Sobald die Arbeitsmaschine alle zur Bearbeitung des Rohstoffs nöthigen\n Bewegungen ohne menschliche Beihilfe verrichtet und nur noch mensch-\n licher Nachhilfe bedarf, haben wir ein automatisches System der\n Maschinerie, das indess beständiger Ausarbeitung im Detail fähig ist, wie\n z. B. ein Apparat, der die Spinnmaschine von selbst still setzt, sobald ein\n einzelner Faden reisst, und der selfacting stop, der den verbesserten\n Dampfwebstuhl still setzt, sobald der Spule des Weberschiffs der Ein-\n schlagsfaden ausgeht, ganz moderne Erfindungen sind. Als ein Beispiel\n sowohl der Kontinuität der Produktion als der Durchführung des automa-\n tischen Princips kann die moderne Papierfabrik gelten. An der Papier-\n produktion kann überhaupt der Unterschied verschiedner Produktions-\n weisen, auf Basis verschiedner Produktionsmittel, wie der Zusammenhang\n der gesellschaftlichen Produktionsverhältnisse mit dieser Produktionsweise,\n im Einzelnen vortheilhaft studirt werden, da uns die ältere deutsche\n Papiermacherei Muster der handwerksmässigen Produktion, Holland im\n 17. und Frankreich im 18. Jahrhundert Muster der eigentlichen Manufak-\n tur, und das moderne England Muster der automatischen Fabrikation in\n diesem Zweig liefern, ausserdem in China und Indien noch zwei ver-\n schiedne uraltasiatische Formen derselben Industrie existiren.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0019","text":"Als gegliedertes System automatischer Arbeitsmaschinen, die ihre Be-\n wegung durch Transmissionsmaschinerie von einem centralen Automa-\n ten empfangen, besitzt der Maschinenbetrieb seine entwickeltste Gestalt.\n An die Stelle der einzelnen Maschine tritt hier ein mechanisches Ungeheuer,\n dessen Leib ganze Fabrikgebäude füllt, und dessen dämonische Kraft, erst\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"368\", \"facs\": \"#f0387\"} --> versteckt durch die fast feierlich gemessne Bewegung seiner Riesenglieder,\n im fieberhaft tollen Wirbeltanz seiner zahllosen eigentlichen Arbeits-\n organe ausbricht.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0020","text":"Es gab Mules, Dampfmaschinen u. s. w., bevor es Arbeiter gab,\n deren ausschliessliches Geschäft es war, Dampfmaschinen, Mules u. s. w.\n zu machen, ganz wie der Mensch Kleider trug, bevor es Schneider gab.\n Die Erfindungen von Vauconson, Arkwright, Watt u. s. w. waren jedoch nur\n ausführbar, weil jene Erfinder ein von der Manufakturperiode fertig ge-\n liefertes und beträchtliches Quantum geschickter mechanischer Arbeiter\n vorfanden. Ein Theil dieser Arbeiter bestand aus selbstständigen Hand-\n werkern verschiedner Profession, ein andrer Theil war in Manufakturen ver-\n einigt, worin, wie früher erwähnt, die Theilung der Arbeit mit besondrer\n Strenge durchgeführt. Mit der Zunahme der Erfindungen und der wach-\n senden Nachfrage nach den neu erfundnen Maschinen entwickelte sich\n mehr und mehr einerseits die Sonderung der Maschinenfabrikation in man-\n nichfaltige selbstständige Zweige, andrerseits die Theilung der Arbeit im\n Innern der maschinenbauenden Manufakturen. Wir erblicken hier also\n die Manufaktur als unmittelbare technologische Grundlage der gros-\n sen Industrie. Jene producirte die Maschinerie, womit diese, in den\n Produktionssphären, die sie zunächst ergriff, den handwerks- und manu-\n fakturmässigen Betrieb aufhob. Der Maschinenbetrieb erhob sich also\nnaturwüchsig auf einer ihm unangemessnen materiellen\n Grundlage. Auf einem gewissen Entwicklungsgrad musste er diese\n erst fertig vorgefundne und dann in ihrer alten Form weiter ausgearbeitete\n Grundlage selbst revolutioniren und sich eine seiner eignen Produktions-\n weise entsprechende neue Basis schaffen. Wie die einzelne Maschine\n zwergmässig bleibt, so lange sie nur durch Menschen bewegt wird, wie\n das Maschinensystem sich nicht frei entwickeln konnte, bevor an die Stelle\n der vorgefundnen Triebkräfte, Thier, Wind und selbst Wasser, die\n Dampfmaschine trat, ebenso war die grosse Industrie in ihrer ganzen Ent-\n wicklung gelähmt, so lange ihr charakteristisches Produktionsmittel, die\n Maschine selbst, seine Existenz persönlicher Kraft und persönlichem Ge-\n schick verdankte, also abhing von der Muskelentwicklung, der Schärfe des\n Blicks, und der Virtuosität der Hand, womit der Theilarbeiter in der Ma-\n nufaktur und der Handwerker ausserhalb derselben ihr Zwerginstrument\n führten. Abgesehn von der Vertheurung der Maschinen in Folge ihrer\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"369\", \"facs\": \"#f0388\"} --> Ursprungsweise — und dieser Umstand beherrscht das Kapital als be-\n wusstes Motiv — blieb so die Ausdehnung der bereits maschinenmässig\n betriebenen Industrie und das Eindringen der Maschinerie in neue Produk-\n tionszweige rein bedingt durch das Wachsthum einer Arbeiterkategorie, die\n wegen der halbkünstlerischen Natur ihres Geschäfts nur allmälig und nicht\n sprungweis vermehrt werden konnte. Aber auf einer gewissen Entwick-\n lungsstufe gerieth die grosse Industrie auch technologisch in Wider-\n streit mit ihrer handwerks- und manufakturmässigen Unterlage. Die Aus-\n reckung des Umfangs der Bewegungsmaschinen, des Transmissionsmecha-\n nismus und der Werkzeugmaschinen, die grössere Komplikation, Mannich-\n faltigkeit und erforderte Regelmässigkeit ihrer Bestandtheile, im Masse wie\n die Werkzeugmaschine sich von dem handwerksmässigen Model, das ihren\n Bau ursprünglich beherrscht, losriss, und eine freie, nur durch ihre mecha-\n nische Aufgabe bestimmte Gestalt erhielt [Note: Der mechanische Webstuhl in seiner ersten Form besteht hauptsächlich\n aus Holz, der verbesserte, moderne aus Eisen. Wie sehr im Anfang die alte Form\n des Produktionsmittels seine neue Form beherrscht, zeigt u. a. die oberflächlichste\n Vergleichung des modernen Dampfwebstuhls mit dem alten, der modernen Blas-\n instrumente in Eisengiessereien mit der ersten unbehilflichen mechanischen Wieder-\n geburt des gewöhnlichen Blasbalgs, und vielleicht schlagender als alles andre eine\n vor der Erfindung der jetzigen Lokomotiven versuchte Lokomotive, die in der That\n zwei Füsse hatte, welche sie abwechselnd wie ein Pferd aufhob. Erst nach weiterer\n Entwicklung der Mechanik und gehäufter praktischer Erfahrung wird die Form\n gänzlich durch das mechanische Princip bestimmt und daher gänzlich emancipirt\n von der überlieferten Körperform des Werkzeugs, das sich zur Maschine entpuppt.] , die Ausbildung des automa-\n tischen Systems und die stets unvermeidlichere Anwendung von schwer zu\n bewältigendem Material, z. B. Eisen statt Holz — die Lösung aller dieser\n naturwüchsig entspringenden Aufgaben stiess überall auf die persönlichen\n Schranken, die auch das in der Manufaktur kombinirte Arbeiterpersonal nur\n dem Grad, nicht dem Wesen nach durchbricht. Maschinen z. B. wie die\n moderne Druckerpresse, der moderne Dampfwebstuhl und die moderne\n Kardirmaschine konnten nicht von der Manufaktur geliefert werden.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0021","text":"Die Umwälzung der Produktionsweise in einer Sphäre der Industrie\n bedingt ihre Umwälzung in der andern. Es gilt diess zunächst für solche\n Industriezweige, welche, trotz der Isolation durch die gesellschaft-\n liche Theilung der Arbeit, so dass jeder derselben eine selbstständige\n [Running matter: I. 24] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"370\", \"facs\": \"#f0389\"} --> Waare producirt, sich dennoch als Phasen eines Gesammtprozesses ver-\n schlingen. So machte die Maschinenspinnerei Maschinenweberei nöthig\n und beide zusammen die mechanisch-chemische Revolution der Bleicherei,\n Druckerei und Färberei, wie andrerseits die Revolution in der Baumwoll-\n spinnerei die Erfindung des gin zur Trennung der Baumwollfaser vom\n Samen hervorrief, womit erst die Baumwollproduktion auf dem nun erheisch-\n ten grossen Massstab möglich ward [Note: Des Yankee Eli Whitney cottongin war bis zur neusten Zeit im\n wesentlichen weniger verändert worden, als irgend eine andre Maschine des 18.\n Jahrhunderts. Erst in den letzten Decennien hat ein andrer Amerikaner, Herr\n Emery von Albany, New-York, Whitney’s Maschine durch eine ebenso einfache als\n wirksame Verbesserung antiquirt.] . Die Revolution in der Pro-\n duktionsweise der Industrie und Agrikultur ernöthigte namentlich aber\n auch eine Revolution in den allgemeinen Bedingungen des gesell-\n schaftlichen Produktionsprozesses, d. h. den Kommunikations- und\n Transportmitteln. Wie die Kommunikations- und Transportmittel\n einer Gesellschaft, deren Pivôt, um mich eines Ausdrucks Fourier’s zu\n bedienen, die kleine Agrikultur mit ihrer häuslichen Nebenindustrie und das\n städtische Handwerk waren, den Produktionsbedürfnissen der Manufaktur-\n periode mit ihrer erweiterten Theilung der gesellschaftlichen Arbeit, ihrer\n Koncentration von Arbeitsmitteln und Arbeitern, und ihren Kolonialmärk-\n ten durchaus nicht genügen konnten, daher auch in der That umgewälzt\n wurden, so verwandelten sich die von der Manufakturperiode überlieferten\n Transport- und Kommunikationsmittel bald in unerträgliche Hemmschuhe\n für die grosse Industrie mit ihrer fieberhaften Geschwindigkeit der Produk-\n tion, ihrer massenhaften Stufenleiter, ihrem beständigen Werfen von Ka-\n pital- und Arbeitermassen aus einer Produktionssphäre in die andre,\n und ihren neugeschaffenen weltmarktlichen Zusammenhängen. Neben\n einem ganz umgewälzten Segelschiffbau, wurde das Kommunikations- und\n Transportwesen daher allmälig durch ein System von Flussdampfschiffen,\n Eisenbahnen, oceanischen Dampfschiffen und Telegraphen der Produktions-\n weise der grossen Industrie angeeignet. Die furchtbaren Eisenmassen\n aber, die jetzt zu schmieden, zu schweissen, zu schneiden, zu bohren und\n zu formen waren, erforderten ihrerseits cyklopische Maschinen, deren\n Schöpfung der manufakturmässige Maschinenbau versagte.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0022","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"371\", \"facs\": \"#f0390\"} -->","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0023","text":"Die grosse Industrie musste sich also ihres charakteristischen Produk-\n tionsmittels, der Maschine selbst bemächtigen, um Maschinen durch\n Maschinen zu produciren. So erst schuf sie sich eine adäquate\n technologische Unterlage und stellte sich auf ihre eignen Füsse. Mit dem\n wachsenden Maschinenbetrieb in den ersten Decennien des 19. Jahrhunderts\nbemächtigte sich die Maschinerie in der That allmälig der Fabri-\n kation der Werkzeugmaschinen, während erst in den letztverflosse-\n nen Decennien ungeheurer Eisenbahnbau und oceanische Dampfschifffahrt\n die in der Konstruktion von ersten Motoren angewandten cyklo-\n pischen Maschinen ins Leben riefen.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0024","text":"Die wesentlichste Produktionsbedingung für die Fabrikation von\n Maschinen durch Maschinen war eine jeder Kraftpotenz fähige und doch\n zugleich ganz kontrolirbare Bewegungsmaschine. Sie existirte bereits\n in der Dampfmaschine. Aber es galt zugleich die für die einzelnen Ma-\n schinentheile nöthigen streng geometrischen Formen wie Linie, Ebene, Kreis,\n Cylinder, Kegel und Kugel maschinenmässig zu produciren. Diess Pro-\n blem löste Henry Maudsley im ersten Decennium des 19. Jahrhun-\n derts durch die Erfindung des slide-rest, der bald automatisch ge-\n macht und in modificirter Form von der Drechselbank, wofür er zuerst\n bestimmt war, auf andre Konstruktionsmaschinen übertragen wurde.\n Diese mechanische Vorrichtung ersetzt nicht irgend ein besondres Werk-\n zeug, sondern die menschliche Hand selbst, die eine bestimmte Form\n hervorbringt durch Vorhalten, Anpassen und Richtung der Schärfe von\n Schneideinstrumenten u. s. w. gegen oder über das Arbeitsmaterial, z. B.\n Eisen. So gelang es die geometrischen Formen der einzelnen Maschinen-\n theile „mit einem Grad von Leichtigkeit, Genauigkeit und Raschheit zu\n produciren, den keine gehäufte Erfahrung der Hand des geschicktesten\n Arbeiters verleihen konnte“ [Note: „The Industry of Nations. Lond. 1855,“ Part II, p. 239.\n Es heisst eben daselbst: „Simple and outwardly unimportant as this appendage\n to lathes may appear, it is not, we believe, averring too much to state, that its in-\n fluence in improving and extending the use of machinery has been as great as that\n produced by Watt’s improvements of the steam-engine itself. Its introduction went] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0025","text":"Betrachten wir nun in der zum Maschinenbau angewandten Ma-\n schinerie den Theil derselben, der die eigentliche Werkzeugmaschine\n [Running matter: 24*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"372\", \"facs\": \"#f0391\"} --> bildet, so erscheint das handwerksmässige Instrument wieder, aber in\n cyklopischem Umfang. Der Operateur der Bohrmaschine z. B. ist\n ein ungeheurer Bohrer, der durch eine Dampfmaschine getrieben wird und\n ohne den umgekehrt die Cylinder grosser Dampfmaschinen und hydrau-\n lischer Pressen nicht producirt werden könnten. Die mechanische Drechsel-\n bank ist die cyklopische Wiedergeburt der gewöhnlichen Fussdrechselbank,\n die Hobelmaschine ein eiserner Zimmermann, der mit denselben Werkzeugen\n in Eisen arbeitet, womit der Zimmermann in Holz; das Werkzeug, welches\n in den Londoner Schiffswerften das Furnirwerk schneidet, ist ein riesen-\n artiges Rasirmesser, das Werkzeug der Scheermaschine, welche Eisen\n schneidet, wie die Schneiderscheere Tuch, eine Monsterscheere, und der\n Dampfhammer operirt mit einem gewöhnlichen Hammerkopf, aber von\n solchem Gewicht, dass Thor selbst ihn nicht schwingen könnte [Note: Eine dieser Maschinen in London zum Schmieden von paddle-wheel shafts\n führt den Namen „Thor“. Sie schmiedet einen Schaft von 16½ Tonnen Gewicht\n mit derselben Leichtigkeit, wie der Schmied ein Hufeisen.] . Einer\n dieser Dampfhämmer z. B., die eine Erfindung von Nasmyth sind, wiegt\n über 6 Tonnen und stürzt mit einem perpendikulären Fall von 7 Fuss auf\n einen Amboss von 36 Tonnen Gewicht. Er pulverisirt spielend einen\n Granitblock, und ist nicht minder fähig einen Nagel in weiches Holz mit\n einer Aufeinanderfolge leiser Schläge einzutreiben [Note: Die in Holz arbeitenden Maschinen, die auch auf kleinem Massstab an-\n gewandt werden können, sind meist amerikanische Erfindung.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0026","text":"Das Arbeitsmittel erhält in der Maschinerie eine materielle Existenz-\n weise, welche Ersetzung der Menschenkraft durch Naturkräfte und erfah-\n rungsmässiger Routine durch bewusste Anwendung der Naturwissenschaft\n bedingt. In der Manufaktur ist die Gliederung des gesellschaftlichen Ar-\n beitsprozesses rein subjektiv, Kombination von Theilarbeitern;\n im Maschinensystem schafft die grosse Industrie einen ganz objektiven\n Produktionsorganismus, den der Arbeiter als fertige materielle Produktions-\n bedingung vorfindet. In der einfachen Cooperation und selbst in der\n durch Theilung der Arbeit spezificirten erscheint die Verdrängung des ver-\n einzelten Arbeiters durch den vergesellschafteten immer noch\n mehr oder minder zufällig. Die Maschinerie, mit einigen später zu er-\n [Note: at once to perfect all machinery, to cheapen it, and to stimulate invention and\n improvement.“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"373\", \"facs\": \"#f0392\"} --> wähnenden Ausnahmen, funktionirt nur in der Hand unmittelbar vergesell-\n schafteter oder gemeinsamer Arbeit. Der cooperative Charakter\n des Arbeitsprozesses wird jetzt also durch die Natur des Arbeitsmit-\n tels selbst diktirte technologische Nothwendigkeit.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0027","text":"Man sah, dass die aus der Cooperation und der Theilung der Arbeit\n entspringenden Produktivkräfte dem Kapital nichts kosten. Sie sind Na-\n turkräfte der gesellschaftlichen Arbeit. Naturkräfte, wie\n Dampf, Wasser u. s. w., die zu produktiven Prozessen angeeignet werden,\n kosten ebenfalls nichts. Wie aber der Mensch eine Lunge zum Athmen\n braucht, braucht er ein „Gebild von Menschenhand,“ um Naturkräfte produk-\n tiv zu konsumiren. Ein Wasserrad ist nöthig, um die Bewegungskraft des\n Wassers, eine Dampfmaschine, um die Elasticität des Dampfs auszubeuten.\n Wie mit den Naturkräften, verhält es sich mit der Wissenschaft. Einmal\n entdeckt, kostet das Gesetz über die Abweichung der Magnetnadel im\n Wirkungskreise eines elektrischen Stroms oder über Erzeugung von Magne-\n tismus im Eisen, um das ein elektrischer Strom kreist, keinen Deut [Note: Die Wissenschaft kostet dem Kapitalisten überhaupt „Nichts“, was ihn\n durchaus nicht hindert, sie zu exploitiren. Die „fremde“ Wissenschaft wird dem\n Kapital einverleibt, wie die fremde Arbeit. „Kapitalistische“ Aneignung und\n „persönliche“ Aneignung, sei es von Wissenschaft, sei es von materiellem Reich-\n thum, sind aber ganz und gar disparate Dinge. Dr. Ure selbst bejammert die\n grobe Unbekanntschaft seiner lieben, Maschinenexploitirenden Fabrikanten mit\n der Mechanik und Liebig weiss von der haarsträubenden Unwissenheit der eng-\n lischen chemischen Fabrikanten in der Chemie zu erzählen.] .\n Aber zur Ausbeutung dieser Gesetze für Telegraphie u. s. w. bedarf es\n eines sehr kostspieligen und weitläufigen Apparats. Durch die Maschine\n wird, wie wir sahen, das Werkzeug nicht verdrängt. Aus einem\n Zwerg-Werkzeug des menschlichen Organismus reckt es sich in Um-\n fang und Zahl zum Werkzeug eines vom Menschen geschaffenen Mechanis-\n mus. Statt mit dem Handwerkszeug, lässt das Kapital den Arbeiter jetzt\n mit einer Maschine arbeiten, die ihre Werkzeuge selbst führt. Wenn es\n daher auf den ersten Blick klar ist, dass die grosse Industrie durch Einver-\n leibung ungeheurer Naturkräfte und der Naturwissenschaft in den Produk-\n tionsprozess die Produktivität der Arbeit ausserordentlich steigern muss, ist\n es keineswegs eben so klar, dass diese gesteigerte Produktivkraft nicht durch\n vermehrte Arbeitsausgabe auf der andern Seite erkauft wird. Gleich jedem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"374\", \"facs\": \"#f0393\"} --> andern Bestandtheil des constanten Kapitals, schafft die Maschinerie\n keinen Werth, giebt aber ihren eignen Werth an das Produkt ab, zu des-\n sen Erzeugung sie dient. Soweit sie Werth hat und daher Werth auf das\n Produkt überträgt, bildet sie einen Werthbestandtheil desselben. Statt\n es zu verwohlfeilern, vertheuert sie es im Verhältniss zu ihrem\n eignen Werth. Und es ist handgreiflich, dass Maschine und systematisch\n entwickelte Maschinerie, das charakteristische Arbeitsmittel der grossen\n Industrie, unverhältnissmässig an Werth schwillt, verglichen mit den Ar-\n beitsmitteln des Handwerks- und Manufakturbetriebs.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0028","text":"Es ist nun zunächst zu bemerken, dass die Maschinerie stets\n ganz in den Arbeitsprozess und immer nur theilweis in den Ver-\n werthungsprozess eingeht. Sie setzt dem Produkt nie mehr Werth\n zu als sie im Durchschnitt durch ihre tägliche Abnutzung verliert. Es\n findet also grosse Differenz statt zwischen dem Maschinenwerth und dem\n Werththeil der Maschine, der im täglichen Produkt derselben wiederer-\n scheint. Es findet eine grosse Differenz statt zwischen der Maschine\n als werthbildendem und als produktbildendem Element. Je\n grösser die Periode, während welcher dieselbe Maschinerie wiederholt in\n demselben Arbeitsprozess dient, desto grösser jene Differenz. Allerdings\n haben wir gesehn, dass jedes eigentliche Arbeitsmittel oder Produk-\n tionsinstrument immer ganz in den Arbeitsprozess und stets nur stückweis,\n im Verhältniss zu seinem täglichen Durchschnittsverschleiss, in den Verwer-\n thungsprozess eingeht. Diese Differenz jedoch zwischen der Benutzung\n und der Abnutzung des Arbeitsmittels, zwischen dem Dienst, den es in der\n Waarenproduktion leistet, und der Vertheurung der Waare, die es durch\n Uebertragen seines Werths auf dieselbe hervorbringt, ist viel grösser bei\n der Maschinerie als bei dem Werkzeug, weil sie, aus dauerhafterem Ma-\n terial gebaut, eine längere Lebensperiode besitzt, weil ihre Anwendung im\n Arbeitsprozess, durch streng wissenschaftliche Gesetze geregelt, grössere\n Oekonomie in der Verausgabung ihrer Bestandtheile und ihrer Konsum-\n tionsmittel ermöglicht, und endlich, weil ihr Produktionsfeld unverhält-\n nissmässig grösser ist als das des Werkzeugs. Ziehn wir von beiden,\n von Maschinerie und Werkzeug, ihre tägliche Durchschnittskost ab, oder\n den Werthbestandtheil, den sie durch täglichen Durchschnittsverschleiss\n und den Konsum von Hilfsstoffen, wie Oel, Kohlen u. s. w. dem Produkt\n zusetzen, so wirken sie umsonst, ganz wie ohne Zuthun menschlicher\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"375\", \"facs\": \"#f0394\"} --> Arbeit vorhandne Naturkräfte. Um so grösser der produktive Wirkungs-\n umfang der Maschinerie als der des Werkzeugs, um so grösser ist der Um-\n fang ihres unentgeldlichen Dienstes verglichen mit dem des Werk-\n zeugs. Erst in der grossen Industrie lernt der Mensch das Produkt seiner\n vergangnen, bereits vergegenständlichten Arbeit auf grossem Massstab\n gleich einer Naturkraft umsonst wirken zu lassen [Note: Ricardo fasst diese, übrigens von ihm ebensowenig wie der allgemeine\n Unterschied zwischen Arbeitsprozess und Verwerthungsprozess entwickelte Wir-\n kung der Maschinen manchmal so vorzugsweise ins Auge, dass er gelegentlich den\n Werthbestandtheil vergisst, den Maschinen an das Produkt abgeben, und sie ganz\n und gar mit den Naturkräften zusammenwirft. So z. B. „Adam Smith nowhere\n undervalues the services which the natural agents and machinery perform for us,\n but he very justly distinguishes the nature of the value which they add to commo-\n dities .... as they perform their work gratuitously, the assistance which\n they afford us, adds nothing to value in exchange.“ (Ric. l. c. p. 336, 337.)\n Ricardo’s Bemerkung ist natürlich richtig gegen J. B. Say, der sich vorfaselt,\n die Maschinen leisteten den „Dienst“ Werthzuschaffen, der einen Theil des\n Profits der Kapitalisten bilde.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0029","text":"Es ergab sich bei Betrachtung der Cooperation und Manufaktur, dass\n gewisse allgemeine Produktionsbedingungen, wie Baulichkeiten u. s. w.,\n im Vergleich mit den zersplitterten Produktionsbedingungen vereinzelter Ar-\n beiter durch den gemeinsamen Konsum ökonomisirt werden, daher das\n Produkt weniger vertheuern. Bei der Maschinerie wird nicht nur der\n Körper einer Arbeitsmaschine von ihren vielen Werkzeugen, sondern die-\n selbe Bewegungsmaschine nebst einem Theil des Transmissionsmechanis-\n mus von vielen Arbeitsmaschinen gemeinsam verbraucht.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0030","text":"Gegeben die Differenz zwischen dem Werth der Maschinerie und dem\n von ihr auf das Tagesprodukt übertragenen Werththeil, hängt der Grad,\n worin dieser Werththeil das Produkt vertheuert, zunächst vom Umfang\n des Produkts ab, gleichsam von seiner Oberfläche. Herr Baynes\n aus Blackburn schätzt in einer 1858 veröffentlichten Vorlesung, dass\n „jede reale mechanische Pferdekraft 450 selfacting Mulespindeln nebst\n Vorgeschirr treibt oder 200 Throstlespindeln oder 15 Webstühle für 40\n inch cloth nebst den Vorrichtungen zum Aufziehn der Kette, Schlichten\n u. s. w.“ Es ist im ersten Fall das Tagesprodukt von 450 Mulespin-\n deln, im zweiten von 200 Throstlespindeln, im dritten von 15 mecha-\n nischen Webstühlen, worüber sich die täglichen Kosten einer Dampf-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"376\", \"facs\": \"#f0395\"} --> pferdekraft und der Verschleiss der von ihr in Bewegung gesetzten Ma-\n schinerie vertheilen, so dass hierdurch auf eine Unze Garn oder eine Elle\n Geweb nur ein winziger Werththeil übertragen wird. Ebenso im obigen\n Beispiel mit dem Dampfhammer. Da sich sein täglicher Verschleiss,\n Kohlenkonsum u. s. w. vertheilen auf die furchtbaren Eisenmassen, die er\n täglich hämmert, hängt sich jedem Centner Eisen nur ein geringer Werth-\n theil an, der sehr gross wäre, sollte das cyklopische Instrument kleine\n Nägel eintreiben.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0031","text":"Den Wirkungskreis der Arbeitsmaschine, also die Anzahl ihrer\n Werkzeuge, oder, wo es sich um Kraft handelt, deren Umfang gegeben,\n wird die Produktenmasse von der Geschwindigkeit abhängen, womit\n sie operirt, also z. B. von der Geschwindigkeit, womit sich die Spindel\n dreht, oder der Anzahl Schläge, die der Hammer in einer Minute austheilt.\n Manche jener kolossalen Hämmer geben 70 Schläge, Ryder’s Schmiede-\n patentmaschine, die Dampfhämmer in kleineren Dimensionen zum Schmie-\n den von Spindeln anwendet, 700 Schläge in einer Minute.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0032","text":"Die Proportion gegeben, worin die Maschinerie Werth auf das Pro-\n dukt überträgt, hängt die Grösse dieses Werththeils von ihrer eignen\n Werthgrösse ab [Note: Der in kapitalistischen Vorstellungen befangene Leser vermisst hier natür-\n lich den „Zins“, den die Maschine, pro rata ihres Kapitalwerthes, dem Produkt\n zusetzt. Es ist jedoch leicht einzusehn, dass die Maschine, da sie so wenig als\n irgend ein andrer Bestandtheil des constanten Kapitals Neuwerth erzeugt,\n keinen solchen unter dem Namen „Zins“ zusetzen kann. Es ist ferner klar, dass\n hier, wo es sich um die Produktion des Mehrwerths handelt, kein Theil\n desselben unter dem Namen „Zins“ a priori vorausgesetzt werden kann. Die\n kapitalistische Rechnungsweise, die prima facie abgeschmackt und den Ge-\n setzen der Werthbildung widersprechend scheint, findet im dritten Buch dieser\n Schrift ihre Erklärung.] . Je weniger Arbeit sie selbst enthält, desto\n weniger Werth setzt sie dem Produkt zu. Je weniger Werth bildend,\n desto produktiver ist sie und desto mehr nähert sich ihr Dienst dem der\n Naturkräfte. Die Produktion der Maschinerie durch Ma-\n schinerie verringert aber ihren Werth, verhältnissmässig zu ihrer\n Ausdehnung und Wirkung.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0033","text":"Eine vergleichende Analyse der Preise handwerks- oder manufaktur-\n mässig producirter Waaren und der Preise derselben Waaren als Maschi-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"377\", \"facs\": \"#f0396\"} --> nenprodukt ergiebt im Allgemeinen das Resultat, dass beim Maschinenpro-\n dukt der dem Arbeitsmittel geschuldete Werthbestandtheil rela-\n tiv wächst, aber absolut abnimmt. Das heisst, seine absolute\n Grösse nimmt ab, aber seine Grösse im Verhältniss zum Gesammtwerth\n des Produkts, z. B. eines Pfundes Garn, nimmt zu [Note: Dieser von der Maschine zugesetzte Werthbestandtheil fällt absolut und\n relativ, wo sie Pferde verdrängt, überhaupt Arbeitsthiere, die nur als Bewegungs-\n kraft, nicht als Stoffwechselmaschinen benutzt werden. Nebenbei bemerkt, Des-\n cartes mit seiner Definition der Thiere als blosser Maschinen sieht mit den Augen\n der Manufakturperiode im Unterschied zum Mittelalter, dem das Thier als Ge-\n hilfe des Menschen galt, wie später wieder dem Herrn v. Haller in seiner\n „Restauration der Staatswissenschaften“. Dass Descartes ebenso wie Baco eine\n veränderte Gestalt der Produktion und praktische Beherrschung der Natur durch\n den Menschen als Resultat der veränderten Denkmethode betrachtete, zeigt sein\n „Discours dela Méthode“, wo es u. a. heisst: „il est possible (durch die\n von ihm in die Philosophie eingeführte Methode) de parvenir à des connaissances\n fort utiles à la vie, et qu’au lieu de cette philosophie spéculative qu’on enseigne\n dans les écoles, on en peut trouver une pratique, par laquelle, connaissant la force\n et les actions du feu, de l’eau, de l’air, des astres, et de tous les autres corps qui\n nous environnent, aussi distinctement que nous connaissons les divers métiers de nos\n artisans, nous les pourrions employer en même façon à tous les usages auxquels ils\n sont propres, et ainsi nous rendre comme maîtres et possesseurs de la\n nature“, und so „contribuer au perfectionnement de la vie humaine.“\n In der Vorrede zu Sir Dudley North’s „Discourses upon Trade“\n (1691) heisst es, die Methode des Descartes, auf die politische Oekonomie ange-\n wandt, habe sie von alten Mährchen und abergläubigen Vorstellungen über Geld,\n Handel u. s. w. zu befreien angefangen. Im Durchschnitt schliessen sich jedoch\n die englischen Oekonomen der früheren Zeit an Baco und Hobbes als ihre Philo-\n sophen an, während Locke später der „Philosoph“ der politischen Oekonomie\nϰατ᾽ ἐξοχὴν für England, Frankreich und Italien ward.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0034","text":"Es ist klar, dass blosses Deplacement der Arbeit stattfindet, also die\n Gesammtsumme der zur Produktion einer Waare erheischten Arbeit nicht\n vermindert oder die Produktivkraft der Arbeit nicht vermehrt wird, wenn\n die Produktion einer Maschine so viel Arbeit kostet als ihre Anwendung\n erspart. Die Differenz jedoch zwischen der Arbeit, die sie kostet, und\n der Arbeit, die sie erspart, oder der Grad ihrer Produktivität hängt offen-\n bar nicht ab von der Differenz zwischen ihrem eigenen Werth und dem\n Werth des von ihr ersetzten Werkzeugs. Die Differenz dauert so lange\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"378\", \"facs\": \"#f0397\"} --> als die Arbeitskost der Maschine und daher der von ihr dem Produkt zu-\n gesetzte Werththeil kleiner bleiben als der Werth, den der Arbeiter mit\n seinem Werkzeug dem Arbeitsgegenstand zusetzen würde. Die Produk-\n tivität der Maschine misst sich daher an dem Grad, worin sie\n menschliche Arbeitskraft ersetzt. Nach Herrn Baynes kom-\n men auf 450 Mulespindeln nebst Vormaschinerie, die von einer Dampf-\n pferdekraft getrieben werden, 2½ Arbeiter [Note: Nach einem Jahresbericht der Handelskammer zu Essen (October 1863)\n produzirte 1862 die Krupp’sche Gussstahlfabrik mittelst 161 Schmelz-, Glüh- und\n Cementöfen, 32 Dampfmaschinen (im Jahr 1800 war das ungefähr die Gesammt-\n zahl der in Manchester angewandten Dampfmaschinen) und 14 Dampfhäm-\n mern, welche zusammen 1236 Pferdekraft repräsentiren, 49 Schmiedeessen, 203\n Werkzeugmaschinen und circa 2400 Arbeitern 13 Millionen Pfund Gussstahl. Hier\n noch nicht 2 Arbeiter auf 1 Pferdekraft.]  und werden mit jeder self-\n acting mule spindle bei zehnstündigem Arbeitstag 13 Unzen Garn (Durch-\n schnittsnumer), also wöchentlich 365⅝ lbs. Garn von 2½ Arbeitern\n gesponnen. Bei ihrer Verwandlung in Garn absorbiren ungefähr 366\n Pfund Baumwolle (wir sehn der Vereinfachung halber vom Abfall ab) also\n nur 150 Arbeitsstunden oder 15 zehnstündige Arbeitstage, während mit\n dem Spinnrad, wenn der Handspinner 13 Unzen Garn in 60 Stunden lie-\n fert, dasselbe Quantum Baumwolle 2700 Arbeitstage von 10 Stunden oder\n 27,000 Arbeitsstunden absorbiren würde [Note: Babbage berechnet, dass in Java im Spinnprozess 117 % der Baum-\n wolle auf ihren ursprünglichen Werth zugesetzt wird, ein Werthzusatz, der fast nur\n aus der Spinnarbeit entspringt. Zur selben Zeit (1832) betrug in England der Ge-\n sammtwerth, den Maschinerie und Arbeit der Baumwolle bei der Feinspinnerei zu-\n setzten, ungefähr 33 % auf den Werth des Rohmaterials. („On the Economy\n of Machinery“, p. 214.)] . Wo die alte Methode des\n blockprinting oder der Handkattundruckerei durch Maschinendruck ver-\n drängt ist, druckt eine einzige Maschine mit dem Beistand eines Mannes\n oder Jungen so viel vierfarbigen Kattun in einer Stunde als früher 200\n Männer [Note: Beim Maschinendruck ausserdem Farbe erspart.] . Bevor Eli Whitney 1793 den cottongin erfand, kostete die\n Trennung eines Pfundes Baumwolle vom Samen einen Durchschnittsar-\n beitstag. In Folge seiner Erfindung konnten täglich 100 lbs. Baumwolle\n von einer Negerin gewonnen werden und die Wirksamkeit des gin ward\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"379\", \"facs\": \"#f0398\"} --> seit seiner ersten Einführung noch bedeutend erhöht. Die Baumwollfaser,\n früher zu 50 cents per Pfund producirt, wird später mit grösserem Profit,\n d. h. mit Einschluss von mehr unbezahlter Arbeit, zu 10 cents verkauft.\n In Indien wird zur Trennung der Faser vom Samen ein halbmaschinen-\n artiges Instrument, die Churka, angewandt, womit ein Mann und eine Frau\n 28 lbs. täglich reinigen können. Mit der von Dr. Forbes vor einigen\n Jahren erfundenen Churka produciren 1 Mann und 1 Junge täglich 250 lbs.;\n wo Ochsen, Dampf oder Wasser als Triebkräfte gebraucht werden, sind\n nur wenige Jungen und Mädchen als feeders (Handlanger des Materials\n für die Maschine) erheischt. Sechszehn dieser Maschinen, mit Ochsen\n getrieben, verrichten täglich das frühere Durchschnitts-Tagewerk von 750\n Leuten [Note: Vgl. Paper read by Dr. Watson, Reporter on Products to\n the Government of India, before the Society of Arts, 17. April\n 1860.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0035","text":"Wie bereits erwähnt, verrichtet die Dampfmaschine, beim Dampf-\n pflug z. B., in einer Stunde zu 3 d. oder ¼ sh. so viel Werk als 66 Men-\n schen zu 15 sh. per Stunde. Ich komme auf diess Beispiel zurück gegen\n eine falsche Vorstellung. Die 15 sh. sind nämlich keineswegs der\n Ausdruck der während einer Stunde von den 66 Menschen zuge-\n fügten Arbeit. War das Verhältniss von Mehrarbeit zu nothwendiger\n Arbeit 100 %, so producirten diese 66 Arbeiter per Stunde einen Werth\n von 30 sh., obgleich sich nur 33 Stunden in einem Aequivalent für sie\n selbst, d. h. im Arbeitslohn von 15 sh. darstellen. Gesetzt also, eine Ma-\n schine koste eben so viel als z. B. der Jahreslohn von 150 durch sie ver-\n drängten Arbeitern, sage 3000 Pfd. St., so sind 3000 Pfd. St. keineswegs\n der Geldausdruck der Gesammtarbeit, welche die 150 Arbeiter lieferten und\n dem Arbeitsgegenstand zusetzten, sondern nur des Theils ihrer Jah-\n resarbeit, der sich in einem Aequivalent für sie selbst, vulgo Arbeits-\n lohn, darstellte. Ist dagegen der Werth der Maschine 3000 Pfd. St.,\n so sind diese 3000 Pfd. St. der Geldausdruck aller zu ihrer Produktion\n verausgabten Arbeit, in welchem Verhältniss immer diese Arbeit Arbeits-\n lohn für den Arbeiter und Mehrwerth  [Editorial alternatives: sic = fur; corr = für]  den Kapitalisten bilde. Kostet die\n Maschine also ebensoviel als die von ihr ersetzte Arbeitskraft, so ist die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"380\", \"facs\": \"#f0399\"} --> in ihr selbst vergegenständlichte Arbeit stets viel kleiner als die von ihr\n ersetzte lebendige Arbeit [Note: „The mute agents (die Maschinen) are always the produce of much less\n labour than that which they displace, even when they are of the same money\n value.“ (Ricardo l. c. p. 40.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0036","text":"Ausschliesslich als Mittel zur Verwohlfeilerung des Produkts\n betrachtet, ist die Grenze für den Gebrauch der Maschinerie darin gegeben,\n dass ihre eigne Produktion weniger Arbeit kostet als ihre Anwendung Ar-\n beit ersetzt. Für das Kapital jedoch drückt sich diese Grenze enger\n aus. Da es nicht die angewandte Arbeit zahlt, sondern den Werth\n der angewandten Arbeitskraft, wird ihm der Maschinengebrauch begrenzt\n durch die Differenz zwischen dem Maschinenwerth und dem\n Werth der von ihr ersetzten Arbeitskraft. Da die Theilung\n des Arbeitstags in nothwendige Arbeit und Mehrarbeit, oder, populär aus-\n gedrückt, in bezahlte Arbeit und unbezahlte Arbeit, sowohl in verschie-\n denen Ländern als in demselben Lande zu verschiedenen Perioden oder in\n verschiedenen Geschäftszweigen während derselben Periode sehr verschie-\n den sein kann, da ferner der wirkliche Lohn des Arbeiters bald unter den\n Werth seiner Arbeitskraft sinkt, bald über ihn steigt, kann die Diffe-\n renz zwischen dem Preise der Maschinerie und dem\n Preise der von ihr zu ersetzenden Arbeitskraft sehr\nvariiren, wenn auch die Differenz zwischen dem zur Pro-\n duktion der Maschine nöthigen Arbeitsquantum und\n dem Gesammtquantum der von ihr ersetzten Arbeit die-\n selbe bleibt. Es ist aber nur die erste Differenz, welche die Kost\n der Waare für den Kapitalisten selbst bestimmt und ihn durch die\n Zwangsgesetze der Konkurrenz beeinflusst. Es werden daher heute Ma-\n schinen in England erfunden, die nur in Nordamerika angewandt werden,\n wie Deutschland im 16. und 17. Jahrhundert Maschinen erfand, die nur\n Holland anwandte, und wie manche französische Erfindung des 18. Jahr-\n hunderts nur in England ausgebeutet ward. Die Maschine selbst produ-\n cirt in älter entwickelten Ländern durch ihre Anwendung auf einige Ge-\n schäftszweige in anderen Zweigen solchen Arbeitsüberfluss (redundancy\n of labour, sagt Ricardo), dass hier der Fall des Arbeitslohns unter den\n Werth der Arbeitskraft den Gebrauch der Maschinerie verhindert und\n ihn vom Standpunkt des Kapitals, dessen Gewinn ohnehin aus der Ver-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"381\", \"facs\": \"#f0400\"} --> minderung nicht der angewandten, sondern der bezahlten Arbeit\n entspringt, überflüssig, oft unmöglich macht. In einigen Zweigen der\n englischen Wollmanufaktur ist während der letzten Jahre die Kinder-\n arbeit sehr vermindert, hier und da fast verdrängt worden. Warum? Der\n Fabrikakt ernöthigte eine doppelte Kinderreihe, von denen je eine 6,\n die andere 4 Stunden, oder jede nur 5 Stunden arbeitet. Die Aeltern\n wollten aber die half-times (Halbzeitler) nicht wohlfeiler verkaufen als früher\n die full-times (Vollzeitler). Daher Ersetzung der half-times durch\n Maschinerie [Note: „Employers of labour would not unnecessarily retain two sets of children\n under thirteen … In fact one class of manufacturers, the spinners of woollen\n yarn, now rarely employ children under thirteen years of ages, i. e. half-times.\n They have introduced improved and new machinery of various kinds, which alto-\n gether supersedes the employment of children (d. h. unter 13 J.); f. i.: I will\n mention one process as an illustration of this diminution in the number of children,\n wherein, by the addition of an apparatus, called a piecing machine, to existing\n machines, the work of six or four half-times, according to the peculiarity of each\n machine, can be performed by one young person (über 13 J.) … the half-time\n system“ stimulirte „the invention of the piecing machine.“ (Reports of Insp.\n of Fact. for 31. Oct. 1858.)] . Vor dem Verbot der Arbeit von Weibern und Kindern\n (unter 10 Jahren) in Minen, fand das Kapital die Methode, nackte Weiber\n und Mädchen, oft mit Männern zusammengebunden, in Kohlen- und an-\n dern Minen zu vernutzen, so übereinstimmend mit seinem Moralkodex und\n namentlich auch seinem Hauptbuch, dass erst nach dem Verbot Maschinerie\n sie für einige Funktionen ersetzte. Die Yankees haben Maschinen zum\n Steinklopfen erfunden. Die Engländer wenden sie nicht an, weil der\n „Elende“ („wretch“ ist Kunstausdruck der englischen politischen\n Oekonomie für den Agrikulturarbeiter), der diese Arbeit verrichtet, einen\n so geringen Theil seiner Arbeit bezahlt erhält, dass Maschinerie die Pro-\n duktion für den Kapitalisten vertheuern würde [Note: „Machinery … can frequently not be employed until labour (er\n meint wages) rises.“ (Ricardo l. c. p. 479.)] . In England\n werden gelegentlich statt der Pferde immer noch Weiber zum Ziehn\n u. s. w. bei den Kanalbauten verwandt [Note: Sieh: „Report of the Social Science Congress at Edin-\n burgh. October 1863.“] , weil die zur Produktion\n von Pferden und Maschinen erheischte Arbeit ein mathematisch ge-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"382\", \"facs\": \"#f0401\"} --> gebnes Quantum, die zur Erhaltung von Weibern der Surpluspopulation\n dagegen unter aller Berechnung steht. Man findet daher nirgendwo scham-\n losere Verschwendung von Menschenkraft für Lumpereien als grade in\n England, dem Land der Maschinen.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0037","text":"Den Ausgangspunkt der grossen Industrie bildet, wie gezeigt, die\n Revolution des Arbeitsmittels, und das umgewälzte Arbeitsmittel erhält\n seine meist entwickelte Gestalt im gegliederten Maschinensystem der Fabrik.\n Bevor wir zusehn, wie diesem objektiven Organismus Menschenmaterial\n einverleibt wird, betrachten wir einige allgemeine Rückwirkungen jener\n Revolution auf den Arbeiter selbst.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0038","text":"Sofern die Maschinerie Muskelkraft entbehrlich macht, wird sie zum\nMittel Arbeiter ohne Muskelkraft oder von unreifer Körperent-\n wicklung, aber grösserer Geschmeidigkeit der Glieder anzuwenden.\nWeiber- und Kinderarbeit war daher das erste Wort der kapi-\n talistischen Anwendung der Maschinerie! Diess gewaltige Ersatz-\n mittel von Arbeit und Arbeitern verwandelte sich damit sofort in ein Mittel\ndie Zahl der Lohnarbeiter zu vermehren durch Einrollirung\n aller Mitglieder der Arbeiterfamilie, ohne Unterschied von Geschlecht und\n Alter, unter die unmittelbare Botmässigkeit des Kapitals. Die Zwangs-\n arbeit für den Kapitalisten usurpirte nicht nur die Stelle des Kinderspiels,\n sondern auch der freien Arbeit im häuslichen Kreis, innerhalb sittlicher\n Schranke, für die Familie selbst [Note: Dr. Edward Smith wurde während der den amerikanischen Bürger-\n krieg begleitenden Baumwollkrise von der englischen Regierung nach Lancashire,\n Cheshire u. s. w. geschickt, zur Berichterstattung über den Gesundheitszustand der\n Baumwollarbeiter. Er berichtet u. a.: Hygienisch habe die Krise, abgesehn von\n der Verbannung der Arbeiter aus der Fabrikatmosphäre, vielerlei andre Vortheile.\n Die Arbeiterfrauen fänden jetzt die nöthige Musse, ihren Kindern die Brust zu\n reichen, statt sie mit Godfrey’s Cordial zu vergiften. Sie hätten die Zeit ge-\n wonnen, kochen zu lernen. Unglücklicher Weise fiel diese Kochkunst in einen\n Augenblick, wo sie nichts zu essen hatten. Aber man sieht, wie das Kapital die für\n die Konsumtion nöthige Familienarbeit usurpirt hat zu seiner Selbstverwerthung.\n Ebenso wurde die Krise benutzt, um in eignen Schulen die Töchter der Arbeiter\nnähen zu lehren. Eine amerikanische Revolution und eine Weltkrise erheischt,\n damit die Arbeitermädchen, die für die ganze Welt spinnen, nähen lernen!] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0039","text":"Der Werth der Arbeitskraft war bestimmt nicht nur durch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"383\", \"facs\": \"#f0402\"} --> die zur Erhaltung des individuellen erwachsnen Arbeiters, sondern durch\n die zur Erhaltung der Arbeitsfamilie nöthige Arbeitszeit. Indem die\n Maschinerie alle Glieder der Arbeiterfamilie auf den Arbeitsmarkt wirft,\n vertheilt sie den Werth der Arbeitskraft des Mannes über seine ganze Fa-\n milie. Sie entwerthet daher seine Arbeitskraft. Der Ankauf der in\n 4 Arbeitskräfte z. B. parcellirten Familie kostet vielleicht mehr als früher\n der Ankauf der Arbeitskraft des Familienhaupts, aber dafür treten 4 Ar-\n beitstage an die Stelle von Einem, und ihr Preis fällt im Verhältniss zum\n Ueberschuss der Mehrarbeit der Vier über die Mehrarbeit des Einen. Vier\n müssen nun nicht nur Arbeit, sondern Mehrarbeit für das Kapital liefern,\n damit eine Familie lebe. So erweitert die Maschinerie von vorn herein\n mit dem menschlichen Exploitationsmaterial, dem eigensten\n Ausbeutungsfeld des Kapitals [Note: „The numerical increase of labourers has been great, through the grow-\n ing substitution of female for male, and above all of childish for adult, labour.\n Three girls of 13, at wages from of 6 sh. to 8 sh. a week, have replaced the one\n man of mature age, of wages varying from 18 sh. to 45 sh.“ (Th. de Quin-\n cey: „The Logic of Politic. Econ. Lond. 1845“, Note zu p. 147.)\n Da gewisse Funktionen der Familie, z. B. Warten und Säugen der Kinder u. s. w.,\n nicht ganz unterdrückt werden können, müssen die vom Kapital konfiscirten Fa-\n milienmütter mehr oder minder Stellvertreter dingen. Die Arbeiten, welche der\n Familienkonsum erheischt, wie Nähen, Flicken u. s. w., müssen durch Kauf fer-\n tiger Waaren ersetzt werden. Der verminderten Ausgabe von häuslicher Arbeit ent-\n spricht also vermehrte Geldausgabe. Die Produktionskosten der Arbeiterfamilie\n wachsen daher und balanciren die Mehreinnahme. Es kommt hinzu, dass Oeko-\n nomie und Zweckmässigkeit in Vernutzung und Bereitung der Lebensmittel\n unmöglich werden. Ueber diese von der officiellen politischen Oekonomie ver-\n heimlichten Thatsachen findet man reichliches Material in den „Reports“ der\n Fabrikinspektoren, der „Children’s Employment Commission“ und\n namentlich auch des „Board of Health“.] , zugleich den Exploitationsgrad.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0040","text":"Sie revolutionirt eben so von Grund aus die formelle Vermittlung des\n Kapitalverhältnisses, den Kontrakt zwischen Arbeiter und Kapitalist.\n Auf Grundlage des Waarenaustausches war es erste Voraussetzung, dass\n sich Kapitalist und Arbeiter als freie Personen, als unabhängige Waa-\n renbesitzer, der eine Besitzer von Geld und Produktionsmitteln, der andre\n Besitzer von Arbeitskraft, gegenübertraten. Aber jetzt kauft das Kapital\n Unmündige oder Halbmündige. Der Arbeiter verkaufte früher seine eigne\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"384\", \"facs\": \"#f0403\"} --> Arbeitskraft, worüber er als formell freie Person verfügte. Er verkauft\n jetzt Weib und Kind. Er wird Sklavenhändler [Note: Im Kontrast zur grossen Thatsache, dass die Beschränkung der Weiber-\n und Kinderarbeit in den englischen Fabriken von den erwachsnen männlichen Ar-\n beitern dem Kapital aberobert wurde, findet man noch in den jüngsten Berichten\n der „Children’s Employment Commission“ wahrhaft empörende und\n durchaus sklavenhändlerische Züge der Arbeitereltern mit Bezug auf den Kinder-\n schacher. Der kapitalistische Pharisäer aber, wie man aus denselben „Reports“\n sehn kann, denuncirt diese von ihm selbst geschaffene, verewigte und exploitirte\n Bestialität, die er sonst „Freiheit der Arbeit“ tauft. „Infant labour has been\n called into aid … even to work for their own daily bread. Withoutstrength to endure\n such disproportionate toil, without instruction to guide their future life, they have\n been thrown into a situation physically and morally polluted. The Jewish historian\n has remarked upon the overthrow of Jerusalem by Titus, that is was no wonder\n it should have been destroyed, with such a signal destruction, when an inhuman\n mother sacrificed her own offspring to satisfy the cravings of absolute hunger.“\n „Public Economy Concentrated. Carlisle 1833“, p. 56.] . Die Nachfrage\n nach Kinderarbeit gleicht oft auch in der Form der Nachfrage nach Neger-\n sklaven, wie man sie in amerikanischen Zeitungsinseraten zu lesen gewohnt\n war. „Meine Aufmerksamkeit“, sagt z. B. ein englischer Fabrikinspektor,\n „wurde gelenkt auf eine Annonce in dem Lokalblatt einer der bedeutend-\n sten Manufakturstädte meines Distrikts, wovon Folgendes die Kopie: Ge-\n braucht 12 bis 20 Jungen, nicht jünger als was für 13\n Jahre passiren kann. Lohn 4 sh. per Woche. Anzufragen etc.“ [Note: A. Redgrave in „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31. Oct.\n 1858“, p. 40, 41.] \n Der unterstrichne Passus bezieht sich darauf, dass nach dem Factory Act\nKinder unter 13 Jahren nur 6 Stunden arbeiten dürfen. Ein amt-\n lich qualificirter Arzt (certifying surgeon) muss das Alter bescheinigen.\n Der Fabrikant verlangt also Jungen, die so aussehn, als ob sie schon\n dreizehnjährig. Die manchmal sprungweise Abnahme in der Anzahl der\n von Fabrikanten beschäftigten Kinder unter 13 Jahren, überraschend in\n der englischen Statistik der letzten 20 Jahre, war, nach Aussage der\n Fabrikinspektoren selbst, grossentheils das Werk von certifying sur-\n geons, welche das Kindesalter der Exploitationslust der Kapitalisten und\n dem Schacherbedürfniss der Eltern gemäss verschoben. In dem berüch-\n tigten Londoner Distrikt von Bethnal Green wird jeden Montag und Dien-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"385\", \"facs\": \"#f0404\"} --> stag Morgen offner Markt gehalten, worin Kinder beiderlei Geschlechts\n vom 9. Jahre an sich selbst an die Londoner Seidenmanufakturen\n vermiethen. „Die gewöhnlichen Bedingungen sind 1 sh. 8 d. die Woche\n (die den Eltern gehören) und 2 d. für mich selbst nebst Thee.“ Die\n Kontrakte gelten nur für die Woche. Die Scenen und die Sprache wäh-\n rend der Dauer dieses Markts sind wahrhaft empörend [Note: „Children’s Employment Commission. V. Report. Lon-\n don 1866“, p. 81, n. 31.] . Es kömmt\n immer noch in England vor, dass Weiber „Jungen vom Workhouse neh-\n men und sie jedem beliebigen Käufer für 2 sh. 6 d. wöchentlich ver-\n miethen“ [Note: „Child. Employm. Comm. III. Report. Lond. 1864“, p. 53,\n n. 15.] . Trotz der Gesetzgebung werden immer noch mindestens\n 2000 Jungen in Grossbritannien als lebendige Schornsteinfegmaschinen (ob-\n gleich Maschinen zu ihrem Ersatz existiren) von ihren eigenen Eltern ver-\n kauft [Note: l. c. V. Report, p. XXII, n. 137.] . Die von der Maschinerie bewirkte Revolution im Rechts-\n verhältniss zwischen Käufer und Verkäufer der Arbeitskraft, so dass die\n ganze Transaktion selbst den Schein eines Kontrakts zwischen freien\n Personen verliert, bot dem englischen Parlament später den juristischen\nEntschuldigungsgrund für Staatseinmischung in das Fabrikwesen.\n So oft das Fabrikgesetz die Kinderarbeit in bisher unangefochtnen Indu-\n striezweigen auf 6 Stunden beschränkt, ertönt stets neu der Fabrikanten-\n jammer: ein Theil der Eltern entziehe die Kinder nun der gemassregelten\n Industrie, um sie in solche zu verkaufen, wo noch „Freiheit der Ar-\n beit“ herrscht, d. h. wo Kinder unter 13 Jahren gezwungen wer-\n den wie Erwachsene zu arbeiten, also auch theurer loszuschlagen sind.\n Da aber das Kapital von Natur ein leveller ist, d. h. in allen Produk-\n tionssphären Gleichheit der Exploitationsbedingungen der Arbeit als\n sein angebornes Menschenrecht verlangt, wird die legale Beschränkung der\n Kinderarbeit in einem Industriezweig Ursache ihrer Beschränkung in dem\n andern.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0041","text":"Bereits früher wurde der physische Verderb der Kinder und\n jungen Personen angedeutet, wie der Arbeiterweiber, welche die Maschi-\n nerie erst direkt, in den auf ihrer Grundlage aufschiessenden Fabriken,\n [Running matter: I. 25] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"386\", \"facs\": \"#f0405\"} --> und dann indirekt in allen übrigen Industriezweigen der Ex-\n ploitation des Kapitals unterwirft. Hier verweilen wir daher nur bei einem\n Punkt, der ungeheuren Sterblichkeit von Arbeiterkindern\n in ihren ersten Lebensjahren. In England giebt es 16 Regi-\n strations-Distrikte, wo im jährlichen Durchschnitt auf 100,000 lebende\n Kinder unter einem Jahr nur 9000 Todesfälle (in einem Distrikt nur\n 7,047) kommen, in 24 Distrikten über 10,000, aber unter 11,000, in\n 39 Distrikten über 11,000, aber unter 12,000, in 48 Distrikten über\n 12,000, aber unter 13,000, in 22 Distrikten über 20,000, in 25 Distrik-\n ten über 21,000, in 17 über 22,000, in 11 über 23,000, in Hoo, Wol-\n verhampton, Ashton-under-Lyne und Preston über 24,000, in Notting-\n ham, Stockport und Bradford über 25,000, in Wisbeach 26,000, und in\n Manchester 26,125 [Note: „Sixth Report“ des „Board of Health. Lond. 1864“, p. 34.] . Wie eine officielle ärztliche Untersuchung im\n Jahre 1861 nachwies, sind, von Lokalumständen abgesehn, die hohen\n Sterblichkeitsraten vorzugsweise der ausserhäuslichen Beschäf-\n tigung der Mütter geschuldet und der daher entspringenden Vernach-\n lässigung und Misshandlung der Kinder, u. a. unpassender Nahrung, Mangel\n an Nahrung, Fütterung mit Opiaten u. s. w., dazu die unnatürliche Entfrem-\n dung der Mütter gegen ihre Kinder, im Gefolge davon absichtliche Aushunge-\n rung und Vergiftung [Note: „It (the inquiry of 1861) . . . . showed, moreover, that while, with the\n described circumstances, infants perish under the neglect and mismanagement\n which their mothers’ occupations imply, the mothers become to a grievous extent\n denaturalized towards their offspring — commonly not troubling themselves much\n at the death, and even sometimes … taking direct measures to ensure it.“ (l. c.)] . In solchen Agrikulturdistrikten, „wo ein Minimum\n weiblicher Beschäftigung existirt, ist dagegen die Sterblichkeitsrate am\n niedrigsten“ [Note: l. c. p. 454.] . Die Untersuchungskommission von 1861 ergab jedoch\n das unerwartete Resultat, dass in einigen an der Nordsee gelegnen rein\n ackerbauenden Distrikten die Sterblichkeitsrate von Kindern unter einem\n Jahr fast die der verrufensten Fabrikdistrikte erreicht. Dr. Julian\n Hunter wurde daher vom „Board of Health“ beauftragt, diess Phäno-\n men an Ort und Stelle zu erforschen. Sein Bericht ist dem „VI. Report\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"387\", \"facs\": \"#f0406\"} --> of the Board of Health“ einverleibt [Note: l. c. p. 454—463. „Report by Dr. Henry Julian Hunter on\nthe excessive mortality of infants in some rural districts of\n England.“] . Man hatte bisher vermuthet,\n Malaria und andre niedrig gelegnen und sumpfigen Landstrichen eigenthüm-\n liche Krankheiten decimirten die Kinder. Die Untersuchung ergab das\n grade Gegentheil, nämlich, „dass dieselbe Ursache, welche die Ma-\n laria vertrieb, nämlich die Verwandlung des Bodens aus Morast im Winter\n und dürftiger Weide im Sommer in fruchtbares Kornland, die ausserordent-\n liche Todesrate der Säuglinge schuf“ [Note: l. c. p. 35 u. p. 455, 456.] . Die 70 ärztlichen Praktiker,\n die Dr. Hunter in jenen Distrikten verhörte, waren „wunderbar einstim-\n mig“ über diesen Punkt. Mit der Revolution der Bodenkultur wurde nämlich\ndas industrielle System eingeführt. „Verheirathete Weiber, die\n in Banden mit Mädchen und Jungen zusammenarbeiten, werden dem\n Pächter von einem Manne, welcher der „Gangmeister“ heisst und die\n Banden im Ganzen miethet, für eine bestimmte Summe zur Verfügung ge-\n stellt. Diese Banden wandern oft viele Meilen von ihren Dörfern weg,\n man trifft sie Morgens und Abends auf den Landstrassen, die Weiber be-\n kleidet mit kurzen Unterröcken und entsprechenden Röcken und Stiefeln\n und manchmal Hosen, sehr kräftig und gesund von Aussehn, aber verdor-\n ben durch gewohnheitsmässige Liederlichkeit und rücksichtslos gegen die\n unheilvollen Folgen, welche ihre Vorliebe für diese thätige und unab-\n hängige Lebensart auf ihre Sprösslinge wälzt, die zu Haus verküm-\n mern“ [Note: l. c. p. 456.] . Alle Phänomene der Fabrikdistrikte reproduciren sich hier,\n in noch höherem Grad versteckter Kindermord und Behandlung der Kin-\n der mit Opiaten [Note: Wie in den englischen Fabrikdistrikten, so dehnt sich auch in den Agri-\n kulturdistrikten der Opiumkonsum unter den erwachsnen Arbeitern und\n Arbeiterinnen täglich aus. „To push the sale of opiate … is the great aim of\n some enterprising wholesale merchants. By druggists it is considered the lead-\n ingarticle.“ (l. c. p. 459.) Säuglinge, die Opiate empfingen, „verrumpelten\n in kleine alte Männchen oder verschrumpften zu kleinen Affen.“ (l. c. p. 460.)\n Man sieht, wie Indien und China sich an England rächen.] . „Meine Kenntniss der von ihr erzeugten Uebel“,\n sagt Dr. Simon, der ärztliche Beamte des englischen Privy Council\n [Running matter: 25*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"388\", \"facs\": \"#f0407\"} --> und Redacteur en chef der Berichte des „Board of Health“, „muss\n den tiefen Abscheu entschuldigen, womit ich jede umfassende indu-\n strielle Beschäftigung erwachsner Weiber betrachte“ [Note: l. c. p. 37.] . „Es wird“,\n ruft Fabrikinspektor R. Baker in einem officiellen Bericht aus, „es wird\n in der That ein Glück für die Manufakturdistrikte Englands sein, wenn\n jeder verheiratheten Frau, die Familie hat, verboten wird in irgend\n einer Fabrik zu arbeiten“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 59. Dieser\n Fabrikinspektor war früher Arzt.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0042","text":"Die aus der kapitalistischen Exploitation der Weiber- und Kinder-\n arbeit entspringende moralische Verkümmerung ist von F. En-\n gels in seiner „Lage der arbeitenden Klassen Englands“\n und von andern Schriftstellern so erschöpfend dargestellt worden, dass ich\n hier nur daran erinnere. Die intellektuelle Verödung aber, künstlich\n producirt durch die Verwandlung unreifer Menschen in blosse Maschinen\n zum Fabrikat von Mehrwerth, und sehr zu unterscheiden von jener natur-\n wüchsigen Unwissenheit, welche den Geist in Brache legt ohne Verderb\n seiner Entwicklungsfähigkeit, seiner natürlichen Frucht-\n barkeit selbst, zwang endlich sogar das englische Parlament in allen\n dem Fabrikgesetz unterworfenen Industrien den Elementarunterricht zur\ngesetzlichen Bedingung für den „produktiven“ Verbrauch von Kin-\n dern unter 14 Jahren zu machen. Der Geist der kapitalistischen Pro-\n duktion leuchtet hell aus der liederlichen Redaktion der s. g. Erzie-\n hungsklauseln der Fabrikakte, aus dem Mangel administrativer Ma-\n schinerie, wodurch dieser Zwangsunterricht grossentheils wieder illusorisch\n wird, aus der Fabrikantenopposition selbst gegen diess Unterrichtsgesetz\n und ihren praktischen Kniffen und Schlichen zu seiner Umgehung. „Die\n Gesetzgebung allein ist zu tadeln, weil sie ein Truggesetz (delusive law)\n erlassen hat, das unter dem Schein für die Erziehung der Kinder zu sor-\n gen, keine einzige Bestimmung enthält, wodurch dieser vorgeschützte\n Zweck gesichert werden kann. Es bestimmt nichts, ausser dass die Kinder\n für eine bestimmte Stundenzahl (3 Stunden) per Tag innerhalb der vier\n Wände eines Platzes, Schule benamst, eingeschlossen werden sollen, und dass\n der Anwender des Kindes hierüber wöchentlich ein Certifikat von einer Person\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"389\", \"facs\": \"#f0408\"} --> erhalten muss, die sich als Schullehrer oder Schullehrerin mit ihrem\n Namen unterzeichnet“ [Note: Leonhard Horner in „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 30th\n June 1857“, p. 17.] . Vor dem Erlass des amendirten Fabrikakts von\n 1844 waren Schulbesuchscertifikate nicht selten, die von Schulmeister oder\n Schulmeisterin mit einem Kreuz unterzeichnet wurden, da letztre selbst nicht\n schreiben konnten. „Beim Besuch, den ich einer solchen Certifikate aus-\n stellenden Schule abstattete, war ich so betroffen von der Unwissenheit\n des Schulmeisters, dass ich zu ihm sagte: ‘Bitte, mein Herr, können Sie\n lesen?‘ Seine Antwort war: ‘Ih jeh, Ebbes (summat)‘. Zu seiner Recht-\n fertigung fügte er hinzu: ‘Jedenfalls stehe ich vor meinen Schülern‘. Wäh-\n rend der Vorbereitung des Akts von 1844 denuncirten die Fabrikinspektoren\n den schmählichen Zustand der Plätze, Schulen benamst, deren Certifikate\n sie als zu Gesetz vollgültig zulassen mussten. Alles was sie durchsetzten,\n war, dass seit 1844 die Zahlen im Schulcertifikat in der Handschrift des\n Schulmeisters ausgefüllt, ditto sein Vor- und Zuname von ihm selbst unter-\n schrieben sein müssen“ [Note: id. in „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1855“,\n p. 18, 19.] . Sir John Kincaid, Fabrikinspektor\n für Schottland, erzählt von ähnlichen amtlichen Erfahrungen. „Die erste\n Schule, die wir besuchten, wurde von einer Mrs. Ann Killin gehalten.\n Auf meine Aufforderung, ihren Namen zu buchstabiren, machte sie gleich\n einen Schnitzer, indem sie mit dem Buchstaben C begann, aber sich sofort\n korrigirend sagte, ihr Name fange mit K an. Bei Ansicht ihrer Unter-\n schrift in den Schulcertifikatbüchern bemerkte ich jedoch, dass sie ihn ver-\n schiedenartig buchstabirte, während die Handschrift keinen Zweifel über\n ihre Lehrunfähigkeit liess. Auch gab sie selbst zu, sie könne das Regi-\n ster nicht führen … In einer zweiten Schule fand ich das Schulzimmer\n 15 Fuss lang und 10 Fuss breit und zählte in diesem Raum 75 Kinder,\n die etwas unverständliches herquiekten“ [Note: Sir John Kincaid in „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st\n Oct. 1858“, p. 31, 32.] . „Es sind jedoch nicht nur\n solche Jammerhöhlen, worin die Kinder Schulcertifikate, aber keinen\n Unterricht erhalten, denn in vielen Schulen, wo der Lehrer kompetent ist,\n scheitern seine Bemühungen fast ganz an dem sinnverwirrenden Knäuel\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"390\", \"facs\": \"#f0409\"} --> von Kindern aller Alter, aufwärts von Dreijährigen. Sein Auskommen,\n elend im besten Fall, hängt ganz von der Zahl der Pence ab, empfangen\n von der grössten Anzahl Kinder, die es möglich ist in ein Zimmer zu\n stopfen. Dazu kommt spärliche Schulmöblirung, Mangel an Büchern und\n andrem Lehrmaterial, und die niederschlagende Wirkung einer benauten\n und ekelhaften Luft auf die armen Kinder selbst. Ich war in vielen sol-\n chen Schulen, wo ich ganze Reihen Kinder sah, die absolut nichts thaten;\n und diess wird als Schulbesuch bescheinigt, und solche Kinder figuriren in\n der officiellen Statistik als erzogen (educated)“ [Note: Leonhard Horner in „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1856“,\n p. 17, 18.] . In Schottland suchen\n die Fabrikanten dem Schulbesuch unterworfne Kinder möglichst auszu-\n schliessen. „Diess genügt, um die grosse Missgunst der Fabri-\n kanten gegen die Erziehungsklauseln zu beweisen“ [Note: id. l. c. p. 66.] .\n Grotesk-entsetzlich erscheint diess in den Kattun- u. s. w. Drucke-\n reien, die durch ein eignes Fabrikgesetz geregelt sind. Nach den Be-\n stimmungen des Gesetzes „muss jedes Kind, bevor es in einer solchen\n Druckerei beschäftigt wird, Schule besucht haben für mindestens 30 Tage\n und nicht weniger als 150 Stunden während der 6 Monate, die dem ersten\n Tag seiner Beschäftigung unmittelbar vorhergehn. Während der Fort-\n dauer seiner Beschäftigung in der Druckerei muss es Schule besuchen\n ebenfalls für eine Periode von 30 Tagen und 150 Stunden während jeder\n Wechselperiode von 6 Monaten … Der Schulbesuch muss zwischen\n 8 Uhr Morgens und 6 Uhr Nachmittags stattfinden. Kein Besuch von\n weniger als 2½ oder mehr als 5 Stunden an demselben Tag soll als Theil\n der 150 Stunden gezählt werden. Unter gewöhnlichen Umständen besu-\n chen die Kinder die Schule Vormittags und Nachmittags für 30 Tage,\n 5 Stunden per Tag, und nach Ablauf der 30 Tage, wenn die statuten-\n mässige Gesammtsumme von 150 Stunden erreicht ist, wenn sie, in ihrer\n eignen Sprache zu reden, ihr Buch abgemacht haben, kehren sie zur\n Druckerei zurück, wo sie wieder 6 Monate bleiben, bis ein andrer Ab-\n schlagstermin des Schulbesuchs fällig wird, und dann bleiben sie wieder\n in der Schule, bis das Buch wieder abgemacht ist . . . . Sehr viele Jungen,\n welche die Schule während der vorschriftsmässigen 150 Stunden besucht,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"391\", \"facs\": \"#f0410\"} --> sind bei ihrer Rückkehr aus dem sechsmonatlichen Aufenthalt in der\n Druckerei grade so weit wie im Anfang … Sie haben natürlich alles wie-\n der verloren, was sie durch den früheren Schulbesuch gewonnen hatten.\n In andern Kattundruckereien wird der Schulbesuch ganz und gar abhängig\n gemacht von den Geschäftsbedürfnissen der Fabrik. Die erforderliche\n Stundenzahl wird vollgemacht während jeder sechsmonatlichen Periode durch\n Abschlagszahlungen von 3 bis 5 Stunden auf einmal, die vielleicht über 6 Mo-\n nate zerstreut sind. Z. B. an einem Tage wird die Schule besucht von\n 8 bis 11 Uhr Morgens, an einem andern Tag von 1 bis 4 Uhr Nachmit-\n tags, und nachdem das Kind dann wieder für eine Reihe Tage weggeblie-\n ben, kömmt es plötzlich wieder von 3 bis 6 Uhr Nachmittags; dann er-\n scheint es vielleicht für 3 oder 4 Tage hinter einander, oder für eine Woche,\n verschwindet dann wieder für 3 Wochen oder einen ganzen Monat und\n kehrt zurück an einigen Abfallstagen für einige Sparstunden, wenn seine\n Anwender seiner zufällig nicht bedürfen; und so wird das Kind so zu sa-\n gen hin und her gepufft (buffeted) von der Schule in die Fabrik, von der\n Fabrik in die Schule, bis die Summe der 150 Stunden abgezählt ist“ [Note: A. Redgrave in „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 30th June\n 1857“, p. 41, 42. In den englischen Industriezweigen, wo der eigentliche Fabrik-\n akt (nicht der zuletzt im Text angeführte Print Works’ Act) seit längerer Zeit\n herrscht, sind die Hindernisse gegen die Erziehungsklauseln in den letzten Jahren\n einigermassen überwältigt worden. In den nicht dem Fabrikgesetz unterworfenen\n Industrien herrschen noch sehr die Ansichten des Glasfabrikanten J. Geddes, der\n den Untersuchungskommissär White dahin belehrt: „So viel ich sehn kann, ist das\n grössere Quantum Erziehung, welches ein Theil der Arbeiterklasse seit den letzten\n Jahren genoss, vom Uebel. Es ist gefährlich, indem es sie zu unabhängig macht.“\n („Children’s Empl. Commission. IV. Report. London 1865“,\n p. 253.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0043","text":"Durch den überwiegenden Zusatz von Kindern und Weibern zum kom-\n binirten Arbeitspersonal bricht die Maschinerie endlich den Widerstand,\n den der männliche Arbeiter in der Manufaktur der Despotie des Kapitals\n noch entgegensetzte [Note: „Herr E., ein Fabrikant, unterrichtete mich, dass er ausschliesslich\n Weiber bei seinen mechanischen Webstühlen beschäftigt; er gebe verheirathe-\n ten Weibern den Vorzug, besonders solchen mit Familie zu Hause, die von ihnen\n für den Unterhalt abhängen; sie sind viel aufmerksamer und gelehriger als unver-] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0044","text":"Wenn die Maschinerie das gewaltigste Mittel ist, die Produktivität der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"392\", \"facs\": \"#f0411\"} --> Arbeit zu steigern, d. h. die zur Produktion einer Waare nöthige Arbeits-\n zeit zu verkürzen, wird sie als Träger des Kapitals, zunächst\n in den unmittelbar von ihr ergriffenen Industrien, zum gewaltigsten Mittel\n den Arbeitstag über jede naturgemässe Schranke hinaus zu verlän-\n gern. Sie schafft einerseits neue Bedingungen, welche das\n Kapital befähigen, dieser seiner beständigen Tendenz die Zügel frei\n schiessen zu lassen, andrerseits neue Motive zur Wetzung seines Heiss-\n hungers nach fremder Arbeit.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0045","text":"Zunächst verselbstständigt sich in der Maschinerie die Bewegung und\n Werkthätigkeit des Arbeitsmittels gegenüber dem Arbeiter. Es\n wird an und für sich ein industrielles Perpetuum mobile, das ununterbrochen\n fortproduciren würde, stiesse es nicht auf gewisse Naturschranken in seinen\n menschlichen Gehilfen, ihre Körperschwäche und ihren Eigenwillen. Als\nKapital, und als solches besitzt der Automat im Kapitalisten Bewusst-\n sein und Willen, ist es daher mit dem Trieb begeistet, die widerstrebende,\n aber elastische menschliche Naturschranke auf den Minimalwiderstand ein-\n zuzwängen [Note: „Since the general introduction of expensive machinery, human nature\n has been forced far beyond its average strength.“ (Robert Owen: „Ob-\n servations on the effects of the manufacturing system. 2nd ed.\n Lond. 1817.“)] . Dieser ist ohnehin vermindert durch die scheinbare Leich-\n tigkeit der Arbeit an der Maschine und das füg- und biegsamere Weiber-\n und Kinderelement [Note: Die Engländer, die gern die erste empirische Erscheinungsform einer\n Sache als ihren Grund betrachten, geben oft den grossen herodischen Kinderraub,\n den das Kapital in den Anfängen des Fabriksystems an den Armen- und Waisen-\n häusern verübte und wodurch es sich ein ganz willenloses Menschenmaterial\n einverleibte, als Grund der langen Arbeitszeit in den Fabriken an. So z. B.\nFielden, selbst englischer Fabrikant: „It is evident that the long hours of work\n were brought about by the circumstance of so great a number of destitute children\n being supplied from different parts of the country, that the masters were indepen-] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0046","text":"Die Produktivität der Maschinerie steht, wie wir sahen, in umgekehr-\n [Note: heirathete, und zur äussersten Anstrengung ihrer Kräfte gezwungen, um die noth-\n wendigen Lebensmittel beizuschaffen. So werden die Tugenden, die eigenthüm-\n lichen Tugenden des weiblichen Charakters zu seinem Schaden verkehrt, — so\n wird alles Sittliche und Zarte ihrer Natur zum Mittel ihrer Sklaverei und ihres\n Leidens gemacht.“ („Ten Hours’ Factory Bill. The Speech of Lord\n Ashley. Lond. 1844“, p. 20.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"393\", \"facs\": \"#f0412\"} --> tem Verhältniss zur Grösse des von ihr auf das Machwerk übertragenen\n Werthbestandtheils. Je länger die Periode, worin sie funktionirt, desto\n grösser die Produktenmasse, worüber sich der von ihr zugesetzte Werth\n vertheilt, und desto kleiner der Werththeil, den sie der einzelnen Waare zu-\n fügt. Die aktive Lebensperiode der Maschinerie ist aber offenbar bestimmt\n durch die Länge des Arbeitstags oder die Dauer des täglichen Ar-\n beitsprozesses, multiplicirt mit der Anzahl Tage, worin er sich wie-\n derholt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0047","text":"Der Maschinenverschleiss entspricht keineswegs exakt mathematisch\n ihrer Benutzungszeit. Und selbst diess vorausgesetzt, umfasst eine Ma-\n schine, die während 7½ Jahren täglich 16 Stunden dient, eine ebenso\n grosse Produktionsperiode und setzt dem Gesammtprodukt nicht mehr\n Werth zu als dieselbe Maschine, die während 15 Jahren nur 8 Stunden\n täglich dient. Im erstern Fall aber wäre der Maschinenwerth doppelt so\n rasch reproducirt als im letztern und der Kapitalist hätte vermittelst der-\n selben in 7½ Jahren so viel Mehrarbeit eingeschluckt als sonst in 15.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0048","text":"Der materielle Verschleiss der Maschine ist doppelt. Der eine ent-\n springt aus ihrem Gebrauch, wie Geldstücke durch Cirkulation verschleis-\n sen, der andre aus ihrem Nichtgebrauch, wie ein unthätig Schwert in der\n Scheide verrostet. Es ist diess ihr Verzehr durch die Elemente. Der\n Verschleiss erster Art steht mehr oder minder in direktem Verhältniss,\n der letztere, zu gewissem Grad, in umgekehrtem Verhältniss zu ihrem\n Gebrauch [Note: „Le domage que cause l’inaction des machines à des pièces de metal\n mobiles et délicates.“ (Ure l. c. t. II, p. 8.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0049","text":"Neben dem materiellen unterliegt die Maschine aber auch einem so\n zu sagen moralischen Verschleiss. Sie verliert Tauschwerth im\n [Note: dent of the hands, and that, having once established the custom by means of the\n miserable materials they had procured in this way, they could impose it on their\n neighbours with the greater facility.“ (J. Fielden: „The Curse of the\n Factory System. Lond. 1836.“) Mit Bezug auf Weiberarbeit sagt Fabrik-\n inspektor Saunders im Fabrikbericht von 1844: „Unter den Arbeiterinnen giebt\n es Frauen, die hinter einander für viele Wochen, mit Ausfall nur weniger Tage,\n von 6 Uhr Morgens bis 12 Uhr Nachts beschäftigt werden, mit weniger als 2 Stun-\n den für Mahlzeiten, so dass ihnen für 5 Tage in der Woche von den 24 Tages-\n stunden nur 6 bleiben, um von und nach Haus zu gehn und im Bett auszuruhn.“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"394\", \"facs\": \"#f0413\"} --> Masse, worin entweder Maschinen derselben Konstruktion wohlfeiler reprodu-\n cirt werden können oder bessere Maschinen konkurrirend neben sie treten [Note: Der schon früher erwähnte „Manchester Spinner“ (Times, 26. Nov.\n 1862) zählt unter den Kosten der Maschinerie auf: „It (nämlich die „allowance\n for deterioration of machinery“) is also intended to cover the loss which is con-\n stantly arising from the superseding of machines before they are worn out by\n others of a new and better construction.“] .\n In beiden Fällen ist ihr Werth, so jung und lebenskräftig sie sonst noch sein\n mag, nicht mehr bestimmt durch die thatsächlich in ihr selbst vergegen-\n ständlichte, sondern durch die zu ihrer eignen Reproduktion oder zur Repro-\n duktion der besseren Maschine nothwendige Arbeitszeit. Sie ist daher mehr\n oder minder entwerthet. Je kürzer die Periode, worin ihr Gesammt-\n werth reproducirt wird, desto geringer die Gefahr des moralischen Ver-\n schleisses, und je länger der Arbeitstag, um so kürzer jene Periode.\n Bei der ersten Einführung der Maschinerie in irgend einen Produktions-\n zweig folgen Schlag auf Schlag neue Methoden zu ihrer wohlfeileren Re-\n produktion [Note: „Man schätzt im Grossen, dass eine einzige Maschine nach einem neuen\n Model zu konstruiren fünfmal so viel kostet, als die Rekonstruktion derselben\n Maschine nach demselben Model.“ (Babbage l. c. p. 349.)] , und Verbesserungen, die nicht nur einzelne Theile oder\n Apparate, sondern ihre ganze Konstruktion ergreifen. In ihrer ersten\n Lebensperiode wirkt daher diess besondre Motiv zur Verlängerung\n des Arbeitstags am akutesten [Note: „Seit einigen Jahren sind so bedeutende und zahlreiche Verbesserungen\n in der Tüllfabrikation gemacht worden, dass eine gut erhaltene Maschine zum ur-\n sprünglichen Kostenpreis von 1200 Pfd. St. einige Jahre später zu 60 Pfd. St.\n verkauft wurde . . . . Die Verbesserungen folgten sich mit solcher Geschwindig-\n keit, dass Maschinen unvollendet in der Hand ihrer Bauer blieben, weil sie durch\n glücklichere Erfindungen bereits veraltet waren.“ In dieser Sturm- und Drang-\n periode dehnten daher die Tüllfabrikanten bald die ursprüngliche Arbeitszeit von\n 8 Stunden mit doppelter Mannschaft auf 24 Stunden aus. (l. c. p. 377, 378\n u. 279.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0050","text":"Unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen und bei gegebnem Arbeitstag\n erheischt Exploitation verdoppelter Arbeiteranzahl ebensowohl Ver-\n dopplung des in Maschinerie und Baulichkeiten ausgelegten Theils des\n constanten Kapitals als des in Rohmaterial, Hilfsstoffen u. s. w. ausgelegten.\n Mit verlängertem Arbeitstag dehnt sich die Stufenleiter der Pro-\n duktion, während der in Maschinerie und Baulichkeiten ausgelegte Kapi-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"395\", \"facs\": \"#f0414\"} --> taltheil unverändert bleibt [Note: „It is self-evident, that, amid the ebbings and flowings of the market,\n and the alternate expansions and contractions of demand, occasions will con-\n stantly recur, in which the manufacturer may employ additional floating capital\n without employing additional fixed capital … if additional quantities of raw\n material can be worked up without incurring an additional expence for buildings\n and machinery.“ (R. Torrens: „On Wages and Combination. Lond.\n 1834“, p. 63.)] . Nicht nur der Mehrwerth wächst daher, son-\n dern die zur Erbeutung desselben nothwendigen Auslagen nehmen ab.\n Zwar findet diess auch sonst mehr oder minder bei aller Verlängerung des Ar-\n beitstags statt, fällt aber hier entscheidender ins Gewicht, weil der in Arbeits-\n mittel verwandelte Kapitaltheil überhaupt mehr ins Gewicht fällt [Note: Der im Text erwähnte Umstand ist nur der Vollständigkeit wegen er-\n wähnt, da ich erst im dritten Buch die Profitrate, d. h. das Verhältniss des\n Mehrwerths zum vorgeschossenen Gesammtkapital, behandle.] . Die\n Entwicklung des Maschinenbetriebs bindet nämlich einen stets wachsenden\n Bestandtheil des Kapitals in eine Form, worin es einerseits fortwährend\nverwerthbar ist, andrerseits Gebrauchswerth und Tauschwerth ver-\n liert, sobald sein Kontakt mit der lebendigen Arbeit unterbrochen wird.\n „Wenn“, belehrte Herr Ashworth, ein englischer Baumwollmagnat,\n den Professor Nassau W. Senior, „wenn ein Ackersmann seinen Spaten\n niederlegt, macht er für diese Periode ein Kapital von 18 d. nutzlos.\n Wenn einer von unsern Leuten (d. h. den Fabrikarbeitern) die Fabrik ver-\n lässt, macht er ein Kapital nutzlos, das 100000 Pfd. St.\n gekostet hat“ [Note: „When a labourer“, said Mr. Ashworth, „lays down his spade, he\n renders useless, for that period, a capital worth 18 d. When one of our people\n leaves the mill, he renders useless a capital that has cost 100,000 Pd. St.\n (Senior: „Letters on the Factory Act. Lond. 1837“, p. 13, 14.)] . Man denke nur! Ein Kapital, das 100000 Pfd. St. ge-\n kostet hat, auch nur für einen Augenblick „nutzlos“ zu machen! Es ist\n in der That himmelschreiend, dass einer unsrer Leute überhaupt jemals\n die Fabrik verlässt! Der wachsende Umfang der Maschinerie macht, wie\n der von Ashworth belehrte Senior einsieht, eine stets wachsende Verlän-\n gerung des Arbeitstags „wünschenswerth“ [Note: „The great proportion of fixed to circulating capital … makes long\n hours of work desirable.“ Mit dem wachsenden Umfang der Maschinerie\n u. s. w. „the motives to long hours of work will become greater, as the only\n means by which a large proportion of fixed capital can be made profitable.“ (l. c.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0051","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"396\", \"facs\": \"#f0415\"} -->","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0052","text":"Die Maschine producirt relativen Mehrwerth, nicht nur indem sie\n die Arbeitskraft direkt entwerthet und dieselbe indirekt durch Verwohl-\n feilerung der in ihre Reproduktion eingehenden Waaren verwohlfeilert,\n sondern auch, indem sie bei ihrer ersten sporadischen Einführung die vom\n Maschinenbesitzer verwandte Arbeit in potenzirte Arbeit verwandelt, den\n gesellschaftlichen Werth des Maschinenprodukts über seinen individuellen\n Werth erhöht und den Kapitalisten so befähigt mit geringerem Werththeil\n des Tagesprodukts den Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft zu ersetzen. Wäh-\n rend dieser Uebergangsperiode, worin der Maschinenbetrieb eine Art Mo-\n nopol bleibt, sind daher die Gewinne ausserordentlich und der Kapitalist\n sucht „diese erste Zeit der jungen Liebe“ gründlichst auszubeuten durch\n möglichste Verlängerung des Arbeitstags. Die Grösse des Ge-\n winns wetzt den Heisshunger nach mehr Gewinn.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0053","text":"Mit der Verallgemeinerung der Maschinerie im selben Produktions-\n zweig sinkt der gesellschaftliche Werth des Maschinenprodukts auf seinen in-\n dividuellen Werth und macht sich das Gesetz geltend, dass der Mehrwerth\n nicht aus den Arbeitskräften entspringt, welche der\n Kapitalist durch die Maschine ersetzt hat, sondern\n umgekehrt aus den Arbeitskräften, welche er an ihr\n beschäftigt. Der Mehrwerth entspringt nur aus dem variab-\n len Theil des Kapitals, und wir sahen, dass die Masse des\n Mehrwerths durch zwei Faktoren bestimmt ist, die Rate des Mehr-\n werths und die Anzahl der gleichzeitig beschäftigten Ar-\n beiter. Bei gegebner Länge des Arbeitstags wird die Rate des Mehr-\n werths bestimmt durch das Verhältniss, worin der Arbeitstag in nothwen-\n dige Arbeit und Mehrarbeit zerfällt. Die Anzahl der gleichzeitig beschäf-\n tigten Arbeiter hängt ihrerseits ab von dem Verhältniss des variablen Ka-\n pitaltheils zum constanten. Es ist nun klar, dass wie er immer durch\n Steigerung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit die Mehrarbeit auf Kosten der\n [Note: p. 11—13.) „Es giebt verschiedne Auslagen bei einer Fabrik, welche constant\n bleiben, ob die Fabrik mehr oder weniger Zeit arbeite, z. B. Rente für die Bau-\n lichkeiten, lokale und allgemeine Steuern, Feuerversicherung, Arbeitslohn für ver-\n schiedne permanente Arbeiter, Verschlechterung der Maschinerie nebst verschied-\n nen andren Lasten, deren Proportion zum Profit im selben Verhältniss wächst, wie\n der Umfang der Produktion zunimmt.“ („Reports of the Insp. of Fact.\n for 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 19.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"397\", \"facs\": \"#f0416\"} --> nothwendigen Arbeit ausdehne, der Maschinenbetrieb diess Resultat nur\n hervorbringt, indem er die Anzahl der von einem gegebnen Kapital\n beschäftigten Arbeiter vermindert. Er verwandelt einen Theil des\n Kapitals, der früher variabel war, d. h. sich in lebendige Arbeitskraft\n umsetzte, in Maschinerie, also in constantes Kapital, das keinen Mehr-\n werth producirt. Es ist unmöglich z. B. aus zwei Arbeitern so viel Mehr-\n werth auszupressen als aus 24. Wenn jeder der 24 Arbeiter auf 12\n Stunden nur eine Stunde Mehrarbeit liefert, liefern sie zusammen 24 Stun-\n den Mehrarbeit, während die Gesammtarbeit der zwei Arbeiter\n nur 24 Stunden beträgt. Es liegt also in der Anwendung der Maschine-\n rie zur Produktion von Mehrwerth ein immanenter Widerspruch,\n indem sie von den beiden Faktoren des Mehrwerths, den ein Kapital von\n gegebner Grösse liefert, den einen Faktor, die Rate des Mehrwerths,\n nur dadurch vergrössert, dass sie den andern Faktor, die Arbeiterzahl,\nverkleinert. Dieser immanente Widerspruch tritt hervor, sobald mit der\n Verallgemeinerung der Maschinerie in einem Industriezweig der Werth der\n maschinenmässig producirten Waare zum regelnden gesellschaftlichen\n Werth aller Waaren derselben Art wird, und es ist dieser Widerspruch, der\n wiederum das Kapital, ohne dass es sich dessen bewusst wäre [Note: Warum dieser immanente Widerspruch dem einzelnen Kapitalisten und\n daher auch der in seinen Anschauungen befangenen politischen Oekonomie nicht\n zum Bewusstsein kommt, wird man aus den ersten Kapiteln des dritten Buchs\n ersehn.] , zur ge-\n waltsamsten Verlängerung des Arbeitstags treibt, um die Ab-\n nahme in der verhältnissmässigen Anzahl der exploitirten Arbeiter\n durch Zunahme nicht nur der relativen, sondern auch der absoluten\n Mehrarbeit zu kompensiren.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0054","text":"Wenn also die kapitalistische Anwendung der Maschinerie\n einerseits neue mächtige Motive zur masslosen Verlängerung des Arbeitstags\n schafft und die Arbeitsweise selbst wie den Charakter des gesell-\n schaftlichen Arbeitskörpers in einer Art umwälzt, die den Wider-\n stand gegen diese Tendenz bricht, producirt sie andrerseits, theils durch Ein-\n rollirung dem Kapital früher unzugänglicher Schichten der Arbeiterklasse,\n theils durch Freisetzung der von der Maschine verdrängten Arbeiter, eine\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"398\", \"facs\": \"#f0417\"} -->überflüssige Arbeiterpopulation [Note: Es ist eins der grossen Verdienste Ricardo’s, die Maschinerie nicht nur\n als Produktionsmittel von Waaren, sondern auch von „redundant population“\n begriffen zu haben.] , die sich das Gesetz vom\n Kapital diktiren lassen muss. Daher das merkwürdige Phänomen in der\n Geschichte der modernen Industrie, dass die Maschine alle sittlichen und\n natürlichen Schranken des Arbeitstags über den Haufen wirft. Daher das\n ökonomische Paradoxon, dass das gewaltigste Mittel zur Verkür-\n zung der Arbeitszeit in das unfehlbarste Mittel umschlägt alle\n Lebenszeit des Arbeiters und seiner Familie in disponible Ar-\n beitszeit für die Verwerthung des Kapitals zu verwandeln. „Wenn“,\n träumte Aristoteles, der grösste Denker des Alterthums, „wenn jedes\n Werkzeug auf Geheiss, oder auch vorausahnend, das ihm zukommende\n Werk verrichten könnte, wie des Dädalus Kunstwerke sich von selbst be-\n wegten, oder die Dreifüsse des Hephästos aus eignem Antrieb an die heilige\n Arbeit gingen, wenn so die Weberschiffe von selbst webten,\n so bedürfte es weder für den Werkmeister der Gehilfen, noch für die Herrn\n der Sklaven“ [Note: F. Biese: „Die Philosophie des Aristoteles.“ Zweiter\n Band. Berl. 1842, p. 408.] . Und Antiparos, ein griechischer Dichter aus der\n Zeit des Cicero, begrüsste die Erfindung der Wassermühle zum Mah-\n len des Getreides, diese Elementarform aller produktiven Maschinerie, als\n Befreierin der Sklavinnen und Herstellerin des goldnen Zeitalters! [Note: Ich gebe hier die Stolbergsche Uebersetzung des Gedichts, weil es ganz\n so wie die früheren Citate über Theilung der Arbeit den Gegensatz der antiken\n Anschauung zur modernen charakterisirt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0055","text":"„Schonet der mahlenden Hand, o Müllerinnen, und schlafet","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0056","text":"Sanft! es verkünde der Hahn euch den Morgen umsonst!","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0057","text":"Däo hat die Arbeit der Mädchen den Nymphen befohlen,","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0058","text":"Und itzt hüpfen sie leicht über die Räder dahin,","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0059","text":"Dass die erschütterten Achsen mit ihren Speichen sich wälzen,","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0060","text":"Und im Kreise die Last drehen des wälzenden Steins.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0061","text":"Lasst uns leben das Leben der Väter, und lasst uns der Gaben","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0062","text":"Arbeitslos uns freuen, welche die Göttin uns schenkt.“","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0063","text":"(„Gedichte aus dem Griechischen übersetzt von Christian\n Graf zu Stolberg. Hamburg 1782.“)] \n „Die Heiden, ja die Heiden!“ Sie begriffen, wie der gescheidte Bastiat\n entdeckt hat, und schon vor ihm der noch klügere Mac Culloch, nichts\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"399\", \"facs\": \"#f0418\"} --> von politischer Oekonomie und Christent um. Sie begriffen u. a. nicht,\n dass die Maschine das probateste Mittel zur Verlängerung des Arbeitstags\n ist. Sie entschuldigten etwa die Sklaverei des Einen als Mittel zur vollen\n menschlichen Entwicklung des Andern. Aber Sklaverei der Massen pre-\n digen, um einige rohe oder halbgebildete Parvenüs zu „eminent spinners“,\n „extensive sausage makers“ und „influential shoe black dealers“ zu machen,\n dazu fehlte ihnen das specifisch christliche Organ.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0064","text":"Die masslose Verlängerung des Arbeitstags, welche die Ma-\n schinerie in der Hand des Kapitals producirt, führt, wie wir sahen, später eine\nReaktion der in ihrer Lebenswurzel bedrohten Gesellschaft herbei, und\n damit einen gesetzlich beschränkten Normal-Arbeitstag.\n Auf Grundlage des letztern entwickelt sich ein Phänomen, das uns schon\n früher begegnete, zu entscheidender Wichtigkeit — nämlich die Inten-\n sifikation der Arbeit. Bei der Analyse des absoluten Mehrwerths\n handelte es sich zunächst um die extensive Grösse der Arbeit, wäh-\n rend der Grad ihrer Intensivität als gegeben vorausgesetzt war. Wir ha-\n ben jetzt den Umschlag dieser extensiven in eine intensive oder\nGradgrösse zu betrachten.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0065","text":"Es ist selbstverständlich, dass mit dem Fortschritt des Maschinen-\n wesens und der gehäuften Erfahrung einer eignen Klasse von Maschinen-\n arbeitern die Geschwindigkeit und damit die Intensivität der Arbeit natur-\n wüchsig zunehmen. So geht in England während eines halben Jahr-\n hunderts die Verlängerung des Arbeitstags Hand in Hand mit\n der wachsenden Intensivität der Fabrikarbeit. Indess begreift\n man, dass bei einer Arbeit, wo es sich nicht um vorübergehende Paroxys-\n men handelt, sondern um Tag aus, Tag ein wiederholte, regelmässige\n Gleichförmigkeit, ein Knotenpunkt eintreten muss, wo Ausdehnung des\n Arbeitstags und Intensivität der Arbeit einander ausschliessen, so dass die\n Verlängerung des Arbeitstags nur mit schwächerem Intensivitätsgrad der\n Arbeit und umgekehrt ein erhöhter Intensivitätsgrad nur mit Verkürzung\n des Arbeitstags verträglich bleibt. Sobald die allmälig anschwellende Em-\n pörung der Arbeiterklasse den Staat zwang, die Arbeitszeit gewaltsam zu\n verkürzen und zunächst der eigentlichen Fabrik einen Normal-Arbeitstag zu\n diktiren, von diesem Augenblick also, wo gesteigerte Produktion von Mehr-\n werth durch Verlängerung des Arbeitstags ein für allemal abge-\n schnitten war, warf sich das Kapital mit aller Macht und vollem Bewusst-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"400\", \"facs\": \"#f0419\"} --> sein auf die Produktion von relativem Mehrwerth durch beschleu-\n nigte Entwicklung des Maschinensystems. Gleichzeitig tritt eine Aende-\n rung in dem Charakter des relativen Mehrwerths ein. Im Allgemeinen\n besteht die Produktionsmethode des relativen Mehrwerths darin, durch\n gesteigerte Produktivkraft der Arbeit den Arbeiter zu befähigen mit der-\n selben Arbeitsausgabe in derselben Zeit mehr zu produciren.\nDieselbe Arbeitszeit setzt nach wie vor dem Gesammtprodukt\ndenselben Werth zu, obgleich dieser unveränderte Tauschwerth sich\n jetzt in mehr Gebrauchswerthen darstellt und daher der Werth der ein-\n zelnen Waare sinkt. Anders jedoch, sobald die gewaltsame Verkür-\n zung des Arbeitstags mit dem ungeheuren Anstoss, den sie der\nEntwicklung der Produktivkraft und der Oekonomisirung\n der Produktionsbedingungen giebt, zugleich vergrösserte\n Arbeitsausgabe in derselben Zeit, erhöhte Anspannung der\n Arbeitskraft, dichtere Ausfüllung der Poren der Arbeitszeit, d. h. Konden-\n sation der Arbeit dem Arbeiter zu einem Grad aufzwingt, der nur inner-\n halb des verkürzten Arbeitstags erreichbar ist. Diese Zusam-\n menpressung einer grösseren Masse Arbeit in eine gegebene Zeitperiode\n zählt jetzt als was sie ist, als grösseres Arbeitsquantum. Neben\n das Mass der Arbeitszeit als „ausgedehnter Grösse“ tritt jetzt das Mass\n ihres Verdichtungsgrads [Note: Es finden natürlich überhaupt Unterschiede in der Intensivität der Arbei-\n ten verschiedner Produktionszweige statt. Diese kompensiren sich,\n wie schon A. Smith gezeigt hat, zum Theil durch jeder Arbeitsart eigne Nebenum-\n stände. Einwirkung auf die Arbeitszeit als Werthmass findet aber auch hier nur\n statt, soweit intensive und extensive Grösse sich als entgegengesetzte und\n einander ausschliessende Ausdrücke desselben Arbeitsquantums darstellen.] . Die intensivere Stunde des zehnstün-\n digen Arbeitstags enthält jetzt so viel oder mehr Arbeit, d. h. veraus-\n gabte Arbeitskraft als die porösere Stunde des zwölfstündigen Arbeits-\n tags. Ihr Produkt hat daher so viel oder mehr Werth als das der poröseren\n 1⅕ Stunden. Abgesehn von der Erhöhung des relativen Mehrwerths\n durch die gesteigerte Produktivkraft der Arbeit, liefern jetzt z. B. 3⅓\n Stunden Mehrarbeit auf 6⅔ Stunden nothwendiger Arbeit dem Kapitali-\n sten dieselbe Werthmasse wie vorher 4 Stunden Mehrarbeit auf 8 Stun-\n den nothwendiger Arbeit.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0066","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"401\", \"facs\": \"#f0420\"} -->","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0067","text":"Es fragt sich nun, wie wird die Arbeit intensificirt?","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0068","text":"Die erste Wirkung des verkürzten Arbeitstags beruht auf\n dem selbstverständlichen Gesetz, dass die Wirkungsfähigkeit der Arbeits-\n kraft im umgekehrten Verhältniss zu ihrer Wirkungszeit steht. Es wird daher,\n innerhalb gewisser Grenzen, am Grad der Kraftäusserung gewonnen, was\n an ihrer Dauer verloren geht. Dass der Arbeiter aber auch wirklich mehr\n Arbeitskraft flüssig macht, dafür sorgt das Kapital durch die Methode\n der Zahlung [Note: Namentlich durch den Stücklohn, eine Form, die im nächsten Kapitel\n entwickelt wird.] . In Manufakturen, der Töpferei z. B., wo die Maschinerie\n keine oder unbedeutende Rolle spielt, hat die Einführung des Fabrikge-\n setzes schlagend bewiesen, dass blosse Verkürzung des Arbeitstags\n die Regelmässigkeit, Gleichförmigkeit, Ordnung, Kontinuität und Energie der\n Arbeit wundervoll erhöht [Note: Sieh „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1865.“] . Diese Wirkung schien jedoch zweifelhaft in der\neigentlichen Fabrik, weil die Abhängigkeit des Arbeiters von der\n kontinuirlichen und gleichförmigen Bewegung der Maschine hier längst die\n strengste Disciplin geschaffen hatte. Als daher 1844 die Herabsetzung des\n Arbeitstags unter 12 Stunden verhandelt ward, erklärten die Fabrikanten fast\n einstimmig, „ihre Aufseher passten in den verschiednen Arbeitsräumen auf,\n dass die Hände keine Zeit verlören“, „der Grad der Wachsamkeit und\n Aufmerksamkeit auf Seiten der Arbeiter („the extent of vigilance and atten-\n tion on the part of the workmen“) sei kaum steigerungsfähig“, und alle\n anderen Umstände, wie Gang der Maschinerie u. s. w. als gleichblei-\n bend vorausgesetzt, „sei es daher Unsinn in wohlgeführten Fabriken von\n der gesteigerten Aufmerksamkeit u. s. w. der Arbeiter irgend ein erkleck-\n liches Resultat zu erwarten“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 1844 and the quarter ending\n 30th April 1845“, p. 20, 21.] . Diese Behauptung ward durch Ex-\n perimente widerlegt. Herr R. Gardner liess in seinen zwei grossen\n Fabriken zu Preston vom 20. April 1844 an statt 12 nur noch 11 Stunden\n per Tag arbeiten. Nach ungefähr Jahresfrist ergab sich das Resultat,\n dass „dasselbe Quantum Produkt zur selben Kost erhalten ward, und\n sämmtliche Arbeiter in 11 Stunden eben so viel Arbeitslohn verdienten,\n [Running matter: I. 26] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"402\", \"facs\": \"#f0421\"} --> wie früher in 12“ [Note: l. c. p. 19. Da der Stücklohn derselbe blieb, hing die Höhe des Wochen-\n lohns vom Quantum des Produkts ab.] . Ich übergehe hier die Experimente in den Spinn-\n und Kardirräumen, weil sie mit Zunahme in der Geschwindigkeit der Ma-\n schinerie (um 2 %) verbunden waren. In dem Webedepartement dagegen,\n wo zudem sehr verschiedene Sorten leichter, figurenhaltiger Phanta-\n sieartikel gewebt wurden, fand durchaus keine Aenderung in den objek-\n tiven Produktionsbedingungen statt. Das Resultat war: „Vom 6. Januar\n bis 20. April 1844, mit zwölfstündigem Arbeitstag, wöchentlicher Durch-\n schnittslohn jedes Arbeiters 10 sh. 1½ d., vom 20. April bis 29. Juni\n 1844, mit elfstündigem Arbeitstag, wöchentlicher Durchschnittslohn 10 sh.\n 3½ d.“ [Note: l. c. p. 20.] . Es wurde hier in 11 Stunden mehr producirt als früher in\n 12, ausschliesslich in Folge grösserer gleichmässiger Ausdauer der Arbei-\n ter und Oekonomie ihrer Zeit. Während sie denselben Lohn empfingen\n und 1 Stunde freie Zeit gewannen, erhielt der Kapitalist dieselbe Pro-\n duktenmasse und sparte Verausgabung von Kohle, Gas u. s. w. für eine\n Stunde. Aehnliche Experimente wurden mit gleichem Erfolg in den Fa-\n briken der Herren Horrocks und Jacson ausgeführt [Note: l. c. p. 21. Das moralische Element spielte bedeutende Rolle in den\n oben erwähnten Experimenten. „We“, erklärten die Arbeiter dem Fabrikinspek-\n tor, „we work with more spirit, we have the reward ever before us of getting\n away sooner at night, and one active and cheerful spirit pervades the whole mill,\n from the youngest piecer to the oldest hand, and we can greatly help each\n other.“ (l. c.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0069","text":"Sobald die Verkürzung des Arbeitstags, welche zunächst die\nsubjektive Bedingung der Kondensation der Arbeit schafft, nämlich die\n Fähigkeit des Arbeiters mehr Kraft in gegebner Zeit flüssig zu machen,\nzwangsgesetzlich wird, wandelt sich die Maschine in der Hand des Ka-\n pitals zum objektiven und systematisch angewandten Mit-\n tel mehr Arbeit in derselben Zeit zu erpressen. Es geschieht diess in\n doppelter Weise, durch die erhöhte Geschwindigkeit der Ma-\n schinen und den erweiterten Umfang der von demselben Arbeiter\n zu überwachenden Maschinerie oder seines Arbeitsfeldes. Verbesserte\n Konstruktion der Maschinerie ist theils nothwendig zur Ausübung des\n grösseren Drucks auf den Arbeiter, theils begleitet sie von selbst die Inten-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"403\", \"facs\": \"#f0422\"} --> sifikation der Arbeit, weil die Schranke des Arbeitstags den Kapitalisten\n zu strengstem Haushalt der Produktionskosten zwingt. Die Verbesserung\n der Dampfmaschine erhöht die Anzahl ihrer Kolbenschläge in einer Minute\n und erlaubt zugleich, durch grössere Oekonomie der Kraft, einen umfang-\n reicheren Mechanismus mit demselben Motor zu treiben, bei gleichbleiben-\n dem oder selbst fallendem Kohlenverzehr. Die Verbesserung des Trans-\n missionsmechanismus vermindert die Friktion und, was die moderne\n Maschinerie so augenfällig vor der älteren auszeichnet, reducirt Durch-\n messer und Gewicht der grossen und kleinen Wellenbäume auf ein stets\n fallendes Minimum. Die Verbesserungen der Arbeitsmaschinerie endlich\nvermindern bei erhöhter Geschwindigkeit und ausgedehnterer Wirkung\n ihren Umfang, wie beim modernen Dampfwebstuhl, oder vergrössern mit\n dem Rumpf Umfang und Zahl der von ihr geführten Werkzeuge, wie bei der\n Spinnmaschine, oder vermehren die Beweglichkeit dieser Werkzeuge durch\n unscheinbare Detailveränderung, wie durch solche bei der selfacting mule\n vor etwa 10 Jahren die Geschwindigkeit der Spindeln um ⅕ gesteigert wurde.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0070","text":"Die Verkürzung des Arbeitstags auf 12 Stunden datirt in Eng-\n land von 1832. Schon 1836 erklärte ein englischer Fabrikant: „Ver-\n glichen mit früher ist die Arbeit, die in den Fabriken zu verrichten, sehr\n gewachsen, in Folge der grösseren Aufmerksamkeit und Thätigkeit, welche\n die bedeutend vermehrte Geschwindigkeit der Maschinerie vom Arbeiter\n erheischt“ [Note: John Fielden l. c. p. 32.] . Im Jahr 1844 machte Lord Ashley, jetzt Graf Shaftes-\n bury, folgende dokumentarisch belegte Aufstellungen im Hause der Gemeinen:","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0071","text":"„Die Arbeit der in den Fabrikprozessen Beschäftigten ist jetzt drei-\n mal so gross, als bei der Einführung solcher Operationen. Die Maschinerie\n hat zweifelsohne ein Werk verrichtet, welches die Sehnen und Muskeln von\n Millionen Menschen ersetzt, aber sie hat auch erstaunlich (prodigiously)\n die Arbeit der durch ihre furchtbare Bewegung beherrschten Menschen\n vermehrt. . . . Die Arbeit einem Paar Mules während 12 Stunden auf- und\n abzufolgen, zum Spinnen von Garn No. 40, bedang im Jahre 1815 die\n Nothwendigkeit einer Reise von 8 Meilen. Im Jahre 1812 betrug die im\n Gefolge eines Mulepaars, zum Spinnen derselben Nummer, während 12\n Stunden zu durchreisende Distanz 20 Meilen und oft mehr. Im Jahre\n 1825 hatte der Spinner während 12 Stunden 820 Auszüge an jeder Mule\n [Running matter: 26*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"404\", \"facs\": \"#f0423\"} --> zu machen, was eine Gesammtsumme von 1640 für 12 Stunden ergab.\n Im Jahre 1832 hatte der Spinner während seines zwölfstündigen Arbeits-\n tags an jeder Mule 2,200 Auszüge zu machen, zusammen 4,400, im Jahre\n 1844 an jeder Mule 2,400, zusammen 4,800; und in einigen Fällen ist die\n erheischte Arbeitsmasse (amount of labour) noch grösser....Ich habe hier\n ein andres Dokument von 1842 in der Hand, worin nachgewiesen wird,\n dass die Arbeit progressiv zunimmt, nicht nur, weil eine grössere\n Entfernung zu durchreisen ist, sondern weil die Quantität der producirten\n Waaren sich vermehrt, während die Hände proportionell abnehmen; und\n ferner, weil nun oft eine schlechtere Baumwolle gesponnen wird, die mehr\n Arbeit erfordert. . . . Im Kardirraum hat auch grosse Zunahme der Arbeit\n stattgefunden. Eine Person thut jetzt die Arbeit, die früher zwischen\n zwei vertheilt war. . . . In der Weberei, worin eine grosse Anzahl Per-\n sonen, meist weiblichen Geschlechts beschäftigt ist, ist die Arbeit während\n der letzten Jahre um volle 10 % gewachsen, in Folge der vermehrten\n Geschwindigkeit der Maschinerie. Im Jahre 1838 war die Zahl der\n hanks, die wöchentlich gesponnen wurde, 18,000, im Jahre 1843 belief\n sie sich auf 21,000. Im Jahr 1819 war die Zahl der picks beim Dampf-\n webestuhl 60 per Minute, im Jahre 1842 betrug sie 140, was einen\n grossen Zuwachs von Arbeit anzeigt“ [Note: Lord Ashley l. c. p. 6—9 passim.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0072","text":"Angesichts dieser merkwürdigen Intensivität, welche die Arbeit unter\n der Herrschaft des Zwölfstundengesetzes bereits 1844 erreicht hatte, schien\n damals die Erklärung der englischen Fabrikanten berechtigt: jeder weitere\n Fortschritt in dieser Richtung sei unmöglich, daher jede weitere Abnahme\n der Arbeitszeit identisch mit Abnahme der Produktion. Die scheinbare\n Richtigkeit ihres Raisonnements wird am besten bewiesen durch folgende\n gleichzeitige Aeusserung ihres rastlosen Censors, des Fabrikinspektors\nLeonhard Horner:","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0073","text":"„Da die producirte Quantität hauptsächlich geregelt wird durch die\n Geschwindigkeit der Maschinerie, muss es das Interesse des Fabrikanten\n sein, sie mit dem äussersten Geschwindigkeitsgrad zu treiben, der mit fol-\n genden Bedingungen vereinbar ist: Bewahrung der Maschinerie vor zu\n raschem Verderb, Erhaltung der Qualität des fabricirten Artikels, und\n Fähigkeit des Arbeiters der Bewegung zu folgen ohne grössere Anstren-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"405\", \"facs\": \"#f0424\"} --> gung als er kontinuirlich leisten kann. Es ereignet sich oft, dass der Fabri-\n kant in seiner Hast die Bewegung zu sehr beschleunigt. Brüche und\n schlechtes Machwerk wiegen dann die Geschwindigkeit mehr als auf und\n er ist gezwungen den Gang der Maschinerie zu mässigen. Ich schloss\n daher, da ein aktiver und einsichtsvoller Fabrikant das sichre Maximum\n ausfindet, dass es unmöglich ist in 11 Stunden so viel zu produciren als\n in 12. Ich nahm ausserdem an, dass der per Stücklohn bezahlte Arbeiter\n sich auf’s äusserste anstrengt, soweit er denselben Arbeitsgrad kontinuir-\n lich aushalten kann“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 1845“, p. 20.] . Horner schloss daher, trotz der Experimente\n von Gardner u. s. w., dass eine weitere Herabsetzung des Ar-\n beitstags unter 12 Stunden die Quantität des Produkts\n vermindern müsse [Note: l. c. p. 22.] . Er selbst citirt 10 Jahre später sein Be-\n denken von 1845 zum Beweis, wie wenig er damals noch die Elastici-\n tät der Maschinerie und der menschlichen Arbeitskraft, die beide\n gleichmässig durch die zwangsweise Verkürzung des Ar-\n beitstags aufs Höchste gespannt werden, begriffen habe.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0074","text":"Kommen wir nun zur Periode nach 1847, seit Einführung des\nZehnstundengesetzes in die englischen Baumwoll-, Woll-, Seiden-\n und Flachsfabriken.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0075","text":"„Die Geschwindigkeit der Spindeln ist auf Throstles um 500, auf\n Mules um 1000 Drehungen in einer Minute gewachsen, d. h. die Geschwin-\n digkeit der Throstlespindel, die 1839 4500 Drehungen in einer Minute\n zählte, beträgt nun (1862) 5000, und die der Mulespindel, die 5000\n zählte, beträgt jetzt 6000 in der Minute; diess beläuft sich im ersten Fall\n auf ⅒ und im zweiten auf ⅕ zusätzlicher Geschwindigkeit“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 62.] . Jos.\n Nasmyth, der berühmte Civilingenieur von Paticroft, bei Manchester,\n setzte 1852 in einem Brief an Leonhard Horner die von 1848—1852\n gemachten Verbesserungen in der Dampfmaschine auseinander. Nachdem\n er bemerkt, dass die Dampfpferdekraft, in der officiellen Fabrikstatistik\n fortwährend geschätzt nach ihrer Wirkung im Jahr 1828 [Note: Diess hat sich geändert mit dem „Parliamentary Return“ von 1862.\n Hier tritt die wirkliche Dampfpferdekraft der modernen Dampfmaschinen und\n Wasserräder an die Stelle der nominellen. Auch sind die Dublirspindeln nicht] , nur noch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"406\", \"facs\": \"#f0425\"} -->nominell ist und nur als Index der wirklichen Kraft dienen kann, sagt\n er u. a.: „Es unterliegt keinem Zweifel, dass Dampfmaschinerie von dem-\n selben Gewicht, oft dieselben identischen Maschinen, an denen nur die\n modernen Verbesserungen angebracht sind, im Durchschnitt 50 % mehr\n Werk als früher verrichten, und dass in vielen Fällen dieselben identischen\n Dampfmaschinen, die in den Tagen der beschränkten Geschwindigkeit von\n 220 Fuss per Minute 50 Pferdekraft lieferten, heute, mit vermindertem\n Kohlenkonsum über 100 liefern. . . . Die moderne Dampfmaschine von\n derselben nominellen Pferdekraft wird mit viel grösserer Gewalt als früher\n getrieben, in Folge der Verbesserungen in ihrer Konstruktion, vermindertem\n Umfang und Bau der Dampfkessel u. s. w. … Obgleich daher die-\n selbe Händezahl wie früher im Verhältniss zur nominellen Pferde-\n kraft beschäftigt wird, werden weniger Hände verwandt im Ver-\n hältniss zur Arbeitsmaschinerie“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1856“, p. 11.] . Im Jahr 1850 ver-\n wandten die Fabriken des Vereinigten Königreichs 134, 217 nominelle Pferde-\n kraft zur Bewegung von 25,638,716 Spindeln und 301,495 Webstühlen.\n Im Jahr 1856 betrug die Zahl der Spindeln und Webstühle respective\n 33,503,580 und 369,205. Wäre die erheischte Pferdekraft dieselbe\n geblieben wie 1850, so waren 1856: 175,000 Pferdekraft nöthig. Sie\n betrug aber nach dem officiellen Ausweis nur 161,435, also über 10,000\n Pferdekraft weniger, als wenn man nach der Basis von 1850 rechnet [Note: l c. p. 14, 15.] .\n „Die durch den letzten Return von 1856 (officielle Statistik) festgestellten\n Thatsachen sind, dass das Fabriksystem reissend rasch um sich greift,\ndie Zahl der Hände im Verhältniss zur Maschinerie ab-\n genommen hat, die Dampfmaschine durch Oekonomie der Kraft und\n andre Methoden ein grösseres Maschinengewicht treibt, und ein vermehrtes\n Quantum Machwerk erzielt wird in Folge verbesserter Arbeitsmaschinen,\n veränderter Methoden der Fabrikation, erhöhter Geschwindigkeit der Ma-\n [Note: mehr zusammengeworfen mit den eigentlichen Spinnspindeln (wie in den „Re-\n turns“ von 1839, 1850 und 1856); ferner ist für die Wollfabriken die Zahl der\n „gigs“ hinzugefügt, Scheidung eingeführt zwischen Jute- und Hanffabriken einer-\n seits, Flachsfabriken andrerseits, endlich zum erstenmal die Strumpfwirkerei in den\n Bericht aufgenommen.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"407\", \"facs\": \"#f0426\"} --> schinerie und vieler andrer Ursachen“ [Note: l. c. p. 20.] . „Die grossen in Maschinen\n jeder Art eingeführten Verbesserungen haben deren Produktivkraft sehr\n gesteigert. Ohne allen Zweifel gab die Verkürzung des Arbeits-\n tags … den Stachel zu diesen Verbesserungen. Letztere und die in-\n tensivere Anstrengung des Arbeiters bewirkten, dass wenig-\n stens eben so viel Machwerk in dem (um zwei Stunden oder ⅙)\nverkürzten Arbeitstag als früher während des längeren\n geliefert wird“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1858“, p. 9, 10. Vgl. „Reports etc.\nfor 30th April 1860“, p. 30 sqq.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0076","text":"Wie die Bereicherung der Fabrikanten mit der intensiveren Ausbeu-\n tung der Arbeitskraft zunahm, beweist schon der eine Umstand, dass das\n durchschnittliche proportionelle Wachsthum der englischen Baumwollen-\n u. s. w.-Fabriken von 1838 bis 1850 32 %, von 1850 bis 1856 dagegen\n 86 % jährlich betrug.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0077","text":"So gross in den 8 Jahren 1848 bis 1856, unter der Herrschaft des\n zehnstündigen Arbeitstags, der Fortschritt der englischen Industrie, wurde\n er wieder weit überflügelt in der folgenden sechsjährigen Periode von 1856\n bis 1862. In der Seidenfabrik z. B. 1856: Spindeln 1,093,799,\n 1862: 1,388,544; 1856: Webstühle 9,260 und 1862: 10,709. Da-\n gegen 1856: Arbeiteranzahl 56,131 und 1862: 52,429. Diess ergiebt\nZunahme der Spindelzahl 26.9 % und der Webstühle 15.6 % mit\ngleichzeitiger Abnahme der Arbeiteranzahl um 7 %. Im\n Jahre 1850 wurden in der Worsted-Fabrik angewandt 875,830 Spin-\n deln, 1856: 1,324,549 (Zunahme von 51.2 %) und 1862: 1,289,172\n (Abnahme von 2.7 %). Zählt man aber die Dublirspindeln ab, die in der\n Aufzählung für das Jahr 1856 figuriren, aber nicht für 1862, so blieb die\n Anzahl der Spindeln seit 1856 ziemlich stationär. Dagegen ward seit\n 1850 in vielen Fällen die Geschwindigkeit der Spindeln und Webstühle\nverdoppelt. Die Zahl der Dampfwebstühle in der Worsted-Fabrik\n1850: 32,617, 1856: 38,956 und 1862: 43,048. Es waren\n dabei beschäftigt 1850: 79,737 Personen, 1856: 87,794 und 1862:\n 86,063, aber davon Kinder unter 14 Jahren 1850: 9,956,\n1856: 11,228 und 1862: 13,178. Trotz sehr vermehrter Anzahl\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"408\", \"facs\": \"#f0427\"} --> der Webstühle, 1862 verglichen mit 1856, nahm also die Gesammt-\n zahl der beschäftigten Arbeiter ab, die der exploitirten Kinder zu [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 100 u. 130.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0078","text":"Am 27. April 1863 erklärte das Parlamentsglied Ferrand im\n Unterhause: „Arbeiterdelegirte von 16 Distrikten von Lancashire und\n Cheshire, in deren Auftrag ich spreche, haben mir mitgetheilt, dass die\n Arbeit in den Fabriken in Folge der Verbesserung in der Maschinerie\n beständig wachse. Statt dass früher eine Person mit Gehilfen zwei\n Webstühle bediente, bedient sie jetzt drei ohne Gehilfen und es ist gar\n nichts ungewöhnliches, dass eine Person ihrer vier bedient u. s. w.\nZwölf Stunden Arbeit, wie aus den mitgetheilten Thatsachen her-\n vorgeht, werden jetzt in weniger als 10 Arbeitsstunden ge-\n presst. Es ist daher selbstverständlich, in welchem ungeheuren Umfang\n die Mühen der Fabrikarbeiter sich seit den letzten Jahren vermehrt\n haben“ [Note: Mit dem modernen Dampfwebstuhl fabricirt ein Weber jetzt in 60 Stunden\n per Woche auf 2 Stühlen 26 Stück einer gewissen Art von bestimmter Länge und\n Breite, wovon er auf dem alten Dampfwebstuhl nur 4 fabriciren konnte. Die Web-\n kosten eines solchen Stücks waren schon Anfang der 1850er Jahre von 2 sh. 9 d.\n auf 5⅛ d. gefallen.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0079","text":"Obgleich daher die Fabrikinspektoren die günstigen Resultate der\n Fabrikgesetze von 1844 und 1850 unermüdlich und mit vollem Recht\n lobpreisen, gestehen sie doch, dass die Verkürzung des Arbeitstags bereits\n eine die Gesundheit der Arbeiter, also die Arbeitskraft selbst zer-\n störende Kondensation der Arbeit hervorgerufen habe. „In den meisten\n Baumwoll-, Worsted- und Seidenfabriken scheint der erschöpfende Zustand\n von Aufregung, nöthig für die Arbeit an der Maschinerie, deren Bewegung\n in den letzten Jahren so ausserordentlich beschleunigt worden ist, eine der\n Ursachen des Ueberschusses der Sterblichkeit an Lungenkrankheiten, den\n Dr. Greenhow in seinem jüngsten bewundernswerthen Bericht nachge-\n wiesen hat“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1861“, p. 25, 26.] . Es unterliegt nicht dem geringsten Zweifel, dass die\n Tendenz des Kapitals, sobald ihm Verlängerung des Arbeitstags ein für\n allemal durch das Gesetz abgeschnitten ist, sich durch systematische Steige-\n rung des Intensivitätsgrads der Arbeit gütlich zu thun und jede Ver-\n besserung der Maschinerie in ein Mittel zu grösserer Aussaugung der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"409\", \"facs\": \"#f0428\"} --> Arbeitskraft zu verkehren, bald wieder zu einem Wendepunkt treiben muss,\n wo abermalige Abnahme der Arbeitsstunden unvermeidlich wird [Note: Die Achtstundenagitation hat jetzt (1867) in Lancashire unter den Fabrik-\n arbeitern begonnen.] . Andrer-\n seits überflügelt der Sturmmarsch der englischen Industrie von 1848 bis\n zur Gegenwart, d. h. während der Periode des zehnstündigen\n Arbeitstags, noch weit mehr die Zeit von 1833 bis 1847, d. h. die\nPeriode des zwölfstündigen Arbeitstags, als letztre das halbe\n Jahrhundert seit Einführung des Fabriksystems, d. h. die Periode des\n unbeschränkten Arbeitstags [Note: Folgende wenige Zahlen zeigen den Fortschritt der eigentlichen „Facto-\n ries“ im U. Kingd. seit 1848:","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0080","text":" | ","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0081","text":" | ","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0082","text":"] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0083","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"410\", \"facs\": \"#f0429\"} -->","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0084","text":"Wir betrachteten im Beginn dieses Kapitels den Leib der Fabrik,\n die Gliederung des Maschinensystems. Wir sahen dann, wie die Maschi-\n nerie das menschliche Exploitationsmaterial des Kapitals vermehrt durch\n Aneignung der Weiber- und Kinderarbeit, wie sie die ganze Lebenszeit\n des Arbeiters confiscirt durch masslose Ausdehnung des Arbeitstags, und\n wie ihr Fortschritt, der ein ungeheuer wachsendes Produkt in stets kürzerer\n Zeit zu liefern erlaubt, endlich zum Mittel umschlägt in jedem Zeitmo-\n ment mehr Arbeit flüssig zu machen oder die Arbeitskraft\n stets intensiver auszubeuten. Wir wenden uns nun zum Fa-\n brikganzen, und zwar in seiner ausgebildetsten Gestalt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0085","text":"Dr. Ure, der Pindar der automatischen Fabrik, beschreibt sie einer-\n seits als „Cooperation verschiedener Klassen von Arbeitern, erwachs-\n nen und nicht erwachsnen, die mit Gewandtheit und Fleiss ein System\n produktiver Maschinerie überwachen, das ununterbrochen durch eine Cen-\n tralkraft (den ersten Motor) in Thätigkeit gesetzt wird“, andrerseits als\n „einen ungeheuren Automaten, zusammengesetzt aus zahllosen\n mechanischen und selbstbewussten Organen, die im Einverständniss und\n ohne Unterbrechung wirken, um einen und denselben Gegenstand zu pro-\n duciren, so dass alle diese Organe einer Bewegungskraft untergeord-\n net sind, die sich von selbst bewegt.“ Diese beiden Ausdrücke sind\n keineswegs identisch. In dem einen erscheint der kombinirte Gesammt-\n arbeiter oder gesellschaftliche Arbeitskörper als übergreifendes Subjekt\n und der mechanische Automat als Objekt; in dem andern ist der Automat\n selbst das Subjekt und die Arbeiter sind seinen bewusstlosen Organen nur\n [Note: (Sieh die Blaubücher: „Statistical Abstract for the U. Kingd.“\n Nr. 8 und Nr. 13. Lond. 1861 und 1866.)\n In Lancashire vermehrten sich die Fabriken zwischen 1839 und 1850 nur um\n 4 %, zwischen 1850 und 1856 um 19 %, zwischen 1856 und 1862 um 33 %, wäh-\n rend in beiden elfjährigen Perioden die Zahl der beschäftigten Personen absolut\n zunahm, relativ fiel. Cf. Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1862, p. 63.\n In Lancashire herrscht die Baumwollfabrik vor. Welchen proportionellen Raum\n sie aber in der Fabrikation von Garn und Gewebe überhaupt einnimmt, sieht man\n daraus, dass auf sie allein von allen derartigen Fabriken in England, Wales,\n Schottland und Irland 45.2 % fallen, von allen Spindeln des U. Kingd. 83.3 %, von\n allen Dampfwebstühlen 81.4 %, von aller sie bewegenden Dampfpferdekraft 72.6 %\n und von der Gesammtzahl der beschäftigten Personen 58.2 %. (l. c. p. 62, 63.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"411\", \"facs\": \"#f0430\"} --> als bewusste Organe beigeordnet und mit den mechanischen Organen\n der centralen Bewegungskraft untergeordnet. Der erstere Ausdruck\n gilt von jeder möglichen Anwendung der Maschinerie im Grossen, der\n andre charakterisirt ihre kapitalistische Anwendung und daher\ndas moderne Fabriksystem. Ure liebt es daher auch die Central-\n maschine, von der die Bewegung ausgeht, nicht nur als Automat, son-\n dern als Autokrat darzustellen. „In diesen grossen Werkstätten ver-\n sammelt die wohlthätige Macht des Dampfes ihre Myriaden von Unter-\n thanen um sich“ [Note: Ure l. c. t. I, p. 19, 20.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0086","text":"Mit dem Arbeitswerkzeug geht auch die Virtuosität in seiner Führung\n vom Arbeiter auf die Maschine über. Die Leistungsfähigkeit des Werkzeugs\n ist emancipirt von den persönlichen Schranken menschlicher Arbeitskraft.\n Damit ist die technologische Grundlage aufgehoben, wo-\n rauf die Theilung der Arbeit in der Manufaktur beruht.\n An die Stelle der sie charakterisirenden Hierarchie der spezialisirten\n Arbeiter tritt daher in der automatischen Fabrik die Tendenz der\n Gleichmachung oder Nivellirung der Arbeiten, welche die\n Gehilfen der Maschinerie zu verrichten haben [Note: l. c. p. 31. Vgl. Karl Marx l. c. p. 140, 141.] , an die Stelle der künst-\n lich erzeugten Unterschiede der Theilarbeiter treten vorwiegend die natür-\n lichen Unterschiede des Alters und Geschlechts.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0087","text":"Soweit in der automatischen Fabrik die Theilung der Arbeit\n wiedererscheint, ist sie zunächst Vertheilung von Arbeitern\n unter die spezialisirten Maschinen, und von Arbeitermassen,\n die jedoch keine kombinirten Gruppen bilden, unter die verschiedenen Depar-\n tements der Fabrik, wo sie an neben einander gereihten gleichartigen Werk-\n zeugmaschinen arbeiten, also nur einfache Cooperation unter ihnen\n stattfindet. Die kombinirte Gruppe der Manufaktur ist ersetzt durch den\n Zusammenhang des Hauptarbeiters mit wenigen Gehilfen. Die wesent-\n liche Scheidung ist die von Arbeitern, die wirklich an den Werkzeugma-\n schinen beschäftigt sind (es kommen hiezu einige Arbeiter zur Bewachung,\n resp. Fütterung der Bewegungsmaschine) und von blossen Hand-\n langern (fast ausschliesslich Kinder) dieser Maschinenarbeiter. Zu\n den Handlangern zählen mehr oder minder alle „feeders“ (die den Ma-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"412\", \"facs\": \"#f0431\"} --> schinen bloss Arbeitsstoff darreichen). Neben diese Hauptklassen tritt\n ein numerisch unbedeutendes Personal, das mit der Kontrole der gesamm-\n ten Maschinerie und ihrer beständigen Reparatur beschäftigt ist, wie In-\n genieure, Mechaniker, Schreiner u. s. w. Es ist eine höhere, theils wissen-\n schaftlich gebildete, theils handwerksmässige Arbeiterklasse, ausserhalb\n des Kreises der Fabrikarbeiter und ihnen nur aggregirt [Note: Es ist charakteristisch für die Absicht des statistischen Betrugs, die auch\n sonst noch im Detail nachweisbar wäre, wenn die englische Fabrikgesetzgebung\n die zuletzt im Text erwähnten Arbeiter ausdrücklich als Nicht-Fabrikarbei-\n ter von ihrem Wirkungskreis ausschliesst, andrerseits die vom Parlament ver-\n öffentlichten „Returns“ ebenso ausdrücklich nicht nur Ingenieure, Mechaniker\n u. s. w., sondern auch Fabrikdirigenten, Commis, Ausläufer, Lageraufseher, Ver-\n packer u. s. w., kurz alle Leute, mit Ausschluss des Fabrikeigenthümers selbst,\n in die Kategorie der Fabrikarbeiter einschliesst.] . Diese Thei-\n lung der Arbeit ist rein technologisch.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0088","text":"Alle Arbeit an der Maschine erfordert frühzeitigen Einbruch des Ar-\n beiters, damit er seine eigne Bewegung der gleichförmig kontinuirlichen\n Bewegung eines Automaten anpassen lerne. Soweit die Gesammtmaschi-\n nerie selbst ein System verschiedenartiger, gleichzeitig wirkender\n und kombinirter Maschinen bildet, erfordert die auf ihr beruhende Coope-\n ration nicht minder Vertheilung besondrer Arbeiter unter die beson-\n derten Maschinen. Aber der Maschinenbetrieb hebt die Nothwendigkeit auf\n diese Vertheilung manufakturmässig zu befestigen durch fortwährende\n Aneignung derselben Arbeiter an dieselbe Funktion [Note: Ure giebt diess zu. Er sagt, dass die Arbeiter „im Nothfall“ nach dem\n Willen des Dirigenten von einer Maschine zur andern versetzt werden können und\n ruft triumphirend aus: „Dergleichen Wechsel steht im offnen Widerspruch mit der\n alten Routine, die die Arbeit theilt und dem einen Arbeiter die Aufgabe zuweist,\n den Kopf einer Stecknadel zu façonniren, dem andern ihre Spitze zu schleifen.“\n Er hätte sich vielmehr fragen sollen, warum diese „alte Routine“ in der auto-\n matischen Fabrik nur „im Nothfall“ verlassen wird?] . Da die Ge-\n sammtbewegung der Fabrik nicht vom Arbeiter ausgeht, sondern von\n der Maschine, kann fortwährender Personenwechsel stattfinden ohne\n Unterbrechung des Arbeitsprozesses. Den schlagendsten Beweis hierzu\n liefert das während der englischen Fabrikantenrevolte von 1848—50 ins\n Werk gesetzte Relaissystem. Die Geschwindigkeit endlich, womit die\n Arbeit an der Maschine im jugendlichen Alter erlernt wird, beseitigt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"413\", \"facs\": \"#f0432\"} --> ebenso die Nothwendigkeit, eine besondre Klasse Arbeiter ausschliesslich\n zu Maschinenarbeitern zu machen [Note: Wenn Noth an Mann ist, wie z. B. während des amerikanischen Bürger-\n kriegs, wird der Fabrikarbeiter ausnahmsweis vom Bourgeois zu den gröbsten Ar-\n beiten, wie Strassenbau u. s. w. verwandt. Die englischen „atéliers nationaux“\n des Jahres 1862 sq. für die beschäftigungslosen Baumwollarbeiter unterschieden\n sich dadurch von den französischen von 1848, dass in diesen der Arbeiter auf\n Kosten des Staats unproduktive Arbeiten, in jenen zum Vortheil des Bourgeois\n produktive städtische Arbeiten und zwar wohlfeiler als die regelmässigen Ar-\n beiter, mit denen er so in Konkurrenz geworfen ward, zu verrichten hatte. „The\n physical appearance of the cotton operatives is unquestionably improved. This\n I attribute … as to the men, to outdoor labour on public works.“ (Es\n handelt sich hier von den Preston-Fabrikarbeitern, die am „Preston Moor“ be-\n schäftigt wurden.) „Rep. of Insp. of Fact. Oct. 1865“, p. 59.)] . Die Dienste der blossen Hand-\n langer aber sind in der Fabrik grossentheils durch Maschinen ersetzbar [Note: Beispiel: Die verschiednen mechanischen Apparate, die zum Ersatz von\n Kinderarbeit seit dem Gesetz von 1844 in der Wollfabrik eingeführt wurden. So-\n bald die Kinder der Herrn Fabrikanten selbst ihre „Schule“ als Handlanger der\n Fabrik durchzumachen haben, wird diess fast noch unangebaute Gebiet der Mecha-\n nik bald einen merkwürdigen Aufschwung nehmen.] ,\n theils erlauben sie andrerseits wegen ihrer völligen Einfachheit raschen und\n beständigen Wechsel der mit dieser Plackerei belasteten Personen.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0089","text":"Obgleich nun die Maschinerie das alte System der Theilung der Ar-\n beit technologisch über den Haufen wirft, schleppt es sich zunächst als\n Tradition der Manufaktur gewohnheitsmässig in der Fabrik fort, um dann\nsystematisch vom Kapital als Exploitationsmittel der Arbeitskraft in\n noch ekelhafterer Form reproducirt und befestigt zu werden. Aus der lebens-\n langen Specialität ein Theilwerkzeug zu führen, wird die lebenslange\n Specialität einer Theilmaschine zu dienen. Die Maschinerie wird miss-\n braucht, um den Arbeiter selbst von Kindesbeinen in den Theil einer Theil-\n maschine zu verwandeln [Note: Man würdigt daher den fabelhaften Einfall Proudhon’s, der die\n Maschinerie nicht als Synthese von Arbeitsmitteln, sondern als Synthese von Theil-\n arbeiten für die Arbeiter selbst — „konstruirt“.] . Nicht nur werden so die zu seiner eignen\n Reproduktion nöthigen Kosten bedeutend vermindert, sondern zugleich\n seine hilflose Abhängigkeit vom Fabrikganzen, also vom Kapitalisten, voll-\n endet. Hier wie überall muss man unterscheiden zwischen der grössern\n Produktivität, die der Entwicklung des gesellschaftlichen Produktions-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"414\", \"facs\": \"#f0433\"} --> prozesses, und der grössern Produktivität, die seiner kapitalistischen Aus-\n beutung geschuldet ist.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0090","text":"In Manufaktur und Handwerk bedient sich der Arbeiter des Werk-\n zeugs, in der Fabrik dient er der Maschine. Dort geht von ihm die Be-\n wegung des Arbeitsmittels aus, dessen Bewegung er hier zu folgen hat.\n In der Manufaktur bilden die Arbeiter Glieder eines lebendigen Mechanis-\n mus. In der Fabrik existirt ein todter Mechanismus unabhängig von ihnen\n und sie werden ihm als lebendige Anhängsel einverleibt. „Der trübselige\n Schlendrian einer endlosen Arbeitsqual, worin derselbe mechanische Pro-\n zess immer wieder durchgemacht wird, gleicht der Arbeit des Sisyphus;\n die Last der Arbeit, gleich dem Felsen, fällt immer wieder auf den abge-\n matteten Arbeiter zurück“ [Note: F. Engels l. c. p. 217. Selbst ein ganz ordinärer, optimistischer Frei-\n händler, Herr Molinari, bemerkt: „Un homme s’use plus vite en surveillant,\n quinze heurs par jour l’évolution uniforme d’un mécanisme, qu’en exerçant dans\n le même espace de temps, sa force physique. Ce travail de surveillance, qui\n servirait peut-être d’utile gymnastique à l’intelligence, s’il n’était pas trop prolongé,\n détruit à la longue, par son excès, et l’intelligence et le corps même.“ (G. de\n Molinari: „Études Économiques.“ Paris 1846.)] . Während die Maschinenarbeit das Ner-\n vensystem aufs äusserste angreift, unterdrückt sie das vielseitige Spiel der\n Muskeln und konfiscirt alle freie körperliche und geistige Thätigkeit [Note: F. Engels l. c. p. 216.] .\n Selbst die Erleichterung der Arbeit wird zum Mittel der Tortur, indem die\n Maschine nicht den Arbeiter von der Arbeit befreit, sondern seine Arbeit\n vom Inhalt. Aller kapitalistischen Produktion, soweit sie nicht nur Ar-\n beitsprozess, sondern zugleich Verwerthungsprozess des Ka-\n pitals, ist es gemeinsam, dass nicht der Arbeiter die Arbeitsbedingung,\n sondern umgekehrt die Arbeitsbedingung den Arbeiter anwendet, aber erst\n mit der Maschinerie erhält diese Verkehrung technologisch hand-\n greifliche Wirklichkeit. Durch seine Verwandlung in einen Auto-\n maten tritt das Arbeitsmittel während des Arbeitsprozesses selbst dem\n Arbeiter als Kapital gegenüber, als todte Arbeit, welche die lebendige\n Arbeitskraft beherrscht und aussaugt. Die Scheidung der geistigen\n Potenzen des Produktionsprozesses von der Handarbeit und die Ver-\n wandlung derselben in Mächte des Kapitals über die Arbeit\n vollendet sich, wie bereits früher angedeutet, in der auf Grundlage der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"415\", \"facs\": \"#f0434\"} --> Maschinerie aufgebauten grossen Industrie. Das Detailgeschick des indi-\n viduellen, entleerten Maschinenarbeiters verschwindet als ein winzig Neben-\n ding vor der Wissenschaft, den ungeheuren Naturkräften und der gesell-\n schaftlichen Massenarbeit, die im Maschinensystem verkörpert sind und mit\n ihm die Macht „des Meisters“ bilden. Dieser Meister, in dessen Hirn\n die Maschinerie und sein Monopol an derselben unzertrennlich verwachsen\n sind, ruft daher in Kollisionsfällen den „Händen“ verächtlich zu: „Die\n Fabrikarbeiter sollten in heilsamer Erinnerung halten, dass ihre Arbeit in\n der That eine sehr niedrige Sorte geschickter Arbeit ist; dass keine leich-\n ter aneigenbar und in Anbetracht ihrer Qualität besser belohnt ist, dass\n keine durch kurze Unterweisung des mindest Erfahrnen in so kurzer Zeit\n und in solchem Ueberfluss zugeführt werden kann. Des Meisters\n Maschinerie spielt in der That eine viel wichtigere Rolle in dem Ge-\n schäft der Produktion als die Arbeit und das Geschick des Arbei-\n ters, die eine Erziehung von 6 Monaten lehren und jeder Bauernknecht\n lernen kann“ [Note: „The factory operatives should keep in wholesome remembrance the\n fact that theirs is really a low species of skilled labour; and that there is none\n which is more easily acquired or of its quality more amply remunerated, or which,\n by a short training of the least expert can be more quickly as well as abundantly\n acquired . . . . The master’s machinery really plays a far more im-\n portant part in the business of production than the labour and\n the skill of the operative, which six months’ education can teach, and a\n common labourer can learn.“ („The Master Spinners’ and Manufac-\n turers’ Defence Fund. Report of the Committee. Manchester\n 1854“, p. 17.) Man wird später sehn, dass der „Master“ aus einem andern Loch\n pfeift, sobald er mit Verlust seiner „lebendigen“ Automaten bedroht ist.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0091","text":"Die technologische Unterordnung des Arbeiters unter den gleichför-\n migen Gang des Arbeitsmittels und die eigenthümliche Zusammensetzung\n des Arbeitskörpers aus Individuen beider Geschlechter und aller Alters-\n stufen schafft eine kasernenmässige Disciplin, die sich zum vollständigen\nFabrikregime ausbildet und die schon früher erwähnte Arbeit\n der Oberaufsicht, damit zugleich die Theilung der Arbeiter\n in Handarbeiter und Arbeitsaufseher, gemeine Industriesoldaten und In-\n dustrieunterofficiere, völlig entwickelt. „Die Hauptschwierigkeit in der auto-\n matischen Fabrik bestand in der nothwendigen Disciplin, um die Menschen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"416\", \"facs\": \"#f0435\"} --> auf ihre unregelmässigen Gewohnheiten in der Arbeit verzichten zu machen,\n und sie zu identificiren mit der unveränderlichen Regel-\n mässigkeit des grossen Automaten. Aber einen den Bedürf-\n nissen und der Geschwindigkeit des automatischen Systems entsprechen-\n den Disciplinarcodex zu erfinden und mit Erfolg auszuführen, war ein Unter-\n nehmen des Herkules würdig, das ist das edle Werk Arkwright’s!\n Selbst heut zu Tage, wo das System in seiner ganzen Vollendung organi-\n sirt ist, ist es fast unmöglich unter den Arbeitern, die das Alter der\n Mannbarkeit zurückgelegt haben, nützliche Gehilfen für das automatische\n System zu finden“ [Note: Ure l. c. p. 22, 23. Wer Arkwright’s Lebensgeschichte kennt, wird\n das Wort „edel“ diesem genialen Barbier nie an den Kopf werfen. Von allen\n grossen Erfindern des 18. Jahrhunderts war er unstreitig der grösste Dieb fremder\n Erfindungen und der gemeinste Kerl.] . Der Fabrikcodex, worin das Kapital seine Auto-\n kratie über seine Arbeiter, ohne die sonst vom Bürgerthum so beliebte\n Theilung der Gewalten und das noch beliebtere Repräsentativsystem, pri-\n vatgesetzlich und eigenherrlich formulirt, ist nur die kapitalistische\n Karrikatur der gesellschaftlichen Reglung des Arbeits-\n prozesses, welche mit der Cooperation auf grosser Stufenleiter und der\n Anwendung gemeinsamer Arbeitsmittel, wie namentlich der Maschinerie,\n nöthig wird. An die Stelle der Peitsche des Sklaventreibers tritt das Straf-\n buch des Aufsehers. Alle Strafen lösen sich natürlich auf in Geldstrafen\n und Lohnabzüge und der gesetzgeberische Scharfsinn der Fabrik-Lykurge\n macht ihnen die Verletzung ihrer Gesetze wo möglich noch einbringlicher\n als deren Befolgung [Note: „Die Sklaverei, in der die Bourgeoisie das Proletariat gefesselt hält,\n kommt nirgends deutlicher ans Tageslicht, als im Fabriksystem. Hier hört alle\n Freiheit rechtlich und faktisch auf. Der Arbeiter muss Morgens um halb 6 in der\n Fabrik sein; kommt er ein paar Minuten zu spät, so wird er gestraft, kommt er\n 10 Minuten zu spät, so wird er gar nicht hereingelassen, bis das Frühstück vorüber\n ist, und verliert einen Vierteltag am Lohn. Er muss auf Kommando essen, trinken\n und schlafen … Die despotische Glocke ruft ihn vom Bette, ruft ihn vom Früh-\n stück und Mittagstisch. Und wie geht es nun gar erst in der Fabrik? Hier ist\n der Fabrikant absoluter Gesetzgeber. Er erlässt Fabrikregulationen, wie er Lust\n hat; er ändert und macht Zusätze zu seinem Codex, wie es ihm beliebt; und wenn\n er das tollste Zeug hineinsetzt, so sagen doch die Gerichte zum Arbeiter: Da ihr\n unter diesen Kontrakt euch freiwillig begeben habt, jetzt müsst ihr ihn auch be-] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0092","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"417\", \"facs\": \"#f0436\"} -->","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0093","text":"Wir deuten nur hin auf die materiellen Bedingungen, unter denen\n die Fabrikarbeit verrichtet wird. Alle Sinnesorgane werden gleichmässig\n [Note: folgen … Diese Arbeiter sind dazu verdammt, vom neunten Jahr bis zu ihrem\n Tod unter der geistigen und körperlichen Fuchtel zu leben.“ (F. Engels l. c.\n p. 217 sq.) Was „die Gerichte sagen“, will ich an zwei Beispielen erläutern. Der\n eine Fall spielt in Sheffield, Ende 1866. Dort hatte sich ein Arbeiter für 2\n Jahre in eine Metallfabrik verdingt. In Folge eines Zwistes mit dem Fabrikanten\n verliess er die Fabrik und erklärte unter keinen Umständen mehr für ihn arbeiten\n zu wollen. Wurde wegen Kontraktsbruch verklagt, zu zwei Monaten Geldbusse\n verurtheilt. (Bricht der Fabrikant den Kontrakt, so kann er nur civiliter verklagt\n werden und riskirt nur eine Geldbusse.) Nach Absitzen der zwei Monate stellt\n derselbe Fabrikant ihm Ladung zu, dem alten Kontrakt gemäss in die Fabrik zu-\n rückzukehren. Arbeiter erklärt, Nein. Den Kontraktsbruch habe er bereits ab-\n gebüsst. Fabrikant verklagt von neuem, Gericht verurtheilt von neuem, obgleich\n Einer der Richter, Mr. Shee, diess öffentlich als juristische Ungeheuerlichkeit\n denuncirt, wonach ein Mann sein ganzes Leben durch periodisch für dasselbe\n identische Vergehn, resp. Verbrechen, wieder und wieder bestraft werden könne.\n Diess Urtheil wurde gefällt nicht von dem „Great Unpaid“, provinzialen Dog-\n berries, sondern zu London, von einem der nöchsten Gerichtshöfe. — Der zweite\n Fall spielt in Wiltshire, Ende November 1863. Ungefähr 30 Dampfstuhl-\n weberinnen, in der Beschäftigung eines gewissen Harrupp, Tuchfabrikant von\n Leower’s Mill, Westbury Leigh, machten einen strike, weil dieser selbe Harrupp\n die angenehme Gewohnheit hatte, ihnen für Verspätung am Morgen Lohnabzug\n zu machen, und zwar 6 d. für 2 Minuten, 1 sh. für 3 Minuten, und 1 sh. 6 d. für\n 10 Minuten. Diess macht, bei 9 sh. per Stunde, 4 Pfd. St. 10 sh. per Tag, wäh-\n rend ihr Durchschnittslohn im Jahr nie über 10 bis 12 sh. wöchentlich steigt.\n Harrupp hat ebenfalls einen Jungen bestallt, um die Fabrikstunde zu blasen, was er\n selber manchmal vor 6 Morgens thut, und wenn die Hände nicht grade da sind, sobald\n er aufhört, werden die Thore geschlossen und die draussen in Geldbusse genommen;\n und da keine Uhr im Gebäude, sind die unglücklichen Hände in der Gewalt des\n von Harrupp inspirirten jugendlichen Zeitwächters. Die im „strike“ begriffenen\n Hände, Familienmütter und Mädchen, erklärten, sie wollten wieder ans Werk\n gehn, wenn der Zeitwächter durch eine Uhr ersetzt, und ein rationellerer Straftarif\n eingeführt würde. Harrupp citirte 19 Weiber und Mädchen vor die Magistrate,\n wegen Kontraktsbruch. Sie wurden verurtheilt zu je 6 d. Strafe und 2 sh. 6 d.\n Kosten, unter lauter Entrüstung des Auditoriums. Harrupp wurde vom Gericht\n weg von einer zischenden Volksmasse begleitet. — Eine Lieblingsoperation der\n Fabrikanten ist, die Arbeiter durch Lohnabzüge für die Fehler des ihnen gelieferten\n Materials zu züchtigen. Diese Methode rief 1866 allgemeinen strike in den eng-\n lischen Töpferdistrikten hervor. Die Berichte der „Ch. Employm.\n Commiss.“ (1863—1866) geben Fälle, wo der Arbeiter, statt Lohn zu erhalten,] \n [Running matter: I. 27] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"418\", \"facs\": \"#f0437\"} --> verletzt durch die künstlich gesteigerte Temperatur, die mit Abfällen des\n Rohmaterials geschwängerte Atmosphäre, den betäubenden Lärm u. s. w.,\n abgesehn von der Lebensgefahr unter dicht gehäufter Maschinerie, die mit\n der Regelmässigkeit der Jahreszeiten ihre industriellen Schlachtbülletins\n producirt. Die Oekonomisirung der gesellschaftlichen\n Produktionsmittel, erst im Fabriksystem treibhausmässig gereift,\n wird in der Hand des Kapitals zugleich zum systematischen Raub\n an den Lebensbedingungen des Arbeiters während der\n Arbeit, wie an Raum, Luft, Licht und persönlichen Schutzmitteln wider\n die lebensgefährlichen oder gesundheitswidrigen Umstände des Produk-\n tionsprozesses, von Vorrichtungen zur Bequemlichkeit des Arbeiters gar\n nicht zu sprechen [Note: Im Ersten Kapitel des Dritten Buchs werde ich berichten über einen\n jüngster Zeit angehörigen Feldzug der englischen Fabrikanten gegen die Klauseln\n des Fabrikakts zum Schutz der Gliedmassen der „Hände“ vor lebensgefährlicher\n Maschinerie. Hier genüge ein Citat aus einem officiellen Bericht des Fabrik-\n inspektor Leonhard Horner: „Ich habe Fabrikanten mit unentschuldbarer\n Frivolität von einigen der Unglücksfälle sprechen hören, z. B. der Verlust eines\n Fingers sei eine Kleinigkeit. Das Leben und die Aussichten eines Arbeiters hängen\n so sehr von seinen Fingern ab, dass ein solcher Verlust ein äusserst ernstes Ereig-\n niss für ihn ist. Wenn ich solch gedankenlos Geschwätz höre, stelle ich die Frage:\n Unterstellt, Sie brauchten einen zusätzlichen Arbeiter, und ihrer zwei meldeten] . Nennt Fourier mit Unrecht die Fabriken „gemäs-\n [Note: durch seine Arbeit, und vermittelst des Strafreglements, noch obendrein „Schuld-\n ner“ seines erlauchten „Master“ wird. Erbauliche Züge über den Lohnabzugs-\n Scharfsinn der Fabrikautokraten lieferte auch die jüngste Baumwollkrise. „Ich\n hatte selbst“, sagt Fabrikinspektor R. Baker, „vor kurzem gerichtliche Verfol-\n gung wider einen Baumwollfabrikanten einzuleiten, weil er in diesen schweren und\n qualvollen Zeitläuften 10 d. von einigen der von ihm beschäftigten „jungen“\n (mehr als dreizehnjährigen) Arbeiter abzog für das ärztliche Alterscertifikat, das\n ihm nur 6 d. kostet, und wofür das Gesetz nur einen Abzug von 3 d., das Her-\n kommen gar keinen erlaubt … Ein andrer Fabrikant, um denselben Zweck ohne\n Konflikt mit dem Gesetz zu erreichen, belastet jedes der armen Kinder, die für ihn\n arbeiten, mit einem Shilling als Sportel für Erlernung der Kunst und des Myste-\n riums zu spinnen, sobald das ärztliche Zeugniss sie reif für diese Beschäftigung er-\n klärt. Es existiren also Unterströmungen, die man kennen muss, um solche\n ausserordentliche Phänomene, wie strikes zu Zeiten wie die gegenwärtige (es\n handelt sich um einen strike in der Fabrik zu Darwen, Juni 1863, unter den Maschi-\n nenwebern) zu begreifen.“ Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 30th April\n 1863. (Die Fabrikberichte gehn immer weiter als ihr officielles Datum.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"419\", \"facs\": \"#f0438\"} --> sigte Bagnos“ [Note: In den Fabriken, die am längsten dem Fabrikakt, mit der Zwangsbe-\n schränkung der Arbeitszeit und seinen sonstigen Regulationen unterworfen sind,\n sind manche frühere Missstände verschwunden. Die Verbesserung der Maschinerie\n selbst erheischt auf einem gewissen Punkt eine „verbesserte Konstruktion\n der Fabrikgebäude“, die den Arbeitern zu gut kommt. (cf. Reports etc.\nfor 31st Oct. 1863, p. 109.)] ? — Der Kampf zwischen Kapitalist und Lohnarbeiter be-\n ginnt mit dem Kapitalverhältniss selbst. Er tobt fort während der ganzen\n Manufakturperiode [Note: Sieh u. a.: John Houghton: „Husbandry and Trade improv-\n ed. Lond. 1727“, „The Advantages of the East India Trade 1720“,\nJohn Bellers l. c. „The masters and the men are unhappily in a perpetual\n war with each other. The invariable object of the former is to get their work done\n as cheap as possibly; and they do not fail to employ every artifice to this purpose,\n whilst the latter are equally attentive to every occasion of distressing their masters\n into a compliance with higher demands.“ „An Inquiry into the causes\n of the Present High Prices of Provisions.“ (Verf. Rev. Mr. Na-\n thaniel Forster, ganz auf Seite der Arbeiter.)] . Erst seit der Einführung der Maschinerie\n bekämpft der Arbeiter das Arbeitsmittel selbst, die materielle Exi-\n stenzweise des Kapitals. Er revoltirt gegen diese bestimmte Form\n des Produktionsmittels als die materielle Grundlage der kapi-\n talistischen Produktionsweise.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0094","text":"Ziemlich ganz Europa erlebte während des 17. Jahrhunderts Ar-\n beiterrevolten gegen die s. g. Bandmühle (auch Schnurmühle oder\n Mühlenstuhl genannt), eine Maschine zum Weben von Bändern und Bor-\n ten [Note: Die Bandmühle ward in Deutschland erfunden. Der italienische Abbé\n Lancellotti in einer Schrift, die 1636 zu Venedig erschien, erzählt: „Anton\n Müller aus Danzig habe vor ungefähr 50 Jahren (L. schrieb 1579) eine sehr künst-\n liche Maschine in Danzig gesehn, die 4—6 Gewebe auf einmal verfertigte; weil\n der Stadtrath aber besorgt habe, diese Erfindung möchte eine Masse Arbeiter zu\n Bettlern machen, so habe er die Erfindung unterdrückt und den Erfinder heimlich] . Ende des ersten Dritttheils des 17. Jahrhunderts erlag eine\n [Note: sich, beide in jeder andern Hinsicht gleich tüchtig, aber der Eine ohne Daumen\n oder Vorfinger, welchen würden Sie wählen? Sie zögerten nie einen Augenblick\n für den Vollfingrigen zu entscheiden. . . . Diese Herrn Fabrikanten haben falsche\n Vorurtheile gegen was sie pseudo-philanthropische Gesetzgebung\n nennen.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1855.“) Diese\n Herrn Fabrikanten sind „gescheidte Leut’“ und schwärmten nicht umsonst für die\n Sklavenhalter-Rebellion!] \n [Running matter: 27*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"420\", \"facs\": \"#f0439\"} --> Windsägemühle, von einem Holländer in der Nähe Londons angelegt, vor\n Pöbelexcessen. Noch Anfang des 18. Jahrhunderts überwanden durch\n Wasser getriebne Sägemaschinen in England nur mühsam den parlamen-\n tarisch unterstützten Volkswiderstand. Als Everet 1758 die erste vom\n Wasser getriebene Maschine zum Wollscheeren erbaut hatte, wurde sie\n von 100,000 ausser Arbeit gesetzten Menschen in Brand gesteckt. Gegen\n die scribbling mills und Kardirmaschinen Arkwright’s petitionirten 50,000\n Arbeiter, die bisher vom Wollkratzen gelebt, beim Parlament. Die mas-\n senhafte Zerstörung von Maschinen in den englischen Manufaktur-\n distrikten während der ersten 15 Jahre des 19. Jahrhunderts, namentlich\n in Folge der Ausbeutung des Dampfwebstuhls, bot, unter dem Namen\n der Ludditenbewegung, der Antijakobiner-Regierung eines Sid-\n mouth, Castlereagh u. s. w. den Vorwand zu reaktionärsten Gewalt-\n schritten. Es bedarf Zeit und Erfahrung, bevor der Arbeiter die Maschi-\n nerie von ihrer kapitalistischen Anwendung unterscheiden und\n [Note: ersticken oder ersäufen lassen.“ In Leyden wurde dieselbe Maschine zuerst 1629\n angewandt. Die Emeuten der Bortenwirker zwangen den Magistrat erst zu ihrem\n Verbot; durch verschiedne Verordnungen von 1623, 1639 u. s. w. von Seiten der\n Generalstaaten sollte ihr Gebrauch beschränkt werden, endlich erlaubt, unter ge-\n wissen Bedingungen, durch Verordnung vom 15. December 1661. „In hac urbe“,\n sagt Boxhorn („Inst. Pol. 1663“) von der Einführung der Bandmühle in\n Leyden, „ante hos viginti circiter annos instrumentum quidam invenerunt texto-\n rium, quo solus quis plus panni et facilius conficere poterat, quam plures\n aequali tempore. Hinc turbae ortae et querulae textorum, tandemque usus\n hujus instrumenti a magistratu prohibitus est.“ Dieselbe Maschine ward 1676 in\n Köln verboten, während ihre Einführung in England gleichzeitige Arbeiterunruhen\n hervorrief. Durch kaiserliches Edikt vom 19. Februar 1685 wurde ihr Gebrauch\n in ganz Deutschland untersagt. In Hamburg wurde sie öffentlich auf Befehl des\n Magistrats verbrannt. Karl VI. erneuerte 9. Februar 1719 das Edikt von 1685\n und Chursachsen erlaubte ihren öffentlichen Gebrauch erst 1765. Diese Maschine,\n die so viel Lärm in der Welt gemacht hat, war in der That Vorläufer der Spinn-\n und Webmaschinen, also der industriellen Revolution des 18. Jahrhunderts. Sie\n befähigte einen in der Weberei ganz unerfahrenen Jungen durch blosses Ab- und\n Zustossen einer Treibstange den ganzen Stuhl mit allen seinen Schützen in Be-\n wegung zu setzen und lieferte, in ihrer verbesserten Form, 40—50 Stück auf\n einmal.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"421\", \"facs\": \"#f0440\"} --> daher seine Angriffe vom materiellen Produktionsmittel selbst auf\n dessen gesellschaftliche Exploitationsform übertragen lernt [Note: In altmodischen Manufakturen wiederholt sich noch heut zuweilen die\n rohe Form der Arbeiterempörung gegen die Maschinerie. So z. B. im file grind-\n ing zu Sheffield 1865.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0095","text":"Die Kämpfe um den Arbeitslohn innerhalb der Manufaktur setzen die\n Manufaktur voraus und sind keineswegs gegen ihre Existenz gerichtet.\n So weit die Bildung der Manufakturen bekämpft wird, geschieht es von\n den Zunftmeistern und privilegirten Städten, nicht von den Lohnarbeitern.\n Bei Schriftstellern der Manufakturperiode wird die Theilung der Ar-\n beit daher vorherrschend als Mittel aufgefasst virtuell Arbeiter zu er-\n setzen, aber nicht wirklich Arbeiter zu verdrängen. Dieser Unterschied\n ist selbstverständlich. Sagt man z. B., es würden 100 Millionen Menschen\n in England erheischt sein um mit dem alten Spinnrad die Baumwolle zu\n verspinnen, die jetzt von 500,000 mit der Maschine versponnen wird, so\n heisst das natürlich nicht, dass die Maschine den Platz dieser Millionen,\n die nie existirt haben, einnahm. Es heisst nur, dass viele Millionen Arbeiter\n erheischt wären, um die Spinnmaschinerie zu ersetzen. Sagt man\n dagegen, dass der Dampfwebstuhl in England 800,000 Weber auf das\n Pflaster warf, so spricht man nicht von existirender Maschinerie, die durch\n eine bestimmte Arbeiterzahl ersetzt werden müsste, sondern von einer exi-\n stirenden Arbeiterzahl, die faktisch durch Maschinerie ersetzt oder ver-\n drängt worden ist. Während der Manufakturperiode blieb der handwerks-\n mässige Betrieb, wenn auch zerlegt, die Grundlage. Die neuen Kolonialmärkte\n konnten durch die relativ schwache Anzahl der vom Mittelalter überliefer-\n ten städtischen Arbeiter nicht befriedigt werden und die eigentlichen Ma-\n nufakturen öffneten zugleich dem mit Auflösung der Feudalität von Grund\n und Boden verjagten Landvolke neue Produktionsgebiete. Damals trat also\n an der Theilung der Arbeit und der Cooperation in den Werkstätten mehr\n die positive Seite hervor, dass sie beschäftigte Arbeiter produktiver\n machen [Note: Sir James Steuart fasst auch die Wirkung der Maschinerie noch ganz\n in diesem Sinn. „Je considère donc les machines comme des moyens d’augmenter\n (virtuellement) le nombre des gens industrieux qu’on n’est pas obligé de nourrir\n … En quoi l’effet d’une machine differe-t-il de celui de nouveaux habitants?“] . Cooperation und Kombination der Arbeitsmittel in den Hän-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"422\", \"facs\": \"#f0441\"} --> den Weniger rufen, auf die Agrikultur angewandt, zwar grosse, plötzliche\n und gewaltsame Revolutionen der Produktionsweise und daher der\n Lebensbedingungen und Beschäftigungsmittel der Landbevölkerung her-\n vor, die zum Theil der Periode der grossen Industrie lang vorhergehn.\n Aber dieser Kampf spielt ursprünglich mehr zwischen grossen und kleinen\n Landeigenthümern als zwischen Kapital und Lohnarbeit; andrerseits, so-\n weit Arbeiter durch Arbeitsmittel, Schafe, Pferde u. s. w. verdrängt wer-\n den, bilden unmittelbare Gewaltakte hier in erster Instanz die Voraus-\n setzung der industriellen Revolution. Erst werden die Arbeiter vom\n Grund und Boden verjagt, und dann kommen die Schafe. Der Landdieb-\n stahl auf grosser Stufenleiter, wie in England, schafft der grossen Agri-\n kultur erst ihr Anwendungsfeld. In ihren Anfängen hat diese Umwäl-\n zung der Agrikultur daher mehr den Schein einer politischen Re-\n volution.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0096","text":"Als Maschine wird das Arbeitsmittel sofort zum Konkurrenten\n des Arbeiters selbst [Note: „Machinery and Labour are in constant competition.“ (Ricardo l. c.\n p. 479.)] . Die Selbstverwerthung des Kapitals durch die\n Maschine steht im direkten Verhältniss zur Arbeiterzahl, deren Existenz-\n bedingungen sie vernichtet. Das ganze System der kapitalistischen Pro-\n duktion beruht darauf, dass der Arbeiter seine Arbeitskraft als Waare ver-\n kauft. Die Theilung der Arbeit vereinseitigt diese Arbeitskraft zum ganz\n partikularisirten Geschick ein Theilwerkzeug zu führen. Sobald die Füh-\n rung des Werkzeugs der Maschine anheimfällt, erlischt mit dem Gebrauchs-\n werth der Tauschwerth der Arbeitskraft. Der Arbeiter wird unverkäuf-\n lich, wie ausser Kurs gesetztes Papiergeld. Der Theil der Arbeiterklasse, den\n [Note: (Fzs. Uebers. t. I, l. I, ch. XIX.) Viel naiver Petty, der sagt, dass sie die\n „Polygamie“ ersetze. Dieser Gesichtspunkt passt höchstens für einige Theile\n der Ver. Staaten. Dagegen: „Machinery can seldom be used with success to\n abridge the labour of an individual; more time would be lost in its construction\n than could be saved by its application. It is only really useful when it acts on\n great masses, when a single machine can assist the work of thousands. It is\n accordingly in the most populous countries, where there are most idle men, that it\n is most abundant … It is not called into use by a scarcity of men, but\n by the facility with which they can be brought to work in\n masses.“ (Piercy Ravenstone: Thoughts on the Funding\n System and its Effects. Lond. 1824, p. 45.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"423\", \"facs\": \"#f0442\"} --> die Maschinerie so in überflüssige, d. h. nicht länger zur Selbst-\n verwerthung des Kapitals unmittelbar nothwendige Bevölke-\n rung verwandelt, geht einerseits unter in dem ungleichen Kampf des alten\n handwerksmässigen und manufakturmässigen Betriebs wider den maschinen-\n mässigen, überfluthet andrerseits alle leichter zugänglichen Industriezweige,\n überfüllt den Arbeitsmarkt und senkt daher den Preis der Arbeitskraft unter\n ihren Werth. Ein grosser Trost für die pauperisirten Arbeiter soll sein, dass\n ihre Leiden theils nur „zeitlich“ („a temporary inconvenience“), theils\n dass die Maschinerie sich nur allmälig eines ganzen Produktionsfelds be-\n mächtigt, wodurch Umfang und Intensivität ihrer vernichtenden Wirkung\n gebrochen werde. Der eine Trost schlägt den andern. Wo die Maschine\n allmälig ein Produktionsfeld ergreift, producirt sie chronisches Elend in der\n mit ihr konkurrirenden Arbeiterschichte. Wo der Uebergang rasch, wirkt sie\n massenhaft und akut. Die Weltgeschichte bietet kein entsetzlicheres Schau-\n spiel als den allmäligen, über Decennien verschleppten, endlich 1838 besie-\n gelten Untergang der englischen Handbaumwollweber. Viele von ihnen star-\n ben am Hungertod, viele vegetirten lange mit ihren Familien auf 2½ d.\n taglich [Note: Die Konkurrenz zwischen Handgeweb und Maschinengeweb wurde in\n England, vor der Einführung des Armengesetzes von 1833, dadurch verlängert,\n dass man die tief unter das Minimum gefallenen Löhne durch Pfarreiunterstützung\n ergänzte. „The Rev. Mr. Turner was in 1827 rector of Wilmstone, in Che-\n shire, a manufacturing district. The questions of the Committee of Emigration,\n and Mr. Turner’s answers show how the competition of human labour is maintained\n against machinery. Question: ‚Has not the use of the power-loom superseded\n the use of the hand-loom?‘ Answer: ‚Undoubtedly; it would have superseded\n them much more than it has done, if the hand-loom weavers were not enabled to\n submit to a reduction of wages.‘ Question: ‚But in submitting he has accepted\n wages which are insufficient to support him, and looks to parochial contribution\n as the remainder of his support?‘ Answer: ‚Yes, and in fact the competition\n between the hand-loom and the power-loom is maintened out of the poor-rates.‘\n Thus degrading pauperism or expatriation, is the benefit which the industrious\n receive from the introduction of machinery, to be reduced from the respectable\n and in some degree independent mechanic, to the cringing wretch who lives on\n the debasing bread of charity. This they call a temporary inconvenience.“\n („A Prize Essay on the comparative merits of Competition and\n Cooperation. Lond. 1834“, p. 29.)] . Akut dagegen wirkte die englische Baumwollmaschinerie auf\n Ostindien, dessen Generalgouverneur 1834—35 konstatirte: „Das Elend\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"424\", \"facs\": \"#f0443\"} --> findet kaum eine Parallele in der Geschichte des Handels. Die Kno-\n chen der Baumwollweber bleichen die Ebenen von In-\n dien.“ Allerdings, sofern diese Weber das Zeitliche segneten, bereitete\n ihnen die Maschine nur „zeitliche Missstände“. Uebrigens ist die „zeit-\n liche“ Wirkung der Maschinerie permanent, indem sie beständig neue\n Produktionsgebiete ergreift. Die verselbstständigte und entfremdete Gestalt,\n welche die kapitalistische Produktionsweise überhaupt den Arbeitsbedin-\n gungen und dem Arbeitsprodukt gegenüber dem Arbeiter giebt, entwickelt\n sich also mit der Maschinerie zum vollständigen Gegensatz [Note: „The same cause which may increase the revenue of the coun-\n try (d. h., wie Ricardo an derselben Stelle erläutert, the revenues of land-\n lords and capitalists, deren Wealth, ökonomisch betrachtet, überhaupt =\n Wealth of the Nation) may at the same time render the population redun-\n dant and deteriorate the condition of the labourer.“ (Ricardo\n l. c. p. 469.) „Der beständige Zweck und die Tendenz jeder Vervollkommnung\n des Mechanismus ist in der That sich der Arbeit des Menschen ganz zu entschlagen\n oder ihren Preis zu vermindern durch Substitution von Weiber- und Kinder-\n arbeit für die der erwachsnen männlichen Arbeiter, oder roher Arbeiter für ge-\n schickte.“ (Ure l. c. t. I, p. 35.)] . Daher\n mit ihr zum erstenmal die brutale Revolte des Arbeiters gegen das Arbeits-\n mittel.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0097","text":"Das Arbeitsmittel erschlägt den Arbeiter. Dieser\n direkte Gegensatz erscheint allerdings am handgreiflichsten, so oft neu\n eingeführte Maschinerie konkurrirt mit überliefertem Handwerks- oder\n Manufakturbetrieb. Aber innerhalb der grossen Industrie selbst wirkt\n fortwährende Verbesserung der Maschinerie und Entwicklung des automa-\n tischen Systems analog „Der beständige Zweck verbesserter Maschinerie\n ist die Handarbeit zu vermindern oder einen Ring in der Produktionskette\n der Fabrik durch Substitution eiserner für menschliche Apparate zu voll-\n enden“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1858“, p. 43.] . „Die Anwendung von Dampf- und Wasserkraft auf Maschi-\n nerie, die bisher mit der Hand bewegt wurde, ist das Ereigniss jeden Ta-\n ges … Die kleineren Verbesserungen in der Maschinerie, welche Oekonomie\n der Bewegungskraft, Verbesserung des Machwerks, vermehrte Produktion\n in derselben Zeit oder Verdrängung eines Kindes, eines Frauenzimmers\n oder eines Mannes bezwecken, sind constant, und obgleich scheinbar\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"425\", \"facs\": \"#f0444\"} --> nicht von grossem Gewicht, haben sie dennoch wichtige Resultate“ [Note: „Reports etc. 31. Oct. 1856“, p. 15.] .\n „Ueberall, wo eine Operation viel Geschick und eine sichre Hand verlangt,\n entzieht man sie so schnell als möglich den Armen des zu geschickten\n und oft zu Unregelmässigkeiten aller Art geneigten Arbeiters, um einen\n besondern Mechanismus damit zu betrauen, der so gut geregelt ist, dass\n ein Kind ihn überwachen kann“ [Note: Ure l. c. t. I, p. 29. „Der grosse Vortheil der im Ziegelbacken an-\n gewandten Maschinerie besteht darin, den Anwender ganz und gar von geschickten\n Arbeitern unabhängig zu machen.“ („Ch. Empl. Comm. V. Report. Lond.\n 1866“, p. 180, n. 46.)] . „Im automatischen System wird\ndas Talent des Arbeiters progressiv verdrängt“ [Note: Ure l. c. p. 30.] . „Die\n Verbesserung der Maschinerie erfordert nicht nur Verminderung in der\n Anzahl der beschäftigten erwachsnen Arbeiter zur Erzielung eines bestimm-\n ten Resultats, sondern sie substituirt eine Klasse von Individuen einer an-\n dern Klasse, eine minder geschickte einer geschickteren, Kinder den Er-\n wachsnen, Frauen den Männern. Alle diese Wechsel verursachen be-\n ständige Fluktuationen in der Rate des Arbeitslohns“ [Note: l. c. t. II, p. 67.] .\n „Die Maschinerie wirft unaufhörlich Erwachsne aus der Fa-\n brik heraus“ [Note: l. c.] . Die ausserordentliche Elasticität des Ma-\n schinenwesens in Folge gehäufter praktischer Erfahrung, des schon\n vorhandnen Umfangs mechanischer Mittel, und des beständigen Fortschritts\n der Technologie, zeigte uns sein Sturmmarsch unter dem Druck eines ver-\n kürzten Arbeitstags. Aber wer hätte 1860, im Zenithjahr der englischen\n Baumwollindustrie, die galoppirenden Verbesserungen der Maschinerie und\n die entsprechende Deplacirung von Handarbeit geahnt, welche die drei fol-\n genden Jahre unter dem Stachel des amerikanischen Bürger-\n kriegs hervorriefen? Von den offiziellen Anführungen der englischen Fabrik-\n inspektoren über diesen Punkt genügen hier ein paar Beispiele. Ein Manchester\n Fabrikant erklärt: „Statt 75 Kardirmaschinen brauchen wir jetzt nur 12,\n welche dieselbe Quantität von ebenso guter, wenn nicht besserer Qualität\n liefern … Die Ersparung an Arbeitslohn beträgt 10 Pfd. St. wöchent-\n lich, die an Baumwollabfall 10 %.“ In einer Manchester Feinspinnerei\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"426\", \"facs\": \"#f0445\"} --> wurde „vermittelst beschleunigter Bewegung und Einführung verschiedner\n self-acting Prozesse in einem Departement ¼, in einem über ½ des Ar-\n beiterpersonals beseitigt, während die Kämmmaschine an der Stelle der\n zweiten Kardirmaschine die Zahl der früher im Kardirraum beschäftigten\n Hände sehr vermindert hat.“ Eine andre Spinnfabrik schätzt ihre allge-\n meine Ersparung von „Händen“ auf 10 %. Die Herren Gilmore,\n Spinner zu Manchester, erklären: „In unsrem blowing Departement\n schätzen wir die in Folge neuer Maschinerie gemachte Ersparung an Hän-\n den und Arbeitslohn auf ein volles Drittel … in dem jack frame und draw-\n ing frame room ungefähr ⅓ weniger in Auslage und Händen; im Spinn-\n raum ungefähr ⅓ weniger in Auslage. Aber das ist nicht alles; wenn\n unser Garn jetzt zum Weber geht, ist es so sehr verbessert durch die An-\n wendung der neuen Maschinerie, dass sie mehr und besseres Gewebe\n als mit dem alten Maschinengarn produciren“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1863“, p. 108 sqq.] . Fabrikinspektor\n A. Redgrave bemerkt hierzu: „Die Verminderung der Arbeiter bei gestei-\n gerter Produktion schreitet rasch vorwärts; in den Wollfabriken begann\n kürzlich eine neue Reduktion der Hände, und sie dauert fort; vor wenigen\n Tagen sagte mir ein Schulmeister, der bei Rochdale wohnt, die grosse Ab-\n nahme in den Mädchenschulen sei nicht nur dem Druck der Krise ge-\n schuldet, sondern auch den Aenderungen in der Maschinerie der Wollfabrik,\n in Folge deren eine Durchschnittsreduktion von 70 Halbzeitlern statt-\n gefunden“ [Note: l. c. p. 109. Die rasche Verbesserung der Maschinerie während der\n Baumwollkrise erlaubte den englischen Fabrikanten gleich nach Beendigung des\n amerikanischen Bürgerkriegs im Umsehn den Weltmarkt wieder zu überfüllen.\n Die Gewebe wurden schon während der letzten 6 Monate von 1866 fast unverkäuf-\n lich. Damit fing die Konsignation der Waaren nach China und Indien an, was\n den „glut“ natürlich noch intensiver machte. Anfang 1867 nahmen die Fabri-\n kanten zu ihrem gewöhnlichen Ausfluchtsmittel Zuflucht, Herabsetzung des Arbeits-\n lohns um 5 %. Die Arbeiter widersetzten sich und erklärten, theoretisch ganz\n richtig, das einzige Heilmittel sei, kurze Zeit, 4 Tage per Woche, zu arbeiten.\n Nach längerem Sträuben mussten die selbsternannten Industriekapitäne sich hierzu\n entschliessen, an einigen Stellen mit, an andern ohne Lohnherabsetzung\n um 5 %.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0098","text":"Die Maschinerie wirkt jedoch nicht nur als übermächtiger Konkur-\n rent, stets auf dem Sprung den Lohnarbeiter „überflüssig“ zu machen.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"427\", \"facs\": \"#f0446\"} --> Als ihm feindliche Potenz wird sie laut und tendenziell vom Kapital\n proklamirt und gehandhabt. Sie wird das machtvollste Kriegs-\n mittel zur Niederschlagung der periodischen Arbeiteraufstände, strikes\n u. s. w. wider die Autokratie des Kapitals [Note: „Das Verhältniss zwischen Meistern und Händen in den Flint- und\n Flaschenglas-Blasereien ist ein chronischer strike.“ Daher der Aufschwung der\n Manufaktur des gepressten Glases, wo die Hauptoperationen durch Maschinerie\n ausgeführt werden. Eine Firma bei Newcastle, die früher jährlich 350,000 ge-\n blasnes Flintglas producirte, producirt jetzt statt dessen 3,000,500 Pfund ge-\n presstes Glas. („Ch. Empl. Comm. IV. Rep. 1865“, p. 262, 263.)] . Nach Gaskell\n war gleich die Dampfmaschine ein Antagonist der „Menschenkraft“, der\n den Kapitalisten befähigte die steigenden Ansprüche der Arbeiter nieder-\n zuschmettern, die das beginnende Fabriksystem zur Krise zu treiben droh-\n ten [Note: Gaskell: „The Manufacturing Population of England.\n Lond. 1833“, p. 3, 4.] . Man könnte eine ganze Geschichte der Erfindungen seit 1830\n schreiben, die bloss als Kriegsmittel des Kapitals wider Arbeiteremeuten\n ins Leben traten. Wir erinnern vor allem an die selfacting mule, weil\n sie eine neue Epoche des automatischen Systems eröffnet [Note: Einige sehr bedeutende Anwendungen von Maschinen zum Maschinenbau\n erfand Herr Fairbairn in Folge von strikes in seiner eignen Maschinenfabrik.] . Ure sagt\n von einer Maschine zum Farbendruck in den Kattundruckereien: „Endlich\n suchten sich die Kapitalisten von dieser unerträglichen Sklaverei\n (nämlich den ihnen lästigen Kontraktsbedingungen der Arbeiter) zu be-\n freien, indem sie die Hilfsquellen der Wissenschaft anriefen, und bald wa-\n ren sie reintegrirt in ihre legitimen Rechte, die des Kopfes\n über die andern Körpertheile.“ Er sagt von einer Erfindung zum Ketten-\n schlichten, deren unmittelbarer Anlass ein strike: „Die Horde der Unzu-\n friednen, die sich hinter den alten Linien der Theilung der Arbeit unbesiegbar\n verschanzt wähnte, sah sich so in die Flanke genommen und ihre Vertheidi-\n gungsmittel vernichtet durch die moderne Taktik der Maschinisten.\n Sie mussten sich auf Gnade und Ungnade ergeben.“ Er sagt von\n der Erfindung der selfácting mule: „Sie war berufen die Ordnung unter\n den industriellen Klassen wieder herzustellen … Diese Erfindung bestä-\n tigt die von uns bereits entwickelte Doktrin, dass das Ka-\n pital, indem es die Wissenschaft in seinen Dienst presst,\n stets die rebellische Hand der Industrie zum Gehorsam\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"428\", \"facs\": \"#f0447\"} --> zwingt“ [Note: Ure l. c. t. II, p. 141, 142, 140] . Obgleich Ure’s Schrift vor 30 Jahren erschien, also zur\n Zeit eines relativ noch schwach entwickelten Fabriksystems, bleibt sie der\n klassische Ausdruck des Fabrikgeists, nicht nur wegen ihres\noffenherzigen Cynismus, sondern auch wegen der Naivetät, womit\n er die gedankenlosen Widersprüche des Kapitalhirns ausplaudert. Nach-\n dem er z. B. die „Doktrin“ entwickelt, dass das Kapital mit Hilfe der von\n ihm in Sold genommenen Wissenschaft „stets die rebellische Hand der In-\n dustrie zum Gehorsam zwingt“, entrüstet er sich darüber, „dass man von\n gewisser Seite die mechanisch-physische Wissenschaft anklagt, sich dem\n Despotismus reicher Kapitalisten zu leihen und zum Unterdrückungsmittel\n der armen Klassen herzugeben.“ Nachdem er weit und breit gepredigt,\n wie vortheilhaft rasche Entwicklung der Maschinerie den Arbeitern, warnt\n er sie, dass sie durch ihre Widersetzlichkeit, Strikes u. s. w., die Ent-\n wicklung der Maschinerie beschleunigen. „Derartige Re-\n volten“, sagt er, „zeigen die menschliche Verblendung in ihrem verächt-\n lichsten Charakter, dem Charakter eines Menschen, der sich zu sei-\n nem eignen Henker macht.“ Wenige Seiten vorher heisst es um-\n gekehrt: „Ohne die Kollisionen und heftigen Unterbrechungen, verursacht\n durch die irrigen Ansichten der Arbeiter, hätte sich das Fabrik-\n system noch viel rascher entwickelt und viel nützlicher für alle\n interessirten Parteien.“ Dann ruft er wieder aus: „Zum Glück für die\n Bevölkerung der Fabrikstädte Grossbritaniens finden die Verbesserungen\n in der Mechanik nur allmälig statt.“ „Mit Unrecht“, sagt er, „klagt man\n die Maschinen an, dass sie den Arbeitslohn der Erwachsnen vermindern,\n indem sie einen Theil derselben deplaciren, wodurch ihre Anzahl das Be-\n dürfniss nach Arbeit übersteigt. Aber es findet vermehrte Anwendung\n der Kinderarbeit statt und der Gewinn der Erwachsnen ist dadurch um so\n beträchtlicher.“ Derselbe Trostspender vertheidigt andrerseits die Niedrig-\n keit der Kinderlöhne damit, dass „sie die Aeltern abhalten ihre Kinder\n zu früh in die Fabriken zu schicken.“ Sein ganzes Buch ist eine Apo-\n logie des unbeschränkten Arbeitstags und es erinnert seine liberale Seele\n an die dunkelsten Zeiten des Mittelalters, wenn die Gesetzgebung verbietet\n Kinder von 13 Jahren mehr als 12 Stunden per Tag abzurackern. Diess\n hält ihn nicht ab die Fabrikarbeiter zu einem Dankgebet an die Vorsehung\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"429\", \"facs\": \"#f0448\"} --> aufzufordern, die ihnen durch die Maschinerie „die Musse verschafft habe\n über ihre unsterblichen Interessen nachzudenken“ [Note: l. c. und p. 68, 143, 5, 6.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0099","text":"Eine ganze Reihe bürgerlicher Oekonomen, wie James Mill, Mac Cul-\n loch, Torrens, Senior, J. St. Mill u. s. w., behauptet, dass alle Maschinerie,\n die Arbeiter verdrängt, stets gleichzeitig und nothwendig ein adäquates\n Kapital zur Beschäftigung derselben identischen Arbeiter freisetzt [Note: Ricardo theilte ursprünglich diese Ansicht, widerrief sie aber später\n ausdrücklich mit seiner charakteristischen wissenschaftlichen Unbefangenheit und\n Wahrheitsliebe. Sieh l. c. ch. XXXI. „On Machinery“.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0100","text":"Man unterstelle, ein Kapitalist wende 100 Arbeiter an z. B. in einer\n Tapetenmanufaktur, den Mann zu 30 Pfd. St. jährlich. Das von ihm\n jährlich ausgelegte variable Kapital beträgt also 3000 Pfd. St. Er\n entlasse 50 Arbeiter und beschäftige die übrigbleibenden 50 mit einer\n Maschinerie, die ihm 1500 Pfd. St. kostet. Der Vereinfachung halber\n wird von Baulichkeiten, Kohlen u. s. w. abgesehn. Man nimmt ferner an,\n das jährlich verzehrte Rohmaterial koste nach wie vor 3000 Pfd. St. [Note: Nb., ich gebe die Illustration ganz in der Weise der obengenannten\n Oekonomen.] .\n Ist durch diese Metamorphose irgend ein Kapital „freigesetzt“? In der\n alten Betriebsweise bestand die ausgelegte Gesammtsumme von 6000 Pfd. St.\n halb aus constantem und halb aus variablem Kapital. Sie besteht jetzt\n aus 4500 Pfd. St. (3000 Pfd. St. für Rohmaterial und 1500 Pfd. St.\n für Maschinerie) constantem und 1500 Pfd. St. variablem Kapital.\n Statt der Hälfte bildet der variable oder in lebendige Arbeitskraft umge-\n setzte Kapitaltheil nur noch ¼ des Gesammtkapitals. Statt der Frei-\n setzung findet hier Bindung von Kapital in einer Form statt, worin es\n aufhört sich gegen Arbeitskraft auszutauschen, d. h. Verwandlung von\n variablem in constantes Kapital. Das Kapital von 6000 Pfd. St. kann,\n unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen, jetzt niemals mehr als 50 Arbeiter\n beschäftigen. Mit jeder Verbesserung der Maschinerie beschäftigt es we-\n niger. Kostete die neu eingeführte Maschinerie weniger als die Summe\n der von ihr verdrängten Arbeitskraft und Arbeitswerkzeuge, also z. B.\n statt 1500 nur 1000 Pfd. St., so würde ein variables Kapital von 1000\n Pfd. St. in constantes verwandelt oder gebunden, während ein Kapital von\n 500 Pfd. St. freigesetzt würde. Letzteres, denselben Jahreslohn unter-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"430\", \"facs\": \"#f0449\"} --> stellt, bildet einen Beschäftigungsfonds für ungefähr 16 Arbeiter, während\n 50 entlassen sind, ja für viel weniger als 16 Arbeiter, da die 500 Pfd. St.\n zu ihrer Verwandlung in Kapital wieder zum Theil in constantes Kapital\n verwandelt werden müssen, also auch nur zum Theil in Arbeitskraft um-\n gesetzt werden können.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0101","text":"In der That meinen jene Apologeten auch nicht diese Art Freisetzung\n von Kapital. Sie meinen die Lebensmittel der freigesetzten\n Arbeiter. Es kann nicht geläugnet werden, dass im obigen Fall z. B.\n die Maschinerie nicht nur 50 Arbeiter freisetzt und dadurch „disponibel“\n macht, sondern zugleich ihren Zusammenhang mit Lebensmitteln zum\n Werth von 1500 Pfd. St. aufhebt und so diese Lebensmittel „freisetzt“.\n Die einfache und keineswegs neue Thatsache, dass die Maschinerie\n den Arbeiter von Lebensmitteln freisetzt, lautet also ökono-\n misch, dass die Maschinerie Lebensmittel für den Arbeiter\n freisetzt oder in Kapital zu seiner Anwendung verwandelt. Man sieht,\n es kommt alles auf die Ausdrucksweise an. Nominibus mollire licet mala.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0102","text":"Die Lebensmittel zum Betrag von 1500 Pfd. St. standen den ent-\n lassnen Arbeitern niemals als Kapital gegenüber. Was ihnen als Ka-\n pital gegenüberstand, waren die jetzt in Maschinerie verwandel-\n ten 1500 Pfd. St. Näher betrachtet repräsentirten diese 1500 Pfd. St.\n nur einen Theil der vermittelst der entlassnen 50 Arbeiter jährlich produ-\n cirten Tapeten, die sie in Geldform statt in natura von ihrem Anwender zum\n Lohn erhielten. Mit den in 1500 Pfd. St. verwandelten Tapeten kauften sie\n Lebensmittel zu demselben Betrag. Diese existirten für sie daher nicht als\n Kapital, sondern als Waaren, und sie selbst existirten für diese Waaren\n nicht als Lohnarbeiter, sondern als Käufer. Der Umstand, dass die Ma-\n schinerie sie von Kaufmitteln „freigesetzt“ hat, verwandelt sie aus Käufern\n in Nicht-Käufer. Daher verminderte Nachfrage für jene Waaren. Voilà tout.\n Wird diese verminderte Nachfrage nicht durch vermehrte Nachfrage von an-\n derer Seite kompensirt, so sinkt der Marktpreis der Waaren. Dauert diess\n länger und in grössrem Umfange, so erfolgt ein Deplacement der in der Pro-\n duktion jener Waaren beschäftigten Arbeiter. Ein Theil des Kapitals,\n das früher nothwendige Lebensmittel producirte, wird in anderer Form\n reproducirt. Während des Falls der Marktpreise und des Deplacements\n von Kapital, werden auch die in der Produktion der nothwendigen Lebens-\n mittel beschäftigten Arbeiter von einem Theil ihres Lohns „freigesetzt“.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"431\", \"facs\": \"#f0450\"} --> Statt also zu beweisen, dass die Maschinerie durch die Freisetzung der\n Arbeiter von Lebensmitteln letztre gleichzeitig in Kapital zur Anwendung\n der erstern verwandelt, beweist der Herr Apologet mit dem probaten Ge-\n setz von Nachfrage und Zufuhr umgekehrt, dass die Maschinerie nicht nur\n in dem Produktionszweig, worin sie eingeführt, sondern auch in den Pro-\n duktionszweigen, worin sie nicht eingeführt wird, Arbeiter aufs Pflaster\n wirft.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0103","text":"Ausser der guten Absicht der Vertuschung liegt jener abgeschmack-\n ten Kompensationstheorie zu Grunde, erstens, dass die Maschinerie früher\n gebundne Arbeitskraft freisetzt, und falls zuschüssiges Kapital\n nach Anlage drängt, ihm mit der disponiblen Arbeitskraft gleich-\n zeitig disponibel gemachte Lebensmittel zur Verfügung stellt. Aber die\n Maschinerie deplacirt nicht nur die zunächst „überzählig“ gemachten,\n sondern zugleich den neuen Menschenstrom, der jedem Industriezweig sein\n Kontingent zum regelmässigen Ersatz und Wachsthum liefert. Diese Er-\n satzmannschaft wird neu vertheilt und in andern Arbeitszweigen absorbirt,\n während die ursprünglichen Opfer grossentheils in der Uebergangs-\n periode verkommen und verkümmern. Zudem ist ihre Arbeitskraft\n durch die Theilung der Arbeit so vereinseitigt, dass sie nur in wenigen und\n daher beständig überfüllten niedrigen Arbeitszweigen Zugang finden [Note: Ein Ricardianer bemerkt hierüber gegen die Fadaisen J. B. Say’s:\n „Bei entwickelter Theilung der Arbeit ist das Geschick der Arbeiter nur in dem\n besondern Zweig anwendbar, worin sie aufgebracht wurden; sie selbst sind\n eine Art von Maschinen. Es hilft daher absolut nichts papageimässig zu\n plappern, dass die Dinge eine Tendenz haben, ihr Niveau zu finden. Wir müssen\n um uns schauen und sehn, dass sie für lange Zeit ihr Niveau nicht finden\n können; dass wenn sie es finden, das Niveau niedriger steht als beim Aus-\n gang des Prozesses.“ („An Inquiry into those Principles respecting\n the Nature of Demand etc. Lond. 1821“, p. 72.)] .\n Zweitens aber wird die unzweifelhafte Thatsache ausgesprochen, dass die\n Maschinerie an sich nicht verantwortlich ist für die „Freisetzung“\n der Arbeiter von Lebensmitteln. Sie verwohlfeilert und vermehrt das\n Produkt in dem Zweig, den sie ergreift, und lässt die in andern Industrie-\n zweigen producirte Lebensmittel-Masse zunächst unverändert. Nach wie\n vor ihrer Einführung besitzt die Gesellschaft also gleichviel oder mehr\n Lebensmittel für die deplacirten Arbeiter, ganz abgesehen von dem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"432\", \"facs\": \"#f0451\"} --> enormen Theil des jährlichen Produkts, der von Nichtarbeitern vergeudet\n wird. Und diess ist die Pointe der ökonomistischen Apologetik! Die von\n der kapitalistischen Anwendung der Maschinerie untrennbaren\n Widersprüche und Antagonismen existiren nicht, weil sie nicht\n aus der Maschinerie selbst erwachsen, sondern aus ihrer kapi-\n talistischen Anwendung! Da also die Maschinerie an sich betrachtet\n die Arbeitszeit verkürzt, während sie kapitalistisch angewandt den Arbeits-\n tag verlängert, an sich die Arbeit erleichtert, kapitalistisch angewandt ihre\n Intensivität steigert, an sich ein Sieg des Menschen über die Naturkraft ist,\n kapitalistisch angewandt den Menschen durch die Naturkraft unterjocht,\n an sich den Reichthum des Producenten vermehrt, kapitalistisch angewandt\n ihn verpaupert u. s. w., erklärt der bürgerliche Oekonom einfach, das An-\n sichbetrachten der Maschinerie beweise haarscharf, dass alle jene\n handgreiflichen Widersprüche blosser Schein der gemeinen Wirklichkeit,\n aber an sich, also auch in der Theorie, gar nicht vorhanden sind.\n Er spart sich so alles weitere Kopfbrechen und bürdet seinem Gegner\n obendrein die Dummheit auf, nicht die kapitalistische Anwen-\n dung der Maschinerie zu bekämpfen, sondern die Maschinerie\n selbst [Note: Ein Virtuose in diesem anmasslichen Cretinismus ist u. a. Mac Cul-\n loch. „Wenn es vortheilhaft ist“, sagt er z. B. mit der affektirten Naivetät eines\n Kindes von 8 Jahren, „das Geschick des Arbeiters mehr und mehr zu entwickeln,\n so dass er fähig wird ein stets wachsendes Waarenquantum mit demselben oder\n geringerem Arbeitsquantum zu produciren, so muss es auch vortheilhaft sein, dass\ner sich solcher Maschinerie zu seinem Beistande bediene als ihn\n am wirksamsten in der Erreichung dieses Resultats unterstützt.“ (Mac\n Culloch: „Princ. of Pol. Econ. Lond. 1830“, p. 166.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4MUG:DISPLAY-0104","text":"Da jedes Maschinenprodukt, z. B. eine Elle Maschinengeweb, wohl-\n feiler ist als das von ihm verdrängte gleichartige Handprodukt, folgt als\n absolutes Gesetz: Bleibt das Gesammtquantum des maschinen-\n mässig producirten Artikels gleich dem Gesammtquantum des\n von ihm ersetzten handwerks- oder manufakturmässig producirten Artikels,\nso vermindert sich die Gesammtsumme der angewandten\n Arbeit. Die etwa zur Produktion der Arbeitsmittel selbst, der Maschi-\n nerie, Kohle u. s. w., erheischte Arbeitszunahme muss kleiner sein\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"433\", \"facs\": \"#f0452\"} --> als die durch Anwendung der Maschinerie bewirkte Arbeitsabnahme.\n Das Maschinenprodukt wäre sonst eben so theuer oder theurer als das\n Handprodukt. Statt aber gleich zu bleiben, wächst thatsächlich die Ge-\n sammtmasse des von einer verminderten Arbeiteranzahl pro-\n ducirten Maschinenartikels weit über die Gesammtmasse des verdrängten\n Handwerksartikels. Gesetzt 400,000 Ellen Maschinengeweb würden von\n weniger Arbeitern producirt als 100,000 Ellen Handgeweb. In dem ver-\n vierfachten Produkt steckt viermal mehr Rohmaterial. Die Produktion\n des Rohmaterials muss also vervierfacht werden. Was aber die verzehr-\n ten Arbeitsmittel, wie Baulichkeiten, Kohlen, Maschinen u. s. w. betrifft,\n so ändert sich die Grenze, innerhalb deren die zu ihrer Produktion er-\n heischte zusätzliche Arbeit wachsen kann, mit der Differenz\n zwischen der Masse des Maschinenprodukts und der Masse des von der-\n selben Arbeiterzahl herstellbaren Handprodukts.","kind":"display"}],"sha256":"8133475ace44ef03616b59cb67f662da1662132d8bc38e1331489994d6225c67","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2","title":"4) Maschinerie und grosse Industrie. · reading part 2/2","order":15,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_4M_2","text":"Mit der Ausdehnung des Maschinenbetriebs in einem Industriezweig\n steigert sich also zunächst die Produktion in den andern Zweigen, die ihm\n seine Produktionsmittel liefern. Wie weit dadurch die beschäftigte Arbei-\n termasse wächst, hängt, Länge des Arbeitstags und Intensivität der Ar-\n beit gegeben, von der Zusammensetzung der verwandten Kapitale ab, d. h.\n vom Verhältniss ihrer constanten und variablen Bestandtheile. Diess\n Verhältniss seinerseits variirt sehr mit dem Umfang, worin die Maschinerie\n jene Gewerbe selbst schon ergriffen hat oder ergreift. Die Anzahl zu\n Kohlen- und Metallbergwerken verurtheilter Menschen schwoll ungeheuer\n mit dem Fortschritt des englischen Maschinenwesens, obgleich ihr Anwachs\n in den letzten Decennien durch Gebrauch neuer Maschinerie für den Berg-\n bau verlangsamt wird [Note: Nach dem Census von 1861 (Vol. II. Lond. 1863) betrug die Zahl\n der in den Kohlenbergwerken von England und Wales beschäftigten Arbeiter\n 246, 613, wovon 73,546 unter und 173,067 über 20 Jahre. Zur ersten Rubrik\n gehören 835 fünf- bis zehnjährige, 30,701 zehn- bis fünfzehnjährige, 42,010 fünf-\n zehn- bis neunzehnjährige. Die Zahl der in Eisen-, Kupfer-, Blei-, Zinn- und\n allen andern Metallminen Beschäftigten: 319,222.] . Eine neue Arbeiterart springt mit der Maschine\n ins Leben, ihr Producent. Wir wissen bereits, dass der Maschinenbetrieb\n sich dieses Produktionszweigs selbst auf stets massenhafterer Stufenleiter be-\n [Running matter: I. 28] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"434\", \"facs\": \"#f0453\"} --> mächtigt [Note: In England und Wales 1861 in der Produktion von Maschinerie beschäf-\n tigt: 60,807 Personen, eingezählt die Fabrikanten sammt ihren Commis u. s. w.,\n ditto alle Agenten und Handelsleute in diesem Fach. Ausgeschlossen dagegen die\n Producenten kleinerer Maschinen, wie Nähmaschinen u. s. w., ebenso die Produ-\n centen der Werkzeuge für die Arbeitsmaschinen, wie Spindeln u. s. w. Zahl\n sämmtlicher Civilingenieure betrug 3329.] . Was ferner das Rohmaterial betrifft [Note: Da Eisen einer der wichtigsten Rohstoffe, so sei hier bemerkt, dass 1861 in\n England und Wales 125,771 Eisengiesser, wovon 123,430 männlich, 2341 weib-\n lich. Von den erstern 30,810 unter und 92,620 über 20 Jahre.] , so unterliegt es\n z. B. keinem Zweifel, dass der Sturmmarsch der Baumwollspinnerei den\n Baumwollbau der Vereinigten Staaten und mit ihm nicht nur den afrika-\n nischen Sklavenhandel treibhausmässig förderte, sondern zugleich die\n Negerzucht zum Hauptgeschäft der sogenannten Border slaves states\n machte. Als 1790 der erste Sklavencensus in den Vereinigten Staaten\n aufgenommen ward, betrug ihre Zahl 697,000, dagegen 1861 ungefähr\n vier Millionen. Andrerseits ist es nicht minder gewiss, dass das Auf-\n blühen der mechanischen Wollfabrik mit der progressiven Verwandlung\n von Ackerland in Schafweide die massenhafte Verjagung und „Ueber-\n zähligmachung“ der Landarbeiter hervorrief. Irland untergeht noch in\n diesem Augenblick den Prozess, seine seit 20 Jahren beinahe um die\n Hälfte verkürzte Bevölkerung noch weiter auf das dem Bedürfniss seiner\n Landlords und der englischen Herrn Wollfabrikanten exakt entsprechende\n Mass zu reduciren.\n\n\n\n\nErgreift die Maschinerie eine Vor- oder Zwischenstufe des Gesammt-\n cursus, den ein Arbeitsgegenstand bis zu seiner letzten Form zu durch-\n laufen hat, so vermehrt sich mit dem Arbeitsmaterial die Arbeitsnachfrage\n in den noch handwerks- oder manufakturmässig betriebnen Gewerken,\n welche das Maschinenfabrikat weiter formen. Die Maschinenspinnerei\n z. B. lieferte das Garn so wohlfeil und so reichlich, dass die Handweber zu-\n nächst, ohne vermehrte Auslage, volle Zeit arbeiten konnten. So stieg ihr\n Einkommen [Note: „Eine Familie von 4 erwachsnen Personen (Baumwollwebern) mit zwei\n Kindern als winders gewann Ende des letzten und Anfang des gegenwärtigen Jahr-\n hunderts 4 Pfd. St. per Woche bei 10stündiger Tagesarbeit; war die Arbeit sehr\n dringend, so konnten sie mehr verdienen … Früher hatten sie immer gelitten\n von mangelnder Garnzufuhr.“ (Gaskell l. c. p. 25—27.)] . Daher Menschenzufluss in die Baumwollweberei, bis\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"435\", \"facs\": \"#f0454\"} --> schliesslich die von Jenny, Throstle und Mule in England z. B. ins Leben\n gerufenen 800,000 Baumwollweber wieder vom Dampfwebstuhl er-\n schlagen wurden. So wächst mit dem Ueberfluss der maschinenmässig pro-\n ducirten Kleidungsstoffe die Zahl der Schneider, Kleidermacherinnen,\n Nähterinnen u. s. w., bis die Nähmaschine erscheint.\n\n\n\n\nEntsprechend der steigenden Masse von Rohstoffen, Halbfabrikaten,\n Arbeitsinstrumenten u. s. w., die der Maschinenbetrieb mit relativ geringer\n Arbeiterzahl liefert, differenzirt sich die Bearbeitung dieser Rohstoffe\n und Halbfabrikate in zahllose Unterarten, also die Mannigfaltigkeit der\n gesellschaftlichen Produktionszweige. Der Maschinenbetrieb vermehrt die\ngesellschaftliche Theilung der Arbeit weit mehr als die Manufaktur,\n weil er die Produktivkraft der von ihm ergriffenen Gewerbe ungleich höher\n spannt.\n\n\n\n\nDas nächste Resultat der Maschinerie ist den Mehrwerth und zu-\n gleich die Produktenmasse, worin er sich darstellt, also mit der Substanz,\n wovon die Kapitalistenklasse sammt Anhang zehrt, diese Gesellschafts-\n schichten selbst zu vergrössern. Ihr wachsender Reichthum und die relativ\n beständig fallende Anzahl der zur Produktion der ersten Lebensmittel er-\n heischten Arbeiter, erzeugen mit neuem Luxusbedürfniss zugleich neue\n Mittel seiner Befriedigung. Ein grösserer Theil des gesellschaftlichen Pro-\n dukts verwandelt sich in Surplusprodukt und ein grösserer Theil des Sur-\n plusprodukts wird in verfeinerten und vermannigfachten Formen repro-\n ducirt und verzehrt. In andern Worten: Die Luxusproduktion\n wächst [Note: F. Engels in „Lage u. s. w.“ weist den jämmerlichen Zustand eines\n grossen Theils grade dieser Luxusarbeiter nach. Massenhafte neue Belege hierzu\n in den Berichten der „Child. Empl. Comm.“] . Die Verfeinerung und Vermannigfachung der Produkte ent-\n springt ebenso aus den neuen weltmarktlichen Beziehungen, welche die\n grosse Industrie schafft. Es werden nicht nur mehr ausländische Genuss-\n mittel gegen das heimische Produkt ausgetauscht, sondern es geht auch\n eine grössere Masse fremder Rohstoffe, Ingredienzen, Halbfabrikate u. s. w.\n als Produktionsmittel in die heimische Industrie ein. Mit denselben welt-\n marktlichen Beziehungen steigt die Arbeitsnachfrage in der Transport-\n industrie und spaltet sich letztere in zahlreiche neue Unterarten [Note: 1861 in England und Wales 94,665 in der Handelsmarine beschäftigte\n Seeleute.] .\n\n\n [Running matter: 28*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"436\", \"facs\": \"#f0455\"} -->\n\nDie Vermehrung von Produktions- und Lebensmitteln durch relativ ab-\n nehmende Arbeiterzahl treibt zur Ausdehnung der Arbeit in Industrie-\n zweigen, deren Produkte, wie Kanäle, Waarendocks, Tunnels, Brücken\n u. s. w. nur in fernerer Zukunft Früchte tragen. Es bilden sich, ent-\n weder direkt auf der Grundlage der Maschinerie, oder doch der ihr ent-\n sprechenden allgemeinen industriellen Umwälzung, ganz neue Produktions-\n zweige und daher neue Arbeitsfelder. Ihr Raumantheil an der Gesammt-\n produktion ist jedoch selbst in den meist entwickelten Ländern keineswegs\n bedeutend. Die Anzahl der von ihnen beschäftigten Arbeiter steigt im direkten\n Verhältniss, worin die Nothwendigkeit rohster Handarbeit reproducirt wird.\n Als Hauptindustrieen dieser Art kann man gegenwärtig Gaswerke, Tele-\n graphie, Photographie, Dampfschifffahrt und Eisenbahnwesen betrachten.\n Der Census von 1861 (für England und Wales) ergiebt in der Gasin-\n dustrie (Gaswerke, Produktion der mechanischen Apparate, Agenten\n der Gascompagnien u. s. w.) 15,211 Personen, Telegraphie 2399,\nPhotographie 2366, Dampfschiffdienst 3570 und Eisen-\n bahnen 70,599, worunter ungefähr 28,000 mehr oder minder perma-\n nent beschäftigte „ungeschickte“ Erdarbeiter nebst dem ganzen admini-\n strativen und kommerciellen Personal. Also Gesammtzahl der Individuen\n in diesen fünf neuen Industrieen 94,145.\n\n\n\n\nEndlich erlaubt die ausserordentlich erhöhte Produktivkraft in den\n Sphären der grossen Industrie, begleitet, wie sie ist, von intensiv und extensiv\n gesteigerter Ausbeutung der Arbeitskraft in allen übrigen Produktions-\n sphären, einen stets grösseren Theil der Arbeiterklasse unproduktiv zu\n verwenden und so namentlich die alten Haussklaven unter dem Namen\n der „dienenden Klasse“, wie Bediente, Mägde, Lakaien u. s. w., stets\n massenhafter zu reproduciren. Nach dem Census von 1861 zählte die\n Gesammtbevölkerung von England und Wales 20,066,244 Personen, wo-\n von 9,776,259 männlich und 10,289,965 weiblich. Zieht man hiervon\n ab, was zu alt oder zu jung zur Arbeit, alle „unproduktiven“ Weiber,\n jungen Personen und Kinder, dann die „ideologischen“ Stände, wie Re-\n gierung, Pfaffen, Juristen, Militär u. s. w., ferner alle, deren ausschliess-\n liches Geschäft der Verzehr fremder Arbeit in der Form von Grundrente,\n Zins u. s. w., endlich Paupers, Vagabunden, Verbrecher u. s. w., so bleiben\n in rauher Zahl 8 Millionen beiderlei Geschlechts und der verschiedensten\n Altersstufen, mit Einschluss sämmtlicher irgendwie in der Produktion, dem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"437\", \"facs\": \"#f0456\"} --> Handel, der Finanz u. s. w. funktionirenden Kapitalisten. Von diesen\n 8 Millionen kommen auf:\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAckerbauarbeiter (mit Einschluss der Hirten\n und bei Pächtern wohnenden Ackersknechte\n und Mägde) . . . . . . . . . . . 1,098,261 Personen.\n\n\n\n\nAlle in Baumwoll-, Woll-, Worsted-, Flachs-,\n Hanf-, Seide-, Jutefabriken und in der mecha-\n nischen Strumpfwirkerei und Spitzenfabrika-\n tion Beschäftigten . . . . . . . . . 642,607 [Note: Davon nur 177,596 männlichen Geschlechts über 13 Jahre.]  „\n\n\n\n\nAlle in Kohlen- und Metallbergwerken Beschäf-\n tigten . . . . . . . . . . . . . 565,835 „\n\n\n\n\nIn sämmtlichen Metallwerken (Hochöfen, Walz-\n werke u. s. w.) und Metallmanufakturen aller\n Art Beschäftigte . . . . . . . . . 396,998 [Note: Davon weiblichen Geschlechts 30,501.]  „\n\n\n\n\nDienende Klasse . . . . . . . . . . . 1,208,648 [Note: Davon männlichen Geschlechts: 137,447. Ausgeschlossen von den\n 1,208,648 alles Personal, das nicht in Privathäusern dient.]  „\n\n\n\n\n\n\nRechnen wir die in allen textilen Fabriken Beschäftigten zusammen\n mit dem Personal der Kohlen- und Metallbergwerke, so erhalten wir\n 1,208,442; rechnen wir sie zusammen mit dem Personal aller Metall-\n Werke und Manufakturen, so die Gesammtzahl 1,039,605, beidemal\n kleiner als die Zahl der modernen Haussklaven. Welch erhebendes Re-\n sultat der kapitalistisch exploitirten Maschinerie!\n\n\n\n\nAlle zurechnungsfähigen Repräsentanten der politischen Oekonomie\n geben zu, dass neue Einführung der Maschinerie pestartig wirkt auf die Arbei-\n ter in den überlieferten Handwerken und Manufakturen, womit sie zunächst\n konkurrirt. Fast alle beächzen die Sklaverei des Fabrikarbeiters. Und\n was ist der grosse Trumpf, den alle ausspielen? Dass die Maschinerie,\n nach der Tortur, wovon ihre Einführung und ihre Entwicklung begleitet\n sind, die Arbeitssklaven in letzter Instanz vermehrt, statt sie\n schliesslich zu vermindern! Ja, die politische Oekonomie jubelt\n sich aus in dem abscheulichen Theorem, abscheulich für jeden „Philan-\n throp“, der an die ewige Naturnothwendigkeit der kapitalistischen Produk-\n tionsweise glaubt, dass selbst die bereits auf Maschinenbetrieb begründete\n Fabrik, nach bestimmter Periode des Wachsthums, nach kürzerer oder\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"438\", \"facs\": \"#f0457\"} --> längerer „Uebergangszeit“, mehr Arbeiter abplackt als sie ursprüng-\n lich aufs Pflaster warf [Note: Ganilh betrachtet dagegen als Schluss-Resultat des Maschinenbetriebs\n absolut verminderte Anzahl der Arbeitssklaven, auf deren Kosten dann eine ver-\n mehrte Anzahl der „gens honnêtes“ zehrt und ihre bekannte „perfectibilité per-\n fectible“ entwickelt. So wenig er die Bewegung der Produktion versteht, fühlt\n er wenigstens, dass die Maschinerie, wenn sie nur dazu dient, erstens durch ihre\nEinführung beschäftigte Arbeiter in Paupers zu verwandeln und zweitens durch\n ihre Entwicklung mehr Arbeitssklaven zu reproduciren, als sie erschlagen hat, eine\n sehr fatale Institution ist. Den Cretinismus seines eignen Standpunkts kann man\n nur in seinen eignen Worten ausdrücken: „Les classes condamnées à produire\n et à consommer diminuent, et les classes qui dirigentle travail, qui soulagent,\n consolent et éclairent toute la population, se multiplient … et s’appro-\n prient tous les bienfaits qui résultent de la diminution des frais du\n travail, de l’abondance des productions et du bon marché des consommations.\n Dans cette direction, l’espèce humaine s’élève aux plus hautes conceptions du\n génie, pénètre dans les profondeurs mystérieuses de la religion, établit les\n principes salutaires de la morale (die darin besteht de „s’approprier\n tous les bienfaits etc.“), les lois tutélaires de la liberté (der liberté pour „les\n classes condamnées à produire“?) et du pouvoir, de l’obéissance et de la\n justice, du devoir et de l’humanité.“ Diess Kauderwelsch in: „Des Systèmes\n d’Économie Politique etc. Par M. Ch. Ganilh“. 2ème éd. Paris\n 1821, t. II, p. 224. cf. ib. p. 212.] !\n\n\n\n\nZwar zeigte sich schon an einigen Beispielen, z. B. den englischen\n Worsted- und Seidenfabriken, dass auf einem gewissen Entwicklungsgrad\n ausserordentliche Ausdehnung von Fabrikzweigen mit nicht nur rela-\n tiver, sondern absoluter Abnahme der angewandten Ar-\n beiteranzahl verbunden sein kann. Im Jahr 1860, als ein Special-\n census aller Fabriken des Vereinigten Königreichs auf Befehl des Parla-\n ments aufgenommen ward, zählte die dem Fabrikinspektor R. Baker\n zugewiesne Abtheilung der Fabrikdistrikte von Lancashire, Cheshire und\n Yorkshire 652 Fabriken; von diesen enthielten 570: Dampfwebstühle\n 85,622, Spindeln (mit Ausschluss der Dublirspindeln) 6,819,146,\nPferdekraft in Dampfmaschinen 27,439, in Wasserrädern 1390, be-\n schäftigte Personen 94,119. Im Jahr 1865 dagegen enthielten die-\n selben Fabriken: Webstühle 95,163, Spindeln 7,025,031,\nPferdekraft in Dampfmaschinen 28,925, in Wasserrädern 1445, be-\n schäftigte Personen 88,913. Von 1860 bis 1865 betrug also die Zu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"439\", \"facs\": \"#f0458\"} --> nahme dieser Fabriken an Dampfwebstühlen 11 %, an Spindeln 3 %,\n an Dampfpferdekraft 5 %, während gleichzeitig die Zahl der beschäftigten\n Personen um 5,5 % abnahm [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1865“, p. 58 sq. Gleich-\n zeitig war aber auch schon die materielle Grundlage für Beschäftigung einer wach-\n senden Arbeiterzahl gegeben in 110 neuen Fabriken mit 11,625 Dampfweb-\n stühlen, 628,756 Spindeln, 2695 Dampf- und Wasser-Pferdekraft. (l. c.)] . Zwischen 1852 und 1862 fand be-\n trächtliches Wachsthum der englischen Wollfabrikation statt, wäh-\n rend die Zahl der angewandten Arbeiter beinahe stationär blieb. „Diess\n zeigt, in wie grossem Masse neu eingeführte Maschinerie die Arbeit vor-\n hergehender Perioden verdrängt hatte“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 79.] . In empirisch gegebnen\n Fällen ist die Zunahme der beschäftigten Fabrikarbeiter oft nur schein-\n bar, d. h. nicht der Ausdehnung der bereits auf Maschinenbetrieb be-\n ruhenden Fabrik geschuldet, sondern der allmäligen Annexation von Neben-\n zweigen. Z. B. „die Zunahme der mechanischen Webstühle und der\n durch sie beschäftigten Fabrikarbeiter von 1838—1858 war\n in der (britischen) Baumwollfabrik einfach der Ausdehnung dieses\n Geschäftszweigs geschuldet; in den andern Fabriken dagegen der\nNeuanwendung von Dampfkraft auf den Teppich-, Band-,\n Leinenwebstuhl u. s. w., die vorher durch menschliche Muskelkraft ge-\n trieben wurden“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1856“, p. 16.] . Die Zunahme dieser Fabrikarbeiter war also nur\n der Ausdruck einer Abnahme in der Gesammtzahl der beschäftigten Ar-\n beiter. Es wird hier endlich ganz davon abgesehen, dass überall, mit\n Ausnahme der Metallfabriken, jugendliche Arbeiter (unter 18 Jahren), Weiber\n und Kinder das weit vorwiegende Element des Fabrikpersonals bilden.\n\n\n\n\nMan begreift jedoch, trotz der vom Maschinenbetrieb faktisch ver-\n drängten und virtuell ersetzten Arbeitermasse, wie mit seinem eignen\n Wachsthum, ausgedrückt in vermehrter Anzahl von Fabriken dersel-\n ben Art oder den erweiterten Dimensionen vorhandner Fabriken, die Fa-\n brikarbeiter schliesslich zahlreicher sein können als die von ihnen ver-\n drängten Manufakturarbeiter oder Handwerker. Das wöchentlich ange-\n wandte Kapital von 500 Pfd. St. bestehe z. B. in der alten Betriebsweise\n aus ⅖ constantem und ⅗ variablem Bestandtheil, d. h. 200 Pfd. St.\n seien in Produktionsmitteln ausgelegt, 300 Pfd. St. in Arbeitskraft, sage\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"440\", \"facs\": \"#f0459\"} --> 1 Pfd. St. per Arbeiter. Mit dem Maschinenbetrieb verwandelt sich die\n Zusammensetzung des Gesammtkapitals. Es zerfällt jetzt z. B. in ⅘\n constanten und ⅕ variablen Bestandtheil, oder es werden nur noch 100\n Pfd. St. in Arbeitskraft ausgelegt. Zwei Drittel der früher beschäftigten\n Arbeiter werden also entlassen. Dehnt sich dieser Fabrikbetrieb aus und\n wächst, bei sonst gleichbleibenden Produktionsbedingungen, das ange-\n wandte Gesammtkapital von 500 auf 1500, so werden jetzt 300 Arbeiter\n beschäftigt, so viele wie vor der industriellen Revolution. Wächst das\n angewandte Kapital weiter auf 2000, so werden 400 Arbeiter beschäf-\n tigt, also ⅓ mehr als mit der alten Betriebsweise. Absolut ist die an-\n gewandte Arbeiterzahl um 100 gestiegen, relativ, d. h. im Verhältniss\n zum vorgeschossnen Gesammtkapital, ist sie um 800 gefallen, denn das\n Kapital von 2000 Pfd. St. hätte in der alten Betriebsweise 1200 statt\n 400 Arbeiter beschäftigt. Relative Abnahme der beschäftigten Ar-\n beiterzahl verträgt sich also mit ihrer absoluten Zunahme. Es\n wurde oben angenommen, dass mit dem Wachsthum des Gesammtkapitals\n seine Zusammensetzung constant bleibt, weil die Produktionsbedingungen.\n Man weiss aber bereits, dass mit jedem Fortschritt des Maschinenwesens\n der constante, aus Maschinerie, Rohmaterial u. s. w. bestehende Kapital-\n theil wächst, während der variable, in Arbeitskraft ausgelegte fällt, und\n man weiss zugleich, dass in keiner Betriebsweise die Verbesserung so con-\n stant, daher die Zusammensetzung des Gesammtkapitals so variabel ist.\n Dieser beständige Wechsel ist aber ebenso beständig unterbrochen durch\n Ruhepunkte und bloss quantitative Ausdehnung auf gegebner tech-\n nologischer Grundlage. Damit wächst die Anzahl der beschäftigten Ar-\n beiter. So betrug die Anzahl aller Arbeiter in den Baumwoll-, Woll-,\n Worsted-, Flachs- und Seidenfabriken des Vereinigten Königreichs 1835 nur\n 354,684, während 1861 allein die Zahl der Dampfweber (beiderlei Ge-\n schlechts und der verschiedensten Altersstufen vom 8. Jahr an) 230,654\n betrug. Allerdings erscheint diess Wachsthum minder gross, wenn man\n erwägt, dass die britischen Handbaumwollweber mit den von ihnen selbst\n beschäftigten Familien 1838 noch 800,000 zählten [Note: „Die Leiden der Handweber (von Baumwolle und mit Baumwolle ge-\n mischten Stoffen) waren Gegenstand der Untersuchung durch eine königl. Kom-] , ganz abgesehn\n von den in Asien und auf dem europäischen Kontinent Deplacirten.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"441\", \"facs\": \"#f0460\"} -->\n\nIn den wenigen Bemerkungen, die über diesen Punkt noch zu machen,\n berühren wir zum Theil rein thatsächlich, so zu sagen exote-\n risch, Verhältnisse, wozu unsre theoretische Darstellung selbst noch\n nicht geführt hat.\n\n\n\n\nSo lange sich der Maschinenbetrieb in einem Industriezweig auf\n Kosten des überlieferten Handwerks oder der Manufaktur ausdehnt, sind\n seine Erfolge so sicher, wie etwa der Erfolg einer mit dem Zündnadelge-\n wehr bewaffneten Armee gegen eine Armee von Bogenschützen wäre.\n Diese erste Periode, worin die Maschine erst ihren Wirkungskreis erobert,\n ist entscheidend wichtig wegen der ausserordentlichen Profite, die sie pro-\n duciren hilft. Diese bilden nicht nur an und für sich eine Quelle beschleu-\n nigter Akkumulation, sondern ziehn grossen Theil des beständig neuge-\n bildeten und nach neuer Anlage drängenden gesellschaftlichen Zusatzkapi-\n tals in die begünstigte Produktionssphäre. Die besondern Vortheile der\n ersten Sturm- und Drangperiode wiederholen sich beständig in den Pro-\n duktionszweigen, worin die Maschinerie neu eingeführt wird. Sobald\n aber das Fabrikwesen eine gewisse Breite des Daseins und bestimmten\n Reifegrad gewonnen hat, sobald namentlich seine eigne technologische\n Grundlage, die Maschinerie, selbst wieder durch Maschinen producirt wird,\n sobald Kohlen- und Eisengewinnung, wie die Verarbeitung der Metalle\n und das Transportwesen revolutionirt, überhaupt die der grossen Industrie\n entsprechenden allgemeinen Produktionsbedingungen hergestellt sind, er-\n wirbt diese Betriebsweise eine Elasticität, eine plötzliche sprung-\n weise Ausdehnungsfähigkeit, die nur an dem Rohmaterial und\n dem Absatzmarkt Schranken findet. Die Maschinerie bewirkt einerseits\n direkte Vermehrung des Rohmaterials, wie z. B. der cotton gin die Baum-\n wollproduktion vermehrte [Note: Andre Methoden, wodurch die Maschinerie auf die Produktion des Roh-\n materials einwirkt, werden im Dritten Buch erwähnt.] . Andrerseits sind Wohlfeilheit des Maschi-\n nenprodukts und das umgewälzte Transport- und Kommunikationswesen\n Waffen zur Eroberung fremder Märkte. Durch den Ruin ihres handwerks-\n [Note: mission, aber obgleich ihr Elend anerkannt und bejammert wurde, überliess man\n die Verbesserung (!) ihrer Lage dem Zufall und dem Wechsel der Zeit, und man\n darf hoffen, dass diese Leiden jetzt (20 Jahre später!) beinahe (nearly) ver-\n wischt sind, wozu die jetzige grosse Ausdehnung der Dampfwebstühle aller Wahr-\n scheinlichkeit nach beigetragen hat.“ (l. c. p. 15.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"442\", \"facs\": \"#f0461\"} --> mässigen Produkts verwandelt der Maschinenbetrieb sie zwangsweise in\n Produktionsfelder seines Rohmaterials. So wurde Ostindien zur Produk-\n tion von Baumwolle, Wolle, Hanf, Jute, Indigo u. s. w. für Grossbritanien\n gezwungen [Note: Baumwollausfuhr von Ostindien nach Grossbritanien.\n\n\n\n\n1846 lbs. 34,540,143.\n\n\n\n\n1860 lbs. 204,141,168.\n\n\n\n\n1865 lbs. 445,947,600.\n\n\n\n\nWollausfuhr von Ostindien nach Grossbritanien.\n\n\n\n\n1846 lbs. 4,570,581.\n\n\n\n\n1860 lbs. 20,214,173.\n\n\n\n\n1863 lbs. 20,679,111.\n\n\n\n] . Die beständige „Ueberzähligmachung“ der Arbeiter in\n den Ländern der grossen Industrie befördert treibhausmässige Auswande-\n rung und Kolonisation fremder Länder, die sich in Pflanzstätten für das\n Rohmaterial des Mutterlands verwandeln, wie Australien z. B. in eine\n Pflanzstätte von Wolle [Note: Wollausfuhr vom Kap der guten Hoffnung nach Gross-\n britanien.\n\n\n\n\n1846 lbs. 2,958,457.\n\n\n\n\n1860 lbs. 16,574,345.\n\n\n\n\n1865 lbs. 29,220,623.\n\n\n\n\nWollausfuhr von Australien nach Grossbritanien.\n\n\n\n\n1846 lbs. 21,789,346.\n\n\n\n\n1860 lbs. 59,166,616.\n\n\n\n\n1865 lbs. 109,734,261.\n\n\n\n] . Es wird eine neue, den Hauptsitzen des Ma-\n schinenbetriebs entsprechende internationale Theilung der Arbeit ge-\n schaffen, die einen Theil des Erdballs in vorzugsweis agrikoles Produk-\n tionsfeld für den andern als vorzugsweis industrielles Produktionsfeld\n umwandelt. Diese Revolution hängt zusammen mit Umwälzungen in der\n Agrikultur, die hier noch nicht weiter zu erörtern sind [Note: Die ökonomische Entwicklung der Vereinigten Staaten ist selbst ein Produkt\n der europäischen, näher englischen grossen Industrie. In ihrer jetzigen Gestalt\n müssen sie stets noch als Kolonialland von Europa betrachtet werden.\nBaumwollausfuhr der Vereinigten Staaten nach Grossbritanien\n in lbs.\n\n\n\n\n1846 401,949,393.\n\n\n\n\n1852 765,630,544.\n\n\n\n\n1859 961,707,264.\n\n\n\n\n1860 1,115,890,608.\n\n\n\n] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"443\", \"facs\": \"#f0462\"} -->\n\nDie ungeheure, stossweise Ausdehnbarkeit des Fabrikwesens und\n seine Abhängigkeit vom Weltmarkt erzeugen nothwendig fieberhafte Pro-\n duktion und darauf folgende Ueberfüllung der Märkte, mit deren Kontrak-\n tion Lähmung eintritt. Das Leben der Industrie verwandelt sich in eine\n Reihenfolge von Perioden mittlerer Lebendigkeit, Prosperität, Ueberpro-\n duktion, Krise und Stagnation. Die Unsicherheit und Unstätigkeit, denen\n der Maschinenbetrieb die Beschäftigung und damit die Lebenslage des Ar-\n beiters unterwirft, werden normal mit diesem Periodenwechsel des indu-\n striellen Cyklus. Die Zeiten der Prosperität abgerechnet, rast zwischen\n den Kapitalisten heftigster Kampf um ihren individuellen Raumantheil am\n Markt. Dieser Antheil steht in direktem Verhältniss zur Wohlfeilheit des\n Produkts. Ausser der hierdurch erzeugten Rivalität im Gebrauch ver-\n besserter, Arbeiter ersetzender Maschinerie und neuer Produktionsmethoden,\n tritt jedesmal ein Punkt ein, wo Verwohlfeilerung der Waare durch ge-\n [Note: Ausfuhr von Korn u. s. w. aus den Vereinigten Staaten nach\n Grossbritanien (1850 und 1862):\n\n\n\n\nWeizen cwts. 1850 16,202,312.\n1862 41,033,503.\n\n\n\n\nGerste cwts. 1850 3,669,653.\n1862 6,624,800.\n\n\n\n\nHafer cwts. 1850 3,174,801.\n1862 4,426,994.\n\n\n\n\nRoggen cwts. 1850 388,749.\n1862 7,108.\n\n\n\n\nWeizenmehl cwts. 1850 3,819,440.\n1862 7,207,113.\n\n\n\n\nBuchweizen cwts. 1850 1054.\n1862 19,571.\n\n\n\n\nMais cwts. 1850 5,473,161.\n1862 11,694,818.\n\n\n\n\nBere oder Bigg (bes. Gerstenart) cwts. 1850 2039.\n1862 7675.\n\n\n\n\nErbsen cwts. 1850 811,620.\n1862 1,024,722.\n\n\n\n\nBohnen cwts. 1850 1,822,972.\n1862 2,037,137.\n\n\n\n\nGesammteinfuhr cwts.: 1850 34,365,801.\n1862 74,083,351.\n\n\n\n] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"444\", \"facs\": \"#f0463\"} --> waltsamen Druck des Arbeitslohnes unter den Werth der Arbeitskraft er-\n strebt wird [Note: In einem Aufruf der von den Schuhfabrikanten zu Leicester durch\n einen „lock out“ aufs Pflaster geworfenen Arbeiter an die „Trade Societies\n of England“, Juli 1866, heisst es u. a.: „Seit etwa 20 Jahren wurde die\n Schuhmacherei in Leicester umgewälzt durch Einführung des Nietens statt des\n Nähens. Gute Löhne konnten damals verdient werden. Bald dehute sich diess\n neue Geschäft sehr aus. Grosse Konkurrenz zeigte sich unter den verschiednen\n Firmen, welche den geschmackvollsten Artikel liefern könne. Kurz nachher jedoch\n entsprang eine schlechtere Art Konkurrenz, nämlich die, einander im Markt zu\n unterverkaufen (undersell). Die schädlichen Folgen offenbarten sich bald in\n Lohnherabsetzung, und so reissend schnell war der Fall im Preise der Arbeit, dass\n viele Firmen jetzt nur noch die Hälfte des ursprünglichen Lohns zahlen. Und\n dennoch, obgleich die Löhne tiefer und tiefer sinken, scheinen die Profite mit\n jeder Aenderung des Arbeitstarifs zu wachsen.“ — Ungünstige Perioden der In-\n dustrie werden selbst von den Fabrikanten benutzt, um durch übertriebne Lohn-\n herabsetzung, d. h. direkten Diebstahl an den nothwendigsten Lebensmitteln des\n Arbeiters, ausserordentliche Profite zu machen. Ein Beispiel. Es handelt sich um\n die Krise in der Seidenweberei zu Coventry: „Aus Nachweisen, die ich sowohl\n von Fabrikanten als Arbeitern erhielt, folgt zweifelsohne, dass die Löhne in einem\n grössern Umfang verkürzt wurden, als die Konkurrenz ausländischer Producenten\n oder andre Umstände ernöthigten. Die Majorität der Weber arbeitet zu einer\n Lohnherabsetzung von 30 bis 40 %. Ein Stück Band, wofür der Weber fünf Jahre\n früher 6 oder 7 sh. erhielt, bringt ihm jetzt nur 3 sh. 3 d. oder 3 sh. 6 d. ein;\n andere Arbeit, früher zu 4 sh. und 4 sh. 3 d. bezahlt, erhält jetzt nur 2 sh. oder\n 2 sh. 3 d. Die Lohnherabsetzung ist grösser als zum Stachel der Nachfrage er-\n heischt ist. In der That, bei vielen Arten von Band war die Lohn-\n herabsetzung nicht einmal begleitet von irgend einer Herab-\n setzung im Preise des Artikels.“ (Bericht des Kommissärs\n F. D. Longe in „Ch. Empl. Comm. V. Rep. 1866“, p. 114, n. 1.)] .\n\n\n\n\nWachsthum in der Anzahl der Fabrikarbeiter ist also bedingt durch\nproportionell viel rascheres Wachsthum des in den Fabriken ange-\n legten Gesammtkapitals. Dieser Prozess vollzieht sich aber nur inner-\n halb der Ebb- und Fluthperioden des industriellen Cyklus. Er wird zudem\n stets unterbrochen durch den technologischen Fortschritt, der Arbeiter bald\n virtuell ersetzt, bald faktisch verdrängt. Dieser qualitative Wech-\n sel im Maschinenbetrieb entfernt beständig Arbeiter aus der Fabrik oder\n verschliesst ihr Thor dem neuen Rekrutenstrom, während die bloss quanti-\n tative Ausdehnung der Fabriken neben den Herausgeworfenen frische\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"445\", \"facs\": \"#f0464\"} --> Kontingente verschlingt. Die Arbeiter werden so fortwährend repellirt\n und attrahirt, hin- und hergeschleudert, und diess bei beständigem Wechsel\n in Geschlecht, Alter und Geschick der Angeworbenen.\n\n\n\n\nDie Schicksale des Fabrikarbeiters werden am besten veranschaulicht\n durch raschen Ueberblick der Schicksale der englischen Baum-\n wollindustrie.\n\n\n\n\nVon 1770 bis 1815 Baumwollindustrie gedrückt oder stagnant\n 5 Jahre. Während dieser ersten 45jährigen Periode besassen die eng-\n lischen Fabrikanten das Monopol der Maschinerie und des Weltmarkts.\n1815 bis 1821 gedrückt, 1822 und 1823 prosperirend, 1824\n Aufhebung der Kombinationsgesetze, allgemeine grosse Ausdehnung\n der Fabriken, 1825 Krise, 1826 grosses Elend und Aufstände unter den\n Baumwollarbeitern, 1827 leise Besserung, 1828 grosser Anwachs von\n Dampfwebstühlen und Ausfuhr, 1829 die Ausfuhr, besonders nach In-\n dien, übergipfelt alle früheren Jahre, 1830 überfüllte Märkte, grosser\n Nothstand, 1831 bis 1833 fortdauernder Druck; der Handel nach Ost-\n asien (Indien und China) wird dem Monopol der ostindischen Kompagnie\n entzogen. 1834 grosses Wachsthum von Fabriken und Maschinerie,\n Mangel an Händen. Das neue Armengesetz befördert die Wande-\n rung der Landarbeiter in die Fabrikdistrikte. Fegung der ländlichen\n Grafschaften von Kindern. Weisser Sklavenhandel. 1835 grosse Pros-\n perität. Gleichzeitige Todhungerung der Baumwollhandweber. 1836\n grosse Prosperität. 1837 und 1838 gedrückter Zustand und Krise.\n1839 Wiederaufleben. 1840 grosse Depression, Aufstände, Einschrei-\n ten des Militärs. 1841 und 1842 furchtbares Leiden der Fabrikarbeiter.\n 1842 schliessen die Fabrikanten die Hände von den Fabriken aus, um den\n Widerruf der Korngesetze zu erzwingen. Die Arbeiter strömen zu vielen Tau-\n senden nach Yorkshire, vom Militär zurückgetrieben, ihre Führer vor’s\n Gericht zu Lancaster gestellt. 1843 grosses Elend. 1844 Wieder-\n aufleben. 1845 grosse Prosperität. 1846 erst fortdauernder Auf-\n schwung, dann Symptome der Reaktion. Widerruf der Kornge-\n setze. 1847 Krise. Allgemeine Herabsetzung der Löhne um 10 und\n mehr Procent zur Feier des „big loaf“. 1848 fortdauernder Druck.\n Manchester unter militärischem Schutz. 1849 Wiederaufleben. 1850\n Prosperität. 1851 fallende Waarenpreise, niedrige Löhne, häufige\n Strikes. 1852 beginnende Verbesserung, Fortdauer der Strikes, Fabri-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"446\", \"facs\": \"#f0465\"} --> kanten drohn mit Import fremder Arbeiter. 1853 steigende Ausfuhr.\n Achtmonatlicher Strike und grosses Elend zu Preston. 1854 Prosperi-\n tät, Ueberfüllung der Märkte. 1855 Berichte von Bankerotten strömen\n ein aus den Vereinigten Staaten, Kanada, ostasiatischen Märkten. 1856\n grosse Prosperität. 1857 Krise. 1858 Verbesserung. 1859 grosse\n Prosperität, Zunahme der Fabriken. 1860 Zenith der englischen Baum-\n wollindustrie. Indische, australische und andere Märkte so überführt,\n dass sie noch 1863 kaum den ganzen Quark absorbirt haben. Franzö-\n sischer Handelsvertrag. Enormes Wachsthum von Fabriken und\n Maschinerie. 1861 Aufschwung dauert Zeitlang fort, Reaktion, ameri-\n kanischer Bürgerkrieg, Baumwollnoth. 1862 bis 63 vollständiger Zu-\n sammenbruch.\n\n\n\n\nDie Geschichte des cotton famine ist zu charakteristisch, um nicht\n einen Augenblick dabei zu verweilen. Aus den Andeutungen über die\n Zustände des Weltmarkts 1860 bis 61 ersieht man, dass die Baumwoll-\n noth den Fabrikanten gelegen kam und zum Theil vortheilhaft war, eine\n Thatsache anerkannt in Berichten der Manchester Handelskammer, im Par-\n lament von Palmerston und Derby proklamirt, durch die Ereignisse be-\n stätigt [Note: Vgl. „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 30.] . Allerdings gab es 1861 unter den 2887 Baumwollfabriken\n des Vereinigten Königreichs viel kleine. Nach dem Bericht des Fabrik-\n inspektors A. Redgrave, dessen Verwaltungsbezirk von jenen 2887\n Fabriken 2109 einschliesst, wendeten von letztern 392 oder 19 % nur\nunter 10 Dampf-Pferdekraft an, 345 oder 16 % 10 und unter 20, und\n 1372 20 und mehr Pferdekraft [Note: l. c. p. 19.] . Die Mehrzahl der kleinen Fabriken\n waren Webereien, während der Prosperitätsperiode seit 1858 errichtet,\n meist durch Spekulanten, wovon der eine das Garn, der andre die Maschi-\n nerie, der dritte die Baulichkeit lieferte, unter dem Betrieb ehemaliger\noverlookers oder andrer unbemittelter Leute. Diese kleinen Fabri-\n kanten gingen meist unter. Dasselbe Schicksal hätte ihnen die durch das\n Baumwollpech verhinderte Handelskrise bereitet. Obgleich sie ⅓ der\n Fabrikantenzahl bildeten, absorbirten ihre Fabriken einen ungleich geringeren\n Theil des in der Baumwollindustrie angelegten Kapitals. Was den Um-\n fang der Lähmung betrifft, so standen nach den authentischen Schätzungen\n im Oktober 1862 60.3 % der Spindeln und 58 % der Webstühle still.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"447\", \"facs\": \"#f0466\"} --> Diess bezieht sich auf den ganzen Industriezweig und war natürlich sehr\n modificirt in den einzelnen Distrikten. Nur sehr wenige Fabriken arbei-\n teten volle Zeit (60 Stunden per Woche), die übrigen mit Unterbrechungen.\n Selbst für die wenigen Arbeiter, die volle Zeit und zu dem gewohn-\n ten Stücklohn beschäftigt, schmälerte sich nothwendig der Wochen-\n lohn in Folge der Ersetzung besserer Baumwolle durch schlechtere, der\n South Sea Island durch ägyptische (in Feinspinnereien), amerikanischer\n und ägyptischer durch Surat (ostindisch), und reiner Baumwolle durch\n Mischungen von Baumwollabfall mit Surat. Die kürzere Fiber der Surat-\n baumwolle, ihre schmutzige Beschaffenheit, die grössere Brüchigkeit der\n Fäden, der Ersatz des Mehls durch alle Art schwerer Ingredienzen beim\n Schlichten des Kettengarns u. s. w. verminderten die Geschwindigkeit der\n Maschinerie oder die Zahl der Webstühle, die ein Weber überwachen konnte,\n vermehrten die Arbeit mit den Irrthümern der Maschine und beschränkten\n mit der Produktenmasse den Stücklohn. Beim Gebrauch von Surat und\n mit voller Beschäftigung belief sich der Verlust des Arbeiters auf\n 20, 30 und mehr Procent. Die Mehrzahl der Fabrikanten setzte aber\n auch die Rate des Stücklohns um 5, 7½ und 10 Procent herab.\n Man begreift daher die Lage der nur 3, 3½, 4 Tage wöchentlich\n oder nur 6 Stunden per Tag Beschäftigten. Nachdem schon eine rela-\n tive Verbesserung eingetreten war, 1863, für Weber, Spinner u. s. w.\n Wochenlöhne von 3 sh. 4 d., 3 sh. 10 d., 4 sh. 6 d., 5 sh. 1 d. u. s. w. [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1863“, p. 41, 51.] .\n Selbst unter diesen qualvollen Zuständen stand der Erfindungs-\n geist des Fabrikanten in Lohnabzügen nicht still. Diese wurden\n zum Theil verhängt als Strafe für die seiner schlechten Baumwolle, unpas-\n senden Maschinerie u. s. w. geschuldeten Fehler des Machwerks. Wo der\n Fabrikant aber Eigenthümer der cottages der Arbeiter, vergütete er sich\n selbst für Hausrente durch Abzüge vom nominellen Arbeitslohn. Fabrik-\n inspektor Redgrave erzählt von self-acting minders (sie über-\n wachen ein paar self-acting mules), die „am Ende vierzehntägi-\n ger voller Arbeit 8 sh. 11 d. verdienten und von dieser Summe\n wurde die Hausrente abgezogen, wovon der Fabrikant jedoch die Hälfte\nals Geschenk zurückgab, so dass die minders volle 6 sh. 11 d. nach\n Hause trugen. Der Wochenlohn der Weber rangirte von 2 sh. 6 d. auf-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"448\", \"facs\": \"#f0467\"} --> wärts während der Schlusszeit von 1862“ [Note: „Reports etc. 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 41, 42.] . „Selbst dann wurde die\n Hausmiethe von den Löhnen häufig abgezogen, wenn die Hände nur kurze\n Zeit arbeiteten“ [Note: l. c. p. 57.] . Kein Wunder, dass in einigen Theilen Lancashire’s\n eine Art Hungerpest ausbrach! Charakteristischer als alles diess aber\n war es, wie die Revolutionirung des Produktionsprozesses\n auf Kosten des Arbeiters vor sich ging. Es waren förmliche experimenta\n in corpore vili, wie die der Anatomen an Fröschen. „Obgleich ich,“\n sagt Fabrikinspektor Redgrave, „die wirklichen Einnahmen der Arbeiter in\n vielen Fabriken gegeben habe, muss man nicht schliessen, dass sie den-\n selben Betrag Woche für Woche beziehn. Die Arbeiter erliegen den\n grössten Fluktuationen wegen des beständigen Experimenta-\n lisirens („experimentalizing“) der Fabrikanten … ihre Löhne\n steigen und fallen mit der Qualität des Baumwollgemischs; bald nähern\n sie sich um 15 % ihren früheren Einnahmen, und die nächste oder zweit-\n folgende Woche fallen sie um 50 bis 60 %“ [Note: l. c. p. 50, 51.] . Diese Experimente\n wurden nicht nur auf Kosten der Lebensmittel der Arbeiter gemacht. Mit\n allen ihren fünf Sinnen hatten sie zu büssen. „Die im Oeffnen der Baum-\n wollballen Beschäftigten unterrichten mich, dass der unerträgliche Gestank\n sie übel macht … Den in den Misch-, Scribbling- und Kardirräumen An-\n gewandten irritirt der freigesetzte Staub und Schmutz alle Kopföffnungen,\n erregt Husten und Schwierigkeit des Athmens … Wegen der Kürze der\n Fiber wird dem Garn beim Schlichten eine grosse Menge Stoff zugesetzt\n und zwar allerlei Substitute statt des früher gebrauchten Mehls. Daher\n Uebelkeit und Dispepsie der Weber. Bronchitis herrscht vor wegen des\n Staubs, ebenso Halsentzündung, ferner eine Hautkrankheit in Folge der Irri-\n tation der Haut durch den im Surat enthaltenen Schmutz.“ Andrerseits waren\n die Substitute für Mehl ein Fortunatussäckel für die Herrn Fabrikanten\n durch Vermehrung des Garngewichts. Sie machten „15 Pfund Rohmate-\n rial, wenn verwebt, 20 Pfund wiegen“ [Note: l. c. p. 62, 63.] . In dem Bericht der Fabrik-\n inspektoren vom 30. April 1864 liest man: „Die Industrie ver-\n werthet diese Hilfsquelle jetzt in wahrhaft unanständigem Mass. Ich weiss\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"449\", \"facs\": \"#f0468\"} --> von guter Autorität, dass achtpfündiges Geweb von 5¼ Pfund Baumwolle\n und 2¾ Pfund Schlichte gemacht wird. Ein andres 5¼pfündiges\n Geweb enthielt zwei Pfund Schlichte. Diess waren ordinäre Shirtings\n für den Export. In andern Arten wurden manchmal 50 % Schlichte\n zugesetzt, so dass Fabrikanten sich rühmen können und sich auch\n wirklich rühmen, dass sie reich werden durch den Verkauf von\n Geweben für weniger Geld als das nominell in ihnen enthaltene\n Garn kostet“ [Note: „Reports etc. 30th April 1864“, p. 27.] . Die Arbeiter aber hatten nicht nur unter den Ex-\n perimenten der Fabrikanten in den Fabriken, und der Municipalitäten\n ausserhalb der Fabriken, nicht nur von Lohnherabsetzung und Arbeits-\n losigkeit, von Mangel und Almosen, von den Elogen der Lords und Unter-\n häusler zu leiden. „Unglückliche Frauenzimmer, beschäftigungslos in\n Folge der Baumwollnoth, wurden Auswürflinge der Gesellschaft und blie-\n ben es ‥ Die Zahl junger Prostituirten hat mehr zugenommen als seit den\n letzten 25 Jahren“ [Note: Aus Brief des Chief Constable Harris von Bolton in „Reports of\n Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1865“, p. 61, 62.] .\n\n\n\n\nMan findet also in den ersten 45 Jahren der britischen Baumwoll-\n industrie, von 1770—1815, nur 5 Jahre der Krise und Stagnation, aber\n diess war die Periode ihres Weltmonopols. Die zweite 48jährige Periode\n von 1815—1863 zählt nur 20 Jahre des Wiederauflebens und der Pros-\n perität auf 28 Jahre des Drucks und der Stagnation. Von 1815—1830\n beginnt die Konkurrenz mit dem kontinentalen Europa und den Vereinigten\n Staaten. Seit 1833 wird Ausdehnung der asiatischen Märkte erzwungen\n durch „Zerstörung der Menschenrace“. Seit Widerruf der Korngesetze,\n von 1846—1863, auf 8 Jahre mittlerer Lebendigkeit und Prosperität\n 9 Jahre Druck und Stagnation. Die Lage der erwachsnen männlichen\n Baumwoll-Arbeiter, selbst während der Prosperitätszeit, zu beurtheilen\n aus der beigefügten Note [Note: In einem Aufruf der Baumwollarbeiter, Frühling 1863, zur Bil-\n dung einer Emigrationsgesellschaft heisst es u. a.: „Dass eine grosse Emigration von\n Fabrikarbeitern jetzt absolut nothwendig ist, werden nur wenige läugnen. Dass\n aber ein beständiger Emigrationsstrom zu allen Zeiten erheischt und es ohne\n denselben unmöglich ist, unsre Stellung unter gewöhnlichen Umständen] .\n\n\n\n\nMan hat gesehn, wie die Maschinerie die auf dem Handwerk beruhende\n [Running matter: I. 29] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"450\", \"facs\": \"#f0469\"} -->Cooperation und die auf Theilung der handwerksmässigen Arbeit beruhende\nManufaktur aufhebt. Ein Beispiel der ersten Art ist die Mähmaschine,\n sie ersetzt die Cooperation von Mähern. Ein schlagendes Beispiel der zwei-\n ten Art ist die Maschine zur Fabrikation von Nähnadeln. Nach Adam\n Smith verfertigten zu seiner Zeit 10 Männer durch Theilung der Arbeit\n täglich über 48,000 Nähnadeln. Eine einzige Maschine liefert dagegen\n 145,000 in einem Arbeitstag von 11 Stunden. Eine Frau oder ein\n Mädchen überwacht im Durchschnitt 4 solche Maschinen und producirt\n daher mit der Maschinerie täglich an 600,000, in der Woche über 3,000,000\n Nähnadeln [Note: „Ch. Empl. Comm. IV. Report. 1864“, p. 108, n. 447.] . Sofern eine einzelne Arbeitsmaschine an die Stelle\n der Cooperation oder der Manufaktur tritt, kann sie selbst wieder zur\n Grundlage handwerksmässigen Betriebs werden. Indess bildet diese\n auf Maschinerie beruhende Reproduktion des Handwerksbetriebs nur den\nUebergang zum Fabrikbetrieb, der in der Regel jedesmal eintritt, so-\n bald mechanische Triebkraft, Dampf oder Wasser, die menschlichen\n Muskeln in der Bewegung der Maschine ersetzt. Sporadisch und eben-\n [Note: zu behaupten, zeigen folgende Thatsachen: Im Jahr 1814 betrug der officielle\n Werth (der nur Index der Quantität) der exportirten Baumwollgüter 17,665,378\n Pfd. St., ihr wirklicher Marktwerth 20,070,824 Pfd. St. Im Jahr 1858 betrug\n der officielle Werth der exportirten Baumwollgüter 182,221,681 Pfd. St., ihr\n wirklicher Marktwerth nur 43,001,322 Pfd. St., so dass die Verzehnfachung der\n Quantität wenig mehr als Verdopplung des Aequivalents bewirkte. Diess für das\n Land überhaupt und die Fabrikarbeiter im Besondern so ruinirende Resultat ward\n durch verschiedne cooperirende Ursachen producirt … Eine der hervorstechend-\n sten ist der beständige Ueberfluss von Arbeit, unentbehrlich für diesen Geschäfts-\n zweig, der, unter Strafe der Vernichtung, beständiger Expansion des Markts be-\n darf. Unsre Baumwollfabriken können stillgesetzt werden durch die periodische\n Stagnation des Handels, welche, unter gegenwärtiger Einrichtung, so unvermeid-\n lich ist, wie der Tod selbst. Aber desswegen steht der menschliche Erfindungs-\n geist nicht still. Obgleich, niedrig angeschlagen, 6 Millionen diess Land während\n der letzten 25 Jahre verlassen haben, befindet sich dennoch in Folge fortwäh-\n render Deplacirung der Arbeit, um das Produkt zu verwohlfeilern, ein grosser\n Procentsatz der erwachsnen Männer selbst in den Zeiten höch-\n ster Prosperität ausser Stand, Beschäftigung irgend einer Art auf irgend\n welche Bedingungen in den Fabriken zu finden.“ („Reports of Insp. of\n Fact. 30th April 1863“, p. 51, 52.) Man wird in einem späteren Kapitel\n sehn, wie die Herrn Fabrikanten während der Baumwollkatastrophe die Emigration\n der Fabrikarbeiter auf alle Art, selbst von Staatswegen zu verhindern suchten.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"451\", \"facs\": \"#f0470\"} --> falls nur vorübergehend kann kleiner Betrieb sich verbinden mit mecha-\n nischer Triebkraft durch Miethe des Dampfs, wie in einigen Manufakturen\n Birmingham’s, durch Gebrauch kleiner kalorischer Maschinen, wie in ge-\n wissen Zweigen der Weberei u. s. w. [Note: In den Vereinigten Staaten ist derartige Reproduktion des Handwerks auf\n technologischer Grundlage der Maschinerie häufig. Die Koncentration, bei dem un-\n vermeidlichen Uebergang in den Fabrikbetrieb, wird eben desswegen, im Vergleich\n zu Europa und selbst zu England, mit Siebenmeilenstiefeln marschiren.] . In der Seidenweberei zu Coven-\n try entwickelte sich naturwüchsig das Experiment der „Cottage-Fa-\n briken“. In der Mitte von Cottage-Reihen, quadratmässig gebaut,\n wurde ein s. g. Engine House errichtet für die Dampfmaschine und diese\n durch Schäfte mit den Webstühlen in den cottages verbunden. In allen\n Fällen war der Dampf gemiethet, z. B. zu 2½ sh. per Webstuhl. Diese\n Dampfrente war wöchentlich zahlbar, die Webstühle mochten laufen oder\n nicht. Jede cottage enthielt 2—6 Webstühle, den Arbeitern gehörig,\n oder auf Kredit gekauft, oder gemiethet. Der Kampf zwischen der\n Cottage-Fabrik und der Fabrik per se währte über 12 Jahre. Er hat ge-\n endet mit dem gänzlichen Ruin der 300 cottage factories [Note: Vgl. „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1865“, p. 64.] . Wo die\n Natur des Prozesses nicht von vorn herein Produktion auf grosser Stufen-\n leiter bedang, durchliefen in der Regel die in den letzten Decennien neu\n aufkommenden Industrieen, wie z. B. Enveloppe-, Stahlfedermachen u. s. w.,\n erst den Handwerksbetrieb und dann den Manufakturbetrieb als kurzlebige\n Uebergangsphasen zum Fabrikbetrieb. Diese Metamorphose bleibt dort am\n schwierigsten, wo die manufakturmässige Produktion des Machwerks\n keine Stufenfolge von Entwicklungsprozessen, sondern eine Vielheit disparater\n Prozesse einschliesst. Diess bildete z. B. ein grosses Hinderniss der Stahl-\n federfabrik. Jedoch wurde schon vor ungefähr anderthalb Decennien ein\n Automat erfunden, der 6 disparate Prozesse auf einen Schlag verrichtet.\n Das Handwerk lieferte die ersten 12 Dutzend Stahlfedern 1820 zu\n 7 Pfd. St. 4 sh., die Manufaktur lieferte sie 1830 zu 8 sh., und die\nFabrik liefert sie heute dem Grosshandel zu 2 bis 6 d. [Note: Herr Gillot errichtete zu Birmingham die erste Stahlfedermanufaktur auf\n grosser Stufenleiter. Sie lieferte schon 1851 über 180 Millionen Federn und verzehrte\n jährlich 120 Tonnen Stahlblech. Birmingham, das diese Industrie im Vereinigten] \n\n\n [Running matter: 29*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"452\", \"facs\": \"#f0471\"} -->\n\nMit der Entwicklung des Fabrikwesens und der sie begleitenden Umwäl-\n zung der Agrikultur dehnt sich nicht nur die Produktionsleiter in allen\n andern Industriezweigen aus, sondern verändert sich auch ihr\n Charakter. Das Princip des Maschinenbetriebs, den Produktionspro-\n zess in seine konstituirenden Phasen zu analysiren und die so gegebnen\n Probleme durch Anwendung der Mechanik, Chemie u. s. w., kurz der\n Naturwissenschaften zu lösen, wird überall bestimmend. Maschinerie\n drängt sich daher bald für diesen, bald für jenen Theilprozess in die Manu-\n fakturen. Die feste Krystallisation ihrer Gliederung, der alten Thei-\n lung der Arbeit entstammend, löst sich damit auf und macht fortwähren-\n dem Wechsel Platz. Abgesehn hiervon wird die Zusammensetzung des\n Gesammtarbeiters oder des kombinirten Arbeitspersonals von Grund aus umge-\n wälzt. Im Gegensatz zur Manufakturperiode gründet sich der Plan\n der Arbeitstheilung jetzt auf Anwendung der Weiberarbeit, der Arbeit\n von Kindern aller Altersstufen, ungeschickter Arbeiter, wo es immer thubar,\n kurz der „cheap labour“, wohlfeilen Arbeit, wie der Engländer sie cha-\n rakteristisch nennt. Diess gilt nicht nur für alle auf grosser Stufen-\n leiter kombinirte Produktion, ob sie Maschinerie anwende oder\n nicht, sondern auch für die s. g. Hausindustrie, ob ausgeübt in den\n Privatwohnungen der Arbeiter oder in kleinen Werkstätten. Diese s. g.\n moderne Hausindustrie hat mit der altmodischen, die unabhängiges städti-\n sches Handwerk, selbstständige Bauernwirthschaft und vor allem ein\n Haus der Arbeiterfamilie voraussetzt, nichts gemein als den\n Namen. Sie ist jetzt verwandelt in das auswärtige Departement\n der Fabrik, der Manufaktur oder des Waarenmagazins.\n Neben den Fabrikarbeitern, Manufakturarbeitern und Handwerkern, die es\n in grossen Massen räumlich koncentrirt und direkt kommandirt, bewegt\n das Kapital durch unsichtbare Fäden eine andre Armee in den grossen\n Städten und über das flache Land zerstreuter Hausarbeiter. Beispiel: die\n Hemdenfabrik der Herrn Tillie zu Londonderry, Irland, die 1000 Fabrik-\n arbeiter und 9000 auf dem Land zerstreute Hausarbeiter beschäftigt [Note: „Ch. Empl. Comm. II. Rep. 1864“, p. LXVIII, n. 415.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Exploitation wohlfeiler und unreifer Arbeitskräfte wird in der\n [Note: Königreich monopolisirt, producirt jetzt jährlich Milliarden von Stahlfedern. Die\n Zahl der beschäftigten Personen betrug nach dem Census von 1861: 1428, dar-\n unter 1268 Arbeiterinnen, vom 5. Jahr an einrollirt.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"453\", \"facs\": \"#f0472\"} -->modernen Manufaktur schamloser als in der eigentlichen\n Fabrik, weil die hier existirende, technologische Grundlage, Ersatz der\n Muskelkraft durch Maschinen und Leichtigkeit der Arbeit, dort grossentheils\n wegfällt, zugleich der weibliche oder noch unreife Körper den Einflüssen\n giftiger Substanzen u. s. w. aufs gewissenloseste preisgegeben wird. Sie\n wird in der s. g. Hausarbeit schamloser als in der Manufaktur, weil\n die Widerstandsfähigkeit der Arbeiter mit ihrer Zersplitterung abnimmt,\n eine ganze Reihe räuberischer Parasiten sich zwischen den eigentlichen\n Arbeitgeber und den Arbeiter drängt, die Hausarbeit überall mit Maschi-\n nen- oder wenigstens Manufakturbetrieb in demselben Produktionszweig\n kämpft, die Armuth den Arbeiter der nöthigsten Arbeitsbedingungen,\n Raum, Licht, Ventilation u. s. w. beraubt, die Unregelmässigkeit der Be-\n schäftigung wächst, und endlich in diesen letzten Zufluchtsstätten der durch\n die grosse Industrie und Agrikultur „überzählig“ Gemachten die Arbeiter-\n konkurrenz nothwendig ihr Maximum erreicht. Die durch den Maschinen-\n betrieb erst systematisch ausgebildete Oekonomisirung der Produk-\n tionsmittel, von vorn herein zugleich rücksichtsloseste Verschwen-\n dung der Arbeitskraft und Raub an den normalen Voraussetzun-\n gen der Arbeitsfunktion, kehrt jetzt diese ihre antagonistische und men-\n schenmörderische Seite um so mehr heraus, je weniger in einem Industrie-\n zweig die gesellschaftliche Produktivkraft der Arbeit und\n die technologische Grundlage kombinirter Arbeitsprozesse\n entwickelt sind.\n\n\n\n\nIch will nun an einigen Beispielen die oben aufgestellten Sätze er-\n läutern. Der Leser kennt in der That schon massenhafte Belege aus dem\n Abschnitt über den Arbeitstag. Die Metallmanufakturen in Birming-\n ham und Umgegend wenden grossentheils für sehr schwere Arbeit 30,000\n Kinder und junge Personen nebst 10,000 Weibern an. Man findet sie\n hier in den gesundheitswidrigen Gelbgiessereien, Knopffabriken, Glasur-,\n Galvanisirungs- und Lackirarbeiten [Note: Und nun gar Kinder im File-grinding zu Sheffield!] . Die Arbeitsexcesse für Erwachsne\n und Unerwachsne haben verschiedenen Londoner Zeitungs- und Buch-\n druckereien den rühmlichen Namen: „Das Schlachthaus“ ge-\n sichert [Note: „Ch. Empl. Comm. V. Rep. 1866“, p. 3, n. 24, p. 6, n. 55, 56,\n p. 7, n. 59, 60.] . Dieselben Excesse, deren Schlachtopfer hier namentlich Weiber,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"454\", \"facs\": \"#f0473\"} --> Mädchen und Kinder, in der Buchbinderei. Schwere Arbeit für\n Unerwachsne in den Seilereien, Nachtarbeit in Salzwerken, Lichter- und\n andren chemischen Fabriken; mörderischer Verbrauch von Jungen in\n Seidenwebereien, die nicht mechanisch betrieben werden, zum Drehen der\n Webstühle [Note: l. c. p. 114, 115, n. 6—7. Der Kommissär bemerkt richtig, dass wenn\n sonst die Maschine den Menschen, hier der Junge verbatim die Maschine ersetzt.] . Eine der infamsten, schmutzigsten und schlecht bezahlte-\n sten Arbeiten, wozu mit Vorliebe junge Mädchen und Weiber verwandt\n werden, ist das Sortiren der Lumpen. Man weiss, dass Gross-\n britanien, abgesehn von seinen eignen unzähligen Lumpen, das Emporium für\n den Lumpenhandel der ganzen Welt bildet. Sie strömen dahin von Japan,\n den entferntesten Staaten Südamerikas und den kanarischen Inseln. Ihre\n Hauptzufuhrquellen aber sind Deutschland, Frankreich, Russland, Italien,\n Aegypten, Türkei, Belgien und Holland. Sie dienen zur Düngung, Fabri-\n kation von Flocken (für Bettzeug), Shoddy, und als Rohmaterial des Pa-\n piers. Die weiblichen Lumpensortirer dienen als Mediums, um Pocken\n und andre ansteckende Seuchen, deren erste Opfer sie selbst sind, zu col-\n portiren [Note: Sieh Bericht über den Lumpenhandel und zahlreiche Belege: „Public\n Health. VIII. Report. London 1866.“ Appendix, p. 196—208.] . Als klassisches Beispiel für Ueberarbeit, schwere und un-\n passende Arbeit, und daher folgende Brutalisirung der von Kindesbeinen\n an konsumirten Arbeiter kann, neben der Minen- und Kohlenproduktion,\n die Ziegel- oder Backsteinmacherei gelten, wozu in England\n nur noch sporadisch die neuerfundne Maschinerie angewandt wird. Zwi-\n schen Mai und September dauert die Arbeit von 5 Uhr Morgens bis\n 8 Uhr Abends, und, wo Trocknung in freier Luft stattfindet, oft von 4 Uhr\n Morgens bis 9 Uhr Abends. Der Arbeitstag von 5 Uhr Morgens bis\n 7 Uhr Abends gilt für „reducirt“, „mässig“. Kinder beiderlei Geschlechts\n werden vom 6. und selbst vom 4. Jahr an verwandt. Sie arbeiten die-\n selbe Stundenzahl, oft mehr als die Erwachsnen. Die Arbeit ist hart\n und die Sommerhitze steigert noch die Erschöpfung. In einer Ziegelei zu\n Mosley z. B. machte ein 24jähriges Mädchen 2000 Ziegel täglich, unter-\n stützt von zwei unerwachsnen Mädchen als Gehilfen, welche den Lehm\n trugen und die Ziegelsteine aufhäuften. Diese Mädchen schleppten täg-\n lich 10 Tonnen die schlüpfrigen Seiten der Ziegelgrube von einer Tiefe von\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"455\", \"facs\": \"#f0474\"} --> 30 Fuss herauf und über eine Entfernung von 210 Fuss. „Es ist un-\n möglich für ein Kind durch das Fegfeuer einer Ziegelei zu passiren ohne\n grosse moralische Degradation. . . . Die nichtswürdige Sprache, die sie\n vom zartesten Alter an zu hören bekommen, die unfläthigen, unanständigen\n und schamlosen Gewohnheiten, unter denen sie unwissend und verwildert\n aufwachsen, machen sie für die spätere Lebenszeit gesetzlos, verworfen,\n liederlich. . . . Eine furchtbare Quelle der Demoralisation ist die Art der\n Wohnlichkeit. Jeder moulder (der eigentlich geschickte Arbeiter und\n Chef einer Arbeiterg ruppe) liefert seiner Bande von 7 Personen Logis und\n Tisch in seiner Hütte oder cottage. Ob zu seiner Familie gehörig oder\n nicht, Männer, Jungen, Mädchen schlafen in der Hütte. Diese besteht\n gewöhnlich aus 2, nur ausnahmsweis aus 3 Zimmern, alle auf dem Erdge-\n schoss, mit wenig Ventilation. Die Körper sind so erschöpft durch die\n grosse Transpiration während des Tags, dass weder Gesundheitsregeln,\n Reinlichkeit noch Anstand irgendwie beobachtet werden. Viele dieser\n Hütten sind wahre Modelle von Unordnung, Schmutz und Staub. . . . Das\n grösste Uebel des Systems, welches junge Mädchen zu dieser Art Arbeit\n verwendet, besteht darin, dass es sie in der Regel von Kindheit an für ihr\n ganzes späteres Leben an das verworfenste Gesindel festkettet. Sie wer-\n den rohe, bösmäulige Buben („rough, foul-mouthed boys“), bevor die\n Natur sie gelehrt hat, dass sie Weiber sind. Gekleidet in wenige\n schmutzige Lumpen, die Beine weit über das Knie entblösst, Haar und\n Gesicht mit Dreck beschmiert, lernen sie alle Gefühle der Bescheidenheit\n und der Scham mit Verachtung behandeln. Während der Essenszeit lie-\n gen sie auf den Feldern ausgestreckt oder gucken den Jungen zu, die in\n einem benachbarten Kanal baden. Ist ihr schweres Tagwerk endlich voll-\n bracht, so ziehn sie bessere Kleider an und begleiten die Männer in Bier-\n kneipen.“ Dass die grösste Versoffenheit von Kindesbeinen an in dieser\n ganzen Klasse herrscht, ist nur naturgemäss. „Das Schlimmste ist, dass\n die Ziegelmacher an sich selbst verzweifeln. Sie könnten, sagte einer der\n Besseren zum Kaplan von Southallfields, ebensowohl versuchen den Teufel\n zu erheben und zu bessern als einen Ziegler, mein Herr!“ („You might as\n well try to raise and improve the devil as a brickie, Sir!“) [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. V. Report. 1866“, XVI, n. 86—97 und\n p. 130, n. 39—71. Vgl. auch ib. III. Rep. 1864, p. 48, 56.] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"456\", \"facs\": \"#f0475\"} -->\n\nUeber die kapitalistische Oekonomisirung der Arbeits-\n bedingungen in der modernen Manufaktur (worunter hier alle\n Werkstätten auf grosser Stufenleiter, ausser eigentlichen Fabriken, zu\n verstehn) findet man officielles und reichlichstes Material in dem IV. (1861)\n und VI. (1864) „Public Health Report“. Die Beschreibung der\n workshops (Arbeitslokale) namentlich der Londoner Drucker und Schnei-\n der überbietet die ekelhaftesten Phantasieen unsrer Romanschreiber. Die\n Wirkung auf den Gesundheitszustand der Arbeiter ist selbstverständlich.\nDr. Simon, der oberste ärztliche Beamte des Privy Council und officielle\n Herausgeber der „Public Health Reports“, sagt u. a.: „In meinem vierten\n Bericht (1863) zeigte ich, wie es für die Arbeiter praktisch unmöglich ist\n darauf zu bestehen, was ihr erstes Gesundheitsrecht ist, das Recht,\n dass zu welchem Werk immer ihr Anwender sie versammelt, die Arbeit,\n so weit es von ihm abhängt, von allen vermeidbaren gesundheitswidrigen\n Umständen befreit sein soll. Ich wies nach, dass während die Arbeiter\n praktisch unfähig sind, sich selbst diese Gesundheitsjustiz zu verschaffen, sie\n keinen wirksamen Beistand von den bestallten Administratoren der Gesund-\n heitspolizei erlangen können. . . . Das Leben von Myriaden von Arbeitern\n und Arbeiterinnen wird jetzt nutzlos torturirt und verkürzt durch das end-\n lose physische Leiden, welches ihre blosse Beschäftigung erzeugt“ [Note: „Public Health“. VI. Rep. Lond. 1864, p. 31.] .\n Zur Illustration des Einflusses der Arbeitslokale auf den Gesundheitszustand\n giebt Dr. Simon folgende Sterblichkeitsliste:\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"457\", \"facs\": \"#f0476\"} -->\n\nIch wende mich jetzt zur s. g. Hausarbeit. Um sich eine Vor-\n stellung von dieser auf dem Hintergrund der grossen Industrie aufgebauten\nExploitationssphäre des Kapitals und ihren Ungeheuerlich-\n keiten zu machen, betrachte man z. B. die scheinbar ganz idyllische, in\n einigen abgelegnen Dörfern Englands betriebne Nägelmacherei [Note: Es handelt sich hier um gehämmerte Nägel im Unterschied von den\n maschinenmässig fabricirten geschnittenen Nägeln. Siehe „Child. Empl.\n Comm. III. Report“, p. XI, p. XIX, n. 125—130, p. 53, n. 11, p. 114,\n n. 487, p. 137, n. 674.] .\n Hier genügen einige Beispiele aus den noch gar nicht maschinenmässig\n betriebnen oder mit Maschinen- und Manufakturtrieb konkurrirenden Zwei-\n gen der Spitzenfabrik und Strohflechterei.\n\n\n\n\nVon den 150,000 Personen, die in der englischen Spitzenproduktion\n beschäftigt, fallen ungefähr 10,000 unter die Botmässigkeit des Fabrik-\n akts von 1861. Die ungeheure Mehrzahl der übrig bleibenden 140,000\n sind Weiber, junge Personen und Kinder beiderlei Geschlechts, obgleich\n das männliche Geschlecht nur schwach vertreten ist. Der Gesundheitszu-\n stand dieses „wohlfeilen“ Exploitationsmaterials ergiebt sich aus folgender\n Aufstellung des Dr. Trueman, Arzt beim General Dispensary von Not-\n tingham. Von je 686 Patienten, Spitzenmacherinnen, meist\n zwischen dem 17. und 24. Jahr, waren schwindsüchtig:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1852 1 auf 45,\n\n\n\n\n1853 1 auf 28,\n\n\n\n\n1854 1 auf 17,\n\n\n\n\n1855 1 auf 18,\n\n\n\n\n1856 1 auf 15,\n\n\n\n\n1857 1 auf 13,\n\n\n\n\n1861 1 auf 8 [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. II. Report“, p. XXII, n. 166.] .\n\n\n\n\n1858 1 auf 15,\n\n\n\n\n1859 1 auf 9,\n\n\n\n\n1860 1 auf 8,\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDieser Fortschritt in der Rate der Schwindsucht muss dem opti-\n mistischsten Fortschrittler und lügenfauchendsten deutschen Freihandels-\n hausirburschen genügen.\n\n\n\n\nDer Fabrikakt von 1861 regelt das eigentliche Mächen der\n Spitzen, soweit es durch Maschinerie geschieht, und diess ist die Regel\n in England. Die Zweige, die wir hier kurz berücksichtigen, und zwar nicht,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"458\", \"facs\": \"#f0477\"} --> soweit die Arbeiter in Manufakturen, Waarenhäusern u. s. w. koncentrirt,\n sondern nur sofern sie s. g. Hausarbeiter sind, zerfallen 1) in das\nfinishing (letztes Zurechtmachen der maschinenmässig fabricirten\n Spitzen, eine Kategorie, die wieder zahlreiche Unterabtheilungen ein-\n schliesst), 2) Spitzenklöppeln.\n\n\n\n\nDas Lace finishing wird als Hausarbeit betrieben entweder in\n s. g. „Mistresses Houses“ oder von Weibern, einzeln oder mit ihren\n Kindern, in ihren Privatwohnungen. Die Weiber, welche die „Mistresses\n Houses“ halten, sind selbst arm. Das Arbeitslokal bildet Theil ihrer\n Privatwohnung. Sie erhalten Aufträge von Fabrikanten, Besitzern von\n Waarenmagazinen u. s. w. und wenden Weiber, Mädchen und junge Kin-\n der an, je nach dem Umfang ihrer Zimmer und der fluktuirenden Nach-\n frage des Geschäfts. Die Zahl der beschäftigten Arbeiterinnen wechselt\n von 20 zu 40 in einigen, von 10 zu 20 in andern dieser Lokale. Das\n durchschnittliche Minimalalter, worin Kinder beginnen, ist 6 Jahre, manche\n jedoch unter 5 Jahren. Die gewöhnliche Arbeitszeit währt von 8 Uhr\n Morgens bis 8 Uhr Abends, mit 1½ Stunden für Mahlzeiten, die unregel-\n mässig und oft iu den stinkigen Arbeitslöchern selbst genommen werden.\n Bei gutem Geschäft währt die Arbeit oft von 8 Uhr (manchmal 6 Uhr)\n Morgens bis 10, 11 oder 12 Uhr Nachts. In englischen Kasernen be-\n trägt der vorschriftsmässige Raum für jeden Soldaten 500—600 Kubik-\n fuss, in den Militärlazarethen 1200. In jenen Arbeitslöchern kommen\n 67—100 Kubikfuss auf jede Person. Gleichzeitig verzehrt Gaslicht den\n Sauerstoff der Luft. Um die Spitzen rein zu halten, müssen die Kinder\n oft die Schuhe ausziehn, auch im Winter, obgleich das Estrich aus Pflaster\n oder Ziegeln besteht. „Es ist nichts ungewöhnliches in Nottingham 14\n bis 20 Kinder in einem kleinen Zimmer von vielleicht nicht mehr als 12\n Quadratfuss zusammengepökelt zu finden, während 15 Stunden aus 24 be-\n schäftigt an einer Arbeit, an sich selbst erschöpfend durch Ueberdruss und\n Monotonie, zudem unter allen nur möglichen gesundheitszerstörenden Um-\n ständen ausgeübt … Selbst die jüngsten Kinder arbeiten mit einer gespann-\n ten Aufmerksamkeit und Geschwindigkeit, die erstaunlich sind, fast niemals\n ihren Fingern Ruhe oder langsamere Bewegung gönnend. Richtet man\n Fragen an sie, so erheben sie das Auge nicht von der Arbeit, aus Furcht\n einen Moment zu verlieren.“ Der „lange Stock“ dient den „mis-\n tresses“ als Anregungsmittel im Verhältniss, worin die Arbeitszeit ver-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"459\", \"facs\": \"#f0478\"} --> längert wird. „Die Kinder ermüden allmälig und werden so rastlos wie\n Vögel gegen das Ende ihrer langen Gebundenheit an eine Beschäftigung,\n eintönig, für die Augen angreifend, erschöpfend durch die Uniformität der\n Körperhaltung. Es ist wahres Sklavenwerk.“ („Their work like sla-\n very“ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. II. Report 1864“, p. XIX, XX, XXI.] .) Wo Frauen mit ihren eigenen Kindern zu Hause, d. h. im\n modernen Sinn, in einem gemietheten Zimmer, häufig in einer Dachstube\n arbeiten, sind die Zustände wo möglich noch schlimmer. Diese Art Ar-\n beit wird 80 Meilen im Umkreis von Nottingham ausgegeben. Wenn das\n in den Waarenhäusern beschäftigte Kind sie 9 oder 10 Uhr Abends\n verlässt, giebt man ihm oft noch ein Bündel mit auf den Weg, um es zu\n Haus fertig zu machen. Der kapitalistische Pharisäer, vertreten durch\n einen seiner Lohnknechte, thut das natürlich mit der salbungsvollen Phrase:\n ‚das sei für Mutter‘, weiss aber sehr wohl, dass das arme Kind aufsitzen\n und helfen muss [Note: l. c. p. XXI, XXVI.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Industrie des Spitzenklöppelns wird hauptsächlich in zwei\n englischen Agrikulturdistrikten betrieben, dem Honiton Spitzen-\n distrikt, 20 bis 30 Meilen längs der Südküste von Devonshire, mit\n Einschluss weniger Plätze von Nord-Devon, und einem andern Distrikt, der\n grossen Theil der Grafschaften von Buckingham, Bedford, Northampton\n und die benachbarten Theile von Oxfordshire und Huntingdonshire um-\n fasst. Die cottages der Ackerbautaglöhner bilden durchschnittlich die Ar-\n beitslokale. Manche Manufakturherrn wenden über 3000 dieser Hausar-\n beiter an, hauptsächlich Kinder und junge Personen, ausschliesslich weib-\n lichen Geschlechts. Die beim Lace finishing beschriebenen Zustände\n wiederholen sich. Nur treten an die Stelle der „mistresses houses“ die\n s. g. „lace schools“ (Spitzenschulen), gehalten von armen Weibern\n in ihren Hütten. Vom 5. Jahr an, manchmal jünger, bis zum 12. oder\n 15. arbeiten die Kinder in diesen Schulen, während des ersten Jahres die\n Jüngsten von 4 bis 8 Stunden, später von 6 Uhr Morgens bis 8 und 10\n Uhr Abends. „Die Zimmer sind im allgemeinen gewöhnliche Wohnstuben\n kleiner cottages, der Kamin zugestopft zur Abwehr von Luftzug, die In-\n sassen manchmal auch im Winter nur von ihrer eignen animalischen Wärme\n geheizt. In andern Fällen sind diese s. g. Schulzimmer kleinen Vorraths-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"460\", \"facs\": \"#f0479\"} --> kammern ähnliche Räume, ohne Feuerplatz … Die Ueberfüllung dieser\n Löcher und die dadurch producirte Luftverpestung sind oft extrem. Dazu\n kömmt die schädliche Wirkung von Gerinnen, Kloaken, verwesenden Stoffen\n und anderem Unrath, gewöhnlich in den Zugängen zu kleineren cottages.“\n Mit Bezug auf den Raum: „In einer Spitzenschule 18 Mädchen und Mei-\n sterin, 35 Kubikfuss für jede Person; in einer andern, wo unerträglicher\n Gestank, 18 Personen, per Kopf 24½ Kubikfuss. Man findet in dieser\n Industrie Kinder von 2 und 2½ Jahren verwandt“ [Note: l. c. p. XXIX, XXX.] .\n\n\n\n\nWo das Spitzenklöppeln in den ländlichen Grafschaften von Buckingham\n und Bedford aufhört, beginnt die Strohflechterei. Sie erstreckt sich\n über grossen Theil von Hertfordshire und die westlichen und nördlichen\n Theile von Essex. Es waren 1861 beschäftigt im Strohflechten und Stroh-\n hutmachen 40,043 Personen, 3815 davon männlichen Geschlechts aller\n Altersstufen, die andern weiblichen Geschlechts, und zwar 14,913 unter\n 20 Jahren, davon an 7000 Kinder. An die Stelle der Spitzenschulen\n treten hier die „straw plait schools“ (Strohflechtschulen). Die\n Kinder beginnen hier den Unterricht im Strohflechten gewöhnlich vom 4.,\n manchmal zwischen dem 3. und 4. Jahr Erziehung erhalten sie natürlich\n keine. Die Kinder selbst nennen die Elementarschulen „natural\n schools“ (natürliche Schulen) im Unterschied zu diesen Blutaussau-\n gungsanstalten, worin sie einfach an der Arbeit gehalten werden, um das von\n ihren halbverhungerten Müttern vorgeschriebne Machwerk, meist 30 Yards\n per Tag, zu verfertigen. Diese Mütter lassen sie dann oft noch zu Haus\n bis 10, 11, 12 Uhr Nachts arbeiten. Das Stroh schneidet ihnen Finger\n und Mund, durch den sie es beständig anfeuchten. Nach der von\nDr. Ballard resumirten Gesammtansicht der medizinischen Beamten\n Londons bilden 300 Kubikfuss den Minimalraum für jede Person in\n einem Schlaf- oder Arbeitszimmer. In den Strohflechtschulen ist der Raum\n aber noch spärlicher zugemessen als in den Spitzenschulen, „12⅔, 17,\n 18½ und unter 22 Kubikfuss für jede Person.“ Die kleineren dieser\n Zahlen, sagt Kommissair White, „repräsentiren weniger Raum als die\n Hälfte von dem, den ein Kind einnehmen würde, wenn verpackt in eine\n Schachtel von 3 Fuss nach allen Dimensionen.“ Diess der Lebensge-\n nuss der Kinder bis zum 12. oder 14. Jahr. Die elenden, verkommenen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"461\", \"facs\": \"#f0480\"} --> Eltern sinnen nur darauf aus den Kindern so viel als möglich herauszu-\n schlagen. Aufgewachsen fragen die Kinder natürlich keinen Deut nach\n den Eltern und verlassen sie. „Es ist kein Wunder, dass Unwissenheit\n und Laster überströmen in einer so aufgezüchteten Bevölkerung … Ihre\n Moral steht auf der niedrigsten Stufe . . . . Eine grosse Anzahl der Weiber\n hat illegitime Kinder und manche in so unreifem Alter, dass selbst die\n Vertrauten der Kriminalstatistik darüber erstarren“ [Note: l. c. p. XL, XLI.] . Und das Hei-\n mathsland dieser Musterfamilien ist, so sagt der sicher im Christenthum\n kompetente Graf Montalembert, Europa’s christliches Musterland!\n\n\n\n\nDer Arbeitslohn, in den eben behandelten Industriezweigen überhaupt\n jämmerlich (der ausnahmsweise Maximallohn der Kinder in den\n Strohflechtschulen 3 sh.), wird noch tief unter seinen Nominalbetrag herab-\n gedrückt durch das namentlich in den Spitzendistrikten allgemein vorherr-\n schende Trucksystem [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. I. Rep. 1863“, p. 185.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Verwohlfeilerung der Arbeitskraft durch blossen\n Missbrauch weiblicher und unreifer Arbeitskräfte, blossen Raub aller nor-\n malen Arbeits- und Lebensbedingungen, und blosse Brutalität der Ueber-\n und Nachtarbeit, stösst zuletzt auf gewisse nicht weiter überschreitbare Na-\n turschranken, also auch die auf diesen Grundlagen beruhende Verwohl-\n feilerung der Waaren und kapitalistische Exploitation überhaupt.\n Sobald dieser Punkt endlich erreicht ist, und es dauert lange, schlägt die\n Stunde für Einführung der Maschinerie und die nun rasche Verwand-\n lung der zersplitterten Hausarbeit (oder auch Manufaktur) in Fabrik-\n betrieb.\n\n\n\n\nDas kolossalste Beispiel dieser Bewegung liefert die Produktion von\n „Wearing Apparel“ (zum Anzug gehörige Artikel). Nach der Klas-\n sifikation der „Child. Empl. Comm.“ umfasst diese Industrie Strohhut-\n und Damenhutmacher, Kappenmacher, Schneider, milliners und dressma-\n kers [Note: Millinery bezieht sich eigentlich nur auf den Kopfputz, doch auch\n Damenmäntel und Mantillen, während Dressmakers mit unsern Putzmache-\n rinnen identisch sind.] , Hemdenmacher und Nätherinnen, Korsetten-, Handschuh-, Schuh-\n macher, nebst vielen kleineren Zweigen, wie Fabrikation von Kravatten, Hals-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"462\", \"facs\": \"#f0481\"} --> bändern u. s. w. Das in England und Wales in diesen Industrieen be-\n schäftigte weibliche Personal betrug 1861: 586,298, wovon minde-\n stens 115,242 unter 20, 16,650 unter 15 Jahren. Zahl dieser Ar-\n beiterinnen im Vereinigten Königreich (1861): 750,334. Die Zahl der\n gleichzeitig in Hut-, Schuh-, Handschuhmacherei und Schneiderei beschäf-\n tigten männlichen Arbeiter in England und Wales: 437,969, wovon\n 14,964 unter 15 Jahren, 89,285 fünfzehn- bis zwanzigjährig, 333,117\n über 20 Jahren. Es fehlen in dieser Angabe viele hierher gehörige\n kleinere Zweige. Nehmen wir aber die Zahlen, wie sie stehn, so ergiebt\n sich für England und Wales allein, nach dem Census von 1861, eine\n Summe von 1,024,277 Personen, also ungefähr so viel wie Ackerbau und\n Viehzucht absorbiren. Man fängt an zu verstehn, wozu die Maschinerie\n so ungeheure Produktenmassen hervorzaubern und so ungeheure Arbeiter-\n massen „freisetzen“ hilft.\n\n\n\n\nDie Produktion des „Wearing Apparel“ wird betrieben durch Ma-\n nufakturen, welche in ihrem Innern nur die Theilung der Arbeit repro-\n ducirten, deren membra disjecta sie fertig vorfanden, durch kleinere\n Handwerksmeister, die aber nicht wie früher für individuelle Kon-\n sumenten, sondern für Manufakturen und Waarenmagazine arbeiten, so\n dass oft ganze Städte und Landstriche solche Zweige wie Schusterei u. s. w.\n als Specialität ausüben, endlich im grössten Umfang durch s. g. Haus-\n arbeiter, welche das auswärtige Departement der. Manufakturen,\n Waarenmagazine und selbst der kleineren Meister bilden [Note: Die englische millinery und das dressmaking werden meist in den Bau-\n lichkeiten der Anwender, theils durch dort wohnhafte und engagirte Arbeiterinnen,\n theils durch auswärts wohnende Taglöhnerinnen betrieben.] . Die Masse\n des Arbeitsstoffs, Rohstoffe, Halbfabrikate u. s. w. liefert die grosse In-\n dustrie, die Masse des wohlfeilen Menschenmaterials (taillable à merci et\n misericorde) die durch grosse Industrie und Agrikultur „Freigesetzten“.\n Die Manufakturen dieser Sphäre verdankten ihren Ursprung hauptsächlich\n dem Bedürfniss des Kapitalisten eine jeder Bewegung der Nachfrage ent-\n sprechende schlagfertige Armee unter der Hand zu haben [Note: Kommissär White besuchte eine Manufaktur für Militärkleider, die 1000\n bis 1200 Personen, fast alle weiblichen Geschlechts, beschäftigte, eine Schuhmanu-\n faktur mit 1300 Personen, wovon beinahe die Hälfte Kinder und junge Personen\n u. s. w. („Child. Empl. Comm. II. Rep.“, p. XVII, n. 319.)] . Diese\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"463\", \"facs\": \"#f0482\"} --> Manufakturen liessen jedoch neben sich den zerstreuten handwerksmässigen\n und Hausbetrieb als breite Grundlage fortbestehn. Die grosse Produktion\n von Mehrwerth in diesen Arbeitszweigen, zugleich mit der progressiven\n Verwohlfeilerung ihrer Artikel, war und ist hauptsächlich geschuldet dem\n Minimum des zu kümmerlicher Vegetation nöthigen Arbeitslohns, verbun-\n den mit dem Maximum menschenmöglicher Arbeitszeit. Es war eben die\n Wohlfeilheit des in Waare verwandelten Menschenschweisses und Menschen-\n bluts, welche den Absatzmarkt beständig erweiterte und täglich erweitert,\n für England namentlich auch den Kolonialmarkt, wo überdem englische\n Gewohnheit und Geschmack vorherrschen. Endlich trat ein Knotenpunkt\n ein. Die Grundlage der alten Methode, bloss brutale Ausbeutung des Ar-\n beitermaterials, mehr oder minder begleitet von systematisch entwickelter\n Arbeitstheilung, genügte dem wachsenden Markt und der noch rascher\n wachsenden Konkurrenz der Kapitalisten nicht länger. Die Stunde der\n Maschinerie schlug. Die entscheidend revolutionäre Maschine,\n welche die sämmtlichen zahllosen Zweige dieser Produktionssphäre, wie\n Putzmacherei, Schneiderei, Schusterei, Nätherei, Hutmacherei u. s. w.\n gleichmässig ergreift, ist die — Nähmaschine.\n\n\n\n\nIhre unmittelbare Wirkung auf die Arbeiter ist ungefähr die aller\n Maschinerie, welche in der Periode der grossen Industrie neue Geschäfts-\n zweige erobert. Kinder im unreifsten Alter werden entfernt. Der Lohn\n der Maschinenarbeiter steigt verhältnissmässig zu dem der Hausar-\n beiter, wovon viele zu „den Aermsten der Armen“ („the poorest of the\n poor“) gehören. Der Lohn der besser gestellten Handwerker, mit denen\n die Maschine konkurrirt, sinkt. Die neuen Maschinenarbeiter sind aus-\n schliesslich Mädchen und junge Frauen. Mit Hilfe der mechanischen\n Kraft vernichten sie das Monopol der männlichen Arbeit in schwererem\n Werk und verjagen aus leichterem Massen alter Weiber und unreifer\n Kinder. Die übermächtige Konkurrenz erschlägt die schwächsten Hand-\n arbeiter. Das gräuliche Wachsthum des Hungertods (death from\n starvation) in London während des letzten Decenniums läuft parallel\n mit der Ausdehnung der Maschinennäherei [Note: Ein Beispiel. Am 26. Februar 1864 enthält der wöchentliche Sterblich-\n keitsbericht des Registrar General 5 Fälle von Hungertod. Am selben Tag\n berichtet die Times einen neuen Fall von Hungertod. Sechs Opfer des Hunger-\n tods in einer Woche!] . Die neuen Arbeiterinnen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"464\", \"facs\": \"#f0483\"} --> an der Nähmaschine, welche von ihnen mit Hand und Fuss oder mit der\n Hand allein, sitzend und stehend, je nach Schwere, Grösse und Specialität\n der Maschine, bewegt wird, verausgaben grosse Arbeitskraft. Ihre Be-\n schäftigung wird gesundheitswidrig durch die Dauer des Prozesses, ob-\n gleich er meist kürzer als im alten System. Ueberall, wo die Nähma-\n schine, wie beim Schuh-, Korsett-, Hutmachen u. s. w., ohnehin enge und\n überfüllte Werkstätten heimsucht, vermehrt sie die gesundheitswidrigen\n Einflüsse. „Die Wirkung,“ sagt Kommissär Lord, „beim Eintritt in\n niedrig gestochne Arbeitslokale, wo 30 bis 40 Maschinenarbeiter zusam-\n menwirken, ist unerträglich . . . . Die Hitze, theilweis den Gasöfen zur\n Wärmung der Bügeleisen geschuldet, ist schrecklich . . . . Wenn selbst in\n solchen Lokalen s. g. mässige Arbeitsstunden, d. h. von 8 Uhr Morgens\n bis 6 Uhr Abends, vorherrschen, fallen dennoch jeden Tag 3 oder 4 Per-\n sonen regelmässig in Ohnmacht“ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm.“ II. Rep. 1864, p. LXVII, n. 406—9,\n p. 84, n. 124, p. LXXIII, n. 441, p. 66, n. 6, p. 84, n. 126, p. 78, n. 85, p. 76,\n n. 69, p. LXXII, n. 483.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Umwälzung der gesellschaftlichen Betriebsweise,\n diess nothwendige Produkt der Umwandlung des Produktions-\n mittels, vollzieht sich in einem bunten Wirrwarr von Uebergangsfor-\n men. Sie wechseln mit dem Umfang, worin, und der Zeitlänge, während\n welcher die Nähmaschine den einen oder andern Industriezweig bereits er-\n griffen hat, mit der vorgefundnen Lage der Arbeiter, dem Uebergewicht des\n Manufaktur-, Handwerks- oder Hausbetriebs, dem Miethpreis der Arbeitslo-\n kale [Note: „The rental of premises required for work rooms seems the element\n which ultimately determines the point, and consequently it is in the metropolis,\n that the old system of giving work out to small employers and families has been\n longest retained, and earliest returned to.“ (l. c. p. 83, n. 123.) Die Schluss-\n phrase bezieht sich ausschliesslich auf Schusterei.]  u. s. w. In der Putzmacherei z. B., wo die Arbeit meist schon\n organisirt war, hauptsächlich durch einfache Cooperation, bildet die Nähma-\n schine zunächst nur einen neuen Faktor des Manufakturbetriebs. In der\n Schneiderei, Hemdenmacherei, Schusterei u. s. w. durchkreuzen sich alle For-\n men. Hier eigentlicher Fabrikbetrieb. Dort erhalten Zwischenanwender das\n Rohmaterial vom Kapitalisten en chef und gruppiren in „Kammern“ oder\n „Dachstuben“ 10 bis 50 und mehr Lohnarbeiter um Nähmaschinen.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"465\", \"facs\": \"#f0484\"} --> Endlich, wie bei aller Maschinerie, die kein gegliedertes System bildet, und\n im Zwergformat anwendbar ist, benutzen Handwerker oder Hausarbeiter,\n mit eigner Familie oder Zuziehung weniger fremder Arbeiter, auch ihnen\n selbst gehörige Nähmaschinen [Note: In der Handschuhmacherei u. s. w., wo die Lage der Arbeiter von der\n der Paupers kaum unterscheidbar, kömmt diess nicht vor.] . Thatsächlich überwiegt jetzt in Eng-\n land das System, dass der Kapitalist eine grössere Maschinenanzahl in\n seinen Baulichkeiten koncentrirt und dann das Maschinenprodukt zur wei-\n teren Verarbeitung unter die Armee der Hausarbeiter vertheilt [Note: l. c. n. 122.] . Die\n Buntheit der Uebergangsformen versteckt jedoch nicht die Tendenz zur\n Verwandlung in eigentlichen Fabrikbetrieb. Diese Tendenz wird\n genährt durch den Charakter der Nähmaschine selbst, deren mannigfaltige\n Anwendbarkeit zur Vereinigung früher getrennter Geschäftszweige in der-\n selben Baulichkeit und unter dem Kommando desselben Kapitals drängt;\n durch den Umstand, dass vorläufiges Nadelwerk und einige andere Operatio-\n nen am geeignetsten am Sitz der Maschine verrichtet werden; endlich durch\n die unvermeidliche Expropriation der Handwerker und Hausarbei-\n ter, die mit eignen Maschinen produciren. Diess Fatum hat sie zum Theil\n schon jetzt erreicht. Die stets wachsende Masse des in Nähmaschinen\n angelegten Kapitals [Note: In der für den Grossverkauf producirenden Stiefel- und Schuhmacherei\n von Leicester allein waren 1864 bereits 800 Nähmaschinen im Gebrauch.]  spornt die Produktion und erzeugt Marktstockun-\n gen, welche das Signal zum Verkauf der Nähmaschinen durch die Haus-\n arbeiter läuten. Die Ueberproduktion von solchen Maschinen selbst zwingt\n ihre absatzbedürftigen Producenten sie auf wöchentliche Miethe zu ver-\n leihn und schafft damit eine für die kleinen Maschineneigner tödtliche Kon-\n kurrenz [Note: l. c. p. 84, n. 124.] . Stets noch fortdauernde Konstruktionswechsel und Verwohl-\n feilerung der Maschinen depreciiren eben so beständig ihre alten Exem-\n plare und lassen sie nur noch massenhaft, zu Spottpreisen gekauft, in\n der Hand grosser Kapitalisten, profitlich anwenden. Endlich giebt die\n Substitution der Dampfmaschine für den Menschen, hier wie in allen\n ähnlichen Umwälzungsprozessen, den Ausschlag. Die Anwendung der\n Dampfkraft stösst im Anfang auf rein technische Hindernisse, wie Schütteln\n der Maschinen, Schwierigkeit in der Beherrschung ihrer Geschwindigkeit,\n [Running matter: I. 30] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"466\", \"facs\": \"#f0485\"} --> raschen Verderb der leichteren Maschinen u. s. w., lauter Hindernisse,\n welche die Erfahrung bald überwinden lehrt [Note: So im Armee-Kleidungsdepot zu Pimlico, London, in der Hemdenfabrik\n von Tillie und Henderson zu Londonderry, in der Kleiderfabrik der Firma Tait, zu\n Limerick, die an 1200 „Hände“ vernutzt.] . Wenn einerseits die\n Koncentration vieler Arbeitsmaschinen in grösseren Manufakturen zur An-\n wendung der Dampfkraft treibt, beschleunigt andrerseits die Konkurrenz\n des Dampfes mit Menschenmuskeln Koncentration von Arbeitern und Ar-\n beitsmaschinen in grossen Fabriken. So erlebt England gegenwärtig in\n der kolossalen Produktionssphäre des „Wearing Apparel“, wie den mei-\n sten übrigen Gewerken, die Umwälzung der Manufaktur, des Handwerks\n und der Hausarbeit in Fabrikbetrieb, nachdem alle jene Formen, unter\n dem Einfluss der grossen Industrie gänzlich verändert, zersetzt, ent-\n stellt, bereits längst alle Ungeheuerlichkeiten des Fabriksystems ohne\n seine positiven Entwicklungsmomente reproducirt und selbst übertrieben\n hatten [Note: „Tendency to factory system.“ (l. c. p. LXVII.) „The whole\n employment is at this time in a state of transition, and is undergoing the same\n change as that effected in the lace trade, weaving etc.“ (l. c. n. 405). „A com-\n plete Revolution.“ (l. c. p. XLVI, n. 318.) Zur Zeit der „Child. Empl.\n Comm.“ von 1840 war die Strumpfwirkerei noch Handarbeit. Seit 1846\n wurde verschiedenartige Maschinerie eingeführt, jetzt durch Dampf getrieben. Die\n Gesammtzahl der in der englischen Strumpfwirkerei beschäftigten Personen\n beiderlei Geschlechts und aller Altersstufen vom 3. Jahr an betrug 1862 ungefähr\n 120,000 Personen. Davon, nach Parliamentary Return vom 11. Februar, 1862\n doch nur 4063 unter der Botmässigkeit des Fabrikakts.] .\n\n\n\n\nDiese naturwüchsig vorgehende industrielle Revolution wird künstlich\n beschleunigt durch die Ausdehnung der Fabrikgesetze auf alle Indu-\n striezweige, worin Weiber, junge Personen und Kinder arbeiten. Die zwangs-\n mässige Regulation des Arbeitstags nach Länge, Pausen, Anfangs- und\n Endpunkt, das System der Ablösung für Kinder, der Ausschluss aller Kin-\n der unter einem gewissen Alter u. s. w. ernöthigen einerseits vermehrte\n Maschinerie [Note: So z. B. in der Töpferei berichtet die Firma Cochrane von der „Britain\n Pottery, Glasgow“: „To keep up our quantity, we have gone extensively into\n machines wrought by unskilled labour, and every day convinces us that we\n can produce a greater quantity than by the old method.“ („Reports of Insp.]  und Ersatz von Muskeln durch Dampf als Trieb-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"467\", \"facs\": \"#f0486\"} --> kraft [Note: So nach Einführung des Fabrikakts in die Töpferei grosse Zunahme der\n power jiggers statt der handmoved jiggers.] . Andrerseits, um im Raum zu gewinnen, was in der Zeit verloren geht,\n findet Streckung der gemeinschaftlich vernutzten Produktionsmittel statt, der\n Oefen, Baulichkeiten u. s. w., also in einem Wort grössere Koncentration der\n Produktionsmittel und entsprechende grössere Konglomeration von Arbeitern.\n Der leidenschaftlich wiederholte Haupteinwand jeder mit dem Fabrikgesetz\n bedrohten Manufaktur ist in der That die Nothwendigkeit grösserer Kapital-\n auslage, um das Geschäft in seinem alten Umfang fortzuführen. Was aber die\n Zwischenformen zwischen Manufaktur und Hausarbeit und letztre selbst\n betrifft, so versinkt ihr Boden mit der Schranke des Arbeitstags und der\n Kinderarbeit. Schrankenlose Ausbeutung wohlfeiler Arbeitskräfte\n bildet die einzige Grundlage ihrer Konkurrenzfähigkeit.\n\n\n\n\nWesentliche Bedingung des Fabrikbetriebs, namentlich sobald er der\n Regulation des Arbeitstags unterliegt, ist normale Sicherheit des Re-\n sultats, d. h. Produktion eines bestimmten Quantums Waare oder eines\n bezweckten Nutzeffekts in gegebnem Zeitraum. Die gesetzlichen Pausen\n des regulirten Arbeitstags unterstellen ferner plötzlichen und periodischen\n Stillstand der Arbeit ohne Schaden für das im Produktionsprozess befind-\n liche Machwerk. Diese Sicherheit des Resultats und Unterbrechungs-\n fähigkeit der Arbeit sind natürlich in rein mechanischen Gewerken leichter\n erzielbar als dort wo chemische und andere physische Prozesse eine Rolle\n spielen, wie z. B. in Töpferei, Bleicherei, Färberei, Bäckerei, den meisten\n Metallmanufakturen. Mit dem Schlendrian des unbeschränkten Arbeits-\n tags, der Nachtarbeit, und freier Menschenverwüstung, gilt jedes natur-\n wüchsige Hinderniss bald für eine ewige „Naturschranke“ der Pro-\n duktion. Kein Gift vertilgt Ungeziefer sichrer als das Fabrikgesetz solche\n „Naturschranken“. Niemand schrie lauter über „Unmöglichkeiten“ als\n die Herren von der Töpferei. 1864 wurde ihnen das Fabrikgesetz\n oktroyirt und alle Unmöglichkeiten waren schon 16 Monate später ver-\n schwunden. „Die“ durch das Fabrikgesetz hervorgerufene „verbesserte\n Methode Schliff durch Druck statt durch Ausdünstung zu machen, die neue\n Konstruktion der Oefen zum Trocknen der frischen Waare u. s. w. sind\n Ereignisse von grosser Wichtigkeit in der Kunst der Töpferei und bezeich-\n [Note: of Fact. 31. Oct. 1865“, p. 13.) „Die Wirkung des Fabrikakts ist zu weiterer\n Einführung von Maschinerie zu treiben.“ (l. c. p. 13, 14.)] \n [Running matter: 30*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"468\", \"facs\": \"#f0487\"} --> nen einen Fortschritt derselben, wie ihn das letzte Jahrhundert nicht auf-\n weisen kann … Die Temperatur der Oefen ist beträchtlich vermindert,\n bei beträchtlicher Abnahme im Kohlenkonsum und rascherer Wirkung auf\n die Waare“ [Note: l. c. p. 96 und 127.] . Trotz aller Prophezeiung stieg nicht der Kostpreis\n des Erdenguts, wohl aber die Produktenmasse, so dass die Ausfuhr\n der 12 Monate von December 1864 bis December 1865 einen Werth-\n überschuss von 138,628 Pfd. St. über den Durchschnitt der drei vorigen\n Jahre ergab. In der Fabrikation von Schwefelhölzern galt es als Natur-\n gesetz, dass Jungen, selbst während der Herunterwürgung ihres Mittags-\n mahls, die Hölzer in eine warme Phosphorkomposition tunkten, deren gifti-\n ger Dampf ihnen in das Gesicht stieg. Mit der Nothwendigkeit Zeit zu\n ökonomisiren, erzwang der Fabrikakt (1864) eine „dipping machine“\n (Eintauchungsmaschine), deren Dämpfe den Arbeiter nicht erreichen kön-\n nen [Note: Die Einführung dieser und andrer Maschinerie in die Schwefelholzfabrik\n hat in einem Departement derselben 230 junge Personen durch 32 Jungen und\n Mädchen von 14 bis 17 Jahren ersetzt. Diese Ersparung von Arbeitern wurde 1865\n weiter geführt durch Anwendung der Dampfkraft.] . So wird jetzt in den noch nicht dem Fabrikgesetz unterworfenen\n Zweigen der Spitzenmanufaktur behauptet, die Mahlzeiten könnten nicht\n regelmässig sein „wegen der verschiednen Zeitlängen, die verschiedne Spitzen-\n materialien zur Trocknung brauchen, und die von 3 Minuten auf eine Stunde\n und mehr variiren.“ Hierauf antworten die Kommissäre der „Children’s\n Employment Comm.“: „Die Umstände sind genau dieselben wie in der\n Tapetendruckerei. Einige der Hauptfabrikanten in diesem Zweig machten\n lebhaft geltend, die Natur der verwandten Materialien und die Verschie-\n denartigkeit der Prozesse, die sie durchlaufen, erlaubten ohne grossen\n Verlust keine plötzliche Stillsetzung der Arbeit für Mahlzeiten. . . . Durch\n die 6. Klausel der 6. Sektion des Factory Act’s Extension Act\n (1864) ward ihnen eine achtzehnmonatliche Frist vom Erlassungsdatum\n des Akts an eingeräumt, nach deren Ablauf sie sich den durch den Fabrik-\n akt specificirten Erfrischungspausen fügen müssten [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. II. Rep. 1864“, p. IX, n. 50.] . Kaum hatte\n das Gesetz parlamentarische Sanktion erhalten, als die Herren Fabrikanten\n auch entdeckten: „Die Missstände, die wir von der Einführung des Fabrik-\n gesetzes erwarteten, sind nicht eingetreten. Wir finden nicht, dass die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"469\", \"facs\": \"#f0488\"} --> Produktion irgendwie gelähmt ist. In der That, wir produciren mehr in\n derselben Zeit“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1865“, p. 22.] . Man sieht, das englische Parlament, dem sicher\n Niemand Genialität vorwerfen wird, ist durch Erfahrung zur Einsicht ge-\n langt, dass ein Zwangsgesetz alle s. g. Naturhindernisse der Pro-\n duktion gegen Beschränkung und Reglung des Arbeitstags einfach weg-\n diktiren kann. Bei Einführung des Fabrikakts in einen Industriezweig\n wird daher ein Termin von 6 bis 18 Monaten gestellt, innerhalb dessen es\n Sache der Fabrikanten ist die technischen Hindernisse wegzuräumen.\n Mirabeau’s: „Impossible! Ne me nommez jamais cet imbécil de mot!“ gilt\n namentlich für die moderne Technologie. Wenn aber das Fabrikgesetz so\n die zur Verwandlung des Manufakturbetriebs in Fabrikbetrieb nothwendi-\n gen materiellen Elemente treibhausmässig reift, beschleunigt es zugleich\n durch die Nothwendigkeit vergrösserter Kapitalauslage den Untergang der\n kleineren Meister und die Koncentration des Kapitals [Note: „Die nöthigen Verbesserungen können in vielen alten Manufakturen\n nicht eingeführt werden, ohne Kapitalauslage über die Mittel vieler gegenwärtiger\n Besitzer … Eine vorübergehende Disorganisation begleitet nothwendig die Ein-\n führung der Fabrikakte. Der Umfang dieser Disorganisation steht in direktem\n Verhältniss zur Grösse der zu heilenden Missstände.“ (l. c. p. 96, 97.)] .\n\n\n\n\nAbgesehn von den rein technischen und technisch beseitbaren Hinder-\n nissen stösst die Regulation des Arbeitstags auf unregelmässige Gewohnheiten\n der Arbeiter selbst, namentlich wo Stücklohn vorherrscht und Verbumm-\n lung der Zeit in einem Tages- oder Wochenabschnitt durch nachträgliche\n Ueberarbeit oder Nachtarbeit aufgemacht werden kann, eine Methode, die\n den erwachsnen Arbeiter brutalisirt, seine unreifen und weiblichen Genos-\n sen ruinirt [Note: In den Hochöfen z. B. „work towards the end of the week is generally much\n increased in duration, in consequence of the habit of the men of idling on Monday\n and occasionally during a part or the whole of Tuesdays also.“ („Child.\n Empl. Comm. IV. Rep.“, p. VI.) „The little masters generally have very\n irregular hours. They lose 2 or 3 days, and then work all night to make it up …\n They always employ their own children, if they have any.“ (l. c. p. VII.) „The\n want of regularity in coming to work, encouraged by the possibility and practice\n of making up for this by working longer hours.“ (l. c. p. XVIII.) „Enormous\n loss of time in Birmingham … idling part of the time, slaving the rest.“ (l. c.\n p. XI.)] . Obgleich diese Regellosigkeit in Verausgabung der Ar-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"470\", \"facs\": \"#f0489\"} --> beitskraft eine naturwüchsig rohe Reaktion gegen die Langweile mono-\n toner Arbeitsplackerei ist, entspringt sie jedoch in ungleich höherem Grad\n aus der Anarchie der Produktion selbst, die ihrerseits wieder ungezügelte\n Exploitation der Arbeitskraft durch das Kapital voraussetzt. Neben die\nallgemeinen periodischen Wechselfälle des industriellen Cyklus und\n die besondern Marktschwankungen in jedem Produktionszweig, treten\n namentlich die s. g. Saison, beruhe sie nun auf Periodicität der Schiff-\n fahrt günstiger Jahreszeiten oder auf der Mode, und die Plötzlichkeit grosser\n und in kürzester Frist auszuführender Ordres. Die Gewohnheit der letz-\n tern dehnte sich mit Eisenbahnen und Telegraphie aus. „Die Ausdeh-\n nung des Eisenbahnsystems“, sagt z. B. ein Londoner Fabrikant, „durch\n das ganze Land hat die Gewohnheit kurzer Ordres sehr gefördert. Käufer\n kommen jetzt von Glasgow, Manchester und Edinburg einmal in 14 Tagen\n oder so zu den City-Waarenhäusern für den Grossverkauf, denen wir die\n Waaren liefern. Sie geben Ordres, die unmittelbar ausgeführt werden\n müssen, statt vom Lager zu kaufen, wie es Gewohnheit war. In früheren\n Jahren waren wir stets fähig während der schlaffen Zeit für die Nachfrage\n der nächsten Saison vorauszuarbeiten, aber jetzt kann Niemand vorher-\n sagen, was dann in Nachfrage sein wird“ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. IV. Rep.“, p. XXXII, XXXIII. „The\n extension of the railway system is said to have contributed greatly to this custom\n of giving sudden orders, and the consequent hurry, neglect of mealtimes, and late\n hours of the work people.“ (l. c.)] .\n\n\n\n\nIn den noch nicht dem Fabrikgesetz unterworfenen Fabriken und\n Manufakturen herrscht periodisch die furchtbarste Ueberarbeit während der\n s. g. Saison, stossweis in Folge plötzlicher Ordres. Im auswärtigen De-\n partement der Fabrik, der Manufaktur und des Waarenmagazins, in der\n Sphäre der Hausarbeit, ohnehin durchaus unregelmässig, für ihr Rohma-\n terial und ihre Ordres ganz abhängig von den Launen des Kapitalisten,\n den hier keine Rücksicht auf Verwerthung von Baulichkeiten, Maschinen\n u. s. w. bindet und der hier nichts riskirt als die Haut der Arbeiter selbst,\n wird so systematisch eine stets disponible, industrielle Reservearmee gross-\n gezüchtet, decimirt während eines Theils des Jahrs durch unmenschlich-\n sten Arbeitszwang, während des andern Theils verlumpt durch Arbeits-\n mangel. „Die Anwender“, sagt die „Child. Empl. Comm.“, „exploitiren\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"471\", \"facs\": \"#f0490\"} --> die gewohnheitsmässige Unregelmässigkeit der Hausarbeit, um sie in Zei-\n ten, wo Extrawerk nöthig, bis 11, 12, 2 Uhr Nachts, in der That, wie\n die stehende Phrase lautet, auf alle Stunden hinaufzuforciren, und\n diess in Lokalen, „wo der Gestank hinreicht, euch niederzuschmettern\n („the stench is enough to knock you down“). Ihr geht vielleicht bis an\n die Thüre und öffnet sie, aber schaudert zurück vor weiterem Vorgehn“ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. II. Rep.“, p. XXXV, n. 235 und 237.] .\n „Es sind komische Käuze, unsre Anwender“, sagt einer der verhörten\n Zeugen, ein Schuster, „sie glauben, es thue einem Jungen keinen Harm,\n wenn er während eines halben Jahrs todtgerackert und während der an-\n dern Hälfte fast gezwungen wird herumzuludern“ [Note: l. c. p. 127, n. 56.] .\n\n\n\n\nWie die technischen Hindernisse, so wurden und werden diese s. g.\n „Geschäftsgewohnheiten“ („usages which have grown with the\n growth of trade“) von interessirten Kapitalisten als „Naturschranken“\n der Produktion behauptet, ein Lieblingsschrei diess der Baumwolllords zur\n Zeit als das Fabrikgesetz sie zuerst bedrohte. Obgleich ihre Industrie mehr\n als jede andre auf dem Weltmarkt und daher der Schifffahrt beruht, strafte\n die Erfahrung sie Lügen. Seitdem wird jedes angebliche „Geschäftshin-\n derniss“ von den englischen Fabrikinspektoren als hohle Flause behandelt [Note: „With respect to the loss of trade by the non-completion of shipping\n orders in time, I remember that this was the pet argument of the factory masters\n in 1832 and 1833. Nothing that can be advanced now on this subject could have\n the force that it had then, before steam had halved all distances and established\n new regulations for transit. It quite failed at that time of proof when put to the\n test, and again it will certainly fail should it have to be tried.“ („Reports of\n Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 54, 55.] .\n Die gründlich gewissenhaften Untersuchungen der „Child. Empl.\n Comm.“ beweisen in der That, dass in einigen Industrieen die bereits an-\n gewandte Arbeitsmasse nur gleichmässiger über das ganze Jahr vertheilt\n würde durch die Regulation des Arbeitstags [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. IV. Rep.“, p. XVIII, n. 118.] , dass letztere der erste\n rationelle Zügel für die menschenmörderischen, inhaltslosen und an sich\n dem System der grossen Industrie unangemessnen Flatterlaunen der\nMode [Note: John Bellers bemerkt schon 1699: „The uncertainty of fashions does in-\n crease necessitous Poor. It has two great mischiefs in it: 1st) The journeymen are mi-] , dass die Entwicklung der oceanischen Schifffahrt und der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"472\", \"facs\": \"#f0491\"} --> Kommunikationsmittel überhaupt den eigentlich technischen Grund der\n Saison-Arbeit aufgehoben hat [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. V. Rep.“, p. 171, n. 31.] , dass alle andern angeblich unkontro-\n lirbaren Umstände weggeräumt werden durch weitere Baulichkeiten, zu-\n sätzliche Maschinerie, vermehrte Anzahl der gleichzeitig beschäftigten Ar-\n beiter [Note: So heisst es z. B. in den Zeugenaussagen von Bradforder Exporthändlern:\n „Unter diesen Umständen ist es klar, dass Jungen nicht länger als von 8 Uhr\n Morgens bis 7 oder 7½ Uhr Abends in den Waarenhäusern beschäftigt zu werden\n brauchen. Es ist nur eine Frage von Extra-Auslage und Extra-Händen. Die\n Jungen brauchten nicht so spät in die Nacht hineinzuarbeiten, wären einige An-\n wender nicht so profithungrig; eine Extramaschine kostet nur 16 oder 18 Pfd. St.\n … Alle Schwierigkeiten entspringen aus ungenügenden Vorrichtungen und\n Raummangel.“ (l. c. p. 171, n. 35 und 38.)]  und von selbst folgenden Rückschlag auf das System des\n Grosshandels [Note: l. c. Ein Londoner Fabrikant, der übrigens die zwangsweise Regulation\n des Arbeitstags als Schutzmittel der Arbeiter gegen die Fabrikanten und der\n Fabrikanten selbst gegen den Grosshandel betrachtet, sagt aus:\n „Der Druck in unsrem Geschäft ist verursacht durch die Verschiffer, die z. B.\n Waare mit einem Segelschiff verschicken wollen, um für eine bestimmte Saison\n an Ort und Stelle zu sein und zugleich die Frachtdifferenz zwischen Segelschiff\n und Dampfschiff einzustecken, oder von zwei Dampfschiffen das frühere wählen,\n um vor ihren Konkurrenten auf dem auswärtigen Markt zu erscheinen.“ (l. c.\n p. 8, n. 32.)] . Jedoch versteht sich das Kapital, wie es wiederholt\n durch den Mund seiner Repräsentanten erklärt, zu solcher Umwälzung\n „nur unter dem Druck eines allgemeinen Parlaments-\n akts“ [Note: „This could be obviated“, sagt ein Fabrikant, „at the expense of an\n enlargement of the works under the Pressure of a General Act of Par-\n liament.“ (l. c. p. X, n. 38.)] , der den Arbeitstag zwangsgesetzlich regulirt.\n\n\n\n\nDie Fabrikgesetzgebung, diese erste bewusste und plan-\n [Note: serable in winter for want of work, the mercers and master - weavers not daring to\n lay out their stocks to keep the journeymen imployed before the spring comes and\n they know what the fashion will then be; 2dly) In the spring the journeymen are\n not sufficient, but the master-weavers must draw in many practices, that they may\n supply the trade of the kingdom in a quarter or half a year, which robs the plow\n of hands, drains the country of labourers, and in a great part stocks the city with\n beggars, and starves some in winter that are ashamed to beg.“ („Essays about\n the Poor, Manufactures etc.“, p. 9.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"473\", \"facs\": \"#f0492\"} -->mässige Rückwirkung der Gesellschaft auf die naturwüchsige Gestalt\n ihres Produktionsprozesses, ist, wie man gesehn, ebenso sehr ein nothwen-\n diges Produkt der grossen Industrie, als Baumwollgarn, Selfactors und der\n elektrische Telegraph. Bevor wir zu ihrer bevorstehenden Verallge-\n meinerung in England übergehn, sind noch einige nicht auf die Stun-\n denzahl des Arbeitstags bezügliche Klauseln des englischen Fabrikakts\n kurz zu erwähnen.\n\n\n\n\nAbgesehn von ihrer Redaktion, welche dem Kapitalisten ihre Um-\n gehung erleichtert, sind die Gesundheitsklauseln äusserst mager,\n in der That beschränkt auf Vorschriften für Weisswaschung und einige\n sonstige Reinlichkeitsmassregeln, Ventilation, und Schutz gegen gefähr-\n liche Maschinerie. Wir kommen im dritten Buch auf den fanatischen\n Kampf der Fabrikanten gegen die Klausel zurück, die ihnen eine geringe\n Ausgabe zum Schutz der Gliedmassen ihrer „Hände“ aufoktroyirt. Hier\n bewährt sich wieder glänzend das Freihandelsdogma, dass in einer Gesell-\n schaft antagonistischer Interessen Jeder das Gemeinwohl durch Verfolgung\n seines Eigennutzes fördert. Ein Beispiel genügt. Man weiss, dass sich\n während der letztverflossenen zwanzigjährigen Periode die Flachsindu-\n strie und mit ihr die scutching mills (Fabriken zum Schlagen und\n Brechen des Flachses) in Irland sehr vermehrt haben. Es gab dort 1864\n an 1800 dieser mills. Periodisch im Herbst und Winter werden haupt-\n sächlich junge Personen und Weiber, die Söhne, Töchter und Frauen der\n benachbarten kleinen Pächter, lauter mit Maschinerie ganz unbekannte\n Personen, von der Feldarbeit weggeholt, um die Walzwerke der scutch-\n ing mills mit Flachs zu füttern. Die Unfälle sind nach Umfang und\n Intensivität gänzlich beispiellos in der Geschichte der Maschinerie. Eine\n einzige scutching mill zu Kildinan (bei Cork) zählte von 1852 bis 1856\n sechs Todesfälle und 60 schwere Verstümmelungen, welchen allen durch die\n einfachsten Anstalten, zur Kost von wenigen Schillingen, vorgebeugt wer-\n den konnte. Dr. W. White, der certifying surgeon der Fabriken zu\n Downpatrick, erklärt in einem officiellen Bericht vom 15. December 1865:\n „Die Unfälle in scutching mills sind furchtbarster Art. In vielen Fällen\n wird ein Viertheil des Körpers vom Rumpfe gerissen. Tod oder eine Zu-\n kunft elenden Unvermögens und Leidens sind gewöhnliche Folgen der\n Wunden. Die Zunahme der Fabriken in diesem Lande wird natürlich\n diese schauderhaften Resultate ausdehnen. Ich bin überzeugt, dass durch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"474\", \"facs\": \"#f0493\"} --> geeignete Staatsüberwachung der scutching mills grosse Opfer von\n Leib und Leben zu vermeiden sind“ [Note: l. c. p. XV, n. 72 sqq.] . Was könnte die kapitalistische\n Produktionsweise besser charakterisiren als die Nothwendigkeit, ihr durch\n Zwangsgesetz von Staatswegen die einfachsten Reinlichkeits- und Gesund-\n heitsvorrichtungen aufzuherrschen? „Der Fabrikakt von 1864 hat in den\n Töpfereien über 200 Werkstätten geweisst und gereinigt, nach zwan-\n zigjähriger oder gänzlicher Enthaltung von jeder sol-\n chen Operation (diess ist die „Abstinenz“ des Kapitals!), in\n Plätzen, wo 27,800 Arbeiter beschäftigt sind, und bisher, während übermäs-\n siger Tages-, oft Nacht-Arbeit, eine mephitische Atmosphäre einathmeten,\n welche eine sonst vergleichungsweis harmlose Beschäftigung mit Krank-\n heit und Tod schwängerte. Der Akt hat die Ventilationsmittel sehr ver-\n mehrt“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1865“, p. 96.] . Zugleich zeigt dieser Zweig des Fabrikakts schlagend, wie\n die kapitalistische Produktionsweise ihrem Wesen nach über einen gewissen\n Punkt hinaus jede rationelle Verbesserung ausschliesst. Es ward wiederholt\n bemerkt, dass die englischen Aerzte aus einem Munde 500 Kubikfuss Luft-\n raum per Person für kaum genügen des Minimum bei fortgesetzter Arbeit erklä-\n ren. Nun wohl! Wenn der Fabrikakt indirekt durch alle seine Zwangsmass-\n regeln die Verwandlung kleinerer Werkstätten in Fabriken beschleunigt,\n daher indirekt in das Eigenthumsrecht der kleineren Kapitalisten eingreift\n und den grossen das Monopol sichert, so würde die gesetzliche Aufherr-\n schung des nöthigen Luftraums für jeden Arbeiter in der Werkstätte Tau-\n sende von kleinen Kapitalisten mit einem Schlag direkt expropriiren! Sie\n würde die Wurzel der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise angreifen, d. h. die\n Selbstverwerthung des Kapitals, ob gross oder klein, durch „freien“ An-\n kauf und Konsum der Arbeitskraft. Vor diesen 500 Kubikfuss Luft geht\n daher der Fabrikgesetzgebung der Athem aus. Der „Board of Health“,\n die industriellen Untersuchungskommissionen, die Fabrikinspektoren wie-\n derholen wieder und wieder die Nothwendigkeit der 500 Kubikfüsse und\n die Unmöglichkeit, sie dem Kapital aufzuoktroyiren. Sie erklären so in\n der That Schwindsucht und andre Lungenkrankheiten der Arbeit für eine\n Lebensbedingung des Kapitals [Note: Man hat erfahrungsmässig gefunden, dass ungefähr 25 Kubikzoll Luft\n bei jeder Athmung mittlerer Intensivität von einem gesunden Durchschnittsindi-] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"475\", \"facs\": \"#f0494\"} -->\n\nArmselig wie die Erziehungsklauseln des Fabrikakts im Ganzen\n erscheinen, proklamirten sie den Elementarunterricht als Zwangs-\n bedingung der Arbeit [Note: Nach dem englischen Fabrikakt können die Eltern Kinder unter 14\n Jahren nicht in die „kontrolirten“ Fabriken schicken, ohne ihnen zugleich Ele-\n mentarunterricht ertheilen zu lassen. Der Fabrikant ist verantwortlich für die Be-\n folgung des Gesetzes. „Factory education is compulsory, and it is a condi-\n tion of labour.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1865“, p. 111.)] . Ihr Erfolg bewies zuerst die Möglichkeit\n der Verbindung von Unterricht und Gymnastik [Note: Ueber die vortheilhaften Erfolge der Verbindung von Gymnastik (für\n Jungen auch militärischer Exercitien) mit Zwangsunterricht der Fabrikkinder und\n Armenschüler sieh die Rede von N. W. Senior im 7. jährlichen Kongress der\n „National Association for the Promotion of Social Science“ in\n „Report of Proceedings etc. Lond. 1863“, p. 63, 64, ebenso den Bericht\n der Fabrikinspektoren für 31. Oct. 1865, p. 118, 119, 120, 126 sqq.]  mit Handarbeit, also\n auch von Handarbeit mit Unterricht und Gymnastik. Die Fabrikinspektoren\n entdeckten bald aus den Zeugenverhören der Schulmeister, dass die Fa-\n brikkinder, obgleich sie nur halb so viel Unterricht geniessen als die\n regelmässigen Tagesschüler, eben so viel und oft mehr lernen. „Die\n Sache ist einfach. Diejenigen, die sich nur einen halben Tag in der\n Schule aufhalten, sind stets frisch und fast immer fähig und willig Unter-\n richt zu empfangen. Das System halber Arbeit und halber\n Schule macht jede der beiden Beschäftigungen zur Ausruhung und\n Erholung von der andern und folglich viel angemessner für das Kind als\n die ununterbrochne Fortdauer einer von beiden. Ein Junge, der von Mor-\n gens früh in der Schule sitzt, und nun gar bei heissem Wetter, kann un-\n möglich mit einem andern wetteifern, der munter und aufgeweckt von\n seiner Arbeit kommt“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact.“ l. c. p. 118. Ein naiver Seiden-\n fabrikant erklärt den Untersuchungskommissären der „Child. Empl. Comm.“:] . Weitere Belege findet man in Senior’s\n [Note: viduum konsumirt wird, und ungefähr 20 Athmungen per Minute vorgehn. Der\n Luftkonsum eines Individuums in 24 Stunden ergäbe danach ungefähr 720,000\n Kubikzoll oder 416 Kubikfüsse. Man weiss aber, dass die einmal eingeathmete\n Luft nicht mehr zu demselben Prozess dienen kann, bevor sie in der grossen Werk-\n stätte der Natur gereinigt wird. Nach den Experimenten von Valentin und Brunner\n scheint ein gesunder Mann ungefähr 1300 Kubikzoll Kohlensäure per Stunde aus-\n zuathmen; diess ergäbe ungefähr 8 Unzen solider Kohle, von der Lunge in 24\n Stunden abgeworfen. „Jeder Mann sollte wenigstens 800 Kubikfuss haben.“\n (Huxley)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"476\", \"facs\": \"#f0495\"} --> Rede auf dem sociologischen Kongress zu Edinburg, 1863. Er zeigt hier\n auch u. a. nach, wie der einseitige, unproduktive und verlängerte Schul-\n tag der Kinder der höheren und mittleren Klassen die Arbeit der Lehrer\n nutzlos vermehrt, „während er Zeit, Gesundheit und Energie der Kinder\n nicht nur fruchtlos, sondern absolut schädlich verwüstet“ [Note: Senior l. c. p. 66. Wie die grosse Industrie, auf einem gewissen Höhe-\n grad, durch die Umwälzung der materiellen Produktionsweise und der gesellschaft-\n lichen Produktionsverhältnisse auch die Köpfe umwälzt, zeigt schlagend ein Ver-\n gleich zwischen der Rede des N. W. Senior von 1863 und seiner Philippika gegen\n das Fabrikgesetz von 1833, oder ein Vergleich der Ansichten des erwähnten Kon-\n gresses mit der Thatsache, dass es in gewissen ländlichen Theilen Englands armen\n Eltern immer noch bei Strafe des Hungertods verboten ist, ihre Kinder zu\n erziehen. So z. B. berichtet Herr Snell als gewöhnliche Praxis in Sommersetshire,\n dass wenn eine arme Person Pfarreihilfe anspricht, sie gezwungen wird, ihre Kinder\n aus der Schule zu nehmen. So erzählt Herr Wollaston, Pfarrer zu Feltham, von\n Fällen, wo alle Unterstützung gewissen Familien versagt wurde, „weil sie ihre\n Jungen zur Schule schickten“!] . Aus dem\nFabriksystem, wie man im Detail bei Robert Owen verfolgen\n kann, entspross der Keim der Erziehung der Zukunft, welche für\nalle Kinder über einem gewissen Alter produktive Arbeit mit\nUnterricht und Gymnastik verbinden wird, nicht nur als eine Me-\n thode zur Steigerung der gesellschaftlichen Produktion, sondern als die\n einzige Methode zur Produktion vollseitig entwickelter Menschen.\n\n\n\n\nMan hat gesehn, dass die grosse Industrie die manufakturmässige Thei-\n lung der Arbeit mit ihrer lebenslänglichen Annexation eines ganzen Menschen\n an eine Detailoperation technologisch aufhebt, während zugleich die kapi-\n talistische Form der grossen Industrie jene Arbeitstheilung\n noch monströser reproducirt, in der eigentlichen Fabrik durch Verwand-\n lung des Arbeiters in den selbstbewussten Zubehör einer Theilmaschine,\n überall sonst theils durch sporadischen Gebrauch der Maschinen und Ma-\n [Note: „Ich bin durchaus überzeugt, dass das wahre Geheimniss der Produktion tüchtiger\n Arbeiter gefunden ist in der Vereinigung der Arbeit mit Unterricht von der Periode\n der Kindheit an. Natürlich muss die Arbeit weder zu anstrengend, noch widerlich\n und ungesund sein. Ich wünschte, meine eignen Kinder hätten Arbeit und Spiel\n zur Abwechslung von der Schule.“ („Child. Empl. Comm.“ V. Rep.,\n p. 82, n. 36.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"477\", \"facs\": \"#f0496\"} --> schinenarbeit [Note: Wo handwerksmässige Maschinen, durch Menschenkraft getrieben, direkt\n oder indirekt mit entwickelter und daher mechanische Triebkraft voraussetzender\n Maschinerie konkurriren, geht eine grosse Umwandlung vor mit Bezug auf den\n Arbeiter, der die Maschine treibt. Ursprünglich ersetzte die Dampfmaschine diesen\n Arbeiter, jetzt soll er die Dampfmaschine ersetzen. Die Spannung und Veraus-\n gabung seiner Arbeitskraft wird daher monströs, und nun gar für Unerwachsne,\n die zu dieser Tortur verurtheilt sind! So fand der Kommissär Longe in Coventry\n und Umgebung Jungen von 10 bis 15 Jahren zum Drehn der Bandstühle verwandt,\n abgesehn von jüngeren Kindern, die Stühle von kleinerer Dimension zu drehn\n hatten. „Es ist ausserordentlich mühsame Arbeit. The boy is a mere sub-\n stitute for steam-power.“ („Child. Empl. Comm. V. Rep. 1866“,\n p. 114, n. 6.) Ueber die mörderischen Folgen „dieses Systems der Skla-\n verei“, wie der officielle Bericht es nennt, l. c. sq.] , theils durch Einführung von Weiber-, Kinder- und un-\n geschickter Arbeit als neuer Grundlage der Arbeitstheilung. Der Wider-\n spruch zwischen der manufakturmässigen Theilung der Arbeit und dem\n Wesen der grossen Industrie macht sich gewaltsam geltend. Er er-\n scheint u. a. in der furchtbaren Thatsache, dass ein grosser Theil der in\n den modernen Fabriken und Manufakturen beschäftigten Kinder, vom zar-\n testen Alter festgeschmiedet an die einfachsten Manipulationen, Jahrelang\n exploitirt wird, ohne Erlernung irgend einer Arbeit, die sie später auch\n nur in derselben Manufaktur oder Fabrik brauchbar machte. In den\n englischen Buchdruckereien z. B. fand früher ein dem System der alten\n Manufaktur und des Handwerks entsprechender Uebergang der Lehrlinge\n von leichteren zu inhaltsvolleren Arbeiten statt. Sie machten einen Lern-\n gang durch, bis sie fertige Drucker waren. Lesen und schreiben zu\n können war für alle ein Handwerkserforderniss. Alles das änderte sich\n mit der Druckmaschine. Sie verwendet zwei Sorten von Arbeitern, einen\n erwachsnen Arbeiter, den Maschinenaufseher, und Maschinenjungen, meist\n von 11 bis 17 Jahren, deren Geschäft ausschliesslich darin besteht, einen\n Bogen Papier der Maschine zu unterbreiten oder ihr den gedruckten Bogen\n zu entziehen. Sie verrichten, in London namentlich, diese Plackerei\n 14, 15, 16 Stunden ununterbrochen während einiger Tage in der Woche\n und oft 36 Stunden nach einander mit nur zwei Stunden Rast für Mahlzeit\n und Schlaf [Note: l. c. p. 3, n. 24.] ! Ein grosser Theil von ihnen kann nicht lesen und sie\n sind in der Regel ganz verwilderte, abnorme Geschöpfe. „Um sie zu ihrem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"478\", \"facs\": \"#f0497\"} --> Werk zu befähigen, ist keine intellektuelle Ziehung irgend einer Art nöthig;\n sie haben wenig Gelegenheit für Geschick und noch weniger für Urtheil;\n ihr Lohn, obgleich gewissermassen hoch für Jungen, wächst nicht verhält-\n mässig, wie sie selbst heranwachsen und die grosse Mehrzahl hat keine\n Aussicht auf den einträglicheren und verantwortlicheren Posten des Ma-\n schinenaufsehers, weil auf jede Maschine nur ein Aufseher und oft 4 Jungen\n kommen“ [Note: l. c. p. 7, n. 59, 60.] . Sobald sie zu alt für ihre kindische Arbeit werden, also\n wenigstens im 17. Jahr, entlässt man sie aus der Druckerei. Sie werden\n zu Rekruten des Verbrechens. Einige Versuche ihnen anderswo Beschäf-\n tigung zu verschaffen, scheiterten an ihrer Unwissenheit, Rohheit, körper-\n lichen und geistigen Verkommenheit.\n\n\n\n\nWas von der manufakturmässigen Theilung der Arbeit im\n Innern der Werkstatt, gilt von der Theilung der Arbeit im Innern der\n Gesellschaft. So lange Handwerk und Manufaktur die allgemeine Grund-\n lage der gesellschaftlichen Produktion bilden, ist die Subsumtion des Produ-\n centen unter einen ausschliesslichen Produktionszweig, die Zerreissung der\n ursprünglichen Mannigfaltigkeit seiner Beschäftigungen [Note: „In einigen Theilen von Hochschottland … erschienen viele Schaf-\n hirten und cotters mit Frau und Kind, nach dem Statistical Account, in\n Schuhen, die sie selbst gemacht aus Leder, das sie selbst gegerbt, in Kleidern, die\n keine Hand ausser ihrer eignen angetastet, deren Material sie selbst von den\n Schafen geschoren oder wofür sie den Flachs selbst gebaut hatten. In die Zu-\n bereitung der Kleider ging kaum irgend ein gekaufter Artikel ein, mit Ausnahme\n von Pfrieme, Nadel, Fingerhut und sehr wenigen Theilen des im Weben ange-\n wandten Eisenwerks. Die Farben wurden von den Weibern selbst von Bäumen,\n Gesträuchen und Kräutern gewonnen u. s. w.“ (Dugald Stewart l. c.\n p. 327.)] , ein nothwendiges\n Entwicklungsmoment. Auf jener Grundlage findet jeder besondre Produk-\n tionszweig empirisch die ihm entsprechende technologische Gestalt, vervoll-\n kommnet sie langsam und krystallisirt sie rasch, sobald ein gewisser Reifegrad\n erlangt ist. Was hier und da Wechsel hervorruft, ist ausser neuem Arbeits-\n stoff, den der Handel liefert, die allmälige Aenderung des Arbeitsinstru-\n ments. Die erfahrungsmässig entsprechende Form einmal gewonnen, ver-\n knöchert auch es, wie sein oft jahrtausendlanger Uebergang aus der Hand einer\n Generation in die der andern beweist. Es ist charakteristisch, dass bis ins\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"479\", \"facs\": \"#f0498\"} --> 18. Jahrhundert hinein die besondern Gewerke mysteries (mystères) [Note: In dem berühmten „Livre des métiers“ des Etienne Boileau\n wird unter andrem vorgeschrieben, dass ein Geselle bei seiner Aufnahme unter die\n Meister einen Eid leiste, „seine Brüder brüderlich zu lieben, sie zu stützen, jeder\n in seinem métier, d. h. nicht freiwillig die Gewerksgeheimnisse zu\n verrathen, und sogar im Interesse der Gesammtheit nicht zur Empfehlung seiner\n eignen Waare den Käufer auf die Fehler des Machwerks von Andern aufmerk-\n sam zu machen.“] \n hiessen, in deren Dunkel nur der empirisch und professionell Eingeweihte\n eindringen konnte. Die grosse Industrie zerriss den Schleier, der den\n Menschen ihren eignen gesellschaftlichen Produktionsprocess versteckte\n und die verschiednen naturwüchsig besonderten Produktionszweige gegen\n einander und sogar dem in jedem Zweig Eingeweihten zu Räthseln machte.\n Ihr Princip, jeden Produktionsprozess an und für sich, und zunächst ohne alle\n Rücksicht auf die menschliche Hand, in seine constituirenden Elemente auf-\n zulösen, schuf die ganz moderne Wissenschaft der Technologie. Die bunt-\n scheckigen, scheinbar zusammenhangslosen und verknöcherten Gestalten des\n gesellschaftlichen Produktionsprozesses lösten sich auf in bewusst plan-\n mässige und je nach dem bezweckten Nutzeffekt systematisch besonderte\n Anwendungen der Naturwissenschaft. Die Technologie entdeckte ebenso\n die wenigen grossen Grundformen der Bewegung, worin alles pro-\n duktive Thun des menschlichen Körpers, trotz aller Mannigfaltigkeit der an-\n gewandten Instrumente, nothwendig vorgeht, ganz so wie die Mechanik\n durch die grösste Komplikation der Maschinerie sich über die beständige\n Wiederholung der einfachen mechanischen Potenzen nicht täuschen lässt.\n Die moderne Industrie betrachtet und behandelt die vorhandne Form eines\n Produktionsprozesses nie als definitiv. Ihre technologische Basis ist daher\n revolutionär, während die aller früheren Produktionsweisen wesentlich kon-\n servativ war [Note: „Die Bourgeoisie kann nicht existiren ohne die Produktionsinstrumente,\n also die Produktionsverhältnisse, also sämmtliche gesellschaftlichen Verhältnisse\n fortwährend zu revolutioniren. Unveränderte Beibehaltung der alten Produktions-\n weise war dagegen die erste Existenzbedingung aller früheren industriellen Klassen.\n Die fortwährende Umwälzung der Produktion, die ununterbrochene Erschütterung\n aller gesellschaftlichen Zustände, die ewige Unsicherheit und Bewegung zeichnen\n die Bourgeoisepoche vor allen früheren aus. Alle festen, eingerosteten Verhält-\n nisse mit ihrem Gefolge von altehrwürdigen Vorstellungen und Anschauungen] . Durch Maschinen, chemische Prozesse und andre Me-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"480\", \"facs\": \"#f0499\"} --> thoden wälzt sie beständig mit der technischen Grundlage der materiellen\n Produktion die Funktionen der Arbeiter und die gesellschaftlichen Kombi-\n nationen des Arbeitsprozesses um. Sie revolutionirt damit ebenso bestän-\n dig die Theilung der Arbeit im Innern der Gesellschaft und schleudert un-\n aufhörlich Kapitalmassen und Arbeitermassen aus einem Produktionszweig\n in den andern. Die Natur der grossen Industrie bedingt daher Wechsel\n der Arbeit, Fluss der Funktion, allseitige Beweglichkeit des Arbeiters.\n Andrerseits reproducirt sie in ihrer kapitalistischen Form die\n alte Theilung der Arbeit mit ihren knöchernen Partikularitäten. Man hat\n gesehn, wie dieser absolute Widerspruch alle Ruhe, Festigkeit, Sicher-\n heit der Lebenslage des Arbeiters aufhebt, ihm mit dem Arbeitsmittel bestän-\n dig das Lebensmittel aus der Hand zu schlagen [Note: „You take my life\n When you do take the means whereby I live.“ (Shakespeare.)]  und mit seiner Theil-\n funktion ihn selbst überflüssig zu machen droht, wie dieser Widerspruch\n im ununterbrochnen Opferfest der Arbeiterklasse, masslosester Vergeudung\n der Arbeitskräfte und den Verheerungen gesellschaftlicher Anarchie sich\n austobt. Diess ist die negative Seite. Wenn aber der Wechsel der Arbeit\n sich jetzt nur als überwältigendes Naturgesetz und mit der blind zerstö-\n renden Wirkung eines Naturgesetzes durchsetzt, das überall auf Hinder-\n nisse stösst [Note: Ein französischer Arbeiter schreibt bei seiner Rückkehr von San-Fran-\n cisco: „Ich hätte nie geglaubt, dass ich fähig wäre alle die Gewerbe auszuüben,\n die ich in Californien betrieben habe. Ich war fest überzeugt, dass ich ausser zur\n Buchdruckerei zu nichts gut sei . . . . Einmal in der Mitte dieser Welt von Aben-\n teurern, welche ihr Handwerk leichter wechseln als ihr Hemde, meiner Treu!\n ich that wie die andern. Da das Geschäft der Minenarbeit sich nicht einträglich\n genug auswies, verliess ich es und zog in die Stadt, wo ich der Reihe nach Typo-\n graph, Dachdecker, Bleigiesser u. s. w. wurde. In Folge dieser Erfahrung, zu\n allen Arbeiten tauglich zu sein, fühle ich mich weniger als Molluske und mehr\n als Mensch.“ (A. Corbon: „De l’enseignement professionnel.“\n 2ème éd. p. 50.)] , macht die grosse Industrie durch ihre Katastrophen selbst\n [Note: werden aufgelöst, alle neugebildeten veralten, ehe sie verknöchern können. Alles\n Ständische und Stehende verdampft, alles Heilige wird entweiht, und die Menschen\n sind endlich gezwungen, ihre Lebensstellungen, ihre gegenseitigen Beziehungen\n mit nüchternen Augen anzusehn.“ (F. Engels und Karl Marx: „Mani-\n fest der Kommunistischen Partei. Lond. 1848“, p. 5.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"481\", \"facs\": \"#f0500\"} --> es zur Frage von Leben oder Tod, den Wechsel der Arbeiten und daher\n möglichste Vielseitigkeit des Arbeiters als allgemeines gesellschaftliches\n Gesetz der Produktion anzuerkennen, und die Verhältnisse seiner normalen\n Verwirklichung gemäss umzugestalten. Sie macht es zu einer Frage von\n Leben oder Tod, die Ungeheuerlichkeit einer elenden, für die wechselnden\n Exploitationsbedürfnisse des Kapitals in Reserve gehaltenen, disponiblen\n Arbeiterbevölkerung zu ersetzen durch die absolute Disponibilität des Men-\n schen für wechselnde Arbeitserfordernisse; das Theilindividuum, welches\n blosser Träger einer gesellschaftlichen Detailfunktion ist, durch das\n total entwickelte Individuum, für welches die gesellschaftlichen Funktionen\n eben so viele verschiedne Bethätigungsweisen sind. Ein auf Grundlage\n der grossen Industrie naturwüchsig entwickeltes Moment dieses Umwäl-\n zungsprozesses sind polytechnische und agronomische Schulen, ein anderes\n sind die „écoles d’enseignement professionnel“, worin die\n Kinder der Arbeiter einigen Unterricht in der Technologie und praktischen\n Handhabe der verschiednen Produktionsinstrumente erhalten. Wenn die\n Fabrikgesetzgebung als erste, dem Kapital nothdürftig abgerungene Kon-\n cession nur Elementarunterricht mit fabrikmässiger Arbeit verbindet, unter-\n liegt es keinem Zweifel, dass die unvermeidliche Eroberung der politischen\n Gewalt durch die Arbeiterklasse auch dem technologischen Unterricht,\n theoretisch und praktisch, seinen Platz in den Arbeiterschulen erobern\n wird. Es unterliegt ebenso wenig einem Zweifel, dass die kapitalisti-\n sche Form der Produktion und die ihr entsprechenden ökonomischen\n Arbeiterverhältnisse im diametralsten Widerspruch stehn mit solchen Um-\n wälzungsfermenten und ihrem Ziel, der Aufhebung der alten\n Theilung der Arbeit. Die Entwicklung der Widersprüche einer\n geschichtlichen Produktionsform ist jedoch der einzig geschichtliche Weg\n ihrer Auflösung und Neugestaltung. „Ne sutor ultra crepidam“!, diess\n nec plus ultra hand werksmässiger Weisheit, wurde zur furchtbaren Narr-\n heit von dem Moment, wo der Uhrmacher Watt die Dampfmaschine, der\n Barbier Arkwright den Kettenstuhl, der Juwelierarbeiter Fulton das\n Dampfschiff erfunden hatte [Note: John Bellers, ein wahres Phänomen in der Geschichte der politischen\n Oekonomie, begriff schon Ende des 17. Jahrhunderts mit vollster Klarheit die\n nothwendige Aufhebung der jetzigen Erziehung und Arbeitstheilung, welche\n Hypertrophie und Atrophie auf beiden Extremen der Gesellschaft, wenn auch in] .\n\n\n [Running matter: I. 31] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"482\", \"facs\": \"#f0501\"} -->\n\nSoweit die Fabrikgesetzgebung die Arbeit in Fabriken, Manufakturen\n u. s. w. regulirt, erscheint diess zunächst nur als Einmischung in die Ex-\n ploitationsrechte des Kapitals. Jede Regulation der s. g. Hausar-\n beit [Note: Diese geht übrigens grossentheils auch in kleineren Werkstätten\n vor, wie wir gesehn bei der Spitzenmanufaktur und Strohflechterei, und wie nament-\n lich auch an den Metallmanufakturen in Sheffield, Birmingham u. s. w. ausführ-\n licher gezeigt werden könnte.]  stellt sich dagegen sofort als direkter Eingriff in die patria\n Potestas dar, d. h. modern interpretirt, in die elterliche Auto-\n rität, ein Schritt, wovor das zartfühlende englische Parlament lang zu-\n rückzubeben affektirte. Die Gewalt der Thatsachen zwang jedoch endlich\n anzuerkennen, dass die grosse Industrie mit der ökonomischen Grundlage\n des alten Familienwesens und der ihr entsprechenden Familienarbeit auch\n die alten Familienverhältnisse selbst auflöst. Das Recht der Kinder\n musste proklamirt werden. „Unglücklicher Weise“, heisst es im Schluss-\n bericht der „Child. Empl. Comm.“ von 1866, „leuchtet aus der Gesammt-\n heit der Zeugenaussagen hervor, dass die Kinder beiderlei Geschlechts gegen\n Niemand so sehr des Schutzes bedürfen als gegen ihre Eltern.“ Das System\n der masslosen Exploitation der Kinderarbeit überhaupt und der Hausarbeit\n im Besondern wird dadurch „erhalten, dass die Eltern über ihre jungen\n und zarten Sprösslinge eine willkührliche und heillose Gewalt ohne Zügel\n oder Kontrole ausüben … Eltern dürfen nicht die absolute Macht be-\n sitzen, ihre Kinder zu reinen Maschinen zu machen, um so und so viel\n wöchentlichen Lohn herauszuschlagen … Kinder und junge Personen\n haben ein Recht auf den Schutz der Legislatur wider den Missbrauch der\n elterlichen Gewalt, der ihre physische Kraft vorzeitig bricht und sie degra-\n dirt auf der Staffel moralischer und intellektueller Wesen“ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. V. Rep.“, p. XXV, n. 162 und II. Rep.,\n p. XXXVIII, n. 285, 289, p. XXXV, n. 191.] . Es ist\n [Note: entgegengesetzter Richtung, erzeugen. Er sagt u. a. schön: „An idle learning\n being little better than the Learning of Idleness … Bodily Labour, it’s a\n primitive institution of God . . . . Labour being as proper for the bodies health,\n as eating is for its living; for what pains a man saves by Ease, he will find in\n Disease … Labour adds oyl to the lamp of life when thinking inflames it …\n A childish silly employ (diess ahnungsvoll gegen die Basedows und ihre mo-\n dernen Nachstümper), leaves the children’s minds silly.“ („Proposals for\n raising a Colledge of Industry of all useful Trades and Husban-\n dry. Lond. 1696“, p. 12, 14, 18.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"483\", \"facs\": \"#f0502\"} --> jedoch nicht der Missbrauch der elterlichen Gewalt, der die direkte oder\n indirekte Exploitation unreifer Arbeitskräfte durch das Kapital schuf, son-\n dern es ist umgekehrt die kapitalistische Exploitationsweise, welche die\n elterliche Gewalt, durch Aufhebung der ihr entsprechenden ökonomischen\n Grundlage, zu einem Missbrauch gemacht hat. So furchtbar und ekelhaft\n nun die Auflösung des alten Familienwesens innerhalb des kapitalistischen\n Systems erscheint, so schafft nichtsdestoweniger die grosse Industrie mit\n der entscheidenden Rolle, die sie den Weibern, jungen Personen und Kin-\n dern beiderlei Geschlechts in gesellschaftlich organisirten Produktionspro-\n zessen jenseits der Sphäre des Hauswesens zuweist, die neue ökonomische\n Grundlage für eine höhere Form der Familie und des Verhältnisses beider\n Geschlechter. Es ist natürlich ebenso albern die christlich germanische\n Form der Familie für absolut zu halten als die altrömische Form, oder die\n altgriechische, oder die orientalische, die übrigens untereinander eine ge-\n schichtliche Entwicklungsreihe bilden. Ebenso leuchtet ein, dass die Zu-\n sammensetzung des kombinirten Arbeitspersonals aus Individuen beiderlei\n Geschlechts und der verschiedensten Altersstufen, obgleich in ihrer naturwüch-\n sig brutalen, kapitalistischen Form, wo der Arbeiter für den Produktions-\n prozess, nicht der Produktionsprozess für den Arbeiter da ist, Pestquelle\n des Verderbs und der Sklaverei, unter entsprechenden Verhältnissen um-\n gekehrt zur Quelle humaner Entwicklung umschlagen muss [Note: „Factory labour may be as pure and as excellent as domestic labour,\n and perhaps more so.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1865“,\n p. 127.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Nothwendigkeit, das Fabrikgesetz aus einem Ausnahmsgesetz\n für Spinnereien und Webereien, diese ersten Gebilde des Maschinenbetriebs,\n in ein Gesetz aller gesellschaftlichen Produktion zu verallgemeinern, ent-\n springt, wie man sah, aus dem geschichtlichen Entwicklungsgang der\n grossen Industrie, auf deren Hintergrund die überlieferte Gestalt von Manu-\n faktur, Handwerk und Hausarbeit gänzlich umgewälzt wird, die Manufaktur\n beständig in die Fabrik, das Handwerk beständig in die Manufaktur um-\n schlägt, und endlich die Sphären des Handwerks und der Hausarbeit sich\n in relativ wunderbar kurzer Zeit zu Jammerhöhlen gestalten, wo die tollsten\n Ungeheuerlichkeiten der kapitalistischen Exploitation ihr freies Spiel trei-\n ben. Es sind zwei Umstände, welche zuletzt den Ausschlag geben, erstens\n die stets neu wiederholte Erfahrung, dass das Kapital, sobald es der\n [Running matter: 31*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"484\", \"facs\": \"#f0503\"} --> Staatskontrole nur auf einzelnen Punkten der gesellschaftlichen Peripherie\n anheimfällt, sich um so massloser auf den andern Punkten entschädigt [Note: l. c. p. 27, 32.] ,\n zweitens der Schrei der Kapitalisten selbst nach Gleichheit der Kon-\n kurrenzbedingungen, d. h. gleichen Schranken der Ar-\n beitsexploitation [Note: Massenhafte Belege dazu in den „Rep. of Insp. of Fact.“] . Hören wir hierüber zwei Herzensstösse. Die\n Herrn W. Cooksley (Nagel-, Ketten- u. s. w. Fabrikanten zu Bristol) führ-\n ten die Fabrikregulation freiwillig in ihrem Geschäft ein. „Da das alte,\n unregelmässige System in den benachbarten Werken fortdauert, sind sie\n der Unbill ausgesetzt ihre Arbeitsjungen zur Fortsetzung der Arbeit an-\n derswo nach 6 Uhr Abends verlockt (enticed) zu sehn. ‚Diess‘, sagen\n sie natürlich, ‚ist eine Ungerechtigkeit gegen uns und ein Verlust, da es\n einen Theil der Kraft der Jungen erschöpft, deren voller\n Vortheil uns gebührt‘ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. V. Rep.“, p. IX, n. 35.] . Herr J. Simpson (Paper-Box Bag maker,\n London) erklärt den Kommissären der „Child. Empl. Comm.“: „Er wolle\n jede Petition für Einführung der Fabrikakte unterzeichnen. Wie es sei,\nfühle er sich stets rastlos des Nachts („he always felt restless at\n night“), nach Schluss seiner Werkstatt, bei dem Gedanken, dass andre\n länger arbeiten liessen und ihm Aufträge vor der Nase wegschnappten“ [Note: l. c. n. 28.] .\n „Es wäre ein Unrecht,“ sagt die Child. Empl. Comm. zusammenfassend,\n „gegen die grösseren Arbeitsanwender ihre Fabriken der Regulation zu unter-\n werfen, während in ihrem eignen Geschäftszweig der Kleinbetrieb keiner\n gesetzlichen Beschränkung der Arbeitszeit unterliegt. Zur Ungerechtigkeit\n ungleicher Konkurrenzbedingungen in Bezug auf die Arbeitsstunden bei\n Ausnahme kleinerer Werkstätten, käme noch der andere Nachtheil für die\n grösseren Fabrikanten hinzu, dass ihre Zufuhr von jugendlicher und weib-\n licher Arbeit abgelenkt würde nach den vom Gesetz verschonten Werk-\n stätten. Endlich gäbe diess Anstoss zur Vermehrung der kleineren Werk-\n stätten, die fast ausnahmslos die mindest günstigen für Gesundheit, Kom-\n fort, Erziehung und allgemeine Verbesserung des Volks sind“ [Note: l. c. n. 165—167. Vgl. über die Vorzüge des Grossbetriebes verglichen\n mit dem Zwergbetrieb „Child. Empl. Comm. III. Rep.“, p. 13, n. 144,\n p. 25, n. 121, p. 26, n. 125, p. 27, n. 140 u. s. w.] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"485\", \"facs\": \"#f0504\"} -->\n\nIn ihrem Schlussbericht schlägt die „Children’s Employment“ Kommis-\n sion vor, über 1,400,000 Kinder, junge Personen und Weiber, wovon unge-\n fähr die Hälfte vom Kleinbetrieb und der Hausarbeit exploitirt wird, dem\n Fabrikakt zu unterwerfen [Note: Die zu massregelnden Industriezweige sind: Spitzenmanufaktur, Strumpf-\n wirkerei, Strohflechten, Manufaktur von Wearing Apparel mit ihren zahlreichen\n Arten, künstliche Blumenmacherei, Schuh-, Hut- und Handschuhmacherei,\n Schneiderei, alle Metallfabriken, von den Hochöfen bis zu Nadelfabriken u. s. w.,\n Papierfabrik, Glasmanufaktur, Tabaksmanufaktur, India-Rubber Werke, Litzen-\n fabrikation (für die Weberei), Handteppich-Weberei, Regenschirm- und Parasol-\n manufaktur, Fabrikation von Spindeln und Spulen, Buchdruckerei, Buchbinderei,\n Schreibmaterialienhandel (Stationery, dazu gehörig Verfertigung von Papier-\n schachteln, Karten, Papierfärben u. s. w.), Seilerei, Manufaktur von Gagatschmuck,\n Ziegeleien, Hand-Seidenmanufaktur, Coventry-Weberei, Salz-, Talglicht- und\n Cementirwerke, Zuckerraffinerie, Zwiebackmachen, verschiedne Holz- und andre\n vermischte Arbeiten.] . „Sollte,“ sagt sie, „das Parlament unsern\n Vorschlag in seinem ganzen Umfang annehmen, so ist es zweifellos, dass\n solche Gesetzgebung den wohlthätigsten Einfluss ausüben würde, nicht nur\n auf die Jungen und Schwachen, mit denen sie sich zunächst beschäftigt,\n sondern auf die noch grössere Masse von erwachsnen Arbeitern, die direkt\n (die Weiber) und indirekt (die Männer) unter ihren Wirkungskreis fallen.\n Sie würde ihnen regelmässige und ermässigte Arbeitsstunden aufzwingen;\n sie würde einen gesunderen und reinlicheren Zustand der Arbeitslokale her-\n beiführen; sie würde den Vorrath physischer Kraft, wovon ihr eignes\n Wohlergehen und das des Landes so sehr abhängt, haushalten und häufen;\n sie würde die aufsprossende Generation vor der Ueberanstrengung in frühem\n Alter schützen, welche ihre Konstitution untergräbt und zu vorzeitigem\n Verfall führt; sie würde schliesslich, wenigstens bis zum 13. Jahr die Ge-\n legenheit des Elementarunterrichts bieten und damit der unglaublichen Un-\n wissenheit ein Ende machen, die so treu in den Kommissionsberichten ge-\n schildert ist und nur mit qualvollster Empfindung und dem tiefen Gefühl\n nationaler Erniedrigung betrachtet werden kann“ [Note: l. c. p. XXV, n. 169.] . Das Toryministerium\n kündigte in der Thronrede vom 5. Februar 1867 an, dass es die Vorschläge\n der industriellen Untersuchungskommission in „Bills“ formulirt habe. Dazu\n bedurfte es eines neuen zwanzigjährigen Experimentum in corpore vili.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"486\", \"facs\": \"#f0505\"} --> Bereits im Jahr 1840 war eine parlamentarische Kommission zur Unter-\n suchung über Kinderarbeit ernannt worden. Ihr Bericht von 1842 ent-\n rollte in den Worten N. W. Senior’s „das furchtbarste Gemälde von\n Habsucht, Selbstsucht und Grausamkeit der Kapitalisten und Eltern, von\n Elend, Degradation und Zerstörung der Kinder und jungen Personen, das\n jemals das Auge der Welt schlug … Man wähnt vielleicht, der Bericht\n beschreibe die Greuel eines vergangenen Zeitalters … Diese Greuel dauern\n fort, intensiver als jemals … Die 1842 denuncirten Missbräuche stehn\n heut zu Tage (Oktober 1863) in voller Blüthe … Der Bericht von\n 1842 wurde ohne weitere Notiznahme zu den Akten gelegt und da lag er\n zwanzig volle Jahre, während deren man den physisch, geistig und mora-\n lisch niedergetretenen Kindern erlaubte, die Eltern der jetzigen Generation\n zu werden“ [Note: Senior l. c. p. 55 sqq.] . Die jetzige Untersuchungskommission schlägt ebenfalls\n neue Reglung der Minenindustrie vor [Note: Das letzte Blaubuch über die Minen: „Report from the Select\n Committee on Mines together with etc. Evidence. 23. July 1866“,\n ist ein starker Folioband, enthält aber nur das Zeugenverhör. Der Bericht des\n vom Unterhaus aus seinem eignen Schoss ernannten Comité’s selbst besteht aus 5,\n sage fünf Zeilen, des Inhalts, dass es nichts zu sagen weiss und noch mehr (!)\n Zeugen verhört werden müssen. In der Minenindustrie, wohlbemerkt, gehn\n die Interessen der Landlords und industriellen Kapitalisten Hand in Hand. Die\n Art der Zeugenexamination erinnert an die cross examinations vor den englischen\n Gerichten, wo der Advokat durch unverschämte, sinnverwirrende Kreuz- und\n Querfragen den Zeugen aus der Fassung zu bringen und ihm die Worte im Munde\n zu verdrehn sucht. Die Advokaten hier sind die parlamentarischen Examinatoren\n selbst; darunter Minen-Eigner und Exploiteurs, die Zeugen Minen-\n arbeiter, meist in Kohlenbergwerken. Die ganze Farce ist zu charakteristisch\n für den Geist des Kapitals, um hier nicht einige Auszüge zu geben. Vorher\n sei noch bemerkt, dass durch Gesetz von 1842 die Arbeit des weiblichen Ge-\n schlechts und aller Kinder unter 10 Jahren in den Minen verboten wurde. Ein\n neuer Akt, „The Mines Inspecting Act“ von 1860, schreibt ausser Minen-\n inspektion u. s. w. vor, dass Jungen zwischen 10 und 12 Jahren nicht beschäftigt\n werden sollen, wenn sie kein Schulcertifikat haben oder während bestimmter\n Stundenzahl Schule besuchen. Das ganze Gesetz ist eine Nullität, schon wegen\n der lächerlich kleinen Anzahl der Inspektoren, der Nichtigkeit ihrer Vollmachten\n und andrer Umstände, die man im Verlauf sehn wird. Zur leichteren Uebersicht] . Endlich brachte Professor\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"487\", \"facs\": \"#f0506\"} -->Fawcett im Unterhaus (1867) ähnliche Resolutionen für die Agrikultur-\n arbeiter ein, das Kabinet übernahm jedoch die Initiative.\n\n\n\n\n [Note: gebe ich die Resultate der Untersuchung u. s. w. in Rubriken. Ich erinnere, dass\n Frage und obligate Antwort in den englischen Blue Books numerirt sind, und dass\n die Zeugen, deren Aussagen hier citirt werden, Arbeiter in Kohlenbergwerken.\n 1) Beschäftigung der Jungen vom 10. Jahr an in den Minen.\n Die Arbeit, nebst obligatem Gang von und zu den Bergwerken, dauert in der Regel\n 14 bis 15 Stunden, ausnahmsweise länger, von 3, 4, 5 Uhr Morgens bis 4 und 5\n Uhr Abends. (n. 6, 452, 83.) Die erwachsnen Arbeiter arbeiten in zwei\n Gängen, jeder 8 Stunden, aber kein solcher Wechsel für die Jungen, um die\n Kosten zu sparen. (n. 80, 203, 204.) Die jungen Kinder hauptsächlich verwandt\n zum Oeffnen und Schliessen der Zugthüren in den verschiednen Abtheilungen des\n Bergwerks, die älteren zu schwerer Arbeit, Kohlentransport u. s. w. (n. 122,\n 739, 1747.) Die langen Arbeitsstunden unter der Erde dauern bis zum 18. oder 22.\n Jahr, wenn der Uebergang zur eigentlichen Minenarbeit stattfindet. (n. 161.)\nDie Kinder und jungen Personen werden heutzutag härter abge-\n plackt als zu irgend einer früheren Periode. (n. 1663—67.) Die Minen-\n arbeiter verlangen fast einstimmig einen Parlamentsakt zum Verbot der Minenar-\n beit bis zum 14. Jahr. Und nun fragt Vivian Hussey (selbst Minenexploiteur):\n „Hängt diess Verlangen nicht von der grössern oder geringern Armuth der Eltern\n ab? Wäre es nicht hart, wo der Vater todt, oder verstümmelt u. s. w., der Familie\n diese Ressource zu entziehn? Und es muss doch eine allgemeine Regel herrschen.\n Wollt ihr in allen Fällen die Beschäftigung der Kinder bis zum 14. Jahr unter\n der Erde verbieten?“ Antwort: „In allen Fällen.“ (n. 107—110.) Hussey:\n „Wenn die Arbeit vor 14 Jahren in den Minen verboten, würden die Eltern die\n Kinder nicht in Fabriken u. s. w. stecken? — In der Regel, nein.“ (n. 174.)\nArbeiter: „Das Auf- und Zuschliessen der Thüren sieht leicht aus. Es ist ein\n sehr qualvolles Geschäft. Vom beständigen Zug abgesehn, ist der Junge gefangen\n gesetzt, ganz so gut wie in einer dunkeln Kerkerzelle.“ Bourgeois Hussey:\n „Kann der Junge nicht lesen während der Thürwacht, wenn er ein Licht hat? —\n Erstens müsste er sich die Kerzen kaufen. Aber ausserdem würde es ihm nicht\n erlaubt werden. Er ist da, um auf sein Geschäft aufzupassen, er hat eine Pflicht\n zu erfüllen. Ich habe nie einen Jungen in der Grube lesen sehn.“ (n. 141—60.)\n 2) Erziehung. Die Minenarbeiter verlangen Gesetz für Zwangsunter-\n richt der Kinder, wie in den Fabriken. Sie erklären die Klausel des Akts von\n 1860, wonach Erziehungscertifikat zur Verwendung von 10—12 Jahren erfordert,\n für rein illusorisch. Das „peinliche“ Verhörverfahren der kapitalistischen In-\n struktionsrichter wird hier wahrhaft drollig. (n. 115.) „Ist der Akt mehr nöthig\n gegen Anwender oder Eltern? — Gegen Beide.“ (n. 116.) „Mehr gegen den\n einen als den andern? — Wie soll ich das beantworten?“ (n. 137.) „Zeigen\n die Anwender irgend ein Verlangen, die Arbeitsstunden dem Schulunterricht anzu-] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"488\", \"facs\": \"#f0507\"} -->\n\nWenn die Verallgemeinerung der Fabrikgesetzgebung als physisches\n und geistiges Schutzmittel der Arbeiterklasse unvermeidlich geworden ist,\n [Note: passen? — Niemals.“ (n. 211.) „Verbessern die Minenarbeiter hinterher ihre\n Erziehung? — Sie verschlechtern sich im Allgemeinen; sie nehmen böse Gewohn-\n heiten an; sie verlegen sich auf Trunk und Spiel und dergleichen, und werden\n ganz und gar schiffbrüchig.“ (n. 109.) „Warum nicht die Kinder in Abend-\n schulen schicken? — In den meisten Kohlendistrikten existiren keine. Aber die\n Hauptsache ist, von der langen Ueberarbeit sind sie so erschöpft, dass ihnen die\n Augen vor Müdigkeit zufallen.“ „Also“, schliesst der Bourgeois, „Ihr seid gegen\n Erziehung? — Bei Leibe nicht, aber u. s. w.“ (n. 443.) „Sind die Minenbe-\n sitzer u. s. w. nicht durch den Akt von 1860 gezwungen, Schulcertifikate zu ver-\n langen, wenn die Kinder zwischen 10 und 12 Jahren? — Durch das Gesetz, ja,\n aber die Anwender thun es nicht.“ (n. 444.) „Nach eurer Ansicht ist diese\n Gesetzklausel nicht allgemein ausgeführt? — Sie wird gar nicht ausge-\n führt.“ (n. 717.) „Sind die Minenarbeiter sehr interessirt an der Erziehungs-\n frage? — Die grosse Mehrzahl.“ (n. 718.) „Sind sie ängstlich für Erzwingung\n des Gesetzes? — Die grosse Mehrzahl.“ (n. 720.) „Warum denn erzwingen sie\n das Gesetz nicht? — Mancher Arbeiter wünscht einen Jungen ohne Schulcertifikat\n zu verweigern, aber er wird ein gezeichneter Mann (a marked man).“ (n.\n 721.) „Gezeichnet durch wen? — Durch seinen Anwender.“ (n. 722.) „Ihr\n glaubt doch nicht etwa, dass die Anwender einen Mann für Gehorsam gegen das\n Gesetz verfolgen würden? — Ich glaube, sie würden es thun.“ (n. 723.) „Warum\n verweigern die Arbeiter nicht solche Jungen anzuwenden? — Es ist nicht ihrer\n Wahl überlassen.“ (n. 1634.) „Ihr verlangt Parlamentsintervention? — Wenn\n irgend etwas Wirksames für die Erziehung der Kinder der Grubenarbeiter geschehn\n soll, so muss sie durch Parlamentsakt zwangsmässig gemacht werden.“\n (n. 1636.) „Soll das für die Kinder aller Arbeiter von Grossbritanien gelten oder\n nur für die Grubenarbeiter? — Ich bin hier, um im Namen der Grubenarbeiter zu\n sprechen.“ (n. 1638.) „Warum Grubenkinder von andern unterscheiden? —\n Weil sie eine Ausnahme von der Regel bilden.“ (n. 1639.) „In welcher Hin-\n sicht? — In physischer.“ (n. 1640.) „Warum sollte Erziehung für sie werth-\n voller sein, als für Knaben von andern Klassen? — Ich sage nicht, dass sie werth-\n voller für sie ist, aber wegen ihrer Ueberarbeitung in den Minen haben sie weniger\n Chancen für Erziehung in Tags- und Sonntagsschulen.“ (n. 1644.) „Nicht\n wahr, es ist unmöglich Fragen dieser Art absolut zu behandeln?“ (n. 1646.)\n „Sind genug Schulen in den Distrikten? — Nein.“ (n. 1647.) „Wenn der\n Staat verlangte, dass jedes Kind zur Schule geschickt, wo sollen dann die Schulen\n für alle die Kinder herkommen? — Ich glaube, sobald es die Umstände gebieten,\n werden die Schulen von selbst entspringen. Die grosse Mehrzahl nicht nur der\n Kinder, sondern der erwachsnen Minenarbeiter kann weder schreiben, noch lesen.“\n (n. 705, 726.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"489\", \"facs\": \"#f0508\"} --> verallgemeinert und beschleunigt sie andrerseits, wie bereits angedeutet,\n die Verwandlung zerstreuter Arbeitsprozesse auf Zwergmassstab in kom-\n [Note: 3) Weiberarbeit. Arbeiterinnen werden zwar seit 1842 nicht mehr\nunter, wohl aber über der Erde zum Aufladen der Kohlen u. s. w., Schleppen\n der Kufen zu den Kanälen und Eisenbahnwagen, Sortiren der Kohlen u. s. w. ver-\n braucht. Ihre Anwendung hat sehr zugenommen in den letzten 3 bis 4 Jahren.\n (n. 1727.) Es sind meist Weiber, Töchter und Witwen von Grubenarbeitern, vom\n 12. bis zum 50. und 60. Jahre. (n. 645, 1779.) (n. 648.) „Was denken die\n Minenarbeiter von weiblicher Beschäftigung bei Bergwerken? — Sie verdammen\n sie allgemein.“ (n. 649.) „Warum? — Sie betrachten es erniedrigend für das\n Geschlecht … Sie tragen Art von Mannskleidern. In vielen Fällen wird alle\n Scham unterdrückt. Manche Weiber rauchen. Die Arbeit ist so schmutzig, wie\n die in den Gruben selbst. Darunter sind viele verheirathete Frauen, die ihre häus-\n lichen Pflichten nicht erfüllen können.“ (n. 651 sqq.) (n. 709.) „Können\n die Witwen ein so einträgliches Geschäft (8—10 sh. wöchentlich) anderswo finden?\n — Ich kann darüber nichts sagen.“ (n. 710.) „Und dennoch (Herz von Stein!)\n seid Ihr entschlossen, ihnen diesen Lebensunterhalt abzuschneiden? — Sicher.“\n (n. 1715.) „Woher diese Stimmung? — Wir, Minenarbeiter, haben zu viel\n Respekt für das schöne Geschlecht, um sie zur Kohlengrube verdammt zu sehn …\n Diese Arbeit ist grossentheils sehr schwer. Viele dieser Mädchen heben 10 Tonnen\n per Tag.“ (n. 1732.) „Glaubt Ihr, dass die in den Bergwerken beschäftigten\n Arbeiterinnen unmoralischer sind als die in den Fabriken beschäftigten? — Der\n Prozentsatz der Schlechten ist grösser als unter den Fabrikmädchen.“ (n. 1733.)\n „Aber Ihr seid auch mit dem Stand der Moralität in den Fabriken nicht zufrieden?\n — Nein.“ (n. 1734.) „Wollt Ihr denn auch die Weiberarbeit in den Fabriken\n verbieten? — Nein, ich will nicht.“ (n. 1735.) „Warum nicht? — Sie ist für\n das weibliche Geschlecht ehrenvoller und passender.“ (n. 1736.) „Dennoch\n ist sie schädlich für ihre Moralität, meint Ihr? — Nein, lange nicht so sehr wie\n die Arbeit in der Grube. Ich spreche übrigens nicht nur aus moralischen, sondern\n auch aus physischen und socialen Gründen. Die sociale Degradation der Mädchen\n ist jammervoll und extrem. Wenn diese Mädchen Frauen der Minenarbeiter\n werden, leiden die Männer tief unter dieser Degradation, und es treibt sie fort\n von Haus und in den Soff.“ (n. 1737.) „Aber gälte nicht dasselbe für die in\n Eisenwerken beschäftigten Weiber? — Ich kann nicht für andre Geschäfts-\n zweige sprechen.“ (n. 1740.) „Aber welcher Unterschied ist denn zwischen\n den in Eisenwerken und Bergwerken beschäftigten Weibern? — Ich habe mich\n nicht mit dieser Frage beschäftigt.“ (n. 1741.) „Könnt Ihr einen Unterschied\n zwischen der einen und der andern Klasse entdecken? — Ich habe nichts darüber\n vergewissert, kenne aber durch Visite von Haus zu Haus den schmählichen Zustand\n der Dinge in unsrem Distrikt.“ (n. 1750.) „Hättet Ihr nicht grosse Lust Weiber-\n beschäftigung überall abzuschaffen, wo sie degradirend ist? — Ja … die besten] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"490\", \"facs\": \"#f0509\"} --> binirte Arbeitsprozesse auf grosser, gesellschaftlicher Stufenleiter, die Kon-\n centration des Kapitals und das Fabrikregime selbst. Sie zerstört alle\n [Note: Gefühle der Kinder müssen von mütterlicher Zucht herkommen.“ (n. 1751.)\n „Aber das passt ja auch auf agrikole Beschäftigung der Weiber? — Die dauert nur\n zwei Saisons, bei uns arbeiten sie alle 4 Saisons durch, manchmal Tag und Nacht,\n nass bis auf die Haut, ihre Konstitution geschwächt, ihre Gesundheit gebrochen.“\n (n. 1753.) „Ihr habt die Frage (nämlich der Weiberbeschäftigung) nicht all-\n gemein studirt? — Ich habe um mich her geschaut, und kann so viel sagen,\n dass ich nirgendwo etwas der weiblichen Beschäftigung an den Kohlengruben\n Paralleles gefunden habe.“ „Es ist Mannsarbeit und Arbeit für starke Männer.“\n „Die bessere Klasse der Minenarbeiter, die sich zu heben und zu humanisiren\n sucht, statt irgend Stütze an ihren Weibern zu finden, wird durch sie herunter-\n gezerrt.“ Nachdem die Bourgeois noch weiter in die Kreuz und Quere gefragt,\n kömmt endlich das Geheimniss ihres „Mitleidens“ für Witwen, arme Familien\n u. s. w. heraus: „Der Kohleneigenthümer ernennt gewisse Gentlemen zur Ober-\n aufsicht und deren Politik ist es, um Beifall zu ernten, alles auf den möglichst\n ökonomischen Fuss zu setzen, und die beschäftigten Mädchen erhalten 1 bis 1 sh.\n 6 d. täglich, wo ein Mann 2 sh. 6 d. erhalten müsste.“ (n. 1816.)\n 4) Todtenschau-Juries. (n. 360.) „Mit Bezug auf die coroner’s\n inquests in Euren Distrikten, sind die Arbeiter zufrieden mit dem Gerichtsver-\n fahren, wenn Unfälle vorkommen? — Nein, sie sind es nicht.“ (n. 361.) „Warum\n nicht? — Namentlich weil man Leute zu Juries macht, die absolut nichts von\n Minen wissen. Arbeiter werden nie zugezogen, ausser als Zeugen. Im Ganzen\n nimmt man Krämer aus der Nachbarschaft, welche unter dem Einfluss der Minen-\n besitzer, ihrer Kunden, stehn und nicht einmal die technischen Ausdrücke der\n Zeugen verstehn. Wir verlangen, dass Minenarbeiter einen Theil der Jury\n bilden. Im Durchschnitt steht der Urtheilsspruch im Widerspruch zu den Zeugen-\n aussagen.“ (n. 378) „Sollen Juries nicht unparteiisch sein? — Ja.“ (n. 379.)\n „Würden die Arbeiter es sein? — Ich sehe keine Motive, warum sie nicht unpar-\n teiisch sein sollten. Sie haben Sachkenntniss.“ (n. 380.) „Aber würden sie\n nicht die Tendenz haben, im Interesse der Arbeiter ungerecht harte Urtheile zu\n fällen? — Nein, ich glaube es nicht.“\n 5) Falsches Mass und Gewicht u. s. w. Die Arbeiter verlangen\n wöchentliche, statt vierzehntägige Zahlung, Mass nach Gewicht, statt nach Kubik-\n raum der Kufen, Schutz gegen die Anwendung falschen Gewichts u. s. w.\n (n. 1071.) „Wenn die Kufen fraudulent vergrössert werden, so kann ein Mann\n ja die Mine verlassen nach 14tägiger Notiz? — Aber, wenn er zu einem andern\n Platz geht, findet er dasselbe.“ (n. 1072.) „Aber er kann den Platz doch ver-\n lassen, wo das Unrecht verübt wird? — Es ist allgemein herrschend.“ (n. 1073.)\n „Aber der Mann kann seinen jedesmaligen Platz nach 14tägiger Notiz verlassen?\n — Ja.“ Streusand drauf!] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"491\", \"facs\": \"#f0510\"} --> alterthümlichen und Uebergangsformen, wohinter sich die Herrschaft des\n Kapitals noch theilweis versteckt, und ersetzt sie durch seine direkte, un-\n [Note: 6) Mineninspektion. Die Arbeiter leiden nicht nur von den Zufällen\n durch explodirende Gase. (n. 234 sqq.) „Wir haben uns ebenso sehr zu be-\n klagen über die schlechte Ventilation der Kohlengruben, so dass die Leute kaum\n darin athmen können; sie werden dadurch zu jeder Art Beschäftigung unfähig.\n So hat z. B. grade jetzt in dem Theil der Mine, wo ich arbeite, die Pestluft viele\n Leute für Wochen aufs Krankenbett geworfen. Die Hauptgänge sind meist luftig\n genug, aber grade nicht die Plätze, worin wir arbeiten. Sendet ein Mann Klage\n über Ventilation an den Inspektor, so wird er entlassen und ist ein „gezeich-\n neter“ Mann, der auch sonstwo keine Beschäftigung findet. Der „Mining In-\n specting Act“ von 1860 ist ein reiner Papierlappen. Der Inspektor, und ihre\n Zahl ist viel zu klein, macht vielleicht in 7 Jahren einmal eine formelle Visite.\n Unser Inspektor ist ein ganz unfähiger, siebzigjähriger Mann, der mehr als 130\n Kohlenbergwerken vorsteht. Neben mehr Inspektoren brauchen wir Subinspek-\n toren.“ (n. 280.) „Soll dann die Regierung solch eine Armee von In-\n spektoren halten, dass sie alles, was Ihr verlangt, ohne Information der Ar-\n beiter selbst thun können? — Das ist unmöglich, aber sie sollen sich die Infor-\n mation in den Minen selbst holen kommen.“ (n. 285.) „Glaubt Ihr nicht, dass\n die Wirkung sein würde die Verantwortlichkeit (!) für die Ventilation\n u. s. w. von dem Minenbesitzer auf die Regierungsbeamten zu wälzen? — Keines-\n wegs; es muss ihr Geschäft sein, die Befolgung der bereits bestehenden Gesetze\n zu erzwingen.“ (n. 294.) „Wenn Ihr von Subinspektoren sprecht, meint Ihr\n Leute mit weniger Gehalt und von niedrigerem Charakter als die gegenwärtigen\n Inspektoren? — Ich wünsche sie keineswegs niedriger, wenn Ihr sie besser\n haben könnt.“ (n. 295.) „Wollt Ihr mehr Inspektoren oder eine niedrigere\n Klasse von Leuten als die Inspektoren? — Wir brauchen Leute, die sich in den\n Minen selbst umtummeln, Leute, die keine Angst für ihre eigne Haut haben.“\n (n. 296.) „Wenn man Euren Wunsch nach Inspektoren von einer\n schlechteren Sorte erfüllte, würde ihr Mangel an Geschick nicht Gefahren\n erzeugen u. s. w.? — Nein; es ist Sache der Regierung, passende Subjekte anzu-\n stellen.“ Diese Art Examination wird endlich selbst dem Präsidenten des Unter-\n suchungscomité’s zu toll. „Ihr wollt“, fährt er dazwischen, „praktische Leute,\n die sich in den Minen selbst umsehn und an den Inspektor berichten, der dann\n seine höhere Wissenschaft verwenden kann.“ (n. 531.) „Würde die Ven-\n tilation aller dieser alten Werke nicht viel Kosten verursachen? — Ja, Unkosten\n möchten erwachsen, aber Menschenleben würden beschützt.“ (n. 581.) Ein\n Kohlenarbeiter protestirt gegen die 17. Sektion des Akts von 1860: „Gegen-\n wärtig, wenn der Mineninspektor irgend einen Theil der Mine in nicht bearbeits-\n fähigem Zustand findet, so muss er es an den Minenbesitzer und den Minister des\n Innern berichten. Danach hat der Minenbesitzer 20 Tage Bedenkzeit; am Ende] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"492\", \"facs\": \"#f0511\"} --> verhüllte Herrschaft. Sie verallgemeinert damit auch den direkten Kampf\n gegen diese Herrschaft. Während sie in den individuellen Werkstätten\n Gleichförmigkeit, Regelmässigkeit, Ordnung und Oekonomie erzwingt,\n vermehrt sie durch den ungeheuren Sporn, den Schranke und Regel des\n Arbeitstags der Technik aufdrücken, die Anarchie und Katastrophen der\n kapitalistischen Produktion im Grossen und Ganzen, die Intensivität der\n Arbeit und die Konkurrenz der Maschinerie mit dem Arbeiter. Mit den\n Sphären des Kleinbetriebs und der Hausarbeit vernichtet sie die letzten\n Zufluchtsstätten der unaufhörlich „überzählig“ gemachten und damit\n das bisherige Sicherheitsventil des ganzen Gesellschaftsmechanismus. Mit\n den materiellen Bedingungen und der gesellschaftlichen Kombination des\n Produktionsprozesses reift sie die Widersprüche und Antagonismen seiner\nkapitalistischen Form, daher gleichzeitig die Bildungselemente\n [Note: der 20 Tage kann er jede Veränderung verweigern. Thut er das aber, so hat er\n an den Minister des Innern zu schreiben und ihm 5 Bergwerksingenieure vorzu-\n schlagen, worunter der Minister die Schiedsrichter erwählen muss. Wir behaup-\n ten, dass in diesem Fall der Minenbesitzer virtuell seinen eignen Richter ernennt.“\n (n. 586.) Der Bourgeoisexaminator, selbst Minenbesitzer: „Diess ist\n ein rein spekulativer Einwand.“ (n. 588.) „Ihr habt also eine sehr geringe\n Ansicht von der Redlichkeit der Bergwerksingenieure? — Ich sage, es ist sehr un-\n billig und ungerecht.“ (n. 589.) „Besitzen Bergwerksingenieure nicht eine Art\n von öffentlichem Charakter, der ihre Entscheidungen über die von Euch befürchtete\n Parteilichkeit erhebt? — Ich verweigre Fragen über den persönlichen Charakter\n dieser Leute zu beantworten. Ich bin überzeugt, dass sie in vielen Fällen sehr\n parteiisch handeln und dass diese Macht ihnen genommen werden sollte, wo Men-\n schenleben auf dem Spiel stehn.“ Derselbe Bourgeois hat die Unverschämtheit\n zu fragen: „Glaubt Ihr nicht, dass auch die Minenbesitzer Verluste bei den Ex-\n plosionen haben?“ — Endlich (n. 1042): „Könnt Ihr Arbeiter Eure eigenen In-\n teressen nicht selbst wahrnehmen, ohne die Hilfe der Regierung anzurufen? —\nNein.“ — Im Jahr 1865 gab es 3217 Kohlenbergwerke in Grossbritanien und —\n 12 Inspektoren. Ein Minenbesitzer von Yorkshire (Times, 26. Januar 1867)\n berechnet selbst, dass abgesehn von ihren rein bureaukratischen Geschäften, die\n ihre ganze Zeit absorbiren, jede Mine nur einmal in 10 Jahren besichtigt werden\n könnte. Kein Wunder, dass die Katastrophen in den letzten Jahren (namentlich\n auch 1866 und 1867) progressiv in Anzahl und Umfang (manchmal mit einem\n Opfer von 200—300 Arbeitern) zugenommen haben. Diess sind die Schönheiten\n der „freien“ kapitalistischen Produktion!] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"493\", \"facs\": \"#f0512\"} -->einer neuen und die Umwälzungsmomente der alten Ge-\n sellschaft [Note: Robert Owen, der Vater der Cooperativfabriken und -Boutiquen, der\n jedoch, wie früher bemerkt, die Illusionen seiner Nachtreter über die Tragweite\n dieser isolirten Umwandlungselemente keineswegs theilte, ging nicht nur thatsäch-\n lich in seinen Versuchen vom Fabriksystem aus, sondern erklärte es auch theo-\n retisch für den Ausgangspunkt der socialen Revolution. Herr Vissering, Pro-\n fessor der politischen Oekonomie an der Universität zu Leiden, scheint so etwas\n zu ahnen, wenn er in seinem „Handboek van Praktische Staatshuis-\n kunde. 1860—62“, welches die Plattheiten der Vulgärökonomie in entspre-\n chendster Form vorträgt, für den Handwerksbetrieb gegen die grosse Industrie\n eifert.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Revolution, welche die grosse Industrie im Ackerbau und den\n socialen Verhältnissen seiner Produktionsagenten hervorruft, kann erst\n später dargestellt werden. Hier genügt kurze Andeutung einiger vorweg-\n genommener Resultate. Wenn der Gebrauch der Maschinerie im Acker-\n bau grossentheils frei ist von den physischen Nachtheilen, die sie dem\n Fabrikarbeiter zufügt [Note: Ausführliche Darstellung der im englischen Ackerbau angewandten\n Maschinerie findet man in: „Die landwirthschaftlichen Geräthe und\n Maschinen Englands von Dr. W. Hamm. 2. Aufl. 1856.“ In seiner\n Skizze über den Entwicklungsgang der englischen Agrikultur folgt Herr Hamm zu\n kritiklos dem Herrn Leonce de Lavergne.] , wirkt sie hier noch intensiver und ohne Gegen-\n stoss auf die „Ueberzähligmachung“ der Arbeiter, wie man später im De-\n tail sehn wird. In den Grafschaften von Cambridge und Suffolk z. B. hat\n sich das Areal des bebauten Landes seit den letzten zwanzig Jahren sehr\n ausgedehnt, während die Landbevölkerung in derselben Periode nicht nur\nrelativ, sondern absolut abnahm. In den Vereinigten Staaten von\n Nordamerika ersetzen Agrikultur - Maschinen einstweilen nur virtuell\n Arbeiter, d. h. sie erlauben dem Producenten Bebauung einer grösseren\n Fläche, verjagen aber nicht wirklich beschäftigte Arbeiter. In England\n und Wales betrug 1861 die Zahl der in der Fabrikation von Ackerbau-\n Maschinen betheiligten Personen 1034, während die Zahl der an\n Dampf- und Arbeitsmaschinen beschäftigten Agrikulturarbei-\n ter nur 1205 betrug.\n\n\n\n\nIn der Sphäre der Agrikultur wirkt die grosse Industrie in sofern\n am revolutionärsten, als sie das Bollwerk der alten Gesellschaft vernichtet,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"494\", \"facs\": \"#f0513\"} -->den „Bauer“, und ihm den Lohnarbeiter unterschiebt. Die socialen\n Umwälzungsbedürfnisse und Gegensätze des Landes werden so mit denen\n der Stadt ausgeglichen. An die Stelle des gewohnheitsfaulsten und irra-\n tionellsten Betriebs tritt bewusste, technologische Anwendung der Wissen-\n schaft. Die Zerreissung des ursprünglichen Familienbandes von Agri-\n kultur und Manufaktur, welches die kindlich unentwickelte Gestalt beider\n umschlang, wird durch die kapitalistische Produktionsweise vollendet. Sie\n schafft aber zugleich die materiellen Voraussetzungen einer neuen, höheren\n Synthese, des Vereins von Agrikultur und Industrie, auf Grundlage ihrer\n gegensätzlich ausgearbeiteten Gestalten. Mit dem stets wachsenden Ueber-\n gewicht der städtischen Bevölkerung, die sie in grossen Centren zusammen-\n häuft, häuft die kapitalistische Produktion einerseits die geschichtliche\n Bewegungskraft der Gesellschaft, stört sie andrerseits den Stoffwechsel\n zwischen Mensch und Erde, d. h. die Rückkehr der vom Menschen in der\n Form von Nahrungs- und Kleidungsmitteln vernutzten Bodenbestandtheile\n zum Boden, also die ewige Naturbedingung dauernder Bodenfruchtbarkeit.\n Sie zerstört damit zugleich die physische Gesundheit der Stadtarbeiter und\n das geistige Leben der Landarbeiter [Note: „You divide the people into two hostile camps of clownish boors and\n emasculated dwarfs. Good heavens! a nation divided into agricultural and\n commercial interests calling itself sane nay styling itself enlightened and civilized,\n not only in spite of, but in consequence of this monstrous and unnatural division.“\n (David Urquhart l. c. p. 119.) Diese Stelle zeigt zugleich die Stärke und die\n Schwäche einer Art von Kritik, welche die Gegenwart zu be- und verurtheilen, aber\n nicht zu begreifen weiss.] . Aber sie zwingt zugleich durch\n die Zerstörung der bloss naturwüchsig entstandenen Umstände jenes Stoff-\n wechsels ihn systematisch als regelndes Gesetz der gesellschaftlichen Pro-\n duktion und in einer der vollen menschlichen Entwicklung adäquaten Form\n herzustellen. In der Agrikultur wie in der Manufaktur erscheint die kapi-\n talistische Umwandlung des Produktionsprozesses zugleich als Martyrologie\n der Producenten, das Arbeitsmittel als Unterjochungsmittel, Exploitationsmit-\n tel und Verarmungsmittel des Arbeiters, die gesellschaftliche Kombina-\n tion der Arbeitsprozesse als organisirte Unterdrückung seiner individu-\n ellen Lebendigkeit, Freiheit und Selbstständigkeit. Die Zerstreuung der\n Landarbeiter über grössere Flächen bricht zugleich ihre Widerstandskraft,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"495\", \"facs\": \"#f0514\"} --> während Koncentration die der städtischen Arbeiter steigert. Wie in der\n städtischen Industrie wird in der modernen Agrikultur die gesteigerte Pro-\n duktivkraft und grössere Flüssigmachung der Arbeit erkauft durch Verwü-\n stung und Versiechung der Arbeitskraft selbst. Und jeder Fortschritt der\n kapitalistischen Agrikultur ist nicht nur ein Fortschritt in der Kunst den\nArbeiter, sondern zugleich in der Kunst den Boden zu berauben,\n jeder Fortschritt in Steigerung seiner Fruchtbarkeit für eine gegebne Zeitfrist\n zugleich ein Fortschritt im Ruin der dauernden Quellen dieser Fruchtbarkeit.\n Je mehr ein Land, wie die Vereinigten Staaten von Nordamerika z. B.,\n von der grossen Industrie als dem Hintergrund seiner Entwicklung ausgeht,\n desto rascher dieser Zerstörungsprozess [Note: Vgl. Liebig: „Die Chemie in ihrer Anwendung auf Agri-\n kultur und Physiologie. 7. Auflage 1862“, namentlich auch im Ersten\n Band die „Einleitung in die Naturgesetze des Feldbaus.“ Die\n Entwicklung der negativen Seite der modernen Agrikultur, vom naturwissenschaft-\n lichen Standpunkt, ist eins der unsterblichen Verdienste Liebig’s. Auch seine\n historischen Aperçus über die Geschichte der Agrikultur, obgleich nicht ohne\n grobe Irrthümer, enthalten mehr Lichtblicke als die Schriften sämmtlicher moder-\n nen politischen Oekonomen zusammengenommen. Zu bedauern bleibt, dass er\n aufs Gradewohl Aeusserungen wagt, wie folgende: „Durch eine weiter getriebene\n Pulverisirung und häufigeres Pflügen wird der Luftwechsel im Innern der porösen\n Erdtheile befördert, und die Oberfläche der Erdtheile, auf welche die Luft ein-\n wirken soll, vergrössert und erneuert, aber es ist leicht verständlich, dass die\nMehrerträge des Feldes nicht proportionell der auf das Feld\n verwandten Arbeit sein können, sondern dass sie in einem weit kleineren\n Verhältnisse steigen. Dieses Gesetz“, fügt Liebig hinzu, „ist von J. St. Mill\n zuerst in seinen Princ. of Pol. Econ. v. I, p. 17 in folgender Weise ausge-\n sprochen: ‚That the produce of land increases caeteris paribus in a diminishing\n ratio to the increase of the labourers employed, (Herr Mill wiederholt\n sogar das Ricardo’sche Schulgesetz in falscher Formel, denn da „the decrease of\n the labourers employed“ in England beständig war, im Fortschritt der Agrikultur,\n hätte das für und in England erfundene Gesetz wenigstens in England keine An-\n wendung) is the universal law of agricultural industry‘, merkwürdig genug, da\n ihm dessen Grund unbekannt war.“ (Liebig l. c. Bd. I, p. 143 u. Note.) Ab-\n gesehn von irriger Deutung des Wortes „Arbeit“, worunter Liebig etwas anderes\n versteht, als die politische Oekonomie, ist es jedenfalls „merkwürdig genug“, dass\n er Herrn J. St. Mill zum ersten Verkünder einer Theorie macht, die James\n Anderson zur Zeit A. Smith’s zuerst veröffentlichte und in verschiednen Schriften\n bis in den Anfang des 19. Jahrhunderts hinein wiederholte, die Malthus, über-] . Die kapitalistische Produk-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"496\", \"facs\": \"#f0515\"} --> tion entwickelt daher nur die Technik und Kombination des gesellschaft-\n lichen Produktionsprozesses, indem sie zugleich die Springquellen allen\n Reichthums untergräbt: Die Erde und den Arbeiter.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0001","text":"Mit der Ausdehnung des Maschinenbetriebs in einem Industriezweig\n steigert sich also zunächst die Produktion in den andern Zweigen, die ihm\n seine Produktionsmittel liefern. Wie weit dadurch die beschäftigte Arbei-\n termasse wächst, hängt, Länge des Arbeitstags und Intensivität der Ar-\n beit gegeben, von der Zusammensetzung der verwandten Kapitale ab, d. h.\n vom Verhältniss ihrer constanten und variablen Bestandtheile. Diess\n Verhältniss seinerseits variirt sehr mit dem Umfang, worin die Maschinerie\n jene Gewerbe selbst schon ergriffen hat oder ergreift. Die Anzahl zu\n Kohlen- und Metallbergwerken verurtheilter Menschen schwoll ungeheuer\n mit dem Fortschritt des englischen Maschinenwesens, obgleich ihr Anwachs\n in den letzten Decennien durch Gebrauch neuer Maschinerie für den Berg-\n bau verlangsamt wird [Note: Nach dem Census von 1861 (Vol. II. Lond.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"15.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0002","text":"1863) betrug die Zahl\n der in den Kohlenbergwerken von England und Wales beschäftigten Arbeiter\n 246, 613, wovon 73,546 unter und 173,067 über 20 Jahre. Zur ersten Rubrik\n gehören 835 fünf- bis zehnjährige, 30,701 zehn- bis fünfzehnjährige, 42,010 fünf-\n zehn- bis neunzehnjährige. Die Zahl der in Eisen-, Kupfer-, Blei-, Zinn- und\n allen andern Metallminen Beschäftigten: 319,222.] . Eine neue Arbeiterart springt mit der Maschine\n ins Leben, ihr Producent. Wir wissen bereits, dass der Maschinenbetrieb\n sich dieses Produktionszweigs selbst auf stets massenhafterer Stufenleiter be-\n [Running matter: I. 28] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"434\", \"facs\": \"#f0453\"} --> mächtigt [Note: In England und Wales 1861 in der Produktion von Maschinerie beschäf-\n tigt: 60,807 Personen, eingezählt die Fabrikanten sammt ihren Commis u. s. w.,\n ditto alle Agenten und Handelsleute in diesem Fach.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"15.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0003","text":"Ausgeschlossen dagegen die\n Producenten kleinerer Maschinen, wie Nähmaschinen u. s. w., ebenso die Produ-\n centen der Werkzeuge für die Arbeitsmaschinen, wie Spindeln u. s. w. Zahl\n sämmtlicher Civilingenieure betrug 3329.] . Was ferner das Rohmaterial betrifft [Note: Da Eisen einer der wichtigsten Rohstoffe, so sei hier bemerkt, dass 1861 in\n England und Wales 125,771 Eisengiesser, wovon 123,430 männlich, 2341 weib-\n lich. Von den erstern 30,810 unter und 92,620 über 20 Jahre.] , so unterliegt es\n z. B. keinem Zweifel, dass der Sturmmarsch der Baumwollspinnerei den\n Baumwollbau der Vereinigten Staaten und mit ihm nicht nur den afrika-\n nischen Sklavenhandel treibhausmässig förderte, sondern zugleich die\n Negerzucht zum Hauptgeschäft der sogenannten Border slaves states\n machte.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"15.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0004","text":"Als 1790 der erste Sklavencensus in den Vereinigten Staaten\n aufgenommen ward, betrug ihre Zahl 697,000, dagegen 1861 ungefähr\n vier Millionen. Andrerseits ist es nicht minder gewiss, dass das Auf-\n blühen der mechanischen Wollfabrik mit der progressiven Verwandlung\n von Ackerland in Schafweide die massenhafte Verjagung und „Ueber-\n zähligmachung“ der Landarbeiter hervorrief. Irland untergeht noch in\n diesem Augenblick den Prozess, seine seit 20 Jahren beinahe um die\n Hälfte verkürzte Bevölkerung noch weiter auf das dem Bedürfniss seiner\n Landlords und der englischen Herrn Wollfabrikanten exakt entsprechende\n Mass zu reduciren.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"15.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0005","text":"Ergreift die Maschinerie eine Vor- oder Zwischenstufe des Gesammt-\n cursus, den ein Arbeitsgegenstand bis zu seiner letzten Form zu durch-\n laufen hat, so vermehrt sich mit dem Arbeitsmaterial die Arbeitsnachfrage\n in den noch handwerks- oder manufakturmässig betriebnen Gewerken,\n welche das Maschinenfabrikat weiter formen. Die Maschinenspinnerei\n z. B. lieferte das Garn so wohlfeil und so reichlich, dass die Handweber zu-\n nächst, ohne vermehrte Auslage, volle Zeit arbeiten konnten. So stieg ihr\n Einkommen [Note: „Eine Familie von 4 erwachsnen Personen (Baumwollwebern) mit zwei\n Kindern als winders gewann Ende des letzten und Anfang des gegenwärtigen Jahr-\n hunderts 4 Pfd. St. per Woche bei 10stündiger Tagesarbeit; war die Arbeit sehr\n dringend, so konnten sie mehr verdienen … Früher hatten sie immer gelitten\n von mangelnder Garnzufuhr.“ (Gaskell l. c. p. 25—27.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"15.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0006","text":"Daher Menschenzufluss in die Baumwollweberei, bis\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"435\", \"facs\": \"#f0454\"} --> schliesslich die von Jenny, Throstle und Mule in England z. B. ins Leben\n gerufenen 800,000 Baumwollweber wieder vom Dampfwebstuhl er-\n schlagen wurden. So wächst mit dem Ueberfluss der maschinenmässig pro-\n ducirten Kleidungsstoffe die Zahl der Schneider, Kleidermacherinnen,\n Nähterinnen u. s. w., bis die Nähmaschine erscheint.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"15.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0007","text":"Entsprechend der steigenden Masse von Rohstoffen, Halbfabrikaten,\n Arbeitsinstrumenten u. s. w., die der Maschinenbetrieb mit relativ geringer\n Arbeiterzahl liefert, differenzirt sich die Bearbeitung dieser Rohstoffe\n und Halbfabrikate in zahllose Unterarten, also die Mannigfaltigkeit der\n gesellschaftlichen Produktionszweige. Der Maschinenbetrieb vermehrt die\ngesellschaftliche Theilung der Arbeit weit mehr als die Manufaktur,\n weil er die Produktivkraft der von ihm ergriffenen Gewerbe ungleich höher\n spannt.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"15.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0008","text":"Das nächste Resultat der Maschinerie ist den Mehrwerth und zu-\n gleich die Produktenmasse, worin er sich darstellt, also mit der Substanz,\n wovon die Kapitalistenklasse sammt Anhang zehrt, diese Gesellschafts-\n schichten selbst zu vergrössern. Ihr wachsender Reichthum und die relativ\n beständig fallende Anzahl der zur Produktion der ersten Lebensmittel er-\n heischten Arbeiter, erzeugen mit neuem Luxusbedürfniss zugleich neue\n Mittel seiner Befriedigung. Ein grösserer Theil des gesellschaftlichen Pro-\n dukts verwandelt sich in Surplusprodukt und ein grösserer Theil des Sur-\n plusprodukts wird in verfeinerten und vermannigfachten Formen repro-\n ducirt und verzehrt. In andern Worten: Die Luxusproduktion\n wächst [Note: F. Engels in „Lage u. s. w.“ weist den jämmerlichen Zustand eines\n grossen Theils grade dieser Luxusarbeiter nach.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"15.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0009","text":"Massenhafte neue Belege hierzu\n in den Berichten der „Child. Empl. Comm.“] . Die Verfeinerung und Vermannigfachung der Produkte ent-\n springt ebenso aus den neuen weltmarktlichen Beziehungen, welche die\n grosse Industrie schafft. Es werden nicht nur mehr ausländische Genuss-\n mittel gegen das heimische Produkt ausgetauscht, sondern es geht auch\n eine grössere Masse fremder Rohstoffe, Ingredienzen, Halbfabrikate u. s. w.\n als Produktionsmittel in die heimische Industrie ein. Mit denselben welt-\n marktlichen Beziehungen steigt die Arbeitsnachfrage in der Transport-\n industrie und spaltet sich letztere in zahlreiche neue Unterarten [Note: 1861 in England und Wales 94,665 in der Handelsmarine beschäftigte\n Seeleute.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"15.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0010","text":"[Running matter: 28*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"436\", \"facs\": \"#f0455\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"15.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0011","text":"Die Vermehrung von Produktions- und Lebensmitteln durch relativ ab-\n nehmende Arbeiterzahl treibt zur Ausdehnung der Arbeit in Industrie-\n zweigen, deren Produkte, wie Kanäle, Waarendocks, Tunnels, Brücken\n u. s. w. nur in fernerer Zukunft Früchte tragen. Es bilden sich, ent-\n weder direkt auf der Grundlage der Maschinerie, oder doch der ihr ent-\n sprechenden allgemeinen industriellen Umwälzung, ganz neue Produktions-\n zweige und daher neue Arbeitsfelder. Ihr Raumantheil an der Gesammt-\n produktion ist jedoch selbst in den meist entwickelten Ländern keineswegs\n bedeutend. Die Anzahl der von ihnen beschäftigten Arbeiter steigt im direkten\n Verhältniss, worin die Nothwendigkeit rohster Handarbeit reproducirt wird.\n Als Hauptindustrieen dieser Art kann man gegenwärtig Gaswerke, Tele-\n graphie, Photographie, Dampfschifffahrt und Eisenbahnwesen betrachten.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"15.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0012","text":"Der Census von 1861 (für England und Wales) ergiebt in der Gasin-\n dustrie (Gaswerke, Produktion der mechanischen Apparate, Agenten\n der Gascompagnien u. s. w.) 15,211 Personen, Telegraphie 2399,\nPhotographie 2366, Dampfschiffdienst 3570 und Eisen-\n bahnen 70,599, worunter ungefähr 28,000 mehr oder minder perma-\n nent beschäftigte „ungeschickte“ Erdarbeiter nebst dem ganzen admini-\n strativen und kommerciellen Personal. Also Gesammtzahl der Individuen\n in diesen fünf neuen Industrieen 94,145.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"15.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0013","text":"Endlich erlaubt die ausserordentlich erhöhte Produktivkraft in den\n Sphären der grossen Industrie, begleitet, wie sie ist, von intensiv und extensiv\n gesteigerter Ausbeutung der Arbeitskraft in allen übrigen Produktions-\n sphären, einen stets grösseren Theil der Arbeiterklasse unproduktiv zu\n verwenden und so namentlich die alten Haussklaven unter dem Namen\n der „dienenden Klasse“, wie Bediente, Mägde, Lakaien u. s. w., stets\n massenhafter zu reproduciren. Nach dem Census von 1861 zählte die\n Gesammtbevölkerung von England und Wales 20,066,244 Personen, wo-\n von 9,776,259 männlich und 10,289,965 weiblich. Zieht man hiervon\n ab, was zu alt oder zu jung zur Arbeit, alle „unproduktiven“ Weiber,\n jungen Personen und Kinder, dann die „ideologischen“ Stände, wie Re-\n gierung, Pfaffen, Juristen, Militär u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"15.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0014","text":"w., ferner alle, deren ausschliess-\n liches Geschäft der Verzehr fremder Arbeit in der Form von Grundrente,\n Zins u. s. w., endlich Paupers, Vagabunden, Verbrecher u. s. w., so bleiben\n in rauher Zahl 8 Millionen beiderlei Geschlechts und der verschiedensten\n Altersstufen, mit Einschluss sämmtlicher irgendwie in der Produktion, dem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"437\", \"facs\": \"#f0456\"} --> Handel, der Finanz u. s. w. funktionirenden Kapitalisten. Von diesen\n 8 Millionen kommen auf:","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"15.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0015","text":"Ackerbauarbeiter (mit Einschluss der Hirten\n und bei Pächtern wohnenden Ackersknechte\n und Mägde) . . . . . . . . . . . 1,098,261 Personen.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"15.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0016","text":"Alle in Baumwoll-, Woll-, Worsted-, Flachs-,\n Hanf-, Seide-, Jutefabriken und in der mecha-\n nischen Strumpfwirkerei und Spitzenfabrika-\n tion Beschäftigten . . . . . . . . . 642,607 [Note: Davon nur 177,596 männlichen Geschlechts über 13 Jahre.]  „","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"15.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0017","text":"Alle in Kohlen- und Metallbergwerken Beschäf-\n tigten . . . . . . . . . . . . . 565,835 „","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"15.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0018","text":"In sämmtlichen Metallwerken (Hochöfen, Walz-\n werke u. s. w.) und Metallmanufakturen aller\n Art Beschäftigte . . . . . . . . . 396,998 [Note: Davon weiblichen Geschlechts 30,501.]  „","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"15.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0019","text":"Dienende Klasse . . . . . . . . . . . 1,208,648 [Note: Davon männlichen Geschlechts: 137,447. Ausgeschlossen von den\n 1,208,648 alles Personal, das nicht in Privathäusern dient.]  „","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"15.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0020","text":"Rechnen wir die in allen textilen Fabriken Beschäftigten zusammen\n mit dem Personal der Kohlen- und Metallbergwerke, so erhalten wir\n 1,208,442; rechnen wir sie zusammen mit dem Personal aller Metall-\n Werke und Manufakturen, so die Gesammtzahl 1,039,605, beidemal\n kleiner als die Zahl der modernen Haussklaven. Welch erhebendes Re-\n sultat der kapitalistisch exploitirten Maschinerie!","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"15.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0021","text":"Alle zurechnungsfähigen Repräsentanten der politischen Oekonomie\n geben zu, dass neue Einführung der Maschinerie pestartig wirkt auf die Arbei-\n ter in den überlieferten Handwerken und Manufakturen, womit sie zunächst\n konkurrirt. Fast alle beächzen die Sklaverei des Fabrikarbeiters. Und\n was ist der grosse Trumpf, den alle ausspielen? Dass die Maschinerie,\n nach der Tortur, wovon ihre Einführung und ihre Entwicklung begleitet\n sind, die Arbeitssklaven in letzter Instanz vermehrt, statt sie\n schliesslich zu vermindern!","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"15.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0022","text":"Ja, die politische Oekonomie jubelt\n sich aus in dem abscheulichen Theorem, abscheulich für jeden „Philan-\n throp“, der an die ewige Naturnothwendigkeit der kapitalistischen Produk-\n tionsweise glaubt, dass selbst die bereits auf Maschinenbetrieb begründete\n Fabrik, nach bestimmter Periode des Wachsthums, nach kürzerer oder\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"438\", \"facs\": \"#f0457\"} --> längerer „Uebergangszeit“, mehr Arbeiter abplackt als sie ursprüng-\n lich aufs Pflaster warf [Note: Ganilh betrachtet dagegen als Schluss-Resultat des Maschinenbetriebs\n absolut verminderte Anzahl der Arbeitssklaven, auf deren Kosten dann eine ver-\n mehrte Anzahl der „gens honnêtes“ zehrt und ihre bekannte „perfectibilité per-\n fectible“ entwickelt.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"15.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0023","text":"So wenig er die Bewegung der Produktion versteht, fühlt\n er wenigstens, dass die Maschinerie, wenn sie nur dazu dient, erstens durch ihre\nEinführung beschäftigte Arbeiter in Paupers zu verwandeln und zweitens durch\n ihre Entwicklung mehr Arbeitssklaven zu reproduciren, als sie erschlagen hat, eine\n sehr fatale Institution ist. Den Cretinismus seines eignen Standpunkts kann man\n nur in seinen eignen Worten ausdrücken: „Les classes condamnées à produire\n et à consommer diminuent, et les classes qui dirigentle travail, qui soulagent,\n consolent et éclairent toute la population, se multiplient … et s’appro-\n prient tous les bienfaits qui résultent de la diminution des frais du\n travail, de l’abondance des productions et du bon marché des consommations.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"15.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0024","text":"Dans cette direction, l’espèce humaine s’élève aux plus hautes conceptions du\n génie, pénètre dans les profondeurs mystérieuses de la religion, établit les\n principes salutaires de la morale (die darin besteht de „s’approprier\n tous les bienfaits etc.“), les lois tutélaires de la liberté (der liberté pour „les\n classes condamnées à produire“?) et du pouvoir, de l’obéissance et de la\n justice, du devoir et de l’humanité.“ Diess Kauderwelsch in: „Des Systèmes\n d’Économie Politique etc. Par M. Ch. Ganilh“. 2ème éd. Paris\n 1821, t. II, p. 224. cf. ib. p. 212.] !","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"15.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0025","text":"Zwar zeigte sich schon an einigen Beispielen, z. B. den englischen\n Worsted- und Seidenfabriken, dass auf einem gewissen Entwicklungsgrad\n ausserordentliche Ausdehnung von Fabrikzweigen mit nicht nur rela-\n tiver, sondern absoluter Abnahme der angewandten Ar-\n beiteranzahl verbunden sein kann. Im Jahr 1860, als ein Special-\n census aller Fabriken des Vereinigten Königreichs auf Befehl des Parla-\n ments aufgenommen ward, zählte die dem Fabrikinspektor R. Baker\n zugewiesne Abtheilung der Fabrikdistrikte von Lancashire, Cheshire und\n Yorkshire 652 Fabriken; von diesen enthielten 570: Dampfwebstühle\n 85,622, Spindeln (mit Ausschluss der Dublirspindeln) 6,819,146,\nPferdekraft in Dampfmaschinen 27,439, in Wasserrädern 1390, be-\n schäftigte Personen 94,119.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"15.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0026","text":"Im Jahr 1865 dagegen enthielten die-\n selben Fabriken: Webstühle 95,163, Spindeln 7,025,031,\nPferdekraft in Dampfmaschinen 28,925, in Wasserrädern 1445, be-\n schäftigte Personen 88,913. Von 1860 bis 1865 betrug also die Zu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"439\", \"facs\": \"#f0458\"} --> nahme dieser Fabriken an Dampfwebstühlen 11 %, an Spindeln 3 %,\n an Dampfpferdekraft 5 %, während gleichzeitig die Zahl der beschäftigten\n Personen um 5,5 % abnahm [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1865“, p. 58 sq. Gleich-\n zeitig war aber auch schon die materielle Grundlage für Beschäftigung einer wach-\n senden Arbeiterzahl gegeben in 110 neuen Fabriken mit 11,625 Dampfweb-\n stühlen, 628,756 Spindeln, 2695 Dampf- und Wasser-Pferdekraft. (l. c.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"15.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0027","text":"Zwischen 1852 und 1862 fand be-\n trächtliches Wachsthum der englischen Wollfabrikation statt, wäh-\n rend die Zahl der angewandten Arbeiter beinahe stationär blieb. „Diess\n zeigt, in wie grossem Masse neu eingeführte Maschinerie die Arbeit vor-\n hergehender Perioden verdrängt hatte“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 79.] . In empirisch gegebnen\n Fällen ist die Zunahme der beschäftigten Fabrikarbeiter oft nur schein-\n bar, d. h. nicht der Ausdehnung der bereits auf Maschinenbetrieb be-\n ruhenden Fabrik geschuldet, sondern der allmäligen Annexation von Neben-\n zweigen. Z. B. „die Zunahme der mechanischen Webstühle und der\n durch sie beschäftigten Fabrikarbeiter von 1838—1858 war\n in der (britischen) Baumwollfabrik einfach der Ausdehnung dieses\n Geschäftszweigs geschuldet; in den andern Fabriken dagegen der\nNeuanwendung von Dampfkraft auf den Teppich-, Band-,\n Leinenwebstuhl u.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"15.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0028","text":"s. w., die vorher durch menschliche Muskelkraft ge-\n trieben wurden“ [Note: „Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1856“, p. 16.] . Die Zunahme dieser Fabrikarbeiter war also nur\n der Ausdruck einer Abnahme in der Gesammtzahl der beschäftigten Ar-\n beiter. Es wird hier endlich ganz davon abgesehen, dass überall, mit\n Ausnahme der Metallfabriken, jugendliche Arbeiter (unter 18 Jahren), Weiber\n und Kinder das weit vorwiegende Element des Fabrikpersonals bilden.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"15.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0029","text":"Man begreift jedoch, trotz der vom Maschinenbetrieb faktisch ver-\n drängten und virtuell ersetzten Arbeitermasse, wie mit seinem eignen\n Wachsthum, ausgedrückt in vermehrter Anzahl von Fabriken dersel-\n ben Art oder den erweiterten Dimensionen vorhandner Fabriken, die Fa-\n brikarbeiter schliesslich zahlreicher sein können als die von ihnen ver-\n drängten Manufakturarbeiter oder Handwerker. Das wöchentlich ange-\n wandte Kapital von 500 Pfd. St. bestehe z. B. in der alten Betriebsweise\n aus ⅖ constantem und ⅗ variablem Bestandtheil, d. h. 200 Pfd. St.\n seien in Produktionsmitteln ausgelegt, 300 Pfd. St. in Arbeitskraft, sage\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"440\", \"facs\": \"#f0459\"} --> 1 Pfd. St. per Arbeiter. Mit dem Maschinenbetrieb verwandelt sich die\n Zusammensetzung des Gesammtkapitals. Es zerfällt jetzt z. B. in ⅘\n constanten und ⅕ variablen Bestandtheil, oder es werden nur noch 100\n Pfd.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"15.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0030","text":"St. in Arbeitskraft ausgelegt. Zwei Drittel der früher beschäftigten\n Arbeiter werden also entlassen. Dehnt sich dieser Fabrikbetrieb aus und\n wächst, bei sonst gleichbleibenden Produktionsbedingungen, das ange-\n wandte Gesammtkapital von 500 auf 1500, so werden jetzt 300 Arbeiter\n beschäftigt, so viele wie vor der industriellen Revolution. Wächst das\n angewandte Kapital weiter auf 2000, so werden 400 Arbeiter beschäf-\n tigt, also ⅓ mehr als mit der alten Betriebsweise. Absolut ist die an-\n gewandte Arbeiterzahl um 100 gestiegen, relativ, d. h. im Verhältniss\n zum vorgeschossnen Gesammtkapital, ist sie um 800 gefallen, denn das\n Kapital von 2000 Pfd. St. hätte in der alten Betriebsweise 1200 statt\n 400 Arbeiter beschäftigt. Relative Abnahme der beschäftigten Ar-\n beiterzahl verträgt sich also mit ihrer absoluten Zunahme.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"15.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0031","text":"Es\n wurde oben angenommen, dass mit dem Wachsthum des Gesammtkapitals\n seine Zusammensetzung constant bleibt, weil die Produktionsbedingungen.\n Man weiss aber bereits, dass mit jedem Fortschritt des Maschinenwesens\n der constante, aus Maschinerie, Rohmaterial u. s. w. bestehende Kapital-\n theil wächst, während der variable, in Arbeitskraft ausgelegte fällt, und\n man weiss zugleich, dass in keiner Betriebsweise die Verbesserung so con-\n stant, daher die Zusammensetzung des Gesammtkapitals so variabel ist.\n Dieser beständige Wechsel ist aber ebenso beständig unterbrochen durch\n Ruhepunkte und bloss quantitative Ausdehnung auf gegebner tech-\n nologischer Grundlage. Damit wächst die Anzahl der beschäftigten Ar-\n beiter.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"15.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0032","text":"So betrug die Anzahl aller Arbeiter in den Baumwoll-, Woll-,\n Worsted-, Flachs- und Seidenfabriken des Vereinigten Königreichs 1835 nur\n 354,684, während 1861 allein die Zahl der Dampfweber (beiderlei Ge-\n schlechts und der verschiedensten Altersstufen vom 8. Jahr an) 230,654\n betrug. Allerdings erscheint diess Wachsthum minder gross, wenn man\n erwägt, dass die britischen Handbaumwollweber mit den von ihnen selbst\n beschäftigten Familien 1838 noch 800,000 zählten [Note: „Die Leiden der Handweber (von Baumwolle und mit Baumwolle ge-\n mischten Stoffen) waren Gegenstand der Untersuchung durch eine königl. Kom-] , ganz abgesehn\n von den in Asien und auf dem europäischen Kontinent Deplacirten.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"15.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0033","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"441\", \"facs\": \"#f0460\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"15.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0034","text":"In den wenigen Bemerkungen, die über diesen Punkt noch zu machen,\n berühren wir zum Theil rein thatsächlich, so zu sagen exote-\n risch, Verhältnisse, wozu unsre theoretische Darstellung selbst noch\n nicht geführt hat.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"15.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0035","text":"So lange sich der Maschinenbetrieb in einem Industriezweig auf\n Kosten des überlieferten Handwerks oder der Manufaktur ausdehnt, sind\n seine Erfolge so sicher, wie etwa der Erfolg einer mit dem Zündnadelge-\n wehr bewaffneten Armee gegen eine Armee von Bogenschützen wäre.\n Diese erste Periode, worin die Maschine erst ihren Wirkungskreis erobert,\n ist entscheidend wichtig wegen der ausserordentlichen Profite, die sie pro-\n duciren hilft. Diese bilden nicht nur an und für sich eine Quelle beschleu-\n nigter Akkumulation, sondern ziehn grossen Theil des beständig neuge-\n bildeten und nach neuer Anlage drängenden gesellschaftlichen Zusatzkapi-\n tals in die begünstigte Produktionssphäre. Die besondern Vortheile der\n ersten Sturm- und Drangperiode wiederholen sich beständig in den Pro-\n duktionszweigen, worin die Maschinerie neu eingeführt wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"15.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0036","text":"Sobald\n aber das Fabrikwesen eine gewisse Breite des Daseins und bestimmten\n Reifegrad gewonnen hat, sobald namentlich seine eigne technologische\n Grundlage, die Maschinerie, selbst wieder durch Maschinen producirt wird,\n sobald Kohlen- und Eisengewinnung, wie die Verarbeitung der Metalle\n und das Transportwesen revolutionirt, überhaupt die der grossen Industrie\n entsprechenden allgemeinen Produktionsbedingungen hergestellt sind, er-\n wirbt diese Betriebsweise eine Elasticität, eine plötzliche sprung-\n weise Ausdehnungsfähigkeit, die nur an dem Rohmaterial und\n dem Absatzmarkt Schranken findet. Die Maschinerie bewirkt einerseits\n direkte Vermehrung des Rohmaterials, wie z. B. der cotton gin die Baum-\n wollproduktion vermehrte [Note: Andre Methoden, wodurch die Maschinerie auf die Produktion des Roh-\n materials einwirkt, werden im Dritten Buch erwähnt.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"15.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0037","text":"Andrerseits sind Wohlfeilheit des Maschi-\n nenprodukts und das umgewälzte Transport- und Kommunikationswesen\n Waffen zur Eroberung fremder Märkte. Durch den Ruin ihres handwerks-\n [Note: mission, aber obgleich ihr Elend anerkannt und bejammert wurde, überliess man\n die Verbesserung (!) ihrer Lage dem Zufall und dem Wechsel der Zeit, und man\n darf hoffen, dass diese Leiden jetzt (20 Jahre später!) beinahe (nearly) ver-\n wischt sind, wozu die jetzige grosse Ausdehnung der Dampfwebstühle aller Wahr-\n scheinlichkeit nach beigetragen hat.“ (l. c. p. 15.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"442\", \"facs\": \"#f0461\"} --> mässigen Produkts verwandelt der Maschinenbetrieb sie zwangsweise in\n Produktionsfelder seines Rohmaterials. So wurde Ostindien zur Produk-\n tion von Baumwolle, Wolle, Hanf, Jute, Indigo u. s. w. für Grossbritanien\n gezwungen [Note: Baumwollausfuhr von Ostindien nach Grossbritanien.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"15.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0038","text":"1846 lbs. 34,540,143.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"15.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0039","text":"1860 lbs. 204,141,168.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"15.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0040","text":"1865 lbs. 445,947,600.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"15.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0041","text":"Wollausfuhr von Ostindien nach Grossbritanien.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"15.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0042","text":"1846 lbs. 4,570,581.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"15.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0043","text":"1860 lbs. 20,214,173.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"15.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0044","text":"1863 lbs. 20,679,111.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"15.44"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0045","text":"] . Die beständige „Ueberzähligmachung“ der Arbeiter in\n den Ländern der grossen Industrie befördert treibhausmässige Auswande-\n rung und Kolonisation fremder Länder, die sich in Pflanzstätten für das\n Rohmaterial des Mutterlands verwandeln, wie Australien z. B. in eine\n Pflanzstätte von Wolle [Note: Wollausfuhr vom Kap der guten Hoffnung nach Gross-\n britanien.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"15.45"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0046","text":"1846 lbs. 2,958,457.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"15.46"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0047","text":"1860 lbs. 16,574,345.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"15.47"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0048","text":"1865 lbs. 29,220,623.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"15.48"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0049","text":"Wollausfuhr von Australien nach Grossbritanien.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"15.49"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0050","text":"1846 lbs. 21,789,346.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"15.50"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0051","text":"1860 lbs. 59,166,616.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"15.51"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0052","text":"1865 lbs. 109,734,261.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"15.52"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0053","text":"] . Es wird eine neue, den Hauptsitzen des Ma-\n schinenbetriebs entsprechende internationale Theilung der Arbeit ge-\n schaffen, die einen Theil des Erdballs in vorzugsweis agrikoles Produk-\n tionsfeld für den andern als vorzugsweis industrielles Produktionsfeld\n umwandelt. Diese Revolution hängt zusammen mit Umwälzungen in der\n Agrikultur, die hier noch nicht weiter zu erörtern sind [Note: Die ökonomische Entwicklung der Vereinigten Staaten ist selbst ein Produkt\n der europäischen, näher englischen grossen Industrie. In ihrer jetzigen Gestalt\n müssen sie stets noch als Kolonialland von Europa betrachtet werden.\nBaumwollausfuhr der Vereinigten Staaten nach Grossbritanien\n in lbs.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"15.53"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0054","text":"1846 401,949,393.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"15.54"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0055","text":"1852 765,630,544.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"15.55"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0056","text":"1859 961,707,264.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"15.56"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0057","text":"1860 1,115,890,608.","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"15.57"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0058","text":"] .","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"15.58"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0059","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"443\", \"facs\": \"#f0462\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"15.59"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0060","text":"Die ungeheure, stossweise Ausdehnbarkeit des Fabrikwesens und\n seine Abhängigkeit vom Weltmarkt erzeugen nothwendig fieberhafte Pro-\n duktion und darauf folgende Ueberfüllung der Märkte, mit deren Kontrak-\n tion Lähmung eintritt. Das Leben der Industrie verwandelt sich in eine\n Reihenfolge von Perioden mittlerer Lebendigkeit, Prosperität, Ueberpro-\n duktion, Krise und Stagnation. Die Unsicherheit und Unstätigkeit, denen\n der Maschinenbetrieb die Beschäftigung und damit die Lebenslage des Ar-\n beiters unterwirft, werden normal mit diesem Periodenwechsel des indu-\n striellen Cyklus. Die Zeiten der Prosperität abgerechnet, rast zwischen\n den Kapitalisten heftigster Kampf um ihren individuellen Raumantheil am\n Markt. Dieser Antheil steht in direktem Verhältniss zur Wohlfeilheit des\n Produkts.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"15.60"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0061","text":"Ausser der hierdurch erzeugten Rivalität im Gebrauch ver-\n besserter, Arbeiter ersetzender Maschinerie und neuer Produktionsmethoden,\n tritt jedesmal ein Punkt ein, wo Verwohlfeilerung der Waare durch ge-\n [Note: Ausfuhr von Korn u. s. w. aus den Vereinigten Staaten nach\n Grossbritanien (1850 und 1862):","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"15.61"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0062","text":"Weizen cwts. 1850 16,202,312.\n1862 41,033,503.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"15.62"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0063","text":"Gerste cwts. 1850 3,669,653.\n1862 6,624,800.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"15.63"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0064","text":"Hafer cwts. 1850 3,174,801.\n1862 4,426,994.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"15.64"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0065","text":"Roggen cwts. 1850 388,749.\n1862 7,108.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"15.65"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0066","text":"Weizenmehl cwts. 1850 3,819,440.\n1862 7,207,113.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"15.66"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0067","text":"Buchweizen cwts. 1850 1054.\n1862 19,571.","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"15.67"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0068","text":"Mais cwts. 1850 5,473,161.\n1862 11,694,818.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"15.68"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0069","text":"Bere oder Bigg (bes. Gerstenart) cwts. 1850 2039.\n1862 7675.","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"15.69"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0070","text":"Erbsen cwts. 1850 811,620.\n1862 1,024,722.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"15.70"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0071","text":"Bohnen cwts. 1850 1,822,972.\n1862 2,037,137.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"15.71"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0072","text":"Gesammteinfuhr cwts.: 1850 34,365,801.\n1862 74,083,351.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"15.72"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0073","text":"] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"444\", \"facs\": \"#f0463\"} --> waltsamen Druck des Arbeitslohnes unter den Werth der Arbeitskraft er-\n strebt wird [Note: In einem Aufruf der von den Schuhfabrikanten zu Leicester durch\n einen „lock out“ aufs Pflaster geworfenen Arbeiter an die „Trade Societies\n of England“, Juli 1866, heisst es u. a.: „Seit etwa 20 Jahren wurde die\n Schuhmacherei in Leicester umgewälzt durch Einführung des Nietens statt des\n Nähens. Gute Löhne konnten damals verdient werden. Bald dehute sich diess\n neue Geschäft sehr aus. Grosse Konkurrenz zeigte sich unter den verschiednen\n Firmen, welche den geschmackvollsten Artikel liefern könne. Kurz nachher jedoch\n entsprang eine schlechtere Art Konkurrenz, nämlich die, einander im Markt zu\n unterverkaufen (undersell).","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"15.73"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0074","text":"Die schädlichen Folgen offenbarten sich bald in\n Lohnherabsetzung, und so reissend schnell war der Fall im Preise der Arbeit, dass\n viele Firmen jetzt nur noch die Hälfte des ursprünglichen Lohns zahlen. Und\n dennoch, obgleich die Löhne tiefer und tiefer sinken, scheinen die Profite mit\n jeder Aenderung des Arbeitstarifs zu wachsen.“ — Ungünstige Perioden der In-\n dustrie werden selbst von den Fabrikanten benutzt, um durch übertriebne Lohn-\n herabsetzung, d. h. direkten Diebstahl an den nothwendigsten Lebensmitteln des\n Arbeiters, ausserordentliche Profite zu machen. Ein Beispiel. Es handelt sich um\n die Krise in der Seidenweberei zu Coventry: „Aus Nachweisen, die ich sowohl\n von Fabrikanten als Arbeitern erhielt, folgt zweifelsohne, dass die Löhne in einem\n grössern Umfang verkürzt wurden, als die Konkurrenz ausländischer Producenten\n oder andre Umstände ernöthigten.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"15.74"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0075","text":"Die Majorität der Weber arbeitet zu einer\n Lohnherabsetzung von 30 bis 40 %. Ein Stück Band, wofür der Weber fünf Jahre\n früher 6 oder 7 sh. erhielt, bringt ihm jetzt nur 3 sh. 3 d. oder 3 sh. 6 d. ein;\n andere Arbeit, früher zu 4 sh. und 4 sh. 3 d. bezahlt, erhält jetzt nur 2 sh. oder\n 2 sh. 3 d. Die Lohnherabsetzung ist grösser als zum Stachel der Nachfrage er-\n heischt ist. In der That, bei vielen Arten von Band war die Lohn-\n herabsetzung nicht einmal begleitet von irgend einer Herab-\n setzung im Preise des Artikels.“ (Bericht des Kommissärs\n F. D. Longe in „Ch. Empl. Comm. V. Rep. 1866“, p. 114, n. 1.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"15.75"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0076","text":"Wachsthum in der Anzahl der Fabrikarbeiter ist also bedingt durch\nproportionell viel rascheres Wachsthum des in den Fabriken ange-\n legten Gesammtkapitals. Dieser Prozess vollzieht sich aber nur inner-\n halb der Ebb- und Fluthperioden des industriellen Cyklus. Er wird zudem\n stets unterbrochen durch den technologischen Fortschritt, der Arbeiter bald\n virtuell ersetzt, bald faktisch verdrängt. Dieser qualitative Wech-\n sel im Maschinenbetrieb entfernt beständig Arbeiter aus der Fabrik oder\n verschliesst ihr Thor dem neuen Rekrutenstrom, während die bloss quanti-\n tative Ausdehnung der Fabriken neben den Herausgeworfenen frische\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"445\", \"facs\": \"#f0464\"} --> Kontingente verschlingt. Die Arbeiter werden so fortwährend repellirt\n und attrahirt, hin- und hergeschleudert, und diess bei beständigem Wechsel\n in Geschlecht, Alter und Geschick der Angeworbenen.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"15.76"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0077","text":"Die Schicksale des Fabrikarbeiters werden am besten veranschaulicht\n durch raschen Ueberblick der Schicksale der englischen Baum-\n wollindustrie.","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"15.77"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0078","text":"Von 1770 bis 1815 Baumwollindustrie gedrückt oder stagnant\n 5 Jahre. Während dieser ersten 45jährigen Periode besassen die eng-\n lischen Fabrikanten das Monopol der Maschinerie und des Weltmarkts.\n1815 bis 1821 gedrückt, 1822 und 1823 prosperirend, 1824\n Aufhebung der Kombinationsgesetze, allgemeine grosse Ausdehnung\n der Fabriken, 1825 Krise, 1826 grosses Elend und Aufstände unter den\n Baumwollarbeitern, 1827 leise Besserung, 1828 grosser Anwachs von\n Dampfwebstühlen und Ausfuhr, 1829 die Ausfuhr, besonders nach In-\n dien, übergipfelt alle früheren Jahre, 1830 überfüllte Märkte, grosser\n Nothstand, 1831 bis 1833 fortdauernder Druck; der Handel nach Ost-\n asien (Indien und China) wird dem Monopol der ostindischen Kompagnie\n entzogen. 1834 grosses Wachsthum von Fabriken und Maschinerie,\n Mangel an Händen.","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"15.78"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0079","text":"Das neue Armengesetz befördert die Wande-\n rung der Landarbeiter in die Fabrikdistrikte. Fegung der ländlichen\n Grafschaften von Kindern. Weisser Sklavenhandel. 1835 grosse Pros-\n perität. Gleichzeitige Todhungerung der Baumwollhandweber. 1836\n grosse Prosperität. 1837 und 1838 gedrückter Zustand und Krise.\n1839 Wiederaufleben. 1840 grosse Depression, Aufstände, Einschrei-\n ten des Militärs. 1841 und 1842 furchtbares Leiden der Fabrikarbeiter.\n 1842 schliessen die Fabrikanten die Hände von den Fabriken aus, um den\n Widerruf der Korngesetze zu erzwingen. Die Arbeiter strömen zu vielen Tau-\n senden nach Yorkshire, vom Militär zurückgetrieben, ihre Führer vor’s\n Gericht zu Lancaster gestellt. 1843 grosses Elend. 1844 Wieder-\n aufleben. 1845 grosse Prosperität. 1846 erst fortdauernder Auf-\n schwung, dann Symptome der Reaktion. Widerruf der Kornge-\n setze. 1847 Krise.","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"15.79"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0080","text":"Allgemeine Herabsetzung der Löhne um 10 und\n mehr Procent zur Feier des „big loaf“. 1848 fortdauernder Druck.\n Manchester unter militärischem Schutz. 1849 Wiederaufleben. 1850\n Prosperität. 1851 fallende Waarenpreise, niedrige Löhne, häufige\n Strikes. 1852 beginnende Verbesserung, Fortdauer der Strikes, Fabri-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"446\", \"facs\": \"#f0465\"} --> kanten drohn mit Import fremder Arbeiter. 1853 steigende Ausfuhr.\n Achtmonatlicher Strike und grosses Elend zu Preston. 1854 Prosperi-\n tät, Ueberfüllung der Märkte. 1855 Berichte von Bankerotten strömen\n ein aus den Vereinigten Staaten, Kanada, ostasiatischen Märkten. 1856\n grosse Prosperität. 1857 Krise. 1858 Verbesserung. 1859 grosse\n Prosperität, Zunahme der Fabriken. 1860 Zenith der englischen Baum-\n wollindustrie.","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"15.80"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0081","text":"Indische, australische und andere Märkte so überführt,\n dass sie noch 1863 kaum den ganzen Quark absorbirt haben. Franzö-\n sischer Handelsvertrag. Enormes Wachsthum von Fabriken und\n Maschinerie. 1861 Aufschwung dauert Zeitlang fort, Reaktion, ameri-\n kanischer Bürgerkrieg, Baumwollnoth. 1862 bis 63 vollständiger Zu-\n sammenbruch.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"15.81"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0082","text":"Die Geschichte des cotton famine ist zu charakteristisch, um nicht\n einen Augenblick dabei zu verweilen. Aus den Andeutungen über die\n Zustände des Weltmarkts 1860 bis 61 ersieht man, dass die Baumwoll-\n noth den Fabrikanten gelegen kam und zum Theil vortheilhaft war, eine\n Thatsache anerkannt in Berichten der Manchester Handelskammer, im Par-\n lament von Palmerston und Derby proklamirt, durch die Ereignisse be-\n stätigt [Note: Vgl. „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 30.] . Allerdings gab es 1861 unter den 2887 Baumwollfabriken\n des Vereinigten Königreichs viel kleine. Nach dem Bericht des Fabrik-\n inspektors A. Redgrave, dessen Verwaltungsbezirk von jenen 2887\n Fabriken 2109 einschliesst, wendeten von letztern 392 oder 19 % nur\nunter 10 Dampf-Pferdekraft an, 345 oder 16 % 10 und unter 20, und\n 1372 20 und mehr Pferdekraft [Note: l. c. p. 19.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"15.82"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0083","text":"Die Mehrzahl der kleinen Fabriken\n waren Webereien, während der Prosperitätsperiode seit 1858 errichtet,\n meist durch Spekulanten, wovon der eine das Garn, der andre die Maschi-\n nerie, der dritte die Baulichkeit lieferte, unter dem Betrieb ehemaliger\noverlookers oder andrer unbemittelter Leute. Diese kleinen Fabri-\n kanten gingen meist unter. Dasselbe Schicksal hätte ihnen die durch das\n Baumwollpech verhinderte Handelskrise bereitet. Obgleich sie ⅓ der\n Fabrikantenzahl bildeten, absorbirten ihre Fabriken einen ungleich geringeren\n Theil des in der Baumwollindustrie angelegten Kapitals. Was den Um-\n fang der Lähmung betrifft, so standen nach den authentischen Schätzungen\n im Oktober 1862 60.3 % der Spindeln und 58 % der Webstühle still.","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"15.83"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0084","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"447\", \"facs\": \"#f0466\"} --> Diess bezieht sich auf den ganzen Industriezweig und war natürlich sehr\n modificirt in den einzelnen Distrikten. Nur sehr wenige Fabriken arbei-\n teten volle Zeit (60 Stunden per Woche), die übrigen mit Unterbrechungen.\n Selbst für die wenigen Arbeiter, die volle Zeit und zu dem gewohn-\n ten Stücklohn beschäftigt, schmälerte sich nothwendig der Wochen-\n lohn in Folge der Ersetzung besserer Baumwolle durch schlechtere, der\n South Sea Island durch ägyptische (in Feinspinnereien), amerikanischer\n und ägyptischer durch Surat (ostindisch), und reiner Baumwolle durch\n Mischungen von Baumwollabfall mit Surat. Die kürzere Fiber der Surat-\n baumwolle, ihre schmutzige Beschaffenheit, die grössere Brüchigkeit der\n Fäden, der Ersatz des Mehls durch alle Art schwerer Ingredienzen beim\n Schlichten des Kettengarns u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"15.84"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0085","text":"verminderten die Geschwindigkeit der\n Maschinerie oder die Zahl der Webstühle, die ein Weber überwachen konnte,\n vermehrten die Arbeit mit den Irrthümern der Maschine und beschränkten\n mit der Produktenmasse den Stücklohn. Beim Gebrauch von Surat und\n mit voller Beschäftigung belief sich der Verlust des Arbeiters auf\n 20, 30 und mehr Procent. Die Mehrzahl der Fabrikanten setzte aber\n auch die Rate des Stücklohns um 5, 7½ und 10 Procent herab.\n Man begreift daher die Lage der nur 3, 3½, 4 Tage wöchentlich\n oder nur 6 Stunden per Tag Beschäftigten. Nachdem schon eine rela-\n tive Verbesserung eingetreten war, 1863, für Weber, Spinner u. s. w.\n Wochenlöhne von 3 sh. 4 d., 3 sh. 10 d., 4 sh. 6 d., 5 sh. 1 d. u. s. w. [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1863“, p. 41, 51.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"15.85"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0086","text":"Selbst unter diesen qualvollen Zuständen stand der Erfindungs-\n geist des Fabrikanten in Lohnabzügen nicht still. Diese wurden\n zum Theil verhängt als Strafe für die seiner schlechten Baumwolle, unpas-\n senden Maschinerie u. s. w. geschuldeten Fehler des Machwerks. Wo der\n Fabrikant aber Eigenthümer der cottages der Arbeiter, vergütete er sich\n selbst für Hausrente durch Abzüge vom nominellen Arbeitslohn. Fabrik-\n inspektor Redgrave erzählt von self-acting minders (sie über-\n wachen ein paar self-acting mules), die „am Ende vierzehntägi-\n ger voller Arbeit 8 sh. 11 d. verdienten und von dieser Summe\n wurde die Hausrente abgezogen, wovon der Fabrikant jedoch die Hälfte\nals Geschenk zurückgab, so dass die minders volle 6 sh. 11 d. nach\n Hause trugen. Der Wochenlohn der Weber rangirte von 2 sh. 6 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"15.86"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0087","text":"auf-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"448\", \"facs\": \"#f0467\"} --> wärts während der Schlusszeit von 1862“ [Note: „Reports etc. 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 41, 42.] . „Selbst dann wurde die\n Hausmiethe von den Löhnen häufig abgezogen, wenn die Hände nur kurze\n Zeit arbeiteten“ [Note: l. c. p. 57.] . Kein Wunder, dass in einigen Theilen Lancashire’s\n eine Art Hungerpest ausbrach! Charakteristischer als alles diess aber\n war es, wie die Revolutionirung des Produktionsprozesses\n auf Kosten des Arbeiters vor sich ging. Es waren förmliche experimenta\n in corpore vili, wie die der Anatomen an Fröschen. „Obgleich ich,“\n sagt Fabrikinspektor Redgrave, „die wirklichen Einnahmen der Arbeiter in\n vielen Fabriken gegeben habe, muss man nicht schliessen, dass sie den-\n selben Betrag Woche für Woche beziehn.","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"15.87"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0088","text":"Die Arbeiter erliegen den\n grössten Fluktuationen wegen des beständigen Experimenta-\n lisirens („experimentalizing“) der Fabrikanten … ihre Löhne\n steigen und fallen mit der Qualität des Baumwollgemischs; bald nähern\n sie sich um 15 % ihren früheren Einnahmen, und die nächste oder zweit-\n folgende Woche fallen sie um 50 bis 60 %“ [Note: l. c. p. 50, 51.] . Diese Experimente\n wurden nicht nur auf Kosten der Lebensmittel der Arbeiter gemacht. Mit\n allen ihren fünf Sinnen hatten sie zu büssen.","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"15.88"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0089","text":"„Die im Oeffnen der Baum-\n wollballen Beschäftigten unterrichten mich, dass der unerträgliche Gestank\n sie übel macht … Den in den Misch-, Scribbling- und Kardirräumen An-\n gewandten irritirt der freigesetzte Staub und Schmutz alle Kopföffnungen,\n erregt Husten und Schwierigkeit des Athmens … Wegen der Kürze der\n Fiber wird dem Garn beim Schlichten eine grosse Menge Stoff zugesetzt\n und zwar allerlei Substitute statt des früher gebrauchten Mehls. Daher\n Uebelkeit und Dispepsie der Weber. Bronchitis herrscht vor wegen des\n Staubs, ebenso Halsentzündung, ferner eine Hautkrankheit in Folge der Irri-\n tation der Haut durch den im Surat enthaltenen Schmutz.“ Andrerseits waren\n die Substitute für Mehl ein Fortunatussäckel für die Herrn Fabrikanten\n durch Vermehrung des Garngewichts. Sie machten „15 Pfund Rohmate-\n rial, wenn verwebt, 20 Pfund wiegen“ [Note: l. c. p. 62, 63.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"15.89"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0090","text":"In dem Bericht der Fabrik-\n inspektoren vom 30. April 1864 liest man: „Die Industrie ver-\n werthet diese Hilfsquelle jetzt in wahrhaft unanständigem Mass. Ich weiss\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"449\", \"facs\": \"#f0468\"} --> von guter Autorität, dass achtpfündiges Geweb von 5¼ Pfund Baumwolle\n und 2¾ Pfund Schlichte gemacht wird. Ein andres 5¼pfündiges\n Geweb enthielt zwei Pfund Schlichte. Diess waren ordinäre Shirtings\n für den Export. In andern Arten wurden manchmal 50 % Schlichte\n zugesetzt, so dass Fabrikanten sich rühmen können und sich auch\n wirklich rühmen, dass sie reich werden durch den Verkauf von\n Geweben für weniger Geld als das nominell in ihnen enthaltene\n Garn kostet“ [Note: „Reports etc. 30th April 1864“, p. 27.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"15.90"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0091","text":"Die Arbeiter aber hatten nicht nur unter den Ex-\n perimenten der Fabrikanten in den Fabriken, und der Municipalitäten\n ausserhalb der Fabriken, nicht nur von Lohnherabsetzung und Arbeits-\n losigkeit, von Mangel und Almosen, von den Elogen der Lords und Unter-\n häusler zu leiden. „Unglückliche Frauenzimmer, beschäftigungslos in\n Folge der Baumwollnoth, wurden Auswürflinge der Gesellschaft und blie-\n ben es ‥ Die Zahl junger Prostituirten hat mehr zugenommen als seit den\n letzten 25 Jahren“ [Note: Aus Brief des Chief Constable Harris von Bolton in „Reports of\n Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1865“, p. 61, 62.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"15.91"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0092","text":"Man findet also in den ersten 45 Jahren der britischen Baumwoll-\n industrie, von 1770—1815, nur 5 Jahre der Krise und Stagnation, aber\n diess war die Periode ihres Weltmonopols. Die zweite 48jährige Periode\n von 1815—1863 zählt nur 20 Jahre des Wiederauflebens und der Pros-\n perität auf 28 Jahre des Drucks und der Stagnation. Von 1815—1830\n beginnt die Konkurrenz mit dem kontinentalen Europa und den Vereinigten\n Staaten. Seit 1833 wird Ausdehnung der asiatischen Märkte erzwungen\n durch „Zerstörung der Menschenrace“. Seit Widerruf der Korngesetze,\n von 1846—1863, auf 8 Jahre mittlerer Lebendigkeit und Prosperität\n 9 Jahre Druck und Stagnation.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"15.92"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0093","text":"Die Lage der erwachsnen männlichen\n Baumwoll-Arbeiter, selbst während der Prosperitätszeit, zu beurtheilen\n aus der beigefügten Note [Note: In einem Aufruf der Baumwollarbeiter, Frühling 1863, zur Bil-\n dung einer Emigrationsgesellschaft heisst es u. a.: „Dass eine grosse Emigration von\n Fabrikarbeitern jetzt absolut nothwendig ist, werden nur wenige läugnen. Dass\n aber ein beständiger Emigrationsstrom zu allen Zeiten erheischt und es ohne\n denselben unmöglich ist, unsre Stellung unter gewöhnlichen Umständen] .","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"15.93"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0094","text":"Man hat gesehn, wie die Maschinerie die auf dem Handwerk beruhende\n [Running matter: I. 29] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"450\", \"facs\": \"#f0469\"} -->Cooperation und die auf Theilung der handwerksmässigen Arbeit beruhende\nManufaktur aufhebt. Ein Beispiel der ersten Art ist die Mähmaschine,\n sie ersetzt die Cooperation von Mähern. Ein schlagendes Beispiel der zwei-\n ten Art ist die Maschine zur Fabrikation von Nähnadeln. Nach Adam\n Smith verfertigten zu seiner Zeit 10 Männer durch Theilung der Arbeit\n täglich über 48,000 Nähnadeln. Eine einzige Maschine liefert dagegen\n 145,000 in einem Arbeitstag von 11 Stunden. Eine Frau oder ein\n Mädchen überwacht im Durchschnitt 4 solche Maschinen und producirt\n daher mit der Maschinerie täglich an 600,000, in der Woche über 3,000,000\n Nähnadeln [Note: „Ch. Empl. Comm. IV. Report. 1864“, p. 108, n. 447.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"15.94"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0095","text":"Sofern eine einzelne Arbeitsmaschine an die Stelle\n der Cooperation oder der Manufaktur tritt, kann sie selbst wieder zur\n Grundlage handwerksmässigen Betriebs werden. Indess bildet diese\n auf Maschinerie beruhende Reproduktion des Handwerksbetriebs nur den\nUebergang zum Fabrikbetrieb, der in der Regel jedesmal eintritt, so-\n bald mechanische Triebkraft, Dampf oder Wasser, die menschlichen\n Muskeln in der Bewegung der Maschine ersetzt. Sporadisch und eben-\n [Note: zu behaupten, zeigen folgende Thatsachen: Im Jahr 1814 betrug der officielle\n Werth (der nur Index der Quantität) der exportirten Baumwollgüter 17,665,378\n Pfd. St., ihr wirklicher Marktwerth 20,070,824 Pfd. St. Im Jahr 1858 betrug\n der officielle Werth der exportirten Baumwollgüter 182,221,681 Pfd. St., ihr\n wirklicher Marktwerth nur 43,001,322 Pfd.","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"15.95"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0096","text":"St., so dass die Verzehnfachung der\n Quantität wenig mehr als Verdopplung des Aequivalents bewirkte. Diess für das\n Land überhaupt und die Fabrikarbeiter im Besondern so ruinirende Resultat ward\n durch verschiedne cooperirende Ursachen producirt … Eine der hervorstechend-\n sten ist der beständige Ueberfluss von Arbeit, unentbehrlich für diesen Geschäfts-\n zweig, der, unter Strafe der Vernichtung, beständiger Expansion des Markts be-\n darf. Unsre Baumwollfabriken können stillgesetzt werden durch die periodische\n Stagnation des Handels, welche, unter gegenwärtiger Einrichtung, so unvermeid-\n lich ist, wie der Tod selbst. Aber desswegen steht der menschliche Erfindungs-\n geist nicht still.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"15.96"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0097","text":"Obgleich, niedrig angeschlagen, 6 Millionen diess Land während\n der letzten 25 Jahre verlassen haben, befindet sich dennoch in Folge fortwäh-\n render Deplacirung der Arbeit, um das Produkt zu verwohlfeilern, ein grosser\n Procentsatz der erwachsnen Männer selbst in den Zeiten höch-\n ster Prosperität ausser Stand, Beschäftigung irgend einer Art auf irgend\n welche Bedingungen in den Fabriken zu finden.“ („Reports of Insp. of\n Fact. 30th April 1863“, p. 51, 52.)","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"15.97"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0098","text":"Man wird in einem späteren Kapitel\n sehn, wie die Herrn Fabrikanten während der Baumwollkatastrophe die Emigration\n der Fabrikarbeiter auf alle Art, selbst von Staatswegen zu verhindern suchten.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"451\", \"facs\": \"#f0470\"} --> falls nur vorübergehend kann kleiner Betrieb sich verbinden mit mecha-\n nischer Triebkraft durch Miethe des Dampfs, wie in einigen Manufakturen\n Birmingham’s, durch Gebrauch kleiner kalorischer Maschinen, wie in ge-\n wissen Zweigen der Weberei u. s. w. [Note: In den Vereinigten Staaten ist derartige Reproduktion des Handwerks auf\n technologischer Grundlage der Maschinerie häufig. Die Koncentration, bei dem un-\n vermeidlichen Uebergang in den Fabrikbetrieb, wird eben desswegen, im Vergleich\n zu Europa und selbst zu England, mit Siebenmeilenstiefeln marschiren.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"15.98"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0099","text":"In der Seidenweberei zu Coven-\n try entwickelte sich naturwüchsig das Experiment der „Cottage-Fa-\n briken“. In der Mitte von Cottage-Reihen, quadratmässig gebaut,\n wurde ein s. g. Engine House errichtet für die Dampfmaschine und diese\n durch Schäfte mit den Webstühlen in den cottages verbunden. In allen\n Fällen war der Dampf gemiethet, z. B. zu 2½ sh. per Webstuhl. Diese\n Dampfrente war wöchentlich zahlbar, die Webstühle mochten laufen oder\n nicht. Jede cottage enthielt 2—6 Webstühle, den Arbeitern gehörig,\n oder auf Kredit gekauft, oder gemiethet. Der Kampf zwischen der\n Cottage-Fabrik und der Fabrik per se währte über 12 Jahre. Er hat ge-\n endet mit dem gänzlichen Ruin der 300 cottage factories [Note: Vgl. „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1865“, p. 64.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"15.99"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0100","text":"Wo die\n Natur des Prozesses nicht von vorn herein Produktion auf grosser Stufen-\n leiter bedang, durchliefen in der Regel die in den letzten Decennien neu\n aufkommenden Industrieen, wie z. B. Enveloppe-, Stahlfedermachen u. s. w.,\n erst den Handwerksbetrieb und dann den Manufakturbetrieb als kurzlebige\n Uebergangsphasen zum Fabrikbetrieb. Diese Metamorphose bleibt dort am\n schwierigsten, wo die manufakturmässige Produktion des Machwerks\n keine Stufenfolge von Entwicklungsprozessen, sondern eine Vielheit disparater\n Prozesse einschliesst. Diess bildete z. B. ein grosses Hinderniss der Stahl-\n federfabrik. Jedoch wurde schon vor ungefähr anderthalb Decennien ein\n Automat erfunden, der 6 disparate Prozesse auf einen Schlag verrichtet.\n Das Handwerk lieferte die ersten 12 Dutzend Stahlfedern 1820 zu\n 7 Pfd. St.","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"15.100"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0101","text":"4 sh., die Manufaktur lieferte sie 1830 zu 8 sh., und die\nFabrik liefert sie heute dem Grosshandel zu 2 bis 6 d. [Note: Herr Gillot errichtete zu Birmingham die erste Stahlfedermanufaktur auf\n grosser Stufenleiter. Sie lieferte schon 1851 über 180 Millionen Federn und verzehrte\n jährlich 120 Tonnen Stahlblech. Birmingham, das diese Industrie im Vereinigten]","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"15.101"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0102","text":"[Running matter: 29*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"452\", \"facs\": \"#f0471\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"15.102"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0103","text":"Mit der Entwicklung des Fabrikwesens und der sie begleitenden Umwäl-\n zung der Agrikultur dehnt sich nicht nur die Produktionsleiter in allen\n andern Industriezweigen aus, sondern verändert sich auch ihr\n Charakter. Das Princip des Maschinenbetriebs, den Produktionspro-\n zess in seine konstituirenden Phasen zu analysiren und die so gegebnen\n Probleme durch Anwendung der Mechanik, Chemie u. s. w., kurz der\n Naturwissenschaften zu lösen, wird überall bestimmend. Maschinerie\n drängt sich daher bald für diesen, bald für jenen Theilprozess in die Manu-\n fakturen. Die feste Krystallisation ihrer Gliederung, der alten Thei-\n lung der Arbeit entstammend, löst sich damit auf und macht fortwähren-\n dem Wechsel Platz. Abgesehn hiervon wird die Zusammensetzung des\n Gesammtarbeiters oder des kombinirten Arbeitspersonals von Grund aus umge-\n wälzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"15.103"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0104","text":"Im Gegensatz zur Manufakturperiode gründet sich der Plan\n der Arbeitstheilung jetzt auf Anwendung der Weiberarbeit, der Arbeit\n von Kindern aller Altersstufen, ungeschickter Arbeiter, wo es immer thubar,\n kurz der „cheap labour“, wohlfeilen Arbeit, wie der Engländer sie cha-\n rakteristisch nennt. Diess gilt nicht nur für alle auf grosser Stufen-\n leiter kombinirte Produktion, ob sie Maschinerie anwende oder\n nicht, sondern auch für die s. g. Hausindustrie, ob ausgeübt in den\n Privatwohnungen der Arbeiter oder in kleinen Werkstätten. Diese s. g.\n moderne Hausindustrie hat mit der altmodischen, die unabhängiges städti-\n sches Handwerk, selbstständige Bauernwirthschaft und vor allem ein\n Haus der Arbeiterfamilie voraussetzt, nichts gemein als den\n Namen. Sie ist jetzt verwandelt in das auswärtige Departement\n der Fabrik, der Manufaktur oder des Waarenmagazins.","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"15.104"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0105","text":"Neben den Fabrikarbeitern, Manufakturarbeitern und Handwerkern, die es\n in grossen Massen räumlich koncentrirt und direkt kommandirt, bewegt\n das Kapital durch unsichtbare Fäden eine andre Armee in den grossen\n Städten und über das flache Land zerstreuter Hausarbeiter. Beispiel: die\n Hemdenfabrik der Herrn Tillie zu Londonderry, Irland, die 1000 Fabrik-\n arbeiter und 9000 auf dem Land zerstreute Hausarbeiter beschäftigt [Note: „Ch. Empl. Comm. II. Rep. 1864“, p. LXVIII, n. 415.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"15.105"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0106","text":"Die Exploitation wohlfeiler und unreifer Arbeitskräfte wird in der\n [Note: Königreich monopolisirt, producirt jetzt jährlich Milliarden von Stahlfedern. Die\n Zahl der beschäftigten Personen betrug nach dem Census von 1861: 1428, dar-\n unter 1268 Arbeiterinnen, vom 5. Jahr an einrollirt.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"453\", \"facs\": \"#f0472\"} -->modernen Manufaktur schamloser als in der eigentlichen\n Fabrik, weil die hier existirende, technologische Grundlage, Ersatz der\n Muskelkraft durch Maschinen und Leichtigkeit der Arbeit, dort grossentheils\n wegfällt, zugleich der weibliche oder noch unreife Körper den Einflüssen\n giftiger Substanzen u. s. w. aufs gewissenloseste preisgegeben wird. Sie\n wird in der s. g.","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"15.106"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0107","text":"Hausarbeit schamloser als in der Manufaktur, weil\n die Widerstandsfähigkeit der Arbeiter mit ihrer Zersplitterung abnimmt,\n eine ganze Reihe räuberischer Parasiten sich zwischen den eigentlichen\n Arbeitgeber und den Arbeiter drängt, die Hausarbeit überall mit Maschi-\n nen- oder wenigstens Manufakturbetrieb in demselben Produktionszweig\n kämpft, die Armuth den Arbeiter der nöthigsten Arbeitsbedingungen,\n Raum, Licht, Ventilation u. s. w. beraubt, die Unregelmässigkeit der Be-\n schäftigung wächst, und endlich in diesen letzten Zufluchtsstätten der durch\n die grosse Industrie und Agrikultur „überzählig“ Gemachten die Arbeiter-\n konkurrenz nothwendig ihr Maximum erreicht.","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"15.107"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0108","text":"Die durch den Maschinen-\n betrieb erst systematisch ausgebildete Oekonomisirung der Produk-\n tionsmittel, von vorn herein zugleich rücksichtsloseste Verschwen-\n dung der Arbeitskraft und Raub an den normalen Voraussetzun-\n gen der Arbeitsfunktion, kehrt jetzt diese ihre antagonistische und men-\n schenmörderische Seite um so mehr heraus, je weniger in einem Industrie-\n zweig die gesellschaftliche Produktivkraft der Arbeit und\n die technologische Grundlage kombinirter Arbeitsprozesse\n entwickelt sind.","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"15.108"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0109","text":"Ich will nun an einigen Beispielen die oben aufgestellten Sätze er-\n läutern. Der Leser kennt in der That schon massenhafte Belege aus dem\n Abschnitt über den Arbeitstag. Die Metallmanufakturen in Birming-\n ham und Umgegend wenden grossentheils für sehr schwere Arbeit 30,000\n Kinder und junge Personen nebst 10,000 Weibern an. Man findet sie\n hier in den gesundheitswidrigen Gelbgiessereien, Knopffabriken, Glasur-,\n Galvanisirungs- und Lackirarbeiten [Note: Und nun gar Kinder im File-grinding zu Sheffield!] . Die Arbeitsexcesse für Erwachsne\n und Unerwachsne haben verschiedenen Londoner Zeitungs- und Buch-\n druckereien den rühmlichen Namen: „Das Schlachthaus“ ge-\n sichert [Note: „Ch. Empl. Comm. V. Rep. 1866“, p. 3, n. 24, p. 6, n. 55, 56,\n p. 7, n. 59, 60.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"15.109"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0110","text":"Dieselben Excesse, deren Schlachtopfer hier namentlich Weiber,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"454\", \"facs\": \"#f0473\"} --> Mädchen und Kinder, in der Buchbinderei. Schwere Arbeit für\n Unerwachsne in den Seilereien, Nachtarbeit in Salzwerken, Lichter- und\n andren chemischen Fabriken; mörderischer Verbrauch von Jungen in\n Seidenwebereien, die nicht mechanisch betrieben werden, zum Drehen der\n Webstühle [Note: l. c. p. 114, 115, n. 6—7. Der Kommissär bemerkt richtig, dass wenn\n sonst die Maschine den Menschen, hier der Junge verbatim die Maschine ersetzt.] . Eine der infamsten, schmutzigsten und schlecht bezahlte-\n sten Arbeiten, wozu mit Vorliebe junge Mädchen und Weiber verwandt\n werden, ist das Sortiren der Lumpen. Man weiss, dass Gross-\n britanien, abgesehn von seinen eignen unzähligen Lumpen, das Emporium für\n den Lumpenhandel der ganzen Welt bildet.","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"15.110"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0111","text":"Sie strömen dahin von Japan,\n den entferntesten Staaten Südamerikas und den kanarischen Inseln. Ihre\n Hauptzufuhrquellen aber sind Deutschland, Frankreich, Russland, Italien,\n Aegypten, Türkei, Belgien und Holland. Sie dienen zur Düngung, Fabri-\n kation von Flocken (für Bettzeug), Shoddy, und als Rohmaterial des Pa-\n piers. Die weiblichen Lumpensortirer dienen als Mediums, um Pocken\n und andre ansteckende Seuchen, deren erste Opfer sie selbst sind, zu col-\n portiren [Note: Sieh Bericht über den Lumpenhandel und zahlreiche Belege: „Public\n Health. VIII. Report. London 1866.“ Appendix, p. 196—208.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":111,"address":"15.111"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0112","text":"Als klassisches Beispiel für Ueberarbeit, schwere und un-\n passende Arbeit, und daher folgende Brutalisirung der von Kindesbeinen\n an konsumirten Arbeiter kann, neben der Minen- und Kohlenproduktion,\n die Ziegel- oder Backsteinmacherei gelten, wozu in England\n nur noch sporadisch die neuerfundne Maschinerie angewandt wird. Zwi-\n schen Mai und September dauert die Arbeit von 5 Uhr Morgens bis\n 8 Uhr Abends, und, wo Trocknung in freier Luft stattfindet, oft von 4 Uhr\n Morgens bis 9 Uhr Abends. Der Arbeitstag von 5 Uhr Morgens bis\n 7 Uhr Abends gilt für „reducirt“, „mässig“. Kinder beiderlei Geschlechts\n werden vom 6. und selbst vom 4. Jahr an verwandt. Sie arbeiten die-\n selbe Stundenzahl, oft mehr als die Erwachsnen. Die Arbeit ist hart\n und die Sommerhitze steigert noch die Erschöpfung. In einer Ziegelei zu\n Mosley z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":112,"address":"15.112"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0113","text":"machte ein 24jähriges Mädchen 2000 Ziegel täglich, unter-\n stützt von zwei unerwachsnen Mädchen als Gehilfen, welche den Lehm\n trugen und die Ziegelsteine aufhäuften. Diese Mädchen schleppten täg-\n lich 10 Tonnen die schlüpfrigen Seiten der Ziegelgrube von einer Tiefe von\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"455\", \"facs\": \"#f0474\"} --> 30 Fuss herauf und über eine Entfernung von 210 Fuss. „Es ist un-\n möglich für ein Kind durch das Fegfeuer einer Ziegelei zu passiren ohne\n grosse moralische Degradation. . . . Die nichtswürdige Sprache, die sie\n vom zartesten Alter an zu hören bekommen, die unfläthigen, unanständigen\n und schamlosen Gewohnheiten, unter denen sie unwissend und verwildert\n aufwachsen, machen sie für die spätere Lebenszeit gesetzlos, verworfen,\n liederlich. . . . Eine furchtbare Quelle der Demoralisation ist die Art der\n Wohnlichkeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":113,"address":"15.113"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0114","text":"Jeder moulder (der eigentlich geschickte Arbeiter und\n Chef einer Arbeiterg ruppe) liefert seiner Bande von 7 Personen Logis und\n Tisch in seiner Hütte oder cottage. Ob zu seiner Familie gehörig oder\n nicht, Männer, Jungen, Mädchen schlafen in der Hütte. Diese besteht\n gewöhnlich aus 2, nur ausnahmsweis aus 3 Zimmern, alle auf dem Erdge-\n schoss, mit wenig Ventilation. Die Körper sind so erschöpft durch die\n grosse Transpiration während des Tags, dass weder Gesundheitsregeln,\n Reinlichkeit noch Anstand irgendwie beobachtet werden. Viele dieser\n Hütten sind wahre Modelle von Unordnung, Schmutz und Staub. . . . Das\n grösste Uebel des Systems, welches junge Mädchen zu dieser Art Arbeit\n verwendet, besteht darin, dass es sie in der Regel von Kindheit an für ihr\n ganzes späteres Leben an das verworfenste Gesindel festkettet.","kind":"passage","sequence":114,"address":"15.114"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0115","text":"Sie wer-\n den rohe, bösmäulige Buben („rough, foul-mouthed boys“), bevor die\n Natur sie gelehrt hat, dass sie Weiber sind. Gekleidet in wenige\n schmutzige Lumpen, die Beine weit über das Knie entblösst, Haar und\n Gesicht mit Dreck beschmiert, lernen sie alle Gefühle der Bescheidenheit\n und der Scham mit Verachtung behandeln. Während der Essenszeit lie-\n gen sie auf den Feldern ausgestreckt oder gucken den Jungen zu, die in\n einem benachbarten Kanal baden. Ist ihr schweres Tagwerk endlich voll-\n bracht, so ziehn sie bessere Kleider an und begleiten die Männer in Bier-\n kneipen.“ Dass die grösste Versoffenheit von Kindesbeinen an in dieser\n ganzen Klasse herrscht, ist nur naturgemäss. „Das Schlimmste ist, dass\n die Ziegelmacher an sich selbst verzweifeln.","kind":"passage","sequence":115,"address":"15.115"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0116","text":"Sie könnten, sagte einer der\n Besseren zum Kaplan von Southallfields, ebensowohl versuchen den Teufel\n zu erheben und zu bessern als einen Ziegler, mein Herr!“ („You might as\n well try to raise and improve the devil as a brickie, Sir!“) [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. V. Report. 1866“, XVI, n. 86—97 und\n p. 130, n. 39—71. Vgl. auch ib. III. Rep. 1864, p. 48, 56.]","kind":"passage","sequence":116,"address":"15.116"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0117","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"456\", \"facs\": \"#f0475\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":117,"address":"15.117"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0118","text":"Ueber die kapitalistische Oekonomisirung der Arbeits-\n bedingungen in der modernen Manufaktur (worunter hier alle\n Werkstätten auf grosser Stufenleiter, ausser eigentlichen Fabriken, zu\n verstehn) findet man officielles und reichlichstes Material in dem IV. (1861)\n und VI. (1864) „Public Health Report“. Die Beschreibung der\n workshops (Arbeitslokale) namentlich der Londoner Drucker und Schnei-\n der überbietet die ekelhaftesten Phantasieen unsrer Romanschreiber. Die\n Wirkung auf den Gesundheitszustand der Arbeiter ist selbstverständlich.\nDr. Simon, der oberste ärztliche Beamte des Privy Council und officielle\n Herausgeber der „Public Health Reports“, sagt u.","kind":"passage","sequence":118,"address":"15.118"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0119","text":"a.: „In meinem vierten\n Bericht (1863) zeigte ich, wie es für die Arbeiter praktisch unmöglich ist\n darauf zu bestehen, was ihr erstes Gesundheitsrecht ist, das Recht,\n dass zu welchem Werk immer ihr Anwender sie versammelt, die Arbeit,\n so weit es von ihm abhängt, von allen vermeidbaren gesundheitswidrigen\n Umständen befreit sein soll. Ich wies nach, dass während die Arbeiter\n praktisch unfähig sind, sich selbst diese Gesundheitsjustiz zu verschaffen, sie\n keinen wirksamen Beistand von den bestallten Administratoren der Gesund-\n heitspolizei erlangen können. . . . Das Leben von Myriaden von Arbeitern\n und Arbeiterinnen wird jetzt nutzlos torturirt und verkürzt durch das end-\n lose physische Leiden, welches ihre blosse Beschäftigung erzeugt“ [Note: „Public Health“. VI. Rep. Lond. 1864, p. 31.] .\n Zur Illustration des Einflusses der Arbeitslokale auf den Gesundheitszustand\n giebt Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":119,"address":"15.119"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0120","text":"Simon folgende Sterblichkeitsliste:","kind":"passage","sequence":120,"address":"15.120"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0121","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"457\", \"facs\": \"#f0476\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":121,"address":"15.121"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0122","text":"Ich wende mich jetzt zur s. g. Hausarbeit. Um sich eine Vor-\n stellung von dieser auf dem Hintergrund der grossen Industrie aufgebauten\nExploitationssphäre des Kapitals und ihren Ungeheuerlich-\n keiten zu machen, betrachte man z. B. die scheinbar ganz idyllische, in\n einigen abgelegnen Dörfern Englands betriebne Nägelmacherei [Note: Es handelt sich hier um gehämmerte Nägel im Unterschied von den\n maschinenmässig fabricirten geschnittenen Nägeln. Siehe „Child. Empl.\n Comm. III. Report“, p. XI, p. XIX, n. 125—130, p. 53, n. 11, p. 114,\n n. 487, p. 137, n. 674.] .\n Hier genügen einige Beispiele aus den noch gar nicht maschinenmässig\n betriebnen oder mit Maschinen- und Manufakturtrieb konkurrirenden Zwei-\n gen der Spitzenfabrik und Strohflechterei.","kind":"passage","sequence":122,"address":"15.122"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0123","text":"Von den 150,000 Personen, die in der englischen Spitzenproduktion\n beschäftigt, fallen ungefähr 10,000 unter die Botmässigkeit des Fabrik-\n akts von 1861. Die ungeheure Mehrzahl der übrig bleibenden 140,000\n sind Weiber, junge Personen und Kinder beiderlei Geschlechts, obgleich\n das männliche Geschlecht nur schwach vertreten ist. Der Gesundheitszu-\n stand dieses „wohlfeilen“ Exploitationsmaterials ergiebt sich aus folgender\n Aufstellung des Dr. Trueman, Arzt beim General Dispensary von Not-\n tingham. Von je 686 Patienten, Spitzenmacherinnen, meist\n zwischen dem 17. und 24. Jahr, waren schwindsüchtig:","kind":"passage","sequence":123,"address":"15.123"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0124","text":"1852 1 auf 45,","kind":"passage","sequence":124,"address":"15.124"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0125","text":"1853 1 auf 28,","kind":"passage","sequence":125,"address":"15.125"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0126","text":"1854 1 auf 17,","kind":"passage","sequence":126,"address":"15.126"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0127","text":"1855 1 auf 18,","kind":"passage","sequence":127,"address":"15.127"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0128","text":"1856 1 auf 15,","kind":"passage","sequence":128,"address":"15.128"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0129","text":"1857 1 auf 13,","kind":"passage","sequence":129,"address":"15.129"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0130","text":"1861 1 auf 8 [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. II. Report“, p. XXII, n. 166.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":130,"address":"15.130"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0131","text":"1858 1 auf 15,","kind":"passage","sequence":131,"address":"15.131"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0132","text":"1859 1 auf 9,","kind":"passage","sequence":132,"address":"15.132"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0133","text":"1860 1 auf 8,","kind":"passage","sequence":133,"address":"15.133"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0134","text":"Dieser Fortschritt in der Rate der Schwindsucht muss dem opti-\n mistischsten Fortschrittler und lügenfauchendsten deutschen Freihandels-\n hausirburschen genügen.","kind":"passage","sequence":134,"address":"15.134"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0135","text":"Der Fabrikakt von 1861 regelt das eigentliche Mächen der\n Spitzen, soweit es durch Maschinerie geschieht, und diess ist die Regel\n in England. Die Zweige, die wir hier kurz berücksichtigen, und zwar nicht,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"458\", \"facs\": \"#f0477\"} --> soweit die Arbeiter in Manufakturen, Waarenhäusern u. s. w. koncentrirt,\n sondern nur sofern sie s. g. Hausarbeiter sind, zerfallen 1) in das\nfinishing (letztes Zurechtmachen der maschinenmässig fabricirten\n Spitzen, eine Kategorie, die wieder zahlreiche Unterabtheilungen ein-\n schliesst), 2) Spitzenklöppeln.","kind":"passage","sequence":135,"address":"15.135"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0136","text":"Das Lace finishing wird als Hausarbeit betrieben entweder in\n s. g. „Mistresses Houses“ oder von Weibern, einzeln oder mit ihren\n Kindern, in ihren Privatwohnungen. Die Weiber, welche die „Mistresses\n Houses“ halten, sind selbst arm. Das Arbeitslokal bildet Theil ihrer\n Privatwohnung. Sie erhalten Aufträge von Fabrikanten, Besitzern von\n Waarenmagazinen u. s. w. und wenden Weiber, Mädchen und junge Kin-\n der an, je nach dem Umfang ihrer Zimmer und der fluktuirenden Nach-\n frage des Geschäfts. Die Zahl der beschäftigten Arbeiterinnen wechselt\n von 20 zu 40 in einigen, von 10 zu 20 in andern dieser Lokale. Das\n durchschnittliche Minimalalter, worin Kinder beginnen, ist 6 Jahre, manche\n jedoch unter 5 Jahren.","kind":"passage","sequence":136,"address":"15.136"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0137","text":"Die gewöhnliche Arbeitszeit währt von 8 Uhr\n Morgens bis 8 Uhr Abends, mit 1½ Stunden für Mahlzeiten, die unregel-\n mässig und oft iu den stinkigen Arbeitslöchern selbst genommen werden.\n Bei gutem Geschäft währt die Arbeit oft von 8 Uhr (manchmal 6 Uhr)\n Morgens bis 10, 11 oder 12 Uhr Nachts. In englischen Kasernen be-\n trägt der vorschriftsmässige Raum für jeden Soldaten 500—600 Kubik-\n fuss, in den Militärlazarethen 1200. In jenen Arbeitslöchern kommen\n 67—100 Kubikfuss auf jede Person. Gleichzeitig verzehrt Gaslicht den\n Sauerstoff der Luft. Um die Spitzen rein zu halten, müssen die Kinder\n oft die Schuhe ausziehn, auch im Winter, obgleich das Estrich aus Pflaster\n oder Ziegeln besteht.","kind":"passage","sequence":137,"address":"15.137"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0138","text":"„Es ist nichts ungewöhnliches in Nottingham 14\n bis 20 Kinder in einem kleinen Zimmer von vielleicht nicht mehr als 12\n Quadratfuss zusammengepökelt zu finden, während 15 Stunden aus 24 be-\n schäftigt an einer Arbeit, an sich selbst erschöpfend durch Ueberdruss und\n Monotonie, zudem unter allen nur möglichen gesundheitszerstörenden Um-\n ständen ausgeübt … Selbst die jüngsten Kinder arbeiten mit einer gespann-\n ten Aufmerksamkeit und Geschwindigkeit, die erstaunlich sind, fast niemals\n ihren Fingern Ruhe oder langsamere Bewegung gönnend. Richtet man\n Fragen an sie, so erheben sie das Auge nicht von der Arbeit, aus Furcht\n einen Moment zu verlieren.“ Der „lange Stock“ dient den „mis-\n tresses“ als Anregungsmittel im Verhältniss, worin die Arbeitszeit ver-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"459\", \"facs\": \"#f0478\"} --> längert wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":138,"address":"15.138"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0139","text":"„Die Kinder ermüden allmälig und werden so rastlos wie\n Vögel gegen das Ende ihrer langen Gebundenheit an eine Beschäftigung,\n eintönig, für die Augen angreifend, erschöpfend durch die Uniformität der\n Körperhaltung. Es ist wahres Sklavenwerk.“ („Their work like sla-\n very“ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. II. Report 1864“, p. XIX, XX, XXI.] .) Wo Frauen mit ihren eigenen Kindern zu Hause, d. h. im\n modernen Sinn, in einem gemietheten Zimmer, häufig in einer Dachstube\n arbeiten, sind die Zustände wo möglich noch schlimmer. Diese Art Ar-\n beit wird 80 Meilen im Umkreis von Nottingham ausgegeben. Wenn das\n in den Waarenhäusern beschäftigte Kind sie 9 oder 10 Uhr Abends\n verlässt, giebt man ihm oft noch ein Bündel mit auf den Weg, um es zu\n Haus fertig zu machen.","kind":"passage","sequence":139,"address":"15.139"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0140","text":"Der kapitalistische Pharisäer, vertreten durch\n einen seiner Lohnknechte, thut das natürlich mit der salbungsvollen Phrase:\n ‚das sei für Mutter‘, weiss aber sehr wohl, dass das arme Kind aufsitzen\n und helfen muss [Note: l. c. p. XXI, XXVI.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":140,"address":"15.140"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0141","text":"Die Industrie des Spitzenklöppelns wird hauptsächlich in zwei\n englischen Agrikulturdistrikten betrieben, dem Honiton Spitzen-\n distrikt, 20 bis 30 Meilen längs der Südküste von Devonshire, mit\n Einschluss weniger Plätze von Nord-Devon, und einem andern Distrikt, der\n grossen Theil der Grafschaften von Buckingham, Bedford, Northampton\n und die benachbarten Theile von Oxfordshire und Huntingdonshire um-\n fasst. Die cottages der Ackerbautaglöhner bilden durchschnittlich die Ar-\n beitslokale. Manche Manufakturherrn wenden über 3000 dieser Hausar-\n beiter an, hauptsächlich Kinder und junge Personen, ausschliesslich weib-\n lichen Geschlechts. Die beim Lace finishing beschriebenen Zustände\n wiederholen sich. Nur treten an die Stelle der „mistresses houses“ die\n s. g. „lace schools“ (Spitzenschulen), gehalten von armen Weibern\n in ihren Hütten. Vom 5. Jahr an, manchmal jünger, bis zum 12.","kind":"passage","sequence":141,"address":"15.141"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0142","text":"oder\n 15. arbeiten die Kinder in diesen Schulen, während des ersten Jahres die\n Jüngsten von 4 bis 8 Stunden, später von 6 Uhr Morgens bis 8 und 10\n Uhr Abends. „Die Zimmer sind im allgemeinen gewöhnliche Wohnstuben\n kleiner cottages, der Kamin zugestopft zur Abwehr von Luftzug, die In-\n sassen manchmal auch im Winter nur von ihrer eignen animalischen Wärme\n geheizt. In andern Fällen sind diese s. g. Schulzimmer kleinen Vorraths-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"460\", \"facs\": \"#f0479\"} --> kammern ähnliche Räume, ohne Feuerplatz … Die Ueberfüllung dieser\n Löcher und die dadurch producirte Luftverpestung sind oft extrem.","kind":"passage","sequence":142,"address":"15.142"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0143","text":"Dazu\n kömmt die schädliche Wirkung von Gerinnen, Kloaken, verwesenden Stoffen\n und anderem Unrath, gewöhnlich in den Zugängen zu kleineren cottages.“\n Mit Bezug auf den Raum: „In einer Spitzenschule 18 Mädchen und Mei-\n sterin, 35 Kubikfuss für jede Person; in einer andern, wo unerträglicher\n Gestank, 18 Personen, per Kopf 24½ Kubikfuss. Man findet in dieser\n Industrie Kinder von 2 und 2½ Jahren verwandt“ [Note: l. c. p. XXIX, XXX.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":143,"address":"15.143"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0144","text":"Wo das Spitzenklöppeln in den ländlichen Grafschaften von Buckingham\n und Bedford aufhört, beginnt die Strohflechterei. Sie erstreckt sich\n über grossen Theil von Hertfordshire und die westlichen und nördlichen\n Theile von Essex. Es waren 1861 beschäftigt im Strohflechten und Stroh-\n hutmachen 40,043 Personen, 3815 davon männlichen Geschlechts aller\n Altersstufen, die andern weiblichen Geschlechts, und zwar 14,913 unter\n 20 Jahren, davon an 7000 Kinder. An die Stelle der Spitzenschulen\n treten hier die „straw plait schools“ (Strohflechtschulen). Die\n Kinder beginnen hier den Unterricht im Strohflechten gewöhnlich vom 4.,\n manchmal zwischen dem 3. und 4. Jahr Erziehung erhalten sie natürlich\n keine.","kind":"passage","sequence":144,"address":"15.144"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0145","text":"Die Kinder selbst nennen die Elementarschulen „natural\n schools“ (natürliche Schulen) im Unterschied zu diesen Blutaussau-\n gungsanstalten, worin sie einfach an der Arbeit gehalten werden, um das von\n ihren halbverhungerten Müttern vorgeschriebne Machwerk, meist 30 Yards\n per Tag, zu verfertigen. Diese Mütter lassen sie dann oft noch zu Haus\n bis 10, 11, 12 Uhr Nachts arbeiten. Das Stroh schneidet ihnen Finger\n und Mund, durch den sie es beständig anfeuchten. Nach der von\nDr. Ballard resumirten Gesammtansicht der medizinischen Beamten\n Londons bilden 300 Kubikfuss den Minimalraum für jede Person in\n einem Schlaf- oder Arbeitszimmer.","kind":"passage","sequence":145,"address":"15.145"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0146","text":"In den Strohflechtschulen ist der Raum\n aber noch spärlicher zugemessen als in den Spitzenschulen, „12⅔, 17,\n 18½ und unter 22 Kubikfuss für jede Person.“ Die kleineren dieser\n Zahlen, sagt Kommissair White, „repräsentiren weniger Raum als die\n Hälfte von dem, den ein Kind einnehmen würde, wenn verpackt in eine\n Schachtel von 3 Fuss nach allen Dimensionen.“ Diess der Lebensge-\n nuss der Kinder bis zum 12. oder 14. Jahr. Die elenden, verkommenen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"461\", \"facs\": \"#f0480\"} --> Eltern sinnen nur darauf aus den Kindern so viel als möglich herauszu-\n schlagen. Aufgewachsen fragen die Kinder natürlich keinen Deut nach\n den Eltern und verlassen sie. „Es ist kein Wunder, dass Unwissenheit\n und Laster überströmen in einer so aufgezüchteten Bevölkerung … Ihre\n Moral steht auf der niedrigsten Stufe . . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":146,"address":"15.146"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0147","text":"Eine grosse Anzahl der Weiber\n hat illegitime Kinder und manche in so unreifem Alter, dass selbst die\n Vertrauten der Kriminalstatistik darüber erstarren“ [Note: l. c. p. XL, XLI.] . Und das Hei-\n mathsland dieser Musterfamilien ist, so sagt der sicher im Christenthum\n kompetente Graf Montalembert, Europa’s christliches Musterland!","kind":"passage","sequence":147,"address":"15.147"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0148","text":"Der Arbeitslohn, in den eben behandelten Industriezweigen überhaupt\n jämmerlich (der ausnahmsweise Maximallohn der Kinder in den\n Strohflechtschulen 3 sh.), wird noch tief unter seinen Nominalbetrag herab-\n gedrückt durch das namentlich in den Spitzendistrikten allgemein vorherr-\n schende Trucksystem [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. I. Rep. 1863“, p. 185.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":148,"address":"15.148"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0149","text":"Die Verwohlfeilerung der Arbeitskraft durch blossen\n Missbrauch weiblicher und unreifer Arbeitskräfte, blossen Raub aller nor-\n malen Arbeits- und Lebensbedingungen, und blosse Brutalität der Ueber-\n und Nachtarbeit, stösst zuletzt auf gewisse nicht weiter überschreitbare Na-\n turschranken, also auch die auf diesen Grundlagen beruhende Verwohl-\n feilerung der Waaren und kapitalistische Exploitation überhaupt.\n Sobald dieser Punkt endlich erreicht ist, und es dauert lange, schlägt die\n Stunde für Einführung der Maschinerie und die nun rasche Verwand-\n lung der zersplitterten Hausarbeit (oder auch Manufaktur) in Fabrik-\n betrieb.","kind":"passage","sequence":149,"address":"15.149"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0150","text":"Das kolossalste Beispiel dieser Bewegung liefert die Produktion von\n „Wearing Apparel“ (zum Anzug gehörige Artikel). Nach der Klas-\n sifikation der „Child. Empl. Comm.“ umfasst diese Industrie Strohhut-\n und Damenhutmacher, Kappenmacher, Schneider, milliners und dressma-\n kers [Note: Millinery bezieht sich eigentlich nur auf den Kopfputz, doch auch\n Damenmäntel und Mantillen, während Dressmakers mit unsern Putzmache-\n rinnen identisch sind.] , Hemdenmacher und Nätherinnen, Korsetten-, Handschuh-, Schuh-\n macher, nebst vielen kleineren Zweigen, wie Fabrikation von Kravatten, Hals-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"462\", \"facs\": \"#f0481\"} --> bändern u. s. w. Das in England und Wales in diesen Industrieen be-\n schäftigte weibliche Personal betrug 1861: 586,298, wovon minde-\n stens 115,242 unter 20, 16,650 unter 15 Jahren. Zahl dieser Ar-\n beiterinnen im Vereinigten Königreich (1861): 750,334.","kind":"passage","sequence":150,"address":"15.150"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0151","text":"Die Zahl der\n gleichzeitig in Hut-, Schuh-, Handschuhmacherei und Schneiderei beschäf-\n tigten männlichen Arbeiter in England und Wales: 437,969, wovon\n 14,964 unter 15 Jahren, 89,285 fünfzehn- bis zwanzigjährig, 333,117\n über 20 Jahren. Es fehlen in dieser Angabe viele hierher gehörige\n kleinere Zweige. Nehmen wir aber die Zahlen, wie sie stehn, so ergiebt\n sich für England und Wales allein, nach dem Census von 1861, eine\n Summe von 1,024,277 Personen, also ungefähr so viel wie Ackerbau und\n Viehzucht absorbiren. Man fängt an zu verstehn, wozu die Maschinerie\n so ungeheure Produktenmassen hervorzaubern und so ungeheure Arbeiter-\n massen „freisetzen“ hilft.","kind":"passage","sequence":151,"address":"15.151"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0152","text":"Die Produktion des „Wearing Apparel“ wird betrieben durch Ma-\n nufakturen, welche in ihrem Innern nur die Theilung der Arbeit repro-\n ducirten, deren membra disjecta sie fertig vorfanden, durch kleinere\n Handwerksmeister, die aber nicht wie früher für individuelle Kon-\n sumenten, sondern für Manufakturen und Waarenmagazine arbeiten, so\n dass oft ganze Städte und Landstriche solche Zweige wie Schusterei u. s. w.\n als Specialität ausüben, endlich im grössten Umfang durch s. g. Haus-\n arbeiter, welche das auswärtige Departement der. Manufakturen,\n Waarenmagazine und selbst der kleineren Meister bilden [Note: Die englische millinery und das dressmaking werden meist in den Bau-\n lichkeiten der Anwender, theils durch dort wohnhafte und engagirte Arbeiterinnen,\n theils durch auswärts wohnende Taglöhnerinnen betrieben.] . Die Masse\n des Arbeitsstoffs, Rohstoffe, Halbfabrikate u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":152,"address":"15.152"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0153","text":"liefert die grosse In-\n dustrie, die Masse des wohlfeilen Menschenmaterials (taillable à merci et\n misericorde) die durch grosse Industrie und Agrikultur „Freigesetzten“.\n Die Manufakturen dieser Sphäre verdankten ihren Ursprung hauptsächlich\n dem Bedürfniss des Kapitalisten eine jeder Bewegung der Nachfrage ent-\n sprechende schlagfertige Armee unter der Hand zu haben [Note: Kommissär White besuchte eine Manufaktur für Militärkleider, die 1000\n bis 1200 Personen, fast alle weiblichen Geschlechts, beschäftigte, eine Schuhmanu-\n faktur mit 1300 Personen, wovon beinahe die Hälfte Kinder und junge Personen\n u. s. w. („Child. Empl. Comm. II. Rep.“, p. XVII, n. 319.)] . Diese\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"463\", \"facs\": \"#f0482\"} --> Manufakturen liessen jedoch neben sich den zerstreuten handwerksmässigen\n und Hausbetrieb als breite Grundlage fortbestehn.","kind":"passage","sequence":153,"address":"15.153"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0154","text":"Die grosse Produktion\n von Mehrwerth in diesen Arbeitszweigen, zugleich mit der progressiven\n Verwohlfeilerung ihrer Artikel, war und ist hauptsächlich geschuldet dem\n Minimum des zu kümmerlicher Vegetation nöthigen Arbeitslohns, verbun-\n den mit dem Maximum menschenmöglicher Arbeitszeit. Es war eben die\n Wohlfeilheit des in Waare verwandelten Menschenschweisses und Menschen-\n bluts, welche den Absatzmarkt beständig erweiterte und täglich erweitert,\n für England namentlich auch den Kolonialmarkt, wo überdem englische\n Gewohnheit und Geschmack vorherrschen. Endlich trat ein Knotenpunkt\n ein. Die Grundlage der alten Methode, bloss brutale Ausbeutung des Ar-\n beitermaterials, mehr oder minder begleitet von systematisch entwickelter\n Arbeitstheilung, genügte dem wachsenden Markt und der noch rascher\n wachsenden Konkurrenz der Kapitalisten nicht länger. Die Stunde der\n Maschinerie schlug.","kind":"passage","sequence":154,"address":"15.154"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0155","text":"Die entscheidend revolutionäre Maschine,\n welche die sämmtlichen zahllosen Zweige dieser Produktionssphäre, wie\n Putzmacherei, Schneiderei, Schusterei, Nätherei, Hutmacherei u. s. w.\n gleichmässig ergreift, ist die — Nähmaschine.","kind":"passage","sequence":155,"address":"15.155"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0156","text":"Ihre unmittelbare Wirkung auf die Arbeiter ist ungefähr die aller\n Maschinerie, welche in der Periode der grossen Industrie neue Geschäfts-\n zweige erobert. Kinder im unreifsten Alter werden entfernt. Der Lohn\n der Maschinenarbeiter steigt verhältnissmässig zu dem der Hausar-\n beiter, wovon viele zu „den Aermsten der Armen“ („the poorest of the\n poor“) gehören. Der Lohn der besser gestellten Handwerker, mit denen\n die Maschine konkurrirt, sinkt. Die neuen Maschinenarbeiter sind aus-\n schliesslich Mädchen und junge Frauen. Mit Hilfe der mechanischen\n Kraft vernichten sie das Monopol der männlichen Arbeit in schwererem\n Werk und verjagen aus leichterem Massen alter Weiber und unreifer\n Kinder. Die übermächtige Konkurrenz erschlägt die schwächsten Hand-\n arbeiter.","kind":"passage","sequence":156,"address":"15.156"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0157","text":"Das gräuliche Wachsthum des Hungertods (death from\n starvation) in London während des letzten Decenniums läuft parallel\n mit der Ausdehnung der Maschinennäherei [Note: Ein Beispiel. Am 26. Februar 1864 enthält der wöchentliche Sterblich-\n keitsbericht des Registrar General 5 Fälle von Hungertod. Am selben Tag\n berichtet die Times einen neuen Fall von Hungertod. Sechs Opfer des Hunger-\n tods in einer Woche!] . Die neuen Arbeiterinnen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"464\", \"facs\": \"#f0483\"} --> an der Nähmaschine, welche von ihnen mit Hand und Fuss oder mit der\n Hand allein, sitzend und stehend, je nach Schwere, Grösse und Specialität\n der Maschine, bewegt wird, verausgaben grosse Arbeitskraft. Ihre Be-\n schäftigung wird gesundheitswidrig durch die Dauer des Prozesses, ob-\n gleich er meist kürzer als im alten System. Ueberall, wo die Nähma-\n schine, wie beim Schuh-, Korsett-, Hutmachen u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":157,"address":"15.157"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0158","text":"w., ohnehin enge und\n überfüllte Werkstätten heimsucht, vermehrt sie die gesundheitswidrigen\n Einflüsse. „Die Wirkung,“ sagt Kommissär Lord, „beim Eintritt in\n niedrig gestochne Arbeitslokale, wo 30 bis 40 Maschinenarbeiter zusam-\n menwirken, ist unerträglich . . . . Die Hitze, theilweis den Gasöfen zur\n Wärmung der Bügeleisen geschuldet, ist schrecklich . . . . Wenn selbst in\n solchen Lokalen s. g. mässige Arbeitsstunden, d. h. von 8 Uhr Morgens\n bis 6 Uhr Abends, vorherrschen, fallen dennoch jeden Tag 3 oder 4 Per-\n sonen regelmässig in Ohnmacht“ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm.“ II. Rep. 1864, p. LXVII, n. 406—9,\n p. 84, n. 124, p. LXXIII, n. 441, p. 66, n. 6, p. 84, n. 126, p. 78, n. 85, p. 76,\n n. 69, p. LXXII, n. 483.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":158,"address":"15.158"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0159","text":"Die Umwälzung der gesellschaftlichen Betriebsweise,\n diess nothwendige Produkt der Umwandlung des Produktions-\n mittels, vollzieht sich in einem bunten Wirrwarr von Uebergangsfor-\n men. Sie wechseln mit dem Umfang, worin, und der Zeitlänge, während\n welcher die Nähmaschine den einen oder andern Industriezweig bereits er-\n griffen hat, mit der vorgefundnen Lage der Arbeiter, dem Uebergewicht des\n Manufaktur-, Handwerks- oder Hausbetriebs, dem Miethpreis der Arbeitslo-\n kale [Note: „The rental of premises required for work rooms seems the element\n which ultimately determines the point, and consequently it is in the metropolis,\n that the old system of giving work out to small employers and families has been\n longest retained, and earliest returned to.“ (l. c. p. 83, n. 123.) Die Schluss-\n phrase bezieht sich ausschliesslich auf Schusterei.]  u. s. w. In der Putzmacherei z.","kind":"passage","sequence":159,"address":"15.159"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0160","text":"B., wo die Arbeit meist schon\n organisirt war, hauptsächlich durch einfache Cooperation, bildet die Nähma-\n schine zunächst nur einen neuen Faktor des Manufakturbetriebs. In der\n Schneiderei, Hemdenmacherei, Schusterei u. s. w. durchkreuzen sich alle For-\n men. Hier eigentlicher Fabrikbetrieb. Dort erhalten Zwischenanwender das\n Rohmaterial vom Kapitalisten en chef und gruppiren in „Kammern“ oder\n „Dachstuben“ 10 bis 50 und mehr Lohnarbeiter um Nähmaschinen.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"465\", \"facs\": \"#f0484\"} --> Endlich, wie bei aller Maschinerie, die kein gegliedertes System bildet, und\n im Zwergformat anwendbar ist, benutzen Handwerker oder Hausarbeiter,\n mit eigner Familie oder Zuziehung weniger fremder Arbeiter, auch ihnen\n selbst gehörige Nähmaschinen [Note: In der Handschuhmacherei u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":160,"address":"15.160"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0161","text":"w., wo die Lage der Arbeiter von der\n der Paupers kaum unterscheidbar, kömmt diess nicht vor.] . Thatsächlich überwiegt jetzt in Eng-\n land das System, dass der Kapitalist eine grössere Maschinenanzahl in\n seinen Baulichkeiten koncentrirt und dann das Maschinenprodukt zur wei-\n teren Verarbeitung unter die Armee der Hausarbeiter vertheilt [Note: l. c. n. 122.] . Die\n Buntheit der Uebergangsformen versteckt jedoch nicht die Tendenz zur\n Verwandlung in eigentlichen Fabrikbetrieb.","kind":"passage","sequence":161,"address":"15.161"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0162","text":"Diese Tendenz wird\n genährt durch den Charakter der Nähmaschine selbst, deren mannigfaltige\n Anwendbarkeit zur Vereinigung früher getrennter Geschäftszweige in der-\n selben Baulichkeit und unter dem Kommando desselben Kapitals drängt;\n durch den Umstand, dass vorläufiges Nadelwerk und einige andere Operatio-\n nen am geeignetsten am Sitz der Maschine verrichtet werden; endlich durch\n die unvermeidliche Expropriation der Handwerker und Hausarbei-\n ter, die mit eignen Maschinen produciren. Diess Fatum hat sie zum Theil\n schon jetzt erreicht. Die stets wachsende Masse des in Nähmaschinen\n angelegten Kapitals [Note: In der für den Grossverkauf producirenden Stiefel- und Schuhmacherei\n von Leicester allein waren 1864 bereits 800 Nähmaschinen im Gebrauch.]  spornt die Produktion und erzeugt Marktstockun-\n gen, welche das Signal zum Verkauf der Nähmaschinen durch die Haus-\n arbeiter läuten.","kind":"passage","sequence":162,"address":"15.162"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0163","text":"Die Ueberproduktion von solchen Maschinen selbst zwingt\n ihre absatzbedürftigen Producenten sie auf wöchentliche Miethe zu ver-\n leihn und schafft damit eine für die kleinen Maschineneigner tödtliche Kon-\n kurrenz [Note: l. c. p. 84, n. 124.] . Stets noch fortdauernde Konstruktionswechsel und Verwohl-\n feilerung der Maschinen depreciiren eben so beständig ihre alten Exem-\n plare und lassen sie nur noch massenhaft, zu Spottpreisen gekauft, in\n der Hand grosser Kapitalisten, profitlich anwenden. Endlich giebt die\n Substitution der Dampfmaschine für den Menschen, hier wie in allen\n ähnlichen Umwälzungsprozessen, den Ausschlag. Die Anwendung der\n Dampfkraft stösst im Anfang auf rein technische Hindernisse, wie Schütteln\n der Maschinen, Schwierigkeit in der Beherrschung ihrer Geschwindigkeit,\n [Running matter: I.","kind":"passage","sequence":163,"address":"15.163"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0164","text":"30] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"466\", \"facs\": \"#f0485\"} --> raschen Verderb der leichteren Maschinen u. s. w., lauter Hindernisse,\n welche die Erfahrung bald überwinden lehrt [Note: So im Armee-Kleidungsdepot zu Pimlico, London, in der Hemdenfabrik\n von Tillie und Henderson zu Londonderry, in der Kleiderfabrik der Firma Tait, zu\n Limerick, die an 1200 „Hände“ vernutzt.] . Wenn einerseits die\n Koncentration vieler Arbeitsmaschinen in grösseren Manufakturen zur An-\n wendung der Dampfkraft treibt, beschleunigt andrerseits die Konkurrenz\n des Dampfes mit Menschenmuskeln Koncentration von Arbeitern und Ar-\n beitsmaschinen in grossen Fabriken.","kind":"passage","sequence":164,"address":"15.164"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0165","text":"So erlebt England gegenwärtig in\n der kolossalen Produktionssphäre des „Wearing Apparel“, wie den mei-\n sten übrigen Gewerken, die Umwälzung der Manufaktur, des Handwerks\n und der Hausarbeit in Fabrikbetrieb, nachdem alle jene Formen, unter\n dem Einfluss der grossen Industrie gänzlich verändert, zersetzt, ent-\n stellt, bereits längst alle Ungeheuerlichkeiten des Fabriksystems ohne\n seine positiven Entwicklungsmomente reproducirt und selbst übertrieben\n hatten [Note: „Tendency to factory system.“ (l. c. p. LXVII.) „The whole\n employment is at this time in a state of transition, and is undergoing the same\n change as that effected in the lace trade, weaving etc.“ (l. c. n. 405). „A com-\n plete Revolution.“ (l. c. p. XLVI, n. 318.) Zur Zeit der „Child. Empl.\n Comm.“ von 1840 war die Strumpfwirkerei noch Handarbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":165,"address":"15.165"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0166","text":"Seit 1846\n wurde verschiedenartige Maschinerie eingeführt, jetzt durch Dampf getrieben. Die\n Gesammtzahl der in der englischen Strumpfwirkerei beschäftigten Personen\n beiderlei Geschlechts und aller Altersstufen vom 3. Jahr an betrug 1862 ungefähr\n 120,000 Personen. Davon, nach Parliamentary Return vom 11. Februar, 1862\n doch nur 4063 unter der Botmässigkeit des Fabrikakts.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":166,"address":"15.166"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0167","text":"Diese naturwüchsig vorgehende industrielle Revolution wird künstlich\n beschleunigt durch die Ausdehnung der Fabrikgesetze auf alle Indu-\n striezweige, worin Weiber, junge Personen und Kinder arbeiten. Die zwangs-\n mässige Regulation des Arbeitstags nach Länge, Pausen, Anfangs- und\n Endpunkt, das System der Ablösung für Kinder, der Ausschluss aller Kin-\n der unter einem gewissen Alter u. s. w. ernöthigen einerseits vermehrte\n Maschinerie [Note: So z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":167,"address":"15.167"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0168","text":"in der Töpferei berichtet die Firma Cochrane von der „Britain\n Pottery, Glasgow“: „To keep up our quantity, we have gone extensively into\n machines wrought by unskilled labour, and every day convinces us that we\n can produce a greater quantity than by the old method.“ („Reports of Insp.]  und Ersatz von Muskeln durch Dampf als Trieb-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"467\", \"facs\": \"#f0486\"} --> kraft [Note: So nach Einführung des Fabrikakts in die Töpferei grosse Zunahme der\n power jiggers statt der handmoved jiggers.] . Andrerseits, um im Raum zu gewinnen, was in der Zeit verloren geht,\n findet Streckung der gemeinschaftlich vernutzten Produktionsmittel statt, der\n Oefen, Baulichkeiten u. s. w., also in einem Wort grössere Koncentration der\n Produktionsmittel und entsprechende grössere Konglomeration von Arbeitern.","kind":"passage","sequence":168,"address":"15.168"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0169","text":"Der leidenschaftlich wiederholte Haupteinwand jeder mit dem Fabrikgesetz\n bedrohten Manufaktur ist in der That die Nothwendigkeit grösserer Kapital-\n auslage, um das Geschäft in seinem alten Umfang fortzuführen. Was aber die\n Zwischenformen zwischen Manufaktur und Hausarbeit und letztre selbst\n betrifft, so versinkt ihr Boden mit der Schranke des Arbeitstags und der\n Kinderarbeit. Schrankenlose Ausbeutung wohlfeiler Arbeitskräfte\n bildet die einzige Grundlage ihrer Konkurrenzfähigkeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":169,"address":"15.169"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0170","text":"Wesentliche Bedingung des Fabrikbetriebs, namentlich sobald er der\n Regulation des Arbeitstags unterliegt, ist normale Sicherheit des Re-\n sultats, d. h. Produktion eines bestimmten Quantums Waare oder eines\n bezweckten Nutzeffekts in gegebnem Zeitraum. Die gesetzlichen Pausen\n des regulirten Arbeitstags unterstellen ferner plötzlichen und periodischen\n Stillstand der Arbeit ohne Schaden für das im Produktionsprozess befind-\n liche Machwerk. Diese Sicherheit des Resultats und Unterbrechungs-\n fähigkeit der Arbeit sind natürlich in rein mechanischen Gewerken leichter\n erzielbar als dort wo chemische und andere physische Prozesse eine Rolle\n spielen, wie z. B. in Töpferei, Bleicherei, Färberei, Bäckerei, den meisten\n Metallmanufakturen.","kind":"passage","sequence":170,"address":"15.170"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0171","text":"Mit dem Schlendrian des unbeschränkten Arbeits-\n tags, der Nachtarbeit, und freier Menschenverwüstung, gilt jedes natur-\n wüchsige Hinderniss bald für eine ewige „Naturschranke“ der Pro-\n duktion. Kein Gift vertilgt Ungeziefer sichrer als das Fabrikgesetz solche\n „Naturschranken“. Niemand schrie lauter über „Unmöglichkeiten“ als\n die Herren von der Töpferei. 1864 wurde ihnen das Fabrikgesetz\n oktroyirt und alle Unmöglichkeiten waren schon 16 Monate später ver-\n schwunden. „Die“ durch das Fabrikgesetz hervorgerufene „verbesserte\n Methode Schliff durch Druck statt durch Ausdünstung zu machen, die neue\n Konstruktion der Oefen zum Trocknen der frischen Waare u. s. w. sind\n Ereignisse von grosser Wichtigkeit in der Kunst der Töpferei und bezeich-\n [Note: of Fact. 31. Oct. 1865“, p. 13.) „Die Wirkung des Fabrikakts ist zu weiterer\n Einführung von Maschinerie zu treiben.“ (l. c. p.","kind":"passage","sequence":171,"address":"15.171"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0172","text":"13, 14.)] \n [Running matter: 30*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"468\", \"facs\": \"#f0487\"} --> nen einen Fortschritt derselben, wie ihn das letzte Jahrhundert nicht auf-\n weisen kann … Die Temperatur der Oefen ist beträchtlich vermindert,\n bei beträchtlicher Abnahme im Kohlenkonsum und rascherer Wirkung auf\n die Waare“ [Note: l. c. p. 96 und 127.] . Trotz aller Prophezeiung stieg nicht der Kostpreis\n des Erdenguts, wohl aber die Produktenmasse, so dass die Ausfuhr\n der 12 Monate von December 1864 bis December 1865 einen Werth-\n überschuss von 138,628 Pfd. St. über den Durchschnitt der drei vorigen\n Jahre ergab. In der Fabrikation von Schwefelhölzern galt es als Natur-\n gesetz, dass Jungen, selbst während der Herunterwürgung ihres Mittags-\n mahls, die Hölzer in eine warme Phosphorkomposition tunkten, deren gifti-\n ger Dampf ihnen in das Gesicht stieg.","kind":"passage","sequence":172,"address":"15.172"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0173","text":"Mit der Nothwendigkeit Zeit zu\n ökonomisiren, erzwang der Fabrikakt (1864) eine „dipping machine“\n (Eintauchungsmaschine), deren Dämpfe den Arbeiter nicht erreichen kön-\n nen [Note: Die Einführung dieser und andrer Maschinerie in die Schwefelholzfabrik\n hat in einem Departement derselben 230 junge Personen durch 32 Jungen und\n Mädchen von 14 bis 17 Jahren ersetzt. Diese Ersparung von Arbeitern wurde 1865\n weiter geführt durch Anwendung der Dampfkraft.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":173,"address":"15.173"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0174","text":"So wird jetzt in den noch nicht dem Fabrikgesetz unterworfenen\n Zweigen der Spitzenmanufaktur behauptet, die Mahlzeiten könnten nicht\n regelmässig sein „wegen der verschiednen Zeitlängen, die verschiedne Spitzen-\n materialien zur Trocknung brauchen, und die von 3 Minuten auf eine Stunde\n und mehr variiren.“ Hierauf antworten die Kommissäre der „Children’s\n Employment Comm.“: „Die Umstände sind genau dieselben wie in der\n Tapetendruckerei. Einige der Hauptfabrikanten in diesem Zweig machten\n lebhaft geltend, die Natur der verwandten Materialien und die Verschie-\n denartigkeit der Prozesse, die sie durchlaufen, erlaubten ohne grossen\n Verlust keine plötzliche Stillsetzung der Arbeit für Mahlzeiten. . . . Durch\n die 6. Klausel der 6.","kind":"passage","sequence":174,"address":"15.174"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0175","text":"Sektion des Factory Act’s Extension Act\n (1864) ward ihnen eine achtzehnmonatliche Frist vom Erlassungsdatum\n des Akts an eingeräumt, nach deren Ablauf sie sich den durch den Fabrik-\n akt specificirten Erfrischungspausen fügen müssten [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. II. Rep. 1864“, p. IX, n. 50.] . Kaum hatte\n das Gesetz parlamentarische Sanktion erhalten, als die Herren Fabrikanten\n auch entdeckten: „Die Missstände, die wir von der Einführung des Fabrik-\n gesetzes erwarteten, sind nicht eingetreten. Wir finden nicht, dass die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"469\", \"facs\": \"#f0488\"} --> Produktion irgendwie gelähmt ist. In der That, wir produciren mehr in\n derselben Zeit“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1865“, p. 22.] . Man sieht, das englische Parlament, dem sicher\n Niemand Genialität vorwerfen wird, ist durch Erfahrung zur Einsicht ge-\n langt, dass ein Zwangsgesetz alle s. g.","kind":"passage","sequence":175,"address":"15.175"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0176","text":"Naturhindernisse der Pro-\n duktion gegen Beschränkung und Reglung des Arbeitstags einfach weg-\n diktiren kann. Bei Einführung des Fabrikakts in einen Industriezweig\n wird daher ein Termin von 6 bis 18 Monaten gestellt, innerhalb dessen es\n Sache der Fabrikanten ist die technischen Hindernisse wegzuräumen.\n Mirabeau’s: „Impossible! Ne me nommez jamais cet imbécil de mot!“ gilt\n namentlich für die moderne Technologie.","kind":"passage","sequence":176,"address":"15.176"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0177","text":"Wenn aber das Fabrikgesetz so\n die zur Verwandlung des Manufakturbetriebs in Fabrikbetrieb nothwendi-\n gen materiellen Elemente treibhausmässig reift, beschleunigt es zugleich\n durch die Nothwendigkeit vergrösserter Kapitalauslage den Untergang der\n kleineren Meister und die Koncentration des Kapitals [Note: „Die nöthigen Verbesserungen können in vielen alten Manufakturen\n nicht eingeführt werden, ohne Kapitalauslage über die Mittel vieler gegenwärtiger\n Besitzer … Eine vorübergehende Disorganisation begleitet nothwendig die Ein-\n führung der Fabrikakte. Der Umfang dieser Disorganisation steht in direktem\n Verhältniss zur Grösse der zu heilenden Missstände.“ (l. c. p. 96, 97.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":177,"address":"15.177"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0178","text":"Abgesehn von den rein technischen und technisch beseitbaren Hinder-\n nissen stösst die Regulation des Arbeitstags auf unregelmässige Gewohnheiten\n der Arbeiter selbst, namentlich wo Stücklohn vorherrscht und Verbumm-\n lung der Zeit in einem Tages- oder Wochenabschnitt durch nachträgliche\n Ueberarbeit oder Nachtarbeit aufgemacht werden kann, eine Methode, die\n den erwachsnen Arbeiter brutalisirt, seine unreifen und weiblichen Genos-\n sen ruinirt [Note: In den Hochöfen z. B. „work towards the end of the week is generally much\n increased in duration, in consequence of the habit of the men of idling on Monday\n and occasionally during a part or the whole of Tuesdays also.“ („Child.\n Empl. Comm. IV. Rep.“, p. VI.) „The little masters generally have very\n irregular hours.","kind":"passage","sequence":178,"address":"15.178"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0179","text":"They lose 2 or 3 days, and then work all night to make it up …\n They always employ their own children, if they have any.“ (l. c. p. VII.) „The\n want of regularity in coming to work, encouraged by the possibility and practice\n of making up for this by working longer hours.“ (l. c. p. XVIII.) „Enormous\n loss of time in Birmingham … idling part of the time, slaving the rest.“ (l. c.\n p. XI.)] . Obgleich diese Regellosigkeit in Verausgabung der Ar-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"470\", \"facs\": \"#f0489\"} --> beitskraft eine naturwüchsig rohe Reaktion gegen die Langweile mono-\n toner Arbeitsplackerei ist, entspringt sie jedoch in ungleich höherem Grad\n aus der Anarchie der Produktion selbst, die ihrerseits wieder ungezügelte\n Exploitation der Arbeitskraft durch das Kapital voraussetzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":179,"address":"15.179"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0180","text":"Neben die\nallgemeinen periodischen Wechselfälle des industriellen Cyklus und\n die besondern Marktschwankungen in jedem Produktionszweig, treten\n namentlich die s. g. Saison, beruhe sie nun auf Periodicität der Schiff-\n fahrt günstiger Jahreszeiten oder auf der Mode, und die Plötzlichkeit grosser\n und in kürzester Frist auszuführender Ordres. Die Gewohnheit der letz-\n tern dehnte sich mit Eisenbahnen und Telegraphie aus. „Die Ausdeh-\n nung des Eisenbahnsystems“, sagt z. B. ein Londoner Fabrikant, „durch\n das ganze Land hat die Gewohnheit kurzer Ordres sehr gefördert. Käufer\n kommen jetzt von Glasgow, Manchester und Edinburg einmal in 14 Tagen\n oder so zu den City-Waarenhäusern für den Grossverkauf, denen wir die\n Waaren liefern. Sie geben Ordres, die unmittelbar ausgeführt werden\n müssen, statt vom Lager zu kaufen, wie es Gewohnheit war.","kind":"passage","sequence":180,"address":"15.180"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0181","text":"In früheren\n Jahren waren wir stets fähig während der schlaffen Zeit für die Nachfrage\n der nächsten Saison vorauszuarbeiten, aber jetzt kann Niemand vorher-\n sagen, was dann in Nachfrage sein wird“ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. IV. Rep.“, p. XXXII, XXXIII. „The\n extension of the railway system is said to have contributed greatly to this custom\n of giving sudden orders, and the consequent hurry, neglect of mealtimes, and late\n hours of the work people.“ (l. c.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":181,"address":"15.181"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0182","text":"In den noch nicht dem Fabrikgesetz unterworfenen Fabriken und\n Manufakturen herrscht periodisch die furchtbarste Ueberarbeit während der\n s. g. Saison, stossweis in Folge plötzlicher Ordres. Im auswärtigen De-\n partement der Fabrik, der Manufaktur und des Waarenmagazins, in der\n Sphäre der Hausarbeit, ohnehin durchaus unregelmässig, für ihr Rohma-\n terial und ihre Ordres ganz abhängig von den Launen des Kapitalisten,\n den hier keine Rücksicht auf Verwerthung von Baulichkeiten, Maschinen\n u. s. w. bindet und der hier nichts riskirt als die Haut der Arbeiter selbst,\n wird so systematisch eine stets disponible, industrielle Reservearmee gross-\n gezüchtet, decimirt während eines Theils des Jahrs durch unmenschlich-\n sten Arbeitszwang, während des andern Theils verlumpt durch Arbeits-\n mangel. „Die Anwender“, sagt die „Child. Empl.","kind":"passage","sequence":182,"address":"15.182"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0183","text":"Comm.“, „exploitiren\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"471\", \"facs\": \"#f0490\"} --> die gewohnheitsmässige Unregelmässigkeit der Hausarbeit, um sie in Zei-\n ten, wo Extrawerk nöthig, bis 11, 12, 2 Uhr Nachts, in der That, wie\n die stehende Phrase lautet, auf alle Stunden hinaufzuforciren, und\n diess in Lokalen, „wo der Gestank hinreicht, euch niederzuschmettern\n („the stench is enough to knock you down“). Ihr geht vielleicht bis an\n die Thüre und öffnet sie, aber schaudert zurück vor weiterem Vorgehn“ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. II. Rep.“, p. XXXV, n. 235 und 237.] .\n „Es sind komische Käuze, unsre Anwender“, sagt einer der verhörten\n Zeugen, ein Schuster, „sie glauben, es thue einem Jungen keinen Harm,\n wenn er während eines halben Jahrs todtgerackert und während der an-\n dern Hälfte fast gezwungen wird herumzuludern“ [Note: l. c. p. 127, n. 56.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":183,"address":"15.183"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0184","text":"Wie die technischen Hindernisse, so wurden und werden diese s. g.\n „Geschäftsgewohnheiten“ („usages which have grown with the\n growth of trade“) von interessirten Kapitalisten als „Naturschranken“\n der Produktion behauptet, ein Lieblingsschrei diess der Baumwolllords zur\n Zeit als das Fabrikgesetz sie zuerst bedrohte. Obgleich ihre Industrie mehr\n als jede andre auf dem Weltmarkt und daher der Schifffahrt beruht, strafte\n die Erfahrung sie Lügen. Seitdem wird jedes angebliche „Geschäftshin-\n derniss“ von den englischen Fabrikinspektoren als hohle Flause behandelt [Note: „With respect to the loss of trade by the non-completion of shipping\n orders in time, I remember that this was the pet argument of the factory masters\n in 1832 and 1833.","kind":"passage","sequence":184,"address":"15.184"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0185","text":"Nothing that can be advanced now on this subject could have\n the force that it had then, before steam had halved all distances and established\n new regulations for transit. It quite failed at that time of proof when put to the\n test, and again it will certainly fail should it have to be tried.“ („Reports of\n Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1862“, p. 54, 55.] .\n Die gründlich gewissenhaften Untersuchungen der „Child. Empl.\n Comm.“ beweisen in der That, dass in einigen Industrieen die bereits an-\n gewandte Arbeitsmasse nur gleichmässiger über das ganze Jahr vertheilt\n würde durch die Regulation des Arbeitstags [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. IV. Rep.“, p. XVIII, n.","kind":"passage","sequence":185,"address":"15.185"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0186","text":"118.] , dass letztere der erste\n rationelle Zügel für die menschenmörderischen, inhaltslosen und an sich\n dem System der grossen Industrie unangemessnen Flatterlaunen der\nMode [Note: John Bellers bemerkt schon 1699: „The uncertainty of fashions does in-\n crease necessitous Poor. It has two great mischiefs in it: 1st) The journeymen are mi-] , dass die Entwicklung der oceanischen Schifffahrt und der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"472\", \"facs\": \"#f0491\"} --> Kommunikationsmittel überhaupt den eigentlich technischen Grund der\n Saison-Arbeit aufgehoben hat [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. V. Rep.“, p. 171, n. 31.] , dass alle andern angeblich unkontro-\n lirbaren Umstände weggeräumt werden durch weitere Baulichkeiten, zu-\n sätzliche Maschinerie, vermehrte Anzahl der gleichzeitig beschäftigten Ar-\n beiter [Note: So heisst es z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":186,"address":"15.186"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0187","text":"in den Zeugenaussagen von Bradforder Exporthändlern:\n „Unter diesen Umständen ist es klar, dass Jungen nicht länger als von 8 Uhr\n Morgens bis 7 oder 7½ Uhr Abends in den Waarenhäusern beschäftigt zu werden\n brauchen. Es ist nur eine Frage von Extra-Auslage und Extra-Händen. Die\n Jungen brauchten nicht so spät in die Nacht hineinzuarbeiten, wären einige An-\n wender nicht so profithungrig; eine Extramaschine kostet nur 16 oder 18 Pfd. St.\n … Alle Schwierigkeiten entspringen aus ungenügenden Vorrichtungen und\n Raummangel.“ (l. c. p. 171, n. 35 und 38.)]  und von selbst folgenden Rückschlag auf das System des\n Grosshandels [Note: l. c.","kind":"passage","sequence":187,"address":"15.187"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0188","text":"Ein Londoner Fabrikant, der übrigens die zwangsweise Regulation\n des Arbeitstags als Schutzmittel der Arbeiter gegen die Fabrikanten und der\n Fabrikanten selbst gegen den Grosshandel betrachtet, sagt aus:\n „Der Druck in unsrem Geschäft ist verursacht durch die Verschiffer, die z. B.\n Waare mit einem Segelschiff verschicken wollen, um für eine bestimmte Saison\n an Ort und Stelle zu sein und zugleich die Frachtdifferenz zwischen Segelschiff\n und Dampfschiff einzustecken, oder von zwei Dampfschiffen das frühere wählen,\n um vor ihren Konkurrenten auf dem auswärtigen Markt zu erscheinen.“ (l. c.\n p. 8, n. 32.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":188,"address":"15.188"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0189","text":"Jedoch versteht sich das Kapital, wie es wiederholt\n durch den Mund seiner Repräsentanten erklärt, zu solcher Umwälzung\n „nur unter dem Druck eines allgemeinen Parlaments-\n akts“ [Note: „This could be obviated“, sagt ein Fabrikant, „at the expense of an\n enlargement of the works under the Pressure of a General Act of Par-\n liament.“ (l. c. p. X, n. 38.)] , der den Arbeitstag zwangsgesetzlich regulirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":189,"address":"15.189"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0190","text":"Die Fabrikgesetzgebung, diese erste bewusste und plan-\n [Note: serable in winter for want of work, the mercers and master - weavers not daring to\n lay out their stocks to keep the journeymen imployed before the spring comes and\n they know what the fashion will then be; 2dly) In the spring the journeymen are\n not sufficient, but the master-weavers must draw in many practices, that they may\n supply the trade of the kingdom in a quarter or half a year, which robs the plow\n of hands, drains the country of labourers, and in a great part stocks the city with\n beggars, and starves some in winter that are ashamed to beg.“ („Essays about\n the Poor, Manufactures etc.“, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":190,"address":"15.190"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0191","text":"9.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"473\", \"facs\": \"#f0492\"} -->mässige Rückwirkung der Gesellschaft auf die naturwüchsige Gestalt\n ihres Produktionsprozesses, ist, wie man gesehn, ebenso sehr ein nothwen-\n diges Produkt der grossen Industrie, als Baumwollgarn, Selfactors und der\n elektrische Telegraph. Bevor wir zu ihrer bevorstehenden Verallge-\n meinerung in England übergehn, sind noch einige nicht auf die Stun-\n denzahl des Arbeitstags bezügliche Klauseln des englischen Fabrikakts\n kurz zu erwähnen.","kind":"passage","sequence":191,"address":"15.191"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0192","text":"Abgesehn von ihrer Redaktion, welche dem Kapitalisten ihre Um-\n gehung erleichtert, sind die Gesundheitsklauseln äusserst mager,\n in der That beschränkt auf Vorschriften für Weisswaschung und einige\n sonstige Reinlichkeitsmassregeln, Ventilation, und Schutz gegen gefähr-\n liche Maschinerie. Wir kommen im dritten Buch auf den fanatischen\n Kampf der Fabrikanten gegen die Klausel zurück, die ihnen eine geringe\n Ausgabe zum Schutz der Gliedmassen ihrer „Hände“ aufoktroyirt. Hier\n bewährt sich wieder glänzend das Freihandelsdogma, dass in einer Gesell-\n schaft antagonistischer Interessen Jeder das Gemeinwohl durch Verfolgung\n seines Eigennutzes fördert. Ein Beispiel genügt. Man weiss, dass sich\n während der letztverflossenen zwanzigjährigen Periode die Flachsindu-\n strie und mit ihr die scutching mills (Fabriken zum Schlagen und\n Brechen des Flachses) in Irland sehr vermehrt haben.","kind":"passage","sequence":192,"address":"15.192"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0193","text":"Es gab dort 1864\n an 1800 dieser mills. Periodisch im Herbst und Winter werden haupt-\n sächlich junge Personen und Weiber, die Söhne, Töchter und Frauen der\n benachbarten kleinen Pächter, lauter mit Maschinerie ganz unbekannte\n Personen, von der Feldarbeit weggeholt, um die Walzwerke der scutch-\n ing mills mit Flachs zu füttern. Die Unfälle sind nach Umfang und\n Intensivität gänzlich beispiellos in der Geschichte der Maschinerie. Eine\n einzige scutching mill zu Kildinan (bei Cork) zählte von 1852 bis 1856\n sechs Todesfälle und 60 schwere Verstümmelungen, welchen allen durch die\n einfachsten Anstalten, zur Kost von wenigen Schillingen, vorgebeugt wer-\n den konnte. Dr. W. White, der certifying surgeon der Fabriken zu\n Downpatrick, erklärt in einem officiellen Bericht vom 15. December 1865:\n „Die Unfälle in scutching mills sind furchtbarster Art.","kind":"passage","sequence":193,"address":"15.193"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0194","text":"In vielen Fällen\n wird ein Viertheil des Körpers vom Rumpfe gerissen. Tod oder eine Zu-\n kunft elenden Unvermögens und Leidens sind gewöhnliche Folgen der\n Wunden. Die Zunahme der Fabriken in diesem Lande wird natürlich\n diese schauderhaften Resultate ausdehnen. Ich bin überzeugt, dass durch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"474\", \"facs\": \"#f0493\"} --> geeignete Staatsüberwachung der scutching mills grosse Opfer von\n Leib und Leben zu vermeiden sind“ [Note: l. c. p. XV, n. 72 sqq.] . Was könnte die kapitalistische\n Produktionsweise besser charakterisiren als die Nothwendigkeit, ihr durch\n Zwangsgesetz von Staatswegen die einfachsten Reinlichkeits- und Gesund-\n heitsvorrichtungen aufzuherrschen?","kind":"passage","sequence":194,"address":"15.194"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0195","text":"„Der Fabrikakt von 1864 hat in den\n Töpfereien über 200 Werkstätten geweisst und gereinigt, nach zwan-\n zigjähriger oder gänzlicher Enthaltung von jeder sol-\n chen Operation (diess ist die „Abstinenz“ des Kapitals!), in\n Plätzen, wo 27,800 Arbeiter beschäftigt sind, und bisher, während übermäs-\n siger Tages-, oft Nacht-Arbeit, eine mephitische Atmosphäre einathmeten,\n welche eine sonst vergleichungsweis harmlose Beschäftigung mit Krank-\n heit und Tod schwängerte. Der Akt hat die Ventilationsmittel sehr ver-\n mehrt“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1865“, p. 96.] . Zugleich zeigt dieser Zweig des Fabrikakts schlagend, wie\n die kapitalistische Produktionsweise ihrem Wesen nach über einen gewissen\n Punkt hinaus jede rationelle Verbesserung ausschliesst.","kind":"passage","sequence":195,"address":"15.195"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0196","text":"Es ward wiederholt\n bemerkt, dass die englischen Aerzte aus einem Munde 500 Kubikfuss Luft-\n raum per Person für kaum genügen des Minimum bei fortgesetzter Arbeit erklä-\n ren. Nun wohl! Wenn der Fabrikakt indirekt durch alle seine Zwangsmass-\n regeln die Verwandlung kleinerer Werkstätten in Fabriken beschleunigt,\n daher indirekt in das Eigenthumsrecht der kleineren Kapitalisten eingreift\n und den grossen das Monopol sichert, so würde die gesetzliche Aufherr-\n schung des nöthigen Luftraums für jeden Arbeiter in der Werkstätte Tau-\n sende von kleinen Kapitalisten mit einem Schlag direkt expropriiren! Sie\n würde die Wurzel der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise angreifen, d. h. die\n Selbstverwerthung des Kapitals, ob gross oder klein, durch „freien“ An-\n kauf und Konsum der Arbeitskraft. Vor diesen 500 Kubikfuss Luft geht\n daher der Fabrikgesetzgebung der Athem aus.","kind":"passage","sequence":196,"address":"15.196"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0197","text":"Der „Board of Health“,\n die industriellen Untersuchungskommissionen, die Fabrikinspektoren wie-\n derholen wieder und wieder die Nothwendigkeit der 500 Kubikfüsse und\n die Unmöglichkeit, sie dem Kapital aufzuoktroyiren. Sie erklären so in\n der That Schwindsucht und andre Lungenkrankheiten der Arbeit für eine\n Lebensbedingung des Kapitals [Note: Man hat erfahrungsmässig gefunden, dass ungefähr 25 Kubikzoll Luft\n bei jeder Athmung mittlerer Intensivität von einem gesunden Durchschnittsindi-] .","kind":"passage","sequence":197,"address":"15.197"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0198","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"475\", \"facs\": \"#f0494\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":198,"address":"15.198"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0199","text":"Armselig wie die Erziehungsklauseln des Fabrikakts im Ganzen\n erscheinen, proklamirten sie den Elementarunterricht als Zwangs-\n bedingung der Arbeit [Note: Nach dem englischen Fabrikakt können die Eltern Kinder unter 14\n Jahren nicht in die „kontrolirten“ Fabriken schicken, ohne ihnen zugleich Ele-\n mentarunterricht ertheilen zu lassen. Der Fabrikant ist verantwortlich für die Be-\n folgung des Gesetzes. „Factory education is compulsory, and it is a condi-\n tion of labour.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1865“, p. 111.)] . Ihr Erfolg bewies zuerst die Möglichkeit\n der Verbindung von Unterricht und Gymnastik [Note: Ueber die vortheilhaften Erfolge der Verbindung von Gymnastik (für\n Jungen auch militärischer Exercitien) mit Zwangsunterricht der Fabrikkinder und\n Armenschüler sieh die Rede von N. W. Senior im 7.","kind":"passage","sequence":199,"address":"15.199"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0200","text":"jährlichen Kongress der\n „National Association for the Promotion of Social Science“ in\n „Report of Proceedings etc. Lond. 1863“, p. 63, 64, ebenso den Bericht\n der Fabrikinspektoren für 31. Oct. 1865, p. 118, 119, 120, 126 sqq.]  mit Handarbeit, also\n auch von Handarbeit mit Unterricht und Gymnastik. Die Fabrikinspektoren\n entdeckten bald aus den Zeugenverhören der Schulmeister, dass die Fa-\n brikkinder, obgleich sie nur halb so viel Unterricht geniessen als die\n regelmässigen Tagesschüler, eben so viel und oft mehr lernen. „Die\n Sache ist einfach. Diejenigen, die sich nur einen halben Tag in der\n Schule aufhalten, sind stets frisch und fast immer fähig und willig Unter-\n richt zu empfangen.","kind":"passage","sequence":200,"address":"15.200"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0201","text":"Das System halber Arbeit und halber\n Schule macht jede der beiden Beschäftigungen zur Ausruhung und\n Erholung von der andern und folglich viel angemessner für das Kind als\n die ununterbrochne Fortdauer einer von beiden. Ein Junge, der von Mor-\n gens früh in der Schule sitzt, und nun gar bei heissem Wetter, kann un-\n möglich mit einem andern wetteifern, der munter und aufgeweckt von\n seiner Arbeit kommt“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact.“ l. c. p. 118. Ein naiver Seiden-\n fabrikant erklärt den Untersuchungskommissären der „Child. Empl. Comm.“:] . Weitere Belege findet man in Senior’s\n [Note: viduum konsumirt wird, und ungefähr 20 Athmungen per Minute vorgehn. Der\n Luftkonsum eines Individuums in 24 Stunden ergäbe danach ungefähr 720,000\n Kubikzoll oder 416 Kubikfüsse.","kind":"passage","sequence":201,"address":"15.201"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0202","text":"Man weiss aber, dass die einmal eingeathmete\n Luft nicht mehr zu demselben Prozess dienen kann, bevor sie in der grossen Werk-\n stätte der Natur gereinigt wird. Nach den Experimenten von Valentin und Brunner\n scheint ein gesunder Mann ungefähr 1300 Kubikzoll Kohlensäure per Stunde aus-\n zuathmen; diess ergäbe ungefähr 8 Unzen solider Kohle, von der Lunge in 24\n Stunden abgeworfen. „Jeder Mann sollte wenigstens 800 Kubikfuss haben.“\n (Huxley)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"476\", \"facs\": \"#f0495\"} --> Rede auf dem sociologischen Kongress zu Edinburg, 1863. Er zeigt hier\n auch u. a. nach, wie der einseitige, unproduktive und verlängerte Schul-\n tag der Kinder der höheren und mittleren Klassen die Arbeit der Lehrer\n nutzlos vermehrt, „während er Zeit, Gesundheit und Energie der Kinder\n nicht nur fruchtlos, sondern absolut schädlich verwüstet“ [Note: Senior l. c. p. 66.","kind":"passage","sequence":202,"address":"15.202"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0203","text":"Wie die grosse Industrie, auf einem gewissen Höhe-\n grad, durch die Umwälzung der materiellen Produktionsweise und der gesellschaft-\n lichen Produktionsverhältnisse auch die Köpfe umwälzt, zeigt schlagend ein Ver-\n gleich zwischen der Rede des N. W. Senior von 1863 und seiner Philippika gegen\n das Fabrikgesetz von 1833, oder ein Vergleich der Ansichten des erwähnten Kon-\n gresses mit der Thatsache, dass es in gewissen ländlichen Theilen Englands armen\n Eltern immer noch bei Strafe des Hungertods verboten ist, ihre Kinder zu\n erziehen. So z. B. berichtet Herr Snell als gewöhnliche Praxis in Sommersetshire,\n dass wenn eine arme Person Pfarreihilfe anspricht, sie gezwungen wird, ihre Kinder\n aus der Schule zu nehmen. So erzählt Herr Wollaston, Pfarrer zu Feltham, von\n Fällen, wo alle Unterstützung gewissen Familien versagt wurde, „weil sie ihre\n Jungen zur Schule schickten“!] .","kind":"passage","sequence":203,"address":"15.203"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0204","text":"Aus dem\nFabriksystem, wie man im Detail bei Robert Owen verfolgen\n kann, entspross der Keim der Erziehung der Zukunft, welche für\nalle Kinder über einem gewissen Alter produktive Arbeit mit\nUnterricht und Gymnastik verbinden wird, nicht nur als eine Me-\n thode zur Steigerung der gesellschaftlichen Produktion, sondern als die\n einzige Methode zur Produktion vollseitig entwickelter Menschen.","kind":"passage","sequence":204,"address":"15.204"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0205","text":"Man hat gesehn, dass die grosse Industrie die manufakturmässige Thei-\n lung der Arbeit mit ihrer lebenslänglichen Annexation eines ganzen Menschen\n an eine Detailoperation technologisch aufhebt, während zugleich die kapi-\n talistische Form der grossen Industrie jene Arbeitstheilung\n noch monströser reproducirt, in der eigentlichen Fabrik durch Verwand-\n lung des Arbeiters in den selbstbewussten Zubehör einer Theilmaschine,\n überall sonst theils durch sporadischen Gebrauch der Maschinen und Ma-\n [Note: „Ich bin durchaus überzeugt, dass das wahre Geheimniss der Produktion tüchtiger\n Arbeiter gefunden ist in der Vereinigung der Arbeit mit Unterricht von der Periode\n der Kindheit an. Natürlich muss die Arbeit weder zu anstrengend, noch widerlich\n und ungesund sein. Ich wünschte, meine eignen Kinder hätten Arbeit und Spiel\n zur Abwechslung von der Schule.“ („Child. Empl. Comm.“ V. Rep.,\n p.","kind":"passage","sequence":205,"address":"15.205"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0206","text":"82, n. 36.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"477\", \"facs\": \"#f0496\"} --> schinenarbeit [Note: Wo handwerksmässige Maschinen, durch Menschenkraft getrieben, direkt\n oder indirekt mit entwickelter und daher mechanische Triebkraft voraussetzender\n Maschinerie konkurriren, geht eine grosse Umwandlung vor mit Bezug auf den\n Arbeiter, der die Maschine treibt. Ursprünglich ersetzte die Dampfmaschine diesen\n Arbeiter, jetzt soll er die Dampfmaschine ersetzen. Die Spannung und Veraus-\n gabung seiner Arbeitskraft wird daher monströs, und nun gar für Unerwachsne,\n die zu dieser Tortur verurtheilt sind! So fand der Kommissär Longe in Coventry\n und Umgebung Jungen von 10 bis 15 Jahren zum Drehn der Bandstühle verwandt,\n abgesehn von jüngeren Kindern, die Stühle von kleinerer Dimension zu drehn\n hatten. „Es ist ausserordentlich mühsame Arbeit. The boy is a mere sub-\n stitute for steam-power.“ („Child.","kind":"passage","sequence":206,"address":"15.206"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0207","text":"Empl. Comm. V. Rep. 1866“,\n p. 114, n. 6.) Ueber die mörderischen Folgen „dieses Systems der Skla-\n verei“, wie der officielle Bericht es nennt, l. c. sq.] , theils durch Einführung von Weiber-, Kinder- und un-\n geschickter Arbeit als neuer Grundlage der Arbeitstheilung. Der Wider-\n spruch zwischen der manufakturmässigen Theilung der Arbeit und dem\n Wesen der grossen Industrie macht sich gewaltsam geltend. Er er-\n scheint u. a. in der furchtbaren Thatsache, dass ein grosser Theil der in\n den modernen Fabriken und Manufakturen beschäftigten Kinder, vom zar-\n testen Alter festgeschmiedet an die einfachsten Manipulationen, Jahrelang\n exploitirt wird, ohne Erlernung irgend einer Arbeit, die sie später auch\n nur in derselben Manufaktur oder Fabrik brauchbar machte. In den\n englischen Buchdruckereien z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":207,"address":"15.207"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0208","text":"fand früher ein dem System der alten\n Manufaktur und des Handwerks entsprechender Uebergang der Lehrlinge\n von leichteren zu inhaltsvolleren Arbeiten statt. Sie machten einen Lern-\n gang durch, bis sie fertige Drucker waren. Lesen und schreiben zu\n können war für alle ein Handwerkserforderniss. Alles das änderte sich\n mit der Druckmaschine. Sie verwendet zwei Sorten von Arbeitern, einen\n erwachsnen Arbeiter, den Maschinenaufseher, und Maschinenjungen, meist\n von 11 bis 17 Jahren, deren Geschäft ausschliesslich darin besteht, einen\n Bogen Papier der Maschine zu unterbreiten oder ihr den gedruckten Bogen\n zu entziehen. Sie verrichten, in London namentlich, diese Plackerei\n 14, 15, 16 Stunden ununterbrochen während einiger Tage in der Woche\n und oft 36 Stunden nach einander mit nur zwei Stunden Rast für Mahlzeit\n und Schlaf [Note: l. c. p. 3, n. 24.] !","kind":"passage","sequence":208,"address":"15.208"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0209","text":"Ein grosser Theil von ihnen kann nicht lesen und sie\n sind in der Regel ganz verwilderte, abnorme Geschöpfe. „Um sie zu ihrem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"478\", \"facs\": \"#f0497\"} --> Werk zu befähigen, ist keine intellektuelle Ziehung irgend einer Art nöthig;\n sie haben wenig Gelegenheit für Geschick und noch weniger für Urtheil;\n ihr Lohn, obgleich gewissermassen hoch für Jungen, wächst nicht verhält-\n mässig, wie sie selbst heranwachsen und die grosse Mehrzahl hat keine\n Aussicht auf den einträglicheren und verantwortlicheren Posten des Ma-\n schinenaufsehers, weil auf jede Maschine nur ein Aufseher und oft 4 Jungen\n kommen“ [Note: l. c. p. 7, n. 59, 60.] . Sobald sie zu alt für ihre kindische Arbeit werden, also\n wenigstens im 17. Jahr, entlässt man sie aus der Druckerei. Sie werden\n zu Rekruten des Verbrechens.","kind":"passage","sequence":209,"address":"15.209"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0210","text":"Einige Versuche ihnen anderswo Beschäf-\n tigung zu verschaffen, scheiterten an ihrer Unwissenheit, Rohheit, körper-\n lichen und geistigen Verkommenheit.","kind":"passage","sequence":210,"address":"15.210"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0211","text":"Was von der manufakturmässigen Theilung der Arbeit im\n Innern der Werkstatt, gilt von der Theilung der Arbeit im Innern der\n Gesellschaft. So lange Handwerk und Manufaktur die allgemeine Grund-\n lage der gesellschaftlichen Produktion bilden, ist die Subsumtion des Produ-\n centen unter einen ausschliesslichen Produktionszweig, die Zerreissung der\n ursprünglichen Mannigfaltigkeit seiner Beschäftigungen [Note: „In einigen Theilen von Hochschottland … erschienen viele Schaf-\n hirten und cotters mit Frau und Kind, nach dem Statistical Account, in\n Schuhen, die sie selbst gemacht aus Leder, das sie selbst gegerbt, in Kleidern, die\n keine Hand ausser ihrer eignen angetastet, deren Material sie selbst von den\n Schafen geschoren oder wofür sie den Flachs selbst gebaut hatten.","kind":"passage","sequence":211,"address":"15.211"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0212","text":"In die Zu-\n bereitung der Kleider ging kaum irgend ein gekaufter Artikel ein, mit Ausnahme\n von Pfrieme, Nadel, Fingerhut und sehr wenigen Theilen des im Weben ange-\n wandten Eisenwerks. Die Farben wurden von den Weibern selbst von Bäumen,\n Gesträuchen und Kräutern gewonnen u. s. w.“ (Dugald Stewart l. c.\n p. 327.)] , ein nothwendiges\n Entwicklungsmoment. Auf jener Grundlage findet jeder besondre Produk-\n tionszweig empirisch die ihm entsprechende technologische Gestalt, vervoll-\n kommnet sie langsam und krystallisirt sie rasch, sobald ein gewisser Reifegrad\n erlangt ist. Was hier und da Wechsel hervorruft, ist ausser neuem Arbeits-\n stoff, den der Handel liefert, die allmälige Aenderung des Arbeitsinstru-\n ments.","kind":"passage","sequence":212,"address":"15.212"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0213","text":"Die erfahrungsmässig entsprechende Form einmal gewonnen, ver-\n knöchert auch es, wie sein oft jahrtausendlanger Uebergang aus der Hand einer\n Generation in die der andern beweist. Es ist charakteristisch, dass bis ins\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"479\", \"facs\": \"#f0498\"} --> 18. Jahrhundert hinein die besondern Gewerke mysteries (mystères) [Note: In dem berühmten „Livre des métiers“ des Etienne Boileau\n wird unter andrem vorgeschrieben, dass ein Geselle bei seiner Aufnahme unter die\n Meister einen Eid leiste, „seine Brüder brüderlich zu lieben, sie zu stützen, jeder\n in seinem métier, d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":213,"address":"15.213"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0214","text":"nicht freiwillig die Gewerksgeheimnisse zu\n verrathen, und sogar im Interesse der Gesammtheit nicht zur Empfehlung seiner\n eignen Waare den Käufer auf die Fehler des Machwerks von Andern aufmerk-\n sam zu machen.“] \n hiessen, in deren Dunkel nur der empirisch und professionell Eingeweihte\n eindringen konnte. Die grosse Industrie zerriss den Schleier, der den\n Menschen ihren eignen gesellschaftlichen Produktionsprocess versteckte\n und die verschiednen naturwüchsig besonderten Produktionszweige gegen\n einander und sogar dem in jedem Zweig Eingeweihten zu Räthseln machte.\n Ihr Princip, jeden Produktionsprozess an und für sich, und zunächst ohne alle\n Rücksicht auf die menschliche Hand, in seine constituirenden Elemente auf-\n zulösen, schuf die ganz moderne Wissenschaft der Technologie.","kind":"passage","sequence":214,"address":"15.214"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0215","text":"Die bunt-\n scheckigen, scheinbar zusammenhangslosen und verknöcherten Gestalten des\n gesellschaftlichen Produktionsprozesses lösten sich auf in bewusst plan-\n mässige und je nach dem bezweckten Nutzeffekt systematisch besonderte\n Anwendungen der Naturwissenschaft. Die Technologie entdeckte ebenso\n die wenigen grossen Grundformen der Bewegung, worin alles pro-\n duktive Thun des menschlichen Körpers, trotz aller Mannigfaltigkeit der an-\n gewandten Instrumente, nothwendig vorgeht, ganz so wie die Mechanik\n durch die grösste Komplikation der Maschinerie sich über die beständige\n Wiederholung der einfachen mechanischen Potenzen nicht täuschen lässt.\n Die moderne Industrie betrachtet und behandelt die vorhandne Form eines\n Produktionsprozesses nie als definitiv.","kind":"passage","sequence":215,"address":"15.215"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0216","text":"Ihre technologische Basis ist daher\n revolutionär, während die aller früheren Produktionsweisen wesentlich kon-\n servativ war [Note: „Die Bourgeoisie kann nicht existiren ohne die Produktionsinstrumente,\n also die Produktionsverhältnisse, also sämmtliche gesellschaftlichen Verhältnisse\n fortwährend zu revolutioniren. Unveränderte Beibehaltung der alten Produktions-\n weise war dagegen die erste Existenzbedingung aller früheren industriellen Klassen.\n Die fortwährende Umwälzung der Produktion, die ununterbrochene Erschütterung\n aller gesellschaftlichen Zustände, die ewige Unsicherheit und Bewegung zeichnen\n die Bourgeoisepoche vor allen früheren aus. Alle festen, eingerosteten Verhält-\n nisse mit ihrem Gefolge von altehrwürdigen Vorstellungen und Anschauungen] .","kind":"passage","sequence":216,"address":"15.216"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0217","text":"Durch Maschinen, chemische Prozesse und andre Me-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"480\", \"facs\": \"#f0499\"} --> thoden wälzt sie beständig mit der technischen Grundlage der materiellen\n Produktion die Funktionen der Arbeiter und die gesellschaftlichen Kombi-\n nationen des Arbeitsprozesses um. Sie revolutionirt damit ebenso bestän-\n dig die Theilung der Arbeit im Innern der Gesellschaft und schleudert un-\n aufhörlich Kapitalmassen und Arbeitermassen aus einem Produktionszweig\n in den andern. Die Natur der grossen Industrie bedingt daher Wechsel\n der Arbeit, Fluss der Funktion, allseitige Beweglichkeit des Arbeiters.\n Andrerseits reproducirt sie in ihrer kapitalistischen Form die\n alte Theilung der Arbeit mit ihren knöchernen Partikularitäten.","kind":"passage","sequence":217,"address":"15.217"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0218","text":"Man hat\n gesehn, wie dieser absolute Widerspruch alle Ruhe, Festigkeit, Sicher-\n heit der Lebenslage des Arbeiters aufhebt, ihm mit dem Arbeitsmittel bestän-\n dig das Lebensmittel aus der Hand zu schlagen [Note: „You take my life\n When you do take the means whereby I live.“ (Shakespeare.)]  und mit seiner Theil-\n funktion ihn selbst überflüssig zu machen droht, wie dieser Widerspruch\n im ununterbrochnen Opferfest der Arbeiterklasse, masslosester Vergeudung\n der Arbeitskräfte und den Verheerungen gesellschaftlicher Anarchie sich\n austobt. Diess ist die negative Seite.","kind":"passage","sequence":218,"address":"15.218"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0219","text":"Wenn aber der Wechsel der Arbeit\n sich jetzt nur als überwältigendes Naturgesetz und mit der blind zerstö-\n renden Wirkung eines Naturgesetzes durchsetzt, das überall auf Hinder-\n nisse stösst [Note: Ein französischer Arbeiter schreibt bei seiner Rückkehr von San-Fran-\n cisco: „Ich hätte nie geglaubt, dass ich fähig wäre alle die Gewerbe auszuüben,\n die ich in Californien betrieben habe. Ich war fest überzeugt, dass ich ausser zur\n Buchdruckerei zu nichts gut sei . . . . Einmal in der Mitte dieser Welt von Aben-\n teurern, welche ihr Handwerk leichter wechseln als ihr Hemde, meiner Treu!\n ich that wie die andern. Da das Geschäft der Minenarbeit sich nicht einträglich\n genug auswies, verliess ich es und zog in die Stadt, wo ich der Reihe nach Typo-\n graph, Dachdecker, Bleigiesser u. s. w. wurde.","kind":"passage","sequence":219,"address":"15.219"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0220","text":"In Folge dieser Erfahrung, zu\n allen Arbeiten tauglich zu sein, fühle ich mich weniger als Molluske und mehr\n als Mensch.“ (A. Corbon: „De l’enseignement professionnel.“\n 2ème éd. p. 50.)] , macht die grosse Industrie durch ihre Katastrophen selbst\n [Note: werden aufgelöst, alle neugebildeten veralten, ehe sie verknöchern können. Alles\n Ständische und Stehende verdampft, alles Heilige wird entweiht, und die Menschen\n sind endlich gezwungen, ihre Lebensstellungen, ihre gegenseitigen Beziehungen\n mit nüchternen Augen anzusehn.“ (F. Engels und Karl Marx: „Mani-\n fest der Kommunistischen Partei. Lond. 1848“, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":220,"address":"15.220"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0221","text":"5.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"481\", \"facs\": \"#f0500\"} --> es zur Frage von Leben oder Tod, den Wechsel der Arbeiten und daher\n möglichste Vielseitigkeit des Arbeiters als allgemeines gesellschaftliches\n Gesetz der Produktion anzuerkennen, und die Verhältnisse seiner normalen\n Verwirklichung gemäss umzugestalten. Sie macht es zu einer Frage von\n Leben oder Tod, die Ungeheuerlichkeit einer elenden, für die wechselnden\n Exploitationsbedürfnisse des Kapitals in Reserve gehaltenen, disponiblen\n Arbeiterbevölkerung zu ersetzen durch die absolute Disponibilität des Men-\n schen für wechselnde Arbeitserfordernisse; das Theilindividuum, welches\n blosser Träger einer gesellschaftlichen Detailfunktion ist, durch das\n total entwickelte Individuum, für welches die gesellschaftlichen Funktionen\n eben so viele verschiedne Bethätigungsweisen sind.","kind":"passage","sequence":221,"address":"15.221"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0222","text":"Ein auf Grundlage\n der grossen Industrie naturwüchsig entwickeltes Moment dieses Umwäl-\n zungsprozesses sind polytechnische und agronomische Schulen, ein anderes\n sind die „écoles d’enseignement professionnel“, worin die\n Kinder der Arbeiter einigen Unterricht in der Technologie und praktischen\n Handhabe der verschiednen Produktionsinstrumente erhalten. Wenn die\n Fabrikgesetzgebung als erste, dem Kapital nothdürftig abgerungene Kon-\n cession nur Elementarunterricht mit fabrikmässiger Arbeit verbindet, unter-\n liegt es keinem Zweifel, dass die unvermeidliche Eroberung der politischen\n Gewalt durch die Arbeiterklasse auch dem technologischen Unterricht,\n theoretisch und praktisch, seinen Platz in den Arbeiterschulen erobern\n wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":222,"address":"15.222"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0223","text":"Es unterliegt ebenso wenig einem Zweifel, dass die kapitalisti-\n sche Form der Produktion und die ihr entsprechenden ökonomischen\n Arbeiterverhältnisse im diametralsten Widerspruch stehn mit solchen Um-\n wälzungsfermenten und ihrem Ziel, der Aufhebung der alten\n Theilung der Arbeit. Die Entwicklung der Widersprüche einer\n geschichtlichen Produktionsform ist jedoch der einzig geschichtliche Weg\n ihrer Auflösung und Neugestaltung. „Ne sutor ultra crepidam“!, diess\n nec plus ultra hand werksmässiger Weisheit, wurde zur furchtbaren Narr-\n heit von dem Moment, wo der Uhrmacher Watt die Dampfmaschine, der\n Barbier Arkwright den Kettenstuhl, der Juwelierarbeiter Fulton das\n Dampfschiff erfunden hatte [Note: John Bellers, ein wahres Phänomen in der Geschichte der politischen\n Oekonomie, begriff schon Ende des 17.","kind":"passage","sequence":223,"address":"15.223"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0224","text":"Jahrhunderts mit vollster Klarheit die\n nothwendige Aufhebung der jetzigen Erziehung und Arbeitstheilung, welche\n Hypertrophie und Atrophie auf beiden Extremen der Gesellschaft, wenn auch in] .","kind":"passage","sequence":224,"address":"15.224"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0225","text":"[Running matter: I. 31] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"482\", \"facs\": \"#f0501\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":225,"address":"15.225"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0226","text":"Soweit die Fabrikgesetzgebung die Arbeit in Fabriken, Manufakturen\n u. s. w. regulirt, erscheint diess zunächst nur als Einmischung in die Ex-\n ploitationsrechte des Kapitals. Jede Regulation der s. g. Hausar-\n beit [Note: Diese geht übrigens grossentheils auch in kleineren Werkstätten\n vor, wie wir gesehn bei der Spitzenmanufaktur und Strohflechterei, und wie nament-\n lich auch an den Metallmanufakturen in Sheffield, Birmingham u. s. w. ausführ-\n licher gezeigt werden könnte.]  stellt sich dagegen sofort als direkter Eingriff in die patria\n Potestas dar, d. h. modern interpretirt, in die elterliche Auto-\n rität, ein Schritt, wovor das zartfühlende englische Parlament lang zu-\n rückzubeben affektirte.","kind":"passage","sequence":226,"address":"15.226"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0227","text":"Die Gewalt der Thatsachen zwang jedoch endlich\n anzuerkennen, dass die grosse Industrie mit der ökonomischen Grundlage\n des alten Familienwesens und der ihr entsprechenden Familienarbeit auch\n die alten Familienverhältnisse selbst auflöst. Das Recht der Kinder\n musste proklamirt werden. „Unglücklicher Weise“, heisst es im Schluss-\n bericht der „Child. Empl.","kind":"passage","sequence":227,"address":"15.227"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0228","text":"Comm.“ von 1866, „leuchtet aus der Gesammt-\n heit der Zeugenaussagen hervor, dass die Kinder beiderlei Geschlechts gegen\n Niemand so sehr des Schutzes bedürfen als gegen ihre Eltern.“ Das System\n der masslosen Exploitation der Kinderarbeit überhaupt und der Hausarbeit\n im Besondern wird dadurch „erhalten, dass die Eltern über ihre jungen\n und zarten Sprösslinge eine willkührliche und heillose Gewalt ohne Zügel\n oder Kontrole ausüben … Eltern dürfen nicht die absolute Macht be-\n sitzen, ihre Kinder zu reinen Maschinen zu machen, um so und so viel\n wöchentlichen Lohn herauszuschlagen … Kinder und junge Personen\n haben ein Recht auf den Schutz der Legislatur wider den Missbrauch der\n elterlichen Gewalt, der ihre physische Kraft vorzeitig bricht und sie degra-\n dirt auf der Staffel moralischer und intellektueller Wesen“ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. V. Rep.“, p. XXV, n. 162 und II. Rep.,\n p.","kind":"passage","sequence":228,"address":"15.228"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0229","text":"XXXVIII, n. 285, 289, p. XXXV, n. 191.] . Es ist\n [Note: entgegengesetzter Richtung, erzeugen. Er sagt u. a. schön: „An idle learning\n being little better than the Learning of Idleness … Bodily Labour, it’s a\n primitive institution of God . . . . Labour being as proper for the bodies health,\n as eating is for its living; for what pains a man saves by Ease, he will find in\n Disease … Labour adds oyl to the lamp of life when thinking inflames it …\n A childish silly employ (diess ahnungsvoll gegen die Basedows und ihre mo-\n dernen Nachstümper), leaves the children’s minds silly.“ („Proposals for\n raising a Colledge of Industry of all useful Trades and Husban-\n dry. Lond. 1696“, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":229,"address":"15.229"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0230","text":"12, 14, 18.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"483\", \"facs\": \"#f0502\"} --> jedoch nicht der Missbrauch der elterlichen Gewalt, der die direkte oder\n indirekte Exploitation unreifer Arbeitskräfte durch das Kapital schuf, son-\n dern es ist umgekehrt die kapitalistische Exploitationsweise, welche die\n elterliche Gewalt, durch Aufhebung der ihr entsprechenden ökonomischen\n Grundlage, zu einem Missbrauch gemacht hat. So furchtbar und ekelhaft\n nun die Auflösung des alten Familienwesens innerhalb des kapitalistischen\n Systems erscheint, so schafft nichtsdestoweniger die grosse Industrie mit\n der entscheidenden Rolle, die sie den Weibern, jungen Personen und Kin-\n dern beiderlei Geschlechts in gesellschaftlich organisirten Produktionspro-\n zessen jenseits der Sphäre des Hauswesens zuweist, die neue ökonomische\n Grundlage für eine höhere Form der Familie und des Verhältnisses beider\n Geschlechter.","kind":"passage","sequence":230,"address":"15.230"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0231","text":"Es ist natürlich ebenso albern die christlich germanische\n Form der Familie für absolut zu halten als die altrömische Form, oder die\n altgriechische, oder die orientalische, die übrigens untereinander eine ge-\n schichtliche Entwicklungsreihe bilden. Ebenso leuchtet ein, dass die Zu-\n sammensetzung des kombinirten Arbeitspersonals aus Individuen beiderlei\n Geschlechts und der verschiedensten Altersstufen, obgleich in ihrer naturwüch-\n sig brutalen, kapitalistischen Form, wo der Arbeiter für den Produktions-\n prozess, nicht der Produktionsprozess für den Arbeiter da ist, Pestquelle\n des Verderbs und der Sklaverei, unter entsprechenden Verhältnissen um-\n gekehrt zur Quelle humaner Entwicklung umschlagen muss [Note: „Factory labour may be as pure and as excellent as domestic labour,\n and perhaps more so.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1865“,\n p. 127.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":231,"address":"15.231"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0232","text":"Die Nothwendigkeit, das Fabrikgesetz aus einem Ausnahmsgesetz\n für Spinnereien und Webereien, diese ersten Gebilde des Maschinenbetriebs,\n in ein Gesetz aller gesellschaftlichen Produktion zu verallgemeinern, ent-\n springt, wie man sah, aus dem geschichtlichen Entwicklungsgang der\n grossen Industrie, auf deren Hintergrund die überlieferte Gestalt von Manu-\n faktur, Handwerk und Hausarbeit gänzlich umgewälzt wird, die Manufaktur\n beständig in die Fabrik, das Handwerk beständig in die Manufaktur um-\n schlägt, und endlich die Sphären des Handwerks und der Hausarbeit sich\n in relativ wunderbar kurzer Zeit zu Jammerhöhlen gestalten, wo die tollsten\n Ungeheuerlichkeiten der kapitalistischen Exploitation ihr freies Spiel trei-\n ben.","kind":"passage","sequence":232,"address":"15.232"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0233","text":"Es sind zwei Umstände, welche zuletzt den Ausschlag geben, erstens\n die stets neu wiederholte Erfahrung, dass das Kapital, sobald es der\n [Running matter: 31*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"484\", \"facs\": \"#f0503\"} --> Staatskontrole nur auf einzelnen Punkten der gesellschaftlichen Peripherie\n anheimfällt, sich um so massloser auf den andern Punkten entschädigt [Note: l. c. p. 27, 32.] ,\n zweitens der Schrei der Kapitalisten selbst nach Gleichheit der Kon-\n kurrenzbedingungen, d. h. gleichen Schranken der Ar-\n beitsexploitation [Note: Massenhafte Belege dazu in den „Rep. of Insp. of Fact.“] . Hören wir hierüber zwei Herzensstösse. Die\n Herrn W. Cooksley (Nagel-, Ketten- u. s. w. Fabrikanten zu Bristol) führ-\n ten die Fabrikregulation freiwillig in ihrem Geschäft ein.","kind":"passage","sequence":233,"address":"15.233"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0234","text":"„Da das alte,\n unregelmässige System in den benachbarten Werken fortdauert, sind sie\n der Unbill ausgesetzt ihre Arbeitsjungen zur Fortsetzung der Arbeit an-\n derswo nach 6 Uhr Abends verlockt (enticed) zu sehn. ‚Diess‘, sagen\n sie natürlich, ‚ist eine Ungerechtigkeit gegen uns und ein Verlust, da es\n einen Theil der Kraft der Jungen erschöpft, deren voller\n Vortheil uns gebührt‘ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. V. Rep.“, p. IX, n. 35.] . Herr J. Simpson (Paper-Box Bag maker,\n London) erklärt den Kommissären der „Child. Empl. Comm.“: „Er wolle\n jede Petition für Einführung der Fabrikakte unterzeichnen. Wie es sei,\nfühle er sich stets rastlos des Nachts („he always felt restless at\n night“), nach Schluss seiner Werkstatt, bei dem Gedanken, dass andre\n länger arbeiten liessen und ihm Aufträge vor der Nase wegschnappten“ [Note: l. c. n. 28.] .\n „Es wäre ein Unrecht,“ sagt die Child. Empl. Comm.","kind":"passage","sequence":234,"address":"15.234"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0235","text":"zusammenfassend,\n „gegen die grösseren Arbeitsanwender ihre Fabriken der Regulation zu unter-\n werfen, während in ihrem eignen Geschäftszweig der Kleinbetrieb keiner\n gesetzlichen Beschränkung der Arbeitszeit unterliegt. Zur Ungerechtigkeit\n ungleicher Konkurrenzbedingungen in Bezug auf die Arbeitsstunden bei\n Ausnahme kleinerer Werkstätten, käme noch der andere Nachtheil für die\n grösseren Fabrikanten hinzu, dass ihre Zufuhr von jugendlicher und weib-\n licher Arbeit abgelenkt würde nach den vom Gesetz verschonten Werk-\n stätten. Endlich gäbe diess Anstoss zur Vermehrung der kleineren Werk-\n stätten, die fast ausnahmslos die mindest günstigen für Gesundheit, Kom-\n fort, Erziehung und allgemeine Verbesserung des Volks sind“ [Note: l. c. n. 165—167. Vgl. über die Vorzüge des Grossbetriebes verglichen\n mit dem Zwergbetrieb „Child. Empl. Comm. III. Rep.“, p. 13, n. 144,\n p. 25, n. 121, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":235,"address":"15.235"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0236","text":"26, n. 125, p. 27, n. 140 u. s. w.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":236,"address":"15.236"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0237","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"485\", \"facs\": \"#f0504\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":237,"address":"15.237"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0238","text":"In ihrem Schlussbericht schlägt die „Children’s Employment“ Kommis-\n sion vor, über 1,400,000 Kinder, junge Personen und Weiber, wovon unge-\n fähr die Hälfte vom Kleinbetrieb und der Hausarbeit exploitirt wird, dem\n Fabrikakt zu unterwerfen [Note: Die zu massregelnden Industriezweige sind: Spitzenmanufaktur, Strumpf-\n wirkerei, Strohflechten, Manufaktur von Wearing Apparel mit ihren zahlreichen\n Arten, künstliche Blumenmacherei, Schuh-, Hut- und Handschuhmacherei,\n Schneiderei, alle Metallfabriken, von den Hochöfen bis zu Nadelfabriken u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":238,"address":"15.238"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0239","text":"w.,\n Papierfabrik, Glasmanufaktur, Tabaksmanufaktur, India-Rubber Werke, Litzen-\n fabrikation (für die Weberei), Handteppich-Weberei, Regenschirm- und Parasol-\n manufaktur, Fabrikation von Spindeln und Spulen, Buchdruckerei, Buchbinderei,\n Schreibmaterialienhandel (Stationery, dazu gehörig Verfertigung von Papier-\n schachteln, Karten, Papierfärben u. s. w.), Seilerei, Manufaktur von Gagatschmuck,\n Ziegeleien, Hand-Seidenmanufaktur, Coventry-Weberei, Salz-, Talglicht- und\n Cementirwerke, Zuckerraffinerie, Zwiebackmachen, verschiedne Holz- und andre\n vermischte Arbeiten.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":239,"address":"15.239"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0240","text":"„Sollte,“ sagt sie, „das Parlament unsern\n Vorschlag in seinem ganzen Umfang annehmen, so ist es zweifellos, dass\n solche Gesetzgebung den wohlthätigsten Einfluss ausüben würde, nicht nur\n auf die Jungen und Schwachen, mit denen sie sich zunächst beschäftigt,\n sondern auf die noch grössere Masse von erwachsnen Arbeitern, die direkt\n (die Weiber) und indirekt (die Männer) unter ihren Wirkungskreis fallen.","kind":"passage","sequence":240,"address":"15.240"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0241","text":"Sie würde ihnen regelmässige und ermässigte Arbeitsstunden aufzwingen;\n sie würde einen gesunderen und reinlicheren Zustand der Arbeitslokale her-\n beiführen; sie würde den Vorrath physischer Kraft, wovon ihr eignes\n Wohlergehen und das des Landes so sehr abhängt, haushalten und häufen;\n sie würde die aufsprossende Generation vor der Ueberanstrengung in frühem\n Alter schützen, welche ihre Konstitution untergräbt und zu vorzeitigem\n Verfall führt; sie würde schliesslich, wenigstens bis zum 13. Jahr die Ge-\n legenheit des Elementarunterrichts bieten und damit der unglaublichen Un-\n wissenheit ein Ende machen, die so treu in den Kommissionsberichten ge-\n schildert ist und nur mit qualvollster Empfindung und dem tiefen Gefühl\n nationaler Erniedrigung betrachtet werden kann“ [Note: l. c. p. XXV, n. 169.] . Das Toryministerium\n kündigte in der Thronrede vom 5.","kind":"passage","sequence":241,"address":"15.241"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0242","text":"Februar 1867 an, dass es die Vorschläge\n der industriellen Untersuchungskommission in „Bills“ formulirt habe. Dazu\n bedurfte es eines neuen zwanzigjährigen Experimentum in corpore vili.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"486\", \"facs\": \"#f0505\"} --> Bereits im Jahr 1840 war eine parlamentarische Kommission zur Unter-\n suchung über Kinderarbeit ernannt worden. Ihr Bericht von 1842 ent-\n rollte in den Worten N. W.","kind":"passage","sequence":242,"address":"15.242"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0243","text":"Senior’s „das furchtbarste Gemälde von\n Habsucht, Selbstsucht und Grausamkeit der Kapitalisten und Eltern, von\n Elend, Degradation und Zerstörung der Kinder und jungen Personen, das\n jemals das Auge der Welt schlug … Man wähnt vielleicht, der Bericht\n beschreibe die Greuel eines vergangenen Zeitalters … Diese Greuel dauern\n fort, intensiver als jemals … Die 1842 denuncirten Missbräuche stehn\n heut zu Tage (Oktober 1863) in voller Blüthe … Der Bericht von\n 1842 wurde ohne weitere Notiznahme zu den Akten gelegt und da lag er\n zwanzig volle Jahre, während deren man den physisch, geistig und mora-\n lisch niedergetretenen Kindern erlaubte, die Eltern der jetzigen Generation\n zu werden“ [Note: Senior l. c. p. 55 sqq.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":243,"address":"15.243"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0244","text":"Die jetzige Untersuchungskommission schlägt ebenfalls\n neue Reglung der Minenindustrie vor [Note: Das letzte Blaubuch über die Minen: „Report from the Select\n Committee on Mines together with etc. Evidence. 23. July 1866“,\n ist ein starker Folioband, enthält aber nur das Zeugenverhör. Der Bericht des\n vom Unterhaus aus seinem eignen Schoss ernannten Comité’s selbst besteht aus 5,\n sage fünf Zeilen, des Inhalts, dass es nichts zu sagen weiss und noch mehr (!)\n Zeugen verhört werden müssen. In der Minenindustrie, wohlbemerkt, gehn\n die Interessen der Landlords und industriellen Kapitalisten Hand in Hand. Die\n Art der Zeugenexamination erinnert an die cross examinations vor den englischen\n Gerichten, wo der Advokat durch unverschämte, sinnverwirrende Kreuz- und\n Querfragen den Zeugen aus der Fassung zu bringen und ihm die Worte im Munde\n zu verdrehn sucht.","kind":"passage","sequence":244,"address":"15.244"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0245","text":"Die Advokaten hier sind die parlamentarischen Examinatoren\n selbst; darunter Minen-Eigner und Exploiteurs, die Zeugen Minen-\n arbeiter, meist in Kohlenbergwerken. Die ganze Farce ist zu charakteristisch\n für den Geist des Kapitals, um hier nicht einige Auszüge zu geben. Vorher\n sei noch bemerkt, dass durch Gesetz von 1842 die Arbeit des weiblichen Ge-\n schlechts und aller Kinder unter 10 Jahren in den Minen verboten wurde. Ein\n neuer Akt, „The Mines Inspecting Act“ von 1860, schreibt ausser Minen-\n inspektion u. s. w. vor, dass Jungen zwischen 10 und 12 Jahren nicht beschäftigt\n werden sollen, wenn sie kein Schulcertifikat haben oder während bestimmter\n Stundenzahl Schule besuchen. Das ganze Gesetz ist eine Nullität, schon wegen\n der lächerlich kleinen Anzahl der Inspektoren, der Nichtigkeit ihrer Vollmachten\n und andrer Umstände, die man im Verlauf sehn wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":245,"address":"15.245"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0246","text":"Zur leichteren Uebersicht] . Endlich brachte Professor\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"487\", \"facs\": \"#f0506\"} -->Fawcett im Unterhaus (1867) ähnliche Resolutionen für die Agrikultur-\n arbeiter ein, das Kabinet übernahm jedoch die Initiative.","kind":"passage","sequence":246,"address":"15.246"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0247","text":"[Note: gebe ich die Resultate der Untersuchung u. s. w. in Rubriken. Ich erinnere, dass\n Frage und obligate Antwort in den englischen Blue Books numerirt sind, und dass\n die Zeugen, deren Aussagen hier citirt werden, Arbeiter in Kohlenbergwerken.\n 1) Beschäftigung der Jungen vom 10. Jahr an in den Minen.\n Die Arbeit, nebst obligatem Gang von und zu den Bergwerken, dauert in der Regel\n 14 bis 15 Stunden, ausnahmsweise länger, von 3, 4, 5 Uhr Morgens bis 4 und 5\n Uhr Abends. (n. 6, 452, 83.) Die erwachsnen Arbeiter arbeiten in zwei\n Gängen, jeder 8 Stunden, aber kein solcher Wechsel für die Jungen, um die\n Kosten zu sparen. (n. 80, 203, 204.) Die jungen Kinder hauptsächlich verwandt\n zum Oeffnen und Schliessen der Zugthüren in den verschiednen Abtheilungen des\n Bergwerks, die älteren zu schwerer Arbeit, Kohlentransport u. s. w. (n. 122,\n 739, 1747.)","kind":"passage","sequence":247,"address":"15.247"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0248","text":"Die langen Arbeitsstunden unter der Erde dauern bis zum 18. oder 22.\n Jahr, wenn der Uebergang zur eigentlichen Minenarbeit stattfindet. (n. 161.)\nDie Kinder und jungen Personen werden heutzutag härter abge-\n plackt als zu irgend einer früheren Periode. (n. 1663—67.) Die Minen-\n arbeiter verlangen fast einstimmig einen Parlamentsakt zum Verbot der Minenar-\n beit bis zum 14. Jahr. Und nun fragt Vivian Hussey (selbst Minenexploiteur):\n „Hängt diess Verlangen nicht von der grössern oder geringern Armuth der Eltern\n ab? Wäre es nicht hart, wo der Vater todt, oder verstümmelt u. s. w., der Familie\n diese Ressource zu entziehn? Und es muss doch eine allgemeine Regel herrschen.\n Wollt ihr in allen Fällen die Beschäftigung der Kinder bis zum 14. Jahr unter\n der Erde verbieten?“ Antwort: „In allen Fällen.“ (n. 107—110.)","kind":"passage","sequence":248,"address":"15.248"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0249","text":"Hussey:\n „Wenn die Arbeit vor 14 Jahren in den Minen verboten, würden die Eltern die\n Kinder nicht in Fabriken u. s. w. stecken? — In der Regel, nein.“ (n. 174.)\nArbeiter: „Das Auf- und Zuschliessen der Thüren sieht leicht aus. Es ist ein\n sehr qualvolles Geschäft. Vom beständigen Zug abgesehn, ist der Junge gefangen\n gesetzt, ganz so gut wie in einer dunkeln Kerkerzelle.“ Bourgeois Hussey:\n „Kann der Junge nicht lesen während der Thürwacht, wenn er ein Licht hat? —\n Erstens müsste er sich die Kerzen kaufen. Aber ausserdem würde es ihm nicht\n erlaubt werden. Er ist da, um auf sein Geschäft aufzupassen, er hat eine Pflicht\n zu erfüllen. Ich habe nie einen Jungen in der Grube lesen sehn.“ (n. 141—60.)\n 2) Erziehung. Die Minenarbeiter verlangen Gesetz für Zwangsunter-\n richt der Kinder, wie in den Fabriken.","kind":"passage","sequence":249,"address":"15.249"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0250","text":"Sie erklären die Klausel des Akts von\n 1860, wonach Erziehungscertifikat zur Verwendung von 10—12 Jahren erfordert,\n für rein illusorisch. Das „peinliche“ Verhörverfahren der kapitalistischen In-\n struktionsrichter wird hier wahrhaft drollig. (n. 115.) „Ist der Akt mehr nöthig\n gegen Anwender oder Eltern? — Gegen Beide.“ (n. 116.) „Mehr gegen den\n einen als den andern? — Wie soll ich das beantworten?“ (n. 137.) „Zeigen\n die Anwender irgend ein Verlangen, die Arbeitsstunden dem Schulunterricht anzu-]","kind":"passage","sequence":250,"address":"15.250"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0251","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"488\", \"facs\": \"#f0507\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":251,"address":"15.251"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0252","text":"Wenn die Verallgemeinerung der Fabrikgesetzgebung als physisches\n und geistiges Schutzmittel der Arbeiterklasse unvermeidlich geworden ist,\n [Note: passen? — Niemals.“ (n. 211.) „Verbessern die Minenarbeiter hinterher ihre\n Erziehung? — Sie verschlechtern sich im Allgemeinen; sie nehmen böse Gewohn-\n heiten an; sie verlegen sich auf Trunk und Spiel und dergleichen, und werden\n ganz und gar schiffbrüchig.“ (n. 109.) „Warum nicht die Kinder in Abend-\n schulen schicken? — In den meisten Kohlendistrikten existiren keine. Aber die\n Hauptsache ist, von der langen Ueberarbeit sind sie so erschöpft, dass ihnen die\n Augen vor Müdigkeit zufallen.“ „Also“, schliesst der Bourgeois, „Ihr seid gegen\n Erziehung? — Bei Leibe nicht, aber u. s. w.“ (n. 443.) „Sind die Minenbe-\n sitzer u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":252,"address":"15.252"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0253","text":"nicht durch den Akt von 1860 gezwungen, Schulcertifikate zu ver-\n langen, wenn die Kinder zwischen 10 und 12 Jahren? — Durch das Gesetz, ja,\n aber die Anwender thun es nicht.“ (n. 444.) „Nach eurer Ansicht ist diese\n Gesetzklausel nicht allgemein ausgeführt? — Sie wird gar nicht ausge-\n führt.“ (n. 717.) „Sind die Minenarbeiter sehr interessirt an der Erziehungs-\n frage? — Die grosse Mehrzahl.“ (n. 718.) „Sind sie ängstlich für Erzwingung\n des Gesetzes? — Die grosse Mehrzahl.“ (n. 720.) „Warum denn erzwingen sie\n das Gesetz nicht? — Mancher Arbeiter wünscht einen Jungen ohne Schulcertifikat\n zu verweigern, aber er wird ein gezeichneter Mann (a marked man).“ (n.\n 721.) „Gezeichnet durch wen? — Durch seinen Anwender.“ (n. 722.) „Ihr\n glaubt doch nicht etwa, dass die Anwender einen Mann für Gehorsam gegen das\n Gesetz verfolgen würden? — Ich glaube, sie würden es thun.“ (n. 723.)","kind":"passage","sequence":253,"address":"15.253"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0254","text":"„Warum\n verweigern die Arbeiter nicht solche Jungen anzuwenden? — Es ist nicht ihrer\n Wahl überlassen.“ (n. 1634.) „Ihr verlangt Parlamentsintervention? — Wenn\n irgend etwas Wirksames für die Erziehung der Kinder der Grubenarbeiter geschehn\n soll, so muss sie durch Parlamentsakt zwangsmässig gemacht werden.“\n (n. 1636.) „Soll das für die Kinder aller Arbeiter von Grossbritanien gelten oder\n nur für die Grubenarbeiter? — Ich bin hier, um im Namen der Grubenarbeiter zu\n sprechen.“ (n. 1638.) „Warum Grubenkinder von andern unterscheiden? —\n Weil sie eine Ausnahme von der Regel bilden.“ (n. 1639.) „In welcher Hin-\n sicht? — In physischer.“ (n. 1640.) „Warum sollte Erziehung für sie werth-\n voller sein, als für Knaben von andern Klassen?","kind":"passage","sequence":254,"address":"15.254"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0255","text":"— Ich sage nicht, dass sie werth-\n voller für sie ist, aber wegen ihrer Ueberarbeitung in den Minen haben sie weniger\n Chancen für Erziehung in Tags- und Sonntagsschulen.“ (n. 1644.) „Nicht\n wahr, es ist unmöglich Fragen dieser Art absolut zu behandeln?“ (n. 1646.)\n „Sind genug Schulen in den Distrikten? — Nein.“ (n. 1647.) „Wenn der\n Staat verlangte, dass jedes Kind zur Schule geschickt, wo sollen dann die Schulen\n für alle die Kinder herkommen? — Ich glaube, sobald es die Umstände gebieten,\n werden die Schulen von selbst entspringen. Die grosse Mehrzahl nicht nur der\n Kinder, sondern der erwachsnen Minenarbeiter kann weder schreiben, noch lesen.“\n (n. 705, 726.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"489\", \"facs\": \"#f0508\"} --> verallgemeinert und beschleunigt sie andrerseits, wie bereits angedeutet,\n die Verwandlung zerstreuter Arbeitsprozesse auf Zwergmassstab in kom-\n [Note: 3) Weiberarbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":255,"address":"15.255"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0256","text":"Arbeiterinnen werden zwar seit 1842 nicht mehr\nunter, wohl aber über der Erde zum Aufladen der Kohlen u. s. w., Schleppen\n der Kufen zu den Kanälen und Eisenbahnwagen, Sortiren der Kohlen u. s. w. ver-\n braucht. Ihre Anwendung hat sehr zugenommen in den letzten 3 bis 4 Jahren.\n (n. 1727.) Es sind meist Weiber, Töchter und Witwen von Grubenarbeitern, vom\n 12. bis zum 50. und 60. Jahre. (n. 645, 1779.) (n. 648.) „Was denken die\n Minenarbeiter von weiblicher Beschäftigung bei Bergwerken? — Sie verdammen\n sie allgemein.“ (n. 649.) „Warum? — Sie betrachten es erniedrigend für das\n Geschlecht … Sie tragen Art von Mannskleidern. In vielen Fällen wird alle\n Scham unterdrückt. Manche Weiber rauchen. Die Arbeit ist so schmutzig, wie\n die in den Gruben selbst. Darunter sind viele verheirathete Frauen, die ihre häus-\n lichen Pflichten nicht erfüllen können.“ (n. 651 sqq.) (n. 709.)","kind":"passage","sequence":256,"address":"15.256"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0257","text":"„Können\n die Witwen ein so einträgliches Geschäft (8—10 sh. wöchentlich) anderswo finden?\n — Ich kann darüber nichts sagen.“ (n. 710.) „Und dennoch (Herz von Stein!)\n seid Ihr entschlossen, ihnen diesen Lebensunterhalt abzuschneiden? — Sicher.“\n (n. 1715.) „Woher diese Stimmung? — Wir, Minenarbeiter, haben zu viel\n Respekt für das schöne Geschlecht, um sie zur Kohlengrube verdammt zu sehn …\n Diese Arbeit ist grossentheils sehr schwer. Viele dieser Mädchen heben 10 Tonnen\n per Tag.“ (n. 1732.) „Glaubt Ihr, dass die in den Bergwerken beschäftigten\n Arbeiterinnen unmoralischer sind als die in den Fabriken beschäftigten? — Der\n Prozentsatz der Schlechten ist grösser als unter den Fabrikmädchen.“ (n. 1733.)\n „Aber Ihr seid auch mit dem Stand der Moralität in den Fabriken nicht zufrieden?\n — Nein.“ (n. 1734.) „Wollt Ihr denn auch die Weiberarbeit in den Fabriken\n verbieten?","kind":"passage","sequence":257,"address":"15.257"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0258","text":"— Nein, ich will nicht.“ (n. 1735.) „Warum nicht? — Sie ist für\n das weibliche Geschlecht ehrenvoller und passender.“ (n. 1736.) „Dennoch\n ist sie schädlich für ihre Moralität, meint Ihr? — Nein, lange nicht so sehr wie\n die Arbeit in der Grube. Ich spreche übrigens nicht nur aus moralischen, sondern\n auch aus physischen und socialen Gründen. Die sociale Degradation der Mädchen\n ist jammervoll und extrem. Wenn diese Mädchen Frauen der Minenarbeiter\n werden, leiden die Männer tief unter dieser Degradation, und es treibt sie fort\n von Haus und in den Soff.“ (n. 1737.) „Aber gälte nicht dasselbe für die in\n Eisenwerken beschäftigten Weiber? — Ich kann nicht für andre Geschäfts-\n zweige sprechen.“ (n. 1740.) „Aber welcher Unterschied ist denn zwischen\n den in Eisenwerken und Bergwerken beschäftigten Weibern? — Ich habe mich\n nicht mit dieser Frage beschäftigt.“ (n. 1741.)","kind":"passage","sequence":258,"address":"15.258"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0259","text":"„Könnt Ihr einen Unterschied\n zwischen der einen und der andern Klasse entdecken? — Ich habe nichts darüber\n vergewissert, kenne aber durch Visite von Haus zu Haus den schmählichen Zustand\n der Dinge in unsrem Distrikt.“ (n. 1750.) „Hättet Ihr nicht grosse Lust Weiber-\n beschäftigung überall abzuschaffen, wo sie degradirend ist? — Ja … die besten] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"490\", \"facs\": \"#f0509\"} --> binirte Arbeitsprozesse auf grosser, gesellschaftlicher Stufenleiter, die Kon-\n centration des Kapitals und das Fabrikregime selbst. Sie zerstört alle\n [Note: Gefühle der Kinder müssen von mütterlicher Zucht herkommen.“ (n. 1751.)\n „Aber das passt ja auch auf agrikole Beschäftigung der Weiber? — Die dauert nur\n zwei Saisons, bei uns arbeiten sie alle 4 Saisons durch, manchmal Tag und Nacht,\n nass bis auf die Haut, ihre Konstitution geschwächt, ihre Gesundheit gebrochen.“\n (n. 1753.)","kind":"passage","sequence":259,"address":"15.259"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0260","text":"„Ihr habt die Frage (nämlich der Weiberbeschäftigung) nicht all-\n gemein studirt? — Ich habe um mich her geschaut, und kann so viel sagen,\n dass ich nirgendwo etwas der weiblichen Beschäftigung an den Kohlengruben\n Paralleles gefunden habe.“ „Es ist Mannsarbeit und Arbeit für starke Männer.“\n „Die bessere Klasse der Minenarbeiter, die sich zu heben und zu humanisiren\n sucht, statt irgend Stütze an ihren Weibern zu finden, wird durch sie herunter-\n gezerrt.“ Nachdem die Bourgeois noch weiter in die Kreuz und Quere gefragt,\n kömmt endlich das Geheimniss ihres „Mitleidens“ für Witwen, arme Familien\n u. s. w. heraus: „Der Kohleneigenthümer ernennt gewisse Gentlemen zur Ober-\n aufsicht und deren Politik ist es, um Beifall zu ernten, alles auf den möglichst\n ökonomischen Fuss zu setzen, und die beschäftigten Mädchen erhalten 1 bis 1 sh.\n 6 d. täglich, wo ein Mann 2 sh. 6 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":260,"address":"15.260"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0261","text":"erhalten müsste.“ (n. 1816.)\n 4) Todtenschau-Juries. (n. 360.) „Mit Bezug auf die coroner’s\n inquests in Euren Distrikten, sind die Arbeiter zufrieden mit dem Gerichtsver-\n fahren, wenn Unfälle vorkommen? — Nein, sie sind es nicht.“ (n. 361.) „Warum\n nicht? — Namentlich weil man Leute zu Juries macht, die absolut nichts von\n Minen wissen. Arbeiter werden nie zugezogen, ausser als Zeugen. Im Ganzen\n nimmt man Krämer aus der Nachbarschaft, welche unter dem Einfluss der Minen-\n besitzer, ihrer Kunden, stehn und nicht einmal die technischen Ausdrücke der\n Zeugen verstehn. Wir verlangen, dass Minenarbeiter einen Theil der Jury\n bilden. Im Durchschnitt steht der Urtheilsspruch im Widerspruch zu den Zeugen-\n aussagen.“ (n. 378) „Sollen Juries nicht unparteiisch sein? — Ja.“ (n. 379.)\n „Würden die Arbeiter es sein? — Ich sehe keine Motive, warum sie nicht unpar-\n teiisch sein sollten.","kind":"passage","sequence":261,"address":"15.261"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0262","text":"Sie haben Sachkenntniss.“ (n. 380.) „Aber würden sie\n nicht die Tendenz haben, im Interesse der Arbeiter ungerecht harte Urtheile zu\n fällen? — Nein, ich glaube es nicht.“\n 5) Falsches Mass und Gewicht u. s. w. Die Arbeiter verlangen\n wöchentliche, statt vierzehntägige Zahlung, Mass nach Gewicht, statt nach Kubik-\n raum der Kufen, Schutz gegen die Anwendung falschen Gewichts u. s. w.\n (n. 1071.) „Wenn die Kufen fraudulent vergrössert werden, so kann ein Mann\n ja die Mine verlassen nach 14tägiger Notiz? — Aber, wenn er zu einem andern\n Platz geht, findet er dasselbe.“ (n. 1072.) „Aber er kann den Platz doch ver-\n lassen, wo das Unrecht verübt wird? — Es ist allgemein herrschend.“ (n. 1073.)\n „Aber der Mann kann seinen jedesmaligen Platz nach 14tägiger Notiz verlassen?","kind":"passage","sequence":262,"address":"15.262"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0263","text":"— Ja.“ Streusand drauf!] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"491\", \"facs\": \"#f0510\"} --> alterthümlichen und Uebergangsformen, wohinter sich die Herrschaft des\n Kapitals noch theilweis versteckt, und ersetzt sie durch seine direkte, un-\n [Note: 6) Mineninspektion. Die Arbeiter leiden nicht nur von den Zufällen\n durch explodirende Gase. (n. 234 sqq.) „Wir haben uns ebenso sehr zu be-\n klagen über die schlechte Ventilation der Kohlengruben, so dass die Leute kaum\n darin athmen können; sie werden dadurch zu jeder Art Beschäftigung unfähig.\n So hat z. B. grade jetzt in dem Theil der Mine, wo ich arbeite, die Pestluft viele\n Leute für Wochen aufs Krankenbett geworfen. Die Hauptgänge sind meist luftig\n genug, aber grade nicht die Plätze, worin wir arbeiten.","kind":"passage","sequence":263,"address":"15.263"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0264","text":"Sendet ein Mann Klage\n über Ventilation an den Inspektor, so wird er entlassen und ist ein „gezeich-\n neter“ Mann, der auch sonstwo keine Beschäftigung findet. Der „Mining In-\n specting Act“ von 1860 ist ein reiner Papierlappen. Der Inspektor, und ihre\n Zahl ist viel zu klein, macht vielleicht in 7 Jahren einmal eine formelle Visite.\n Unser Inspektor ist ein ganz unfähiger, siebzigjähriger Mann, der mehr als 130\n Kohlenbergwerken vorsteht. Neben mehr Inspektoren brauchen wir Subinspek-\n toren.“ (n. 280.) „Soll dann die Regierung solch eine Armee von In-\n spektoren halten, dass sie alles, was Ihr verlangt, ohne Information der Ar-\n beiter selbst thun können? — Das ist unmöglich, aber sie sollen sich die Infor-\n mation in den Minen selbst holen kommen.“ (n. 285.) „Glaubt Ihr nicht, dass\n die Wirkung sein würde die Verantwortlichkeit (!) für die Ventilation\n u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":264,"address":"15.264"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0265","text":"von dem Minenbesitzer auf die Regierungsbeamten zu wälzen? — Keines-\n wegs; es muss ihr Geschäft sein, die Befolgung der bereits bestehenden Gesetze\n zu erzwingen.“ (n. 294.) „Wenn Ihr von Subinspektoren sprecht, meint Ihr\n Leute mit weniger Gehalt und von niedrigerem Charakter als die gegenwärtigen\n Inspektoren? — Ich wünsche sie keineswegs niedriger, wenn Ihr sie besser\n haben könnt.“ (n. 295.) „Wollt Ihr mehr Inspektoren oder eine niedrigere\n Klasse von Leuten als die Inspektoren? — Wir brauchen Leute, die sich in den\n Minen selbst umtummeln, Leute, die keine Angst für ihre eigne Haut haben.“\n (n. 296.) „Wenn man Euren Wunsch nach Inspektoren von einer\n schlechteren Sorte erfüllte, würde ihr Mangel an Geschick nicht Gefahren\n erzeugen u. s. w.?","kind":"passage","sequence":265,"address":"15.265"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0266","text":"— Nein; es ist Sache der Regierung, passende Subjekte anzu-\n stellen.“ Diese Art Examination wird endlich selbst dem Präsidenten des Unter-\n suchungscomité’s zu toll. „Ihr wollt“, fährt er dazwischen, „praktische Leute,\n die sich in den Minen selbst umsehn und an den Inspektor berichten, der dann\n seine höhere Wissenschaft verwenden kann.“ (n. 531.) „Würde die Ven-\n tilation aller dieser alten Werke nicht viel Kosten verursachen? — Ja, Unkosten\n möchten erwachsen, aber Menschenleben würden beschützt.“ (n. 581.) Ein\n Kohlenarbeiter protestirt gegen die 17. Sektion des Akts von 1860: „Gegen-\n wärtig, wenn der Mineninspektor irgend einen Theil der Mine in nicht bearbeits-\n fähigem Zustand findet, so muss er es an den Minenbesitzer und den Minister des\n Innern berichten.","kind":"passage","sequence":266,"address":"15.266"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0267","text":"Danach hat der Minenbesitzer 20 Tage Bedenkzeit; am Ende] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"492\", \"facs\": \"#f0511\"} --> verhüllte Herrschaft. Sie verallgemeinert damit auch den direkten Kampf\n gegen diese Herrschaft. Während sie in den individuellen Werkstätten\n Gleichförmigkeit, Regelmässigkeit, Ordnung und Oekonomie erzwingt,\n vermehrt sie durch den ungeheuren Sporn, den Schranke und Regel des\n Arbeitstags der Technik aufdrücken, die Anarchie und Katastrophen der\n kapitalistischen Produktion im Grossen und Ganzen, die Intensivität der\n Arbeit und die Konkurrenz der Maschinerie mit dem Arbeiter. Mit den\n Sphären des Kleinbetriebs und der Hausarbeit vernichtet sie die letzten\n Zufluchtsstätten der unaufhörlich „überzählig“ gemachten und damit\n das bisherige Sicherheitsventil des ganzen Gesellschaftsmechanismus.","kind":"passage","sequence":267,"address":"15.267"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0268","text":"Mit\n den materiellen Bedingungen und der gesellschaftlichen Kombination des\n Produktionsprozesses reift sie die Widersprüche und Antagonismen seiner\nkapitalistischen Form, daher gleichzeitig die Bildungselemente\n [Note: der 20 Tage kann er jede Veränderung verweigern. Thut er das aber, so hat er\n an den Minister des Innern zu schreiben und ihm 5 Bergwerksingenieure vorzu-\n schlagen, worunter der Minister die Schiedsrichter erwählen muss. Wir behaup-\n ten, dass in diesem Fall der Minenbesitzer virtuell seinen eignen Richter ernennt.“\n (n. 586.) Der Bourgeoisexaminator, selbst Minenbesitzer: „Diess ist\n ein rein spekulativer Einwand.“ (n. 588.) „Ihr habt also eine sehr geringe\n Ansicht von der Redlichkeit der Bergwerksingenieure? — Ich sage, es ist sehr un-\n billig und ungerecht.“ (n. 589.)","kind":"passage","sequence":268,"address":"15.268"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0269","text":"„Besitzen Bergwerksingenieure nicht eine Art\n von öffentlichem Charakter, der ihre Entscheidungen über die von Euch befürchtete\n Parteilichkeit erhebt? — Ich verweigre Fragen über den persönlichen Charakter\n dieser Leute zu beantworten. Ich bin überzeugt, dass sie in vielen Fällen sehr\n parteiisch handeln und dass diese Macht ihnen genommen werden sollte, wo Men-\n schenleben auf dem Spiel stehn.“ Derselbe Bourgeois hat die Unverschämtheit\n zu fragen: „Glaubt Ihr nicht, dass auch die Minenbesitzer Verluste bei den Ex-\n plosionen haben?“ — Endlich (n. 1042): „Könnt Ihr Arbeiter Eure eigenen In-\n teressen nicht selbst wahrnehmen, ohne die Hilfe der Regierung anzurufen? —\nNein.“ — Im Jahr 1865 gab es 3217 Kohlenbergwerke in Grossbritanien und —\n 12 Inspektoren. Ein Minenbesitzer von Yorkshire (Times, 26.","kind":"passage","sequence":269,"address":"15.269"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0270","text":"Januar 1867)\n berechnet selbst, dass abgesehn von ihren rein bureaukratischen Geschäften, die\n ihre ganze Zeit absorbiren, jede Mine nur einmal in 10 Jahren besichtigt werden\n könnte. Kein Wunder, dass die Katastrophen in den letzten Jahren (namentlich\n auch 1866 und 1867) progressiv in Anzahl und Umfang (manchmal mit einem\n Opfer von 200—300 Arbeitern) zugenommen haben.","kind":"passage","sequence":270,"address":"15.270"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0271","text":"Diess sind die Schönheiten\n der „freien“ kapitalistischen Produktion!] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"493\", \"facs\": \"#f0512\"} -->einer neuen und die Umwälzungsmomente der alten Ge-\n sellschaft [Note: Robert Owen, der Vater der Cooperativfabriken und -Boutiquen, der\n jedoch, wie früher bemerkt, die Illusionen seiner Nachtreter über die Tragweite\n dieser isolirten Umwandlungselemente keineswegs theilte, ging nicht nur thatsäch-\n lich in seinen Versuchen vom Fabriksystem aus, sondern erklärte es auch theo-\n retisch für den Ausgangspunkt der socialen Revolution. Herr Vissering, Pro-\n fessor der politischen Oekonomie an der Universität zu Leiden, scheint so etwas\n zu ahnen, wenn er in seinem „Handboek van Praktische Staatshuis-\n kunde. 1860—62“, welches die Plattheiten der Vulgärökonomie in entspre-\n chendster Form vorträgt, für den Handwerksbetrieb gegen die grosse Industrie\n eifert.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":271,"address":"15.271"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0272","text":"Die Revolution, welche die grosse Industrie im Ackerbau und den\n socialen Verhältnissen seiner Produktionsagenten hervorruft, kann erst\n später dargestellt werden. Hier genügt kurze Andeutung einiger vorweg-\n genommener Resultate. Wenn der Gebrauch der Maschinerie im Acker-\n bau grossentheils frei ist von den physischen Nachtheilen, die sie dem\n Fabrikarbeiter zufügt [Note: Ausführliche Darstellung der im englischen Ackerbau angewandten\n Maschinerie findet man in: „Die landwirthschaftlichen Geräthe und\n Maschinen Englands von Dr. W. Hamm. 2. Aufl. 1856.“ In seiner\n Skizze über den Entwicklungsgang der englischen Agrikultur folgt Herr Hamm zu\n kritiklos dem Herrn Leonce de Lavergne.] , wirkt sie hier noch intensiver und ohne Gegen-\n stoss auf die „Ueberzähligmachung“ der Arbeiter, wie man später im De-\n tail sehn wird. In den Grafschaften von Cambridge und Suffolk z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":272,"address":"15.272"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0273","text":"hat\n sich das Areal des bebauten Landes seit den letzten zwanzig Jahren sehr\n ausgedehnt, während die Landbevölkerung in derselben Periode nicht nur\nrelativ, sondern absolut abnahm. In den Vereinigten Staaten von\n Nordamerika ersetzen Agrikultur - Maschinen einstweilen nur virtuell\n Arbeiter, d. h. sie erlauben dem Producenten Bebauung einer grösseren\n Fläche, verjagen aber nicht wirklich beschäftigte Arbeiter. In England\n und Wales betrug 1861 die Zahl der in der Fabrikation von Ackerbau-\n Maschinen betheiligten Personen 1034, während die Zahl der an\n Dampf- und Arbeitsmaschinen beschäftigten Agrikulturarbei-\n ter nur 1205 betrug.","kind":"passage","sequence":273,"address":"15.273"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0274","text":"In der Sphäre der Agrikultur wirkt die grosse Industrie in sofern\n am revolutionärsten, als sie das Bollwerk der alten Gesellschaft vernichtet,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"494\", \"facs\": \"#f0513\"} -->den „Bauer“, und ihm den Lohnarbeiter unterschiebt. Die socialen\n Umwälzungsbedürfnisse und Gegensätze des Landes werden so mit denen\n der Stadt ausgeglichen. An die Stelle des gewohnheitsfaulsten und irra-\n tionellsten Betriebs tritt bewusste, technologische Anwendung der Wissen-\n schaft. Die Zerreissung des ursprünglichen Familienbandes von Agri-\n kultur und Manufaktur, welches die kindlich unentwickelte Gestalt beider\n umschlang, wird durch die kapitalistische Produktionsweise vollendet. Sie\n schafft aber zugleich die materiellen Voraussetzungen einer neuen, höheren\n Synthese, des Vereins von Agrikultur und Industrie, auf Grundlage ihrer\n gegensätzlich ausgearbeiteten Gestalten.","kind":"passage","sequence":274,"address":"15.274"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0275","text":"Mit dem stets wachsenden Ueber-\n gewicht der städtischen Bevölkerung, die sie in grossen Centren zusammen-\n häuft, häuft die kapitalistische Produktion einerseits die geschichtliche\n Bewegungskraft der Gesellschaft, stört sie andrerseits den Stoffwechsel\n zwischen Mensch und Erde, d. h. die Rückkehr der vom Menschen in der\n Form von Nahrungs- und Kleidungsmitteln vernutzten Bodenbestandtheile\n zum Boden, also die ewige Naturbedingung dauernder Bodenfruchtbarkeit.\n Sie zerstört damit zugleich die physische Gesundheit der Stadtarbeiter und\n das geistige Leben der Landarbeiter [Note: „You divide the people into two hostile camps of clownish boors and\n emasculated dwarfs. Good heavens!","kind":"passage","sequence":275,"address":"15.275"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0276","text":"a nation divided into agricultural and\n commercial interests calling itself sane nay styling itself enlightened and civilized,\n not only in spite of, but in consequence of this monstrous and unnatural division.“\n (David Urquhart l. c. p. 119.) Diese Stelle zeigt zugleich die Stärke und die\n Schwäche einer Art von Kritik, welche die Gegenwart zu be- und verurtheilen, aber\n nicht zu begreifen weiss.] . Aber sie zwingt zugleich durch\n die Zerstörung der bloss naturwüchsig entstandenen Umstände jenes Stoff-\n wechsels ihn systematisch als regelndes Gesetz der gesellschaftlichen Pro-\n duktion und in einer der vollen menschlichen Entwicklung adäquaten Form\n herzustellen.","kind":"passage","sequence":276,"address":"15.276"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0277","text":"In der Agrikultur wie in der Manufaktur erscheint die kapi-\n talistische Umwandlung des Produktionsprozesses zugleich als Martyrologie\n der Producenten, das Arbeitsmittel als Unterjochungsmittel, Exploitationsmit-\n tel und Verarmungsmittel des Arbeiters, die gesellschaftliche Kombina-\n tion der Arbeitsprozesse als organisirte Unterdrückung seiner individu-\n ellen Lebendigkeit, Freiheit und Selbstständigkeit. Die Zerstreuung der\n Landarbeiter über grössere Flächen bricht zugleich ihre Widerstandskraft,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"495\", \"facs\": \"#f0514\"} --> während Koncentration die der städtischen Arbeiter steigert. Wie in der\n städtischen Industrie wird in der modernen Agrikultur die gesteigerte Pro-\n duktivkraft und grössere Flüssigmachung der Arbeit erkauft durch Verwü-\n stung und Versiechung der Arbeitskraft selbst.","kind":"passage","sequence":277,"address":"15.277"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0278","text":"Und jeder Fortschritt der\n kapitalistischen Agrikultur ist nicht nur ein Fortschritt in der Kunst den\nArbeiter, sondern zugleich in der Kunst den Boden zu berauben,\n jeder Fortschritt in Steigerung seiner Fruchtbarkeit für eine gegebne Zeitfrist\n zugleich ein Fortschritt im Ruin der dauernden Quellen dieser Fruchtbarkeit.\n Je mehr ein Land, wie die Vereinigten Staaten von Nordamerika z. B.,\n von der grossen Industrie als dem Hintergrund seiner Entwicklung ausgeht,\n desto rascher dieser Zerstörungsprozess [Note: Vgl. Liebig: „Die Chemie in ihrer Anwendung auf Agri-\n kultur und Physiologie. 7. Auflage 1862“, namentlich auch im Ersten\n Band die „Einleitung in die Naturgesetze des Feldbaus.“ Die\n Entwicklung der negativen Seite der modernen Agrikultur, vom naturwissenschaft-\n lichen Standpunkt, ist eins der unsterblichen Verdienste Liebig’s.","kind":"passage","sequence":278,"address":"15.278"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0279","text":"Auch seine\n historischen Aperçus über die Geschichte der Agrikultur, obgleich nicht ohne\n grobe Irrthümer, enthalten mehr Lichtblicke als die Schriften sämmtlicher moder-\n nen politischen Oekonomen zusammengenommen. Zu bedauern bleibt, dass er\n aufs Gradewohl Aeusserungen wagt, wie folgende: „Durch eine weiter getriebene\n Pulverisirung und häufigeres Pflügen wird der Luftwechsel im Innern der porösen\n Erdtheile befördert, und die Oberfläche der Erdtheile, auf welche die Luft ein-\n wirken soll, vergrössert und erneuert, aber es ist leicht verständlich, dass die\nMehrerträge des Feldes nicht proportionell der auf das Feld\n verwandten Arbeit sein können, sondern dass sie in einem weit kleineren\n Verhältnisse steigen. Dieses Gesetz“, fügt Liebig hinzu, „ist von J. St. Mill\n zuerst in seinen Princ. of Pol. Econ. v. I, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":279,"address":"15.279"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0280","text":"17 in folgender Weise ausge-\n sprochen: ‚That the produce of land increases caeteris paribus in a diminishing\n ratio to the increase of the labourers employed, (Herr Mill wiederholt\n sogar das Ricardo’sche Schulgesetz in falscher Formel, denn da „the decrease of\n the labourers employed“ in England beständig war, im Fortschritt der Agrikultur,\n hätte das für und in England erfundene Gesetz wenigstens in England keine An-\n wendung) is the universal law of agricultural industry‘, merkwürdig genug, da\n ihm dessen Grund unbekannt war.“ (Liebig l. c. Bd. I, p. 143 u. Note.) Ab-\n gesehn von irriger Deutung des Wortes „Arbeit“, worunter Liebig etwas anderes\n versteht, als die politische Oekonomie, ist es jedenfalls „merkwürdig genug“, dass\n er Herrn J. St. Mill zum ersten Verkünder einer Theorie macht, die James\n Anderson zur Zeit A.","kind":"passage","sequence":280,"address":"15.280"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0281","text":"Smith’s zuerst veröffentlichte und in verschiednen Schriften\n bis in den Anfang des 19. Jahrhunderts hinein wiederholte, die Malthus, über-] . Die kapitalistische Produk-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"496\", \"facs\": \"#f0515\"} --> tion entwickelt daher nur die Technik und Kombination des gesellschaft-\n lichen Produktionsprozesses, indem sie zugleich die Springquellen allen\n Reichthums untergräbt: Die Erde und den Arbeiter.","kind":"passage","sequence":281,"address":"15.281"}],"sha256":"8076bba948b10bf509764ea7d75da7a5ecb59806923bec1e6a8594a0f19f93f7","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T205019110795Z_9c391856","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"4) Maschinerie und grosse Industrie. · reading part 2/2","source_sha256":"8076bba948b10bf509764ea7d75da7a5ecb59806923bec1e6a8594a0f19f93f7","sha256":"4da5e1b9d19270eb999dd65adfe7fcd5f9f07c3535eec772024c0edaee6a2b4b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:50:19.110855+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0001","text":"With the extension of machine operation in one branch of industry, therefore, production initially increases in the other branches that supply it with its means of production. How far the mass of labourers employed thereby grows depends, given the length of the working day and the intensity of labour, on the composition of the capitals employed, i.e. on the ratio of their constant and variable components. This ratio in turn varies greatly with the extent to which machinery has already seized, or is seizing, those trades themselves. The number of people condemned to coal and metal mines swelled enormously with the progress of English machinery, although its increase has been slowed in recent decades by the use of new machinery for mining [Note: According to the Census of 1861 (Vol. II, Lond.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"15.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0002","text":"1863), the number\nof labourers employed in the coal mines of England and Wales was\n246,613, of whom 73,546 were under and 173,067 over 20 years of age. The first category\nincludes 835 from five to ten years old, 30,701 from ten to fifteen, 42,010 from fif-\nteen to nineteen. The number employed in iron, copper, lead, tin, and\nall other metal mines: 319,222.] . A new kind of labourer springs into\nlife with the machine, its producer. We already know that machine operation\nis itself taking possession of this branch of production on an ever more massive scale—\n[Running matter: I. 28]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"434\", \"facs\": \"#f0453\"} --> [Note: In England and Wales in 1861, employed in the production of machinery:\n60,807 persons, including the manufacturers together with their clerks, etc.,\nlikewise all agents and merchants in this field.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"15.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0003","text":"Excluded, on the other hand, are the\nproducers of smaller machines, such as sewing machines, etc., as likewise the produc-\ners of tools for working machines, such as spindles, etc. The number\nof all civil engineers was 3,329.] . As regards raw material furthermore [Note: Since iron is one of the most important raw materials, it should here be noted that in 1861 in\nEngland and Wales there were 125,771 iron-founders, of whom 123,430 were male, 2,341 fe-\nmale. Of the former, 30,810 were under and 92,620 over 20 years of age.] , there is,\nfor example, no doubt that the stormy march of cotton spinning promoted,\nin hothouse fashion, cotton cultivation in the United States and, along\nwith it, not only the African slave trade, but at the same time made\nNegro breeding the principal business of the so-called Border slave states.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"15.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0004","text":"When the first slave census was taken in the United States\nin 1790, their number amounted to 697,000, whereas in 1861 it was approximately\nfour million. On the other hand, it is no less certain that the flour-\nishing of the mechanical woollen factory, together with the progressive transformation\nof arable land into sheep pasture, brought about the mass expulsion and “rendering super-\nfluous” of agricultural labourers. Ireland is still at this very moment undergoing\nthe process of further reducing its population, diminished by almost one half during the last 20 years,\nto the measure exactly corresponding to the requirements of its\nlandlords and the English gentlemen wool manufacturers.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"15.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0005","text":"If machinery seizes upon a preliminary or intermediate stage of the total\ncourse which an object of labour has to traverse before reaching its final form,\nthen, along with the material of labour, the demand for labour increases\nin the trades still carried on in handicraft or manufacture, which further shape\nthe machine-made product. Machine spinning, for instance, supplied yarn\nso cheaply and so abundantly that hand-weavers could at first work full\ntime without increased outlay. Thus their income rose [Note: “A family of 4 adult persons (cotton weavers) with two\nchildren as winders earned at the end of the last and beginning of the present cen-\ntury £4 per week with 10 hours’ daily labour; if work was very\nurgent, they could earn more … Previously they had always suffered\nfrom an insufficient supply of yarn.” (Gaskell l. c. pp. 25–27.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"15.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0006","text":"Hence an influx of people into cotton weaving, until\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"435\", \"facs\": \"#f0454\"} --> finally the 800,000 cotton weavers called into life in England, for example,\nby the Jenny, Throstle, and Mule were in turn struck down by the power loom.\nThus, with the abundance of machine-produced articles of clothing, the number\nof tailors, dressmakers, seamstresses, etc. grows, until the sewing machine appears.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"15.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0007","text":"Corresponding to the increasing mass of raw materials, semi-finished goods,\ninstruments of labour, etc., which machine operation supplies with a relatively small\nnumber of labourers, the working-up of these raw materials\nand semi-finished goods differentiates into innumerable sub-species, and hence the diversity\nof social branches of production. Machine operation increases the\nsocial division of labour far more than manufacture,\nbecause it raises to an incomparably higher degree the productive power of the trades\nit seizes upon.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"15.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0008","text":"The immediate result of machinery is to increase surplus value and at the\nsame time the mass of products in which it is represented, and thus, along with the substance\non which the capitalist class and its appendage feed, to enlarge these social\nstrata themselves. Their growing wealth and the relatively steadily declining\nnumber of labourers required for the production of the primary necessaries of life\nproduce, together with a new need for luxury, new means of satisfying it.\nA greater part of the social product is transformed into surplus product,\nand a greater part of the surplus product is repro-\nduced and consumed in refined and diversified forms. In other words: luxury production\ngrows [Note: F. Engels, in “The Condition etc.,” demonstrates the wretched condition of a\ngreat part of precisely these luxury labourers.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"15.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0009","text":"Massive new evidence for this\nis found in the reports of the “Child. Empl. Comm.”] . The refinement and diversification of products likewise\nspring from the new world-market relations which large-scale industry creates.\nNot only are more foreign articles of consumption exchanged for the domestic\nproduct, but a greater mass of foreign raw materials, ingredients,\nsemi-finished goods, etc. also enters domestic industry as means of production. With these world-\nmarket relations, the demand for labour in the transport\nindustry rises, and the latter divides into numerous new sub-species [Note: In 1861, in England and Wales, 94,665\nseamen were employed in the merchant marine.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"15.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0010","text":"[Running matter: 28*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"436\", \"facs\": \"#f0455\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"15.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0011","text":"The increase of means of production and subsistence by means of a relatively de-\ncreasing number of labourers drives the expansion of labour in branches of industry\nwhose products, such as canals, commercial docks, tunnels, bridges,\netc., bear fruit only in the more distant future. Entirely new branches of production,\nand hence new fields of labour, arise either directly on the basis of machinery,\nor at least on the basis of the general industrial revolution corresponding to it. Their\nshare of total production, however, is by no means significant, even in the\nmost developed countries. The number of labourers employed by them rises in direct\nproportion to the extent that the necessity of the crudest manual labour is reproduced.\nAt present, gasworks, telegraphy, photography, steam navigation, and the railway\nsystem may be regarded as the principal industries of this kind.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"15.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0012","text":"The Census of 1861 (for England and Wales) gives for the gas in-\ndustry (gasworks, production of mechanical apparatuses, agents\nof the gas companies, etc.) 15,211 persons; telegraphy 2,399;\nphotography 2,366; steamship service 3,570; and rail-\nways 70,599, among whom approximately 28,000 “unskilled” earthworkers employed more or less perma-\nnently, together with the entire administrative and commercial personnel. Thus the\ntotal number of individuals in these five new industries is 94,145.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"15.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0013","text":"Finally, the extraordinarily increased productive power in the spheres of\nlarge-scale industry, accompanied as it is by intensified and extensive exploitation\nof labour-power in all other spheres of production, permits an ever greater\npart of the working class to be employed unproductively and thus, in particular,\nto reproduce ever more massively the old domestic slaves under the name\nof the “serving class,” such as servants, maids, lackeys, etc. According to the\nCensus of 1861, the total population of England and Wales numbered 20,066,244 persons,\nof whom 9,776,259 were male and 10,289,965 female. If one deducts from this\nthose too old or too young for work, all “unproductive” women,\nyoung persons, and children; then the “ideological” estates, such as the gov-\nernment, priests, jurists, military, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"15.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0014","text":"etc.; furthermore all those whose exclu-\nsive business is the consumption of others’ labour in the form of ground rent,\ninterest, etc.; finally paupers, vagabonds, criminals, etc., there remain,\nin round numbers, 8 million persons of both sexes and of the most diverse\nages, including all capitalists functioning in any way in production,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"437\", \"facs\": \"#f0456\"} --> commerce, finance, etc. Of these\n8 million, there are:","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"15.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0015","text":"Agricultural labourers (including shepherds\nand farmhands and maids living with tenant farmers)\n. . . . . . . . . . . 1,098,261 persons.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"15.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0016","text":"All those employed in cotton, woollen, worsted, flax,\nhemp, silk, and jute factories, and in mechani-\ncal hosiery manufacture and lace manufac-\nture . . . . . . . . . 642,607 [Note: Of these, only 177,596 were males over 13 years of age.]  “","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"15.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0017","text":"All those employed in coal and metal mines\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . 565,835 “","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"15.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0018","text":"Those employed in all metal works (blast furnaces, rolling\nmills, etc.) and metal manufactures of every\nkind . . . . . . . . . 396,998 [Note: Of these, 30,501 were female.]  “","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"15.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0019","text":"Serving class . . . . . . . . . . . 1,208,648 [Note: Of these, male: 137,447. Excluded from the\n1,208,648 is all personnel not serving in private houses.]  “","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"15.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0020","text":"If we reckon together those employed in all textile factories\nwith the personnel of coal and metal mines, we obtain\n1,208,442; if we reckon them together with the personnel of all metal\nworks and manufactures, the total is 1,039,605, in both cases\nsmaller than the number of modern domestic slaves. What an elevating re-\nsult of capitalistically exploited machinery!","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"15.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0021","text":"All accountable representatives of political economy admit that the new introduction\nof machinery acts like a pestilence upon the labourers in the traditional trades and manufactures\nwith which it initially competes. Almost all bemoan the slavery of the factory labourer.\nAnd what is the great trump card that they all play? That machinery,\nafter the torture accompanying its introduction and development,\nin the last instance increases the labour slaves, instead of ultimately\ndiminishing them!","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"15.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0022","text":"Yes, political economy\nexults in the abominable theorem—abominable to every “philan-\nthropist” who believes in the eternal natural necessity of the capitalist mode of produc-\ntion—that even the factory already based on machine operation, after a definite\nperiod of growth, after a shorter or\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"438\", \"facs\": \"#f0457\"} --> longer “transitional period,” sweats more workers\nthan it originally threw onto the pavement [Note: Ganilh, on the contrary, regards the absolute diminution in the number of\nlabour slaves as the final result of machine operation, at whose expense an increased num-\nber of the “gens honnêtes” then feeds and develops its well-known “perfectibilité per-\nfectible.”","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"15.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0023","text":"Little as he understands the movement of production,\nhe at least feels that machinery, if it serves only, first, through its\nintroduction to transform employed labourers into paupers and, secondly, through\nits development to reproduce more labour slaves than it has struck down, is a\nvery fatal institution. The cretinism of his own standpoint can only be expressed\nin his own words: “Les classes condamnées à produire\net à consommer diminuent, et les classes qui dirigent le travail, qui soulagent,\nconsolent et éclairent toute la population, se multiplient … et s’appro-\nprient tous les bienfaits qui résultent de la diminution des frais du\ntravail, de l’abondance des productions et du bon marché des consommations.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"15.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0024","text":"Dans cette direction, l’espèce humaine s’élève aux plus hautes conceptions du\ngénie, pénètre dans les profondeurs mystérieuses de la religion, établit les\nprincipes salutaires de la morale (which consists therein de “s’approprier\ntous les bienfaits etc.”), les lois tutélaires de la liberté (the liberté for “les\nclasses condamnées à produire”?) et du pouvoir, de l’obéissance et de la\njustice, du devoir et de l’humanité.” This gibberish in: “Des Systèmes\nd’Économie Politique etc. Par M. Ch. Ganilh.” 2nd ed. Paris\n1821, vol. II, p. 224. cf. ibid. p. 212.] !","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"15.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0025","text":"It had indeed already appeared in some examples, e.g. the English\nworsted and silk factories, that at a certain stage of development an\nextraordinary expansion of branches of industry can be associated with\nnot merely relative, but absolute decrease in the num-\nber of workers employed. In the year 1860, when a special\ncensus of all the factories of the United Kingdom was taken by order of Parlia-\nment, the division of the factory districts of Lancashire, Cheshire, and\nYorkshire assigned to factory inspector R. Baker numbered 652 factories;\nof these, 570 contained: power looms 85,622, spindles\n(excluding doubling spindles) 6,819,146,\nhorsepower in steam engines 27,439, in water wheels 1390, per-\nsons employed 94,119.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"15.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0026","text":"In the year 1865, on the other hand, the\nsame factories contained: looms 95,163, spindles 7,025,031,\nhorsepower in steam engines 28,925, in water wheels 1445, per-\nsons employed 88,913. From 1860 to 1865, therefore, the in-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"439\", \"facs\": \"#f0458\"} --> crease of these factories in power looms amounted to 11 %, in spindles to 3 %,\nin steam horsepower to 5 %, while at the same time the number of persons employed\ndecreased by 5.5 % [Note: “Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1865,” p. 58 sq. At the same\n time, however, the material basis for the employment of a grow-\ning number of workers was already provided in 110 new factories with 11,625 power looms,\n628,756 spindles, 2695 steam- and water-horsepower. (l. c.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"15.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0027","text":"Between 1852 and 1862, con-\nsiderable growth of English wool manufacture took place, while\nthe number of workers employed remained almost stationary. “This\nshows to what great extent newly introduced machinery had displaced the labour of pre-\nceding periods” [Note: “Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1862,” p. 79.] . In empirically given\ncases, the increase in factory workers employed is often only ap-\nparent, i.e. owing not to the expansion of the factory already based on machine operation,\nbut to the gradual annexation of ancillary\nbranches. For example, “the increase in mechanical looms and in the\nfactory workers employed by them from 1838—1858 was\nin the (British) cotton factory simply owing to the expansion of this\nbranch of business; in the other factories, on the other hand, to the\nnew application of steam power to the carpet, ribbon,\nlinen loom, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"15.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0028","text":"and so forth, which were previously driven by human muscular power” [Note: “Reports etc. for 31st Oct. 1856,” p. 16.] . The increase in these factory workers was thus only\nthe expression of a decrease in the total number of workers em-\nployed. Finally, we leave entirely aside here the fact that everywhere, with\nthe exception of the metal factories, youthful workers (under 18 years), women\nand children form the by far predominant element of factory personnel.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"15.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0029","text":"One understands, however, despite the mass of workers actually dis-\nplaced and virtually replaced by machine operation, how, with its own\ngrowth, expressed in an increased number of factories of the same\nkind or the enlarged dimensions of existing factories, factory\nworkers can ultimately be more numerous than the manufacturing workers or artisans dis-\nplaced by them. Let the capital applied weekly, for example, of £500 consist\nin the old mode of operation of ⅖ constant and ⅗ variable component, i.e. £200\nbe laid out in means of production, £300 in labour-power, say\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"440\", \"facs\": \"#f0459\"} --> £1 per worker. With machine operation, the\ncomposition of the total capital is transformed. It now divides, for example, into ⅘\nconstant and ⅕ variable component, or only £100","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"15.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0030","text":"is laid out in labour-power. Two thirds of the workers previously employed\nare thus dismissed. If this factory operation expands and, other conditions of production remaining the same, the\ntotal capital applied grows from 500 to 1500, 300 workers are now\nemployed, as many as before the industrial revolution. If the\ncapital applied grows further to 2000, 400 workers are em-\nployed, thus ⅓ more than under the old mode of operation. Absolutely, the num-\nber of workers employed has risen by 100; relatively, i.e. in proportion\nto the total capital advanced, it has fallen by 800, since the\ncapital of £2000 would, under the old mode of operation, have employed 1200 instead of\n400 workers. Relative decrease in the number of workers em-\nployed is thus compatible with its absolute increase.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"15.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0031","text":"It\nwas assumed above that with the growth of total capital\nits composition remains constant, because the conditions of production.\nBut it is already known that with every advance of machinery, the constant part of capital,\nconsisting of machinery, raw material, etc., grows, while the variable part\nlaid out in labour-power falls; and it is at the same time known that in no mode of operation is improvement so con-\nstant, and hence the composition of total capital so variable.\nBut this constant change is just as constantly interrupted by\npoints of rest and merely quantitative expansion upon a given tech-\nnological basis. With this, the number of workers employed grows.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"15.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0032","text":"Thus the number of all workers in the cotton, wool,\nworsted, flax, and silk factories of the United Kingdom in 1835 amounted to only\n354,684, whereas in 1861 the number of power weavers alone (of both sexes\nand of the most various age groups from the age of 8 onward) amounted to 230,654.\nCertainly this growth appears less great when one\nconsiders that the British hand cotton weavers, together with the families employed by them, still numbered 800,000 in 1838 [Note: “The sufferings of the hand weavers (of cotton and fabrics mixed with cotton) were the subject of investigation by a royal com-] , quite apart\nfrom those displaced in Asia and on the European continent.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"15.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0033","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"441\", \"facs\": \"#f0460\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"15.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0034","text":"In the few remarks still to be made on this point,\nwe touch in part upon conditions that are purely factual, so to speak exote-\nric, to which our theoretical exposition itself has not yet\nled us.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"15.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0035","text":"So long as machine operation expands in a branch of industry at\nthe expense of traditional handicraft or manufacture, its\nsuccesses are as certain as, say, the success of an army armed with the needle-\ngun against an army of archers would be.\nThis first period, in which the machine first conquers its sphere of operation,\nis decisively important because of the extraordinary profits which it helps to pro-\nduce. These form not only in and for themselves a source of accelerated\naccumulation, but draw a large part of the continually newly formed\nsocial additional capital pressing for new investment into the favoured sphere of\nproduction. The particular advantages of the first period of storm and stress\nare continually repeated in the branches of pro-\nduction into which machinery is newly introduced.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"15.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0036","text":"As soon\nas the factory system has attained a certain breadth of existence and a determinate\ndegree of maturity, as soon as in particular its own technological\nbasis, machinery, is itself again produced by machines,\nas soon as coal and iron extraction, as well as metalworking\nand transport, have been revolutionized, in general the general conditions of production\ncorresponding to large-scale industry have been established, this\nmode of operation acquires an elasticity, a sudden capacity for expan-\nsion by leaps and bounds, which finds limits only in raw material and\nthe market. Machinery causes, on the one hand, a direct multiplication of\nraw material, as, for example, the cotton gin increased cot-\nton production [Note: Other methods by which machinery acts upon the production of raw\nmaterial are mentioned in Book Three.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"15.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0037","text":"On the other hand, the cheapness of the ma-\nchine product and the revolutionized system of transport and communication\nare weapons for conquering foreign markets. By ruining their handi-\n[Note: mission, but although their misery was acknowledged and lamented, the\nimprovement (!) of their condition was left to chance and the passage of time, and one\nmay hope that these sufferings are now (20 years later!) almost (nearly) effaced,\nto which the present great expansion of power looms has in all prob-\nability contributed.” (l. c. p. 15.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"442\", \"facs\": \"#f0461\"} --> craft-like product, machine operation forcibly transforms them into\nfields of production for its raw material. Thus East India was compelled to the produc-\ntion of cotton, wool, hemp, jute, indigo, etc. for Great Britain\n[Note: Cotton exports from East India to Great Britain.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"15.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0038","text":"1846 lbs. 34,540,143.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"15.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0039","text":"1860 lbs. 204,141,168.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"15.39","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0040","text":"1865 lbs. 445,947,600.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"15.40","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0041","text":"Wool exports from East India to Great Britain.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"15.41","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0042","text":"1846 lbs. 4,570,581.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"15.42","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0043","text":"1860 lbs. 20,214,173.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"15.43","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0044","text":"1863 lbs. 20,679,111.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"15.44","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0045","text":"] . The continual “rendering superfluous” of workers in\nthe countries of large-scale industry promotes greenhouse-like emigra-\ntion and colonization of foreign countries, which are transformed into\nplantations for the raw material of the mother country, as Australia, for example, into a\nplantation of wool [Note: Wool exports from the Cape of Good Hope to Great\nBritain.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"15.45","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0046","text":"1846 lbs. 2,958,457.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"15.46","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0047","text":"1860 lbs. 16,574,345.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"15.47","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0048","text":"1865 lbs. 29,220,623.]","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"15.48","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0049","text":"Wool exports from Australia to Great Britain.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"15.49","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0050","text":"1846 lbs. 21,789,346.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"15.50","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0051","text":"1860 lbs. 59,166,616.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"15.51","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0052","text":"1865 lbs. 109,734,261.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"15.52","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0053","text":"] . A new international division of labour, corresponding to the chief seats of machine industry, is created, which transforms one part of the globe into a pre-eminently agricultural field of production for the other part as a pre-eminently industrial field of production. This revolution is connected with revolutions in agriculture, which are not to be further discussed here [Note: The economic development of the United States is itself a product of European, more specifically English, large-scale industry. In their present form they must still always be regarded as a colonial country of Europe.\nCotton exports of the United States to Great Britain\nin lbs.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"15.53","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0054","text":"1846 401,949,393.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"15.54","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0055","text":"1852 765,630,544.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"15.55","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0056","text":"1859 961,707,264.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"15.56","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0057","text":"1860 1,115,890,608.","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"15.57","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0058","text":"] .","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"15.58","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0059","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"443\", \"facs\": \"#f0462\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"15.59","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0060","text":"The enormous capacity of the factory system for expansion by leaps and bounds, and its dependence on the world market, necessarily produce feverish production and the subsequent glutting of markets, with the contraction of which paralysis sets in. The life of industry is transformed into a sequence of periods of medium activity, prosperity, overproduction, crisis, and stagnation. The insecurity and instability to which machine industry subjects the employment, and thereby the conditions of life, of the worker, become normal with this periodic alternation of the industrial cycle. Apart from periods of prosperity, the most violent struggle rages among the capitalists for their individual share of space in the market. This share stands in direct proportion to the cheapness of the product.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"15.60","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0061","text":"Besides the rivalry thereby generated in the use of improved, labour-replacing machinery and new methods of production, a point is reached each time at which cheapening of the commodity by\n[Note: Exports of grain etc. from the United States to\nGreat Britain (1850 and 1862):","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"15.61","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0062","text":"Wheat cwts. 1850 16,202,312.\n1862 41,033,503.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"15.62","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0063","text":"Barley cwts. 1850 3,669,653.\n1862 6,624,800.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"15.63","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0064","text":"Oats cwts. 1850 3,174,801.\n1862 4,426,994.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"15.64","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0065","text":"Rye cwts. 1850 388,749.\n1862 7,108.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"15.65","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0066","text":"Wheat flour cwts. 1850 3,819,440.\n1862 7,207,113.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"15.66","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0067","text":"Buckwheat cwts. 1850 1054.\n1862 19,571.","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"15.67","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0068","text":"Maize cwts. 1850 5,473,161.\n1862 11,694,818.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"15.68","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0069","text":"Bere or Bigg (special variety of barley) cwts. 1850 2039.\n1862 7675.","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"15.69","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0070","text":"Peas cwts. 1850 811,620.\n1862 1,024,722.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"15.70","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0071","text":"Beans cwts. 1850 1,822,972.\n1862 2,037,137.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"15.71","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0072","text":"Total imports cwts.: 1850 34,365,801.\n1862 74,083,351.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"15.72","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0073","text":"]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"444\", \"facs\": \"#f0463\"} --> forcible depression of wages below the value of labour-power is striven for [Note: In an appeal by the workers thrown onto the streets by the Leicester shoe manufacturers through a “lock-out” to the “Trade Societies of England,” July 1866, it says, among other things: “About 20 years ago the shoemaking industry in Leicester was revolutionised by the introduction of riveting instead of sewing. Good wages could then be earned. Soon this new business expanded greatly. Great competition appeared among the various firms as to which could supply the most tasteful article. Shortly afterwards, however, a worse kind of competition arose, namely that of underselling one another in the market (undersell).","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"15.73","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0074","text":"The harmful consequences soon revealed themselves in\nreduction of wages, and so precipitously rapid was the fall in the price of labour that\nmany firms now pay only half the original wage. And\nyet, although wages sink lower and lower, profits seem\nto grow with every alteration in the wage tariff.” — Unfavourable periods of in-\ndustry are themselves used by the manufacturers to make extraordinary profits\nthrough excessive wage reductions, i.e. direct theft from the worker’s most necessary means of subsistence. An example. The matter concerns\nthe crisis in the silk weaving industry at Coventry: “From evidence that I received both\nfrom manufacturers and workers, it follows beyond doubt that wages were curtailed to a\ngreater extent than competition from foreign producers\nor other circumstances necessitated.”","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"15.74","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0075","text":"The majority of the weavers work under a\nwage reduction of 30 to 40 %. A piece of ribbon for which the weaver five years\nearlier received 6 or 7 sh., now brings him only 3 sh. 3 d. or 3 sh. 6 d.;\nother work, formerly paid at 4 sh. and 4 sh. 3 d., now receives only 2 sh. or\n2 sh. 3 d. The wage reduction is greater than is required as a spur to demand.\nIndeed, in many kinds of ribbon the wage\nreduction was not even accompanied by any re-\nduction in the price of the article.” (Report of Commissioner\nF. D. Longe in “Ch. Empl. Comm. V. Rep. 1866,” p. 114, n. 1.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"15.75","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0076","text":"Growth in the number of factory workers is thus conditional upon\nproportionately much more rapid growth of the total capital invested in the fac-\ntories. But this process takes place only within the ebb and flow\nperiods of the industrial cycle. It is, moreover,\nconstantly interrupted by technological progress, which now virtually replaces workers,\nnow actually displaces them. This qualitative change\nin the operation of machinery constantly removes workers from the factory or\ncloses its gate against the fresh stream of recruits, while the merely quanti-\ntative expansion of the factories, alongside those cast out, devours fresh\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"445\", \"facs\": \"#f0464\"} --> contingents. Thus the workers are continually repelled\nand attracted, tossed to and fro, and this amid constant change\nin the sex, age, and skill of those recruited.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"15.76","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0077","text":"The fortunes of the factory worker are best illustrated\nby a rapid survey of the fortunes of the English cotton\nindustry.","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"15.77","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0078","text":"From 1770 to 1815 the cotton industry depressed or stagnant\nfor 5 years. During this first period of 45 years the Eng-\nlish manufacturers possessed the monopoly of machinery and of the world market.\n1815 to 1821 depressed, 1822 and 1823 prosperous, 1824\nrepeal of the Combination Laws, general great expansion\nof the factories, 1825 crisis, 1826 great misery and uprisings among the\ncotton workers, 1827 slight improvement, 1828 great increase in\npower-looms and exports, 1829 exports, especially to In-\ndia, surpass all previous years, 1830 glutted markets, great\ndistress, 1831 to 1833 continuing depression; trade with East\nAsia (India and China) is withdrawn from the monopoly of the East India Company.\n1834 great growth of factories and machinery,\nshortage of hands.","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"15.78","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0079","text":"The new Poor Law promotes the migra-\ntion of agricultural labourers into the factory districts. Sweeping of the rural\ncounties clear of children. White slave trade. 1835 great pros-\nperity. Simultaneous starvation of the cotton hand-weavers. 1836\ngreat prosperity. 1837 and 1838 depressed condition and crisis.\n1839 revival. 1840 great depression, uprisings, inter-\nvention of the military. 1841 and 1842 dreadful suffering of the factory workers.\nIn 1842 the manufacturers lock the hands out of the factories in order to\nforce the repeal of the Corn Laws. The workers stream by many thou-\nsands to Yorkshire, driven back by the military, their leaders brought before\nthe court at Lancaster. 1843 great misery. 1844 re-\nvival. 1845 great prosperity. 1846 first continued up-\nswing, then symptoms of reaction. Repeal of the Corn\nLaws. 1847 crisis.","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"15.79","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0080","text":"General reduction of wages by 10 and\nmore per cent to celebrate the “big loaf.” 1848 continuing depression.\nManchester under military protection. 1849 revival. 1850\nprosperity. 1851 falling commodity prices, low wages, frequent\nstrikes. 1852 beginning improvement, continuance of strikes, manu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"446\", \"facs\": \"#f0465\"} --> facturers threaten to import foreign workers. 1853 rising exports.\nEight-month strike and great misery at Preston. 1854 prosperi-\nty, glutted markets. 1855 reports of bankruptcies pour\nin from the United States, Canada, East Asian markets. 1856\ngreat prosperity. 1857 crisis. 1858 improvement. 1859 great\nprosperity, increase of factories. 1860 zenith of the English cotton\nindustry.","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"15.80","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0081","text":"Indian, Australian, and other markets so overstocked,\nthat even in 1863 they could scarcely absorb the whole mass of rubbish. Franco-\nBritish commercial treaty. Enormous growth of factories and\nmachinery. 1861 the upswing continues for a time, reaction, Ameri-\ncan Civil War, cotton famine. 1862 to 63 complete col-\nlapse.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"15.81","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0082","text":"The history of the cotton famine is too characteristic not to\nlinger over it for a moment. From the indications concerning the\ncondition of the world market in 1860 to 61, one sees that the cotton\nfamine was welcome to the manufacturers and was in part advantageous to them, a\nfact acknowledged in reports of the Manchester Chamber of Commerce, pro-\nclaimed in Parliament by Palmerston and Derby, and confirmed by events [Note: Cf. “Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1862,” p. 30.] . Certainly, in 1861 there were many small establishments among the 2887 cotton factories\nof the United Kingdom. According to the report of Factory\nInspector A. Redgrave, whose administrative district includes 2109\nof those 2887 factories, of the latter 392, or 19%, employed only\nunder 10 steam horsepower, 345, or 16%, 10 and under 20, and\n1372 20 and more horsepower [Note: l. c. p. 19.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"15.82","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0083","text":"The majority of the small factories\nwere weaving mills, erected during the period of prosperity since 1858,\nmostly by speculators, one of whom supplied the yarn, another the machi-\nnery, the third the building, under the management of former\noverlookers or other impecunious persons. These small manufac-\nturers mostly went under. The commercial crisis prevented by the\ncotton scarcity would have brought them the same fate. Although they formed ⅓ of\nthe number of manufacturers, their factories absorbed an incomparably smaller\npart of the capital invested in the cotton industry. As to the ex-\ntent of the paralysis, according to authentic estimates\nin October 1862 60.3% of the spindles and 58% of the looms stood idle.","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"15.83","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0084","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"447\", \"facs\": \"#f0466\"} --> This relates to the whole branch of industry and was naturally greatly\nmodified in the individual districts. Very few factories worked\nfull time (60 hours per week), the rest with interruptions.\nEven for the few workers employed full time and at the customary\npiece-wage, the weekly wage necessarily dwindled as a consequence of\nthe substitution of inferior cotton for superior, of South Sea Island\nby Egyptian (in fine spinning), of American and Egyptian by Surat\n(East Indian), and of pure cotton by mixtures of cotton waste with\nSurat. The shorter fibre of Surat cotton, its dirty character, the\ngreater brittleness of the threads, the substitution of all manner of heavy\ningredients for flour in the sizing of warp yarn, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"15.84","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0085","text":"diminished the speed of the\nmachinery or the number of looms that a weaver could supervise,\nincreased the labour caused by the errors of the machine, and limited\nthe piece-wage with the mass of products. With the use of Surat and\nwith full employment, the worker’s loss amounted to\n20, 30, and more per cent. But the majority of manufacturers also\nreduced the rate of the piece-wage by 5, 7½, and 10 per cent.\nOne can therefore understand the situation of those employed only 3, 3½, or 4 days a week,\nor only 6 hours per day. After a relative improvement had already\noccurred, in 1863, weekly wages for weavers, spinners, etc. were\n3 sh. 4 d., 3 sh. 10 d., 4 sh. 6 d., 5 sh. 1 d., etc. [Note: “Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31st Oct. 1863,” p. 41, 51.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"15.85","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0086","text":"Even under these agonising conditions, the inventive\nspirit of the manufacturer did not stand still in deductions from wages. These were\nin part imposed as punishment for defects in the work owed to his inferior cotton, unsuitable\nmachinery, etc. But where the manufacturer was owner of the workers’ cottages,\nhe reimbursed himself for house rent by deductions from nominal wages. Factory\nInspector Redgrave tells of self-acting minders (they super-\nintend a couple of self-acting mules), who “at the end of a fortnight’s\nfull work earned 8 sh. 11 d., and house rent was deducted\nfrom this sum, of which, however, the manufacturer returned half\nas a gift, so that the minders carried home the full 6 sh. 11 d.\nThe weekly wage of weavers ranged from 2 sh. 6 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"15.86","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0087","text":"up-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"448\", \"facs\": \"#f0467\"} --> wards during the closing period of 1862” [Note: “Reports etc. 31st Oct. 1862,” p. 41, 42.] . “Even then house\nrent was frequently deducted from wages when the hands worked only a short\ntime” [Note: l. c. p. 57.] . No wonder that in some parts of Lancashire\na kind of hunger plague broke out! But more characteristic than all this\nwas how the revolutionising of the process of production\nproceeded at the worker’s expense. They were regular experimenta\nin corpore vili, like those of anatomists on frogs. “Although I,”\nsays Factory Inspector Redgrave, “have given the actual earnings of workers in\nmany factories, it must not be concluded that they receive the\nsame amount week after week.”","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"15.87","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0088","text":"The workers succumb to the\ngreatest fluctuations because of the constant experimenta-\ntion (“experimentalizing”) of the manufacturers … their wages\nrise and fall with the quality of the cotton mixture; at one time\nthey approach their former earnings within 15%, and the next or following\nweek they fall by 50 to 60%” [Note: l. c. p. 50, 51.] . These experiments\nwere not made merely at the expense of the workers’ foodstuffs. They had to pay\nwith all their five senses.","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"15.88","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0089","text":"“Those employed in opening the cot-\nton bales inform me that the unbearable stench makes\nthem ill … Those employed in the mixing, scribbling, and carding rooms are irri-\ntated by the dust and dirt released in all the openings of the head,\ncausing cough and difficulty in breathing … Owing to the shortness of the\nfibre, a great quantity of matter is added to the yarn in sizing,\nnamely all kinds of substitutes instead of the flour formerly used. Hence\nnausea and dyspepsia among the weavers. Bronchitis prevails because of the\ndust, likewise inflammation of the throat, and further a skin disease as a consequence of the irri-\ntation of the skin by the dirt contained in Surat.” On the other hand,\nthe substitutes for flour were a Fortunatus’s purse for the gentlemen manufacturers,\nby increasing the weight of the yarn. They made “15 pounds of raw mate-\nrial, when woven, weigh 20 pounds” [Note: l. c. p. 62, 63.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"15.89","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0090","text":"In the report of the factory\ninspectors of 30 April 1864 one reads: “Industry is making use\nof this resource now to a truly indecent extent. I know on\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"449\", \"facs\": \"#f0468\"} --> good authority that eight-pound cloth is made from 5¼ pounds of cotton\nand 2¾ pounds of sizing. Another cloth weighing 5¼ pounds\ncontained two pounds of sizing. These were ordinary shirtings\nfor export. In other kinds, as much as 50% sizing was sometimes\nadded, so that manufacturers can boast, and really do\nboast, that they grow rich by selling\ncloths for less money than the yarn nominally contained in them\ncosts” [Note: “Reports etc. 30th April 1864,” p. 27.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"15.90","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0091","text":"But the workers had to suffer not only from the ex-\nperiments of the manufacturers in the factories and of the municipalities\noutside the factories, not only from wage reductions and unemploy-\nment, from want and alms, from the eulogies of Lords and members of the House of\nCommons. “Unfortunate women, unemployed as a consequence of\nthe cotton famine, became outcasts of society and re-\nmained so ‥ The number of young prostitutes has increased more than at any time during the\nlast 25 years” [Note: From a letter of Chief Constable Harris of Bolton in “Reports of\nInsp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1865,” p. 61, 62.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"15.91","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0092","text":"Thus, in the first 45 years of the British cotton industry, from 1770—1815, one finds only 5 years of crisis and stagnation, but this was the period of its world monopoly. The second period of 48 years, from 1815—1863, counts only 20 years of revival and prosperity against 28 years of pressure and stagnation. From 1815—1830 competition with continental Europe and the United States begins. Since 1833, expansion of the Asian markets has been enforced by “destruction of the human race”. Since the repeal of the Corn Laws, from 1846—1863, 8 years of moderate liveliness and prosperity, 9 years of pressure and stagnation.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"15.92","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0093","text":"The condition of the adult male cotton workers, even during the period of prosperity, may be judged from the appended note [Note: In an appeal by the cotton workers, spring 1863, for the formation of an emigration society, it says inter alia: “That a great emigration of factory workers is now absolutely necessary, few will deny. But that a constant stream of emigration is required at all times and that without it it is impossible [to maintain our position under ordinary circumstances] .","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"15.93","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0094","text":"We have seen how machinery abolishes the co-operation based on handicraft and the manufacture based on the division of handicraft labour. An example of the first kind is the mowing machine; it replaces the co-operation of mowers. A striking example of the second kind is the machine for manufacturing sewing needles. According to Adam Smith, in his time 10 men, through division of labour, manufactured over 48,000 sewing needles daily. A single machine, on the other hand, supplies 145,000 in a working day of 11 hours. One woman or girl supervises on average 4 such machines and therefore produces by means of the machinery about 600,000, and in the week over 3,000,000 sewing needles [Note: “Ch. Empl. Comm. IV. Report. 1864”, p. 108, n. 447.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"15.94","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0095","text":"Insofar as an individual working machine takes the place of co-operation or manufacture, it can itself again become the basis of handicraft operation. However, this reproduction of handicraft operation based on machinery forms only the transition to factory operation, which as a rule occurs whenever mechanical motive power, steam or water, replaces human muscles in the movement of the machine. Sporadically, and only temporarily, small-scale operation can combine with mechanical motive power through the hiring of steam, as in some manufactures in Birmingham, through the use of small caloric machines, as in certain branches of weaving, etc. [Note: To maintain this, the following facts show: In the year 1814 the official value (which is only an index of quantity) of the exported cotton goods amounted to £17,665,378, their actual market value to £20,070,824. In the year 1858 the official value of the exported cotton goods amounted to £182,221,681, their actual market value to only £43,001,322.","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"15.95","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0096","text":"sterling, so that the tenfold increase of quantity effected little more than a doubling of the equivalent. This result, so ruinous for the country in general and factory workers in particular, was produced by various co-operating causes … One of the most conspicuous is the constant surplus of labour, indispensable for this branch of business, which, on pain of destruction, requires constant expansion of the market. Our cotton factories may be brought to a standstill by the periodic stagnation of trade, which, under the present arrangement, is as unavoidable as death itself. But for that reason the human spirit of invention does not stand still.”","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"15.96","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0097","text":"Although, at a low estimate, 6 million have left this country during the last 25 years, nevertheless, as a consequence of continual displacement of labour in order to cheapen the product, a large percentage of adult men, even in times of the highest prosperity, are unable to find employment of any kind in the factories on any conditions whatever.” (“Reports of Insp. of Fact. 30th April 1863”, p. 51, 52.)","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"15.97","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0098","text":"In a later chapter we shall see how the gentlemen manufacturers, during the cotton catastrophe, sought in every way, even through the state, to prevent the emigration of factory workers.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"451\", \"facs\": \"#f0470\"} --> only temporarily can small-scale operation combine with mechanical motive power through the hiring of steam, as in some manufactures in Birmingham, through the use of small caloric machines, as in certain branches of weaving, etc. [Note: In the United States, such reproduction of handicraft on the technological basis of machinery is frequent. Concentration, in the inevitable transition to factory operation, will for this very reason march in seven-league boots, in comparison with Europe and even with England.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"15.98","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0099","text":"In silk weaving at Coventry, the experiment of “cottage factories” developed spontaneously. In the middle of rows of cottages, built square-wise, a so-called engine house was erected for the steam engine, and this was connected by shafts with the looms in the cottages. In every case the steam was hired, e.g. at 2½ sh. per loom. This steam rent was payable weekly, whether the looms ran or not. Each cottage contained 2—6 looms, belonging to the workers, or bought on credit, or hired. The struggle between the cottage factory and the factory per se lasted over 12 years. It ended with the complete ruin of the 300 cottage factories [Note: Cf. “Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1865”, p. 64.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"15.99","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0100","text":"Where the nature of the process did not from the outset require production on a large scale, the industries newly arising in recent decades, such as envelope-making, steel-pen-making, etc., as a rule passed first through handicraft operation and then manufacture as short-lived transitional phases to factory operation. This metamorphosis remains most difficult where manufacture-like production of the article does not include a sequence of processes of development, but a multiplicity of disparate processes. This formed, for example, a great obstacle to the steel-pen factory. Yet already about one and a half decades ago an automaton was invented which performs 6 disparate processes at one stroke. Handicraft supplied the first 12 dozen steel pens in 1820 for £7","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"15.100","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0101","text":"4 sh., manufacture supplied them in 1830 for 8 sh., and the factory supplies them today to the wholesale trade for 2 to 6 d. [Note: Mr. Gillot established in Birmingham the first steel-pen manufacture on a large scale. Already in 1851 it supplied over 180 million pens and consumed annually 120 tons of sheet steel. Birmingham, which monopolises this industry in the United","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"15.101","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0102","text":"[Running matter: 29*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"452\", \"facs\": \"#f0471\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"15.102","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0103","text":"With the development of the factory system and the revolution in agriculture accompanying it, not only does the scale of production expand in all other branches of industry, but its character also changes. The principle of machine operation, that of analysing the process of production into its constituent phases and solving the problems thereby given through the application of mechanics, chemistry, etc., in short, of the natural sciences, becomes determinant everywhere. Machinery therefore soon forces its way into manufactures for this or that partial process. The fixed crystallisation of their articulation, sprung from the old division of labour, thereby dissolves and gives place to continual change. Apart from this, the composition of the aggregate worker or of the combined personnel of labour is revolutionised from the ground up.","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"15.103","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0104","text":"In contrast to the period of manufacture, the plan of the division of labour is now based upon the employment of women’s labour, the labour of children of all ages, unskilled workers, wherever feasible, in short, upon “cheap labour,” inexpensive labour, as the Englishman characteristically calls it. This applies not only to all production combined on a large scale, whether it employs machinery or not, but also to so-called domestic industry, whether carried on in the private dwellings of the workers or in small workshops. This so-called modern domestic industry has nothing in common with the old-fashioned kind, which presupposes independent urban handicraft, independent peasant economy, and above all a house of the working family, except the name. It has now been transformed into the external department of the factory, manufacture, or warehouse.","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"15.104","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0105","text":"Alongside the factory workers, manufacture workers, and handicraftsmen, whom it concentrates spatially in large masses and directly commands, capital moves, by invisible threads, another army of domestic workers scattered throughout the great towns and over the countryside. Example: the shirt factory of Messrs. Tillie in Londonderry, Ireland, which employs 1,000 factory workers and 9,000 domestic workers scattered over the countryside [Note: “Ch. Empl. Comm. II. Rep. 1864”, p. LXVIII, n. 415.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"15.105","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0106","text":"The exploitation of cheap and immature labour-power is more shameless in modern manufacture than in the factory proper, because the technological basis existing there, replacement of muscular force by machines and ease of labour, for the most part falls away here, while at the same time the female or still immature body is most unscrupulously exposed to the influences of poisonous substances, etc. It is more shameless in so-called [Note: Kingdom, now produces annually billions of steel pens. According to the Census of 1861, the number of persons employed was 1,428, among them 1,268 female workers, enrolled from the 5th year onward.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"453\", \"facs\": \"#f0472\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"15.106","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0107","text":"domestic work than in manufacture, because the workers’ capacity for resistance declines with their dispersal, a whole series of predatory parasites thrusts itself between the actual employer and the worker, domestic work everywhere struggles with machine or at least manufacture operation in the same branch of production, poverty deprives the worker of the most necessary conditions of labour, space, light, ventilation, etc., the irregularity of employment increases, and finally, in these last refuges of those made “supernumerary” by large-scale industry and agriculture, competition among workers necessarily reaches its maximum.","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"15.107","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0108","text":"The economising of the means of production, first systematically developed by machine operation, from the outset at the same time the most ruthless squandering of labour-power and robbery of the normal prerequisites of the labour-function, now displays this antagonistic and man-destroying side all the more sharply, the less the social productive power of labour and the technological basis of combined labour-processes are developed in a branch of industry.","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"15.108","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0109","text":"I shall now illustrate the propositions set out above by a few examples. The reader already knows, in fact, massive evidence from the section on the working day. The metal manufactures in Birmingham and the surrounding area employ, for the most part in very heavy labour, 30,000 children and young persons, together with 10,000 women. They are found here in the unhealthy brass foundries, button factories, glazing, electroplating, and lacquering work [Note: And not to mention children in file-grinding at Sheffield!] . The excesses of labour for adults and minors have earned various London newspaper and book printing establishments the honourable name: “the slaughterhouse” [Note: “Ch. Empl. Comm. V. Rep. 1866”, p. 3, n. 24, p. 6, n. 55, 56, p. 7, n. 59, 60.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"15.109","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0110","text":"The same excesses, whose victims of slaughter are here especially women, girls, and children, occur in bookbinding. Heavy labour for minors in rope-making, night labour in salt works, candle and other chemical factories; murderous consumption of boys in silk weaving not mechanically operated, for turning the looms [Note: l. c. p. 114, 115, n. 6—7. The commissioner rightly remarks that whereas otherwise the machine replaces the human being, here the boy verbatim replaces the machine.] . One of the most infamous, filthy, and poorly paid jobs, for which young girls and women are preferentially employed, is the sorting of rags. It is known that Great Britain, apart from its own countless rags, is the emporium for the rag trade of the whole world.","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"15.110","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0111","text":"They stream thither from Japan, the remotest states of South America, and the Canary Islands. But their principal sources of supply are Germany, France, Russia, Italy, Egypt, Turkey, Belgium, and Holland. They serve for manure, the manufacture of flock (for bedding), shoddy, and as raw material for paper. The female rag-sorters serve as mediums for disseminating smallpox and other contagious diseases, whose first victims they themselves are [Note: See report on the rag trade and numerous proofs: “Public Health. VIII. Report. London 1866.” Appendix, p. 196—208.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":111,"address":"15.111","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0112","text":"As a classic example of overwork, heavy and unsuitable labour, and the consequent brutalisation of workers consumed from childhood onward, alongside mining and coal production, brick or tile making may be cited, for which in England newly invented machinery is still employed only sporadically. Between May and September work lasts from 5 o’clock in the morning until 8 o’clock in the evening, and, where drying takes place in the open air, often from 4 o’clock in the morning until 9 o’clock in the evening. The working day from 5 o’clock in the morning until 7 o’clock in the evening counts as “reduced,” “moderate.” Children of both sexes are employed from the 6th and even the 4th year onward. They work the same number of hours, often more than the adults. The work is hard and the summer heat further increases exhaustion. In a brickworks at Mosley, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":112,"address":"15.112","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0113","text":"a girl of 24 made 2,000 bricks daily, assisted by two under-age girls as helpers, who carried the clay and piled up the bricks. These girls daily dragged 10 tons up the slippery sides of the brick-pit from a depth of\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"455\", \"facs\": \"#f0474\"} --> 30 feet and over a distance of 210 feet. “It is impossible for a child to pass through the purgatory of a brickworks without great moral degradation. . . . The worthless language which they are made to hear from the tenderest age, the filthy, indecent, and shameless habits under which they grow up ignorant and brutalised, make them lawless, reprobate, dissolute for their later life. . . . A frightful source of demoralisation is the manner of dwelling.”","kind":"passage","sequence":113,"address":"15.113","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0114","text":"Every moulder (the actually skilled worker and chief of a work-gang) provides his gang of 7 persons with lodging and board in his hut or cottage. Whether belonging to his family or not, men, boys, and girls sleep in the hut. This generally consists of 2, only exceptionally of 3 rooms, all on the ground floor, with little ventilation. The bodies are so exhausted by the great perspiration during the day that rules of health, cleanliness, or decency are in no way observed. Many of these huts are veritable models of disorder, filth, and dust. . . . The greatest evil of the system which employs young girls in this kind of work consists in the fact that it generally chains them, from childhood onward, to the most depraved rabble for their entire later life.”","kind":"passage","sequence":114,"address":"15.114","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0115","text":"They be-\ncome rough, foul-mouthed boys (“rough, foul-mouthed boys”) before nature has taught them that they are women. Clad in a few dirty rags, their legs bared far above the knee, hair and face smeared with dirt, they learn to treat all feelings of modesty and shame with contempt. During meal-times they lie stretched out in the fields or watch the boys bathing in a neighbouring canal. When their hard day's work is finally accomplished, they put on better clothes and accompany the men to public houses.” That the greatest drunkenness prevails from childhood onward throughout this whole class is only natural. “The worst thing is that the brickmakers despair of themselves.”","kind":"passage","sequence":115,"address":"15.115","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0116","text":"“You could,” said one of the better ones to the chaplain of Southallfields, “just as well try to raise and improve the devil as a brickie, Sir!” (“You might as well try to raise and improve the devil as a brickie, Sir!”) [Note: “Child. Empl. Comm. V. Report. 1866”, XVI, n. 86—97 and p. 130, n. 39—71. Cf. also ib. III. Rep. 1864, p. 48, 56.]","kind":"passage","sequence":116,"address":"15.116","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0117","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"456\", \"facs\": \"#f0475\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":117,"address":"15.117","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0118","text":"On the capitalist economising of the conditions of labour in modern manufacture (by which are here understood all workshops on a large scale, apart from factories proper), one finds official and most abundant material in the IVth (1861) and VIth (1864) “Public Health Report”. The description of the workshops (work premises), especially of London printers and tailors, surpasses the most disgusting fantasies of our novelists. The effect upon the workers' state of health is self-evident.\nDr. Simon, the chief medical officer of the Privy Council and official editor of the “Public Health Reports”, says, inter ali","kind":"passage","sequence":118,"address":"15.118","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0119","text":"a: “In my fourth report (1863) I showed how it is practically impossible for the workers to insist upon what is their first right to health, the right that, whatever work their employer assembles them for, the work shall, so far as it depends on him, be freed from all avoidable circumstances injurious to health. I showed that while the workers are practically unable to procure this health justice for themselves, they can obtain no effective assistance from the appointed administrators of sanitary police. . . . The lives of myriads of male and female workers are now uselessly tortured and shortened by the endless physical suffering which their mere employment produces” [Note: “Public Health”. VI. Rep. Lond. 1864, p. 31.] .\nTo illustrate the influence of work premises upon the state of health Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":119,"address":"15.119","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0120","text":"Simon gives the following mortality list:","kind":"passage","sequence":120,"address":"15.120","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0121","text":"|\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"457\", \"facs\": \"#f0476\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":121,"address":"15.121","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0122","text":"I now turn to so-called domestic labour. In order to form an idea of this sphere of exploitation by capital, erected upon the background of large-scale industry, and of its monstrosities, let us consider, for example, the apparently quite idyllic nail-making carried on in some remote villages of England [Note: This concerns hammered nails as distinct from machine-made cut nails. See “Child. Empl. Comm. III. Report”, p. XI, p. XIX, n. 125—130, p. 53, n. 11, p. 114, n. 487, p. 137, n. 674.] .\nHere a few examples from the branches of lace manufacture and straw-plaiting that are not yet operated by machinery at all, or that compete with machinery and manufacturing industry, will suffice.","kind":"passage","sequence":122,"address":"15.122","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0123","text":"Of the 150,000 persons employed in English lace production, approximately 10,000 fall under the sway of the Factory Act of 1861. The overwhelming majority of the remaining 140,000 are women, young persons, and children of both sexes, although the male sex is only weakly represented. The state of health of this “cheap” exploitation material emerges from the following statement by Dr. Trueman, physician at the General Dispensary of Nottingham. Out of every 686 patients, lace-makers, mostly between 17 and 24 years of age, the following were consumptive:","kind":"passage","sequence":123,"address":"15.123","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0124","text":"1852 1 in 45,","kind":"passage","sequence":124,"address":"15.124","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0125","text":"1853 1 in 28,","kind":"passage","sequence":125,"address":"15.125","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0126","text":"1854 1 in 17,","kind":"passage","sequence":126,"address":"15.126","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0127","text":"1855 1 in 18,","kind":"passage","sequence":127,"address":"15.127","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0128","text":"1856 1 in 15,","kind":"passage","sequence":128,"address":"15.128","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0129","text":"1857 1 in 13,","kind":"passage","sequence":129,"address":"15.129","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0130","text":"1861 1 in 8 [Note: “Child. Empl. Comm. II. Report”, p. XXII, n. 166.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":130,"address":"15.130","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0131","text":"1858 1 in 15,","kind":"passage","sequence":131,"address":"15.131","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0132","text":"1859 1 in 9,","kind":"passage","sequence":132,"address":"15.132","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0133","text":"1860 1 in 8,","kind":"passage","sequence":133,"address":"15.133","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0134","text":"This progress in the rate of consumption must satisfy the most optimistic believer in progress and the most lie-spewing German free-trade pedlar.","kind":"passage","sequence":134,"address":"15.134","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0135","text":"The Factory Act of 1861 regulates lace-making proper, insofar as it takes place by machinery, and this is the rule in England. The branches which we consider here briefly, and indeed not\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"458\", \"facs\": \"#f0477\"} --> insofar as the workers are concentrated in manufactories, warehouses, etc., but only insofar as they are so-called domestic workers, fall into 1) finishing (the final preparation of machine-made lace, a category which in turn includes numerous subdivisions), 2) lace-making on the pillow.","kind":"passage","sequence":135,"address":"15.135","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0136","text":"Lace finishing is carried on as domestic labour either in so-called “Mistresses' Houses” or by women, singly or with their children, in their private dwellings. The women who keep the “Mistresses' Houses” are themselves poor. The work premises form part of their private dwelling. They receive orders from manufacturers, owners of warehouses, etc., and employ women, girls, and young children, according to the size of their rooms and the fluctuating demand of business. The number of female workers employed varies from 20 to 40 in some, from 10 to 20 in other such premises. The average minimum age at which children begin is 6 years, though some are under 5 years old.","kind":"passage","sequence":136,"address":"15.136","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0137","text":"The usual working day lasts from 8 o’clock\nin the morning until 8 o’clock in the evening, with 1½ hours for meals, which are taken irregularly and often in the stinking work-holes themselves.\nWhen business is good, work often lasts from 8 o’clock (sometimes 6 o’clock)\nin the morning until 10, 11, or 12 o’clock at night. In English barracks the prescribed space for each soldier amounts to 500—600 cubic\nfeet, in military hospitals to 1200. In those work-holes\nthere are 67—100 cubic feet for each person. At the same time gaslight consumes the oxygen\nof the air. In order to keep the lace-points clean, the children\noften have to take off their shoes, even in winter, although the floor consists of paving-stones\nor bricks.","kind":"passage","sequence":137,"address":"15.137","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0138","text":"“It is nothing unusual in Nottingham to find 14\nto 20 children pickled together in a small room of perhaps no more than 12\nsquare feet, while occupied for 15 hours out of 24 in work, in itself exhausting through tedium and\nmonotony, moreover performed under every possible health-destroying circumstance … Even the youngest children work with a strained\nattention and speed which are astonishing, almost never\ngranting their fingers rest or slower movement. If\nquestions are put to them, they do not raise their eyes from their work, for fear\nof losing a moment.” The “long stick” serves the “mis-\ntresses” as a means of stimulation in proportion as the working day is pro-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"459\", \"facs\": \"#f0478\"} --> longed.","kind":"passage","sequence":138,"address":"15.138","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0139","text":"“The children gradually grow weary and become as restless as\nbirds towards the end of their long confinement to an occupation,\nmonotonous, trying to the eyes, exhausting through the uniformity of bodily\nposture. It is genuine slave-labour.” (“Their work like sla-\nvery” [Note: “Child. Empl. Comm. II. Report 1864”, p. XIX, XX, XXI.] .) Where women work at home with their own children, i.e. in\nthe modern sense, in a rented room, frequently in an attic,\nconditions are, if possible, even worse. This kind of work is given out\nwithin a radius of 80 miles around Nottingham. When the child employed\nin the warehouses leaves them at 9 or 10 o’clock in the evening,\nit is often given a bundle to take along, in order to finish it\nat home.","kind":"passage","sequence":139,"address":"15.139","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0140","text":"The capitalist Pharisee, represented by\none of his wage-servants, naturally does this with the unctuous phrase:\n‘that is for mother,’ while knowing very well\nthat the poor child must sit up and help [Note: l. c. p. XXI, XXVI.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":140,"address":"15.140","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0141","text":"The industry of lace-making is carried on chiefly in two\nEnglish agricultural districts, the Honiton lace-\ndistrict, 20 to 30 miles along the southern coast of Devonshire, including\na few places in North Devon, and another district, which\ncomprises a large part of the counties of Buckingham, Bedford, Northampton\nand the neighbouring parts of Oxfordshire and Huntingdonshire. The cottages of the agricultural day-labourers form on average the work-\nplaces. Some manufacturing masters employ over 3000 of these home-\nworkers, chiefly children and young persons, exclusively of the female\nsex. The conditions described in lace finishing\nrecur. Only, in place of the “mistresses’ houses” there are the\nso-called “lace schools,” held by poor women\nin their cottages. From the 5th year, sometimes younger, until the 12th,","kind":"passage","sequence":141,"address":"15.141","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0142","text":"or\n15th, the children work in these schools; during the first year the\nyoungest from 4 to 8 hours, later from 6 o’clock in the morning until 8 and 10\no’clock in the evening. “The rooms are generally ordinary living-rooms\nof small cottages, the fireplace stopped up to ward off draughts, the in-\nmates sometimes heated even in winter only by their own animal warmth.\nIn other cases these so-called schoolrooms are rooms resembling small store-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"460\", \"facs\": \"#f0479\"} --> chambers, without a fireplace … The overcrowding of these\nholes and the resulting pollution of the air are often extreme.”","kind":"passage","sequence":142,"address":"15.142","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0143","text":"To this\nis added the harmful effect of gutters, sewers, decaying substances\nand other filth, usually in the approaches to smaller cottages.”\nWith regard to space: “In one lace school 18 girls and the mis-\ntress, 35 cubic feet for each person; in another, where there was an unbearable\nstench, 18 persons, 24½ cubic feet per head. In this\nindustry one finds children employed from 2 and 2½ years old” [Note: l. c. p. XXIX, XXX.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":143,"address":"15.143","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0144","text":"Where lace-making ceases in the rural counties of Buckingham\nand Bedford, straw-plaiting begins. It extends\nover a large part of Hertfordshire and the western and northern\nparts of Essex. In 1861, 40,043 persons were employed in straw-plaiting and straw-\nhat-making, 3815 of them of the male sex, of all\nages, the others of the female sex, namely 14,913 under\n20 years, of whom about 7000 were children. In place of the lace schools\nthere appear here the “straw plait schools” (straw-plaiting schools). The\nchildren here begin instruction in straw-plaiting usually from the 4th,\nsometimes between the 3rd and 4th year. Education they naturally receive\nnone.","kind":"passage","sequence":144,"address":"15.144","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0145","text":"The children themselves call the elementary schools “natural\nschools” in distinction from these blood-sucking establish-\nments, in which they are simply kept at work in order to\nproduce the piece-work prescribed by their half-starved mothers, generally 30 yards\nper day. These mothers then often make them work at home as well\nuntil 10, 11, 12 o’clock at night. The straw cuts their fingers\nand mouth, through which they constantly moisten it. According to the overall view\nsummarised by Dr. Ballard, the medical officers\nof London consider 300 cubic feet the minimum space for each person in\na sleeping or working room.","kind":"passage","sequence":145,"address":"15.145","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0146","text":"In the straw-plait schools, however, space\nis apportioned even more scantily than in the lace schools, “12⅔, 17,\n18½ and under 22 cubic feet for each person.” The smaller of these\nfigures, says Commissioner White, “represent less space than half of what\na child would occupy if packed in a\nbox of 3 feet in every dimension.” This is the enjoyment of life of the children until their 12th or 14th year. The wretched, degraded\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"461\", \"facs\": \"#f0480\"} --> parents think only of getting as much as possible\nout of the children. Grown up, the children naturally care not a whit for\ntheir parents and leave them. “It is no wonder that ignorance\nand vice overflow among a population reared in this manner … Their\nmorality stands at the lowest level . . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":146,"address":"15.146","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0146"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0147","text":"A great number of the women\nhave illegitimate children, and many at so immature an age that even those\nfamiliar with criminal statistics are aghast at it” [Note: l. c. p. XL, XLI.] . And the homeland\nof these model families is, says Count Montalembert, certainly competent in Christianity,\nEurope’s Christian model country!","kind":"passage","sequence":147,"address":"15.147","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0147"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0148","text":"Wages, altogether miserable in the branches of industry just dealt\nwith (the exceptional maximum wage of children in the\nstraw-plait schools being 3 sh.), are further depressed far below their nominal amount\nby the truck system prevailing universally, particularly in the lace districts [Note: “Child. Empl. Comm. I. Rep. 1863”, p. 185.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":148,"address":"15.148","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0148"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0149","text":"The cheapening of labour-power through mere\nabuse of female and immature labour-powers, mere robbery of all normal\nconditions of labour and life, and mere brutality of overtime\nand night-work, ultimately encounters certain natural limits which it cannot overstep, and thus also does the cheapening of commodities and capitalist exploitation\nin general based upon these foundations.\nAs soon as this point is finally reached, and it takes a long time, the hour strikes for the introduction of machinery and the now rapid trans-\nformation of scattered domestic work (or also manufacture) into factory\noperation.","kind":"passage","sequence":149,"address":"15.149","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0149"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0150","text":"The most colossal example of this movement is afforded by the production of\n“Wearing Apparel” (articles belonging to dress). According to the clas-\nsification of the “Child. Empl. Comm.” this industry comprises straw-hat-\nand ladies’-hat-makers, cap-makers, tailors, milliners and dressma-\nkers [Note: Millinery properly relates only to head-dress, but also to\nladies’ cloaks and mantillas, whereas dressmakers are identical with our millin-\ners.] , shirt-makers and seamstresses, corset-, glove-, shoe-\nmakers, together with many smaller branches, such as the manufacture of cravats, neck-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"462\", \"facs\": \"#f0481\"} --> bands, etc. The female personnel employed in these industries in England and Wales\namounted in 1861 to 586,298, of whom at least 115,242 were under\n20, 16,650 under 15 years of age. Number of these female workers in the United Kingdom (1861): 750,334.","kind":"passage","sequence":150,"address":"15.150","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0150"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0151","text":"The number of male workers\nsimultaneously employed in hat-making, shoemaking, glove-making and tailoring\nin England and Wales: 437,969, of whom\n14,964 were under 15 years, 89,285 between fifteen and twenty years old, 333,117\nover 20 years. Many smaller branches belonging here\nare missing from this statement. But if we take the figures as they stand, there results\nfor England and Wales alone, according to the Census of 1861, a\nsum of 1,024,277 persons, thus approximately as many as agriculture and\ncattle-breeding absorb. One begins to understand for what purpose machinery\nhelps to conjure up such enormous masses of products and to “set free” such enormous masses of workers.","kind":"passage","sequence":151,"address":"15.151","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0151"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0152","text":"The production of “Wearing Apparel” is carried on by ma-\nnufactures which reproduced within themselves only the division of labour, the dis-\niecta membra of which they found ready-made, by smaller handicraft masters, who, however, no longer work as formerly for individual con-\nsumers, but for manufactures and warehouses, so\nthat often entire towns and rural districts practise such branches as shoemaking, etc.,\nas a speciality, and finally on the largest scale by so-called home-\nworkers, who form the external department of the manufactures,\nwarehouses, and even the smaller masters [Note: English millinery and dressmaking are for the most part carried on in the build-\nings of the employers, partly by female workers living and engaged there,\npartly by female day-labourers living elsewhere.] . The mass\nof the material of labour, raw materials, semi-finished goods, etc.,","kind":"passage","sequence":152,"address":"15.152","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0152"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0153","text":"is supplied by large in-\ndustry; the mass of cheap human material (taillable à merci et\nmisericorde) by those “set free” through large industry and agriculture.\nThe manufactures of this sphere owed their origin chiefly\nto the capitalist’s need to have at hand a ready army corresponding to every movement of demand [Note: Commissioner White visited a manufacture of military clothing which employed 1000\nto 1200 persons, almost all of the female sex; a shoe manu-\nfactory with 1300 persons, nearly half of them children and young persons,\netc. (“Child. Empl. Comm. II. Rep.”, p. XVII, n. 319.)] . These\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"463\", \"facs\": \"#f0482\"} --> manufactures, however, allowed scattered handicraft and\ndomestic operation to continue alongside them as a broad foundation.","kind":"passage","sequence":153,"address":"15.153","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0153"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0154","text":"The great production\nof surplus-value in these branches of labour, together with the progressive\ncheapening of their articles, was and is chiefly owed to the\nminimum wage necessary for meagre vegetation, combined\nwith the maximum working-time humanly possible. It was precisely the\ncheapness of human sweat and human blood transformed into commodities which constantly expanded the market and expands it daily,\nfor England in particular also the colonial market, where English\ncustom and taste moreover predominate. Finally a nodal point\noccurred. The basis of the old method, mere brutal exploitation of the labour-\nmaterial, more or less accompanied by systematically developed\ndivision of labour, no longer sufficed for the growing market and the still more rapidly\ngrowing competition of the capitalists. The hour of machinery struck.","kind":"passage","sequence":154,"address":"15.154","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0154"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0155","text":"The decisively revolutionary machine,\nwhich seizes all the countless branches of this sphere of production alike, such\nas millinery, tailoring, shoemaking, sewing, hat-making, etc.,\nis—the sewing machine.","kind":"passage","sequence":155,"address":"15.155","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0155"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0156","text":"Its immediate effect upon the workers is approximately that of all\nmachinery which conquers new branches of business in the period of large-scale industry. Children of the most immature age are removed. The wage\nof machine-workers rises proportionately in relation to that of home-\nworkers, many of whom belong to “the poorest of the\npoor.” The wage of the better-situated handicraftsmen with whom\nthe machine competes falls. The new machine-workers are ex-\nclusively girls and young women. With the help of mechanical\npower, they destroy the monopoly of male labour in heavier\nwork and drive masses of old women and immature\nchildren out of lighter work. The overwhelming competition kills off the weakest hand-\nworkers.","kind":"passage","sequence":156,"address":"15.156","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0156"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0157","text":"The greyish growth of death from hunger (death from\n starvation) in London during the last decade runs parallel\n with the expansion of machine sewing [Note: An example. On 26 February 1864 the weekly mortality\n report of the Registrar General contains 5 cases of death from hunger. On the same day\n the Times reports a new case of death from hunger. Six victims of death from hunger\n in one week!] . The new women workers\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"464\", \"facs\": \"#f0483\"} --> at the sewing machine, which is set in motion by them with hand and foot or with the\n hand alone, sitting and standing, according to the weight, size, and speciality\n of the machine, expend great labour-power. Their em-\n ployment becomes injurious to health through the duration of the process, al-\n though it is mostly shorter than in the old system. Everywhere that the sewing ma-\n chine, as in shoe-making, corset-making, hat-making, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":157,"address":"15.157","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0157"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0158","text":"etc., visits already cramped and\n overcrowded workshops, it increases the influences injurious to health.\n “The effect,” says Commissioner Lord, “on entering\n low-ceilinged workrooms, where 30 to 40 machine workers work to-\n gether, is unbearable . . . . The heat, partly owing to the gas stoves used\n for warming the irons, is dreadful . . . . Even where in\n such premises so-called moderate working hours, i.e. from 8 o’clock in the morning\n to 6 o’clock in the evening, prevail, 3 or 4 per-\n sons nevertheless regularly faint every day” [Note: “Child. Empl. Comm.” II. Rep. 1864, p. LXVII, n. 406—9,\n p. 84, n. 124, p. LXXIII, n. 441, p. 66, n. 6, p. 84, n. 126, p. 78, n. 85, p. 76,\n n. 69, p. LXXII, n. 483.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":158,"address":"15.158","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0158"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0159","text":"The revolution in the social mode of production,\n this necessary product of the transformation of the means of pro-\n duction, is accomplished in a motley confusion of transitional forms.\n They vary with the extent to which, and the length of time during\n which, the sewing machine has already seized upon one or another branch of industry;\n with the situation in which the workers are found; with the preponderance of\n manufacture, handicraft, or domestic industry; with the rent of the work-\n rooms [Note: “The rental of premises required for work rooms seems the element\n which ultimately determines the point, and consequently it is in the metropolis,\n that the old system of giving work out to small employers and families has been\n longest retained, and earliest returned to.” (l. c. p. 83, n. 123.) The concluding\n phrase refers exclusively to shoemaking.]  etc. In millinery, for ex-","kind":"passage","sequence":159,"address":"15.159","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0159"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0160","text":"ample, where labour was for the most part already\n organised, chiefly through simple co-operation, the sewing ma-\n chine at first forms merely a new factor of manufacture. In\n tailoring, shirt-making, shoemaking, etc., all forms intercross. Here,\n factory industry proper. There, middlemen receive the raw material\n from the capitalist en chef and group in “chambers” or\n “garrets” 10 to 50 and more wage-workers around sewing machines.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"465\", \"facs\": \"#f0484\"} --> Finally, as with all machinery which does not form an articulated system,\n and is applicable in dwarf form, handicraftsmen or domestic workers,\n with their own families or with the addition of a few outside workers, use sewing\n machines that themselves belong to them [Note: In glove-making, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":160,"address":"15.160","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0160"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0161","text":"etc., where the situation of the workers is scarcely distinguishable from\n that of paupers, this does not occur.] . In fact, in Eng-\n land the system now predominates whereby the capitalist concentrates a\n larger number of machines in his buildings and then distributes the machine product\n among the army of domestic workers for further processing [Note: l. c. n. 122.] . The\n motley character of the transitional forms, however, does not conceal the\n tendency towards transformation into factory industry proper.","kind":"passage","sequence":161,"address":"15.161","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0161"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0162","text":"This tendency is\n nourished by the character of the sewing machine itself, whose manifold\n applicability urges the union of formerly separate branches of business in the\n same building and under the command of the same capital;\n by the circumstance that preliminary needlework and certain other opera-\n tions are most suitably performed at the seat of the machine; and finally,\n by the inevitable expropriation of the handicraftsmen and domestic work-\n ers who produce with machines of their own. This fate has, in part,\n already overtaken them now. The ever-growing mass of capital invested\n in sewing machines [Note: In the boot and shoemaking of Leicester alone, producing for wholesale,\n 800 sewing machines were already in use in 1864.]  spurs production and produces gluts in the market,\n which sound the signal for the sale of sewing machines by the domestic\n workers.","kind":"passage","sequence":162,"address":"15.162","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0162"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0163","text":"The overproduction of such machines itself compels\n their producers, who need a market for them, to hire them out at weekly rent,\n and thereby creates competition fatal to the small owners of machines [Note: l. c. p. 84, n. 124.] . Still continually occurring changes in construction and cheapen-\n ing of the machines likewise constantly depreciate their old speci-\n mens and permit them to be profitably employed only when bought\n in masses, at derisory prices, in the hands of great capitalists. Finally,\n the substitution of the steam engine for the human being, here as in all\n similar processes of revolution, gives the decisive impulse. The application of\n steam power at first encounters purely technical obstacles, such as the shaking\n of the machines, difficulty in controlling their speed,\n [Running matter: I.","kind":"passage","sequence":163,"address":"15.163","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0163"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0164","text":"30]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"466\", \"facs\": \"#f0485\"} --> rapid deterioration of the lighter machines, etc.—obstacles all of which\n experience soon teaches how to overcome [Note: Thus in the Army Clothing Depot at Pimlico, London, in the shirt factory\n of Tillie and Henderson at Londonderry, in the clothing factory of the firm Tait, at\n Limerick, which employs some 1,200 “hands”.] . If, on the one\n hand, the concentration of many working machines in larger manufactures drives towards\n the application of steam power, on the other hand the competition\n of steam with human muscle accelerates the concentration of workers and working\n machines in great factories.","kind":"passage","sequence":164,"address":"15.164","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0164"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0165","text":"Thus England is presently experiencing in\n the colossal sphere of production of “Wearing Apparel,” as in most\n other trades, the revolution of manufacture, handicraft,\n and domestic labour into factory industry, after all those forms, under\n the influence of large-scale industry, thoroughly transformed, disintegrated, dis-\n figured, had long since reproduced and even exaggerated all the monstrosities\n of the factory system without its positive moments of development\n [Note: “Tendency to factory system.” (l. c. p. LXVII.) “The whole\n employment is at this time in a state of transition, and is undergoing the same\n change as that effected in the lace trade, weaving etc.” (l. c. n. 405). “A com-\n plete Revolution.” (l. c. p. XLVI, n. 318.) At the time of the “Child. Empl.\n Comm.” of 1840, stocking-making was still hand labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":165,"address":"15.165","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0165"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0166","text":"Since 1846\n machinery of various kinds has been introduced, now driven by steam. The\n total number of persons of both sexes and of all ages, from the age of 3 upwards,\n employed in English stocking-making amounted in 1862 to approximately\n 120,000 persons. Of these, according to Parliamentary Return of 11 February 1862,\n only 4,063, however, were under the sway of the Factory Act.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":166,"address":"15.166","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0166"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0167","text":"This industrial revolution, proceeding spontaneously, is artificially\n accelerated by the extension of the Factory Acts to all branches of in-\n dustry in which women, young persons, and children work. The compul-\n sory regulation of the working day as to length, pauses, beginning and\n end point, the system of relays for children, the exclusion of all chil-\n dren below a certain age, etc., necessitate, on the one hand, increased\n machinery [Note: Thus, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":167,"address":"15.167","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0167"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0168","text":"in pottery the firm of Cochrane of the “Britain\n Pottery, Glasgow” reports: “To keep up our quantity, we have gone extensively into\n machines wrought by unskilled labour, and every day convinces us that we\n can produce a greater quantity than by the old method.” (“Reports of Insp.]  and the replacement of muscles by steam as motive\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"467\", \"facs\": \"#f0486\"} --> power [Note: Thus, after the introduction of the Factory Act into pottery, a great increase in\n power jiggers in place of handmoved jiggers.] . On the other hand, in order to gain in space what is lost in time,\n there occurs an expansion of the means of production jointly employed—the\n kilns, buildings, etc.; in a word, greater concentration of the means of\n production and a correspondingly greater conglomeration of workers.","kind":"passage","sequence":168,"address":"15.168","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0168"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0169","text":"The passionately repeated chief objection of every manufacture threatened\n with the Factory Act is in fact the necessity of a greater outlay of capital\n in order to continue the business on its old scale. But as regards the\n intermediate forms between manufacture and domestic labour, and the latter\n itself, their ground sinks away with the limitation of the working day and\n of child labour. Boundless exploitation of cheap labour-powers\n forms the sole foundation of their competitive capacity.","kind":"passage","sequence":169,"address":"15.169","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0169"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0170","text":"An essential condition of factory industry, especially once it is subject\n to regulation of the working day, is normal certainty of the re-\n sult, i.e. production of a definite quantity of commodities or of an\n intended useful effect within a given period. The legal pauses of the\n regulated working day further presuppose a sudden and periodical\n suspension of labour without detriment to the work in process of pro-\n duction. This certainty of the result and interrupt-\n ibility of labour are naturally more easily attainable in purely mechanical\n trades than where chemical and other physical processes play a part,\n as, for example, in pottery, bleaching, dyeing, baking, and most\n metal manufactures.","kind":"passage","sequence":170,"address":"15.170","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0170"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0171","text":"With the routine of the unrestricted working-\n day, night labour, and free devastation of human beings, every spon-\n taneously arising obstacle soon counts as an eternal “natural barrier” to pro-\n duction. No poison exterminates vermin more surely than the Factory Act does such\n “natural barriers.” No one cried louder about “impossibilities” than\n the gentlemen of the pottery trade. In 1864 the Factory Act\n was imposed upon them, and all impossibilities had already vanished\n 16 months later. “The” “improved method of making slip by pressure\n instead of by evaporation, the new construction of the kilns for drying\n the fresh ware, etc.,” called forth by the Factory Act, “are events of great\n importance in the art of pottery and denote\n [Note: of Fact. 31. Oct. 1865”, p. 13.) “The effect of the Factory Act is to drive further\n introduction of machinery.” (l. c. p.","kind":"passage","sequence":171,"address":"15.171","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0171"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0172","text":"13, 14.)]\n [Running matter: 30*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"468\", \"facs\": \"#f0487\"} --> a progress in it such as the last century cannot show … The temperature of\n the kilns is considerably reduced, with a considerable decrease in the consumption\n of coal and more rapid effect upon the ware” [Note: l. c. p. 96 and 127.] . Despite all prophecy, the cost price\n of earthenware did not rise, but the mass of products did, so that the export\n of the 12 months from December 1864 to December 1865 yielded an excess\n in value of £138,628 over the average of the three preceding\n years. In the manufacture of matches it was held to be a law of nature\n that boys, even while gulping down their midday\n meal, should dip the matches into a warm phosphorous composition, whose poi-\n sonous vapour rose into their faces.","kind":"passage","sequence":172,"address":"15.172","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0172"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0173","text":"With the necessity of\n economising time, the Factory Act (1864) compelled the introduction of a “dipping machine”\n (immersion machine), whose vapours cannot reach the worker\n [Note: The introduction of this and other machinery into the match factory\n has in one department of it replaced 230 young persons by 32 boys and\n girls from 14 to 17 years of age. This saving of workers was carried further\n in 1865 through the application of steam power.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":173,"address":"15.173","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0173"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0174","text":"Thus it is now asserted in those branches of lace manufacture not yet subject\n to the Factory Act that meal-times could not be regular “because of the different\n lengths of time which different lace materials require for drying, and which\n vary from 3 minutes to an hour and more.” To this the Commissioners\n of the “Children’s Employment Comm.” reply: “The circumstances are\n exactly the same as in wallpaper printing. Some of the principal manufacturers\n in this branch urged vehemently that the nature of the materials employed and the diver-\n sity of the processes through which they pass did not permit, without great\n loss, any sudden suspension of labour for meals. . . . By\n the 6th clause of the 6th","kind":"passage","sequence":174,"address":"15.174","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0174"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0175","text":"section of the Factory Act’s Extension Act\n (1864), they were granted a period of eighteen months from the date of the\n enactment of the Act, after the expiry of which they would have to conform to the refreshment\n pauses specified by the Factory Act [Note: “Child. Empl. Comm. II. Rep. 1864”, p. IX, n. 50.] . Hardly had\n the law received parliamentary sanction when the gentlemen manufacturers\n also discovered: “The inconveniences which we expected from the introduction of the Factory\n Act have not arisen. We do not find that the\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"469\", \"facs\": \"#f0488\"} --> production is in any way paralysed. In fact, we produce more in\n the same time” [Note: “Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1865”, p. 22.] . One sees that the English Parliament, to which surely\n no one will impute genius, has through experience arrived at the insight\n that a coercive law can remove all so-called","kind":"passage","sequence":175,"address":"15.175","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0175"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0176","text":"natural obstacles to production can simply be dictated away against the restriction and regulation of the working day. When the Factory Act is introduced into a branch of industry, a period of 6 to 18 months is therefore set, within which it is the manufacturers’ business to clear away the technical obstacles. Mirabeau’s: “Impossible! Ne me nommez jamais cet imbécil de mot!” applies especially to modern technology.","kind":"passage","sequence":176,"address":"15.176","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0176"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0177","text":"But while the Factory Act thus hothouse-ripens the material elements necessary for the transformation of manufacture into factory production, it simultaneously accelerates, through the necessity of increased capital outlay, the downfall of the smaller masters and the concentration of capital [Note: “The necessary improvements cannot be introduced into many old manufactories without an outlay of capital beyond the means of many present owners … A temporary disorganisation necessarily accompanies the introduction of the Factory Acts. The extent of this disorganisation stands in direct proportion to the magnitude of the abuses to be remedied.” (l. c. p. 96, 97.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":177,"address":"15.177","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0177"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0178","text":"Apart from the purely technical obstacles and those removable by technical means, the regulation of the working day encounters the irregular habits of the workers themselves, especially where piece-wages prevail and the wasting of time in one part of a day or week can be made up for by subsequent overtime or night-work, a method which brutalises the adult worker and ruins his immature and female companions [Note: In the blast-furnaces, for example, “work towards the end of the week is generally much increased in duration, in consequence of the habit of the men of idling on Monday and occasionally during a part or the whole of Tuesdays also.” (“Child. Empl. Comm. IV. Rep.”, p. VI.) “The little masters generally have very irregular hours.”","kind":"passage","sequence":178,"address":"15.178","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0178"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0179","text":"They lose 2 or 3 days, and then work all night to make it up …\nThey always employ their own children, if they have any.” (l. c. p. VII.) “The\nwant of regularity in coming to work, encouraged by the possibility and practice\nof making up for this by working longer hours.” (l. c. p. XVIII.) “Enormous\nloss of time in Birmingham … idling part of the time, slaving the rest.” (l. c.\np. XI.)] . Although this irregularity in the expenditure of labour-power is a spontaneously crude reaction against the tedium of monotonous labour-drudgery, it arises to an incomparably greater degree from the anarchy of production itself, which in turn presupposes the unbridled exploitation of labour-power by capital.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"470\", \"facs\": \"#f0489\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":179,"address":"15.179","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0179"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0180","text":"Alongside the general periodic vicissitudes of the industrial cycle and the particular fluctuations of the market in every branch of production, there appear especially the so-called season, whether it rests on the periodicity of the seasons favourable to navigation or on fashion, and the suddenness of large orders to be executed within the shortest time. The habit of the latter expanded with railways and telegraphy. “The extension of the railway system,” says, for example, a London manufacturer, “throughout the country has greatly encouraged the habit of short orders. Buyers now come from Glasgow, Manchester, and Edinburgh once every 14 days or so to the City wholesale warehouses, to which we supply the goods. They give orders which must be executed immediately, instead of buying from stock, as was customary.”","kind":"passage","sequence":180,"address":"15.180","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0180"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0181","text":"“In earlier years we were always able to work in advance during the slack time for the demand of the next season, but now no one can predict what will then be in demand” [Note: “Child. Empl. Comm. IV. Rep.”, p. XXXII, XXXIII. “The extension of the railway system is said to have contributed greatly to this custom of giving sudden orders, and the consequent hurry, neglect of mealtimes, and late hours of the work people.” (l. c.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":181,"address":"15.181","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0181"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0182","text":"In factories and manufactures not yet subject to the Factory Act, the most terrible overwork prevails periodically during the so-called season, spasmodically as a result of sudden orders. In the external department of the factory, manufacture, and warehouse, in the sphere of domestic labour, already thoroughly irregular, wholly dependent for its raw material and its orders on the whims of the capitalist, who here is bound by no consideration for the valorisation of buildings, machinery, etc., and who here risks nothing but the skin of the workers themselves, there is thus systematically reared a constantly available industrial reserve army, decimated during one part of the year by the most inhuman compulsion to work, during the other reduced to vagrancy by lack of work. “The employers,” says the “Child. Empl.”","kind":"passage","sequence":182,"address":"15.182","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0182"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0183","text":"Comm.”, “exploit\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"471\", \"facs\": \"#f0490\"} --> the habitual irregularity of domestic labour in order, at times when extra work is necessary, to force it up to 11, 12, 2 o’clock at night—indeed, as the standing phrase has it, to every hour—and this in premises ‘where the stench is enough to knock you down’ (‘the stench is enough to knock you down’). You perhaps go as far as the door and open it, but recoil in horror from proceeding further” [Note: “Child. Empl. Comm. II. Rep.”, p. XXXV, n. 235 and 237.] . “They are funny fellows, our employers,” says one of the examined witnesses, a shoemaker, “they think it does a lad no harm if he is worked to death during one half of the year and during the other half is almost compelled to loaf about” [Note: l. c. p. 127, n. 56.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":183,"address":"15.183","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0183"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0184","text":"Like the technical obstacles, these so-called “business customs” (“usages which have grown with the growth of trade”) were and are asserted by interested capitalists to be “natural barriers” to production; this was a favourite cry of the cotton lords at the time when the Factory Act first threatened them. Although their industry depends more than any other on the world market and hence on shipping, experience gave them the lie. Since then, every alleged “business obstacle” has been treated by the English factory inspectors as hollow nonsense [Note: “With respect to the loss of trade by the non-completion of shipping\norders in time, I remember that this was the pet argument of the factory masters\nin 1832 and 1833.”","kind":"passage","sequence":184,"address":"15.184","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0184"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0185","text":"Nothing that can be advanced now on this subject could have\nthe force that it had then, before steam had halved all distances and established\nnew regulations for transit. It quite failed at that time of proof when put to the\ntest, and again it will certainly fail should it have to be tried.” (“Reports of\nInsp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1862”, p. 54, 55.] .\nThe thoroughly conscientious investigations of the “Child. Empl.\nComm.” prove, in fact, that in some industries the mass of labour already employed would merely be distributed more evenly throughout the whole year by the regulation of the working day [Note: “Child. Empl. Comm. IV. Rep.”, p. XVIII, n.","kind":"passage","sequence":185,"address":"15.185","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0185"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0186","text":"118.] , that the latter is the first rational curb upon the murderous, contentless, and in themselves inappropriate caprices of fashion fluttering about under the system of large-scale industry [Note: John Bellers already remarked in 1699: “The uncertainty of fashions does in-\ncrease necessitous Poor. It has two great mischiefs in it: 1st) The journeymen are mi-] , that the development of oceanic shipping and of the means of communication in general has abolished the properly technical basis of seasonal labour [Note: “Child. Empl. Comm. V. Rep.”, p. 171, n. 31.] , that all other allegedly uncontrollable circumstances are removed by additional buildings, supplementary machinery, an increased number of workers employed simultaneously [Note: So, for example, it is said","kind":"passage","sequence":186,"address":"15.186","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0186"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0187","text":"in the testimony of Bradford export merchants:\n“Under these circumstances it is clear that lads need not be employed longer than from 8 o’clock in the morning until 7 or 7½ o’clock in the evening in the warehouses. It is merely a question of extra outlay and extra hands. The lads would not need to work so late into the night if some employers were not so hungry for profit; an extra machine costs only £16 or £18. … All difficulties arise from insufficient equipment and lack of space.” (l. c. p. 171, n. 35 and 38.)] and by the consequential repercussion upon the system of wholesale trade [Note: l. c.","kind":"passage","sequence":187,"address":"15.187","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0187"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0188","text":"A London manufacturer, who moreover regards the compulsory regulation of the working day as a means of protecting the workers against the manufacturers and the manufacturers themselves against wholesale trade, testifies:\n“The pressure in our business is caused by the shippers, who, for example, wish to dispatch goods by sailing ship in order to be on the spot for a particular season and at the same time pocket the difference in freight between sailing ship and steamship, or who choose the earlier of two steamships in order to appear before their competitors on the foreign market.” (l. c.\np. 8, n. 32.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":188,"address":"15.188","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0188"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0189","text":"However, capital understands, as it has repeatedly declared through the mouth of its representatives, that such a revolution can take place “only under the pressure of a general Act of Parliament” [Note: “This could be obviated”, says a manufacturer, “at the expense of an enlargement of the works under the Pressure of a General Act of Parliament.” (l. c. p. X, n. 38.)] , which regulates the working day by coercive law.","kind":"passage","sequence":189,"address":"15.189","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0189"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0190","text":"Factory legislation, this first conscious and planned\n[Note: serable in winter for want of work, the mercers and master - weavers not daring to\nlay out their stocks to keep the journeymen imployed before the spring comes and\nthey know what the fashion will then be; 2dly) In the spring the journeymen are\nnot sufficient, but the master-weavers must draw in many practices, that they may\nsupply the trade of the kingdom in a quarter or half a year, which robs the plow\nof hands, drains the country of labourers, and in a great part stocks the city with\nbeggars, and starves some in winter that are ashamed to beg.” (“Essays about\nthe Poor, Manufactures etc.”, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":190,"address":"15.190","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0190"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0191","text":"9.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"473\", \"facs\": \"#f0492\"} -->reaction of society upon the spontaneously developed form of its process of production, is, as we have seen, just as much a necessary product of large-scale industry as cotton yarn, self-actors, and the electric telegraph. Before proceeding to its impending generalisation in England, a few clauses of the English Factory Act not relating to the number of hours in the working day must be briefly mentioned.","kind":"passage","sequence":191,"address":"15.191","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0191"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0192","text":"Apart from their wording, which facilitates their evasion by the capitalist, the health clauses are extremely meagre, in fact limited to prescriptions for whitewashing and a few other measures of cleanliness, ventilation, and protection against dangerous machinery. We shall return in the third book to the fanatical struggle of the manufacturers against the clause which imposes upon them a small expenditure for protecting the limbs of their “hands.” Here once again the free-trade dogma splendidly proves itself: that in a society of antagonistic interests, everyone promotes the common good by pursuing his self-interest. One example suffices. It is known that during the last twenty-year period the flax industry, and with it the scutching mills (factories for beating and breaking flax), in Ireland have greatly multiplied.","kind":"passage","sequence":192,"address":"15.192","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0192"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0193","text":"There were about 1,800 of these mills there in 1864. Periodically in autumn and winter, chiefly young persons and women, the sons, daughters, and wives of the neighbouring small tenants, all persons wholly unfamiliar with machinery, are withdrawn from agricultural labour in order to feed flax into the rollers of the scutching mills. The accidents are altogether unprecedented in the history of machinery in extent and intensity. A single scutching mill at Kildinan (near Cork) counted from 1852 to 1856 six deaths and 60 serious mutilations, all of which could have been prevented by the simplest contrivances, at a cost of a few shillings. Dr. W. White, the certifying surgeon of the factories at Downpatrick, declares in an official report of 15 December 1865: “The accidents in scutching mills are of the most dreadful kind.”","kind":"passage","sequence":193,"address":"15.193","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0193"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0194","text":"In many cases one quarter of the body is torn from the trunk. Death, or a future of miserable incapacity and suffering, are the usual consequences of the wounds. The increase of factories in this country will naturally extend these appalling results. I am convinced that, by appropriate state supervision of the scutching mills, great sacrifices of limb and life are to be avoided” [Note: l. c. p. XV, n. 72 sqq.] . What could better characterise the capitalist mode of production than the necessity of imposing upon it, by coercive law from the state, the most elementary provisions of cleanliness and health?\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"474\", \"facs\": \"#f0493\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":194,"address":"15.194","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0194"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0195","text":"“The Factory Act of 1864 has whitewashed and cleansed more than 200 workshops in the potteries, after twenty years’ or complete abstention from every such operation (this is the ‘abstinence’ of capital!), in places where 27,800 workers are employed and had hitherto, during excessive day, often night, work, inhaled a mephitic atmosphere which impregnated an otherwise comparatively harmless occupation with sickness and death. The Act has greatly increased the means of ventilation” [Note: “Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1865”, p. 96.] . At the same time, this branch of the Factory Act strikingly demonstrates how the capitalist mode of production, by its very nature, excludes every rational improvement beyond a certain point.","kind":"passage","sequence":195,"address":"15.195","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0195"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0196","text":"It has repeatedly been observed that the English physicians with one voice declare 500 cubic feet of air-space per person to be a scarcely sufficient minimum for continued work. Very well! If the Factory Act indirectly accelerates, through all its coercive measures, the transformation of smaller workshops into factories, and hence indirectly encroaches upon the property right of the smaller capitalists and secures the monopoly to the large ones, then the legal enforcement of the necessary air-space for each worker in the workshop would directly expropriate thousands of small capitalists at a stroke! It would attack the root of the capitalist mode of production, i.e. the self-expansion of capital, whether large or small, through the “free” purchase and consumption of labour-power. Before these 500 cubic feet of air, the Factory legislation therefore runs out of breath.","kind":"passage","sequence":196,"address":"15.196","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0196"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0197","text":"The “Board of Health,” the industrial commissions of inquiry, and the factory inspectors repeat again and again the necessity of the 500 cubic feet and the impossibility of imposing them upon capital. Thus they in fact declare consumption and other diseases of the lungs resulting from labour to be a condition of life for capital [Note: Experience has shown that approximately 25 cubic inches of air\nare consumed by a healthy average indi-] .","kind":"passage","sequence":197,"address":"15.197","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0197"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0198","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"475\", \"facs\": \"#f0494\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":198,"address":"15.198","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0198"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0199","text":"Miserable as the educational clauses of the Factory Act appear as a whole, they proclaimed elementary instruction as a compulsory condition of labour [Note: Under the English Factory Act, parents cannot send children under 14 years of age to the “controlled” factories without at the same time having them receive elementary instruction. The manufacturer is responsible for compliance with the law. “Factory education is compulsory, and it is a condition of labour.” (“Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1865”, p. 111.)] . Their success proved, first, the possibility of combining instruction and gymnastics [Note: On the advantageous results of combining gymnastics (for boys, military exercises as well) with compulsory instruction for factory children and pauper-school pupils, see the speech by N. W. Senior at the 7th","kind":"passage","sequence":199,"address":"15.199","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0199"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0200","text":"annual congress of the “National Association for the Promotion of Social Science” in “Report of Proceedings etc. Lond. 1863”, pp. 63, 64; likewise the report of the factory inspectors for 31 Oct. 1865, pp. 118, 119, 120, 126 sqq.] with manual labour, and thus also of manual labour with instruction and gymnastics. From the examinations of schoolmasters, the factory inspectors soon discovered that factory children, although they receive only half as much instruction as regular day pupils, learn just as much and often more. “The matter is simple. Those who spend only half a day at school are always fresh and almost always able and willing to receive instruction.”","kind":"passage","sequence":200,"address":"15.200","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0200"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0201","text":"The system of half work and half school makes each of the two occupations a rest and recreation from the other, and consequently far more suitable for the child than the uninterrupted continuance of either. A boy who sits at school from early morning, and especially in hot weather, cannot possibly compete with another who comes from his work cheerful and alert” [Note: “Reports of Insp. of Fact.” l. c. p. 118. A naive silk manufacturer declares to the commissioners of inquiry of the “Child. Empl. Comm.”:] . Further evidence is to be found in Senior’s\n[Note: vidual, and approximately 20 respirations per minute take place. The air consumption of an individual in 24 hours would accordingly amount to approximately 720,000 cubic inches, or 416 cubic feet.","kind":"passage","sequence":201,"address":"15.201","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0201"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0202","text":"But it is known that air once inhaled cannot serve the same process again before it has been purified in the great workshop of nature. According to the experiments of Valentin and Brunner, a healthy man appears to exhale approximately 1,300 cubic inches of carbonic acid per hour; this would yield approximately 8 ounces of solid carbon, thrown off by the lungs in 24 hours. “Every man ought to have at least 800 cubic feet.” (Huxley)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"476\", \"facs\": \"#f0495\"} --> speech at the sociological congress in Edinburgh, 1863. There he also shows, among other things, how the one-sided, unproductive, and prolonged school day of children of the upper and middle classes uselessly increases the labour of teachers, “while it wastes the time, health, and energy of the children not only fruitlessly, but in an absolutely injurious manner” [Note: Senior l. c. p. 66.","kind":"passage","sequence":202,"address":"15.202","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0202"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0203","text":"How large-scale industry, at a certain stage of development, also revolutionises minds by revolutionising the material mode of production and the social relations of production, is strikingly shown by a comparison between N. W. Senior’s speech of 1863 and his philippic against the Factory Act of 1833, or by a comparison of the views of the aforementioned congress with the fact that in certain rural parts of England poor parents are still forbidden, on pain of death by starvation, to educate their children. Thus, for example, Mr. Snell reports as common practice in Somersetshire that, when a poor person applies for parish relief, he or she is compelled to take the children out of school. Thus Mr. Wollaston, clergyman at Feltham, tells of cases in which all support was refused to certain families “because they sent their boys to school”!] .","kind":"passage","sequence":203,"address":"15.203","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0203"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0204","text":"From the factory system, as can be traced in detail in Robert Owen, there sprang the germ of the education of the future, which will combine productive labour with instruction and gymnastics for all children above a certain age, not only as a method for increasing social production, but as the sole method for producing fully developed human beings.","kind":"passage","sequence":204,"address":"15.204","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0204"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0205","text":"We have seen that large-scale industry technologically abolishes the manufacturing division of labour with its lifelong annexation of an entire human being to a detail operation, while at the same time the capitalist form of large-scale industry reproduces that division of labour still more monstrously: in the factory proper, by transforming the worker into the self-conscious appendage of a detail-machine; everywhere else, partly through the sporadic use of machines and machine labour [Note: “I am thoroughly convinced that the true secret of producing capable workers has been found in the combination of labour with instruction from the period of childhood onward. Naturally, the labour must be neither too strenuous nor repulsive and unhealthy. I should wish my own children to have labour and play as a change from school.” (“Child. Empl. Comm.” V. Rep.,\np.","kind":"passage","sequence":205,"address":"15.205","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0205"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0206","text":"82, n. 36.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"477\", \"facs\": \"#f0496\"} --> [Note: Where handicraft machines, driven by human power, compete directly or indirectly with developed machinery and thus with machinery presupposing mechanical motive power, a great transformation occurs with regard to the worker who drives the machine. Originally the steam engine replaced this worker; now he is to replace the steam engine. The strain and expenditure of his labour-power therefore become monstrous, and all the more so for minors condemned to this torture! Thus Commissioner Longe found in Coventry and its vicinity boys aged 10 to 15 employed in turning ribbon looms, apart from younger children who had to turn looms of smaller dimensions. “It is extraordinarily laborious work. The boy is a mere substitute for steam-power.” (“Child.","kind":"passage","sequence":206,"address":"15.206","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0206"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0207","text":"Empl. Comm. V. Rep. 1866”, p. 114, n. 6.) On the murderous consequences of “this system of slavery,” as the official report calls it, l. c. sq.] , and partly through the introduction of women’s, children’s, and unskilled labour as the new foundation of the division of labour. The contradiction between the manufacturing division of labour and the nature of large-scale industry asserts itself violently. It appears, among other things, in the terrible fact that a great part of the children employed in modern factories and manufactures, riveted from the tenderest age to the simplest manipulations, are exploited for years without learning any labour that would later make them useful even in the same manufacture or factory. In English printing works, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":207,"address":"15.207","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0207"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0208","text":"there formerly took place a transition of apprentices from lighter to more substantial work corresponding to the system of the old manufacture and handicraft. They went through a course of training until they became finished printers. Being able to read and write was for all a requirement of the trade. All this changed with the printing machine. It employs two kinds of workers: an adult worker, the machine overseer, and machine boys, mostly from 11 to 17 years old, whose business consists exclusively in feeding a sheet of paper into the machine or withdrawing the printed sheet from it. In London especially, they perform this drudgery for 14, 15, 16 hours uninterruptedly on several days of the week, and often for 36 hours in succession with only two hours’ respite for meals and sleep [Note: l. c. p. 3, n. 24.] !","kind":"passage","sequence":208,"address":"15.208","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0208"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0209","text":"A great part of them cannot read, and as a rule they are wholly wild, abnormal creatures. “To fit them for their work, no intellectual training of any kind is necessary; they have little opportunity for skill and still less for judgement; their wage, although in some measure high for boys, does not grow proportionately as they themselves grow up, and the great majority have no prospect of the more lucrative and responsible post of machine overseer, because there is only one overseer, and often 4 boys, to each machine” [Note: l. c. p. 7, nn. 59, 60.] . As soon as they become too old for their childish work, that is, at least in their seventeenth year, they are dismissed from the printing works. They become recruits for crime.","kind":"passage","sequence":209,"address":"15.209","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0209"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0210","text":"Some attempts to procure employment for them elsewhere failed because of their ignorance, coarseness, physical and mental degradation.","kind":"passage","sequence":210,"address":"15.210","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0210"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0211","text":"What holds for the manufacturing division of labour within the workshop holds for the division of labour within society. So long as handicraft and manufacture form the general basis of social production, the subsumption of the producer under an exclusive branch of production, the tearing apart of the original diversity of his occupations [Note: “In some parts of the Highlands of Scotland … many shepherds and cotters appeared, according to the Statistical Account, with wife and child, in shoes they had themselves made from leather they had themselves tanned, in clothes that no hand but their own had touched, the material for which they had themselves shorn from the sheep or had themselves grown as flax.”","kind":"passage","sequence":211,"address":"15.211","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0211"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0212","text":"“Hardly any purchased article entered into the preparation of the clothes, except awl, needle, thimble, and very few parts of the ironware used in weaving. The dyes were obtained by the women themselves from trees, shrubs, and herbs, etc.” (Dugald Stewart l. c. p. 327.)] , is a necessary moment of development. On that basis, each particular branch of production empirically finds the technological form corresponding to it, slowly perfects it, and rapidly crystallises it as soon as a certain degree of maturity has been attained. What brings about change here and there is, apart from new material of labour supplied by trade, the gradual alteration of the instrument of labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":212,"address":"15.212","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0212"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0213","text":"Once the empirically appropriate form has been attained, it too ossifies, as its often millennia-long passage from the hand of one generation into that of another proves. It is characteristic that down to the\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"479\", \"facs\": \"#f0498\"} --> 18th century the particular trades were called mysteries (mystères) [Note: In the famous “Livre des métiers” of Etienne Boileau, it is prescribed, among other things, that a journeyman, upon his admission among the masters, take an oath “to love his brothers fraternally, to support them, each in his métier, i.e.","kind":"passage","sequence":213,"address":"15.213","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0213"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0214","text":"not voluntarily to reveal the secrets of the trade, and even in the interest of the community not to draw the buyer’s attention to the faults of the work of others in order to recommend his own wares.”]\ninto whose obscurity only the empirically and professionally initiated could penetrate. Large-scale industry tore aside the veil that concealed from human beings their own social process of production and made the different naturally distinct branches of production into riddles for one another and even for the person initiated in each branch. Its principle of resolving every process of production in and for itself, and initially without any regard to the human hand, into its constituent elements, created the wholly modern science of technology.","kind":"passage","sequence":214,"address":"15.214","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0214"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0215","text":"The motley, apparently disconnected and ossified forms of the social process of production dissolved into consciously planned and, according to the intended useful effect, systematically distinct applications of natural science. Technology likewise discovered the few great fundamental forms of motion in which all productive activity of the human body necessarily proceeds, despite all the diversity of the instruments employed, just as mechanics is not deceived by the greatest complication of machinery as to the constant repetition of the simple mechanical powers. Modern industry never regards and treats the existing form of a process of production as definitive.","kind":"passage","sequence":215,"address":"15.215","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0215"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0216","text":"Its technological basis is therefore\n revolutionary, while that of all earlier modes of production was essentially con-\n servative [Note: “The bourgeoisie cannot exist without constantly revolutionising the instruments of production,\n hence the relations of production, hence all social relations.\n The unchanged preservation of the old mode of production, on the other hand, was the first condition of existence of all earlier industrial classes.\n The constant revolutionising of production, the uninterrupted shaking\n of all social conditions, eternal uncertainty and movement distinguish\n the bourgeois epoch from all earlier ones. All fixed, rusted rela-\n tions, with their train of time-honoured ideas and views] .","kind":"passage","sequence":216,"address":"15.216","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0216"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0217","text":"By means of machines, chemical processes, and other me-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"480\", \"facs\": \"#f0499\"} --> thods, it constantly revolutionises, together with the technical basis of material\n production, the functions of the workers and the social combi-\n nations of the labour process. It thereby just as constantly revolu-\n tionises the division of labour within society and incessantly hurls\n masses of capital and masses of workers from one branch of production\n into another. The nature of large-scale industry therefore entails change\n of labour, flux of function, all-round mobility of the worker.\n On the other hand, in its capitalist form it reproduces\n the old division of labour with its ossified particularities.","kind":"passage","sequence":217,"address":"15.217","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0217"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0218","text":"One has\n seen how this absolute contradiction abolishes all repose, firmness, secu-\n rity in the worker’s condition of life, constantly threatens to strike from his hand,\n together with the instrument of labour, the means of subsistence [Note: “You take my life\n When you do take the means whereby I live.” (Shakespeare.)]  and, together with his partial\n function, to make him himself superfluous; how this contradiction\n rages in the uninterrupted sacrificial feast of the working class, the most immoderate squandering\n of labour-powers, and the devastations of social anarchy.\n This is the negative side.","kind":"passage","sequence":218,"address":"15.218","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0218"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0219","text":"But if the change of labour\n now asserts itself only as an overwhelming natural law and with the blindly destruc-\n tive effect of a natural law, which everywhere encounters obsta-\n cles [Note: A French worker writes on his return from San Fran-\n cisco: “I should never have believed that I was capable of practising all those trades\n which I pursued in California. I was firmly convinced that, except for\n printing, I was good for nothing . . . . Once in the midst of this world of adven-\n turers, who change their trade more readily than their shirt, upon my word!\n I did as the others did. Since the business of mining did not prove sufficiently\n profitable, I left it and went to the city, where I became successively a typo-\n grapher, roofer, lead-caster, etc.”","kind":"passage","sequence":219,"address":"15.219","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0219"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0220","text":"As a result of this experience, of\n being fit for all kinds of work, I feel less like a mollusc and more\n like a human being.” (A. Corbon: “De l’enseignement professionnel.”\n 2ème éd. p. 50.)] , large-scale industry, through its catastrophes themselves,\n [Note: are dissolved, all newly formed ones become antiquated before they can ossify. Everything\n estate-like and fixed evaporates, everything holy is profaned, and human beings\n are finally compelled to look upon their positions in life, their mutual relations,\n with sober eyes.” (F. Engels and Karl Marx: “Mani-\n festo of the Communist Party. Lond. 1848”, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":220,"address":"15.220","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0220"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0221","text":"5.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"481\", \"facs\": \"#f0500\"} --> makes it a question of life or death to recognise the change of labours, and hence\n the greatest possible versatility of the worker, as a general social\n law of production, and to reshape conditions in accordance with its normal\n realisation. It makes it a question of life or death to replace\n the monstrosity of a miserable, available working population held in reserve\n for the changing needs of capital’s exploitation by the absolute availability of the human be-\n ing for changing requirements of labour; to replace the partial individual, who\n is the mere bearer of a social detail-function, by the\n totally developed individual, for whom social functions\n are but so many different modes of activity.","kind":"passage","sequence":221,"address":"15.221","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0221"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0222","text":"A moment\n naturally developed on the basis of large-scale industry in this process of transfor-\n mation is polytechnic and agronomic schools; another\n is the “écoles d’enseignement professionnel”, in which\n the children of workers receive some instruction in technology and in the practical\n handling of the various instruments of production. If factory legislation,\n as the first concession wrested from capital with difficulty, combines only elementary\n instruction with factory labour, there can be no doubt that the inevitable conquest of political\n power by the working class will also win for technological instruction,\n theoretical and practical, its place in workers’ schools.","kind":"passage","sequence":222,"address":"15.222","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0222"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0223","text":"There is just as little doubt that the capitalist\n form of production and the economic workers’ relations corresponding to it\n stand in the most diametrical contradiction to such ferments of trans-\n formation and to their aim, the abolition of the old\n division of labour. Yet the development of the contradictions of a\n historical form of production is the only historical path\n of its dissolution and reformation. “Ne sutor ultra crepidam”!, this\n nec plus ultra of handicraft wisdom, became a terrible piece of folly\n from the moment when the clockmaker Watt invented the steam-engine, the\n barber Arkwright the power-loom, and the jeweller-worker Fulton the\n steamship [Note: John Bellers, a true phenomenon in the history of political\n economy, already understood, at the end of the 17th","kind":"passage","sequence":223,"address":"15.223","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0223"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0224","text":"century, with the utmost clarity, the\n necessary abolition of the present education and division of labour, which\n produce hypertrophy and atrophy at both extremes of society, albeit in] .","kind":"passage","sequence":224,"address":"15.224","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0224"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0225","text":"[Running matter: I. 31]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"482\", \"facs\": \"#f0501\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":225,"address":"15.225","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0225"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0226","text":"Insofar as factory legislation regulates labour in factories, manufactories,\n etc., this initially appears merely as interference with capital’s rights of ex-\n ploitation. Every regulation of so-called domestic la-\n bour [Note: This, moreover, is for the most part also carried on in smaller workshops,\n as we have seen in lace manufacture and straw-plaiting, and as could be shown more fully,\n especially in the metal manufactures in Sheffield, Birmingham, etc.]  on the other hand at once presents itself as a direct intervention in patria\n potestas, i.e., modernly interpreted, in parental autho-\n rity, a step before which the sensitive English Parliament long affected\n to recoil.","kind":"passage","sequence":226,"address":"15.226","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0226"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0227","text":"The force of facts, however, finally compelled\n recognition that large-scale industry, together with the economic basis\n of the old family system and the family labour corresponding to it, also\n dissolves the old family relations themselves. The right of children\n had to be proclaimed. “Unfortunately,” it says in the final\n report of the “Child. Empl.","kind":"passage","sequence":227,"address":"15.227","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0227"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0228","text":"Comm.” of 1866, “it is evident from the totality\n of the testimony that children of both sexes need protection against\n no one so much as against their parents.” The system of the immoderate ex-\n ploitation of child labour in general and domestic labour\n in particular is thereby “maintained by the parents’ exercising an arbi-\n trary and pernicious power, without curb or control, over\n their young and tender offspring … Parents must not possess the absolute power\n to make their children into mere machines for extracting\n so and so much weekly wage … Children and young persons\n have a right to the protection of legislation against the abuse of\n parental power, which prematurely breaks their physical strength and degra-\n des them on the scale of moral and intellectual beings” [Note: “Child. Empl. Comm. V. Rep.”, p. XXV, n. 162 and II. Rep.,\n p.","kind":"passage","sequence":228,"address":"15.228","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0228"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0229","text":"XXXVIII, n. 285, 289, p. XXXV, n. 191.] . It is\n [Note: in the opposite direction. He says, among other things, beautifully: “An idle learning\n being little better than the Learning of Idleness … Bodily Labour, it’s a\n primitive institution of God . . . . Labour being as proper for the bodies health,\n as eating is for its living; for what pains a man saves by Ease, he will find in\n Disease … Labour adds oyl to the lamp of life when thinking inflames it …\n A childish silly employ (this, prophetically, against the Basedows and their mo-\n dern bungling imitators), leaves the children’s minds silly.” (“Proposals for\n raising a Colledge of Industry of all useful Trades and Husban-\n dry. Lond. 1696”, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":229,"address":"15.229","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0229"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0230","text":"12, 14, 18.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"483\", \"facs\": \"#f0502\"} --> however, not the abuse of parental power which created the direct or\n indirect exploitation of immature labour-powers by capital; rather,\n conversely, it is the capitalist mode of exploitation which has made\n parental power, by abolishing its corresponding economic\n foundation, into an abuse. However terrible and repugnant\n the dissolution of the old family system within the capitalist\n system may appear, large-scale industry nevertheless, through the decisive role\n it assigns to women, young persons, and children\n of both sexes in socially organised processes of production beyond the sphere\n of the household, creates the new economic foundation for a higher\n form of the family and of the relation between the sexes.","kind":"passage","sequence":230,"address":"15.230","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0230"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0231","text":"It is naturally just as absurd to regard the Christian-Germanic\n form of the family as absolute as the ancient Roman form, or the\n ancient Greek, or the Oriental, which moreover form a historical\n series of development among themselves. It is equally evident that the com-\n position of the combined working personnel from individuals of both sexes\n and of the most various age levels, although in its naturally evolved, brutal,\n capitalist form, where the worker exists for the production\n process, not the production process for the worker, it is a pestilential source\n of corruption and slavery, must, under corresponding circumstances, con-\n versely become a source of humane development [Note: “Factory labour may be as pure and as excellent as domestic labour,\n and perhaps more so.” (“Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1865”,\n p. 127.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":231,"address":"15.231","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0231"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0232","text":"The necessity of generalising factory legislation from an exceptional law\n for spinning and weaving mills, these first formations of machine production,\n into a law of all social production, springs, as was seen, from the\n historical course of development of large-scale industry, against whose background\n the traditional form of manufacture, handicraft, and domestic labour is completely\n revolutionised: manufacture constantly changes into factory production,\n handicraft constantly into manufacture, and finally the spheres of handicraft\n and domestic labour, in a relatively wonderfully short time, take shape as dens of misery,\n where the maddest monstrosities of capitalist exploitation have free\n play.","kind":"passage","sequence":232,"address":"15.232","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0232"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0233","text":"Two circumstances are decisive in the end: first,\n the ever newly repeated experience that capital, as soon as it falls prey to\n [Running matter: 31*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"484\", \"facs\": \"#f0503\"} --> state control only at individual points on the social periphery,\n compensates itself all the more immoderately at the other points [Note: l. c. p. 27, 32.] ;\n secondly, the capitalists’ own cry for equality of the conditions of com-\n petition, i.e. equal barriers to the ex-\n ploitation of labour [Note: Massive evidence for this in the “Rep. of Insp. of Fact.”] . Let us hear two outbursts from the heart on this matter. The\n Messrs. W. Cooksley (manufacturers of nails, chains, etc. in Bristol) volun-\n tarily introduced factory regulation into their business.","kind":"passage","sequence":233,"address":"15.233","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0233"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0234","text":"“Since the old,\n irregular system continues in the neighbouring works, they\n are exposed to the unfairness of seeing their working boys enticed to continue work\n elsewhere after 6 o’clock in the evening. ‘This,’ they naturally say, ‘is\n an injustice to us and a loss, since it exhausts a part of the boys’\n strength, the full benefit of which belongs to us’ [Note: “Child. Empl. Comm. V. Rep.”, p. IX, n. 35.] . Mr J. Simpson (Paper-Box Bag maker,\n London) declares to the commissioners of the “Child. Empl. Comm.”: “He\n would sign every petition for the introduction of the Factory Acts. As it is,\nhe always felt restless at\n night, after the closing of his workshop, at the thought that others\n let work continue longer and snatch orders from under his nose” [Note: l. c. n. 28.] .\n “It would be an injustice,” says the Child. Empl. Comm.","kind":"passage","sequence":234,"address":"15.234","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0234"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0235","text":"in summary,\n “to subject the larger employers of labour to regulation of their factories,\n while in their own branch of business small-scale production is subject to no\n legal restriction of working time. To the injustice of unequal conditions of competition\n with respect to working hours, in the event of exempting smaller workshops,\n would be added the other disadvantage for the\n larger manufacturers that their supply of youthful and female\n labour would be diverted to the workshops spared by the law.\n Finally, this would give an impulse to the multiplication of smaller work-\n shops, which almost without exception are the least favourable for health, com-\n fort, education, and the general improvement of the people” [Note: l. c. n. 165—167. Cf. on the advantages of large-scale enterprise compared\n with dwarf enterprise “Child. Empl. Comm. III. Rep.”, p. 13, n. 144,\n p. 25, n. 121, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":235,"address":"15.235","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0235"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0236","text":"26, n. 125, p. 27, n. 140 etc.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":236,"address":"15.236","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0236"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0237","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"485\", \"facs\": \"#f0504\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":237,"address":"15.237","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0237"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0238","text":"In its final report, the “Children’s Employment” Commis-\nsion proposes to subject more than 1,400,000 children, young persons, and women, of whom approximately half are exploited by small-scale enterprise and domestic labour, to the\nFactory Act [Note: The branches of industry to be regulated are: lace manufacture, stocking-\nknitting, straw-plaiting, the manufacture of wearing apparel with its numerous\nvarieties, artificial-flower making, shoe-, hat-, and glove-making,\ntailoring, all metal factories, from blast furnaces to needle factories, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":238,"address":"15.238","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0238"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0239","text":"etc.,\npaper manufacture, glass manufacture, tobacco manufacture, India-rubber works, heddle-\nmanufacture (for weaving), hand-carpet weaving, umbrella and parasol-\nmanufacture, manufacture of spindles and bobbins, printing, bookbinding,\nstationery trade (Stationery, including the manufacture of paper-\nboxes, cards, paper-dyeing, etc.), rope-making, manufacture of jet ornaments,\nbrickworks, hand silk manufacture, Coventry weaving, salt-, tallow-candle-, and\ncement works, sugar refining, biscuit-making, various wood and other\nmiscellaneous work.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":239,"address":"15.239","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0239"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0240","text":"“Should,” it says, “Parliament adopt our\nproposal in its full extent, there is no doubt that\nsuch legislation would exert the most beneficial influence, not only\nupon the young and weak, with whom it is immediately concerned,\nbut upon the still greater mass of adult workers who fall directly\n(the women) and indirectly (the men) within its sphere of action.”","kind":"passage","sequence":240,"address":"15.240","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0240"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0241","text":"It would impose regular and moderate working hours upon them;\nit would bring about a healthier and cleaner condition of the workplaces;\nit would economise and accumulate the stock of physical strength upon which its own\nwell-being and that of the country so greatly depend;\nit would protect the rising generation from over-exertion at an early\nage, which undermines their constitution and leads to premature\ndecay; it would finally, at least up to the 13th year, offer the op-\nportunity of elementary instruction and thereby put an end to the incredible ig-\nnorance which is so faithfully de-\npicted in the Commission reports and can only be regarded with the most painful feeling and the profound sense\nof national degradation” [Note: l. c. p. XXV, n. 169.] . The Tory ministry\nannounced in the Speech from the Throne of the 5th","kind":"passage","sequence":241,"address":"15.241","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0241"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0242","text":"February 1867 that it had formulated the proposals\nof the industrial commission of inquiry into “Bills.” For this,\na new twenty-year experimentum in corpore vili was required.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"486\", \"facs\": \"#f0505\"} --> As early as 1840 a parliamentary commission had been appointed for the in-\nvestigation of child labour. Its report of 1842 un-\nfolded, in the words of N. W.","kind":"passage","sequence":242,"address":"15.242","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0242"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0243","text":"Senior, “the most terrible picture of\navarice, selfishness, and cruelty on the part of capitalists and parents, of\nmisery, degradation, and destruction of children and young persons,\nthat ever struck the eye of the world … One may perhaps suppose that the report\ndescribes the horrors of an age gone by … These horrors continue,\nmore intensely than ever … The abuses denounced in 1842 stand\nat the present day (October 1863) in full bloom … The report of\n1842 was consigned to the files without further notice, and there it lay for\ntwenty full years, during which the children physically, mentally, and morally\ntrampled down were allowed to become the parents of the present generation” [Note: Senior l. c. p. 55 sqq.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":243,"address":"15.243","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0243"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0244","text":"The present commission of inquiry likewise proposes\nnew regulation of the mining industry [Note: The latest Blue Book on mines: “Report from the Select\nCommittee on Mines together with etc. Evidence. 23. July 1866”,\nis a thick folio volume, but contains only the examination of witnesses. The report of the\nCommittee itself, appointed by the House of Commons out of its own bosom, consists of 5,\nsay five lines, to the effect that it knows nothing to say and that still more (!)\nwitnesses must be examined. In the mining industry, it should be noted, the\ninterests of the landlords and industrial capitalists go hand in hand. The\nmanner of examining witnesses recalls the cross-examinations before the English\ncourts, where the advocate seeks, by impudent, bewildering questions in every direction,\nto throw the witness off his guard and to twist the words in his mouth.","kind":"passage","sequence":244,"address":"15.244","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0244"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0245","text":"The advocates here are the parliamentary examiners\nthemselves; among them mine-owners and exploiters, the witnesses mine-\nworkers, mostly in coal mines. The whole farce is too characteristic\nof the spirit of capital for us not to give some extracts here. Beforehand\nit should also be noted that by the Act of 1842 the labour of the female sex and of all children under 10 years of age in the mines was prohibited. A\nnew Act, “The Mines Inspecting Act” of 1860, prescribes, in addition to mine\ninspection etc., that boys between 10 and 12 years of age shall not be employed\nif they do not have a school certificate or attend school for a specified\nnumber of hours. The entire law is a nullity, if only because of the ridiculously small number of inspectors, the insignificance of their powers,\nand other circumstances which will be seen in the course of what follows.","kind":"passage","sequence":245,"address":"15.245","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0245"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0246","text":"For easier survey] . Finally Professor\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"487\", \"facs\": \"#f0506\"} -->Fawcett introduced similar resolutions in the House of Commons (1867) on behalf of agricultural\nlabourers, but the Cabinet nevertheless took the initiative.","kind":"passage","sequence":246,"address":"15.246","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0246"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0247","text":"[Note: I give the results of the inquiry etc. under headings. I recall that\nquestion and obligatory answer in the English Blue Books are numbered, and that\nthe witnesses whose statements are cited here are workers in coal mines.\n1) Employment of boys from their 10th year in the mines.\nThe labour, together with the obligatory journey to and from the mines, as a rule\nlasts 14 to 15 hours, exceptionally longer, from 3, 4, 5 o’clock in the morning until 4 and 5\no’clock in the evening. (n. 6, 452, 83.) The adult workers labour in two\nshifts, each 8 hours, but there is no such alternation for the boys, in order to\nsave costs. (n. 80, 203, 204.) The young children are chiefly employed\nin opening and closing the ventilation doors in the various sections of the\nmine, the older ones in heavy work, transport of coal, etc. (n. 122,\n739, 1747.)","kind":"passage","sequence":247,"address":"15.247","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0247"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0248","text":"The long hours of labour underground continue until the 18th or 22nd\nyear, when the transition to actual mining labour takes place. (n. 161.)\nChildren and young persons are nowadays more harshly\nworked to death than in any earlier period. (n. 1663—67.) The mine-\nworkers demand almost unanimously an Act of Parliament prohibiting mine labour\nuntil the 14th year. And now Vivian Hussey (himself a mine exploiter) asks:\n“Does not this demand depend upon the greater or lesser poverty of the parents?\nWould it not be hard, where the father is dead, or maimed etc., to deprive the family\nof this resource? Yet there must be a general rule. Would you in all cases prohibit the employment of children below the age of 14 underground?” Answer: “In all cases.” (n. 107—110.)","kind":"passage","sequence":248,"address":"15.248","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0248"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0249","text":"Hussey:\n“If work in the mines were prohibited before 14 years, would not the parents put the\nchildren into factories etc.? — As a rule, no.” (n. 174.)\nWorker: “Opening and shutting the doors looks easy. It is a\nvery tormenting occupation. Apart from the constant draught, the boy is imprisoned,\njust as surely as in a dark dungeon cell.” Bourgeois Hussey:\n“Can the boy not read while keeping watch at the door, if he has a light? —\nFirst he would have to buy the candles. But besides, he would not be allowed to do so. He is there to attend to his business; he has a duty\nto perform. I have never seen a boy reading in the pit.” (n. 141—60.)\n2) Education. The mineworkers demand a law for compulsory in-\nstruction of children, as in the factories.","kind":"passage","sequence":249,"address":"15.249","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0249"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0250","text":"They declare the clause of the Act of\n1860, according to which a certificate of education is required for the employment of those aged 10—12,\nto be purely illusory. The “painful” examination procedure of the capitalist in-\nstruction judges becomes here truly comical. (n. 115.) “Is the Act more necessary\nagainst employers or parents? — Against both.” (n. 116.) “More against the\none than the other? — How am I to answer that?” (n. 137.) “Do\nthe employers show any desire to adapt working hours to school instruction? —]","kind":"passage","sequence":250,"address":"15.250","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0250"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0251","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"488\", \"facs\": \"#f0507\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":251,"address":"15.251","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0251"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0252","text":"If the generalisation of factory legislation has become unavoidable as a physical\nand mental means of protection for the working class,\n[Note: adapt? — Never.” (n. 211.) “Do mineworkers subsequently improve their\neducation? — They deteriorate in general; they acquire bad habits;\ntake to drink and gambling and the like, and become\ncompletely shipwrecked.” (n. 109.) “Why not send the children to evening\nschools? — In most coal districts none exist. But the main point is that, from long overwork, they are so exhausted that their\neyes close with fatigue.” “So,” concludes the bourgeois, “you are against\neducation? — By no means, but etc.” (n. 443.) “Are the mine-\nowners etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":252,"address":"15.252","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0252"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0253","text":"not compelled by the Act of 1860 to demand school certificates when the children are between 10 and 12 years old? — By the law, yes,\nbut the employers do not do it.” (n. 444.) “In your opinion, then, is this\nlegal clause not generally carried out? — It is not carried out at all.”\n(n. 717.) “Are the mineworkers greatly interested in the question of education? — The\ngreat majority.” (n. 718.) “Are they anxious for enforcement\nof the law? — The great majority.” (n. 720.) “Why then do they not enforce\nthe law? — Many a worker wishes to refuse a boy without a school certificate,\nbut he becomes a marked man.” (n.\n721.) “Marked by whom? — By his employer.” (n. 722.) “You surely do not\nthink that the employers would prosecute a man for obedience to the\nlaw? — I believe they would do so.” (n. 723.)","kind":"passage","sequence":253,"address":"15.253","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0253"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0254","text":"“Why\nwill the workers not refuse to employ such boys? — It is not left to their\nchoice.” (n. 1634.) “You demand parliamentary intervention? — If anything effective is to be done for the education of the children of pit workers,\nit must be made compulsory by Act of Parliament.”\n(n. 1636.) “Should this apply to the children of all workers in Great Britain or\nonly to pit workers? — I am here to speak in the name of the pit workers.”\n(n. 1638.) “Why distinguish pit children from others? —\nBecause they form an exception to the rule.” (n. 1639.) “In what re-\nspect? — In a physical one.” (n. 1640.) “Why should education be more valu-\nable to them than to boys of other classes?","kind":"passage","sequence":254,"address":"15.254","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0254"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0255","text":"— I do not say that it is more valu-\nable for them, but because of their overwork in the mines they have fewer\nopportunities for education in day and Sunday schools.” (n. 1644.) “Is it not\ntrue that it is impossible to treat questions of this kind absolutely?” (n. 1646.)\n“Are there enough schools in the districts? — No.” (n. 1647.) “If the\nState required that every child be sent to school, where then are the schools\nfor all the children to come from? — I believe that, as soon as circumstances require it,\nthe schools will spring up of themselves. The great majority not only of the\nchildren, but of the adult mineworkers, can neither write nor read.”\n(n. 705, 726.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"489\", \"facs\": \"#f0508\"} --> on the other hand, as already indicated, it generalises and accelerates\nthe transformation of scattered labour processes on a dwarf scale into com-\n[Note: 3) Women’s labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":255,"address":"15.255","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0255"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0256","text":"Women workers have indeed not since 1842 been employed\nunder, but above ground for loading coal etc., hauling\nthe tubs to the canals and railway wagons, sorting coal etc. Their em-\nployment has increased greatly during the last 3 to 4 years.\n(n. 1727.) They are mostly wives, daughters, and widows of pit workers, from\n12 to 50 and 60 years of age. (n. 645, 1779.) (n. 648.) “What do the\nmineworkers think of female employment at mines? — They condemn\nit universally.” (n. 649.) “Why? — They regard it as degrading for the\nsex … They wear a kind of men’s clothing. In many cases all\nshame is suppressed. Some women smoke. The work is as dirty as\nthat in the pits themselves. Among them are many married women, who cannot fulfil their domestic\nduties.” (n. 651 sqq.) (n. 709.)","kind":"passage","sequence":256,"address":"15.256","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0256"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0257","text":"“Can\nthe widows find such a remunerative occupation (8—10 sh. weekly) elsewhere?\n— I can say nothing about that.” (n. 710.) “And yet (heart of stone!)\nyou are resolved to cut off this livelihood from them? — Certainly.”\n(n. 1715.) “Whence this sentiment? — We mineworkers have too much\nrespect for the fair sex to see them condemned to the coal pit …\nThis work is for the most part very heavy. Many of these girls lift 10 tons\nper day.” (n. 1732.) “Do you believe that women workers employed in mines\nare more immoral than those employed in factories? — The\npercentage of the bad is greater than among factory girls.” (n. 1733.)\n“But you are not satisfied either with the state of morality in the factories?\n— No.” (n. 1734.) “Then do you also wish to prohibit women’s labour in the factories?”}]}േശ```? Wait final malformed has extra. Need correct. We accidentally ending `}]}...`. Ensure valid only JSON. Need resend final. +#+#+#+#+#+】【。assistant final  大发娱乐{","kind":"passage","sequence":257,"address":"15.257","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0257"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0258","text":"— No, I do not want to.” (n. 1735.) “Why not? — It is more honourable and more suitable for\n the female sex.” (n. 1736.) “Nevertheless,\n you think it is harmful to their morality? — No, by far not so much as\n work in the pit. Moreover, I speak not only on moral, but\n also on physical and social grounds. The social degradation of the girls\n is deplorable and extreme. When these girls become wives of miners,\n the men suffer deeply from this degradation, and it drives them away\n from home and into drink.” (n. 1737.) “But would not the same apply to women\n employed in ironworks? — I cannot speak for other branches\n of business.” (n. 1740.) “But what difference is there between\n women employed in ironworks and in mines? — I have\n not occupied myself with this question.” (n. 1741.)","kind":"passage","sequence":258,"address":"15.258","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0258"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0259","text":"“Can you discover any difference\n between the one class and the other? — I have not ascertained anything about it,\n but through visits from house to house I know the disgraceful state\n of things in our district.” (n. 1750.) “Would you not have a great desire to abolish women’s\n employment everywhere where it is degrading? — Yes … the best]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"490\", \"facs\": \"#f0509\"} --> combined labour-processes on a large, social scale, the con-\n centration of capital and the factory regime themselves. It destroys all\n [Note: The feelings of children must come from maternal discipline.” (n. 1751.)\n “But that also applies to agricultural employment of women? — That lasts only\n two seasons; with us they work through all 4 seasons, sometimes day and night,\n wet to the skin, their constitution weakened, their health broken.”\n (n. 1753.)","kind":"passage","sequence":259,"address":"15.259","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0259"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0260","text":"“You have not studied the question (namely that of women’s employment) in a\n general way? — I have looked about me, and can say this much,\n that nowhere have I found anything parallel to female employment in coal mines.”\n “It is men’s work and work for strong men.”\n “The better class of miners, which seeks to raise and humanise itself,\n instead of finding any support in their wives, is dragged down\n by them.” After the bourgeois have questioned him further crosswise and in every direction,\n at last the secret of their “compassion” for widows, poor families,\n etc., comes out: “The coal owner appoints certain gentlemen to super-\n vision, and it is their policy, in order to win approval, to put everything on the most\n economical footing possible, and the girls employed receive 1 to 1 sh.\n 6 d. daily, where a man would have to receive 2 sh. 6 d.”","kind":"passage","sequence":260,"address":"15.260","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0260"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0261","text":"would have to receive.” (n. 1816.)\n 4) Coroners’ juries. (n. 360.) “With reference to the coroner’s\n inquests in your districts, are the workers satisfied with the judicial pro-\n cedure when accidents occur? — No, they are not.” (n. 361.) “Why\n not? — In particular because people are made jurors who know absolutely\n nothing about mines. Workers are never called in, except as witnesses. In general,\n shopkeepers from the neighbourhood are taken, who stand under the influence of the mine-\n owners, their customers, and do not even understand the technical expressions of\n the witnesses. We demand that miners form a part of the jury.\n On average, the verdict stands in contradiction to the testimony of the wit-\n nesses.” (n. 378) “Should juries not be impartial? — Yes.” (n. 379.)\n “Would the workers be so? — I see no motives why they should not be impar-\n tial.”","kind":"passage","sequence":261,"address":"15.261","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0261"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0262","text":"They have expert knowledge.” (n. 380.) “But would they\n not have the tendency to deliver unjustly harsh judgments in the interest of the\n workers? — No, I do not believe so.”\n 5) False measure and weight, etc. The workers demand\n weekly instead of fortnightly payment, measurement by weight instead of by the cubic\n capacity of the tubs, protection against the use of false weight, etc.\n (n. 1071.) “If the tubs are fraudulently enlarged, then a man can surely leave\n the mine upon 14 days’ notice? — But if he goes to another\n place, he finds the same thing.” (n. 1072.) “But he can nevertheless leave the place\n where the wrong is committed? — It is universally prevalent.” (n. 1073.)\n “But the man can leave his respective place upon 14 days’ notice?”","kind":"passage","sequence":262,"address":"15.262","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0262"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0263","text":"— Yes.” Throw sand over it!]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"491\", \"facs\": \"#f0510\"} --> archaic and transitional forms behind which the domination of\n capital still partly conceals itself, and replaces them with its direct, un-\n [Note: 6) Mine inspection. The workers suffer not only from the accidents\n caused by exploding gases. (n. 234 sqq.) “We have just as much to com-\n plain of concerning the bad ventilation of the coal mines, so that people can scarcely\n breathe in them; they are thereby rendered incapable of every sort of employment.\n Thus, for example, just now in the part of the mine where I work, the pestilential air has thrown many\n people onto sickbeds for weeks. The main passages are mostly airy\n enough, but precisely not the places in which we work.”","kind":"passage","sequence":263,"address":"15.263","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0263"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0264","text":"If a man sends a complaint\n about ventilation to the inspector, he is dismissed and is a “marked\n man,” who finds no employment elsewhere either. The “Mining In-\n specting Act” of 1860 is a mere scrap of paper. The inspector—and\n their number is far too small—perhaps makes one formal visit in 7 years.\n Our inspector is a wholly incapable man of seventy, who has charge of more than 130\n coal mines. Besides more inspectors, we need subinspec-\n tors.” (n. 280.) “Should the government then maintain such an army of in-\n spectors that they can do everything you demand without information from the work-\n ers themselves? — That is impossible, but they should come and obtain the infor-\n mation in the mines themselves.” (n. 285.) “Do you not think that\n the effect would be to shift the responsibility (!) for ventilation\n etc.”","kind":"passage","sequence":264,"address":"15.264","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0264"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0265","text":"from the mine-owner onto the government officials? — By no\n means; it must be their business to enforce observance of the laws\n already in existence.” (n. 294.) “When you speak of subinspectors, do you mean\n people with less salary and of lower character than the present\n inspectors? — I by no means wish them lower, if you can have them better.”\n (n. 295.) “Do you want more inspectors or a lower\n class of people than the inspectors? — We need people who bustle about in the\n mines themselves, people who have no fear for their own skin.”\n (n. 296.) “If your wish for inspectors of an\n inferior sort were fulfilled, would not their lack of skill create\n dangers, etc.?”","kind":"passage","sequence":265,"address":"15.265","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0265"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0266","text":"— No; it is the business of the government to appoint suitable sub-\n jects.” This kind of examination finally becomes too much even for the president of the committee of\n inquiry. “You want,” he interposes, “practical people,\n who look about in the mines themselves and report to the inspector, who can then\n apply his higher science.” (n. 531.) “Would the ven-\n tilation of all these old works not cause much expense? — Yes, expenses\n might arise, but human lives would be protected.” (n. 581.) A\n coal worker protests against the 17th section of the Act of 1860: “At\n present, if the mine inspector finds any part of the mine in a condition unfit for\n working, he must report it to the mine-owner and the Home Secretary.”","kind":"passage","sequence":266,"address":"15.266","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0266"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0267","text":"Thereupon the mine-owner has 20 days for consideration; at the end]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"492\", \"facs\": \"#f0511\"} --> veiled domination. It thereby also generalises the direct struggle\n against this domination. While in the individual workshops\n it enforces uniformity, regularity, order, and economy,\n through the enormous spur which the limit and regulation of the\n working day give to technology, it multiplies the anarchy and catastrophes of\n capitalist production on the large scale and as a whole, the intensity of\n labour and the competition of machinery with the worker. Along with the\n spheres of small-scale enterprise and domestic labour, it destroys the last\n refuges of those made incessantly “supernumerary,” and thereby\n the hitherto existing safety valve of the entire social mechanism.”","kind":"passage","sequence":267,"address":"15.267","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0267"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0268","text":"With\n the material conditions and the social combination of the\n process of production, it brings to maturity the contradictions and antagonisms of its\ncapitalist form, hence at the same time the elements of formation\n [Note: of the 20 days he can refuse every alteration. But if he does so, he has\n to write to the Home Secretary and propose to him 5 mining engineers,\n from among whom the minister must choose the arbitrators. We main-\n tain that in this case the mine-owner virtually appoints his own judge.”\n (n. 586.) The bourgeois examiner, himself a mine-owner: “This is\n a purely speculative objection.” (n. 588.) “So you have a very low\n opinion of the honesty of mining engineers? — I say that it is very un-\n fair and unjust.” (n. 589.)","kind":"passage","sequence":268,"address":"15.268","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0268"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0269","text":"“Do mining engineers not possess a sort\n of public character which raises their decisions above the partiality you fear?\n — I refuse to answer questions about the personal character\n of these people. I am convinced that in many cases they act very\n partially and that this power should be taken from them where human\n lives are at stake.” The same bourgeois has the impudence\n to ask: “Do you not think that the mine-owners too suffer losses in ex-\n plosions?” — Finally (n. 1042): “Can you workers not look after your own in-\n terests yourselves, without calling upon the help of the government? —\nNo.” — In the year 1865 there were 3,217 coal mines in Great Britain and —\n 12 inspectors. A mine-owner from Yorkshire (Times, 26.","kind":"passage","sequence":269,"address":"15.269","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0269"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0270","text":"January 1867)\n himself calculates that, apart from their purely bureaucratic business,\n which absorbs all their time, every mine could be inspected only once in 10 years.\n No wonder that the catastrophes in recent years (in particular\n also 1866 and 1867) have progressively increased in number and extent (sometimes\n with a sacrifice of 200—300 workers).","kind":"passage","sequence":270,"address":"15.270","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0270"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0271","text":"These are the beauties\n of “free” capitalist production!]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"493\", \"facs\": \"#f0512\"} -->of a new society and the revolutionary moments of the old so-\n ciety [Note: Robert Owen, the father of co-operative factories and shops, who,\n however, as remarked earlier, by no means shared the illusions of his successors concerning the scope\n of these isolated elements of transformation, not only actually proceeded in his experiments from the factory system,\n but also declared it theoretically to be the point of departure of the social revolution. Mr Vissering, pro-\n fessor of political economy at the University of Leiden, seems to suspect something of the kind, when in his “Handboek van Praktische Staatshuis-\n kunde. 1860—62,” which presents the platitudes of vulgar economics in the most appro-\n priate form, he zealously advocates handicraft production against large-scale industry.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":271,"address":"15.271","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0271"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0272","text":"The revolution which large-scale industry brings about in agriculture and in the\n social relations of its agents of production can only be presented\n later. Here a brief indication of some anticipated results\n suffices. If the use of machinery in agri-\n culture is for the most part free from the physical disadvantages which it inflicts on the\n factory worker [Note: A detailed account of the machinery employed in English agriculture\n is to be found in: “Die landwirthschaftlichen Geräthe und\n Maschinen Englands von Dr. W. Hamm. 2. Aufl. 1856.” In his\n sketch of the course of development of English agriculture, Mr Hamm follows Mr Leonce de Lavergne\n too uncritically.] , it acts here still more intensively and without counter-\n blow in making workers “supernumerary,” as will later be seen in de-\n tail. In the counties of Cambridge and Suffolk, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":272,"address":"15.272","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0272"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0273","text":"the\n area of cultivated land has expanded greatly during the last twenty years,\n while the rural population in the same period declined not only\nrelatively, but absolutely. In the United States of\n North America, agricultural machines meanwhile replace workers only virtually,\n i.e. they permit the producer to cultivate a larger\n area, but do not actually drive out workers already employed. In England\n and Wales, in 1861, the number of persons involved in the manufacture of agricultural\n machinery amounted to 1,034, while the number of agricultural work-\n ers employed with steam and working machines\n amounted only to 1,205.","kind":"passage","sequence":273,"address":"15.273","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0273"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0274","text":"In the sphere of agriculture, large-scale industry acts in so far\n most revolutionarily as it destroys the bulwark of the old society,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"494\", \"facs\": \"#f0513\"} -->the “peasant,” and substitutes for him the wage-worker. The social\n needs of transformation and antagonisms of the countryside are thus levelled with those\n of the town. In place of the most habitually indolent and irra-\n tional operation comes the conscious, technological application of science.\n The tearing apart of the original family bond of agri-\n culture and manufacture, which enveloped the childishly undeveloped form of both,\n is completed by the capitalist mode of production. But it\n simultaneously creates the material prerequisites for a new, higher\n synthesis, the union of agriculture and industry, on the basis of their\n oppositely elaborated forms.","kind":"passage","sequence":274,"address":"15.274","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0274"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0275","text":"With the ever-growing preponderance of the urban population, which it amasses in great centres, capitalist production, on the one hand, accumulates the historical motive force of society; on the other hand, it disturbs the metabolism between man and earth, i.e. the return to the soil of those constituents of the soil consumed by man in the form of food and clothing, hence the eternal natural condition of lasting soil fertility. It thereby destroys at the same time the physical health of the urban workers and the intellectual life of the rural workers [Note: “You divide the people into two hostile camps of clownish boors and\nemasculated dwarfs. Good heavens!”","kind":"passage","sequence":275,"address":"15.275","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0275"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0276","text":"a nation divided into agricultural and\ncommercial interests calling itself sane nay styling itself enlightened and civilized,\nnot only in spite of, but in consequence of this monstrous and unnatural division.”\n(David Urquhart l. c. p. 119.) This passage simultaneously displays the strength and the weakness of a kind of critique which knows how to approve and condemn the present, but not to comprehend it.] . But at the same time, through the destruction of the merely spontaneously evolved circumstances of that metabolism, it compels its systematic restoration as a regulative law of social production, and in a form adequate to full human development.","kind":"passage","sequence":276,"address":"15.276","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0276"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0277","text":"In agriculture as in manufacture, the capitalist transformation of the production process appears at the same time as the martyrology of the producers; the instrument of labour as a means of subjugation, exploitation, and impoverishment of the worker; the social combination of labour processes as the organized oppression of his individual vitality, freedom, and independence. The dispersal of rural workers over larger areas simultaneously breaks their power of resistance,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"495\", \"facs\": \"#f0514\"} --> whereas concentration increases that of the urban workers. As in urban industry, so in modern agriculture, the increased productivity and greater fluidization of labour are purchased through the devastation and atrophy of labour-power itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":277,"address":"15.277","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0277"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0278","text":"And every advance in capitalist agriculture is not only an advance in the art of robbing the worker, but at the same time in the art of robbing the soil; every advance in increasing its fertility for a given period of time is at the same time an advance in ruining the lasting sources of this fertility. The more a country, such as the United States of North America, for example, proceeds from large-scale industry as the background of its development, the more rapid this process of destruction [Note: Cf. Liebig: “Chemistry in Its Application to Agriculture and Physiology. 7th edition, 1862,” especially also in the First Volume the “Introduction to the Natural Laws of Agriculture.” The development of the negative side of modern agriculture, from the natural-scientific standpoint, is one of Liebig’s immortal merits.","kind":"passage","sequence":278,"address":"15.278","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0278"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0279","text":"His historical aperçus on the history of agriculture, too, although not without gross errors, contain more flashes of insight than the writings of all modern political economists taken together. It remains regrettable that he ventures statements at random such as the following: “By more extensive pulverization and more frequent ploughing, the exchange of air within the porous parts of the earth is promoted, and the surface of the parts of the earth upon which the air is to act is enlarged and renewed; but it is easily understandable that the increased yields of the field cannot be proportional to the labour applied to the field, but that they increase in a far smaller ratio. This law,” Liebig adds, “was first stated by J. St. Mill in his Princ. of Pol. Econ. v. I, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":279,"address":"15.279","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0279"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0280","text":"17 in the following manner: ‘That the produce of land increases caeteris paribus in a diminishing ratio to the increase of the labourers employed, (Mr. Mill even repeats Ricardo’s scholastic law in a false formula, since “the decrease of the labourers employed” in England was constant in the progress of agriculture, the law invented for and in England would at least have no application in England) is the universal law of agricultural industry’, remarkable enough, since its ground was unknown to him.” (Liebig l. c. vol. I, p. 143 and note.) Apart from the erroneous interpretation of the word “labour,” by which Liebig understands something different from political economy, it is in any case “remarkable enough” that he makes Mr. J. St. Mill the first proclaimer of a theory which James Anderson first published in the time of A.","kind":"passage","sequence":280,"address":"15.280","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0280"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0281","text":"Smith and repeated in various writings down to the beginning of the 19th century, which Malthus, over-] . Capitalist production therefore develops only the technique and combination of the social process of production by simultaneously undermining the fountains of all wealth: the earth and the worker.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"496\", \"facs\": \"#f0515\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":281,"address":"15.281","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_4M_2:P0281"]}],"source_digest":"292cbf92755439dd2fe2d9bc79aba38977ef7e40dca6c740fb2beb4c283dd6fb","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW","title":"Fünftes Kapitel. Weitere Untersuchungen über die Produktion des absoluten und relativen Mehrwerths.","order":16,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_FNKW","text":"## Fünftes Kapitel.\n Weitere Untersuchungen über die Produktion des absoluten\n und relativen Mehrwerths.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## 1) Absoluter und relativer Mehrwerth.\n\n\n\n\nDer Arbeitsprozess wurde (sieh drittes Kapitel) zunächst ab-\n strakt betrachtet, unabhängig von seinen geschichtlichen Formen, als Pro-\n zess zwischen Mensch und Natur. Soweit der Prozess rein individuell,\n vereinigt derselbe Arbeiter alle Funktionen, die sich später trennen. In\n der individuellen Aneignung von Naturgegenständen zu seinen Lebens-\n zwecken kontrolirt er sich selbst. Später wird er kontrolirt. Der ein-\n zelne Mensch kann nicht auf die Natur wirken ohne Bethätigung seiner\n eignen Muskeln unter Kontrole seines eignen Hirns. Wie im Natursystem\n Kopf und Hand zusammengehören, vereint der Arbeitsprozess Kopfarbeit\n und Handarbeit. Später scheiden sie sich, bis zum feindlichen Gegensatz.\n Das Produkt verwandelt sich überhaupt aus dem unmittelbaren Produkt\n des individuellen Producenten in das gemeinsame Produkt eines kombinir-\n ten Arbeitspersonals, dessen Glieder der Handhabung des Arbeitsgegen-\n standes näher oder ferner stehn. Mit dem cooperativen Charakter des\n [Note: haupt ein Meister des Plagiats (seine ganze Bevölkerungstheorie ist ein schamloses\n Plagiat), sich 1815 annexirte, die West zur selben Zeit und unabhängig von\n Anderson entwickelte, die Ricardo 1817 in Zusammenhang mit der allgemeinen\n Werththeorie brachte und die von da an unter dem Namen Ricardo’s die Runde\n der Welt gemacht hat, die 1820 von James Mill (dem Vater J. St. Mill’s) vul-\n garisirt, und endlich u. a. auch von Herrn J. St. Mill als bereits Gemeinplatz\n gewordnes Schuldogma repetirt wird. Es ist unläugbar, dass J. St. Mill seine\n jedenfalls „merkwürdige“ Autorität fast nur ähnlichen qui pro quo verdankt.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"497\", \"facs\": \"#f0516\"} --> Arbeitsprozesses selbst erweitert sich daher nothwendig der Begriff\n der produktiven Arbeit und ihres Trägers, des produktiven\n Arbeiters. Andrerseits verengt er sich. Die kapitalistische Produk-\n tion ist nicht nur Produktion von Waare, sie ist wesentlich Pro-\n duktion von Mehrwerth. Der Arbeiter producirt nicht für sich,\n sondern für das Kapital. Es genügt daher nicht länger, dass er über-\n haupt producirt. Er muss Mehrwerth produciren. Nur der Arbei-\n ter ist produktiv, der Mehrwerth für den Kapitalisten\n producirt oder zur Selbstverwerthung des Kapitals dient.\n Steht es frei ein Beispiel ausserhalb der Sphäre der materiellen Produktion\n zu wählen, so ist ein Schulmeister produktiver Arbeiter, wenn er nicht nur\n Kinderköpfe bearbeitet, sondern sich selbst abarbeitet zur Bereicherung\n des Unternehmers. Dass der sein Kapital in einer Lehrfabrik angelegt hat,\n statt in einer Wurstfabrik, ändert nichts an dem Verhältniss. Der Be-\n griff des produktiven Arbeiters schliesst daher keineswegs bloss ein Ver-\n hältniss zwischen Thätigkeit und Nutzeffekt, zwischen Arbeiter und Arbeits-\n produkt ein, sondern auch ein spezifisch gesellschaftliches Produktionsverhält-\n niss, welches den Arbeiter zum unmittelbaren Verwerthungsmittel des Ka-\n pitals stempelt. Produktiver Arbeiter zu sein, ist daher kein Glück, sondern\n ein Pech. Im vierten Buch dieser Schrift, welches die Geschichte der\n Theorie behandelt, wird man näher sehn, dass die klassische politische\n Oekonomie von jeher die Produktion von Mehrwerth zum ent-\n scheidenden Charakter des produktiven Arbeiters machte. Mit ihrer Auf-\n fassung von der Natur des Mehrwerths wechselt daher ihre Definition\n des produktiven Arbeiters.\n\n\n\n\nZunächst erschienen uns die Produktion von absolutem Mehrwerth\n und die Produktion von relativem Mehrwerth als zwei verschiedne,\n verschiednen Entwicklungsepochen des Kapitals angehörige, Produk-\n tionsarten. Die Produktion des absoluten Mehrwerths\n bedingt, dass die sachlichen Arbeitsbedingungen in Kapital und die Arbeiter\n in Lohnarbeiter verwandelt sind, dass die Produkte als Waaren, d. h.\n für den Verkauf producirt werden, dass der Produktionsprozess zu-\n gleich Konsumtionsprozess der Arbeitskraft durch das Kapital und daher\n der direkten Kontrole des Kapitalisten unterworfen ist, endlich, dass der\n Arbeitsprozess, also der Arbeitstag, über den Punkt hinaus verlängert\n wird, wo der Arbeiter nur ein Aequivalent für den Werth seiner Arbeits-\n [Running matter: I. 32] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"498\", \"facs\": \"#f0517\"} --> kraft producirt hätte. Die allgemeinen Bedingungen aller Pro-\n duktion von Waaren vorausgesetzt, besteht die Produktion des abso-\n luten Mehrwerths einfach in der Verlängerung des Arbeitstags\n über die Grenze der zum Leben des Arbeiters selbst nothwendigen\n Arbeitszeit, und in der Aneignung der Mehrarbeit durch das\n Kapital. Dieser Prozess kann vorgehn und geht vor auf Grundlage von\n Betriebsweisen, die ohne Zuthun des Kapitals historisch überliefert sind.\n Es findet dann nur eine formelle Metamorphose statt, oder die kapitali-\n stische Ausbeutungsweise unterscheidet sich von den früheren, wie Skla-\n vensystem u. s. w., nur dadurch, dass die Mehrarbeit hier durch direkten\n Zwang abgerungen, dort durch „freiwilligen“ Verkauf der Arbeitskraft\n vermittelt wird. Die Produktion des absoluten Mehrwerths unterstellt\n also nur formelle Subsumtion der Arbeit unter das Ka-\n pital.\n\n\n\n\nDie Produktion des relativen Mehrwerths setzt die Pro-\n duktion des absoluten Mehrwerths voraus, also auch die entsprechende\n allgemeine Form der kapitalistischen Produktion. Ihr Zweck ist Erhöhung\n des Mehrwerths durch Verkürzung der nothwendigen Arbeitszeit, unabhängig\n von den Grenzen des Arbeitstags. Das Ziel wird erreicht durch Ent-\n wicklung der Produktivkräfte der Arbeit. Diess bedingt jedoch eine Re-\n volution des Arbeitsprozesses selbst. Es genügt nicht mehr ihn zu ver-\n längern, er muss neu gestaltet werden. Die Produktion des relativen\n Mehrwerths unterstellt also eine specifisch kapitalistische\n Produktionsweise, die mit ihren Methoden, Mitteln und Bedingungen\n selbst erst auf Grundlage der formellen Subsumtion der Arbeit unter das\n Kapital naturwüchsig entsteht und ausgebildet wird. An die Stelle der\n formellen tritt die reelle Subsumtion der Arbeit unter das\n Kapital.\n\n\n\n\nEs genügt blosser Hinweis auf Zwitterformen, worin die\n Mehrarbeit weder durch direkten Zwang dem Producenten ausgepumpt\n wird, noch auch dessen formelle Unterordnung unter das Kapital ein-\n getreten ist. Das Kapital hat sich hier noch nicht unmittelbar des Arbeits-\n prozesses bemächtigt. Neben die selbstständigen Producenten,\n die in überlieferter, urväterlicher Betriebsweise handwerkern oder acker-\n bauen, tritt der Wucherer oder der Kaufmann, dasWucherkapital oder das\n Handelskapital, das sie parasitenmässig aussaugt. Vorherrschaft dieser\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"499\", \"facs\": \"#f0518\"} --> Exploitationsform in einer Gesellschaft schliesst die kapitalistische\n Produktionsweise aus, zu der sie andrerseits, wie im späteren Mittel-\n alter, den Uebergang bilden kann. Endlich, wie das Beispiel der\n modernen Hausarbeit gezeigt, werden gewisse Zwitterformen auf dem Hin-\n tergrund der grossen Industrie stellenweis reproducirt, wenn auch mit\n gänzlich veränderter Physiognomie.\n\n\n\n\nWenn zur Produktion des absoluten Mehrwerths die bloss formelle\n Subsumtion der Arbeit unter das Kapital genügt, z. B. dass Handwerker,\n die früher für sich selbst oder auch als Gesellen eines Zunftmeisters arbei-\n teten, nun als Lohnarbeiter unter die direkte Kontrole des Kapitalisten\n treten, zeigte sich andrerseits, wie die Methoden zur Produktion des rela-\n tiven Mehrwerths zugleich Methoden zur Produktion des absoluten Mehr-\n werths sind. Ja die masslose Verlängerung des Arbeitstags stellte sich\n als eigenstes Produkt der grossen Industrie dar. Ueberhaupt hört die\nspezifisch kapitalistische Produktionsweise auf blosses\n Mittel zur Produktion des relativen Mehrwerths zu sein, sobald\n sie sich eines ganzen Produktionszweigs und noch mehr, sobald sie sich\naller entscheidenden Produktionszweige bemächtigt hat. Sie wird jetzt\n allgemeine, gesellschaftlich herrschende Form des Produktionsprozesses.\n Als besondre Methode zur Produktion des relativen Mehr-\n werths wirkt sie nur noch, erstens soweit sie dem Kapital bisher nur\n formell untergeordnete Industrieen ergreift, also in ihrer Propaganda.\n Zweitens, soweit in den ihr bereits anheimgefallenen Industrieen fort-\n währende Revolution in der Anwendung der Maschinerie, der Natur-\n kräfte und der Produktionsmethode überhaupt stattfindet.\n\n\n\n\nVon gewissem Gesichtspunkt scheint der Unterschied zwischen abso-\n lutem und relativem Mehrwerth überhaupt illusorisch. Der relative\n Mehrwerth ist absolut, denn er bedingt absolute Verlängerung\n des Arbeitstags über die zur Existenz des Arbeiters selbst nothwen-\n dige Arbeitszeit. Der absolute Mehrwerth ist relativ, denn er bedingt\n eine Entwicklung der Arbeitsproduktivität, welche erlaubt, die nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit auf einen Theil des Arbeitstags zu beschränken. Fasst\n man aber die Bewegung des Mehrwerths ins Auge, so verschwindet\n dieser Schein der Einerleiheit. Die Produktivkraft der Arbeit und ihren\n Normalgrad von Intensivität gegeben, ist die Rate des Mehrwerths nur\n erhöhbar durch absolute Verlängerung des Arbeitstags.\n [Running matter: 32*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"500\", \"facs\": \"#f0519\"} --> Andrerseits ist die Rate des Mehrwerths nur erhöhbar, bei gegebner Grenze\n des Arbeitstags, durch relativen Grössenwechsel seiner Be-\n standtheile, der nothwendigen Arbeit und der Mehrarbeit, was sei-\n nerseits, soll der Lohn nicht unter den Werth der Arbeitskraft sinken,\n Wechsel in der Produktivität oder Intensivität der Arbeit voraussetzt.\n\n\n\n\nBraucht der Arbeiter alle seine Zeit, um die zur Erhaltung seiner\n selbst und seiner Race nöthigen Lebensmittel zu produciren, so bleibt ihm\n keine Zeit, um unentgeldlich für dritte Personen zu arbeiten. Ohne einen\n gewissen Produktivitätsgrad der Arbeit keine solche disponible Zeit des\n Arbeiters, ohne solche überschüssige Zeit keine Mehrarbeit und daher\n keine Kapitalistenklasse. Ein gewisser Höhepunkt der Arbeitsproduktivität\n ist also überhaupt Existenzbedingung der kapitalistischen Produktion, wie\n aller früheren Produktionsweisen, worin ein Theil der Gesellschaft nicht\n nur für sich selbst, sondern auch für den andern arbeitet [Note: „The very existence of the master-capitalists as a distinct class is depen-\n dent on the productiveness of industry.“ (Ramsay l. c. p. 206.) „If each\n man’s labour were but enough to produce his own food, there could be no pro-\n perty.“ (Ravenstone l. c. p. 14, 15.)] .\n\n\n\n\nSo kann von einer Naturbasis des Mehrwerths gesprochen wer-\n den, aber nur in dem ganz allgemeinen Sinn, dass kein absolutes Natur-\n hinderniss den einen abhält die zu seiner eignen Existenz nöthige Arbeit\n von sich selbst ab- und einem andern aufzuwälzen. Es sind durchaus nicht,\n wie es hier und da geschehn, mystische Vorstellungen mit dieser natur-\n wüchsigen Produktivität der Arbeit zu verbinden. Nur sobald\n die Menschen sich aus ihren ersten Thierzuständen herausgearbeitet, ihre\n Arbeit selbst also schon in gewissem Grad vergesellschaftet ist, treten\n Verhältnisse ein, worin die Mehrarbeit des einen zur Existenzbedingung\n des andern wird. In den Kulturanfängen sind die erworbnen Produktiv-\n kräfte der Arbeit gering, aber so sind die Bedürfnisse, die sich mit und an\n den Mitteln ihrer Befriedigung entwickeln. Ferner ist in jenen Anfängen die\n Proportion der Gesellschaftstheile, die von fremder Arbeit leben, ver-\n schwindend klein gegen die Masse der unmittelbaren Producenten. Mit\n dem Fortschritt der gesellschaftlichen Produktivkraft der Arbeit wächst\n diese Proportion absolut und relativ [Note: „Among the wild Indians in America, almost every thing is the labourer’s,\n 99 parts of an hundred are to be put upon the account of Labour: In England,] . Das Kapitalverhältniss\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"501\", \"facs\": \"#f0520\"} --> entspringt übrigens auf einem ökonomischen Boden, der das Produkt einer\n langen Reihe früherer Entwicklungsphasen ist. Die vorhandne Pro-\n duktivität der Arbeit, wovon es als Grundlage ausgeht, ist nicht\n Gabe der Natur, sondern der Geschichte.\n\n\n\n\nVon der mehr oder minder entwickelten Gestalt des gesellschaftlichen\n Produktionsprozesses abgesehn, bleibt die Produktivität der Arbeit an\nNaturbedingungen gebunden, und wechselt der Grad ihrer Produk-\n tivität mit dem Reichthum dieser Naturbedingungen. Sie sind alle rück-\n führbar auf die Natur des Menschen selbst und die ihn umgebende Natur.\n Der grössere oder geringere Reichthum der menschlichen Natur hängt ab\n von Race, Boden und Klima. Die äussern Naturbedingungen zerfallen\n ökonomisch in zwei grosse Klassen, natürlicher Reichthum an Lebens-\n mitteln, also Bodenfruchtbarkeit, fischreiche Gewässer u. s. w., und\n natürlicher Reichthum an Arbeitsmitteln, wie lebendige Wasserge-\n fälle, schiffbare Flüsse, Holz, Metalle, Kohle u. s. w. In den Kulturan-\n fängen giebt die erstere, auf höherer Entwicklungsstufe die zweite Art des\n natürlichen Reichthums den Ausschlag. Man vergleiche z. B. England\n mit Indien oder, in der antiken Welt, Athen und Korinth mit den Ufer-\n ländern des schwarzen Meeres.\n\n\n\n\nJe geringer die Zahl der absolut zu befriedigenden Naturbedürfnisse,\n und je grösser die natürliche Bodenfruchtbarkeit und Gunst des Klimas, desto\n geringer die zur Erhaltung und Reproduktion des Producenten nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit. Desto grösser kann also der Ueberschuss seiner Arbeit für An-\n dere über seine Arbeit für sich selbst sein. So bemerkt schon Diodor über die\n alten Aegypter: „Es ist ganz unglaublich, wie wenig Mühe und Kosten die\n Erziehung ihrer Kinder ihnen verursacht. Sie kochen ihnen die nächste\n beste einfache Speise; auch geben sie ihnen von der Papierstaude den\n unteren Theil zu essen, soweit man ihn im Feuer rösten kann, und die\n Wurzel und Stengel der Sumpfgewächse, theils roh, theils gesotten und\n gebraten. Die meisten Kinder gehn ohne Schuhe und unbekleidet, da die\n Luft so mild ist. Daher kostet ein Kind seinen Aeltern, bis es erwachsen\n ist, im Ganzen nicht über zwanzig Drachmen. Hieraus ist es hauptsäch-\n lich zu erklären, dass in Aegypten die Bevölkerung so zahlreich ist und\n [Note: perhaps the labourer has not ⅔.“ („The Advantages of the East India\n Trade etc.“, p. 73.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"502\", \"facs\": \"#f0521\"} --> darum so viel grosse Werke angelegt werden konnten“ [Note: Diodor l. c. l. I, c. 80.] .\n Indess sind die grossen Bauwerke des alten Aegyptens dem Umfang seiner\n Bevölkerung weniger geschuldet als der grossen Proportion, worin sie dis-\n ponibel war. Wie der individuelle Arbeiter um so mehr Mehrarbeit liefern\n kann, je geringer seine nothwendige Arbeitszeit, so, je geringer der zur\n Produktion der nothwendigen Lebensmittel erheischte Theil der Arbeiter-\n bevölkerung, desto grösser der für andres Werk disponible Theil.\n\n\n\n\nDie kapitalistische Produktion einmal vorausgesetzt, wird, unter sonst\n gleichbleibenden Umständen und bei gegebner Länge des Arbeitstags, die\n Grösse der Mehrarbeit mit den Naturbedingungen der Arbeit, namentlich\n auch der Bodenfruchtbarkeit, variiren. Es folgt aber keineswegs umge-\n kehrt, dass der fruchtbarste Boden der geeignetste zum Wachsthum der\n kapitalistischen Produktionsweise. Sie unterstellt Herrschaft des Men-\n schen über die Natur. Eine zu verschwenderische Natur „hält ihn an ihrer\n Hand wie ein Kind am Gängelband“. Sie macht seine eigne Entwicklung\n nicht zu einer Naturnothwendigkeit [Note: „The first (natural wealth), as it is most noble and advantageous, so\n doth it make the people careless, proud, and given to all excesses; whereas the\n second enforceth vigilancy, literature, arts and policy.“ („England’s Trea-\n sure by Foreign Trade. Or the Balance of our Foreign Trade is\n the Rule of our Treasure. Written by Thomas Mun, of London,\n Merchant, and now published for the common good by his son\n John Mun. Lond. 1669“, p. 181, 182.) „Nor can I conceive a greater\n course upon a body of people, than to be thrown upon a spot of land, where the\n productions for subsistence and food were, in great measure, spon-\n taneous, and the climate required or admitted little care for raiment and covering\n . . . . there may be an extreme on the other side. A soil incapable of produce\n by labour is quite as bad as a soil that produces plentifully without any labour.“\n („An Inquiry into the Present High Price of Provisions. Lond.\n 1767“, p. 10.)] . Nicht das tropische Klima mit\n seiner überwuchernden Vegetation, sondern die gemässigte Zone ist das\n Mutterland des Kapitals. Es ist nicht die absolute Fruchtbarkeit des\n Bodens, sondern seine Differenzirung, die Mannigfaltigkeit seiner natür-\n lichen Produkte, welche die Naturgrundlage der gesellschaftlichen Theilung\n der Arbeit bildet, und den Menschen durch den Wechsel der Naturum-\n stände, innerhalb deren er haust, zur Vermannigfachung seiner eignen Be-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"503\", \"facs\": \"#f0522\"} --> dürfnisse, Fähigkeiten, Arbeitsmittel und Arbeitsweisen spornt. Die Noth-\n wendigkeit eine Naturkraft gesellschaftlich zu kontroliren, da-\n mit Haus zu halten, sie durch Werke von Menschenhand auf grossem Mass-\n stab erst anzueignen oder zu zähmen, spielt die entscheidendste Rolle in der\n Geschichte der Industrie. So z. B. die Wasserreglung in Aegypten [Note: Die Nothwendigkeit, die Perioden der Nilbewegung zu berechnen, schuf\n die ägyptische Astronomie und mit ihr die Herrschaft der Priesterkaste als Leiterin\n der Agrikultur. „Le solstice est le moment de l’année où commence la crue du\n Nil, et celui que les Égyptiens ont dû observer avec le plus d’attention … C’était\n cette année tropique qu’il leur importait de marquer pour se diriger dans leurs\n opérations agricoles. Ils durent donc chercher dans le ciel un signe apparent de\n son retour.“ (Cuvier: „Discours sur les révolutions du globe ed.\n Hoefer. Paris 1863“, p. 141.)] ,\n Lombardei, Holland u. s. w. Oder in Indien, Persien u. s. w., wo die\n Ueberrieselung durch künstliche Kanäle dem Boden nicht nur das unent-\n behrliche Wasser, sondern mit dessen Geschlämme zugleich den Mineral-\n dünger von den Bergen zuführt. Das Geheimniss der Industrieblüthe von\n Spanien und Sicilien unter arabischer Herrschaft war die Kanalisation [Note: Eine der materiellen Grundlagen der Staatsmacht über die zusammen-\n hangslosen kleinen Produktionsorganismen Indiens war Reglung der Wasserzu-\n fuhr. Die muhamedanischen Herrscher Indiens verstanden diess besser als ihre\n englischen Nachfolger. Wir erinnern nur an die Hungersnoth von 1866, die mehr\n als einer Million Hindus in dem Distrikt von Orissa, Präsidentschaft Bengal, das\n Leben kostete.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Gunst der Naturbedingungen liefert immer nur die Möglich-\n keit, niemals die Wirklichkeit der Mehrarbeit, also des Mehrwerths\n oder des Surplusprodukts. Die verschiednen Naturbedingungen der Arbeit\n bewirken, dass dieselbe Quantität Arbeit in verschiednen Ländern\n verschiedne Bedürfnissmassen befriedigt [Note: „There are no two countries which furnish an equal number of the\n necessaries of life in equal plenty, and with the same quantity of labour\n man’s wants increase or diminish with the severity or temperateness of the climate\n they live in; consequently the proportion of trade which the inhabitants of\n different countries are obliged to carry on through necessity, cannot be the same,\n nor is it practicable to ascertain the degree of variation farther than by the Degrees\n of Heat and Cold; from whence one may make this general conclusion, that quan-\n tity of labour required for a certain number of people is greatest in cold climates,\n and least in hot ones; for in the former men not only want more clothes, but the] , dass also, unter sonst analogen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"504\", \"facs\": \"#f0523\"} --> Umständen, die nothwendige Arbeitszeit verschieden ist. Auf\n die Mehrarbeit wirken sie nur als Naturschranke, d. h. durch die Bestim-\n mung des Punkts, wo die Arbeit für Andre beginnen kann. In dem-\n selben Mass, worin die Industrie vortritt, weicht diese Naturschranke zu-\n rück. Mitten in der westeuropäischen Gesellschaft, wo der Arbeiter die\n Erlaubniss für seine eigne Existenz zu arbeiten nur durch Mehrarbeit er-\n kauft, wird sich leicht eingebildet, es sei eine der menschlichen Arbeit\n eingeborne Qualität, ein Surplusprodukt zu liefern [Note: „Chaque travail doit (scheint auch zu den droits und devoirs du citoyen\n zu gehören) laisser un excédant.“ (Proudhon.)] . Man nehme aber\n z. B. den Einwohner der östlichen Inseln des asiatischen Archipelagus,\n wo der Sago wild im Walde wächst. „Wenn die Bewohner, indem sie\n ein Loch in den Baum bohren, sich davon überzeugt haben, dass das\n Mark reif ist, so wird der Stamm umgeschlagen und in mehrere Stücke\n getheilt, das Mark wird herausgekratzt, mit Wasser gemischt und geseiht,\n es ist dann vollkommen brauchbares Sagomehl. Ein Baum giebt ge-\n meiniglich 300 Pfund und kann 5 bis 600 Pfund geben. Man geht dort\n also in den Wald und schneidet sich sein Brod, wie man bei uns sein Brenn-\n holz schlägt“ [Note: F. Shouw: „Die Erde, die Pflanze und der Mensch.“\n 2. Aufl. Leipzig 1854, p. 148.] . Gesetzt ein solcher ostasiatischer Brodschneider brauche\n 12 Arbeitsstunden in der Woche zur Befriedigung aller seiner Bedürfnisse.\n Was ihm die Gunst der Natur unmittelbar giebt, ist viel Mussezeit.\n Damit er diese produktiv für sich selbst verwende, ist eine ganze Reihe\n geschichtlicher Umstände, damit er sie in Mehrarbeit für fremde Personen\n verausgabe, ist äusserer Zwang erheischt. Würde kapitalistische Produk-\n tion eingeführt, so müsste der Brave vielleicht 6 Tage in der Woche arbeiten,\n um sich selbst das Produkt eines Arbeitstags anzueignen. Die Gunst der\n Natur erklärt nicht, warum er jetzt 6 Tage in der Woche arbeitet oder warum\n er 5 Tage Mehrarbeit liefert. Sie erklärt nur, warum seine nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit auf einen Tag in der Woche beschränkt ist. In keinem Fall\n [Note: earth more cultivating than in the latter.“ („An Essay on the Governing\n Causes of the Natural Rate of Interest. Lond. 1750“, p. 60.) Der\n Verfasser dieser epochemachenden anonymen Schrift ist J. Massey. Hume\n nahm daraus seine Zinstheorie.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"505\", \"facs\": \"#f0524\"} --> aber entspränge sein Mehrprodukt aus einer der menschlichen Arbeit ein-\n gebornen, occulten Qualität.\n\n\n\n\nWie die geschichtlich entwickelten, gesellschaftlichen, so er-\n scheinen die naturbedingten Produktivkräfte der Arbeit\n als Produktivkräfte des Kapitals, dem sie einverleibt wird.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## 2) Grössenwechsel von Preis der Arbeitskraft und\n Mehrwerth.\n\n\n\n\nIn Kapitel III, 3. Abschnitt, analysirten wir die Rate des Mehr-\n werths, aber nur vom Standpunkt der Produktion des absoluten Mehr-\n werths. In Kapitel IV fanden wir zusätzliche Bestimmungen. Das\n Wesentliche ist hier zu späterem Gebrauch kurz zusammenzufassen.\n\n\n\n\nDer Werth der Arbeitskraft ist bestimmt durch den Werth der ge-\n wohnheitsmässig nothwendigen Lebensmittel des Durchschnittsarbeiters.\n Die Masse dieser Lebensmittel, obgleich ihre Form wechseln mag, ist\n in einer bestimmten Epoche einer bestimmten Gesellschaft gegeben, und\n daher als constante Grösse zu behandeln. Was wechselt, ist der Werth\n dieser Masse. Zwei andre Faktoren gehn in die Werthbestimmung\n der Arbeitskraft ein. Einerseits ihre Entwicklungskosten, die sich\n mit der Produktionsweise ändern, andrerseits ihre Naturdifferenz,\n ob sie männlich oder weiblich, reif oder unreif. Der Verbrauch dieser\n differenten Arbeitskräfte, wieder bedingt durch die Produktionsweise,\n macht grossen Unterschied in den Reproduktionskosten der Arbeiterfamilie\n und dem Werth des erwachsnen männlichen Arbeiters. Beide Faktoren\n bleiben jedoch bei der folgenden Untersuchung ausgeschlossen.\n\n\n\n\nWir unterstellen, 1) dass die Waaren zu ihrem Werth verkauft\n werden, 2) dass der Preis der Arbeitskraft wohl gelegentlich über\n ihren Werth steigt, aber nie unter ihn sinkt.\n\n\n\n\nDiess einmal unterstellt, fand sich, dass die relativen Grössen von\n Preis der Arbeitskraft und von Mehrwerth durch drei Umstände bedingt sind,\ndie Länge des Arbeitstags oder die extensive Grösse der\n Arbeit, die normale Intensivität der Arbeit, oder ihre\n intensive Grösse, so dass bestimmtes Arbeitsquantum in bestimmter\n Zeit verausgabt wird, endlich die Produktivkraft der Arbeit,\n so dass je nach dem Entwicklungsgrad der Produktionsbedingungen das-\n selbe Quantum Arbeit in derselben Zeit ein grösseres oder kleineres Quan-\n tum Produkt liefert. Sehr verschiedne Kombinationen sind offenbar\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"506\", \"facs\": \"#f0525\"} --> möglich, je nachdem einer der drei Faktoren constant und zwei variabel,\n oder zwei Faktoren constant und einer variabel, oder endlich alle drei\n gleichzeitig variabel sind. Diese Kombinationen werden noch dadurch\n vermannigfacht, dass bei gleichzeitiger Variation verschiedner Faktoren die\n Grösse und Richtung der Variation verschieden sein können. Im Folgen-\n den sind nur die Hauptkombinationen dargestellt.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## A) Grösse des Arbeitstags und Intensivität der Ar-\n beit constant (gegeben), Produktivkraft der Arbeit va-\n riabel.\n\n\n\n\nUnter dieser Voraussetzung sind Werth der Arbeitskraft und Mehr-\n werth durch drei Gesetze bestimmt:\n\n\n\n\nErstens: Der Arbeitstag von gegebner Grösse stellt\n sich stets in demselben Werthprodukt dar, wie auch die\n Produktivität der Arbeit, mit ihr die Produktenmasse\n und daher der Preis der einzelnen Waare wechsle.\n\n\n\n\nDas Werthprodukt eines zwölfstündigen Arbeitstags ist 6 sh.\n z. B., obgleich die Masse des producirten Gebrauchswerths mit der Pro-\n duktivkraft der Arbeit wechselt, der Werth von 6 sh. sich also über mehr\n oder weniger Waaren vertheilt.\n\n\n\n\nZweitens: Werth der Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth\n wechseln in umgekehrter Richtung zu einander und zum\n Wechsel in der Produktivkraft der Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nDas Werthprodukt des zwölfstündigen Arbeitstags ist eine constante\n Grösse, z. B. 6 sh. Diese constante Grösse ist gleich der Summe des\n Mehrwerths plus dem Werth der Arbeitskraft, den der Ar-\n beiter durch ein Aequivalent ersetzt. Es ist selbstverständlich, dass von\n zwei Theilen einer constanten Grösse keiner zunehmen kann, ohne dass\n der andre abnimmt und keiner abnehmen, ohne dass der andre zunimmt.\n Der Werth der Arbeitskraft kann nicht von 3 sh. auf 4 steigen, ohne dass\n der Mehrwerth von 3 sh. auf 2 fällt und der Mehrwerth kann nicht von\n 3 auf 4 sh. steigen, ohne dass der Werth der Arbeitskraft von 3 sh. auf\n 2 fällt. Unter diesen Umständen also ist kein Wechsel in der abso-\n luten Grösse, sei es des Werths der Arbeitskraft, sei es des Mehr-\n werths, möglich ohne einen Wechsel ihrer relativen oder verhält-\n nissmässigen Grössen. Es ist unmöglich, dass sie gleich-\n zeitig fallen oder steigen.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"507\", \"facs\": \"#f0526\"} -->\n\nDer Werth der Arbeitskraft kann ferner nicht fallen, also der Mehr-\n werth nicht steigen, ohne dass die Produktivkraft der Arbeit\n steigt, z. B. im obigen Fall kann der Werth der Arbeitskraft nicht von\n 3 auf 2 sh. sinken, ohne dass erhöhte Produktivkraft der Arbeit erlaubt in\n 4 Stunden dieselbe Masse Lebensmittel zu produciren, die vorher 6 Stun-\n den zu ihrer Produktion erheischten. Umgekehrt kann der Werth der Ar-\n beitskraft nicht von 3 auf 4 sh. steigen, ohne dass die Produktivkraft der\n Arbeit fällt, also 8 Stunden zur Produktion derselben Masse von Lebens-\n mitteln erheischt sind, wozu früher 6 Stunden genügten. Dieselbe\n Richtung im Wechsel der Produktivkraft der Arbeit, ihre Zunahme oder\n Abnahme, wirkt in entgegengesetzter Richtung auf den gleichzei-\n tigen Grössenwechsel von Werth der Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth.\n\n\n\n\nBei Formulirung dieses Gesetzes übersah Ricardo einen Umstand:\n Obgleich der Wechsel in der Grösse des Mehrwerths oder der Mehr-\n arbeit einen umgekehrten Wechsel in der Grösse des Werths der Ar-\n beitskraft oder der nothwendigen Arbeit bedingt, folgt keineswegs, dass\n sie in demselben Verhältniss wechseln. Sie nehmen zu oder ab\num dieselbe Grösse. Das Verhältniss aber, worin jeder Theil\n des Werthprodukts oder des Arbeitstags zu- oder abnimmt, hängt von der\nursprünglichen Theilung ab, die vor dem Wechsel in der Produktiv-\n kraft der Arbeit stattfand. War der Weith der Arbeitskraft z. B. 4 sh.\n oder die nothwendige Arbeitszeit gleich 8 Stunden, also der Mehrwerth\n 2 sh. oder die Mehrarbeit gleich 4 Stunden, und fällt, in Folge erhöhter\n Produktivkraft der Arbeit, der Werth der Arbeitskraft auf 3 sh. oder die\n nothwendige Arbeit auf 6 Stunden, so steigt der Mehrwerth auf 3 sh. oder\n die Mehrarbeit auf 6 Stunden. Es ist dieselbe Grösse von zwei\n Stunden oder 1 sh., die dort zugefügt, hier weggenommen wird. Aber\ndas Verhältniss des Grössenwechsels ist auf beiden Seiten\nverschieden. Während der Werth der Arbeitskraft von 4 sh. auf 3,\n also um ¼ oder 25 % sinkt, steigt der Mehrwerth von 2 sh. auf 3, also\n um ½ oder 50 %. Es folgt daher, dass die proportionelle Zu- oder\n Abnahme des Mehrwerths, in Folge eines gegebnen Wechsels in der Pro-\n duktivkraft der Arbeit, um so grösser, je kleiner, und um so kleiner, je\n grösser ursprünglich der Theil des Arbeitstags war, der sich in Mehrwerth\n darstellt.\n\n\n\n\nDrittens: Zu- oder Abnahme des Mehrwerths ist stets\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"508\", \"facs\": \"#f0527\"} --> Folge und nie Grund der entsprechenden Ab- oder Zu-\n nahme des Werths der Arbeitskraft [Note: Zu diesem dritten Gesetz hat Mac Culloch u. A. den abgeschmackten\n Zusatz gemacht, dass der Mehrwerth ohne Fall im Werth der Arbeitskraft steigen\n kann durch Abschaffung von Steuern, die der Kapitalist früher zu zahlen hatte.\n Die Abschaffung solcher Steuern ändert absolut nichts an dem Quantum Mehr-\n werth, das der industrielle Kapitalist in erster Hand dem Arbeiter auspumpt. Sie\n ändert nur die Proportion, worin er diesen Mehrwerth in seine eigne Tasche steckt\n oder mit dritten Personen theilen muss. Sie ändert also nichts an dem Verhältniss\n zwischen Werth der Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth. Die „Ausnahme“ des Mac\n Culloch beweist also nur sein Missverständniss der Regel, ein Malheur, das ihm\n in der Vulgarisation Ricardo’s eben so oft passirt als dem J. B. Say in der Vul-\n garisation A. Smith’s.] .\n\n\n\n\nDa der Arbeitstag von constanter Grösse ist, sich in einer constanten\n Werthgrösse darstellt, jedem Grössenwechsel des Mehrwerths ein umge-\n kehrter Grössenwechsel im Werth der Arbeitskraft entspricht und der Werth\n der Arbeitskraft nur wechseln kann mit einem Wechsel in der Produktiv-\n kraft der Arbeit, folgt unter diesen Bedingungen offenbar, dass jeder\n Grössenwechsel des Mehrwerths aus einem Grössenwechsel im Werth der\n Arbeitskraft entspringt. Wenn man daher gesehn, dass kein absoluter\n Grössenwechsel im Werth der Arbeitskraft und des Mehrwerths\n möglich ist ohne einen Wechsel ihrer relativen Grössen, so folgt jetzt,\n dass kein Wechsel ihrer relativen Werthgrössen möglich ist\n ohne einen Wechsel der absoluten Werthgrösse der Arbeits-\n kraft.\n\n\n\n\nRicardo hat die eben aufgestellten drei Gesetze zuerst streng for-\n mulirt. Die Mängel seiner Darstellung sind, 1) dass er die besondern\n Bedingungen, innerhalb deren jene Gesetze gelten, als sich von selbst ver-\n stehende allgemeine und ausschliessliche Bedingungen der kapi-\n talistischen Produktion voraussetzt; 2), und diess verfälscht seine Analyse\n in viel höherem Grad, dass er überhaupt den Mehrwerth nicht rein dar-\n stellt, d. h. nicht unabhängig von seinen besondern Formen, wie Profit, Grund-\n rente u. s. w. Er wirft daher die Gesetze über die Rate des\n Mehrwerths unmittelbar zusammen mit den Gesetzen der Profit-\n rate. Ich werde später, im 3. Buch dieser Schrift beweisen, dass die-\n selbe Rate des Mehrwerths sich in den verschiedensten\n Profitraten und verschiedne Raten des Mehrwerths, unter\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"509\", \"facs\": \"#f0528\"} --> bestimmten Umständen, sich in derselben Profitrate ausdrücken\n können.\n\n\n\n\nNach dem dritten Gesetz unterstellt der Grössenwechsel des Mehr-\n werths eine durch Wechsel in der Produktivkraft der Arbeit verursachte\n Werthbewegung der Arbeitskraft. Die Grenze jenes Wechsels ist\n durch die neue Werthgrenze der Arbeitskraft gegeben. Es\n können aber, auch wenn die Umstände dem Gesetz zu wirken erlauben,\n Zwischenbewegungen stattfinden. Fällt z. B. in Folge erhöhter Produk-\n tivkraft der Arbeit der Werth der Arbeitskraft von 4 sh. auf 3, oder die\n nothwendige Arbeitszeit von 8 Stunden auf 6, so könnte der Preis der\n Arbeitskraft nur auf 3 sh. 8 d., 3 sh. 6 d., 3 sh. 2 d. u. s. w. fallen, der\n Mehrwerth daher nur auf 3 sh. 4 d., 3 sh. 6 d., 3 sh. 10 d. u. s. w.\n steigen. Der Grad des Falls, dessen Minimalgrenze 3 sh., hängt\n von dem relativen Gewicht ab, das der Druck des Kapitals von der einen\n Seite, der Widerstand der Arbeiter von der andern Seite in die Wag-\n schale wirft.\n\n\n\n\nDer Werth der Arbeitskraft ist bestimmt durch den Werth eines be-\n stimmten Quantums von Lebensmitteln. Was mit der Produktivkraft der\n Arbeit wechselt, ist der Werth dieser Lebensmittel, nicht ihre Masse.\n Die Masse selbst kann, bei steigender Produktivkraft der Arbeit, für Ar-\n beiter und Kapitalist gleichzeitig und in demselben Verhält-\n niss wachsen, ohne irgend einen Grössenwechsel zwischen Preis der\n Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth. Ist z. B. der ursprüngliche Werth der Ar-\n beitskraft gleich 3 sh. und beträgt die nothwendige Arbeitszeit 6 Stunden,\n ist der Mehrwerth ebenfalls gleich 3 sh. oder beträgt die Mehrarbeit auch 6\n Stunden, so würde eine Verdopplung in der Produktivkraft der Arbeit, bei\n gleichbleibender Theilung des Arbeitstags, Preis der Arbeitskraft und Mehr-\n werth unverändert lassen. Nur stellte sich jeder derselben in doppelt\n so vielen, aber verhältnissmässig verwohlfeilerten Gebrauchswerthen dar. Ob-\n gleich der Preis der Arbeitskraft unverändert, wäre er über ihren\nWerth gestiegen. Fiele der Preis der Arbeitskraft, aber nicht zur\n Minimalgrenze ihres neuen Werths von 1½ sh., sondern nur auf 2 sh.\n 10 d., 2 sh. 6 d. u. s. w., so repräsentirte dieser fallende Preis immer\n noch eine wachsende Masse von Lebensmitteln. Der Preis der Arbeits-\n kraft könnte so bei steigender Produktivkraft der Arbeit beständig fallen\n mit gleichzeitigem, fortwährendem Wachsthum der Lebensmittelmasse des\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"510\", \"facs\": \"#f0529\"} --> Arbeiters. Relativ aber, d. h. verglichen mit dem Mehrwerth, sänke der\n Werth der Arbeitskraft beständig, und erweiterte sich also die Kluft zwi-\n schen den Lebenslagen von Arbeiter und Kapitalist [Note: „When an alteration takes place in the productiveness of industry, and\n that either more or less is produced by a given quantity of labour and capital, the\n proportion of wages may obviously vary, whilst the quantity, which that proportion\n represents, remains the same, or the quantity may vary, whilst the proportion\n remains the same.“ („Outlines of Political Economy etc.“, p. 67.)] .\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## B) Constanter Arbeitstag, constante Produktivkraft\n der Arbeit, Intensivität der Arbeit variabel.\n\n\n\n\nWachsende Intensivität der Arbeit unterstellt vermehrte Ausgabe von\n Arbeit in demselben Zeitraum. Der intensivere Arbeitstag verkörpert sich\n daher in mehr Produkten als der minder intensive von gleicher Stunden-\n zahl. Mit erhöhter Produktivkraft liefert zwar auch derselbe Arbeitstag\n mehr Produkte. Aber im letztern Fall sinkt der Werth des einzelnen\n Produkts, weil es weniger Arbeit als vorher kostet, im erstern Fall bleibt\n er unverändert, weil das Produkt nach wie vor gleich viel Arbeit kostet.\n Die Zahl der Produkte steigt hier ohne Fall ihres Preises. Mit ihrer An-\n zahl wächst ihre Preissumme, während dort dieselbe Werthsumme sich\n nur in vergrösserter Produktenmasse darstellt. Bei gleichbleibender\n Stundenzahl verkörpert sich also der intensivere Arbeitstag in höherem\n Werthprodukt, also, bei gleichbleibendem Werth des Geldes, in mehr Geld.\n Sein Werthprodukt variirt mit den Abweichungen seiner Intensivität von\n dem gesellschaftlichen Normalgrad. Derselbe Arbeitstag stellt sich also\n nicht wie vorher in einem constanten, sondern in einem variablen\n Werthprodukt dar, der intensivere, zwölfstündige Arbeitstag z. B. in\n 7 sh., 8 sh u. s. w. statt in 6 sh. wie der zwölfstündige Arbeitstag von\n gewöhnlicher Intensivität. Es ist klar: Variirt das Werthprodukt\n des Arbeitstags, etwa von 6 auf 8 sh., so können beide Theile\n dieses Werthprodukts, Preis der Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth,\ngleichzeitig wachsen, sei es in gleichem oder ungleichem Grad. Preis\n der Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth können beide zur selben Zeit von 3 sh.\n auf 4 wachsen, wenn das Werthprodukt von 6 auf 8 sh. steigt. Preis-\n erhöhung der Arbeitskraft schliesst hier nicht nothwendig Steigerung ihres\n Preises über ihren Werth ein. Sie kann umgekehrt von einem Fall\n ihres Werths begleitet scin. Diess findet stets statt, wenn die Preiser-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"511\", \"facs\": \"#f0530\"} --> höhung der Arbeitskraft ihren beschleunigten Versch’eiss nicht kom-\n pensirt.\n\n\n\n\nMan weiss, dass mit vorübergehenden, im vorigen Kapitel erklärten\n Ausnahmen, veränderte Produktivität der Arbeit nur dann einen Grössen-\n wechsel im Werth der Arbeitskraft und daher in der Grösse des Mehr-\n werths bewirkt, wenn die Produkte der betroffenen Industriezweige in den\n gewohnheitsmässigen Konsum des Arbeiters eingehen. Diese Schranke\n fällt hier fort. Ob die Grösse der Arbeit extensiv oder intensiv wechsle,\n ihrem Grössenwechsel entspricht ein Wechsel in der Grösse ihres Werth-\n produkts, unabhängig von der Natur des Artikels, worin sich dieser\n Werth darstellt.\n\n\n\n\nSteigerte sich die Intensivität der Arbeit in allen Industriezweigen, so\n würde der neue höhere Intensivitätsgrad nun seinerseits zum gewöhnlichen\n gesellschaftlichen Normalgrad der Arbeit und hörte damit auf als extensive\n Grösse zu zählen. Indess blieben selbst dann die durchschnittlichen Inten-\n sivitätsgrade der Arbeit bei verschiednen Nationen verschieden und modi-\n ficirten daher die Anwendung des Werthgesetzes auf unterschiedne Natio-\n nalarbeitstage. Der intensivere Arbeitstag der einen Nation stellt sich in\n höherem Geldausdruck dar als der minder intensive der andern [Note: „All things being equal, the English manufacturer can turn out a con-\n siderably larger amount of work in a given time than a foreign manufacturer, so\n much as to counterbalance the difference of the working days, between 60 hours\n a week here and 72 or 80 elsewhere.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact. for\n 31st Oct. 1855“, p. 65.) Grössere gesetzliche Verkürzung des Arbeitstags in\n den kontinentalen Fabriken wäre das unfehlbarste Mittel zur Verminderung dieser\n Differenz zwischen der kontinentalen und der englischen Arbeitsstunde.] .\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## C) Produktivkraft und Intensivität der Arbeit con-\n stant, Arbeitstag variabel.\n\n\n\n\nDer Arbeitstag kann nach zwei Richtungen variiren. Er kann ver-\n kürzt oder verlängert werden.\n\n\n\n\nVerkürzung des Arbeitstags unter den gegebnen Be-\n dingungen, d. h. gleichbleibender Produktivkraft und Intensivität der Ar-\n beit, lässt den Werth der Arbeitskraft und daher die nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit unverändert. Sie verkürzt die Mehrarbeit und den Mehrwerth.\n Mit der absoluten Grösse des letztern fällt auch seine relative Grösse, d. h.\n seine Grösse im Verhältniss zur gleichbleibenden Werthgrösse der Arbeits-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"512\", \"facs\": \"#f0531\"} --> kraft. Nur durch Herabdrückung ihres Preises unter ihren Werth könnte\n der Kapitalist sich schadlos halten.\n\n\n\n\nAlle hergebrachten Redensarten wider die Verkürzung des Arbeits-\n tags unterstellen, dass das Phänomen sich unter den hier vorausgesetzten\n Umständen ereignet, während in der Wirklichkeit umgekehrt Wechsel in\n der Produktivität und Intensivität der Arbeit entweder der Verkürzung des\n Arbeitstags vorhergehn oder ihr unmittelbar nachfolgen [Note: „There are compensating circumstances … which the working of the\n Ten Hours’ Act has brought to light.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact. for\n 1. December 1848“, p. 7.)] .\n\n\n\n\nVerlängerung des Arbeitstags. Die nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit sei gleich 6 Stunden oder der Werth der Arbeitskraft gleich 3 sh.,\n ebenso Mehrarbeit gleich 6 Stunden und Mehrwerth gleich 3 sh. Der\n Gesammtarbeitstag beträgt dann 12 Stunden und stellt sich in einem\n Werthprodukt von 6 sh. dar. Wird der Arbeitstag um 2 Stunden ver-\n längert und bleibt der Preis der Arbeitskraft unverändert, so wächst mit\n der absoluten die relative Grösse des Mehrwerths. Obgleich die\n Werthgrösse der Arbeitskraft absolut unverändert bleibt, fällt sie relativ.\n Unter den Bedingungen von A) konnte die relative Werthgrösse der Ar-\n beitskraft nicht wechseln ohne einen Wechsel ihrer absoluten Grösse. Hier,\n im Gegentheil, ist der relative Grössenwechsel im Werth der Arbeitskraft\n das Resultat eines absoluten Grössenwechsels des Mehrwerths.\n\n\n\n\nDa das Werthprodukt, worin sich der Arbeitstag darstellt, mit seiner\n eignen Verlängerung wächst, können Preis der Arbeitskraft und\n Mehrwerth gleichzeitig wachsen, sei es um gleiches oder un-\n gleiches Increment. Diess gleichzeitige Wachsthum ist also in zwei\n Fällen möglich, bei absoluter Verlängerung des Arbeitstags, und bei\n wachsender Intensivität der Arbeit ohne solche Verlängerung.\n\n\n\n\nMit verlängertem Arbeitstag kann der Preis der Arbeitskraft unter\n ihren Werth fallen, obgleich er nominell unverändert bleibt oder\n selbst steigt. Der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft ist nämlich, wie man\n sich erinnern wird, geschätzt auf ihre normale Durchschnittsdauer oder\n die normale Lebensperiode des Arbeiters, und auf entsprechenden, normalen,\n der Menschennatur angemessenen Umsatz von Lebenssubstanz in Bewe-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"513\", \"facs\": \"#f0532\"} --> gung [Note: „The amount of labour which a man had undergone in the course of 24\n hours might be approximatively arrived at by an examination of the chymical\n changes which had taken place in his body, changed forms in matter indicating\n the anterior exercise of dynamic force.“ (Grove: „On the Correlation\n of Physical Forces.“)] . Bis zu einem gewissen Punkt kann der von Verlängerung des\n Arbeitstags untrennbare grössere Verschleiss der Arbeitskraft durch grös-\n seren Ersatz kompensirt werden. Ueber diesen Punkt hinaus wächst der\n Verschleiss in geometrischer Progression und werden zugleich alle normalen\n Reproduktions- und Bethätigungsbedingungen der Arbeitskraft zerstört.\n Der Preis der Arbeitskraft und ihr Exploitationsgrad hören auf mit ein-\n ander kommensurable Grössen zu sein.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## D) Gleichzeitige Variationen in Länge des Arbeits-\n tags, Produktivkraft und Intensivität der Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nEs ist hier offenbar eine grosse Anzahl Kombinationen möglich. Je\n zwei Faktoren können variiren und einer constant bleiben, oder alle drei\n können gleichzeitig variiren. Sie können in gleichem oder ungleichem\n Grad variiren, in derselben oder entgegengesetzter Richtung, ihre Varia-\n tionen sich daher theilweis oder ganz aufheben. Indess ist die Analyse\n aller möglichen Fälle nach den unter A) B) und C) gegebnen Aufschlüssen\n leicht. Man findet das Resultat jeder möglichen Kombination, indem man\n der Reihe nach je einen Faktor als variabel und die andern zunächst als\n constant behandelt. Wir nehmen hier daher nur noch kurze Notiz von\n zwei wichtigen Fällen.\n\n\n\n\nAbnehmende Produktivkraft der Arbeit mit gleich-\n zeitiger Verlängerung des Arbeitstags.\n\n\n\n\nWenn wir hier von abnehmender Produktivkraft der Arbeit sprechen, so\n handelt es sich von Arbeitszweigen, deren Produkte den Werth der Arbeitskraft\n bestimmen, also z. B. von abnehmender Produktivkraft der Arbeit in Folge\n zunehmender Unfruchtbarkeit des Bodens und entsprechender Vertheurung\n der Bodenprodukte. Der Arbeitstag sei zwölfstündig, sein Werthprodukt\n 6 sh., wovon die Hälfte den Werth der Arbeitskraft ersetzt, die andre\n Hälfte Mehrwerth bildet. Der Arbeitstag zerfällt also in 6 Stunden noth-\n wendiger Arbeit und 6 Stunden Mehrarbeit. In Folge der Vertheurung\n der Bodenprodukte steige der Werth der Arbeitskraft von 3 auf 4 sh.,\n [Running matter: I. 33] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"514\", \"facs\": \"#f0533\"} --> also die nothwendige Arbeitszeit von 6 auf 8 Stunden. Bleibt der Arbeits-\n tag unverändert, so fällt die Mehrarbeit von 6 auf 4 Stunden, der Mehr-\n werth von 3 auf 2 sh. Wird der Arbeitstag um 2 Stunden verlängert,\n also von 12 auf 14 Stunden, so bleibt die Mehrarbeit 6 Stunden, der\n Mehrwerth 3 sh., aber seine Grösse im Vergleich zum Werth der Arbeitskraft,\n gemessen durch die nothwendige Arbeit, fällt. Wird der Arbeitstag um 4\n Stunden verlängert, von 12 auf 16 Stunden, so bleiben die propor-\n tionellen Grössen von Mehrwerth und Werth der Arbeitskraft, Mehr-\n arbeit und nothwendiger Arbeit unverändert, aber die absolute\n Grösse des Mehrwerths wächst von 3 auf 4 sh., die der Mehrarbeit von\n 6 auf 8 Arbeitsstunden, also um ⅓ oder 33⅓ %. Bei abnehmen-\n der Produktivkraft der Arbeit und gleichzeitiger Ver-\n längerung des Arbeitstags kann also die absolute Grösse des\n Mehrwerths unverändert bleiben, während seine proportionelle Grösse fällt;\n seine proportionelle Grösse kann unverändert bleiben, während seine abso-\n lute Grösse wächst, und, je nach dem Grad der Verlängerung, können\n beide wachsen. Diess ist eine der Ursachen, warum in England von\n 1799—1815, grade als West, Ricardo u. s. w. den nur in ihrer Phan-\n tasie durch Vertheurung der Bodenprodukte bewirkten Fall der Rate des\n Mehrwerths zum Ausgangspunkt wichtiger Analysen machten, der Mehr-\n werth absolut und relativ stieg, und daher zugleich beschleunigtes Wachs-\n thum des Kapitals und Verpauperung der Arbeiter stattfanden [Note: „A principal cause of the increase of capital, during the war, pro-\n ceeded from the greater exertions, and perhaps the greater privations\n of the labouring classes, the most numerous in every society. More women\n and children were compelled, by necessitous circumstances, to enter upon labori-\n ous occupations; and former workmen were, from the same cause, obliged to\n devote a greater portion of their time to increase production.“\n („Essays on Political Econ. in which are illustrated the Prin-\n cipal Causes of the Present National Distress. London 1830“,\n p. 248.)] . Es\n war diess die Periode, worin die masslose Verlängerung des Arbeitstags\n sich Bürgerrecht erwarb [Note: „Corn and Labour rarely march quite abreast; but there is an obvious\n limit, beyond which they cannot be separated. With regard to the unusual\n exertions made by the labouring classes in periods of dearness, which pro-\n duce the fall of wages noticed in the evidence (nämlich vor den Par-] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"515\", \"facs\": \"#f0534\"} -->\n\nZunehmende Intensivität und Produktivkraft der\n Arbeit mit gleichzeitiger Verkürzung des Arbeitstags.\n\n\n\n\nGesteigerte Produktivkraft der Arbeit und ihre wachsende Intensivi-\n tät wirken nach einer Seite hin gleichförmig. Beide vermehren die Pro-\n duktenmasse in gegebnem Zeitraum. Beide verkürzen also den Theil des\n Arbeitstags, den der Arbeiter zur Produktion seiner Lebensmittel oder\n ihres Aequivalents braucht. Die absolute Grenze des Arbeitstags\n wird überhaupt gebildet durch diesen seinen nothwendigen, aber\nkontraktilen Bestandtheil. Schrumpfte darauf der ganze Arbeits-\n tag zusammen, so verschwände die Mehrarbeit, was unter dem Regime des\n Kapitals unmöglich. Die Beseitigung der kapitalistischen Produktionsform\n erlaubt den Arbeitstag durch die nothwendige Arbeit zu be-\n schränken. Jedoch würde die letztere mit ihrem Begriff, unter sonst gleich-\n bleibenden Umständen, auch ihren Raum ausdehnen. Einerseits weil die\n Lebensbedingungen des Arbeiters reicher und seine Lebensansprüche\n grösser. Andrerseits würde ein Theil der jetzigen Mehrarbeit zur noth-\n [Note: liamentary Committees of Inquiry 1814—15), they are most meritorious in the\n individuals, and certainly favour the growth of capital. But no man of\n humanity could wish to see them constant and unremitted. They are\n most admirable as a temporary relief; but if they were constantly in action,\n effects of a similar kind would result from them, as from the population of a\n country being pushed to the very extreme limits of its food.“ (Malthus:\n „Inquiry into the Nature and Progress of Rent. Lond. 1815“,\n p. 48 Note.) Es macht Malthus alle Ehre, dass er die auch an andrer Stelle in\n seinem Pamphlet direkt ausgesprochne Verlängerung des Arbeitstags\n betont, während Ricardo und Andre, im Angesicht der schreiendsten Thatsachen,\n an ihrer Schulansicht von der constanten Grösse des Arbeitstags dogmatisch\n festhielten, und sie in der That allen ihren Untersuchungen zu Grund legten. Aber\n die konservativen Interessen, deren Knecht Malthus war, hinderten ihn zu sehn,\n dass die masslose Verlängerung des Arbeitstags, zugleich mit ausserordentlicher\n Entwicklung der Maschinerie und der Exploitation der Weiber- und Kinderarbeit,\n einen grossen Theil der Arbeiterklasse „überzählig“ machen mussten, namentlich\n sobald die Kriegsnachfrage und das englische Monopol des Weltmarkts aufhörten.\n Es war natürlich weit bequemer und den Interessen der herrschenden Klassen, die\n Malthus ächt pfäffisch idolatrisirt, viel entsprechender, diese „Uebervölkerung“\n aus den ewigen Gesetzen der Natur, als aus den nur historischen Naturgesetzen\n der kapitalistischen Produktion zu erklären.] \n [Running matter: 33*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"516\", \"facs\": \"#f0535\"} --> wendigen Arbeit zählen, nämlich die zur Erzielung eines gesellschaftlichen\n Reservefonds und Accumulationsfonds nöthige Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nJe mehr die Produktivkraft der Arbeit wächst, um so mehr kann der\n Arbeitstag verkürzt werden, und je mehr der Arbeitstag verkürzt wird,\n desto mehr kann die Intensivität der Arbeit wachsen. Die Produk-\n tivkraft der Arbeit wächst, gesellschaftlich betrachtet, auch mit der\n Oekonomie derselben. Diese schliesst nicht nur die Oekonomisi-\n rung der Produktionsmittel ein, sondern die Vermeidung aller nutzlosen\n Arbeit. Während die kapitalistische Produktionsweise in jedem indivi-\n duellen Geschäft Oekonomie erzwingt, erzeugt ihr anarchisches System\n der Konkurrenz die massloseste Verschwendung der gesellschaftlichen\n Produktionsmittel und Arbeitskräfte, neben einer Unzahljetzt unentbehrlicher,\n aber an und für sich überflüssiger Funktionen.\n\n\n\n\nIntensivität und Produktivkraft der Arbeit gegeben, ist der zur\nmateriellen Produktion nothwendige Theil des gesell-\n schaftlichen Arbeitstags um so kürzer, der für freie, geistige\n und gesellschaftliche Bethätigung der Individuen eroberte Zeittheil also\n um so grösser, je gleichmässiger die Arbeit unter alle werkfähigen\n Glieder der Gesellschaft vertheilt ist, je weniger eine Gesellschaftsschichte\n die Naturnothwendigkeit der Arbeit von sich selbst ab- und einer andern\n Schichte zuwälzen kann. Die absolute Grenze für die Verkürzung des\n Arbeitstags ist nach dieser Seite hin die Allgemeinheit der Arbeit.\n In der kapitalistischen Gesellschaft wird freie Zeit für eine Klasse produ-\n cirt durch Verwandlung aller Lebenszeit der Massen in Arbeitszeit.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## 3) Verschiedne Formeln für die Rate des Mehrwerths.\n\n\n\n\nMan hat gesehn, dass die Rate des Mehrwerths sich dar-\n stellt in den Formeln:\n\n\n\n\nI)\n.\n Die zwei ersten Formeln stellen als Verhältniss von Werthen dar, was die\n dritte als Verhältniss der Zeiten, worin diese Werthe producirt werden.\n Diese einander ersetzenden Formeln sind begrifflich streng. Man findet\n sie daher wohl der Sache nach, aber nicht bewusst ausgearbeitet in der\n klassischen politischen Oekonomie. Hier begegnen wir dagegen den fol-\n genden abgeleiteten Formeln.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"517\", \"facs\": \"#f0536\"} -->\n\nII)\n. Eine und die-\n selbe Proportion ist hier abwechselnd ausgedrückt in der Form der Arbeits-\n zeiten, der Werthe, worin sie sich verkörpern, der Produkte, worin diese\n Werthe existiren. Es wird natürlich unterstellt, dass unter Werth des\n Produkts nur das Werthprodukt des Arbeitstags zu verstehn, der\n constante Theil des Produktenwerths aber ausgeschlossen ist.\n\n\n\n\nIn allen diesen Formeln ist der wirkliche Exploitations-\n grad der Arbeit oder die Rate des Mehrwerths falsch ausge-\n drückt. Der Arbeitstag sei 12 Stunden. Mit den andern Annahmen unsres\n früheren Beispiels stellt sich in diesem Fall der wirkliche Exploitations-\n grad der Arbeit dar in den Proportionen: \n. Nach den Formeln II) er-\n halten wir dagegen: \n = 50 %.\n\n\n\n\nDiese abgeleiteten Formeln drücken in der That die Proportion\n aus, worin der Arbeitstag oder sein Werthprodukt sich zwischen\n Kapitalist und Arbeiter theilt. Gelten sie daher als unmittelbare Aus-\n drücke des Selbstverwerthungsgrades des Kapitals, so gilt das falsche\n Gesetz: die Mehrarbeit oder der Mehrwerth kann nie\n 100 % erreichen [Note: So z. B. in: „Dritter Brief an v. Kirchmann von Rodbertus.\n Widerlegung der Ricardo’schen Theorie von der Grundrente\n und Begründung einer neuen Rententheorie. Berlin 1851“. Ich\n komme später auf diese Schrift zurück, die trotz ihrer falschen Theorie von der\n Grundrente das Wesen der kapitalistischen Produktion durchschaut.] . Da die Mehrarbeit stets nur einen aliquoten\n Theil des Arbeitstags oder der Mehrwerth stets nur einen aliquoten Theil\n des Werthprodukts bilden kann, ist die Mehrarbeit nothwendiger Weise\n stets kleiner als der Arbeitstag oder der Mehrwerth stets kleiner als das\n Werthprodukt. Um sich zu verhalten wie \\frac{100}{100}, müssten sie aber gleich\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"518\", \"facs\": \"#f0537\"} --> sein. Damit die Mehrarbeit den ganzen Arbeitstag absorbire (es handelt\n sich hier um den Durchschnittstag der Arbeitswoche, des Arbeitsjahrs\n u. s. w.), müsste die nothwendige Arbeit auf Null sinken. Verschwindet\n aber die nothwendige Arbeit, so verschwindet auch die Mehrarbeit, da\n letztre nur eine Funktion der erstern. Die Proportion \\frac{Mehrarbeit}{Arbeitstag} =\n\\frac{Mehrwerth}{Werthprodukt} kann also niemals die Grenze \\frac{100}{100} erreichen und noch we-\n niger auf \\frac{100 + x}{100} steigen. Wohl aber die Rate des Mehrwerths oder\n der wirkliche Exploitationsgrad der Arbeit. Nimm z. B. die Schätzung\n des Herrn L. de Lavergne, wonach der englische Ackerbauarbeiter nur\n ¼, der Kapitalist (Pächter) dagegen ¾ des Produkts [Note: Der Theil des Produkts, der nur das ausgelegte constante Kapital ersetzt,\n ist bei dieser Rechnung selbstverständlich abgezogen. — Herr L. de Lavergne,\n blinder Bewunderer Englands, giebt eher zu niedriges als zu hohes Verhältniss.]  oder seines\n Werths erhält, wie die Beute sich immer zwischen Kapitalist und Grund-\n eigenthümer u. s. w. nachträglich weiter vertheile. Die Mehrarbeit des\n englischen Landarbeiters verhält sich danach zu seiner nothwendigen Arbeit\n = 3 : 1, ein Prozentsatz der Exploitation von 300 %.\n\n\n\n\nDie Schulmethode, den Arbeitstag als constante Grösse zu be-\n handeln, wurde durch Anwendung der Formeln II) befestigt, weil man hier\n die Mehrarbeit stets mit einem Arbeitstag von gegebner Grösse vergleicht.\n Ebenso, wenn die Theilung des Werthprodukts ausschliesslich\n in’s Auge gefasst wird. Der Arbeitstag, der sich bereits in einem Werth-\n produkt vergegenständlicht hat, ist stets ein Arbeitstag von gegebnen\n Grenzen.\n\n\n\n\nDie Darstellung von Mehrwerth und Werth der Arbeitskraft als\n Bruchtheilen des Werthprodukts — eine Darstellungsweise, die übrigens\n aus der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise selbst erwächst und deren Be-\n deutung sich später erschliessen wird — versteckt den spezifischen\n Charakter des Kapitalverhältnisses, nämlich den Austausch des variablen\n Kapitals mit der lebendigen Arbeitskraft, und den entsprechenden\n Ausschluss des Arbeiters vom Produkt. An die Stelle tritt der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"519\", \"facs\": \"#f0538\"} --> falsche Schein eines Associationsverhältnisses, worin Arbeiter und Kapi-\n talist das Produkt nach dem Verhältniss seiner verschiednen Bildungs-\n faktoren theilen [Note: Da alle entwickelten Formen des kapitalistischen Produktionsprozesses\n Formen der Cooperation sind, ist natürlich nichts leichter, als von ihrem spezi-\n fisch antagonistischen Charakter zu abstrahiren und sie so in freie Associations-\n formen umzufabeln, wie in des Grafen A. de Laborde: „De l’Esprit de\n l’Association dans tous les intérêts de la Communauté. Paris\n 1818“. Der Yankee H. Carey bringt diess Kunststück mit demselben Erfolg\n gelegentlich selbst an den Verhältnissen des Sklavensystems fertig.] .\n\n\n\n\nUebrigens sind die Formeln II stets in die Formeln I rückverwan-\n delbar. Haben wir z. B. \\frac{Mehrarbeit von 6 Stunden}{Arbeitstag von 12 Stunden}, so ist die noth-\n wendige Arbeitszeit = Arbeitstag von zwölf Stunden\n — Mehrarbeit von sechs Stunden, und so ergiebt sich:\n\\frac{Mehrarbeit von 6 Stunden}{Nothwendige Arbeit von 6 Stunden} = \\frac{100}{100}.\n\n\n\n\nEine dritte Formel, die ich gelegentlich schon anticipirt habe, ist:\n\n\n\n\nIII)\n\\frac{Mehrwerth}{Werth der Arbeitskraft} = \\frac{Mehrarbeit}{Nothwendige Arbeit} = \\frac{Unbezahlte Arbeit}{Bezahlte Arbeit}.\n\n\n\n\nDas Missverständniss, wozu die Formel \\frac{Unbezahlte Arbeit}{Bezahlte Arbeit} verleiten\n könnte, als zahle der Kapitalist die Arbeit und nicht die Arbeitskraft, fällt\n nach der früher gegebnen Entwicklung fort. \\frac{Unbezahlte Arbeit}{Bezahlte Arbeit} ist nur\npopulärer Ausdruck für \\frac{Mehrarbeit}{Nothwendige Arbeit}. Der Kapitalist zahlt\n den Werth, resp. davon abweichenden Preis der Arbeitskraft, und erhält im\n Austausch die Verfügung über die lebendige Arbeitskraft selbst. Seine\n Nutzniessung dieser Arbeitskraft zerfällt in zwei Perioden. Während der\n einen Periode producirt der Arbeiter nur einen Werth = Werth seiner\n Arbeitskraft, also nur ein Aequivalent. Für den vorgeschossnen Preis der\n Arbeitskraft erhält der Kapitalist so ein Produkt vom selben Preis. Es ist\n als ob er das Produkt fertig auf dem Markt gekauft hätte. In der Periode\n der Mehrarbeit dagegen bildet die Nutzniessung der Arbeitskraft Werth für\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"520\", \"facs\": \"#f0539\"} --> den Kapitalisten, ohne ihm einen Werthersatz zu kosten [Note: Obgleich die Physiokraten das Geheimniss des Mehrwerths nicht durch-\n schauten, war ihnen doch so viel klar, dass er „une richesse indépendante et\n disponible, qu’il (der Besitzer davon) n’a point achetée et qu’il vend.“\n (Turgot: „Réflexions sur la Formation et la Distribution des\n Richesses“, p. 11.)] . Er hat diese\n Flüssigmachung der Arbeitskraft umsonst. In diesem Sinn kann diese\n Mehrarbeit unbezahlte Arbeit heissen.\n\n\n\n\nDas Kapital ist also nicht nur Kommando über Arbeit, wie\n A. Smith sagt. Es ist wesentlich Kommando über unbezahlte\n Arbeit. Aller Mehrwerth, in welcher besondern Gestalt von Profit,\n Zins, Rente u. s. w. er sich später krystallisire, ist seiner Substanz nach\nMateriatur unbezahlter Arbeitszeit. Das Geheimniss von der\nSelbstverwerthung des Kapitals löst sich auf in seine Ver-\n fügung über ein bestimmtes Quantum unbezahlter frem-\n der Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## 4) Werth, resp. Preis der Arbeitskraft in der ver-\n wandelten Form des Arbeitslohns.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## a) Die Formverwandlung.\n\n\n\n\nAuf der Oberfläche der bürgerlichen Gesellschaft erscheint der\n Lohn des Arbeiters als Lohn der Arbeit, ein bestimmtes Quantum\n Geld, das für ein bestimmtes Quantum Arbeit gezahlt wird. Man spricht\n hier von einem Werth der Arbeit und nennt den Geldausdruck dieses\n Werths den nothwendigen oder natürlichen Preis der Ar-\n beit. Man spricht andrerseits von Marktpreisen der Arbeit,\n d. h. über oder unter ihrem nothwendigen Preis oscillirenden Preisen.\n\n\n\n\nAber was ist der Werth einer Waare? Die Vergegenständlichung\n der zu ihrer Produktion gesellschaftlich nothwendigen Arbeit. Und wo-\n durch messen wir die Grösse ihres Werths? Durch die Grösse der in\n ihr enthaltenen Arbeitszeit. Wodurch wäre also der Werth z. B.\n eines zwölfstündigen Arbeitstags bestimmt? Durch die in einem Arbeits-\n tag von 12 Stunden enthaltenen 12 Arbeitsstunden, was eine abge-\n schmackte Tautologie ist [Note: „Mr. Ricardo, ingeniously enough, avoids a difficulty which, on a first\n view, threatens to encumber his doctrine, that value depends on the quantity of\n labour employed in production. If this principle is rigidly adhered to, it follows] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"521\", \"facs\": \"#f0540\"} -->\n\nUm als Waare auf dem Markt verkauft zu werden, müsste die Arbeit\n jedenfalls existiren, bevor sie verkauft wird. Könnte der Arbeiter ihr\n aber eine selbstständige Existenz geben, so würde er Waare verkaufen\n und nicht Arbeit [Note: „If you call labour a commodity, it is not like a commodity which is\n first produced in order to exchange, and then brought to market where it must\n exchange with other commodities according to the respective quantities of each\n which there may be at the market in the time; labour is created at the moment\n it is brought to market; nay, it is brought to market before it is created.“\n („Observations on some verbal disputes etc.“, p. 75, 76.)] .\n\n\n\n\nVon diesen Widersprüchen abgesehn, würde ein direkter Austausch\n von Geld, d. h. vergegenständlichter Arbeit, mit lebendiger Arbeit entweder\n das Werthgesetz aufheben, welches sich grade erst auf Grundlage\n der kapitalistischen Produktion frei entwickelt, oder die kapitalistische\n Produktion selbst aufheben, welche grade auf der Lohnarbeit\n beruht. Der Arbeitstag von 12 Stunden stellt sich z. B. in einem Geldwerth\n von 6 sh. dar. Werden Aequivalente ausgetauscht, so erhält der Arbeiter\n für zwölfstündige Arbeit 6 sh. Der Preis seiner Arbeit wäre gleich dem\n Preis seines Produkts. In diesem Fall producirte er keinen Mehrwerth\n für den Käufer seiner Arbeit, die 6 sh. verwandelten sich nicht in Kapital,\n die Grundlage der kapitalistischen Produktion verschwände, aber grade\n auf dieser Grundlage verkauft er seine Arbeit und ist seine Arbeit\n wesentlich Lohnarbeit. Oder er erhält für 12 Stunden Arbeit weniger\n als 6 sh., d. h. weniger als 12 Stunden Arbeit. Zwölf Stunden Arbeit\n tauschen sich aus gegen 10, 6 u. s. w. Stunden Arbeit. Diese Gleichsetzung\n ungleicher Grössen hebt nicht nur die Werthbestimmung auf. Ein\n [Note: that the value of labour depends of the quantity of labour em-\n ployed in producing it — which is evidently absurd. By a dexterous turn,\n therefore, Mr. Ricardo makes the value of labour depend on the quantity of labour\n required to produce wages, or, to give him the benefit of his own language, he\n maintains, that the value of labour is to be estimated by the quantity of labour\n required to produce wages; by which he means the quantity of labour required to\n produce the money or commodities given to the labourer. This is similar to\n saying, that the value of cloth is estimated, not by the quantity of labour bestowed\n on its production, but by the quantity of labour bestowed on the production of\n the silver, for which the cloth is exchanged.“ („A Critical Dissertation on\n the Nature etc. of Value“, p. 50, 51.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"522\", \"facs\": \"#f0541\"} --> solcher sich selbst aufhebender Widerspruch kann überhaupt nicht als Ge-\n setz auch nur ausgesprochen oder formulirt werden [Note: „Treating Labour as a commodity, and Capital, the produce of labour,\n as another, then, if the values of those two commodities were regulated by equal\n quantities of labour, a given amount of labour would . . . . exchange for that\n quantity of capital which had been produced by the same amount of labour;\nantecedent labour would … exchange for the same amount as present\n labour. But the value of labour, in relation to other commodities … is de-\n termined not by equal quantities of labour.“ (E. G. Wakefield in\n s. Edit. von A. Smith’s „Wealth of Nations“, v. I. Lond. 1836. p. 231\n Note.)] .\n\n\n\n\nEs nützt nichts den Austausch von mehr Arbeit gegen weniger Arbeit\n aus dem Formunterschied der Arbeit herzuleiten, dass sie nämlich\n das einemal vergegenständlicht, das andremal lebendig ist [Note: „Il a fallu convenir (auch eine Ausgabe des „contrat social“) que toutes\n les fois qu’il échangerait du travail fait contre du travail à faire, le der-\n nier (le capitaliste) aurait une valeur supérieure au premier (le travailleur).“\n (Simonde (i. e. Sismondi): „De la Richesse Commerciale. Genève\n 1803“, t. I, p. 37.)] .\n Diess ist um so abgeschmackter als der Werth einer Waare nicht\n durch das Quantum wirklich in ihr vergegenständlichter, sondern durch\n das Quantum der zu ihrer Reproduktion nothwendigen lebendigen Arbeit\n bestimmt wird. Eine Waare stelle z. B. 6 Arbeitsstunden dar. Werden\n Erfindungen gemacht, wodurch sie in 3 Stunden producirt werden kann,\n so sinkt der Werth auch der bereits producirten Waare um die Hälfte.\n Sie stellt jetzt 3 statt früher 6 Stunden nothwendige gesellschaftliche\n Arbeit dar. Es ist also das zu ihrer Produktion erheischte Quantum\n Arbeit, nicht deren gegenständliche Form, wodurch ihre Werth-\n grösse bestimmt wird.\n\n\n\n\nWas dem Geldbesitzer auf dem Waarenmarkt direkt gegenübertritt,\n ist in der That nicht die Arbeit, sondern der Arbeiter. Was\n letztrer verkauft, ist seine Arbeitskraft. Sobald seine Arbeit wirk-\n lich beginnt, hat sie bereits aufgehört ihm zu gehören, kann also nicht\n mehr von ihm verkauft werden. Die Arbeit ist die Substanz und das im-\n manente Mass der Werthe, aber sie selbst hat keinen Werth [Note: „Labour, the exclusive standard of value … the creator of all wealth,\nno commodity.“ (Th. Hodgskin l. c. p. 186.)] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"523\", \"facs\": \"#f0542\"} -->\n\nIm Ausdruck: Werth der Arbeit ist der Werthbegriff nicht nur\n völlig ausgelöscht, sondern in sein Gegentheil verkehrt. Es ist ein ima-\n ginärer Ausdruck wie etwa Werth der Erde. Diese imaginären Aus-\n drücke entspringen jedoch aus den Produktionsverhältnissen selbst. Sie\n sind Kategorieen für Erscheinungsformen wesentlicher Werth-\n verhältnisse. Dass in der Erscheinung die Dinge sich oft\n verkehrt darstellen, ist ziemlich in allen Wissenschaften bekannt, ausser\n in der politischen Oekonomie [Note: Diese Ausdrücke dagegen für blosse licentia poetica zu erklären, zeigt nur\n die Ohnmacht der Analyse. Ich bemerke daher gegen Proudhon’s Phrase:\n „Le travail est dit valoir, non pas en tant que marchandise lui-même, mais en\n vue des valeurs qu’on suppose renfermées puissanciellement en lui: La valeur\n du travail est une expression figurée etc.“: „Dans le travail mar-\n chandise, qui est d’une réalité effrayante, il ne voit qu’une ellipse grammaticale.\n Donc toute la société actuelle, fondée sur le travail marchandise, est désormais\n fondée sur une licence poétique, sur une expression figurée. La société veut-elle\n „éliminer tous les inconvénients“, qui la travaillent, eh bien! qu’elle élimine les\n termes malsonnants, qu’elle change de langage, et pour cela elle n’a qu’à s’ad-\n dresser à l’Académie pour lui demander une nouvelle édition de son dictionnaire.“\n (K. Marx: „Misère de la Philosophie“, p. 34, 35.) Noch bequemer\n ist es natürlich, sich unter Werth gar nichts zu denken. Man kann dann ohne\n Umstände alles unter diese Kategorie subsumiren. So z. B. J. B. Say. Was\n ist „valeur“? Antwort: „C’est qu’une chose vaut“, und was ist\n „Prix“? Antwort: „La valeur d’une chose exprimée en monnaie.“ Und\n warum hat „le travail de la terre … une valeur? Parce qu’on y met\nun prix.“ Also Werth ist was ein Ding werth ist, und die Erde hat einen\n „Werth“, weil man ihren Werth „in Geld ausdrückt“. Diess ist jedenfalls\n eine sehr einfache Methode, sich über das why und wherefore der Dinge zu ver-\n ständigen.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie klassische politische Oekonomie entlehnte dem Alltagsleben ohne\n weitere Kritik die Kategorie Preis der Arbeit, um sich dann hinter-\n her zu fragen, wie wird dieser Preis bestimmt? Sie erkannte bald, dass\n der Wechsel im Verhältniss von Nachfrage und Zufuhr für den Preis der\n Arbeit, gleich dem jeder andern Waare, nichts erklärt ausser dem Wech-\n sel des Preises, die Oscillationen der Marktpreise unter oder über eine\n gewisse Grösse. Decken sich Nachfrage und Zufuhr, so hört, unter sonst\n gleichbleibenden Umständen, die Preisoscillation auf. Aber dann hören\n auch Nachfrage und Zufuhr auf irgend etwas zu erklären. Der Preis der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"524\", \"facs\": \"#f0543\"} --> Arbeit, wenn Nachfrage und Zufuhr sich decken, ist der vom Verhältniss der\n Nachfrage und Zufuhr unabhängig bestimmte Preis der Arbeit, ihr natür-\n licher Preis, der so als der eigentliche Gegenstand der Analyse gefunden\n ward. Oder man nahm eine längere Periode der Oscillationen des Markt-\n preises, z. B. ein Jahr, und fand dann, dass sich ihr Auf und Ab aus-\n gleicht zu einer mittlern Durchschnittsgrösse, einer constanten Grösse.\n Sie musste natürlich anders bestimmt werden als die sich kompensirenden\n Abweichungen von ihr selbst. Dieser über die zufälligen Marktpreise der\n Arbeit übergreifende und sie regulirende Preis, nothwendige Preis\n (Physiokraten) oder „natürliche Preis der Arbeit“ (Adam\n Smith) kann, wie bei andern Waaren, nur ihr in Geld ausgedrückter\nWerth sein. In dieser Art glaubte die politische Oekonomie durch die\n zufälligen Preise der Arbeit zu ihrem Werth vorzudringen. Wie bei\n den andern Waaren wurde dieser Werth dann weiter durch die Produk-\n tionskosten bestimmt. Aber was sind die Produktionskosten — des Ar-\n beiters, d. h. die Kosten, um den Arbeiter selbst zu produciren\n oder zu reproduciren? Diese Frage schob sich der politischen Oekonomie\nbewusstlos für die ursprüngliche unter, da sie mit den Produk-\n tionskosten der Arbeit als solcher sich im Kreise drehte und\n nicht vom Flecke kam. Was sie also Werth der Arbeit (value of la-\n bour) nennt, ist in der That der Werth der Arbeitskraft, die in\n der Persönlichkeit des Arbeiters existirt, und von ihrer Funktion, der Ar-\n beit, ebenso verschieden ist, wie eine Maschine von ihren Operationen.\n Beschäftigt mit dem Unterschied zwischen den Marktpreisen der Arbeit\n und ihrem s. g. Werth, mit dem Verhältniss dieses Werths zur Profitrate,\n zu den vermittelst der Arbeit producirten Waarenwerthen u. s. w., ent-\n deckte man niemals, dass der Gang der Analyse nicht nur von den Markt-\n preisen der Arbeit zu ihrem Werth, sondern dahin geführt hatte, diesen\nWerth der Arbeit selbst wieder aufzulösen in den Werth der Ar-\n beitskraft. Die Bewusstlosigkeit über diess Resultat ihrer eignen\n Analyse, die kritiklose Annahme der Kategorieen Werth der Arbeit,\n natürlicher Preis der Arbeit u. s. w. als letzter adäquater Aus-\n drücke des behandelten Werthverhältnisses, verwickelte, wie man später\n sehn wird, die klassische politische Oekonomie in unauflösbare Wirren\n und Widersprüche, während sie der Vulgärökonomie eine sichre Operations-\n basis für ihre principiell nur dem Schein huldigende Flachheit bot.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"525\", \"facs\": \"#f0544\"} -->\n\nSehn wir nun zunächst, wie Werth und Preise der Arbeitskraft in\n ihrer verwandelten Form als Arbeitslohn sich darstellen.\n\n\n\n\nMan weiss, dass der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft berechnet ist auf\n eine gewisse Lebensdauer des Arbeiters, welcher eine gewisse Länge des\n Arbeitstags entspricht. Nimm an, der gewohnheitsmässige Arbeitstag\n betrage 12 Stunden und der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft 3 sh., der\n Geldausdruck eines Werths, worin sich 6 Arbeitsstunden darstellen.\n Erhält der Arbeiter 3 sh., so erhält er den Werth seiner während\n 12 Stunden funktionirenden Arbeitskraft. Wird nun dieser Tages-\n werth der Arbeitskraft als Werth der Tagesarbeit aus-\n gedrückt, so ergiebt sich die Formel: Die zwölfstündige Arbeit hat\n einen Werth von 3 sh. Der Werth der Arbeitskraft bestimmt so\n den Werth der Arbeit oder, in Geld ausgedrückt, ihren nothwen-\n digen Preis. Weicht dagegen der Preis der Arbeitskraft von ihrem\n Werth ab, so ebenfalls der Preis der Arbeit von ihrem s. g. Werth.\n\n\n\n\nDa der Werth der Arbeit nur ein irrationeller Ausdruck für den\n Werth der Arbeitskraft, ergiebt sich von selbst, dass der Werth der\n Arbeit stets kleiner sein muss als ihr Werthprodukt,\n denn der Kapitalist lässt die Arbeitskraft stets länger funktioniren als zur\n Reproduktion ihres eignen Werths nöthig ist. Im obigen Beispiel ist der\n Werth der während 12 Stunden funktionirenden Arbeitskraft 3 sh., ein\n Werth, zu dessen Reproduktion sie 6 Stunden braucht. Ihr Werthprodukt\n ist dagegen 6 sh., weil sie in der That während 12 Stunden funktionirt,\n und ihr Werthprodukt nicht von ihrem eignen Werthe, sondern von der\n Zeitdauer ihrer Funktion abhängt. Man erhält so das auf den ersten\n Blick abgeschmackte Resultat, dass Arbeit, die einen Werth von 6 sh.\n schafft, einen Werth von 3 sh. besitzt [Note: Vgl. „Zur Kritik der politischen Oekonomie“, p. 40, wo ich\n ankündige, dass bei Betrachtung des Kapitals das Problem gelöst werden soll:\n „Wie führt Produktion auf Basis des durch blosse Arbeitszeit bestimmten Tausch-\n werths zum Resultat, dass der Tauschwerth der Arbeit kleiner ist als der Tausch-\n werth ihres Produkts?“] .\n\n\n\n\nMan sieht ferner, dass der Werth von 3 sh., worin sich der\nbezahlte Theil des Arbeitstags, d. h. sechsstündige\n Arbeit darstellt, als Werth oder Preis des Gesammtarbeits-\n tags von 12 Stunden, darunter 6 unbezahlte Stunden, er-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"526\", \"facs\": \"#f0545\"} --> scheint. Die Form des Arbeitslohnes löscht also jede Spur\n der Theilung des Arbeitstags in nothwendige Arbeit\n und Mehrarbeit, in bezahlte und unbezahlte Arbeit völ-\n lig aus. Alle Arbeit erscheint als bezahlte Arbeit. Bei der Frohn-\n arbeit unterscheiden sich räumlich und zeitlich, handgreiflich sinnlich,\n die Arbeit des Fröhners für sich selbst und die Zwangsarbeit für seinen\n Grundherrn. Bei der Sklavenarbeit erscheint selbst der Theil des\n Arbeitstags, worin der Sklave nur den Werth seiner eignen Lebensmittel\n ersetzt, den er in der That also für sich selbst arbeitet, als Arbeit für\n seinen Meister. Alle seine Arbeit erscheint als unbezahlte Arbeit [Note: Der Morning Star, ein bis zur silliness naives Londoner Freihandels-\n organ, betheuerte während des amerikanischen Bürgerkriegs wieder und wieder\n mit aller menschenmöglichen moralischen Indignation, dass die Neger in den\n „Confederate States“ ganz umsonst arbeiteten. Es hätte gefälligst die Tages-\n kost eines solchen Negers mit der des freien Arbeiters im East End von London\n z. B. vergleichen sollen.] . Bei\n der Lohnarbeit erscheint umgekehrt selbst die Mehrarbeit oder unbe-\n zahlte Arbeit als bezahlt. Dort verbirgt das Eigenthumsverhältniss das\nFürsichselbst arbeiten des Sklaven, hier das Geldverhältniss\n das Umsonstarbeiten des Lohnarbeiters.\n\n\n\n\nMan begreift also, von welcher entscheidenden Wichtigkeit die Form-\n verwandlung von Werth und Preis der Arbeitskraft in Arbeitslohn\n oder in Werth und Preis der Arbeit selbst. Auf dieser Erscheinungs-\n form, die das wirkliche Verhältniss unsichtbar macht und grade sein\n Gegentheil zeigt, beruhn alle Rechtsvorstellungen des Arbeiters wie des\n Kapitalisten, alle Mystifikationen der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise,\n alle ihre Freiheitsillusionen, alle apologetischen Flausen der Vulgär-\n ökonomie.\n\n\n\n\nBraucht die Weltgeschichte viele Zeit, um hinter das Geheimniss\n des Arbeitslohns zu kommen, so ist dagegen nichts leichter zu ver-\n stehn als die Nothwendigkeit, die raisons d’être dieser Erscheinungs-\n form.\n\n\n\n\nDer Austausch zwischen Kapital und Arbeit stellt sich der Wahrneh-\n mung zunächst ganz in derselben Art dar wie der Kauf und Verkauf aller\n andern Waaren. Der Käufer giebt eine gewisse Geldsumme, der Verkäu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"527\", \"facs\": \"#f0546\"} --> fer einen von Geld verschiedenen Artikel. Das Rechtsbewusstsein erkennt\n hier höchstens einen stofflichen Unterschied, der sich in den rechtlich äqui-\n valenten Formeln: Do ut des, do ut facias, facio ut des und facio ut facias,\n ausdrückt.\n\n\n\n\nAller Kauf und Verkauf von Waaren ist ferner von der Illusion be-\n gleitet, dass das, was gezahlt wird, der Gebrauchswerth der Waare\n ist, obgleich diese Illusion schon über die einfache Thatsache stolpert, dass\n die verschiedensten Artikel denselben Preis und derselbe Artikel,\n ohne dass sich sein Gebrauchswerth oder das Bedürfniss dafür ändert,\nwechselnde Preise hat. Da aber Tauschwerth und Gebrauchswerth\nan und für sich inkommensurable Grössen sind, so existirt\n von diesem Standpunkt keine grössere Irrationalität in dem Ausdruck\n „Werth der Arbeit“, „Preis der Arbeit“ als in dem Ausdruck „Werth der\n Baumwolle“, „Preis der Baumwolle“. Das Missverständniss ist bei Kauf\n und Verkauf der Arbeit noch unvermeidlicher als bei andern Waaren.\nErstens, weil das Geld im Kauf der Arbeit als Zahlungsmittel\n funktionirt. Der Arbeiter wird gezahlt, nachdem er seine Arbeit geliefert\n hat. Begrifflich aber enthält die Funktion des Geldes als Zahlungsmittel,\n dass es den Werth oder Preis des gelieferten Artikels nachträglich\nrealisirt, also im gegebnen Fall den Werth oder Preis der gelieferten\n Arbeit. Zweitens: Der Gebrauchswerth, den der Arbeiter dem\n Kapitalisten liefert, ist in der That nicht seine Arbeitskraft, sondern ihre\n besondre Funktion, Arbeit von besondrem Inhalt, Schneiderarbeit, Schuster-\n arbeit, Spinnarbeit u. s. w. Dass dieselbe Arbeit nach einer andern\n Seite hin allgemeines werthbildendes Element ist, eine Eigen-\n schaft, wodurch sie sich von allen andern Waaren unterscheidet, fällt\n ausserhalb des Bereichs des gewöhnlichen Bewusstseins.\n\n\n\n\nStellen wir uns auf den Standpunkt des Arbeiters, der für zwölfstün-\n dige Arbeit z. B. das Werthprodukt sechsstündiger Arbeit erhält, sage\n 3 sh., so ist für ihn in der That seine zwölfstündige Arbeit das\n Kaufmittel der 3 sh. Der Werth seiner Arbeitskraft mag variiren\n mit dem Werth seiner gewohnheitsmässigen Lebensmittel von 3 auf 4 sh.\n oder von 3 auf 2 sh., oder bei gleichbleibendem Werth seiner Arbeitskraft\n mag ihr Preis, in Folge wechselnden Verhältnisses von Nachfrage und\n Zufuhr, auf 4 sh. steigen oder auf 2 sh. fallen, er giebt stets 12 Ar-\n beitsstunden. Jeder Wechsel in der Grösse des Aequivalents, das er\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"528\", \"facs\": \"#f0547\"} --> erhält, erscheint ihm daher nothwendig als Wechsel im Werth oder Preis\n dieser 12 Arbeitsstunden. Dieser Umstand verleitete umgekehrt Adam\n Smith, der den Arbeitstag als eine constante Grösse behandelt [Note: A. Smith spielt nur zufällig auf die Variation des Arbeitstags an bei seiner\n Behandlung des Stücklohns.] ,\n zur Behauptung, der Werth der Arbeit sei constant, obgleich\n der Werth der Lebensmittel wechsle und derselbe Arbeitstag sich daher in\n mehr oder weniger Geld für den Arbeiter darstelle.\n\n\n\n\nNehmen wir andrerseits den Kapitalisten, so will er zwar möglichst\n viel Arbeit für möglichst wenig Geld erhalten. Praktisch interessirt ihn\n daher nur die Differenz zwischen dem Preis der Arbeitskraft und dem\n Werth, den ihre Funktion schafft. Aber er sucht alle Waare möglichst\n wohlfeil zu kaufen und erklärt sich überall seinen Profit aus der einfachen\n Prellerei, dem Verkauf über dem Werth. Er kommt daher nicht zur\n Einsicht, dass wenn so ein Ding wie Werth der Arbeit wirklich\n existirte, und er diesen Werth wirklich zahlte, kein Kapital existiren, sein\n Geld sich nicht in Kapital verwandeln würde.\n\n\n\n\nEs kommt hinzu, dass die wirkliche Bewegung des Ar-\n beitslohns Phänomene zeigt, die zu beweisen scheinen, dass nicht der\nWerth der Arbeitskraft, sondern der Werth ihrer Funktion, der\n Arbeitselbst, bezahlt wird. Diese Phänomene können wir auf zwei\n grosse Klassen zurückführen. Erstens: Wechsel des Arbeitslohns mit\n wechselnder Länge des Arbeitstags. Man könnte eben so wohl schliessen,\n dass nicht der Werth der Maschine, sondern der ihrer Operation bezahlt wird,\n weil es mehr kostet eine Maschine für eine Woche als für einen Tag zu\n dingen. Zweitens: Der individuelle Unterschied in den Arbeitslöhnen\n verschiedner Arbeiter, welche dieselbe Funktion verrichten. Diesen indi-\n viduellen Unterschied findet man, aber ohne Anlass zu Illusionen, wieder\n im System der Sklaverei, wo frank und frei, ohne Schnörkel, die Ar-\n beitskraft selbst verkauft wird. Nur fällt der Vortheil einer Ar-\n beitskraft, die über dem Durchschnitt, oder der Nachtheil einer Arbeits-\n kraft, die unter dem Durchschnitt steht, im Sklavensystem dem Sklaven-\n eigner zu, im System der Lohnarbeit dem Arbeiter selbst, weil seine Ar-\n beitskraft in dem einen Fall von ihm selbst, in dem andern von einer dritten\n Person verkauft wird.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"529\", \"facs\": \"#f0548\"} -->\n\nUebrigens gilt von der Erscheinungsform, „Werth und Preis\n der Arbeit“ oder „Arbeitslohn“, im Unterschied zum wesentlichen\n Verhältniss, welches erscheint, dem Werth und Preis der Arbeitskraft,\n dasselbe, was von allen Erscheinungsformen und ihrem verborgnen\n Hintergrund. Die ersteren reproduciren sich unmittelbar, spontan, als gang\n und gäbe Denkformen, der andere muss durch die Wissenschaft erst\nentdeckt werden. Die klassische politische Oekonomie stösst annähernd\n auf den wahren Sachverhalt, ohne ihn jemals bewusst zu formuliren.\n Sie kann das nicht, so lange sie in ihrer bürgerlichen Haut steckt.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## b) Die beiden Grundformen des Arbeitslohns: Zeit-\n lohn und Stücklohn.\n\n\n\n\nDer Arbeitslohn nimmt selbst wieder sehr mannigfaltige\n Formen an, ein Umstand, nicht erkennbar aus den ökonomischen Kom-\n pendien, die in ihrer brutalen Interessirtheit für den Stoff jeden Formunter-\n schied vernachlässigen. Eine Darstellung aller dieser Formen gehört je-\n doch in die specielle Lehre von der Lohnarbeit, also nicht in diess Werk. Da-\n gegen sind die zwei herrschenden Grundformen hier kurz zu entwickeln.\n\n\n\n\nDer Verkauf der Arbeitskraft findet, wie man sich erinnert, stets\nfür bestimmte Zeitperiode statt. Die verwandelte Form, worin\n der Tageswerth, Wochenwerth u. s. w. der Arbeitskraft unmittelbar er-\n scheint, ist daher die des Zeitlohns, also Tageslohn, Wochenlohn u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nEs ist nun zunächst zu bemerken, dass die im 2. Abschnitt dieses\n Kapitels dargestellten Gesetze über den Grössenwechsel von Preis der\n Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth sich durch einfache Formveränderung in Ge-\n setze des Arbeitslohns verwandeln. Ebenso erscheint der Unterschied\n zwischen dem Tauschwerth der Arbeitskraft und der Masse der\n Lebensmittel, worin sich dieser Werth umsetzt, jetzt als Unterschied von\nnominellem und reellem Arbeitslohn. Es wäre nutzlos in der Er-\n scheinungsform zu wiederholen, was in der wesentlichen Form bereits ent-\n wickelt. Wir beschränken uns daher auf wenige, den Zeitlohn charak-\n terisirende Punkte.\n\n\n\n\nDie Geldsumme [Note: Der Geldwerth selbst wird hier immer als constant vorausgesetzt.] , die der Arbeiter für seine Tagesarbeit, Wochenar-\n beit u. s. w. erhält, bildet den wirklichen Betrag seines nominellen oder\ndem Werth nach geschätzten Arbeitslohns. Es ist aber klar, dass\n [Running matter: I. 34] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"530\", \"facs\": \"#f0549\"} --> je nach der Länge des Arbeitstags, also je nach der täglich von ihm gelie-\n ferten Quantität Arbeit, derselbe Tageslohn, Wochenlohn u. s. w.\n einen sehr verschiednen Preis der Arbeit d. h. sehr verschiedne Geld-\n summen für dasselbe Quantum Arbeit darstellen kann [Note: „The price of labour is the sum paid for a given quantity of\n labour.“ (Sir Edward West: „Price of Corn and Wages of Labour.\n Lond. 1826“, p. 67.) Dieser West ist der Verfasser der in der Geschichte der\n politischen Oekonomie epochemachenden anonymen Schrift: „Essay on the\n Application of Capital to Land. By a Fellow of Univ. College of\n Oxford. Lond. 1815“.] . Man\n muss also bei dem Zeitlohn wieder unterscheiden zwischen dem Gesammt-\n betrag des Arbeitslohns, Taglohns, Wochenlohns u. s. w. und\n dem Preis der Arbeit. Wie nun den Preis der Arbeit finden, d. h.\n den Geldwerth eines gegebnen Quantums Arbeit? Der\n durchschnittliche Preis der Arbeit ergiebt sich, indem man den durch-\n schnittlichen Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft durch die\n Stundenzahl des durchschnittlichen Arbeitstags divi-\n dirt. Ist z. B. der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft 3 sh., das Werthprodukt\n von 6 Arbeitsstunden, und ist der Arbeitstag zwölfstündig, so ist der Preis\n einer Arbeitsstunde = \\frac{3 sh.}{12} = 3 d. Der so gefundne Preis der Ar-\n beitsstunde dient als Einheitsmass für den Preis der\n Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nEs folgt daher, dass der Taglohn, Wochenlohn u. s. w. derselbe\n bleiben kann, obgleich der Preis der Arbeit fortwährend sinkt.\n War z. B. der gewohnheitsmässige Arbeitstag 10 Stunden und der Tages-\n werth der Arbeitskraft 3 sh., so betrug der Preis der Arbeitsstunde 3⅗d.;\n er sinkt auf 3 d., sobald der Arbeitstag zu 12 Stunden, und auf 2⅖ d.,\n sobald er zu 15 Stunden steigt. Tages- oder Wochenlohn bleiben trotz-\n dem unverändert. Umgekehrt kann der Taglohn oder Wochenlohn stei-\n gen, obgleich der Preis der Arbeit constant bleibt oder selbst sinkt.\n War z. B. der Arbeitstag zehnstündig und ist der Tageswerth der Arbeits-\n kraft 3 sh., so der Preis einer Arbeitsstunde 3⅗ d. Arbeitet der Arbeiter\n in Folge zunehmender Beschäftigung und bei gleichbleibendem Preise der\n Arbeit 12 Stunden, so steigt sein Tageslohn nun auf 3 sh. 7⅕d. ohne Varia-\n tion im Preise der Arbeit. Dasselbe Resultat könnte herauskommen, wenn\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"531\", \"facs\": \"#f0550\"} --> statt der extensiven Grösse der Arbeit ihre intensive Grösse zunähme [Note: „The wages of labour depend upon the price of labour and the quantity\n of labour performed … An increase in the wages of labour does not necessarily\n imply an enhancement of the price of labour. From fuller employment, and greater\n exertions, the wages of labour may be considerably increased, while the price of\n labour may continue the same.“ (West l. c. p. 67, 68 u. 112.) Die Haupt-\n frage: wie wird der „price of labour“ bestimmt? fertigt West übrigens mit banalen\n Redensarten ab.] .\n Steigen des nominellen Tages- oder Wochenlohns mag daher begleitet sein\n von gleichbleibendem oder sinkendem Preis der Arbeit. Dasselbe gilt von\n der Einnahme der Arbeiterfamilie, sobald das vom Familienhaupt gelie-\n ferte Arbeitsquantum durch die Arbeit der Familienglieder vermehrt wird.\n Es giebt also von der Schmälerung des nominellen Tages- oder Wochen-\n lohns unabhängige Methoden zur Herabsetzung des Preises der Ar-\n beit [Note: Diess fühlt der fanatischste Vertreter der industriellen Bourgeoisie des\n 18. Jahrhunderts, der oft von uns citirte Verfasser des „Essay on Trade and\n Commerce“ richtig heraus, obgleich er die Sache konfus darstellt: „It is the\n quantity of labour and not the price of it (versteht darunter den nominellen Tages-\n oder Wochenlohn), that is determined by the price of provisions and\n other necessaries: reduce the price of necessaries very low, and of course\n you reduce the quantity of labour in proportion … Master-manufacturers know,\n that there are various ways of rising and felling the price of labour, besides\n that of altering its nominal amount.“ (l. c. p. 48 u. 61.) In seinen „Three\n Lectures on the Rate of Wages. Lond. 1830“, worin N. W. Senior\n West’s Schrift benutzt, ohne sie anzuführen, sagt er u. a.: „The labourer is\n principally interested in the amount of wages“ (p. 14). Also der Arbeiter\n ist hauptsächlich interessirt in dem, was er erhält, dem nominellen Betrag des\n Lohns, nicht in dem, was er giebt, der Quantität der Arbeit!] . Als allgemeines Gesetz aber folgt: Ist die Quantität der\n Tages-, Wochenarbeit u. s. w. gegeben, so hängt der Tages-\n oder Wochenlohn vom Preise der Arbeit ab, der selbst variirt,\n entweder mit dem Werth der Arbeitskraft oder den Abweichungen ihres\n Preises von ihrem Werthe. Ist dagegen der Preis der Arbeit gege-\n ben, so hängt der Tages- oder Wochenlohn von der Quan-\n tität der Tages- oder Wochenarbeit ab.\n\n\n\n\nDie Masseinheit des Zeitlohns, der Preis der Arbeitsstunde, ist der\n Quotient des Tageswerths der Arbeitskraft, dividirt durch die Stundenzahl\n des gewohnheitsmässigen Arbeitstags. Gesetzt letztrer betrage 12 Stunden,\n [Running matter: 34*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"532\", \"facs\": \"#f0551\"} --> der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft 3 sh., das Werthprodukt von 6 Arbeits-\n stunden. Der Preis der Arbeitsstunde ist unter diesen Umständen 3 d.,\n ihr Werthprodukt 6 d. Wird der Arbeiter nun weniger als 12 Stunden\n täglich (oder weniger als 6 Tage in der Woche) beschäftigt, z. B. nur 6\n oder 8 Stunden, so erhält er, bei diesem Preise der Arbeit, nur 2 oder\n 1½ sh. Taglohn [Note: Die Wirkung solcher anormalen Unterbeschäftigung ist durchaus ver-\n schieden von der einer allgemeinen zwangsgesetzlichen Reduktion des\n Arbeitstags. Erstere hat mit der absoluten Länge des Arbeitstags nichts zu\n schaffen und kann ebensowohl bei 15stündigem als bei 6stündigem Arbeitstag ein-\n treten. Der normale Preis der Arbeit ist im ersten Fall darauf berechnet, dass\n der Arbeiter 15 Stunden, im zweiten darauf, dass er 6 Stunden per Tag durch-\n schnittlich arbeitet. Die Wirkung bleibt daher dieselbe, wenn er in dem einen Fall\n nur 7½, in dem andern nur 3 Stunden beschäftigt wird.] . Da er nach der Voraussetzung im Durchschnitt 6\nStunden täglich arbeiten muss, um nur einen dem Werth seiner Ar-\n beitskraft entsprechenden Taglohn zu produciren, da er nach derselben\n Voraussetzung von jeder Stunde nur ½ für sich selbst, ½ aber für den\n Kapitalisten arbeitet, so ist es klar, dass er das Werthprodukt von 6\nStunden nicht herausschlagen kann, wenn er weniger als 12 Stun-\n den beschäftigt wird. Sah man früher die zerstörenden Folgen der\n Ueberarbeit, so entdeckt man hier die Quellen der Leiden, die für den Ar-\n beiter aus seiner Unterbeschäftigung entspringen.\n\n\n\n\nWird der Stundenlohn in der Weise fixirt, dass der Kapitalist\n sich nicht zur Zahlung eines Tages- oder Wochenlohns verpflichtet, son-\n dern nur zur Zahlung der Arbeitsstunden, während deren es ihm beliebt,\n den Arbeiter zu beschäftigen, so kann er ihn unter der Zeit beschäf-\n tigen, die der Schätzung des Stundenlohns oder der Masseinheit für den\n Preis der Arbeit ursprünglich zu Grunde liegt. Da diese Masseinheit be-\n stimmt ist durch die Proportion ,\n verliert sie natürlich allen Sinn, sobald der Arbeitstag aufhört, eine be-\n stimmte Stundenzahl zu zählen. Der Zusammenhang zwischen der bezahl-\n ten und unbezahlten Arbeitszeit ist aufgehoben. Der Kapitalist kann jetzt\n ein bestimmtes Quantum Mehrarbeit aus dem Arbeiter herausschlagen, ohne\n ihm die zu seiner Selbsterhaltung nothwendige Arbeitszeit einzuräumen.\n Er kann jede Regelmässigkeit der Beschäftigung vernichten und ganz nach\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"533\", \"facs\": \"#f0552\"} --> Bequemlichkeit, Willkühr und augenblicklichem Interesse die ungeheuerste\n Ueberarbeit mit relativer oder gänzlicher Arbeitslosigkeit abwechseln lassen.\n Er kann, unter dem Vorwand, den „normalen Preis der Arbeit“ zu zahlen,\n den Arbeitstag, ohne irgend entsprechende Kompensation für den Arbeiter,\n anormal verlängern. Daher der durchaus rationelle Aufstand (1860) der\n im Baufach beschäftigten Londoner Arbeiter gegen den Versuch der Kapi-\n talisten diesen Stundenlohn aufzuherrschen. Die gesetzliche Be-\n schränkung des Arbeitstags macht solchem Unfug ein Ende, ob-\n gleich natürlich nicht der aus Konkurrenz der Maschinerie, Wechsel in der\n Qualität der angewandten Arbeiter, partiellen und allgemeinen Krisen ent-\n springenden Unterbeschäftigung.\n\n\n\n\nBei wachsendem Tages- oder Wochenlohn kann der Preis der Ar-\n beit nominell constant bleiben und dennoch unter sein normales Niveau\n sinken. Diess findet jedesmal statt, sobald mit constantem Preis der Ar-\n beit, resp. der Arbeitsstunde, der Arbeitstag über seine gewohnheitsmässige\n Dauer verlängert wird. Wenn in dem Bruch \n der Nenner wächst, wächst der Zähler noch rascher. Der Werth der Ar-\n beitskraft, weil ihr Verschleiss, wächst mit der Dauer ihrer Funktion, und\n in rascherer Proportion als das Increment ihrer Funktionsdauer. In vie-\n len Industriezweigen, wo Zeitlohn vorherrscht, ohne gesetzliche\n Schranken der Arbeitszeit, hat sich daher naturwüchsig die Gewohnheit\n herausgebildet, dass der Arbeitstag nur bis zu einem gewissen Punkt, z. B.\n bis zum Ablauf der zehnten Stunde, als normal gilt („normal working day“,\n „the day’s work“, „the regular hours of work“). Jenseits dieser Grenze bildet\n die Arbeitszeit Ueberzeit (overtime) und wird, die Stunde als Masseinheit\n genommen, besser bezahlt (extra pay), obgleich oft in lächerlich kleiner\n Proportion [Note: „Die Rate der Zahlung für Ueberzeit (in der Spitzenmanufaktur) ist so\n klein, ½ d. u. s. w. per Stunde, dass sie in peinlichem Kontrast steht zur massen-\n haften Unbill, die sie der Gesundheit und Lebenskraft der Arbeiter anthut …\n Der so gewonnene kleine Ueberschuss muss ausserdem oft in Extra-Erfrischungs-\n mitteln wieder verausgabt werden.“ („Child. Empl. Comm.“ II. Rep.,\n p. XVI, n. 117.)] . Der normale Arbeitstag existirt hier als Bruchtheil des\n wirklichen Arbeitstags und der letztere währt oft während des ganzen Jahres\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"534\", \"facs\": \"#f0553\"} --> länger als der erstere [Note: Z. B. in der Tapetendruckerei vor der neulichen Einführung des Fabrik-\n akts. „Wir arbeiten ohne Pause für Mahlzeiten, so dass das Tageswerk von\n 10½ Stunden um halb 5 Uhr Nachmittags beendet ist, und alles spätere ist\nUeberzeit, die selten vor 6 Uhr Abends aufhört, so dass wir in der That das\n ganze Jahr durch Ueberzeit arbeiten.“ (Mr. Smith’s Evidence in\n „Child. Empl. Comm.“ I. Rep., p. 125.)] . Das Wachsthum im Preis der Arbeit mit der\n Verlängerung des Arbeitstags über eine gewisse Normalgrenze gestaltet\n sich in verschiedenen britischen Industriezweigen so, dass der niedrige\n Preis der Arbeit während der s. g. Normalzeit dem Arbeiter die besser\n bezahlte Ueberzeit aufzwingt, will er überhaupt einen genügenden Arbeits-\n lohn herausschlagen [Note: Z. B. in den schottischen Bleichereien. „In einigen Theilen Schottlands\n wurde diese Industrie (vor Einführung des Fabrikakts 1862) nach dem System der\n Ueberzeit betrieben, d. h. 10 Stunden galten als normaler Arbeitstag. Dafür\n erhielt der Mann 1. sh. 2 d. Hierzu kam aber täglich eine Ueberzeit von 3 oder 4\n Stunden, wofür 3 d. per Stunde gezahlt wurde. Folge dieses Systems: Ein Mann,\n der nur die Normalzeit arbeitete, konnte nur 8 sh. Wochenlohn verdienen. Ohne\n Ueberzeit reichte der Lohn nicht aus.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact.\n 30th April 1863“, p. 10.) Die „Extrazahlung für Ueberzeit ist eine Ver-\n suchung, der die Arbeiter nicht widerstehen können.“ („Rep. of Insp. of\n Fact. 30th April 1848“, p. 5.) Die Buchbinderei in der City von London\n verwendet sehr viele junge Mädchen vom 14.—15. Jahr an, und zwar unter\n dem Lehrlingskontrakt, der bestimmte Arbeitsstunden vorschreibt. Nichtsdesto-\n weniger arbeiten sie in der Schlusswoche jeden Monats bis 10, 11, 12 und 1 Uhr\n Nachts, zusammen mit den älteren Arbeitern, in sehr gemischter Gesellschaft.\n „Die Meister versuchen (tempt) sie durch Extralohn und Geld für ein gutes\n Nachtessen“, das sie in benachbarten Kneipen zu sich nehmen. Die grosse Lieder-\n lichkeit, so unter diesen „young immortals“ producirt („Child. Empl.\n Comm. V. Rep.“, p. 44, n. 191), findet ihre Kompensation darin, dass von\n ihnen unter anderm auch viele Bibeln und Erbauungsbücher gebunden werden.] . Gesetzliche Beschränkung des Arbeitstags macht\n diesem Vergnügen ein Ende [Note: Sieh „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 30th April 1863“, l. c. Mit\n ganz richtiger Kritik des Sachverhältnisses erklärten die im Baufach beschäftigten\n Londoner Arbeiter während des grossen strike und lock out von 1860 den Stun-\n denlohn nur annehmen zu wollen unter zwei Bedingungen: 1) dass mit dem\n Preis der Arbeitsstunde ein Normalarbeitstag von resp. 9 und 10 Stunden fest-\n gesetzt werde und der Preis für die Stunde des zehntägigen Arbeitstags grösser\n sei als für die des neunstündigen; 2) dass jede Stunde über den Normaltag hinaus\n als Ueberzeit verhältnissmässig höher bezahlt werde.] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"535\", \"facs\": \"#f0554\"} -->\n\nEs ist allgemein bekannte Thatsache, dass je länger der Arbeitstag\n in einem Industriezweig, um so niedriger der Arbeitslohn [Note: „It is a very notable thing, too, that where long hours are the rule, small\n wages are also so.“ („Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1863“, p. 9.)\n „The work which obtains the scanty pittance of food is for the most part ex-\n cessively prolonged.“ („Public Health, Sixth Rep. 1864“, p. 15.)] . Fabrikin-\n spektor A. Redgrave illustrirt diess durch eine vergleichende Uebersicht\n der zwanzigjährigen Periode von 1839—1859, wonach der Arbeitslohn\n in den dem Zehnstundengesetz unterworfenen Fabriken stieg, während er in\n den Fabriken, wo 14 bis 15 Stunden täglich gearbeitet wird, fiel [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 30th April 1860“, p. 31, 32.] .\n\n\n\n\nZunächst folgt aus dem Gesetz: „bei gegebnem Preis der Ar-\n beit hängt der Tages- oder Wochenlohn von der Quantität der gelie-\n ferten Arbeit ab“, dass je niedriger der Preis der Arbeit, desto grösser\n das Arbeitsquantum sein muss oder desto länger der Arbeitstag, damit der\n Arbeiter auch nur einen kümmerlichen Durchschnittslohn sichre. Die\n Niedrigkeit des Arbeitspreises wirkt hier als Sporn zur Verlängerung der\n Arbeitszeit [Note: Die Hand-Nägelmacher in England haben z. B. wegen des niedrigen\n Arbeitspreises 15 Stunden täglich zu arbeiten, um den kümmerlichsten Wochen-\n lohn herauszuschlagen. „Es sind viele, viele Stunden des Tags, und während aller\n der Zeit muss er hart schanzen um 11 d. oder 1 sh. herauszuschlagen, und davon\n gehn 2½ bis 3 d. ab für Verschleiss der Werkzeuge, Feuerung, Eisenabfall.“\n („Child. Empl. Comm. III. Rep.“, p. 136, n. 671.) Die Weiber verdienen\n bei derselben Arbeitszeit nur einen Wochenlohn von 5 sh. (l. c. p. 137, n. 674.)] .\n\n\n\n\nUmgekehrt aber producirt ihrerseits die Verlängerung der Arbeitszeit\n einen Fall im Arbeitspreise und damit im Tages- oder Wochenlohn.\n\n\n\n\nDie Bestimmung des Arbeitspreises durch\n ergiebt, dass blosse Verlängerung des Arbeitstags den Arbeitspreis senkt,\n wenn keine Kompensation eintritt. Aber dieselben Umstände, welche den\n Kapitalisten befähigen, den Arbeitstag auf die Dauer zu verlängern, be-\n fähigen ihn erst und zwingen ihn schliesslich, den Arbeitspreis auch nomi-\n nell zu senken, bis der Gesammtpreis der vermehrten Stundenzahl sinkt,\n also der Tages- oder Wochenlohn. Hinweis auf zwei Umstände genügt\n hier. Verrichtet ein Mann das Werk von 1½ oder 2 Männern, so wächst\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"536\", \"facs\": \"#f0555\"} --> die Zufuhr der Arbeit, wenn auch die Zufuhr der auf dem Markt befind-\n lichen Arbeitskräfte constant bleibt. Die so unter den Arbeitern erzeugte\n Konkurrenz befähigt den Kapitalisten den Preis der Arbeit herabzudrücken,\n während der fallende Preis der Arbeit ihn umgekehrt befähigt, die Arbeits-\n zeit noch weiter heraufzuschrauben [Note: Wenn ein Fabrikarbeiter z. B. verweigerte, die hergebrachte lange Stun-\n denzahl zu arbeiten, „he would very shortly be replaced by somebody who would\n work any length of time and thus be thrown out of employment.“ („Reports\n of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1848.“ Evidence p. 39, n. 58.) „If one man\n performs the work of two … the rate of profits will generally be raised … in\n consequence of the additional supply of labour having diminished its price.“\n (Senior l. c. p. 14.)] . Bald jedoch wird diese Verfügung\n über anormale, d. h. das gesellschaftliche Durchschnittsniveau über-\n fliessende Quanta unbezahlter Arbeit zum Konkurrenzmit-\n tel unter den Kapitalisten selbst. Ein Theil des Waaren-\n preises besteht aus dem Preis der Arbeit. Der nicht gezahlte\n Theil des Arbeitspreises braucht nicht im Waarenpreis zu rechnen. Er\nkann dem Waarenkäufer geschenkt werden. Diess ist der erste Schritt,\n wozu die Konkurrenz treibt. Der zweite Schritt, wozu sie zwingt, ist we-\n nigstens einen Theil des durch die Verlängerung des Arbeitstags erzeug-\n ten anormalen Mehrwerths ebenfalls aus dem Verkaufspreis\n der Waare zu eliminiren. In dieser Weise bildet sich erst sporadisch\n und fixirt sich nach und nach ein anormal niedriger Verkaufspreis der\n Waare, der von nun an zur constanten Grundlage kümmerlichen Arbeits-\n lohns bei übermässiger Arbeitszeit wird, wie er ursprünglich das Produkt\n dieser Umstände war. Wir deuten diese Bewegung bloss an, da die Ana-\n lyse der Konkurrenz nicht hierhin gehört. Doch mag für einen Augen-\n blick der Kapitalist selbst sprechen. „In Birmingham ist so grosse\n Konkurrenz unter den Meistern, dass mancher von uns gezwungen ist als\n Arbeitsanwender zu thun, was er sich schämen würde sonst zu thun; und\n dennoch wird nicht mehr Geld gemacht (and yet no more money is made),\n sondern das Publikum allein hat den Vortheil davon“ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm.“ III. Rep. Evidence p. 66, n. 22.] . Man erinnert\n sich der zwei Sorten Londoner Bäcker, wovon die eine Brod zum\n vollen Preise (the „fullpriced“ bakers), die andre es unter seinem normalen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"537\", \"facs\": \"#f0556\"} --> Preise verkauft („the underpriced“, „the undersellers“). Die „fullpri-\n ced“ denunciren ihre Konkurrenten vor der parlamentarischen Unter-\n suchungskommission: „Sie existiren nur, indem sie erstens das Publikum\n betrügen (durch Fälschung der Waare) und zweitens 18 Arbeitsstunden\n aus ihren Leuten für den Lohn zwölfstündiger Arbeit herausschinden. …\nDie unbezahlte Arbeit (the unpaid labour) der Arbeiter ist\n das Mittel, wodurch der Konkurrenzkampf geführt wird. … Die Kon-\n kurrenz unter den Bäckermeistern ist die Ursache der Schwierigkeit in\n Beseitigung der Nachtarbeit. Ein Unterverkäufer, der sein Brod unter\n dem mit dem Mehlpreis wechselnden Kostpreis verkauft, hält sich schadlos,\n indem er mehr Arbeit aus seinen Leuten herausschlägt. Wenn ich nur\n 12 Stunden Arbeit aus meinen Leuten herausschlage, mein Nachbar da-\n gegen 18 oder 20, muss er mich im Verkaufspreis schlagen. Könnten\n die Arbeiter auf Zahlung für Ueberzeit bestehn, so wäre es mit diesem\n Manöver bald zu Ende. … Eine grosse Anzahl der von den Unterver-\n käufern Beschäftigten sind Fremde, Jungen und Andre, die fast mit jedem\n Arbeitslohn, den sie kriegen können, vorlieb zu nehmen gezwungen\n sind“ [Note: „Report etc. relative to the Grievances complained of by\n the journeymen bakers. Lond. 1862“, p. LII und ib. Evidence,\n p. 479, 359, 27. Indess lassen auch die fullpriced, wie früher erwähnt, und wie\n ihr Wortführer Bennett selbst zugesteht, ihre Leute „Arbeit beginnen um 11 Uhr\n Abends oder früher und verlängern sie oft bis 7 Uhr des folgenden Abends.“\n (l. c. p. 27.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDiese Jeremiade ist auch desswegen interessant, weil sie zeigt, wie\nnur der Schein der Produktionsverhältnisse sich im Kapi-\n talistenhirn widerspiegelt. Der Kapitalist weiss nicht, dass auch der\nnormale Preis der Arbeit ein bestimmtes Quantum unbezahlter\n Arbeit einschliesst und eben diese unbezahlte Arbeit die normale Quelle\n seines Gewinns ist. Die Kategorie der Mehrarbeitszeit existirt überhaupt\n nicht für ihn, denn sie ist eingeschlossen im normalen Arbeitstag, den er\n im Taglohn zu zahlen glaubt. Wohl aber existirt für ihn die Ueber-\n zeit, die Verlängerung des Arbeitstags über die dem gewohnten Preis\n der Arbeit entsprechende Schranke. Seinem unterverkaufenden\n Konkurrenten gegenüber besteht er sogar auf Extrazahlung (extra pay)\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"538\", \"facs\": \"#f0557\"} --> für diese Ueberzeit. Er weiss wieder nicht, dass diese Extrazahlung eben-\n sowohl unbezahlte Arbeit einschliesst, wie der Preis der gewöhn-\n lichen Arbeitsstunde. Z. B. der Preis einer Stunde des zwölfstündigen\n Arbeitstags ist 3 d., das Werthprodukt von ½ Arbeitsstunde, während der\n Preis der überzeitigen Arbeitsstunde 4 d., das Werthprodukt von ⅔\n Arbeitsstunde. Im ersten Fall eignet sich der Kapitalist von einer Ar-\n beitsstunde die Hälfte, im andern ⅓ ohne Zahlung an.\n\n\n\n\nDer Stücklohn ist nichts als eine verwandelte Form des\n Zeitlohns, wie der Zeitlohn die verwandelte Form des Werthes oder\n Preises der Arbeitskraft.\n\n\n\n\nBeim Stücklohn sieht es auf den ersten Blick aus, als ob der vom\n Arbeiter verkaufte Gebrauchswerth nicht die Funktion seiner Ar-\n beitskraft sei, lebendige Arbeit, sondern bereits im Produkt\n vergegenständlichte Arbeit, und als ob der Preis dieser Arbeit\n nicht wie beim Zeitlohn durch die Bruchzahl\n,\n sondern durch die Leistungsfähigkeit des Producenten be-\n stimmt werde [Note: „The system of piece-work illustrates an epoch in the history of the\n working man; it is half-way between the position of the mere day-labourer, de-\n pending upon the will of the capitalist and the cooperative artizan, who in the not\n distant future promises to combine the artizan and the capitalist in his own person.\n Piece-workers are in fact their own masters, even whilst working upon the capital\n of the employer.“ (John Watts: „Trade Societies and Strikes,\n Machinery and Co-operative Societies. Manchester 1865“, p. 52,\n 53.) Ich citire diess Schriftchen, weil es eine wahre Gosse aller längst verfaulten,\n apologetischen Gemeinplätze. Derselbe Herr Watts machte früher in Owenismus\n und publicirte 1842 ein andres Schriftchen: „Facts and Fictions of Poli-\n tical Economy“, worin er u. a. Property für Robbery erklärt. Es ist schon\n lange her.] .\n\n\n\n\nZunächst müsste die Zuversicht, die an diesen Schein glaubt, bereits\n stark erschüttert werden durch die Thatsache, dass beide Formen des\n Arbeitslohns zur selben Zeit in denselben Geschäftszweigen neben einander\n bestehen. Z. B. „Die Setzer von London arbeiten in der Regel nach\n Stücklohn, während Zeitlohn bei ihnen die Ausnahme bildet. Umgekehrt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"539\", \"facs\": \"#f0558\"} --> bei den Setzern in den Provinzen, wo der Zeitlohn die Regel und der\n Stücklohn die Ausnahme. Die Schiffszimmerleute im Hafen von London\n werden nach Stücklohn bezahlt, in allen andern englischen Häfen nach\n Zeitlohn“ [Note: T. J. Dunning: „Trade’s Unions and Strikes. Lond.\n 1860“, p. 22.] . In denselben Londoner Sattlerwerkstätten wird oft für die-\n selbe Arbeit den Franzosen Stücklohn und den Engländern Zeitlohn ge-\n zahlt. In den eigentlichen Fabriken, wo Stücklohn allgemein vorherrscht,\n entziehn sich einzelne Arbeitsfunktionen aus technischen Gründen dieser\n Messung und werden daher nach Zeitlohn gezahlt [Note: Wie das gleichzeitige Nebeneinander dieser zwei Formen des Arbeitslohns\n Fabrikantenprellereien begünstigt: „A factory employs 400 people, the half of\n which work by the piece, and have a direct interest in working longer hours. The\n other 200 are paid by the day, work equally long with the others, and get no more\n money for their overtime … The work of these 200 people for half an hour a\n day is equal to one person’s work for 50 hours, or ⅚ of one persons labour in a\n week, and is a positive gain to the employer.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact.\n 31st October 1860“, p. 9.) „Overworking, to a very considerable extent,\n still prevails; and, in most instances, with that security against detection and\n punishment which the law itself affords. I have in many former reports shown …\n the injury to all the workpeople who are not employed on piece-work, but\n receive weekly wages.“ Leonhard Horner in „Reports of Insp. of\n Fact. 30th April 1859“, p. 8, 9.] . An und für sich ist\n es jedoch klar, dass die Formverschieden heit in der Auszahlung\n des Arbeitslohns an seinem Wesen nichts ändert, obgleich die eine\n Form der Entwicklung der kapitalistischen Produktion günstiger sein mag\n als die andre.\n\n\n\n\nDer gewöhnliche Arbeitstag betrage 12 Stunden, wovon 6 bezahlt,\n 6 unbezahlt. Sein Werthprodukt sei 6 sh., das einer Arbeitsstunde da-\n her 6 d. Es stelle sich erfahrungsmässig heraus, dass ein Arbeiter, der\n mit dem Durchschnittsgrad von Intensivität und Geschick arbeitet, in der\n That also nur die gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit zur Produktion eines Artikels verwendet, 24 Stücke, ob diskret,\n oder messbare Theile eines kontinuirlichen Machwerks, in 12 Stunden\n liefert. So ist der Werth dieser 24 Stücke, nach Abzug des in ihnen ent-\n haltenen constanten Kapitaltheils, 6 sh. und der Werth des einzelnen\n Stücks 3 d. Der Arbeiter erhält per Stück 1½ d. und verdient so in\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"540\", \"facs\": \"#f0559\"} --> 12 Stunden 3 sh. Wie es beim Zeitlohn gleichgültig ist, ob man annimmt,\n dass der Arbeiter 6 Stunden für sich und 6 für den Kapitalisten, oder von\n jeder Stunde die eine Hälfte für sich und die andre für den Kapitalisten\n arbeitet, so auch hier, ob man sagt, jedes einzelne Stück sei halb bezahlt\n und halb unbezahlt, oder der Preis von 12 Stücken ersetze nur den Werth\n der Arbeitskraft, während in den 12 andern sich der Mehrwerth ver-\n körpere.\n\n\n\n\nDie Form des Stücklohns ist ebenso irrationell als die des Zeit-\n lohns. Während z. B. zwei Stück Waare, nach Abzug des Werths der\n in ihnen aufgezehrten Produktionsmittel, als Produkt einer Arbeitsstunde\n 6 d. werth sind, erhält der Arbeiter für sie einen Preis von 3 d. Der\n Stücklohn drückt unmittelbar in der That kein Werthverhält-\n niss aus. Es handelt sich nicht darum den Werth des Stücks durch die\n in ihm verkörperte Arbeitszeit zu messen, sondern umgekehrt die vom\n Arbeiter verausgabte Arbeit durch die Zahl der von ihm producirten\n Stücke. Beim Zeitlohn misst sich die Arbeit an ihrer unmittelbaren Zeit-\n dauer, beim Stücklohn am Produktenquantum, worin Arbeit während be-\n stimmter Zeitdauer verdichtet [Note: „Le salaire peut se mesurer de deux manières: ou sur la durée du tra-\n vail, ou sur son produit.“ („Abrégé Élémentaire des principes de\n l’Écon. Pol. Paris 1796“, p. 32.) Verfasser dieser anonymen Schrift:\n G. Garnier.] . Der Preis der Arbeitszeit selbst ist\n schliesslich bestimmt durch die Gleichung: Werth der Tagesarbeit\n = Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft. Der Stücklohn ist also nur\n eine modificirte Form des Zeitlohns.\n\n\n\n\nBetrachten wir nun etwas näher die charakteristischen Eigenthüm-\n lichkeiten des Stücklohns.\n\n\n\n\nDie Qualität der Arbeit ist hier durch das Werk selbst kon-\n trolirt, das die durchschnittliche Güte besitzen muss, soll der Stückpreis\n voll bezahlt werden. Der Stücklohn wird nach dieser Seite hin zu frucht-\n barster Quelle von Lohnabzügen und kapitalistischer Prellerei.\n\n\n\n\nEr bietet dem Kapitalisten ein ganz bestimmtes Mass für die Inten-\n sivität der Arbeit. Nur Arbeitszeit, die sich in einem vorher be-\n stimmten und erfahrungsmässig festgesetzten Waarenquantum verkörpert,\n gilt als gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit und wird\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"541\", \"facs\": \"#f0560\"} --> als solche bezahlt. In den grösseren Schneiderwerkstätten Londons heisst\n daher ein gewisses Stück Arbeit, z. B. eine Weste u. s. w., Stunde, halbe\n Stunde u. s. w., die Stunde zu 6 d. Aus der Praxis ist bekannt, wie viel\n das Durchschnittsprodukt einer Stunde. Bei neuen Moden, Reparaturen\n u. s. w. entsteht Streit zwischen Anwender und Arbeiter, ob ein bestimmtes\n Arbeitsstück = einer Stunde u. s. w., bis auch hier die Erfahrung ent-\n scheidet. Aehnlich in den Londoner Möbelschreinereien u. s. w. Besitzt\n der Arbeiter nicht die durchschnittliche Leistungsfähigkeit, kann er daher\n ein bestimmtes Minimum von Tagwerk nicht liefern, so entlässt man\n ihn [Note: „Le fileur reçoit un certain poids de coton préparé pour lequel il doit\n rendre, dans un espace de temps donné, une quantité voulue de fil ou coton file,\n et il est payé à raison de tant par livre d’ouvrage rendu. Si le produit pèche en\n qualité, la faute retombe sur lui; s’il y a moins que la quantité fixée par le mini-\n mum, dans un temps donné, on le congédie et on le remplace par un ouvrier plus\n habile.“ (Ure l. c. t. II, p. 61.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDa Qualität und Intensivität der Arbeit hier durch die Form des\n Arbeitslohns selbst kontrolirt werden, macht sie grossen Theil der Ar-\n beitsaufsicht überflüssig. Sie bildet daher sowohl die Grundlage\n der früher geschilderten modernen Hausarbeit als eines hierarchisch\n gegliederten Systems der Exploitation und Unterdrückung. Das letztere be-\n sitzt zwei Grundformen. Der Stücklohn erleichtert einerseits das Zwischen-\n schieben von Parasiten zwischen Kapitalist und Lohnarbeiter, Unterver-\n pachtung der Arbeit (subletting of labour). Der Gewinn der Zwi-\n schenpersonen fliesst ausschliesslich aus der Differenz zwischen dem\n Arbeitspreis, den der Kapitalist zahlt, und dem Theil dieses Preises, den\n sie dem Arbeiter wirklich zukommen lassen [Note: „It is when work passes through several hands, each of which is to take\n its share of profits, while only the last does the work, that the pay which reaches\n the workwoman is miserably disproportioned.“ („Child. Empl. Comm.“\n II. Rep., p. LXX, n. 424.)] . Diess System heisst in\n England charakteristisch das „Sweating System“ (Ausschweissungs-\n system). Andrerseits erlaubt der Stücklohn dem Kapitalisten mit dem\n Hauptarbeiter — in der Manufaktur mit dem Chef einer Gruppe, in den Mi-\n nen mit dem Ausbrecher der Kohle u. s. w., in der Fabrik mit dem eigentlichen\n Maschinenarbeiter — einen Kontrakt für so viel per Stück zu schliessen,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"542\", \"facs\": \"#f0561\"} --> ein Preis, wofür der Hauptarbeiter selbst die Anwerbung und Zahlung\n seiner Hilfsarbeiter übernimmt. Die Exploitation der Arbeiter durch das\n Kapital verwirklicht sich hier vermittelst der Exploitation des Arbeiters\n durch den Arbeiter [Note: Selbst der apologetische Watts bemerkt: „It would be a great improve-\n ment to the system of piece-work, if all the men employed on a job were partners\n in the contract, each according to his abilities, instead of one man being\n interested in overworking his fellows for his own benefit.“ (l. c.\n p. 53.) Ueber die Gemeinheiten dieses Systems vgl. „Child. Empl. Comm.“\nRep. III, p. 66, n. 22, p. 11, n. 124, p. XI, n. 13, 53, 59 u. s. w.] .\n\n\n\n\nDen Stücklohn gegeben, ist es natürlich das persönliche Interesse des\n Arbeiters, seine Arbeitskraft möglichst intensiv anzuspannen, was dem\n Kapitalisten eine Erhöhung des Normalgrads der Intensivität\n erleichtert [Note: Diesem naturwüchsigen Resultat wird oft künstlich unter die Arme ge-\n griffen. Z. B. im Engineering Trade von London gilt es als herkömmlicher\n trick, „dass der Kapitalist als Chef einer Arbeiteranzahl einen Mann von über-\n legner physischer Kraft und Geschwindigkeit auswählt. Er zahlt ihm vierteljährlich\n oder in andern Terminen einen Zuschusslohn unter der Uebereinkunft, alles mög-\n liche aufzubieten, um seine Mitarbeiter, die nur den gewöhnlichen Lohn er-\n halten, zur äussersten Nacheiferung anzustacheln … Ohne weiteren Kommentar\n erklärt diess die Kapitalistenklage über „Lähmung der Thätigkeit oder überlegener\n Geschicklichkeit und Arbeitskraft („stinting the action, superior skill and work-\n ing power“) durch die Trade’s Unions.“ (Dunning l. c. p. 22, 23.) Da der Ver-\n fasser selbst Arbeiter und Sekretär einer Trade’s Union, könnte diess für Ueber-\n treibung gelten. Aber man sehe z. B. die „highly respectable“ agronomische\n Cyclopädie von J. Ch. Morton, Art. „Labourer“, wo diese Methode den\n Pächtern als probat empfohlen wird.] . Es ist ebenso das persönliche Interesse des Arbeiters, den\nArbeitstag zu verlängern, weil damit sein Tages- oder Wochen-\n lohn steigt [Note: „All those who are paid by piece-work … profit by the transgression\n of the legal limits of work. This observation as to the willingness to work over-\n time, is especially applicable to the women employed as weavers and reelers.“\n („Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 30th April 1858“, p. 9.) „Diess Stücklohn-\n system, so vortheilhaft für den Kapitalisten … strebt direkt den\n jungen Töpfer zu grosser Ueberarbeit zu ermuntern, während der 4 oder 5 Jahre,\n worin er per Stück, aber zu niedrigem Preis, bezahlt wird. Es ist diess eine der\n grossen Ursachen, denen die physische Degeneration der Töpfer zuzuschreiben\n ist.“ („Child. Empl. Comm.“ I. Rep., p. XIII.)] . Es tritt damit die beim Zeitlohn bereits geschilderte Re-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"543\", \"facs\": \"#f0562\"} --> aktion ein, abgesehn davon, dass die Verlängerung des Arbeitstags, selbst\n bei constant bleibendem Stücklohn, an und für sich eine Senkung im\nPreise der Arbeit einschliesst.\n\n\n\n\nBeim Zeitlohn herrscht mit wenigen Ausnahmen gleicher Arbeits-\n lohn für dieselben Funktionen, während beim Stücklohn der Preis der\n Arbeitszeit zwar durch ein bestimmtes Produktquantum gemessen ist, der\n Tages- oder Wochenlohn dagegen wechselt mit der individuellen Ver-\n schiedenheit der Arbeiter, wovon der Eine nur das Minimum des Produkts\n in einer gegebnen Zeit liefert, der Andre den Durchschnitt, der Dritte\n mehr als den Durchschnitt. In Bezug auf die wirkliche Einnahme treten\n hier also grosse Differenzen ein je nach dem verschiednen Geschick, Kraft,\n Energie, Ausdauer u. s. w. der individuellen Arbeiter [Note: „Where the work in any trade is paid for by the piece at so much per\n job … wages may very materially differ in amount … But in work by the\n day there is generally an uniform rate … recognized by both employer and\n employed as the standard of wages for the general run of workmen in the trade.“\n (Dunning l. c. p. 17.)] . Diess ändert\n natürlich nichts an dem allgemeinen Verhältniss zwischen Kapital und\n Lohnarbeit. Erstens gleichen sich diese individuellen Unterschiede für das\n Gesammtatelier aus, so dass es in einer bestimmten Arbeitszeit das Durch-\n schnittsprodukt liefert und der gezahlte Gesammtlohn der Durchschnitts-\n lohn des Geschäftszweigs sein wird. Zweitens bleibt die Proportion zwi-\n schen Arbeitslohn und Mehrwerth unverändert, da dem individuellen Lohn\n des einzelnen Arbeiters die von ihm individuell gelieferte Masse von Mehr-\n werth entspricht. Aber der grössere Spielraum, den der Stücklohn der\n Individualität bietet, strebt einerseits dahin die Individualität und damit\n Freiheitsgefühl, Selbstständigkeit und Selbstkontrole der Arbeiter zu ent-\n wickeln, andrerseits ihre Konkurrenz unter und gegen einan-\n der. Er hat daher eine Tendenz mit der Erhebung individueller Arbeits-\n löhne über das Durchschnittsniveau diess Niveau selbst zu senken. Wo\n aber bestimmter Stücklohn sich seit lange traditionell befestigt hatte und\n seine Herabsetzung daher besondre Schwierigkeiten bot, flüchteten die\n Meister ausnahmsweis auch zu seiner gewaltsamen Verwandlung in\n Zeitlohn. Hiergegen z. B. 1860 grosser Strike unter den Bandwebern von\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"544\", \"facs\": \"#f0563\"} --> Coventry [Note: „Die Arbeit der Handwerksgesellen regelt sich nach dem Tag oder nach\n dem Stück (à la journée ou à la pièce) … Die Meister wissen ungefähr, wie viel\n Werk die Arbeiter täglich in jedem métier verrichten können und zahlen sie daher\n oft im Verhältniss zum Werk, das sie verrichten; so arbeiten diese Gesellen so\n viel sie können, in ihrem eignen Interesse, ohne weitere Beaufsichtigung.“ (Can-\n tillon: „Essai sur la Nature du Commerce en Général.“ Amst.\n Ed. 1756, p. 185 u. 202.) Cantillon, aus dem Quesnay, Sir James Steuart und\n A. Smith reichlich geschöpft haben, stellt hier also schon den Stücklohn als bloss\n modificirte Form des Zeitlohns dar. Die französische Ausgabe Cantillon’s kündigt\n sich auf dem Titel als Uebersetzung aus dem Englischen an, aber die englische\n Ausgabe: „The Analysis of Trade, Commerce etc. by Philip Can-\n tillon, late of the City of London, Merchant“, ist nicht nur späteren\n Datums (von 1759), sondern erweist sich durch ihren Inhalt als eine spätere Be-\n arbeitung. So z. B. findet sich in der französischen Ausgabe Hume noch nicht\n erwähnt, während umgekehrt in der englischen Petty kaum mehr figurirt. Die\n englische Ausgabe ist theoretisch unbedeutender, enthält aber allerlei spezifisch\n auf englischen Handel, Bullionhandel u. s. w. Bezügliches, was im französischen\n Text fehlt. Die Worte im Titel der englischen Ausgabe, wonach die Schrift\n „Taken chiefly from the Manuscript of a very ingenious Gentleman deceased, and\n adapted etc.“, scheinen daher mehr als blosse, damals sehr übliche, Fiktion.] . Der Stücklohn ist endlich eine Hauptstütze des früher ge-\n schilderten Stundensystems [Note: „Combien de fois n’avons nous pas vu, dans certains ateliers, embaucher\n beaucoup plus d’ouvriers que ne le demandait le travail à mettre en main? Souvent,\n dans la prévision d’un travail aléatoire, quelquefois même imaginaire, on admet\n des ouvriers: comme on les paie aux pièces, on se dit qu’on court aucun\n risque, parce que toutes les pertes de temps seront à la charge des in-\n occupés.“ (H. Gregoir: „Les Typographes devant le Tribunal\n Correctionnel de Bruxelles.“ Bruxelles 1865, p. 9.)] .\n\n\n\n\nAus der bisherigen Darstellung ergiebt sich, dass der Stücklohn\n die der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise entsprechendste Form des Arbeits-\n lohns ist. Obgleich keineswegs neu, — er figurirt neben dem Zeitlohn\n officiell u. a. in den französischen und englischen Arbeiterstatuten des vier-\n zehnten Jahrhunderts — gewinnt er doch erst grösseren Spielraum während\n der eigentlichen Manufakturperiode. In der Sturm- und Drangperiode der\n grossen Industrie, namentlich von 1797 bis 1815, dient er als Hebel zur\n Verlängerung der Arbeitszeit und Herabsetzung des Arbeitslohns. Sehr\n wichtiges Material für die Bewegung des Arbeitslohns während jener Pe-\n riode findet man in den Blaubüchern: „Report and Evidence from\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"545\", \"facs\": \"#f0564\"} --> the select Committee on Petitions respecting the Corn\n Laws“ (Parlamentssession 1813—14) und: „Reports from the\n Lords’ Committee, on the state of Growth, Commerce,\n and Consumption of Grain, and all Laws relating there-\n to“. (Session 1814—15.) Man findet hier den dokumentarischen\n Nachweis für die fortwährende Senkung des Arbeitspreises seit dem Be-\n ginn des Antijakobinerkriegs. In der Weberei z. B. war der Stücklohn\n so gefallen, dass trotz des sehr verlängerten Arbeitstags der Taglohn jetzt\n niedriger stand als vorher. „Die reale Einnahme des Webers ist sehr\n viel weniger als früher: seine Superiorität über den gewöhnlichen Arbeiter,\n die erst sehr gross war, ist fast ganz verschwunden. In der That, der\n Unterschied in den Löhnen geschickter und gewöhnlicher Arbeit ist jetzt\n viel unbedeutender als während irgend einer früheren Periode“ [Note: „Remarks on the Commercial Policy of Great Britain.\n London 1815“, p. 48.] . Wie\n wenig die mit dem Stücklohn gesteigerte Intensivität und Ausdehnung der\n Arbeit dem ländlichen Proletariat fruchteten, zeige folgende einer Partei-\n schrift für Landlords und Pächter entlehnte Stelle: „Bei\n weitem der grössere Theil der Agrikulturoperationen ist durch Leute ver-\n richtet, die für den Tag oder auf Stückwerk gedungen werden. Ihr\n Wochenlohn beträgt ungefähr 12 sh.; und obgleich man voraussetzen\n mag, dass ein Mann bei Stücklohn, unter dem grösseren Arbeitssporn,\n 1 sh. oder vielleicht 2 sh. mehr verdient als beim Wochenlohn, so findet man\n dennoch, bei Schätzung seiner Gesammteinnahme, dass sein Verlust an Be-\n schäftigung im Lauf des Jahrs diesen Zuschuss aufwiegt. … Man wird ferner\n im Allgemeinen finden, dass die Löhne dieser Männer ein gewisses Ver-\n hältniss zum Preis der nothwendigen Lebensmittel haben; so dass ein\n Mann mit zwei Kindern fähig ist seine Familie ohne Zu-\n flucht zur Pfarreiunterstützung zu erhalten“ [Note: „A Defence of the Landowners and Farmers of Great\n Britain. Lond. 1814“, p. 4, 5.] . Mal-\n thus bemerkte damals mit Bezug auf die vom Parlament veröffentlichten\n Thatsachen: „Ich gestehe, ich sehe mit Missvergnügen die grosse\n Ausdehnung der Praxis des Stücklohns. Wirklich harte\n [Running matter: I. 35] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"546\", \"facs\": \"#f0565\"} --> Arbeit während 12 oder 14 Stunden des Tags, für irgend längere Zeit-\n perioden, ist zu viel für ein menschliches Wesen“ [Note: Malthus l. c.] .\n\n\n\n\nIn den dem Fabrikgesetz unterworfenen Werkstätten wird Stücklohn\n allgemeine Regel, weil das Kapital dort den Arbeitstag nur noch intensiv\n ausweiten kann [Note: „Die Arbeiter auf Stücklohn bilden wahrscheinlich ⅘ aller Arbeiter in\n den Fabriken.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 30th April 1858“,\n p. 9.)] .\n\n\n\n\nMit der wechselnden Produktivität der Arbeit stellt dasselbe Pro-\n duktenquantum wechselnde Arbeitszeit dar. Also wechselt auch der\n Stücklohn, da er Preisausdruck einer bestimmten Arbeitszeit. In unsrem\n obigen Beispiel wurden in 12 Stunden 24 Stück producirt, während das\n Werthprodukt der 12 Stunden 6 sh. war, der Tageswerth der Arbeits-\n kraft 3 sh., der Preis der Arbeitsstunde 3 d. und der Lohn für ein Stück\n 1½ d. In einem Stück war ½ Arbeitsstunde eingesaugt. Liefert der-\n selbe Arbeitstag nun etwa in Folge verdoppelter Produktivität der Arbeit\n 48 Stück statt 24, und bleiben alle andern Umstände unverändert, so sinkt\n der Stücklohn von 1½ d. auf ¾ d. oder 3 Farthing, da jedes Stück jetzt\n nur noch ¼ statt ½ Arbeitsstunde darstellt. 24 × 1½ d. = 3 sh.\n und ebenso 48 × ¾ d. = 3 sh. In andern Worten: Der Stücklohn\n wird in demselben Verhältniss heruntergesetzt, worin die Zahl der während\n derselben Zeit producirten Stücke wächst [Note: „On se rend un compte exact de la force productive de son métier (du\n fileur), et l’on diminue la rétribution du travail à mésure que la force productive\n augmente … sans cependant que cette augmentation soit proportionnée à l’aug-\n mentation de la force.“ (Ure l. c. p. 61.) Letztere apologetische Wendung\n hebt Ure selbst wieder auf. Bei einer Verlängerung der mule jenny z. B.\n „quelque surcroit de travail provient de l’allongement.“ (l. c. II, p. 34.) Die\n Arbeit nimmt also nicht in demselben Masse ab, worin ihre Produktivität wächst.\n Ferner: „Ce surcroit augmentera la force productive d’un cinquième. Dans ce\n cas on baisera le prix du fileur; mais comme on ne le réduira pas d’un cinquième\n le perfectionnement augmentera son gain dans le même nombre d’heures donné“,\n aber „il y a une certaine modification à faire … C’est que le fileur a des frais\n additionnels à déduire sur les 6 d., attendu qu’il faut qu’il augmente le\n nombre de ses aides non-adultes“, begleitet von einem „déplacement d’une\n partie des adultes“ (l. c. p. 66, 67), was keineswegs Tendenz zur Steigerung\n des Arbeitslohns hat.] , also die auf dasselbe Stück\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"547\", \"facs\": \"#f0566\"} --> verwandte Arbeitszeit abnimmt. Dieser Wechsel des Stücklohns, soweit rein\n nominell, ruft beständige Kämpfe zwischen Kapitalist und Arbeiter\n hervor. Entweder, weil der Kapitalist den Vorwand benutzt, um wirklich\n den Preis der Arbeit herabzusetzen. Oder weil die gesteigerte Produktiv-\n kraft der Arbeit von gesteigerter Intensivität derselben begleitet ist.\n Oder, weil der Arbeiter den Schein des Stücklohns, als ob ihm sein\n Produkt gezahlt werde und nicht seine Arbeitskraft, ernst nimmt und sich\n daher gegen eine Lohnherabsetzung sträubt, welcher die Herabsetzung im\n Verkaufspreis der Waare nicht entspricht. „Die Arbeiter überwachen sorg-\n fältig den Preis des Rohmaterials und den Preis der fabricirten Güter und\n sind so fähig die Profite ihrer Meister genau zu veranschlagen“ [Note: H. Fawcett: „The Economic Position of the British La-\n bourer“. Cambridge and London 1865, p. 178.] . Sol-\n chen Anspruch fertigt das Kapital mit Recht als groben Irrthum über die\n Natur der Lohnarbeit ab [Note: Im Londoner Standard vom 26. October 1861 findet man Bericht über\n einen Prozess der Firma John Brightet Co. vor den Rochdale Magistrates „to\n prosecute for intimidation the agents of the Carpet Weavers Trades’\n Union. Bright’s partners had introduced new machinery which would turn out\n 240 yards of carpet in the time and with the labour (!) previously required to produce\n 160 yards. The workmen had no claim whatever to share in the profits made by\n the investment of their employer’s capital in mechanical improvements. Accord-\n ingly, Mssrs. Bright proposed to lower the rate of pay from 1½ d. per yard to\n 1 d., leaving the earnings of the men exactly the same as before for the same\n labour. But there was a nominal reduction, of which the operatives, it is asserted,\n had not fair warning before hand.“] . Es zetert über diese Anmassung Steuern\n auf den Fortschritt der Industrie zu legen und erklärt rundweg, dass die\n Produktivität der Arbeit den Arbeiter überhaupt nichts angeht [Note: „Trades Unions in ihrer Sucht den Arbeitslohn aufrecht zu halten, suchen\n an dem Profit verbesserter Maschinerie Theil zu nehmen! (Quelle horreur!)\n … sie verlangen höheren Lohn, weil die Arbeit verkürzt ist … in anderen Worten,\n sie streben eine Steuer auf industrielle Verbesserungen zu legen.“ („On Com-\n bination of Trades. New Edit. Lond. 1834“, p. 42.)] .\n\n\n\n\nIm zweiten Abschnitt dieses Kapitels beschäftigten uns die mannig-\n fachen Kombinationen, welche ein Wechsel in der absoluten oder relativen\n (d. h. mit dem Mehrwerth verglichenen) Werthgrösse der Arbeitskraft her-\n vorbringen kann, während andrerseits wieder das Quantum von Lebens-\n mitteln, worin der Preis der Arbeitskraft realisirt wird, von dem\n [Running matter: 35*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"548\", \"facs\": \"#f0567\"} --> Wechsel dieses Preises unabhängige [Note: „It is not accurate to say that wages (handelt sich hier von ihrem Preise)\n are increased, because they purchase more of a cheaper article.“ (David\n Buchanan in seiner Ausgabe von A Smith’s „Wealth etc.“ 1814, v. I,\n p. 417 Note.)]  oder verschiedne Bewegungen durch-\n laufen konnte. Wie bereits bemerkt, verwandeln sich durch einfache Ueber-\n setzung des Werths, resp. Preises der Arbeitskraft in die exoteri-\n sche Form des Arbeitslohns alle jene Gesetze in Gesetze der Bewe-\n gung des Arbeitslohns. Was innerhalb dieser Bewegung als wechselnde\n Kombination, kann für verschiedne Länder als gleichzeitige Ver-\n schiedenheit nationaler Arbeitslöhne erscheinen. Beim Ver-\n gleich nationaler Arbeitslöhne sind also alle den Grössenwechsel des\n Werths der Arbeitskraft bestimmende Momente zu erwägen, Preis und Umfang\n der natürlichen und historisch entwickelten ersten Lebensbedürfnisse, Erzie-\n hungskosten des Arbeiters, Rolle der Weiber- und Kinderarbeit, Produk-\n tivität der Arbeit, ihre extensive und intensive Grösse. Selbst die ober-\n flächlichste Vergleichung erheischt, zunächst den Durchschnitts-Taglohn für\n dieselben Gewerbe in verschiednen Ländern auf gleich grosse Arbeitstage\n zu reduciren. Nach solcher Ausgleichung der Taglöhne, muss der Zeit-\n lohn wieder in Stücklohn übersetzt werden, da nur der letztere ein Grad-\n messer sowohl für die Produktivität als die intensive Grösse der Arbeit.\n Es wird sich dann meist finden, dass der niedrigere Taglohn bei einer\n Nation einen höheren Arbeitspreis und der höhere Taglohn bei einer an-\n dern Nation einen niedrigeren Arbeitspreis ausdrückt, ganz wie die Be-\n wegung des Taglohns überhaupt die Möglichkeit dieser Kombination\n zeigte [Note: James Anderson bemerkt in Polemik gegen A. Smith: „It deserves\n likewise to be remarked, that although the apparent price of labour is usually lower\n in poor countries, where the produce of the soil, and grain in general, is cheap;\n yet it is in fact for the most part really higher than in other countries. For it is\n not the wages that is given to the labourer per day that constitutes the real price\n of labour, although it is its apparent price. The real price is that which a\ncertain quantity of work performed actually costs the employer; and\n considered in this light, labour is in almost all cases cheaper in rich coun-\n tries than in those that are poorer, although the price of grain, and other\n provisions, is usually much lower in the last than in the first … Labour\n estimated by the day, is much lower in Scotland than in England …] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"549\", \"facs\": \"#f0568\"} -->\n\nAuf dem Weltmarkt zählt nicht nur der intensivere nationale Ar-\n beitstag als Arbeitstag von grösserer Stundenzahl, als extensiv grösserer Ar-\n beitstag, sondern der produktivere nationale Arbeitstag zählt als intensi-\n ver, so oft die produktivere Nation nicht durch die Konkurrenz gezwungen\n wird den Verkaufspreis der Waare auf ihren Werth zu senken. Der in-\n tensivere und produktivere nationale Arbeitstag stellt sich also im Ganzen\n auf dem Weltmarkt in höherem Geldausdruck dar als der minder in-\n tensive oder produktive nationale Arbeitstag. Was von dem Arbeitstag,\n gilt von jedem seiner aliquoten Theile. Der absolute Geldpreis\n der Arbeit kann also bei einer Nation höher stehn als bei der andern,\n obgleich der relative Arbeitslohn, d. h. der Arbeitslohn verglichen\n mit dem vom Arbeiter producirten Mehrwerth, oder seinem ganzen Werth-\n produkt, oder dem Preis der Nahrungsmittel, niedriger steht [Note: „Mr. Cowell, qui a fait une analyse très soignée de la filature, cherche à\n prouver, dans un rapport supplémentaire („supplement to the Report on Manu-\n factures“), que les salaires en Angleterre, sont virtuellement inférieurs pour\n le capitaliste, quoique pour l’ouvrier ils soient peut-être plus élevés que sur le con-\n tinent européen.“ (Ure l. c. t. II, p. 58.)] .\n\n\n\n\nIn „Versuch über die Rate des Arbeitslohns“ [Note: „Essay on the Rate of Wages: with an Examination of the\n Causes of the Differences in the Conditions of the Labouring\n Population throughout the World. Philadelphia 1835.“] , einer seiner frühsten\n ökonomischen Schriften, sucht H. Carey nachzuweisen, dass die ver-\n schiednen nationalen Arbeitslöhne sich direkt verhalten wie die Produk-\n tivitätsgrade der nationalen Arbeitstage, um aus diesem internationalen\n Verhältniss den Schluss zu ziehn, dass der Arbeitslohn überhaupt steigt\n und fällt wie die Produktivität der Arbeit. Unsre ganze Analyse der Pro-\n duktion des Mehrwerths beweist die Abgeschmacktheit dieser Schlussfolge-\n rung, hätte Carey selbst seine Prämisse bewiesen, statt seiner Gewohn-\n heit gemäss unkritisch und oberflächlich zusammengerafftes statistisches\n Material kunterbunt durch einander zu würfeln. Das Beste ist, dass er\n nicht behauptet, die Sache verhalte sich wirklich so, wie sie sich der\n Theorie nach verhalten sollte. Die Staatseinmischung hat nämlich das\n naturgemässe ökonomische Verhältniss verfälscht. Man muss\n [Note: Labour by the piece is generally cheaper in England.“ (James Ander-\n son: „Observations on the means of exciting a spirit of National\n Industry etc. Edinb. 1777“, p. 350, 351.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"550\", \"facs\": \"#f0569\"} --> daher die nationalen Arbeitslöhne so berechnen, als ob der Theil derselben,\n der dem Staat in der Form von Steuern zufällt, dem Arbeiter selbst zufiele.\n Sollte Herr Carey nicht weiter darüber nachdenken, ob diese „Staats-\n kosten“ nicht auch „naturgemässe“ Früchte der kapitalistischen Entwick-\n lung sind? Das Raisonnement ist ganz des Mannes würdig, der die kapita-\n listischen Produktionsverhältnisse erst für ewige Natur- und Vernunftge-\n setze erklärte, deren frei harmonisches Spiel nur durch die Staatsein-\n mischung gestört werde, um hinterher zu entdecken, dass Englands dia-\n bolischer Einfluss auf den Weltmarkt, ein Einfluss, der, wie es scheint,\n nicht den Naturgesetzen der kapitalistischen Produktion entspringt, die\n Staatseinmischung nöthig macht, nämlich den Schutz jener Natur- und\n Vernunftgesetze durch den Staat, alias das Protektionssystem. Er ent-\n deckte ferner, dass die Theoreme Ricardo’s u. s. w., worin existirende gesell-\n schaftliche Gegensätze und Widersprüche formulirt sind, nicht das ideale Pro-\n dukt der wirklichen ökonomischen Bewegung, sondern dass umgekehrt die\n wirklichen Gegensätze der kapitalistischen Produktion in England und\n anderswo das Resultat der Ricardo’schen u. s. w. Theorie sind! Er ent-\n deckte schliesslich, dass es in letzter Instanz der Handel ist, der die ein-\n gebornen Schönheiten und Harmonieen der kapitalistischen Produktions-\n weise vernichtet. Noch einen Schritt weiter, und er entdeckt vielleicht,\n dass der einzige Missstand an der kapitalistischen Produktion das Kapi-\n tal selbst ist. Nur ein Mann von so entsetzlicher Kritiklosigkeit und sol-\n cher Gelehrsamkeit de faux aloi verdiente, trotz seiner protektionistischen\n Ketzerei, die Geheimquelle der harmonischen Weisheit eines\n Bastiat und aller andern freihändlerischen Optimisten der Gegenwart zu\n werden [Note: Ich werde im Vierten Buch die Seichtigkeit seiner Wissenschaft näher\n nachweisen.] .\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"551\", \"facs\": \"#f0570\"} -->","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0001","text":"## Fünftes Kapitel.\n Weitere Untersuchungen über die Produktion des absoluten\n und relativen Mehrwerths.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"16.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0002","text":"## 1) Absoluter und relativer Mehrwerth.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"16.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0003","text":"Der Arbeitsprozess wurde (sieh drittes Kapitel) zunächst ab-\n strakt betrachtet, unabhängig von seinen geschichtlichen Formen, als Pro-\n zess zwischen Mensch und Natur. Soweit der Prozess rein individuell,\n vereinigt derselbe Arbeiter alle Funktionen, die sich später trennen. In\n der individuellen Aneignung von Naturgegenständen zu seinen Lebens-\n zwecken kontrolirt er sich selbst. Später wird er kontrolirt. Der ein-\n zelne Mensch kann nicht auf die Natur wirken ohne Bethätigung seiner\n eignen Muskeln unter Kontrole seines eignen Hirns. Wie im Natursystem\n Kopf und Hand zusammengehören, vereint der Arbeitsprozess Kopfarbeit\n und Handarbeit. Später scheiden sie sich, bis zum feindlichen Gegensatz.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"16.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0004","text":"Das Produkt verwandelt sich überhaupt aus dem unmittelbaren Produkt\n des individuellen Producenten in das gemeinsame Produkt eines kombinir-\n ten Arbeitspersonals, dessen Glieder der Handhabung des Arbeitsgegen-\n standes näher oder ferner stehn. Mit dem cooperativen Charakter des\n [Note: haupt ein Meister des Plagiats (seine ganze Bevölkerungstheorie ist ein schamloses\n Plagiat), sich 1815 annexirte, die West zur selben Zeit und unabhängig von\n Anderson entwickelte, die Ricardo 1817 in Zusammenhang mit der allgemeinen\n Werththeorie brachte und die von da an unter dem Namen Ricardo’s die Runde\n der Welt gemacht hat, die 1820 von James Mill (dem Vater J. St. Mill’s) vul-\n garisirt, und endlich u. a. auch von Herrn J. St. Mill als bereits Gemeinplatz\n gewordnes Schuldogma repetirt wird. Es ist unläugbar, dass J. St.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"16.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0005","text":"Mill seine\n jedenfalls „merkwürdige“ Autorität fast nur ähnlichen qui pro quo verdankt.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"497\", \"facs\": \"#f0516\"} --> Arbeitsprozesses selbst erweitert sich daher nothwendig der Begriff\n der produktiven Arbeit und ihres Trägers, des produktiven\n Arbeiters. Andrerseits verengt er sich. Die kapitalistische Produk-\n tion ist nicht nur Produktion von Waare, sie ist wesentlich Pro-\n duktion von Mehrwerth. Der Arbeiter producirt nicht für sich,\n sondern für das Kapital. Es genügt daher nicht länger, dass er über-\n haupt producirt. Er muss Mehrwerth produciren. Nur der Arbei-\n ter ist produktiv, der Mehrwerth für den Kapitalisten\n producirt oder zur Selbstverwerthung des Kapitals dient.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"16.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0006","text":"Steht es frei ein Beispiel ausserhalb der Sphäre der materiellen Produktion\n zu wählen, so ist ein Schulmeister produktiver Arbeiter, wenn er nicht nur\n Kinderköpfe bearbeitet, sondern sich selbst abarbeitet zur Bereicherung\n des Unternehmers. Dass der sein Kapital in einer Lehrfabrik angelegt hat,\n statt in einer Wurstfabrik, ändert nichts an dem Verhältniss. Der Be-\n griff des produktiven Arbeiters schliesst daher keineswegs bloss ein Ver-\n hältniss zwischen Thätigkeit und Nutzeffekt, zwischen Arbeiter und Arbeits-\n produkt ein, sondern auch ein spezifisch gesellschaftliches Produktionsverhält-\n niss, welches den Arbeiter zum unmittelbaren Verwerthungsmittel des Ka-\n pitals stempelt. Produktiver Arbeiter zu sein, ist daher kein Glück, sondern\n ein Pech.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"16.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0007","text":"Im vierten Buch dieser Schrift, welches die Geschichte der\n Theorie behandelt, wird man näher sehn, dass die klassische politische\n Oekonomie von jeher die Produktion von Mehrwerth zum ent-\n scheidenden Charakter des produktiven Arbeiters machte. Mit ihrer Auf-\n fassung von der Natur des Mehrwerths wechselt daher ihre Definition\n des produktiven Arbeiters.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"16.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0008","text":"Zunächst erschienen uns die Produktion von absolutem Mehrwerth\n und die Produktion von relativem Mehrwerth als zwei verschiedne,\n verschiednen Entwicklungsepochen des Kapitals angehörige, Produk-\n tionsarten. Die Produktion des absoluten Mehrwerths\n bedingt, dass die sachlichen Arbeitsbedingungen in Kapital und die Arbeiter\n in Lohnarbeiter verwandelt sind, dass die Produkte als Waaren, d. h.\n für den Verkauf producirt werden, dass der Produktionsprozess zu-\n gleich Konsumtionsprozess der Arbeitskraft durch das Kapital und daher\n der direkten Kontrole des Kapitalisten unterworfen ist, endlich, dass der\n Arbeitsprozess, also der Arbeitstag, über den Punkt hinaus verlängert\n wird, wo der Arbeiter nur ein Aequivalent für den Werth seiner Arbeits-\n [Running matter: I. 32] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"498\", \"facs\": \"#f0517\"} --> kraft producirt hätte.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"16.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0009","text":"Die allgemeinen Bedingungen aller Pro-\n duktion von Waaren vorausgesetzt, besteht die Produktion des abso-\n luten Mehrwerths einfach in der Verlängerung des Arbeitstags\n über die Grenze der zum Leben des Arbeiters selbst nothwendigen\n Arbeitszeit, und in der Aneignung der Mehrarbeit durch das\n Kapital. Dieser Prozess kann vorgehn und geht vor auf Grundlage von\n Betriebsweisen, die ohne Zuthun des Kapitals historisch überliefert sind.\n Es findet dann nur eine formelle Metamorphose statt, oder die kapitali-\n stische Ausbeutungsweise unterscheidet sich von den früheren, wie Skla-\n vensystem u. s. w., nur dadurch, dass die Mehrarbeit hier durch direkten\n Zwang abgerungen, dort durch „freiwilligen“ Verkauf der Arbeitskraft\n vermittelt wird. Die Produktion des absoluten Mehrwerths unterstellt\n also nur formelle Subsumtion der Arbeit unter das Ka-\n pital.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"16.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0010","text":"Die Produktion des relativen Mehrwerths setzt die Pro-\n duktion des absoluten Mehrwerths voraus, also auch die entsprechende\n allgemeine Form der kapitalistischen Produktion. Ihr Zweck ist Erhöhung\n des Mehrwerths durch Verkürzung der nothwendigen Arbeitszeit, unabhängig\n von den Grenzen des Arbeitstags. Das Ziel wird erreicht durch Ent-\n wicklung der Produktivkräfte der Arbeit. Diess bedingt jedoch eine Re-\n volution des Arbeitsprozesses selbst. Es genügt nicht mehr ihn zu ver-\n längern, er muss neu gestaltet werden. Die Produktion des relativen\n Mehrwerths unterstellt also eine specifisch kapitalistische\n Produktionsweise, die mit ihren Methoden, Mitteln und Bedingungen\n selbst erst auf Grundlage der formellen Subsumtion der Arbeit unter das\n Kapital naturwüchsig entsteht und ausgebildet wird. An die Stelle der\n formellen tritt die reelle Subsumtion der Arbeit unter das\n Kapital.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"16.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0011","text":"Es genügt blosser Hinweis auf Zwitterformen, worin die\n Mehrarbeit weder durch direkten Zwang dem Producenten ausgepumpt\n wird, noch auch dessen formelle Unterordnung unter das Kapital ein-\n getreten ist. Das Kapital hat sich hier noch nicht unmittelbar des Arbeits-\n prozesses bemächtigt. Neben die selbstständigen Producenten,\n die in überlieferter, urväterlicher Betriebsweise handwerkern oder acker-\n bauen, tritt der Wucherer oder der Kaufmann, dasWucherkapital oder das\n Handelskapital, das sie parasitenmässig aussaugt. Vorherrschaft dieser\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"499\", \"facs\": \"#f0518\"} --> Exploitationsform in einer Gesellschaft schliesst die kapitalistische\n Produktionsweise aus, zu der sie andrerseits, wie im späteren Mittel-\n alter, den Uebergang bilden kann.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"16.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0012","text":"Endlich, wie das Beispiel der\n modernen Hausarbeit gezeigt, werden gewisse Zwitterformen auf dem Hin-\n tergrund der grossen Industrie stellenweis reproducirt, wenn auch mit\n gänzlich veränderter Physiognomie.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"16.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0013","text":"Wenn zur Produktion des absoluten Mehrwerths die bloss formelle\n Subsumtion der Arbeit unter das Kapital genügt, z. B. dass Handwerker,\n die früher für sich selbst oder auch als Gesellen eines Zunftmeisters arbei-\n teten, nun als Lohnarbeiter unter die direkte Kontrole des Kapitalisten\n treten, zeigte sich andrerseits, wie die Methoden zur Produktion des rela-\n tiven Mehrwerths zugleich Methoden zur Produktion des absoluten Mehr-\n werths sind. Ja die masslose Verlängerung des Arbeitstags stellte sich\n als eigenstes Produkt der grossen Industrie dar. Ueberhaupt hört die\nspezifisch kapitalistische Produktionsweise auf blosses\n Mittel zur Produktion des relativen Mehrwerths zu sein, sobald\n sie sich eines ganzen Produktionszweigs und noch mehr, sobald sie sich\naller entscheidenden Produktionszweige bemächtigt hat.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"16.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0014","text":"Sie wird jetzt\n allgemeine, gesellschaftlich herrschende Form des Produktionsprozesses.\n Als besondre Methode zur Produktion des relativen Mehr-\n werths wirkt sie nur noch, erstens soweit sie dem Kapital bisher nur\n formell untergeordnete Industrieen ergreift, also in ihrer Propaganda.\n Zweitens, soweit in den ihr bereits anheimgefallenen Industrieen fort-\n währende Revolution in der Anwendung der Maschinerie, der Natur-\n kräfte und der Produktionsmethode überhaupt stattfindet.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"16.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0015","text":"Von gewissem Gesichtspunkt scheint der Unterschied zwischen abso-\n lutem und relativem Mehrwerth überhaupt illusorisch. Der relative\n Mehrwerth ist absolut, denn er bedingt absolute Verlängerung\n des Arbeitstags über die zur Existenz des Arbeiters selbst nothwen-\n dige Arbeitszeit. Der absolute Mehrwerth ist relativ, denn er bedingt\n eine Entwicklung der Arbeitsproduktivität, welche erlaubt, die nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit auf einen Theil des Arbeitstags zu beschränken. Fasst\n man aber die Bewegung des Mehrwerths ins Auge, so verschwindet\n dieser Schein der Einerleiheit. Die Produktivkraft der Arbeit und ihren\n Normalgrad von Intensivität gegeben, ist die Rate des Mehrwerths nur\n erhöhbar durch absolute Verlängerung des Arbeitstags.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"16.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0016","text":"[Running matter: 32*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"500\", \"facs\": \"#f0519\"} --> Andrerseits ist die Rate des Mehrwerths nur erhöhbar, bei gegebner Grenze\n des Arbeitstags, durch relativen Grössenwechsel seiner Be-\n standtheile, der nothwendigen Arbeit und der Mehrarbeit, was sei-\n nerseits, soll der Lohn nicht unter den Werth der Arbeitskraft sinken,\n Wechsel in der Produktivität oder Intensivität der Arbeit voraussetzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"16.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0017","text":"Braucht der Arbeiter alle seine Zeit, um die zur Erhaltung seiner\n selbst und seiner Race nöthigen Lebensmittel zu produciren, so bleibt ihm\n keine Zeit, um unentgeldlich für dritte Personen zu arbeiten. Ohne einen\n gewissen Produktivitätsgrad der Arbeit keine solche disponible Zeit des\n Arbeiters, ohne solche überschüssige Zeit keine Mehrarbeit und daher\n keine Kapitalistenklasse. Ein gewisser Höhepunkt der Arbeitsproduktivität\n ist also überhaupt Existenzbedingung der kapitalistischen Produktion, wie\n aller früheren Produktionsweisen, worin ein Theil der Gesellschaft nicht\n nur für sich selbst, sondern auch für den andern arbeitet [Note: „The very existence of the master-capitalists as a distinct class is depen-\n dent on the productiveness of industry.“ (Ramsay l. c. p. 206.) „If each\n man’s labour were but enough to produce his own food, there could be no pro-\n perty.“ (Ravenstone l.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"16.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0018","text":"c. p. 14, 15.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"16.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0019","text":"So kann von einer Naturbasis des Mehrwerths gesprochen wer-\n den, aber nur in dem ganz allgemeinen Sinn, dass kein absolutes Natur-\n hinderniss den einen abhält die zu seiner eignen Existenz nöthige Arbeit\n von sich selbst ab- und einem andern aufzuwälzen. Es sind durchaus nicht,\n wie es hier und da geschehn, mystische Vorstellungen mit dieser natur-\n wüchsigen Produktivität der Arbeit zu verbinden. Nur sobald\n die Menschen sich aus ihren ersten Thierzuständen herausgearbeitet, ihre\n Arbeit selbst also schon in gewissem Grad vergesellschaftet ist, treten\n Verhältnisse ein, worin die Mehrarbeit des einen zur Existenzbedingung\n des andern wird. In den Kulturanfängen sind die erworbnen Produktiv-\n kräfte der Arbeit gering, aber so sind die Bedürfnisse, die sich mit und an\n den Mitteln ihrer Befriedigung entwickeln.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"16.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0020","text":"Ferner ist in jenen Anfängen die\n Proportion der Gesellschaftstheile, die von fremder Arbeit leben, ver-\n schwindend klein gegen die Masse der unmittelbaren Producenten. Mit\n dem Fortschritt der gesellschaftlichen Produktivkraft der Arbeit wächst\n diese Proportion absolut und relativ [Note: „Among the wild Indians in America, almost every thing is the labourer’s,\n 99 parts of an hundred are to be put upon the account of Labour: In England,] . Das Kapitalverhältniss\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"501\", \"facs\": \"#f0520\"} --> entspringt übrigens auf einem ökonomischen Boden, der das Produkt einer\n langen Reihe früherer Entwicklungsphasen ist. Die vorhandne Pro-\n duktivität der Arbeit, wovon es als Grundlage ausgeht, ist nicht\n Gabe der Natur, sondern der Geschichte.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"16.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0021","text":"Von der mehr oder minder entwickelten Gestalt des gesellschaftlichen\n Produktionsprozesses abgesehn, bleibt die Produktivität der Arbeit an\nNaturbedingungen gebunden, und wechselt der Grad ihrer Produk-\n tivität mit dem Reichthum dieser Naturbedingungen. Sie sind alle rück-\n führbar auf die Natur des Menschen selbst und die ihn umgebende Natur.\n Der grössere oder geringere Reichthum der menschlichen Natur hängt ab\n von Race, Boden und Klima. Die äussern Naturbedingungen zerfallen\n ökonomisch in zwei grosse Klassen, natürlicher Reichthum an Lebens-\n mitteln, also Bodenfruchtbarkeit, fischreiche Gewässer u. s. w., und\n natürlicher Reichthum an Arbeitsmitteln, wie lebendige Wasserge-\n fälle, schiffbare Flüsse, Holz, Metalle, Kohle u. s. w. In den Kulturan-\n fängen giebt die erstere, auf höherer Entwicklungsstufe die zweite Art des\n natürlichen Reichthums den Ausschlag. Man vergleiche z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"16.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0022","text":"England\n mit Indien oder, in der antiken Welt, Athen und Korinth mit den Ufer-\n ländern des schwarzen Meeres.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"16.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0023","text":"Je geringer die Zahl der absolut zu befriedigenden Naturbedürfnisse,\n und je grösser die natürliche Bodenfruchtbarkeit und Gunst des Klimas, desto\n geringer die zur Erhaltung und Reproduktion des Producenten nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit. Desto grösser kann also der Ueberschuss seiner Arbeit für An-\n dere über seine Arbeit für sich selbst sein. So bemerkt schon Diodor über die\n alten Aegypter: „Es ist ganz unglaublich, wie wenig Mühe und Kosten die\n Erziehung ihrer Kinder ihnen verursacht. Sie kochen ihnen die nächste\n beste einfache Speise; auch geben sie ihnen von der Papierstaude den\n unteren Theil zu essen, soweit man ihn im Feuer rösten kann, und die\n Wurzel und Stengel der Sumpfgewächse, theils roh, theils gesotten und\n gebraten. Die meisten Kinder gehn ohne Schuhe und unbekleidet, da die\n Luft so mild ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"16.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0024","text":"Daher kostet ein Kind seinen Aeltern, bis es erwachsen\n ist, im Ganzen nicht über zwanzig Drachmen. Hieraus ist es hauptsäch-\n lich zu erklären, dass in Aegypten die Bevölkerung so zahlreich ist und\n [Note: perhaps the labourer has not ⅔.“ („The Advantages of the East India\n Trade etc.“, p. 73.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"502\", \"facs\": \"#f0521\"} --> darum so viel grosse Werke angelegt werden konnten“ [Note: Diodor l. c. l. I, c. 80.] .\n Indess sind die grossen Bauwerke des alten Aegyptens dem Umfang seiner\n Bevölkerung weniger geschuldet als der grossen Proportion, worin sie dis-\n ponibel war. Wie der individuelle Arbeiter um so mehr Mehrarbeit liefern\n kann, je geringer seine nothwendige Arbeitszeit, so, je geringer der zur\n Produktion der nothwendigen Lebensmittel erheischte Theil der Arbeiter-\n bevölkerung, desto grösser der für andres Werk disponible Theil.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"16.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0025","text":"Die kapitalistische Produktion einmal vorausgesetzt, wird, unter sonst\n gleichbleibenden Umständen und bei gegebner Länge des Arbeitstags, die\n Grösse der Mehrarbeit mit den Naturbedingungen der Arbeit, namentlich\n auch der Bodenfruchtbarkeit, variiren. Es folgt aber keineswegs umge-\n kehrt, dass der fruchtbarste Boden der geeignetste zum Wachsthum der\n kapitalistischen Produktionsweise. Sie unterstellt Herrschaft des Men-\n schen über die Natur. Eine zu verschwenderische Natur „hält ihn an ihrer\n Hand wie ein Kind am Gängelband“. Sie macht seine eigne Entwicklung\n nicht zu einer Naturnothwendigkeit [Note: „The first (natural wealth), as it is most noble and advantageous, so\n doth it make the people careless, proud, and given to all excesses; whereas the\n second enforceth vigilancy, literature, arts and policy.“ („England’s Trea-\n sure by Foreign Trade.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"16.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0026","text":"Or the Balance of our Foreign Trade is\n the Rule of our Treasure. Written by Thomas Mun, of London,\n Merchant, and now published for the common good by his son\n John Mun. Lond. 1669“, p. 181, 182.) „Nor can I conceive a greater\n course upon a body of people, than to be thrown upon a spot of land, where the\n productions for subsistence and food were, in great measure, spon-\n taneous, and the climate required or admitted little care for raiment and covering\n . . . . there may be an extreme on the other side. A soil incapable of produce\n by labour is quite as bad as a soil that produces plentifully without any labour.“\n („An Inquiry into the Present High Price of Provisions. Lond.\n 1767“, p. 10.)] . Nicht das tropische Klima mit\n seiner überwuchernden Vegetation, sondern die gemässigte Zone ist das\n Mutterland des Kapitals.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"16.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0027","text":"Es ist nicht die absolute Fruchtbarkeit des\n Bodens, sondern seine Differenzirung, die Mannigfaltigkeit seiner natür-\n lichen Produkte, welche die Naturgrundlage der gesellschaftlichen Theilung\n der Arbeit bildet, und den Menschen durch den Wechsel der Naturum-\n stände, innerhalb deren er haust, zur Vermannigfachung seiner eignen Be-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"503\", \"facs\": \"#f0522\"} --> dürfnisse, Fähigkeiten, Arbeitsmittel und Arbeitsweisen spornt. Die Noth-\n wendigkeit eine Naturkraft gesellschaftlich zu kontroliren, da-\n mit Haus zu halten, sie durch Werke von Menschenhand auf grossem Mass-\n stab erst anzueignen oder zu zähmen, spielt die entscheidendste Rolle in der\n Geschichte der Industrie. So z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"16.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0028","text":"die Wasserreglung in Aegypten [Note: Die Nothwendigkeit, die Perioden der Nilbewegung zu berechnen, schuf\n die ägyptische Astronomie und mit ihr die Herrschaft der Priesterkaste als Leiterin\n der Agrikultur. „Le solstice est le moment de l’année où commence la crue du\n Nil, et celui que les Égyptiens ont dû observer avec le plus d’attention … C’était\n cette année tropique qu’il leur importait de marquer pour se diriger dans leurs\n opérations agricoles. Ils durent donc chercher dans le ciel un signe apparent de\n son retour.“ (Cuvier: „Discours sur les révolutions du globe ed.\n Hoefer. Paris 1863“, p. 141.)] ,\n Lombardei, Holland u. s. w. Oder in Indien, Persien u. s. w., wo die\n Ueberrieselung durch künstliche Kanäle dem Boden nicht nur das unent-\n behrliche Wasser, sondern mit dessen Geschlämme zugleich den Mineral-\n dünger von den Bergen zuführt.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"16.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0029","text":"Das Geheimniss der Industrieblüthe von\n Spanien und Sicilien unter arabischer Herrschaft war die Kanalisation [Note: Eine der materiellen Grundlagen der Staatsmacht über die zusammen-\n hangslosen kleinen Produktionsorganismen Indiens war Reglung der Wasserzu-\n fuhr. Die muhamedanischen Herrscher Indiens verstanden diess besser als ihre\n englischen Nachfolger. Wir erinnern nur an die Hungersnoth von 1866, die mehr\n als einer Million Hindus in dem Distrikt von Orissa, Präsidentschaft Bengal, das\n Leben kostete.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"16.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0030","text":"Die Gunst der Naturbedingungen liefert immer nur die Möglich-\n keit, niemals die Wirklichkeit der Mehrarbeit, also des Mehrwerths\n oder des Surplusprodukts. Die verschiednen Naturbedingungen der Arbeit\n bewirken, dass dieselbe Quantität Arbeit in verschiednen Ländern\n verschiedne Bedürfnissmassen befriedigt [Note: „There are no two countries which furnish an equal number of the\n necessaries of life in equal plenty, and with the same quantity of labour\n man’s wants increase or diminish with the severity or temperateness of the climate\n they live in; consequently the proportion of trade which the inhabitants of\n different countries are obliged to carry on through necessity, cannot be the same,\n nor is it practicable to ascertain the degree of variation farther than by the Degrees\n of Heat and Cold; from whence one may make this general conclusion, that quan-\n tity of labour required for a","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"16.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0031","text":"certain number of people is greatest in cold climates,\n and least in hot ones; for in the former men not only want more clothes, but the] , dass also, unter sonst analogen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"504\", \"facs\": \"#f0523\"} --> Umständen, die nothwendige Arbeitszeit verschieden ist. Auf\n die Mehrarbeit wirken sie nur als Naturschranke, d. h. durch die Bestim-\n mung des Punkts, wo die Arbeit für Andre beginnen kann. In dem-\n selben Mass, worin die Industrie vortritt, weicht diese Naturschranke zu-\n rück.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"16.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0032","text":"Mitten in der westeuropäischen Gesellschaft, wo der Arbeiter die\n Erlaubniss für seine eigne Existenz zu arbeiten nur durch Mehrarbeit er-\n kauft, wird sich leicht eingebildet, es sei eine der menschlichen Arbeit\n eingeborne Qualität, ein Surplusprodukt zu liefern [Note: „Chaque travail doit (scheint auch zu den droits und devoirs du citoyen\n zu gehören) laisser un excédant.“ (Proudhon.)] . Man nehme aber\n z. B. den Einwohner der östlichen Inseln des asiatischen Archipelagus,\n wo der Sago wild im Walde wächst. „Wenn die Bewohner, indem sie\n ein Loch in den Baum bohren, sich davon überzeugt haben, dass das\n Mark reif ist, so wird der Stamm umgeschlagen und in mehrere Stücke\n getheilt, das Mark wird herausgekratzt, mit Wasser gemischt und geseiht,\n es ist dann vollkommen brauchbares Sagomehl. Ein Baum giebt ge-\n meiniglich 300 Pfund und kann 5 bis 600 Pfund geben.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"16.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0033","text":"Man geht dort\n also in den Wald und schneidet sich sein Brod, wie man bei uns sein Brenn-\n holz schlägt“ [Note: F. Shouw: „Die Erde, die Pflanze und der Mensch.“\n 2. Aufl. Leipzig 1854, p. 148.] . Gesetzt ein solcher ostasiatischer Brodschneider brauche\n 12 Arbeitsstunden in der Woche zur Befriedigung aller seiner Bedürfnisse.\n Was ihm die Gunst der Natur unmittelbar giebt, ist viel Mussezeit.\n Damit er diese produktiv für sich selbst verwende, ist eine ganze Reihe\n geschichtlicher Umstände, damit er sie in Mehrarbeit für fremde Personen\n verausgabe, ist äusserer Zwang erheischt. Würde kapitalistische Produk-\n tion eingeführt, so müsste der Brave vielleicht 6 Tage in der Woche arbeiten,\n um sich selbst das Produkt eines Arbeitstags anzueignen. Die Gunst der\n Natur erklärt nicht, warum er jetzt 6 Tage in der Woche arbeitet oder warum\n er 5 Tage Mehrarbeit liefert.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"16.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0034","text":"Sie erklärt nur, warum seine nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit auf einen Tag in der Woche beschränkt ist. In keinem Fall\n [Note: earth more cultivating than in the latter.“ („An Essay on the Governing\n Causes of the Natural Rate of Interest. Lond. 1750“, p. 60.) Der\n Verfasser dieser epochemachenden anonymen Schrift ist J. Massey. Hume\n nahm daraus seine Zinstheorie.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"505\", \"facs\": \"#f0524\"} --> aber entspränge sein Mehrprodukt aus einer der menschlichen Arbeit ein-\n gebornen, occulten Qualität.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"16.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0035","text":"Wie die geschichtlich entwickelten, gesellschaftlichen, so er-\n scheinen die naturbedingten Produktivkräfte der Arbeit\n als Produktivkräfte des Kapitals, dem sie einverleibt wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"16.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0036","text":"## 2) Grössenwechsel von Preis der Arbeitskraft und\n Mehrwerth.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"16.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0037","text":"In Kapitel III, 3. Abschnitt, analysirten wir die Rate des Mehr-\n werths, aber nur vom Standpunkt der Produktion des absoluten Mehr-\n werths. In Kapitel IV fanden wir zusätzliche Bestimmungen. Das\n Wesentliche ist hier zu späterem Gebrauch kurz zusammenzufassen.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"16.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0038","text":"Der Werth der Arbeitskraft ist bestimmt durch den Werth der ge-\n wohnheitsmässig nothwendigen Lebensmittel des Durchschnittsarbeiters.\n Die Masse dieser Lebensmittel, obgleich ihre Form wechseln mag, ist\n in einer bestimmten Epoche einer bestimmten Gesellschaft gegeben, und\n daher als constante Grösse zu behandeln. Was wechselt, ist der Werth\n dieser Masse. Zwei andre Faktoren gehn in die Werthbestimmung\n der Arbeitskraft ein. Einerseits ihre Entwicklungskosten, die sich\n mit der Produktionsweise ändern, andrerseits ihre Naturdifferenz,\n ob sie männlich oder weiblich, reif oder unreif. Der Verbrauch dieser\n differenten Arbeitskräfte, wieder bedingt durch die Produktionsweise,\n macht grossen Unterschied in den Reproduktionskosten der Arbeiterfamilie\n und dem Werth des erwachsnen männlichen Arbeiters. Beide Faktoren\n bleiben jedoch bei der folgenden Untersuchung ausgeschlossen.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"16.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0039","text":"Wir unterstellen, 1) dass die Waaren zu ihrem Werth verkauft\n werden, 2) dass der Preis der Arbeitskraft wohl gelegentlich über\n ihren Werth steigt, aber nie unter ihn sinkt.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"16.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0040","text":"Diess einmal unterstellt, fand sich, dass die relativen Grössen von\n Preis der Arbeitskraft und von Mehrwerth durch drei Umstände bedingt sind,\ndie Länge des Arbeitstags oder die extensive Grösse der\n Arbeit, die normale Intensivität der Arbeit, oder ihre\n intensive Grösse, so dass bestimmtes Arbeitsquantum in bestimmter\n Zeit verausgabt wird, endlich die Produktivkraft der Arbeit,\n so dass je nach dem Entwicklungsgrad der Produktionsbedingungen das-\n selbe Quantum Arbeit in derselben Zeit ein grösseres oder kleineres Quan-\n tum Produkt liefert. Sehr verschiedne Kombinationen sind offenbar\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"506\", \"facs\": \"#f0525\"} --> möglich, je nachdem einer der drei Faktoren constant und zwei variabel,\n oder zwei Faktoren constant und einer variabel, oder endlich alle drei\n gleichzeitig variabel sind.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"16.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0041","text":"Diese Kombinationen werden noch dadurch\n vermannigfacht, dass bei gleichzeitiger Variation verschiedner Faktoren die\n Grösse und Richtung der Variation verschieden sein können. Im Folgen-\n den sind nur die Hauptkombinationen dargestellt.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"16.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0042","text":"## A) Grösse des Arbeitstags und Intensivität der Ar-\n beit constant (gegeben), Produktivkraft der Arbeit va-\n riabel.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"16.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0043","text":"Unter dieser Voraussetzung sind Werth der Arbeitskraft und Mehr-\n werth durch drei Gesetze bestimmt:","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"16.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0044","text":"Erstens: Der Arbeitstag von gegebner Grösse stellt\n sich stets in demselben Werthprodukt dar, wie auch die\n Produktivität der Arbeit, mit ihr die Produktenmasse\n und daher der Preis der einzelnen Waare wechsle.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"16.44"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0045","text":"Das Werthprodukt eines zwölfstündigen Arbeitstags ist 6 sh.\n z. B., obgleich die Masse des producirten Gebrauchswerths mit der Pro-\n duktivkraft der Arbeit wechselt, der Werth von 6 sh. sich also über mehr\n oder weniger Waaren vertheilt.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"16.45"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0046","text":"Zweitens: Werth der Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth\n wechseln in umgekehrter Richtung zu einander und zum\n Wechsel in der Produktivkraft der Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"16.46"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0047","text":"Das Werthprodukt des zwölfstündigen Arbeitstags ist eine constante\n Grösse, z. B. 6 sh. Diese constante Grösse ist gleich der Summe des\n Mehrwerths plus dem Werth der Arbeitskraft, den der Ar-\n beiter durch ein Aequivalent ersetzt. Es ist selbstverständlich, dass von\n zwei Theilen einer constanten Grösse keiner zunehmen kann, ohne dass\n der andre abnimmt und keiner abnehmen, ohne dass der andre zunimmt.\n Der Werth der Arbeitskraft kann nicht von 3 sh. auf 4 steigen, ohne dass\n der Mehrwerth von 3 sh. auf 2 fällt und der Mehrwerth kann nicht von\n 3 auf 4 sh. steigen, ohne dass der Werth der Arbeitskraft von 3 sh. auf\n 2 fällt. Unter diesen Umständen also ist kein Wechsel in der abso-\n luten Grösse, sei es des Werths der Arbeitskraft, sei es des Mehr-\n werths, möglich ohne einen Wechsel ihrer relativen oder verhält-\n nissmässigen Grössen.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"16.47"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0048","text":"Es ist unmöglich, dass sie gleich-\n zeitig fallen oder steigen.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"16.48"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0049","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"507\", \"facs\": \"#f0526\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"16.49"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0050","text":"Der Werth der Arbeitskraft kann ferner nicht fallen, also der Mehr-\n werth nicht steigen, ohne dass die Produktivkraft der Arbeit\n steigt, z. B. im obigen Fall kann der Werth der Arbeitskraft nicht von\n 3 auf 2 sh. sinken, ohne dass erhöhte Produktivkraft der Arbeit erlaubt in\n 4 Stunden dieselbe Masse Lebensmittel zu produciren, die vorher 6 Stun-\n den zu ihrer Produktion erheischten. Umgekehrt kann der Werth der Ar-\n beitskraft nicht von 3 auf 4 sh. steigen, ohne dass die Produktivkraft der\n Arbeit fällt, also 8 Stunden zur Produktion derselben Masse von Lebens-\n mitteln erheischt sind, wozu früher 6 Stunden genügten. Dieselbe\n Richtung im Wechsel der Produktivkraft der Arbeit, ihre Zunahme oder\n Abnahme, wirkt in entgegengesetzter Richtung auf den gleichzei-\n tigen Grössenwechsel von Werth der Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"16.50"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0051","text":"Bei Formulirung dieses Gesetzes übersah Ricardo einen Umstand:\n Obgleich der Wechsel in der Grösse des Mehrwerths oder der Mehr-\n arbeit einen umgekehrten Wechsel in der Grösse des Werths der Ar-\n beitskraft oder der nothwendigen Arbeit bedingt, folgt keineswegs, dass\n sie in demselben Verhältniss wechseln. Sie nehmen zu oder ab\num dieselbe Grösse. Das Verhältniss aber, worin jeder Theil\n des Werthprodukts oder des Arbeitstags zu- oder abnimmt, hängt von der\nursprünglichen Theilung ab, die vor dem Wechsel in der Produktiv-\n kraft der Arbeit stattfand. War der Weith der Arbeitskraft z. B. 4 sh.\n oder die nothwendige Arbeitszeit gleich 8 Stunden, also der Mehrwerth\n 2 sh. oder die Mehrarbeit gleich 4 Stunden, und fällt, in Folge erhöhter\n Produktivkraft der Arbeit, der Werth der Arbeitskraft auf 3 sh. oder die\n nothwendige Arbeit auf 6 Stunden, so steigt der Mehrwerth auf 3 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"16.51"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0052","text":"oder\n die Mehrarbeit auf 6 Stunden. Es ist dieselbe Grösse von zwei\n Stunden oder 1 sh., die dort zugefügt, hier weggenommen wird. Aber\ndas Verhältniss des Grössenwechsels ist auf beiden Seiten\nverschieden. Während der Werth der Arbeitskraft von 4 sh. auf 3,\n also um ¼ oder 25 % sinkt, steigt der Mehrwerth von 2 sh. auf 3, also\n um ½ oder 50 %. Es folgt daher, dass die proportionelle Zu- oder\n Abnahme des Mehrwerths, in Folge eines gegebnen Wechsels in der Pro-\n duktivkraft der Arbeit, um so grösser, je kleiner, und um so kleiner, je\n grösser ursprünglich der Theil des Arbeitstags war, der sich in Mehrwerth\n darstellt.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"16.52"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0053","text":"Drittens: Zu- oder Abnahme des Mehrwerths ist stets\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"508\", \"facs\": \"#f0527\"} --> Folge und nie Grund der entsprechenden Ab- oder Zu-\n nahme des Werths der Arbeitskraft [Note: Zu diesem dritten Gesetz hat Mac Culloch u. A. den abgeschmackten\n Zusatz gemacht, dass der Mehrwerth ohne Fall im Werth der Arbeitskraft steigen\n kann durch Abschaffung von Steuern, die der Kapitalist früher zu zahlen hatte.\n Die Abschaffung solcher Steuern ändert absolut nichts an dem Quantum Mehr-\n werth, das der industrielle Kapitalist in erster Hand dem Arbeiter auspumpt. Sie\n ändert nur die Proportion, worin er diesen Mehrwerth in seine eigne Tasche steckt\n oder mit dritten Personen theilen muss. Sie ändert also nichts an dem Verhältniss\n zwischen Werth der Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"16.53"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0054","text":"Die „Ausnahme“ des Mac\n Culloch beweist also nur sein Missverständniss der Regel, ein Malheur, das ihm\n in der Vulgarisation Ricardo’s eben so oft passirt als dem J. B. Say in der Vul-\n garisation A. Smith’s.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"16.54"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0055","text":"Da der Arbeitstag von constanter Grösse ist, sich in einer constanten\n Werthgrösse darstellt, jedem Grössenwechsel des Mehrwerths ein umge-\n kehrter Grössenwechsel im Werth der Arbeitskraft entspricht und der Werth\n der Arbeitskraft nur wechseln kann mit einem Wechsel in der Produktiv-\n kraft der Arbeit, folgt unter diesen Bedingungen offenbar, dass jeder\n Grössenwechsel des Mehrwerths aus einem Grössenwechsel im Werth der\n Arbeitskraft entspringt. Wenn man daher gesehn, dass kein absoluter\n Grössenwechsel im Werth der Arbeitskraft und des Mehrwerths\n möglich ist ohne einen Wechsel ihrer relativen Grössen, so folgt jetzt,\n dass kein Wechsel ihrer relativen Werthgrössen möglich ist\n ohne einen Wechsel der absoluten Werthgrösse der Arbeits-\n kraft.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"16.55"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0056","text":"Ricardo hat die eben aufgestellten drei Gesetze zuerst streng for-\n mulirt. Die Mängel seiner Darstellung sind, 1) dass er die besondern\n Bedingungen, innerhalb deren jene Gesetze gelten, als sich von selbst ver-\n stehende allgemeine und ausschliessliche Bedingungen der kapi-\n talistischen Produktion voraussetzt; 2), und diess verfälscht seine Analyse\n in viel höherem Grad, dass er überhaupt den Mehrwerth nicht rein dar-\n stellt, d. h. nicht unabhängig von seinen besondern Formen, wie Profit, Grund-\n rente u. s. w. Er wirft daher die Gesetze über die Rate des\n Mehrwerths unmittelbar zusammen mit den Gesetzen der Profit-\n rate. Ich werde später, im 3.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"16.56"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0057","text":"Buch dieser Schrift beweisen, dass die-\n selbe Rate des Mehrwerths sich in den verschiedensten\n Profitraten und verschiedne Raten des Mehrwerths, unter\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"509\", \"facs\": \"#f0528\"} --> bestimmten Umständen, sich in derselben Profitrate ausdrücken\n können.","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"16.57"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0058","text":"Nach dem dritten Gesetz unterstellt der Grössenwechsel des Mehr-\n werths eine durch Wechsel in der Produktivkraft der Arbeit verursachte\n Werthbewegung der Arbeitskraft. Die Grenze jenes Wechsels ist\n durch die neue Werthgrenze der Arbeitskraft gegeben. Es\n können aber, auch wenn die Umstände dem Gesetz zu wirken erlauben,\n Zwischenbewegungen stattfinden. Fällt z. B. in Folge erhöhter Produk-\n tivkraft der Arbeit der Werth der Arbeitskraft von 4 sh. auf 3, oder die\n nothwendige Arbeitszeit von 8 Stunden auf 6, so könnte der Preis der\n Arbeitskraft nur auf 3 sh. 8 d., 3 sh. 6 d., 3 sh. 2 d. u. s. w. fallen, der\n Mehrwerth daher nur auf 3 sh. 4 d., 3 sh. 6 d., 3 sh. 10 d. u. s. w.\n steigen.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"16.58"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0059","text":"Der Grad des Falls, dessen Minimalgrenze 3 sh., hängt\n von dem relativen Gewicht ab, das der Druck des Kapitals von der einen\n Seite, der Widerstand der Arbeiter von der andern Seite in die Wag-\n schale wirft.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"16.59"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0060","text":"Der Werth der Arbeitskraft ist bestimmt durch den Werth eines be-\n stimmten Quantums von Lebensmitteln. Was mit der Produktivkraft der\n Arbeit wechselt, ist der Werth dieser Lebensmittel, nicht ihre Masse.\n Die Masse selbst kann, bei steigender Produktivkraft der Arbeit, für Ar-\n beiter und Kapitalist gleichzeitig und in demselben Verhält-\n niss wachsen, ohne irgend einen Grössenwechsel zwischen Preis der\n Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth. Ist z. B. der ursprüngliche Werth der Ar-\n beitskraft gleich 3 sh. und beträgt die nothwendige Arbeitszeit 6 Stunden,\n ist der Mehrwerth ebenfalls gleich 3 sh. oder beträgt die Mehrarbeit auch 6\n Stunden, so würde eine Verdopplung in der Produktivkraft der Arbeit, bei\n gleichbleibender Theilung des Arbeitstags, Preis der Arbeitskraft und Mehr-\n werth unverändert lassen.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"16.60"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0061","text":"Nur stellte sich jeder derselben in doppelt\n so vielen, aber verhältnissmässig verwohlfeilerten Gebrauchswerthen dar. Ob-\n gleich der Preis der Arbeitskraft unverändert, wäre er über ihren\nWerth gestiegen. Fiele der Preis der Arbeitskraft, aber nicht zur\n Minimalgrenze ihres neuen Werths von 1½ sh., sondern nur auf 2 sh.\n 10 d., 2 sh. 6 d. u. s. w., so repräsentirte dieser fallende Preis immer\n noch eine wachsende Masse von Lebensmitteln. Der Preis der Arbeits-\n kraft könnte so bei steigender Produktivkraft der Arbeit beständig fallen\n mit gleichzeitigem, fortwährendem Wachsthum der Lebensmittelmasse des\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"510\", \"facs\": \"#f0529\"} --> Arbeiters. Relativ aber, d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"16.61"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0062","text":"verglichen mit dem Mehrwerth, sänke der\n Werth der Arbeitskraft beständig, und erweiterte sich also die Kluft zwi-\n schen den Lebenslagen von Arbeiter und Kapitalist [Note: „When an alteration takes place in the productiveness of industry, and\n that either more or less is produced by a given quantity of labour and capital, the\n proportion of wages may obviously vary, whilst the quantity, which that proportion\n represents, remains the same, or the quantity may vary, whilst the proportion\n remains the same.“ („Outlines of Political Economy etc.“, p. 67.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"16.62"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0063","text":"## B) Constanter Arbeitstag, constante Produktivkraft\n der Arbeit, Intensivität der Arbeit variabel.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"16.63"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0064","text":"Wachsende Intensivität der Arbeit unterstellt vermehrte Ausgabe von\n Arbeit in demselben Zeitraum. Der intensivere Arbeitstag verkörpert sich\n daher in mehr Produkten als der minder intensive von gleicher Stunden-\n zahl. Mit erhöhter Produktivkraft liefert zwar auch derselbe Arbeitstag\n mehr Produkte. Aber im letztern Fall sinkt der Werth des einzelnen\n Produkts, weil es weniger Arbeit als vorher kostet, im erstern Fall bleibt\n er unverändert, weil das Produkt nach wie vor gleich viel Arbeit kostet.\n Die Zahl der Produkte steigt hier ohne Fall ihres Preises. Mit ihrer An-\n zahl wächst ihre Preissumme, während dort dieselbe Werthsumme sich\n nur in vergrösserter Produktenmasse darstellt. Bei gleichbleibender\n Stundenzahl verkörpert sich also der intensivere Arbeitstag in höherem\n Werthprodukt, also, bei gleichbleibendem Werth des Geldes, in mehr Geld.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"16.64"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0065","text":"Sein Werthprodukt variirt mit den Abweichungen seiner Intensivität von\n dem gesellschaftlichen Normalgrad. Derselbe Arbeitstag stellt sich also\n nicht wie vorher in einem constanten, sondern in einem variablen\n Werthprodukt dar, der intensivere, zwölfstündige Arbeitstag z. B. in\n 7 sh., 8 sh u. s. w. statt in 6 sh. wie der zwölfstündige Arbeitstag von\n gewöhnlicher Intensivität. Es ist klar: Variirt das Werthprodukt\n des Arbeitstags, etwa von 6 auf 8 sh., so können beide Theile\n dieses Werthprodukts, Preis der Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth,\ngleichzeitig wachsen, sei es in gleichem oder ungleichem Grad. Preis\n der Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth können beide zur selben Zeit von 3 sh.\n auf 4 wachsen, wenn das Werthprodukt von 6 auf 8 sh. steigt. Preis-\n erhöhung der Arbeitskraft schliesst hier nicht nothwendig Steigerung ihres\n Preises über ihren Werth ein.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"16.65"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0066","text":"Sie kann umgekehrt von einem Fall\n ihres Werths begleitet scin. Diess findet stets statt, wenn die Preiser-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"511\", \"facs\": \"#f0530\"} --> höhung der Arbeitskraft ihren beschleunigten Versch’eiss nicht kom-\n pensirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"16.66"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0067","text":"Man weiss, dass mit vorübergehenden, im vorigen Kapitel erklärten\n Ausnahmen, veränderte Produktivität der Arbeit nur dann einen Grössen-\n wechsel im Werth der Arbeitskraft und daher in der Grösse des Mehr-\n werths bewirkt, wenn die Produkte der betroffenen Industriezweige in den\n gewohnheitsmässigen Konsum des Arbeiters eingehen. Diese Schranke\n fällt hier fort. Ob die Grösse der Arbeit extensiv oder intensiv wechsle,\n ihrem Grössenwechsel entspricht ein Wechsel in der Grösse ihres Werth-\n produkts, unabhängig von der Natur des Artikels, worin sich dieser\n Werth darstellt.","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"16.67"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0068","text":"Steigerte sich die Intensivität der Arbeit in allen Industriezweigen, so\n würde der neue höhere Intensivitätsgrad nun seinerseits zum gewöhnlichen\n gesellschaftlichen Normalgrad der Arbeit und hörte damit auf als extensive\n Grösse zu zählen. Indess blieben selbst dann die durchschnittlichen Inten-\n sivitätsgrade der Arbeit bei verschiednen Nationen verschieden und modi-\n ficirten daher die Anwendung des Werthgesetzes auf unterschiedne Natio-\n nalarbeitstage. Der intensivere Arbeitstag der einen Nation stellt sich in\n höherem Geldausdruck dar als der minder intensive der andern [Note: „All things being equal, the English manufacturer can turn out a con-\n siderably larger amount of work in a given time than a foreign manufacturer, so\n much as to counterbalance the difference of the working days, between 60 hours\n a week here and 72 or 80 elsewhere.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"16.68"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0069","text":"for\n 31st Oct. 1855“, p. 65.) Grössere gesetzliche Verkürzung des Arbeitstags in\n den kontinentalen Fabriken wäre das unfehlbarste Mittel zur Verminderung dieser\n Differenz zwischen der kontinentalen und der englischen Arbeitsstunde.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"16.69"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0070","text":"## C) Produktivkraft und Intensivität der Arbeit con-\n stant, Arbeitstag variabel.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"16.70"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0071","text":"Der Arbeitstag kann nach zwei Richtungen variiren. Er kann ver-\n kürzt oder verlängert werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"16.71"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0072","text":"Verkürzung des Arbeitstags unter den gegebnen Be-\n dingungen, d. h. gleichbleibender Produktivkraft und Intensivität der Ar-\n beit, lässt den Werth der Arbeitskraft und daher die nothwendige\n Arbeitszeit unverändert. Sie verkürzt die Mehrarbeit und den Mehrwerth.\n Mit der absoluten Grösse des letztern fällt auch seine relative Grösse, d. h.\n seine Grösse im Verhältniss zur gleichbleibenden Werthgrösse der Arbeits-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"512\", \"facs\": \"#f0531\"} --> kraft. Nur durch Herabdrückung ihres Preises unter ihren Werth könnte\n der Kapitalist sich schadlos halten.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"16.72"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0073","text":"Alle hergebrachten Redensarten wider die Verkürzung des Arbeits-\n tags unterstellen, dass das Phänomen sich unter den hier vorausgesetzten\n Umständen ereignet, während in der Wirklichkeit umgekehrt Wechsel in\n der Produktivität und Intensivität der Arbeit entweder der Verkürzung des\n Arbeitstags vorhergehn oder ihr unmittelbar nachfolgen [Note: „There are compensating circumstances … which the working of the\n Ten Hours’ Act has brought to light.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact. for\n 1. December 1848“, p. 7.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"16.73"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0074","text":"Verlängerung des Arbeitstags. Die nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit sei gleich 6 Stunden oder der Werth der Arbeitskraft gleich 3 sh.,\n ebenso Mehrarbeit gleich 6 Stunden und Mehrwerth gleich 3 sh. Der\n Gesammtarbeitstag beträgt dann 12 Stunden und stellt sich in einem\n Werthprodukt von 6 sh. dar. Wird der Arbeitstag um 2 Stunden ver-\n längert und bleibt der Preis der Arbeitskraft unverändert, so wächst mit\n der absoluten die relative Grösse des Mehrwerths. Obgleich die\n Werthgrösse der Arbeitskraft absolut unverändert bleibt, fällt sie relativ.\n Unter den Bedingungen von A) konnte die relative Werthgrösse der Ar-\n beitskraft nicht wechseln ohne einen Wechsel ihrer absoluten Grösse. Hier,\n im Gegentheil, ist der relative Grössenwechsel im Werth der Arbeitskraft\n das Resultat eines absoluten Grössenwechsels des Mehrwerths.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"16.74"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0075","text":"Da das Werthprodukt, worin sich der Arbeitstag darstellt, mit seiner\n eignen Verlängerung wächst, können Preis der Arbeitskraft und\n Mehrwerth gleichzeitig wachsen, sei es um gleiches oder un-\n gleiches Increment. Diess gleichzeitige Wachsthum ist also in zwei\n Fällen möglich, bei absoluter Verlängerung des Arbeitstags, und bei\n wachsender Intensivität der Arbeit ohne solche Verlängerung.","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"16.75"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0076","text":"Mit verlängertem Arbeitstag kann der Preis der Arbeitskraft unter\n ihren Werth fallen, obgleich er nominell unverändert bleibt oder\n selbst steigt. Der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft ist nämlich, wie man\n sich erinnern wird, geschätzt auf ihre normale Durchschnittsdauer oder\n die normale Lebensperiode des Arbeiters, und auf entsprechenden, normalen,\n der Menschennatur angemessenen Umsatz von Lebenssubstanz in Bewe-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"513\", \"facs\": \"#f0532\"} --> gung [Note: „The amount of labour which a man had undergone in the course of 24\n hours might be approximatively arrived at by an examination of the chymical\n changes which had taken place in his body, changed forms in matter indicating\n the anterior exercise of dynamic force.“ (Grove: „On the Correlation\n of Physical Forces.“)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"16.76"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0077","text":"Bis zu einem gewissen Punkt kann der von Verlängerung des\n Arbeitstags untrennbare grössere Verschleiss der Arbeitskraft durch grös-\n seren Ersatz kompensirt werden. Ueber diesen Punkt hinaus wächst der\n Verschleiss in geometrischer Progression und werden zugleich alle normalen\n Reproduktions- und Bethätigungsbedingungen der Arbeitskraft zerstört.\n Der Preis der Arbeitskraft und ihr Exploitationsgrad hören auf mit ein-\n ander kommensurable Grössen zu sein.","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"16.77"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0078","text":"## D) Gleichzeitige Variationen in Länge des Arbeits-\n tags, Produktivkraft und Intensivität der Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"16.78"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0079","text":"Es ist hier offenbar eine grosse Anzahl Kombinationen möglich. Je\n zwei Faktoren können variiren und einer constant bleiben, oder alle drei\n können gleichzeitig variiren. Sie können in gleichem oder ungleichem\n Grad variiren, in derselben oder entgegengesetzter Richtung, ihre Varia-\n tionen sich daher theilweis oder ganz aufheben. Indess ist die Analyse\n aller möglichen Fälle nach den unter A) B) und C) gegebnen Aufschlüssen\n leicht. Man findet das Resultat jeder möglichen Kombination, indem man\n der Reihe nach je einen Faktor als variabel und die andern zunächst als\n constant behandelt. Wir nehmen hier daher nur noch kurze Notiz von\n zwei wichtigen Fällen.","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"16.79"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0080","text":"Abnehmende Produktivkraft der Arbeit mit gleich-\n zeitiger Verlängerung des Arbeitstags.","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"16.80"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0081","text":"Wenn wir hier von abnehmender Produktivkraft der Arbeit sprechen, so\n handelt es sich von Arbeitszweigen, deren Produkte den Werth der Arbeitskraft\n bestimmen, also z. B. von abnehmender Produktivkraft der Arbeit in Folge\n zunehmender Unfruchtbarkeit des Bodens und entsprechender Vertheurung\n der Bodenprodukte. Der Arbeitstag sei zwölfstündig, sein Werthprodukt\n 6 sh., wovon die Hälfte den Werth der Arbeitskraft ersetzt, die andre\n Hälfte Mehrwerth bildet. Der Arbeitstag zerfällt also in 6 Stunden noth-\n wendiger Arbeit und 6 Stunden Mehrarbeit. In Folge der Vertheurung\n der Bodenprodukte steige der Werth der Arbeitskraft von 3 auf 4 sh.,\n [Running matter: I. 33] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"514\", \"facs\": \"#f0533\"} --> also die nothwendige Arbeitszeit von 6 auf 8 Stunden. Bleibt der Arbeits-\n tag unverändert, so fällt die Mehrarbeit von 6 auf 4 Stunden, der Mehr-\n werth von 3 auf 2 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"16.81"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0082","text":"Wird der Arbeitstag um 2 Stunden verlängert,\n also von 12 auf 14 Stunden, so bleibt die Mehrarbeit 6 Stunden, der\n Mehrwerth 3 sh., aber seine Grösse im Vergleich zum Werth der Arbeitskraft,\n gemessen durch die nothwendige Arbeit, fällt. Wird der Arbeitstag um 4\n Stunden verlängert, von 12 auf 16 Stunden, so bleiben die propor-\n tionellen Grössen von Mehrwerth und Werth der Arbeitskraft, Mehr-\n arbeit und nothwendiger Arbeit unverändert, aber die absolute\n Grösse des Mehrwerths wächst von 3 auf 4 sh., die der Mehrarbeit von\n 6 auf 8 Arbeitsstunden, also um ⅓ oder 33⅓ %.","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"16.82"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0083","text":"Bei abnehmen-\n der Produktivkraft der Arbeit und gleichzeitiger Ver-\n längerung des Arbeitstags kann also die absolute Grösse des\n Mehrwerths unverändert bleiben, während seine proportionelle Grösse fällt;\n seine proportionelle Grösse kann unverändert bleiben, während seine abso-\n lute Grösse wächst, und, je nach dem Grad der Verlängerung, können\n beide wachsen. Diess ist eine der Ursachen, warum in England von\n 1799—1815, grade als West, Ricardo u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"16.83"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0084","text":"den nur in ihrer Phan-\n tasie durch Vertheurung der Bodenprodukte bewirkten Fall der Rate des\n Mehrwerths zum Ausgangspunkt wichtiger Analysen machten, der Mehr-\n werth absolut und relativ stieg, und daher zugleich beschleunigtes Wachs-\n thum des Kapitals und Verpauperung der Arbeiter stattfanden [Note: „A principal cause of the increase of capital, during the war, pro-\n ceeded from the greater exertions, and perhaps the greater privations\n of the labouring classes, the most numerous in every society. More women\n and children were compelled, by necessitous circumstances, to enter upon labori-\n ous occupations; and former workmen were, from the same cause, obliged to\n devote a greater portion of their time to increase production.“\n („Essays on Political Econ. in which are illustrated the Prin-\n cipal Causes of the Present National Distress. London 1830“,\n p. 248.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"16.84"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0085","text":"Es\n war diess die Periode, worin die masslose Verlängerung des Arbeitstags\n sich Bürgerrecht erwarb [Note: „Corn and Labour rarely march quite abreast; but there is an obvious\n limit, beyond which they cannot be separated. With regard to the unusual\n exertions made by the labouring classes in periods of dearness, which pro-\n duce the fall of wages noticed in the evidence (nämlich vor den Par-] .","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"16.85"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0086","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"515\", \"facs\": \"#f0534\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"16.86"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0087","text":"Zunehmende Intensivität und Produktivkraft der\n Arbeit mit gleichzeitiger Verkürzung des Arbeitstags.","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"16.87"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0088","text":"Gesteigerte Produktivkraft der Arbeit und ihre wachsende Intensivi-\n tät wirken nach einer Seite hin gleichförmig. Beide vermehren die Pro-\n duktenmasse in gegebnem Zeitraum. Beide verkürzen also den Theil des\n Arbeitstags, den der Arbeiter zur Produktion seiner Lebensmittel oder\n ihres Aequivalents braucht. Die absolute Grenze des Arbeitstags\n wird überhaupt gebildet durch diesen seinen nothwendigen, aber\nkontraktilen Bestandtheil. Schrumpfte darauf der ganze Arbeits-\n tag zusammen, so verschwände die Mehrarbeit, was unter dem Regime des\n Kapitals unmöglich. Die Beseitigung der kapitalistischen Produktionsform\n erlaubt den Arbeitstag durch die nothwendige Arbeit zu be-\n schränken. Jedoch würde die letztere mit ihrem Begriff, unter sonst gleich-\n bleibenden Umständen, auch ihren Raum ausdehnen.","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"16.88"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0089","text":"Einerseits weil die\n Lebensbedingungen des Arbeiters reicher und seine Lebensansprüche\n grösser. Andrerseits würde ein Theil der jetzigen Mehrarbeit zur noth-\n [Note: liamentary Committees of Inquiry 1814—15), they are most meritorious in the\n individuals, and certainly favour the growth of capital. But no man of\n humanity could wish to see them constant and unremitted. They are\n most admirable as a temporary relief; but if they were constantly in action,\n effects of a similar kind would result from them, as from the population of a\n country being pushed to the very extreme limits of its food.“ (Malthus:\n „Inquiry into the Nature and Progress of Rent. Lond. 1815“,\n p. 48 Note.)","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"16.89"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0090","text":"Es macht Malthus alle Ehre, dass er die auch an andrer Stelle in\n seinem Pamphlet direkt ausgesprochne Verlängerung des Arbeitstags\n betont, während Ricardo und Andre, im Angesicht der schreiendsten Thatsachen,\n an ihrer Schulansicht von der constanten Grösse des Arbeitstags dogmatisch\n festhielten, und sie in der That allen ihren Untersuchungen zu Grund legten. Aber\n die konservativen Interessen, deren Knecht Malthus war, hinderten ihn zu sehn,\n dass die masslose Verlängerung des Arbeitstags, zugleich mit ausserordentlicher\n Entwicklung der Maschinerie und der Exploitation der Weiber- und Kinderarbeit,\n einen grossen Theil der Arbeiterklasse „überzählig“ machen mussten, namentlich\n sobald die Kriegsnachfrage und das englische Monopol des Weltmarkts aufhörten.","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"16.90"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0091","text":"Es war natürlich weit bequemer und den Interessen der herrschenden Klassen, die\n Malthus ächt pfäffisch idolatrisirt, viel entsprechender, diese „Uebervölkerung“\n aus den ewigen Gesetzen der Natur, als aus den nur historischen Naturgesetzen\n der kapitalistischen Produktion zu erklären.] \n [Running matter: 33*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"516\", \"facs\": \"#f0535\"} --> wendigen Arbeit zählen, nämlich die zur Erzielung eines gesellschaftlichen\n Reservefonds und Accumulationsfonds nöthige Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"16.91"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0092","text":"Je mehr die Produktivkraft der Arbeit wächst, um so mehr kann der\n Arbeitstag verkürzt werden, und je mehr der Arbeitstag verkürzt wird,\n desto mehr kann die Intensivität der Arbeit wachsen. Die Produk-\n tivkraft der Arbeit wächst, gesellschaftlich betrachtet, auch mit der\n Oekonomie derselben. Diese schliesst nicht nur die Oekonomisi-\n rung der Produktionsmittel ein, sondern die Vermeidung aller nutzlosen\n Arbeit. Während die kapitalistische Produktionsweise in jedem indivi-\n duellen Geschäft Oekonomie erzwingt, erzeugt ihr anarchisches System\n der Konkurrenz die massloseste Verschwendung der gesellschaftlichen\n Produktionsmittel und Arbeitskräfte, neben einer Unzahljetzt unentbehrlicher,\n aber an und für sich überflüssiger Funktionen.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"16.92"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0093","text":"Intensivität und Produktivkraft der Arbeit gegeben, ist der zur\nmateriellen Produktion nothwendige Theil des gesell-\n schaftlichen Arbeitstags um so kürzer, der für freie, geistige\n und gesellschaftliche Bethätigung der Individuen eroberte Zeittheil also\n um so grösser, je gleichmässiger die Arbeit unter alle werkfähigen\n Glieder der Gesellschaft vertheilt ist, je weniger eine Gesellschaftsschichte\n die Naturnothwendigkeit der Arbeit von sich selbst ab- und einer andern\n Schichte zuwälzen kann. Die absolute Grenze für die Verkürzung des\n Arbeitstags ist nach dieser Seite hin die Allgemeinheit der Arbeit.\n In der kapitalistischen Gesellschaft wird freie Zeit für eine Klasse produ-\n cirt durch Verwandlung aller Lebenszeit der Massen in Arbeitszeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"16.93"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0094","text":"## 3) Verschiedne Formeln für die Rate des Mehrwerths.","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"16.94"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0095","text":"Man hat gesehn, dass die Rate des Mehrwerths sich dar-\n stellt in den Formeln:","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"16.95"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0096","text":"I)\n.\n Die zwei ersten Formeln stellen als Verhältniss von Werthen dar, was die\n dritte als Verhältniss der Zeiten, worin diese Werthe producirt werden.\n Diese einander ersetzenden Formeln sind begrifflich streng. Man findet\n sie daher wohl der Sache nach, aber nicht bewusst ausgearbeitet in der\n klassischen politischen Oekonomie. Hier begegnen wir dagegen den fol-\n genden abgeleiteten Formeln.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"16.96"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0097","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"517\", \"facs\": \"#f0536\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"16.97"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0098","text":"II)\n. Eine und die-\n selbe Proportion ist hier abwechselnd ausgedrückt in der Form der Arbeits-\n zeiten, der Werthe, worin sie sich verkörpern, der Produkte, worin diese\n Werthe existiren. Es wird natürlich unterstellt, dass unter Werth des\n Produkts nur das Werthprodukt des Arbeitstags zu verstehn, der\n constante Theil des Produktenwerths aber ausgeschlossen ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"16.98"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0099","text":"In allen diesen Formeln ist der wirkliche Exploitations-\n grad der Arbeit oder die Rate des Mehrwerths falsch ausge-\n drückt. Der Arbeitstag sei 12 Stunden. Mit den andern Annahmen unsres\n früheren Beispiels stellt sich in diesem Fall der wirkliche Exploitations-\n grad der Arbeit dar in den Proportionen: \n. Nach den Formeln II) er-\n halten wir dagegen: \n = 50 %.","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"16.99"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0100","text":"Diese abgeleiteten Formeln drücken in der That die Proportion\n aus, worin der Arbeitstag oder sein Werthprodukt sich zwischen\n Kapitalist und Arbeiter theilt. Gelten sie daher als unmittelbare Aus-\n drücke des Selbstverwerthungsgrades des Kapitals, so gilt das falsche\n Gesetz: die Mehrarbeit oder der Mehrwerth kann nie\n 100 % erreichen [Note: So z. B. in: „Dritter Brief an v. Kirchmann von Rodbertus.\n Widerlegung der Ricardo’schen Theorie von der Grundrente\n und Begründung einer neuen Rententheorie. Berlin 1851“. Ich\n komme später auf diese Schrift zurück, die trotz ihrer falschen Theorie von der\n Grundrente das Wesen der kapitalistischen Produktion durchschaut.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"16.100"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0101","text":"Da die Mehrarbeit stets nur einen aliquoten\n Theil des Arbeitstags oder der Mehrwerth stets nur einen aliquoten Theil\n des Werthprodukts bilden kann, ist die Mehrarbeit nothwendiger Weise\n stets kleiner als der Arbeitstag oder der Mehrwerth stets kleiner als das\n Werthprodukt. Um sich zu verhalten wie \\frac{100}{100}, müssten sie aber gleich\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"518\", \"facs\": \"#f0537\"} --> sein. Damit die Mehrarbeit den ganzen Arbeitstag absorbire (es handelt\n sich hier um den Durchschnittstag der Arbeitswoche, des Arbeitsjahrs\n u. s. w.), müsste die nothwendige Arbeit auf Null sinken. Verschwindet\n aber die nothwendige Arbeit, so verschwindet auch die Mehrarbeit, da\n letztre nur eine Funktion der erstern.","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"16.101"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0102","text":"Die Proportion \\frac{Mehrarbeit}{Arbeitstag} =\n\\frac{Mehrwerth}{Werthprodukt} kann also niemals die Grenze \\frac{100}{100} erreichen und noch we-\n niger auf \\frac{100 + x}{100} steigen. Wohl aber die Rate des Mehrwerths oder\n der wirkliche Exploitationsgrad der Arbeit. Nimm z. B. die Schätzung\n des Herrn L. de Lavergne, wonach der englische Ackerbauarbeiter nur\n ¼, der Kapitalist (Pächter) dagegen ¾ des Produkts [Note: Der Theil des Produkts, der nur das ausgelegte constante Kapital ersetzt,\n ist bei dieser Rechnung selbstverständlich abgezogen. — Herr L. de Lavergne,\n blinder Bewunderer Englands, giebt eher zu niedriges als zu hohes Verhältniss.]  oder seines\n Werths erhält, wie die Beute sich immer zwischen Kapitalist und Grund-\n eigenthümer u. s. w. nachträglich weiter vertheile.","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"16.102"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0103","text":"Die Mehrarbeit des\n englischen Landarbeiters verhält sich danach zu seiner nothwendigen Arbeit\n = 3 : 1, ein Prozentsatz der Exploitation von 300 %.","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"16.103"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0104","text":"Die Schulmethode, den Arbeitstag als constante Grösse zu be-\n handeln, wurde durch Anwendung der Formeln II) befestigt, weil man hier\n die Mehrarbeit stets mit einem Arbeitstag von gegebner Grösse vergleicht.\n Ebenso, wenn die Theilung des Werthprodukts ausschliesslich\n in’s Auge gefasst wird. Der Arbeitstag, der sich bereits in einem Werth-\n produkt vergegenständlicht hat, ist stets ein Arbeitstag von gegebnen\n Grenzen.","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"16.104"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0105","text":"Die Darstellung von Mehrwerth und Werth der Arbeitskraft als\n Bruchtheilen des Werthprodukts — eine Darstellungsweise, die übrigens\n aus der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise selbst erwächst und deren Be-\n deutung sich später erschliessen wird — versteckt den spezifischen\n Charakter des Kapitalverhältnisses, nämlich den Austausch des variablen\n Kapitals mit der lebendigen Arbeitskraft, und den entsprechenden\n Ausschluss des Arbeiters vom Produkt.","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"16.105"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0106","text":"An die Stelle tritt der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"519\", \"facs\": \"#f0538\"} --> falsche Schein eines Associationsverhältnisses, worin Arbeiter und Kapi-\n talist das Produkt nach dem Verhältniss seiner verschiednen Bildungs-\n faktoren theilen [Note: Da alle entwickelten Formen des kapitalistischen Produktionsprozesses\n Formen der Cooperation sind, ist natürlich nichts leichter, als von ihrem spezi-\n fisch antagonistischen Charakter zu abstrahiren und sie so in freie Associations-\n formen umzufabeln, wie in des Grafen A. de Laborde: „De l’Esprit de\n l’Association dans tous les intérêts de la Communauté. Paris\n 1818“. Der Yankee H. Carey bringt diess Kunststück mit demselben Erfolg\n gelegentlich selbst an den Verhältnissen des Sklavensystems fertig.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"16.106"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0107","text":"Uebrigens sind die Formeln II stets in die Formeln I rückverwan-\n delbar. Haben wir z. B. \\frac{Mehrarbeit von 6 Stunden}{Arbeitstag von 12 Stunden}, so ist die noth-\n wendige Arbeitszeit = Arbeitstag von zwölf Stunden\n — Mehrarbeit von sechs Stunden, und so ergiebt sich:\n\\frac{Mehrarbeit von 6 Stunden}{Nothwendige Arbeit von 6 Stunden} = \\frac{100}{100}.","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"16.107"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0108","text":"Eine dritte Formel, die ich gelegentlich schon anticipirt habe, ist:","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"16.108"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0109","text":"III)\n\\frac{Mehrwerth}{Werth der Arbeitskraft} = \\frac{Mehrarbeit}{Nothwendige Arbeit} = \\frac{Unbezahlte Arbeit}{Bezahlte Arbeit}.","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"16.109"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0110","text":"Das Missverständniss, wozu die Formel \\frac{Unbezahlte Arbeit}{Bezahlte Arbeit} verleiten\n könnte, als zahle der Kapitalist die Arbeit und nicht die Arbeitskraft, fällt\n nach der früher gegebnen Entwicklung fort. \\frac{Unbezahlte Arbeit}{Bezahlte Arbeit} ist nur\npopulärer Ausdruck für \\frac{Mehrarbeit}{Nothwendige Arbeit}. Der Kapitalist zahlt\n den Werth, resp. davon abweichenden Preis der Arbeitskraft, und erhält im\n Austausch die Verfügung über die lebendige Arbeitskraft selbst. Seine\n Nutzniessung dieser Arbeitskraft zerfällt in zwei Perioden. Während der\n einen Periode producirt der Arbeiter nur einen Werth = Werth seiner\n Arbeitskraft, also nur ein Aequivalent. Für den vorgeschossnen Preis der\n Arbeitskraft erhält der Kapitalist so ein Produkt vom selben Preis. Es ist\n als ob er das Produkt fertig auf dem Markt gekauft hätte.","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"16.110"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0111","text":"In der Periode\n der Mehrarbeit dagegen bildet die Nutzniessung der Arbeitskraft Werth für\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"520\", \"facs\": \"#f0539\"} --> den Kapitalisten, ohne ihm einen Werthersatz zu kosten [Note: Obgleich die Physiokraten das Geheimniss des Mehrwerths nicht durch-\n schauten, war ihnen doch so viel klar, dass er „une richesse indépendante et\n disponible, qu’il (der Besitzer davon) n’a point achetée et qu’il vend.“\n (Turgot: „Réflexions sur la Formation et la Distribution des\n Richesses“, p. 11.)] . Er hat diese\n Flüssigmachung der Arbeitskraft umsonst. In diesem Sinn kann diese\n Mehrarbeit unbezahlte Arbeit heissen.","kind":"passage","sequence":111,"address":"16.111"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0112","text":"Das Kapital ist also nicht nur Kommando über Arbeit, wie\n A. Smith sagt. Es ist wesentlich Kommando über unbezahlte\n Arbeit. Aller Mehrwerth, in welcher besondern Gestalt von Profit,\n Zins, Rente u. s. w. er sich später krystallisire, ist seiner Substanz nach\nMateriatur unbezahlter Arbeitszeit. Das Geheimniss von der\nSelbstverwerthung des Kapitals löst sich auf in seine Ver-\n fügung über ein bestimmtes Quantum unbezahlter frem-\n der Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":112,"address":"16.112"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0113","text":"## 4) Werth, resp. Preis der Arbeitskraft in der ver-\n wandelten Form des Arbeitslohns.","kind":"passage","sequence":113,"address":"16.113"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0114","text":"## a) Die Formverwandlung.","kind":"passage","sequence":114,"address":"16.114"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0115","text":"Auf der Oberfläche der bürgerlichen Gesellschaft erscheint der\n Lohn des Arbeiters als Lohn der Arbeit, ein bestimmtes Quantum\n Geld, das für ein bestimmtes Quantum Arbeit gezahlt wird. Man spricht\n hier von einem Werth der Arbeit und nennt den Geldausdruck dieses\n Werths den nothwendigen oder natürlichen Preis der Ar-\n beit. Man spricht andrerseits von Marktpreisen der Arbeit,\n d. h. über oder unter ihrem nothwendigen Preis oscillirenden Preisen.","kind":"passage","sequence":115,"address":"16.115"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0116","text":"Aber was ist der Werth einer Waare? Die Vergegenständlichung\n der zu ihrer Produktion gesellschaftlich nothwendigen Arbeit. Und wo-\n durch messen wir die Grösse ihres Werths? Durch die Grösse der in\n ihr enthaltenen Arbeitszeit. Wodurch wäre also der Werth z. B.\n eines zwölfstündigen Arbeitstags bestimmt? Durch die in einem Arbeits-\n tag von 12 Stunden enthaltenen 12 Arbeitsstunden, was eine abge-\n schmackte Tautologie ist [Note: „Mr. Ricardo, ingeniously enough, avoids a difficulty which, on a first\n view, threatens to encumber his doctrine, that value depends on the quantity of\n labour employed in production. If this principle is rigidly adhered to, it follows] .","kind":"passage","sequence":116,"address":"16.116"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0117","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"521\", \"facs\": \"#f0540\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":117,"address":"16.117"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0118","text":"Um als Waare auf dem Markt verkauft zu werden, müsste die Arbeit\n jedenfalls existiren, bevor sie verkauft wird. Könnte der Arbeiter ihr\n aber eine selbstständige Existenz geben, so würde er Waare verkaufen\n und nicht Arbeit [Note: „If you call labour a commodity, it is not like a commodity which is\n first produced in order to exchange, and then brought to market where it must\n exchange with other commodities according to the respective quantities of each\n which there may be at the market in the time; labour is created at the moment\n it is brought to market; nay, it is brought to market before it is created.“\n („Observations on some verbal disputes etc.“, p. 75, 76.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":118,"address":"16.118"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0119","text":"Von diesen Widersprüchen abgesehn, würde ein direkter Austausch\n von Geld, d. h. vergegenständlichter Arbeit, mit lebendiger Arbeit entweder\n das Werthgesetz aufheben, welches sich grade erst auf Grundlage\n der kapitalistischen Produktion frei entwickelt, oder die kapitalistische\n Produktion selbst aufheben, welche grade auf der Lohnarbeit\n beruht. Der Arbeitstag von 12 Stunden stellt sich z. B. in einem Geldwerth\n von 6 sh. dar. Werden Aequivalente ausgetauscht, so erhält der Arbeiter\n für zwölfstündige Arbeit 6 sh. Der Preis seiner Arbeit wäre gleich dem\n Preis seines Produkts. In diesem Fall producirte er keinen Mehrwerth\n für den Käufer seiner Arbeit, die 6 sh. verwandelten sich nicht in Kapital,\n die Grundlage der kapitalistischen Produktion verschwände, aber grade\n auf dieser Grundlage verkauft er seine Arbeit und ist seine Arbeit\n wesentlich Lohnarbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":119,"address":"16.119"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0120","text":"Oder er erhält für 12 Stunden Arbeit weniger\n als 6 sh., d. h. weniger als 12 Stunden Arbeit. Zwölf Stunden Arbeit\n tauschen sich aus gegen 10, 6 u. s. w. Stunden Arbeit. Diese Gleichsetzung\n ungleicher Grössen hebt nicht nur die Werthbestimmung auf. Ein\n [Note: that the value of labour depends of the quantity of labour em-\n ployed in producing it — which is evidently absurd. By a dexterous turn,\n therefore, Mr. Ricardo makes the value of labour depend on the quantity of labour\n required to produce wages, or, to give him the benefit of his own language, he\n maintains, that the value of labour is to be estimated by the quantity of labour\n required to produce wages; by which he means the quantity of labour required to\n produce the money or commodities given to the labourer.","kind":"passage","sequence":120,"address":"16.120"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0121","text":"This is similar to\n saying, that the value of cloth is estimated, not by the quantity of labour bestowed\n on its production, but by the quantity of labour bestowed on the production of\n the silver, for which the cloth is exchanged.“ („A Critical Dissertation on\n the Nature etc. of Value“, p. 50, 51.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"522\", \"facs\": \"#f0541\"} --> solcher sich selbst aufhebender Widerspruch kann überhaupt nicht als Ge-\n setz auch nur ausgesprochen oder formulirt werden [Note: „Treating Labour as a commodity, and Capital, the produce of labour,\n as another, then, if the values of those two commodities were regulated by equal\n quantities of labour, a given amount of labour would . . . . exchange for that\n quantity of capital which had been produced by the same amount of labour;\nantecedent labour would … exchange for the same amount as present\n labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":121,"address":"16.121"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0122","text":"But the value of labour, in relation to other commodities … is de-\n termined not by equal quantities of labour.“ (E. G. Wakefield in\n s. Edit. von A. Smith’s „Wealth of Nations“, v. I. Lond. 1836. p. 231\n Note.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":122,"address":"16.122"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0123","text":"Es nützt nichts den Austausch von mehr Arbeit gegen weniger Arbeit\n aus dem Formunterschied der Arbeit herzuleiten, dass sie nämlich\n das einemal vergegenständlicht, das andremal lebendig ist [Note: „Il a fallu convenir (auch eine Ausgabe des „contrat social“) que toutes\n les fois qu’il échangerait du travail fait contre du travail à faire, le der-\n nier (le capitaliste) aurait une valeur supérieure au premier (le travailleur).“\n (Simonde (i. e. Sismondi): „De la Richesse Commerciale. Genève\n 1803“, t. I, p. 37.)] .\n Diess ist um so abgeschmackter als der Werth einer Waare nicht\n durch das Quantum wirklich in ihr vergegenständlichter, sondern durch\n das Quantum der zu ihrer Reproduktion nothwendigen lebendigen Arbeit\n bestimmt wird. Eine Waare stelle z. B. 6 Arbeitsstunden dar.","kind":"passage","sequence":123,"address":"16.123"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0124","text":"Werden\n Erfindungen gemacht, wodurch sie in 3 Stunden producirt werden kann,\n so sinkt der Werth auch der bereits producirten Waare um die Hälfte.\n Sie stellt jetzt 3 statt früher 6 Stunden nothwendige gesellschaftliche\n Arbeit dar. Es ist also das zu ihrer Produktion erheischte Quantum\n Arbeit, nicht deren gegenständliche Form, wodurch ihre Werth-\n grösse bestimmt wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":124,"address":"16.124"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0125","text":"Was dem Geldbesitzer auf dem Waarenmarkt direkt gegenübertritt,\n ist in der That nicht die Arbeit, sondern der Arbeiter. Was\n letztrer verkauft, ist seine Arbeitskraft. Sobald seine Arbeit wirk-\n lich beginnt, hat sie bereits aufgehört ihm zu gehören, kann also nicht\n mehr von ihm verkauft werden. Die Arbeit ist die Substanz und das im-\n manente Mass der Werthe, aber sie selbst hat keinen Werth [Note: „Labour, the exclusive standard of value … the creator of all wealth,\nno commodity.“ (Th. Hodgskin l. c. p. 186.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":125,"address":"16.125"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0126","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"523\", \"facs\": \"#f0542\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":126,"address":"16.126"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0127","text":"Im Ausdruck: Werth der Arbeit ist der Werthbegriff nicht nur\n völlig ausgelöscht, sondern in sein Gegentheil verkehrt. Es ist ein ima-\n ginärer Ausdruck wie etwa Werth der Erde. Diese imaginären Aus-\n drücke entspringen jedoch aus den Produktionsverhältnissen selbst. Sie\n sind Kategorieen für Erscheinungsformen wesentlicher Werth-\n verhältnisse. Dass in der Erscheinung die Dinge sich oft\n verkehrt darstellen, ist ziemlich in allen Wissenschaften bekannt, ausser\n in der politischen Oekonomie [Note: Diese Ausdrücke dagegen für blosse licentia poetica zu erklären, zeigt nur\n die Ohnmacht der Analyse.","kind":"passage","sequence":127,"address":"16.127"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0128","text":"Ich bemerke daher gegen Proudhon’s Phrase:\n „Le travail est dit valoir, non pas en tant que marchandise lui-même, mais en\n vue des valeurs qu’on suppose renfermées puissanciellement en lui: La valeur\n du travail est une expression figurée etc.“: „Dans le travail mar-\n chandise, qui est d’une réalité effrayante, il ne voit qu’une ellipse grammaticale.\n Donc toute la société actuelle, fondée sur le travail marchandise, est désormais\n fondée sur une licence poétique, sur une expression figurée. La société veut-elle\n „éliminer tous les inconvénients“, qui la travaillent, eh bien! qu’elle élimine les\n termes malsonnants, qu’elle change de langage, et pour cela elle n’a qu’à s’ad-\n dresser à l’Académie pour lui demander une nouvelle édition de son dictionnaire.“\n (K. Marx: „Misère de la Philosophie“, p. 34, 35.) Noch bequemer\n ist es natürlich, sich unter Werth gar nichts zu denken.","kind":"passage","sequence":128,"address":"16.128"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0129","text":"Man kann dann ohne\n Umstände alles unter diese Kategorie subsumiren. So z. B. J. B. Say. Was\n ist „valeur“? Antwort: „C’est qu’une chose vaut“, und was ist\n „Prix“? Antwort: „La valeur d’une chose exprimée en monnaie.“ Und\n warum hat „le travail de la terre … une valeur? Parce qu’on y met\nun prix.“ Also Werth ist was ein Ding werth ist, und die Erde hat einen\n „Werth“, weil man ihren Werth „in Geld ausdrückt“. Diess ist jedenfalls\n eine sehr einfache Methode, sich über das why und wherefore der Dinge zu ver-\n ständigen.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":129,"address":"16.129"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0130","text":"Die klassische politische Oekonomie entlehnte dem Alltagsleben ohne\n weitere Kritik die Kategorie Preis der Arbeit, um sich dann hinter-\n her zu fragen, wie wird dieser Preis bestimmt? Sie erkannte bald, dass\n der Wechsel im Verhältniss von Nachfrage und Zufuhr für den Preis der\n Arbeit, gleich dem jeder andern Waare, nichts erklärt ausser dem Wech-\n sel des Preises, die Oscillationen der Marktpreise unter oder über eine\n gewisse Grösse. Decken sich Nachfrage und Zufuhr, so hört, unter sonst\n gleichbleibenden Umständen, die Preisoscillation auf. Aber dann hören\n auch Nachfrage und Zufuhr auf irgend etwas zu erklären.","kind":"passage","sequence":130,"address":"16.130"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0131","text":"Der Preis der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"524\", \"facs\": \"#f0543\"} --> Arbeit, wenn Nachfrage und Zufuhr sich decken, ist der vom Verhältniss der\n Nachfrage und Zufuhr unabhängig bestimmte Preis der Arbeit, ihr natür-\n licher Preis, der so als der eigentliche Gegenstand der Analyse gefunden\n ward. Oder man nahm eine längere Periode der Oscillationen des Markt-\n preises, z. B. ein Jahr, und fand dann, dass sich ihr Auf und Ab aus-\n gleicht zu einer mittlern Durchschnittsgrösse, einer constanten Grösse.\n Sie musste natürlich anders bestimmt werden als die sich kompensirenden\n Abweichungen von ihr selbst. Dieser über die zufälligen Marktpreise der\n Arbeit übergreifende und sie regulirende Preis, nothwendige Preis\n (Physiokraten) oder „natürliche Preis der Arbeit“ (Adam\n Smith) kann, wie bei andern Waaren, nur ihr in Geld ausgedrückter\nWerth sein.","kind":"passage","sequence":131,"address":"16.131"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0132","text":"In dieser Art glaubte die politische Oekonomie durch die\n zufälligen Preise der Arbeit zu ihrem Werth vorzudringen. Wie bei\n den andern Waaren wurde dieser Werth dann weiter durch die Produk-\n tionskosten bestimmt. Aber was sind die Produktionskosten — des Ar-\n beiters, d. h. die Kosten, um den Arbeiter selbst zu produciren\n oder zu reproduciren? Diese Frage schob sich der politischen Oekonomie\nbewusstlos für die ursprüngliche unter, da sie mit den Produk-\n tionskosten der Arbeit als solcher sich im Kreise drehte und\n nicht vom Flecke kam. Was sie also Werth der Arbeit (value of la-\n bour) nennt, ist in der That der Werth der Arbeitskraft, die in\n der Persönlichkeit des Arbeiters existirt, und von ihrer Funktion, der Ar-\n beit, ebenso verschieden ist, wie eine Maschine von ihren Operationen.\n Beschäftigt mit dem Unterschied zwischen den Marktpreisen der Arbeit\n und ihrem s. g.","kind":"passage","sequence":132,"address":"16.132"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0133","text":"Werth, mit dem Verhältniss dieses Werths zur Profitrate,\n zu den vermittelst der Arbeit producirten Waarenwerthen u. s. w., ent-\n deckte man niemals, dass der Gang der Analyse nicht nur von den Markt-\n preisen der Arbeit zu ihrem Werth, sondern dahin geführt hatte, diesen\nWerth der Arbeit selbst wieder aufzulösen in den Werth der Ar-\n beitskraft. Die Bewusstlosigkeit über diess Resultat ihrer eignen\n Analyse, die kritiklose Annahme der Kategorieen Werth der Arbeit,\n natürlicher Preis der Arbeit u. s. w. als letzter adäquater Aus-\n drücke des behandelten Werthverhältnisses, verwickelte, wie man später\n sehn wird, die klassische politische Oekonomie in unauflösbare Wirren\n und Widersprüche, während sie der Vulgärökonomie eine sichre Operations-\n basis für ihre principiell nur dem Schein huldigende Flachheit bot.","kind":"passage","sequence":133,"address":"16.133"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0134","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"525\", \"facs\": \"#f0544\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":134,"address":"16.134"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0135","text":"Sehn wir nun zunächst, wie Werth und Preise der Arbeitskraft in\n ihrer verwandelten Form als Arbeitslohn sich darstellen.","kind":"passage","sequence":135,"address":"16.135"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0136","text":"Man weiss, dass der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft berechnet ist auf\n eine gewisse Lebensdauer des Arbeiters, welcher eine gewisse Länge des\n Arbeitstags entspricht. Nimm an, der gewohnheitsmässige Arbeitstag\n betrage 12 Stunden und der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft 3 sh., der\n Geldausdruck eines Werths, worin sich 6 Arbeitsstunden darstellen.\n Erhält der Arbeiter 3 sh., so erhält er den Werth seiner während\n 12 Stunden funktionirenden Arbeitskraft. Wird nun dieser Tages-\n werth der Arbeitskraft als Werth der Tagesarbeit aus-\n gedrückt, so ergiebt sich die Formel: Die zwölfstündige Arbeit hat\n einen Werth von 3 sh. Der Werth der Arbeitskraft bestimmt so\n den Werth der Arbeit oder, in Geld ausgedrückt, ihren nothwen-\n digen Preis. Weicht dagegen der Preis der Arbeitskraft von ihrem\n Werth ab, so ebenfalls der Preis der Arbeit von ihrem s. g. Werth.","kind":"passage","sequence":136,"address":"16.136"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0137","text":"Da der Werth der Arbeit nur ein irrationeller Ausdruck für den\n Werth der Arbeitskraft, ergiebt sich von selbst, dass der Werth der\n Arbeit stets kleiner sein muss als ihr Werthprodukt,\n denn der Kapitalist lässt die Arbeitskraft stets länger funktioniren als zur\n Reproduktion ihres eignen Werths nöthig ist. Im obigen Beispiel ist der\n Werth der während 12 Stunden funktionirenden Arbeitskraft 3 sh., ein\n Werth, zu dessen Reproduktion sie 6 Stunden braucht. Ihr Werthprodukt\n ist dagegen 6 sh., weil sie in der That während 12 Stunden funktionirt,\n und ihr Werthprodukt nicht von ihrem eignen Werthe, sondern von der\n Zeitdauer ihrer Funktion abhängt. Man erhält so das auf den ersten\n Blick abgeschmackte Resultat, dass Arbeit, die einen Werth von 6 sh.\n schafft, einen Werth von 3 sh. besitzt [Note: Vgl. „Zur Kritik der politischen Oekonomie“, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":137,"address":"16.137"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0138","text":"40, wo ich\n ankündige, dass bei Betrachtung des Kapitals das Problem gelöst werden soll:\n „Wie führt Produktion auf Basis des durch blosse Arbeitszeit bestimmten Tausch-\n werths zum Resultat, dass der Tauschwerth der Arbeit kleiner ist als der Tausch-\n werth ihres Produkts?“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":138,"address":"16.138"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0139","text":"Man sieht ferner, dass der Werth von 3 sh., worin sich der\nbezahlte Theil des Arbeitstags, d. h. sechsstündige\n Arbeit darstellt, als Werth oder Preis des Gesammtarbeits-\n tags von 12 Stunden, darunter 6 unbezahlte Stunden, er-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"526\", \"facs\": \"#f0545\"} --> scheint. Die Form des Arbeitslohnes löscht also jede Spur\n der Theilung des Arbeitstags in nothwendige Arbeit\n und Mehrarbeit, in bezahlte und unbezahlte Arbeit völ-\n lig aus. Alle Arbeit erscheint als bezahlte Arbeit. Bei der Frohn-\n arbeit unterscheiden sich räumlich und zeitlich, handgreiflich sinnlich,\n die Arbeit des Fröhners für sich selbst und die Zwangsarbeit für seinen\n Grundherrn. Bei der Sklavenarbeit erscheint selbst der Theil des\n Arbeitstags, worin der Sklave nur den Werth seiner eignen Lebensmittel\n ersetzt, den er in der That also für sich selbst arbeitet, als Arbeit für\n seinen Meister.","kind":"passage","sequence":139,"address":"16.139"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0140","text":"Alle seine Arbeit erscheint als unbezahlte Arbeit [Note: Der Morning Star, ein bis zur silliness naives Londoner Freihandels-\n organ, betheuerte während des amerikanischen Bürgerkriegs wieder und wieder\n mit aller menschenmöglichen moralischen Indignation, dass die Neger in den\n „Confederate States“ ganz umsonst arbeiteten. Es hätte gefälligst die Tages-\n kost eines solchen Negers mit der des freien Arbeiters im East End von London\n z. B. vergleichen sollen.] . Bei\n der Lohnarbeit erscheint umgekehrt selbst die Mehrarbeit oder unbe-\n zahlte Arbeit als bezahlt. Dort verbirgt das Eigenthumsverhältniss das\nFürsichselbst arbeiten des Sklaven, hier das Geldverhältniss\n das Umsonstarbeiten des Lohnarbeiters.","kind":"passage","sequence":140,"address":"16.140"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0141","text":"Man begreift also, von welcher entscheidenden Wichtigkeit die Form-\n verwandlung von Werth und Preis der Arbeitskraft in Arbeitslohn\n oder in Werth und Preis der Arbeit selbst. Auf dieser Erscheinungs-\n form, die das wirkliche Verhältniss unsichtbar macht und grade sein\n Gegentheil zeigt, beruhn alle Rechtsvorstellungen des Arbeiters wie des\n Kapitalisten, alle Mystifikationen der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise,\n alle ihre Freiheitsillusionen, alle apologetischen Flausen der Vulgär-\n ökonomie.","kind":"passage","sequence":141,"address":"16.141"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0142","text":"Braucht die Weltgeschichte viele Zeit, um hinter das Geheimniss\n des Arbeitslohns zu kommen, so ist dagegen nichts leichter zu ver-\n stehn als die Nothwendigkeit, die raisons d’être dieser Erscheinungs-\n form.","kind":"passage","sequence":142,"address":"16.142"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0143","text":"Der Austausch zwischen Kapital und Arbeit stellt sich der Wahrneh-\n mung zunächst ganz in derselben Art dar wie der Kauf und Verkauf aller\n andern Waaren. Der Käufer giebt eine gewisse Geldsumme, der Verkäu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"527\", \"facs\": \"#f0546\"} --> fer einen von Geld verschiedenen Artikel. Das Rechtsbewusstsein erkennt\n hier höchstens einen stofflichen Unterschied, der sich in den rechtlich äqui-\n valenten Formeln: Do ut des, do ut facias, facio ut des und facio ut facias,\n ausdrückt.","kind":"passage","sequence":143,"address":"16.143"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0144","text":"Aller Kauf und Verkauf von Waaren ist ferner von der Illusion be-\n gleitet, dass das, was gezahlt wird, der Gebrauchswerth der Waare\n ist, obgleich diese Illusion schon über die einfache Thatsache stolpert, dass\n die verschiedensten Artikel denselben Preis und derselbe Artikel,\n ohne dass sich sein Gebrauchswerth oder das Bedürfniss dafür ändert,\nwechselnde Preise hat. Da aber Tauschwerth und Gebrauchswerth\nan und für sich inkommensurable Grössen sind, so existirt\n von diesem Standpunkt keine grössere Irrationalität in dem Ausdruck\n „Werth der Arbeit“, „Preis der Arbeit“ als in dem Ausdruck „Werth der\n Baumwolle“, „Preis der Baumwolle“. Das Missverständniss ist bei Kauf\n und Verkauf der Arbeit noch unvermeidlicher als bei andern Waaren.\nErstens, weil das Geld im Kauf der Arbeit als Zahlungsmittel\n funktionirt. Der Arbeiter wird gezahlt, nachdem er seine Arbeit geliefert\n hat.","kind":"passage","sequence":144,"address":"16.144"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0145","text":"Begrifflich aber enthält die Funktion des Geldes als Zahlungsmittel,\n dass es den Werth oder Preis des gelieferten Artikels nachträglich\nrealisirt, also im gegebnen Fall den Werth oder Preis der gelieferten\n Arbeit. Zweitens: Der Gebrauchswerth, den der Arbeiter dem\n Kapitalisten liefert, ist in der That nicht seine Arbeitskraft, sondern ihre\n besondre Funktion, Arbeit von besondrem Inhalt, Schneiderarbeit, Schuster-\n arbeit, Spinnarbeit u. s. w. Dass dieselbe Arbeit nach einer andern\n Seite hin allgemeines werthbildendes Element ist, eine Eigen-\n schaft, wodurch sie sich von allen andern Waaren unterscheidet, fällt\n ausserhalb des Bereichs des gewöhnlichen Bewusstseins.","kind":"passage","sequence":145,"address":"16.145"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0146","text":"Stellen wir uns auf den Standpunkt des Arbeiters, der für zwölfstün-\n dige Arbeit z. B. das Werthprodukt sechsstündiger Arbeit erhält, sage\n 3 sh., so ist für ihn in der That seine zwölfstündige Arbeit das\n Kaufmittel der 3 sh. Der Werth seiner Arbeitskraft mag variiren\n mit dem Werth seiner gewohnheitsmässigen Lebensmittel von 3 auf 4 sh.\n oder von 3 auf 2 sh., oder bei gleichbleibendem Werth seiner Arbeitskraft\n mag ihr Preis, in Folge wechselnden Verhältnisses von Nachfrage und\n Zufuhr, auf 4 sh. steigen oder auf 2 sh. fallen, er giebt stets 12 Ar-\n beitsstunden. Jeder Wechsel in der Grösse des Aequivalents, das er\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"528\", \"facs\": \"#f0547\"} --> erhält, erscheint ihm daher nothwendig als Wechsel im Werth oder Preis\n dieser 12 Arbeitsstunden. Dieser Umstand verleitete umgekehrt Adam\n Smith, der den Arbeitstag als eine constante Grösse behandelt [Note: A.","kind":"passage","sequence":146,"address":"16.146"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0147","text":"Smith spielt nur zufällig auf die Variation des Arbeitstags an bei seiner\n Behandlung des Stücklohns.] ,\n zur Behauptung, der Werth der Arbeit sei constant, obgleich\n der Werth der Lebensmittel wechsle und derselbe Arbeitstag sich daher in\n mehr oder weniger Geld für den Arbeiter darstelle.","kind":"passage","sequence":147,"address":"16.147"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0148","text":"Nehmen wir andrerseits den Kapitalisten, so will er zwar möglichst\n viel Arbeit für möglichst wenig Geld erhalten. Praktisch interessirt ihn\n daher nur die Differenz zwischen dem Preis der Arbeitskraft und dem\n Werth, den ihre Funktion schafft. Aber er sucht alle Waare möglichst\n wohlfeil zu kaufen und erklärt sich überall seinen Profit aus der einfachen\n Prellerei, dem Verkauf über dem Werth. Er kommt daher nicht zur\n Einsicht, dass wenn so ein Ding wie Werth der Arbeit wirklich\n existirte, und er diesen Werth wirklich zahlte, kein Kapital existiren, sein\n Geld sich nicht in Kapital verwandeln würde.","kind":"passage","sequence":148,"address":"16.148"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0149","text":"Es kommt hinzu, dass die wirkliche Bewegung des Ar-\n beitslohns Phänomene zeigt, die zu beweisen scheinen, dass nicht der\nWerth der Arbeitskraft, sondern der Werth ihrer Funktion, der\n Arbeitselbst, bezahlt wird. Diese Phänomene können wir auf zwei\n grosse Klassen zurückführen. Erstens: Wechsel des Arbeitslohns mit\n wechselnder Länge des Arbeitstags. Man könnte eben so wohl schliessen,\n dass nicht der Werth der Maschine, sondern der ihrer Operation bezahlt wird,\n weil es mehr kostet eine Maschine für eine Woche als für einen Tag zu\n dingen. Zweitens: Der individuelle Unterschied in den Arbeitslöhnen\n verschiedner Arbeiter, welche dieselbe Funktion verrichten. Diesen indi-\n viduellen Unterschied findet man, aber ohne Anlass zu Illusionen, wieder\n im System der Sklaverei, wo frank und frei, ohne Schnörkel, die Ar-\n beitskraft selbst verkauft wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":149,"address":"16.149"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0150","text":"Nur fällt der Vortheil einer Ar-\n beitskraft, die über dem Durchschnitt, oder der Nachtheil einer Arbeits-\n kraft, die unter dem Durchschnitt steht, im Sklavensystem dem Sklaven-\n eigner zu, im System der Lohnarbeit dem Arbeiter selbst, weil seine Ar-\n beitskraft in dem einen Fall von ihm selbst, in dem andern von einer dritten\n Person verkauft wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":150,"address":"16.150"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0151","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"529\", \"facs\": \"#f0548\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":151,"address":"16.151"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0152","text":"Uebrigens gilt von der Erscheinungsform, „Werth und Preis\n der Arbeit“ oder „Arbeitslohn“, im Unterschied zum wesentlichen\n Verhältniss, welches erscheint, dem Werth und Preis der Arbeitskraft,\n dasselbe, was von allen Erscheinungsformen und ihrem verborgnen\n Hintergrund. Die ersteren reproduciren sich unmittelbar, spontan, als gang\n und gäbe Denkformen, der andere muss durch die Wissenschaft erst\nentdeckt werden. Die klassische politische Oekonomie stösst annähernd\n auf den wahren Sachverhalt, ohne ihn jemals bewusst zu formuliren.\n Sie kann das nicht, so lange sie in ihrer bürgerlichen Haut steckt.","kind":"passage","sequence":152,"address":"16.152"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0153","text":"## b) Die beiden Grundformen des Arbeitslohns: Zeit-\n lohn und Stücklohn.","kind":"passage","sequence":153,"address":"16.153"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0154","text":"Der Arbeitslohn nimmt selbst wieder sehr mannigfaltige\n Formen an, ein Umstand, nicht erkennbar aus den ökonomischen Kom-\n pendien, die in ihrer brutalen Interessirtheit für den Stoff jeden Formunter-\n schied vernachlässigen. Eine Darstellung aller dieser Formen gehört je-\n doch in die specielle Lehre von der Lohnarbeit, also nicht in diess Werk. Da-\n gegen sind die zwei herrschenden Grundformen hier kurz zu entwickeln.","kind":"passage","sequence":154,"address":"16.154"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0155","text":"Der Verkauf der Arbeitskraft findet, wie man sich erinnert, stets\nfür bestimmte Zeitperiode statt. Die verwandelte Form, worin\n der Tageswerth, Wochenwerth u. s. w. der Arbeitskraft unmittelbar er-\n scheint, ist daher die des Zeitlohns, also Tageslohn, Wochenlohn u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":155,"address":"16.155"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0156","text":"Es ist nun zunächst zu bemerken, dass die im 2. Abschnitt dieses\n Kapitels dargestellten Gesetze über den Grössenwechsel von Preis der\n Arbeitskraft und Mehrwerth sich durch einfache Formveränderung in Ge-\n setze des Arbeitslohns verwandeln. Ebenso erscheint der Unterschied\n zwischen dem Tauschwerth der Arbeitskraft und der Masse der\n Lebensmittel, worin sich dieser Werth umsetzt, jetzt als Unterschied von\nnominellem und reellem Arbeitslohn. Es wäre nutzlos in der Er-\n scheinungsform zu wiederholen, was in der wesentlichen Form bereits ent-\n wickelt. Wir beschränken uns daher auf wenige, den Zeitlohn charak-\n terisirende Punkte.","kind":"passage","sequence":156,"address":"16.156"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0157","text":"Die Geldsumme [Note: Der Geldwerth selbst wird hier immer als constant vorausgesetzt.] , die der Arbeiter für seine Tagesarbeit, Wochenar-\n beit u. s. w. erhält, bildet den wirklichen Betrag seines nominellen oder\ndem Werth nach geschätzten Arbeitslohns. Es ist aber klar, dass\n [Running matter: I. 34] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"530\", \"facs\": \"#f0549\"} --> je nach der Länge des Arbeitstags, also je nach der täglich von ihm gelie-\n ferten Quantität Arbeit, derselbe Tageslohn, Wochenlohn u. s. w.\n einen sehr verschiednen Preis der Arbeit d. h. sehr verschiedne Geld-\n summen für dasselbe Quantum Arbeit darstellen kann [Note: „The price of labour is the sum paid for a given quantity of\n labour.“ (Sir Edward West: „Price of Corn and Wages of Labour.\n Lond. 1826“, p. 67.)","kind":"passage","sequence":157,"address":"16.157"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0158","text":"Dieser West ist der Verfasser der in der Geschichte der\n politischen Oekonomie epochemachenden anonymen Schrift: „Essay on the\n Application of Capital to Land. By a Fellow of Univ. College of\n Oxford. Lond. 1815“.] . Man\n muss also bei dem Zeitlohn wieder unterscheiden zwischen dem Gesammt-\n betrag des Arbeitslohns, Taglohns, Wochenlohns u. s. w. und\n dem Preis der Arbeit. Wie nun den Preis der Arbeit finden, d. h.\n den Geldwerth eines gegebnen Quantums Arbeit? Der\n durchschnittliche Preis der Arbeit ergiebt sich, indem man den durch-\n schnittlichen Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft durch die\n Stundenzahl des durchschnittlichen Arbeitstags divi-\n dirt. Ist z. B. der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft 3 sh., das Werthprodukt\n von 6 Arbeitsstunden, und ist der Arbeitstag zwölfstündig, so ist der Preis\n einer Arbeitsstunde = \\frac{3 sh.}{12} = 3 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":158,"address":"16.158"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0159","text":"Der so gefundne Preis der Ar-\n beitsstunde dient als Einheitsmass für den Preis der\n Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":159,"address":"16.159"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0160","text":"Es folgt daher, dass der Taglohn, Wochenlohn u. s. w. derselbe\n bleiben kann, obgleich der Preis der Arbeit fortwährend sinkt.\n War z. B. der gewohnheitsmässige Arbeitstag 10 Stunden und der Tages-\n werth der Arbeitskraft 3 sh., so betrug der Preis der Arbeitsstunde 3⅗d.;\n er sinkt auf 3 d., sobald der Arbeitstag zu 12 Stunden, und auf 2⅖ d.,\n sobald er zu 15 Stunden steigt. Tages- oder Wochenlohn bleiben trotz-\n dem unverändert. Umgekehrt kann der Taglohn oder Wochenlohn stei-\n gen, obgleich der Preis der Arbeit constant bleibt oder selbst sinkt.\n War z. B. der Arbeitstag zehnstündig und ist der Tageswerth der Arbeits-\n kraft 3 sh., so der Preis einer Arbeitsstunde 3⅗ d. Arbeitet der Arbeiter\n in Folge zunehmender Beschäftigung und bei gleichbleibendem Preise der\n Arbeit 12 Stunden, so steigt sein Tageslohn nun auf 3 sh. 7⅕d. ohne Varia-\n tion im Preise der Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":160,"address":"16.160"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0161","text":"Dasselbe Resultat könnte herauskommen, wenn\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"531\", \"facs\": \"#f0550\"} --> statt der extensiven Grösse der Arbeit ihre intensive Grösse zunähme [Note: „The wages of labour depend upon the price of labour and the quantity\n of labour performed … An increase in the wages of labour does not necessarily\n imply an enhancement of the price of labour. From fuller employment, and greater\n exertions, the wages of labour may be considerably increased, while the price of\n labour may continue the same.“ (West l. c. p. 67, 68 u. 112.) Die Haupt-\n frage: wie wird der „price of labour“ bestimmt? fertigt West übrigens mit banalen\n Redensarten ab.] .\n Steigen des nominellen Tages- oder Wochenlohns mag daher begleitet sein\n von gleichbleibendem oder sinkendem Preis der Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":161,"address":"16.161"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0162","text":"Dasselbe gilt von\n der Einnahme der Arbeiterfamilie, sobald das vom Familienhaupt gelie-\n ferte Arbeitsquantum durch die Arbeit der Familienglieder vermehrt wird.\n Es giebt also von der Schmälerung des nominellen Tages- oder Wochen-\n lohns unabhängige Methoden zur Herabsetzung des Preises der Ar-\n beit [Note: Diess fühlt der fanatischste Vertreter der industriellen Bourgeoisie des\n 18.","kind":"passage","sequence":162,"address":"16.162"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0163","text":"Jahrhunderts, der oft von uns citirte Verfasser des „Essay on Trade and\n Commerce“ richtig heraus, obgleich er die Sache konfus darstellt: „It is the\n quantity of labour and not the price of it (versteht darunter den nominellen Tages-\n oder Wochenlohn), that is determined by the price of provisions and\n other necessaries: reduce the price of necessaries very low, and of course\n you reduce the quantity of labour in proportion … Master-manufacturers know,\n that there are various ways of rising and felling the price of labour, besides\n that of altering its nominal amount.“ (l. c. p. 48 u. 61.) In seinen „Three\n Lectures on the Rate of Wages. Lond. 1830“, worin N. W. Senior\n West’s Schrift benutzt, ohne sie anzuführen, sagt er u. a.: „The labourer is\n principally interested in the amount of wages“ (p. 14).","kind":"passage","sequence":163,"address":"16.163"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0164","text":"Also der Arbeiter\n ist hauptsächlich interessirt in dem, was er erhält, dem nominellen Betrag des\n Lohns, nicht in dem, was er giebt, der Quantität der Arbeit!] . Als allgemeines Gesetz aber folgt: Ist die Quantität der\n Tages-, Wochenarbeit u. s. w. gegeben, so hängt der Tages-\n oder Wochenlohn vom Preise der Arbeit ab, der selbst variirt,\n entweder mit dem Werth der Arbeitskraft oder den Abweichungen ihres\n Preises von ihrem Werthe. Ist dagegen der Preis der Arbeit gege-\n ben, so hängt der Tages- oder Wochenlohn von der Quan-\n tität der Tages- oder Wochenarbeit ab.","kind":"passage","sequence":164,"address":"16.164"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0165","text":"Die Masseinheit des Zeitlohns, der Preis der Arbeitsstunde, ist der\n Quotient des Tageswerths der Arbeitskraft, dividirt durch die Stundenzahl\n des gewohnheitsmässigen Arbeitstags. Gesetzt letztrer betrage 12 Stunden,\n [Running matter: 34*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"532\", \"facs\": \"#f0551\"} --> der Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft 3 sh., das Werthprodukt von 6 Arbeits-\n stunden. Der Preis der Arbeitsstunde ist unter diesen Umständen 3 d.,\n ihr Werthprodukt 6 d. Wird der Arbeiter nun weniger als 12 Stunden\n täglich (oder weniger als 6 Tage in der Woche) beschäftigt, z. B. nur 6\n oder 8 Stunden, so erhält er, bei diesem Preise der Arbeit, nur 2 oder\n 1½ sh. Taglohn [Note: Die Wirkung solcher anormalen Unterbeschäftigung ist durchaus ver-\n schieden von der einer allgemeinen zwangsgesetzlichen Reduktion des\n Arbeitstags.","kind":"passage","sequence":165,"address":"16.165"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0166","text":"Erstere hat mit der absoluten Länge des Arbeitstags nichts zu\n schaffen und kann ebensowohl bei 15stündigem als bei 6stündigem Arbeitstag ein-\n treten. Der normale Preis der Arbeit ist im ersten Fall darauf berechnet, dass\n der Arbeiter 15 Stunden, im zweiten darauf, dass er 6 Stunden per Tag durch-\n schnittlich arbeitet. Die Wirkung bleibt daher dieselbe, wenn er in dem einen Fall\n nur 7½, in dem andern nur 3 Stunden beschäftigt wird.] . Da er nach der Voraussetzung im Durchschnitt 6\nStunden täglich arbeiten muss, um nur einen dem Werth seiner Ar-\n beitskraft entsprechenden Taglohn zu produciren, da er nach derselben\n Voraussetzung von jeder Stunde nur ½ für sich selbst, ½ aber für den\n Kapitalisten arbeitet, so ist es klar, dass er das Werthprodukt von 6\nStunden nicht herausschlagen kann, wenn er weniger als 12 Stun-\n den beschäftigt wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":166,"address":"16.166"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0167","text":"Sah man früher die zerstörenden Folgen der\n Ueberarbeit, so entdeckt man hier die Quellen der Leiden, die für den Ar-\n beiter aus seiner Unterbeschäftigung entspringen.","kind":"passage","sequence":167,"address":"16.167"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0168","text":"Wird der Stundenlohn in der Weise fixirt, dass der Kapitalist\n sich nicht zur Zahlung eines Tages- oder Wochenlohns verpflichtet, son-\n dern nur zur Zahlung der Arbeitsstunden, während deren es ihm beliebt,\n den Arbeiter zu beschäftigen, so kann er ihn unter der Zeit beschäf-\n tigen, die der Schätzung des Stundenlohns oder der Masseinheit für den\n Preis der Arbeit ursprünglich zu Grunde liegt. Da diese Masseinheit be-\n stimmt ist durch die Proportion ,\n verliert sie natürlich allen Sinn, sobald der Arbeitstag aufhört, eine be-\n stimmte Stundenzahl zu zählen. Der Zusammenhang zwischen der bezahl-\n ten und unbezahlten Arbeitszeit ist aufgehoben. Der Kapitalist kann jetzt\n ein bestimmtes Quantum Mehrarbeit aus dem Arbeiter herausschlagen, ohne\n ihm die zu seiner Selbsterhaltung nothwendige Arbeitszeit einzuräumen.","kind":"passage","sequence":168,"address":"16.168"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0169","text":"Er kann jede Regelmässigkeit der Beschäftigung vernichten und ganz nach\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"533\", \"facs\": \"#f0552\"} --> Bequemlichkeit, Willkühr und augenblicklichem Interesse die ungeheuerste\n Ueberarbeit mit relativer oder gänzlicher Arbeitslosigkeit abwechseln lassen.\n Er kann, unter dem Vorwand, den „normalen Preis der Arbeit“ zu zahlen,\n den Arbeitstag, ohne irgend entsprechende Kompensation für den Arbeiter,\n anormal verlängern. Daher der durchaus rationelle Aufstand (1860) der\n im Baufach beschäftigten Londoner Arbeiter gegen den Versuch der Kapi-\n talisten diesen Stundenlohn aufzuherrschen. Die gesetzliche Be-\n schränkung des Arbeitstags macht solchem Unfug ein Ende, ob-\n gleich natürlich nicht der aus Konkurrenz der Maschinerie, Wechsel in der\n Qualität der angewandten Arbeiter, partiellen und allgemeinen Krisen ent-\n springenden Unterbeschäftigung.","kind":"passage","sequence":169,"address":"16.169"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0170","text":"Bei wachsendem Tages- oder Wochenlohn kann der Preis der Ar-\n beit nominell constant bleiben und dennoch unter sein normales Niveau\n sinken. Diess findet jedesmal statt, sobald mit constantem Preis der Ar-\n beit, resp. der Arbeitsstunde, der Arbeitstag über seine gewohnheitsmässige\n Dauer verlängert wird. Wenn in dem Bruch \n der Nenner wächst, wächst der Zähler noch rascher. Der Werth der Ar-\n beitskraft, weil ihr Verschleiss, wächst mit der Dauer ihrer Funktion, und\n in rascherer Proportion als das Increment ihrer Funktionsdauer. In vie-\n len Industriezweigen, wo Zeitlohn vorherrscht, ohne gesetzliche\n Schranken der Arbeitszeit, hat sich daher naturwüchsig die Gewohnheit\n herausgebildet, dass der Arbeitstag nur bis zu einem gewissen Punkt, z. B.\n bis zum Ablauf der zehnten Stunde, als normal gilt („normal working day“,\n „the day’s work“, „the regular hours of work“).","kind":"passage","sequence":170,"address":"16.170"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0171","text":"Jenseits dieser Grenze bildet\n die Arbeitszeit Ueberzeit (overtime) und wird, die Stunde als Masseinheit\n genommen, besser bezahlt (extra pay), obgleich oft in lächerlich kleiner\n Proportion [Note: „Die Rate der Zahlung für Ueberzeit (in der Spitzenmanufaktur) ist so\n klein, ½ d. u. s. w. per Stunde, dass sie in peinlichem Kontrast steht zur massen-\n haften Unbill, die sie der Gesundheit und Lebenskraft der Arbeiter anthut …\n Der so gewonnene kleine Ueberschuss muss ausserdem oft in Extra-Erfrischungs-\n mitteln wieder verausgabt werden.“ („Child. Empl. Comm.“ II. Rep.,\n p. XVI, n. 117.)] . Der normale Arbeitstag existirt hier als Bruchtheil des\n wirklichen Arbeitstags und der letztere währt oft während des ganzen Jahres\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"534\", \"facs\": \"#f0553\"} --> länger als der erstere [Note: Z. B. in der Tapetendruckerei vor der neulichen Einführung des Fabrik-\n akts.","kind":"passage","sequence":171,"address":"16.171"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0172","text":"„Wir arbeiten ohne Pause für Mahlzeiten, so dass das Tageswerk von\n 10½ Stunden um halb 5 Uhr Nachmittags beendet ist, und alles spätere ist\nUeberzeit, die selten vor 6 Uhr Abends aufhört, so dass wir in der That das\n ganze Jahr durch Ueberzeit arbeiten.“ (Mr. Smith’s Evidence in\n „Child. Empl. Comm.“ I. Rep., p. 125.)] . Das Wachsthum im Preis der Arbeit mit der\n Verlängerung des Arbeitstags über eine gewisse Normalgrenze gestaltet\n sich in verschiedenen britischen Industriezweigen so, dass der niedrige\n Preis der Arbeit während der s. g. Normalzeit dem Arbeiter die besser\n bezahlte Ueberzeit aufzwingt, will er überhaupt einen genügenden Arbeits-\n lohn herausschlagen [Note: Z. B. in den schottischen Bleichereien. „In einigen Theilen Schottlands\n wurde diese Industrie (vor Einführung des Fabrikakts 1862) nach dem System der\n Ueberzeit betrieben, d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":172,"address":"16.172"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0173","text":"10 Stunden galten als normaler Arbeitstag. Dafür\n erhielt der Mann 1. sh. 2 d. Hierzu kam aber täglich eine Ueberzeit von 3 oder 4\n Stunden, wofür 3 d. per Stunde gezahlt wurde. Folge dieses Systems: Ein Mann,\n der nur die Normalzeit arbeitete, konnte nur 8 sh. Wochenlohn verdienen. Ohne\n Ueberzeit reichte der Lohn nicht aus.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact.\n 30th April 1863“, p. 10.) Die „Extrazahlung für Ueberzeit ist eine Ver-\n suchung, der die Arbeiter nicht widerstehen können.“ („Rep. of Insp. of\n Fact. 30th April 1848“, p. 5.) Die Buchbinderei in der City von London\n verwendet sehr viele junge Mädchen vom 14.—15. Jahr an, und zwar unter\n dem Lehrlingskontrakt, der bestimmte Arbeitsstunden vorschreibt. Nichtsdesto-\n weniger arbeiten sie in der Schlusswoche jeden Monats bis 10, 11, 12 und 1 Uhr\n Nachts, zusammen mit den älteren Arbeitern, in sehr gemischter Gesellschaft.","kind":"passage","sequence":173,"address":"16.173"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0174","text":"„Die Meister versuchen (tempt) sie durch Extralohn und Geld für ein gutes\n Nachtessen“, das sie in benachbarten Kneipen zu sich nehmen. Die grosse Lieder-\n lichkeit, so unter diesen „young immortals“ producirt („Child. Empl.\n Comm. V. Rep.“, p. 44, n. 191), findet ihre Kompensation darin, dass von\n ihnen unter anderm auch viele Bibeln und Erbauungsbücher gebunden werden.] . Gesetzliche Beschränkung des Arbeitstags macht\n diesem Vergnügen ein Ende [Note: Sieh „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 30th April 1863“, l. c. Mit\n ganz richtiger Kritik des Sachverhältnisses erklärten die im Baufach beschäftigten\n Londoner Arbeiter während des grossen strike und lock out von 1860 den Stun-\n denlohn nur annehmen zu wollen unter zwei Bedingungen: 1) dass mit dem\n Preis der Arbeitsstunde ein Normalarbeitstag von resp.","kind":"passage","sequence":174,"address":"16.174"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0175","text":"9 und 10 Stunden fest-\n gesetzt werde und der Preis für die Stunde des zehntägigen Arbeitstags grösser\n sei als für die des neunstündigen; 2) dass jede Stunde über den Normaltag hinaus\n als Ueberzeit verhältnissmässig höher bezahlt werde.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":175,"address":"16.175"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0176","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"535\", \"facs\": \"#f0554\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":176,"address":"16.176"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0177","text":"Es ist allgemein bekannte Thatsache, dass je länger der Arbeitstag\n in einem Industriezweig, um so niedriger der Arbeitslohn [Note: „It is a very notable thing, too, that where long hours are the rule, small\n wages are also so.“ („Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 31st Oct. 1863“, p. 9.)\n „The work which obtains the scanty pittance of food is for the most part ex-\n cessively prolonged.“ („Public Health, Sixth Rep. 1864“, p. 15.)] . Fabrikin-\n spektor A. Redgrave illustrirt diess durch eine vergleichende Uebersicht\n der zwanzigjährigen Periode von 1839—1859, wonach der Arbeitslohn\n in den dem Zehnstundengesetz unterworfenen Fabriken stieg, während er in\n den Fabriken, wo 14 bis 15 Stunden täglich gearbeitet wird, fiel [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. 30th April 1860“, p. 31, 32.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":177,"address":"16.177"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0178","text":"Zunächst folgt aus dem Gesetz: „bei gegebnem Preis der Ar-\n beit hängt der Tages- oder Wochenlohn von der Quantität der gelie-\n ferten Arbeit ab“, dass je niedriger der Preis der Arbeit, desto grösser\n das Arbeitsquantum sein muss oder desto länger der Arbeitstag, damit der\n Arbeiter auch nur einen kümmerlichen Durchschnittslohn sichre. Die\n Niedrigkeit des Arbeitspreises wirkt hier als Sporn zur Verlängerung der\n Arbeitszeit [Note: Die Hand-Nägelmacher in England haben z. B. wegen des niedrigen\n Arbeitspreises 15 Stunden täglich zu arbeiten, um den kümmerlichsten Wochen-\n lohn herauszuschlagen. „Es sind viele, viele Stunden des Tags, und während aller\n der Zeit muss er hart schanzen um 11 d. oder 1 sh. herauszuschlagen, und davon\n gehn 2½ bis 3 d. ab für Verschleiss der Werkzeuge, Feuerung, Eisenabfall.“\n („Child. Empl. Comm. III. Rep.“, p. 136, n. 671.)","kind":"passage","sequence":178,"address":"16.178"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0179","text":"Die Weiber verdienen\n bei derselben Arbeitszeit nur einen Wochenlohn von 5 sh. (l. c. p. 137, n. 674.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":179,"address":"16.179"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0180","text":"Umgekehrt aber producirt ihrerseits die Verlängerung der Arbeitszeit\n einen Fall im Arbeitspreise und damit im Tages- oder Wochenlohn.","kind":"passage","sequence":180,"address":"16.180"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0181","text":"Die Bestimmung des Arbeitspreises durch\n ergiebt, dass blosse Verlängerung des Arbeitstags den Arbeitspreis senkt,\n wenn keine Kompensation eintritt. Aber dieselben Umstände, welche den\n Kapitalisten befähigen, den Arbeitstag auf die Dauer zu verlängern, be-\n fähigen ihn erst und zwingen ihn schliesslich, den Arbeitspreis auch nomi-\n nell zu senken, bis der Gesammtpreis der vermehrten Stundenzahl sinkt,\n also der Tages- oder Wochenlohn. Hinweis auf zwei Umstände genügt\n hier. Verrichtet ein Mann das Werk von 1½ oder 2 Männern, so wächst\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"536\", \"facs\": \"#f0555\"} --> die Zufuhr der Arbeit, wenn auch die Zufuhr der auf dem Markt befind-\n lichen Arbeitskräfte constant bleibt.","kind":"passage","sequence":181,"address":"16.181"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0182","text":"Die so unter den Arbeitern erzeugte\n Konkurrenz befähigt den Kapitalisten den Preis der Arbeit herabzudrücken,\n während der fallende Preis der Arbeit ihn umgekehrt befähigt, die Arbeits-\n zeit noch weiter heraufzuschrauben [Note: Wenn ein Fabrikarbeiter z. B. verweigerte, die hergebrachte lange Stun-\n denzahl zu arbeiten, „he would very shortly be replaced by somebody who would\n work any length of time and thus be thrown out of employment.“ („Reports\n of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1848.“ Evidence p. 39, n. 58.) „If one man\n performs the work of two … the rate of profits will generally be raised … in\n consequence of the additional supply of labour having diminished its price.“\n (Senior l. c. p. 14.)] . Bald jedoch wird diese Verfügung\n über anormale, d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":182,"address":"16.182"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0183","text":"das gesellschaftliche Durchschnittsniveau über-\n fliessende Quanta unbezahlter Arbeit zum Konkurrenzmit-\n tel unter den Kapitalisten selbst. Ein Theil des Waaren-\n preises besteht aus dem Preis der Arbeit. Der nicht gezahlte\n Theil des Arbeitspreises braucht nicht im Waarenpreis zu rechnen. Er\nkann dem Waarenkäufer geschenkt werden. Diess ist der erste Schritt,\n wozu die Konkurrenz treibt. Der zweite Schritt, wozu sie zwingt, ist we-\n nigstens einen Theil des durch die Verlängerung des Arbeitstags erzeug-\n ten anormalen Mehrwerths ebenfalls aus dem Verkaufspreis\n der Waare zu eliminiren. In dieser Weise bildet sich erst sporadisch\n und fixirt sich nach und nach ein anormal niedriger Verkaufspreis der\n Waare, der von nun an zur constanten Grundlage kümmerlichen Arbeits-\n lohns bei übermässiger Arbeitszeit wird, wie er ursprünglich das Produkt\n dieser Umstände war.","kind":"passage","sequence":183,"address":"16.183"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0184","text":"Wir deuten diese Bewegung bloss an, da die Ana-\n lyse der Konkurrenz nicht hierhin gehört. Doch mag für einen Augen-\n blick der Kapitalist selbst sprechen. „In Birmingham ist so grosse\n Konkurrenz unter den Meistern, dass mancher von uns gezwungen ist als\n Arbeitsanwender zu thun, was er sich schämen würde sonst zu thun; und\n dennoch wird nicht mehr Geld gemacht (and yet no more money is made),\n sondern das Publikum allein hat den Vortheil davon“ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm.“ III. Rep. Evidence p. 66, n. 22.] . Man erinnert\n sich der zwei Sorten Londoner Bäcker, wovon die eine Brod zum\n vollen Preise (the „fullpriced“ bakers), die andre es unter seinem normalen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"537\", \"facs\": \"#f0556\"} --> Preise verkauft („the underpriced“, „the undersellers“).","kind":"passage","sequence":184,"address":"16.184"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0185","text":"Die „fullpri-\n ced“ denunciren ihre Konkurrenten vor der parlamentarischen Unter-\n suchungskommission: „Sie existiren nur, indem sie erstens das Publikum\n betrügen (durch Fälschung der Waare) und zweitens 18 Arbeitsstunden\n aus ihren Leuten für den Lohn zwölfstündiger Arbeit herausschinden. …\nDie unbezahlte Arbeit (the unpaid labour) der Arbeiter ist\n das Mittel, wodurch der Konkurrenzkampf geführt wird. … Die Kon-\n kurrenz unter den Bäckermeistern ist die Ursache der Schwierigkeit in\n Beseitigung der Nachtarbeit. Ein Unterverkäufer, der sein Brod unter\n dem mit dem Mehlpreis wechselnden Kostpreis verkauft, hält sich schadlos,\n indem er mehr Arbeit aus seinen Leuten herausschlägt. Wenn ich nur\n 12 Stunden Arbeit aus meinen Leuten herausschlage, mein Nachbar da-\n gegen 18 oder 20, muss er mich im Verkaufspreis schlagen.","kind":"passage","sequence":185,"address":"16.185"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0186","text":"Könnten\n die Arbeiter auf Zahlung für Ueberzeit bestehn, so wäre es mit diesem\n Manöver bald zu Ende. … Eine grosse Anzahl der von den Unterver-\n käufern Beschäftigten sind Fremde, Jungen und Andre, die fast mit jedem\n Arbeitslohn, den sie kriegen können, vorlieb zu nehmen gezwungen\n sind“ [Note: „Report etc. relative to the Grievances complained of by\n the journeymen bakers. Lond. 1862“, p. LII und ib. Evidence,\n p. 479, 359, 27. Indess lassen auch die fullpriced, wie früher erwähnt, und wie\n ihr Wortführer Bennett selbst zugesteht, ihre Leute „Arbeit beginnen um 11 Uhr\n Abends oder früher und verlängern sie oft bis 7 Uhr des folgenden Abends.“\n (l. c. p. 27.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":186,"address":"16.186"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0187","text":"Diese Jeremiade ist auch desswegen interessant, weil sie zeigt, wie\nnur der Schein der Produktionsverhältnisse sich im Kapi-\n talistenhirn widerspiegelt. Der Kapitalist weiss nicht, dass auch der\nnormale Preis der Arbeit ein bestimmtes Quantum unbezahlter\n Arbeit einschliesst und eben diese unbezahlte Arbeit die normale Quelle\n seines Gewinns ist. Die Kategorie der Mehrarbeitszeit existirt überhaupt\n nicht für ihn, denn sie ist eingeschlossen im normalen Arbeitstag, den er\n im Taglohn zu zahlen glaubt. Wohl aber existirt für ihn die Ueber-\n zeit, die Verlängerung des Arbeitstags über die dem gewohnten Preis\n der Arbeit entsprechende Schranke. Seinem unterverkaufenden\n Konkurrenten gegenüber besteht er sogar auf Extrazahlung (extra pay)\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"538\", \"facs\": \"#f0557\"} --> für diese Ueberzeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":187,"address":"16.187"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0188","text":"Er weiss wieder nicht, dass diese Extrazahlung eben-\n sowohl unbezahlte Arbeit einschliesst, wie der Preis der gewöhn-\n lichen Arbeitsstunde. Z. B. der Preis einer Stunde des zwölfstündigen\n Arbeitstags ist 3 d., das Werthprodukt von ½ Arbeitsstunde, während der\n Preis der überzeitigen Arbeitsstunde 4 d., das Werthprodukt von ⅔\n Arbeitsstunde. Im ersten Fall eignet sich der Kapitalist von einer Ar-\n beitsstunde die Hälfte, im andern ⅓ ohne Zahlung an.","kind":"passage","sequence":188,"address":"16.188"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0189","text":"Der Stücklohn ist nichts als eine verwandelte Form des\n Zeitlohns, wie der Zeitlohn die verwandelte Form des Werthes oder\n Preises der Arbeitskraft.","kind":"passage","sequence":189,"address":"16.189"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0190","text":"Beim Stücklohn sieht es auf den ersten Blick aus, als ob der vom\n Arbeiter verkaufte Gebrauchswerth nicht die Funktion seiner Ar-\n beitskraft sei, lebendige Arbeit, sondern bereits im Produkt\n vergegenständlichte Arbeit, und als ob der Preis dieser Arbeit\n nicht wie beim Zeitlohn durch die Bruchzahl\n,\n sondern durch die Leistungsfähigkeit des Producenten be-\n stimmt werde [Note: „The system of piece-work illustrates an epoch in the history of the\n working man; it is half-way between the position of the mere day-labourer, de-\n pending upon the will of the capitalist and the cooperative artizan, who in the not\n distant future promises to combine the artizan and the capitalist in his own person.\n Piece-workers are in fact their own masters, even whilst working upon the capital\n of the employer.“ (John Watts: „Trade Societies and Strikes,\n Machinery and Co-operative Societies.","kind":"passage","sequence":190,"address":"16.190"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0191","text":"Manchester 1865“, p. 52,\n 53.) Ich citire diess Schriftchen, weil es eine wahre Gosse aller längst verfaulten,\n apologetischen Gemeinplätze. Derselbe Herr Watts machte früher in Owenismus\n und publicirte 1842 ein andres Schriftchen: „Facts and Fictions of Poli-\n tical Economy“, worin er u. a. Property für Robbery erklärt. Es ist schon\n lange her.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":191,"address":"16.191"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0192","text":"Zunächst müsste die Zuversicht, die an diesen Schein glaubt, bereits\n stark erschüttert werden durch die Thatsache, dass beide Formen des\n Arbeitslohns zur selben Zeit in denselben Geschäftszweigen neben einander\n bestehen. Z. B. „Die Setzer von London arbeiten in der Regel nach\n Stücklohn, während Zeitlohn bei ihnen die Ausnahme bildet. Umgekehrt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"539\", \"facs\": \"#f0558\"} --> bei den Setzern in den Provinzen, wo der Zeitlohn die Regel und der\n Stücklohn die Ausnahme. Die Schiffszimmerleute im Hafen von London\n werden nach Stücklohn bezahlt, in allen andern englischen Häfen nach\n Zeitlohn“ [Note: T. J. Dunning: „Trade’s Unions and Strikes. Lond.\n 1860“, p. 22.] . In denselben Londoner Sattlerwerkstätten wird oft für die-\n selbe Arbeit den Franzosen Stücklohn und den Engländern Zeitlohn ge-\n zahlt.","kind":"passage","sequence":192,"address":"16.192"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0193","text":"In den eigentlichen Fabriken, wo Stücklohn allgemein vorherrscht,\n entziehn sich einzelne Arbeitsfunktionen aus technischen Gründen dieser\n Messung und werden daher nach Zeitlohn gezahlt [Note: Wie das gleichzeitige Nebeneinander dieser zwei Formen des Arbeitslohns\n Fabrikantenprellereien begünstigt: „A factory employs 400 people, the half of\n which work by the piece, and have a direct interest in working longer hours. The\n other 200 are paid by the day, work equally long with the others, and get no more\n money for their overtime … The work of these 200 people for half an hour a\n day is equal to one person’s work for 50 hours, or ⅚ of one persons labour in a\n week, and is a positive gain to the employer.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact.\n 31st October 1860“, p. 9.)","kind":"passage","sequence":193,"address":"16.193"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0194","text":"„Overworking, to a very considerable extent,\n still prevails; and, in most instances, with that security against detection and\n punishment which the law itself affords. I have in many former reports shown …\n the injury to all the workpeople who are not employed on piece-work, but\n receive weekly wages.“ Leonhard Horner in „Reports of Insp. of\n Fact. 30th April 1859“, p. 8, 9.] . An und für sich ist\n es jedoch klar, dass die Formverschieden heit in der Auszahlung\n des Arbeitslohns an seinem Wesen nichts ändert, obgleich die eine\n Form der Entwicklung der kapitalistischen Produktion günstiger sein mag\n als die andre.","kind":"passage","sequence":194,"address":"16.194"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0195","text":"Der gewöhnliche Arbeitstag betrage 12 Stunden, wovon 6 bezahlt,\n 6 unbezahlt. Sein Werthprodukt sei 6 sh., das einer Arbeitsstunde da-\n her 6 d. Es stelle sich erfahrungsmässig heraus, dass ein Arbeiter, der\n mit dem Durchschnittsgrad von Intensivität und Geschick arbeitet, in der\n That also nur die gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeits-\n zeit zur Produktion eines Artikels verwendet, 24 Stücke, ob diskret,\n oder messbare Theile eines kontinuirlichen Machwerks, in 12 Stunden\n liefert. So ist der Werth dieser 24 Stücke, nach Abzug des in ihnen ent-\n haltenen constanten Kapitaltheils, 6 sh. und der Werth des einzelnen\n Stücks 3 d. Der Arbeiter erhält per Stück 1½ d. und verdient so in\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"540\", \"facs\": \"#f0559\"} --> 12 Stunden 3 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":195,"address":"16.195"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0196","text":"Wie es beim Zeitlohn gleichgültig ist, ob man annimmt,\n dass der Arbeiter 6 Stunden für sich und 6 für den Kapitalisten, oder von\n jeder Stunde die eine Hälfte für sich und die andre für den Kapitalisten\n arbeitet, so auch hier, ob man sagt, jedes einzelne Stück sei halb bezahlt\n und halb unbezahlt, oder der Preis von 12 Stücken ersetze nur den Werth\n der Arbeitskraft, während in den 12 andern sich der Mehrwerth ver-\n körpere.","kind":"passage","sequence":196,"address":"16.196"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0197","text":"Die Form des Stücklohns ist ebenso irrationell als die des Zeit-\n lohns. Während z. B. zwei Stück Waare, nach Abzug des Werths der\n in ihnen aufgezehrten Produktionsmittel, als Produkt einer Arbeitsstunde\n 6 d. werth sind, erhält der Arbeiter für sie einen Preis von 3 d. Der\n Stücklohn drückt unmittelbar in der That kein Werthverhält-\n niss aus. Es handelt sich nicht darum den Werth des Stücks durch die\n in ihm verkörperte Arbeitszeit zu messen, sondern umgekehrt die vom\n Arbeiter verausgabte Arbeit durch die Zahl der von ihm producirten\n Stücke. Beim Zeitlohn misst sich die Arbeit an ihrer unmittelbaren Zeit-\n dauer, beim Stücklohn am Produktenquantum, worin Arbeit während be-\n stimmter Zeitdauer verdichtet [Note: „Le salaire peut se mesurer de deux manières: ou sur la durée du tra-\n vail, ou sur son produit.“ („Abrégé Élémentaire des principes de\n l’Écon. Pol. Paris 1796“, p. 32.)","kind":"passage","sequence":197,"address":"16.197"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0198","text":"Verfasser dieser anonymen Schrift:\n G. Garnier.] . Der Preis der Arbeitszeit selbst ist\n schliesslich bestimmt durch die Gleichung: Werth der Tagesarbeit\n = Tageswerth der Arbeitskraft. Der Stücklohn ist also nur\n eine modificirte Form des Zeitlohns.","kind":"passage","sequence":198,"address":"16.198"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0199","text":"Betrachten wir nun etwas näher die charakteristischen Eigenthüm-\n lichkeiten des Stücklohns.","kind":"passage","sequence":199,"address":"16.199"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0200","text":"Die Qualität der Arbeit ist hier durch das Werk selbst kon-\n trolirt, das die durchschnittliche Güte besitzen muss, soll der Stückpreis\n voll bezahlt werden. Der Stücklohn wird nach dieser Seite hin zu frucht-\n barster Quelle von Lohnabzügen und kapitalistischer Prellerei.","kind":"passage","sequence":200,"address":"16.200"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0201","text":"Er bietet dem Kapitalisten ein ganz bestimmtes Mass für die Inten-\n sivität der Arbeit. Nur Arbeitszeit, die sich in einem vorher be-\n stimmten und erfahrungsmässig festgesetzten Waarenquantum verkörpert,\n gilt als gesellschaftlich nothwendige Arbeitszeit und wird\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"541\", \"facs\": \"#f0560\"} --> als solche bezahlt. In den grösseren Schneiderwerkstätten Londons heisst\n daher ein gewisses Stück Arbeit, z. B. eine Weste u. s. w., Stunde, halbe\n Stunde u. s. w., die Stunde zu 6 d. Aus der Praxis ist bekannt, wie viel\n das Durchschnittsprodukt einer Stunde. Bei neuen Moden, Reparaturen\n u. s. w. entsteht Streit zwischen Anwender und Arbeiter, ob ein bestimmtes\n Arbeitsstück = einer Stunde u. s. w., bis auch hier die Erfahrung ent-\n scheidet. Aehnlich in den Londoner Möbelschreinereien u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":201,"address":"16.201"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0202","text":"Besitzt\n der Arbeiter nicht die durchschnittliche Leistungsfähigkeit, kann er daher\n ein bestimmtes Minimum von Tagwerk nicht liefern, so entlässt man\n ihn [Note: „Le fileur reçoit un certain poids de coton préparé pour lequel il doit\n rendre, dans un espace de temps donné, une quantité voulue de fil ou coton file,\n et il est payé à raison de tant par livre d’ouvrage rendu. Si le produit pèche en\n qualité, la faute retombe sur lui; s’il y a moins que la quantité fixée par le mini-\n mum, dans un temps donné, on le congédie et on le remplace par un ouvrier plus\n habile.“ (Ure l. c. t. II, p. 61.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":202,"address":"16.202"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0203","text":"Da Qualität und Intensivität der Arbeit hier durch die Form des\n Arbeitslohns selbst kontrolirt werden, macht sie grossen Theil der Ar-\n beitsaufsicht überflüssig. Sie bildet daher sowohl die Grundlage\n der früher geschilderten modernen Hausarbeit als eines hierarchisch\n gegliederten Systems der Exploitation und Unterdrückung. Das letztere be-\n sitzt zwei Grundformen. Der Stücklohn erleichtert einerseits das Zwischen-\n schieben von Parasiten zwischen Kapitalist und Lohnarbeiter, Unterver-\n pachtung der Arbeit (subletting of labour).","kind":"passage","sequence":203,"address":"16.203"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0204","text":"Der Gewinn der Zwi-\n schenpersonen fliesst ausschliesslich aus der Differenz zwischen dem\n Arbeitspreis, den der Kapitalist zahlt, und dem Theil dieses Preises, den\n sie dem Arbeiter wirklich zukommen lassen [Note: „It is when work passes through several hands, each of which is to take\n its share of profits, while only the last does the work, that the pay which reaches\n the workwoman is miserably disproportioned.“ („Child. Empl. Comm.“\n II. Rep., p. LXX, n. 424.)] . Diess System heisst in\n England charakteristisch das „Sweating System“ (Ausschweissungs-\n system). Andrerseits erlaubt der Stücklohn dem Kapitalisten mit dem\n Hauptarbeiter — in der Manufaktur mit dem Chef einer Gruppe, in den Mi-\n nen mit dem Ausbrecher der Kohle u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":204,"address":"16.204"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0205","text":"w., in der Fabrik mit dem eigentlichen\n Maschinenarbeiter — einen Kontrakt für so viel per Stück zu schliessen,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"542\", \"facs\": \"#f0561\"} --> ein Preis, wofür der Hauptarbeiter selbst die Anwerbung und Zahlung\n seiner Hilfsarbeiter übernimmt. Die Exploitation der Arbeiter durch das\n Kapital verwirklicht sich hier vermittelst der Exploitation des Arbeiters\n durch den Arbeiter [Note: Selbst der apologetische Watts bemerkt: „It would be a great improve-\n ment to the system of piece-work, if all the men employed on a job were partners\n in the contract, each according to his abilities, instead of one man being\n interested in overworking his fellows for his own benefit.“ (l. c.\n p. 53.) Ueber die Gemeinheiten dieses Systems vgl. „Child. Empl. Comm.“\nRep. III, p. 66, n. 22, p. 11, n. 124, p. XI, n. 13, 53, 59 u. s. w.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":205,"address":"16.205"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0206","text":"Den Stücklohn gegeben, ist es natürlich das persönliche Interesse des\n Arbeiters, seine Arbeitskraft möglichst intensiv anzuspannen, was dem\n Kapitalisten eine Erhöhung des Normalgrads der Intensivität\n erleichtert [Note: Diesem naturwüchsigen Resultat wird oft künstlich unter die Arme ge-\n griffen. Z. B. im Engineering Trade von London gilt es als herkömmlicher\n trick, „dass der Kapitalist als Chef einer Arbeiteranzahl einen Mann von über-\n legner physischer Kraft und Geschwindigkeit auswählt.","kind":"passage","sequence":206,"address":"16.206"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0207","text":"Er zahlt ihm vierteljährlich\n oder in andern Terminen einen Zuschusslohn unter der Uebereinkunft, alles mög-\n liche aufzubieten, um seine Mitarbeiter, die nur den gewöhnlichen Lohn er-\n halten, zur äussersten Nacheiferung anzustacheln … Ohne weiteren Kommentar\n erklärt diess die Kapitalistenklage über „Lähmung der Thätigkeit oder überlegener\n Geschicklichkeit und Arbeitskraft („stinting the action, superior skill and work-\n ing power“) durch die Trade’s Unions.“ (Dunning l. c. p. 22, 23.) Da der Ver-\n fasser selbst Arbeiter und Sekretär einer Trade’s Union, könnte diess für Ueber-\n treibung gelten. Aber man sehe z. B. die „highly respectable“ agronomische\n Cyclopädie von J. Ch. Morton, Art. „Labourer“, wo diese Methode den\n Pächtern als probat empfohlen wird.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":207,"address":"16.207"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0208","text":"Es ist ebenso das persönliche Interesse des Arbeiters, den\nArbeitstag zu verlängern, weil damit sein Tages- oder Wochen-\n lohn steigt [Note: „All those who are paid by piece-work … profit by the transgression\n of the legal limits of work. This observation as to the willingness to work over-\n time, is especially applicable to the women employed as weavers and reelers.“\n („Rep. of Insp. of Fact. 30th April 1858“, p. 9.) „Diess Stücklohn-\n system, so vortheilhaft für den Kapitalisten … strebt direkt den\n jungen Töpfer zu grosser Ueberarbeit zu ermuntern, während der 4 oder 5 Jahre,\n worin er per Stück, aber zu niedrigem Preis, bezahlt wird. Es ist diess eine der\n grossen Ursachen, denen die physische Degeneration der Töpfer zuzuschreiben\n ist.“ („Child. Empl. Comm.“ I. Rep., p. XIII.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":208,"address":"16.208"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0209","text":"Es tritt damit die beim Zeitlohn bereits geschilderte Re-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"543\", \"facs\": \"#f0562\"} --> aktion ein, abgesehn davon, dass die Verlängerung des Arbeitstags, selbst\n bei constant bleibendem Stücklohn, an und für sich eine Senkung im\nPreise der Arbeit einschliesst.","kind":"passage","sequence":209,"address":"16.209"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0210","text":"Beim Zeitlohn herrscht mit wenigen Ausnahmen gleicher Arbeits-\n lohn für dieselben Funktionen, während beim Stücklohn der Preis der\n Arbeitszeit zwar durch ein bestimmtes Produktquantum gemessen ist, der\n Tages- oder Wochenlohn dagegen wechselt mit der individuellen Ver-\n schiedenheit der Arbeiter, wovon der Eine nur das Minimum des Produkts\n in einer gegebnen Zeit liefert, der Andre den Durchschnitt, der Dritte\n mehr als den Durchschnitt. In Bezug auf die wirkliche Einnahme treten\n hier also grosse Differenzen ein je nach dem verschiednen Geschick, Kraft,\n Energie, Ausdauer u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":210,"address":"16.210"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0211","text":"der individuellen Arbeiter [Note: „Where the work in any trade is paid for by the piece at so much per\n job … wages may very materially differ in amount … But in work by the\n day there is generally an uniform rate … recognized by both employer and\n employed as the standard of wages for the general run of workmen in the trade.“\n (Dunning l. c. p. 17.)] . Diess ändert\n natürlich nichts an dem allgemeinen Verhältniss zwischen Kapital und\n Lohnarbeit. Erstens gleichen sich diese individuellen Unterschiede für das\n Gesammtatelier aus, so dass es in einer bestimmten Arbeitszeit das Durch-\n schnittsprodukt liefert und der gezahlte Gesammtlohn der Durchschnitts-\n lohn des Geschäftszweigs sein wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":211,"address":"16.211"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0212","text":"Zweitens bleibt die Proportion zwi-\n schen Arbeitslohn und Mehrwerth unverändert, da dem individuellen Lohn\n des einzelnen Arbeiters die von ihm individuell gelieferte Masse von Mehr-\n werth entspricht. Aber der grössere Spielraum, den der Stücklohn der\n Individualität bietet, strebt einerseits dahin die Individualität und damit\n Freiheitsgefühl, Selbstständigkeit und Selbstkontrole der Arbeiter zu ent-\n wickeln, andrerseits ihre Konkurrenz unter und gegen einan-\n der. Er hat daher eine Tendenz mit der Erhebung individueller Arbeits-\n löhne über das Durchschnittsniveau diess Niveau selbst zu senken. Wo\n aber bestimmter Stücklohn sich seit lange traditionell befestigt hatte und\n seine Herabsetzung daher besondre Schwierigkeiten bot, flüchteten die\n Meister ausnahmsweis auch zu seiner gewaltsamen Verwandlung in\n Zeitlohn. Hiergegen z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":212,"address":"16.212"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0213","text":"1860 grosser Strike unter den Bandwebern von\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"544\", \"facs\": \"#f0563\"} --> Coventry [Note: „Die Arbeit der Handwerksgesellen regelt sich nach dem Tag oder nach\n dem Stück (à la journée ou à la pièce) … Die Meister wissen ungefähr, wie viel\n Werk die Arbeiter täglich in jedem métier verrichten können und zahlen sie daher\n oft im Verhältniss zum Werk, das sie verrichten; so arbeiten diese Gesellen so\n viel sie können, in ihrem eignen Interesse, ohne weitere Beaufsichtigung.“ (Can-\n tillon: „Essai sur la Nature du Commerce en Général.“ Amst.\n Ed. 1756, p. 185 u. 202.) Cantillon, aus dem Quesnay, Sir James Steuart und\n A. Smith reichlich geschöpft haben, stellt hier also schon den Stücklohn als bloss\n modificirte Form des Zeitlohns dar.","kind":"passage","sequence":213,"address":"16.213"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0214","text":"Die französische Ausgabe Cantillon’s kündigt\n sich auf dem Titel als Uebersetzung aus dem Englischen an, aber die englische\n Ausgabe: „The Analysis of Trade, Commerce etc. by Philip Can-\n tillon, late of the City of London, Merchant“, ist nicht nur späteren\n Datums (von 1759), sondern erweist sich durch ihren Inhalt als eine spätere Be-\n arbeitung. So z. B. findet sich in der französischen Ausgabe Hume noch nicht\n erwähnt, während umgekehrt in der englischen Petty kaum mehr figurirt. Die\n englische Ausgabe ist theoretisch unbedeutender, enthält aber allerlei spezifisch\n auf englischen Handel, Bullionhandel u. s. w. Bezügliches, was im französischen\n Text fehlt. Die Worte im Titel der englischen Ausgabe, wonach die Schrift\n „Taken chiefly from the Manuscript of a very ingenious Gentleman deceased, and\n adapted etc.“, scheinen daher mehr als blosse, damals sehr übliche, Fiktion.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":214,"address":"16.214"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0215","text":"Der Stücklohn ist endlich eine Hauptstütze des früher ge-\n schilderten Stundensystems [Note: „Combien de fois n’avons nous pas vu, dans certains ateliers, embaucher\n beaucoup plus d’ouvriers que ne le demandait le travail à mettre en main? Souvent,\n dans la prévision d’un travail aléatoire, quelquefois même imaginaire, on admet\n des ouvriers: comme on les paie aux pièces, on se dit qu’on court aucun\n risque, parce que toutes les pertes de temps seront à la charge des in-\n occupés.“ (H. Gregoir: „Les Typographes devant le Tribunal\n Correctionnel de Bruxelles.“ Bruxelles 1865, p. 9.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":215,"address":"16.215"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0216","text":"Aus der bisherigen Darstellung ergiebt sich, dass der Stücklohn\n die der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise entsprechendste Form des Arbeits-\n lohns ist. Obgleich keineswegs neu, — er figurirt neben dem Zeitlohn\n officiell u. a. in den französischen und englischen Arbeiterstatuten des vier-\n zehnten Jahrhunderts — gewinnt er doch erst grösseren Spielraum während\n der eigentlichen Manufakturperiode. In der Sturm- und Drangperiode der\n grossen Industrie, namentlich von 1797 bis 1815, dient er als Hebel zur\n Verlängerung der Arbeitszeit und Herabsetzung des Arbeitslohns.","kind":"passage","sequence":216,"address":"16.216"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0217","text":"Sehr\n wichtiges Material für die Bewegung des Arbeitslohns während jener Pe-\n riode findet man in den Blaubüchern: „Report and Evidence from\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"545\", \"facs\": \"#f0564\"} --> the select Committee on Petitions respecting the Corn\n Laws“ (Parlamentssession 1813—14) und: „Reports from the\n Lords’ Committee, on the state of Growth, Commerce,\n and Consumption of Grain, and all Laws relating there-\n to“. (Session 1814—15.) Man findet hier den dokumentarischen\n Nachweis für die fortwährende Senkung des Arbeitspreises seit dem Be-\n ginn des Antijakobinerkriegs. In der Weberei z. B. war der Stücklohn\n so gefallen, dass trotz des sehr verlängerten Arbeitstags der Taglohn jetzt\n niedriger stand als vorher. „Die reale Einnahme des Webers ist sehr\n viel weniger als früher: seine Superiorität über den gewöhnlichen Arbeiter,\n die erst sehr gross war, ist fast ganz verschwunden.","kind":"passage","sequence":217,"address":"16.217"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0218","text":"In der That, der\n Unterschied in den Löhnen geschickter und gewöhnlicher Arbeit ist jetzt\n viel unbedeutender als während irgend einer früheren Periode“ [Note: „Remarks on the Commercial Policy of Great Britain.\n London 1815“, p. 48.] . Wie\n wenig die mit dem Stücklohn gesteigerte Intensivität und Ausdehnung der\n Arbeit dem ländlichen Proletariat fruchteten, zeige folgende einer Partei-\n schrift für Landlords und Pächter entlehnte Stelle: „Bei\n weitem der grössere Theil der Agrikulturoperationen ist durch Leute ver-\n richtet, die für den Tag oder auf Stückwerk gedungen werden. Ihr\n Wochenlohn beträgt ungefähr 12 sh.; und obgleich man voraussetzen\n mag, dass ein Mann bei Stücklohn, unter dem grösseren Arbeitssporn,\n 1 sh. oder vielleicht 2 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":218,"address":"16.218"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0219","text":"mehr verdient als beim Wochenlohn, so findet man\n dennoch, bei Schätzung seiner Gesammteinnahme, dass sein Verlust an Be-\n schäftigung im Lauf des Jahrs diesen Zuschuss aufwiegt. … Man wird ferner\n im Allgemeinen finden, dass die Löhne dieser Männer ein gewisses Ver-\n hältniss zum Preis der nothwendigen Lebensmittel haben; so dass ein\n Mann mit zwei Kindern fähig ist seine Familie ohne Zu-\n flucht zur Pfarreiunterstützung zu erhalten“ [Note: „A Defence of the Landowners and Farmers of Great\n Britain. Lond. 1814“, p. 4, 5.] . Mal-\n thus bemerkte damals mit Bezug auf die vom Parlament veröffentlichten\n Thatsachen: „Ich gestehe, ich sehe mit Missvergnügen die grosse\n Ausdehnung der Praxis des Stücklohns. Wirklich harte\n [Running matter: I.","kind":"passage","sequence":219,"address":"16.219"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0220","text":"35] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"546\", \"facs\": \"#f0565\"} --> Arbeit während 12 oder 14 Stunden des Tags, für irgend längere Zeit-\n perioden, ist zu viel für ein menschliches Wesen“ [Note: Malthus l. c.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":220,"address":"16.220"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0221","text":"In den dem Fabrikgesetz unterworfenen Werkstätten wird Stücklohn\n allgemeine Regel, weil das Kapital dort den Arbeitstag nur noch intensiv\n ausweiten kann [Note: „Die Arbeiter auf Stücklohn bilden wahrscheinlich ⅘ aller Arbeiter in\n den Fabriken.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 30th April 1858“,\n p. 9.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":221,"address":"16.221"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0222","text":"Mit der wechselnden Produktivität der Arbeit stellt dasselbe Pro-\n duktenquantum wechselnde Arbeitszeit dar. Also wechselt auch der\n Stücklohn, da er Preisausdruck einer bestimmten Arbeitszeit. In unsrem\n obigen Beispiel wurden in 12 Stunden 24 Stück producirt, während das\n Werthprodukt der 12 Stunden 6 sh. war, der Tageswerth der Arbeits-\n kraft 3 sh., der Preis der Arbeitsstunde 3 d. und der Lohn für ein Stück\n 1½ d. In einem Stück war ½ Arbeitsstunde eingesaugt. Liefert der-\n selbe Arbeitstag nun etwa in Folge verdoppelter Produktivität der Arbeit\n 48 Stück statt 24, und bleiben alle andern Umstände unverändert, so sinkt\n der Stücklohn von 1½ d. auf ¾ d. oder 3 Farthing, da jedes Stück jetzt\n nur noch ¼ statt ½ Arbeitsstunde darstellt. 24 × 1½ d. = 3 sh.\n und ebenso 48 × ¾ d. = 3 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":222,"address":"16.222"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0223","text":"In andern Worten: Der Stücklohn\n wird in demselben Verhältniss heruntergesetzt, worin die Zahl der während\n derselben Zeit producirten Stücke wächst [Note: „On se rend un compte exact de la force productive de son métier (du\n fileur), et l’on diminue la rétribution du travail à mésure que la force productive\n augmente … sans cependant que cette augmentation soit proportionnée à l’aug-\n mentation de la force.“ (Ure l. c. p. 61.) Letztere apologetische Wendung\n hebt Ure selbst wieder auf. Bei einer Verlängerung der mule jenny z. B.\n „quelque surcroit de travail provient de l’allongement.“ (l. c. II, p. 34.) Die\n Arbeit nimmt also nicht in demselben Masse ab, worin ihre Produktivität wächst.\n Ferner: „Ce surcroit augmentera la force productive d’un cinquième.","kind":"passage","sequence":223,"address":"16.223"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0224","text":"Dans ce\n cas on baisera le prix du fileur; mais comme on ne le réduira pas d’un cinquième\n le perfectionnement augmentera son gain dans le même nombre d’heures donné“,\n aber „il y a une certaine modification à faire … C’est que le fileur a des frais\n additionnels à déduire sur les 6 d., attendu qu’il faut qu’il augmente le\n nombre de ses aides non-adultes“, begleitet von einem „déplacement d’une\n partie des adultes“ (l. c. p. 66, 67), was keineswegs Tendenz zur Steigerung\n des Arbeitslohns hat.] , also die auf dasselbe Stück\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"547\", \"facs\": \"#f0566\"} --> verwandte Arbeitszeit abnimmt. Dieser Wechsel des Stücklohns, soweit rein\n nominell, ruft beständige Kämpfe zwischen Kapitalist und Arbeiter\n hervor. Entweder, weil der Kapitalist den Vorwand benutzt, um wirklich\n den Preis der Arbeit herabzusetzen.","kind":"passage","sequence":224,"address":"16.224"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0225","text":"Oder weil die gesteigerte Produktiv-\n kraft der Arbeit von gesteigerter Intensivität derselben begleitet ist.\n Oder, weil der Arbeiter den Schein des Stücklohns, als ob ihm sein\n Produkt gezahlt werde und nicht seine Arbeitskraft, ernst nimmt und sich\n daher gegen eine Lohnherabsetzung sträubt, welcher die Herabsetzung im\n Verkaufspreis der Waare nicht entspricht. „Die Arbeiter überwachen sorg-\n fältig den Preis des Rohmaterials und den Preis der fabricirten Güter und\n sind so fähig die Profite ihrer Meister genau zu veranschlagen“ [Note: H. Fawcett: „The Economic Position of the British La-\n bourer“. Cambridge and London 1865, p. 178.] . Sol-\n chen Anspruch fertigt das Kapital mit Recht als groben Irrthum über die\n Natur der Lohnarbeit ab [Note: Im Londoner Standard vom 26. October 1861 findet man Bericht über\n einen Prozess der Firma John Brightet Co.","kind":"passage","sequence":225,"address":"16.225"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0226","text":"vor den Rochdale Magistrates „to\n prosecute for intimidation the agents of the Carpet Weavers Trades’\n Union. Bright’s partners had introduced new machinery which would turn out\n 240 yards of carpet in the time and with the labour (!) previously required to produce\n 160 yards. The workmen had no claim whatever to share in the profits made by\n the investment of their employer’s capital in mechanical improvements. Accord-\n ingly, Mssrs. Bright proposed to lower the rate of pay from 1½ d. per yard to\n 1 d., leaving the earnings of the men exactly the same as before for the same\n labour. But there was a nominal reduction, of which the operatives, it is asserted,\n had not fair warning before hand.“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":226,"address":"16.226"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0227","text":"Es zetert über diese Anmassung Steuern\n auf den Fortschritt der Industrie zu legen und erklärt rundweg, dass die\n Produktivität der Arbeit den Arbeiter überhaupt nichts angeht [Note: „Trades Unions in ihrer Sucht den Arbeitslohn aufrecht zu halten, suchen\n an dem Profit verbesserter Maschinerie Theil zu nehmen! (Quelle horreur!)\n … sie verlangen höheren Lohn, weil die Arbeit verkürzt ist … in anderen Worten,\n sie streben eine Steuer auf industrielle Verbesserungen zu legen.“ („On Com-\n bination of Trades. New Edit. Lond. 1834“, p. 42.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":227,"address":"16.227"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0228","text":"Im zweiten Abschnitt dieses Kapitels beschäftigten uns die mannig-\n fachen Kombinationen, welche ein Wechsel in der absoluten oder relativen\n (d. h. mit dem Mehrwerth verglichenen) Werthgrösse der Arbeitskraft her-\n vorbringen kann, während andrerseits wieder das Quantum von Lebens-\n mitteln, worin der Preis der Arbeitskraft realisirt wird, von dem\n [Running matter: 35*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"548\", \"facs\": \"#f0567\"} --> Wechsel dieses Preises unabhängige [Note: „It is not accurate to say that wages (handelt sich hier von ihrem Preise)\n are increased, because they purchase more of a cheaper article.“ (David\n Buchanan in seiner Ausgabe von A Smith’s „Wealth etc.“ 1814, v. I,\n p. 417 Note.)]  oder verschiedne Bewegungen durch-\n laufen konnte. Wie bereits bemerkt, verwandeln sich durch einfache Ueber-\n setzung des Werths, resp.","kind":"passage","sequence":228,"address":"16.228"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0229","text":"Preises der Arbeitskraft in die exoteri-\n sche Form des Arbeitslohns alle jene Gesetze in Gesetze der Bewe-\n gung des Arbeitslohns. Was innerhalb dieser Bewegung als wechselnde\n Kombination, kann für verschiedne Länder als gleichzeitige Ver-\n schiedenheit nationaler Arbeitslöhne erscheinen. Beim Ver-\n gleich nationaler Arbeitslöhne sind also alle den Grössenwechsel des\n Werths der Arbeitskraft bestimmende Momente zu erwägen, Preis und Umfang\n der natürlichen und historisch entwickelten ersten Lebensbedürfnisse, Erzie-\n hungskosten des Arbeiters, Rolle der Weiber- und Kinderarbeit, Produk-\n tivität der Arbeit, ihre extensive und intensive Grösse. Selbst die ober-\n flächlichste Vergleichung erheischt, zunächst den Durchschnitts-Taglohn für\n dieselben Gewerbe in verschiednen Ländern auf gleich grosse Arbeitstage\n zu reduciren.","kind":"passage","sequence":229,"address":"16.229"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0230","text":"Nach solcher Ausgleichung der Taglöhne, muss der Zeit-\n lohn wieder in Stücklohn übersetzt werden, da nur der letztere ein Grad-\n messer sowohl für die Produktivität als die intensive Grösse der Arbeit.\n Es wird sich dann meist finden, dass der niedrigere Taglohn bei einer\n Nation einen höheren Arbeitspreis und der höhere Taglohn bei einer an-\n dern Nation einen niedrigeren Arbeitspreis ausdrückt, ganz wie die Be-\n wegung des Taglohns überhaupt die Möglichkeit dieser Kombination\n zeigte [Note: James Anderson bemerkt in Polemik gegen A. Smith: „It deserves\n likewise to be remarked, that although the apparent price of labour is usually lower\n in poor countries, where the produce of the soil, and grain in general, is cheap;\n yet it is in fact for the most part really higher than in other countries.","kind":"passage","sequence":230,"address":"16.230"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0231","text":"For it is\n not the wages that is given to the labourer per day that constitutes the real price\n of labour, although it is its apparent price. The real price is that which a\ncertain quantity of work performed actually costs the employer; and\n considered in this light, labour is in almost all cases cheaper in rich coun-\n tries than in those that are poorer, although the price of grain, and other\n provisions, is usually much lower in the last than in the first … Labour\n estimated by the day, is much lower in Scotland than in England …] .","kind":"passage","sequence":231,"address":"16.231"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0232","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"549\", \"facs\": \"#f0568\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":232,"address":"16.232"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0233","text":"Auf dem Weltmarkt zählt nicht nur der intensivere nationale Ar-\n beitstag als Arbeitstag von grösserer Stundenzahl, als extensiv grösserer Ar-\n beitstag, sondern der produktivere nationale Arbeitstag zählt als intensi-\n ver, so oft die produktivere Nation nicht durch die Konkurrenz gezwungen\n wird den Verkaufspreis der Waare auf ihren Werth zu senken. Der in-\n tensivere und produktivere nationale Arbeitstag stellt sich also im Ganzen\n auf dem Weltmarkt in höherem Geldausdruck dar als der minder in-\n tensive oder produktive nationale Arbeitstag. Was von dem Arbeitstag,\n gilt von jedem seiner aliquoten Theile. Der absolute Geldpreis\n der Arbeit kann also bei einer Nation höher stehn als bei der andern,\n obgleich der relative Arbeitslohn, d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":233,"address":"16.233"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0234","text":"der Arbeitslohn verglichen\n mit dem vom Arbeiter producirten Mehrwerth, oder seinem ganzen Werth-\n produkt, oder dem Preis der Nahrungsmittel, niedriger steht [Note: „Mr. Cowell, qui a fait une analyse très soignée de la filature, cherche à\n prouver, dans un rapport supplémentaire („supplement to the Report on Manu-\n factures“), que les salaires en Angleterre, sont virtuellement inférieurs pour\n le capitaliste, quoique pour l’ouvrier ils soient peut-être plus élevés que sur le con-\n tinent européen.“ (Ure l. c. t. II, p. 58.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":234,"address":"16.234"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0235","text":"In „Versuch über die Rate des Arbeitslohns“ [Note: „Essay on the Rate of Wages: with an Examination of the\n Causes of the Differences in the Conditions of the Labouring\n Population throughout the World. Philadelphia 1835.“] , einer seiner frühsten\n ökonomischen Schriften, sucht H. Carey nachzuweisen, dass die ver-\n schiednen nationalen Arbeitslöhne sich direkt verhalten wie die Produk-\n tivitätsgrade der nationalen Arbeitstage, um aus diesem internationalen\n Verhältniss den Schluss zu ziehn, dass der Arbeitslohn überhaupt steigt\n und fällt wie die Produktivität der Arbeit. Unsre ganze Analyse der Pro-\n duktion des Mehrwerths beweist die Abgeschmacktheit dieser Schlussfolge-\n rung, hätte Carey selbst seine Prämisse bewiesen, statt seiner Gewohn-\n heit gemäss unkritisch und oberflächlich zusammengerafftes statistisches\n Material kunterbunt durch einander zu würfeln.","kind":"passage","sequence":235,"address":"16.235"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0236","text":"Das Beste ist, dass er\n nicht behauptet, die Sache verhalte sich wirklich so, wie sie sich der\n Theorie nach verhalten sollte. Die Staatseinmischung hat nämlich das\n naturgemässe ökonomische Verhältniss verfälscht. Man muss\n [Note: Labour by the piece is generally cheaper in England.“ (James Ander-\n son: „Observations on the means of exciting a spirit of National\n Industry etc. Edinb. 1777“, p. 350, 351.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"550\", \"facs\": \"#f0569\"} --> daher die nationalen Arbeitslöhne so berechnen, als ob der Theil derselben,\n der dem Staat in der Form von Steuern zufällt, dem Arbeiter selbst zufiele.\n Sollte Herr Carey nicht weiter darüber nachdenken, ob diese „Staats-\n kosten“ nicht auch „naturgemässe“ Früchte der kapitalistischen Entwick-\n lung sind?","kind":"passage","sequence":236,"address":"16.236"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0237","text":"Das Raisonnement ist ganz des Mannes würdig, der die kapita-\n listischen Produktionsverhältnisse erst für ewige Natur- und Vernunftge-\n setze erklärte, deren frei harmonisches Spiel nur durch die Staatsein-\n mischung gestört werde, um hinterher zu entdecken, dass Englands dia-\n bolischer Einfluss auf den Weltmarkt, ein Einfluss, der, wie es scheint,\n nicht den Naturgesetzen der kapitalistischen Produktion entspringt, die\n Staatseinmischung nöthig macht, nämlich den Schutz jener Natur- und\n Vernunftgesetze durch den Staat, alias das Protektionssystem. Er ent-\n deckte ferner, dass die Theoreme Ricardo’s u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":237,"address":"16.237"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0238","text":"w., worin existirende gesell-\n schaftliche Gegensätze und Widersprüche formulirt sind, nicht das ideale Pro-\n dukt der wirklichen ökonomischen Bewegung, sondern dass umgekehrt die\n wirklichen Gegensätze der kapitalistischen Produktion in England und\n anderswo das Resultat der Ricardo’schen u. s. w. Theorie sind! Er ent-\n deckte schliesslich, dass es in letzter Instanz der Handel ist, der die ein-\n gebornen Schönheiten und Harmonieen der kapitalistischen Produktions-\n weise vernichtet. Noch einen Schritt weiter, und er entdeckt vielleicht,\n dass der einzige Missstand an der kapitalistischen Produktion das Kapi-\n tal selbst ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":238,"address":"16.238"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0239","text":"Nur ein Mann von so entsetzlicher Kritiklosigkeit und sol-\n cher Gelehrsamkeit de faux aloi verdiente, trotz seiner protektionistischen\n Ketzerei, die Geheimquelle der harmonischen Weisheit eines\n Bastiat und aller andern freihändlerischen Optimisten der Gegenwart zu\n werden [Note: Ich werde im Vierten Buch die Seichtigkeit seiner Wissenschaft näher\n nachweisen.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":239,"address":"16.239"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_FNKW:P0240","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"551\", \"facs\": \"#f0570\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":240,"address":"16.240"}],"sha256":"b3c1356fb84014cbcb2f4b0fe08659c7489bcacb5281f4eebe52e4736893e561","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA","title":"Sechstes Kapitel. Der Accumulationsprozess des Kapitals.","order":17,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_SKDA","text":"## Sechstes Kapitel.\n Der Accumulationsprozess des Kapitals.\n\n\n\n\nMan hat gesehn, wie das Kapital in der Form der Waare Mehr-\n werth producirt. Nur durch den Verkauf der Waare wird der\n in ihr steckende Mehrwerth zusammen mit dem in ihrer Produktion vorge-\n schossenen Kapitalwerth realisirt. Der Accumulationsprozess\n des Kapitals unterstellt daher seinen Cirkulationsprozess. Die\n Betrachtung des letzteren bleibt aber dem folgenden Buch vorbehalten.\n Die realen Bedingungen der Reproduktion, d. h. der kontinuir-\n lichen Produktion, erscheinen theils erst innerhalb der Cirkula-\n tion, theils können sie erst nach der Analyse des Cirkulationsprozesses\n behandelt werden.\n\n\n\n\nDas ist jedoch nicht alles. Der Kapitalist, der den Mehrwerth pro-\n ducirt, d. h. unbezahlte Arbeit unmittelbar aus den Arbeitern auspumpt\n und in Waaren fixirt, ist zwar der erste Aneigner, aber keineswegs der\n letzte Eigenthümer dieses Mehrwerths. Er hat ihn hinterher zu theilen\n mit Kapitalisten, die andre Funktionen im Grossen und Ganzen der gesell-\n schaftlichen Produktion vollziehn, mit dem Grundeigenthümer u. s. w.\n Der Mehrwerth spaltet sich daher in verschiedne Theile. Seine Bruchstücke\n fallen verschiednen Kategorieen von Personen zu und krystallisiren zu\n verschiednen, gegen einander selbstständigen Formen, wie Profit, Zins,\n Handelsgewinn, Grundrente u. s. w. Diese verwandelten Formen\n des Mehrwerths können erst im dritten Buch behandelt werden.\n\n\n\n\nWir unterstellen hier also einerseits, dass der Kapitalist, der die\n Waare producirt, sie zu ihrem Werth verkauft, ohne bei seiner Rückkehr\n zum Waarenmarkt weiter zu verweilen, weder bei den neuen Formen, die\n dem Kapital anschiessen in der Cirkulationssphäre, noch den darin ein-\n gehüllten konkreten Bedingungen der Reproduktion. Andrerseits gilt uns\n der kapitalistische Producent als Eigenthümer des ganzen Mehrwerths\n oder, wenn man will, als Repräsentant aller seiner Theilnehmer an der\n Beute. Wir betrachten also zunächst die Accumulation abstrakt, d. h.\n als blosses Moment des unmittelbaren Produktionsprozesses.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"552\", \"facs\": \"#f0571\"} -->\n\nSo weit übrigens Accumulation stattfindet, gelingt dem Kapitalisten\n der Verkauf der producirten Waare und die Rückverwandlung des aus ihr\n gelösten Geldes in Kapital. Ferner: Der Bruch des Mehrwerths in ver-\n schiedne Stücke ändert nichts an seiner Natur, noch an den nothwendigen\n Bedingungen, worin er zum Element der Accumulation wird. Welche\n Proportion des Mehrwerths der kapitalistische Producent immer für sich\n selbst festhalte oder an andere abtrete, er eignet ihn stets in erster Hand\n an. Was also bei unsrer Darstellung der Accumulation unterstellt wird,\n ist bei ihrem wirklichen Vorgang unterstellt. Andrerseits verdunkeln die\n Zerspaltung des Mehrwerths und die vermittelnde Bewegung der Circulation\n die einfache Grundform des Accumulationsprozesses. Seine reine\n Analyse erheischt daher vorläufiges Wegsehn von allen Phänomenen,\n welche das innere Spiel seines Mechanismus verstecken. Der Fortgang\n der Darstellung führt später durch seine eigne Dialektik zu jenen konkre-\n teren Formen.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0001","text":"## Sechstes Kapitel.\n Der Accumulationsprozess des Kapitals.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"17.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0002","text":"Man hat gesehn, wie das Kapital in der Form der Waare Mehr-\n werth producirt. Nur durch den Verkauf der Waare wird der\n in ihr steckende Mehrwerth zusammen mit dem in ihrer Produktion vorge-\n schossenen Kapitalwerth realisirt. Der Accumulationsprozess\n des Kapitals unterstellt daher seinen Cirkulationsprozess. Die\n Betrachtung des letzteren bleibt aber dem folgenden Buch vorbehalten.\n Die realen Bedingungen der Reproduktion, d. h. der kontinuir-\n lichen Produktion, erscheinen theils erst innerhalb der Cirkula-\n tion, theils können sie erst nach der Analyse des Cirkulationsprozesses\n behandelt werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"17.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0003","text":"Das ist jedoch nicht alles. Der Kapitalist, der den Mehrwerth pro-\n ducirt, d. h. unbezahlte Arbeit unmittelbar aus den Arbeitern auspumpt\n und in Waaren fixirt, ist zwar der erste Aneigner, aber keineswegs der\n letzte Eigenthümer dieses Mehrwerths. Er hat ihn hinterher zu theilen\n mit Kapitalisten, die andre Funktionen im Grossen und Ganzen der gesell-\n schaftlichen Produktion vollziehn, mit dem Grundeigenthümer u. s. w.\n Der Mehrwerth spaltet sich daher in verschiedne Theile. Seine Bruchstücke\n fallen verschiednen Kategorieen von Personen zu und krystallisiren zu\n verschiednen, gegen einander selbstständigen Formen, wie Profit, Zins,\n Handelsgewinn, Grundrente u. s. w. Diese verwandelten Formen\n des Mehrwerths können erst im dritten Buch behandelt werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"17.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0004","text":"Wir unterstellen hier also einerseits, dass der Kapitalist, der die\n Waare producirt, sie zu ihrem Werth verkauft, ohne bei seiner Rückkehr\n zum Waarenmarkt weiter zu verweilen, weder bei den neuen Formen, die\n dem Kapital anschiessen in der Cirkulationssphäre, noch den darin ein-\n gehüllten konkreten Bedingungen der Reproduktion. Andrerseits gilt uns\n der kapitalistische Producent als Eigenthümer des ganzen Mehrwerths\n oder, wenn man will, als Repräsentant aller seiner Theilnehmer an der\n Beute. Wir betrachten also zunächst die Accumulation abstrakt, d. h.\n als blosses Moment des unmittelbaren Produktionsprozesses.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"17.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0005","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"552\", \"facs\": \"#f0571\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"17.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0006","text":"So weit übrigens Accumulation stattfindet, gelingt dem Kapitalisten\n der Verkauf der producirten Waare und die Rückverwandlung des aus ihr\n gelösten Geldes in Kapital. Ferner: Der Bruch des Mehrwerths in ver-\n schiedne Stücke ändert nichts an seiner Natur, noch an den nothwendigen\n Bedingungen, worin er zum Element der Accumulation wird. Welche\n Proportion des Mehrwerths der kapitalistische Producent immer für sich\n selbst festhalte oder an andere abtrete, er eignet ihn stets in erster Hand\n an. Was also bei unsrer Darstellung der Accumulation unterstellt wird,\n ist bei ihrem wirklichen Vorgang unterstellt. Andrerseits verdunkeln die\n Zerspaltung des Mehrwerths und die vermittelnde Bewegung der Circulation\n die einfache Grundform des Accumulationsprozesses.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"17.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0007","text":"Seine reine\n Analyse erheischt daher vorläufiges Wegsehn von allen Phänomenen,\n welche das innere Spiel seines Mechanismus verstecken. Der Fortgang\n der Darstellung führt später durch seine eigne Dialektik zu jenen konkre-\n teren Formen.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"17.7"}],"sha256":"9c4d87a44d5d3e7fda92ab56b63b87bac05403a2abc712c80976e078c1966928","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T205241182564Z_9202aeba","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Sechstes Kapitel. Der Accumulationsprozess des Kapitals.","source_sha256":"9c4d87a44d5d3e7fda92ab56b63b87bac05403a2abc712c80976e078c1966928","sha256":"31e00817a5c6c019da56b509466b4989b43367a5abea5d1a3ca08c710d8404b1","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:52:41.182610+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0001","text":"## Chapter Six.\n The Process of Accumulation of Capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"17.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0002","text":"We have seen how capital produces surplus-\n value in the form of commodities. Only through the sale of the commodity is the\n surplus-value contained in it realised, together with the capital-value ad-\n vanced in its production. The process of accumulation\n of capital therefore presupposes its process of circulation. The\n consideration of the latter, however, is reserved for the following book.\n The real conditions of reproduction, i.e. of continuous\n production, appear partly only within circulation, and partly can be treated\n only after the analysis of the process of circulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"17.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0003","text":"However, that is not all. The capitalist who pro-\n duces surplus-value, i.e. directly pumps unpaid labour out of the workers\n and fixes it in commodities, is indeed the first appropriator, but by no means the\n last owner of this surplus-value. He subsequently has to share it\n with capitalists who perform other functions in the great whole of social\n production, with the landowner, etc.\n Surplus-value therefore splits into various parts. Its fragments\n fall to various categories of persons and crystallise into\n various forms independent of one another, such as profit, interest,\n commercial profit, ground-rent, etc. These transformed forms\n of surplus-value can be treated only in the third book.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"17.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0004","text":"Here, therefore, we presuppose, on the one hand, that the capitalist who produces\n the commodity sells it at its value, without lingering any further upon his return\n to the commodity market, either over the new forms that attach themselves\n to capital in the sphere of circulation, or over the concrete conditions of reproduction\n enveloped therein. On the other hand, the capitalist producer counts for us\n as the owner of the entire surplus-value or, if one will,\n as the representative of all his fellow participants in the\n booty. Thus we consider accumulation initially in the abstract, i.e.\n as a mere moment of the immediate process of production.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"17.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0005","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"552\", \"facs\": \"#f0571\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"17.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0006","text":"Moreover, insofar as accumulation takes place, the capitalist succeeds in\n selling the produced commodity and reconverting the money released from it\n into capital. Further: The breaking up of surplus-value into various\n pieces changes nothing either in its nature or in the necessary\n conditions under which it becomes an element of accumulation. Whatever\n proportion of surplus-value the capitalist producer may retain\n for himself or cede to others, he always appropriates it first-hand.\n What is therefore presupposed in our presentation of accumulation\n is presupposed in its actual course. On the other hand, the\n splitting up of surplus-value and the mediating movement of circulation\n obscure the simple fundamental form of the process of accumulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"17.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0007","text":"Its pure\n analysis therefore requires, for the time being, looking away from all phenomena\n that conceal the inner play of its mechanism. The further course\n of the exposition leads later, through its own dialectic, to those more concre-\n te forms.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"17.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_SKDA:P0007"]}],"source_digest":"02d1f38d1d7b825acc1e94c6fbe31c368feacf2c9c583045f8e25456c237f9c5","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR","title":"a) Einfache Reproduktion.","order":18,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_EINR","text":"## 1) Die kapitalistische Accumulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## a) Einfache Reproduktion.\n\n\n\n\nWelches immer die gesellschaftliche Form des Produktionsprozesses,\n er muss kontinuirlich sein oder periodisch stets von neuem dieselben Sta-\n dien durchlaufen. So wenig eine Gesellschaft aufhören kann zu konsu-\n miren, so wenig kann sie aufhören zu produciren. In seinem stetigen Zu-\n sammenhang und dem beständigen Fluss seiner Erneuerung betrachtet, ist\n jeder gesellschaftliche Produktionsprozess daher zugleich Reproduk-\n tionsprozess.\n\n\n\n\nDie Bedingungen der Produktion sind zugleich die Bedingungen der Re-\n produktion. Keine Gesellschaft kann fortwährend produciren, d. h. repro-\n duciren, ohne fortwährend einen Theil ihrer Produkte in Produktionsmittel\n oder Elemente der Neuproduktion rückzuverwandeln. Unter sonst gleich-\n bleib nden Umständen kann sie ihren Reichthum nur auf derselben Stufen-\n leiter reproduciren oder erhalten, indem sie die während des Jahrs z. B.\n verbrauchten Produktionsmittel, d. h. Arbeitsmittel, Rohmateriale und\n Hilfsstoffe, in natura durch ein gleiches Quantum neuer Exemplare ersetzt,\n welches von der jährlichen Produktenmasse abgeschieden und von neuem\n dem Produktionsprozess anheimgegeben wird. Ein bestimmtes Quantum\n des jährlichen Produkts gehört also der Produktion. Von Haus aus für\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"553\", \"facs\": \"#f0572\"} --> die produktive Konsumtion bestimmt, existirt es grossentheils in Natural-\n formen, die von selbst die individuelle Konsumtion ausschliessen.\n\n\n\n\nHat die Produktion kapitalistische Form, so die Reproduktion. Wie\n in der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise der Arbeitsprozess nur als ein\n Mittel für den Verwerthungsprozess erscheint, so die Reproduktion nur als\n ein Mittel den vorgeschossnen Werth als Kapital zu reproduciren, d. h.\n als sich erhaltenden und verwerthenden Werth. Die ökonomische Charak-\n termaske des Kapitalisten hängt nur dadurch an einem Menschen fest,\n dass sein Geld fortwährend als Kapital funktionirt. Hat z. B. die\n vorgeschossne Geldsumme von 100 Pfd. St. sich dieses Jahr in Kapital\n verwandelt und einen Mehrwerth von 20 Pfd. St. producirt, so muss sie\n das nächste Jahr u. s. f. dieselbe Operation wiederholen. Als periodi-\n sches Increment des Kapitalwerths, oder periodische Frucht\n des prozessirenden Kapitals, erhält der Mehrwerth die Form einer aus\n dem Kapital entspringenden Revenue [Note: „Die Reichen, welche die Produkte der Arbeit Andrer verzehren, erhalten\n sie nur durch Austauschakte (Waarenkäufe). Sie sind daher einer beständigen\n Erschöpfung ihrer Fonds ausgesetzt … Aber in der gesellschaftlichen Ordnung\n hat der Reichthum die Kraft erhalten, sich durch fremde Arbeit zu repro-\n duciren . . . . Der Reichthum, wie die Arbeit, und durch die Arbeit,\n liefert eine jährliche Frucht, welche jedes Jahr vernichtet werden kann, ohne dass\n der Reiche ärmer wird. Die Frucht ist die Revenue, die aus dem Kapital ent-\n springt.“ (Sismondi: „Nouv. Princ. d’Écon. Pol.“ t. I, p. 82.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDient diese Revenue dem Kapitalisten nur als Konsumtionsfonds oder\n wird sie ebenso periodisch von ihm verzehrt wie gewonnen, so findet, unter\n sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen, einfache Reproduktion statt.\n Obgleich letztere nun blosse Wiederholung des Produktions-\n prozesses auf derselben Stufenleiter, drückt die blosse Wieder-\n holung oder Kontinuität dem Prozesse gewisse neue Charaktere auf oder\n löst vielmehr die Scheincharaktere seines nur vereinzelten Vorgangs auf.\n\n\n\n\nDer Produktionsprozess wird eingeleitet mit dem Kauf der Arbeits-\n kraft für eine bestimmte Zeit und diese Einleitung erneuert sich beständig,\n sobald der Verkaufstermin der Arbeit fällig und damit eine bestimmte Pro-\n duktionsperiode, Woche, Monat u. s. w. abgelaufen ist. Gezahlt wird\n der Arbeiter aber erst, nachdem seine Arbeitskraft gewirkt und sowohl\n ihren eignen Werth, als den Mehrwerth, in Waaren realisirt hat. Er hat\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"554\", \"facs\": \"#f0573\"} --> also wie den Mehrwerth, den wir einstweilen nur als Konsumtionsfonds\n des Kapitalisten betrachten, so den Fonds seiner eignen Zahlung, das va-\n riable Kapital, producirt, bevor es ihm in der Form des Arbeitslohns\n zurückfliesst, und er wird nur so lang beschäftigt als er ihn beständig\n reproducirt. Daher die im vorigen Kapitel erwähnte Formel der Oekono-\n men, die das Salair als Antheil am Produkt selbst darstellt [Note: „Wages as well as profits are to be considered each of them as really a\nportion of the finished product.“ (Ramsay l. c. p. 142.) „Der\n Antheil an dem Produkt, der dem Arbeiter unter der Form des Sa-\n lairs zukömmt.“ (J. Mill: „Elements etc. Uebers. von Parissot, Paris\n 1823“, p. 34.)] . Es ist ein\n Theil des vom Arbeiter selbst beständig reproducirten Produkts, das ihm\n in der Form des Arbeitslohns beständig zurückfliesst. Der Kapitalist\n zahlt ihm den Waarenwerth allerdings in Geld. Diess Geld ist aber nur\n die verwandelte Form des Arbeitsprodukts oder vielmehr eines Theils des\n Arbeitsprodukts. Während der Arbeiter einen Theil der Produktions-\n mittel in Produkt verwandelt, rückverwandelt sich ein Theil seines früheren\n Produkts in Geld. Es ist seine Arbeit von voriger Woche oder vom letz-\n ten halben Jahr, womit seine Arbeit von heute oder vom nächsten halben\n Jahr gezahlt wird. Die Illusion, welche die Geldform erzeugt, verschwin-\n det sofort, sobald statt des einzelnen Kapitalisten und des einzelnen Ar-\n beiters Kapitalistenklasse und Arbeiterklasse betrachtet werden. Die\n Kapitalistenklasse giebt der Arbeiterklasse beständig in Geldform Anwei-\n sungen auf einen Theil des von der letztern producirten und von der erstern\n angeeigneten Produkts. Diese Anweisungen giebt der Arbeiter der Kapi-\n talistenklasse ebenso beständig zurück und entzieht ihm damit den ihm\n selbst zufallenden Theil seines eignen Produkts. Die Waarenform des\n Produkts und die Geldform der Waare verkleiden die Transaktion.\n\n\n\n\nDas variable Kapital ist also nur eine besondre histo-\n rische Erscheinungsform des Fonds von Lebensmitteln oder des\nArbeitsfonds, den der Arbeiter zu seiner Selbsterhaltung und Repro-\n duktion bedarf, und den er in allen Systemen der gesellschaftlichen\n Produktion stets selbst produciren und reproduciren muss. Der Arbeits-\n fonds fliesst ihm nur beständig in Form von Zahlungsmitteln sei-\n ner Arbeit zu, weil sein eignes Produkt sich beständig in der Form des\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"555\", \"facs\": \"#f0574\"} --> Kapitals von ihm entfernt. Aber diese Erscheinungsform des\n Arbeitsfonds ändert nichts daran, dass dem Arbeiter seine eigne\n vergegenständlichte Arbeit vom Kapitalisten vorgeschossen\n wird [Note: „When capital is employed in advancing to the workmen his wages, it\n adds nothing to the funds for the maintenance of labour.“ (Cazenove in\n Note zu seiner ed. von Malthus’s: „Definitions in Polit. Econ. Lon-\n don 1853“, p. 22.)] . Nehmen wir einen Frohnbauer. Er arbeitet mit seinen eignen\n Produktionsmitteln auf seinem eignen Acker z. B. 3 Tage in der Woche.\n Die 3 andern Wochentage verrichtet er Frohnarbeit auf dem herrschaft-\n lichen Gut. Er reproducirt seinen eignen Arbeitsfonds beständig und die-\n ser nimmt ihm gegenüber nie die Form von einem Dritten für\n seine Arbeit vorgeschossener Zahlungsmittel an. Im\n Ersatz nimmt auch niemals seine unbezahlte Zwangsarbeit die\nForm freiwilliger und bezahlter Arbeit an. Wenn morgen\n der Gutsherr den Acker, das Zugvieh, die Samen, kurz die Produktions-\n mittel des Frohnbauern sich selbst aneignet, so hat dieser von nun an seine\n Arbeitskraft an den Frohnherrn zu verkaufen. Unter sonst gleichbleiben-\n den Umständen wird er nach wie vor 6 Tage in der Woche arbeiten,\n 3 Tage für sich selbst, 3 für den Ex-Frohnherrn, der jetzt in einen Lohn-\n herrn verwandelt ist. Er wird nach wie vor die Produktionsmittel als\n Produktionsmittel vernützen und ihren Werth auf das Produkt übertragen.\n Nach wie vor wird ein bestimmter Theil des Produkts in die Reproduk-\n tion eingehn. Wie aber die Frohnarbeit die Form der Lohn-\n arbeit, nimmt der vom Frohnbauer nach wie vor producirte und repro-\n ducirte Arbeitsfonds die Form eines ihm vom Ex-Frohn-\n herrn vorgeschossenen Kapitals an. Der bürgerliche Oeko-\n nom, dessen beschränktes Hirn die Erscheinungsform von dem was darin\n erscheint nicht trennen kann, schliesst die Augen vor der Thatsache, dass\n selbst noch heutzutag der Arbeitsfonds nur ausnahmsweis auf dem Erdrund\nin der Form von Kapital erscheint [Note: „Die Subsistenzmittel der Arbeiter werden noch nicht auf einem Viertel der\n Erde den Arbeitern durch Kapitalisten vorgeschossen.“ (Richard Jones:\n „Textbook of Lectures on the Polit. Economy of Nations.“\n Hertford 1852, p. 16.)] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"556\", \"facs\": \"#f0575\"} -->\n\nAllerdings verliert das variable Kapital nur den Sinn eines aus\n dem eignen Fonds des Kapitalisten vorgeschossenen Werthes, sobald wir den\n kapitalistischen Produktionsprozess im beständigen Fluss seiner Erneue-\n rung betrachten. Aber er muss doch irgendwo und irgendwann anfangen.\n Von unsrem bisherigen Standpunkt ist es daher wahrscheinlich, dass der\n Kapitalist irgend einmal durch irgend eine, von unbezahlter fremder Arbeit\n unabhängige, ursprüngliche Accumulation Geldbesitzer ward, und\n daher den Markt als Käufer von Arbeitskraft beschreiten konnte. Indess\n bewirkt die blosse Kontinuität des kapitalistischen Produktionsprozesses,\n oder die einfache Reproduktion, noch andre sonderbare Wechsel, die nicht\n nur den variablen Kapitaltheil, sondern das Gesammtkapital er-\n greifen.\n\n\n\n\nBeträgt der mit einem Kapital von 1000 Pfd. St. periodisch, z. B.\n jährlich, erzeugte Mehrwerth 200 Pfd. St. und wird dieser Mehrwerth\n jährlich verzehrt, so ist klar, dass nach fünfjähriger Wiederholung dessel-\n ben Prozesses die Summe des verzehrten Mehrwerths = 5 × 200 ist\n oder gleich dem ursprünglich vorgeschossenen Kapital-\n werth von 1000 Pfd. St. Würde der jährliche Mehrwerth nur theil-\n weis verzehrt, z. B. nur zur Hälfte, so ergäbe sich dasselbe Resultat nach\n zehnjähriger Wiederholung des Produktionsprozesses, denn 10 × 100 =\n 1000. Allgemein: Der vorgeschossene Kapitalwerth, divi-\n dirt durch den jährlich verzehrten Mehrwerth, ergiebt\n die Jahresanzahl, oder die Anzahl von Reproduktionsperioden,\n nach deren Ablauf der ursprünglich vorgeschossene Kapi-\n talwerth vom Kapitalisten aufgezehrt und daher verschwunden\n ist. Die Vorstellung des Kapitalisten, dass er das Produkt der fremden\n unbezahlten Arbeit, den Mehrwerth, verzehrt und den ursprünglichen\n Kapitalwerth erhält, ändert absolut nichts an der Thatsache, dass nach\n Abfluss einer gewissen Jahreszahl der von ihm geeignete Kapitalwerth\n gleich der Summe des während derselben Jahreszahl ohne Aequivalent an-\n geeigneten Mehrwerths, und die von ihm verzehrte Werthsumme gleich\n dem ursprünglichen Kapitalwerth ist. Kein Werthatom seines\n alten Kapitals existirt fort. Ganz abgesehn von aller Accu-\n mulation verwandelt also die blosse Kontinuität des Produktionspro-\n zesses, oder die einfache Reproduktion, nach kürzerer oder längerer Pe-\n riode, jedes Kapital nothwendig in accumulirtes Kapital oder\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"557\", \"facs\": \"#f0576\"} -->kapitalisirten Mehrwerth. War es selbst bei seinem Eintritt in\n den Produktionsprozess persönlich erarbeitetes Eigenthum seines Anwen-\n ders, früher oder später wird es ohne Aequivalent angeeigneter\n Werth oder Materiatur, ob in Geldform oder anders, unbezahlter\n fremder Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nDie ursprüngliche Voraussetzung für die Verwandlung von Geld in\n Kapital waren nicht nur Waarenproduktion und Waarencirkulation. Auf\n dem Waarenmarkt mussten Besitzer von Werth oder Geld und Besitzer der\n werthschaffenden Substanz, Besitzer von Produktions- und Lebensmitteln\n und Besitzer der Arbeitskraft, einander als Käufer und Verkäufer gegen-\n übertreten. Scheidung zwischen dem Arbeitsprodukt und der Arbeit\n selbst, zwischen den objektiven Arbeitsbedingungen und der subjektiven\n Arbeitskraft, war also die thatsächlich gegebne Grundlage des\n kapitalistischen Produktionsprozesses. Seine blosse Kontinui-\n tät, oder die einfache Reproduktion, reproducirt und verewigt diesen\n seinen Ausgangspunkt als sein eignes Resultat. Der Produk-\n tionsprozess verwandelt fortwährend das Geld in Kapital, die Produktions-\n mittel in Verwerthungsmittel. Andrerseits kommt der Arbeiter beständig\n aus dem Prozess heraus, wie er in ihn eintritt. Da seine eigne Arbeit\n ihm selbst entfremdet, dem Kapitalisten angeeignet und dem Kapital\n einverleibt ist, bevor er in den Prozess eintritt, vergegenständlicht\n sie sich während des Prozesses beständig in fremdem Produkt. Da\n der Produktionsprozess zugleich der Konsumtionsprozess der Arbeitskraft\n durch den Kapitalisten, verwandelt sich das Produkt des Arbeiters\n nicht nur fortwährend in Waare, sondern in Kapital, Werth, der die\n werthschöpfende Kraft aussaugt, Lebensmittel, die Personen kaufen, Pro-\n duktionsmittel, die den Producenten anwenden [Note: „Das ist eine besonders merkwürdige Eigenschaft der produktiven Kon-\n sumtion. Was produktiv konsumirt wird ist Kapital und es wird Kapital\n durch die Konsumtion.“ (James Mill l. c. p. 242.) J. Mill ist jedoch dieser\n „besonders merkwürdigen Eigenschaft“ nicht auf die Spur gekommen.] . Der Arbeiter selbst\n producirt daher beständig den objektiven Reichthum als Kapital, ihm\n fremde, ihn beherrschende und ausbeutende Macht, und der Kapitalist produ-\n cirt ebenso beständig die Arbeitskraft als subjektive, von ihren eignen\n Vergegenständlichungs- und Verwirklichungsmitteln getrennte, abstrakte,\n in der blossen Leiblichkeit des Arbeiters existirende Reichthumsquelle,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"558\", \"facs\": \"#f0577\"} --> kurz den Arbeiter als Lohnarbeiter [Note: „It is true indeed that the first introducing a manufacture emploies\n many poor, but they cease not to be so, and the continuance of it\nmakes many.“ („Reasons for a limited Exportation of Wool.\n Lond. 1677“, p. 19.) „The farmer now absurdly asserts, that he keeps the\n poor. They are indeed kept in misery.“ („Reasons for the late\n Increase of the Poor Rates: oracomparative view of the prices\n of labour and provisions. Lond. 1777“, p. 37.)] . Diese beständige Re-\n produktion oder Verewigung des Arbeiters als Lohnar-\n beiter ist das sine qua der kapitalistischen Produktion.\n\n\n\n\nMan weiss, die Transaktion zwischen Kapitalist und Arbeiter ist fol-\n gende: Einen Theil seines Kapitals, das variable Kapital, tauscht der Ka-\n pitalist aus gegen Arbeitskraft, die er als lebendige Verwerthungskraft\n seinen todten Produktionsmitteln einverleibt. Eben dadurch\n wird der Arbeitsprozess zugleich kapitalistischer Verwerthungsprozess.\n Andrerseits verausgabt der Arbeiter das für seine Arbeitskraft einge-\n tauschte Geld in Lebensmitteln, durch die er sich erhält und re-\n producirt. Es ist diess seine individuelle Konsumtion. Der\n Arbeitsprozess, worin er die Produktionsmittel konsumirt und dadurch in\n Produkte verwandelt, bildet seine produktive Konsumtion und zu-\n gleich Konsumtion seiner Arbeitskraft durch den Kapitalisten.\n Beide Konsumtionen sind wesentlich verschieden. In der einen gehört\n der Arbeiter als Arbeitskraft dem Kapital an und ist dem Produktionspro-\n zess einverleibt; in der andern gehört er sich selbst und verrichtet indivi-\n duelle Lebensakte ausserhalb des Produktionsprozesses.\n\n\n\n\nBei Betrachtung des „Arbeitstags“ u. s. w. zeigte sich gelegentlich,\n dass der Arbeiter oft gezwungen ist, seine individuelle Konsumtion zu\n einem blossen Incident des Produktionsprozesses zu machen. In diesem\n Fall setzt er sich Lebensmittel zu, um seine Arbeitskraft im Gang zu hal-\n ten, wie der Dampfmaschine Kohle und Wasser, dem Rad Oel zugesetzt\n wird. Seine Konsumtionsmittel sind dann bloss Konsumtionsmittel eines Pro-\n duktionsmittels, seine individuelle Konsumtion direkt produktive Konsum-\n tion. Diess erscheint jedoch als ein dem kapitalistischen Produktions-\n prozess unwesentlicher Missbrauch [Note: Rossi würde nicht so emphatisch diesen Punkt verdeklamiren, wäre er\n wirklich in das Geheimniss der „productive consumption“ eingedrungen.] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"559\", \"facs\": \"#f0578\"} -->\n\nBetrachtet man aber nicht den vereinzelten Produktionsprozess der\n Waare, sondern den kapitalistischen Produktionsprozess in seinem zu-\n sammenhängenden Fluss und in seinem gesellschaftlichen Umfang, so\n bleibt auch die individuelle Konsumtion des Arbeiters ein\n Moment der Produktion und Reproduktion des Kapitals, ob sie innerhalb\n oder ausserhalb der Werkstatt, Fabrik u. s. w., innerhalb oder ausserhalb\n des Arbeitsprozesses vorgeht, ganz wie die Reinigung der Maschine, ob sie\n während des Arbeitsprozesses oder bestimmter Pausen desselben geschieht.\n Es thut nichts zur Sache, dass der Arbeiter diese Konsumtion sich selbst\n und nicht dem Kapitalisten zu lieb vollzieht. So bleibt der Konsum des\n Lastviehs nicht minder ein nothwendiges Moment des Produktionsprozesses,\n weil das Vieh selbst geniesst, was es frisst. Die beständige Erhaltung\n und Reproduktion der Arbeiterklasse bleibt beständige Bedingung für die\n Reproduktion des Kapitals. Der Kapitalist kann ihre Erfüllung getrost\n dem Selbsterhaltungs- und Fortpflanzungstrieb der Arbeiter überlassen.\n Er sorgt nur dafür ihre individuelle Konsumtion so viel als möglich auf\n das Nothwendige einzuschränken und ist himmelweit entfernt von jener\n südamerikanischen Rohheit, die den Arbeiter zwingt substantiellere statt\n weniger substantieller Nahrungsmittel einzunehmen [Note: „Die Arbeiter in den Bergwerken Südamerika’s, deren tägliches Geschäft\n (das schwerste vielleicht in der Welt) darin besteht, eine Last Erz, im Gewicht\n von 180—200 Pfund, aus einer Tiefe von 450 Fuss, auf ihren Schultern zu Tage\n zu fördern, leben nur von Brod und Bohnen, sie würden das Brod allein zur Nah-\n rung vorziehn, allein ihre Herrn, welche gefunden haben, dass sie mit Brod\n nicht so stark arbeiten können, behandeln sie wie Pferde, und zwingen\n sie die Bohnen zu essen; die Bohnen sind aber verhältnissmässig an Knochenerde\n weit reicher als das Brod.“ (Liebig l. c. 1. Theil, p. 194, Note.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDurch den Umsatz eines Kapitaltheils in Arbeitskraft schlägt der\n Kapitalist zwei Fliegen mit einer Klappe. Er verwandelt dadurch einen\n Theil seines Kapitals in variables Kapital und verwerthet so sein Gesammt-\n kapital. Er einverleibt die Arbeitskraft seinen Produktionsmitteln. Er\n verzehrt die Arbeitskraft produktiv, indem er den Arbeiter die Produktions-\n mittel durch seine Arbeit produktiv verzehren lässt. Andrerseits verwandeln\n sich die Lebensmittel oder der an den Arbeiter veräusserte Theil des Kapitals\n in Muskel, Nerven, Knochen, Hirn u.s.w. von Arbeitern. Innerhalb ih-\n rer nothwendigen Grenzen ist daher die individuelle Konsumtion der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"560\", \"facs\": \"#f0579\"} --> Arbeiterklasse Rückverwandlung der vom Kapital gegen Arbeitskraft ver-\n äusserten Lebensmittel in die vom Kapital neu exploitirbare Arbeitskraft,\nProduktion und Reproduktion seines nothwendigsten\n Produktionsmittels, des Arbeiters selbst. Die indivi-\n duelle Konsumtion des Arbeiters bildet daher ein Moment des Repro-\n duktionsprozesses des Kapitals im Grossen und Ganzen.\n\n\n\n\nDaher betrachtet auch der Kapitalist und sein Ideolog, der politische\n Oekonom, nur den Theil der individuellen Konsumtion des Arbeiters als\nproduktiv, der zur Verewigung der Arbeiterklasse erheischt ist, also\n in der That verzehrt werden muss, damit das Kapital die Arbeitskraft ver-\n zehre; was der Arbeiter ausserdem zu seinem Vergnügen verzehren mag,\n ist unproduktive Konsumtion [Note: James Mill l. c. p. 238 sqq.] . Würde die Accumulation des\n Kapitals eine Erhöhung des Arbeitslohns und daher Vermehrung der Kon-\n sumtionsmittel des Arbeiters verursachen ohne Konsum von mehr Arbeits-\n kraft durch das Kapital, so wäre das zuschüssige Kapital unproduktiv\n konsumirt [Note: „Stiege der Preis der Arbeit so hoch, dass trotz des Zuwachses von Kapi-\n tal nicht mehr Arbeit angewandt werden könnte, so würde ich sagen, dass\n solcher Zuwachs von Kapital unproduktiv konsumirt wird.“ (Ricardo l. c.\n p. 163.)] . In der That: die individuelle Konsumtion des Arbei-\n ters ist für ihn selbst unproduktiv, denn sie reproducirt nur das\nbedürftige Individuum: sie ist produktiv für den Kapita-\n listen und den Staat, denn sie ist Produktion der den fremden\n Reichthum producirenden Kraft [Note: „Die einzig produktive Konsumtion im eigentlichen Sinn ist die Kon-\n sumtion oder Zerstörung von Reichthum (er meint den Verbrauch der Produktions-\n mittel) durch Kapitalisten zum Zwecke der Reproduktion … Der Arbeiter ist ein\n produktiver Konsument für die Person, die ihn anwendet, und für den Staat, aber,\n genau gesprochen, nicht für sich selbst.“ (Malthus: „Definitions etc.“,\n p. 30.)] .\n\n\n\n\nVon gesellschaftlichem Standpunkt ist also die Arbeiterklasse, auch\n ausserhalb des unmittelbaren Arbeitsprozesses, ebenso sehr Zubehör des\n Kapitals als die todten Arbeitsinstrumente. Ihre individuelle Konsumtion\n selbst ist innerhalb gewisser Grenzen nur ein Moment des Reproduktions-\n prozesses des Kapitals. Der Prozess aber sorgt dafür, dass diese selbst-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"561\", \"facs\": \"#f0580\"} --> bewussten Produktionsinstrumente nicht weglaufen, indem er beständig ihr\n Produkt von ihrem Pol zum Gegenpol des Kapitals entfernt. Die indi-\n viduelle Konsumtion sorgt für ihre eigne Erhaltung und Reproduktion, an-\n drerseits, durch Vernichtung der Lebensmittel, für ihr beständiges Wieder-\n erscheinen auf dem Arbeitsmarkt. Der römische Sklave war durch Ketten,\n der Lohnarbeiter ist durch unsichtbare Fäden an seinen Eigenthümer ge-\n bunden. Der Schein seiner Unabhängigkeit wird durch den bestän-\n digen Wechsel der individuellen Lohnherrn und die fictio juris des Kon-\n trakts aufrecht erhalten.\n\n\n\n\nFrüher machte das Kapital, wo es ihm nöthig schien, sein Eigen-\n thumsrecht auf den freien Arbeiter durch Zwangsgesetz gel-\n tend. So war z. B. die Emigration der Maschinenarbeiter in England bis\n 1815 bei schwerer Strafe verboten.\n\n\n\n\nDie Reproduktion der Arbeiterklasse schliesst zugleich die Ueberliefe-\n rung und Häufung des Geschicks von einer Generation zur andern ein [Note: „Das einzige Ding, wovon man sagen kann, dass es aufgespeichert und\n vorher präparirt ist, ist das Geschick des Arbeiters … Die Accumulation und\n Aufspeicherung geschickter Arbeit, diese wichtigste Operation wird, was die grosse\n Masse der Arbeiter betrifft, ohne irgend welches Kapital vollbracht.“ (Hodgskin:\n „Labour Defended etc.“, p. 13.)] . Wie\n sehr der Kapitalist das Dasein einer solchen geschickten Arbeiterklasse unter\ndie ihm angehörigen Produktionsbedingungen zählt,\n sie in der That als die reale Existenz seines variablen Kapitals ansieht,\n zeigt sich, sobald eine Krise deren Verlust androht. In Folge des ameri-\n kanischen Bürgerkriegs und der ihn begleitenden Baumwollnoth wurde be-\n kanntlich die Mehrzahl der Baumwollarbeiter in Lancashire u. s. w aufs\n Pflaster geworfen. Aus dem Schoss der Arbeiterklasse selbst, wie andrer\n Gesellschaftsschichten, schrie man nach Staatsunterstützung oder frei-\n williger Nationalkollekte, um die Emigration der „Ueberflüssigen“ in eng-\n lische Kolonien oder die Vereinigten Staaten zu ermöglichen. Damals\n veröffentlichte die Times (24. März 1863) einen Brief von Edmund\n Potter, früher Präsident der Manchester Handelskammer. Sein Brief\n ward mit Recht im Unterhaus als „das Manifest der Fabrikan-\n ten“ bezeichnet [Note: „That letter might be looked upon as the manifesto of the manufacturers.“\n (Ferrand: Motion über den cotton famine, Sitzung des H. o. C. vom 27. April\n 1863.)] . Wir geben hier einige charakteristische Stellen,\n [Running matter: I. 36] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"562\", \"facs\": \"#f0581\"} --> worin der Eigenthumstitel des Kapitals auf die Arbeits-\n kraft unverblümt ausgesprochen wird.\n\n\n\n\n„Den Baumwollarbeitern mag gesagt werden, dass ihre Zufuhr zu\n gross ist. . . . sie müsse vielleicht um ein Drittheil reducirt werden, und\n dann würde eine gesunde Nachfrage für die übrigen zwei Drittheile ein-\n treten. . . . Die öffentliche Meinung dringt auf Emigration. . . . Der\n Meister (d. h. der Baumwollfabrikant) kann nicht willig seine Arbeitszu-\n fuhr entfernt sehn; er mag denken, dass das ebenso ungerecht als unrich-\n tig ist.… Wenn die Emigration aus öffentlichen Fonds unterstützt wird, hat er\n ein Recht Gehör zu verlangen und vielleicht zu protestiren.“ Selbiger Pot-\n ter setzt dann weiter aus einander, wie nützlich die Baumwollindustrie, wie\n „sie unzweifelhaft die Uebervölkerung aus Irland und den englischen Agri-\n kulturdistrikten wegdrainirt hat“, wie ungeheuer ihr Umfang, wie sie im\n Jahr 1860 \\frac{5}{13} des ganzen englischen Exporthandels lieferte, wie sie nach\n wenigen Jahren sich wieder ausdehnen werde durch Erweiterung des\n Markts, besonders Indiens, und durch Erzwingung hinreichender „Baum-\n wollzufuhr, zu 6 d. das Pfund“. Er fährt dann fort: „Zeit — eins, zwei,\n drei Jahre vielleicht — wird die nöthige Quantität produciren . . . . Ich\n möchte dann die Frage stellen, ist diese Industrie werth sie festzuhalten, ist\n es der Mühe werth die Maschinerie (nämlich die lebendigen\n Arbeitsmaschinen) in Ordnung zu halten, und ist es nicht die\n grösste Narrheit, daran zu denken, sie aufzugeben! Ich\n glaube so. Ich will zugeben, dass die Arbeiter nicht Eigen-\n thum sind („I allow that the workers are not a property“), nicht das\n Eigenthum Lancashire’s und der Meister; aber sie sind die\n Stärke beider; sie sind die geistige und geschulte Kraft, die in einer Ge-\n neration nicht ersetzt werden kann; die andere Maschinerie dagegen,\n woran sie arbeiten („the mere machinery which they work“), könnte zum\n grossen Theil mit Vortheil ersetzt und verbessert werden in\n zwölf Monaten [Note: Man erinnert sich, dass dasselbe Kapital aus einem andern Loch pfeift\n unter gewöhnlichen Umständen, wenn es gilt, den Arbeitslohn herabzusetzen.\n Dann erklären „die Meister“ aus einem Munde (sieh Viertes Kapitel, Note\n 188): „Fabrikarbeiter sollten in heilsamer Erinnerung halten, dass ihre Arbeit\n in der That eine sehr niedrige Sorte geschickter Arbeit ist; dass keine leichter\n aneigenbar und in Anbetracht ihrer Qualität besser belohnt ist, dass keine durch] . Ermuntert oder erlaubt (!) die Emi-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"563\", \"facs\": \"#f0582\"} --> gration der Arbeitskraft und was wird aus dem Kapitalisten?\n („Encourage or allow the working power to emigrate, and what of the\n capitalist?“ Dieser Herzensstoss erinnert an Hofmarschall Kalb.) … Nimm\n den Rahm der Arbeiter weg, und das fixe Kapital wird in hohem Grad\n entwerthet und das cirkulirende Kapital wird sich nicht dem Kampf mit\n schmaler Zufuhr einer niedrigeren Sorte von Arbeit aussetzen. . . . Man\n sagt uns, die Arbeiter selbst wünschen die Emigration. Es\n ist sehr natürlich, dass sie das thun. . . . Reducire, komprimire das\n Baumwollgeschäft durch Wegnahme seiner Arbeitskraft (by\n taking away its working power), durch Verminderung ihrer Lohnveraus-\n gabung sage um ⅓ oder 5 Millionen, und was wird dann aus der nächsten\n Klasse über ihnen, den Kleinkrämern? Was aus den Grundrenten, was\n aus der Miethe der cottages? … was aus dem kleinen Pächter, dem\n besseren Hausbesitzer und dem Grundeigenthümer? Und sagt nun, ob\n irgend ein Plan für alle Klassen des Landes selbstmörderischer sein kann\n als dieser, dieN ation zu schwächen durch den Export ihrer besten\n Fabrikarbeiter und die Entwerthung eines Theils ihres produktiv-\n sten Kapitals und Reichthums?“ „Ich rathe zu einer Anleihe von 5 bis\n 6 Millionen, über 2 oder 3 Jahre vertheilt, administrirt durch Spezial-\n kommissäre, beigeordnet den Armenverwaltungen in den Baumwolldistrik-\n ten, unter speziellen gesetzlichen Regulationen, mit gewisser Zwangs-\n arbeit, um die moralische Valuta der Almosenempfänger aufrecht zu\n erhalten. . . . Kann es irgend etwas Schlimmeres geben für\n Grundeigenthümer oder Meister („can anything be worse for\n landowners or masters“) als ihre besten Arbeiter aufzugeben und die\nübrigbleibenden zu demoralisiren und zu verstimmen\n durch eine ausgedehnte entleerende Emigration und Entleerung von Werth\n und Kapital in einer ganzen Provinz?“\n\n\n\n\n [Note: kurze Unterweisung des mindest Erfahrenen in so kurzer Zeit und in solchem\n Ueberfluss zugeführt werden kann. Des Meisters Maschinerie (die, wie\n wir jetzt hören, in 12 Monaten mit Vortheil und verbessert ersetzt werden kann)\n spielt in der That eine viel wichtigere Rolle in dem Geschäft der Produktion\n als die Arbeit und das Geschick des Arbeiters (die jetzt in 30 Jahren\n nicht ersetzbar sind), die eine Erziehung von 6 Monaten lehren und jeder\n Bauernknecht lernen kann.“] \n\n\n [Running matter: 36*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"564\", \"facs\": \"#f0583\"} -->\n\nPotter, das auserwählte Organ der Baumwollfabrikanten, unterscheidet\n doppelte „Maschinerie“, deren jede dem Kapitalisten gehört, und\n wovon die eine in seiner Fabrik steht, die andre des Nachts und Sonntags\n auswärtig in cottages haust. Die eine ist todt, die andre lebendig. Die\n todte Maschinerie verschlechtert und entwerthet sich nicht nur jeden\n Tag, sondern von ihrer existirenden Masse ist ein grosser Theil durch den\n beständigen technologischen Fortschritt beständig so sehr antiquirt, dass sie\n vortheilhaft und in wenigen Monaten durch neuere Maschinerie ersetzbar.\n Die lebendige Maschinerie verbessert sich umgekehrt, je länger sie währt,\n je mehr sie das Geschick von Generationen in sich aufhäuft. Die Times\n antwortete dem Fabrikmagnaten u. a.:\n\n\n\n\n„Herr E. Potter ist so impressionirt mit der ausserordentlichen und\n absoluten Wichtigkeit der Baumwollmeister, dass er, um diese Klasse\n zu erhalten und ihr Metier zu verewigen, eine halbe Million der Arbeiter-\n klasse wider ihren Willen in ein grosses moralisches Workhouse ein-\n sperren will. Ist diese Industrie werth sie festzuhalten? fragt Herr Pot-\n ter. Sicher, durch alle ehrbaren Mittel! antworten wir. Ist es der Mühe\n werth die Maschinerie in Ordnung zu halten? fragt wieder Herr Potter.\n Hier stutzen wir. Unter der Maschinerie versteht Herr Potter die\nmenschliche Maschinerie, denn er betheuert, dass er sie nicht\n als absolutes Eigenthum zu behandeln vorhat. Wir müssen\n gestehn, wir halten es nicht ‚der Mühe werth‘ oder selbst für möglich, die\n menschliche Maschinerie in Ordnung zu halten, d. h. einzusperren und\n einzuölen bis ihrer bedurft wird. Menschliche Maschinerie hat die\n Eigenschaft während der Unthätigkeit zu verrosten, ihr mögt noch soviel\n dran ölen oder reiben. Zudem ist menschliche Maschinerie, wie der\n Augenschein uns eben lehrt, im Stand von eignen Stücken den Dampf an-\n zulassen und zu platzen oder einen Veitstanz in unsren grossen Städten zu\n tollen. Es mag, wie Herr Potter sagt, längere Zeit zur Repro-\n duktion der Arbeiter erheischt sein, aber mit Maschinisten\n und Geld zur Hand werden wir stets betriebsame, harte, industrielle Män-\n ner finden, um daraus mehr Fabrikmeister zu fabriciren als\n wir je verbrauchen können. . . . Herr Potter plaudert von einer\n Wiederbelebung der Industrie in 1, 2, 3 Jahren und verlangt von uns die\nEmigration der Arbeitskraft nicht zu ermuntern oder nicht zu\nerlauben! Er sagt, es sei natürlich, dass die Arbeiter zu emigriren wün-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"565\", \"facs\": \"#f0584\"} --> schen, aber er meint, dass die Nation diese halbe Million Arbeiter mit den\n 700,000, die an ihnen hängen, ihrem Verlangen zum Trotz in die\n Baumwolldistrikte einsperren und, eine nothwendige Konsequenz, ihr Miss-\n vergnügen durch Gewalt niederschlagen und sie selbst durch Almosen\n fristen muss, alles das auf die Chance hin, dass die Baumwollmeister\n ihrer an einem beliebigen Tag wieder bedürfen mögen. . . . Die Zeit ist\n gekommen, wo die grosse öffentliche Meinung dieser Eilande etwas thun\n muss, um ‚diese Arbeitskraft‘ vor denen zu retten, die sie behan-\n deln wollen, wie sie Kohle, Eisen und Baumwolle behandeln.“ („to save\n this ‘working power’ from those who would deal with it as they deal with\n iron, coal and cotton.“) [Note: Times, 24. March 1863.] \n\n\n\n\nDer Times-Artikel war nur ein jeu d’esprit. Die „grosse öffent-\n liche Meinung“ war in der That der Meinung des Herrn Potter, dass die\n Fabrikarbeiter Mobiliarzubehör der Fabriken. Ihre Emigration wurde\n verhindert [Note: Das Parlament votirte keinen Farthing für Emigration, sondern nur Ge-\n setze, welche die Municipalitäten befähigten, die Arbeiter zwischen Leben und\n Sterben zu halten oder sie zu exploitiren, ohne Zahlung von Normallöhnen. Als\n dagegen drei Jahre später die Rinderseuche ausbrach, durchbrach das Parlament\n wild sogar alle parlamentarische Etiquette und votirte im Umsehn Millionen zur\n Schadloshaltung der Millionäre von Landlords, deren Pächter sich ohnehin durch\n Steigerung der Fleischpreise schadlos hielten. Das bestiale Gebrüll der Grund-\n eigenthümer bei Eröffnung des Parlaments von 1866 bewies, dass man nicht Hindu\n zu sein braucht, um die Kuh Sabala anzubeten, noch Jupiter, um sich in einen\n Ochsen zu verwandeln.] . Man sperrte sie in das „moralische Workhouse“ der Baum-\n wolldistrikte, und sie bilden nach wie vor „die Stärke (the strength) der\n Baumwollmeister von Lancashire.“\n\n\n\n\nDer kapitalistische Produktionsprozess reproducirt also durch seinen\n eignen Vorgang die Scheidung zwischen Arbeitskraft und Arbeitsbe-\n dingungen. Er reproducirt und verewigt damit die Exploitationsbe-\n dingungen des Arbeiters. Er zwingt beständig den Arbeiter zum Verkauf\n seiner Arbeitskraft, um zu leben, und befähigt beständig den Kapitalisten\n zu ihrem Kauf, um sich zu bereichern [Note: „L’ouvrier demandait de la subsistance pour vivre, le chef demandait du\n travail pour gagner.“ (Sismondi l. c. p. 91.)] . Es ist nicht mehr der Zufall,\n welcher Kapitalist und Arbeiter als Käufer und Verkäufer auf dem Waaren-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"566\", \"facs\": \"#f0585\"} --> markt gegenüberstellt. Es ist die Zwickmühle des Prozesses selbst, die\n den Einen stets als Verkäufer seiner Arbeitskraft auf den Waarenmarkt\n zurückschleudert und sein eignes Produkt stets in das Kaufmittel des An-\n dern verwandelt. In der That gehört der Arbeiter dem Kapital, bevor er\n sich dem Kapitalisten verkauft. Seine ökonomische Hörigkeit [Note: Eine bäuerlich plumpe Form dieser Hörigkeit existirt in der Grafschaft\nDurham. Es ist diess eine der wenigen Grafschaften, worin die Verhältnisse\n dem Pächter nicht unbestrittenen Eigenthumstitel auf die Ackerbautaglöhner sichern.\n Die Bergwerksindustrie erlaubt letzteren eine Wahl. Der Pächter, im Gegensatz\n zur Regel, übernimmt hier daher nur Pacht von Ländereien, worauf sich cottages\n für die Arbeiter befinden. Der Miethpreis der cottage bildet Theil des Arbeits-\n lohns. Diese cottages heissen „hind’s houses“. Sie werden den Arbeitern unter\n gewissen Feudalverpflichtungen vermiethet, unter einem Vertrag, der „bondage“\n (Hörigkeit) heisst und den Arbeiter z. B. bindet, für die Zeit, während deren er\n anderswo beschäftigt ist, seine Tochter u. s. w. zu stellen. Der Arbeiter selbst\n heisst bondsman, Höriger. Diess Verhältniss zeigt auch die individuelle\n Konsumtion des Arbeiters als Konsumtion für das Kapital oder pro-\n duktive Konsumtion von einer ganz neuen Seite. „Es ist merkwürdig zu\n beobachten, wie selbst der Koth dieses bondsman zu den Sporteln an seinen kal-\n kulirenden Gebieter zählt . . . . Der Pächter erlaubt in der ganzen Nachbarschaft\n keinen Abtritt ausser seinem eignen und duldet in dieser Beziehung keinen Ab-\n schlag von seinen Suzerainrechten.“ („Public Health. VII. Rep. 1865“,\n p. 188.)]  ist zu-\n gleich vermittelt und zugleich versteckt durch die periodische Erneurung\n seines Selbstverkaufs, den Wechsel seiner individuellen Lohnherrn und die\n Oscillation im Marktpreise der Arbeit [Note: Man erinnert sich, dass bei der Arbeit der Kinder u. s. w. selbst die For-\n malität des Selbstverkaufs verschwindet.] .\n\n\n\n\nDer kapitalistische Produktionsprozess, im Zusammenhang betrachtet,\n oder als Reproduktionsprozess, producirt also nicht nur Waare, nicht nur\n Mehrwerth, er producirt und reproducirt das Kapitalverhältniss\n selbst, auf der einen Seite den Kapitalisten, auf der andern den\n Lohnarbeiter [Note: „Das Kapital setzt die Lohnarbeit, die Lohnarbeit setzt das Kapital vor-\n aus. Sie bedingen sich wechselseitig, sie bringen sich wechselseitig hervor. Ein\n Arbeiter in einer Baumwollfabrik, producirt er nur Baumwollstoffe? Nein, er\n producirt Kapital. Er producirt Werthe, die von neuem dazu dienen, seine\n Arbeit zu kommandiren, und vermittelst derselben neue Werthe zu schaffen.“\n (Karl Marx: „Lohnarbeit und Kapital“ in N. Rh. Z. Nr. 266,] .\n\n\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"567\", \"facs\": \"#f0586\"} -->","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0001","text":"## 1) Die kapitalistische Accumulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"18.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0002","text":"## a) Einfache Reproduktion.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"18.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0003","text":"Welches immer die gesellschaftliche Form des Produktionsprozesses,\n er muss kontinuirlich sein oder periodisch stets von neuem dieselben Sta-\n dien durchlaufen. So wenig eine Gesellschaft aufhören kann zu konsu-\n miren, so wenig kann sie aufhören zu produciren. In seinem stetigen Zu-\n sammenhang und dem beständigen Fluss seiner Erneuerung betrachtet, ist\n jeder gesellschaftliche Produktionsprozess daher zugleich Reproduk-\n tionsprozess.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"18.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0004","text":"Die Bedingungen der Produktion sind zugleich die Bedingungen der Re-\n produktion. Keine Gesellschaft kann fortwährend produciren, d. h. repro-\n duciren, ohne fortwährend einen Theil ihrer Produkte in Produktionsmittel\n oder Elemente der Neuproduktion rückzuverwandeln. Unter sonst gleich-\n bleib nden Umständen kann sie ihren Reichthum nur auf derselben Stufen-\n leiter reproduciren oder erhalten, indem sie die während des Jahrs z. B.\n verbrauchten Produktionsmittel, d. h. Arbeitsmittel, Rohmateriale und\n Hilfsstoffe, in natura durch ein gleiches Quantum neuer Exemplare ersetzt,\n welches von der jährlichen Produktenmasse abgeschieden und von neuem\n dem Produktionsprozess anheimgegeben wird. Ein bestimmtes Quantum\n des jährlichen Produkts gehört also der Produktion.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"18.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0005","text":"Von Haus aus für\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"553\", \"facs\": \"#f0572\"} --> die produktive Konsumtion bestimmt, existirt es grossentheils in Natural-\n formen, die von selbst die individuelle Konsumtion ausschliessen.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"18.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0006","text":"Hat die Produktion kapitalistische Form, so die Reproduktion. Wie\n in der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise der Arbeitsprozess nur als ein\n Mittel für den Verwerthungsprozess erscheint, so die Reproduktion nur als\n ein Mittel den vorgeschossnen Werth als Kapital zu reproduciren, d. h.\n als sich erhaltenden und verwerthenden Werth. Die ökonomische Charak-\n termaske des Kapitalisten hängt nur dadurch an einem Menschen fest,\n dass sein Geld fortwährend als Kapital funktionirt. Hat z. B. die\n vorgeschossne Geldsumme von 100 Pfd. St. sich dieses Jahr in Kapital\n verwandelt und einen Mehrwerth von 20 Pfd. St. producirt, so muss sie\n das nächste Jahr u. s. f. dieselbe Operation wiederholen.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"18.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0007","text":"Als periodi-\n sches Increment des Kapitalwerths, oder periodische Frucht\n des prozessirenden Kapitals, erhält der Mehrwerth die Form einer aus\n dem Kapital entspringenden Revenue [Note: „Die Reichen, welche die Produkte der Arbeit Andrer verzehren, erhalten\n sie nur durch Austauschakte (Waarenkäufe). Sie sind daher einer beständigen\n Erschöpfung ihrer Fonds ausgesetzt … Aber in der gesellschaftlichen Ordnung\n hat der Reichthum die Kraft erhalten, sich durch fremde Arbeit zu repro-\n duciren . . . . Der Reichthum, wie die Arbeit, und durch die Arbeit,\n liefert eine jährliche Frucht, welche jedes Jahr vernichtet werden kann, ohne dass\n der Reiche ärmer wird. Die Frucht ist die Revenue, die aus dem Kapital ent-\n springt.“ (Sismondi: „Nouv. Princ. d’Écon. Pol.“ t. I, p. 82.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"18.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0008","text":"Dient diese Revenue dem Kapitalisten nur als Konsumtionsfonds oder\n wird sie ebenso periodisch von ihm verzehrt wie gewonnen, so findet, unter\n sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen, einfache Reproduktion statt.\n Obgleich letztere nun blosse Wiederholung des Produktions-\n prozesses auf derselben Stufenleiter, drückt die blosse Wieder-\n holung oder Kontinuität dem Prozesse gewisse neue Charaktere auf oder\n löst vielmehr die Scheincharaktere seines nur vereinzelten Vorgangs auf.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"18.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0009","text":"Der Produktionsprozess wird eingeleitet mit dem Kauf der Arbeits-\n kraft für eine bestimmte Zeit und diese Einleitung erneuert sich beständig,\n sobald der Verkaufstermin der Arbeit fällig und damit eine bestimmte Pro-\n duktionsperiode, Woche, Monat u. s. w. abgelaufen ist. Gezahlt wird\n der Arbeiter aber erst, nachdem seine Arbeitskraft gewirkt und sowohl\n ihren eignen Werth, als den Mehrwerth, in Waaren realisirt hat. Er hat\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"554\", \"facs\": \"#f0573\"} --> also wie den Mehrwerth, den wir einstweilen nur als Konsumtionsfonds\n des Kapitalisten betrachten, so den Fonds seiner eignen Zahlung, das va-\n riable Kapital, producirt, bevor es ihm in der Form des Arbeitslohns\n zurückfliesst, und er wird nur so lang beschäftigt als er ihn beständig\n reproducirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"18.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0010","text":"Daher die im vorigen Kapitel erwähnte Formel der Oekono-\n men, die das Salair als Antheil am Produkt selbst darstellt [Note: „Wages as well as profits are to be considered each of them as really a\nportion of the finished product.“ (Ramsay l. c. p. 142.) „Der\n Antheil an dem Produkt, der dem Arbeiter unter der Form des Sa-\n lairs zukömmt.“ (J. Mill: „Elements etc. Uebers. von Parissot, Paris\n 1823“, p. 34.)] . Es ist ein\n Theil des vom Arbeiter selbst beständig reproducirten Produkts, das ihm\n in der Form des Arbeitslohns beständig zurückfliesst. Der Kapitalist\n zahlt ihm den Waarenwerth allerdings in Geld. Diess Geld ist aber nur\n die verwandelte Form des Arbeitsprodukts oder vielmehr eines Theils des\n Arbeitsprodukts. Während der Arbeiter einen Theil der Produktions-\n mittel in Produkt verwandelt, rückverwandelt sich ein Theil seines früheren\n Produkts in Geld.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"18.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0011","text":"Es ist seine Arbeit von voriger Woche oder vom letz-\n ten halben Jahr, womit seine Arbeit von heute oder vom nächsten halben\n Jahr gezahlt wird. Die Illusion, welche die Geldform erzeugt, verschwin-\n det sofort, sobald statt des einzelnen Kapitalisten und des einzelnen Ar-\n beiters Kapitalistenklasse und Arbeiterklasse betrachtet werden. Die\n Kapitalistenklasse giebt der Arbeiterklasse beständig in Geldform Anwei-\n sungen auf einen Theil des von der letztern producirten und von der erstern\n angeeigneten Produkts. Diese Anweisungen giebt der Arbeiter der Kapi-\n talistenklasse ebenso beständig zurück und entzieht ihm damit den ihm\n selbst zufallenden Theil seines eignen Produkts. Die Waarenform des\n Produkts und die Geldform der Waare verkleiden die Transaktion.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"18.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0012","text":"Das variable Kapital ist also nur eine besondre histo-\n rische Erscheinungsform des Fonds von Lebensmitteln oder des\nArbeitsfonds, den der Arbeiter zu seiner Selbsterhaltung und Repro-\n duktion bedarf, und den er in allen Systemen der gesellschaftlichen\n Produktion stets selbst produciren und reproduciren muss. Der Arbeits-\n fonds fliesst ihm nur beständig in Form von Zahlungsmitteln sei-\n ner Arbeit zu, weil sein eignes Produkt sich beständig in der Form des\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"555\", \"facs\": \"#f0574\"} --> Kapitals von ihm entfernt. Aber diese Erscheinungsform des\n Arbeitsfonds ändert nichts daran, dass dem Arbeiter seine eigne\n vergegenständlichte Arbeit vom Kapitalisten vorgeschossen\n wird [Note: „When capital is employed in advancing to the workmen his wages, it\n adds nothing to the funds for the maintenance of labour.“ (Cazenove in\n Note zu seiner ed.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"18.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0013","text":"von Malthus’s: „Definitions in Polit. Econ. Lon-\n don 1853“, p. 22.)] . Nehmen wir einen Frohnbauer. Er arbeitet mit seinen eignen\n Produktionsmitteln auf seinem eignen Acker z. B. 3 Tage in der Woche.\n Die 3 andern Wochentage verrichtet er Frohnarbeit auf dem herrschaft-\n lichen Gut. Er reproducirt seinen eignen Arbeitsfonds beständig und die-\n ser nimmt ihm gegenüber nie die Form von einem Dritten für\n seine Arbeit vorgeschossener Zahlungsmittel an. Im\n Ersatz nimmt auch niemals seine unbezahlte Zwangsarbeit die\nForm freiwilliger und bezahlter Arbeit an. Wenn morgen\n der Gutsherr den Acker, das Zugvieh, die Samen, kurz die Produktions-\n mittel des Frohnbauern sich selbst aneignet, so hat dieser von nun an seine\n Arbeitskraft an den Frohnherrn zu verkaufen.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"18.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0014","text":"Unter sonst gleichbleiben-\n den Umständen wird er nach wie vor 6 Tage in der Woche arbeiten,\n 3 Tage für sich selbst, 3 für den Ex-Frohnherrn, der jetzt in einen Lohn-\n herrn verwandelt ist. Er wird nach wie vor die Produktionsmittel als\n Produktionsmittel vernützen und ihren Werth auf das Produkt übertragen.\n Nach wie vor wird ein bestimmter Theil des Produkts in die Reproduk-\n tion eingehn. Wie aber die Frohnarbeit die Form der Lohn-\n arbeit, nimmt der vom Frohnbauer nach wie vor producirte und repro-\n ducirte Arbeitsfonds die Form eines ihm vom Ex-Frohn-\n herrn vorgeschossenen Kapitals an.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"18.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0015","text":"Der bürgerliche Oeko-\n nom, dessen beschränktes Hirn die Erscheinungsform von dem was darin\n erscheint nicht trennen kann, schliesst die Augen vor der Thatsache, dass\n selbst noch heutzutag der Arbeitsfonds nur ausnahmsweis auf dem Erdrund\nin der Form von Kapital erscheint [Note: „Die Subsistenzmittel der Arbeiter werden noch nicht auf einem Viertel der\n Erde den Arbeitern durch Kapitalisten vorgeschossen.“ (Richard Jones:\n „Textbook of Lectures on the Polit. Economy of Nations.“\n Hertford 1852, p. 16.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"18.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0016","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"556\", \"facs\": \"#f0575\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"18.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0017","text":"Allerdings verliert das variable Kapital nur den Sinn eines aus\n dem eignen Fonds des Kapitalisten vorgeschossenen Werthes, sobald wir den\n kapitalistischen Produktionsprozess im beständigen Fluss seiner Erneue-\n rung betrachten. Aber er muss doch irgendwo und irgendwann anfangen.\n Von unsrem bisherigen Standpunkt ist es daher wahrscheinlich, dass der\n Kapitalist irgend einmal durch irgend eine, von unbezahlter fremder Arbeit\n unabhängige, ursprüngliche Accumulation Geldbesitzer ward, und\n daher den Markt als Käufer von Arbeitskraft beschreiten konnte. Indess\n bewirkt die blosse Kontinuität des kapitalistischen Produktionsprozesses,\n oder die einfache Reproduktion, noch andre sonderbare Wechsel, die nicht\n nur den variablen Kapitaltheil, sondern das Gesammtkapital er-\n greifen.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"18.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0018","text":"Beträgt der mit einem Kapital von 1000 Pfd. St. periodisch, z. B.\n jährlich, erzeugte Mehrwerth 200 Pfd. St. und wird dieser Mehrwerth\n jährlich verzehrt, so ist klar, dass nach fünfjähriger Wiederholung dessel-\n ben Prozesses die Summe des verzehrten Mehrwerths = 5 × 200 ist\n oder gleich dem ursprünglich vorgeschossenen Kapital-\n werth von 1000 Pfd. St. Würde der jährliche Mehrwerth nur theil-\n weis verzehrt, z. B. nur zur Hälfte, so ergäbe sich dasselbe Resultat nach\n zehnjähriger Wiederholung des Produktionsprozesses, denn 10 × 100 =\n 1000. Allgemein: Der vorgeschossene Kapitalwerth, divi-\n dirt durch den jährlich verzehrten Mehrwerth, ergiebt\n die Jahresanzahl, oder die Anzahl von Reproduktionsperioden,\n nach deren Ablauf der ursprünglich vorgeschossene Kapi-\n talwerth vom Kapitalisten aufgezehrt und daher verschwunden\n ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"18.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0019","text":"Die Vorstellung des Kapitalisten, dass er das Produkt der fremden\n unbezahlten Arbeit, den Mehrwerth, verzehrt und den ursprünglichen\n Kapitalwerth erhält, ändert absolut nichts an der Thatsache, dass nach\n Abfluss einer gewissen Jahreszahl der von ihm geeignete Kapitalwerth\n gleich der Summe des während derselben Jahreszahl ohne Aequivalent an-\n geeigneten Mehrwerths, und die von ihm verzehrte Werthsumme gleich\n dem ursprünglichen Kapitalwerth ist. Kein Werthatom seines\n alten Kapitals existirt fort. Ganz abgesehn von aller Accu-\n mulation verwandelt also die blosse Kontinuität des Produktionspro-\n zesses, oder die einfache Reproduktion, nach kürzerer oder längerer Pe-\n riode, jedes Kapital nothwendig in accumulirtes Kapital oder\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"557\", \"facs\": \"#f0576\"} -->kapitalisirten Mehrwerth.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"18.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0020","text":"War es selbst bei seinem Eintritt in\n den Produktionsprozess persönlich erarbeitetes Eigenthum seines Anwen-\n ders, früher oder später wird es ohne Aequivalent angeeigneter\n Werth oder Materiatur, ob in Geldform oder anders, unbezahlter\n fremder Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"18.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0021","text":"Die ursprüngliche Voraussetzung für die Verwandlung von Geld in\n Kapital waren nicht nur Waarenproduktion und Waarencirkulation. Auf\n dem Waarenmarkt mussten Besitzer von Werth oder Geld und Besitzer der\n werthschaffenden Substanz, Besitzer von Produktions- und Lebensmitteln\n und Besitzer der Arbeitskraft, einander als Käufer und Verkäufer gegen-\n übertreten. Scheidung zwischen dem Arbeitsprodukt und der Arbeit\n selbst, zwischen den objektiven Arbeitsbedingungen und der subjektiven\n Arbeitskraft, war also die thatsächlich gegebne Grundlage des\n kapitalistischen Produktionsprozesses. Seine blosse Kontinui-\n tät, oder die einfache Reproduktion, reproducirt und verewigt diesen\n seinen Ausgangspunkt als sein eignes Resultat. Der Produk-\n tionsprozess verwandelt fortwährend das Geld in Kapital, die Produktions-\n mittel in Verwerthungsmittel.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"18.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0022","text":"Andrerseits kommt der Arbeiter beständig\n aus dem Prozess heraus, wie er in ihn eintritt. Da seine eigne Arbeit\n ihm selbst entfremdet, dem Kapitalisten angeeignet und dem Kapital\n einverleibt ist, bevor er in den Prozess eintritt, vergegenständlicht\n sie sich während des Prozesses beständig in fremdem Produkt. Da\n der Produktionsprozess zugleich der Konsumtionsprozess der Arbeitskraft\n durch den Kapitalisten, verwandelt sich das Produkt des Arbeiters\n nicht nur fortwährend in Waare, sondern in Kapital, Werth, der die\n werthschöpfende Kraft aussaugt, Lebensmittel, die Personen kaufen, Pro-\n duktionsmittel, die den Producenten anwenden [Note: „Das ist eine besonders merkwürdige Eigenschaft der produktiven Kon-\n sumtion. Was produktiv konsumirt wird ist Kapital und es wird Kapital\n durch die Konsumtion.“ (James Mill l. c. p. 242.) J.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"18.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0023","text":"Mill ist jedoch dieser\n „besonders merkwürdigen Eigenschaft“ nicht auf die Spur gekommen.] . Der Arbeiter selbst\n producirt daher beständig den objektiven Reichthum als Kapital, ihm\n fremde, ihn beherrschende und ausbeutende Macht, und der Kapitalist produ-\n cirt ebenso beständig die Arbeitskraft als subjektive, von ihren eignen\n Vergegenständlichungs- und Verwirklichungsmitteln getrennte, abstrakte,\n in der blossen Leiblichkeit des Arbeiters existirende Reichthumsquelle,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"558\", \"facs\": \"#f0577\"} --> kurz den Arbeiter als Lohnarbeiter [Note: „It is true indeed that the first introducing a manufacture emploies\n many poor, but they cease not to be so, and the continuance of it\nmakes many.“ („Reasons for a limited Exportation of Wool.\n Lond. 1677“, p. 19.) „The farmer now absurdly asserts, that he keeps the\n poor.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"18.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0024","text":"They are indeed kept in misery.“ („Reasons for the late\n Increase of the Poor Rates: oracomparative view of the prices\n of labour and provisions. Lond. 1777“, p. 37.)] . Diese beständige Re-\n produktion oder Verewigung des Arbeiters als Lohnar-\n beiter ist das sine qua der kapitalistischen Produktion.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"18.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0025","text":"Man weiss, die Transaktion zwischen Kapitalist und Arbeiter ist fol-\n gende: Einen Theil seines Kapitals, das variable Kapital, tauscht der Ka-\n pitalist aus gegen Arbeitskraft, die er als lebendige Verwerthungskraft\n seinen todten Produktionsmitteln einverleibt. Eben dadurch\n wird der Arbeitsprozess zugleich kapitalistischer Verwerthungsprozess.\n Andrerseits verausgabt der Arbeiter das für seine Arbeitskraft einge-\n tauschte Geld in Lebensmitteln, durch die er sich erhält und re-\n producirt. Es ist diess seine individuelle Konsumtion. Der\n Arbeitsprozess, worin er die Produktionsmittel konsumirt und dadurch in\n Produkte verwandelt, bildet seine produktive Konsumtion und zu-\n gleich Konsumtion seiner Arbeitskraft durch den Kapitalisten.\n Beide Konsumtionen sind wesentlich verschieden.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"18.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0026","text":"In der einen gehört\n der Arbeiter als Arbeitskraft dem Kapital an und ist dem Produktionspro-\n zess einverleibt; in der andern gehört er sich selbst und verrichtet indivi-\n duelle Lebensakte ausserhalb des Produktionsprozesses.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"18.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0027","text":"Bei Betrachtung des „Arbeitstags“ u. s. w. zeigte sich gelegentlich,\n dass der Arbeiter oft gezwungen ist, seine individuelle Konsumtion zu\n einem blossen Incident des Produktionsprozesses zu machen. In diesem\n Fall setzt er sich Lebensmittel zu, um seine Arbeitskraft im Gang zu hal-\n ten, wie der Dampfmaschine Kohle und Wasser, dem Rad Oel zugesetzt\n wird. Seine Konsumtionsmittel sind dann bloss Konsumtionsmittel eines Pro-\n duktionsmittels, seine individuelle Konsumtion direkt produktive Konsum-\n tion. Diess erscheint jedoch als ein dem kapitalistischen Produktions-\n prozess unwesentlicher Missbrauch [Note: Rossi würde nicht so emphatisch diesen Punkt verdeklamiren, wäre er\n wirklich in das Geheimniss der „productive consumption“ eingedrungen.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"18.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0028","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"559\", \"facs\": \"#f0578\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"18.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0029","text":"Betrachtet man aber nicht den vereinzelten Produktionsprozess der\n Waare, sondern den kapitalistischen Produktionsprozess in seinem zu-\n sammenhängenden Fluss und in seinem gesellschaftlichen Umfang, so\n bleibt auch die individuelle Konsumtion des Arbeiters ein\n Moment der Produktion und Reproduktion des Kapitals, ob sie innerhalb\n oder ausserhalb der Werkstatt, Fabrik u. s. w., innerhalb oder ausserhalb\n des Arbeitsprozesses vorgeht, ganz wie die Reinigung der Maschine, ob sie\n während des Arbeitsprozesses oder bestimmter Pausen desselben geschieht.\n Es thut nichts zur Sache, dass der Arbeiter diese Konsumtion sich selbst\n und nicht dem Kapitalisten zu lieb vollzieht. So bleibt der Konsum des\n Lastviehs nicht minder ein nothwendiges Moment des Produktionsprozesses,\n weil das Vieh selbst geniesst, was es frisst.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"18.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0030","text":"Die beständige Erhaltung\n und Reproduktion der Arbeiterklasse bleibt beständige Bedingung für die\n Reproduktion des Kapitals. Der Kapitalist kann ihre Erfüllung getrost\n dem Selbsterhaltungs- und Fortpflanzungstrieb der Arbeiter überlassen.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"18.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0031","text":"Er sorgt nur dafür ihre individuelle Konsumtion so viel als möglich auf\n das Nothwendige einzuschränken und ist himmelweit entfernt von jener\n südamerikanischen Rohheit, die den Arbeiter zwingt substantiellere statt\n weniger substantieller Nahrungsmittel einzunehmen [Note: „Die Arbeiter in den Bergwerken Südamerika’s, deren tägliches Geschäft\n (das schwerste vielleicht in der Welt) darin besteht, eine Last Erz, im Gewicht\n von 180—200 Pfund, aus einer Tiefe von 450 Fuss, auf ihren Schultern zu Tage\n zu fördern, leben nur von Brod und Bohnen, sie würden das Brod allein zur Nah-\n rung vorziehn, allein ihre Herrn, welche gefunden haben, dass sie mit Brod\n nicht so stark arbeiten können, behandeln sie wie Pferde, und zwingen\n sie die Bohnen zu essen; die Bohnen sind aber verhältnissmässig an Knochenerde\n weit reicher als das Brod.“ (Liebig l. c. 1. Theil, p. 194, Note.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"18.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0032","text":"Durch den Umsatz eines Kapitaltheils in Arbeitskraft schlägt der\n Kapitalist zwei Fliegen mit einer Klappe. Er verwandelt dadurch einen\n Theil seines Kapitals in variables Kapital und verwerthet so sein Gesammt-\n kapital. Er einverleibt die Arbeitskraft seinen Produktionsmitteln. Er\n verzehrt die Arbeitskraft produktiv, indem er den Arbeiter die Produktions-\n mittel durch seine Arbeit produktiv verzehren lässt. Andrerseits verwandeln\n sich die Lebensmittel oder der an den Arbeiter veräusserte Theil des Kapitals\n in Muskel, Nerven, Knochen, Hirn u.s.w. von Arbeitern.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"18.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0033","text":"Innerhalb ih-\n rer nothwendigen Grenzen ist daher die individuelle Konsumtion der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"560\", \"facs\": \"#f0579\"} --> Arbeiterklasse Rückverwandlung der vom Kapital gegen Arbeitskraft ver-\n äusserten Lebensmittel in die vom Kapital neu exploitirbare Arbeitskraft,\nProduktion und Reproduktion seines nothwendigsten\n Produktionsmittels, des Arbeiters selbst. Die indivi-\n duelle Konsumtion des Arbeiters bildet daher ein Moment des Repro-\n duktionsprozesses des Kapitals im Grossen und Ganzen.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"18.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0034","text":"Daher betrachtet auch der Kapitalist und sein Ideolog, der politische\n Oekonom, nur den Theil der individuellen Konsumtion des Arbeiters als\nproduktiv, der zur Verewigung der Arbeiterklasse erheischt ist, also\n in der That verzehrt werden muss, damit das Kapital die Arbeitskraft ver-\n zehre; was der Arbeiter ausserdem zu seinem Vergnügen verzehren mag,\n ist unproduktive Konsumtion [Note: James Mill l. c. p. 238 sqq.] . Würde die Accumulation des\n Kapitals eine Erhöhung des Arbeitslohns und daher Vermehrung der Kon-\n sumtionsmittel des Arbeiters verursachen ohne Konsum von mehr Arbeits-\n kraft durch das Kapital, so wäre das zuschüssige Kapital unproduktiv\n konsumirt [Note: „Stiege der Preis der Arbeit so hoch, dass trotz des Zuwachses von Kapi-\n tal nicht mehr Arbeit angewandt werden könnte, so würde ich sagen, dass\n solcher Zuwachs von Kapital unproduktiv konsumirt wird.“ (Ricardo l. c.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"18.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0035","text":"p. 163.)] . In der That: die individuelle Konsumtion des Arbei-\n ters ist für ihn selbst unproduktiv, denn sie reproducirt nur das\nbedürftige Individuum: sie ist produktiv für den Kapita-\n listen und den Staat, denn sie ist Produktion der den fremden\n Reichthum producirenden Kraft [Note: „Die einzig produktive Konsumtion im eigentlichen Sinn ist die Kon-\n sumtion oder Zerstörung von Reichthum (er meint den Verbrauch der Produktions-\n mittel) durch Kapitalisten zum Zwecke der Reproduktion … Der Arbeiter ist ein\n produktiver Konsument für die Person, die ihn anwendet, und für den Staat, aber,\n genau gesprochen, nicht für sich selbst.“ (Malthus: „Definitions etc.“,\n p. 30.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"18.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0036","text":"Von gesellschaftlichem Standpunkt ist also die Arbeiterklasse, auch\n ausserhalb des unmittelbaren Arbeitsprozesses, ebenso sehr Zubehör des\n Kapitals als die todten Arbeitsinstrumente. Ihre individuelle Konsumtion\n selbst ist innerhalb gewisser Grenzen nur ein Moment des Reproduktions-\n prozesses des Kapitals. Der Prozess aber sorgt dafür, dass diese selbst-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"561\", \"facs\": \"#f0580\"} --> bewussten Produktionsinstrumente nicht weglaufen, indem er beständig ihr\n Produkt von ihrem Pol zum Gegenpol des Kapitals entfernt. Die indi-\n viduelle Konsumtion sorgt für ihre eigne Erhaltung und Reproduktion, an-\n drerseits, durch Vernichtung der Lebensmittel, für ihr beständiges Wieder-\n erscheinen auf dem Arbeitsmarkt. Der römische Sklave war durch Ketten,\n der Lohnarbeiter ist durch unsichtbare Fäden an seinen Eigenthümer ge-\n bunden.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"18.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0037","text":"Der Schein seiner Unabhängigkeit wird durch den bestän-\n digen Wechsel der individuellen Lohnherrn und die fictio juris des Kon-\n trakts aufrecht erhalten.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"18.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0038","text":"Früher machte das Kapital, wo es ihm nöthig schien, sein Eigen-\n thumsrecht auf den freien Arbeiter durch Zwangsgesetz gel-\n tend. So war z. B. die Emigration der Maschinenarbeiter in England bis\n 1815 bei schwerer Strafe verboten.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"18.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0039","text":"Die Reproduktion der Arbeiterklasse schliesst zugleich die Ueberliefe-\n rung und Häufung des Geschicks von einer Generation zur andern ein [Note: „Das einzige Ding, wovon man sagen kann, dass es aufgespeichert und\n vorher präparirt ist, ist das Geschick des Arbeiters … Die Accumulation und\n Aufspeicherung geschickter Arbeit, diese wichtigste Operation wird, was die grosse\n Masse der Arbeiter betrifft, ohne irgend welches Kapital vollbracht.“ (Hodgskin:\n „Labour Defended etc.“, p. 13.)] . Wie\n sehr der Kapitalist das Dasein einer solchen geschickten Arbeiterklasse unter\ndie ihm angehörigen Produktionsbedingungen zählt,\n sie in der That als die reale Existenz seines variablen Kapitals ansieht,\n zeigt sich, sobald eine Krise deren Verlust androht.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"18.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0040","text":"In Folge des ameri-\n kanischen Bürgerkriegs und der ihn begleitenden Baumwollnoth wurde be-\n kanntlich die Mehrzahl der Baumwollarbeiter in Lancashire u. s. w aufs\n Pflaster geworfen. Aus dem Schoss der Arbeiterklasse selbst, wie andrer\n Gesellschaftsschichten, schrie man nach Staatsunterstützung oder frei-\n williger Nationalkollekte, um die Emigration der „Ueberflüssigen“ in eng-\n lische Kolonien oder die Vereinigten Staaten zu ermöglichen. Damals\n veröffentlichte die Times (24. März 1863) einen Brief von Edmund\n Potter, früher Präsident der Manchester Handelskammer. Sein Brief\n ward mit Recht im Unterhaus als „das Manifest der Fabrikan-\n ten“ bezeichnet [Note: „That letter might be looked upon as the manifesto of the manufacturers.“\n (Ferrand: Motion über den cotton famine, Sitzung des H. o. C. vom 27. April\n 1863.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"18.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0041","text":"Wir geben hier einige charakteristische Stellen,\n [Running matter: I. 36] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"562\", \"facs\": \"#f0581\"} --> worin der Eigenthumstitel des Kapitals auf die Arbeits-\n kraft unverblümt ausgesprochen wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"18.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0042","text":"„Den Baumwollarbeitern mag gesagt werden, dass ihre Zufuhr zu\n gross ist. . . . sie müsse vielleicht um ein Drittheil reducirt werden, und\n dann würde eine gesunde Nachfrage für die übrigen zwei Drittheile ein-\n treten. . . . Die öffentliche Meinung dringt auf Emigration. . . . Der\n Meister (d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"18.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0043","text":"der Baumwollfabrikant) kann nicht willig seine Arbeitszu-\n fuhr entfernt sehn; er mag denken, dass das ebenso ungerecht als unrich-\n tig ist.… Wenn die Emigration aus öffentlichen Fonds unterstützt wird, hat er\n ein Recht Gehör zu verlangen und vielleicht zu protestiren.“ Selbiger Pot-\n ter setzt dann weiter aus einander, wie nützlich die Baumwollindustrie, wie\n „sie unzweifelhaft die Uebervölkerung aus Irland und den englischen Agri-\n kulturdistrikten wegdrainirt hat“, wie ungeheuer ihr Umfang, wie sie im\n Jahr 1860 \\frac{5}{13} des ganzen englischen Exporthandels lieferte, wie sie nach\n wenigen Jahren sich wieder ausdehnen werde durch Erweiterung des\n Markts, besonders Indiens, und durch Erzwingung hinreichender „Baum-\n wollzufuhr, zu 6 d. das Pfund“. Er fährt dann fort: „Zeit — eins, zwei,\n drei Jahre vielleicht — wird die nöthige Quantität produciren . . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"18.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0044","text":"Ich\n möchte dann die Frage stellen, ist diese Industrie werth sie festzuhalten, ist\n es der Mühe werth die Maschinerie (nämlich die lebendigen\n Arbeitsmaschinen) in Ordnung zu halten, und ist es nicht die\n grösste Narrheit, daran zu denken, sie aufzugeben! Ich\n glaube so. Ich will zugeben, dass die Arbeiter nicht Eigen-\n thum sind („I allow that the workers are not a property“), nicht das\n Eigenthum Lancashire’s und der Meister; aber sie sind die\n Stärke beider; sie sind die geistige und geschulte Kraft, die in einer Ge-\n neration nicht ersetzt werden kann; die andere Maschinerie dagegen,\n woran sie arbeiten („the mere machinery which they work“), könnte zum\n grossen Theil mit Vortheil ersetzt und verbessert werden in\n zwölf Monaten [Note: Man erinnert sich, dass dasselbe Kapital aus einem andern Loch pfeift\n unter gewöhnlichen Umständen, wenn es gilt, den Arbeitslohn herabzusetzen.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"18.44"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0045","text":"Dann erklären „die Meister“ aus einem Munde (sieh Viertes Kapitel, Note\n 188): „Fabrikarbeiter sollten in heilsamer Erinnerung halten, dass ihre Arbeit\n in der That eine sehr niedrige Sorte geschickter Arbeit ist; dass keine leichter\n aneigenbar und in Anbetracht ihrer Qualität besser belohnt ist, dass keine durch] . Ermuntert oder erlaubt (!) die Emi-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"563\", \"facs\": \"#f0582\"} --> gration der Arbeitskraft und was wird aus dem Kapitalisten?\n („Encourage or allow the working power to emigrate, and what of the\n capitalist?“ Dieser Herzensstoss erinnert an Hofmarschall Kalb.) … Nimm\n den Rahm der Arbeiter weg, und das fixe Kapital wird in hohem Grad\n entwerthet und das cirkulirende Kapital wird sich nicht dem Kampf mit\n schmaler Zufuhr einer niedrigeren Sorte von Arbeit aussetzen. . . . Man\n sagt uns, die Arbeiter selbst wünschen die Emigration.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"18.45"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0046","text":"Es\n ist sehr natürlich, dass sie das thun. . . . Reducire, komprimire das\n Baumwollgeschäft durch Wegnahme seiner Arbeitskraft (by\n taking away its working power), durch Verminderung ihrer Lohnveraus-\n gabung sage um ⅓ oder 5 Millionen, und was wird dann aus der nächsten\n Klasse über ihnen, den Kleinkrämern? Was aus den Grundrenten, was\n aus der Miethe der cottages? … was aus dem kleinen Pächter, dem\n besseren Hausbesitzer und dem Grundeigenthümer?","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"18.46"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0047","text":"Und sagt nun, ob\n irgend ein Plan für alle Klassen des Landes selbstmörderischer sein kann\n als dieser, dieN ation zu schwächen durch den Export ihrer besten\n Fabrikarbeiter und die Entwerthung eines Theils ihres produktiv-\n sten Kapitals und Reichthums?“ „Ich rathe zu einer Anleihe von 5 bis\n 6 Millionen, über 2 oder 3 Jahre vertheilt, administrirt durch Spezial-\n kommissäre, beigeordnet den Armenverwaltungen in den Baumwolldistrik-\n ten, unter speziellen gesetzlichen Regulationen, mit gewisser Zwangs-\n arbeit, um die moralische Valuta der Almosenempfänger aufrecht zu\n erhalten. . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"18.47"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0048","text":"Kann es irgend etwas Schlimmeres geben für\n Grundeigenthümer oder Meister („can anything be worse for\n landowners or masters“) als ihre besten Arbeiter aufzugeben und die\nübrigbleibenden zu demoralisiren und zu verstimmen\n durch eine ausgedehnte entleerende Emigration und Entleerung von Werth\n und Kapital in einer ganzen Provinz?“","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"18.48"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0049","text":"[Note: kurze Unterweisung des mindest Erfahrenen in so kurzer Zeit und in solchem\n Ueberfluss zugeführt werden kann. Des Meisters Maschinerie (die, wie\n wir jetzt hören, in 12 Monaten mit Vortheil und verbessert ersetzt werden kann)\n spielt in der That eine viel wichtigere Rolle in dem Geschäft der Produktion\n als die Arbeit und das Geschick des Arbeiters (die jetzt in 30 Jahren\n nicht ersetzbar sind), die eine Erziehung von 6 Monaten lehren und jeder\n Bauernknecht lernen kann.“]","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"18.49"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0050","text":"[Running matter: 36*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"564\", \"facs\": \"#f0583\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"18.50"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0051","text":"Potter, das auserwählte Organ der Baumwollfabrikanten, unterscheidet\n doppelte „Maschinerie“, deren jede dem Kapitalisten gehört, und\n wovon die eine in seiner Fabrik steht, die andre des Nachts und Sonntags\n auswärtig in cottages haust. Die eine ist todt, die andre lebendig. Die\n todte Maschinerie verschlechtert und entwerthet sich nicht nur jeden\n Tag, sondern von ihrer existirenden Masse ist ein grosser Theil durch den\n beständigen technologischen Fortschritt beständig so sehr antiquirt, dass sie\n vortheilhaft und in wenigen Monaten durch neuere Maschinerie ersetzbar.\n Die lebendige Maschinerie verbessert sich umgekehrt, je länger sie währt,\n je mehr sie das Geschick von Generationen in sich aufhäuft. Die Times\n antwortete dem Fabrikmagnaten u. a.:","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"18.51"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0052","text":"„Herr E. Potter ist so impressionirt mit der ausserordentlichen und\n absoluten Wichtigkeit der Baumwollmeister, dass er, um diese Klasse\n zu erhalten und ihr Metier zu verewigen, eine halbe Million der Arbeiter-\n klasse wider ihren Willen in ein grosses moralisches Workhouse ein-\n sperren will. Ist diese Industrie werth sie festzuhalten? fragt Herr Pot-\n ter. Sicher, durch alle ehrbaren Mittel! antworten wir. Ist es der Mühe\n werth die Maschinerie in Ordnung zu halten? fragt wieder Herr Potter.\n Hier stutzen wir. Unter der Maschinerie versteht Herr Potter die\nmenschliche Maschinerie, denn er betheuert, dass er sie nicht\n als absolutes Eigenthum zu behandeln vorhat. Wir müssen\n gestehn, wir halten es nicht ‚der Mühe werth‘ oder selbst für möglich, die\n menschliche Maschinerie in Ordnung zu halten, d. h. einzusperren und\n einzuölen bis ihrer bedurft wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"18.52"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0053","text":"Menschliche Maschinerie hat die\n Eigenschaft während der Unthätigkeit zu verrosten, ihr mögt noch soviel\n dran ölen oder reiben. Zudem ist menschliche Maschinerie, wie der\n Augenschein uns eben lehrt, im Stand von eignen Stücken den Dampf an-\n zulassen und zu platzen oder einen Veitstanz in unsren grossen Städten zu\n tollen. Es mag, wie Herr Potter sagt, längere Zeit zur Repro-\n duktion der Arbeiter erheischt sein, aber mit Maschinisten\n und Geld zur Hand werden wir stets betriebsame, harte, industrielle Män-\n ner finden, um daraus mehr Fabrikmeister zu fabriciren als\n wir je verbrauchen können. . . . Herr Potter plaudert von einer\n Wiederbelebung der Industrie in 1, 2, 3 Jahren und verlangt von uns die\nEmigration der Arbeitskraft nicht zu ermuntern oder nicht zu\nerlauben!","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"18.53"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0054","text":"Er sagt, es sei natürlich, dass die Arbeiter zu emigriren wün-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"565\", \"facs\": \"#f0584\"} --> schen, aber er meint, dass die Nation diese halbe Million Arbeiter mit den\n 700,000, die an ihnen hängen, ihrem Verlangen zum Trotz in die\n Baumwolldistrikte einsperren und, eine nothwendige Konsequenz, ihr Miss-\n vergnügen durch Gewalt niederschlagen und sie selbst durch Almosen\n fristen muss, alles das auf die Chance hin, dass die Baumwollmeister\n ihrer an einem beliebigen Tag wieder bedürfen mögen. . . . Die Zeit ist\n gekommen, wo die grosse öffentliche Meinung dieser Eilande etwas thun\n muss, um ‚diese Arbeitskraft‘ vor denen zu retten, die sie behan-\n deln wollen, wie sie Kohle, Eisen und Baumwolle behandeln.“ („to save\n this ‘working power’ from those who would deal with it as they deal with\n iron, coal and cotton.“) [Note: Times, 24. March 1863.]","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"18.54"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0055","text":"Der Times-Artikel war nur ein jeu d’esprit. Die „grosse öffent-\n liche Meinung“ war in der That der Meinung des Herrn Potter, dass die\n Fabrikarbeiter Mobiliarzubehör der Fabriken. Ihre Emigration wurde\n verhindert [Note: Das Parlament votirte keinen Farthing für Emigration, sondern nur Ge-\n setze, welche die Municipalitäten befähigten, die Arbeiter zwischen Leben und\n Sterben zu halten oder sie zu exploitiren, ohne Zahlung von Normallöhnen. Als\n dagegen drei Jahre später die Rinderseuche ausbrach, durchbrach das Parlament\n wild sogar alle parlamentarische Etiquette und votirte im Umsehn Millionen zur\n Schadloshaltung der Millionäre von Landlords, deren Pächter sich ohnehin durch\n Steigerung der Fleischpreise schadlos hielten.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"18.55"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0056","text":"Das bestiale Gebrüll der Grund-\n eigenthümer bei Eröffnung des Parlaments von 1866 bewies, dass man nicht Hindu\n zu sein braucht, um die Kuh Sabala anzubeten, noch Jupiter, um sich in einen\n Ochsen zu verwandeln.] . Man sperrte sie in das „moralische Workhouse“ der Baum-\n wolldistrikte, und sie bilden nach wie vor „die Stärke (the strength) der\n Baumwollmeister von Lancashire.“","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"18.56"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0057","text":"Der kapitalistische Produktionsprozess reproducirt also durch seinen\n eignen Vorgang die Scheidung zwischen Arbeitskraft und Arbeitsbe-\n dingungen. Er reproducirt und verewigt damit die Exploitationsbe-\n dingungen des Arbeiters. Er zwingt beständig den Arbeiter zum Verkauf\n seiner Arbeitskraft, um zu leben, und befähigt beständig den Kapitalisten\n zu ihrem Kauf, um sich zu bereichern [Note: „L’ouvrier demandait de la subsistance pour vivre, le chef demandait du\n travail pour gagner.“ (Sismondi l. c. p. 91.)] . Es ist nicht mehr der Zufall,\n welcher Kapitalist und Arbeiter als Käufer und Verkäufer auf dem Waaren-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"566\", \"facs\": \"#f0585\"} --> markt gegenüberstellt.","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"18.57"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0058","text":"Es ist die Zwickmühle des Prozesses selbst, die\n den Einen stets als Verkäufer seiner Arbeitskraft auf den Waarenmarkt\n zurückschleudert und sein eignes Produkt stets in das Kaufmittel des An-\n dern verwandelt. In der That gehört der Arbeiter dem Kapital, bevor er\n sich dem Kapitalisten verkauft. Seine ökonomische Hörigkeit [Note: Eine bäuerlich plumpe Form dieser Hörigkeit existirt in der Grafschaft\nDurham. Es ist diess eine der wenigen Grafschaften, worin die Verhältnisse\n dem Pächter nicht unbestrittenen Eigenthumstitel auf die Ackerbautaglöhner sichern.\n Die Bergwerksindustrie erlaubt letzteren eine Wahl. Der Pächter, im Gegensatz\n zur Regel, übernimmt hier daher nur Pacht von Ländereien, worauf sich cottages\n für die Arbeiter befinden. Der Miethpreis der cottage bildet Theil des Arbeits-\n lohns. Diese cottages heissen „hind’s houses“.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"18.58"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0059","text":"Sie werden den Arbeitern unter\n gewissen Feudalverpflichtungen vermiethet, unter einem Vertrag, der „bondage“\n (Hörigkeit) heisst und den Arbeiter z. B. bindet, für die Zeit, während deren er\n anderswo beschäftigt ist, seine Tochter u. s. w. zu stellen. Der Arbeiter selbst\n heisst bondsman, Höriger. Diess Verhältniss zeigt auch die individuelle\n Konsumtion des Arbeiters als Konsumtion für das Kapital oder pro-\n duktive Konsumtion von einer ganz neuen Seite. „Es ist merkwürdig zu\n beobachten, wie selbst der Koth dieses bondsman zu den Sporteln an seinen kal-\n kulirenden Gebieter zählt . . . . Der Pächter erlaubt in der ganzen Nachbarschaft\n keinen Abtritt ausser seinem eignen und duldet in dieser Beziehung keinen Ab-\n schlag von seinen Suzerainrechten.“ („Public Health. VII. Rep. 1865“,\n p.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"18.59"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0060","text":"188.)]  ist zu-\n gleich vermittelt und zugleich versteckt durch die periodische Erneurung\n seines Selbstverkaufs, den Wechsel seiner individuellen Lohnherrn und die\n Oscillation im Marktpreise der Arbeit [Note: Man erinnert sich, dass bei der Arbeit der Kinder u. s. w. selbst die For-\n malität des Selbstverkaufs verschwindet.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"18.60"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0061","text":"Der kapitalistische Produktionsprozess, im Zusammenhang betrachtet,\n oder als Reproduktionsprozess, producirt also nicht nur Waare, nicht nur\n Mehrwerth, er producirt und reproducirt das Kapitalverhältniss\n selbst, auf der einen Seite den Kapitalisten, auf der andern den\n Lohnarbeiter [Note: „Das Kapital setzt die Lohnarbeit, die Lohnarbeit setzt das Kapital vor-\n aus. Sie bedingen sich wechselseitig, sie bringen sich wechselseitig hervor. Ein\n Arbeiter in einer Baumwollfabrik, producirt er nur Baumwollstoffe? Nein, er\n producirt Kapital. Er producirt Werthe, die von neuem dazu dienen, seine\n Arbeit zu kommandiren, und vermittelst derselben neue Werthe zu schaffen.“\n (Karl Marx: „Lohnarbeit und Kapital“ in N. Rh. Z. Nr. 266,] .","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"18.61"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0062","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"567\", \"facs\": \"#f0586\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"18.62"}],"sha256":"9882b9a7d5836c93d8a9e0b0d56e70869e6b1b806eadbd7711b227163ef9c146","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T205351490773Z_31aec33f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"a) Einfache Reproduktion.","source_sha256":"9882b9a7d5836c93d8a9e0b0d56e70869e6b1b806eadbd7711b227163ef9c146","sha256":"e2b0138f181ad023416c5e5acc4e0691b1ea78ce253ae89a1c1da0a4660b1fb9","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:53:51.490788+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0001","text":"## 1) Capitalist Accumulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"18.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0002","text":"## a) Simple Reproduction.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"18.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0003","text":"Whatever the social form of the process of production,\nit must be continuous, or periodically pass through the same stages anew. A society can no more cease to consume than it can cease to produce. Considered in its continuous connexion and the constant flow of its renewal, every social process of production is therefore at the same time a process of reproduction.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"18.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0004","text":"The conditions of production are at the same time the conditions of reproduction. No society can continuously produce, i.e. reproduce, without continuously reconverting a part of its products into means of production or elements of new production. Other circumstances remaining the same, it can reproduce or maintain its wealth only on the same scale by replacing in kind, for example, the means of production consumed during the year, i.e. instruments of labour, raw materials, and auxiliary materials, by an equal quantity of new specimens, which is separated from the annual mass of products and again surrendered to the process of production. A definite quantity of the annual product thus belongs to production.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"18.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0005","text":"Originally intended for\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"553\", \"facs\": \"#f0572\"} --> productive consumption, it exists for the most part in natural forms which of themselves exclude individual consumption.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"18.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0006","text":"If production has capitalist form, so does reproduction. Just as in the capitalist mode of production the labour-process appears only as a means for the process of valorisation, so reproduction appears only as a means of reproducing the advanced value as capital, i.e. as self-preserving and self-valorising value. The economic character-mask of the capitalist clings firmly to a man only because his money constantly functions as capital. If, for example, the advanced sum of money of £100 has transformed itself into capital this year and produced a surplus-value of £20, it must repeat the same operation next year, and so forth.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"18.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0007","text":"As a periodic increment of capital-value, or periodic fruit of capital in process, surplus-value assumes the form of a revenue springing from capital [Note: “The rich, who consume the products of the labour of others, receive them only through acts of exchange (purchases of commodities). They are therefore exposed to a constant exhaustion of their funds … But in the social order wealth has received the power to reproduce itself through the labour of others . . . . Wealth, like labour, and through labour, yields an annual fruit, which may be destroyed each year without the rich man becoming poorer. The fruit is the revenue that springs from capital.” (Sismondi: “Nouv. Princ. d’Écon. Pol.” t. I, p. 82.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"18.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0008","text":"If this revenue serves the capitalist only as a consumption fund, or if it is consumed by him as periodically as it is gained, then, other circumstances remaining the same, simple reproduction takes place. Although the latter is now a mere repetition of the process of production on the same scale, this mere repetition or continuity impresses certain new characteristics upon the process, or rather dissolves the apparent characteristics of its merely isolated occurrence.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"18.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0009","text":"The process of production is initiated by the purchase of labour-power for a definite time, and this initiation is constantly renewed whenever the term of sale of labour falls due and thus a definite period of production, week, month, etc., has elapsed. But the worker is paid only after his labour-power has operated and realised both its own value and the surplus-value in commodities. He has therefore\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"554\", \"facs\": \"#f0573\"} --> produced both the surplus-value, which for the time being we regard only as the capitalist's consumption fund, and the fund for his own payment, variable capital, before it flows back to him in the form of wages, and he is employed only so long as he constantly reproduces it.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"18.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0010","text":"Hence the formula of the economists mentioned in the preceding chapter, which represents wages as a share in the product itself [Note: “Wages as well as profits are to be considered each of them as really a portion of the finished product.” (Ramsay l. c. p. 142.) “The share in the product which accrues to the worker in the form of wages.” (J. Mill: “Elements etc. Trans. by Parissot, Paris 1823”, p. 34.)] . It is a part of the product constantly reproduced by the worker himself that constantly flows back to him in the form of wages. The capitalist indeed pays him the value of the commodity in money. But this money is only the transformed form of the labour-product, or rather of a part of the labour-product. While the worker transforms a part of the means of production into product, a part of his former product is retransformed into money.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"18.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0011","text":"It is his labour of the preceding week or of the last half-year by which his labour of today or of the next half-year is paid. The illusion generated by the money-form disappears at once as soon as, instead of the individual capitalist and the individual worker, the capitalist class and the working class are considered. The capitalist class constantly gives the working class, in the form of money, drafts upon a part of the product produced by the latter and appropriated by the former. The worker just as constantly returns these drafts to the capitalist class and thereby withdraws from it the part of his own product that falls to himself. The commodity-form of the product and the money-form of the commodity disguise the transaction.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"18.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0012","text":"Variable capital is thus only a particular historical form of appearance of the fund of means of subsistence, or labour-fund, which the worker requires for his self-maintenance and reproduction, and which in every system of social production he must always himself produce and reproduce. The labour-fund constantly flows to him only in the form of means of payment for his labour, because his own product constantly removes itself from him in the form of\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"555\", \"facs\": \"#f0574\"} --> capital. But this form of appearance of the labour-fund changes nothing in the fact that his own objectified labour is advanced to the worker by the capitalist [Note: “When capital is employed in advancing to the workmen his wages, it adds nothing to the funds for the maintenance of labour.” (Cazenove in a note to his ed.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"18.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0013","text":"of Malthus’s: “Definitions in Polit. Econ. London 1853”, p. 22.)] . Let us take a corvée peasant. He works with his own means of production on his own field, for example, 3 days of the week. The other 3 weekdays he performs corvée labour on the lord's estate. He constantly reproduces his own labour-fund, and this never assumes, in relation to him, the form of means of payment advanced by a third party for his labour. In return, neither does his unpaid forced labour ever assume the form of voluntary and paid labour. If tomorrow the lord appropriates to himself the field, the draught animals, the seed, in short the means of production of the corvée peasant, the latter will henceforth have to sell his labour-power to the corvée lord.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"18.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0014","text":"Other circumstances remaining the same, he will continue to work 6 days a week, 3 days for himself, 3 for the former corvée lord, who has now been transformed into a wage-lord. As before, he will employ the means of production as means of production and transfer their value to the product. As before, a definite part of the product will enter into reproduction. But just as corvée labour assumes the form of wage-labour, so the labour-fund produced and reproduced as before by the corvée peasant assumes the form of capital advanced to him by the former corvée lord.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"18.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0015","text":"The bourgeois economist, whose narrow brain cannot separate the form of appearance from that which appears in it, closes his eyes to the fact that even today the labour-fund appears only exceptionally upon the globe in the form of capital [Note: “The means of subsistence of the workers are not advanced to workers by capitalists on even a quarter of the earth.” (Richard Jones: “Textbook of Lectures on the Polit. Economy of Nations.” Hertford 1852, p. 16.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"18.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0016","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"556\", \"facs\": \"#f0575\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"18.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0017","text":"Certainly, variable capital loses the meaning of value advanced out of the capitalist's own fund only when we consider the capitalist process of production in the constant flow of its renewal. But it must nevertheless begin somewhere and sometime. From our standpoint hitherto, it is therefore probable that at some time, through some original accumulation independent of unpaid alien labour, the capitalist became the possessor of money, and was therefore able to enter the market as purchaser of labour-power. Yet the mere continuity of the capitalist process of production, or simple reproduction, brings about other peculiar changes which affect not only the variable part of capital, but total capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"18.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0018","text":"If the surplus-value periodically produced, for example annually, with a capital of £1000 amounts to £200, and if this surplus-value is consumed annually, it is clear that after a five-year repetition of the same process the sum of consumed surplus-value = 5 × 200, or equal to the capital-value originally advanced, of £1000. If the annual surplus-value were consumed only in part, for example only by half, the same result would arise after a ten-year repetition of the process of production, for 10 × 100 = 1000. In general: the capital-value advanced, divided by the annually consumed surplus-value, yields the number of years, or the number of periods of reproduction, after whose expiration the capital-value originally advanced has been consumed by the capitalist and has therefore disappeared.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"18.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0019","text":"The capitalist's notion that he consumes the product of alien unpaid labour, the surplus-value, and preserves the original capital-value, alters absolutely nothing in the fact that, after the lapse of a certain number of years, the capital-value appropriated by him is equal to the sum of surplus-value appropriated without equivalent during the same number of years, and that the sum of value consumed by him is equal to the original capital-value. Not a single atom of value of his old capital continues to exist. Quite apart from all accumulation, the mere continuity of the process of production, or simple reproduction, thus necessarily transforms every capital, after a shorter or longer period, into accumulated capital or capitalised surplus-value.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"557\", \"facs\": \"#f0576\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"18.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0020","text":"Even if, upon its entry into the process of production, it was personally earned property of its employer, sooner or later it becomes value or materiality appropriated without equivalent, whether in money-form or otherwise, of unpaid alien labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"18.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0021","text":"The original presupposition for the transformation of money into capital was not merely commodity production and commodity circulation. On the commodity market, possessors of value or money and possessors of the value-creating substance, possessors of means of production and subsistence and possessors of labour-power, had to confront one another as buyers and sellers. Separation between the product of labour and labour itself, between the objective conditions of labour and subjective labour-power, was thus the actually given basis of the capitalist process of production. Its mere continuity, or simple reproduction, reproduces and perpetuates this its point of departure as its own result. The process of production constantly transforms money into capital, the means of production into means of valorisation.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"18.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0022","text":"On the other hand, the worker constantly emerges from the process as he enters it. Since his own labour is alienated from himself, appropriated by the capitalist, and incorporated into capital before he enters the process, it constantly objectifies itself during the process in an alien product. Since the process of production is at the same time the process of consumption of labour-power by the capitalist, the worker's product is constantly transformed not only into commodity, but into capital, value which sucks up the value-creating force, means of subsistence which buy persons, means of production which employ the producer [Note: “This is a particularly remarkable property of productive consumption. What is consumed productively is capital and becomes capital through consumption.” (James Mill l. c. p. 242.) J.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"18.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0023","text":"Mill, however, did not get on the track of this “particularly remarkable property”.] . The worker himself therefore constantly produces objective wealth as capital, a power alien to him, dominating and exploiting him, and the capitalist just as constantly produces labour-power as a subjective source of wealth, separated from its own means of objectification and realisation, abstract, existing in the mere corporeality of the worker, in short the worker as a wage-worker [Note: “It is true indeed that the first introducing a manufacture emploies many poor, but they cease not to be so, and the continuance of it\nmakes many.” (“Reasons for a limited Exportation of Wool.\nLond. 1677”, p. 19.) “The farmer now absurdly asserts, that he keeps the\npoor.”]","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"18.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0024","text":"They are indeed kept in misery.“ („Reasons for the late\n Increase of the Poor Rates: oracomparative view of the prices\n of labour and provisions. Lond. 1777“, p. 37.)] . This constant re-\n production or perpetuation of the worker as a wage-\n worker is the sine qua of capitalist production.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"18.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0025","text":"It is known that the transaction between capitalist and worker is as fol-\n lows: The capitalist exchanges one part of his capital, variable capital, for\n labour-power, which he incorporates, as living value-creating power,\n into his dead means of production. Precisely thereby\n the labour-process becomes at the same time the capitalist process of valorisation.\n On the other hand, the worker expends the money exchanged for his labour-\n power on means of subsistence, by which he maintains and re-\n produces himself. This is his individual consumption. The\n labour-process, in which he consumes the means of production and thereby transforms them into\n products, constitutes his productive consumption and at the same time the\n consumption of his labour-power by the capitalist.\n Both consumptions are essentially different.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"18.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0026","text":"In the one,\n the worker as labour-power belongs to capital and is incorporated into the process\n of production; in the other he belongs to himself and performs indi-\n vidual acts of life outside the process of production.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"18.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0027","text":"In considering the “Working Day,” etc., it was incidentally shown\n that the worker is often compelled to make his individual consumption\n a mere incident of the process of production. In that\n case he supplies himself with means of subsistence in order to keep his labour-power go-\n ing, just as coal and water are supplied to the steam-engine, oil to the wheel.\n His means of consumption are then merely means of consumption of a means of pro-\n duction; his individual consumption directly productive consump-\n tion. This, however, appears as an abuse inessential to the capitalist process\n of production [Note: Rossi would not declaim this point so emphatically, if he had\n really penetrated the secret of “productive consumption.”] .","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"18.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0028","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"559\", \"facs\": \"#f0578\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"18.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0029","text":"But if we consider not the isolated process of production of the\n commodity, but the capitalist process of production in its connected\n flow and on its social scale, then the individual consumption of the worker also remains\n a moment of the production and reproduction of capital, whether it takes place within\n or outside the workshop, factory, etc., within or outside\n the labour-process, just as the cleaning of the machine, whether it occurs\n during the labour-process or during definite pauses in it.\n It makes no difference that the worker carries out this consumption for his own sake\n and not for that of the capitalist. Thus the consumption of draught cattle remains no less\n a necessary moment of the process of production, because the cattle themselves enjoy what they eat.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"18.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0030","text":"The constant maintenance\n and reproduction of the working class remains the constant condition for the\n reproduction of capital. The capitalist can safely leave its fulfilment\n to the workers’ instinct of self-preservation and propagation.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"18.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0031","text":"He merely takes care to restrict their individual consumption as far as possible\n to what is necessary, and is worlds away from that South American\n brutality which compels the worker to consume more substantial rather than\n less substantial articles of food [Note: “The workers in the mines of South America, whose daily occupation\n (perhaps the heaviest in the world) consists in bringing to the surface on their shoulders\n a load of ore, weighing 180—200 pounds, from a depth of 450 feet,\n live only on bread and beans; they would prefer bread alone as nourish-\n ment, but their masters, who have found that they cannot work as hard on bread,\n treat them like horses, and compel\n them to eat the beans; but beans are proportionately much richer in bone-earth\n than bread.” (Liebig l. c. Part 1, p. 194, Note.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"18.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0032","text":"By converting a part of capital into labour-power, the capitalist kills\n two birds with one stone. Thereby he transforms one\n part of his capital into variable capital and thus valorises his total\n capital. He incorporates labour-power into his means of production. He\n consumes labour-power productively, by having the worker productively consume the means\n of production through his labour. On the other hand, the means of subsistence, or the part of capital alienated to the worker,\n are transformed into muscle, nerves, bones, brain, etc., of workers.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"18.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0033","text":"Within its neces-\n sary limits, therefore, the individual consumption of the\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"560\", \"facs\": \"#f0579\"} --> working class is the reconversion of the means of subsistence alienated by capital in exchange for labour-power into labour-power newly exploitable by capital,\nproduction and reproduction of its most necessary\n means of production, the worker himself. The indi-\n vidual consumption of the worker therefore forms a moment of the repro-\n ductive process of capital as a whole.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"18.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0034","text":"Therefore the capitalist too, and his ideologist, the political\n economist, regards as productive only that part of the worker’s individual consumption\nwhich is required for the perpetuation of the working class, that is,\n which must in fact be consumed in order that capital may consume labour-power;\n whatever else the worker may consume for his pleasure\n is unproductive consumption [Note: James Mill l. c. p. 238 sqq.] . If the accumulation of\n capital were to cause a rise in wages and therefore an increase in the worker’s means of con-\n sumption, without consumption of more labour-power by capital,\n then the additional capital would be consumed unproductively\n [Note: “If the price of labour were to rise so high that, despite the increase of capi-\n tal, no more labour could be employed, I should say that\n such increase of capital is consumed unproductively.” (Ricardo l. c.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"18.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0035","text":"p. 163.)] . In fact: the individual consumption of the work-\n er is unproductive for himself, since it merely reproduces the\nneedy individual; it is productive for the capi-\n talist and the state, since it is production of the force producing another’s\n wealth [Note: “The only productive consumption in the proper sense is the con-\n sumption or destruction of wealth (by which he means the consumption of the means of pro-\n duction) by capitalists for the purpose of reproduction … The worker is a\n productive consumer for the person who employs him, and for the state, but,\n strictly speaking, not for himself.” (Malthus: “Definitions etc.”,\n p. 30.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"18.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0036","text":"From the social standpoint, therefore, the working class, even\n outside the immediate labour-process, is just as much an appurtenance of\n capital as the dead instruments of labour. Its individual consumption\n itself is, within certain limits, only a moment of the reproductive\n process of capital. But the process ensures that these self-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"561\", \"facs\": \"#f0580\"} --> conscious instruments of production do not run away, by constantly removing\n their product from their pole to the opposite pole of capital. Individual\n consumption provides for their own maintenance and reproduction, and,\n by the destruction of means of subsistence, for their constant re-\n appearance on the labour-market. The Roman slave was bound to his owner by chains,\n the wage-worker by invisible threads.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"18.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0037","text":"The appearance of his independence is maintained by the con-\n stant change of individual wage-masters and by the fictio juris of the con-\n tract.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"18.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0038","text":"Formerly capital, where it seemed necessary to it, enforced its right\n of property over the free worker by coercive legis-\n lation. Thus, for example, the emigration of machine-workers in England was\n forbidden under severe penalty until 1815.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"18.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0039","text":"The reproduction of the working class includes at the same time the transmission\n and accumulation of skill from one generation to another [Note: “The only thing of which it can be said that it is stored up and\n prepared beforehand is the skill of the worker … The accumulation and\n storage of skilled labour, this most important operation, is accomplished, as regards the great\n mass of workers, without any capital whatever.” (Hodgskin:\n “Labour Defended etc.”, p. 13.)] . How\n greatly the capitalist counts the existence of such a skilled working class among\nthe conditions of production belonging to him,\n indeed regards it as the real existence of his variable capital,\n becomes apparent as soon as a crisis threatens its loss.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"18.39","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0040","text":"As a consequence of the Ameri-\n can Civil War and the accompanying cotton famine, the greater number of cotton workers\n in Lancashire, etc., were notoriously thrown onto the streets. From the bosom of the working class itself, as from other\n social strata, there arose cries for state assistance or voluntary national subscription,\n in order to make possible the emigration of the “superfluous” to English\n colonies or the United States. At that time\n the Times (24 March 1863) published a letter by Edmund\n Potter, formerly President of the Manchester Chamber of Commerce. His letter\n was rightly described in the House of Commons as “the manufacturers’\n manifesto” [Note: “That letter might be looked upon as the manifesto of the manufacturers.”\n (Ferrand: Motion on the cotton famine, sitting of the H. o. C. of 27 April\n 1863.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"18.40","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0041","text":"We give here some characteristic passages,\n [Running matter: I. 36]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"562\", \"facs\": \"#f0581\"} --> in which the capital’s title of ownership to labour-\n power is bluntly expressed.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"18.41","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0042","text":"“The cotton workers may be told that their supply is too\n great. . . . it must perhaps be reduced by a third, and\n then a healthy demand would arise for the remaining two thirds. . . . Public\n opinion urges emigration. . . . The\n master (i.e.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"18.42","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0043","text":"the cotton manufacturer) cannot willingly see his labour sup-\n ply removed; he may think that this is as unjust as it is incor-\n rect.… If emigration is supported out of public funds, he\n has a right to demand a hearing and perhaps to protest.” Potter then proceeds to set forth\n how useful the cotton industry is, how\n “it has undoubtedly drained away the overpopulation from Ireland and the English agri-\n cultural districts,” how enormous is its scale, how in\n 1860 it supplied \\frac{5}{13} of the entire English export trade, how in a\n few years it will expand again through the enlargement of the\n market, especially India, and through the securing of an adequate “cotton sup-\n ply, at 6 d. per pound.” He then continues: “Time—one, two,\n three years perhaps—will produce the requisite quantity . . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"18.43","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0044","text":"I\n would then ask the question, is this industry worth retaining, is\n it worth the trouble to keep the machinery (namely the living\n labour-machines) in order, and is it not the greatest folly to contemplate abandoning\n it! I think so. I will admit that the workers are not prop-\n erty (“I allow that the workers are not a property”), not the\n property of Lancashire and of the masters; but they are the\n strength of both; they are the intellectual and trained force which cannot be re-\n placed in one generation; whereas the other machinery\n which they work (“the mere machinery which they work”) could for the\n most part be advantageously replaced and improved within\n twelve months [Note: One recalls that the same capital whistles a different tune\n under ordinary circumstances, when it is a question of lowering wages.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"18.44","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0045","text":"Then “the masters” declare with one voice (see Chapter Four, Note\n 188): “Factory workers should keep in salutary remembrance that their labour\n is in fact a very low kind of skilled labour; that none is more easily\n acquired and, in view of its quality, better rewarded; that none by] . Encourages or permits (!) the emi-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"563\", \"facs\": \"#f0582\"} --> gration of labour-power, and what becomes of the capitalist?\n (“Encourage or allow the working power to emigrate, and what of the\n capitalist?” This outcry from the heart recalls Court Marshal Kalb.) … Take\n away the cream of the workers, and fixed capital will be depreciated to a high degree,\n and circulating capital will not expose itself to the struggle with a scant\n supply of a lower kind of labour. . . . We are told that the workers themselves desire emigration.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"18.45","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0046","text":"It\n is very natural that they should do so. . . . Reduce, compress the\n cotton business by removing its labour-power (by\n taking away its working power), by reducing its wage expenditure,\n say by ⅓ or 5 million, and what then becomes of the class immediately above them,\n the small shopkeepers? What becomes of ground-rents, what\n of the rent of cottages? … what becomes of the small farmer, the\n better householder, and the landowner?”","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"18.46","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0047","text":"And now say whether\n any plan can be more suicidal for all classes of the country than this,\n to weaken the nation by the export of its best\n factory workers and the depreciation of a part of its most produc-\n tive capital and wealth?” “I advise a loan of 5 to\n 6 million, spread over 2 or 3 years, administered by special\n commissioners attached to the poor-law administrations in the cotton districts,\n under special legal regulations, with a certain compulsory\n labour, in order to maintain the moral currency of the recipients of alms.\n . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"18.47","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0048","text":"Can anything be worse for\n landowners or masters (“can anything be worse for\n landowners or masters”) than to give up their best workers and to demoralise and discontent\n those who remain through an extensive draining emigration and drainage of value\n and capital in a whole province?”","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"18.48","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0049","text":"[Note: brief instruction of the least experienced can be supplied in so short a time and in such\n abundance. The master’s machinery (which, as\n we now hear, can be replaced with advantage and improved within 12 months)\n in fact plays a far more important role in the business of production\n than the labour and skill of the worker (which are now\n irreplaceable in 30 years), which an education of 6 months teaches and every\n farm labourer can learn.”]","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"18.49","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0050","text":"[Running matter: 36*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"564\", \"facs\": \"#f0583\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"18.50","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0051","text":"Potter, the chosen organ of the cotton manufacturers, distinguishes\n a double “machinery,” each of which belongs to the capitalist, and\n of which one stands in his factory, while the other lodges outside in cottages\n at night and on Sundays. One is dead, the other living. The\n dead machinery not only deteriorates and loses value every\n day, but a great part of its existing mass is constantly rendered so obsolete\n by constant technological progress that it can advantageously be replaced\n by newer machinery within a few months. The living machinery, conversely, improves\n the longer it lasts, the more it accumulates within itself the skill of generations. The Times\n answered the factory magnate, among other things:","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"18.51","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0052","text":"“Mr. E. Potter is so impressed by the extraordinary and\n absolute importance of the cotton masters that, in order to preserve this class\n and perpetuate its métier, he wishes to lock up half a million of the working\n class against their will in a great moral workhouse.\n Is this industry worth retaining? asks Mr. Potter. Certainly,\n by every honourable means! we answer. Is it worth\n while to keep the machinery in order? asks Mr. Potter again.\n Here we hesitate. By machinery Mr. Potter means\nhuman machinery, for he protests that he does not intend\nto treat it as absolute property. We must\n confess that we do not consider it ‘worth while,’ or even possible, to keep\n human machinery in order, i.e. to lock it up and\n oil it until it is needed.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"18.52","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0053","text":"Human machinery has the\n property of rusting during idleness, however much\n you may oil or rub it. Moreover, human machinery, as the\n evidence of our eyes has just taught us, is capable of letting on steam of its own\n accord and bursting, or of raving in a St. Vitus’s dance in our great cities. It may,\n as Mr. Potter says, require a longer time for the repro-\n duction of the workers, but with machinists\n and money at hand we shall always find enterprising, hard, industrial men\n in order to manufacture from them more factory masters than\n we can ever consume. . . . Mr. Potter prates of a\n revival of industry in 1, 2, 3 years and demands that we should not\nencourage or permit the emigration of labour-power!”","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"18.53","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0054","text":"He says that it is natural for the workers to wish to emi-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"565\", \"facs\": \"#f0584\"} --> grate, but he means that the nation must lock up these half-million workers, together with the\n 700,000 dependent upon them, in the cotton districts against\n their wishes and, as a necessary consequence, suppress their discontent\n by force and maintain them themselves by alms, all this upon the chance\n that the cotton masters may need them again on any given day. . . . The time has\n come when the great public opinion of these islands must do something\n to save ‘this working power’ from those who wish to treat\n it as they treat coal, iron, and cotton.” (“to save this ‘working power’ from those who would deal with it as they deal with\n iron, coal and cotton.”) [Note: Times, 24 March 1863.]","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"18.54","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0055","text":"The Times article was merely a jeu d’esprit. The “great public\n opinion” was in fact of Mr. Potter’s opinion, namely that the\n factory workers were movable appurtenances of the factories. Their emigration was\n prevented [Note: Parliament voted not a farthing for emigration, but only laws\n which empowered the municipalities to keep the workers between life and\n death or to exploit them, without payment of normal wages. When,\n on the other hand, the cattle plague broke out three years later, Parliament\n wildly broke through even all parliamentary etiquette and voted millions at once for the\n indemnification of the millionaire landlords, whose tenants had in any case indemnified themselves through\n rises in meat prices.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"18.55","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0056","text":"The bestial roar of the land-\n owners at the opening of Parliament in 1866 proved that one need not be a Hindu\n to worship the cow Sabala, nor Jupiter in order to transform oneself into an\n ox.] . They were shut up in the “moral workhouse” of the cotton\n districts, and still form “the strength (the strength) of\n the cotton masters of Lancashire.”","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"18.56","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0057","text":"The capitalist process of production thus reproduces, through its\n own course, the separation between labour-power and the conditions of labour.\n It thereby reproduces and perpetuates the conditions of exploitation\n of the worker. It constantly compels the worker to sell\n his labour-power in order to live, and constantly enables the capitalist\n to purchase it in order to enrich himself [Note: “L’ouvrier demandait de la subsistance pour vivre, le chef demandait du\n travail pour gagner.” (Sismondi l. c. p. 91.)] . It is no longer chance\n that confronts capitalist and worker as buyer and seller on the commodity\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"566\", \"facs\": \"#f0585\"} --> market.","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"18.57","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0058","text":"It is the vice of the process itself that\n constantly hurls the one back onto the commodity market as seller of his labour-power,\n and constantly transforms his own product into the means of purchase of the other.\n In fact the worker belongs to capital before he sells himself\n to the capitalist. His economic bondage [Note: A peasant-coarse form of this bondage exists in the county of\nDurham. This is one of the few counties in which circumstances\n do not secure the tenant an undisputed title of ownership over the agricultural day labourers.\n The mining industry permits the latter a choice. The tenant, contrary\n to the rule, therefore here undertakes leases only of lands upon which cottages\n for the workers are situated. The rental price of the cottage forms part of the wage.\n These cottages are called “hind’s houses.”","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"18.58","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0059","text":"They are rented to the workers under\n certain feudal obligations, under a contract called “bondage”\n (servitude), and which binds the worker, for example,\n during the time in which he is employed elsewhere, to supply his daughter, etc. The worker himself\n is called a bondsman, a serf. This relationship also displays the individual\n consumption of the worker as consumption for capital, or pro-\n ductive consumption, from a wholly new side. “It is remarkable to\n observe how even the dung of this bondsman counts among the emoluments of his cal-\n culating master . . . . The tenant permits no privy in the whole neighbourhood except his own and\n tolerates no encroachment in this respect upon his rights as suzerain.” (“Public Health. VII. Rep. 1865”,\n p.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"18.59","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0060","text":"188.)] is at the same\n time mediated and at the same time concealed by the periodic renewal\n of his self-sale, the change of his individual wage-masters, and the\n oscillation in the market price of labour [Note: It will be remembered that in the case of the labour of children, etc., even the for-\n mality of self-sale disappears.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"18.60","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0061","text":"The capitalist process of production, considered in its connection,\n or as a process of reproduction, thus produces not only commodity, not only\n surplus-value; it produces and reproduces the capital relation\n itself—on the one side the capitalist, on the other the\n wage-worker [Note: “Capital presupposes wage-labour, wage-labour presupposes capital.\n They condition one another, they reciprocally bring one another forth. Does a\n worker in a cotton factory produce only cotton fabrics? No, he\n produces capital. He produces values which again serve to command his\n labour and, through it, to create new values.”\n (Karl Marx: “Wage-Labour and Capital” in N. Rh. Z. No. 266,] .","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"18.61","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0062","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"567\", \"facs\": \"#f0586\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"18.62","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_EINR:P0062"]}],"source_digest":"37d2620c451500acf6448f1f1f1384fdf1b0b6422b3b012c9287ef5086febb2a","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM","title":"b) Verwandlung von Mehrwerth in Kapital.","order":19,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_BVVM","text":"Früher hatten wir zu betrachten, wie der Mehrwerth aus dem Kapital,\n jetzt wie das Kapital aus dem Mehrwerth entspringt. Anwendung\n von Mehrwerth als Kapital oder Rückverwandlung von\n Mehrwerth in Kapital heisst Accumulation des Kapitals [Note: „Accumulation of Capital: the employment of a portion of reve-\n nue as capital.“ (Malthus: „Definitions etc.“ ed. Cazenove p. 11.)\n „Conversion of revenue into Capital.“ (Malthus: „Princ. of Pol.\n Econ. 2nd ed. Lond. 1836“, p. 319.)] .\n\n\n\n\nEin Kapital betrage 10,000 Pfd. St., sein variabler Bestandtheil\n 2000 Pfd. St. Bei einer Rate des Mehrwerths von 100 % producirt es\n in einer gewissen Periode, jährlich z. B., einen Mehrwerth von 2000\n Pfd. St. Werden diese 2000 Pfd. St. wieder als Kapital vorgeschossen,\n so wächst das ursprüngliche Kapital von 10,000 auf 12,000 Pfd. St. oder\n es hat accumulirt. Es ist zunächst gleichgiltig, ob das Zuschusskapital\n zum alten geschlagen oder selbstständig verwerthet wird.\n\n\n\n\nEine Werthsumme von 2000 Pfd. St. ist eine Werthsumme von\n 2000 Pfd. St. Man riecht und sieht diesem Geld nicht an, dass es\nMehrwerth ist. Der Charakter eines Werths als Mehrwerth zeigt,\nwie er zu seinem Eigner kam, ändert aber nichts an der Natur des Werths\n oder des Geldes. Die Verwandlung der zuschüssigen 2000 Pfd. St. in\n Kapital geht also in derselben Weise vor, wie die Verwandlung der ur-\n sprünglichen 10,000 Pfd. St. Die Bedingungen der Metamorphose blei-\n ben dieselben. Ein Theil der 2000 Pfd. St. muss in constantes, der\n andre in variables Kapital verwandelt werden, der eine in die objektiven\n Faktoren des Arbeitsprozesses, Arbeitsmaterial und Arbeitsmittel, der\n andre in seinen subjektiven Faktor, Arbeitskraft. Der Kapitalist muss\n also diese Elemente auf dem Waarenmarkt vorfinden. So stellt sich der Vor-\n gang dar vom Standpunkt des individuellen Kapitalisten, der die Geld-\n summe von 10,000 Pfd. St. in einen Waarenwerth von 12,000 Pfd. St. ver-\n wandelt, diesen Waarenwerth in Geld zum Belauf von 12,000 Pfd. St. rück-\n verwandelt, und nun neben dem ursprünglichen Werth von 10,000 Pfd. St.\n [Note: 7. April 1849.) Die unter diesem Titel in der N. Rh. Z. veröffentlichten\n Artikel sind Bruchstücke der Vorlesungen, die ich über jenes Thema 1847 im\n deutschen Arbeiterverein zu Brüssel hielt und deren Druck durch die Februar-\n revolution unterbrochen wurde.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"568\", \"facs\": \"#f0587\"} --> auch den zuschüssigen Werth von 2000 Pfd. St. als sein Kapital funktio-\n niren lässt. Betrachten wir aber die 10,000 Pfd. St. als das gesell-\n schaftliche Kapital oder als das Gesammtkapital der Ka-\n pitalistenklasse, und die 2000 Pfd. St. als ihren während des Jahrs\n z. B. producirten Mehrwerth! Der Mehrwerth ist verkörpert in einem\n zuschüssigen Produkt oder Mehrprodukt. Ein Theil dieses Mehrpro-\n dukts geht in den Konsumtionsfonds der Kapitalisten ein oder wird\n von ihnen als Revenue verzehrt. Abgesehn von diesem Theil, ebenso vom\n internationalen Handel, der inländische durch ausländische Waarensorten\n ersetzt, besteht das Mehrprodukt, in seiner Naturalform, nur aus\nProduktionsmitteln, Rohstoffen, Hilfsstoffen, Arbeitsmitteln, und\n aus nothwendigen Lebensmitteln, also aus den stofflichen\n Elementen des constanten und variablen Kapitals. Diese finden sich\n also nicht zufällig auf dem Markt vor, sondern sind bereits vorhandne\n Existenzweisen des producirten Mehrwerths selbst. Was aber die er-\n heischte zuschüssige Arbeit angeht, so können bis zu einem gewissen Grad\n die bereits funktionirenden Arbeitskräfte voller beschäftigt, extensiv oder\n intensiv höher angespannt werden. Andrerseits hat der kapitalistische\n Produktionsprozess mit den sachlichen Elementen des zuschüssigen Kapi-\n tals auch bereits zuschüssige Arbeitskräfte geliefert. Da nämlich die Ar-\n beiterklasse aus dem Prozess herauskömmt, wie sie in ihn eintrat, müssen\n verschiedne Altersklassen ihrer Kinder, deren Existenz der Durchschnitts-\n lohn sichert, beständig neben sie auf den Arbeitsmarkt treten. Konkret\n betrachtet ist die Accumulation also kapitalistischer Reproduk-\n tionsprozess auf erweiterter Stufenleiter.\n\n\n\n\nDen in Zuschusskapital verwandelten Mehrwerth von 2000 Pfd. St.\n wollen wir Surpluskapital Nr. I nennen. Der Vereinfachung wegen\n vorausgesetzt, seine Theilung in constanten und variablen Bestandtheil\n bleibe dieselbe wie beim ursprünglichen Kapital, ebenso die Rate des Mehr-\n werths von 100 %, und wir kennen die Methode, worin diess Kapital von\n 2000 Pfd. St. einen Mehrwerth von 400 Pfd. St. producirt. Dieser Mehr-\n werth werde wieder in Kapital verwandelt. So erhalten wir Surplus-\n kapital Nr. II von 400 Pfd. St. u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nWas hat sich nun geändert? Die 10,000 Pfd. St., die sich ur-\n sprünglich in Kapital verwandelten, waren das Eigenthum ihres Besitzers.\n Er warf sie auf den Waaren- und Arbeitsmarkt. Wo hat er sie her? Wir\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"569\", \"facs\": \"#f0588\"} --> wissen es nicht. Das Gesetz des Waarenaustausches, wonach sich im\n Durchschnitt Aequivalente austauschen und jeder nur mit Waare Waare\n kauft, begünstigt die Annahme, dass die 10,000 Pfd. St. nur die Geld-\n form seiner eignen Produkte und daher seiner eignen Arbeit sind, oder\n der Arbeit von Personen, als deren rechtmässiger Stellvertreter er funk-\n tionirt.\n\n\n\n\nDen Entstehungsprozess des Surpluskapitals Nr. I kennen wir dagegen\n ganz genau. Es ist nur die verwandelte Form von Mehrwerth,\n also von Mehrarbeit, unbezahlter fremder Arbeit. Es ist kein Werth-\n atomdarin, wofür sein Besitzer ein Aequivalent gezahlt hätte. Allerdings\n kauft der Kapitalist, wie vorher mit einem Theil des ursprünglichen, so jetzt\n mit einem Theil des Surplus-Kapitals von neuem Arbeitskraft, woraus er von\n neuem Mehrarbeit pumpt und daher von neuem Mehrwerth producirt. Aber\n er kauft den Arbeiter jetzt mit dessen eignem, ihm vorher ohne\n Aequivalent weggenommenen Produkt oder Produktenwerth,\n ganz wie er ihn mit Produktionsmitteln beschäftigt, die in natura\n oder deren Werth ohne Aequivalent weggenommenes Produkt\n des Arbeiters sind. Es ändert durchaus nichts an der Sache, ob die-\n selben individuellen Arbeiter, die das Surpluskapital producirt haben, auch\n damit beschäftigt werden, oder ob mit der in Geld verwandelten unbezahl-\n ten Arbeit des Arbeiters A der Arbeiter B geworben wird. Diess ändert\n nur die Erscheinung, ohne sie zu verschönern. Da das Verhältniss\n des individuellen Kapitalisten und des individuellen Arbeiters das von ein-\n ander unabhängiger Waarenbesitzer ist, von denen der eine Arbeitskraft\n kauft, der andre verkauft, ist ihr Zusammenhang zufällig. Der Kapi-\n talist verwandelt vielleicht das Surpluskapital in eine Maschine, die den\n Producenten des Surpluskapitals aufs Pflaster wirft und durch ein paar\n Kinder ersetzt.\n\n\n\n\nIn dem Surpluskapital Nr. I sind alle Bestandtheile Produkt unbezahlter\n fremder Arbeit, kapitalisirter Mehrwerth. Es verschwindet der Schein\n der ersten Darstellung des Produktionsprozesses oder des ersten Akts der\n Kapitalbildung, als ob der Kapitalist irgend welche Werthe aus seinem\n eignen Fonds in die Cirkulation würfe. Erst entführt die unsichtbare\n Magie des Prozesses das Mehrprodukt des Arbeiters von seinem Pol zum\n Gegenpol des Kapitalisten. Dann verwandelt der Kapitalist diesen Reich-\n thum, der für ihn eine Schöpfung aus Nichts ist, in Kapital, in ein Mittel\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"570\", \"facs\": \"#f0589\"} --> zur Anwendung, Beherrschung und Exploitation zuschüssiger Arbeits-\n kraft [Note: „Die Arbeit schafft das Kapital, bevor das Kapital die Arbeit anwendet.“\n („Labour creates capital, before capital employs labour.“) E. G. Wakefield:\n „England and America. Lond. 1833“, v. II, p. 110.] .\n\n\n\n\nIm kapitalistischen Produktionsprozess wird ursprünglich\n nur eine dem Geldbesitzer, wir wissen nicht auf welche Titel hin, gehörige\nWerthsumme in Kapital und daher Quelle von Mehrwerth ver-\n wandelt. Es geht eine Veränderung mit dieser Werthsumme vor, aber sie\n selbst ist nicht das Resultat des Prozesses, sondern vielmehr seine\n von ihm unabhängige Voraussetzung. Im einfachen Repro-\n duktionsprozess, oder dem kontinuirlichen Produktionsprozess, ist es\n ein Theil vom Produkt des Arbeiters, der ihm stets von neuem als varia-\n bles Kapital gegenübertritt, aber sein Produkt nimmt stets von neuem\n diese Form an, weil er ursprünglich seine Arbeitskraft für das\n Geld des Kapitalisten verkaufte. Endlich verwandelt sich im\n Verlauf der Reproduktion aller vom Kapitalisten vorgeschossene Kapi-\n talwerth in kapitalisirten Mehrwerth, aber diese Verwandlung\n selbst unterstellt, dass der Fonds ursprünglich aus seinen eignen Mitteln\n herstammt. Anders im Accumulationsprozess oder dem Repro-\n duktionsprozess auf erweiterter Stufenleiter. Das Geld, oder stofflich aus-\n gedrückt, die Produktions- und Lebensmittel, die Substanz des neuen\n Kapitals, ist selbst das Produkt des Prozesses, der fremde unbezahlte Ar-\n beit auspumpt. Das Kapital hat Kapital producirt.\n\n\n\n\nEine dem Kapitalisten gehörige Werthsumme von 10,000 Pfd. St.\n war die Voraussetzung für Bildung des Surpluskapitals\n Nr. I von 2000 Pfd. St. Die Voraussetzung des Surpluskapi-\n tals Nr. II von 400 Pfd. St. ist nichts anders als die Existenz des\n Surpluskapitals Nr. I. Eigenthum an vergangner unbezahlter\n Arbeit erscheint jetzt als die einzige Bedingung für gegenwärtige An-\n eignung unbezahlter lebendiger Arbeit in stets wachsendem Umfang.\n\n\n\n\nInsofern der Mehrwerth, woraus Surpluskapital Nr. I besteht, das Re-\n sultat des Ankaufs der Arbeitskraft durch einen Theil des Originalkapitals\n war, ein Kauf, der den Gesetzen des Waarenaustausches entsprach, und,\n juristisch betrachtet, nichts voraussetzt als freie Verfügung, auf Seiten des\n Arbeiters über seine eignen Fähigkeiten, auf Seiten des Geld- oder Waaren-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"571\", \"facs\": \"#f0590\"} --> besitzers über ihm gehörige Werthe; sofern Surpluskapital Nr. II u. s. w.\n bloss Resultat von Surpluskapital Nr. I, also Konsequenz jenes ersten Ver-\n hältnisses; sofern jede einzelne Transaktion fortwährend dem Gesetz des\n Waarenaustausches entspricht, der Kapitalist stets die Arbeitskraft kauft,\n der Arbeiter sie stets verkauft, und wir wollen annehmen selbst zu ihrem\n wirklichen Werth, schlägt offenbar das auf Waarenproduktion und\n Waarencirkulation beruhende Gesetz der Aneignung oder\nGesetz des Privateigenthums durch seine eigne, innere,\n unvermeidliche Dialektik in sein direktes Gegentheil\n um [Note: Ganz so nothwendig, wie die Waarenproduktion auf einem gewissen\n Entwicklungsgrad kapitalistische Waarenproduktion wird — ja nur auf\n der Grundlage der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise wird die Waare zur allge-\n meinen, herrschenden Form des Produkts, — ganz so nothwendig schlagen\n die Eigenthumsgesetze der Waarenproduktion in Gesetze der\n kapitalistischen Aneignung um. Man bewundere daher die Pfiffigkeit\nProudhon’s, der das kapitalistische Eigenthum abschaffen will, indem er — die\n ewigen Eigenthumsgesetze der Waarenproduktion geltend macht!] . Der Austausch von Aequivalenten, der als die ursprüngliche\n Operation erschien, hat sich so gedreht, dass nur zum Schein ausge-\n tauscht wird, indem erstens der gegen Arbeitskraft ausgetauschte Kapital-\n theil selbst nur ein Theil des ohne Aequivalent angeeigneten\n fremden Arbeitsproduktes ist, und zweitens von seinem Producenten,\n dem Arbeiter, nicht nur ersetzt, sondern mit neuem Surplus ersetzt wer-\n den muss. Das Verhältniss des Austausches zwischen Ka-\n pitalist und Arbeiter wird also nur dem Cirkulationspro-\n zess angehöriger Schein, blosse Form, die dem Inhalt selbst\n fremd ist und ihn nur mystificirt. Der beständige Kauf und Verkauf der\n Arbeitskraft ist die Form. Der Inhalt ist, dass der Kapitalist einen Theil\n der bereits vergegenständlichten fremden Arbeit, die er sich unauf hörlich\n ohne Aequivalent aneignet, stets wieder gegen grösseres Quantum leben-\n diger fremder Arbeit umsetzt. Ursprünglich erschien uns das Eigenthums-\n recht gegründet auf eigne Arbeit. Wenigstens musste diese Annahme gel-\n ten, da sich nur gleichberechtigte Waarenbesitzer gegenüberstehn, das Mit-\n tel zur Aneignung fremder Waare aber nur die Veräusserung der eignen\n Waare, und letztere nur durch Arbeit herstellbar ist. Eigenthum erscheint\n jetzt, auf Seite des Kapitalisten, als das Recht fremde unbezahlte\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"572\", \"facs\": \"#f0591\"} --> Arbeit oder ihr Produkt, auf Seite des Arbeiters, als Unmöglichkeit, sich\n sein eignes Produkt anzueignen. Die Scheidung zwischen Eigen-\n thum und Arbeit wird zur nothwendigen Konsequenz eines Gesetzes,\n das scheinbar von ihrer Identität ausging [Note: Das Eigenthum des Kapitalisten an dem fremden Arbeitsprodukt „ist\n strenge Konsequenz des Gesetzes der Aneignung, dessen Fundamentalprin-\n cip umgekehrt der ausschliessliche Eigenthumstitel jeden Arbeiters am Pro-\n dukt seiner eignen Arbeit war.“ (Cherbuliez: „Riche ou Pauvre.\n Paris 1841“, p. 58, wo jedoch dieser dialektische Umschlag nicht richtig\n entwickelt wird.)] . Man sah, dass selbst\n bei einfacher Reproduktion alles vorgeschossene Kapital, wie immer ur-\n sprünglich erworben, sich in accumulirtes Kapital oder kapitalisirten Mehr-\n werth verwandelt. Aber im Strom der Produktion wird überhaupt alles\n ursprünglich vorgeschossene Kapital eine verschwindende Grösse (magni-\n tudo evanescens im mathematischen Sinn) verglichen mit dem direkt accu-\n mulirten Kapital, d. h. in Kapital rückverwandelten Mehrwerth oder Mehr-\n produkt, ob nun funktionirend in der Hand, die accumulirt hat, oder in\n fremder Hand. Die politische Oekonomie stellt das Kapital daher über-\n haupt als „accumulirten Reichthum (verwandelten Mehrwerth oder\n Revenue) dar, der von neuem zur Produktion von Mehrwerth verwandt\n wird“ [Note: „Capital viz: accumulated wealth employed with a view to profit.“\nMalthus. l. c. „Capital … consists of wealth saved from revenue,\n and used with a view to profit.“ (R. Jones: „An Introductory Lecture\n on Polit. Econ. Lond. 1833“, p. 16.)] , oder auch den Kapitalisten als „Besitzer des Mehrprodukts“ [Note: „The possessors of surplusproduce or capital.“ („The Source and\n Remedy of the National Difficulties. A Letter to Lord John\n Russell. Lond. 1821.“)] .\n Dieselbe Anschauungsweise besitzt nur andre Form in dem Ausdruck, dass\n alles vorhandne Kapital accumulirter oder kapitalisirter Zins sei, denn\n der Zins ist ein blosses Bruchstück des Mehrwerths [Note: „Capital, with compound interest on every portion of capital saved, is so\n all engrossing, that all the wealth in the world from which income is derived, has\n long ago become the interest on capital.“ (London Economist, 19. July,\n 1859.)] .\n\n\n\n\nBevor wir nun auf einige nähere Bestimmungen der Accumula-\n tion oder der Rückverwandlung von Mehrwerth in Kapital eingehn, ist\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"573\", \"facs\": \"#f0592\"} --> eine von der klassischen Oekonomie ausgeheckte Zweideutigkeit zu be-\n seitigen.\n\n\n\n\nSo wenig die Waaren, die der Kapitalist mit einem Theil des\n Mehrwerths für seine eigne Konsumtion kauft, ihm als Produktions- und\n Verwerthungsmittel dienen, so wenig ist die Arbeit, die er zur Befriedi-\n gung seiner natürlichen und socialen Bedürfnisse kauft, produktive\n Arbeit. Statt durch den Kauf jener Waaren und Arbeit den Mehrwerth\n in Kapital zu verwandeln, verzehrt oder verausgabt er ihn umgekehrt\n als Revenue. Gegenüber der altadlichen Gesinnung, die, wie Hegel richtig\n sagt, „im Verzehren des Vorhandenen besteht“ und namentlich auch im\n Luxus persönlicher Dienste sich breit macht, war es entscheidend wichtig\n für die bürgerliche Oekonomie hervorzuheben, dass das Evangelium der\n neuen Gesellschaft, nämlich Accumulation von Kapital, die Auslage von\n Mehrwerth im Ankauf produktiver Arbeiter als conditio sine qua\n predigt. Andrerseits hatte man gegen das Volksvorurtheil zu polemisiren,\n welches die kapitalistische Produktion mit der Schatzbildung verwechselt [Note: „No political economist of the present day can by saving mean mere\n hoarding: and beyond this contracted and insufficient proceeding, no use of the\n term in reference to the national wealth can well be imagined, but that which must\n arise from a different application of what is saved, founded upon a real distinc-\n tion between the different kinds of labour maintained by it.“ (Malthus l. c.\n p. 38, 39.)] \n und daher wähnt, accumulirter Reichthum sei dem Verbrauch, also der\n Zerstörung in seiner vorhandnen Naturalform, entzogner oder auch vor der\n Cirkulation geretteter Reichthum. Verschluss des Geldes gegen die Cir-\n kulation wäre grade das Gegentheil seiner Verwerthung als Kapital, und\n Waarenaccumulation im schatzbildnerischen Sinn reine Narrheit. Accumu-\n lation von Waaren in grossen Massen ist Resultat einer Cirkulationsstockung\n oder der Ueberproduktion [Note: „Accumulation of stocks … non-exchange … overproduction.“\n (Th. Corbet l. c. p. 14.)] . Allerdings läuft in der Volksvorstellung\n das Bild der im Konsumtionsfonds der Reichen gehäuften, lang-\n sam sich verzehrenden Güter unter. Andrerseits die Vorrathbildung,\n ein Phänomen, das allen Produktionsweisen angehört und wobei wir einen\n Augenblick in der Analyse des Cirkulationsprozesses verweilen werden.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"574\", \"facs\": \"#f0593\"} --> Soweit also ist die klassische Oekonomie im Recht, wenn sie den Verzehr von\n Surplusprodukt durch produktive Arbeiter statt durch unproduktive\n als charakteristisches Phänomen des Accumulationsprozesses\n betont. Aber hier beginnt auch ihr Irrthum. A. Smith hat es zur\n Mode gemacht, die Accumulation als Konsumtion des Mehr-\n produkts durch produktive Arbeiter oder die Kapitalisi-\n rung des Mehrwerths als dessen blossen Umsatz in Ar-\n beitskraft darzustellen. Hören wir z. B. Ricardo: „Man muss\n verstehn, dass alle Produkte eines Landes konsumirt werden; aber es\n macht den grössten Unterschied, den man denken kann, ob sie konsumirt\n werden durch solche, die einen andern Werth reproduciren oder durch\n solche, die ihn nicht reproduciren. Wenn wir sagen, dass Revenue erspart\n und zum Kapital geschlagen wird, so meinen wir, dass der Theil der\n Revenue, von dem es heisst, er sei zum Kapital geschla-\n gen, durch produktive statt durch unproduktive Arbei-\n ter verzehrt wird“ [Note: Ricardo l. c. p. 163 Note] . Es giebt keinen grösseren Irrthum als der\n dem A. Smith von Ricardo und allen Späteren nachgeplauderte, dass „der\n Theil der Revenue, von dem es heisst, er sei zum Kapital geschlagen,\nvon produktiven Arbeitern verzehrt wird.“ Nach dieser\n Vorstellung würde aller Mehrwerth, der in Kapital verwandelt wird, zu\nvariablem Kapital. Er theilt sich vielmehr, wie der ursprünglich\n vorgeschossene Werth, in constantes Kapital und variables\n Kapital, in Produktionsmittel und Arbeitskraft. Arbeitskraft ist die\n Form, worin das variable Kapital innerhalb des Produktionsprozesses\n existirt. In diesem Prozess wird sie selbst vom Kapitalisten verzehrt. Sie\n verzehrt durch ihre Funktion — die Arbeit — Produktionsmittel. Zugleich\n verwandelt sich das im Ankauf der Arbeitskraft gezahlte Geld in\n Lebensmittel, die nicht von der „produktiven Arbeit“, sondern vom\n „produktiven Arbeiter“ verzehrt werden. A. Smith gelangt durch\n eine grundverkehrte Analyse zu dem abgeschmackten Resultat, dass wenn\n auch jedes individuelle Kapital sich in constanten und variablen\n Bestandtheil theilt, das gesellschaftliche Kapital sich in nur varia-\n bles Kapital auflöst oder nur in Zahlung von Arbeitslohn verausgabt wird.\n Z. B. ein Tuchfabrikant verwandle 2000 Pfd. St. in Kapital. Er legt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"575\", \"facs\": \"#f0594\"} --> einen Theil des Geldes im Ankauf von Webern aus, den andern Theil\n in Wollengarn, Wollenmaschinerie u. s. w. Aber die Leute, von\n denen er das Garn und die Maschinerie kauft, zahlen wieder mit einem\n Theil davon Arbeit u. s. w., bis die ganzen 2000 Pfd. St. in Zahlung\n von Arbeitslohn verausgabt sind, oder das ganze durch die 2000 Pfd. St.\n repräsentirte Produkt durch produktive Arbeiter verzehrt ist. Man\n sieht: die ganze Wucht dieses Arguments liegt in dem Wort „u. s. w.“,\n das uns von Pontius zu Pilatus schickt. In der That, A. Smith\n bricht die Untersuchung grade da ab, wo ihre Schwierigkeit beginnt [Note: Trotz seiner „Logik“ kommt Herr J. St. Mill nirgendwo auch nur\n solcher fehlerhaften Analyse seiner Vorgänger auf die Sprünge, welche selbst\n innerhalb des bürgerlichen Horizonts, vom reinen Fachstandpunkt aus, nach Be-\n richtigung schreit. Ueberall registrirt er mit schülermässigem Dogmatismus\n die Gedankenwirren seiner Meister. Auch hier: „The capital itself in the\n long run becomes entirely wages, and when replaced by the sale of produce\nbecomes wages again.“] .\n Im dritten Kapitel des zweiten Buchs werde ich die Analyse\n des wirklichen Zusammenhangs geben. Es wird sich dort zeigen, dass\n A. Smith’s auf alle seine Nachfolger vererbtes Dogma die politische Oeko-\n nomie verhindert hat auch nur den Elementarmechanismus des gesellschaft-\n lichen Reproduktionsprozesses zu begreifen [Note: A. Smith hat in der Darstellung des Reproduktionsprozesses, daher auch\n der Accumulation, nach mancher Seite hin nicht nur keine Fortschritte, sondern\n entschiedene Rückschritte gemacht im Vergleich zu seinen Vorgängern, nament-\n lich den Physiokraten. Mit seiner im Text erwähnten Illusion hängt das eben-\n falls von ihm der politischen Oekonomie vererbte, wahrhaft fabelhafte Dogma zu-\n sammen, dass der Preis der Waaren aus Arbeitslohn, Profit (Zins) und\n Grundrente, also bloss aus Arbeitslohn und Mehrwerth zusammengesetzt\n ist. Von dieser Basis ausgehend, gesteht wenigstens Storch naiv: „Il est im-\n possible de résoudre le prix nécessaire dans ses élémens les plus simples.“\n (Storch l. c. Petersb. Edit. 1815, t. I, p. 140, Note.) Eine schöne ökonomische\n Wissenschaft, die es für unmöglich erklärt, den Preis der Waaren auf seine\n einfachsten Elemente zu analysiren! Das Nähere hierüber wird man erörtert finden\n im 7. Kap. des dritten Buchs.] .\n\n\n\n\nIm ersten Abschnitt dieses Kapitels betrachteten wir den ganzen\n Mehrwerth, resp. das Mehrprodukt, nur als individuellen Konsumtions-\n fonds des Kapitalisten, in diesem Abschnitt bisher nur als seinen\nAccumulationsfonds. Er ist aber weder nur das eine, noch das\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"576\", \"facs\": \"#f0595\"} --> andre, sondern beides zugleich. Ein Theil des Mehrwerths wird vom\n Kapitalisten als Revenue verzehrt [Note: Der Leser wird bemerken, dass das Wort Revenue doppelt gebraucht\n wird, erstens um den Mehrwerth als periodisch aus dem Kapital entspringende\n Frucht, zweitens um den Theil dieser Frucht zu bezeichnen, der vom Kapitalisten\n periodisch verzehrt oder zu seinem Konsumtionsfonds geschlagen wird. Ich behalte\n diesen Doppelsinn bei, weil er mit dem Sprachgebrauch der englischen und fran-\n zösischen Oekonomen harmonirt.] , ein andrer Theil als Kapital\n angewandt oder accumulirt.\n\n\n\n\nDie Masse des Mehrwerths gegeben, hängt die Grösse der\n Accumulation offenbar ab von der Theilung des Mehrwerths in\n Accumulationsfonds und Konsumtionsfonds, in Kapital\n und Revenue. Je grösser der eine Theil, desto kleiner der andre. Die\n Masse des Mehrwerths oder des Mehrprodukts, daher des disponiblen Reich-\n thums eines Landes, die in Kapital verwandelt werden kann, ist daher stets\n grösser als der wirklich in Kapital verwandelte Theil des Mehr-\n werths. Je entwickelter die kapitalistische Produktion in einem Lande, je\n rascher und massenhafter die Accumulation, je reicher das Land, je kolossaler\n daher Luxus und Verschwendung, desto grösser diese Differenz. Vom\n jährlichen Zuwachs des Reichthums abgesehn, besitzt der im Konsumtions-\n fonds der Kapitalisten befindliche, nur allmälig zerstörbare Reichthum zum\n Theil Naturalformen, worin er unmittelbar als Kapital funktioniren\n könnte. Zu den vorhandnen Elementen des Reichthums, die im Produk-\n tionsprozess funktioniren könnten, zählen alle Arbeitskräfte, die gar\n nicht oder zu rein konventionell persönlichen, oft infamen, Dienstleistungen\n verbraucht werden. Die Proportion, worin der Mehrwerth in Kapi-\n tal und Revenue getheilt wird, wechselt unaufhörlich und ist durch Um-\n stände beherrscht, die hier nicht weiter zu entwickeln sind. Das in einem\n Land angewandte Kapital ist daher keine fixe, sondern\n eine fluktuirende Grösse, ein stets variabler und elasti-\n scher Bruchtheil des vorhandnen Reichthums, der als\n Kapital funktioniren kann.\n\n\n\n\nDa die beständige Aneignung des vom Arbeiter producirten Mehrwerths\n oder Mehrprodukts für den Kapitalisten als periodische Fruchttragung seines\n Kapitals erscheint, oder das fremde Arbeitsprodukt, welches er ohne Aequiva-\n lent irgend einer Art usurpirt, einen periodischen Zuwachs seines Privat-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"577\", \"facs\": \"#f0596\"} --> vermögens bildet, ist natürlich auch die Theilung dieses Mehrwerths\n oder Mehrprodukts in Zuschusskapital und Konsumtionsfonds durch einen\n Willensakt seinerseits vermittelt.\n\n\n\n\nNur soweit der Kapitalist personificirtes Kapital ist, hat\n er einen historischen Werth und jenes historische Existenzrecht, das,\n wie der geistreiche Lichnowsky sagt, keinen Datum nicht hat. Nur soweit\n steckt seine eigne transitorische Nothwendigkeit in der transitorischen\n Nothwendigkeit der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise.\n Aber soweit sind auch nicht Gebrauchswerth und Genuss, sondern Tausch-\n werth und dessen Vermehrung sein treibendes Motiv. Als Fanatiker der Ver-\n werthung des Werths zwingt er rücksichtslos die Menschheit zur Produk-\n tion um der Produktion willen, daher zu einer Entwicklung der ge-\n sellschaftlichen Produktivkräfte und zur Schöpfung von materiel-\n len Produktionsbedingungen, welche allein die reale Basis einer\n höheren Gesellschaftsform bilden können, deren Grundprincip die volle und\n freie Entwicklung jedes Individuums ist. Nur als Personifikation des Kapi-\n tals ist der Kapitalist respektabel. Als solche theilt er mit dem Schatzbild-\n ner den absoluten Bereicherungstrieb. Ausserdem zwingen ihn die immanen-\n ten Gesetze der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise, welche die Konkurrenz\n jedem individuellen Kapitalisten als äussere Zwangsgesetze oktroyirt,\n sein Kapital fortwährend auszudehnen, um es zu erhalten. Soweit daher\n sein Thun und Lassen nur Funktion des in ihm mit Willen und Bewusst-\n sein begabten Kapitals, gilt ihm sein eigner Privatkonsum als ein Raub\n an der Accumulation seines Kapitals, wie in der italienischen Buchhal-\n tung Privatausgaben auf der Debetseite des Kapitalisten gegen das Ka-\n pital figuriren. Die Accumulation ist Eroberung der Welt des gesellschaft-\n lichen Reichthums. Sie dehnt mit der Masse des exploitirten Menschen-\n materials zugleich die direkte und indirekte Herrschaft des Kapita-\n listen aus [Note: In der altmodischen, wenn auch sporadisch fortexistirenden, Form des\n Kapitalisten, im Wucherer veranschaulicht Luther sehr gut die Herrschsucht\n als Element des Bereicherungstriebs. „Die Heiden haben können aus der Ver-\n nunfft rechnen, das ein Wucherer sey ein vierfaltiger Dieb und Mörder. Wir\n Christen aber halten sie in solchen ehren, das wir sie schier anbeten umb ihres\n Geldes willen … Wer einem andern seine Narung aussauget, raubet und stilet,\n der thut eben so grossen Mord (so viel an jm ligt) als der einen Hungers sterbet] .\n\n\n [Running matter: I. 37] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"578\", \"facs\": \"#f0597\"} -->\n\nAber die Erbsünde wirkt überall. Mit der Entwicklung der kapi-\n talistischen Produktionsweise, der Accumulation, und des Reichthums, hört\n der Kapitalist auf, blosse Inkarnation des Kapitals zu sein. Er fühlt ein\n „menschliches Rühren“ für seinen eignen Adam und wird so gebildet, die\n Schwärmerei für Ascese als Vorurtheil des altmodischen Schatzbildners zu\n belächeln. Während der klassische Kapitalist den individuellen Konsum\n als Sünde gegen seine Funktion und „Enthaltung“ von der Accumulation\n brandmarkt, ist der modernisirte Kapitalist im Stande, die Accumulation\n als „Entsagung“ seines Genusstriebs aufzufassen. „Zwei Seelen wohnen\n Ach! in seiner Brust, die eine will sich von der andern trennen!“\n\n\n\n\n [Note: und zu Grunde verterbet. Solches thut aber ein Wucherer, und sitzet die weil auf\n seinem Stuel sicher, so er billicher hangen solt am Galgen, und von soviel Raben\n gefressen werden, als er gülden gestolen hatte, wo anders so viel fleisches an jm\n were, das so viel Raben sich drein stücken und teilen kündten. Dieweil hanget\n man die kleinen Diebe … Kleine Diebe ligen in Stöcken gefangen, grosse Diebe\n gehn in gold und seiden prangen … Also ist auch kein grösser Menschenfeind\n auff Erden (nach dem Teuffel) denn ein Geitzhals und Wucherer, denn er will\n über alle menschen Gott sein. Türcken, Krieger, Tyrannen sind auch\n böse Menschen, doch müssen sie lassen die Leute leben und bekennen, dass sie Böse\n und Feinde sind, Und können, ja müssen wol zu weilen sich über etliche erbarmen.\n Aber ein Wucherer und Geitzwanst, der wilt das alle Welt im müsste in Hunger und\n Durst, Trauer und Not verderben, so viel an jm ist, auff das ers alles allein möcht\n haben, und jedermann von jm, als von einem Gott empfahen und ewiglich\nsein Leibeigener sein. Schauben, güldne Kette, Ringe tragen, das maul wischen,\n sich für einen theuren, frommen Mann lassen ansehen und rhümen … Wucher\n ist ein gros und ungeheur monstrum, wie ein Beerwolff, der alles wüstet, mehr den\n kein Cacus, Gerion oder Antus. Und schmückt sich doch und wil fromm sein,\n das man nicht sehen sol, wo die Ochsen, die er rücklings in sein Loch\n zieht, hinkommen. Aber Hercules sol der Ochsen und der Gefangenen Geschrey\n hören und den Cacum suchen auch in Klippen und Felsen, die Ochsen wider\n lösen, von dem Bösewicht. Denn Cacus heisst ein Bösewicht, der ein frommer\n Wucherer ist, stilet, raubet, frisst alles. Und wils doch nicht gethan haben, und\n sol ja nimand finden, weil die Ochsen rücklings in sein Loch ge-\n zogen, schein und fussstapffen geben, als seien sie herausge-\n lassen. Also wil der Wucherer auch die Welt effen, als nütze er und gebe\n der welt ochsen, so er sie doch zu sich allein reisst und frisst …\n Und so man die Strassenreuber, Mörder und Beuheder, redert und köpffet, wie viel\n mehr solt man alle Wucherer redern und edern … verjagen, verfluchen und\n köpffen.“ (Martin Luther l. c.)] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"579\", \"facs\": \"#f0598\"} -->\n\nIn den historischen Anfängen der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise\n — und jeder kapitalistische Parvenü macht diess historische Stadium in-\n dividuell durch — herrschen Bereicherungstrieb und Geiz als absolute\n Leidenschaften vor. Aber der Fortschritt der kapitalistischen Produk-\n tion schafft nicht nur eine Welt von Genüssen. Er öffnet mit der Speku-\n lation und dem Kreditwesen tausend Quellen plötzlicher Bereicherung.\n Auf einer gewissen Entwicklungshöhe wird ein konventioneller Grad von\n Verschwendung, die zugleich Schaustellung des Reichthums und daher\n Kreditmittel ist, sogar zu einer Geschäftsnothwendigkeit des „unglück-\n lichen“ Kapitalisten. Der Luxus geht in die Repräsentationskosten des Ka-\n pitals ein. Ohnehin bereichert sich der Kapitalist nicht, gleich dem Schatz-\n bildner, im Verhältniss seiner persönlichen Arbeit und seines persönlichen\n Nichtkonsums, sondern im Mass, worin er fremde Arbeitskraft aussaugt\n und dem Arbeiter Entsagung aller Lebensgenüsse aufzwingt. Obgleich\n daher die Verschwendung des Kapitalisten nie den bona fide. Charakter\n der Verschwendung des flotten Feudalherrn besitzt, in ihrem Hin-\n tergrund vielmehr stets schmutzigster Geiz und ängstlichste Berechnung\n lauern, wächst dennoch seine Verschwendung mit seiner Accumulation,\n ohne dass die eine die andre zu beabbruchen braucht. Damit entwickelt\n sich gleichzeitig in der Hochbrust des Kapitalindividuums ein faustischer\n Konflikt zwischen Accumulations- und Genusstrieb.\n\n\n\n\n„Die Industrie von Manchester“, heisst es in einer Schrift, die Dr.\n Aikin 1795 veröffentlichte, „kann in vier Perioden getheilt werden.\n In der ersten waren die Fabrikanten gezwungen, hart für ihren Lebens-\n unterhalt zu arbeiten.“ Sie bereicherten sich besonders durch Bestehlung\n der Eltern, die ihnen Jungen als apprentices (Lehrlinge) zuwiesen und da-\n für schwer blechen mussten, während die Lehrlinge ausgehungert wurden.\n Andrerseits waren die Durchschnittsprofite niedrig und die Accumulation\n verlangte grosse Sparsamkeit. Sie lebten wie Schatzbildner und verzehr-\n ten bei weitem nicht einmal die Zinsen ihres Kapitals. „In der zweiten\n Periode hatten sie begonnen, kleine Vermögen zu erwerben, arbeiteten\n aber ebenso hart als zuvor,“ denn die unmittelbare Exploitation der\n Arbeit kostet Arbeit, wie jeder Sklaventreiber weiss, „und lebten nach\n wie vor in demselben frugalen Styl. … In der dritten Periode begann der\n Luxus und das Geschäft wurde ausgedehnt durch Aussendung von Reitern\n (berittenen Commis Voyageurs) für Ordres in jeder Marktstadt des König-\n [Running matter: 37*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"580\", \"facs\": \"#f0599\"} --> reichs. Es ist wahrscheinlich, dass wenige oder keine Kapitalien von\n 3000 bis 4000 Pfd. St., in der Industrie erworben, vor 1690 existirten.\n Um diese Zeit jedoch oder etwas später hatten die Industriellen schon Geld\n accumulirt und begannen steinerne Häuser statt der von Holz und Mörtel\n aufzuführen. … Noch in den ersten Decennien des 18. Jahrhunderts\n setzte sich ein Manchester Fabrikant, der eine Pint fremden Weins seinen\n Gästen vorsetzte, den Glossen und dem Kopfschütteln aller seiner Nach-\n barn aus.“ Vor dem Aufkommen der Maschinerie betrug der abendliche\n Konsum der Fabrikanten in den Kneipen, wo sie zusammenkamen, nie mehr\n als 6 d. für ein Glas Punsch und 1 d. für eine Rolle Taback. Erst 1758,\n und diess macht Epoche, sah man „eine im Geschäft wirklich engagirte\n Person mit eigner Equipage!“ „Die vierte Periode“, das letzte Drittheil\n des 18. Jahrhunderts, „ist die von grossem Luxus und Verschwendung,\n unterstützt durch die Ausdehnung des Geschäfts“ [Note: Dr. Aikin: „Description of the Country from 30 to 40\nmiles round Manchester. Lond. 1795“, p. 182 sqq.] . Was würde der\n gute Dr. Aikin sagen, wenn er heutzutag in Manchester auferstände!\n\n\n\n\nAccumulirt, accumulirt! Das ist Moses und die Propheten! „Die\n Industrie liefert das Material, welches die Sparsamkeit accumulirt“ [Note: A. Smith l. c. b. III, ch. III.] .\n Also spart, spart, d. h. rückverwandelt möglichst grossen Theil des Mehr-\n werths oder Mehrprodukts in Kapital! Accumulation um der Accumula-\n tion, Produktion um der Produktion willen, in dieser Formel sprach die\n klassische Oekonomie den historischen Beruf der Bourgeoisperiode aus.\n Sie täuschte sich keinen Augenblick über die Geburtswehn des Reich-\n thums [Note: Selbst J. B. Say sagt: „Les épargnes des riches se font aux dépens des\n pauvres.“ „Der römische Proletarier lebte fast ganz auf Kosten der Ge-\n sellschaft . . . . Man könnte fast sagen, dass die moderne Gesellschaft auf Kosten\n der Proletarier lebt, von dem Theil, den sie auf Belohnung der Arbeit ihnen ent-\n zieht.“ (Sismondi: „Études etc.“ t. 1, p. 24.)] , aber was nützt der Jammer über historische Nothwendigkeit?\n Wenn der klassischen Oekonomie der Proletarier nur als Maschine zur\n Produktion von Mehrwerth, gilt ihr aber auch der Kapitalist nur als Ma-\n schine zur Verwandlung dieses Mehrwerths in Mehrkapital. Sie nimmt\n seine historische Funktion in bitterm Ernst. Um seinen Busen vor dem\n unheilvollen Konflikt zwischen Genusstrieb und Bereicherungstrieb zu feien,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"581\", \"facs\": \"#f0600\"} --> vertheidigte Malthus, im Anfang der zwanziger Jahre dieses Jahrhun-\n derts, eine Theilung der Arbeit, welche dem wirklich in der Produktion\n begriffenen Kapitalisten das Geschäft der Accumulation, den andern Theil-\n nehmern am Mehrwerth, der Landaristokratie, Staats-, Kirchenpfründnern\n u. s. w. das Geschäft der Verschwendung zuweist. Es ist von der höch-\n sten Wichtigkeit, sagt er, „die Leidenschaft für Ausgabe und die Leiden-\n schaft für Accumulation („the passion for expenditure and the passion for\n accumulation“) getrennt zu halten“ [Note: Malthus l. c. p. 319, 320.] . Die Herrn Kapitalisten, seit lange\n in Lebe- und Weltmänner verwandelt, schrieen auf. Was, rief einer ihrer\n Wortführer, ein Ricardianer, Herr Malthus predigt hohe Grundrenten, hohe\n Steuern u. s. w., um dem Industriellen einen fortwährenden Stachel durch\n unproduktive Konsumenten aufzudrücken! Allerdings Produktion, Pro-\n duktion auf stets erweiterter Stufenleiter, lautet das Schiboleth, aber „Pro-\n duktion wird durch einen solchen Prozess weit mehr gehemmt als geför-\n dert. Auch ist es nicht ganz billig (nor is it quite fair) eine\n Anzahl Personen so im Müssiggang zu erhalten, nur um andre zu kneipen,\n aus deren Charakter man schliessen darf, („who are likely, from their\n characters“), dass wenn ihr sie zu funktioniren zwingen könnt, sie mit\n Erfolg funktioniren“ [Note: „An Inquiry into those principles respecting the Nature\n of Demand etc.“, p. 67.] . So unbillig er es findet den industriellen Kapi-\n talisten zur Accumulation zu stacheln, indem man ihm das Fett von der\n Suppe wegschöpft, so nothwendig dünkt ihm den Arbeiter möglichst auf\n den Minimallohn zu beschränken, „um ihn arbeitsam zu erhalten“. Auch\n verheimlicht er keinen Augenblick, dass Aneignung unbezahlter Arbeit das\n Geheimniss der Plusmacherei ist. „Vermehrte Nachfrage von Seite der\n Arbeiter meint durchaus nichts als ihre Geneigtheit weniger von ihrem\n eignen Produkt für sich selbst zu nehmen und einen grössren\n Theil davon ihren Anwendern zu überlassen; und wenn man sagt,\n dass diess, durch Verminderung der Konsumtion (auf Seiten der Arbeiter)\nglut (Marktüberfüllung, Ueberproduktion) erzeugt, so kann ich nur ant-\n worten, dass glut synonym mit hohem Profit ist“ [Note: l. c. p. 50.] .\n\n\n\n\nDer gelehrte Zank, wie die dem Arbeiter ausgepumpte Beute för-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"582\", \"facs\": \"#f0601\"} --> derlichst für die Accumulation zu vertheilen sei zwischen industriellem Kapi-\n talist und müssigem Grundeigenthümer u. s. w., verstummte vor der Juli-\n revolution. Kurz nachher läutete das städtische Proletariat die Sturm-\n glocke zu Lyon und liess das Landproletariat den rothen Hahn in England\n fliegen. Diesseits des Kanals grassirte der Owenismus, jenseits St. Simo-\n nismus und Fourierismus. Die Stunde der Vulgärökonomie hatte ge-\n schlagen. Grade ein Jahr, bevor Nassau W. Senior zu Manchester\n ausfand, dass der Profit (incl. Zins) des Kapitals das Produkt der un-\n bezahlten „letzten zwölften Arbeitsstunde“ ist, hatte er der\n Welt eine andere Entdeckung angekündigt. „Ich“, sagte er feierlich, „ich\nersetze das Wort Kapital, als Produktionsinstrument betrachtet,\ndurch das Wort Abstinenz (Enthaltung)“ [Note: Senior: „Principes fondamentaux del’Écon. Pol.“ trad.\n Arrivabene. Paris 1836, p. 308. Diess war den Anhängern der alten\n klassischen Schule doch etwas zu toll. „Herr Senior schiebt dem Ausdruck\n Arbeit und Kapital den Ausdruck Arbeit und Abstinenz unter … Abstinenz ist\n eine blosse Negation. Es ist nicht die Abstinenz, sondern der Gebrauch des\n produktiv verwandten Kapitals, welcher die Quelle des Profits bildet.“ (John\n Cazenove l. c. p. 130 Note.) Herr John St. Mill excerpirt dagegen auf der\n einen Seite Ricardo’s Profittheorie und annexirt auf der andern Senior’s „remune-\n ration of abstinence.“ So fremd ihm der Hegel’sche „Widerspruch“, die Spring-\n quelle aller Dialektik, so heimisch ist er in platten Widersprüchen.] . Ein unübertroffenes\n Muster diess von den „Entdeckungen“ der Vulgärökonomie! Sie ersetzt\n eine ökonomische Kategorie durch eine sykophantische Phrase. Voilà\n tout. „Wenn der Wilde“, docirt Senior, „Bogen fabricirt, so übt er\n eine Industrie aus, aber er prakticirt nicht die Absti-\n nenz.“ Diess erklärt uns, wie und warum in früheren Gesellschaftszu-\n ständen „ohne die Abstinenz“ des Kapitalisten Arbeitsmittel fabricirt\n wurden. „Je mehr die Gesellschaft fortschreitet, um so mehr Abstinenz\n erfordert sie“ [Note: Senior l. c. p. 342.] , nämlich von denen, welche die Industrie ausüben, sich\n die fremde Industrie und ihr Produkt anzueignen. Alle Bedingun-\n gen des Arbeitsprozesses verwandeln sich von nun in\n ebenso viele Abstinenzpraktiken des Kapitalisten.\n Dass Korn nicht nur gegessen, sondern auch ausgesät wird,\n Abstinenz des Kapitalisten! Dass der Wein die Zeit erhält auszu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"583\", \"facs\": \"#f0602\"} --> gähren, Abstinenz des Kapitalisten! [Note: „No one … will sow his wheat, f. i., and allow it to remain a twelve-\n month in the ground, or leave his wine in a cellar for years, instead of consuming\n these things or their equivalent at once — — unless he expects to acquire ad-\n ditional value etc.“ (Scrope: „Polit. Econ.“ edit. von A. Potter,\n New-York 1841, p. 133, 134.)]  Der Kapitalist beraubt seinen\n eignen Adam, wenn er die „Produktionsinstrumente dem Arbeiter\n leiht“ (!), alias sie durch Einverleibung der Arbeitskraft als Kapi-\n tal verwerthet, statt Dampfmaschinen, Baumwolle, Eisenbahnen, Dünger,\n Zugpferde u. s. f. aufzuessen oder, wie der Vulgärökonom sich das\n kindlich vorstellt, „ihren Werth“ in Luxus- und andren Konsum-\n tionsmitteln zu verprassen [Note: „La privation que s’impose le capitaliste, en prêtant (dieser\n Euphemismus gebraucht, um nach probater vulgärökonomischer Manier den vom\n industriellen Kapitalisten exploitirten Lohnarbeiter mit dem industriellen\n Kapitalisten selbst zu identificiren, welcher vom Geld verleihenden Ka-\n pitalisten pumpt!) ses instruments de production au travailleur au lieu d’en\n consacrer la valeurà son propre usage, en la transformant en objets\n d’utilité ou d’agrément.“ (G. de Molinari l. c. p. 49.)] . Wie die Kapitalistenklasse das\n anstellen soll, ist ein von der Vulgärökonomie bisher hartnäckig bewahrtes\n Geheimniss. Genug, die Welt lebt nur noch von der Selbstkasteiung\n dieses modernen Büssers des Wischnu, des Kapitalisten. Nicht nur die\n Accumulation, die einfache „Erhaltung eines Kapitals erheischt beständige\n Kraftanstrengung, um der Versuchung zu widerstehn, es aufzuessen“ [Note: „La conservation d’un capital exige … un effort constant pour résister\n à la temptation de le consommer.“ (Courceuil-Senelles l. c. p. 57.)] .\n Die einfache Humanität gebeut also offenbar den Kapitalisten von Martyr-\n thum und Versuchung zu erlösen, in derselben Weise wie der georgische\n Sklavenhalter jüngst durch Abschaffung der Sklaverei von dem schmerz-\n lichen Dilemma erlöst ward, ob das dem Negersklaven ausgepeitschte\n Mehrprodukt ganz in Champagner zu verjubeln oder auch theilweis in\n mehr Neger und mehr Land rückzuverwandeln.\n\n\n\n\nIn den verschiedensten ökonomischen Gesellschaftsformationen findet\n nicht nur einfache Reproduktion, sondern, obgleich auf verschiednem Mass-\n stab, Reproduktion auf erweiterter Stufenleiter statt. Es\n wird progressiv mehr producirt und mehr konsumirt, also auch mehr Pro-\n dukt in Produktionsmittel verwandelt. Dieser Prozess erscheint aber\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"584\", \"facs\": \"#f0603\"} --> nicht als Accumulation von Kapital und daher auch nicht als\nFunktion des Kapitalisten, so lange dem Arbeiter seine Produk-\n tionsmittel, daher auch sein Produkt und seine Lebensmittel, noch nicht\nin der Form von Kapital gegenüberstehn [Note: „The particular classes of income which yield the most abundantly to the\n progress of national capital, change at different stages of their progress, and are\n therefore entirely different in nations occupying different positions in that progress\n … Profit … unimportant source of accumulation, compared with wages and\n rents, in the earlier stages of society … When a considerable advance in the\n powers of national industry has actually taken place, profits rise into comparative\n importance as a source of accumulation.“ (Richard Jones: „Textbook\n etc.“, p. 16, 21.)] . Der vor eini-\n gen Jahren verstorbene Richard Jones, Nachfolger von Malthus auf\n dem Lehrstuhl der politischen Oekonomie zu Herford, erörtert diess gut an\n zwei grossen Thatsachen. Da der zahlreichste Theil des indischen Volks\n selbstwirthschaftende Bauern, existirt ihr Produkt, ihre Arbeits- und\n Lebensmittel, auch nie „in der Form („in the shape“) eines Fonds, der\n aus fremder Revenue erspart wird („saved from revenue“) und\n daher einen vorläufigen Prozess der Accumulation („a previous\n process of accumulation“) durchlaufen hat“ [Note: l. c. p. 36 sq.] . Andrerseits werden die\n nicht-agrikolen Arbeiter in den Provinzen, wo die englische Herrschaft das alte\n System am wenigsten aufgelöst hat, direkt von den Grossen beschäftigt,\n denen eine Portion des ländlichen Mehrprodukts als Tribut oder Grund-\n rente zufliesst. Ein Theil dieses Produkts wird in Naturalform von den\n Grossen verzehrt, ein andrer Theil für sie von den Arbeitern in Luxus-\n und sonstige Konsumtionsmittel verwandelt, während der Rest den Lohn\n der Arbeiter bildet, die Eigenthümer ihrer Arbeitsinstrumente sind. Pro-\n duktion und Reproduktion auf erweiterter Stufenleiter gehn hier ihren\n Gang ohne alle Dazwischenkunft jenes wunderlichen Heiligen, jenes Rit-\n ters von der traurigen Gestalt, des „entsagenden“ Kapitalisten.\n\n\n\n\nWir betrachteten bisher die Masse des Mehrwerths als ge-\n gebne Grösse. In diesem Fall bestimmte seine proportionelle\n Theilung in Revenue und Surpluskapital den Umfang der\n Accumulation. Aber letztere wechselt unabhängig von jener Theilung\n mit dem Wechsel in der Grösse des Mehrwerths selbst.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"585\", \"facs\": \"#f0604\"} --> Die Umstände, welche die Grösse des Mehrwerths regeln, sind in den Ka-\n piteln über seine Produktion ausführlich entwickelt worden. Sie reguliren,\n unter sonst gleichbleibenden Verhältnissen, die Bewegung der Accumula-\n tion. Wir kehren hier nur so weit zu ihnen zurück, als sie mit Bezug\n auf die Accumulation neue Gesichtspunkte bieten.\n\n\n\n\nMan erinnert sich, welche Rolle der Exploitationsgrad der\n Arbeit in der Produktion des Mehrwerths spielt. Die politische Oeko-\n nomie würdigt diese Rolle so sehr, dass sie gelegentlich die Beschleunigung\n der Accumulation durch erhöhte Produktivkraft der Arbeit\n mit ihrer Beschleunigung durch erhöhte Exploitation des Arbei-\n ters identificirt [Note: „Ricardo sagt: ‚In verschiednen Stadien der Gesellschaft ist die Accu-\n mulation des Kapitals oder der Mittel Arbeit anzuwenden (sc. zu exploitiren) mehr\n oder weniger rasch und muss in allen Fällen von den Produktivkräften der Arbeit\n abhängen. Die Produktivkräfte der Arbeit sind im Allgemeinen am grössten, wo\n Ueberfluss von fruchtbarem Boden existirt.‘ Bedeuten in dieser Sentenz die\n Produktivkräfte der Arbeit die Kleinheit des aliquoten Theils\n jeden Produkts, der denen zufällt, deren Handarbeit es produ-\n cirt, so ist der Satz tautologisch, weil der übrig bleibende Theil der\n Fonds ist, woraus, wenn es seinem Eigner beliebt („if the owner pleases“),\n Kapital accumulirt werden kann. Aber diess ist meistens nicht der Fall, wo das\n Land am fruchtbarsten ist.“ („Observations on certain verbal dis-\n putes etc.“, p. 74, 75.)] . In den Abschnitten über die Produktion des Mehr-\n werths ward beständig unterstellt, dass der Arbeitslohn wenigstens gleich\n dem Werth der Arbeitskraft ist. Es ward ferner gezeigt, dass der Ar-\n beitslohn, sei es seinem Werth nach, sei es nach der Masse von Lebensmitteln,\n die er repräsentirt, bei wachsendem Exploitationsgrad des Arbeiters\nwachsen kann. In der praktischen Bewegung des Kapitals jedoch wird\n auch Mehrwerth producirt durch gewaltsame Herabsetzung des Ar-\n beitslohns unter den Werth der Arbeitskraft. Faktisch wird\n so ein Theil des nothwendigen Konsumtionsfonds des\n Arbeiters in einen Accumulationsfonds von Kapital ver-\n wandelt.\n\n\n\n\n„Arbeitslöhne“, sagt J. St. Mill, „haben keine Produktivkraft; sie\n sind der Preis einer Produktivkraft. Arbeitslöhne tragen nicht, neben der\n Arbeit selbst, zur Waarenproduktion bei, sowenig als der Preis der Ma-\n schinerie neben der Maschinerie selbst. Könnte Arbeit ohne Kauf\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"586\", \"facs\": \"#f0605\"} --> gehabt werden, so wären Arbeitslöhne überflüssig“ [Note: J. St. Mill: „Essays on some unsettled Questions of Polit.\n Economy. Lond. 1844“, p. 90.] . Wenn\n aber die Arbeiter von der Luft leben könnten, so wären sie auch um\nkeinen Preis zu kaufen. Ihr Nichtskosten ist also eine Grenze\n im mathematischen Sinn, stets unerreichbar, obgleich stets annäher-\n bar. Es ist die beständige Tendenz des Kapitals sie auf diesen\nnihilistischen Standpunkt herabzudrücken. Ein oft von mir citir-\n ter Schriftsteller des 18. Jahrhunderts, der Verfasser des „Essay\n on Trade and Commerce“, verräth nur das innerste Seelenge-\n heimniss des englischen Kapitals, wenn er es für die historische\n Lebensaufgabe Englands erklärt, den englischen Arbeitslohn auf\n das französische und holländische Niveau herabzudrücken [Note: „An Essay on Trade and Commerce. Lond. 1770“, p. 44.\n Aehnlich brachte die Times vom December 1866 und Januar 1867 Herzenser-\n giessungen englischer Minenbesitzer, worin der glückliche Zustand der belgi-\n schen Minenarbeiter geschildert ward, die nicht mehr verlangten und nicht mehr\n erhielten, als strikt nöthig, um für ihre „masters“ zu leben. Die belgischen Ar-\n beiter dulden viel, aber als Musterarbeiter in der Times zu figuriren! Anfang\n Februar 1867 antwortete der mit Pulver und Blei unterdrückte Strike der belgischen\n Minenarbeiter (bei Marchienne).] . Er sagt\n u. a. naiv: „Wenn aber unsre Armen (Kunstausdruck für Arbeiter)\n luxuriös leben wollen … muss ihre Arbeit natürlich theuer sein.\n … Man betrachte nur die haarsträubende Masse von Ueber-\n flüssigkeiten („heap of superfluities“), die unsre Manufakturarbeiter\n verzehren, als da sind Branntwein, Gin, Thee, Zucker, fremde Früchte,\n starkes Bier, gedruckte Leinwand, Schnupf- und Rauchtabak u. s. w.“ [Note: l. c. p. 46.] .\n Er citirt die Schrift eines Fabrikanten von Northamptonshire, der mit him-\n melwärts schielendem Blick jammert: „Arbeitist ein ganzes Drit-\n theil wohlfeiler in Frankreich als in England: denn die franzö-\n sischen Armen arbeiten hart und fahren hart an Nahrung und Kleidung\n und ihr Hauptkonsum sind Brod, Früchte, Kräuter, Wurzeln und getrock-\n neter Fisch; denn sie essen sehr selten Fleisch, und wenn der Weizen\n theuer ist, sehr wenig Brod“ [Note: Der Fabrikant von Northamptonshire begeht eine im Herzensdrang ent-\n schuldbare pia fraus. Er vergleicht angeblich das Leben englischer und französi-] . „Wozu,“ fährt der Essay man fort,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"587\", \"facs\": \"#f0606\"} --> „wozu noch kömmt, dass ihr Getränk aus Wasser besteht oder ähn-\n lichen schwachen Likören, so dass sie in der That erstaunlich\n wenig Geld ausgeben. . . . Ein derartiger Zustand der Dinge ist sicher-\n lich schwer herbeizuführen, aber er ist nicht unerreichbar, wie seine\n Existenz sowohl in Frankreich als Holland schlagend beweist“ [Note: l. c. p. 70.] . Zwei\n Jahrzehnte später verfolgte ein amerikanischer Humbug, der baronisirte\n Yankee Benjamin Thomson (alias Graf Rumford), dieselbe Philan-\n thropielinie mit grossem Wohlgefallen vor Gott und den Menschen. Seine\n „Essays“ sind ein Kochbuch mit Recepten aller Art um Surrogate an\n die Stelle der theuren Normalspeisen des Arbeiters zu setzen. Ein be-\n sonders gelungenes Recept dieses wunderlichen „Philosophen“ ist folgen-\n des: „Fünf Pfund Gerste, fünf Pfund Mais, für 3 d. Häringe, 1 d. Salz,\n 1 d. Essig, 2 d. Pfeffer und Kräuter — Summa von 20¾ d. giebt eine\n Suppe für 64 Menschen, ja mit den Durchschnittspreisen von Korn kann\n die Kost auf ¼ d. per Kopf (noch nicht 3 Pfennige) herabgedrückt\n werden“ [Note: Benjamin Thomson: „Essays, political, economical,\n and philosophical etc. 3 vol. Lond. 1796—1802.“ In seinem „The\n State of the Poor, or an History of the Labouring Classes in\n England etc.“ empfiehlt Sir M. F. Eden die Rumford’sche Bettelsuppe bestens\n den Vorstehern von Workhouses und mahnt die englischen Arbeiter vorwurfsvoll,\n dass „es bei den Schotten viele Familien giebt, die, statt von Weizen, Roggen\n und Fleisch, Monate lang von Hafergrütze und Gerstenmehl, nur mit Salz und\n Wasser gemischt, leben und das obendrein noch sehr komfortabel“ („and that\n very comfortably too“). (l. c. v. I, b. II, ch. II.) Aehnliche „Fingerzeige“ im\n 19. Jahrhundert. „Die englischen Ackerbauarbeiter“, heisst es z. B., „wollen\n keine Mischungen niederer Kornarten essen. In Schottland, wo die Erziehung\n besser ist, ist diess Vorurtheil wahrscheinlich unbekannt.“ („Charles H.\n Parry M. D.: „The Question of the Necessity of the existing\n Cornlaws considered. Lond. 1816“, p. 69.) Derselbe Parry klagt\n jedoch, dass der englische Arbeiter jetzt (1815) sehr heruntergekommen sei, ver-\n glichen mit Eden’s Zeit (1797).] . Mit dem Fortschritt der kapitalistischen Produktion hat\n die Waarenfälschung Thomson’s Ideale überflüssig gemacht [Note: Aus den Berichten der letzten parlamentarischen Untersuchungskommission\n über Fälschung von Lebensmitteln sieht man, dass selbst die Fälschung der Arz-] .\n [Note: scher Manufakturarbeiter, schildert aber, wie er später in seiner Verdadde-\n rung selbst gesteht, mit den eben citirten Worten französische Agrikultur-\n arbeiter!] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"588\", \"facs\": \"#f0607\"} --> Ende des 18. und während der ersten Decennien des 19. Jahrhunderts\n erzwangen die englischen Pächter und Landlords das absolute Minimal-\n salair, indem sie den Ackerbautaglöhnern weniger als das Minimum in der\n Form des Arbeitslohns, den Rest aber in der Form von Pfarreiunter-\n stützung auszahlten. Ein Beispiel der Possenreisserei, womit die eng-\n lischen Dogberries in ihrer „legalen“ Festsetzung des Lohntarifs ver-\n fuhren: „Als die Squires die Arbeitslöhne für Speenhamland 1795 fest-\n setzten, hatten sie zu Mittag gespeist, dachten aber offenbar, dass die\n Arbeiter nicht desgleichen nöthig hätten. … Sie entschieden, der Wochen-\n lohn solle 3 sh. per Mann sein, wenn das Laib Brod von 8 Pfund 11\n Unzen auf 1 sh. stünde, und er solle regelmässig wachsen, bis das Laib\n 1 sh. 5 d. koste. Sobald es über diesen Preis stiege, sollte der Lohn pro-\n portionell abnehmen, bis der Preis des Laibes 2 sh. erreicht hätte: und\n dann sollte die Nahrung des Mannes ⅕ weniger als vorher sein“ [Note: G. B. Newnham (barrister at law): „A Review of the Evidence\n before the Committees of the two Houses of Parliament on the\n Cornlaws. Lond. 1815“, p. 20 Note.] . Vor\n dem Untersuchungscomité des House of Lords, 1814, wird ein ge-\n wisser A. Bennett, grosser Pächter, Magistrat, Armenhausverwalter und\n Lohnregulator, gefragt: „Wird irgend eine Proportion zwischen dem Werth\n der Tagesarbeit und der Pfarreiunterstützung der Arbeiter beobachtet?“\nAntwort: „Ja. Das wöchentliche Einkommen jeder Familie wird über\n ihren Nominallohn hinaus vollgemacht bis zum Gallonlaib Brod (8 Pfd.\n 11 Unzen) und 3 d. per Kopf. . . . Wir unterstellen das Gallonlaib hin-\n reichend für die Erhaltung jeder Person in der Familie während der\n Woche; und die 3 d. sind für Kleider; und wenn es der Pfairei beliebt,\n die Kleider selbst zu stellen, werden die 3 d. abgezogen. Diese Praxis\n herrscht nicht nur im ganzen Westen von Wiltshire, sondern, wie ich\n glaube, im ganzen Land“ [Note: l. c.] . So, ruft ein Burgeoisschriftsteller jener\n Zeit, „haben die Pächter Jahre lang eine respektable Klasse ihrer Lands-\n leute degradirt, indem sie dieselben zwangen zum Workhouse ihre Zu-\n [Note: neistoffe in England nicht Ausnahme, sondern Regel bildet. Z. B. die Examination\n von 34 Proben von Opium, gekauft in eben so viel verschiednen Londoner Apo-\n theken, ergab, dass 31 verfälscht waren mit Mohnkapsel, Weizenmehl, Gummi-\n schleim, Thon, Sand u. s. w. Viele enthielten kein Atom Morphin.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"589\", \"facs\": \"#f0608\"} --> flucht zu nehmen … Der Pächter hat seine eignen Gewinne ver-\n mehrt, indem er selbst die Accumulation des unentbehr-\n lichsten Konsumtionsfonds auf Seite der Arbeiter ver-\n hinderte“ [Note: Ch. H. Parry l. c. p. 78. Die Herrn Landlords ihrerseits „indemni-\n ficirten“ sich nicht nur für den Antijakobinerkrieg, den sie im Namen Englands\n führten, sondern bereicherten sich enorm. „Ihre Renten verdoppelten, verdrei-\n fachten, vervierfachten und, in Ausnahmsfällen, versechsfachten sich in 18\n Jahren.“ (l. c. p. 100, 101.)] . Welche Rolle heutzutag der direkte Raub am noth-\n wendigen Konsumtionsfonds des Arbeiters in der Bildung des Mehrwerths\n und daher des Accumulationsfonds des Kapitals spielt, hat\n beispielweis die s. g. Hausarbeit gezeigt. Weitere Thatsachen im Ver-\n lauf dieses Kapitels.\n\n\n\n\nDie Elasticität der Arbeitskraft oder ihre Fähigkeit grösserer inten-\n siver oder extensiver Spannung bildet, innerhalb gewisser Grenzen, eine\n vom gegebnen Umfang der bereits funktionirenden und producirten Produk-\n tionsmittel, oder der stofflichen Elemente des constanten\n Kapitals, unabhängige Quelle der Schöpfung zusätzlichen Reichthums\n und daher des Accumulationsfonds. In der extraktiven Industrie,\n der Minenindustrie z. B., ist der Arbeitsgegenstand von Natur vorhan-\n den. Die nothwendigen Arbeitsmittel selbst also gegeben, — und die ex-\n traktive Industrie liefert grossentheils selbst wieder das Rohmaterial dieser\n Arbeitsinstrumente, Metalle, Holz u. s. w., und die Hilfsmittel, wie Kohle,\n — ist das Produkt keineswegs durch den Umfang dieser\n Arbeitsmittel beschränkt. Sie werden durch die grössere Ver-\n ausgabung der Arbeitskraft nur rascher vernutzt, also nur ihre Reproduk-\n tionsperiode abgekürzt. Die Produktenmasse selbst, wie Kohle,\n Eisen, wächst dagegen, unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen, im Ver-\n hältniss zu der auf den Naturgegenstand verausgabten Arbeit. Wie am\n ersten Tag der Produktion gehn hier die ursprünglichen Produktbildner,\n daher auch die Bildner der stofflichen Elemente des Kapitals, Mensch und\n Natur, zusammen. In der eigentlichen Agrikultur spielen zwar Samen\n und Dünger dieselbe Rolle, wie das Rohmaterial in der Manufaktur, und\n man kann nicht mehr Land besäen, ohne vorher mehr Samen zu haben.\n Aber diess Rohmaterial und die Arbeitsinstrumente gegeben, ist bekannt,\n welche wunderthätige Wirkung selbst die rein mechanische Bearbeitung\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"590\", \"facs\": \"#f0609\"} --> des Bodens, deren Intensivität von der Spannung der Arbeitskraft abhängt,\n auf die Massenhaftigkeit des Produkts ausübt. Es ist wieder direkte Wir-\n kung des Menschen auf den Naturgegenstand, welche zur unmittelbaren Quelle\n des Reichthums wird. Extraktive Industrie und Agrikultur liefern andrer-\n seits der Manufaktur das Rohmaterial und die Hilfsstoffe, also die stofflichen\n Elemente, welche hier jeder grösseren Arbeitsausgabe vorausgesetzt sind,\n während die eigentlichen Arbeitsmittel auch in dieser Sphäre durch exten-\n sivere oder intensivere Spannung der Arbeitskraft nur ihre Reproduktions-\n periode verkürzen. Indem das Kapital sich also die beiden Urbildner des\n Reichthums, Arbeitskraft und Erde, einverleibt, erwirbt es in ihnen von\n seinem eignen stofflichen Umfang unabhängige und\n dehnbare Faktoren der Reproduktion auf erweiterter Stufenleiter\n und daher der Accumulation.\n\n\n\n\nAbgesehn vom Exploitationsgrad der Arbeit wird die Pro-\n duktion des Mehrwerths, also die Accumulation des Kapitals,\n deren Bildungselement der Mehrwerth, wesentlich bestimmt durch die\nProduktivkraft der Arbeit.\n\n\n\n\nMit der Produktivkraft der Arbeit wächst die Produktenmasse,\n worin sich ein bestimmter Werth, also auch Mehrwerth von gegebner\n Grösse darstellt. Bei gleichbleibender und selbst bei fallender Rate des\n Mehrwerths, sofern sie nur langsamer fällt als die Produktivkraft der Arbeit\n steigt, wächst die Masse des Mehrprodukts. Bei gleichbleibender Theilung\n desselben in Revenue und Surpluskapital kann daher die Konsumtion des\n Kapitalisten wachsen ohne Abnahme des Accumulationsfonds. Die propor-\n tionelle Grösse des Accumulationsfonds kann selbst auf Kosten des Kon-\n sumtionsfonds wachsen, während die Verwohlfeilerung der Waaren dem\n Kapitalisten eben so viele oder mehr Genussmittel als vorher zur Verfügung\n stellt. Aber mit der wachsenden Produktivität der Arbeit geht, wie man\n gesehn, die Verwohlfeilerung des Arbeiters, also wachsende Rate des Mehr-\n werths, Hand in Hand, selbst wenn der reelle Arbeitslohn steigt. Er\n steigt nie verhältnissmässig mit der Produktivität der Arbeit. Derselbe\nvariable Kapitalwerth setzt also mehr Arbeitskraft und daher mehr\n Arbeit in Bewegung. Derselbe constante Kapitalwerth stellt sich\n in mehr Produktionsmitteln, d. h. mehr Arbeitsmitteln, Arbeitsmaterial und\n Hilfsstoffen dar, liefert also sowohl mehr Produktbildner als Werthbildner,\n oder Arbeitseinsauger. Bei gleichbleibendem und selbst abnehmendem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"591\", \"facs\": \"#f0610\"} -->Werth des Surpluskapitals findet daher beschleunigte Accumulation statt.\n Nicht nur erweitert sich die Stufenleiter der Reproduktion stofflich, son-\n dern die Produktion des Mehrwerths wächst schneller als der Werth des\n Surpluskapitals.\n\n\n\n\nDie Entwicklung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit reagirt auch auf\n das Originalkapital oder das bereits im Produktionspro-\n zess befindliche Kapital. Ein Theil des funktionirenden constan-\n ten Kapitals besteht aus Arbeitsmitteln, wie Maschinerie u. s. w., die nur\n in längeren Perioden konsumirt und daher reproducirt oder durch neue\n Exemplare derselben Art ersetzt werden. Aber ein Theil dieser Arbeits-\n mittel stirbt jedes Jahr ab, oder erreicht das Endziel seiner produktiven Funk-\n tion. Er befindet sich daher jedes Jahr im Stadium seiner periodischen Repro-\n duktion oder seines Ersatzes durch neue Exemplare derselben Art. Hat die\n Produktivkraft der Arbeit sich in der Geburtsstätte dieser Arbeitsmittel\n erweitert, und sie entwickelt sich fortwährend mit dem ununterbrochenen\n Fluss der Wissenschaft und der Technologie, so tritt wirkungsvollere und,\n ihren Leistungsumfang betrachtet, wohlfeilere Maschine, Werkzeug, Ap-\n parat u. s. w. an die Stelle der alten. Das alte Kapital wird in\n einer produktiveren Form reproducirt, abgesehn von der fort-\n währenden Detailveränderung an den vorhandnen Arbeitsmitteln. Der\n andere Theil des constanten Kapitals, Rohmaterial und Hilfsstoffe, wird\n fortwährend innerhalb des Jahrs, der der Agrikultur entstammende meist\n jährlich reproducirt. Jede Einführung bessrer Methoden u. s. w. wirkt\n hier also fast gleichzeitig auf Zuschusskapital und bereits in Funktion\n begriffenes Kapital. Jeder Fortschritt der Chemie vermannigfacht nicht nur\n die Nutzanwendungen desselben Materials und dehnt daher mit dem\n Wachsthum des Kapitals seine Anlagesphären aus. Er lehrt zugleich die\nExcremente des Produktions- und Konsumtionsprozesses in den Kreislauf\n des Reproduktionsprozesses zurückschleudern, schafft also ohne vorherige\n Kapitalauslage neuen Kapitalstoff. Gleich vermehrter Ausbeutung des\nNaturreichthums durch bloss höhere Spannung der Arbeitskraft, bildet\ndie Wissenschaft eine von der gegebnen Grösse des funktionirenden\n Kapitals unabhängige Potenz seiner Expansion. Sie reagirt zugleich\n auf den in sein Erneurungsstadium eingetretenen Theil des Originalkapitals.\n In seine neue Form einverleibt es gratis den hinter dem Rücken seiner\n alten Form vollzogenen gesellschaftlichen Fortschritt. Allerdings ist diese\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"592\", \"facs\": \"#f0611\"} --> Entwicklung der Produktivkraft zugleich begleitet von theilweiser Depre-\n ciation funktionirender Kapitale. Soweit diese Depreciation sich durch\n die Konkurrenz akut fühlbar macht, fällt die Hauptwucht auf den Arbeiter,\n in dessen gesteigerter Exploitation der Kapitalist Schadenersatz sucht.\n\n\n\n\nEs zeigte sich bei Analyse des relativen Mehrwerths, wie die Ent-\n wicklung der gesellschaftlichen Produktivkraft der Arbeit eine stets wach-\n sende Masse des von derselben Arbeitskraft in Bewegung gesetzten con-\n stanten Kapitals bedingt. Mit dem Reichthum oder der Fülle und Wirk-\n samkeit der in Maschinerie u. s. w. vergegenständlichten Arbeit, wovon der\n Arbeiter als bereits producirter Bedingung des Produktionsprozesses aus-\n geht, wächst die Masse des alten Kapitalwerths, der durch\n blossen Zusatz neuer Arbeit, also neue Werthproduktion, erhalten und\n in diesem Sinn reproducirt wird. Man vergleiche z. B. einen eng-\n lischen Spinner mit einem indischen. Man unterstelle der Vereinfachung\n halber gleiche Extension und Intensivität des englischen und indischen Ar-\n beitstags. Der englische Spinner verwandelt in einem Tag viel hundert-\n mal grössere Massen von Baumwolle, Spinninstrumenten u. s. w. in Garn.\n Er erhält also auch vielhundertmal grösseren Kapitalwerth in seinem Pro-\n dukt. Wäre selbst das Werthprodukt seiner Tagesarbeit, d. h. der\n durch dieselbe den Produktionsmitteln neu zugesetzte Werth, nur gleich\n dem des Indiers, dennoch resultirt seine Tagesarbeit nicht nur in grösserem\nProduktenquantum, sondern in unendlich grösserem Produkten-\n werth, altem Werth, den er auf das neue Produkt überträgt, und der\n von neuem als Kapital funktioniren kann. „1782“, belehrt uns F. Engels,\n „lag die ganze Wollerndte der vorhergehenden drei Jahre (in England) aus\n Mangel an Arbeitern noch unverarbeitet da, und hätte liegen bleiben müssen,\n wenn nicht die neu erfundne Maschinerie zu Hilfe gekommen wäre und sie ver-\n sponnen hätte“ [Note: F. Engels: „Lage der arbeitenden Klasse in England“,\n p. 20.] . Die in der Form von Maschinerie vergegenständlichte\n Arbeit stampfte natürlich unmittelbar keinen Menschen aus dem Boden, aber\n sie erlaubte einer geringen Arbeiteranzahl durch Zusatz von relativ wenig\n lebendiger Arbeit nicht nur die Wolle produktiv zu konsumiren, und ihr\n Neuwerth zuzusetzen, sondern in der Form von Garn u. s. w. ihren\nalten Werth zu erhalten. Sie lieferte damit zugleich Mittel und Sporn\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"593\", \"facs\": \"#f0612\"} --> zur erweiterten Reproduktion von Wolle. Es ist die Naturgabe der leben-\n digen Arbeit alten Werth zu erhalten, während sie Neuwerth schafft.\n Mit dem Wachsthum von Wirksamkeit, Umfang und Werth ihrer Produk-\n tionsmittel, also mit der die Entwicklung ihrer Produktivkraft begleitenden\nAccumulation erhält und verewigt die Arbeit daher in stets neuer\n Form einen stets schwellenden Kapitalwerth [Note: Die klassische Oekonomie hat wegen mangelhafter Analyse des Arbeits-\n und Verwerthungsprozesses diess wichtige Moment der Reproduktion nie ordentlich\n begriffen, wie man z. B. bei Ricardo sehn kann. Er sagt z. B.: Welches immer der\n Wechsel der Produktivkraft, „Eine Million Menschen producirt in den Fabri-\n ken stets denselben Werth.“ Diess richtig, wenn Extension und Intensivgrad ihrer\n Arbeit gegeben. Es verhindert aber nicht, und Ricardo übersieht diess in gewissen\n Schlussfolgerungen, dass eine Million Menschen sehr verschiedne Massen von Pro-\n duktionsmitteln, bei verschiedner Produktivkraft ihrer Arbeit, in Produkt verwan-\n delt, daher sehr verschiedne Werthmassen in ihrem Produkt erhält, die von ihr\n gelieferten Produktenwerthe also sehr verschieden sind. Ricardo hat, neben-\n bei bemerkt, an jenem Beispiel umsonst versucht, dem J. B. Say den Unterschied\n zwischen Gebrauchswerth (den er hier wealth nennt, stofflichen Reichthum) und\n Tauschwerth klar zu machen. Say antwortet: „Quant à la difficulté qu’élève\n Mr. Ricardo en disant que, par des procédés mieux extendus, un million de per-\n sonnes peuvent produire deux fois, trois fois autant de richesses, sans produire\n plus de valeurs, cette difficulté n’est pas une lorsque l’on considère, ainsi\n qu’on le doit, la production comme un échange dans lesquels on donne\n les services productifs de son travail, de sa terre, et de ses capitaux, pour obtenir\n des produits. C’est par le moyen de ses services productifs que nous acquérons\n tous les produits qui sont au monde. Or … nous sommes d’autant plus riches,\n nos services productifs ont d’autant plus de valeur, qu’ils obtiennent dans\nl’échange appelé production, une plus grande quantité de choses\n utiles.“ (J. B. Say: „Lettresà M. Malthus. Paris 1820“, p. 168, 169.)\n Die „difficulté“ — sie existirt für ihn, nicht für Ricardo — die Say erklären soll,\n ist die: Warum vermehrt sich nicht der Werth der Gebrauchswerthe, wenn ihre\n Quantität in Folge gesteigerter Produktivkraft der Arbeit wächst? Antwort:\n Die Schwierigkeit wird dadurch gelöst, dass man den Gebrauchswerth ge-\n fälligst Tauschwerth nennt. Tauschwerth ist ein Ding, das one way or\n another mit Austausch zusammenhängt. Man nenne also die Produktion\n einen „Austausch“ von Arbeit und Produktionsmitteln gegen das Produkt,\n und es ist klar wie Wasser, dass man um so mehr Tauschwerth erhält, je mehr\n Gebrauchswerth einem die Produktion liefert. In andern Worten: Je mehr Ge-\n brauchswerthe, z. B. Strümpfe, ein Arbeitstag dem Strumpffabrikanten liefert, desto\nreicher ist er an Strümpfen. Plötzlich fällt Say jedoch ein, dass „mit der] . Diese Naturkraft der\n [Running matter: I. 38] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"594\", \"facs\": \"#f0613\"} --> Arbeit erscheint als Selbsterhaltungskraft des Kapitals, dem sie\n einverleibt ist, ganz wie ihre gesellschaftlichen Produktivkräfte als\nseine Eigenschaften und wie die beständige Aneignung der Mehrarbeit\n durch den Kapitalisten als beständige Selbstverwerthung des Ka-\n pitals. Alle Kräfte der Arbeit projektiren sich als Kräfte des Kapitals,\n wie alle Werthformen der Waare als Formen des Geldes.\n\n\n\n\nUnter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen ist die Grösse des produ-\n cirten Mehrwerths und daher die Accumulation endlich bestimmt durch die\n Grösse des vorgeschossenen Kapitals. Mit dem Gesammtka-\n pital wächst auch sein variabler Bestandtheil, wenn auch nicht in demsel-\n ben Verhältniss. Auf je grösserer Stufenleiter der individuelle Kapitalist\n producirt, desto grösser die Arbeiteranzahl, die er gleichzeitig exploitirt,\n oder die Masse der unbezahlten Arbeit, die er aneignet [Note: Man wird im Dritten Buch sehn, dass die durchschnittliche Pro-\n fitrate verschiedner Produktionssphären nicht afficirt wird von der jeder derselben] . Je mehr\n [Note: grössern Quantität“ der Strümpfe ihr „Preis“ (der natürlich nichts mit dem Tausch-\n werth zu thun hat) fällt, „parce que la concurrence les (les producteurs)\noblige à donner les produits pour ce qu’ils leur coûtent.“ Aber wo\n denn kommt der Profit her, wenn der Kapitalist die Waaren zu dem Preis ver-\n kauft, den sie ihm kosten? Doch never mind. Say erklärt, dass in Folge der gesteiger-\n ten Produktivität jeder im Ersatz für dasselbe Aequivalent jetzt zwei statt früher ein\n paar Strümpfe u. s. w. erhält. Das Resultat, wobei er anlangt, ist grade der Satz Ricar-\n do’s, den er widerlegen wollte. Nach dieser gewaltigen Denkanstrengung apostrophirt\n er Malthus triumphirend mit den Worten: „Telle est, monsieur, la doctrine\n bien liée sans laquelle il est impossible, je le déclare, d’expliquer les plus\n grandes difficultés de l’économie politique et notamment, comment il se peut\n qu’une nation soit plus riche lorsque ses produits diminuent de valeur, quoique\n la richesse soit de la valeur.“ (l. c. p. 170.) Ein englischer Oekonom bemerkt\n über ähnliche Kunststücke in Say’s „Lettres“: „Diese affektirten Manieren zu\n schwatzen („those affected ways of talking“) bilden im Ganzen das, was Herr Say\nseine Doktrin zu nennen beliebt und die er dem Malthus ans Herz legt zu\n Hertford zu lehren, wie das schon ‚dans plusieurs parties de l’Europe‘ geschehe.\n Er sagt: „Si vous trouvez une physionomie de paradoxe à toutes ces propo-\n sitions, voyez les choses qu’elles expriment, et j’ose croire qu’elles vous paraî-\n tront fort simples et fort raisonnables.“ Zweifelsohne, und zugleich werden sie in\n Folge desselben Prozesses alles andre, nur nicht original oder wichtig erscheinen.“\n („An Inquiry into those Principles respecting the Nature of\n Demand etc.“, p. 116, 110.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"595\", \"facs\": \"#f0614\"} --> also das individuelle Kapital wächst, desto grösser der Fonds, der\n sich in Konsumtionsfonds und Accumulationsfonds spaltet. Der Kapita-\n list kann daher flotter leben und zugleich mehr „entsagen“.\n\n\n\n\nMit dem Wachsthum des Kapitals wächst die Differenz zwi-\n schen angewandtem und konsumirtem Kapital. In andern\n Worten: Es wächst die Werth- und Stoffmasse der Arbeitsmittel, wie\n Baulichkeiten, Maschinerie, Drainirungsröhren, Arbeitsvieh, Apparate\n jeder Art, die stets ihrem ganzen Umfang nach, während längerer oder\n kürzerer Periode, in beständig wiederholten Produktionsprozessen, funktio-\n niren, oder zur Erzielung bestimmter Nutzeffekte dienen, während sie nur\n allmälig verschleissen, daher ihren Werth nur stückweis verlieren,\n also auch nur stückweis auf das Produkt übertragen. Im Verhältniss,\n worin diese Arbeitsmittel als Produktbildner dienen, ohne dem Produkt\n Werth zuzusetzen, also ganz angewandt, aber nur theilweis konsumirt wer-\n den, leisten sie, wie früher erwähnt, denselben Gratisdienst wie Natur-\n kräfte, Wasser, Dampf, Luft, Elektricität u. s. w. Dieser Gratisdienst\n der vergangnen Arbeit, wenn ergriffen und beseelt von der lebendigen Ar-\n beit, accumulirt mit der wachsenden Stufenleiter der Accumulation.\n\n\n\n\nDa die vergangne Arbeit sich stets in Kapital verkleidet, d. h. das\n Passivum der Arbeit von A, B, C u. s. w. im Aktivum des Nichtarbeiters X\n figurirt, sind Bürger und politische Oekonomen voll des Lobes für die Ver-\n dienste der vergangnen Arbeit, welche nach dem schottischen Genie\n Mac Culloch sogar einen eignen Sold beziehn muss [Note: Mac Culloch löste das Patent auf „wages of past labour“, lange\n bevor Senior das Patent auf die „wages of abstinence“.] . Das stets wach-\n sende Gewicht der unter der Form von Produktionsmitteln im lebendigen\n Arbeitsprozess mitwirkenden vergangnen Arbeit wird also ihrer dem Ar-\n beiter selbst, dessen vergangne und unbezahlte Arbeit sie ist, entfrem-\n deten Gestalt, ihrer Kapitalgestalt vindicirt. Die praktischen\n Agenten der kapitalistischen Produktion und ihre ideologischen Zungen-\n drescher sind ebenso unfähig das Produktionsmittel von seiner an-\n tagonistischen gesellschaftlichen Charaktermaske, die ihm heutzutag an-\n [Note: eigenthümlichen Theilung des Kapitals in constantes und variables Element, und\n zugleich dass diess Phänomen nur scheinbar den über die Natur und Produktion\n des Mehrwerths entwickelten Gesetzen widerspricht.] \n [Running matter: 38*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"596\", \"facs\": \"#f0615\"} --> klebt, getrennt zu denken, als ein Sklavenhalter den Arbeiter selbst\n von seinem Charakter als Sklave.\n\n\n\n\nMan hat im Verlauf dieses Abschnitts gesehn, dass das Kapital keine\n fixe Grösse ist, sondern ein elastischer und mit der Theilung des Mehr-\n werths in Revenue und Surpluskapital beständig fluktuirender Theil des\n gesellschaftlichen Reichthums. Man hat ferner gesehn, dass selbst die\n Grösse des funktionirenden Kapitals gegeben, die ihm einverleibte Arbeits-\n kraft, Wissenschaft und Erde (worunter ökonomisch alle ohne Zuthat\n des Menschen von Natur vorhandnen Arbeitsgegenstände zu verstehn\n sind) elastische Potenzen desselben bilden, die ihm innerhalb gewisser Gren-\n zen einen von seiner eignen Grösse unabhängigen Spielraum\n gestatten. Es wurde dabei von allen Verhältnissen des Cirkulationspro-\n zesses abgesehn, die sehr verschiedne Wirkungsgrade derselben Kapital-\n masse verursachen. Es wurde, da wir die Schranken der kapitalistischen Pro-\n duktion voraussetzen, also eine rein naturwüchsige Gestalt des gesellschaft-\n lichen Produktionsprozesses, abgesehn von jeder mit den vorhandnen Pro-\n duktionsmitteln und Arbeitskräften unmittelbar und planmässig bewirk-\n baren rationelleren Kombination. Die klassische Oekonomie liebte es von\n jeher das gesellschaftliche Kapital als eine fixe Grösse von fixem Wir-\n kungsgrad aufzufassen. Aber das Vorurtheil ward erst zum Dogma be-\n festigt durch den Urphilister Jeremias Bentham, diess nüchtern pe-\n dantische, schwatzlederne Orakel des gemeinen Bürgerverstandes des\n 19. Jahrhunderts [Note: Vgl. u. a.: J. Bentham: „Theorie des Peines et des Ré-\n compenses, trad. Et. Dumont. 3ème éd.“ Paris 1826, t. II, l. IV,\n ch. II.] . Bentham ist unter den Philosophen, was Martin\n Tupper unter den Dichtern. Beide waren nur in England fabricirbar [Note: Jeremias Bentham ist ein rein englisches Phänomen. Selbst unsern\n Philosophen Christian Wolf nicht ausgenommen, hat zu keiner Zeit und in\n keinem Land der hausbackenste Gemeinplatz sich jemals so selbstgefällig breit\n gemacht. Das Nützlichkeitsprincip war keine Erfindung Bentham’s. Er\n reproducirte nur geistlos, was Helvetius und andre Franzosen des 18. Jahrhunderts\n geistreich gesagt hatten. Wenn man z. B. wissen will, was ist einem Hunde nütz-\n lich?, so muss man die Hundenatur ergründen. Diese Natur selbst ist nicht aus\n dem „Nützlichkeitsprincip“ zu konstruiren. Auf den Menschen angewandt, wenn\n man alle menschliche That, Bewegung, Verhältnisse u. s. w. nach dem Nützlich-] .\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"597\", \"facs\": \"#f0616\"} --> Mit seinem Dogma werden die gewöhnlichsten Erscheinungen des Produk-\n tionsprozesses, wie z. B. dessen plötzliche Expansionen und Kontrak-\n tionen, ja die Accumulation, völlig unbegreifbar [Note: „Politische Oekonomen sind zu geneigt, eine bestimmte Quantität von\n Kapital und eine bestimmte Anzahl Arbeiter als Produktionsinstrumente von gleich-\n förmiger Kraft und als mit einer gewissen gleichförmigen Intensivität wirkend zu\n behandeln . . . . Diejenigen, die behaupten, dass Waaren die einzigen Agenten\n der Produktion sind, beweisen, dass die Produktion überhaupt nicht erweitert\n werden kann, denn zu einer solchen Erweiterung müssten Lebensmittel, Rohmate-\n rialien und Werkzeuge vorher vermehrt werden, was in der That darauf hinaus\n kömmt, dass kein Wachsthum der Produktion ohne ihr vorheriges Wachsthum\n stattfinden kann, oder, in andern Worten, dass jedes Wachsthum unmöglich ist.“\n (S. Bailey: „Money and its Vicissitudes“, p. 26 u. 70.) Bailey\n kritisirt das Dogma hauptsächlich vom Standpunkt des Cirkulationspro-\n zesses.] . Das Dogma wurde\n sowohl von Bentham selbst als von Malthus, James Mill, MacCulloch u. s. w.\n zu apologetischen Zwecken vernutzt, namentlich um einen Theil des\n Kapitals, das variable oder in Arbeitskraft umsetzbare\n Kapital als eine fixe Grösse darzustellen. Die stoffliche Existenz\n des variablen Kapitals, d. h. die Masse der Lebensmittel, die es für den\n Arbeiter repräsentirt, oder der s. g. Arbeitsfonds, wurde in einen\n durch Naturketten abgeringten und unüberschreitbaren Sondertheil des\n gesellschaftlichen Reichthums verfabelt. Um den Theil des gesellschaft-\n lichen Reichthums, der als constantes Kapital oder, stofflich ausge-\n drückt, als Produktionsmittel funktioniren soll, in Bewegung zu setzen, ist\n eine bestimmte Masse lebendiger Arbeit erheischt. Diese ist\n [Note: keitsprincip beurtheilen will, handelt es sich erst um die menschliche Natur im\n Allgemeinen und dann um die in jeder Epoche historisch modificirte Menschen-\n natur. Bentham macht kein Federlesens. Mit der naivsten Trockenheit unter-\n stellt er den modernen Spiessbürger, speciell den englischen Spiessbürger,\n als den Normalmenschen. Was diesem Kauz von Normalmensch und seiner\n Welt nützlich, ist an und für sich nützlich. An diesem Massstab beurtheilt er dann\n Vergangenheit, Gegenwart und Zukunft. Z. B. die christliche Religion ist „nütz-\n lich“, weil sie dieselben Missethaten religiös verpönt, die der Strafcodex juristisch\n verdammt. Kunstkritik ist „schädlich“, weil sie ehrbare Leute in ihrem Genuss\n an Martin Tupper stört u. s. w. Mit solchem Schund hat der brave Mann, dessen\n Devise: „nulla dies sine linea“, Berge von Büchern gefüllt. Wenn ich die\n Courage meines Freundes H. Heine hätte, würde ich Herrn Jeremias ein Genie in\n der bürgerlichen Dummheit nennen.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"598\", \"facs\": \"#f0617\"} --> technologisch gegeben. Aber weder ist die Anzahl der Arbeiter gegeben,\n erheischt um diese Arbeitsmasse flüssig zu machen, denn das wechselt mit\n dem Exploitationsgrad der individuellen Arbeitskraft, noch der Preis dieser\n Arbeitskraft, sondern nur seine zudem sehr elastische Minimalschranke.\n Die Thatsachen, die dem Dogma zu Grund liegen, sind die. Einerseits\n hat der Arbeiter nicht mitzusprechen bei der Theilung des gesell-\n schaftlichen Reichthums in Genussmittel der Nichtarbeiter und in Produk-\n tionsmittel. Andrerseits kann er nur in günstigen Ausnahmsfällen den s. g.\n „Arbeitsfonds“ auf Kosten der „Revenue“ des Reichen erwei-\n tern [Note: J. St. Mill sagt in seinen „Principles of Polit. Economy“:\n „Das Produkt der Arbeit wird heutzutag vertheilt im umgekehrten Ver-\n hältniss zur Arbeit — der grösste Theil an die, die niemals arbeiten, der\n nächst grösste an die, deren Arbeit fast nur nominell ist, und so, auf absteigender\n Skala, schrumpft die Belohnung zusammen, im Masse wie die Arbeit härter und\n unangenehmer wird, bis die ermüdendste und erschöpfendste körperliche Arbeit\n nicht mit Sicherheit auch nur auf Gewinnung der Lebensnothwendigkeiten rechnen\n kann.“ Zur Vermeidung von Missverständniss bemerke ich, dass, wenn Männer\n wie J. St. Mill, Fawcett u. s. w. wegen des Widerspruchs ihrer altökonomischen\n Dogmen und ihrer modernen Tendenzen zu rügen sind, es durchaus unrecht wäre,\n sie mit dem Tross der vulgärökonomischen Apologeten zusammenzuwerfen.] . Zu welch abgeschmackter Tautologie es führt, die kapita-\n listische Schranke des Arbeitsfonds in seine gesell-\n schaftliche Naturschranke umzudichten, zeige u. a. Professor\nFawcett: „Das cirkulirende Kapital [Note: H. Fawcett, Prof. of Polit. Econ. at Cambridge: „The\n Economic Position of the British Labourer. Lond. 1865“, p. 120.]  eines Landes,“ sagt er,\n „ist sein Arbeitsfonds. Um daher den durchschnittlichen Geldlohn, den\n jeder Arbeiter erhält, zu berechnen, haben wir nur einfach diess Kapital\n durch die Anzahl der Arbeiterbevölkerung zu dividiren“ [Note: Ich erinnere hier den Leser, dass die Kategorieen: variables und\nconstantes Kapital von mir zuerst gebraucht werden. Die politische Oeko-\n nomie seit A. Smith wirft die darin enthaltenen Bestimmungen mit den aus dem\nCirkulationsprozess entspringenden Formunterschieden von fixem und\ncirkulirendem Kapital kunterbunt zusammen. Das Nähere darüber im\n Zweiten Buch, zweites Kapitel.] . D. h. also,\n erst rechnen wir die wirklich gezahlten individuellen Arbeitslöhne in eine\n Summe zusammen, dann behaupten wir, dass diese Addition die Werth-\n summe des von Gott und Natur oktroyirten „Arbeitsfonds“ bildet. End-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"599\", \"facs\": \"#f0618\"} --> lich dividiren wir die so erhaltne Summe durch die Kopfzahl der Arbeiter,\n um hinwiederum zu entdecken, wie viel jedem Arbeiter individuell im\n Durchschnitt zufallen kann. Eine ungemein pfiffige Procedur diess. Sie\n verhindert Herrn Fawcett nicht im selben Athemzug zu sagen: „Der in\n England jährlich accumulirte Gesammtreichthum wird in zwei Theile ge-\n theilt. Ein Theil wird in England zur Erhaltung unsrer eignen Industrie\n verwandt. Ein andrer Theil wird in andere Länder exportirt … Der\n in unsrer Industrie angewandte Theil bildet keine bedeutende\n Portion des jährlich in diesem Land accumulirten Reich-\n thums“ [Note: Fawcett l. c. p. 123, 122.] . Der grössere Theil des jährlich zuwachsenden Mehrpro-\n dukts, dem englischen Arbeiter ohne Aequivalent entwandt, wird also\n nicht in England, sondern in fremden Ländern verkapitalisirt. Aber mit\n dem so exportirten Surpluskapital wird ja auch ein Theil des von Gott und\n Bentham erfundnen „Arbeitsfonds“ exportirt [Note: Man könnte sagen, dass nicht nur Kapital, sondern auch Arbeiter, in\n Form der Emigration, jährlich aus England exportirt werden. Im Text ist jedoch\n gar nicht die Rede vom Peculium der Auswanderer, die zum grossen Theil keine\n Arbeiter sind. Die Pächterssöhne liefern grosse Portion. Das jährlich zur Ver-\n zinsung ins Ausland versandte englische Surpluskapital steht in ungleich grösserem\n Verhältniss zur jährlichen Accumulation als die jährliche Auswanderung zum\n jährlichen Zuwachs der Bevölkerung.] .","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0001","text":"Früher hatten wir zu betrachten, wie der Mehrwerth aus dem Kapital,\n jetzt wie das Kapital aus dem Mehrwerth entspringt. Anwendung\n von Mehrwerth als Kapital oder Rückverwandlung von\n Mehrwerth in Kapital heisst Accumulation des Kapitals [Note: „Accumulation of Capital: the employment of a portion of reve-\n nue as capital.“ (Malthus: „Definitions etc.“ ed. Cazenove p. 11.)\n „Conversion of revenue into Capital.“ (Malthus: „Princ. of Pol.\n Econ. 2nd ed. Lond. 1836“, p. 319.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"19.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0002","text":"Ein Kapital betrage 10,000 Pfd. St., sein variabler Bestandtheil\n 2000 Pfd. St. Bei einer Rate des Mehrwerths von 100 % producirt es\n in einer gewissen Periode, jährlich z. B., einen Mehrwerth von 2000\n Pfd. St. Werden diese 2000 Pfd. St. wieder als Kapital vorgeschossen,\n so wächst das ursprüngliche Kapital von 10,000 auf 12,000 Pfd. St. oder\n es hat accumulirt. Es ist zunächst gleichgiltig, ob das Zuschusskapital\n zum alten geschlagen oder selbstständig verwerthet wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"19.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0003","text":"Eine Werthsumme von 2000 Pfd. St. ist eine Werthsumme von\n 2000 Pfd. St. Man riecht und sieht diesem Geld nicht an, dass es\nMehrwerth ist. Der Charakter eines Werths als Mehrwerth zeigt,\nwie er zu seinem Eigner kam, ändert aber nichts an der Natur des Werths\n oder des Geldes. Die Verwandlung der zuschüssigen 2000 Pfd. St. in\n Kapital geht also in derselben Weise vor, wie die Verwandlung der ur-\n sprünglichen 10,000 Pfd. St. Die Bedingungen der Metamorphose blei-\n ben dieselben. Ein Theil der 2000 Pfd. St. muss in constantes, der\n andre in variables Kapital verwandelt werden, der eine in die objektiven\n Faktoren des Arbeitsprozesses, Arbeitsmaterial und Arbeitsmittel, der\n andre in seinen subjektiven Faktor, Arbeitskraft. Der Kapitalist muss\n also diese Elemente auf dem Waarenmarkt vorfinden.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"19.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0004","text":"So stellt sich der Vor-\n gang dar vom Standpunkt des individuellen Kapitalisten, der die Geld-\n summe von 10,000 Pfd. St. in einen Waarenwerth von 12,000 Pfd. St. ver-\n wandelt, diesen Waarenwerth in Geld zum Belauf von 12,000 Pfd. St. rück-\n verwandelt, und nun neben dem ursprünglichen Werth von 10,000 Pfd. St.\n [Note: 7. April 1849.) Die unter diesem Titel in der N. Rh. Z. veröffentlichten\n Artikel sind Bruchstücke der Vorlesungen, die ich über jenes Thema 1847 im\n deutschen Arbeiterverein zu Brüssel hielt und deren Druck durch die Februar-\n revolution unterbrochen wurde.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"568\", \"facs\": \"#f0587\"} --> auch den zuschüssigen Werth von 2000 Pfd. St. als sein Kapital funktio-\n niren lässt. Betrachten wir aber die 10,000 Pfd. St. als das gesell-\n schaftliche Kapital oder als das Gesammtkapital der Ka-\n pitalistenklasse, und die 2000 Pfd. St.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"19.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0005","text":"als ihren während des Jahrs\n z. B. producirten Mehrwerth! Der Mehrwerth ist verkörpert in einem\n zuschüssigen Produkt oder Mehrprodukt. Ein Theil dieses Mehrpro-\n dukts geht in den Konsumtionsfonds der Kapitalisten ein oder wird\n von ihnen als Revenue verzehrt. Abgesehn von diesem Theil, ebenso vom\n internationalen Handel, der inländische durch ausländische Waarensorten\n ersetzt, besteht das Mehrprodukt, in seiner Naturalform, nur aus\nProduktionsmitteln, Rohstoffen, Hilfsstoffen, Arbeitsmitteln, und\n aus nothwendigen Lebensmitteln, also aus den stofflichen\n Elementen des constanten und variablen Kapitals. Diese finden sich\n also nicht zufällig auf dem Markt vor, sondern sind bereits vorhandne\n Existenzweisen des producirten Mehrwerths selbst.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"19.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0006","text":"Was aber die er-\n heischte zuschüssige Arbeit angeht, so können bis zu einem gewissen Grad\n die bereits funktionirenden Arbeitskräfte voller beschäftigt, extensiv oder\n intensiv höher angespannt werden. Andrerseits hat der kapitalistische\n Produktionsprozess mit den sachlichen Elementen des zuschüssigen Kapi-\n tals auch bereits zuschüssige Arbeitskräfte geliefert. Da nämlich die Ar-\n beiterklasse aus dem Prozess herauskömmt, wie sie in ihn eintrat, müssen\n verschiedne Altersklassen ihrer Kinder, deren Existenz der Durchschnitts-\n lohn sichert, beständig neben sie auf den Arbeitsmarkt treten. Konkret\n betrachtet ist die Accumulation also kapitalistischer Reproduk-\n tionsprozess auf erweiterter Stufenleiter.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"19.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0007","text":"Den in Zuschusskapital verwandelten Mehrwerth von 2000 Pfd. St.\n wollen wir Surpluskapital Nr. I nennen. Der Vereinfachung wegen\n vorausgesetzt, seine Theilung in constanten und variablen Bestandtheil\n bleibe dieselbe wie beim ursprünglichen Kapital, ebenso die Rate des Mehr-\n werths von 100 %, und wir kennen die Methode, worin diess Kapital von\n 2000 Pfd. St. einen Mehrwerth von 400 Pfd. St. producirt. Dieser Mehr-\n werth werde wieder in Kapital verwandelt. So erhalten wir Surplus-\n kapital Nr. II von 400 Pfd. St. u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"19.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0008","text":"Was hat sich nun geändert? Die 10,000 Pfd. St., die sich ur-\n sprünglich in Kapital verwandelten, waren das Eigenthum ihres Besitzers.\n Er warf sie auf den Waaren- und Arbeitsmarkt. Wo hat er sie her? Wir\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"569\", \"facs\": \"#f0588\"} --> wissen es nicht. Das Gesetz des Waarenaustausches, wonach sich im\n Durchschnitt Aequivalente austauschen und jeder nur mit Waare Waare\n kauft, begünstigt die Annahme, dass die 10,000 Pfd. St. nur die Geld-\n form seiner eignen Produkte und daher seiner eignen Arbeit sind, oder\n der Arbeit von Personen, als deren rechtmässiger Stellvertreter er funk-\n tionirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"19.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0009","text":"Den Entstehungsprozess des Surpluskapitals Nr. I kennen wir dagegen\n ganz genau. Es ist nur die verwandelte Form von Mehrwerth,\n also von Mehrarbeit, unbezahlter fremder Arbeit. Es ist kein Werth-\n atomdarin, wofür sein Besitzer ein Aequivalent gezahlt hätte. Allerdings\n kauft der Kapitalist, wie vorher mit einem Theil des ursprünglichen, so jetzt\n mit einem Theil des Surplus-Kapitals von neuem Arbeitskraft, woraus er von\n neuem Mehrarbeit pumpt und daher von neuem Mehrwerth producirt. Aber\n er kauft den Arbeiter jetzt mit dessen eignem, ihm vorher ohne\n Aequivalent weggenommenen Produkt oder Produktenwerth,\n ganz wie er ihn mit Produktionsmitteln beschäftigt, die in natura\n oder deren Werth ohne Aequivalent weggenommenes Produkt\n des Arbeiters sind.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"19.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0010","text":"Es ändert durchaus nichts an der Sache, ob die-\n selben individuellen Arbeiter, die das Surpluskapital producirt haben, auch\n damit beschäftigt werden, oder ob mit der in Geld verwandelten unbezahl-\n ten Arbeit des Arbeiters A der Arbeiter B geworben wird. Diess ändert\n nur die Erscheinung, ohne sie zu verschönern. Da das Verhältniss\n des individuellen Kapitalisten und des individuellen Arbeiters das von ein-\n ander unabhängiger Waarenbesitzer ist, von denen der eine Arbeitskraft\n kauft, der andre verkauft, ist ihr Zusammenhang zufällig. Der Kapi-\n talist verwandelt vielleicht das Surpluskapital in eine Maschine, die den\n Producenten des Surpluskapitals aufs Pflaster wirft und durch ein paar\n Kinder ersetzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"19.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0011","text":"In dem Surpluskapital Nr. I sind alle Bestandtheile Produkt unbezahlter\n fremder Arbeit, kapitalisirter Mehrwerth. Es verschwindet der Schein\n der ersten Darstellung des Produktionsprozesses oder des ersten Akts der\n Kapitalbildung, als ob der Kapitalist irgend welche Werthe aus seinem\n eignen Fonds in die Cirkulation würfe. Erst entführt die unsichtbare\n Magie des Prozesses das Mehrprodukt des Arbeiters von seinem Pol zum\n Gegenpol des Kapitalisten. Dann verwandelt der Kapitalist diesen Reich-\n thum, der für ihn eine Schöpfung aus Nichts ist, in Kapital, in ein Mittel\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"570\", \"facs\": \"#f0589\"} --> zur Anwendung, Beherrschung und Exploitation zuschüssiger Arbeits-\n kraft [Note: „Die Arbeit schafft das Kapital, bevor das Kapital die Arbeit anwendet.“\n („Labour creates capital, before capital employs labour.“) E. G. Wakefield:\n „England and America. Lond. 1833“, v.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"19.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0012","text":"II, p. 110.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"19.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0013","text":"Im kapitalistischen Produktionsprozess wird ursprünglich\n nur eine dem Geldbesitzer, wir wissen nicht auf welche Titel hin, gehörige\nWerthsumme in Kapital und daher Quelle von Mehrwerth ver-\n wandelt. Es geht eine Veränderung mit dieser Werthsumme vor, aber sie\n selbst ist nicht das Resultat des Prozesses, sondern vielmehr seine\n von ihm unabhängige Voraussetzung. Im einfachen Repro-\n duktionsprozess, oder dem kontinuirlichen Produktionsprozess, ist es\n ein Theil vom Produkt des Arbeiters, der ihm stets von neuem als varia-\n bles Kapital gegenübertritt, aber sein Produkt nimmt stets von neuem\n diese Form an, weil er ursprünglich seine Arbeitskraft für das\n Geld des Kapitalisten verkaufte.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"19.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0014","text":"Endlich verwandelt sich im\n Verlauf der Reproduktion aller vom Kapitalisten vorgeschossene Kapi-\n talwerth in kapitalisirten Mehrwerth, aber diese Verwandlung\n selbst unterstellt, dass der Fonds ursprünglich aus seinen eignen Mitteln\n herstammt. Anders im Accumulationsprozess oder dem Repro-\n duktionsprozess auf erweiterter Stufenleiter. Das Geld, oder stofflich aus-\n gedrückt, die Produktions- und Lebensmittel, die Substanz des neuen\n Kapitals, ist selbst das Produkt des Prozesses, der fremde unbezahlte Ar-\n beit auspumpt. Das Kapital hat Kapital producirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"19.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0015","text":"Eine dem Kapitalisten gehörige Werthsumme von 10,000 Pfd. St.\n war die Voraussetzung für Bildung des Surpluskapitals\n Nr. I von 2000 Pfd. St. Die Voraussetzung des Surpluskapi-\n tals Nr. II von 400 Pfd. St. ist nichts anders als die Existenz des\n Surpluskapitals Nr. I. Eigenthum an vergangner unbezahlter\n Arbeit erscheint jetzt als die einzige Bedingung für gegenwärtige An-\n eignung unbezahlter lebendiger Arbeit in stets wachsendem Umfang.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"19.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0016","text":"Insofern der Mehrwerth, woraus Surpluskapital Nr. I besteht, das Re-\n sultat des Ankaufs der Arbeitskraft durch einen Theil des Originalkapitals\n war, ein Kauf, der den Gesetzen des Waarenaustausches entsprach, und,\n juristisch betrachtet, nichts voraussetzt als freie Verfügung, auf Seiten des\n Arbeiters über seine eignen Fähigkeiten, auf Seiten des Geld- oder Waaren-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"571\", \"facs\": \"#f0590\"} --> besitzers über ihm gehörige Werthe; sofern Surpluskapital Nr. II u. s. w.\n bloss Resultat von Surpluskapital Nr.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"19.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0017","text":"I, also Konsequenz jenes ersten Ver-\n hältnisses; sofern jede einzelne Transaktion fortwährend dem Gesetz des\n Waarenaustausches entspricht, der Kapitalist stets die Arbeitskraft kauft,\n der Arbeiter sie stets verkauft, und wir wollen annehmen selbst zu ihrem\n wirklichen Werth, schlägt offenbar das auf Waarenproduktion und\n Waarencirkulation beruhende Gesetz der Aneignung oder\nGesetz des Privateigenthums durch seine eigne, innere,\n unvermeidliche Dialektik in sein direktes Gegentheil\n um [Note: Ganz so nothwendig, wie die Waarenproduktion auf einem gewissen\n Entwicklungsgrad kapitalistische Waarenproduktion wird — ja nur auf\n der Grundlage der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise wird die Waare zur allge-\n meinen, herrschenden Form des Produkts, — ganz so nothwendig schlagen\n die Eigenthumsgesetze der Waarenproduktion in Gesetze der\n kapitalistischen Aneignung um.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"19.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0018","text":"Man bewundere daher die Pfiffigkeit\nProudhon’s, der das kapitalistische Eigenthum abschaffen will, indem er — die\n ewigen Eigenthumsgesetze der Waarenproduktion geltend macht!] . Der Austausch von Aequivalenten, der als die ursprüngliche\n Operation erschien, hat sich so gedreht, dass nur zum Schein ausge-\n tauscht wird, indem erstens der gegen Arbeitskraft ausgetauschte Kapital-\n theil selbst nur ein Theil des ohne Aequivalent angeeigneten\n fremden Arbeitsproduktes ist, und zweitens von seinem Producenten,\n dem Arbeiter, nicht nur ersetzt, sondern mit neuem Surplus ersetzt wer-\n den muss. Das Verhältniss des Austausches zwischen Ka-\n pitalist und Arbeiter wird also nur dem Cirkulationspro-\n zess angehöriger Schein, blosse Form, die dem Inhalt selbst\n fremd ist und ihn nur mystificirt. Der beständige Kauf und Verkauf der\n Arbeitskraft ist die Form.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"19.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0019","text":"Der Inhalt ist, dass der Kapitalist einen Theil\n der bereits vergegenständlichten fremden Arbeit, die er sich unauf hörlich\n ohne Aequivalent aneignet, stets wieder gegen grösseres Quantum leben-\n diger fremder Arbeit umsetzt. Ursprünglich erschien uns das Eigenthums-\n recht gegründet auf eigne Arbeit. Wenigstens musste diese Annahme gel-\n ten, da sich nur gleichberechtigte Waarenbesitzer gegenüberstehn, das Mit-\n tel zur Aneignung fremder Waare aber nur die Veräusserung der eignen\n Waare, und letztere nur durch Arbeit herstellbar ist. Eigenthum erscheint\n jetzt, auf Seite des Kapitalisten, als das Recht fremde unbezahlte\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"572\", \"facs\": \"#f0591\"} --> Arbeit oder ihr Produkt, auf Seite des Arbeiters, als Unmöglichkeit, sich\n sein eignes Produkt anzueignen.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"19.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0020","text":"Die Scheidung zwischen Eigen-\n thum und Arbeit wird zur nothwendigen Konsequenz eines Gesetzes,\n das scheinbar von ihrer Identität ausging [Note: Das Eigenthum des Kapitalisten an dem fremden Arbeitsprodukt „ist\n strenge Konsequenz des Gesetzes der Aneignung, dessen Fundamentalprin-\n cip umgekehrt der ausschliessliche Eigenthumstitel jeden Arbeiters am Pro-\n dukt seiner eignen Arbeit war.“ (Cherbuliez: „Riche ou Pauvre.\n Paris 1841“, p. 58, wo jedoch dieser dialektische Umschlag nicht richtig\n entwickelt wird.)] . Man sah, dass selbst\n bei einfacher Reproduktion alles vorgeschossene Kapital, wie immer ur-\n sprünglich erworben, sich in accumulirtes Kapital oder kapitalisirten Mehr-\n werth verwandelt.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"19.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0021","text":"Aber im Strom der Produktion wird überhaupt alles\n ursprünglich vorgeschossene Kapital eine verschwindende Grösse (magni-\n tudo evanescens im mathematischen Sinn) verglichen mit dem direkt accu-\n mulirten Kapital, d. h. in Kapital rückverwandelten Mehrwerth oder Mehr-\n produkt, ob nun funktionirend in der Hand, die accumulirt hat, oder in\n fremder Hand. Die politische Oekonomie stellt das Kapital daher über-\n haupt als „accumulirten Reichthum (verwandelten Mehrwerth oder\n Revenue) dar, der von neuem zur Produktion von Mehrwerth verwandt\n wird“ [Note: „Capital viz: accumulated wealth employed with a view to profit.“\nMalthus. l. c. „Capital … consists of wealth saved from revenue,\n and used with a view to profit.“ (R. Jones: „An Introductory Lecture\n on Polit. Econ. Lond. 1833“, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"19.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0022","text":"16.)] , oder auch den Kapitalisten als „Besitzer des Mehrprodukts“ [Note: „The possessors of surplusproduce or capital.“ („The Source and\n Remedy of the National Difficulties. A Letter to Lord John\n Russell. Lond. 1821.“)] .\n Dieselbe Anschauungsweise besitzt nur andre Form in dem Ausdruck, dass\n alles vorhandne Kapital accumulirter oder kapitalisirter Zins sei, denn\n der Zins ist ein blosses Bruchstück des Mehrwerths [Note: „Capital, with compound interest on every portion of capital saved, is so\n all engrossing, that all the wealth in the world from which income is derived, has\n long ago become the interest on capital.“ (London Economist, 19. July,\n 1859.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"19.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0023","text":"Bevor wir nun auf einige nähere Bestimmungen der Accumula-\n tion oder der Rückverwandlung von Mehrwerth in Kapital eingehn, ist\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"573\", \"facs\": \"#f0592\"} --> eine von der klassischen Oekonomie ausgeheckte Zweideutigkeit zu be-\n seitigen.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"19.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0024","text":"So wenig die Waaren, die der Kapitalist mit einem Theil des\n Mehrwerths für seine eigne Konsumtion kauft, ihm als Produktions- und\n Verwerthungsmittel dienen, so wenig ist die Arbeit, die er zur Befriedi-\n gung seiner natürlichen und socialen Bedürfnisse kauft, produktive\n Arbeit. Statt durch den Kauf jener Waaren und Arbeit den Mehrwerth\n in Kapital zu verwandeln, verzehrt oder verausgabt er ihn umgekehrt\n als Revenue. Gegenüber der altadlichen Gesinnung, die, wie Hegel richtig\n sagt, „im Verzehren des Vorhandenen besteht“ und namentlich auch im\n Luxus persönlicher Dienste sich breit macht, war es entscheidend wichtig\n für die bürgerliche Oekonomie hervorzuheben, dass das Evangelium der\n neuen Gesellschaft, nämlich Accumulation von Kapital, die Auslage von\n Mehrwerth im Ankauf produktiver Arbeiter als conditio sine qua\n predigt.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"19.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0025","text":"Andrerseits hatte man gegen das Volksvorurtheil zu polemisiren,\n welches die kapitalistische Produktion mit der Schatzbildung verwechselt [Note: „No political economist of the present day can by saving mean mere\n hoarding: and beyond this contracted and insufficient proceeding, no use of the\n term in reference to the national wealth can well be imagined, but that which must\n arise from a different application of what is saved, founded upon a real distinc-\n tion between the different kinds of labour maintained by it.“ (Malthus l. c.\n p. 38, 39.)] \n und daher wähnt, accumulirter Reichthum sei dem Verbrauch, also der\n Zerstörung in seiner vorhandnen Naturalform, entzogner oder auch vor der\n Cirkulation geretteter Reichthum.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"19.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0026","text":"Verschluss des Geldes gegen die Cir-\n kulation wäre grade das Gegentheil seiner Verwerthung als Kapital, und\n Waarenaccumulation im schatzbildnerischen Sinn reine Narrheit. Accumu-\n lation von Waaren in grossen Massen ist Resultat einer Cirkulationsstockung\n oder der Ueberproduktion [Note: „Accumulation of stocks … non-exchange … overproduction.“\n (Th. Corbet l. c. p. 14.)] . Allerdings läuft in der Volksvorstellung\n das Bild der im Konsumtionsfonds der Reichen gehäuften, lang-\n sam sich verzehrenden Güter unter. Andrerseits die Vorrathbildung,\n ein Phänomen, das allen Produktionsweisen angehört und wobei wir einen\n Augenblick in der Analyse des Cirkulationsprozesses verweilen werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"19.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0027","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"574\", \"facs\": \"#f0593\"} --> Soweit also ist die klassische Oekonomie im Recht, wenn sie den Verzehr von\n Surplusprodukt durch produktive Arbeiter statt durch unproduktive\n als charakteristisches Phänomen des Accumulationsprozesses\n betont. Aber hier beginnt auch ihr Irrthum. A. Smith hat es zur\n Mode gemacht, die Accumulation als Konsumtion des Mehr-\n produkts durch produktive Arbeiter oder die Kapitalisi-\n rung des Mehrwerths als dessen blossen Umsatz in Ar-\n beitskraft darzustellen. Hören wir z. B. Ricardo: „Man muss\n verstehn, dass alle Produkte eines Landes konsumirt werden; aber es\n macht den grössten Unterschied, den man denken kann, ob sie konsumirt\n werden durch solche, die einen andern Werth reproduciren oder durch\n solche, die ihn nicht reproduciren.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"19.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0028","text":"Wenn wir sagen, dass Revenue erspart\n und zum Kapital geschlagen wird, so meinen wir, dass der Theil der\n Revenue, von dem es heisst, er sei zum Kapital geschla-\n gen, durch produktive statt durch unproduktive Arbei-\n ter verzehrt wird“ [Note: Ricardo l. c. p. 163 Note] . Es giebt keinen grösseren Irrthum als der\n dem A. Smith von Ricardo und allen Späteren nachgeplauderte, dass „der\n Theil der Revenue, von dem es heisst, er sei zum Kapital geschlagen,\nvon produktiven Arbeitern verzehrt wird.“ Nach dieser\n Vorstellung würde aller Mehrwerth, der in Kapital verwandelt wird, zu\nvariablem Kapital. Er theilt sich vielmehr, wie der ursprünglich\n vorgeschossene Werth, in constantes Kapital und variables\n Kapital, in Produktionsmittel und Arbeitskraft. Arbeitskraft ist die\n Form, worin das variable Kapital innerhalb des Produktionsprozesses\n existirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"19.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0029","text":"In diesem Prozess wird sie selbst vom Kapitalisten verzehrt. Sie\n verzehrt durch ihre Funktion — die Arbeit — Produktionsmittel. Zugleich\n verwandelt sich das im Ankauf der Arbeitskraft gezahlte Geld in\n Lebensmittel, die nicht von der „produktiven Arbeit“, sondern vom\n „produktiven Arbeiter“ verzehrt werden. A. Smith gelangt durch\n eine grundverkehrte Analyse zu dem abgeschmackten Resultat, dass wenn\n auch jedes individuelle Kapital sich in constanten und variablen\n Bestandtheil theilt, das gesellschaftliche Kapital sich in nur varia-\n bles Kapital auflöst oder nur in Zahlung von Arbeitslohn verausgabt wird.\n Z. B. ein Tuchfabrikant verwandle 2000 Pfd. St. in Kapital. Er legt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"575\", \"facs\": \"#f0594\"} --> einen Theil des Geldes im Ankauf von Webern aus, den andern Theil\n in Wollengarn, Wollenmaschinerie u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"19.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0030","text":"Aber die Leute, von\n denen er das Garn und die Maschinerie kauft, zahlen wieder mit einem\n Theil davon Arbeit u. s. w., bis die ganzen 2000 Pfd. St. in Zahlung\n von Arbeitslohn verausgabt sind, oder das ganze durch die 2000 Pfd. St.\n repräsentirte Produkt durch produktive Arbeiter verzehrt ist. Man\n sieht: die ganze Wucht dieses Arguments liegt in dem Wort „u. s. w.“,\n das uns von Pontius zu Pilatus schickt. In der That, A. Smith\n bricht die Untersuchung grade da ab, wo ihre Schwierigkeit beginnt [Note: Trotz seiner „Logik“ kommt Herr J. St. Mill nirgendwo auch nur\n solcher fehlerhaften Analyse seiner Vorgänger auf die Sprünge, welche selbst\n innerhalb des bürgerlichen Horizonts, vom reinen Fachstandpunkt aus, nach Be-\n richtigung schreit. Ueberall registrirt er mit schülermässigem Dogmatismus\n die Gedankenwirren seiner Meister.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"19.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0031","text":"Auch hier: „The capital itself in the\n long run becomes entirely wages, and when replaced by the sale of produce\nbecomes wages again.“] .\n Im dritten Kapitel des zweiten Buchs werde ich die Analyse\n des wirklichen Zusammenhangs geben. Es wird sich dort zeigen, dass\n A. Smith’s auf alle seine Nachfolger vererbtes Dogma die politische Oeko-\n nomie verhindert hat auch nur den Elementarmechanismus des gesellschaft-\n lichen Reproduktionsprozesses zu begreifen [Note: A. Smith hat in der Darstellung des Reproduktionsprozesses, daher auch\n der Accumulation, nach mancher Seite hin nicht nur keine Fortschritte, sondern\n entschiedene Rückschritte gemacht im Vergleich zu seinen Vorgängern, nament-\n lich den Physiokraten.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"19.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0032","text":"Mit seiner im Text erwähnten Illusion hängt das eben-\n falls von ihm der politischen Oekonomie vererbte, wahrhaft fabelhafte Dogma zu-\n sammen, dass der Preis der Waaren aus Arbeitslohn, Profit (Zins) und\n Grundrente, also bloss aus Arbeitslohn und Mehrwerth zusammengesetzt\n ist. Von dieser Basis ausgehend, gesteht wenigstens Storch naiv: „Il est im-\n possible de résoudre le prix nécessaire dans ses élémens les plus simples.“\n (Storch l. c. Petersb. Edit. 1815, t. I, p. 140, Note.) Eine schöne ökonomische\n Wissenschaft, die es für unmöglich erklärt, den Preis der Waaren auf seine\n einfachsten Elemente zu analysiren! Das Nähere hierüber wird man erörtert finden\n im 7. Kap. des dritten Buchs.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"19.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0033","text":"Im ersten Abschnitt dieses Kapitels betrachteten wir den ganzen\n Mehrwerth, resp. das Mehrprodukt, nur als individuellen Konsumtions-\n fonds des Kapitalisten, in diesem Abschnitt bisher nur als seinen\nAccumulationsfonds. Er ist aber weder nur das eine, noch das\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"576\", \"facs\": \"#f0595\"} --> andre, sondern beides zugleich. Ein Theil des Mehrwerths wird vom\n Kapitalisten als Revenue verzehrt [Note: Der Leser wird bemerken, dass das Wort Revenue doppelt gebraucht\n wird, erstens um den Mehrwerth als periodisch aus dem Kapital entspringende\n Frucht, zweitens um den Theil dieser Frucht zu bezeichnen, der vom Kapitalisten\n periodisch verzehrt oder zu seinem Konsumtionsfonds geschlagen wird. Ich behalte\n diesen Doppelsinn bei, weil er mit dem Sprachgebrauch der englischen und fran-\n zösischen Oekonomen harmonirt.] , ein andrer Theil als Kapital\n angewandt oder accumulirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"19.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0034","text":"Die Masse des Mehrwerths gegeben, hängt die Grösse der\n Accumulation offenbar ab von der Theilung des Mehrwerths in\n Accumulationsfonds und Konsumtionsfonds, in Kapital\n und Revenue. Je grösser der eine Theil, desto kleiner der andre. Die\n Masse des Mehrwerths oder des Mehrprodukts, daher des disponiblen Reich-\n thums eines Landes, die in Kapital verwandelt werden kann, ist daher stets\n grösser als der wirklich in Kapital verwandelte Theil des Mehr-\n werths. Je entwickelter die kapitalistische Produktion in einem Lande, je\n rascher und massenhafter die Accumulation, je reicher das Land, je kolossaler\n daher Luxus und Verschwendung, desto grösser diese Differenz.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"19.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0035","text":"Vom\n jährlichen Zuwachs des Reichthums abgesehn, besitzt der im Konsumtions-\n fonds der Kapitalisten befindliche, nur allmälig zerstörbare Reichthum zum\n Theil Naturalformen, worin er unmittelbar als Kapital funktioniren\n könnte. Zu den vorhandnen Elementen des Reichthums, die im Produk-\n tionsprozess funktioniren könnten, zählen alle Arbeitskräfte, die gar\n nicht oder zu rein konventionell persönlichen, oft infamen, Dienstleistungen\n verbraucht werden. Die Proportion, worin der Mehrwerth in Kapi-\n tal und Revenue getheilt wird, wechselt unaufhörlich und ist durch Um-\n stände beherrscht, die hier nicht weiter zu entwickeln sind. Das in einem\n Land angewandte Kapital ist daher keine fixe, sondern\n eine fluktuirende Grösse, ein stets variabler und elasti-\n scher Bruchtheil des vorhandnen Reichthums, der als\n Kapital funktioniren kann.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"19.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0036","text":"Da die beständige Aneignung des vom Arbeiter producirten Mehrwerths\n oder Mehrprodukts für den Kapitalisten als periodische Fruchttragung seines\n Kapitals erscheint, oder das fremde Arbeitsprodukt, welches er ohne Aequiva-\n lent irgend einer Art usurpirt, einen periodischen Zuwachs seines Privat-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"577\", \"facs\": \"#f0596\"} --> vermögens bildet, ist natürlich auch die Theilung dieses Mehrwerths\n oder Mehrprodukts in Zuschusskapital und Konsumtionsfonds durch einen\n Willensakt seinerseits vermittelt.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"19.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0037","text":"Nur soweit der Kapitalist personificirtes Kapital ist, hat\n er einen historischen Werth und jenes historische Existenzrecht, das,\n wie der geistreiche Lichnowsky sagt, keinen Datum nicht hat. Nur soweit\n steckt seine eigne transitorische Nothwendigkeit in der transitorischen\n Nothwendigkeit der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise.\n Aber soweit sind auch nicht Gebrauchswerth und Genuss, sondern Tausch-\n werth und dessen Vermehrung sein treibendes Motiv. Als Fanatiker der Ver-\n werthung des Werths zwingt er rücksichtslos die Menschheit zur Produk-\n tion um der Produktion willen, daher zu einer Entwicklung der ge-\n sellschaftlichen Produktivkräfte und zur Schöpfung von materiel-\n len Produktionsbedingungen, welche allein die reale Basis einer\n höheren Gesellschaftsform bilden können, deren Grundprincip die volle und\n freie Entwicklung jedes Individuums ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"19.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0038","text":"Nur als Personifikation des Kapi-\n tals ist der Kapitalist respektabel. Als solche theilt er mit dem Schatzbild-\n ner den absoluten Bereicherungstrieb. Ausserdem zwingen ihn die immanen-\n ten Gesetze der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise, welche die Konkurrenz\n jedem individuellen Kapitalisten als äussere Zwangsgesetze oktroyirt,\n sein Kapital fortwährend auszudehnen, um es zu erhalten. Soweit daher\n sein Thun und Lassen nur Funktion des in ihm mit Willen und Bewusst-\n sein begabten Kapitals, gilt ihm sein eigner Privatkonsum als ein Raub\n an der Accumulation seines Kapitals, wie in der italienischen Buchhal-\n tung Privatausgaben auf der Debetseite des Kapitalisten gegen das Ka-\n pital figuriren. Die Accumulation ist Eroberung der Welt des gesellschaft-\n lichen Reichthums.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"19.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0039","text":"Sie dehnt mit der Masse des exploitirten Menschen-\n materials zugleich die direkte und indirekte Herrschaft des Kapita-\n listen aus [Note: In der altmodischen, wenn auch sporadisch fortexistirenden, Form des\n Kapitalisten, im Wucherer veranschaulicht Luther sehr gut die Herrschsucht\n als Element des Bereicherungstriebs. „Die Heiden haben können aus der Ver-\n nunfft rechnen, das ein Wucherer sey ein vierfaltiger Dieb und Mörder. Wir\n Christen aber halten sie in solchen ehren, das wir sie schier anbeten umb ihres\n Geldes willen … Wer einem andern seine Narung aussauget, raubet und stilet,\n der thut eben so grossen Mord (so viel an jm ligt) als der einen Hungers sterbet] .","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"19.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0040","text":"[Running matter: I. 37] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"578\", \"facs\": \"#f0597\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"19.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0041","text":"Aber die Erbsünde wirkt überall. Mit der Entwicklung der kapi-\n talistischen Produktionsweise, der Accumulation, und des Reichthums, hört\n der Kapitalist auf, blosse Inkarnation des Kapitals zu sein. Er fühlt ein\n „menschliches Rühren“ für seinen eignen Adam und wird so gebildet, die\n Schwärmerei für Ascese als Vorurtheil des altmodischen Schatzbildners zu\n belächeln. Während der klassische Kapitalist den individuellen Konsum\n als Sünde gegen seine Funktion und „Enthaltung“ von der Accumulation\n brandmarkt, ist der modernisirte Kapitalist im Stande, die Accumulation\n als „Entsagung“ seines Genusstriebs aufzufassen. „Zwei Seelen wohnen\n Ach! in seiner Brust, die eine will sich von der andern trennen!“","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"19.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0042","text":"[Note: und zu Grunde verterbet. Solches thut aber ein Wucherer, und sitzet die weil auf\n seinem Stuel sicher, so er billicher hangen solt am Galgen, und von soviel Raben\n gefressen werden, als er gülden gestolen hatte, wo anders so viel fleisches an jm\n were, das so viel Raben sich drein stücken und teilen kündten. Dieweil hanget\n man die kleinen Diebe … Kleine Diebe ligen in Stöcken gefangen, grosse Diebe\n gehn in gold und seiden prangen … Also ist auch kein grösser Menschenfeind\n auff Erden (nach dem Teuffel) denn ein Geitzhals und Wucherer, denn er will\n über alle menschen Gott sein. Türcken, Krieger, Tyrannen sind auch\n böse Menschen, doch müssen sie lassen die Leute leben und bekennen, dass sie Böse\n und Feinde sind, Und können, ja müssen wol zu weilen sich über etliche erbarmen.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"19.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0043","text":"Aber ein Wucherer und Geitzwanst, der wilt das alle Welt im müsste in Hunger und\n Durst, Trauer und Not verderben, so viel an jm ist, auff das ers alles allein möcht\n haben, und jedermann von jm, als von einem Gott empfahen und ewiglich\nsein Leibeigener sein. Schauben, güldne Kette, Ringe tragen, das maul wischen,\n sich für einen theuren, frommen Mann lassen ansehen und rhümen … Wucher\n ist ein gros und ungeheur monstrum, wie ein Beerwolff, der alles wüstet, mehr den\n kein Cacus, Gerion oder Antus. Und schmückt sich doch und wil fromm sein,\n das man nicht sehen sol, wo die Ochsen, die er rücklings in sein Loch\n zieht, hinkommen. Aber Hercules sol der Ochsen und der Gefangenen Geschrey\n hören und den Cacum suchen auch in Klippen und Felsen, die Ochsen wider\n lösen, von dem Bösewicht. Denn Cacus heisst ein Bösewicht, der ein frommer\n Wucherer ist, stilet, raubet, frisst alles.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"19.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0044","text":"Und wils doch nicht gethan haben, und\n sol ja nimand finden, weil die Ochsen rücklings in sein Loch ge-\n zogen, schein und fussstapffen geben, als seien sie herausge-\n lassen. Also wil der Wucherer auch die Welt effen, als nütze er und gebe\n der welt ochsen, so er sie doch zu sich allein reisst und frisst …\n Und so man die Strassenreuber, Mörder und Beuheder, redert und köpffet, wie viel\n mehr solt man alle Wucherer redern und edern … verjagen, verfluchen und\n köpffen.“ (Martin Luther l. c.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"19.44"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0045","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"579\", \"facs\": \"#f0598\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"19.45"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0046","text":"In den historischen Anfängen der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise\n — und jeder kapitalistische Parvenü macht diess historische Stadium in-\n dividuell durch — herrschen Bereicherungstrieb und Geiz als absolute\n Leidenschaften vor. Aber der Fortschritt der kapitalistischen Produk-\n tion schafft nicht nur eine Welt von Genüssen. Er öffnet mit der Speku-\n lation und dem Kreditwesen tausend Quellen plötzlicher Bereicherung.\n Auf einer gewissen Entwicklungshöhe wird ein konventioneller Grad von\n Verschwendung, die zugleich Schaustellung des Reichthums und daher\n Kreditmittel ist, sogar zu einer Geschäftsnothwendigkeit des „unglück-\n lichen“ Kapitalisten. Der Luxus geht in die Repräsentationskosten des Ka-\n pitals ein.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"19.46"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0047","text":"Ohnehin bereichert sich der Kapitalist nicht, gleich dem Schatz-\n bildner, im Verhältniss seiner persönlichen Arbeit und seines persönlichen\n Nichtkonsums, sondern im Mass, worin er fremde Arbeitskraft aussaugt\n und dem Arbeiter Entsagung aller Lebensgenüsse aufzwingt. Obgleich\n daher die Verschwendung des Kapitalisten nie den bona fide. Charakter\n der Verschwendung des flotten Feudalherrn besitzt, in ihrem Hin-\n tergrund vielmehr stets schmutzigster Geiz und ängstlichste Berechnung\n lauern, wächst dennoch seine Verschwendung mit seiner Accumulation,\n ohne dass die eine die andre zu beabbruchen braucht. Damit entwickelt\n sich gleichzeitig in der Hochbrust des Kapitalindividuums ein faustischer\n Konflikt zwischen Accumulations- und Genusstrieb.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"19.47"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0048","text":"„Die Industrie von Manchester“, heisst es in einer Schrift, die Dr.\n Aikin 1795 veröffentlichte, „kann in vier Perioden getheilt werden.\n In der ersten waren die Fabrikanten gezwungen, hart für ihren Lebens-\n unterhalt zu arbeiten.“ Sie bereicherten sich besonders durch Bestehlung\n der Eltern, die ihnen Jungen als apprentices (Lehrlinge) zuwiesen und da-\n für schwer blechen mussten, während die Lehrlinge ausgehungert wurden.\n Andrerseits waren die Durchschnittsprofite niedrig und die Accumulation\n verlangte grosse Sparsamkeit. Sie lebten wie Schatzbildner und verzehr-\n ten bei weitem nicht einmal die Zinsen ihres Kapitals. „In der zweiten\n Periode hatten sie begonnen, kleine Vermögen zu erwerben, arbeiteten\n aber ebenso hart als zuvor,“ denn die unmittelbare Exploitation der\n Arbeit kostet Arbeit, wie jeder Sklaventreiber weiss, „und lebten nach\n wie vor in demselben frugalen Styl.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"19.48"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0049","text":"… In der dritten Periode begann der\n Luxus und das Geschäft wurde ausgedehnt durch Aussendung von Reitern\n (berittenen Commis Voyageurs) für Ordres in jeder Marktstadt des König-\n [Running matter: 37*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"580\", \"facs\": \"#f0599\"} --> reichs. Es ist wahrscheinlich, dass wenige oder keine Kapitalien von\n 3000 bis 4000 Pfd. St., in der Industrie erworben, vor 1690 existirten.\n Um diese Zeit jedoch oder etwas später hatten die Industriellen schon Geld\n accumulirt und begannen steinerne Häuser statt der von Holz und Mörtel\n aufzuführen. … Noch in den ersten Decennien des 18.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"19.49"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0050","text":"Jahrhunderts\n setzte sich ein Manchester Fabrikant, der eine Pint fremden Weins seinen\n Gästen vorsetzte, den Glossen und dem Kopfschütteln aller seiner Nach-\n barn aus.“ Vor dem Aufkommen der Maschinerie betrug der abendliche\n Konsum der Fabrikanten in den Kneipen, wo sie zusammenkamen, nie mehr\n als 6 d. für ein Glas Punsch und 1 d. für eine Rolle Taback. Erst 1758,\n und diess macht Epoche, sah man „eine im Geschäft wirklich engagirte\n Person mit eigner Equipage!“ „Die vierte Periode“, das letzte Drittheil\n des 18. Jahrhunderts, „ist die von grossem Luxus und Verschwendung,\n unterstützt durch die Ausdehnung des Geschäfts“ [Note: Dr. Aikin: „Description of the Country from 30 to 40\nmiles round Manchester. Lond. 1795“, p. 182 sqq.] . Was würde der\n gute Dr. Aikin sagen, wenn er heutzutag in Manchester auferstände!","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"19.50"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0051","text":"Accumulirt, accumulirt! Das ist Moses und die Propheten! „Die\n Industrie liefert das Material, welches die Sparsamkeit accumulirt“ [Note: A. Smith l. c. b. III, ch. III.] .\n Also spart, spart, d. h. rückverwandelt möglichst grossen Theil des Mehr-\n werths oder Mehrprodukts in Kapital! Accumulation um der Accumula-\n tion, Produktion um der Produktion willen, in dieser Formel sprach die\n klassische Oekonomie den historischen Beruf der Bourgeoisperiode aus.\n Sie täuschte sich keinen Augenblick über die Geburtswehn des Reich-\n thums [Note: Selbst J. B. Say sagt: „Les épargnes des riches se font aux dépens des\n pauvres.“ „Der römische Proletarier lebte fast ganz auf Kosten der Ge-\n sellschaft . . . . Man könnte fast sagen, dass die moderne Gesellschaft auf Kosten\n der Proletarier lebt, von dem Theil, den sie auf Belohnung der Arbeit ihnen ent-\n zieht.“ (Sismondi: „Études etc.“ t. 1, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"19.51"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0052","text":"24.)] , aber was nützt der Jammer über historische Nothwendigkeit?\n Wenn der klassischen Oekonomie der Proletarier nur als Maschine zur\n Produktion von Mehrwerth, gilt ihr aber auch der Kapitalist nur als Ma-\n schine zur Verwandlung dieses Mehrwerths in Mehrkapital. Sie nimmt\n seine historische Funktion in bitterm Ernst. Um seinen Busen vor dem\n unheilvollen Konflikt zwischen Genusstrieb und Bereicherungstrieb zu feien,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"581\", \"facs\": \"#f0600\"} --> vertheidigte Malthus, im Anfang der zwanziger Jahre dieses Jahrhun-\n derts, eine Theilung der Arbeit, welche dem wirklich in der Produktion\n begriffenen Kapitalisten das Geschäft der Accumulation, den andern Theil-\n nehmern am Mehrwerth, der Landaristokratie, Staats-, Kirchenpfründnern\n u. s. w. das Geschäft der Verschwendung zuweist.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"19.52"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0053","text":"Es ist von der höch-\n sten Wichtigkeit, sagt er, „die Leidenschaft für Ausgabe und die Leiden-\n schaft für Accumulation („the passion for expenditure and the passion for\n accumulation“) getrennt zu halten“ [Note: Malthus l. c. p. 319, 320.] . Die Herrn Kapitalisten, seit lange\n in Lebe- und Weltmänner verwandelt, schrieen auf. Was, rief einer ihrer\n Wortführer, ein Ricardianer, Herr Malthus predigt hohe Grundrenten, hohe\n Steuern u. s. w., um dem Industriellen einen fortwährenden Stachel durch\n unproduktive Konsumenten aufzudrücken! Allerdings Produktion, Pro-\n duktion auf stets erweiterter Stufenleiter, lautet das Schiboleth, aber „Pro-\n duktion wird durch einen solchen Prozess weit mehr gehemmt als geför-\n dert.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"19.53"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0054","text":"Auch ist es nicht ganz billig (nor is it quite fair) eine\n Anzahl Personen so im Müssiggang zu erhalten, nur um andre zu kneipen,\n aus deren Charakter man schliessen darf, („who are likely, from their\n characters“), dass wenn ihr sie zu funktioniren zwingen könnt, sie mit\n Erfolg funktioniren“ [Note: „An Inquiry into those principles respecting the Nature\n of Demand etc.“, p. 67.] . So unbillig er es findet den industriellen Kapi-\n talisten zur Accumulation zu stacheln, indem man ihm das Fett von der\n Suppe wegschöpft, so nothwendig dünkt ihm den Arbeiter möglichst auf\n den Minimallohn zu beschränken, „um ihn arbeitsam zu erhalten“. Auch\n verheimlicht er keinen Augenblick, dass Aneignung unbezahlter Arbeit das\n Geheimniss der Plusmacherei ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"19.54"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0055","text":"„Vermehrte Nachfrage von Seite der\n Arbeiter meint durchaus nichts als ihre Geneigtheit weniger von ihrem\n eignen Produkt für sich selbst zu nehmen und einen grössren\n Theil davon ihren Anwendern zu überlassen; und wenn man sagt,\n dass diess, durch Verminderung der Konsumtion (auf Seiten der Arbeiter)\nglut (Marktüberfüllung, Ueberproduktion) erzeugt, so kann ich nur ant-\n worten, dass glut synonym mit hohem Profit ist“ [Note: l. c. p. 50.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"19.55"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0056","text":"Der gelehrte Zank, wie die dem Arbeiter ausgepumpte Beute för-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"582\", \"facs\": \"#f0601\"} --> derlichst für die Accumulation zu vertheilen sei zwischen industriellem Kapi-\n talist und müssigem Grundeigenthümer u. s. w., verstummte vor der Juli-\n revolution. Kurz nachher läutete das städtische Proletariat die Sturm-\n glocke zu Lyon und liess das Landproletariat den rothen Hahn in England\n fliegen. Diesseits des Kanals grassirte der Owenismus, jenseits St. Simo-\n nismus und Fourierismus. Die Stunde der Vulgärökonomie hatte ge-\n schlagen. Grade ein Jahr, bevor Nassau W. Senior zu Manchester\n ausfand, dass der Profit (incl. Zins) des Kapitals das Produkt der un-\n bezahlten „letzten zwölften Arbeitsstunde“ ist, hatte er der\n Welt eine andere Entdeckung angekündigt.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"19.56"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0057","text":"„Ich“, sagte er feierlich, „ich\nersetze das Wort Kapital, als Produktionsinstrument betrachtet,\ndurch das Wort Abstinenz (Enthaltung)“ [Note: Senior: „Principes fondamentaux del’Écon. Pol.“ trad.\n Arrivabene. Paris 1836, p. 308. Diess war den Anhängern der alten\n klassischen Schule doch etwas zu toll. „Herr Senior schiebt dem Ausdruck\n Arbeit und Kapital den Ausdruck Arbeit und Abstinenz unter … Abstinenz ist\n eine blosse Negation. Es ist nicht die Abstinenz, sondern der Gebrauch des\n produktiv verwandten Kapitals, welcher die Quelle des Profits bildet.“ (John\n Cazenove l. c. p. 130 Note.) Herr John St. Mill excerpirt dagegen auf der\n einen Seite Ricardo’s Profittheorie und annexirt auf der andern Senior’s „remune-\n ration of abstinence.“ So fremd ihm der Hegel’sche „Widerspruch“, die Spring-\n quelle aller Dialektik, so heimisch ist er in platten Widersprüchen.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"19.57"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0058","text":"Ein unübertroffenes\n Muster diess von den „Entdeckungen“ der Vulgärökonomie! Sie ersetzt\n eine ökonomische Kategorie durch eine sykophantische Phrase. Voilà\n tout. „Wenn der Wilde“, docirt Senior, „Bogen fabricirt, so übt er\n eine Industrie aus, aber er prakticirt nicht die Absti-\n nenz.“ Diess erklärt uns, wie und warum in früheren Gesellschaftszu-\n ständen „ohne die Abstinenz“ des Kapitalisten Arbeitsmittel fabricirt\n wurden. „Je mehr die Gesellschaft fortschreitet, um so mehr Abstinenz\n erfordert sie“ [Note: Senior l. c. p. 342.] , nämlich von denen, welche die Industrie ausüben, sich\n die fremde Industrie und ihr Produkt anzueignen. Alle Bedingun-\n gen des Arbeitsprozesses verwandeln sich von nun in\n ebenso viele Abstinenzpraktiken des Kapitalisten.\n Dass Korn nicht nur gegessen, sondern auch ausgesät wird,\n Abstinenz des Kapitalisten!","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"19.58"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0059","text":"Dass der Wein die Zeit erhält auszu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"583\", \"facs\": \"#f0602\"} --> gähren, Abstinenz des Kapitalisten! [Note: „No one … will sow his wheat, f. i., and allow it to remain a twelve-\n month in the ground, or leave his wine in a cellar for years, instead of consuming\n these things or their equivalent at once — — unless he expects to acquire ad-\n ditional value etc.“ (Scrope: „Polit. Econ.“ edit. von A. Potter,\n New-York 1841, p. 133, 134.)]  Der Kapitalist beraubt seinen\n eignen Adam, wenn er die „Produktionsinstrumente dem Arbeiter\n leiht“ (!), alias sie durch Einverleibung der Arbeitskraft als Kapi-\n tal verwerthet, statt Dampfmaschinen, Baumwolle, Eisenbahnen, Dünger,\n Zugpferde u. s. f.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"19.59"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0060","text":"aufzuessen oder, wie der Vulgärökonom sich das\n kindlich vorstellt, „ihren Werth“ in Luxus- und andren Konsum-\n tionsmitteln zu verprassen [Note: „La privation que s’impose le capitaliste, en prêtant (dieser\n Euphemismus gebraucht, um nach probater vulgärökonomischer Manier den vom\n industriellen Kapitalisten exploitirten Lohnarbeiter mit dem industriellen\n Kapitalisten selbst zu identificiren, welcher vom Geld verleihenden Ka-\n pitalisten pumpt!) ses instruments de production au travailleur au lieu d’en\n consacrer la valeurà son propre usage, en la transformant en objets\n d’utilité ou d’agrément.“ (G. de Molinari l. c. p. 49.)] . Wie die Kapitalistenklasse das\n anstellen soll, ist ein von der Vulgärökonomie bisher hartnäckig bewahrtes\n Geheimniss. Genug, die Welt lebt nur noch von der Selbstkasteiung\n dieses modernen Büssers des Wischnu, des Kapitalisten.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"19.60"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0061","text":"Nicht nur die\n Accumulation, die einfache „Erhaltung eines Kapitals erheischt beständige\n Kraftanstrengung, um der Versuchung zu widerstehn, es aufzuessen“ [Note: „La conservation d’un capital exige … un effort constant pour résister\n à la temptation de le consommer.“ (Courceuil-Senelles l. c. p. 57.)] .\n Die einfache Humanität gebeut also offenbar den Kapitalisten von Martyr-\n thum und Versuchung zu erlösen, in derselben Weise wie der georgische\n Sklavenhalter jüngst durch Abschaffung der Sklaverei von dem schmerz-\n lichen Dilemma erlöst ward, ob das dem Negersklaven ausgepeitschte\n Mehrprodukt ganz in Champagner zu verjubeln oder auch theilweis in\n mehr Neger und mehr Land rückzuverwandeln.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"19.61"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0062","text":"In den verschiedensten ökonomischen Gesellschaftsformationen findet\n nicht nur einfache Reproduktion, sondern, obgleich auf verschiednem Mass-\n stab, Reproduktion auf erweiterter Stufenleiter statt. Es\n wird progressiv mehr producirt und mehr konsumirt, also auch mehr Pro-\n dukt in Produktionsmittel verwandelt.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"19.62"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0063","text":"Dieser Prozess erscheint aber\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"584\", \"facs\": \"#f0603\"} --> nicht als Accumulation von Kapital und daher auch nicht als\nFunktion des Kapitalisten, so lange dem Arbeiter seine Produk-\n tionsmittel, daher auch sein Produkt und seine Lebensmittel, noch nicht\nin der Form von Kapital gegenüberstehn [Note: „The particular classes of income which yield the most abundantly to the\n progress of national capital, change at different stages of their progress, and are\n therefore entirely different in nations occupying different positions in that progress\n … Profit … unimportant source of accumulation, compared with wages and\n rents, in the earlier stages of society … When a considerable advance in the\n powers of national industry has actually taken place, profits rise into comparative\n importance as a source of accumulation.“ (Richard Jones: „Textbook\n etc.“, p. 16, 21.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"19.63"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0064","text":"Der vor eini-\n gen Jahren verstorbene Richard Jones, Nachfolger von Malthus auf\n dem Lehrstuhl der politischen Oekonomie zu Herford, erörtert diess gut an\n zwei grossen Thatsachen. Da der zahlreichste Theil des indischen Volks\n selbstwirthschaftende Bauern, existirt ihr Produkt, ihre Arbeits- und\n Lebensmittel, auch nie „in der Form („in the shape“) eines Fonds, der\n aus fremder Revenue erspart wird („saved from revenue“) und\n daher einen vorläufigen Prozess der Accumulation („a previous\n process of accumulation“) durchlaufen hat“ [Note: l. c. p. 36 sq.] . Andrerseits werden die\n nicht-agrikolen Arbeiter in den Provinzen, wo die englische Herrschaft das alte\n System am wenigsten aufgelöst hat, direkt von den Grossen beschäftigt,\n denen eine Portion des ländlichen Mehrprodukts als Tribut oder Grund-\n rente zufliesst.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"19.64"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0065","text":"Ein Theil dieses Produkts wird in Naturalform von den\n Grossen verzehrt, ein andrer Theil für sie von den Arbeitern in Luxus-\n und sonstige Konsumtionsmittel verwandelt, während der Rest den Lohn\n der Arbeiter bildet, die Eigenthümer ihrer Arbeitsinstrumente sind. Pro-\n duktion und Reproduktion auf erweiterter Stufenleiter gehn hier ihren\n Gang ohne alle Dazwischenkunft jenes wunderlichen Heiligen, jenes Rit-\n ters von der traurigen Gestalt, des „entsagenden“ Kapitalisten.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"19.65"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0066","text":"Wir betrachteten bisher die Masse des Mehrwerths als ge-\n gebne Grösse. In diesem Fall bestimmte seine proportionelle\n Theilung in Revenue und Surpluskapital den Umfang der\n Accumulation. Aber letztere wechselt unabhängig von jener Theilung\n mit dem Wechsel in der Grösse des Mehrwerths selbst.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"585\", \"facs\": \"#f0604\"} --> Die Umstände, welche die Grösse des Mehrwerths regeln, sind in den Ka-\n piteln über seine Produktion ausführlich entwickelt worden. Sie reguliren,\n unter sonst gleichbleibenden Verhältnissen, die Bewegung der Accumula-\n tion. Wir kehren hier nur so weit zu ihnen zurück, als sie mit Bezug\n auf die Accumulation neue Gesichtspunkte bieten.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"19.66"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0067","text":"Man erinnert sich, welche Rolle der Exploitationsgrad der\n Arbeit in der Produktion des Mehrwerths spielt. Die politische Oeko-\n nomie würdigt diese Rolle so sehr, dass sie gelegentlich die Beschleunigung\n der Accumulation durch erhöhte Produktivkraft der Arbeit\n mit ihrer Beschleunigung durch erhöhte Exploitation des Arbei-\n ters identificirt [Note: „Ricardo sagt: ‚In verschiednen Stadien der Gesellschaft ist die Accu-\n mulation des Kapitals oder der Mittel Arbeit anzuwenden (sc. zu exploitiren) mehr\n oder weniger rasch und muss in allen Fällen von den Produktivkräften der Arbeit\n abhängen.","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"19.67"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0068","text":"Die Produktivkräfte der Arbeit sind im Allgemeinen am grössten, wo\n Ueberfluss von fruchtbarem Boden existirt.‘ Bedeuten in dieser Sentenz die\n Produktivkräfte der Arbeit die Kleinheit des aliquoten Theils\n jeden Produkts, der denen zufällt, deren Handarbeit es produ-\n cirt, so ist der Satz tautologisch, weil der übrig bleibende Theil der\n Fonds ist, woraus, wenn es seinem Eigner beliebt („if the owner pleases“),\n Kapital accumulirt werden kann. Aber diess ist meistens nicht der Fall, wo das\n Land am fruchtbarsten ist.“ („Observations on certain verbal dis-\n putes etc.“, p. 74, 75.)] . In den Abschnitten über die Produktion des Mehr-\n werths ward beständig unterstellt, dass der Arbeitslohn wenigstens gleich\n dem Werth der Arbeitskraft ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"19.68"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0069","text":"Es ward ferner gezeigt, dass der Ar-\n beitslohn, sei es seinem Werth nach, sei es nach der Masse von Lebensmitteln,\n die er repräsentirt, bei wachsendem Exploitationsgrad des Arbeiters\nwachsen kann. In der praktischen Bewegung des Kapitals jedoch wird\n auch Mehrwerth producirt durch gewaltsame Herabsetzung des Ar-\n beitslohns unter den Werth der Arbeitskraft. Faktisch wird\n so ein Theil des nothwendigen Konsumtionsfonds des\n Arbeiters in einen Accumulationsfonds von Kapital ver-\n wandelt.","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"19.69"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0070","text":"„Arbeitslöhne“, sagt J. St. Mill, „haben keine Produktivkraft; sie\n sind der Preis einer Produktivkraft. Arbeitslöhne tragen nicht, neben der\n Arbeit selbst, zur Waarenproduktion bei, sowenig als der Preis der Ma-\n schinerie neben der Maschinerie selbst. Könnte Arbeit ohne Kauf\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"586\", \"facs\": \"#f0605\"} --> gehabt werden, so wären Arbeitslöhne überflüssig“ [Note: J. St. Mill: „Essays on some unsettled Questions of Polit.\n Economy. Lond. 1844“, p. 90.] . Wenn\n aber die Arbeiter von der Luft leben könnten, so wären sie auch um\nkeinen Preis zu kaufen. Ihr Nichtskosten ist also eine Grenze\n im mathematischen Sinn, stets unerreichbar, obgleich stets annäher-\n bar. Es ist die beständige Tendenz des Kapitals sie auf diesen\nnihilistischen Standpunkt herabzudrücken. Ein oft von mir citir-\n ter Schriftsteller des 18.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"19.70"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0071","text":"Jahrhunderts, der Verfasser des „Essay\n on Trade and Commerce“, verräth nur das innerste Seelenge-\n heimniss des englischen Kapitals, wenn er es für die historische\n Lebensaufgabe Englands erklärt, den englischen Arbeitslohn auf\n das französische und holländische Niveau herabzudrücken [Note: „An Essay on Trade and Commerce. Lond. 1770“, p. 44.\n Aehnlich brachte die Times vom December 1866 und Januar 1867 Herzenser-\n giessungen englischer Minenbesitzer, worin der glückliche Zustand der belgi-\n schen Minenarbeiter geschildert ward, die nicht mehr verlangten und nicht mehr\n erhielten, als strikt nöthig, um für ihre „masters“ zu leben. Die belgischen Ar-\n beiter dulden viel, aber als Musterarbeiter in der Times zu figuriren! Anfang\n Februar 1867 antwortete der mit Pulver und Blei unterdrückte Strike der belgischen\n Minenarbeiter (bei Marchienne).] . Er sagt\n u. a.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"19.71"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0072","text":"naiv: „Wenn aber unsre Armen (Kunstausdruck für Arbeiter)\n luxuriös leben wollen … muss ihre Arbeit natürlich theuer sein.\n … Man betrachte nur die haarsträubende Masse von Ueber-\n flüssigkeiten („heap of superfluities“), die unsre Manufakturarbeiter\n verzehren, als da sind Branntwein, Gin, Thee, Zucker, fremde Früchte,\n starkes Bier, gedruckte Leinwand, Schnupf- und Rauchtabak u. s. w.“ [Note: l. c. p. 46.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"19.72"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0073","text":"Er citirt die Schrift eines Fabrikanten von Northamptonshire, der mit him-\n melwärts schielendem Blick jammert: „Arbeitist ein ganzes Drit-\n theil wohlfeiler in Frankreich als in England: denn die franzö-\n sischen Armen arbeiten hart und fahren hart an Nahrung und Kleidung\n und ihr Hauptkonsum sind Brod, Früchte, Kräuter, Wurzeln und getrock-\n neter Fisch; denn sie essen sehr selten Fleisch, und wenn der Weizen\n theuer ist, sehr wenig Brod“ [Note: Der Fabrikant von Northamptonshire begeht eine im Herzensdrang ent-\n schuldbare pia fraus. Er vergleicht angeblich das Leben englischer und französi-] . „Wozu,“ fährt der Essay man fort,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"587\", \"facs\": \"#f0606\"} --> „wozu noch kömmt, dass ihr Getränk aus Wasser besteht oder ähn-\n lichen schwachen Likören, so dass sie in der That erstaunlich\n wenig Geld ausgeben. . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"19.73"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0074","text":"Ein derartiger Zustand der Dinge ist sicher-\n lich schwer herbeizuführen, aber er ist nicht unerreichbar, wie seine\n Existenz sowohl in Frankreich als Holland schlagend beweist“ [Note: l. c. p. 70.] . Zwei\n Jahrzehnte später verfolgte ein amerikanischer Humbug, der baronisirte\n Yankee Benjamin Thomson (alias Graf Rumford), dieselbe Philan-\n thropielinie mit grossem Wohlgefallen vor Gott und den Menschen. Seine\n „Essays“ sind ein Kochbuch mit Recepten aller Art um Surrogate an\n die Stelle der theuren Normalspeisen des Arbeiters zu setzen. Ein be-\n sonders gelungenes Recept dieses wunderlichen „Philosophen“ ist folgen-\n des: „Fünf Pfund Gerste, fünf Pfund Mais, für 3 d. Häringe, 1 d. Salz,\n 1 d. Essig, 2 d. Pfeffer und Kräuter — Summa von 20¾ d. giebt eine\n Suppe für 64 Menschen, ja mit den Durchschnittspreisen von Korn kann\n die Kost auf ¼ d.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"19.74"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0075","text":"per Kopf (noch nicht 3 Pfennige) herabgedrückt\n werden“ [Note: Benjamin Thomson: „Essays, political, economical,\n and philosophical etc. 3 vol. Lond. 1796—1802.“ In seinem „The\n State of the Poor, or an History of the Labouring Classes in\n England etc.“ empfiehlt Sir M. F. Eden die Rumford’sche Bettelsuppe bestens\n den Vorstehern von Workhouses und mahnt die englischen Arbeiter vorwurfsvoll,\n dass „es bei den Schotten viele Familien giebt, die, statt von Weizen, Roggen\n und Fleisch, Monate lang von Hafergrütze und Gerstenmehl, nur mit Salz und\n Wasser gemischt, leben und das obendrein noch sehr komfortabel“ („and that\n very comfortably too“). (l. c. v. I, b. II, ch. II.) Aehnliche „Fingerzeige“ im\n 19. Jahrhundert. „Die englischen Ackerbauarbeiter“, heisst es z. B., „wollen\n keine Mischungen niederer Kornarten essen.","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"19.75"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0076","text":"In Schottland, wo die Erziehung\n besser ist, ist diess Vorurtheil wahrscheinlich unbekannt.“ („Charles H.\n Parry M. D.: „The Question of the Necessity of the existing\n Cornlaws considered. Lond. 1816“, p. 69.) Derselbe Parry klagt\n jedoch, dass der englische Arbeiter jetzt (1815) sehr heruntergekommen sei, ver-\n glichen mit Eden’s Zeit (1797).] . Mit dem Fortschritt der kapitalistischen Produktion hat\n die Waarenfälschung Thomson’s Ideale überflüssig gemacht [Note: Aus den Berichten der letzten parlamentarischen Untersuchungskommission\n über Fälschung von Lebensmitteln sieht man, dass selbst die Fälschung der Arz-] .\n [Note: scher Manufakturarbeiter, schildert aber, wie er später in seiner Verdadde-\n rung selbst gesteht, mit den eben citirten Worten französische Agrikultur-\n arbeiter!] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"588\", \"facs\": \"#f0607\"} --> Ende des 18.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"19.76"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0077","text":"und während der ersten Decennien des 19. Jahrhunderts\n erzwangen die englischen Pächter und Landlords das absolute Minimal-\n salair, indem sie den Ackerbautaglöhnern weniger als das Minimum in der\n Form des Arbeitslohns, den Rest aber in der Form von Pfarreiunter-\n stützung auszahlten. Ein Beispiel der Possenreisserei, womit die eng-\n lischen Dogberries in ihrer „legalen“ Festsetzung des Lohntarifs ver-\n fuhren: „Als die Squires die Arbeitslöhne für Speenhamland 1795 fest-\n setzten, hatten sie zu Mittag gespeist, dachten aber offenbar, dass die\n Arbeiter nicht desgleichen nöthig hätten. … Sie entschieden, der Wochen-\n lohn solle 3 sh. per Mann sein, wenn das Laib Brod von 8 Pfund 11\n Unzen auf 1 sh. stünde, und er solle regelmässig wachsen, bis das Laib\n 1 sh. 5 d. koste. Sobald es über diesen Preis stiege, sollte der Lohn pro-\n portionell abnehmen, bis der Preis des Laibes 2 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"19.77"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0078","text":"erreicht hätte: und\n dann sollte die Nahrung des Mannes ⅕ weniger als vorher sein“ [Note: G. B. Newnham (barrister at law): „A Review of the Evidence\n before the Committees of the two Houses of Parliament on the\n Cornlaws. Lond. 1815“, p. 20 Note.] . Vor\n dem Untersuchungscomité des House of Lords, 1814, wird ein ge-\n wisser A. Bennett, grosser Pächter, Magistrat, Armenhausverwalter und\n Lohnregulator, gefragt: „Wird irgend eine Proportion zwischen dem Werth\n der Tagesarbeit und der Pfarreiunterstützung der Arbeiter beobachtet?“\nAntwort: „Ja. Das wöchentliche Einkommen jeder Familie wird über\n ihren Nominallohn hinaus vollgemacht bis zum Gallonlaib Brod (8 Pfd.\n 11 Unzen) und 3 d. per Kopf. . . . Wir unterstellen das Gallonlaib hin-\n reichend für die Erhaltung jeder Person in der Familie während der\n Woche; und die 3 d.","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"19.78"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0079","text":"sind für Kleider; und wenn es der Pfairei beliebt,\n die Kleider selbst zu stellen, werden die 3 d. abgezogen. Diese Praxis\n herrscht nicht nur im ganzen Westen von Wiltshire, sondern, wie ich\n glaube, im ganzen Land“ [Note: l. c.] . So, ruft ein Burgeoisschriftsteller jener\n Zeit, „haben die Pächter Jahre lang eine respektable Klasse ihrer Lands-\n leute degradirt, indem sie dieselben zwangen zum Workhouse ihre Zu-\n [Note: neistoffe in England nicht Ausnahme, sondern Regel bildet. Z. B. die Examination\n von 34 Proben von Opium, gekauft in eben so viel verschiednen Londoner Apo-\n theken, ergab, dass 31 verfälscht waren mit Mohnkapsel, Weizenmehl, Gummi-\n schleim, Thon, Sand u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"19.79"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0080","text":"Viele enthielten kein Atom Morphin.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"589\", \"facs\": \"#f0608\"} --> flucht zu nehmen … Der Pächter hat seine eignen Gewinne ver-\n mehrt, indem er selbst die Accumulation des unentbehr-\n lichsten Konsumtionsfonds auf Seite der Arbeiter ver-\n hinderte“ [Note: Ch. H. Parry l. c. p. 78. Die Herrn Landlords ihrerseits „indemni-\n ficirten“ sich nicht nur für den Antijakobinerkrieg, den sie im Namen Englands\n führten, sondern bereicherten sich enorm. „Ihre Renten verdoppelten, verdrei-\n fachten, vervierfachten und, in Ausnahmsfällen, versechsfachten sich in 18\n Jahren.“ (l. c. p. 100, 101.)] . Welche Rolle heutzutag der direkte Raub am noth-\n wendigen Konsumtionsfonds des Arbeiters in der Bildung des Mehrwerths\n und daher des Accumulationsfonds des Kapitals spielt, hat\n beispielweis die s. g. Hausarbeit gezeigt. Weitere Thatsachen im Ver-\n lauf dieses Kapitels.","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"19.80"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0081","text":"Die Elasticität der Arbeitskraft oder ihre Fähigkeit grösserer inten-\n siver oder extensiver Spannung bildet, innerhalb gewisser Grenzen, eine\n vom gegebnen Umfang der bereits funktionirenden und producirten Produk-\n tionsmittel, oder der stofflichen Elemente des constanten\n Kapitals, unabhängige Quelle der Schöpfung zusätzlichen Reichthums\n und daher des Accumulationsfonds. In der extraktiven Industrie,\n der Minenindustrie z. B., ist der Arbeitsgegenstand von Natur vorhan-\n den. Die nothwendigen Arbeitsmittel selbst also gegeben, — und die ex-\n traktive Industrie liefert grossentheils selbst wieder das Rohmaterial dieser\n Arbeitsinstrumente, Metalle, Holz u. s. w., und die Hilfsmittel, wie Kohle,\n — ist das Produkt keineswegs durch den Umfang dieser\n Arbeitsmittel beschränkt.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"19.81"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0082","text":"Sie werden durch die grössere Ver-\n ausgabung der Arbeitskraft nur rascher vernutzt, also nur ihre Reproduk-\n tionsperiode abgekürzt. Die Produktenmasse selbst, wie Kohle,\n Eisen, wächst dagegen, unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen, im Ver-\n hältniss zu der auf den Naturgegenstand verausgabten Arbeit. Wie am\n ersten Tag der Produktion gehn hier die ursprünglichen Produktbildner,\n daher auch die Bildner der stofflichen Elemente des Kapitals, Mensch und\n Natur, zusammen. In der eigentlichen Agrikultur spielen zwar Samen\n und Dünger dieselbe Rolle, wie das Rohmaterial in der Manufaktur, und\n man kann nicht mehr Land besäen, ohne vorher mehr Samen zu haben.","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"19.82"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0083","text":"Aber diess Rohmaterial und die Arbeitsinstrumente gegeben, ist bekannt,\n welche wunderthätige Wirkung selbst die rein mechanische Bearbeitung\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"590\", \"facs\": \"#f0609\"} --> des Bodens, deren Intensivität von der Spannung der Arbeitskraft abhängt,\n auf die Massenhaftigkeit des Produkts ausübt. Es ist wieder direkte Wir-\n kung des Menschen auf den Naturgegenstand, welche zur unmittelbaren Quelle\n des Reichthums wird. Extraktive Industrie und Agrikultur liefern andrer-\n seits der Manufaktur das Rohmaterial und die Hilfsstoffe, also die stofflichen\n Elemente, welche hier jeder grösseren Arbeitsausgabe vorausgesetzt sind,\n während die eigentlichen Arbeitsmittel auch in dieser Sphäre durch exten-\n sivere oder intensivere Spannung der Arbeitskraft nur ihre Reproduktions-\n periode verkürzen.","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"19.83"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0084","text":"Indem das Kapital sich also die beiden Urbildner des\n Reichthums, Arbeitskraft und Erde, einverleibt, erwirbt es in ihnen von\n seinem eignen stofflichen Umfang unabhängige und\n dehnbare Faktoren der Reproduktion auf erweiterter Stufenleiter\n und daher der Accumulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"19.84"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0085","text":"Abgesehn vom Exploitationsgrad der Arbeit wird die Pro-\n duktion des Mehrwerths, also die Accumulation des Kapitals,\n deren Bildungselement der Mehrwerth, wesentlich bestimmt durch die\nProduktivkraft der Arbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"19.85"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0086","text":"Mit der Produktivkraft der Arbeit wächst die Produktenmasse,\n worin sich ein bestimmter Werth, also auch Mehrwerth von gegebner\n Grösse darstellt. Bei gleichbleibender und selbst bei fallender Rate des\n Mehrwerths, sofern sie nur langsamer fällt als die Produktivkraft der Arbeit\n steigt, wächst die Masse des Mehrprodukts. Bei gleichbleibender Theilung\n desselben in Revenue und Surpluskapital kann daher die Konsumtion des\n Kapitalisten wachsen ohne Abnahme des Accumulationsfonds. Die propor-\n tionelle Grösse des Accumulationsfonds kann selbst auf Kosten des Kon-\n sumtionsfonds wachsen, während die Verwohlfeilerung der Waaren dem\n Kapitalisten eben so viele oder mehr Genussmittel als vorher zur Verfügung\n stellt.","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"19.86"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0087","text":"Aber mit der wachsenden Produktivität der Arbeit geht, wie man\n gesehn, die Verwohlfeilerung des Arbeiters, also wachsende Rate des Mehr-\n werths, Hand in Hand, selbst wenn der reelle Arbeitslohn steigt. Er\n steigt nie verhältnissmässig mit der Produktivität der Arbeit. Derselbe\nvariable Kapitalwerth setzt also mehr Arbeitskraft und daher mehr\n Arbeit in Bewegung. Derselbe constante Kapitalwerth stellt sich\n in mehr Produktionsmitteln, d. h. mehr Arbeitsmitteln, Arbeitsmaterial und\n Hilfsstoffen dar, liefert also sowohl mehr Produktbildner als Werthbildner,\n oder Arbeitseinsauger. Bei gleichbleibendem und selbst abnehmendem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"591\", \"facs\": \"#f0610\"} -->Werth des Surpluskapitals findet daher beschleunigte Accumulation statt.","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"19.87"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0088","text":"Nicht nur erweitert sich die Stufenleiter der Reproduktion stofflich, son-\n dern die Produktion des Mehrwerths wächst schneller als der Werth des\n Surpluskapitals.","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"19.88"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0089","text":"Die Entwicklung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit reagirt auch auf\n das Originalkapital oder das bereits im Produktionspro-\n zess befindliche Kapital. Ein Theil des funktionirenden constan-\n ten Kapitals besteht aus Arbeitsmitteln, wie Maschinerie u. s. w., die nur\n in längeren Perioden konsumirt und daher reproducirt oder durch neue\n Exemplare derselben Art ersetzt werden. Aber ein Theil dieser Arbeits-\n mittel stirbt jedes Jahr ab, oder erreicht das Endziel seiner produktiven Funk-\n tion. Er befindet sich daher jedes Jahr im Stadium seiner periodischen Repro-\n duktion oder seines Ersatzes durch neue Exemplare derselben Art.","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"19.89"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0090","text":"Hat die\n Produktivkraft der Arbeit sich in der Geburtsstätte dieser Arbeitsmittel\n erweitert, und sie entwickelt sich fortwährend mit dem ununterbrochenen\n Fluss der Wissenschaft und der Technologie, so tritt wirkungsvollere und,\n ihren Leistungsumfang betrachtet, wohlfeilere Maschine, Werkzeug, Ap-\n parat u. s. w. an die Stelle der alten. Das alte Kapital wird in\n einer produktiveren Form reproducirt, abgesehn von der fort-\n währenden Detailveränderung an den vorhandnen Arbeitsmitteln. Der\n andere Theil des constanten Kapitals, Rohmaterial und Hilfsstoffe, wird\n fortwährend innerhalb des Jahrs, der der Agrikultur entstammende meist\n jährlich reproducirt. Jede Einführung bessrer Methoden u. s. w. wirkt\n hier also fast gleichzeitig auf Zuschusskapital und bereits in Funktion\n begriffenes Kapital.","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"19.90"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0091","text":"Jeder Fortschritt der Chemie vermannigfacht nicht nur\n die Nutzanwendungen desselben Materials und dehnt daher mit dem\n Wachsthum des Kapitals seine Anlagesphären aus. Er lehrt zugleich die\nExcremente des Produktions- und Konsumtionsprozesses in den Kreislauf\n des Reproduktionsprozesses zurückschleudern, schafft also ohne vorherige\n Kapitalauslage neuen Kapitalstoff. Gleich vermehrter Ausbeutung des\nNaturreichthums durch bloss höhere Spannung der Arbeitskraft, bildet\ndie Wissenschaft eine von der gegebnen Grösse des funktionirenden\n Kapitals unabhängige Potenz seiner Expansion. Sie reagirt zugleich\n auf den in sein Erneurungsstadium eingetretenen Theil des Originalkapitals.\n In seine neue Form einverleibt es gratis den hinter dem Rücken seiner\n alten Form vollzogenen gesellschaftlichen Fortschritt.","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"19.91"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0092","text":"Allerdings ist diese\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"592\", \"facs\": \"#f0611\"} --> Entwicklung der Produktivkraft zugleich begleitet von theilweiser Depre-\n ciation funktionirender Kapitale. Soweit diese Depreciation sich durch\n die Konkurrenz akut fühlbar macht, fällt die Hauptwucht auf den Arbeiter,\n in dessen gesteigerter Exploitation der Kapitalist Schadenersatz sucht.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"19.92"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0093","text":"Es zeigte sich bei Analyse des relativen Mehrwerths, wie die Ent-\n wicklung der gesellschaftlichen Produktivkraft der Arbeit eine stets wach-\n sende Masse des von derselben Arbeitskraft in Bewegung gesetzten con-\n stanten Kapitals bedingt. Mit dem Reichthum oder der Fülle und Wirk-\n samkeit der in Maschinerie u. s. w. vergegenständlichten Arbeit, wovon der\n Arbeiter als bereits producirter Bedingung des Produktionsprozesses aus-\n geht, wächst die Masse des alten Kapitalwerths, der durch\n blossen Zusatz neuer Arbeit, also neue Werthproduktion, erhalten und\n in diesem Sinn reproducirt wird. Man vergleiche z. B. einen eng-\n lischen Spinner mit einem indischen. Man unterstelle der Vereinfachung\n halber gleiche Extension und Intensivität des englischen und indischen Ar-\n beitstags.","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"19.93"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0094","text":"Der englische Spinner verwandelt in einem Tag viel hundert-\n mal grössere Massen von Baumwolle, Spinninstrumenten u. s. w. in Garn.\n Er erhält also auch vielhundertmal grösseren Kapitalwerth in seinem Pro-\n dukt. Wäre selbst das Werthprodukt seiner Tagesarbeit, d. h. der\n durch dieselbe den Produktionsmitteln neu zugesetzte Werth, nur gleich\n dem des Indiers, dennoch resultirt seine Tagesarbeit nicht nur in grösserem\nProduktenquantum, sondern in unendlich grösserem Produkten-\n werth, altem Werth, den er auf das neue Produkt überträgt, und der\n von neuem als Kapital funktioniren kann. „1782“, belehrt uns F. Engels,\n „lag die ganze Wollerndte der vorhergehenden drei Jahre (in England) aus\n Mangel an Arbeitern noch unverarbeitet da, und hätte liegen bleiben müssen,\n wenn nicht die neu erfundne Maschinerie zu Hilfe gekommen wäre und sie ver-\n sponnen hätte“ [Note: F.","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"19.94"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0095","text":"Engels: „Lage der arbeitenden Klasse in England“,\n p. 20.] . Die in der Form von Maschinerie vergegenständlichte\n Arbeit stampfte natürlich unmittelbar keinen Menschen aus dem Boden, aber\n sie erlaubte einer geringen Arbeiteranzahl durch Zusatz von relativ wenig\n lebendiger Arbeit nicht nur die Wolle produktiv zu konsumiren, und ihr\n Neuwerth zuzusetzen, sondern in der Form von Garn u. s. w. ihren\nalten Werth zu erhalten. Sie lieferte damit zugleich Mittel und Sporn\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"593\", \"facs\": \"#f0612\"} --> zur erweiterten Reproduktion von Wolle. Es ist die Naturgabe der leben-\n digen Arbeit alten Werth zu erhalten, während sie Neuwerth schafft.","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"19.95"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0096","text":"Mit dem Wachsthum von Wirksamkeit, Umfang und Werth ihrer Produk-\n tionsmittel, also mit der die Entwicklung ihrer Produktivkraft begleitenden\nAccumulation erhält und verewigt die Arbeit daher in stets neuer\n Form einen stets schwellenden Kapitalwerth [Note: Die klassische Oekonomie hat wegen mangelhafter Analyse des Arbeits-\n und Verwerthungsprozesses diess wichtige Moment der Reproduktion nie ordentlich\n begriffen, wie man z. B. bei Ricardo sehn kann. Er sagt z. B.: Welches immer der\n Wechsel der Produktivkraft, „Eine Million Menschen producirt in den Fabri-\n ken stets denselben Werth.“ Diess richtig, wenn Extension und Intensivgrad ihrer\n Arbeit gegeben.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"19.96"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0097","text":"Es verhindert aber nicht, und Ricardo übersieht diess in gewissen\n Schlussfolgerungen, dass eine Million Menschen sehr verschiedne Massen von Pro-\n duktionsmitteln, bei verschiedner Produktivkraft ihrer Arbeit, in Produkt verwan-\n delt, daher sehr verschiedne Werthmassen in ihrem Produkt erhält, die von ihr\n gelieferten Produktenwerthe also sehr verschieden sind. Ricardo hat, neben-\n bei bemerkt, an jenem Beispiel umsonst versucht, dem J. B. Say den Unterschied\n zwischen Gebrauchswerth (den er hier wealth nennt, stofflichen Reichthum) und\n Tauschwerth klar zu machen. Say antwortet: „Quant à la difficulté qu’élève\n Mr.","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"19.97"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0098","text":"Ricardo en disant que, par des procédés mieux extendus, un million de per-\n sonnes peuvent produire deux fois, trois fois autant de richesses, sans produire\n plus de valeurs, cette difficulté n’est pas une lorsque l’on considère, ainsi\n qu’on le doit, la production comme un échange dans lesquels on donne\n les services productifs de son travail, de sa terre, et de ses capitaux, pour obtenir\n des produits. C’est par le moyen de ses services productifs que nous acquérons\n tous les produits qui sont au monde. Or … nous sommes d’autant plus riches,\n nos services productifs ont d’autant plus de valeur, qu’ils obtiennent dans\nl’échange appelé production, une plus grande quantité de choses\n utiles.“ (J. B. Say: „Lettresà M. Malthus. Paris 1820“, p. 168, 169.)","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"19.98"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0099","text":"Die „difficulté“ — sie existirt für ihn, nicht für Ricardo — die Say erklären soll,\n ist die: Warum vermehrt sich nicht der Werth der Gebrauchswerthe, wenn ihre\n Quantität in Folge gesteigerter Produktivkraft der Arbeit wächst? Antwort:\n Die Schwierigkeit wird dadurch gelöst, dass man den Gebrauchswerth ge-\n fälligst Tauschwerth nennt. Tauschwerth ist ein Ding, das one way or\n another mit Austausch zusammenhängt. Man nenne also die Produktion\n einen „Austausch“ von Arbeit und Produktionsmitteln gegen das Produkt,\n und es ist klar wie Wasser, dass man um so mehr Tauschwerth erhält, je mehr\n Gebrauchswerth einem die Produktion liefert. In andern Worten: Je mehr Ge-\n brauchswerthe, z. B. Strümpfe, ein Arbeitstag dem Strumpffabrikanten liefert, desto\nreicher ist er an Strümpfen. Plötzlich fällt Say jedoch ein, dass „mit der] . Diese Naturkraft der\n [Running matter: I.","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"19.99"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0100","text":"38] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"594\", \"facs\": \"#f0613\"} --> Arbeit erscheint als Selbsterhaltungskraft des Kapitals, dem sie\n einverleibt ist, ganz wie ihre gesellschaftlichen Produktivkräfte als\nseine Eigenschaften und wie die beständige Aneignung der Mehrarbeit\n durch den Kapitalisten als beständige Selbstverwerthung des Ka-\n pitals. Alle Kräfte der Arbeit projektiren sich als Kräfte des Kapitals,\n wie alle Werthformen der Waare als Formen des Geldes.","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"19.100"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0101","text":"Unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen ist die Grösse des produ-\n cirten Mehrwerths und daher die Accumulation endlich bestimmt durch die\n Grösse des vorgeschossenen Kapitals. Mit dem Gesammtka-\n pital wächst auch sein variabler Bestandtheil, wenn auch nicht in demsel-\n ben Verhältniss. Auf je grösserer Stufenleiter der individuelle Kapitalist\n producirt, desto grösser die Arbeiteranzahl, die er gleichzeitig exploitirt,\n oder die Masse der unbezahlten Arbeit, die er aneignet [Note: Man wird im Dritten Buch sehn, dass die durchschnittliche Pro-\n fitrate verschiedner Produktionssphären nicht afficirt wird von der jeder derselben] .","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"19.101"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0102","text":"Je mehr\n [Note: grössern Quantität“ der Strümpfe ihr „Preis“ (der natürlich nichts mit dem Tausch-\n werth zu thun hat) fällt, „parce que la concurrence les (les producteurs)\noblige à donner les produits pour ce qu’ils leur coûtent.“ Aber wo\n denn kommt der Profit her, wenn der Kapitalist die Waaren zu dem Preis ver-\n kauft, den sie ihm kosten? Doch never mind. Say erklärt, dass in Folge der gesteiger-\n ten Produktivität jeder im Ersatz für dasselbe Aequivalent jetzt zwei statt früher ein\n paar Strümpfe u. s. w. erhält. Das Resultat, wobei er anlangt, ist grade der Satz Ricar-\n do’s, den er widerlegen wollte.","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"19.102"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0103","text":"Nach dieser gewaltigen Denkanstrengung apostrophirt\n er Malthus triumphirend mit den Worten: „Telle est, monsieur, la doctrine\n bien liée sans laquelle il est impossible, je le déclare, d’expliquer les plus\n grandes difficultés de l’économie politique et notamment, comment il se peut\n qu’une nation soit plus riche lorsque ses produits diminuent de valeur, quoique\n la richesse soit de la valeur.“ (l. c. p. 170.) Ein englischer Oekonom bemerkt\n über ähnliche Kunststücke in Say’s „Lettres“: „Diese affektirten Manieren zu\n schwatzen („those affected ways of talking“) bilden im Ganzen das, was Herr Say\nseine Doktrin zu nennen beliebt und die er dem Malthus ans Herz legt zu\n Hertford zu lehren, wie das schon ‚dans plusieurs parties de l’Europe‘ geschehe.","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"19.103"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0104","text":"Er sagt: „Si vous trouvez une physionomie de paradoxe à toutes ces propo-\n sitions, voyez les choses qu’elles expriment, et j’ose croire qu’elles vous paraî-\n tront fort simples et fort raisonnables.“ Zweifelsohne, und zugleich werden sie in\n Folge desselben Prozesses alles andre, nur nicht original oder wichtig erscheinen.“\n („An Inquiry into those Principles respecting the Nature of\n Demand etc.“, p. 116, 110.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"595\", \"facs\": \"#f0614\"} --> also das individuelle Kapital wächst, desto grösser der Fonds, der\n sich in Konsumtionsfonds und Accumulationsfonds spaltet. Der Kapita-\n list kann daher flotter leben und zugleich mehr „entsagen“.","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"19.104"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0105","text":"Mit dem Wachsthum des Kapitals wächst die Differenz zwi-\n schen angewandtem und konsumirtem Kapital. In andern\n Worten: Es wächst die Werth- und Stoffmasse der Arbeitsmittel, wie\n Baulichkeiten, Maschinerie, Drainirungsröhren, Arbeitsvieh, Apparate\n jeder Art, die stets ihrem ganzen Umfang nach, während längerer oder\n kürzerer Periode, in beständig wiederholten Produktionsprozessen, funktio-\n niren, oder zur Erzielung bestimmter Nutzeffekte dienen, während sie nur\n allmälig verschleissen, daher ihren Werth nur stückweis verlieren,\n also auch nur stückweis auf das Produkt übertragen. Im Verhältniss,\n worin diese Arbeitsmittel als Produktbildner dienen, ohne dem Produkt\n Werth zuzusetzen, also ganz angewandt, aber nur theilweis konsumirt wer-\n den, leisten sie, wie früher erwähnt, denselben Gratisdienst wie Natur-\n kräfte, Wasser, Dampf, Luft, Elektricität u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"19.105"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0106","text":"Dieser Gratisdienst\n der vergangnen Arbeit, wenn ergriffen und beseelt von der lebendigen Ar-\n beit, accumulirt mit der wachsenden Stufenleiter der Accumulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"19.106"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0107","text":"Da die vergangne Arbeit sich stets in Kapital verkleidet, d. h. das\n Passivum der Arbeit von A, B, C u. s. w. im Aktivum des Nichtarbeiters X\n figurirt, sind Bürger und politische Oekonomen voll des Lobes für die Ver-\n dienste der vergangnen Arbeit, welche nach dem schottischen Genie\n Mac Culloch sogar einen eignen Sold beziehn muss [Note: Mac Culloch löste das Patent auf „wages of past labour“, lange\n bevor Senior das Patent auf die „wages of abstinence“.] . Das stets wach-\n sende Gewicht der unter der Form von Produktionsmitteln im lebendigen\n Arbeitsprozess mitwirkenden vergangnen Arbeit wird also ihrer dem Ar-\n beiter selbst, dessen vergangne und unbezahlte Arbeit sie ist, entfrem-\n deten Gestalt, ihrer Kapitalgestalt vindicirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"19.107"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0108","text":"Die praktischen\n Agenten der kapitalistischen Produktion und ihre ideologischen Zungen-\n drescher sind ebenso unfähig das Produktionsmittel von seiner an-\n tagonistischen gesellschaftlichen Charaktermaske, die ihm heutzutag an-\n [Note: eigenthümlichen Theilung des Kapitals in constantes und variables Element, und\n zugleich dass diess Phänomen nur scheinbar den über die Natur und Produktion\n des Mehrwerths entwickelten Gesetzen widerspricht.] \n [Running matter: 38*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"596\", \"facs\": \"#f0615\"} --> klebt, getrennt zu denken, als ein Sklavenhalter den Arbeiter selbst\n von seinem Charakter als Sklave.","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"19.108"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0109","text":"Man hat im Verlauf dieses Abschnitts gesehn, dass das Kapital keine\n fixe Grösse ist, sondern ein elastischer und mit der Theilung des Mehr-\n werths in Revenue und Surpluskapital beständig fluktuirender Theil des\n gesellschaftlichen Reichthums. Man hat ferner gesehn, dass selbst die\n Grösse des funktionirenden Kapitals gegeben, die ihm einverleibte Arbeits-\n kraft, Wissenschaft und Erde (worunter ökonomisch alle ohne Zuthat\n des Menschen von Natur vorhandnen Arbeitsgegenstände zu verstehn\n sind) elastische Potenzen desselben bilden, die ihm innerhalb gewisser Gren-\n zen einen von seiner eignen Grösse unabhängigen Spielraum\n gestatten. Es wurde dabei von allen Verhältnissen des Cirkulationspro-\n zesses abgesehn, die sehr verschiedne Wirkungsgrade derselben Kapital-\n masse verursachen.","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"19.109"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0110","text":"Es wurde, da wir die Schranken der kapitalistischen Pro-\n duktion voraussetzen, also eine rein naturwüchsige Gestalt des gesellschaft-\n lichen Produktionsprozesses, abgesehn von jeder mit den vorhandnen Pro-\n duktionsmitteln und Arbeitskräften unmittelbar und planmässig bewirk-\n baren rationelleren Kombination. Die klassische Oekonomie liebte es von\n jeher das gesellschaftliche Kapital als eine fixe Grösse von fixem Wir-\n kungsgrad aufzufassen. Aber das Vorurtheil ward erst zum Dogma be-\n festigt durch den Urphilister Jeremias Bentham, diess nüchtern pe-\n dantische, schwatzlederne Orakel des gemeinen Bürgerverstandes des\n 19. Jahrhunderts [Note: Vgl. u. a.: J. Bentham: „Theorie des Peines et des Ré-\n compenses, trad. Et. Dumont. 3ème éd.“ Paris 1826, t. II, l. IV,\n ch. II.] . Bentham ist unter den Philosophen, was Martin\n Tupper unter den Dichtern.","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"19.110"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0111","text":"Beide waren nur in England fabricirbar [Note: Jeremias Bentham ist ein rein englisches Phänomen. Selbst unsern\n Philosophen Christian Wolf nicht ausgenommen, hat zu keiner Zeit und in\n keinem Land der hausbackenste Gemeinplatz sich jemals so selbstgefällig breit\n gemacht. Das Nützlichkeitsprincip war keine Erfindung Bentham’s. Er\n reproducirte nur geistlos, was Helvetius und andre Franzosen des 18. Jahrhunderts\n geistreich gesagt hatten. Wenn man z. B. wissen will, was ist einem Hunde nütz-\n lich?, so muss man die Hundenatur ergründen. Diese Natur selbst ist nicht aus\n dem „Nützlichkeitsprincip“ zu konstruiren. Auf den Menschen angewandt, wenn\n man alle menschliche That, Bewegung, Verhältnisse u. s. w. nach dem Nützlich-] .\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"597\", \"facs\": \"#f0616\"} --> Mit seinem Dogma werden die gewöhnlichsten Erscheinungen des Produk-\n tionsprozesses, wie z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":111,"address":"19.111"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0112","text":"dessen plötzliche Expansionen und Kontrak-\n tionen, ja die Accumulation, völlig unbegreifbar [Note: „Politische Oekonomen sind zu geneigt, eine bestimmte Quantität von\n Kapital und eine bestimmte Anzahl Arbeiter als Produktionsinstrumente von gleich-\n förmiger Kraft und als mit einer gewissen gleichförmigen Intensivität wirkend zu\n behandeln . . . . Diejenigen, die behaupten, dass Waaren die einzigen Agenten\n der Produktion sind, beweisen, dass die Produktion überhaupt nicht erweitert\n werden kann, denn zu einer solchen Erweiterung müssten Lebensmittel, Rohmate-\n rialien und Werkzeuge vorher vermehrt werden, was in der That darauf hinaus\n kömmt, dass kein Wachsthum der Produktion ohne ihr vorheriges Wachsthum\n stattfinden kann, oder, in andern Worten, dass jedes Wachsthum unmöglich ist.“\n (S. Bailey: „Money and its Vicissitudes“, p. 26 u. 70.)","kind":"passage","sequence":112,"address":"19.112"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0113","text":"Bailey\n kritisirt das Dogma hauptsächlich vom Standpunkt des Cirkulationspro-\n zesses.] . Das Dogma wurde\n sowohl von Bentham selbst als von Malthus, James Mill, MacCulloch u. s. w.\n zu apologetischen Zwecken vernutzt, namentlich um einen Theil des\n Kapitals, das variable oder in Arbeitskraft umsetzbare\n Kapital als eine fixe Grösse darzustellen. Die stoffliche Existenz\n des variablen Kapitals, d. h. die Masse der Lebensmittel, die es für den\n Arbeiter repräsentirt, oder der s. g. Arbeitsfonds, wurde in einen\n durch Naturketten abgeringten und unüberschreitbaren Sondertheil des\n gesellschaftlichen Reichthums verfabelt. Um den Theil des gesellschaft-\n lichen Reichthums, der als constantes Kapital oder, stofflich ausge-\n drückt, als Produktionsmittel funktioniren soll, in Bewegung zu setzen, ist\n eine bestimmte Masse lebendiger Arbeit erheischt.","kind":"passage","sequence":113,"address":"19.113"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0114","text":"Diese ist\n [Note: keitsprincip beurtheilen will, handelt es sich erst um die menschliche Natur im\n Allgemeinen und dann um die in jeder Epoche historisch modificirte Menschen-\n natur. Bentham macht kein Federlesens. Mit der naivsten Trockenheit unter-\n stellt er den modernen Spiessbürger, speciell den englischen Spiessbürger,\n als den Normalmenschen. Was diesem Kauz von Normalmensch und seiner\n Welt nützlich, ist an und für sich nützlich. An diesem Massstab beurtheilt er dann\n Vergangenheit, Gegenwart und Zukunft. Z. B. die christliche Religion ist „nütz-\n lich“, weil sie dieselben Missethaten religiös verpönt, die der Strafcodex juristisch\n verdammt. Kunstkritik ist „schädlich“, weil sie ehrbare Leute in ihrem Genuss\n an Martin Tupper stört u. s. w. Mit solchem Schund hat der brave Mann, dessen\n Devise: „nulla dies sine linea“, Berge von Büchern gefüllt.","kind":"passage","sequence":114,"address":"19.114"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0115","text":"Wenn ich die\n Courage meines Freundes H. Heine hätte, würde ich Herrn Jeremias ein Genie in\n der bürgerlichen Dummheit nennen.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"598\", \"facs\": \"#f0617\"} --> technologisch gegeben. Aber weder ist die Anzahl der Arbeiter gegeben,\n erheischt um diese Arbeitsmasse flüssig zu machen, denn das wechselt mit\n dem Exploitationsgrad der individuellen Arbeitskraft, noch der Preis dieser\n Arbeitskraft, sondern nur seine zudem sehr elastische Minimalschranke.\n Die Thatsachen, die dem Dogma zu Grund liegen, sind die. Einerseits\n hat der Arbeiter nicht mitzusprechen bei der Theilung des gesell-\n schaftlichen Reichthums in Genussmittel der Nichtarbeiter und in Produk-\n tionsmittel. Andrerseits kann er nur in günstigen Ausnahmsfällen den s. g.\n „Arbeitsfonds“ auf Kosten der „Revenue“ des Reichen erwei-\n tern [Note: J. St. Mill sagt in seinen „Principles of Polit.","kind":"passage","sequence":115,"address":"19.115"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0116","text":"Economy“:\n „Das Produkt der Arbeit wird heutzutag vertheilt im umgekehrten Ver-\n hältniss zur Arbeit — der grösste Theil an die, die niemals arbeiten, der\n nächst grösste an die, deren Arbeit fast nur nominell ist, und so, auf absteigender\n Skala, schrumpft die Belohnung zusammen, im Masse wie die Arbeit härter und\n unangenehmer wird, bis die ermüdendste und erschöpfendste körperliche Arbeit\n nicht mit Sicherheit auch nur auf Gewinnung der Lebensnothwendigkeiten rechnen\n kann.“ Zur Vermeidung von Missverständniss bemerke ich, dass, wenn Männer\n wie J. St. Mill, Fawcett u. s. w. wegen des Widerspruchs ihrer altökonomischen\n Dogmen und ihrer modernen Tendenzen zu rügen sind, es durchaus unrecht wäre,\n sie mit dem Tross der vulgärökonomischen Apologeten zusammenzuwerfen.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":116,"address":"19.116"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0117","text":"Zu welch abgeschmackter Tautologie es führt, die kapita-\n listische Schranke des Arbeitsfonds in seine gesell-\n schaftliche Naturschranke umzudichten, zeige u. a. Professor\nFawcett: „Das cirkulirende Kapital [Note: H. Fawcett, Prof. of Polit. Econ. at Cambridge: „The\n Economic Position of the British Labourer. Lond. 1865“, p. 120.]  eines Landes,“ sagt er,\n „ist sein Arbeitsfonds. Um daher den durchschnittlichen Geldlohn, den\n jeder Arbeiter erhält, zu berechnen, haben wir nur einfach diess Kapital\n durch die Anzahl der Arbeiterbevölkerung zu dividiren“ [Note: Ich erinnere hier den Leser, dass die Kategorieen: variables und\nconstantes Kapital von mir zuerst gebraucht werden. Die politische Oeko-\n nomie seit A. Smith wirft die darin enthaltenen Bestimmungen mit den aus dem\nCirkulationsprozess entspringenden Formunterschieden von fixem und\ncirkulirendem Kapital kunterbunt zusammen.","kind":"passage","sequence":117,"address":"19.117"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0118","text":"Das Nähere darüber im\n Zweiten Buch, zweites Kapitel.] . D. h. also,\n erst rechnen wir die wirklich gezahlten individuellen Arbeitslöhne in eine\n Summe zusammen, dann behaupten wir, dass diese Addition die Werth-\n summe des von Gott und Natur oktroyirten „Arbeitsfonds“ bildet. End-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"599\", \"facs\": \"#f0618\"} --> lich dividiren wir die so erhaltne Summe durch die Kopfzahl der Arbeiter,\n um hinwiederum zu entdecken, wie viel jedem Arbeiter individuell im\n Durchschnitt zufallen kann. Eine ungemein pfiffige Procedur diess. Sie\n verhindert Herrn Fawcett nicht im selben Athemzug zu sagen: „Der in\n England jährlich accumulirte Gesammtreichthum wird in zwei Theile ge-\n theilt. Ein Theil wird in England zur Erhaltung unsrer eignen Industrie\n verwandt.","kind":"passage","sequence":118,"address":"19.118"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0119","text":"Ein andrer Theil wird in andere Länder exportirt … Der\n in unsrer Industrie angewandte Theil bildet keine bedeutende\n Portion des jährlich in diesem Land accumulirten Reich-\n thums“ [Note: Fawcett l. c. p. 123, 122.] . Der grössere Theil des jährlich zuwachsenden Mehrpro-\n dukts, dem englischen Arbeiter ohne Aequivalent entwandt, wird also\n nicht in England, sondern in fremden Ländern verkapitalisirt. Aber mit\n dem so exportirten Surpluskapital wird ja auch ein Theil des von Gott und\n Bentham erfundnen „Arbeitsfonds“ exportirt [Note: Man könnte sagen, dass nicht nur Kapital, sondern auch Arbeiter, in\n Form der Emigration, jährlich aus England exportirt werden. Im Text ist jedoch\n gar nicht die Rede vom Peculium der Auswanderer, die zum grossen Theil keine\n Arbeiter sind. Die Pächterssöhne liefern grosse Portion.","kind":"passage","sequence":119,"address":"19.119"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0120","text":"Das jährlich zur Ver-\n zinsung ins Ausland versandte englische Surpluskapital steht in ungleich grösserem\n Verhältniss zur jährlichen Accumulation als die jährliche Auswanderung zum\n jährlichen Zuwachs der Bevölkerung.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":120,"address":"19.120"}],"sha256":"f2778f72f973a638f54e1876548ec12170be8bbd92ab4309e5d1584589d9b090","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T205656776022Z_cf55a788","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"b) Verwandlung von Mehrwerth in Kapital.","source_sha256":"f2778f72f973a638f54e1876548ec12170be8bbd92ab4309e5d1584589d9b090","sha256":"d99ad75f67bfadaa68a429c553990125f30ebae89798cde23b5c3ea1e98c9e32","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T20:56:56.776037+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0001","text":"Earlier we had to consider how surplus-value springs from capital,\n now how capital springs from surplus-value. The application\n of surplus-value as capital, or the reconversion of\n surplus-value into capital, is called accumulation of capital [Note: “Accumulation of Capital: the employment of a portion of reve-\n nue as capital.” (Malthus: “Definitions etc.” ed. Cazenove p. 11.)\n “Conversion of revenue into Capital.” (Malthus: “Princ. of Pol.\n Econ. 2nd ed. Lond. 1836”, p. 319.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"19.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0002","text":"Let a capital amount to £10,000, its variable component\n £2,000. At a rate of surplus-value of 100%, it produces\n in a certain period, annually for example, a surplus-value of £2,000.\n If these £2,000 are advanced again as capital,\n the original capital grows from £10,000 to £12,000, or\n it has accumulated. It is at first immaterial whether the additional capital\n is added to the old one or valorised independently.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"19.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0003","text":"A sum of value of £2,000 is a sum of value of\n £2,000. One cannot smell or see from this money that it\nis surplus-value. The character of a value as surplus-value shows\nhow it came to its owner, but changes nothing in the nature of value\n or money. The transformation of the additional £2,000 into\n capital therefore proceeds in the same way as the transformation of the orig-\n inal £10,000. The conditions of the metamorphosis remain\n the same. One part of the £2,000 must be transformed into constant,\n the other into variable capital, the one into the objective\n factors of the labour process, material of labour and instruments of labour,\n the other into its subjective factor, labour-power. The capitalist must\n therefore find these elements on the commodity market.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"19.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0004","text":"Thus the pro-\n cess presents itself from the standpoint of the individual capitalist, who trans-\n forms the sum of money of £10,000 into a commodity-value of £12,000, recon-\n verts this commodity-value into money to the amount of £12,000, and now, alongside the original value of £10,000\n [Note: 7 April 1849.) The articles published under this title in the N. Rh. Z. are fragments of the lectures which I gave on that subject in 1847 in the German workers’ association at Brussels and whose printing was interrupted by the February Revolution.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"568\", \"facs\": \"#f0587\"} --> also causes the additional value of £2,000 to func-\n tion as his capital. But let us regard the £10,000 as the social\n capital or as the total capital of the capi-\n talist class, and the £2,000","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"19.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0005","text":"as its surplus-value produced during the year,\n for example! Surplus-value is embodied in an additional product, or surplus\n product. One part of this surplus product enters\n the consumption fund of the capitalists, or is consumed by them\n as revenue. Apart from this part, and likewise apart from foreign trade,\n which replaces domestic by foreign kinds of commodities, the surplus\n product, in its natural form, consists only of\nmeans of production, raw materials, auxiliary materials, instruments of labour, and\n necessary means of subsistence, that is, of the material\n elements of constant and variable capital. These are thus not\n accidentally present on the market, but are already existing\n modes of existence of the produced surplus-value itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"19.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0006","text":"But as regards the re-\n quired additional labour, the labour-powers already functioning may, up to a certain degree,\n be more fully employed, be stretched to a higher degree extensively or\n intensively. On the other hand, with the material elements of the additional capi-\n tal the capitalist process of production has also already supplied\n additional labour-powers. For since the working class emerges from the process\n as it entered it, different age groups of its children, whose existence the average\n wage secures, must continually enter the labour market alongside it. Considered\n concretely, accumulation is therefore the capitalist process of repro-\n duction on an enlarged scale.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"19.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0007","text":"Let us call the surplus-value of £2,000 transformed into additional capital\n surplus-capital No. I. For the sake of simplification,\n let it be assumed that its division into constant and variable component\n remains the same as in the case of the original capital, likewise the rate of sur-\n plus-value of 100%, and we know the method by which this capital of\n £2,000 produces a surplus-value of £400. Let this surplus-\n value be transformed into capital again. Thus we obtain surplus-\n capital No. II of £400, and so on.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"19.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0008","text":"What, then, has changed? The £10,000 which were orig-\n inally transformed into capital were the property of their possessor.\n He threw them upon the commodity and labour market. Where did he get\n them? We\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"569\", \"facs\": \"#f0588\"} --> do not know. The law of commodity exchange, according to which,\n on average, equivalents are exchanged and everyone buys commodity with commodity alone,\n favours the assumption that the £10,000 are only the money-\n form of his own products and therefore of his own labour,\n or of the labour of persons whose lawful representative he func-\n tions as.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"19.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0009","text":"By contrast, we know the process of origin of surplus-capital No. I\n quite exactly. It is only the transformed form of surplus-value,\n that is, of surplus-labour, unpaid alien labour. There is no atom of value\nin it for which its possessor would have paid an equivalent. Certainly,\n as before with one part of the original capital, so now with one part of\n the surplus-capital the capitalist purchases labour-power anew, from which he pumps\n surplus-labour anew and therefore produces surplus-value anew. But\n he now buys the worker with the latter’s own product, previously taken from him\n without an equivalent, or product-value,\n just as he employs him with means of production which in natura\n or whose value is a product of the worker taken without an equivalent.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"19.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0010","text":"It changes absolutely nothing in the matter whether the-\n same individual workers who have produced the surplus-capital are also\n employed with it, or whether worker B is recruited with the worker A’s unpaid\n labour transformed into money. This changes\n only the appearance, without beautifying it. Since the relation\n of the individual capitalist and the individual worker is that of mutually\n independent commodity-owners, one of whom buys labour-power,\n the other sells it, their connection is accidental. The capi-\n talist perhaps transforms the surplus-capital into a machine which throws the\n producer of the surplus-capital onto the pavement and replaces him with a few\n children.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"19.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0011","text":"In surplus-capital No. I all components are the product of unpaid\n alien labour, capitalised surplus-value. The appearance\n of the first presentation of the process of production, or of the first act of\n capital formation, disappears, as though the capitalist threw any values\n from his own fund into circulation. First the invisible\n magic of the process carries off the worker’s surplus-product from his pole to\n the capitalist’s opposite pole. Then the capitalist transforms this wealth,\n which for him is a creation from nothing, into capital, into a means\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"570\", \"facs\": \"#f0589\"} --> for the employment, domination, and exploitation of additional labour-\n power [Note: “Labour creates capital, before capital employs labour.”\n (“Labour creates capital, before capital employs labour.”) E. G. Wakefield:\n “England and America. Lond. 1833”, v.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"19.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0012","text":"II, p. 110.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"19.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0013","text":"In the capitalist process of production, originally\n only a sum of value belonging to the money-owner, on what title we do not know,\nis transformed into capital and hence into a source of surplus-value.\n A change takes place with this sum of value, but it itself is not the result\n of the process, but rather its presupposition, independent\n of it. In the simple process of reproduction, or the continuous\n process of production, it is a part of the worker’s product which constantly confronts him anew\n as variable capital, but his product constantly assumes this form anew\n because he originally sold his labour-power for the capitalist’s\n money.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"19.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0014","text":"Finally, in the\n course of reproduction, all capital-value advanced by the capitalist transforms itself\n into capitalised surplus-value, but this transformation itself\n presupposes that the fund originally derives from his own means. Otherwise in the process of accumulation, or the process\n of reproduction on an enlarged scale. The money, or, expressed materially,\n the means of production and subsistence, the substance of the new\n capital, is itself the product of the process that pumps out unpaid alien la-\n bour. Capital has produced capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"19.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0015","text":"A sum of value of £10,000 belonging to the capitalist\n was the presupposition for the formation of surplus-capital\n No. I of £2,000. The presupposition of surplus-capi-\n tal No. II of £400 is nothing other than the existence of\n surplus-capital No. I. Ownership of past unpaid\n labour now appears as the sole condition for the present appro-\n priation of unpaid living labour on an ever-growing scale.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"19.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0016","text":"Insofar as the surplus-value of which surplus-capital No. I consists was the re-\n sult of the purchase of labour-power by a part of the original capital,\n a purchase which corresponded to the laws of commodity exchange and,\n juridically considered, presupposes nothing but free disposal, on the part of\n the worker, over his own capacities, and on the part of the money- or commodity-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"571\", \"facs\": \"#f0590\"} --> owner, over values belonging to him; insofar as surplus-capital No. II, and so forth,\n is merely the result of surplus-capital No.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"19.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0017","text":"I, hence the consequence of that first rela-\n tion; insofar as every individual transaction continually corresponds to the law of\n commodity exchange, the capitalist always buying labour-power,\n the worker always selling it, and let us assume even at its\n actual value, evidently the law of appropriation based on commodity production and\n commodity circulation, or the law of private property, by its own, inner,\n inevitable dialectic, turns into its direct opposite\n [Note: Just as necessarily as commodity production becomes capitalist commodity production at a certain\n stage of development — indeed, only on the basis of the capitalist mode of production does the commodity become the general, ruling form of the product —,\n just as necessarily do the laws of property of commodity production\n turn into laws of capitalist appropriation.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"19.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0018","text":"Let one therefore admire the shrewdness\nof Proudhon, who wishes to abolish capitalist property by making valid — the\n eternal laws of property of commodity production!] . The exchange of equivalents, which appeared as the original\n operation, has turned in such a way that exchange takes place only in appearance,\n inasmuch as, firstly, the part of capital exchanged for labour-power\n is itself only a part of the alien labour-product appropriated\n without an equivalent, and, secondly, it must not only be replaced by\n its producer, the worker, but be replaced with a new surplus. The relation of exchange between capi-\n talist and worker is therefore only an appearance belonging to the process of circu-\n lation, a mere form alien to the content itself\n and only mystifying it. The continual purchase and sale of\n labour-power is the form.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"19.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0019","text":"The content is that the capitalist continually\n converts a part of already objectified alien labour, which he unceasingly\n appropriates without an equivalent, anew into a greater quantity of living\n alien labour. Originally, the right of property appeared to us founded on one’s own labour. At least this assumption had to be valid,\n since only commodity-owners with equal rights confront one another, while the means\n for appropriating an alien commodity is only the alienation of one’s own\n commodity, and the latter can be produced only through labour. Property now appears,\n on the side of the capitalist, as the right to alien unpaid\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"572\", \"facs\": \"#f0591\"} --> labour or its product; on the side of the worker, as the impossibility of appropriating\n his own product.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"19.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0020","text":"The separation between prop-\n erty and labour becomes the necessary consequence of a law\n that apparently proceeded from their identity [Note: The capitalist’s property in the alien labour-product “is\n the strict consequence of the law of appropriation, whose fundamental prin-\n ciple, conversely, was the exclusive title of every worker to property in the pro-\n duct of his own labour.” (Cherbuliez: “Riche ou Pauvre.\n Paris 1841”, p. 58, though this dialectical reversal is not correctly\n developed there.)] . It was seen that even\n in simple reproduction all capital advanced, however originally ac-\n quired, transforms itself into accumulated capital or capitalised sur-\n plus-value.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"19.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0021","text":"But in the stream of production all\n originally advanced capital becomes an evanescent magnitude (magni-\n tudo evanescens in the mathematical sense) in comparison with directly accu-\n mulated capital, i.e. surplus-value or surplus-product reconverted into capital,\n whether functioning in the hand that has accumulated it, or in\n another’s hand. Political economy therefore represents capital in gen-\n eral as “accumulated wealth (transformed surplus-value or\n revenue) employed anew for the production of surplus-value”\n [Note: “Capital viz: accumulated wealth employed with a view to profit.”\nMalthus. l. c. “Capital … consists of wealth saved from revenue,\n and used with a view to profit.” (R. Jones: “An Introductory Lecture\n on Polit. Econ. Lond. 1833”, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"19.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0022","text":"16.)] , or likewise the capitalist as “owner of the surplus product” [Note: “The possessors of surplusproduce or capital.” (“The Source and\n Remedy of the National Difficulties. A Letter to Lord John\n Russell. Lond. 1821.”)] .\n The same mode of view merely takes another form in the expression that\n all existing capital is accumulated or capitalised interest, for interest\n is a mere fraction of surplus-value [Note: “Capital, with compound interest on every portion of capital saved, is so\n all engrossing, that all the wealth in the world from which income is derived, has\n long ago become the interest on capital.” (London Economist, 19. July,\n 1859.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"19.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0023","text":"Before we now enter into some more precise determinations of accumu-\n lation or the reconversion of surplus-value into capital, it is\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"573\", \"facs\": \"#f0592\"} --> necessary to remove an ambiguity hatched by classical political economy.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"19.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0024","text":"Just as little as the commodities which the capitalist buys with a part of\n the surplus-value for his own consumption serve him as means of production\n and valorisation, so little is the labour which he buys for the satisfaction\n of his natural and social needs productive labour. Instead of transforming\n the surplus-value into capital by the purchase of those commodities and\n labour, he, conversely, consumes or expends it as revenue. In opposition\n to the old aristocratic mentality, which, as Hegel correctly says, “consists\n in consuming what is at hand,” and which displays itself particularly in\n the luxury of personal services, it was decisively important for bourgeois\n economy to stress that the gospel of the new society, namely accumulation\n of capital, preaches the expenditure of surplus-value in the purchase of\n productive workers as conditio sine qua.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"19.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0025","text":"On the other hand, it was necessary to polemicise against the popular\n prejudice which confuses capitalist production with hoard formation [Note: “No political economist of the present day can by saving mean mere\n hoarding: and beyond this contracted and insufficient proceeding, no use of the\n term in reference to the national wealth can well be imagined, but that which must\n arise from a different application of what is saved, founded upon a real distinc-\n tion between the different kinds of labour maintained by it.” (Malthus l. c.\n p. 38, 39.)]\n and therefore imagines that accumulated wealth is wealth withdrawn from\n consumption, hence from destruction in its existing natural form, or also\n wealth saved from circulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"19.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0026","text":"Withholding money from cir-\n culation would be precisely the opposite of its valorisation as capital, and\n accumulation of commodities in the hoard-forming sense sheer folly. Accumu-\n lation of commodities in large masses is the result of a stoppage of circulation\n or of overproduction [Note: “Accumulation of stocks … non-exchange … overproduction.”\n (Th. Corbet l. c. p. 14.)] . Certainly, in the popular conception there\n appears the image of goods heaped up in the consumption fund of the rich,\n slowly being consumed. On the other hand, there is stock formation,\n a phenomenon belonging to all modes of production and at which we shall\n pause for a moment in the analysis of the process of circulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"19.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0027","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"574\", \"facs\": \"#f0593\"} --> To this extent, then, classical political economy is right when it stresses the consumption of\n surplus product by productive workers rather than by unproductive ones\n as the characteristic phenomenon of the process of accumulation.\n But here its error also begins. A. Smith made it fashionable to represent\n accumulation as consumption of the surplus product by productive\n workers, or the capitalisation of surplus-value as its mere conversion\n into labour-power. Let us hear Ricardo, for example: “It must\n be understood, that all the productions of a country are consumed; but it\n makes the greatest difference imaginable whether they are consumed by\n those who reproduce another value, or by those who do not reproduce it.”","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"19.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0028","text":"When we say that revenue is saved\n and added to capital, we mean that the part of the revenue said to have\n been added to capital is consumed by productive rather than unproductive\n workers” [Note: Ricardo l. c. p. 163 Note] . There is no greater error than that parroted after\n A. Smith by Ricardo and all later writers, namely that “the part of the\n revenue said to have been added to capital is consumed\n by productive workers.” According to this conception, all surplus-value\n transformed into capital would become variable capital. In fact, like the\n value originally advanced, it divides into constant capital and variable\n capital, into means of production and labour-power. Labour-power is the\n form in which variable capital exists within the production process.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"19.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0029","text":"In this process it is itself consumed by the capitalist. It\n consumes, through its function—labour—means of production. At the same\n time, the money paid in the purchase of labour-power is transformed into\n means of subsistence, which are consumed not by “productive labour” but\n by the “productive worker.” By a thoroughly erroneous analysis, A. Smith\n arrives at the absurd result that, although every individual capital divides\n into a constant and variable component, social capital resolves itself into\n variable capital alone, or is expended solely in the payment of wages.\n For example, a cloth manufacturer transforms £2,000 into capital. He lays out\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"575\", \"facs\": \"#f0594\"} --> one part of the money in the purchase of weavers, the other in woollen yarn,\n woollen machinery, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"19.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0030","text":"But the people from\n whom he buys the yarn and machinery in turn pay out part of it for labour,\n etc., until the whole £2,000 has been expended in the payment of wages,\n or the entire product represented by the £2,000 has been consumed by\n productive workers. One sees: the whole force of this argument lies in the\n word “etc.,” which sends us from Pontius to Pilate. In fact, A. Smith\n breaks off the investigation precisely where its difficulty begins [Note: Despite his “logic,” Mr. J. St. Mill nowhere gets even to the bottom\n of such faulty analysis by his predecessors, which, even within the bourgeois\n horizon, from the purely specialist standpoint, cries out for correction. Every-\n where he records with schoolboyish dogmatism the confused thoughts of his\n masters.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"19.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0031","text":"Here too: “The capital itself in the\n long run becomes entirely wages, and when replaced by the sale of produce\nbecomes wages again.”] .\n In the third chapter of the second book I shall give the analysis\n of the real connection. It will there be shown that A. Smith’s dogma,\n inherited by all his successors, has prevented political economy from\n grasping even the elementary mechanism of the social process of reproduc-\n tion [Note: In his presentation of the process of reproduction, and therefore also\n of accumulation, A. Smith has in many respects not only made no progress, but\n taken decided backward steps compared with his predecessors, especially the\n Physiocrats.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"19.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0032","text":"The illusion mentioned in the text is connected with the equally\n truly fabulous dogma, likewise bequeathed by him to political economy, that the\n price of commodities is composed of wages, profit (interest), and ground rent,\n thus merely of wages and surplus-value. Proceeding from this basis, at least\n Storch naively admits: “Il est impossible de résoudre le prix nécessaire dans ses élémens les plus simples.”\n (Storch l. c. Petersb. Edit. 1815, t. I, p. 140, Note.) A fine economic\n science which declares it impossible to analyse the price of commodities into its\n simplest elements! Further details on this will be found discussed in the\n 7th chapter of the third book.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"19.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0033","text":"In the first section of this chapter we considered the entire\n surplus-value, or respectively the surplus product, only as the individual\n consumption fund of the capitalist; in this section hitherto only as his\naccumulation fund. But it is neither merely the one nor the\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"576\", \"facs\": \"#f0595\"} --> other, but both at once. One part of the surplus-value is consumed by\n the capitalist as revenue [Note: The reader will observe that the word revenue is used in a double sense:\n first, to designate surplus-value as fruit periodically springing from capital;\n second, to designate the part of this fruit which is periodically consumed by the\n capitalist or added to his consumption fund. I retain this double sense because it\n accords with the usage of English and French economists.] , another part is\n applied as capital or accumulated.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"19.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0034","text":"Given the mass of surplus-value, the magnitude of\n accumulation obviously depends upon the division of surplus-value into\n accumulation fund and consumption fund, into capital\n and revenue. The greater the one part, the smaller the other. The\n mass of surplus-value or of surplus product, hence of the disposable wealth\n of a country that can be transformed into capital, is therefore always\n greater than the part of surplus-value actually transformed into capital.\n The more developed capitalist production is in a country, the more rapid\n and massive accumulation, the richer the country, and hence the more colossal\n luxury and waste, the greater this difference.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"19.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0035","text":"Apart\n from the annual increase of wealth, the wealth contained in the consumption\n fund of the capitalists, which is destructible only gradually, possesses in\n part natural forms in which it could function directly as capital. Among\n the existing elements of wealth which could function in the production\n process are all labour-powers which are consumed not at all, or only in\n purely conventional personal, often infamous, services. The proportion in\n which surplus-value is divided into capital and revenue changes\n incessantly and is governed by circumstances which cannot be further developed\n here. The capital employed in a country is therefore not a fixed,\n but a fluctuating magnitude, a constantly variable and elastic\n fraction of the existing wealth which can function as\n capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"19.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0036","text":"Since the continual appropriation of the surplus-value\n or surplus product produced by the worker appears to the capitalist as the\n periodic fruit-bearing of his capital, or since the product of another’s labour,\n which he usurps without an equivalent of any kind, forms a periodic increase of his private\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"577\", \"facs\": \"#f0596\"} --> wealth, it is natural that the division of this surplus-value\n or surplus product into additional capital and consumption fund is mediated\n by an act of will on his part.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"19.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0037","text":"Only in so far as the capitalist is personified capital does\n he have a historical value and that historical right to exist which, as the\n witty Lichnowsky says, has no date whatever. Only in so far is his own\n transitory necessity contained in the transitory necessity of the capitalist\n mode of production. But to that extent, use-value and enjoyment are not,\n but exchange-value and its augmentation are, his driving motive. As a\n fanatic of the valorisation of value, he ruthlessly compels humanity to\n produce for production’s sake, and thus to a development of the social\n productive forces and to the creation of material conditions of production\n which alone can form the real basis of a higher form of society, whose\n fundamental principle is the full and free development of every individual.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"19.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0038","text":"Only as the personification of capi-\n tal is the capitalist respectable. As such he shares with the hoard-former\n the absolute drive to enrich himself. Moreover, the immanent laws of the\n capitalist mode of production, which competition imposes upon every individual\n capitalist as external coercive laws, compel him continually to expand\n his capital in order to preserve it. In so far as his doing and refraining\n are only functions of capital endowed in him with will and consciousness,\n his own private consumption counts for him as a robbery from the accumulation\n of his capital, just as in Italian bookkeeping private expenditures figure\n on the debit side of the capitalist against capital. Accumulation is conquest\n of the world of social wealth.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"19.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0039","text":"It extends, together with the mass of exploited human\n material, the direct and indirect domination of the capitalist [Note: In the old-fashioned, though sporadically surviving, form of the\n capitalist, the usurer, Luther illustrates very well the lust for domination\n as an element of the drive to enrich oneself. “The heathens have been able to\n reckon by reason that a usurer is a fourfold thief and murderer. But we\n Christians hold them in such honour that we almost worship them for their\n money … He who sucks out another’s sustenance, robs and steals from him,\n commits just as great a murder (as far as lies in him) as one who lets another die of hunger] .","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"19.39","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0040","text":"[Running matter: I. 37]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"578\", \"facs\": \"#f0597\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"19.40","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0041","text":"But original sin is at work everywhere. With the development of the capi-\ntalist mode of production, of accumulation, and of wealth, the capitalist\nceases to be the mere incarnation of capital. He feels a\n“human stirring” for his own Adam and becomes so cultivated as to\nsmile at enthusiasm for asceticism as the prejudice of the old-fashioned hoard-\nformer. Whereas the classical capitalist brands individual consumption\nas a sin against his function and as “abstention” from accumulation,\nthe modernised capitalist is capable of conceiving accumulation\nas “renunciation” of his instinct for enjoyment. “Two souls dwell,\nalas! in his breast, one would fain part from the other!”","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"19.41","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0042","text":"[Note: and corrupts them utterly. But this is what a usurer does, and meanwhile sits\nsecurely on his chair, though he ought rather to hang from the gallows and be\neaten by as many ravens as he had stolen guilders, if only there were so much flesh in him\nthat so many ravens could tear and divide it among themselves. Meanwhile\none hangs the little thieves … Little thieves lie captive in stocks, great thieves\ngo forth decked in gold and silk … Thus there is likewise no greater enemy of mankind\non earth (after the Devil) than a miser and usurer, for he wishes\nto be God over all men. Turks, soldiers, tyrants are also\nevil men, yet they must let people live and acknowledge that they are evil\nand enemies, and can, indeed must, well at times take pity on some. ","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"19.42","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0043","text":"But a usurer and greedy paunch wishes that all the world should perish in hunger and\nthirst, sorrow and distress, as far as he is concerned, so that he alone may have everything,\nand everyone may receive from him, as from a God, and be eternally\nhis serf. To wear fine clothes, golden chains, rings, to wipe his mouth,\nto let himself be regarded and praised as a dear, pious man … Usury\nis a great and monstrous monster, like a werewolf, that lays everything waste, more than\nany Cacus, Geryon, or Antaeus. And yet he adorns himself and wishes to be pious,\nso that one shall not see where the oxen that he draws backwards\ninto his hole have gone. But Hercules shall hear the cries of the oxen\nand the captives, and seek Cacus even among crags and rocks, and release\nthe oxen again from the evildoer. For Cacus means an evildoer, who is a pious\nusurer, steals, robs, devours everything.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"19.43","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0044","text":"And yet he will not admit having done it, and\nno one is to find out, because the oxen have been drawn backwards into his hole,\ngiving the appearance and footprints as though they had been let out.\nThus the usurer too wishes to fool the world, as though he were useful and gave\nthe world oxen, while in fact he tears them to himself alone and devours them …\nAnd if highway robbers, murderers, and burglars are broken on the wheel and beheaded, how much\nmore should all usurers be broken on the wheel and … driven out, cursed, and\nbeheaded.” (Martin Luther l. c.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"19.44","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0045","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"579\", \"facs\": \"#f0598\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"19.45","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0046","text":"In the historical beginnings of the capitalist mode of production\n—and every capitalist parvenu individually passes through this historical stage—\nthe drive to enrichment and avarice predominate as absolute\npassions. But the progress of capitalist produc-\ntion creates not only a world of enjoyments. With speculation\nand the credit system it opens a thousand sources of sudden enrichment.\nAt a certain level of development, a conventional degree of\nextravagance, which is at the same time an exhibition of wealth and hence\na means of credit, even becomes a business necessity for the “unfortunate”\ncapitalist. Luxury enters into the costs of representation of capi-\ntal.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"19.46","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0047","text":"Moreover, the capitalist does not enrich himself, like the hoard-\nformer, in proportion to his personal labour and his personal\nnon-consumption, but to the extent that he sucks out alien labour-power\nand compels the worker to renounce all enjoyments of life. Although,\ntherefore, the capitalist’s extravagance never possesses the bona fide\ncharacter of the extravagance of the carefree feudal lord, but rather, in its back-\nground, the most sordid avarice and the most anxious calculation\nalways lurk, his extravagance nevertheless grows with his accumulation,\nwithout one needing to interrupt the other. At the same time there develops\nin the high breast of the individual capitalist a Faustian\nconflict between the drive to accumulation and the drive to enjoyment.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"19.47","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0048","text":"“The industry of Manchester,” it is said in a work which Dr.\nAikin published in 1795, “may be divided into four periods.\nIn the first, the manufacturers were compelled to work hard for their liveli-\nhood.” They enriched themselves especially by robbing the parents, who assigned\nboys to them as apprentices and had to pay heavily for this, while\nthe apprentices were starved. On the other hand, average profits\nwere low and accumulation demanded great thrift. They lived like hoard-\nformers and by no means consumed even the interest on their capital. “In\nthe second period they had begun to acquire small fortunes,\nbut worked just as hard as before,” for the direct exploitation of\nlabour costs labour, as every slave-driver knows, “and lived in\nthe same frugal style as before.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"19.48","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0049","text":"… In the third period,\nluxury began and business was extended by sending out riders\n(mounted commercial travellers) for orders in every market town of the King-\n[Running matter: 37*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"580\", \"facs\": \"#f0599\"} --> dom. It is probable that few or no capitals of\n£3,000 to £4,000, acquired in industry, existed before 1690.\nAt this time, however, or somewhat later, the industrialists had already\naccumulated money and began to erect stone houses instead of those\nof wood and mortar. … Even in the first decades of the 18th","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"19.49","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0050","text":"century,\na Manchester manufacturer who set a pint of foreign wine before his\nguests exposed himself to the gossip and head-shaking of all his neigh-\nbours.” Before the rise of machinery, the evening consumption of the\nmanufacturers in the taverns where they met never amounted to more\nthan 6 d. for a glass of punch and 1 d. for a roll of tobacco. Only in 1758,\nand this marks an epoch, did one see “a person actually engaged\nin business with his own equipage!” “The fourth period,” the last third\nof the 18th century, “is one of great luxury and extravagance,\nsupported by the extension of business” [Note: Dr. Aikin: “Description of the Country from 30 to 40\nmiles round Manchester. Lond. 1795”, p. 182 sqq.] . What would the\ngood Dr. Aikin say if he rose again in Manchester today!","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"19.50","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0051","text":"Accumulate, accumulate! That is Moses and the Prophets! “Industry\nprovides the material which thrift accumulates” [Note: A. Smith l. c. b. III, ch. III.] .\nSo save, save, i.e. reconvert as large a portion as possible of surplus-\nvalue or surplus-product into capital! Accumulation for the sake of accumula-\ntion, production for the sake of production: in this formula\nclassical economy expressed the historical vocation of the bourgeois period.\nIt did not deceive itself for a moment about the birth-pangs of wealth [Note: Even J. B. Say says: “Les épargnes des riches se font aux dépens des\npauvres.” “The Roman proletarian lived almost entirely at the expense of so-\nciety . . . . One might almost say that modern society lives at the expense\nof the proletarians, from the portion which it withdraws from them from the remuneration\nof labour.” (Sismondi: “Études etc.” t. 1, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"19.51","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0052","text":"24.)] , but what use is lamentation over historical necessity?\nIf, for classical economy, the proletarian counts only as a machine for\nthe production of surplus-value, so too does the capitalist count only as a ma-\nchine for transforming this surplus-value into additional capital. It takes\nhis historical function in bitter earnest. In order to safeguard his breast\nfrom the fateful conflict between the drive to enjoyment and the drive to enrichment,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"581\", \"facs\": \"#f0600\"} --> Malthus defended, at the beginning of the twenties of this cen-\ntury, a division of labour which assigns to the capitalist actually\nengaged in production the business of accumulation, and to the other par-\nticipants in surplus-value—the landed aristocracy, state and church sinecurists,\netc.—the business of extravagance.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"19.52","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0053","text":"It is of the high-\nest importance, he says, “to keep the passion for expenditure and the pas-\nsion for accumulation (‘the passion for expenditure and the passion for\naccumulation’) separate” [Note: Malthus l. c. p. 319, 320.] . The gentlemen capitalists, long\ntransformed into men of pleasure and men of the world, cried out. What,\ncried one of their spokesmen, a Ricardian, Mr. Malthus preaches high ground rents, high\ntaxes, etc., in order to impose a continual spur upon the industrialist through\nunproductive consumers! To be sure, production, pro-\nduction on an ever-expanding scale, is the shibboleth, but “pro-\nduction is much more impeded than promoted by such a process.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"19.53","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0054","text":"Nor is it quite fair to maintain\na number of persons in idleness merely in order to squeeze others, from whose\ncharacters one may infer, (“who are likely, from their characters”),\nthat if you can compel them to function, they will function successfully” [Note: “An Inquiry into those principles respecting the Nature\nof Demand etc.”, p. 67.] . However unfair he finds it to spur the industrial capi-\ntalist to accumulation by skimming the fat from his soup, he considers it\nnecessary to restrict the worker as far as possible to the minimum wage,\n“in order to keep him industrious.” Nor does he conceal for a moment\nthat appropriation of unpaid labour is the secret of money-making.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"19.54","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0055","text":"“Increased demand on the part of the\nworkers means absolutely nothing but their inclination to take less of their\nown product for themselves and to leave a greater\npart of it to their employers; and if it is said that this, by diminishing\nconsumption (on the part of the workers), produces a\nglut (market glut, overproduction), I can only an-\nswer that glut is synonymous with high profit” [Note: l. c. p. 50.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"19.55","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0056","text":"The learned dispute over how the booty pumped out of the worker was to be distributed most\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"582\", \"facs\": \"#f0601\"} --> advantageously for accumulation between the industrial capi-\ntalist and the idle landed proprietor, etc., fell silent before the July\nRevolution. Shortly afterwards the urban proletariat rang the alarm bell\nat Lyons and made the rural proletariat set the red cock flying in England.\nOn this side of the Channel Owenism raged; on the other, Saint-Si-\nmonism and Fourierism. The hour of vulgar economy had\nstruck. Exactly one year before Nassau W. Senior\nfound out at Manchester that the profit (including interest) of capital was the product of the un-\npaid “last twelfth hour of labour,” he had announced another\ndiscovery to the world.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"19.56","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0057","text":"“I,” he said solemnly, “I\nreplace the word capital, considered as an instrument of production,\nwith the word abstinence (abstention)” [Note: Senior: “Principes fondamentaux del’Écon. Pol.” trad.\nArrivabene. Paris 1836, p. 308. This was a little too absurd even for the adherents of\nthe old classical school. “Mr. Senior substitutes for the expression\nlabour and capital the expression labour and abstinence … Abstinence is\na mere negation. It is not abstinence, but the use of productively transformed\ncapital, which constitutes the source of profit.” (John\nCazenove l. c. p. 130 Note.) Mr. John St. Mill, on the other hand, excerpts\nRicardo’s theory of profit on one side and annexes Senior’s “remune-\nration of abstinence” on the other. As alien as the Hegelian “contradiction,” the fountain-\nhead of all dialectic, is to him, so much at home is he in flat contradictions.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"19.57","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0058","text":"An unsurpassed\nexample of the “discoveries” of vulgar economy! It replaces\nan economic category by a sycophantic phrase. Voilà\ntout. “When the savage,” Senior lectures, “makes bows,\nhe exercises an industry, but he does not practise absti-\nnence.” This explains to us how and why, in earlier social condi-\ntions, means of labour were manufactured “without the abstinence”\nof the capitalist. “The more society progresses, the more abstinence\nit requires” [Note: Senior l. c. p. 342.] , namely from those who exercise industry, to appropriate for\nthemselves the industry of others and its product. All the condi-\ntions of the labour-process henceforth turn into\njust so many practices of abstinence on the part of the capitalist.\nThat grain is not only eaten but also sown:\nabstinence of the capitalist!","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"19.58","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0059","text":"That wine is allowed time to fer-\nment: abstinence of the capitalist! [Note: “No one … will sow his wheat, f. i., and allow it to remain a twelve-\nmonth in the ground, or leave his wine in a cellar for years, instead of consuming\nthese things or their equivalent at once — — unless he expects to acquire ad-\nditional value etc.” (Scrope: “Polit. Econ.” edit. von A. Potter,\nNew-York 1841, p. 133, 134.)] The capitalist deprives\nhis own Adam when he “lends the instruments of production\nto the worker” (!), i.e., values them as capital by incorporating labour-\npower, instead of consuming steam-engines, cotton, railways, manure,\ndraught-horses, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"19.59","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0060","text":"to eat it up or, as the vulgar economist childishly imagines it, to squander “its value” on luxury and other articles of consumption [Note: “La privation que s’impose le capitaliste, en prêtant (this euphemism is employed, in the tried vulgar-economic manner, in order to identify the wage-worker exploited by the industrial capitalist with the industrial capitalist himself, who borrows from the money-lending capitalist!) ses instruments de production au travailleur au lieu d’en consacrer la valeurà son propre usage, en la transformant en objets d’utilité ou d’agrément.” (G. de Molinari l. c. p. 49.)] . How the capitalist class is to manage this is a secret hitherto stubbornly preserved by vulgar economy. Enough, the world now lives only by the self-mortification of this modern penitent of Vishnu, the capitalist.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"19.60","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0061","text":"Not only\naccumulation, the simple “preservation of a capital requires a constant\neffort of strength in order to resist the temptation to eat it up” [Note: “La conservation d’un capital exige … un effort constant pour résister\nà la temptation de le consommer.” (Courceuil-Senelles l. c. p. 57.)] .\nSimple humanity thus evidently commands that the capitalist be delivered\nfrom martyrdom and temptation, in the same way as the Georgian\nslaveholder was recently delivered, through the abolition of slavery, from the painful\ndilemma whether to squander entirely in champagne the surplus-product\nwhipped out of the Negro slave, or also partially to reconvert it into\nmore Negroes and more land.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"19.61","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0062","text":"In the most diverse economic formations of society there occurs\nnot only simple reproduction but, although on a different scale,\nreproduction on an expanded scale. Progressively more is\nproduced and more consumed, hence also more product transformed into\nmeans of production.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"19.62","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0063","text":"But this process does not appear\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"584\", \"facs\": \"#f0603\"} --> as accumulation of capital, and therefore not as a\nfunction of the capitalist, so long as the worker’s means of produc-\ntion, and hence also his product and his means of subsistence, do not yet\nconfront him in the form of capital [Note: “The particular classes of income which yield the most abundantly to the\nprogress of national capital, change at different stages of their progress, and are\ntherefore entirely different in nations occupying different positions in that progress\n… Profit … unimportant source of accumulation, compared with wages and\nrents, in the earlier stages of society … When a considerable advance in the\npowers of national industry has actually taken place, profits rise into comparative\nimportance as a source of accumulation.” (Richard Jones: “Textbook\netc.”, p. 16, 21.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"19.63","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0064","text":"Richard Jones, who died a few\nyears ago, Malthus’s successor in the chair of political economy at\nHerford, discusses this well by means of two great facts. Since the most numerous\npart of the Indian people consists of self-sustaining peasants, their product, their means\nof labour and subsistence, likewise never exists “in the form (“in the shape”) of a fund, which\nis saved from foreign revenue (“saved from revenue”) and\nhas therefore passed through a preliminary process of accumulation (“a previous\nprocess of accumulation”)” [Note: l. c. p. 36 sq.] . On the other hand, the\nnon-agricultural workers in the provinces where English rule has least dissolved the old\nsystem are employed directly by the great men,\nto whom a portion of the rural surplus-product flows as tribute or ground-\nrent.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"19.64","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0065","text":"One part of this product is consumed in kind by the\ngreat men, another part transformed for them by the workers into luxury\nand other articles of consumption, while the remainder forms the wages\nof the workers, who are the owners of their instruments of labour. Pro-\nduction and reproduction on an expanded scale proceed here in their\ncourse without any intervention by that wondrous saint, that knight\nof the doleful countenance, the “abstaining” capitalist.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"19.65","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0066","text":"Hitherto we have regarded the mass of surplus-value as a given\nmagnitude. In that case its proportional division into revenue and\nsurplus-capital determined the extent of accumulation. But the latter varies\nindependently of that division with the variation in the magnitude of surplus-value\nitself.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"585\", \"facs\": \"#f0604\"} --> The circumstances that regulate the magnitude of surplus-value have been developed in detail in the chap-\nters on its production. Other conditions remaining the same, they regulate\nthe movement of accumulation. We return to them here only insofar\nas they offer new points of view with reference to accumulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"19.66","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0067","text":"It will be recalled what a role the degree of exploitation of\nlabour plays in the production of surplus-value. Political econ-\nomy values this role so highly that it occasionally identifies the acceleration\nof accumulation through increased productivity of labour\nwith its acceleration through increased exploitation of the work-\ner [Note: “Ricardo says: ‘In different stages of society the accu-\nmulation of capital, or of the means of employing labour (sc. of exploiting it), is more\nor less rapid and must in all cases depend on the productive powers of labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"19.67","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0068","text":"The productive powers of labour are generally greatest where\nan abundance of fertile soil exists.’ If in this sentence the productive\npowers of labour mean the smallness of the aliquot part\nof each product which falls to those whose manual labour pro-\nduces it, then the proposition is tautological, since the part\nremaining over is the fund from which, if its owner pleases (“if the owner pleases”),\ncapital can be accumulated. But this is mostly not the case where the\nland is most fertile.” (“Observations on certain verbal dis-\nputes etc.”, p. 74, 75.)] . In the sections on the production of surplus-\nvalue it was constantly assumed that wages were at least equal to the value\nof labour-power.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"19.68","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0069","text":"It was further shown that wages,\nwhether according to their value or according to the mass of means of subsistence\nwhich they represent, can rise with an increasing degree of exploitation of the worker.\nIn the practical movement of capital, however, surplus-value is also\nproduced by the forcible reduction of wages below the value of labour-power. In\nfact, in this way a part of the necessary consumption fund of\nthe worker is transformed into an accumulation fund of capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"19.69","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0070","text":"“Wages,” says J. St. Mill, “have no productive power; they\nare the price of a productive power. Wages do not contribute, alongside\nlabour itself, to the production of commodities, any more than the price of ma-\nchinery contributes alongside machinery itself. If labour could be had without purchase,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"586\", \"facs\": \"#f0605\"} --> wages would be superfluous” [Note: J. St. Mill: “Essays on some unsettled Questions of Polit.\nEconomy. Lond. 1844”, p. 90.] . But\nif the workers could live on air, they could not be bought at\nany price. Their costlessness is therefore a limit\nin the mathematical sense, always unattainable, although always approximable.\nIt is the constant tendency of capital to press them down to this\nnihilistic standpoint. A writer of the 18th century often cited by me,","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"19.70","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0071","text":"the author of the “Essay\non Trade and Commerce”, betrays only the innermost secret of the\nEnglish capital’s soul when he declares it England’s historical\nlife-task to depress English wages to\nthe French and Dutch level [Note: “An Essay on Trade and Commerce. Lond. 1770”, p. 44.\nSimilarly, in December 1866 and January 1867 the Times published heartfelt effu-\nsions by English mine-owners, in which the happy condition of the Belgian\nmine-workers was depicted, who demanded and received no more\nthan was strictly necessary to live for their “masters”. The Belgian work-\ners endure much, but to figure as model workers in the Times! At the beginning\nof February 1867, the strike of the Belgian mine-workers (at Marchienne), suppressed with\npowder and lead, supplied the answer.] . He says,\namong other things:","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"19.71","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0072","text":"naively: “But if our poor (a technical term for workers)\nwill live luxuriously … their labour must naturally be dear.\n… One need only consider the hair-raising mass of super-\nfluities (“heap of superfluities”) consumed by our manufacturing workers,\nsuch as brandy, gin, tea, sugar, foreign fruits,\nstrong beer, printed linen, snuff and smoking tobacco, etc.” [Note: l. c. p. 46.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"19.72","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0073","text":"He cites the writing of a manufacturer from Northamptonshire, who,\nwith eyes turned heavenward, laments: “Labour is a full third\ncheaper in France than in England: for the French\npoor work hard and fare hard in food and clothing,\nand their chief consumption is bread, fruit, herbs, roots, and dried\nfish; for they very seldom eat meat, and when wheat is\ndear, very little bread” [Note: The Northamptonshire manufacturer commits a pia fraus excusable in the ardour of his heart. He allegedly compares the life of English and French-] . “To what,” the Essay man continues,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"587\", \"facs\": \"#f0606\"} --> “to what must be added that their drink consists of water or simi-\nlar weak liquors, so that in fact they expend astonishingly\nlittle money. . . .","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"19.73","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0074","text":"Such a state of affairs is cer-\ntainly difficult to bring about, but it is not unattainable, as its\nexistence in both France and Holland strikingly proves” [Note: l. c. p. 70.] . Two\ndecades later an American humbug, the ennobled Yankee Benjamin Thomson (alias Count Rumford), pursued the same line of philan-\nthropy with great satisfaction before God and men. His\n“Essays” are a cookbook with recipes of every kind for putting surrogates\nin place of the worker’s dear normal foods. One particularly successful\nrecipe of this wondrous “philosopher” is the follow-\ning: “Five pounds of barley, five pounds of maize, 3 d. worth of herrings, 1 d. of salt,\n1 d. of vinegar, 2 d. of pepper and herbs—a total of 20¾ d.—makes a\nsoup for 64 persons; indeed, at the average prices of grain,\nthe cost can be reduced to ¼ d.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"19.74","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0075","text":"per head (not yet 3 pfennigs)” [Note: Benjamin Thomson: “Essays, political, economical,\nand philosophical etc. 3 vol. Lond. 1796—1802.” In his “The\nState of the Poor, or an History of the Labouring Classes in\nEngland etc.” Sir M. F. Eden strongly recommends Rumford’s beggar’s soup\nto the overseers of workhouses and reproachfully admonishes the English workers that\n“in Scotland there are many families who, instead of wheat, rye\nand meat, live for months on oatmeal and barley meal, mixed only with salt and\nwater, and that very comfortably too” (“and that\nvery comfortably too”). (l. c. v. I, b. II, ch. II.) Similar “hints” in\nthe 19th century. “The English agricultural labourers,” it is said, for example, “will\nnot eat mixtures of inferior kinds of grain.","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"19.75","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0076","text":"In Scotland, where education\nis better, this prejudice is probably unknown.” (“Charles H.\nParry M. D.: “The Question of the Necessity of the existing\nCornlaws considered. Lond. 1816”, p. 69.) The same Parry complains,\nhowever, that the English worker has now (1815) greatly deteriorated, compared\nwith Eden’s time (1797).] . With the progress of capitalist production,\nadulteration of commodities has made Thomson’s ideals superfluous [Note: From the reports of the latest parliamentary commission of inquiry\ninto the adulteration of foodstuffs it can be seen that even the adulteration of medi-] .\n[Note: cal manufactures, but, as he later admits in his dotage, depicts with the words just cited French agricultural\nworkers!]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"588\", \"facs\": \"#f0607\"} --> At the end of the 18th","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"19.76","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0077","text":"and during the first decades of the 19th century,\nthe English farmers and landlords enforced the absolute minimum\nwage by paying agricultural day-labourers less than the minimum in the\nform of wages, and paying the remainder in the form of parish relief. An example of the buffoonery with which the Eng-\nlish Dogberries proceeded in their “legal” fixing of the wage tariff:\n“When the squires fixed wages for Speenhamland in 1795, they had\ndined, but evidently thought that the workers did not need the same. … They decided that the weekly\nwage should be 3 sh. per man, when the loaf of bread of 8 pounds 11\nounces stood at 1 sh., and that it should regularly increase until the loaf\ncost 1 sh. 5 d. As soon as it rose above this price, the wage was to de-\ncrease proportionately, until the price of the loaf reached 2 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"19.77","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0078","text":"and\nthen the man’s food was to be ⅕ less than before” [Note: G. B. Newnham (barrister at law): “A Review of the Evidence\nbefore the Committees of the two Houses of Parliament on the\nCornlaws. Lond. 1815”, p. 20 Note.] . Before\nthe committee of inquiry of the House of Lords, in 1814, a cer-\ntain A. Bennett, large farmer, magistrate, administrator of the poorhouse and\nwage regulator, is asked: “Is any proportion observed between the value\nof the day’s labour and the parish relief of the workers?”\nAnswer: “Yes. The weekly income of each family is made up\nbeyond its nominal wage to the gallon loaf of bread (8 lb.\n11 oz.) and 3 d. per head. . . . We assume the gallon loaf to be suf-\nficient for the maintenance of every person in the family during the\nweek; and the 3 d.”","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"19.78","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0079","text":"are for clothes; and if it pleases the parish\n to provide the clothes themselves, the 3 d. are deducted. This practice\n prevails not only throughout the whole west of Wiltshire, but, I\n believe, throughout the whole country” [Note: l. c.]. Thus, exclaims a bourgeois writer of that\n time, “the farmers have for years degraded a respectable class of their coun-\n trymen by forcing them to take re-\n [Note: the adulteration of foodstuffs in England constitutes not an exception, but the rule. Thus, e.g., the examination\n of 34 samples of opium, purchased in as many different London apo-\n thecaries, showed that 31 were adulterated with poppy capsules, wheat flour, gummy\n mucilage, clay, sand, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"19.79","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0080","text":"Many contained not an atom of morphine.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"589\", \"facs\": \"#f0608\"} -->fuge in the workhouse … The farmer has increased his own profits\n by himself preventing the accumulation of the most indis-\n pensable consumption fund on the side of the workers” [Note: Ch. H. Parry l. c. p. 78. The gentlemen landlords, for their part, not only “indemni-\n fied” themselves for the anti-Jacobin war which they waged in England’s name,\n but enriched themselves enormously. “Their rents doubled, trebled,\n quadrupled and, in exceptional cases, sextupled in 18\n years.” (l. c. p. 100, 101.)]. What role the direct robbery of the worker’s neces-\n sary consumption fund plays nowadays in the formation of surplus-value\n and hence of the accumulation fund of capital has been shown,\n for example, by so-called domestic labour. Further facts in the course\n of this chapter.","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"19.80","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0081","text":"The elasticity of labour-power, or its capacity for greater inten-\n sive or extensive exertion, constitutes, within certain limits, a\n source, independent of the given volume of the means of production already\n functioning and produced, or of the material elements of constant\n capital, for the creation of additional wealth and hence of the accumu-\n lation fund. In extractive industry, mining, for example, the object of labour\n is naturally present. The necessary means of labour themselves being thus\n given,—and extractive industry itself in turn for the most part supplies the raw material of these\n instruments of labour, metals, wood, etc., and the auxiliary materials, such as coal,\n —the product is by no means limited by the extent of these\n means of labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"19.81","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0082","text":"They are merely worn out more rapidly by the greater ex-\n penditure of labour-power, and thus merely their period of repro-\n duction is shortened. The mass of products itself, such as coal\n and iron, on the other hand grows, other circumstances remaining the same, in pro-\n portion to the labour expended upon the natural object. As on\n the first day of production, the original creators of products,\n and hence also the creators of the material elements of capital, man and\n nature, here go together. In agriculture proper, seeds\n and manure admittedly play the same role as raw material in manufacture, and\n one cannot sow more land without first having more seed.","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"19.82","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0083","text":"But once this raw material and the instruments of labour are given, it is known\n what miraculous effect even the purely mechanical cultivation\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"590\", \"facs\": \"#f0609\"} --> of the soil, the intensity of which depends upon the exertion of labour-power,\n exercises upon the massiveness of the product. It is again the direct ef-\n fect of man upon the natural object which becomes the immediate source\n of wealth. Extractive industry and agriculture, on the other hand,\n supply manufacture with raw material and auxiliary materials, hence the material\n elements which here are presupposed by every greater expenditure of labour,\n while the instruments of labour proper, even in this sphere, by more exten-\n sive or intensive exertion of labour-power merely shorten their period of repro-\n duction.","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"19.83","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0084","text":"Thus, in incorporating the two original creators of\n wealth, labour-power and land, capital acquires in them factors of\n reproduction on an extended scale, and hence of accumulation, that are\n independent of its own material magnitude and elastic.","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"19.84","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0085","text":"Apart from the degree of exploitation of labour, the pro-\n duction of surplus-value, and hence the accumulation of capital,\n of whose formation surplus-value is the element, is essentially determined by\nthe productive power of labour.","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"19.85","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0086","text":"With the productive power of labour grows the mass of products\n in which a definite value, and hence also surplus-value of a given\n magnitude, is represented. With an unchanged, and even with a falling, rate of\n surplus-value, provided only that it falls more slowly than the productive power of labour\n rises, the mass of surplus product grows. With an unchanged division\n of it into revenue and surplus-capital, therefore, the consumption of the\n capitalist can grow without diminution of the accumulation fund. The propor-\n tional magnitude of the accumulation fund can even grow at the expense of the con-\n sumption fund, while the cheapening of commodities places at the\n capitalist’s disposal as many or more means of enjoyment as before.","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"19.86","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0087","text":"But with the growing productivity of labour, as we have\n seen, the cheapening of the worker, and hence a growing rate of surplus-\n value, goes hand in hand, even when real wages rise. They\n never rise proportionately to the productivity of labour. The same\nvariable capital-value therefore sets in motion more labour-power and hence more\n labour. The same constant capital-value is represented\n in more means of production, i.e. more instruments of labour, materials of labour and\n auxiliary materials, and hence provides both more creators of products and absorbers of value,\n or labour. With an unchanged and even diminishing\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"591\", \"facs\": \"#f0610\"} -->value of surplus-capital, accelerated accumulation therefore takes place.","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"19.87","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0088","text":"Not only is the scale of reproduction materially enlarged, but\n the production of surplus-value grows more rapidly than the value of\n surplus-capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"19.88","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0089","text":"The development of the productive power of labour also reacts upon\n the original capital, or the capital already situated in the process of pro-\n duction. One part of the functioning constant\n capital consists of instruments of labour, such as machinery, etc., which are consumed only\n over longer periods and therefore reproduced, or replaced by new\n specimens of the same kind. But one part of these instruments of labour\n dies off every year, or reaches the final goal of its productive func-\n tion. It is therefore every year in the stage of its periodical repro-\n duction or of replacement by new specimens of the same kind.","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"19.89","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0090","text":"If the\n productive power of labour has expanded in the birthplace of these instruments of labour,\n and it develops continuously with the uninterrupted\n flow of science and technology, more effective and, considering\n their capacity for performance, cheaper machines, tools, apparatuses,\n etc. take the place of the old. The old capital is reproduced\n in a more productive form, apart from the continu-\n ous detailed alteration of the existing instruments of labour. The\n other part of constant capital, raw material and auxiliary materials, is\n continuously reproduced within the year, that originating from agriculture for the most part\n annually. Every introduction of better methods, etc., therefore operates\n here almost simultaneously upon additional capital and capital already\n entering into operation.","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"19.90","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0091","text":"Every advance in chemistry not only multiplies\n the useful applications of the same material and thereby extends its spheres of\n investment with the growth of capital. It also teaches how to\n hurl the excrements of the process of production and consumption back into the circuit\n of the process of reproduction, thus creating new capital material without prior\n outlay of capital. Like the increased exploitation of the\n realm of nature through the mere higher exertion of labour-power,\n science forms a potentiality for expansion independent of the given magnitude of functioning\n capital. At the same time it reacts\n upon the part of the original capital which has entered its stage of renewal.\n Incorporated into its new form, it gratuitously incorporates the social progress\n accomplished behind the back of its old form.","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"19.91","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0092","text":"Admittedly, this\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"592\", \"facs\": \"#f0611\"} --> development of the productive power is at the same time accompanied by partial depre-\n ciation of functioning capitals. Insofar as this depreciation makes itself acutely\n felt through competition, the principal weight falls upon the worker,\n in whose increased exploitation the capitalist seeks compensation for the loss.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"19.92","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0093","text":"The analysis of relative surplus-value showed how the devel-\n opment of the social productive power of labour necessitates an ever grow-\n ing mass of constant capital set in motion by the same labour-power.\n With the wealth, or the abundance and effectiveness, of the labour objectified\n in machinery, etc., from which the worker proceeds as an already produced\n condition of the process of production, grows the mass of old capital-value\n which is preserved by the mere addition of new labour, i.e. new production\n of value, and in this sense reproduced. Compare, for ex-\n ample, an English spinner with an Indian one. For the sake of simplification,\n let the extension and intensity of the English and Indian working day be the same.","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"19.93","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0094","text":"In one day the English spinner transforms masses of cotton,\n spinning instruments, etc. many hundreds of times greater into yarn.\n He therefore also preserves in his product a capital-value many hundreds of times greater. Even if the value-product of his day’s labour, i.e. the\n value newly added by it to the means of production, were only equal\n to that of the Indian, his day’s labour would nevertheless result not only in a greater\n quantity of products, but in an infinitely greater value of products—\n old value, which he transfers to the new product and which can once more\n function as capital. “In 1782,” F. Engels instructs us,\n “the entire wool harvest of the preceding three years (in England) lay\n still unworked for want of workers, and would have had to remain so if the newly invented machinery\n had not come to the rescue and spun it” [Note: F.","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"19.94","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0095","text":"Engels: “The Condition of the Working Class in England”,\n p. 20.]. The labour objectified in the form of machinery\n naturally did not immediately stamp any human beings out of the ground, but\n it enabled a small number of workers, by the addition of relatively little\n living labour, not only to consume the wool productively and add\n new value to it, but to preserve its old value in the form of yarn, etc.\n It thereby simultaneously supplied the means and spur\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"593\", \"facs\": \"#f0612\"} --> for the expanded reproduction of wool. It is the natural gift of liv-\n ing labour to preserve old value while it creates new value.","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"19.95","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0096","text":"With the growth in the effectiveness, extent, and value of its means of produc-\n tion, therefore, with the accumulation accompanying the development of its productive power,\n labour preserves and perpetuates in ever new\n form an ever swelling capital-value [Note: Classical political economy, owing to its defective analysis of the labour-\n process and the process of valorization, never properly understood this important moment of reproduction,\n as can be seen, for example, in Ricardo. He says, for instance: Whatever the\n change in productive power, “One million men always produce the same value in manu-\n factures.” This is correct if the extension and degree of intensity of their\n labour are given.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"19.96","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0097","text":"But it does not prevent, and Ricardo overlooks this in certain\n conclusions, one million men, with different productive powers of their labour, from transforming very different masses of means of pro-\n duction into product, and hence preserving very different masses of value in their\n product; the product-values supplied by them are thus very different. Ricardo,\n incidentally, vainly attempted in that example to make clear to J. B. Say the distinction\n between use-value (which he here calls wealth, material wealth) and\n exchange-value. Say answers: “As for the difficulty raised by\n Mr.","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"19.97","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0098","text":"Ricardo in saying that, by more extended processes, one million per-\n sons can produce twice, three times as much wealth, without producing\n more values, this difficulty is not one when one considers,\n as one ought, production as an exchange in which one gives\n the productive services of one’s labour, land, and capitals, in order to obtain\n products. It is by means of these productive services that we acquire\n all the products that are in the world. Now … we are all the richer,\n our productive services have all the more value, inasmuch as they obtain in\n the exchange called production, a greater quantity of useful\n things.” (J. B. Say: “Lettres à M. Malthus. Paris 1820”, p. 168, 169.)","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"19.98","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0099","text":"The “difficulté”—it exists for him, not for Ricardo—which Say is to explain,\n is this: Why does the value of use-values not increase when their\n quantity grows in consequence of the increased productive power of labour? Answer:\n The difficulty is solved by obligingly calling use-value ex-\n change-value. Exchange-value is a thing that one way or\n another is connected with exchange. Let production, then, be called an\n “exchange” of labour and means of production for the product,\n and it is clear as water that one receives all the more exchange-value the more\n use-value production supplies. In other words: The more use-\n values, e.g. stockings, a working day supplies to the stocking manufacturer, the\nricher he is in stockings. Suddenly, however, Say recollects that “with the] . This natural force of\n [Running matter: I.","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"19.99","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0100","text":"38]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"594\", \"facs\": \"#f0613\"} --> Labour appears as the self-preserving power of capital, into which it is\nincorporated, just as its social productive powers appear as\nits properties and as the constant appropriation of surplus labour\nby the capitalist appears as the constant self-expansion of capi-\ntal. All powers of labour project themselves as powers of capital,\njust as all value-forms of the commodity project themselves as forms of money.","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"19.100","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0101","text":"Other circumstances remaining the same, the magnitude of the pro-\nduced surplus-value, and hence accumulation, is ultimately determined by the\nmagnitude of the capital advanced. With the total capi-\ntal, its variable component also grows, although not in the same pro-\nportion. The larger the scale on which the individual capitalist\nproduces, the greater the number of workers whom he simultaneously exploits,\nor the mass of unpaid labour which he appropriates [Note: We shall see in the Third Book that the average rate of pro-\nfit in different spheres of production is not affected by that of each of them] .","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"19.101","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0102","text":"The more\n[Note: greater quantity” of stockings their “price” (which naturally has nothing to do with exchange-\nvalue) falls, “parce que la concurrence les (les producteurs)\noblige à donner les produits pour ce qu’ils leur coûtent.” But where,\nthen, does profit come from, if the capitalist sells the commodities at the price that they cost\nhim? But never mind. Say explains that, as a consequence of increased produc-\ntivity, everyone now receives, in exchange for the same equivalent, two instead of formerly one\npair of stockings, etc. The result at which he arrives is precisely Ricardo’s proposition,\nwhich he wished to refute.","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"19.102","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0103","text":"After this mighty exertion of thought, he addresses\nMalthus triumphantly in the words: “Telle est, monsieur, la doctrine\nbien liée sans laquelle il est impossible, je le déclare, d’expliquer les plus\ngrandes difficultés de l’économie politique et notamment, comment il se peut\nqu’une nation soit plus riche lorsque ses produits diminuent de valeur, quoique\nla richesse soit de la valeur.” (l. c. p. 170.) An English economist remarks\non similar feats in Say’s “Lettres”: “These affected ways of talking” (“those affected ways of talking”)\non the whole constitute what Mr Say is pleased to call\nhis doctrine and which he commends to Malthus’s heart to\nteach at Hertford, as is already done ‘dans plusieurs parties de l’Europe’.","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"19.103","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0104","text":"He says: “Si vous trouvez une physionomie de paradoxe à toutes ces propo-\nsitions, voyez les choses qu’elles expriment, et j’ose croire qu’elles vous paraî-\ntront fort simples et fort raisonnables.” Doubtless, and at the same time, as a\nconsequence of the same process, they will appear anything but original or important.”\n(“An Inquiry into those Principles respecting the Nature of\nDemand etc.”, p. 116, 110.)]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"595\", \"facs\": \"#f0614\"} --> Thus, the more the individual capital grows, the larger the fund which\nsplits into a consumption fund and an accumulation fund. The capi-\ntalist can therefore live more briskly and at the same time “abstain” more.","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"19.104","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0105","text":"With the growth of capital, the difference be-\ntween capital employed and capital consumed grows. In other\nwords: the mass of value and material of the means of labour grows, such as\nbuildings, machinery, drainage pipes, draught animals, apparatus\nof every kind, which always function to their full extent, over a longer or\nshorter period, in constantly repeated processes of production, or serve\nfor the production of definite useful effects, while they wear out only\ngradually, hence lose their value only piecemeal,\nand therefore transfer it to the product only piecemeal. In the proportion\nin which these means of labour serve as formers of the product without adding\nvalue to the product, hence are entirely employed but only partially con-\nsumed, they render, as mentioned earlier, the same gratuitous service as natural\nforces, water, steam, air, electricity, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"19.105","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0106","text":"This gratuitous service\nof past labour, when seized upon and animated by living la-\nbour, accumulates with the growing scale of accumulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"19.106","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0107","text":"Since past labour always disguises itself as capital, i.e. since the\nliability of the labour of A, B, C, etc. figures as the asset of the non-worker X,\nbourgeois and political economists are full of praise for the mer-\nits of past labour, which, according to the Scottish genius\nMac Culloch, must even draw a wage of its own [Note: Mac Culloch promulgated the patent for “wages of past labour” long\nbefore Senior took out the patent for the “wages of abstinence”.] . The ever-growing\nweight of past labour co-operating in the living labour process under the form\nof means of production is thus vindicated as belonging to its alien-\nated form, its form as capital, which is alien to the worker himself,\nwhose past and unpaid labour it is.","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"19.107","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0108","text":"The practical\nagents of capitalist production and their ideological tongue-\nthrashers are just as incapable of thinking of the means of production as\nseparated from the antagonistic social character-mask which nowadays\n[Note: peculiar division of capital into constant and variable elements, and\nat the same time that this phenomenon only apparently contradicts the laws\nexpounded concerning the nature and production of surplus-value.]\n[Running matter: 38*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"596\", \"facs\": \"#f0615\"} --> adheres to it, as a slaveholder is incapable of thinking of the worker himself\nas separated from his character as slave.","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"19.108","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0109","text":"In the course of this section it has been seen that capital is not a\nfixed magnitude, but an elastic and constantly fluctuating part of social\nwealth, owing to the division of surplus-value into revenue and surplus\ncapital. It has further been seen that, even with the magnitude of the\nfunctioning capital given, the labour-power, science, and land incorporated in it\n(by which, economically, are to be understood all objects of labour present in\nnature without human intervention) form elastic powers of it, which within certain lim-\nits allow it a scope for action independent of its own magnitude. In so doing,\nall the circumstances of the process of circulation, which cause very different degrees\nof effectiveness of the same mass of capital, were left out of account.","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"19.109","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0110","text":"Since we presuppose the barriers of capitalist pro-\nduction, and therefore a purely spontaneously grown form of the social\nprocess of production, we left out of account every more rational combination\nimmediately and systematically practicable with the existing means of pro-\nduction and labour-powers. Classical economy always liked to conceive\nsocial capital as a fixed magnitude of fixed effec-\ntiveness. But the prejudice was first consolidated into a dogma by the arch-philistine\nJeremias Bentham, that sober, pe-\ndantic, loquacious oracle of common bourgeois understanding of the\nnineteenth century [Note: Cf., among others: J. Bentham: “Theorie des Peines et des Ré-\ncompenses, trad. Et. Dumont. 3ème éd.” Paris 1826, t. II, l. IV,\nch. II.] . Bentham is among philosophers what Martin\nTupper is among poets.","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"19.110","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0111","text":"Both could only have been manufactured in England [Note: Jeremias Bentham is a purely English phenomenon. Even excepting\nour philosopher Christian Wolf, at no time and in no country has the most\nbanal commonplace ever strutted about with such self-satisfied complacency. The\nprinciple of utility was not Bentham’s invention. He merely reproduced without spirit\nwhat Helvetius and other Frenchmen of the eighteenth century had said with\nwit. If, for example, one wishes to know what is useful to a dog,\none must investigate the nature of the dog. This nature itself cannot be constructed from\nthe “principle of utility.” Applied to man, if one wishes to judge\nall human deed, movement, relations, etc. according to the principle of utility-] .\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"597\", \"facs\": \"#f0616\"} --> With his dogma, the most ordinary phenomena of the process of produc-\ntion, such as, for example,","kind":"passage","sequence":111,"address":"19.111","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0112","text":"its sudden expansions and contrac-\ntions, indeed accumulation, become wholly incomprehensible [Note: “Political economists are too inclined to\ntreat a definite quantity of capital and a definite number of workers as instruments of production of uniform\nforce and as acting with a certain uniform intensity . . . . Those who maintain\nthat commodities are the sole agents of production prove that production cannot be\nexpanded at all, for in order for such an expansion to take place, means of subsistence, raw mate-\nrials, and tools would first have to be increased, which in fact amounts\nto saying that no growth of production can take place without its prior growth,\nor, in other words, that all growth is impossible.”\n(S. Bailey: “Money and its Vicissitudes”, p. 26 and 70.)","kind":"passage","sequence":112,"address":"19.112","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0113","text":"Bailey\ncriticizes the dogma chiefly from the standpoint of the process of circu-\nlation.] . The dogma was\nused for apologetic purposes both by Bentham himself and by Malthus, James Mill, MacCulloch, etc.,\nin particular in order to represent one part of\ncapital, the variable capital, or capital convertible into labour-\npower, as a fixed magnitude. The material existence\nof variable capital, i.e. the mass of means of subsistence which it represents for the\nworker, or the so-called labour fund, was turned into a special part\nof social wealth hemmed in and insurmountable by chains of\nnature. In order to set in motion that part of social\nwealth which is to function as constant capital or, expressed materially,\nas means of production, a definite mass of living labour is required.","kind":"passage","sequence":113,"address":"19.113","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0114","text":"This is\n[Note: principle, one must first deal with human nature in\ngeneral and then with human nature historically modified in every epoch. Bentham makes no ceremony.\nWith the most naïve dryness, he presupposes the modern philistine, specifically the English philistine,\nas the normal man. Whatever is useful to this fellow as normal man and to his\nworld is useful in and for itself. By this standard he then judges\npast, present, and future. For example, the Christian religion is “use-\nful” because it religiously proscribes the same misdeeds that the penal code juridically\ncondemns. Art criticism is “harmful” because it disturbs respectable people in their enjoyment\nof Martin Tupper, etc. With such rubbish the worthy man, whose\nmotto was “nulla dies sine linea,” filled mountains of books.","kind":"passage","sequence":114,"address":"19.114","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0115","text":"If I had the\ncourage of my friend H. Heine, I should call Mr Jeremias a genius of\nbourgeois stupidity.]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"598\", \"facs\": \"#f0617\"} --> given technologically. But neither is the number of workers given\nwhich is required to make this mass of labour fluid, for that varies with\nthe degree of exploitation of individual labour-power; nor is the price of this\nlabour-power given, but only its, moreover very elastic, minimum limit. The\nfacts underlying the dogma are these. On the one hand,\nthe worker has no say in the division of social\nwealth into means of enjoyment for non-workers and means of produc-\ntion. On the other hand, only in favourable exceptional cases can he extend\nthe so-called “labour fund” at the expense of the “revenue” of the rich\n[Note: J. St. Mill says in his “Principles of Polit.","kind":"passage","sequence":115,"address":"19.115","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0116","text":"Economy”:\n“The product of labour is nowadays distributed in inverse pro-\nportion to labour—the largest part to those who never labour, the\nnext largest to those whose labour is almost merely nominal, and so, on a descending\nscale, the reward shrinks, in proportion as labour becomes harder and\nmore disagreeable, until the most tiring and exhausting physical labour\ncannot with certainty count even on obtaining the necessities of life.” To avoid misunderstanding,\nI note that, while men such as J. St. Mill, Fawcett, etc. must be reproached for the contradiction\nbetween their old economic dogmas and their modern tendencies, it would be entirely wrong\nto throw them together with the rabble of vulgar-economic apologists.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":116,"address":"19.116","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0117","text":"To what absurd tautology the transfiguration of the capital-\nist barrier of the labour fund into its social natural barrier\nleads is shown, among others, by Professor\nFawcett: “The circulating capital [Note: H. Fawcett, Prof. of Polit. Econ. at Cambridge: “The\nEconomic Position of the British Labourer. Lond. 1865”, p. 120.] of a country,” he says,\n“is its labour fund. In order, therefore, to calculate the average money wage\nwhich every worker receives, we have simply to divide this capital\nby the number of the working population” [Note: I remind the reader here that the categories variable and\nconstant capital were first employed by me. Political economy\nsince A. Smith jumbles together the determinations contained therein with the formal distinctions\nof fixed and circulating capital arising from the process of\ncirculation.","kind":"passage","sequence":117,"address":"19.117","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0118","text":"More on this in\nthe Second Book, second chapter.] . That is,\nfirst we add together the actually paid individual wages into a\nsum, then maintain that this addition forms the sum of value\nof the “labour fund” decreed by God and nature. Finally,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"599\", \"facs\": \"#f0618\"} --> we divide the sum thus obtained by the number of workers,\nin order in turn to discover how much can fall to each worker individually\non average. An exceedingly ingenious procedure. It does not prevent\nMr Fawcett from saying in the same breath: “The total wealth annually\naccumulated in England is divided into two parts. One part is employed\nin England for the maintenance of our own industry.","kind":"passage","sequence":118,"address":"19.118","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0119","text":"Another part is exported to other countries … The\n part employed in our industry forms no considerable\n portion of the wealth annually accumulated in this coun-\n try” [Note: Fawcett l. c. pp. 123, 122.] . The greater part of the annually increasing surplus pro-\n duct, wrested from the English worker without equivalent, is thus\n capitalised not in England, but in foreign countries. But along with\n the surplus capital thus exported, is not also a part of the “labour fund”\n invented by God and Bentham exported [Note: One might say that not only capital, but also workers, in\n the form of emigration, are annually exported from England. In the text, however,\n there is no question at all of the peculium of the emigrants, who for the most part\n are not workers. The sons of tenant farmers supply large portions.","kind":"passage","sequence":119,"address":"19.119","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0120","text":"The English surplus capital annually sent abroad for in-\n terest stands in an incomparably greater proportion to annual accumu-\n lation than annual emigration does to the annual increase of the population.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":120,"address":"19.120","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_BVVM:P0120"]}],"source_digest":"792bfd7c38a53fc28afcb4e017ff734121814e9fdb6305af3a779618c38d5529","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG","title":"c) Das allgemeine Gesetz der kapitalistischen Accu- mulation. · reading part 1/2","order":20,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_CDAG","text":"## c) Das allgemeine Gesetz der kapitalistischen Accu-\n mulation.\n\n\n\n\nWachsthum des Kapitals schliesst Wachsthum seines vari-\n ablen oder in Arbeitskraft umgesetzten Bestandtheils ein. Ein Theil des in\n Surpluskapital verwandelten Mehrwerths muss stets rückverwandelt werden\n in variables Kapital oder zuschüssigen Arbeitsfonds. Unterstellen wir, dass,\n nebst sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen, die technologische Zusammensetzung\n des Kapitals unverändert bleibt, d. h. eine bestimmte Masse von Produk-\n tionsmitteln oder constantem Kapital stets von gleich viel lebendiger Arbeit\n in Bewegung gesetzt wird, so wächst die Nachfrage nach Arbeit und der\n Subsistenzfonds der Arbeiter verhältnissmässig mit dem Kapital und um so\n rascher, je rascher das Kapital wächst. Da der Mehrwerth ein jährliches\n Produkt des Kapitals ist, da er dem Originalkapital jährlich ein Increment\n zusetzt, da diess Increment selbst jährlich wächst mit dem jährlich zuneh-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"600\", \"facs\": \"#f0619\"} --> menden Umfang des bereits in Funktion begriffenen Kapitals, und da endlich,\n unter besonderem Sporn des Bereicherungstriebs, wie z. B. Oeffnung neuer\n Märkte, neuer Sphären der Kapitalanlage in Folge neu entwickelter gesell-\n schaftlicher Bedürfnisse u. s. w., die Stufenleiter der Accumulation\n plötzlich ausdehnbar ist durch bloss veränderte Theilung des Mehr-\n werths oder Mehrprodukts in Kapital und Revenue, so treten hier\n Knotenpunkte ein, wo das Wachsthum der Arbeitskraft oder der Arbeiter-\n anzahl überflügelt wird von den Accumulationsbedürfnissen des Kapitals, und\n daher der Preis der Arbeit steigt. Klage hierüber ertönt in England wäh-\n rend der ganzen ersten Hälfte des achtzehnten Jahrhunderts. Die mehr\n oder minder günstigen Umstände, worunter sich die Lohnarbeiter repro-\n duciren und vermehren, ändern jedoch nichts an dem Verhältniss selbst.\n Wie die einfache Reproduktion fortwährend das Kapitalver-\n hältniss selbst reproducirt, Kapitalisten auf der einen Seite, Lohn-\n arbeiter auf der andern, so reproducirt die Reproduktion auf er-\n weiterter Stufenleiter oder die Accumulation das Kapi-\n talverhältniss auf erweiterter Stufenleiter, mehr Kapita-\n listen oder grössere Kapitalisten auf diesem Pol, mehr Lohnarbeiter auf\n jenem. Man sah früher bereits: Die Reproduktion der Arbeitskraft, die\n sich dem Kapital unaufhörlich als Verwerthungsmittel einverleiben muss,\n nicht von ihm loskommen kann, und deren Hörigkeit zum Kapital nur ver-\n steckt wird durch den Wechsel der individuellen Kapitalisten, woran sie\n sich verkauft, bildet in der That ein Moment der Reproduktion des Kapi-\n tals selbst. Accumulation des Kapitals ist also Vermeh-\n rung des Proletariats [Note: Karl Marx l. c. „A égalité d’oppression des masses, plus un pays a de\n prolétaires et plus il est riche.“ (Colins: „L’Economie Politique.\n Source des Révolutions et des Utopies prétendues Socialistes.\n Paris 1857“, t. III, p. 331.) Unter „Proletarier“ ist ökonomisch nichts zu ver-\n stehn als der Lohnarbeiter, der „Kapital“ producirt und verwerthet und aufs\n Pflaster geworfen wird, sobald er für die Verwerthungsbedürfnisse des „Mon-\n sieur Kapital“, wie Dr. Pecqueur diese Person nennt, überflüssig ist.\n „Der kränkliche Proletarier des Urwalds“ ist ein schönes Roscher’-\n sches Phantom. Der Urwäldler ist Eigenthümer des Urwalds, und behandelt den\n Urwald, ganz so ungenirt wie der Orang Utang, als sein Eigenthum. Er ist also\n nicht Proletarier. Diess wäre nur der Fall, wenn der Urwald ihn, statt er den] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"601\", \"facs\": \"#f0620\"} -->\n\nDie klassische Oekonomie begriff diesen Satz so wohl, dass A. Smith,\n Ricardo u. s. w., wie früher erwähnt, die Accumulation sogar fälschlich\n identificiren mit Konsum des ganzen kapitalisirten Theils des Mehrpro-\n dukts durch produktive Arbeiter, oder mit seiner Verwandlung in zuschüs-\n sige Lohnarbeiter. Schon 1696 sagt John Bellers: „Wenn Jemand\n 100,000 Acres hätte und eben so viele Pfunde Geld und eben so viel Vieh,\n was wäre der reiche Mann ohne den Arbeiter ausser selbst ein\n Arbeiter? Und wie die Arbeiter Leute reich machen, so desto\n mehr Arbeiter, desto mehr Reiche … Die Arbeit des Armen ist\n die Mine des Reichen“ [Note: „As the Labourers make men rich, so the more Labourers, there will be\n the more rich men … the Labour of the Poor being the Mines of the Rich.“\n (John Bellers l. c. p. 2.)] . So Bertrand de Mandeville im Anfang\n des 18. Jahrhunderts: „Wo das Eigenthum hinreichend geschützt ist,\n wäre es leichter ohne Geld zu leben als ohne Arme, denn wer würde\n die Arbeit thun? … Wie die Arbeiter vor Aushungerung zu bewahren\n sind, so sollten sie nichts erhalten, was der Ersparung werth ist. Wenn hier\n und da Einer aus der untersten Klasse durch ungewöhnlichen Fleiss und\n Bauchkneipen sich über die Lage erhebt, worin er aufgebracht war, so muss\n ihn keiner daran hindern: ja es ist unläugbar der weiseste Plan für jede\n Privatperson, für jede Privatfamilie in der Gesellschaft frugal zu sein, aber\n es ist das Interesse aller reichen Nationen, dass der grösste\n Theil der Armen nie unthätig sei und sie dennoch stets verausgaben, was\n sie einnehmen … Diejenigen, die ihr Leben durch ihre tägliche Arbeit\n gewinnen, haben nichts, was sie anstachelt dienstlich zu sein ausser ihren\n Bedürfnissen, welche es Klugheit ist zu lindern, aber Narrheit wäre zu\n kuriren. Das einzige Ding, das den arbeitenden Mann fleissig machen\n kann, ist ein mässiger Arbeitslohn. Ein zu geringer macht ihn je nach\n seinem Temperament kleinmüthig oder verzweifelt, ein zu grosser insolent\n und faul … Aus dem bisher Entwickelten folgt, dass in einer freien\n Nation, wo Sklaven nicht erlaubt sind, der sicherste Reich-\n thum aus einer Menge arbeitsamer Armen besteht. Aus-\n [Note: Urwald exploitirte. Was seinen Gesundheitszustand betrifft, steht der wohl den\n Vergleich aus nicht nur mit dem des modernen Proletariers, sondern auch dem\n der syphilitischen und skrophulösen „Ehrbarkeit“. Doch versteht Herr Wil-\n helm Roscher unter Urwald wahrscheinlich die stammverwandte Lüneburger Heide.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"602\", \"facs\": \"#f0621\"} --> serdem dass sie die nie versagende Zufuhrquelle für Flotte und Armee, gäbe\n es ohne sie keinen Genuss und wäre das Produkt keines Landes verwerth-\n bar. Um die Gesellschaft (die natürlich aus den Nichtarbeitern be-\n steht) glücklich und das Volk selbst in kümmerlichen Zuständen zu-\n frieden zu machen, ist es nöthig, dass die grosse Majorität\n sowohl unwissend als arm bleibt. Kenntniss erweitert und ver-\n vielfacht unsere Wünsche, und je weniger ein Mann wünscht, desto leich-\n ter können seine Nothwendigkeiten befriedigt werden“ [Note: B. de Mandeville: „The Fable of the Bees. 5th ed. Lond.\n 1728“, Remarks, p. 212, 213, 328.) „Mässiges Leben und beständige Arbeit\n sind für den Armen der Weg zum materiellen Glücke (worunter er möglichst\n langen Arbeitstag und möglichst wenig Lebensmittel versteht) und zum Reich-\n thum für den Staat (nämlich Grundeigenthümer, Kapitalisten und ihre poli-\n tischen Würdeträger und Agenten).“ („An Essay on Trade and Com-\n merce. Lond. 1770“, p. 54.)] . Was Mande-\n ville, ein ehrlicher Mann und heller Kopf, noch nicht begreift, ist, dass der\n Mechanismus des Accumulationsprozesses selbst mit dem Kapital die\n Masse der „arbeitsamen Armen“ vermehrt, d. h. der Lohnarbeiter,\n die ihre Arbeitskraft in wachsende Verwerthungskraft des wachsenden\n Kapitals verwandeln und eben dadurch ihr Abhängigkeitsverhält-\n niss von ihrem eignen, im Kapitalisten personificirten Produkt verewigen\n müssen. Mit Bezug auf diess Abhängigkeitsverhältniss bemerkt Sir\n F. M. Eden in seiner „Lage der Armen, oder Geschichte der arbeiten-\n den Klasse Englands“: „Unsere Zone erfordert Arbeit zur Befriedigung\n der Bedärfnisse, und desshalb muss wenigstens ein Theil der Ge-\n sellschaft unermüdet arbeiten … Einige, die nicht arbeiten,\n haben dennoch die Produkte des Fleisses zu ihrer Verfügung. Das ver-\n danken diese Eigenthümer aber nur der Civilisation und Ordnung,\n sie sind reine Kreatur der Civilinstitutionen [Note: Eden hätte fragen sollen, wessen Kreatur sind denn „die Civilinstitu-\n tionen“? Vom Standpunkt der juristischen Illusion betrachtet er nicht das\n Gesetz als Produkt der materiellen Produktionsverhältnisse, sondern umgekehrt\n die Produktionsverhältnisse als Produkt des Gesetzes. Linguet warf Montes-\n quieu’s illusorischen „Esprit des Lois“ mit dem einen Wort über den\n Haufen: „L’esprit des lois, c’est la propriété.“] . Denn diese\n haben es anerkannt, dass man die Früchte der Arbeit auch an-\n ders als durch Arbeit sich aneignen kann. Die Leute von\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"603\", \"facs\": \"#f0622\"} --> unabhängigem Vermögen verdanken ihr Vermögen fast ganz der Ar-\n beit Andrer, nicht ihrer eignen Fähigkeit, die durchaus nicht besser\n ist als die der Andern; es ist nicht der Besitz von Land und\n Geld, sondern das Kommando über Arbeit („the command of la-\n bour“), das die Reichen von den Armen unterscheidet … Was dem\n Armen zusagt, ist nicht eine verworfene oder servile Lage, sondern ein\nbequemes und liberales Abhängigkeitsverhältniss („a\n state of easy and liberal dependance“), und für die Leute von Eigenthum\n hinreichender Einfluss und Autorität über die, die für sie arbeiten …\n Ein solches Abhängigkeitsverhältniss ist, wie jeder Kenner der mensch-\n lichen Natur weiss, nothwendig für den Komfort der Arbeiter\n selbst“ [Note: Eden l. c. v. I, l. I, ch. I und Preface.] . Sir F. M. Eden, beiläufig bemerkt, ist der einzige\n Schüler Adam Smith’s, der während des achtzehnten Jahrhunderts\n etwas Bedeutendes geleistet hat [Note: Sollte der Leser an Malthus erinnern, dessen „Essay on Popu-\n lation“ 1798 erschien, so erinnere ich, dass diese Schrift in ihrer ersten Form\n (und die späteren Ausgaben stopften nur Material in das alte Schema und fügten\nneues, aber nicht von Malthus entdecktes, sondern nur annexirtes zu) nichts\n als ein schülerhaft oberflächliches und pfäffisch verdeklamirtes Plagiat aus Sir\n James Steuart, Townsend, Franklin, Wallace u. s. w. ist und nicht einen\n einzigen selbstgedachten Satz enthält. Nebenbei bemerkt. Obgleich\n Malthus Pfaffe der englischen Hochkirche, hatte er das Mönchsgelübde des Cöli-\n bats abgelegt. Diess ist nämlich eine der Bedingungen der fellowship der prote-\n stantischen Universität zu Cambridge. „Socios collegiorum maritos esse\n non permittimus, sed statim postquam quis uxorem duxerit,\n socius collegii desinatesse.“ („Reports of Cambridge Univer-\n sity Commission“, p. 172.) Dieser Umstand unterscheidet Malthus vortheil-\n haft von den andern protestantischen Pfaffen, die das katholische Gebot des\n Priestercölibats von sich selbst abgeschüttelt und das „Seid fruchtbar und mehret\n euch“ in solchem Mass als ihre specifisch biblische Mission vindicirt haben, dass\n sie überall in wahrhaft unanständigem Grad zur Vermehrung der Bevölkerung bei-\n tragen, während sie gleichzeitig den Arbeitern das „Populationsprincip“ predigen.\n Es ist charakteristisch, dass der ökonomisch travestirte Sündenfall, der Adams-\n apfel, der „urgent appetite“, „the checks which tend to blunt the shafts of\n Cupid“, wie Pfaff Townsend munter sagt, dass dieser kitzliche Punkt von den\n Herrn von der protestantischen Theologie oder vielmehr Kirche monopoli-\n sirt ward und wird. Mit Ausnahme des venetianischen Mönches Ortes, eines\n originellen und geistreichen Schriftstellers, sind die meisten Populationslehrer] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"604\", \"facs\": \"#f0623\"} -->\n\nUnter den bisher unterstellten, den Arbeitern günstigsten Accumula-\n tionsbedingungen kleidet sich ihr Abhängigkeitsverhältniss vom\n [Note: protestantische Pfaffen. So Bruckner: „Théorie du Système\n animal. Leyde 1767, worin die ganze moderne Bevölkerungstheorie erschöpft\n ist und wozu der vorübergehende Zank zwischen Quesnay und seinem Schüler,\nMirabeau père, über dasselbe Thema Ideen lieferte, dann Pfaffe Wallace,\n Pfaffe Townsend, Pfaffe Malthus und sein Schüler, der Erzpfaff Th. Chal-\n mers, von kleineren pfäffischen Skribenten in this line gar nicht zu reden. Ur-\n sprünglich ward die politische Oekonomie betrieben von Philosophen, wie Hobbes,\n Locke, Hume, Geschäfts- und Staatsleuten, wie Thomas Morus, Temple, Sully,\n de Witt, North, Law, Vanderlint, Cantillon, Franklin, und theoretisch namentlich,\n und mit dem grössten Erfolg, von Medizinern wie Petty, Barbon, Mandeville,\n Quesnay. Noch Mitte des 18. Jahrhunderts entschuldigt sich Rev. Mr. Tucker,\n ein bedeutender Oekonom für seine Zeit, dass er sich mit dem Mammon beschäf-\n tigt. Später, und zwar mit dem „Bevölkerungsprincip“ schlug die Stunde der\n protestantischen Pfaffen. Als ob er diese Geschäftsverpfuschung geahnt, sagt\nPetty, der die Population als Basis des Reichthums behandelt, und, gleich Adam\n Smith, abgesagter Pfaffenfeind: „Die Religion blüht am besten, wenn die\n Priester am meisten kasteit werden, wie das Gesetz am besten, wo die Advokaten\n verhungern.“ Er räth daher den protestantischen Pfaffen, wenn sie einmal dem\n Apostel Paulus nicht folgen und sich nicht durch das Cölibat „mortificiren“\n wollen, „doch ja nicht mehr Pfaffen zu hecken („not to breed more Churchmen“)\n als die vorhandenen Pfründen (benefices) absorbiren können; d. h. wenn es nur\n 12,000 Pfründen in England und Wales giebt, ist es unweis 24,000 Pfaffen zu\n hecken („it will not be safe to breed 24,000 ministers“), denn die 12,000 Unver-\n sorgten werden stets einen Lebensunterhalt zu gewinnen suchen, und wie könnten\n sie das leichter thun, als indem sie unter das Volk gehn und es überreden, die\n 12,000 Pfründner vergifteten die Seelen, und hungerten selbige Seelen aus, und\n zeigten ihnen den Holzweg zum Himmel?“ (Petty: „A Treatise on Taxes\n and Contributions. Lond. 1667“, p. 57.) Adam Smith’s Stellung\n zum protestantischen Pfaffenthum seiner Zeit ist durch folgendes charakterisirt.\n In: „A Letter to A. Smith, L. L. D. On the Life, Death and Phi-\n losophy of his Friend David Hume. By One of the People ealled\n Christians. 4th ed. Oxford 1784“, kanzelt Dr. Horne, hochkirchlicher\n Bischof von Norwich, den A. Smith ab, weil er in einem öffentlichen Sendschreiben\n an Herrn Strahan, seinen „Freund David (sc. Hume) einbalsamire“, weil er dem\n Publikum erzählte, wie „Hume auf seinem Sterbebett sich mit Lucian und Whist\n amüsirte“, und sogar die Frechheit hatte zu schreiben: „Ich habe Hume stets, so-\n wohl während seines Lebens wie nach seinem Tode so nahe dem Ideal eines voll-\n kommen weisen und tugendhaften Mannes betrachtet, als die Schwäche der mensch-\n lichen Natur erlaubt.“ Der Bischof ruft entrüstet: „Ist es recht von Ihnen,] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"605\", \"facs\": \"#f0624\"} --> Kapital in erträgliche oder, wie Eden sagt, „bequeme und liberale For-\n men“. Statt intensiver zu werden mit dem Wachsthum des Kapitals, wird\n es nur extensiver, d. h. die Exploitations- und Herrschaftssphäre des Kapi-\n tals dehnt sich nur aus mit seiner eignen Dimension und der Anzahl seiner\n Lohnarbeiter. Von ihrem eignen anschwellenden und schwellend in\n Surpluskapital verwandelten Mehrprodukt strömt ihnen ein reichlicherer Theil\n in der Form von Zahlungsmitteln zurück, so dass sie den Kreis ihrer Genüsse\n erweitern, ihren Konsumtionsfonds von Kleidern, Möbeln u. s. w. besser aus-\n statten und kleine Reservefonds von Geld bilden können. So wenig aber bessere\n Kleidung, Nahrung, Behandlung und ein grösseres Peculium das Abhängig-\n keitsverhältniss und die Exploitation des Sklaven aufheben, so wenig die\n des Lohnarbeiters. Steigender Preis der Arbeit in Folge der Accumula-\n tion des Kapitals besagt in der That nur, dass der Umfang und die Wucht\n der goldnen Kette, die der Lohnarbeiter sich selbst bereits geschmiedet\n hat, ihre losere Spannung erlauben. In den Kontroversen über diesen\n Gegenstand hat man meist die Hauptsache übersehn, nämlich die differentia\n [Note: mein Herr, uns als vollkommen weis und tugendhaft den Charakter und Lebens-\n wandel eines Menschen zu schildern, der von einer unheilbaren Antipathie besessen\n war wider alles, was Religion heisst, und der jeden Nerv anspannte, um so viel\n an ihm selbst ihren Namen aus dem Gedachtniss der Menschen zu löschen?“\n (l. c. p. 8.) „Aber lasst euch nicht entmuthigen. Liebhaber der Wahrheit, der\n Atheismus ist kurzlebig.“ (p. 17.) Adam Smith „hat die grässliche Ruchlosig-\n keit („the atrocious wickedness“) den Atheismus durch das Land zu propagan-\n diren (nämlich durch seine „Theory of moral sentiments“) … Wir kennen\n Eure Schliche, Herr Doktor! Ihr meint’s gut, rechnet aber diessmal ohne den\n Wirth. Ihr wollt uns durch das Beispiel von David Hume, Esq., weismachen,\n dass Atheismus der einzige Schnaps („cordial“) für ein niedergeschlagnes Gemüth\n und das einzige Gegengift wider Todesfurcht ist … Lacht nur über Babylon\n in Ruinen und beglückwünscht nur den verhärteten Bösewicht Pharao!“ (l. c.\n p. 21, 22.) Ein orthodoxer Kopf unter A. Smith’s Kollegienbesuchern schreibt\n nach dessen Tod: „Smith’s Freundschaft für Hume verhinderte ihn ein Christ zu\n sein … Er glaubte Hume alles aufs Wort. Wenn Hume ihm gesagt, der Mond\n sei ein grüner Käs, er hätt’s geglaubt. Er glaubte ihm daher auch, dass es keinen\n Gott und keine Mirakel gebe … In seinen politischen Principien streifte er an\n Republikanismus.“ („The Bee.“ By James Anderson. 18 vls. Edinb.\n 1791—93.) Pfaff Th. Chalmers hat A. Smith in Verdacht, dass er aus reiner\n Malice die Kategorie der „unproduktiven Arbeiter“ eigens für die protestantischen\n Pfaffen erfand, trotz ihrer gesegneten Arbeit im Weinberg des Herrn.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"606\", \"facs\": \"#f0625\"} --> specifica der kapitalistischen Produktion. Arbeitskraft wird hier gekauft,\n nicht um durch ihren Dienst oder ihr Produkt die persönlichen Bedürf-\n nisse des Käufers zu befriedigen. Sein Zweck ist Verwerthung\n seines Kapitals, Produktion von Waaren, die mehr Arbeit enthalten als er\n zahlt, also einen Werththeil enthalten, der ihm nichts kostet und den-\n noch durch den Waarenverkauf realisirt wird. Produktion von\n Mehrwerth oder Plusmacherei ist das absolute Gesetz dieser Produktionsweise.\n Nur soweit sie die Produktionsmittel als Kapital erhält, ihren eignen Werth\n als Kapital reproducirt und in unbezahlter Arbeit eine Quelle von Surplus-\n kapital liefert, ist die Arbeitskraft verkaufbar. Die Bedingungen ihres Ver-\n kaufs, ob mehr oder minder günstig für den Arbeiter, schliessen also die\n Nothwendigkeit ihres steten Wiederverkaufs und die stets erweiterte Repro-\n duktion des Reichthums als Kapital ein. Der Arbeitslohn, wie man\n gesehn, bedingt seiner Natur nach, stets Lieferung eines bestimmten Quan-\n tums unbezahlter Arbeit auf Seiten des Arbeiters. Ganz abgesehn vom Stei-\n gen des Arbeitslohns mit sinkendem Preis der Arbeit u. s. w.,\n besagt seine Zunahme im besten Fall nur quantitative Abnahme der\n unbezahlten Arbeit, die der Arbeiter leisten muss. Diese Abnahme kann\n nie bis zu einem Punkt fortgehn, wo sie den kapitalistischen Charakter des\n Produktionsprozesses ernsthaft gefährden würde und die Reproduktion\n seiner eignen Bedingungen, auf der einen Seite der Produktions- und\n Lebensmittel als Kapital, auf der andern der Arbeitskraft als Waare, auf\n dem einen Pol des Kapitalisten, auf dem andern des Lohnarbeiters. Abge-\n sehn von gewaltsamen Konflikten über die Rate des Arbeitslohns, und\nAdam Smith hat bereits gezeigt, dass im Grossen und Ganzen in solchem\n Konflikt der Meister stets Meister bleibt, unterstellt ein aus Accumulation\n des Kapitals entspringendes Steigen des Arbeitspreises folgende Alter-\n native. Entweder der steigende oder gestiegne Preis der Arbeit ist be-\n gleitet von gleich grossem oder grösserem absoluten Wachsthum der Accu-\n mulation. Man weiss, dass selbst unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umstän-\n den, wie Produktivgrad der Arbeit u. s. w., mit der wachsenden Grösse\n des vorgeschossenen Kapitals sein absoluter Zuwachs\n gleichförmig bleiben oder selbst beschleunigt werden kann, obgleich die Rate\n der Accumulation abnimmt, wie man Kapitel III, Abschnitt 5 sah,\n dass die Masse des Mehrwerths trotz dessen abnehmender Rate mit\n Vermehrung der gleichzeitig exploitirten Arbeiterzahl gleichbleiben und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"607\", \"facs\": \"#f0626\"} --> selbst wachsen kann. In diesem Fall ist es blosse Tautologie zu sagen,\n dass der verminderte Exploitationsgrad der Arbeitskraft die Ausdehnung\n der Kapitalherrschaft nicht beeinträchtigt. Oder, das ist die andre\n Seite der Alternative, die Accumulation erschlafft in Folge des steigenden\n Arbeitspreises, weil der Stachel grossen Gewinns abstumpft. Die\n Accumulation nimmt ab. Aber mit ihrer Abnahme verschwindet die Ur-\n sache ihrer Abnahme, nämlich die Disproportion zwischen Kapital und\n exploitabler Arbeitskraft. Der Arbeitspreis sinkt also wieder zu einem\nden Verwerthungsbedürfnissen des Kapitals entsprechen-\n den Niveau. Es folgt daher keineswegs, dass der Arbeitslohn auf\n sein Minimalniveau fällt, oder auch nur auf das Niveau, worauf er vor\n der Preiserhöhung der Arbeit stand. Der Mechanismus des kapitalisti-\n schen Produktionsprozesses beseitigt selbst die Hindernisse, die er vorüber-\n gehend schafft. Man sieht: Im ersten Fall ist es keine Abnahme im\n absoluten oder proportionellen Wachsthum der Arbeitskraft oder Arbeits-\n bevölkerung, welche das Kapital überschüssig, sondern umgekehrt die\n Zunahme des Kapitals, welche die exploitable Arbeitskraft unzureichend\n macht. Im zweiten Fall ist es keine Zunahme im absoluten oder propor-\n tionellen Wachsthum der Arbeitskraft oder der Arbeiterbevölkerung, welche\n das Kapital unzureichend, sondern umgekehrt die Abnahme des Kapitals,\n welche die exploitable Arbeitskraft, oder vielmehr ihren Preis, überschüssig\n macht. Es sind diese absoluten Bewegungen in der Accumula-\n tion des Kapitals, welche sich als relative Bewegungen in der\n Masse der exploitablen Arbeitskraft wiederspiegeln und daher der\n eignen Bewegung der letztern geschuldet scheinen. So drückt\n sich in der Krisenphase des industriellen Cyklus der allgemeine Fall der Waa-\n renpreise als Steigen des relativen Geldwerths, und in der Pros-\n peritätsphase das allgemeine Steigen der Waarenpreise als Fall des rela-\n tiven Geldwerths aus. Die s. g. Currency-Schule schliesst\n daher, dass das einemal zu wenig, das andremal zu viel Geld cirkulirt.\n Ihre Ignoranz und völlige Verkennung der Thatsachen [Note: Vgl. Karl Marx: „Zur Kritik der politischen Oekonomie“,\n p. 165 sqq.]  finden würdige\n Parallele in den Oekonomen, welche jene Phänomene der Accumulation\n dahin deuten, dass das einemal zu wenig und das andremal zu viel Lohn-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"608\", \"facs\": \"#f0627\"} --> arbeiter existiren. Das so in ein Naturgesetz mystificirte Gesetz der kapi-\n talistischen Accumulation drückt in der That nur aus, dass ihre Natur\n jede solche Abnahme im Exploitationsgrad der Arbeit oder jede solche\n Steigerung des Arbeitspreises ausschliesst, welche die stetige Reproduktion\n des Kapitalverhältnisses und seine Reproduktion auf stets erweiterter\n Stufenleiter ernsthaft gefährden könnte. Es kann nicht anders sein in\n einer Produktionsweise, worin der Arbeiter für die Verwerthungsbe-\n dürfnisse vorhandner Werthe, statt umgekehrt der gegenständliche Reich-\n thum für die Entwicklungsbedürfnisse des Arbeiters da ist. Wie der\n Mensch in der Religion vom Machwerk seines eignen Kopfes, so wird er in\n der kapitalistischen Produktion vom Machwerk seiner eignen Hand be-\n herrscht.\n\n\n\n\nDas bisher Entwickelte gilt unter der Voraussetzung, dass im Fort-\n gang der Accumulation das Verhältniss zwischen der Masse\n der Produktionsmittel und der Masse der sie bewegen-\n den Arbeitskraft gleichbleibt, also die Nachfrage nach\n Arbeit mit dem Wachsthum des Kapitals verhältnissmässig\n wächst. Diese Voraussetzung figurirt in Adam Smith’s Analyse der\n Accumulation als selbstverständliches Axiom. In gewissem Grad bleibt sie\n immer richtig, denn wie auch die technologischen Bedingungen des Pro-\n duktionsprozesses umgewälzt werden mögen, während kürzerer oder\n längerer Zeitfrist findet bald in dieser, bald in jener Produktions-\n sphäre Accumulation des Kapitals oder Erweiterung der Produktionsleiter\nauf der einmal gegebnen technologischen Basis statt. Inner-\n halb dieser Schranken wächst also die Nachfrage nach Arbeit mit der\n Accumulation. Aber die vorhandne Basis wird selbst fortwährend umge-\n wälzt. Im Fortgang der Accumulation geht eine grosse Revolution vor\n im Verhältniss von Masse der Produktionsmittel und Masse der sie\n bewegenden Arbeitskraft. Diese Revolution spiegelt sich wieder in der\n wechselnden Zusammensetzung des Kapitalwerths aus\n constantem und variablem Bestandtheil, oder im wechseln-\n den Verhältniss seiner in Produktionsmittel und Arbeitskraft umgesetzten\nWerththeile. Ich nenne diese Zusammensetzung die organische\n Zusammensetzung des Kapitals.\n\n\n\n\nAbgesehn vom Produktivgrad der Arbeit, soweit er ausschliesslich\n durch den Naturreichthum, wie Fruchtbarkeit des Bodens u. s. w., bedingt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"609\", \"facs\": \"#f0628\"} --> ist, oder durch das Geschick unabhängiger und isolirt arbeitender Produ-\n centen, das sich jedoch mehr qualitativ in der Güte des Machwerks als\n quantitativ in seiner Masse bewährt, drückt sich der gesellschaft-\n liche Produktivgrad der Arbeit aus im relativen Grössen-\n umfang der Produktionsmittel, welche ein Arbeiter, während\n gegebner Zeit, mit derselben Anspannung von Arbeitskraft, in Produkt\n verwandelt. Die Masse der Produktionsmittel, womit er funktionirt,\n wächst mit der Produktivität seiner Arbeit. Diese Produktionsmittel spie-\n len dabei eine doppelte Rolle. Das Wachsthum der einen ist Folge,\n das der andern Bedingung der wachsenden Produktivität der Arbeit.\n Z. B. mit der manufakturmässigen Theilung der Arbeit und der Anwen-\n dung von Maschinerie wird in derselben Zeit mehr Rohmaterial verarbeitet,\n tritt also grössere Masse von Rohmaterial und Hilfsstoffen in den Arbeits-\n prozess ein. Das ist Folge der wachsenden Produktivität der Arbeit.\n Andrerseits ist die Masse der angewandten Maschinerie, Arbeitsviehs, mi-\n neralischen Düngers, Drainirungsröhren u. s. w. Bedingung der wach-\n senden Produktivität der Arbeit. Ebenso die Masse der in Baulichkeiten,\n Riesenöfen, Transportmitteln u. s. w. koncentrirten Produktionsmittel, welche\n trotz erweitertem Umfang in Folge ihres gemeinsamen Verbrauchs weniger\n Werth an jeden aliquoten Theil des Gesammtprodukts abgeben oder ökonomi-\n schere Verwendung erláuben als die in Diminutivformat zersplitterten Arbeits-\n mittel derselben Art. Ob aber Bedingung oder Folge, der wachsende\n Grössenumfang der Produktionsmittel im Vergleich zu der ihnen einverleib-\n ten Arbeitskraft drückt die wachsende Produktivität der Arbeit aus.\n Die Zunahme der letzteren erscheint also in der Abnahme der Arbeits-\n masse verhältnissmässig zu der von ihr bewegten Masse von\n Produktionsmitteln, oder in der Grössenabnahme des subjektiven Fak-\n tors des Arbeitsprozesses verglichen mit seinen objektiven Faktoren.\n\n\n\n\nDas Wachsthum in der Masse der Produktionsmittel, ver-\n glichen mit der Masse der sie belebenden Arbeitskraft, spiegelt\n sich wieder in der Zunahme des constanten Bestandtheils\n des Kapital werths auf Kosten seines variablen Bestand-\n theils. Es werden z. B. von einem Kapital, prozentweis berechnet ursprüng-\n lich je 50 Pfd. St. in Produktionsmitteln und je 50 Pfd. St. in Arbeitskraft,\n später, mit der Entwicklung des Produktivgrads der Arbeit, je 80 Pfd. St. in\n Produktionsmitteln und je 20 Pfd. St. in Arbeitskraft ausgelegt u. s. w. Wir\n [Running matter: I. 39] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"610\", \"facs\": \"#f0629\"} --> sagen „spiegelt sich wieder“, weil die Abnahme des variablen Kapitaltheils\n gegenüber dem constanten, oder die veränderte Zusammensetzung\n des Kapitalwerths, nur annähernd den Wechsel in der Zusam-\n mensetzung seiner stofflichen Bestandtheile anzeigt. Wenn z. B.\n heute der in der Spinnerei angelegte Kapitalwerth zu ⅞ constant und ⅛ vari-\n abel ist, während er Anfang des 18. Jahrhunderts ½ constant und ½ variabel\n war, so ist dagegen die Masse von Rohstoff, Arbeitsmitteln u. s. w., die ein\n bestimmtes Quantum Spinnarbeit heute produktiv konsumirt, viel hundertmal\n grösser als im Anfang des 18. Jahrhunderts. Der Grund ist einfach der, dass\n mit der wachsenden Produktivität der Arbeit nicht nur der Umfang der von\n ihr vernutzten Produktionsmittel steigt, sondern deren Werth, verglichen\n mit ihrem Umfang, fällt. Ihr Werth steigt also absolut, aber nicht propor-\n tionell mit ihrem Umfang. Das Wachsthum der Differenz zwischen\n constantem und variablem Kapitaltheil ist daher viel kleiner als\n das der Differenz zwischen der Masse der Produktionsmittel, worin das con-\n stante, und der Masse Arbeitskraft, worin das variable Kapital umgesetzt wird.\n Die erstere Differenz nimmt zu mit der letzteren, aber in viel geringerem\n Grad. Es giebt andre Umstände, welche das Verhältniss zwischen\n Werth der Produktionsmittel und Werth der sie bewegenden Arbeits-\n kraft von dem technologischen Verhältniss ihrer Massen abweichen\n machen. Diese modificirenden Umstände können wir erst im Dritten Buch\n betrachten. Die Rücksichtnahme darauf ist hier aber auch überflüssig,\n da die Abnahme des variablen Kapitaltheils gegen den constanten im\n Grossen und Ganzen, wenn auch nur andeutungsweise, die Zunahme der\n von derselben Masse Arbeitskraft in Bewegung gesetzten oder produktiv\n konsumirten Masse von Produktionsmitteln ausdrückt.\n\n\n\n\nIm vierten Kapitel wurde gezeigt, wie die Entwicklung der gesell-\n schaftlichen Produktivkraft der Arbeit Cooperation auf grosser Stufenleiter\n voraussetzt, wie nur unter dieser Voraussetzung Theilung und Kombina-\n tion der Arbeit organisirt, Produktionsmittel durch massenhafte Koncen-\n tration ökonomisirt, schon stofflich nur gemeinsam anwendbare Arbeits-\n mittel, z. B. System der Maschinerie u. s. w., ins Leben gerufen, unge-\n heure Naturkräfte in den Dienst der Produktion gepresst und die Ver-\n wandlung des Produktionsprozesses in technologische Anwendung der Wis-\n senschaft vollzogen werden können. Auf Grundlage der Waaren-\n produktion, wo die Produktionsmittel Eigenthum von Privatpersonen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"611\", \"facs\": \"#f0630\"} --> sind, wo der Handarbeiter daher entweder isolirt und selbstständig Waaren\n producirt oder seine Arbeitskraft als Waare verkauft, weil ihm die Mittel\n zum Selbstbetrieb fehlen, realisirt sich jene Voraussetzung nur durch das\nWachsthum der individuellen Kapitale, oder im Masse, wor-\n in die gesellschaftlichen Produktions- und Lebensmittel\n in das Privateigenthum individueller Kapitalisten ver-\n wandelt werden. Der Boden der Waarenproduktion kann die Pro-\n duktion auf grosser Stufenleiter nur in kapitalistischer Form tra-\n gen. Eine gewisse Accumulation von Kapital in den Händen in-\n dividueller Waarenproducenten bildet die Voraussetzung der speci-\n fisch kapitalistischen Produktionsweise. Wir mussten sie\n daher unterstellen bei dem Uebergang aus dem Handwerk in den kapi-\n talistischen Betrieb. Sie mag die ursprüngliche Accumulation heis-\n sen, weil sie statt historisches Resultat historische Grundlage der speci-\n fisch kapitalistischen Produktion. Wie sie selbst entspringt, brauchen\n wir hier nicht zu untersuchen. Genug, sie bildet den Ausgangspunkt.\n Aber alle Methoden zur Steigerung der gesellschaftlichen Produktivkraft\n der Arbeit, die auf dieser Grundlage erwachsen, sind zugleich Methoden\n zur Produktion des Mehrwerths oder Mehrprodukts, welches seinerseits das\n Bildungselement der Accumulation. Sie sind also zugleich Methoden der Pro-\n duktion von Kapital durch Kapital oder Methoden seiner beschleunigten Ac-\n cumulation ist. Die kontinuirliche Rückverwandlung von Mehrwerth in Kapital\n stellt sich dar als wachsende Grösse des in den Produktions-\n prozess eingehenden Kapitals. Diese wird ihrerseits Grundlage\neiner erweiterten Stufenleiter der Produktion, der sie be-\n gleitenden Methoden zur Steigerung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit, und\n beschleunigter Produktion von Mehrwerth. Wenn also ein gewisser Grad\n der Kapitalaccumulation als Bedingung der specifisch kapitalistischen Pro-\n duktionsweise erscheint, verursacht die letztere rückschlagend eine beschleu-\n nigte Accumulation des Kapitals. Mit der Accumulation des Ka-\n pitals entwickelt sich daher die specifisch kapitalisti-\n sche Produktionsweise und mit der specifisch kapitali-\n stischen Produktionsweise die Accumulation des Kapitals.\n\n\n\n\nJedes individuelle Kapital ist eine grössere oder kleinere Koncen-\n tration von Produktionsmitteln mit entsprechendem Kommando\n über eine grössere oder kleinere Arbeiterarmee. Jede Accumulation wird das\n [Running matter: 39*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"612\", \"facs\": \"#f0631\"} --> Mittel neuer Accumulation. Sie erweitert mit der vermehrten Masse\n des als Kapital funktionirenden Reichthums seine Koncentration in\n den Händen individueller Kapitalisten, daher die Grundlage der Pro-\n duktion auf grosser Stufenleiter und der specifisch kapitalistischen Pro-\n duktionsmethoden. Das Wachsthum des gesellschaftlichen Kapitals voll-\n zieht sich im Wachsthum vieler individuellen Kapitale. Alle andern Um-\n stände als gleichbleibend vorausgesetzt, wächst jedes dieser individuellen\n Kapitale, und die in ihm gegebne Koncentration der Produktionsmittel,\n im Verhältniss worin es einen aliquoten Theil des gesellschaftlichen Ge-\n sammtkapitals bildet. Zugleich reissen sich Ableger von den Original-\n kapitalen los und funktioniren als neue selbstständige Kapitale. Eine\n grosse Rolle spielt dabei nothwendig die Theilung des Vermögens in Ka-\n pitalistenfamilien. Mit der Accumulation des Kapitals wächst daher auch\n mehr oder minder die Anzahl der Kapitalisten. Zwei Punkte charakteri-\n siren diese Art Koncentration, welche unmittelbar auf der Accumu-\n lation beruht oder vielmehr mit ihr identisch ist. Erstens: Die wach-\n sende Koncentration der gesellschaftlichen Produktionsmittel in den Händen\n individueller Kapitalisten ist, unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen, be-\n schränkt durch den Wachsthumgrad des gesellschaftlichen\n Reichthums. Zweitens: Der in jeder besondern Produktions-\n sphäre ansässige Theil des gesellschaftlichen Kapitals ist ver-\n theilt unter viele Kapitalisten, welche einander als unabhängige und\n mit einander konkurrirende Waarenproducenten gegenüberstehn. Die Ac-\n cumulation und die sie begleitende Koncentration sind also nicht nur auf viele\n Punkte zersplittert, sondern das Wachsthum der funktionirenden Kapitale ist\n durchkreuzt durch die Bildung neuer und die Spaltung alter Kapitale, Stellt\n sich die Accumulation daher einerseits dar als wachsende Koncentration\n der Produktionsmittel und des Kommando’s über Arbeit, so andrerseits als\nRepulsion vieler individueller Kapitale von einander.\n\n\n\n\nDieser Zersplitterung des gesellschaftlichen Gesammtkapitals in viele\n individuelle Kapitale oder der Repulsion seiner Bruchtheile von einander\n wirkt entgegen ihre Attraktion. Es ist diess nicht mehr einfache,\n mit der Accumulation identische Koncentration von Produktionsmitteln und\n Kommando über Arbeit. Es ist Koncentration bereits gebil-\n deter Kapitale, Aufhebung ihrer individuellen Selbstständigkeit, Ex-\n propriation von Kapitalist durch Kapitalist, Verwandlung vieler kleinerer\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"613\", \"facs\": \"#f0632\"} --> in weniger grössere Kapitale. Dieser Prozess unterscheidet sich von dem\n ersten dadurch, dass er nur veränderte Vertheilung der bereits\n vorhandnen und funktionirenden Kapitale voraussetzt,\n sein Spielraum also durch dasabsolute Wachsthum des gesell-\n schaftlichen Reichthums oder die absoluten Grenzen der\n Accumulation nicht beschränktist. Das Kapital schwillt hier in\n einer Hand zu grossen Massen, weil es dort in vielen Händen verloren geht. Es\n ist die eigentliche Koncentration im Unterschied zur Accumulation.\n\n\n\n\nDie Gesetze dieser Koncentration der Kapitale oder der Attraktion\n von Kapital durch Kapital können hier nicht entwickelt werden. Kurze\n thatsächliche Andeutung genügt. Der Konkurrenzkampf wird durch Ver-\n wohlfeilerung der Waaren geführt. Die Wohlfeilheit der Waaren hängt,\n caeteris paribus, von der Produktivität der Arbeit, diese aber von der\n Stufenleiter der Produktion ab. Die grösseren Kapitale schlagen daher\n die kleineren. Man erinnert sich ferner, dass mit der Entwicklung der\n kapitalistischen Produktionsweise der Minimalumfang des indivi-\n duellen Kapitals wächst, der erheischt ist, um ein Geschäft unter sei-\n nen normalen Bedingungen zu betreiben. Die kleineren Kapitale drängen\n sich daher in Produktionssphären, deren sich die grosse Industrie nur noch\n sporadisch oder unvollkommen bemächtigt hat. Die Konkurrenz rast\n hier im direkten Verhältniss zur Anzahl und im umgekehrten Verhältniss\n zur Grösse der rivalisirenden Kapitale. Sie endet stets mit Untergang\n vieler kleiner Kapitalisten und Uebergang ihrer Kapitale in die Hand des\n Siegers. Abgesehn hiervon bildet sich mit der kapitalistischen Produktion\n eine ganz neue Macht, das Kreditwesen. Es wird nicht nur selbst\n zu einer neuen gewaltigen Waffe im Konkurrenzkampfe. Durch unsichtbare\n Fäden zieht es die über die Oberfläche der Gesellschaft in grösseren oder\n kleineren Massen zersplitterten Geldmittel in die Hände individueller Ka-\n pitalisten. Es ist die specifische Maschine zur Koncentration der Kapitale.\n\n\n\n\nDie Koncentration der Kapitale, oder der Prozess ihrer Attraktion, wird\n intensiver im Verhältniss, worin sich mit der Accumulation die specifisch kapi-\n talistische Produktionsweise entwickelt. Ihrerseits wird die Koncentration\n einer der grossen Hebel jener Entwicklung. Sie verkürzt und beschleu-\n nigt die Verwandlung zersplitterter Produktionsprozesse in ge-\n sellschaftlich kombinirte und auf grosser Stufenleiter\n ausgeführte.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"614\", \"facs\": \"#f0633\"} -->\n\nDer wachsende Umfang der individuellen Kapitalmassen wird zur mate-\n riellen Basis einer beständigen Umwälzung der Produktionsweise\n selbst. Fortwährend erobert die kapitalistische Produktionsweise ihr noch gar\n nicht, oder nur sporadisch, oder nur formell unterworfene Arbeitszweige. Da-\n neben erwachsen auf ihrem Boden neue, ihr von vorn herein angehörige\n Arbeitszweige. Endlich wird in den bereits kapitalistisch betriebenen Arbeits-\n zweigen die Produktivkraft der Arbeit treibhausmässig gereift. In allen\n diesen Fällen sinkt die Arbeiterzahl verhältnissmässig zur Masse der von ihr\n verarbeiteten Produktionsmittel. Ein stets grösserer Theil des Kapitals\n wird in Produktionsmittel umgesetzt, ein stets kleinerer in Arbeitskraft. Mit\n dem Umfang, der Koncentration und der technischen Wirksamkeit der\nProduktionsmittel vermindert sich progressiv der Grad, worin sie\nBeschäftigungs mittel der Arbeiter sind. Ein Dampfpflug ist ein\n ungleich wirksameres Produktionsmittel als der gewöhnliche Pflug,\n aber der in ihm ausgelegte Kapitalwerth ist ein ungleich geringeres Be-\n schäftigungsmittel, als wenn er in gewöhnlichen Pflügen realisirt wäre.\n Zunächst ist es grade die Zufügung von neuem Kapital zum alten, welche\n die gegenständlichen Bedingungen des Produktionsprozesses auszuweiten\n und technologisch umzuwälzen erlaubt. Bald aber ergreift die veränderte\n Zusammensetzung und technologische Umgestaltung mehr oder minder\n alles alte Kapital, das seinen Reproduktionstermin er-\n reicht hat und daher neu ersetzt wird. Diese Metamorphose\n des alten Kapitals ist vom absoluten Wachsthum des gesell-\n schaftlichen Kapitals zu gewissem Grad unabhängig, wie es die Koncentra-\n tion ist. Letztre aber, welche vorhandnes gesellschaftliches Kapital nur\n anders vertheilt und viele alte Kapitale in eins verschmilzt, wirkt wieder\n als mächtiges Agens in dieser Metamorphose des alten Kapitals.\n\n\n\n\nEinerseits attrahirt also das im Fortgang der Accumulation gebildete\n Zuschusskapital, verhältnissmässig zu seiner Grösse, weniger und weniger\n Arbeiter. Andrerseits repellirt das in neuer Zusammensetzung reprodu-\n cirte alte Kapital mehr und mehr früher von ihm beschäftigte Arbeiter.\n\n\n\n\nDie Accumulation des Kapitals, welche ursprünglich nur als seine\nquantitative Erweiterung erscheint, vollzieht sich also in fortwährendem\nqualitativen Wechsel seiner Zusammensetzung, in bestän-\n diger Zunahme seines constanten auf Kosten seines variablen Bestandtheils.\n\n\n\n\nDie specifisch kapitalistische Produktionsweise, die ihr entsprechende\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"615\", \"facs\": \"#f0634\"} --> Entwicklung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit, der dadurch verursachte Wechsel\n in der organischen Zusammensetzung des Kapitals halten nicht nur Schritt\n mit dem Fortschritt der Accumulation oder dem Wachsthum des gesell-\n schaftlichen Reichthums. Sie schreiten ungleich schneller, weil\n die einfache Accumulation oder die absolute Ausdehnung des Gesammt-\n kapitals von der Koncentration seiner individuellen Elemente, und die tech-\n nologische Umwälzung des Surpluskapitals von technologischer Umwälzung\n des Originalkapitals begleitet sind. Mit dem Fortgang der Accumulation\n wandelt sich also das Verhältniss von constantem zu variablem Kapital-\n theil, wenn ursprünglich 1 : 1, in 2 : 1, 3 : 1, 4 : 1, 5 : 1, 7 : 1 u. s. w., so\n dass, wie das Kapital wächst, statt ½ seines Gesammtwerths progressiv\n nur ⅓, ¼, ⅕, ⅙, ⅛ u. s. w. in Arbeitskraft, dagegen ⅔, ¾, ⅘, ⅚,\n ⅞ u. s. w. in Produktionsmittel umgesetzt wird. Da die Nachfrage\n nach Arbeit nicht durch den Umfang des Gesammtkapitals, sondern durch\n den seines variablen Bestandtheils bestimmt ist, fällt sie also progressiv\n mit dem Wachsthum des Gesammtkapitals, statt, wie vorhin unter-\n stellt, verhältnissmässig mit ihm zu wachsen. Sie fällt relativ zur Grösse\n des Gesammtkapitals und in beschleunigter Progression mit dem Wachsthum\n dieser Grösse. Mit dem Wachsthum des Gesammtkapitals wächst zwar auch\n sein variabler Bestandtheil, oder die ihm einverleibte Arbeitskraft, aber in\n beständig abnehmender Proportion. Die Zwischenpausen, worin\n die Accumulation als blosse Erweiterung der Produktion auf gegebner\n technologischer Grundlage wirkt, verkürzen sich. Nicht nur wird eine in\n wachsender Progression beschleunigte Accumulation des Gesammt-\n kapitals erheischt, um eine additionelle Arbeiterzahl von gegebner Grösse zu\n absorbiren oder selbst, wegen der beständigen Metamorphose des alten Ka-\n pitals, die bereits funktionirende zu beschäftigen. Ihrerseits schlägt diese\n wachsende Accumulation und Koncentration selbst wieder um in\n eine Quelle neuer Wechsel der Zusammensetzung des Kapitals oder aber-\n malig beschleunigter Abnahme seines variablen gegen seinen constanten\n Bestandtheil. Diese mit dem Wachsthum des Gesammtkapitals be-\n schleunigte und rascher denn sein eignes Wachsthum beschleunigte relative\n Abnahme seines variablen Bestandtheils scheint auf der andern\n Seite umgekehrt stets rascheres absolutes Wachsthum der\n Arbeiterbevölkerung als das des variablen Kapitals oder\n ihrer Beschäftigungsmittel. Die kapitalistische Accu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"616\", \"facs\": \"#f0635\"} --> mulation producirt vielmehr, und zwar im Verhältniss zu ihrer Energie\n und ihrem Umfang, beständig eine relative, d. h. für die mittleren\n Verwerthungsbedürfnisse des Kapitals überschüssige,\n daher überflüssige oder Surplus-Arbeiterbevölkerung.\n\n\n\n\nDas gesellschaftliche Gesammtkapital betrachtet, ruft die Be wegung\n seiner Accumulation bald periodischen Wechsel hervor, bald verthei-\n len sich ihre Momente gleichzeitig über die verschiednen Produktions-\n sphären. In einigen Sphären findet Wechsel in der Zusammensetzung des Ka-\n pitals statt ohne Wachsthum seiner absoluten Grösse, in Folge blosser Kon-\n centration; in andern ist das absolute Wachsthum des Kapitals mit abso-\n luter Abnahme seines variablen Bestandtheils oder der von ihm absor-\n birten Arbeitskraft verbunden; in andern wächst das Kapital bald auf sei-\n ner gegebnen technischen Grundlage fort, und attrahirt zuschüssige Arbeits-\n kraft im Verhältniss seines Wachsthums, bald tritt organischer Wechsel\n ein und kontrahirt sich sein variabler Bestandtheil; in allen Sphären ist\n das Wachsthum des variablen Kapitaltheils und daher der beschäftigten\n Arbeiterzahl stets verbunden mit heftigen Fluktuationen und vorüber-\n gehender Produktion von Surpluspopulation, ob diese nun die auffallendere\n Form von Repulsion bereits beschäftigter Arbeiter annimmt oder die mehr\n unscheinbare, aber nicht minder wirksame, erschwerter Absorption der zu-\n schüssigen Arbeiterbevölkerung in ihre gewohnten Abzugskanäle [Note: Der Census für England und Wales zeigt u. a.:\nAlle in der Agrikultur beschäftigte Personen (Eigenthümer,\n Pächter, Gärtner, Hirten u. s. w. eingeschlossen): 1851: 2,011,447, 1861:\n 1,924,110, Abnahme: 87,337. Worsted Manufaktur: 1851: 102,714\n Personen, 1861: 79,242; Seidenfabrik: 1851: 111,940, 1861: 101,678;\nKattundrucker: 1851: 12,098, 1861: 12,556, welche geringe Zunahme\n trotz des enorm ausgedehnten Geschäfts grosse proportionelle Abnahme\n in der Zahl der beschäftigten Arbeiter bedingt. Hutmacher: 1851: 15,957,\n 1861: 13,814; Strohhut- und Bonnetmacher: 1851: 20,393, 1861:\n 18,176; Malzer: 1851: 10,566, 1861: 10,677; Lichtgiesser: 1851:\n 4949, 1861: 4686. Diese Abnahme ist u. a. der Zunahme der Gasbeleuchtung ge-\n schuldet. Kammmacher: 1851: 2,038, 1861: 1,478; Holzsäger. 1851:\n 30,552, 1861: 31,647, geringe Zunahme in Folge des Aufschwungs von Sägemaschi-\n nen; Nagelmacher: 1851: 26,940, 1861: 26,130, Abnahme in Folge der Ma-\n schinenkonkurrenz; Arbeiter in Zinn- und Kupferbergwerken: 1851:\n 31,360, 1861: 32,041. Dagegen: Baumwollspinnereien und Webe-\n reien: 1851: 371,777, 1861: 456,646; Kohlenbergwerke: 1851:] . Mit\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"617\", \"facs\": \"#f0636\"} --> der Grösse des bereits funktionirenden Gesellschaftskapitals und dem Grad\n seines Wachsthums, mit der Ausdehnung der Produktionsleiter und der\n Masse der in Bewegung gesetzten Arbeiter, mit der Entwicklung der Produk-\n tivkraft ihrer Arbeit, mit dem breiteren und volleren Strom aller Springquel-\n len des Reichthums dehnt sich auch die Stufenleiter, worin\n grössere Attraktion der Arbeiter durch das Kapital mit grösserer Repul-\n sion derselben verbunden ist, nimmt die Raschheit der Wechsel in der\n organischen Zusammensetzung des Kapitals und seiner technischen Form\n zu, und schwillt der Umkreis der Produktionssphären, die bald gleichzeitig,\n bald abwechselnd davon ergriffen werden. Mit der von ihnen producirten Accu-\n mulation des Kapitals producirt die Arbeiterbevölkerung also in wachsen-\n dem Umfang die Mittel ihrer eignen relativen Ueberzählig-\n machung [Note: „The demand for labour depends on the increase of circulating and not\n of fixed capital. Were it true that the proportion between these two sorts of\n capital is the same at all times, and in all circumstances, then, indeed, it follows\n that the number of labourers employed is in proportion to the wealth of the state.\n But such a proposition has not the semblance of probability. As arts are culti-\n vated, and civilization is extended, fixed capital bears a larger and larger propor-\n tion to circulating capital. The amount of fixed capital employed in the produc-\n tion of a piece of British muslin is at least a hundred, probably a thousand times\n greater than that employed in a similar piece of Indian muslin. And the propor-\n tion of circulating capital is a hundred or thousand times less … the whole of\n the annual savings, added to the fixed capital, would have no effect in increasing\n the demand for labour.“ (John Barton: „Observations on the circum-\n stances which influence the Condition of the Labouring Classes\n of Society.“ Lond. 1817, p. 16, 17.) „The same cause which may increase\n the net revenue of the country may at the same time render the population\n redundant, and deteriorate the condition of the labourer.“ (Ri-\n cardo l. c. p. 469.) Mit der Zunahme des Kapitals „the demand (for labour)\n will be in a diminishing ratio.“ (l. c. p. 480, Note.) „The amount of capital\n devoted to the maintenance of labour may vary, independently of any changes in\n the whole amount of capital … Great fluctuations in the amount of em-\n ployment, and great suffering may become more frequent as capi-] . Es ist diess ein der kapitalistischen Produk-\n tionsweise eigenthümliches Populationsgesetz, wie in der That\n [Note: 183,389, 1861: 246,613. „Die Zunahme von Arbeitern ist im Allgemeinen am\n grössten seit 1851 in solchen Zweigen, worin die Maschinerie bisher noch nicht\n mit Erfolg angewandt worden.“ („Census of England and Wales for\n 1862“, vol. III. Lond. 1863, p. 36.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"618\", \"facs\": \"#f0637\"} --> jede besondre historische Produktionsweise ihre besondern, historisch gül-\n tigen Populationsgesetze hat. Ein abstraktes Populationsgesetz existirt nur\n für Pflanze und Thier.\n\n\n\n\nWenn aber eine Surplusarbeiterpopulation nothwendiges Produkt der\n Accumulation oder der Entwicklung des Reichthums auf kapitalistischer\n Grundlage ist, wird diese Surpluspopulation umgekehrt zum Hebel der kapita-\n listischen Accumulation, ja zu einer Existenzbedingung der kapita-\n listischen Produktionsweise. Sie bildet eine disponible indu-\n strielle Reservearmee, die dem Kapital ganz so absolut gehört,\n als ob es sie auf seine eignen Kosten grossgezüchtet hätte. Sie schafft\n für seine wechselnden Verwerthungsbedürfnisse das stets bereite exploi-\n table Menschenmaterial, unabhängig von den Schranken der wirklichen\n Bevölkerungszunahme. Mit der Accumulation und der sie begleitenden\n Entwicklung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit wächst die plötzliche Expan-\n sionskraft des Kapitals, nicht nur, weil die Elasticität des funk-\n tionirenden Kapitals wächst, und der absolute Reichthum, wovon das\n Kapital nur einen elastischen Theil bildet, nicht nur, weil der Kredit, unter\n jedem besondern Reiz, im Umsehn ungewöhnlichen Theil dieses Reich-\n thums der Produktion als Surpluskapital zur Verfügung stellt. Der tech-\n nologische Charakter des Produktionsprozesses selbst, Maschinerie, Trans-\n portmittel u. s. w. ermöglichen die rascheste Verwandlung von Surplus-\n produkt auf grösster Stufenleiter in zuschüssige Produktionsmittel. Die\n mit dem Fortschritt der Accumulation überschwellende und in Surplus-\n kapital verwandelbare Masse des gesellschaftlichen Reichthums drängt\n sich mit Frenesie in alte Produktionszweige, deren Markt sich plötzlich\n erweitert, oder in neu eröffnete, wie Eisenbahnen u. s. w., deren Bedürf-\n niss aus der Entwicklung der alten entspringt. In allen solchen Fällen\n müssen grosse Menschenmassen plötzlich und ohne Abbruch der Produk-\n tionsleiter in andern Sphären auf die entscheidenden Punkte werfbar sein.\n Die Surpluspopulation liefert sie. Der charakteristische Lebenslauf der\n [Note: talitself becomes more plentiful.“ (Richard Jones: „An Intro-\n ductory Lecture on Pol. Econ. Lond. 1833“, p. 13.) „Demand (for la-\n bour) will rise … not in proportion to the accumulation of the gene-\n ral capital … Every augmentation, therefore, in the national stock destined for\n reproduction, comes, in the progress of society, to have less and less\n influence upon the condition of the labourer.“ (Ramsay l. c. p. 90, 91.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"619\", \"facs\": \"#f0638\"} --> modernen Industrie, die Form eines durch kleinere Schwankungen unter-\n brochnen zehnjährigen Cyklus von Perioden mittlerer Lebendigkeit, Pro-\n duktion unter Hochdruck, Krise und Stagnation, beruht auf der bestän-\n digen Bildung, grösseren oder geringeren Absorption, und Wiederbildung\n der industriellen Reservearmee oder Surpluspopulation. Ihrerseits rekru-\n tiren die Wechselfälle des industriellen Cyklus die Surpluspopulation und\n werden zu einem ihrer energischsten Reproduktionsagentien. Dieser\n eigenthümliche Lebenslauf der modernen Industrie, der uns in keinem\n früheren Zeitalter der Menschheit begegnet, war auch in der Kindheits-\n periode der kapitalistischen Produktion unmöglich. Die Zusammensetzung\n des Kapitals veränderte sich nur sehr allmälig. Seiner Accumulation ent-\n sprach also im Ganzen verhältnissmässiges Wachsthum der Arbeitsnach-\n frage. Langsam wie der Fortschritt seiner Accumulation, verglichen mit\n der modernen Epoche, stiess er auf Naturschranken der exploitablen Arbei-\n terbevölkerung, welche nur durch später zu erwähnende Gewaltmittel weg-\n räumbar war. Die plötzliche und ruckweise Expansion der Produktionsleiter\n ist die Voraussetzung ihrer plötzlichen Kontraktion; letztere ruft wieder\n die erstere hervor, aber die erstere ist unmöglich ohne disponibles Men-\n schenmaterial, ohne eine vom absoluten Wachsthum der Bevölkerung unab-\n hängige Vermehrung von Arbeitern. Sie wird geschaffen durch\n den einfachen Prozess, der einen Theil der Arbeiter beständig „freisetzt“,\n durch Methoden, welche die Anzahl der beschäftigten Arbeiter im Verhält-\n niss zur vermehrten Produktion vermindern. Die ganze Bewegungsform\n der modernen Industrie erwächst also aus der beständigen Verwandlung\n eines Theils der Arbeiterbevölkerung in unbeschäftigte oder halbbeschäf-\n tigte Hände. Die Oberflächlichkeit der politischen Oekonomie zeigt sich\n u. a. darin, dass sie die Expansion und Kontraktion des Kredits, das\n blosse Symptom der Wechselperioden des industriellen Cyklus, zu deren\n Ursache macht. Ganz wie die Himmelskörper, sobald sie durch ersten\n Stoss in eine bestimmte Bewegung geschleudert sind, dieselbe Bewegung\n stets reproduciren, so die gesellschaftliche Produktion, sobald sie einmal\n in jene Bewegung wechselnder Expansion und Kontraktion geworfen ist.\n Wirkungen werden ihrerseits zu Ursachen und die Wechselfälle des ganzen\n Prozesses, der seine eignen Bedingungen stets reproducirt, nehmen die\n Form der Periodicität an. Ist letztere einmal konsolidirt, so begreift\n selbst die politische Oekonomie die Produktion einer relativen, d. h. für\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"620\", \"facs\": \"#f0639\"} --> das mittlere Verwerthungsbedürfniss des Kapitals relativen Surpluspopu-\n lation, als Lebensbedingung der modernen Industrie.\n\n\n\n\n„Gesetzt“, sagt H. Merivale, früher Professor der politischen\n Oekonomie zu Oxford, später Beamter des englischen Kolonialministe-\n riums, „gesetzt, bei Gelegenheit einer Krise raffe die Nation sich zu einer\n Kraftanstrengung auf, um durch Emigration einiger 100,000 überflüssiger\n Arme los zu werden, was würde die Folge sein? Dass bei der ersten\n Wiederkehr der Arbeitsnachfrage ein Mangel vorhanden wäre. Wie rasch\n immer die Reproduktion von Menschen sein mag, sie braucht jedenfalls\n den Zwischenraum einer Generation zum Ersatz erwachsner Arbeiter.\n Nun hängen die Profite unsrer Fabrikanten hauptsächlich von der Macht\n ab, den günstigen Moment lebhafter Nachfrage zu exploitiren und sich so\n für die Periode der Erlahmung schadlos zu halten. Diese Macht ist\n ihnen nur gesichert durch Kommando über Maschinerie\n und Handarbeit. Sie müssen disponible Hände vorfin-\n den; sie müssen fähig sein die Aktivität ihrer Operationen, wenn nöthig,\n höher zu spannen oder abzuspannen, je nach dem Stand des Markts, oder\n sie können platterdings nicht in der Hetzjagd der Konkurrenz das Ueber-\n gewicht behaupten, worauf der Reichthum dieses Landes ge-\n gründet ist“ [Note: H. Merivale: „Lectures on Colonization and Colonies.“\n Lond. 1841 and 1842, v. I, p. 146.] . Selbst Malthus erkennt in der Surpluspopulation, die er,\n nach seiner bornirten Weise, aus absolutem Ueberwachs der Arbeiterbevölke-\n rung, nicht aus ihrer relativen Ueberzähligmachung deutet, eine Nothwen-\n digkeit der modernen Industrie. Er sagt: „Weise Gewohnheiten\n in Bezug auf die Ehe, wenn zu einer gewissen Höhe getrieben unter der\n Arbeiterklasse eines Landes, das hauptsächlich von Manufaktur und Han-\n del abhängt, würden ihm schädlich sein … Der Natur der Bevölkerung\n gemäss, kann ein Zuwachs von Arbeitern nicht zu Markt geliefert werden,\n in Folge besondrer Nachfrage, bis nach Verlauf von 16 oder 18 Jahren,\n und die Verwandlung von Revenue in Kapital durch Ersparung kann sehr\n viel rascher platzgreifen; ein Land ist stets dem ausgesetzt, dass sein\n Arbeitsfonds rascher wächst als die Bevölkerung“ [Note: „Prudential habits with regard to marriage carried to a considerable\n extent among the labouring class of a country mainly depending upon manufac-\n tures and commerce might injure it … From the nature of a population, an in-] . Nachdem die politische\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"621\", \"facs\": \"#f0640\"} --> Oekonomie so die beständige Produktion einer relativen Sur-\n pluspopulation von Arbeitern für eine Nothwendigkeit der\n kapitalistischen Accumulation erklärt hat, legt sie, und zwar\n adäquat in der Figur einer alten Jungfer, dem „beau ideal“ ihres Kapitalisten\n folgende Worte an die durch ihre eigne Schöpfung von Surpluskapital aufs\n Pflaster geworfenen „Ueberzähligen“ in den Mund: „Wir Fabrikanten thun\n was wir können für euch, indem wir das Kapital vermehren, von\n dem ihr subsistiren müsst; und ihr müsst das übrige thun, indem ihr eure\n Zahlen den Subsistenzmitteln proportionirt“ [Note: Harriet Martineau: „The Manchester Strike. 1842“, p. 101.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie kapitalistische Produktion schliesst die Schranken disponibler\n Arbeitskraft durch bloss natürlichen Zuwachs der Bevölkerung aus. Sie\n bedarf zu ihrem freien Spiel einer von dieser Schranke unabhän-\n gigen industriellen Reservearmee.\n\n\n\n\nBisher wurde unterstellt, dass der Zu- oder Abnahme des vari-\n ablen Kapitals genau die Zu- oder Abnahme der beschäftigten\n Arbeiterzahl entspricht. Die Abweichungen des Grössenwechsels des\n variablen Kapitals vom Grössenwechsel der Arbeiterzahl, worin es sich um-\n setzt, werden im Dritten Buch weiter erörtert. Hier interessiren sie uns\n nur, soweit sie das allgemeine Gesetz der Accumulation betreffen.\n\n\n\n\nBei gleichbleibender oder selbst verminderter Zahl der von ihm kom-\n mandirten Arbeiter wächst das variable Kapital, wenn der individuelle Ar-\n beiter mehr Arbeit liefert und daher sein Arbeitslohn wächst, obgleich\n der Arbeitspreis gleich bleibt, oder selbst sinkt, nur langsamer als die\n Arbeitsmasse steigt. Der Zuwachs des variablen Kapitals wird dann In-\n dex von mehr Arbeit, aber nicht von mehr beschäftigten Arbeitern. Jeder\n [Note: crease of labourers cannot be brought into market, in consequence of a particular\n demand, till after the lapse of 16 or 18 years, and the conversion of revenue into\n capital by saving, may take place much more rapidly; a country is always liable\n to an increase in the quantity of the funds for the maintenance of labour faster\n than the increase of population.“ (Malthus: „Princ. of Pol. Econ.“,\n p. 254, 319, 320.) In diesem Werk entdeckt Malthus endlich, vermittelst Sis-\n mondi’s, die schöne Dreieinigkeit der kapitalistischen Produktion: Ueber-\n produktion — Ueberpopulation — Ueberkonsumtion, three very\n delicate monsters, indeed! Vgl. F. Engels: „Umrisse zu einer Kritik\n der Nationalökonomie“ l. c. p. 107 sqq.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"622\", \"facs\": \"#f0641\"} --> Kapitalist hat das absolute Interesse, ein bestimmtes Arbeitsquantum aus\n kleinerer, statt eben so wohlfeil oder selbst wohlfeiler aus grösserer\n Arbeiterzahl auszupressen. In dem letzten Fall wächst die Auslage von\n constantem Kapital verhältnissmässig zur Masse der in Fluss gesetzten Ar-\n beit, im ersten Fall viel langsamer. Je grösser die Stufenleiter der Pro-\n duktion, desto entscheidender diess Motiv. Seine Wucht wächst mit der\n Accumulation des Kapitals.\n\n\n\n\nMan hat gesehn, dass die Entwicklung der kapitalistischen Produk-\n tionsweise und Produktivkraft der Arbeit — zugleich Ursache und Wirkung\n der Accumulation — den Kapitalisten befähigt, mit derselben Aus-\n lage von variablem Kapital mehr Arbeit durch grössere extensive\n oder intensive Exploitation der individuellen Arbeitskräfte flüssig zu\n machen. Man hat ferner gesehn, dass er mit demselben Kapital-\n werth mehr Arbeitskräfte kauft, indem er progressiv geschicktere\n Arbeiter durch ungeschicktere, reife durch unreife, männliche durch weib-\n liche, erwachsne Arbeitskraft durch jugendliche oder kindliche verdrängt.\n\n\n\n\nEinerseits macht also, im Fortgang der Accumulation, grösseres\n variables Kapital mehr Arbeit flüssig, ohne mehr Arbeiter zu werben,\n andrerseits macht variables Kapital von derselben Grösse mehr\n Arbeit mit derselben Masse Arbeitskraft flüssig und endlich mehr niedere\n Arbeitskräfte durch Verdrängung höherer.\n\n\n\n\nDie Produktion einer relativen Surpluspopulation oder\n die Freisetzung von Arbeitern geht daher noch rascher voran als die\n ohnehin mit dem Fortschritt der Accumulation beschleunigte\n technologische Umwälzung des Produktionsprozesses und die entsprechende\n proportionelle Abnahme des variablen Kapitaltheils gegen den constanten.\n Wenn die Produktionsmittel, wie sie an Umfang und Wirkungskraft zu-\n nehmen, in geringerem Grad Beschäftigungsmittel der Arbeiter wer-\n den, wird diess Verhältniss selbst wieder dadurch modificirt, dass im Mass wie\n die Produktivkraft der Arbeit wächst, das Kapital seine Zufuhr von Ar-\n beit rascher steigert als seine Nachfrage nach Arbeitern. Die Ueber-\n arbeit des beschäftigten Theils der Arbeiterklasse schwellt die Reihen ihrer Re-\n serve, während umgekehrt der vermehrte Druck, den die letztere durch ihre\n Konkurrenz auf die erstere ausübt, diese zur Ueberarbeit und Unterwerfung\n unter die Diktate des Kapitals zwingt. Die Verdammung eines Theils\n der Arbeiterklasse zu erzwungnem Müssiggang durch Ueberarbeit des andern\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"623\", \"facs\": \"#f0642\"} --> Theils, und vice versa, wird Bereicherungsmittel des einzelnen Kapitalisten [Note: Selbst während der Baumwollnoth von 1863 findet man in einem Pam-\n phlet der Baumwollspinner von Blackburn heftige Denunciation gegen die Ueber-\n arbeit, die kraft des Fabrikgesetzes natürlich nur erwachsne männliche Arbeiter\n traf. „The adult operatives at this mill have been asked to work from 12 to 13\n hours per day, while there are hundreds who are compelled to be idle who would\n willingly work partial time, in order to maintain their families and save their\n brethren from a premature grave through being overworked.“ „Wir“, heisst es\n weiter, „möchten fragen, ob diese Praxis Ueberzeit zu arbeiten irgend wie erträg-\n liche Verhältnisse zwischen Meistern und ‚Dienern‘ möglich macht? Die Opfer\n der Ueberarbeit fühlen die Unbill eben so sehr als die dadurch zu erzwungnem\n Müssiggang Verdammten (condemned to forced idleness). In diesem\n Distrikt reicht das zu verrichtende Werk hin, um alle theilweis zu beschäftigen,\n würde die Arbeit billig vertheilt. Wir verlangen nur ein Recht, indem wir die\n Meister auffordern, allgemein nur kurze Zeit zu arbeiten, wenigstens so lange der\n jetzige Stand der Dinge währt, statt einen Theil zu überarbeiten, während der\n andre durch Arbeitsmangel gezwungen wird, von der Wohlthätigkeit seine Existenz\n zu fristen.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1863“, p. 8.) — Die\n Wirkung einer relativen Surpluspopulation auf die beschäftigten Arbeiter begreift\n der Verfasser des „Essay on Trade and Commerce“ mit seinem gewohnten\n unfehlbaren Bourgeoisinstinkt. „Eine andre Ursache der Faulenzerei (idleness)\n in diesem Konigreich ist der Mangel einer hinreichenden Anzahlarbei-\n tender Hände. So oft durch irgend eine ungewöhnliche Nachfrage für Fabri-\n kate die Arbeitsmasse ungenügend wird, fühlen die Arbeiter ihre eigne Wichtig-\n keit und wollen sie ihren Meistern ebenfalls fühlbar machen; es ist erstaunlich;\n aber so depravirt ist die Gesinnung dieser Kerle, dass in solchen Fällen Gruppen\n von Arbeitern sich kombinirt haben, um ihre Meister dadurch in Verlegenheit zu\n setzen, dass sie einen ganzen Tag durch faulenzten.“ („Essay etc.“, p. 27,\n 28.) Die Kerle verlangten nämlich Lohnerhöhung.] \n und beschleunigt zugleich die Produktion der industriel-\n len Reservearmee auf einem dem Fortschritt der gesell-\n schaftlichen Accumulation entsprechenden Massstab. Wie\n wichtig diess Moment in der Bildung der relativen Surpluspopulation, beweist\n z. B. England. Seine technischen Mittel zur „Ersparung“ von Arbeit sind ko-\n lossal. Dennoch, würde morgen allgemein die Arbeit auf ein rationelles\n Mass beschränkt, und für die verschiednen Schichten der Arbeiterklasse wie-\n der entsprechend nach Alter und Geschlecht abgestuft, so wäre die vorhandne\n Arbeiterpopulation absolut unzureichend zur Fortführung der nationalen\n Produktion auf ihrer jetzigen Stufenleiter. Die grosse Mehrheit der jetzt\n „unproduktiven“ Arbeiter müsste in „produktive“ verwandelt werden.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"624\", \"facs\": \"#f0643\"} -->\n\nIm Grossen und Ganzen sind die allgemeinen Bewegungen des Ar-\n beitslohns ausschliesslich regulirt durch die Expansion und Kontraktion\n der industriellen Reservearmee, welche dem Periodenwechsel\n des industriellen Cyklus entsprechen. Sie sind also nicht bestimmt\ndurch die Bewegung der absoluten Anzahl der Arbeiterbevöl-\n kerung, sondern durch das wechselnde Verhältniss, worin die Arbei-\n terklasse in aktive Armee und Reservearmee zerfällt, durch die Zunahme und\n Abnahme des relativen Umfangs der Surpluspopulation, durch den Grad, wor-\n in sie bald absorbirt, bald wieder freigesetzt wird. Für die moderne Industrie\n mit ihrem zehnjährigen Cyklus und seinem regelmässigen Periodenwechsel,\n der ausserdem im Fortgang der Accumulation durch stets rascher auf ein-\n ander folgende unregelmässige Oscillationen durchkreuzt wird, wäre es in der\n That ein schönes Gesetz, welches die Nachfrage und Zufuhr von Arbeit nicht\n durch die Expansion und Kontraktion des Kapitals, also nach sei-\n nen jedesmaligen Verwerthungsbedürfnissen regelte, so dass der\n Arbeitsmarkt bald relativ untervoll erscheint, weil das Kapital sich expandirt,\n bald wieder übervoll, weil es sich kontrahirt, sondern umgekehrt die Bewegung\n des Kapitals von der absoluten Bewegung der Populationsmenge\n abhängig machte. Diess jedoch ist das ökonomische Dogma. Nach demselben\n steigt in Folge der Kapitalaccumulation der Arbeitslohn. Der erhöhte\n Arbeitslohn spornt zur rascheren Vermehrung der Arbeiterbevölkerung und\n diese dauert fort, bis der Arbeitsmarkt überfüllt, also das Kapital relativ\n zur Arbeiterzufuhr unzureichend geworden ist. Der Arbeitslohn sinkt, und\n nun die Kehrseite der Medaille. Durch den fallenden Arbeitslohn wird\n die Arbeiterbevölkerung nach und nach decimirt, so dass ihr gegenüber\n das Kapital wieder überschüssig wird, oder auch, wie Andre es erklären,\n der fallende Arbeitslohn und die entsprechende erhöhte Exploitation des\n Arbeiters beschleunigt wieder die Accumulation, während gleichzeitig der\n niedere Lohn das Wachsthum der Arbeiterklasse in Schach hält. So tritt\n wieder das Verhältniss ein, worin die Arbeitszufuhr niedriger als die Ar-\n beitsnachfrage, der Lohn steigt u. s. w. Eine schöne Bewegungsmethode\n diess für die entwickelte kapitalistische Produktion! Bevor in Folge der\n Lohnerhöhung irgend ein positives Wachsthum der wirklich arbeitsfähigen\n Bevölkerung eintreten könnte, wäre die Frist aber und abermal abgelaufen,\n worin der industrielle Feldzug geführt, die Schlacht geschlagen und ent-\n schieden sein muss.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"625\", \"facs\": \"#f0644\"} -->\n\nZwischen 1849 und 1859 trat, zugleich mit fallenden Getreidepreisen,\n eine praktisch betrachtet nur nominelle Lohnerhöhung in den englischen\n Agrikulturdistrikten ein, z. B. in Wiltshire stieg der Wochenlohn von 7\n auf 8 sh., in Dorsetshire von 7 oder 8 auf 9 sh. u. s. w. Es war diess\n Folge des übergewöhnlichen Abflusses der agrikolen Surpluspopu-\n lation, verursacht durch Kriegsnachfrage, massenhafte Ausdehnung der\n Eisenbahnbauten, Fabriken, Bergwerke u. s. w. Je niedriger der Arbeitslohn,\n desto höher drückt sich jedes noch so unbedeutende Steigen desselben\nin Procentzahlen aus. Ist der Wochenlohn z. B. 20 sh. und steigt\n er auf 22, so um 10 %; ist er dagegen nur 7 sh. und steigt auf 9, so um\n 28\\frac{4}{7} %, was sehr erklecklich klingt. Jedenfalls heulten die Pächter und\n schwatzte sogar der „London Economist“ ganz ernsthaft von „a\n general and substantial advance“ [Note: Economist, Jan. 21, 1860.]  mit Bezug auf diese Hungerlöhne.\n Was thaten nun die Pächter? Warteten sie, bis die Landarbeiter sich in\n Folge dieser brillanten Zahlung so vermehrt hatten, dass ihr Lohn wieder fal-\n len musste, wie die Sache sich im dogmatisch ökonomischen Hirn zuträgt?\n Sie führten mehr Maschinerie ein, und im Umsehn waren die Arbeiter wieder\n „überzählig“ in einem selbst den Pächtern genügenden Verhältniss. Es\n war jetzt „mehr Kapital“ in der Agrikultur angelegt als vorher und in\n einer produktiveren Form. Damit fiel die Nachfrage nach Arbeit nicht\n nur relativ, sondern absolut.\n\n\n\n\nJene ökonomische Fiktion verwechselt die Gesetze, welche die all-\n gemeine Bewegung des Arbeitslohns oder das Verhältniss zwi-\n schen Arbeiterklasse und gesellschaftlichem Gesammtkapital regeln, mit den\n Gesetzen, welche die Arbeiterbevölkerung unter die beson-\n dern Produktionssphären vertheilen. Wenn z. B. in Folge\n günstiger Konjunktur die Accumulation in einer bestimmten Produktionssphäre\n besonders lebhaft, die Profite hier grösser als die Durchschnittsprofite, Zu-\n schusskapital dahin drängt, so steigt natürlich Arbeitsnachfrage und Arbeits-\n lohn. Der höhere Arbeitslohn zieht einen grösseren Theil der Arbeiterbevölke-\n rung in die begünstigte Sphäre, bis sie mit Arbeitskraft gesättigt ist, und der\n Lohn auf die Dauer wieder auf sein früheres Durchschnittsniveau oder unter\n dasselbe fällt, falls der Zudrang zu gross war. Hier sieht der politische\n Oekonom „wo und wie“, mit Zunahme des Lohns eine absolute Zunahme\n [Running matter: I. 40] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"626\", \"facs\": \"#f0645\"} --> von Arbeitern, und mit der absoluten Zunahme der Arbeiter eine Abnahme\n des Lohns, aber er sieht in der That nur die lokale Oscillation des\n Arbeitsmarkts einer besondern Produktionssphäre, er sieht nur Phä-\n nomene der Vertheilung der Arbeiterbevöl kerung in die\n verschiednen Anlagesphären des Kapitals, je nach seinen wechselnden\n Bedürfnissen.\n\n\n\n\nDie industrielle Reservearmee oder relative Surpluspopulation drückt\n während der Perioden der Stagnation und mittleren Prosperität auf die aktive\n Arbeiterarmee und hält ihre Ansprüche während der Periode der Ueberpro-\n duktion und des Paroxysmus im Zaum. Die relative Surpluspopula-\n tion ist also der Hintergrund, worauf das Gesetz der Nach-\n frage und Zufuhr von Arbeit sich bewegt. Sie zwängt den Spiel-\n raum dieses Gesetzes in die der Exploitationsgier und Herrsch-\n sucht des Kapitals abso lut zusagenden Schranken ein. Es ist hier\n der Ort auf eine der Grossthaten der ökonomischen Apologetik zurückzukom-\n men. Man erinnert sich, dass wenn durch Einführung neuer oder Ausdehnung\n alter Maschinerie ein Stück variables Kapital in constantes verwandelt wird,\n der ökonomische Apologet diese Operation, welche Kapital „bindet“ und eben\n dadurch Arbeiter „freisetzt“, umgekehrt so deutet, das sie Kapital für den\n Arbeiter freisetzt. Erst jetzt kann man die Unverschämtheit des Apologeten\n vollständig würdigen. Was freigesetzt wird, sind nicht nur die unmittelbar\n durch die Maschine verdrängten Arbeiter, sondern ebenso ihre Ersatzmann-\n schaft und das, bei gewohnter Ausdehnung des Geschäfts auf seiner alten\n Basis, regelmässig absorbirte Zuschusskontingent. Es ist nicht altes Kapital\n für Arbeiter freigesetzt, aber es sind Arbeiter für etwa „zuschüssiges“ Ka-\n pital freigesetzt. D. h. also, der Mechanismus der kapitalistischen Produk-\n tion sorgt dafür, dass der absolute Zuwachs von Kapital von keiner\n entsprechenden Steigerung der allgemeinen Arbeitsnachfrage\n begleitet ist. Und diess nennt der Apologet eine Kompensation für das\n Elend, die Leiden und den möglichen Untergang der deplacirten Arbeiter wäh-\n rend der Uebergangsperiode, welche sie in die industrielle Reservearmee\n bannt! Die Nachfrage nach Arbeit ist nicht identisch mit Wachsthum des\n Kapitals, die Zufuhr der Arbeiter nicht mit dem Wachsthum der Arbeiter-\n klasse, so dass zwei von einander unabhängige Potenzen auf einander\n einwirkten. Les dés sont pipés. Das Kapital agirt auf beiden Seiten\n zugleich. Wenn seine Accumulation einerseits die Nachfrage nach Arbeit\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"627\", \"facs\": \"#f0646\"} --> vermehrt, vermehrt sie andrerseits die Zufuhr von Arbeitern durch deren\n „Freisetzung“, während zugleich der Druck der Unbeschäftigten die Be-\n schäftigten zur Flüssigmachung von mehr Arbeit zwingt, also in gewissem\n Grad die Arbeitszufuhr von der Zufuhr von Arbeitern unabhän-\n gig macht. Die Bewegung des Gesetzes der Nachfrage und Zu-\n fuhr von Arbeit auf dieser Basis vollendet die Despotie des Ka-\n pitals. Sobald daher die Arbeiter hinter das Geheimniss kommen, wie es zu-\n geht, dass im selben Mass, wie sie mehr arbeiten, mehr fremden Reichthum\n produciren, und die Produktivkraft ihrer Arbeit wächst, sogar ihre Funktion als\n Verwerthungsmittel des Kapitals immer prekärer für sie wird; sobald sie ent-\n decken, dass der Intensivitätsgrad der Konkurrenz unter ihnen selbst ganz und\n gar von dem Druck der relativen Surpluspopulation abhängt; sobald sie daher\n durch Trade’s Unions u. s. w. eine planmässige Zusammenwirkung zwischen\n den Beschäftigten und Unbeschäftigten zu organisiren suchen, um die ruini-\n renden Folgen jenes Naturgesetzes der kapitalistischen Produk-\n tion auf ihre Klasse zu brechen oder zu schwächen, zetert das Kapital, und sein\n Sykophant, der politische Oekonom, über Verletzung des „ewigen“ und so zu\n sagen „heiligen“ Gesetzes der Nachfrage und Zufuhr. Jeder Zusammenhalt\n zwischen den Beschäftigten und Unbeschäftigten stört nämlich das „reine“\n Spiel jenes Gesetzes. Sobald andrerseits, in den Kolonieen z. B., widrige\n Umstände die Schöpfung der industriellen Reservearmee,\n und mit ihr die absolute Abhängigkeit der Arbeiterklasse von der Ka-\n pitalistenklasse, verhindern, rebellirt das Kapital, sammt seinem gemein-\n plätzlichen Sancho Pansa, gegen das „heilige“ Gesetz der Nachfrage und Zu-\n fuhr, und sucht ihm durch Zwangsmittel unter die Arme zu greifen.\n\n\n\n\nDie relative Surpluspopulation existirt in allen möglichen Schattirungen.\n Jeder Arbeiter gehört dazu während der Zeit, wo er halb oder gar nicht be-\n schäftigt ist. Ohne hier auf Details einzugehn, genügen einige allgemeine\n Andeutungen. Abgesehn von den periodischen Formverschiedenheiten\n der Surpluspopulation in dem Phasenwechsel des industriellen\n Cyklus, wo sie bald akut in den Krisen erscheint, bald chronisch in\n der Periode der Stagnation, besitzt sie fortwährend drei Formen, die\nflüssige, die latente und die stagnante.\n\n\n\n\nMan hat gesehn, wie die Fabrikarbeiter bald repellirt, bald in\n grösserem Umfang wieder attrahirt werden, so dass im Grossen und Ganzen\n die Zahl der beschäftigten Arbeiter zunimmt, wenn auch in stets abnehmendem\n [Running matter: 40*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"628\", \"facs\": \"#f0647\"} --> Verhältniss zur Produktionsleiter. Die Surpluspopulation existirt hier in\nfliessender Form. Wir machen nur auf zwei Umstände aufmerksam. So-\n wohl in den eigentlichen Fabriken, wie in allen grossen Werkstätten, wo-\n rin die Maschinerie als Faktor eingeht oder auch nur die moderne Thei-\n lung der Arbeit durchgeführt ist, werden männliche Arbeiter bis zur Zu-\n rücklegung des Jugendalters in Massen verbraucht, wovon später nur sehr\n geringe Proportion in demselben Zweig verwendbar bleibt, daher beständig\n grosse Anzahl herausgeworfen wird. Sie bilden ein Element der fliessen-\n den Surpluspopulation, das mit dem Umfang der Industrie wächst. Ein Theil\n davon emigrirt und reist in der That nur dem emigrirenden Kapital nach.\n Eine der Folgen ist, dass die weibliche Bevölkerung rascher wächst als\n die männliche, teste England. Der Widerspruch, dass der natürliche Zu-\n wachs der Arbeiterbevölkerung den Accumulationsbedürfnissen des Kapitals\nnicht genügt, und andrerseits zu gross für seine Absorption ist, ist\n ein Widerspruch seiner Bewegung selbst. Es braucht grössere Massen\n davon im früheren Alter, weniger im männlichen. Der Widerspruch ist\n nicht grösser als der andre, dass während Arbeiter zu vielen Tausenden\n auf dem Pflaster liegen, weil die Theilung der Arbeit sie an einen be-\n stimmten Geschäftszweig kettet, gleichzeitig über Mangel an Händen\n geklagt wird [Note: Während im letzten Halbjahr von 1866 80—90,000 Arbeiter in London\n ausser Arbeit geworfen wurden, heisst es im Fabrikbericht über dasselbe Halbjahr:\n „It does not appear absolutely true to say that demand will always produce supply\n just at the moment when it is needed. It has not done it with labour, for much\n machinery has been idle last year for want of hands.“ („Reports of Insp.\n of Fact. for 31. Oct. 1866“, Lond. 1867, p. 81.)] . Bei dem raschen Konsum der Arbeitskraft durch das Kapi-\n tal ist der Arbeiter von mittlerem Alter meist schon überlebt. Er fällt in die\n Reihen der Surpluspopulation oder rückt von einer höhern Staffel auf eine\n niedrigere, während das Kapital seinen Platz durch frischere Arbeitskraft er-\n setzt. Das absolute Wachsthum der Arbeiterklasse erheischt so eine\n Form, welche ihre Zahl schwellt, obgleich ihre Elemente sich rasch abnutzen.\n Es ist daher rasche Ablösung der Arbeitergenerationen nöthig.\n (Dasselbe Gesetz gilt nicht für die übrigen Klassen der Bevölkerung.)\n Diess wird erreicht durch frühe Ehen, nothwendige Folge der Verhältnisse,\n worin die Arbeiter der grossen Industrie leben, und durch die Prämie,\n welche die Exploitation der Arbeiterkinder auf ihre Produktion setzt.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"629\", \"facs\": \"#f0648\"} -->\n\nSobald sich die kapitalistische Produktion der Agrikultur oder im\n Grad, worin sie sich derselben bemächtigt hat, nimmt mit der Accumula-\n tion des hier funktionirenden Kapitals die Nachfrage für\n die ländliche Arbeiterbevölkerung absolut ab, ohne dass\n ihre Repulsion, wie in der nicht agrikolen Industrie, durch grössere Attraktion\n ergänzt wäre. Ein Theil der Landbevölkerung befindet sich daher fortwährend\nim Uebergang zur Metamorphose in städtische oder Manu-\n fakturbevölkerung. (Manufaktur hier im Sinn aller nicht-agrikolen In-\n dustrie [Note: „781 Städte sind aufgezählt im Census von 1861 für England und Wales,\n mit 10,960,998 Einwohnern, während die Dörfer und Landkirchspiele nur\n 9,105,226 zählen … Im Jahr 1851 figurirten 580 Städte im Census, deren Be-\n völkerung ungefähr gleich der Bevölkerung der sie umgebenden Landdistrikte war.\n Während aber in den letzteren die Bevölkerung während der folgenden 10 Jahre\nnur um eine halbe Million wuchs, wuchs sie in den 580 Städten um 1,554,067.\n Der Bevölkerungszuwachs in den Landkirchspielen ist 6.5 %, in den Städten\n 17.3 %. Der Unterschied in der Rate des Wachsthums ist der Wanderung\n vom Land in die Stadt geschuldet. Drei Viertel des Gesammtwachsthums der\n Bevölkerung gehört den Stäten.“ („Census etc.“, v. III, p. 11, 12.)] .) Diese Quelle der relativen Surpluspopulation fliesst also\n beständig. Aber ihr beständiger Fluss setzt auf dem Lande selbst\n eine fortwährend latente Surpluspopulation voraus, deren Umfang nur\n sichtbar wird, sobald sich die Abzugskanäle ausnahmsweise weit öffnen.\n Der Landarbeiter wird daher auf das Minimum des Salairs herabgedrückt\n und steht mit einem Fuss stets im Sumpf des Pauperismus.\n\n\n\n\nDie stagnante Surpluspopulation bildet einen Theil der aktiven\n Arbeiterarmee, aber mit durchaus unregelmässiger Beschäftigung, so dass\n das Kapital hier stets eine ausserordentliche Masse latenter Arbeitskraft\n zur Verfügung hat. Ihre Lebenslage sinkt unter das durchschnittliche\n Normalniveau der arbeitenden Klasse und grade diess macht sie zur breiten\n Grundlage eigner Exploitationszweige des Kapitals. Maximum der Arbeitszeit\n und Minimum des Salairs charakterisiren sie. Wir haben unter der Rubrik\n der Hausarbeit ihre Hauptgestalt bereits kennen gelernt. Sie rekrutirt\n sich fortwährend aus den Ueberzähligen der grossen Industrie und Agri-\n kultur, und namentlich auch in untergehenden Industriezweigen, wo der\n Handwerksbetrieb dem Manufakturbetrieb, letztrer dem Maschinenbetrieb\n erliegt. Ihr Umfang dehnt sich, wie mit Umfang und Energie der Accu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"630\", \"facs\": \"#f0649\"} --> mulation die „Ueberzähligmachung“ fortschreitet. Aber sie bildet zu-\n gleich ein sich selbst reproducirendes und verewigendes Element der Ar-\n beiterklasse, das verhältnissmässig grösseren Antheil am Gesammtwachs-\n thum derselben nimmt als die übrigen Elemente. In der That steht\n nicht nur die Masse der Geburten und Todesfälle, sondern\n die absolute Grösse der Familien in umgekehrtem Verhält-\n niss zur Höhe des Arbeitslohns, also zur Masse der Lebens-\n mittel, worüber die verschiednen Arbeiterkategorieen verfü-\n gen. Diess Gesetz der kapitalistischen Gesellschaft klänge unsinnig\n unter Wilden, oder selbst civilisirten Kolonisten. Es erinnert an die massenhafte\n Reproduktion individuell schwacher und vielgehetzter Thierarten [Note: „Poverty seems favourable to generation.“ (A. Smith.) Diess ist sogar\n eine besonders weise Einrichtung Gottes nach dem galanten und geistreichen Abbé\nGaliani: „Iddio fa che gli uomini che esercitano mestieri di prima utilità nascono\n abbondanemente.“ (Galiani l. c. p. 78.) „Misery, up to the extreme point of\n famine and pestilence, instead of checking, tends to increase popula-\n tion.“ (S. Laing: „National Distress. 1844“, p. 69.) Nachdem Laing\n diess statistisch illustrirt, fährt er fort: „Befände sich alle Welt in bequemen Um-\n ständen, so wäre die Welt bald entvölkert.“ („If the people were all in easy\n circumstances, the world would soon be depopulated.“)] .\n\n\n\n\nDer tiefste Niederschlag der relativen Surpluspopulation endlich bil-\n det die Sphäre des Pauperismus. Sie besteht — wir sehn hier ab von\n Vagabunden, Verbrechern, Prostituirten, kurz dem eigentlichen Lumpen-\n proletariat — aus drei Kategorieen: erstens, Arbeitsfähige.\n Man braucht die Statistik des englischen Pauperismus nur oberflächlich an-\n zusehn und man findet, dass seine Masse mit jeder Krise schwillt und mit\n jeder Wiederbelebung des Geschäfts abnimmt. Zweitens: Waisen-\n und Pauperkinder. Sie sind Kandidaten der industriellen Reservearmee\n und werden in Zeiten grosser Prosperität, wie 1860 z. B., rasch und mas-\n senhaft in die aktive Arbeiterarmee einrollirt. Drittens: Verkommene,\n Verlumpte, Arbeitsunfähige. Es sind namentlich Arbeiter, die an ihrer\n durch die Theilung der Arbeit verursachten Unbeweglichkeit untergehn,\n solche, die über das Normalalter eines Arbeiters hinausleben, endlich die\n Opfer der Industrie, deren Zahl mit gefährlicher Maschinerie, Bergwerks-\n bau, chemischen Fabriken u. s. w. wächst, Verstümmelte, Verkrankte,\n Wittwen u. s. w. Der Pauperismus bildet das Invalidenhaus der aktiven\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"631\", \"facs\": \"#f0650\"} --> Arbeiterarmee und das todte Gewicht der industriellen Reservearmee. Seine\n Produktion ist eingeschlossen in der Produktion der Surpluspopulation,\n seine Nothwendigkeit in ihrer Nothwendigkeit, mit ihr bildet er eine Ex-\n istenzbedingung der kapitalistischen Produktion und Entwicklung des Reich-\n thums. Er gehört zu den faux frais der kapitalistischen Produktion, die\n das Kapital jedoch grossentheils von sich selbst ab auf die Schultern der\n Arbeiterklasse und der kleinen Mittelklasse zu wälzen weiss.\n\n\n\n\nJe grösser der gesellschaftliche Reichthum, das funktionirende\n Kapital, Umfang und Energie seines Wachsthums, also auch die absolute\n Grösse der Arbeiterbevölkerung und die Produktivkraft ihrer\n Arbeit, desto grösser die relative Surpluspopulation oder industrielle Reserve-\n armee. Die disponible Arbeitskraft wird durch dieselben Ursa-\n chen entwickelt, wie die Expansivkraft des Kapitals. Die verhält-\n nissmässige Grösse der industriellen Reservearmee wächst also mit den Poten-\n zen des Reichthums. Je grösser aber diese Reservearmee im Verhältniss zur\n aktiven Arbeiterarmee, desto massenhafter die konsolidirte Surpluspopu-\n lation oder die Arbeiterschichten, deren Elend im umgekehrten Verhältniss\n zu ihrer Arbeitsqual steht. Je grösser endlich die Lazarusschichte der\n Arbeiterklasse und die industrielle Reservearmee, desto grösser der offi-\n cielle Pauperismus. Diess ist das absolute, allgemeine Gesetz\n der kapitalistischen Accumulation. Es wird gleich allen all-\n gemeinen Gesetzen in seiner Verwirklichung durch mannigfache Umstände\n modificirt, deren Analyse nicht hierher gehört.\n\n\n\n\nMan begreift die Narrheit der ökonomischen Weisheit, die den Arbeitern\n predigt, ihre Zahl den Verwerthungsbedürfnissen des Kapitals\n anzupassen. Der Mechanismus der kapitalistischen Produktion und Ac-\n cumulation passt sie beständig diesen Verwerthungsbedürfnissen an. Erstes\n Wort dieser Anpassung ist die Schöpfung einer relativen Surpluspopulation\n oder industriellen Reservearmee, letztes Wort das Elend stets wachsender\n Schichten der aktiven Arbeiterarmee und das todte Gewicht des Pauperismus.\n\n\n\n\nDas Gesetz, dass die Entwicklung der gesellschaftlichen Produktiv-\n kraft der Arbeit die Masse der zu verausgabenden Arbeitskraft im Verhält-\n niss zur Wirkung und Masse ihrer Produktionsmittel progressiv senkt,\n drückt sich auf kapitalistischer Grundlage, wo nicht der Arbeiter\n die Arbeitsmittel, sondern die Arbeitsmittel den Arbeiter anwenden, darin\n aus, dass je höher die Produktivkraft der Arbeit, desto grös-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"632\", \"facs\": \"#f0651\"} --> ser der Druck der Arbeiter auf ihre Beschäftigungsmittel\n und desto prekärer die Existenzbedingung des Lohnarbeiters, Verkauf\n seiner Arbeitskraft zur Vermehrung des fremden Reichthums oder\n Selbstverwerthung des Kapitals. Rascheres Wachsthum der Pro-\n duktionsmittel und der Produktivkraft der Arbeit als der\n produktiven Bevölkerung drücktsich kapitalistisch um-\n gekehrt darin aus, dass die Arbeiterbevölkerung stets rascher\n wächst als das Verwerthungsbedürfniss des Kapitals.\n\n\n\n\nEs zeigte sich im vierten Kapitel bei Analyse der Produktion des\n relativen Mehrwerths, dass alle Methoden zur Steigerung der gesellschaft-\n lichen Produktivkraft der Arbeit in der kapitalistischen Form sich auf\n Kosten des individuellen Arbeiters entwickeln, dass alle Mittel zur Berei-\n cherung der Produktion in Beherrschungs- und Exploitationsmittel des\n Producenten umschlagen, dass sie den Arbeiter in einen Theilmenschen\n verstümmeln, ihn zum Anhängsel der Maschine entwürdigen, mit der Qual\n der Arbeit ihren Inhalt vernichten, ihm die geistigen Potenzen des Arbeits-\n prozesses entfremden, im selben Masse, worin derselbe sich die Wissen-\n schaft als selbstständige Potenz einverleibt, die Bedingungen, innerhalb\n deren er arbeitet, beständig anormaler machen, ihn während des Arbeits-\n prozesses der kleinlichst gehässigen Despotie unterwerfen, seine Lebens-\n zeit in Arbeitszeit verwandeln, sein Weib und Kind unter das Juggernaut-\n rad des Kapitals schleudern. Aber alle Methoden zur Produktion des Mehr-\n werths sind zugleich Methoden der Accumulation und jede Ausdehnung der Ac-\n cumulation wird umgekehrt Mittel zur Entwicklung jener Methoden. Es folgt\n daher, dass im Masse wie Kapital accumulirt, die Lage des Arbeiters, wel-\n ches immer seine Zahlung, sich verschlechtert. Das Gesetz endlich,\n welches die relativeSurpluspopulation oder industrielle Re-\n servearmee stets mit Umfang und Energie der Accumu-\n lation in Gleichgewicht hält, schmiedet den Arbeiter fester an\n das Kapital als den Prometheus die Keile des Hephästos an den Felsen.\n Es bedingt eine der Accumulation von Kapital entsprechende Ac-\n cumulation von Elend. Die Accumulation von Reichthum auf dem\n einen Pol ist also zugleich Accumulation von Elend, Arbeitsqual, Skla-\n verei, Unwissenheit, Brutalisirung und moralischer Degradation auf dem\n Gegenpol, d. h. auf Seite der Klasse, die ihr eignes Produkt als\n Kapital producirt.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"633\", \"facs\": \"#f0652\"} -->\n\nDieser antagonistische Charakter der kapitalisti-\n schen Accumulation [Note: „De jour en jour il devient donc plus clair que les rapports de production\n dans lesquels se meut la bourgeoisie n’ont pas un caractère un, un caractère\n simple, mais un caractère de duplicité; que dans les mêmes rapports dans lesquels\n se produit la richesse, la misère se produit aussi; que dans les mêmes rapports\n dans lesquels il y a développement des forces productives, il y a une force produc-\n tive de répression; que ces rapports ne produisent la richesse bourgeoise,\n c’est à dire la richesse de la classe bourgeoise, qu’en anéantissant continuellement\n la richesse des membres intégrants de cette classe et en produisant un prolétariat\n toujours croissant.“ (Karl Marx: „Misère de la Philosophie“, p. 116.)]  ist in verschiedenen Formen von politischen\n Oekonomen ausgesprochen, obgleich sie zum Theil zwar analoge, aber\n dennoch wesentlich verschiedene Erscheinungen vorkapitalistischer\n Produktionsweisen damit zusammenwerfen.\n\n\n\n\nDer venetianische Mönch Ortes, einer der grossen ökonomischen\n Schriftsteller des 18. Jahrhunderts, fasst den Antagonismus der kapi-\n talistischen Produktion als allgemeines Naturgesetz des\n gesellschaftlichen Reichthums. „Das Ökonomisch Gute und ökono-\n misch Böse halten sich in einer Nation stets das Gleichgewicht („il bene\n ed il male economico in una nazione sempre all’ istessa misura“), die Fülle\n der Güter für Einige ist immer gleich dem Mangel derselben für Andre\n („la copia dei beni in alcuni sempre equale alla mancanza di esse in altri“).\n Grosser Reichthum in Einigen ist stets begleitet von absoluter Beraubung\n des Nothwendigen in viel mehr andern“ [Note: G. Ortes: „Della Economia Nazionale, libri sei 1777“, bei\nCustodi, Parte Moderna. t. XXI, p. 6, 9, 22, 25 etc. Ortes sagt l. c.\n p. 32: „In luoco di progettar sistimi inutili per la felicità de’ popoli, mi limiterò\n a investigare la ragione della loro infelicità.“] . Der Reichthum einer Nation\n entspricht ihrer Bevölkerung und ihr Elend entspricht ihrem Reichthum.\n Die Arbeitsamkeit in Einigen erzwingt den Müssiggang in Andern.\n Die Armen und Müssigen sind eine nothwendige Frucht der Reichen\n und Thätigen u. s. w. In ganz grober Weise verherrlichte ungefähr\n 10 Jahre nach Ortes der hochkirchliche protestantische Pfaffe Town-\n send die Armuth als nothwendige Bedingung des Reichthums. „Ge-\n setzlicher Zwang zur Arbeit ist verbunden mit zu viel\n Mühe, Gewaltsamkeit und Geräusch, während der Hunger nicht nur\n ein friedlicher, schweigsamer, unaufhörlicher Druck, sondern als natürlich-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"634\", \"facs\": \"#f0653\"} --> stes Motiv zur Industrie und Arbeit die machtvollste Anstrengung hervor-\n ruft.“ Alles kömmt also darauf an, den Hunger unter der Arbeiterklasse\n permanent zu machen, und dafür sorgt, nach Townsend, das Bevölkerungs-\n princip, das besonders unter den Armen thätig ist. „Es scheint ein\n Naturgesetz, dass die Armen zu einem gewissen Grad leichtsinnig\n (improvident) sind (nämlich so leichtsinnig auf die Welt zu kommen ohne\n goldne Löffel im Mund), so dass stets welche da sind („that there al-\n ways may be some“) zur Erfüllung der servilsten, schmutzigsten und ge-\n meinsten Funktionen des Gemeinwesens. Der Fonds von menschlichem\n Glück („the fonds of human happiness“) wird dadurch sehr vermehrt, die\n Delikateren („the more delicate“) sind von der Plackerei befreit und kön-\n nen höherem Beruf u. s. w. ungestört nachgehn … Das Armengesetz hat\n die Tendenz, die Harmonie und Schönheit, die Symmetrie und Ordnung die-\n ses Systems, welches Gott und die Natur in der Welt errichtet\n haben, zu zerstören“ [Note: „A Dissertation on the Poor Laws. By a Wellwisher of\n Mankind. (The Rev. Mr. J. Townsend.) 1786“, republished Lond.\n 1817, p. 15. Dieser „delikate“ Pfaffe, dessen eben angeführte Schrift, nebst seiner\n Reise durch Spanien, Malthus oft Seiten lang abschreibt, entlehnte den grössten\n Theil seiner Doktrin aus Sir J. Steuart, den er jedoch verdreht. Z. B. wenn\n Steuart sagt: „Hier, in der Sklaverei, existirte eine gewaltsame Methode\n die Menschheit arbeitsam [für die Nichtarbeiter] zu machen … Die Menschen\n wurden damals zur Arbeit [d. h. zur Gratisarbeit für Andere] gezwungen, weil sie\nSklaven von andern waren; die Menschen sind jetzt zur Arbeit [d. h. zur\n Gratisarbeit für Nichtarbeiter] gezwungen, weil sie die Sklaven ihrer eignen\n Bedürfnisse sind“, so schliesst er desswegen nicht, wie der fette Pfründner,\n dass — die Lohnarbeiter stets am Hungertuch nagen sollen. Er will umgekehrt\n ihre Bedürfnisse vermehren und die wachsende Zahl ihrer Bedürfnisse zugleich zum\n Sporn ihrer Arbeit für „die Delikateren“ machen.] . „Der Fortschritt des gesellschaft-\n lichen Reichthums“, sagt Storch, „erzeugt jene nützliche\n Klasse der Gesellschaft … welche die langweiligsten, gemeinsten\n und ekelhaftesten Beschäftigungen ausübt, in einem Wort alles, was das\n Leben unangenehmes und knechtendes hat, auf ihre Schultern nimmt und\n eben dadurch den andern Klassen die Zeit, die Heiterkeit des Geistes\n und die konventionelle (c’est bon!) Charakterwürde ver-\n schafft etc.“ [Note: Storch l. c. t. III, p. 223.] . Storch fragt sich, welches denn eigentlich der Vorzug\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"635\", \"facs\": \"#f0654\"} --> dieser kapitalistischen Civilisation mit ihrem Elend und ihrer Degradation der\n Massen vor der Barbarei? Er findet nur eine Antwort — die Sicher-\n heit! „Durch den Fortschritt der Industrie und Wissenschaft,“ sagt\nSismondi, „kann jeder Arbeiter jeden Tag viel mehr produciren als er\n zu seinem Konsum braucht. Aber zu gleicher Zeit, während seine Arbeit\n den Reichthum producirt, würde der Reichthum, wäre er berufen ihn selbst\n zu konsumiren, ihn wenig geeignet zur Arbeit machen“ [Note: Sismondi l. c. t. I, p. 85.] . „Die armen\n Nationen,“ sagt Destutt de Tracy, „sind die, wo das Volk gut dran\n ist, und die reichen Nationen sind die, wo es gewöhnlich arm ist“ [Note: Destutt de Tracy l. c. p. 231: „Les nations pauvres, c’est là où\n le peuple est à son aise; et les nations riches, c’est là où il est ordinairement\n pauvre.“] .\n\n\n\n\nKeine Periode der modernen Gesellschaft ist so günstig für das Stu-\n dium der kapitalistischen Accumulation als die Periode der letztver-\n flossenen 20 Jahre. Es ist als ob sie den Fortunatussäckel gefunden\n hätte. Von allen Ländern aber bietet England wieder das klassische Bei-\n spiel, weil es den ersten Rang auf dem Weltmarkt behauptet, die kapita-\n listische Produktionsweise hier allein völlig entwickelt ist, und endlich die\n Einführung des tausendjährigen Reichs des Freihandels seit 1846 der\n Vulgärökonomie den letzten Schlupfwinkel abgeschnitten hat. Der tita-\n nische Fortschritt der Produktion, so dass die letzte Hälfte der zwanzig-\n jährigen Periode die erste wieder weit überflügelt, ward bereits im vierten\n Kapitel hinreichend angedeutet.\n\n\n\n\nObgleich das absolute Wachsthum der englischen Bevölkerung im\n letzten halben Jahrhundert sehr gross war, fiel das verhältnissmäs-\n sige Wachsthum oder die Rate des Zuwachses fortwäh-\n rend, wie folgende dem officiellen Census entlehnte Tabelle zeigt:\nJährlicher procentmässiger Zuwachs der Bevölkerung\n von England und Wales in Decimalzahlen.\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"636\", \"facs\": \"#f0655\"} -->\n\nBetrachten wir nun andrerseits das Wachsthum des Reichthums.\n Den sichersten Anhaltspunkt bietet hier die Bewegung der der Einkommen-\n steuer unterworfenen Profite, Grundrenten u. s. w. Der Zuwachs der steuer-\n pflichtigen Profite (Pächter und einige andre Rubriken nicht eingeschlos-\n sen) betrug für Grossbritanien von 1853—1864 50.47 % (oder 4.58 % im\n jährlichen Durchschnitt) [Note: „Tenth Report of the Commissioners of H. M.’s Inland\n Revenue. Lond. 1866“, p. 38.] , der der Bevölkerung während der-\n selben Periode ungefähr 12 %. Die Zunahme der besteuerbaren Ren-\n ten von Land (Häuser, Eisenbahnen, Minen, Fischereien u. s. w. einge-\n schlossen) betrug von 1853—1864 38 %, oder 3\\frac{5}{12} % jährlich, woran fol-\n gende Rubriken den stärksten Antheil nahmen:\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nVergleicht man je vier Jahre der Periode von 1853—1864, so\n wächst der Zunahmegrad der Einkommen fortwährend. Er ist z. B.\n für die aus Profit stammenden von 1853—1857 jährlich 1.73 %,\n 1857—1861 jährlich 2.74 %, und 9.30 % jährlich für 1861—1864.\n Die Gesammtsumme der der Einkommensteuer unterworfenen Einkommen\n des Vereinigten Königreichs betrug 1856: 307,068,898 Pfd. St., 1859:\n 328,127,416 Pfd. St., 1862: 351,745,241 Pfd. St., 1863: 359,142,897\n Pfd. St., 1864: 362,462,279 Pfd. St., 1865: 385,530,020 Pfd. St. [Note: Diese Zahlen sind hinreichend für die Vergleichung, aber, absolut be-\n trachtet, falsch, da vielleicht 100 Millionen Pfd. St. Einkommen jährlich „ver-\n schwiegen“ werden. Die Klage der Commissioners of the Inland Revenue über\n systematischen Betrug, namentlich von kommercieller und industrieller Seite,\n wiederholt sich in jedem ihrer Berichte. So heisst es z. B.: „Eine Aktiengesell-\n schaft gab ihre besteuerbaren Profite auf 6000 Pfd. St. an, der Taxator veran-] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"637\", \"facs\": \"#f0656\"} -->\n\nDie Accumulation des Kapitals war zugleich von seiner Kon-\n centration begleitet. Obgleich keine officielle Agrikulturstatistik für Eng-\n land (wohl aber für Irland) existirt, wurde sie von 10 Grafschaften freiwillig\n geliefert. Sie ergab hier das Resultat, dass von 1851 bis 1861 die\n Pachten unter 100 Acres von 31,583 auf 26,567 vermindert, also 5,016\n mit grösseren Pachten zusammengeschlagen waren [Note: Census etc. l. c. p. 29. Bright’s Behauptung, dass 150 Landlords die\n Hälfte des englischen und 12 die Hälfte des schottischen Bodens eignen, ist nicht\n widerlegt worden.] . Von 1815 bis\n 1825 fiel kein Mobiliarvermögen über 1 Million Pfd. St. unter die Erb-\n schaftssteuer, von 1825 bis 1855 dagegen 8, von 1856 bis Juni 1859,\n d. h. in 4½ Jahren, 4 [Note: „Fourth Report etc. of Inland Revenue. Lond. 1860“, p. 17.] . Die Koncentration wird man jedoch am besten\n ersehn aus einer kurzen Analyse der Einkommensteuer für Rubrik D\n (Profite mit Ausschluss von Pächtern u. s. w.) in den Jahren 1864 und\n 1865. Ich bemerke vorher, dass Einkommen aus dieser Quelle bis zu\n 60 Pfd. St. hinab Income Taxe zahlen. Diese steuerpflichtigen Ein-\n kommen betrugen in England, Wales und Schottland 1864: 95,844,222\n Pfd. St. und 1865: 105,435,579 Pfd. St. [Note: Es sind diess die Reineinkommen, also nach gewissen gesetzlich gültigen\n Abzügen.] , die Zahl der Be-\n steuerten 1864 : 308,416 Personen auf eine Gesammtbevölkerung von\n 23,891,009, 1865 : 332,431 Personen auf Gesammtbevölkerung von\n 24,127,003. Ueber die Vertheilung dieser Einkommen in beiden Jahren\n folgende Tabelle:\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n [Note: schlagte sie zu 88,000 Pfd. St., und für diese Summe ward schliesslich die Steuer\n gezahlt. Eine andre Kompagnie gab 190,000 Pfd. St. an, sie ward gezwungen zu\n gestehn, dass der wirkliche Betrag 250,000 Pfd. St.“ (l. c. p. 42.)] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"638\", \"facs\": \"#f0657\"} -->\n\nEs wurden im Vereinigten Königreich 1855 producirt 61,453,079\n Tonnen Kohlen zum Werth von 16,113,267 Pfd. St., 1864 :\n 92,787,873 Tonnen zum Werth von 23,197,968 Pfd. St., 1855 :\n 3,218,154 Tonnen Roheisen zum Werth von 8,045,385 Pfd. St.,\n1864: 4,767,951 Tonnen zum Werth von 11,919,877 Pfd. St.\n1854 betrug die Länge der im Vereinigten Königreich eröffneten Eisen-\n bahnen 8054 Meilen, mit aufgezahltem Kapital von 286,068,794 Pfd. St.,\n1864 die Meilenlänge 12,789 mit aufgezahltem Kapital von 425,719,613\n Pfd. St. 1854 betrug Gesammtexport und Import des Vereinigten\n Königreichs 268,210,145 Pfd. St., 1865: 489,923,285. Folgende\n Tabelle zeigt die Bewegung des Exports:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1846 58,842,377 Pfd. St.\n\n\n\n\n1849 63,596,052 „ „\n\n\n\n\n1856 115,826,948 „ „\n\n\n\n\n1860 135,842,817 „ „\n\n\n\n\n1865 165,862,402 „ „\n\n\n\n\n1866 ungefähr 190,000,000 „ „  [Note: In diesem Augenblick, März 1867, ist der indisch-chinesische Markt\n durch die Konsignationen der britischen Baumwollfabrikanten schon wieder völlig\n überführt. Lohnherabsetzung um 5 % begann unter den Baumwollarbeitern 1866,\n jetzt in Folge ähnlicher Operation Strike von 20,000 Mann in Preston.] .\n\n\n\n\n\n\nMan begreift, nach diesen wenigen Angaben, den Triumphschrei des\nGeneralregistrators des brit. Volks: „Rasch wie die Bevölkerung\n anwuchs, hat sie nicht Schritt gehalten mit dem Fortschritt der\n Industrie und des Reichthums“ [Note: Census etc. l. c. p. 11.] . Wenden wir uns jetzt zu den\n unmittelbaren Agenten dieser Industrie oder den Producenten dieses Reich-\n thums, zur Arbeiterklasse. „Es ist einer der melancholischsten Charak-\n terzüge im socialen Zustand des Landes“, sagt Gladstone, „dass mit einer\n Abnahme in der Konsumtionsmacht des Volks und einer Zunahme in den\n Entbehrungen und dem Elend der arbeitenden Klasse, gleichzeitig eine\n beständige Accumulation von Reichthum in den höheren Klassen und ein\n beständiger Anwachs von Kapital stattfinden“ [Note: Gladstone im Hause der Gemeinen, 14. Febr. 1843: „It is one of\n the most melancholy features in the social state of the country, that while there\n was a decrease in the consuming power of the people, and an increase in the pri-] . Der berühmte Mini-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"639\", \"facs\": \"#f0658\"} --> ster erklärte diess dem Hause der Gemeinen am 14. Februar 1843. Am\n 16. April 1863, zwanzig Jahre später, in der Rede, worin er sein Budget\n vorlegt: „Von 1842 bis 1852 wuchs das besteuerbare Einkommen dieses\n Landes um 6 % … In den 8 Jahren von 1853 bis 1861 wuchs es, wenn\n wir von der Basis von 1853 ausgehn, um 20 %. Die Thatsache ist so\n erstaunlich, dass sie beinahe unglaublich ist … Diese berauschende\n Vermehrung von Reichthum und Macht ist ganz und gar auf\n die Klassen des Eigenthums beschränkt, aber … aber, sie\n muss von indirektem Vortheil für die Arbeiterbevölkerung sein,\n weil sie die Artikel der allgemeinen Konsumtion verwohlfeilert —\n während die Reichen reicher, sind die Armen jedenfalls weniger arm ge-\n worden. Dass die Extreme der Armuth sich verändert\n haben, wage ich nicht zu sagen“ [Note: „From 1842 to 1852 the taxable income of the country increased by 6\n per cent … In the 8 years from 1853 to 1861, it had increased from the basis\n taken in 1853, 20 per cent! The fact is so astonishing as to be almost incredible\n … This intoxicating augmentation of wealth and power is entirely confined to\n classes of property, but must be of indirect benefit to the labouring population,\n because it cheapens the commodities of general consumption — while the rich have\n been growing richer the poor have been growing less poor! at any rate, whether\n the extremes of poverty are less, I do not presume to say.“ Gladstone im\n H. o. C. 16. April 1863.] . Welch lahmer Antiklimax!\n Wenn die Arbeiterklasse „arm“ geblieben ist, nur „weniger arm“ im Ver-\n haltniss, worin sie eine „berauschende Vermehrung von Reichthum und\n Macht“ für die Klasse des Eigenthums producirte, so ist sie relativ gleich\n arm geblieben. Wenn die Extreme der Armuth sich nicht vermin-\n dert haben, haben sie sich vermehrt, weil die Extreme des Reichthums.\n Was die Verwohlfeilerung der Lebensmittel betrifft, so zeigt die\n officielle Statistik, z. B. die Angaben des London Orphan Asylum,\n eine Vertheurung von 20 % für den Durchschnitt der drei Jahre von\n 1860—1862, verglichen mit 1851—1853. In den folgenden 3 Jahren\n 1863—1865 progressive Vertheurung von Fleisch, Butter, Milch, Zucker,\n [Note: vations and distress of the labouring class and operatives, there was at the same\n time a constant accumulation of wealth in the upper classes and a constant in-\n crease of capital.“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"640\", \"facs\": \"#f0659\"} --> Salz, Kohlen und einer Masse andrer nothwendiger Lebensmittel [Note: Sieh die officiellen Angaben in dem Blaubuch: „Miscellaneous\n Statistics of the Un. Kingdom. Part VI. Lond. 1866“, p. 260—273\npassim.] .\n Gladstone’s folgende Budgetrede, vom 7. April 1864, ist ein pindarischer\n Dithyrambus auf den Fortschritt der Plusmacherei und das durch „Ar-\n muth“ gemässigte Glück des Volks. Er spricht von Massen „am Rand\n des Pauperismus“, von den Geschäftszweigen, „worin der Lohn nicht\n gestiegen“, und fasst schliesslich das Glück der Arbeiterklasse zusammen\n in den Worten: „das menschliche Leben ist in neun Fällen von 10 ein\n blosser Kampf um die Existenz“ [Note: „Think of those who are on the border of that region (pauperism)“,\n „wages … in others not increased … human life is but, in nine cases out of\n ten, a struggle for existence.“ (Gladstone, H. o. C. 7. April 1864.) Die\n fortlaufenden, schreienden Widersprüche in Gladstone’s Budgetreden von 1863\n und 1864 charakterisirt ein englischer Schriftsteller durch folgendes Citat aus\n Moliere:\n„Voilà l’homme en effet. Il va du blanc au noir.\n Il condamne au matin ses sentimens du soir.\n Importun à tout autre, à soi même incommode,\n Il change à tous momens d’esprit comme de mode.“\n („The Theory of Exchanges etc. Lond. 1864“, p. 135.)] . Professor Fawcett, nicht wie\n Gladstone durch officielle Rücksicht gebunden, erklärt rund heraus: „Ich\n läugne natürlich nicht, dass der Geldlohn mit dieser Vermehrung des Ka-\n pitals (in den letzten Decennien) gestiegen ist, aber dieser scheinbare Vor-\n theil geht in grossem Umfang wieder verloren, weil viele Lebensnothwen-\n digkeiten beständig theurer werden (er glaubt, wegen Werthfall der edlen\n Metalle) … Die Reichen werden rasch reicher („the rich grow rapidly\n richer“), während keine Zunahme im Komfort der arbeitenden Klassen\n wahrnehmbar ist … Die Arbeiter werden fast Sklaven der Krämer,\n deren Schuldner sie sind“ [Note: H. Fawcett l. c. p. 67, 82. Was die wachsende Abhängigkeit der\n Arbeiter von dem Krämer betrifft, so ist sie Folge der zunehmenden Schwankungen\n und Unterbrechungen ihrer Beschäftigung.] .\n\n\n\n\nDer Leser hat in den Abschnitten über den „Arbeitstag“ und die „Ma-\n schinerie“ die Bedingungen kennen gelernt, unter welchen die britische\n Arbeiterklasse während der letzten Decennien die „berauschende Ver-\n mehrung von Reichthum und Macht“ für die Klassen des Eigenthums pro-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"641\", \"facs\": \"#f0660\"} --> ducirt hat. Jedoch beschäftigte uns damals vorzugsweise der Arbeiter\ninnerhalb des Produktionsprozesses selbst. Zur gründlichen Einsicht\n in das Gesetz der kapitalistischen Accumulation ist es nöthig einen Augen-\n blick bei der Lage des Arbeiters ausserhalb jenes Prozesses, bei seinen\n Nahrungs- und Wohnungszuständen zu verweilen. Die Grenze dieses\n Buchs gebietet mir hier namentlich den schlechtbezahlten Theil der\n industriellen Arbeiter und den Agrikulturarbeiter ins Auge zu fassen,\n welche zusammen die Majorität der Arbeiterklasse bilden [Note: Es steht zu hoffen, dass F. Engels bald sein Werk über die Lage der\n englischen Arbeiterklasse mit der Periode seit 1844 bereichern, oder auch die Dar-\n stellung der letztern Periode in einem besondern zweiten Band herausgeben wird.] .\n\n\n\n\nVorher noch ein Wort über den officiellen Pauperismus,\n d. h. den Theil der Arbeiterklasse, der seine Existenzbedingung, Verkauf\n der Arbeitskraft, eingebüsst hat und von öffentlichem Almosen vegetirt.\n Die officielle Pauperliste zählte in England [Note: In England ist immer Wales eingeschlossen, in Grossbritanien\n England, Wales und Schottland, im Vereinigten Königreich jene drei\n Länder und Irland.] 1855: 851,369 Per-\n sonen, 1856: 877,767, 1865: 971,433. In Folge der Baumwoll-\n noth schwoll sie in den Jahren 1863 und 1864 zu 1,079,382 und\n 1,014,978. Die Krise von 1866, die London am schwersten traf, schuf\n in diesem Sitz des Weltmarkts, einwohnerreicher als das Königreich Schott-\n land, für 1866 einen Pauperzuwachs um 19.5 %, verglichen mit 1865,\n und um 24.4 %, verglichen mit 1864, einen noch grössern Zu-\n wachs für die ersten Monate von 1867, verglichen mit 1866. Bei\n Analyse der Pauperstatistik sind zwei Punkte hervorzuheben. Einerseits\n spiegelt die Bewegung im Ab und Zu der Paupermasse die Perioden-\n wechsel des industriellen Cyklus wieder. Andrerseits wird die offi-\n cielle Pauperstatistik ein mehr und mehr trüglicher Index des wirk-\n lichen Pauperismus im Grad, worin mit der Accumulation des Kapitals der\nKlassenkampf und daher das Selbstgefühl der Arbeiter sich entwickeln.\n Z. B. die Barbarei in der Behandlung der Paupers, worüber die englische\n Presse (Times, Pall Mall Gazette u. s. w.) während der letzten\n zwei Jahre so laut schrie, ist alten Datums. F. Engels konstatirt\n 1844 ganz dieselben Greuel und ganz dasselbe vorübergehende, zur „Sen-\n [Running matter: I. 41] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"642\", \"facs\": \"#f0661\"} --> sationliteratur“ gehörige Geschrei. Aber die furchtbare Zunahme des\n Hungertods („deaths of starvation“) in London, während des letzten De-\n cenniums, beweist unbedingt den zunehmenden Abscheu der Arbeiter vor\n der Sklaverei des Workhouse, dieser Strafanstalt des Elends.\n\n\n\n\nWenden wir uns jetzt zu den schlechtbezahlten Schichten der indu-\n striellen Arbeiterklasse. Während der Baumwollnoth, 1862, wurde\nDr. Smith vom Privy Council mit einer Untersuchung über den\n Nahrungsstand der verkümmernden Baumwollarbeiter in Lancashire und\n Cheshire beauftragt. Langjährige frühere Beobachtung hatte ihn zum\n Resultat geführt, dass „um Hungerkrankheiten („starvation\n diseases“) zu vermeiden“, die tägliche Nahrung eines Durchschnitts-\n Frauenzimmers mindestens 3,900 Gran Kohlenstoff mit 180 Gran Stick-\n stoff enthalten müsse, die tägliche Nahrung eines Durchschnitts-Mannes\n mindestens 4,300 Gran Kohlenstoff mit 200 Gran Stickstoff, für die\n Frauenzimmer ungefähr so viel Nahrungsstoff als in zwei Pfund gutem Wei-\n zenbrod enthalten ist, für Männer ⅑ mehr, für den Wochendurchschnitt\n von weiblichen und männlichen Erwachsnen, mindestens 28,600 Gran\n Kohlenstoff und 1330 Gran Stickstoff. Seine Berechnung ward in über-\n raschender Weise praktisch dadurch bestätigt, dass sie im Ganzen über-\n einstimmte mit der kümmerlichen Nahrungsmenge, worauf der Nothstand\n die Konsumtion der Baumwollarbeiter herabgedrückt hatte. Sie erhielten\n im December 1862: 29,211 Gran Kohlenstoff und 1295 Gran Stickstoff\n wöchentlich.\n\n\n\n\nIm Jahre 1863 verordnete der Privy Council eine Untersuchung\n über den Nothstand des schlechtgenährtesten Theils der englischen Arbei-\n terklasse. Dr. Simon, der ärztliche Beamte des Privy Council, er-\n kieste zu dieser Arbeit den obenerwähnten Dr. Smith. Seine Unter-\n suchung erstreckt sich auf die Agrikulturarbeiter einerseits, andrerseits\n auf Seidenweber, Nätherinnen, Lederhandschuhmacher, Strumpfwirker,\n Handschuhweber und Schuster. Die letzteren Kategorieen sind, mit Aus-\n nahme der Strumpfwirker, ausschliesslich städtisch. Es wurde zur Regel der\n Untersuchung gemacht, die gesundesten und relativ bestgestellten Familien\n in jeder Kategorie auszuwählen.\n\n\n\n\nAls allgemeines Resultat ergab sich, dass „nur in einer der unter-\n suchten Klassen der städtischen Arbeiter die Zufuhr von Stickstoff das\n absolute Minimalmass, unter welchem Hungerkrankheiten eintreten,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"643\", \"facs\": \"#f0662\"} --> ein wenig überschritt, dass in zwei Klassen Mangel, und zwar in der\n einen sehr grosser Mangel, sowohl an der Zufuhr von stickstoff- als\n kohlenstoffhaltiger Nahrung stattfand, dass von den untersuchten Acker-\n baufamilien mehr als ein Fünftheil weniger als die unentbehrliche Zufuhr\n von kohlenstoffhaltiger Nahrung erhielt, mehr als ⅓ weniger als die un-\n entbehrliche Zufuhr stickstoffhaltiger Nahrung, und dass in drei Grafschaf-\n ten (Berkshire, Oxfordshire und Somersetshire) Mangel an dem Minimum\n der stickstoffhaltigen Nahrung durchschnittlich herrschte“ [Note: „Public Health. Sixth Report etc. for 1863.“ Lond. 1864,\n p. 13.] . Unter den\n Agrikulturarbeitern waren die von England, dem reichsten Theile des Ver-\n einigten Königreichs, die schlechtest genährten [Note: l. c. p 17.] . Die Unternahrung\n fiel unter den Landarbeitern überhaupt hauptsächlich auf Frau und Kin-\n der, denn „der Mann muss essen, um sein Werk zu verrichten“. Noch\n grösserer Mangel wüthete unter den untersuchten städtischen Arbeiter-\n kategorieen. „Sie sind so schlecht genährt, dass viele Fälle grausamer\n und gesundheitsruinirender Entbehrung („Entsagung“ des Kapita-\n listen alles diess! nämlich Entsagung auf Zahlung der zur blossen\n Vegetation seiner Hände unentbehrlichen Lebensmittel!) vorkommen\n müssen“ [Note: l. c. p. 13.] .\n\n\n\n\nFolgende Tabelle zeigt das Verhältniss des Nahrungsstandes der oben\n erwähnten rein städtischen Arbeiterkategorieen zu dem von Dr. Smith\n angenommenen Minimalmass und zum Nahrungsmass der Baumwollarbeiter\n während der Zeit ihrer grössten Noth:\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEine Hälfte, \\frac{60}{125}, der untersuchten industriellen Arbeiterkategorieen\n [Running matter: 41*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"644\", \"facs\": \"#f0663\"} --> erhielt absolut kein Bier, 28 % keine Milch. Der Wochendurchschnitt\n der flüssigen Nahrungsmittel in den Familien schwankte von 7 Unzen bei\n den Nätherinnen auf 24¾ Unzen bei den Strumpfwirkern. Die Mehrzahl\n derer, die keine Milch erhielten, bestand aus den Nätherinnen von\n London. Die Quantität der wöchentlich konsumirten Brodstoffe wechselte\n von 7¾ Pfund bei den Nätherinnen zu 11¼ Pfund bei den Schustern\n und ergab einen Totaldurchschnitt von 9.9 Pfund wöchentlich auf den Er-\n wachsenen. Zucker (Syrup u. s. w.) wechselte von 4 Unzen wöchentlich\n für die Lederhandschuhmacher auf 11 Unzen für Strumpfwirker; der Total-\n durchschnitt per Woche für alle Kategorieen, per Erwachsenen, 8 Unzen.\n Gesammter Wochendurchschnitt von Butter (Fett u. s. w.) 5 Unzen per\n Erwachsenen. Der Wochendurchschnitt von Fleisch (Speck u. s. w.)\n schwankte, per Erwachsenen, von 7¼ Unzen bei den Seidenwebern auf 18¼\n Unzen bei den Lederhandschuhmachern; Gesammtdurchschnitt für die\n verschiedenen Kategorieen 13.6 Unzen. Die wöchentliche Kost\n für Nahrung per Erwachsenen ergab folgende allgemeine Durch-\n schnittszahlen: Seidenweber 2 sh. 2½ d., Nätherinnen 2 sh.\n 7 d., Lederhandschuhmacher 2 sh. 9½ d., Schuster 2 sh.\n 7¾ d., Strumpfwirker 2 sh. 6¼ d. Für die Seidenweber von\n Macclesfield betrug der Wochendurchschnitt nur 1 sh. 8½ d. Die\n schlecht genährtesten Kategorieen waren die Nätherinnen, die Seidenweber\n und die Lederhandschuhmacher [Note: l. c. p. 232, 233.] .\n\n\n\n\nDr. Simon sagt in seinem allgemeinen Gesundheitsbericht über\n diesen Nahrungszustand: „Dass die Fälle zahllos sind, worin Nah-\n rungsmangel Krankheiten erzeugt oder erschwert, wird Jeder bestä-\n tigen, der mit medizinischer Armenpraxis oder mit den Patienten der\n Spitäler, seien sie Insassen oder ausserhalb wohnend, vertraut ist …\n Jedoch kommen hier vom sanitären Standpunkt noch andre, sehr entschei-\n dende Umstände hinzu … Man muss sich erinnern, dass Beraubung an\n Nahrungsmitteln nur sehr widerstrebsam ertragen wird, und dass in der\n Regel grosse Dürftigkeit der Diät nur im Gefolge andrer, vorhergegangner\n Entbehrungen nachhinkt. Lange bevor der Nahrungsmangel hygienisch\n ins Gewicht fällt, lange bevor der Physiolog daran denkt, die Grane Stick-\n stoff und Kohlenstoff zu zählen, zwischen denen Leben und Hungertod\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"645\", \"facs\": \"#f0664\"} --> schwebt, wird der Haushalt von allem materiellen Komfort ganz und gar\n entblösst sein. Kleidung und Heizung werden noch dürftiger gewesen\n sein als die Speise. Kein hinreichender Schutz wider die Härte des Wet-\n ters; Abknappung des Wohnraums zu einem Grad, der Krankheiten er-\n zeugt oder erschwert; kaum eine Spur von Hausgeräth oder Möbeln; die\n Reinlichkeit selbst wird kostspielig oder schwierig geworden sein. Werden\n noch aus Selbstachtung Versuche gemacht, sie aufrecht zu erhalten, so\n repräsentirt jeder solcher Versuch zuschüssige Hungerpein. Die Häus-\n lichkeit wird dort sein, wo Obdach am wohlfeilsten kaufbar; in Quartieren,\n wo die Gesundheitspolizei die geringste Frucht trägt, das jämmerlichste\n Gerinne, wenigster Verkehr, der meiste öffentliche Unrath, kümmerlichste\n oder schlechteste Wasserzufuhr, und, in Städten, grösster Mangel an Licht\n und Luft. Diess sind die Gesundheitsgefahren, denen die Armuth unver-\n meidlich ausgesetzt ist, wenn diese Armuth Nahrungsmangel einschliesst.\n Wenn die Summe dieser Uebel von furchtbarer Grösse für das Leben ist,\n so ist der blosse Nahrungsmangel an sich selbst entsetzlich … Diess sind\n qualvolle Gedanken, namentlich wenn man sich erinnert, dass die Armuth,\n wovon es sich handelt, nicht die selbstverschuldete Armuth des Müssig-\n gangs ist. Es ist die Armuth von Arbeitern. Ja, mit Bezug auf die\n städtischen Arbeiter, ist die Arbeit, wodurch der knappe Bissen Nahrung\n erkauft wird, meist über alles Mass verlängert. Und dennoch kann man\n nur in sehr bedingtem Sinn sagen, dass diese Arbeit selbsterhaltend ist.\n … Auf sehr grossem Massstab kann der nominelle Selbsterhalt nur ein\n kürzerer oder längerer Umweg zum Pauperismus sein“ [Note: l. c. p. 15.] .\n\n\n\n\nDas innere Band zwischen Hungerpein der fleissigsten Arbeiter-\n schichten und kapitalistischer Accumulation, nebst dem sie begleitenden\n groben oder raffinirten Ueberkonsum der Reichen, diess Band, sage ich,\n sieht nur der Kenner der ökonomischen Gesetze. Anders mit dem Woh-\n nungszustand. Jeder unbefangne Beobachter sieht, dass je mas-\n senhafter die Koncentration der Produktionsmittel, desto grösser die\n entsprechende Agglomeration von Arbeitern auf geringem Raum,\n dass daher je rascher die kapitalistische Accumulation, desto elender der\n Wohnungszustand der Arbeiter. Jeder sieht, dass die das Wachsthum\n des Reichthums begleitenden Verbesserungen der Städte (improve-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"646\", \"facs\": \"#f0665\"} --> ments) durch Niederreissung der schlechtgebauten Viertel, Errichtung von\n Palästen für Banken, Waarenhäuser u. s. w., Dehnung der Strassen für\n Geschäftsverkehr und Luxuskarossen, Einführung städtischer Eisenbahnen\n u. s. w. mit Verjagung der Armen in stets schlechtere und dichter gefüllte\n Schlupfwinkel verknüpft sind. Andrerseits weiss jeder, dass die Theuer-\n keit der Wohnungen im umgekehrten Verhältniss zu ihrer Güte steht und\n dass die Minen des Elends von Spekulanten mit mehr Profit und weniger\n Kosten ausgebeutet werden als jemals die Minen von Potosi. Der antago-\n nistische Charakter der kapitalistischen Accumulation und daher der ka-\n pitalistischen Eigenthumsverhältnisse überhaupt [Note: „Nirgendwo sind so offen und so schamlos die Rechte der Person dem\n Recht des Eigenthums geopfert worden, als in den Wohnungsverhältnissen der ar-\n beitenden Klasse. Jede grosse Stadt ist eine Stätte des Menschenopfers, ein Altar,\n worauf Tausende jährlich dem Moloch der Habsucht geschlachtet werden.“\n (S. Laing l. c. p. 150.)]  wird\n hier so handgreifbar, dass selbst die officiellen englischen Berichte über\n diesen Gegenstand voll heterodoxer Ausfälle auf das „Eigenthum und\n seine Rechte sind“. Das Uebel hielt solchen Schritt mit der Entwicklung der\n Industrie, der Accumulation des Kapitals, dem Wachsthum und der „Ver-\n schönerung“ der Städte, dass die blosse Furcht vor ansteckenden Krank-\n heiten, welche auch der „Ehrbarkeit“ nicht schonen, von 1847 bis 1864\n nicht weniger als 10 gesundheitspolizeiliche Parlamentsakte ins Leben\n rief, und dass die erschreckte Bürgerschaft in einigen Städten wie Liver-\n pool, Glasgow u. s. w. durch ihre Municipalitäten eingriff. Dennoch, ruft\nDr. Simon in seinem Bericht von 1865: „Allgemein zu sprechen, sind\n die Uebelstände in England unkontrolirt.“ Auf Befehl des Privy Coun-\n cil fand 1864 Untersuchung über die Wohnungsverhältnisse der Land-\n arbeiter, 1865 über die der ärmeren Klassen in den Städten statt. Die\n meisterhaften Arbeiten des Dr. Julian Hunter findet man im sie-\n benten (1865) und achten (1866) Bericht über „Public Health“. Auf\n die Landarbeiter komme ich später. Ueber den städtischen Wohnungs-\n zustand schicke ich eine allgemeine Bemerkung des Dr. Simon voraus:\n „Obgleich mein officieller Gesichtspunkt“, sagt er, „ausschliesslich\n physisch ist, erlaubt die gewöhnlichste Humanität nicht die andre Seite\n dieses Uebels zu ignoriren. In seinem höheren Grad bedingt es fast noth-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"647\", \"facs\": \"#f0666\"} --> wendig eine solche Negation aller Delikatesse, so schmutzige Konfusion\n von Körpern und körperlichen Verrichtungen, solche Blossstellung ge-\n schlechtlicher Nacktheit, die bestial, nicht menschlich sind. Diesen Ein-\n flüssen unterworfen zu sein ist eine Degradation, die sich vertieft, je länger\n sie fortwirkt. Für die Kinder, die unter diesem Fluch geboren sind, ist er\nTaufe in Infamie („baptism into infamy“). Und über alles\n Mass hoffnungslos ist der Wunsch, dass unter solche Umstände gestellte\n Personen in andern Rücksichten nach jener Atmosphäre der Civilisation\n aufstreben sollten, die ihr Wesen in physischer und moralischer Reinheit\n besitzt“ [Note: „Public Health. Eight Report. Lond. 1866“, p. 14, Note.] .\n\n\n\n\nDen ersten Rang in überfüllten oder auch für menschliche Behausung\n absolut unmöglichen Wohnlichkeiten nimmt London ein. „Zwei Punkte“,\n sagt Dr. Hunter, „sind sicher; erstens, dass es ungefähr 20 grosse\n Kolonieen in London giebt, jede ungefähr 10,000 Personen stark, deren\n elende Lage alles übersteigt, was jemals anderswo in England gesehn\n worden ist, und sie ist fast ganz das Resultat ihrer schlechten Hausaccom-\n modation; und zweitens, dass der überfüllte und verfallene Zustand der\n Häuser dieser Kolonieen viel schlechter ist als 20 Jahre zu-\n vor“ [Note: l. c. p. 89. Mit Bezug auf die Kinder in diesen Kolonieen sagt\n Dr. Hunter: „Wir wissen nicht, wie Kinder vor diesem Zeitalter dichter Agglo-\n meration der Armen aufgebracht wurden, und er wäre ein kühner Prophet, der\n vorhersagen wollte, welches Betragen zu erwarten von Kindern, die unter Zu-\n ständen ohne Parallele in diesem Land jetzt ihre Erziehung für künftige Praxis als\ngefährliche Klassen durchmachen, indem sie die halbe Nacht aufsitzen mit\n Personen jeden Alters, trunken, obscön und zanksüchtig.“ (l. c. p. 56.)] . „Es ist nicht zu viel zu sagen, dass das Leben in vielen\n Theilen von London und Newcastle infernal ist“ [Note: l. c. p. 62.] .\n\n\n\n\nAuch der besser gestellte Theil der Arbeiterklasse, zusammt\n Kleinkrämern und andern Elementen der kleinen Mittelklasse, fällt in\n London mehr und mehr unter den Fluch dieser nichtswürdigen Behausungsver-\n hältnisse, im Masse, wie die „Verbesserungen“ und mit ihnen die Niederreis-\n sung alter Strassen und Häuser fortschreiten, wie Fabriken und Menschenzu-\n strom in der Metropole wachsen, endlich die Hausmiethen mit der städtischen\n Grundrente steigen. „Die Hausmiethen sind so übermässig geworden, dass\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"648\", \"facs\": \"#f0667\"} --> wenige Arbeiter mehr als ein Zimmer zahlen können“ [Note: „Report of the Officer of Health of St. Martin’s in the\n Fields. 1865.“] . Es giebt fast kein\n Londoner Hauseigenthum, das nicht mit einer Unzahl von „middlemen“ be-\n lastet wäre. Der Preis des Bodens in London steht nämlich stets sehr\n hoch im Vergleich zu seinen jährlichen Einkünften, indem jeder Käufer\n darauf spekulirt früher oder später zu einem Jury Price (durch Geschworne\n festgesetzte Taxe bei Expropriationen) wieder loszuschlagen oder durch\n Nähe irgend eines grossen Unternehmens ausserordentliche Wertherhöhung\n zu erschwindeln. Folge davon ist ein regelmässiger Handel im Ankauf\n von Miethkontrakten, die ihrem Verfall nahen. „Von den Gentlemen in\n diesem Geschäft kann man erwarten, dass sie handeln, wie sie handeln,\n so viel wie möglich aus den Hausbewohnern herausschlagen, und das Haus\n selbst in so schlechtem Zustand wie möglich ihren Nachfolgern über-\n lassen“ [Note: „Public Health. Eight Report. Lond. 1866“, p. 93.] . Die Miethen sind wöchentlich und die Herren laufen kein\n Risico. In Folge der Eisenbahnbauten innerhalb der Stadt „sah man kürz-\n lich im Osten Londons eine Anzahl aus ihren alten Wohnungen verjagter\n Familien umherwandern eines Samstags Abends mit ihren wenigen welt-\n lichen Habseligkeiten auf dem Rücken, ohne irgend einen Haltplatz ausser\n dem Workhouse“ [Note: l. c. p. 83.] . Die Workhouses sind bereits überfüllt und die vom\n Parlament bereits bewilligten „improvements“ sind erst im Beginn ihrer\n Ausführung. Werden die Arbeiter verjagt durch Zerstörung ihrer alten\n Häuser, so verlassen sie nicht ihr Kirchspiel, oder siedeln sich höchstens an\n seiner Grenze, im nächsten fest. „Sie suchen natürlich möglichst in der\n Nähe ihrer Arbeitslokale zu hausen. Folge, dass an die Stelle von zwei Zim-\n mern, eins die Familie aufnehmen muss. Selbst zu erhöhter Miethe\n wird die Wohnlichkeit schlechter als die schlechte, woraus man sie ver-\n jagt. Die Hälfte der Arbeiter im Strand braucht bereits zwei Meilen\n Reise zum Arbeitslokal.“ Dieser Strand, dessen Hauptstrasse auf den Frem-\n den einen imposanten Eindruck vom Reichthum Londons macht, kann als\n Beispiel der Londoner Menschenverpackung dienen. In einer Pfarrei des-\n selben zählte der Gesundheitsbeamte 581 Personen auf den Acre, obgleich\n die Hälfte der Themse mit eingemessen war. Es versteht sich von selbst,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"649\", \"facs\": \"#f0668\"} --> dass jede gesundheitspolizeiliche Massregel, die, wie das bisher in London\n der Fall, durch Niederschleifen untauglicher Häuser die Arbeiter aus einem\n Viertel verjagt, nur dazu dient sie in ein andres desto dichter zusammen\n zu drängen. „Entweder“, sagt Dr. Hunter, „muss die ganze Proce-\n dur als eine Abgeschmacktheit nothwendig zum Stillstand kommen, oder\n die öffentliche Sympathie(!) muss erwachen für das, was man jetzt ohne\n Uebertreibung eine nationale Pflicht nennen kann, nämlich Obdach\n für Leute zu verschaffen, welche aus Mangel an Kapital sich selbst\n keins verschaffen können, obgleich sie durch periodische Zahlung die Ver-\n miether belohnen können“ [Note: l. c. p. 89.] . Man bewundre die kapitalistische Justiz!\n Der Grundeigenthümer, Hauseigner, Geschäftsmann, wenn expropriirt durch\n „improvements“, wie Eisenbahnen, Neubau der Strassen u. s. w., erhält\n nicht nur volle Entschädigung. Er muss für seine erzwungne „Ent-\n sagung“ von Gott und Rechtswegen obendrein noch durch einen erklecklichen\nProfit getröstet werden. Der Arbeiter wird mit Frau und Kind und Habe\n aufs Pflaster geworfen und — wenn er zu massenhaft nach Stadtvierteln\n drängt, wo die Municipalität auf Anstand hält, gesundheitspolizei-\n lich verfolgt!\n\n\n\n\nAusser London gab es Anfang des 19. Jahrhunderts keine einzige\n Stadt in England, die 100,000 Einwohner zählte. Nur 5 zählten mehr\n als 50,000. Jetzt existiren 28 Städte mit mehr als 50,000 Einwohnern.\n „Das Resultat dieses Wechsels war nicht nur enormer Zuwachs der städti-\n schen Bevölkerung, sondern die alten dichtgepackten kleinen Städte sind\n nun Centra, die von allen Seiten umbaut sind, nirgendwo mit freiem Luft-\n zutritt. Da sie für die Reichen nicht länger angenehm sind, werden sie von\n ihnen für die amüsanteren Vorstädte verlassen. Die Nachfolger dieser Rei-\n chen beziehn die grösseren Häuser, eine Familie, oft noch mit Untermiethern,\nfür jedes Zimmer. So ward eine Bevölkerung gedrängt in Häuser,\n nicht für sie bestimmt, und wofür sie durchaus unpassend, mit einer Um-\n gebung, die wahrhaft erniedrigend für die Erwachsnen und ruinirend für die\n Kinder ist“ [Note: l. c. p. 56.] . Je rascher das Kapital in einer industriellen oder com-\n merciellen Stadt accumulirt, um so rascher der Zustrom des exploitablen\n Menschenmaterials, um so elender die improvisirten Wohnlichkeiten der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"650\", \"facs\": \"#f0669\"} --> Arbeiter. Newcastle-upon-Tyne, als Centrum eines fortwährend\n ergiebigeren Kohlen- und Bergwerksbaudistrikts, behauptet daher nach\n London die zweite Stelle in dem Wohnungsinferno. Nicht minder als\n 34,000 Menschen hausen dort in Einzelkammern. In Folge absoluter\n Gemeinschädlichkeit sind kürzlich in Newcastle und Gateshead Häuser in\n bedeutender Anzahl von Polizei wegen zerstört worden. Der Bau der neuen\n Häuser geht sehr langsam voran, das Geschäft sehr rasch. Die Stadt war\n daher 1865 überfüllter als je zuvor. Kaum eine einzelne Kammer war\n zu vermiethen. Dr. Embleton vom Newcastle Fieberhospital sagt:\n „Ohne allen Zweifel liegt die Ursache der Fortdauer und Verbreitung des\n Typhus in der Ueberhäufung menschlicher Wesen und der Unreinlichkeit\n ihrer Wohnungen. Die Häuser, worin die Arbeiter häufig leben, liegen\n in abgeschlossnen Winkelgassen und-Höfen. Sie sind mit Bezug auf Licht,\n Luft, Raum und Reinlichkeit wahre Muster von Mangelhaftigkeit und Un-\n gesundheit, eine Schmach für jedes civilisirte Land. Dort liegen Männer,\n Weiber und Kinder des Nachts zusammengehudelt. Was die Männer an-\n geht, folgt die Nachtreihe der Tagesreihe in ununterbrochenem Strom, so\n dass die Betten kaum Zeit zur Abkühlung finden. Die Häuser sind\n schlecht mit Wasser versehn und schlechter mit Abtritten, unfläthig, un-\n ventilirt, pestilenzialisch“ [Note: l. c. p. 149.] . Der Wochenpreis solcher Löcher steigt\n von 8 d. zu 3 sh. „Newcastle-upon-Tyne“, sagt Dr. Hunter, „bietet das\n Beispiel eines der schönsten Stämme unsrer Landsleute, der durch die\n äussern Umstände von Behausung und Strasse oft in eine beinah wilde\n Degradation versunken ist“ [Note: l. c. p. 50.] .\n\n\n\n\nIn Folge des Hin- und Herwogens von Kapital und Arbeit mag der\n Wohnungszustand einer industriellen Stadt heute erträglich sein, morgen\n ist er abominabel. Oder die städtische Aedilität mag endlich sich aufge-\n rafft haben zur Beseitigung der ärgsten Missstände. Morgen wandert\n ein Heuschreckenschwarm von verlumpten Irländern oder verkommenen\n englischen Agrikulturarbeitern ein. Man steckt sie weg in Keller und\n Speicher oder verwandelt das früher respektable Arbeiterhaus in ein Logis,\n worin das Personal so rasch wechselt wie die Einquartirung während des\n dreissigjährigen Kriegs. Beispiel: Bradford. Dort war der Municipal-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"651\", \"facs\": \"#f0670\"} --> philister eben mit Stadtreform beschäftigt. Zudem gab es daselbst 1861\n noch 1751 unbewohnte Häuser. Aber nun das gute Geschäft, worüber der\n sanft liberale Herr Forster, der Negerfreund, jüngst so artig gekräht hat.\n Mit dem guten Geschäft natürlich Ueberfluthung von den Wellen der stets\n wogenden „Reservearmee“ oder „relativen Surpluspopulation“. Die scheuss-\n lichen Kellerwohnungen und Kammern, in der Note beschrieben [Note: Liste des Agenten einer Arbeiter-Assekuranzgesellschaft zu Bradford.\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\nKeller.\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n]  durch\n eine dem Dr. Hunter vom Agenten einer Assekuranzgesellschaft ausgefer-\n tigte Liste, waren meist von gutbezahlten Arbeitern bewohnt. Sie er-\n klärten, sie würden gern bessere Wohnungen zahlen, wenn sie zu haben\n wären. Unterdess verlumpen und verkranken sie mit Mann und Maus,\n während der sanftliberale Forster, M P., Thränen vergiesst über die\n Segnungen des Freihandels und die Profite der eminenten Bradforder\n Köpfe, die in Worsted machen. Im Bericht vom 5. September 1865\n erklärt Dr. Bell, einer der Armenärzte von Bradford, die furchtbare\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"652\", \"facs\": \"#f0671\"} --> Sterblichkeit der Fieberkranken seines Bezirks aus ihren Wohnungsver-\n hältnissen: „In einem Keller von 1500 Kubikfuss wohnen 10 Perso-\n nen … Die Vincentstrasse, Green Air Place und the Leys bergen 223\n Häuser mit 1450 Einwohnern, 435 Betten und 36 Abtritten … Die\n Betten, und darunter verstehe ich jede Rolle von schmutzigen Lumpen oder\n Handvoll von Hobelspänen, halten jedes per Durchschnitt 3.3 Personen,\n manches 4 und 6 Personen. Viele schlafen ohne Bett auf nacktem Boden\n in ihren Kleidern, junge Männer und Weiber, verheirathet und unverhei-\n rathet, alles kunterbunt durch einander. Ist es nöthig hinzuzufügen, dass\n diese Hausungen meist dunkle, feuchte, schmutzige Stinkhöhlen sind, ganz\n und gar unpassend für menschliche Wohnung? Es sind die Centra,\n wovon Krankheit und Tod ausgehn und ihre Opfer auch unter den\n Gutgestellten („of good circumstances“) packen, welche diesen Pest-\n beulen erlaubt haben in unsrer Mitte zu eitern“ [Note: l. c. p. 114.] .\n\n\n\n\nBristol behauptet den dritten Rang nach London im Wohnungs-\n elend. „Hier, in einer der reichsten Städte Europa’s, grösster Ueberfluss\n an baarster Armuth („blank poverty“) und häuslicher Misère“ [Note: l. c. p. 50.] .\n\n\n\n\nWir wenden uns nun zu einer Wandervölkerung, deren Quelle\n ländlich, deren Beschäftigung grossentheils industriell ist. Es ist die leichte\n Infanterie des Kapitals, je nach seinem Bedürfniss bald auf diesen\n Punkt geworfen, bald auf jenen. Wenn nicht auf dem Marsch, „campirt“\n sie. Die Wanderarbeit wird verbraucht für verschiedne Bau- und Draini-\n rungsoperationen, Backsteinmachen, Kalkbrennen, Eisenbahnen u. s. w.\n Eine wandelnde Säule der Pestilenz importirt sie in die Orte, in deren\n Nachbarschaft sie ihr Lager aufschlägt, Pocken, Typhus, Cholera, Schar-\n lachfieber u. s. w. [Note: „Public Health. Seventh Report. Lond. 1865“, p. 18.] . In Unternehmen von bedeutender Kapitalauslage,\n wie Eisenbahnbau u. s. w., liefert meist der Unternehmer selbst seiner\n Armee Holzhütten oder dgl., improvisirte Dörfer ohne alle Gesundheits-\n vorkehrung, jenseits der Kontrole der Lokalbehörden, sehr profitlich für\n den Herrn Contractor, der die Arbeiter doppelt ausbeutet, als Industrie-\n soldaten und als Miether. Je nachdem die Holzhütte 1, 2 oder 3 Löcher\n enthält, hat ihr Insasse, Erdarbeiter u. s. w., 1, 3, 4 sh. wöchentlich zu\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"653\", \"facs\": \"#f0672\"} --> zahlen [Note: l. c. p. 165.] . Ein Beispiel genüge. Im September 1864, berichtet\n Dr. Simon, ging dem Minister des Innern, Sir George Grey, folgende\n Denunciation Seitens des Vorstehers des Nuisance Removal Com-\n mittee der Pfarrei von Sevenoaks zu: „Pocken waren dieser Pfarrei fast\n ganz unbekannt bis etwa vor 12 Monaten. Kurz vor dieser Zeit wurden Ar-\n beiten für eine Eisenbahn von Lewisham nach Tunbridge eröffnet. Aus-\n serdem dass die Hauptarbeiten in der unmittelbaren Nachbarschaft dieser\n Stadt ausgeführt wurden, ward hier auch das Hauptdepot des ganzen Werks\n errichtet. Grosse Personenzahl daher hier beschäftigt. Da es unmöglich\n war sie alle in Cottages unterzubringen, liess der Contractor, Herr Jay,\n längst der Linie der Bahn auf verschiednen Punkten Hütten aufschlagen\n zur Behausung der Arbeiter. Diese Hütten besassen weder Ventilation\n noch Abzugsgerinne und waren ausserdem nothwendig überfüllt, weil jeder\n Miether andre Logirer aufnehmen musste, wie zahlreich immer seine eigne\n Familie, und obgleich jede Hütte nur zweizimmerig. Nach dem ärzt-\n lichen Bericht, den wir erhielten, war die Folge, dass diese armen Leute\n zur Nachtzeit alle Qualen der Erstickung zu erdulden hatten, zur Vermei-\n dung der pestilenzialischen Dünste von dem schmutzigen stehenden Wasser\n und den Abtritten dicht unter den Fenstern. Endlich wurden unsrem\n Comité Klagen eingehändigt von einem Arzte, der Gelegenheit hatte\n diese Hütten zu besuchen. Er sprach über den Zustand dieser s. g.\n Wohnlichkeiten in den bittersten Ausdrücken und befürchtete sehr ernst-\n hafte Folgen, falls nicht einige Gesundheitsvorkehrungen getroffen wür-\n den. Ungefähr vor einem Jahr verpflichtete sich p. p. Jay ein Haus ein-\n zurichten, wohin die von ihm beschäftigten Personen, beim Ausbruch an-\n steckender Krankheiten, sofort entfernt werden sollten. Er wiederholte\n diess Versprechen Ende letzten Juli’s, that aber nie den geringsten Schritt\n zur Ausführung, obgleich seit diesem Datum verschiedne Fälle von Pocken\n und in Folge davon zwei Todesfälle vorkamen. Am 9. September be-\n richtete mir Surgeon Kelson weitere Pockenfälle in denselben Hütten und\n beschrieb ihren Zustand als entsetzlich. Zu Ihrer (des Ministers) Infor-\n mation muss ich hinzufügen, dass unsre Pfarrei ein isolirtes Haus besitzt,\n das s. g. Pesthaus, wo die Pfarreigenossen, die von ansteckenden Krank-\n heiten leiden, verpflegt werden. Diess Haus ist jetzt seit Monaten fort-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"654\", \"facs\": \"#f0673\"} --> während mit Patienten überfüllt. In einer Familie starben fünf Kinder an\n Pocken und Fieber. Vom 1. April bis 1. September dieses Jahres kamen\n nicht weniger als 10 Todesfälle an Pocken vor, 4 in den besagten Hütten,\n den Pestquellen. Es ist unmöglich die Zahl der Krankheitsfälle\n anzugeben, da die heimgesuchten Familien sie so geheim als möglich\n halten“ [Note: l. c. p. 18, Note. Der Armenpfleger der Chapel-en-le-Frith-Union be-\n richtet an den Registrar General: „Zu Doveholes hat man eine Anzahl kleiner\n Aushöhlungen in einem grossen Hügel von Kalkasche gemacht. Diese Höhlen\n dienen den Erd- und andern am Eisenbahnbau beschäftigten Arbeitern zur Woh-\n nung. Die Höhlen sind eng, feucht, ohne Abzug für Unreinigkeiten und ohne\n Abtritte. Sie entbehren aller Ventilationsmittel, mit Ausnahme eines Lochs durch\n die Wölbung, das zugleich als Schornstein dient. Die Pocken wüthen und haben\n schon verschiedne Todesfälle (unter den Troglodyten) verursacht.“ (l. c. n. 2.)] .\n\n\n\n\nKohlen- und andre Bergwerksarbeiter gehören zu den best-\n bezahlten Kategorieen der britischen Arbeiterklasse. Zu welchem Preis sie\n ihren Lohn erkaufen, wurde an einer früheren Stelle gezeigt [Note: Die Ende des 4. Kapitels gegebne Note bezieht sich namentlich auf Ar-\n beiter in Kohlenbergwerken. Ueber den noch schlechteren Zustand in den Metall-\n minen vgl. den gewissenhaften Bericht der „Royal Commission“ von 1864.] . Ich werfe\n hier einen raschen Blick auf ihre Wohnlichkeitsverhältnisse. In der Regel\n errichtet der Exploiteur des Bergwerks, ob Eigenthümer oder Miether des-\n selben, eine Anzahl Cottages für seine Hände. Sie erhalten Cottages und\n Kohlen zur Feurung „umsonst“, d. h. letztre bilden einen in natura ge-\n lieferten Theil des Lohns. Die nicht in dieser Art Unterbringbaren er-\n halten zum Ersatz 4 Pfd. St. per Jahr. Die Bergwerksdistrikte ziehn\n rasch eine grosse Bevölkerung an, zusammengesetzt aus der Minenbevöl-\n kerung selbst und den Handwerkern, Krämern u. s. w., die sich um sie\n gruppiren. Wie überall, wo die Bevölkerung dicht, ist die Bodenrente\n hier hoch. Der Bergbauunternehmer sucht daher auf möglichst engem\n Bauplatz am Mund der Minen so viel Cottages aufzuwerfen als grade nöthig\n sind um seine Hände und ihre Familien zusammenzupacken. Werden\n neue Minen in der Nähe eröffnet oder alte wieder in Angriff genommen, so\n wächst das Gedränge. Bei der Konstruktion der Cottages waltet nur ein\n Gesichtspunkt, „Entsagung“ des Kapitalisten auf alle nicht absolut\n unvermeidliche Ausgaben von Baarem. „Die Wohnungen der Gruben-\n und andrer Arbeiter, die mit den Bergwerken von Northumberland und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"655\", \"facs\": \"#f0674\"} --> Durham verknüpft sind,“ sagt Dr. Julian Hunter, „sind vielleicht\n im Durchschnitt das Schlechteste und Theuerste, was England auf grosser\n Stufenleiter in dieser Art bietet, mit Ausnahme jedoch ähnlicher Distrikte\n in Monmouthshire. Die extreme Schlechtigkeit liegt in der hohen Men-\n schenzahl, die ein Zimmer füllen, in der Enge des Bauplatzes, worauf eine\n grosse Häusermasse geworfen wird, im Wassermangel und Abwesenheit\n von Abtritten, in der häufig angewandten Methode ein Haus über ein an-\n dres zu stellen oder sie in flats (so dass die verschiednen Cottages hori-\n zontal über einander liegende Stockwerke bilden) zu vertheilen … Der\n Unternehmer behandelt die ganze Kolonie, als ob sie nur campire, nicht\n residire“ [Note: l. c. p. 180, 182.] . „In Ausführung meiner Instruktionen,“ sagt Dr. Stevens,\n „habe ich die meisten grossen Bergwerksdörfer der Durham Union be-\n sucht … Mit sehr wenigen Ausnahmen gilt von allen, dass jedes Mittel\n zur Sicherung der Gesundheit der Einwohner vernachlässigt wird … Alle\n Grubenarbeiter sind an den Pächter („lessee“) oder Eigenthümer des Berg-\n werks für 12 Monate gebunden („bound“, Ausdruck, der wie bondage aus der\nZeit der Leibeigenschaft stammt). Wenn sie ihrer Unzufriedenheit\n Luft machen oder in irgend einer Art den Aufseher („viewer“) belästigen, so\n setzt er eine Marke oder ein Memorandum hinter ihre Namen in seinem Auf-\n sichtsbuch, und entlässt sie bei der jährlichen Neu-Bindung … Es scheint\n mir, dass kein Theil des Trucksystems schlechter sein kann als das in\n diesen dichtbevölkerten Distrikten herrschende. Der Arbeiter ist gezwun-\n gen als Theil seines Lohns ein mit pestilenzialischen Einflüssen umgebnes\n Haus zu empfangen. Er kann sich nicht selbst helfen. Erist in jeder\n Rücksicht ein Leibeigner („he is to all intents and pur-\n posesaserf“). Es scheint fraglich, ob jemand sonst ihm helfen kann\n ausser seinem Eigenthümer und dieser Eigenthümer zieht vor allem sein\n Bilanzkonto zu Rath, und das Resultat ist ziemlich unfehlbar. Der\n Arbeiter erhält von dem Eigenthümer auch seine Zufuhr an Wasser. Es sei\n gut oder schlecht, es werde geliefert oder zurückgehalten, er muss dafür\n zahlen oder sich vielmehr einen Lohnabzug gefallen lassen“ [Note: l. c. p. 515, 517.] .\n\n\n\n\nIm Konflikt mit der „öffentlichen Meinung“ oder auch der Gesundheits-\n polizei genirt sich das Kapital durchaus nicht, die theils gefährlichen,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"656\", \"facs\": \"#f0675\"} --> theils entwürdigenden Bedingungen, worin es Funktion und Häuslichkeit des\n Arbeiters bannt, damit zu „rechtfertigen“, das sei nöthig, um ihn\n profitlicher auszubeuten. So, wenn es entsagt auf Vorrichtungen\n zum Schutz gegen gefährliche Maschinerie in der Fabrik, auf Ventilations-\n und Sicherheitsmittel in den Minen u. s. w. So hier mit der Behausung der\n Minenarbeiter. „Als Entschuldigung“, sagt Dr. Simon, der ärztliche\n Beamte des Privy Council, in seinem officiellen Bericht, „als Entschuldi-\n gung für die nichtswürdige Hauseinrichtung wird angeführt, dass Mi-\n nen gewöhnlich pachtweise exploitirt werden, dass die Dauer des Pachtkon-\n trakts (in Kohlenwerken meist 21 Jahre) zu kurz ist, damit der Minen-\n pächter es der Mühe werth halte, gute Hausaccommodation für das Arbeits-\n volk und die Gewerbsleute u. s. w. zu liefern, welche die Unternehmung\n anzieht; hätte er selbst die Absicht, nach dieser Seite hin liberal\n zu verfahren, so würde sie vereitelt werden durch den Grundeigenthümer.\n Der habe nämlich die Tendenz, sofort exorbitante Zuschussrente zu ver-\n langen für das Privilegium, ein anständiges und komfortables Dorf auf\n der Grundoberfläche zu errichten zur Behausung der Bearbeiter des unter-\n irdischen Eigenthums. Dieser prohibitorische Preis, wenn nicht direkte\n Prohibition, schrecke ebenfalls andre ab, welche sonst wohl bauen möch-\n ten … Ich will den Werth dieser Apologie nicht weiter untersuchen,\n auch nicht, auf wen denn in letzter Hand die zuschüssige Ausgabe für\n anständige Wohnlichkeit fallen würde, auf den Grundherrn, den Minen-\n pächter, die Arbeiter oder das Publikum … Aber Angesichts solcher\n schmählichen Thatsachen, wie die beigefügten Berichte (des Dr. Hunter,\n Stevens u. s. w.) sie enthüllen, muss ein Heilmittel angewandt werden …\nGrundeigenthumstitel werden so gebraucht um ein grosses öffentliches\n Unrecht zu begehn. In seiner Eigenschaft als Mineneigner ladet der\n Grundherr eine industrielle Kolonie zur Arbeit auf seiner Domaine ein,\n und macht dann, in seiner Eigenschaft als Eigenthümer der Grund-\n oberfläche, den von ihm versammelten Arbeitern unmöglich die zu\n ihrem Leben unentbehrliche, geeignete Wohnlichkeit zu finden. Der Minen-\n pächter (der kapitalistische Exploiteur) hat kein Geldinteresse dieser\n Theilung des Handels zu widerstehn, da er wohl weiss, dass wenn die letztern\n Ansprüche exorbitant sind, die Folgen nicht auf ihn fallen, und dass\n die Arbeiter, auf die sie fallen, zu unerzogen sind, um ihre Gesundheits-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"657\", \"facs\": \"#f0676\"} --> rechte zu kennen, dass weder obscönste Wohnlichkeit noch faulstes\n Trinkwasser besondre Anlässe zu einem Strike liefern“ [Note: l. c. p. 16.] .\n\n\n\n\nBevor ich zu den eigentlichen Agrikulturarbeitern übergehe,\n soll an einem Beispiel noch gezeigt werden, wie die Krisen selbst auf den\n bestbezahlten Theil der Arbeiterklasse, auf ihre Aristokratie, wirken. Man\n erinnert sich: das Jahr 1857 brachte eine der grossen Krisen, womit der\n industrielle Cyklus jedesmal abschliesst. Der nächste Termin wurde\n1866 fällig. Bereits diskontirt in den eigentlichen Fabrikdistrikten\n durch die Baumwollnoth, welche viel Kapital aus der gewohnten Anlagesphäre\n zu den grossen Centralsitzen des Geldmarkts jagte, nahm die Krise diess-\n mal einen vorwiegend finanziellen Charakter an. Ihr Ausbruch im Mai 1866\n wurde signalisirt durch den Fall einer Londoner Riesenbank, dem der Zusam-\n mensturz zahlloser finanzieller Schwindelgesellschaften auf dem Fuss nach-\n folgte. Einer der grossen Londoner Geschäftszweige, welche die Kata-\n strophe traf, war der eiserne Schiffsbau. Die Magnaten dieses Fachs\n hatten während der Schwindelzeit nicht nur masslos überprodueirt, son-\n dern zudem enorme Lieferungskontrakte übernommen, auf die Spekulation\n hin, dass die Kreditquelle gleich reichlich fort fliessen werde. Jetzt trat\n eine furchtbare Reaktion ein, die auch in andern Londoner Industrien [Note: „Massenhafte Verhungerung der Londoner Armen! („Wholesale starva-\n tion of the London Poor!“) … Während der letzten Tage waren die Wälle\n Londons überklebt mit grossen Plakaten, die folgende merkwürdige Anzeige\n bringen: ‚Fette Ochsen, verhungernde Menschen! Die fetten Ochsen haben ihre\n Glaspaläste verlassen, um die Reichen in ihren Luxusgemächern zu mästen, wäh-\n rend die verhungernden Menschen in ihren Jammerhöhlen verderben und sterben.‘\n Die Plakate mit dieser unheilkündenden Inschrift werden beständig erneuert.\n Kaum ist eine Partie ausgemerzt und überklebt, wenn sofort eine neue Partie an dem-\n selben oder einem gleich öffentlichen Platz wiedererscheint … Das erinnert an\n die omina, die das französische Volk auf die Ereignisse von 1789 vorbereiteten …\n In diesem Augenblick, während englische Arbeiter mit Weib und Kind an Kälte\n und Hunger sterben, werden Millionen von englischem Geld, dem Produkt eng-\n lischer Arbeit, in russischen, spanischen, italienischen und andern fremden An-\n leihen angelegt.“ („Reynold’s Newspaper, 20. Jan. 1867.)] \n bis zur Stunde, Ende März 1867, fortdauert. Zur Charakteristik der\n Lage der Arbeiter folgende Stelle aus dem ausführlichen Bericht eines\n [Running matter: I. 42] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"658\", \"facs\": \"#f0677\"} --> Korrespondenten des Morning Star, welcher Anfang Januar 1867\n die Hauptsitze des Leidens besuchte. „Im Osten von London, den Distrik-\n ten von Poplar, Millwall, Greenwich, Deptford, Limehouse und Canning\n Town befinden sich mindestens 15,000 Arbeiter sammt Familien in\n einem Zustand äusserster Noth, darunter über 3000 geschickte Mecha-\n niker. Ihre Reservefonds sind erschöpft in Folge sechs- oder achtmonat-\n licher Arbeitslosigkeit … Ich hatte grosse Mühe zum Thor des Work-\n house (von Poplar) vorzudringen, denn es war belagert von einem ausge-\n hungerten Haufen. Er wartete auf Brodbillets, aber die Zeit zur Ver-\n theilung war noch nicht gekommen. Der Hof bildet ein grosses Quadrat\n mit einem Pultdach, das rings um seine Mauern läuft. Dichte Schnee-\n haufen bedeckten die Pflastersteine in der Mitte des Hofes. Hier waren\n gewisse kleine Plätze mit Weidengeflecht abgeschlossen, gleich Schaf-\n hürden, worin die Männer bei besserem Wetter arbeiten. Am Tage meines\n Besuchs waren die Hürden so verschneit, dass Niemand in ihnen sitzen\n konnte. Die Männer waren jedoch unter dem Schutz der Dachvorsprünge\n mit Macadamisirung von Pflastersteinen beschäftigt. Jeder hatte einen\n dicken Pflasterstein zum Sitz und klopfte mit schwerem Hammer auf den\n frostbedeckten Granit, bis er 5 Bushel davon abgehauen hatte. Dann\n war sein Tagewerk verrichtet und erhielt er 3 d. (1 Silbergroschen, 8\n Pfennige) und ein Billet für Brod. In einem andern Theil des Hofes\n stand ein rhachitisches kleines Holzhaus. Beim Oeffnen der Thüre fanden\n wir es gefüllt mit Männern, Schulter an Schulter gedrängt, um einander\n warm zu halten. Sie zupften Schiffstau und stritten mit einander, wer\n von ihnen mit einem Minimum von Nahrung am längsten arbeiten könne,\n denn Ausdauer war der point d’honneur. In diesem einen Workhouse\n allein erhielten 7000 Unterstützung, darunter viele Hunderte, die 6\n oder 8 Monate zuvor die höchsten Löhne geschickter Arbeit in\n diesem Land verdienten. Ihre Zahl wäre doppelt so gross gewesen,\n gäbe es nicht so viele, welche nach Erschöpfung ihrer ganzen Geldreserve\n dennoch vor Zuflucht zur Pfarrei zurückbeben, so lange sie noch irgend\n etwas zu versetzen haben … Das Workhouse verlassend, machte ich\n einen Gang durch die Strassen von meist einstöckigen Häusern, die in\n Poplar so zahlreich. Mein Führer war Mitglied des Comités für die\n Arbeitslosen. Das erste Haus, worin wir eintraten, war das eines Eisen-\n arbeiters, seit 27 Wochen ausser Beschäftigung. Ich fand den Mann mit\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"659\", \"facs\": \"#f0678\"} --> seiner ganzen Familie in einem Hinterzimmer sitzend. Das Zimmer war\n noch nicht ganz von Möbeln entblösst und es war Feuer darin. Diess\n war nöthig, um die nackten Füsse der jungen Kinder vor Frost zu\n schützen, denn es war ein grimmig kalter Tag. Auf einem Teller gegen-\n über dem Feuer lag ein Quantum Werg, welches Frau und Kinder zupften\n in Erstattung des Brods vom Workhouse. Der Mann arbeitete in einem\n der oben beschriebenen Höfe für ein Brodbillet und 3 d. per Tag. Er\n kam jetzt nach Haus zum Mittagsessen, sehr hungrig, wie er uns mit\n einem bittern Lächeln sagte, und sein Mittagsessen bestand aus einigen\n Brodschnitten mit Schmalz und einer Tasse milchlosen Thees … Die\n nächste Thüre, an der wir anklopften, wurde geöffnet durch ein Frauen-\n zimmer mittleren Alters, die, ohne ein Wort zu sagen, uns in ein kleines\n Hinterzimmer führte, wo ihre ganze Familie sass, schweigend, die Augen\n auf ein rasch ersterbendes Feuer geheftet. Solche Verödung, solche Hoff-\n nungslosigkeit hing um diese Leute und ihr kleines Zimmer, dass ich nicht\n wünsche je eine ähnliche Scene wieder zu sehn. „Nichts haben sie ver-\n dient, mein Herr“, sagte die Frau, auf ihre Jungen zeigend, „nichts für\n 26 Wochen, und all unser Geld ist hingegangen, alles Geld, das ich und\n der Vater in den bessern Zeiten zurücklegten, in dem Wahn eine Reserve\n während schlechten Geschäfts zu sichern. Sehn Sie es“, schrie sie fast wild, in-\n dem sie ein Bankbuch hervorholte mit allen seinen regelmässigen Nachweisen\n über eingezahltes und rückerhaltnes Geld, so dass wir sehn konnten, wie das\n kleine Vermögen begonnen hatte mit dem ersten Deposit von 5 Shilling, wie\n es nach und nach zu 20 Pfd. St. aufwuchs, und dann wieder zusammen-\n schmolz, von Pfunden zu Shillingen, bis der letzte Eintrag das Buch so\n werthlos machte, wie ein blankes Stück Papier. Diese Familie erhielt\n ein nothdürftiges Mahl täglich vom Workhouse … Unsre folgende Visite\n war zur Frau eines Irländers, der an den Schiffswerften gearbeitet hatte.\n Wir fanden sie krank von Nahrungsmangel, in ihren Kleidern auf eine\n Matratze gestreckt, knapp bedeckt mit einem Stück Teppich, denn alles\n Bettzeug war im Pfandhaus. Die elenden Kinder warteten sie und sahen\n aus als bedürften sie umgekehrt der mütterlichen Pflege. Neunzehn\n Wochen erzwungnen Müssiggangs hatten sie so weit heruntergebracht,\n und während sie die Geschichte der bitteren Vergangenheit erzählte,\n stöhnte sie als ob alle Hoffnung auf eine bessere Zukunft verloren wäre\n … Beim Austritt aus dem Hause rannte ein junger Mann auf uns zu und\n [Running matter: 42*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"660\", \"facs\": \"#f0679\"} --> bat uns in sein Haus zu gehn und zu sehn, ob irgend etwas für ihn ge-\n schehn könne. Ein junges Weib, zwei hübsche Kinder, ein Kluster von\n Pfandzetteln und ein ganz kahles Zimmer war alles, was er zu zeigen\n hatte“ [Note: Da es grade jetzt Mode unter den englischen Kapitalisten ist, Belgien\n als das Paradies des Arbeiters zu schildern, weil „die Freiheit der Arbeit“\n dort weder durch den Despotismus der Trade’s Unions, noch durch Fabrikgesetze\n verkümmert sei, hier ein paar Worte über das „Glück“ des nur durch Klerisei,\n Grundaristokratie, liberale Bourgeois und Bureaukraten, aber bei Leibe nicht durch\n Trade’s Unions und Fabrikgesetze gehänselten „freien“ belgischen Arbeiters.\n Herr Ducpetiaux, eine gute Autorität, früher, ich weiss nicht ob noch, Gene-\n ralinspektor der belgischen Gefängnisse, sagt in seinen: „Budgets écono-\n miques des classes ouvrières en Belgique“: „Im Durchschnitt zählt\n eine Arbeiterfamilie 4 Kinder, also mit Vater und Mutter 6 Personen. Von diesen\n 6 Personen können 4 nützlich beschäftigt werden, wenn Krankheit u. dgl. nicht\n dazwischen kömmt. Unter diesen Umständen sind folgendes die Hilfsquellen der\n Familie auf ihrem Maximalgrad:\n\n\n\n\n | Der Vater | 300 Tage | zu | 1 fc. 56 c. | per Jahr | 468 fcs.\n\n\n\n\n | Die Mutter | 〃 | „ | 0 fc. 89 c. | 〃 | 267 fcs.\n\n\n\n\n | Der älteste Junge | 〃 | 〃 | 0 fc. 56 c. | 〃 | 168 fcs.\n\n\n\n\n | Die älteste Tochter | 〃 | 〃 | 0 fc. 55 c. | 〃 | 165 fcs.\n\n\n\n\n |  |  |  |  |  | 1,068 fcs.\n\n\n\n\n Die Jahresausgabe der Familie und ihr Deficit würden sich erheben, falls\n der Arbeiter die Nahrung hätte:\n\n\n\n\n | Des Seesoldaten, | auf | 1828 fcs. | Deficit: | 760 fcs.\n\n\n\n\n | Des Soldaten, | 〃 | 1473 fcs. | 〃 | 405 fcs.\n\n\n\n\n | Des Gefangnen, | 〃 | 1112 fcs. | 〃 | 44 fcs.\n\n\n\n\n In jener Musterfamilie haben wir alle möglichen Hilfsquellen vereinigt. Aber\n indem wir der Familienmutter ein Salair gaben. entzogen wir dem Haushalt ihre\n Leitung. Wer wird dem Inneren vorstehn, das Essen bereiten, Wäsche, Flicke-\n reien u. s. w. besorgen? Wie bringt es die grosse Mehrzahl der Arbeiter, welche\n die Waaren weder im Grossen kauft, noch im Halbgrossen, gleich der Gefängniss-\n verwaltung, wie bringt sie es fertig zu leben? Indem sie zu Nothbehelfen flüchtet,\n wovon der Arbeiter allein das Geheimniss hat, indem sie an ihrer täglichen Ration\n abknappt, wenig oder gar kein Fleisch isst, ebenso mit Butter und Gewürzen, indem\n sie die Familie in eine oder zwei Kammern packt, wo Mädchen und Jungen zusammen-\n schlafen, oft auf demselben Strohsack, indem sie an der Kleidung ökonomisirt, an\n Wäsche und Reinigungsmitteln, den Sonntagsvergnügungen entsagt u. s. w. Einmal\n bei dieser letzten Grenze angelangt, wirft die geringste Erhöhung der Preise der\n Lebensmittel u. s. w. sie auf die Armenliste.“ In der That ist in diesem „Paradies\n der Kapitalisten“ die geringste Variation der Getreidepreise be-] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"661\", \"facs\": \"#f0680\"} -->\n\nDer antagonistische Charakter der kapitalistischen Produktion und\n Accumulation bewährt sich nirgendwo brutaler als in dem Fortschritt\n des englischen Landbaus (Viehzucht eingeschlossen) und dem Rück-\n schritt des englischen Landarbeiters. Bevor ich zu seiner gegen-\n wärtigen Lage übergehe, ein rascher Rückblick. Die moderne Agri-\n kultur datirt in England von der Mitte des 18. Jahrhunderts, obgleich die\n Umwälzung der Grundeigenthumsverhältnisse, wovon die veränderte Pro-\n duktionsweise als Grundlage ausgeht, viel früheren Datums.\n\n\n\n\nNehmen wir die Angaben Arthur Young’s, eines genauen Be-\n obachters, obgleich elenden Denkers, über den Landarbeiter von 1771, so\n spieltletztrer eine sehr elende Rolle, verglichen mit seinem Vorgänger Ende\n des 14. Jahrhunderts, „wo er in Fülle leben und Reichthum\n accumuliren konnte“ [Note: James E. Th. Rogers (Prof. of Polit. Econ. in the Uni-\n versity of Oxford): „A History of Agriculture and Prices in\n England. Oxford 1866“, v. I, p. 690. Diess fleissig gearbeitete Werk um-\n fasst in den bisher erschienenen zwei ersten Bänden nur noch die Periode von\n 1259—1400. Der zweite Band enthält bloss statistisches Material. Es ist die erste\n authentische „History of Prices“, die wir für jene Zeit besitzen.] , gar nicht zu sprechen vom 15. Jahrhun-\n dert, „dem goldnen Zeitalter der englischen Arbeiter in Stadt und\n Land“. Wir brauchen jedoch nicht so weit zurückzugehn. In einer sehr\n gehaltreichen Schrift von 1777 liest man: „Der grosse Pächter hat sich\n beinahe erhoben zum Niveau des Gentleman, während der arme Land-\n arbeiter fast zu Boden gedrückt ist … Seine unglückliche Lage zeigt\n sich klar durch eine vergleichende Uebersicht seiner Verhältnisse von\n heute und von 40 Jahr früher … Grundeigenthümer und Pächter wirken\n Hand in Hand zur Unterdrückung des Arbeiters“ [Note: „Reasons for the late Increase of the Poorlaws; or, a\n comparative view of the price of labour and provisions. Lond.\n 1777“, p. 5, 14—16.] . Es wird dann im\n [Note: gleitet von einer Variation in Todesfällen und Verbrechen! (Sieh\n „Manifest der Maatschappij: De Vlaemingen Vooruit! Brussel\n 1860“, p. 15, 16.) Ganz Belgien zählt 930,000 Familien, davon, nach officieller\n Statistik: 90,000 Reiche (Wähler), 450,000 Personen; 190,000 Familien der kleinen\n Mittelklasse, in Stadt und Dorf, grosser Theil davon stets in’s Proletariat fallend,\n 1,950,000 Personen. Endlich 450,000 Arbeiterfamilien mit 2,250,000 Personen, von\n welchen die Musterfamilien das durch Ducpetiaux geschilderte Glück geniessen.\n Unter den 450,000 Arbeiterfamilien über 200,000 auf der Armenliste!] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"662\", \"facs\": \"#f0681\"} --> Detail nachgewiesen, dass der reelle Arbeitslohn auf dem Lande von 1737\n bis 1777 um beinahe ¼ oder 25 % gefallen ist. „Die moderne Politik“,\n sagt Dr. Richard Pricc zur selben Zeit, „begünstigt die höheren\n Volksklassen; die Folge wird sein, dass früher oder später das ganze\n Königreich nur aus Gentlemen und Bettlern, aus Grandees und Sklaven\n besteht“ [Note: Dr. Richard Price: „Observations on Reversionary Pay-\n ments.“ 6. ed. By W. Morgan. Lond. 1805, v. II, p. 158, 159. Price\n bemerkt p. 159: „The nominal price of day labour is at present no more than\n about four times, or at most five times higher than it was in the year 1514. But\n the price of corn is seven times, and of flesh-meat and raiment about fifteen times\n higher. So far, therefore, has the price of labour been even from advancing in\n proportion to the increase in the expences of living, that it does not appear that\n it bears now half the proportion to those expences that it did bear.“] .\n\n\n\n\nDennoch ist die Lage des englischen Landarbeiters von 1770 1780,\n sowohl was seine Nahrungs- und Wohnlichkeitszustände, als sein Selbst-\n gefühl, Belustigungen u. s. w. betrifft, ein später nie wieder erreichtes\nIdeal. In Pints Weizen ausgedrückt betrug sein Durchschnittslohn\n 1770—1771 90 Pints, zu Eden’s Zeit (1797) nur noch 65, 1808\n aber 60 [Note: Barton l. c. p. 26. Für Ende des 18. Jahrhunderts vgl. Eden l. c.] .\n\n\n\n\nDer Zustand der Landarbeiter Ende des Antijakobinerkriegs,\n während dessen sich Grundaristokratie, Pächter, Fabrikanten, Kaufleute,\n Banquiers, Börsenritter, Armeelieferanten u. s. w. so ausserordentlich be-\n reichert hatten, ist bereits früher angedeutet worden. Der nominelle\n Arbeitslohn stieg natürlich in Folge theils der Depreciation des Geldes theils\n eines von dieser Depreciation unabhängigen Steigens im Preis der noth-\n wendigsten Lebensmittel. Die wirkliche Bewegung des Arbeitslohns ist\n aber auf sehr einfache Art zu konstatiren, ohne Zuflucht zu hier unzu-\n lässigen Details. Das Armengesetz und seine Administration waren 1795\n und 1814 dieselben. Man erinnert sich: diess Gesetz wurde auf dem\n Land in der Art gehandhabt, dass die Differenz zwischen dem dem\n Arbeiter gezahlten Arbeitslohn und der Minimalsumme, die zu\n seiner blossen Vegetation erheischt ist, von der Pfarrei in der Form der\n Armenunterstützung ergänzt wurde. Das Verhältniss zwischen dem vom\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"663\", \"facs\": \"#f0682\"} --> Pächter gezahlten Arbeitslohn und dem von der Pfarrei gutge-\n machten Deficit dieses Arbeitslohns zeigt uns zweierlei, erstens\n die Senkung des Arbeitslohns unter sein Minimum, zweitens den Grad,\n worin der Landarbeiter aus Lohnarbeiter und Pauper zusammengesetzt\n war, oder den Grad, worin er in einen Leibeignen seiner Pfarrei verwan-\n delt ward. Wir nehmen eine Grafschaft, die das Durchschnittsverhältniss\n in allen andern Grafschaften repräsentirt. 1795 betrug der durch-\n schnittliche Wochenlohn in Northampton 7 sh. 6d., die jährliche Total-\n ausgabe einer Familie von 6 Personen 36 Pfd. St. 12 sh. 5 d., ihre\nTotaleinnahme 29 Pfd. St. 18 sh., das von der Pfarrei gutgemachte\nDeficit: 6 Pfd. St. 14 sh. 5 d. In derselben Grafschaft betrug 1814\n der Wochenlohn 12 sh. 2 d., die jährliche Totalausgabe einer Fa-\n milie von 5 Personen 54 Pfd. St. 18 sh. 4 d., ihre Totaleinnahme\n 36 Pfd. St. 2 sh., das von der Pfarrei gutgemachte Deficit: 18 Pfd.\n St. 6 sh. 4 d. [Note: Parry l. c. p. 86.] . 1795 betrug das Deficit weniger als ¼ des Ar-\n beitslohns, 1814 mehr als die Hälfte. Dass unter diesen Umständen die\n geringen Komforts, die bei Eden noch in der Cottage des Landarbei-\n ters figuriren, 1814 verschwunden waren, versteht sich von selbst [Note: id. p. 213.] .\n Unter allen Thieren, die der Pächter hält, blieb von nun an der Arbeiter,\n das instrumentum vocale, das meist geplackte, schlechtest gefütterte und\n am brutalsten behandelte.\n\n\n\n\nDerselbe Zustand der Dinge dauerte ruhig fort, bis „die swing Auf-\n stände 1830 uns (d. h. den herrschenden Klassen) beim Lichtflammen der\n Kornschober enthüllten, dass Elend und dunkle aufrührerische Unzufrieden-\n heit ebenso wild unter der Oberfläche des agrikolen als des industriellen\n Englands lodre“ [Note: S. Laing l c. p. 62.] . Sadler taufte damals im Unterhaus die Land-\n arbeiter „weisse Sklaven“ („white slaves“), ein Bischof hallte das Epithet\n im Oberhaus wieder. Der bedeutendste politische Oekonom jener Pe-\n riode, E. G. Wakefield, sagt: „der Landarbeiter Südenglands ist\n kein Sklave, er ist kein freier Mann, er ist ein Pauper“ [Note: England and America. Lond. 1833, v. I, p. 45.] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"664\", \"facs\": \"#f0683\"} -->\n\nDie Zeit unmittelbar vor der Aufhebung der Kornge-\n setze warf neues Licht auf die Lage der Landarbeiter. Einerseits lag\n es natürlich im Interesse der bürgerlichen Anticornlawagitatoren nachzu-\n weisen, wie wenig jene Schutzgesetze den wirklichen Kornproducen-\n ten beschützten. Andrerseits schäumte die industrielle Bourgeoisie auf von\n Ingrimm über die Denunciation der Fabrikzustände seitens der Grund-\n aristokraten, über die affektirte Sympathie dieser grundverdorbnen, herz-\n losen und vornehmen Müssiggänger mit den Leiden des Fabrikarbeiters,\n und ihren „diplomatischen“ Eifer für Fabrikgesetzgebung. Es ist ein\n altes englisches Sprichwort, dass wenn zwei Diebe sich in die Haare fallen,\n immer etwas Nützliches geschieht. Und in der That, der geräuschvolle,\n leidenschaftliche Zank zwischen den zwei Fraktionen der herrschenden\n Klasse, welche von beiden den Arbeiter am schamlosesten ausbeute und\ndas meiste Produkt fremder Arbeit als Privateigenthum\n des Nichtarbeiters usurpire, dieser Zank wurde rechts und links\n Geburtshelfer der Wahrheit. Graf Shaftesbury, alias Lord Ashley,\n war Vorkämpfer im aristokratischen Fabrikphilanthropiefeldzug. Er bildet\n daher 1844—1845 ein Lieblingsthema in den Enthüllungen des Mor-\n ning Chronicle über die Zustände der Agrikulturarbeiter. Jenes\n Blatt, damals das bedeutendste liberale Organ, entsandte eigne Kommis-\n säre in die Agrikulturdistrikte, welche sich keineswegs mit allgemeiner\n Schilderung und Statistik begnügten, sondern die Namen sowohl der\n untersuchten Arbeiterfamilien als ihrer Landlords veröffentlichten. Die\n folgende Liste giebt Löhne, gezahlt auf drei Dörfern, in der Nachbarschaft\n von Blandford, Wimbourne und Poole. Die Dörfer sind Eigenthum des Mr.\n G. Bankes und des Grafen von Shaftesbury. Man wird bemerken,\n dass dieser Pabst der „low church“, diess Haupt der englischen Pietisten,\n und p. p. Bankes von den Hundelöhnen ihrer Arbeiter wieder einen bedeu-\n tenden Theil unter dem Vorwand von Hausrente einstecken.\n\n\n\n\n(Siehe nebenstehende Tabelle.)\n\n\n\n\nDer Widerruf der Korngesetze gab dem englischen Landbau einen\n ungeheuren Ruck. Drainirung auf der grössten Stufenleiter147), neues\n System der Stallfütterung und des Anbaus der künstlichen Futterkräuter,\n Einführung mechanischer Düngapparate, neue Behandlung der Thonerde,\n gesteigerter Gebrauch mineralischer Düngmittel, Anwendung der Dampf-\n maschine und aller Art neuer Maschinerie u. s. w., intensivere Kultur\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"665\", \"facs\": \"#f0684\"} -->\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n überhaupt charakterisiren diese Epoche. Der Präsident der königlichen\n Gesellschaft für Agrikultur, Herr Pusey, behauptet, dass die (relativen)\n Wirthschaftskosten durch die neu eingeführte Maschinerie beinahe um die\n Hälfte verringert worden sind. Andrerseits ward der positive Boden-\n ertrag rasch erhöht. Grössere Kapitalauslage per Acre war Grundbe-\n dingung der neuen Methode, also auch beschleunigte Koncentration der\n Pachten [Note: Die Abnahme der mittleren Pächter ersieht man namentlich aus den\n Rubriken des Census: „Pächters Sohn, Enkel, Bruder, Neffe, Tochter, Enkelin,\n Schwester, Nichte“, kurz der vom Pächter beschäftigten Glieder seiner eignen\n Familie. Diese Rubriken zählten 1851: 216, 851 Personen, 1861 nur 176,151.] . Zugleich dehnte sich das Areal der Bebauung von 1846 bis\n 1865 um 464,119 Acres aus, nicht zu sprechen von den grossen Flächen\n [Note: 147) Die Grundaristokratie schoss sich selbst zu diesem Zweck Fonds, natür-\n lich per Parlament, aus der Staatskasse vor, zu sehr niedrigem Zins, welchen\n die Pächter ihr doppelt zu erstatten haben.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"666\", \"facs\": \"#f0685\"} --> der östlichen Grafschaften, welche aus Kaninchengeheg und armer Vieh-\n weide in üppige Kornfelder umgezaubert wurden. Man weiss bereits,\n dass gleichzeitig die Gesammtzahl der in der Agrikultur betheiligten\n Personen abnahm. Was die eigentlichen Ackerbauer, bei-\n derlei Geschlechts und aller Altersstufen, betrifft, so sank ihre Zahl von\n 1,241,269 im Jahr 1851 auf 1,163,227 im Jahr 1861 [Note: Die Zahl der Schafhirten wuchs von 12,517 auf 25,559.] . Wenn der\n englische Generalregistrator daher mit Recht bemerkt: „Der Zuwachs von\n Pächtern und Landarbeitern seit 1801 steht in gar keinem Verhältniss\n zum Zuwachs des agrikolen Produkts“ [Note: Census etc. l. c. p. 36.] , so gilt diess Missverhältniss\n noch viel mehr von der letzten Periode, wo positive Abnahme der\n ländlichen Arbeiterbevölkerung Hand in Hand ging mit Aus-\n dehnung des bebauten Areals, intensiverer Kultur, unerhörter Accumula-\n tion des dem Boden einverleibten und des seiner Bearbeitung gewidmeten\n Kapitals, Steigerung des Bodenprodukts ohne Parallele in der Geschichte\n der englischen Agronomie, strotzenden Rentrollen der Grundeigenthümer\n und schwellendem Reichthum der kapitalistischen Pächter. Nimmt man\n diess zusammen mit der ununterbrochen raschen Erweiterung des städtischen\n Absatzmarkts und der Herrschaft des Freihandels, so war der Landar-\n beiter post tot discrimina rerum endlich in Verhältnisse gestellt, die ihn,\nsecundum artem, glückstoll machen mussten.\n\n\n\n\nProfessor Rogers gelangt dagegen zum Resultat, dass der englische\n Landarbeiter heutigen Tags, gar nicht zu sprechen von seinem Vorgänger\n in der letzten Hälfte des 14. Jahrhunderts und im 15. Jahrhundert, sondern\n nur verglichen mit seinem Vorgänger aus der Periode 1770—1780, seine\n Lage ausserordentlich verschlechtert hat, dass „er wieder ein Leibeigner\n geworden ist“ und zwar schlecht gefütterter und behauster Leibeigner [Note: Rogers l. c. p. 693. „The peasant has again become a serf.“ l. c.\n p. 10. Herr Rogers gehört zur liberalen Schule, ist persönlicher Freund von\n Cobden und Bright, also kein laudator temporis acti.] .\nDr. Julian Hunter, in seinem epochemachenden Bericht über die\n Wohnlichkeit der Landarbeiter, sagt: „Die Existenzkost des hind (der\n Zeit der Leibeigenschaft angehöriger Name für den Landarbeiter) ist fixirt\n zu dem möglichst niedrigen Betrag, womit er leben kann … seine Zufuhr\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"667\", \"facs\": \"#f0686\"} --> von Lohn und Obdach ist nicht berechnet auf den aus ihm herauszu-\n schlagenden Profit. Er ist der Nullpunkt in den Berechnungen des Päch-\n ters“ [Note: „Public Health. Seventh Report. Lond. 1865“, p. 242.\n „The cost of the hind is fixed at the lowest possible amount on which he can live\n … the supplies of wages or shelter are not calculated on the profit to be derived\n from him. He is a zero in farming calculations.“ Es ist daher nichts ungewöhn-\n liches, dass entweder der Hausvermiether die Miethe für einen Arbeiter erhöht, so-\n bald er hört, dass er etwas mehr verdient, oder dass der Pächter den Lohn des Ar-\n beiters heruntersetzt, „weil seine Frau eine Beschäftigung gefunden hat.“ (l. c.)] . „Seine Subsistenzmittel werden stets als eine fixe Quantität be-\n handelt“ [Note: l. c. p. 135.] . „Was irgend eine weitere Reduktion seines Einkommens\n angeht, so kann er sagen: nihil habeo, nihil curo. Er hat keine Furcht\n für die Zukunft, weil er über nichts verfügt ausser dem, was zu seiner\n Existenz absolut unentbehrlich ist. Er hat den Gefrierpunkt erreicht,\n von dem die Berechnungen des Pächters als Datum ausgehn. Komme was\n wolle, er hat keinen Antheil an Glück oder Unglück“ [Note: l. c. p. 134.] .\n\n\n\n\nIm Jahr 1863 fand eine officielle Untersuchung über die Verpflegungs-\n und Beschäftigungszustände der zu Transportation und öffentlicher Zwangs-\n arbeit verurtheilten Verbrecher statt. Die Resultate sind in zwei dickleibigen\n Blaubüchern niedergelegt. „Eine sorgfältige Vergleichung,“ heisst es\n unter anderem, „zwischen der Diät der Verbrecher in den Gefängnissen von\n England und der der Paupers in Workhouses und der freien Landarbeiter\n desselben Landes zeigt unstreitig, dass die erstern viel besser genährt sind\n als irgend eine der beiden andern Klassen“ [Note: „Report of the Commissioners … relating to Transpor-\n tation and Penal Servitude. Lond. 1863“, v. I, n. 50.] , während „die Arbeitsmasse,\n die von einem zu öffentlicher Zwangsarbeit Verurtheilten verlangt wird,\n ungefähr die Hälfte der vom gewöhnlichen Landarbeiter verrichteten be-\n trägt“ [Note: l. c. p. 77. „Memorandum by the Lord Chief Justice.“] . Einige wenige charakteristische Zeugenaussagen: John Smith,\n Direktor des Gefängnisses von Edinburg, verhört. Nr. 5056: „Die Diät\n in den englischen Gefängnissen ist viel besser als die der gewöhnlichen\n Landarbeiter.“ Nr. 5075: „Es ist Thatsache, dass die gewöhnlichen\n Agrikulturarbeiter Schottlands sehr selten irgend welches Fleisch erhal-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"668\", \"facs\": \"#f0687\"} --> ten.“ Nr. 3047: „Kennen Sie irgend einen Grund für die Nothwendig-\n keit, die Verbrecher viel besser (much better) zu nähren als gewöhnliche\n Landarbeiter? — Sicher nicht.“ Nr. 3048: „Halten Sie es für ange-\n messen weitere Experimente zu machen, um die Diät zu öffentlichen\n Zwangsarbeiten verurtheilter Gefangenen der Diät freier Landarbeiter\n nahe zu bringen?“ [Note: l. c. v. II. Evidence.]  „Der Landarbeiter,“ heisst es, „könnte sagen:\n Ich arbeite hart und habe nicht genug zu essen. Als ich im Gefängniss\n war, arbeitete ich nicht so hart und hatte Essen in Fülle, und darum ist\n es besser für mich im Gefängniss als im Freien zu sein“ [Note: l. c. v. I. Appendix p. 280] . Aus den\n dem ersten Band des Berichts angehängten Tabellen ist folgende verglei-\n chende Uebersicht zusammengestellt:\nWöchentlicher Nahrungsbetrag.\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDas allgemeine Resultat der ärztlichen Untersuchungskommission von\n 1863 über den Nahrungszustand der schlechter genährten Volksklassen\n ist dem Leser bereits bekannt. Er erinnert sich, dass die Diät eines\n grossen Theils der Landarbeiterfamilien unter dem Minimalmass „zur\n Abwehr von Hungerkrankheiten“ steht. Es ist diess namentlich der Fall\n in allen rein agrikolen Distrikten von Cornwall, Devon, Somerset, Dorset,\n Wilts, Stafford, Oxford, Berks und Herts. „Die Nahrung, die der Land-\n arbeiter erhält,“ sagt Dr. Simon, „ist grösser als das Durchschnitts-\n quantum anzeigt, da er selbst einen viel grösseren,  [Editorial alternatives: sic = fur; corr = für]  seine Arbeit unentbehr-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"669\", \"facs\": \"#f0688\"} --> lichen, Theil der Lebensmittel erhält als seine übrigen Familienglieder, in den\n ärmeren Distrikten fast alles Fleisch oder Speck. Das Quantum Nahrung, das\n der Frau zufällt, und ebenso den Kindern in ihrer Periode raschen Wachsthums,\n ist in vielen Fällen, und zwar in fast allen Grafschaften, mangelhaft,\n hauptsächlich an Stickstoff“ [Note: „Public Health. Sixth Report. 1863“. Lond. 1864, p. 238,\n 249, 261, 262.] . Die bei den Pächtern selbst wohnenden\n Knechte und Mägde werden reichlich genährt. Ihre Zahl fiel von 288,277\n im Jahr 1851 auf 204,962 im Jahr 1861. „Die Arbeit der Weiber auf\n freiem Feld,“ sagt Dr. Smith, „von welchen sonstigen Nachtheilen auch\n immer begleitet, ist unter gegenwärtigen Umständen von grossem Vortheil\n für die Familie, denn sie liefert derselben Mittel für Beschuhung, Kleidung,\n Zahlung der Hausrente, und befähigt sie so besser zu essen“ [Note: l. c. p. 262.] . Eins\n der merkwürdigsten Resultate dieser Untersuchung war, dass der Landar-\n beiter in England bei weitem schlechter genährt ist als in den andern\n Theilen des Vereinigten Königreichs („is considerably the worst fed“),\n wie die folgende Tabelle zeigt:\nWöchentlicher Konsum von Kohle und Stickstoff durch den ländlichen Durch-\n schnittsarbeiter.\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"670\", \"facs\": \"#f0689\"} -->\n\n„Jede Seite von Dr. Hunters Bericht“, sagt Dr. Simon in seinem\n officiellen Gesundheitsbericht, „giebt Zeugniss von der unzureichenden\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"671\", \"facs\": \"#f0690\"} --> Quantität und miserablen Qualität der Wohnlichkeit unsres Land-\n arbeiters. Und progressiv seit vielen Jahren hat sich sein Zustand in\n dieser Hinsicht verschlechtert. Es ist jetzt viel schwerer für ihn, Haus-\n raum zu finden, und, wenn gefunden, ist er seinen Bedürfnissen viel weni-\n ger entsprechend, als vielleicht seit Jahrhunderten der Fall war.\nBesonders innerhalb der letzten 30 oder 20 Jahre ist das\n Uebel in raschem Wachsthum begriffen, und die Wohnlichkeitsverhältnisse\n des Landmanns sind jetzt im höchsten Grad kläglich. Ausser\n soweit diejenigen, die seine Arbeit bereichert, es der Mühe werth\n halten ihn mit einer Art von mitleidiger Nachsicht zu behandeln, ist er\n ganz hilflos in der Sache. Ob er Behausung findet auf dem Land, wel-\n ches er bebaut, ob sie menschlich oder schweinisch ist, ob mit kleinem\n Garten, der den Druck der Armuth so sehr erleichtert, alles das hängt\n nicht von seiner Bereitheit oder Fähigkeit zur Zahlung einer angemessnen\n Miethe ab, sondern von dem Gebrauch, den Andre von ‚dem Recht mit\n ihrem Eigenthum zu thun was sie wollen‘ zu machen belieben. Eine\n Pacht mag noch so gross sein, es existirt kein Gesetz, dass auf ihr eine\n bestimmte Anzahl von Arbeiterwohnungen, und nun gar anständigen,\n stehen muss; noch behält das Gesetz dem Arbeiter auch nur das kleinste\n Recht auf den Boden vor, für welchen seine Arbeit so nothwendig ist wie\n Regen und Sonnenschein … Ein notorischer Umstand wirft noch ein\n schweres Gewicht in die Wagschale gegen ihn …, der Einfluss des Ar-\n mengesetzes mit seinen Bestimmungen über Niederlassung und Belastung\n zur Armentaxe [Note: 1865 ist diess Gesetz etwas verbessert worden. Man wird bald durch die\n Erfahrung lernen, dass dergleichen Pfuscherei nichts hilft.] . Unter seinem Einfluss hat jede Pfarrei ein Geldinter-\n esse die Zahl ihrer residirenden Landarbeiter auf ein Mini-\n mum zu beschränken; denn, unglücklicher Weise, die Land-\n arbeit, statt sichre und permanente Unabhängigkeit des hartschanzenden\n Arbeiters und seiner Familie zu verbürgen, involvirt meist nur einen\n längern oder kürzern Umweg zu eventuellem Pauperismus,\n ein Pauperismus, der während des ganzen Wegs so nahe ist, dass jede\n Krankheit oder irgend ein vorübergehender Mangel an Beschäftigung unmit-\n telbar die Zuflucht zur Pfarreihilfe ernöthigt; und daher ist alle Resi-\n denz einer Ackerbaubevölkerung in einer Pfarrei augenschein-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"672\", \"facs\": \"#f0691\"} --> lich ein Zuschuss zu ihrer Armensteuer … Grosse Grund-\n eigenthümer [Note: Zum Verständniss des folgenden: Close Villages (geschlossene Dörfer)\n heissen die, deren Grundeigenthümer ein oder ein paar grosse Landlords. Open\n Villages (offene Dörfer) die, deren Boden vielen kleineren Eigenthümern ge-\n hört. Es sind die letzteren Orte, wo Bauspekulanten Cottages und Logirhäuser\n errichten können.]  haben nur zu beschliessen, dass keine Arbeiterwohnungen\n auf ihren Gütern stehn sollen, und sie befreien sich sofort von der Hälfte\n ihrer Verantwortlichkeit für die Armen. Wie weit die englische Konsti-\n tution und das Gesetz diese Art unbedingtes Grundeigenthum beabsichtig-\n ten, welches einen Landlord, der „mit seinem Eignen thut was er will“,\n befähigt, die Bebauer des Bodens wie Fremde zu behandeln und sie von\n seinem Territorium zu verjagen, ist eine Frage, deren Diskussion nicht in\n meinen Bereich fällt … Diese Macht der Eviktion ist keine blosse\n Theorie. Sie wird praktisch auf der grössten Stufenleiter geltend ge-\n macht. Sie ist einer der Umstände, welche die Wohnlichkeitsverhältnisse\n des Landarbeiters beherrschen … Den Umfang des Uebels mag man aus\n dem letzten Census beurtheilen, wonach die Zerstörung von Häu-\n sern, trotz vermehrter lokaler Nachfrage für dieselben,\n während der letzten 10 Jahre, in 821 verschiednen Distrikten von Eng-\n land fortschritt, so dass, abgesehn von den Personen, die gezwungen wur-\n den Nichtresidirende (nämlich in dem Kirchspiel, worin sie arbei-\n ten) zu werden, 1861 verglichen mit 1851 eine um 5⅓ % grössere Be-\n völkerung in einen um 4½ % kleineren Hausraum gedrängt wurde …\n Sobald der Entvölkerungsprozess sein Ziel erreicht hat, ist das Resultat,\n sagt Dr. Hunter, ein Schaudorf (show-village), wo die Cottages auf\n wenige reducirt sind und wo niemand leben darf ausser Schafhirten,\n Gärtnern und Wildhütern, reguläre Bediente, welche die ihrer Klasse ge-\n wohnte gute Behandlung von der gnädigen Herrschaft erhalten [Note: Ein solches Schaudorf sieht sehr nett aus, aber es ist so unreal, wie die\n Dörfer, welche Catharina II. auf der Reise nach der Krim sah. In der letzteren\n Zeit wird auch der Schafhirt häufig aus diesen show-villages verbannt. Z. B. bei\n Market Harborough ist eine Schäferei von ungefähr 500 Acres, die nur die Arbeit\neines Mannes erheischt. Zur Verminderung der langen Märsche über diese\n weiten Flächen, die schönen Weiden von Leicester und Northampton, pflegte der\n Hirt eine Cottage auf der Meierei zu erhalten. Jetzt giebt man ihm einen drei-\n zehnten Shilling für Logis, das er weitab in dem offenen Dorf suchen muss.] . Aber\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"673\", \"facs\": \"#f0692\"} --> das Land bedarf der Bebauung, und man wird finden, dass die darauf\n beschäftigten Arbeiter keine Haussassen des Grundeigenthümers sind, son-\n dern von einem offnen Dorf herkommen, vielleicht 3 Meilen weit ent-\n fernt, wo eine zahlreiche kleine Hauseigenthümerschaft sie aufnahm, nach\n Zerstörung ihrer Cottages in den geschlossnen Dörfern. Wo die Dinge\n diesem Resultat zustreben, bezeugen die Cottages meist durch ihr elendes\n Aussehn das Schicksal, zu dem sie verdammt sind. Man findet sie auf\n den verschiednen Stufen natürlichen Verfalls. So lange das Obdach zu-\n sammenhält, wird dem Arbeiter erlaubt, Rente dafür zu zahlen, und er ist\n oft sehr froh diess thun zu dürfen, selbst wenn er den Preis einer guten\n Wohnung zu zahlen hat. Aber keine Reparatur, keine Ausbesserung,\n ausser die der pfenniglose Inhaber leisten kann. Wird es endlich zuletzt\n ganz unbewohnbar, so ist es nur eine zerstörte Cottage mehr und so viel\n künftige Armensteuer weniger. Während die grossen Eigenthümer die\n Armentaxe so von sich abwälzen durch Entvölkerung des von ihnen kon-\n trolirten Grund und Bodens, nimmt das nächste Landstädtchen oder offne\n Ortschaft die hinausgeworfnen Arbeiter auf; die nächste, sage ich; aber\n diess „nächste“ mag 3 oder 4 Meilen vom Pachthof entfernt sein, wo der\n Arbeiter sich täglich abzuplacken hat. So wird seinem Tages-\n werk, als ob es gar nichts sei, die Nothwendigkeit eines\n täglichen Marsches von 6 oder 8 Meilen zur Verdienung seines\n täglichen Brodes hinzugefügt. Alle von seiner Frau und seinen Kindern\n verrichtete Landarbeit geht jetzt unter denselben erschwerenden Umstän-\n den vor. Und diess ist nicht das ganze Uebel, welches ihm die Entfer-\n nung verursacht. In der offnen Ortschaft kaufen Bauspekulanten Boden-\n fetzen, welche sie so dicht wie möglich mit den wohlfeilsten aller mög-\n lichen Spelunken besäen. Und in diesen elenden Wohnlichkeiten, die\n sogar, wenn sie auf das offne Land münden, die ungeheuerlichsten\n Charakterzüge der schlechtesten Stadtwohnungen theilen,\n hocken die Agrikulturarbeiter Englands [Note: „Die Häuser der Arbeiter (in den offenen Ortschaften, die natürlich stets\n überfüllt sind) sind gewöhnlich in Reihen gebaut, mit dem Rücken auf der äusser-\n sten Kante des Bodenfetzens, den der Bauspekulant sein nennt. Sie sind daher\n ohne Zutritt von Licht und Luft, ausser von der Frontseite.“ (Dr. Hunter’s\n Report l. c. p. 135.) Sehr oft ist der Bierwirth oder Krämer des Dorfs zugleich]  … Andrerseits muss man\n [Running matter: I. 43] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"674\", \"facs\": \"#f0693\"} --> sich nur nicht einbilden, dass selbst der auf dem Grund und Boden, den er\n bebaut, behauste Arbeiter eine Wohnlichkeit findet, wie sie sein Leben\n produktiver Industrie verdient. Selbst auf den fürstlichsten Gütern ist seine\n Cottage oft von der allerjämmerlichsten Art. Es giebt Landlords, die\n einen Stall gut genug für ihre Arbeiter und deren Familien glauben, und\n die es dennoch nicht verschmähn aus ihrer Miethe so viel Baares als\n möglich herauszuschlagen [Note: „Der Hausvermiether (der Pächter oder Landlord) bereichert sich direkt oder\n indirekt durch die Arbeit eines Mannes, dem er 10 sh. per Woche zahlt, und zwackt\n dann wieder von diesem armen Teufel 4 oder 5 Pfd. St. jährliche Miethe\n für Häuser ab, die keine 20 Pfd. St. auf offenem Markt werth sind, aber auf\n ihrem künstlichen Preis erhalten werden durch die Macht des Eigenthümers zu\n sagen: „Nimm mein Haus, oder pack’ Dich und suche anderswo ein Unterkommen,\n ohne Charaktercertifikat von mir“ … Wünscht ein Mann sich zu verbessern und\n als Schienenleger zu einer Eisenbahn zu gehn oder einem Steinbruch, wieder ist\n dieselbe Macht bereit mit einem: „Arbeite für mich zu diesem niedrigen Arbeits-\n lohn, oder pack’ Dich auf eine Woche Kündigung; nimm Dein Schwein mit Dir,\n wenn Du eins hast, und schau’ zu, was Du aus den Kartoffeln herausschlägst, die\n in Deinem Garten wachsen.“ Steht jedoch das Interesse nach der andern Seite,\n so zieht in solchen Fällen der Eigenthümer [resp. Pächter] manchmal eine er-\n höhte Hausmiethe vor als Strafe für die Desertion aus seinem Dienst.“\n (Dr. Hunter l. c. p. 131.)] . Es mag nur eine verfallende Hütte mit\n [Note: Hausvermiether. In diesem Fall findet der Landarbeiter in ihm einen zweiten\n Meister neben dem Pächter. Er muss zugleich sein Miether und Kunde sein. „Mit\n 10 sh. per Woche, minus einer jährlichen Rente von 4 Pfd. St., ist er verpflichtet,\n sein modicum von Thee, Zucker, Mehl, Seife, Kerzen und Bier zu den dem Krämer\n beliebten Preisen zu kaufen.“ (l. c. p. 131.) Diese offenen Dorfschaften bilden\n in der That die „Strafkolonieen“ des englischen Ackerbauprole-\n tariats. Viele der Cottages sind reine Logirhäuser, wo alles vagabundirende\n Gesindel der Umgegend durchpassirt. Der Landmann und seine Familie, die oft\n wahrhaft wunderbar in den schmutzigsten Verhältnissen Tüchtigkeit und Reinheit\n des Charakters bewahrt hatten, gehn hier platterdings zum Teufel. Es ist natürlich\n Mode unter den vornehmen Shylocks über die Bauspekulanten und die kleinen\n Eigenthümer und die offenen Orte pharisäisch die Achsel zu zucken. Sie wissen\n sehr wohl, dass ihre „geschlossenen Dörfer“ und „Schaudörfer“ die Geburts-\n stätten der „offenen Orte“ sind und ohne dieselben nicht existiren könnten.\n „Ohne die kleinen Eigenthümer der offenen Orte müsste der grösste Theil der\n Landarbeiter unter den Bäumen der Güter schlafen, worauf sie arbeiten.“\n l. c. p. 135.) Das System der „offenen“ und „geschlossenen“ Dörfer herrscht\n in allen Midlands und im ganzen Osten Englands.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"675\", \"facs\": \"#f0694\"} -->einer Schlafstube sein, ohne Feuerherd, ohne Abtritt, ohne öffenbare\n Fenster, ohne Wasserzufuhr ausser dem Graben, ohne Garten, der Arbeiter\n ist hilflos gegen die Unbill. Und unsere gesundheitspolizeilichen Gesetze\n („The Nuisances Removal Acts“) sind ein todter Buchstabe. Ihre Aus-\n führung ist ja grade den Eigenthümern anvertraut, welche solche Löcher\n vermiethen … Man muss sich durch ausnahmsweise lichtvollere Scenen\n nicht blenden lassen über das erdrückende Uebergewicht der Thatsachen,\n die ein Schandfleck der englischen Civilisation sind. Schauderhaft muss\n in der That die Lage der Dinge sein, wenn, trotz der augenfälligen Unge-\n heuerlichkeit der gegenwärtigen Behausung, kompetente Beobachter ein-\n stimmig zu dem Schlussresultat gelangen, dass selbst die allgemeine\n Nichtswürdigkeit der Wohnungen noch ein unendlich minder drückendes\n Uebel ist als ihr bloss numerischer Mangel. Seit Jahren war die Ueber-\n stopfung der Wohnungen der Landarbeiter ein Gegenstand tiefen Kum-\n mers nicht nur für Personen, die auf Gesundheit, sondern für alle, die auf\n anständiges und moralisches Leben halten. Denn, wieder und wieder, in\n Phrasen so gleichförmig, dass sie stereotypirt zu sein scheinen, denunciren\n die Berichterstatter über die Verbreitung epidemischer Krankheiten in den\n Ruraldistrikten Haus-Ueberfüllung als einen Einfluss, der jeden Versuch,\n den Fortschritt einer einmal eingeführten Epidemie aufzuhalten, durchaus\n vereitelt. Und wieder und wieder ward nachgewiesen, dass den vielen\n gesundheitlichen Einflüssen des Landlebens zum Trotz die Agglomeration,\n welche das Umsichgreifen ansteckender Krankheiten so sehr beschleunigt,\n auch die Entstehung nicht ansteckender Krankheiten fördert. Und die\n Personen, welche diesen Zustand denuncirt haben, verschwiegen weiteres\n Unheil nicht. Selbst wo ihr ursprüngliches Thema nur die Gesundheits-\n pflege betraf, waren sie beinahe gezwungen auf die andern Seiten des\n Gegenstandes einzugehn. Indem sie nachwiesen, wie häufig es sich er-\n eignet, dass erwachsne Personen beiderlei Geschlechts, verheirathet und\n unverheirathet, zusammengehudelt („huddled“) werden in engen Schlaf-\n stuben, mussten ihre Berichte die Ueberzeugung hervorrufen, dass unter\n den beschriebenen Umständen Scham- und Anstandsgefühl aufs gröbste\n verletzt und alle Moralität fast nothwendig ruinirt wird [Note: „Jung verheirathete Paare sind kein erbauliches Studium für erwachsne\n Brüder und Schwestern in derselben Schlafstube; und obgleich Beispiele nicht] .\n [Running matter: 43*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"676\", \"facs\": \"#f0695\"} --> … Z. B. im Appendix meines letzten Berichts erwähnt Dr. Ord, in sei-\n nem Bericht über den Fieberausbruch zu Wing in Buckinghamshire, wie\n ein junger Mann von Wingrave mit Fieber dorthin kam. In den ersten\n Tagen seiner Krankheit schlief er mit 9 andern Personen in einem Gemach\n zusammen. In zwei Wochen wurden verschiedne dieser Personen ange-\n griffen, im Verlauf weniger Wochen verfielen 5 von den 9 Personen dem\n Fieber und eine starb! Gleichzeitig berichtete mir Dr. Harvey vom St. Geor-\n ge’s Spital, der Wing während der Epidemiezeit in Angelegenheiten seiner\n Privatpraxis besuchte, in demselben Sinne: „Ein junges, fieberkrankes\n Frauenzimmer schlief Nachts in derselben Stube mit Vater, Mutter, ihrem\n Bastardkind, zwei jungen Männern, ihren Brüdern, und ihren zwei Schwe-\n stern, jede mit einem Bastard, in allem 10 Personen. Wenige Wochen\n vorher schliefen 13 Kinder in demselben Raume“ [Note: Public Health. Seventh Report. Lond. 1865, p. 9—14\npassim.] .\n\n\n\n\nDr. Hunter untersuchte 5375 Landarbeiter-Cottages, nicht nur\n in den reinen Agrikulturdistrikten, sondern in allen Grafschaften Englands.\n Unter diesen 5375 hatten 2195 nur eine Schlafstube (oft zugleich Wohn-\n stube), 2930 nur 2 und 250 mehr als 2. Ich will für ein Dutzend Graf-\n schaften eine kurze Blüthenlese geben.\n\n\n\n\n1) Bedfordshire.\n\n\n\n\nWrestlingworth: Schlafzimmer ungefähr 12 Fuss lang und\n 10 breit, obgleich viele kleiner sind. Die kleine einstöckige Hütte wird\n oft durch Bretter in zwei Schlafstuben getheilt, oft ein Bett in einer Küche\n 5 Fuss 6 Zoll hoch. Miethe 3 Pfd. St. Die Miether haben ihre eignen\n Abtritte zu bauen, der Hauseigenthümer liefert nur ein Loch. So oft einer\n [Note: registrirt werden dürfen, liegen hinreichende Data vor, um die Bemerkung zu\n rechtfertigen, dass grosses Leid und oft der Tod das Loos der weiblichen Theil-\n nehmer am Verbrechen der Blutschande ist.“ (Dr. Hunter l. c. p. 137.) Ein\n ländlicher Polizeibeamter, der viele Jahre durch als Detectiv in den schlechtesten\n Vierteln von London funktionirte, sagt von den Mädchen seines Dorfs aus: „Ihre\n grobe Immoralität im frühen Alter, ihre Frechheit und Schamlosigkeit habe ich\n niemals während meines Polizeilebens in den schlechtesten Theilen von London\n erreicht gesehn … Sie leben wie Schweine, grosse Jungen und Mädchen, Mütter\n und Väter, alles schläft zusammen in derselben Stube.“ („Child. Empl.\n Comm. Sixth Report. Lond. 1867“, Appendix, p. 77, n. 155.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"677\", \"facs\": \"#f0696\"} --> einen Abtritt baut, wird letzterer von der ganzen Nachbarschaft benutzt. Ein\n Haus Namens Richardson von unerreichbarer Schöne. Seine Mörtelwände\n bauschten aus wie ein Damenkleid beim Knix. Ein Giebelende war kon-\n vex, das andre konkav, und auf dem letztern stand unglücklicher Weise\n der Schornstein, ein krummes Rohr von Lehm und Holz gleich einem\n Elephantenrüssel. Ein langer Stock diente als Stütze, um den Fall des\n Schornsteins zu verhindern, Thüre und Fenster rautenförmig. Von 17 be-\n suchten Häusern nur 4 mit mehr als 1 Schlafzimmer und diese 4 über-\n stopft. Die einschläfrigen Cots bargen 3 Erwachsne mit 3 Kindern, ein\n verheirathetes Paar mit 6 Kindern u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nDunton: Hohe Haus-Renten, von 4 bis 5 Pfd. St., Wochenlohn der\n Männer 10 sh. Sie hoffen durch Strohflechten der Familie die Miethe\n herauszuschlagen. Je höher die Hausmiethe, desto grösser die Zahl, die\n sich zusammenthun muss, um sie zu zahlen. Sechs Erwachsne, die mit\n 4 Kindern in einer Schlafstube, zahlen dafür 3 Pfd. St. 10 sh. Das\n wohlfeilste Haus in Dunton, von der Aussenseite 15 Fuss lang, 10 breit,\n vermiethet für 3 Pfd. St. Nur eins von den 14 untersuchten Häusern\n hatte zwei Schlafstuben. Etwas vor dem Dorf ein Haus, von den Insas-\n sen bekothet vor seinen Aussenwänden, die untern 5 Zoll der Thüre ver-\n schwunden durch reinen Verfaulungsprozess, einige Ziegelsteine von innen\n sinnreich des Abends beim Zuschliessen vorgeschoben und mit etwas Matte\n verhangen. Ein halbes Fenster, sammt Glas und Rahmen, war ganz den\n Weg allen Fleisches gegangen. Hier, ohne Möbel, hudelten 3 Erwachsne\n und 5 Kinder zusammen. Dunton ist nicht schlimmer als der Rest der\n Biggleswude Union.\n\n\n\n\n2) Berkshire.\n\n\n\n\nBeenham: Juni 1864 lebte ein Mann, Frau, 4 Kinder in einem\n Cot (einstöckigen Cottage). Eine Tochter kam heim aus dem Dienst mit\n Scharlachfieber. Sie starb. Ein Kind erkrankte und starb. Die Mutter\n und ein Kind litten an Typhus, als Dr. Hunter gerufen wurde. Der\n Vater und ein Kind schliefen auswärts, aber die Schwierigkeit Isolirung\n zu sichern, zeigte sich hier, denn im vollgepfropften Markt des elenden\n Dorfs lag das Leinen des fiebergeschlagnen Hauses, auf Wäsche wartend.\n — Die Miethe von H.’s Haus 1 sh. wöchentlich; das eine Schlafzimmer\n für 1 Paar und 6 Kinder. Ein Haus vermiethet zu 8 d. (wöchentlich),\n 14 Fuss 6 Zoll lang, 7 Fuss breit, Küche 6 Fuss hoch; das Schlafzimmer\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"678\", \"facs\": \"#f0697\"} --> ohne Fenster, Feuerplatz, Thüre, noch Oeffnung, ausser nach dem Gang\n zu, kein Garten. Ein Mann lebte hier vor kurzem mit zwei erwachsnen\n Töchtern und einem aufwachsenden Sohn; Vater und Sohn schliefen auf\n dem Bett, die Mädchen auf dem Hausgang. Jede hatte ein Kind, so lange\n die Familie hier lebte, aber eine ging zum Workhouse für ihre Entbindung\n und kehrte dann heim.\n\n\n\n\n3) Buckinghamshire.\n\n\n\n\n30 Cottages — auf 1000 Acres Land — enthalten hier ungefähr\n 130—140 Personen. Die Pfarrei von Bradenham umfasst 1000 Acres;\n sie hatte 1851 36 Häuser und eine Bevölkerung von 84 Manns- und 54\n Weibspersonen. Diese geschlechtliche Ungleichheit geheilt 1861, wo sie\n 98 männlichen und 87 weiblichen Geschlechts zählte, Zuwachs in 10 Jah-\n ren von 14 Männern und 33 Weibern. Unterdess hatte die Häuserzahl\n um 1 abgenommen.\n\n\n\n\nWinslow: Grosser Theil davon neu gebaut in gutem Styl; Nach-\n frage nach Häusern scheint bedeutend, weil sehr armselige Cots vermie-\n thet zu 1 sh. und 1 sh. 3 d. per Woche.\n\n\n\n\nWater Eaton: Hier haben die Eigenthümer im Angesicht wach-\n sender Bevölkerung ungefähr 20 % der existirenden Häuser zerstört.\n Ein armer Arbeiter, der ungefähr 4 Meilen zu seinem Werk zu gehn hatte,\n antwortete auf die Frage, ob er kein Cot näher finden könnte: „Nein, sie\n werden sich verdammt hüten, einen Mann mit meiner grossen Familie auf-\n zunehmen.“\n\n\n\n\nTinker’s End, bei Winslow: Eine Schlafstube, worin 4 Er-\n wachsne und 4 Kinder, 11 Fuss lang, 9 Fuss breit, 6 Fuss 5 Zoll hoch\n am höchsten Punkt; ein andres 11 Fuss 3 Zoll lang, 9 Fuss breit, 5 Fuss\n 10 Zoll hoch, beherbergte 6 Personen. Jede dieser Familien hatte weni-\n ger Raum als nöthig für einen Galeerensträfling. Kein Haus hatte mehr\n als ein Schlafzimmer, keins eine Hinterthür, Wasser sehr selten, Wochen-\n miethe von 1 sh. 4 d. zu 2 sh. In 16 untersuchten Häusern nur ein ein-\n ziger Mann, der 10 sh. wöchentlich verdiente. Das Luftreservoir, jeder\n Person in dem erwähnten Falle gegönnt, entspricht dem, das ihr zu gut\n käme, wenn des Nachts eingeschlossen in eine Schachtel von 4 Kubikfuss.\n Allerdings bieten die alten Hütten eine Masse naturwüchsiger Ventilation.\n\n\n\n\n4) Cambridgeshire.\n\n\n\n\nGamblingay gehört verschiednen Eigenthümern. Es enthält die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"679\", \"facs\": \"#f0698\"} --> lumpigsten Cots, die man irgendwo finden kann. Viel Strohflechterei. Eine\n tödtliche Mattheit, eine hoffnungslose Ergebung in Schmutz beherrscht Gam-\n blingay. Die Vernachlässigung in seinem Centrum wird zur Tortur an den Ex-\n tremitäten, Nord und Süd, wo die Häuser stückweis abfaulen. Die abwesenden\n Landlords lassen dem armen Nest frei zur Ader. Die Miethen sind sehr\n hoch, 8 bis 9 Personen gepackt in ein einschläfriges Zimmer, in zwei\n Fällen 6 Erwachsne mit je 1 und 2 Kindern in einer kleinen Schlafstube.\n\n\n\n\n5) Essex: In dieser Grafschaft gehn in vielen Pfarreien Abnahme von\n Personen und Cottages Hand in Hand. In nicht weniger als 22 Pfarreien\n hat jedoch die Häuserzerstörung den Bevölkerungsanwachs nicht aufgehalten,\n oder nicht die Expulsion bewirkt, welche unter dem Namen: „Wanderung\n nach den Städten“ überall vorgeht. In Fingringhoe, einer Pfarrei\n von 3443 Acres, standen 1851 145 Häuser, 1861 nur noch 110, aber\n das Volk wollte nicht fort und brachte es fertig selbst unter dieser Be-\n handlung zuzunehmen. Zu Ramsden Crays bewohnten 1851 252\n Personen 61 Häuser, aber 1861 waren 262 Personen in 49 Häuser ge-\n quetscht. In Basilden lebten 1851 auf 1827 Acres 157 Personen in\n 35 Häusern, am Ende des Decenniums 180 Personen in 27 Häusern. In\n den Pfarreien von Fingringhoe, South Fambridge, Widford, Basilden und\n Ramsden Crays lebten 1851 auf 8449 Acres 1392 Personen in 316\n Häusern, 1861 auf demselben Areal 1473 Personen in 249 Häusern.\n\n\n\n\n6) Herefordshire: Diese kleine Grafschaft hat mehr gelitten vom\n „Evictionsgeist“ als irgend eine andre in England. Zu Madby gehören\n die überstopften Cottages, meist mit 2 Schlafzimmern, grossentheils den\n Pächtern. Sie vermiethen selbe leicht zu 3 oder 4 Pfd. St. per Jahr und\n zahlen Wochenlohn von 9 sh.!\n\n\n\n\n7) Huntingdonshire.\n\n\n\n\nHartford hatte 1851 87 Häuser, kurz nachher 19 Cottages zer-\n stört in dieser kleinen Pfarrei von 1720 Acres; Einwohnerschaft\n 1831 : 452 Personen, 1852: 832 und 1861: 341. Vierzehn ein-\n schläfrige Cots untersucht. In einem 1 verheirathetes Paar, 3 erwachsne\n söhne, 1 erwachsnes Mädchen, 4 Kinder, zusammen 10; in einem andern\n 3 Erwachsne, 6 Kinder. Eine dieser Stuben, worin 8 Personen schlie-\n fen, mass 12 Fuss, 10 Zoll in der Länge, 12 Fuss, 2 Zoll breit, 6 Fuss,\n 9 Zoll hoch; Durchschnittsmass, ohne Abzug der Vorsprünge, ergab unge-\n fähr 130 Kubikfuss per Kopf. In den 14 Schlafstuben 34 Erwachsne\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"680\", \"facs\": \"#f0699\"} --> und 33 Kinder. Diese Cottages selten mit Gärtchen versehn, aber viele\n der Insassen konnten kleine Fetzen Land, 10 oder 12 sh. per rood (etwa\n 17 Fuss) pachten. Diese allotments sind entfernt von den abtrittlosen\n Häusern. Die Familie muss entweder zu ihrer Parcelle gehn, um ihre\n Exkremente abzulagern, oder, wie es, mit Respekt zu melden, hier ge-\n schieht, die Schublade eines Schranks damit füllen. Sobald er voll, wird\n er ausgezogen und dort entleert, wo sein Inhalt nöthig ist. In Japan\n geht der Cirkellauf der Lebensbedingungen reinlicher von statten.\n\n\n\n\n8) Lincolnshire.\n\n\n\n\nLangtofft: Ein Mann wohnt hier in Wright’s Haus mit seiner Frau,\n ihrer Mutter, und 5 Kindern; das Haus hat Vorderküche, Spülkammer,\n Schlafzimmer über der Vorderküche; Vorderküche und Schlafstube 12 Fuss\n 2 Zoll lang, 9 Fuss 5 Zoll breit, die ganze Grundfläche 21 Fuss 3 Zoll\n lang, 9 Fuss 5 Zoll breit. Die Schlafstube ist ein Dachraum, die Wände\n laufen zuckerhutig an der Decke zusammen, und ein Kappfenster öffnet\n sich in der Front. Warum lebte er hier? Garten? Ausserordentlich win-\n zig. Miethe? Hoch, 1 sh. 3 d. per Woche. Nah seiner Arbeit? Nein,\n 6 Meilen entfernt, so dass er täglich 12 Meilen hin und her vermarschirt.\n Er lebte da, weil es ein vermiethbares Cot war und weil er ein Cot für\n sich allein haben wollte, irgendwo, zu irgend einem Preis, in irgend einem\n Zustand. Folgendes ist die Statistik von 12 Häusern in Langtofft mit 12\n Schlafstuben, 38 Erwachsnen und 36 Kindern:\n12 Häuser in Langtofft.\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n9) Kent.\n\n\n\n\nKennington, höchst traurig überfüllt 1859, als die Diphtherie\n erschien und der Surgeon des Kirchspiels eine amtliche Untersuchung über\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"681\", \"facs\": \"#f0700\"} --> die Lage der ärmeren Volksklasse veranstaltete. Er fand, dass in dieser\n Ortschaft, wo viel Arbeit nöthig, verschiedne Cots zerstört und keine neuen\n erbaut worden waren. In einem Bezirk standen 4 Häuser, birdcages\n (Vogelkäfige) benamst; jedes hatte 4 Zimmer mit den folgenden Dimen-\n sionen in Fuss und Zoll:\n\n\n\n\n\n\nKüche 9.5 × 8.11 × 6.6\n\n\n\n\nSpülkammer 8.6 × 4.6 × 6.6\n\n\n\n\nSchlafzimmer 8.5 × 5.10 × 6.3\n\n\n\n\nSchlafzimmer 8.3 × 8.4 × 6.3.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n10) Northamptonshire.\n\n\n\n\nBrinworth, Pickford und Floore: In diesen Dörfern lun-\n gern im Winter 20—30 Mann aus Arbeitsmangel auf den Strassen herum.\n Die Pächter bestellen nicht immer hinreichend das Korn- und Wurzelland,\n und der Landlord hat es passend gefunden alle seine Pachten in 2 oder 3\n zusammenzuwerfen. Daher Mangel an Beschäftigung. Während von der\n einen Seite des Grabens das Feld nach Arbeit schreit, werfen die geprell-\n ten Arbeiter von der anderen Seite sehnsüchtige Blicke danach. Fieber-\n haft überarbeitet im Sommer und halbverhungert im Winter, ist es kein\n Wunder, wenn sie in ihrem eignen Patois sagen, dass „the parson and\n gentlefolks seem frit to death at them.“\n\n\n\n\nZu Floore Beispiele von Paaren mit 4, 5, 6 Kindern in einer Schlaf-\n stube kleinster Ausgabe, ditto 3 Erwachsne mit 5 Kindern, ditto ein Paar\n mit Grossvater und 6 scharlachkranken Kindern u. s. w.; in 2 Häusern\n mit 2 Schlafstuben 2 Familien von je 8 und 9 Erwachsnen.\n\n\n\n\n11) Wiltshire.\n\n\n\n\nStratton: 31 Häuser besucht, 8 mit nur einer Schlafstube; Pen-\n till in derselben Pfarrei. Ein Cot vermiethet zu 1 sh. 3 d. wöchentlich\n an 4 Erwachsne und 4 Kinder, hatte ausser guten Wänden nichts Gutes\n an sich, vom Estrich aus rauhgehaunen Steinen bis zum faulen Stroh-\n dach.\n\n\n\n\n12) Worcestershire. Hauszerstörung hier nicht ganz so arg;\n doch von 1851—1861 vermehrte sich das Personal per Haus von 4.2 zu\n 4.6 Individuen.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0001","text":"## c) Das allgemeine Gesetz der kapitalistischen Accu-\n mulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"20.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0002","text":"Wachsthum des Kapitals schliesst Wachsthum seines vari-\n ablen oder in Arbeitskraft umgesetzten Bestandtheils ein. Ein Theil des in\n Surpluskapital verwandelten Mehrwerths muss stets rückverwandelt werden\n in variables Kapital oder zuschüssigen Arbeitsfonds. Unterstellen wir, dass,\n nebst sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen, die technologische Zusammensetzung\n des Kapitals unverändert bleibt, d. h. eine bestimmte Masse von Produk-\n tionsmitteln oder constantem Kapital stets von gleich viel lebendiger Arbeit\n in Bewegung gesetzt wird, so wächst die Nachfrage nach Arbeit und der\n Subsistenzfonds der Arbeiter verhältnissmässig mit dem Kapital und um so\n rascher, je rascher das Kapital wächst.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"20.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0003","text":"Da der Mehrwerth ein jährliches\n Produkt des Kapitals ist, da er dem Originalkapital jährlich ein Increment\n zusetzt, da diess Increment selbst jährlich wächst mit dem jährlich zuneh-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"600\", \"facs\": \"#f0619\"} --> menden Umfang des bereits in Funktion begriffenen Kapitals, und da endlich,\n unter besonderem Sporn des Bereicherungstriebs, wie z. B. Oeffnung neuer\n Märkte, neuer Sphären der Kapitalanlage in Folge neu entwickelter gesell-\n schaftlicher Bedürfnisse u. s. w., die Stufenleiter der Accumulation\n plötzlich ausdehnbar ist durch bloss veränderte Theilung des Mehr-\n werths oder Mehrprodukts in Kapital und Revenue, so treten hier\n Knotenpunkte ein, wo das Wachsthum der Arbeitskraft oder der Arbeiter-\n anzahl überflügelt wird von den Accumulationsbedürfnissen des Kapitals, und\n daher der Preis der Arbeit steigt.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"20.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0004","text":"Klage hierüber ertönt in England wäh-\n rend der ganzen ersten Hälfte des achtzehnten Jahrhunderts. Die mehr\n oder minder günstigen Umstände, worunter sich die Lohnarbeiter repro-\n duciren und vermehren, ändern jedoch nichts an dem Verhältniss selbst.\n Wie die einfache Reproduktion fortwährend das Kapitalver-\n hältniss selbst reproducirt, Kapitalisten auf der einen Seite, Lohn-\n arbeiter auf der andern, so reproducirt die Reproduktion auf er-\n weiterter Stufenleiter oder die Accumulation das Kapi-\n talverhältniss auf erweiterter Stufenleiter, mehr Kapita-\n listen oder grössere Kapitalisten auf diesem Pol, mehr Lohnarbeiter auf\n jenem.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"20.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0005","text":"Man sah früher bereits: Die Reproduktion der Arbeitskraft, die\n sich dem Kapital unaufhörlich als Verwerthungsmittel einverleiben muss,\n nicht von ihm loskommen kann, und deren Hörigkeit zum Kapital nur ver-\n steckt wird durch den Wechsel der individuellen Kapitalisten, woran sie\n sich verkauft, bildet in der That ein Moment der Reproduktion des Kapi-\n tals selbst. Accumulation des Kapitals ist also Vermeh-\n rung des Proletariats [Note: Karl Marx l. c. „A égalité d’oppression des masses, plus un pays a de\n prolétaires et plus il est riche.“ (Colins: „L’Economie Politique.\n Source des Révolutions et des Utopies prétendues Socialistes.\n Paris 1857“, t. III, p. 331.)","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"20.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0006","text":"Unter „Proletarier“ ist ökonomisch nichts zu ver-\n stehn als der Lohnarbeiter, der „Kapital“ producirt und verwerthet und aufs\n Pflaster geworfen wird, sobald er für die Verwerthungsbedürfnisse des „Mon-\n sieur Kapital“, wie Dr. Pecqueur diese Person nennt, überflüssig ist.\n „Der kränkliche Proletarier des Urwalds“ ist ein schönes Roscher’-\n sches Phantom. Der Urwäldler ist Eigenthümer des Urwalds, und behandelt den\n Urwald, ganz so ungenirt wie der Orang Utang, als sein Eigenthum. Er ist also\n nicht Proletarier. Diess wäre nur der Fall, wenn der Urwald ihn, statt er den] .","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"20.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0007","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"601\", \"facs\": \"#f0620\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"20.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0008","text":"Die klassische Oekonomie begriff diesen Satz so wohl, dass A. Smith,\n Ricardo u. s. w., wie früher erwähnt, die Accumulation sogar fälschlich\n identificiren mit Konsum des ganzen kapitalisirten Theils des Mehrpro-\n dukts durch produktive Arbeiter, oder mit seiner Verwandlung in zuschüs-\n sige Lohnarbeiter. Schon 1696 sagt John Bellers: „Wenn Jemand\n 100,000 Acres hätte und eben so viele Pfunde Geld und eben so viel Vieh,\n was wäre der reiche Mann ohne den Arbeiter ausser selbst ein\n Arbeiter? Und wie die Arbeiter Leute reich machen, so desto\n mehr Arbeiter, desto mehr Reiche … Die Arbeit des Armen ist\n die Mine des Reichen“ [Note: „As the Labourers make men rich, so the more Labourers, there will be\n the more rich men … the Labour of the Poor being the Mines of the Rich.“\n (John Bellers l. c. p. 2.)] . So Bertrand de Mandeville im Anfang\n des 18.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"20.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0009","text":"Jahrhunderts: „Wo das Eigenthum hinreichend geschützt ist,\n wäre es leichter ohne Geld zu leben als ohne Arme, denn wer würde\n die Arbeit thun? … Wie die Arbeiter vor Aushungerung zu bewahren\n sind, so sollten sie nichts erhalten, was der Ersparung werth ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"20.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0010","text":"Wenn hier\n und da Einer aus der untersten Klasse durch ungewöhnlichen Fleiss und\n Bauchkneipen sich über die Lage erhebt, worin er aufgebracht war, so muss\n ihn keiner daran hindern: ja es ist unläugbar der weiseste Plan für jede\n Privatperson, für jede Privatfamilie in der Gesellschaft frugal zu sein, aber\n es ist das Interesse aller reichen Nationen, dass der grösste\n Theil der Armen nie unthätig sei und sie dennoch stets verausgaben, was\n sie einnehmen … Diejenigen, die ihr Leben durch ihre tägliche Arbeit\n gewinnen, haben nichts, was sie anstachelt dienstlich zu sein ausser ihren\n Bedürfnissen, welche es Klugheit ist zu lindern, aber Narrheit wäre zu\n kuriren. Das einzige Ding, das den arbeitenden Mann fleissig machen\n kann, ist ein mässiger Arbeitslohn.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"20.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0011","text":"Ein zu geringer macht ihn je nach\n seinem Temperament kleinmüthig oder verzweifelt, ein zu grosser insolent\n und faul … Aus dem bisher Entwickelten folgt, dass in einer freien\n Nation, wo Sklaven nicht erlaubt sind, der sicherste Reich-\n thum aus einer Menge arbeitsamer Armen besteht. Aus-\n [Note: Urwald exploitirte. Was seinen Gesundheitszustand betrifft, steht der wohl den\n Vergleich aus nicht nur mit dem des modernen Proletariers, sondern auch dem\n der syphilitischen und skrophulösen „Ehrbarkeit“. Doch versteht Herr Wil-\n helm Roscher unter Urwald wahrscheinlich die stammverwandte Lüneburger Heide.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"602\", \"facs\": \"#f0621\"} --> serdem dass sie die nie versagende Zufuhrquelle für Flotte und Armee, gäbe\n es ohne sie keinen Genuss und wäre das Produkt keines Landes verwerth-\n bar.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"20.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0012","text":"Um die Gesellschaft (die natürlich aus den Nichtarbeitern be-\n steht) glücklich und das Volk selbst in kümmerlichen Zuständen zu-\n frieden zu machen, ist es nöthig, dass die grosse Majorität\n sowohl unwissend als arm bleibt. Kenntniss erweitert und ver-\n vielfacht unsere Wünsche, und je weniger ein Mann wünscht, desto leich-\n ter können seine Nothwendigkeiten befriedigt werden“ [Note: B. de Mandeville: „The Fable of the Bees. 5th ed. Lond.\n 1728“, Remarks, p. 212, 213, 328.) „Mässiges Leben und beständige Arbeit\n sind für den Armen der Weg zum materiellen Glücke (worunter er möglichst\n langen Arbeitstag und möglichst wenig Lebensmittel versteht) und zum Reich-\n thum für den Staat (nämlich Grundeigenthümer, Kapitalisten und ihre poli-\n tischen Würdeträger und Agenten).“ („An Essay on Trade and Com-\n merce. Lond. 1770“, p. 54.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"20.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0013","text":"Was Mande-\n ville, ein ehrlicher Mann und heller Kopf, noch nicht begreift, ist, dass der\n Mechanismus des Accumulationsprozesses selbst mit dem Kapital die\n Masse der „arbeitsamen Armen“ vermehrt, d. h. der Lohnarbeiter,\n die ihre Arbeitskraft in wachsende Verwerthungskraft des wachsenden\n Kapitals verwandeln und eben dadurch ihr Abhängigkeitsverhält-\n niss von ihrem eignen, im Kapitalisten personificirten Produkt verewigen\n müssen. Mit Bezug auf diess Abhängigkeitsverhältniss bemerkt Sir\n F. M. Eden in seiner „Lage der Armen, oder Geschichte der arbeiten-\n den Klasse Englands“: „Unsere Zone erfordert Arbeit zur Befriedigung\n der Bedärfnisse, und desshalb muss wenigstens ein Theil der Ge-\n sellschaft unermüdet arbeiten … Einige, die nicht arbeiten,\n haben dennoch die Produkte des Fleisses zu ihrer Verfügung.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"20.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0014","text":"Das ver-\n danken diese Eigenthümer aber nur der Civilisation und Ordnung,\n sie sind reine Kreatur der Civilinstitutionen [Note: Eden hätte fragen sollen, wessen Kreatur sind denn „die Civilinstitu-\n tionen“? Vom Standpunkt der juristischen Illusion betrachtet er nicht das\n Gesetz als Produkt der materiellen Produktionsverhältnisse, sondern umgekehrt\n die Produktionsverhältnisse als Produkt des Gesetzes. Linguet warf Montes-\n quieu’s illusorischen „Esprit des Lois“ mit dem einen Wort über den\n Haufen: „L’esprit des lois, c’est la propriété.“] . Denn diese\n haben es anerkannt, dass man die Früchte der Arbeit auch an-\n ders als durch Arbeit sich aneignen kann.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"20.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0015","text":"Die Leute von\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"603\", \"facs\": \"#f0622\"} --> unabhängigem Vermögen verdanken ihr Vermögen fast ganz der Ar-\n beit Andrer, nicht ihrer eignen Fähigkeit, die durchaus nicht besser\n ist als die der Andern; es ist nicht der Besitz von Land und\n Geld, sondern das Kommando über Arbeit („the command of la-\n bour“), das die Reichen von den Armen unterscheidet … Was dem\n Armen zusagt, ist nicht eine verworfene oder servile Lage, sondern ein\nbequemes und liberales Abhängigkeitsverhältniss („a\n state of easy and liberal dependance“), und für die Leute von Eigenthum\n hinreichender Einfluss und Autorität über die, die für sie arbeiten …\n Ein solches Abhängigkeitsverhältniss ist, wie jeder Kenner der mensch-\n lichen Natur weiss, nothwendig für den Komfort der Arbeiter\n selbst“ [Note: Eden l. c. v. I, l. I, ch. I und Preface.] . Sir F. M.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"20.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0016","text":"Eden, beiläufig bemerkt, ist der einzige\n Schüler Adam Smith’s, der während des achtzehnten Jahrhunderts\n etwas Bedeutendes geleistet hat [Note: Sollte der Leser an Malthus erinnern, dessen „Essay on Popu-\n lation“ 1798 erschien, so erinnere ich, dass diese Schrift in ihrer ersten Form\n (und die späteren Ausgaben stopften nur Material in das alte Schema und fügten\nneues, aber nicht von Malthus entdecktes, sondern nur annexirtes zu) nichts\n als ein schülerhaft oberflächliches und pfäffisch verdeklamirtes Plagiat aus Sir\n James Steuart, Townsend, Franklin, Wallace u. s. w. ist und nicht einen\n einzigen selbstgedachten Satz enthält. Nebenbei bemerkt. Obgleich\n Malthus Pfaffe der englischen Hochkirche, hatte er das Mönchsgelübde des Cöli-\n bats abgelegt. Diess ist nämlich eine der Bedingungen der fellowship der prote-\n stantischen Universität zu Cambridge.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"20.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0017","text":"„Socios collegiorum maritos esse\n non permittimus, sed statim postquam quis uxorem duxerit,\n socius collegii desinatesse.“ („Reports of Cambridge Univer-\n sity Commission“, p. 172.) Dieser Umstand unterscheidet Malthus vortheil-\n haft von den andern protestantischen Pfaffen, die das katholische Gebot des\n Priestercölibats von sich selbst abgeschüttelt und das „Seid fruchtbar und mehret\n euch“ in solchem Mass als ihre specifisch biblische Mission vindicirt haben, dass\n sie überall in wahrhaft unanständigem Grad zur Vermehrung der Bevölkerung bei-\n tragen, während sie gleichzeitig den Arbeitern das „Populationsprincip“ predigen.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"20.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0018","text":"Es ist charakteristisch, dass der ökonomisch travestirte Sündenfall, der Adams-\n apfel, der „urgent appetite“, „the checks which tend to blunt the shafts of\n Cupid“, wie Pfaff Townsend munter sagt, dass dieser kitzliche Punkt von den\n Herrn von der protestantischen Theologie oder vielmehr Kirche monopoli-\n sirt ward und wird. Mit Ausnahme des venetianischen Mönches Ortes, eines\n originellen und geistreichen Schriftstellers, sind die meisten Populationslehrer] .","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"20.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0019","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"604\", \"facs\": \"#f0623\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"20.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0020","text":"Unter den bisher unterstellten, den Arbeitern günstigsten Accumula-\n tionsbedingungen kleidet sich ihr Abhängigkeitsverhältniss vom\n [Note: protestantische Pfaffen. So Bruckner: „Théorie du Système\n animal. Leyde 1767, worin die ganze moderne Bevölkerungstheorie erschöpft\n ist und wozu der vorübergehende Zank zwischen Quesnay und seinem Schüler,\nMirabeau père, über dasselbe Thema Ideen lieferte, dann Pfaffe Wallace,\n Pfaffe Townsend, Pfaffe Malthus und sein Schüler, der Erzpfaff Th. Chal-\n mers, von kleineren pfäffischen Skribenten in this line gar nicht zu reden.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"20.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0021","text":"Ur-\n sprünglich ward die politische Oekonomie betrieben von Philosophen, wie Hobbes,\n Locke, Hume, Geschäfts- und Staatsleuten, wie Thomas Morus, Temple, Sully,\n de Witt, North, Law, Vanderlint, Cantillon, Franklin, und theoretisch namentlich,\n und mit dem grössten Erfolg, von Medizinern wie Petty, Barbon, Mandeville,\n Quesnay. Noch Mitte des 18. Jahrhunderts entschuldigt sich Rev. Mr. Tucker,\n ein bedeutender Oekonom für seine Zeit, dass er sich mit dem Mammon beschäf-\n tigt. Später, und zwar mit dem „Bevölkerungsprincip“ schlug die Stunde der\n protestantischen Pfaffen.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"20.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0022","text":"Als ob er diese Geschäftsverpfuschung geahnt, sagt\nPetty, der die Population als Basis des Reichthums behandelt, und, gleich Adam\n Smith, abgesagter Pfaffenfeind: „Die Religion blüht am besten, wenn die\n Priester am meisten kasteit werden, wie das Gesetz am besten, wo die Advokaten\n verhungern.“ Er räth daher den protestantischen Pfaffen, wenn sie einmal dem\n Apostel Paulus nicht folgen und sich nicht durch das Cölibat „mortificiren“\n wollen, „doch ja nicht mehr Pfaffen zu hecken („not to breed more Churchmen“)\n als die vorhandenen Pfründen (benefices) absorbiren können; d. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"20.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0023","text":"wenn es nur\n 12,000 Pfründen in England und Wales giebt, ist es unweis 24,000 Pfaffen zu\n hecken („it will not be safe to breed 24,000 ministers“), denn die 12,000 Unver-\n sorgten werden stets einen Lebensunterhalt zu gewinnen suchen, und wie könnten\n sie das leichter thun, als indem sie unter das Volk gehn und es überreden, die\n 12,000 Pfründner vergifteten die Seelen, und hungerten selbige Seelen aus, und\n zeigten ihnen den Holzweg zum Himmel?“ (Petty: „A Treatise on Taxes\n and Contributions. Lond. 1667“, p. 57.) Adam Smith’s Stellung\n zum protestantischen Pfaffenthum seiner Zeit ist durch folgendes charakterisirt.\n In: „A Letter to A. Smith, L. L. D. On the Life, Death and Phi-\n losophy of his Friend David Hume. By One of the People ealled\n Christians. 4th ed. Oxford 1784“, kanzelt Dr. Horne, hochkirchlicher\n Bischof von Norwich, den A.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"20.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0024","text":"Smith ab, weil er in einem öffentlichen Sendschreiben\n an Herrn Strahan, seinen „Freund David (sc. Hume) einbalsamire“, weil er dem\n Publikum erzählte, wie „Hume auf seinem Sterbebett sich mit Lucian und Whist\n amüsirte“, und sogar die Frechheit hatte zu schreiben: „Ich habe Hume stets, so-\n wohl während seines Lebens wie nach seinem Tode so nahe dem Ideal eines voll-\n kommen weisen und tugendhaften Mannes betrachtet, als die Schwäche der mensch-\n lichen Natur erlaubt.“ Der Bischof ruft entrüstet: „Ist es recht von Ihnen,] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"605\", \"facs\": \"#f0624\"} --> Kapital in erträgliche oder, wie Eden sagt, „bequeme und liberale For-\n men“. Statt intensiver zu werden mit dem Wachsthum des Kapitals, wird\n es nur extensiver, d. h. die Exploitations- und Herrschaftssphäre des Kapi-\n tals dehnt sich nur aus mit seiner eignen Dimension und der Anzahl seiner\n Lohnarbeiter.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"20.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0025","text":"Von ihrem eignen anschwellenden und schwellend in\n Surpluskapital verwandelten Mehrprodukt strömt ihnen ein reichlicherer Theil\n in der Form von Zahlungsmitteln zurück, so dass sie den Kreis ihrer Genüsse\n erweitern, ihren Konsumtionsfonds von Kleidern, Möbeln u. s. w. besser aus-\n statten und kleine Reservefonds von Geld bilden können. So wenig aber bessere\n Kleidung, Nahrung, Behandlung und ein grösseres Peculium das Abhängig-\n keitsverhältniss und die Exploitation des Sklaven aufheben, so wenig die\n des Lohnarbeiters. Steigender Preis der Arbeit in Folge der Accumula-\n tion des Kapitals besagt in der That nur, dass der Umfang und die Wucht\n der goldnen Kette, die der Lohnarbeiter sich selbst bereits geschmiedet\n hat, ihre losere Spannung erlauben.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"20.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0026","text":"In den Kontroversen über diesen\n Gegenstand hat man meist die Hauptsache übersehn, nämlich die differentia\n [Note: mein Herr, uns als vollkommen weis und tugendhaft den Charakter und Lebens-\n wandel eines Menschen zu schildern, der von einer unheilbaren Antipathie besessen\n war wider alles, was Religion heisst, und der jeden Nerv anspannte, um so viel\n an ihm selbst ihren Namen aus dem Gedachtniss der Menschen zu löschen?“\n (l. c. p. 8.) „Aber lasst euch nicht entmuthigen. Liebhaber der Wahrheit, der\n Atheismus ist kurzlebig.“ (p. 17.) Adam Smith „hat die grässliche Ruchlosig-\n keit („the atrocious wickedness“) den Atheismus durch das Land zu propagan-\n diren (nämlich durch seine „Theory of moral sentiments“) … Wir kennen\n Eure Schliche, Herr Doktor! Ihr meint’s gut, rechnet aber diessmal ohne den\n Wirth.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"20.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0027","text":"Ihr wollt uns durch das Beispiel von David Hume, Esq., weismachen,\n dass Atheismus der einzige Schnaps („cordial“) für ein niedergeschlagnes Gemüth\n und das einzige Gegengift wider Todesfurcht ist … Lacht nur über Babylon\n in Ruinen und beglückwünscht nur den verhärteten Bösewicht Pharao!“ (l. c.\n p. 21, 22.) Ein orthodoxer Kopf unter A. Smith’s Kollegienbesuchern schreibt\n nach dessen Tod: „Smith’s Freundschaft für Hume verhinderte ihn ein Christ zu\n sein … Er glaubte Hume alles aufs Wort. Wenn Hume ihm gesagt, der Mond\n sei ein grüner Käs, er hätt’s geglaubt. Er glaubte ihm daher auch, dass es keinen\n Gott und keine Mirakel gebe … In seinen politischen Principien streifte er an\n Republikanismus.“ („The Bee.“ By James Anderson. 18 vls. Edinb.\n 1791—93.) Pfaff Th. Chalmers hat A.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"20.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0028","text":"Smith in Verdacht, dass er aus reiner\n Malice die Kategorie der „unproduktiven Arbeiter“ eigens für die protestantischen\n Pfaffen erfand, trotz ihrer gesegneten Arbeit im Weinberg des Herrn.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"606\", \"facs\": \"#f0625\"} --> specifica der kapitalistischen Produktion. Arbeitskraft wird hier gekauft,\n nicht um durch ihren Dienst oder ihr Produkt die persönlichen Bedürf-\n nisse des Käufers zu befriedigen. Sein Zweck ist Verwerthung\n seines Kapitals, Produktion von Waaren, die mehr Arbeit enthalten als er\n zahlt, also einen Werththeil enthalten, der ihm nichts kostet und den-\n noch durch den Waarenverkauf realisirt wird. Produktion von\n Mehrwerth oder Plusmacherei ist das absolute Gesetz dieser Produktionsweise.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"20.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0029","text":"Nur soweit sie die Produktionsmittel als Kapital erhält, ihren eignen Werth\n als Kapital reproducirt und in unbezahlter Arbeit eine Quelle von Surplus-\n kapital liefert, ist die Arbeitskraft verkaufbar. Die Bedingungen ihres Ver-\n kaufs, ob mehr oder minder günstig für den Arbeiter, schliessen also die\n Nothwendigkeit ihres steten Wiederverkaufs und die stets erweiterte Repro-\n duktion des Reichthums als Kapital ein. Der Arbeitslohn, wie man\n gesehn, bedingt seiner Natur nach, stets Lieferung eines bestimmten Quan-\n tums unbezahlter Arbeit auf Seiten des Arbeiters. Ganz abgesehn vom Stei-\n gen des Arbeitslohns mit sinkendem Preis der Arbeit u. s. w.,\n besagt seine Zunahme im besten Fall nur quantitative Abnahme der\n unbezahlten Arbeit, die der Arbeiter leisten muss.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"20.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0030","text":"Diese Abnahme kann\n nie bis zu einem Punkt fortgehn, wo sie den kapitalistischen Charakter des\n Produktionsprozesses ernsthaft gefährden würde und die Reproduktion\n seiner eignen Bedingungen, auf der einen Seite der Produktions- und\n Lebensmittel als Kapital, auf der andern der Arbeitskraft als Waare, auf\n dem einen Pol des Kapitalisten, auf dem andern des Lohnarbeiters. Abge-\n sehn von gewaltsamen Konflikten über die Rate des Arbeitslohns, und\nAdam Smith hat bereits gezeigt, dass im Grossen und Ganzen in solchem\n Konflikt der Meister stets Meister bleibt, unterstellt ein aus Accumulation\n des Kapitals entspringendes Steigen des Arbeitspreises folgende Alter-\n native. Entweder der steigende oder gestiegne Preis der Arbeit ist be-\n gleitet von gleich grossem oder grösserem absoluten Wachsthum der Accu-\n mulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"20.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0031","text":"Man weiss, dass selbst unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umstän-\n den, wie Produktivgrad der Arbeit u. s. w., mit der wachsenden Grösse\n des vorgeschossenen Kapitals sein absoluter Zuwachs\n gleichförmig bleiben oder selbst beschleunigt werden kann, obgleich die Rate\n der Accumulation abnimmt, wie man Kapitel III, Abschnitt 5 sah,\n dass die Masse des Mehrwerths trotz dessen abnehmender Rate mit\n Vermehrung der gleichzeitig exploitirten Arbeiterzahl gleichbleiben und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"607\", \"facs\": \"#f0626\"} --> selbst wachsen kann. In diesem Fall ist es blosse Tautologie zu sagen,\n dass der verminderte Exploitationsgrad der Arbeitskraft die Ausdehnung\n der Kapitalherrschaft nicht beeinträchtigt. Oder, das ist die andre\n Seite der Alternative, die Accumulation erschlafft in Folge des steigenden\n Arbeitspreises, weil der Stachel grossen Gewinns abstumpft. Die\n Accumulation nimmt ab.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"20.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0032","text":"Aber mit ihrer Abnahme verschwindet die Ur-\n sache ihrer Abnahme, nämlich die Disproportion zwischen Kapital und\n exploitabler Arbeitskraft. Der Arbeitspreis sinkt also wieder zu einem\nden Verwerthungsbedürfnissen des Kapitals entsprechen-\n den Niveau. Es folgt daher keineswegs, dass der Arbeitslohn auf\n sein Minimalniveau fällt, oder auch nur auf das Niveau, worauf er vor\n der Preiserhöhung der Arbeit stand. Der Mechanismus des kapitalisti-\n schen Produktionsprozesses beseitigt selbst die Hindernisse, die er vorüber-\n gehend schafft. Man sieht: Im ersten Fall ist es keine Abnahme im\n absoluten oder proportionellen Wachsthum der Arbeitskraft oder Arbeits-\n bevölkerung, welche das Kapital überschüssig, sondern umgekehrt die\n Zunahme des Kapitals, welche die exploitable Arbeitskraft unzureichend\n macht.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"20.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0033","text":"Im zweiten Fall ist es keine Zunahme im absoluten oder propor-\n tionellen Wachsthum der Arbeitskraft oder der Arbeiterbevölkerung, welche\n das Kapital unzureichend, sondern umgekehrt die Abnahme des Kapitals,\n welche die exploitable Arbeitskraft, oder vielmehr ihren Preis, überschüssig\n macht. Es sind diese absoluten Bewegungen in der Accumula-\n tion des Kapitals, welche sich als relative Bewegungen in der\n Masse der exploitablen Arbeitskraft wiederspiegeln und daher der\n eignen Bewegung der letztern geschuldet scheinen. So drückt\n sich in der Krisenphase des industriellen Cyklus der allgemeine Fall der Waa-\n renpreise als Steigen des relativen Geldwerths, und in der Pros-\n peritätsphase das allgemeine Steigen der Waarenpreise als Fall des rela-\n tiven Geldwerths aus. Die s. g. Currency-Schule schliesst\n daher, dass das einemal zu wenig, das andremal zu viel Geld cirkulirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"20.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0034","text":"Ihre Ignoranz und völlige Verkennung der Thatsachen [Note: Vgl. Karl Marx: „Zur Kritik der politischen Oekonomie“,\n p. 165 sqq.]  finden würdige\n Parallele in den Oekonomen, welche jene Phänomene der Accumulation\n dahin deuten, dass das einemal zu wenig und das andremal zu viel Lohn-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"608\", \"facs\": \"#f0627\"} --> arbeiter existiren. Das so in ein Naturgesetz mystificirte Gesetz der kapi-\n talistischen Accumulation drückt in der That nur aus, dass ihre Natur\n jede solche Abnahme im Exploitationsgrad der Arbeit oder jede solche\n Steigerung des Arbeitspreises ausschliesst, welche die stetige Reproduktion\n des Kapitalverhältnisses und seine Reproduktion auf stets erweiterter\n Stufenleiter ernsthaft gefährden könnte.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"20.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0035","text":"Es kann nicht anders sein in\n einer Produktionsweise, worin der Arbeiter für die Verwerthungsbe-\n dürfnisse vorhandner Werthe, statt umgekehrt der gegenständliche Reich-\n thum für die Entwicklungsbedürfnisse des Arbeiters da ist. Wie der\n Mensch in der Religion vom Machwerk seines eignen Kopfes, so wird er in\n der kapitalistischen Produktion vom Machwerk seiner eignen Hand be-\n herrscht.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"20.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0036","text":"Das bisher Entwickelte gilt unter der Voraussetzung, dass im Fort-\n gang der Accumulation das Verhältniss zwischen der Masse\n der Produktionsmittel und der Masse der sie bewegen-\n den Arbeitskraft gleichbleibt, also die Nachfrage nach\n Arbeit mit dem Wachsthum des Kapitals verhältnissmässig\n wächst. Diese Voraussetzung figurirt in Adam Smith’s Analyse der\n Accumulation als selbstverständliches Axiom. In gewissem Grad bleibt sie\n immer richtig, denn wie auch die technologischen Bedingungen des Pro-\n duktionsprozesses umgewälzt werden mögen, während kürzerer oder\n längerer Zeitfrist findet bald in dieser, bald in jener Produktions-\n sphäre Accumulation des Kapitals oder Erweiterung der Produktionsleiter\nauf der einmal gegebnen technologischen Basis statt. Inner-\n halb dieser Schranken wächst also die Nachfrage nach Arbeit mit der\n Accumulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"20.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0037","text":"Aber die vorhandne Basis wird selbst fortwährend umge-\n wälzt. Im Fortgang der Accumulation geht eine grosse Revolution vor\n im Verhältniss von Masse der Produktionsmittel und Masse der sie\n bewegenden Arbeitskraft. Diese Revolution spiegelt sich wieder in der\n wechselnden Zusammensetzung des Kapitalwerths aus\n constantem und variablem Bestandtheil, oder im wechseln-\n den Verhältniss seiner in Produktionsmittel und Arbeitskraft umgesetzten\nWerththeile. Ich nenne diese Zusammensetzung die organische\n Zusammensetzung des Kapitals.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"20.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0038","text":"Abgesehn vom Produktivgrad der Arbeit, soweit er ausschliesslich\n durch den Naturreichthum, wie Fruchtbarkeit des Bodens u. s. w., bedingt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"609\", \"facs\": \"#f0628\"} --> ist, oder durch das Geschick unabhängiger und isolirt arbeitender Produ-\n centen, das sich jedoch mehr qualitativ in der Güte des Machwerks als\n quantitativ in seiner Masse bewährt, drückt sich der gesellschaft-\n liche Produktivgrad der Arbeit aus im relativen Grössen-\n umfang der Produktionsmittel, welche ein Arbeiter, während\n gegebner Zeit, mit derselben Anspannung von Arbeitskraft, in Produkt\n verwandelt. Die Masse der Produktionsmittel, womit er funktionirt,\n wächst mit der Produktivität seiner Arbeit. Diese Produktionsmittel spie-\n len dabei eine doppelte Rolle. Das Wachsthum der einen ist Folge,\n das der andern Bedingung der wachsenden Produktivität der Arbeit.\n Z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"20.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0039","text":"mit der manufakturmässigen Theilung der Arbeit und der Anwen-\n dung von Maschinerie wird in derselben Zeit mehr Rohmaterial verarbeitet,\n tritt also grössere Masse von Rohmaterial und Hilfsstoffen in den Arbeits-\n prozess ein. Das ist Folge der wachsenden Produktivität der Arbeit.\n Andrerseits ist die Masse der angewandten Maschinerie, Arbeitsviehs, mi-\n neralischen Düngers, Drainirungsröhren u. s. w. Bedingung der wach-\n senden Produktivität der Arbeit. Ebenso die Masse der in Baulichkeiten,\n Riesenöfen, Transportmitteln u. s. w. koncentrirten Produktionsmittel, welche\n trotz erweitertem Umfang in Folge ihres gemeinsamen Verbrauchs weniger\n Werth an jeden aliquoten Theil des Gesammtprodukts abgeben oder ökonomi-\n schere Verwendung erláuben als die in Diminutivformat zersplitterten Arbeits-\n mittel derselben Art.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"20.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0040","text":"Ob aber Bedingung oder Folge, der wachsende\n Grössenumfang der Produktionsmittel im Vergleich zu der ihnen einverleib-\n ten Arbeitskraft drückt die wachsende Produktivität der Arbeit aus.\n Die Zunahme der letzteren erscheint also in der Abnahme der Arbeits-\n masse verhältnissmässig zu der von ihr bewegten Masse von\n Produktionsmitteln, oder in der Grössenabnahme des subjektiven Fak-\n tors des Arbeitsprozesses verglichen mit seinen objektiven Faktoren.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"20.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0041","text":"Das Wachsthum in der Masse der Produktionsmittel, ver-\n glichen mit der Masse der sie belebenden Arbeitskraft, spiegelt\n sich wieder in der Zunahme des constanten Bestandtheils\n des Kapital werths auf Kosten seines variablen Bestand-\n theils. Es werden z. B. von einem Kapital, prozentweis berechnet ursprüng-\n lich je 50 Pfd. St. in Produktionsmitteln und je 50 Pfd. St. in Arbeitskraft,\n später, mit der Entwicklung des Produktivgrads der Arbeit, je 80 Pfd. St. in\n Produktionsmitteln und je 20 Pfd. St. in Arbeitskraft ausgelegt u. s. w. Wir\n [Running matter: I. 39] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"610\", \"facs\": \"#f0629\"} --> sagen „spiegelt sich wieder“, weil die Abnahme des variablen Kapitaltheils\n gegenüber dem constanten, oder die veränderte Zusammensetzung\n des Kapitalwerths, nur annähernd den Wechsel in der Zusam-\n mensetzung seiner stofflichen Bestandtheile anzeigt. Wenn z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"20.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0042","text":"heute der in der Spinnerei angelegte Kapitalwerth zu ⅞ constant und ⅛ vari-\n abel ist, während er Anfang des 18. Jahrhunderts ½ constant und ½ variabel\n war, so ist dagegen die Masse von Rohstoff, Arbeitsmitteln u. s. w., die ein\n bestimmtes Quantum Spinnarbeit heute produktiv konsumirt, viel hundertmal\n grösser als im Anfang des 18. Jahrhunderts. Der Grund ist einfach der, dass\n mit der wachsenden Produktivität der Arbeit nicht nur der Umfang der von\n ihr vernutzten Produktionsmittel steigt, sondern deren Werth, verglichen\n mit ihrem Umfang, fällt. Ihr Werth steigt also absolut, aber nicht propor-\n tionell mit ihrem Umfang. Das Wachsthum der Differenz zwischen\n constantem und variablem Kapitaltheil ist daher viel kleiner als\n das der Differenz zwischen der Masse der Produktionsmittel, worin das con-\n stante, und der Masse Arbeitskraft, worin das variable Kapital umgesetzt wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"20.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0043","text":"Die erstere Differenz nimmt zu mit der letzteren, aber in viel geringerem\n Grad. Es giebt andre Umstände, welche das Verhältniss zwischen\n Werth der Produktionsmittel und Werth der sie bewegenden Arbeits-\n kraft von dem technologischen Verhältniss ihrer Massen abweichen\n machen. Diese modificirenden Umstände können wir erst im Dritten Buch\n betrachten. Die Rücksichtnahme darauf ist hier aber auch überflüssig,\n da die Abnahme des variablen Kapitaltheils gegen den constanten im\n Grossen und Ganzen, wenn auch nur andeutungsweise, die Zunahme der\n von derselben Masse Arbeitskraft in Bewegung gesetzten oder produktiv\n konsumirten Masse von Produktionsmitteln ausdrückt.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"20.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0044","text":"Im vierten Kapitel wurde gezeigt, wie die Entwicklung der gesell-\n schaftlichen Produktivkraft der Arbeit Cooperation auf grosser Stufenleiter\n voraussetzt, wie nur unter dieser Voraussetzung Theilung und Kombina-\n tion der Arbeit organisirt, Produktionsmittel durch massenhafte Koncen-\n tration ökonomisirt, schon stofflich nur gemeinsam anwendbare Arbeits-\n mittel, z. B. System der Maschinerie u. s. w., ins Leben gerufen, unge-\n heure Naturkräfte in den Dienst der Produktion gepresst und die Ver-\n wandlung des Produktionsprozesses in technologische Anwendung der Wis-\n senschaft vollzogen werden können.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"20.44"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0045","text":"Auf Grundlage der Waaren-\n produktion, wo die Produktionsmittel Eigenthum von Privatpersonen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"611\", \"facs\": \"#f0630\"} --> sind, wo der Handarbeiter daher entweder isolirt und selbstständig Waaren\n producirt oder seine Arbeitskraft als Waare verkauft, weil ihm die Mittel\n zum Selbstbetrieb fehlen, realisirt sich jene Voraussetzung nur durch das\nWachsthum der individuellen Kapitale, oder im Masse, wor-\n in die gesellschaftlichen Produktions- und Lebensmittel\n in das Privateigenthum individueller Kapitalisten ver-\n wandelt werden. Der Boden der Waarenproduktion kann die Pro-\n duktion auf grosser Stufenleiter nur in kapitalistischer Form tra-\n gen. Eine gewisse Accumulation von Kapital in den Händen in-\n dividueller Waarenproducenten bildet die Voraussetzung der speci-\n fisch kapitalistischen Produktionsweise.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"20.45"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0046","text":"Wir mussten sie\n daher unterstellen bei dem Uebergang aus dem Handwerk in den kapi-\n talistischen Betrieb. Sie mag die ursprüngliche Accumulation heis-\n sen, weil sie statt historisches Resultat historische Grundlage der speci-\n fisch kapitalistischen Produktion. Wie sie selbst entspringt, brauchen\n wir hier nicht zu untersuchen. Genug, sie bildet den Ausgangspunkt.\n Aber alle Methoden zur Steigerung der gesellschaftlichen Produktivkraft\n der Arbeit, die auf dieser Grundlage erwachsen, sind zugleich Methoden\n zur Produktion des Mehrwerths oder Mehrprodukts, welches seinerseits das\n Bildungselement der Accumulation. Sie sind also zugleich Methoden der Pro-\n duktion von Kapital durch Kapital oder Methoden seiner beschleunigten Ac-\n cumulation ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"20.46"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0047","text":"Die kontinuirliche Rückverwandlung von Mehrwerth in Kapital\n stellt sich dar als wachsende Grösse des in den Produktions-\n prozess eingehenden Kapitals. Diese wird ihrerseits Grundlage\neiner erweiterten Stufenleiter der Produktion, der sie be-\n gleitenden Methoden zur Steigerung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit, und\n beschleunigter Produktion von Mehrwerth. Wenn also ein gewisser Grad\n der Kapitalaccumulation als Bedingung der specifisch kapitalistischen Pro-\n duktionsweise erscheint, verursacht die letztere rückschlagend eine beschleu-\n nigte Accumulation des Kapitals. Mit der Accumulation des Ka-\n pitals entwickelt sich daher die specifisch kapitalisti-\n sche Produktionsweise und mit der specifisch kapitali-\n stischen Produktionsweise die Accumulation des Kapitals.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"20.47"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0048","text":"Jedes individuelle Kapital ist eine grössere oder kleinere Koncen-\n tration von Produktionsmitteln mit entsprechendem Kommando\n über eine grössere oder kleinere Arbeiterarmee. Jede Accumulation wird das\n [Running matter: 39*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"612\", \"facs\": \"#f0631\"} --> Mittel neuer Accumulation. Sie erweitert mit der vermehrten Masse\n des als Kapital funktionirenden Reichthums seine Koncentration in\n den Händen individueller Kapitalisten, daher die Grundlage der Pro-\n duktion auf grosser Stufenleiter und der specifisch kapitalistischen Pro-\n duktionsmethoden. Das Wachsthum des gesellschaftlichen Kapitals voll-\n zieht sich im Wachsthum vieler individuellen Kapitale.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"20.48"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0049","text":"Alle andern Um-\n stände als gleichbleibend vorausgesetzt, wächst jedes dieser individuellen\n Kapitale, und die in ihm gegebne Koncentration der Produktionsmittel,\n im Verhältniss worin es einen aliquoten Theil des gesellschaftlichen Ge-\n sammtkapitals bildet. Zugleich reissen sich Ableger von den Original-\n kapitalen los und funktioniren als neue selbstständige Kapitale. Eine\n grosse Rolle spielt dabei nothwendig die Theilung des Vermögens in Ka-\n pitalistenfamilien. Mit der Accumulation des Kapitals wächst daher auch\n mehr oder minder die Anzahl der Kapitalisten. Zwei Punkte charakteri-\n siren diese Art Koncentration, welche unmittelbar auf der Accumu-\n lation beruht oder vielmehr mit ihr identisch ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"20.49"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0050","text":"Erstens: Die wach-\n sende Koncentration der gesellschaftlichen Produktionsmittel in den Händen\n individueller Kapitalisten ist, unter sonst gleichbleibenden Umständen, be-\n schränkt durch den Wachsthumgrad des gesellschaftlichen\n Reichthums. Zweitens: Der in jeder besondern Produktions-\n sphäre ansässige Theil des gesellschaftlichen Kapitals ist ver-\n theilt unter viele Kapitalisten, welche einander als unabhängige und\n mit einander konkurrirende Waarenproducenten gegenüberstehn.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"20.50"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0051","text":"Die Ac-\n cumulation und die sie begleitende Koncentration sind also nicht nur auf viele\n Punkte zersplittert, sondern das Wachsthum der funktionirenden Kapitale ist\n durchkreuzt durch die Bildung neuer und die Spaltung alter Kapitale, Stellt\n sich die Accumulation daher einerseits dar als wachsende Koncentration\n der Produktionsmittel und des Kommando’s über Arbeit, so andrerseits als\nRepulsion vieler individueller Kapitale von einander.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"20.51"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0052","text":"Dieser Zersplitterung des gesellschaftlichen Gesammtkapitals in viele\n individuelle Kapitale oder der Repulsion seiner Bruchtheile von einander\n wirkt entgegen ihre Attraktion. Es ist diess nicht mehr einfache,\n mit der Accumulation identische Koncentration von Produktionsmitteln und\n Kommando über Arbeit. Es ist Koncentration bereits gebil-\n deter Kapitale, Aufhebung ihrer individuellen Selbstständigkeit, Ex-\n propriation von Kapitalist durch Kapitalist, Verwandlung vieler kleinerer\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"613\", \"facs\": \"#f0632\"} --> in weniger grössere Kapitale. Dieser Prozess unterscheidet sich von dem\n ersten dadurch, dass er nur veränderte Vertheilung der bereits\n vorhandnen und funktionirenden Kapitale voraussetzt,\n sein Spielraum also durch dasabsolute Wachsthum des gesell-\n schaftlichen Reichthums oder die absoluten Grenzen der\n Accumulation nicht beschränktist.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"20.52"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0053","text":"Das Kapital schwillt hier in\n einer Hand zu grossen Massen, weil es dort in vielen Händen verloren geht. Es\n ist die eigentliche Koncentration im Unterschied zur Accumulation.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"20.53"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0054","text":"Die Gesetze dieser Koncentration der Kapitale oder der Attraktion\n von Kapital durch Kapital können hier nicht entwickelt werden. Kurze\n thatsächliche Andeutung genügt. Der Konkurrenzkampf wird durch Ver-\n wohlfeilerung der Waaren geführt. Die Wohlfeilheit der Waaren hängt,\n caeteris paribus, von der Produktivität der Arbeit, diese aber von der\n Stufenleiter der Produktion ab. Die grösseren Kapitale schlagen daher\n die kleineren. Man erinnert sich ferner, dass mit der Entwicklung der\n kapitalistischen Produktionsweise der Minimalumfang des indivi-\n duellen Kapitals wächst, der erheischt ist, um ein Geschäft unter sei-\n nen normalen Bedingungen zu betreiben. Die kleineren Kapitale drängen\n sich daher in Produktionssphären, deren sich die grosse Industrie nur noch\n sporadisch oder unvollkommen bemächtigt hat.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"20.54"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0055","text":"Die Konkurrenz rast\n hier im direkten Verhältniss zur Anzahl und im umgekehrten Verhältniss\n zur Grösse der rivalisirenden Kapitale. Sie endet stets mit Untergang\n vieler kleiner Kapitalisten und Uebergang ihrer Kapitale in die Hand des\n Siegers. Abgesehn hiervon bildet sich mit der kapitalistischen Produktion\n eine ganz neue Macht, das Kreditwesen. Es wird nicht nur selbst\n zu einer neuen gewaltigen Waffe im Konkurrenzkampfe. Durch unsichtbare\n Fäden zieht es die über die Oberfläche der Gesellschaft in grösseren oder\n kleineren Massen zersplitterten Geldmittel in die Hände individueller Ka-\n pitalisten. Es ist die specifische Maschine zur Koncentration der Kapitale.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"20.55"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0056","text":"Die Koncentration der Kapitale, oder der Prozess ihrer Attraktion, wird\n intensiver im Verhältniss, worin sich mit der Accumulation die specifisch kapi-\n talistische Produktionsweise entwickelt. Ihrerseits wird die Koncentration\n einer der grossen Hebel jener Entwicklung. Sie verkürzt und beschleu-\n nigt die Verwandlung zersplitterter Produktionsprozesse in ge-\n sellschaftlich kombinirte und auf grosser Stufenleiter\n ausgeführte.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"20.56"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0057","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"614\", \"facs\": \"#f0633\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"20.57"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0058","text":"Der wachsende Umfang der individuellen Kapitalmassen wird zur mate-\n riellen Basis einer beständigen Umwälzung der Produktionsweise\n selbst. Fortwährend erobert die kapitalistische Produktionsweise ihr noch gar\n nicht, oder nur sporadisch, oder nur formell unterworfene Arbeitszweige. Da-\n neben erwachsen auf ihrem Boden neue, ihr von vorn herein angehörige\n Arbeitszweige. Endlich wird in den bereits kapitalistisch betriebenen Arbeits-\n zweigen die Produktivkraft der Arbeit treibhausmässig gereift. In allen\n diesen Fällen sinkt die Arbeiterzahl verhältnissmässig zur Masse der von ihr\n verarbeiteten Produktionsmittel. Ein stets grösserer Theil des Kapitals\n wird in Produktionsmittel umgesetzt, ein stets kleinerer in Arbeitskraft.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"20.58"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0059","text":"Mit\n dem Umfang, der Koncentration und der technischen Wirksamkeit der\nProduktionsmittel vermindert sich progressiv der Grad, worin sie\nBeschäftigungs mittel der Arbeiter sind. Ein Dampfpflug ist ein\n ungleich wirksameres Produktionsmittel als der gewöhnliche Pflug,\n aber der in ihm ausgelegte Kapitalwerth ist ein ungleich geringeres Be-\n schäftigungsmittel, als wenn er in gewöhnlichen Pflügen realisirt wäre.\n Zunächst ist es grade die Zufügung von neuem Kapital zum alten, welche\n die gegenständlichen Bedingungen des Produktionsprozesses auszuweiten\n und technologisch umzuwälzen erlaubt. Bald aber ergreift die veränderte\n Zusammensetzung und technologische Umgestaltung mehr oder minder\n alles alte Kapital, das seinen Reproduktionstermin er-\n reicht hat und daher neu ersetzt wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"20.59"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0060","text":"Diese Metamorphose\n des alten Kapitals ist vom absoluten Wachsthum des gesell-\n schaftlichen Kapitals zu gewissem Grad unabhängig, wie es die Koncentra-\n tion ist. Letztre aber, welche vorhandnes gesellschaftliches Kapital nur\n anders vertheilt und viele alte Kapitale in eins verschmilzt, wirkt wieder\n als mächtiges Agens in dieser Metamorphose des alten Kapitals.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"20.60"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0061","text":"Einerseits attrahirt also das im Fortgang der Accumulation gebildete\n Zuschusskapital, verhältnissmässig zu seiner Grösse, weniger und weniger\n Arbeiter. Andrerseits repellirt das in neuer Zusammensetzung reprodu-\n cirte alte Kapital mehr und mehr früher von ihm beschäftigte Arbeiter.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"20.61"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0062","text":"Die Accumulation des Kapitals, welche ursprünglich nur als seine\nquantitative Erweiterung erscheint, vollzieht sich also in fortwährendem\nqualitativen Wechsel seiner Zusammensetzung, in bestän-\n diger Zunahme seines constanten auf Kosten seines variablen Bestandtheils.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"20.62"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0063","text":"Die specifisch kapitalistische Produktionsweise, die ihr entsprechende\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"615\", \"facs\": \"#f0634\"} --> Entwicklung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit, der dadurch verursachte Wechsel\n in der organischen Zusammensetzung des Kapitals halten nicht nur Schritt\n mit dem Fortschritt der Accumulation oder dem Wachsthum des gesell-\n schaftlichen Reichthums. Sie schreiten ungleich schneller, weil\n die einfache Accumulation oder die absolute Ausdehnung des Gesammt-\n kapitals von der Koncentration seiner individuellen Elemente, und die tech-\n nologische Umwälzung des Surpluskapitals von technologischer Umwälzung\n des Originalkapitals begleitet sind. Mit dem Fortgang der Accumulation\n wandelt sich also das Verhältniss von constantem zu variablem Kapital-\n theil, wenn ursprünglich 1 : 1, in 2 : 1, 3 : 1, 4 : 1, 5 : 1, 7 : 1 u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"20.63"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0064","text":"w., so\n dass, wie das Kapital wächst, statt ½ seines Gesammtwerths progressiv\n nur ⅓, ¼, ⅕, ⅙, ⅛ u. s. w. in Arbeitskraft, dagegen ⅔, ¾, ⅘, ⅚,\n ⅞ u. s. w. in Produktionsmittel umgesetzt wird. Da die Nachfrage\n nach Arbeit nicht durch den Umfang des Gesammtkapitals, sondern durch\n den seines variablen Bestandtheils bestimmt ist, fällt sie also progressiv\n mit dem Wachsthum des Gesammtkapitals, statt, wie vorhin unter-\n stellt, verhältnissmässig mit ihm zu wachsen. Sie fällt relativ zur Grösse\n des Gesammtkapitals und in beschleunigter Progression mit dem Wachsthum\n dieser Grösse. Mit dem Wachsthum des Gesammtkapitals wächst zwar auch\n sein variabler Bestandtheil, oder die ihm einverleibte Arbeitskraft, aber in\n beständig abnehmender Proportion. Die Zwischenpausen, worin\n die Accumulation als blosse Erweiterung der Produktion auf gegebner\n technologischer Grundlage wirkt, verkürzen sich.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"20.64"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0065","text":"Nicht nur wird eine in\n wachsender Progression beschleunigte Accumulation des Gesammt-\n kapitals erheischt, um eine additionelle Arbeiterzahl von gegebner Grösse zu\n absorbiren oder selbst, wegen der beständigen Metamorphose des alten Ka-\n pitals, die bereits funktionirende zu beschäftigen. Ihrerseits schlägt diese\n wachsende Accumulation und Koncentration selbst wieder um in\n eine Quelle neuer Wechsel der Zusammensetzung des Kapitals oder aber-\n malig beschleunigter Abnahme seines variablen gegen seinen constanten\n Bestandtheil. Diese mit dem Wachsthum des Gesammtkapitals be-\n schleunigte und rascher denn sein eignes Wachsthum beschleunigte relative\n Abnahme seines variablen Bestandtheils scheint auf der andern\n Seite umgekehrt stets rascheres absolutes Wachsthum der\n Arbeiterbevölkerung als das des variablen Kapitals oder\n ihrer Beschäftigungsmittel.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"20.65"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0066","text":"Die kapitalistische Accu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"616\", \"facs\": \"#f0635\"} --> mulation producirt vielmehr, und zwar im Verhältniss zu ihrer Energie\n und ihrem Umfang, beständig eine relative, d. h. für die mittleren\n Verwerthungsbedürfnisse des Kapitals überschüssige,\n daher überflüssige oder Surplus-Arbeiterbevölkerung.","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"20.66"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0067","text":"Das gesellschaftliche Gesammtkapital betrachtet, ruft die Be wegung\n seiner Accumulation bald periodischen Wechsel hervor, bald verthei-\n len sich ihre Momente gleichzeitig über die verschiednen Produktions-\n sphären.","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"20.67"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0068","text":"In einigen Sphären findet Wechsel in der Zusammensetzung des Ka-\n pitals statt ohne Wachsthum seiner absoluten Grösse, in Folge blosser Kon-\n centration; in andern ist das absolute Wachsthum des Kapitals mit abso-\n luter Abnahme seines variablen Bestandtheils oder der von ihm absor-\n birten Arbeitskraft verbunden; in andern wächst das Kapital bald auf sei-\n ner gegebnen technischen Grundlage fort, und attrahirt zuschüssige Arbeits-\n kraft im Verhältniss seines Wachsthums, bald tritt organischer Wechsel\n ein und kontrahirt sich sein variabler Bestandtheil; in allen Sphären ist\n das Wachsthum des variablen Kapitaltheils und daher der beschäftigten\n Arbeiterzahl stets verbunden mit heftigen Fluktuationen und vorüber-\n gehender Produktion von Surpluspopulation, ob diese nun die auffallendere\n Form von Repulsion bereits beschäftigter Arbeiter annimmt oder die mehr\n unscheinbare, aber nicht","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"20.68"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0069","text":"minder wirksame, erschwerter Absorption der zu-\n schüssigen Arbeiterbevölkerung in ihre gewohnten Abzugskanäle [Note: Der Census für England und Wales zeigt u. a.:\nAlle in der Agrikultur beschäftigte Personen (Eigenthümer,\n Pächter, Gärtner, Hirten u. s. w. eingeschlossen): 1851: 2,011,447, 1861:\n 1,924,110, Abnahme: 87,337. Worsted Manufaktur: 1851: 102,714\n Personen, 1861: 79,242; Seidenfabrik: 1851: 111,940, 1861: 101,678;\nKattundrucker: 1851: 12,098, 1861: 12,556, welche geringe Zunahme\n trotz des enorm ausgedehnten Geschäfts grosse proportionelle Abnahme\n in der Zahl der beschäftigten Arbeiter bedingt. Hutmacher: 1851: 15,957,\n 1861: 13,814; Strohhut- und Bonnetmacher: 1851: 20,393, 1861:\n 18,176; Malzer: 1851: 10,566, 1861: 10,677; Lichtgiesser: 1851:\n 4949, 1861: 4686. Diese Abnahme ist u. a. der Zunahme der Gasbeleuchtung ge-\n schuldet.","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"20.69"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0070","text":"Kammmacher: 1851: 2,038, 1861: 1,478; Holzsäger. 1851:\n 30,552, 1861: 31,647, geringe Zunahme in Folge des Aufschwungs von Sägemaschi-\n nen; Nagelmacher: 1851: 26,940, 1861: 26,130, Abnahme in Folge der Ma-\n schinenkonkurrenz; Arbeiter in Zinn- und Kupferbergwerken: 1851:\n 31,360, 1861: 32,041. Dagegen: Baumwollspinnereien und Webe-\n reien: 1851: 371,777, 1861: 456,646; Kohlenbergwerke: 1851:] .","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"20.70"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0071","text":"Mit\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"617\", \"facs\": \"#f0636\"} --> der Grösse des bereits funktionirenden Gesellschaftskapitals und dem Grad\n seines Wachsthums, mit der Ausdehnung der Produktionsleiter und der\n Masse der in Bewegung gesetzten Arbeiter, mit der Entwicklung der Produk-\n tivkraft ihrer Arbeit, mit dem breiteren und volleren Strom aller Springquel-\n len des Reichthums dehnt sich auch die Stufenleiter, worin\n grössere Attraktion der Arbeiter durch das Kapital mit grösserer Repul-\n sion derselben verbunden ist, nimmt die Raschheit der Wechsel in der\n organischen Zusammensetzung des Kapitals und seiner technischen Form\n zu, und schwillt der Umkreis der Produktionssphären, die bald gleichzeitig,\n bald abwechselnd davon ergriffen werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"20.71"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0072","text":"Mit der von ihnen producirten Accu-\n mulation des Kapitals producirt die Arbeiterbevölkerung also in wachsen-\n dem Umfang die Mittel ihrer eignen relativen Ueberzählig-\n machung [Note: „The demand for labour depends on the increase of circulating and not\n of fixed capital. Were it true that the proportion between these two sorts of\n capital is the same at all times, and in all circumstances, then, indeed, it follows\n that the number of labourers employed is in proportion to the wealth of the state.\n But such a proposition has not the semblance of probability. As arts are culti-\n vated, and civilization is extended, fixed capital bears a larger and larger propor-\n tion to circulating capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"20.72"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0073","text":"The amount of fixed capital employed in the produc-\n tion of a piece of British muslin is at least a hundred, probably a thousand times\n greater than that employed in a similar piece of Indian muslin. And the propor-\n tion of circulating capital is a hundred or thousand times less … the whole of\n the annual savings, added to the fixed capital, would have no effect in increasing\n the demand for labour.“ (John Barton: „Observations on the circum-\n stances which influence the Condition of the Labouring Classes\n of Society.“ Lond. 1817, p. 16, 17.) „The same cause which may increase\n the net revenue of the country may at the same time render the population\n redundant, and deteriorate the condition of the labourer.“ (Ri-\n cardo l. c. p. 469.) Mit der Zunahme des Kapitals „the demand (for labour)\n will be in a diminishing ratio.“ (l. c. p. 480, Note.)","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"20.73"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0074","text":"„The amount of capital\n devoted to the maintenance of labour may vary, independently of any changes in\n the whole amount of capital … Great fluctuations in the amount of em-\n ployment, and great suffering may become more frequent as capi-] . Es ist diess ein der kapitalistischen Produk-\n tionsweise eigenthümliches Populationsgesetz, wie in der That\n [Note: 183,389, 1861: 246,613. „Die Zunahme von Arbeitern ist im Allgemeinen am\n grössten seit 1851 in solchen Zweigen, worin die Maschinerie bisher noch nicht\n mit Erfolg angewandt worden.“ („Census of England and Wales for\n 1862“, vol. III. Lond. 1863, p. 36.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"618\", \"facs\": \"#f0637\"} --> jede besondre historische Produktionsweise ihre besondern, historisch gül-\n tigen Populationsgesetze hat. Ein abstraktes Populationsgesetz existirt nur\n für Pflanze und Thier.","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"20.74"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0075","text":"Wenn aber eine Surplusarbeiterpopulation nothwendiges Produkt der\n Accumulation oder der Entwicklung des Reichthums auf kapitalistischer\n Grundlage ist, wird diese Surpluspopulation umgekehrt zum Hebel der kapita-\n listischen Accumulation, ja zu einer Existenzbedingung der kapita-\n listischen Produktionsweise. Sie bildet eine disponible indu-\n strielle Reservearmee, die dem Kapital ganz so absolut gehört,\n als ob es sie auf seine eignen Kosten grossgezüchtet hätte. Sie schafft\n für seine wechselnden Verwerthungsbedürfnisse das stets bereite exploi-\n table Menschenmaterial, unabhängig von den Schranken der wirklichen\n Bevölkerungszunahme.","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"20.75"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0076","text":"Mit der Accumulation und der sie begleitenden\n Entwicklung der Produktivkraft der Arbeit wächst die plötzliche Expan-\n sionskraft des Kapitals, nicht nur, weil die Elasticität des funk-\n tionirenden Kapitals wächst, und der absolute Reichthum, wovon das\n Kapital nur einen elastischen Theil bildet, nicht nur, weil der Kredit, unter\n jedem besondern Reiz, im Umsehn ungewöhnlichen Theil dieses Reich-\n thums der Produktion als Surpluskapital zur Verfügung stellt. Der tech-\n nologische Charakter des Produktionsprozesses selbst, Maschinerie, Trans-\n portmittel u. s. w. ermöglichen die rascheste Verwandlung von Surplus-\n produkt auf grösster Stufenleiter in zuschüssige Produktionsmittel.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"20.76"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0077","text":"Die\n mit dem Fortschritt der Accumulation überschwellende und in Surplus-\n kapital verwandelbare Masse des gesellschaftlichen Reichthums drängt\n sich mit Frenesie in alte Produktionszweige, deren Markt sich plötzlich\n erweitert, oder in neu eröffnete, wie Eisenbahnen u. s. w., deren Bedürf-\n niss aus der Entwicklung der alten entspringt. In allen solchen Fällen\n müssen grosse Menschenmassen plötzlich und ohne Abbruch der Produk-\n tionsleiter in andern Sphären auf die entscheidenden Punkte werfbar sein.\n Die Surpluspopulation liefert sie. Der charakteristische Lebenslauf der\n [Note: talitself becomes more plentiful.“ (Richard Jones: „An Intro-\n ductory Lecture on Pol. Econ. Lond. 1833“, p. 13.)","kind":"passage","sequence":77,"address":"20.77"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0078","text":"„Demand (for la-\n bour) will rise … not in proportion to the accumulation of the gene-\n ral capital … Every augmentation, therefore, in the national stock destined for\n reproduction, comes, in the progress of society, to have less and less\n influence upon the condition of the labourer.“ (Ramsay l. c. p. 90, 91.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"619\", \"facs\": \"#f0638\"} --> modernen Industrie, die Form eines durch kleinere Schwankungen unter-\n brochnen zehnjährigen Cyklus von Perioden mittlerer Lebendigkeit, Pro-\n duktion unter Hochdruck, Krise und Stagnation, beruht auf der bestän-\n digen Bildung, grösseren oder geringeren Absorption, und Wiederbildung\n der industriellen Reservearmee oder Surpluspopulation. Ihrerseits rekru-\n tiren die Wechselfälle des industriellen Cyklus die Surpluspopulation und\n werden zu einem ihrer energischsten Reproduktionsagentien.","kind":"passage","sequence":78,"address":"20.78"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0079","text":"Dieser\n eigenthümliche Lebenslauf der modernen Industrie, der uns in keinem\n früheren Zeitalter der Menschheit begegnet, war auch in der Kindheits-\n periode der kapitalistischen Produktion unmöglich. Die Zusammensetzung\n des Kapitals veränderte sich nur sehr allmälig. Seiner Accumulation ent-\n sprach also im Ganzen verhältnissmässiges Wachsthum der Arbeitsnach-\n frage. Langsam wie der Fortschritt seiner Accumulation, verglichen mit\n der modernen Epoche, stiess er auf Naturschranken der exploitablen Arbei-\n terbevölkerung, welche nur durch später zu erwähnende Gewaltmittel weg-\n räumbar war.","kind":"passage","sequence":79,"address":"20.79"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0080","text":"Die plötzliche und ruckweise Expansion der Produktionsleiter\n ist die Voraussetzung ihrer plötzlichen Kontraktion; letztere ruft wieder\n die erstere hervor, aber die erstere ist unmöglich ohne disponibles Men-\n schenmaterial, ohne eine vom absoluten Wachsthum der Bevölkerung unab-\n hängige Vermehrung von Arbeitern. Sie wird geschaffen durch\n den einfachen Prozess, der einen Theil der Arbeiter beständig „freisetzt“,\n durch Methoden, welche die Anzahl der beschäftigten Arbeiter im Verhält-\n niss zur vermehrten Produktion vermindern. Die ganze Bewegungsform\n der modernen Industrie erwächst also aus der beständigen Verwandlung\n eines Theils der Arbeiterbevölkerung in unbeschäftigte oder halbbeschäf-\n tigte Hände. Die Oberflächlichkeit der politischen Oekonomie zeigt sich\n u. a.","kind":"passage","sequence":80,"address":"20.80"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0081","text":"darin, dass sie die Expansion und Kontraktion des Kredits, das\n blosse Symptom der Wechselperioden des industriellen Cyklus, zu deren\n Ursache macht. Ganz wie die Himmelskörper, sobald sie durch ersten\n Stoss in eine bestimmte Bewegung geschleudert sind, dieselbe Bewegung\n stets reproduciren, so die gesellschaftliche Produktion, sobald sie einmal\n in jene Bewegung wechselnder Expansion und Kontraktion geworfen ist.\n Wirkungen werden ihrerseits zu Ursachen und die Wechselfälle des ganzen\n Prozesses, der seine eignen Bedingungen stets reproducirt, nehmen die\n Form der Periodicität an. Ist letztere einmal konsolidirt, so begreift\n selbst die politische Oekonomie die Produktion einer relativen, d. h. für\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"620\", \"facs\": \"#f0639\"} --> das mittlere Verwerthungsbedürfniss des Kapitals relativen Surpluspopu-\n lation, als Lebensbedingung der modernen Industrie.","kind":"passage","sequence":81,"address":"20.81"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0082","text":"„Gesetzt“, sagt H. Merivale, früher Professor der politischen\n Oekonomie zu Oxford, später Beamter des englischen Kolonialministe-\n riums, „gesetzt, bei Gelegenheit einer Krise raffe die Nation sich zu einer\n Kraftanstrengung auf, um durch Emigration einiger 100,000 überflüssiger\n Arme los zu werden, was würde die Folge sein? Dass bei der ersten\n Wiederkehr der Arbeitsnachfrage ein Mangel vorhanden wäre. Wie rasch\n immer die Reproduktion von Menschen sein mag, sie braucht jedenfalls\n den Zwischenraum einer Generation zum Ersatz erwachsner Arbeiter.\n Nun hängen die Profite unsrer Fabrikanten hauptsächlich von der Macht\n ab, den günstigen Moment lebhafter Nachfrage zu exploitiren und sich so\n für die Periode der Erlahmung schadlos zu halten. Diese Macht ist\n ihnen nur gesichert durch Kommando über Maschinerie\n und Handarbeit.","kind":"passage","sequence":82,"address":"20.82"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0083","text":"Sie müssen disponible Hände vorfin-\n den; sie müssen fähig sein die Aktivität ihrer Operationen, wenn nöthig,\n höher zu spannen oder abzuspannen, je nach dem Stand des Markts, oder\n sie können platterdings nicht in der Hetzjagd der Konkurrenz das Ueber-\n gewicht behaupten, worauf der Reichthum dieses Landes ge-\n gründet ist“ [Note: H. Merivale: „Lectures on Colonization and Colonies.“\n Lond. 1841 and 1842, v. I, p. 146.] . Selbst Malthus erkennt in der Surpluspopulation, die er,\n nach seiner bornirten Weise, aus absolutem Ueberwachs der Arbeiterbevölke-\n rung, nicht aus ihrer relativen Ueberzähligmachung deutet, eine Nothwen-\n digkeit der modernen Industrie.","kind":"passage","sequence":83,"address":"20.83"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0084","text":"Er sagt: „Weise Gewohnheiten\n in Bezug auf die Ehe, wenn zu einer gewissen Höhe getrieben unter der\n Arbeiterklasse eines Landes, das hauptsächlich von Manufaktur und Han-\n del abhängt, würden ihm schädlich sein … Der Natur der Bevölkerung\n gemäss, kann ein Zuwachs von Arbeitern nicht zu Markt geliefert werden,\n in Folge besondrer Nachfrage, bis nach Verlauf von 16 oder 18 Jahren,\n und die Verwandlung von Revenue in Kapital durch Ersparung kann sehr\n viel rascher platzgreifen; ein Land ist stets dem ausgesetzt, dass sein\n Arbeitsfonds rascher wächst als die Bevölkerung“ [Note: „Prudential habits with regard to marriage carried to a considerable\n extent among the labouring class of a country mainly depending upon manufac-\n tures and commerce might injure it … From the nature of a population, an in-] .","kind":"passage","sequence":84,"address":"20.84"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0085","text":"Nachdem die politische\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"621\", \"facs\": \"#f0640\"} --> Oekonomie so die beständige Produktion einer relativen Sur-\n pluspopulation von Arbeitern für eine Nothwendigkeit der\n kapitalistischen Accumulation erklärt hat, legt sie, und zwar\n adäquat in der Figur einer alten Jungfer, dem „beau ideal“ ihres Kapitalisten\n folgende Worte an die durch ihre eigne Schöpfung von Surpluskapital aufs\n Pflaster geworfenen „Ueberzähligen“ in den Mund: „Wir Fabrikanten thun\n was wir können für euch, indem wir das Kapital vermehren, von\n dem ihr subsistiren müsst; und ihr müsst das übrige thun, indem ihr eure\n Zahlen den Subsistenzmitteln proportionirt“ [Note: Harriet Martineau: „The Manchester Strike. 1842“, p. 101.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":85,"address":"20.85"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0086","text":"Die kapitalistische Produktion schliesst die Schranken disponibler\n Arbeitskraft durch bloss natürlichen Zuwachs der Bevölkerung aus. Sie\n bedarf zu ihrem freien Spiel einer von dieser Schranke unabhän-\n gigen industriellen Reservearmee.","kind":"passage","sequence":86,"address":"20.86"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0087","text":"Bisher wurde unterstellt, dass der Zu- oder Abnahme des vari-\n ablen Kapitals genau die Zu- oder Abnahme der beschäftigten\n Arbeiterzahl entspricht. Die Abweichungen des Grössenwechsels des\n variablen Kapitals vom Grössenwechsel der Arbeiterzahl, worin es sich um-\n setzt, werden im Dritten Buch weiter erörtert. Hier interessiren sie uns\n nur, soweit sie das allgemeine Gesetz der Accumulation betreffen.","kind":"passage","sequence":87,"address":"20.87"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0088","text":"Bei gleichbleibender oder selbst verminderter Zahl der von ihm kom-\n mandirten Arbeiter wächst das variable Kapital, wenn der individuelle Ar-\n beiter mehr Arbeit liefert und daher sein Arbeitslohn wächst, obgleich\n der Arbeitspreis gleich bleibt, oder selbst sinkt, nur langsamer als die\n Arbeitsmasse steigt. Der Zuwachs des variablen Kapitals wird dann In-\n dex von mehr Arbeit, aber nicht von mehr beschäftigten Arbeitern. Jeder\n [Note: crease of labourers cannot be brought into market, in consequence of a particular\n demand, till after the lapse of 16 or 18 years, and the conversion of revenue into\n capital by saving, may take place much more rapidly; a country is always liable\n to an increase in the quantity of the funds for the maintenance of labour faster\n than the increase of population.“ (Malthus: „Princ. of Pol. Econ.“,\n p. 254, 319, 320.)","kind":"passage","sequence":88,"address":"20.88"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0089","text":"In diesem Werk entdeckt Malthus endlich, vermittelst Sis-\n mondi’s, die schöne Dreieinigkeit der kapitalistischen Produktion: Ueber-\n produktion — Ueberpopulation — Ueberkonsumtion, three very\n delicate monsters, indeed! Vgl. F. Engels: „Umrisse zu einer Kritik\n der Nationalökonomie“ l. c. p. 107 sqq.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"622\", \"facs\": \"#f0641\"} --> Kapitalist hat das absolute Interesse, ein bestimmtes Arbeitsquantum aus\n kleinerer, statt eben so wohlfeil oder selbst wohlfeiler aus grösserer\n Arbeiterzahl auszupressen. In dem letzten Fall wächst die Auslage von\n constantem Kapital verhältnissmässig zur Masse der in Fluss gesetzten Ar-\n beit, im ersten Fall viel langsamer. Je grösser die Stufenleiter der Pro-\n duktion, desto entscheidender diess Motiv. Seine Wucht wächst mit der\n Accumulation des Kapitals.","kind":"passage","sequence":89,"address":"20.89"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0090","text":"Man hat gesehn, dass die Entwicklung der kapitalistischen Produk-\n tionsweise und Produktivkraft der Arbeit — zugleich Ursache und Wirkung\n der Accumulation — den Kapitalisten befähigt, mit derselben Aus-\n lage von variablem Kapital mehr Arbeit durch grössere extensive\n oder intensive Exploitation der individuellen Arbeitskräfte flüssig zu\n machen. Man hat ferner gesehn, dass er mit demselben Kapital-\n werth mehr Arbeitskräfte kauft, indem er progressiv geschicktere\n Arbeiter durch ungeschicktere, reife durch unreife, männliche durch weib-\n liche, erwachsne Arbeitskraft durch jugendliche oder kindliche verdrängt.","kind":"passage","sequence":90,"address":"20.90"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0091","text":"Einerseits macht also, im Fortgang der Accumulation, grösseres\n variables Kapital mehr Arbeit flüssig, ohne mehr Arbeiter zu werben,\n andrerseits macht variables Kapital von derselben Grösse mehr\n Arbeit mit derselben Masse Arbeitskraft flüssig und endlich mehr niedere\n Arbeitskräfte durch Verdrängung höherer.","kind":"passage","sequence":91,"address":"20.91"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0092","text":"Die Produktion einer relativen Surpluspopulation oder\n die Freisetzung von Arbeitern geht daher noch rascher voran als die\n ohnehin mit dem Fortschritt der Accumulation beschleunigte\n technologische Umwälzung des Produktionsprozesses und die entsprechende\n proportionelle Abnahme des variablen Kapitaltheils gegen den constanten.\n Wenn die Produktionsmittel, wie sie an Umfang und Wirkungskraft zu-\n nehmen, in geringerem Grad Beschäftigungsmittel der Arbeiter wer-\n den, wird diess Verhältniss selbst wieder dadurch modificirt, dass im Mass wie\n die Produktivkraft der Arbeit wächst, das Kapital seine Zufuhr von Ar-\n beit rascher steigert als seine Nachfrage nach Arbeitern.","kind":"passage","sequence":92,"address":"20.92"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0093","text":"Die Ueber-\n arbeit des beschäftigten Theils der Arbeiterklasse schwellt die Reihen ihrer Re-\n serve, während umgekehrt der vermehrte Druck, den die letztere durch ihre\n Konkurrenz auf die erstere ausübt, diese zur Ueberarbeit und Unterwerfung\n unter die Diktate des Kapitals zwingt. Die Verdammung eines Theils\n der Arbeiterklasse zu erzwungnem Müssiggang durch Ueberarbeit des andern\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"623\", \"facs\": \"#f0642\"} --> Theils, und vice versa, wird Bereicherungsmittel des einzelnen Kapitalisten [Note: Selbst während der Baumwollnoth von 1863 findet man in einem Pam-\n phlet der Baumwollspinner von Blackburn heftige Denunciation gegen die Ueber-\n arbeit, die kraft des Fabrikgesetzes natürlich nur erwachsne männliche Arbeiter\n traf.","kind":"passage","sequence":93,"address":"20.93"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0094","text":"„The adult operatives at this mill have been asked to work from 12 to 13\n hours per day, while there are hundreds who are compelled to be idle who would\n willingly work partial time, in order to maintain their families and save their\n brethren from a premature grave through being overworked.“ „Wir“, heisst es\n weiter, „möchten fragen, ob diese Praxis Ueberzeit zu arbeiten irgend wie erträg-\n liche Verhältnisse zwischen Meistern und ‚Dienern‘ möglich macht? Die Opfer\n der Ueberarbeit fühlen die Unbill eben so sehr als die dadurch zu erzwungnem\n Müssiggang Verdammten (condemned to forced idleness). In diesem\n Distrikt reicht das zu verrichtende Werk hin, um alle theilweis zu beschäftigen,\n würde die Arbeit billig vertheilt.","kind":"passage","sequence":94,"address":"20.94"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0095","text":"Wir verlangen nur ein Recht, indem wir die\n Meister auffordern, allgemein nur kurze Zeit zu arbeiten, wenigstens so lange der\n jetzige Stand der Dinge währt, statt einen Theil zu überarbeiten, während der\n andre durch Arbeitsmangel gezwungen wird, von der Wohlthätigkeit seine Existenz\n zu fristen.“ („Reports of Insp. of Fact. 31. Oct. 1863“, p. 8.) — Die\n Wirkung einer relativen Surpluspopulation auf die beschäftigten Arbeiter begreift\n der Verfasser des „Essay on Trade and Commerce“ mit seinem gewohnten\n unfehlbaren Bourgeoisinstinkt. „Eine andre Ursache der Faulenzerei (idleness)\n in diesem Konigreich ist der Mangel einer hinreichenden Anzahlarbei-\n tender Hände.","kind":"passage","sequence":95,"address":"20.95"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0096","text":"So oft durch irgend eine ungewöhnliche Nachfrage für Fabri-\n kate die Arbeitsmasse ungenügend wird, fühlen die Arbeiter ihre eigne Wichtig-\n keit und wollen sie ihren Meistern ebenfalls fühlbar machen; es ist erstaunlich;\n aber so depravirt ist die Gesinnung dieser Kerle, dass in solchen Fällen Gruppen\n von Arbeitern sich kombinirt haben, um ihre Meister dadurch in Verlegenheit zu\n setzen, dass sie einen ganzen Tag durch faulenzten.“ („Essay etc.“, p. 27,\n 28.) Die Kerle verlangten nämlich Lohnerhöhung.] \n und beschleunigt zugleich die Produktion der industriel-\n len Reservearmee auf einem dem Fortschritt der gesell-\n schaftlichen Accumulation entsprechenden Massstab. Wie\n wichtig diess Moment in der Bildung der relativen Surpluspopulation, beweist\n z. B. England. Seine technischen Mittel zur „Ersparung“ von Arbeit sind ko-\n lossal.","kind":"passage","sequence":96,"address":"20.96"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0097","text":"Dennoch, würde morgen allgemein die Arbeit auf ein rationelles\n Mass beschränkt, und für die verschiednen Schichten der Arbeiterklasse wie-\n der entsprechend nach Alter und Geschlecht abgestuft, so wäre die vorhandne\n Arbeiterpopulation absolut unzureichend zur Fortführung der nationalen\n Produktion auf ihrer jetzigen Stufenleiter. Die grosse Mehrheit der jetzt\n „unproduktiven“ Arbeiter müsste in „produktive“ verwandelt werden.","kind":"passage","sequence":97,"address":"20.97"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0098","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"624\", \"facs\": \"#f0643\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":98,"address":"20.98"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0099","text":"Im Grossen und Ganzen sind die allgemeinen Bewegungen des Ar-\n beitslohns ausschliesslich regulirt durch die Expansion und Kontraktion\n der industriellen Reservearmee, welche dem Periodenwechsel\n des industriellen Cyklus entsprechen. Sie sind also nicht bestimmt\ndurch die Bewegung der absoluten Anzahl der Arbeiterbevöl-\n kerung, sondern durch das wechselnde Verhältniss, worin die Arbei-\n terklasse in aktive Armee und Reservearmee zerfällt, durch die Zunahme und\n Abnahme des relativen Umfangs der Surpluspopulation, durch den Grad, wor-\n in sie bald absorbirt, bald wieder freigesetzt wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":99,"address":"20.99"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0100","text":"Für die moderne Industrie\n mit ihrem zehnjährigen Cyklus und seinem regelmässigen Periodenwechsel,\n der ausserdem im Fortgang der Accumulation durch stets rascher auf ein-\n ander folgende unregelmässige Oscillationen durchkreuzt wird, wäre es in der\n That ein schönes Gesetz, welches die Nachfrage und Zufuhr von Arbeit nicht\n durch die Expansion und Kontraktion des Kapitals, also nach sei-\n nen jedesmaligen Verwerthungsbedürfnissen regelte, so dass der\n Arbeitsmarkt bald relativ untervoll erscheint, weil das Kapital sich expandirt,\n bald wieder übervoll, weil es sich kontrahirt, sondern umgekehrt die Bewegung\n des Kapitals von der absoluten Bewegung der Populationsmenge\n abhängig machte. Diess jedoch ist das ökonomische Dogma. Nach demselben\n steigt in Folge der Kapitalaccumulation der Arbeitslohn.","kind":"passage","sequence":100,"address":"20.100"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0101","text":"Der erhöhte\n Arbeitslohn spornt zur rascheren Vermehrung der Arbeiterbevölkerung und\n diese dauert fort, bis der Arbeitsmarkt überfüllt, also das Kapital relativ\n zur Arbeiterzufuhr unzureichend geworden ist. Der Arbeitslohn sinkt, und\n nun die Kehrseite der Medaille. Durch den fallenden Arbeitslohn wird\n die Arbeiterbevölkerung nach und nach decimirt, so dass ihr gegenüber\n das Kapital wieder überschüssig wird, oder auch, wie Andre es erklären,\n der fallende Arbeitslohn und die entsprechende erhöhte Exploitation des\n Arbeiters beschleunigt wieder die Accumulation, während gleichzeitig der\n niedere Lohn das Wachsthum der Arbeiterklasse in Schach hält. So tritt\n wieder das Verhältniss ein, worin die Arbeitszufuhr niedriger als die Ar-\n beitsnachfrage, der Lohn steigt u. s. w. Eine schöne Bewegungsmethode\n diess für die entwickelte kapitalistische Produktion!","kind":"passage","sequence":101,"address":"20.101"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0102","text":"Bevor in Folge der\n Lohnerhöhung irgend ein positives Wachsthum der wirklich arbeitsfähigen\n Bevölkerung eintreten könnte, wäre die Frist aber und abermal abgelaufen,\n worin der industrielle Feldzug geführt, die Schlacht geschlagen und ent-\n schieden sein muss.","kind":"passage","sequence":102,"address":"20.102"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0103","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"625\", \"facs\": \"#f0644\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":103,"address":"20.103"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0104","text":"Zwischen 1849 und 1859 trat, zugleich mit fallenden Getreidepreisen,\n eine praktisch betrachtet nur nominelle Lohnerhöhung in den englischen\n Agrikulturdistrikten ein, z. B. in Wiltshire stieg der Wochenlohn von 7\n auf 8 sh., in Dorsetshire von 7 oder 8 auf 9 sh. u. s. w. Es war diess\n Folge des übergewöhnlichen Abflusses der agrikolen Surpluspopu-\n lation, verursacht durch Kriegsnachfrage, massenhafte Ausdehnung der\n Eisenbahnbauten, Fabriken, Bergwerke u. s. w. Je niedriger der Arbeitslohn,\n desto höher drückt sich jedes noch so unbedeutende Steigen desselben\nin Procentzahlen aus. Ist der Wochenlohn z. B. 20 sh. und steigt\n er auf 22, so um 10 %; ist er dagegen nur 7 sh. und steigt auf 9, so um\n 28\\frac{4}{7} %, was sehr erklecklich klingt.","kind":"passage","sequence":104,"address":"20.104"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0105","text":"Jedenfalls heulten die Pächter und\n schwatzte sogar der „London Economist“ ganz ernsthaft von „a\n general and substantial advance“ [Note: Economist, Jan. 21, 1860.]  mit Bezug auf diese Hungerlöhne.\n Was thaten nun die Pächter? Warteten sie, bis die Landarbeiter sich in\n Folge dieser brillanten Zahlung so vermehrt hatten, dass ihr Lohn wieder fal-\n len musste, wie die Sache sich im dogmatisch ökonomischen Hirn zuträgt?\n Sie führten mehr Maschinerie ein, und im Umsehn waren die Arbeiter wieder\n „überzählig“ in einem selbst den Pächtern genügenden Verhältniss. Es\n war jetzt „mehr Kapital“ in der Agrikultur angelegt als vorher und in\n einer produktiveren Form. Damit fiel die Nachfrage nach Arbeit nicht\n nur relativ, sondern absolut.","kind":"passage","sequence":105,"address":"20.105"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0106","text":"Jene ökonomische Fiktion verwechselt die Gesetze, welche die all-\n gemeine Bewegung des Arbeitslohns oder das Verhältniss zwi-\n schen Arbeiterklasse und gesellschaftlichem Gesammtkapital regeln, mit den\n Gesetzen, welche die Arbeiterbevölkerung unter die beson-\n dern Produktionssphären vertheilen. Wenn z. B. in Folge\n günstiger Konjunktur die Accumulation in einer bestimmten Produktionssphäre\n besonders lebhaft, die Profite hier grösser als die Durchschnittsprofite, Zu-\n schusskapital dahin drängt, so steigt natürlich Arbeitsnachfrage und Arbeits-\n lohn. Der höhere Arbeitslohn zieht einen grösseren Theil der Arbeiterbevölke-\n rung in die begünstigte Sphäre, bis sie mit Arbeitskraft gesättigt ist, und der\n Lohn auf die Dauer wieder auf sein früheres Durchschnittsniveau oder unter\n dasselbe fällt, falls der Zudrang zu gross war.","kind":"passage","sequence":106,"address":"20.106"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0107","text":"Hier sieht der politische\n Oekonom „wo und wie“, mit Zunahme des Lohns eine absolute Zunahme\n [Running matter: I. 40] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"626\", \"facs\": \"#f0645\"} --> von Arbeitern, und mit der absoluten Zunahme der Arbeiter eine Abnahme\n des Lohns, aber er sieht in der That nur die lokale Oscillation des\n Arbeitsmarkts einer besondern Produktionssphäre, er sieht nur Phä-\n nomene der Vertheilung der Arbeiterbevöl kerung in die\n verschiednen Anlagesphären des Kapitals, je nach seinen wechselnden\n Bedürfnissen.","kind":"passage","sequence":107,"address":"20.107"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0108","text":"Die industrielle Reservearmee oder relative Surpluspopulation drückt\n während der Perioden der Stagnation und mittleren Prosperität auf die aktive\n Arbeiterarmee und hält ihre Ansprüche während der Periode der Ueberpro-\n duktion und des Paroxysmus im Zaum. Die relative Surpluspopula-\n tion ist also der Hintergrund, worauf das Gesetz der Nach-\n frage und Zufuhr von Arbeit sich bewegt. Sie zwängt den Spiel-\n raum dieses Gesetzes in die der Exploitationsgier und Herrsch-\n sucht des Kapitals abso lut zusagenden Schranken ein. Es ist hier\n der Ort auf eine der Grossthaten der ökonomischen Apologetik zurückzukom-\n men.","kind":"passage","sequence":108,"address":"20.108"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0109","text":"Man erinnert sich, dass wenn durch Einführung neuer oder Ausdehnung\n alter Maschinerie ein Stück variables Kapital in constantes verwandelt wird,\n der ökonomische Apologet diese Operation, welche Kapital „bindet“ und eben\n dadurch Arbeiter „freisetzt“, umgekehrt so deutet, das sie Kapital für den\n Arbeiter freisetzt. Erst jetzt kann man die Unverschämtheit des Apologeten\n vollständig würdigen. Was freigesetzt wird, sind nicht nur die unmittelbar\n durch die Maschine verdrängten Arbeiter, sondern ebenso ihre Ersatzmann-\n schaft und das, bei gewohnter Ausdehnung des Geschäfts auf seiner alten\n Basis, regelmässig absorbirte Zuschusskontingent. Es ist nicht altes Kapital\n für Arbeiter freigesetzt, aber es sind Arbeiter für etwa „zuschüssiges“ Ka-\n pital freigesetzt. D. h.","kind":"passage","sequence":109,"address":"20.109"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0110","text":"also, der Mechanismus der kapitalistischen Produk-\n tion sorgt dafür, dass der absolute Zuwachs von Kapital von keiner\n entsprechenden Steigerung der allgemeinen Arbeitsnachfrage\n begleitet ist. Und diess nennt der Apologet eine Kompensation für das\n Elend, die Leiden und den möglichen Untergang der deplacirten Arbeiter wäh-\n rend der Uebergangsperiode, welche sie in die industrielle Reservearmee\n bannt! Die Nachfrage nach Arbeit ist nicht identisch mit Wachsthum des\n Kapitals, die Zufuhr der Arbeiter nicht mit dem Wachsthum der Arbeiter-\n klasse, so dass zwei von einander unabhängige Potenzen auf einander\n einwirkten. Les dés sont pipés. Das Kapital agirt auf beiden Seiten\n zugleich.","kind":"passage","sequence":110,"address":"20.110"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0111","text":"Wenn seine Accumulation einerseits die Nachfrage nach Arbeit\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"627\", \"facs\": \"#f0646\"} --> vermehrt, vermehrt sie andrerseits die Zufuhr von Arbeitern durch deren\n „Freisetzung“, während zugleich der Druck der Unbeschäftigten die Be-\n schäftigten zur Flüssigmachung von mehr Arbeit zwingt, also in gewissem\n Grad die Arbeitszufuhr von der Zufuhr von Arbeitern unabhän-\n gig macht. Die Bewegung des Gesetzes der Nachfrage und Zu-\n fuhr von Arbeit auf dieser Basis vollendet die Despotie des Ka-\n pitals.","kind":"passage","sequence":111,"address":"20.111"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0112","text":"Sobald daher die Arbeiter hinter das Geheimniss kommen, wie es zu-\n geht, dass im selben Mass, wie sie mehr arbeiten, mehr fremden Reichthum\n produciren, und die Produktivkraft ihrer Arbeit wächst, sogar ihre Funktion als\n Verwerthungsmittel des Kapitals immer prekärer für sie wird; sobald sie ent-\n decken, dass der Intensivitätsgrad der Konkurrenz unter ihnen selbst ganz und\n gar von dem Druck der relativen Surpluspopulation abhängt; sobald sie daher\n durch Trade’s Unions u. s. w. eine planmässige Zusammenwirkung zwischen\n den Beschäftigten und Unbeschäftigten zu organisiren suchen, um die ruini-\n renden Folgen jenes Naturgesetzes der kapitalistischen Produk-\n tion auf ihre Klasse zu brechen oder zu schwächen, zetert das Kapital, und sein\n Sykophant, der politische Oekonom, über Verletzung des „ewigen“ und so zu\n sagen „heiligen“ Gesetzes der Nachfrage und Zufuhr.","kind":"passage","sequence":112,"address":"20.112"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0113","text":"Jeder Zusammenhalt\n zwischen den Beschäftigten und Unbeschäftigten stört nämlich das „reine“\n Spiel jenes Gesetzes. Sobald andrerseits, in den Kolonieen z. B., widrige\n Umstände die Schöpfung der industriellen Reservearmee,\n und mit ihr die absolute Abhängigkeit der Arbeiterklasse von der Ka-\n pitalistenklasse, verhindern, rebellirt das Kapital, sammt seinem gemein-\n plätzlichen Sancho Pansa, gegen das „heilige“ Gesetz der Nachfrage und Zu-\n fuhr, und sucht ihm durch Zwangsmittel unter die Arme zu greifen.","kind":"passage","sequence":113,"address":"20.113"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0114","text":"Die relative Surpluspopulation existirt in allen möglichen Schattirungen.\n Jeder Arbeiter gehört dazu während der Zeit, wo er halb oder gar nicht be-\n schäftigt ist. Ohne hier auf Details einzugehn, genügen einige allgemeine\n Andeutungen. Abgesehn von den periodischen Formverschiedenheiten\n der Surpluspopulation in dem Phasenwechsel des industriellen\n Cyklus, wo sie bald akut in den Krisen erscheint, bald chronisch in\n der Periode der Stagnation, besitzt sie fortwährend drei Formen, die\nflüssige, die latente und die stagnante.","kind":"passage","sequence":114,"address":"20.114"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0115","text":"Man hat gesehn, wie die Fabrikarbeiter bald repellirt, bald in\n grösserem Umfang wieder attrahirt werden, so dass im Grossen und Ganzen\n die Zahl der beschäftigten Arbeiter zunimmt, wenn auch in stets abnehmendem\n [Running matter: 40*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"628\", \"facs\": \"#f0647\"} --> Verhältniss zur Produktionsleiter. Die Surpluspopulation existirt hier in\nfliessender Form. Wir machen nur auf zwei Umstände aufmerksam. So-\n wohl in den eigentlichen Fabriken, wie in allen grossen Werkstätten, wo-\n rin die Maschinerie als Faktor eingeht oder auch nur die moderne Thei-\n lung der Arbeit durchgeführt ist, werden männliche Arbeiter bis zur Zu-\n rücklegung des Jugendalters in Massen verbraucht, wovon später nur sehr\n geringe Proportion in demselben Zweig verwendbar bleibt, daher beständig\n grosse Anzahl herausgeworfen wird.","kind":"passage","sequence":115,"address":"20.115"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0116","text":"Sie bilden ein Element der fliessen-\n den Surpluspopulation, das mit dem Umfang der Industrie wächst. Ein Theil\n davon emigrirt und reist in der That nur dem emigrirenden Kapital nach.\n Eine der Folgen ist, dass die weibliche Bevölkerung rascher wächst als\n die männliche, teste England. Der Widerspruch, dass der natürliche Zu-\n wachs der Arbeiterbevölkerung den Accumulationsbedürfnissen des Kapitals\nnicht genügt, und andrerseits zu gross für seine Absorption ist, ist\n ein Widerspruch seiner Bewegung selbst. Es braucht grössere Massen\n davon im früheren Alter, weniger im männlichen.","kind":"passage","sequence":116,"address":"20.116"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0117","text":"Der Widerspruch ist\n nicht grösser als der andre, dass während Arbeiter zu vielen Tausenden\n auf dem Pflaster liegen, weil die Theilung der Arbeit sie an einen be-\n stimmten Geschäftszweig kettet, gleichzeitig über Mangel an Händen\n geklagt wird [Note: Während im letzten Halbjahr von 1866 80—90,000 Arbeiter in London\n ausser Arbeit geworfen wurden, heisst es im Fabrikbericht über dasselbe Halbjahr:\n „It does not appear absolutely true to say that demand will always produce supply\n just at the moment when it is needed. It has not done it with labour, for much\n machinery has been idle last year for want of hands.“ („Reports of Insp.\n of Fact. for 31. Oct. 1866“, Lond. 1867, p. 81.)] . Bei dem raschen Konsum der Arbeitskraft durch das Kapi-\n tal ist der Arbeiter von mittlerem Alter meist schon überlebt.","kind":"passage","sequence":117,"address":"20.117"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0118","text":"Er fällt in die\n Reihen der Surpluspopulation oder rückt von einer höhern Staffel auf eine\n niedrigere, während das Kapital seinen Platz durch frischere Arbeitskraft er-\n setzt. Das absolute Wachsthum der Arbeiterklasse erheischt so eine\n Form, welche ihre Zahl schwellt, obgleich ihre Elemente sich rasch abnutzen.\n Es ist daher rasche Ablösung der Arbeitergenerationen nöthig.\n (Dasselbe Gesetz gilt nicht für die übrigen Klassen der Bevölkerung.)\n Diess wird erreicht durch frühe Ehen, nothwendige Folge der Verhältnisse,\n worin die Arbeiter der grossen Industrie leben, und durch die Prämie,\n welche die Exploitation der Arbeiterkinder auf ihre Produktion setzt.","kind":"passage","sequence":118,"address":"20.118"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0119","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"629\", \"facs\": \"#f0648\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":119,"address":"20.119"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0120","text":"Sobald sich die kapitalistische Produktion der Agrikultur oder im\n Grad, worin sie sich derselben bemächtigt hat, nimmt mit der Accumula-\n tion des hier funktionirenden Kapitals die Nachfrage für\n die ländliche Arbeiterbevölkerung absolut ab, ohne dass\n ihre Repulsion, wie in der nicht agrikolen Industrie, durch grössere Attraktion\n ergänzt wäre. Ein Theil der Landbevölkerung befindet sich daher fortwährend\nim Uebergang zur Metamorphose in städtische oder Manu-\n fakturbevölkerung. (Manufaktur hier im Sinn aller nicht-agrikolen In-\n dustrie [Note: „781 Städte sind aufgezählt im Census von 1861 für England und Wales,\n mit 10,960,998 Einwohnern, während die Dörfer und Landkirchspiele nur\n 9,105,226 zählen … Im Jahr 1851 figurirten 580 Städte im Census, deren Be-\n völkerung ungefähr gleich der Bevölkerung der sie umgebenden Landdistrikte war.","kind":"passage","sequence":120,"address":"20.120"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0121","text":"Während aber in den letzteren die Bevölkerung während der folgenden 10 Jahre\nnur um eine halbe Million wuchs, wuchs sie in den 580 Städten um 1,554,067.\n Der Bevölkerungszuwachs in den Landkirchspielen ist 6.5 %, in den Städten\n 17.3 %. Der Unterschied in der Rate des Wachsthums ist der Wanderung\n vom Land in die Stadt geschuldet. Drei Viertel des Gesammtwachsthums der\n Bevölkerung gehört den Stäten.“ („Census etc.“, v. III, p. 11, 12.)] .) Diese Quelle der relativen Surpluspopulation fliesst also\n beständig. Aber ihr beständiger Fluss setzt auf dem Lande selbst\n eine fortwährend latente Surpluspopulation voraus, deren Umfang nur\n sichtbar wird, sobald sich die Abzugskanäle ausnahmsweise weit öffnen.\n Der Landarbeiter wird daher auf das Minimum des Salairs herabgedrückt\n und steht mit einem Fuss stets im Sumpf des Pauperismus.","kind":"passage","sequence":121,"address":"20.121"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0122","text":"Die stagnante Surpluspopulation bildet einen Theil der aktiven\n Arbeiterarmee, aber mit durchaus unregelmässiger Beschäftigung, so dass\n das Kapital hier stets eine ausserordentliche Masse latenter Arbeitskraft\n zur Verfügung hat. Ihre Lebenslage sinkt unter das durchschnittliche\n Normalniveau der arbeitenden Klasse und grade diess macht sie zur breiten\n Grundlage eigner Exploitationszweige des Kapitals. Maximum der Arbeitszeit\n und Minimum des Salairs charakterisiren sie. Wir haben unter der Rubrik\n der Hausarbeit ihre Hauptgestalt bereits kennen gelernt. Sie rekrutirt\n sich fortwährend aus den Ueberzähligen der grossen Industrie und Agri-\n kultur, und namentlich auch in untergehenden Industriezweigen, wo der\n Handwerksbetrieb dem Manufakturbetrieb, letztrer dem Maschinenbetrieb\n erliegt.","kind":"passage","sequence":122,"address":"20.122"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0123","text":"Ihr Umfang dehnt sich, wie mit Umfang und Energie der Accu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"630\", \"facs\": \"#f0649\"} --> mulation die „Ueberzähligmachung“ fortschreitet. Aber sie bildet zu-\n gleich ein sich selbst reproducirendes und verewigendes Element der Ar-\n beiterklasse, das verhältnissmässig grösseren Antheil am Gesammtwachs-\n thum derselben nimmt als die übrigen Elemente. In der That steht\n nicht nur die Masse der Geburten und Todesfälle, sondern\n die absolute Grösse der Familien in umgekehrtem Verhält-\n niss zur Höhe des Arbeitslohns, also zur Masse der Lebens-\n mittel, worüber die verschiednen Arbeiterkategorieen verfü-\n gen. Diess Gesetz der kapitalistischen Gesellschaft klänge unsinnig\n unter Wilden, oder selbst civilisirten Kolonisten.","kind":"passage","sequence":123,"address":"20.123"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0124","text":"Es erinnert an die massenhafte\n Reproduktion individuell schwacher und vielgehetzter Thierarten [Note: „Poverty seems favourable to generation.“ (A. Smith.) Diess ist sogar\n eine besonders weise Einrichtung Gottes nach dem galanten und geistreichen Abbé\nGaliani: „Iddio fa che gli uomini che esercitano mestieri di prima utilità nascono\n abbondanemente.“ (Galiani l. c. p. 78.) „Misery, up to the extreme point of\n famine and pestilence, instead of checking, tends to increase popula-\n tion.“ (S. Laing: „National Distress. 1844“, p. 69.) Nachdem Laing\n diess statistisch illustrirt, fährt er fort: „Befände sich alle Welt in bequemen Um-\n ständen, so wäre die Welt bald entvölkert.“ („If the people were all in easy\n circumstances, the world would soon be depopulated.“)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":124,"address":"20.124"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0125","text":"Der tiefste Niederschlag der relativen Surpluspopulation endlich bil-\n det die Sphäre des Pauperismus. Sie besteht — wir sehn hier ab von\n Vagabunden, Verbrechern, Prostituirten, kurz dem eigentlichen Lumpen-\n proletariat — aus drei Kategorieen: erstens, Arbeitsfähige.\n Man braucht die Statistik des englischen Pauperismus nur oberflächlich an-\n zusehn und man findet, dass seine Masse mit jeder Krise schwillt und mit\n jeder Wiederbelebung des Geschäfts abnimmt. Zweitens: Waisen-\n und Pauperkinder. Sie sind Kandidaten der industriellen Reservearmee\n und werden in Zeiten grosser Prosperität, wie 1860 z. B., rasch und mas-\n senhaft in die aktive Arbeiterarmee einrollirt. Drittens: Verkommene,\n Verlumpte, Arbeitsunfähige.","kind":"passage","sequence":125,"address":"20.125"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0126","text":"Es sind namentlich Arbeiter, die an ihrer\n durch die Theilung der Arbeit verursachten Unbeweglichkeit untergehn,\n solche, die über das Normalalter eines Arbeiters hinausleben, endlich die\n Opfer der Industrie, deren Zahl mit gefährlicher Maschinerie, Bergwerks-\n bau, chemischen Fabriken u. s. w. wächst, Verstümmelte, Verkrankte,\n Wittwen u. s. w. Der Pauperismus bildet das Invalidenhaus der aktiven\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"631\", \"facs\": \"#f0650\"} --> Arbeiterarmee und das todte Gewicht der industriellen Reservearmee. Seine\n Produktion ist eingeschlossen in der Produktion der Surpluspopulation,\n seine Nothwendigkeit in ihrer Nothwendigkeit, mit ihr bildet er eine Ex-\n istenzbedingung der kapitalistischen Produktion und Entwicklung des Reich-\n thums.","kind":"passage","sequence":126,"address":"20.126"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0127","text":"Er gehört zu den faux frais der kapitalistischen Produktion, die\n das Kapital jedoch grossentheils von sich selbst ab auf die Schultern der\n Arbeiterklasse und der kleinen Mittelklasse zu wälzen weiss.","kind":"passage","sequence":127,"address":"20.127"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0128","text":"Je grösser der gesellschaftliche Reichthum, das funktionirende\n Kapital, Umfang und Energie seines Wachsthums, also auch die absolute\n Grösse der Arbeiterbevölkerung und die Produktivkraft ihrer\n Arbeit, desto grösser die relative Surpluspopulation oder industrielle Reserve-\n armee. Die disponible Arbeitskraft wird durch dieselben Ursa-\n chen entwickelt, wie die Expansivkraft des Kapitals. Die verhält-\n nissmässige Grösse der industriellen Reservearmee wächst also mit den Poten-\n zen des Reichthums. Je grösser aber diese Reservearmee im Verhältniss zur\n aktiven Arbeiterarmee, desto massenhafter die konsolidirte Surpluspopu-\n lation oder die Arbeiterschichten, deren Elend im umgekehrten Verhältniss\n zu ihrer Arbeitsqual steht. Je grösser endlich die Lazarusschichte der\n Arbeiterklasse und die industrielle Reservearmee, desto grösser der offi-\n cielle Pauperismus.","kind":"passage","sequence":128,"address":"20.128"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0129","text":"Diess ist das absolute, allgemeine Gesetz\n der kapitalistischen Accumulation. Es wird gleich allen all-\n gemeinen Gesetzen in seiner Verwirklichung durch mannigfache Umstände\n modificirt, deren Analyse nicht hierher gehört.","kind":"passage","sequence":129,"address":"20.129"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0130","text":"Man begreift die Narrheit der ökonomischen Weisheit, die den Arbeitern\n predigt, ihre Zahl den Verwerthungsbedürfnissen des Kapitals\n anzupassen. Der Mechanismus der kapitalistischen Produktion und Ac-\n cumulation passt sie beständig diesen Verwerthungsbedürfnissen an. Erstes\n Wort dieser Anpassung ist die Schöpfung einer relativen Surpluspopulation\n oder industriellen Reservearmee, letztes Wort das Elend stets wachsender\n Schichten der aktiven Arbeiterarmee und das todte Gewicht des Pauperismus.","kind":"passage","sequence":130,"address":"20.130"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0131","text":"Das Gesetz, dass die Entwicklung der gesellschaftlichen Produktiv-\n kraft der Arbeit die Masse der zu verausgabenden Arbeitskraft im Verhält-\n niss zur Wirkung und Masse ihrer Produktionsmittel progressiv senkt,\n drückt sich auf kapitalistischer Grundlage, wo nicht der Arbeiter\n die Arbeitsmittel, sondern die Arbeitsmittel den Arbeiter anwenden, darin\n aus, dass je höher die Produktivkraft der Arbeit, desto grös-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"632\", \"facs\": \"#f0651\"} --> ser der Druck der Arbeiter auf ihre Beschäftigungsmittel\n und desto prekärer die Existenzbedingung des Lohnarbeiters, Verkauf\n seiner Arbeitskraft zur Vermehrung des fremden Reichthums oder\n Selbstverwerthung des Kapitals.","kind":"passage","sequence":131,"address":"20.131"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0132","text":"Rascheres Wachsthum der Pro-\n duktionsmittel und der Produktivkraft der Arbeit als der\n produktiven Bevölkerung drücktsich kapitalistisch um-\n gekehrt darin aus, dass die Arbeiterbevölkerung stets rascher\n wächst als das Verwerthungsbedürfniss des Kapitals.","kind":"passage","sequence":132,"address":"20.132"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0133","text":"Es zeigte sich im vierten Kapitel bei Analyse der Produktion des\n relativen Mehrwerths, dass alle Methoden zur Steigerung der gesellschaft-\n lichen Produktivkraft der Arbeit in der kapitalistischen Form sich auf\n Kosten des individuellen Arbeiters entwickeln, dass alle Mittel zur Berei-\n cherung der Produktion in Beherrschungs- und Exploitationsmittel des\n Producenten umschlagen, dass sie den Arbeiter in einen Theilmenschen\n verstümmeln, ihn zum Anhängsel der Maschine entwürdigen, mit der Qual\n der Arbeit ihren Inhalt vernichten, ihm die geistigen Potenzen des Arbeits-\n prozesses entfremden, im selben Masse, worin derselbe sich die Wissen-\n schaft als selbstständige Potenz einverleibt, die Bedingungen, innerhalb\n deren er arbeitet, beständig anormaler machen, ihn während des Arbeits-\n prozesses der kleinlichst gehässigen Despotie unterwerfen, seine Lebens-\n zeit in Arbeitszeit","kind":"passage","sequence":133,"address":"20.133"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0134","text":"verwandeln, sein Weib und Kind unter das Juggernaut-\n rad des Kapitals schleudern. Aber alle Methoden zur Produktion des Mehr-\n werths sind zugleich Methoden der Accumulation und jede Ausdehnung der Ac-\n cumulation wird umgekehrt Mittel zur Entwicklung jener Methoden. Es folgt\n daher, dass im Masse wie Kapital accumulirt, die Lage des Arbeiters, wel-\n ches immer seine Zahlung, sich verschlechtert. Das Gesetz endlich,\n welches die relativeSurpluspopulation oder industrielle Re-\n servearmee stets mit Umfang und Energie der Accumu-\n lation in Gleichgewicht hält, schmiedet den Arbeiter fester an\n das Kapital als den Prometheus die Keile des Hephästos an den Felsen.\n Es bedingt eine der Accumulation von Kapital entsprechende Ac-\n cumulation von Elend.","kind":"passage","sequence":134,"address":"20.134"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0135","text":"Die Accumulation von Reichthum auf dem\n einen Pol ist also zugleich Accumulation von Elend, Arbeitsqual, Skla-\n verei, Unwissenheit, Brutalisirung und moralischer Degradation auf dem\n Gegenpol, d. h. auf Seite der Klasse, die ihr eignes Produkt als\n Kapital producirt.","kind":"passage","sequence":135,"address":"20.135"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0136","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"633\", \"facs\": \"#f0652\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":136,"address":"20.136"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0137","text":"Dieser antagonistische Charakter der kapitalisti-\n schen Accumulation [Note: „De jour en jour il devient donc plus clair que les rapports de production\n dans lesquels se meut la bourgeoisie n’ont pas un caractère un, un caractère\n simple, mais un caractère de duplicité; que dans les mêmes rapports dans lesquels\n se produit la richesse, la misère se produit aussi; que dans les mêmes rapports\n dans lesquels il y a développement des forces productives, il y a une force produc-\n tive de répression; que ces rapports ne produisent la richesse bourgeoise,\n c’est à dire la richesse de la classe bourgeoise, qu’en anéantissant continuellement\n la richesse des membres intégrants de cette classe et en produisant un prolétariat\n toujours croissant.“ (Karl Marx: „Misère de la Philosophie“, p.","kind":"passage","sequence":137,"address":"20.137"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0138","text":"116.)]  ist in verschiedenen Formen von politischen\n Oekonomen ausgesprochen, obgleich sie zum Theil zwar analoge, aber\n dennoch wesentlich verschiedene Erscheinungen vorkapitalistischer\n Produktionsweisen damit zusammenwerfen.","kind":"passage","sequence":138,"address":"20.138"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0139","text":"Der venetianische Mönch Ortes, einer der grossen ökonomischen\n Schriftsteller des 18. Jahrhunderts, fasst den Antagonismus der kapi-\n talistischen Produktion als allgemeines Naturgesetz des\n gesellschaftlichen Reichthums. „Das Ökonomisch Gute und ökono-\n misch Böse halten sich in einer Nation stets das Gleichgewicht („il bene\n ed il male economico in una nazione sempre all’ istessa misura“), die Fülle\n der Güter für Einige ist immer gleich dem Mangel derselben für Andre\n („la copia dei beni in alcuni sempre equale alla mancanza di esse in altri“).\n Grosser Reichthum in Einigen ist stets begleitet von absoluter Beraubung\n des Nothwendigen in viel mehr andern“ [Note: G. Ortes: „Della Economia Nazionale, libri sei 1777“, bei\nCustodi, Parte Moderna. t. XXI, p. 6, 9, 22, 25 etc. Ortes sagt l. c.\n p.","kind":"passage","sequence":139,"address":"20.139"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0140","text":"32: „In luoco di progettar sistimi inutili per la felicità de’ popoli, mi limiterò\n a investigare la ragione della loro infelicità.“] . Der Reichthum einer Nation\n entspricht ihrer Bevölkerung und ihr Elend entspricht ihrem Reichthum.\n Die Arbeitsamkeit in Einigen erzwingt den Müssiggang in Andern.\n Die Armen und Müssigen sind eine nothwendige Frucht der Reichen\n und Thätigen u. s. w. In ganz grober Weise verherrlichte ungefähr\n 10 Jahre nach Ortes der hochkirchliche protestantische Pfaffe Town-\n send die Armuth als nothwendige Bedingung des Reichthums.","kind":"passage","sequence":140,"address":"20.140"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0141","text":"„Ge-\n setzlicher Zwang zur Arbeit ist verbunden mit zu viel\n Mühe, Gewaltsamkeit und Geräusch, während der Hunger nicht nur\n ein friedlicher, schweigsamer, unaufhörlicher Druck, sondern als natürlich-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"634\", \"facs\": \"#f0653\"} --> stes Motiv zur Industrie und Arbeit die machtvollste Anstrengung hervor-\n ruft.“ Alles kömmt also darauf an, den Hunger unter der Arbeiterklasse\n permanent zu machen, und dafür sorgt, nach Townsend, das Bevölkerungs-\n princip, das besonders unter den Armen thätig ist. „Es scheint ein\n Naturgesetz, dass die Armen zu einem gewissen Grad leichtsinnig\n (improvident) sind (nämlich so leichtsinnig auf die Welt zu kommen ohne\n goldne Löffel im Mund), so dass stets welche da sind („that there al-\n ways may be some“) zur Erfüllung der servilsten, schmutzigsten und ge-\n meinsten Funktionen des Gemeinwesens.","kind":"passage","sequence":141,"address":"20.141"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0142","text":"Der Fonds von menschlichem\n Glück („the fonds of human happiness“) wird dadurch sehr vermehrt, die\n Delikateren („the more delicate“) sind von der Plackerei befreit und kön-\n nen höherem Beruf u. s. w. ungestört nachgehn … Das Armengesetz hat\n die Tendenz, die Harmonie und Schönheit, die Symmetrie und Ordnung die-\n ses Systems, welches Gott und die Natur in der Welt errichtet\n haben, zu zerstören“ [Note: „A Dissertation on the Poor Laws. By a Wellwisher of\n Mankind. (The Rev. Mr. J. Townsend.) 1786“, republished Lond.\n 1817, p. 15. Dieser „delikate“ Pfaffe, dessen eben angeführte Schrift, nebst seiner\n Reise durch Spanien, Malthus oft Seiten lang abschreibt, entlehnte den grössten\n Theil seiner Doktrin aus Sir J. Steuart, den er jedoch verdreht. Z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":142,"address":"20.142"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0143","text":"wenn\n Steuart sagt: „Hier, in der Sklaverei, existirte eine gewaltsame Methode\n die Menschheit arbeitsam [für die Nichtarbeiter] zu machen … Die Menschen\n wurden damals zur Arbeit [d. h. zur Gratisarbeit für Andere] gezwungen, weil sie\nSklaven von andern waren; die Menschen sind jetzt zur Arbeit [d. h. zur\n Gratisarbeit für Nichtarbeiter] gezwungen, weil sie die Sklaven ihrer eignen\n Bedürfnisse sind“, so schliesst er desswegen nicht, wie der fette Pfründner,\n dass — die Lohnarbeiter stets am Hungertuch nagen sollen. Er will umgekehrt\n ihre Bedürfnisse vermehren und die wachsende Zahl ihrer Bedürfnisse zugleich zum\n Sporn ihrer Arbeit für „die Delikateren“ machen.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":143,"address":"20.143"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0144","text":"„Der Fortschritt des gesellschaft-\n lichen Reichthums“, sagt Storch, „erzeugt jene nützliche\n Klasse der Gesellschaft … welche die langweiligsten, gemeinsten\n und ekelhaftesten Beschäftigungen ausübt, in einem Wort alles, was das\n Leben unangenehmes und knechtendes hat, auf ihre Schultern nimmt und\n eben dadurch den andern Klassen die Zeit, die Heiterkeit des Geistes\n und die konventionelle (c’est bon!) Charakterwürde ver-\n schafft etc.“ [Note: Storch l. c. t. III, p. 223.] . Storch fragt sich, welches denn eigentlich der Vorzug\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"635\", \"facs\": \"#f0654\"} --> dieser kapitalistischen Civilisation mit ihrem Elend und ihrer Degradation der\n Massen vor der Barbarei? Er findet nur eine Antwort — die Sicher-\n heit! „Durch den Fortschritt der Industrie und Wissenschaft,“ sagt\nSismondi, „kann jeder Arbeiter jeden Tag viel mehr produciren als er\n zu seinem Konsum braucht.","kind":"passage","sequence":144,"address":"20.144"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0145","text":"Aber zu gleicher Zeit, während seine Arbeit\n den Reichthum producirt, würde der Reichthum, wäre er berufen ihn selbst\n zu konsumiren, ihn wenig geeignet zur Arbeit machen“ [Note: Sismondi l. c. t. I, p. 85.] . „Die armen\n Nationen,“ sagt Destutt de Tracy, „sind die, wo das Volk gut dran\n ist, und die reichen Nationen sind die, wo es gewöhnlich arm ist“ [Note: Destutt de Tracy l. c. p. 231: „Les nations pauvres, c’est là où\n le peuple est à son aise; et les nations riches, c’est là où il est ordinairement\n pauvre.“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":145,"address":"20.145"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0146","text":"Keine Periode der modernen Gesellschaft ist so günstig für das Stu-\n dium der kapitalistischen Accumulation als die Periode der letztver-\n flossenen 20 Jahre. Es ist als ob sie den Fortunatussäckel gefunden\n hätte. Von allen Ländern aber bietet England wieder das klassische Bei-\n spiel, weil es den ersten Rang auf dem Weltmarkt behauptet, die kapita-\n listische Produktionsweise hier allein völlig entwickelt ist, und endlich die\n Einführung des tausendjährigen Reichs des Freihandels seit 1846 der\n Vulgärökonomie den letzten Schlupfwinkel abgeschnitten hat. Der tita-\n nische Fortschritt der Produktion, so dass die letzte Hälfte der zwanzig-\n jährigen Periode die erste wieder weit überflügelt, ward bereits im vierten\n Kapitel hinreichend angedeutet.","kind":"passage","sequence":146,"address":"20.146"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0147","text":"Obgleich das absolute Wachsthum der englischen Bevölkerung im\n letzten halben Jahrhundert sehr gross war, fiel das verhältnissmäs-\n sige Wachsthum oder die Rate des Zuwachses fortwäh-\n rend, wie folgende dem officiellen Census entlehnte Tabelle zeigt:\nJährlicher procentmässiger Zuwachs der Bevölkerung\n von England und Wales in Decimalzahlen.","kind":"passage","sequence":147,"address":"20.147"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0148","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"636\", \"facs\": \"#f0655\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":148,"address":"20.148"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0149","text":"Betrachten wir nun andrerseits das Wachsthum des Reichthums.\n Den sichersten Anhaltspunkt bietet hier die Bewegung der der Einkommen-\n steuer unterworfenen Profite, Grundrenten u. s. w. Der Zuwachs der steuer-\n pflichtigen Profite (Pächter und einige andre Rubriken nicht eingeschlos-\n sen) betrug für Grossbritanien von 1853—1864 50.47 % (oder 4.58 % im\n jährlichen Durchschnitt) [Note: „Tenth Report of the Commissioners of H. M.’s Inland\n Revenue. Lond. 1866“, p. 38.] , der der Bevölkerung während der-\n selben Periode ungefähr 12 %. Die Zunahme der besteuerbaren Ren-\n ten von Land (Häuser, Eisenbahnen, Minen, Fischereien u. s. w. einge-\n schlossen) betrug von 1853—1864 38 %, oder 3\\frac{5}{12} % jährlich, woran fol-\n gende Rubriken den stärksten Antheil nahmen:","kind":"passage","sequence":149,"address":"20.149"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0150","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nVergleicht man je vier Jahre der Periode von 1853—1864, so\n wächst der Zunahmegrad der Einkommen fortwährend. Er ist z. B.\n für die aus Profit stammenden von 1853—1857 jährlich 1.73 %,\n 1857—1861 jährlich 2.74 %, und 9.30 % jährlich für 1861—1864.\n Die Gesammtsumme der der Einkommensteuer unterworfenen Einkommen\n des Vereinigten Königreichs betrug 1856: 307,068,898 Pfd. St., 1859:\n 328,127,416 Pfd. St., 1862: 351,745,241 Pfd. St., 1863: 359,142,897\n Pfd. St., 1864: 362,462,279 Pfd. St., 1865: 385,530,020 Pfd. St. [Note: Diese Zahlen sind hinreichend für die Vergleichung, aber, absolut be-\n trachtet, falsch, da vielleicht 100 Millionen Pfd. St. Einkommen jährlich „ver-\n schwiegen“ werden. Die Klage der Commissioners of the Inland Revenue über\n systematischen Betrug, namentlich von kommercieller und industrieller Seite,\n wiederholt sich in jedem ihrer Berichte. So heisst es z.","kind":"passage","sequence":150,"address":"20.150"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0151","text":"B.: „Eine Aktiengesell-\n schaft gab ihre besteuerbaren Profite auf 6000 Pfd. St. an, der Taxator veran-] .","kind":"passage","sequence":151,"address":"20.151"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0152","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"637\", \"facs\": \"#f0656\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":152,"address":"20.152"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0153","text":"Die Accumulation des Kapitals war zugleich von seiner Kon-\n centration begleitet. Obgleich keine officielle Agrikulturstatistik für Eng-\n land (wohl aber für Irland) existirt, wurde sie von 10 Grafschaften freiwillig\n geliefert. Sie ergab hier das Resultat, dass von 1851 bis 1861 die\n Pachten unter 100 Acres von 31,583 auf 26,567 vermindert, also 5,016\n mit grösseren Pachten zusammengeschlagen waren [Note: Census etc. l. c. p. 29. Bright’s Behauptung, dass 150 Landlords die\n Hälfte des englischen und 12 die Hälfte des schottischen Bodens eignen, ist nicht\n widerlegt worden.] . Von 1815 bis\n 1825 fiel kein Mobiliarvermögen über 1 Million Pfd. St. unter die Erb-\n schaftssteuer, von 1825 bis 1855 dagegen 8, von 1856 bis Juni 1859,\n d. h. in 4½ Jahren, 4 [Note: „Fourth Report etc. of Inland Revenue. Lond. 1860“, p. 17.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":153,"address":"20.153"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0154","text":"Die Koncentration wird man jedoch am besten\n ersehn aus einer kurzen Analyse der Einkommensteuer für Rubrik D\n (Profite mit Ausschluss von Pächtern u. s. w.) in den Jahren 1864 und\n 1865. Ich bemerke vorher, dass Einkommen aus dieser Quelle bis zu\n 60 Pfd. St. hinab Income Taxe zahlen. Diese steuerpflichtigen Ein-\n kommen betrugen in England, Wales und Schottland 1864: 95,844,222\n Pfd. St. und 1865: 105,435,579 Pfd. St. [Note: Es sind diess die Reineinkommen, also nach gewissen gesetzlich gültigen\n Abzügen.] , die Zahl der Be-\n steuerten 1864 : 308,416 Personen auf eine Gesammtbevölkerung von\n 23,891,009, 1865 : 332,431 Personen auf Gesammtbevölkerung von\n 24,127,003. Ueber die Vertheilung dieser Einkommen in beiden Jahren\n folgende Tabelle:","kind":"passage","sequence":154,"address":"20.154"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0155","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n [Note: schlagte sie zu 88,000 Pfd. St., und für diese Summe ward schliesslich die Steuer\n gezahlt. Eine andre Kompagnie gab 190,000 Pfd. St. an, sie ward gezwungen zu\n gestehn, dass der wirkliche Betrag 250,000 Pfd. St.“ (l. c. p. 42.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":155,"address":"20.155"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0156","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"638\", \"facs\": \"#f0657\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":156,"address":"20.156"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0157","text":"Es wurden im Vereinigten Königreich 1855 producirt 61,453,079\n Tonnen Kohlen zum Werth von 16,113,267 Pfd. St., 1864 :\n 92,787,873 Tonnen zum Werth von 23,197,968 Pfd. St., 1855 :\n 3,218,154 Tonnen Roheisen zum Werth von 8,045,385 Pfd. St.,\n1864: 4,767,951 Tonnen zum Werth von 11,919,877 Pfd. St.\n1854 betrug die Länge der im Vereinigten Königreich eröffneten Eisen-\n bahnen 8054 Meilen, mit aufgezahltem Kapital von 286,068,794 Pfd. St.,\n1864 die Meilenlänge 12,789 mit aufgezahltem Kapital von 425,719,613\n Pfd. St. 1854 betrug Gesammtexport und Import des Vereinigten\n Königreichs 268,210,145 Pfd. St., 1865: 489,923,285. Folgende\n Tabelle zeigt die Bewegung des Exports:","kind":"passage","sequence":157,"address":"20.157"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0158","text":"1846 58,842,377 Pfd. St.","kind":"passage","sequence":158,"address":"20.158"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0159","text":"1849 63,596,052 „ „","kind":"passage","sequence":159,"address":"20.159"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0160","text":"1856 115,826,948 „ „","kind":"passage","sequence":160,"address":"20.160"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0161","text":"1860 135,842,817 „ „","kind":"passage","sequence":161,"address":"20.161"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0162","text":"1865 165,862,402 „ „","kind":"passage","sequence":162,"address":"20.162"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0163","text":"1866 ungefähr 190,000,000 „ „  [Note: In diesem Augenblick, März 1867, ist der indisch-chinesische Markt\n durch die Konsignationen der britischen Baumwollfabrikanten schon wieder völlig\n überführt. Lohnherabsetzung um 5 % begann unter den Baumwollarbeitern 1866,\n jetzt in Folge ähnlicher Operation Strike von 20,000 Mann in Preston.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":163,"address":"20.163"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0164","text":"Man begreift, nach diesen wenigen Angaben, den Triumphschrei des\nGeneralregistrators des brit. Volks: „Rasch wie die Bevölkerung\n anwuchs, hat sie nicht Schritt gehalten mit dem Fortschritt der\n Industrie und des Reichthums“ [Note: Census etc. l. c. p. 11.] . Wenden wir uns jetzt zu den\n unmittelbaren Agenten dieser Industrie oder den Producenten dieses Reich-\n thums, zur Arbeiterklasse. „Es ist einer der melancholischsten Charak-\n terzüge im socialen Zustand des Landes“, sagt Gladstone, „dass mit einer\n Abnahme in der Konsumtionsmacht des Volks und einer Zunahme in den\n Entbehrungen und dem Elend der arbeitenden Klasse, gleichzeitig eine\n beständige Accumulation von Reichthum in den höheren Klassen und ein\n beständiger Anwachs von Kapital stattfinden“ [Note: Gladstone im Hause der Gemeinen, 14. Febr.","kind":"passage","sequence":164,"address":"20.164"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0165","text":"1843: „It is one of\n the most melancholy features in the social state of the country, that while there\n was a decrease in the consuming power of the people, and an increase in the pri-] . Der berühmte Mini-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"639\", \"facs\": \"#f0658\"} --> ster erklärte diess dem Hause der Gemeinen am 14. Februar 1843. Am\n 16. April 1863, zwanzig Jahre später, in der Rede, worin er sein Budget\n vorlegt: „Von 1842 bis 1852 wuchs das besteuerbare Einkommen dieses\n Landes um 6 % … In den 8 Jahren von 1853 bis 1861 wuchs es, wenn\n wir von der Basis von 1853 ausgehn, um 20 %.","kind":"passage","sequence":165,"address":"20.165"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0166","text":"Die Thatsache ist so\n erstaunlich, dass sie beinahe unglaublich ist … Diese berauschende\n Vermehrung von Reichthum und Macht ist ganz und gar auf\n die Klassen des Eigenthums beschränkt, aber … aber, sie\n muss von indirektem Vortheil für die Arbeiterbevölkerung sein,\n weil sie die Artikel der allgemeinen Konsumtion verwohlfeilert —\n während die Reichen reicher, sind die Armen jedenfalls weniger arm ge-\n worden. Dass die Extreme der Armuth sich verändert\n haben, wage ich nicht zu sagen“ [Note: „From 1842 to 1852 the taxable income of the country increased by 6\n per cent … In the 8 years from 1853 to 1861, it had increased from the basis\n taken in 1853, 20 per cent!","kind":"passage","sequence":166,"address":"20.166"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0167","text":"The fact is so astonishing as to be almost incredible\n … This intoxicating augmentation of wealth and power is entirely confined to\n classes of property, but must be of indirect benefit to the labouring population,\n because it cheapens the commodities of general consumption — while the rich have\n been growing richer the poor have been growing less poor! at any rate, whether\n the extremes of poverty are less, I do not presume to say.“ Gladstone im\n H. o. C. 16. April 1863.] . Welch lahmer Antiklimax!\n Wenn die Arbeiterklasse „arm“ geblieben ist, nur „weniger arm“ im Ver-\n haltniss, worin sie eine „berauschende Vermehrung von Reichthum und\n Macht“ für die Klasse des Eigenthums producirte, so ist sie relativ gleich\n arm geblieben. Wenn die Extreme der Armuth sich nicht vermin-\n dert haben, haben sie sich vermehrt, weil die Extreme des Reichthums.","kind":"passage","sequence":167,"address":"20.167"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0168","text":"Was die Verwohlfeilerung der Lebensmittel betrifft, so zeigt die\n officielle Statistik, z. B. die Angaben des London Orphan Asylum,\n eine Vertheurung von 20 % für den Durchschnitt der drei Jahre von\n 1860—1862, verglichen mit 1851—1853. In den folgenden 3 Jahren\n 1863—1865 progressive Vertheurung von Fleisch, Butter, Milch, Zucker,\n [Note: vations and distress of the labouring class and operatives, there was at the same\n time a constant accumulation of wealth in the upper classes and a constant in-\n crease of capital.“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"640\", \"facs\": \"#f0659\"} --> Salz, Kohlen und einer Masse andrer nothwendiger Lebensmittel [Note: Sieh die officiellen Angaben in dem Blaubuch: „Miscellaneous\n Statistics of the Un. Kingdom. Part VI. Lond. 1866“, p. 260—273\npassim.] .\n Gladstone’s folgende Budgetrede, vom 7.","kind":"passage","sequence":168,"address":"20.168"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0169","text":"April 1864, ist ein pindarischer\n Dithyrambus auf den Fortschritt der Plusmacherei und das durch „Ar-\n muth“ gemässigte Glück des Volks. Er spricht von Massen „am Rand\n des Pauperismus“, von den Geschäftszweigen, „worin der Lohn nicht\n gestiegen“, und fasst schliesslich das Glück der Arbeiterklasse zusammen\n in den Worten: „das menschliche Leben ist in neun Fällen von 10 ein\n blosser Kampf um die Existenz“ [Note: „Think of those who are on the border of that region (pauperism)“,\n „wages … in others not increased … human life is but, in nine cases out of\n ten, a struggle for existence.“ (Gladstone, H. o. C. 7. April 1864.) Die\n fortlaufenden, schreienden Widersprüche in Gladstone’s Budgetreden von 1863\n und 1864 charakterisirt ein englischer Schriftsteller durch folgendes Citat aus\n Moliere:\n„Voilà l’homme en effet. Il va du blanc au noir.\n Il condamne au matin ses sentimens du soir.","kind":"passage","sequence":169,"address":"20.169"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0170","text":"Importun à tout autre, à soi même incommode,\n Il change à tous momens d’esprit comme de mode.“\n („The Theory of Exchanges etc. Lond. 1864“, p. 135.)] . Professor Fawcett, nicht wie\n Gladstone durch officielle Rücksicht gebunden, erklärt rund heraus: „Ich\n läugne natürlich nicht, dass der Geldlohn mit dieser Vermehrung des Ka-\n pitals (in den letzten Decennien) gestiegen ist, aber dieser scheinbare Vor-\n theil geht in grossem Umfang wieder verloren, weil viele Lebensnothwen-\n digkeiten beständig theurer werden (er glaubt, wegen Werthfall der edlen\n Metalle) … Die Reichen werden rasch reicher („the rich grow rapidly\n richer“), während keine Zunahme im Komfort der arbeitenden Klassen\n wahrnehmbar ist … Die Arbeiter werden fast Sklaven der Krämer,\n deren Schuldner sie sind“ [Note: H. Fawcett l. c. p. 67, 82.","kind":"passage","sequence":170,"address":"20.170"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0171","text":"Was die wachsende Abhängigkeit der\n Arbeiter von dem Krämer betrifft, so ist sie Folge der zunehmenden Schwankungen\n und Unterbrechungen ihrer Beschäftigung.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":171,"address":"20.171"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0172","text":"Der Leser hat in den Abschnitten über den „Arbeitstag“ und die „Ma-\n schinerie“ die Bedingungen kennen gelernt, unter welchen die britische\n Arbeiterklasse während der letzten Decennien die „berauschende Ver-\n mehrung von Reichthum und Macht“ für die Klassen des Eigenthums pro-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"641\", \"facs\": \"#f0660\"} --> ducirt hat. Jedoch beschäftigte uns damals vorzugsweise der Arbeiter\ninnerhalb des Produktionsprozesses selbst. Zur gründlichen Einsicht\n in das Gesetz der kapitalistischen Accumulation ist es nöthig einen Augen-\n blick bei der Lage des Arbeiters ausserhalb jenes Prozesses, bei seinen\n Nahrungs- und Wohnungszuständen zu verweilen.","kind":"passage","sequence":172,"address":"20.172"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0173","text":"Die Grenze dieses\n Buchs gebietet mir hier namentlich den schlechtbezahlten Theil der\n industriellen Arbeiter und den Agrikulturarbeiter ins Auge zu fassen,\n welche zusammen die Majorität der Arbeiterklasse bilden [Note: Es steht zu hoffen, dass F. Engels bald sein Werk über die Lage der\n englischen Arbeiterklasse mit der Periode seit 1844 bereichern, oder auch die Dar-\n stellung der letztern Periode in einem besondern zweiten Band herausgeben wird.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":173,"address":"20.173"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0174","text":"Vorher noch ein Wort über den officiellen Pauperismus,\n d. h. den Theil der Arbeiterklasse, der seine Existenzbedingung, Verkauf\n der Arbeitskraft, eingebüsst hat und von öffentlichem Almosen vegetirt.\n Die officielle Pauperliste zählte in England [Note: In England ist immer Wales eingeschlossen, in Grossbritanien\n England, Wales und Schottland, im Vereinigten Königreich jene drei\n Länder und Irland.] 1855: 851,369 Per-\n sonen, 1856: 877,767, 1865: 971,433. In Folge der Baumwoll-\n noth schwoll sie in den Jahren 1863 und 1864 zu 1,079,382 und\n 1,014,978. Die Krise von 1866, die London am schwersten traf, schuf\n in diesem Sitz des Weltmarkts, einwohnerreicher als das Königreich Schott-\n land, für 1866 einen Pauperzuwachs um 19.5 %, verglichen mit 1865,\n und um 24.4 %, verglichen mit 1864, einen noch grössern Zu-\n wachs für die ersten Monate von 1867, verglichen mit 1866.","kind":"passage","sequence":174,"address":"20.174"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0175","text":"Bei\n Analyse der Pauperstatistik sind zwei Punkte hervorzuheben. Einerseits\n spiegelt die Bewegung im Ab und Zu der Paupermasse die Perioden-\n wechsel des industriellen Cyklus wieder. Andrerseits wird die offi-\n cielle Pauperstatistik ein mehr und mehr trüglicher Index des wirk-\n lichen Pauperismus im Grad, worin mit der Accumulation des Kapitals der\nKlassenkampf und daher das Selbstgefühl der Arbeiter sich entwickeln.\n Z. B. die Barbarei in der Behandlung der Paupers, worüber die englische\n Presse (Times, Pall Mall Gazette u. s. w.) während der letzten\n zwei Jahre so laut schrie, ist alten Datums. F. Engels konstatirt\n 1844 ganz dieselben Greuel und ganz dasselbe vorübergehende, zur „Sen-\n [Running matter: I. 41] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"642\", \"facs\": \"#f0661\"} --> sationliteratur“ gehörige Geschrei.","kind":"passage","sequence":175,"address":"20.175"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0176","text":"Aber die furchtbare Zunahme des\n Hungertods („deaths of starvation“) in London, während des letzten De-\n cenniums, beweist unbedingt den zunehmenden Abscheu der Arbeiter vor\n der Sklaverei des Workhouse, dieser Strafanstalt des Elends.","kind":"passage","sequence":176,"address":"20.176"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0177","text":"Wenden wir uns jetzt zu den schlechtbezahlten Schichten der indu-\n striellen Arbeiterklasse. Während der Baumwollnoth, 1862, wurde\nDr. Smith vom Privy Council mit einer Untersuchung über den\n Nahrungsstand der verkümmernden Baumwollarbeiter in Lancashire und\n Cheshire beauftragt.","kind":"passage","sequence":177,"address":"20.177"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0178","text":"Langjährige frühere Beobachtung hatte ihn zum\n Resultat geführt, dass „um Hungerkrankheiten („starvation\n diseases“) zu vermeiden“, die tägliche Nahrung eines Durchschnitts-\n Frauenzimmers mindestens 3,900 Gran Kohlenstoff mit 180 Gran Stick-\n stoff enthalten müsse, die tägliche Nahrung eines Durchschnitts-Mannes\n mindestens 4,300 Gran Kohlenstoff mit 200 Gran Stickstoff, für die\n Frauenzimmer ungefähr so viel Nahrungsstoff als in zwei Pfund gutem Wei-\n zenbrod enthalten ist, für Männer ⅑ mehr, für den Wochendurchschnitt\n von weiblichen und männlichen Erwachsnen, mindestens 28,600 Gran\n Kohlenstoff und 1330 Gran Stickstoff. Seine Berechnung ward in über-\n raschender Weise praktisch dadurch bestätigt, dass sie im Ganzen über-\n einstimmte mit der kümmerlichen Nahrungsmenge, worauf der Nothstand\n die Konsumtion der Baumwollarbeiter herabgedrückt hatte.","kind":"passage","sequence":178,"address":"20.178"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0179","text":"Sie erhielten\n im December 1862: 29,211 Gran Kohlenstoff und 1295 Gran Stickstoff\n wöchentlich.","kind":"passage","sequence":179,"address":"20.179"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0180","text":"Im Jahre 1863 verordnete der Privy Council eine Untersuchung\n über den Nothstand des schlechtgenährtesten Theils der englischen Arbei-\n terklasse. Dr. Simon, der ärztliche Beamte des Privy Council, er-\n kieste zu dieser Arbeit den obenerwähnten Dr. Smith. Seine Unter-\n suchung erstreckt sich auf die Agrikulturarbeiter einerseits, andrerseits\n auf Seidenweber, Nätherinnen, Lederhandschuhmacher, Strumpfwirker,\n Handschuhweber und Schuster. Die letzteren Kategorieen sind, mit Aus-\n nahme der Strumpfwirker, ausschliesslich städtisch. Es wurde zur Regel der\n Untersuchung gemacht, die gesundesten und relativ bestgestellten Familien\n in jeder Kategorie auszuwählen.","kind":"passage","sequence":180,"address":"20.180"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0181","text":"Als allgemeines Resultat ergab sich, dass „nur in einer der unter-\n suchten Klassen der städtischen Arbeiter die Zufuhr von Stickstoff das\n absolute Minimalmass, unter welchem Hungerkrankheiten eintreten,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"643\", \"facs\": \"#f0662\"} --> ein wenig überschritt, dass in zwei Klassen Mangel, und zwar in der\n einen sehr grosser Mangel, sowohl an der Zufuhr von stickstoff- als\n kohlenstoffhaltiger Nahrung stattfand, dass von den untersuchten Acker-\n baufamilien mehr als ein Fünftheil weniger als die unentbehrliche Zufuhr\n von kohlenstoffhaltiger Nahrung erhielt, mehr als ⅓ weniger als die un-\n entbehrliche Zufuhr stickstoffhaltiger Nahrung, und dass in drei Grafschaf-\n ten (Berkshire, Oxfordshire und Somersetshire) Mangel an dem Minimum\n der stickstoffhaltigen Nahrung durchschnittlich herrschte“ [Note: „Public Health. Sixth Report etc. for 1863.“ Lond. 1864,\n p. 13.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":181,"address":"20.181"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0182","text":"Unter den\n Agrikulturarbeitern waren die von England, dem reichsten Theile des Ver-\n einigten Königreichs, die schlechtest genährten [Note: l. c. p 17.] . Die Unternahrung\n fiel unter den Landarbeitern überhaupt hauptsächlich auf Frau und Kin-\n der, denn „der Mann muss essen, um sein Werk zu verrichten“. Noch\n grösserer Mangel wüthete unter den untersuchten städtischen Arbeiter-\n kategorieen. „Sie sind so schlecht genährt, dass viele Fälle grausamer\n und gesundheitsruinirender Entbehrung („Entsagung“ des Kapita-\n listen alles diess! nämlich Entsagung auf Zahlung der zur blossen\n Vegetation seiner Hände unentbehrlichen Lebensmittel!) vorkommen\n müssen“ [Note: l. c. p. 13.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":182,"address":"20.182"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0183","text":"Folgende Tabelle zeigt das Verhältniss des Nahrungsstandes der oben\n erwähnten rein städtischen Arbeiterkategorieen zu dem von Dr. Smith\n angenommenen Minimalmass und zum Nahrungsmass der Baumwollarbeiter\n während der Zeit ihrer grössten Noth:","kind":"passage","sequence":183,"address":"20.183"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0184","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEine Hälfte, \\frac{60}{125}, der untersuchten industriellen Arbeiterkategorieen\n [Running matter: 41*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"644\", \"facs\": \"#f0663\"} --> erhielt absolut kein Bier, 28 % keine Milch. Der Wochendurchschnitt\n der flüssigen Nahrungsmittel in den Familien schwankte von 7 Unzen bei\n den Nätherinnen auf 24¾ Unzen bei den Strumpfwirkern. Die Mehrzahl\n derer, die keine Milch erhielten, bestand aus den Nätherinnen von\n London. Die Quantität der wöchentlich konsumirten Brodstoffe wechselte\n von 7¾ Pfund bei den Nätherinnen zu 11¼ Pfund bei den Schustern\n und ergab einen Totaldurchschnitt von 9.9 Pfund wöchentlich auf den Er-\n wachsenen. Zucker (Syrup u. s. w.) wechselte von 4 Unzen wöchentlich\n für die Lederhandschuhmacher auf 11 Unzen für Strumpfwirker; der Total-\n durchschnitt per Woche für alle Kategorieen, per Erwachsenen, 8 Unzen.","kind":"passage","sequence":184,"address":"20.184"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0185","text":"Gesammter Wochendurchschnitt von Butter (Fett u. s. w.) 5 Unzen per\n Erwachsenen. Der Wochendurchschnitt von Fleisch (Speck u. s. w.)\n schwankte, per Erwachsenen, von 7¼ Unzen bei den Seidenwebern auf 18¼\n Unzen bei den Lederhandschuhmachern; Gesammtdurchschnitt für die\n verschiedenen Kategorieen 13.6 Unzen. Die wöchentliche Kost\n für Nahrung per Erwachsenen ergab folgende allgemeine Durch-\n schnittszahlen: Seidenweber 2 sh. 2½ d., Nätherinnen 2 sh.\n 7 d., Lederhandschuhmacher 2 sh. 9½ d., Schuster 2 sh.\n 7¾ d., Strumpfwirker 2 sh. 6¼ d. Für die Seidenweber von\n Macclesfield betrug der Wochendurchschnitt nur 1 sh. 8½ d. Die\n schlecht genährtesten Kategorieen waren die Nätherinnen, die Seidenweber\n und die Lederhandschuhmacher [Note: l. c. p. 232, 233.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":185,"address":"20.185"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0186","text":"Dr. Simon sagt in seinem allgemeinen Gesundheitsbericht über\n diesen Nahrungszustand: „Dass die Fälle zahllos sind, worin Nah-\n rungsmangel Krankheiten erzeugt oder erschwert, wird Jeder bestä-\n tigen, der mit medizinischer Armenpraxis oder mit den Patienten der\n Spitäler, seien sie Insassen oder ausserhalb wohnend, vertraut ist …\n Jedoch kommen hier vom sanitären Standpunkt noch andre, sehr entschei-\n dende Umstände hinzu … Man muss sich erinnern, dass Beraubung an\n Nahrungsmitteln nur sehr widerstrebsam ertragen wird, und dass in der\n Regel grosse Dürftigkeit der Diät nur im Gefolge andrer, vorhergegangner\n Entbehrungen nachhinkt.","kind":"passage","sequence":186,"address":"20.186"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0187","text":"Lange bevor der Nahrungsmangel hygienisch\n ins Gewicht fällt, lange bevor der Physiolog daran denkt, die Grane Stick-\n stoff und Kohlenstoff zu zählen, zwischen denen Leben und Hungertod\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"645\", \"facs\": \"#f0664\"} --> schwebt, wird der Haushalt von allem materiellen Komfort ganz und gar\n entblösst sein. Kleidung und Heizung werden noch dürftiger gewesen\n sein als die Speise. Kein hinreichender Schutz wider die Härte des Wet-\n ters; Abknappung des Wohnraums zu einem Grad, der Krankheiten er-\n zeugt oder erschwert; kaum eine Spur von Hausgeräth oder Möbeln; die\n Reinlichkeit selbst wird kostspielig oder schwierig geworden sein. Werden\n noch aus Selbstachtung Versuche gemacht, sie aufrecht zu erhalten, so\n repräsentirt jeder solcher Versuch zuschüssige Hungerpein.","kind":"passage","sequence":187,"address":"20.187"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0188","text":"Die Häus-\n lichkeit wird dort sein, wo Obdach am wohlfeilsten kaufbar; in Quartieren,\n wo die Gesundheitspolizei die geringste Frucht trägt, das jämmerlichste\n Gerinne, wenigster Verkehr, der meiste öffentliche Unrath, kümmerlichste\n oder schlechteste Wasserzufuhr, und, in Städten, grösster Mangel an Licht\n und Luft. Diess sind die Gesundheitsgefahren, denen die Armuth unver-\n meidlich ausgesetzt ist, wenn diese Armuth Nahrungsmangel einschliesst.\n Wenn die Summe dieser Uebel von furchtbarer Grösse für das Leben ist,\n so ist der blosse Nahrungsmangel an sich selbst entsetzlich … Diess sind\n qualvolle Gedanken, namentlich wenn man sich erinnert, dass die Armuth,\n wovon es sich handelt, nicht die selbstverschuldete Armuth des Müssig-\n gangs ist. Es ist die Armuth von Arbeitern.","kind":"passage","sequence":188,"address":"20.188"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0189","text":"Ja, mit Bezug auf die\n städtischen Arbeiter, ist die Arbeit, wodurch der knappe Bissen Nahrung\n erkauft wird, meist über alles Mass verlängert. Und dennoch kann man\n nur in sehr bedingtem Sinn sagen, dass diese Arbeit selbsterhaltend ist.\n … Auf sehr grossem Massstab kann der nominelle Selbsterhalt nur ein\n kürzerer oder längerer Umweg zum Pauperismus sein“ [Note: l. c. p. 15.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":189,"address":"20.189"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0190","text":"Das innere Band zwischen Hungerpein der fleissigsten Arbeiter-\n schichten und kapitalistischer Accumulation, nebst dem sie begleitenden\n groben oder raffinirten Ueberkonsum der Reichen, diess Band, sage ich,\n sieht nur der Kenner der ökonomischen Gesetze. Anders mit dem Woh-\n nungszustand. Jeder unbefangne Beobachter sieht, dass je mas-\n senhafter die Koncentration der Produktionsmittel, desto grösser die\n entsprechende Agglomeration von Arbeitern auf geringem Raum,\n dass daher je rascher die kapitalistische Accumulation, desto elender der\n Wohnungszustand der Arbeiter. Jeder sieht, dass die das Wachsthum\n des Reichthums begleitenden Verbesserungen der Städte (improve-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"646\", \"facs\": \"#f0665\"} --> ments) durch Niederreissung der schlechtgebauten Viertel, Errichtung von\n Palästen für Banken, Waarenhäuser u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":190,"address":"20.190"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0191","text":"w., Dehnung der Strassen für\n Geschäftsverkehr und Luxuskarossen, Einführung städtischer Eisenbahnen\n u. s. w. mit Verjagung der Armen in stets schlechtere und dichter gefüllte\n Schlupfwinkel verknüpft sind. Andrerseits weiss jeder, dass die Theuer-\n keit der Wohnungen im umgekehrten Verhältniss zu ihrer Güte steht und\n dass die Minen des Elends von Spekulanten mit mehr Profit und weniger\n Kosten ausgebeutet werden als jemals die Minen von Potosi. Der antago-\n nistische Charakter der kapitalistischen Accumulation und daher der ka-\n pitalistischen Eigenthumsverhältnisse überhaupt [Note: „Nirgendwo sind so offen und so schamlos die Rechte der Person dem\n Recht des Eigenthums geopfert worden, als in den Wohnungsverhältnissen der ar-\n beitenden Klasse.","kind":"passage","sequence":191,"address":"20.191"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0192","text":"Jede grosse Stadt ist eine Stätte des Menschenopfers, ein Altar,\n worauf Tausende jährlich dem Moloch der Habsucht geschlachtet werden.“\n (S. Laing l. c. p. 150.)]  wird\n hier so handgreifbar, dass selbst die officiellen englischen Berichte über\n diesen Gegenstand voll heterodoxer Ausfälle auf das „Eigenthum und\n seine Rechte sind“. Das Uebel hielt solchen Schritt mit der Entwicklung der\n Industrie, der Accumulation des Kapitals, dem Wachsthum und der „Ver-\n schönerung“ der Städte, dass die blosse Furcht vor ansteckenden Krank-\n heiten, welche auch der „Ehrbarkeit“ nicht schonen, von 1847 bis 1864\n nicht weniger als 10 gesundheitspolizeiliche Parlamentsakte ins Leben\n rief, und dass die erschreckte Bürgerschaft in einigen Städten wie Liver-\n pool, Glasgow u. s. w. durch ihre Municipalitäten eingriff. Dennoch, ruft\nDr.","kind":"passage","sequence":192,"address":"20.192"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0193","text":"Simon in seinem Bericht von 1865: „Allgemein zu sprechen, sind\n die Uebelstände in England unkontrolirt.“ Auf Befehl des Privy Coun-\n cil fand 1864 Untersuchung über die Wohnungsverhältnisse der Land-\n arbeiter, 1865 über die der ärmeren Klassen in den Städten statt. Die\n meisterhaften Arbeiten des Dr. Julian Hunter findet man im sie-\n benten (1865) und achten (1866) Bericht über „Public Health“. Auf\n die Landarbeiter komme ich später. Ueber den städtischen Wohnungs-\n zustand schicke ich eine allgemeine Bemerkung des Dr. Simon voraus:\n „Obgleich mein officieller Gesichtspunkt“, sagt er, „ausschliesslich\n physisch ist, erlaubt die gewöhnlichste Humanität nicht die andre Seite\n dieses Uebels zu ignoriren.","kind":"passage","sequence":193,"address":"20.193"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0194","text":"In seinem höheren Grad bedingt es fast noth-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"647\", \"facs\": \"#f0666\"} --> wendig eine solche Negation aller Delikatesse, so schmutzige Konfusion\n von Körpern und körperlichen Verrichtungen, solche Blossstellung ge-\n schlechtlicher Nacktheit, die bestial, nicht menschlich sind. Diesen Ein-\n flüssen unterworfen zu sein ist eine Degradation, die sich vertieft, je länger\n sie fortwirkt. Für die Kinder, die unter diesem Fluch geboren sind, ist er\nTaufe in Infamie („baptism into infamy“). Und über alles\n Mass hoffnungslos ist der Wunsch, dass unter solche Umstände gestellte\n Personen in andern Rücksichten nach jener Atmosphäre der Civilisation\n aufstreben sollten, die ihr Wesen in physischer und moralischer Reinheit\n besitzt“ [Note: „Public Health. Eight Report. Lond. 1866“, p. 14, Note.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":194,"address":"20.194"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0195","text":"Den ersten Rang in überfüllten oder auch für menschliche Behausung\n absolut unmöglichen Wohnlichkeiten nimmt London ein. „Zwei Punkte“,\n sagt Dr. Hunter, „sind sicher; erstens, dass es ungefähr 20 grosse\n Kolonieen in London giebt, jede ungefähr 10,000 Personen stark, deren\n elende Lage alles übersteigt, was jemals anderswo in England gesehn\n worden ist, und sie ist fast ganz das Resultat ihrer schlechten Hausaccom-\n modation; und zweitens, dass der überfüllte und verfallene Zustand der\n Häuser dieser Kolonieen viel schlechter ist als 20 Jahre zu-\n vor“ [Note: l. c. p. 89. Mit Bezug auf die Kinder in diesen Kolonieen sagt\n Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":195,"address":"20.195"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0196","text":"Hunter: „Wir wissen nicht, wie Kinder vor diesem Zeitalter dichter Agglo-\n meration der Armen aufgebracht wurden, und er wäre ein kühner Prophet, der\n vorhersagen wollte, welches Betragen zu erwarten von Kindern, die unter Zu-\n ständen ohne Parallele in diesem Land jetzt ihre Erziehung für künftige Praxis als\ngefährliche Klassen durchmachen, indem sie die halbe Nacht aufsitzen mit\n Personen jeden Alters, trunken, obscön und zanksüchtig.“ (l. c. p. 56.)] . „Es ist nicht zu viel zu sagen, dass das Leben in vielen\n Theilen von London und Newcastle infernal ist“ [Note: l. c. p. 62.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":196,"address":"20.196"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0197","text":"Auch der besser gestellte Theil der Arbeiterklasse, zusammt\n Kleinkrämern und andern Elementen der kleinen Mittelklasse, fällt in\n London mehr und mehr unter den Fluch dieser nichtswürdigen Behausungsver-\n hältnisse, im Masse, wie die „Verbesserungen“ und mit ihnen die Niederreis-\n sung alter Strassen und Häuser fortschreiten, wie Fabriken und Menschenzu-\n strom in der Metropole wachsen, endlich die Hausmiethen mit der städtischen\n Grundrente steigen. „Die Hausmiethen sind so übermässig geworden, dass\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"648\", \"facs\": \"#f0667\"} --> wenige Arbeiter mehr als ein Zimmer zahlen können“ [Note: „Report of the Officer of Health of St. Martin’s in the\n Fields. 1865.“] . Es giebt fast kein\n Londoner Hauseigenthum, das nicht mit einer Unzahl von „middlemen“ be-\n lastet wäre.","kind":"passage","sequence":197,"address":"20.197"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0198","text":"Der Preis des Bodens in London steht nämlich stets sehr\n hoch im Vergleich zu seinen jährlichen Einkünften, indem jeder Käufer\n darauf spekulirt früher oder später zu einem Jury Price (durch Geschworne\n festgesetzte Taxe bei Expropriationen) wieder loszuschlagen oder durch\n Nähe irgend eines grossen Unternehmens ausserordentliche Wertherhöhung\n zu erschwindeln. Folge davon ist ein regelmässiger Handel im Ankauf\n von Miethkontrakten, die ihrem Verfall nahen. „Von den Gentlemen in\n diesem Geschäft kann man erwarten, dass sie handeln, wie sie handeln,\n so viel wie möglich aus den Hausbewohnern herausschlagen, und das Haus\n selbst in so schlechtem Zustand wie möglich ihren Nachfolgern über-\n lassen“ [Note: „Public Health. Eight Report. Lond. 1866“, p. 93.] . Die Miethen sind wöchentlich und die Herren laufen kein\n Risico.","kind":"passage","sequence":198,"address":"20.198"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0199","text":"In Folge der Eisenbahnbauten innerhalb der Stadt „sah man kürz-\n lich im Osten Londons eine Anzahl aus ihren alten Wohnungen verjagter\n Familien umherwandern eines Samstags Abends mit ihren wenigen welt-\n lichen Habseligkeiten auf dem Rücken, ohne irgend einen Haltplatz ausser\n dem Workhouse“ [Note: l. c. p. 83.] . Die Workhouses sind bereits überfüllt und die vom\n Parlament bereits bewilligten „improvements“ sind erst im Beginn ihrer\n Ausführung. Werden die Arbeiter verjagt durch Zerstörung ihrer alten\n Häuser, so verlassen sie nicht ihr Kirchspiel, oder siedeln sich höchstens an\n seiner Grenze, im nächsten fest. „Sie suchen natürlich möglichst in der\n Nähe ihrer Arbeitslokale zu hausen. Folge, dass an die Stelle von zwei Zim-\n mern, eins die Familie aufnehmen muss. Selbst zu erhöhter Miethe\n wird die Wohnlichkeit schlechter als die schlechte, woraus man sie ver-\n jagt.","kind":"passage","sequence":199,"address":"20.199"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0200","text":"Die Hälfte der Arbeiter im Strand braucht bereits zwei Meilen\n Reise zum Arbeitslokal.“ Dieser Strand, dessen Hauptstrasse auf den Frem-\n den einen imposanten Eindruck vom Reichthum Londons macht, kann als\n Beispiel der Londoner Menschenverpackung dienen. In einer Pfarrei des-\n selben zählte der Gesundheitsbeamte 581 Personen auf den Acre, obgleich\n die Hälfte der Themse mit eingemessen war. Es versteht sich von selbst,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"649\", \"facs\": \"#f0668\"} --> dass jede gesundheitspolizeiliche Massregel, die, wie das bisher in London\n der Fall, durch Niederschleifen untauglicher Häuser die Arbeiter aus einem\n Viertel verjagt, nur dazu dient sie in ein andres desto dichter zusammen\n zu drängen. „Entweder“, sagt Dr. Hunter, „muss die ganze Proce-\n dur als eine Abgeschmacktheit nothwendig zum Stillstand kommen, oder\n die öffentliche Sympathie(!)","kind":"passage","sequence":200,"address":"20.200"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0201","text":"muss erwachen für das, was man jetzt ohne\n Uebertreibung eine nationale Pflicht nennen kann, nämlich Obdach\n für Leute zu verschaffen, welche aus Mangel an Kapital sich selbst\n keins verschaffen können, obgleich sie durch periodische Zahlung die Ver-\n miether belohnen können“ [Note: l. c. p. 89.] . Man bewundre die kapitalistische Justiz!\n Der Grundeigenthümer, Hauseigner, Geschäftsmann, wenn expropriirt durch\n „improvements“, wie Eisenbahnen, Neubau der Strassen u. s. w., erhält\n nicht nur volle Entschädigung. Er muss für seine erzwungne „Ent-\n sagung“ von Gott und Rechtswegen obendrein noch durch einen erklecklichen\nProfit getröstet werden. Der Arbeiter wird mit Frau und Kind und Habe\n aufs Pflaster geworfen und — wenn er zu massenhaft nach Stadtvierteln\n drängt, wo die Municipalität auf Anstand hält, gesundheitspolizei-\n lich verfolgt!","kind":"passage","sequence":201,"address":"20.201"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0202","text":"Ausser London gab es Anfang des 19. Jahrhunderts keine einzige\n Stadt in England, die 100,000 Einwohner zählte. Nur 5 zählten mehr\n als 50,000. Jetzt existiren 28 Städte mit mehr als 50,000 Einwohnern.\n „Das Resultat dieses Wechsels war nicht nur enormer Zuwachs der städti-\n schen Bevölkerung, sondern die alten dichtgepackten kleinen Städte sind\n nun Centra, die von allen Seiten umbaut sind, nirgendwo mit freiem Luft-\n zutritt. Da sie für die Reichen nicht länger angenehm sind, werden sie von\n ihnen für die amüsanteren Vorstädte verlassen. Die Nachfolger dieser Rei-\n chen beziehn die grösseren Häuser, eine Familie, oft noch mit Untermiethern,\nfür jedes Zimmer. So ward eine Bevölkerung gedrängt in Häuser,\n nicht für sie bestimmt, und wofür sie durchaus unpassend, mit einer Um-\n gebung, die wahrhaft erniedrigend für die Erwachsnen und ruinirend für die\n Kinder ist“ [Note: l. c. p. 56.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":202,"address":"20.202"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0203","text":"Je rascher das Kapital in einer industriellen oder com-\n merciellen Stadt accumulirt, um so rascher der Zustrom des exploitablen\n Menschenmaterials, um so elender die improvisirten Wohnlichkeiten der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"650\", \"facs\": \"#f0669\"} --> Arbeiter. Newcastle-upon-Tyne, als Centrum eines fortwährend\n ergiebigeren Kohlen- und Bergwerksbaudistrikts, behauptet daher nach\n London die zweite Stelle in dem Wohnungsinferno. Nicht minder als\n 34,000 Menschen hausen dort in Einzelkammern. In Folge absoluter\n Gemeinschädlichkeit sind kürzlich in Newcastle und Gateshead Häuser in\n bedeutender Anzahl von Polizei wegen zerstört worden. Der Bau der neuen\n Häuser geht sehr langsam voran, das Geschäft sehr rasch. Die Stadt war\n daher 1865 überfüllter als je zuvor. Kaum eine einzelne Kammer war\n zu vermiethen. Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":203,"address":"20.203"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0204","text":"Embleton vom Newcastle Fieberhospital sagt:\n „Ohne allen Zweifel liegt die Ursache der Fortdauer und Verbreitung des\n Typhus in der Ueberhäufung menschlicher Wesen und der Unreinlichkeit\n ihrer Wohnungen. Die Häuser, worin die Arbeiter häufig leben, liegen\n in abgeschlossnen Winkelgassen und-Höfen. Sie sind mit Bezug auf Licht,\n Luft, Raum und Reinlichkeit wahre Muster von Mangelhaftigkeit und Un-\n gesundheit, eine Schmach für jedes civilisirte Land. Dort liegen Männer,\n Weiber und Kinder des Nachts zusammengehudelt. Was die Männer an-\n geht, folgt die Nachtreihe der Tagesreihe in ununterbrochenem Strom, so\n dass die Betten kaum Zeit zur Abkühlung finden. Die Häuser sind\n schlecht mit Wasser versehn und schlechter mit Abtritten, unfläthig, un-\n ventilirt, pestilenzialisch“ [Note: l. c. p. 149.] . Der Wochenpreis solcher Löcher steigt\n von 8 d. zu 3 sh. „Newcastle-upon-Tyne“, sagt Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":204,"address":"20.204"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0205","text":"Hunter, „bietet das\n Beispiel eines der schönsten Stämme unsrer Landsleute, der durch die\n äussern Umstände von Behausung und Strasse oft in eine beinah wilde\n Degradation versunken ist“ [Note: l. c. p. 50.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":205,"address":"20.205"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0206","text":"In Folge des Hin- und Herwogens von Kapital und Arbeit mag der\n Wohnungszustand einer industriellen Stadt heute erträglich sein, morgen\n ist er abominabel. Oder die städtische Aedilität mag endlich sich aufge-\n rafft haben zur Beseitigung der ärgsten Missstände. Morgen wandert\n ein Heuschreckenschwarm von verlumpten Irländern oder verkommenen\n englischen Agrikulturarbeitern ein. Man steckt sie weg in Keller und\n Speicher oder verwandelt das früher respektable Arbeiterhaus in ein Logis,\n worin das Personal so rasch wechselt wie die Einquartirung während des\n dreissigjährigen Kriegs. Beispiel: Bradford. Dort war der Municipal-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"651\", \"facs\": \"#f0670\"} --> philister eben mit Stadtreform beschäftigt. Zudem gab es daselbst 1861\n noch 1751 unbewohnte Häuser.","kind":"passage","sequence":206,"address":"20.206"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0207","text":"Aber nun das gute Geschäft, worüber der\n sanft liberale Herr Forster, der Negerfreund, jüngst so artig gekräht hat.\n Mit dem guten Geschäft natürlich Ueberfluthung von den Wellen der stets\n wogenden „Reservearmee“ oder „relativen Surpluspopulation“. Die scheuss-\n lichen Kellerwohnungen und Kammern, in der Note beschrieben [Note: Liste des Agenten einer Arbeiter-Assekuranzgesellschaft zu Bradford.","kind":"passage","sequence":207,"address":"20.207"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0208","text":"| \n\n\n\nKeller.","kind":"passage","sequence":208,"address":"20.208"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0209","text":"| \n\n\n\n]  durch\n eine dem Dr. Hunter vom Agenten einer Assekuranzgesellschaft ausgefer-\n tigte Liste, waren meist von gutbezahlten Arbeitern bewohnt. Sie er-\n klärten, sie würden gern bessere Wohnungen zahlen, wenn sie zu haben\n wären. Unterdess verlumpen und verkranken sie mit Mann und Maus,\n während der sanftliberale Forster, M P., Thränen vergiesst über die\n Segnungen des Freihandels und die Profite der eminenten Bradforder\n Köpfe, die in Worsted machen. Im Bericht vom 5. September 1865\n erklärt Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":209,"address":"20.209"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0210","text":"Bell, einer der Armenärzte von Bradford, die furchtbare\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"652\", \"facs\": \"#f0671\"} --> Sterblichkeit der Fieberkranken seines Bezirks aus ihren Wohnungsver-\n hältnissen: „In einem Keller von 1500 Kubikfuss wohnen 10 Perso-\n nen … Die Vincentstrasse, Green Air Place und the Leys bergen 223\n Häuser mit 1450 Einwohnern, 435 Betten und 36 Abtritten … Die\n Betten, und darunter verstehe ich jede Rolle von schmutzigen Lumpen oder\n Handvoll von Hobelspänen, halten jedes per Durchschnitt 3.3 Personen,\n manches 4 und 6 Personen. Viele schlafen ohne Bett auf nacktem Boden\n in ihren Kleidern, junge Männer und Weiber, verheirathet und unverhei-\n rathet, alles kunterbunt durch einander. Ist es nöthig hinzuzufügen, dass\n diese Hausungen meist dunkle, feuchte, schmutzige Stinkhöhlen sind, ganz\n und gar unpassend für menschliche Wohnung?","kind":"passage","sequence":210,"address":"20.210"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0211","text":"Es sind die Centra,\n wovon Krankheit und Tod ausgehn und ihre Opfer auch unter den\n Gutgestellten („of good circumstances“) packen, welche diesen Pest-\n beulen erlaubt haben in unsrer Mitte zu eitern“ [Note: l. c. p. 114.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":211,"address":"20.211"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0212","text":"Bristol behauptet den dritten Rang nach London im Wohnungs-\n elend. „Hier, in einer der reichsten Städte Europa’s, grösster Ueberfluss\n an baarster Armuth („blank poverty“) und häuslicher Misère“ [Note: l. c. p. 50.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":212,"address":"20.212"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0213","text":"Wir wenden uns nun zu einer Wandervölkerung, deren Quelle\n ländlich, deren Beschäftigung grossentheils industriell ist. Es ist die leichte\n Infanterie des Kapitals, je nach seinem Bedürfniss bald auf diesen\n Punkt geworfen, bald auf jenen. Wenn nicht auf dem Marsch, „campirt“\n sie. Die Wanderarbeit wird verbraucht für verschiedne Bau- und Draini-\n rungsoperationen, Backsteinmachen, Kalkbrennen, Eisenbahnen u. s. w.\n Eine wandelnde Säule der Pestilenz importirt sie in die Orte, in deren\n Nachbarschaft sie ihr Lager aufschlägt, Pocken, Typhus, Cholera, Schar-\n lachfieber u. s. w. [Note: „Public Health. Seventh Report. Lond. 1865“, p. 18.] . In Unternehmen von bedeutender Kapitalauslage,\n wie Eisenbahnbau u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":213,"address":"20.213"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0214","text":"w., liefert meist der Unternehmer selbst seiner\n Armee Holzhütten oder dgl., improvisirte Dörfer ohne alle Gesundheits-\n vorkehrung, jenseits der Kontrole der Lokalbehörden, sehr profitlich für\n den Herrn Contractor, der die Arbeiter doppelt ausbeutet, als Industrie-\n soldaten und als Miether. Je nachdem die Holzhütte 1, 2 oder 3 Löcher\n enthält, hat ihr Insasse, Erdarbeiter u. s. w., 1, 3, 4 sh. wöchentlich zu\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"653\", \"facs\": \"#f0672\"} --> zahlen [Note: l. c. p. 165.] . Ein Beispiel genüge. Im September 1864, berichtet\n Dr. Simon, ging dem Minister des Innern, Sir George Grey, folgende\n Denunciation Seitens des Vorstehers des Nuisance Removal Com-\n mittee der Pfarrei von Sevenoaks zu: „Pocken waren dieser Pfarrei fast\n ganz unbekannt bis etwa vor 12 Monaten. Kurz vor dieser Zeit wurden Ar-\n beiten für eine Eisenbahn von Lewisham nach Tunbridge eröffnet.","kind":"passage","sequence":214,"address":"20.214"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0215","text":"Aus-\n serdem dass die Hauptarbeiten in der unmittelbaren Nachbarschaft dieser\n Stadt ausgeführt wurden, ward hier auch das Hauptdepot des ganzen Werks\n errichtet. Grosse Personenzahl daher hier beschäftigt. Da es unmöglich\n war sie alle in Cottages unterzubringen, liess der Contractor, Herr Jay,\n längst der Linie der Bahn auf verschiednen Punkten Hütten aufschlagen\n zur Behausung der Arbeiter. Diese Hütten besassen weder Ventilation\n noch Abzugsgerinne und waren ausserdem nothwendig überfüllt, weil jeder\n Miether andre Logirer aufnehmen musste, wie zahlreich immer seine eigne\n Familie, und obgleich jede Hütte nur zweizimmerig.","kind":"passage","sequence":215,"address":"20.215"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0216","text":"Nach dem ärzt-\n lichen Bericht, den wir erhielten, war die Folge, dass diese armen Leute\n zur Nachtzeit alle Qualen der Erstickung zu erdulden hatten, zur Vermei-\n dung der pestilenzialischen Dünste von dem schmutzigen stehenden Wasser\n und den Abtritten dicht unter den Fenstern. Endlich wurden unsrem\n Comité Klagen eingehändigt von einem Arzte, der Gelegenheit hatte\n diese Hütten zu besuchen. Er sprach über den Zustand dieser s. g.\n Wohnlichkeiten in den bittersten Ausdrücken und befürchtete sehr ernst-\n hafte Folgen, falls nicht einige Gesundheitsvorkehrungen getroffen wür-\n den. Ungefähr vor einem Jahr verpflichtete sich p. p. Jay ein Haus ein-\n zurichten, wohin die von ihm beschäftigten Personen, beim Ausbruch an-\n steckender Krankheiten, sofort entfernt werden sollten.","kind":"passage","sequence":216,"address":"20.216"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0217","text":"Er wiederholte\n diess Versprechen Ende letzten Juli’s, that aber nie den geringsten Schritt\n zur Ausführung, obgleich seit diesem Datum verschiedne Fälle von Pocken\n und in Folge davon zwei Todesfälle vorkamen. Am 9. September be-\n richtete mir Surgeon Kelson weitere Pockenfälle in denselben Hütten und\n beschrieb ihren Zustand als entsetzlich. Zu Ihrer (des Ministers) Infor-\n mation muss ich hinzufügen, dass unsre Pfarrei ein isolirtes Haus besitzt,\n das s. g. Pesthaus, wo die Pfarreigenossen, die von ansteckenden Krank-\n heiten leiden, verpflegt werden. Diess Haus ist jetzt seit Monaten fort-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"654\", \"facs\": \"#f0673\"} --> während mit Patienten überfüllt. In einer Familie starben fünf Kinder an\n Pocken und Fieber. Vom 1. April bis 1. September dieses Jahres kamen\n nicht weniger als 10 Todesfälle an Pocken vor, 4 in den besagten Hütten,\n den Pestquellen.","kind":"passage","sequence":217,"address":"20.217"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0218","text":"Es ist unmöglich die Zahl der Krankheitsfälle\n anzugeben, da die heimgesuchten Familien sie so geheim als möglich\n halten“ [Note: l. c. p. 18, Note. Der Armenpfleger der Chapel-en-le-Frith-Union be-\n richtet an den Registrar General: „Zu Doveholes hat man eine Anzahl kleiner\n Aushöhlungen in einem grossen Hügel von Kalkasche gemacht. Diese Höhlen\n dienen den Erd- und andern am Eisenbahnbau beschäftigten Arbeitern zur Woh-\n nung. Die Höhlen sind eng, feucht, ohne Abzug für Unreinigkeiten und ohne\n Abtritte. Sie entbehren aller Ventilationsmittel, mit Ausnahme eines Lochs durch\n die Wölbung, das zugleich als Schornstein dient. Die Pocken wüthen und haben\n schon verschiedne Todesfälle (unter den Troglodyten) verursacht.“ (l. c. n. 2.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":218,"address":"20.218"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0219","text":"Kohlen- und andre Bergwerksarbeiter gehören zu den best-\n bezahlten Kategorieen der britischen Arbeiterklasse. Zu welchem Preis sie\n ihren Lohn erkaufen, wurde an einer früheren Stelle gezeigt [Note: Die Ende des 4. Kapitels gegebne Note bezieht sich namentlich auf Ar-\n beiter in Kohlenbergwerken. Ueber den noch schlechteren Zustand in den Metall-\n minen vgl. den gewissenhaften Bericht der „Royal Commission“ von 1864.] . Ich werfe\n hier einen raschen Blick auf ihre Wohnlichkeitsverhältnisse. In der Regel\n errichtet der Exploiteur des Bergwerks, ob Eigenthümer oder Miether des-\n selben, eine Anzahl Cottages für seine Hände. Sie erhalten Cottages und\n Kohlen zur Feurung „umsonst“, d. h. letztre bilden einen in natura ge-\n lieferten Theil des Lohns. Die nicht in dieser Art Unterbringbaren er-\n halten zum Ersatz 4 Pfd. St. per Jahr.","kind":"passage","sequence":219,"address":"20.219"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0220","text":"Die Bergwerksdistrikte ziehn\n rasch eine grosse Bevölkerung an, zusammengesetzt aus der Minenbevöl-\n kerung selbst und den Handwerkern, Krämern u. s. w., die sich um sie\n gruppiren. Wie überall, wo die Bevölkerung dicht, ist die Bodenrente\n hier hoch. Der Bergbauunternehmer sucht daher auf möglichst engem\n Bauplatz am Mund der Minen so viel Cottages aufzuwerfen als grade nöthig\n sind um seine Hände und ihre Familien zusammenzupacken. Werden\n neue Minen in der Nähe eröffnet oder alte wieder in Angriff genommen, so\n wächst das Gedränge. Bei der Konstruktion der Cottages waltet nur ein\n Gesichtspunkt, „Entsagung“ des Kapitalisten auf alle nicht absolut\n unvermeidliche Ausgaben von Baarem. „Die Wohnungen der Gruben-\n und andrer Arbeiter, die mit den Bergwerken von Northumberland und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"655\", \"facs\": \"#f0674\"} --> Durham verknüpft sind,“ sagt Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":220,"address":"20.220"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0221","text":"Julian Hunter, „sind vielleicht\n im Durchschnitt das Schlechteste und Theuerste, was England auf grosser\n Stufenleiter in dieser Art bietet, mit Ausnahme jedoch ähnlicher Distrikte\n in Monmouthshire. Die extreme Schlechtigkeit liegt in der hohen Men-\n schenzahl, die ein Zimmer füllen, in der Enge des Bauplatzes, worauf eine\n grosse Häusermasse geworfen wird, im Wassermangel und Abwesenheit\n von Abtritten, in der häufig angewandten Methode ein Haus über ein an-\n dres zu stellen oder sie in flats (so dass die verschiednen Cottages hori-\n zontal über einander liegende Stockwerke bilden) zu vertheilen … Der\n Unternehmer behandelt die ganze Kolonie, als ob sie nur campire, nicht\n residire“ [Note: l. c. p. 180, 182.] . „In Ausführung meiner Instruktionen,“ sagt Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":221,"address":"20.221"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0222","text":"Stevens,\n „habe ich die meisten grossen Bergwerksdörfer der Durham Union be-\n sucht … Mit sehr wenigen Ausnahmen gilt von allen, dass jedes Mittel\n zur Sicherung der Gesundheit der Einwohner vernachlässigt wird … Alle\n Grubenarbeiter sind an den Pächter („lessee“) oder Eigenthümer des Berg-\n werks für 12 Monate gebunden („bound“, Ausdruck, der wie bondage aus der\nZeit der Leibeigenschaft stammt). Wenn sie ihrer Unzufriedenheit\n Luft machen oder in irgend einer Art den Aufseher („viewer“) belästigen, so\n setzt er eine Marke oder ein Memorandum hinter ihre Namen in seinem Auf-\n sichtsbuch, und entlässt sie bei der jährlichen Neu-Bindung … Es scheint\n mir, dass kein Theil des Trucksystems schlechter sein kann als das in\n diesen dichtbevölkerten Distrikten herrschende. Der Arbeiter ist gezwun-\n gen als Theil seines Lohns ein mit pestilenzialischen Einflüssen umgebnes\n Haus zu empfangen.","kind":"passage","sequence":222,"address":"20.222"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0223","text":"Er kann sich nicht selbst helfen. Erist in jeder\n Rücksicht ein Leibeigner („he is to all intents and pur-\n posesaserf“). Es scheint fraglich, ob jemand sonst ihm helfen kann\n ausser seinem Eigenthümer und dieser Eigenthümer zieht vor allem sein\n Bilanzkonto zu Rath, und das Resultat ist ziemlich unfehlbar. Der\n Arbeiter erhält von dem Eigenthümer auch seine Zufuhr an Wasser. Es sei\n gut oder schlecht, es werde geliefert oder zurückgehalten, er muss dafür\n zahlen oder sich vielmehr einen Lohnabzug gefallen lassen“ [Note: l. c. p. 515, 517.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":223,"address":"20.223"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0224","text":"Im Konflikt mit der „öffentlichen Meinung“ oder auch der Gesundheits-\n polizei genirt sich das Kapital durchaus nicht, die theils gefährlichen,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"656\", \"facs\": \"#f0675\"} --> theils entwürdigenden Bedingungen, worin es Funktion und Häuslichkeit des\n Arbeiters bannt, damit zu „rechtfertigen“, das sei nöthig, um ihn\n profitlicher auszubeuten. So, wenn es entsagt auf Vorrichtungen\n zum Schutz gegen gefährliche Maschinerie in der Fabrik, auf Ventilations-\n und Sicherheitsmittel in den Minen u. s. w. So hier mit der Behausung der\n Minenarbeiter. „Als Entschuldigung“, sagt Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":224,"address":"20.224"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0225","text":"Simon, der ärztliche\n Beamte des Privy Council, in seinem officiellen Bericht, „als Entschuldi-\n gung für die nichtswürdige Hauseinrichtung wird angeführt, dass Mi-\n nen gewöhnlich pachtweise exploitirt werden, dass die Dauer des Pachtkon-\n trakts (in Kohlenwerken meist 21 Jahre) zu kurz ist, damit der Minen-\n pächter es der Mühe werth halte, gute Hausaccommodation für das Arbeits-\n volk und die Gewerbsleute u. s. w. zu liefern, welche die Unternehmung\n anzieht; hätte er selbst die Absicht, nach dieser Seite hin liberal\n zu verfahren, so würde sie vereitelt werden durch den Grundeigenthümer.\n Der habe nämlich die Tendenz, sofort exorbitante Zuschussrente zu ver-\n langen für das Privilegium, ein anständiges und komfortables Dorf auf\n der Grundoberfläche zu errichten zur Behausung der Bearbeiter des unter-\n irdischen Eigenthums.","kind":"passage","sequence":225,"address":"20.225"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0226","text":"Dieser prohibitorische Preis, wenn nicht direkte\n Prohibition, schrecke ebenfalls andre ab, welche sonst wohl bauen möch-\n ten … Ich will den Werth dieser Apologie nicht weiter untersuchen,\n auch nicht, auf wen denn in letzter Hand die zuschüssige Ausgabe für\n anständige Wohnlichkeit fallen würde, auf den Grundherrn, den Minen-\n pächter, die Arbeiter oder das Publikum … Aber Angesichts solcher\n schmählichen Thatsachen, wie die beigefügten Berichte (des Dr. Hunter,\n Stevens u. s. w.) sie enthüllen, muss ein Heilmittel angewandt werden …\nGrundeigenthumstitel werden so gebraucht um ein grosses öffentliches\n Unrecht zu begehn.","kind":"passage","sequence":226,"address":"20.226"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0227","text":"In seiner Eigenschaft als Mineneigner ladet der\n Grundherr eine industrielle Kolonie zur Arbeit auf seiner Domaine ein,\n und macht dann, in seiner Eigenschaft als Eigenthümer der Grund-\n oberfläche, den von ihm versammelten Arbeitern unmöglich die zu\n ihrem Leben unentbehrliche, geeignete Wohnlichkeit zu finden. Der Minen-\n pächter (der kapitalistische Exploiteur) hat kein Geldinteresse dieser\n Theilung des Handels zu widerstehn, da er wohl weiss, dass wenn die letztern\n Ansprüche exorbitant sind, die Folgen nicht auf ihn fallen, und dass\n die Arbeiter, auf die sie fallen, zu unerzogen sind, um ihre Gesundheits-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"657\", \"facs\": \"#f0676\"} --> rechte zu kennen, dass weder obscönste Wohnlichkeit noch faulstes\n Trinkwasser besondre Anlässe zu einem Strike liefern“ [Note: l. c. p. 16.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":227,"address":"20.227"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0228","text":"Bevor ich zu den eigentlichen Agrikulturarbeitern übergehe,\n soll an einem Beispiel noch gezeigt werden, wie die Krisen selbst auf den\n bestbezahlten Theil der Arbeiterklasse, auf ihre Aristokratie, wirken. Man\n erinnert sich: das Jahr 1857 brachte eine der grossen Krisen, womit der\n industrielle Cyklus jedesmal abschliesst. Der nächste Termin wurde\n1866 fällig. Bereits diskontirt in den eigentlichen Fabrikdistrikten\n durch die Baumwollnoth, welche viel Kapital aus der gewohnten Anlagesphäre\n zu den grossen Centralsitzen des Geldmarkts jagte, nahm die Krise diess-\n mal einen vorwiegend finanziellen Charakter an. Ihr Ausbruch im Mai 1866\n wurde signalisirt durch den Fall einer Londoner Riesenbank, dem der Zusam-\n mensturz zahlloser finanzieller Schwindelgesellschaften auf dem Fuss nach-\n folgte.","kind":"passage","sequence":228,"address":"20.228"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0229","text":"Einer der grossen Londoner Geschäftszweige, welche die Kata-\n strophe traf, war der eiserne Schiffsbau. Die Magnaten dieses Fachs\n hatten während der Schwindelzeit nicht nur masslos überprodueirt, son-\n dern zudem enorme Lieferungskontrakte übernommen, auf die Spekulation\n hin, dass die Kreditquelle gleich reichlich fort fliessen werde. Jetzt trat\n eine furchtbare Reaktion ein, die auch in andern Londoner Industrien [Note: „Massenhafte Verhungerung der Londoner Armen! („Wholesale starva-\n tion of the London Poor!“) … Während der letzten Tage waren die Wälle\n Londons überklebt mit grossen Plakaten, die folgende merkwürdige Anzeige\n bringen: ‚Fette Ochsen, verhungernde Menschen!","kind":"passage","sequence":229,"address":"20.229"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0230","text":"Die fetten Ochsen haben ihre\n Glaspaläste verlassen, um die Reichen in ihren Luxusgemächern zu mästen, wäh-\n rend die verhungernden Menschen in ihren Jammerhöhlen verderben und sterben.‘\n Die Plakate mit dieser unheilkündenden Inschrift werden beständig erneuert.\n Kaum ist eine Partie ausgemerzt und überklebt, wenn sofort eine neue Partie an dem-\n selben oder einem gleich öffentlichen Platz wiedererscheint … Das erinnert an\n die omina, die das französische Volk auf die Ereignisse von 1789 vorbereiteten …\n In diesem Augenblick, während englische Arbeiter mit Weib und Kind an Kälte\n und Hunger sterben, werden Millionen von englischem Geld, dem Produkt eng-\n lischer Arbeit, in russischen, spanischen, italienischen und andern fremden An-\n leihen angelegt.“ („Reynold’s Newspaper, 20. Jan. 1867.)] \n bis zur Stunde, Ende März 1867, fortdauert.","kind":"passage","sequence":230,"address":"20.230"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0231","text":"Zur Charakteristik der\n Lage der Arbeiter folgende Stelle aus dem ausführlichen Bericht eines\n [Running matter: I. 42] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"658\", \"facs\": \"#f0677\"} --> Korrespondenten des Morning Star, welcher Anfang Januar 1867\n die Hauptsitze des Leidens besuchte. „Im Osten von London, den Distrik-\n ten von Poplar, Millwall, Greenwich, Deptford, Limehouse und Canning\n Town befinden sich mindestens 15,000 Arbeiter sammt Familien in\n einem Zustand äusserster Noth, darunter über 3000 geschickte Mecha-\n niker. Ihre Reservefonds sind erschöpft in Folge sechs- oder achtmonat-\n licher Arbeitslosigkeit … Ich hatte grosse Mühe zum Thor des Work-\n house (von Poplar) vorzudringen, denn es war belagert von einem ausge-\n hungerten Haufen. Er wartete auf Brodbillets, aber die Zeit zur Ver-\n theilung war noch nicht gekommen.","kind":"passage","sequence":231,"address":"20.231"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0232","text":"Der Hof bildet ein grosses Quadrat\n mit einem Pultdach, das rings um seine Mauern läuft. Dichte Schnee-\n haufen bedeckten die Pflastersteine in der Mitte des Hofes. Hier waren\n gewisse kleine Plätze mit Weidengeflecht abgeschlossen, gleich Schaf-\n hürden, worin die Männer bei besserem Wetter arbeiten. Am Tage meines\n Besuchs waren die Hürden so verschneit, dass Niemand in ihnen sitzen\n konnte. Die Männer waren jedoch unter dem Schutz der Dachvorsprünge\n mit Macadamisirung von Pflastersteinen beschäftigt. Jeder hatte einen\n dicken Pflasterstein zum Sitz und klopfte mit schwerem Hammer auf den\n frostbedeckten Granit, bis er 5 Bushel davon abgehauen hatte. Dann\n war sein Tagewerk verrichtet und erhielt er 3 d. (1 Silbergroschen, 8\n Pfennige) und ein Billet für Brod. In einem andern Theil des Hofes\n stand ein rhachitisches kleines Holzhaus.","kind":"passage","sequence":232,"address":"20.232"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0233","text":"Beim Oeffnen der Thüre fanden\n wir es gefüllt mit Männern, Schulter an Schulter gedrängt, um einander\n warm zu halten. Sie zupften Schiffstau und stritten mit einander, wer\n von ihnen mit einem Minimum von Nahrung am längsten arbeiten könne,\n denn Ausdauer war der point d’honneur. In diesem einen Workhouse\n allein erhielten 7000 Unterstützung, darunter viele Hunderte, die 6\n oder 8 Monate zuvor die höchsten Löhne geschickter Arbeit in\n diesem Land verdienten. Ihre Zahl wäre doppelt so gross gewesen,\n gäbe es nicht so viele, welche nach Erschöpfung ihrer ganzen Geldreserve\n dennoch vor Zuflucht zur Pfarrei zurückbeben, so lange sie noch irgend\n etwas zu versetzen haben … Das Workhouse verlassend, machte ich\n einen Gang durch die Strassen von meist einstöckigen Häusern, die in\n Poplar so zahlreich. Mein Führer war Mitglied des Comités für die\n Arbeitslosen.","kind":"passage","sequence":233,"address":"20.233"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0234","text":"Das erste Haus, worin wir eintraten, war das eines Eisen-\n arbeiters, seit 27 Wochen ausser Beschäftigung. Ich fand den Mann mit\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"659\", \"facs\": \"#f0678\"} --> seiner ganzen Familie in einem Hinterzimmer sitzend. Das Zimmer war\n noch nicht ganz von Möbeln entblösst und es war Feuer darin. Diess\n war nöthig, um die nackten Füsse der jungen Kinder vor Frost zu\n schützen, denn es war ein grimmig kalter Tag. Auf einem Teller gegen-\n über dem Feuer lag ein Quantum Werg, welches Frau und Kinder zupften\n in Erstattung des Brods vom Workhouse. Der Mann arbeitete in einem\n der oben beschriebenen Höfe für ein Brodbillet und 3 d. per Tag.","kind":"passage","sequence":234,"address":"20.234"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0235","text":"Er\n kam jetzt nach Haus zum Mittagsessen, sehr hungrig, wie er uns mit\n einem bittern Lächeln sagte, und sein Mittagsessen bestand aus einigen\n Brodschnitten mit Schmalz und einer Tasse milchlosen Thees … Die\n nächste Thüre, an der wir anklopften, wurde geöffnet durch ein Frauen-\n zimmer mittleren Alters, die, ohne ein Wort zu sagen, uns in ein kleines\n Hinterzimmer führte, wo ihre ganze Familie sass, schweigend, die Augen\n auf ein rasch ersterbendes Feuer geheftet. Solche Verödung, solche Hoff-\n nungslosigkeit hing um diese Leute und ihr kleines Zimmer, dass ich nicht\n wünsche je eine ähnliche Scene wieder zu sehn.","kind":"passage","sequence":235,"address":"20.235"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0236","text":"„Nichts haben sie ver-\n dient, mein Herr“, sagte die Frau, auf ihre Jungen zeigend, „nichts für\n 26 Wochen, und all unser Geld ist hingegangen, alles Geld, das ich und\n der Vater in den bessern Zeiten zurücklegten, in dem Wahn eine Reserve\n während schlechten Geschäfts zu sichern. Sehn Sie es“, schrie sie fast wild, in-\n dem sie ein Bankbuch hervorholte mit allen seinen regelmässigen Nachweisen\n über eingezahltes und rückerhaltnes Geld, so dass wir sehn konnten, wie das\n kleine Vermögen begonnen hatte mit dem ersten Deposit von 5 Shilling, wie\n es nach und nach zu 20 Pfd. St. aufwuchs, und dann wieder zusammen-\n schmolz, von Pfunden zu Shillingen, bis der letzte Eintrag das Buch so\n werthlos machte, wie ein blankes Stück Papier.","kind":"passage","sequence":236,"address":"20.236"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0237","text":"Diese Familie erhielt\n ein nothdürftiges Mahl täglich vom Workhouse … Unsre folgende Visite\n war zur Frau eines Irländers, der an den Schiffswerften gearbeitet hatte.\n Wir fanden sie krank von Nahrungsmangel, in ihren Kleidern auf eine\n Matratze gestreckt, knapp bedeckt mit einem Stück Teppich, denn alles\n Bettzeug war im Pfandhaus. Die elenden Kinder warteten sie und sahen\n aus als bedürften sie umgekehrt der mütterlichen Pflege. Neunzehn\n Wochen erzwungnen Müssiggangs hatten sie so weit heruntergebracht,\n und während sie die Geschichte der bitteren Vergangenheit erzählte,\n stöhnte sie als ob alle Hoffnung auf eine bessere Zukunft verloren wäre\n … Beim Austritt aus dem Hause rannte ein junger Mann auf uns zu und\n [Running matter: 42*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"660\", \"facs\": \"#f0679\"} --> bat uns in sein Haus zu gehn und zu sehn, ob irgend etwas für ihn ge-\n schehn könne.","kind":"passage","sequence":237,"address":"20.237"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0238","text":"Ein junges Weib, zwei hübsche Kinder, ein Kluster von\n Pfandzetteln und ein ganz kahles Zimmer war alles, was er zu zeigen\n hatte“ [Note: Da es grade jetzt Mode unter den englischen Kapitalisten ist, Belgien\n als das Paradies des Arbeiters zu schildern, weil „die Freiheit der Arbeit“\n dort weder durch den Despotismus der Trade’s Unions, noch durch Fabrikgesetze\n verkümmert sei, hier ein paar Worte über das „Glück“ des nur durch Klerisei,\n Grundaristokratie, liberale Bourgeois und Bureaukraten, aber bei Leibe nicht durch\n Trade’s Unions und Fabrikgesetze gehänselten „freien“ belgischen Arbeiters.\n Herr Ducpetiaux, eine gute Autorität, früher, ich weiss nicht ob noch, Gene-\n ralinspektor der belgischen Gefängnisse, sagt in seinen: „Budgets écono-\n miques des classes ouvrières en Belgique“: „Im Durchschnitt zählt\n eine Arbeiterfamilie 4 Kinder, also mit Vater und Mutter 6 Personen.","kind":"passage","sequence":238,"address":"20.238"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0239","text":"Von diesen\n 6 Personen können 4 nützlich beschäftigt werden, wenn Krankheit u. dgl. nicht\n dazwischen kömmt. Unter diesen Umständen sind folgendes die Hilfsquellen der\n Familie auf ihrem Maximalgrad:","kind":"passage","sequence":239,"address":"20.239"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0240","text":"| Der Vater | 300 Tage | zu | 1 fc. 56 c. | per Jahr | 468 fcs.","kind":"passage","sequence":240,"address":"20.240"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0241","text":"| Die Mutter | 〃 | „ | 0 fc. 89 c. | 〃 | 267 fcs.","kind":"passage","sequence":241,"address":"20.241"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0242","text":"| Der älteste Junge | 〃 | 〃 | 0 fc. 56 c. | 〃 | 168 fcs.","kind":"passage","sequence":242,"address":"20.242"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0243","text":"| Die älteste Tochter | 〃 | 〃 | 0 fc. 55 c. | 〃 | 165 fcs.","kind":"passage","sequence":243,"address":"20.243"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0244","text":"|  |  |  |  |  | 1,068 fcs.","kind":"passage","sequence":244,"address":"20.244"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0245","text":"Die Jahresausgabe der Familie und ihr Deficit würden sich erheben, falls\n der Arbeiter die Nahrung hätte:","kind":"passage","sequence":245,"address":"20.245"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0246","text":"| Des Seesoldaten, | auf | 1828 fcs. | Deficit: | 760 fcs.","kind":"passage","sequence":246,"address":"20.246"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0247","text":"| Des Soldaten, | 〃 | 1473 fcs. | 〃 | 405 fcs.","kind":"passage","sequence":247,"address":"20.247"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0248","text":"| Des Gefangnen, | 〃 | 1112 fcs. | 〃 | 44 fcs.","kind":"passage","sequence":248,"address":"20.248"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0249","text":"In jener Musterfamilie haben wir alle möglichen Hilfsquellen vereinigt. Aber\n indem wir der Familienmutter ein Salair gaben. entzogen wir dem Haushalt ihre\n Leitung. Wer wird dem Inneren vorstehn, das Essen bereiten, Wäsche, Flicke-\n reien u. s. w. besorgen? Wie bringt es die grosse Mehrzahl der Arbeiter, welche\n die Waaren weder im Grossen kauft, noch im Halbgrossen, gleich der Gefängniss-\n verwaltung, wie bringt sie es fertig zu leben? Indem sie zu Nothbehelfen flüchtet,\n wovon der Arbeiter allein das Geheimniss hat, indem sie an ihrer täglichen Ration\n abknappt, wenig oder gar kein Fleisch isst, ebenso mit Butter und Gewürzen, indem\n sie die Familie in eine oder zwei Kammern packt, wo Mädchen und Jungen zusammen-\n schlafen, oft auf demselben Strohsack, indem sie an der Kleidung ökonomisirt, an\n Wäsche und Reinigungsmitteln, den Sonntagsvergnügungen entsagt u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":249,"address":"20.249"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0250","text":"Einmal\n bei dieser letzten Grenze angelangt, wirft die geringste Erhöhung der Preise der\n Lebensmittel u. s. w. sie auf die Armenliste.“ In der That ist in diesem „Paradies\n der Kapitalisten“ die geringste Variation der Getreidepreise be-] .","kind":"passage","sequence":250,"address":"20.250"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0251","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"661\", \"facs\": \"#f0680\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":251,"address":"20.251"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0252","text":"Der antagonistische Charakter der kapitalistischen Produktion und\n Accumulation bewährt sich nirgendwo brutaler als in dem Fortschritt\n des englischen Landbaus (Viehzucht eingeschlossen) und dem Rück-\n schritt des englischen Landarbeiters. Bevor ich zu seiner gegen-\n wärtigen Lage übergehe, ein rascher Rückblick. Die moderne Agri-\n kultur datirt in England von der Mitte des 18. Jahrhunderts, obgleich die\n Umwälzung der Grundeigenthumsverhältnisse, wovon die veränderte Pro-\n duktionsweise als Grundlage ausgeht, viel früheren Datums.","kind":"passage","sequence":252,"address":"20.252"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0253","text":"Nehmen wir die Angaben Arthur Young’s, eines genauen Be-\n obachters, obgleich elenden Denkers, über den Landarbeiter von 1771, so\n spieltletztrer eine sehr elende Rolle, verglichen mit seinem Vorgänger Ende\n des 14. Jahrhunderts, „wo er in Fülle leben und Reichthum\n accumuliren konnte“ [Note: James E. Th. Rogers (Prof. of Polit. Econ. in the Uni-\n versity of Oxford): „A History of Agriculture and Prices in\n England. Oxford 1866“, v. I, p. 690. Diess fleissig gearbeitete Werk um-\n fasst in den bisher erschienenen zwei ersten Bänden nur noch die Periode von\n 1259—1400. Der zweite Band enthält bloss statistisches Material. Es ist die erste\n authentische „History of Prices“, die wir für jene Zeit besitzen.] , gar nicht zu sprechen vom 15. Jahrhun-\n dert, „dem goldnen Zeitalter der englischen Arbeiter in Stadt und\n Land“. Wir brauchen jedoch nicht so weit zurückzugehn.","kind":"passage","sequence":253,"address":"20.253"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0254","text":"In einer sehr\n gehaltreichen Schrift von 1777 liest man: „Der grosse Pächter hat sich\n beinahe erhoben zum Niveau des Gentleman, während der arme Land-\n arbeiter fast zu Boden gedrückt ist … Seine unglückliche Lage zeigt\n sich klar durch eine vergleichende Uebersicht seiner Verhältnisse von\n heute und von 40 Jahr früher … Grundeigenthümer und Pächter wirken\n Hand in Hand zur Unterdrückung des Arbeiters“ [Note: „Reasons for the late Increase of the Poorlaws; or, a\n comparative view of the price of labour and provisions. Lond.\n 1777“, p. 5, 14—16.] . Es wird dann im\n [Note: gleitet von einer Variation in Todesfällen und Verbrechen! (Sieh\n „Manifest der Maatschappij: De Vlaemingen Vooruit! Brussel\n 1860“, p. 15, 16.)","kind":"passage","sequence":254,"address":"20.254"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0255","text":"Ganz Belgien zählt 930,000 Familien, davon, nach officieller\n Statistik: 90,000 Reiche (Wähler), 450,000 Personen; 190,000 Familien der kleinen\n Mittelklasse, in Stadt und Dorf, grosser Theil davon stets in’s Proletariat fallend,\n 1,950,000 Personen. Endlich 450,000 Arbeiterfamilien mit 2,250,000 Personen, von\n welchen die Musterfamilien das durch Ducpetiaux geschilderte Glück geniessen.\n Unter den 450,000 Arbeiterfamilien über 200,000 auf der Armenliste!] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"662\", \"facs\": \"#f0681\"} --> Detail nachgewiesen, dass der reelle Arbeitslohn auf dem Lande von 1737\n bis 1777 um beinahe ¼ oder 25 % gefallen ist. „Die moderne Politik“,\n sagt Dr. Richard Pricc zur selben Zeit, „begünstigt die höheren\n Volksklassen; die Folge wird sein, dass früher oder später das ganze\n Königreich nur aus Gentlemen und Bettlern, aus Grandees und Sklaven\n besteht“ [Note: Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":255,"address":"20.255"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0256","text":"Richard Price: „Observations on Reversionary Pay-\n ments.“ 6. ed. By W. Morgan. Lond. 1805, v. II, p. 158, 159. Price\n bemerkt p. 159: „The nominal price of day labour is at present no more than\n about four times, or at most five times higher than it was in the year 1514. But\n the price of corn is seven times, and of flesh-meat and raiment about fifteen times\n higher. So far, therefore, has the price of labour been even from advancing in\n proportion to the increase in the expences of living, that it does not appear that\n it bears now half the proportion to those expences that it did bear.“] .","kind":"passage","sequence":256,"address":"20.256"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0257","text":"Dennoch ist die Lage des englischen Landarbeiters von 1770 1780,\n sowohl was seine Nahrungs- und Wohnlichkeitszustände, als sein Selbst-\n gefühl, Belustigungen u. s. w. betrifft, ein später nie wieder erreichtes\nIdeal. In Pints Weizen ausgedrückt betrug sein Durchschnittslohn\n 1770—1771 90 Pints, zu Eden’s Zeit (1797) nur noch 65, 1808\n aber 60 [Note: Barton l. c. p. 26. Für Ende des 18. Jahrhunderts vgl. Eden l. c.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":257,"address":"20.257"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0258","text":"Der Zustand der Landarbeiter Ende des Antijakobinerkriegs,\n während dessen sich Grundaristokratie, Pächter, Fabrikanten, Kaufleute,\n Banquiers, Börsenritter, Armeelieferanten u. s. w. so ausserordentlich be-\n reichert hatten, ist bereits früher angedeutet worden. Der nominelle\n Arbeitslohn stieg natürlich in Folge theils der Depreciation des Geldes theils\n eines von dieser Depreciation unabhängigen Steigens im Preis der noth-\n wendigsten Lebensmittel. Die wirkliche Bewegung des Arbeitslohns ist\n aber auf sehr einfache Art zu konstatiren, ohne Zuflucht zu hier unzu-\n lässigen Details. Das Armengesetz und seine Administration waren 1795\n und 1814 dieselben.","kind":"passage","sequence":258,"address":"20.258"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0259","text":"Man erinnert sich: diess Gesetz wurde auf dem\n Land in der Art gehandhabt, dass die Differenz zwischen dem dem\n Arbeiter gezahlten Arbeitslohn und der Minimalsumme, die zu\n seiner blossen Vegetation erheischt ist, von der Pfarrei in der Form der\n Armenunterstützung ergänzt wurde. Das Verhältniss zwischen dem vom\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"663\", \"facs\": \"#f0682\"} --> Pächter gezahlten Arbeitslohn und dem von der Pfarrei gutge-\n machten Deficit dieses Arbeitslohns zeigt uns zweierlei, erstens\n die Senkung des Arbeitslohns unter sein Minimum, zweitens den Grad,\n worin der Landarbeiter aus Lohnarbeiter und Pauper zusammengesetzt\n war, oder den Grad, worin er in einen Leibeignen seiner Pfarrei verwan-\n delt ward. Wir nehmen eine Grafschaft, die das Durchschnittsverhältniss\n in allen andern Grafschaften repräsentirt. 1795 betrug der durch-\n schnittliche Wochenlohn in Northampton 7 sh.","kind":"passage","sequence":259,"address":"20.259"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0260","text":"6d., die jährliche Total-\n ausgabe einer Familie von 6 Personen 36 Pfd. St. 12 sh. 5 d., ihre\nTotaleinnahme 29 Pfd. St. 18 sh., das von der Pfarrei gutgemachte\nDeficit: 6 Pfd. St. 14 sh. 5 d. In derselben Grafschaft betrug 1814\n der Wochenlohn 12 sh. 2 d., die jährliche Totalausgabe einer Fa-\n milie von 5 Personen 54 Pfd. St. 18 sh. 4 d., ihre Totaleinnahme\n 36 Pfd. St. 2 sh., das von der Pfarrei gutgemachte Deficit: 18 Pfd.\n St. 6 sh. 4 d. [Note: Parry l. c. p. 86.] . 1795 betrug das Deficit weniger als ¼ des Ar-\n beitslohns, 1814 mehr als die Hälfte. Dass unter diesen Umständen die\n geringen Komforts, die bei Eden noch in der Cottage des Landarbei-\n ters figuriren, 1814 verschwunden waren, versteht sich von selbst [Note: id. p. 213.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":260,"address":"20.260"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0261","text":"Unter allen Thieren, die der Pächter hält, blieb von nun an der Arbeiter,\n das instrumentum vocale, das meist geplackte, schlechtest gefütterte und\n am brutalsten behandelte.","kind":"passage","sequence":261,"address":"20.261"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0262","text":"Derselbe Zustand der Dinge dauerte ruhig fort, bis „die swing Auf-\n stände 1830 uns (d. h. den herrschenden Klassen) beim Lichtflammen der\n Kornschober enthüllten, dass Elend und dunkle aufrührerische Unzufrieden-\n heit ebenso wild unter der Oberfläche des agrikolen als des industriellen\n Englands lodre“ [Note: S. Laing l c. p. 62.] . Sadler taufte damals im Unterhaus die Land-\n arbeiter „weisse Sklaven“ („white slaves“), ein Bischof hallte das Epithet\n im Oberhaus wieder. Der bedeutendste politische Oekonom jener Pe-\n riode, E. G. Wakefield, sagt: „der Landarbeiter Südenglands ist\n kein Sklave, er ist kein freier Mann, er ist ein Pauper“ [Note: England and America. Lond. 1833, v. I, p. 45.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":262,"address":"20.262"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0263","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"664\", \"facs\": \"#f0683\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":263,"address":"20.263"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0264","text":"Die Zeit unmittelbar vor der Aufhebung der Kornge-\n setze warf neues Licht auf die Lage der Landarbeiter. Einerseits lag\n es natürlich im Interesse der bürgerlichen Anticornlawagitatoren nachzu-\n weisen, wie wenig jene Schutzgesetze den wirklichen Kornproducen-\n ten beschützten. Andrerseits schäumte die industrielle Bourgeoisie auf von\n Ingrimm über die Denunciation der Fabrikzustände seitens der Grund-\n aristokraten, über die affektirte Sympathie dieser grundverdorbnen, herz-\n losen und vornehmen Müssiggänger mit den Leiden des Fabrikarbeiters,\n und ihren „diplomatischen“ Eifer für Fabrikgesetzgebung. Es ist ein\n altes englisches Sprichwort, dass wenn zwei Diebe sich in die Haare fallen,\n immer etwas Nützliches geschieht.","kind":"passage","sequence":264,"address":"20.264"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0265","text":"Und in der That, der geräuschvolle,\n leidenschaftliche Zank zwischen den zwei Fraktionen der herrschenden\n Klasse, welche von beiden den Arbeiter am schamlosesten ausbeute und\ndas meiste Produkt fremder Arbeit als Privateigenthum\n des Nichtarbeiters usurpire, dieser Zank wurde rechts und links\n Geburtshelfer der Wahrheit. Graf Shaftesbury, alias Lord Ashley,\n war Vorkämpfer im aristokratischen Fabrikphilanthropiefeldzug. Er bildet\n daher 1844—1845 ein Lieblingsthema in den Enthüllungen des Mor-\n ning Chronicle über die Zustände der Agrikulturarbeiter. Jenes\n Blatt, damals das bedeutendste liberale Organ, entsandte eigne Kommis-\n säre in die Agrikulturdistrikte, welche sich keineswegs mit allgemeiner\n Schilderung und Statistik begnügten, sondern die Namen sowohl der\n untersuchten Arbeiterfamilien als ihrer Landlords veröffentlichten.","kind":"passage","sequence":265,"address":"20.265"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0266","text":"Die\n folgende Liste giebt Löhne, gezahlt auf drei Dörfern, in der Nachbarschaft\n von Blandford, Wimbourne und Poole. Die Dörfer sind Eigenthum des Mr.\n G. Bankes und des Grafen von Shaftesbury. Man wird bemerken,\n dass dieser Pabst der „low church“, diess Haupt der englischen Pietisten,\n und p. p. Bankes von den Hundelöhnen ihrer Arbeiter wieder einen bedeu-\n tenden Theil unter dem Vorwand von Hausrente einstecken.","kind":"passage","sequence":266,"address":"20.266"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0267","text":"(Siehe nebenstehende Tabelle.)","kind":"passage","sequence":267,"address":"20.267"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0268","text":"Der Widerruf der Korngesetze gab dem englischen Landbau einen\n ungeheuren Ruck. Drainirung auf der grössten Stufenleiter147), neues\n System der Stallfütterung und des Anbaus der künstlichen Futterkräuter,\n Einführung mechanischer Düngapparate, neue Behandlung der Thonerde,\n gesteigerter Gebrauch mineralischer Düngmittel, Anwendung der Dampf-\n maschine und aller Art neuer Maschinerie u. s. w., intensivere Kultur\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"665\", \"facs\": \"#f0684\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":268,"address":"20.268"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0269","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n |","kind":"passage","sequence":269,"address":"20.269"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0270","text":"| \n\n\n\n überhaupt charakterisiren diese Epoche. Der Präsident der königlichen\n Gesellschaft für Agrikultur, Herr Pusey, behauptet, dass die (relativen)\n Wirthschaftskosten durch die neu eingeführte Maschinerie beinahe um die\n Hälfte verringert worden sind. Andrerseits ward der positive Boden-\n ertrag rasch erhöht. Grössere Kapitalauslage per Acre war Grundbe-\n dingung der neuen Methode, also auch beschleunigte Koncentration der\n Pachten [Note: Die Abnahme der mittleren Pächter ersieht man namentlich aus den\n Rubriken des Census: „Pächters Sohn, Enkel, Bruder, Neffe, Tochter, Enkelin,\n Schwester, Nichte“, kurz der vom Pächter beschäftigten Glieder seiner eignen\n Familie. Diese Rubriken zählten 1851: 216, 851 Personen, 1861 nur 176,151.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":270,"address":"20.270"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0271","text":"Zugleich dehnte sich das Areal der Bebauung von 1846 bis\n 1865 um 464,119 Acres aus, nicht zu sprechen von den grossen Flächen\n [Note: 147) Die Grundaristokratie schoss sich selbst zu diesem Zweck Fonds, natür-\n lich per Parlament, aus der Staatskasse vor, zu sehr niedrigem Zins, welchen\n die Pächter ihr doppelt zu erstatten haben.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"666\", \"facs\": \"#f0685\"} --> der östlichen Grafschaften, welche aus Kaninchengeheg und armer Vieh-\n weide in üppige Kornfelder umgezaubert wurden. Man weiss bereits,\n dass gleichzeitig die Gesammtzahl der in der Agrikultur betheiligten\n Personen abnahm. Was die eigentlichen Ackerbauer, bei-\n derlei Geschlechts und aller Altersstufen, betrifft, so sank ihre Zahl von\n 1,241,269 im Jahr 1851 auf 1,163,227 im Jahr 1861 [Note: Die Zahl der Schafhirten wuchs von 12,517 auf 25,559.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":271,"address":"20.271"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0272","text":"Wenn der\n englische Generalregistrator daher mit Recht bemerkt: „Der Zuwachs von\n Pächtern und Landarbeitern seit 1801 steht in gar keinem Verhältniss\n zum Zuwachs des agrikolen Produkts“ [Note: Census etc. l. c. p. 36.] , so gilt diess Missverhältniss\n noch viel mehr von der letzten Periode, wo positive Abnahme der\n ländlichen Arbeiterbevölkerung Hand in Hand ging mit Aus-\n dehnung des bebauten Areals, intensiverer Kultur, unerhörter Accumula-\n tion des dem Boden einverleibten und des seiner Bearbeitung gewidmeten\n Kapitals, Steigerung des Bodenprodukts ohne Parallele in der Geschichte\n der englischen Agronomie, strotzenden Rentrollen der Grundeigenthümer\n und schwellendem Reichthum der kapitalistischen Pächter.","kind":"passage","sequence":272,"address":"20.272"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0273","text":"Nimmt man\n diess zusammen mit der ununterbrochen raschen Erweiterung des städtischen\n Absatzmarkts und der Herrschaft des Freihandels, so war der Landar-\n beiter post tot discrimina rerum endlich in Verhältnisse gestellt, die ihn,\nsecundum artem, glückstoll machen mussten.","kind":"passage","sequence":273,"address":"20.273"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0274","text":"Professor Rogers gelangt dagegen zum Resultat, dass der englische\n Landarbeiter heutigen Tags, gar nicht zu sprechen von seinem Vorgänger\n in der letzten Hälfte des 14. Jahrhunderts und im 15. Jahrhundert, sondern\n nur verglichen mit seinem Vorgänger aus der Periode 1770—1780, seine\n Lage ausserordentlich verschlechtert hat, dass „er wieder ein Leibeigner\n geworden ist“ und zwar schlecht gefütterter und behauster Leibeigner [Note: Rogers l. c. p. 693. „The peasant has again become a serf.“ l. c.\n p. 10. Herr Rogers gehört zur liberalen Schule, ist persönlicher Freund von\n Cobden und Bright, also kein laudator temporis acti.] .\nDr.","kind":"passage","sequence":274,"address":"20.274"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0275","text":"Julian Hunter, in seinem epochemachenden Bericht über die\n Wohnlichkeit der Landarbeiter, sagt: „Die Existenzkost des hind (der\n Zeit der Leibeigenschaft angehöriger Name für den Landarbeiter) ist fixirt\n zu dem möglichst niedrigen Betrag, womit er leben kann … seine Zufuhr\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"667\", \"facs\": \"#f0686\"} --> von Lohn und Obdach ist nicht berechnet auf den aus ihm herauszu-\n schlagenden Profit. Er ist der Nullpunkt in den Berechnungen des Päch-\n ters“ [Note: „Public Health. Seventh Report. Lond. 1865“, p. 242.\n „The cost of the hind is fixed at the lowest possible amount on which he can live\n … the supplies of wages or shelter are not calculated on the profit to be derived\n from him.","kind":"passage","sequence":275,"address":"20.275"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0276","text":"He is a zero in farming calculations.“ Es ist daher nichts ungewöhn-\n liches, dass entweder der Hausvermiether die Miethe für einen Arbeiter erhöht, so-\n bald er hört, dass er etwas mehr verdient, oder dass der Pächter den Lohn des Ar-\n beiters heruntersetzt, „weil seine Frau eine Beschäftigung gefunden hat.“ (l. c.)] . „Seine Subsistenzmittel werden stets als eine fixe Quantität be-\n handelt“ [Note: l. c. p. 135.] . „Was irgend eine weitere Reduktion seines Einkommens\n angeht, so kann er sagen: nihil habeo, nihil curo. Er hat keine Furcht\n für die Zukunft, weil er über nichts verfügt ausser dem, was zu seiner\n Existenz absolut unentbehrlich ist. Er hat den Gefrierpunkt erreicht,\n von dem die Berechnungen des Pächters als Datum ausgehn. Komme was\n wolle, er hat keinen Antheil an Glück oder Unglück“ [Note: l. c. p. 134.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":276,"address":"20.276"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0277","text":"Im Jahr 1863 fand eine officielle Untersuchung über die Verpflegungs-\n und Beschäftigungszustände der zu Transportation und öffentlicher Zwangs-\n arbeit verurtheilten Verbrecher statt. Die Resultate sind in zwei dickleibigen\n Blaubüchern niedergelegt. „Eine sorgfältige Vergleichung,“ heisst es\n unter anderem, „zwischen der Diät der Verbrecher in den Gefängnissen von\n England und der der Paupers in Workhouses und der freien Landarbeiter\n desselben Landes zeigt unstreitig, dass die erstern viel besser genährt sind\n als irgend eine der beiden andern Klassen“ [Note: „Report of the Commissioners … relating to Transpor-\n tation and Penal Servitude. Lond. 1863“, v. I, n. 50.] , während „die Arbeitsmasse,\n die von einem zu öffentlicher Zwangsarbeit Verurtheilten verlangt wird,\n ungefähr die Hälfte der vom gewöhnlichen Landarbeiter verrichteten be-\n trägt“ [Note: l. c. p. 77.","kind":"passage","sequence":277,"address":"20.277"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0278","text":"„Memorandum by the Lord Chief Justice.“] . Einige wenige charakteristische Zeugenaussagen: John Smith,\n Direktor des Gefängnisses von Edinburg, verhört. Nr. 5056: „Die Diät\n in den englischen Gefängnissen ist viel besser als die der gewöhnlichen\n Landarbeiter.“ Nr. 5075: „Es ist Thatsache, dass die gewöhnlichen\n Agrikulturarbeiter Schottlands sehr selten irgend welches Fleisch erhal-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"668\", \"facs\": \"#f0687\"} --> ten.“ Nr. 3047: „Kennen Sie irgend einen Grund für die Nothwendig-\n keit, die Verbrecher viel besser (much better) zu nähren als gewöhnliche\n Landarbeiter? — Sicher nicht.“ Nr. 3048: „Halten Sie es für ange-\n messen weitere Experimente zu machen, um die Diät zu öffentlichen\n Zwangsarbeiten verurtheilter Gefangenen der Diät freier Landarbeiter\n nahe zu bringen?“ [Note: l. c. v. II.","kind":"passage","sequence":278,"address":"20.278"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0279","text":"Evidence.]  „Der Landarbeiter,“ heisst es, „könnte sagen:\n Ich arbeite hart und habe nicht genug zu essen. Als ich im Gefängniss\n war, arbeitete ich nicht so hart und hatte Essen in Fülle, und darum ist\n es besser für mich im Gefängniss als im Freien zu sein“ [Note: l. c. v. I. Appendix p. 280] . Aus den\n dem ersten Band des Berichts angehängten Tabellen ist folgende verglei-\n chende Uebersicht zusammengestellt:\nWöchentlicher Nahrungsbetrag.","kind":"passage","sequence":279,"address":"20.279"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0280","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDas allgemeine Resultat der ärztlichen Untersuchungskommission von\n 1863 über den Nahrungszustand der schlechter genährten Volksklassen\n ist dem Leser bereits bekannt. Er erinnert sich, dass die Diät eines\n grossen Theils der Landarbeiterfamilien unter dem Minimalmass „zur\n Abwehr von Hungerkrankheiten“ steht. Es ist diess namentlich der Fall\n in allen rein agrikolen Distrikten von Cornwall, Devon, Somerset, Dorset,\n Wilts, Stafford, Oxford, Berks und Herts. „Die Nahrung, die der Land-\n arbeiter erhält,“ sagt Dr. Simon, „ist grösser als das Durchschnitts-\n quantum anzeigt, da er selbst einen viel grösseren,  [Editorial alternatives: sic = fur; corr = für]  seine Arbeit unentbehr-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"669\", \"facs\": \"#f0688\"} --> lichen, Theil der Lebensmittel erhält als seine übrigen Familienglieder, in den\n ärmeren Distrikten fast alles Fleisch oder Speck.","kind":"passage","sequence":280,"address":"20.280"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0281","text":"Das Quantum Nahrung, das\n der Frau zufällt, und ebenso den Kindern in ihrer Periode raschen Wachsthums,\n ist in vielen Fällen, und zwar in fast allen Grafschaften, mangelhaft,\n hauptsächlich an Stickstoff“ [Note: „Public Health. Sixth Report. 1863“. Lond. 1864, p. 238,\n 249, 261, 262.] . Die bei den Pächtern selbst wohnenden\n Knechte und Mägde werden reichlich genährt. Ihre Zahl fiel von 288,277\n im Jahr 1851 auf 204,962 im Jahr 1861. „Die Arbeit der Weiber auf\n freiem Feld,“ sagt Dr. Smith, „von welchen sonstigen Nachtheilen auch\n immer begleitet, ist unter gegenwärtigen Umständen von grossem Vortheil\n für die Familie, denn sie liefert derselben Mittel für Beschuhung, Kleidung,\n Zahlung der Hausrente, und befähigt sie so besser zu essen“ [Note: l. c. p. 262.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":281,"address":"20.281"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0282","text":"Eins\n der merkwürdigsten Resultate dieser Untersuchung war, dass der Landar-\n beiter in England bei weitem schlechter genährt ist als in den andern\n Theilen des Vereinigten Königreichs („is considerably the worst fed“),\n wie die folgende Tabelle zeigt:\nWöchentlicher Konsum von Kohle und Stickstoff durch den ländlichen Durch-\n schnittsarbeiter.","kind":"passage","sequence":282,"address":"20.282"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0283","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"670\", \"facs\": \"#f0689\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":283,"address":"20.283"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0284","text":"„Jede Seite von Dr. Hunters Bericht“, sagt Dr. Simon in seinem\n officiellen Gesundheitsbericht, „giebt Zeugniss von der unzureichenden\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"671\", \"facs\": \"#f0690\"} --> Quantität und miserablen Qualität der Wohnlichkeit unsres Land-\n arbeiters. Und progressiv seit vielen Jahren hat sich sein Zustand in\n dieser Hinsicht verschlechtert. Es ist jetzt viel schwerer für ihn, Haus-\n raum zu finden, und, wenn gefunden, ist er seinen Bedürfnissen viel weni-\n ger entsprechend, als vielleicht seit Jahrhunderten der Fall war.\nBesonders innerhalb der letzten 30 oder 20 Jahre ist das\n Uebel in raschem Wachsthum begriffen, und die Wohnlichkeitsverhältnisse\n des Landmanns sind jetzt im höchsten Grad kläglich. Ausser\n soweit diejenigen, die seine Arbeit bereichert, es der Mühe werth\n halten ihn mit einer Art von mitleidiger Nachsicht zu behandeln, ist er\n ganz hilflos in der Sache.","kind":"passage","sequence":284,"address":"20.284"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0285","text":"Ob er Behausung findet auf dem Land, wel-\n ches er bebaut, ob sie menschlich oder schweinisch ist, ob mit kleinem\n Garten, der den Druck der Armuth so sehr erleichtert, alles das hängt\n nicht von seiner Bereitheit oder Fähigkeit zur Zahlung einer angemessnen\n Miethe ab, sondern von dem Gebrauch, den Andre von ‚dem Recht mit\n ihrem Eigenthum zu thun was sie wollen‘ zu machen belieben.","kind":"passage","sequence":285,"address":"20.285"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0286","text":"Eine\n Pacht mag noch so gross sein, es existirt kein Gesetz, dass auf ihr eine\n bestimmte Anzahl von Arbeiterwohnungen, und nun gar anständigen,\n stehen muss; noch behält das Gesetz dem Arbeiter auch nur das kleinste\n Recht auf den Boden vor, für welchen seine Arbeit so nothwendig ist wie\n Regen und Sonnenschein … Ein notorischer Umstand wirft noch ein\n schweres Gewicht in die Wagschale gegen ihn …, der Einfluss des Ar-\n mengesetzes mit seinen Bestimmungen über Niederlassung und Belastung\n zur Armentaxe [Note: 1865 ist diess Gesetz etwas verbessert worden. Man wird bald durch die\n Erfahrung lernen, dass dergleichen Pfuscherei nichts hilft.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":286,"address":"20.286"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0287","text":"Unter seinem Einfluss hat jede Pfarrei ein Geldinter-\n esse die Zahl ihrer residirenden Landarbeiter auf ein Mini-\n mum zu beschränken; denn, unglücklicher Weise, die Land-\n arbeit, statt sichre und permanente Unabhängigkeit des hartschanzenden\n Arbeiters und seiner Familie zu verbürgen, involvirt meist nur einen\n längern oder kürzern Umweg zu eventuellem Pauperismus,\n ein Pauperismus, der während des ganzen Wegs so nahe ist, dass jede\n Krankheit oder irgend ein vorübergehender Mangel an Beschäftigung unmit-\n telbar die Zuflucht zur Pfarreihilfe ernöthigt; und daher ist alle Resi-\n denz einer Ackerbaubevölkerung in einer Pfarrei augenschein-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"672\", \"facs\": \"#f0691\"} --> lich ein Zuschuss zu ihrer Armensteuer … Grosse Grund-\n eigenthümer [Note: Zum Verständniss des folgenden: Close Villages (geschlossene Dörfer)\n heissen die, deren Grundeigenthümer ein oder ein","kind":"passage","sequence":287,"address":"20.287"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0288","text":"paar grosse Landlords. Open\n Villages (offene Dörfer) die, deren Boden vielen kleineren Eigenthümern ge-\n hört. Es sind die letzteren Orte, wo Bauspekulanten Cottages und Logirhäuser\n errichten können.]  haben nur zu beschliessen, dass keine Arbeiterwohnungen\n auf ihren Gütern stehn sollen, und sie befreien sich sofort von der Hälfte\n ihrer Verantwortlichkeit für die Armen. Wie weit die englische Konsti-\n tution und das Gesetz diese Art unbedingtes Grundeigenthum beabsichtig-\n ten, welches einen Landlord, der „mit seinem Eignen thut was er will“,\n befähigt, die Bebauer des Bodens wie Fremde zu behandeln und sie von\n seinem Territorium zu verjagen, ist eine Frage, deren Diskussion nicht in\n meinen Bereich fällt … Diese Macht der Eviktion ist keine blosse\n Theorie. Sie wird praktisch auf der grössten Stufenleiter geltend ge-\n macht.","kind":"passage","sequence":288,"address":"20.288"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0289","text":"Sie ist einer der Umstände, welche die Wohnlichkeitsverhältnisse\n des Landarbeiters beherrschen … Den Umfang des Uebels mag man aus\n dem letzten Census beurtheilen, wonach die Zerstörung von Häu-\n sern, trotz vermehrter lokaler Nachfrage für dieselben,\n während der letzten 10 Jahre, in 821 verschiednen Distrikten von Eng-\n land fortschritt, so dass, abgesehn von den Personen, die gezwungen wur-\n den Nichtresidirende (nämlich in dem Kirchspiel, worin sie arbei-\n ten) zu werden, 1861 verglichen mit 1851 eine um 5⅓ % grössere Be-\n völkerung in einen um 4½ % kleineren Hausraum gedrängt wurde …\n Sobald der Entvölkerungsprozess sein Ziel erreicht hat, ist das Resultat,\n sagt Dr.","kind":"passage","sequence":289,"address":"20.289"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0290","text":"Hunter, ein Schaudorf (show-village), wo die Cottages auf\n wenige reducirt sind und wo niemand leben darf ausser Schafhirten,\n Gärtnern und Wildhütern, reguläre Bediente, welche die ihrer Klasse ge-\n wohnte gute Behandlung von der gnädigen Herrschaft erhalten [Note: Ein solches Schaudorf sieht sehr nett aus, aber es ist so unreal, wie die\n Dörfer, welche Catharina II. auf der Reise nach der Krim sah. In der letzteren\n Zeit wird auch der Schafhirt häufig aus diesen show-villages verbannt. Z. B. bei\n Market Harborough ist eine Schäferei von ungefähr 500 Acres, die nur die Arbeit\neines Mannes erheischt. Zur Verminderung der langen Märsche über diese\n weiten Flächen, die schönen Weiden von Leicester und Northampton, pflegte der\n Hirt eine Cottage auf der Meierei zu erhalten. Jetzt giebt man ihm einen drei-\n zehnten Shilling für Logis, das er weitab in dem offenen Dorf suchen muss.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":290,"address":"20.290"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0291","text":"Aber\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"673\", \"facs\": \"#f0692\"} --> das Land bedarf der Bebauung, und man wird finden, dass die darauf\n beschäftigten Arbeiter keine Haussassen des Grundeigenthümers sind, son-\n dern von einem offnen Dorf herkommen, vielleicht 3 Meilen weit ent-\n fernt, wo eine zahlreiche kleine Hauseigenthümerschaft sie aufnahm, nach\n Zerstörung ihrer Cottages in den geschlossnen Dörfern. Wo die Dinge\n diesem Resultat zustreben, bezeugen die Cottages meist durch ihr elendes\n Aussehn das Schicksal, zu dem sie verdammt sind. Man findet sie auf\n den verschiednen Stufen natürlichen Verfalls. So lange das Obdach zu-\n sammenhält, wird dem Arbeiter erlaubt, Rente dafür zu zahlen, und er ist\n oft sehr froh diess thun zu dürfen, selbst wenn er den Preis einer guten\n Wohnung zu zahlen hat. Aber keine Reparatur, keine Ausbesserung,\n ausser die der pfenniglose Inhaber leisten kann.","kind":"passage","sequence":291,"address":"20.291"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0292","text":"Wird es endlich zuletzt\n ganz unbewohnbar, so ist es nur eine zerstörte Cottage mehr und so viel\n künftige Armensteuer weniger. Während die grossen Eigenthümer die\n Armentaxe so von sich abwälzen durch Entvölkerung des von ihnen kon-\n trolirten Grund und Bodens, nimmt das nächste Landstädtchen oder offne\n Ortschaft die hinausgeworfnen Arbeiter auf; die nächste, sage ich; aber\n diess „nächste“ mag 3 oder 4 Meilen vom Pachthof entfernt sein, wo der\n Arbeiter sich täglich abzuplacken hat. So wird seinem Tages-\n werk, als ob es gar nichts sei, die Nothwendigkeit eines\n täglichen Marsches von 6 oder 8 Meilen zur Verdienung seines\n täglichen Brodes hinzugefügt. Alle von seiner Frau und seinen Kindern\n verrichtete Landarbeit geht jetzt unter denselben erschwerenden Umstän-\n den vor. Und diess ist nicht das ganze Uebel, welches ihm die Entfer-\n nung verursacht.","kind":"passage","sequence":292,"address":"20.292"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0293","text":"In der offnen Ortschaft kaufen Bauspekulanten Boden-\n fetzen, welche sie so dicht wie möglich mit den wohlfeilsten aller mög-\n lichen Spelunken besäen. Und in diesen elenden Wohnlichkeiten, die\n sogar, wenn sie auf das offne Land münden, die ungeheuerlichsten\n Charakterzüge der schlechtesten Stadtwohnungen theilen,\n hocken die Agrikulturarbeiter Englands [Note: „Die Häuser der Arbeiter (in den offenen Ortschaften, die natürlich stets\n überfüllt sind) sind gewöhnlich in Reihen gebaut, mit dem Rücken auf der äusser-\n sten Kante des Bodenfetzens, den der Bauspekulant sein nennt. Sie sind daher\n ohne Zutritt von Licht und Luft, ausser von der Frontseite.“ (Dr. Hunter’s\n Report l. c. p. 135.) Sehr oft ist der Bierwirth oder Krämer des Dorfs zugleich]  … Andrerseits muss man\n [Running matter: I.","kind":"passage","sequence":293,"address":"20.293"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0294","text":"43] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"674\", \"facs\": \"#f0693\"} --> sich nur nicht einbilden, dass selbst der auf dem Grund und Boden, den er\n bebaut, behauste Arbeiter eine Wohnlichkeit findet, wie sie sein Leben\n produktiver Industrie verdient. Selbst auf den fürstlichsten Gütern ist seine\n Cottage oft von der allerjämmerlichsten Art. Es giebt Landlords, die\n einen Stall gut genug für ihre Arbeiter und deren Familien glauben, und\n die es dennoch nicht verschmähn aus ihrer Miethe so viel Baares als\n möglich herauszuschlagen [Note: „Der Hausvermiether (der Pächter oder Landlord) bereichert sich direkt oder\n indirekt durch die Arbeit eines Mannes, dem er 10 sh. per Woche zahlt, und zwackt\n dann wieder von diesem armen Teufel 4 oder 5 Pfd. St. jährliche Miethe\n für Häuser ab, die keine 20 Pfd. St.","kind":"passage","sequence":294,"address":"20.294"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0295","text":"auf offenem Markt werth sind, aber auf\n ihrem künstlichen Preis erhalten werden durch die Macht des Eigenthümers zu\n sagen: „Nimm mein Haus, oder pack’ Dich und suche anderswo ein Unterkommen,\n ohne Charaktercertifikat von mir“ … Wünscht ein Mann sich zu verbessern und\n als Schienenleger zu einer Eisenbahn zu gehn oder einem Steinbruch, wieder ist\n dieselbe Macht bereit mit einem: „Arbeite für mich zu diesem niedrigen Arbeits-\n lohn, oder pack’ Dich auf eine Woche Kündigung; nimm Dein Schwein mit Dir,\n wenn Du eins hast, und schau’ zu, was Du aus den Kartoffeln herausschlägst, die\n in Deinem Garten wachsen.“ Steht jedoch das Interesse nach der andern Seite,\n so zieht in solchen Fällen der Eigenthümer [resp. Pächter] manchmal eine er-\n höhte Hausmiethe vor als Strafe für die Desertion aus seinem Dienst.“\n (Dr. Hunter l. c. p. 131.)] .","kind":"passage","sequence":295,"address":"20.295"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0296","text":"Es mag nur eine verfallende Hütte mit\n [Note: Hausvermiether. In diesem Fall findet der Landarbeiter in ihm einen zweiten\n Meister neben dem Pächter. Er muss zugleich sein Miether und Kunde sein. „Mit\n 10 sh. per Woche, minus einer jährlichen Rente von 4 Pfd. St., ist er verpflichtet,\n sein modicum von Thee, Zucker, Mehl, Seife, Kerzen und Bier zu den dem Krämer\n beliebten Preisen zu kaufen.“ (l. c. p. 131.) Diese offenen Dorfschaften bilden\n in der That die „Strafkolonieen“ des englischen Ackerbauprole-\n tariats. Viele der Cottages sind reine Logirhäuser, wo alles vagabundirende\n Gesindel der Umgegend durchpassirt. Der Landmann und seine Familie, die oft\n wahrhaft wunderbar in den schmutzigsten Verhältnissen Tüchtigkeit und Reinheit\n des Charakters bewahrt hatten, gehn hier platterdings zum Teufel.","kind":"passage","sequence":296,"address":"20.296"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0297","text":"Es ist natürlich\n Mode unter den vornehmen Shylocks über die Bauspekulanten und die kleinen\n Eigenthümer und die offenen Orte pharisäisch die Achsel zu zucken. Sie wissen\n sehr wohl, dass ihre „geschlossenen Dörfer“ und „Schaudörfer“ die Geburts-\n stätten der „offenen Orte“ sind und ohne dieselben nicht existiren könnten.\n „Ohne die kleinen Eigenthümer der offenen Orte müsste der grösste Theil der\n Landarbeiter unter den Bäumen der Güter schlafen, worauf sie arbeiten.“\n l. c. p. 135.) Das System der „offenen“ und „geschlossenen“ Dörfer herrscht\n in allen Midlands und im ganzen Osten Englands.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"675\", \"facs\": \"#f0694\"} -->einer Schlafstube sein, ohne Feuerherd, ohne Abtritt, ohne öffenbare\n Fenster, ohne Wasserzufuhr ausser dem Graben, ohne Garten, der Arbeiter\n ist hilflos gegen die Unbill.","kind":"passage","sequence":297,"address":"20.297"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0298","text":"Und unsere gesundheitspolizeilichen Gesetze\n („The Nuisances Removal Acts“) sind ein todter Buchstabe. Ihre Aus-\n führung ist ja grade den Eigenthümern anvertraut, welche solche Löcher\n vermiethen … Man muss sich durch ausnahmsweise lichtvollere Scenen\n nicht blenden lassen über das erdrückende Uebergewicht der Thatsachen,\n die ein Schandfleck der englischen Civilisation sind. Schauderhaft muss\n in der That die Lage der Dinge sein, wenn, trotz der augenfälligen Unge-\n heuerlichkeit der gegenwärtigen Behausung, kompetente Beobachter ein-\n stimmig zu dem Schlussresultat gelangen, dass selbst die allgemeine\n Nichtswürdigkeit der Wohnungen noch ein unendlich minder drückendes\n Uebel ist als ihr bloss numerischer Mangel.","kind":"passage","sequence":298,"address":"20.298"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0299","text":"Seit Jahren war die Ueber-\n stopfung der Wohnungen der Landarbeiter ein Gegenstand tiefen Kum-\n mers nicht nur für Personen, die auf Gesundheit, sondern für alle, die auf\n anständiges und moralisches Leben halten. Denn, wieder und wieder, in\n Phrasen so gleichförmig, dass sie stereotypirt zu sein scheinen, denunciren\n die Berichterstatter über die Verbreitung epidemischer Krankheiten in den\n Ruraldistrikten Haus-Ueberfüllung als einen Einfluss, der jeden Versuch,\n den Fortschritt einer einmal eingeführten Epidemie aufzuhalten, durchaus\n vereitelt. Und wieder und wieder ward nachgewiesen, dass den vielen\n gesundheitlichen Einflüssen des Landlebens zum Trotz die Agglomeration,\n welche das Umsichgreifen ansteckender Krankheiten so sehr beschleunigt,\n auch die Entstehung nicht ansteckender Krankheiten fördert.","kind":"passage","sequence":299,"address":"20.299"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0300","text":"Und die\n Personen, welche diesen Zustand denuncirt haben, verschwiegen weiteres\n Unheil nicht. Selbst wo ihr ursprüngliches Thema nur die Gesundheits-\n pflege betraf, waren sie beinahe gezwungen auf die andern Seiten des\n Gegenstandes einzugehn. Indem sie nachwiesen, wie häufig es sich er-\n eignet, dass erwachsne Personen beiderlei Geschlechts, verheirathet und\n unverheirathet, zusammengehudelt („huddled“) werden in engen Schlaf-\n stuben, mussten ihre Berichte die Ueberzeugung hervorrufen, dass unter\n den beschriebenen Umständen Scham- und Anstandsgefühl aufs gröbste\n verletzt und alle Moralität fast nothwendig ruinirt wird [Note: „Jung verheirathete Paare sind kein erbauliches Studium für erwachsne\n Brüder und Schwestern in derselben Schlafstube; und obgleich Beispiele nicht] .\n [Running matter: 43*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"676\", \"facs\": \"#f0695\"} --> … Z. B.","kind":"passage","sequence":300,"address":"20.300"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0301","text":"im Appendix meines letzten Berichts erwähnt Dr. Ord, in sei-\n nem Bericht über den Fieberausbruch zu Wing in Buckinghamshire, wie\n ein junger Mann von Wingrave mit Fieber dorthin kam. In den ersten\n Tagen seiner Krankheit schlief er mit 9 andern Personen in einem Gemach\n zusammen. In zwei Wochen wurden verschiedne dieser Personen ange-\n griffen, im Verlauf weniger Wochen verfielen 5 von den 9 Personen dem\n Fieber und eine starb! Gleichzeitig berichtete mir Dr. Harvey vom St. Geor-\n ge’s Spital, der Wing während der Epidemiezeit in Angelegenheiten seiner\n Privatpraxis besuchte, in demselben Sinne: „Ein junges, fieberkrankes\n Frauenzimmer schlief Nachts in derselben Stube mit Vater, Mutter, ihrem\n Bastardkind, zwei jungen Männern, ihren Brüdern, und ihren zwei Schwe-\n stern, jede mit einem Bastard, in allem 10 Personen.","kind":"passage","sequence":301,"address":"20.301"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0302","text":"Wenige Wochen\n vorher schliefen 13 Kinder in demselben Raume“ [Note: Public Health. Seventh Report. Lond. 1865, p. 9—14\npassim.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":302,"address":"20.302"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0303","text":"Dr. Hunter untersuchte 5375 Landarbeiter-Cottages, nicht nur\n in den reinen Agrikulturdistrikten, sondern in allen Grafschaften Englands.\n Unter diesen 5375 hatten 2195 nur eine Schlafstube (oft zugleich Wohn-\n stube), 2930 nur 2 und 250 mehr als 2. Ich will für ein Dutzend Graf-\n schaften eine kurze Blüthenlese geben.","kind":"passage","sequence":303,"address":"20.303"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0304","text":"1) Bedfordshire.","kind":"passage","sequence":304,"address":"20.304"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0305","text":"Wrestlingworth: Schlafzimmer ungefähr 12 Fuss lang und\n 10 breit, obgleich viele kleiner sind. Die kleine einstöckige Hütte wird\n oft durch Bretter in zwei Schlafstuben getheilt, oft ein Bett in einer Küche\n 5 Fuss 6 Zoll hoch. Miethe 3 Pfd. St. Die Miether haben ihre eignen\n Abtritte zu bauen, der Hauseigenthümer liefert nur ein Loch. So oft einer\n [Note: registrirt werden dürfen, liegen hinreichende Data vor, um die Bemerkung zu\n rechtfertigen, dass grosses Leid und oft der Tod das Loos der weiblichen Theil-\n nehmer am Verbrechen der Blutschande ist.“ (Dr. Hunter l. c. p. 137.)","kind":"passage","sequence":305,"address":"20.305"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0306","text":"Ein\n ländlicher Polizeibeamter, der viele Jahre durch als Detectiv in den schlechtesten\n Vierteln von London funktionirte, sagt von den Mädchen seines Dorfs aus: „Ihre\n grobe Immoralität im frühen Alter, ihre Frechheit und Schamlosigkeit habe ich\n niemals während meines Polizeilebens in den schlechtesten Theilen von London\n erreicht gesehn … Sie leben wie Schweine, grosse Jungen und Mädchen, Mütter\n und Väter, alles schläft zusammen in derselben Stube.“ („Child. Empl.\n Comm. Sixth Report. Lond. 1867“, Appendix, p. 77, n. 155.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"677\", \"facs\": \"#f0696\"} --> einen Abtritt baut, wird letzterer von der ganzen Nachbarschaft benutzt. Ein\n Haus Namens Richardson von unerreichbarer Schöne. Seine Mörtelwände\n bauschten aus wie ein Damenkleid beim Knix.","kind":"passage","sequence":306,"address":"20.306"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0307","text":"Ein Giebelende war kon-\n vex, das andre konkav, und auf dem letztern stand unglücklicher Weise\n der Schornstein, ein krummes Rohr von Lehm und Holz gleich einem\n Elephantenrüssel. Ein langer Stock diente als Stütze, um den Fall des\n Schornsteins zu verhindern, Thüre und Fenster rautenförmig. Von 17 be-\n suchten Häusern nur 4 mit mehr als 1 Schlafzimmer und diese 4 über-\n stopft. Die einschläfrigen Cots bargen 3 Erwachsne mit 3 Kindern, ein\n verheirathetes Paar mit 6 Kindern u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":307,"address":"20.307"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0308","text":"Dunton: Hohe Haus-Renten, von 4 bis 5 Pfd. St., Wochenlohn der\n Männer 10 sh. Sie hoffen durch Strohflechten der Familie die Miethe\n herauszuschlagen. Je höher die Hausmiethe, desto grösser die Zahl, die\n sich zusammenthun muss, um sie zu zahlen. Sechs Erwachsne, die mit\n 4 Kindern in einer Schlafstube, zahlen dafür 3 Pfd. St. 10 sh. Das\n wohlfeilste Haus in Dunton, von der Aussenseite 15 Fuss lang, 10 breit,\n vermiethet für 3 Pfd. St. Nur eins von den 14 untersuchten Häusern\n hatte zwei Schlafstuben. Etwas vor dem Dorf ein Haus, von den Insas-\n sen bekothet vor seinen Aussenwänden, die untern 5 Zoll der Thüre ver-\n schwunden durch reinen Verfaulungsprozess, einige Ziegelsteine von innen\n sinnreich des Abends beim Zuschliessen vorgeschoben und mit etwas Matte\n verhangen. Ein halbes Fenster, sammt Glas und Rahmen, war ganz den\n Weg allen Fleisches gegangen.","kind":"passage","sequence":308,"address":"20.308"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0309","text":"Hier, ohne Möbel, hudelten 3 Erwachsne\n und 5 Kinder zusammen. Dunton ist nicht schlimmer als der Rest der\n Biggleswude Union.","kind":"passage","sequence":309,"address":"20.309"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0310","text":"2) Berkshire.","kind":"passage","sequence":310,"address":"20.310"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0311","text":"Beenham: Juni 1864 lebte ein Mann, Frau, 4 Kinder in einem\n Cot (einstöckigen Cottage). Eine Tochter kam heim aus dem Dienst mit\n Scharlachfieber. Sie starb. Ein Kind erkrankte und starb. Die Mutter\n und ein Kind litten an Typhus, als Dr. Hunter gerufen wurde. Der\n Vater und ein Kind schliefen auswärts, aber die Schwierigkeit Isolirung\n zu sichern, zeigte sich hier, denn im vollgepfropften Markt des elenden\n Dorfs lag das Leinen des fiebergeschlagnen Hauses, auf Wäsche wartend.\n — Die Miethe von H.’s Haus 1 sh. wöchentlich; das eine Schlafzimmer\n für 1 Paar und 6 Kinder. Ein Haus vermiethet zu 8 d. (wöchentlich),\n 14 Fuss 6 Zoll lang, 7 Fuss breit, Küche 6 Fuss hoch; das Schlafzimmer\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"678\", \"facs\": \"#f0697\"} --> ohne Fenster, Feuerplatz, Thüre, noch Oeffnung, ausser nach dem Gang\n zu, kein Garten.","kind":"passage","sequence":311,"address":"20.311"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0312","text":"Ein Mann lebte hier vor kurzem mit zwei erwachsnen\n Töchtern und einem aufwachsenden Sohn; Vater und Sohn schliefen auf\n dem Bett, die Mädchen auf dem Hausgang. Jede hatte ein Kind, so lange\n die Familie hier lebte, aber eine ging zum Workhouse für ihre Entbindung\n und kehrte dann heim.","kind":"passage","sequence":312,"address":"20.312"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0313","text":"3) Buckinghamshire.","kind":"passage","sequence":313,"address":"20.313"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0314","text":"30 Cottages — auf 1000 Acres Land — enthalten hier ungefähr\n 130—140 Personen. Die Pfarrei von Bradenham umfasst 1000 Acres;\n sie hatte 1851 36 Häuser und eine Bevölkerung von 84 Manns- und 54\n Weibspersonen. Diese geschlechtliche Ungleichheit geheilt 1861, wo sie\n 98 männlichen und 87 weiblichen Geschlechts zählte, Zuwachs in 10 Jah-\n ren von 14 Männern und 33 Weibern. Unterdess hatte die Häuserzahl\n um 1 abgenommen.","kind":"passage","sequence":314,"address":"20.314"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0315","text":"Winslow: Grosser Theil davon neu gebaut in gutem Styl; Nach-\n frage nach Häusern scheint bedeutend, weil sehr armselige Cots vermie-\n thet zu 1 sh. und 1 sh. 3 d. per Woche.","kind":"passage","sequence":315,"address":"20.315"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0316","text":"Water Eaton: Hier haben die Eigenthümer im Angesicht wach-\n sender Bevölkerung ungefähr 20 % der existirenden Häuser zerstört.\n Ein armer Arbeiter, der ungefähr 4 Meilen zu seinem Werk zu gehn hatte,\n antwortete auf die Frage, ob er kein Cot näher finden könnte: „Nein, sie\n werden sich verdammt hüten, einen Mann mit meiner grossen Familie auf-\n zunehmen.“","kind":"passage","sequence":316,"address":"20.316"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0317","text":"Tinker’s End, bei Winslow: Eine Schlafstube, worin 4 Er-\n wachsne und 4 Kinder, 11 Fuss lang, 9 Fuss breit, 6 Fuss 5 Zoll hoch\n am höchsten Punkt; ein andres 11 Fuss 3 Zoll lang, 9 Fuss breit, 5 Fuss\n 10 Zoll hoch, beherbergte 6 Personen. Jede dieser Familien hatte weni-\n ger Raum als nöthig für einen Galeerensträfling. Kein Haus hatte mehr\n als ein Schlafzimmer, keins eine Hinterthür, Wasser sehr selten, Wochen-\n miethe von 1 sh. 4 d. zu 2 sh. In 16 untersuchten Häusern nur ein ein-\n ziger Mann, der 10 sh. wöchentlich verdiente. Das Luftreservoir, jeder\n Person in dem erwähnten Falle gegönnt, entspricht dem, das ihr zu gut\n käme, wenn des Nachts eingeschlossen in eine Schachtel von 4 Kubikfuss.\n Allerdings bieten die alten Hütten eine Masse naturwüchsiger Ventilation.","kind":"passage","sequence":317,"address":"20.317"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0318","text":"4) Cambridgeshire.","kind":"passage","sequence":318,"address":"20.318"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0319","text":"Gamblingay gehört verschiednen Eigenthümern. Es enthält die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"679\", \"facs\": \"#f0698\"} --> lumpigsten Cots, die man irgendwo finden kann. Viel Strohflechterei. Eine\n tödtliche Mattheit, eine hoffnungslose Ergebung in Schmutz beherrscht Gam-\n blingay. Die Vernachlässigung in seinem Centrum wird zur Tortur an den Ex-\n tremitäten, Nord und Süd, wo die Häuser stückweis abfaulen. Die abwesenden\n Landlords lassen dem armen Nest frei zur Ader. Die Miethen sind sehr\n hoch, 8 bis 9 Personen gepackt in ein einschläfriges Zimmer, in zwei\n Fällen 6 Erwachsne mit je 1 und 2 Kindern in einer kleinen Schlafstube.","kind":"passage","sequence":319,"address":"20.319"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0320","text":"5) Essex: In dieser Grafschaft gehn in vielen Pfarreien Abnahme von\n Personen und Cottages Hand in Hand. In nicht weniger als 22 Pfarreien\n hat jedoch die Häuserzerstörung den Bevölkerungsanwachs nicht aufgehalten,\n oder nicht die Expulsion bewirkt, welche unter dem Namen: „Wanderung\n nach den Städten“ überall vorgeht. In Fingringhoe, einer Pfarrei\n von 3443 Acres, standen 1851 145 Häuser, 1861 nur noch 110, aber\n das Volk wollte nicht fort und brachte es fertig selbst unter dieser Be-\n handlung zuzunehmen. Zu Ramsden Crays bewohnten 1851 252\n Personen 61 Häuser, aber 1861 waren 262 Personen in 49 Häuser ge-\n quetscht. In Basilden lebten 1851 auf 1827 Acres 157 Personen in\n 35 Häusern, am Ende des Decenniums 180 Personen in 27 Häusern.","kind":"passage","sequence":320,"address":"20.320"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0321","text":"In\n den Pfarreien von Fingringhoe, South Fambridge, Widford, Basilden und\n Ramsden Crays lebten 1851 auf 8449 Acres 1392 Personen in 316\n Häusern, 1861 auf demselben Areal 1473 Personen in 249 Häusern.","kind":"passage","sequence":321,"address":"20.321"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0322","text":"6) Herefordshire: Diese kleine Grafschaft hat mehr gelitten vom\n „Evictionsgeist“ als irgend eine andre in England. Zu Madby gehören\n die überstopften Cottages, meist mit 2 Schlafzimmern, grossentheils den\n Pächtern. Sie vermiethen selbe leicht zu 3 oder 4 Pfd. St. per Jahr und\n zahlen Wochenlohn von 9 sh.!","kind":"passage","sequence":322,"address":"20.322"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0323","text":"7) Huntingdonshire.","kind":"passage","sequence":323,"address":"20.323"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0324","text":"Hartford hatte 1851 87 Häuser, kurz nachher 19 Cottages zer-\n stört in dieser kleinen Pfarrei von 1720 Acres; Einwohnerschaft\n 1831 : 452 Personen, 1852: 832 und 1861: 341. Vierzehn ein-\n schläfrige Cots untersucht. In einem 1 verheirathetes Paar, 3 erwachsne\n söhne, 1 erwachsnes Mädchen, 4 Kinder, zusammen 10; in einem andern\n 3 Erwachsne, 6 Kinder. Eine dieser Stuben, worin 8 Personen schlie-\n fen, mass 12 Fuss, 10 Zoll in der Länge, 12 Fuss, 2 Zoll breit, 6 Fuss,\n 9 Zoll hoch; Durchschnittsmass, ohne Abzug der Vorsprünge, ergab unge-\n fähr 130 Kubikfuss per Kopf. In den 14 Schlafstuben 34 Erwachsne\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"680\", \"facs\": \"#f0699\"} --> und 33 Kinder. Diese Cottages selten mit Gärtchen versehn, aber viele\n der Insassen konnten kleine Fetzen Land, 10 oder 12 sh. per rood (etwa\n 17 Fuss) pachten. Diese allotments sind entfernt von den abtrittlosen\n Häusern.","kind":"passage","sequence":324,"address":"20.324"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0325","text":"Die Familie muss entweder zu ihrer Parcelle gehn, um ihre\n Exkremente abzulagern, oder, wie es, mit Respekt zu melden, hier ge-\n schieht, die Schublade eines Schranks damit füllen. Sobald er voll, wird\n er ausgezogen und dort entleert, wo sein Inhalt nöthig ist. In Japan\n geht der Cirkellauf der Lebensbedingungen reinlicher von statten.","kind":"passage","sequence":325,"address":"20.325"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0326","text":"8) Lincolnshire.","kind":"passage","sequence":326,"address":"20.326"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0327","text":"Langtofft: Ein Mann wohnt hier in Wright’s Haus mit seiner Frau,\n ihrer Mutter, und 5 Kindern; das Haus hat Vorderküche, Spülkammer,\n Schlafzimmer über der Vorderküche; Vorderküche und Schlafstube 12 Fuss\n 2 Zoll lang, 9 Fuss 5 Zoll breit, die ganze Grundfläche 21 Fuss 3 Zoll\n lang, 9 Fuss 5 Zoll breit. Die Schlafstube ist ein Dachraum, die Wände\n laufen zuckerhutig an der Decke zusammen, und ein Kappfenster öffnet\n sich in der Front. Warum lebte er hier? Garten? Ausserordentlich win-\n zig. Miethe? Hoch, 1 sh. 3 d. per Woche. Nah seiner Arbeit? Nein,\n 6 Meilen entfernt, so dass er täglich 12 Meilen hin und her vermarschirt.\n Er lebte da, weil es ein vermiethbares Cot war und weil er ein Cot für\n sich allein haben wollte, irgendwo, zu irgend einem Preis, in irgend einem\n Zustand.","kind":"passage","sequence":327,"address":"20.327"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0328","text":"Folgendes ist die Statistik von 12 Häusern in Langtofft mit 12\n Schlafstuben, 38 Erwachsnen und 36 Kindern:\n12 Häuser in Langtofft.","kind":"passage","sequence":328,"address":"20.328"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0329","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n9) Kent.","kind":"passage","sequence":329,"address":"20.329"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0330","text":"Kennington, höchst traurig überfüllt 1859, als die Diphtherie\n erschien und der Surgeon des Kirchspiels eine amtliche Untersuchung über\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"681\", \"facs\": \"#f0700\"} --> die Lage der ärmeren Volksklasse veranstaltete. Er fand, dass in dieser\n Ortschaft, wo viel Arbeit nöthig, verschiedne Cots zerstört und keine neuen\n erbaut worden waren. In einem Bezirk standen 4 Häuser, birdcages\n (Vogelkäfige) benamst; jedes hatte 4 Zimmer mit den folgenden Dimen-\n sionen in Fuss und Zoll:","kind":"passage","sequence":330,"address":"20.330"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0331","text":"Küche 9.5 × 8.11 × 6.6","kind":"passage","sequence":331,"address":"20.331"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0332","text":"Spülkammer 8.6 × 4.6 × 6.6","kind":"passage","sequence":332,"address":"20.332"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0333","text":"Schlafzimmer 8.5 × 5.10 × 6.3","kind":"passage","sequence":333,"address":"20.333"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0334","text":"Schlafzimmer 8.3 × 8.4 × 6.3.","kind":"passage","sequence":334,"address":"20.334"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0335","text":"10) Northamptonshire.","kind":"passage","sequence":335,"address":"20.335"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0336","text":"Brinworth, Pickford und Floore: In diesen Dörfern lun-\n gern im Winter 20—30 Mann aus Arbeitsmangel auf den Strassen herum.\n Die Pächter bestellen nicht immer hinreichend das Korn- und Wurzelland,\n und der Landlord hat es passend gefunden alle seine Pachten in 2 oder 3\n zusammenzuwerfen. Daher Mangel an Beschäftigung. Während von der\n einen Seite des Grabens das Feld nach Arbeit schreit, werfen die geprell-\n ten Arbeiter von der anderen Seite sehnsüchtige Blicke danach. Fieber-\n haft überarbeitet im Sommer und halbverhungert im Winter, ist es kein\n Wunder, wenn sie in ihrem eignen Patois sagen, dass „the parson and\n gentlefolks seem frit to death at them.“","kind":"passage","sequence":336,"address":"20.336"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0337","text":"Zu Floore Beispiele von Paaren mit 4, 5, 6 Kindern in einer Schlaf-\n stube kleinster Ausgabe, ditto 3 Erwachsne mit 5 Kindern, ditto ein Paar\n mit Grossvater und 6 scharlachkranken Kindern u. s. w.; in 2 Häusern\n mit 2 Schlafstuben 2 Familien von je 8 und 9 Erwachsnen.","kind":"passage","sequence":337,"address":"20.337"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0338","text":"11) Wiltshire.","kind":"passage","sequence":338,"address":"20.338"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0339","text":"Stratton: 31 Häuser besucht, 8 mit nur einer Schlafstube; Pen-\n till in derselben Pfarrei. Ein Cot vermiethet zu 1 sh. 3 d. wöchentlich\n an 4 Erwachsne und 4 Kinder, hatte ausser guten Wänden nichts Gutes\n an sich, vom Estrich aus rauhgehaunen Steinen bis zum faulen Stroh-\n dach.","kind":"passage","sequence":339,"address":"20.339"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CDAG:P0340","text":"12) Worcestershire. Hauszerstörung hier nicht ganz so arg;\n doch von 1851—1861 vermehrte sich das Personal per Haus von 4.2 zu\n 4.6 Individuen.","kind":"passage","sequence":340,"address":"20.340"}],"sha256":"5206f6f2320a0fd960fa009f23ac9cfba471e4890d2acf58150e918548e9770b","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2","title":"c) Das allgemeine Gesetz der kapitalistischen Accu- mulation. · reading part 2/2","order":21,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_CD_2","text":"Badsey. Viele Cots und Gärtchen hier. Einige Pächter erklären\n die Cots „a great nuisance here, because they bring the poor“. (Die\n Cots grosser Missstand, weil sie die Armen herbringen.) Auf die Aeusse-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"682\", \"facs\": \"#f0701\"} --> rung eines Gentleman: „Die Armen sind desswegen um nichts besser\n dran, wenn man 500 Cots baut, gehn sie wie die Wecken ab, in der That\n je mehr man davon baut, desto mehr sind nöthig“ — die Häuser bringen\n nach ihm die Einwohner hervor, die naturgesetzlich auf „die Mittel der\n Behausung“ drücken —, bemerkt Dr. Hunter: „Nun diese Armen müssen\n irgendwoher kommen, und da keine besondre Attraktion, wie milde Gaben,\n in Badsey existirt, muss Repulsion von einem noch unbequemeren Platz\n existiren, der sie hierhin treibt. Könnte jeder ein Cot und ein Stückchen\n Land in der Nähe seines Arbeitsplatzes finden, so würde er solche sicher\n Badsey vorziehn, wo er für seine Handvoll Boden zweimal soviel zahlt als\n der Pächter für den seinen.“\n\n\n\n\nDie beständige Emigration nach den Städten, die beständige\n „Ueberzähligmachung“ auf dem Land durch Koncentration, Ver-\n wandlung von Acker in Weide, Maschinerie u. s. w., und die bestän-\n dige Eviktion der Landbevölkerung durch Zerstörung der Cotta-\n ges gehn Hand in Hand. Je menschenleerer der Distrikt, desto grösser seine\n „relative Uebervölkerung“, desto grösser ihr Druck auf die Beschäftigungsmit-\n tel, desto grösser der absolute Ueberschuss des Landvolks über seine Behau-\n sungsmittel, desto grösser also in den Dörfern die lokale Ueberpopulation\n und die pestilenzialischste Menschenzusammenpackung. Die Verdichtung des\n Menschenknäuels in zerstreuten kleinen Dörfern und Marktflecken entspricht\n der gewaltsamen Menschenentleerung auf der Oberfläche des Landes. Die\n ununterbrochene „Ueberzähligmachung“ der Landarbeiter trotz ihrer ab-\n nehmenden Anzahl und mit der wachsenden Masse ihres Produkts, ist die\n Wiege ihres Pauperismus. Ihr eventueller Pauperismus ist ein Motiv ihrer\n Eviktion und die Hauptquelle ihrer Wohnlichkeitsmisère, welche die letzte\n Widerstandsfähigkeit bricht und sie zu reinen Sklaven der Landlords [Note: „The heaven-born employment of the hind gives dignity even to his\n position. He is not a slave, but a soldier of peace, and deserves his\n place in married man’s quarters, to be provided by the landlord, who has\n claimed a power of enforced labour similar to that the country demands\n of a military soldier. He no more receives market-price for his work than does a\n soldier. Like the soldier he is caught young, ignorant, knowing only his own\n trade and his own locality. Early marriage and the operation of the various laws\n of settlement affect the one as enlistment and the Mutiny Act affect the other.“\n (Dr. Hunter l. c. p. 132.) Manchmal erweicht sich irgend ein ausnahmsweis] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"683\", \"facs\": \"#f0702\"} --> und Pächter macht, so dass das Minimum des Arbeitslohns sich zum Natur-\n gesetz für sie befestigt. Andrerseits ist das Land trotz seiner bestän-\n digen „relativen Uebervölkerung“ zugleich untervölkert. Diess zeigt\n sich nicht nur lokal auf solchen Punkten, wo der Menschenabfluss nach\n den Städten, Minen, Eisenbahnbauten u. s. w. zu rasch vorgeht, es zeigt\n sich überall sowohl zur Herbstzeit als im Frühling und Sommer während\n der zahlreichen Momente, wo die sehr sorgfältige und intensive englische\n Agrikultur Extrahände braucht. Es sind der Landarbeiter stets zu viel\n für die mittleren und stets zu wenig für die ausnahmsweisen oder\n temporären Bedürfnisse des Landbaus [Note: Aehnliche Bewegung seit den letzten Decennien in Frankreich, im\n Mass wie sich dort die kapitalistische Produktion der Agrikultur bemächtigt und\n die „überzählige“ Landbevölkerung nach den Städten treibt. Ebenso hier ver-\n schlechterte Wohnlichkeits- und sonstige Verhältnisse an der Quelle der „Ueber-\n zähligen“. Ueber das eigenthümliche „Prolétariat foncier“, welches das Parzel-\n lensystem ausgebrütet hat, sieh u. a. die früher citirte Schrift von Collins und:\nKarl Marx: „Der Achtzehnte Brumaire des Louis Bonaparte.\n New-York 1852“, p. 56 sqq.) 1846 betrug die städtische Bevölkerung in\n Frankreich 24.42, die ländliche 75.58, 1861 die städtische 28.86, die ländliche\n 71.41. In den letzten 5 Jahren ist die Abnahme der ländlichen Procenttheile der\n Bevölkerung noch grösser. Schon 1846 sang Pierre Dupont in seinen\n „Ouvriers“:\n\n\n\n\n„Mal vêtus, logés dans les trous,\n\n\n\n\nSous les combles, dans les décombres,\n\n\n\n\nNous vivons avec les hiboux\n\n\n\n\nEt les larrons, amis des ombres.“\n\n\n\n] . Daher findet man in den\n officiellen Dokumenten die widerspruchsvolle Klage derselben Orte\n über gleichzeitigen Arbeitsmangel und Arbeitsüberfluss registrirt. Der\n temporäre oder lokale Arbeitsmangel bewirkt keine Erhöhung des Arbeits-\n lohns, sondern Pressung von Weibern und Kindern in den Feldbau und\n Herabsteigen zu stets niedrigeren Altersstufen. Sobald die Weiber- und\n Kinderausbeutung grösseren Spielraum gewinnt, wird sie ihrerseits ein\n neues Mittel zur Ueberzähligmachung des männlichen Landarbeiters und\n [Note: schwachherziger Landlord über die von ihm geschaffene Einöde. „Est ist ein\n melancholisch Ding allein in seinem Land zu sein“, sagte der Graf von Leicester,\n als man ihm zum Fertigbau von Holkham gratulirte; „ich schaue um mich und\n sehe kein Haus ausser meinem eignen. Ich bin der Riese vom Riesenthurm und\n habe alle meine Nachbarn aufgegessen.“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"684\", \"facs\": \"#f0703\"} --> Niederhaltung seines Lohns. Im Osten Englands blüht eine schöne\n Frucht dieses cercle vicieux — das s. g. Gangsystem (Gang- oder\n Bandensystem), worauf ich hier kurz zurückkomme [Note: Der „Sechste“ und schliessliche „Report“ der „Child. Empl.\n Comm.“, publicirt Ende März 1867, behandelt nur das agrikole Gangsystem.] .\n\n\n\n\nDas Gangsystem haust fast ausschliesslich in Lincolnshire, Hunting-\n donshire, Cambridgeshire, Norfolk, Suffolk und Nottinghamshire, sporadisch\n in den benachbarten Grafschaften von Northampton, Bedford und Rutland.\n Als Beispiel diene hier Lincolnshire. Ein grosser Theil dieser Graf-\n schaft ist neu, früherer Moor oder auch, wie in andern der genannten öst-\n lichen Grafschaften, der See erst abgewonnenes Land. Die Dampf-\n maschine hat für die Entwässerung Wunder gewirkt. Früherer Morast\n und Sandboden trägt jetzt ein üppiges Kornmeer und die höchsten Grund-\n renten. Dasselbe gilt von dem künstlich gewonnenen Alluvialland, wie in\n der Insel von Axholme und den andren Pfarreien am Ufer des Trent. Im\n Mass wie die neuen Pachten entstanden, wurden nicht nur keine neuen\n Cottages gebaut, sondern alte niedergerissen, die Arbeitszufuhr aber ver-\n schafft aus den meilenweit entfernten offnen Dörfern längst den Land-\n strassen, die an Hügelrücken vorbeischlängeln. Dort hatte die Bevöl-\n kerung früher allein Schutz vor den langanhaltenden Winterüberschwem-\n mungen gefunden. Auf den Pachten von 400 bis 1000 Acres ansässige\n Arbeiter (sie heissen hier „confined labourers“) dienen ausschliesslich zur\n permanenten schweren und mit Pferden verrichteten Landarbeit. Auf je\n 100 Acres (1 Acre = 1.584 preussische Morgen) kommt im Durch-\n schnitt kaum eine Cottage. Ein Fenlandpächter z. B. sagt aus vor der\n Untersuchungskommission: „Meine Pacht erstreckt sich über 320 Acres,\n alles Kornland. Sie hat keine Cottage. Ein Arbeiter wohnt jetzt bei\n mir. Ich habe vier Pferdemänner in der Umgegend logirend. Das leichte\n Werk, wozu zahlreiche Hände nöthig, wird durch Gänge vollbracht“ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. VI. Report.“ Evidence, p. 173.] .\n Der Boden erheischt viel leichtes Feldwerk wie Ausjäten des Unkrauts,\n Behackung, gewisse Düngeroperationen, Auflesen der Steine u. s. w.\n Es wird verrichtet durch die Gänge oder organisirten Banden, deren\n Wohnsitz in den offenen Ortschaften.\n\n\n\n\nDer Gang besteht aus 10 bis 40 oder 50 Personen, nämlich Wei-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"685\", \"facs\": \"#f0704\"} --> bern, jungen Personen beiderlei Geschlechts (13—18 Jahr), obgleich Jun-\n gen meist mit dem 13. Jahr ausscheiden, endlich Kindern beiderlei\n Geschlechts (6—13. Jahr). An der Spitze steht der Gangmaster\n (Gangmeister), immer ein gewöhnlicher Landarbeiter, meist ein s. g.\n schlechter Kerl, Liederjahn, unstät, versoffen, aber mit einem ge-\n wissen Unternehmungsgeist und savoir faire. Er wirbt den Gang,\n der unter ihm arbeitet, nicht unter dem Pächter. Mit letztrem\n akkordirt er meist auf Stückwerk, und sein Einkommen, das im Durch-\n schnitt nicht sehr hoch über das eines gewöhnlichen Landarbeiters\n steigt [Note: Einzelne Gangmeister jedoch haben sich zu Pächtern von 500 Acres oder\n Besitzern ganzer Häuserreihen heraufgearbeitet.] , hängt fast ganz ab vom Geschick, womit er in kürzester Zeit\n möglichst viel Arbeit aus seiner Bande flüssig zu machen weiss. Die\n Pächter haben entdeckt, dass Frauenzimmer nur unter männlicher Dikta-\n tur ordentlich arbeiten, dass aber Frauenzimmer und Kinder, wenn einmal\n im Zug, mit wahrem Ungestüm, was schon Fourier wusste, ihre Lebens-\n kraft verausgaben, während der erwachsne männliche Arbeiter so heim-\n tückisch ist damit, soviel er kann, hauszuhalten. Der Gangmeister zieht von\n einem Gut zum andern und beschäftigt so seine Bande 6—8 Monate im Jahr.\n Er ist daher ein viel einträglicherer und sicherer Kunde für die Arbeiterfa-\n milien als der einzelne Pächter, welcher die Kinder nur gelegentlich beschäf-\n tigt. Dieser Umstand befestigt seinen Einfluss so sehr in den offnen Ort-\n schaften, dass in vielen die Kinder nur durch seine Dazwischenkunft hab-\n bar sind. Das individuelle Verpumpen derselben, ausserhalb des Gangs,\n an die Pächter, bildet sein Nebengeschäft.\n\n\n\n\nDie „Schattenseiten“ des Systems sind die Ueberarbeit der Kinder\n und jungen Personen, die ungeheuren Märsche, die sie täglich zu und von\n den 5, 6 und manchmal 7 Meilen entfernten Gütern zu machen haben, end-\n lich die Demoralisation des „Gangs“. Obgleich der Gangmeister, der in\n einigen Gegenden „the driver“ (Treiber) heisst, mit einem langen Stabe\n ausgerüstet ist, wendet er solchen jedoch nur selten an, und Klage über\n brutale Behandlung ist Ausnahme. Er ist ein demokratischer Kaiser\n oder eine Art Rattenfänger von Hameln. Er bedarf also der Popularität\n unter seinen Unterthanen und fesselt sie an sich durch das unter seinen\n Auspicien blühende Zigeunerthum. Rohe Ungebundenheit, lustige Ausge-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"686\", \"facs\": \"#f0705\"} --> lassenheit und obscönste Frechheit leihen dem Gang Flügel. Meist zahlt\n der Gangmeister in einer Kneipe aus und kehrt dann wohl wankend,\n rechts und links gestützt auf ein stämmiges Frauenmensch, an der Spitze\n des Zugs heim, die Kinder und jungen Personen hinterher tollend, Spott-\n und Zotenlieder singend. Auf dem Rückweg ist das, was Fourier ‚Phane-\n rogamie‘ nennt, an der Tagesordnung. Die Schwängerung dreizehn- und\n vierzehnjähriger Mädchen durch ihre männlichen Altersgenossen ist häufig.\n Die offenen Dörfer, welche das Kontingent des Gangs stellen, werden\n Sodoms und Gomorrhas [Note: „Die Hälfte der Mädchen von Bidford ist ruinirt worden durch den\n Gang.“ l. c. Appendix, p. 6, n. 32.]  und liefern doppelt so viel unehliche Geburten\n als der Rest des Königreichs. Was in dieser Schule gezüchtete Mädchen\n als verheirathete Frauen in der Moralität leisten, ward schon früher ange-\n deutet. Ihre Kinder, soweit sie selbe nicht durch Opium u. s. w. besei-\n tigen, sind geborne Rekruten des Gangs.\n\n\n\n\nDer Gang in seiner eben beschriebenen klassischen Form heisst\n öffentlicher, gemeiner oder Wandergang (public, common or tramping\n gang). Es giebt nämlich auch Privatgänge (private gangs). Sie\n sind zusammengesetzt wie der Gemeingang, zählen aber weniger Köpfe\n und arbeiten, statt unter dem Gangmeister, unter einem alten Bauern-\n knecht, den der Pächter nicht besser zu verwenden weiss. Der Zigeuner-\n humor verschwindet hier, aber nach allen Zeugenaussagen verschlechtern\n sich Zahlung und Behandlung der Kinder.\n\n\n\n\nDas Gangsystem, das sich seit den letzten Jahren beständig aus-\n dehnt [Note: „Das System hat sehr zugenommen in den letzten Jahren. In einigen\n Plätzen ist es erst seit kurzem eingeführt, in andern, wo es älter, werden mehr und\n jüngere Kinder in den Gang einrollirt.“ (l. c. p. 79, n. 174.)] , existirt offenbar nicht dem Gangmeister zu lieb. Es existirt zur\n Bereicherung der grossen Pächter [Note: „Kleine Pächter wenden die Gangarbeit nicht an.“ „Sie wird nicht ange-\n wandt auf armem Land, sondern auf Land, was 2 Pfd. St. bis 2 Pfd. St. 10 sh.\n Rente per Acre bringt.“ (l. c. p. 17 u. 14.)] , resp. Landlords [Note: Einem dieser Herrn schmecken seine Renten so gut, dass er der Unter-\n suchungskommission entrüstet erklärt, der ganze Schrei sei nur dem Namen des\n Systems geschuldet. Wenn man es statt „Gang“ dahingegen „jugendliche in-\n dustriell-agrikol-cooperative Selbsterhaltungsassociation“ taufe, so wäre alles all\n right.] . Für den Päch-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"687\", \"facs\": \"#f0706\"} --> ter giebt’s keine sinnreichere Methode, sein Arbeiterpersonal tief unter dem\n normalen Niveau zu halten und dennoch für alles Extrawerk stets die\n Extrahand bereit zu haben, mit möglichst wenig Geld möglichst viel\n Arbeit herauszuschlagen [Note: „Gangarbeit ist wohlfeiler als andre Arbeit, das ist die Ursache, warum\n sie angewandt wird“, sagt ein ehemaliger Gangmeister. (l. c. p. 17, n. 11.)\n „Das Gangsystem ist entschieden das wohlfeilste für den Pächter und eben so ent-\n schieden das verderblichste für die Kinder“, sagt ein Pächter. (l. c. p. 16, n. 4.)]  und den erwachsnen männlichen Arbeiter\n „überzählig“ zu machen. Nach der frühern Auseinandersetzung versteht\n man, wenn einerseits die grössere oder geringere Beschäftigungslosigkeit\n des Landmanns zugestanden, andrerseits zugleich das Gangsystem wegen\n Mangels an männlicher Arbeit und ihrer Wanderung nach den Städten für\n „nothwendig“ erklärt wird [Note: „Zweifelsohne vieles jetzt von den Kindern in Gängen verrichtete Werk\n wurde früher von Männern und Weibern verrichtet. Wo Weiber und Kinder an-\n gewandt werden, sind jetzt mehr Männer arbeitslos („more men are out of work“)\n als früher.“ (l. c. p. 43, n. 202.) Dagegen u. a.: „Die Arbeitsfrage\n (labour question) in vielen Agrikulturdistrikten, besonders den kornproduciren-\n den, wird so ernsthaft in Folge der Auswanderung und der Leichtigkeit, welche\n die Eisenbahnen zur Entfernung nach den grossen Städten bieten, dass ich (das\n „Ich“ ist das des Landagenten eines grossen Herrn) die Kinderdienste für absolut\n unentbehrlich halte.“ (l. c. p. 80, n. 180.) The Labour Question (die\n Arbeitsfrage) bedeutet nämlich in den englischen Agrikulturdistrikten, im Unter-\n schied von der übrigen civilisirten Welt, the landlords’ and farmers’\n Question (Landlords- und Pächterfrage), wie, trotz stets vermehrtem Abzug der\n Landleute, eine genügende „relative Uebervölkerung“ auf dem Land und dadurch\n das „Minimum des Arbeitslohns“ für den Landarbeiter zu verewigen sei?] . Das unkrautreine Feld und das\n Menschenunkraut von Lincolnshire u. s. w. sind Pol und Gegenpol der\n kapitalistischen Produktion [Note: Der früher von mir citirte „Public Health Report“, worin bei Gelegen-\n heit der Kindersterblichkeit vorübergehend vom Gangsystem gehandelt wird, blieb\n der Presse und daher dem englischen Publikum unbekannt. Dagegen bot der\n neuliche Bericht der „Child. Empl. Comm.“ willkommenes „sensational“\n Pressfutter. Während die liberale Presse frug, wie doch die feinen Gentlemen\n und Ladies und Staatskirchpfründner, womit Lincolnshire schwärmt, ein solches\n System auf ihren Gütern, unter ihren Augen aufwachsen lassen konnten, Perso-\n nagen, die eigne „Missionen zur Sittenverbesserung der Südseewilden“ nach den\n Antipoden entsenden, stellte die feinere Presse ausschliesslich Betrachtungen über\n die rohe Verdorbenheit der Landleute an, die fähig sind, ihre Kinder in] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"688\", \"facs\": \"#f0707\"} -->\n\nZum Schluss dieses Abschnitts müssen wir noch einen Augenblick\n nach Irland wandern. Zunächst die Thatsachen, worauf es hier\n ankommt.\n\n\n\n\nIrlands Bevölkerung war 1841 auf 8,222,664 Personen ange-\n wachsen, 1851 auf 6,623,982 zusammengeschmolzen, 1861 auf\n 5,850,309, 1866 auf 5½ Mill., ungefähr auf ihr Niveau von 1801.\n Die Abnahme begann mit dem Hungerjahr 1846, so dass Irland in\n weniger als 20 Jahren mehr als \\frac{5}{16} seiner Volksmenge verlor [Note: Bevölkerung von Irland: 1801: 5,319,867 Personen, 1811:\n 6,084,996, 1821: 6,869,544, 1831: 7,828,347, 1841: 8,222,664.] . Seine\n Gesammtemigration von Mai 1851 bis Juli 1865 zählte 1,591,487 Per-\n sonen, die Emigration während der letzten 5 Jahre 1861—1865 mehr\n als eine halbe Million. Die Zahl der bewohnten Häuser verminderte\n sich von 1851—1861 um 52,990. Von 1851—1861 wuchs die Zahl\n der Pachthöfe von 15—30 Acres um 61,000, die der Pachthöfe über\n 30 Acres um 109,000, während die Gesammtzahl aller Pach-\n ten um 120,000 abnahm, eine Abnahme, die also ausschliesslich der\n Vernichtung von Pachten unter 15 Acres, alias ihrer Koncen-\n tration geschuldet ist.\n\n\n\n\n [Note: solche Sklaverei zu verkaufen! Unter den fluchwürdigen Umständen, worin „die\n Delikatern“ den Landmann gebannt, wäre es erklärlich, wenn er seine eigenen\n Kinder aufässe. Was wirklich wunderbar, ist die Charaktertüchtigkeit, die er\n grossentheils bewahrt hat. Die officiellen Berichterstatter beweisen, dass die\n Eltern selbst in den Gangdistrikten das Gangsystem verabscheuen. „Man findet\n reichlichen Beweis in den von uns gesammelten Zeugenaussagen, dass die Eltern\n in vielen Fällen dankbar sein würden für ein Zwangsgesetz, welches sie be-\n fähigen würde, den Versuchungen und dem Druck zu widerstehn, denen sie oft\n unterworfen sind. Bald treibt sie der Pfarreibeamte, bald der Anwender unter\n Androhung ihrer eignen Entlassung, die Kinder auf den Verdienst, statt in die\n Schule zu schicken … Alle verwüstete Zeit und Kraft, alles Leid, welches ausser-\n ordentliche und nutzlose Ermüdung für den Landmann und seine Familie produ-\n cirt, jeder Fall, worin die Eltern den moralischen Ruin ihres Kindes auf die Ueber-\n füllung der Cottages oder die besudelnden Einflüsse des Gangsystems zurückleiten,\n stacheln in der Brust der arbeitenden Armen Gefühle auf, die man wohl verstehn\n wird, und die es unnöthig ist zu detailliren. Sie haben ein Bewusstsein darüber,\n dass ihnen viel körperliche und geistige Qual angethan wird durch Umstände,\n wofür sie in keiner Weise verantwortlich sind, welchen sie, hätte es in ihrer Macht\n gestanden, niemals ihre Zustimmung gegeben hätten, und wider welche anzu-\n kämpfen sie ohnmächtig sind.“ (l. c. p. XX, n. 82 und XXIII, n. 96.)] \n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"689\", \"facs\": \"#f0708\"} -->\n\nDie Abnahme der Volksmenge war natürlich im Grossen und Ganzen\n von einer Abnahme der Produktenmasse begleitet. Für unsren Zweck\n genügt es, die 5 Jahre 1861—1865 zu betrachten, während deren über\n ½ Million emigrirte und die absolute Volkszahl um mehr als ⅓\n Million sank.\n\n\n\n\nTabelle A.\nViehstand.\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAus der vorhergehenden Tabelle ergiebt sich:\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWenden wir uns jetzt zum Ackerbau, der die Lebensmittel für Vieh\n und Mensch liefert. In der folgenden Tabelle ist Ab- oder Zunahme für das\n Jahr 1861 auf das Jahr 1860 berechnet, kurz für jedes einzelne Jahr\n [Running matter: I. 44] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"690\", \"facs\": \"#f0709\"} --> mit Bezug auf das unmittelbar vorhergehende. Die Kornfrucht um-\n fasst Weizen, Hafer, Gerste, Bere, Roggen, Bohnen und Erbsen, die\nGrünfrucht Kartoffeln, Turnips, Mangel- und Runkelrübe, Kohl, gelbe\n Rüben, Parsnips, Wicke u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nTabelle B.\n Zu- oder Abnahme des zum Fruchtbau und als Wiese (resp. Weide) benutzten\n Bodenareals in Acres.\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIm Jahr 1865 kamen unter der Rubrik „Grasland“ 127,470 Acres\n hinzu, hauptsächlich weil das Areal unter der Rubrik „unbenutztes\n wüstes Land und Bog (Sumpfland)“ um 101,543 Acres abnahm. Ver-\n gleichen wir 1865 mit 1864, so Abnahme in Kornfrucht\n 246,667 Qrs., wovon 48,999 Weizen, 166,605 Hafer, 29,892 Gerste\n u. s. w., Abnahme von Kartoffelertrag, obgleich das Areal\n ihrer Bebauung 1865 wuchs, 446,398 Tonnen u. s. w.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"691\", \"facs\": \"#f0710\"} -->\n\nTabelle C.\n Zu- oder Abnahme in dem Areal des bebauten Bodens, dem Produkt per Acre, und dem Gesammtprodukt,\n 1865 verglichen mit 1864.\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n [Running matter: 44*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"692\", \"facs\": \"#f0711\"} -->\n\nVon der Bewegung der Bevölkerung und Bodenproduktion Irlands\n gehn wir über zur Bewegung in der Börse seiner Landlords,\n grösseren Pächter und industriellen Kapitalisten. Sie reflektirt sich im\n Ab und Zu der der Einkommensteuer unterworfenen jährlichen Ein-\n kommen. Zum Verständniss der folgenden Tabelle bemerken wir, dass Ru-\n brik D (Profite mit Ausnahme der Pächterprofite) auch s. g. „profes-\n sionelle“ Profite einbegreift, d. h. die Einkommen von Advokaten, Aerzten\n u. s. w., die nicht besonders aufgezählten Rubriken C und E aber die\n Einnahmen von Beamten, Officieren, Staatssynekuristen, Staatsgläu-\n bigern u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nTabelle D.\n Der Einkommensteuer unterliegende Einkommen in Pfd. St.\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nUnter Rubrik D betrug die Zunahme des Einkommens im Jahres-\n durchschnitt von 1853—1864 nur 0.93, während sie in derselben Pe-\n riode in Grossbritanien 4.58 betrug. Die folgende Tabelle zeigt die\nVertheilung der Profite (mit Ausschluss der Pächterprofite)\n für die Jahre 1864 und 1865:\nSiehe nebenstehende Tabelle E.\n\n\n\n\nEngland, ein Land entwickelter kapitalistischer Produktion und vor-\n zugsweis industriell, wäre verblutet an einem Volksaderlass, gleich dem\n irischen. Aber Irland ist gegenwärtig nur ein durch einen breiten Was-\n sergraben abgezäunter Agrikulturdistrikt Englands, dem es Korn, Wolle,\n Vieh, industrielle und militärische Rekruten liefert.\n\n\n\n\nDie Entvölkerung hat viel Land ausser Bebauung geworfen, das\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"693\", \"facs\": \"#f0712\"} -->Tabelle E.\n Rubrik D. Einkommen aus Profiten (über 60 Pfd. St.) in Irland.\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n Bodenprodukt sehr vermindert [Note: Wenn das Produkt auch verhältnissmässig per Acre abnimmt\n vergesse man nicht, dass England seit 1½ Jahrhunderten den Boden von Irland\n exportirt hat, ohne seinen Bebauern auch nur die Mittel zum Ersatz der\n Bodenbestandtheile zu gönnen.] , und selbst, trotz des erweiterten Areals\n der Viehzucht, in einigen ihrer Zweige absolute Abnahme erzeugt, in an-\n dern kaum nennenswerthen und durch beständige Rückschritte unterbroch-\n nen Fortschritt. Dennoch stiegen Grundrenten und Pachtprofite, obgleich\n letztere nicht so constant wie die erstern, fortwährend mit dem Fall der\n Volksmasse. Der Grund ist leicht verständlich. Einerseits verwandelte\n sich mit der Zusammenwerfung der Pachten und der Verwandlung von\n Ackerland in Viehweide ein grösserer Theil des Gesammtprodukts\n in Mehrprodukt. Die absolute Masse des Mehrprodukts wuchs, ob-\n gleich das Gesammtprodukt, wovon sie einen Bruchtheil bildet, absolut\n abnahm. Andrerseits stieg der Geldwerth dieses Mehrprodukts noch rascher\n als seine Masse, in Folge der seit den letzten 20 und ganz besonders seit\n den letzten 10 Jahren steigenden englischen Marktpreise für Fleisch,\n Wolle u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nZersplitterte Produktionsmittel, die den Producenten selbst als Be-\n schäftigungs- und Subsistenzmittel dienen, ohne sich durch Einverleibung\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"694\", \"facs\": \"#f0713\"} --> fremder Arbeit zu verwerthen, sind eben so wenig Kapital als das von\n seinem eignen Producenten verzehrte Produkt Waare ist. Wenn also\n auch mit der Volksmasse die Masse der in der Agrikultur angewandten\nProduktionsmittel sich verminderte, vermehrte sich die Masse des\n in ihr angewandten Kapitals, weil ein Theil jener vorher zersplitterten\n Produktionsmittel in Kapital verwandelt ward.\n\n\n\n\nDas ausserhalb der Agrikultur, in Industrie und Handel angelegte\n Gesammtkapital Irlands accumulirte langsam während der letzten zwei\n Decennien und unter beständiger grosser Fluktuation. Andrerseits ent-\n wickelte sich um so rascher die Koncentration seiner individuellen Bestand-\n theile. Endlich, wie langsam immerhin das Kapital absolut wuchs, rela-\n tiv, im Verhältniss zur zusammengeschmolzenen Volkszahl, war es ausser-\n ordentlich angeschwollen.\n\n\n\n\nHier entrollt sich also, unter unsern Augen, auf grosser Stufenleiter,\n ein Prozess wie die orthodoxe Oekonomie ihn nicht schöner wünschen\n konnte zur Bewähr ihres Dogma’s, welches das Elend aus absoluter Ueber-\n völkerung erklärt und das Gleichgewicht durch Entvölkerung wieder her-\n stellt. Es ist diess ein ganz anders wichtiges Experiment als die von den\n Malthusianern so sehr verherrlichte Pest in der Mitte des vierzehnten Jahr-\n hunderts. Nebenbei bemerkt. War es an sich schulmeisterlich naiv, den\n Produktions- und entsprechenden Bevölkerungsverhältnissen des 19. Jahr-\n hunderts den Massstab des 14. Jahrhunderts anzulegen, so übersah diese\n Naivetät noch obendrein, dass wenn jener Pest und der sie begleitenden\n Decimation, diesseits des Kanals, in England, Befreiung und Bereicherung\n des Landvolks, ihr jenseits, in Frankreich, grössere Knechtung und er-\n höhtes Elend auf dem Fuss nachfolgten.\n\n\n\n\nDie Hungersnoth erschlug 1846 in Irland über eine Menschenmillion,\n aber nur arme Teufel, ohne den geringsten Abbruch am Reichthum des Lan-\n des. Der nachfolgende zwanzigjährige und stets noch anschwellende\n Exodus decimirte nicht, wie etwa der dreissigjährige Krieg, mit den Men-\n schen zugleich ihre Produktionsmittel. Das irische Genie erfand eine ganz\n neue Methode, ein armes Volk Tausende von Meilen vom Schauplatz sei-\n nes Elends wegzuhexen. Die in die Vereinigten Staaten übergesiedelten\n Auswanderer schicken jährlich Geldsummen nach Haus, die Reisemittel\n der Zurückgebliebenen. Jeder Trupp, der dieses Jahr auswandert, zieht\n nächstes Jahr einen andern Trupp nach. Statt Irland zu kosten, bildet\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"695\", \"facs\": \"#f0714\"} --> die Auswanderung so einen der einträglichsten Zweige seines Exportge-\n schäfts. Endlich ist diess ein systematischer Prozess, der nicht etwa ein\n vorübergehendes Loch in die Volksmasse bohrt, sondern jährlich mehr\n Menschen aus ihr auspumpt als der Nachwuchs ersetzt, so dass das ab-\n solute Bevölkerungsniveau von Jahr zu Jahr sinkt.\n\n\n\n\nWelches waren die Folgen für die zurückbleibende, von der „Sur-\n pluspopulation“ befreite Arbeiterbevölkerung Irlands? Dass die rela-\n tive Uebervölkerung heute so gross ist wie vor 1846, dass der Arbeits-\n lohn eben so niedrig steht und die Arbeitsplackerei zugenommen hat,\n dass die Misère auf dem Land wieder zu einer neuen Krise drängt. Die\n Ursachen sind einfach. Die Revolution in der Agrikultur hielt Schritt mit der\n Emigration. Die Produktion der relativen Uebervölkerung hielt mehr\n als Schritt mit der absoluten Entvölkerung. Ein Blick auf Tabelle C\n zeigt, wie die Verwandlung von Ackerbau in Viehweide in Irland noch\n akuter wirken muss als in England. Hier wächst mit der Viehzucht der\n Bau von Grünfrucht, dort nimmt er ab. Während grosse Massen früher\n bestellter Aecker brachgelegt oder in permanentes Grasland verwandelt\n werden, wird ein grosser Theil des früher unbenutzten wüsten Landes und\n Bog’s zur Ausdehnung der Viehzucht benutzt. Die kleineren und mittle-\n ren Pächter — ich rechne dazu alle, die nicht über 100 Acres bebauen\n — haben immer noch ungefähr \\frac{8}{10} des irischen Bodens inne. Sie wer-\n den progressiv in ganz anderem Grad als zuvor von der Konkurrenz des\n kapitalistisch betriebenen Ackerbaus erdrückt und liefern daher der eigent-\n lichen Arbeiterklasse beständig neue Rekruten. Die einzige grosse In-\n dustrie Irlands, die Leinenfabrikation, braucht verhältnissmässig wenig er-\n wachsne männliche Arbeiter und beschäftigt überhaupt, trotz ihrer Expan-\n sion seit der Vertheurung der Baumwolle, nur einen verhältnissmässig un-\n bedeutenden Theil der Arbeitermasse. Gleich jeder andern grossen In-\n dustrie producirt sie durch die beständigen Schwankungen in ihrer eignen\n Sphäre beständig eine relative Uebervölkerung, selbst bei absolutem Wachs-\n thum der von ihr absorbirten Arbeiterzahl. Die Misère des Landvolks\n bildet das Piedestal riesenhafter Hemdenfabriken u. s. w., deren Arbeiter-\n armee zum grössten Theil über das flache Land zerstreut ist. Wir finden\n hier das früher geschilderte System der Hausarbeit wieder, welches in\n Unterzahlung und Ueberarbeit seine methodischen Mittel der „Ueberzäh-\n ligmachung“ besitzt. Endlich, obschon die Entvölkerung nicht so zer-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"696\", \"facs\": \"#f0715\"} --> störende Folgen hat, wie in einem Land entwickelter kapitalistischer Pro-\n duktion, vollzieht sie sich nicht ohne beständigen Rückschlag. Die Emi-\n gration lässt nicht nur leere Häuser zurück, sondern auch ruinirte Haus-\n vermiether. Klein wie der Konsum jedes ihrer individuellen Bestandtheile,\n producirt ihr Gesammtausfall eine beständige Lücke im innern Markt, der\n namentlich Kleinkrämer, Handwerker und kleine Gewerbsleute überhaupt\n trifft. Jeder neue Exodus schleudert einen Theil der kleinen Mittelklasse ins\n Proletariat. Sieh Tabelle E die Abnahme der Einkommen unter 100 Pfd. St.\n\n\n\n\nDer Wochenlohn des Ackerbauers in der Umgegend von Dublin —\n der Maximallohn des irischen Ackerbauers — steht in diesem Augenblick,\n bei hohem Preis der ersten Lebensmittel, auf 7 sh. Man kann daraus\n auf seinen Stand in den rein agrikolen und entlegenen Distrikten zurück-\n schliessen. Zur Schilderung der Lage selbst des geschickten irischen\n industriellen Arbeiters genügt ein Beispiel.\n\n\n\n\n„Bei meiner neulichen Inspektion des Nordens von Irland“, sagt der\n englische Fabrikinspektor Robert Baker, „frappirte mich die Bemü-\n hung eines geschickten irischen Arbeiters aus den allerdürftigsten Mitteln\n seinen Kindern Erziehung zu verschaffen. Ich gebe seine Aussage ver-\n batim, wie ich sie aus seinem Mund erhielt. Dass er eine geschickte\n Fabrikhand, weiss man, wenn ich sage, dass man ihn zu Artikeln für den Man-\n chester Markt verwandte. Johnson: Ich bin ein beetler, und arbeite von\n 6 Uhr Morgens bis 11 in die Nacht, von Montag bis Freitag; Samstags endigen\n wir um 6 Uhr Abends und haben 3 Stunden für Mahlzeit und Erholung.\n Ich habe 5 Kinder. Für diese Arbeit erhalte ich 10 sh. 6 d. wöchent-\n lich; meine Frau arbeitet auch und verdient 5 sh. die Woche. Das älteste\n Mädchen, zwölfjährig, wartet das Haus. Sie ist unser Koch und einziger\n Gehülfe. Sie macht die jüngeren zur Schule fertig. Meine Frau steht\n mit mir auf und geht mit mir fort. Ein Mädchen, die unser Haus entlang\n geht, weckt mich um halb 6 Uhr Morgens. Wir essen nichts, bevor wir\n zur Arbeit gehn. Das zwölfjährige Kind sorgt für die Kleineren des Tags\n über. Wir frühstücken um 8 und gehn dazu nach Hause. Wir haben Thee ein-\n mal die Woche; sonst haben wir einen Brei (stirabout), manchmal von Hafer-\n mehl, manchmal von indischem Mehl, je nachdem wir fähig sind es zu verschaf-\n fen. Im Winter haben wir ein wenig Zucker und Wasser zu unserm indischen\n Mehl. Im Sommer erndten wir einige Kartoffeln, womit wir selbst ein Boden-\n fetzchen bepflanzen, und wenn sie zu Ende sind, kehren wir zum Brei zu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"697\", \"facs\": \"#f0716\"} --> rück. So geht’s Tag aus Tag ein, Sonntag und Werkeltag, das ganze\n Jahr durch. Ich bin stets sehr müde des Abends nach vollbrachtem Tag-\n werk. Einen Bissen Fleisch sehn wir ausnahmsweis, aber sehr selten.\n Drei unsrer Kinder besuchen Schule, wofür wir 1 d. per Kopf wöchentlich\n zahlen. Unsere Hausmiethe ist 9 d. die Woche, Torf für Feuerung kostet\n mindestens 1 sh. 6 d. vierzehntägig“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31. Oct. 1866.“ Lond. 1867,\n p. 96.] . Das sind irische Löhne, das\n ist irisches Leben!\n\n\n\n\nIn der That, das Elend Irlands ist wieder Tagesthema in England.\n Ende 1866 und Anfang 1867 machte sich in der Times einer der\n irischen Landmagnaten, Lord Dufferin, an die Lösung. „Wie mensch-\n lich von solch’ grossem Herrn!“\n\n\n\n\nAus Tabelle E sah man, dass während 1864 von 4,368,610 Pfd.\n St. Gesammtprofit 3 Plusmacher nur 262,610, dieselben 3 Virtuosen der\n „Entsagung“ 1865 von 4,669,979 Pfd. St. Gesammtprofit dagegen\n 274,448 Pfd. St. einsteckten, 1864: 26 Plusmacher 646,377 Pfd. St.,\n1865 : 28 Plusmacher 736,448 Pfd. St., 1864 : 121 Plusmacher\n 1,066,912 Pfd. St., 1865 : 186 Plusmacher 1,320,996 Pfd. St., 1864 :\n 1131 Plusmacher 2,150,818 Pfd. St., beinahe die Hälfte des jährlichen\n Gesammtprofits, 1865 : 1194 Plusmacher 2,418,933 Pfd. St., mehr als\n die Hälfte des jährlichen Gesammtprofits. Der Löwenantheil aber, welchen\n eine verschwindend kleine Anzahl Landmagnaten in England, Schottland\n und Irland vom jährlichen Nationalrental verschlingt, ist so monströs,\n dass die englische Staatsweisheit es angemessen findet, für die Vertheilung\n der Grundrente nicht dasselbe statistische Material zu liefern wie für die\n Vertheilung des Profits. Lord Dufferin ist einer dieser Landmag-\n naten. Dass Rentrollen und Profite jemals „überzählig“ sein können, oder\n dass ihre Plethora mit der Plethora des Volkselends irgendwie zusammen-\n hängt, ist natürlich eine ebenso „irrespektable“ als „ungesunde“ (unsound)\n Vorstellung. Er hält sich an Thatsachen. Die Thatsache ist, dass wie\n die irische Volkszahl abnimmt, die irischen Rentrollen schwellen, dass die\n Entvölkerung dem Grundeigenthümer „wohlthut“, also auch dem Grund,\n also auch dem Volk, das nur ein Accessorium des Bodens ist. Er erklärt also,\n Irland sei immer noch übervölkert und der Strom der Emigration fliesse\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"698\", \"facs\": \"#f0717\"} --> stets noch zu träg. Um vollständig glücklich zu sein, müsse Irland wenig-\n stens noch ⅓ Million Arbeitsmenschen ablassen. Man wähne nicht, die-\n ser obendrein noch poetische Lord sei ein Arzt aus der Schule Sangrado’s,\n der, so oft er seinen Kranken nicht besser fand, Aderlass verordnete, neuen\n Aderlass, bis der Patient mit seinem Blut auch seine Krankheit verlor.\n Lord Dufferin verlangt einen neuen Aderlass von nur ⅓ Million, statt von\n ungefähr 2 Millionen, ohne deren Ablass in der That das Millennium in\n Erin nicht herstellbar ist. Der Beweis ist leicht geliefert.\n\n\n\n\nAnzahl und Umfang der Pachten in Irland 1864.\n\n\n\n\n | \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDie Koncentration hat von 1851 bis 1861 hauptsächlich Pachten\n der ersten 3 Kategorieen, unter 1 und nicht über 15 Acres, vernichtet. Sie\n müssen vor allem verschwinden. Diess giebt 307,058 „überzählige“\n Pächter, und die Familie zum niedrigen Durchschnitt von 4 Köpfen ge-\n rechnet, 1,228,232 Personen. Unter der extravaganten Unterstellung,\n dass ¼ davon nach vollbrachter agrikoler Revolution wieder absorbirbar,\n bleiben auszuwandern: 921,174 Personen. Die Kategorieen 4, 5, 6,\n von über 15 und nicht über 100 Acres, sind, wie man längst in England\n weiss, für den kapitalistischen Kornbau zu klein, für Schafzucht aber fast\n verschwindende Grössen. Unter denselben Unterstellungen wie vorher\n sind also fernere 788,761 Personen auszuwandern, Summe: 1,709,532.\n Und, comme l’appétit vient en mangeant, werden die Augen der Rentrolle\n bald entdecken, dass Irland mit 3½ Millionen immer noch elend, und\n elend, weil übervölkert ist, also seine Entvölkerung noch viel weiter\n gehen muss, damit es seinen wahren Beruf erfülle, den einer englischen\n Schaftrift und Viehweide.\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"699\", \"facs\": \"#f0718\"} -->\n\nDiese einbringliche Methode hat wie alles Gute in dieser Welt ihren\n Missstand. Mit der Accumulation der Grundrente in Irland hält Schritt\n die Accumulation der Irländer in Amerika. Der durch Schaf und Ochs\n beseitigte Ire ersteht auf der andern Seite des Oceans als Fenian. Und\n gegenüber der alten Seekönigin erhebt sich drohend und drohender die\n junge Riesenrepublik.\n\n\nAcerba fata Romanos agunt\n Scelusque fraternae necis.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0001","text":"Badsey. Viele Cots und Gärtchen hier. Einige Pächter erklären\n die Cots „a great nuisance here, because they bring the poor“. (Die\n Cots grosser Missstand, weil sie die Armen herbringen.) Auf die Aeusse-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"682\", \"facs\": \"#f0701\"} --> rung eines Gentleman: „Die Armen sind desswegen um nichts besser\n dran, wenn man 500 Cots baut, gehn sie wie die Wecken ab, in der That\n je mehr man davon baut, desto mehr sind nöthig“ — die Häuser bringen\n nach ihm die Einwohner hervor, die naturgesetzlich auf „die Mittel der\n Behausung“ drücken —, bemerkt Dr. Hunter: „Nun diese Armen müssen\n irgendwoher kommen, und da keine besondre Attraktion, wie milde Gaben,\n in Badsey existirt, muss Repulsion von einem noch unbequemeren Platz\n existiren, der sie hierhin treibt.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"21.1"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0002","text":"Könnte jeder ein Cot und ein Stückchen\n Land in der Nähe seines Arbeitsplatzes finden, so würde er solche sicher\n Badsey vorziehn, wo er für seine Handvoll Boden zweimal soviel zahlt als\n der Pächter für den seinen.“","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"21.2"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0003","text":"Die beständige Emigration nach den Städten, die beständige\n „Ueberzähligmachung“ auf dem Land durch Koncentration, Ver-\n wandlung von Acker in Weide, Maschinerie u. s. w., und die bestän-\n dige Eviktion der Landbevölkerung durch Zerstörung der Cotta-\n ges gehn Hand in Hand. Je menschenleerer der Distrikt, desto grösser seine\n „relative Uebervölkerung“, desto grösser ihr Druck auf die Beschäftigungsmit-\n tel, desto grösser der absolute Ueberschuss des Landvolks über seine Behau-\n sungsmittel, desto grösser also in den Dörfern die lokale Ueberpopulation\n und die pestilenzialischste Menschenzusammenpackung. Die Verdichtung des\n Menschenknäuels in zerstreuten kleinen Dörfern und Marktflecken entspricht\n der gewaltsamen Menschenentleerung auf der Oberfläche des Landes.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"21.3"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0004","text":"Die\n ununterbrochene „Ueberzähligmachung“ der Landarbeiter trotz ihrer ab-\n nehmenden Anzahl und mit der wachsenden Masse ihres Produkts, ist die\n Wiege ihres Pauperismus. Ihr eventueller Pauperismus ist ein Motiv ihrer\n Eviktion und die Hauptquelle ihrer Wohnlichkeitsmisère, welche die letzte\n Widerstandsfähigkeit bricht und sie zu reinen Sklaven der Landlords [Note: „The heaven-born employment of the hind gives dignity even to his\n position. He is not a slave, but a soldier of peace, and deserves his\n place in married man’s quarters, to be provided by the landlord, who has\n claimed a power of enforced labour similar to that the country demands\n of a military soldier. He no more receives market-price for his work than does a\n soldier. Like the soldier he is caught young, ignorant, knowing only his own\n trade and his own locality.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"21.4"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0005","text":"Early marriage and the operation of the various laws\n of settlement affect the one as enlistment and the Mutiny Act affect the other.“\n (Dr. Hunter l. c. p. 132.) Manchmal erweicht sich irgend ein ausnahmsweis] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"683\", \"facs\": \"#f0702\"} --> und Pächter macht, so dass das Minimum des Arbeitslohns sich zum Natur-\n gesetz für sie befestigt. Andrerseits ist das Land trotz seiner bestän-\n digen „relativen Uebervölkerung“ zugleich untervölkert. Diess zeigt\n sich nicht nur lokal auf solchen Punkten, wo der Menschenabfluss nach\n den Städten, Minen, Eisenbahnbauten u. s. w. zu rasch vorgeht, es zeigt\n sich überall sowohl zur Herbstzeit als im Frühling und Sommer während\n der zahlreichen Momente, wo die sehr sorgfältige und intensive englische\n Agrikultur Extrahände braucht.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"21.5"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0006","text":"Es sind der Landarbeiter stets zu viel\n für die mittleren und stets zu wenig für die ausnahmsweisen oder\n temporären Bedürfnisse des Landbaus [Note: Aehnliche Bewegung seit den letzten Decennien in Frankreich, im\n Mass wie sich dort die kapitalistische Produktion der Agrikultur bemächtigt und\n die „überzählige“ Landbevölkerung nach den Städten treibt. Ebenso hier ver-\n schlechterte Wohnlichkeits- und sonstige Verhältnisse an der Quelle der „Ueber-\n zähligen“. Ueber das eigenthümliche „Prolétariat foncier“, welches das Parzel-\n lensystem ausgebrütet hat, sieh u. a. die früher citirte Schrift von Collins und:\nKarl Marx: „Der Achtzehnte Brumaire des Louis Bonaparte.\n New-York 1852“, p. 56 sqq.) 1846 betrug die städtische Bevölkerung in\n Frankreich 24.42, die ländliche 75.58, 1861 die städtische 28.86, die ländliche\n 71.41.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"21.6"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0007","text":"In den letzten 5 Jahren ist die Abnahme der ländlichen Procenttheile der\n Bevölkerung noch grösser. Schon 1846 sang Pierre Dupont in seinen\n „Ouvriers“:","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"21.7"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0008","text":"„Mal vêtus, logés dans les trous,","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"21.8"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0009","text":"Sous les combles, dans les décombres,","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"21.9"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0010","text":"Nous vivons avec les hiboux","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"21.10"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0011","text":"Et les larrons, amis des ombres.“","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"21.11"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0012","text":"] . Daher findet man in den\n officiellen Dokumenten die widerspruchsvolle Klage derselben Orte\n über gleichzeitigen Arbeitsmangel und Arbeitsüberfluss registrirt. Der\n temporäre oder lokale Arbeitsmangel bewirkt keine Erhöhung des Arbeits-\n lohns, sondern Pressung von Weibern und Kindern in den Feldbau und\n Herabsteigen zu stets niedrigeren Altersstufen. Sobald die Weiber- und\n Kinderausbeutung grösseren Spielraum gewinnt, wird sie ihrerseits ein\n neues Mittel zur Ueberzähligmachung des männlichen Landarbeiters und\n [Note: schwachherziger Landlord über die von ihm geschaffene Einöde. „Est ist ein\n melancholisch Ding allein in seinem Land zu sein“, sagte der Graf von Leicester,\n als man ihm zum Fertigbau von Holkham gratulirte; „ich schaue um mich und\n sehe kein Haus ausser meinem eignen.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"21.12"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0013","text":"Ich bin der Riese vom Riesenthurm und\n habe alle meine Nachbarn aufgegessen.“] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"684\", \"facs\": \"#f0703\"} --> Niederhaltung seines Lohns. Im Osten Englands blüht eine schöne\n Frucht dieses cercle vicieux — das s. g. Gangsystem (Gang- oder\n Bandensystem), worauf ich hier kurz zurückkomme [Note: Der „Sechste“ und schliessliche „Report“ der „Child. Empl.\n Comm.“, publicirt Ende März 1867, behandelt nur das agrikole Gangsystem.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"21.13"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0014","text":"Das Gangsystem haust fast ausschliesslich in Lincolnshire, Hunting-\n donshire, Cambridgeshire, Norfolk, Suffolk und Nottinghamshire, sporadisch\n in den benachbarten Grafschaften von Northampton, Bedford und Rutland.\n Als Beispiel diene hier Lincolnshire. Ein grosser Theil dieser Graf-\n schaft ist neu, früherer Moor oder auch, wie in andern der genannten öst-\n lichen Grafschaften, der See erst abgewonnenes Land. Die Dampf-\n maschine hat für die Entwässerung Wunder gewirkt. Früherer Morast\n und Sandboden trägt jetzt ein üppiges Kornmeer und die höchsten Grund-\n renten. Dasselbe gilt von dem künstlich gewonnenen Alluvialland, wie in\n der Insel von Axholme und den andren Pfarreien am Ufer des Trent.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"21.14"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0015","text":"Im\n Mass wie die neuen Pachten entstanden, wurden nicht nur keine neuen\n Cottages gebaut, sondern alte niedergerissen, die Arbeitszufuhr aber ver-\n schafft aus den meilenweit entfernten offnen Dörfern längst den Land-\n strassen, die an Hügelrücken vorbeischlängeln. Dort hatte die Bevöl-\n kerung früher allein Schutz vor den langanhaltenden Winterüberschwem-\n mungen gefunden. Auf den Pachten von 400 bis 1000 Acres ansässige\n Arbeiter (sie heissen hier „confined labourers“) dienen ausschliesslich zur\n permanenten schweren und mit Pferden verrichteten Landarbeit. Auf je\n 100 Acres (1 Acre = 1.584 preussische Morgen) kommt im Durch-\n schnitt kaum eine Cottage. Ein Fenlandpächter z. B. sagt aus vor der\n Untersuchungskommission: „Meine Pacht erstreckt sich über 320 Acres,\n alles Kornland. Sie hat keine Cottage. Ein Arbeiter wohnt jetzt bei\n mir.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"21.15"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0016","text":"Ich habe vier Pferdemänner in der Umgegend logirend. Das leichte\n Werk, wozu zahlreiche Hände nöthig, wird durch Gänge vollbracht“ [Note: „Child. Empl. Comm. VI. Report.“ Evidence, p. 173.] .\n Der Boden erheischt viel leichtes Feldwerk wie Ausjäten des Unkrauts,\n Behackung, gewisse Düngeroperationen, Auflesen der Steine u. s. w.\n Es wird verrichtet durch die Gänge oder organisirten Banden, deren\n Wohnsitz in den offenen Ortschaften.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"21.16"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0017","text":"Der Gang besteht aus 10 bis 40 oder 50 Personen, nämlich Wei-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"685\", \"facs\": \"#f0704\"} --> bern, jungen Personen beiderlei Geschlechts (13—18 Jahr), obgleich Jun-\n gen meist mit dem 13. Jahr ausscheiden, endlich Kindern beiderlei\n Geschlechts (6—13. Jahr). An der Spitze steht der Gangmaster\n (Gangmeister), immer ein gewöhnlicher Landarbeiter, meist ein s. g.\n schlechter Kerl, Liederjahn, unstät, versoffen, aber mit einem ge-\n wissen Unternehmungsgeist und savoir faire. Er wirbt den Gang,\n der unter ihm arbeitet, nicht unter dem Pächter.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"21.17"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0018","text":"Mit letztrem\n akkordirt er meist auf Stückwerk, und sein Einkommen, das im Durch-\n schnitt nicht sehr hoch über das eines gewöhnlichen Landarbeiters\n steigt [Note: Einzelne Gangmeister jedoch haben sich zu Pächtern von 500 Acres oder\n Besitzern ganzer Häuserreihen heraufgearbeitet.] , hängt fast ganz ab vom Geschick, womit er in kürzester Zeit\n möglichst viel Arbeit aus seiner Bande flüssig zu machen weiss. Die\n Pächter haben entdeckt, dass Frauenzimmer nur unter männlicher Dikta-\n tur ordentlich arbeiten, dass aber Frauenzimmer und Kinder, wenn einmal\n im Zug, mit wahrem Ungestüm, was schon Fourier wusste, ihre Lebens-\n kraft verausgaben, während der erwachsne männliche Arbeiter so heim-\n tückisch ist damit, soviel er kann, hauszuhalten. Der Gangmeister zieht von\n einem Gut zum andern und beschäftigt so seine Bande 6—8 Monate im Jahr.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"21.18"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0019","text":"Er ist daher ein viel einträglicherer und sicherer Kunde für die Arbeiterfa-\n milien als der einzelne Pächter, welcher die Kinder nur gelegentlich beschäf-\n tigt. Dieser Umstand befestigt seinen Einfluss so sehr in den offnen Ort-\n schaften, dass in vielen die Kinder nur durch seine Dazwischenkunft hab-\n bar sind. Das individuelle Verpumpen derselben, ausserhalb des Gangs,\n an die Pächter, bildet sein Nebengeschäft.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"21.19"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0020","text":"Die „Schattenseiten“ des Systems sind die Ueberarbeit der Kinder\n und jungen Personen, die ungeheuren Märsche, die sie täglich zu und von\n den 5, 6 und manchmal 7 Meilen entfernten Gütern zu machen haben, end-\n lich die Demoralisation des „Gangs“. Obgleich der Gangmeister, der in\n einigen Gegenden „the driver“ (Treiber) heisst, mit einem langen Stabe\n ausgerüstet ist, wendet er solchen jedoch nur selten an, und Klage über\n brutale Behandlung ist Ausnahme. Er ist ein demokratischer Kaiser\n oder eine Art Rattenfänger von Hameln. Er bedarf also der Popularität\n unter seinen Unterthanen und fesselt sie an sich durch das unter seinen\n Auspicien blühende Zigeunerthum. Rohe Ungebundenheit, lustige Ausge-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"686\", \"facs\": \"#f0705\"} --> lassenheit und obscönste Frechheit leihen dem Gang Flügel.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"21.20"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0021","text":"Meist zahlt\n der Gangmeister in einer Kneipe aus und kehrt dann wohl wankend,\n rechts und links gestützt auf ein stämmiges Frauenmensch, an der Spitze\n des Zugs heim, die Kinder und jungen Personen hinterher tollend, Spott-\n und Zotenlieder singend. Auf dem Rückweg ist das, was Fourier ‚Phane-\n rogamie‘ nennt, an der Tagesordnung. Die Schwängerung dreizehn- und\n vierzehnjähriger Mädchen durch ihre männlichen Altersgenossen ist häufig.\n Die offenen Dörfer, welche das Kontingent des Gangs stellen, werden\n Sodoms und Gomorrhas [Note: „Die Hälfte der Mädchen von Bidford ist ruinirt worden durch den\n Gang.“ l. c. Appendix, p. 6, n. 32.]  und liefern doppelt so viel unehliche Geburten\n als der Rest des Königreichs. Was in dieser Schule gezüchtete Mädchen\n als verheirathete Frauen in der Moralität leisten, ward schon früher ange-\n deutet. Ihre Kinder, soweit sie selbe nicht durch Opium u. s.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"21.21"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0022","text":"w. besei-\n tigen, sind geborne Rekruten des Gangs.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"21.22"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0023","text":"Der Gang in seiner eben beschriebenen klassischen Form heisst\n öffentlicher, gemeiner oder Wandergang (public, common or tramping\n gang). Es giebt nämlich auch Privatgänge (private gangs). Sie\n sind zusammengesetzt wie der Gemeingang, zählen aber weniger Köpfe\n und arbeiten, statt unter dem Gangmeister, unter einem alten Bauern-\n knecht, den der Pächter nicht besser zu verwenden weiss. Der Zigeuner-\n humor verschwindet hier, aber nach allen Zeugenaussagen verschlechtern\n sich Zahlung und Behandlung der Kinder.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"21.23"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0024","text":"Das Gangsystem, das sich seit den letzten Jahren beständig aus-\n dehnt [Note: „Das System hat sehr zugenommen in den letzten Jahren. In einigen\n Plätzen ist es erst seit kurzem eingeführt, in andern, wo es älter, werden mehr und\n jüngere Kinder in den Gang einrollirt.“ (l. c. p. 79, n. 174.)] , existirt offenbar nicht dem Gangmeister zu lieb. Es existirt zur\n Bereicherung der grossen Pächter [Note: „Kleine Pächter wenden die Gangarbeit nicht an.“ „Sie wird nicht ange-\n wandt auf armem Land, sondern auf Land, was 2 Pfd. St. bis 2 Pfd. St. 10 sh.\n Rente per Acre bringt.“ (l. c. p. 17 u. 14.)] , resp. Landlords [Note: Einem dieser Herrn schmecken seine Renten so gut, dass er der Unter-\n suchungskommission entrüstet erklärt, der ganze Schrei sei nur dem Namen des\n Systems geschuldet.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"21.24"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0025","text":"Wenn man es statt „Gang“ dahingegen „jugendliche in-\n dustriell-agrikol-cooperative Selbsterhaltungsassociation“ taufe, so wäre alles all\n right.] . Für den Päch-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"687\", \"facs\": \"#f0706\"} --> ter giebt’s keine sinnreichere Methode, sein Arbeiterpersonal tief unter dem\n normalen Niveau zu halten und dennoch für alles Extrawerk stets die\n Extrahand bereit zu haben, mit möglichst wenig Geld möglichst viel\n Arbeit herauszuschlagen [Note: „Gangarbeit ist wohlfeiler als andre Arbeit, das ist die Ursache, warum\n sie angewandt wird“, sagt ein ehemaliger Gangmeister. (l. c. p. 17, n. 11.)\n „Das Gangsystem ist entschieden das wohlfeilste für den Pächter und eben so ent-\n schieden das verderblichste für die Kinder“, sagt ein Pächter. (l. c. p. 16, n. 4.)]  und den erwachsnen männlichen Arbeiter\n „überzählig“ zu machen.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"21.25"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0026","text":"Nach der frühern Auseinandersetzung versteht\n man, wenn einerseits die grössere oder geringere Beschäftigungslosigkeit\n des Landmanns zugestanden, andrerseits zugleich das Gangsystem wegen\n Mangels an männlicher Arbeit und ihrer Wanderung nach den Städten für\n „nothwendig“ erklärt wird [Note: „Zweifelsohne vieles jetzt von den Kindern in Gängen verrichtete Werk\n wurde früher von Männern und Weibern verrichtet. Wo Weiber und Kinder an-\n gewandt werden, sind jetzt mehr Männer arbeitslos („more men are out of work“)\n als früher.“ (l. c. p. 43, n. 202.) Dagegen u.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"21.26"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0027","text":"a.: „Die Arbeitsfrage\n (labour question) in vielen Agrikulturdistrikten, besonders den kornproduciren-\n den, wird so ernsthaft in Folge der Auswanderung und der Leichtigkeit, welche\n die Eisenbahnen zur Entfernung nach den grossen Städten bieten, dass ich (das\n „Ich“ ist das des Landagenten eines grossen Herrn) die Kinderdienste für absolut\n unentbehrlich halte.“ (l. c. p. 80, n. 180.) The Labour Question (die\n Arbeitsfrage) bedeutet nämlich in den englischen Agrikulturdistrikten, im Unter-\n schied von der übrigen civilisirten Welt, the landlords’ and farmers’\n Question (Landlords- und Pächterfrage), wie, trotz stets vermehrtem Abzug der\n Landleute, eine genügende „relative Uebervölkerung“ auf dem Land und dadurch\n das „Minimum des Arbeitslohns“ für den Landarbeiter zu verewigen sei?] . Das unkrautreine Feld und das\n Menschenunkraut von Lincolnshire u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"21.27"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0028","text":"sind Pol und Gegenpol der\n kapitalistischen Produktion [Note: Der früher von mir citirte „Public Health Report“, worin bei Gelegen-\n heit der Kindersterblichkeit vorübergehend vom Gangsystem gehandelt wird, blieb\n der Presse und daher dem englischen Publikum unbekannt. Dagegen bot der\n neuliche Bericht der „Child. Empl. Comm.“ willkommenes „sensational“\n Pressfutter. Während die liberale Presse frug, wie doch die feinen Gentlemen\n und Ladies und Staatskirchpfründner, womit Lincolnshire schwärmt, ein solches\n System auf ihren Gütern, unter ihren Augen aufwachsen lassen konnten, Perso-\n nagen, die eigne „Missionen zur Sittenverbesserung der Südseewilden“ nach den\n Antipoden entsenden, stellte die feinere Presse ausschliesslich Betrachtungen über\n die rohe Verdorbenheit der Landleute an, die fähig sind, ihre Kinder in] .","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"21.28"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0029","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"688\", \"facs\": \"#f0707\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"21.29"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0030","text":"Zum Schluss dieses Abschnitts müssen wir noch einen Augenblick\n nach Irland wandern. Zunächst die Thatsachen, worauf es hier\n ankommt.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"21.30"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0031","text":"Irlands Bevölkerung war 1841 auf 8,222,664 Personen ange-\n wachsen, 1851 auf 6,623,982 zusammengeschmolzen, 1861 auf\n 5,850,309, 1866 auf 5½ Mill., ungefähr auf ihr Niveau von 1801.\n Die Abnahme begann mit dem Hungerjahr 1846, so dass Irland in\n weniger als 20 Jahren mehr als \\frac{5}{16} seiner Volksmenge verlor [Note: Bevölkerung von Irland: 1801: 5,319,867 Personen, 1811:\n 6,084,996, 1821: 6,869,544, 1831: 7,828,347, 1841: 8,222,664.] . Seine\n Gesammtemigration von Mai 1851 bis Juli 1865 zählte 1,591,487 Per-\n sonen, die Emigration während der letzten 5 Jahre 1861—1865 mehr\n als eine halbe Million. Die Zahl der bewohnten Häuser verminderte\n sich von 1851—1861 um 52,990.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"21.31"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0032","text":"Von 1851—1861 wuchs die Zahl\n der Pachthöfe von 15—30 Acres um 61,000, die der Pachthöfe über\n 30 Acres um 109,000, während die Gesammtzahl aller Pach-\n ten um 120,000 abnahm, eine Abnahme, die also ausschliesslich der\n Vernichtung von Pachten unter 15 Acres, alias ihrer Koncen-\n tration geschuldet ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"21.32"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0033","text":"[Note: solche Sklaverei zu verkaufen! Unter den fluchwürdigen Umständen, worin „die\n Delikatern“ den Landmann gebannt, wäre es erklärlich, wenn er seine eigenen\n Kinder aufässe. Was wirklich wunderbar, ist die Charaktertüchtigkeit, die er\n grossentheils bewahrt hat. Die officiellen Berichterstatter beweisen, dass die\n Eltern selbst in den Gangdistrikten das Gangsystem verabscheuen. „Man findet\n reichlichen Beweis in den von uns gesammelten Zeugenaussagen, dass die Eltern\n in vielen Fällen dankbar sein würden für ein Zwangsgesetz, welches sie be-\n fähigen würde, den Versuchungen und dem Druck zu widerstehn, denen sie oft\n unterworfen sind.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"21.33"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0034","text":"Bald treibt sie der Pfarreibeamte, bald der Anwender unter\n Androhung ihrer eignen Entlassung, die Kinder auf den Verdienst, statt in die\n Schule zu schicken … Alle verwüstete Zeit und Kraft, alles Leid, welches ausser-\n ordentliche und nutzlose Ermüdung für den Landmann und seine Familie produ-\n cirt, jeder Fall, worin die Eltern den moralischen Ruin ihres Kindes auf die Ueber-\n füllung der Cottages oder die besudelnden Einflüsse des Gangsystems zurückleiten,\n stacheln in der Brust der arbeitenden Armen Gefühle auf, die man wohl verstehn\n wird, und die es unnöthig ist zu detailliren. Sie haben ein Bewusstsein darüber,\n dass ihnen viel körperliche und geistige Qual angethan wird durch Umstände,\n wofür sie in keiner Weise verantwortlich sind, welchen sie, hätte es in ihrer Macht\n gestanden, niemals ihre Zustimmung gegeben hätten, und wider welche anzu-\n kämpfen sie ohnmächtig sind.“ (l.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"21.34"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0035","text":"c. p. XX, n. 82 und XXIII, n. 96.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"21.35"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0036","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"689\", \"facs\": \"#f0708\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"21.36"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0037","text":"Die Abnahme der Volksmenge war natürlich im Grossen und Ganzen\n von einer Abnahme der Produktenmasse begleitet. Für unsren Zweck\n genügt es, die 5 Jahre 1861—1865 zu betrachten, während deren über\n ½ Million emigrirte und die absolute Volkszahl um mehr als ⅓\n Million sank.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"21.37"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0038","text":"Tabelle A.\nViehstand.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"21.38"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0039","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAus der vorhergehenden Tabelle ergiebt sich:","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"21.39"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0040","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWenden wir uns jetzt zum Ackerbau, der die Lebensmittel für Vieh\n und Mensch liefert. In der folgenden Tabelle ist Ab- oder Zunahme für das\n Jahr 1861 auf das Jahr 1860 berechnet, kurz für jedes einzelne Jahr\n [Running matter: I. 44] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"690\", \"facs\": \"#f0709\"} --> mit Bezug auf das unmittelbar vorhergehende. Die Kornfrucht um-\n fasst Weizen, Hafer, Gerste, Bere, Roggen, Bohnen und Erbsen, die\nGrünfrucht Kartoffeln, Turnips, Mangel- und Runkelrübe, Kohl, gelbe\n Rüben, Parsnips, Wicke u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"21.40"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0041","text":"Tabelle B.\n Zu- oder Abnahme des zum Fruchtbau und als Wiese (resp. Weide) benutzten\n Bodenareals in Acres.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"21.41"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0042","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIm Jahr 1865 kamen unter der Rubrik „Grasland“ 127,470 Acres\n hinzu, hauptsächlich weil das Areal unter der Rubrik „unbenutztes\n wüstes Land und Bog (Sumpfland)“ um 101,543 Acres abnahm. Ver-\n gleichen wir 1865 mit 1864, so Abnahme in Kornfrucht\n 246,667 Qrs., wovon 48,999 Weizen, 166,605 Hafer, 29,892 Gerste\n u. s. w., Abnahme von Kartoffelertrag, obgleich das Areal\n ihrer Bebauung 1865 wuchs, 446,398 Tonnen u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"21.42"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0043","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"691\", \"facs\": \"#f0710\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"21.43"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0044","text":"Tabelle C.\n Zu- oder Abnahme in dem Areal des bebauten Bodens, dem Produkt per Acre, und dem Gesammtprodukt,\n 1865 verglichen mit 1864.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"21.44"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0045","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n [Running matter: 44*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"692\", \"facs\": \"#f0711\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"21.45"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0046","text":"Von der Bewegung der Bevölkerung und Bodenproduktion Irlands\n gehn wir über zur Bewegung in der Börse seiner Landlords,\n grösseren Pächter und industriellen Kapitalisten. Sie reflektirt sich im\n Ab und Zu der der Einkommensteuer unterworfenen jährlichen Ein-\n kommen. Zum Verständniss der folgenden Tabelle bemerken wir, dass Ru-\n brik D (Profite mit Ausnahme der Pächterprofite) auch s. g. „profes-\n sionelle“ Profite einbegreift, d. h. die Einkommen von Advokaten, Aerzten\n u. s. w., die nicht besonders aufgezählten Rubriken C und E aber die\n Einnahmen von Beamten, Officieren, Staatssynekuristen, Staatsgläu-\n bigern u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"21.46"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0047","text":"Tabelle D.\n Der Einkommensteuer unterliegende Einkommen in Pfd. St.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"21.47"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0048","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nUnter Rubrik D betrug die Zunahme des Einkommens im Jahres-\n durchschnitt von 1853—1864 nur 0.93, während sie in derselben Pe-\n riode in Grossbritanien 4.58 betrug. Die folgende Tabelle zeigt die\nVertheilung der Profite (mit Ausschluss der Pächterprofite)\n für die Jahre 1864 und 1865:\nSiehe nebenstehende Tabelle E.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"21.48"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0049","text":"England, ein Land entwickelter kapitalistischer Produktion und vor-\n zugsweis industriell, wäre verblutet an einem Volksaderlass, gleich dem\n irischen. Aber Irland ist gegenwärtig nur ein durch einen breiten Was-\n sergraben abgezäunter Agrikulturdistrikt Englands, dem es Korn, Wolle,\n Vieh, industrielle und militärische Rekruten liefert.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"21.49"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0050","text":"Die Entvölkerung hat viel Land ausser Bebauung geworfen, das\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"693\", \"facs\": \"#f0712\"} -->Tabelle E.\n Rubrik D. Einkommen aus Profiten (über 60 Pfd. St.) in Irland.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"21.50"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0051","text":"| \n\n\n\n Bodenprodukt sehr vermindert [Note: Wenn das Produkt auch verhältnissmässig per Acre abnimmt\n vergesse man nicht, dass England seit 1½ Jahrhunderten den Boden von Irland\n exportirt hat, ohne seinen Bebauern auch nur die Mittel zum Ersatz der\n Bodenbestandtheile zu gönnen.] , und selbst, trotz des erweiterten Areals\n der Viehzucht, in einigen ihrer Zweige absolute Abnahme erzeugt, in an-\n dern kaum nennenswerthen und durch beständige Rückschritte unterbroch-\n nen Fortschritt. Dennoch stiegen Grundrenten und Pachtprofite, obgleich\n letztere nicht so constant wie die erstern, fortwährend mit dem Fall der\n Volksmasse. Der Grund ist leicht verständlich. Einerseits verwandelte\n sich mit der Zusammenwerfung der Pachten und der Verwandlung von\n Ackerland in Viehweide ein grösserer Theil des Gesammtprodukts\n in Mehrprodukt.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"21.51"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0052","text":"Die absolute Masse des Mehrprodukts wuchs, ob-\n gleich das Gesammtprodukt, wovon sie einen Bruchtheil bildet, absolut\n abnahm. Andrerseits stieg der Geldwerth dieses Mehrprodukts noch rascher\n als seine Masse, in Folge der seit den letzten 20 und ganz besonders seit\n den letzten 10 Jahren steigenden englischen Marktpreise für Fleisch,\n Wolle u. s. w.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"21.52"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0053","text":"Zersplitterte Produktionsmittel, die den Producenten selbst als Be-\n schäftigungs- und Subsistenzmittel dienen, ohne sich durch Einverleibung\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"694\", \"facs\": \"#f0713\"} --> fremder Arbeit zu verwerthen, sind eben so wenig Kapital als das von\n seinem eignen Producenten verzehrte Produkt Waare ist. Wenn also\n auch mit der Volksmasse die Masse der in der Agrikultur angewandten\nProduktionsmittel sich verminderte, vermehrte sich die Masse des\n in ihr angewandten Kapitals, weil ein Theil jener vorher zersplitterten\n Produktionsmittel in Kapital verwandelt ward.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"21.53"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0054","text":"Das ausserhalb der Agrikultur, in Industrie und Handel angelegte\n Gesammtkapital Irlands accumulirte langsam während der letzten zwei\n Decennien und unter beständiger grosser Fluktuation. Andrerseits ent-\n wickelte sich um so rascher die Koncentration seiner individuellen Bestand-\n theile. Endlich, wie langsam immerhin das Kapital absolut wuchs, rela-\n tiv, im Verhältniss zur zusammengeschmolzenen Volkszahl, war es ausser-\n ordentlich angeschwollen.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"21.54"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0055","text":"Hier entrollt sich also, unter unsern Augen, auf grosser Stufenleiter,\n ein Prozess wie die orthodoxe Oekonomie ihn nicht schöner wünschen\n konnte zur Bewähr ihres Dogma’s, welches das Elend aus absoluter Ueber-\n völkerung erklärt und das Gleichgewicht durch Entvölkerung wieder her-\n stellt. Es ist diess ein ganz anders wichtiges Experiment als die von den\n Malthusianern so sehr verherrlichte Pest in der Mitte des vierzehnten Jahr-\n hunderts. Nebenbei bemerkt. War es an sich schulmeisterlich naiv, den\n Produktions- und entsprechenden Bevölkerungsverhältnissen des 19. Jahr-\n hunderts den Massstab des 14.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"21.55"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0056","text":"Jahrhunderts anzulegen, so übersah diese\n Naivetät noch obendrein, dass wenn jener Pest und der sie begleitenden\n Decimation, diesseits des Kanals, in England, Befreiung und Bereicherung\n des Landvolks, ihr jenseits, in Frankreich, grössere Knechtung und er-\n höhtes Elend auf dem Fuss nachfolgten.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"21.56"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0057","text":"Die Hungersnoth erschlug 1846 in Irland über eine Menschenmillion,\n aber nur arme Teufel, ohne den geringsten Abbruch am Reichthum des Lan-\n des. Der nachfolgende zwanzigjährige und stets noch anschwellende\n Exodus decimirte nicht, wie etwa der dreissigjährige Krieg, mit den Men-\n schen zugleich ihre Produktionsmittel. Das irische Genie erfand eine ganz\n neue Methode, ein armes Volk Tausende von Meilen vom Schauplatz sei-\n nes Elends wegzuhexen. Die in die Vereinigten Staaten übergesiedelten\n Auswanderer schicken jährlich Geldsummen nach Haus, die Reisemittel\n der Zurückgebliebenen. Jeder Trupp, der dieses Jahr auswandert, zieht\n nächstes Jahr einen andern Trupp nach. Statt Irland zu kosten, bildet\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"695\", \"facs\": \"#f0714\"} --> die Auswanderung so einen der einträglichsten Zweige seines Exportge-\n schäfts.","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"21.57"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0058","text":"Endlich ist diess ein systematischer Prozess, der nicht etwa ein\n vorübergehendes Loch in die Volksmasse bohrt, sondern jährlich mehr\n Menschen aus ihr auspumpt als der Nachwuchs ersetzt, so dass das ab-\n solute Bevölkerungsniveau von Jahr zu Jahr sinkt.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"21.58"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0059","text":"Welches waren die Folgen für die zurückbleibende, von der „Sur-\n pluspopulation“ befreite Arbeiterbevölkerung Irlands? Dass die rela-\n tive Uebervölkerung heute so gross ist wie vor 1846, dass der Arbeits-\n lohn eben so niedrig steht und die Arbeitsplackerei zugenommen hat,\n dass die Misère auf dem Land wieder zu einer neuen Krise drängt. Die\n Ursachen sind einfach. Die Revolution in der Agrikultur hielt Schritt mit der\n Emigration. Die Produktion der relativen Uebervölkerung hielt mehr\n als Schritt mit der absoluten Entvölkerung. Ein Blick auf Tabelle C\n zeigt, wie die Verwandlung von Ackerbau in Viehweide in Irland noch\n akuter wirken muss als in England. Hier wächst mit der Viehzucht der\n Bau von Grünfrucht, dort nimmt er ab.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"21.59"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0060","text":"Während grosse Massen früher\n bestellter Aecker brachgelegt oder in permanentes Grasland verwandelt\n werden, wird ein grosser Theil des früher unbenutzten wüsten Landes und\n Bog’s zur Ausdehnung der Viehzucht benutzt. Die kleineren und mittle-\n ren Pächter — ich rechne dazu alle, die nicht über 100 Acres bebauen\n — haben immer noch ungefähr \\frac{8}{10} des irischen Bodens inne. Sie wer-\n den progressiv in ganz anderem Grad als zuvor von der Konkurrenz des\n kapitalistisch betriebenen Ackerbaus erdrückt und liefern daher der eigent-\n lichen Arbeiterklasse beständig neue Rekruten. Die einzige grosse In-\n dustrie Irlands, die Leinenfabrikation, braucht verhältnissmässig wenig er-\n wachsne männliche Arbeiter und beschäftigt überhaupt, trotz ihrer Expan-\n sion seit der Vertheurung der Baumwolle, nur einen verhältnissmässig un-\n bedeutenden Theil der Arbeitermasse.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"21.60"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0061","text":"Gleich jeder andern grossen In-\n dustrie producirt sie durch die beständigen Schwankungen in ihrer eignen\n Sphäre beständig eine relative Uebervölkerung, selbst bei absolutem Wachs-\n thum der von ihr absorbirten Arbeiterzahl. Die Misère des Landvolks\n bildet das Piedestal riesenhafter Hemdenfabriken u. s. w., deren Arbeiter-\n armee zum grössten Theil über das flache Land zerstreut ist. Wir finden\n hier das früher geschilderte System der Hausarbeit wieder, welches in\n Unterzahlung und Ueberarbeit seine methodischen Mittel der „Ueberzäh-\n ligmachung“ besitzt. Endlich, obschon die Entvölkerung nicht so zer-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"696\", \"facs\": \"#f0715\"} --> störende Folgen hat, wie in einem Land entwickelter kapitalistischer Pro-\n duktion, vollzieht sie sich nicht ohne beständigen Rückschlag. Die Emi-\n gration lässt nicht nur leere Häuser zurück, sondern auch ruinirte Haus-\n vermiether.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"21.61"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0062","text":"Klein wie der Konsum jedes ihrer individuellen Bestandtheile,\n producirt ihr Gesammtausfall eine beständige Lücke im innern Markt, der\n namentlich Kleinkrämer, Handwerker und kleine Gewerbsleute überhaupt\n trifft. Jeder neue Exodus schleudert einen Theil der kleinen Mittelklasse ins\n Proletariat. Sieh Tabelle E die Abnahme der Einkommen unter 100 Pfd. St.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"21.62"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0063","text":"Der Wochenlohn des Ackerbauers in der Umgegend von Dublin —\n der Maximallohn des irischen Ackerbauers — steht in diesem Augenblick,\n bei hohem Preis der ersten Lebensmittel, auf 7 sh. Man kann daraus\n auf seinen Stand in den rein agrikolen und entlegenen Distrikten zurück-\n schliessen. Zur Schilderung der Lage selbst des geschickten irischen\n industriellen Arbeiters genügt ein Beispiel.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"21.63"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0064","text":"„Bei meiner neulichen Inspektion des Nordens von Irland“, sagt der\n englische Fabrikinspektor Robert Baker, „frappirte mich die Bemü-\n hung eines geschickten irischen Arbeiters aus den allerdürftigsten Mitteln\n seinen Kindern Erziehung zu verschaffen. Ich gebe seine Aussage ver-\n batim, wie ich sie aus seinem Mund erhielt. Dass er eine geschickte\n Fabrikhand, weiss man, wenn ich sage, dass man ihn zu Artikeln für den Man-\n chester Markt verwandte. Johnson: Ich bin ein beetler, und arbeite von\n 6 Uhr Morgens bis 11 in die Nacht, von Montag bis Freitag; Samstags endigen\n wir um 6 Uhr Abends und haben 3 Stunden für Mahlzeit und Erholung.\n Ich habe 5 Kinder. Für diese Arbeit erhalte ich 10 sh. 6 d. wöchent-\n lich; meine Frau arbeitet auch und verdient 5 sh. die Woche. Das älteste\n Mädchen, zwölfjährig, wartet das Haus. Sie ist unser Koch und einziger\n Gehülfe.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"21.64"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0065","text":"Sie macht die jüngeren zur Schule fertig. Meine Frau steht\n mit mir auf und geht mit mir fort. Ein Mädchen, die unser Haus entlang\n geht, weckt mich um halb 6 Uhr Morgens. Wir essen nichts, bevor wir\n zur Arbeit gehn. Das zwölfjährige Kind sorgt für die Kleineren des Tags\n über. Wir frühstücken um 8 und gehn dazu nach Hause. Wir haben Thee ein-\n mal die Woche; sonst haben wir einen Brei (stirabout), manchmal von Hafer-\n mehl, manchmal von indischem Mehl, je nachdem wir fähig sind es zu verschaf-\n fen. Im Winter haben wir ein wenig Zucker und Wasser zu unserm indischen\n Mehl. Im Sommer erndten wir einige Kartoffeln, womit wir selbst ein Boden-\n fetzchen bepflanzen, und wenn sie zu Ende sind, kehren wir zum Brei zu-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"697\", \"facs\": \"#f0716\"} --> rück. So geht’s Tag aus Tag ein, Sonntag und Werkeltag, das ganze\n Jahr durch.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"21.65"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0066","text":"Ich bin stets sehr müde des Abends nach vollbrachtem Tag-\n werk. Einen Bissen Fleisch sehn wir ausnahmsweis, aber sehr selten.\n Drei unsrer Kinder besuchen Schule, wofür wir 1 d. per Kopf wöchentlich\n zahlen. Unsere Hausmiethe ist 9 d. die Woche, Torf für Feuerung kostet\n mindestens 1 sh. 6 d. vierzehntägig“ [Note: „Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31. Oct. 1866.“ Lond. 1867,\n p. 96.] . Das sind irische Löhne, das\n ist irisches Leben!","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"21.66"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0067","text":"In der That, das Elend Irlands ist wieder Tagesthema in England.\n Ende 1866 und Anfang 1867 machte sich in der Times einer der\n irischen Landmagnaten, Lord Dufferin, an die Lösung. „Wie mensch-\n lich von solch’ grossem Herrn!“","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"21.67"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0068","text":"Aus Tabelle E sah man, dass während 1864 von 4,368,610 Pfd.\n St. Gesammtprofit 3 Plusmacher nur 262,610, dieselben 3 Virtuosen der\n „Entsagung“ 1865 von 4,669,979 Pfd. St. Gesammtprofit dagegen\n 274,448 Pfd. St. einsteckten, 1864: 26 Plusmacher 646,377 Pfd. St.,\n1865 : 28 Plusmacher 736,448 Pfd. St., 1864 : 121 Plusmacher\n 1,066,912 Pfd. St., 1865 : 186 Plusmacher 1,320,996 Pfd. St., 1864 :\n 1131 Plusmacher 2,150,818 Pfd. St., beinahe die Hälfte des jährlichen\n Gesammtprofits, 1865 : 1194 Plusmacher 2,418,933 Pfd. St., mehr als\n die Hälfte des jährlichen Gesammtprofits.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"21.68"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0069","text":"Der Löwenantheil aber, welchen\n eine verschwindend kleine Anzahl Landmagnaten in England, Schottland\n und Irland vom jährlichen Nationalrental verschlingt, ist so monströs,\n dass die englische Staatsweisheit es angemessen findet, für die Vertheilung\n der Grundrente nicht dasselbe statistische Material zu liefern wie für die\n Vertheilung des Profits. Lord Dufferin ist einer dieser Landmag-\n naten. Dass Rentrollen und Profite jemals „überzählig“ sein können, oder\n dass ihre Plethora mit der Plethora des Volkselends irgendwie zusammen-\n hängt, ist natürlich eine ebenso „irrespektable“ als „ungesunde“ (unsound)\n Vorstellung. Er hält sich an Thatsachen. Die Thatsache ist, dass wie\n die irische Volkszahl abnimmt, die irischen Rentrollen schwellen, dass die\n Entvölkerung dem Grundeigenthümer „wohlthut“, also auch dem Grund,\n also auch dem Volk, das nur ein Accessorium des Bodens ist.","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"21.69"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0070","text":"Er erklärt also,\n Irland sei immer noch übervölkert und der Strom der Emigration fliesse\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"698\", \"facs\": \"#f0717\"} --> stets noch zu träg. Um vollständig glücklich zu sein, müsse Irland wenig-\n stens noch ⅓ Million Arbeitsmenschen ablassen. Man wähne nicht, die-\n ser obendrein noch poetische Lord sei ein Arzt aus der Schule Sangrado’s,\n der, so oft er seinen Kranken nicht besser fand, Aderlass verordnete, neuen\n Aderlass, bis der Patient mit seinem Blut auch seine Krankheit verlor.\n Lord Dufferin verlangt einen neuen Aderlass von nur ⅓ Million, statt von\n ungefähr 2 Millionen, ohne deren Ablass in der That das Millennium in\n Erin nicht herstellbar ist. Der Beweis ist leicht geliefert.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"21.70"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0071","text":"Anzahl und Umfang der Pachten in Irland 1864.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"21.71"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0072","text":"| \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDie Koncentration hat von 1851 bis 1861 hauptsächlich Pachten\n der ersten 3 Kategorieen, unter 1 und nicht über 15 Acres, vernichtet. Sie\n müssen vor allem verschwinden. Diess giebt 307,058 „überzählige“\n Pächter, und die Familie zum niedrigen Durchschnitt von 4 Köpfen ge-\n rechnet, 1,228,232 Personen. Unter der extravaganten Unterstellung,\n dass ¼ davon nach vollbrachter agrikoler Revolution wieder absorbirbar,\n bleiben auszuwandern: 921,174 Personen. Die Kategorieen 4, 5, 6,\n von über 15 und nicht über 100 Acres, sind, wie man längst in England\n weiss, für den kapitalistischen Kornbau zu klein, für Schafzucht aber fast\n verschwindende Grössen. Unter denselben Unterstellungen wie vorher\n sind also fernere 788,761 Personen auszuwandern, Summe: 1,709,532.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"21.72"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0073","text":"Und, comme l’appétit vient en mangeant, werden die Augen der Rentrolle\n bald entdecken, dass Irland mit 3½ Millionen immer noch elend, und\n elend, weil übervölkert ist, also seine Entvölkerung noch viel weiter\n gehen muss, damit es seinen wahren Beruf erfülle, den einer englischen\n Schaftrift und Viehweide.","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"21.73"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0074","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"699\", \"facs\": \"#f0718\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"21.74"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0075","text":"Diese einbringliche Methode hat wie alles Gute in dieser Welt ihren\n Missstand. Mit der Accumulation der Grundrente in Irland hält Schritt\n die Accumulation der Irländer in Amerika. Der durch Schaf und Ochs\n beseitigte Ire ersteht auf der andern Seite des Oceans als Fenian. Und\n gegenüber der alten Seekönigin erhebt sich drohend und drohender die\n junge Riesenrepublik.","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"21.75"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0076","text":"Acerba fata Romanos agunt\n Scelusque fraternae necis.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"21.76"}],"sha256":"20765d98c7a925a9a0a59786b3ea7683c3a9e93613990b24029e40e55d78d40f","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T224826821306Z_9551656a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"c) Das allgemeine Gesetz der kapitalistischen Accu- mulation. · reading part 2/2","source_sha256":"20765d98c7a925a9a0a59786b3ea7683c3a9e93613990b24029e40e55d78d40f","sha256":"cf1ae2b65bbd840c1b2ed8c11f1c051b42ad9a30b59182a761a1ec21b5951bda","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T22:48:26.821334+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0001","text":"Badsey. Many cots and small gardens here. Some tenants declare\nthe cots “a great nuisance here, because they bring the poor”. (The\ncots [are] a great evil, because they bring the poor here.) To the remark\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"682\", \"facs\": \"#f0701\"} --> of a gentleman: “The poor are not one whit better off if one builds\n500 cots; they go off like hot cakes; in fact, the more of them are built,\nthe more are needed” — according to him the houses produce the inhabitants,\nwho, by a natural law, press upon “the means of habitation” — Dr. Hunter\nremarks: “Well, these poor must come from somewhere, and since no special\nattraction, such as alms, exists in Badsey, repulsion from a still more\nuncomfortable place must exist, which drives them hither.”","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"21.1","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0002","text":"If everyone could find a cot and a little piece\nof land near his place of work, he would surely prefer this to\nBadsey, where he pays twice as much for his handful of ground as\nthe tenant does for his.”","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"21.2","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0003","text":"The constant emigration to the towns, the constant\n“rendering supernumerary” in the countryside through concentration, trans-\nformation of arable land into pasture, machinery, etc., and the constant\neviction of the rural population through destruction of the cotta-\nges go hand in hand. The more devoid of people the district, the greater its\n“relative overpopulation”, the greater its pressure upon the means of employ-\nment, the greater the absolute surplus of the rural people over its means of habi-\ntation, the greater therefore in the villages the local overpopulation\nand the most pestilential packing together of human beings. The condensation of\nthe human knot in scattered small villages and market towns corresponds\nto the forcible evacuation of human beings over the surface of the land.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"21.3","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0004","text":"The\nuninterrupted “rendering supernumerary” of agricultural labourers despite their de-\ncreasing number and with the growing mass of their product, is the\ncradle of their pauperism. Their eventual pauperism is a motive for their\neviction and the main source of their misery of dwelling, which breaks\nthe last power of resistance and makes them pure slaves of the landlords [Note: “The heaven-born employment of the hind gives dignity even to his\nposition. He is not a slave, but a soldier of peace, and deserves his\nplace in married man’s quarters, to be provided by the landlord, who has\nclaimed a power of enforced labour similar to that the country demands\nof a military soldier. He no more receives market-price for his work than does a\nsoldier. Like the soldier he is caught young, ignorant, knowing only his own\ntrade and his own locality.”","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"21.4","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0005","text":"Early marriage and the operation of the various laws\nof settlement affect the one as enlistment and the Mutiny Act affect the other.”\n(Dr. Hunter l. c. p. 132.) Sometimes some exceptional]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"683\", \"facs\": \"#f0702\"} --> and tenant softens, so that the minimum of wages becomes established\nas a natural law for them. On the other hand, the land, despite its constant\n“relative overpopulation”, is at the same time underpopulated. This\nshows itself not only locally at such points where the outflow of people to\nthe towns, mines, railway construction, etc. proceeds too rapidly; it shows\nitself everywhere both in autumn and in spring and summer during the\nnumerous moments when very careful and intensive English\nagriculture requires extra hands.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"21.5","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0006","text":"Agricultural labourers are always too numerous\nfor the average and always too few for the exceptional or\ntemporary needs of agriculture [Note: A similar movement has taken place during recent decades in France, in\nproportion as capitalist production has taken possession of agriculture there and\ndrives the “supernumerary” rural population to the towns. Here too, worsened\nconditions of dwelling and otherwise at the source of the “supernumeraries”. On\nthe peculiar “prolétariat foncier” which the system of parcels has hatched, see,\namong other things, the previously cited work by Collins and:\nKarl Marx: “The Eighteenth Brumaire of Louis Bonaparte.\nNew York 1852”, p. 56 sqq.) In 1846 the urban population in\nFrance amounted to 24.42, the rural to 75.58; in 1861, the urban to 28.86, the rural to\n71.41.”","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"21.6","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0007","text":"In the last 5 years the decrease in the rural percentage of the\npopulation has been still greater. As early as 1846 Pierre Dupont sang in his\n“Ouvriers”:","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"21.7","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0008","text":"“Ill-clad, lodged in holes,”","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"21.8","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0009","text":"“Under the roofs, amid the rubble,”","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"21.9","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0010","text":"“We live with the owls”","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"21.10","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0011","text":"“And the thieves, friends of shadows.”","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"21.11","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0012","text":"] . Hence one finds in the\nofficial documents the contradictory complaint of the same places\nabout simultaneous lack of labour and surplus of labour recorded. The\ntemporary or local lack of labour causes no increase in wages, but rather\nthe pressing of women and children into agriculture and a descent to ever\nlower age levels. As soon as the exploitation of women and children gains\ngreater scope, it in turn becomes a new means of rendering the male\nagricultural labourer supernumerary and [Note: faint-hearted landlord upon the desolation he has created. “It is a\nmelancholy thing to be alone on one’s land,” said the Earl of Leicester when he was\ncongratulated on the completion of Holkham; “I look around me and\nsee no house but my own.”","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"21.12","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0013","text":"I am the giant of the giant tower and\nhave eaten all my neighbours.”]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"684\", \"facs\": \"#f0703\"} --> keeping down his wage. In the east of England a fine\nfruit of this cercle vicieux flourishes — the so-called gang system (gang or\nband system), to which I shall briefly return here [Note: The “Sixth” and final “Report” of the “Child. Empl.\nComm.”, published at the end of March 1867, deals only with the agricultural gang system.] .","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"21.13","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0014","text":"The gang system prevails almost exclusively in Lincolnshire, Hunting-\ndonshire, Cambridgeshire, Norfolk, Suffolk, and Nottinghamshire, sporadically\nin the neighbouring counties of Northampton, Bedford, and Rutland.\nLincolnshire may serve here as an example. A large part of this county is\nnew land, land only recently reclaimed from former moor or, as in the other named eastern\ncounties, from the sea. The steam engine has worked\nwonders for drainage. Former marshland\nand sandy soil now bear a luxuriant sea of grain and the highest ground\nrents. The same applies to artificially reclaimed alluvial land, as on\nthe Isle of Axholme and the other parishes on the banks of the Trent.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"21.14","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0015","text":"In\nproportion as the new tenancies arose, not only were no new\ncottages built, but old ones were pulled down, while the supply of labour was pro-\ncured from the open villages miles away along the country\nroads winding past hill ridges. There the popu-\nlation had formerly found its sole protection from the long-lasting winter floods.\nLabourers settled on tenancies of 400 to 1000 acres (they are here called\n“confined labourers”) serve exclusively for\npermanent heavy agricultural work performed with horses. On every\n100 acres (1 acre = 1.584 Prussian Morgen), on average scarcely one cottage\nfalls. A Fenland tenant, for example, testifies before the\ncommission of inquiry: “My tenancy extends over 320 acres,\nall corn land. It has no cottage. One labourer now lives with\nme.”","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"21.15","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0016","text":"I have four horsemen lodged in the neighbourhood. The light\nwork, for which numerous hands are required, is performed by gangs” [Note: “Child. Empl. Comm. VI. Report.” Evidence, p. 173.] .\nThe soil requires much light field work, such as weeding,\nhoeing, certain operations with manure, picking up stones, etc.\nIt is performed by the gangs or organised bands, whose\nresidence is in the open localities.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"21.16","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0017","text":"The gang consists of 10 to 40 or 50 persons, namely wo-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"685\", \"facs\": \"#f0704\"} --> men, young persons of both sexes (13–18 years), although boys\nusually leave upon reaching their 13th year, and finally children of both\nsexes (6–13 years). At its head stands the gangmaster\n(gang master), always an ordinary agricultural labourer, usually a so-called\nbad fellow, rake, unsteady, drunken, but with a cer-\ntain enterprising spirit and savoir faire. He recruits the gang,\nwhich works under him, not under the tenant.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"21.17","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0018","text":"With the latter\nhe usually contracts for piecework, and his income, which on average does not rise very\nfar above that of an ordinary agricultural labourer\n[Note: Some gangmasters, however, have worked their way up to become tenants of 500 acres or\nowners of entire rows of houses.] , depends almost entirely upon the skill with which he knows how to extract\nas much labour as possible from his band in the shortest time. The\ntenants have discovered that females work properly only under male dicta-\ntorship, but that females and children, once set in motion, expend\ntheir vital force with true vehemence, as Fourier already knew, whereas\nthe adult male labourer is so crafty as to husband it as much as he can.\nThe gangmaster moves from one estate to another and thus employs\nhis band 6–8 months of the year.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"21.18","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0019","text":"He is therefore a much more profitable and dependable customer for the labouring fami-\nlies than the individual tenant, who employs the children only occasionally.\nThis circumstance strengthens his influence so greatly in the open local-\nities that in many of them children can only be had through his intercession. Their\nindividual pawning-out, outside the gang,\nto the tenants, forms his side business.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"21.19","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0020","text":"The “dark sides” of the system are the overwork of children\nand young persons, the enormous marches they must make daily to and from\nthe estates 5, 6, and sometimes 7 miles distant, and finally the demoralisation of\nthe “gang”. Although the gangmaster, who in\nsome districts is called “the driver”, is equipped with a long stick,\nhe nevertheless uses it only rarely, and complaint of\nbrutal treatment is exceptional. He is a democratic emperor\nor a kind of Pied Piper of Hamelin. He therefore requires popularity\namong his subjects and binds them to himself through the gypsyism\nflourishing under his auspices. Crude licence, merry exuberance,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"686\", \"facs\": \"#f0705\"} --> and the most obscene impudence lend wings to the gang.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"21.20","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0021","text":"For the most part\nthe gangmaster pays out in a public house and then returns, perhaps staggering,\nsupported on right and left by a sturdy woman, at the head\nof the procession, with the children and young persons frolicking behind, singing songs\nof mockery and ribaldry. On the way back, that which Fourier calls ‘phane-\nrogamy’ is the order of the day. The impregnation of thirteen- and\nfourteen-year-old girls by their male contemporaries is frequent.\nThe open villages which furnish the contingent of the gang become\nSodoms and Gomorrahs [Note: “Half the girls of Bidford have been ruined by the\ngang.” l. c. Appendix, p. 6, n. 32.] and furnish twice as many illegitimate births\nas the rest of the kingdom. What girls reared in this school accomplish\nin morality as married women has already been indicated above. Their\nchildren, insofar as they do not dispose of them through opium, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"21.21","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0022","text":"etc., are\nborn recruits of the gang.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"21.22","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0023","text":"The gang in its classical form just described is called\nthe public, common, or tramping gang (public, common or tramping\ngang). There are also private gangs (private gangs). They\nare composed like the common gang, but number fewer heads\nand work, instead of under the gangmaster, under an old farm-\nservant whom the tenant does not know how to employ better. The gypsy\nhumour disappears here, but according to all testimony the payment and\ntreatment of the children deteriorate.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"21.23","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0024","text":"The gang system, which has steadily ex-\npanded in recent years [Note: “The system has increased greatly in recent years. In some\nplaces it has only recently been introduced; in others, where it is older, more and\nyounger children are enrolled in the gang.” (l. c. p. 79, n. 174.)] , obviously does not exist for the sake of the gangmaster. It exists for\nthe enrichment of the large tenants [Note: “Small tenants do not employ gang labour.” “It is not em-\nployed on poor land, but on land which brings £2 to £2 10s.\nrent per acre.” (l. c. p. 17 and 14.)] , respectively landlords [Note: The rents of one of these gentlemen taste so good to him that he indignantly declares to the com-\nmission of inquiry that the whole outcry is due only to the name of the\nsystem.]}","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"21.24","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0025","text":"If, instead of “gang,” it were christened “youthful in-\ndustrial-agricultural-cooperative self-preservation association,” then all would be all\nright.] . For the tenant there is no more ingenious method of keeping his labouring personnel far below the\nnormal level and yet always having the extra hand at hand for all extra work,\nof extracting as much labour as possible with as little money as possible [Note: “Gang labour is cheaper than other labour; that is the reason why\nit is employed,” says a former gang-master. (l. c. p. 17, n. 11.)\n“The gang system is decidedly the cheapest for the tenant and just as de-\ncidedly the most ruinous for the children,” says a tenant. (l. c. p. 16, n. 4.)] and of making the adult male labourer\n“superfluous.”","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"21.25","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0026","text":"After the earlier exposition one understands\nhow, on the one hand, the greater or lesser unemployment of the countryman\nis admitted, while, on the other hand, the gang system is at the same time declared\n“necessary” because of the lack of male labour and its migration to the towns [Note: “Undoubtedly much work now performed by children in gangs\nwas formerly performed by men and women. Where women and children are em-\nployed, more men are now unemployed (“more men are out of work”)\nthan formerly.” (l. c. p. 43, n. 202.) On the other hand, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"21.26","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0027","text":"a.: “The labour question\nin many agricultural districts, especially the corn-produc-\ning ones, becomes so serious as a consequence of emigration and of the facility\nwhich the railways afford for removal to the great towns, that I (the\n‘I’ is that of the land-agent of a great lord) hold the services of children to be absolutely\nindispensable.” (l. c. p. 80, n. 180.) The Labour Question (the\nlabour question), namely, in the English agricultural districts, in contradistinction to\nthe rest of the civilised world, means the landlords’ and farmers’\nQuestion, namely, how, despite the ever-increasing withdrawal of country people,\na sufficient “relative overpopulation” in the countryside, and thereby\nthe “minimum of wages” for the agricultural labourer, is to be perpetuated?] . The field clean of weeds and the\nhuman weeds of Lincolnshire, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"21.27","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0028","text":"are pole and counter-pole of\ncapitalist production [Note: The “Public Health Report” previously cited by me, in which the gang system was dealt with incidentally on the occasion\nof child mortality, remained unknown to the press and hence to the English\npublic. On the other hand, the recent report of the “Child. Empl. Comm.” provided welcome “sensational”\npress fodder. While the liberal press asked how indeed the fine gentlemen\nand ladies and state-church prebendaries, with whom Lincolnshire swarms, could\nhave allowed such a system to grow up on their estates, under their eyes, person-\nages who send their own “missions for the moral improvement of South Sea savages” to the\nAntipodes, the finer press made exclusively reflections on\nthe crude depravity of the country people, who are capable of [doing this to] their children] .","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"21.28","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0029","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"688\", \"facs\": \"#f0707\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"21.29","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0030","text":"To conclude this section, we must still travel for a moment\nto Ireland. First, the facts that matter here.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"21.30","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0031","text":"Ireland’s population had in 1841 grown to 8,222,664 per-\nsons, had shrunk in 1851 to 6,623,982, in 1861 to\n5,850,309, in 1866 to 5½ million, approximately to its level of 1801.\nThe decline began with the famine year 1846, so that Ireland in\nless than 20 years lost more than \\frac{5}{16} of its population [Note: Population of Ireland: 1801: 5,319,867 persons, 1811:\n6,084,996, 1821: 6,869,544, 1831: 7,828,347, 1841: 8,222,664.] . Its\ntotal emigration from May 1851 to July 1865 numbered 1,591,487 per-\nsons, emigration during the last 5 years, 1861—1865, more\nthan half a million. The number of inhabited houses diminished\nfrom 1851—1861 by 52,990.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"21.31","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0032","text":"From 1851—1861 the number\nof tenancies of 15—30 acres increased by 61,000, that of tenancies over\n30 acres by 109,000, while the total number of all ten-\nancies diminished by 120,000, a decline therefore owing exclusively to\nthe destruction of tenancies under 15 acres, alias their concen-\ntration.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"21.32","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0033","text":"[Note: selling such slavery! Under the accursed circumstances in which “the\nDelicaters” have enchained the countryman, it would be understandable if he\nate his own children. What is truly wonderful is the soundness of character that he has\nfor the most part preserved. The official reporters prove that the\nparents themselves, even in the gang districts, abhor the gang system. “One finds\nabundant proof in the evidence collected by us that the parents\nwould in many cases be grateful for a compulsory law which would en-\nable them to withstand the temptations and pressure to which they are often\nsubjected.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"21.33","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0034","text":"Now the parish official, now the employer drives them,\nunder threat of their own dismissal, to send the children out to earn, instead of sending them\nto school … All time and strength laid waste, all suffering which extra-\nordinary and useless exhaustion produces for the countryman and his family, every case\nin which the parents trace the moral ruin of their child to the over-\ncrowding of the cottages or the defiling influences of the gang system,\nstirs up in the breast of the labouring poor feelings which one will readily understand,\nand which it is unnecessary to detail. They have a consciousness that much\nphysical and mental torment is inflicted upon them by circumstances\nfor which they are in no way responsible, to which, had it been in their power,\nthey would never have given their assent, and against which to struggle they are power-\nless.” (l.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"21.34","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0035","text":"c. p. XX, n. 82 and XXIII, n. 96.)]","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"21.35","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0036","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"689\", \"facs\": \"#f0708\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"21.36","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0037","text":"The decline in the population was naturally, on the whole,\naccompanied by a decline in the mass of products. For our purpose\nit suffices to consider the 5 years 1861—1865, during which over\n½ million emigrated and the absolute population fell by more than ⅓\nmillion.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"21.37","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0038","text":"Table A.\nLivestock.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"21.38","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0039","text":"|\n\nIt follows from the preceding table:","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"21.39","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0040","text":"|\n\nLet us now turn to agriculture, which supplies the means of subsistence for livestock\nand man. In the following table, decrease or increase for the\nyear 1861 is calculated in relation to the year 1860, in short, for each individual year\n[Running matter: I. 44]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"690\", \"facs\": \"#f0709\"} --> in relation to the immediately preceding one. The cereal crop em-\nbraces wheat, oats, barley, bere, rye, beans and peas; the\ngreen crop, potatoes, turnips, mangel and mangel-wurzel, cabbage, yellow\ncarrots, parsnips, vetches, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"21.40","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0041","text":"Table B.\nIncrease or decrease of the acreage of land used for tillage and as meadow (respectively pasture).","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"21.41","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0042","text":"|\n\nIn the year 1865, 127,470 acres were added under the heading “grassland,”\nprincipally because the area under the heading “unused\nwaste land and bog (marshland)” decreased by 101,543 acres. If we com-\npare 1865 with 1864, there was a decrease in cereal crops of\n246,667 qrs., of which 48,999 wheat, 166,605 oats, 29,892 barley,\netc.; a decrease in potato yield, although the area\nunder their cultivation grew in 1865, of 446,398 tons, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"21.42","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0043","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"691\", \"facs\": \"#f0710\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"21.43","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0044","text":"Table C.\nIncrease or decrease in the area of cultivated land, the product per acre, and the total product,\n1865 compared with 1864.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"21.44","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0045","text":"|\n\n [Running matter: 44*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"692\", \"facs\": \"#f0711\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"21.45","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0046","text":"From the movement of Ireland’s population and soil production\nwe pass to the movement in the purses of its landlords,\nlarger tenants, and industrial capitalists. It is reflected in the\nrise and fall of the annual incomes subject to income tax. For an understanding of the following table, we note that ru-\nbric D (profits excluding tenants’ profits) also includes so-called “profes-\nsional” profits, i.e. the incomes of barristers, physicians,\netc., while the rubrics C and E not specially enumerated include the\nreceipts of officials, officers, state sinecurists, state credi-\ntors, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"21.46","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0047","text":"Table D.\nIncomes subject to income tax in £ st.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"21.47","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0048","text":"|\n\nUnder rubric D the increase in income in the annual\naverage from 1853—1864 amounted only to 0.93, whereas in the same pe-\nriod in Great Britain it amounted to 4.58. The following table shows the\ndistribution of profits (excluding tenants’ profits)\nfor the years 1864 and 1865:\nSee Table E alongside.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"21.48","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0049","text":"England, a country of developed capitalist production and pre-\neminently industrial, would have bled to death from a bloodletting of the\npeople, like the Irish one. But Ireland is at present only an agricultural\ndistrict of England, fenced off by a broad water-ditch, which supplies it\nwith grain, wool, cattle, industrial and military recruits.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"21.49","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0050","text":"Depopulation has thrown much land out of cultivation, has\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"693\", \"facs\": \"#f0712\"} -->Table E.\n Heading D. Income from profits (over £60) in Ireland.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"21.50","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0051","text":"|\n\n greatly diminished the product of the soil [Note: Even if the product also diminishes proportionately per acre,\nlet it not be forgotten that for 1½ centuries England has exported the soil of Ireland,\nwithout granting its cultivators even the means of replacing the\nconstituents of the soil.] , and, even despite the extended area\nof cattle-breeding, has produced in some of its branches an absolute decline, in oth-\ners progress scarcely worth mentioning and interrupted by continual retrogressions.\nNevertheless, ground-rents and farming profits, although\nthe latter not so constantly as the former, rose continually with the decline\nof the mass of the people. The reason is easily understood. On the one\nhand, with the amalgamation of farms and the conversion of\narable land into cattle pasture, a greater part of the total product\nwas transformed into surplus-product.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"21.51","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0052","text":"The absolute mass of the surplus-product grew, al-\nthough the total product, of which it forms a fraction, diminished\nabsolutely. On the other hand, the money-value of this surplus-product rose still more rapidly\nthan its mass, as a consequence of the rising English market prices for meat,\nwool, etc., over the last 20 years and quite particularly over\nthe last 10 years.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"21.52","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0053","text":"Fragmented means of production, which serve the producers themselves as\nmeans of employment and subsistence, without valorizing themselves through the incorporation\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"694\", \"facs\": \"#f0713\"} --> of alien labour, are no more capital than the product\nconsumed by its own producer is a commodity. If, therefore,\nwith the mass of the people the mass of means of production employed in agriculture\ndiminished, the mass of capital employed in it\nincreased, because a part of those previously fragmented\nmeans of production was transformed into capital.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"21.53","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0054","text":"The total capital of Ireland invested outside agriculture, in industry and commerce,\naccumulated slowly during the last two\ndecades and amid constant great fluctuation. On the other hand, the concentration of its individual constituent\nparts developed all the more rapidly. Finally, however slowly capital grew absolutely,\nrelatively, in proportion to the dwindled number of the people, it had\nswollen extraordinarily.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"21.54","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0055","text":"Here, then, before our eyes, on a large scale,\na process unfolds such as orthodox economics could not have wished more beautifully\nfor the vindication of its dogma, which explains misery by absolute over-\npopulation and re-establishes equilibrium by depopulation. This\nis an experiment of quite other importance than the plague in the middle of the fourteenth cen-\ntury, so greatly glorified by the Malthusians. Incidentally. It was in itself schoolmasterishly naive to apply to the conditions of production and corresponding population of the 19th cen-\ntury the standard of the 14th","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"21.55","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0056","text":"century; moreover, this\nnaivety overlooked that while that plague and the decimation accompanying it,\non this side of the Channel, in England, were followed hard upon by the liberation and enrichment\nof the rural people, on the other side, in France, they were followed by greater enslavement and increased\nmisery.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"21.56","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0057","text":"The famine killed more than a million human beings in Ireland in 1846,\nbut only poor devils, without the slightest diminution of the wealth of the coun-\ntry. The ensuing twenty-year and ever-swelling\nexodus did not decimate, as the Thirty Years' War did, the means of production\nalong with the people. Irish genius invented an entirely\nnew method of conjuring away a poor people thousands of miles from the scene of its\nmisery. The emigrants who have settled in the United States\nsend home sums of money every year, the travelling means\nof those left behind. Each troop that emigrates this year draws\nanother troop after it next year. Instead of costing Ireland,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"695\", \"facs\": \"#f0714\"} --> emigration thus forms one of the most profitable branches of its export\nbusiness.","kind":"passage","sequence":57,"address":"21.57","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0058","text":"Finally, this is a systematic process which does not merely bore\na temporary hole in the mass of the people, but annually pumps more\npeople out of it than natural increase replaces, so that the ab-\nsolute level of population sinks from year to year.","kind":"passage","sequence":58,"address":"21.58","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0059","text":"What were the consequences for the Irish working population left behind, freed from the “sur-\nplus population”? That relative overpopulation is today as great as before 1846, that wages\nstand just as low and the drudgery of labour has increased,\nthat misery in the countryside is again pressing towards a new crisis. The\ncauses are simple. The revolution in agriculture kept pace with emi-\ngration. The production of relative overpopulation more than\nkept pace with absolute depopulation. A glance at Table C\nshows how the conversion of tillage into cattle pasture in Ireland must operate still\nmore acutely than in England. Here, with cattle-breeding, the cultivation of green crops\nincreases; there it decreases.","kind":"passage","sequence":59,"address":"21.59","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0060","text":"While great masses of formerly\ncultivated fields are laid waste or transformed into permanent grassland,\na great part of the previously unused waste land and\nbogs is used for the extension of cattle-breeding. The smaller and middle-\nsized farmers — I include all those who cultivate no more than 100 acres\n— still occupy approximately \\frac{8}{10} of Irish soil. They are pro-\ngressively crushed, to a quite different degree than before, by the competition of\ncapitalistically conducted agriculture and therefore continually supply new\nrecruits to the working class proper. The only great in-\ndustry of Ireland, linen manufacture, requires relatively few adult male\nworkers and, despite its expansion since the dearness of cotton, employs altogether only a relatively in-\nsignificant part of the mass of workers.","kind":"passage","sequence":60,"address":"21.60","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0061","text":"Like every other great in-\ndustry, through the constant fluctuations in its own\nsphere it continually produces a relative overpopulation, even with absolute growth\nin the number of workers it absorbs. The misery of the rural people\nforms the pedestal of gigantic shirt factories, etc., whose army of workers\nis for the most part scattered over the flat countryside. We find\nhere again the previously described system of domestic labour, which\npossesses in underpayment and overwork its methodical means of “making superflu-\nous.” Finally, although depopulation does not have consequences so de-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"696\", \"facs\": \"#f0715\"} --> structive as in a country of developed capitalist pro-\nduction, it does not proceed without constant repercussion. Emi-\ngration leaves behind not only empty houses, but also ruined house-\nlandlords.","kind":"passage","sequence":61,"address":"21.61","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0062","text":"Small as the consumption of each of their individual components,\ntheir total disappearance produces a constant gap in the internal market, which\nparticularly strikes small shopkeepers, artisans, and small tradespeople in general.\nEach new exodus hurls a part of the small middle class into the\nproletariat. See Table E for the decrease of incomes below £100.","kind":"passage","sequence":62,"address":"21.62","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0063","text":"The weekly wage of the agricultural labourer in the vicinity of Dublin —\nthe maximum wage of the Irish agricultural labourer — stands at this moment,\nwith a high price of basic foodstuffs, at 7 sh. From this\none may infer its level in the purely agricultural and remote districts.\nTo describe the condition even of the skilled Irish\nindustrial worker, one example suffices.","kind":"passage","sequence":63,"address":"21.63","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0064","text":"“During my recent inspection of the North of Ireland,” says the\nEnglish factory inspector Robert Baker, “I was struck by the\nendeavour of a skilled Irish worker to procure education for\nhis children out of the most meagre means. I give his statement ver-\nbatim, as I received it from his mouth. That he is a skilled\nfactory hand is known when I say that he was employed on articles for the Man-\nchester market. Johnson: I am a beetler, and work from\n6 o’clock in the morning until 11 at night, from Monday to Friday; on Saturdays\nwe finish at 6 o’clock in the evening and have 3 hours for meals and recreation.\nI have 5 children. For this work I receive 10 sh. 6 d. weekly;\nmy wife also works and earns 5 sh. a week. The eldest\ngirl, twelve years old, keeps house. She is our cook and only helper.","kind":"passage","sequence":64,"address":"21.64","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0065","text":"She gets the younger ones ready for school. My wife rises\nwith me and leaves with me. A girl passing our house\nwakes me at half past 5 in the morning. We eat nothing before we go\nto work. The twelve-year-old child looks after the little ones throughout the day.\nWe breakfast at 8 and go home for it. We have tea once\na week; otherwise we have porridge (stirabout), sometimes of oat-\nmeal, sometimes of Indian meal, according as we are able to procure it.\nIn winter we have a little sugar and water with our Indian\nmeal. In summer we harvest a few potatoes, with which we ourselves plant a little patch of\nland, and when they are finished, we return to porridge.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"697\", \"facs\": \"#f0716\"} --> Thus it goes day in, day out, Sunday and weekday, throughout the\nwhole year.","kind":"passage","sequence":65,"address":"21.65","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0066","text":"I am always very tired in the evening after the day's\nwork is done. We see a morsel of meat exceptionally, but very rarely.\nThree of our children attend school, for which we pay 1 d. per head weekly.\nOur house rent is 9 d. a week, peat for fuel costs\nat least 1 sh. 6 d. per fortnight” [Note: “Reports of Insp. of Fact. for 31 Oct. 1866.” Lond. 1867,\np. 96.] . These are Irish wages, this\nis Irish life!","kind":"passage","sequence":66,"address":"21.66","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0067","text":"In fact, the misery of Ireland is once again a daily topic in England.\nAt the end of 1866 and beginning of 1867 one of the\nIrish land magnates, Lord Dufferin, set himself in the Times to solve it. “How humane of such a great\nlord!”","kind":"passage","sequence":67,"address":"21.67","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0068","text":"From Table E one saw that, while in 1864, of £4,368,610\nof total profit, 3 profit-makers pocketed only £262,610, the same 3 virtuosi\nof “abstinence” pocketed in 1865, by contrast, out of £4,669,979\nof total profit, £274,448; in 1864: 26 profit-makers £646,377;\n1865: 28 profit-makers £736,448; in 1864: 121 profit-makers\n£1,066,912; 1865: 186 profit-makers £1,320,996; in 1864:\n1,131 profit-makers £2,150,818, nearly half of annual\ntotal profit; in 1865: 1,194 profit-makers £2,418,933, more than\nhalf of annual total profit.","kind":"passage","sequence":68,"address":"21.68","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0069","text":"But the lion’s share which\na vanishingly small number of land magnates in England, Scotland\nand Ireland devours from the annual national rental income is so monstrous\nthat English statesmanship finds it appropriate not to furnish for the distribution\nof ground-rent the same statistical material as for the distribution of profit. Lord Dufferin is one of these land mag-\nnates. That rent rolls and profits could ever be “superfluous,” or\nthat their plethora is in any way connected with the plethora of the people’s misery,\nis naturally an idea just as “disrespectable” as it is “unsound.”\nHe adheres to facts. The fact is that as the Irish population diminishes,\nthe Irish rent rolls swell, that depopulation “benefits” the landowner,\nhence also the land, hence also the people, who are merely an accessory of the soil.","kind":"passage","sequence":69,"address":"21.69","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0070","text":"He therefore declares that\nIreland is still overpopulated and that the stream of emigration flows\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"698\", \"facs\": \"#f0717\"} --> still too sluggishly. To be completely happy, Ireland would have to let go at\nleast another ⅓ million working people. Let no one imagine that this additionally poetic\nlord is a physician from Sangrado’s school,\nwho, whenever he found his patient no better, prescribed bloodletting, fresh\nbloodletting, until the patient lost his illness along with his blood.\nLord Dufferin demands a fresh bloodletting of only ⅓ million, instead of approximately 2 million,\nwithout whose removal the millennium in\nErin cannot in fact be brought about. The proof is easily furnished.","kind":"passage","sequence":70,"address":"21.70","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0071","text":"Number and extent of farms in Ireland, 1864.","kind":"passage","sequence":71,"address":"21.71","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0072","text":"|\n\nFrom 1851 to 1861 concentration has chiefly destroyed farms\nof the first 3 categories, under 1 and not over 15 acres. They\nmust disappear above all. This yields 307,058 “superfluous”\nfarmers, and, reckoning the family at the low average of 4 heads,\n1,228,232 persons. Under the extravagant assumption\nthat ¼ of these can again be absorbed after the completion of the agricultural revolution,\nthere remain to emigrate: 921,174 persons. The categories 4, 5, 6,\nof over 15 and not over 100 acres are, as has long been known in England,\ntoo small for capitalist grain cultivation, but for sheep-breeding they are almost\nvanishing magnitudes. Under the same assumptions as before,\na further 788,761 persons must therefore emigrate, total: 1,709,532.","kind":"passage","sequence":72,"address":"21.72","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0073","text":"And, comme l’appétit vient en mangeant, the eyes of the rent-roll\n will soon discover that Ireland, with 3½ million, is still wretched, and\n wretched because overpopulated, and that therefore its depopulation must\n proceed much further, in order that it may fulfil its true vocation, that of an English\n sheep-run and cattle-pasture.","kind":"passage","sequence":73,"address":"21.73","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0074","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"699\", \"facs\": \"#f0718\"} -->","kind":"passage","sequence":74,"address":"21.74","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0075","text":"This profitable method, like all good things in this world, has its\n drawback. The accumulation of ground-rent in Ireland keeps pace with\n the accumulation of Irishmen in America. The Irishman displaced by sheep and ox\n rises again on the other side of the ocean as a Fenian. And\n opposite the old queen of the seas, the young giant republic rises, menacingly and ever more menacingly.","kind":"passage","sequence":75,"address":"21.75","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0076","text":"Bitter fates drive the Romans on,\n And the crime of fraternal slaughter.","kind":"passage","sequence":76,"address":"21.76","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_CD_2:P0076"]}],"source_digest":"ee5ab416035f7b3f2d47b65d6c9630abac92be0798e3086dc2c61d47fdea0d0a","source_language_code":"de"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG","title":"2) Die s. g. Ursprüngliche Accumulation.","order":22,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_2DSG","text":"Man hat gesehn, wie Geld in Kapital verwandelt, mit dem Kapital\n Mehrwerth und aus dem Mehrwerth mehr Kapital gemacht wird. Indess\n setzt die Accumulation des Kapitals den Mehrwerth, der Mehrwerth die\n kapitalistische Produktion, diese aber das Vorhandensein grösserer Kapi-\n talmassen in den Händen von Waarenproducenten voraus. Der ganze Pro-\n zess scheint also eine der kapitalistischen Accumulation vor-\n ausgehende „ursprüngliche“ Accumulation („previous accumu-\n lation“ bei Adam Smith) zu unterstellen, eine Accumulation, welche\n nicht das Resultat der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise ist, sondern\n ihr Ausgangspunkt.\n\n\n\n\nDiese ursprüngliche Accumulation spielt in der politischen\n Oekonomie ungefähr dieselbe Rolle wie der Sündenfall in der Theologie.\n Adam biss in den Apfel und damit kam über das Menschengeschlecht die\n Sünde. Ihr Ursprung wird erklärt, indem er als Anekdote der Ver-\n gangenheit erzählt wird. In einer längst verflossenen Zeit gab es auf der\n einen Seite eine fleissige Elite und auf der andern faulenzende Lumpen.\n So kam es, dass die ersten Reichthum accumulirten und die letz-\n teren schliesslich nichts zu verkaufen hatten als ihre eigne Haut. Und von\n diesem Sündenfall datirt die Armuth der grossen Masse, die immer noch,\n aller Arbeit zum Trotz, nichts zu verkaufen hat als sich selbst, und der\n Reichthum der Wenigen, der fortwährend wächst, obgleich sie längst\n aufgehört haben zu arbeiten. Solche fade Kinderei kaut Herr Thiers\n z. B. noch mit staatsfeierlichem Ernst, zur Vertheidigung der propriété,\n den einst so geistreichen Franzosen vor. Aber sobald die Eigenthums-\n frage ins Spiel kommt, wird es heilige Pflicht, den Standpunkt der Kinder-\n fibel als den allen Altersklassen und Entwicklungsstufen allein gerechten\n festzuhalten. In der wirklichen Geschichte spielen bekanntlich Erobe-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"700\", \"facs\": \"#f0719\"} --> rung, Unterjochung, Raubmord, kurz Gewalt die grosse Rolle. In der\n sanften politischen Oekonomie herrschte von jeher die Idylle. Recht und\n „Arbeit“ waren von jeher die einzigen Bereicherungsmittel, natürlich mit\n jedesmaliger Ausnahme von „diesem Jahr“. In der That sind\n die Methoden der ursprünglichen Accumulation alles andre, nur nicht\n idyllisch.\n\n\n\n\nGeld und Waare sind nicht von vornherein Kapital, so wenig wie\n Produktions- und Lebensmittel. Sie bedürfen der Verwandlung in\n Kapital. Dieser Verwandlungsprozess selbst kann aber nur unter be-\n stimmten Umständen vorgehn. Sie spitzen sich dahin zusammen: Zweier-\n lei sehr verschiedne Sorten von Waarenbesitzern müssen sich gegenüber und\n in Kontakt treten, einerseits Eigner von Geld, Produktions- und Le-\n bensmitteln, welche die von ihnen geeignete Werthsumme verwerthen\n wollen durch Ankauf fremder Arbeitskraft; andrerseits freie Arbeiter,\n Verkäufer der eignen Arbeitskraft und daher Verkäufer von Arbeit.\nFreie Arbeiter in dem Doppelsinn, dass sie weder selbst unmit-\n telbar zu den Produktionsbedingungen gehören, wie Sklaven, Leibeigne\n u. s. w., noch auch die Produktionsbedingungen ihnen gehören, wie beim\n selbstwirthschaftenden Bauer u. s. w., sie davon vielmehr frei, los und le-\n dig sind. Mit dieser Polarisation des Waarenmarkts sind die\n Grundbedingungen der kapitalistischen Produktion gegeben. Das Kapi-\n talverhältniss setzt die Scheidung zwischen den Arbeitern und\ndem Eigenthum an den Verwirklichungsbedingungen der\n Arbeit voraus. Sobald die kapitalistische Produktion einmal auf eignen\n Füssen steht, erhält sie nicht nur jene Scheidung, sondern reproducirt sie\n auf stets wachsender Stufenleiter. Der Prozess, der das Kapitalverhält-\n niss schafft, kann also nichts anders sein als der Scheidungsprozess\n des Arbeiters von den Arbeitsbedingungen, ein Prozess, der\n einerseits die gesellschaftlichen Lebens- und Produktionsmittel in Kapi-\n tal verwandelt, andrerseits die unmittelbaren Producenten in Lohnar-\n beiter. Die s. g. ursprüngliche Accumulation ist also nichts\n als der historische Scheidungsprozess von Producent und\n Produktionsmittel. Er erscheint als „ursprünglich“, weil er die\nVorgeschichte des Kapitals und der ihm entsprechenden Produk-\n tionsweise bildet.\n\n\n\n\nMan sieht auf den ersten Blick, dass dieser Scheidungsprozess eine\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"701\", \"facs\": \"#f0720\"} --> ganze Reihe historischer Prozesse einschliesst, und eine doppelseitige Reihe,\n einerseits Auflösung der Verhältnisse, die den Arbeiter selbst zum Eigen\n thum dritter Personen machten und zu einem selbst angeeigneten Produk-\n tionsmittel, andrerseits Auflösung des Eigenthums der unmittelbaren Pro-\n ducenten an ihren Produktionsmitteln. Der Scheidungsprozess umfasst\n also in der That die ganze Entwicklungsgeschichte der modernen bürger-\n lichen Gesellschaft, eine Geschichte, welche gar keine Schwierigkeiten\n böte, hätten die bürgerlichen Geschichtschreiber die Auflösung der feuda-\n len Produktionsweise nicht ausschliesslich unter dem clair obscur der Eman-\n cipation des Arbeiters dargestellt, statt zugleich als Verwandlung der\n feudalen in die kapitalistische Exploitationsweise. Der\n Ausgangspunkt der Entwicklung war die Knechtschaft des Arbei-\n ters. Ihr Fortgang bestand in einem Formwechsel dieser\n Knechtung. Jedoch erheischt unser Zweck keineswegs Analyse der\n mittelaltrigen Bewegung. Obgleich die kapitalistische Produktion schon\n im 14. und 15. Jahrhundert sporadisch ihren Sitz in den Ländern am\n Mittelmeer aufschlug, datirt die kapitalistische Aera erst vom\n16. Jahrhundert. Dort, wo sie aufblüht, ist die Aufhebung der Leib-\n eigenschaft längst vollbracht und das mittelaltrige Städtewesen bereits\n in das Stadium seines Verfalls getreten.\n\n\n\n\nHistorisch epochemachend in der Geschichte des Scheidungsprozesses\n sind die Momente, worin grosse Menschenmassen plötzlich und gewaltsam von\n ihren Subsistenz- und Produktionsmitteln geschieden und als vogelfreie\n Proletarier auf den Arbeitsmarkt geschleudert werden. Die Expropri-\n ation der Arbeiter von Grund und Boden bildet die Grund-\n lage des ganzen Prozesses. Wir haben sie also zuerst zu betrachten. Ihre\n Geschichte nimmt in verschiednen Ländern verschiedne Färbung an und\n durchläuft die verschiedenen Phasen in verschiedner Reihenfolge. Nur in\n England, das wir daher als Beispiel nehmen, besitzt sie klassische\n Form [Note: In Italien, wo die kapitalistische Produktion sich am frühsten entwickelt,\n findet auch die Auflösung der Leibeigenschaftsverhältnisse am frühsten statt. Der\n Leibeigene wird hier emancipirt, bevor er irgend ein Recht der Verjährung an\n Grund und Boden gesichert hat. Seine Emancipation verwandelt ihn also sofort\n in einen vogelfreien Proletarier, der überdem in den meist schon aus der Römer-\n zeit überlieferten Städten die neuen Herren fertig vorfindet.] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"702\", \"facs\": \"#f0721\"} -->\n\nIn England war die Leibeigenschaft im letzten Theil des 14. Jahr-\n hunderts faktisch verschwunden. Die ungeheure Mehrzahl der Bevölke-\n rung [Note: Noch Ende des 17. Jahrhunderts waren mehr als ⅘ der englischen Ge-\n sammtbevölkerung agrikol. (Macaulay: „The History of England.“\nLond. 1854, v. I, p. 413.) Ich citire Macaulay, weil er als systematischer Ge-\n schichtsfälscher derartige Thatsachen möglichst „beschneidet“.]  bestand damals und noch mehr im 15. Jahrhundert aus freien, selbst-\n wirthschaftenden Bauern, durch welch feudales Aushängeschild ihr Eigen-\n thum immer versteckt sein mochte. Auf den grösseren herrschaftlichen\n Gütern war der früher selbst leibeigne bailiff (Vogt) durch den freien\n Pächter verdrängt. Die Lohnarbeiter der Agrikultur bestanden theils aus\n Bauern, die ihre Mussezeit durch Arbeit bei grossen Grundeigenthümern\n verwertheten, theils aus einer selbstständigen, relativ und absolut wenig\n zahlreichen Klasse eigentlicher Lohnarbeiter. Auch letztre waren faktisch\n zugleich selbstwirthschaftende Bauern, indem sie ausser ihrem Lohn Acker-\n land zum Belauf von 4 und mehr Acres nebst Cottages angewiesen er-\n hielten. Sie genossen zudem mit den eigentlichen Bauern die Nutznies-\n sung des Gemeindelandes, worauf ihr Vieh weidete und das ihnen\n zugleich die Mittel der Feuerung, Holz, Torf u. s. w. bot [Note: Man muss nie vergessen, dass selbst der Leibeigene nicht nur Eigen-\n thümer, wenn auch tributpflichtiger Eigenthümer, der zu seinem Haus gehörigen\n Bodenparcellen war, sondern auch Miteigenthümer des Gemeinde-\n landes. „Le paysan y (en Silésie) est serf.“ Nichtsdestoweniger besitzen\n diese serfs Gemeindegüter. „On n’a pas pu encore engager les Silésiens au\n partage des communes, tandis que dans la nouvelle Marche, il n’y a guère de\n village où ce partage ne soit exécuté avec le plus grand succès.“ (Mirabeau:\n „De la Monarchie Prussienne. Londres 1788“, t. II, p. 125, 126.)] . In allen\n Ländern Europa’s ist die feudale Produktion durch Theilung des Bodens\n unter möglichst viele Untersassen charakterisirt. Die Macht des Feudal-\n herrn, wie die jeden Souverains, beruhte nicht auf der Länge seiner Rent-\n rolle, sondern auf der Zahl seiner Unterthanen, und letztere hing von der\n Zahl selbstwirthschaftender Bauern ab [Note: Japan, mit seiner rein feudalen Organisation des Grundeigenthums und\n seiner entwickelten Kleinbauernwirthschaft, liefert ein viel treueres Bild des euro-\n päischen Mittelalters als unsre sämmtlichen, meist von bürgerlichen Vorurtheilen\n diktirten Geschichtsbücher. Es ist gar zu bequem, „liberal“ auf Kosten des\n Mittelalters zu sein.] . Obgleich der englische Boden\n daher nach der normännischen Eroberung in riesenhafte Baronien vertheilt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"703\", \"facs\": \"#f0722\"} --> ward, wovon eine einzige oft 900 alte angelsächsische Lordschaften ein-\n schloss, war er besät von kleinen Bauernwirthschaften, nur hier und da\n durchbrochen von grösseren herrschaftlichen Gütern. Solche Verhältnisse,\n bei gleichzeitiger Blüthe des Städtewesens, wie sie das 15. Jahrhundert\n auszeichnet, erlaubten jenen Volksreichthum, den der Staatskanzler\nFortescue so beredt in seinen „Laudibus Legum Angliae“\n schildert, aber sie schlossen den Kapitalreichthum aus.\n\n\n\n\nDas Vorspiel der Umwälzung, welche die Grundlage der kapitalisti-\n schen Produktionsweise schuf, ereignet sich im letzten Drittheil des 15.\n und den ersten Decennien des 16. Jahrhunderts. Eine Masse vogel-\n freier Proletarier ward auf den Arbeitsmarkt geschleudert durch\n die Auflösung der feudalen Gefolgschaften, die, wie Sir James\n Steuart richtig bemerkt, „überall nutzlos Haus und Hof füllten.“ Obgleich\n die königliche Macht, selbst ein Produkt der bürgerlichen Entwicklung, in\n ihrem Streben nach absoluter Souverainität die Auflösung dieser Gefolg-\n schaften gewaltsam beschleunigte, war sie keineswegs deren einzige Ursache.\n Vielmehr im trotzigsten Gegensatz zu Königthum und Parlament, schuf der\n grosse Feudalherr ein ungleich grösseres Proletariat durch ge-\n waltsame Verjagung der Bauernschaft von dem Grund und Boden, worauf\n sie denselben feudalen Rechtstitel besass wie er selbst, und durch Usurpa-\n tion ihres Gemeindelandes. Den unmittelbaren Anstoss dazu gab in Eng-\n land namentlich das Aufblühn der flandrischen Wollmanufaktur, und\n das entsprechende Steigen der Wollpreise. Den alten Feudaladel hatten\n die grossen Feudalkriege verschlungen, der neue war ein Kind seiner Zeit,\n für welche Geld die Macht aller Mächte. Verwandlung von Ackerland in\n Schafweide ward also sein Losungswort. Harrison, in seiner „De-\n scription of England. Prefixed to Holinshed’s Chronicles“,\n beschreibt, wie die Expropriation der kleinen Bauern das Land ruinirt.\n „What care our great incroachers!“ (Was fragen unsre grossen Usurpa-\n toren danach?) Die Wohnungen der Bauern und die Cottages der Arbeiter\n wurden gewaltsam niedergerissen oder dem Verfall geweiht. „Wenn\n man“, sagt Harrison, „die älteren Inventarien jeden Ritterguts vergleichen\n will, so wird man finden, dass unzählige Häuser und kleine Bauernwirth-\n schaften verschwunden sind, dass das Land viel weniger Leute nährt, dass\n viele Städte verfallen sind, obgleich einige neue aufblühn . . . . Von Städten\n und Dörfern, die man für Schaftriften zerstört hat, und worin nur noch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"704\", \"facs\": \"#f0723\"} --> die Herrschaftshäuser stehn, könnte ich etwas erzählen.“ Die Klagen\n jener alten Chroniken sind immer übertrieben, aber sie zeichnen genau den\n Eindruck der Revolution in den Produktionsverhältnissen auf die Zeitge-\n nossen selbst. Ein Vergleich zwischen den Schriften der Schatzkanzler For-\n tescue und Thomas Morus veranschaulicht die Kluft zwischen dem\n 15. und 16. Jahrhundert. Aus ihrem goldnen Zeitalter, wie Thornton\n richtig sagt, stürzte die englische Arbeiterklasse ohne alle Zwischenüber-\n gänge in das eiserne.\n\n\n\n\nDie Gesetzgebung erschrak vor dieser Umwälzung. Sie stand noch\n nicht auf der Civilisationshöhe, wo „Wealth of the Nation“,\n d. h. Kapitalbildung und rücksichtslose Exploitation und Verarmung der\n Volksmasse als ultima Thule aller Staatsweisheit gelten. In seiner „Ge-\n schichte Heinrich’s VII.“ sagt Baco: „Um diese Zeit (1489)\n mehrten sich die Klagen über Verwandlung von Ackerland in Weide (zur\n Schaftrift u. s. w.), leicht zu versehn durch wenige Hände. Diess brachte\n einen Verfall des Volks hervor … König und Parlament ergriffen Mass-\n regeln wider diese entvölkernde Usurpation der Gemeindeländereien (depo-\n pulating inclosures) und die ihr auf dem Fuss folgende entvölkernde Weide-\n wirthschaft (depopulating pasture).“ Ein Akt Heinrich des VII., 1489,\n c. 19, verbot die Zerstörung aller Bauernhäuser, wozu wenigstens 20\n Acres Land gehörten. In einem Akt 25, Heinrich VIII., wird dasselbe\n Gesetz erneuert. Es heisst darin u. a., dass „viele Pachten und grosse\n Viehmassen, besonders Schafe, sich in wenigen Händen aufhäufen, wo-\n durch die Grundrenten sehr gewachsen und der Ackerbau (tillage) sehr\n verfallen, Kirchen und Häuser niedergerissen, wunderbare Volksmassen\n verunfähigt seien, sich selbst und Familien zu erhalten.“ Das Gesetz\n verordnet daher den Wiederbau der verfallnen Pachthäuser, bestimmt den\n Kornbau im Verhältniss zur Schafweide u. s. w. Ein Akt von 1533\n klagt, dass manche Eigenthümer 24,000 Schafe besitzen und beschränkt\n deren Zahl auf 2000. Die Volksklage [Note: Sieh u. a.: „Examination of certayne complaints. Lon-\n don 1581.“]  und die seit Heinrich dem VII.\n an 150 Jahre fortdauernde Gesetzgebung wider die Expropriation der\n kleinen Pächter und Bauern waren gleich fruchtlos. Das Geheimniss ihrer\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"705\", \"facs\": \"#f0724\"} --> Erfolglosigkeit verräth uns Baco wider Wissen. „Der Akt Hein-\n rich’s VII.,“ sagt er in seinen „Essays, civil and moral“ Sect. 20,\n „war tief und bewunderungswürdig, indem er Landwirthschaften und\n Ackerbauhäuser von bestimmtem Normalmass schuf, d. h. eine Proportion\n von Land für sie erhielt, die sie befähigte Unterthanen von genügendem\n Reichthum und ohne servile Lage auf die Welt zu setzen und den Pflug\n in der Hand von Eigenthümern, nicht von Miethlingen zu\n halten“ („to keep the plough in the hand of the owners and not hire-\n lings“). Was die kapitalistische Produktion erheischte, war umge-\n kehrt servile Lage der Volksmasse, ihre eigne Verwandlung in Miethlinge, und\n Verwandlung ihrer Arbeitsmittel in Kapital. Jene ältere Gesetzgebung\n sucht auch die 4 Acres Land bei der Cottage des ländlichen Lohnar-\n beiters zu erhalten, wie sie ihm die Aufnahme von Miethsleuten in seine\n Cottage verbot. Noch 1627, unter Jakob I., wurde Roger Crocker von\n Frontmill verurtheilt wegen Bau’s einer Cottage im Manor von Frontmill\n ohne 4 Acres Land als beständiges Annex an dieselbe; noch 1638, unter\n Karl I., wurde eine königliche Kommission ernannt, um die alten Gesetze,\n namentlich auch über die 4 Acres Land, zu erzwingen; noch Cromwell ver-\n bot Erbauung eines Hauses in 4 Meilen weitem Umkreis von London ohne Aus-\n stattung desselben mit 4 Acres Land. Noch in der ersten Hälfte des 18.\n Jahrhunderts wird geklagt, wenn die Cottage des Landarbeiters kein Zu-\n behör von 1 bis 2 Acres hat. Heutzutag ist er glücklich, wenn sie mit\n einem Gärtchen ausgestattet ist, oder wenn er weitab von ihr ein Paar\n Ruthen Land miethen kann. „Landlords und Pächter“, sagt Dr. Hunter,\n „handeln hier Hand in Hand. Wenige Acres zur Cottage würden den Ar-\n beiter zu unabhängig machen“ [Note: Dr. Hunter l. c. p. 134: „The quantity of land assigned (in den\n alten Gesetzen) would now be judged too great for labourers, and rather as likely\n to convert them into small farmers.“ (George Roberts: „The Social\n History of the People of the Southern Counties of England in\n pastcenturies. Lond. 1856“, p. 184, 185.)] .\n\n\n\n\nEinen neuen furchtbaren Anstoss erhielt der gewaltsame Expro-\n priationsprozess der Volksmasse im 16. Jahrh. durch die Refor-\n mation und, in ihrem Gefolge, den kolossalen Diebstahl der Kirchen-\n güter. Die katholische Kirche war zur Zeit der Reformation Feudal-\n [Running matter: I. 45] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"706\", \"facs\": \"#f0725\"} --> eigenthümer eines grossen Theils des englischen Grund und Bodens. Die\n Unterdrückung der Klöster u. s. w. schleuderte deren Einwohner ins Prole-\n tariat. Die Kirchengüter selbst wurden grossentheils an raubsüchtige\n königliche Günstlinge verschenkt oder zu einem Spottpreis an spekulirende\n Pächter und Stadtbürger verkauft, welche die alten erblichen Untersassen\n massenhaft verjagten und ihre Wirthschaften zusammenwarfen. Das ge-\n setzlich garantirte Eigenthum verarmter Landleute an einem Theil der\n Kirchenzehnten ward stillschweigend konfiscirt [Note: „The right of the poor to share in the tithe, is established by the tenure\n of ancient statutes.“ (Tuckett l. c. v. II, p. 804, 805.)] . „Pauper ubique\n jacet“, rief Königin Elisabeth nach einer Rundreise durch England. Im\n 43. Jahre ihrer Regierung war man endlich gezwungen, den Pauperis-\n mus officiell anzuerkennen durch Einführung der Armensteuer. „Die\n Urheber dieses Gesetzes schämten sich seine Gründe auszusprechen und\n schickten es daher, wider alles Herkommen, ohne irgend ein pre-\n amble (Eingangsworte) in die Welt“ [Note: William Cobbett: „A History of the Protestant Refor-\n mation“, §. 471.] . Durch 16. Carolus I., 4\n wurde es perpetuell erklärt und erhielt in der That erst 1834 eine neue\n härtere Form [Note: Den protestantischen „Geist“ ersieht man u. a. aus folgendem. Im\n Süden Englands steckten verschiedne Grundeigenthümer und wohlhabende Pächter\n die Köpfe zusammen, und setzten über die richtige Interpretation des Armen-\n gesetzes der Elisabeth 10 Fragen auf, welche sie einem berühmten Juristen jener\n Zeit, Sergeant Snigge (später Richter unter Jakob I.), zum Gutachten vorlegten.\n „Neunte Frage: Einige der reichen Pächter der Pfarrei haben einen klugen\n Plan ausgeheckt, wodurch alle Wirre in Ausübung des Akts beseitigt werden kann.\n Sie schlagen den Bau eines Gefängnisses in der Pfarrei vor. Jedem Armen,\n der sich nicht in vorbesagtes Gefängniss einsperren lassen will, soll die Unter-\n stützung versagt werden. Es soll dann der Nachbarschaft Notiz gegeben werden,\n dass wenn irgend eine Person geneigt, die Armen dieser Pfarrei zu pachten, sie\n versiegelte Vorschläge eingeben soll, an einem bestimmten Tag, zum niedrigsten\n Preis, wozu sie selbe uns abnehmen will. Die Urheber dieses Plans unter-\n stellen, dass es in den Nachbargrafschaften Personen giebt, die unwillig sind zu\n arbeiten, und ohne Vermögen oder Kredit, um eine Pacht oder ein Schiff zu er-\n werben, so dass sie ohne Arbeit leben könnten („so as to live without labour“).\n Solche dürften geneigt sein, der Pfarrei sehr vortheilhafte Vorschläge zu machen.\nSollten hier und da Arme unter des Kontraktors Obhut kaput] . Diese unmittelbaren Wirkungen der Reformation\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"707\", \"facs\": \"#f0726\"} --> waren nicht ihre nachhaltigsten. Das Kircheneigenthum bildete\n das religiöse Bollwerk der alterthümlichen Grundeigenthums-\n verhältnisse. Mit seinem Fall waren sie nicht länger vertheidigbar [Note: Herr Rogers, obgleich Professor der politischen Oekonomie an der Uni-\n versität zu Oxford, dem Stammsitz protestantischer Orthodoxie, betont in seiner Vor-\n rede zur „History of Agriculture“ die Pauperisirung der Volksmasse durch\n die Reformation.] .\n\n\n\n\nNoch in den letzten Decennien des 17. Jahrhunderts war die Yeomanry,\n eine unabhängige Bauernschaft, zahlreicher als die Klasse der Pächter.\n Sie hatte die Hauptstärke Cromwell’s gebildet und stand, selbst nach Ma-\n caulay’s Geständniss, in vortheilhaftem Gegensatz zu den versoffenen Mist-\n junkern und ihren Bedienten, den Landpfaffen, welche die herrschaftliche\n „Lieblingsmagd“ unter die Haube bringen mussten. Noch waren selbst\n die ländlichen Lohnarbeiter Miteigenthümer am Gemeindeeigenthum.\n [Note: gehn, so wird die Sünde an seiner Thüreliegen, da die Pfarrei ihre\n Pflichten gegen selbige Arme erfüllt hätte. Wir fürchten jedoch, dass der gegen-\n wärtige Akt keine Klugheitsmassregel („prudential measure“) dieser Art erlaubt;\n aber Sie müssen wissen, dass der Rest der freeholders dieser Grafschaft und der\n anliegenden sich uns anschliessen wird, um ihre Unterhausmitglieder zur Vorlage\n eines Gesetzes anzutreiben, welches Einsperrung und Zwangsarbeit der Armen ge-\n stattet, so dass jede Person, welche sich der Einsperrung widersetzt, zu keiner\n Unterstützung berechtigt sein soll. Diess, so hoffen wir, wird Personen im Elend\n abhalten, Unterstützung zu bedürfen“ („will prevent persons in distress from\n wanting relief“). (R. Blakey: „The History of Political Literature\n from the earliest times. Lond. 1855“, v. II, p. 83 sqq.) — In Schott-\n land fand die Abschaffung der Leibeigenschaft Jahrhunderte später statt\n als in England. Noch 1698 erklärte Salhoun im schottischen Parlament:\n „Die Zahl der Bettler ist in Schottland auf nicht weniger als 200,000 geschätzt.\n Das einzige Hilfsmittel, welches ich, ein Republikaner von Prinzip, vorschlagen\n kann, ist den alten Zustand der Leibeigenschaft zu restauriren\n und aus allen denen Sklaven zu machen, die unfähig sind für ihre eigne\n Subsistenz zu sorgen.“ So Eden l. c. b. I, ch. I: „Von der Freiheit der\n Ackerbauer datirt der Pauperismus … Manufakturen und Handel sind die\n wahren Aeltern unsrer nationalen Armen.“ Eden, wie jener schottische Repub-\n likaner von Prinzip, irrt nur darin, dass nicht die Aufhebung der Leibeigenschaft,\n sondern die Aufhebung des Eigenthums des Ackerbauers an Grund und Boden ihn\n zum Proletarier, resp. Pauper machte. — Englands Armengesetzen entspricht in\n Frankreich, wo sich die Expropriation in anderer Weise vollzog, die Ordonnanz\n von Moulins, 1571, und das Edikt von 1656.] \n [Running matter: 45*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"708\", \"facs\": \"#f0727\"} --> 1750 ungefähr war die Yeomanry verschwunden [Note: „A Letter to Sir T. C. Banbury, Brt.: „On the High Price\n of Provisions. By a Suffolk Gentleman.“ Ipswich, 1795, p. 4.\n Selbst der fanatische Vertheidiger des grossen Pachtwesens, der Verfasser der\n „Inquiry into the Connection of large farms etc. Lond. 1773“,\n p. 133, sagt: „I most lament the loss of our yeomanry, that set of men, who really\n kept up the independence of this nation; and sorry I am, to see their lands now\nin the hands of monopolizing lords, tenanted out to small farmers, who\n hold their leases on such conditions as to be little better than vassals ready to\n attend a summons on every mischievous occasion.“] , und in den letzten De-\n cennien des 18. Jahrhunderts die letzte Spur vom Gemeindeeigenthum der\n Ackerbauer. Wir sehn hier ab von den rein ökonomischen Agen-\n tien der Agrikulturrevolution. Wir fragen nach ihren gewalt-\n samen Hebeln.\n\n\n\n\nUnter der Restauration der Stuarts setzten die Grundeigen-\n thümer eine Usurpation gesetzlich durch, die sich überall auf dem Kon-\n tinent auch ohne gesetzliche Weitläufigkeit vollzog. Sie hoben die Feudal-\n verfassung des Bodens auf, d. h. sie schüttelten seine Leistungspflich-\n ten an den Staat ab, „entschädigten“ den Staat durch Steuern auf die\n Bauernschaft und übrige Volksmasse, vindicirten modernes Privateigenthum\n an Gütern, worauf sie nur Feudaltitel besassen, und oktroyirten schliesslich\n jene Niederlassungsgesetze (laws of settlement), die, mutatis mu-\n tandis, auf die englischen Ackerbauer wirkten, wie des Tartaren Boris Go\n dunof Edikt auf die russische Bauernschaft.\n\n\n\n\nDie „glorious Revolution“ (glorreiche Revolution) brachte\n mit dem Oranier Wilhelm III. [Note: Ueber die Privatmoral dieses bürgerlichen Helden u. a.: „The large\n grant of lands in Ireland to Lady Orkney, in 1695, is a public instance of the\n king’s affection, and the lady’s influence … Lady Orkney’s endearing offices,\n are supposed to have been — foeda labiorum ministeria.“ (In der Sloane\n Manuscript Collection, auf dem britischen Museum, Nr. 4224. Das\n Manuskript ist betitelt: „The character and behaviour of king Wil-\n liam, Sunderland etc. as represented in Original Letters to the\n Duke of Shrewsbury from Somers, Halifax, Oxford, Secretary\n Vernon etc.“ Es ist voller Kuriosa.)]  die grundeigenthümlichen und kapi-\n talistischen Plusmacher zur Herrschaft. Sie weihten die neue Aera ein,\n indem sie den bisher nur bescheiden betriebenen Diebstahl an den\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"709\", \"facs\": \"#f0728\"} --> Staatsdomänen auf kolossaler Stufenleiter ausübten. Diese Län-\n dereien wurden verschenkt, zu Spottpreisen verkauft, oder auch durch\n direkte Usurpation an Privatgüter annexirt [Note: „Die illegale Veräusserung der Krongüter, theils durch Verkauf und\n theils durch Schenkung, bildet ein skandalöses Kapitel in der englischen Ge-\n schichte … eine gigantische Prellerei der Nation (gigantic fraud on the nation).“\n (F. W. Newman: „Lectures on Political Econ. Lond. 1851“,\n p. 129, 130.)] . Alles das geschah ohne\n die geringste Beobachtung gesetzlicher Etiquette. Das so fraudulent an-\n geeignete Staatsgut sammt dem Kirchenplunder, so weit er während der\n republikanischen Revolution nicht abhanden kam, bildet die Grundlage der\n heutigen fürstlichen Domänen der englischen Oligarchie [Note: Man lese z. B. E. Burke’s Pamphlet über das herzogliche Haus von\n Bedford, dessen Sprosse Lord John Russell, „the tomtit of liberalism“.] . Die bürger-\n lichen Kapitalisten begünstigten die Operation, u. a. um den Grund und\n Boden in einen reinen Handelsartikel zu verwandeln, um ihre Zufuhr vogel-\n freier Proletarier vom Land zu vermehren u. s. w. Sie handelten für ihr Inter-\n esse ganz so richtig als die schwedischen Stadtbürger, deren ökono-\n misches Bollwerk die Bauernschaft war, wesshalb sie Hand in Hand\n mit derselben die Könige in der gewaltsamen Resumption der Kronlän-\n dereien von der Oligarchie (seit 1604, später unter Karl X. und\n Karl XI.) unterstützten.\n\n\n\n\nGemeindeeigenthum war eine altgermanische Einrichtung,\n die unter der Decke der Feudalität fortlebte. Man hat gesehn, wie die\n gewaltsame Usurpation desselben, meist begleitet von Verwandlung des\n Ackerlands in Viehweide, Ende des 15. Jahrhunderts beginnt und im\n 16. Jahrhundert fortdauert. Aber damals vollzog sich der Prozess als\nindividuelle Gewaltthat, wogegen die Gesetzgebung 150 Jahre\n durch vergeblich ankämpft. Der Fortschritt des 18. Jahrh. offenbart sich\n darin, dass das Gesetz selbst jetzt zum Vehikel des Raubs am\n Volksland wird, obgleich die grossen Pächter nebenbei auch ihre kleinen\n unabhängigen Privatmethoden anwenden [Note: „Die Pächter verbieten den Cottagers (Häuslern) irgend eine lebendige\n Kreatur ausser sich selbst zu erhalten, unter dem Vorwand, dass wenn sie Vieh\n oder Geflügel hielten, sie von den Scheunen Futter stehlen würden. Sie sagen\n auch, haltet die Cottagers arm, und ihr haltet sie fleissig. Die wirkliche That-] . Die parlamentarische Form\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"710\", \"facs\": \"#f0729\"} --> des Raubs ist die der „Bills for Inclosures of Commons“ (Bills\n für Einschluss der Gemeindeländereien), in andern Worten Dekrete, wo-\n durch die Landlords Volkseigenthum sich selbst als Privateigenthum schen-\n ken, Dekrete der Volksexpropriation. Sir F. M. Eden widerlegt sein\n pfiffiges Advokatenplaidoyer, worin er das Gemeindeeigenthum als Privat-\n eigenthum der an die Stelle der Feudalen getretenen Landlords darzustellen\n sucht, indem er selbst einen „allgemeinen Parlamentsakt für die\n inclosure der Gemeindeländerei“ verlangt, also zugiebt, dass ein parla-\n mentarischer Staatsstreich zu ihrer Verwandlung in Privat-\n eigenthum nöthig ist, andrerseits aber von der Legislatur „Schaden-\n ersatz“ für die exproprürten Armen fordert [Note: Eden l. c. Preface.] .\n\n\n\n\nWährend an die Stelle der unabhängigen Yeomen tenants-atwill\n traten, kleinere Pächter auf einjährige Kündigung, eine servile und von\n der Willkühr der Landlords abhängige Rotte, half, neben dem Raub der\n Staatsdomänen, namentlich der systematisch betriebne Diebstahl des Ge-\n meindeeigenthums jene grossen Pachten anschwellen, die man im 18. Jahr-\n hundert Kapital-Pachten [Note: „Capital-farms.“ („Two Letters on the Flour Trade and the\n Dearness of Corn. By a Person in Business. Lond. 1767“, p.\n 19, 20.)]  oder Kaufmanns-Pachten [Note: „Merchant-farms.“ (An Inquiry into the Present High Prices\n of Provisions. Lond. 1767, p. 11, Note. Diese gute Schrift, die anonym\n erschien, verfasst von dem Rev. Nathaniel Forster.)] \n nannte, und das Landvolk als Proletariat für die Industrie „freisetzen“.\n\n\n\n\nDas 18. Jahrh. begriff jedoch noch nicht in demselben Mass wie das\n 19. die Identität zwischen Nationalreichthum und Volks-\n armuth. Daher heftigste Polemik in der ökonomischen Literatur jener\n Zeit über die „inclosure of commons“. Ich gebe aus dem massen-\n haften Material, das mir vorliegt, einige wenige Stellen, weil dadurch leb-\n haft die Zustände veranschaulicht werden.\n\n\n\n\n„In vielen Pfarreien von Hertfordshire“, schreibt eine entrüstete\n Feder, „sind 24 Pachten von 50 bis 150 Acres auf 3 zusammenge-\n [Note: sache aber ist, dass die Pächter so das ganze Recht an den Gemeinde-\n ländereien usurpiren.“ („A Political Enquiry into the Conse-\n quences of enclosing Waste Lands. Lond. 1785“, p. 75.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"711\", \"facs\": \"#f0730\"} --> schmolzen“ [Note: Thomas Wright: „A short address to the Public on the\n Monopoly of large farms. 1778“, p. 2, 3.] . „In Northamptonshire und Lincolnshire hat der Ein-\n schluss der Gemeindeländereien sehr vorgeherrscht und die meisten aus\n den enclosures entsprungenen neuen Lordschaften sind in Weideland ver-\n wandelt; in Folge davon haben viele Lordschaften jetzt nicht 50 Acres\n unter dem Pflug, wo früher 1500 gepflügt wurden … Ruinen früherer\n Wohnhäuser, Scheunen, Ställe u. s. w. sind die einzigen Spuren der früheren\n Einwohner. Hunderte von Häusern und Familien sind an manchen Plätzen\n zusammengeschrumpft auf 8 oder 10. Der Grundeigenthümer in den\n meisten Pfarreien, wo der Einschluss erst seit 15 oder 20 Jahren vor-\n ging, sind sehr wenige in Vergleich zu den Zahlen, von denen das Land im\n offnen Feldzustand bebaut wurde. Es ist nichts Ungewöhnliches, 4 oder\n 5 reiche Viehmäster grosse, jüngst eingeschlossene Lordschaften usurpiren\n zu sehn, die sich früher in der Hand von 20—30 Pächtern und vielen\n kleineren Eigenthümern und Insassen befanden. Alle diese sind mit\n ihren Familien aus ihrem Besitzthum herausgeworfen\n worden, nebst vielen andren Familien, die durch sie beschäftigt und er-\n halten wurden“ [Note: Rev. Mr. Addington: „Enquiry into the Reasons for or\n against enclosing open fields. Lond. 1772“, p. 37—43 passim.] . Es war nicht nur brachliegendes, sondern oft, unter\n bestimmter Zahlung an die Gemeinde, oder gemeinschaftlich bebautes\n Land, das unter dem Vorwand der „enclosure“ vom angränzenden Landlord\n annexirt wurde. „Ich spreche hier vom Einschluss offner Felder und Lände-\n reien, die bereits bébaut sind. Selbst die Schriftsteller, welche die Inclosures\n vertheidigen, geben zu, dass sie in diesem Fall den Feldbau vermindern, die\n Preise der Lebensmittel erhöhen und Entvölkerung produciren … und\n selbst die inclosure wüster Ländereien, wie jetzt betrieben, raubt dem\nArmen einen Theil seiner Subsistenzmittel und schwellt\n Pachten auf, die bereits zu gross sind“ [Note: Dr. R. Price l. c. v. II, p. 155. Man lese Forster, Addington, Kent,\n Price und James Anderson, und vergleiche das elende Sykophantengeschwätz Mac\n Culloch’s in seinem Katalog: „The Literature of Political Economy.\n Lond. 1845.“] . „Wenn“, sagt Dr. Price,\n „das Land in die Hände einiger weniger grossen Pächter geräth, werden\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"712\", \"facs\": \"#f0731\"} --> die kleinen Pächter (früher von ihm bezeichnet als „eine Menge kleiner\n Eigenthümer und Pächter, die sich selbst und Familien erhalten durch das\n Produkt des von ihnen bestellten Landes, durch Schafe, Geflügel, Schweine\n u. s. w., die sie auf das Gemeindeland schicken, so dass sie wenig Ge-\n legenheit zum Kauf von Subsistenzmitteln haben“) verwandelt in\n Leute, die ihre Subsistenz durch Arbeit für Andre gewinnen müssen\n und gezwungen sind, für alles, was sie brauchen, zu Markt zu gehn\n … Es wird vielleicht mehr Arbeit verrichtet, weil mehr Zwang\n dazu herrscht … Städte und Manufakturen werden wachsen, weil mehr\n Leute zu ihnen verjagt werden, welche Beschäftigung suchen.\n Diess ist der Weg, worin die Koncentration der Pachten naturgemäss\n wirkt und worin sie, seit vielen Jahren, in diesem Königreich thatsächlich\n gewirkt hat“ [Note: l. c. p. 147.] . Er fasst die Gesammtwirkung der inclosures so zu-\n sammen: „Im Ganzen hat sich die Lage der niederen Volksklassen fast in\n ieder Hinsicht verschlechtert, die kleineren Grundbesitzer und Pächter sind\n herabgedrückt auf den Stand von Taglöhnern und Miethlingen; und zur sel-\n ben Zeit ist der Lebensgewinn in diesem Zustand schwieriger ge-\n worden“ [Note: l. c. p. 159. Man erinnert sich an das alte Rom. „Die Reichen hatten sich\n des grössten Theils der ungetheilten Ländereien bemächtigt. Sie vertrauten den\n Zeitumständen, dass sie ihnen nicht mehr abgenommen würden, und kauften daher\n die in ihrer Nähe gelegenen Stücke der Armen, zum Theil mit deren Willen, zum\n Theil nahmen sie ihnen mit Gewalt, so dass sie nur mehr weit ausgedehnte Do-\n mänen statt einzelner Felder bebauten. Sie gebrauchten dabei Sklaven zum Land-\n bau und zur Viehzucht, weil ihnen freie Leute weg von der Arbeit zum Kriegs-\n dienst genommen worden wären. Der Besitz von Sklaven brachte ihnen auch\n insofern grossen Gewinn, als sich diese wegen ihrer Befreiung vom Kriegsdienst\n ungefährdet vermehren konnten und eine Menge Kinder bekamen. So zogen die\n Mächtigen durchaus allen Reichthum an sich und die ganze Gegend wimmelte von\n Sklaven. Der Italer dagegen wurden immer weniger, aufgerieben wie sie waren\n durch Armuth, Abgaben und Kriegsdienst. Traten aber auch Zeiten des Friedens\n ein, so waren sie zu vollkommener Unthätigkeit verdammt, weil die Reichen im\n Besitze des Bodens waren, und statt freier Leute Sklaven zum Ackerbau ge-\n brauchten.“ (Appian: „Römische Geschichte“ 1, 7.) Diese Stelle\n bezieht sich auf die Zeit vor den licinischen Gesetzen. Der Kriegsdienst, der den\n Ruin der römischen Plebejer so sehr beschleunigte, war auch ein Hauptmittel,] . In der That wirkten Usurpation des Gemeindelands und die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"713\", \"facs\": \"#f0732\"} --> sie begleitende Revolution der Agrikultur so akut auf die Ackerbauar-\n beiter, dass, nach Eden selbst, zwischen 1765 und 1780 ihr Lohn anfing\nunter das Minimum zu fallen und durch officielle Armenunterstützung\n ergänzt zu werden. Ihr Arbeitslohn, sagt er, „genügte nicht mehr für die\n absoluten Lebensbedürfnisse.“\n\n\n\n\nHören wir noch einen Augenblick einen Vertheidiger der enclosures\n und Gegner des Dr. Price. „Es ist ein durchaus falscher Schluss, dass\nEntvölkerung vorhanden, weil man Leute nicht länger ihre Arbeit im\n offnen Feld verwüsten sieht. Sind ihrer jetzt weniger auf dem Land,\n so sind ihrer desto mehr in den Städten … Wenn nach Verwandlung\n kleiner Bauern in Leute, die für andere arbeiten müssen, mehr\n Arbeit flüssig gemacht wird, so ist das ja ein Vortheil, den die\n Nation (wozu die Verwandelten natürlich nicht gehören) wünschen\n muss … Das Produkt wird grösser sein, wenn ihre kombinirte Arbeit\n auf einer Pacht angewandt wird: so wird Surplusprodukt für die Manu-\n fakturen gebildet, und dadurch werden Manufakturen, eine der Minen die-\n ser Nation, im Verhältniss zum producirten Kornquantum vermehrt“ [Note: „An Inquiry into the Connection between the present\n Prices of Provision etc.“, p. 124, 129. Aehnlich, aber mit entgegengesetzter\n Tendenz: „Working men are driven from their cottages, and forced into the\n towns to seek for employment; — but then a larger surplus is obtained, and\n thus Capital is augmented.“ (The Perils of the Nation. 2nd ed.\n Lond. 1843, p. XIV.)] .\n\n\n\n\nDie stoische Seelenruhe, womit der politische Oekonom frechste Schän\n dung des „heiligen Rechts des Eigenthums“ und gröbste Gewaltthat wider Per-\n sonen betrachtet, soweit sie erheischt sind, um die Grundlage der kapita-\n listischen Produktionsweise herzustellen, zeigt uns u.a. der über-\n dem noch torystisch gefärbte und „philanthropische“ Sir F. M. Eden. Die\n ganze Reihe von Raubthaten, Greueln und Volkselend, welche die gewaltsame\n Volksexpropriation vom letzten Drittel des 15. bis zum Ende des 18. Jahrh.\n begleiten, treibt ihn nur zur „comfortablen“ Schlussreflexion: „Die rich-\n tige (due) Proportion zwischen Acker- und Viehland musste\n hergestellt werden. Noch im ganzen 14. und grössten Theil des 15.\n Jahrh. kam 1 Acre Viehweide auf 2, 3, und selbst 4 Acres Ackerland. In der\n [Note: wodurch Karl der Grosse die Verwandlung freier deutscher Bauern in Leibeigne\n treibhausmässig förderte.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"714\", \"facs\": \"#f0733\"} --> Mitte des 16 Jahrhunderts verwandelte sich die Proportion in 3 Acres\n Viehland auf 2, später von 2 Acres Viehweide auf 1 Acre Ackerland, bis\n endlich die richtige Proportion von 3 Acres Viehland auf\n 1 Acre Ackerland herauskam.“\n\n\n\n\nIm 19. Jahrhundert verlor sich natürlich selbst die Erinnerung des\n Zusammenhangs zwischen Ackerbauer und Gemeindeeigenthum. Von\n späterer Zeit gar nicht zu reden, welchen Farthing Ersatz erhielt das\n Landvolk jemals für die 3,511,770 Acres Gemeinland, die ihm zwischen\n1801 und 1831 geraubt und parlamentarisch den Landlords von den\n Landlords geschenkt wurden?\n\n\n\n\nDer letzte grosse Expropriationsprozess der Ackerbauer\n von Grund und Boden endlich ist das s. g. „Clearing of Estates“\n (Lichten der Güter, in der That Wegfegung der Menschen von den-\n selben). Alle bisher betrachteten englischen Methoden kulminirten im\n „Lichten“. Wie man bei der Schilderung des modernen Zustands im vori-\n gen Abschnitt sah, geht es jetzt, wo unabhängige Bauern nicht mehr weg-\n zufegen sind, bis zum „Lichten“ der Cottages fort, so dass die Acker-\n bauarbeiter auf dem Boden, den sie bestellen, selbst nicht mehr den nöthigen\n Raum zur eignen Behausung finden. Indess unterscheidet sich das eigent-\n liche „Clearing of Estates“ durch den mehr systematischen Charak-\n ter, die Grösse der Stufenleiter, worauf die Operation auf einmal ausgeführt\n wird (in Schottland auf Arealen so gross, wie deutsche Fürstenthümer), und\n durch die eigenthümliche Form des Grundeigenthums, welches so gewaltsam\n in modernes Privateigenthum verwandelt wird. Diess Eigenthum war Eigen-\n thum des Clans, der Chef oder „grosse Mann“ nur Titulareigenthümer\n als Repräsentant des Clans, wie die Königin von England Titulareigenthü-\n merin des englischen Grund und Bodens ist [Note: „A king of England might as well claim to drive his subjects into the\n tsea.“ (F. W. Newman l. c. p. 132.)] . Diese Revolution, welche\n in Schottland nach der letzten Schilderhebung des Prätendenten begann,\n kann man in ihren ersten Phasen verfolgen bei Sir James Steuart [Note: Steuart sagt: „Die Rente dieser Länder (er überträgt irrthümlich diese\n ökonomische Kategorie auf den Tribut der taskmen an den Clanchef) ist durch-\n aus unbedeutend im Vergleich zu ihrem Umfang, aber, was die Personenzahl be-\n rifft, welche eine Pacht erhält, wird man vielleicht finden, dass ein Stück Boden]  und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"715\", \"facs\": \"#f0734\"} --> James Anderson [Note: James Anderson: „Observations on the means of excit-\n ing a spirit of National Industry etc.“ Edinburgh 1777.] . Im 18. Jahrh. wurde zugleich den vom Land verjag-\n ten Gaelen die Auswanderung verboten, um sie gewaltsam nach Glasgow\n und anderen Fabrikstädten zu treiben [Note: 1860 wurden gewaltsam Expropriirte nach Kanada exportirt unter\n falschen Versprechungen. Einige flohen in die Berge und benachbarten Eilande.\n Sie wurden von Polizisten verfolgt, kamen zum Handgemenge mit ihnen, und ent-\n kamen.] . Als Beispiel der im 19. Jahrh.\n herrschenden Methode [Note: „In den Hochlanden“, sagt Buchanan, der Kommentator A. Smith’s,\n 1814, „wird der alte Eigenthumszustand täglich gewaltsam umgewälzt …\n Der Landlord, ohne Rücksicht auf die Erbpächter (auch diess ist hier irrig ange-\n wandte Kategorie), bietet das Land dem höchsten Bieter an, und wenn dieser ein\n Verbesserer (improver) ist, führt er unmittelbar ein neues Kultursystem ein. Der\n Boden, früher übersät mit kleinen Bauern, war im Verhältniss zu seinem Produkt\n bevölkert; unter dem neuen System verbesserter Kultur und vermehrter Renten,\n wird grösstmöglichstes Produkt zu möglichst geringen Kosten erhalten und zu\n diesem Behuf werden die nun nutzlos gewordenen Hände entfernt. Die Auswürf-\n linge des Heimlands suchen Subsistenz in den Fabrikstädten u. s. w.“ (David\n Buchanan: „Observations on etc. A. Smith’s Wealth of Nations.\n Edinb. 1814.“) „Die schottischen Grossen haben Familien expropriirt, wie sie\n Unkraut ausroden würden, sie haben Dorfschaften und ihre Bevölkerung behan-\n delt, wie die Indier in ihrer Rache die Höhlen wilder Bestien … Der Mensch\n wird verschachert für ein Schafvliess oder eine Hammelkeule, ja für weniger …\n Bei dem Einfall in die Nordprovinzen China’s schlug man im Mongolenrath vor,\n die Einwohner auszurotten und ihr Land in Weide zu verwandeln. Diesen Vor-\n schlag haben viele hochschottische Landlords in ihrem eignen Land gegen\n ihre eignen Landsleute ausgeführt.“ (George Ensor: „An Inquiry\n concerning the Population of Nations. Lond. 1818“, p. 215, 216.)]  genügen hier die „Lichtungen“ der Gräfin\n von Sutherland. Diese ökonomisch geschulte Person beschloss gleich\n bei ihrem Regierungsantritt eine ökonomische Radikalkur vorzunehmen und\n die ganze Grafschaft, deren Einwohnerschaft durch frühere, ähnliche Pro-\n zesse bereits auf 15,000 zusammengeschmolzen war, in Schaftriften zu\n verwandeln. Von 1814 bis 1820 wurden diese 15,000 Einwohner, un-\n gefähr 3000 Familien, systematisch verjagt und ausgerottet. Alle ihre\n Dörfer wurden zerstört und niedergebrannt, alle ihre Felder in Weide ver-\n [Note: in den Hochlanden von Schottland zehnmal mehr Leute ernährt, als Land von der-\n selben Grösse in den reichsten Provinzen.“ (l. c. v. I, ch. XVI.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"716\", \"facs\": \"#f0735\"} --> wandelt. Britische Soldaten wurden zur Execution kommandirt und kamen\n zu Schlägen mit den Eingebornen. Eine alte Frau verbrannte in den\n Flammen der Hütte, die sie zu verlassen sich weigerte. So eignete sich diese\n Madame 794,000 Acres Land an, das seit undenklicher Zeit dem\n Clan gehörte. Den vertriebenen Eingebornen wies sie am Seegestad un-\n gefähr 6000 Acres zu, 2 Acres per Familie. Die 6000 Acres hatten bis-\n her wüst gelegen und den Eigenthümern kein Einkommen abgeworfen.\n Die Gräfin ging in ihrem Nobelgefühl so weit den Acre zu 2 sh. 6 d. Rente\n im Durchschnitt den Clanleuten zu verpachten, die seit Jahrhunderten ihr\n Blut für die Familie vergossen hatten. Das ganze geraubte Clanland theilte\n sie in 29 grosse Schafpachten, jede bewohnt von einer einzigen Familie,\n meist englische Pachtknechte. Im Jahr 1825 waren die 15,000 Gaelen\n bereits ersetzt durch 131,000 Schafe. Der an das Seegestad geworfne\n Theil der Aborigines suchte vom Fischfang zu leben. Sie wurden Amphi-\n bien, und lebten, wie ein englischer Schriftsteller sagt, halb auf dem\n Land und halb auf dem Wasser und lebten mit alledem nur halb von\n beiden [Note: Als die jetzige Gräfin Sutherland die Mrs. Beecher Stowe, Verfasserin\n von „Uncle Tom’s Cabin“, mit grossem Prunk in London empfing, um ihre\n Sympathie für die Negersklaven der amerikanischen Republik auszustellen — was\n sie, nebst ihren Mitaristokratinnen, wohlweise während des Bürgerkriegs unterliess,\n wo jedes „noble“ englische Herz für die Sklavenhalter schlug — stellte ich in der\nNew-York Tribune die Verhältnisse der Sutherlandschen Sklaven dar.\n (Stellenweis ausgezogen von Carey in „The Slave Trade. London 1853“,\n p. 202, 203.) Mein Artikel ward in einem schottischen Blatt abgedruckt und rief eine\n artige Polemik zwischen letzterem und den Sykophanten der Sutherlands hervor.] .\n\n\n\n\nAber die braven Gaelen sollten noch schwerer ihre bergromantische\n Idolatrie für die „grossen Männer“ des Clans abbüssen. Der Fischge-\n ruch stieg den grossen Männern in die Nase. Sie witterten etwas Profit-\n liches dahinter und verpachteten das Seegestade den grossen Fischhänd-\n lern von London. Die Gaelen wurden zum zweitenmal verjagt [Note: Interessantes über diesen Fischhandel findet man in Herrn David\n Urquhart’s „Portfolio. New Series.“] .\n\n\n\n\nEndlich aber wird ein Theil der Schaftriften rückverwandelt in\nJagdrevier. Man weiss, dass es keine ernsthaften Wälder in Eng-\n land giebt. Das Wild in den Parks der Grossen ist konstitutionelles Haus-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"717\", \"facs\": \"#f0736\"} --> vieh, fett wie Londoner Aldermen. Schottland ist daher das letzte Asyl\n der „noblen Passion“. „In den Hochlanden,“ sagt Somers, 1848,\n „sind die Waldungen sehr ausgedehnt worden. Hier auf der einen Seite\n von Gaick habt ihr den neuen Wald von Glenfeshie, und dort, auf der\n andern Seite, den neuen Wald von Ardverikie. In derselben Linie habt\n ihr den Bleak-Mount, eine ungeheure Wüste, neulich errichtet. Von Ost zu\n West, von der Nachbarschaft von Aberdeen bis zu den Klippen von Obon,\n habt ihr jetzt eine fortlaufende Waldlinie, während sich in andern Theilen\n der Hochlande die neuen Wälder von Loch Archaig, Glengarry, Glen-\n moriston u. s. w. befinden … Die Verwandlung ihres Landes in Schaf-\n weide trieb die Gaelen auf unfruchtbareren Boden. Jetzt fängt Rothwild\n an das Schaf zu ersetzen und treibt jene in noch zermalmenderes Elend …\n Die Wildwaldungen und das Volk können nicht neben einander existiren.\n Eins oder das andre muss jedenfalls den Platz räumen. Lasst die\n Jagden in Zahl und Umfang im nächsten Vierteljahrhundert wachsen wie\n im vergangenen, und ihr werdet keinen Gaelen mehr auf seiner heimischen\n Erde finden. Diese Bewegung unter den Hochlands-Eigenthümern ist\n theils der Mode geschuldet, aristokratischem Kitzel, Jagdliebhaberei u. s. w.,\n theils aber betreiben sie den Wildhandel ausschliesslich mit einem\n Auge auf den Profit. Denn es ist Thatsache, dass ein Stück Berg-\n land, in Jagdung angelegt, in vielen Fällen ungleich profitabler ist denn\n als Schaftrift … Der Liebhaber, der ein Jagdrevier sucht, beschränkt\n sein Angebot nur durch die Weite seiner Börse … Leiden sind über die\n Hochlande verhängt worden nicht minder grausam als die Politik normän-\n nischer Könige sie über England verhing. Rothwild hat freieren Spiel-\n raum erhalten, während die Menschen in engen und engeren Zirkel ge-\n hetzt wurden … Eine Freiheit des Volks nach der andern ward ihm\n geraubt … Und die Unterdrückung wächst noch täglich. Die Lichtung\n und Vertreibung des Volks werden von den Eigenthümern als festes Princip\n verfolgt, als eine agrikole Nothwendigkeit, ganz wie Bäume und Gesträuch\n in den Wildnissen Amerika’s und Australiens weggefegt werden, und die\n Operation geht ihren ruhigen, geschäftsmässigen Gang“ [Note: Robert Somers: „Letters from the Highlands; or, the\n Famine of 1847. Lond. 1848“, p. 12—28 passim. Diese Briefe erschienen\n ursprünglich in der Times. Der Oekonom erklärte natürlich die Hungersnoth] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"718\", \"facs\": \"#f0737\"} -->\n\nDer Raub der Kirchengüter, die fraudulente Veräusserung der Staats-\n domänen, der Diebstahl des Gemeindeeigenthums, die usurpatorische und\n mit rücksichtslosem Terrorismus vollzogne Verwandlung von feudalem und\n Claneigenthum in modernes Privateigenthum, es waren ebenso viele idylli-\n sche Methoden der ursprünglichen Accumulation. Durch\n sie ward das Feld für die kapitalistische Agrikultur erobert, der Grund\n [Note: der Gaelen von 1847 aus ihrer — Uebervölkerung. Jedenfalls „drückten“ sie\n auf ihre Nahrungsmittel. — Das „Clearing of Estates“, oder, wie es in Deutsch-\n land hiess, „Bauernlegen“, machte sich hier besonders geltend nach dem\n dreissigjährigen Krieg und rief noch 1790 in Kursachsen Bauernaufstände hervor.\n Es herrschte namentlich in Ostdeutschland. In den meisten Provinzen Preussens\n sicherte erst Friedrich II. den Bauern Eigenthumsrecht. Nach der Eroberung\n Schlesiens zwang er die Grundherrn zur Wiederherstellung der Hütten, Scheunen\n u. s. w., zur Ausstattung der Bauerngüter mit Vieh und Geräth. Er brauchte\n Soldaten für seine Armee und Steuerpflichtige für seinen Staatsschatz. Welches ange-\n nehme Leben übrigens der Bauer unter Friedrich’s Finanzunwesen und Regierungs-\n mischmasch von Despotismus, Bureaukratie und Feudalismus führte, mag man\n aus folgender Stelle seines Bewunderers Mirabeau ersehn: „Le lin fait donc\n une des grandes richesses du cultivateur dans le Nord de l’Allemagne. Mal-\n heureusement pour l’espèce humaine, ce n’est qu’une resource contre la misère,\n et non un moyen de bien-être. Les impôts directs, les corvées, les servitudes de\n tout genre, écrasent le cultivateur allemand, qui paie encore des impôts indirects\n dans tout ce qu’il achète … et pour comble de ruine, il n’ose pas vendre ses produc-\n tions où et comme il le veut; il n’ose pas acheter ce dont il a besoin aux mar-\n chands qui pourraient le lui livrer au meilleur prix. Toutes ces causes le ruinent\n insensiblement, et il se trouverait hors d’état de payer les impôts directs à l’éché-\n ance sans la filerie; elle lui offre une resource, en occupant utilement sa femme,\n ses enfants, ses servants, ses valets, et lui-même: mais qu’elle pénible vie même\n aidée de ce secours. En été, il travaille comme un forçat au labourage et à la\n récolte; il se couche à 9 heures et se lève à deux, pour suffire aux travaux; en\n hiver il devrait réparer ses forces par un plus grand repos; mais il manquera de\n grains pour le pain et les semailles, s’il se defait des denrées qu’il faudrait vendre\n pour payer les impôts. Il faut donc filer pour suppléer à ce vide … il faut y\n apporter la plus grande assiduité. Aussi le paysan se couche-t-il en hiver à minuit,\n une heure, et se lève à cinq ou six; ou bien il se couche à neuf, et se lève à deux,\n et cela tous les jours de sa vie, si ce n’est le dimanche. Ces excès de veille et de\n travail usent la nature humaine, et de là vient qu’ hommes et femmes vieillissent\n beaucoup plûtot dans les campagnes que dans les villes.“ (Mirabeau l. c.\n t. III, p. 212 sqq.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"719\", \"facs\": \"#f0738\"} --> und Boden dem Kapital einverleibt, der städtischen Industrie die nöthige\n Zufuhr von vogelfreiem Proletariat geschaffen.\n\n\n\n\nDie durch Auflösung der feudalen Gefolgschaften und durch stoss-\n weise, gewaltsame Expropriation von Grund und Boden Verjagten,\n diess vogelfreie Proletariat konnte unmöglich eben so rasch von der auf-\n kommenden Manufaktur absorbirt werden als es auf die Welt gesetzt\n ward. Andrerseits konnten die plötzlich aus ihrer gewohnten Lebensbahn\n Herausgeschleuderten sich nicht eben so plötzlich in die Disciplin des neuen\n Zustandes finden. Sie verwandelten sich massenhaft in Bettler, Räuber, Va-\n gabunden, zum Theil aus Neigung, in den meisten Fällen durch den Zwang\n der Umstände. Ende des 15. und während des ganzen 16. Jahrh. daher\nin ganz Westeuropa eine Blutgesetzgebung wider Vagabun-\n dage. Die Väter der jetzigen Arbeiterklasse wurden zunächst für die ihnen\n angethane Verwandlung in Vagabunden und Paupers gezüchtigt. Die\n Gesetzgebung behandelte sie als „freiwillige“ Verbrecher und unter-\n stellte, dass es von ihrem guten Willen abhänge, in den nicht\n mehr existirenden alten Verhältnissen fortzuarbeiten.\n\n\n\n\nIn England begann jene Gesetzgebung unter Heinrich VII.\n\n\n\n\nHeinrich VIII., 1530: Alte und arbeitsunfähige Bettler erhal-\n ten eine Bettellicenz. Dagegen Auspeitschung und Einsperrung für hand-\n feste Vagabunden. Sie sollen an einen Karren hinten angebunden und\n gegeisselt werden, bis das Blut von ihrem Körper strömt, dann einen Eid\n schwören, zu ihrem Geburtsplatz, oder dorthin, wo sie die letzten drei\n Jahre gewohnt, zurückzukehren und „sich an die Arbeit zu setzen“ (to\n put himself to labour). Welche grausame Ironie! 27 Heinrich VIII.\n wird das vorige Statut wiederholt, aber durch neue Zusätze verschärft.\n Bei zweiter Ertappung auf Vagabundage soll die Auspeitschung wieder-\n holt und das halbe Ohr abgeschnitten, bei dritter Recidive aber der Be-\n troffene als schwerer Verbrecher und Feind des Gemeinwesens hingerichtet\n werden.\n\n\n\n\nEdward VI.: Ein Statut aus seinem ersten Regierungsjahr, 1547,\n verordnet, dass wenn Jemand zu arbeiten weigert, soll er als Sklave der\n Person zugeurtheilt werden, die ihn als Müssiggänger denuncirt hat. Der\n Meister soll seinen Sklaven mit Brod und Wasser nähren, schwachem Ge-\n tränk und solchen Fleischabfällen, die er passend dünkt. Er hat das\n Recht, ihn zu jeder auch noch so eklen Arbeit durch Auspeitschung und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"720\", \"facs\": \"#f0739\"} --> Ankettung zu treiben. Wenn sich der Sklave für 14 Tage entfernt, ist\n er zur Sklaverei auf Lebenszeit verurtheilt und soll auf Stirn oder Backen\n mit dem Buchstaben S gebrandmarkt, wenn er zum drittenmal fortläuft,\n als Staatsverräther hingerichtet werden. Der Meister kann ihn verkaufen,\n vermachen, als Sklaven ausdingen, ganz wie anderes bewegliches Gut und\n Vieh. Unternehmen die Sklaven etwas gegen die Herrschaft, so sollen sie\n ebenfalls hingerichtet werden. Friedensrichter sollen auf Information den\n Kerls nachspüren. Findet sich, dass ein Herumstreicher drei Tage gelun-\n gert hat, so soll er nach seinem Geburtsort gebracht, mit rothglühendem\n Eisen auf die Brust mit dem Zeichen V gebrandmarkt, und dort in Ketten\n auf der Strasse oder zu sonstigen Diensten verwandt werden. Giebt der\n Vagabund einen falschen Geburtsort an, so soll er zur Strafe der lebens-\n längliche Sklave dieses Orts, der Einwohner oder Korporation sein und\n mit S gebrandmarkt werden. Alle Personen haben das Recht, den Vaga-\n bunden ihre Kinder wegzunehmen und als Lehrlinge, Jungen bis zum\n 24. Jahr, Mädchen bis zum 20. Jahr, zu halten. Laufen sie weg, so sol-\n len sie bis zu diesem Alter die Sklaven der Lehrmeister sein, die sie in\n Ketten legen, geisseln u. s. w. können, wie sie wollen. Jeder Meister\n darf einen eisernen Ring um Hals, Arme oder Beine seines Sklaven legen,\n damit er ihn besser kennt und seiner sichrer ist [Note: Der Verfasser des „Essay on Trade etc. 1770“ bemerkt: „Unter\n der Regierung Edward’s VI. scheinen sich die Engländer in der That mit vollem\n Ernst auf Encouragirung der Manufakturen und Beschäftigung der Armen verlegt\n zu haben. Diess ersehn wir aus einem merkwürdigen Statut, worin es heisst, dass\n alle Vagabunden gebrandmarkt werden sollen u. s. w.“ (l. c. p. 8.)] . Der letzte Theil\n dieses Statuts sieht vor, dass gewisse Arme von dem Ort oder den Individuen\n beschäftigt werden sollen, die ihnen zu essen und zu trinken geben und\n Arbeit für sie finden wollen. Diese Sorte Pfarreisklaven hat sich bis tief\n ins 19. Jahrhundert in England erhalten unter dem Namen roundsmen\n (Umgeher).\n\n\n\n\nElisabeth, 1572: Bettler ohne Licenz und über 14 Jahre alt\n sollen hart gepeitscht und am linken Ohrlappen gebrandmarkt werden,\nfalls sie keiner für zwei Jahre in Dienst nehmen will; im\n Wiederholungsfall, wenn über 18 Jahre alt, sollen sie — hingerichtet wer-\n den, falls sie Niemand für zwei Jahre in Dienst nehmen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"721\", \"facs\": \"#f0740\"} --> will, bei dritter Recidive aber ohne Gnade als Staatsverräther hingerich-\n tet werden. Aehnliche Statute: 18 Elisabeth c. 13 und 1597.\n\n\n\n\nJakob I.: Eine herumwandernde und bettelnde Person wird für\n einen Landstreicher und Vagabunden erklärt. Die Friedensrichter in den Petty\n Sessions sind bevollmächtigt, sie öffentlich auspeitschen zu lassen und bei\n erster Ertappung 6 Monate, bei zweiter 2 Jahre ins Gefängniss zu sperren.\n Während des Gefängnisses soll sie so oft und so viel gepeitscht werden,\n als die Friedensrichter für gut halten … Die unverbesserlichen und ge-\n fährlichen Landstreicher sollen auf der linken Schulter mit R gebrandmarkt\n und zur Zwangsarbeit gesetzt, und wenn man sie wieder auf dem Bettel\n ertappt, ohne Gnade und ohne geistlichen Beistand hingerichtet werden.\n Diese Anordnungen, gesetzg ültig bis in die erste Zeit des 18. Jahrhun-\n derts, wurden erst aufgehoben durch 12 Anna 23.\n\n\n\n\nAehnliche Gesetze in Frankreich, wo sich Mitte des 17. Jahrhunderts\n ein Vagabundenkönigreich (truands) zu Paris etablirt hatte. Noch in der\n ersten Zeit Ludwig’s XVI. (Ordonnanz vom 13. Juli 1777) sollte jeder ge-\n sund gebaute Mensch vom 16. bis 60. Jahr, wenn ohne Existenzmittel und\n Ausübung einer Profession, auf die Galeeren geschickt werden. Aehnlich\n das Statut Karl’s V. für die Niederlande vom Oktober 1537, das erste\n Edikt der Staaten und Städte von Holland vom 19. März 1614, das Pla-\n kat der Vereinigten Provinzen vom 25. Juni 1649 u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nSo wurde das von Grund und Boden gewaltsam expropriirte, ver-\n jagte und zum grossen Vagabunden gemachte Landvolk durch gro-\n tesk-terroristische Gesetze in eine dem System der Lohnarbeit\n nothwendige Disciplin hineingepeitscht, gebrandmarkt, gefoltert.\n\n\n\n\nEs ist nicht genug, dass die Arbeitsbedingungen auf den einen Pol als\n Kapital treten und auf den andern Pol Menschen, welche nichts zu verkau-\n fen haben als ihre Arbeitskraft. Es genügt auch nicht sie zu zwingen,\n sich freiwillig zu verkaufen. Im Fortgang der kapitalistischen Produktion\n entwickelt sich eine Arbeiterklasse, die aus Erziehung, Tradition, Gewohn-\n heit die Anforderungen jener Produktionsweise als selbstverständliche Na-\n turgesetze anerkennt. Die Organisation des ausgebildeten kapitalisti-\n schen Produktionsprozesses bricht jeden Widerstand, die beständige Erzeu-\n gung einer relativen Uebervölkerung hält das Gesetz der Zufuhr von und\n Nachfrage nach Arbeit, und daher den Arbeitslohn, in einem den Verwer-\n thungsbedürfnissen des Kapitals entsprechenden Gleise, der stumme Zwang\n [Running matter: I. 46] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"722\", \"facs\": \"#f0741\"} --> der ökonomischen Verhältnisse besiegelt die Herrschaft des Kapitalisten\n über den Arbeiter. Ausserökonomische, unmittelbare Gewalt wird daher\n nur ausnahmsweise angewandt. Für den gewöhnlichen Gang der Dinge\n kann der Arbeiter den „Naturgesetzen der Produktion“ über-\n lassen werden, d. h. seiner aus den Produktionsbedingungen selbst ent-\n springenden, durch sie garantirten und verewigten Abhängigkeit vom Ka-\n pital. Anders während der historischen Genesis der kapitalistischen Pro-\n duktion. Die aufkommende Bourgeoisie braucht und verwendet die\nStaatsgewalt, um den Arbeitslohn zu „reguliren“, d. h. in-\n nerhalb der Plusmacherei zusagender Schranken zu zwängen, um den Ar-\n beitstag zu verlängern, und den Arbeiter selbst im normalen Abhän-\n gigkeitsgrad zu erhalten. Es ist diess ein wesentliches Moment der\n s. g. ursprünglichen Accumulation.\n\n\n\n\nDie Klasse der Lohnarbeiter, die in der letzten Hälfte des 14. Jahrh.\n entstand, bildete damals und im folgenden Jahrhundert nur einen sehr ge-\n ringen Volksbestandtheil, der in seiner Stellung stark beschützt war durch\n die selbstständige Bauernwirthschaft auf dem Land und die Zunftorganisa-\n tion der städtischen Industrie. In Land und Stadt standen sich Meister\n und Arbeiter social nahe. Die Subsumtion der Arbeit unter das Kapital\n war nur formell, d. h. die Produktionsweise selbst besass noch keinen\n specifisch kapitalistischen Charakter. Das variable Element des Kapitals\n wog sehr vor über sein constantes. Die Nachfrage nach Lohnarbeit wuchs\n daher rasch mit jeder Accumulation des Kapitals, während die Zufuhr von\n Lohnarbeit nur langsam nachfolgte. Ein grosser Theil des nationalen Pro-\n dukts, später in Accumulationsfonds des Kapitals verwandelt,\n ging damals noch in den Konsumtionsfonds des Arbeiters ein.\n\n\n\n\nDie Gesetzgebung über die Lohnarbeit, von Haus aus auf Exploita-\n tion des Arbeiters gemünzt und ihm in ihrem Fortgang stets gleich feind-\n lich [Note: „Whenever the legislature attempts to regulate the differences between\n masters and their workmen, its counsellors are always the masters“, sagt\n A. Smith. „L’esprit des lois, c’est la propriété“, sagt Linguet.] , wird in England eröffnet durch das Statute of Labourers\n Edward’s III., 1349. Ihm entspricht in Frankreich die Ordonnanz\n von 1350, erlassen im Namen des Königs Jean. Die englische und die\n französische Gesetzgebung laufen parallel und sind dem Inhalt nach iden-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"723\", \"facs\": \"#f0742\"} --> tisch. Soweit die Arbeitsstatuten Verlängerung des Arbeitstags\n zu erzwingen suchen, komme ich nicht auf sie zurück, da dieser Punkt\n früher (III. Kapitel, 4. Abschnitt) erörtert.\n\n\n\n\nDas Statute of Labourers wurde erlassen auf dringende Klage\n des Hauses der Gemeinen. „Früher“, sagt naiv ein Tory, „verlangten\n die Armen so hohen Arbeitslohn, dass sie Industrie und Reichthum bedroh-\n ten. Jetzt ist ihr Lohn so niedrig, dass er ebenfalls Industrie und Reich-\n thum bedroht und vielleicht gefährlicher als damals“ [Note: „Sophisms of Free Trade. By a Barrister. Lond. 1850“,\n p. 206. Er setzt malitiös hinzu: „Die Gesetzgebung war stets bei der Hand für\n den Anwender einzuschreiten. Ist sie ohnmächtig für den Angewandten?“] . Ein gesetz-\n licher Lohntarif ward festgesetzt für Stadt und Land, für Stückwerk und\n Tagwerk. Die ländlichen Arbeiter sollen sich aufs Jahr, die städtischen\n „auf offnem Markt“ verdingen. Es wird bei Gefängnissstrafe untersagt,\n höheren als den statutarischen Lohn zu zahlen, aber der Empfang höheren\n Lohns wird stärker bestraft als seine Zahlung. So wird auch noch in\n Sect. 18 und 19 des Lehrlingsstatuts von Elisabeth zehntägige Gefäng-\n nissstrafe über den verhängt, der höheren Lohn zahlt, dagegen einund-\n zwanzigtägige Gefängnissstrafe über den, der ihn nimmt. Das Statut von\n 1360 verschärft die Strafen und ermächtigt den Meister sogar, durch\n körperlichen Zwang Arbeit zum gesetzlichen Lohntarif zu erpressen.\n Alle Kombination, Verträge, Eide u. s. w., wodurch sich Maurer und Zim-\n merleute wechselseitig banden, werden für null und nichtig erklärt. Arbei-\n terkoalition wird als schweres Verbrechen behandelt vom 14. Jahrhun-\n dert bis 1825, dem Jahr der Abschaffung der Antikoalitionsgesetze. Der\n Geist des Arbeiterstatuts von 1349 und seiner Nachgeburten leuchtet hell\n daraus hervor, dass zwar ein Maximum des Arbeitslohns von\n Staatswegen diktirt wird, aber bei Leibe kein Minimum.\n\n\n\n\nIm 16. Jahrhundert hatte sich, wie man weiss, die Lage der Arbei-\n ter sehr verschlechtert. Der Geldlohn stieg, aber nicht im Verhältniss\n zur Depreciation des Geldes und dem entsprechenden Steigen der Waaren-\n preise. Der Lohn fiel also in der That. Dennoch dauerten die Gesetze\n zum Behuf seiner Herabdrückung fort zugleich mit dem Ohrenabschneiden und\n Brandmarken derjenigen, „die Niemand in Dienst nehmen wollte“.\n Durch das Lehrlingsstatut 5 Elisabeth 3 wurden die Friedensrichter\n [Running matter: 46*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"724\", \"facs\": \"#f0743\"} --> ermächtigt, gewisse Löhne festzusetzen und nach Jahreszeiten und Waaren-\n preisen zu modificiren. Jakob I. dehnte diese Arbeitsregulation auch auf\n Weber, Spinner und alle möglichen Arbeiterkategorieen aus [Note: Aus einer Klausel des Statuts 2, Jakob I., c. 6 ersieht man, dass gewisse\n Tuchmacher sich herausnahmen, als Friedensrichter die Löhne in ihren\n eigenen Werkstätten zu octroyiren. — In Deutschland waren namentlich nach dem\n dreissigjährigen Krieg Statuten zur Niederhaltung des Arbeitslohns häufig. „Sehr\n lästig war den Gutsherrn in dem menschenleeren Boden der Mangel an Dienst-\n boten und Arbeitern. Allen Dorfsassen wurde verboten, Kammern an ledige\n Männer und Frauen zu vermiethen, alle solche Inlieger sollten der Obrigkeit an-\n gezeigt und ins Gefängniss gesteckt werden, falls sie nicht Dienstboten\n werden wollten, auch wenn sie sich von andrer Thätigkeit erhielten, den Bauern\n um Taglohn säeten oder gar mit Geld und Getreide handelten. (Kaiserliche\n Privilegien und Sanctiones für Schlesien I, 125.) Durch ein ganzes\n Jahrhundert wird in den Verordnungen der Landesherrn immer wieder bittere\n Klage geführt über das boshafte und muthwillige Gesindel, das sich\n in die harten Bedingungen nicht fügen, mit dem gesetzlichen Lohn\n nicht zufrieden sein will; dem einzelnen Gutsherrn wird verboten, mehr zu\n geben als die Landschaft in einer Taxe festgesetzt hat. Und doch sind die Be-\n dingungen des Dienstes nach dem Krieg zuweilen noch besser, als sie 100 Jahre\n später waren; noch erhielt das Gesinde 1652 in Schlesien zweimal in der Woche\n Fleisch, noch in unsrem Jahrhundert hat es eben dort Kreise gegeben, wo sie es\n nur dreimal im Jahr erhielten. Auch der Tagelohn war nach dem Kriege höher\n als in den folgenden Jahrhunderten.“ (G. Freitag.)] , Georg II.\n die Gesetze gegen Arbeiterkoalition auf alle Manufakturen. In der eigent-\n lichen Manufakturperiode war die kapitalistische Produktionsweise hin-\n reichend erstarkt, um alle gesetzliche Regulation des Arbeitslohns eben so\n unausführbar als überflüssig zu machen, aber man liebte für den Nothfall\n das alte Arsenal offen zu halten. Noch 8 George II. verbot für Schnei-\n dergesellen in London und Umgegend mehr als 2 sh. 7½ d. Taglohn,\n ausser in Fällen allgemeiner Trauer, noch 13 George III. c. 68 überwies\n die Reglung des Arbeitslohns der Seidenwirker den Friedensrichtern, noch\n 1796 bedurfte es zweier Urtheile der höheren Gerichtshöfe zur Entschei-\n dung, ob friedensrichterliche Befehle über Arbeitslohn auch für Nicht-\n Agrikulturarbeiter gültig seien, noch 1799 bestätigte ein Parlamentsakt,\n dass der Lohn der Grubenarbeiter von Schottland durch ein Statut der\n Elisabeth und zwei schottische Akte von 1661 und 1671 regulirt sei.\n Wie sehr unterdess die Verhältnisse revolutionirt waren, bewies ein im\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"725\", \"facs\": \"#f0744\"} --> englischen Unterhaus unerhörter Vorfall. Hier, wo man seit mehr als\n 400 Jahren ausschliesslich Gesetze fabricirt hatte über das Maxi-\n mum, welches der Arbeitslohn platterdings nicht übersteigen dürfe, schlug\nWhitbread 1796 ein gesetzliches Lohnminimum für Agrikul-\n turarbeiter vor … Obgleich Pitt sich widersetzte, gab er zu, dass die\n „Lage der Armen grausam (cruel) sei“. Endlich, 1813, wurden die Gesetze\n über Lohnregulation abgeschafft. Sie waren eine lächerliche Ano-\n malie, seitdem der Kapitalist durch seine Privatgesetzgebung die\n Fabrik regulirte und durch die Armensteuer den Lohn des Landarbei-\n ters zum unentbehrlichen Minimum ergänzen liess. Die Bestimmungen\n der Arbeiterstatute über Kontrakte zwischen Meister und Lohnarbeiter,\n über Terminkündigung u. dergl., welche nur eine Civilklage gegen den\n kontraktbrüchigen Meister, aber Kriminalklage gegen den kontrakt-\n brüchigen Arbeiter erlauben, stehn bis zur Stunde in voller Blüthe.\n Die grausamen Gesetze gegen Koalition fielen 1825 vor der drohenden\n Haltung des Proletariats. Das Parlament gab sie nur widerwillig auf [Note: Die letzten Reste des Koalitionsgesetzes wurden erst 1859 beseitigt.] ,\n dasselbe Parlament, welches Jahrhunderte durch mit der cynischsten Unver-\n schämtheit als permanente Koalition der Kapitalisten gegen\n die Arbeiter funktionirt hatte.\n\n\n\n\nGleich im Beginn des Revolutionssturms wagte die französische Bour-\n geoisie das eben erst eroberte Associationsrecht den Arbeitern wieder zu\n entziehn. Durch Dekret vom 14. Juni 1791 erklärte sie alle Arbeiter-\nkoalition für ein „Attentat auf die Freiheit und die Er-\n klärung der Menschenrechte“, strafbar mit 500 Livres nebst\n einjähriger Entziehung der aktiven Bürgerrechte [Note: Artikel I dieses Gesetzes lautet: „L’anéantissement de toutes espèces de\ncorporations du même état et profession étant l’une des bases fondamentales\n de la constitution française, il est défendu de les rétablir de fait sous quelque\nprétexte et sous quelque forme que ce soit“. Artikel IV erklärt, dass wenn\n „des citoyens attachés aux mêmes professions, arts et métiers prenaient des déli-\n bérations, faisaient entre eux des conventions tendantes à réfuser de concert ou à\n n’accorder qu’ à un prix déterminé le secours de leur industrie ou de leurs travaux,\n les dites délibérations et conventions … seront déclarées inconstitution-\n nelles, attentatoires à la liberté et à la déclaration des droits de l’homme etc.“,\n also Staatsverbrechen, ganz wie in den alten Arbeiterstatuten. („Révolutions\n de Paris. Paris 1791“, t. III, p. 523.)] . Diess Gesetz,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"726\", \"facs\": \"#f0745\"} --> welches den Konkurrenzkampf zwischen Kapital und Arbeit staatspolizei-\n lich innerhalb der dem Kapital bequemen Schranken einzwängt, überlebte\n Revolutionen und Dynastiewechsel. Selbst die Schreckensregierung liess\n es unangetastet. Es ward erst ganz neulich aus dem Code Pénal ge-\n strichen. Nichts charakteristischer als der Vorwand dieses bürgerlichen\n Staatsstreichs. „Obgleich“, sagt Chapelier, der Berichterstatter,\n „es wünschenswerth, dass der Arbeitslohn höher steige als er jetzt steht,\n damit der, der ihn empfängt, ausserhalb der absoluten Abhängig-\n keit sei, welche die Entbehrung der nothwendigen Lebensmittel produ-\n cirt, und welche fast die Abhängigkeit der Sklaverei ist“,\n dürfen dennoch die Arbeiter sich nicht über ihre Interessen verständigen,\n gemeinsam handeln und dadurch ihre „absolute Abhängigkeit, welche fast\n Sklaverei ist“, mässigen, weil sie eben dadurch „die Freiheit ihrer\nci-devant maîtres, der jetzigen Unternehmer“, verletzen (die Frei-\n heit, die Arbeiter in der Sklaverei zu erhalten!), und weil eine Koali-\n tion gegen die Despotie der ehemaligen Meister der Cor-\n porationen — man rathe! — eine Herstellung der durch die\n französische Konstitution abgeschafften Corporationen ist! [Note: Buchezet Roux: „Histoire Parlamentaire“, t. X, p. 195.] \n\n\n\n\nNachdem wir die gewaltsame Schöpfung vogelfreier Proletarier betrach-\n tet, die blutige Disciplin, welche sie in Lohnarbeiter verwandelt, die schmutzige\n Haupt- und Staatsaktion, die mit dem Exploitationsgrad der Arbeit die Ac-\n cumulation des Kapitals polizeilich steigert, fragt sich, wo kommen die Kapi-\n talisten ursprünglich her? Denn die Expropriation des Landvolks schafft\nunmittelbar nur grosse Grundeigenthümer. Was die Genesis\n des Pächters betrifft, so können wir sie so zu sagen mit der Hand be-\n tappen, weil sie ein langsamer, über viele Jahrhunderte sich fortwälzen-\n der Prozess ist. Die Leibeignen selbst, woneben auch freie kleine Land-\n eigner, befanden sich in sehr verschiednen Besitzverhältnissen und wurden\n daher auch unter sehr verschiednen ökonomischen Bedingungen emancipirt.\n In England ist die erste Form des Pächters der selbst leibeigne Bailiff.\n Seine Stellung ist ähnlich der des altrömischen Villicus, nur in engerer\n Wirkungssphäre. Während der zweiten Hälfte des 14. Jahrhunderts wird\n er ersetzt durch einen freien Pächter, den der Landlord mit Samen, Vieh\n und Ackerwerkzeug versieht. Seine Lage ist nicht sehr verschieden von\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"727\", \"facs\": \"#f0746\"} --> der des Bauern. Nur beutet er mehr Lohnarbeit aus. Er wird bald Metair,\n Halbpächter. Er stellt einen Theil des Ackerbaukapitals, der Landlord\n den andern. Beide theilen das Gesammtprodukt in kontraktlich be-\n stimmter Proportion. Diese Form verschwindet in England rasch, um\n der des eigentlichen Pächters Platz zu machen, welcher sein eignes Ka-\n pital durch Anwendung von Lohnarbeitern verwerthet und einen Theil des\n Surplusprodukts, in Geld oder in natura, dem Landlord als Grundrente\n zahlt. So lange, während des 15. Jahrhunderts, der unabhängige Bauer\n und der neben dem Lohndienst zugleich selbstwirthschaftende Ackerknecht\n sich selbst durch ihre Arbeit bereichern, bleiben die Umstände des Päch-\n ters und sein Produktionsfeld gleich mittelmässig. Die Agrikulturrevolu-\n tion im letzten Drittheil des 15. Jahrhunderts, die fast während des\n ganzen 16. Jahrhunderts (jedoch mit Ausnahme seiner letzten Decennien)\n fortwährt, bereichert ihn eben so rasch als sie das Landvolk verarmt [Note: „Pächter“, sagt Harrison in seiner „Description of England“,\n „denen es früher schwer ward 4 Pfd. St. Rente zu zahlen, zahlen jetzt 40, 50,\n 100 Pfd. St. und glauben doch ein schlechtes Geschäft gemacht zu haben, wenn\n sie nach Ablauf ihres Pachtkontrakts nicht 6—7 Jahre Rente zurücklegen.“] .\n Die Usurpation von Gemeindeweiden u. s. w. erlaubt ihm grosse Vermeh-\n rung seines Viehstands fast ohne Kosten, während ihm das Vieh reich-\n lichere Düngungsmittel zur Bestellung des Bodens liefert. Im 16. Jahrh.\n kommt ein entscheidend wichtiges Moment hinzu. Damals waren die Pacht-\n kontrakte lang, oft für 99 Jahre laufend. Die kontinuirliche Depreciation\n der edlen Metalle und daher des Geldes trug dem Pächter goldne Früchte.\n Von allen andren, früher erörterten Umständen abgesehn, senkte sie den\nArbeitslohn. Ein Bruchstück desselben wurde dem Pacht-\n profit annexirt. Das fortwährende Steigen der Preise von Korn,\n Wolle, Fleisch, kurz sämmtlicher Agrikulturprodukte, schwellte das Geld-\n kapital des Pächters ohne sein Zuthun, während die Grundrente, die er zu\n zahlen hatte, im veralteten Geldwerth kontrahirt war. So berei-\n cherte er sich gleichzeitig auf Kosten seiner Lohnarbeiter und seines Land-\n lords. Kein Wunder also, wenn England Ende des 16. Jahrhunderts eine\n Klasse für die damaligen Verhältnisse reicher „Kapitalpächter“ besass [Note: In Frankreich wird der Régisseur, der Verwalter und Eintreiber der\n Leistungen an den Feudalherrn während des früheren Mittelalters, bald ein] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"728\", \"facs\": \"#f0747\"} -->\n\nDie stossweise und stets erneuerte Expropriation und Verjagung des\n Landvolks lieferte, wie man sah, der städtischen Industrie wieder und\n wieder Massen ganz ausserhalb der Zunftverhältnisse stehender Proletarier,\n ein weiser Umstand, der den alten Anderson (nicht zu verwechseln mit\n James Anderson) in seiner Handelsgeschichte an direkte Intervention der\n Vorsehung glauben lässt. Wir müssen noch einen Augenblick bei diesem\n Element der ursprünglichen Accumulation verweilen. Der Ver-\n dünnung des unabhängigen, selbstwirthschaftenden Landvolks entsprach\n nicht nur die Verdichtung des industriellen Proletariats, wie Geoffroy\n Saint-Hilaire die Verdichtung der Weltmaterie hier durch ihre Verdünnung\n dort erklärt [Note: In seinen „Notions de Philosophie Naturelle. Paris 1838.“] . Trotz der verminderten Zahl seiner Bebauer trug der\n Boden nach wie vor gleich viel oder mehr Produkt, weil die Revolution in\n den Grundeigenthumsverhältnissen von verbesserten Methoden der Kultur,\n grösserer Cooperation, Koncentration der Produktionsmittel u. s. w. be-\n gleitet war, und weil die ländlichen Lohnarbeiter nicht nur intensiver ange-\n spannt wurden [Note: Ein Punkt, den Sir James Steuart betont.] , sondern auch das Produktionsfeld, worauf sie für sich\n [Note: homme d’affaires, der sich durch Erpressung, Prellerei u. s. w. zum Kapi-\n talisten hinaufschwindelt. Diese Régisseurs waren manchmal selbst vornehme\n Herrn. Z. B.: „C’est li compte que messire Jacques de Thoraiue, chevalier\n chastelain sor Besançon rent es seigneur tenant les comptes à Dijon pour mon-\n seigneur le duc et comte de Bourgoigne, des rentes appartenant à la dite chastel-\n lenie, depuis XXVe jour de décembre MCCCLIX jusqu’au XXVIIIe jour de dé-\n cembre MCCCLX.“ (Alexis Monteil: „Histoire des Matériaux\n manuscrits etc.“, p. 244.) Wie in England, so waren in Frankreich die grossen\n Feudalterritorien in unendlich vicle kleine Wirthschaften getheilt, aber unter un-\n gleich ungünstigeren Bedingungen für das Landvolk. Während des 14. Jahrhun-\n derts kamen die Pachten, fermes oder terriers auf. Ihre Zahl wuchs bestän-\n dig, weit über 100,000. Sie zahlten eine vom 12. bis zum 5. Theil des Produkts\n wechselnde Grundrente in Geld oder in natura. Die terriers waren Lehn, Hinter-\n lehn u. s. w. (fiefs, arrière-fiefs), je nach Werth und Umfang der Domänen, wovon\n manche nur wenige arpents zählten. Alle diese terriers besassen Gerichtsbarkeit\n in irgend einem Grad, es gab vier Grade, über die Bodeninsassen. Man begreift\n den Druck des Landvolks unter allen diesen kleinen Tyrannen. Monteil sagt, dass\n es damals 160,000 Gerichte in Frankreich gab, wo heute 4000 Tribunale (Frie-\n densgerichte eingeschlossen) genügen.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"729\", \"facs\": \"#f0748\"} --> selbst arbeiteten, mehr und mehr zusammenschmolz. Mit dem freigesetzten\n Theil des Landvolks werden also auch seine früheren Nahrungsmit-\n tel freigesetzt. Sie verwandeln sich jetzt in stoffliches Element des\nvariablen Kapitals. Der an die Luft gesetzte Bauer muss ihren Werth\n von seinem neuen Herrn, dem industriellen Kapitalisten, in der Form des\n Arbeitslohns erkaufen. Wie mit den Lebensmitteln, verhielt es sich mit\n dem heimischen agrikolen Rohmaterial der Industrie. Es ver-\n wandelte sich in ein Element des constanten Kapitals. Man unter-\n stelle z. B. einen Theil der westphälischen Bauern, die zu Friedrich’s II.\n Zeit alle Flachs, wenn auch keine Seide spannen, gewaltsam expropriirt und\n von Grund und Boden verjagt, den andern zurückbleibenden Theil aber\n in Taglöhner grosser Pächter verwandelt. Gleichzeitig erheben sich\n grosse Flachsspinnereien und Webereien, worin die „Freigesetzten“ nun\n lohnarbeiten. Der Flachs sieht grad aus wie vorher. Keine Fiber an\n ihm ist verändert, aber eine neue sociale Seele ist ihm in den Leib ge-\n fahren. Er bildet jetzt einen Theil des constanten Kapitals der\n Manufakturherrn. Früher vertheilt unter eine Unmasse kleiner Producen-\n ten, die ihn selbst bauten und in kleinen Portionen mit ihren Familien ver-\n spannen, ist er jetzt koncentrirt in der Hand eines Kapitalisten, der andre für\n sich spinnen und weben lässt. Die in der Flachsspinnerei verausgabte Extra-\n arbeit realisirte sich früher in Extraeinkommen zahlloser Bauernfamilien\n oder auch, zu Friedrich’s II. Zeit, in Steuern pour le roi de Prusse. Sie\n realisirt sich jetzt im Profit weniger Kapitalisten. Die Spindeln und\n Webstühle, früher vertheilt über das flache Land, sind jetzt in wenigen\n grossen Arbeitskasernen zusammengerückt, wie die Arbeiter, wie das Roh-\n material. Und Spindeln und Webstühle und Rohmaterial sind aus Mitteln\n unabhängiger Existenz für Spinner und Weber selbst verwandelt in Mit-\n tel sie zu kommandiren [Note: „Je permettrai“, sagt der Kapitalist, „que vous ayez l’honneur de me\n servir, à condition que vous me donnez le peu qui vous reste pour la peine que je\n prends de vous commander.“ (J. J. Rousseau: „Discours sur l’Éco-\n nomie Politique.“)]  und ihnen unbezahlte Arbeit auszusaugen.\n Den grossen Manufakturen sieht man es nicht an, wie den grossen Pach-\n ten, dass sie aus vielen kleinen Produktionsstätten zusammenge-\n schlagen und durch die Expropriation vieler kleiner unabhängiger\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"730\", \"facs\": \"#f0749\"} --> Producenten gebildet sind. Jedoch lässt sich die unbefangne Anschauung\n nicht beirren. Zur Zeit Mirabeau’s, des Revolutionslöwen, hiessen die\n grossen Manufakturen noch manufactures réunies, zusammenge-\n schlagene Werkstätten, wie wir von zusammengeschlagenen Aeckern\n sprechen. „Man sieht nur“, sagt Mirabeau, „die grossen Manufak-\n turen, wo Hunderte von Menschen unter einem Direktor arbeiten,\n und die man gewöhnlich vereinigte Manufakturen (manufactures\n réunies) nennt. Diejenigen dagegen, wo eine sehr grosse Anzahl Arbei-\n ter zersplittert und jeder für seine eigne Rechnung arbeitet, werden kaum\n eines Blicks gewürdigt. Man stellt sie ganz in den Hintergrund. Diess\n ist ein sehr grosser Irrthum, denn sie allein bilden einen wirklich wich-\n tigen Bestandtheil des Volksreichthums … Die vereinigte Fabrik (fabrique\n réunie) wird einen oder zwei Unternehmer wunderbar bereichern, aber die\n Arbeiter sind nur besser oder schlechter bezahlte Taglöhner und nehmen\n in Nichts am Wohlsein des Unternehmers Theil. In der getrennten\n Fabrik (fabrique séparée) dagegen wird Niemand reich, aber eine Menge\n Arbeiter befindet sich im Wohlstand … Die Zahl der fleissigen und wirth-\n schaftlichen Arbeiter wird wachsen, weil sie in weiser Lebensart, in Thätig-\n keit ein Mittel erblicken, ihre Lage wesentlich zu verbessern, statt eine kleine\n Lohnerhöhung zu gewinnen, die niemals ein wichtiger Gegenstand für die\n Zukunft sein kann, sondern die Leute höchstens befähigt etwas besser von der\n Hand in den Mund zu leben. Die getrennten individuellen Manufak-\n turen, meist mit kleiner Landwirthschaft verbunden, sind die freien“ [Note: Mirabeau l. c. t. III, p. 20—109 passim. Wenn Mirabeau die zer-\n splitterten Werkstätten auch für ökonomischer und produktiver hält, als die „ver-\n einigten“, und in letzteren bloss künstliche Treibhauspflanzen unter der Pflege der\n Staatsregierungen sieht, erklärt sich das aus dem damaligen Zustand eines grossen\n Theils der kontinentalen Manufakturen.] .\n Die Expropriation und Verjagung eines Theils des Landvolks setzt mit\n den Arbeitern nicht nur ihre Lebensmittel und ihr Arbeitsma-\n terial für das industrielle Kapital frei, sie schafft den inneren\n Markt [Note: „Twenty pounds of wool converted unobtrusively into the yearly clothing\n of a labourer’s family by its own industry in the intervals of other work — this\n makes no show; but bring it to market, send it to the factory, thence to the broker,] . Der Pächter verkauft nun als Waare und massenhaft Lebens-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"731\", \"facs\": \"#f0750\"} --> mittel und Rohmaterial, die früher grossentheils von ihren ländlichen Produ-\n centen und Verarbeitern als unmittelbare Subsistenzmittel verzehrt\n wurden. Die Manufakturen liefern ihm den Markt. Andrerseits koncen-\n triren sich nicht nur die vielen zerstreuten Kunden, die von den vielen kleinen\n Producenten ihre lokale Detailzufuhr bezogen, in einen grossen Markt\n für das industrielle Kapital; ein grosser Theil der früher auf dem\n Land selbst producirten Artikel wird in Manufakturartikel verwan-\n delt, und das Land selbst in einen Markt für ihren Verkauf. Hand\n in Hand mit der Expropriation und Losscheidung früher selbstwirthschaf-\n tender Bauern von ihren Produktionsmitteln geht so die Vernichtung der\n ländlichen Nebenindustrie, der Scheidungsprocess von\n Manufaktur und Agrikultur. Jedoch bringt es die eigentliche Manu-\n fakturperiode zu keiner radikalen Umgestaltung. Man erinnert sich, dass sie\n sich der nationalen Produktion nur sehr stückweis bemächtigt und immer auf\n städtischem Handwerk und häuslich-ländlicher Nebenindustrie als\n breitem Hintergrund ruht. Wenn sie letztere unter einer Form, in besondern\n Geschäftszweigen, auf gewissen Punkten vernichtet, ruft sie dieselbe auf an-\n dern wieder hervor, weil sie derselben zur Bearbeitung des Rohmaterials\n bis zu einem bestimmten Grad hedarf. Sie producirt daher eine neue\n Klasse kleiner Landleute, welche die Bodenbestellung als Neben-\n zweig und die industrielle Arbeit zum Verkauf des Produkts an die Manu-\n faktur, direkt, oder auf dem Umweg des Kaufmanns, als Hauptgeschäft\n treiben. Diess ist ein Grund, wenn auch nicht der Hauptgrund, eines\n Phänomens, welches den Forscher der englischen Geschichte zunächst\n verwirrt. Vom letzten Drittheil des 15. Jahrhunderts an findet er fort-\n laufende, nur in gewissen Intervallen unterbrochne Klage über die zu-\n nehmende Kapitalwirthschaft auf dem Land und die progressive Vernich-\n tung der Bauernschaft. Andrerseits findet er sie stets wieder von neuem\n vor, wenn auch in verminderter Zahl und unter stets verschlechterter\n [Note: thence to the dealer, and you will have great commercial operations, and\n nominal capital engaged to the amount of twenty times its value … The\n working class is thus emerced to support a wretched factory population, a\n parasitical shopkeeping class, and a fictitious commercial, monetary and financial\n system.“ (David Urquhart l. c. p. 120.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"732\", \"facs\": \"#f0751\"} --> Form [Note: Ausnahme bildet hier Cromwell’s Zeit. So lange die Republik währte,\n erhob sich die englische Volksmasse in allen Schichten aus der Degradation, wozu\n sie unter den Tudors gesunken war.] . Der Hauptgrund ist: England ist vorzugsweise bald Korn-\n bauer, bald Viehzüchter, in Wechselperioden, und mit diesen Schwan-\n kungen, die bald nach mehr als halben Jahrhunderten zählen, bald nach\n wenigen Decennien, schwankt der Umfang des bäuerlichen Betriebs. Erst\n die grosse Industrie liefert der kapitalistischen Agri-\n kultur mit der Maschinerie die constante Grundlage,\n expropriirt radikal die ungeheure Mehrzahl des Land-\n volks und vollendet die Scheidung des Ackerbaus von\n der häuslich ländlichen Industrie, deren Wurzel sie aus-\n reisst — Spinnerei und Weberei [Note: „Aus den eigentlichen Manufakturen und der Zerstörung der\n ländlichen oder häuslichen Manufaktur geht, mit Einführung der\n Maschinerie, die grosse Wollindustrie hervor.“ (Tuckett l. c. v. I,\n p. 144.) „Der Pflug, das Joch waren die Erfindung von Göttern und die Be-\n schäftigung von Heroen; sind Webstuhl, Spindel und Spinnrad minder edler Ab-\n kunft? Ihr trennt das Spinnrad und den Pflug, die Spindel und das Joch, und\n erhaltet Fabriken und Armenhäuser, Kredit und Paniks, zwei feindliche Nationen,\n agrikole und kommercielle.“ (David Urquhart l. c. p. 122.) Nun kömmt\n aber Carey, und klagt, sicher nicht mit Unrecht, England an, dass es jedes\n andre Land in ein blosses Agrikulturvolk zu verwandeln strebt, dessen Fabrikant\n England. Er behauptet, in dieser Art sei die Türkei ruinirt worden, weil „den\n Eignern und Bebauern des Bodens niemals gestattet war (von England) sich selbst\n zu kräftigen durch die natürliche Allianz zwischen dem Pflug und dem Webstuhl,\n dem Hammer und der Egge.“ („The Slave Trade“, p. 125.) Nach ihm ist\nUrquhart selbst einer der Hauptagenten des Ruins der Türkei, wo er im englischen\n Interesse Freihandelspropaganda gemacht habe. Das Beste ist, dass Carey,\n nebenbei grosser Russenknecht, durch das Protektionssystem jenen Scheidungs-\n prozess, den es beschleunigt, verhindern will.] . Sie erobert daher auch erst\n dem industriellen Kapital den ganzen inneren Markt [Note: Die philanthropischen englischen Oekonomen wie Mill, Rogers, Godwin\n Smith, Fawcett u. s. w., und liberale Fabrikanten, wie John Bright und Kons.,\n fragen, wie Gott den Kain nach seinem Bruder Abel, so den englischen Grund-\n aristokraten, wo sind unsre Tausende von Freeholders hingekommen? Aber wo\n seid ihr denn hergekommen? Aus der Vernichtung jener Freeholders. Warum\n fragt ihr nicht weiter, wo sind die unabhängigen Weber, Spinner, Handwerker\n hingekommen?] .\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"733\", \"facs\": \"#f0752\"} -->\n\nDie Genesis des industriellen [Note: Industriell hier im Gegensatz zu agrikol. Im „kategorischen“\n Sinn ist der Pächter ein industrieller Kapitalist so gut wie der Fabrikant.] Kapitalisten ging nicht in\n derselben allmählichen Weise vor wie die des Pächters. Zweifelsohne\n verwandelten sich manche kleine Zunftmeister und noch mehr selbstständige\n kleine Handwerker oder auch Lohnarbeiter in kleine Kapitalisten und durch\n allmählich ausgedehntere Exploitation von Lohnarbeit und entsprechende\n Accumulation in Kapitalisten sans phrase. In der Kindheitsperiode der\n kapitalistischen Produktion ging’s vielfach zu wie in der Kindheitsperiode\n des mittelaltrigen Städtewesens, wo die Frage, wer von den entlaufenen\n Leibeigenen soll Meister sein und wer Diener, grossentheils durch das\n frühere oder spätere Datum ihrer Flucht entschieden wurde. Indess ent-\n sprach der Schneckengang dieser Methode in keiner Weise den Handelsbe-\n dürfnissen des neuen Weltmarkts, welchen die grossen Entdeckungen Ende\n des 15. Jahrh. geschaffen hatten. Aber das Mittelalter hatte zwei\n verschiedne Formen des Kapitals überliefert, die in den verschie-\n densten ökonomischen Gesellschaftsformationen reifen und, vor der Aera der\n kapitalistischen Produktionsweise, als Kapital quand même gelten —\n das Wucherkapital [Note: „Gegenwärtig geht aller Reichthum der Gesellschaft erst in die Hand des\n Kapitalisten … er zahlt dem Grundeigenthümer die Rente, dem Arbeiter den\n Lohn, dem Steuer- und Zehntenkollektor ihre Ansprüche, und behält einen\n grossen, in der That den grössten und täglich anwachsenden Theil des jährlichen\n Produkts der Arbeit für sich selbst. Der Kapitalist kann jetzt als der Eigner des\n ganzen gesellschaftlichen Reichthums in erster Hand betrachtet werden, obgleich\n kein Gesetz ihm das Recht auf diess Eigenthum übertragen hat … Dieser\n Wechsel im Eigenthum wurde durch den Prozess des Wuchers bewirkt: und es\n ist nicht wenig merkwürdig, dass die Gesetzgeber von ganz Europa diess durch Ge-\n setze wider den Wucher präveniren wollten … Die Macht des Kapitalisten über allen\n Reichthum des Landes ist eine vollständige Revolution im Eigenthums-\n recht, und durch welches Gesetz, oder welche Reihe von Gesetzen wurde sie be-\n wirkt?“ („The Natural and Artificial Rights of Property Contrasted.\n Lond. 1832“, p. 98, 99. Verfasser der anonymen Schrift: Th. Hodgskin.)]  und das Kaufmannskapital. Das durch\n Wucher und Handel gebildete Geldkapital wurde durch die Feudal-\n verfassung auf dem Land, durch die Zunftverfassung in den Städten an\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"734\", \"facs\": \"#f0753\"} -->seiner Verwandlung in industrielles Kapital behindert [Note: Sogar noch 1794 schickten die kleinen Tuchmacher von Leeds eine\n Deputation an das Parlament, zur Petition um ein Gesetz, das jedem Kaufmann\n verbieten sollte, Fabrikant zu werden.“ (Dr. Aikin l. c.)] .\n Diese Schranken fielen mit der Auflösung der feudalen Gefolgschaften, mit der\n Expropriation und theilweisen Verjagung des Landvolks. Die neue Manu-\n faktur ward in See-Exporthäfen errichtet oder auf Punkten des flachen Lan-\n des, ausserhalb der Kontrole des alten Städtewesens und seiner Zunftverfas-\n sung. In England daher erbitterter Kampf der incorporated towns\n gegen diese neuen industriellen Pflanzschulen.\n\n\n\n\nDie Entdeckung der Gold- und Silberländer in Amerika, die Ausrot-\n tung, Versklavung, und Vergrabung der eingebornen Bevölkerung in die\n Bergwerke, die Eroberung und Ausplünderung von Ostindien, die Ver-\n wandlung von Afrika in ein Geheg zur Handelsjagd auf Schwarzhäute,\n bezeichnen die Morgenröthe der kapitalistischen Produktionsära. Diese\n idyllischen Prozesse sind Hauptmomente der ursprünglichen Accu-\n mulation. Auf dem Fuss folgt der Handelskrieg der europäischen Na-\n tionen, mit dem Erdrund als Schauplatz. Er beginnt mit dem Abfall der\n Niederlande von Spanien, nimmt Riesendimensionen an in Englands Anti-\n jakobinerkrieg, spielt noch fort in den Opiumkriegen gegen China u. s. w.\n\n\n\n\nDiese Methoden der ursprünglichen Accumulation vertheilen\n sich mehr oder minder, in zeitlicher Reihenfolge, namentlich auf Spanien,\n Portugal, Holland, Frankreich und England. In England werden sie Ende\n des 17. Jahrh. systematisch zusammengefasst im Kolonialsystem,\n Staatsschuldensystem, modernen Steuersystem und Pro-\n tektionssystem. Diese Methoden beruhn zum Theil auf brutalster\n Gewalt, wie das Kolonialsystem. Alle aber benutzen die Staatsmacht,\n die koncentrirte und organisirte Gewalt der Gesellschaft, um den Verwand-\n lungsprozess der feudalen in die kapitalistische Produktionsweise treibhaus-\n mässig zu beschleunigen und die Uebergänge abzukürzen. Die Gewalt\n ist der Geburtshelfer jeder alten Gesellschaft, die mit einer\n neuen schwanger geht. Sie ist selbst eine ökonomische Potenz.\n\n\n\n\nVon dem christlichen Kolonialsystem sagt ein Mann, der aus dem\n Christenthum eine Specialität macht, W. Howitt: „Die Barbareien und\n ruchlosen Greuelthaten der s. g. christlichen Racen, in jeder Region der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"735\", \"facs\": \"#f0754\"} --> Welt und gegen jedes Volk, das sie unterjochen konnten, finden keine\n Parallele in irgend einer Aera der Weltgeschichte, bei irgend einer Race,\n ob noch so wild, ungebildet, mitleidslos und schamlos“ [Note: William Howitt: „Colonization and Christianity. A\n Popular History of the Treatment of the Natives by the Euro-\n peansin all their colonies. Lond. 1838“, p. 9. Ueber die Behandlung\n der Sklaven gute Compilation bei Charles Comte: „Traité de la Legis-\n lation.“ 3me éd. Bruxelles 1837. Man muss diess Zeug im Detail studiren,\n um zu sehn, wozu der Bourgeois sich selbst und den Arbeiter macht, wo er die\n Welt ungenirt nach seinem Bilde modeln kann.] . Die Ge-\n schichte der holländischen Kolonialwirthschaft — und Holland war die\n kapitalistische Musternation des 17. Jahrhunderts — „entrollt ein unüber-\n treffbares Gemälde von Verrath, Bestechung, Meuchelmord und Nieder-\n tracht“ [Note: Thomas Stafford Raffles, late Lieut. Gov. of that island\n (Java) and its dependencies. Lond. 1817.] . Nichts charakteristischer als ihr System des Menschendieb-\n stahls in Celebes, um Sklaven für Java zu erhalten. Die Menschenstehler\n wurden zu diesem Zweck abgerichtet. Der Dieb, der Dolmetscher und\n der Verkäufer waren die Hauptagenten in diesem Handel, eingeborne Prin-\n zen die Hauptverkäufer. Die weggestohlne Jugend wurde in den Geheimge-\n fängnissen von Celebes versteckt, bis reif zur Verschickung auf die Sklaven-\n schiffe. Ein officieller Bericht sagt: „Diese eine Stadt von Makassar z. B.\n ist voll von geheimen Gefängnissen, eins schauderhafter als das andre, ge-\n pfropft mit Elenden, Opfern der Habsucht und Tyrannei, in Ketten gefes-\n selt, ihren Familien gewaltsam entrissen.“ Um sich Malacca’s zu bemäch-\n tigen, bestachen die Holländer den portugiesischen Gouverneur. Er liess\n sie 1641 in die Stadt ein. Sie eilten sofort zu seinem Hause und meuchel-\n mordeten ihn, um auf die Zahlung der Bestechungssumme von 21,875 Pfd. St.\n zu „entsagen“. Wo sie die Füsse hinsetzten, folgte Verödung und\n Entvölkerung. Die Population einer Provinz von Java, Baniyuawngy,\n zählte 1750 über 80,000 Einwohner, 1811 nur noch 8000. Das ist der\ndoux commerce!\n\n\n\n\nDie englisch-ostindische Kompagnie erhielt bekanntlich,\n ausser der politischen Herrschaft in Ostindien, das exklusive Monopol des\n Theehandels, wie des chinesischen Handels überhaupt und des Gütertransports\n von und zu Europa. Aber die Küstenschifffahrt von Indien und zwischen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"736\", \"facs\": \"#f0755\"} --> den Inseln, wie der Handel im Innern Indiens wurden Monopol der höhern\n Beamten der Kompagnie. Die Monopole von Salz, Opium, Betel und\n andern Waaren waren unerschöpfliche Minen des Reichthums. Die Beamten\n selbst setzten die Preise fest und schindeten nach Belieben den unglück-\n lichen Hindu. Der Generalgouverneur nahm Theil an diesem Privathan-\n del. Seine Günstlinge erhielten Kontrakte unter Bedingungen, wodurch sie,\n klüger als die Alchymisten, aus Nichts Gold machten. Grosse Vermö-\n gen sprangen wie die Pilze an einem Tage auf, die ursprüngliche\n Accumulation ging von Statten ohne Vorschuss eines Schillings. Die\n gerichtliche Verfolgung des Warren Hastings wimmelt von solchen Bei-\n spielen. Hier ein Fall. Ein Opiumkontrakt wird einem gewissen Sullivan\n zugetheilt, obgleich er in öffentlichem Auftrag zu einem von den Opium-\n distrikten ganz entlegenen Theil Indiens reiste. Sullivan verkauft seinen\n Kontrakt für 40,000 Pfd. St. an einen gewissen Binn, Binn verkauft ihn\n seinerseits denselben Tag für 60,000 Pfd. St., und der schliessliche Käufer\n und Ausführer des Kontrakts erklärt, dass er hinterher noch einen unge-\n heuren Gewinn herausschlug. Nach einer dem Parlament vorgelegten\n Liste liessen sich die Kompagnie und ihre Beamten von 1757—1766 von\n den Indiern 6 Millionen Pfd. St. schenken! Zwischen 1769 und 1770\n fabricirten die Engländer eine Hungersnoth durch den Aufkauf von allem Reis\n und durch Weigerung des Wiederverkaufs ausser zu fabelhaften Preisen [Note: Im Jahr 1866 starben in der einzigen Provinz Orissa mehr als eine\n Million Hindus am Hungertod. Die Hungersnoth währt bis zu diesem\n Augenblick. Nichtsdestoweniger suchte man die indische Staatskasse zu berei-\n chern durch die Preise, wozu man den Verhungernden Lebensmittel abliess.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie Behandlung der Eingeborenen war natürlich am tollsten in den\n nur zum Exporthandel bestimmten Pflanzungen, wie Westindien, und in\n den dem Raubmord preisgegebnen reichen und dichtbevölkerten Ländern,\n wie Mexico und Ostindien. Jedoch auch in den eigentlichen Kolonien verläug-\n nete sich der christliche Charakter der ursprünglichen Accumula-\n tion nicht. Jene nüchternen Virtuosen des Protestantismus, die Puritaner,\n setzten durch Beschlüsse ihrer Assembly 1703 eine Prämie von 40\n Pfd. St. auf jedes indianische Scalp und jede gefangne Rothhaut, 1720\n Prämie von 100 Pfd. St. auf jedes Scalp, 1744, nachdem Massachussets-\n Bay einen gewissen Tribus zum Rebellen erklärt hatte, folgende Preise:\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"737\", \"facs\": \"#f0756\"} --> für männliches Scalp, 12 Jahre und drüber, 100 Pfd. St. neuer Währung,\n für männliche Gefangene 105 Pfd. St., für gefangene Weiber und Kinder\n 55 Pfd. St., für Scalps von Weibern und Kindern 50 Pfd. St.!\n Einige Decennien später rächte sich das Kolonialsystem an der unterdess\n aufrührisch gewordenen Nachkommenschaft der frommen pilgrim fathers.\n Unter englischem Antrieb und Sold wurden sie tomahawked. Das\n britische Parlament erklärte Bluthunde und Scalpiren für „Mittel, welche\n Gott und die Natur in seine Hand gegeben.“\n\n\n\n\nDas Kolonialsystem reifte treibhausmässig Handel und Schifffahrt.\n Die „Gesellschaften Monopolia“ (Luther) waren gewaltige Hebel der Ka-\n pital-Koncentration. Den aufschiessenden Manufakturen sicherte die Kolonie\n Absatzmarkt und eine durch das Marktmonopol potenzirte Accumulation.\n Der ausserhalb Europa direkt erplünderte, herausgesklavte und herausge-\n mordete Schatz floss ins Mutterland zurück und verwandelte sich hier in\nKapital. Holland, welches das Kolonialsystem zuerst völlig entwickelte,\n stand schon 1648 im Brennpunkt seiner Handelsgrösse. Es war „in fast\n ausschliesslichem Besitz des ostindischen Handels und des Verkehrs zwi-\n schen dem europäischen Südwesten und Nordosten. Seine Fischereien,\n Seewesen, Manufakturen übertrafen die eines jeden anderen Landes. Die\n Kapitalien der Republik waren vielleicht bedeutender als die des übrigen\n Europa insgesammt.“ Jülich vergisst hinzuzusetzen: Hollands Volks-\n masse war schon 1648 mehr überarbeitet, verarmter und brutaler unter-\n drückt als die des übrigen Europa’s insgesammt. Das Kolonialsystem\n warf mit einem Schub und Bautz alle alten Götzen über Haufen. Es pro-\n klamirte die Plusmacherei als letzten und einzigen Zweck der Menschheit.\n Es war die Geburtsstätte des modernen Staatsschulden- und Kre-\n ditsystems.\n\n\n\n\nDie auffallende Rolle des Staatsschulden- und modernen Steuer-\n systems bei der Verwandlung des gesellschaftlichen Reich-\n thums in Kapital, der Expropriation selbstständiger Arbeiter, und der\n Herunterdrückung der Lohnarbeiter, hat manche Schriftsteller, wie W. Cob-\n bett, Doubleday u. s. w. verleitet dort den Grund alles modernen Volkselends zu\n suchen. Mit den Staatsschulden entsprang zugleich ein internationales Kredit-\n wesen, welches oft die Quelle der ursprünglichen Accumulation\n in einem bestimmten Land versteckt. Die Gemeinheiten des venetianischen\n Raubsystems z. B. bilden eine verborgne Grundlage des Kapitalreichthums\n [Running matter: I. 47] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"738\", \"facs\": \"#f0757\"} --> von Holland, dem das verfallende Venedig grosse Geldsummen lieh.\n Ebenso verhält es sich zwischen Holland und England. Schon im Anfang\n des 18. Jahrhunderts sind die Manufakturen Hollands weit überflügelt und\n hat es aufgehört, herrschende Industrie- und Handelsnation zu sein. Eins\n seiner Hauptgeschäfte von 1701—1776 wird daher das Ausleihn unge-\n heurer Kapitalien, speziell an seinen übermächtigen Konkurrenten Eng-\n land. Aehnliches gilt jetzt von England und den Vereinigten Staaten.\n Manch Kapital, das heute in den Vereinigten Staaten ohne Geburtsschein\n auftritt, ist erst gestern in England kapitalisirtes Kinderblut.\n\n\n\n\nDas Protektions system war ein Kunstmittel Fabrikanten zu\n fabriciren, unabhängige Arbeiter zu expropriiren, die nationa-\n len Produktions- und Lebensmittel zu kapitalisiren, den Ueber-\n gangaus der alterthümlichen in die moderne Produktionsweise\n gewaltsam abzukürzen. Die europäischen Staaten rissen sich um das\n Patent dieser Erfindung, und einmal in den Dienst der Plusmacher eingetreten,\n brandschatzten sie zu jenem Behuf nicht nur das eigne Volk, indirekt durch\n Schutzzölle, direkt durch Exportprämien u. s. w. In den abhängigen Neben-\n landen wurde alle Industrie gewaltsam ausgerodet, wie z. B. die irische Woll-\n manufaktur von England. Auf dem europäischen Kontinent ward nach Col-\n bert’s Vorgang der Prozess noch sehr vereinfacht. Das ursprüngliche\n Kapital des Industriellen fliesst hier zum Theil direkt aus dem\n Staatsschatz. „Warum,“ ruft Mirabeau, „so weit die Ursache des Ma-\n nufakturglanzes Sachsens vor dem siebenjährigen Krieg suchen gehn? 180\n Millionen Staatsschulden“ [Note: „Pourquoi aller chercher si loin la cause de l’éclat manufacturier de la\n Saxe avant la guerre? Cent quatre-vingt millions de dettes faites par les souve-\n rains!“ Mirabeau l. c. t. VI, p. 101.] !\n\n\n\n\nKolonialsystem, Staatsschulden, Steuerwucht, Protektion, Handels-\n kriege u. s. w., diese Sprösslinge der eigentlichen Manufakturperiode,\n schwellen riesenhaft während der Kinderperiode der grossen Industrie.\n Die Geburt der letzteren wird gefeiert durch den grossen herodischen Kin-\n derraub. So blasirt Sir F. M. Eden ist über die Greuel der Expropria-\n tion des Landvolks von Grund und Boden seit dem letzten Drittel des\n 15. Jahrhunderts bis zu seiner Zeit, dem Ende des 18. Jahrhunderts; so\n selbstgefällig er gratulirt zu diesem Prozess, „nothwendig“, um die kapi-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"739\", \"facs\": \"#f0758\"} --> talistische Agrikultur und „das wahre Verhältniss von Ackerland und Vieh-\n weide herzustellen“, beweist er dagegen nicht dieselbe ökonomische Einsicht\n in die Nothwendigkeit des Kinderraubs und der Kindersklaverei\n für die Verwandlung des Manufakturbetriebs in den Fabrikbetrieb und die\n Herstellung des wahren Verhältnisses von Kapital und Arbeits-\n kraft. Er sagt: „Es mag vielleicht der Erwägung des Publikums werth sein,\n ob irgend eine Manufaktur, die zu ihrer erfolgreichen Ausführung Cottages\n und Workhouses von armen Kindern ausplündern muss, damit sie, truppweis\n sich ablösend, den grössten Theil der Nacht durch abgerackert und der Ruhe\n beraubt werden, eine Manufaktur, die ausserdem Haufen beiderlei Geschlechts,\n von verschiednen Altersstufen und Neigungen, so zusammenhudelt, dass\n die Ansteckung des Beispiels zu Verworfenheit und Liederlichkeit führen\n muss, ob solch eine Manufaktur die Summe des nationalen und indivi-\n duellen Glücks vermehren kann“ [Note: Eden l. c. b. II, ch. I, p. 421.] ? „In Derbyshire, Nottinghamshire\n und besonders Lancashire,“ sagt Fielden, „wurde die jüngst erfundne\n Maschinerie angewandt in grossen Fabriken, dicht bei Strömen fähig das\n Wasserrad zu drehn. Tausende von Händen waren plötzlich erheischt an\n diesen Plätzen, fern von den Städten; und Lancashire namentlich, bis zu\n jener Zeit vergleichungsweis dünn bevölkert und unfruchtbar, bedurfte jetzt\n vor allem einer Population. Die kleinen und flinken Finger waren vor\n allen in Requisition. Sofort sprang die Gewohnheit auf, Lehrlinge(!)\n aus den verschiedenen Pfarrei-Workhouses von London, Birmingham und\n sonstwo zu beziehn. Tausende dieser kleinen hilflosen Kreaturen wurden\n so nach dem Norden spedirt, vom 7. — 13. oder 14. Jahr. Es war die\n Gewohnheit für den Meister (d. h. den Kinderdieb), seine Lehrlinge zu\n kleiden, nähren und logiren in einem Lehrlingshaus nah bei der Fabrik.\nAufseher wurden bestellt um ihre Arbeit zu überwachen. Es war das In-\n teresse dieser Sklaventreiber die Kinder aufs Aeusserste abzuarbeiten, denn\n ihre Zahlung stand im Verhältniss zur Quantität Produkt, die aus dem Kind\n erpresst werden konnte. Grausamkeit war natürliche Folge . . . . In\n vielen Fabrikdistrikten, besonders Lancashire’s, wurden die herzzerreis-\n sendsten Torturen prakticirt an diesen harmlosen und freundlosen Krea-\n turen, die den Fabrikherrn consignirt waren. Sie wurden zu Tod gehetzt\n durch Arbeitsexcesse; sie wurden gepeitscht, gekettet und gefoltert mit\n [Running matter: 47*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"740\", \"facs\": \"#f0759\"} --> dem ausgesuchtesten Raffinement von Grausamkeit; sie wurden in vielen\n Fällen bis zu den Knochen ausgehungert, während die Peitsche sie an der\n Arbeit hielt. Ja in einigen Fällen wurden sie zum Selbstmord getrieben! …\n Die schönen und romantischen Thäler von Derbyshire, Nottinghamshire\n und Lancashire, abgeschlossen vom öffentlichen Auge, wurden grause Ein-\n öden von Tortur und — oft von Mord! … Die Profite der Fabrikanten\n waren enorm. Das wetzte nur ihren Wehrwolfsheisshunger. Sie began-\n nen die Praxis der Nachtarbeit, d. h. nachdem sie eine Gruppe Hände\n durch das Tagwerk gelähmt, hatten sie eine andre Gruppe für das Nacht-\n werk zur Hand; die Tagesgruppe wanderte in die Betten, welche die\n Nachtgruppe grade verlassen hatte und vice versa. Es ist Volksüberliefe-\n rung in Lancashire, dass die Betten nie abkühlten“ [Note: John Fielden l. c. p. 5, 6. Ueber die ursprünglichen Infamien des\n Fabrikwesens vgl. Dr. Aikin (1795) l. c. p. 219 und Gisbourne: „Enquiry\n into the duties of men. 1795“, v. II. — Da die Dampfmaschine die Fabriken\n von den ländlichen Wasserfällen weg in die Mitte von Städten verpflanzte, fand\n der „entsagungslustige“ Plusmacher das Kindermaterial nun zur Hand, ohne ge-\n waltsame Sklavenzufuhr aus den Workhouses. — Als Sir R. Peel (Vater des\n „Ministers der Plausibilität“) seine Bill zum Schutz der Kinder 1815 einbrachte,\n erklärte F. Horner (lumen des Bullion-Comités und intimer Freund Ricardo’s)\n im Unterhaus: „Es ist notorisch, dass mit den Effekten eines Banqueroutier’s\n eine Bande, wenn er solchen terminus brauchen dürfe, von Fabrikkindern zur\n Auktion öffentlich, als Theil des Eigenthums, annoncirt und losgeschlagen wurde.\n Vorzwei Jahren (1813) kam ein abscheulicher Fall vor die King’s Bench. Es handelte\n sich um eine Anzahl Knaben. Eine Pfarrei von London hatte sie einem Fabri-\n kanten übermacht, der übertrug sie wieder auf einen andern. Sie wurden schliess-\n lich von einigen Menschenfreunden in einem Zustand absoluter Hungers-\n noth (absolute famine) entdeckt. Ein andrer, noch schrecklicherer Fall sei zu\n seiner Kenntniss als Mitglied des parlamentarischen Untersuchungscomités ge-\n bracht worden. Vor nicht vielen Jahren schlossen eine Londoner Pfarrei und ein\n Fabrikant von Lancashire einen Vertrag, wodurch stipulirt wurde, dass er auf je\n 20 gesunde Kinder einen Idioten mit in den Kauf nehme.“] .\n\n\n\n\nMit der Entwicklung der kapitalistischen Produktion während der Manu-\n fakturperiode hatte die öffentliche Meinung von Europa den letzten Rest von\n Schamgefühl und Gewissen eingebüsst. Die Nationen renommirten cynisch\n mit jeder Infamie, die Mittel zu Kapitalaccumulation. Man\n lese z. B. die naiven Handelsannalen des Biedermanns Anderson.\n Hier wird es als Triumph englischer Staatsweisheit ausposaunt, dass Eng-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"741\", \"facs\": \"#f0760\"} --> land im Frieden von Utrecht den Spaniern durch den Asientovertrag das\n Privilegium abzwang, den Negerhandel, den es bisher nur zwischen Afrika\n und dem englischen Westindien betrieb, nun auch zwischen Afrika und dem\n spanischen Amerika betreiben zu dürfen. England erhielt das Recht, das\n spanische Amerika bis 1743 jährlich mit 4800 Negern zu versorgen. Diess\n gewährte zugleich einen officiellen Deckmantel für den britischen Schmug-\n gel. Liverpool wuchs gross auf der Basis des Sklavenhandels. Er bildet\nseine Methode der ursprünglichen Accumulation. Und bis\n heutzutag blieb die Liverpooler „Ehrbarkeit“ Pindar des Sklavenhandels,\n welcher — vgl. die citirte Schrift des Dr. Aikin von 1795 — „den\n commerciellen Unternehmungsgeist bis zur Leidenschaft steigere, famose\n Seeleute bilde, und enormes Geld einbringe.“ Liverpool beschäftigte\n1730 im Sklavenhandel 15 Schiffe, 1751: 53, 1760: 74, 1770: 96\n und 1792: 132.\n\n\n\n\nWährend sie die Kindersklaverei in England einführte, gab die Baum-\n wollindustrie zugleich den Anstoss zur Verwandlung der früher mehr oder\n minder patriarchalischen Sklavenwirthschaft der Vereinigten Staaten in\n ein commercielles Exploitationssystem. Ueberhaupt bedurfte die verhüllte\n Sklaverei der Lohnarbeiter in Europa zum Piedestal die Sklaverei sans\n phrase in der neuen Welt [Note: 1790 kamen im englischen Westindien 10 Sklaven auf 1 Freien, im\n französischen 14 auf 1, im holländischen 23 auf 1. (Henry Brougham:\n „An Inquiry into the Colonial Policy of the European Powers.\n Edinb. 1803“, v. II, p. 74.)] .\n\n\n\n\nTantae molis erat, die „ewigen Naturgesetze“ der kapita-\n listischen Produktionsweise zu entbinden, den Scheidungsprozess zwischen Ar-\n beitern und Arbeitsbedingungen zu vollziehn, auf dem einen Pol die gesell-\n schaftlichen Produktions- und Lebensmittel in Kapital zu verwandeln, auf\n dem Gegenpol die Volksmasse in Lohnarbeiter, in freie „arbeitende\n Arme“, diess Kunstprodukt der modernen Geschichte [Note: Der Ausdruck „labouring poor“ findet sich in den englischen Ge-\n setzen vom Augenblick, wo die Klasse der Lohnarbeiter bemerkenswerth wird.\n Der „labouring poor“ steht im Gegensatz, einerseits zum „idle poor“, Bettler\n u. s. w., andrerseits zum Arbeiter, der noch kein gepflücktes Huhn, sondern Eigen-\n thümer seiner Arbeitsmittel ist. Aus dem Gesetz ging der Ausdruck „labouring\n poor“ in die politische Oekonomie über, von Culpeper, J. Child u. s. w. bis\n A. Smith und Eden. Danach beurtheile man die bonne foi des „execrable poli-] . Wenn\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"742\", \"facs\": \"#f0761\"} --> das Geld, nach Augier, „mit natürlichen Blutflecken auf einer Backe\n zur Welt kömmt“ [Note: Marie Augier: „Du Credit Public.“] , so das Kapital von Kopf bis Zeh, aus allen\n Poren, blut- und schmutztriefend [Note: „Kapital, sagt der Quarterly Reviewer, flieht Tumult und Streit\n und ist ängstlicher Natur. Das ist sehr wahr, aber doch nicht die ganze Wahrheit.\n Das Kapital hat einen horror vor Abwesenheit von Profit, oder sehr kleinem Profit,\n wie die Natur vor der Leere. Mit entsprechendem Profit wird Kapital kühn. Zehn\n Procent sicher, und man kann es überall anwenden; 20 Procent, es wird lebhaft;\n 50 Procent, positiv waghalsig; für 100 Procent stampft es alle menschlichen Ge-\n setze unter seinen Fuss; 300 Procent, und es existirt kein Verbrechen, das es\n nicht riskirt, selbst auf Gefahr des Galgens. Wenn Tumult und Streit Profit tragen,\n wird es sie beide encouragiren. Beweis: Schmuggel und Sklavenhandel.“ (T. J.\nDunning l. c. p. 36.)] .\n\n\n\n\nWorauf kömmt die ursprüngliche Accumulation des Ka-\n pitals, d. h. seine historische Genesis, hinaus? Soweit sie nicht un-\n mittelbare Verwandlung von Sklaven und Leibeigenen in Lohnarbeiter, also\nblosser Formwechsel ist, bedeutet sie nur die Expropria-\n tion der unmittelbaren Producenten, d. h. die Auflösung\n des auf eigner Arbeit beruhenden Privateigenthums. Das\n Privateigenthum des Arbeiters an seinen Produktionsmitteln ist die Grund-\n lage des Kleinbetriebs, der Kleinbetrieb eine nothwendige Bedingung für die\n Entwicklung der gesellschaftlichen Produktion und der freien Individuali-\n tät des Arbeiters selbst. Allerdings existirt diese Produktionsweise auch\n [Note: tical cantmonger“ Edmund Burke, wenn er den Ausdruck „labouring poor“\n für „execrable political cant“ erklärt. Dieser Sykophant, der im Sold der eng-\n lischen Oligarchie den Romantiker gegenüber der französischen Revolution spielte,\n ganz wie er beim Beginn der amerikanischen Wirren im Sold der nordamerikani-\n schen Kolonien gegenüber der englischen Oligarchie den Liberalen gespielt hatte,\n war durch und durch ordinärer Bourgeois: „Die Gesetze des Handels sind die\n Gesetze der Natur und folglich die Gesetze Gottes.“ (E. Burke l. c. p. 31, 32.)\n Kein Wunder, dass er, den Gesetzen Gottes und der Natur getreu, stets sich selbst\n auf dem besten Markt verkauft hat! Man findet in des Rev. Mr. Tucker’s Schrif-\n ten — Tucker war Pfaff und Tory, im übrigen aber anständiger Mann und tüch-\n tiger politischer Oekonom — sehr gute Charakteristik dieses Edmund Burke\n während seiner liberalen Zeit. Bei der infamen Charakterlosigkeit, die heutzutag\n herrscht und devotest an „die Gesetze des Handels“ glaubt, ist es Pflicht wieder\n und wieder die Burkes zu brandmarken, die sich von ihren Nachfolgern nur durch\n eins unterscheiden — Talent!] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"743\", \"facs\": \"#f0762\"} --> innerhalb der Sklaverei, Leibeigenschaft und anderer Abhängigkeitsver-\n hältnisse. Aber sie blüht nur, schnellt nur ihre ganze Energie, erobert nur\n die adäquate klassische Form, wo der Arbeiter freier Privateigen-\n thümer seiner von ihm selbst gehandhabten Arbeitsbe-\n dingungen ist, der Bauer des Ackers, den er bestellt, der Handwer-\n ker des Instruments, womit er als Virtuose spielt. Diese Produktionsweise\n unterstellt Zersplitterung des Bodens und der übrigen Produktions-\n mittel. Mit der Koncentration der letztern schliesst sie die Cooperation,\n Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb derselben Produktionsprozesse, gesellschaft-\n liche Beherrschung und Reglung der Natur, Entwicklung gesellschaft-\n licher Produktivkraft aus. Sie ist nur verträglich mit engen natur-\n wüchsigen Schranken der Produktion und der Gesellschaft. Auf einem\n gewissen Höhegrad bringt sie die materiellen Mittel ihrer eignen Vernich-\n tung zur Welt. Von diesem Augenblick regen sich Kräfte und Leiden-\n schaften im Gesellschaftsschoose, welche sich von ihr gefesselt fühlen. Sie muss\n vernichtet werden, sie wird vernichtet. Ihre Vernichtung, die Verwand-\n lung der individuellen und zersplitterten Produktions-\n mittel in gesellschaftlich concentrirte, daher des zwerg-\n haften Eigenthums Vieler in das massenhafte Eigenthum Weniger, daher\n die Expropriation der grossen Volksmasse von Grund und\n Boden und Lebensmitteln und Arbeitsinstrumenten, diese\n furchtbare und schwierige Expropriation der Volksmasse bildet\n die Vorgeschichte des Kapitals. Sie umfasst eine Reihe gewaltsamer\n Methoden, wovon wir nur die epochemachenden als Methoden derur-\n sprünglichen Accumulation des Kapitals Revue passiren\n liessen. Die Expropriation der unmittelbaren Producenten wird mit\n schonungslosestem Vandalismus und unter dem Trieb der infamsten,\n schmutzigsten, kleinlichst gehässigsten Leidenschaften vollbracht. Das\nselbst erarbeitete, sozusagen auf Verwachsung des isolirten, unab-\n hängigen Arbeitsindividuums mit seinen Arbeitsbedin-\n gungen beruhende Privateigenthum wird verdrängt durch das\n kapitalistische Privateigenthum, welches auf Exploitation\n fremder, aber formell freier Arbeit beruht [Note: „Nous sommes dans une condition tout-à-fait nouvelle de la société …\n nous tendons a séparer toute espèce de propriété d’avec toute espèce de travail.“\n (Sismondi: „Nouveaux Principes de l’Écon. Polit.“ t. II, p. 434.)] . Sobald dieser Umwand-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"744\", \"facs\": \"#f0763\"} --> lungsprozess nach Tiefe und Umfang die alte Gesellschaft hinreichend\n zersetzt hat, sobald die Arbeiter in Proletarier, ihre Arbeitsbeding ungen\n in Kapital verwandelt sind, sobald die kapitalistische Produktionsweise\n auf eignen Füssen steht, gewinnt die weitere Vergesellschaftung der Ar-\n beit und weitere Verwandlung der Erde und andrer Produktionsmittel in\n gesellschaftlich ausgebeutete, also gemeinschaftliche Produk-\n tionsmittel, daher die weitere Expropriation der Privateigen-\n thümer, eine neue Form. Was jetzt zu expropriiren ist, ist nicht\n länger der selbstwirthschaftende Arbeiter, sondern der viele Arbeiter ex-\n ploitirende Kapitalist. Diese Expropriation vollzieht sich durch das\n Spiel der immanenten Gesetze der kapitalistischen Produktion\n selbst, durch die Koncentration der Kapitalien. Je ein Ka-\n pitalist schlägt viele todt. Hand in Hand mit dieser Koncentration oder\n der Expropriation vieler Kapitalisten durch Wenige ent-\n wickelt sich die cooperative Form des Arbeitsprozesses auf stets wachsen-\n der Stufenleiter, die bewusste technologische Anwendung der Wissenschaft,\n die planmässig gemeinsame Ausbeutung der Erde, die Verwandlung der\n Arbeitsmittel in nur gemeinsam verwendbare Arbeitsmittel, und die Oeko-\n nomisirung aller Produktionsmittel durch ihren Gebrauch als gemeinsame\n Produktionsmittel kombinirter, gesellschaftlicher Arbeit. Mit der bestän-\n dig abnehmenden Zahl der Kapitalmagnaten, welche alle Vortheile dieses\n Umwandlungsprozesses usurpiren und monopolisiren, wächst die Masse\n des Elends, des Drucks, der Knechtung, der Degradation, der Ausbeutung,\n aber auch die Empörung der stets anschwellenden und durch den Mecha-\n nismus des kapitalistischen Produktionsprozesses selbst geschulten, verein-\n ten und organisirten Arbeiterklasse. Das Kapitalmonopol wird\n zur Fessel der Produktionsweise, die mit und unter ihm aufge-\n blüht ist. Die Koncentration der Produktionsmittel und die Vergesellschaf-\n tung der Arbeit erreichen einen Punkt, wo sie unverträglich werden mit\n ihrer kapitalistischen Hülle. Sie wird gesprengt. Die Stunde des\n kapitalistischen Privateigenthums schlägt. Die Expro-\n priateurs werden expropriirt.\n\n\n\n\nDie kapitalistische Produktions- und Aneignungsweise, daher das kapi-\n talistische Privateigenthum, ist die erste Negation des\n individuellen, aufeigne Arbeit gegründeten Privateigen-\n thums. Die Negation der kapitalistischen Produktion wird durch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"745\", \"facs\": \"#f0764\"} --> sie selbst, mit der Nothwendigkeit eines Naturprozesses, producirt. Es ist\nNegation der Negation. Diese stellt das individuelle Eigen-\n thum wieder her, aber auf Grundlage der Errungenschaft der kapi-\n talistischen Aera, der Cooperation freier Arbeiter und ihrem\n Gemeineigenthum an der Erde und den durch die Arbeit\n selbst producirten Produktionsmitteln.\n\n\n\n\nDie Verwandlung des zersplitterten, auf eigner Arbeit der Indivi-\n duen beruhenden Privateigenthums in kapitalistisches ist natürlich\n ein ungleich mehr langwieriger, harter und schwieriger Prozess als die\n Verwandlung des faktisch bereits auf gesellschaftlicher Exploitation der\n Produktionsmittel beruhenden kapitalistischen Privateigenthums in ge-\n sellschaftliches Eigenthum. Dort handelte es sich um die\n Expropriation der Volksmasse durch wenige Usurpatoren, hier handelt es\n sich um die Expropriation weniger Usurpatoren durch die Volksmasse [Note: „Der Fortschritt der Industrie, dessen willenloser und widerstandloser\n Träger die Bourgeoisie ist, setzt an die Stelle der Isolirung der Arbeiter durch die\n Konkurrenz ihre revolutionäre Vereinigung durch die Association. Mit der Ent-\n wicklung der grossen Industrie wird also unter den Füssen der Bourgeoisie die\n Grundlage selbst weggezogen, worauf sie producirt und die Produkte sich aneignet.\n Sie producirt also vor allem ihre eignen Todtengräber. Ihr Untergang und der\n Sieg des Proletariats sind gleich unvermeidlich … Von allen Klassen, welche\n heutzutage der Bourgeoisie gegenüberstehn, ist nur das Proletariat eine wirklich\n revolutionäre Klasse. Die übrigen Klassen verkommen und gehn unter mit der\n grossen Industrie, das Proletariat ist ihr eigenstes Produkt … Die Mittelstände,\n der kleine Industrielle, der kleine Kaufmann, der Handwerker, der Bauer, sie alle\n bekämpfen die Bourgeoisie, um ihre Existenz als Mittelstände vor dem Untergang\n zu sichern … sie sind reaktionär, denn sie suchen das Rad der Geschichte zu-\n rückzudrehn.“ (F. Engels und Karl Marx: „Manifest der kommu-\n nistischen Partei. London 1847“, p. 9, 11.)] .","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0001","text":"Man hat gesehn, wie Geld in Kapital verwandelt, mit dem Kapital\n Mehrwerth und aus dem Mehrwerth mehr Kapital gemacht wird. Indess\n setzt die Accumulation des Kapitals den Mehrwerth, der Mehrwerth die\n kapitalistische Produktion, diese aber das Vorhandensein grösserer Kapi-\n talmassen in den Händen von Waarenproducenten voraus. Der ganze Pro-\n zess scheint also eine der kapitalistischen Accumulation vor-\n ausgehende „ursprüngliche“ Accumulation („previous accumu-\n lation“ bei Adam Smith) zu unterstellen, eine Accumulation, welche\n nicht das Resultat der kapitalistischen Produktionsweise ist, sondern\n ihr Ausgangspunkt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0002","text":"Diese ursprüngliche Accumulation spielt in der politischen\n Oekonomie ungefähr dieselbe Rolle wie der Sündenfall in der Theologie.\n Adam biss in den Apfel und damit kam über das Menschengeschlecht die\n Sünde. Ihr Ursprung wird erklärt, indem er als Anekdote der Ver-\n gangenheit erzählt wird. In einer längst verflossenen Zeit gab es auf der\n einen Seite eine fleissige Elite und auf der andern faulenzende Lumpen.\n So kam es, dass die ersten Reichthum accumulirten und die letz-\n teren schliesslich nichts zu verkaufen hatten als ihre eigne Haut. Und von\n diesem Sündenfall datirt die Armuth der grossen Masse, die immer noch,\n aller Arbeit zum Trotz, nichts zu verkaufen hat als sich selbst, und der\n Reichthum der Wenigen, der fortwährend wächst, obgleich sie längst\n aufgehört haben zu arbeiten. Solche fade Kinderei kaut Herr Thiers\n z. B. noch mit staatsfeierlichem Ernst, zur Vertheidigung der propriété,\n den einst so geistreichen Franzosen vor. Aber sobald die Eigenthums-\n frage ins Spiel kommt, wird es heilige Pflicht, den Standpunkt der Kinder-\n fibel als den allen Altersklassen und Entwicklungsstufen allein gerechten\n festzuhalten. In der wirklichen Geschichte spielen bekanntlich Erobe-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"700\", \"facs\": \"#f0719\"} --> rung, Unterjochung, Raubmord, kurz Gewalt die grosse Rolle. In der\n sanften politischen Oekonomie herrschte von jeher die Idylle. Recht und\n „Arbeit“ waren von jeher die einzigen Bereicherungsmittel, natürlich mit\n jedesmaliger Ausnahme von „diesem Jahr“. In der That sind\n die Methoden der ursprünglichen Accumulation alles andre, nur nicht\n idyllisch.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0003","text":"Geld und Waare sind nicht von vornherein Kapital, so wenig wie\n Produktions- und Lebensmittel. Sie bedürfen der Verwandlung in\n Kapital. Dieser Verwandlungsprozess selbst kann aber nur unter be-\n stimmten Umständen vorgehn. Sie spitzen sich dahin zusammen: Zweier-\n lei sehr verschiedne Sorten von Waarenbesitzern müssen sich gegenüber und\n in Kontakt treten, einerseits Eigner von Geld, Produktions- und Le-\n bensmitteln, welche die von ihnen geeignete Werthsumme verwerthen\n wollen durch Ankauf fremder Arbeitskraft; andrerseits freie Arbeiter,\n Verkäufer der eignen Arbeitskraft und daher Verkäufer von Arbeit.\nFreie Arbeiter in dem Doppelsinn, dass sie weder selbst unmit-\n telbar zu den Produktionsbedingungen gehören, wie Sklaven, Leibeigne\n u. s. w., noch auch die Produktionsbedingungen ihnen gehören, wie beim\n selbstwirthschaftenden Bauer u. s. w., sie davon vielmehr frei, los und le-\n dig sind. Mit dieser Polarisation des Waarenmarkts sind die\n Grundbedingungen der kapitalistischen Produktion gegeben. Das Kapi-\n talverhältniss setzt die Scheidung zwischen den Arbeitern und\ndem Eigenthum an den Verwirklichungsbedingungen der\n Arbeit voraus. Sobald die kapitalistische Produktion einmal auf eignen\n Füssen steht, erhält sie nicht nur jene Scheidung, sondern reproducirt sie\n auf stets wachsender Stufenleiter. Der Prozess, der das Kapitalverhält-\n niss schafft, kann also nichts anders sein als der Scheidungsprozess\n des Arbeiters von den Arbeitsbedingungen, ein Prozess, der\n einerseits die gesellschaftlichen Lebens- und Produktionsmittel in Kapi-\n tal verwandelt, andrerseits die unmittelbaren Producenten in Lohnar-\n beiter. Die s. g. ursprüngliche Accumulation ist also nichts\n als der historische Scheidungsprozess von Producent und\n Produktionsmittel. Er erscheint als „ursprünglich“, weil er die\nVorgeschichte des Kapitals und der ihm entsprechenden Produk-\n tionsweise bildet.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0004","text":"Man sieht auf den ersten Blick, dass dieser Scheidungsprozess eine\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"701\", \"facs\": \"#f0720\"} --> ganze Reihe historischer Prozesse einschliesst, und eine doppelseitige Reihe,\n einerseits Auflösung der Verhältnisse, die den Arbeiter selbst zum Eigen\n thum dritter Personen machten und zu einem selbst angeeigneten Produk-\n tionsmittel, andrerseits Auflösung des Eigenthums der unmittelbaren Pro-\n ducenten an ihren Produktionsmitteln. Der Scheidungsprozess umfasst\n also in der That die ganze Entwicklungsgeschichte der modernen bürger-\n lichen Gesellschaft, eine Geschichte, welche gar keine Schwierigkeiten\n böte, hätten die bürgerlichen Geschichtschreiber die Auflösung der feuda-\n len Produktionsweise nicht ausschliesslich unter dem clair obscur der Eman-\n cipation des Arbeiters dargestellt, statt zugleich als Verwandlung der\n feudalen in die kapitalistische Exploitationsweise. Der\n Ausgangspunkt der Entwicklung war die Knechtschaft des Arbei-\n ters. Ihr Fortgang bestand in einem Formwechsel dieser\n Knechtung. Jedoch erheischt unser Zweck keineswegs Analyse der\n mittelaltrigen Bewegung. Obgleich die kapitalistische Produktion schon\n im 14. und 15. Jahrhundert sporadisch ihren Sitz in den Ländern am\n Mittelmeer aufschlug, datirt die kapitalistische Aera erst vom\n16. Jahrhundert. Dort, wo sie aufblüht, ist die Aufhebung der Leib-\n eigenschaft längst vollbracht und das mittelaltrige Städtewesen bereits\n in das Stadium seines Verfalls getreten.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0005","text":"Historisch epochemachend in der Geschichte des Scheidungsprozesses\n sind die Momente, worin grosse Menschenmassen plötzlich und gewaltsam von\n ihren Subsistenz- und Produktionsmitteln geschieden und als vogelfreie\n Proletarier auf den Arbeitsmarkt geschleudert werden. Die Expropri-\n ation der Arbeiter von Grund und Boden bildet die Grund-\n lage des ganzen Prozesses. Wir haben sie also zuerst zu betrachten. Ihre\n Geschichte nimmt in verschiednen Ländern verschiedne Färbung an und\n durchläuft die verschiedenen Phasen in verschiedner Reihenfolge. Nur in\n England, das wir daher als Beispiel nehmen, besitzt sie klassische\n Form [Note: In Italien, wo die kapitalistische Produktion sich am frühsten entwickelt,\n findet auch die Auflösung der Leibeigenschaftsverhältnisse am frühsten statt. Der\n Leibeigene wird hier emancipirt, bevor er irgend ein Recht der Verjährung an\n Grund und Boden gesichert hat. Seine Emancipation verwandelt ihn also sofort\n in einen vogelfreien Proletarier, der überdem in den meist schon aus der Römer-\n zeit überlieferten Städten die neuen Herren fertig vorfindet.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0006","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"702\", \"facs\": \"#f0721\"} -->","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0007","text":"In England war die Leibeigenschaft im letzten Theil des 14. Jahr-\n hunderts faktisch verschwunden. Die ungeheure Mehrzahl der Bevölke-\n rung [Note: Noch Ende des 17. Jahrhunderts waren mehr als ⅘ der englischen Ge-\n sammtbevölkerung agrikol. (Macaulay: „The History of England.“\nLond. 1854, v. I, p. 413.) Ich citire Macaulay, weil er als systematischer Ge-\n schichtsfälscher derartige Thatsachen möglichst „beschneidet“.]  bestand damals und noch mehr im 15. Jahrhundert aus freien, selbst-\n wirthschaftenden Bauern, durch welch feudales Aushängeschild ihr Eigen-\n thum immer versteckt sein mochte. Auf den grösseren herrschaftlichen\n Gütern war der früher selbst leibeigne bailiff (Vogt) durch den freien\n Pächter verdrängt. Die Lohnarbeiter der Agrikultur bestanden theils aus\n Bauern, die ihre Mussezeit durch Arbeit bei grossen Grundeigenthümern\n verwertheten, theils aus einer selbstständigen, relativ und absolut wenig\n zahlreichen Klasse eigentlicher Lohnarbeiter. Auch letztre waren faktisch\n zugleich selbstwirthschaftende Bauern, indem sie ausser ihrem Lohn Acker-\n land zum Belauf von 4 und mehr Acres nebst Cottages angewiesen er-\n hielten. Sie genossen zudem mit den eigentlichen Bauern die Nutznies-\n sung des Gemeindelandes, worauf ihr Vieh weidete und das ihnen\n zugleich die Mittel der Feuerung, Holz, Torf u. s. w. bot [Note: Man muss nie vergessen, dass selbst der Leibeigene nicht nur Eigen-\n thümer, wenn auch tributpflichtiger Eigenthümer, der zu seinem Haus gehörigen\n Bodenparcellen war, sondern auch Miteigenthümer des Gemeinde-\n landes. „Le paysan y (en Silésie) est serf.“ Nichtsdestoweniger besitzen\n diese serfs Gemeindegüter. „On n’a pas pu encore engager les Silésiens au\n partage des communes, tandis que dans la nouvelle Marche, il n’y a guère de\n village où ce partage ne soit exécuté avec le plus grand succès.“ (Mirabeau:\n „De la Monarchie Prussienne. Londres 1788“, t. II, p. 125, 126.)] . In allen\n Ländern Europa’s ist die feudale Produktion durch Theilung des Bodens\n unter möglichst viele Untersassen charakterisirt. Die Macht des Feudal-\n herrn, wie die jeden Souverains, beruhte nicht auf der Länge seiner Rent-\n rolle, sondern auf der Zahl seiner Unterthanen, und letztere hing von der\n Zahl selbstwirthschaftender Bauern ab [Note: Japan, mit seiner rein feudalen Organisation des Grundeigenthums und\n seiner entwickelten Kleinbauernwirthschaft, liefert ein viel treueres Bild des euro-\n päischen Mittelalters als unsre sämmtlichen, meist von bürgerlichen Vorurtheilen\n diktirten Geschichtsbücher. Es ist gar zu bequem, „liberal“ auf Kosten des\n Mittelalters zu sein.] . Obgleich der englische Boden\n daher nach der normännischen Eroberung in riesenhafte Baronien vertheilt\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"703\", \"facs\": \"#f0722\"} --> ward, wovon eine einzige oft 900 alte angelsächsische Lordschaften ein-\n schloss, war er besät von kleinen Bauernwirthschaften, nur hier und da\n durchbrochen von grösseren herrschaftlichen Gütern. Solche Verhältnisse,\n bei gleichzeitiger Blüthe des Städtewesens, wie sie das 15. Jahrhundert\n auszeichnet, erlaubten jenen Volksreichthum, den der Staatskanzler\nFortescue so beredt in seinen „Laudibus Legum Angliae“\n schildert, aber sie schlossen den Kapitalreichthum aus.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0008","text":"Das Vorspiel der Umwälzung, welche die Grundlage der kapitalisti-\n schen Produktionsweise schuf, ereignet sich im letzten Drittheil des 15.\n und den ersten Decennien des 16. Jahrhunderts. Eine Masse vogel-\n freier Proletarier ward auf den Arbeitsmarkt geschleudert durch\n die Auflösung der feudalen Gefolgschaften, die, wie Sir James\n Steuart richtig bemerkt, „überall nutzlos Haus und Hof füllten.“ Obgleich\n die königliche Macht, selbst ein Produkt der bürgerlichen Entwicklung, in\n ihrem Streben nach absoluter Souverainität die Auflösung dieser Gefolg-\n schaften gewaltsam beschleunigte, war sie keineswegs deren einzige Ursache.\n Vielmehr im trotzigsten Gegensatz zu Königthum und Parlament, schuf der\n grosse Feudalherr ein ungleich grösseres Proletariat durch ge-\n waltsame Verjagung der Bauernschaft von dem Grund und Boden, worauf\n sie denselben feudalen Rechtstitel besass wie er selbst, und durch Usurpa-\n tion ihres Gemeindelandes. Den unmittelbaren Anstoss dazu gab in Eng-\n land namentlich das Aufblühn der flandrischen Wollmanufaktur, und\n das entsprechende Steigen der Wollpreise. Den alten Feudaladel hatten\n die grossen Feudalkriege verschlungen, der neue war ein Kind seiner Zeit,\n für welche Geld die Macht aller Mächte. Verwandlung von Ackerland in\n Schafweide ward also sein Losungswort. Harrison, in seiner „De-\n scription of England. Prefixed to Holinshed’s Chronicles“,\n beschreibt, wie die Expropriation der kleinen Bauern das Land ruinirt.\n „What care our great incroachers!“ (Was fragen unsre grossen Usurpa-\n toren danach?) Die Wohnungen der Bauern und die Cottages der Arbeiter\n wurden gewaltsam niedergerissen oder dem Verfall geweiht. „Wenn\n man“, sagt Harrison, „die älteren Inventarien jeden Ritterguts vergleichen\n will, so wird man finden, dass unzählige Häuser und kleine Bauernwirth-\n schaften verschwunden sind, dass das Land viel weniger Leute nährt, dass\n viele Städte verfallen sind, obgleich einige neue aufblühn . . . . Von Städten\n und Dörfern, die man für Schaftriften zerstört hat, und worin nur noch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"704\", \"facs\": \"#f0723\"} --> die Herrschaftshäuser stehn, könnte ich etwas erzählen.“ Die Klagen\n jener alten Chroniken sind immer übertrieben, aber sie zeichnen genau den\n Eindruck der Revolution in den Produktionsverhältnissen auf die Zeitge-\n nossen selbst. Ein Vergleich zwischen den Schriften der Schatzkanzler For-\n tescue und Thomas Morus veranschaulicht die Kluft zwischen dem\n 15. und 16. Jahrhundert. Aus ihrem goldnen Zeitalter, wie Thornton\n richtig sagt, stürzte die englische Arbeiterklasse ohne alle Zwischenüber-\n gänge in das eiserne.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0009","text":"Die Gesetzgebung erschrak vor dieser Umwälzung. Sie stand noch\n nicht auf der Civilisationshöhe, wo „Wealth of the Nation“,\n d. h. Kapitalbildung und rücksichtslose Exploitation und Verarmung der\n Volksmasse als ultima Thule aller Staatsweisheit gelten. In seiner „Ge-\n schichte Heinrich’s VII.“ sagt Baco: „Um diese Zeit (1489)\n mehrten sich die Klagen über Verwandlung von Ackerland in Weide (zur\n Schaftrift u. s. w.), leicht zu versehn durch wenige Hände. Diess brachte\n einen Verfall des Volks hervor … König und Parlament ergriffen Mass-\n regeln wider diese entvölkernde Usurpation der Gemeindeländereien (depo-\n pulating inclosures) und die ihr auf dem Fuss folgende entvölkernde Weide-\n wirthschaft (depopulating pasture).“ Ein Akt Heinrich des VII., 1489,\n c. 19, verbot die Zerstörung aller Bauernhäuser, wozu wenigstens 20\n Acres Land gehörten. In einem Akt 25, Heinrich VIII., wird dasselbe\n Gesetz erneuert. Es heisst darin u. a., dass „viele Pachten und grosse\n Viehmassen, besonders Schafe, sich in wenigen Händen aufhäufen, wo-\n durch die Grundrenten sehr gewachsen und der Ackerbau (tillage) sehr\n verfallen, Kirchen und Häuser niedergerissen, wunderbare Volksmassen\n verunfähigt seien, sich selbst und Familien zu erhalten.“ Das Gesetz\n verordnet daher den Wiederbau der verfallnen Pachthäuser, bestimmt den\n Kornbau im Verhältniss zur Schafweide u. s. w. Ein Akt von 1533\n klagt, dass manche Eigenthümer 24,000 Schafe besitzen und beschränkt\n deren Zahl auf 2000. Die Volksklage [Note: Sieh u. a.: „Examination of certayne complaints. Lon-\n don 1581.“]  und die seit Heinrich dem VII.\n an 150 Jahre fortdauernde Gesetzgebung wider die Expropriation der\n kleinen Pächter und Bauern waren gleich fruchtlos. Das Geheimniss ihrer\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"705\", \"facs\": \"#f0724\"} --> Erfolglosigkeit verräth uns Baco wider Wissen. „Der Akt Hein-\n rich’s VII.,“ sagt er in seinen „Essays, civil and moral“ Sect. 20,\n „war tief und bewunderungswürdig, indem er Landwirthschaften und\n Ackerbauhäuser von bestimmtem Normalmass schuf, d. h. eine Proportion\n von Land für sie erhielt, die sie befähigte Unterthanen von genügendem\n Reichthum und ohne servile Lage auf die Welt zu setzen und den Pflug\n in der Hand von Eigenthümern, nicht von Miethlingen zu\n halten“ („to keep the plough in the hand of the owners and not hire-\n lings“). Was die kapitalistische Produktion erheischte, war umge-\n kehrt servile Lage der Volksmasse, ihre eigne Verwandlung in Miethlinge, und\n Verwandlung ihrer Arbeitsmittel in Kapital. Jene ältere Gesetzgebung\n sucht auch die 4 Acres Land bei der Cottage des ländlichen Lohnar-\n beiters zu erhalten, wie sie ihm die Aufnahme von Miethsleuten in seine\n Cottage verbot. Noch 1627, unter Jakob I., wurde Roger Crocker von\n Frontmill verurtheilt wegen Bau’s einer Cottage im Manor von Frontmill\n ohne 4 Acres Land als beständiges Annex an dieselbe; noch 1638, unter\n Karl I., wurde eine königliche Kommission ernannt, um die alten Gesetze,\n namentlich auch über die 4 Acres Land, zu erzwingen; noch Cromwell ver-\n bot Erbauung eines Hauses in 4 Meilen weitem Umkreis von London ohne Aus-\n stattung desselben mit 4 Acres Land. Noch in der ersten Hälfte des 18.\n Jahrhunderts wird geklagt, wenn die Cottage des Landarbeiters kein Zu-\n behör von 1 bis 2 Acres hat. Heutzutag ist er glücklich, wenn sie mit\n einem Gärtchen ausgestattet ist, oder wenn er weitab von ihr ein Paar\n Ruthen Land miethen kann. „Landlords und Pächter“, sagt Dr. Hunter,\n „handeln hier Hand in Hand. Wenige Acres zur Cottage würden den Ar-\n beiter zu unabhängig machen“ [Note: Dr. Hunter l. c. p. 134: „The quantity of land assigned (in den\n alten Gesetzen) would now be judged too great for labourers, and rather as likely\n to convert them into small farmers.“ (George Roberts: „The Social\n History of the People of the Southern Counties of England in\n pastcenturies. Lond. 1856“, p. 184, 185.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0010","text":"Einen neuen furchtbaren Anstoss erhielt der gewaltsame Expro-\n priationsprozess der Volksmasse im 16. Jahrh. durch die Refor-\n mation und, in ihrem Gefolge, den kolossalen Diebstahl der Kirchen-\n güter. Die katholische Kirche war zur Zeit der Reformation Feudal-\n [Running matter: I. 45] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"706\", \"facs\": \"#f0725\"} --> eigenthümer eines grossen Theils des englischen Grund und Bodens. Die\n Unterdrückung der Klöster u. s. w. schleuderte deren Einwohner ins Prole-\n tariat. Die Kirchengüter selbst wurden grossentheils an raubsüchtige\n königliche Günstlinge verschenkt oder zu einem Spottpreis an spekulirende\n Pächter und Stadtbürger verkauft, welche die alten erblichen Untersassen\n massenhaft verjagten und ihre Wirthschaften zusammenwarfen. Das ge-\n setzlich garantirte Eigenthum verarmter Landleute an einem Theil der\n Kirchenzehnten ward stillschweigend konfiscirt [Note: „The right of the poor to share in the tithe, is established by the tenure\n of ancient statutes.“ (Tuckett l. c. v. II, p. 804, 805.)] . „Pauper ubique\n jacet“, rief Königin Elisabeth nach einer Rundreise durch England. Im\n 43. Jahre ihrer Regierung war man endlich gezwungen, den Pauperis-\n mus officiell anzuerkennen durch Einführung der Armensteuer. „Die\n Urheber dieses Gesetzes schämten sich seine Gründe auszusprechen und\n schickten es daher, wider alles Herkommen, ohne irgend ein pre-\n amble (Eingangsworte) in die Welt“ [Note: William Cobbett: „A History of the Protestant Refor-\n mation“, §. 471.] . Durch 16. Carolus I., 4\n wurde es perpetuell erklärt und erhielt in der That erst 1834 eine neue\n härtere Form [Note: Den protestantischen „Geist“ ersieht man u. a. aus folgendem. Im\n Süden Englands steckten verschiedne Grundeigenthümer und wohlhabende Pächter\n die Köpfe zusammen, und setzten über die richtige Interpretation des Armen-\n gesetzes der Elisabeth 10 Fragen auf, welche sie einem berühmten Juristen jener\n Zeit, Sergeant Snigge (später Richter unter Jakob I.), zum Gutachten vorlegten.\n „Neunte Frage: Einige der reichen Pächter der Pfarrei haben einen klugen\n Plan ausgeheckt, wodurch alle Wirre in Ausübung des Akts beseitigt werden kann.\n Sie schlagen den Bau eines Gefängnisses in der Pfarrei vor. Jedem Armen,\n der sich nicht in vorbesagtes Gefängniss einsperren lassen will, soll die Unter-\n stützung versagt werden. Es soll dann der Nachbarschaft Notiz gegeben werden,\n dass wenn irgend eine Person geneigt, die Armen dieser Pfarrei zu pachten, sie\n versiegelte Vorschläge eingeben soll, an einem bestimmten Tag, zum niedrigsten\n Preis, wozu sie selbe uns abnehmen will. Die Urheber dieses Plans unter-\n stellen, dass es in den Nachbargrafschaften Personen giebt, die unwillig sind zu\n arbeiten, und ohne Vermögen oder Kredit, um eine Pacht oder ein Schiff zu er-\n werben, so dass sie ohne Arbeit leben könnten („so as to live without labour“).\n Solche dürften geneigt sein, der Pfarrei sehr vortheilhafte Vorschläge zu machen.\nSollten hier und da Arme unter des Kontraktors Obhut kaput] . Diese unmittelbaren Wirkungen der Reformation\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"707\", \"facs\": \"#f0726\"} --> waren nicht ihre nachhaltigsten. Das Kircheneigenthum bildete\n das religiöse Bollwerk der alterthümlichen Grundeigenthums-\n verhältnisse. Mit seinem Fall waren sie nicht länger vertheidigbar [Note: Herr Rogers, obgleich Professor der politischen Oekonomie an der Uni-\n versität zu Oxford, dem Stammsitz protestantischer Orthodoxie, betont in seiner Vor-\n rede zur „History of Agriculture“ die Pauperisirung der Volksmasse durch\n die Reformation.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0011","text":"Noch in den letzten Decennien des 17. Jahrhunderts war die Yeomanry,\n eine unabhängige Bauernschaft, zahlreicher als die Klasse der Pächter.\n Sie hatte die Hauptstärke Cromwell’s gebildet und stand, selbst nach Ma-\n caulay’s Geständniss, in vortheilhaftem Gegensatz zu den versoffenen Mist-\n junkern und ihren Bedienten, den Landpfaffen, welche die herrschaftliche\n „Lieblingsmagd“ unter die Haube bringen mussten. Noch waren selbst\n die ländlichen Lohnarbeiter Miteigenthümer am Gemeindeeigenthum.\n [Note: gehn, so wird die Sünde an seiner Thüreliegen, da die Pfarrei ihre\n Pflichten gegen selbige Arme erfüllt hätte. Wir fürchten jedoch, dass der gegen-\n wärtige Akt keine Klugheitsmassregel („prudential measure“) dieser Art erlaubt;\n aber Sie müssen wissen, dass der Rest der freeholders dieser Grafschaft und der\n anliegenden sich uns anschliessen wird, um ihre Unterhausmitglieder zur Vorlage\n eines Gesetzes anzutreiben, welches Einsperrung und Zwangsarbeit der Armen ge-\n stattet, so dass jede Person, welche sich der Einsperrung widersetzt, zu keiner\n Unterstützung berechtigt sein soll. Diess, so hoffen wir, wird Personen im Elend\n abhalten, Unterstützung zu bedürfen“ („will prevent persons in distress from\n wanting relief“). (R. Blakey: „The History of Political Literature\n from the earliest times. Lond. 1855“, v. II, p. 83 sqq.) — In Schott-\n land fand die Abschaffung der Leibeigenschaft Jahrhunderte später statt\n als in England. Noch 1698 erklärte Salhoun im schottischen Parlament:\n „Die Zahl der Bettler ist in Schottland auf nicht weniger als 200,000 geschätzt.\n Das einzige Hilfsmittel, welches ich, ein Republikaner von Prinzip, vorschlagen\n kann, ist den alten Zustand der Leibeigenschaft zu restauriren\n und aus allen denen Sklaven zu machen, die unfähig sind für ihre eigne\n Subsistenz zu sorgen.“ So Eden l. c. b. I, ch. I: „Von der Freiheit der\n Ackerbauer datirt der Pauperismus … Manufakturen und Handel sind die\n wahren Aeltern unsrer nationalen Armen.“ Eden, wie jener schottische Repub-\n likaner von Prinzip, irrt nur darin, dass nicht die Aufhebung der Leibeigenschaft,\n sondern die Aufhebung des Eigenthums des Ackerbauers an Grund und Boden ihn\n zum Proletarier, resp. Pauper machte. — Englands Armengesetzen entspricht in\n Frankreich, wo sich die Expropriation in anderer Weise vollzog, die Ordonnanz\n von Moulins, 1571, und das Edikt von 1656.] \n [Running matter: 45*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"708\", \"facs\": \"#f0727\"} --> 1750 ungefähr war die Yeomanry verschwunden [Note: „A Letter to Sir T. C. Banbury, Brt.: „On the High Price\n of Provisions. By a Suffolk Gentleman.“ Ipswich, 1795, p. 4.\n Selbst der fanatische Vertheidiger des grossen Pachtwesens, der Verfasser der\n „Inquiry into the Connection of large farms etc. Lond. 1773“,\n p. 133, sagt: „I most lament the loss of our yeomanry, that set of men, who really\n kept up the independence of this nation; and sorry I am, to see their lands now\nin the hands of monopolizing lords, tenanted out to small farmers, who\n hold their leases on such conditions as to be little better than vassals ready to\n attend a summons on every mischievous occasion.“] , und in den letzten De-\n cennien des 18. Jahrhunderts die letzte Spur vom Gemeindeeigenthum der\n Ackerbauer. Wir sehn hier ab von den rein ökonomischen Agen-\n tien der Agrikulturrevolution. Wir fragen nach ihren gewalt-\n samen Hebeln.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0012","text":"Unter der Restauration der Stuarts setzten die Grundeigen-\n thümer eine Usurpation gesetzlich durch, die sich überall auf dem Kon-\n tinent auch ohne gesetzliche Weitläufigkeit vollzog. Sie hoben die Feudal-\n verfassung des Bodens auf, d. h. sie schüttelten seine Leistungspflich-\n ten an den Staat ab, „entschädigten“ den Staat durch Steuern auf die\n Bauernschaft und übrige Volksmasse, vindicirten modernes Privateigenthum\n an Gütern, worauf sie nur Feudaltitel besassen, und oktroyirten schliesslich\n jene Niederlassungsgesetze (laws of settlement), die, mutatis mu-\n tandis, auf die englischen Ackerbauer wirkten, wie des Tartaren Boris Go\n dunof Edikt auf die russische Bauernschaft.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0013","text":"Die „glorious Revolution“ (glorreiche Revolution) brachte\n mit dem Oranier Wilhelm III. [Note: Ueber die Privatmoral dieses bürgerlichen Helden u. a.: „The large\n grant of lands in Ireland to Lady Orkney, in 1695, is a public instance of the\n king’s affection, and the lady’s influence … Lady Orkney’s endearing offices,\n are supposed to have been — foeda labiorum ministeria.“ (In der Sloane\n Manuscript Collection, auf dem britischen Museum, Nr. 4224. Das\n Manuskript ist betitelt: „The character and behaviour of king Wil-\n liam, Sunderland etc. as represented in Original Letters to the\n Duke of Shrewsbury from Somers, Halifax, Oxford, Secretary\n Vernon etc.“ Es ist voller Kuriosa.)]  die grundeigenthümlichen und kapi-\n talistischen Plusmacher zur Herrschaft. Sie weihten die neue Aera ein,\n indem sie den bisher nur bescheiden betriebenen Diebstahl an den\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"709\", \"facs\": \"#f0728\"} --> Staatsdomänen auf kolossaler Stufenleiter ausübten. Diese Län-\n dereien wurden verschenkt, zu Spottpreisen verkauft, oder auch durch\n direkte Usurpation an Privatgüter annexirt [Note: „Die illegale Veräusserung der Krongüter, theils durch Verkauf und\n theils durch Schenkung, bildet ein skandalöses Kapitel in der englischen Ge-\n schichte … eine gigantische Prellerei der Nation (gigantic fraud on the nation).“\n (F. W. Newman: „Lectures on Political Econ. Lond. 1851“,\n p. 129, 130.)] . Alles das geschah ohne\n die geringste Beobachtung gesetzlicher Etiquette. Das so fraudulent an-\n geeignete Staatsgut sammt dem Kirchenplunder, so weit er während der\n republikanischen Revolution nicht abhanden kam, bildet die Grundlage der\n heutigen fürstlichen Domänen der englischen Oligarchie [Note: Man lese z. B. E. Burke’s Pamphlet über das herzogliche Haus von\n Bedford, dessen Sprosse Lord John Russell, „the tomtit of liberalism“.] . Die bürger-\n lichen Kapitalisten begünstigten die Operation, u. a. um den Grund und\n Boden in einen reinen Handelsartikel zu verwandeln, um ihre Zufuhr vogel-\n freier Proletarier vom Land zu vermehren u. s. w. Sie handelten für ihr Inter-\n esse ganz so richtig als die schwedischen Stadtbürger, deren ökono-\n misches Bollwerk die Bauernschaft war, wesshalb sie Hand in Hand\n mit derselben die Könige in der gewaltsamen Resumption der Kronlän-\n dereien von der Oligarchie (seit 1604, später unter Karl X. und\n Karl XI.) unterstützten.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0014","text":"Gemeindeeigenthum war eine altgermanische Einrichtung,\n die unter der Decke der Feudalität fortlebte. Man hat gesehn, wie die\n gewaltsame Usurpation desselben, meist begleitet von Verwandlung des\n Ackerlands in Viehweide, Ende des 15. Jahrhunderts beginnt und im\n 16. Jahrhundert fortdauert. Aber damals vollzog sich der Prozess als\nindividuelle Gewaltthat, wogegen die Gesetzgebung 150 Jahre\n durch vergeblich ankämpft. Der Fortschritt des 18. Jahrh. offenbart sich\n darin, dass das Gesetz selbst jetzt zum Vehikel des Raubs am\n Volksland wird, obgleich die grossen Pächter nebenbei auch ihre kleinen\n unabhängigen Privatmethoden anwenden [Note: „Die Pächter verbieten den Cottagers (Häuslern) irgend eine lebendige\n Kreatur ausser sich selbst zu erhalten, unter dem Vorwand, dass wenn sie Vieh\n oder Geflügel hielten, sie von den Scheunen Futter stehlen würden. Sie sagen\n auch, haltet die Cottagers arm, und ihr haltet sie fleissig. Die wirkliche That-] . Die parlamentarische Form\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"710\", \"facs\": \"#f0729\"} --> des Raubs ist die der „Bills for Inclosures of Commons“ (Bills\n für Einschluss der Gemeindeländereien), in andern Worten Dekrete, wo-\n durch die Landlords Volkseigenthum sich selbst als Privateigenthum schen-\n ken, Dekrete der Volksexpropriation. Sir F. M. Eden widerlegt sein\n pfiffiges Advokatenplaidoyer, worin er das Gemeindeeigenthum als Privat-\n eigenthum der an die Stelle der Feudalen getretenen Landlords darzustellen\n sucht, indem er selbst einen „allgemeinen Parlamentsakt für die\n inclosure der Gemeindeländerei“ verlangt, also zugiebt, dass ein parla-\n mentarischer Staatsstreich zu ihrer Verwandlung in Privat-\n eigenthum nöthig ist, andrerseits aber von der Legislatur „Schaden-\n ersatz“ für die exproprürten Armen fordert [Note: Eden l. c. Preface.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0015","text":"Während an die Stelle der unabhängigen Yeomen tenants-atwill\n traten, kleinere Pächter auf einjährige Kündigung, eine servile und von\n der Willkühr der Landlords abhängige Rotte, half, neben dem Raub der\n Staatsdomänen, namentlich der systematisch betriebne Diebstahl des Ge-\n meindeeigenthums jene grossen Pachten anschwellen, die man im 18. Jahr-\n hundert Kapital-Pachten [Note: „Capital-farms.“ („Two Letters on the Flour Trade and the\n Dearness of Corn. By a Person in Business. Lond. 1767“, p.\n 19, 20.)]  oder Kaufmanns-Pachten [Note: „Merchant-farms.“ (An Inquiry into the Present High Prices\n of Provisions. Lond. 1767, p. 11, Note. Diese gute Schrift, die anonym\n erschien, verfasst von dem Rev. Nathaniel Forster.)] \n nannte, und das Landvolk als Proletariat für die Industrie „freisetzen“.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0016","text":"Das 18. Jahrh. begriff jedoch noch nicht in demselben Mass wie das\n 19. die Identität zwischen Nationalreichthum und Volks-\n armuth. Daher heftigste Polemik in der ökonomischen Literatur jener\n Zeit über die „inclosure of commons“. Ich gebe aus dem massen-\n haften Material, das mir vorliegt, einige wenige Stellen, weil dadurch leb-\n haft die Zustände veranschaulicht werden.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0017","text":"„In vielen Pfarreien von Hertfordshire“, schreibt eine entrüstete\n Feder, „sind 24 Pachten von 50 bis 150 Acres auf 3 zusammenge-\n [Note: sache aber ist, dass die Pächter so das ganze Recht an den Gemeinde-\n ländereien usurpiren.“ („A Political Enquiry into the Conse-\n quences of enclosing Waste Lands. Lond. 1785“, p. 75.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"711\", \"facs\": \"#f0730\"} --> schmolzen“ [Note: Thomas Wright: „A short address to the Public on the\n Monopoly of large farms. 1778“, p. 2, 3.] . „In Northamptonshire und Lincolnshire hat der Ein-\n schluss der Gemeindeländereien sehr vorgeherrscht und die meisten aus\n den enclosures entsprungenen neuen Lordschaften sind in Weideland ver-\n wandelt; in Folge davon haben viele Lordschaften jetzt nicht 50 Acres\n unter dem Pflug, wo früher 1500 gepflügt wurden … Ruinen früherer\n Wohnhäuser, Scheunen, Ställe u. s. w. sind die einzigen Spuren der früheren\n Einwohner. Hunderte von Häusern und Familien sind an manchen Plätzen\n zusammengeschrumpft auf 8 oder 10. Der Grundeigenthümer in den\n meisten Pfarreien, wo der Einschluss erst seit 15 oder 20 Jahren vor-\n ging, sind sehr wenige in Vergleich zu den Zahlen, von denen das Land im\n offnen Feldzustand bebaut wurde. Es ist nichts Ungewöhnliches, 4 oder\n 5 reiche Viehmäster grosse, jüngst eingeschlossene Lordschaften usurpiren\n zu sehn, die sich früher in der Hand von 20—30 Pächtern und vielen\n kleineren Eigenthümern und Insassen befanden. Alle diese sind mit\n ihren Familien aus ihrem Besitzthum herausgeworfen\n worden, nebst vielen andren Familien, die durch sie beschäftigt und er-\n halten wurden“ [Note: Rev. Mr. Addington: „Enquiry into the Reasons for or\n against enclosing open fields. Lond. 1772“, p. 37—43 passim.] . Es war nicht nur brachliegendes, sondern oft, unter\n bestimmter Zahlung an die Gemeinde, oder gemeinschaftlich bebautes\n Land, das unter dem Vorwand der „enclosure“ vom angränzenden Landlord\n annexirt wurde. „Ich spreche hier vom Einschluss offner Felder und Lände-\n reien, die bereits bébaut sind. Selbst die Schriftsteller, welche die Inclosures\n vertheidigen, geben zu, dass sie in diesem Fall den Feldbau vermindern, die\n Preise der Lebensmittel erhöhen und Entvölkerung produciren … und\n selbst die inclosure wüster Ländereien, wie jetzt betrieben, raubt dem\nArmen einen Theil seiner Subsistenzmittel und schwellt\n Pachten auf, die bereits zu gross sind“ [Note: Dr. R. Price l. c. v. II, p. 155. Man lese Forster, Addington, Kent,\n Price und James Anderson, und vergleiche das elende Sykophantengeschwätz Mac\n Culloch’s in seinem Katalog: „The Literature of Political Economy.\n Lond. 1845.“] . „Wenn“, sagt Dr. Price,\n „das Land in die Hände einiger weniger grossen Pächter geräth, werden\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"712\", \"facs\": \"#f0731\"} --> die kleinen Pächter (früher von ihm bezeichnet als „eine Menge kleiner\n Eigenthümer und Pächter, die sich selbst und Familien erhalten durch das\n Produkt des von ihnen bestellten Landes, durch Schafe, Geflügel, Schweine\n u. s. w., die sie auf das Gemeindeland schicken, so dass sie wenig Ge-\n legenheit zum Kauf von Subsistenzmitteln haben“) verwandelt in\n Leute, die ihre Subsistenz durch Arbeit für Andre gewinnen müssen\n und gezwungen sind, für alles, was sie brauchen, zu Markt zu gehn\n … Es wird vielleicht mehr Arbeit verrichtet, weil mehr Zwang\n dazu herrscht … Städte und Manufakturen werden wachsen, weil mehr\n Leute zu ihnen verjagt werden, welche Beschäftigung suchen.\n Diess ist der Weg, worin die Koncentration der Pachten naturgemäss\n wirkt und worin sie, seit vielen Jahren, in diesem Königreich thatsächlich\n gewirkt hat“ [Note: l. c. p. 147.] . Er fasst die Gesammtwirkung der inclosures so zu-\n sammen: „Im Ganzen hat sich die Lage der niederen Volksklassen fast in\n ieder Hinsicht verschlechtert, die kleineren Grundbesitzer und Pächter sind\n herabgedrückt auf den Stand von Taglöhnern und Miethlingen; und zur sel-\n ben Zeit ist der Lebensgewinn in diesem Zustand schwieriger ge-\n worden“ [Note: l. c. p. 159. Man erinnert sich an das alte Rom. „Die Reichen hatten sich\n des grössten Theils der ungetheilten Ländereien bemächtigt. Sie vertrauten den\n Zeitumständen, dass sie ihnen nicht mehr abgenommen würden, und kauften daher\n die in ihrer Nähe gelegenen Stücke der Armen, zum Theil mit deren Willen, zum\n Theil nahmen sie ihnen mit Gewalt, so dass sie nur mehr weit ausgedehnte Do-\n mänen statt einzelner Felder bebauten. Sie gebrauchten dabei Sklaven zum Land-\n bau und zur Viehzucht, weil ihnen freie Leute weg von der Arbeit zum Kriegs-\n dienst genommen worden wären. Der Besitz von Sklaven brachte ihnen auch\n insofern grossen Gewinn, als sich diese wegen ihrer Befreiung vom Kriegsdienst\n ungefährdet vermehren konnten und eine Menge Kinder bekamen. So zogen die\n Mächtigen durchaus allen Reichthum an sich und die ganze Gegend wimmelte von\n Sklaven. Der Italer dagegen wurden immer weniger, aufgerieben wie sie waren\n durch Armuth, Abgaben und Kriegsdienst. Traten aber auch Zeiten des Friedens\n ein, so waren sie zu vollkommener Unthätigkeit verdammt, weil die Reichen im\n Besitze des Bodens waren, und statt freier Leute Sklaven zum Ackerbau ge-\n brauchten.“ (Appian: „Römische Geschichte“ 1, 7.) Diese Stelle\n bezieht sich auf die Zeit vor den licinischen Gesetzen. Der Kriegsdienst, der den\n Ruin der römischen Plebejer so sehr beschleunigte, war auch ein Hauptmittel,] . In der That wirkten Usurpation des Gemeindelands und die\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"713\", \"facs\": \"#f0732\"} --> sie begleitende Revolution der Agrikultur so akut auf die Ackerbauar-\n beiter, dass, nach Eden selbst, zwischen 1765 und 1780 ihr Lohn anfing\nunter das Minimum zu fallen und durch officielle Armenunterstützung\n ergänzt zu werden. Ihr Arbeitslohn, sagt er, „genügte nicht mehr für die\n absoluten Lebensbedürfnisse.“","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0018","text":"Hören wir noch einen Augenblick einen Vertheidiger der enclosures\n und Gegner des Dr. Price. „Es ist ein durchaus falscher Schluss, dass\nEntvölkerung vorhanden, weil man Leute nicht länger ihre Arbeit im\n offnen Feld verwüsten sieht. Sind ihrer jetzt weniger auf dem Land,\n so sind ihrer desto mehr in den Städten … Wenn nach Verwandlung\n kleiner Bauern in Leute, die für andere arbeiten müssen, mehr\n Arbeit flüssig gemacht wird, so ist das ja ein Vortheil, den die\n Nation (wozu die Verwandelten natürlich nicht gehören) wünschen\n muss … Das Produkt wird grösser sein, wenn ihre kombinirte Arbeit\n auf einer Pacht angewandt wird: so wird Surplusprodukt für die Manu-\n fakturen gebildet, und dadurch werden Manufakturen, eine der Minen die-\n ser Nation, im Verhältniss zum producirten Kornquantum vermehrt“ [Note: „An Inquiry into the Connection between the present\n Prices of Provision etc.“, p. 124, 129. Aehnlich, aber mit entgegengesetzter\n Tendenz: „Working men are driven from their cottages, and forced into the\n towns to seek for employment; — but then a larger surplus is obtained, and\n thus Capital is augmented.“ (The Perils of the Nation. 2nd ed.\n Lond. 1843, p. XIV.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0019","text":"Die stoische Seelenruhe, womit der politische Oekonom frechste Schän\n dung des „heiligen Rechts des Eigenthums“ und gröbste Gewaltthat wider Per-\n sonen betrachtet, soweit sie erheischt sind, um die Grundlage der kapita-\n listischen Produktionsweise herzustellen, zeigt uns u.a. der über-\n dem noch torystisch gefärbte und „philanthropische“ Sir F. M. Eden. Die\n ganze Reihe von Raubthaten, Greueln und Volkselend, welche die gewaltsame\n Volksexpropriation vom letzten Drittel des 15. bis zum Ende des 18. Jahrh.\n begleiten, treibt ihn nur zur „comfortablen“ Schlussreflexion: „Die rich-\n tige (due) Proportion zwischen Acker- und Viehland musste\n hergestellt werden. Noch im ganzen 14. und grössten Theil des 15.\n Jahrh. kam 1 Acre Viehweide auf 2, 3, und selbst 4 Acres Ackerland. In der\n [Note: wodurch Karl der Grosse die Verwandlung freier deutscher Bauern in Leibeigne\n treibhausmässig förderte.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"714\", \"facs\": \"#f0733\"} --> Mitte des 16 Jahrhunderts verwandelte sich die Proportion in 3 Acres\n Viehland auf 2, später von 2 Acres Viehweide auf 1 Acre Ackerland, bis\n endlich die richtige Proportion von 3 Acres Viehland auf\n 1 Acre Ackerland herauskam.“","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0020","text":"Im 19. Jahrhundert verlor sich natürlich selbst die Erinnerung des\n Zusammenhangs zwischen Ackerbauer und Gemeindeeigenthum. Von\n späterer Zeit gar nicht zu reden, welchen Farthing Ersatz erhielt das\n Landvolk jemals für die 3,511,770 Acres Gemeinland, die ihm zwischen\n1801 und 1831 geraubt und parlamentarisch den Landlords von den\n Landlords geschenkt wurden?","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0021","text":"Der letzte grosse Expropriationsprozess der Ackerbauer\n von Grund und Boden endlich ist das s. g. „Clearing of Estates“\n (Lichten der Güter, in der That Wegfegung der Menschen von den-\n selben). Alle bisher betrachteten englischen Methoden kulminirten im\n „Lichten“. Wie man bei der Schilderung des modernen Zustands im vori-\n gen Abschnitt sah, geht es jetzt, wo unabhängige Bauern nicht mehr weg-\n zufegen sind, bis zum „Lichten“ der Cottages fort, so dass die Acker-\n bauarbeiter auf dem Boden, den sie bestellen, selbst nicht mehr den nöthigen\n Raum zur eignen Behausung finden. Indess unterscheidet sich das eigent-\n liche „Clearing of Estates“ durch den mehr systematischen Charak-\n ter, die Grösse der Stufenleiter, worauf die Operation auf einmal ausgeführt\n wird (in Schottland auf Arealen so gross, wie deutsche Fürstenthümer), und\n durch die eigenthümliche Form des Grundeigenthums, welches so gewaltsam\n in modernes Privateigenthum verwandelt wird. Diess Eigenthum war Eigen-\n thum des Clans, der Chef oder „grosse Mann“ nur Titulareigenthümer\n als Repräsentant des Clans, wie die Königin von England Titulareigenthü-\n merin des englischen Grund und Bodens ist [Note: „A king of England might as well claim to drive his subjects into the\n tsea.“ (F. W. Newman l. c. p. 132.)] . Diese Revolution, welche\n in Schottland nach der letzten Schilderhebung des Prätendenten begann,\n kann man in ihren ersten Phasen verfolgen bei Sir James Steuart [Note: Steuart sagt: „Die Rente dieser Länder (er überträgt irrthümlich diese\n ökonomische Kategorie auf den Tribut der taskmen an den Clanchef) ist durch-\n aus unbedeutend im Vergleich zu ihrem Umfang, aber, was die Personenzahl be-\n rifft, welche eine Pacht erhält, wird man vielleicht finden, dass ein Stück Boden]  und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"715\", \"facs\": \"#f0734\"} --> James Anderson [Note: James Anderson: „Observations on the means of excit-\n ing a spirit of National Industry etc.“ Edinburgh 1777.] . Im 18. Jahrh. wurde zugleich den vom Land verjag-\n ten Gaelen die Auswanderung verboten, um sie gewaltsam nach Glasgow\n und anderen Fabrikstädten zu treiben [Note: 1860 wurden gewaltsam Expropriirte nach Kanada exportirt unter\n falschen Versprechungen. Einige flohen in die Berge und benachbarten Eilande.\n Sie wurden von Polizisten verfolgt, kamen zum Handgemenge mit ihnen, und ent-\n kamen.] . Als Beispiel der im 19. Jahrh.\n herrschenden Methode [Note: „In den Hochlanden“, sagt Buchanan, der Kommentator A. Smith’s,\n 1814, „wird der alte Eigenthumszustand täglich gewaltsam umgewälzt …\n Der Landlord, ohne Rücksicht auf die Erbpächter (auch diess ist hier irrig ange-\n wandte Kategorie), bietet das Land dem höchsten Bieter an, und wenn dieser ein\n Verbesserer (improver) ist, führt er unmittelbar ein neues Kultursystem ein. Der\n Boden, früher übersät mit kleinen Bauern, war im Verhältniss zu seinem Produkt\n bevölkert; unter dem neuen System verbesserter Kultur und vermehrter Renten,\n wird grösstmöglichstes Produkt zu möglichst geringen Kosten erhalten und zu\n diesem Behuf werden die nun nutzlos gewordenen Hände entfernt. Die Auswürf-\n linge des Heimlands suchen Subsistenz in den Fabrikstädten u. s. w.“ (David\n Buchanan: „Observations on etc. A. Smith’s Wealth of Nations.\n Edinb. 1814.“) „Die schottischen Grossen haben Familien expropriirt, wie sie\n Unkraut ausroden würden, sie haben Dorfschaften und ihre Bevölkerung behan-\n delt, wie die Indier in ihrer Rache die Höhlen wilder Bestien … Der Mensch\n wird verschachert für ein Schafvliess oder eine Hammelkeule, ja für weniger …\n Bei dem Einfall in die Nordprovinzen China’s schlug man im Mongolenrath vor,\n die Einwohner auszurotten und ihr Land in Weide zu verwandeln. Diesen Vor-\n schlag haben viele hochschottische Landlords in ihrem eignen Land gegen\n ihre eignen Landsleute ausgeführt.“ (George Ensor: „An Inquiry\n concerning the Population of Nations. Lond. 1818“, p. 215, 216.)]  genügen hier die „Lichtungen“ der Gräfin\n von Sutherland. Diese ökonomisch geschulte Person beschloss gleich\n bei ihrem Regierungsantritt eine ökonomische Radikalkur vorzunehmen und\n die ganze Grafschaft, deren Einwohnerschaft durch frühere, ähnliche Pro-\n zesse bereits auf 15,000 zusammengeschmolzen war, in Schaftriften zu\n verwandeln. Von 1814 bis 1820 wurden diese 15,000 Einwohner, un-\n gefähr 3000 Familien, systematisch verjagt und ausgerottet. Alle ihre\n Dörfer wurden zerstört und niedergebrannt, alle ihre Felder in Weide ver-\n [Note: in den Hochlanden von Schottland zehnmal mehr Leute ernährt, als Land von der-\n selben Grösse in den reichsten Provinzen.“ (l. c. v. I, ch. XVI.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"716\", \"facs\": \"#f0735\"} --> wandelt. Britische Soldaten wurden zur Execution kommandirt und kamen\n zu Schlägen mit den Eingebornen. Eine alte Frau verbrannte in den\n Flammen der Hütte, die sie zu verlassen sich weigerte. So eignete sich diese\n Madame 794,000 Acres Land an, das seit undenklicher Zeit dem\n Clan gehörte. Den vertriebenen Eingebornen wies sie am Seegestad un-\n gefähr 6000 Acres zu, 2 Acres per Familie. Die 6000 Acres hatten bis-\n her wüst gelegen und den Eigenthümern kein Einkommen abgeworfen.\n Die Gräfin ging in ihrem Nobelgefühl so weit den Acre zu 2 sh. 6 d. Rente\n im Durchschnitt den Clanleuten zu verpachten, die seit Jahrhunderten ihr\n Blut für die Familie vergossen hatten. Das ganze geraubte Clanland theilte\n sie in 29 grosse Schafpachten, jede bewohnt von einer einzigen Familie,\n meist englische Pachtknechte. Im Jahr 1825 waren die 15,000 Gaelen\n bereits ersetzt durch 131,000 Schafe. Der an das Seegestad geworfne\n Theil der Aborigines suchte vom Fischfang zu leben. Sie wurden Amphi-\n bien, und lebten, wie ein englischer Schriftsteller sagt, halb auf dem\n Land und halb auf dem Wasser und lebten mit alledem nur halb von\n beiden [Note: Als die jetzige Gräfin Sutherland die Mrs. Beecher Stowe, Verfasserin\n von „Uncle Tom’s Cabin“, mit grossem Prunk in London empfing, um ihre\n Sympathie für die Negersklaven der amerikanischen Republik auszustellen — was\n sie, nebst ihren Mitaristokratinnen, wohlweise während des Bürgerkriegs unterliess,\n wo jedes „noble“ englische Herz für die Sklavenhalter schlug — stellte ich in der\nNew-York Tribune die Verhältnisse der Sutherlandschen Sklaven dar.\n (Stellenweis ausgezogen von Carey in „The Slave Trade. London 1853“,\n p. 202, 203.) Mein Artikel ward in einem schottischen Blatt abgedruckt und rief eine\n artige Polemik zwischen letzterem und den Sykophanten der Sutherlands hervor.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0022","text":"Aber die braven Gaelen sollten noch schwerer ihre bergromantische\n Idolatrie für die „grossen Männer“ des Clans abbüssen. Der Fischge-\n ruch stieg den grossen Männern in die Nase. Sie witterten etwas Profit-\n liches dahinter und verpachteten das Seegestade den grossen Fischhänd-\n lern von London. Die Gaelen wurden zum zweitenmal verjagt [Note: Interessantes über diesen Fischhandel findet man in Herrn David\n Urquhart’s „Portfolio. New Series.“] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0023","text":"Endlich aber wird ein Theil der Schaftriften rückverwandelt in\nJagdrevier. Man weiss, dass es keine ernsthaften Wälder in Eng-\n land giebt. Das Wild in den Parks der Grossen ist konstitutionelles Haus-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"717\", \"facs\": \"#f0736\"} --> vieh, fett wie Londoner Aldermen. Schottland ist daher das letzte Asyl\n der „noblen Passion“. „In den Hochlanden,“ sagt Somers, 1848,\n „sind die Waldungen sehr ausgedehnt worden. Hier auf der einen Seite\n von Gaick habt ihr den neuen Wald von Glenfeshie, und dort, auf der\n andern Seite, den neuen Wald von Ardverikie. In derselben Linie habt\n ihr den Bleak-Mount, eine ungeheure Wüste, neulich errichtet. Von Ost zu\n West, von der Nachbarschaft von Aberdeen bis zu den Klippen von Obon,\n habt ihr jetzt eine fortlaufende Waldlinie, während sich in andern Theilen\n der Hochlande die neuen Wälder von Loch Archaig, Glengarry, Glen-\n moriston u. s. w. befinden … Die Verwandlung ihres Landes in Schaf-\n weide trieb die Gaelen auf unfruchtbareren Boden. Jetzt fängt Rothwild\n an das Schaf zu ersetzen und treibt jene in noch zermalmenderes Elend …\n Die Wildwaldungen und das Volk können nicht neben einander existiren.\n Eins oder das andre muss jedenfalls den Platz räumen. Lasst die\n Jagden in Zahl und Umfang im nächsten Vierteljahrhundert wachsen wie\n im vergangenen, und ihr werdet keinen Gaelen mehr auf seiner heimischen\n Erde finden. Diese Bewegung unter den Hochlands-Eigenthümern ist\n theils der Mode geschuldet, aristokratischem Kitzel, Jagdliebhaberei u. s. w.,\n theils aber betreiben sie den Wildhandel ausschliesslich mit einem\n Auge auf den Profit. Denn es ist Thatsache, dass ein Stück Berg-\n land, in Jagdung angelegt, in vielen Fällen ungleich profitabler ist denn\n als Schaftrift … Der Liebhaber, der ein Jagdrevier sucht, beschränkt\n sein Angebot nur durch die Weite seiner Börse … Leiden sind über die\n Hochlande verhängt worden nicht minder grausam als die Politik normän-\n nischer Könige sie über England verhing. Rothwild hat freieren Spiel-\n raum erhalten, während die Menschen in engen und engeren Zirkel ge-\n hetzt wurden … Eine Freiheit des Volks nach der andern ward ihm\n geraubt … Und die Unterdrückung wächst noch täglich. Die Lichtung\n und Vertreibung des Volks werden von den Eigenthümern als festes Princip\n verfolgt, als eine agrikole Nothwendigkeit, ganz wie Bäume und Gesträuch\n in den Wildnissen Amerika’s und Australiens weggefegt werden, und die\n Operation geht ihren ruhigen, geschäftsmässigen Gang“ [Note: Robert Somers: „Letters from the Highlands; or, the\n Famine of 1847. Lond. 1848“, p. 12—28 passim. Diese Briefe erschienen\n ursprünglich in der Times. Der Oekonom erklärte natürlich die Hungersnoth] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0024","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"718\", \"facs\": \"#f0737\"} -->","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0025","text":"Der Raub der Kirchengüter, die fraudulente Veräusserung der Staats-\n domänen, der Diebstahl des Gemeindeeigenthums, die usurpatorische und\n mit rücksichtslosem Terrorismus vollzogne Verwandlung von feudalem und\n Claneigenthum in modernes Privateigenthum, es waren ebenso viele idylli-\n sche Methoden der ursprünglichen Accumulation. Durch\n sie ward das Feld für die kapitalistische Agrikultur erobert, der Grund\n [Note: der Gaelen von 1847 aus ihrer — Uebervölkerung. Jedenfalls „drückten“ sie\n auf ihre Nahrungsmittel. — Das „Clearing of Estates“, oder, wie es in Deutsch-\n land hiess, „Bauernlegen“, machte sich hier besonders geltend nach dem\n dreissigjährigen Krieg und rief noch 1790 in Kursachsen Bauernaufstände hervor.\n Es herrschte namentlich in Ostdeutschland. In den meisten Provinzen Preussens\n sicherte erst Friedrich II. den Bauern Eigenthumsrecht. Nach der Eroberung\n Schlesiens zwang er die Grundherrn zur Wiederherstellung der Hütten, Scheunen\n u. s. w., zur Ausstattung der Bauerngüter mit Vieh und Geräth. Er brauchte\n Soldaten für seine Armee und Steuerpflichtige für seinen Staatsschatz. Welches ange-\n nehme Leben übrigens der Bauer unter Friedrich’s Finanzunwesen und Regierungs-\n mischmasch von Despotismus, Bureaukratie und Feudalismus führte, mag man\n aus folgender Stelle seines Bewunderers Mirabeau ersehn: „Le lin fait donc\n une des grandes richesses du cultivateur dans le Nord de l’Allemagne. Mal-\n heureusement pour l’espèce humaine, ce n’est qu’une resource contre la misère,\n et non un moyen de bien-être. Les impôts directs, les corvées, les servitudes de\n tout genre, écrasent le cultivateur allemand, qui paie encore des impôts indirects\n dans tout ce qu’il achète … et pour comble de ruine, il n’ose pas vendre ses produc-\n tions où et comme il le veut; il n’ose pas acheter ce dont il a besoin aux mar-\n chands qui pourraient le lui livrer au meilleur prix. Toutes ces causes le ruinent\n insensiblement, et il se trouverait hors d’état de payer les impôts directs à l’éché-\n ance sans la filerie; elle lui offre une resource, en occupant utilement sa femme,\n ses enfants, ses servants, ses valets, et lui-même: mais qu’elle pénible vie même\n aidée de ce secours. En été, il travaille comme un forçat au labourage et à la\n récolte; il se couche à 9 heures et se lève à deux, pour suffire aux travaux; en\n hiver il devrait réparer ses forces par un plus grand repos; mais il manquera de\n grains pour le pain et les semailles, s’il se defait des denrées qu’il faudrait vendre\n pour payer les impôts. Il faut donc filer pour suppléer à ce vide … il faut y\n apporter la plus grande assiduité. Aussi le paysan se couche-t-il en hiver à minuit,\n une heure, et se lève à cinq ou six; ou bien il se couche à neuf, et se lève à deux,\n et cela tous les jours de sa vie, si ce n’est le dimanche. Ces excès de veille et de\n travail usent la nature humaine, et de là vient qu’ hommes et femmes vieillissent\n beaucoup plûtot dans les campagnes que dans les villes.“ (Mirabeau l. c.\n t. III, p. 212 sqq.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"719\", \"facs\": \"#f0738\"} --> und Boden dem Kapital einverleibt, der städtischen Industrie die nöthige\n Zufuhr von vogelfreiem Proletariat geschaffen.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0026","text":"Die durch Auflösung der feudalen Gefolgschaften und durch stoss-\n weise, gewaltsame Expropriation von Grund und Boden Verjagten,\n diess vogelfreie Proletariat konnte unmöglich eben so rasch von der auf-\n kommenden Manufaktur absorbirt werden als es auf die Welt gesetzt\n ward. Andrerseits konnten die plötzlich aus ihrer gewohnten Lebensbahn\n Herausgeschleuderten sich nicht eben so plötzlich in die Disciplin des neuen\n Zustandes finden. Sie verwandelten sich massenhaft in Bettler, Räuber, Va-\n gabunden, zum Theil aus Neigung, in den meisten Fällen durch den Zwang\n der Umstände. Ende des 15. und während des ganzen 16. Jahrh. daher\nin ganz Westeuropa eine Blutgesetzgebung wider Vagabun-\n dage. Die Väter der jetzigen Arbeiterklasse wurden zunächst für die ihnen\n angethane Verwandlung in Vagabunden und Paupers gezüchtigt. Die\n Gesetzgebung behandelte sie als „freiwillige“ Verbrecher und unter-\n stellte, dass es von ihrem guten Willen abhänge, in den nicht\n mehr existirenden alten Verhältnissen fortzuarbeiten.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0027","text":"In England begann jene Gesetzgebung unter Heinrich VII.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0028","text":"Heinrich VIII., 1530: Alte und arbeitsunfähige Bettler erhal-\n ten eine Bettellicenz. Dagegen Auspeitschung und Einsperrung für hand-\n feste Vagabunden. Sie sollen an einen Karren hinten angebunden und\n gegeisselt werden, bis das Blut von ihrem Körper strömt, dann einen Eid\n schwören, zu ihrem Geburtsplatz, oder dorthin, wo sie die letzten drei\n Jahre gewohnt, zurückzukehren und „sich an die Arbeit zu setzen“ (to\n put himself to labour). Welche grausame Ironie! 27 Heinrich VIII.\n wird das vorige Statut wiederholt, aber durch neue Zusätze verschärft.\n Bei zweiter Ertappung auf Vagabundage soll die Auspeitschung wieder-\n holt und das halbe Ohr abgeschnitten, bei dritter Recidive aber der Be-\n troffene als schwerer Verbrecher und Feind des Gemeinwesens hingerichtet\n werden.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0029","text":"Edward VI.: Ein Statut aus seinem ersten Regierungsjahr, 1547,\n verordnet, dass wenn Jemand zu arbeiten weigert, soll er als Sklave der\n Person zugeurtheilt werden, die ihn als Müssiggänger denuncirt hat. Der\n Meister soll seinen Sklaven mit Brod und Wasser nähren, schwachem Ge-\n tränk und solchen Fleischabfällen, die er passend dünkt. Er hat das\n Recht, ihn zu jeder auch noch so eklen Arbeit durch Auspeitschung und\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"720\", \"facs\": \"#f0739\"} --> Ankettung zu treiben. Wenn sich der Sklave für 14 Tage entfernt, ist\n er zur Sklaverei auf Lebenszeit verurtheilt und soll auf Stirn oder Backen\n mit dem Buchstaben S gebrandmarkt, wenn er zum drittenmal fortläuft,\n als Staatsverräther hingerichtet werden. Der Meister kann ihn verkaufen,\n vermachen, als Sklaven ausdingen, ganz wie anderes bewegliches Gut und\n Vieh. Unternehmen die Sklaven etwas gegen die Herrschaft, so sollen sie\n ebenfalls hingerichtet werden. Friedensrichter sollen auf Information den\n Kerls nachspüren. Findet sich, dass ein Herumstreicher drei Tage gelun-\n gert hat, so soll er nach seinem Geburtsort gebracht, mit rothglühendem\n Eisen auf die Brust mit dem Zeichen V gebrandmarkt, und dort in Ketten\n auf der Strasse oder zu sonstigen Diensten verwandt werden. Giebt der\n Vagabund einen falschen Geburtsort an, so soll er zur Strafe der lebens-\n längliche Sklave dieses Orts, der Einwohner oder Korporation sein und\n mit S gebrandmarkt werden. Alle Personen haben das Recht, den Vaga-\n bunden ihre Kinder wegzunehmen und als Lehrlinge, Jungen bis zum\n 24. Jahr, Mädchen bis zum 20. Jahr, zu halten. Laufen sie weg, so sol-\n len sie bis zu diesem Alter die Sklaven der Lehrmeister sein, die sie in\n Ketten legen, geisseln u. s. w. können, wie sie wollen. Jeder Meister\n darf einen eisernen Ring um Hals, Arme oder Beine seines Sklaven legen,\n damit er ihn besser kennt und seiner sichrer ist [Note: Der Verfasser des „Essay on Trade etc. 1770“ bemerkt: „Unter\n der Regierung Edward’s VI. scheinen sich die Engländer in der That mit vollem\n Ernst auf Encouragirung der Manufakturen und Beschäftigung der Armen verlegt\n zu haben. Diess ersehn wir aus einem merkwürdigen Statut, worin es heisst, dass\n alle Vagabunden gebrandmarkt werden sollen u. s. w.“ (l. c. p. 8.)] . Der letzte Theil\n dieses Statuts sieht vor, dass gewisse Arme von dem Ort oder den Individuen\n beschäftigt werden sollen, die ihnen zu essen und zu trinken geben und\n Arbeit für sie finden wollen. Diese Sorte Pfarreisklaven hat sich bis tief\n ins 19. Jahrhundert in England erhalten unter dem Namen roundsmen\n (Umgeher).","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0030","text":"Elisabeth, 1572: Bettler ohne Licenz und über 14 Jahre alt\n sollen hart gepeitscht und am linken Ohrlappen gebrandmarkt werden,\nfalls sie keiner für zwei Jahre in Dienst nehmen will; im\n Wiederholungsfall, wenn über 18 Jahre alt, sollen sie — hingerichtet wer-\n den, falls sie Niemand für zwei Jahre in Dienst nehmen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"721\", \"facs\": \"#f0740\"} --> will, bei dritter Recidive aber ohne Gnade als Staatsverräther hingerich-\n tet werden. Aehnliche Statute: 18 Elisabeth c. 13 und 1597.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0031","text":"Jakob I.: Eine herumwandernde und bettelnde Person wird für\n einen Landstreicher und Vagabunden erklärt. Die Friedensrichter in den Petty\n Sessions sind bevollmächtigt, sie öffentlich auspeitschen zu lassen und bei\n erster Ertappung 6 Monate, bei zweiter 2 Jahre ins Gefängniss zu sperren.\n Während des Gefängnisses soll sie so oft und so viel gepeitscht werden,\n als die Friedensrichter für gut halten … Die unverbesserlichen und ge-\n fährlichen Landstreicher sollen auf der linken Schulter mit R gebrandmarkt\n und zur Zwangsarbeit gesetzt, und wenn man sie wieder auf dem Bettel\n ertappt, ohne Gnade und ohne geistlichen Beistand hingerichtet werden.\n Diese Anordnungen, gesetzg ültig bis in die erste Zeit des 18. Jahrhun-\n derts, wurden erst aufgehoben durch 12 Anna 23.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0032","text":"Aehnliche Gesetze in Frankreich, wo sich Mitte des 17. Jahrhunderts\n ein Vagabundenkönigreich (truands) zu Paris etablirt hatte. Noch in der\n ersten Zeit Ludwig’s XVI. (Ordonnanz vom 13. Juli 1777) sollte jeder ge-\n sund gebaute Mensch vom 16. bis 60. Jahr, wenn ohne Existenzmittel und\n Ausübung einer Profession, auf die Galeeren geschickt werden. Aehnlich\n das Statut Karl’s V. für die Niederlande vom Oktober 1537, das erste\n Edikt der Staaten und Städte von Holland vom 19. März 1614, das Pla-\n kat der Vereinigten Provinzen vom 25. Juni 1649 u. s. w.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0033","text":"So wurde das von Grund und Boden gewaltsam expropriirte, ver-\n jagte und zum grossen Vagabunden gemachte Landvolk durch gro-\n tesk-terroristische Gesetze in eine dem System der Lohnarbeit\n nothwendige Disciplin hineingepeitscht, gebrandmarkt, gefoltert.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0034","text":"Es ist nicht genug, dass die Arbeitsbedingungen auf den einen Pol als\n Kapital treten und auf den andern Pol Menschen, welche nichts zu verkau-\n fen haben als ihre Arbeitskraft. Es genügt auch nicht sie zu zwingen,\n sich freiwillig zu verkaufen. Im Fortgang der kapitalistischen Produktion\n entwickelt sich eine Arbeiterklasse, die aus Erziehung, Tradition, Gewohn-\n heit die Anforderungen jener Produktionsweise als selbstverständliche Na-\n turgesetze anerkennt. Die Organisation des ausgebildeten kapitalisti-\n schen Produktionsprozesses bricht jeden Widerstand, die beständige Erzeu-\n gung einer relativen Uebervölkerung hält das Gesetz der Zufuhr von und\n Nachfrage nach Arbeit, und daher den Arbeitslohn, in einem den Verwer-\n thungsbedürfnissen des Kapitals entsprechenden Gleise, der stumme Zwang\n [Running matter: I. 46] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"722\", \"facs\": \"#f0741\"} --> der ökonomischen Verhältnisse besiegelt die Herrschaft des Kapitalisten\n über den Arbeiter. Ausserökonomische, unmittelbare Gewalt wird daher\n nur ausnahmsweise angewandt. Für den gewöhnlichen Gang der Dinge\n kann der Arbeiter den „Naturgesetzen der Produktion“ über-\n lassen werden, d. h. seiner aus den Produktionsbedingungen selbst ent-\n springenden, durch sie garantirten und verewigten Abhängigkeit vom Ka-\n pital. Anders während der historischen Genesis der kapitalistischen Pro-\n duktion. Die aufkommende Bourgeoisie braucht und verwendet die\nStaatsgewalt, um den Arbeitslohn zu „reguliren“, d. h. in-\n nerhalb der Plusmacherei zusagender Schranken zu zwängen, um den Ar-\n beitstag zu verlängern, und den Arbeiter selbst im normalen Abhän-\n gigkeitsgrad zu erhalten. Es ist diess ein wesentliches Moment der\n s. g. ursprünglichen Accumulation.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0035","text":"Die Klasse der Lohnarbeiter, die in der letzten Hälfte des 14. Jahrh.\n entstand, bildete damals und im folgenden Jahrhundert nur einen sehr ge-\n ringen Volksbestandtheil, der in seiner Stellung stark beschützt war durch\n die selbstständige Bauernwirthschaft auf dem Land und die Zunftorganisa-\n tion der städtischen Industrie. In Land und Stadt standen sich Meister\n und Arbeiter social nahe. Die Subsumtion der Arbeit unter das Kapital\n war nur formell, d. h. die Produktionsweise selbst besass noch keinen\n specifisch kapitalistischen Charakter. Das variable Element des Kapitals\n wog sehr vor über sein constantes. Die Nachfrage nach Lohnarbeit wuchs\n daher rasch mit jeder Accumulation des Kapitals, während die Zufuhr von\n Lohnarbeit nur langsam nachfolgte. Ein grosser Theil des nationalen Pro-\n dukts, später in Accumulationsfonds des Kapitals verwandelt,\n ging damals noch in den Konsumtionsfonds des Arbeiters ein.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0036","text":"Die Gesetzgebung über die Lohnarbeit, von Haus aus auf Exploita-\n tion des Arbeiters gemünzt und ihm in ihrem Fortgang stets gleich feind-\n lich [Note: „Whenever the legislature attempts to regulate the differences between\n masters and their workmen, its counsellors are always the masters“, sagt\n A. Smith. „L’esprit des lois, c’est la propriété“, sagt Linguet.] , wird in England eröffnet durch das Statute of Labourers\n Edward’s III., 1349. Ihm entspricht in Frankreich die Ordonnanz\n von 1350, erlassen im Namen des Königs Jean. Die englische und die\n französische Gesetzgebung laufen parallel und sind dem Inhalt nach iden-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"723\", \"facs\": \"#f0742\"} --> tisch. Soweit die Arbeitsstatuten Verlängerung des Arbeitstags\n zu erzwingen suchen, komme ich nicht auf sie zurück, da dieser Punkt\n früher (III. Kapitel, 4. Abschnitt) erörtert.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0037","text":"Das Statute of Labourers wurde erlassen auf dringende Klage\n des Hauses der Gemeinen. „Früher“, sagt naiv ein Tory, „verlangten\n die Armen so hohen Arbeitslohn, dass sie Industrie und Reichthum bedroh-\n ten. Jetzt ist ihr Lohn so niedrig, dass er ebenfalls Industrie und Reich-\n thum bedroht und vielleicht gefährlicher als damals“ [Note: „Sophisms of Free Trade. By a Barrister. Lond. 1850“,\n p. 206. Er setzt malitiös hinzu: „Die Gesetzgebung war stets bei der Hand für\n den Anwender einzuschreiten. Ist sie ohnmächtig für den Angewandten?“] . Ein gesetz-\n licher Lohntarif ward festgesetzt für Stadt und Land, für Stückwerk und\n Tagwerk. Die ländlichen Arbeiter sollen sich aufs Jahr, die städtischen\n „auf offnem Markt“ verdingen. Es wird bei Gefängnissstrafe untersagt,\n höheren als den statutarischen Lohn zu zahlen, aber der Empfang höheren\n Lohns wird stärker bestraft als seine Zahlung. So wird auch noch in\n Sect. 18 und 19 des Lehrlingsstatuts von Elisabeth zehntägige Gefäng-\n nissstrafe über den verhängt, der höheren Lohn zahlt, dagegen einund-\n zwanzigtägige Gefängnissstrafe über den, der ihn nimmt. Das Statut von\n 1360 verschärft die Strafen und ermächtigt den Meister sogar, durch\n körperlichen Zwang Arbeit zum gesetzlichen Lohntarif zu erpressen.\n Alle Kombination, Verträge, Eide u. s. w., wodurch sich Maurer und Zim-\n merleute wechselseitig banden, werden für null und nichtig erklärt. Arbei-\n terkoalition wird als schweres Verbrechen behandelt vom 14. Jahrhun-\n dert bis 1825, dem Jahr der Abschaffung der Antikoalitionsgesetze. Der\n Geist des Arbeiterstatuts von 1349 und seiner Nachgeburten leuchtet hell\n daraus hervor, dass zwar ein Maximum des Arbeitslohns von\n Staatswegen diktirt wird, aber bei Leibe kein Minimum.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0038","text":"Im 16. Jahrhundert hatte sich, wie man weiss, die Lage der Arbei-\n ter sehr verschlechtert. Der Geldlohn stieg, aber nicht im Verhältniss\n zur Depreciation des Geldes und dem entsprechenden Steigen der Waaren-\n preise. Der Lohn fiel also in der That. Dennoch dauerten die Gesetze\n zum Behuf seiner Herabdrückung fort zugleich mit dem Ohrenabschneiden und\n Brandmarken derjenigen, „die Niemand in Dienst nehmen wollte“.\n Durch das Lehrlingsstatut 5 Elisabeth 3 wurden die Friedensrichter\n [Running matter: 46*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"724\", \"facs\": \"#f0743\"} --> ermächtigt, gewisse Löhne festzusetzen und nach Jahreszeiten und Waaren-\n preisen zu modificiren. Jakob I. dehnte diese Arbeitsregulation auch auf\n Weber, Spinner und alle möglichen Arbeiterkategorieen aus [Note: Aus einer Klausel des Statuts 2, Jakob I., c. 6 ersieht man, dass gewisse\n Tuchmacher sich herausnahmen, als Friedensrichter die Löhne in ihren\n eigenen Werkstätten zu octroyiren. — In Deutschland waren namentlich nach dem\n dreissigjährigen Krieg Statuten zur Niederhaltung des Arbeitslohns häufig. „Sehr\n lästig war den Gutsherrn in dem menschenleeren Boden der Mangel an Dienst-\n boten und Arbeitern. Allen Dorfsassen wurde verboten, Kammern an ledige\n Männer und Frauen zu vermiethen, alle solche Inlieger sollten der Obrigkeit an-\n gezeigt und ins Gefängniss gesteckt werden, falls sie nicht Dienstboten\n werden wollten, auch wenn sie sich von andrer Thätigkeit erhielten, den Bauern\n um Taglohn säeten oder gar mit Geld und Getreide handelten. (Kaiserliche\n Privilegien und Sanctiones für Schlesien I, 125.) Durch ein ganzes\n Jahrhundert wird in den Verordnungen der Landesherrn immer wieder bittere\n Klage geführt über das boshafte und muthwillige Gesindel, das sich\n in die harten Bedingungen nicht fügen, mit dem gesetzlichen Lohn\n nicht zufrieden sein will; dem einzelnen Gutsherrn wird verboten, mehr zu\n geben als die Landschaft in einer Taxe festgesetzt hat. Und doch sind die Be-\n dingungen des Dienstes nach dem Krieg zuweilen noch besser, als sie 100 Jahre\n später waren; noch erhielt das Gesinde 1652 in Schlesien zweimal in der Woche\n Fleisch, noch in unsrem Jahrhundert hat es eben dort Kreise gegeben, wo sie es\n nur dreimal im Jahr erhielten. Auch der Tagelohn war nach dem Kriege höher\n als in den folgenden Jahrhunderten.“ (G. Freitag.)] , Georg II.\n die Gesetze gegen Arbeiterkoalition auf alle Manufakturen. In der eigent-\n lichen Manufakturperiode war die kapitalistische Produktionsweise hin-\n reichend erstarkt, um alle gesetzliche Regulation des Arbeitslohns eben so\n unausführbar als überflüssig zu machen, aber man liebte für den Nothfall\n das alte Arsenal offen zu halten. Noch 8 George II. verbot für Schnei-\n dergesellen in London und Umgegend mehr als 2 sh. 7½ d. Taglohn,\n ausser in Fällen allgemeiner Trauer, noch 13 George III. c. 68 überwies\n die Reglung des Arbeitslohns der Seidenwirker den Friedensrichtern, noch\n 1796 bedurfte es zweier Urtheile der höheren Gerichtshöfe zur Entschei-\n dung, ob friedensrichterliche Befehle über Arbeitslohn auch für Nicht-\n Agrikulturarbeiter gültig seien, noch 1799 bestätigte ein Parlamentsakt,\n dass der Lohn der Grubenarbeiter von Schottland durch ein Statut der\n Elisabeth und zwei schottische Akte von 1661 und 1671 regulirt sei.\n Wie sehr unterdess die Verhältnisse revolutionirt waren, bewies ein im\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"725\", \"facs\": \"#f0744\"} --> englischen Unterhaus unerhörter Vorfall. Hier, wo man seit mehr als\n 400 Jahren ausschliesslich Gesetze fabricirt hatte über das Maxi-\n mum, welches der Arbeitslohn platterdings nicht übersteigen dürfe, schlug\nWhitbread 1796 ein gesetzliches Lohnminimum für Agrikul-\n turarbeiter vor … Obgleich Pitt sich widersetzte, gab er zu, dass die\n „Lage der Armen grausam (cruel) sei“. Endlich, 1813, wurden die Gesetze\n über Lohnregulation abgeschafft. Sie waren eine lächerliche Ano-\n malie, seitdem der Kapitalist durch seine Privatgesetzgebung die\n Fabrik regulirte und durch die Armensteuer den Lohn des Landarbei-\n ters zum unentbehrlichen Minimum ergänzen liess. Die Bestimmungen\n der Arbeiterstatute über Kontrakte zwischen Meister und Lohnarbeiter,\n über Terminkündigung u. dergl., welche nur eine Civilklage gegen den\n kontraktbrüchigen Meister, aber Kriminalklage gegen den kontrakt-\n brüchigen Arbeiter erlauben, stehn bis zur Stunde in voller Blüthe.\n Die grausamen Gesetze gegen Koalition fielen 1825 vor der drohenden\n Haltung des Proletariats. Das Parlament gab sie nur widerwillig auf [Note: Die letzten Reste des Koalitionsgesetzes wurden erst 1859 beseitigt.] ,\n dasselbe Parlament, welches Jahrhunderte durch mit der cynischsten Unver-\n schämtheit als permanente Koalition der Kapitalisten gegen\n die Arbeiter funktionirt hatte.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0039","text":"Gleich im Beginn des Revolutionssturms wagte die französische Bour-\n geoisie das eben erst eroberte Associationsrecht den Arbeitern wieder zu\n entziehn. Durch Dekret vom 14. Juni 1791 erklärte sie alle Arbeiter-\nkoalition für ein „Attentat auf die Freiheit und die Er-\n klärung der Menschenrechte“, strafbar mit 500 Livres nebst\n einjähriger Entziehung der aktiven Bürgerrechte [Note: Artikel I dieses Gesetzes lautet: „L’anéantissement de toutes espèces de\ncorporations du même état et profession étant l’une des bases fondamentales\n de la constitution française, il est défendu de les rétablir de fait sous quelque\nprétexte et sous quelque forme que ce soit“. Artikel IV erklärt, dass wenn\n „des citoyens attachés aux mêmes professions, arts et métiers prenaient des déli-\n bérations, faisaient entre eux des conventions tendantes à réfuser de concert ou à\n n’accorder qu’ à un prix déterminé le secours de leur industrie ou de leurs travaux,\n les dites délibérations et conventions … seront déclarées inconstitution-\n nelles, attentatoires à la liberté et à la déclaration des droits de l’homme etc.“,\n also Staatsverbrechen, ganz wie in den alten Arbeiterstatuten. („Révolutions\n de Paris. Paris 1791“, t. III, p. 523.)] . Diess Gesetz,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"726\", \"facs\": \"#f0745\"} --> welches den Konkurrenzkampf zwischen Kapital und Arbeit staatspolizei-\n lich innerhalb der dem Kapital bequemen Schranken einzwängt, überlebte\n Revolutionen und Dynastiewechsel. Selbst die Schreckensregierung liess\n es unangetastet. Es ward erst ganz neulich aus dem Code Pénal ge-\n strichen. Nichts charakteristischer als der Vorwand dieses bürgerlichen\n Staatsstreichs. „Obgleich“, sagt Chapelier, der Berichterstatter,\n „es wünschenswerth, dass der Arbeitslohn höher steige als er jetzt steht,\n damit der, der ihn empfängt, ausserhalb der absoluten Abhängig-\n keit sei, welche die Entbehrung der nothwendigen Lebensmittel produ-\n cirt, und welche fast die Abhängigkeit der Sklaverei ist“,\n dürfen dennoch die Arbeiter sich nicht über ihre Interessen verständigen,\n gemeinsam handeln und dadurch ihre „absolute Abhängigkeit, welche fast\n Sklaverei ist“, mässigen, weil sie eben dadurch „die Freiheit ihrer\nci-devant maîtres, der jetzigen Unternehmer“, verletzen (die Frei-\n heit, die Arbeiter in der Sklaverei zu erhalten!), und weil eine Koali-\n tion gegen die Despotie der ehemaligen Meister der Cor-\n porationen — man rathe! — eine Herstellung der durch die\n französische Konstitution abgeschafften Corporationen ist! [Note: Buchezet Roux: „Histoire Parlamentaire“, t. X, p. 195.] ","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0040","text":"Nachdem wir die gewaltsame Schöpfung vogelfreier Proletarier betrach-\n tet, die blutige Disciplin, welche sie in Lohnarbeiter verwandelt, die schmutzige\n Haupt- und Staatsaktion, die mit dem Exploitationsgrad der Arbeit die Ac-\n cumulation des Kapitals polizeilich steigert, fragt sich, wo kommen die Kapi-\n talisten ursprünglich her? Denn die Expropriation des Landvolks schafft\nunmittelbar nur grosse Grundeigenthümer. Was die Genesis\n des Pächters betrifft, so können wir sie so zu sagen mit der Hand be-\n tappen, weil sie ein langsamer, über viele Jahrhunderte sich fortwälzen-\n der Prozess ist. Die Leibeignen selbst, woneben auch freie kleine Land-\n eigner, befanden sich in sehr verschiednen Besitzverhältnissen und wurden\n daher auch unter sehr verschiednen ökonomischen Bedingungen emancipirt.\n In England ist die erste Form des Pächters der selbst leibeigne Bailiff.\n Seine Stellung ist ähnlich der des altrömischen Villicus, nur in engerer\n Wirkungssphäre. Während der zweiten Hälfte des 14. Jahrhunderts wird\n er ersetzt durch einen freien Pächter, den der Landlord mit Samen, Vieh\n und Ackerwerkzeug versieht. Seine Lage ist nicht sehr verschieden von\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"727\", \"facs\": \"#f0746\"} --> der des Bauern. Nur beutet er mehr Lohnarbeit aus. Er wird bald Metair,\n Halbpächter. Er stellt einen Theil des Ackerbaukapitals, der Landlord\n den andern. Beide theilen das Gesammtprodukt in kontraktlich be-\n stimmter Proportion. Diese Form verschwindet in England rasch, um\n der des eigentlichen Pächters Platz zu machen, welcher sein eignes Ka-\n pital durch Anwendung von Lohnarbeitern verwerthet und einen Theil des\n Surplusprodukts, in Geld oder in natura, dem Landlord als Grundrente\n zahlt. So lange, während des 15. Jahrhunderts, der unabhängige Bauer\n und der neben dem Lohndienst zugleich selbstwirthschaftende Ackerknecht\n sich selbst durch ihre Arbeit bereichern, bleiben die Umstände des Päch-\n ters und sein Produktionsfeld gleich mittelmässig. Die Agrikulturrevolu-\n tion im letzten Drittheil des 15. Jahrhunderts, die fast während des\n ganzen 16. Jahrhunderts (jedoch mit Ausnahme seiner letzten Decennien)\n fortwährt, bereichert ihn eben so rasch als sie das Landvolk verarmt [Note: „Pächter“, sagt Harrison in seiner „Description of England“,\n „denen es früher schwer ward 4 Pfd. St. Rente zu zahlen, zahlen jetzt 40, 50,\n 100 Pfd. St. und glauben doch ein schlechtes Geschäft gemacht zu haben, wenn\n sie nach Ablauf ihres Pachtkontrakts nicht 6—7 Jahre Rente zurücklegen.“] .\n Die Usurpation von Gemeindeweiden u. s. w. erlaubt ihm grosse Vermeh-\n rung seines Viehstands fast ohne Kosten, während ihm das Vieh reich-\n lichere Düngungsmittel zur Bestellung des Bodens liefert. Im 16. Jahrh.\n kommt ein entscheidend wichtiges Moment hinzu. Damals waren die Pacht-\n kontrakte lang, oft für 99 Jahre laufend. Die kontinuirliche Depreciation\n der edlen Metalle und daher des Geldes trug dem Pächter goldne Früchte.\n Von allen andren, früher erörterten Umständen abgesehn, senkte sie den\nArbeitslohn. Ein Bruchstück desselben wurde dem Pacht-\n profit annexirt. Das fortwährende Steigen der Preise von Korn,\n Wolle, Fleisch, kurz sämmtlicher Agrikulturprodukte, schwellte das Geld-\n kapital des Pächters ohne sein Zuthun, während die Grundrente, die er zu\n zahlen hatte, im veralteten Geldwerth kontrahirt war. So berei-\n cherte er sich gleichzeitig auf Kosten seiner Lohnarbeiter und seines Land-\n lords. Kein Wunder also, wenn England Ende des 16. Jahrhunderts eine\n Klasse für die damaligen Verhältnisse reicher „Kapitalpächter“ besass [Note: In Frankreich wird der Régisseur, der Verwalter und Eintreiber der\n Leistungen an den Feudalherrn während des früheren Mittelalters, bald ein] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0041","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"728\", \"facs\": \"#f0747\"} -->","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0042","text":"Die stossweise und stets erneuerte Expropriation und Verjagung des\n Landvolks lieferte, wie man sah, der städtischen Industrie wieder und\n wieder Massen ganz ausserhalb der Zunftverhältnisse stehender Proletarier,\n ein weiser Umstand, der den alten Anderson (nicht zu verwechseln mit\n James Anderson) in seiner Handelsgeschichte an direkte Intervention der\n Vorsehung glauben lässt. Wir müssen noch einen Augenblick bei diesem\n Element der ursprünglichen Accumulation verweilen. Der Ver-\n dünnung des unabhängigen, selbstwirthschaftenden Landvolks entsprach\n nicht nur die Verdichtung des industriellen Proletariats, wie Geoffroy\n Saint-Hilaire die Verdichtung der Weltmaterie hier durch ihre Verdünnung\n dort erklärt [Note: In seinen „Notions de Philosophie Naturelle. Paris 1838.“] . Trotz der verminderten Zahl seiner Bebauer trug der\n Boden nach wie vor gleich viel oder mehr Produkt, weil die Revolution in\n den Grundeigenthumsverhältnissen von verbesserten Methoden der Kultur,\n grösserer Cooperation, Koncentration der Produktionsmittel u. s. w. be-\n gleitet war, und weil die ländlichen Lohnarbeiter nicht nur intensiver ange-\n spannt wurden [Note: Ein Punkt, den Sir James Steuart betont.] , sondern auch das Produktionsfeld, worauf sie für sich\n [Note: homme d’affaires, der sich durch Erpressung, Prellerei u. s. w. zum Kapi-\n talisten hinaufschwindelt. Diese Régisseurs waren manchmal selbst vornehme\n Herrn. Z. B.: „C’est li compte que messire Jacques de Thoraiue, chevalier\n chastelain sor Besançon rent es seigneur tenant les comptes à Dijon pour mon-\n seigneur le duc et comte de Bourgoigne, des rentes appartenant à la dite chastel-\n lenie, depuis XXVe jour de décembre MCCCLIX jusqu’au XXVIIIe jour de dé-\n cembre MCCCLX.“ (Alexis Monteil: „Histoire des Matériaux\n manuscrits etc.“, p. 244.) Wie in England, so waren in Frankreich die grossen\n Feudalterritorien in unendlich vicle kleine Wirthschaften getheilt, aber unter un-\n gleich ungünstigeren Bedingungen für das Landvolk. Während des 14. Jahrhun-\n derts kamen die Pachten, fermes oder terriers auf. Ihre Zahl wuchs bestän-\n dig, weit über 100,000. Sie zahlten eine vom 12. bis zum 5. Theil des Produkts\n wechselnde Grundrente in Geld oder in natura. Die terriers waren Lehn, Hinter-\n lehn u. s. w. (fiefs, arrière-fiefs), je nach Werth und Umfang der Domänen, wovon\n manche nur wenige arpents zählten. Alle diese terriers besassen Gerichtsbarkeit\n in irgend einem Grad, es gab vier Grade, über die Bodeninsassen. Man begreift\n den Druck des Landvolks unter allen diesen kleinen Tyrannen. Monteil sagt, dass\n es damals 160,000 Gerichte in Frankreich gab, wo heute 4000 Tribunale (Frie-\n densgerichte eingeschlossen) genügen.] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"729\", \"facs\": \"#f0748\"} --> selbst arbeiteten, mehr und mehr zusammenschmolz. Mit dem freigesetzten\n Theil des Landvolks werden also auch seine früheren Nahrungsmit-\n tel freigesetzt. Sie verwandeln sich jetzt in stoffliches Element des\nvariablen Kapitals. Der an die Luft gesetzte Bauer muss ihren Werth\n von seinem neuen Herrn, dem industriellen Kapitalisten, in der Form des\n Arbeitslohns erkaufen. Wie mit den Lebensmitteln, verhielt es sich mit\n dem heimischen agrikolen Rohmaterial der Industrie. Es ver-\n wandelte sich in ein Element des constanten Kapitals. Man unter-\n stelle z. B. einen Theil der westphälischen Bauern, die zu Friedrich’s II.\n Zeit alle Flachs, wenn auch keine Seide spannen, gewaltsam expropriirt und\n von Grund und Boden verjagt, den andern zurückbleibenden Theil aber\n in Taglöhner grosser Pächter verwandelt. Gleichzeitig erheben sich\n grosse Flachsspinnereien und Webereien, worin die „Freigesetzten“ nun\n lohnarbeiten. Der Flachs sieht grad aus wie vorher. Keine Fiber an\n ihm ist verändert, aber eine neue sociale Seele ist ihm in den Leib ge-\n fahren. Er bildet jetzt einen Theil des constanten Kapitals der\n Manufakturherrn. Früher vertheilt unter eine Unmasse kleiner Producen-\n ten, die ihn selbst bauten und in kleinen Portionen mit ihren Familien ver-\n spannen, ist er jetzt koncentrirt in der Hand eines Kapitalisten, der andre für\n sich spinnen und weben lässt. Die in der Flachsspinnerei verausgabte Extra-\n arbeit realisirte sich früher in Extraeinkommen zahlloser Bauernfamilien\n oder auch, zu Friedrich’s II. Zeit, in Steuern pour le roi de Prusse. Sie\n realisirt sich jetzt im Profit weniger Kapitalisten. Die Spindeln und\n Webstühle, früher vertheilt über das flache Land, sind jetzt in wenigen\n grossen Arbeitskasernen zusammengerückt, wie die Arbeiter, wie das Roh-\n material. Und Spindeln und Webstühle und Rohmaterial sind aus Mitteln\n unabhängiger Existenz für Spinner und Weber selbst verwandelt in Mit-\n tel sie zu kommandiren [Note: „Je permettrai“, sagt der Kapitalist, „que vous ayez l’honneur de me\n servir, à condition que vous me donnez le peu qui vous reste pour la peine que je\n prends de vous commander.“ (J. J. Rousseau: „Discours sur l’Éco-\n nomie Politique.“)]  und ihnen unbezahlte Arbeit auszusaugen.\n Den grossen Manufakturen sieht man es nicht an, wie den grossen Pach-\n ten, dass sie aus vielen kleinen Produktionsstätten zusammenge-\n schlagen und durch die Expropriation vieler kleiner unabhängiger\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"730\", \"facs\": \"#f0749\"} --> Producenten gebildet sind. Jedoch lässt sich die unbefangne Anschauung\n nicht beirren. Zur Zeit Mirabeau’s, des Revolutionslöwen, hiessen die\n grossen Manufakturen noch manufactures réunies, zusammenge-\n schlagene Werkstätten, wie wir von zusammengeschlagenen Aeckern\n sprechen. „Man sieht nur“, sagt Mirabeau, „die grossen Manufak-\n turen, wo Hunderte von Menschen unter einem Direktor arbeiten,\n und die man gewöhnlich vereinigte Manufakturen (manufactures\n réunies) nennt. Diejenigen dagegen, wo eine sehr grosse Anzahl Arbei-\n ter zersplittert und jeder für seine eigne Rechnung arbeitet, werden kaum\n eines Blicks gewürdigt. Man stellt sie ganz in den Hintergrund. Diess\n ist ein sehr grosser Irrthum, denn sie allein bilden einen wirklich wich-\n tigen Bestandtheil des Volksreichthums … Die vereinigte Fabrik (fabrique\n réunie) wird einen oder zwei Unternehmer wunderbar bereichern, aber die\n Arbeiter sind nur besser oder schlechter bezahlte Taglöhner und nehmen\n in Nichts am Wohlsein des Unternehmers Theil. In der getrennten\n Fabrik (fabrique séparée) dagegen wird Niemand reich, aber eine Menge\n Arbeiter befindet sich im Wohlstand … Die Zahl der fleissigen und wirth-\n schaftlichen Arbeiter wird wachsen, weil sie in weiser Lebensart, in Thätig-\n keit ein Mittel erblicken, ihre Lage wesentlich zu verbessern, statt eine kleine\n Lohnerhöhung zu gewinnen, die niemals ein wichtiger Gegenstand für die\n Zukunft sein kann, sondern die Leute höchstens befähigt etwas besser von der\n Hand in den Mund zu leben. Die getrennten individuellen Manufak-\n turen, meist mit kleiner Landwirthschaft verbunden, sind die freien“ [Note: Mirabeau l. c. t. III, p. 20—109 passim. Wenn Mirabeau die zer-\n splitterten Werkstätten auch für ökonomischer und produktiver hält, als die „ver-\n einigten“, und in letzteren bloss künstliche Treibhauspflanzen unter der Pflege der\n Staatsregierungen sieht, erklärt sich das aus dem damaligen Zustand eines grossen\n Theils der kontinentalen Manufakturen.] .\n Die Expropriation und Verjagung eines Theils des Landvolks setzt mit\n den Arbeitern nicht nur ihre Lebensmittel und ihr Arbeitsma-\n terial für das industrielle Kapital frei, sie schafft den inneren\n Markt [Note: „Twenty pounds of wool converted unobtrusively into the yearly clothing\n of a labourer’s family by its own industry in the intervals of other work — this\n makes no show; but bring it to market, send it to the factory, thence to the broker,] . Der Pächter verkauft nun als Waare und massenhaft Lebens-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"731\", \"facs\": \"#f0750\"} --> mittel und Rohmaterial, die früher grossentheils von ihren ländlichen Produ-\n centen und Verarbeitern als unmittelbare Subsistenzmittel verzehrt\n wurden. Die Manufakturen liefern ihm den Markt. Andrerseits koncen-\n triren sich nicht nur die vielen zerstreuten Kunden, die von den vielen kleinen\n Producenten ihre lokale Detailzufuhr bezogen, in einen grossen Markt\n für das industrielle Kapital; ein grosser Theil der früher auf dem\n Land selbst producirten Artikel wird in Manufakturartikel verwan-\n delt, und das Land selbst in einen Markt für ihren Verkauf. Hand\n in Hand mit der Expropriation und Losscheidung früher selbstwirthschaf-\n tender Bauern von ihren Produktionsmitteln geht so die Vernichtung der\n ländlichen Nebenindustrie, der Scheidungsprocess von\n Manufaktur und Agrikultur. Jedoch bringt es die eigentliche Manu-\n fakturperiode zu keiner radikalen Umgestaltung. Man erinnert sich, dass sie\n sich der nationalen Produktion nur sehr stückweis bemächtigt und immer auf\n städtischem Handwerk und häuslich-ländlicher Nebenindustrie als\n breitem Hintergrund ruht. Wenn sie letztere unter einer Form, in besondern\n Geschäftszweigen, auf gewissen Punkten vernichtet, ruft sie dieselbe auf an-\n dern wieder hervor, weil sie derselben zur Bearbeitung des Rohmaterials\n bis zu einem bestimmten Grad hedarf. Sie producirt daher eine neue\n Klasse kleiner Landleute, welche die Bodenbestellung als Neben-\n zweig und die industrielle Arbeit zum Verkauf des Produkts an die Manu-\n faktur, direkt, oder auf dem Umweg des Kaufmanns, als Hauptgeschäft\n treiben. Diess ist ein Grund, wenn auch nicht der Hauptgrund, eines\n Phänomens, welches den Forscher der englischen Geschichte zunächst\n verwirrt. Vom letzten Drittheil des 15. Jahrhunderts an findet er fort-\n laufende, nur in gewissen Intervallen unterbrochne Klage über die zu-\n nehmende Kapitalwirthschaft auf dem Land und die progressive Vernich-\n tung der Bauernschaft. Andrerseits findet er sie stets wieder von neuem\n vor, wenn auch in verminderter Zahl und unter stets verschlechterter\n [Note: thence to the dealer, and you will have great commercial operations, and\n nominal capital engaged to the amount of twenty times its value … The\n working class is thus emerced to support a wretched factory population, a\n parasitical shopkeeping class, and a fictitious commercial, monetary and financial\n system.“ (David Urquhart l. c. p. 120.)] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"732\", \"facs\": \"#f0751\"} --> Form [Note: Ausnahme bildet hier Cromwell’s Zeit. So lange die Republik währte,\n erhob sich die englische Volksmasse in allen Schichten aus der Degradation, wozu\n sie unter den Tudors gesunken war.] . Der Hauptgrund ist: England ist vorzugsweise bald Korn-\n bauer, bald Viehzüchter, in Wechselperioden, und mit diesen Schwan-\n kungen, die bald nach mehr als halben Jahrhunderten zählen, bald nach\n wenigen Decennien, schwankt der Umfang des bäuerlichen Betriebs. Erst\n die grosse Industrie liefert der kapitalistischen Agri-\n kultur mit der Maschinerie die constante Grundlage,\n expropriirt radikal die ungeheure Mehrzahl des Land-\n volks und vollendet die Scheidung des Ackerbaus von\n der häuslich ländlichen Industrie, deren Wurzel sie aus-\n reisst — Spinnerei und Weberei [Note: „Aus den eigentlichen Manufakturen und der Zerstörung der\n ländlichen oder häuslichen Manufaktur geht, mit Einführung der\n Maschinerie, die grosse Wollindustrie hervor.“ (Tuckett l. c. v. I,\n p. 144.) „Der Pflug, das Joch waren die Erfindung von Göttern und die Be-\n schäftigung von Heroen; sind Webstuhl, Spindel und Spinnrad minder edler Ab-\n kunft? Ihr trennt das Spinnrad und den Pflug, die Spindel und das Joch, und\n erhaltet Fabriken und Armenhäuser, Kredit und Paniks, zwei feindliche Nationen,\n agrikole und kommercielle.“ (David Urquhart l. c. p. 122.) Nun kömmt\n aber Carey, und klagt, sicher nicht mit Unrecht, England an, dass es jedes\n andre Land in ein blosses Agrikulturvolk zu verwandeln strebt, dessen Fabrikant\n England. Er behauptet, in dieser Art sei die Türkei ruinirt worden, weil „den\n Eignern und Bebauern des Bodens niemals gestattet war (von England) sich selbst\n zu kräftigen durch die natürliche Allianz zwischen dem Pflug und dem Webstuhl,\n dem Hammer und der Egge.“ („The Slave Trade“, p. 125.) Nach ihm ist\nUrquhart selbst einer der Hauptagenten des Ruins der Türkei, wo er im englischen\n Interesse Freihandelspropaganda gemacht habe. Das Beste ist, dass Carey,\n nebenbei grosser Russenknecht, durch das Protektionssystem jenen Scheidungs-\n prozess, den es beschleunigt, verhindern will.] . Sie erobert daher auch erst\n dem industriellen Kapital den ganzen inneren Markt [Note: Die philanthropischen englischen Oekonomen wie Mill, Rogers, Godwin\n Smith, Fawcett u. s. w., und liberale Fabrikanten, wie John Bright und Kons.,\n fragen, wie Gott den Kain nach seinem Bruder Abel, so den englischen Grund-\n aristokraten, wo sind unsre Tausende von Freeholders hingekommen? Aber wo\n seid ihr denn hergekommen? Aus der Vernichtung jener Freeholders. Warum\n fragt ihr nicht weiter, wo sind die unabhängigen Weber, Spinner, Handwerker\n hingekommen?] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0043","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"733\", \"facs\": \"#f0752\"} -->","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0044","text":"Die Genesis des industriellen [Note: Industriell hier im Gegensatz zu agrikol. Im „kategorischen“\n Sinn ist der Pächter ein industrieller Kapitalist so gut wie der Fabrikant.] Kapitalisten ging nicht in\n derselben allmählichen Weise vor wie die des Pächters. Zweifelsohne\n verwandelten sich manche kleine Zunftmeister und noch mehr selbstständige\n kleine Handwerker oder auch Lohnarbeiter in kleine Kapitalisten und durch\n allmählich ausgedehntere Exploitation von Lohnarbeit und entsprechende\n Accumulation in Kapitalisten sans phrase. In der Kindheitsperiode der\n kapitalistischen Produktion ging’s vielfach zu wie in der Kindheitsperiode\n des mittelaltrigen Städtewesens, wo die Frage, wer von den entlaufenen\n Leibeigenen soll Meister sein und wer Diener, grossentheils durch das\n frühere oder spätere Datum ihrer Flucht entschieden wurde. Indess ent-\n sprach der Schneckengang dieser Methode in keiner Weise den Handelsbe-\n dürfnissen des neuen Weltmarkts, welchen die grossen Entdeckungen Ende\n des 15. Jahrh. geschaffen hatten. Aber das Mittelalter hatte zwei\n verschiedne Formen des Kapitals überliefert, die in den verschie-\n densten ökonomischen Gesellschaftsformationen reifen und, vor der Aera der\n kapitalistischen Produktionsweise, als Kapital quand même gelten —\n das Wucherkapital [Note: „Gegenwärtig geht aller Reichthum der Gesellschaft erst in die Hand des\n Kapitalisten … er zahlt dem Grundeigenthümer die Rente, dem Arbeiter den\n Lohn, dem Steuer- und Zehntenkollektor ihre Ansprüche, und behält einen\n grossen, in der That den grössten und täglich anwachsenden Theil des jährlichen\n Produkts der Arbeit für sich selbst. Der Kapitalist kann jetzt als der Eigner des\n ganzen gesellschaftlichen Reichthums in erster Hand betrachtet werden, obgleich\n kein Gesetz ihm das Recht auf diess Eigenthum übertragen hat … Dieser\n Wechsel im Eigenthum wurde durch den Prozess des Wuchers bewirkt: und es\n ist nicht wenig merkwürdig, dass die Gesetzgeber von ganz Europa diess durch Ge-\n setze wider den Wucher präveniren wollten … Die Macht des Kapitalisten über allen\n Reichthum des Landes ist eine vollständige Revolution im Eigenthums-\n recht, und durch welches Gesetz, oder welche Reihe von Gesetzen wurde sie be-\n wirkt?“ („The Natural and Artificial Rights of Property Contrasted.\n Lond. 1832“, p. 98, 99. Verfasser der anonymen Schrift: Th. Hodgskin.)]  und das Kaufmannskapital. Das durch\n Wucher und Handel gebildete Geldkapital wurde durch die Feudal-\n verfassung auf dem Land, durch die Zunftverfassung in den Städten an\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"734\", \"facs\": \"#f0753\"} -->seiner Verwandlung in industrielles Kapital behindert [Note: Sogar noch 1794 schickten die kleinen Tuchmacher von Leeds eine\n Deputation an das Parlament, zur Petition um ein Gesetz, das jedem Kaufmann\n verbieten sollte, Fabrikant zu werden.“ (Dr. Aikin l. c.)] .\n Diese Schranken fielen mit der Auflösung der feudalen Gefolgschaften, mit der\n Expropriation und theilweisen Verjagung des Landvolks. Die neue Manu-\n faktur ward in See-Exporthäfen errichtet oder auf Punkten des flachen Lan-\n des, ausserhalb der Kontrole des alten Städtewesens und seiner Zunftverfas-\n sung. In England daher erbitterter Kampf der incorporated towns\n gegen diese neuen industriellen Pflanzschulen.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0045","text":"Die Entdeckung der Gold- und Silberländer in Amerika, die Ausrot-\n tung, Versklavung, und Vergrabung der eingebornen Bevölkerung in die\n Bergwerke, die Eroberung und Ausplünderung von Ostindien, die Ver-\n wandlung von Afrika in ein Geheg zur Handelsjagd auf Schwarzhäute,\n bezeichnen die Morgenröthe der kapitalistischen Produktionsära. Diese\n idyllischen Prozesse sind Hauptmomente der ursprünglichen Accu-\n mulation. Auf dem Fuss folgt der Handelskrieg der europäischen Na-\n tionen, mit dem Erdrund als Schauplatz. Er beginnt mit dem Abfall der\n Niederlande von Spanien, nimmt Riesendimensionen an in Englands Anti-\n jakobinerkrieg, spielt noch fort in den Opiumkriegen gegen China u. s. w.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0046","text":"Diese Methoden der ursprünglichen Accumulation vertheilen\n sich mehr oder minder, in zeitlicher Reihenfolge, namentlich auf Spanien,\n Portugal, Holland, Frankreich und England. In England werden sie Ende\n des 17. Jahrh. systematisch zusammengefasst im Kolonialsystem,\n Staatsschuldensystem, modernen Steuersystem und Pro-\n tektionssystem. Diese Methoden beruhn zum Theil auf brutalster\n Gewalt, wie das Kolonialsystem. Alle aber benutzen die Staatsmacht,\n die koncentrirte und organisirte Gewalt der Gesellschaft, um den Verwand-\n lungsprozess der feudalen in die kapitalistische Produktionsweise treibhaus-\n mässig zu beschleunigen und die Uebergänge abzukürzen. Die Gewalt\n ist der Geburtshelfer jeder alten Gesellschaft, die mit einer\n neuen schwanger geht. Sie ist selbst eine ökonomische Potenz.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0047","text":"Von dem christlichen Kolonialsystem sagt ein Mann, der aus dem\n Christenthum eine Specialität macht, W. Howitt: „Die Barbareien und\n ruchlosen Greuelthaten der s. g. christlichen Racen, in jeder Region der\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"735\", \"facs\": \"#f0754\"} --> Welt und gegen jedes Volk, das sie unterjochen konnten, finden keine\n Parallele in irgend einer Aera der Weltgeschichte, bei irgend einer Race,\n ob noch so wild, ungebildet, mitleidslos und schamlos“ [Note: William Howitt: „Colonization and Christianity. A\n Popular History of the Treatment of the Natives by the Euro-\n peansin all their colonies. Lond. 1838“, p. 9. Ueber die Behandlung\n der Sklaven gute Compilation bei Charles Comte: „Traité de la Legis-\n lation.“ 3me éd. Bruxelles 1837. Man muss diess Zeug im Detail studiren,\n um zu sehn, wozu der Bourgeois sich selbst und den Arbeiter macht, wo er die\n Welt ungenirt nach seinem Bilde modeln kann.] . Die Ge-\n schichte der holländischen Kolonialwirthschaft — und Holland war die\n kapitalistische Musternation des 17. Jahrhunderts — „entrollt ein unüber-\n treffbares Gemälde von Verrath, Bestechung, Meuchelmord und Nieder-\n tracht“ [Note: Thomas Stafford Raffles, late Lieut. Gov. of that island\n (Java) and its dependencies. Lond. 1817.] . Nichts charakteristischer als ihr System des Menschendieb-\n stahls in Celebes, um Sklaven für Java zu erhalten. Die Menschenstehler\n wurden zu diesem Zweck abgerichtet. Der Dieb, der Dolmetscher und\n der Verkäufer waren die Hauptagenten in diesem Handel, eingeborne Prin-\n zen die Hauptverkäufer. Die weggestohlne Jugend wurde in den Geheimge-\n fängnissen von Celebes versteckt, bis reif zur Verschickung auf die Sklaven-\n schiffe. Ein officieller Bericht sagt: „Diese eine Stadt von Makassar z. B.\n ist voll von geheimen Gefängnissen, eins schauderhafter als das andre, ge-\n pfropft mit Elenden, Opfern der Habsucht und Tyrannei, in Ketten gefes-\n selt, ihren Familien gewaltsam entrissen.“ Um sich Malacca’s zu bemäch-\n tigen, bestachen die Holländer den portugiesischen Gouverneur. Er liess\n sie 1641 in die Stadt ein. Sie eilten sofort zu seinem Hause und meuchel-\n mordeten ihn, um auf die Zahlung der Bestechungssumme von 21,875 Pfd. St.\n zu „entsagen“. Wo sie die Füsse hinsetzten, folgte Verödung und\n Entvölkerung. Die Population einer Provinz von Java, Baniyuawngy,\n zählte 1750 über 80,000 Einwohner, 1811 nur noch 8000. Das ist der\ndoux commerce!","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0048","text":"Die englisch-ostindische Kompagnie erhielt bekanntlich,\n ausser der politischen Herrschaft in Ostindien, das exklusive Monopol des\n Theehandels, wie des chinesischen Handels überhaupt und des Gütertransports\n von und zu Europa. Aber die Küstenschifffahrt von Indien und zwischen\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"736\", \"facs\": \"#f0755\"} --> den Inseln, wie der Handel im Innern Indiens wurden Monopol der höhern\n Beamten der Kompagnie. Die Monopole von Salz, Opium, Betel und\n andern Waaren waren unerschöpfliche Minen des Reichthums. Die Beamten\n selbst setzten die Preise fest und schindeten nach Belieben den unglück-\n lichen Hindu. Der Generalgouverneur nahm Theil an diesem Privathan-\n del. Seine Günstlinge erhielten Kontrakte unter Bedingungen, wodurch sie,\n klüger als die Alchymisten, aus Nichts Gold machten. Grosse Vermö-\n gen sprangen wie die Pilze an einem Tage auf, die ursprüngliche\n Accumulation ging von Statten ohne Vorschuss eines Schillings. Die\n gerichtliche Verfolgung des Warren Hastings wimmelt von solchen Bei-\n spielen. Hier ein Fall. Ein Opiumkontrakt wird einem gewissen Sullivan\n zugetheilt, obgleich er in öffentlichem Auftrag zu einem von den Opium-\n distrikten ganz entlegenen Theil Indiens reiste. Sullivan verkauft seinen\n Kontrakt für 40,000 Pfd. St. an einen gewissen Binn, Binn verkauft ihn\n seinerseits denselben Tag für 60,000 Pfd. St., und der schliessliche Käufer\n und Ausführer des Kontrakts erklärt, dass er hinterher noch einen unge-\n heuren Gewinn herausschlug. Nach einer dem Parlament vorgelegten\n Liste liessen sich die Kompagnie und ihre Beamten von 1757—1766 von\n den Indiern 6 Millionen Pfd. St. schenken! Zwischen 1769 und 1770\n fabricirten die Engländer eine Hungersnoth durch den Aufkauf von allem Reis\n und durch Weigerung des Wiederverkaufs ausser zu fabelhaften Preisen [Note: Im Jahr 1866 starben in der einzigen Provinz Orissa mehr als eine\n Million Hindus am Hungertod. Die Hungersnoth währt bis zu diesem\n Augenblick. Nichtsdestoweniger suchte man die indische Staatskasse zu berei-\n chern durch die Preise, wozu man den Verhungernden Lebensmittel abliess.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0049","text":"Die Behandlung der Eingeborenen war natürlich am tollsten in den\n nur zum Exporthandel bestimmten Pflanzungen, wie Westindien, und in\n den dem Raubmord preisgegebnen reichen und dichtbevölkerten Ländern,\n wie Mexico und Ostindien. Jedoch auch in den eigentlichen Kolonien verläug-\n nete sich der christliche Charakter der ursprünglichen Accumula-\n tion nicht. Jene nüchternen Virtuosen des Protestantismus, die Puritaner,\n setzten durch Beschlüsse ihrer Assembly 1703 eine Prämie von 40\n Pfd. St. auf jedes indianische Scalp und jede gefangne Rothhaut, 1720\n Prämie von 100 Pfd. St. auf jedes Scalp, 1744, nachdem Massachussets-\n Bay einen gewissen Tribus zum Rebellen erklärt hatte, folgende Preise:\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"737\", \"facs\": \"#f0756\"} --> für männliches Scalp, 12 Jahre und drüber, 100 Pfd. St. neuer Währung,\n für männliche Gefangene 105 Pfd. St., für gefangene Weiber und Kinder\n 55 Pfd. St., für Scalps von Weibern und Kindern 50 Pfd. St.!\n Einige Decennien später rächte sich das Kolonialsystem an der unterdess\n aufrührisch gewordenen Nachkommenschaft der frommen pilgrim fathers.\n Unter englischem Antrieb und Sold wurden sie tomahawked. Das\n britische Parlament erklärte Bluthunde und Scalpiren für „Mittel, welche\n Gott und die Natur in seine Hand gegeben.“","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0050","text":"Das Kolonialsystem reifte treibhausmässig Handel und Schifffahrt.\n Die „Gesellschaften Monopolia“ (Luther) waren gewaltige Hebel der Ka-\n pital-Koncentration. Den aufschiessenden Manufakturen sicherte die Kolonie\n Absatzmarkt und eine durch das Marktmonopol potenzirte Accumulation.\n Der ausserhalb Europa direkt erplünderte, herausgesklavte und herausge-\n mordete Schatz floss ins Mutterland zurück und verwandelte sich hier in\nKapital. Holland, welches das Kolonialsystem zuerst völlig entwickelte,\n stand schon 1648 im Brennpunkt seiner Handelsgrösse. Es war „in fast\n ausschliesslichem Besitz des ostindischen Handels und des Verkehrs zwi-\n schen dem europäischen Südwesten und Nordosten. Seine Fischereien,\n Seewesen, Manufakturen übertrafen die eines jeden anderen Landes. Die\n Kapitalien der Republik waren vielleicht bedeutender als die des übrigen\n Europa insgesammt.“ Jülich vergisst hinzuzusetzen: Hollands Volks-\n masse war schon 1648 mehr überarbeitet, verarmter und brutaler unter-\n drückt als die des übrigen Europa’s insgesammt. Das Kolonialsystem\n warf mit einem Schub und Bautz alle alten Götzen über Haufen. Es pro-\n klamirte die Plusmacherei als letzten und einzigen Zweck der Menschheit.\n Es war die Geburtsstätte des modernen Staatsschulden- und Kre-\n ditsystems.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0051","text":"Die auffallende Rolle des Staatsschulden- und modernen Steuer-\n systems bei der Verwandlung des gesellschaftlichen Reich-\n thums in Kapital, der Expropriation selbstständiger Arbeiter, und der\n Herunterdrückung der Lohnarbeiter, hat manche Schriftsteller, wie W. Cob-\n bett, Doubleday u. s. w. verleitet dort den Grund alles modernen Volkselends zu\n suchen. Mit den Staatsschulden entsprang zugleich ein internationales Kredit-\n wesen, welches oft die Quelle der ursprünglichen Accumulation\n in einem bestimmten Land versteckt. Die Gemeinheiten des venetianischen\n Raubsystems z. B. bilden eine verborgne Grundlage des Kapitalreichthums\n [Running matter: I. 47] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"738\", \"facs\": \"#f0757\"} --> von Holland, dem das verfallende Venedig grosse Geldsummen lieh.\n Ebenso verhält es sich zwischen Holland und England. Schon im Anfang\n des 18. Jahrhunderts sind die Manufakturen Hollands weit überflügelt und\n hat es aufgehört, herrschende Industrie- und Handelsnation zu sein. Eins\n seiner Hauptgeschäfte von 1701—1776 wird daher das Ausleihn unge-\n heurer Kapitalien, speziell an seinen übermächtigen Konkurrenten Eng-\n land. Aehnliches gilt jetzt von England und den Vereinigten Staaten.\n Manch Kapital, das heute in den Vereinigten Staaten ohne Geburtsschein\n auftritt, ist erst gestern in England kapitalisirtes Kinderblut.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0052","text":"Das Protektions system war ein Kunstmittel Fabrikanten zu\n fabriciren, unabhängige Arbeiter zu expropriiren, die nationa-\n len Produktions- und Lebensmittel zu kapitalisiren, den Ueber-\n gangaus der alterthümlichen in die moderne Produktionsweise\n gewaltsam abzukürzen. Die europäischen Staaten rissen sich um das\n Patent dieser Erfindung, und einmal in den Dienst der Plusmacher eingetreten,\n brandschatzten sie zu jenem Behuf nicht nur das eigne Volk, indirekt durch\n Schutzzölle, direkt durch Exportprämien u. s. w. In den abhängigen Neben-\n landen wurde alle Industrie gewaltsam ausgerodet, wie z. B. die irische Woll-\n manufaktur von England. Auf dem europäischen Kontinent ward nach Col-\n bert’s Vorgang der Prozess noch sehr vereinfacht. Das ursprüngliche\n Kapital des Industriellen fliesst hier zum Theil direkt aus dem\n Staatsschatz. „Warum,“ ruft Mirabeau, „so weit die Ursache des Ma-\n nufakturglanzes Sachsens vor dem siebenjährigen Krieg suchen gehn? 180\n Millionen Staatsschulden“ [Note: „Pourquoi aller chercher si loin la cause de l’éclat manufacturier de la\n Saxe avant la guerre? Cent quatre-vingt millions de dettes faites par les souve-\n rains!“ Mirabeau l. c. t. VI, p. 101.] !","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0053","text":"Kolonialsystem, Staatsschulden, Steuerwucht, Protektion, Handels-\n kriege u. s. w., diese Sprösslinge der eigentlichen Manufakturperiode,\n schwellen riesenhaft während der Kinderperiode der grossen Industrie.\n Die Geburt der letzteren wird gefeiert durch den grossen herodischen Kin-\n derraub. So blasirt Sir F. M. Eden ist über die Greuel der Expropria-\n tion des Landvolks von Grund und Boden seit dem letzten Drittel des\n 15. Jahrhunderts bis zu seiner Zeit, dem Ende des 18. Jahrhunderts; so\n selbstgefällig er gratulirt zu diesem Prozess, „nothwendig“, um die kapi-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"739\", \"facs\": \"#f0758\"} --> talistische Agrikultur und „das wahre Verhältniss von Ackerland und Vieh-\n weide herzustellen“, beweist er dagegen nicht dieselbe ökonomische Einsicht\n in die Nothwendigkeit des Kinderraubs und der Kindersklaverei\n für die Verwandlung des Manufakturbetriebs in den Fabrikbetrieb und die\n Herstellung des wahren Verhältnisses von Kapital und Arbeits-\n kraft. Er sagt: „Es mag vielleicht der Erwägung des Publikums werth sein,\n ob irgend eine Manufaktur, die zu ihrer erfolgreichen Ausführung Cottages\n und Workhouses von armen Kindern ausplündern muss, damit sie, truppweis\n sich ablösend, den grössten Theil der Nacht durch abgerackert und der Ruhe\n beraubt werden, eine Manufaktur, die ausserdem Haufen beiderlei Geschlechts,\n von verschiednen Altersstufen und Neigungen, so zusammenhudelt, dass\n die Ansteckung des Beispiels zu Verworfenheit und Liederlichkeit führen\n muss, ob solch eine Manufaktur die Summe des nationalen und indivi-\n duellen Glücks vermehren kann“ [Note: Eden l. c. b. II, ch. I, p. 421.] ? „In Derbyshire, Nottinghamshire\n und besonders Lancashire,“ sagt Fielden, „wurde die jüngst erfundne\n Maschinerie angewandt in grossen Fabriken, dicht bei Strömen fähig das\n Wasserrad zu drehn. Tausende von Händen waren plötzlich erheischt an\n diesen Plätzen, fern von den Städten; und Lancashire namentlich, bis zu\n jener Zeit vergleichungsweis dünn bevölkert und unfruchtbar, bedurfte jetzt\n vor allem einer Population. Die kleinen und flinken Finger waren vor\n allen in Requisition. Sofort sprang die Gewohnheit auf, Lehrlinge(!)\n aus den verschiedenen Pfarrei-Workhouses von London, Birmingham und\n sonstwo zu beziehn. Tausende dieser kleinen hilflosen Kreaturen wurden\n so nach dem Norden spedirt, vom 7. — 13. oder 14. Jahr. Es war die\n Gewohnheit für den Meister (d. h. den Kinderdieb), seine Lehrlinge zu\n kleiden, nähren und logiren in einem Lehrlingshaus nah bei der Fabrik.\nAufseher wurden bestellt um ihre Arbeit zu überwachen. Es war das In-\n teresse dieser Sklaventreiber die Kinder aufs Aeusserste abzuarbeiten, denn\n ihre Zahlung stand im Verhältniss zur Quantität Produkt, die aus dem Kind\n erpresst werden konnte. Grausamkeit war natürliche Folge . . . . In\n vielen Fabrikdistrikten, besonders Lancashire’s, wurden die herzzerreis-\n sendsten Torturen prakticirt an diesen harmlosen und freundlosen Krea-\n turen, die den Fabrikherrn consignirt waren. Sie wurden zu Tod gehetzt\n durch Arbeitsexcesse; sie wurden gepeitscht, gekettet und gefoltert mit\n [Running matter: 47*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"740\", \"facs\": \"#f0759\"} --> dem ausgesuchtesten Raffinement von Grausamkeit; sie wurden in vielen\n Fällen bis zu den Knochen ausgehungert, während die Peitsche sie an der\n Arbeit hielt. Ja in einigen Fällen wurden sie zum Selbstmord getrieben! …\n Die schönen und romantischen Thäler von Derbyshire, Nottinghamshire\n und Lancashire, abgeschlossen vom öffentlichen Auge, wurden grause Ein-\n öden von Tortur und — oft von Mord! … Die Profite der Fabrikanten\n waren enorm. Das wetzte nur ihren Wehrwolfsheisshunger. Sie began-\n nen die Praxis der Nachtarbeit, d. h. nachdem sie eine Gruppe Hände\n durch das Tagwerk gelähmt, hatten sie eine andre Gruppe für das Nacht-\n werk zur Hand; die Tagesgruppe wanderte in die Betten, welche die\n Nachtgruppe grade verlassen hatte und vice versa. Es ist Volksüberliefe-\n rung in Lancashire, dass die Betten nie abkühlten“ [Note: John Fielden l. c. p. 5, 6. Ueber die ursprünglichen Infamien des\n Fabrikwesens vgl. Dr. Aikin (1795) l. c. p. 219 und Gisbourne: „Enquiry\n into the duties of men. 1795“, v. II. — Da die Dampfmaschine die Fabriken\n von den ländlichen Wasserfällen weg in die Mitte von Städten verpflanzte, fand\n der „entsagungslustige“ Plusmacher das Kindermaterial nun zur Hand, ohne ge-\n waltsame Sklavenzufuhr aus den Workhouses. — Als Sir R. Peel (Vater des\n „Ministers der Plausibilität“) seine Bill zum Schutz der Kinder 1815 einbrachte,\n erklärte F. Horner (lumen des Bullion-Comités und intimer Freund Ricardo’s)\n im Unterhaus: „Es ist notorisch, dass mit den Effekten eines Banqueroutier’s\n eine Bande, wenn er solchen terminus brauchen dürfe, von Fabrikkindern zur\n Auktion öffentlich, als Theil des Eigenthums, annoncirt und losgeschlagen wurde.\n Vorzwei Jahren (1813) kam ein abscheulicher Fall vor die King’s Bench. Es handelte\n sich um eine Anzahl Knaben. Eine Pfarrei von London hatte sie einem Fabri-\n kanten übermacht, der übertrug sie wieder auf einen andern. Sie wurden schliess-\n lich von einigen Menschenfreunden in einem Zustand absoluter Hungers-\n noth (absolute famine) entdeckt. Ein andrer, noch schrecklicherer Fall sei zu\n seiner Kenntniss als Mitglied des parlamentarischen Untersuchungscomités ge-\n bracht worden. Vor nicht vielen Jahren schlossen eine Londoner Pfarrei und ein\n Fabrikant von Lancashire einen Vertrag, wodurch stipulirt wurde, dass er auf je\n 20 gesunde Kinder einen Idioten mit in den Kauf nehme.“] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0054","text":"Mit der Entwicklung der kapitalistischen Produktion während der Manu-\n fakturperiode hatte die öffentliche Meinung von Europa den letzten Rest von\n Schamgefühl und Gewissen eingebüsst. Die Nationen renommirten cynisch\n mit jeder Infamie, die Mittel zu Kapitalaccumulation. Man\n lese z. B. die naiven Handelsannalen des Biedermanns Anderson.\n Hier wird es als Triumph englischer Staatsweisheit ausposaunt, dass Eng-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"741\", \"facs\": \"#f0760\"} --> land im Frieden von Utrecht den Spaniern durch den Asientovertrag das\n Privilegium abzwang, den Negerhandel, den es bisher nur zwischen Afrika\n und dem englischen Westindien betrieb, nun auch zwischen Afrika und dem\n spanischen Amerika betreiben zu dürfen. England erhielt das Recht, das\n spanische Amerika bis 1743 jährlich mit 4800 Negern zu versorgen. Diess\n gewährte zugleich einen officiellen Deckmantel für den britischen Schmug-\n gel. Liverpool wuchs gross auf der Basis des Sklavenhandels. Er bildet\nseine Methode der ursprünglichen Accumulation. Und bis\n heutzutag blieb die Liverpooler „Ehrbarkeit“ Pindar des Sklavenhandels,\n welcher — vgl. die citirte Schrift des Dr. Aikin von 1795 — „den\n commerciellen Unternehmungsgeist bis zur Leidenschaft steigere, famose\n Seeleute bilde, und enormes Geld einbringe.“ Liverpool beschäftigte\n1730 im Sklavenhandel 15 Schiffe, 1751: 53, 1760: 74, 1770: 96\n und 1792: 132.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0055","text":"Während sie die Kindersklaverei in England einführte, gab die Baum-\n wollindustrie zugleich den Anstoss zur Verwandlung der früher mehr oder\n minder patriarchalischen Sklavenwirthschaft der Vereinigten Staaten in\n ein commercielles Exploitationssystem. Ueberhaupt bedurfte die verhüllte\n Sklaverei der Lohnarbeiter in Europa zum Piedestal die Sklaverei sans\n phrase in der neuen Welt [Note: 1790 kamen im englischen Westindien 10 Sklaven auf 1 Freien, im\n französischen 14 auf 1, im holländischen 23 auf 1. (Henry Brougham:\n „An Inquiry into the Colonial Policy of the European Powers.\n Edinb. 1803“, v. II, p. 74.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0056","text":"Tantae molis erat, die „ewigen Naturgesetze“ der kapita-\n listischen Produktionsweise zu entbinden, den Scheidungsprozess zwischen Ar-\n beitern und Arbeitsbedingungen zu vollziehn, auf dem einen Pol die gesell-\n schaftlichen Produktions- und Lebensmittel in Kapital zu verwandeln, auf\n dem Gegenpol die Volksmasse in Lohnarbeiter, in freie „arbeitende\n Arme“, diess Kunstprodukt der modernen Geschichte [Note: Der Ausdruck „labouring poor“ findet sich in den englischen Ge-\n setzen vom Augenblick, wo die Klasse der Lohnarbeiter bemerkenswerth wird.\n Der „labouring poor“ steht im Gegensatz, einerseits zum „idle poor“, Bettler\n u. s. w., andrerseits zum Arbeiter, der noch kein gepflücktes Huhn, sondern Eigen-\n thümer seiner Arbeitsmittel ist. Aus dem Gesetz ging der Ausdruck „labouring\n poor“ in die politische Oekonomie über, von Culpeper, J. Child u. s. w. bis\n A. Smith und Eden. Danach beurtheile man die bonne foi des „execrable poli-] . Wenn\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"742\", \"facs\": \"#f0761\"} --> das Geld, nach Augier, „mit natürlichen Blutflecken auf einer Backe\n zur Welt kömmt“ [Note: Marie Augier: „Du Credit Public.“] , so das Kapital von Kopf bis Zeh, aus allen\n Poren, blut- und schmutztriefend [Note: „Kapital, sagt der Quarterly Reviewer, flieht Tumult und Streit\n und ist ängstlicher Natur. Das ist sehr wahr, aber doch nicht die ganze Wahrheit.\n Das Kapital hat einen horror vor Abwesenheit von Profit, oder sehr kleinem Profit,\n wie die Natur vor der Leere. Mit entsprechendem Profit wird Kapital kühn. Zehn\n Procent sicher, und man kann es überall anwenden; 20 Procent, es wird lebhaft;\n 50 Procent, positiv waghalsig; für 100 Procent stampft es alle menschlichen Ge-\n setze unter seinen Fuss; 300 Procent, und es existirt kein Verbrechen, das es\n nicht riskirt, selbst auf Gefahr des Galgens. Wenn Tumult und Streit Profit tragen,\n wird es sie beide encouragiren. Beweis: Schmuggel und Sklavenhandel.“ (T. J.\nDunning l. c. p. 36.)] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0057","text":"Worauf kömmt die ursprüngliche Accumulation des Ka-\n pitals, d. h. seine historische Genesis, hinaus? Soweit sie nicht un-\n mittelbare Verwandlung von Sklaven und Leibeigenen in Lohnarbeiter, also\nblosser Formwechsel ist, bedeutet sie nur die Expropria-\n tion der unmittelbaren Producenten, d. h. die Auflösung\n des auf eigner Arbeit beruhenden Privateigenthums. Das\n Privateigenthum des Arbeiters an seinen Produktionsmitteln ist die Grund-\n lage des Kleinbetriebs, der Kleinbetrieb eine nothwendige Bedingung für die\n Entwicklung der gesellschaftlichen Produktion und der freien Individuali-\n tät des Arbeiters selbst. Allerdings existirt diese Produktionsweise auch\n [Note: tical cantmonger“ Edmund Burke, wenn er den Ausdruck „labouring poor“\n für „execrable political cant“ erklärt. Dieser Sykophant, der im Sold der eng-\n lischen Oligarchie den Romantiker gegenüber der französischen Revolution spielte,\n ganz wie er beim Beginn der amerikanischen Wirren im Sold der nordamerikani-\n schen Kolonien gegenüber der englischen Oligarchie den Liberalen gespielt hatte,\n war durch und durch ordinärer Bourgeois: „Die Gesetze des Handels sind die\n Gesetze der Natur und folglich die Gesetze Gottes.“ (E. Burke l. c. p. 31, 32.)\n Kein Wunder, dass er, den Gesetzen Gottes und der Natur getreu, stets sich selbst\n auf dem besten Markt verkauft hat! Man findet in des Rev. Mr. Tucker’s Schrif-\n ten — Tucker war Pfaff und Tory, im übrigen aber anständiger Mann und tüch-\n tiger politischer Oekonom — sehr gute Charakteristik dieses Edmund Burke\n während seiner liberalen Zeit. Bei der infamen Charakterlosigkeit, die heutzutag\n herrscht und devotest an „die Gesetze des Handels“ glaubt, ist es Pflicht wieder\n und wieder die Burkes zu brandmarken, die sich von ihren Nachfolgern nur durch\n eins unterscheiden — Talent!] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"743\", \"facs\": \"#f0762\"} --> innerhalb der Sklaverei, Leibeigenschaft und anderer Abhängigkeitsver-\n hältnisse. Aber sie blüht nur, schnellt nur ihre ganze Energie, erobert nur\n die adäquate klassische Form, wo der Arbeiter freier Privateigen-\n thümer seiner von ihm selbst gehandhabten Arbeitsbe-\n dingungen ist, der Bauer des Ackers, den er bestellt, der Handwer-\n ker des Instruments, womit er als Virtuose spielt. Diese Produktionsweise\n unterstellt Zersplitterung des Bodens und der übrigen Produktions-\n mittel. Mit der Koncentration der letztern schliesst sie die Cooperation,\n Theilung der Arbeit innerhalb derselben Produktionsprozesse, gesellschaft-\n liche Beherrschung und Reglung der Natur, Entwicklung gesellschaft-\n licher Produktivkraft aus. Sie ist nur verträglich mit engen natur-\n wüchsigen Schranken der Produktion und der Gesellschaft. Auf einem\n gewissen Höhegrad bringt sie die materiellen Mittel ihrer eignen Vernich-\n tung zur Welt. Von diesem Augenblick regen sich Kräfte und Leiden-\n schaften im Gesellschaftsschoose, welche sich von ihr gefesselt fühlen. Sie muss\n vernichtet werden, sie wird vernichtet. Ihre Vernichtung, die Verwand-\n lung der individuellen und zersplitterten Produktions-\n mittel in gesellschaftlich concentrirte, daher des zwerg-\n haften Eigenthums Vieler in das massenhafte Eigenthum Weniger, daher\n die Expropriation der grossen Volksmasse von Grund und\n Boden und Lebensmitteln und Arbeitsinstrumenten, diese\n furchtbare und schwierige Expropriation der Volksmasse bildet\n die Vorgeschichte des Kapitals. Sie umfasst eine Reihe gewaltsamer\n Methoden, wovon wir nur die epochemachenden als Methoden derur-\n sprünglichen Accumulation des Kapitals Revue passiren\n liessen. Die Expropriation der unmittelbaren Producenten wird mit\n schonungslosestem Vandalismus und unter dem Trieb der infamsten,\n schmutzigsten, kleinlichst gehässigsten Leidenschaften vollbracht. Das\nselbst erarbeitete, sozusagen auf Verwachsung des isolirten, unab-\n hängigen Arbeitsindividuums mit seinen Arbeitsbedin-\n gungen beruhende Privateigenthum wird verdrängt durch das\n kapitalistische Privateigenthum, welches auf Exploitation\n fremder, aber formell freier Arbeit beruht [Note: „Nous sommes dans une condition tout-à-fait nouvelle de la société …\n nous tendons a séparer toute espèce de propriété d’avec toute espèce de travail.“\n (Sismondi: „Nouveaux Principes de l’Écon. Polit.“ t. II, p. 434.)] . Sobald dieser Umwand-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"744\", \"facs\": \"#f0763\"} --> lungsprozess nach Tiefe und Umfang die alte Gesellschaft hinreichend\n zersetzt hat, sobald die Arbeiter in Proletarier, ihre Arbeitsbeding ungen\n in Kapital verwandelt sind, sobald die kapitalistische Produktionsweise\n auf eignen Füssen steht, gewinnt die weitere Vergesellschaftung der Ar-\n beit und weitere Verwandlung der Erde und andrer Produktionsmittel in\n gesellschaftlich ausgebeutete, also gemeinschaftliche Produk-\n tionsmittel, daher die weitere Expropriation der Privateigen-\n thümer, eine neue Form. Was jetzt zu expropriiren ist, ist nicht\n länger der selbstwirthschaftende Arbeiter, sondern der viele Arbeiter ex-\n ploitirende Kapitalist. Diese Expropriation vollzieht sich durch das\n Spiel der immanenten Gesetze der kapitalistischen Produktion\n selbst, durch die Koncentration der Kapitalien. Je ein Ka-\n pitalist schlägt viele todt. Hand in Hand mit dieser Koncentration oder\n der Expropriation vieler Kapitalisten durch Wenige ent-\n wickelt sich die cooperative Form des Arbeitsprozesses auf stets wachsen-\n der Stufenleiter, die bewusste technologische Anwendung der Wissenschaft,\n die planmässig gemeinsame Ausbeutung der Erde, die Verwandlung der\n Arbeitsmittel in nur gemeinsam verwendbare Arbeitsmittel, und die Oeko-\n nomisirung aller Produktionsmittel durch ihren Gebrauch als gemeinsame\n Produktionsmittel kombinirter, gesellschaftlicher Arbeit. Mit der bestän-\n dig abnehmenden Zahl der Kapitalmagnaten, welche alle Vortheile dieses\n Umwandlungsprozesses usurpiren und monopolisiren, wächst die Masse\n des Elends, des Drucks, der Knechtung, der Degradation, der Ausbeutung,\n aber auch die Empörung der stets anschwellenden und durch den Mecha-\n nismus des kapitalistischen Produktionsprozesses selbst geschulten, verein-\n ten und organisirten Arbeiterklasse. Das Kapitalmonopol wird\n zur Fessel der Produktionsweise, die mit und unter ihm aufge-\n blüht ist. Die Koncentration der Produktionsmittel und die Vergesellschaf-\n tung der Arbeit erreichen einen Punkt, wo sie unverträglich werden mit\n ihrer kapitalistischen Hülle. Sie wird gesprengt. Die Stunde des\n kapitalistischen Privateigenthums schlägt. Die Expro-\n priateurs werden expropriirt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0058","text":"Die kapitalistische Produktions- und Aneignungsweise, daher das kapi-\n talistische Privateigenthum, ist die erste Negation des\n individuellen, aufeigne Arbeit gegründeten Privateigen-\n thums. Die Negation der kapitalistischen Produktion wird durch\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"745\", \"facs\": \"#f0764\"} --> sie selbst, mit der Nothwendigkeit eines Naturprozesses, producirt. Es ist\nNegation der Negation. Diese stellt das individuelle Eigen-\n thum wieder her, aber auf Grundlage der Errungenschaft der kapi-\n talistischen Aera, der Cooperation freier Arbeiter und ihrem\n Gemeineigenthum an der Erde und den durch die Arbeit\n selbst producirten Produktionsmitteln.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DSG:DISPLAY-0059","text":"Die Verwandlung des zersplitterten, auf eigner Arbeit der Indivi-\n duen beruhenden Privateigenthums in kapitalistisches ist natürlich\n ein ungleich mehr langwieriger, harter und schwieriger Prozess als die\n Verwandlung des faktisch bereits auf gesellschaftlicher Exploitation der\n Produktionsmittel beruhenden kapitalistischen Privateigenthums in ge-\n sellschaftliches Eigenthum. Dort handelte es sich um die\n Expropriation der Volksmasse durch wenige Usurpatoren, hier handelt es\n sich um die Expropriation weniger Usurpatoren durch die Volksmasse [Note: „Der Fortschritt der Industrie, dessen willenloser und widerstandloser\n Träger die Bourgeoisie ist, setzt an die Stelle der Isolirung der Arbeiter durch die\n Konkurrenz ihre revolutionäre Vereinigung durch die Association. Mit der Ent-\n wicklung der grossen Industrie wird also unter den Füssen der Bourgeoisie die\n Grundlage selbst weggezogen, worauf sie producirt und die Produkte sich aneignet.\n Sie producirt also vor allem ihre eignen Todtengräber. Ihr Untergang und der\n Sieg des Proletariats sind gleich unvermeidlich … Von allen Klassen, welche\n heutzutage der Bourgeoisie gegenüberstehn, ist nur das Proletariat eine wirklich\n revolutionäre Klasse. Die übrigen Klassen verkommen und gehn unter mit der\n grossen Industrie, das Proletariat ist ihr eigenstes Produkt … Die Mittelstände,\n der kleine Industrielle, der kleine Kaufmann, der Handwerker, der Bauer, sie alle\n bekämpfen die Bourgeoisie, um ihre Existenz als Mittelstände vor dem Untergang\n zu sichern … sie sind reaktionär, denn sie suchen das Rad der Geschichte zu-\n rückzudrehn.“ (F. Engels und Karl Marx: „Manifest der kommu-\n nistischen Partei. London 1847“, p. 9, 11.)] .","kind":"display"}],"sha256":"778da065c8da6e0b7e6cac904ab33d3a99238e5517d5cbf7f9acfcf651ec2054","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK","title":"3) Die moderne Kolonisationstheorie.","order":23,"chapter_id":"CH_V4GB_3DMK","text":"## 3) Die moderne Kolonisationstheorie [Note: Es handelt sich hier von wirklichen Kolonien, jungfräulichem\n Boden, der durch freie Einwanderer kolonisirt wird. Die Vereinigten Staaten\n sind, ökonomisch gesprochen, immer noch Kolonialland Europa’s. Uebrigens\n gehören auch die alten Pflanzungen hierher, wo die Aufhebung der Sklaverei die\n Verhältnisse gänzlich umgewälzt hat.] .\n\n\n\n\nDie politische Oekonomie sucht principiell die angenehmste Verwechs-\n lung aufrecht zu erhalten zwischen dem auf eigner Arbeit be-\n ruhenden Privateigenthum und dem diametral entgegenge-\n setzten, auf Vernichtung dieser Art Eigenthums beruhenden kapita-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"746\", \"facs\": \"#f0765\"} --> listischen Privateigenthum. Im Westen von Europa, dem Hei-\n mathsland der politischen Oekonomie, ist der Prozess der ursprünglichen\n Accumulation vollbracht. Die kapitalistische Produktionsweise hat hier\n entweder die ganze nationale Produktion direkt unterworfen, oder, wo die\n Verhältnisse minder entwickelt sind, kontrolirt sie indirekt die noch neben\n ihr fortexistirenden, verkommenden, der veralteten Produktionsweise an-\n gehörigen Gesellschaftsschichten. Auf diese fertige Welt des Kapitals\n wendet der politische Oekonom mit desto ängstlicherem Eifer und desto\n grösserer Salbung die Rechts- und Eigenthumsvorstellungen der vorkapita-\n listischen Welt an, je lauter die Thatsachen seiner Ideologie ins Gesicht\n schreien. Anders in den Kolonien. Die kapitalistische Produktions-\n und Aneignungsweise stösst hier überall auf das Hinderniss des selbst-\n erarbeiteten Eigenthums, des Producenten, der als Privateigenthümer seiner\n eignen Arbeitsbedingungen durch seine Arbeit sich selbst statt den Kapi-\n talisten bereichert. Der Widerspruch dieser zwei diametral\n entgegengesetzten Produktions- und Aneignungs wei-\n sen existirt hier praktisch. Wo der Kapitalist die Macht des\n Mutterlandes im Rücken hat, sucht er die auf eigner Arbeit be-\n ruhende Produktions- und Aneignungsweise gewaltsam aus\n dem Weg zu räumen. Dasselbe Interesse, welches den Sykophanten des\n Kapitals, den politischen Oekonomen, im Mutterland bestimmt, die kapi-\n talistische Produktionsweise theoretisch für ihr eignes Gegen-\n theil zu erklären, dasselbe Interesse treibt ihn hier „to make a clear breast\n of it“ und den Gegensatz beider Produktionsweisen zu pro-\n klamiren. Zu diesem Behuf weist er nach, wie die Entwicklung der\n gesellschaftlichen Produktivkraft der Arbeit, Cooperation, Arbeitstheilung,\n Anwendung der Maschinerie im Grossen u. s. w. unmöglich sind ohne\n die Expropriation der Arbeiter und entsprechende Verwand-\n lung ihrer Produktionsmittel in Kapital. Im Interesse des\n s. g. Nationalreichthums sucht er nach Kunstmitteln zur Herstel-\n lung der Volksarmuth. Sein apologetischer Panzer zerbröckelt hier\n Stück für Stück wie mürber Zunder. Es ist das grosse Verdienst E. G. Wake-\n field’s, nicht irgend etwas neues über die Kolonien [Note: Die wenigen Lichtblicke Wakefield’s über das Wesen der Kolonien selbst\n sind vollständig anticipirt durch Mirabeau père, den Physiokraten, und noch\n viel früher durch englische Oekonomen.] , aber in den\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"747\", \"facs\": \"#f0766\"} --> Kolonien die Wahrheit über die kapitalistischen Verhältnisse des Mutter-\n lands entdeckt zu haben. Wie das Protektionssystem in seinen Ursprün-\n gen [Note: Es wird später eine temporäre Nothwendigkeit im internationalen Kon-\n kurrenzkampf. Welches aber immer sein Motiv, die Folgen bleiben dieselben.] die Fabrikation von Kapitalisten im Mutter-\n land, so erstrebt Wakefield’s Kolonisationstheorie, welche England eine\n Zeit lang gesetzlich ins Werk zu setzen suchte, die Fabrikation\n von Lohnarbeitern in den Kolonien. Das nennt er „syste-\n matic colonization“ (systematische Kolonisation).\n\n\n\n\nZunächst entdeckte Wakefield in den Kolonien, dass das Eigen-\n thum an Geld, Lebensmitteln, Maschinen und andern Produktionsmitteln einen\n Menschen noch nicht zum Kapitalisten stempelt, wenn die Ergänzung fehlt,\n der Lohnarbeiter, der andre Mensch, der sich selbst freiwillig zu ver-\n kaufen gezwungen ist. Er entdeckte, dass das Kapital nicht eine Sache\n ist, sondern ein durch Sachen vermitteltes gesellschaftliches Verhält-\n niss zwischen Personen [Note: „Ein Neger ist ein Neger. In bestimmten Verhältnissen wird er erst zum\nSklaven. Eine Baumwollmaschine ist eine Maschine zum Baumwollspinnen.\n Nur in bestimmten Verhältnissen wird sie zu Kapital. Aus diesen Verhältnissen\n herausgerissen, ist sie so wenig Kapital, wie Gold an und für sich Geld oder\n der Zucker der Zucker preis ist … Das Kapital ist ein gesellschaft-\n liches Produktionsverhältniss. Es ist ein historisches Produk-\n tionsverhältniss.“ (Karl Marx: „Lohnarbeit und Kapital.“\n N. Rh. Z. Nr. 266 vom 7. April 1849.)] . Herr Peel, jammert er uns vor, nahm Lebens-\n mittel und Produktionsmittel zum Belauf von 50,000 Pfd. St. aus Eng-\n land nach dem Swan River, Neuholland, mit. Herr Peel war so vorsich-\n tig, ausserdem 3000 Personen der arbeitenden Klasse, Männer, Weiber\n und Kinder mitzubringen. Einmal am Bestimmungsplatz angelangt,\n „blieb Herr Peel ohne einen Diener sein Bett zu machen oder ihm Wasser\n aus dem Fluss zu schöpfen“ [Note: E. G. Wakefield: „England and America“, v. II, p. 33.] . Unglücklicher Herr Peel, der alles\n vorsah, nur nicht den Export der englischen Produktionsverhältnisse nach\n dem Swan River!\n\n\n\n\nZum Verständniss der folgenden Entdeckungen Wakefield’s zwei Vor-\n bemerkungen. Man weiss: Produktions- und Lebensmittel als\n Eigenthum des unmittelbaren Producenten, des Arbeiters selbst, sind kein\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"748\", \"facs\": \"#f0767\"} --> Kapital. Sie werden Kapital nur unter Bedingungen, worin sie zu-\n gleich als Exploitations- und Beherrschungsmittel des\n Arbeiters dienen. Diese ihre kapitalistische Seele ist aber im Kopfe\n des politischen Oekonomen so innig mit ihrer stofflichen Substanz ver-\n mählt, dass er sie unter allen Umständen Kapital tauft, auch wo sie das\n grade Gegentheil sind. So bei Wakefield. Ferner: die Zersplitterung\n der Produktionsmittel als individuelles Eigenthum vieler von einander\n unabhängiger, selbstwirthschaftender Arbeiter nennt er gleiche Thei-\n lung des Kapitals. Es geht dem politischen Oekonomen, wie dem\n feudalen Juristen. Letzterer klebte auch auf reine Geldverhältnisse seine\n feudalen Rechtsetiquetten.\n\n\n\n\n„Wäre“, sagt Wakefield, „das Kapital unter alle Mitglieder der Ge-\n sellschaft in gleiche Portionen getheilt, so hätte kein Mensch ein\n Interesse mehr Kapital zu accumuliren als er mit seinen\n eignen Händen anwenden kann. Diess ist in gewissem Grad\n der Fall in neuen amerikanischen Kolonien, wo die Leidenschaft\n für Grundeigenthum die Existenz einer Klasse von Lohn-\n arbeitern verhindert“ [Note: l. c. v. I, p. 17, 18.] . So lange also der Arbeiter für sich\n selbst accumuliren kann, und das kann er, so lange er Eigenthümer seiner\n Produktionsmittel bleibt, ist die kapitalistische Accumulation\n und die kapitalistische Produktionsweise unmöglich. Die\n dazu unentbehrliche Klasse der Lohnarbeiter fehlt. Wie wurde\n nun im alten Europa die Expropriation des Arbeiters von\n seinen Arbeitsbedingungen, daher Kapital und Lohnarbeit, her-\n gestellt? Durch einen contrat social ganz origineller Art. „Die Mensch-\n heit adoptirte eine einfache Methode zur Förderung der Accumula-\n tion des Kapitals“, die ihr natürlich seit Adams Zeiten als letzter und\n einziger Zweck ihres Daseins vorschwebte; „sie theilte sich in\n Eigner von Kapital und Eigner von Arbeit … diese Thei-\n lung war das Resultat freiwilliger Verständigung und\n Kombination“ [Note: l. c. p. 81.] . Mit einem Wort: die Masse der Menschheit\nexpropriirte sich selbst zu Ehren der „Accumulation des Kapi-\n tals“. Nun sollte man glauben, der Instinkt dieses selbstentsagenden\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"749\", \"facs\": \"#f0768\"} --> Fanatismus müsse sich namentlich in Kolonien den Zügel frei schiessen\n lassen, wo allein Menschen und Umstände existiren, welche einen contrat\n social aus dem Traumreich in das der Wirklichkeit übersetzen könnten.\n Aber wozu dann überhaupt die „systematische Kolonisation“\n im Gegensatz zur naturwüchsigen Kolonisation? Aber, aber\n „in den nördlichen Staaten der amerikanischen Union ist es zweifelhaft,\n ob ein Zehntel der Bevölkerung der Kategorie der Lohnarbeiter ange-\n hört … In England besteht die grosse Volksmasse aus Lohnarbeitern“ [Note: l. c. p. 43, 44] .\n Ja der Selbstexpropriationstrieb der arbeitenden Menschheit zu Ehren des\n Kapitals existirt so wenig, dass nach Wakefield selbst Sklaverei die\n einzige naturwüchsige Grundlage des Kolonialreichthums ist. Seine\nsystematische Kolonisation ist ein blosses pis aller, da er nun einmal\n mit Freien, statt mit Sklaven zu operiren hat. „Ohne Sklaverei\n wäre das Kapital in den spanischen Niederlassungen kaput gegangen\n oder wenigstens auf die kleinen Massen zusammengeschrumpft, worin jedes\n Individuum es mit seinen eignen Händen anwenden kann. Diess fand\n wirklich statt in der letzten von den Engländern gegründeten Kolonie, wo\n ein grosses Kapital in Samen, Vieh und Instrumenten unterging am Man-\n gel von Lohnarbeitern und wo kein Ansiedler viel mehr Kapital besitzt als\n er mit seinen eignen Händen anwenden kann“ [Note: l. c. v. II, p. 5] .\n\n\n\n\nMan sah: die Expropriation der Volksmasse von Grund\n und Boden bildet die Grundlage der kapitalistischen\n Produktionsweise. Das Wesen einer freien Kolonie besteht um-\n gekehrt darin, dass die Masse des Bodens noch Volkseigenthum ist und\n jeder Ansiedler daher einen Theil davon in sein Privateigenthum und in-\n dividuelles Produktionsmittel verwandeln kann, ohne den spätern Ansiedler\n an derselben Operation zu verhindern [Note: „Land, um Element der Kolonisation zu werden, muss nicht nur unan-\n gebaut sein, sondern öffentliches Eigenthum, welches in Privateigenthum verwan-\n delt werden kann.“ (l. c. v. II, p. 125.)] . Diess ist das Geheimniss, so-\n wohl der Blüthe der Kolonien, als ihres Krebswurms — ihres Widerstands\n wider die Ansiedlung des Kapitals. „Wo Land sehr wohl-\n feil ist und alle Menschen frei sind, wo jeder nach Wunsch ein\n Stück Land für sich selbst erhalten kann, ist Arbeit nicht nur sehr theuer,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"750\", \"facs\": \"#f0769\"} --> was den Antheil des Arbeiters an seinem Produkt angeht, sondern die\n Schwierigkeit ist, kombinirte Arbeit zu irgend einem\n Preis zu erhalten“ [Note: l. c. v. I, p. 247.] .\n\n\n\n\nDa in den Kolonien die Scheidung des Arbeiters von den Ar-\n beitsbedingungen und ihrer Wurzel, dem Grund und Boden, noch nicht existirt,\n oder nur sporadisch, oder auf zu beschränktem Spielraum, existirt auch noch\n nicht die Losscheidung der Agrikultur von der Industrie, noch\n nicht die Vernichtung der ländlich häuslichen Industrie,\n und wo soll da der innere Markt für das Kapital herkommen? „Kein\n Theil der Bevölkerung Amerikas ist ausschliesslich agrikol, mit\n Ausnahme der Sklaven und ihrer Anwender, die Kapital und Arbeit für\n grosse Werke kombiniren. Freie Amerikaner, die den Boden selbst bauen,\n treiben zugleich viele andre Beschäftigungen. Ein Theil der von ihnen\n gebrauchten Möbel und Werkzeuge wird gewöhnlich von ihnen selbst ge-\n macht. Sie bauen häufig ihre eignen Häuser und bringen das Produkt\n ihrer eignen Industrie zu noch so fernem Markt. Sie sind Spinner und\n Weber, sie fabriciren Seife und Kerzen, Schuhe und Kleider für ihren\n eignen Gebrauch. In Amerika bildet der Landbau oft das Nebenge-\n schäft eines Grobschmidts, Müllers oder Krämers“ [Note: l. c. p. 22, 23.] . Wo bleibt unter\n solchen Käuzen das „Entsagungsfeld“ für den Kapitalisten?\n\n\n\n\nDie grosse Schönheit der kapitalistischen Produktion besteht darin,\n dass sie den Lohnarbeiter nicht nur als Lohnarbeiter beständig\n reproducirt, sondern im Verhältniss zur Accumulation des\n Kapitals stets eine relative Uebervölkerung von Lohnar-\n beitern producirt. So wird das Gesetz von Arbeits nach frage\n und Zufuhr im richtigen Gleis gehalten, die Lohnschwankung innerhalb\n der kapitalistischen Exploitation konvenabler Schranken gebannt, und end-\n lich die so unentbehrliche sociale Abhängigkeit des Arbeiters\n vom Kapitalisten garantirt, ein absolutes Abhängigkeits-\n verhältniss, das der politische Oekonom zu Haus, im Mutterland, brei-\n mäulig in ein freies Kontraktverhältniss von Käufer und\n Verkäufer, von gleich unabhängigen Waaren besitzern,\n Besitzern der Waare Kapital und der Waare Arbeit, nach Belieben um-\n lügen kann. Aber in den Kolonien reisst der schöne Wahn entzwei.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"751\", \"facs\": \"#f0770\"} --> Die absolute Arbeiterbevölkerung wächst hier viel rascher als im Mutter-\n land, indem viele Arbeiter erwachsen auf die Welt kommen, und dennoch\n ist der Arbeitsmarkt stets untervoll. Das Gesetz der Arbeitsnachfrage\n und Zufuhr geräth in die Brüche, wie Taylor’s Formel in einem Fall des\n Differentialkalkuls. Einerseits wirft die alte Welt fortwährend exploita-\n tionslustiges, entsagungsbedürftiges Kapital ein; andrerseits stösst die\nregelmässige Reproduktion des Lohnarbeiters als Lohn-\n arbeiter und daher noch mehr die Produktion einer Surplus-\n Arbeiterbevölkerung im Verhältniss zur Accumulation des Kapitals\n auf die unartigsten und theilweis unüberwindlichen Hindernisse. Der Lohn-\n arbeiter von heute wird morgen unabhängiger, selbst wirthschaftender Bauer\n oder Handwerker. Er verschwindet vom Arbeitsmarkt, aber nicht ins —\n Workhouse. Diese beständige Verwandlung der Lohnarbeiter in\n unabhängige Producenten, die statt für das Kapital, für sich selbst\n arbeiten, und statt den Herrn Kapitalisten sich selbst bereichern, wirkt ihrer-\n seits durchaus schadhaft auf die Zustände des Lohnarbeitsmarkts\n zurück. Nicht nur bleibt der Exploitationsgrad des Lohnarbeiters unan-\n ständig niedrig. Der letztre verliert mit dem Abhängigkeitsverhältniss\n auch das Abhängigkeitsgefühl vom entsagenden Kapitalisten. Daher alle\n die Missstände, die unser E. G. Wakefield so brav, so beredt und so\n rührend schildert.\n\n\n\n\nDie Zufuhr des Lohnarbeitsmarkts, klagt er, ist weder beständig,\n noch regelmässig, noch genügend. „Sie ist stets nicht nur zu klein, son-\n dern unsicher“ [Note: l. c. v. II, p. 116.] . „Obgleich das zwischen Arbeiter und Kapitalist zu\n theilende Produkt gross ist, nimmt der Arbeiter einen so\n grossen Theil, dasser rasch ein Kapitalist wird … Dage-\n gen können Wenige, selbst wenn sie ungewöhnlich lang leben, grosse Reich-\n thummassen accumuliren“ [Note: l. c. v. I, p. 130, 131.] . Die Arbeiter erlauben dem Kapitalisten\n platterdings nicht auf Zahlung des grössten Theils ihrer Arbeit zu\nentsagen. Es hilft ihm nichts, wenn er so schlau ist, mit seinem\n eignen Kapital auch seine eignen Lohnarbeiter aus Europa zu importiren.\n „Sie hören bald auf Lohnarbeiter zu sein, sie verwandeln sich bald in\n unabhängige Bauern oder gar in Konkurrenten ihrer alten Meister auf dem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"752\", \"facs\": \"#f0771\"} --> Lohnarbeitsmarkt selbst“ [Note: l. c. v. II, p. 5.] . Man begreife den Greuel! Der brave Kapi-\n talist hat seine eignen leibhaftigen Konkurrenten selbst aus Europa für\n sein eignes gutes Geld importirt! Da hört denn doch alles auf! Kein\n Wunder, wenn Wakefield klagt über mangelndes Abhängigkeits-\n verhältniss und -Gefühl der Lohnarbeiter in den Kolonien.\n „Wegen der hohen Löhne“, sagt sein Schüler Merivale, „existirt in\n den Kolonien der leidenschaftliche Drang nach wohlfeilerer und\n unterwürfigerer Arbeit, nach einer Klasse, welcher der\n Kapitalist die Bedingungen diktiren kann, statt sie von\n ihr diktirt zu erhalten … In altcivilisirten Ländern ist der Ar-\n beiter, obgleich frei, naturgesetzlich abhängig vom Ka-\n pitalisten, in Kolonien muss diese Abhängigkeit durch\n künstliche Mittel geschaffen werden“ [Note: Merivale l. c. v. II, p. 235—314 passim. Selbst der sanfte, frei-\n händlerische Vulgärökonom Molinari sagt: „Dans les colonies où l’esclavage\n a été aboli sans que le travail forcé se trouvait remplacé par une quan-\n tité équivalente de travail libre, on a vu s’opérer la contre-partie du\nfait quise réalise tous les jours sous nos yeux. On a vu les simples\n travailleurs exploiter à leur tour les entrepreneurs d’industrie, exiger d’eux\n des salaires hors de toute proportion avec la part légitime qui leur revenait\n dans le produit. Les planteurs, ne pouvant obtenir de leurs sucres un prix suffi-\n sant pour couvrir la hausse de salaire, ont été obligés de fournir l’excédant, d’abord\nsurleurs profits, ensuite sur leurs capitaux mêmes. Une foule de planteurs\n ont été ruinés de la sorte, d’autres ont fermé leurs atéliers pour échapper à une\n ruine imminente … Sans doute, il vaut mieux voir périr des accumulations des\n capitaux, que des générations d’hommes (wie generös von dem Herrn Molinari!);\n mais ne vaudrait-il pas mieux que ni les uns ni les autres périssent?“ (Moli-\n nari l. c. p. 51, 52.) Herr Molinari, Herr Molinari! Was wird denn aus den\n zehn Geboten, aus Moses und den Propheten, aus dem Gesetz der Nach-\n frage und Zufuhr, wenn in Europa der „entrepreneur“ dem Arbeiter, und in\n Westindien der Arbeiter dem entrepreneur seine part légitime verkürzen kann?\n Und was ist gefälligst diese „part légitime“, die nach Ihrem Geständniss der Kapi-\n talist in Europa täglich nicht zahlt? Den Herrn Molinari juckt es gewaltig, dort\n drüben, in den Kolonien, wo die Arbeiter so „einfach“ sind, den Kapitalisten zu\n „exploitiren“, das sonst automatisch wirkende Gesetz der Nachfrage und Zufuhr\n polizeilich in den richtigen Gang zu setzen.] .\n\n\n\n\nWas ist nun das Resultat des in den Kolonien herrschenden\nSystems des auf eigner Arbeit, statt auf der Exploita-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"753\", \"facs\": \"#f0772\"} --> tion fremder Arbeit beruhenden Privateigenthums? Ein\n „barbarisirendes System der Zerstreuung der Producenten und des\n Nationalvermögens“ [Note: Wakefield l. c. v. II, p. 52.] . Die Zerstreuung der Produktionsmittel unter unzäh-\n lige, sie eignende und mit ihnen selbst arbeitende Producenten vernichtet mit\nder kapitalistischen Koncentration die kapitalistische Grund-\n lage aller kombinirten Arbeit. Alle langathmigen Kapitalunter-\n nehmungen, die sich über Jahre ausdehnen und Anlage von viel fixem\n Kapital erheischen, werden problematisch. In Europa zögert das Kapital\n keinen Augenblick, denn die Arbeiterklasse bildet seinen stets überfliessenden,\n disponiblen, lebendigen Zubehör. Aber in den Kolonialländern! Wake-\n field erzählt eine äusserst schmerzensreiche Anekdote. Er unterhielt sich\n mit einigen Kapitalisten von Kanada und dem Staat New-York, wo zudem\n die Einwanderungswogen oft stagniren und einen Bodensatz „überzäh-\n liger“ Arbeiter niederschlagen. „Unser Kapital,“ seufzt eine der Per-\n sonen des Melodramas, „unser Kapital lag bereit für viele Operationen,\n die eine beträchtliche Zeitperiode zu ihrer Vollendung brauchen; aber\n konnten wir solche Operationen beginnen mit einer Arbeit, welche, wir\n wussten es, uns bald den Rücken wenden würde? Wären wir sicher ge-\n wesen die Arbeit solcher Einwandrer festhalten zu können, wir hätten\n sie mit Freude sofort engagirt und zu hohem Preis. Trotz der Sicherheit\n ihres Verlustes würden wir sie dennoch engagirt haben, wären wir einer\n frischen Zufuhr je nach unsrem Bedürfniss sicher gewesen“ [Note: l. c. p. 191, 192.] .\n\n\n\n\nNachdem Wakefield die englische kapitalistische Agrikultur und ihre\n „kombinirte“ Arbeit prunkvoll kontrastirt hat mit der zerstrenten ameri-\n kanischen Bauernwirthschaft, entschlüpft ihm auch die Kehrseite der\n Medaille. Er schildert die amerikanische Volksmasse als wohlhabend, unab-\n hängig, unternehmend, und relativ gebildet, während „der englische Agrikul-\n turarbeiter ein elender Lump (a miserable wretch) ist, ein Pauper … In\n welchem Land ausser Nordamerika und einigen neuen Kolonien über-\n steigen die Löhne der auf dem Land angewandten freien Arbeit nennens-\n werth die unentbehrlichsten Subsistenzmittel des Arbeiters? … Zweifels-\n ohne, Ackerpferde in England, da sie ein werthvolles Eigenthum sind,\n werden viel besser genährt als der englische Landbebauer“ [Note: l. . v. I, p. 47, 246, 247.] . Aber,\n [Running matter: I. 48] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"754\", \"facs\": \"#f0773\"} --> never mind, Nationalreichthum ist nun einmal von Natur iden-\n tisch mit Volkselend.\n\n\n\n\nWie nun den antikapitalistischen Krebsschaden der Kolonien kuriren?\n Wollte man allen Grund und Boden mit einem Schlag aus Volkseigenthum\n in Privateigenthum verwandeln, so zerstörte man zwar die Wurzel des\n Uebels, aber auch — die Kolonie. Die Kunst ist zwei Fliegen mit\n einer Klappe zu schlagen. Man gebe von Regierungswegen der jungfräu-\n lichen Erde einen vom Gesetz der Nachfrage und Zufuhr un-\n abhängigen, einen künstlichen Preis, welcher den Einwanderer\n zwingt längere Zeit zu lohnarbeiten, bevor er genug Geld verdient hat,\n um Grund und Boden zu kaufen [Note: „C’est, ajoutez-vous, grâce à l’appropriation du sol et des capitaux que\nl’homme, quin’a que ses bras, trouve de l’occupation, et se fait un revenu\n … c’est, au contraire, grâce à l’appropriation individuelle du sol\n qu’il se trouve des hommes n’ayant queleurs bras … Quand vous\n mettez un homme dans le vide, vous vous emparez de l’atmosphère. Ainsi faites-\n vous, quand vous vous emparez du sol … C’est le mettre dans le vide de richesses,\n pour ne le laisser vivre qu’à votre volonté.“ (Colins l. c. t. III, p. 268—271\npassim.)]  und sich in einen unabhängigen\n Bauern zu verwandeln. Den Fonds, der aus diesem Verkauf der Lände-\n reien zu einem für den Lohnarbeiter relativ prohibitorischen Preis\n fliesst, also diesen aus dem Arbeitslohn durch Verletzung des heiligen\n Gesetzes von Nachfrage und Zufuhr erpressten Geldfonds verwende die\n Regierung andrerseits, um im selben Mass, wie er wächst, Habenichtse\n aus Europa in die Kolonien zu importiren und so dem Herrn Kapitalisten\nseinen Lohnarbeitsmarkt vollzuhalten. Unter diesen Umständen\n „tout sera pour le mieux dans le meilleur des mondes possibles.“ Diess\n ist das grosse Geheimniss der „systematischen Kolonisation“.\n „Nach diesem Plan,“ ruft Wakefield triumphirend aus, „muss die Zu-\n fuhr von Arbeit constant und regelmässig sein; denn\n erstens, da kein Arbeiter fähig ist sich Land zu verschaffen, bevor er für\n Geld gearbeitet hat, würden alle einwandernden Arbeiter dadurch, dass\n sie für Lohn kombinirt arbeiten, ihrem Anwender Kapital zur An-\n wendung von mehr Arbeit produciren; zweitens jeder, der die\n Lohnarbeit an den Nagel hinge und Grundeigner würde, würde grade\ndurch den Ankauf des Landes einen Fonds zur Herüber-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"755\", \"facs\": \"#f0774\"} --> bringung frischer Arbeit nach den Kolonien sichern“ [Note: Wakefield l. c. v. II, p. 192.] .\n Der von Staatswegen oktroyirte Bodenpreis muss natürlich „genügend“\n (sufficient price) sein, d. h. so hoch, „dass er die Arbeiter verhindert, un-\n abhängige Bauern zu werden, bis andre da sind, um ihren Platz auf dem\n Lohnarbeitsmarkt einzunehmen“ [Note: l. c. p. 45.] . Dieser genügende „Boden-\n preis“ ist nichts als eine euphemistische Umschreibung des Lösegelds,\n welches der Arbeiter dem Kapitalisten zahlt für die Erlaubniss, sich vom\n Lohnarbeitsmarkt auf’s Land zurückzuziehn. Erst muss er dem Herrn Kapi-\n talisten „Kapital“ produciren, damit der mehr Arbeiter exploi-\n tiren könne, und dann auf dem Arbeitsmarkt einen „Ersatzmann“\n stellen, den die Regierung auf seine Kosten seinem ehemaligen Herrn Ka-\n pitalisten über die See spedirt.\n\n\n\n\nEs ist höchst charakteristisch, dass die englische Regierung diese\n von Herrn Wakefield eigens zum Gebrauch in Kolonialländern verschrie-\n bene Methode der „ursprünglichen Accumulation“ Jahre lang\n ausgeführt hat. Das Fiasko war natürlich ebenso schmählich als das des\n Peelschen Bankakts. Der Emigrationsstrom wurde nur von den englischen\n Kolonien nach den Vereinigten Staaten abgelenkt. Unterdess hat der Fort-\n schritt der kapitalistischen Produktion in Europa zusammen mit dem wach-\n senden Regierungsdruck Wakefield’s Recept überflüssig gemacht. Der\n ungeheure und continuirliche Menschenstrom, Jahr aus Jahr ein nach Ame-\n rika getrieben, lässt theils stagnirende Niederschläge im Osten der Vereinig-\n ten Staaten zurück, theils wirft die Emigrationswelle von Europa die Men-\n schen rascher auf den Arbeitsmarkt als die Emigrationswelle nach dem\n far West sie abspülen kann. Die kapitalistische Produktion gedeiht da-\n her in den Oststaaten, obgleich Lohntiefe und Abhängigkeit des Lohnar-\n beiters noch lange nicht auf das europäische Normalniveau gefallen sind.\n Die von Wakefield selbst so laut denuncirte, schamlose Verschleuderung\n des unbebauten Kolonialbodens an Aristokraten und Kapitalisten Seitens der\n englischen Regierung hat namentlich in Australien [Note: Sobald Australien sein eigner Gesetzgeber wurde, erliess es natürlich den\n Ansiedlern günstige Gesetze, aber die englische einmal vollzogene Bodenver-\n schleuderung steht im Wege. „The first and main object at which the new] , zusammen mit\n [Running matter: 48*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"756\", \"facs\": \"#f0775\"} --> dem Menschenstrom, den die Diggings hinziehn, und der Konkurrenz,\n welche der Import englischer Waaren selbst dem kleinsten Handwerker\n macht, eine hinreichende „relative Arbeiterübervölkerung“ erzeugt, so\n dass fast jedes Postdampfschiff die Hiobspost eines „glut of the Australian\n labour-market“ bringt, und die Prostitution dort stellenweis so üppig ge-\n deiht wie auf dem Haymarket von London.\n\n\n\n\nJedoch beschäftigt uns hier nicht der Zustand der Kolonien. Was\n uns allein interessirt, ist das in der neuen Welt von der politischen Oeko-\n nomie der alten Welt entdeckte und laut proklamirte Geheimniss, dass die\nkapitalistische Produktions- und Accumulationsweise,\n also auch das kapitalistische Privateigenthum die Vernich-\n tung des auf eigner Arbeit beruhenden Privateigenthums,\n d. h. die Expropriation des Arbeiters voraussetzt.\n\n\n\n\nZum Schluss müssen wir noch einen Augenblick den Faden wieder\n da aufnehmen, wo wir ihn beim Uebergang zur Betrachtung der Accumu-\n lation fallen liessen. Gesetzt der Kapitalist habe 5000 Pfd. St. vorge-\n schossen und im Produktionsprozess aufgezehrt, 4000 Pfd. St. in Produk-\n tionsmitteln, 1000 Pfd. St. in Arbeitskraft, mit einem Exploitationsgrad\n der Arbeit von 100 %. So beträgt der Werth des Produkts, von x Ton-\n nen Eisen z. B. 6000 Pfd. St. Verkauft der Kapitalist das Eisen zu\n seinem Werth, so realisirt er einen Mehrwerth von 1000\n Pfd. St., d. h. die im Eisenwerth materialisirte unbezahlte Arbeit. Aber das\n Eisen muss verkauft werden. Das unmittelbare Resultat der kapitalisti-\n schen Produktion ist Waare, wenn auch mit Mehrwerth geschwängerte\n Waare. Wir sind also zu unserm Ausgangspunkt, der Waare, zurückge-\n schleudert und mit ihr zur Sphäre der Cirkulation. Was wir jedoch im\n folgenden Buch zu betrachten haben, ist nicht mehr die einfache Waa-\n rencirkulation, sondern der Cirkulationsprozess des Ka-\n pitals.\n\n\n\n\n [Note: (Landact of 1862) law aims, is to give increased facilities for the settlement of\n the people.“ („The Land Law of Victoria by the Hon. G. Duffy\n (Minister of Public Lands). Lond. 1862.“] \n\n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"757\", \"facs\": \"#f0776\"} -->","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0001","text":"## 3) Die moderne Kolonisationstheorie [Note: Es handelt sich hier von wirklichen Kolonien, jungfräulichem\n Boden, der durch freie Einwanderer kolonisirt wird. Die Vereinigten Staaten\n sind, ökonomisch gesprochen, immer noch Kolonialland Europa’s. Uebrigens\n gehören auch die alten Pflanzungen hierher, wo die Aufhebung der Sklaverei die\n Verhältnisse gänzlich umgewälzt hat.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0002","text":"Die politische Oekonomie sucht principiell die angenehmste Verwechs-\n lung aufrecht zu erhalten zwischen dem auf eigner Arbeit be-\n ruhenden Privateigenthum und dem diametral entgegenge-\n setzten, auf Vernichtung dieser Art Eigenthums beruhenden kapita-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"746\", \"facs\": \"#f0765\"} --> listischen Privateigenthum. Im Westen von Europa, dem Hei-\n mathsland der politischen Oekonomie, ist der Prozess der ursprünglichen\n Accumulation vollbracht. Die kapitalistische Produktionsweise hat hier\n entweder die ganze nationale Produktion direkt unterworfen, oder, wo die\n Verhältnisse minder entwickelt sind, kontrolirt sie indirekt die noch neben\n ihr fortexistirenden, verkommenden, der veralteten Produktionsweise an-\n gehörigen Gesellschaftsschichten. Auf diese fertige Welt des Kapitals\n wendet der politische Oekonom mit desto ängstlicherem Eifer und desto\n grösserer Salbung die Rechts- und Eigenthumsvorstellungen der vorkapita-\n listischen Welt an, je lauter die Thatsachen seiner Ideologie ins Gesicht\n schreien. Anders in den Kolonien. Die kapitalistische Produktions-\n und Aneignungsweise stösst hier überall auf das Hinderniss des selbst-\n erarbeiteten Eigenthums, des Producenten, der als Privateigenthümer seiner\n eignen Arbeitsbedingungen durch seine Arbeit sich selbst statt den Kapi-\n talisten bereichert. Der Widerspruch dieser zwei diametral\n entgegengesetzten Produktions- und Aneignungs wei-\n sen existirt hier praktisch. Wo der Kapitalist die Macht des\n Mutterlandes im Rücken hat, sucht er die auf eigner Arbeit be-\n ruhende Produktions- und Aneignungsweise gewaltsam aus\n dem Weg zu räumen. Dasselbe Interesse, welches den Sykophanten des\n Kapitals, den politischen Oekonomen, im Mutterland bestimmt, die kapi-\n talistische Produktionsweise theoretisch für ihr eignes Gegen-\n theil zu erklären, dasselbe Interesse treibt ihn hier „to make a clear breast\n of it“ und den Gegensatz beider Produktionsweisen zu pro-\n klamiren. Zu diesem Behuf weist er nach, wie die Entwicklung der\n gesellschaftlichen Produktivkraft der Arbeit, Cooperation, Arbeitstheilung,\n Anwendung der Maschinerie im Grossen u. s. w. unmöglich sind ohne\n die Expropriation der Arbeiter und entsprechende Verwand-\n lung ihrer Produktionsmittel in Kapital. Im Interesse des\n s. g. Nationalreichthums sucht er nach Kunstmitteln zur Herstel-\n lung der Volksarmuth. Sein apologetischer Panzer zerbröckelt hier\n Stück für Stück wie mürber Zunder. Es ist das grosse Verdienst E. G. Wake-\n field’s, nicht irgend etwas neues über die Kolonien [Note: Die wenigen Lichtblicke Wakefield’s über das Wesen der Kolonien selbst\n sind vollständig anticipirt durch Mirabeau père, den Physiokraten, und noch\n viel früher durch englische Oekonomen.] , aber in den\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"747\", \"facs\": \"#f0766\"} --> Kolonien die Wahrheit über die kapitalistischen Verhältnisse des Mutter-\n lands entdeckt zu haben. Wie das Protektionssystem in seinen Ursprün-\n gen [Note: Es wird später eine temporäre Nothwendigkeit im internationalen Kon-\n kurrenzkampf. Welches aber immer sein Motiv, die Folgen bleiben dieselben.] die Fabrikation von Kapitalisten im Mutter-\n land, so erstrebt Wakefield’s Kolonisationstheorie, welche England eine\n Zeit lang gesetzlich ins Werk zu setzen suchte, die Fabrikation\n von Lohnarbeitern in den Kolonien. Das nennt er „syste-\n matic colonization“ (systematische Kolonisation).","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0003","text":"Zunächst entdeckte Wakefield in den Kolonien, dass das Eigen-\n thum an Geld, Lebensmitteln, Maschinen und andern Produktionsmitteln einen\n Menschen noch nicht zum Kapitalisten stempelt, wenn die Ergänzung fehlt,\n der Lohnarbeiter, der andre Mensch, der sich selbst freiwillig zu ver-\n kaufen gezwungen ist. Er entdeckte, dass das Kapital nicht eine Sache\n ist, sondern ein durch Sachen vermitteltes gesellschaftliches Verhält-\n niss zwischen Personen [Note: „Ein Neger ist ein Neger. In bestimmten Verhältnissen wird er erst zum\nSklaven. Eine Baumwollmaschine ist eine Maschine zum Baumwollspinnen.\n Nur in bestimmten Verhältnissen wird sie zu Kapital. Aus diesen Verhältnissen\n herausgerissen, ist sie so wenig Kapital, wie Gold an und für sich Geld oder\n der Zucker der Zucker preis ist … Das Kapital ist ein gesellschaft-\n liches Produktionsverhältniss. Es ist ein historisches Produk-\n tionsverhältniss.“ (Karl Marx: „Lohnarbeit und Kapital.“\n N. Rh. Z. Nr. 266 vom 7. April 1849.)] . Herr Peel, jammert er uns vor, nahm Lebens-\n mittel und Produktionsmittel zum Belauf von 50,000 Pfd. St. aus Eng-\n land nach dem Swan River, Neuholland, mit. Herr Peel war so vorsich-\n tig, ausserdem 3000 Personen der arbeitenden Klasse, Männer, Weiber\n und Kinder mitzubringen. Einmal am Bestimmungsplatz angelangt,\n „blieb Herr Peel ohne einen Diener sein Bett zu machen oder ihm Wasser\n aus dem Fluss zu schöpfen“ [Note: E. G. Wakefield: „England and America“, v. II, p. 33.] . Unglücklicher Herr Peel, der alles\n vorsah, nur nicht den Export der englischen Produktionsverhältnisse nach\n dem Swan River!","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0004","text":"Zum Verständniss der folgenden Entdeckungen Wakefield’s zwei Vor-\n bemerkungen. Man weiss: Produktions- und Lebensmittel als\n Eigenthum des unmittelbaren Producenten, des Arbeiters selbst, sind kein\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"748\", \"facs\": \"#f0767\"} --> Kapital. Sie werden Kapital nur unter Bedingungen, worin sie zu-\n gleich als Exploitations- und Beherrschungsmittel des\n Arbeiters dienen. Diese ihre kapitalistische Seele ist aber im Kopfe\n des politischen Oekonomen so innig mit ihrer stofflichen Substanz ver-\n mählt, dass er sie unter allen Umständen Kapital tauft, auch wo sie das\n grade Gegentheil sind. So bei Wakefield. Ferner: die Zersplitterung\n der Produktionsmittel als individuelles Eigenthum vieler von einander\n unabhängiger, selbstwirthschaftender Arbeiter nennt er gleiche Thei-\n lung des Kapitals. Es geht dem politischen Oekonomen, wie dem\n feudalen Juristen. Letzterer klebte auch auf reine Geldverhältnisse seine\n feudalen Rechtsetiquetten.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0005","text":"„Wäre“, sagt Wakefield, „das Kapital unter alle Mitglieder der Ge-\n sellschaft in gleiche Portionen getheilt, so hätte kein Mensch ein\n Interesse mehr Kapital zu accumuliren als er mit seinen\n eignen Händen anwenden kann. Diess ist in gewissem Grad\n der Fall in neuen amerikanischen Kolonien, wo die Leidenschaft\n für Grundeigenthum die Existenz einer Klasse von Lohn-\n arbeitern verhindert“ [Note: l. c. v. I, p. 17, 18.] . So lange also der Arbeiter für sich\n selbst accumuliren kann, und das kann er, so lange er Eigenthümer seiner\n Produktionsmittel bleibt, ist die kapitalistische Accumulation\n und die kapitalistische Produktionsweise unmöglich. Die\n dazu unentbehrliche Klasse der Lohnarbeiter fehlt. Wie wurde\n nun im alten Europa die Expropriation des Arbeiters von\n seinen Arbeitsbedingungen, daher Kapital und Lohnarbeit, her-\n gestellt? Durch einen contrat social ganz origineller Art. „Die Mensch-\n heit adoptirte eine einfache Methode zur Förderung der Accumula-\n tion des Kapitals“, die ihr natürlich seit Adams Zeiten als letzter und\n einziger Zweck ihres Daseins vorschwebte; „sie theilte sich in\n Eigner von Kapital und Eigner von Arbeit … diese Thei-\n lung war das Resultat freiwilliger Verständigung und\n Kombination“ [Note: l. c. p. 81.] . Mit einem Wort: die Masse der Menschheit\nexpropriirte sich selbst zu Ehren der „Accumulation des Kapi-\n tals“. Nun sollte man glauben, der Instinkt dieses selbstentsagenden\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"749\", \"facs\": \"#f0768\"} --> Fanatismus müsse sich namentlich in Kolonien den Zügel frei schiessen\n lassen, wo allein Menschen und Umstände existiren, welche einen contrat\n social aus dem Traumreich in das der Wirklichkeit übersetzen könnten.\n Aber wozu dann überhaupt die „systematische Kolonisation“\n im Gegensatz zur naturwüchsigen Kolonisation? Aber, aber\n „in den nördlichen Staaten der amerikanischen Union ist es zweifelhaft,\n ob ein Zehntel der Bevölkerung der Kategorie der Lohnarbeiter ange-\n hört … In England besteht die grosse Volksmasse aus Lohnarbeitern“ [Note: l. c. p. 43, 44] .\n Ja der Selbstexpropriationstrieb der arbeitenden Menschheit zu Ehren des\n Kapitals existirt so wenig, dass nach Wakefield selbst Sklaverei die\n einzige naturwüchsige Grundlage des Kolonialreichthums ist. Seine\nsystematische Kolonisation ist ein blosses pis aller, da er nun einmal\n mit Freien, statt mit Sklaven zu operiren hat. „Ohne Sklaverei\n wäre das Kapital in den spanischen Niederlassungen kaput gegangen\n oder wenigstens auf die kleinen Massen zusammengeschrumpft, worin jedes\n Individuum es mit seinen eignen Händen anwenden kann. Diess fand\n wirklich statt in der letzten von den Engländern gegründeten Kolonie, wo\n ein grosses Kapital in Samen, Vieh und Instrumenten unterging am Man-\n gel von Lohnarbeitern und wo kein Ansiedler viel mehr Kapital besitzt als\n er mit seinen eignen Händen anwenden kann“ [Note: l. c. v. II, p. 5] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0006","text":"Man sah: die Expropriation der Volksmasse von Grund\n und Boden bildet die Grundlage der kapitalistischen\n Produktionsweise. Das Wesen einer freien Kolonie besteht um-\n gekehrt darin, dass die Masse des Bodens noch Volkseigenthum ist und\n jeder Ansiedler daher einen Theil davon in sein Privateigenthum und in-\n dividuelles Produktionsmittel verwandeln kann, ohne den spätern Ansiedler\n an derselben Operation zu verhindern [Note: „Land, um Element der Kolonisation zu werden, muss nicht nur unan-\n gebaut sein, sondern öffentliches Eigenthum, welches in Privateigenthum verwan-\n delt werden kann.“ (l. c. v. II, p. 125.)] . Diess ist das Geheimniss, so-\n wohl der Blüthe der Kolonien, als ihres Krebswurms — ihres Widerstands\n wider die Ansiedlung des Kapitals. „Wo Land sehr wohl-\n feil ist und alle Menschen frei sind, wo jeder nach Wunsch ein\n Stück Land für sich selbst erhalten kann, ist Arbeit nicht nur sehr theuer,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"750\", \"facs\": \"#f0769\"} --> was den Antheil des Arbeiters an seinem Produkt angeht, sondern die\n Schwierigkeit ist, kombinirte Arbeit zu irgend einem\n Preis zu erhalten“ [Note: l. c. v. I, p. 247.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0007","text":"Da in den Kolonien die Scheidung des Arbeiters von den Ar-\n beitsbedingungen und ihrer Wurzel, dem Grund und Boden, noch nicht existirt,\n oder nur sporadisch, oder auf zu beschränktem Spielraum, existirt auch noch\n nicht die Losscheidung der Agrikultur von der Industrie, noch\n nicht die Vernichtung der ländlich häuslichen Industrie,\n und wo soll da der innere Markt für das Kapital herkommen? „Kein\n Theil der Bevölkerung Amerikas ist ausschliesslich agrikol, mit\n Ausnahme der Sklaven und ihrer Anwender, die Kapital und Arbeit für\n grosse Werke kombiniren. Freie Amerikaner, die den Boden selbst bauen,\n treiben zugleich viele andre Beschäftigungen. Ein Theil der von ihnen\n gebrauchten Möbel und Werkzeuge wird gewöhnlich von ihnen selbst ge-\n macht. Sie bauen häufig ihre eignen Häuser und bringen das Produkt\n ihrer eignen Industrie zu noch so fernem Markt. Sie sind Spinner und\n Weber, sie fabriciren Seife und Kerzen, Schuhe und Kleider für ihren\n eignen Gebrauch. In Amerika bildet der Landbau oft das Nebenge-\n schäft eines Grobschmidts, Müllers oder Krämers“ [Note: l. c. p. 22, 23.] . Wo bleibt unter\n solchen Käuzen das „Entsagungsfeld“ für den Kapitalisten?","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0008","text":"Die grosse Schönheit der kapitalistischen Produktion besteht darin,\n dass sie den Lohnarbeiter nicht nur als Lohnarbeiter beständig\n reproducirt, sondern im Verhältniss zur Accumulation des\n Kapitals stets eine relative Uebervölkerung von Lohnar-\n beitern producirt. So wird das Gesetz von Arbeits nach frage\n und Zufuhr im richtigen Gleis gehalten, die Lohnschwankung innerhalb\n der kapitalistischen Exploitation konvenabler Schranken gebannt, und end-\n lich die so unentbehrliche sociale Abhängigkeit des Arbeiters\n vom Kapitalisten garantirt, ein absolutes Abhängigkeits-\n verhältniss, das der politische Oekonom zu Haus, im Mutterland, brei-\n mäulig in ein freies Kontraktverhältniss von Käufer und\n Verkäufer, von gleich unabhängigen Waaren besitzern,\n Besitzern der Waare Kapital und der Waare Arbeit, nach Belieben um-\n lügen kann. Aber in den Kolonien reisst der schöne Wahn entzwei.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"751\", \"facs\": \"#f0770\"} --> Die absolute Arbeiterbevölkerung wächst hier viel rascher als im Mutter-\n land, indem viele Arbeiter erwachsen auf die Welt kommen, und dennoch\n ist der Arbeitsmarkt stets untervoll. Das Gesetz der Arbeitsnachfrage\n und Zufuhr geräth in die Brüche, wie Taylor’s Formel in einem Fall des\n Differentialkalkuls. Einerseits wirft die alte Welt fortwährend exploita-\n tionslustiges, entsagungsbedürftiges Kapital ein; andrerseits stösst die\nregelmässige Reproduktion des Lohnarbeiters als Lohn-\n arbeiter und daher noch mehr die Produktion einer Surplus-\n Arbeiterbevölkerung im Verhältniss zur Accumulation des Kapitals\n auf die unartigsten und theilweis unüberwindlichen Hindernisse. Der Lohn-\n arbeiter von heute wird morgen unabhängiger, selbst wirthschaftender Bauer\n oder Handwerker. Er verschwindet vom Arbeitsmarkt, aber nicht ins —\n Workhouse. Diese beständige Verwandlung der Lohnarbeiter in\n unabhängige Producenten, die statt für das Kapital, für sich selbst\n arbeiten, und statt den Herrn Kapitalisten sich selbst bereichern, wirkt ihrer-\n seits durchaus schadhaft auf die Zustände des Lohnarbeitsmarkts\n zurück. Nicht nur bleibt der Exploitationsgrad des Lohnarbeiters unan-\n ständig niedrig. Der letztre verliert mit dem Abhängigkeitsverhältniss\n auch das Abhängigkeitsgefühl vom entsagenden Kapitalisten. Daher alle\n die Missstände, die unser E. G. Wakefield so brav, so beredt und so\n rührend schildert.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0009","text":"Die Zufuhr des Lohnarbeitsmarkts, klagt er, ist weder beständig,\n noch regelmässig, noch genügend. „Sie ist stets nicht nur zu klein, son-\n dern unsicher“ [Note: l. c. v. II, p. 116.] . „Obgleich das zwischen Arbeiter und Kapitalist zu\n theilende Produkt gross ist, nimmt der Arbeiter einen so\n grossen Theil, dasser rasch ein Kapitalist wird … Dage-\n gen können Wenige, selbst wenn sie ungewöhnlich lang leben, grosse Reich-\n thummassen accumuliren“ [Note: l. c. v. I, p. 130, 131.] . Die Arbeiter erlauben dem Kapitalisten\n platterdings nicht auf Zahlung des grössten Theils ihrer Arbeit zu\nentsagen. Es hilft ihm nichts, wenn er so schlau ist, mit seinem\n eignen Kapital auch seine eignen Lohnarbeiter aus Europa zu importiren.\n „Sie hören bald auf Lohnarbeiter zu sein, sie verwandeln sich bald in\n unabhängige Bauern oder gar in Konkurrenten ihrer alten Meister auf dem\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"752\", \"facs\": \"#f0771\"} --> Lohnarbeitsmarkt selbst“ [Note: l. c. v. II, p. 5.] . Man begreife den Greuel! Der brave Kapi-\n talist hat seine eignen leibhaftigen Konkurrenten selbst aus Europa für\n sein eignes gutes Geld importirt! Da hört denn doch alles auf! Kein\n Wunder, wenn Wakefield klagt über mangelndes Abhängigkeits-\n verhältniss und -Gefühl der Lohnarbeiter in den Kolonien.\n „Wegen der hohen Löhne“, sagt sein Schüler Merivale, „existirt in\n den Kolonien der leidenschaftliche Drang nach wohlfeilerer und\n unterwürfigerer Arbeit, nach einer Klasse, welcher der\n Kapitalist die Bedingungen diktiren kann, statt sie von\n ihr diktirt zu erhalten … In altcivilisirten Ländern ist der Ar-\n beiter, obgleich frei, naturgesetzlich abhängig vom Ka-\n pitalisten, in Kolonien muss diese Abhängigkeit durch\n künstliche Mittel geschaffen werden“ [Note: Merivale l. c. v. II, p. 235—314 passim. Selbst der sanfte, frei-\n händlerische Vulgärökonom Molinari sagt: „Dans les colonies où l’esclavage\n a été aboli sans que le travail forcé se trouvait remplacé par une quan-\n tité équivalente de travail libre, on a vu s’opérer la contre-partie du\nfait quise réalise tous les jours sous nos yeux. On a vu les simples\n travailleurs exploiter à leur tour les entrepreneurs d’industrie, exiger d’eux\n des salaires hors de toute proportion avec la part légitime qui leur revenait\n dans le produit. Les planteurs, ne pouvant obtenir de leurs sucres un prix suffi-\n sant pour couvrir la hausse de salaire, ont été obligés de fournir l’excédant, d’abord\nsurleurs profits, ensuite sur leurs capitaux mêmes. Une foule de planteurs\n ont été ruinés de la sorte, d’autres ont fermé leurs atéliers pour échapper à une\n ruine imminente … Sans doute, il vaut mieux voir périr des accumulations des\n capitaux, que des générations d’hommes (wie generös von dem Herrn Molinari!);\n mais ne vaudrait-il pas mieux que ni les uns ni les autres périssent?“ (Moli-\n nari l. c. p. 51, 52.) Herr Molinari, Herr Molinari! Was wird denn aus den\n zehn Geboten, aus Moses und den Propheten, aus dem Gesetz der Nach-\n frage und Zufuhr, wenn in Europa der „entrepreneur“ dem Arbeiter, und in\n Westindien der Arbeiter dem entrepreneur seine part légitime verkürzen kann?\n Und was ist gefälligst diese „part légitime“, die nach Ihrem Geständniss der Kapi-\n talist in Europa täglich nicht zahlt? Den Herrn Molinari juckt es gewaltig, dort\n drüben, in den Kolonien, wo die Arbeiter so „einfach“ sind, den Kapitalisten zu\n „exploitiren“, das sonst automatisch wirkende Gesetz der Nachfrage und Zufuhr\n polizeilich in den richtigen Gang zu setzen.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0010","text":"Was ist nun das Resultat des in den Kolonien herrschenden\nSystems des auf eigner Arbeit, statt auf der Exploita-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"753\", \"facs\": \"#f0772\"} --> tion fremder Arbeit beruhenden Privateigenthums? Ein\n „barbarisirendes System der Zerstreuung der Producenten und des\n Nationalvermögens“ [Note: Wakefield l. c. v. II, p. 52.] . Die Zerstreuung der Produktionsmittel unter unzäh-\n lige, sie eignende und mit ihnen selbst arbeitende Producenten vernichtet mit\nder kapitalistischen Koncentration die kapitalistische Grund-\n lage aller kombinirten Arbeit. Alle langathmigen Kapitalunter-\n nehmungen, die sich über Jahre ausdehnen und Anlage von viel fixem\n Kapital erheischen, werden problematisch. In Europa zögert das Kapital\n keinen Augenblick, denn die Arbeiterklasse bildet seinen stets überfliessenden,\n disponiblen, lebendigen Zubehör. Aber in den Kolonialländern! Wake-\n field erzählt eine äusserst schmerzensreiche Anekdote. Er unterhielt sich\n mit einigen Kapitalisten von Kanada und dem Staat New-York, wo zudem\n die Einwanderungswogen oft stagniren und einen Bodensatz „überzäh-\n liger“ Arbeiter niederschlagen. „Unser Kapital,“ seufzt eine der Per-\n sonen des Melodramas, „unser Kapital lag bereit für viele Operationen,\n die eine beträchtliche Zeitperiode zu ihrer Vollendung brauchen; aber\n konnten wir solche Operationen beginnen mit einer Arbeit, welche, wir\n wussten es, uns bald den Rücken wenden würde? Wären wir sicher ge-\n wesen die Arbeit solcher Einwandrer festhalten zu können, wir hätten\n sie mit Freude sofort engagirt und zu hohem Preis. Trotz der Sicherheit\n ihres Verlustes würden wir sie dennoch engagirt haben, wären wir einer\n frischen Zufuhr je nach unsrem Bedürfniss sicher gewesen“ [Note: l. c. p. 191, 192.] .","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0011","text":"Nachdem Wakefield die englische kapitalistische Agrikultur und ihre\n „kombinirte“ Arbeit prunkvoll kontrastirt hat mit der zerstrenten ameri-\n kanischen Bauernwirthschaft, entschlüpft ihm auch die Kehrseite der\n Medaille. Er schildert die amerikanische Volksmasse als wohlhabend, unab-\n hängig, unternehmend, und relativ gebildet, während „der englische Agrikul-\n turarbeiter ein elender Lump (a miserable wretch) ist, ein Pauper … In\n welchem Land ausser Nordamerika und einigen neuen Kolonien über-\n steigen die Löhne der auf dem Land angewandten freien Arbeit nennens-\n werth die unentbehrlichsten Subsistenzmittel des Arbeiters? … Zweifels-\n ohne, Ackerpferde in England, da sie ein werthvolles Eigenthum sind,\n werden viel besser genährt als der englische Landbebauer“ [Note: l. . v. I, p. 47, 246, 247.] . Aber,\n [Running matter: I. 48] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"754\", \"facs\": \"#f0773\"} --> never mind, Nationalreichthum ist nun einmal von Natur iden-\n tisch mit Volkselend.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0012","text":"Wie nun den antikapitalistischen Krebsschaden der Kolonien kuriren?\n Wollte man allen Grund und Boden mit einem Schlag aus Volkseigenthum\n in Privateigenthum verwandeln, so zerstörte man zwar die Wurzel des\n Uebels, aber auch — die Kolonie. Die Kunst ist zwei Fliegen mit\n einer Klappe zu schlagen. Man gebe von Regierungswegen der jungfräu-\n lichen Erde einen vom Gesetz der Nachfrage und Zufuhr un-\n abhängigen, einen künstlichen Preis, welcher den Einwanderer\n zwingt längere Zeit zu lohnarbeiten, bevor er genug Geld verdient hat,\n um Grund und Boden zu kaufen [Note: „C’est, ajoutez-vous, grâce à l’appropriation du sol et des capitaux que\nl’homme, quin’a que ses bras, trouve de l’occupation, et se fait un revenu\n … c’est, au contraire, grâce à l’appropriation individuelle du sol\n qu’il se trouve des hommes n’ayant queleurs bras … Quand vous\n mettez un homme dans le vide, vous vous emparez de l’atmosphère. Ainsi faites-\n vous, quand vous vous emparez du sol … C’est le mettre dans le vide de richesses,\n pour ne le laisser vivre qu’à votre volonté.“ (Colins l. c. t. III, p. 268—271\npassim.)]  und sich in einen unabhängigen\n Bauern zu verwandeln. Den Fonds, der aus diesem Verkauf der Lände-\n reien zu einem für den Lohnarbeiter relativ prohibitorischen Preis\n fliesst, also diesen aus dem Arbeitslohn durch Verletzung des heiligen\n Gesetzes von Nachfrage und Zufuhr erpressten Geldfonds verwende die\n Regierung andrerseits, um im selben Mass, wie er wächst, Habenichtse\n aus Europa in die Kolonien zu importiren und so dem Herrn Kapitalisten\nseinen Lohnarbeitsmarkt vollzuhalten. Unter diesen Umständen\n „tout sera pour le mieux dans le meilleur des mondes possibles.“ Diess\n ist das grosse Geheimniss der „systematischen Kolonisation“.\n „Nach diesem Plan,“ ruft Wakefield triumphirend aus, „muss die Zu-\n fuhr von Arbeit constant und regelmässig sein; denn\n erstens, da kein Arbeiter fähig ist sich Land zu verschaffen, bevor er für\n Geld gearbeitet hat, würden alle einwandernden Arbeiter dadurch, dass\n sie für Lohn kombinirt arbeiten, ihrem Anwender Kapital zur An-\n wendung von mehr Arbeit produciren; zweitens jeder, der die\n Lohnarbeit an den Nagel hinge und Grundeigner würde, würde grade\ndurch den Ankauf des Landes einen Fonds zur Herüber-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"755\", \"facs\": \"#f0774\"} --> bringung frischer Arbeit nach den Kolonien sichern“ [Note: Wakefield l. c. v. II, p. 192.] .\n Der von Staatswegen oktroyirte Bodenpreis muss natürlich „genügend“\n (sufficient price) sein, d. h. so hoch, „dass er die Arbeiter verhindert, un-\n abhängige Bauern zu werden, bis andre da sind, um ihren Platz auf dem\n Lohnarbeitsmarkt einzunehmen“ [Note: l. c. p. 45.] . Dieser genügende „Boden-\n preis“ ist nichts als eine euphemistische Umschreibung des Lösegelds,\n welches der Arbeiter dem Kapitalisten zahlt für die Erlaubniss, sich vom\n Lohnarbeitsmarkt auf’s Land zurückzuziehn. Erst muss er dem Herrn Kapi-\n talisten „Kapital“ produciren, damit der mehr Arbeiter exploi-\n tiren könne, und dann auf dem Arbeitsmarkt einen „Ersatzmann“\n stellen, den die Regierung auf seine Kosten seinem ehemaligen Herrn Ka-\n pitalisten über die See spedirt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0013","text":"Es ist höchst charakteristisch, dass die englische Regierung diese\n von Herrn Wakefield eigens zum Gebrauch in Kolonialländern verschrie-\n bene Methode der „ursprünglichen Accumulation“ Jahre lang\n ausgeführt hat. Das Fiasko war natürlich ebenso schmählich als das des\n Peelschen Bankakts. Der Emigrationsstrom wurde nur von den englischen\n Kolonien nach den Vereinigten Staaten abgelenkt. Unterdess hat der Fort-\n schritt der kapitalistischen Produktion in Europa zusammen mit dem wach-\n senden Regierungsdruck Wakefield’s Recept überflüssig gemacht. Der\n ungeheure und continuirliche Menschenstrom, Jahr aus Jahr ein nach Ame-\n rika getrieben, lässt theils stagnirende Niederschläge im Osten der Vereinig-\n ten Staaten zurück, theils wirft die Emigrationswelle von Europa die Men-\n schen rascher auf den Arbeitsmarkt als die Emigrationswelle nach dem\n far West sie abspülen kann. Die kapitalistische Produktion gedeiht da-\n her in den Oststaaten, obgleich Lohntiefe und Abhängigkeit des Lohnar-\n beiters noch lange nicht auf das europäische Normalniveau gefallen sind.\n Die von Wakefield selbst so laut denuncirte, schamlose Verschleuderung\n des unbebauten Kolonialbodens an Aristokraten und Kapitalisten Seitens der\n englischen Regierung hat namentlich in Australien [Note: Sobald Australien sein eigner Gesetzgeber wurde, erliess es natürlich den\n Ansiedlern günstige Gesetze, aber die englische einmal vollzogene Bodenver-\n schleuderung steht im Wege. „The first and main object at which the new] , zusammen mit\n [Running matter: 48*] \n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"756\", \"facs\": \"#f0775\"} --> dem Menschenstrom, den die Diggings hinziehn, und der Konkurrenz,\n welche der Import englischer Waaren selbst dem kleinsten Handwerker\n macht, eine hinreichende „relative Arbeiterübervölkerung“ erzeugt, so\n dass fast jedes Postdampfschiff die Hiobspost eines „glut of the Australian\n labour-market“ bringt, und die Prostitution dort stellenweis so üppig ge-\n deiht wie auf dem Haymarket von London.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0014","text":"Jedoch beschäftigt uns hier nicht der Zustand der Kolonien. Was\n uns allein interessirt, ist das in der neuen Welt von der politischen Oeko-\n nomie der alten Welt entdeckte und laut proklamirte Geheimniss, dass die\nkapitalistische Produktions- und Accumulationsweise,\n also auch das kapitalistische Privateigenthum die Vernich-\n tung des auf eigner Arbeit beruhenden Privateigenthums,\n d. h. die Expropriation des Arbeiters voraussetzt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0015","text":"Zum Schluss müssen wir noch einen Augenblick den Faden wieder\n da aufnehmen, wo wir ihn beim Uebergang zur Betrachtung der Accumu-\n lation fallen liessen. Gesetzt der Kapitalist habe 5000 Pfd. St. vorge-\n schossen und im Produktionsprozess aufgezehrt, 4000 Pfd. St. in Produk-\n tionsmitteln, 1000 Pfd. St. in Arbeitskraft, mit einem Exploitationsgrad\n der Arbeit von 100 %. So beträgt der Werth des Produkts, von x Ton-\n nen Eisen z. B. 6000 Pfd. St. Verkauft der Kapitalist das Eisen zu\n seinem Werth, so realisirt er einen Mehrwerth von 1000\n Pfd. St., d. h. die im Eisenwerth materialisirte unbezahlte Arbeit. Aber das\n Eisen muss verkauft werden. Das unmittelbare Resultat der kapitalisti-\n schen Produktion ist Waare, wenn auch mit Mehrwerth geschwängerte\n Waare. Wir sind also zu unserm Ausgangspunkt, der Waare, zurückge-\n schleudert und mit ihr zur Sphäre der Cirkulation. Was wir jedoch im\n folgenden Buch zu betrachten haben, ist nicht mehr die einfache Waa-\n rencirkulation, sondern der Cirkulationsprozess des Ka-\n pitals.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0016","text":" [Note: (Landact of 1862) law aims, is to give increased facilities for the settlement of\n the people.“ („The Land Law of Victoria by the Hon. G. Duffy\n (Minister of Public Lands). Lond. 1862.“] ","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0017","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"757\", \"facs\": \"#f0776\"} -->","kind":"display"}],"sha256":"679739f0bd3f4a7474f164ed8d3fd355feb2bd489c13c6a5fb43c7d1a994e3fe","language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"3) Die moderne Kolonisationstheorie.","source_sha256":"679739f0bd3f4a7474f164ed8d3fd355feb2bd489c13c6a5fb43c7d1a994e3fe","sha256":"b65831c13aa7869a4afaf054ac2c91cefc819e484b440970aee88692f73dc18c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T21:04:24.490301+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0001","text":"## 3) The modern theory of colonisation [Note: What is in question here is actual colonies, virgin\n soil, colonised by free immigrants. The United States,\n economically speaking, are still colonial territory of Europe. Moreover,\n the old plantations also belong here, where the abolition of slavery has\n completely revolutionised conditions.] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0001"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0002","text":"Political economy seeks in principle to maintain the most convenient con-\n fusion between private property based on one’s own labour and the diametrically opposed capi-\n talist private property based on the destruction of this kind of property.\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"746\", \"facs\": \"#f0765\"} --> In Western Europe, the home-\n land of political economy, the process of primitive accumulation has been\n accomplished. Here the capitalist mode of production has either directly\n subordinated the entire national production, or, where conditions are less\n developed, it indirectly controls the decaying social strata, belonging to the\n obsolete mode of production, which still continue to exist alongside it.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0002"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0003","text":"To this finished world of capital\n the political economist applies, with all the more anxious zeal and all the\n greater unction, the legal and property conceptions of the pre-capita-\n list world, the louder the facts cry in the face of his ideology. It is different\n in the colonies. Here the capitalist mode of production\n and appropriation encounters everywhere the obstacle of property earned by one’s own labour, of the producer who, as private owner of his\n own conditions of labour, enriches himself by his labour instead of the capi-\n talist. The contradiction of these two diametrically\n opposed modes of production and appropriation\n exists here in practice. Where the capitalist has the power of the\n mother country behind him, he seeks forcibly to clear out of\n the way the mode of production and appropriation based on one’s own labour.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0002"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0004","text":"The same interest which determines the sycophant of\n capital, the political economist, in the mother country, theoretically to declare\n the capitalist mode of production to be its own opposite, the same interest\n drives him here “to make a clear breast of it” and to proclaim the opposition\n of the two modes of production. For this purpose he demonstrates how the\n development of the social productive power of labour, co-operation, division\n of labour, employment of machinery on a large scale, etc., are impossible\n without the expropriation of the workers and the corresponding transfor-\n mation of their means of production into capital. In the interest of\n so-called national wealth, he seeks artificial devices for the production\n of popular poverty. Here his apologetic armour crumbles piece by\n piece like rotten tinder. It is the great merit of E. G.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0002"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0005","text":"Wake-\n field, not to have discovered anything new about the colonies [Note: The few flashes of insight of Wakefield concerning the nature of the colonies themselves\n are completely anticipated by Mirabeau père, the Physiocrat, and, much\n earlier still, by English economists.] , but to have dis-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"747\", \"facs\": \"#f0766\"} --> covered in the colonies the truth about the capitalist conditions of the mother-\n land. Just as the protection system in its origins [Note: It later becomes a temporary necessity in the struggle of international com-\n petition. But whatever its motive, the consequences remain the same.] seeks the manufacture of capitalists in the mother-\n land, so Wakefield’s theory of colonisation, which England for a time sought\n to put into effect by law, seeks the manufacture\n of wage-labourers in the colonies.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0002"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0006","text":"He calls this “syste-\n matic colonization” (systematic colonisation).","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0002"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0007","text":"First of all, Wakefield discovered in the colonies that property in money,\n means of subsistence, machines, and other means of production does not yet\n stamp a man as a capitalist, if the complement is lacking,\n the wage-labourer, the other man who is compelled voluntarily to sell\n himself. He discovered that capital is not a thing,\n but a social relation between persons mediated by things [Note: “A Negro is a Negro. Only under definite relations does he become a\nslave. A cotton-spinning machine is a machine for spinning cotton.\n Only under definite relations does it become capital. Torn out of these relations,\n it is no more capital than gold is in and for itself money, or\n sugar the price of sugar … Capital is a social relation\n of production.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0003"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0008","text":"It is a historical relation\n of production.” (Karl Marx: “Wage-Labour and Capital.”\n N. Rh. Z. No. 266 of 7 April 1849.)] . Mr Peel, he laments to us, took means of subsis-\n tence and means of production to the value of £50,000 from Eng-\n land to Swan River, New Holland. Mr Peel was so cautious as, in addition,\n to bring 3,000 persons of the working class, men, women,\n and children. Once arrived at the place of destination,\n “Mr Peel was left without a servant to make his bed or fetch him water\n from the river” [Note: E. G. Wakefield: “England and America”, vol. II, p. 33.] . Unfortunate Mr Peel, who foresaw everything\n except the export of English relations of production to Swan River!","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0003"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0009","text":"For the understanding of Wakefield’s following discoveries, two prelimi-\n nary remarks. It is known that means of production and means of subsistence as\n property of the immediate producer, the worker himself, are not\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"748\", \"facs\": \"#f0767\"} --> capital. They become capital only under conditions in which they\n at the same time serve as means of exploitation and domination\n over the worker. But their capitalist soul is so intimately wedded to their\n material substance in the head of the political economist that he christens\n them capital under all circumstances, even where they are the exact opposite.\n Thus with Wakefield. Further: the dispersal\n of the means of production as individual property among many workers,\n independent of one another and engaged in self-sufficient husbandry, he calls equal divi-\n sion of capital. It fares with the political economist as with the\n feudal jurist.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0004"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0010","text":"The latter too stuck his\n feudal legal labels upon mere money relations.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0004"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0011","text":"“If,” says Wakefield, “capital were divided into equal por-\n tions among all members of society, no man would have any\n interest in accumulating more capital than he can employ\n with his own hands. This is to some degree\n the case in new American colonies, where the passion\n for landed property prevents the existence of a class of wage-\n labourers” [Note: l. c. vol. I, p. 17, 18.] . So long, therefore, as the worker can\n accumulate for himself—and he can do so as long as he remains owner of his\n means of production—capitalist accumulation\n and the capitalist mode of production are impossible. The\n class of wage-labourers indispensable for them is lacking.\n How then in old Europe was the expropriation of the worker from\n his conditions of labour, and hence capital and wage-labour, pro-\n duced? By a wholly original contrat social.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0005"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0012","text":"“Human-\n ity adopted a simple method for the promotion of the accumula-\n tion of capital,” which, naturally, since Adam’s time had hovered before it as the last and\n sole purpose of its existence; “it divided itself into\n owners of capital and owners of labour … this divi-\n sion was the result of voluntary understanding and\n combination” [Note: l. c. p. 81.] . In a word: the mass of humanity\nexpropriated itself in honour of the “accumulation of capi-\n tal.” Now one might think that the instinct of this self-denying\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"749\", \"facs\": \"#f0768\"} --> fanaticism would let itself run riot particularly in colonies,\n where alone there exist men and circumstances capable of translating a contrat\n social from the dream realm into that of reality.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0005"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0013","text":"But why then systematic colonisation at all,\n as opposed to spontaneous colonisation? But, but\n “in the northern states of the American Union it is doubtful\n whether a tenth of the population belongs to the category of wage-\n labourers … In England the great mass of the people consists of wage-labourers” [Note: l. c. p. 43, 44] .\n Indeed, the drive to self-expropriation of labouring humanity in honour of\n capital exists so little that, according to Wakefield himself, slavery is the\n sole spontaneous basis of colonial wealth. His\nsystematic colonisation is a mere pis aller, since he has, after all,\n to operate with free men instead of slaves. “Without slavery\n capital in the Spanish settlements would have gone to ruin\n or at least shrunk to the small masses in which each\n individual can employ it with his own hands.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0005"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0014","text":"This\n actually took place in the last colony founded by the English, where\n a large capital in seed, cattle, and implements perished for lack\n of wage-labourers, and where no settler possesses much more capital than\n he can employ with his own hands” [Note: l. c. vol. II, p. 5] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0005"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0015","text":"We saw: the expropriation of the mass of the people from land\n constitutes the foundation of the capitalist\n mode of production. The essence of a free colony consists, on the con-\n trary, in the fact that the mass of the land is still public property and\n each settler can therefore transform a part of it into his private property and indi-\n vidual means of production, without preventing the later settler\n from performing the same operation [Note: “In order to become an element of colonisation, land must not only be uncul-\n tivated, but public property which can be transformed into private prop-\n erty.” (l. c. vol. II, p. 125.)] . This is the secret both\n of the flourishing of the colonies and of their cancerous worm—their resistance\n to the settlement of capital.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0006"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0016","text":"“Where land is very cheap\n and all men are free, where everyone can obtain a\n piece of land for himself as he wishes, labour is not only very dear,\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"750\", \"facs\": \"#f0769\"} --> as concerns the worker’s share in his product, but the difficulty is to obtain\n combined labour at any\n price” [Note: l. c. vol. I, p. 247.] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0006"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0017","text":"Since in the colonies the separation of the worker from the con-\n ditions of labour and their root, the land, does not yet exist,\n or exists only sporadically, or over too confined an area, there also does not yet\n exist the separation of agriculture from industry, nor yet the destruction of rural domestic industry,\n and whence is the internal market for capital to come? “No\n part of the population of America is exclusively agricultural,\n except slaves and their employers, who combine capital and labour for\n great enterprises. Free Americans, who cultivate the soil themselves,\n at the same time pursue many other occupations. A portion of the furniture\n and tools used by them is ordinarily made by themselves.\n They often build their own houses and carry the product\n of their own industry to markets however distant.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0007"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0018","text":"They are spinners and\n weavers; they manufacture soap and candles, shoes and clothes for their\n own use. In America agriculture often forms the subsidiary occu-\n pation of a blacksmith, miller, or shopkeeper” [Note: l. c. p. 22, 23.] . Among\n such odd fellows, where is the “field of abstinence” for the capitalist?","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0007"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0019","text":"The great beauty of capitalist production consists in the fact\n that it not only constantly reproduces the wage-labourer as wage-\n labourer, but, in proportion to the accumulation of\n capital, always produces a relative overpopulation of wage-\n labourers. Thus the law of demand for labour and\n supply is kept on the proper track, the fluctuation of wages confined within\n bounds convenient for capitalist exploitation, and finally the social dependence of the worker upon the capitalist,\n so indispensable, guaranteed: an absolute relation of dependence\n which the political economist at home, in the motherland, can\n brazenly lie into a free contractual relation between buyer and\n seller, between equally independent owners of com-\n modities, owners of the commodity capital and the commodity labour,\n as he pleases. But in the colonies the fine illusion is torn apart.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0008"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0020","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"751\", \"facs\": \"#f0770\"} --> The absolute working population here grows much more rapidly than in the mother-\n land, since many workers come into the world grown up, and yet\n the labour market is always understocked. The law of demand for labour\n and supply breaks down, like Taylor’s formula in a case of\n differential calculus. On the one hand, the old world constantly throws in capital eager for exploita-\n tion and in need of abstinence; on the other hand, the\nregular reproduction of the wage-labourer as wage-\n labourer, and thus still more the production of a surplus-\n working population in proportion to the accumulation of capital,\n encounters the most unruly and partly insurmountable obstacles. Today’s wage-\n labourer becomes tomorrow an independent, self-sufficient peasant\n or artisan.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0008"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0021","text":"He disappears from the labour market, but not into the—\n workhouse. This constant transformation of wage-labourers into\n independent producers, who work for themselves instead of for\n capital, and enrich themselves instead of Mr Capitalist, in turn reacts\n in an utterly damaging manner upon the conditions of the wage-labour market.\n Not only does the degree of exploitation of the wage-labourer remain un-\n seemly low. The latter also loses, with the relation of dependence,\n the feeling of dependence upon the abstinent capitalist. Hence all\n the inconveniences which our E. G. Wakefield describes so well, so eloquently,\n and so touchingly.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0008"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0022","text":"The supply of the wage-labour market, he complains, is neither constant,\n nor regular, nor sufficient. “It is always not only too small, but un-\n certain” [Note: l. c. v. II, p. 116.] . “Although the product to be divided between worker and capitalist is\n large, the worker takes so\n large a share that he rapidly becomes a capitalist … On the other\n hand, few, even if they live unusually long, can accumulate great masses of\n wealth” [Note: l. c. v. I, p. 130, 131.] . The workers simply do not permit the capitalist\n to renounce payment of the greater part of their labour. It does him no good\n if he is clever enough to import, along with his own capital, his own wage-\n labourers from Europe.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0009"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0023","text":"“They soon cease to be wage-labourers, they soon transform themselves into\n independent farmers or even into competitors of their old masters on the\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"752\", \"facs\": \"#f0771\"} --> wage-labour market itself” [Note: l. c. v. II, p. 5.] . Consider the horror! The worthy capi-\n talist has himself imported his own living competitors from Europe for\n his own good money! Then indeed everything comes to an end! No\n wonder Wakefield complains of the lack of a relation-\n ship and feeling of dependence among wage-labourers in the colonies.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0009"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0024","text":"“Because of high wages,” says his pupil Merivale, “there exists in\n the colonies the passionate urge for cheaper and\n more submissive labour, for a class to which the\n capitalist can dictate the conditions, instead of receiving them\n dictated by it … In old-civilised countries the worker,\n although free, is naturally dependent upon the capi-\n talist; in colonies this dependence must be created by\n artificial means” [Note: Merivale l. c. v. II, p. 235—314 passim. Even the gentle, free-\n trading vulgar economist Molinari says: “In the colonies where slavery\n has been abolished without forced labour having been replaced by an equiva-\n lent quantity of free labour, one has seen the counterpart take place of the\nfact which is realised every day before our eyes.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0009"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0025","text":"One has seen the simple\n workers in their turn exploit the industrial entrepreneurs, demand from them\n wages wholly out of proportion to the legitimate share due to them\n in the product. The planters, unable to obtain a sufficient price for their sugars\n to cover the rise in wages, have been obliged to provide the excess, first\nfrom their profits, then from their capitals themselves. A number of planters\n have thus been ruined, others have closed their workshops in order to escape imminent\n ruin … No doubt, it is better to see accumulations of\n capital perish than generations of men (how generous of Monsieur Molinari!);\n but would it not be better if neither the one nor the other perished?” (Moli-\n nari l. c. p. 51, 52.) Monsieur Molinari, Monsieur Molinari!","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0009"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0026","text":"What then becomes of the\n ten commandments, of Moses and the prophets, of the law of de-\n mand and supply, if in Europe the “entrepreneur” can curtail the worker’s, and in\n the West Indies the worker the entrepreneur’s, part légitime?\n And what, pray, is this “part légitime” which, by your own admission, the capi-\n talist in Europe does not pay daily? Monsieur Molinari is mightily itching,\n over there in the colonies, where the workers are so “simple,” to set the capitalists’\n “exploiting” of capitalists, the otherwise automatically operating law of demand and supply,\n into the proper course by police means.] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0009"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0027","text":"What now is the result of the system prevailing in the colonies\nof private property based on one’s own labour, instead of on the exploita-\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"753\", \"facs\": \"#f0772\"} --> tion of another’s labour? A\n “barbarising system of the dispersal of the producers and of\n national wealth” [Note: Wakefield l. c. v. II, p. 52.] . The dispersal of the means of production among innumer-\n able producers who own them and work with them themselves destroys, along with\n capitalist concentration, the capitalist foundation\n of all combined labour. All protracted capital undertakings\n extending over years and requiring the investment of much fixed\n capital become problematic. In Europe capital does not hesitate\n for a moment, since the working class forms its ever overflowing,\n available, living appendage. But in the colonial countries!","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0010"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0028","text":"Wake-\n field tells an exceedingly painful anecdote. He conversed\n with several capitalists from Canada and the State of New York, where moreover\n the waves of immigration often stagnate and deposit a sediment of “superflu-\n ous” workers. “Our capital,” sighs one of the per-\n sons in the melodrama, “our capital lay ready for many operations\n which require a considerable period of time for their completion; but\n could we begin such operations with labour which, as we\n knew, would soon turn its back on us? Had we been sure of being able\n to retain the labour of such immigrants, we should gladly have\n engaged them at once and at a high price. Despite the certainty\n of losing them, we should nevertheless have engaged them, had we been sure\n of a fresh supply according to our need” [Note: l. c. p. 191, 192.] .","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0010"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0029","text":"After Wakefield has splendidly contrasted English capitalist agriculture and its\n “combined” labour with the scattered American\n peasant economy, the reverse side of the medal also slips from him.\n He depicts the American mass of the people as prosperous, inde-\n pendent, enterprising, and relatively educated, while “the English agricul-\n tural labourer is a miserable wretch (a miserable wretch), a pauper … In\n what country apart from North America and some new colonies do\n the wages of free labour employed on the land exceed, to any appreciable extent,\n the worker’s most indispensable means of subsistence? … Undoubt-\n edly, farm horses in England, since they are valuable property,\n are much better fed than the English agricultural labourer” [Note: l. . v. I, p. 47, 246, 247.] . But,\n [Running matter: I.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0011"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0030","text":"48]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"754\", \"facs\": \"#f0773\"} --> never mind, national wealth is, by its very nature, iden-\n tical with popular misery.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0011"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0031","text":"How then is the anti-capitalist cancer of the colonies to be cured?\n If one wished at a stroke to transform all land from public property\n into private property, one would indeed destroy the root of the\n evil, but also — the colony. The art is to kill two birds\n with one stone.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0012"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0032","text":"By governmental action let virgin\n land be given a price independent of the law of demand and supply,\n an artificial price, which forces the immigrant\n to work for wages for a longer time before he has earned enough money\n to buy land [Note: “It is, you add, thanks to the appropriation of land and capital that\nman, who has only his arms, finds employment and obtains an income\n … it is, on the contrary, thanks to the individual appropriation of land\n that there are men who have only their arms … When you\n put a man in a void, you take possession of the atmosphere. Thus you do,\n when you take possession of the land … It is to put him in a void of wealth,\n in order to let him live only at your will.” (Colins l. c. t. III, p. 268—271\npassim.)]  and transform himself into an independent\n farmer.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0012"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0033","text":"The fund flowing from this sale of the lands\n at a price relatively prohibitive for the wage-labourer,\n that is, this money fund extorted from wages through violation of the sacred\n law of demand and supply, the government should use, on the other hand,\n in order, in the same measure as it grows, to import have-nothings\n from Europe into the colonies and thus to keep the wage-labour market full\n for Mr. Capitalist. Under these circumstances\n “tout sera pour le mieux dans le meilleur des mondes possibles.” This\n is the great secret of “systematic colonisation.”","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0012"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0034","text":"“According to this plan,” Wakefield triumphantly exclaims, “the sup-\n ply of labour must be constant and regular; for\n first, since no worker is able to procure land before he has\n worked for money, all immigrant workers, by working combinedly for\n wages, would produce capital for their employer for the employ-\n ment of more labour; secondly, everyone who hung up\n wage-labour and became a landowner would, precisely\n through the purchase of land, secure a fund for the conveyance\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"755\", \"facs\": \"#f0774\"} --> of fresh labour to the colonies” [Note: Wakefield l. c. v. II, p. 192.] .\n The land price imposed by the state must naturally be “sufficient”\n (sufficient price), i.e.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0012"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0035","text":"so high “that it prevents the workers from be-\n coming independent farmers until others are there to take their place in\n the wage-labour market” [Note: l. c. p. 45.] . This sufficient “land-\n price” is nothing but a euphemistic paraphrase for the ransom\n which the worker pays the capitalist for permission to withdraw from the\n wage-labour market onto the land. First he must produce “capital”\n for Mr. Capitalist, so that the latter may exploit more workers,\n and then provide a “substitute” on the labour market, whom the government,\n at his expense, dispatches overseas to his former Mr. Capi-\n talist.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0012"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0036","text":"It is highly characteristic that the English government carried out for\n years this method of “primitive accumulation” loudly pro-\n claimed by Mr. Wakefield especially for use in colonial countries.\n The fiasco was naturally as ignominious as that of Peel’s Bank Act. The stream\n of emigration was merely diverted from the English colonies to the United\n States. Meanwhile the progress of capitalist production in Europe, together with the increas-\n ing pressure of government, has made Wakefield’s recipe superfluous. The\n enormous and continuous stream of human beings driven to Ame-\n rica year in, year out, leaves partly stagnant deposits in the eastern United\n States; partly the wave of emigration from Europe throws people\n onto the labour market more rapidly than the wave of emigration towards the\n far West can wash them away.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0013"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0037","text":"Capitalist production there-\n fore prospers in the Eastern States, although the depth of wages and dependence of the wage-\n labourer have by no means yet fallen to the European normal level.\n The shameless squandering of uncultivated colonial land to aristocrats and capitalists by the\n English government, so loudly denounced by Wakefield himself, has particularly in Australia [Note: As soon as Australia became its own legislator, it naturally enacted laws favourable to\n settlers, but the once accomplished English squandering of land stands in the way.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0013"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0038","text":"“The first and main object at which the new] , together with\n [Running matter: 48*]\n<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"756\", \"facs\": \"#f0775\"} --> the stream of people attracted by the Diggings, and the competition\n which the import of English commodities imposes even upon the smallest artisan,\n produced a sufficient “relative overpopulation of workers,” so\n that almost every mail steamer brings the evil tidings of a “glut of the Australian\n labour-market,” and prostitution there flourishes in places as luxuriantly\n as in London’s Haymarket.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0013"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0014","text":"However, it is not the condition of the colonies that concerns us here. What\n alone interests us is the secret, discovered and loudly proclaimed in the New World\n by the political economy of the Old World, that the capitalist\n mode of production and accumulation,\n hence also capitalist private property, presupposes the destruc-\n tion of private property based on one’s own labour,\n i.e. the expropriation of the worker.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0014"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0040","text":"In conclusion we must for a moment take up again the thread\n where we dropped it in passing to the consideration of accumu-\n lation. Suppose the capitalist has advanced and consumed in the process\n of production £5000, £4000 in means of production,\n £1000 in labour-power, with a rate of exploitation\n of labour of 100%. Then the value of the product, of x ton-\n nes of iron, for example, amounts to £6000. If the capitalist sells the iron at\n its value, he realises a surplus-value of £1000,\n i.e. the unpaid labour materialised in the value of the iron. But the\n iron must be sold. The immediate result of capitalist\n production is commodity, though commodity pregnant with surplus-value.\n Thus we are hurled back to our starting-point, commodity, and with it\n to the sphere of circulation.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0015"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261016T210424490287Z_d8a3f6d4:DISPLAY-0041","text":"What we have to consider, however, in the\n following book is no longer simple commo-\n dity circulation, but the process of circulation of cap-\n ital.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0015"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0016","text":"[Note: (Land Act of 1862) the law aims, is to give increased facilities for the settlement of\n the people.” (“The Land Law of Victoria by the Hon. G. Duffy\n (Minister of Public Lands). Lond. 1862.”]","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0016"]},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0017","text":"<!-- CARE_TEI_PAGE {\"n\": \"757\", \"facs\": \"#f0776\"} -->","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_V4GB_3DMK:DISPLAY-0017"]}],"source_digest":"b4778e0be68f46aaf121c916a25c5e51d9c032bb8170140df6a841dd12c92067","source_language_code":"de"}]}],"units":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0","layer":"CH","title":"1) Die Waare.","order":1,"parents":["RAW_V4GB_1DW0"],"revision":"CH_V4GB_1DW0@R001","sha256":"d574b676c1a60857fd9028f2ea53f82548aeb8a386e88905a6898dd29a821c66","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS1","text":"Zu erklären ist, wie die elementare Form kapitalistischen Reichtums zugleich nützlicher Gegenstand und gesellschaftlicher Wert sein kann.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS2","text":"Die Ware ist eine widersprüchliche Einheit, deren Wert weder aus ihrer Nützlichkeit noch aus ihrer stofflichen Gestalt hervorgeht.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS3","text":"Das Argument verbindet begriffliche Rekonstruktion, Formanalyse, historische Spezifizierung und Fetischismusdiagnose.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0158","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS4","text":"Die Bewegung führt vom wechselnden Austauschverhältnis über die Abstraktion vom Gebrauchswert zur Wertsubstanz, Wertgröße und gesellschaftlich notwendigen Arbeitszeit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS5","text":"Der Doppelcharakter der Ware wird durch den Doppelcharakter derselben Arbeit als konkrete nützliche und abstrakte menschliche Arbeit begründet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS6","text":"Die Wertform entwickelt sich aus der einfachen Warenrelation über ihre Entfaltung und Umkehrung zur Polarität von allgemeiner relativer Wertform und allgemeiner Äquivalentform.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0148","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS7","text":"Die Vermittlung unabhängiger Privatarbeiten durch Warenwerte erzeugt den objektiven Schein gesellschaftlicher Eigenschaften der Dinge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS8","text":"Der unaufgelöste Widerspruch von Gebrauchswert und Wert erzwingt den Übergang vom statischen Warenbegriff zum Austauschprozess.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A1","text":"Der Reichtum kapitalistisch produzierender Gesellschaften erscheint als Warenansammlung und die einzelne Ware als seine Elementarform.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A2","text":"Gebrauchswert ist die im Gebrauch verwirklichte Nützlichkeit eines Gegenstands und bildet den stofflichen Inhalt des Reichtums unabhängig von dessen gesellschaftlicher Form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A3","text":"Die Ware ist die unmittelbare Einheit von Gebrauchswert und Wert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A4","text":"Austauschgleichungen abstrahieren von den Gebrauchswerten und setzen einen von ihren wechselnden Ausdrucksweisen unterscheidbaren gemeinsamen gesellschaftlichen Gehalt voraus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A5","text":"Die gemeinsame gesellschaftliche Substanz der Warenwerte ist vergegenständlichte abstrakte menschliche Arbeit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0124","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","text":"Die Wertgröße einer Ware wird durch die zu ihrer Herstellung gesellschaftlich notwendige Arbeitszeit bestimmt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A7","text":"Die Wertgröße variiert direkt mit der gesellschaftlich notwendigen Arbeitszeit und umgekehrt mit der Produktivkraft der Arbeit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A8","text":"Dieselbe Arbeit gilt konkret und qualitativ als Bildnerin von Gebrauchswerten sowie abstrakt und quantitativ als Substanz des Werts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A9","text":"Gebrauchswerte sind Verbindungen von Naturstoff und konkreter Arbeit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A10","text":"Warenproduktion setzt gesellschaftliche Arbeitsteilung in voneinander unabhängige Privatarbeiten voraus, deren Produkte Gebrauchswerte für andere sind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A11","text":"Unabhängige Privatarbeiten erhalten ihre spezifisch gesellschaftliche Geltung durch die Gleichsetzung ihrer Produkte als Werte.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A12","text":"Wert besitzt keine eigene sinnliche Gestalt und kann nur in der Naturalform einer anderen Ware als Tauschwert erscheinen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A13","text":"Jeder Wertausdruck verteilt relative Wertform und Äquivalentform polarisch auf verschiedene Waren.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A14","text":"In der Äquivalentform fungieren die Naturalform einer Ware und die in ihr enthaltene konkrete Arbeit als unmittelbare Erscheinungsformen von Wert und abstrakter Arbeit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A15","text":"Die Vervielfachung und Umkehrung einfacher Wertausdrücke entwickelt die allgemeine relative Wertform.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0118","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A16","text":"Allgemeine relative Wertform und allgemeine Äquivalentform sind wechselseitig vorausgesetzte und einander ausschließende Pole derselben gesellschaftlichen Form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A17","text":"Die Warenform lässt gesellschaftliche Beziehungen der Arbeiten als Eigenschaften und Bewegungen von Sachen erscheinen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A18","text":"Die Kategorien der Warenökonomie sind objektive Gedankenformen einer historisch bestimmten Produktionsweise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A19","text":"Die unmittelbare Einheit von Gebrauchswert und Wert ist ein Widerspruch, der sich erst in der wirklichen Beziehung der Waren entwickelt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX1","text":"Die Herkunft, Art und unmittelbare oder mittelbare Befriedigung eines Bedürfnisses bestimmt nicht den Warencharakter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX2","text":"Nützlichkeit allein erzeugt keinen Wert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX3","text":"Ein Arbeitsprodukt für den Eigenbedarf ist keine Ware.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX4","text":"Nutzlose Arbeit zählt nicht als wertbildende Arbeit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX5","text":"Die individuell tatsächlich verausgabte Arbeitszeit bestimmt den Wert nicht.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX6","text":"Komplizierte Arbeit bildet keine von einfacher menschlicher Arbeit verschiedene Wertsubstanz.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX7","text":"Arbeit ist nicht die einzige Quelle stofflichen Reichtums.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX8","text":"Gesellschaftliche Arbeitsteilung ist keine hinreichende Bedingung der Warenproduktion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX9","text":"Der Austausch erzeugt die Wertgröße nicht.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX10","text":"Tauschwert ist keine im isolierten Warenkörper enthaltene Natureigenschaft.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX11","text":"Äquivalentsein ist keine der Ware unabhängig von ihrer Relation zukommende dingliche Eigenschaft.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX12","text":"Relative Wertform und Äquivalentform können derselben Ware nicht innerhalb desselben Wertausdrucks zugleich zukommen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX13","text":"Allgemeine unmittelbare Austauschbarkeit kann nicht allen Waren gleichzeitig zukommen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0137","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX14","text":"Der mystische Charakter der Ware entspringt weder ihrem Gebrauchswert noch abstrakter Arbeit oder Arbeitszeit für sich genommen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0166","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX15","text":"Waren-, Wert-, Geld- und Kapitalform sind keine ewigen Naturformen gesellschaftlicher Produktion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D1","text":"Qualitativ verschiedene Waren sind nur vergleichbar, nachdem ihre konkreten Unterschiede auf gleiche abstrakte menschliche Arbeit reduziert worden sind.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A4","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D2","text":"Waren mit gleicher gesellschaftlich notwendiger Arbeitszeit besitzen gleiche Wertgröße; ihre Werte verhalten sich wie ihre notwendigen Arbeitszeiten.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A5","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D3","text":"Komplizierte Arbeit zählt als gesellschaftlich festgesetztes Vielfaches einfacher Arbeit.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A5","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0047","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D4","text":"Gleiche Dauer abstrakter Arbeit stellt dieselbe Wertgröße, bei wechselnder Produktivität jedoch verschiedene Mengen von Gebrauchswerten dar.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A7","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D5","text":"Eine wachsende Masse stofflichen Reichtums kann mit sinkender Wertgröße derselben Gesamtmasse zusammenfallen.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A2","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A7","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D6","text":"Natürliche nützliche Dinge können Gebrauchswert ohne Wert besitzen; nützliche Arbeitsprodukte können Wertvoraussetzungen erfüllen, ohne Waren zu sein.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A2","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A3","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D7","text":"Qualitative Verschiedenheit nützlicher Arbeiten ermöglicht den Warenaustausch, während erst ihre private Selbständigkeit ihn gesellschaftlich notwendig macht.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A8","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A10","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0142","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D8","text":"Derselbe Wechsel des relativen Werts kann aus einer Veränderung des eigenen Werts oder aus einer entgegengesetzten Veränderung des Äquivalentwerts entstehen.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A7","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A12","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D9","text":"Ein konstantes relatives Wertverhältnis kann gleichgerichtete und proportionale Veränderungen beider wirklichen Wertgrößen verdecken.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A12","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D10","text":"Die Äquivalentform enthält keine quantitative Wertbestimmung; das Austauschquantum folgt aus den bereits bestimmten Wertgrößen.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A13","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D11","text":"In der einfachen Wertform wird der Gebrauchswert der Äquivalentware zur Erscheinungsform seines Gegenteils, des Werts, und ihre konkrete Arbeit zur Erscheinungsform abstrakter Arbeit.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A12","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A13","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D12","text":"Die entfaltete Wertform hebt den Schein auf, das Austauschverhältnis zweier Waren erzeuge deren Wertgleichheit.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A4","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A5","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D13","text":"Der einheitliche Ausdruck aller Warenwerte in einer Ware macht sie qualitativ gleichsetzbar und quantitativ vergleichbar.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A15","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D14","text":"Beansprucht jede Ware ihre eigene Naturalform als allgemeines Äquivalent, schließen sich alle Waren gegenseitig von einer gesellschaftlich gültigen Wertdarstellung aus.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0157","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D15","text":"Die unmittelbare gesellschaftliche Form des allgemeinen Äquivalents setzt die nur vermittelte gesellschaftliche Form aller übrigen Waren voraus.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A11","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A14","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0135","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D16","text":"Gesellschaftlich notwendige Arbeitszeit setzt sich gegenüber unabhängigen Produzenten nachträglich als scheinbares Naturgesetz der Warenbewegung durch.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A11","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0177","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D17","text":"Bewusst vergesellschaftete Produktion kann Arbeitszeit zur Planung und Verteilung verwenden, ohne Produkte als Warenwerte aufeinander zu beziehen.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A10","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0171","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0172","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D18","text":"Die einfache Wertform enthält bereits die relationale und polare Grundstruktur der Geldform.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A12","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A13","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A15","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD1","text":"Wert besitzt relationale Objektivität: Er ist weder natürliche Eigenschaft des Dings noch willkürliche Konvention der Austauschenden.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A4","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A5","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A12","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD2","text":"Abstrakte Arbeit ist eine praktisch vollzogene gesellschaftliche Gleichsetzung, bevor sie als theoretische Abstraktion erkannt wird.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A5","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A11","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD3","text":"Gesellschaftliche Allgemeinheit entsteht in der Warenform nicht durch gleichmäßige Teilhabe, sondern durch polare Ausschließung.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A13","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0133","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD4","text":"Die Warenökonomie besitzt zwei gegenläufige Reichtumsmetriken: stoffliche Fülle und gesellschaftliche Wertgröße.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A2","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A7","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A8","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD5","text":"Die Wertformentwicklung erzeugt die strukturelle Stelle des Geldes, bestimmt aber noch nicht, welche Ware diese Stelle dauerhaft monopolisiert.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A15","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0148","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD6","text":"Die Herrschaft der Wertgesetzlichkeit ist unpersönlich, weil die eigene gesellschaftliche Bewegung der Produzenten ihnen als Bewegung selbständiger Sachen gegenübertritt.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A11","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0177","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0178","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD7","text":"Kritik der politischen Ökonomie muss die Genesis gesellschaftlicher Formen erklären und kann nicht bei der quantitativen Messung gegebener Werte stehenbleiben.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A12","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0158","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0189","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD8","text":"Die Aufhebung des Fetischismus verlangt eine Veränderung der materiellen Produktionsvermittlung und nicht nur eine Korrektur des Bewusstseins.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A10","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A17","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0185","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD9","text":"Die Ware kann ihren inneren Gegensatz nicht isoliert lösen; sie muss ihn in eine gesellschaftliche Bewegungsform überführen.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A3","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A19"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX1","text":"Die Gesellschaftlichkeit unabhängiger Privatarbeit kann nicht unmittelbar in ihrer konkreten Tätigkeit liegen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0144","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX2","text":"Wertabstraktion kann nicht auf einer bloßen gedanklichen Verallgemeinerung der Produzenten beruhen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0176","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX3","text":"Die gesellschaftliche Form eines Arbeitsprodukts kann nicht aus seiner physischen Beschaffenheit abgelesen werden.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX4","text":"Gesellschaftliche Objektivität impliziert keine transhistorische Natürlichkeit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX5","text":"Allgemeine Äquivalentgeltung kann nicht als symmetrisch verteilte Eigenschaft aller Waren bestehen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0137","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX6","text":"Die nachträgliche Koordination unabhängiger Privatarbeiten kann nicht zugleich bewusste gesellschaftliche Planung sein.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0172","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0177","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX7","text":"Fetischismus kann nicht als bloßer Erkenntnisfehler innerhalb unveränderter Produktionsverhältnisse bestimmt werden.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0185","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX8","text":"Eine Theorie der Wertgröße ohne Ableitung der Wertform kann den gesellschaftlichen Charakter des Werts nicht erfassen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0189","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX9","text":"Konkrete und abstrakte Arbeit können nicht als zwei voneinander unabhängige Arbeiten behandelt werden.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SNX1","text":"Die Warenform ist um die Abwesenheit einer unmittelbaren gesellschaftlichen Beziehung der Produzenten in ihren Arbeiten organisiert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0181","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SNX2","text":"Der Ware ist die Möglichkeit versperrt, ihren Wert in ihrem eigenen Körper darzustellen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SNX3","text":"Eine Welt, in der alle Waren unmittelbar allgemein austauschbar sind, ist innerhalb der Wertform strukturell ausgeschlossen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0138","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0139","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SNX4","text":"Bewusste kollektive Kontrolle des gesellschaftlichen Produktionszusammenhangs ist innerhalb der Herrschaft unabhängiger Privatarbeiten nicht verfügbar.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0177","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0185","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SNX5","text":"Eine innere Versöhnung von Gebrauchswert und Wert innerhalb der isolierten Ware bleibt unmöglich; der Widerspruch wird an den Austauschprozess weitergegeben.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS1","text":"Kapitalistischer Reichtum → Warenansammlung → einzelne Ware als elementare Analyseeinheit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS2","text":"Ware = Gebrauchswert und Wert → Austauschvergleich → Abstraktion vom Gebrauchswert → abstrakte menschliche Arbeit als Wertsubstanz.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS3","text":"Wertsubstanz → gesellschaftlich notwendige Arbeitszeit als Maß → Produktivkraftänderung → inverse Änderung des Stückwerts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS4","text":"Dieselbe Arbeit → konkrete qualitative Bestimmung für Gebrauchswert / abstrakte quantitative Bestimmung für Wert → mögliche Gegenbewegung von stofflichem Reichtum und Wert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS5","text":"Unabhängige Privatarbeiten → Austausch ihrer Produkte → nachträgliche Gleichsetzung als abstrakte Arbeit → gesellschaftliche Geltung.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS6","text":"Fehlende Eigenform des Werts → einfache Warenrelation → relative Wertform auf der einen und Äquivalentform auf der anderen Seite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS7","text":"Wiederholung einfacher Wertausdrücke → entfaltete Wertform → Umkehrung → allgemeine relative Wertform → exklusives allgemeines Äquivalent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0118","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0133","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS8","text":"Vermittelte Gesellschaftlichkeit → sachliche Erscheinungsform → Verselbständigung der Warenbewegung → Fetischismus → historische Naturalisierung ökonomischer Kategorien.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0178","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS9","text":"Unmittelbare Einheit von Gebrauchswert und Wert → innerer Widerspruch → wirkliche Warenbeziehung → Austauschprozess.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AF1","text":"Erscheinung kapitalistischen Reichtums → Analyse der Ware → Spaltung in Gebrauchswert und Wert → Abstraktion auf Arbeit → Bestimmung von Substanz und Größe → Doppelcharakter der Arbeit → Ableitung der Wertformen → allgemeines Äquivalent → Fetischismusdiagnose → historische Begrenzung → Übergang zum Austauschprozess.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0148","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME1","text":"Der Warenkörper wird in transhistorischen Gebrauchswert und historisch bestimmte gesellschaftliche Wertform aufgespalten.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME2","text":"Reichtum wird in stoffliche Masse und Wertgröße als voneinander unabhängige, potentiell gegenläufige Dimensionen gegliedert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME3","text":"Arbeit wird nicht nach zwei Tätigkeiten, sondern nach zwei gesellschaftlich verschiedenen Geltungsweisen derselben Tätigkeit kartiert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME4","text":"Der Gegensatz zwischen intrinsischem Wert und bloß zufälliger Austauschrelation wird durch das Modell gesellschaftlich-relationaler Objektivität ersetzt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME5","text":"Produktivität wird aus der Wertsubstanz ausgeschlossen und als Vermittlung zwischen Arbeitszeit, Gebrauchswertmenge und Stückwert neu eingeordnet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME6","text":"Das Äquivalent wird von einer dinglichen Eigenschaft zu einer polar zugewiesenen Relationsfunktion umklassifiziert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME7","text":"Allgemeinheit wird als Ergebnis von Ausschließung sichtbar: Eine Ware ist unmittelbar austauschbar, weil die übrigen es nicht sind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0137","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME8","text":"Gesellschaftliche Beziehungen zwischen Produzenten werden als sachliche Beziehungen ihrer Produkte und damit als strukturell verkehrte Erscheinungsformen lesbar.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0181","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME9","text":"Ökonomische Kategorien werden zugleich als objektiv gültig und historisch begrenzt klassifiziert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME10","text":"Bewusst assoziierte Produktion wird als alternative Koordinationsform sichtbar, in der Arbeitszeit fortbesteht, ohne Wertform anzunehmen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0171","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0172","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ALT1","text":"Surface — Die Ware wird nach Gebrauchswert, Wertsubstanz, Wertgröße und Wertform bestimmt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ALT2","text":"Structural — Gesellschaftliche Arbeitsteilung, unabhängige Privatarbeit und Austausch vermitteln die gesellschaftliche Geltung der Arbeiten nachträglich.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Die Untersuchung verschiebt politische Ökonomie von gegebenen Austauschquantitäten zur Genesis ihrer Formen und bereitet aus der Warenform die Geldanalyse vor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0158","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Gesellschaftliche Realität besitzt relationale Gegenständlichkeit; ihre Abstraktionen werden praktisch vollzogen, historisch erzeugt und können erst unter veränderten materiellen Bedingungen bewusst kontrolliert werden.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0185","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT1","text":"Das Axiomensystem reicht aus, um die Bewegung von der elementaren Warenform bis zum Austauschprozess zu rekonstruieren.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0158","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT2","text":"Die stärkste interne Kohärenz liegt in der Korrespondenz von Warenzwiespalt, Doppelcharakter der Arbeit, Produktivitätswirkung und polarer Wertform.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT3","text":"Die Reduktion komplizierter auf einfache Arbeit wird als realer gesellschaftlicher Prozess vorausgesetzt, aber nicht durch einen bestimmten Mechanismus erklärt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT4","text":"Abstrakte Arbeit bleibt zwischen physiologischer Verausgabung menschlicher Arbeitskraft und spezifisch gesellschaftlicher Geltungsform der Privatarbeiten doppeldeutig bestimmt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0044","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0144","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT5","text":"Der Schluss von der Austauschgleichung auf eine gemeinsame Wertsubstanz wird über die Abstraktion vom Gebrauchswert komprimiert und nicht durch eine eigenständige Theorie gesellschaftlicher Gleichsetzung vermittelt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT6","text":"Die Formanalyse erzeugt die Notwendigkeit eines exklusiven allgemeinen Äquivalents, lässt aber dessen gesellschaftliche Fixierung in einer bestimmten Ware offen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0148","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0157","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT7","text":"Die begriffliche Ableitung der Wertform aus dem Wertbegriff und ihre historische Entstehung werden parallel gesetzt, ohne vollständig ineinander vermittelt zu werden.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0159","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT8","text":"Die materiellen Voraussetzungen bewusst vergesellschafteter Produktion werden als notwendig markiert, aber nur als Ergebnis einer langen Entwicklung bestimmt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0185","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT9","text":"Die Rekonstruktion bewahrt die tatsächliche Bewegung vom abstrakten Warenbegriff über seine Formgenese und Fetischisierung zur notwendigen Entwicklung seines Widerspruchs im Austausch.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0158","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261016T210552251153Z_b713429d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"1) The Commodity.","source_sha256":"d574b676c1a60857fd9028f2ea53f82548aeb8a386e88905a6898dd29a821c66","sha256":"54a57d6295865677cc3e6a8648a162343a4311593c9a45936df9ac3779afe516","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T21:05:52.252569+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS1","text":"What must be explained is how the elementary form of capitalist wealth can be both a useful object and social value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS2","text":"The commodity is a contradictory unity whose value derives neither from its usefulness nor from its material form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS3","text":"The argument combines conceptual reconstruction, form analysis, historical specification, and diagnosis of fetishism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0158","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS4","text":"The movement proceeds from the changing exchange relation, through abstraction from use-value, to value substance, magnitude of value, and socially necessary labour-time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS5","text":"The dual character of the commodity is grounded in the dual character of the same labour as concrete useful and abstract human labour.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS6","text":"The value-form develops from the simple commodity relation, through its expansion and inversion, to the polarity of general relative value-form and general equivalent-form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0148","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS7","text":"The mediation of independent private labours through commodity values produces the objective appearance of social properties of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AS8","text":"The unresolved contradiction of use-value and value compels the transition from the static concept of the commodity to the exchange process.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A1","text":"The wealth of capitalist-producing societies appears as an accumulation of commodities, and the individual commodity as its elementary form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A2","text":"Use-value is the usefulness of an object realized in use and forms the material content of wealth independently of its social form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A3","text":"The commodity is the immediate unity of use-value and value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A4","text":"Equations of exchange abstract from use-values and presuppose a common social content distinguishable from their changing modes of expression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A5","text":"The common social substance of commodity values is objectified abstract human labour.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0124","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","text":"The magnitude of value of a commodity is determined by the labour-time socially necessary for its production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A7","text":"The magnitude of value varies directly with socially necessary labour-time and inversely with the productive power of labour.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A8","text":"The same labour counts concretely and qualitatively as the creator of use-values, and abstractly and quantitatively as the substance of value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A9","text":"Use-values are combinations of natural material and concrete labour.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A10","text":"Commodity production presupposes a social division of labour into mutually independent private labours, whose products are use-values for others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A11","text":"Independent private labours receive their specifically social validity through the equation of their products as values.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A12","text":"Value possesses no sensory form of its own and can appear as exchange-value only in the natural form of another commodity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A13","text":"Every expression of value distributes relative value-form and equivalent-form polarly across different commodities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A14","text":"In the equivalent-form, the natural form of a commodity and the concrete labour contained in it function as immediate forms of appearance of value and abstract labour.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A15","text":"The multiplication and inversion of simple expressions of value develops the general relative value-form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0118","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A16","text":"The general relative value-form and the general equivalent-form are mutually presupposed and mutually exclusive poles of the same social form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A17","text":"The commodity-form makes social relations of labours appear as properties and movements of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A18","text":"The categories of commodity economy are objective forms of thought of a historically determinate mode of production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:A19","text":"The immediate unity of use-value and value is a contradiction that develops only in the actual relation of commodities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX1","text":"The origin, kind, and immediate or mediated satisfaction of a need do not determine commodity character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX2","text":"Usefulness alone does not create value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX3","text":"A product of labour for one's own needs is not a commodity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX4","text":"Useless labour does not count as value-creating labour.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX5","text":"The labour-time actually expended individually does not determine value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX6","text":"Complex labour does not form a value substance distinct from simple human labour.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX7","text":"Labour is not the sole source of material wealth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX8","text":"The social division of labour is not a sufficient condition of commodity production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX9","text":"Exchange does not create the magnitude of value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX10","text":"Exchange-value is not a natural property contained in the isolated body of the commodity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX11","text":"Being an equivalent is not a material property accruing to the commodity independently of its relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX12","text":"Relative value-form and equivalent-form cannot accrue simultaneously to the same commodity within the same expression of value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX13","text":"General immediate exchangeability cannot accrue to all commodities simultaneously.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0137","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX14","text":"The mystical character of the commodity arises neither from its use-value nor from abstract labour or labour-time taken by themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0166","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:NX15","text":"Commodity-, value-, money-, and capital-form are not eternal natural forms of social production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D1","text":"Qualitatively different commodities are comparable only after their concrete differences have been reduced to equal abstract human labour.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A4","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D2","text":"Commodities with equal socially necessary labour-time possess equal magnitudes of value; their values are proportional to their necessary labour-times.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A5","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D3","text":"Complex labour counts as a socially established multiple of simple labour.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A5","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0047","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D4","text":"The same duration of abstract labour represents the same magnitude of value, but, with changing productivity, different quantities of use-values.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A7","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D5","text":"A growing mass of material wealth can coincide with a declining magnitude of value of that same total mass.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A2","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A7","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D6","text":"Natural useful things can possess use-value without value; useful products of labour can fulfil the preconditions of value without being commodities.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A2","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A3","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D7","text":"The qualitative difference of useful labours enables commodity exchange, whereas only their private independence makes it socially necessary.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A8","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A10","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0142","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D8","text":"The same change in relative value can arise from a change in its own value or from an opposite change in the value of the equivalent.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A7","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A12","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D9","text":"A constant relative value relation can conceal co-directional and proportional changes in both actual magnitudes of value.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A12","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D10","text":"The equivalent-form contains no quantitative determination of value; the quantity exchanged follows from the already determined magnitudes of value.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A13","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D11","text":"In the simple value-form, the use-value of the equivalent commodity becomes the form of appearance of its opposite, value, and its concrete labour the form of appearance of abstract labour.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A12","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A13","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D12","text":"The expanded value-form abolishes the appearance that the exchange relation of two commodities produces their equality in value.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A4","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A5","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D13","text":"The unified expression of all commodity values in one commodity makes them qualitatively equatable and quantitatively comparable.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A15","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D14","text":"If every commodity claims its own natural form as the general equivalent, all commodities mutually exclude one another from a socially valid representation of value.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0157","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D15","text":"The immediate social form of the general equivalent presupposes the merely mediated social form of all other commodities.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A11","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A14","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0135","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D16","text":"Socially necessary labour-time asserts itself against independent producers retrospectively as an apparent natural law of commodity movement.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A11","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0177","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D17","text":"Consciously socialized production can use labour-time for planning and distribution without relating products to one another as commodity values.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A10","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0171","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0172","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:D18","text":"The simple value-form already contains the relational and polar basic structure of the money-form.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A12","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A13","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A15","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD1","text":"Value possesses relational objectivity: it is neither a natural property of the thing nor an arbitrary convention of those who exchange.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A4","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A5","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A12","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD2","text":"Abstract labour is a socially enacted practical equation before it is recognized as a theoretical abstraction.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A5","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A11","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD3","text":"In the commodity-form, social universality arises not through equal participation but through polar exclusion.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A13","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0133","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD4","text":"Commodity economy possesses two opposing metrics of wealth: material abundance and social magnitude of value.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A2","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A7","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A8","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD5","text":"The development of the value-form produces the structural place of money, but does not yet determine which commodity permanently monopolizes that place.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A15","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0148","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD6","text":"The domination of the law of value is impersonal because the producers' own social movement confronts them as the movement of independent things.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A11","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0177","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0178","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD7","text":"Critique of political economy must explain the genesis of social forms and cannot stop at the quantitative measurement of given values.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A6","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A12","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0158","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0189","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD8","text":"The abolition of fetishism requires a transformation of material production mediation and not merely a correction of consciousness.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A10","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A17","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0185","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SD9","text":"The commodity cannot resolve its inner opposition in isolation; it must transfer it into a social form of movement.","refs":["CH_V4GB_1DW0:A3","CH_V4GB_1DW0:A19"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX1","text":"The sociality of independent private labour cannot lie immediately in its concrete activity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0144","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX2","text":"Value abstraction cannot rest on a merely mental generalization by producers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0176","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX3","text":"The social form of a product of labour cannot be read off from its physical constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX4","text":"Social objectivity does not imply transhistorical naturalness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX5","text":"General equivalent validity cannot exist as a symmetrically distributed property of all commodities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0137","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX6","text":"The retrospective coordination of independent private labours cannot at the same time be conscious social planning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0172","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0177","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX7","text":"Fetishism cannot be determined as a mere error of cognition within unchanged relations of production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0185","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX8","text":"A theory of the magnitude of value without a derivation of the value-form cannot grasp the social character of value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0189","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:DNX9","text":"Concrete and abstract labour cannot be treated as two mutually independent labours.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SNX1","text":"The commodity-form is organized around the absence of an immediate social relation of producers in their labours.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0181","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SNX2","text":"The commodity is barred from the possibility of representing its value in its own body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SNX3","text":"A world in which all commodities are immediately generally exchangeable is structurally excluded within the value-form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0138","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0139","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SNX4","text":"Conscious collective control of the social nexus of production is unavailable within the domination of independent private labours.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0177","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0185","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:SNX5","text":"An inner reconciliation of use-value and value within the isolated commodity remains impossible; the contradiction is passed on to the exchange process.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS1","text":"Capitalist wealth → accumulation of commodities → individual commodity as elementary unit of analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS2","text":"Commodity = use-value and value → exchange comparison → abstraction from use-value → abstract human labour as value substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS3","text":"Value substance → socially necessary labour-time as measure → change in productive power → inverse change in unit value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS4","text":"Same labour → concrete qualitative determination for use-value / abstract quantitative determination for value → possible counter-movement of material wealth and value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS5","text":"Independent private labours → exchange of their products → retrospective equation as abstract labour → social validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS6","text":"Lack of a form of value of its own → simple commodity relation → relative value-form on one side and equivalent-form on the other.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS7","text":"Repetition of simple expressions of value → expanded value-form → inversion → general relative value-form → exclusive general equivalent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0118","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0133","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS8","text":"Mediated sociality → material form of appearance → autonomization of commodity movement → fetishism → historical naturalization of economic categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0178","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:FGS9","text":"Immediate unity of use-value and value → inner contradiction → actual commodity relation → exchange process.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:AF1","text":"Appearance of capitalist wealth → analysis of the commodity → division into use-value and value → abstraction to labour → determination of substance and magnitude → dual character of labour → derivation of the value-forms → general equivalent → diagnosis of fetishism → historical limitation → transition to the exchange process.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0148","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME1","text":"The body of the commodity is divided into transhistorical use-value and historically determinate social value-form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME2","text":"Wealth is divided into material mass and magnitude of value as independent, potentially opposing dimensions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME3","text":"Labour is mapped not according to two activities, but according to two socially distinct modes of validity of the same activity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME4","text":"The opposition between intrinsic value and a merely contingent exchange relation is replaced by the model of socially relational objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME5","text":"Productivity is excluded from value substance and reclassified as mediation between labour-time, quantity of use-values, and unit value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME6","text":"The equivalent is reclassified from a material property into a relational function assigned polarly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME7","text":"Universality becomes visible as the result of exclusion: one commodity is immediately exchangeable because the others are not.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0137","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME8","text":"Social relations between producers become legible as material relations of their products and thus as structurally inverted forms of appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0181","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME9","text":"Economic categories are classified as both objectively valid and historically limited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ME10","text":"Consciously associated production becomes visible as an alternative form of coordination in which labour-time persists without assuming value-form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0171","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0172","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ALT1","text":"Surface — The commodity is determined according to use-value, value substance, magnitude of value, and value-form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ALT2","text":"Structural — The social division of labour, independent private labour, and exchange mediate the social validity of labours retrospectively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The investigation shifts political economy from given quantities exchanged to the genesis of their forms and prepares the analysis of money from the commodity-form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0158","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Social reality possesses relational objectivity; its abstractions are practically enacted, historically produced, and can be consciously controlled only under changed material conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0175","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0180","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0185","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT1","text":"The system of axioms suffices to reconstruct the movement from the elementary commodity-form to the exchange process.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0158","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT2","text":"The strongest internal coherence lies in the correspondence of the commodity's split character, the dual character of labour, the effect of productivity, and the polar value-form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT3","text":"The reduction of complex to simple labour is presupposed as a real social process, but is not explained by a determinate mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT4","text":"Abstract labour remains ambiguously determined between the physiological expenditure of human labour-power and the specifically social form of validity of private labours.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0044","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0144","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT5","text":"The inference from the equation of exchange to a common value substance is compressed through abstraction from use-value and is not mediated by an independent theory of social equation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0143","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT6","text":"The form analysis produces the necessity of an exclusive general equivalent, but leaves open its social fixation in a particular commodity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0148","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0157","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT7","text":"The conceptual derivation of the value-form from the concept of value and its historical emergence are set in parallel without being fully mediated into one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0159","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0162","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT8","text":"The material preconditions of consciously socialized production are marked as necessary, but determined only as the result of a long development.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0185","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_1DW0:MRT9","text":"The reconstruction preserves the actual movement from the abstract concept of the commodity, through its genesis of form and fetishization, to the necessary development of its contradiction in exchange.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0158","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0182","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_1DW0:P0200","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"revision":"CH_V4GB_1DW0@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_V4GB_1DW0:v1","source_language_code":"de"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 01","title":"1) Die Waare.","context":"Chapter 01"}},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD","layer":"CH","title":"2) Der Austauschprozess der Waaren.","order":2,"parents":["RAW_V4GB_2DAD"],"revision":"CH_V4GB_2DAD@R001","sha256":"5b223c6b5b690af29dbfb1da428168a3988de67d2b66373f4d21f72e70e3cc6e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AS1","text":"Der Text rekonstruiert die Geldform aus den immanenten Bedingungen des Warenaustauschs und diagnostiziert ihre Verdinglichung als Fetischform.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AS2","text":"Der Austausch äußerer Dinge setzt Warenhüter voraus, die sich als voneinander unabhängige Privateigentümer anerkennen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AS3","text":"Das Rechts- und Vertragsverhältnis erscheint als Willensform, deren Inhalt durch das ökonomische Verhältnis bestimmt ist.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AS4","text":"Die Ware muss zugleich für ihren Besitzer Nicht-Gebrauchswert, für andere Gebrauchswert und gesellschaftlich realisierter Wert sein.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AS5","text":"Der Konflikt zwischen individuellem Tauschinteresse und allgemeiner Wertrealisierung erzeugt die Notwendigkeit eines gesellschaftlich gesetzten allgemeinen Äquivalents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AS6","text":"Die Kristallisation des allgemeinen Äquivalents zu Geld löst die Austauschform operativ, verdeckt jedoch ihre gesellschaftliche Vermittlung und erzeugt den Geldfetisch.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A1","text":"Waren können sich nicht selbst austauschen; ihre Veräußerung und Aneignung verlangt handelnde Warenbesitzer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A2","text":"Austausch setzt die wechselseitige Anerkennung der Warenbesitzer als Privateigentümer voraus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A3","text":"Das Rechts- oder Willensverhältnis des Vertrags spiegelt ein ökonomisches Verhältnis, dessen Inhalt ihm vorausliegt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A4","text":"Personen treten im Austausch nur als Träger und Personifikationen ökonomischer Verhältnisse auf.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A5","text":"Die Ware ist für ihren Besitzer Nicht-Gebrauchswert und für ihren Nicht-Besitzer Gebrauchswert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A6","text":"Die Realisierung der Ware als Wert und ihre Bewährung als Gebrauchswert für andere sind wechselseitig notwendige Bedingungen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A7","text":"Der Austausch ist für den einzelnen Warenbesitzer bedürfnisbezogener Einzelprozess und zugleich gesellschaftlicher Prozess der Wertrealisierung.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A8","text":"Wenn jede Ware sich als allgemeines Äquivalent aller anderen Waren setzt, existiert kein allgemeines Äquivalent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A9","text":"Nur die gesellschaftliche Ausschließung einer bestimmten Ware verleiht ihrer Naturalform die allgemeine Äquivalentform und macht sie zu Geld.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A10","text":"Der Widerspruch der Ware als Einheit von Gebrauchswert und Tauschwert sowie von privater nützlicher und gesellschaftlich abstrakter Arbeit treibt notwendig ihre Verdopplung in Ware und Geld hervor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A11","text":"Warenaustausch beginnt an den Grenzen fremder Gemeinwesen und verwandelt durch Wiederholung auch Produkte des inneren Gemeinlebens in Waren.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A12","text":"Mit wachsender Zahl und Mannigfaltigkeit der Austauschakte wird eine vom einzelnen Gebrauchswert unabhängige Wertform notwendig und das allgemeine Äquivalent an bestimmte Waren fixiert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A13","text":"Die Geldform haftet zunächst kontingent an bestimmten Warenarten und bindet sich mit der Ausweitung des Austauschs an Waren, die die allgemeine Äquivalentfunktion tragen können.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A14","text":"Eine Geldware muss gleichartige Qualität besitzen sowie quantitativ teilbar und wieder zusammensetzbar sein.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A15","text":"Der Austauschprozess gibt der Geldware nicht ihren Wert, sondern ihre spezifische Wertform; ihr Wert bleibt durch die zu ihrer Produktion erheischte Arbeitszeit bestimmt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A16","text":"Die vollendete Geldform löscht die Spur ihrer gesellschaftlichen Vermittlung aus und lässt eine soziale Form als Natureigenschaft eines Dings erscheinen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:NX1","text":"Das Vertragsverhältnis ist nicht die ursprüngliche Quelle seines ökonomischen Inhalts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:NX2","text":"Eine überhistorische Gerechtigkeit ist kein Maßstab, aus dem Warenproduktion oder Recht abgeleitet oder umgeformt werden können.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:NX3","text":"Die Person des Warenbesitzers ist nicht als autonome soziale Substanz bestimmt, sondern als ökonomische Charaktermaske.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:NX4","text":"Unmittelbarer Produktenaustausch erzeugt bei wachsender Austauschvielfalt keine dauerhafte, vom besonderen Gebrauchswert unabhängige Wertform.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:NX5","text":"Allgemeine Äquivalenz entsteht nicht durch die Selbstsetzung jeder einzelnen Ware.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:NX6","text":"Geld ist weder bloßes Zeichen noch bloße Konvention noch Schöpfer eines imaginären Warenwerts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:NX7","text":"Die Warenproduktion kann nicht fortbestehen, während ihr notwendiger Gegensatz von Ware und Geld abgeschafft wird.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:D1","text":"Die rechtliche Gleichheit der Vertragsparteien ist die Form, in der ein vorgängiges Verhältnis privater Warenbesitzer erscheint.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A1","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A2","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A3","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:D2","text":"Der Markt prüft, ob private Arbeit gesellschaftlich nützlich ist, indem die Ware für andere Gebrauchswert sein muss, bevor sie als Wert gilt.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A5","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:D3","text":"Der Austausch verbindet individuelle Bedürfnisbefriedigung mit einer Wertgeltung, die über jedes einzelne Bedürfnis hinausreicht.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A5","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A6","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:D4","text":"Allgemeine Äquivalenz kann nur als gesellschaftliche Konzentration der Wertdarstellung auf eine ausgeschlossene Ware entstehen.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A7","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A8","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:D5","text":"Die historische Ausweitung des Austauschs verwandelt zufällige Austauschrelationen in regelmäßige Produktion für den Austausch und stabilisiert die Geldform.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A11","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A12","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:D6","text":"Die stoffliche Eignung der Edelmetalle erklärt nicht ihren sozialen Geldcharakter, sondern erfüllt eine bereits gesellschaftlich erzeugte Geldfunktion.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A9","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A13","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:D7","text":"Die Wahrnehmung des Geldes als selbständig wertvoller Gegenstand beruht auf der Auslöschung des Prozesses, der seine Wertform gesellschaftlich hervorgebracht hat.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A15","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SD1","text":"Der Warenaustausch überführt die Fremdheit zwischen Gemeinwesen in eine Form innerer gesellschaftlicher Vermittlung und erweitert damit die Warenform rückschlagend in das Gemeinleben.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A2","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A11","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SD2","text":"Geld ist keine äußere Ergänzung des Austauschs, sondern die dingliche Gestalt einer kollektiven sozialen Handlung aller Warenbesitzer.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A8","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A9","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SD3","text":"Geld beseitigt den Warenwiderspruch nicht, sondern stabilisiert ihn, indem es die Wertgestalt von der besonderen Ware absondert.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A6","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A9","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SD4","text":"Die objektive Erscheinung des Geldes als gesellschaftliche Naturmacht ist die verdichtete Form eines Produktionsverhältnisses, das den Handelnden nicht als ihr eigenes Werk erscheint.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A3","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A4","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SD5","text":"Moralische Kritik am Geld oder seine bloß rechtliche Umgestaltung verfehlt die Warenproduktion, sofern sie deren ökonomische Voraussetzungen unangetastet lässt.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A3","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A10","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:DNX1","text":"Private Arbeit darf innerhalb der Warenform nicht bereits unmittelbar als gesellschaftliche Arbeit gelten.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:DNX2","text":"Der gemeinsame Wille der Vertragspartner darf nicht als souveräne Ursache des ökonomischen Verhältnisses gelten.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:DNX3","text":"Geld darf weder als natürliche Eigenschaft eines Stoffes noch als willkürliche Erfindung eines Bewusstseins begriffen werden.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:DNX4","text":"Ein Gemeinwesen, dessen Glieder nicht als wechselseitig fremde Privateigentümer auftreten, bildet nicht unmittelbar die Voraussetzung des Warenaustauschs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SNX1","text":"Eine unmittelbare gesellschaftliche Geltung privater Arbeiten ist unter der Warenform nicht verfügbar.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SNX2","text":"Eine dauerhafte Warenproduktion ohne einen verselbständigten allgemeinen Vermittler der Wertform ist nicht verfügbar.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SNX3","text":"Transparente bewusste Kontrolle der sozialen Produktionsverhältnisse ist nicht in ihrer fetischisierten Geldgestalt gegeben.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:FGS1","text":"Äußerliche und veräußerliche Dinge + voneinander unabhängige Personen → Privateigentum + wechselseitige Anerkennung.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:FGS2","text":"Nicht-Gebrauchswert für den Besitzer + Gebrauchswert für den Nicht-Besitzer → Veräußerungs- und Aneignungsbewegung.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:FGS3","text":"Nützlichkeitsbewährung für andere ↔ Wertrealisierung der privaten Arbeit → Austausch als individuelle und gesellschaftliche Doppeloperation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:FGS4","text":"Universeller Wertanspruch jeder Ware → Konkurrenz allgemeiner Äquivalente → Abwesenheit eines wirklichen allgemeinen Äquivalents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:FGS5","text":"Gesellschaftliche Ausschließung einer Ware → gesellschaftlich gültige Äquivalentform → Geld.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:FGS6","text":"Wiederholung, räumliche Ausweitung und Vervielfältigung des Austauschs → Fixierung der Äquivalentfunktion → Auswahl eines geeigneten Geldmaterials.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:FGS7","text":"Kristallisierte Geldform + Verschwinden der vermittelnden Bewegung → Naturalisierung sozialer Verhältnisse → Geldfetisch.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AF1","text":"Warenkörper → Warenhüter → private Anerkennung → Vertrag als Rechtsform des ökonomischen Verhältnisses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AF2","text":"Besitzerseitiger Nicht-Gebrauchswert → fremdseitiger Gebrauchswert → wechselseitige Bewährung von Nützlichkeit und Wert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AF3","text":"Individueller Tauschzweck → allgemeine Wertrealisierung → Scheitern der Selbstsetzung jeder Ware als allgemeines Äquivalent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AF4","text":"Gesellschaftliche Ausschließung einer Ware → allgemeine Äquivalentform → Geld als notwendige Verdopplung der Ware.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AF5","text":"Grenzaustausch zwischen Gemeinwesen → Wiederholung → regelmäßige Warenproduktion → Fixierung der Geldform an geeigneten Waren.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AF6","text":"Geld als Ware → Verwechslung von Wert und Wertform → verschwundene Vermittlung → Geldfetisch als sichtbare Form des Warenfetischs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ME1","text":"Rechtliche Gleichheit und Vertrag werden von Ursachen des Austauschs zu abgeleiteten Erscheinungsformen ökonomischer Beziehungen umklassifiziert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ME2","text":"Gebrauchswert erscheint nicht als bloße stoffliche Eigenschaft, sondern als positionsabhängige Eigenschaft einer Ware im Verhältnis von Besitzer und Nicht-Besitzer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ME3","text":"Der Gegensatz von individuellem und gesellschaftlichem Austausch wird als innere Doppelbestimmung desselben Prozesses sichtbar.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ME4","text":"Geld wird weder als äußerer Maßstab noch als Zeichen, sondern als sozial erzeugte spezifische Wertform einer Ware kartiert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ME5","text":"Die scheinbare Sachlichkeit des Geldes wird als Effekt einer gesellschaftlichen Vermittlung sichtbar, die sich in ihrem Resultat verbirgt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ALT1","text":"Surface — Der Text bestimmt die Bedingungen, unter denen Warenbesitzer ihre Waren veräußern, erwerben und austauschen können.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ALT2","text":"Structural — Der Austausch produziert Privateigentum, Vertragsform, allgemeine Äquivalenz und Geld als Formen gesellschaftlicher Vermittlung.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Moralische, juristische und zeichentheoretische Erklärungen werden durch eine Rekonstruktion ihrer ökonomischen Voraussetzungen entmachtet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Gesellschaftliche Verhältnisse kristallisieren in Dingen, verlieren im Resultat ihre Entstehungsgeschichte und erscheinen deshalb als gegenständliche Natureigenschaften.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT1","text":"Die Axiome reichen aus, um die Bewegung von Privateigentum und Austausch über allgemeine Äquivalenz zur Geldform zu rekonstruieren.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT2","text":"Unterbestimmt bleibt, wodurch die gesellschaftliche Ausschließung einer bestimmten Ware jeweils praktisch durchgesetzt und dauerhaft gesichert wird.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT3","text":"Die Kohärenz des Systems hängt an der Unterscheidung zwischen der notwendigen Geldform und der historisch kontingenten Wahl ihres stofflichen Trägers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0035","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT4","text":"Der stärkste Knoten liegt in der Verbindung von Wertrealisierung, gesellschaftlicher Nützlichkeitsbewährung und der Notwendigkeit eines allgemeinen Äquivalents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT5","text":"Eine strukturelle Spannung bleibt zwischen der notwendigen Ableitung des Geldes aus dem Warenwiderspruch und der kontingenten historischen Fixierung bestimmter Geldwaren.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0020","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT6","text":"Die Möglichkeit gewaltsamer Aneignung wird benannt, aber nicht in die aus wechselseitiger Anerkennung abgeleitete Austauschform integriert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT7","text":"Die Polemik gegen ewige Gerechtigkeit, bloßes Zeichen und imaginären Geldwert verstärkt die Ausschlüsse, trägt jedoch keine eigenständige generative Prämisse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT8","text":"Die Rekonstruktion bewahrt die Bewegung des Textes, sofern die historische Genealogie des Tauschs und die logische Notwendigkeit der Geldform als verschiedene Ebenen behandelt werden.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"de","language_name":"Deutsch","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261016T210713702020Z_f70e326b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"2) The Exchange Process of Commodities.","source_sha256":"5b223c6b5b690af29dbfb1da428168a3988de67d2b66373f4d21f72e70e3cc6e","sha256":"a678711a9e35b510e34582eeef14df26abbfaf6fd2fdc19271db119981cb5cca","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-16T21:07:13.705166+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AS1","text":"The text reconstructs the money-form from the immanent conditions of commodity exchange and diagnoses its reification as a fetish-form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AS2","text":"The exchange of external things presupposes commodity owners who recognize one another as independent private proprietors.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AS3","text":"The legal and contractual relation appears as a form of will whose content is determined by the economic relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AS4","text":"The commodity must at once be non-use-value for its owner, use-value for others, and socially realized value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AS5","text":"The conflict between individual exchange interest and general realization of value generates the necessity of a socially posited general equivalent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AS6","text":"The crystallization of the general equivalent into money operationally resolves the form of exchange, yet conceals its social mediation and produces the money fetish.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A1","text":"Commodities cannot exchange themselves; their alienation and appropriation require acting commodity owners.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A2","text":"Exchange presupposes the mutual recognition of commodity owners as private proprietors.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A3","text":"The legal or volitional relation of the contract reflects an economic relation whose content precedes it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A4","text":"In exchange, persons appear only as bearers and personifications of economic relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A5","text":"The commodity is non-use-value for its owner and use-value for its non-owner.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A6","text":"The realization of the commodity as value and its proving itself as use-value for others are mutually necessary conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A7","text":"For the individual commodity owner, exchange is a need-related individual process and at the same time a social process of value realization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A8","text":"If every commodity posits itself as the general equivalent of all other commodities, no general equivalent exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A9","text":"Only the social exclusion of a particular commodity confers the general equivalent form upon its natural form and makes it money.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A10","text":"The contradiction of the commodity as unity of use-value and exchange-value, as well as of private useful and socially abstract labour, necessarily drives its duplication into commodity and money.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A11","text":"Commodity exchange begins at the boundaries of alien communities and, through repetition, also transforms products of internal communal life into commodities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A12","text":"With the growing number and diversity of acts of exchange, a form of value independent of individual use-value becomes necessary, and the general equivalent is fixed to particular commodities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A13","text":"The money-form initially adheres contingently to particular kinds of commodities and, with the expansion of exchange, attaches itself to commodities capable of bearing the general equivalent function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A14","text":"A money commodity must possess uniform quality and be quantitatively divisible and recombinable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A15","text":"The process of exchange does not give the money commodity its value, but its specific value-form; its value remains determined by the labour-time required for its production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:A16","text":"The completed money-form effaces the trace of its social mediation and makes a social form appear as a natural property of a thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:NX1","text":"The contractual relation is not the original source of its economic content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:NX2","text":"A transhistorical justice is not a standard from which commodity production or law can be derived or transformed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:NX3","text":"The person of the commodity owner is not determined as an autonomous social substance, but as an economic character mask.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:NX4","text":"With growing diversity of exchange, direct exchange of products does not produce a durable form of value independent of particular use-value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:NX5","text":"General equivalence does not arise through the self-positing of each individual commodity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:NX6","text":"Money is neither a mere sign nor a mere convention nor the creator of an imaginary commodity value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:NX7","text":"Commodity production cannot persist while its necessary opposition of commodity and money is abolished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:D1","text":"The legal equality of the contracting parties is the form in which a prior relation of private commodity owners appears.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A1","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A2","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A3","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:D2","text":"The market tests whether private labour is socially useful by requiring the commodity to be use-value for others before it counts as value.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A5","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:D3","text":"Exchange connects individual satisfaction of needs with a validity of value that extends beyond every individual need.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A5","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A6","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:D4","text":"General equivalence can arise only as the social concentration of value representation upon an excluded commodity.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A7","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A8","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:D5","text":"The historical expansion of exchange transforms accidental exchange relations into regular production for exchange and stabilizes the money-form.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A11","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A12","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:D6","text":"The material suitability of the precious metals does not explain their social monetary character, but fulfills a money function already socially produced.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A9","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A13","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:D7","text":"The perception of money as an independently valuable object rests on the effacement of the process that socially produced its value-form.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A15","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SD1","text":"Commodity exchange transfers the alienness between communities into a form of internal social mediation and thereby reactively extends the commodity form into communal life.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A2","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A11","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SD2","text":"Money is not an external supplement to exchange, but the objective form of a collective social act of all commodity owners.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A8","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A9","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SD3","text":"Money does not abolish the commodity contradiction, but stabilizes it by separating the value-form from the particular commodity.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A6","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A9","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SD4","text":"The objective appearance of money as a social force of nature is the condensed form of a relation of production that does not appear to the agents as their own work.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A3","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A4","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SD5","text":"Moral criticism of money or its merely legal transformation misses commodity production insofar as it leaves its economic presuppositions untouched.","refs":["CH_V4GB_2DAD:A3","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A10","CH_V4GB_2DAD:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:DNX1","text":"Within the commodity form, private labour may not already count immediately as social labour.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:DNX2","text":"The common will of the contracting parties may not count as the sovereign cause of the economic relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:DNX3","text":"Money may be understood neither as a natural property of a material nor as an arbitrary invention of consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:DNX4","text":"A community whose members do not appear as mutually alien private proprietors does not immediately constitute the presupposition of commodity exchange.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SNX1","text":"An immediate social validity of private labours is unavailable under the commodity form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SNX2","text":"Durable commodity production without an autonomized general mediator of the value-form is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:SNX3","text":"Transparent conscious control of social relations of production is not given in their fetishized money-form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:FGS1","text":"External and alienable things + mutually independent persons → private property + mutual recognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:FGS2","text":"Non-use-value for the owner + use-value for the non-owner → movement of alienation and appropriation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:FGS3","text":"Proof of usefulness for others ↔ realization of the value of private labour → exchange as an individual and social double operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:FGS4","text":"Universal claim to value by every commodity → competition of general equivalents → absence of a real general equivalent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:FGS5","text":"Social exclusion of one commodity → socially valid equivalent form → money.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:FGS6","text":"Repetition, spatial expansion, and multiplication of exchange → fixation of the equivalent function → selection of a suitable money material.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:FGS7","text":"Crystallized money-form + disappearance of the mediating movement → naturalization of social relations → money fetish.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AF1","text":"Commodity body → commodity owner → private recognition → contract as the legal form of the economic relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AF2","text":"Non-use-value on the owner's side → use-value on the other's side → mutual proof of usefulness and value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AF3","text":"Individual purpose of exchange → general realization of value → failure of every commodity's self-positing as general equivalent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AF4","text":"Social exclusion of one commodity → general equivalent form → money as the necessary duplication of the commodity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AF5","text":"Border exchange between communities → repetition → regular commodity production → fixation of the money-form upon suitable commodities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:AF6","text":"Money as commodity → confusion of value and value-form → vanished mediation → money fetish as the visible form of commodity fetishism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ME1","text":"Legal equality and contract are reclassified from causes of exchange into derivative forms of appearance of economic relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ME2","text":"Use-value appears not as a mere material property, but as a position-dependent property of a commodity in the relation between owner and non-owner.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ME3","text":"The opposition between individual and social exchange becomes visible as an internal double determination of the same process.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ME4","text":"Money is mapped neither as an external standard nor as a sign, but as the socially produced specific value-form of a commodity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ME5","text":"The apparent objectivity of money becomes visible as the effect of a social mediation that conceals itself in its result.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text determines the conditions under which commodity owners can alienate, acquire, and exchange their commodities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ALT2","text":"Structural — Exchange produces private property, contractual form, general equivalence, and money as forms of social mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Moral, juridical, and sign-theoretical explanations are disempowered through a reconstruction of their economic presuppositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Social relations crystallize in things, lose their history of emergence in the result, and therefore appear as objective natural properties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the movement from private property and exchange through general equivalence to the money-form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT2","text":"What remains underdetermined is how the social exclusion of a particular commodity is practically enforced and durably secured in each case.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT3","text":"The coherence of the system depends on the distinction between the necessary money-form and the historically contingent choice of its material bearer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0035","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT4","text":"The strongest nexus lies in the connection of value realization, the social proof of usefulness, and the necessity of a general equivalent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT5","text":"A structural tension remains between the necessary derivation of money from the commodity contradiction and the contingent historical fixation of particular money commodities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0020","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0022","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT6","text":"The possibility of violent appropriation is named, but not integrated into the form of exchange derived from mutual recognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT7","text":"The polemic against eternal justice, the mere sign, and imaginary money-value reinforces the exclusions, but does not provide an independent generative premise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_2DAD:MRT8","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text insofar as the historical genealogy of exchange and the logical necessity of the money-form are treated as distinct levels.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_2DAD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"revision":"CH_V4GB_2DAD@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_V4GB_2DAD:v1","source_language_code":"de"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 02","title":"2) Der Austauschprozess der Waaren.","context":"Chapter 02"}},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO","layer":"CH","title":"3) Das Geld oder die Waarencirculation.","order":3,"parents":["RAW_V4GB_3DGO"],"revision":"CH_V4GB_3DGO@R001","sha256":"1b4d7575edf7d5830a4f9795679d3209c407f5f4f358d1bbdaf88c86335598a4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:AS1","text":"Zu erklären ist, wie die unterschiedlichen Geldfunktionen notwendig aus Wertform, Warenaustausch und Warenmetamorphose hervorgehen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:AS2","text":"Geld ist die verselbständigte Erscheinungsform des Warenwerts und nicht ein dem Austausch äußerlich hinzugefügtes Instrument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:AS3","text":"Die Beweisführung ist rekonstruktiv-dialektisch, diagnostisch gegenüber Verkehrungen der Erscheinung und polemisch gegenüber nominalistischen sowie quantitätstheoretischen Erklärungen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0125","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:AS4","text":"Die Bewegung führt vom Wertmaß über Preis und Zirkulationsmittel zur selbständigen Geldform in Schatz, Zahlungsmittel und Weltgeld.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0151","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0171","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0201","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:AS5","text":"Die äußere Gegenüberstellung von Ware und Geld beseitigt den inneren Widerspruch der Ware nicht, sondern erzeugt seine Bewegungsformen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:AS6","text":"Die Analyse kehrt den Erscheinungsschein um: Warenbewegung erzeugt Geldbewegung, obwohl jene als Wirkung dieser erscheint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:AS7","text":"Mit wachsender Verselbständigung des Geldes werden gesellschaftliche Abhängigkeit, Krisenmöglichkeit und die materielle Geltung des Geldes zugleich gesteigert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0182","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:A1","text":"Waren sind als Werte Vergegenständlichungen abstrakt menschlicher Arbeit; Arbeitszeit ist ihr immanentes Wertmaß.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:A2","text":"Die nur mittelbare Vergesellschaftung privater Arbeit erzwingt die Verdopplung des Arbeitsprodukts in Ware und Geldware.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:A3","text":"Eine Ware wird zum allgemeinen Äquivalent, indem alle anderen Waren ihre Werte in ihr ausdrücken.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:A4","text":"Der Preis ist die ideelle Geldform des Warenwerts und bezieht die Ware auf ein bestimmtes Quantum realen Geldmaterials.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:A5","text":"Das Geld als Maß der Werte ist vom Maßstab der Preise als fixierter, geteilter und gesetzlich benannter Metallmenge zu unterscheiden.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:A6","text":"Die Preisform lässt quantitative Abweichungen vom Wert und qualitative Preise von wertlosen Dingen zu.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:A7","text":"Die Preisform setzt die Veräußerlichkeit der Ware und die Verwandlung ihrer ideellen Geldgestalt in wirkliches Geld voraus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:A8","text":"Der Widerspruch von Gebrauchswert und Wert wird in der Polarität von Ware und Geld als Bewegung W–G–W entfaltet, nicht aufgehoben.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:A9","text":"Im Verkauf muss sich private Arbeit nachträglich als gesellschaftlich nützlich, gesellschaftlich notwendig und proportional verteilt bewähren.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:A10","text":"Verkauf und Kauf sind als polarer Austauschakt identisch, bleiben aber als aufeinanderfolgende Handlungen derselben Person zeitlich, räumlich und personell trennbar.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:A11","text":"Die Verschlingung selbständiger Warenmetamorphosen konstituiert die Warenzirkulation als unkontrollierten gesellschaftlichen Zusammenhang.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:A12","text":"Der Geldumlauf ist die erscheinende Form der Warenzirkulation und ihrer Formwechsel.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_V4GB_3DGO:A13","text":"Die erforderliche Masse des Zirkulationsmittels wird durch die zu realisierende Preissumme und die durchschnittliche Umlaufsgeschwindigkeit bestimmt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_V4GB_3DGO:P0105","trace_type":"Dire